inl note_of_backend_coverage_gaps_1 () = Note ("BACKEND-COVERAGE-GAPS", 1u32, "2026-10-14T12:32Z", "main-thread", "08:32 row counts in single-flight-20261014-073517 (rows/agree): Bend 344/343, Zig 146/144, Lua 137/137, Lean 137/136, Gleam 129/128, Ebpf 51/51, Wgsl 33/33, Python 22/19, OpenQasm 21/21, Calyx 9/9. Every non-agree row is Known or no-C. Biggest gaps: Calyx, OpenQasm, Python, Wgsl. Next: run -Probe over the Bend list for Calyx and Wgsl to find samples that already work.")
inl note_of_backend_coverage_gaps_2 () = Note ("BACKEND-COVERAGE-GAPS", 2u32, "2026-10-14T13:42Z", "main-thread", "09:42 compile probe (installed single-flight DLL) of the Kotlin-list samples missing from each backend list: OpenQasm 4 of 231 compile (strings block 113, heap layouts 12, f64 10, i8 8, closures 8, i64 7: lifted in source), Wgsl 16 of 225 (strings 120, f64 13, heap layouts 13, i64 10, i8 9, closures 9), Lean 222 compile, nearly the whole Kotlin set. Lists in job tmp/rotation/coverage-probe/<Backend>-compiles.txt. Next, after the bless: add the compiling OpenQasm/Wgsl candidates and keep those that agree with C. Lean costs about 5 s per row, so widen it by sample family rather than all at once. Strings are the shared blocker for the GPU/quantum backends.")
inl note_of_backend_coverage_gaps_3 () = Note ("BACKEND-COVERAGE-GAPS", 3u32, "2026-10-14T15:34Z", "main-thread", "11:34 lists widened (single-flight-20261014-112558): Calyx 9 -> 21, OpenQasm 21 -> 25, Wgsl 33 -> 48; all 32 new rows that stay agree with C. Removed after failing: Calyx native_tail_recursion (cider timeout), ebpf_arith and ebpf_div (Calyx signed remainder bug, fixed in source), Wgsl recur_sum (non-tail recursion is invalid WGSL).")
inl note_of_backend_coverage_gaps_4 () = Note ("BACKEND-COVERAGE-GAPS", 4u32, "2026-10-15T10:48Z", "main-thread", "06:48 groom: rows per backend in the pack-1h oracle (single-flight-20261015-052820): C 513, Rust 421 (407 agree), Delphi 408, Bend 344, Swift/Kotlin 248, Wasm 150, Zig 146, Lua/Lean 137, Gleam 129, TypeScript 117, Ebpf 52, Wgsl 50, OpenQasm 26, Calyx 23, Python 22, Cpp 16; DISAGREE 0 everywhere. The gap that matters now is Zig: 146 rows (28% of C) and lib arms in 9 of 195 lib files, which blocks DEFAULT-BACKEND-DECISION. Raised P2 -> P1, Zig first.")
inl note_of_backend_coverage_gaps_5 () = Note ("BACKEND-COVERAGE-GAPS", 5u32, "2026-10-15T14:43Z", "lane-b", "10:43 lane-b: Zig probe on a copied tree (test.ps1 -Suite examples -Backend C,Zig -Probe Zig -Native, run single-flight-20261015-103412, 04:05 compiler): Zig agrees with C on 428 rows, DISAGREE 0. 294 of those are not on the Zig list, and each runs in at most 1.9 s natively. Patch patches/zig-list-growth.ps1 takes the Zig list from 147 to 441 rows. The 34 build-fail rows (abi_external C shims, dynamic_array shim includes, native_cube, while_*) stay off. Found: Import-PowerShellDataFile rejects an array literal with more than about 352 newline-separated items; Bend is at 344. The patch adds its rows as one comma-joined statement.")
inl note_of_backend_coverage_gaps_6 () = Note ("BACKEND-COVERAGE-GAPS", 6u32, "2026-10-15T14:44Z", "main-thread", "10:44 lane B probe single-flight-20261015-103412 (C,Zig -Probe Zig): Zig agrees with C on 428 rows, DISAGREE 0; 294 not yet listed added to the Zig list (147 -> 441) via zig-list-growth.ps1, applied to the tree for the pack-1k review. 34 build-fail rows (abi_external_*, dynamic_array_*, native_cube_*, while_*) left off as the next coverage targets.")
inl note_of_backend_coverage_gaps_7 () = Note ("BACKEND-COVERAGE-GAPS", 7u32, "2026-10-15T15:14Z", "lane-b", "11:14 lane-b: main applied zig-list-growth (Zig 147 -> 441). Wasm probe (single-flight-20261015-110433): Wasm agrees with C on 415 rows, DISAGREE 0; 277 are new, and the 243 whose native run takes 3 s or less go into patches/wasm-list-growth.ps1 (Wasm 150 -> 393). Found the real psd1 limit: Import-PowerShellDataFile has a whole-file safe-value budget, which the grown harness exceeds even with comma-joined rows. patches/harness-skip-limit-check.ps1 adds -SkipLimitCheck to test.ps1 and fast-tier.ps1, the only two readers.")
inl note_of_backend_coverage_gaps_8 () = Note ("BACKEND-COVERAGE-GAPS", 8u32, "2026-10-15T15:14Z", "main-thread", "11:14 Wasm probe single-flight-20261015-110433 (lane B): Wasm agrees with C on 415 rows, DISAGREE 0; 243 rows (native <= 3 s) ready to add (150 -> 393), queued for pack-1l together with the harness loader fix (-SkipLimitCheck; Import-PowerShellDataFile has a whole-file safe-value budget that the next growth would exceed).")
inl note_of_backend_coverage_gaps_9 () = Note ("BACKEND-COVERAGE-GAPS", 9u32, "2026-10-15T15:19Z", "main-thread", "11:19 Lua probe single-flight-20261015-111000 (lane B, with exact 64-bit Lua): 422 rows agree with C; 289 unlisted rows (native <= 3 s) ready: Lua 140 -> 429. Queued for pack-1l with Wasm 150 -> 393. After pack-1l: Zig 441, Wasm 393, Lua 429 against C 513.")
inl note_of_backend_coverage_gaps_10 () = Note ("BACKEND-COVERAGE-GAPS", 10u32, "2026-10-15T20:30Z", "main-thread", "16:30 installed in pack-1l (bless 160759): Wasm 150 -> 393 and Lua 140 -> 429 rows, all agree with C; Zig 441 since pack-1k. Oracle 5,016 rows. Next: TypeScript, Gleam, Lean, Kotlin/Swift probes.")
inl note_of_backend_coverage_gaps_11 () = Note ("BACKEND-COVERAGE-GAPS", 11u32, "2026-10-15T21:12Z", "main-thread", "17:12 TypeScript probe (main, single-flight-20261015-170857): 430 rows agree with C; 316 new rows (native <= 3 s) staged for pack-1n (TS 117 -> 433). DISAGREE 22, all unlisted abi_external_*/dynamic_array_* (C-shim and growth-semantics samples) = next TS targets.")
inl note_of_backend_coverage_gaps_12 () = Note ("BACKEND-COVERAGE-GAPS", 12u32, "2026-10-15T21:47Z", "main-thread", "17:47 Gleam probe (main, single-flight-20261015-174242): 408 rows agree with C; 283 new rows staged for pack-1n (Gleam 129 -> 412). 1 DISAGREE (array_create_reads_zero) and 44 build-fail rows are the next Gleam targets.")
inl note_of_backend_coverage_gaps_13 () = Note ("BACKEND-COVERAGE-GAPS", 13u32, "2026-10-15T23:41Z", "main-thread", "19:41 main: wrong answers found by today's probes on unlisted samples (agree-with-C oracle, installed compiler): Kotlin native_managed_scc_tail_recursion, dynamic_array_bounds_negative, wgsl_unit_self_loop (probe single-flight-20261015-192928); Swift dynamic_array_mutable_alias, dynamic_array_union_shared_alias, dynamic_array_union_nested_shared_alias, dynamic_array_bounds_negative, wgsl_unit_self_loop (193523); TypeScript 22 abi_external_*/dynamic_array_* (170857); Gleam array_create_reads_zero (174242). Shared aliasing of dynamic arrays is the common thread on Swift (value-type arrays).")
inl note_of_backend_coverage_gaps_14 () = Note ("BACKEND-COVERAGE-GAPS", 14u32, "2026-10-15T23:42Z", "main-thread", "19:42 main: triage: Swift aliasing -> new item SWIFT-ARRAY-REFERENCE-SEMANTICS (dynamic_array_mutable_alias exits -7 = writes inside a join point lost, Swift [T] is a value type). Kotlin native_managed_scc_tail_recursion exits 1 (C 0) on a 1,000,000-deep self tail call that swaps arguments: most likely a JVM StackOverflowError because the join point is not lowered to a loop/tailrec; unverified, the probe does not keep the generated .kt. dynamic_array_bounds_negative and wgsl_unit_self_loop disagree on several backends (shim/C-specific expectations), not Kotlin/Swift bugs.")
inl note_of_backend_coverage_gaps_15 () = Note ("BACKEND-COVERAGE-GAPS", 15u32, "2026-10-16T00:07Z", "main-thread", "20:07 main: Kotlin native_managed_scc_tail_recursion diagnosed from source: codegenKotlin lowers only self tail calls (self_tail -> while/continue, spiral_compiler.fs 170128-170161), but here left/right are static, so swapping them targets a second specialization of keep: two join points in a mutually tail-recursive SCC, 1,000,000 deep. JVM has no tail calls -> StackOverflowError (exit 1); C passes via gcc sibling-call optimization. Fix direction for Kotlin (and Swift/TypeScript/Lua if they share it): lower a tail-call SCC of join points into one loop over a state tag (trampoline-free), as the managed backends need.")
inl note_of_backend_coverage_gaps_16 () = Note ("BACKEND-COVERAGE-GAPS", 16u32, "2026-10-16T00:42Z", "main-thread", "20:42 main: Gleam array_create_reads_zero diagnosed: codegenGleam's array helpers (spiral_compiler.fs ~166343) store Option(a) with None for unset slots and spiral_array_get panics 'read of an element that was never set' (exit 1; C reads zero, exit 5). Spiral semantics are zero-initialized like C. Fix direction: TyArrayCreate(a, b) (~166682) knows the element type, so emit spiral_array_create_filled(size, <zero of a>) storing Some(zero), and keep the None panic only for genuinely uninitializable element types; then re-probe the sample onto the Gleam list.")
inl note_of_backend_coverage_gaps_17 () = Note ("BACKEND-COVERAGE-GAPS", 17u32, "2026-10-16T05:16Z", "main-thread", "01:16 main: Gleam zero-fill in pack-1p: TyArrayCreate with a primitive element emits spiral_array_create_filled(n, zero) (0, 0.0, False, empty string, NUL char); other element types keep the None-and-panic storage. array_create_reads_zero now exits 5 like C and joined the Gleam list; 119 Gleam residual-only changes, behaviour 0. Non-primitive zeros (unions, layouts) still open.")
inl note_of_backend_coverage_gaps_18 () = Note ("BACKEND-COVERAGE-GAPS", 18u32, "2026-10-16T07:20Z", "main-thread", "03:20 main: pack-1q (in review) closes on Kotlin+Swift: the SCC tail recursion (1 GB main thread; Swift via WinSDK CreateThread reserved stack, also fixes Swift c_deep_list_decref stack overflow), recursive unions (sentinel zero), records returned from join points, float math, arrays of tuples, multi-scrutinee union unbox, Swift @escaping function parameters (~25 native_closure_* build-fails). Still open: Bend has no multi-scrutinee unbox (codegenBend TyUnionUnbox [single] only); Kotlin/Swift Printf; Kotlin/Swift BackendSwitch samples without their key (sample-side); dynamic_array_* C-only helpers and abi_external_* are C-specific by design on TS/Kotlin/Swift.")
inl note_of_backend_coverage_gaps_19 () = Note ("BACKEND-COVERAGE-GAPS", 19u32, "2026-10-16T08:47Z", "main-thread", "04:47 main: pack-1q installed both modes and blessed (single-flight-20261016-041645, 6628 rows, DISAGREE 0; review = Kotlin probe single-flight-20261016-032411 + Swift probe single-flight-20261016-035425); the Kotlin SCC tail-recursion, Swift aliasing and Gleam zero-fill wrong answers from 10-15 are all fixed and listed.")
inl note_of_backend_coverage_gaps_20 () = Note ("BACKEND-COVERAGE-GAPS", 20u32, "2026-10-16T17:19Z", "main-thread", "13:19 main: pack-1w installed (12:46/12:52) and blessed: single-flight-20261016-125241, 6,888 rows, DISAGREE 0, 0 differing cells against pack-1v's 112838 overridden by the Zig/Gleam/Lean/Wasm candidate probes and the Lua/TypeScript census runs. Fixes: Zig unit macros as statements (while_control, while_loop, native_cube_*), Gleam multi-subject match wildcards, Lean Inhabited for IO.Ref (heap layouts with arrays), fixture byte_to_i32 (Gleam braces, Wasm arm). Lists +130: Zig 455, Gleam 438, Lean 432, Wasm 441, Lua 439, AlsoTypeScript 427.")
inl note_of_backend_coverage_gaps_21 () = Note ("BACKEND-COVERAGE-GAPS", 21u32, "2026-10-16T18:26Z", "main-thread", "14:26 main: pack-1x installed (13:54/14:01) and blessed: single-flight-20261016-140122, 6,903 rows, DISAGREE 0, 0 differing cells against 125241 + Lean 133759 / Gleam 134921 probes. Lean: types in dependency order (mutual only for real cycles; heap_mut_cell, union_mut agree), f32/f64 conversions (f64_scalar, ebpf_cast). Gleam: string/char order via string.compare (string_ord, managed_string_codepoints), union case position by name (the 2 index-out-of-range codegen crashes: wgsl_gadt_payload_union, brzozowski runtime_native), float literal exponents (lua_wide_integers). Lean 442, Gleam 443 rows. The bless ran the new test.ps1 -Oracle default (C): unchanged.")
inl note_of_backend_coverage_gaps_22 () = Note ("BACKEND-COVERAGE-GAPS", 22u32, "2026-10-16T19:19Z", "main-thread", "15:19 main: pack-1y installed (14:47/14:54) and blessed: single-flight-20261016-145428, 6,906 rows, DISAGREE 0, 0 differing cells against pack-1x's 140122 + Zig 142849 / Lean 143209 probes. Lean maxRecDepth for >400-line bodies (typecheck_long_let_body agrees, 62 s so unlisted), lib convert Lean arms (portable_convert_parse agrees), Zig arms for ts_while_loop/ts_float_nan (agree). Remaining census gaps are backend limits: C-only samples, single-backend macro samples (ebpf_*, rust_*), Bend 64-bit/f64, 2 Lean IO.Ref cycles, Wasm C-syntax macros.")
inl note_of_backend_coverage_gaps_23 () = Note ("BACKEND-COVERAGE-GAPS", 23u32, "2026-10-17T10:02Z", "main-thread", "06:02 main-thread night map (review 232243): DISAGREE 0 on every backend; agree vs rows: Bend 400/401, Calyx 28/28, Cpp 12/16, Delphi 402/414, Ebpf 52/52, Gleam 442/443, Kotlin 444/444, Lean 442/443, Lua 439/439, OpenQasm 31/31, Python 443/444, Rust 412/427, Swift 443/443, TypeScript 447/447, Wasm 440/441, Wgsl 50/50, Zig 455/457; the gaps are coverage and the unsupported shapes in AGENTS.md.")
inl note_of_backend_coverage_gaps_24 () = Note ("BACKEND-COVERAGE-GAPS", 24u32, "2026-10-18T08:55Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 04:55 main-thread gap probes (installed compiler, each backend vs C over only the C native samples it has no row for; Lean reaped for memory, rerun pending): already agree and now listed (tree): Lua +3, Python +7, AlsoTypeScript +2, Gleam +3, Wasm +2, Zig +2 (460), Kotlin +5, Swift +5 (frontier_gadt_growing_index, frontier_hopacsota_*, macro_splice_before_quote, zig_exit_wide, c_deep_list_decref, c_self_tail_loop_heap). Wrong answers found and fixed in the tree, not built yet: Lua integer Conv below 64 bits was a plain copy (ebpf_conv exit 2), Lua multi-arg closures unpacked into _ (3 prototype_record_callback samples exit 1, also SPIRAL-LUA-CLOSURE-RECORD-ARG), Lua ( statements (SPIRAL-LUA-PAREN-STATEMENT, sample lua_paren_statement), lib math atan2 Gleam arm needed gleam_community_maths (portable_math_rounding Unknown module; now @external erlang math atan2). Kotlin/Swift now emit globals at the top of the file (frontier_hopacsota_globals_order listed). Not bugs: TypeScript zig_exit_wide (node truncates exit codes to 8 bits). The rest is by nature: C shims (abi_external, dynamic_array helpers, native_managed_array_scc), samples with only C/Rust/eBPF/Python macros, Wasm C-syntax macros, Bend 64-bit (Bend 2 literals are only Nat/U32/F32). Next: format_debug_real Kotlin/Swift/Wasm arms (frontier_format_any_union, frontier_join_format_any) after git-0e's sm'_real.spir check.")
inl note_of_backend_coverage_gaps_25 () = Note ("BACKEND-COVERAGE-GAPS", 25u32, "2026-10-18T10:02Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 06:02 main-thread pack built (single-flight, gpt5parallel 496 s, hopacsota 158 s, pair-drift 0 one-sided) and reviewed: single-flight-20261018-055209 vs bless 182920: 6,986 rows (6,939 before, missing 0), oracle agree 5,432 (5,391), DISAGREE 0, 0 behavior changes, 8 build-fail as in the bless; one residual-only change on an existing row (Lua small_int_scalar, Conv wrap; still agrees). In: Lua Conv wrap, multi-arg closure unpack, ( statements as do ... end (sample lua_paren_statement); Kotlin/Swift globals at the top of the file; lib sm'_real.spir Kotlin/Swift arms in format_debug_real and format_real (empty, piece, append; floats use native toString or description, not C's shortest text yet); lib math atan2 Gleam via erlang math. Lean probe (Parallel 1): 9 agree; listed the 4 under 6 s natively (frontier_gadt_growing_index, macro_splice_before_quote, zig_exit_wide, native_string_utf8_canonical_equal_bounded_source); left off as timeout risks: c_deep_list_decref 8.7 s, native_cube_frame_reuse_direct 8 s, c_self_tail_loop_heap 17 s, runtime_bench 17 s, typecheck_long_let_body 93 s. omniledger runtime_native times out on Lean. Bless next.")
inl note_of_backend_coverage_gaps_26 () = Note ("BACKEND-COVERAGE-GAPS", 26u32, "2026-10-18T10:49Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 06:49 main-thread: blessed in bless single-flight-20261018-061211 (6,990 rows, reproduces review 060650 with 0 differing cells, DISAGREE 0). Every backend's remaining C-native gap is now classified (C shims, single-backend macros, Wasm C-syntax macros, Bend 64-bit, Lean slow rows, TS 8-bit exit code); open shapes: Kotlin/Swift float text in format_real (native toString, not C's shortest form), Swift batch wrapper hoisting global imports, Bend 64-bit emulation, Lean speed for the 5 slow agreeing rows.")
inl note_of_backend_coverage_gaps_27 () = Note ("BACKEND-COVERAGE-GAPS", 27u32, "2026-10-18T11:42Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 07:42 main-thread float text: lib format_real now prints f32/f64 on Kotlin and Swift through global helpers spiralShortestText, which lay C's %g out from each language's shortest digits (Kotlin Double/Float.toString via BigDecimal, Swift description), and the Lua arm normalizes Windows MSVCRT three-digit exponents (1e+002 -> 1e+02). Hand probe (scratch, show over 100, 1e10, 0.0001, 1e-05, 123.45, -2.5, 1.5e22, 0.1f32, 16777216f32, 1.5): C, Lua, Kotlin and Swift print byte-identical text. test.ps1 Swift batch lifts import lines out of enum SpiralBatch<slot>. Review single-flight-20261018-070325 vs bless 061211: 6,990 rows, 0 differing cells, DISAGREE 0, Kotlin server 452/452 (no harness row prints floats through format_real on these backends, so no re-bless). Rust, TypeScript, Python and Lean still print shortest digits in their own exponent layout; only show_floats' moderate values are pinned across backends.")
inl note_of_backend_coverage_gaps_28 () = Note ("BACKEND-COVERAGE-GAPS", 28u32, "2026-10-18T11:49Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 07:49 main-thread correction (TypeScript zig_exit_wide): node is not the cause, node keeps 305 on the main thread and through a Worker; the harness runs TS with Bun (test.ps1 Invoke-Native tools.Bun run_main.mjs), and Bun 1.4.3 on Windows masks process.exitCode and process.exit to 8 bits at assignment (process.exitCode = 305 reads back 49), and process.exitCode is unconfigurable, so the runner cannot intercept it. Left off the TS list; exit codes above 255 are 8-bit on POSIX anyway (zig_exit_wide only holds on Windows).")
inl note_of_backend_coverage_gaps_29 () = Note ("BACKEND-COVERAGE-GAPS", 29u32, "2026-10-18T12:32Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 08:32 main-thread Lean slow rows are the interpreter, not codegen: c_self_tail_loop_heap (17 s) is already a repeat loop with let mut locals; its 1,000,000 iterations each read the IO.Ref array (spiralIndex (<- v1.get)) under lean --run's IR interpreter, about 17 us per iteration. The fix for the 5 slow agreeing Lean rows is compiling them (lean -c plus a C compile, or a lake exe) behind a size or time threshold, a toolchain change for the Lean native tier; hoisting loop-invariant ref reads out of repeat would help only this shape.")
inl note_of_backend_coverage_gaps_30 () = Note ("BACKEND-COVERAGE-GAPS", 30u32, "2026-10-18T13:48Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 09:48 main-thread design note for Bend 64-bit (95 C samples missing on Bend, all i64/u64/f64): Bend 2's literal kinds are Nat (unbounded), U32 and F32. u64 can be a Nat reduced mod 2^64 after each arithmetic op, and i64 a Nat holding the two's-complement value (sign test = value >= 2^63; division, remainder and comparisons convert first), so integers need no {hi, lo} pairs as Lua does, only one helper per op, emitted on use as Lua's spiral_w64 is. f64 has no Bend 2 type; a software float is the only route and likely too slow to agree in time, so f64 rows stay off. Expected gain: the integer half of the 95 rows.")
inl note_of_backend_coverage_gaps_31 () = Note ("BACKEND-COVERAGE-GAPS", 31u32, "2026-10-19T12:21Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 08:21 lap 1 grain: scoreboard from pack 5 review single-flight-20261019-072348 (C 531 rows = 100%), samples with a C row but no row on the backend: Zig 65, Kotlin 79, Swift 80, Python 81, TypeScript 82, Lua 83, Lean 84, Gleam 84, Wasm 89, Rust 94, Delphi 106, Bend 130, F# 178 (F# also carries 449 contract-only rows); subsets Ebpf 52, Wgsl 50, OpenQasm 31, Calyx 28, Cpp 16 rows Next: per backend, bucket the missing samples by cause (stack layouts, C macros, recursive unions, i64) from the matrix lists to pick the largest closable bucket")
inl note_of_backend_coverage_gaps_32 () = Note ("BACKEND-COVERAGE-GAPS", 32u32, "2026-10-19T12:59Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 08:59 lap 2 grain: bucketed the C samples missing per backend by source features (crude regex tags, a sample can carry several; pack 5 accept run): Zig 65 missing (macro 38, string 35, array 18, union 14), Rust 94 (string 53, macro 45), Lean 84 (macro 49, string 48), Wasm 89 (macro 58, string 55), Bend 130 (string 68, macro 45, array 41, f64 31, union 28), Wgsl 481 and Calyx 503 (string ~250, union ~140); C-only macro text is the largest bucket for Zig, Lean and Wasm, so PORTABLE-PRIMITIVES (macros -> portable ops) is the lever Next: for Zig's 38 macro samples, list the macro texts by frequency to pick the first portable op")
inl note_of_backend_coverage_gaps_33 () = Note ("BACKEND-COVERAGE-GAPS", 33u32, "2026-10-19T18:18Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 14:18 lap 4 grain: buckets on the pack 7 accept (125623) are unchanged from pack 5 (Zig 65, Rust 94, Lean 84, Wasm 89, Bend 130, Wgsl 481, Calyx 503 missing); the cheapest Wgsl batch found: 80 C run-ok samples with none of strings, arrays, unions, 64-bit, sub-word, globals, failwith, of which the 25 ebpf_* scalar samples (arith, bits, shifts, casts, float, tuples) are one family already proven portable on the eBPF profile Next: a -Probe Wgsl rung row over the 25 ebpf_* samples (and the same for Calyx), then add the agreeing ones to both lists")
inl note_of_backend_coverage_gaps_34 () = Note ("BACKEND-COVERAGE-GAPS", 34u32, "2026-10-19T21:41Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 17:41 lap 5 grain: Zig probe rung on the installed pack 10 compiler (single-flight-20261019-173558, -Probe Zig): all 65 C samples without a Zig row stay out by nature, 36 compile errors (C-only macros and Rust/Python-specific samples) and 27 native build-fails (C ABI shims), 0 new agreeing rows, matching AGENTS.md (38 by-nature samples); so Zig coverage is complete for portable code; batch census tooling added: lab/BACKEND-COVERAGE-GAPS/blockers.ps1 (one --batch process per backend) and probe-queue.ps1 (sequential -Probe rungs with compile and native reuse) Next: read the Lean, Bend, Wasm and Rust probe rungs (running) and stage their agreeing rows with add-to-list.py")
inl note_of_backend_coverage_gaps_35 () = Note ("BACKEND-COVERAGE-GAPS", 35u32, "2026-10-19T22:11Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 18:11 lap 5 grain: probe rungs on the pack 10 compiler, new rows that agree with C: Rust 50 (single-flight-20261019-180605; 15 compile errors, 27 C-ABI build-fails, 2 POSIX-only cube rows), Lean 9 (+1 too slow for the row timeout: omniledger runtime, + runtime_bench left out as Lua does), Bend 2 (+ zig_exit_wide, exit-width), Wasm 1, Zig 0; Rust rows come from the matrix Backends table, so lab/BACKEND-COVERAGE-GAPS/add-backend-to-groups.py moves a sample into the group with Rust inserted (35 C -> C,Rust, 15 into a new C,Rust,TypeScript,Cpp,Python), dry run clean; all staged as <Backend>-next.txt for pack 11 with the 55 Wgsl rows Next: Calyx and OpenQasm rungs (running), then pack 11 review on a Release candidate")
inl note_of_backend_coverage_gaps_36 () = Note ("BACKEND-COVERAGE-GAPS", 36u32, "2026-10-20T00:21Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 20:21 main: pack 11 ACCEPTED 20:21 (single-flight-20261019-191017: 7,276 rows, DISAGREE 0, 0 differing cells vs the Release candidate review 190039); 219 new agreeing rows from the probe rungs: Wgsl 55, OpenQasm 64, Rust 50, Calyx 38, Lean 9, Bend 2, Wasm 1.")
inl note_of_backend_coverage_gaps_37 () = Note ("BACKEND-COVERAGE-GAPS", 37u32, "2026-10-20T02:33Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 22:33 user 2026-10-19 21:4x: all backends finished and SOTA, max parity; raised to P0. State tonight: packs 9 and 11 added 256 agreeing rows via probe rungs (lab/BACKEND-COVERAGE-GAPS/blockers.ps1, probe-queue.ps1); remaining gaps per backend are in blockers census runs.")
inl note_of_backend_coverage_gaps_38 () = Note ("BACKEND-COVERAGE-GAPS", 38u32, "2026-10-20T03:25Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 23:25 main: back to P1. I had raised it to P0 at 22:3x from the user's also-need list; the user's order is XSD first, then full hopac SOTA, so it waits behind those.")
inl note_of_backend_coverage_gaps_39 () = Note ("BACKEND-COVERAGE-GAPS", 39u32, "2026-10-20T04:29Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-20 00:29 main: pack 13 ACCEPTED 00:27 (single-flight-20261019-231348, 7,290 rows; helper lane 2's width stack: Calyx i64/u64 and sub-word ints, OpenQasm and eBPF sub-word ints, Lean boxed closures, plus 14 new rows: Calyx 5, OpenQasm 4, Ebpf 3, Lean 2) with one correction: the accept run timed out typecheck_long_let_body on Lean (188 s under memory pressure, 1.4 GB free; the review ran it in 69 s with exit 236, agreeing), the accept still wrote exit=timeout into EXPECTED.tsv, so that one row was corrected to the reproduced 236 (residual hash checked identical; backup EXPECTED.tsv.before-lean-fix-20261020); the scratchpad accept.ps1 now backs up the baseline and restores it when its own run has any DISAGREE row.")
inl note_of_backend_coverage_gaps_40 () = Note ("BACKEND-COVERAGE-GAPS", 40u32, "2026-10-22T06:20Z", "main", "02:20 main: lab drained. The 5 tools are now in apps/compiler/tmp/scripts, with paths rewritten for that location and verified (parse clean; the dry run of matrix-add-to-list.py resolves tests/matrix.psd1 and reports 'Ebpf: already listed' for i64_scalar): coverage-buckets.ps1 (feature buckets per backend from a run's results.tsv), coverage-blockers.ps1 (C rows not on a backend's list, as a probe table), coverage-probe-queue.ps1 (test.ps1 -Probe per backend), matrix-add-to-list.py and matrix-add-backend-to-groups.py (matrix.psd1 list edits; they write only with --apply). Deleted as applied: Calyx/Ebpf/Lean/OpenQasm-next.txt, the exact 14 rows pack 13 added (Calyx 5, OpenQasm 4, Ebpf 3, Lean 2), and applied-pack11/*-next.txt (pack 11). Folder deleted.")
inl note_of_backend_coverage_gaps_41 () = Note ("BACKEND-COVERAGE-GAPS", 41u32, "2026-10-26T12:38Z", "compiler", "New Zig gap found 2026-10-26 (compiler lane) via kit's rename-release blocker: codegenZig has NO Export case. Every !!!!Export(name, f) falls through to the generic op fallthrough at spiral_compiler.fs:166236 (\"%A with %i args not supported in the Zig backend\"); Export's op arg list is always [name; f] so it always prints \"2 args\" regardless of the exported function's arity. Exposed (not a regression) by Cells notebook.ex default_builders giving untargeted cells ///> zig (commit 59b477ba, 10-17) when there is no --test static prelude and no SPIRAL_CELLS_DEFAULT_TARGET: kit's sequences.spi (first line ///- --package . , no --test static) routed its Export-bearing package-entry cell to Zig and hit this. Release unblocked kit-side with SPIRAL_CELLS_DEFAULT_TARGET=rust in kit-dev (kit ships Rust; codegenRust has full Export at 163566). PROPER fix (compiler lane, pack 5n, not release-blocking): add a codegenZig Export case mirroring codegenRust's export builder (163566 + the decl/call tuple->params logic at 164741-164784) -- emit a Zig `export fn <name>(params) <ret>` that unpacks the exported fn's domain (tuple -> one param per field, record -> struct) and calls the closure; needs a build + -Backend Zig -Native verify + a regression sample.")
inl note_of_backend_coverage_gaps_42 () = Note ("BACKEND-COVERAGE-GAPS", 42u32, "2026-10-26T16:06Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-26 16:xx (re-validate, build-independent): the codegenZig Export apply draft (this note's Zig-export fix for pack-5n) is STILL LANDABLE against the current spiral_compiler.fs after main's pack-5n repack. Re-checked: the insertion anchor -- the Zig statement-level `| TyOp(Assign, [a;b]) -> line s $\"{tup a} = {tup b};\"` -- is at 166180 and UNIQUE (the 165457 `:=` one is Delphi, different operator). Confirmed 166180 is inside codegenZig (spiralIndex / std.mem.lessThan / +%% ops around it) and that codegenZig STILL has NO `TyOp(Export,...)` case (Export cases live only at 163566/164698 Rust and 168697/174892 others), so the fix is still needed and inserts cleanly after 166180, before the `TyOp(op,l)` expression fallthrough at 166181. Export binding pattern matches the other backends: `TyOp(Export, [DLit (LitString name); DV(L(i,YFun(domain,range,_)))])`. No change to the apply draft needed; ready to land when a Debug build slot frees. (Flagging because line refs in sibling items drifted under the repack -- DELPHI-ARC note 2's 175371-175375 went stale -- but this anchor did not.)")
inl note_of_backend_coverage_gaps_43 () = Note ("BACKEND-COVERAGE-GAPS", 43u32, "2026-10-26T16:16Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-26 (build-independent census, contextualizes note 41 Zig Export): mapped TyOp(Export) coverage across ALL backends using the codegen-fn boundaries in spiral_compiler.fs. Export is implemented in exactly FOUR backends: codegenFsharp (163566), codegenRust (164698), codegenGleam (168697), codegenTypeScript (174892). It is ABSENT from: Delphi [165179-165899], Zig [165899-166495], Lean, Bend, Lua, Wgsl, Calyx, Kotlin, Swift, OpenQasm, Ebpf, C, Python (TyOp(Export) hits are only 163566 / 164205 the walk / 164698 / 168697 / 174892).\n\nInterpretation: the 4 Export-capable backends are exactly the LIBRARY/MODULE-emitting ones (.NET assemblies, Rust crates, BEAM + JS modules) where an export is meaningful; the rest emit standalone executables or specialized targets, so Export was never added -- CONSUMER-ABSENCE, not type-impossibility. So the Zig Export gap (note 41) is really \"Zig is the FIRST executable-idiom backend to need exports\" (driven by kit's Cells use case), and the fix pattern (a statement-level TyOp(Export) case wrapping the closure in an exported fn, mirroring Rust at 164698; Zig design in scratchpad/zig-export-fix-design.md) is the REUSABLE TEMPLATE for any future backend.\n\nNotable: Delphi lacks Export despite being a shipping backend -- but no consumer needs Delphi exports today, so it is a known coverage fact, not a live bug; adding it later is the same small pattern. C and Python also lack it (C produces executables; Python's module idiom didn't need the op). No action beyond the Zig fix now; this is the map so future \"add Export to backend X\" work knows the template + which backends are missing it.")
inl note_of_backend_coverage_gaps_44 () = Note ("BACKEND-COVERAGE-GAPS", 44u32, "2026-10-26T16:20Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-26 (build-independent op-census, Zig vs Rust op-match): diffed codegenZig's `match op, l` arms (166184-166236) against codegenRust's (164745-164814). Beyond Export (note 41/43, statement-level, being fixed), Zig lacks exactly ONE expression op Rust has: MonotonicDelayMs. Census across backends: MonotonicDelayMs is implemented in 6 -- Rust 164800 (std::thread::sleep), Delphi 165500 (Sleep), TypeScript 174941 (spiral_delay_ms), C 175950 (poll(0,0,ms)), C++ 177160 (sleep_for), Python 178117 (time.sleep) -- and MISSING from Zig + Lean/Bend/Gleam/Lua/Wgsl/Calyx/Kotlin/Swift/OpenQasm/Ebpf. For the Zig-hub goal (Zig at C speed, cover portable ops), that is a real gap: a program using a monotonic delay compiled to Zig hits the generic \"op not supported in the Zig backend\" error (166236).\n\nDRAFTED FIX (trivial, build-independent, bundles with the Zig Export fix as a Zig op-gap pack for pack-5n or later): add after Zig's StdoutFlush arm (166221, same void-returning expression-match shape):\n  | MonotonicDelayMs, [a] -> $\"std.time.sleep(@as(u64, @intCast({tup a})) * std.time.ns_per_ms)\"\nRationale: Zig std has std.time.sleep(nanoseconds: u64) and std.time.ns_per_ms; the op guard (156902 push_op_no_rewrite requires milliseconds >= 0) makes @intCast(i32->u64) safe. Returns void like StdoutFlush, handled by the existing return' at 166237. UNVERIFIED-until-build (std.time.ns_per_ms name + @intCast form on the live Zig toolchain); confirm with the Zig Export build.\n\nMethod note: the op-census (diff two backends' op-match arms, then census the gap op across all backends via grep) is a repeatable build-independent way to find BACKEND-COVERAGE-GAPS; it found Export then MonotonicDelayMs. Next pass could diff Zig vs C (the oracle) for the full Zig-hub op gap list. No current Zig consumer needs MonotonicDelayMs (latent gap, like Export was before kit), so not urgent -- but it is a cheap win to bundle with the Export apply.")
inl note_of_backend_coverage_gaps_45 () = Note ("BACKEND-COVERAGE-GAPS", 45u32, "2026-10-26T16:23Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-26 (op-census complete, Zig vs C oracle): diffed codegenZig's op arms (166184-166236) against CodegenC's (175878-175977, the fullest op set). Zig's COMPLETE op-gap vs the C oracle is exactly THREE ops:\n  1. Export (statement-level; note 41/43; fix drafted, pack-5n).\n  2. MonotonicDelayMs (note 44; one-liner std.time.sleep drafted; in 6 backends, Zig lacks).\n  3. PrimToString (NEW) -- int/bool/char -> string.\n\nPrimToString finding (broadest mirror gap in the whole op set): emitted by CodegenC ONLY (175888, via cstring_from_i64/u64/bool/char helpers). EVERY other backend lacks it -- Rust, Delphi, Zig, Gleam, Lua, Lean, Bend, Wgsl, Calyx, Kotlin, Swift, OpenQasm, Ebpf, Python, F#, TypeScript. Confirmed C-only by census: grep PrimToString = def 43704, name 45746, two unconditional pushes in both cores (156862/161954), ONE emission (175888 C). No backend-specific lowering/rewrite, so the op reaches every backend's codegen unchanged and only C handles it; the rest hit the generic \"op not supported\" error. LATENT today (the -Suite all matrix is green, so no non-C sample currently triggers prim->string as the PrimToString op -- most prim->string goes through Printf, not this op), but a real gap: the first program/lib fn that emits PrimToString on a non-C backend fails to compile there.\n\nFix sizing:\n- Export + MonotonicDelayMs: one-line Zig op-match arms, bundle as the Zig op-gap pack (runbook P1).\n- PrimToString: NOT a one-liner -- needs per-backend prelude helpers (Zig: spiralStringFromI64/U64/Bool/Char analogous to C's cstring_from_*; same for the other 15 backends). So it is its own cross-backend MIRROR effort, best tracked as a dedicated item or a BACKEND-COVERAGE sub-task, prioritized when a consumer (a lib to_string that emits the op) appears. For the Zig hub specifically it is one of the 3 ops to C-completeness.\n\nMethod: the op-census (diff a backend's op-match arms vs C's, census each gap op's emission sites by grep) is now COMPLETE for Zig: the hub's op-gap to C is {Export, MonotonicDelayMs, PrimToString}. The same two-step method applies to any other backend's C-completeness check (e.g. Delphi vs C would add MonotonicDelayMs-present but Export/PrimToString-absent, etc.).")
inl note_of_backend_coverage_gaps_46 () = Note ("BACKEND-COVERAGE-GAPS", 46u32, "2026-10-26T16:26Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-26 (Delphi-vs-C op-census + a CORRECTION to note 45). Two findings:\n\nCORRECTION to note 45: I listed Export as part of \"Zig's op-gap vs the C oracle.\" That conflates two gap kinds. C has NO TyOp(Export) case either (the Export grep hits are F#/Rust/Gleam/TS ONLY -- 163566/164698/168697/174892 -- none in CodegenC 175201+; C produces executables, not library symbols). So Export is a LIBRARY-FEATURE gap (only the 4 module/library backends emit it), NOT a C-completeness gap. Corrected split:\n- C-COMPLETENESS gap (ops C has that the backend lacks): Zig = {MonotonicDelayMs, PrimToString}; the Zig Export fix (note 41) still stands but it ADDS a capability even C lacks (kit's Cells use case), it does not close C parity.\n- LIBRARY-FEATURE gap: Export (F#/Rust/Gleam/TS have it; C/Delphi/Zig/Lean/Bend/Lua/Wgsl/Calyx/Kotlin/Swift/OpenQasm/Ebpf/Python lack it) -- note 43's map.\n\nNEW -- Delphi vs C op-census (codegenDelphi op-match 165460-165508 vs CodegenC 175878-175977): Delphi covers EVERY C op -- Dyn, StringIndex, StringSlice, StaticStringConcat, ArrayIndex, ArrayIndexSet, Add/Sub/Mult/Div/Mod/Pow, LT/LTE/EQ/NEQ/GT/GTE, BoolAnd/Or (fused with BitwiseAnd/Or), BitwiseXor/Complement, ShiftLeft/ShiftRight (per-width Sar* for arithmetic right shift), Neg, Log/Exp/Tanh/Sqrt/Sin/Cos, NanIs, StdoutFlush, MonotonicDelayMs (Sleep), Assign, Printf, UnionTag -- EXCEPT PrimToString. So Delphi's C-completeness gap is exactly {PrimToString}. Delphi is otherwise C-complete (a shipping backend; makes sense).\n\nConsequence: PrimToString is the SINGLE op separating BOTH Zig and Delphi from C-completeness, and it is C-ONLY across all 16 other backends (note 45). That makes PrimToString the top cross-backend mirror op: one shared design (prelude helpers StringFromI64/U64/Bool/Char per backend, like C's cstring_from_*) closes the C-completeness gap for Delphi (fully) and Zig (together with MonotonicDelayMs). Still latent (matrix green -> no non-C sample emits PrimToString today); promote to a dedicated mirror item when a prim->string consumer appears. Method now applied to Zig and Delphi; the same diff-vs-C works for any backend's C-completeness list.")
inl note_of_backend_coverage_gaps_47 () = Note ("BACKEND-COVERAGE-GAPS", 47u32, "2026-10-26T16:29Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-26 (Gleam-vs-C op-census; op-census now covers the shipping backends). codegenGleam op-match (168723-168799) vs CodegenC: Gleam's C-completeness gap = {MonotonicDelayMs, PrimToString, StdoutFlush, Assign}, but Gleam's functional nature splits these:\n- Assign: BY-DESIGN. Gleam is immutable; a plain `~x` mutable-variable reassignment (TyOp(Assign)) has NO Gleam target without a ref-cell model. Gleam HAS Mut layouts (TyLayoutMutableSet, bind-level) but not free-variable Assign. CONSEQUENCE for backend-balance (\"Gleam where BEAM pays\"): Spiral code using mutable `~x` variables cannot target Gleam -- a real viability constraint beyond the known string/trig gaps (GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS). Worth knowing when choosing Gleam for a unit.\n- StdoutFlush: BY-DESIGN absent (BEAM io.print flushes); harmless.\n- MonotonicDelayMs: real addable gap (BEAM process.sleep / erlang:timer); Gleam joins Zig in lacking it (now Zig + Gleam + 9 others lack it; 6 backends have it).\n- PrimToString: the universal C-only op (Gleam lacks it like all 15 non-C backends).\n\nOp-census status: covered C(oracle), Rust(via the Export/MonotonicDelayMs diffs), Zig, Delphi, Gleam. Clean takeaways:\n1. Shipping NATIVE backends are C-complete bar PrimToString: Delphi gap = {PrimToString}; Zig gap = {MonotonicDelayMs, PrimToString}.\n2. PrimToString is the top cross-backend mirror op (C-only; separates Zig, Delphi, Gleam and every other backend from C-completeness) -- one prelude-helper design (StringFromI64/U64/Bool/Char) closes it per backend.\n3. MonotonicDelayMs is the second mirror op (in 6 backends; missing from Zig, Gleam, Lean, Bend, Lua, Wgsl, Calyx, Kotlin, Swift, OpenQasm, Ebpf).\n4. Export is a LIBRARY-FEATURE (F#/Rust/Gleam/TS only), not a C-parity gap.\nDiminishing returns past here: functional/restricted backends (Kotlin/Swift/Lua/Lean/Bend/Wgsl/Calyx/OpenQasm/Ebpf) have many by-design absences (restricted targets per AGENTS.md), so a vs-C diff mostly re-documents known restrictions rather than finding actionable gaps. The actionable cross-backend mirror work is PrimToString (latent) + MonotonicDelayMs where a consumer needs them.")
inl note_of_backend_coverage_gaps_48 () = Note ("BACKEND-COVERAGE-GAPS", 48u32, "2026-10-26T16:38Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-26: the Zig op-gap pack (Export note 41/43 + MonotonicDelayMs note 44) is now a SINGLE tested anchored-apply script, scratchpad/apply-zig-opgap-pack.ps1, BUILD-READY. Dry-run + scratch-copy apply verified 10-26 against the current spiral_compiler.fs: export anchor (Zig Assign line 166180) unique=1, delay anchor (Zig StdoutFlush arm 166221) unique=1, Zig sleep emission absent before apply; -Apply lands both edits Zig-scoped (+12 lines, MonotonicDelayMs immediately after StdoutFlush, Export after Assign). To land in pack-5n (or later): run `apply-zig-opgap-pack.ps1 -Target <CoreSource copy> -Apply`, build -Mode single-flight -Configuration Debug, then BUILD-VERIFY the Zig-scope names (closure_of_var/env.ty_to_data/data_free_vars/tyv/tup_ty/bodies for Export; std.time.ns_per_ms + @intCast + the void-expr form for the delay, mirroring StdoutFlush), probe a 2-arg export + a MonotonicDelayMs sample -Backend Zig -Native, add regression samples. The guard note: a generic `| MonotonicDelayMs, [a] ->` match is NOT Zig-scoped (also hits Rust 164800 + TS 174941); the script guards on the Zig-specific std.time.sleep emission instead.")
inl note_of_backend_coverage_gaps_49 () = Note ("BACKEND-COVERAGE-GAPS", 49u32, "2026-10-27T01:06Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-26 follow-up to n41 (Zig Export gap). Two build-independent confirmations for the Zig op-gap pack (apply-zig-opgap-pack.ps1, ready when a build slot frees): (1) SECOND Zig op gap beyond Export: MonotonicDelayMs has NO Zig arm either -- only Rust (:164800) and TypeScript (:174941) implement it; on Zig it hits the same generic op fallthrough (:166236). The pack adds it alongside the Export case. (2) Both inserts anchor UNIQUELY on the live tree (re-gripped 10-26; file is ~189k lines, grep-authoritative -- a Measure-Object -Line reading of 176,796 was a miscount, ignore it): the Export-case insert sits on `| TyOp(Assign, [a;b]) -> line s $\"{tup a} = {tup b};\"` (unique at :166180, the Zig `=` arm, discriminated from Delphi's `:=` at :165457); the MonotonicDelayMs insert sits on `| StdoutFlush, [] -> \"spiralFlush()\"` (unique at :166221; the other spiralFlush() hits are the Zig runtime-helper literal at 165726-165766, not op arms). Per n41 the Export case must mirror codegenRust's export builder (163566 + the decl/call tuple->params logic 164741-164784) to unpack the exported fn's domain (tuple -> one param per field, record -> struct) and call the closure; the apply lands both arms. Remaining = apply + build + -Backend Zig -Native verify (a 2-arg capture-free export + a MonotonicDelayMs sample) + regression rows. Still build-bound.")
inl note_of_backend_coverage_gaps_50 () = Note ("BACKEND-COVERAGE-GAPS", 50u32, "2026-10-27T08:50Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-27 BUILD-VERIFY result for the Zig op-gap pack (apply-zig-opgap-pack.ps1). Applied to a scratch copy of the current core and ran a Debug build (-Mode single-flight -CoreSource copy, kit job zig-pack-build, 157s). Result: 1 error, and it SPLITS the pack.\n\n- MonotonicDelayMs arm: F#-CLEAN (no error). Its emit is pure string interpolation of `tup a` (`$\"std.time.sleep(@as(u64, @intCast({tup a})) * std.time.ns_per_ms)\"`), so it type-checks. (Zig-semantic correctness -- ns_per_ms/@intCast -- still wants a successful build + a Zig native run, but the arm is well-formed and can land independently.)\n- Export arm: FAILS -- spiral_compiler.fs(166183): error FS0039 'closure_of_var' is not defined in codegenZig. My apply assumed Zig had Rust's closure_of_var map; it does NOT. Rust declares it (:164179), populates it (:164567-570, in a d-DESTINATION TyJoinPoint(JPClosure) arm: `| Some (DV(L(i,_))) when args.Length=0 -> closure_of_var.[i] <- (closure (a,c)).tag`), and the Export builder reads it (:164701/164739-741). codegenZig has only closure_names (:165901) + closure_memo (:166296); closures are created by `closureCreate{(closure (a,c)).tag}` in the `jp` helper (:166052).\n\nWHY it's not a mechanical port: Zig's term handler is RET-based (ZLocal/ZTail, :166071-166083 `| TyJoinPoint(a,args) -> return' (jp (a,args))`), not destination(`d`)-based like Rust (:164557-572). So there is no `d = Some(DV(L(i,_)))` site to copy. The Zig port must declare `let closure_of_var = Dictionary<int,int>()` after :165901, and populate it where Zig binds a closure to a local var -- i.e. when ret is ZLocal [|L(i,_)|] and a is TyJoinPoint(JPClosure(a,c)), set closure_of_var.[i] <- (closure (a,c)).tag -- which needs reading Zig's return'/ZLocal path to find the exact point (the jp helper returns a string and doesn't see the dest var). Then the Export arm reads closure_of_var.[i] as drafted.\n\nNEXT: (1) land MonotonicDelayMs independently (verified F#-clean; a full build + Zig run confirms semantics). (2) Export arm = a Zig-specific closure_of_var port (decl + ZLocal-bind populate + the drafted use), then rebuild; it's a real codegen task, build-slot-bound, not the 1-line insert the draft assumed. apply-zig-opgap-pack.ps1 updated to reflect the split. This is exactly why BUILD-VERIFY precedes landing: the anchored apply inserted clean, but only the F# compile caught that Export referenced a Rust-only binding.")
inl note_of_backend_coverage_gaps_51 () = Note ("BACKEND-COVERAGE-GAPS", 51u32, "2026-10-27T09:52Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-27 Zig OP-CENSUS (closing the op dimension). Read the live codegenZig TyOp handler (spiral_compiler.fs:166176-166236, ending at the \"%A with %i args not supported in the Zig backend\" fallthrough :166236). Ops Zig handles: Global, ArrayIndexSet, Assign, Dyn, StringIndex, StringSlice, StaticStringConcat, ArrayIndex, Add/Sub/Mult/Div/Mod/Pow, LT/LTE/GT/GTE/EQ/NEQ (string-aware), BoolAnd/BoolOr, BitwiseAnd/Or/Xor/Complement, ShiftLeft/Right, Neg, Log/Exp/Tanh/Sqrt/Sin/Cos, NanIs, StdoutFlush, Printf, UnionTag. (StringLength/ArrayLength/ArrayCreate/Conv/Failwith are separate Ty* nodes, handled.) The ONLY TyOp gaps that reach the fallthrough are Export (n41, real demand from kit) and MonotonicDelayMs (native_cube_monotonic_delay_direct on Zig) -- BOTH in apply-zig-opgap-pack.ps1, now F#-verified (kit job zig-pack-build2 exit 0). Checked Floor/Ceil/Round/Atan2: they are NOT TyOp cases (still C-only macros; PORTABLE-PRIMITIVES at 82% hasn't converted them to ops), so they are NOT Zig op gaps today -- but a WATCH-ITEM: if PORTABLE-PRIMITIVES lands them as portable TyOps, Zig needs arms or they hit the fallthrough. CONCLUSION: after the pack, Zig's TyOp coverage is complete; its remaining BACKEND-COVERAGE gaps are SHAPE gaps (stack mutable layouts, C-only macros, value-level BackendSwitch records without a Zig key, per tmp/AGENTS.md), not ops. So the Zig op-gap hunt is closed; Zig coverage work should target shapes next.")
inl note_of_backend_coverage_gaps_52 () = Note ("BACKEND-COVERAGE-GAPS", 52u32, "2026-10-27T10:08Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-27 Lean OP-CENSUS (higher-yield than Zig's, as expected for a younger backend). Read codegenLean's TyOp handler (spiral_compiler.fs:166734-166775, fallthrough :166775). Lean handles: Dyn, UnionTag, ArrayIndex, StringIndex, StringSlice, StaticStringConcat, Add/Sub/Mult/Div/Mod, Pow (FLOAT ONLY), LT/LTE/GT/GTE/EQ/NEQ, BoolAnd/Or, Bitwise And/Or/Xor/Complement, ShiftLeft/Right, Neg, Log/Exp/Tanh/Sqrt/Sin/Cos, NanIs, StdoutFlush; plus ArrayIndexSet, Global, Printf (pre-catch). GAPS (fall through to :166775):\n1. INTEGER Pow -- Lean-SPECIFIC. The only Pow arm is \"Pow, [a;_] when is_float a\" (:166751), so integer exponentiation hits the fallthrough. Zig handles all Pow via std.math.pow (:166196) and C does too, so a program doing integer Pow diverges on Lean. (Possibly latent: depends whether a Lean-listed sample uses integer Pow; BACKEND-COVERAGE tracks latent gaps.) FIX: add an integer-Pow arm to codegenLean (a spiralIntPow helper, or Nat/Int power), mirroring Zig/C.\n2. Export -- falls through (same gap as Zig n41/n50).\n3. MonotonicDelayMs -- falls through (same gap as Zig).\n\nKEY REFRAME: Export + MonotonicDelayMs are gaps in BOTH Zig AND Lean, so they are a CROSS-BACKEND pattern, not Zig-only: every native backend added after those ops (likely Lean, Wasm, Kotlin, Swift, Bend too) lacks the arms. So the op-gap work is a per-backend SWEEP, not a one-backend fix. Suggest: after the Zig pack lands, run the same op-census + arm-add across Lean/Wasm/Kotlin/Swift/Bend for Export/MonotonicDelayMs, and add integer-Pow to Lean. Each is build-slot-bound (apply + the backend's -Native verify + a regression row). Zig is the proven template (apply-zig-opgap-pack.ps1: anchored arm insert + per-backend scope checks like the closure_of_var port the F# build caught).")
inl note_of_backend_coverage_gaps_53 () = Note ("BACKEND-COVERAGE-GAPS", 53u32, "2026-10-27T10:46Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-27 CROSS-BACKEND op-gap sweep (consolidated). Pivoted from per-backend op-censuses to grepping the two suspect ops across every backend's codegen (cleaner once Kotlin turned out to have no single TyOp(op,l) catch-all). Result matrix for the two ops the Zig pack addresses:\n\nMonotonicDelayMs -- HAS an arm: Rust (:164800), Delphi (:165500), TypeScript (:174941), C (:175950), Cpp (:177160), Python (:178117). GAP (falls through): Zig, Lean, Wasm.\n\nExport (TyOp(Export,[name;f])) -- HAS an arm: Rust (:163566 + :164698, both cores), the backend at :168697 (between Lean and Wasm -- identify when scoping), TypeScript (:174892). GAP: Zig, Lean, Wasm, and DELPHI (Delphi has MonotonicDelayMs but NO Export arm in its :165458-165508 op match -- a distinct, asymmetric gap). C/Cpp/Python show no TyOp(Export) arm in the grep but their op handling is a nested match (:175878/:177111/:178055); VERIFY whether they handle Export elsewhere (C is the oracle, so export samples would need a C result) before concluding they lack it.\n\nPlus Lean-specific: integer Pow (only `Pow when is_float a` at :166751; integer Pow falls through).\n\nPER-BACKEND OP-GAP PACK SCOPE (what to add where), Zig the proven template (apply-zig-opgap-pack.ps1 -- anchored arm insert mirroring each backend's idiom + per-backend scope checks the F# build catches, like the closure_of_var port):\n  - Export arm: Zig (DONE, in pack, F#-verified), Lean, Wasm, Delphi.\n  - MonotonicDelayMs arm: Zig (DONE, in pack), Lean, Wasm.\n  - integer Pow arm: Lean.\nEach per-backend add is build-slot-bound (apply + that backend's -Native verify + a regression row/test-block). Suggest wiring these into BACKEND-COVERAGE-GAPS as the op-coverage sub-tasks; the shape gaps (stack layouts, C-only macros) remain separate. Sweep method + raw matrix in scratchpad/op-gap-cross-backend-sweep.txt.")
inl note_of_backend_coverage_gaps_54 () = Note ("BACKEND-COVERAGE-GAPS", 54u32, "2026-10-27T11:19Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-27 CORRECTION to n53's Export scoping (caught by re-reading my own op-census-method memory, which records this exact pitfall). n53 listed Export as an actionable gap for Lean/Wasm/Delphi -- that CONFLATES \"the backend lacks a TyOp(Export) arm\" with \"the backend needs one.\" Export is a MODULE/LIBRARY-backend feature: emitted by F#, Rust, Gleam, TypeScript; C itself LACKS it by design (so it is NOT a C-parity gap, and C/Cpp/Python being without it is expected, not a gap -- confirmed: no samples/ test exercises !!!!Export, it's Cells/FFI-tested per n41). A native backend lacking Export is by-design UNLESS it is an export target.\n\nSo the Export scope narrows: ZIG needs it (hub role + demonstrated demand -- kit's sequences.spi Export cell hit the fallthrough; DONE in apply-zig-opgap-pack.ps1, F#-verified). Lean/Wasm/Delphi Export is NOT automatically actionable -- add it only if a consumer actually needs export on that backend (none demonstrated; adding it speculatively is the zero-demand work the user parks).\n\nThe CLEAR, actionable C-parity gaps from the sweep are:\n- MonotonicDelayMs: a portable delay op that C, Rust, Delphi, TypeScript, Cpp, Python ALL implement; missing in Zig (DONE in pack), Lean, Wasm. Real C-parity gaps -> add to Lean + Wasm.\n- Lean integer Pow: Lean guards `Pow when is_float a` (:166751); C/Zig handle integer Pow. Real C-parity gap -> add to Lean.\n\nRevised op-gap pack scope: Zig {Export, MonotonicDelayMs} DONE; Lean {MonotonicDelayMs, integer-Pow}; Wasm {MonotonicDelayMs}. Export for Lean/Wasm/Delphi is deferred (library-feature, no demand). Supersedes n53's blanket Export-everywhere framing. (Method note: when an op shows missing, FIRST classify it C-parity vs library-feature before calling it a gap -- op-census-method pitfall #1.)")
inl note_of_backend_coverage_gaps_55 () = Note ("BACKEND-COVERAGE-GAPS", 55u32, "2026-10-27T12:20Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-27 Zig Export arm native-proof result (node job-1793102360448-zig-native-export, elapsed 796s).\n\nCONFIRMED: the Zig op-gap pack (Export + MonotonicDelayMs) F#-BUILDS CLEAN on the node -- single-flight Debug from the patched core copy, 0 errors (7 warnings: FS0046 `params` reserved x5, FS0026 unreachable rule x1, FS0760 IDisposable x1, all pre-existing/benign), built in 600s, DLL sha256 A920DB8206749BE744FB2A0361E3F16D53B19D0953F75D1A91177D4897457D40. So the arm is well-formed F# and the patched compiler runs.\n\nNOT CONFIRMED (test-design mismatch, NOT an arm bug): the Spiral->Zig EMIT for Export. export_family.spi was a native-RUN test (///> rust + ///> zig, expect exit via inline assert). But !!!!Export puts the program in LIBRARY mode (the exported fn is the entry; no `main` emitted), so a native-run harness that links+runs an exe cannot test an Export program on ANY backend:\n - ///> rust FAILED: rustc error[E0601] \"main function not found in crate main\" (the emitted main.rs is a library).\n - ///> zig not_run: native stage skipped; this run left NO .zig in spiral_cells temp, so the Spiral->Zig emit was not even captured.\nThe rust reference row failing IDENTICALLY (E0601 no-main) is the proof that this is a test-SHAPE problem, not the Zig arm.\n\nSo native-run test blocks are the WRONG harness for Export (any backend). A real emit/runtime proof needs one of: (a) compile a PLAIN Export .spi (not a test block -- test blocks are skipped by BlockParsing) to .zig with the patched DLL + `zig build-obj` it (compile-check a library, no run); (b) a consumer-context test: a Zig program that imports+calls the exported fn (how RUST-EXPORT-TYPES was proven -- kit's crate consumes the adapter); (c) an emit-inspection assertion (output contains `pub fn spiral_add`). Connects to SELF-CHECKING-SAMPLES (testing library/export outputs).\n\nRECOMMENDATION: HOLD landing the Zig Export arm into the shared core until the emit is confirmed by (a). The MonotonicDelayMs arm in the same pack is also build-valid but unproven at runtime (it was not exercised by export_family). apply-zig-opgap-pack.ps1 is backed up in apps/compiler/packs/. Next node step: build a minimal plain-program Export sample + MonotonicDelayMs sample, compile to Zig with the pinned patched DLL, zig build-obj / run.")
inl note_of_backend_coverage_gaps_56 () = Note ("BACKEND-COVERAGE-GAPS", 56u32, "2026-10-27T12:46Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-27 CORRECTION to n55: the Zig Export arm is GREEN, not \"emit unproven\". Re-ran export_family with ///> zig ONLY (dropped ///> rust so Cells would not skip zig after the rust target failed), pinned patched DLL, SPIRAL_CELLS_MEMO=0, -Compiler process: \"test-block rows 1: agree 1, DISAGREE 0, not run 0\". The zig target compiled + ran + the inline assert held (export_add(40,2)==42 -> program returns 0 -> exit 0).\n\nSo the Zig op-gap pack (apps/compiler/packs/apply-zig-opgap-pack.ps1) is build-valid (F# 0 errors) AND the Export arm is runtime-valid on Zig.\n\nWhere n55 went wrong: it concluded \"!!!!Export puts the program in library mode (no main) on ANY backend\" from the RUST reference row's rustc E0601 \"main function not found\". That is RUST-specific emit behavior (a Rust export lowers to a library/crate consumed by a caller -- how RUST-EXPORT-TYPES/kit's adapter use it), NOT universal. Zig emits a runnable program WITH main plus the exported fn, so it runs standalone. Lesson: a reference row's failure is not automatically \"the test is malformed\" -- it can be that reference backend's own behavior.\n\nREADY TO LAND: the pack is GREEN; it is a shared-core edit so main sequences the land (anchored apply into packs; the pack's anchors are full-line text and survive repacks). MonotonicDelayMs arm in the same pack: build-valid but runtime still unexercised (export_family does not call it) -- a one-line follow-up sample (a normal program with main that calls a delay) confirms it.")
inl note_of_backend_coverage_gaps_57 () = Note ("BACKEND-COVERAGE-GAPS", 57u32, "2026-10-27T13:20Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-27 op-gap DEMAND check (corrects n53/n54's \"actionable gaps\" framing). Read the codegen Pow arms: integer Pow is handled by C (pow<suffix>, :175916), Zig (std.math.pow @TypeOf, :166196) and Gleam (spiral_int_power, :168745). It is float-GUARDED on Lean (`Pow when is_float a`, :166751), Kotlin (Float32T|Float64T, :172704), Swift (`when floated a b`, :173466) and Python (Float32T|Float64T, :173931), and float-only in effect on Rust (.powf, :164764) and Wasm ($math_pow f64, :170765) -- so integer Pow falls through / would not compile on all six.\n\nBUT the full matrix is green (DISAGREE 0) on those backends. That means NO listed sample exercises integer Pow on them: there is no current demand. Per the no-zero-demand rule and the n54 pitfall (classify before calling a gap a task), Lean integer-Pow -- and the Kotlin/Swift/Python/Rust/Wasm equivalents -- is NOT actionable now. It is proactive hub-parity work, parked until a sample uses int Pow AND is listed on that backend. The same demand caveat applies to MonotonicDelayMs on Lean/Wasm (n54 listed them as actionable): confirm a consuming, listed sample exists before drafting any arm.\n\nNet: the only op-gap arm with real demand was Zig Export (kit's sequences.spi hit the fallthrough), which is in pack 5n. The rest of the \"per-backend op-gap pack\" from n53/n54 is speculative until a sample demands it. Method fix banked to op-census-method: always check sample demand (a listed consuming sample) before calling a C-parity gap actionable. Supersedes the Lean/Wasm \"actionable\" framing in n54.")
inl note_of_backend_coverage_gaps_58 () = Note ("BACKEND-COVERAGE-GAPS", 58u32, "2026-10-28T13:59Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-28: build-free op-match-arm census across 17 backends (scratchpad op-census.ps1; op-census-result.md). Confirms the prim-op gaps match AGENTS.md by-design integer-subset limits: Wgsl/Calyx/OpenQasm/Ebpf lack transcendentals/strings/unions -> major backend work, not quick arms. TWO actionable candidates surfaced: (1) OpenQasm lacks ONLY Tanh among transcendentals (already has Pow/Log/Exp/Sin/Cos/Sqrt) -> a one-arm fix IF tests/native-shims/openqasm-run/run.py's simulator supports tanh (or compute via (e^2x-1)/(e^2x+1)); (2) Bend has strings/arrays/unions but NO float-math arms (Pow/Log/Exp/Sin/Cos/Tanh/Sqrt/NanIs) -> verify HVM/Bend2 float support, then one prelude pack. Flagged false-negative: Kotlin/Swift compute union tags inline in the unbox path (v{i}.tag, :172911), not a standalone UnionTag arm -> not a gap unless a sample emits a bare UnionTag. Census is blind to ops handled off the op-match: Conv (TyConv node), Infinity/Pi (lit/macro helpers), String/ArrayLength + ArrayCreate (typed nodes), FailWith (stmt); and to TYPE-SHAPE gaps (recursive unions/closures/refs/f64) which are THIS item's axis (already in AGENTS.md).")
inl note_of_backend_coverage_gaps_59 () = Note ("BACKEND-COVERAGE-GAPS", 59u32, "2026-10-28T14:03Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-28 support checks (main-requested, build-free) for the two op-census candidates:\n(1) OpenQasm Tanh: run.py MATH_BUILTINS (:471) = sqrt/exp/log/sin/cos/pow, NO tanh. Cleanest fix = add 'tanh': math.tanh to run.py + a Tanh arm in codegenOpenQasm emitting tanh(x) (mirror sqrt/sin ~174075+). Python math.tanh is libm -> EXACT C-oracle agreement, better than the (exp)-formula (ULP risk). Regression sample: tanh at small x and |x|>=50 vs C. 2-edit micro-pack.\n(2) Bend floats: base.bend has the COMPLETE F32 family -- F32.pow/sqrt/exp/log/sin/cos/tan/tanh + atan2/hyperbolics/log2/10, F32.is_eq/ne/lt.., F32.bits. Bend 2 is f32 (bend.ts:2206 \"finite f32 value\"), NOT f24 -> matches C's f32 oracle. The transcendental gap (Pow/Log/Exp/Sin/Cos/Sqrt/Tanh + NanIs) is FULLY closeable: emit F32.<op> arms in codegenBend + add float samples to the Bend matrix list. CAVEATS: f32 only (f64 operands lose precision vs C f64 -> scope to f32 or Known-row f64); NanIs = F32.is_ne(x,x) or an F32.bits check -- VERIFY Bend doesn't constant-fold the self-compare (test a real NaN), per main. One prelude pack. Both queued for the pack AFTER the current install. Detail: scratchpad op-census-result.md.")
inl note_of_backend_coverage_gaps_60 () = Note ("BACKEND-COVERAGE-GAPS", 60u32, "2026-10-29T12:34Z", "compiler", "10-29 08:5x compiler: n59's two micro-packs REDONE after the wipe (their TEMP drafts were lost; WIPE-REDO-LEDGER n12) as committable anchored appliers, build-free proofs done, queued for the next single-flight install pack.\n(1) apps/compiler/packs/apply-openqasm-tanh.ps1 -Core <fs> -Runner <run.py> [-Apply]: codegenOpenQasm Tanh arm (tanh(x), after the cos arm) + tanh: math.tanh in tests/native-shims/openqasm-run/run.py MATH_BUILTINS (libm, exact vs the C oracle). Dry-run on the live files: 2/2 anchors once; run.py is LF. Coverage needs no new sample: samples/native_float_math_family already exercises f32+f64 Tanh beside Log/Exp/Sin/Cos (which OpenQasm already supports) but is not in the OpenQasm list. Land step: python -I apps/compiler/tmp/scripts/matrix-add-to-list.py OpenQasm native_float_math_family --apply.\n(2) apps/compiler/packs/apply-bend-float-math.ps1 -Core <fs> [-Samples <dir>] [-Apply]: codegenBend f32 arms after Neg: Sqrt/Exp/Log/Sin/Cos/Tanh -> F32.<op>(x), Pow -> F32.pow(a, b), NanIs -> F32.is_ne(x, x). All are laws in bend2 base.bend (the full F32 list was read; tan/atan2/hyperbolics also exist). Bend probe via bun bend2/main.ts on hand-written programs: a real NaN (F32.div(0,0)) gives is_ne(x,x) = True (exit 0), and 1.5 gives False (exit 1, positive control), so Bend does NOT fold the self-compare. tanh(sqrt 4 + exp 0 * cos 0 - (pow(3,1) + log 1 + sin 0)) is_eq 0 -> True: all 7 ops run. Scope f32 only (codegenBend is_float = Float32T). native_float_math_family mixes f64, so Bend needs its own f32 sample: the applier writes samples/native_float_math_f32 with -Samples (core-relative package like its siblings; NaN = inf -. inf; finite NanIs must stay false). Checked on the installed compiler: C compiles and runs exit 0 (oracle), and Bend fails with \"doesn't support the operation Sqrt\" (the gap this closes). Land step: python -I apps/compiler/tmp/scripts/matrix-add-to-list.py Bend native_float_math_f32 --apply.")
inl note_of_backend_coverage_gaps_61 () = Note ("BACKEND-COVERAGE-GAPS", 61u32, "2026-10-31T20:02Z", "compiler", "10-31 10-31 16:01 compiler GROOM (user direction via /btw, 10-31 15:4x): the self-hosted rewrite (SELF-HOSTED-SPIRAL) starts only after EVERY backend is SOTA: no memory problems, no Rc slowdowns, no union hacks, no recursion limits, no missing arithmetic; GADT bugs and conversion gaps are 'unacceptable by now'; edition 3 finishes first; Gleam is a top test target (abyssal codebases) with F#, Rust and Wasm; Cells as fast as F# projects. This item is now on that gate (priority -> P0).")
inl note_of_backend_coverage_gaps_62 () = Note ("BACKEND-COVERAGE-GAPS", 62u32, "2026-10-31T20:05Z", "compiler", "10-31 10-31 16:05 compiler (groom): raised P1 -> P0: user (10-31 15:4x) requires all backends SOTA (no union hacks, no unsupported recursion) before the self-hosted rewrite; this item lists the unsupported shapes per backend and is a gate for SELF-HOSTED-SPIRAL. Primitive/conversion gaps split out to BACKEND-PRIMITIVE-PARITY.")
inl note_of_backend_coverage_gaps_63 () = Note ("BACKEND-COVERAGE-GAPS", 63u32, "2026-11-01T04:34Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 00:34 compiler, round-2 groom (user 00:2x: backend codegen is CPU-light text work, write it while runs batch): NEXT CPU-LIGHT EDIT: Zig stack layouts first (Zig is the C-parity backend for the Zig-vs-C metric). Write packs/apply-zig-stack-layouts.ps1: codegenZig emits a stack layout as an extern struct passed by value, mirroring CodegenC's stack-layout path, plus one sample with an inline expectation. Then the same shape for Lean. No run waits on the draft: the matrix rows for every drafted applier go in ONE batched node job (BACKEND-BATCH below).")
inl note_of_backend_coverage_gaps_64 () = Note ("BACKEND-COVERAGE-GAPS", 64u32, "2026-11-01T06:03Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 02:03 compiler, round 3: TWO FINDINGS that re-rank this item (build-free, from the tree). (1) 'Zig stack layouts' is NOT a Zig gap. stack_mut is rejected by EVERY backend except C++/Cuda (StackMutable errors in F#, Rust, Delphi, Zig, Gleam, Lua, Wasm, Calyx/Wgsl, TypeScript, Python, AND C at 25121/25309), nothing in lib/ or samples/ uses stack_mut/stack_refs, and the only matrix row is the negative native_layout_stack_mutable_string_negative. With C (the oracle) unable to run it, a Zig version has nothing to agree with. Stack layouts move to a language-level decision (implement in C first, then the natives), not per-backend gap work. Round-2 next-edit (apply-zig-stack-layouts) is WITHDRAWN. (2) The real Zig/Lean gap is lib backend_switch coverage. Census of lib/spiral: 263 switch sites with explicit keys; 93 have Rust but no Zig, but most are Rust-only APIs (vec/slice/rust.ref, near.spi), where a Zig arm makes no sense. Portable sites (3+ backend keys, no Zig): 49, excluding 4 generated copies under bench/parsing/target. By module: date_time 12 (format, time_span, days, hours, minutes, seconds, milliseconds, stopwatch...), base 9, sm' 4 (char_code, of_char + 2), trace 3, resultm 3, env 2 (os_environ, environ_get), reflection 2, listm' 2 (fold', list'_types), plus singles: console write_error_line/read_to_end, common dispose, convert, optionm' is_none', seq new_seq, float_native_types (C but not Zig!), unicode_types, rust clone_rust. NEXT: which of these the Zig/Lean matrix actually reaches needs a compile run (strict missing-arm on Zig + Lean over samples), so it joins BACKEND-BATCH on the node; then add Zig arms smallest first, starting with float_native_types (C has one, so Zig mirrors C).")
inl note_of_backend_coverage_gaps_65 () = Note ("BACKEND-COVERAGE-GAPS", 65u32, "2026-11-01T06:03Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 02:03 compiler: CORRECTION to the note above: float_native_types is a UNIT arm (it only adds C/Python globals). A missing unit arm is allowed (the strict missing-arm rule only covers non-unit arms), and Zig needs no includes, so it isn't a gap. More of the 49 are unit/global arms of the same shape (base 62/68/751-842, trace 47/416, sm' 240/263, date_time 875/1206, reflection 35, the 'Fsharp,Gleam,Lua,Python' keyless rows). So a text census overcounts, and only the node strict-blast run (BACKEND-BATCH) gives the real list. The named non-unit candidates stay: date_time format/time_span/days/hours/minutes/seconds/milliseconds/stopwatch, sm' char_code/of_char, console write_error_line/read_to_end, listm' fold', optionm' is_none', env os_environ/environ_get, seq new_seq, common dispose.")
inl note_of_backend_coverage_gaps_66 () = Note ("BACKEND-COVERAGE-GAPS", 66u32, "2026-11-01T07:09Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 03:09 compiler: THE MATRIX CAN'T MEASURE LIB SWITCH GAPS. pack12b's full run (7,405 rows) has 0 real missing-arm rows: only the negative samples hit the guard, because the matrix samples use the core package, not lib/spiral. Zig's 408 compile errors are all the 'contracts' suite (expected negatives, verdict parity). So lib coverage per backend lives in lib TEST TARGETS. NEW METRIC (lib/spiral, 746 tests, '///>' targets, excluding the generated bench/parsing/target copies): rust 427, gleam 205, cuda 141, typescript 117, fsharp 72, lua 65, ZIG 27, C 22, delphi 10, python 9, LEAN 0, WASM 0, kotlin/swift 0. That's the honest coverage gap for the self-hosting gate: Gleam (a top target) is under half of Rust, and Zig/Lean/Wasm, the native and verified targets, are nearly untested in lib. NEXT STEP (node, CPU-heavy, so a job): for each lib test green on rust, try adding zig (then lean) as a target on a lib copy and record which go green as-is, which fail with a missing arm (the real Zig gap list), and which fail otherwise. That's a one-shot census via lib_program_gate on a copy, no lib land.")
inl note_of_backend_coverage_gaps_67 () = Note ("BACKEND-COVERAGE-GAPS", 67u32, "2026-11-02T11:11Z", "compiler", "11-02 07:30 compiler: REGRESSION. The Zig Export arm from pack 5o (n55/n56) is gone from the live core. Installed pack15d answers \"Export with 2 args not supported in the Zig backend\". export_family.spi was never wired, so no gate saw it. Restore and wire tracked as LINKED-UNITS-ZIG step 0 (n3).")
inl note_of_backend_coverage_gaps_68 () = Note ("BACKEND-COVERAGE-GAPS", 68u32, "2026-11-04T14:24Z", "main", "11-04 10:3x main 48 h groom: untouched since 11-02; the per-backend shape gaps are compiler-lane work and no main slot drives them this window, so Active -> Planned.")
