inl kino_daemon_stall () = Task ("KINO-DAEMON-STALL", "Unexplained ~20 min stall on the shared compile daemon (port 13905, ~14:53-15:20 on 2026-10-12): clients connected, the .NET compiler server at 0 CPU, no compiles logged; root cause unknown (the 5 s working-set probe bound is hardening, not a fix); reproduce with xsim against one shared daemon from several BEAMs, log per-request receive/start/finish in the daemon; 2026-10-13 12:15 Kino lane: daemon now logs 'received <client> <file>' per request and 'started <client> <file>' when a compiler takes it (plus the existing finish line), so a stall shows which step stopped; not reproduced today; note: daemon logs show size 0 in directory listings while the daemon runs (NTFS updates the size on close), read the content instead; 2026-10-13 19:10 Kino lane repro attempt, negative: one daemon (port 13945, fresh start, pool default auto = up to 3 compilers, fair per client) shared by 4 BEAM clients for 25 min (18:43-19:08), each looping run_report over a fresh 4-test file (real compiles, stubbed rustc/execute, memo off) under heavy machine load (eoie builds and main's single-flight/hopac timings in parallel): 372 runs, 1488 tests, all passed; daemon log 2976 requests in the window, every received has a started and a compile line; received->done avg 4.1 s, max 16 s; queue wait avg 3.5 s, max 16 s; largest gap between daemon log lines during the run 5 s; no stall; a stall would now show as received without started (queue) or started without a compile line (compiler); side finding: every test whose body ends in a unit expression other than _assert compiles twice (first compile fails with the unit-result error, then Kino retries with 0i32 appended); in this run that doubled the requests (1488 error + 1488 ok lines); 2026-10-13 19:33 side finding fixed (main's go): the test scoping wrapper (apps/kino/spi/notebook.spi scoped_test_body) now ends main with inl _ = spiral_kino_test () then 0i32 when the body does not end in an _assert (an _assert ending keeps the plain statement); the compiler accepts inl _ = for unit and value results alike (checked: a value statement followed by 0i32 is a type error, Got i32 Expected ()); physics.spi: 58 daemon compile requests for 39 tests before, 40 after, 39/39 passed; regression test in notebook_report_test (unit and value endings compile once each); 2026-10-14 00:48 cause found in code (the 10-12 log is gone; current logs scanned for gaps and started-without-compile, none): a compile request whose compiler stops answering holds the serialized daemon (or its pool worker) for the full recv timeout, and on a cold compiler that timeout is @cold_compile_ms = 1_200_000 = exactly 20 min (compiler_daemon.ex session: max(req.timeout, 20 min) when not warm); the bridge waited with ReceiveTimeout = 0 and nothing checked liveness, so the signature is exactly the observed one: clients connected, .NET compiler at 0 CPU, nothing logged after started; fix (uncommitted): compiler_bridge.ps1 Wait-Response polls the outstanding reply every second and, per StallSeconds window (SPIRAL_KINO_COMPILER_STALL_S, default 120, 0 disables), declares 'compiler stalled: N ms CPU in S s with a compile outstanding' when the compiler process used less than 1% of a core in the window (an idle warm compiler measured 0 ms / 30 s, a hung pwsh ~16 ms / 15 s, a compiling one ~100%); the daemon passes -StallSeconds and treats 'compiler stalled' as a lost session: kill the tree, start a fresh compiler, retry once (the log shows 'compiler session lost (compiler stalled ...)'); proof with a fake compiler (pwsh, AF_UNIX server that sleeps forever on its first request at 0 CPU, answers after a restart), daemon from a private copy of apps/kino, private TMP: HEAD code still waiting at 180 s with nothing logged after started (cold timeout 20 min), fixed code: stall detected (109 ms CPU in 30 s), compiler restarted, reply ok after 67 s; real compiler through the new bridge (private port 13988, convert.spi): 2/2 passed, no stall lines, daemon retired; regression test in compiler_daemon_test.exs (5 s window, recovers in ~12 s), file 17/18 green (the one failure is the compiled_ebins path regex under my private MIX_BUILD_PATH); still unknown: why the compiler went idle on 10-12, which now shows up as a stall line instead of a 20 min hang; the shared daemon picks this up through its normal code-stamp replace", TaskKind, P1, D4, 100u32, Done, "KINO-SHARED-DAEMON-QUEUE", "kino,daemon,reliability", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_kino_daemon_stall () = Planning ("KINO-DAEMON-STALL", Minutes 240u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl kino_flaky_tests () = Task ("KINO-FLAKY-TESTS", "Two apps/kino tests are intermittent under machine load (2026-10-12): kino_live_test \"compiles and runs a cell\" got value nil instead of \"49\" (1 of 164; rerun 35/35 green) and runner_test \"kills the whole process tree\" (1 of 157); find the race or timing assumption, never just raise timeouts; 2026-10-13 12:15 Kino lane: kino_live 'compiles and runs a cell' reproduced under load (failed on repeat 5 of --repeat-until-failure, value nil, stdout fine, exit 0): root cause the Runner finished on {exit_status} while the program's last output (SPIRAL_KINO_VALUE:49, printed right before process::exit) had not been delivered yet (Windows port ordering); fix: after exit_status keep reading output until the port closes ({:EXIT, port}) or 2 s; regression test (2000 lines then the value line then halt, 10 runs) in runner_test.exs; after the fix 16/16 repeats green under load; runner 'kills the whole process tree' never failed in 21 loaded repeats, left open; 2026-10-13 14:39 runner kill-tree analysis (main: no Runner API change, revisit only if it fails again): the test starts parent and grandchild escripts under a fixed 40 s Runner timeout and then requires the grandchild's pidfile and a live grandchild; under heavy load escript startup can exceed the timeout, so the timeout kills the parent before the grandchild exists (or kills it before the liveness check), failing 'grandchild never started' or the alive precondition; a robust test would start the timeout only once the program signals readiness; 2026-10-13 17:33 new flaky cause found and fixed (Kino lane): the Erlang port on Windows drops the whole output of a child that prints and exits at once (targets_test 'a C cell whose assertion fails' failed in the 16:10 suite and passed alone); measured at 24 concurrent runs of a print-and-exit-3 C program: raw Port loses output in 4-10% (exit 3 arrives, no data message even after 3 s; hide/stderr/eof port variants all lose), cmd echo 1%; Runner on Windows now runs the child as cmd /d /v:off /s /c with stdout+stderr redirected to a per-run temp file (name carries the BEAM OS pid, since concurrent BEAMs collided on unique_integer) and reads its tail (max_output) after exit; exit codes pass through unchanged incl. crash codes (-1073741819 both ways); arguments with a double quote, percent or newline fall back to the direct port; 400/400 kept output under the same load; regression tests in runner_test (300 concurrent cmd echo runs keep their output; quoting and fallback); full Kino suite 206/206; separately, escript runs hang in about 1 of 5 runs under today's load in both modes (the burst escript test timed out once; environmental); 2026-10-14 00:22 kill-tree test fixed (uncommitted, runner_test.exs, no Runner API change): the race was the fixed 40 s Runner timeout starting at spawn while the test assumed two escript boots finish inside it; now an attempt whose timeout fired before the grandchild was running (pidfile missing or grandchild already gone when the run ends) is inconclusive and reruns in a fresh dir, up to 3 attempts, then flunks naming the load; the kill assertions (grandchild and child dead after the timeout) run only on an attempt where the grandchild was alive before the timeout; mix test runner_test.exs --repeat-until-failure 3 (private MIX_BUILD_PATH) under load (tibia arena runs, another Kino lane's daemons): 4 x 8 tests, 0 failures, 62-87 s per pass; the rerun branch was not hit in these passes; earlier fixes (exit_status before last output, Windows port output loss) stand; escript hangs under extreme load stay environmental", TaskKind, P2, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "kino,tests,reliability", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_kino_flaky_tests () = Planning ("KINO-FLAKY-TESTS", Minutes 120u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl tibia_scenario_hardness () = Task ("TIBIA-SCENARIO-HARDNESS", "The single-Troll hunt saturates on risk: 0 deaths over seeds 1..10 for heal thresholds 20/60/120 and even for the random control (apps/tibia/hunt_sim.spi troll_scenario_saturates_no_deaths_over_ten_seeds, 2026-10-12), so it cannot separate careful from careless policies; add scenario parameters (creature kind from creatures.spi, count, player level/equipment) and keep only scenarios where the random control dies sometimes and the tuned scripted policy rarely does, e.g. Cyclops (260 health, melee 105) or several Trolls at once; first step done 2026-10-12: hunt_sim takes the foe as a compile-time creatures.arena_creature (Troll default, all Troll goldens unchanged) and a Cyclops scenario separates policies: heal below 120 -> 0 deaths, heal below 20 -> 10 deaths, random control -> 10 deaths over seeds 1..10 (Rust and C agree); remaining: creature count, player level/equipment, and kills-subject-to-deaths ranking; 2026-10-13 06:14 the Cyclops scenario (armor/defense 20, cyclops-hunter offense) separates policies: heal at 120 -> 1 death in 10 seeds, at 20 -> 10, random control -> 4", TaskKind, P2, D3, 100u32, Done, "TIBIA-SIM-DEATHS-METRIC", "tibia,arena", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_tibia_scenario_hardness () = Planning ("TIBIA-SCENARIO-HARDNESS", Minutes 180u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl compile_timeout_empty_output () = Task ("COMPILE-TIMEOUT-EMPTY-OUTPUT", "A compile that times out leaves its output file empty instead of untouched: on 2026-10-12 a low-memory kill emptied apps/compiler/tmp/samples/abi_external/main.c and the empty file reached commit 2ef85029 (good copy restored in the index); write outputs to a temp file and rename on success, and make the harness refuse to record an empty output; 19:56 (uncommitted, not yet built): new shared section OutputFiles (between the Graph and ServerUtils pairs) with write_output_atomically_async (write path.tmp, then File.Move overwrite); single-flight's BuildOk handler wrote outputs in place with WriteAllTextAsync (truncated by a kill, the empty abi_external/main.c) and now calls the helper; hopac's 22-line tmp + File.Replace + copy-fallback block now calls the same helper (its copy fallback is gone: a failed move is reported through the existing artifact_filesystem_persistence_failed diagnostic); pair-drift passes (SignalRSupervisor touched on both sides); 20:03: compiles in single-flight Debug (20:01, 0 errors); hopac compile pending; 07:28 harness half done (apps/compiler/tmp/scripts/test.ps1): a compile that reports ok but leaves its output empty or missing is scored error with detail 'empty output', so it shows as REGRESSED in review instead of being blessed; with the atomic writes from 10-12 (shipped in packs 0002-0005) this closes the item; verified by pack 1's suites; 2026-10-13 09:52 main: pack 1 verified (both Release cores, both suites DISAGREE 0, sf vs hopac differ only on the expected Delphi format_any row) and blessed (3843 rows reproduced row for row; baseline 3936 -> 3949 lines)", TaskKind, P1, D2, 100u32, Done, "", "compiler,harness,reliability", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_compile_timeout_empty_output () = Planning ("COMPILE-TIMEOUT-EMPTY-OUTPUT", Minutes 60u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl gleam_js_target_gaps () = Task ("GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS", "Gleam JavaScript target gaps: tanh, sin, cos and arrays/refs are still Erlang-only (spiral_halt and log/exp/sqrt got JavaScript arms in pack 7); deferred until a JavaScript-target Gleam project needs them (options in note 13:56)", TaskKind, P0, D3, 100u32, Done, "PACK-12", "team:compiler,compiler,gleam,javascript,typefeatureset", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_gleam_js_target_gaps () = Planning ("GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS", Minutes 180u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_gleam_js_target_gaps_1 () = Note ("GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS", 1u32, "2026-10-14T12:35Z", "main-thread", "08:35 measured: samples/native_closure_union_rec's main.gleam builds for --target javascript as is; the only build blocker is the harness runner (tests/native-shims/gleam/src/spiral_run.gleam halt is @external(erlang) only). In a scratch copy, a 3-line spiral_exit.mjs (process.exit) plus @external(javascript, ...) runs it: JS exits 15, Erlang 70 (= C). Cause: spiral_wrap_unsigned/signed use int.bitwise_and/shift_left, which are 32-bit on JS, so 1 << 64 collapses the mask; exact 64-bit wrap on JS needs BigInt (same root as LUA-WIDE-INTEGERS). Next: add the JS halt to the shim, wrap via modulo for bits <= 32 on JS, and give a JS-target lane its Known rows for 64-bit.")
inl note_of_gleam_js_target_gaps_2 () = Note ("GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS", 2u32, "2026-10-14T14:22Z", "main-thread", "10:22 harness runner now builds on both targets: tests/native-shims/gleam/src/spiral_exit.mjs (process.exit) plus @external(javascript, ...) on spiral_run.gleam's halt. The Erlang path is unchanged. Remaining gap: 64-bit wrap on JS (int.bitwise_* is 32-bit there).")
inl note_of_gleam_js_target_gaps_3 () = Note ("GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS", 3u32, "2026-10-15T10:48Z", "main-thread", "06:48 groom: Gleam 129 rows, 128 agree, 1 known, 1 build-fail (pack-1h oracle, Erlang target). The JS-target gap list is unchanged.")
inl note_of_gleam_js_target_gaps_4 () = Note ("GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS", 4u32, "2026-10-15T11:04Z", "main-thread", "07:04 decision (user 'we need all of them sota'): raised to P2; every backend stays on the active rotation.")
inl note_of_gleam_js_target_gaps_5 () = Note ("GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS", 5u32, "2026-10-17T10:02Z", "main-thread", "06:02 main-thread night: the remaining JS gap (64-bit wrap) is the same root as LUA-WIDE-INTEGERS (32-bit bitwise ops, doubles past 2^53); separately lib convert.try_convert on Gleam now parses strings for every integer type (was only int), fixed in the tree.")
inl note_of_gleam_js_target_gaps_6 () = Note ("GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS", 6u32, "2026-10-19T12:35Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 08:35 lap 1 grain: related progress today: lib Gleam string units are UTF-8 bytes everywhere (index_of, length', substring through bit_array), and bit_array exists on the JavaScript target too, so string helpers add no new Erlang-only externals; remaining JS gaps unchanged: spiral_halt, erlangMath, arrays/refs, 64-bit wrap Next: check whether gleam/bit_array.slice and byte_size behave identically on the JS target for non-ASCII (one gleam test on --target javascript)")
inl note_of_gleam_js_target_gaps_7 () = Note ("GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS", 7u32, "2026-10-19T13:34Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 09:34 lap 2 grain: verified the byte-unit string helpers are target-identical. lab/GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS/utf8-probe.ps1 builds a tiny project in spiral-bin/runs (gleam 1.19.2, gleam_stdlib) over h + e-acute + llo + space + U+1F600 and prints byte_size, slice(1,2) decoded, a 1-byte cut of the 2-byte char, a slice past the end, and string.length: erlang (scoop OTP first on PATH; the default PATH OTP fails, the kino-mix-erlang-path trap) and javascript both print 11 e-acute cut-rejected past-error 7. So lib's Gleam index_of/length'/substring through bit_array (UTF-8 bytes, 10-19) need no JS-specific code, and a cut UTF-8 sequence is detectable on both targets the same way (to_string Error), which is what spiral_halt's exit 3 needs. Remaining JS gaps unchanged: spiral_halt itself (an Erlang halt external; JS needs process.exit via an @external javascript twin), erlangMath (log, exp, tanh, sqrt, sin, cos: gleam_community_maths or float.* where present), arrays/refs. Next: write the @external(javascript) twin of spiral_halt as a .mjs snippet and check it exits 3 under node")
inl note_of_gleam_js_target_gaps_8 () = Note ("GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS", 8u32, "2026-10-19T13:39Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 09:39 drive slice: closes 2 of the 4 JS gaps as a compiler patch, proven on both Gleam targets by hand. lab/GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS/patch.py (3 anchored edits to codegenGleam, a shared section, anchor-checked on a copy): spiral_halt gets a second attribute @external(javascript, node:process, exit) next to the erlang halt one; Log and Sqrt go through new prelude helpers spiral_math_log/spiral_math_sqrt over float.logarithm/float.square_root (gleam_stdlib, both targets; Error panics like math:log raised badarith); Exp becomes float.exponential directly (stdlib has erlang and javascript externals). Tanh, Sin, Cos stay erlang-only (gleam_stdlib has no portable versions), and arrays/refs (process dictionary) remain the last JS gap. Hand proof, lab/GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS/prelude-probe.ps1 (gleam 1.19.2, stdlib 1.0.5): the exact emitted text prints 2.0 1.5 and exits 3 on erlang AND javascript. Matrix impact predicted from the committed outputs: 25 Gleam residual-only changes (23 outputs that hold spiral_halt gain the JS line; native_float_math_family and native_float_sqrt switch to float.*), native results unchanged. HEAVY QUEUE: apply patch.py with the next compiler pack and read those 25 Gleam rows in its review. Next: tanh/sin/cos for JS (a small .mjs is impossible in a single-file output; candidates: Taylor-free identities via float.exponential for tanh, keep sin/cos erlang-only and document)")
inl note_of_gleam_js_target_gaps_9 () = Note ("GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS", 9u32, "2026-10-19T13:39Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 09:39 decision on tanh/sin/cos for JS: keep them erlang-only. gleam_stdlib 1.0.5 has no portable sin, cos or tanh, and tanh from float.exponential ((1-e)/(1+e) with e = exp(-2|x|)) loses relative precision for small x (cancellation near e = 1, about 1e-6 relative at x = 1e-10), so its printed digits would disagree with the C oracle; an exact JS version needs Math.* through an .mjs FFI file, which a single-file Gleam output cannot carry. Same for arrays/refs (process dictionary and make_ref). So after the queued patch lands, the item's remaining scope is exactly: an optional companion .mjs emitted by the Gleam project builder (Cells builders, spiral CLI) for Math.sin/cos/tanh and a JS array store; no consumer needs it today (near_wallet uses none). Done criterion recorded: patch landed and the 25 Gleam rows reproduce; the .mjs companion becomes its own item only if a JS consumer appears")
inl note_of_gleam_js_target_gaps_10 () = Note ("GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS", 10u32, "2026-10-19T15:23Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 11:23 lap 5 grain: pack 7 readiness: on a scratch copy of today's spiral_compiler.fs the four helper-lane-2 compiler patches stack cleanly in order: GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS patch.py (3 edits), EBPF-C-PROFILE patch.py (2), OPENQASM3-BACKEND patch.py (2), SPIRAL-INDEPENDENT stack-frames.py (2); all exit 0, tree untouched. Recheck after the core switch if main edits the compiler.")
inl note_of_gleam_js_target_gaps_11 () = Note ("GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS", 11u32, "2026-10-19T15:41Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 11:41 lap 6 grain: re-derived the patch's predicted residual impact from today's committed outputs: of 447 samples/**/main.gleam, 23 define spiral_halt and 2 use the erlang math externals for log/exp/sqrt (native_float_math_family, native_float_sqrt), 25 distinct files in all. That matches the queued row's 25 Gleam residual-only changes exactly, so pack 7's review can check the patch against an exact list: any Gleam residual change outside those 25 files is unintended.")
inl note_of_gleam_js_target_gaps_12 () = Note ("GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS", 12u32, "2026-10-19T15:54Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 11:54 lap 7 grain: the doc half of the patch, so the AGENTS.md Gleam paragraph does not go stale when pack 7 lands. lab/GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS/agents-note.py rewrites its last sentence: spiral_halt now has an @external(javascript, node:process, exit) arm, log/exp/sqrt go through gleam/float, and only tanh/sin/cos (no portable gleam_stdlib versions; tanh through float.exponential loses precision near 0) plus arrays/refs stay Erlang-only. Dry run: the anchor matches once. Queued to ride pack 7 after patch.py.")
inl note_of_gleam_js_target_gaps_13 () = Note ("GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS", 13u32, "2026-10-19T16:40Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 12:40 lap 8 grain: pack 7 state check: the Gleam codegen half is in the tree (floatResultMath and the node:process exit external, 4 hits in spiral_compiler.fs), but the AGENTS.md half is not: agents-note.py's anchor still matches once, so apps/compiler/tmp/AGENTS.md still says spiral_halt and erlangMath are Erlang-only. Main is told to apply it together with the pack 7 review (the doc must land with the reviewed code). Done = review shows exactly the predicted 25 changed Gleam outputs, then agents-note --apply")
inl note_of_gleam_js_target_gaps_14 () = Note ("GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS", 14u32, "2026-10-19T17:16Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 13:16 lap 9 grain: both halves now in the tree: agents-note.py's anchor is gone (0 hits) and apps/compiler/tmp/AGENTS.md carries the new sentence (Since pack 7 spiral_halt has a JavaScript implementation, log/exp/sqrt via gleam/float, tanh/sin/cos + arrays/refs stay Erlang-only); review single-flight-20261019-124451 changed exactly 25 Gleam residuals as predicted, DISAGREE 0. Accept single-flight-20261019-125623 is still running (baseline EXPECTED.tsv not yet rewritten). Next: Done when that accept lands (main records it)")
inl note_of_gleam_js_target_gaps_15 () = Note ("GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS", 15u32, "2026-10-19T17:41Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 13:41 lap 10 grain: the only JavaScript-target Gleam project in spiral (lib/spiral/near/wallet, the one gleam.toml with target = javascript) has no @external at all in near_wallet.gleam (erlang 0, javascript 0, no spiral_math_tanh/sin/cos), so the Erlang-only leftovers named in the new AGENTS.md sentence reach no JS build today. Done still waits only on accept single-flight-20261019-125623 (native tier running at 13:41; baseline not yet rewritten)")
inl note_of_gleam_js_target_gaps_16 () = Note ("GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS", 16u32, "2026-10-19T17:43Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 13:43 its pack 7 patch is in the accepted baseline (pack 7 ACCEPTED single-flight-20261019-125623 (7,020 rows, DISAGREE 0, 0 differing cells vs review 124451, C rows unchanged)); the item stays open for its remaining scope.")
inl note_of_gleam_js_target_gaps_17 () = Note ("GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS", 17u32, "2026-10-19T17:46Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 13:46 Done (drive 11), evidence: pack 7 accepted as single-flight-20261019-125623 (main 13:43: 7,020 rows, DISAGREE 0, 0 differing cells vs review 124451); its Gleam residuals differ from the previous baseline run single-flight-20261019-113145 in exactly 25 rows, the predicted set; spiral_compiler.fs carries the JavaScript arms (spiral_halt via node:process exit, log/exp/sqrt through gleam/float); apps/compiler/tmp/AGENTS.md states the new split (only tanh/sin/cos and arrays/refs stay Erlang-only); the only JavaScript-target Gleam project (lib/spiral/near/wallet) has no externals at all. The 3 other modified main.gleam files in git status (ebpf_neq, multiline_comment, portable_math_rounding) predate pack 7")
inl note_of_gleam_js_target_gaps_18 () = Note ("GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS", 18u32, "2026-10-19T17:47Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 13:47 correction: reopened (Active 60) right after my Done, because main's 13:43 note keeps the item open for its remaining scope, which the title still names: tanh, sin, cos and arrays/refs have no JavaScript implementation (Erlang-only externals). The 13:46 evidence holds for the pack 7 half only. Next: a portable tanh with C-oracle digits (the open question from lap 7: float.exponential loses relative precision near 0), sin/cos via a JS external (@external(javascript, Math...)), arrays/refs as JS externals; low priority while no JS-target project reaches them")
inl note_of_gleam_js_target_gaps_19 () = Note ("GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS", 19u32, "2026-10-19T17:56Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 13:56 lap 11 grain, options for the remaining sin/cos/tanh: gleam_community_maths v2.0.2 (hexdocs) exports sin, cos, tanh, exponential and natural_logarithm as pub fn (Float) -> Float (log returns Result), and ships its own Erlang and JavaScript externals, so the codegen could call maths.sin/cos/tanh on both targets; the cost is a hex dependency in every generated Gleam project that uses trig (gleam.toml of the native tier, Cells and lib projects), against today's dependency-free output. The other route is a per-program JS external, which needs an .mjs file next to the module (Gleam JS externals name a module path, and Math is a global, not a module). Decision deferred until a JS-target project needs trig (none does); recorded so the choice is ready")
inl note_of_gleam_js_target_gaps_20 () = Note ("GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS", 20u32, "2026-10-22T06:59Z", "main", "02:59 main: lab drained. patch.py (its spiral_halt anchor is gone; checked on a copy, since it writes without an apply flag) and agents-note.py (anchor consumed; tmp/AGENTS.md has the pack-7 Gleam prelude paragraph) have landed. prelude-probe.ps1 and utf8-probe.ps1 were the pack-7 probes whose results are in notes 13:46/13:47. Folder deleted. The open half (tanh, sin, cos, arrays/refs on the JavaScript target) has its options in note 13:56 and stays deferred until a JS-target project needs them; nothing for it was in lab.")
inl note_of_gleam_js_target_gaps_21 () = Note ("GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS", 21u32, "2026-10-24T11:49Z", "main", "2026-10-19 laps folded from the deleted history ledger (docs/agents/history/2026-10-19-backlog-laps, HISTORY-BACKLOG-LAPS-DRIFT): lap drive2 09:39 (subagent2): compiler patch: spiral_halt JS external + log/exp/sqrt via gleam/float; hand probe exits 3 with identical output on erlang and javascript; queued to ride the next pack (25 Gleam residual changes predicted) -> next: tanh for JS via float.exponential; sin/cos stay erlang-only (document) | lap 3 10:09 (subagent2): driven in drive phase 2 (patch queued, tanh/sin/cos decision) -> next: read the 25 Gleam rows after the pack | lap 4 10:32 (subagent2): waiting: patch rides next pack -> next: read 25 Gleam rows | lap drive11 13:46 (subagent2): Done: accept 125623 (DISAGREE 0, = review), Gleam residuals vs 113145 exactly the 25 predicted, codegen + AGENTS.md in tree, JS-target project has no externals -> next: lab/GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS can be promoted/deleted (user) | lap drive11b 13:47 (subagent2): reopened Active 60: main's 13:43 note keeps it open for tanh/sin/cos + arrays/refs JS implementations; the Done evidence covers pack 7's half only -> next: JS externals for sin/cos/tanh and arrays/refs (low priority) | lap 12 14:28 (subagent2): demand sized: of the committed Gleam outputs only native_float_math_family calls spiral_math_sin/cos/tanh (Erlang target, unaffected); log/exp/sqrt already go through float.* (portable branch, compiler 166832); in lib only math.spi and physics.spi use trig, and no Gleam-JS project imports them -> next: decide when a JS-target project needs trig (keep low priority) | lap 13 14:41 (subagent2): no demand change (lap 12 sizing holds: 0 Gleam-JS consumers of trig or halt) -> next: unblocks: the first JS-target project that needs trig/halt picks hex maths vs per-program .mjs | lap 14 15:03 (subagent2): no demand; low priority -> next: first JS-target project needing trig/halt | lap 17 16:43 (subagent2): the lab gleam_runner ports the JS bundling path (esbuild + trunk) unchanged, so this item's codegen gaps are independent of the CLI split -> next: first JS-target demand | lap 19 18:56 (subagent2): unchanged (trig route decided on first JS-target demand) -> next: first JS-target project | lap 22 19:48 (subagent2): latest check of the recorded trig option: gleam_community_maths 2.0.2 (2026-04-20) is still the newest release, so the lap 11 option text stands -> next: first JS-target project needing trig")
inl note_of_gleam_js_target_gaps_22 () = Note ("GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS", 22u32, "2026-10-26T20:50Z", "main", "10-26 16:50 main: 10-26 16:5x main: demand re-check by the item's own rule (deferred until a JavaScript-target Gleam project needs trig or arrays/refs). Scan over every repo under C:\\home\\git: gleam.toml files with target = javascript, and .ps1/.yml build scripts invoking gleam build/run/test --target javascript. Result: one JS-target project, lib/spiral/near/wallet (near_wallet: lustre, modem, gleam_javascript), and no script invoking --target javascript. near_wallet's .spi and generated .gleam have 0 uses of sin, cos, tanh, spiral_math_*, am' arrays, array_base or refs. Demand is unchanged: 0 consumers of the gaps. The item stays deferred by rule. This matches the 10-19 lap's 'JS-target project has no externals', which was likely the same project.")
inl note_of_gleam_js_target_gaps_23 () = Note ("GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS", 23u32, "2026-10-27T23:43Z", "main", "10-27 19:43 main: Groom 10-27 19:4x: status Active -> Planned. By its own rule the item is deferred until a JavaScript-target Gleam project needs trig or arrays/refs, and the 10-26 16:50 demand scan found 0 consumers (the only JS-target project, lib/spiral/near/wallet, uses none of the gaps). Active made it count as work in progress that nobody can advance. It returns to Active the day a demand scan finds a consumer; the scan is the same one as n.10-26 16:50.")
inl note_of_gleam_js_target_gaps_24 () = Note ("GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS", 24u32, "2026-10-29T09:25Z", "main", "10-29 05:3x main: lib/spiral/lustre.spi (tests at :150, :166) and lib/spiral/near/wallet/src/near_wallet.spi (:60, :76) fail every test in the post-wipe lib gate at 'gleam build': 'Dependency resolution failed ... There's no compatible version of gleam_stdlib: lustre_ui requires gleam_community_maths >= 1.1.1 and < 2.0.0; gleam_community_maths requires gleam_stdlib >= 0.38.0 and < 1.0.0', against Cells' gleam_stdlib '>=1.0.5 and <2.0.0'. Their targets pin '-d lustre_ui=\\\"1.0.0-rc.1\\\"'. Hex today (read-only API): lustre_ui's latest is 1.0.0-rc.1 and its latest stable is 0.6.0, which needs gleam_stdlib ~> 0.34, so NO lustre_ui release resolves with gleam_stdlib 1.x. gleam_community_maths itself is at 2.0.2. Whatever cache made it pass before the wipe (spiral-bin\\cells-gleam-warm, lost), a fresh resolution cannot. Options: drop lustre_ui from these test cells (near/wallet's own gleam.toml locks lustre_ui 0.4.0 for its standalone build), or give these Gleam JavaScript targets their own gleam_stdlib below 1.0. The other Gleam lib tests pass.")
inl note_of_gleam_js_target_gaps_25 () = Note ("GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS", 25u32, "2026-10-29T12:04Z", "main", "10-29 08:1x main: DECIDED and LANDED. lib/spiral/lustre.spi is migrated to lustre 5, and lustre_ui is dropped from the Cells test targets. Measured first, in a scratch gleam project with the cells' own constraints minus lustre_ui: Hex resolves lustre 5.7.1 with gleam_stdlib 1.0.5 and gleam_json 3.1.0. lustre_ui's upstream main does declare gleam_stdlib >= 1.0, but it is unreleased (still labelled 1.0.0-rc.1) and needs lustre >= 5.7, so no Hex release of lustre_ui fits Cells' stdlib 1.x.\nChanges, all through apply.ps1 (14 anchors), with main's scratchpad holding lustre5/apply.ps1 and the undo copies:\n- the attribute type: lustre/internals/vdom.Attribute -> lustre/attribute.Attribute;\n- style -> attribute.styles (lustre 5 split style(name, value) from styles(list));\n- new primitives class, role and attr, each one host call;\n- button is now html.button over (class \"lustre-ui-button\" :: role \"button\" :: attr \"tabindex\" \"0\" :: attributes), which keeps lustre_ui's markup, so its stylesheet still matches;\n- centre is a div with class lustre-ui-centre;\n- the 4 directives change to lustre=\">=5.7.0\" gleam_json=\">=2.0.0\";\n- expected HTML follows lustre 5's attribute order, class first.\nProof (job-1793274523797-main-lustre5-proof): on lib copies, the control arm fails 0 of 4, reproducing the Hex conflict, and the lustre5 arm passes 4 of 4 (lustre.spi tests and tests_2, near_wallet.spi tests and tests_2) with 0 type errors. Landed at 08:1x with the reader guard; the live files equalled the proof's control before the land and the proven copy after.\nFollow-up NOT done: the standalone near/wallet build (its own gleam.toml has lustre < 5, lustre_ui 0.4, modem, decipher, and the manifest locks stdlib 0.57) still matches its committed generated near_wallet.gleam. Regenerating near_wallet.gleam from the new lib needs that gleam.toml bumped to lustre 5.7 and gleam_stdlib 1.x, and its lustre_ui stylesheet link in index.html replaced. See NEAR-WALLET-LUSTRE5.")
inl note_of_gleam_js_target_gaps_26 () = Note ("GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS", 26u32, "2026-10-30T02:06Z", "compiler", "10-29 22:0x compiler (groom): status Planned -> Paused at 60%. The work was started (25 notes, last 12:04) and then stopped, which 'Planned' at 60% misstated. The open follow-up is the near/wallet regeneration, tracked as NEAR-WALLET-LUSTRE5.")
inl note_of_gleam_js_target_gaps_27 () = Note ("GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS", 27u32, "2026-10-31T20:01Z", "compiler", "10-31 10-31 16:01 compiler GROOM (user direction via /btw, 10-31 15:4x): the self-hosted rewrite (SELF-HOSTED-SPIRAL) starts only after EVERY backend is SOTA: no memory problems, no Rc slowdowns, no union hacks, no recursion limits, no missing arithmetic; GADT bugs and conversion gaps are 'unacceptable by now'; edition 3 finishes first; Gleam is a top test target (abyssal codebases) with F#, Rust and Wasm; Cells as fast as F# projects. This item is now on that gate (priority -> P0, status -> Active).")
inl note_of_gleam_js_target_gaps_28 () = Note ("GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS", 28u32, "2026-10-31T20:36Z", "compiler", "10-31 10-31 16:36 compiler: PLAN to close it (gate item now, so no longer 'deferred until a JS project needs them'). The remaining Erlang-only operations (tanh, sin, cos; arrays and refs via the process dictionary / make_ref) have no portable gleam_stdlib versions, and a pure-Gleam sin/cos would drift from the C oracle's libm digits. Gleam's JavaScript externals need a module path, and the backend can already emit SEVERAL files per program (build_many returns a GeneratedFile list). So: codegenGleam writes main.gleam plus spiral_ffi.mjs only when an operation needs it, and each such function carries both externals: @external(erlang, 'math', 'sin') @external(javascript, './spiral_ffi.mjs', 'sin') (Math.sin/cos/tanh; arrays as JS Arrays and refs as {value} cells in the .mjs; the Erlang arms unchanged). Proof: a JS-target native lane for the Gleam rows that use them (gleam build --target javascript, then run), agreeing with C on the math and array samples, and lib/spiral/near/wallet building for JavaScript with an array. Estimate ~4 h (codegen + the extra native lane).")
inl note_of_gleam_js_target_gaps_29 () = Note ("GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS", 29u32, "2026-10-31T20:41Z", "compiler", "10-31 10-31 16:41 compiler: PLAN REFINED from the live codegenGleam (17695-17755) and package research. (1) Precedent in the tree: spiral_halt already pairs '@external(erlang, ...)' with '@external(javascript, 'node:process', 'exit')', and spiral_string_of_checked_bytes shows an Erlang external whose Gleam BODY serves as the fallback on other targets. So any operation with a JS-capable implementation needs no extra file. (2) MATH: gleam_community_maths supports both the Erlang and JavaScript targets (README; 2.x exposes maths.sin flat, 1.x under maths/elementary): https://hexdocs.pm/gleam_community_maths/ , https://preview.hex.pm/preview/gleam_community_maths/2.0.2 . To verify before adopting: that tanh exists, and that its JS digits match C's libm on the matrix's math samples (Erlang stays math:*, unchanged). If tanh is missing or the digits drift, write the JS arms in the generated .mjs instead. (3) ARRAYS/REFS: today spiralArrays/spiralRefs use 'gary' (Erlang arrays) plus the process dictionary (make_ref/put/get): Erlang-only. glearray works on both targets but is IMMUTABLE (https://hexdocs.pm/glearray/ , https://github.com/lunagl/glearray), and Spiral arrays are mutable with aliasing (a callee's write is visible to the caller), so a mutable cell is needed on JS, and Gleam has none without FFI. So arrays/refs need the generated spiral_ffi.mjs (a JS Array in a {value} cell, the same SpiralArray(key, size) surface) or a tiny companion Hex package; the first needs codegenGleam to emit two files (build's GeneratedFile list allows it). Next session: (a) the tanh/digits check of gleam_community_maths in a scratch gleam project (CPU-light), (b) the .mjs emission applier.")
inl note_of_gleam_js_target_gaps_30 () = Note ("GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS", 30u32, "2026-10-31T21:39Z", "compiler", "10-31 10-31 17:39 compiler: (a) of n29 DONE, the math half is settled. Fetched gleam_community_maths 2.0.2 into a scratch gleam project and read its source: pub fn tanh/sin/cos exist (maths.gleam 1076/968/900); each carries @external(erlang, 'math', 'tanh'|'sin'|'cos'), EXACTLY the Erlang externals codegenGleam emits today (erlangMath, 17696), plus @external(javascript, '../maths.mjs', ...) whose bodies are plain Math.tanh/Math.sin/Math.cos. So switching codegenGleam's erlangMath to 'import gleam_community/maths' + maths.tanh/sin/cos changes NOTHING on the Erlang target (same BIFs, same digits) and gives the JavaScript target V8's Math.* (fdlibm-derived). Left for the math half: add gleam_community_maths to the native tier's and Cells' gleam project deps, and check JS digits against C on the matrix's tanh/sin/cos samples (an fdlibm vs msvcrt/glibc last-ulp difference would show as a stdout diff there). The arrays/refs half still needs the generated .mjs (n29 (b)).")
inl note_of_gleam_js_target_gaps_31 () = Note ("GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS", 31u32, "2026-11-01T03:41Z", "compiler", "10-31 10-31 23:41 compiler, cadence groom: TOP-3 CPU-LIGHT #1. Next concrete step: write the applier that emits spiral_ffi.mjs beside the Gleam output (mutable arrays as JS Array, refs as {v} objects) and maps tanh/sin/cos to gleam_community_maths 2.0.2 on the JavaScript target; drafting is CPU-light. NODE-RUNNABLE NEXT: the Gleam JS digits check (gleam run --target javascript on the arrays/refs and maths samples vs the Erlang target's digits) runs on the node, which has bun and gleam; queue it the moment the applier drafts.")
inl note_of_gleam_js_target_gaps_32 () = Note ("GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS", 32u32, "2026-11-01T04:34Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 00:34 compiler, round-2 groom (user 00:2x: backend codegen is CPU-light text work, write it while runs batch): BATCHING: its node digits check joins the batched node job with the other drafted appliers (one candidate build, one matrix pass), so no draft waits for its own run.")
inl note_of_gleam_js_target_gaps_33 () = Note ("GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS", 33u32, "2026-11-01T04:48Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 00:48 compiler: MATH HALF DRAFTED as a kit do program (Spiral-first: no new .ps1 applier). codegenGleam's erlangMath (an Erlang-only external per op) becomes portableMath: 'import gleam_community/maths' + maths.tanh/sin/cos. Per n30 these are the same math:* BIFs on Erlang (no digit change) and Math.* on JavaScript. The dep is added where Gleam projects are generated: the native tier's gleam.toml and Cells' @gleam_deps (targets.ex:346). Dry-applied on copies: 6 replacements. Bases: spiral_compiler.fs C0990B2D590CE965, gleam.toml 14C01FC8F480A476, targets.ex E74FF5BBA52F53E4. LANDING: the .fs part rides the next compiler pack via BACKEND-BATCH; the targets.ex part is a Cells land (reader guard) in the same window. PROOF in BACKEND-BATCH: the Gleam rows with tanh/sin/cos stay byte-identical on Erlang, and a JS-target build of the same rows agrees with C. ARRAYS/REFS half (generated spiral_ffi.mjs) is the next draft.\nPROGRAM (kit do <dir> -, files resolved by name from apps/compiler, apps/compiler/tmp/tests/native-shims/gleam, apps/cells/lib/spiral/cells):\nedit spiral_compiler.fs expect files 1 replacements 4\n  replace once <<<\n        let erlangMath (name : string) (arg : string) =\n            let decl = $\"@external(erlang, \\\"math\\\", \\\"{name}\\\")\\npub fn spiral_math_{name}(x: Float) -> Float\"\n            if not (externals.Contains decl) then externals.Add decl\n            $\"spiral_math_{name}({arg})\"\n  >>> with <<<\n        let portableMath (name : string) (arg : string) =\n            global' \"import gleam_community/maths\"\n            $\"maths.{name}({arg})\"\n  >>>\n  replace once <<<\n                | Tanh, [x] -> erlangMath \"tanh\" (tup x)\n  >>> with <<<\n                | Tanh, [x] -> portableMath \"tanh\" (tup x)\n  >>>\n  replace once <<<\n                | Sin, [x] -> erlangMath \"sin\" (tup x)\n  >>> with <<<\n                | Sin, [x] -> portableMath \"sin\" (tup x)\n  >>>\n  replace once <<<\n                | Cos, [x] -> erlangMath \"cos\" (tup x)\n  >>> with <<<\n                | Cos, [x] -> portableMath \"cos\" (tup x)\n  >>>\nedit gleam.toml expect files 1 replacements 1\n  replace once <<<\ngary = \">= 1.0.0 and < 2.0.0\"\n  >>> with <<<\ngary = \">= 1.0.0 and < 2.0.0\"\ngleam_community_maths = \">= 2.0.2 and < 3.0.0\"\n  >>>\nedit targets.ex expect files 1 replacements 1\n  replace once <<<\n    {\"gary\", \">=1.1.0 and <2.0.0\"},\n  >>> with <<<\n    {\"gary\", \">=1.1.0 and <2.0.0\"},\n    {\"gleam_community_maths\", \">=2.0.2 and <3.0.0\"},\n  >>>")
inl note_of_gleam_js_target_gaps_34 () = Note ("GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS", 34u32, "2026-11-01T06:40Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 02:40 compiler, round 3: ARRAYS/REFS EMISSION ANCHORS (core C0990B2D590CE965). Dispatcher: 'build codegen backend ext' (35003-35006) wraps every single-file backend as [generated_file code ext]; Gleam's arm is at 35008 (build codegenGleam, Gleam, .gleam). codegenGleam (17643) assembles its text at 18359-18449 (globals, then externals at 18361, types, functions, main) and returns one string. PLAN without changing codegenGleam's type: (1) spiralArrays/spiralRefs (17723-17733) register their JS bodies in a jsFfi ResizeArray beside externals, and each Erlang-only decl gains its paired @external(javascript, './<stem>_ffi.mjs', '<fn>') line. Storage moves from gary to OTP's array module (array:new/get/set) and JS Arrays. (2) when jsFfi is non-empty, codegenGleam appends the marker line '//// spiral_ffi.mjs' plus the JS text after main. (3) the dispatcher's Gleam arm splits at the marker BEFORE UnusedGlobals.prune: the .gleam part is pruned as today, the rest becomes generated_file js '_ffi.mjs' (written as main_ffi.mjs by 34795's file + extension). (4) Cells targets.ex run_gleam and the native tier copy *_ffi.mjs beside main.gleam. The stem must be the output file's (main / main_real); build_many already hands 'file' to the lambda.")
inl note_of_gleam_js_target_gaps_35 () = Note ("GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS", 35u32, "2026-11-01T07:24Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 03:23 compiler: math half SHIPPED in PACK-12 (gleam_community/maths on both targets; the Cells and native-tier deps landed with it). Remaining: arrays/refs via the generated _ffi.mjs (anchors in n34).")
inl note_of_gleam_js_target_gaps_36 () = Note ("GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS", 36u32, "2026-11-01T12:34Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 08:34 compiler, ROUND 4 QUALITY SPEC: FIELDS: P0 gate, est 180m left (arrays/refs only), light, dep PACK-12 (math half installed). DEFINITION OF DONE: (a) samples using arrays/refs build and run on the Gleam JavaScript target ('gleam run --target javascript' in a copy of tmp/tests/native-shims/gleam with main.gleam + main_ffi.mjs) with exit codes equal to C for every Gleam-listed sample that uses arrays or refs (count them first from the matrix); (b) the Erlang target is byte-identical in stdout and exit across the Gleam list (predict: compile-status diffs 0; native agree); (c) gary leaves both gleam.toml/targets.ex dep lists. NEXT STEP: change codegenGleam (spiral_compiler.fs 17643) to return a record instead of one string (below), then the dispatcher arm at 35008. TYPEFEATURESET PLAN, REPLACING n34's marker-line idea (a '//// spiral_ffi.mjs' marker split by text is exactly the stringly design to avoid): codegenGleam returns '{ gleam : string; ffi : GleamFfi option }' with 'GleamFfi = { stem : string; functions : FfiFunction list }' and 'FfiFunction = { name : string; erlang : ErlangImpl; javascript : JsImpl }'. The two targets are a UNION 'FfiTarget = Erlang of module * fn | JavaScript of path * fn', rendered into @external lines by one function, so a function can't have one target without the other (the type forces both). The dispatcher pattern-matches the option and emits [generated_file gleam '.gleam'] @ (ffi |> Option.map render |> Option.toList). Storage is an opaque external type (no Option(a) sentinels for 'never set': the JS side throws, the Erlang side uses array:new with {default, undefined} plus a typed check).")
inl note_of_gleam_js_target_gaps_37 () = Note ("GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS", 37u32, "2026-11-01T19:15Z", "compiler", "11-01 15:15 compiler: ARRAYS/REFS HALF DRAFTED, with a simpler shape than n35's record plan and no stringly marker. PROGRAM: %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\spiral\\GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS\\1-gleam-storage-ffi.kitdo (5 replacements on core F6F6879566C5A830 -> 81a865be25d81180; dry run clean; no `gary` left in the core). DESIGN: the storage externals are FIXED code, not per-program code, so nothing needs splitting. codegenGleam takes ffi_module (<output stem>_ffi) and returns (gleam text, GleamFfi), where GleamFfi = NoGleamFfi | GleamStorageFfi is set when spiralArrays or spiralRefs are used. The dispatcher's Gleam arm then also emits <stem>_ffi.erl and <stem>_ffi.mjs through the multi-file GeneratedFile list (the same mechanism as Python's _auto.py), so the output's writer names them. Gleam side: opaque types SpiralArrayStore(a) and SpiralRefCell(a); each external has paired @external(erlang, \"<stem>_ffi\", f) and @external(javascript, \"./<stem>_ffi.mjs\", f) attributes, so neither target can be missing one. Erlang: process dictionary + OTP array (array:new/get/set, fixed size); JS: Array.fill and {value} objects. The public helper API (spiral_array_create/_create_filled/_from_list/_to_list/_get/_set, .size, spiral_ref_new/_get/_set) is unchanged, so no codegen call site moves. The unused listToArray (the last gary/array import) is removed. LANDED NOW (harmless until the pack, since they only act when the files exist): scripts/test.ps1 copies <stem>_ffi.* into the lane project (single-row path) and the batch project (one copy, identical for every row), and bench-native.ps1 copies them for the bench project. Cells needs no copy change: with_cell_sources copies the whole src dir. AFTER THE PACK: drop gary from Cells @gleam_deps (targets.ex:352), from the native shim gleam.toml, and from apps/spiral spiral.spi:762. PROOF RUNNING: node job job-1793560465530-compiler-gleamffi builds the candidate, then compiles the 32 Gleam-listed samples that use arrays or refs (DoD a's count) with installed and candidate, and runs both on Erlang and JS (bun). Prediction: new-erl = old-erl 32/32, new-js = old-erl >= 30/32, old-js 0/32.")
inl note_of_gleam_js_target_gaps_38 () = Note ("GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS", 38u32, "2026-11-01T20:33Z", "compiler", "11-01 16:40 compiler: PROVEN on the node (job-1793562431251-compiler-gleamffi4, candidate core 3B8D4591E4D376C1 = live core + program 1; host = live 98D282B5 + program 2). PROGRAM 2 is new: %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\spiral\\GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS\\2-host-optional-siblings.kitdo, 6 replacements on Program.fs. It was needed because the first two runs failed. Run 1: the host copied only the main file to the requested output, so no _ffi files were written. Run 2: the BuildFile reply is every generated file joined with \"\\n\", and the host wrote that reply as main.gleam, so the Erlang FFI landed inside it ('Syntax error ... -module(main_ffi)'). Fix: for backends with OPTIONAL sibling outputs (Gleam: _ffi.erl, _ffi.mjs), the host reads the main file the core wrote, copies fresh siblings to the output's stem, deletes stale ones there, restores the input-side copies, and skips the source-unchanged cache. Fixed multi-file backends (C++, Python) are unchanged. RESULT over the 32 Gleam-listed array/ref samples. (a) JS target with the generated pair: exit = old Erlang exit on 31/32. The miss is mega_brzozowski_derivatives/runtime_bench, which panics with its own self-check 'brzozowski-bench-ends-with-zero-count' on JS: the known 64-bit wrap gap (32-bit int bitwise on JS), not the FFI. Old compiler on JS: 0/32 (gary). (b) Erlang: new exit = old exit on 32/32 (including 235, 42 and 5); stdout-only re-check on the 9 samples whose run produced output, old vs new: identical 9/9. The first hashes mixed in stderr compiler warnings; those were never program output. NEXT (pack time): ride the next compiler pack with DELPHI-TO-PASCAL step 1 and BACKEND-PRIMITIVE-PARITY's PrimToString arms (all stack cleanly), then drop gary from Cells @gleam_deps (targets.ex:352), from the native shim gleam.toml and from spiral.spi:762, then Done. The JS 64-bit wrap is a separate gap (n-earlier: same root as LUA-WIDE-INTEGERS) and is not part of this item's DoD.")
inl note_of_gleam_js_target_gaps_39 () = Note ("GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS", 39u32, "2026-11-01T22:16Z", "compiler", "11-01 18:20 compiler: PACK15B INTEGRATION (node job-1793567591687, pack15b core A7F3592A598C96A1, which carries both programs plus PrimToString, DELPHI-CASE-INSENSITIVE-NAMES and DELPHI-TO-PASCAL step 1). This time stdout is hashed alone, with stderr dropped, over the 32 array/ref samples. new-erl = old-erl on exit AND stdout: 32/32. new-js = old-erl: 31/32 (runtime_bench, the known JS 64-bit gap). old-js: 0/32. Every new output carries the _ffi pair. pack15b's residual predict is running on the box; then main installs it.")
inl note_of_gleam_js_target_gaps_40 () = Note ("GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS", 40u32, "2026-11-02T01:36Z", "compiler", "11-01 21:37 compiler: DoD (c) STAGED. %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\spiral\\GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS\\3-drop-gary-after-install.kitdo removes gary from Cells @gleam_deps (targets.ex), from the native shim gleam.toml and from apps/spiral/spiral.spi's Gleam dep list (3 replacements, dry run clean, 0 gary left in the three files). Census: nothing outside the compiler's own Gleam output imports gary (x, tibia, kit, fiscal, dice: 0 files). It lands only AFTER pack15c installs, since the installed compiler still emits 'import gary'. Apply from the spiral repo root with kit do, check that the removed lines left no blank entry inside spiral.spi's dep list, then rebuild the CLI through apps/spiral/build.ps1, whose run check now carries the JSON-protocol rows. Then this item is Done.")
inl note_of_gleam_js_target_gaps_41 () = Note ("GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS", 41u32, "2026-11-02T04:49Z", "compiler", "11-02 00:49 compiler: DONE. pack15c (storage FFI + host optional siblings) installed by main 00:29-00:33 (box SpiralCompiler.dll 92820117717425C0 / Core 83CEDAA9DDED49EB, node CAE7CB4C16DA84FD). DoD (c) LANDED unstaged: gary removed from apps/cells/lib/spiral/cells/targets.ex @gleam_deps, apps/compiler/tmp/tests/native-shims/gleam/gleam.toml and apps/spiral/spiral.spi (blank lines left by the program cleaned by hand). CLI rebuilt via apps/spiral/build.ps1 under a compile window: RUST-OK, 29 run-check rows as expected, spiral.exe shipped 00:45:54, 0 'gary' in spiral.rs. PROOF on the installed compiler: samples/array_create_reads_zero -> Gleam with main_ffi.erl/main_ffi.mjs in a gary-free shim project, gleam run exit 5 = C exit 5. Residual known: runtime_bench's 64-bit wrap on the JS target (31/32, n of 16:40) stays its own gap. Vultr runs old core 7B234DAC until its Linux source build. Next step: none on this item; typefeatureset: GleamFfi closed union (NoGleamFfi | GleamStorageFfi) already.")
inl note_of_gleam_js_target_gaps_42 () = Note ("GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS", 42u32, "2026-11-02T05:28Z", "compiler", "11-02 01:28 compiler: REOPENED for a pack15c host regression, reported by x (x-verify49 on the node: escript undef simframes_ffi:array_new/2). The core names the FFI module after the INPUT stem (spiral_compiler.fs:35230, ffi_module = <input stem>_ffi), but host Program.fs:667-673 copied the sidecar under the OUTPUT stem. So '--backend Gleam a.spi out/b.gleam' wrote out/b_ffi.erl containing -module(a_ffi), which the BEAM can't load (JS has the same mismatch: the import is ./a_ffi.mjs). FIX DRAFTED: %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\spiral\\GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS\\4-ffi-sidecar-keeps-module-name.kitdo, 1 replacement on host 615BAAEDCA3FEC24: copy (and the stale-file delete) to <output dir>/<input stem><suffix>, so the file name equals the -module name and the JS import path. PROOF (box, Debug candidate = live core + host with program 4, A/B with the installed host as control; samples/array_create_reads_zero copied as frames.spi -> proj/src/main.gleam): installed wrote main_ffi.erl (-module(frames_ffi)), gleam run exit 1; candidate wrote frames_ffi.erl/.mjs, gleam run exit 5 = C exit 5. Rides pack15d with BACKEND-PRIMITIVE-PARITY program 3 and CELLS-SERVER-EXIT-TAIL. x's workaround (apps/sim/build.ps1 renames *_ffi.* to their -module name) becomes a no-op after install. Next step: hand pack15d to main with a passing install-pack -Programs dry run.")
inl note_of_gleam_js_target_gaps_43 () = Note ("GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS", 43u32, "2026-11-02T07:00Z", "compiler", "11-02 03:00 compiler: DONE again. pack15d installed by main 02:36 (box SpiralCompiler.dll 9489144496A935E6 / Core F16F6B2032F81F2D, which I verified, both equal to the candidate; node F014BE54FA782B8E; Vultr source build core CB8092C4FEE7A01C). Program 4 (the sidecar keeps the input stem, so the file name equals the -module name) is live; the candidate repro (frames.spi -> main.gleam: frames_ffi.erl, gleam run 5 = C 5) ran on the same bytes. x's apps/sim/build.ps1 rename is now a no-op.")
inl tibia_fidelity_step_timing () = Task ("TIBIA-FIDELITY-STEP-TIMING", "Step duration fidelity (speed, ground speed, diagonal penalty) is still unmeasured: a client-side probe timing player:isWalking() around g_game.walk reported ~10 ms per step with no position change, because OTClient pre-walks locally and the GOD character runs at GM speed 1500 (tibia-local/probe-timing.lua, 2026-10-12); measure with a normal-speed character by timing server-confirmed position updates (onCreaturePositionChange / onWalk events) or from the server side, then replace the simulator's one-tile-per-tick movement with the measured step durations; 2026-10-13 04:24 first server-side measurement (tibia-local/probe-steps-body.lua via run-policy-scenario.ps1 -ProbeBody probe-steps-body.lua, monster onPositionChange timestamps): troll straight steps median 1009 ms (n=34, min 999) while idle-wandering near 1932,1349,7; the model (creatures.spi step_ms, Canary formula on grass ground speed 150) predicts 950 ms, so the local ground is slower (about 155-160) or idle walking adds a cost; not yet measured: chase steps (x2 WALK_TARGET_NEARBY_EXTRA_COST; these trolls did not chase), diagonal x3, fleeing, player steps; next: read the tile's ground speed from the map/items.xml, measure a chase; 2026-10-13 11:56 new lead from the live cyclops arena: the simulator's clear mode (hunting_pack.clear_run, pack_sim cyclops_clear_predictions_for_the_live_arena) predicts rules 88.7 s / 15.6 potions and kiting-at-3 92.7 s / 15.2 per clear (20 seeds); live (2 runs each, forge off) shows rules about 104 s / 15 and kiting about 79 s / 12.5; potions match for rules, but live kiting gains where the simulator predicts none; suspect creature re-approach after a retreat (the simulator steps approaching foes every step_ms x 2) and the live probe's strike-readiness guess; measure cyclops chase steps with probe-steps-body; 2026-10-13 12:05 Canary walking rules read from source (creature.cpp getStepDuration, monster.cpp getDistanceStep): diagonal steps cost x3 for every creature (players too); straight steps cost x2 once a monster has been adjacent to its target (a counter that only rises while adjacent and is never decremented by a melee monster, because getDistanceStep returns early beyond the target distance); before first contact x1; the simulator used x2 for every approach, no diagonal cost for anyone; hunting_pack now uses geometry.step_cost (diagonal_step_factor 3) and a foe met_target flag with chase_step_factor 2 for foes and the player; probe-steps-body has a chase mode (TIBIA_STEPS_CHASE=1) to confirm live; 2026-10-13 12:36 first chase-mode run (arena-logs/cyclops-steps-chase-*.log): cyclops straight steps before contact 701-713 ms (x1 on a ~650 ms base after the 50 ms server-beat rounding, as the new model says; the old model used x2), but the knight never walked away, so post-contact and diagonal steps are still unmeasured; the steps probe logs no server text, so the cause is unknown (next: log walk results and texts); 2026-10-13 18:25 chase steps measured live (probe-steps-body chase mode, one cyclops): after contact 701-715 ms (median 712, n=7), the same as before contact (median 712, n=4), so Canary's target-nearby x2 does not apply to a melee chase in practice; the cyclops base step is ~700-715 ms on the arena ground vs 650 by the grass formula (+8%, the troll measurement showed +6%); the simulator now uses chase_step_factor 1 and terrain.ground_step_percent 108 on ArenaSpot for every step; diagonal x3 still unmeasured (no diagonal chase step occurred); 2026-10-14 02:17 live diagonal steps measured (Canary 3.6.1, cyclops, probe-steps-body.lua with TIBIA_STEPS_CHASE=1): the interval after a monster's diagonal step has median 2109 ms (n=17 pooled over 4 runs; chasing-only 2109, n=9, floor 2100), after a straight chasing step 710 ms (n=79, floor 701): diagonal = 3.0x straight, matching Canary's WALK_DIAGONAL_EXTRA_COST 3 in Creature::getStepDuration (straight steps next to the target cost x2 there, live 1412-1479 ms); probe bug fixed: it labelled each interval by the step that ended it, but the server charges a step's duration before the NEXT step, so the old 'diagonal' intervals were the ~708 ms straight wait before the diagonal (re-paired old logs agree with the fixed probe); diagonals are rare with one chasing cyclops (0 in 3 runs, ~35 steps: Canary's A* (AStarNodes::getMapWalkCost) charges 35 for a diagonal vs 10 per straight step, so a diagonal never beats two straight steps and monsters step diagonally only when the orthogonal routes are blocked or occupied); 3 cyclops crowding the knight (level 100 so it survives 45 s unhealed) gave 1-7 diagonal intervals per run; simulator check: diagonal x3 is right, but its diagonal frequency should follow A* cost (diagonals only around blockers), not geometric shortest paths; 2026-10-14 08:31 real terrain: tibia-local/otbm-walkability.py reads canary.otbm (OTBM node tree) and appearances.dat (protobuf: unpass flag 13, bank flag 1 = ground) and agrees with the live client scan on 251 of 252 cells (the one miss is a ground tile, likely a creature at scan time); terrain.ArenaSpot now covers radius 30 (61x61, two u32 masks per row, a balanced lookup, let-bound so it is not inlined) instead of the client's +-7 window, which the simulator had treated as walls (the simulated knight was boxed in); the plain cavebot clear is unchanged (it never leaves the window), but policies that retreat now have room", TaskKind, P3, D3, 100u32, Done, "TIBIA-OFFSCREEN-PROBES", "tibia,oracle", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_tibia_fidelity_step_timing () = Planning ("TIBIA-FIDELITY-STEP-TIMING", Minutes 180u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl tibia_evolved_policy () = Task ("TIBIA-EVOLVED-POLICY", "First learning loop, apps/tibia/hunt_sim.spi 2026-10-12: a (1,lambda)-ES (evolve_policy: 4 generations x 6 offspring, spread 64 halving, keyed RNG, no arrays) tunes Tuned (heal_below, strike_above) against the Cyclops on training seeds 1..6, scored kills - 50 * deaths; on held-out seeds 21..30 it finds heal 124 / strike 23 with score 8, equal to the hand-tuned baseline ScriptedHealingBelow 120 (8) and far above the random control (-448), Rust and C agree, search plus evaluation runs in ~100 ms; per the arena rule a tie is not a promotion: next, richer policy features (creature health, distance, cooldown state), more offspring/generations, and the graph-code network once arrays exist; user decision 2026-10-12 19:18: after the GPU/test blockers, the learning agent comes first (policy/learning loop on the simulator), before the headless client and fidelity work; 2026-10-13 03:53 under the combat model the (1,lambda)-ES finds the same heal 124 / strike 23 and scores 14 on held-out seeds vs the tuned baseline's 16 (control -443): it now trails, test renamed evolved_policy_trails_tuned_baseline_and_beats_random_control_on_held_out_seeds; 2026-10-13 06:14 superseded by TIBIA-FAIR-CHASING-BASELINE and TIBIA-POLICY-FEATURES: the ES on 20 training seeds ties a cavebot tuned on the same seeds (90 vs 90 held-out)", Feature, P1, D4, 100u32, Done, "TIBIA-SCENARIO-HARDNESS", "tibia,arena,ml", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_tibia_evolved_policy () = Planning ("TIBIA-EVOLVED-POLICY", Minutes 600u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl wgsl_payload_unions () = Task ("WGSL-PAYLOAD-UNIONS", "WGSL step 2 (user 2026-10-12: finish the WGSL backend first so GPU work does not clog): unions with payloads as a u32 tag plus a flattened payload struct (one field group per case, or a shared max-width slot layout), union unbox as a switch on the tag; unblocks apps/tibia vitality typestate and spell groups (hunt, hunt_sim, vitality GPU tests); verify every union sample in the harness on WGSL against the C oracle via spiral-wgsl; 19:52 implemented and verified on the GPU (uncommitted, single-flight Debug 19:50, 0 errors): a union with payloads becomes struct SpiralUnionN { tag: u32, c<case>f<field>: T, ... } (every case's flattened fields; zero-value T() for the others), TyUnionBox builds it with the case's values, TyUnionUnbox tests v.tag per arm (any number of scrutinees) and binds the case's fields; recursive unions raise 'recursive unions (WGSL has no pointers)'; probe tmp/wgsl/payload_union (Circle : i32 | Rect : i32 * i32 | Empty, match + union equality): 2,973-byte shader, spiral-wgsl exit 124, C exit 124; nullary probe still 170/170; remaining: harness sample rows and a hopac build; 20:03: nominal-typed unions (a union passed to a let method arrives as YNominal) now resolve through env.nominal_apply; samples/wgsl_nullary_union (170), samples/wgsl_payload_union (124), gleam_match_single_case (49) and gleam_match_narrowed (61) compile to WGSL and spiral-wgsl returns the C exit for each (single-flight Debug 20:01); all four added to the Wgsl list in tests/matrix.psd1; the unsupported-type error now names the Ty constructor; next: a suite run so the four Wgsl rows enter the oracle, and a hopac build", TaskKind, P0, D5, 100u32, Done, "WGSL-NULLARY-UNIONS", "wgsl,compiler,tibia", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_wgsl_payload_unions () = Planning ("WGSL-PAYLOAD-UNIONS", Minutes 360u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl wgsl_storage_arrays () = Task ("WGSL-STORAGE-ARRAYS", "WGSL step 3: arrays as storage buffers (fixed-size first, then runtime-sized with bindings), so array samples and the ML/arena code (TIBIA-AGENT, TIBIA-2048-ANCHOR) can run on the GPU; harness rows against the C oracle via spiral-wgsl; 20:13 step 1 implemented (uncommitted, single-flight Debug 20:11, 0 errors): arrays whose element is one scalar (i32/u32/f32/bool) live in a module-private heap var<private> spiral_heap: array<u32, 16384> with a bump allocator spiral_array_create (length word, then elements bitcast to u32, never freed; emitted only when an array is created); an array value is its u32 offset; ArrayIndex/ArrayIndexSet/ArrayLength map to heap slots; samples/wgsl_array_scalar (i32 + bool arrays, runtime index, length) spiral-wgsl 135 = C 135 (the first draft read an unset bool slot: C does not zero-fill new arrays, so the sample sets every slot); fixed_array_scalar / runtime_index / runtime_update also run on WGSL and agree with C (exit 0); all four on the Wgsl list; next: multi-word elements (unions, tuples: array_union fails with 'array elements of type SpiralUnion0'), a storage-buffer heap bound by the runner for large data, and bounds checks (naga clamps out-of-range indices today); 20:18 step 2: multi-word elements: an element type flattens to words (a payload union = tag + every case's fields, nested unions recursively, nominals resolved), reads rebuild the struct from its words, writes store each field, spiral_array_create takes a stride; single-flight Debug 20:16 (0 errors): all 9 union/array samples on WGSL match C (wgsl_array_scalar 135, fixed_array_* 0, array_union 0, wgsl_payload_union 124, wgsl_nullary_union 170, gleam_match_narrowed 61, gleam_match_single_case 49); array_union added to the Wgsl list (10 new Wgsl rows tonight); still open: elements made of several values (tuples), a storage-buffer heap for large data, bounds checks; 21:25 step 3, storage buffers: the spiral-wgsl runner (tests/native-shims/wgsl-run/src/main.rs, uncommitted; rebuilt into the toolchains/wgsl-run target dir, previous binary kept at tmp/wgsl/spiral-wgsl.before-heap.exe) binds a zero-initialized heap buffer at binding 1 (SPIRAL_WGSL_HEAP_MB, default 64, capped at the device's max_storage_buffer_binding_size; shaders that don't declare it still run); the compiler now declares @group(0) @binding(1) var<storage, read_write> spiral_heap: array<u32> instead of the 64 KB private array; single-flight Debug 21:23: all 9 union/array samples match C on the new runner; remaining: elements that are several values (tuples) and bounds checks; 21:35 step 4: elements made of several values (tuples): words of each component in sequence; a multi-value read assigns each target (or returns the tuple struct from a tail); samples/wgsl_array_tuple ((i32 * bool) array, runtime index): spiral-wgsl 121 = C 121 (single-flight Debug 21:35); on the Wgsl list; remaining: bounds checks (naga clamps today) and a hopac build; 22:24 BLESSED: candidate single-flight-20261012-214348 reviewed (all existing rows parity; 32 new: 11 Wgsl rows agree with C, new samples' Rust/Delphi agree except wgsl_array_tuple Delphi build-fail -> DELPHI-TUPLE-ARRAYS, 6 FOR-D repros), -Bless run single-flight-20261012-220449 reproduces it row for row (3,825 rows, 0 differences), EXPECTED.tsv 3,898 -> 3,929 rows (+31, 0 removed, 0 changed; the runaway timeout row keeps its entry); pre-bless copy tmp/compiler-main/EXPECTED-before-bless-2.tsv; 01:38 BLESSED pack-0002: -Bless run single-flight-20261013-011643 reproduces the reviewed candidate single-flight-20261013-001436 row for row (3,830 rows, 0 differences); EXPECTED.tsv 3,929 -> 3,935 (+6: runaway_inline_match_recursion C error, wgsl_gadt_payload_union x5; 30 changed: 2 GADT C rows error -> ok exit 0, wgsl_array_tuple Delphi build-fail -> 121, Gleam residuals from the spiral_array_to_list prelude); hopac-20261013-003348 had the identical delta; pre-bless copy tmp/compiler-main/EXPECTED-before-bless-3.tsv; 2026-10-13 23:42 closed in the near-done sweep: the gpt5parallel build it waited on is done (hopac-20261013-003348 had pack-0002's identical delta); bounds checks are not added: array index/update is unchecked on C (the oracle), Kotlin and Swift too, so WGSL clamping is within the same contract and a checked index would be a cross-backend decision, not a WGSL gap", TaskKind, P1, D5, 100u32, Done, "WGSL-PAYLOAD-UNIONS", "wgsl,compiler,ml", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_wgsl_storage_arrays () = Planning ("WGSL-STORAGE-ARRAYS", Minutes 480u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl core_pair_unification () = Task ("CORE-PAIR-UNIFICATION", "Stop changing the compiler twice (user 2026-10-12: edits to hopac/single-flight #if pairs doubled the work; do it in the windows while a build is queued): spiral_compiler.fs has 16 SPIRAL_CORE_HOPAC section pairs plus 6 hopac-only blocks (45,080 lines); measured 18:15 with tmp/unify/pairs.py (non-blank lines, hopac/single/same/differ): WDiffPrepass 128/128/121/7, Prepass 1363/1322/1253/110, Infer 2116/2087/1918/198, BlockParsing 1877/1725/1584/293, WDiff 626/506/486/140, startParentWatcher 17/17/13/4, ServerUtils 391/345/298/93, SpiralHub 62/61/46/16, Graph 103/66/58/45, Utils 36/48/25/23, main 95/52/48/47, new_server 105/109/51/58, CodegenFsharp 1143/496/418/725, SignalRSupervisor 3534/787/476/3058, CodegenAdapter 16/19/1/18, PartEval 97891/2834/1921/95970; done 18:15 (uncommitted, in the queued Debug builds): Tokenize unified (the pair differed only by char 39 vs ''' spellings, let inline (+), and an unused private findOrdinalTextFrom; 754 lines removed, edition-3 symbols now live once); next: the remaining differences are real (WDiffPrepass: TermNameId/TypeNameId vs string keys and Hopac start vs queueIgnore; startParentWatcher: Hopac job vs Async + DiagJson exit), so per AGENTS.md make the shared text single-flight's and move hopac needs into hooks in an earlier hopac-only section (name-id types shared first, then a job-start hook); order by smallest differ count; verify each with both builds + each lane's tests; 18:25: both cores build with the unified Tokenize section (single-flight Debug 18:19, 111 s, 0 errors; hopac Debug 18:25, 376 s, 0 errors; same source also carries edition-3 symbols, WGSL nullary unions and the FOR-D offside fix); suites not yet run on these builds; 20:03 verified by single-flight-20261012-193841 (-Suite all -Native, Release built 19:36 from the source with the unified Tokenize section, edition-3 symbols + comma fix, WGSL nullary unions and the FOR-D offside fix): 3,799 jobs, baseline parity 3,793 / new 6 / missing+regressed 0 / residual-differs 0, native 2,775 ran, 11 build-fail, oracle agree 2,307, DISAGREE 0, 23 min: every existing row is byte-identical to the accepted oracle; 20:06 BlockParsing unification candidate (tmp/unify/unify_section.py: shared text = single-flight's 1,930 lines, hopac's two pure additions opStableText + parsedAst seed (246 lines) moved into a new hopac-only section BlockParsingHopac, 2,040 other hopac lines dropped: its older ho_var/forall_var combinators, OperatorCaseNameId table, missing [<NoComparison>]) builds the hopac core (Debug, -CoreSource) with 0 errors; next: single-flight build + both suites before applying it to the real file; 20:08 BlockParsing unified in the real file (backup tmp/unify/spiral_compiler.before-blockparsing.fs): shared = single-flight's 1,939 lines (incl. both FOR-D offside fixes), hopac's 246-line opStableText + parsedAst seed moved to the hopac-only section BlockParsingHopac; 16 -> 15 pairs, file 189,153 -> 187,106 lines; Release builds + single-flight and hopac -Suite all -Native queued to verify; 20:19: Prepass is not mechanical (hopac keys modules by interned TermNameId/TypeNameId instead of string, adds pattern-ref cycle guards and a ConcurrentDictionary; those types flow into hopac's Infer/PartEval): needs either identity adapters in single-flight (type TermNameId = string, in the existing CodegenAdapter style) or string keys in hopac, a design decision; meanwhile tmp/unify/try-all.ps1 builds the hopac core once per remaining pair with that pair unified mechanically (single-flight text shared, hopac's pure additions moved to a hopac-only section; single-flight unchanged by construction) and records builds/fails + first error, to find every pair that is as mechanical as BlockParsing; 21:16 BlockParsing unification VERIFIED: single-flight-20261012-201914 and hopac-20261012-203857 (-Suite all -Native, Release from the unified source): both keep every existing row at parity (contracts 669, examples 3,069, frontier 50, mega 5; 0 missing/regressed, DISAGREE 0; hopac oracle agree 2,321), 27 new rows (WGSL union rows agree with C; FOR-D repros); sweep so far (hopac Debug builds of mechanical unifications): startParentWatcher, SpiralHub, main BUILD but are server/entry code whose hopac-only lines carry shutdown exit codes and diagnostics that the batch suites never exercise (HOPAC-WARM-SERVER territory), so they are review candidates, not applied; WDiffPrepass fails (needs hopac's PrepassNominal), Graph fails (SchemaPathId, 81 errors), Utils fails (duplicate module, 57 errors); 21:41 sweep results (hopac Debug builds of mechanical unifications, tmp/unify/try/summary.tsv): BUILD with single-flight text: startParentWatcher, SpiralHub, main, new_server (server/entry code; hopac-only shutdown/diagnostic lines the batch suites never exercise: review, not applied); FAIL: WDiffPrepass 12 (PrepassNominal), Graph 81 (SchemaPathId), Utils 57 and CodegenAdapter 70 (modules both cores define elsewhere), WDiff 28 (ConcurrentDictionary vs Dictionary), ServerUtils 43 (GraphPathId vs string), Prepass 22 (ConcurrentDictionary); pattern: the failures are two type families, interned ids (TermNameId/TypeNameId/GraphPathId/SchemaPathId vs string) and concurrent memo tables (ConcurrentDictionary vs Dictionary); plan (next step of HOPAC-WATER-OIL): one per-core adapter pair early in the file that defines those types and their few operations (single-flight: type TermNameId = string with identity Ops, MemoTable = Dictionary; hopac: the real interned ids and ConcurrentDictionary), after which most pairs share one text written against the adapter names; 00:22 classification: CodegenAdapter is a legitimate adapter pair (each side maps its core's PartEval result shapes, join-point cells, key tuples and memo tables onto the interface the shared codegens call), so it stays a pair by design: water-oil's target shape is shared text + per-core adapters like this one; Utils pair unification in a candidate (shared member-constrained get_default/memoize over TryGetValue/TryAdd for Dictionary and ConcurrentDictionary, one shared module Utils, hopac's private copy removed), Debug builds of both cores running; 00:34 Utils candidate (tmp/unify/utils) builds both cores in Debug with 0 errors; rides the next pack after the accept; 01:33 applied to spiral_compiler.fs (uncommitted, Debug builds of both cores 0 errors each, Release suites pending in the next pack): Utils shared; Graph -> GraphIdentity adapter pair + shared Graph; ServerUtils shared; WDiff shared (hopac-only DiagJson parse seeding moved behind observeParsedStatement: BlockParsingHopac became an adapter pair whose single-flight side observes nothing; IdentityAdapter gains termMapToModuleMembers/typeMapToModuleMembers identity converters and covers 7 id families; single-flight HopacExtensions gains sequential A/S array modules); pairs 14 -> 12 (+1 adapter pair); remaining real pairs: Infer, Prepass, PartEval, CodegenFsharp, SignalRSupervisor (3,480-line diff) + by-design adapters (CodegenAdapter, GraphIdentity, BlockParsingHopac); 01:44 startParentWatcher + SpiralHub shared behind a ServerHooks adapter pair (hopac: DiagJson/BigStack exits and diag_save_enabled trace gate; single-flight: Environment.Exit/Kill, trace files on), applied (both cores Debug 0 errors); main classified as a by-design entry pair (hopac's entry boots Progress88, BigStack terminal flow and the console HUD: the edge where hopac belongs); new_server candidate building (delivery ledger note behind ServerHooks.noteClientErrorsDelivered); 03:12 pack-0003 VERIFIED on both cores: single-flight-20261013-020515 and hopac-20261013-022459 each 3,822 parity + 8 Gleam residual rows (string prelude panic messages), DISAGREE 0: the Utils/Graph/ServerUtils/WDiff/ServerHooks/startParentWatcher/SpiralHub/new_server unification is behavior-neutral; pairs 14 -> 10 (by-design adapters: BlockParsingHopac, CodegenAdapter, GraphIdentity, ServerHooks, main; real pairs left: Infer, Prepass, PartEval, CodegenFsharp, SignalRSupervisor); 03:21 CodegenFsharp analysed (not applied): hopac text as shared fails single-flight with 94 errors; single-flight text as shared fails hopac with only 14: 4 id comparisons (union tag names via UnionTagIdOps.text, layout keys via LayoutFieldNameIdOps.matchesText, both exist in both cores once the CodegenAdapter pair moves above CodegenFsharp) and the method/closure join-point lookups; hopac's lookups are ~300 lines of fail-closed recovery each (EvalWorklist replay drain, sequential-authority retry, owner-missing aliasing), so the plan is: shared text = single-flight's CodegenFsharp, and the per-core lookup becomes an adapter hook codegenFsharpMethodBody/codegenFsharpClosureBody (hopac side = its current recovery block extracted verbatim, single-flight side = the plain dictionary read, no DeadPureLetElision so F# output stays byte-identical); then Infer/Prepass/PartEval/SignalRSupervisor remain; 2026-10-13 23:34 user decision: stays P0 while gpt5parallel lives (every shared-section change still costs double)", Feature, P0, D6, 100u32, Done, "PACK-10", "compiler,hopac,maintenance,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_core_pair_unification () = Planning ("CORE-PAIR-UNIFICATION", Minutes 1800u32, Load Heavy, Assigned Pair)
inl note_of_core_pair_unification_1 () = Note ("CORE-PAIR-UNIFICATION", 1u32, "2026-10-14T11:38Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-14 07:38 touch: 16 #if SPIRAL_CORE_GPT5PARALLEL blocks remain in spiral_compiler.fs; with gpt5parallel rungs dropped its pairs only need to keep compiling, so next unification is CodegenFsharp per the 03:21 plan")
inl note_of_core_pair_unification_2 () = Note ("CORE-PAIR-UNIFICATION", 2u32, "2026-10-14T21:24Z", "main-thread", "17:24 CodegenFsharp pair mapped (job tmp/rotation/cf.diff, single-flight vs gpt5parallel side, 54 hunks; gpt5parallel has 789 lines single-flight lacks, single-flight 97). (1) ~20 hunks are whitespace only (trailing blank lines). (2) ~25 mechanical hunks share directly with one rule: keep single-flight's text but gpt5parallel's id calls (UnionTagIdOps.text, LayoutFieldNameIdOps.matchesText; the single-flight CodegenAdapter versions are identity/equality, so move CodegenAdapter above CodegenFsharp); HopacExtensions.S.map -> Array.map; the memo tables use one TryGetValue/TryAdd shape on codegenMemoTable (Dictionary has TryAdd), with tag = dict.Count (gpt5parallel's F# emission is already sequential); FastRuntimeFormat vs sprintf: either is fine. (3) One ~300-line hunk is gpt5parallel's method/closure body lookup with recovery; it moves verbatim into hooks codegenFsharpMethodBody/codegenFsharpClosureBody in the CodegenAdapter pair. Gate: both cores build, and the single-flight -Suite all F# rows are residual-identical. Estimated 2-3 h; not started today (window too short to verify both cores).")
inl note_of_core_pair_unification_3 () = Note ("CORE-PAIR-UNIFICATION", 3u32, "2026-10-15T01:15Z", "main-thread", "21:15 NEXT SESSION #4 (P0): CodegenFsharp per the 54-hunk map (job tmp/rotation/cf.diff): shared single-flight text plus gpt5parallel id calls, recovery lookups as CodegenAdapter hooks. Gate: both cores build and the F# rows are residual-identical. 2-3 h.")
inl note_of_core_pair_unification_4 () = Note ("CORE-PAIR-UNIFICATION", 4u32, "2026-10-15T04:49Z", "main-thread", "00:49 CodegenFsharp pair unified (tmp/rotation/cf-unify/unify_codegen_fsharp.ps1, 15 #if pairs left, core 186,572 -> 185,995 lines). Moved the CodegenAdapter pair above a shared CodegenFsharp; gpt5parallel's body-cell waits live in its adapter as codegenFsharpMethodBody/codegenFsharpClosureBody, single-flight's are dictionary reads. Shipping in pack-1g: review single-flight-20261015-002606 is identical to the pack-1f oracle on every cell (4,143 rows) and both cores build. gpt5parallel F#-only cross-core check running.")
inl note_of_core_pair_unification_5 () = Note ("CORE-PAIR-UNIFICATION", 5u32, "2026-10-15T05:14Z", "main-thread", "01:14 gpt5parallel side checked: F#-only suite gpt5parallel-20261015-005048 on the 1g build, 959 rows, all parity, 0 missing. Cross-core vs single-flight review 002606: 958/959 identical. The one difference, wgsl_record_loop (gpt keeps a let-bound struct around a self tail call), already exists in the gpt5parallel runs of 10-13 and 10-14 02:01. Versus the 10-14 02:01 gpt run: 6 differences, 3 load timeouts/errors there, and 3 string rows (multiline_triple_string, string_eq, string_utf8) from pack-1d's F# UTF-8 strings that now match single-flight. So the unification changes no output on either core.")
inl note_of_core_pair_unification_6 () = Note ("CORE-PAIR-UNIFICATION", 6u32, "2026-10-15T06:15Z", "main-thread", "02:15 CodegenFsharp unification installed in pack-1g and blessed: single-flight-20261015-015332 = review 002606 on all 4,143 rows, DISAGREE 0. Pairs left: 15. Next pair candidates: other codegen sections whose differences are only body-cell waits (same adapter-hook pattern).")
inl note_of_core_pair_unification_7 () = Note ("CORE-PAIR-UNIFICATION", 7u32, "2026-10-15T07:06Z", "main-thread", "03:06 remaining 15 pairs sized after pack-1g (lines differing between sides): ServerHooks 11, GraphIdentity 40, CodegenAdapter 677 and BlockParsingGpt5parallel 255 are hook layers by design (per-core adapters), not duplication. Real duplication: main 64, Prepass 237, Infer 456 (2,464 vs 2,322 lines), SignalRSupervisor 3,945, HopacExtensions. PartEval and the Hopac* sections are gpt-only bodies against 2-line single-flight stubs. Next targets in order: main (64), Prepass (237), Infer (456), each with the CodegenFsharp pattern (shared single-flight text plus gpt hooks in an earlier gpt-only section) and an identity proof on both cores.")
inl note_of_core_pair_unification_8 () = Note ("CORE-PAIR-UNIFICATION", 8u32, "2026-10-15T07:45Z", "main-thread", "03:45 main pair unified (candidate pack-1h): shared single-flight main plus 8 hooks in the ServerHooks pair (mainTraceLevel, mainEntered, mainBeforeArguments, mainAnnounceServer, mainBeforeWebHost, mainBeforeRun, mainHostExitCode, mainRun); the gpt bootstrap sentinels move into its ServerHooks side. Both cores build; review single-flight-20261015-032242 = pack-1g oracle on every cell. Install after the renewal window.")
inl note_of_core_pair_unification_9 () = Note ("CORE-PAIR-UNIFICATION", 9u32, "2026-10-15T08:35Z", "main-thread", "04:35 main pair unification installed as pack-1h and blessed (single-flight-20261015-041405 = review 032242 on every cell, DISAGREE 0). Pairs left: 14. Next: Prepass (237 differing lines), then Infer (456).")
inl note_of_core_pair_unification_10 () = Note ("CORE-PAIR-UNIFICATION", 10u32, "2026-10-15T09:02Z", "main-thread", "05:02 Prepass pair mapped (99 hunks ignoring whitespace; sides extracted to the job's tmp/rotation/prepass-pair). Plan: (1) Typed names: gpt uses TermNameId/TypeNameId with TermNameIdOps/TypeNameIdOps; single-flight already has identity aliases in IdentityAdapter (line ~6150), so gpt's text is identity on single-flight. (2) Pattern-ref memoization: gpt caches EPatternRef/TPatternRef by reference in eval, propagate, resolve and lower. In lower that cache ignores env, so it is a behaviour change for single-flight. Make it a hook (single-flight: direct call) in an earlier adapter pair, not shared text. (3) Dictionary vs ConcurrentDictionary in propagate/resolve/lower/typecase metavars: adopt ConcurrentDictionary (single-threaded use, same results) or a type hook. (4) HopacExtensions.A.map vs Array.map: hook. (5) Single-flight's cross-assembly factories (nominal_prepass, resolve_env_value, propagated_vars_env_with_vars): keep in shared text if referenced outside Prepass, else use gpt's inline records. Not started tonight: about 2 h plus a review and bless, which does not fit before 07:00 with the Tibia suite gate at 06:00.")
inl note_of_core_pair_unification_11 () = Note ("CORE-PAIR-UNIFICATION", 11u32, "2026-10-15T09:49Z", "main-thread", "05:49 NEXT SESSION #1: Prepass pair per the 05:02 mapping (pattern-ref memo as a hook, typed names via IdentityAdapter), then Infer (456 lines). Same proof as packs 1g/1h: both cores build, review equals the oracle on every cell, bless reproduces it.")
inl note_of_core_pair_unification_12 () = Note ("CORE-PAIR-UNIFICATION", 12u32, "2026-10-15T11:23Z", "main-thread", "07:23 Prepass unification written (tmp/rotation/prepass-unify/unify_prepass.ps1): shared = gpt5parallel's text with the 7 pattern-ref memo sites behind a new PrepassAdapter pair (single-flight: direct evaluation, gpt5parallel: memo/visit-once), the FError arm as a guarded hook arm (FilledTop has no FError in single-flight), and the PrepassNominal alias kept for single-flight PartEval; single-flight's split-assembly factories dropped (only used inside Prepass). Core 185,973 -> 184,569 lines on top of pack-1i; both-core build queued. Infer mapped next: 367 changed lines, 144 typed-ID, 223 structural (error cases carrying typed IDs, module-member conversions moduleMembersToTermMap/termMapToModuleMembers, a depth-limited type printer at 96, named vs anonymous NominalAux/PrototypeDef records, lit moved out). Estimated 3-4 h.")
inl note_of_core_pair_unification_13 () = Note ("CORE-PAIR-UNIFICATION", 13u32, "2026-10-15T12:18Z", "main-thread", "08:18 Infer mapped in full (diff in the job's tmp/rotation/infer-pair). Plan: shared = gpt5parallel's text (typed IDs, module-member helpers, depth-limited printer, ConcurrentDictionary are neutral or identity on single-flight) plus an InferAdapter pair with hooks for the error-path behaviour differences, so single-flight stays oracle-identical: (1) error rendering: single-flight's messages are what ExpectedErrors match (gpt5parallel already shows WRONG-ERROR on missing_core_negative); (2) ModuleMustBeImmediatelyApplied, reported by single-flight but commented out in gpt5parallel; (3) failed declarations: Promise failure (single-flight) vs FError recovery (gpt5parallel); (4) unused-type-variable list sorted in gpt5parallel; (5) CompilerError payload: string vs OperationalReasonId (type alias hook). Outside Infer only nominals_aux is shared, with structurally identical types. Order: after pack-1j; then PACKAGE-DEFAULTS-PER-PACKAGE on the unified Infer.")
inl note_of_core_pair_unification_14 () = Note ("CORE-PAIR-UNIFICATION", 14u32, "2026-10-15T13:05Z", "main-thread", "09:05 Infer unified (tmp/rotation/infer-unify/unify_infer.ps1): shared = gpt5parallel's text, an InferAdapter pair with 4 hooks (CompilerError payload type, its rendering, the module-index application check that gpt5parallel had commented out, failed declarations as failed promise vs FError recovery), FastRuntimeFormat replaced by sprintf (gpt-only module), and single-flight IdentityAdapter's identity termMapToModuleMembers/typeMapToModuleMembers dropped (the shared ones subsume them). On top of Prepass unification + pack-1i: both cores build (single-flight 202 s, gpt5parallel 599 s); core 182,256 lines, 13 pairs left. Error messages now use gpt5parallel's more precise text (e.g. Unbound term variable), so some ExpectedErrors entries will change; dry review running.")
inl note_of_core_pair_unification_15 () = Note ("CORE-PAIR-UNIFICATION", 15u32, "2026-10-15T14:06Z", "main-thread", "10:06 Prepass + Infer unifications reviewed in pack-1j (single-flight-20261015-094241): no residual change outside the intended Bend/Lua/new-backend rows; the only behaviour change is Infer's more precise error text (one ExpectedErrors entry updated to 'Unbound term variable: +.'). Pairs 15 -> 13. Install after the 12:45 Kino corpus check.")
inl note_of_core_pair_unification_16 () = Note ("CORE-PAIR-UNIFICATION", 16u32, "2026-10-15T15:49Z", "main-thread", "11:49 gpt5parallel side of the Prepass + Infer unifications checked: F#-only suite on the pack-1k gpt5parallel build (pack-c), 963 rows, 0 missing; cross-core vs single-flight review 105101: 962/963 identical, the one difference is the pre-existing wgsl_record_loop (since 10-13). Both cores are output-stable through the unification.")
inl note_of_core_pair_unification_17 () = Note ("CORE-PAIR-UNIFICATION", 17u32, "2026-10-15T18:26Z", "main-thread", "14:26 Prepass + Infer unifications installed in pack-1k (bless 140535). 13 pairs left; the rest are hook layers, gpt-only bodies, or SignalRSupervisor/PartEval (deep divergence that hopacsota replaces).")
inl note_of_core_pair_unification_18 () = Note ("CORE-PAIR-UNIFICATION", 18u32, "2026-10-16T11:36Z", "main-thread", "07:36 main: recount with pair-drift.ps1's parser on the pack-1s tree: 8 core pairs, not 13. By design: BlockParsingGpt5parallel (layer, 256/3), InferAdapter (7/7), PrepassAdapter (13/6), CodegenAdapter (654/29), ServerHooks (63/14). The core difference itself: PartEval (104,779 gpt5parallel lines vs 2,968). Real candidates: GraphIdentity (37/21; gpt5parallel interns path ids, single-flight uses strings: unifying on interned ids changes single-flight Map key order, so it needs an output-order proof, not a mechanical move) and SignalRSupervisor (3,737/830). The item is near its floor; the next step is SignalRSupervisor's diff, then a decision on GraphIdentity.")
inl note_of_core_pair_unification_19 () = Note ("CORE-PAIR-UNIFICATION", 19u32, "2026-10-16T11:38Z", "main-thread", "07:38 main: SignalRSupervisor compared by top-level definitions: 23 shared names, 60 gpt5parallel-only (its parallel build orchestration), 11 single-flight-only. Unifying it means hooks for the gpt5parallel build machinery, which hopacsota (restarted from single-flight's evaluator) is replacing; decision: no further unification work on this lane; the remaining pairs retire with gpt5parallel. Item left Active at its floor for the record; hopacsota's G6 seam gate keeps single-flight changes seam-only.")
inl note_of_core_pair_unification_20 () = Note ("CORE-PAIR-UNIFICATION", 20u32, "2026-10-17T10:02Z", "main-thread", "06:02 main-thread night measurement (tree pack-1z, 186,430 lines): 9 two-sided pairs (PartEval 104,778 gpt5parallel vs 2,976 single-flight; SignalRSupervisor 3,736/829; CodegenAdapter 653/28; BlockParsingGpt5parallel 255/2; PartEvalSeamsHopacsota 131/19; GraphIdentity 36/20; ServerHooks 62/13; PrepassAdapter 12/5; InferAdapter 6/6) plus ~45k one-sided gpt5parallel lines. Single-flight is ~31.7k lines, gpt5parallel ~182k; edits happen twice only inside those 9 pairs.")
