inl ci_compiler_full_suite () = Task ("CI-COMPILER-FULL-SUITE", "Run the compiler's full suite (-Suite all -Native, both lanes as needed) in CI on every push (user decision 2026-10-12 19:09); today it takes ~35-70 min locally; needs the native toolchains on the runners (gcc, rustc, zig, lean, bun, gleam, swift, kotlin, wasmtime, spiral-wgsl with a software adapter or no-toolchain rows), the oracle EXPECTED.tsv available to CI without committing generated results to the tree, and a per-job time budget under GitHub's 6 h limit", TaskKind, P1, D5, 40u32, Blocked, "PIPELINES-GREEN", "ci,team:compiler,harness,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_ci_compiler_full_suite () = Planning ("CI-COMPILER-FULL-SUITE", Minutes 480u32, Load Heavy, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_ci_compiler_full_suite_1 () = Note ("CI-COMPILER-FULL-SUITE", 1u32, "2026-10-14T11:39Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-14 07:39 touch: proposal from today's numbers: CI runs -Suite fast (600 s budget, 671 rows, all 130 flip histories) on every push and -Suite all -Native nightly (16 min quiet, 32-51 min loaded locally); the oracle stays cache-only, fetched as a CI artifact from the last bless")
inl note_of_ci_compiler_full_suite_2 () = Note ("CI-COMPILER-FULL-SUITE", 2u32, "2026-10-14T21:50Z", "main-thread", "17:50 design (not yet in .github; it needs a CI dry run, so the YAML must not land untested and turn every push red). (1) Nightly job (schedule + workflow_dispatch): provision toolchains (scripts/init.ps1 at the repo root, install-dotnet.ps1, setup-rust, setup-zig, setup-beam with gleam, bun, python, swift/kotlin/lean where the runner has them, wgsl via the fallback adapter or no-toolchain rows), build.ps1 -Mode single-flight, test.ps1 -Suite all -Native -Bless in the CI cache. Upload <cache>/runs/<last> plus baseline/EXPECTED.tsv as artifact 'compiler-oracle'. The CI oracle is the previous nightly, separate from the local one, so nothing is committed. (2) Push job: download the latest 'compiler-oracle' artifact into the cache (it gives the baseline and the fast-tier history), build, test.ps1 -Suite fast -Native; fail on DISAGREE/REGRESSED. Without that history -Suite fast runs every row (rows missing from the last full run always run), so the artifact is what keeps push CI near 10 min. (3) Time: full ~16-50 min locally, about 2x on runners, under 6 h. Next: write .github/workflows/compiler.yml and do one workflow_dispatch dry run before enabling the push trigger.")
inl note_of_ci_compiler_full_suite_3 () = Note ("CI-COMPILER-FULL-SUITE", 3u32, "2026-10-14T21:51Z", "main-thread", "17:51 .github/workflows/compiler.yml written (uncommitted): schedule 06:17 UTC + workflow_dispatch (input suite), ubuntu-24.04, setup-beam/bun/python, scripts/init.ps1, build.ps1 -Mode single-flight, test.ps1 -Suite all -Native -Parallel 2 -Bless into SPIRAL_BIN_CACHE_DIR beside the workspace, uploads 'compiler-oracle'. No push trigger: until a dispatch run passes it cannot turn push CI red. Untested (no local Actions runner); toolchains init.ps1 does not provide (zig, lean, swift, kotlin, wasmtime, wgsl adapter) will show as no-toolchain rows, which is acceptable for a first run.")
inl note_of_ci_compiler_full_suite_4 () = Note ("CI-COMPILER-FULL-SUITE", 4u32, "2026-10-15T01:15Z", "main-thread", "21:15 NEXT SESSION #5, after the user commits: one workflow_dispatch run of compiler.yml, then the push -Suite fast job seeded from its artifact.")
inl note_of_ci_compiler_full_suite_5 () = Note ("CI-COMPILER-FULL-SUITE", 5u32, "2026-10-15T09:49Z", "main-thread", "05:49 NEXT SESSION #4 (user): commit and push .github/workflows/compiler.yml, then run one workflow_dispatch; a push-triggered fast job comes after that run is green.")
inl note_of_ci_compiler_full_suite_6 () = Note ("CI-COMPILER-FULL-SUITE", 6u32, "2026-10-17T10:02Z", "main-thread", "06:02 main-thread night: the 10-16 scheduled compiler run failed twice over: workflow.ps1 -Compiler saw a .NET SDK in its check subprocess so install-dotnet.ps1 never ran, then build.ps1 threw No .NET 11 SDK found; and upload-artifact rejected the .. path. Fixed in the tree: workflow.ps1 resolves once, installs only when empty, exports SPIRAL_DOTNET and logs dotnet: path; the cache dir moved under RUNNER_TEMP via GITHUB_ENV. Needs one workflow_dispatch after the push.")
inl note_of_ci_compiler_full_suite_7 () = Note ("CI-COMPILER-FULL-SUITE", 7u32, "2026-10-17T15:57Z", "main-thread", "11:57 main-thread design update from today's native memo: the per-push job can be the FULL suite, not -Suite fast. The nightly bless (it must run every native row) leaves <cache>/native-memo filled, and an actions/cache save keyed by runner OS + hashFiles of the native shims and test.ps1 can persist it. A push job restores it and runs test.ps1 -Suite all -Native -ReuseNative, so it pays only the compile phase (~4 min locally) plus native runs for rows whose output bytes changed (Zig-only change today: 457 rows, 91 s). Not wired yet: the nightly job has never run green since the 10-16 fixes (needs the user's push and one workflow_dispatch). Adding actions/cache also needs a 'gh actions-lock' regeneration of .github/workflows/actions.lock (machine-generated, CI-ACTIONS-LOCKFILE).")
inl note_of_ci_compiler_full_suite_8 () = Note ("CI-COMPILER-FULL-SUITE", 8u32, "2026-10-17T16:42Z", "main-thread", "12:42 main-thread: with -ReuseCompile in the harness, a push that touches neither the compiler nor any Spiral source replays the whole suite in about 1 min locally, if the CI cache restores <cache>/compile-memo and native-memo (same OS, same toolchains). That makes '-Suite all on every push' affordable: a docs- or CI-only push costs about 1 min, and a compiler change costs the compile phase plus the natives whose output changed.")
inl note_of_ci_compiler_full_suite_9 () = Note ("CI-COMPILER-FULL-SUITE", 9u32, "2026-10-18T12:25Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 08:25 main-thread: the scheduled compiler job of 10-18 (run 39121783429, adffe65a) got furthest yet: install, build (71.5 s), formatter report (1,232 files would change, 48 over 120), compile phase of all 6,944 jobs (164 s), gleam batch, and then died entering the native tier on test.ps1's kernel32 SetErrorMode (Linux). Fixed in the tree (guarded by IsWindows); the next push plus a workflow_dispatch should reach the first green nightly bless. Budget note: FSHARP-NATIVE-TIER (in the tree) adds about 540 dotnet fsi runs to every bless (37 min at 2 lanes locally), well inside the 360 min job limit; push jobs replay them from the native memo.")
inl note_of_ci_compiler_full_suite_10 () = Note ("CI-COMPILER-FULL-SUITE", 10u32, "2026-10-19T12:25Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 08:25 lap 1 grain: the SetErrorMode fix is in pushed HEAD 668568ac (guarded by if IsWindows in test.ps1), so the next scheduled compiler job can pass the native tier; no scheduled run fired on 2026-10-19 as of 12:2xZ (last one 39121783429 on 10-18 11:04Z); not dispatching by hand because workflow_dispatch also runs the deploy job Next: read the 10-20 scheduled run: native tier timing and whether it reaches the accept step")
inl note_of_ci_compiler_full_suite_11 () = Note ("CI-COMPILER-FULL-SUITE", 11u32, "2026-10-19T13:53Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 09:53 lap 2 grain: no scheduled compiler run fired on 10-19 (cron 17 6 * * * UTC; GitHub skipped or delayed it), so the SetErrorMode fix in HEAD has not been exercised; the per-backend native keys (test.ps1, uncommitted) will also cut CI's native replay cost once pushed Next: read the 10-20 scheduled run")
inl note_of_ci_compiler_full_suite_12 () = Note ("CI-COMPILER-FULL-SUITE", 12u32, "2026-10-19T18:27Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 14:27 lap 4 grain: the scheduled compiler job did run on 10-19 (run 39258592236, 13:15, HEAD 668568ac) and failed on exactly one row: native 3370 ran, 12 build-fail, oracle agree 2645, DISAGREE 1 = samples/zig_exit_wide [Fsharp] (main returns 305): every spawned backend on Linux exits 49 (305 mod 256), but the warm F# runner reports the raw 305 from its in-process end marker, which only matches on Windows (32-bit exit codes); fix staged as lab/PIPELINES-GREEN/fsharp-warm-exit.py (masks a warm exit with -band 255 when not $IsWindows, anchor matches once, dry run clean); it edits the native tier block, so it rides the next compiler pack (all native memo keys change once) Next: apply with pack 9, then read the next scheduled run")
inl note_of_ci_compiler_full_suite_13 () = Note ("CI-COMPILER-FULL-SUITE", 13u32, "2026-10-21T23:26Z", "main", "19:26 read the scheduled runs (the gh-pages.yml compiler job runs only on schedule or dispatch): 10-19 09:15, 10-20 07:36 and 10-21 07:49 all failed on the same single row, samples/zig_exit_wide [Fsharp] new DISAGREE (oracle agree 2,645-2,790, DISAGREE 1, nothing else). Cause and fix in PIPELINES-GREEN note 16: the warm F# tier's Linux 8-bit exit mask never ran because warm.ps1 returns Exit as a string; test.ps1 now masks any numeric string off Windows (uncommitted, user to commit). Expectation: the first scheduled run after the push is green; that run is this item's confirmation.")
inl note_of_ci_compiler_full_suite_14 () = Note ("CI-COMPILER-FULL-SUITE", 14u32, "2026-10-28T01:33Z", "main", "10-27 21:33 main: Groom 10-27 21:2x (WIP rule, set before applying). main's Active items with no note since 10-24 were classed by the evidence in the tree, not by age alone. (1) SHIPPED -> Done: the item's pass is in the installed tree core (apps/compiler/spiral_compiler.fs, core 8660C34A11F33259, the installed 5m' core), found by its own identifier or anchor, and an accept has passed on that core since (accept-6, run single-flight-20261027-173417, 0 blocking cells). (2) COLD -> Paused: no lane is driving it this week and nothing is queued. Its last note keeps the next step, and it resumes when a lane picks it or its condition fires. (3) KEPT Active: a lane is on it this week, or demand is waiting on it. (4) Blocked: only the user's commit or push unblocks it.\n Verdict: (4) Blocked on the user's commit. The single red row's fix (warm.ps1 exit mask, PIPELINES-GREEN n16) is in the tree, and the scheduled runs build from HEAD.")
inl note_of_ci_compiler_full_suite_15 () = Note ("CI-COMPILER-FULL-SUITE", 15u32, "2026-11-02T14:35Z", "main", "main 11-02: parent epic is PIPELINES-GREEN; recorded here instead of as a dep (sole dep on an unfinished parent epic hid it from 'agile next'; AGILE-EPIC-DEP-LINT).")
inl note_of_ci_compiler_full_suite_16 () = Note ("CI-COMPILER-FULL-SUITE", 16u32, "2026-11-02T14:45Z", "main", "main 11-02 CORRECTION to the previous note: dep on PIPELINES-GREEN RESTORED. It may be a real prerequisite (this item needs the epic's result), not just its parent, so it stays until its owner decides. Cleared parent-only deps hide items from 'agile next'; real prerequisites must stay.")
inl x_compat_check () = Task ("X-COMPAT-CHECK", "X compatibility check for edition-3 sources (user decision 2026-10-12 19:14, spiral3 prioritizes being X compatible): the compiler/Kino fails or warns on anything X rewrites when posted: '#' before a letter (hashtag), '@' before a name (mention), '$' before letters (cashtag; Spiral's \\$\"...\" is fine), autolink-looking text such as x.io / cell.ai in record access (shortened to 23-char t.co links), plus a per-post weight <= 280 using the twitter-text v3 weights already in apps/xsim/lib/xsim/frames.ex (CJK/Hangul count 2); the edition-3 symbol table itself is clean (no # @ \\$-letter tokens); 00:14 checker in (uncommitted): apps/xsim lib/xsim/x_compat.ex (Xsim.XCompat.check/2 -> hashtag/mention/cashtag/autolink findings with line+column and an over_limit finding per post whose X weight > 280; x_weight counts each autolink as 23; autolinks follow twitter-text: IANA TLD (apps/xsim/priv/tlds.txt, IANA 2026101200, 1286 entries without xn--), not after - _ . / @, 2-letter ccTLD only when the label before it is longer than 2, t.co special), mix xsim.x_compat FILE... (exit 1 on findings); test/x_compat_test.exs 7/7; note: record access like listm.map / x.data / s.run / a.name would be linked by X (map, data, run, name, new, one, zip are TLDs); 00:19 survey (rewrite findings): edition-3 corpus 834 files, 40 with findings: autolink 37 (record access: .map 16, .run 6, .id 5, .seven 3, .md 2, .next 2), cashtag 16 (\\/\\/\\ inside macro text), mention 1; lib/spiral 194 files, 42 with findings: autolink 259 (.map 153, .run 20, .to 20, .box 16, .date 9, .now 7, .store 5), hashtag 50, cashtag 24, mention 18 (@dim in am'.spi, @compileError in backend.spi Zig macros); also fixed Xsim.Frames.split: threads were chunked by plain weight, so every post was filled to 279 and any autolink pushed it past 280 once shortened; split now chunks X-weighted units (XCompat.segments keeps each autolink whole at weight 23), xsim tests 23/23; design question for the user: record access like listm.map is what X links most, so edition-3 X-compat needs either a different accessor spelling or posting through a code-block/image path; 02:44 splitter fixed twice more (apps/xsim/lib/xsim/frames.ex, uncommitted): the post budget assumed a 99/99 header, so threads of 100+ posts (the mega samples reach 2,492) overflowed by the extra header digits; the budget now uses the real post count (re-split when the digit count grows); and cuts move back to the last whitespace within 60 units, so a post never ends inside a word (cutting state.mapping to state.map minted a new autolink); xsim tests 24/24; survey now: over_limit 0 everywhere; edition-3 corpus 28 files with rewrite findings (autolink 37, cashtag 16, mention 1), lib 42 files (autolink 259, hashtag 50, cashtag 24, mention 18); remaining work: decide the edition-3 answer to record-access autolinks (.map/.run/.to/.box) and wire the check into Kino/compiler for edition-3 sources; user decision 2026-10-13 07:01: record access stays a.b in edition 3; the X-compat check only warns (no hard error, no TLD-key lint); posting tools wrap code (image/code block) instead; remaining: wire the warning into Kino/compiler for edition-3 sources; user decision 2026-10-13 07:14: surface X-compat warnings everywhere: Kino cell output (next to each edition-3 cell's result), compiler diagnostics (coded warnings editors underline), the posting tool (xsim/X path, right before posting) and a CI report (summary per repo, never failing the build); 2026-10-13 10:03 Kino lane: checker moved to apps/kino (Spiral.Kino.XCompat + priv/tlds.txt; weights too), xsim's Xsim.XCompat keeps check/over_limit and delegates the rest, Xsim.Frames.weight delegates (no cycle: xsim depends on kino); Kino prints x-compat warning lines when the nearest package.spiproj says edition: 3 or --x-compat is passed (never changes the result); CI report: mix spiral.x_compat <file|dir>... always exit 0; tests: kino x_compat_test 3/3, xsim x_compat_test 9/9; README; 2026-10-13 12:25 Kino surfaces done (warnings for edition: 3 packages, mix spiral.x_compat CI report); 2026-10-13 22:59 the spiral-split gear splitter (scripts/gear-dev.ps1, bench-split.ps1, build-splitter.ps1, tmp/splitter, lanes/splitter) was removed at the user's request: idle since early October (234 runs Sep 26-Oct 1, then 21), built for gpt5parallel's 159k lines; use build.ps1 -Configuration Debug -CoreSource <copy> for candidates; 2026-10-13 22:59 correction: the previous note about removing the spiral-split gear splitter does not apply to this item; here 'splitter' means xsim's X-thread splitter (apps/xsim/lib/xsim/frames.ex), which is unchanged", TaskKind, P1, D4, 100u32, Done, "SPIRAL3-EDITIONS", "edition,x,lint", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_x_compat_check () = Planning ("X-COMPAT-CHECK", Minutes 300u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl package_default_numerics () = Task ("PACKAGE-DEFAULT-NUMERICS", "Default numeric types per package (user decision 2026-10-12 19:14): keep i32/f64 as the defaults; package.spiproj may declare default-int / default-float (same values as the CLI's --default-int/--default-float), so mrakgr's ML packages opt into i32/f32 without flags; the host passes the package's choice to startupParse like SPIRAL_DEFAULT_INT/FLOAT", TaskKind, P2, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "compiler,packages", "current", "current")
