inl typecheck_error_codes () = Task ("TYPECHECK-ERROR-CODES", "Give type-checker and package errors stable codes (found by the Kino lane 19:52 while landing TEST-EXPECT-DIAGNOSTIC): 'Unification failure', 'Unbound variable' and the 'FatalError: File main has a type error somewhere in its path.' wrapper print no EJP code, only internal/build diagnostics carry EJPxxxx; assign codes per error kind in both cores' Infer (shared where the pair allows), print them in the rustc-style report, so must-not-compile tests can pin the code (user decision: code exact + message substring); 22:20 done (uncommitted): new shared section TypeErrorCodes before the Infer pair: type_error_codes maps all 74 TypeError cases (identical case names in both cores; single-flight declaration order) to ETY0001..ETY0074 (e.g. KindError ETY0001, UnboundVariable ETY0005, TermError/Unification failure ETY0009) and type_error_report prefixes the code (case name via FSharpValue.GetUnionFields, unknown -> ETY0000); both cores' single call site uses it (Infer pair touched on both sides, lint passes); both cores build (Debug 22:08 / 22:17); verified: 'inl main () : i32 = true' reports main.spi:1:21: ETY0009: Unification failure; the oracle stores only statuses for error rows, so suites are unaffected; Kino fails tests can now pin ETY codes (matcher \\bE[A-Z]{2,4}\\d{4}\\b); 22:58 single-flight Release suite at parity with the codes (single-flight-20261012-223610)", TaskKind, P1, D4, 100u32, Done, "", "compiler,diagnostics,tests", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_typecheck_error_codes () = Planning ("TYPECHECK-ERROR-CODES", Minutes 300u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl alphabet_readme_hangul_pdf () = Task ("ALPHABET-README-HANGUL-PDF", "README.hangul.md.pdf is never produced in alphabet apps/documents (pre-existing, same under xelatex and tectonic): LaTeX stops with \"There's no line here to end\" at a generated \\\\ line break; find which crowbook markdown construct emits a bare \\\\ (likely an empty line or a hard break in the Hangul README) and fix the source or the template; 2026-10-14 03:08 lane 1: cause: documents.spi hangul interleaves every non-empty line with its hangulize transliteration, fence lines included, so a code block became the fence pair '`포웨르제우' + '`powershell' and each original code line twice; the stray fences shift which lines are code, one block starts with an empty line, crowbook emits {\\sloppy \\\\{} and LaTeX stops (result.tex:289 There's no line here to end), on xelatex and tectonic alike; fix (uncommitted, apps/documents/documents.spi hangul fold): fence lines and lines inside a fenced block are copied once, untransliterated (the hangulize output index still advances, so the following lines stay aligned); proof: the fixed binary (compiled from the .spi into a scratch crate) turns alphabet README.md into README.hangul.md.pdf (65124 B, tectonic, 53 s for the whole sample), and crowbook on the new README.hangul.md produces a PDF with tex.command xelatex (64781 B) and tectonic (65120 B) while the old README.hangul.md fails on xelatex too; nota.hangul.md stays byte-identical to build.ps1's expectation; build.ps1's sample run now also requires <name>.hangul.md.pdf (README and nota both produced); needs the NanumGothicCoding font, which Linux CI installs (fonts-nanum-coding) and Windows CI gets from the uncommitted alphabet scripts/init.ps1 change; documents.livemd is not updated (the .spi is the source now and the livemd is pending deletion)", TaskKind, P3, D2, 100u32, Done, "", "alphabet,documents", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_alphabet_readme_hangul_pdf () = Planning ("ALPHABET-README-HANGUL-PDF", Minutes 60u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl spiral_cli_output_normal () = Task ("SPIRAL-CLI-OUTPUT-NORMAL", "Make the spiral <target> CLI (apps/spiral) report program output uniformly so Kino cross-target agreement needs no normalizing (found by the Kino lane 20:46): gleam runs return an empty output (program stdout lost, so agreement cannot check gleam), lua output is Debug-formatted (String(\"...\")), cuda/python append main's return value, fsharp prints +1.570796 style floats; target: raw program stdout per target, the return value reported separately; 21:16: also process_gleam assumes the source dir's parent is the package dir and the CLI exits 0 when gleam run fails (the root of KINO-GLEAM-FALSE-PASS); the CLI must propagate target failures as a non-zero exit; 22:03: also -t javascript never runs (process_gleam only bundles); 00:57 CLI parts done by the Kino lane (uncommitted): lua print/return values use Lua tostring and tab-joined args (print('x', 6*7) -> x<TAB>42); process_gleam failwiths (exit 101 + message) when gleam run fails; edits in apps/spiral/spiral.livemd via mix spiral.edit, spiral.spi re-exported, spiral.rs regenerated (~16k-line diff, mostly compiler drift: review before commit); new lua run check in apps/spiral/build.ps1 (spiral-check-lua<TAB>42), build.ps1 -fast RUST-OK; left: process_gleam's package dir = parent of the source dir (Kino no longer depends on it); 2026-10-13 13:42 Kino lane: process_gleam uses <source dir>/gleam_package (with src/main.gleam copied in) when the source is not inside a src/ directory, instead of writing gleam.toml into the parent of the source dir; erlang runs build first and then run, so the output is the program's stdout only (no dependency compile warnings); new apps/spiral/build.ps1 gleam run check expects exactly 'spiral-check-gleam 42' (scoop Erlang first on PATH for the checks: choco OTP 26 crashes gleam with a corrupt atom table); CLI rebuilt, RUST-OK, shipped; left: the cuda/python trailing main value (Python backend codegen) and fsharp float formatting (lib); 2026-10-13 14:39 the cuda/python trailing main value was Kino's own Cell.patch_python (it rewrote the python entry to print main's result for value cells); Kino test cells now keep the compiler's entry, a cuda test prints only its assertions; left: F# float format; 2026-10-13 15:24 F# float format done in lib (sm'_real format_real): floats render as the shortest round-trip decimal with .0 for integral values and NaN/Infinity/-Infinity on fsharp, rust, python/cuda, typescript, lua (gleam already did); test sm' show_floats one exact string on 6 targets; sm' 36/36, math 14/14, physics 39/39, date_time 32/32, common 5/5; C/Zig/Lean/Delphi keep their old float text; 2026-10-13 18:05 canonical floats now on every backend: C (shortest %.*g checked by strtod/strtof), Zig ({d} plus .0), Lean (one-line Id.run do, Float.ofScientific round-trip check, f32 via toFloat32; probe run with lean +leanprover/lean4:v4.35.0-rc4 --run), Delphi (global SpiralFormatFloat: FloatToStrF ffGeneral 1..17 digits until StrToFloat reads back; the old inline-if arm did not compile under fpc); show_floats asserts one string on 9 Kino targets (gleam, lua, fsharp, cuda, rust, typescript, c, zig, delphi); landed after packs 3+4 bless", TaskKind, P1, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "spiral-cli,kino,backends", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_spiral_cli_output_normal () = Planning ("SPIRAL-CLI-OUTPUT-NORMAL", Minutes 240u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl kino_gleam_false_pass () = Task ("KINO-GLEAM-FALSE-PASS", "Found and fixed 21:16 by the Kino lane (uncommitted): ///> gleam (erlang target, the default) never ran a test's program, so every gleam test passed regardless of its asserts: Kino wrote main.gleam into the cell dir, the spiral CLI's process_gleam treats the source dir's parent as the package dir, so it wrote gleam.toml into the shared %TEMP%/spiral_kino and gleam run failed there (exit 1, stderr dropped) while the CLI still exited 0 with empty output; repro test gleam_fails = 1i32 |> _assert_eq 2i32 reported passed; fix: gleam output goes to <cell>/gleam/src/main.gleam and Kino itself writes gleam.toml (CLI dependency list + the cell's -d deps), runs gleam build then gleam run and uses their exit codes (missing gleam = unsupported; -t javascript still via the CLI); verified: gleam_fails fails with the __assert_eq panic, gleam_passes passes with plain output (agreement can now compare gleam); ~16 s per gleam test (dependencies compiled per cell); every earlier passed gleam target in lib runs was unverified", TaskKind, P0, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "kino,tests,gleam,false-pass", "current", "current")
inl kino_target_false_pass_audit () = Task ("KINO-TARGET-FALSE-PASS-AUDIT", "After two silent false passes on 2026-10-12 (C/cpp/Delphi exit code taken as the cell value; gleam never ran), prove every ///> target can fail: one deliberately failing test per target (rust, c, cpp, delphi, fsharp, python, cuda, typescript, lua, gleam, gleam -t javascript, zig, wgsl, and any other the Builders know) must report failed with the assertion text, and a passing twin must pass; keep these as a permanent Kino test so a regression in any runner fails CI; 22:03 done (uncommitted): apps/kino/test/spiral/kino/target_audit_test.exs, tag :target_audit (excluded from default mix test; run mix test --only target_audit inside the gate, ~2.5 min): per runner a failing test must fail with its assertion text and a passing twin must pass (or both unsupported); green for rust host, c, cpp, fsharp, python, cuda, typescript, lua, gleam, gleam -t javascript, zig, wgsl; delphi unsupported (no dcc32); found a third false pass: gleam -t javascript only built through the CLI (process_gleam bundles JS), fixed: test cells build and run JS Gleam in Kino (gleam build, gleam run --runtime bun); caveats: cpp and gleam-js use a literal failwith instead of lib _assert_eq (compiler/lib gaps), wgsl checks the exit code only; agreement now also ignores double quotes (gleam quotes strings inside records, fsharp does not)", TaskKind, P0, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "kino,tests,false-pass", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_kino_target_false_pass_audit () = Planning ("KINO-TARGET-FALSE-PASS-AUDIT", Minutes 180u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl lib_assert_cpp_gleamjs () = Task ("LIB-ASSERT-CPP-GLEAMJS", "lib testing's _assert_eq does not compile on two targets (found 22:03 by the Kino target audit): Cpp stops with 'In the Cuda backend, the exception string must be a literal' (the shared Cpp/Cuda codegen requires a literal failwith message, lib builds it), and the generated Gleam does not build for -t javascript (Erlang-only externals, see GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS); fix the Cpp failwith codegen for non-literal messages and give lib testing's Gleam path JS-safe helpers so the audit can use _assert_eq everywhere; 01:59 Cpp half: CodegenCpp TyFailwith accepted only literal messages although its strings are const char pointers; candidate tmp/unify/cpp_failwith_strings.py also accepts runtime string values (DV of StringT printed with printf %s); probe verified on single-flight Debug: a runtime message boom compiles with g++, prints boom, exits -1; applies after the pack-0003 freeze; Gleam JS half (lib testing Gleam externals are Erlang-only) still open; 01:59 Gleam JS half analysed: the gap is in codegenGleam's preludes, not lib testing: SpiralArray/SpiralRef keep storage in the Erlang process dictionary (put/get), math uses erlang math, the string helpers used erlang halt; JS needs @external(javascript, ...) twins from a spiral_ffi.mjs (a Map keyed by a counter for array/ref storage, Math.* for math), and the CLI process_gleam + Kino's Gleam builder must write that file into the package src/ next to main.gleam; cross-component, so planned as its own step, see GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS; 03:44 confirmed by the Kino lane: any -t javascript program that pulls in lib testing fails because the SpiralArray prelude helpers (spiral_array_new_key/store/load/from_storage) are Erlang-only externals; user decision 2026-10-13 07:08: no Gleam JS prelude port; JS output goes through the TypeScript backend; Gleam -t javascript stays scoped to Lustre UI cells (they use local expect_html, not lib testing); the Gleam JS half is closed as won't-do, the Cpp half (runtime failwith messages) shipped in pack-0004", TaskKind, P2, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "compiler,lib,cpp,gleam", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_lib_assert_cpp_gleamjs () = Planning ("LIB-ASSERT-CPP-GLEAMJS", Minutes 180u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl delphi_tuple_arrays () = Task ("DELPHI-TUPLE-ARRAYS", "Delphi build-fails on samples/wgsl_array_tuple (an array of (i32 * bool) with a runtime index; C, Rust and WGSL all exit 121) in single-flight-20261012-214348 (22:04); find the Delphi codegen gap for arrays whose element is several values and fix it; 22:32 cause: codegenDelphi's ArrayIndexSet emitted 'v1[0] := v2, True;' for a multi-value element (reads already went through a TTuple temp); fix candidate: an ArrayIndexSet case for DV(L(_,YArray element)) that writes tup_as element c (TupleCreateN(...)); shared text, both cores; in candidate tmp/delphi/candidate with the Gleam and InlineDepth changes; 23:54 VERIFIED single-flight Debug: emits v1[0] := TupleCreate0(v2, True); fpc builds, exit 121 (= C/Rust/WGSL); suite row flips build-fail -> ran 121 at the next bless", TaskKind, P2, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "compiler,delphi,arrays", "current", "current")
