inl wgsl_record_join_points () = Task ("WGSL-RECORD-JOIN-POINTS", "codegenWgsl rejects records as let rec loop state and join-point results ('The WGSL backend doesn't support the type {a : i32; b : i32} (YRecord) yet.') although a record runtime value works; 2026-10-13 02:44 repro: type s = {a : i32; b : i32}; let rec loop (i : i32) (x : s) : s ... and inl f (x : i32) : s = join {a = x; b = 2}; blocks TIBIA-HUNT-SIM-WGSL (hunt state is a record of records + vitality alive); 02:46 fix (main, spiral_compiler.fs, uncommitted, rides pack-0004): codegenWgsl flatten_ty only flattened pairs and unit, so a record as loop state / join-point result reached tyv (YRecord unsupported); now env.ty_to_data |> data_free_vars, the same order the return statements use; test: record loop repro expects WGSL exit 8 = C; the same flatten_ty shape exists in 4 later backends (Calyx/Kotlin/Swift/OpenQASM/eBPF region) and likely shares the record gap; 02:58 second half fixed: the record result came back as let (v9, v10) = if ... then return (v9, v10), with the recursive call inside the else branch as let (v7, v8) = call; return (v7, v8), so self-tail detection missed it and WGSL rejected the recursive declaration; codegenWgsl now normalizes a trailing let-bound op whose bound values are returned unchanged into a tail return of that op (returned_call_as_tail, used by self_tail and by binds under a tail return), so the self call becomes loop/continue; verified single-flight Debug: the Tibia record-loop repro gives WGSL exit 8 = C exit 8; rides pack-0004; 05:11 BLESSED with pack-0004: single-flight-20261013-032803 and hopac-20261013-035644 both 3,797 parity + 33 residual rows (25 WGSL from the tail-return normalization, 8 Gleam from the string prelude), DISAGREE 0; -Bless run single-flight-20261013-044504 reproduces it (0 differences); EXPECTED.tsv 3,935 rows, 33 changed; 07:02 regression sample samples/wgsl_record_loop (Wgsl list in matrix.psd1; WGSL 8 = C 8 on the pack-0005 DLL)", TaskKind, P1, D4, 100u32, Done, "", "wgsl,compiler,tibia", "current", "current")
inl kino_test_body_scope () = Task ("KINO-TEST-BODY-SCOPE", "Kino lowers a test block's body into top-level cell statements, so tuple patterns (inl a, b = f x: 'The first pattern of a global statement should either be a variable') and a let rec that captures a test-local inl ~seed ('Unbound variable: seed') fail inside tests; a test body should be lowered as one local scope (a function body); found 2026-10-13 03:34 in apps/tibia/combat.spi, worked around with match and a module-level helper; serves: every Tibia test that measures a distribution; 2026-10-13 07:41 done: notebook.spi plan lowers a non-static test body as inl spiral_kino_test () = <body> then spiral_kino_test () (static bodies stay top level); real repro (tuple pattern + let rec over a local, rust+fsharp) failed before with 'first pattern of a global statement', passes after; regression test in notebook_report_test.exs; README; 2026-10-13 08:45 follow-up: declarations (type/union/nominal/prototype/instance/open blocks) at the body's top level are hoisted above the scoped wrapper (physics system_kinetic_energy tests declare an instance in their body)", TaskKind, P2, D4, 100u32, Done, "", "kino,tests,tibia", "current", "current")
inl kino_accept_rerun () = Task ("KINO-ACCEPT-RERUN", "--accept false green: it rewrites the first failing _assert_eq of a test, counts the test as accepted and exits 0, but later assertions in the same test never ran (combat.spi needed 3 accept rounds to pin mean/low/high); two identical expected literals in one test are refused as ambiguous; re-run an accepted test until it passes or fails unacceptably, and pick the literal by the failing assertion's position; found 2026-10-13 03:34; serves: pinning fidelity statistics in apps/tibia; 2026-10-13 07:41 done: --accept re-runs accepted tests until they pass or fail unacceptably (max 25 rounds); repeated literals picked by position (count of passing __assert_eq { expected = E } lines before the mismatch), refused when the literal ran more often than written; exit by final result; regression tests in accept_test.exs; README", TaskKind, P2, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "kino,tests,tibia", "current", "current")
inl kino_memo_self_package () = Task ("KINO-MEMO-SELF-PACKAGE", "A .spi that is both listed in package.spiproj and run with ///- --package . puts its own content into the package print, so any edit (including --accept rewrites) misses the memo for every test in the file (combat.spi: memo_hits 0 on unchanged tests, 2026-10-13 03:34); exclude the notebook's own module from its package print; serves: instant re-runs while iterating Tibia modules; 2026-10-13 07:41 done: package print hashes each listed module without its test blocks and /// lines (what the compiler reads), so test-only edits and accept rewrites keep every other test's memo entry while definition edits still miss; regression test in memo_test.exs; README", TaskKind, P3, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "kino,memo,tibia", "current", "current")
inl tibia_live_policy () = Task ("TIBIA-LIVE-POLICY", "2026-10-13 03:34 first version: apps/tibia/policy.spi (typed observation, heals/engages) is the single source for hunt_sim's Tuned policy (golden values unchanged) and, through live.spi compiled by Spiral's Lua backend, for a live OTClient probe (tibia-local/run-policy-scenario.ps1: restart world, reset Knight Sample, GOD summons, policy probe off-screen and muted, stop world); Rust, C and Lua tests agree; next: target choice and chasing in Spiral (TIBIA-FLEE-AND-TARGETING) and the parity live layer layer; serves: BENCHMARK.md parity (same client for agents and bots); 2026-10-13 07:39 live policy uses CurrentFirst targeting from the simulator result: 3 of 3 trolls killed", Feature, P1, D4, 100u32, Done, "", "tibia,arena,lua", "current", "current")
inl tibia_combat_fidelity () = Task ("TIBIA-COMBAT-FIDELITY", "2026-10-13 03:34 apps/tibia/combat.spi models Canary's melee hit: truncated normal roll (Irwin-Hall approximation of N(0.5,0.25) on [0,1] with rejection like Canary's normal_random), defense block (blockCount charge +1/s, max 2), armor block, fight-mode attack factor (offensive 1.0, balanced 0.75, defensive 0.5 in damage.spi max_melee_damage); armor/defense from data-canary (troll 10/10, cyclops 20/20, rat and wolf 5/5); model: balanced axe 30 lands 72% at 10.7 per hit, one live run measured about 8.3 per hit over 15 hits (1.8 standard errors, needs a dedicated live measurement); next: put it into hunt_sim (golden values will move: the old sim rolled uniform 0..max with no blocks) and a live distribution probe; serves: P3 fidelity; 2026-10-13 03:53 hunt_sim now hits through combat.melee_hit (balanced, per-scenario offense: level 8/atk 20/skill 30, cyclops hunters level 30/atk 35/skill 60, health kept at 150 so the cyclops scenario stays dangerous); golden values re-pinned (seed 7: 10 kills -> 3, scripted 220 -> 64 vs control 149 -> 44 over 20 seeds; cyclops still separates: heal at 120 -> 0 deaths, at 20 -> 10); live skeleton measurement (balanced, axe 30): 28 swings, 17 landed (60.7%), 9.76 per landed hit; pooled 45 live hits about 9.3 vs model 10.7 (about 1.7 se, criticals not modeled), landed 60.7% vs 71.8% (1.2 se): model probably slightly generous, needs more live samples; 2026-10-13 06:13 pooled 54 live hits (balanced, axe 30, steel axe 21, level 8, vs troll/skeleton armor 10 defense 10): mean 9.35 per landed hit vs model 10.7 (about 2 se, model ~13% high); ruled out: disableMonsterArmor=false (blocks on), monster mitigation 0 (Monster::getMitigation, no mitigation in data-canary), knight meleeDamageMultiplier 1.0; live includes 5% base criticals at +10% (playerBaseCriticalChance/Damage) that the model lacks, which makes the gap larger; still to check: weapon proficiency stats (WeaponProficiencyBonus ATTACK_DAMAGE), Combat::doCombatHealth modifiers, and whether getWeaponSkill differs from the DB skill_axe; landed-rate measurement from client swing windows is unreliable (61% one run, 9.7% the next: potion use or target loss stops server swings while the probe counts misses), measure landed rate server-side or from blocked-hit effects instead; 2026-10-13 07:22 gap explained, model confirmed: the live 'A skeleton loses N hitpoints' message reports damage capped at the creature's remaining health (a killing blow logs only what was left), the model compared uncapped hits; combat.capped_hit_stats fights 50-health creatures to death with the same cap and gives 13451 / 1435 = 9.37 per landed hit vs the pooled live 9.35 (54 hits), test hits_capped_at_remaining_health_match_live_skeleton_log; still unmeasured live: the landed rate (client swing windows are unreliable), criticals (5% at +10%, not modeled); 2026-10-13 10:17 creature-to-player damage measured live (tibia-local/arena-logs/cyclops-measure-*.log, level-30 knight vs one cyclops, probe-measure-body now records 'You lose N hitpoints due to an attack'): 19 hits, mean 58.42, median 51, max 111 (115 in an earlier run); Canary rolls monster melee with normal_random (truncated N(0.5, 0.25) on [min, max], src/utils/tools.cpp), not uniform, and a further x1.1 fits both the mean (52.5 x 1.1 = 57.75) and the max (105 x 1.1 = 115); source of the 1.1 not found yet (rates are 1.0, no forge or critical); simulators now use combat.melee_roll x live_creature_damage_percent 110 (pack_sim cyclops_hits_match_the_live_measurement: mean 57.58, max 115 over 2000 draws); hunt_sim still uniform, next; 2026-10-13 11:33 the x1.1 was wrong: the forge made almost every monster influenced (forgeInfluencedLimit 300 in a small world; tibia-local config now 0/0); forge-free live cyclops hits: 74, mean 34.74, max 77; Canary's player blocks (defense (shield 11/4 + 2.23) x 26 x 0.75 x 0.16 = 15, armor 3+8+5+1 = 17) applied to a truncated-normal 0-105 roll give 34.97 mean per landed hit, max 86 (combat.knight_sample_gear, combat.creature_hit; pack_sim pins it); the cyclops hunter now wields the live knight's steel axe (attack 21, was 35); hunt_sim still needs its re-pin once the C loop regression is fixed; 2026-10-13 11:49 hunt_sim re-pinned on the gear-blocked creature hits after the compiler's 11:00 C loop fix (all C targets compile again): golden seed 7 ends at full health 150 (was 97, then 82), the GPU twin matches; the six- and twenty-seed evolution tests were deleted, their 'matches the tuned cavebot' claims no longer held and the 100-seed profit benchmark in pack_sim supersedes them; hunt_sim suite 11/11; 2026-10-13 12:05 block charges: Canary gives a creature one defense-block charge per second, capped at 2 (creature.cpp blockTicks/blockCount); three cyclops land ~1.5 hits/s, so about a third get no shield block; the pack simulator now tracks the knight's charges (combat.max_block_charges, block_charge_ms); live evidence it matters: at heal 70 one kiting run died (72 hp then a 72 hit) and rules runs bottomed at 8/16/22 hp, while the simulator saw no deaths at any threshold; 2026-10-13 12:47 simulator bug found and fixed: in hunting_pack.advance (and hunt_sim.advance_against) the player acted after the foes in the same tick before the death check, so a knight taken to 0 hp drank a potion and lived; with heal threshold 0 it drank 12 potions in 60 s; deaths were undercounted all day, which is why the simulator saw no deaths at any heal threshold while heal 70 killed every live competitor once; death is now checked right after the foes act (regression test pack_sim a_knight_slain_by_a_hit_cannot_drink_in_the_same_tick: heal 0, 60 s, 0 potions, 5 deaths); also measured: the knight's client speed is 117 (2 x 117 = 234, the level-8 base speed), ~650 ms per straight step, not the 450 the simulator used; knight_sample_step_ms is now 650; 2026-10-13 14:48 clean-data check of block charges (hunting_pack now tracks hits_taken and damage_taken): simulated open-field clears land 36.0 per hit with charges and 34.6 always shielded; clean live runs (full gear, before 12:02) land 33.6 (about +-1 SE) at only ~0.75 landed hits/s, i.e. about two attackers, because the arena spot is walled in; two attackers never outpace one charge per second, so the live data does not contradict the charges, it says the arena's terrain must be in the simulator (probe-scan-body.lua, next); 2026-10-13 15:30 the arena spot is now in the simulator (tibia-local/probe-scan-body.lua walkability scan around 1933,1350 encoded as integer row masks in terrain.ArenaSpot, so WGSL works too); predicted clear with the heal-160 cavebot over 20 seeds: 84.6 s, 13.1 potions, 0 deaths, 36.1 per landed hit; clean live v4 rules: 87.9 +- 14.3 s, 14.7 +- 2.9 potions, 0 deaths, 35-37 per hit; first sim-to-live agreement within noise on every measure (pack_sim the_arena_spot_simulation_predicts_the_clean_live_clear); 2026-10-14 09:05 two simulator upgrades for real-map scenarios: (1) the pack holds up to six creatures (views.band, a 6-slot HKT container with mapped/counted; camps carry kinds as a band plus a size; unused slots hold buried foes that never act or draw random numbers), bit-exact with every pin before; (2) monster sight from Canary's Creature::canSee (viewport 8+3 by 6+5: a monster follows the knight only within 11 tiles on both axes on its floor, otherwise idles); with sight, a knight that chases a fleeing troll leaves the others behind (seed 7: 8 -> 7 kills; 20-seed troll pack CurrentFirst 332 -> 330), and kiting on the open field walks out of the cyclops' sight so they stop following (300 kills, was 899); the warm-started network now dominates the tuned cavebot by 1550 gp at equal kills; arena-spot cavebot results unchanged", Feature, P1, D4, 100u32, Done, "", "tibia,fidelity,oracle", "current", "current")
inl tibia_flee_and_targeting () = Task ("TIBIA-FLEE-AND-TARGETING", "Live run 2026-10-13 03:34: trolls flee below runHealth (data-canary: troll 15, goblin 15, wolf 8, cave rat 3, skeleton and cyclops 0) and the Lua glue's nearest-monster targeting switches targets as monsters move, so 0 kills in 150 s despite hits; the simulator models neither; add run_health to creatures.spi, fleeing to hunt_sim, and target choice plus chase (an Approach engagement) to policy.spi so the live glue has no decisions left; serves: fidelity and BENCHMARK parity; 2026-10-13 04:18 sim side done: creatures.spi carries run_health and step_ms (Canary's step formula on grass, checked by a Rust/C test: troll 950 ms, cyclops 650, player level 8 550, level 30 450), hunt_sim models fleeing below run_health (escape at distance 8, respawn without loot), chasing creatures step at x2 (WALK_TARGET_NEARBY_EXTRA_COST), the policy's Approach steps the player toward the target, scenarios are 3000 ticks (5 minutes); policy.spi chooses targets with prefers (in reach, current, weakest, nearest) and live.spi exports it to the OTClient probe; live: first kill by the Spiral policy (+30 experience); open: TIBIA-FAIR-CHASING-BASELINE, C rows wait on C-SELF-TAIL-LOOP; 2026-10-13 04:29 live run with Spiral targeting + Approach + potions vs 3 trolls: 1 kill (+30), then every weakened troll fled out of view; policy.prefers ranks in-reach above current, so when the 26% troll fled the policy switched to a fresh adjacent troll (target health 70 -> 26 -> 58 -> 19 -> 76 %) and the weak ones escaped; hunt_sim has one creature at a time and cannot show this: needs a multi-creature scenario (spawn of 3) in the sim, then let the arena decide between finish-the-fleeing and fight-the-adjacent orderings; 2026-10-13 07:39 closed the loop: apps/tibia/pack_sim.spi (3 creatures, per-creature step clocks, fleeing, escapes, policy-chosen target) shows the targeting order decides a troll pack: over 20 seeds x 5 min ReachFirst gets 37 kills / 338 escapes, CurrentFirst 286 kills / 0 escapes (test current_first_targeting_finishes_fleeing_trolls_over_twenty_seeds); policy.spi has prefers_by ReachFirst|CurrentFirst; live.spi now exports CurrentFirst and the live 3-troll run killed all three (+90 experience, chasing a 7% troll ~10 tiles) where ReachFirst killed 1; open: creatures ignore each other's tiles in the sim (no collision), monster melee damage model is still uniform 0..max", TaskKind, P1, D4, 100u32, Done, "", "tibia,policy,fidelity", "current", "current")
inl tibia_p1_recheck () = Task ("TIBIA-P1-RECHECK", "PLAN.md marks P1 (log in, walk, kill a creature) met on 2026-10-12, but that kill came from a probe that counted a creature leaving view as a kill (green frog: experience 0, targetDistance 6); 2026-10-13 03:34 live runs show hits landing but no verified kill yet; re-check with an experience gain or a death message and fix the PLAN row; serves: honesty of the P1 claim; 2026-10-13 03:38 verified: the measurement probe attacked a skeleton until the client saw it dead, experience +52 (tibia-local/probe-measure-body.lua, run-policy-scenario.ps1 -Monster skeleton)", TaskKind, P1, D2, 100u32, Done, "", "tibia,oracle", "current", "current")
inl c_self_tail_loop () = Task ("C-SELF-TAIL-LOOP", "codegenC emits a let rec self-tail call as real recursion (Tuple0 loop0(...) returning loop0(...)), so correctness depends on gcc's sibling-call optimisation: apps/tibia/hunt_sim (3000-tick loop over a 20-field record) dies with 0xC00000FD STATUS_STACK_OVERFLOW at -O0 (Kino builds C as plain gcc -o) and runs at -O2; Rust and WGSL already loop. 2026-10-13 04:18 candidate (not applied; C is the oracle backend, every recursive C residual changes, so the next session applies it in its first pack and blesses after review): patch script C:\\Users\\i574n\\.claude\\jobs\\ac4b4e98\\tmp\\c_self_tail_loop.py (anchored, inside CodegenC only, a shared section so both cores get it): methods whose body ends in a self call and whose parameters hold nothing reference-counted (is_heap false, so every incref/decref around the call is a no-op) print as while (1) { ... } and the self call becomes temporaries, parameter assignment and continue; a printing-depth check keeps closures printed inside the method from turning calls into continue; output stays deterministic; repro C:\\Users\\i574n\\.claude\\jobs\\ac4b4e98\\tmp\\tibia_c\\sim.spi; serves: apps/tibia C rows (CPU oracle); 2026-10-13 04:46 candidate v4 verified on the repro: hunt_sim simulate (3000 ticks) built at -O0 now exits 1 like -O2 and Rust (3 self loops become while (1) + continue, 1 non-tail self call stays); besides the direct form it handles a method that ends in let d = <op>; return d unchanged (the shape refcounting produces), only inside self-loop methods whose parameters and the skipped return's refcount sets are heap-free; it needs main's null-slot fix (pack-0005) because the hunt_sim loop carries a GADT-specialized union and is_heap read tag_cases positionally (the script no longer carries that hunk); 467-row C oracle parity run in progress; 2026-10-13 05:35 candidate v5 (tail checks compare recorded method keys instead of calling method, so no method is created early and nothing renumbers) verified: script c_self_tail_loop.py applies cleanly to main's pack-0005 snapshot (5a6e53fb tmp/compiler-main/spiral_compiler.pack-0005.fs); hunt_sim repro at -O0 exits 1 = -O2 = Rust; all 467 native C oracle rows compiled with the pinned candidate (Debug, tmp/candidate-c-self-tail) vs the pinned pack-0004 Release (tmp/baseline-release-pack0004), built with gcc -std=c11 -O2 -w -lm: 435 rows identical exit+stdout, 13 rows change C text and every one is a self-tail loop with oracle-identical behaviour (ebpf_bacc, ebpf_facc, ebpf_nested, ebpf_t3, ebpf_tacc, ebpf_tworec, ebpf_uacc, native_string_utf8_fold_source, native_string_utf8_fold_source_invalid, native_tail_recursion, python_self_tail_call_depth, tail_loop, ts_tail_loop), 0 rows with changed text and changed behaviour; 22 runtime differences all have byte-identical C (abi_external*, dynamic_array*, managed*, native_* rows that need the harness's Get-CFlags link flags my script does not pass); 7 mega runtime rows fail only in the Debug candidate with a parser stack overflow (fold_offset_term, before codegen; Debug has no tail calls, see BUNDLER-DEBUG-STACK-OVERFLOW); 3 rows fail on both; next session: apply to its first pack, Release build both cores, -Suite all -Native, expect exactly these 13 C residual rows to change, bless after review, then KINO-C-FLAGS-PARITY; user decision 2026-10-13 07:08: land only after KINO-C-FLAGS-PARITY (Kino builds C with -std=c11 -O2 like the harness), then decide; patch stays ready (13 C residual rows, 0 behavior changes); 07:28 user: land now; applied to spiral_compiler.fs for pack 1 (Tibia lane's script c_self_tail_loop.py, after KINO-C-FLAGS-PARITY landed 07:27); 2026-10-13 09:52 main: pack 1 verified (both Release cores, both suites DISAGREE 0, sf vs hopac differ only on the expected Delphi format_any row) and blessed (3843 rows reproduced row for row; baseline 3936 -> 3949 lines)", TaskKind, P1, D4, 100u32, Done, "KINO-C-FLAGS-PARITY", "compiler,c,codegen,tibia", "current", "current")
inl kino_c_flags_parity () = Task ("KINO-C-FLAGS-PARITY", "Kino builds C as gcc -o exe main.c (apps/kino/lib/spiral/kino/targets.ex build :c) while the compiler harness uses -std=c11 -O2 -w (plus -lm); align Kino with the harness, but only after C-SELF-TAIL-LOOP lands so -O2 does not mask unbounded recursion; filed 2026-10-13 04:18; serves: Kino C rows meaning the same as oracle C rows; 2026-10-13 07:22 done: Targets.c_args gives gcc -std=c11 -O2 -w [-include <notebook dir>/c-shim.h] -o cell main.c -lm (cl /nologo /O2), used by build and run; 3 unit tests in targets_test.exs; target_audit c ok", TaskKind, P1, D2, 100u32, Done, "C-SELF-TAIL-LOOP", "kino,c,tibia", "current", "current")
inl tibia_fair_chasing_baseline () = Task ("TIBIA-FAIR-CHASING-BASELINE", "2026-10-13 04:18 with fleeing modeled, the chasing Tuned policy gets 343 kills vs 21 for the standing Scripted policy over 20 seeds x 5 minutes, and the evolved Tuned policy (heal 136, strike 10) scores 31 vs the ScriptedHealingBelow 120 baseline's 23; that comparison is unfair (the baseline cannot chase), so it is not a promotion; add a scripted chasing baseline (what nExBot/vBot cavebots do) and re-run the evolution against it; serves: BENCHMARK.md promotion rule (beat the tuned baseline); 2026-10-13 04:21 added cavebot_baseline () = Tuned (120, 0) (heal below 120, always engage and chase); against it on held-out seeds 21..30 the evolved policy (heal 136, strike 10) scores 31 vs the cavebot's 34 (control -127): the learned policy trails a fair baseline, no promotion; test renamed evolved_policy_trails_chasing_cavebot_baseline_and_beats_random_control_on_held_out_seeds; next: richer features or more search budget, judged against the cavebot; 2026-10-13 05:45 doubled search budget (8 generations x 12 offspring instead of 4 x 6) finds heal 135 / strike 27 and scores 30 on held-out seeds 21..30, still below the cavebot's 34 (test doubled_search_budget_still_trails_chasing_cavebot_on_held_out_seeds): the two-threshold policy class is the limit, not the search; next: policy features (target health percent, distance, healing and strike readiness, number of creatures in reach) before more compute; 2026-10-13 05:54 the gap was overfitting plus an untuned baseline: with 20 training seeds (same 8 x 12 budget) the ES finds heal 166 / strike 18 and scores 90 on held-out seeds 21..30 (vs 34 for the hand-picked cavebot heal<120), but a cavebot whose heal threshold is grid-tuned on the same 20 training seeds (tuned_cavebot_heal: heal 160) also scores 90: a tie, no promotion (test evolved_policy_ties_tuned_cavebot_on_held_out_seeds); lessons: always tune the baseline on the training seeds, use >= 20 training seeds; the two-threshold class is saturated, next is policy features", TaskKind, P1, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "tibia,arena,policy", "current", "current")
inl fsharp_from_pair_names () = Task ("FSHARP-FROM-PAIR-NAMES", "F# backend: two from_pair calls in one scope fail with FS0037 Duplicate definition of value 'b' because the F# arm of from_pair emits fixed let names (found 05:49 by the Kino lane; join works around it); generate fresh names like the other backends; 2026-10-13 07:51 verified by Kino lane: new base.spi test from_pair_twice (two from_pair calls and tuple patterns in one test body) passes and agrees on gleam, lua, fsharp, cuda, rust, typescript", TaskKind, P2, D2, 100u32, Done, "", "compiler,fsharp,lib", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_fsharp_from_pair_names () = Planning ("FSHARP-FROM-PAIR-NAMES", Minutes 90u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl prototype_has_param_nominal () = Task ("PROTOTYPE-HAS-PARAM-NOMINAL", "real_core.prototype_has does not match an instance on a parameterised nominal (instance show_as pair a b never matched) while a parameterless one (timestamp) does (found 05:49 by the Kino lane while adding canonical show); check how the instance lookup keys applied nominals; 07:28 not a compiler bug: probe (tmp/proto_has in main's job dir) with prototype show_as t, nominal box2 a b = a * b + instance show_as box2 a b, and nominal one = i32 + instance: !!!!PrototypeHas matches both on the pack-0005 Release DLL (exit 11 = both); the Kino case most likely reached show with a plain tuple (a * b) rather than a value of the pair nominal, so no instance applied; fix belongs in lib (wrap/convert to the nominal before show) if wanted", TaskKind, P2, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "compiler,prototypes", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_prototype_has_param_nominal () = Planning ("PROTOTYPE-HAS-PARAM-NOMINAL", Minutes 90u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl tibia_policy_features () = Task ("TIBIA-POLICY-FEATURES", "2026-10-13 05:54 the two-threshold policy (heal_below, strike_above) is saturated: evolution trained on 20 seeds ties a cavebot tuned on the same seeds (90 vs 90 on held-out seeds 21..30, apps/tibia/hunt_sim.spi); to beat a tuned baseline the policy needs more inputs: target health percent and fleeing state, distance, heal and strike readiness, number of creatures in reach, supplies left; extend policy.spi's observation and engagement (keeping live.spi and the Lua probe in sync), search with >= 20 training seeds, compare only against baselines tuned on the same seeds; serves: BENCHMARK.md promotion (beat the tuned baseline); 2026-10-13 08:03 after the per-spell cooldown fix, on 50 held-out seeds (101..150) evolution on 6 or 20 training seeds and the cavebot tuned on the same seeds score exactly 1116 each (random control 708): the best two-threshold policy is 'heal whenever ready, always strike', so the class is saturated; 10 held-out seeds were too few (claims flipped with small dynamic changes); evaluate on >= 50 held-out seeds; 2026-10-13 08:36 pack_sim cyclops pack (level-30 knight: health 185 + 15/level = 515, potion heals 125-175, cyclops x3), trained on seeds 1..20, scored on 101..150: a cavebot grid-tuned on the training seeds (heal below 150) scores 1099; adaptive evolution (search.spi, elitist (1+lambda), genome heal_base / heal_per_attacker / third gene) cold-started scores 939 with a strike floor and 948 with a retreat trigger, warm-started from the cavebot it returns the cavebot itself (1099, retreat never); a retreat that stops fighting costs kills; conclusion: the tuned cavebot sits at the optimum of every threshold-style policy tried, so beating it needs a learned agent with richer state (P5 graph code, waits on UPSTREAM-SYNTAX-SYNC in pack 2) or real kiting (step back while striking the single adjacent creature); 2026-10-13 09:13 learned-agent groundwork without the ML library (whose entry points are bound to its CUDA/Python host stack): apps/tibia/network.spi is an integer (Q8) 8-6-3 network (inputs: health fraction, attackers, target health, distance, heal/strike readiness, target fleeing, bias; outputs heal/engage/retreat; weights and inputs are distinct nominals), with an elitist neuroevolution search (keyed noise, spread decay 4/5); it learns 'heal below a quarter' perfectly from zero weights; pack_sim's tactic now holds a brain union (Rules adaptive | Network weights) and every rules number is unchanged; findings: C ArrayCreate returns uninitialized memory (fill explicitly; C-ARRAY-CREATE-INIT), a loop closing over the weights overflows the C stack (C-SELF-TAIL-LOOP-HEAP), Kino's debug Rust is too slow for training tests (KINO-RUST-FLAGS-PARITY; the test now carries ///- --timeout 1800000); 2026-10-13 09:51 first learned-agent result (pack_sim cyclops_pack_neuroevolution_beats_the_equal_data_cavebot_and_ties_the_twenty_seed_cavebot, Rust -O2, whole pack_sim suite ~2 min): network evolved from zero on 10 training seeds scores 1099 on 50 held-out seeds vs 894 for the cavebot tuned on the same 10 seeds; the cavebot tuned on 20 seeds also scores 1099, so the network matches the threshold class's best without beating it; hidden layer now computed once per decide and one decision per phase (bit-exact, pins unchanged); simulator module split out as hunting_pack.spi (pack_sim.spi keeps the tests); next: warm start from the hand-wired cavebot network (network.threshold_healer), richer actions (targeting) if the class stays saturated; 2026-10-13 09:56 saturation confirmed for the network too: network.threshold_healer wired as the tuned cavebot (heal 150 of 515 hp) reproduces it exactly (448 on training seeds 1-20); warm-started evolution (12x8, spread 64) finds no gain (448, held-out 1099); hand-wired kiting (network.with_kiting n: retreat while the strike recovers and >= n attackers) scores 263/434/442 on training for n=1/2/3 vs 448 without, so it is rejected; held-out n=3 would show 1113 vs 1099, a test-set-selection artifact (pinned as such); next: new actions (target choice by the network, positioning), since heal/engage/retreat are saturated in this scenario; 2026-10-13 11:33 benchmark redesigned after the fidelity fixes saturated kills (953 at every heal threshold, 0 deaths): hunting_pack now books gold loot (Canary drop chances), potions (50 gp) and deaths (14000 gp blessings) in a typed ledger; objectives Kills | Profit | ProfitKeepingKills floor; claims by Pareto dominance on held-out (kills, profit); result (pack_sim cyclops_pack_kiting_at_three_attackers_dominates_the_tuned_cavebot_on_a_hundred_held_out_seeds): kiting at 3 attackers (chosen on training) 939 kills / -258597 gp dominates the profit-tuned cavebot 937 / -412293; evolved rules 937 / -412393, network from zero 935 / -1456778, network warm-started from kiting 933 / -298312 (both short of the floor held out); next: larger search budgets on the training host and the kiting policy live (live.spi needs the network/brain path); 2026-10-13 12:57 results after the fidelity fixes (block charges, 650 ms knight step, diagonal x3 and chase x2 steps, 250 ms action gap witness, death before the player's turn): heal 90 dies 7 times per 100 hunts (601 kills) while 130+ tie at 953, so kills are no longer saturated below 130; profit-tuned cavebot heals below 100: 937 kills / -471193 gp held out; evolved rules converge to it; kiting at 3 loses (910 / -849780); network from zero 937 / -1481643; the network warm-started from the wired cavebot dominates it by 150 gp (three potions over 100 hunts), the first learned dominance, but tiny; clear predictions: heal 70 dies in 3/20 clears (live 1/5), kiting 7/20; live arena v2 started at heal 100 for every competitor (arena-results-cyclops-v2.tsv); 2026-10-13 14:22 bigger warm search (12 generations x 10 offspring, spread 32, from the wired heal-100 cavebot, ProfitKeepingKills floor): held out 937 kills / -470343 gp vs the cavebot's 937 / -471193, dominance by 850 gp (17 potions over 100 hunts, ~0.2%); heal and retreat timing barely change damage intake, which drives the cost; next lever: terrain (walls, occupancy, corridors that cap attackers), which the simulator lacks (open field, foes may share a tile); 2026-10-13 14:34 terrain.spi (layout union OpenField | EastCorridor, pure blocked function so every backend incl. WGSL works, steps around taken tiles) plus occupancy in hunting_pack (no shared tiles): heal 90 now dies 156 times per 100 hunts, 130 still dies, 170+ tie at 950 kills; profit-tuned cavebot heals below 160 (955 / -454093 held out); kiting at 2 earns -196624 but kills 788 (no dominance either way); the warm network overfits (949 / -33.8 M); a corridor cuts potions 15.3 -> 6.5 and deaths 3 -> 0 per 20 clears (a_corridor_cuts_potions_and_deaths_in_a_cyclops_clear); heavy pack_sim tests moved to C after the compiler's pack 3 (only golden seed 7 keeps a Rust twin); next: positioning as an action (seek a choke tile), the live arena spot's real layout from probe-scan-body; 2026-10-13 14:44 positioning: terrain RoomAndCorridor (room east, one-tile corridor west); in a clear, retreat-at-2 rules with ReachFirst targeting (CurrentFirst pins the knight on a target stuck behind the front cyclops) use 8.2 potions and 0 deaths vs the cavebot's 15.6 and 7 per 20 clears, 4 s slower; in the respawning hunt benchmark (room_and_corridor_evolved_rules_do_not_beat_the_fully_tuned_cavebot) evolved rules (targeting order chosen on training) pick heal 223 + 3/attacker and never retreat, 942 / -477431 vs the cavebot's 942 / -477181; an earlier 81600 gp 'win' came from the cavebot's heal sweep stopping at 200 (it tuned to the boundary); sweeps now reach 400 (BENCHMARK rule added); 2026-10-13 15:45 FIRST LEARNED DOMINANCE ON THE FAITHFUL SIMULATOR: on the scanned arena spot (terrain.ArenaSpot), objective CheapClearWithin (supply cost of a clear, 50 gp per potion and 14000 per death, with mean clear time within the tuned cavebot's +15%), rules evolved on 100 training clears under both targeting orders choose ReachFirst, heal below 131, retreat at 2 attackers, i.e. they rediscover the hand-found positional policy; on 100 held-out clears: 825 potions, 0 deaths, 88.1 s vs the tuned cavebot (heal 130) 1362 potions, 0 deaths, 88.5 s, 39% fewer potions with no cost in time or deaths (pack_sim on_the_arena_spot_evolution_finds_retreating_at_two_and_dominates_the_tuned_cavebot); live: a smoke run of the retreat policy cleared with 10 potions vs 14.7/14.8 for rules/vBot in v4; arena v5 (vBot, retreat, rules at heal 160) running; 2026-10-13 15:58 network on the arena-spot clear: the wired network (heal 131, kiting at 2, ReachFirst) scores 806 potions, 0 deaths, 86.9 s on 100 held-out clears, marginally better than the evolved rules (825, 88.1 s); a warm-started evolution (6x6, spread 64) improves the training cost (-41550 -> -41100 gp) but not held out (808), a local optimum at this budget; the objective is now a unit-indexed GADT (objective kill_score | objective gold_coins, measured returns amount unit, kills_and_gold_do_not_add fails to compile); 2026-10-13 16:43 why retreating saves potions: live retreat runs that stepped 4-10 times then sat 'cornered' near 1936,1349 used 12-14 potions, the one that retreated 37 times through the lanes used 7; a refuge-seeking step (terrain.refuge: the free neighbor with strictly fewer attack spots) is WORSE in the simulator (14.2 potions vs the cavebot's 12.9) while stepping away saves a third (8.7), so the gain is time off while the cyclops re-approach with slow chase (x2) and diagonal (x3) steps, not choke points; the live effect is smaller because the knight gets cornered; retreat stays step_away, refuge kept as a tested alternative; 2026-10-13 16:48 retreat-direction variants tried and removed: stepping toward the neighbor with the most walkable tiles (to avoid pockets) saved slightly more potions on the arena spot (8.4 vs 8.7 per clear) but gave the evolved policy 1 death in 100 held-out clears and wrecked open-field kiting clears (469 s), so step_away stays; refuge-seeking (fewest attack spots) was worse than the cavebot; neither is kept in code; 2026-10-13 17:23 the threat to step away from is now the adjacent foe with the smallest (x, y) in both the simulator and the live probe (was slot order vs spectator order, which sent the two in different directions in clutter); with it, the arena-spot evolution picks heal 132 + 31/attacker and retreat at 1, 848 potions vs 1362 with 0 deaths but 93.4 s vs 88.5 s per held-out clear: a trade inside the 15% time cap, no longer Pareto dominance (test renamed on_the_arena_spot_evolved_retreating_cuts_potions_by_a_third_within_the_time_cap); live arena rows after ~17:22 use the deterministic threat; 2026-10-13 17:28 with the deterministic threat: in the respawning room-and-corridor hunt, evolved rules (ReachFirst chosen on training, heal 223 + 3/attacker, retreat at 3) dominate the fully tuned cavebot on 100 held-out seeds, 952 kills / -286330 gp vs 942 / -477181 (room_and_corridor_evolved_retreat_dominates_the_fully_tuned_cavebot); open field: kiting 825 / -188256 and the warm network 842 / -203888 trade kills for profit, no dominance; open-field clears at heal 70: kiting deaths 20 -> 5 of 20; 2026-10-13 23:58 richer learner built: a 12-gene scored policy (heal base, heal per attacker, retreat at; a policy.Scored aim over current/reach/health/distance; a terrain.footing step score over away/room/crowd/alignment/diagonal, ranking step_away's own ordered candidates so zero weights reproduce it), searched by search.evolve_vector (antithetic pairs, per-gene spreads); its start genome reproduces the evolved rules exactly (852 potions, 0 deaths, 91.8 s on 100 held-out arena-spot clears); 640 evaluations found no improvement and hand variants are all worse or equal (-40150 start vs -40800..-44850), so on this scenario with this simulator the evolved rules are a local optimum; lesson on the way: retreat direction matters enormously (a different tie-break moved 852 -> 1431 potions), and diagonal retreats are good because the cyclops must then step diagonally too (x3); 2026-10-14 00:21 harder scenario: a potion budget per clear (camp.potion_budget; unlimited by default, so pins are unchanged); at 6 potions the arena-spot clear is lethal (100 held-out clears: cavebot 203 deaths, evolved rules 74), at 12 the rules no longer die; training the 12-gene scored policy there: without a time cap it learned to stall (0 deaths, every clear timed out); with the cap and one fixed training set it overfit (training -13.6%, held out worse); with a fresh 500-seed block per generation (search.evolve_vector_resampled, seeds from 10001, away from held-out) and 500 held-out clears it clears 5.6% faster (88.8 vs 94.1 s, SE ~0.5 s) with deaths within noise (355 vs 362) and the same potions: real but below the 5% cost bar of the stop rule; conclusion so far: with heal/retreat/target/step choices, well-tuned rules leave little to learn on this scenario; 2026-10-14 01:00 mixed arena camp (1 cyclops + 2 trolls, pack_sim mixed_arena_camp_*): held-out 100 clears, tuned cavebot heal 70 = 80 potions/1 death/37.9 s; cyclops-tuned rules 204/0/40.8 s; same rules counting only standing attackers (policy.temperate, kind via flee health) 158/0/40.5 s; evolved kind-blind 74/2/37.8 s, evolved kind-aware 76/2/37.8 s (per-standing heal evolved to 0); nothing dominates the cavebot, kind-aware healing has no headroom with one cyclops; 2026-10-14 07:31 pack v2 candidate probed (respawning cyclops + 2 trolls, RoomAndCorridor, 5-min hunts, cavebot at heal 100/200, 100 hunts): 40+ potions never bind (53 kills, 0 deaths, about -1.2k gp per hunt); 20 potions run dry and the knight dies 2.8 times per hunt (-40k gp); the only decision that matters is when to leave as supplies run low, which a one-parameter rule (leave below k potions) captures, so the scenario would saturate the same way; not built; conclusion across scenarios: at this abstraction (one melee character, one camp) tuned threshold rules leave nothing for learning; learning needs route choice, partial observability or multi-character play; 2026-10-14 08:31 on the real radius-30 arena map, evolution on 100 training clears chooses ReachFirst, heal 154 + 26 per attacker, retreat at 2 (the live retreat competitor's design) and dominates the tuned cavebot on 100 held-out clears: 909 vs 1309 potions, 0 vs 1 deaths, 88.1 vs 88.6 s (was a trade on the boxed map: 852 potions at 91.8 s); mixed camp: cyclops-tuned rules 167 potions at 58.8 s (wander further), standing-only variant 155 at 40.5 s, the tuned cavebot (80, 1 death, 37.9 s) still undominated", Feature, P1, D5, 100u32, Done, "TIBIA-FAIR-CHASING-BASELINE", "tibia,policy,arena,ml", "current", "current")
