inl note_of_eoie_one_call_2 () = Note ("EOIE-ONE-CALL", 2u32, "2026-10-15T10:50Z", "main-thread", "06:50 groom: eoie help on 10-15: 6 verbs (status, patch, agile, bundle, proxy, help), 17 distinct agile subverbs, 70 proxy capabilities (unchanged since 10-14 08:23). No one-call verb landed yet; tonight's agent work still went through pwsh for reads, edits and job control (e.g. the renewal and harness runs). Still the root of the EOIE-READ-QUERY/EDIT-BATCH/JOBS/TABLE-QUERY/REPO-STATE cluster.")
inl note_of_eoie_one_call_3 () = Note ("EOIE-ONE-CALL", 3u32, "2026-10-17T18:23Z", "bundle-wisdom", "14:25 provenance (see BUNDLE-WISDOM note 3): the rule is older than commit 9a5c34c. Downloads\\espiral-comum\\bootstrap_llm_v631-alpha1.toml (2026-01-08) [eoie] parallelism: 'if you feel you would run 3+ commands in a row, try to collapse them into 1'; batch 'patch, rebuild and bench in a single call'. Same lineage: bootstrap_llm_eoie.toml (01-05) allowed a 2-stage pipe with persisted intermediates.")
inl note_of_eoie_one_call_4 () = Note ("EOIE-ONE-CALL", 4u32, "2026-10-18T17:01Z", "claude", "2026-10-18 13:01 eoie lane handoff from the main lane: (a) every verb should take its operands from a file or stdin (--from FILE or -) so arguments with double quotes survive PowerShell native-argument splitting; seen twice today (a backlog title rename had to go through Python subprocess lists, and my own notes avoid quote characters for this reason); (b) agile check prints lease and catalog lines on every call, too noisy for one-call use: quiet by default, details behind a flag. Not eoie lane: scripts/publish.ps1, .github/workflows/gh-pages.yml with actions.lock, /dist/ in .gitignore belong to the main lane TREE-PAGES-EVERY-REPO work; coordinate before touching them")
inl note_of_eoie_one_call_5 () = Note ("EOIE-ONE-CALL", 5u32, "2026-10-18T19:53Z", "claude", "2026-10-18 15:53 landed (finding a): eoie --args-from <file|-> relaunches eoie with one argument per line (CR stripped, remaining argv appended), routed before every verb in eoie_cli_model.argument_file (argument_source union, argument_text nominal); quotes and commas survive; e2e from file and stdin; Spiral test 1/1")
inl note_of_eoie_one_call_6 () = Note ("EOIE-ONE-CALL", 6u32, "2026-10-21T01:38Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-20 21:38 eoie lane, backlog refactor (user: spend 10 minutes refactoring the eoie backlog): the agile model has no parent field, and 11 items listed this epic as a dependency, so none of them could ever be ready while the epic is open: EOIE-JOBS (the only P0) never appeared in agile next and the epic itself scored 'unblocks=2324'. Convention now: an epic depends on its children (it is done when they are), children never depend on their epic. This epic's deps are its 11 children (EOIE-JOBS, EOIE-READ-QUERY, EOIE-EDIT-BATCH, EOIE-TABLE-QUERY, EOIE-REPO-STATE, EOIE-AGENT-GUIDE, EOIE-IDIOMATIC-CORE, EOIE-ONE-CALL-SPEC, EOIE-AGILE-BODY, EOIE-ZED-SWEEP, PS1-TO-SPIRAL). Also reversed or dropped: EOIE-IDIOMATIC-CORE now waits on EOIE-STYLE-CHECKPOINT (the rewrite follows the style decision, not the reverse); EOIE-SPIRAL-TESTS and EOIE-MEMORY-LINKS no longer wait on unrelated items. State backup before the edit: scratchpad eoie-backups/agile.spi.*.before-refactor.")
inl note_of_eoie_one_call_7 () = Note ("EOIE-ONE-CALL", 7u32, "2026-10-21T11:07Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-21 07:07 eoie team, day-run rules until 21:00 (user 07:05: decide everything myself, by tests, benchmarks and scratch source backups; check every hour that no lane waits on a hook). R1 release order: r26d installs -> runner jobs-home fix (-CleanOld) -> snapshot -> r28 (14 slices, 60-min test budget). R2 a failed release gets diagnosed, fixed and relaunched at once; a slice that fails its notebooks twice is pulled (backed up in scratch) and the rest ship. R3 before any release, a slice's notebook files run on the scratch tree when the dev driver can filter files; previews alone missed test-block type errors three times. R4 next work = eoie-team items by WSJF; a design trade-off is built both ways and decided by measured lines/ms, ties to the smaller, reversible one. R5 every generated file <= 1000 lines; no new rust_global. R6 git stays the user's (no stage/commit). R7 hourly hook check at :13 (cron), noted on AUTONOMOUS-STOP-GATE.")
inl note_of_eoie_one_call_8 () = Note ("EOIE-ONE-CALL", 8u32, "2026-10-23T06:46Z", "tibia-lane", "Use case from tibia-lane (2026-10-23 02:4x): appending a sentence to an existing note takes three calls today (agile show, cut out the note text, agile note-edit with the full text), while edit already has title-append. Tonight the cut went wrong once: a regex on the plain show output swallowed the two following notes, and note-edit wrote all three into one note. It was caught from the byte count (1100 to 3439) and repaired with a second note-edit. show --json would have made the cut safe, but it is still read-modify-write. Requested one-call shape: agile note-edit <root> <id> <n> --append <text> (or a note-append verb), appending with one space. Expected: text_bytes grows by exactly the appended length plus one, and no other note changes.")
inl note_of_eoie_one_call_9 () = Note ("EOIE-ONE-CALL", 9u32, "2026-10-23T18:29Z", "eoie", "14:29 eoie: tibia's 06:46 use case drafted (scratch note-append/apply-note-append.ps1, release M): agile note-edit <root> <id> <n> <text> [--as <author>] [--append]. --append joins the text onto the stored note with one space in the same locked write, so adding a sentence is one call instead of show, cut and note-edit. A typed note_change (ReplaceText | AppendText) plus an order-free option fold replace the positional --as check; the author rule is unchanged; the touched integer parse moves to lib convert.try_convert; agile_subcommand's note-edit shape shows [--append]. CLI test in notes_cli: append a sentence, show reads 'first finding. second sentence.', and an unknown option leaves state unchanged. All 7 M slices apply on a fresh post-L tree.")
inl note_of_eoie_one_call_10 () = Note ("EOIE-ONE-CALL", 10u32, "2026-10-26T00:14Z", "eoie", "20:13 eoie: notes_cli test note_edit_append_adds_a_sentence_to_the_stored_note fails on the release candidate: note-edit's usage advertises --append and --as, but its route refuses 7 operands (root id number text --append --as author). Same class as the show arity gap (EOIE-SHOW-NOTE-FILTER note of the same time); fix after KIT-RENAME, tracked meanwhile as a known failure in the release notebook gate.")
inl note_of_eoie_one_call_11 () = Note ("EOIE-ONE-CALL", 11u32, "2026-10-26T06:41Z", "eoie", "02:40 eoie: route fix PROVEN on the worker node. Draft kit scratch clean\\kit-rename\\route-arity.do: AgileShow and AgileNoteEdit route through agile_with_root, so each verb's own parser validates its options (show refuses with 'options are --json, --last <n> or --grep <word>'; note-edit prints its full usage). Candidate: renamed tree plus the kit_named length fix plus this draft; kit_adoption and kit_agile_mutation regenerated; debug build; this box's compiler (core CDD68DC3EE829A67); SPIRAL_LIB from a synced spiral mirror. Strict verdict (executed above 0, passed equal to executed, unsupported 0; positive control kit_lcov executed 2, passed 2): 10/10 agile and notes notebooks pass, notes_cli included, so show_reads_the_newest_or_matching_notes_with_their_numbers and note_edit_append_adds_a_sentence_to_the_stored_note both pass. The help shape string is deliberately unchanged: spec/one-call.md line 296 searches for the literal shape, and README and the subcommand test pin it, so the shape text is its own coordinated change. Lands right after KIT-RENAME; then both rows leave the release known-failures table.")
inl note_of_eoie_one_call_12 () = Note ("EOIE-ONE-CALL", 12u32, "2026-10-29T23:23Z", "main", "10-29 19:23 main, fill-all-task-fields: estimate set to 6135 min by the epic rule (the sum of each open child's estimate x (100 - progress)%, all children estimated): EOIE-JOBS 18, EOIE-READ-QUERY 54, EOIE-EDIT-BATCH 45, EOIE-IDIOMATIC-CORE 1800, EOIE-ONE-CALL-SPEC 78, EOIE-ZED-SWEEP 60, PS1-TO-SPIRAL 4080; the other 4 children are Done. Re-derive it when a child's estimate or progress moves. Epic progress stays at its owner's value.")
inl note_of_eoie_one_call_13 () = Note ("EOIE-ONE-CALL", 13u32, "2026-11-01T13:15Z", "kit", "11-01 09:1x kit groom: dropped EOIE-ZED-SWEEP from this umbrella's deps. It is Paused indefinitely (resume trigger: Zed's threads.db changes; unchanged since 10-12), and an umbrella waiting on an indefinite pause can never close. It stays related: when Zed is used again the sweep adds adoption evidence here.")
inl eoie_read_query () = Task ("EOIE-READ-QUERY", "one-call read and search for every agent: files, line ranges, globs, regex with context, many files at once, rayon-parallel, build/deps/target and junction cycles skipped by default, bounded output, --json; replaces ~22.3k file reads and ~13.2k searches (Get-Content/ReadAllText/Select-String/Get-ChildItem and native Read/Grep/Glob/read_file/grep); 48% of shell calls chain 3+ operations, so read, search, filter and count must compose inside one call; source: transcript sweep 2026-10-13 22:13 over ~45k tool calls (claude 41.6k, codex 2.0k, opencode 1.1k, grok 0.2k; zed 8 threads parsed, tool shape not yet recognized); report in main's job tmp/sweep/intent-report.txt", Story, P1, D5, 70u32, Planned, "", "kit,one-call,adoption,team:kit", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_eoie_read_query () = Planning ("EOIE-READ-QUERY", Minutes 180u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_eoie_read_query_1 () = Note ("EOIE-READ-QUERY", 1u32, "2026-10-14T10:54Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-14 06:54 reprioritized P0 -> P1 with EOIE-EDIT-BATCH, EOIE-JOBS, EOIE-ONE-CALL-SPEC: the formatter comes before further eoie work, and the restart style waits for EOIE-STYLE-CHECKPOINT")
inl note_of_eoie_read_query_2 () = Note ("EOIE-READ-QUERY", 2u32, "2026-10-14T12:25Z", "lane-a", "08:25 lane-a: measured rust_global in eoie/src (392 hits): parallel-search 4.3 s (re-runs serial 3.5 s for parity), fs-search 0.8 s; both print whole lines unbounded (hits of 4 KB+). Spec cases 3,7,8,10,18 = 3,507 recorded calls, all red.")
inl note_of_eoie_read_query_3 () = Note ("EOIE-READ-QUERY", 3u32, "2026-10-15T10:50Z", "main-thread", "06:50 groom: unchanged; tonight's reads were Grep/Read tools plus pwsh Select-String. No new measurement.")
inl note_of_eoie_read_query_4 () = Note ("EOIE-READ-QUERY", 4u32, "2026-10-15T10:55Z", "main-thread", "06:55 groom: related tool friction found while grooming: PowerShell splits a note argument with embedded double quotes (eoie reported 6 operands). A read/query verb that takes its arguments from a file or stdin avoids that class.")
inl note_of_eoie_read_query_5 () = Note ("EOIE-READ-QUERY", 5u32, "2026-10-17T18:23Z", "bundle-wisdom", "14:23 from the bundles (BUNDLE-WISDOM note 8): treat EPIPE (output piped into head) as a normal stop, not a panic; one root sentinel '.' accepted by every read/search/list/manifest verb; native per-file line counts (the most repeated gap, wc in about 15 rounds); one source classifier (own/generated/vendor/reference/evidence), because source-stats counted evidence .spi as own code and disagreed with source-topology on crate max; bounded tail and numbered slices; help schemas generated from the dispatch registry.")
inl note_of_eoie_read_query_6 () = Note ("EOIE-READ-QUERY", 6u32, "2026-10-18T15:38Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 11:38 main-thread evidence: editing an agile title that contains double quotes (KINO-SOTA) from PowerShell splits the argument; the 21-title rename went through Python subprocess argument lists instead. Arguments from a file or stdin (--from <file>/-) would remove the whole class for every verb.")
inl note_of_eoie_read_query_7 () = Note ("EOIE-READ-QUERY", 7u32, "2026-10-18T23:35Z", "claude", "eoie read/lines/grep e2e (lab/EOIE-READ-QUERY/read-e2e.ps1) 5/5 on build 1 (19:12), including the receipt line and the missing-file failure; receipt crate tests green in the build 1 suite (eoie_receipt 1, read_query model 1).")
inl note_of_eoie_read_query_8 () = Note ("EOIE-READ-QUERY", 8u32, "2026-10-20T12:20Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-20 08:20 eoie lane groom: most of this item exists (read <path>[:from-to] [--max-lines], lines <glob>, grep <root> <needle> [--glob --context --max --ignore-case], plus eoie do search/read/files/head/tail), rayon-parallel, build/deps/target skipped, bounded output; the 0% here was stale. Missing was --json: grep ignored it and read took it for a path. Now in source: grep and read --json, by splitting the two print-everything host blobs into thin host loaders plus Spiral renderers (search.spi, slice.spi); the text form must stay byte-identical, checked by a parity step in the next release run (old vs new eoie on 4 grep and 4 read queries) and notebook src/eoie_read_query/grep_cli.spi.")
inl note_of_eoie_read_query_9 () = Note ("EOIE-READ-QUERY", 9u32, "2026-10-21T01:38Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-20 21:38 eoie lane, status catch-up: grep and read moved from rust_global into Spiral with --json (release 19, 10-20, parity-checked against the old text output on 8 real queries); remaining from the title: globs and many-file reads in one call, regex context options as typed queries.")
inl note_of_eoie_read_query_10 () = Note ("EOIE-READ-QUERY", 10u32, "2026-10-21T11:28Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-21 07:28 eoie team: the quoted-argument friction in notes 4 and 6 is covered by 'eoie --args-from <file|->' (eoie_cli_model/argument_file.spi: one argument per line, relaunch without a shell, so quotes survive). Checked live just now: a plain 'agile note' with embedded double quotes failed with 6 operands, and the same note through --args-from landed. Remaining scope from the title: globs and many-file reads in one call.")
inl note_of_eoie_read_query_11 () = Note ("EOIE-READ-QUERY", 11u32, "2026-10-21T13:56Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-21 09:56 eoie team: globs in read drafted for r30 (scratch apply-read-globs.ps1, 3 base-hash guards). lines already expanded a one-level glob inside its print blob; that expansion is now one glob_paths primitive in eoie_read_query/model.spi (same rule: one * in the last path segment, files only, sorted, a plain path passes through) used by both verbs. read src/x/*.spi:1-20 reads every match with the same range in one call; a glob matching nothing reports one unreadable entry (exit 2), like a missing file. lines' blob now only reads the paths it is given, so its output must stay byte-identical: the r30 release job adds a lines parity step (old vs new binary on a glob, plain paths and misses). CLI test read_and_lines_expand_a_glob_in_one_call in grep_cli.spi. Recursive ** globs are in the same slice as their own primitive (walks below the ** prefix, skips target, .git and node_modules, matches the file name against the mask after **, sorted, / separators), chosen in Spiral by the pattern shape so the flat rule lines relied on stays as it was; the CLI test covers notes/**/*.spi. The title scope is then complete once r30 installs.")
inl note_of_eoie_read_query_12 () = Note ("EOIE-READ-QUERY", 12u32, "2026-10-21T22:33Z", "eoie-lane", "18:33 eoie team: r30's read globs are in the r30-r33 release (queued for a heavy slot, install ~19:55). r35 adds two fixes found by dogfooding today (EOIE-GREP-FILE-ROOT): grep on a single file (a file root searched 0 files with exit 0) and read <file>:-N for the last N lines (':-3' silently printed the whole file). read_query.rs is 993 generated lines after both: the next change to this crate splits it first. After r30 installs: progress 80; Done when r35 ships and the one-call guide points agents at read/grep instead of Get-Content/Select-String (still the largest habits: 664 and 595 calls in the eoie lane on 10-21).")
inl note_of_eoie_read_query_13 () = Note ("EOIE-READ-QUERY", 13u32, "2026-10-21T22:46Z", "eoie-lane", "18:46 eoie team: drafted the read_query split for r36 (scratch apply-grep-split.ps1, needs r35): grep moves into a new crate eoie_grep_query (search.spi moved whole; grep.spi takes search_options, search_receipt and the grep branch from read.spi; usage text still comes from eoie_read_query.model through the package import), VerbGrep routes to eoie_grep_run, read_query keeps read/lines (slice.spi takes json_text and stdout_text). Workspace member, eoie-cli dependency and a Cargo.lock entry inserted in sorted order. This makes room for the next read/grep features (e.g. grep --regex; the needle is literal today) without crossing the 1000-line gate. Preview chained (r36-early on top of the r35 check tree).")
inl note_of_eoie_read_query_14 () = Note ("EOIE-READ-QUERY", 14u32, "2026-10-21T23:07Z", "eoie-lane", "19:07 eoie team: r36 slice 2 drafted (scratch apply-grep-regex.ps1, after the split): eoie grep <root> <needle> --regex. A needle_kind union (LiteralNeedle | CaselessNeedle | PatternNeedle caseless) picks a line_matcher primitive (an Arc<dyn Fn(&str) -> bool + Send + Sync> for the rayon walk); hits_found takes the matcher instead of needle + ignore_case, so the matching rule lives in Spiral and the blob only applies it. An invalid pattern is refused before the walk ('grep: invalid --regex pattern: ...', exit 2). Usage updated in read_query and the command spec; grep_cli test for alternation and a bad pattern; regex dependency + lock entry for eoie-grep-query. Preview and notebook typecheck chained.")
inl note_of_eoie_read_query_15 () = Note ("EOIE-READ-QUERY", 15u32, "2026-10-21T23:09Z", "eoie-lane", "19:09 r36 slice 2 checked: preview exit 0 (grep_query.rs 473, read_query.rs 632, command_spec.rs 962), read_query model and command spec notebooks pass; grep_cli's new regex test fails only at runtime against the pre-r36 debug binary, like the other new CLI tests.")
inl note_of_eoie_read_query_16 () = Note ("EOIE-READ-QUERY", 16u32, "2026-10-24T02:23Z", "main", "2026-10-23 22:2x main: consolidated pendencies covered here (EOIE-CONSOLIDATED-INTAKE, class a): READ-1 (root sentinel, absolute roots), READ-2 (count-only, resume, and an absence receipt with a positive control, per grep-brace-glob-silent), READ-3 (EPIPE), FS-8 (non-UTF-8 paths and lossy reads), CENSUS-1 (all-language census with origin classes). Spec text: Downloads/eoie-consolidated/agile_eoie.md.")
inl note_of_eoie_read_query_17 () = Note ("EOIE-READ-QUERY", 17u32, "2026-10-24T23:43Z", "eoie", "19:43 Provisional estimate 180m from the grooming rule (EOIE-AGILE-CHECK-FIELDS note): D5 at 65%. Replace it with milestone sizing.")
inl note_of_eoie_read_query_18 () = Note ("EOIE-READ-QUERY", 18u32, "2026-10-27T16:04Z", "kit", "12:0x kit: pendencies checked on the installed kit.exe. READ-1 holds for grep: a '.' root and an absolute root both search (files 6 searched, 2 matched). READ-3 was still open: 'kit grep <src> fn' piped into a reader that closes after one line panicked with 'failed printing to stdout: The pipe is being closed. (os error 232)'. Fix: kit_cli/main.spi installs one panic hook before run that ends the process with exit 0 on a stdout-printing panic and prints any other panic as before (clean/kit-rename/cli-closed-pipe.do, one host expression; kit.rs 971, +1). Proven in a harness with the same print pattern: without the hook the identical panic reproduces (positive control); with it, stderr is empty and the exit is clean. Queued as next-train step 5d. Left here: READ-2 (budget-aware search: depth, count-only, absence receipt), FS-8 (non-UTF-8 paths), CENSUS-1, plus --limit/--offset pagination from READ-3's text.")
inl note_of_eoie_read_query_19 () = Note ("EOIE-READ-QUERY", 19u32, "2026-10-27T16:05Z", "kit", "12:0x kit: READ-2 re-measured. Count-only already works as 'grep <root> <needle> --max 0': only the receipt prints, with full counts ('files 6 searched 2 matched matches 5 shown 0'). The absence receipt exists: an absent needle reports 'files 6 searched 0 matched matches 0', and the files-searched count is the positive control that the walk ran (with the existing literal-needle hint against the silent-glob trap). Still missing from READ-2: --depth, and shallow listing by name for very large trees.")
inl note_of_eoie_read_query_20 () = Note ("EOIE-READ-QUERY", 20u32, "2026-10-27T21:25Z", "kit", "kit 17:2x, where --depth (READ-2) has to go: the walk lives in parallel_search_contract/search_terminal.spi, a large legacy raw-Rust terminal with three hand-written fs::read_dir recursions (lines ~77, ~147, ~246). Under the AGENTS contract, touching it means migrating it, so --depth is a slice of its own: move the walk into the rust_std_fs boundary as one primitive (a bounded directory listing with a depth, reparse points skipped, as tonight's scratch-archive fix does) and keep the search logic in Spiral. Then a depth test plus a spec case. Shallow listing by name for very large trees falls out of the same primitive (depth 1). Not started; ranked after TRAIN's post-install work.")
inl note_of_eoie_read_query_21 () = Note ("EOIE-READ-QUERY", 21u32, "2026-10-30T23:42Z", "main", "10-30 19:4x main R3 groom (BACKLOG-DANGLING-1030): owner kit; next step per its last note: READ-2 --depth as its own slice, moving the walk from search_terminal.spi's three raw fs::read_dir recursions into one rust_std_fs bounded-listing primitive.")
inl note_of_eoie_read_query_22 () = Note ("EOIE-READ-QUERY", 22u32, "2026-11-01T03:36Z", "kit", "10-31 23:4x kit groom (main 23:33, user: cadence/drift): Active -> PLANNED. No note in over 12 h, so nobody is working it this week; Planned keeps it in agile next at its priority (never auto-Paused, R3). First step when picked = the next step in its latest note.")
inl eoie_edit_batch () = Task ("EOIE-EDIT-BATCH", "one-call multi-file edit: inline anchored replacements with expected counts, whole-file writes and apply_patch-style hunks, validated, rehearsed and applied atomically with before/after hashes in one receipt, LF preserved; replaces ~16.1k edits/writes (.Replace+WriteAllText, python re.sub, Edit/apply_patch/search_replace/write) and today's compiler pack scripts; source: transcript sweep 2026-10-13 22:13 over ~45k tool calls (claude 41.6k, codex 2.0k, opencode 1.1k, grok 0.2k; zed 8 threads parsed, tool shape not yet recognized); report in main's job tmp/sweep/intent-report.txt", Story, P1, D6, 100u32, Done, "", "kit,one-call,adoption,team:kit", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_eoie_edit_batch () = Planning ("EOIE-EDIT-BATCH", Minutes 300u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_eoie_edit_batch_1 () = Note ("EOIE-EDIT-BATCH", 1u32, "2026-10-14T12:24Z", "lane-a", "08:24 lane-a: spec gap found: one-call.md anchors are all one line, but compiler pack scripts use multi-line indented anchors. Drafted case 34 (+35 companion) with a <<< >>> block string, copy-to-scratch and hash: lane-a\\one-call\\case-34-multiline-anchors.md")
inl note_of_eoie_edit_batch_2 () = Note ("EOIE-EDIT-BATCH", 2u32, "2026-10-15T10:50Z", "main-thread", "06:50 groom: evidence from tonight: compiler patches were content-anchored pwsh scripts (unify_codegen_fsharp.ps1, unify_main.ps1, r4_deferred.ps1) with multi-line anchors and throw-on-mismatch. These are the multi-line anchor cases the spec lacks.")
inl note_of_eoie_edit_batch_3 () = Note ("EOIE-EDIT-BATCH", 3u32, "2026-10-17T18:23Z", "bundle-wisdom", "14:25 from the bundles (compiler alpha3006, samples alpha705, brzozowski alpha54, workspace alpha2891; details in BUNDLE-WISDOM note 7): OLD/NEW payloads from files or stdin (argv E2BIG at 497 KiB); an exact-N mode for repeated matches; row-local idempotency (the old apply-spec treated a row as done when its replacement text existed anywhere else in the file); refuse an empty write over a non-empty file without expected-old-hash (a source was wiped to 0 bytes); content-addressed inverse-patch receipts and restore --expect-hash; a text-only plan must not need the compiler; hash-checked append; --parents. Spec cases worth adding: the row-local idempotency trap and the empty-payload trap.")
inl note_of_eoie_edit_batch_4 () = Note ("EOIE-EDIT-BATCH", 4u32, "2026-10-17T20:28Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-17 16:28 evidence from a long agent day: most edits were anchored replacements with an expected count of 1, written as ad-hoc PowerShell helpers; failures came from line-ending changes (a WriteAllLines rewrite turned a whole LF file into CRLF), here-strings that dropped the final newline (gluing two lines), and anchors that matched zero times silently. Needed: preserve each file's line endings and encoding, refuse whole-file EOL changes unless asked, report per-anchor match counts.")
inl note_of_eoie_edit_batch_5 () = Note ("EOIE-EDIT-BATCH", 5u32, "2026-10-18T05:58Z", "claude", "(2026-10-18 01:58 -04:00, Claude fc1568a6) Evidence: the harness->matrix/live/cli_driver rename touched 5 repos, ~45 files, 251 regex replacements plus 4 file/folder moves. text-replace is one file and one literal pair per call (hundreds of calls), so it ran through a scratch Python plan (ordered regex rules per file glob, LF/CRLF kept, plain moves so nothing is staged, dry run first). The verb wants exactly that: a plan of (glob, exclude, ordered regex subs) + moves across roots, with a dry-run listing per file.")
inl note_of_eoie_edit_batch_6 () = Note ("EOIE-EDIT-BATCH", 6u32, "2026-10-19T00:33Z", "claude", "eoie do (EOIE-EDIT-BATCH surface) now covers edits + runs in one call (case 24 green on build 6). Edit failure leaves files untouched and starts no run; a rejected run statement is caught before edits are written. Checked by hand 20:32 (bad anchor -> exit 1, no run; unsupported statement -> exit 2, nothing written).")
inl note_of_eoie_edit_batch_7 () = Note ("EOIE-EDIT-BATCH", 7u32, "2026-10-21T11:39Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-21 07:39 eoie team: multi-line anchors without escaping, decided by measurement (rule R4). eoie do statements accept raw blocks: <<< then a newline opens, the first line whose text starts with >>> closes, and text after >>> keeps tokenizing, so 'replace once <<< ... >>> with <<< ... >>>' works with no quote or backslash escaping. Two builds: (A) a typed Spiral tokenizer replacing the host tokenizer: do_program.rs 806 -> 1209 lines, over the 1000 gate; (B) the same block rule as a branch of the existing one-line tokenizer: no new census line, crate near 806. Chose B (A would need its own crate with an encoded token string across the boundary, which the census counts as drift). Scratch apply-block-strings.ps1, r28 order 15; notebook block_strings_carry_raw_lines_without_escapes (CRLF inside blocks becomes LF, an unclosed block is an unterminated string, an empty block is an empty string).")
inl note_of_eoie_edit_batch_8 () = Note ("EOIE-EDIT-BATCH", 8u32, "2026-10-21T14:17Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-21 10:17 eoie team: progress 0 -> 55 from what ships (checked in source, eoie_do_edit): anchored replacements across files with expected counts (once|all|n times), dry-run, a failed anchor writes nothing and starts no run, edit then build in one call (case 24), before/after sha256 in the receipt, CRLF files keep CRLF in replacements (host.spi line 35), writes staged and applied together through temporaries; r28 adds raw <<< >>> blocks for multi-line anchors. Left: (1) apply_patch-style hunks, (2) a multi-root plan of (glob, excludes, ordered regex subs) plus moves with a per-file dry-run listing (the 10-18 rename evidence), (3) refuse an empty whole-file write over a non-empty file without an expected-old hash, (4) spec cases for the row-local idempotency and empty-payload traps.")
inl note_of_eoie_edit_batch_9 () = Note ("EOIE-EDIT-BATCH", 9u32, "2026-10-21T15:33Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-21 11:33 eoie team: left item 3 drafted for r31 (scratch apply-empty-write-guard.ps1): fs-write's typed proxy_write_action gains RejectEmptyPayload; replacing a non-empty file with an empty payload is refused unless --expect-hash is given, while creating an empty file or replacing an empty one stays allowed. Notebook an_empty_payload_never_wipes_a_nonempty_file. eoie do has no whole-file writes, so fs-write is the only path that needs it. Applies cleanly on scratch copies; compiles wait for tibia's live blocks.")
inl note_of_eoie_edit_batch_10 () = Note ("EOIE-EDIT-BATCH", 10u32, "2026-10-21T15:56Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-21 11:56 eoie team: r31 drafts cover left items 1, 3 and 4, and all are previewed and notebook-checked on r31-tree. (1) eoie do accepts patch <<< apply_patch hunks >>> inside an edit block. Each hunk becomes one replace-once rule (context plus removed lines as the anchor, context plus added lines as the replacement), so validation, all-or-nothing writes and hash receipts are shared. Stray lines such as *** Begin Patch, no-op hunks and anchorless pure insertions are refused. (3) fs-write refuses an empty payload over a non-empty file without --expect-hash, and eoie do refuses edits that would empty a file. (4) spec cases 35-37 plus notebooks; the row-local idempotency case already passes on the current binary. Generated sizes: do_program.rs 920, do_edit.rs 882, operations.rs 804. Left: item 2, a multi-root regex plan with moves. Progress stays 55 until r31 ships.")
inl note_of_eoie_edit_batch_11 () = Note ("EOIE-EDIT-BATCH", 11u32, "2026-10-21T20:35Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-21 16:35 eoie team: shipped in the combined r28+r29 release, installed 15:38 (one-call spec 34/34, all 67 notebooks green): raw <<< >>> blocks in eoie do, so multi-line anchors need no escaping.")
inl note_of_eoie_edit_batch_12 () = Note ("EOIE-EDIT-BATCH", 12u32, "2026-10-21T20:36Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-21 16:36 eoie team: excludes drafted for r34 (scratch apply-do-except.ps1; applies cleanly on r34-tree). 'edit src/**/*.spi except src/vendor/*,*_test.spi' keeps matching files out of a globbed edit, and the receipt's 'files n of m matched' counts after the exclusion. The model, parser, encoding and runner each change once; parser and CLI notebooks added. Moves remain: do_edit.rs and do_program.rs sit near 950 lines, so moves come with a crate split. Checks run after tibia's live block.")
inl note_of_eoie_edit_batch_13 () = Note ("EOIE-EDIT-BATCH", 13u32, "2026-10-21T20:39Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-21 16:39 eoie team: moves drafted for r34 (scratch apply-do-move.ps1; applies cleanly on r34-tree after the excludes slice). 'move <path> to <path>' joins the eoie do write family (append, copy): it refuses an existing target, creates parent folders, renames, and falls back to copy-and-remove for a file across volumes. Absolute paths reach other roots, so the 10-18 rename case (regex rules over several roots plus 4 moves) becomes one program per root, or one call with absolute globs. With r33's pattern rules and absolute globs and r34's excludes and moves, all four left items are drafted. Progress moves when r33 and r34 ship.")
inl note_of_eoie_edit_batch_14 () = Note ("EOIE-EDIT-BATCH", 14u32, "2026-10-22T02:21Z", "x", "2026-10-21 22:21 x: this item holds eoie/lab/EOIE-DO-EDIT (apply-do-except.ps1 and apply-do-move.ps1, named in notes 12-13). The folder name doesn't match the item ID, so the 22:19 lab inventory saw it as an orphan. Ranked under LAB-SESSION-SWEEP note 6 (tag lab-held, priority floor). Renaming the folder to lab/EOIE-EDIT-BATCH, or landing it, is the eoie team's call.")
inl note_of_eoie_edit_batch_15 () = Note ("EOIE-EDIT-BATCH", 15u32, "2026-10-22T02:24Z", "eoie", "22:24 eoie team: lab/EOIE-DO-EDIT renamed to lab/EOIE-EDIT-BATCH (its holder, per x's lab-held sweep); holds apply-do-except.ps1 and apply-do-move.ps1, both slices of r34 (applied from the scratch copies by apply-r34.ps1). The folder goes when r34 lands in the tree.")
inl note_of_eoie_edit_batch_16 () = Note ("EOIE-EDIT-BATCH", 16u32, "2026-10-22T10:41Z", "eoie", "06:41 lab drained by the folder's own exit condition (note of 10-21 22:24: 'the folder goes when r34 lands in the tree'): r34 is in the live tree since release ABC applied it (do write has except and MovePath), the folder was untracked, and the only other mention is an older lab-handoff.ps1 copy map (writes into the folder, never reads it). lab/EOIE-EDIT-BATCH deleted; the slices also live on in the night carry's r34 history.")
inl note_of_eoie_edit_batch_17 () = Note ("EOIE-EDIT-BATCH", 17u32, "2026-10-22T10:42Z", "eoie", "06:42 correction to the previous note: the night carry does not hold the r34 slices. The applied code is what lives on (the live src under eoie_do_write), and the two slice scripts remain only in this session's scratch folder, which next sessions do not see. Nothing depends on them once the tree is committed.")
inl note_of_eoie_edit_batch_18 () = Note ("EOIE-EDIT-BATCH", 18u32, "2026-10-22T22:27Z", "eoie-lane", "18:27 dogfood measurement from today's release train: 81 apply-*.ps1 slices were written or changed in this session today. 55 are plain anchored replacements with count checks (the Swapped helper: exact-once anchor, nothing written on mismatch), 19 also create crates or files, and 7 are regex rewrites with callbacks. Probe on the installed binary: 'eoie do <root>' with 'edit a.spi expect files 1 replacements 1 / replace once \"<multi-line old>\" with \"<multi-line new>\"' applies a 2-line anchor in 2 ms with sha256 before and after; a missing anchor fails with 'anchor not found ... (0 matches, expected 1) / nothing written', exit 1. So the 55 plain slices could each be one do program file (root = the tree to patch, which replaces the -Src parameter). Gaps found: (1) no whole-file write ('write new.spi \"...\"' is rejected as an unsupported statement), needed by the 19 crate-creating slices; (2) no regex-with-captures rewrite for the 7 heal slices. Both are this item's open scope (whole-file writes, apply_patch hunks). Habit change for the next session: write new plain slices as do programs, not PowerShell.")
inl note_of_eoie_edit_batch_19 () = Note ("EOIE-EDIT-BATCH", 19u32, "2026-10-22T22:29Z", "eoie-lane", "18:29 escape rule measured for do programs: in a do string, \\\" is a quote, \\\\ is one backslash, and \\t becomes a real TAB byte (probe: \"a\\tb\\\\c\" wrote a<TAB>b\\c). Spiral source holds literal \\t and \\n escapes inside string literals, so pasting Spiral text into a do replace changes it silently unless every backslash is doubled. A text slice that carries Spiral escapes either doubles them (checked by a byte compare against the intended text) or stays a PowerShell slice. The H claim slice keeps its PowerShell form for that reason; the host-home slice (no escapes) moved to job-host-home.do.")
inl note_of_eoie_edit_batch_20 () = Note ("EOIE-EDIT-BATCH", 20u32, "2026-10-23T18:04Z", "eoie", "14:05 eoie (corrected 14:0x): dogfood from 10-23. I wrote about 12 PowerShell slices today, all carrying Spiral escapes, and 6 of them create crates. Before proposing anything I misread the state: the live do usage already offers raw lines between <<< and a >>> line (no escape processing, so note 19's escape problem is solved) and 'replace pattern' with \\ groups (gap 2 solved). The one real gap left is gap 1, whole-file writes and creates: the write family has append (one quoted line), copy and move, no write or create. Proposal: 'write <path> <<< ... >>>' (refusing an existing path unless 'overwrite') in the write family, so a crate-creating slice becomes one program. Habit for the next session: plain slices as do programs with <<< >>> payloads; only regex-callback heals stay PowerShell.")
inl note_of_eoie_edit_batch_21 () = Note ("EOIE-EDIT-BATCH", 21u32, "2026-10-23T18:06Z", "eoie", "14:06 eoie: path for the whole-file write, read from the code. eoie_do/decode.spi routes by verb (has_write: append|copy|move -> Writes -> eoie_do_write, 785 generated lines, room to grow); the statements are parsed earlier by eoie_do_program_compiled (eoie_do_program, 988 lines), which is where <<< >>> raw blocks are captured for replace, and which rejects unknown verbs as 'unsupported statement'. A 'write <path> <<< ... >>>' therefore needs (1) the verb in the parser's verb union (GE2's heal-do-verbs makes those a reflection union) with raw-block capture extended from replace to write, which needs a crate boundary first because do_program is at the gate; (2) a WriteFile (path, text, overwrite) step in eoie_do_write's write_step union with lib file_system writes; (3) 'write' added to has_write. Order: after GE2, starting with the do_program split.")
inl note_of_eoie_edit_batch_22 () = Note ("EOIE-EDIT-BATCH", 22u32, "2026-10-23T22:13Z", "eoie", "18:13 eoie: gap 1 (whole-file writes) drafted as scratch do-write-file/apply-do-write-file.ps1; dry run on the post-O tree is clean and idempotent; typecheck waits for tibia's live block to end. Correction to my 14:06 note: no crate boundary in eoie_do_program is needed. Its tokenizer already turns any <<< ... >>> block into a quoted token wherever it appears, and write-family verbs reach eoie_do_write as generic query steps, so do_program (988 lines) is untouched. The slice: eoie_do_verbs gains WriteFileVerb (word write), picked up by reflection. eoie_do_write gains a typed write_mode (CreateOnly | Overwrite), a WriteFile step and file_write_parsed (write <file> [overwrite] <<< text >>>; refuses a quoted file name or a missing block). write_run goes through lib file_system (file_exists, directory_get_parent plus create_dir_all, write_all_text), not new host blobs, and refuses an existing file without overwrite, naming the fix. The receipt reads 'files N written bytes B', and the do help line in direct_verbs lists the statement. A grammar test covers create, overwrite with an empty block and both refusals. Absence check: write is not a read verb (0 hits; control 'append' 3 hits on the same glob).")
inl note_of_eoie_edit_batch_23 () = Note ("EOIE-EDIT-BATCH", 23u32, "2026-10-23T23:32Z", "eoie", "19:32 eoie: dogfood proof that slices can be eoie do programs. Heal wave 1, written as a PowerShell slice tonight, was rewritten as one do program (scratch heal-wave-1/heal-wave-1.do: 4 edit blocks, 7 replace-once rules with <<< >>> raw payloads, so zero escaping, and expect files 1 replacements N on each block). Run with 'eoie do <tree> -' on an emulated post-N tree: ok files 4 replacements 7 in 15 ms, sha256 before and after per file, and all 4 files byte-identical to the PowerShell slice's result. A rerun refuses with 'anchor not found, nothing written', so it is fail-safe without hand-written guards. Plan, after tonight's train settles: fmt-rebase accepts .do slices (pipe the program into eoie do <work> -), and new plain slices are written as .do; crate-creating slices follow once release Q's do write installs. This removes the class of PowerShell slips I hit today (here-strings losing their newline, merged statements, backslash-quote).")
inl note_of_eoie_edit_batch_24 () = Note ("EOIE-EDIT-BATCH", 24u32, "2026-10-23T23:33Z", "eoie", "19:33 eoie: probe on the installed binary: 'append f.txt <<< ... >>>' already appends a multi-line raw block (3 lines, 27 bytes, the newline before it added), so 'append a test block at a file's end' needs no new verb. The real remaining gap for one-call slices is the mix rule: eoie_do_write's grammar refuses a program that has both edit and write statements ('edit and write statements cannot share one program yet; run them as two calls'). Most slices edit some files and append a test or create a file, so today they would take 2 calls. Gap 3: let one program run its edit blocks, then its write statements, as one guarded batch, with a single receipt counting files, replacements, appended and written. That makes a typical slice one call.")
inl note_of_eoie_edit_batch_25 () = Note ("EOIE-EDIT-BATCH", 25u32, "2026-10-23T23:34Z", "eoie", "19:34 eoie: gap 3 placement, read from eoie_do (decode.spi and run.spi, 270 generated lines, so there is room; eoie_do_edit sits at 976 and can't grow). program_decoded already classifies the compiled records by family (Runs becomes Edits when has_edit). Plan: a compiled_program case Split of edit_records * write_records when the program holds edit blocks and write-family queries. program_run delegates edits to eoie_do_edit first and, only on exit 0, the writes to eoie_do_write, returning the first nonzero code. v1 prints two receipts; a merged receipt can come later. Needed first: a record encoder in eoie_do_records (records_encoded of a subset: the ok header plus chosen records with their 30/31 separators), replacing do_write's host-blob record_alone. eoie_do/host.spi's stdin_text blob is the one lib console.read_to_end replaces (EOIE-AGILE-NOTE-STDIN step 3).")
inl note_of_eoie_edit_batch_26 () = Note ("EOIE-EDIT-BATCH", 26u32, "2026-10-23T23:35Z", "eoie", "19:35 eoie: train support drafted: scratch fmt-rebase/apply-do-slices.ps1 makes fmt-rebase run a slice whose name ends in .do as 'eoie do <stage work> -' (program on stdin), stopping the stage on a nonzero exit, while .ps1 slices run as before. Dry run on a copy of fmt-rebase.ps1 is idempotent and parses. Applied after tonight's train settles, since train scripts stay frozen while it runs; from then on, plain slices are written as .do.")
inl note_of_eoie_edit_batch_27 () = Note ("EOIE-EDIT-BATCH", 27u32, "2026-10-23T23:43Z", "eoie", "19:43 eoie: gap 3 drafted (scratch do-split/apply-do-split.ps1; dry run on the real post-P tree is clean and idempotent). eoie_do_records gains records_by_kinds, which keeps the ok header plus records whose kind is or isn't in a |-list, rejoined with the 30/31 separators (a host decoder, which is that package's rule). decode.spi: a program the family classifier calls Writes that also has edit records becomes Split of the edit set and the write set. The edit family, read from eoie_do_program compiled.spi, is edit, except, replace, pattern and show. My first draft filtered on 'edit' alone and would have sent the replace rules to the write crate; reading compiled.spi caught it before any compile. run.spi: Split runs eoie_do_edit on the edit set and, only on exit 0, eoie_do_write on the write set. Acceptance probe for its release: one program that edits a file, appends a test block and writes a new file, run once, then all three effects checked; and an edit whose anchor is missing must leave the append and write undone.")
inl note_of_eoie_edit_batch_28 () = Note ("EOIE-EDIT-BATCH", 28u32, "2026-10-23T23:54Z", "eoie", "19:54 eoie: second conversion: note-as-first as scratch note-as-first/note-as-first.do (2 replace-once rules) plus note-as-first-test.do (an append of the test block). On an emulated post-M tree, task_notes.spi is byte-identical to the PowerShell slice's result, and notes_cli.spi is equal except that do ends the file with a newline, the better form. Hazard found on the way, which argues for gap 3: run on a base that already had the change, the edit program refused correctly (files 0), but the separate append program still ran and duplicated the test block, while the PowerShell slice's 'already applied' guard skipped both. In one mixed program (do-split, release Q) a failed edit skips the writes, so the pair becomes fail-safe. Until Q installs, a .do slice that appends must be applied once per stage on a fresh raw copy, which is exactly what fmt-rebase does.")
inl note_of_eoie_edit_batch_29 () = Note ("EOIE-EDIT-BATCH", 29u32, "2026-10-24T00:00Z", "eoie", "20:00 eoie: third conversion probe, the file-creating part. log-sections' two new modules came from one do statement, copy <absolute scratch>/log-sections/blocks/section_*.spi to eoie_log_sequences (2 files, 13,035 bytes, 7 ms, both byte-identical to the drafts). So crate-creating slices are do programs today without waiting for write: copy the draft crate folder (its .spi files plus a pre-written Cargo.toml) into place, then edit blocks wire it in. Until do-split installs (release Q) that is 2 calls, copy then edits; after it, 1 call. The slice kinds I wrote this week, anchored edits, test appends and crate or file creation, are therefore all expressible as eoie do programs.")
inl note_of_eoie_edit_batch_30 () = Note ("EOIE-EDIT-BATCH", 30u32, "2026-10-24T00:18Z", "eoie", "20:18 gap 4 from tonight's lib-reuse heals: five heal programs (51 edit blocks) were generated by ad-hoc pwsh loops only because 'edit <glob> replace all' cannot say 'only where the file's package lists spiral-'; one generator forgot that check and targeted 2 packages without it (caught by an audit, reverted with an inverse program). Proposed grammar: edit <glob> where sibling <name> ~ <re> [expect files n replacements n], reusing the dirs-having matcher, so a lib swap is one statement and the precondition is part of the program instead of each generator. Also a 'skipped n (where)' count in the receipt so the excluded packages stay visible.")
inl note_of_eoie_edit_batch_31 () = Note ("EOIE-EDIT-BATCH", 31u32, "2026-10-24T00:38Z", "eoie", "20:38 gap 4 drafted as eoie do programs (scratch edit-where\\edit-where.do, 8 files 16 replacements, plus edit-where-scope.do appending the new module): header option 'where sibling <name> has line \"<text>\"' (exact trimmed line, no regex: lib has no portable matcher and package lists want exact lines). Model: union path_condition = AnyPath | SiblingHasLine name line, a field of edit_block. Parser branch + test, encoder 'where' record between except and the rules, decoder, files_run skips paths out of scope, do-split's kind list gains where. New module eoie_do_edit/scope.spi is lib only (sm'.last_index_of/substring/trim/lines, am'.any, file_system.file_exists/read_all_text, </>) with 2 tests; eoie_do_program and eoie_do_edit gain spiral-. Applies on the emulated post-train tree. Typecheck job-1792802262400 queued behind the post-block checks. Receipt skip count deferred: excluded files simply don't count toward expect.")
inl note_of_eoie_edit_batch_32 () = Note ("EOIE-EDIT-BATCH", 32u32, "2026-10-24T02:23Z", "main", "2026-10-23 22:2x main: consolidated pendencies covered here (EOIE-CONSOLIDATED-INTAKE, class a): EDIT-1 (stdin and file payloads; raw <<< >>> blocks shipped, whole-file writes drafted as gap 1), EDIT-2 (empty-write guard, r31 RejectEmptyPayload), EDIT-3 (create, delete and move in the same program; rollback after a failed gate goes to EOIE-DO-UNDO), EDIT-4 (byte-exact payloads; CRLF kept already), EDIT-8 (append with an expected-tail hash). Spec text: Downloads/eoie-consolidated/agile_eoie.md.")
inl note_of_eoie_edit_batch_33 () = Note ("EOIE-EDIT-BATCH", 33u32, "2026-10-24T02:50Z", "eoie", "22:50 two write-family gaps hit while drafting a crate split: (1) 'copy src/Cargo.toml to <dir>' copies 0 files and fails, because a plain file path is not treated as a one-file glob (src/Cargo.* works); (2) 'append <new-dir>/<file> <<< >>>' fails with os error 3 when the folder doesn't exist, so a program that creates a new package needs a mkdir outside eoie. Both should just work: copy accepts a file path, and append/write create missing parent folders and report them in the receipt (folders N created).")
inl note_of_eoie_edit_batch_34 () = Note ("EOIE-EDIT-BATCH", 34u32, "2026-10-24T23:43Z", "eoie", "19:43 Provisional estimate 300m from the grooming rule (EOIE-AGILE-CHECK-FIELDS note): D6 at 60%. Replace it with milestone sizing.")
inl note_of_eoie_edit_batch_35 () = Note ("EOIE-EDIT-BATCH", 35u32, "2026-10-25T14:29Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-25 10:29 tibia use case for one program mixing edit and write: a lib land that adds a module (6 files edited, 1 copied in) runs as land.do then copy.do ('edit and write statements cannot share one program yet'), so a failed copy needs the runner to undo the edits itself, and the undo program can't remove the copied file (no delete statement), the runner removes it by literal path. Wanted: one program for both, with the generated undo covering created files. Related bug: EOIE-DO-COPY-LITERAL.")
inl note_of_eoie_edit_batch_36 () = Note ("EOIE-EDIT-BATCH", 36u32, "2026-10-25T20:50Z", "eoie", "16:50 kit: gap hit 7 times today. Proving that a do program still applies to the current tree needs a copy of its targets plus a run against the copy, because dry-run exists only per edit block, not for a whole program. I wrote a scratch stand-in (clean\\do-dry.ps1: parse the edit/append/write targets, copy them to a temp root, run eoie do on the copy with the program on stdin, delete the copy). The real feature: a do --dry-run flag that applies the whole program in memory, prints the same per-file receipt (lines, sha256 before and after) and writes nothing. It runs safely during a tree lease, since it only reads. That would replace every per-draft dry-run script from today (standards, CI fixture, lint wiring, cases-find, 3 S stages).")
inl note_of_eoie_edit_batch_37 () = Note ("EOIE-EDIT-BATCH", 37u32, "2026-10-27T17:04Z", "kit", "kit (fc1568a6): whole-program dry run drafted and staged for the next release train as step 5e of next-train.ps1 (scratch clean/edit-batch/dry-run.do, one do program over 5 files). 'kit do <root> --dry-run <program>|-' chains every edit block in memory through the same pending-writes table the real run uses, so block 2 sees block 1's result. It prints the same per-file rows with would-be sha256 values, ends with 'dry run, nothing written', and commits nothing. Design: the flag travels as the encoded program's header word ('dry' in place of 'ok'). Every consumer skips record 0, so kit_do_edit needs no second export. Edit programs with run statements preview the edits and report 'runs not started'. Read and fact programs run unchanged. Write, git, job and lab programs, and edit+write splits, are refused with exit 2, because they have no in-memory form yet. Size: do_edit.rs is 981 -> 992 lines after migrating 7 legacy concat5 sites to ++# and inlining 2 single-use macro wrappers; kit_do_edit now references the lib (spiral-). Test: edit_cli.spi a_dry_run_chains_every_edit_in_memory_and_writes_nothing, added to TRAIN's candidate notebooks. Left on this item: dry runs for write programs (an in-memory write table), then mixed edit+write programs with delete and undo. Proof: kit built from the probe crates on the node (mirror dryrun, cargo build -p kit-cli), smoke clean/edit-batch/dry-run-smoke.ps1 11/11 pass: a chained dry run leaves the file untouched; stdin works; a write program is refused (exit 2) and writes nothing; a read program runs; an edit+run program previews and starts no run; a failing anchor still exits 1; a missing program prints usage. Each refusal or preview has a positive control that applies without --dry-run.")
inl note_of_eoie_edit_batch_38 () = Note ("EOIE-EDIT-BATCH", 38u32, "2026-10-28T09:35Z", "eoie", "05:3x kit: 'kit do --dry-run' smoke on the installed binary: 11 checks, 0 failed (in-memory chained edits, file untouched, write and run programs refused, each with a positive control).")
inl note_of_eoie_edit_batch_39 () = Note ("EOIE-EDIT-BATCH", 39u32, "2026-10-31T09:59Z", "kit", "10-31 06:0x kit groom (WIP limit, dangling rule 'no Active silent > 3 d'): Active -> Planned at 85%. No note for about 3 days, so this item isn't in progress. Planned keeps it in 'agile next --team kit' as ready work (Paused would park it out of the queue). Kit had 42 Active items, with Active meaning anything ever touched rather than being worked now. The progress and notes stay; whoever pulls it next sets it Active again.")
inl note_of_eoie_edit_batch_40 () = Note ("EOIE-EDIT-BATCH", 40u32, "2026-11-04T08:04Z", "kit", "DONE 11-04 04:5x (kit, closing with evidence): the last milestone shipped. The installed kit's `kit do` usage shows `--dry-run` (\"chains every edit in memory and writes nothing\"), program on stdin with `-`, and the edit statements with expected counts ([expect files <n> replacements <n>], replace once). Anchored replacements, whole-file writes, rehearsal and atomic receipts with hashes were in earlier milestones. Further edit-language work belongs to the KIT-ENTRY-X-S4 do-verb typing.")
inl eoie_jobs () = Task ("EOIE-JOBS", "one-call build/test/run with receipts plus a job registry: background runs with wall-clock timeout, completion notification instead of polling, process listing scoped to the calling session with CPU deltas, and stop by job id; replaces ~10k build/test/wait/process calls (build.ps1, test.ps1, monitor.ps1 752 calls, Start-Sleep, Get-CimInstance, Stop-Process) and would have prevented the 14 h runaway searches of 2026-10-13; source: transcript sweep 2026-10-13 22:13 over ~45k tool calls (claude 41.6k, codex 2.0k, opencode 1.1k, grok 0.2k; zed 8 threads parsed, tool shape not yet recognized); report in main's job tmp/sweep/intent-report.txt", Story, P0, D6, 85u32, Planned, "", "kit,one-call,adoption,wipe-redo,team:kit", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_eoie_jobs () = Planning ("EOIE-JOBS", Minutes 120u32, Load Heavy, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_eoie_jobs_1 () = Note ("EOIE-JOBS", 1u32, "2026-10-14T12:25Z", "lane-a", "08:25 lane-a: spec coverage map: cases 6,13,14,15,16,24,30,33 = 1,069 recorded calls, all red. Today's evidence: heavy-gate jobs queue silently (Invoke-Heavy waited 5+ min with only a heartbeat); a job registry should show queue position.")
inl note_of_eoie_jobs_2 () = Note ("EOIE-JOBS", 2u32, "2026-10-14T17:08Z", "lane-a", "13:08 lane-a: today's heavy-gate waits for lane A's small jobs (seconds to acquire): 965, 72, 5003, 987, 1092, 950, 93, 177, 245, 338; the 83-min wait gave only a 5-min heartbeat. A job registry should show queue position, holders and an ETA.")
inl note_of_eoie_jobs_3 () = Note ("EOIE-JOBS", 3u32, "2026-10-15T10:50Z", "main-thread", "06:50 groom: evidence from tonight: every heavy job went through the job-local pwsh heavy.ps1 gate (Invoke-Heavy) with notifier-driven waits. That is exactly the slot/queue/memory-admission job model this verb should own. Raised P1 -> P0 inside the cluster because it is used most.")
inl note_of_eoie_jobs_4 () = Note ("EOIE-JOBS", 4u32, "2026-10-17T10:02Z", "main-thread", "06:02 main-thread night use case: about 15 heavy jobs ran through tmp/shared/heavy.ps1 Invoke-Heavy (2 slots, memory admission only); each needed by hand: a log path, a wall-clock bound, the exit code and the result lines pulled from the log, the run dir, and chaining (build then suite); one inventory walk hung 85 min unnoticed because nothing watched CPU against output. A job registry should record command, slot, start, log, exit and a liveness rule (output silence vs CPU) per job.")
inl note_of_eoie_jobs_5 () = Note ("EOIE-JOBS", 5u32, "2026-10-17T11:29Z", "main-thread", "07:29 main-thread day: interim receipts outside eoie: tmp/shared/heavy.ps1 Invoke-Heavy and new Invoke-Timed append every run to %LOCALAPPDATA%\\spiral-bin\\ledger\\runs.tsv (queued/start/end, wait_s, run_s, slot, label, outcome with exit code, free MB before/after, command). apps/compiler/tmp/scripts/cost-report.ps1 [-Runs N -Top N -Notebooks paths] summarizes ledger, harness runs, cost per backend, a signal table (rows changed over the last 5 full runs) and Kino per-target ms from ipynb metadata.spiral_test.targets. First readings: full harness ~26 min wall / 95 native min summed, Lean 26 of it; ~70 native min per full run re-verify rows unchanged across 5 runs; Kino sm' target minutes: gleam 8.6, fsharp 8.1, cuda 5.0 of ~30. This is the receipt schema the eoie job registry should own.")
inl note_of_eoie_jobs_6 () = Note ("EOIE-JOBS", 6u32, "2026-10-17T18:23Z", "bundle-wisdom", "14:25 from the bundles (spiral-bin alpha417, brzozowski alpha54, 2026-06..09; full list in BUNDLE-WISDOM note 5): the job registry should (1) write the job record before spawn; (2) own the process group and reap descendants on timeout (an orphan compiler facade ate 3.8 cores and faked regressions); (3) re-read the child's completion receipt before reporting timed_out; (4) type outcomes as semantic_failure|time_budget|yield|environment_failure; (5) declare expected exits per job (rc5 negatives, diff rc1, installer rc75 = resumable); (6) on a first semantic failure cancel undispatched work and record the frontier, retry only the timed-out cells; (7) keep the stop/status path out of a saturated worker pool; (8) judge liveness by material progress, not heartbeat churn; (9) store argv, the exact tool paths and the first diagnostic from both streams.")
inl note_of_eoie_jobs_7 () = Note ("EOIE-JOBS", 7u32, "2026-10-17T20:28Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-17 16:28 process hygiene belongs here: find processes by command-line pattern with their trees, stop them, check listening ports, and retire known daemons by name; agents repeated these by hand after every test run.")
inl note_of_eoie_jobs_8 () = Note ("EOIE-JOBS", 8u32, "2026-10-17T22:21Z", "main-thread", "18:21 main-thread handoff for the next (overnight) run, in order: (1) bless single-flight so the 6 new negative samples (reactors_negative_port_type, conversation_negative_*, routes_negative_*) get baseline rows; (2) add -Oracle C,Zig to test.ps1 and run the first Zig bless (CONFIGURABLE-ORACLE); (3) formatter distance over all repos, with tracked-but-missing files skipped first (SPIRAL-FORMATTER notes 60-61); (4) agile todos --apply (TODO-AS-SOURCE). All today's changes are unstaged in spiral, eoie, x, i574n.github, polyglot, dice and alphabet; the user commits.")
inl note_of_eoie_jobs_9 () = Note ("EOIE-JOBS", 9u32, "2026-10-17T23:04Z", "main-thread", "19:04 main-thread handoff item 1 done: blessed single-flight-20261017-182920 (6,939 rows) after review single-flight-20261017-182437 (6,915 parity + exactly the 24 new rows of the 6 negative samples, DISAGREE 0); compare-runs review vs bless: 0 differing cells.")
inl note_of_eoie_jobs_10 () = Note ("EOIE-JOBS", 10u32, "2026-10-18T19:53Z", "claude", "2026-10-18 15:53 eoie lane slice 1 landed: new granular crate eoie_jobs (model.spi job_command and job_state unions + record classification, host.spi thin primitives with nominal child_process/spawn_outcome, jobs.spi supervisor as a tail-recursive Spiral loop). Verbs routed in eoie_cli_model before the legacy catalog: job start <label> <timeout-ms> <program> [args] (detached supervisor: CREATE_BREAKAWAY_FROM_JOB|DETACHED_PROCESS|CREATE_NEW_PROCESS_GROUP with a fallback without breakaway, so the session reaper cannot kill it), job status|wait <id> <max-ms>|list [n]|tail <id> [lines]. Registry under %LOCALAPPDATA%\\eoie\\jobs (EOIE_JOBS_HOME overrides): label, argv, cwd, timeout_ms, started_ms, pid, heartbeat_ms every second, exit (code, ended_ms, finished|timed-out|spawn-failed). wait exits with the job code, 124 timed-out, 125 spawn failure or lost (heartbeat older than 15 s), 3 still running. E2E: exit 7 finished, 1.5 s timeout killed at 1639 ms, missing program spawn-failed, list newest first; Spiral tests 2/2. 686 generated lines (inl primitives turned let to pass the gate). Next: heavy-gate integration and completion notification")
inl note_of_eoie_jobs_11 () = Note ("EOIE-JOBS", 11u32, "2026-10-18T20:28Z", "claude", "2026-10-18 16:28 slice 2: job start --heavy runs the program inside spiral's Invoke-Heavy through a generated job-dir wrapper (heavy.ps1 from EOIE_HEAVY_GATE or a spiral checkout above the cwd; args read from argv.inner, no quoting), so gated runs also survive the reaper; e2e: ledger row e2e-heavy (eoie job ...) ok. Supervisor now runs from a copy under <jobs>/supervisor/eoie-<size>-<mtime>.exe so a running job never locks target/debug/eoie.exe against cargo builds (the main lane adopted job start for its pack reviews after the reaper killed a run at 15:35). job list/status only count job-* folders")
inl note_of_eoie_jobs_12 () = Note ("EOIE-JOBS", 12u32, "2026-10-18T22:08Z", "claude", "2026-10-18 18:08 eoie lane dogfood: the session's low-memory reaper killed my Invoke-Heavy background shell while it waited at the gate (18:04, nothing had run). Restarted as eoie job start --heavy from the supervisor copy: the starting shell hung on the inherited stdout pipe even with *> file (PowerShell reads native output through a pipe and writes the file itself), so *> is not a workaround; TaskStop on that shell left supervisor 99576 running with a live heartbeat, then job wait from the copy picked it up. Fix in the pending build: job start clears HANDLE_FLAG_INHERIT on stdin/stdout/stderr (SetHandleInformation in an unsafe extern block, Rust 2024) before spawning; proof is lab/EOIE-JOBS/inherit-pipe-check.ps1 (captured pipes must close within 10 s).")
inl note_of_eoie_jobs_13 () = Note ("EOIE-JOBS", 13u32, "2026-10-18T23:35Z", "claude", "Pipe hang root cause (verified 19:05): eoie --args-from relaunches itself with Stdio::inherit; Rust duplicates the std handles as inheritable for the child, so the child also inherits the caller's ORIGINAL pipe handles as extra inheritable handles, which then leak into the supervisor even after it clears its own std handles. Fix: argument_file.run_from clears HANDLE_FLAG_INHERIT on the std handles at CLI start (one place, covers every relaunch); supervisor_launched no longer needs its own copy. Direct job start from a captured tool shell already returns at once with the current build. lab/EOIE-JOBS/inherit-pipe-check.ps1 recheck pending on build 3.")
inl note_of_eoie_jobs_14 () = Note ("EOIE-JOBS", 14u32, "2026-10-18T23:35Z", "claude", "Timeout now stops the whole tree (main lane report 2: the 2 h timeout killed only the pwsh wrapper, review-pack3 + test.ps1 + dotnet/erl survived and the heavy slot was released under them). The supervisor assigns the child to a Windows job object (BREAKAWAY_OK so nested eoie job start stays independent), TerminateJobObject on timeout, waits up to 3 s and counts ActiveProcesses; unix uses process_group(0) + kill -KILL -pgid + a /proc pgrp count. Exit detail and job status/wait/list show detail=wall-clock timeout; process tree stopped | N processes left alive (tree_stop union + test). Live timeout e2e pending on build 3.")
inl note_of_eoie_jobs_15 () = Note ("EOIE-JOBS", 15u32, "2026-10-18T23:35Z", "claude", "ps/kill (eoie_process_table): lab/EOIE-JOBS/ps-e2e.ps1 3/4 on build 1, the miss was the test expecting 2 tree processes while pwsh brings conhost (3); test fixed to tree: N. Also fixed: full sysinfo refresh made ps/kill take 2-3 s (now targeted ProcessRefreshKind), and kill of a missing pid counted as stopped; now a stop_result union (NoSuchProcess | StoppedOf stopped,total) makes missing or partial stops failures, receipt stopped N  failed F.")
inl note_of_eoie_jobs_16 () = Note ("EOIE-JOBS", 16u32, "2026-10-18T23:55Z", "claude", "Verified on build 3 (19:55): lab/EOIE-JOBS/inherit-pipe-check.ps1 PASS (captured pipes close after 306 ms); lab/EOIE-JOBS/timeout-tree-check.ps1 2/2 (pwsh job with a grandchild, 3 s timeout: exit 124, detail=wall-clock timeout; process tree stopped, zero survivors); lab/EOIE-JOBS/ps-e2e.ps1 5/5; ps 226 ms (was 2.2 s). eoie --help now prints every verb line (help sat at usage index 5 behind repo, so read/table/do/ps/job never printed and help errors quoted repo usage); new export eoie_command_more_usages.")
inl note_of_eoie_jobs_17 () = Note ("EOIE-JOBS", 17u32, "2026-10-19T00:24Z", "claude", "Dogfood find (20:12): eoie kill <supervisor> --tree on a running build job reported stopped 2 of 8 and exit 1, yet all 8 processes were gone. Children that died with their parent made sysinfo kill() return false. Build 5b counts by survivors after a 200 ms recheck instead (stopped = total - still alive). ps-e2e 5/5 on 5b. Open: that kill took 5.7 s on a cargo/rustc tree (163 ms on small trees); not profiled yet.")
inl note_of_eoie_jobs_18 () = Note ("EOIE-JOBS", 18u32, "2026-10-19T01:01Z", "claude", "kill speed (21:01, build 8b): sysinfo 0.38 Windows Process::kill spawns taskkill.exe /F per process (~0.7 s each; 8-process tree 5.7 s). process_stopped now calls OpenProcess(PROCESS_TERMINATE) + TerminateProcess directly (sysinfo kill only off Windows); a 3-process tree stops in 394 ms including the 200 ms survivor recheck. All EOIE-JOBS lab checks green on 8b: inherit-pipe PASS (249 ms), timeout-tree 2/2, ps-e2e 5/5.")
inl note_of_eoie_jobs_19 () = Note ("EOIE-JOBS", 19u32, "2026-10-19T07:31Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-19 03:31 eoie lane: job children were visible black windows. The supervisor runs detached (no console), so each console program it spawned got a new window. Fix: CREATE_NO_WINDOW on the job child plus env_remove of NoDefaultCurrentDirectoryInExePath (cmd /c x.cmd in the job cwd). Proof: a pwsh child under the fixed supervisor has MainWindowHandle 0 (live, 03:30); lab/EOIE-JOBS/hidden-window-check.ps1 checks it. New exports for eoie do: eoie_job_launched (label, timeout, argv, cwd), eoie_job_settled (summary + pid + timeout_ms), eoie_job_output, eoie_job_labeled; job_started now wraps a job_launched with a launch union (CLI messages unchanged). eoie_jobs at 938 lines. Release blocked since 10-18: the topology gate refused publishing because agile_task_notes (1034) and eoie_coverage_prune (1072) were over 1000; note-edit moved to agile_note_edit (538, notes back to 623) and the lcov parser to eoie_lcov (309, coverage.rs 459).")
inl note_of_eoie_jobs_20 () = Note ("EOIE-JOBS", 20u32, "2026-10-19T08:24Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-19 04:24 eoie lane, follow-up: supervisor copies are always named eoie-<len>-<stamp>.exe, so a test binary that starts a job (tonight's probe) puts a fake eoie in the shared folder, and lanes that pick the newest copy got one without ps. Fix: name the copy after current_exe's file stem. Also job wait's relaunch keeps the original process alive to pass on the exit code, so src\\target\\debug\\eoie.exe stays locked anyway; build scripts now move the exe aside before cargo (renaming a running exe works on Windows).")
inl note_of_eoie_jobs_21 () = Note ("EOIE-JOBS", 21u32, "2026-10-19T09:40Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-19 05:40 eoie lane: release eoie.exe installed 05:39:54 (sha256 608C85E2...), the first since 10-17. Job eoie-release-install-6: regen published, debug build, Spiral tests (8 notebooks incl. eoie-jobs, eoie-lcov, eoie-do-write/git/jobs/lab), one-call 34/34, read e2e 5/5, ps e2e 5/5, hidden window 2/2, timeout tree 2/2, release build, topology gate passed; the installed binary re-ran hidden window 2/2 and one-call 34/34. Lessons: Spiral test blocks compile with plain rustc (no crate deps), so a test must not call a regex/serde primitive; build.ps1 throws, so a job script that calls it ends there unless wrapped.")
inl note_of_eoie_jobs_22 () = Note ("EOIE-JOBS", 22u32, "2026-10-19T10:20Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-19 06:20 eoie lane: final eoie.exe for the night installed 06:19:33 (sha256 18C9ECA1...); regen, debug build, Spiral tests, one-call 34/34, read e2e 5/5, ps e2e 5/5, hidden window 2/2 and timeout tree 2/2 passed before publishing, and the installed binary passed 34/34 and 2/2. Supervisor copies are named <exe stem>-<len>-<stamp> (real builds keep the eoie- prefix), and making a new copy removes same-stem copies more than a day old; copies in use survive because Windows refuses to delete a running exe. The supervisor folder held 15 copies (~400 MB) at 06:15.")
inl note_of_eoie_jobs_23 () = Note ("EOIE-JOBS", 23u32, "2026-10-19T12:11Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-19 08:11 eoie lane (user: 'there are eoie processes running for hours, fix this'): job wait outlived its caller. When the reaper killed a waiting shell, 'eoie job wait <id> 7200000' and its relaunched supervisor copy kept polling for up to two hours with nobody listening. Fix: the wait loop (CLI job wait and eoie do job wait) stops when the shell that started it is gone. parent_pid from a Toolhelp snapshot (parent_id on Unix), process_alive via OpenProcess(SYNCHRONIZE)/WaitForSingleObject (access denied counts as alive), and the relaunch passes the shell pid down as EOIE_JOB_WATCH. lab/EOIE-JOBS/orphan-wait-check.ps1 kills only the waiting shell: the 06:19 release leaves 2 waiters alive (fails 1/2), the fix passes 2/2. Installed in eoie.exe 08:10:44 (sha256 36C3CD19...) after the full gate (spec 34/34, read 5/5, ps 5/5, hidden window 2/2, timeout tree 2/2, orphan wait 2/2, re-run on the installed binary). eoie_jobs 965 lines. Supervisors still live as long as their job, by design.")
inl note_of_eoie_jobs_24 () = Note ("EOIE-JOBS", 24u32, "2026-10-19T16:57Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-19 12:57 Publish-EoieBinary failed with access denied while another lane's eoie job wait ran from the installed eoie.exe (the original process stays alive to forward the exit code). Fix in compiler-contracts/workspace.ps1: on Windows the locked exe is renamed into %LOCALAPPDATA%\\eoie\\retired (a running exe can be renamed, not overwritten), the new one moves in, and on failure the old exe is restored; retired copies are deleted on the next publish once they are unlocked. Verified live: published=True while x's wait held the old exe.")
inl note_of_eoie_jobs_25 () = Note ("EOIE-JOBS", 25u32, "2026-10-20T02:44Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-19 22:43 main-lane friction (also on EOIE-ONE-CALL): no job stop verb; a mistaken job can only be stopped by walking its supervisor tree by hand. Design for the next eoie run: job stop <id> reuses the timeout path already in eoie_jobs (jobs.spi line 124: tree_stop_of plus timeout_detail), adds a JobStopped arm to the exit union in model.spi (word stopped, its own exit code), writes the exit file once, and refuses an id with no job folder, a job that already has an exit file, or a supervisor PID whose command line is not this eoie job. Test: Spiral notebook starts a sleeping child job, stops it, checks status stopped and that no process of the tree survives; mutant check. Priority: first item next eoie run, not tonight (time box ended 21:00).")
inl note_of_eoie_jobs_26 () = Note ("EOIE-JOBS", 26u32, "2026-10-20T11:09Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-20 07:09 eoie lane: job stop <id> in source, release job eoie-release-17 running. The verb writes a stop request file into the job folder; the supervisor checks it every 200 ms tick and stops its own Job Object tree like a timeout (exit 143, state stopped, detail 'stopped on request; process tree stopped'), so no caller ever kills a PID. Refuses unknown ids and settled jobs; fails after 15 s when the supervisor predates the verb. 1k gate: eoie_jobs reached 1226 lines (the halt reason became a runtime union, so 7-arm state matches got inlined); halt terms now map in one match, state_word/exit/settled are let functions, and stop+tail moved to a new crate eoie_job_control (eoie_jobs 965, control 441). Spiral notebook src/eoie_jobs/stop_cli.spi: a parent+child sleeper stops with both heartbeats frozen.")
inl note_of_eoie_jobs_27 () = Note ("EOIE-JOBS", 27u32, "2026-10-20T18:00Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-20 14:00 eoie lane, three findings from today's queueing, fixed in source for release 20: (1) a --heavy job's timeout counted time spent queued at the window or the heavy gate (a 15 min quick compile timed out at 13:46 without ever getting a slot while x-round19 and redo-apply held both); the supervisor now pushes its deadline by the queued time on each heartbeat until stdout shows 'Invoke-Heavy: acquired slot' (gate_queued shared with job_summary; windows_cli covers it with a 3 s timeout queued 4 s behind a live window); (2) job wait <id> without max-ms waits until the job settles (was max 0, main's EOIE-JOB-STOP note 7); (3) a quick single-owner compile does not need a heavy slot: window request compile <min> --wait + direct run + release shares the box with heavy builds and still respects live-gui/compiler-install windows (used for the experiments crates at 13:56-14:00 while both heavy slots were held for hours).")
inl note_of_eoie_jobs_28 () = Note ("EOIE-JOBS", 28u32, "2026-10-21T00:34Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-20 20:34 eoie lane, release 22 installed 20:32 (user 18:45-19:15: dangling processes; keep eoie and erl processes in their parent's tree; stop blocking on job wait): job objects are kill-on-close with no breakaway (stop_cli: killing only the supervisor stops child and grandchild); job wait no longer relaunches from the supervisor copy (orphan e2e asserts one eoie per waiter); new 'job run [--heavy]' supervises in the caller's process and exits with the job's code, meant to run as an agent background task so the completion arrives as a notification; --heavy supervisors wait for their window and heavy-gate slot themselves (eoie_job_gate: ticket {UtcNow.Ticks:D20}-{supervisor pid}.t, first live ticket + mutex probe, then SPIRAL_HEAVY_TICKET for Invoke-Heavy, main's contract), so a queued job is one 6 MB process: verified live at 20:34 (supervisor only, jobs/<id>/window waiting behind a compiler-install, stop during the wait removes the window). The eoie do glue exports moved to eoie_job_control (eoie_jobs 920 lines).")
inl note_of_eoie_jobs_29 () = Note ("EOIE-JOBS", 29u32, "2026-10-21T00:46Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-20 20:46 eoie lane: ticket handoff proven end to end (main, 20:46): two 'eoie job run --heavy' jobs (sefaz-family-gates3, formatter-c9) adopted their supervisor tickets: jobs/<id>/window 'granted ... waited_ms=0', jobs/<id>/slot 'ready ticket=00639281403243756206-53768.t waited_ms=0', Invoke-Heavy 'acquired slot 1 after 0 s', tickets removed from the queue, holder-1.h/holder-2.h naming both jobs.")
inl note_of_eoie_jobs_30 () = Note ("EOIE-JOBS", 30u32, "2026-10-21T01:23Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-20 21:23 eoie lane: release 23 installed (one-call 34/34, orphan 3/3): lost jobs settle on first observation and orphaned job windows drop with their dead supervisor; checked on main's two reaped 'job run --heavy' jobs of 20:46: both now read state=lost exit=125 with the detail and a recorded exit file, elapsed frozen at 334 s and 332 s; job run's usage, help and README warn that an attached job ends with its caller.")
inl note_of_eoie_jobs_31 () = Note ("EOIE-JOBS", 31u32, "2026-10-21T11:32Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-21 07:32 eoie team: drafted job chaining (scratch apply-job-after.ps1, after r28 slice 2): job start/run [--after <id>|--after-ok <id>] [--heavy|--compile] ... The supervisor waits for the named job to settle before its window gate and spawn, beating its own heartbeat and honoring job stop, so the timeout starts when work starts. --after-ok skips with a new typed state JobSkipped (word skipped, exit 125, detail '--after-ok: <id> ended <state> exit <n>'). Status shows queued while waiting, and start validates that the named job exists. Replaces the hand-written follow-on scripts (follow-r26b..e) that release chaining needed tonight. Notebook: stop_cli after_waits_for_the_named_job_and_after_ok_skips_when_it_failed (rustc exiter fixture). Preview queued behind tibia's live block.")
inl note_of_eoie_jobs_32 () = Note ("EOIE-JOBS", 32u32, "2026-10-21T20:35Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-21 16:35 eoie team: shipped in the combined r28+r29 release, installed 15:38 (one-call spec 34/34, all 67 notebooks green): job start --after/--after-ok (eoie_job_after), the run summary printed by eoie_jobs (launch.rs 1044 -> 737), and job_state as Live/Settled.")
inl note_of_eoie_jobs_33 () = Note ("EOIE-JOBS", 33u32, "2026-10-21T20:59Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-21 16:59 eoie team, progress review: shipped since 75%: --after/--after-ok chaining, the run summary, job_state Live/Settled, --compile; r33 adds the --max-private-mb memory cap; left: session-scoped process listing with CPU deltas (eoie machine watch part 1 covers the machine-wide view).")
inl note_of_eoie_jobs_34 () = Note ("EOIE-JOBS", 34u32, "2026-10-21T21:34Z", "eoie-lane", "17:34 eoie team: last item drafted for r35 (scratch apply-ps-cores.ps1, dry-run clean): eoie ps gains a cores column (500 ms sample) and --mine (descendants of the nearest claude/codex ancestor; the chain is pwsh -> pwsh shim -> cmd -> claude.exe). The process_table Rust blob goes away: the listing is granular Spiral over eoie_machine_watch's process rows (ps_scope union EveryProcess|SessionTree|NoSession). Found by dogfooding: eoie ps could not tell a busy scan from a stuck one (no CPU column), and 'stop only own processes' needs a session scope.")
inl note_of_eoie_jobs_35 () = Note ("EOIE-JOBS", 35u32, "2026-10-21T22:00Z", "eoie-lane", "18:00 papercut: 'job list [count]' shows the newest 20 jobs, so a queued or running job started earlier (tonight: the r30-r33 release, queued 35 min for a heavy slot) drops out once a few check jobs finish after it; I had to ask job status by id. Rule for the fix: unsettled jobs (queued, running) always print, before the newest settled ones that fill the count.")
inl note_of_eoie_jobs_36 () = Note ("EOIE-JOBS", 36u32, "2026-10-22T03:32Z", "eoie", "23:32 user 23:2x: 'heal what's causing eoie to generate all these dangling processes without parent stealing cpu'. Measured: the parentless processes are eoie job supervisors (eoie-<size>-<stamp>.exe job supervise), detached on purpose so jobs outlive their caller; at 23:28 there were 10 (2 running jobs, 7 queued tibia jobs, 1 just ended); 0 processes alive under finished/stopped jobs, no Cells daemon or compiler server orphaned. The waste is in the queue: each waiting supervisor wakes every 2 s (window gate) or 3 s (slot loop), rewrites heartbeat_ms and status files, lists the queue dir and probes every ticket's PID (O(n^2) in waiters): 80-110 ms CPU per 20 s each, 2.7% of a core for 10. Worse, job_window_gate takes the heavy-build window BEFORE queuing for the slot, so a queued job holds a counting window and can block other lanes' window requests (tibia's report). Fix apply-gate-queue.ps1 for release C: slot first, window granted fresh at slot time, waits back off to a 10 s cap (heartbeat silence limit is 15 s). Next: the supervisor's own run loop and window run clients get the same backoff; eoie ps --tree shows supervisors as job roots so detached is not mistaken for dangling.")
inl note_of_eoie_jobs_37 () = Note ("EOIE-JOBS", 37u32, "2026-10-22T10:54Z", "eoie", "06:54 feature from tonight's ABC tail: it queued from 03:42 behind two other lanes' heavy jobs, and the rule 'hold a heavy slot by 04:00 or stop' (EOIE-RELEASE-TRAIN R14) had to be enforced by hand with a cron and a job stop. As a job option: eoie job start --heavy ... --slot-by HH:MM makes the supervisor give up its queue ticket and window at that time if no slot was acquired (state=expired-in-queue, its own exit code, no process tree started), and --done-by HH:MM stops a started job at a hard deadline (the R12 rule). Same shape as the release-verb deadlines in EOIE-RELEASE-TRAIN note 45; the job registry is the right home since every lane's heavy work goes through it. Needs a compile.")
inl note_of_eoie_jobs_38 () = Note ("EOIE-JOBS", 38u32, "2026-10-22T11:42Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-22 07:42 --slot-within <minutes> drafted as an anchored slice (scratch apply-slot-within.ps1): launch parses it after --max-private-mb, rejects 0 and plain starts, writes slot_by_ms; slot_loop's new branch removes the ticket and exits 75 'no heavy slot within --slot-within; the job never started'; windowed_gate caps its limit at slot_by_ms. Dry-run on a src copy applies cleanly and composes with F. Lands with release F (size check: launch.rs 891 before).")
inl note_of_eoie_jobs_39 () = Note ("EOIE-JOBS", 39u32, "2026-10-22T13:10Z", "eoie-lane", "09:10 eoie team: the second half of the 06:54 request is drafted as --done-within <minutes> (apply-done-within.ps1, in F after slot-within; prelaunch-check clean on F+G+E2). It is minutes-based like --slot-within, so there is no clock parsing; it replaces the requested --done-by HH:MM. It is a hard wall-clock cap from submission that is never stretched by queue time, unlike timeout-ms since r20. Launch writes done_by_ms; slot_by_ms becomes the earlier of slot-within and done-within, so a job still queued at the cap expires in the queue (expired-in-queue, 75); supervised takes a hard deadline and a started job past it halts as timed-out (124). Allowed on plain jobs too. The receipt shows done_within_min. stop_cli test a_job_past_its_done_within_minutes_stops_as_timed_out (~60 s) plus the zero refusal. Also from this pass: queue deadlines (--slot-within and the 24 h window limit) settle as a typed JobExpiredInQueue (state=expired-in-queue exit=75) instead of finished exit=75, as the request asked.")
inl note_of_eoie_jobs_40 () = Note ("EOIE-JOBS", 40u32, "2026-10-22T20:24Z", "eoie-lane", "16:24 lab drain rule, written before F lands (memory lab-in-repo: delete lab copies only after landing): scratch drain-after-F.ps1 removes a lab copy only when (1) no tree lease is live, (2) the installed eoie's 'machine watch' prints the '== eoie jobs running' section (F installed), and (3) at most 3 of the lab file's non-blank lines are missing from its src counterpart (src supersedes it). Measured now: lab/EOIE-MACHINE-WATCH (machine-watch/eoie_machine_watch: model identical; host, run, Cargo.toml and spiproj have src-side additions or 1-2 changed lines; machine-watch-2/jobs_watch.spi identical) and lab/EOIE-JOBS/ps-cores (listing.spi and ps_cli.spi identical to src/eoie_process_table). EOIE-JOBS' five .ps1 behavior checks stay until each is a CLI notebook case.")
inl note_of_eoie_jobs_41 () = Note ("EOIE-JOBS", 41u32, "2026-10-23T00:07Z", "eoie-lane", "20:07 kill under the job host (found in F4's candidate stop_cli, fixed in the tree, verified by job-1792712662424 and then F5): kill counted survivors by presence in a process snapshot 200 ms after TerminateProcess, and a supervisor whose handle the host still holds (500 ms sweep) stays listed as a zombie, so kill reported 'stopped 0 of 1'. Kill now waits up to 2 s on each pid's handle (SYNCHRONIZE) and counts a signaled one as stopped. This also corrects the 08:10 DH diagnosis of the same stop_cli failure (blamed then on the host-start gap). EOIE-JOBS' install probes ride with F5.")
inl note_of_eoie_jobs_42 () = Note ("EOIE-JOBS", 42u32, "2026-10-23T03:16Z", "x", "2026-10-22 23:16 x (dogfood): the installed eoie.exe (10-21 15:38) silently mis-parses 'job start --after <id> --heavy --done-within 459 <label> <ms> <program>...'. It took '--done-within' as the label and '459' as timeout_ms, and accepted the job (exit 0, label=--done-within, timeout_ms=459). With the flag before --heavy, the program became '--heavy' and the job only failed at spawn. The 10-22 source (launch.spi:92) parses --done-within, so a release fixes the flag itself. The remaining bug is generic: a label or program that starts with '--' should be rejected at submit ('unknown option --x'), not queued. A silently mis-parsed heavy job holds a queue position and then wastes a slot. Two mis-parsed jobs of mine were stopped before they ran.")
inl note_of_eoie_jobs_43 () = Note ("EOIE-JOBS", 43u32, "2026-10-23T03:25Z", "x", "2026-10-22 23:25 x (dogfood): 'job start --after <id> --heavy' took a heavy-build window at submit (23:16) although its --after job was still queued, so the window sat idle for what would have been hours. It also overtook tibia's older waiting live-gui request (23:13). tibia is filing that admission order gap. Suggested here: a job waiting on --after should request its window only once the dependency has settled. Workaround used: a plain job submits the heavy job only once no live-gui request is waiting.")
inl note_of_eoie_jobs_44 () = Note ("EOIE-JOBS", 44u32, "2026-10-23T03:37Z", "x", "2026-10-22 23:37 x (dogfood): a job started with 'eoie job start' from INSIDE another eoie job dies when that outer job ends. My plain waiter job submitted a heavy job and exited, and the new job went to state=lost after 209 ms ('the supervisor stopped beating'). It follows from the documented kill-on-close job object (everything a job started ends with it), but nothing warns at submit time and the receipt still says 'job start ok'. Suggested: when job start runs inside a job (an EOIE job env marker or a parent supervisor in the tree), hand the new supervisor to the job host outside the caller's job object, or refuse with 'nested job start: the new job would end with job <id>'. Workaround: submit from the agent shell.")
inl note_of_eoie_jobs_45 () = Note ("EOIE-JOBS", 45u32, "2026-10-23T07:06Z", "eoie", "03:06 eoie: answers to x's three notes (23:16-23:37). (1) Options mis-parsed as label/program: release K turns job_started's positional chain into a typed order-free fold; adding there the refusal of a label or program that starts with '--' ('unknown option'), as x asks. (2) A --after job taking its window at submit: in the tree (ships with F) supervise_job waits on --after before any gate, and the gate takes the slot before the window, so a dependent job holds no window while it waits. (3) A nested job start dying with its outer job: with the host installed, supervisors are the host's children, outside the caller's job, but only if the host itself broke away. It didn't, because eoie's containers lack BREAKAWAY_OK; fixed for GE2 (EOIE-JOB-HOST note 22), with nested-host-check as GE2's probe. Until GE2 installs, submit from an agent shell, as x does.")
inl note_of_eoie_jobs_46 () = Note ("EOIE-JOBS", 46u32, "2026-10-23T09:11Z", "eoie-lane", "05:11 install probe (EOIE-JOBS slot-within): PASS. exit 2 eoie error: job --slot-within applies to --heavy or --compile jobs, which wait for a slot or window")
inl note_of_eoie_jobs_47 () = Note ("EOIE-JOBS", 47u32, "2026-10-23T09:11Z", "eoie-lane", "05:11 install probe (EOIE-JOBS done-within): PASS. eoie job start ok id=job-1792746541275-done-probe supervisor_pid=126080 timeout_ms=60000 gate=plain label=done-probe done_within_min=1 | eoie job wait ok id=job-1792746541275-done-probe state=finished exit=0 elapsed_ms=338 stdout_bytes=0 stderr_bytes=0 label=done-probe")
inl note_of_eoie_jobs_48 () = Note ("EOIE-JOBS", 48u32, "2026-10-24T02:32Z", "main", "2026-10-23 22:32 main: gap from eoie's 22:33 process trace: job start has no way to request a completion notification, so main's two --after chains (17 queued, 2 running) had no waiter until main armed background 'job wait' calls on each tail. The item's title promises completion notification; wanted: job start --notify (or a chain-tail waiter the job host owns) that pings the caller's session when a chain settles or a link is skipped (exit 125).")
inl note_of_eoie_jobs_49 () = Note ("EOIE-JOBS", 49u32, "2026-10-24T02:35Z", "eoie", "22:35 doctor check proposal from the 22:14-22:45 audit: 'eoie job doctor' lists unwatched jobs, meaning live jobs with no waiter (job wait/run process) on the job itself or on any successor reachable through after links. It prints the sample time, because a snapshot reported 19 minutes late misled two teams tonight. x's case shows why chains count: a waiter on its recheck covered the gate. Pairs with main's note here on completion notification (job start --notify or a host-owned chain waiter).")
inl note_of_eoie_jobs_50 () = Note ("EOIE-JOBS", 50u32, "2026-10-24T03:08Z", "eoie", "23:08 groom: progress 95 -> 85, because completion notification (in the title) is still missing. Constraint: eoie can't create a session's notifying background task; only the session can (a background 'eoie job wait' or 'job run'). Design within that: (1) job start prints the exact waiter command for the chain tail in its receipt ('watch: eoie job wait <tail-id>'), the tail found through after links; (2) job doctor's unwatched check (EOIE-JOBS-DOCTOR note 6) catches the rest; (3) with EOIE-JOB-OWNER-PARENT the host knows its owner session, so a host-owned chain waiter becomes possible once a session-side inbox exists. Milestones: receipt hint, doctor check, owner-aware waiter.")
inl note_of_eoie_jobs_51 () = Note ("EOIE-JOBS", 51u32, "2026-10-24T04:57Z", "eoie", "00:57 milestone 1 (receipt hint) dropped by rule: job start's receipt is a parsed contract. Lanes extract id=, label= and take the last line ('Select-Object -Last 1'), so either form of hint breaks someone: appended watch= breaks greedy label=(.+)$ parses, and a second line breaks -Last 1 consumers (several of my own scripts). The unwatched-jobs check in job doctor (EOIE-JOBS-DOCTOR note 6) delivers the same signal without touching the receipt; that's milestone 2 and it rides with doctor (stage M ships doctor itself).")
inl note_of_eoie_jobs_52 () = Note ("EOIE-JOBS", 52u32, "2026-10-24T23:43Z", "eoie", "19:43 Provisional estimate 120m from the grooming rule (EOIE-AGILE-CHECK-FIELDS note): D6 at 85%. Replace it with milestone sizing.")
inl note_of_eoie_jobs_53 () = Note ("EOIE-JOBS", 53u32, "2026-10-28T10:29Z", "eoie", "10-28 groom (user via main): Paused. Last note 2026-10-24, nothing queued. Next step: check whether its remaining milestone shipped in TRAIN2c (installed kit.exe A5B55974, 10-28 05:25). If it did, close it with that evidence; if not, size the remaining slice from its last milestone note and resume it through agile next.")
inl note_of_eoie_jobs_54 () = Note ("EOIE-JOBS", 54u32, "2026-10-31T21:25Z", "kit", "10-31 17:2x kit (KIT-PAUSE-RULE-REWORK repair): Paused -> Planned. The 10-28 pause was for silence only ('Last note 2026-10-24, nothing queued'), the pattern the user rejected today: a pause needs a reason AND a resume condition. Its recorded next step still stands: check whether the remaining milestone shipped (now against the installed E14F1F0B, not TRAIN2c), close it with that evidence if so, otherwise size the remaining slice. Back in 'agile next' as ready work.")
inl note_of_eoie_jobs_55 () = Note ("EOIE-JOBS", 55u32, "2026-11-04T15:50Z", "tibia", "11-04 11:50 tibia (cross-team pull, the recorded next step: check + size): NOT SHIPPED in the installed kit (5B9082C1). 'kit job doctor' exists and audits processes, job files and after-chains (finding union in kit/src/kit_jobs_doctor_model/model.spi:40-47: IdleOrphanHost, OrphanCompilerServer, SettledButSupervised, WaitOnSettled, WaitOnMissing, ChainToMissing, HostEnvironmentDiffers). Waiters are checked only for jobs that already settled or vanished; there is no finding for a LIVE supervised job that nobody waits on, which is milestone 2 (n51). KIT_JOB_WATCH (control.rs watched_caller) only names the pid a 'job wait' stays tied to; it is not a completion notification. REMAINING SLICE, sized: in process_findings (model.spi:95-110) collect the job ids of JobWaiter processes, and for each JobSupervisor job whose standing is JobLive and whose id is not among them add a new finding UnwatchedLive : u64 * string (supervisor pid, job id); render it in the doctor CLI; one test block with a live supervised job and no waiter (one finding) and a counter-example with a waiter (none). About 20-30 lines in one module, D2, about 45 min, team kit. Field evidence for why it matters: on 11-04 the node's job start reported 'supervisor never recorded its pid' three times while the jobs ran (KIT-JOB-START-SATURATED), and only a separate waiter showed they were alive.")
inl eoie_table_query () = Task ("EOIE-TABLE-QUERY", "one-call table query over TSV/CSV/JSON: filter, group, count, join and diff two files by key columns (e.g. two suite results.tsv by id+backend), human table plus --json; replaces ~4.8k Import-Csv/Group-Object/ConvertFrom-Json/Measure-Object pipelines and the python difflib/json scripts; source: transcript sweep 2026-10-13 22:13 over ~45k tool calls (claude 41.6k, codex 2.0k, opencode 1.1k, grok 0.2k; zed 8 threads parsed, tool shape not yet recognized); report in main's job tmp/sweep/intent-report.txt", Story, P1, D5, 100u32, Done, "none", "eoie,one-call,adoption", "current", "current")
inl eoie_repo_state () = Task ("EOIE-REPO-STATE", "one-call repo and workspace state: git status grouped by area, diff classification by pattern, file hashes, clock with offset in every receipt; replaces ~3k git, ~3.6k Get-Date and ~0.7k hash calls; source: transcript sweep 2026-10-13 22:13 over ~45k tool calls (claude 41.6k, codex 2.0k, opencode 1.1k, grok 0.2k; zed 8 threads parsed, tool shape not yet recognized); report in main's job tmp/sweep/intent-report.txt", Story, P1, D3, 100u32, Done, "none", "eoie,one-call,adoption", "current", "current")
inl eoie_agent_guide () = Task ("EOIE-AGENT-GUIDE", "generated guide every client reads (AGENTS.md section, Claude/Codex/Grok/OpenCode/Zed): per intent, the eoie call that replaces the pwsh/python habit, with worked examples, generated from the command spec so it never drifts; plus a recurring sweep that reports each client's remaining pwsh/python share as the adoption metric; source: transcript sweep 2026-10-13 22:13 over ~45k tool calls (claude 41.6k, codex 2.0k, opencode 1.1k, grok 0.2k; zed 8 threads parsed, tool shape not yet recognized); report in main's job tmp/sweep/intent-report.txt; 2026-10-14 02:16 lane 3 draft in scratch (C:\\Users\\i574n\\.claude\\jobs\\5a6e53fb\\tmp\\one-call\\agent-guide.md, the real guide is generated once eoie do exists): how each client makes the one call (Claude Code PowerShell here-string, Codex one exec_command instead of Promise.allSettled fan-outs, OpenCode shell, Grok run_terminal_command replacing read_file 101 + grep 58 + search_replace 29, Zed pending thread parsing), then 27 habit -> eoie rows grouped read/search, edit, tables, git, processes, each with calls per client from the 2026-10-14 cluster re-mine (largest: read+search 1,829 C1629/X173/O30, read+walk 841, read+write+replace 725, read+clock 716), mapped to spec/one-call.md cases 1-33; adoption metric: multi-intent pwsh/python share per client and cluster; typo in the note above: read+search is 1,832 calls, not 1,829", Story, P1, D3, 100u32, Done, "none", "eoie,one-call,adoption", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_eoie_agent_guide () = Planning ("EOIE-AGENT-GUIDE", Minutes 30u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_eoie_agent_guide_1 () = Note ("EOIE-AGENT-GUIDE", 1u32, "2026-10-14T12:25Z", "lane-a", "08:25 lane-a: generator prototype lane-a\\one-call\\guide-from-spec.ps1: one-call.md -> guide section (33 intents, recorded-call counts, the exact eoie do program each) so the guide cannot drift from the spec; output agent-guide.generated.md. Real one lands with eoie do.")
inl note_of_eoie_agent_guide_2 () = Note ("EOIE-AGENT-GUIDE", 2u32, "2026-10-14T15:16Z", "main-thread", "11:16 friction (main): 'agile set <root> ID progress 90' answered 'progress must be 0..100'. A non-numeric word in the progress slot reads as an out-of-range number; better: name the slot, show the word received, print the usage line.")
inl note_of_eoie_agent_guide_3 () = Note ("EOIE-AGENT-GUIDE", 3u32, "2026-10-15T10:50Z", "main-thread", "06:50 groom: new friction for the guide: agile note text with embedded double quotes is split by PowerShell's native argument passing (received 6 operands). The guide should say to avoid inner double quotes or pass the text from a file.")
inl note_of_eoie_agent_guide_4 () = Note ("EOIE-AGENT-GUIDE", 4u32, "2026-10-20T12:20Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-20 08:20 eoie lane: the 10-14 friction is fixed in source: agile set names the slot and echoes the word, e.g. agile set: progress must be a whole number 0..100, received '90%'; usage: agile set <root> <id> <progress> <Planned|Active|Blocked|Paused|Done> (same for status). The quoting friction is covered by eoie --args-from (10-18).")
inl note_of_eoie_agent_guide_5 () = Note ("EOIE-AGENT-GUIDE", 5u32, "2026-10-21T06:47Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-21 02:47 in-repo half drafted (scratch agent-guide + apply-guide.ps1, dry-run clean, compile pending): eoie guide <spec/one-call.md> [--out file.md] walks the spec and writes, per '## N.' intent, the first Habit line as 'Instead of:' and the command block as 'One call:'; expected/fixture blocks are left out, so the guide cannot drift from the spec. Routed as direct verb VerbGuide (its name comes from the reflected union). CLI notebook over a two-case mini spec. Inserting the guide into each client's AGENTS.md stays with the bootstrap work that needs the user's go; the adoption sweep is not started.")
inl note_of_eoie_agent_guide_6 () = Note ("EOIE-AGENT-GUIDE", 6u32, "2026-10-21T11:47Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-21 07:47 eoie team, insertion decided under the user's 07:05 direction (decide myself, by evidence). No active repo has an AGENTS.md or CLAUDE.md, and there is no global ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md or ~/.codex/AGENTS.md, so 'insertion' would mean creating global instruction files for every client: the widest-reaching form. Chosen instead (smallest and reversible): after r28 installs, 'eoie guide spec/one-call.md --out docs/one-call-guide.md' writes the guide into the eoie repo, and one Claude memory entry (the project memory all three lanes share) points to it with the top habit -> one-call rows. Global client config files stay untouched until a client is shown not to read the repo doc.")
inl note_of_eoie_agent_guide_7 () = Note ("EOIE-AGENT-GUIDE", 7u32, "2026-10-21T20:35Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-21 16:35 eoie team: shipped in the combined r28+r29 release, installed 15:38 (one-call spec 34/34, all 67 notebooks green): eoie guide spec/one-call.md --out <file> (34 intents, checked 15:42); docs/one-call-guide.md is written at the r30 apply.")
inl note_of_eoie_agent_guide_8 () = Note ("EOIE-AGENT-GUIDE", 8u32, "2026-10-21T20:41Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-21 16:41 eoie team: adoption metric defined before the first run (scratch adoption-sweep/adoption-sweep.ps1, a lab script once the next release publishes). Per client and day: shell_calls = Claude PowerShell/Bash tool_use and Codex shell/exec_command function_call entries; eoie_calls = those invoking the eoie executable; habit_calls = the rest that use the file and text cmdlets or python that eoie replaces (Get-Content, Select-String, Get-ChildItem, Set/Add-Content, ForEach-Object, Measure-Object, Copy/Remove/Move-Item, Test-Path, Start-Sleep, robocopy, findstr, python, [IO.File]); eoie_share = eoie/shell. Output is a TSV for eoie table query. Dedicated tools (Read, Grep, Edit) are not shell calls and are left out. OpenCode keeps no transcripts on this machine; zed is not parsed yet.")
inl note_of_eoie_agent_guide_9 () = Note ("EOIE-AGENT-GUIDE", 9u32, "2026-10-21T20:41Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-21 16:41 eoie team: first adoption sweep (20 s over 1.2 GB of transcripts, last 7 days). Claude eoie_share per day: 10-15 0.116, 10-16 0.067, 10-17 0.211, 10-18 0.197, 10-19 0.310, 10-20 0.382, 10-21 0.518 (3443 shell calls today: 1785 eoie, 1557 habit). 7-day totals: 23804 shell, 6401 eoie, 15537 habit. Codex: no sessions in the window. So the habit share falls as verbs land, and half of today's shell calls are already one eoie call. The remaining habits are the next mining source (the same intent clustering as the 10-13 sweep). Left on the item: make the sweep recurring (an eoie verb or a scheduled job writing this TSV daily) and the shared memory pointer to docs/one-call-guide.md once the r30 apply writes it.")
inl note_of_eoie_agent_guide_10 () = Note ("EOIE-AGENT-GUIDE", 10u32, "2026-10-21T20:46Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-21 16:46 eoie team: what today's 1557 habit calls are (Claude, all sessions, by cmdlet mention): Get-Content 1110, Select-String 1074, ForEach-Object 854, Get-ChildItem 469, Get-Date 345, Measure-Object 143, Copy-Item 136, Get-FileHash 114, Test-Path 85, Remove-Item 81. (A first count put 'git' at 2243, but that was the path C:\\\\home\\\\git in every command; real git calls were about 130: diff 35, status 27, apply 12, log 12.) So the largest remaining habits are reads and searches that eoie read/grep already cover: an adoption gap the guide addresses, not missing verbs. One concrete source of Get-Date: lanes prefix every agile note with local time because notes record only UTC 'at'. If agile note and show printed local time with its offset, that habit would go away; filing it as an EOIE-AGILE-QUERIES papercut.")
inl note_of_eoie_agent_guide_11 () = Note ("EOIE-AGENT-GUIDE", 11u32, "2026-10-21T21:05Z", "claude", "17:05 r35 slice drafted: 'eoie adoption [--days N] [--out f]' (crate eoie_adoption: shell_call/agent_client unions, day_tally, classify notebook with 9 commands, offset line walk). Classification now needs a verb word after eoie (cd into the eoie folder no longer counts); patterns checked in .NET against this session's log (3748 commands). Applies after r34 per r35-plan.md.")
inl note_of_eoie_agent_guide_12 () = Note ("EOIE-AGENT-GUIDE", 12u32, "2026-10-22T02:57Z", "eoie", "22:57 heal plan: the generated guide gets an eoie style contract section and eoie gets a generated AGENTS.md (none exists today in eoie or any sibling): eoie code older than the heal is not a style model; copy from lib/spiral functions, x boundary modules, tibia domain modules and the healed crates listed in standards/; the lib comes first and missing generic helpers go into lib/spiral.")
inl note_of_eoie_agent_guide_13 () = Note ("EOIE-AGENT-GUIDE", 13u32, "2026-10-22T03:42Z", "eoie", "23:42 style contract drafted (scratch agents-contract/AGENTS.md, placed in eoie after release A publishes, then generated by eoie guide once the guide gains a contract section): eoie is generic; code here is not a style model unless listed in standards/healed.tsv; copy from lib/spiral first, then x's boundary modules, tibia's domain modules, healed eoie packages; model first (GADTs, existentials, result unions); host code only in the OS boundary package; missing generic helpers go into lib/spiral; every package references spiral-; touching legacy migrates it; Spiral test blocks; 1000-line crates. Points at eoie standards --ratchet and the edit-time hook.")
inl note_of_eoie_agent_guide_14 () = Note ("EOIE-AGENT-GUIDE", 14u32, "2026-10-22T04:24Z", "eoie", "00:24 placed (before release ABC snapshots the tree): eoie/AGENTS.md style contract (generic project; legacy code is not a model unless listed healed; copy order lib/spiral > x boundary modules > tibia domain modules > healed eoie packages; host code only in the OS boundary; gaps go into lib/spiral; spiral- everywhere; touching legacy migrates it) and standards/healed.spi as Spiral data (union healed_package = Healed : string * string; first row eoie_tree_lease). Next: eoie guide generates the contract section so it can't drift from the rules the census checks.")
inl note_of_eoie_agent_guide_15 () = Note ("EOIE-AGENT-GUIDE", 15u32, "2026-10-22T05:07Z", "eoie", "01:07 evidence the contract works at edit time: reading a file under C:\\home\\git\\eoie (compiler-contracts/test-regeneration.ps1) made Claude Code attach eoie/AGENTS.md to the tool result automatically, so any agent opening eoie files now sees 'legacy code here is not a style model; copy from lib/spiral, x boundary modules, tibia domain modules, healed packages' right where it works, which is the countermeasure to imitating surrounding code.")
inl note_of_eoie_agent_guide_16 () = Note ("EOIE-AGENT-GUIDE", 16u32, "2026-10-22T05:09Z", "eoie", "01:09 peer replies: x and tibia both declined adding an AGENTS.md on a peer suggestion (an agent-instructions file is the user's call; x asks the user in its 07:00 report). Tibia's point stands: rules belong in checks first (tibia's build refuses host calls in lua.spi; eoie standards reads per-package src/standards.spi rules), prose last. So eoie/AGENTS.md stays a pointer for the user to confirm or veto, and the enforceable rules go into eoie standards (census lib-first metrics in ABC, per-package standards.spi rules next) and the edit hook.")
inl note_of_eoie_agent_guide_17 () = Note ("EOIE-AGENT-GUIDE", 17u32, "2026-10-22T09:33Z", "eoie", "05:33 decided by rule instead of asking (memory decide-autonomously, 10-22 version): eoie/AGENTS.md stays if (1) it names no external project, user or path, and (2) every rule it states points at an enforcing check that exists. Measured: 0 external names since the 04:01 generic-drift slice; it points at standards/healed.spi and standards/census.spi (both present), the eoie standards --ratchet verb and the PostToolUse style hook (installed). Both hold, so it stays. It is the edit-time half of the style contract: Claude Code attaches it when any file under eoie is read. Revisit when release D's standards rules replace healed.spi.")
inl note_of_eoie_agent_guide_18 () = Note ("EOIE-AGENT-GUIDE", 18u32, "2026-10-22T19:57Z", "eoie-lane", "15:57 revisit (the 05:33 rule's trigger fired: AGENTS.md now points at per-package standards.spi rules, not healed.spi). Re-checking condition (2), every stated rule has an enforcing check: host code (HostFree + style hook host-macro patterns), lib reference (LibReferenced + hook spiproj check), legacy migration (census ratchet), Spiral test blocks (TestTargets), 1000 lines (topology gate), joined-string rows (census encoded-row), number tables (census number-arm, pipe-table) all hold. Missing: 'no empty-string success sentinels' and 'a missing helper goes into lib/spiral' (only the hook's host-macro advice). Rule written before measuring: a census probe empty-sentinel (pattern '= \\x22\\x22 then', a ratchet so it may not rise per package) is added only if at least 14 of 20 sampled live-tree hits (every k-th hit) are success or absence sentinels rather than ordinary empty-text checks; otherwise the AGENTS.md line is reworded to what the checks enforce. The lib-helper line stays as advice under the host-macro check (it is the hook's own message), so it counts as pointing at that check.")
inl note_of_eoie_agent_guide_19 () = Note ("EOIE-AGENT-GUIDE", 19u32, "2026-10-22T19:59Z", "eoie-lane", "15:59 measured (287 hits of '= \\x22\\x22 then' in src/*.spi; every 14th hit sampled, 20 rows): 10 are absence or success sentinels (missing argument or option, rejection = empty, missing row or status), 10 are ordinary checks (accumulator joins, blank lines, empty joined lists). 10/20 is below the 14/20 bar, so by the rule no empty-sentinel probe is added and the AGENTS.md line is reworded to what the checks enforce: scratch agent-guide/apply-agents-checked-lines.ps1 (dry run on a copy: 1 line changed), applied once F's tree lease ends. New text: 'Model the domain first: unions, GADTs, existentials, records, result unions for outcomes. No joined-string rows or number tables: the census counts both per package and the ratchet refuses growth.' A narrower probe keyed on success-named bindings (problem/rejection/refusal = empty) would need its own item and a rule written before measuring; it is not adopted here.")
inl note_of_eoie_agent_guide_20 () = Note ("EOIE-AGENT-GUIDE", 20u32, "2026-10-22T21:17Z", "eoie-lane", "17:17 applied: AGENTS.md's domain line now states what the census enforces (1 line changed).")
inl note_of_eoie_agent_guide_21 () = Note ("EOIE-AGENT-GUIDE", 21u32, "2026-10-23T10:26Z", "eoie", "06:26 adoption measured with the installed verb (eoie adoption, release F): eoie share of Claude shell calls per day 10-16 0.041, 10-17 0.141, 10-18 0.127, 10-19 0.258, 10-20 0.340, 10-21 0.481, 10-22 0.470, 10-23 (to 06:2x) 0.380; total 32,687 shell calls, 10,317 eoie, 19,447 habit (file/text cmdlets, python), share 0.316. The receipt's days=7 is the requested window (days_back, default 7) while the table also prints today, so 8 rows: correct, but the label reads like a row count; a clearer receipt would say days_back=7 rows=8 (eoie_adoption run.spi:83). Only the claude client appears, and that is right: ~/.codex/sessions and ~/.local/share/opencode hold 0 session files written in the last 8 days (checked 06:28, credential and account files skipped).")
inl note_of_eoie_agent_guide_22 () = Note ("EOIE-AGENT-GUIDE", 22u32, "2026-10-24T02:23Z", "main", "2026-10-23 22:2x main: consolidated pendencies covered here (EOIE-CONSOLIDATED-INTAKE, class a): CLI-1 (help with signature, valid values, preconditions and an example, derived from dispatch; release self-check), CLI-3 (map a need to an existing verb). Spec text: Downloads/eoie-consolidated/agile_eoie.md.")
