inl note_of_hopacsota_p2_4 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P2", 4u32, "2026-10-16T23:38Z", "main-thread", "19:38 main-thread: grain 1 designed in lanes/hopacsota/PLAN.md (Phase 2): one PartEvalSeams.claim kind table key -> Hit | Pending | Owner at the 4 memo sites (closure, type, method join points; backend_switch_validated), single-flight side = today's code inlined (G6 -Seams counts only the 4 calls); also listed the shared state the claim does not cover (per-body HashConsTables, outer Utils.memoize, backend_strings, backend_switch_validated_hc, RustGapTrace.validating). Proof target: G3 all policies at 1 worker, apps/spiral identical, G1/G2. Not started in code.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p2_5 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P2", 5u32, "2026-10-17T10:02Z", "main-thread", "06:02 main-thread night: grain 1 (claim seam, Found | Owner at the type, closure and method sites) complete in staging: builds pass, G1 140 lines, G2 0, G6 only seam calls, G3 -Suite all compile-only 6,906 rows x 3 (single-flight, hopacsota inline, random:23) equal except one host diagnostic-order noise row, apps/spiral Rust byte-identical under all four builds (sha 5F98C7410C3D). In PACK-NIGHT-1017. Grain 2 is designed in lanes/hopacsota/PLAN.md (Claim gains Waiting, seams fill/announce/await replace the 3 final writes and the method annotation write, D4 ancestor rule inside await; proof parallel at 1 worker = inline, then Gate 2). Pack: night pack 10-17 (scripts %LOCALAPPDATA%\\spiral-bin\\packs\\night-1017, each with -DryRun; final review single-flight-20261017-053315 on the exact pack: 6,906 rows, DISAGREE 0, residual-only Zig 457 and Rust 415, 3 intended Rust exit cells (shown as 2 NATIVE-DIFF rows); builds in single-flight, hopacsota and gpt5parallel; hopacsota inline and random:23 equal single-flight on every compile and residual cell; corpus 144 of 144; Zig at C speed on the bench).")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p2_6 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P2", 6u32, "2026-10-17T15:30Z", "main-thread", "11:30 main-thread: the study lane's Hopac and LF measurements are now in lanes/hopacsota/PLAN.md under Decision changes (2026-10-17): a per-build Scheduler.create/kill (2.2 ms; rules out the fix-57 leak class), Latch for fork-join, conCollect for the D6 order-preserving map, the withNackJob loser on the left, critical-path-first worklist order (within 4% of optimal; leaves first is up to 33% worse), and key-table costs that keep D3 server-owned. G2 now bans Stream.mapPipelined* (it hung in 25-45% of runs); the lint passes, G1 140 / G2 0. New shared state from the GADT fix, uninhabited_nesting, becomes a per-body env value under the parallel policy.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p2_7 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P2", 7u32, "2026-10-17T16:23Z", "main-thread", "12:23 main-thread: grain 2a is in the tree and proven. PartEvalSeams.fill replaces the three final memo writes (closure, type, method) and PartEvalSeams.announce the method's annotation write; both are plain writes on every policy today. Gates: G6 (4 added single-flight lines, all seam calls), pair-drift clean, G1 142, G2 0, and G3 with all three modes rebuilt (121/135/378 s): single-flight-20261017-121042, hopacsota-20261017-121442 (inline) and hopacsota-20261017-121849 (random:23) each equal the day-pack bless 111908 on all 6,915 rows (0 cells, DISAGREE 0), about 4 min each with -ReuseNative. apps/spiral byte identity was not rerun for 2a (writes only rerouted); it's part of 2b's proof. Grain 2b's design is in PLAN.md (Phase 2, grain 2 split): the wait lives inside claim, so the sites need no Waiting arms. Patch: lab/hopacsota-p2/grain2a-fill-seams.ps1; the proof runner is lab/hopacsota-p2/g3-proof.ps1.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p2_8 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P2", 8u32, "2026-10-17T18:13Z", "hopacsota-2b-design", "14:12 hopacsota-2b-design (read-only lane) 1/6, recommendation: option (a), adjusted. Owner bodies run direct-style on dedicated body threads with 1536 <<< 20 stacks, the Thread shape that already runs peval at the BuildFile entry (spiral_compiler.fs 186043-186052, IVar.fill result |> Hopac.start); the root stays on that peval thread. A per-build local control scheduler (Scheduler.create, 1 worker, normal stacks, killed at the end) runs only servers: the D3 shard table, the worklist, the D4 wait-for graph. A body reaches them through one bridge, Scheduler.run control job (in Hopac 0.5.1: Hopac.xml line 119, blocks the calling thread), called only from non-worker threads. Rejected (b), job-shaped claim sites: the 4 sites sit inside ty/term recursion, so every caller up the stack becomes a Job, the gpt5parallel shape that D1 and G1 forbid. (c) as stated does not avoid blocking: a direct-style body inside a Hopac job still needs a synchronous table answer on every claim miss, so it needs the same bridge, and blocking workers of the scheduler that runs the servers deadlocks once all workers wait. (c) with restart-on-wait (unwind by exception, rerun after the key fills) repeats the prefix every time, and the root body is 8.6 s self on apps/spiral (R3); it survives only as the rare conflict fallback in 3/6.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p2_9 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P2", 9u32, "2026-10-17T18:14Z", "hopacsota-2b-design", "2/6 what blocks where. The 12:10 split says claim blocks until the key is filled; for closures and annotated methods that serializes them and adds spurious cycles (body A inside its own type key T hits closure k owned by B, whose body claims T). Claim waits only until the entry is usable: a closure always (fun_ty known; today a pending closure is a hit); a method once announced with Some annot (unannotated: until fill, unreachable from type-checked source per D4 2026-10-14); a type join point and backend_switch_validated only when filled. So claim takes a ready predicate per site (or a closure announces right after Owner). Under parallel, a claim of a non-ready key owned by a non-ancestor blocks its body thread in the bridge until announce, fill or abandon. Every such owner is a body thread evaluating that key inline at that moment: type join points and bsv are never deferred, and a deferred body key is ready before it is queued. So nothing ever waits on a queued body: at 1 thread no wait can arise, and at N threads queued bodies cannot starve a waiter. An owner that unwinds without fill (an exception, e.g. swallowed by ty_is_uninhabited at 159212 or by the bsv key try at 160107) must publish Abandoned in a finally; otherwise its waiters hang where single-flight leaves the entry sticky-pending.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p2_10 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P2", 10u32, "2026-10-17T18:14Z", "hopacsota-2b-design", "3/6 D4. A key is an ancestor when it is on the claiming thread own chain (keys it owns and has not filled, in stack order), or on the chain its parent held at the defer point, passed to the deferred body as an immutable snapshot. Single-flight runs a deferred body nested at its call, so a type key pending on the parent chain there is pending for the child; bfs/random would read it filled (R4 and G3 show no such row; the snapshot makes it exact and saves a spurious wait). Ancestor claims return the pending entry as today. Before blocking, the waiter posts the edge waiter body -> owner body to the wait-for server, which rejects an edge that closes a cycle. Without cycles the graph is a forest whose roots are running (control workers never block), and EJP0040/EJP0011 bound a runaway owner, so every wait ends. Two order-sensitive conflicts remain, both cases where single-flight lets the canonically first claimant decide: a cycle (single-flight nests one key in the other, then errors or swallows), and a waiter or later foreign claimant that sees an Abandoned or sticky-pending key (single-flight shows sticky-pending only if the abandoning claim came first in its order). First grain: both raise a conflict that restarts the whole peval under inline, a deterministic restart of a pure evaluation (D6, D16.3; prints are still markers), counted by cause. Nonzero counts on apps/spiral open a canonical-position arbitration grain.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p2_11 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P2", 11u32, "2026-10-17T18:14Z", "hopacsota-2b-design", "4/6 D6 and markers. Each globals list has one writer, the body thread that created it (root, deferred child list, key list from key_body). Found and Owner put the same key marker, so the walk does not depend on who owned a key (the Phase 1 argument holds unchanged). What breaks under threads is the Session itself (53344-53438). It names markers by children.Count, key_lists.Count, failures.Count and prints.Count, keeps them in shared Dictionaries, and only the owner registers a key marker (KeyBody, after claim), while a hitter on another thread may need it first. Under parallel the key marker and its list live in the D3 cell, returned with Found and Owner. Every other marker is named by body path plus local index. Registries are per body thread and are unioned at Finish, after the barrier (root filled, worklist empty, all body threads idle), on the peval thread. After Root, claim falls back to the grain-1 Dictionary path, because codegen-time fills of join_point_type (P5) run single-threaded on the same thread. The first-error rule (fail markers, first on the depth-first walk) and the P1 depth restart are unchanged.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p2_12 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P2", 12u32, "2026-10-17T18:14Z", "hopacsota-2b-design", "5/6 shared state. DynamicIfNesting.depth and RustGapTrace.validating need no env field: a direct-style body never migrates, a body thread runs one body at a time, nested inline owners inherit them as in single-flight, and Defer already resets both to 0 (P1). The G2 ThreadLocal rationale (jobs move between threads) does not apply to dedicated body threads. uninhabited_nesting is one peval-wide ref 0 (159006) shared by every thread; one added seam line in ty_is_uninhabited returns the body-context cell. HashConsTables (families, backend_strings, bsv_hc) Add under lock sync_root (7171), so they are safe (allowlisted, ids never reach text). The outer Utils.memoize tables race (159072, 159233, 159654): PartEvalSeams.family, created by the server and cached per body. backend_switch_validated has no claim yet (160109, 160116): it needs claim and fill. RUST-GAP does an immediate eprintfn into a process-wide seen HashSet; until it is a D13 event, parallel runs 1 body thread when SPIRAL_RUST_GAP_TRACE=1. Body identity for claims (owner id, chain, caches, nesting cell) comes from one ThreadLocal<BodyContext> in the engine; the claim sites carry no env.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p2_13 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P2", 13u32, "2026-10-17T18:14Z", "hopacsota-2b-design", "6/6 budget, decision changes, grain, proof. G1: the engine (BodyContext, body threads, bridge, shard and worklist servers, wait-for server, cells, per-body caches, registry merge, conflict restart) is estimated at 350-500 lines in a new PartEvalEngineHopacsota section; the layer stays near 650 of 5,934, every section under 1,000. G6: 2b needs about 8-10 seam lines, not 0 as 12:10 says: 3 family, 2 bsv, 1 nesting, 3 claim ready args, 1 restart wrapper at the BuildFile peval call. Decision changes to write first: D5 (MaxStackSize goes to body threads; Scheduler.run is the one blocking bridge); G2 (lint Scheduler.run, which today regex misses, plus ThreadStatic, ManualResetEvent, SemaphoreSlim, Mutex, SpinLock and new Thread; allowlist one bridge, one ThreadLocal<BodyContext> and one thread start); D3 (ready-based claim, Abandoned); D4 (chain snapshot, conflict restart). Risk: a claim miss is a cross-thread round trip of a few us, so hits come from per-body caches of ready entries. 2b-1, the first grain: the full engine at SPIRAL_HOPACSOTA_WORKERS=1 (no wait is possible). Proof: G1/G2; G6 -Seams; G3 -Suite all compile-only, parallel@1, inline and random:23 each 0 cells against the bless; apps/spiral F# and Rust byte-identical; G5 parallel@1 within 1.05x, printing claims, bridge calls and cache hits. 2b-2: Gate 2 at 2/4/8, probe -Repeat 5 at 8 on the mega roots, the frontier_hopacsota fixtures and apps/spiral, conflict counts per run.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p2_14 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P2", 14u32, "2026-10-17T22:05Z", "main-thread", "18:05 main-thread lab cleanup: the G3 gate script is promoted to apps/compiler/tmp/scripts/hopacsota-g3.ps1 (-Against <run> [-SkipBuild]; outputs in <cache>/runs/hopacsota-g3-<label>); grain2a-fill-seams.ps1 (applied one-shot edit) deleted; lab/hopacsota-p2 removed.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p2_15 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P2", 15u32, "2026-10-19T12:25Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 08:25 lap 1 grain: PLAN.md already records the 2b decision changes the design note asked to write first (Decision changes section: D5 engine shape at line 873, G2 lint bans Scheduler.run, ThreadStatic, ManualResetEvent, SemaphoreSlim, SpinLock, Mutex, new Thread at line 884); the gate script is apps/compiler/tmp/scripts/hopacsota-g3.ps1; the next step is the PartEvalEngineHopacsota section (350-500 lines est.) Next: write the engine's BodyContext + body-thread bridge as the first engine slice and run hopacsota-g3.ps1 -Against the pack 5 run at 1 worker")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p2_16 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P2", 16u32, "2026-10-19T14:22Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 10:22 lap 2 grain: scoped the engine slice against the gates: the hopacsota layer today is the PartEvalSeamsHopacsota section (spiral_compiler.fs 53397-53539, about 143 lines under #if SPIRAL_CORE_HOPACSOTA) beside single-flight's no-op PartEvalSeams (53540), wired at one call site (159080, PartEvalSeams.start); hopacsota-lint.ps1 budgets the layer at 5,934 lines and each section at 1,000, so the 350-500 line engine fits as one new PartEvalEngineHopacsota section without touching the seam count Next: write the engine section skeleton (BodyContext record, body-thread start with 1.5 GB stack, Scheduler per build) in a candidate copy and run hopacsota-lint.ps1 on it (light)")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p2_17 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P2", 17u32, "2026-10-19T18:28Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 14:28 lap 4 grain: gate prep: hopacsota-g3.ps1 -Against must now name the pack 7 accept single-flight-20261019-125623 (core v1 + Zig,C oracle); hazard found reading it: without -SkipBuild it rebuilds single-flight Debug and the Release builds of all three modes from the tree, so it overwrites any Debug candidate DLL (the same Get-SpiralCompilerDll single-flight Debug path that pack candidates and helper bisects use) and replaces the shared Release DLL; run it only when no candidate review is in flight Next: engine slice (PartEvalEngineHopacsota, 350-500 lines) still the next code step; give hopacsota-g3 a private SPIRAL_BIN_CACHE_DIR for its Debug build when it next runs")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p2_18 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P2", 18u32, "2026-10-20T03:25Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 23:25 user 2026-10-19 23:1x: full hopac SOTA right after XSD; the whole critical path is P0 now (P2 engine, P3 scheduling, P4 diagnostics, P6 promotion, HOPAC-DEPTH real Hopac API); P5 HUD and P7 engine interface stay P1.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p2_19 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P2", 19u32, "2026-10-20T03:57Z", "hopacsota-lane-a", "2026-10-19 23:57 lane A coordination: 2b-1 engine slice in progress as lab/HOPACSOTA-P2/engine-slice.ps1 (dry run by default, --apply writes; 10 anchored edits to spiral_compiler.fs, 1 to scripts/hopacsota-lint.ps1). Shared design points touched: a new standalone #if SPIRAL_CORE_HOPACSOTA block /// ## PartEvalEngineHopacsota (module PartEvalEngine: BodyContext, 4 table shards as Job.foreverServer over Ch with *<+=>- replies, Worklist server with evaluating-thread tokens, Engine with a per-build 1-worker control Scheduler, one bridge Scheduler.run, W body threads at 1.5 GB) placed before PartEvalSeamsHopacsota; in PartEvalSeamsHopacsota: Policy gains Parallel (SPIRAL_HOPACSOTA_POLICY=parallel, refused unless SPIRAL_HOPACSOTA_WORKERS=1 until 2b-2), Session gains a mutable engine created at Root and closed in its finally, Defer dispatches to engine.Spawn, Drain to engine.Drain, and claim/fill/announce keep their signatures but route through the engine while a Parallel session is open. Workers counts concurrently evaluating threads including the root, so at 1 the deferred bodies run on the body thread only during Drain. G2 allowlist gains 3 lines (bridge, ThreadLocal of BodyContext, new Thread). No single-flight line changes. Lane B: please avoid editing the Session/claim text in PartEvalSeamsHopacsota until main stacks this, or anchor around it.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p2_20 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P2", 20u32, "2026-10-20T04:14Z", "hopacsota-lane-a", "2026-10-20 00:14 lane A reply to lane B note 15 (relayed by main): (1) adopted now: the shard is chosen by the table's identity (LanguagePrimitives.PhysicalHash of the Dictionary, or of the outer family Dictionary), so each Dictionary is only ever touched by one shard server, race-free whatever the control worker count; both lab/HOPACSOTA-P2/engine-slice.ps1 (slice 2b-1) and parallel-slice.ps1 (2b-2, stacked) carry it. (2) caller-side guarded decision: not adopted yet, pending my own numbers. From a body thread or the peval thread (never a Hopac worker) any Hopac job, MVar.modifyFun and Lock.duringFun included, still needs a Scheduler.run round trip, so 0.27 us looks like an in-job figure; a caller-thread guard without Hopac would be lock or Monitor (G2-banned). The Ready cache (per thread, filled values only) takes repeat hits off the bridge; I am measuring claims, ready hits and bridges per build with SPIRAL_HOPACSOTA_STATS=1 plus a G5 bench-pair, and will switch if the bridge costs more than the 1.05x budget.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p2_21 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P2", 21u32, "2026-10-20T04:26Z", "hopacsota-lane-a", "2026-10-20 00:26 lane A: ADOPTED lane B's caller-side decision (HOPAC-DEPTH notes 03:53-04:05Z, Scheduler.run runs a non-blocking job on the caller; server Ask 12.5 us vs MVar.modifyFun 0.27 us). Both of my patches now carry it themselves: lab/HOPACSOTA-P2/engine-slice.ps1 (2b-1) has one Table with a total Decide(id, op) under one guard MVar<Table>, Ask = bridge (MVar.modifyFun ...), no shard servers, no shard_count; lab/HOPACSOTA-P2/parallel-slice.ps1 (2b-2, stacked, replaces the whole hopacsota layer region) has the same guard for claim, fill, announce, abandon and the family seam, plus IVar waiters filled after modifyFun, while the worklist and the D4 wait-for graph stay server jobs. So lab/HOPACSOTA-HOPAC-DEPTH/claim-on-caller.ps1 is not needed on top any more (its anchors no longer match engine-slice since 00:12, when Ask took the table); apply order for main: engine-slice.ps1, then parallel-slice.ps1. G1 343 / 576 lines, G2 0 with 3 allowlisted. Builds and gates next.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p2_22 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P2", 22u32, "2026-10-20T04:59Z", "hopacsota-lane-b", "2026-10-20 00:59 hopacsota-lane-b to lane A and main, consolidated (details are on HOPACSOTA-HOPAC-DEPTH notes 14-20). (1) SPEED: the 'replace the server Ask' in note 15 there is WITHDRAWN as a speed requirement. Your Ask costs 12.5 us per claim miss from a body thread, against 0.27 us for a caller-side MVar.modifyFun. But apps/spiral makes only 8,134 bridges per build, with 93.5 pct of 45,599 claims served by BodyContext.Ready, so the Ask costs about 0.1 s, roughly 0.13 pct. G5 at parallel@1 is still OPEN: the loaded A/B (inline min 51.6 s vs parallel-a 70.2 to 90.2 s) cannot resolve a structural gap and does not rule one out. Count rules out the bridge as the cause. (2) CORRECTNESS, the real issue: engine-slice's 4 shard servers all write the same per-family Dictionary through their probe closures. That is race-free only while control has 1 worker AND Hopac keeps running server iterations on the control worker. The probe measured 0 of all iterations on a caller thread, but Hopac's docs do not promise that. Two ways out. (a) Collapse to 1 shard server: still D3 as written, no decision change. (b) Stack lab/HOPACSOTA-HOPAC-DEPTH/claim-on-caller.ps1, one MVar guard with the decision on the caller. That moves D3 off server ownership, so PLAN rule 5 needs this Decision-changes entry first (draft): 'D3 2b: the claim table is owned by one MVar guard taken inside the Scheduler.run bridge, not by server jobs. Measured 2026-10-20 (lab/HOPACSOTA-HOPAC-DEPTH, Hopac 0.5.1, net11 ServerGC, loaded machine): a server claim through *<-=>- from a body thread 12.5 us, 4.7 us aggregate at 8 threads, 44-81 us with control workers = threads; the MVar decision 0.27 us, 1.9 us aggregate at 8 contending threads. The 2026-10-17 rationale (server 1,221 ns) was measured inside jobs, not across the 2b bridge. The functions under the guard are total (an exception inside MVar.modifyFun leaves the MVar empty).' Either way the claim path stays D15-portable (fill-once cell, request/reply). (3) Patch state: claim-on-caller.ps1 applies on top of engine-slice. Dry run checked; applied only to a copy, built, and run on frontier_hopacsota_globals_order and 3 rounds of apps/spiral: byte-identical output, the same stats as engine-a. G3 has not been run on it.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p2_23 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P2", 23u32, "2026-10-20T06:17Z", "hopacsota-lane-a", "2026-10-20 02:17 lane A progress: both slices build (2b-1 Release and Debug, 2b-2 Debug; private caches candidates/hopacsota-p2 and hopacsota-p2b). Lint: G1 343 (2b-1) and 576 (2b-2) lines of 5,934, largest section 178 / 376 of 1,000, G2 0 with 3 allowlisted. pair-drift -Tracked clean (2b-2: PartEval touched on its single-flight side by seam calls only). G6 (lab/HOPACSOTA-P2/g6.ps1, projection of the copy vs the tree): 2b-1 single-flight projection hash unchanged (4b94f6eb1e7ee41e); 2b-2 adds 7 seam lines, 0 foreign. Smoke with lab/HOPACSOTA-P2/g3-batch.ps1 (host --batch, outputs in the candidate dir, never in samples/), frontier_hopacsota_* plus the 5 mega roots, 51 rows against the accept run single-flight-20261019-231348 (same core e00c68fc): 2b-2 Debug parallel at 1, 2, 4 and 8 workers gives the reference residual on every row that returned; one row per run (1 of 51 at 1, 2 and 4 workers, 0 at 8) timed out at 20 s and the batch process then exited 3, on a different fixture each time, so an intermittent hang or stall is open. A second hunt (84 rows, 2b-2 Debug, 1 worker) and a 204-row hunt on 2b-1 Release at 1 worker had none. Next: 2b-2 Release, full-suite G3 at parallel 1 and 8 with stack dumps on stalls.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p2_24 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P2", 24u32, "2026-10-20T07:39Z", "hopacsota-lane-b", "2026-10-20 03:39 lane B, MEASURED bsv numbers. Counter copy now tracks backend_switch_validated keys with structural equality (string * RData[]); apps/spiral to F#, dfs, output 3217A5625053. Up to the end of peval: 23,239 bsv owners and 280,331 bsv hits. Of the hits, 82,895 were in the body that owns the key, 141,364 repeated a key the body had already seen, and 56,072 were the first time a body met a key another body owns. Once bsv gets claim and fill in 2b-2, BodyContext.Ready covers everything except owners, fills and first sightings: about 23.2k + 23.2k + 56.1k = 102.5k bridges per build, on top of the 8.1k from the three claim sites. With engine-slice's server Ask that is about 110k x 8.5-12.5 us = 0.9-1.4 s per build. That is 1.2-1.8 pct of a 75 s build, but 3-4.6 pct when apps/spiral runs in 30 s, which tonight's best runs did. So it eats most of the 1.05x margin by itself. With the caller-side guard (claim-on-caller.ps1, 0.23-0.27 us) it is about 30 ms. REVISED VERDICT: the server Ask is harmless at 2b-1 (bsv still uses a plain lookup) but becomes a G5 risk at 2b-2. Adopt claim-on-caller, or an equivalent caller-side guard, before bsv joins the claim path. Under contention Lock.duringFun holds better than MVar (0.44 vs 3.4 us aggregate at 8 threads), but Lock() needs one lint allowlist line.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p2_25 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P2", 25u32, "2026-10-20T07:52Z", "hopacsota-lane-a", "2026-10-20 03:52 lane A G3 numbers (lab/HOPACSOTA-P2/g3-batch.ps1: the 7,290 job rows of the accept run single-flight-20261019-231348, host --batch at 2 processes, outputs in the candidate dir; compile status and residual hash compared with scripts/compare-runs.ps1): 2b-1 Release (engine-slice.ps1, guard version) at SPIRAL_HOPACSOTA_POLICY=parallel, WORKERS=1: 7,290 rows, 0 differing cells, 499 error / 6,791 ok as the reference, 338 s, no timeout. Engine stats summed over 6,829 builds: 953,292 claims, 877,522 served by the per-thread Ready cache (92 pct), 189,897 bridge calls, 9,931 deferred bodies. 2b-2 Release (parallel-slice.ps1 on top) at WORKERS=1: 7,290 rows, 0 differing cells, 572 s, no timeout; two 8 s stall dumps showed peval waiting in Scheduler.run inside Engine.Fill and then progressing. The 2b-2 family seam hashed the full (backend, body) key a second time per call; the per-thread family cache now uses the outer table's own comparer (parallel-layer.fs.txt), building as candidates/hopacsota-p2c, then G3 at 8, 2 and 4 workers.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p2_26 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P2", 26u32, "2026-10-20T07:57Z", "hopacsota-lane-b", "2026-10-20 03:57 lane B, D4 API RESULT for 2b-2, with a recommended change to how the wait-for graph is held (probe suite graph in lab/HOPACSOTA-HOPAC-DEPTH/Program.fs). Model: 5,000 keys, 0-3 children each, each key evaluated depth-first on 1.5 GB body threads behind one MVar claim guard. A cell is an IVar; a wait is Scheduler.run (IVar.read cell); an abandon is fillFailure. Variant S keeps the wait-for graph as a separate iterateServer: AddEdge with a *<-=>- reply before blocking, RemoveEdge with Ch.send after waking. Variant G keeps it INSIDE the claim guard: the claim walks owner -> the cell that owner waits on -> its owner, stops at a cell already Done, and records waitingOn[thread] in the same atomic step. Result on an acyclic graph, where no true cycle exists, at 2/4/8 threads: S raised 24/184/106 FALSE conflicts and left 55/460/257 keys abandoned. G raised 0 conflicts and got 0 wrong values. Cause of S's false conflicts: the waiter's RemoveEdge lags its wakeup by an OS wake (about 16 us). Meanwhile the owner moves on and asks to wait on the waiter, and the stale edge closes a cycle. In G, stale entries are harmless because the walk ignores cells that are Done, so no RemoveEdge is needed. With 1/50 back edges, so real cross-thread cycles exist, both variants hit conflicts. Those counts include the cascade from abandoned cells; per the 2b design, the first one restarts peval under inline. RECOMMENDATION for 2b-2: put D4's wait-for map in the same guarded state as the claim table and decide Wait/Cycle inside the claim. Fill and abandon mark the cell Done under the guard, then IVar.fill / IVar.fillFailure. Do not use a separate wait-for server. That removes one server and one round trip per wait. It means the 2b decision change's wording 'the wait-for graph, which is owned by a server job' (D4) would need amending; that is main's call under PLAN rule 5.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p2_27 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P2", 27u32, "2026-10-20T07:59Z", "hopacsota-lane-b", "2026-10-20 03:59 lane B, D4 result CONFIRMED at full scale, with one correction (graph suite: 20,000 keys, 5 runs at 2/4/8/8/8 threads, CPU 70-100 pct busy). Correction to my own probe: a root claim that only peeks, finds the key busy and skips must NOT record waitingOn, or the stale entry fakes a cycle later. Record the wait only when the thread really blocks. That bug gave G 1 false conflict in one earlier rerun; fixed. Acyclic graph: guard-resident D4 (G) found 0 cycles and 0 wrong values in 5 of 5 runs. The separate iterateServer (S) found 0-3 false cycles per run and left up to 34 keys abandoned (3 of 5 runs bad). Graph with 1/50 back edges, where real cross-thread cycles exist: G detects exactly 1 cycle in every run, the first one, and the rest is the cascade from abandoned cells; the 2b design restarts peval under inline at that first one. S reports 628-2272 'cycles' there, mostly stale edges. The recommendation in note 26 stands. Cost caveat: with only about 4-20 us of work per key, the 3 bridges per key plus the waits make the parallel run 5-10x SLOWER than sequential at 2-8 threads. This engine shape pays off only for coarse bodies, as in apps/spiral: about 600 claims/s, specializations of ms each. Do not read the graph suite's speed column as engine speed.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p2_28 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P2", 28u32, "2026-10-20T08:13Z", "hopacsota-lane-a", "2026-10-20 04:13 lane A Gate 2 numbers (2b-2 = engine-slice.ps1 + parallel-slice.ps1, built as candidates/hopacsota-p2c; core e00c68fc): G3 at SPIRAL_HOPACSOTA_WORKERS=8: 7,290 rows, 0 differing cells (compile and residual vs the accept run single-flight-20261019-231348), 411 s. apps/spiral at 8 workers: F# sha 76650bfce75f4058 twice and Rust sha f00897e2450bcc30, both byte-identical to the single-flight reference; 731-907 cross-thread waits per build, 0 abandoned, 0 conflicts, 0 wait-for cycles. At 1 worker also identical (2b-1 and 2b-2). Fix found by the first 8-worker apps/spiral run: an unannotated method key exists in practice (lib/spiral trace.spi via backend.spi), and treating its announce as usable gave a foreign thread Recursive join points must be annotated; announce now takes a usable flag (method site: Option.isSome annot; closure seam: true), one more seam argument. G3 at 2 and 4 workers, G4 (5 rounds at 8 workers over frontier_* and the mega roots) and G5 (interleaved apps/spiral, 3 rounds) are queued. Not built: the canonical-order restart for conflicts (0 seen so far).")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p2_29 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P2", 29u32, "2026-10-20T08:36Z", "hopacsota-lane-b", "2026-10-20 04:36 lane B, ROOT CAUSE FOUND for slow bridges; it hits the 2b engine directly. Measured inside apps/spiral with timed copies of the engine (SPIRAL_HOPACSOTA_STATS, Stopwatch only in my copies), WORKERS=1, 5,492 asks, 2 rounds each: engine-a (server Ask) 55-101 us per ask, engine-b (claim-on-caller MVar) 49-65 us per ask. That is 100x the flat-loop 0.27-0.5 us, and the MVar did not help. Cause, read from Hopac 0.5.1's IL and confirmed by probe (suites stack and stacklimit): Scheduler.run goes to Worker.RunOnThisThread, whose Worker.Init stores a [ThreadStatic] Hopac.Core.Worker.ThreadStackLimit the FIRST time a thread calls Scheduler.run: that call's stack pointer minus about 8.5 KB, measured 8,572 bytes. Any later Scheduler.run from more than about 8 KB deeper is past that limit, so Hopac pushes the work to the scheduler instead of running it inline, and the caller blocks in Condition.Wait (Monitor.Wait) until the control worker runs it. Probe numbers, on a thread whose first call was at the top: a trivial MVar bridge 16 MB deeper takes 11.9 us min / 16.6 us median. The same thread with ThreadStackLimit lowered by 1 GB takes 0.39 us. A thread whose first call was already deep takes 0.25-0.77 us at any depth up to 64 MB. In the engine, every body thread makes its first bridge (Take) at the top of the thread and the peval thread at Root, and then claims come from deep inside ty/term recursion. So EVERY claim takes the slow path. With bsv claims in 2b-2 (about 100k asks) that would be 5-10 s per apps/spiral build. FIX (API-level, lab/HOPACSOTA-HOPAC-DEPTH/stack-limit.ps1, stacks after engine-slice, one anchored edit): after each thread's first bridge (Engine constructor on the peval thread, body_loop on each body thread), lower Worker.ThreadStackLimit through reflection to the anchor minus (body_stack_bytes - 128 MB). It is a private field: Byte*, Pointer.Unbox/Box. Hopac's limit only protects inline job recursion, and bridge jobs are a few frames, so a 128 MB margin above the real 1.5 GB stack end is safe. Dry run by default; refuses an already-applied core and a core without the engine. engine-b3 (b2 plus this) is building now to measure ask_ms in apps/spiral.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p2_30 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P2", 30u32, "2026-10-20T08:39Z", "hopacsota-lane-a", "2026-10-20 04:39 lane A: G3 at 2 workers: 7,290 rows, 0 differing cells (366 s). G4: 5 builds of each frontier_* and mega-root row at 8 workers in one warm batch process (895 jobs, 181 outputs): 0 outputs with differing hashes across rounds, and all 895 equal to the reference compile status and residual. G5 interleaved apps/spiral F#, 3 rounds, machine loaded by other lanes (so noisy): single-flight 39.5/64.2/59.9 s (median 59.9), 2b-1 parallel@1 44.1/52.9/58.0 (52.9), 2b-2 parallel@1 41.9/45.1/69.8 (45.1), 2b-2 parallel@8 58.9/72.1/85.9 (72.1), every output sha 76650bfce75f4058. Reading: 1 worker sits inside the 1.05x budget on medians, and 8 workers is about 1.2x slower than 1 worker. That matches lane B's note 29 (deep bridges fall off Hopac's inline path, 93k bridges per 8-worker build serialize on the single control worker). Adopted for measurement: lab/HOPACSOTA-HOPAC-DEPTH/stack-limit.ps1 stacks cleanly AFTER parallel-slice.ps1 (anchor body_loop, still present in the replaced layer); built as candidates/hopacsota-p2d (G1 593, G2 0), interleaved A/B with and without it at 1 and 8 workers plus G3 at 8 are queued.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p2_31 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P2", 31u32, "2026-10-20T08:48Z", "hopacsota-lane-b", "2026-10-20 04:48 lane B, stack-limit FIX MEASURED in the real engine. apps/spiral to F#, WORKERS=1, parallel policy, 2 interleaved rounds, timed copies. engine-b2 (lane A's slice + claim-on-caller): 5,492 asks took 211 / 230 ms, 38-42 us per ask. engine-b3 (b2 + stack-limit.ps1): 14.4 / 16.7 ms, 2.6-3.0 us per ask, 14x faster. All outputs are byte-identical (3217A5625053). The 4 frontier_hopacsota fixtures and the 5 mega roots under b3 match inline-a hash for hash, and two_errors still exits 5. Asks on the mega roots: lean_cic 1,067 in 8.4 ms, spiral_proves 874 in 6.4 ms, omniledger 284 in 3.2 ms, zeta 190 in 2.1 ms. Wall time stays within noise (b2 27.0 / 37.2 s, b3 27.4 / 31.8 s, inline-a 33.1 / 53.5 s; machine loaded). Projected for 2b-2 with bsv claims (about 110k asks): about 0.3 s with the fix, against about 4-5 s without it. RECOMMENDATION for lane A: stack stack-limit.ps1, and claim-on-caller.ps1 for the bsv phase. Without the stack-limit fix the caller-side guard gains nothing, because both paths block in Condition.Wait. The engine-a2 (server Ask) number is 55-101 us per ask; the fix does not help a server round trip, which always crosses threads.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p2_32 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P2", 32u32, "2026-10-20T09:22Z", "hopacsota-lane-b", "2026-10-20 05:22 hopacsota-lane-b, SUPERSEDING SUMMARY for lane A and main. It replaces the cost figures in my P2 notes 22, 24, 29 and 31. WITHDRAWN as engine costs: the flat-loop 0.27 us claim and the 8.5-12.5 us server Ask, and note 24's bsv projections (0.9-1.4 s server, 30 ms caller-side), which assumed those figures. IN-ENGINE (apps/spiral to F#, WORKERS=1, parallel, 5,492 asks per build, timed copies, output 3217A5625053 identical everywhere): a2, lane A's server Ask, 55-101 us per ask; a3, the same plus stack-limit, 50-121 us per ask, because the fix cannot help a call that always crosses threads; b2, claim-on-caller, 38-42 us per ask; b3, claim-on-caller plus stack-limit, 2.6-3.4 us per ask, over 4 builds. Projected for 2b-2 with bsv claims (about 110k asks): server Ask, with or without the fix, 5.5-13 s per build, which rules out option (a), collapsing to one shard server; b3 about 0.3-0.4 s. So the D3 decision change is REQUIRED, not optional. Draft entry: 'D3 2b: the claim table and the D4 wait-for map are owned by one MVar guard taken inside the Scheduler.run bridge, on the caller; servers keep only the worklist. Hopac anchors a per-thread stack limit at each thread's first Scheduler.run (Worker.ThreadStackLimit, about 8.5 KB below it), so each body thread and the peval thread lower it once after their first bridge. Measured 2026-10-20: a server Ask costs 55-121 us per ask in the engine, the caller-side guard with the lowered limit 2.6-3.4 us.' D4 wording change: the wait-for graph lives in the guard, not in a server job; a separate server raised false cycles (0-184 per run on a DAG). STACK ORDER: engine-slice.ps1, then claim-on-caller.ps1, then stack-limit.ps1. spawn-send.ps1 is optional, low value, never built. stack-limit assumptions: every lowered thread has a 1.5 GB stack (body threads, and BuildFile starts a fresh 1536 <<< 20 peval thread per build); its first bridge happens within 128 MB of the top. It is idempotent per thread: it skips when the limit already sits more than 512 MB below the current frame. G5 preview, 5 interleaved rounds, loaded machine, min / median seconds: inline-a 26.9 / 35.7, bfs-a 30.6 / 36.9, parallel-b3 32.3 / 34.0. The spread is 27-64 s, so G5 stays OPEN: no variant is consistently slower, and the minimum ratio of b3 to inline is 1.20 while the median ratio is 0.95. Deep waits (waiter 16 MB down) cost 46-200 us per round trip with or without the fix; they are rare (21 per build at 1 worker).")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p2_33 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P2", 33u32, "2026-10-20T09:24Z", "hopacsota-lane-b", "2026-10-20 05:24 lane B, CORRECTION to my note 20's first lever ('create the Engine lazily at the first Spawn'): WITHDRAWN. In engine-slice, claim and fill take the plain Dictionary path while session.Engine is null. Keys filled before a lazily created engine would then be missing from Shard.filled and answer Present(value, filled=false) forever. At 2b-2 the ready predicate would make type join points and bsv wait for a fill that already happened, so the waiter would hang. Keep Engine creation at Root; only the body threads may start lazily. It is not needed for speed anyway: with stack-limit, a mega root that defers nothing pays little (lean_cic: 1,067 asks in 8.4 ms). On HOPACSOTA-HOPAC-DEPTH, note 33 supersedes the cost verdicts of notes 15, 18, 19, 23 and 24 (flat-loop or count-based).")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p2_34 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P2", 34u32, "2026-10-20T09:38Z", "hopacsota-lane-a", "2026-10-20 05:38 lane A, stack-limit A/B (main's request, lane B note 29): interleaved apps/spiral F#, 3 rounds on a quieter machine, every output sha 76650bfce75f4058. Medians: single-flight 33.3 s (32.8/33.3/48.4); 2b-2 without stack-limit 36.4 s at 1 worker (36.4/39.6/34.8, 1.09x) and 44.5 s at 8 (44.5/44.8/44.5, 1.34x); with lab/HOPACSOTA-HOPAC-DEPTH/stack-limit.ps1 stacked after parallel-slice.ps1 34.1 s at 1 worker (34.1/35.1/34.0, 1.02x, inside 1.05x) and 45.6 s at 8 (43.6/49.8/45.6, 1.37x). Bridges per build 70.8k at 1 worker and 92-93k at 8, waits 629-952. G3 at 8 workers with stack-limit: 7,290 rows, 0 cells. Reading: stack-limit is worth about 6 pct at 1 worker and nothing visible at 8. At 8 workers the build is slower than at 1 (R3 bound 2.76x), and that is the first Phase 3 measurement: MVar guard contention, workstation GC under 8 allocating threads (the batch runs set DOTNET_gcServer=0), wait handoffs. G3 2b-2 at 1 worker (final text): 7,290 rows, 0 cells, 454 s.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p2_35 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P2", 35u32, "2026-10-20T09:41Z", "hopacsota-lane-b", "2026-10-20 05:41 lane B, final composition check on a FRESH copy of the tree core (candidates/hopac-depth/stack-final). Lane A's CURRENT engine-slice.ps1 (modified 00:25) already contains the caller-side guard (Table.Decide under MVar.modifyFun), so claim-on-caller.ps1 is subsumed: on the current slice it refuses as already applied, which is the intended behaviour. Order tried: engine-slice (dry run, then apply) and stack-limit.ps1 in its current idempotent form (dry run, then apply, then a rerun that is refused). Lint: G1 355 lines, G2 0 hits with lane A's 3 allowlisted exceptions; stack-limit needs no new line. Release build OK. At WORKERS=1, parallel: the 4 frontier_hopacsota fixtures and the 5 mega roots are hash-identical to inline-a, and two_errors exits 5. apps/spiral gives 3217A5625053 (47.3 s, one loaded run), engine stats bridges=8136. So the stack to land is lane A's current engine-slice.ps1 plus lab/HOPACSOTA-HOPAC-DEPTH/stack-limit.ps1, with the D3/D4 decision-change text from note 33. Fragility: stack-limit reads Hopac 0.5.1's private Worker.ThreadStackLimit. After a Hopac upgrade GetField can return null; then it silently does nothing, and every deep claim falls back to the 40-100 us path. Lane A may want the stats line to report whether the limit was lowered.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p2_36 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P2", 36u32, "2026-10-20T10:01Z", "hopacsota-lane-b", "2026-10-20 06:01 lane B, last G5 preview, for the exact stack to land: lane A's current engine-slice plus stack-limit (stack-final, untimed). apps/spiral to F#, 5 interleaved rounds, CPU 100 pct busy. Seconds per round, inline-a then parallel-final: 41.2 / 48.6, 30.6 / 31.9, 30.2 / 37.6, 36.7 / 39.8, 46.1 / 30.6. Min 30.2 vs 30.6 (1.013x), median 36.7 vs 37.6 (1.025x); parallel-final was slower in 4 of 5 pairs. All 10 outputs give 3217A5625053. Min and median both sit inside 1.05x, but on a saturated machine this is a preview, not a G5 pass; G5 needs the quiet-machine run. End of lane B's night.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p2_37 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P2", 37u32, "2026-10-20T10:17Z", "hopacsota-lane-a", "2026-10-20 06:17 lane A HANDOFF. Ready for a pack, in this order on tree core E00C68FCDAA3E950: (1) lab/HOPACSOTA-P2/engine-slice.ps1 (2b-1; edits spiral_compiler.fs and scripts/hopacsota-lint.ps1, no single-flight line); (2) lab/HOPACSOTA-P2/parallel-slice.ps1 (2b-2; replaces the hopacsota layer region from lab/HOPACSOTA-P2/parallel-layer.fs.txt, which must sit beside it, plus 9 single-flight seam edits); (3) lane B's lab/HOPACSOTA-HOPAC-DEPTH/stack-limit.ps1; (4) lab/HOPACSOTA-P2/plan-entry.ps1 (PLAN.md decision-change entry and Phase 2 scoreboard row, from plan-entry.md.txt and plan-row.md.txt). All dry-run by default, --apply writes, anchors exactly once, refuse re-apply. (1)+(2) on the tree give 60E03C031C7A4365, the gated candidate. Verified on it: G3 7,290 rows 0 cells at parallel 1/2/4/8 and random:23; single-flight built from it 0 cells; G4 5 fresh processes at 8 workers 0 cells; apps/spiral F#/Rust identical at 1 and 8 workers. (3) was gated in its 04:40 text (G3 at 8: 0 cells; G5); its 04:53 text (idempotent lowering) gives 4BEAEF0AE366FB07 and was not gated by me. Unproven: G5 on a quiet machine (1 worker 1.09x without stack-limit, 1.02x with; 8 workers 1.34-1.37x), the native tier (residuals identical, native memo is keyed by output bytes), the gpt5parallel build, the D4 canonical restart (conflicts raise; 0 seen), and my D4 graph is a separate server where lane B recommends the claim guard (0 cycles in all runs; a waiter adds its edge before it blocks, so a stale-edge false cycle is possible).")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p2_38 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P2", 38u32, "2026-10-20T10:18Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-20 06:18 main: lane A and lane B are done. The pack for next session, in order: (1) lab/HOPACSOTA-P2/engine-slice.ps1, (2) lab/HOPACSOTA-P2/parallel-slice.ps1 with parallel-layer.fs.txt beside it, (3) lab/HOPACSOTA-HOPAC-DEPTH/stack-limit.ps1, (4) lab/HOPACSOTA-P2/plan-entry.ps1 (PLAN.md decision-change entry and Phase 2 row). Steps 1 and 2 on core E00C68FCDAA3E950 give 60E03C031C7A4365, the file lane A gated. Before packing, re-dry-run if the tree moved, and gate lane B's newer stack-limit text (04:53, once per thread; gives 4BEAEF0AE366FB07, ungated). Evidence: full suite 7,290 rows identical to single-flight-20261019-231348 at 1, 2, 4, 8 workers and random:23; 5-process determinism at 8 workers; apps/spiral F# and Rust byte-identical at 1 and 8 workers. Speed on a loaded machine: 1 worker plus stack-limit 1.02x (passes 1.05x), 8 workers 1.34-1.37x slower. Still open: G5 on a quiet machine, why 8 workers is slower (first Phase 3 measurement), restart on conflict (none seen), the wait-for graph inside the claim guard, and main's own hopacsota-g3.ps1 run (lane A used a lab equivalent).")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p2_39 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P2", 39u32, "2026-10-20T20:46Z", "main", "16:46 G3 at 8 workers on the redone tree (lab g3-batch.ps1, jobs from single-flight-20261019-231348, Parallel 2): installed hopacsota core 814F3B00E65A162E with SPIRAL_HOPACSOTA_WORKERS=8 vs installed single-flight core EA7AC11D786629F2, 7290 jobs each. Compile status and residual hash are the same on all 7290 (ok 6791, error 499 on both), differing 0. Wall: single-flight 274 s, hopacsota w8 264 s (policy inline, so no speedup claim).")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p2_40 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P2", 40u32, "2026-10-20T20:47Z", "main", "16:47 Phase 3 first experiment queued (heavy job hopacsota-gc-ab, lab HOPACSOTA-P2/gc-ab.ps1): apps/spiral F# with the installed hopacsota DLL and policy parallel, 3 interleaved rounds of w1-workstation, w8-workstation and w8-server GC, recording seconds, peak working set, output hash and engine stats. Reasoning: 8 workers cost about 8 s over about 92k bridges, roughly 87 us per bridge, far above a Scheduler.run handoff. So per-thread allocation under workstation GC (the batch runs pin DOTNET_gcServer=0) is tested before the guard/bridge design.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p2_41 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P2", 41u32, "2026-10-20T22:35Z", "main", "18:35 GC A/B result (hopacsota core 2F2586DB0277DEA2, apps/spiral F#, policy parallel, 3 interleaved rounds, loaded machine): w1-workstation 52.5/30.9/37.5 s (median 37.5), w8-workstation 44.7/38.2/45.8 (median 44.7), w8-server 54.6/37.9/41.5 (median 41.5). Peak 357-372 MB workstation, about 497 MB server. All 9 outputs 3217A5625053. Engine stats: w1 bridges 70.8k, waits 0; w8 bridges 93.0k, waits 686-832, conflicts 0. Server GC gains about 7 pct at 8 workers for +130 MB but 8 workers stay slower than 1 (1.11-1.19x). GC is not the dominant cost. Next suspects: the 22k extra bridges at 8 workers (ready_hits fall 307k -> 288k because per-thread Ready caches split) and the single-worker control scheduler that serializes every bridge.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p2_42 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P2", 42u32, "2026-10-20T22:40Z", "main", "18:40 Phase 3 candidate queued (heavy job published-ready, lab HOPACSOTA-P2/published-ready.ps1 + ready-ab.ps1). The engine table publishes every filled entry (value and marker) into an ImmutableDictionary: one writer, the control scheduler, with Volatile.Write; body threads read it with Volatile.Read. A Claim that misses its thread's Ready cache takes a published entry without a bridge, doing exactly what Present(filled=true) does (marker into Marks.Keys, value into Ready). FillOp carries the value. No banned construct (no ConcurrentDictionary, lock, Interlocked). Gates: lint, G3 at w8 and w2 vs after-redo-single-flight, apps/spiral A/B base vs candidate at w1/w8 with bridge counts. Expectation: w8 bridges 93k -> about 71k.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p2_43 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P2", 43u32, "2026-10-20T23:53Z", "main", "19:53 published-ready result: correct but not the fix. G3 at w8 and w2 vs single-flight: 7,290/7,290 identical; apps/spiral output 3217A5625053 in all 12 runs. Bridges at w8 fell only 93.0k -> 91.3-92.2k, and ready_hits rose by about 1.5k: the 22k extra bridges at 8 workers are not repeated claims of already-filled entries, they are first claims racing on keys still being evaluated (waits 651-928). Timings (fixed order base-w1, cand-w1, base-w8, cand-w8, loaded box): w8 median 55.2 -> 38.2 s, but w1 also moved 36.3 -> 28.6 s on identical work (same bridges), so ordering and load noise dominate and no speed claim is made. Keep the candidate out of the evening pack until an interleaved, order-alternating A/B on a quiet box shows a real gain; next hypothesis to test: the single-worker control scheduler serializing every bridge.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p2_44 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P2", 44u32, "2026-10-21T02:44Z", "main", "22:44 next experiment written (runs after tibia's PR-16 block, after formatter-c9b because every candidate build compiles the tree host): lab HOPACSOTA-P2/control-workers.ps1 adds SPIRAL_HOPACSOTA_CONTROL_WORKERS (default 1, so the default build is unchanged) for the control scheduler that serves every bridge. The Table stays behind one MVar and the graph and worklist stay single servers fed by channels, so more control workers only remove the handoff queue at 8 body workers. Gates: lint, G3 at w8 with cw8 vs single-flight, then lab alternating-ab.ps1 on apps/spiral F#: 5 rounds of w1-cw1, w8-cw1, w8-cw2, w8-cw8, order reversed on even rounds (tonight's fixed-order A/B was order noise), medians relative to w1-cw1.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p2_45 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P2", 45u32, "2026-10-21T18:58Z", "main", "14:58 control-workers result (job main-control-workers, capped-run peak 1.9 GB; candidate core B6F8476368F65B98, lint G1 603 lines, G2 0): correct but not the fix. G3 w8 cw8 vs after-redo-single-flight: 7,290/7,290 same, 0 differing (533 s). apps/spiral F# order-alternating A/B, 5 rounds: medians w1-cw1 35.8 s; w8-cw1 52.2 (1.46x); w8-cw2 52.8 (1.47x); w8-cw8 51.5 (1.44x); waits 494-933 at w8, 0 at w1. The control-worker count makes no difference, so the single-worker control scheduler is not the serializer (hypothesis refuted). Output hashes agree across variants in every round. The hash moved from 3217A5625053 to 544AEC410394 during round 2 for all variants: my sm'.spi concat_list edit at 14:46 (apps/spiral reads lib) landed mid-run, and it changes only output bytes, not the comparison. Keep SPIRAL_HOPACSOTA_CONTROL_WORKERS out of the pack. Remaining suspects for w8 > w1: the waits (body threads blocked on keys still being evaluated, first-claim races) and 8 body threads on an 8-core box shared with other lanes. Next experiment: a waits census (key depth and wait duration per wait) at w8 vs w2/w4, plus w2/w4 points in the alternating A/B, to see whether slowdown scales with waits or with thread count.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p2_46 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P2", 46u32, "2026-10-21T19:04Z", "main", "15:04 waits census staged: lab HOPACSOTA-P2/waits-census.ps1 (4 anchors, dry run matches once each on the tree core) adds per body thread WaitTicks, LongestWaitTicks and BridgeTicks (Stopwatch.GetTimestamp, no banned construct) and a 'hopacsota census: wait_ms longest_wait_ms bridge_ms' stats line. alternating-ab.ps1 now records bridges and the census columns. Job script scratchpad waits-census-job.ps1: lint, build a hopacsota Release candidate, 5 order-alternating rounds of w1/w2/w4/w8 on apps/spiral F#. Reading rule, fixed before measuring, with blocked = wait_ms / (seconds x workers x 1000) and bridge share = bridge_ms / (seconds x workers x 1000) at w8: (a) blocked >= 25% and wait_ms rises w2 < w4 < w8 -> waits are the cause; next: claim ordering so threads don't start keys another thread is about to need. (b) blocked < 10% and bridge share >= 25% -> the bridge round trip is the cause; next: batch claims per bridge. (c) both < 10% -> oversubscription or GC (8 body threads on 8 cores shared with other lanes); next: w8 on a quiet box and a w4 cap. Otherwise: mixed, report the numbers. Runs after tibia's live block and the night-pack install.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p2_47 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P2", 47u32, "2026-10-21T19:19Z", "main", "15:19 waits census not run: job main-waits-census stayed queued from 15:05 (both heavy slots held by x-split-fix3 and eoie-release-r29) and could not finish before tibia's 15:45-17:20 live block, so I stopped it at 15:19. Requeued after tibia's 'over' and the night-pack install, with the same script and reading rule (note 46).")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p2_48 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P2", 48u32, "2026-10-21T20:41Z", "main", "16:41 compile-free re-analysis of today's control-workers A/B (alternating-ab/20261021-143915, engine stats per run): (1) Among the 15 w8 runs, seconds correlate NEGATIVELY with waits (r = -0.58) and bridges (r = -0.83): slower runs had fewer races, the signature of an externally loaded box throttling the body threads, not of waits causing slowness. (2) Round-matched w8-median/w1 ratios swing 2.37, 1.32, 0.81, 1.46, 1.26; round 2 had every variant above 77 s, so load dominates the medians. (3) Structural cost of w8: +22k bridges (70.8k -> 92.7k) and -20k ready_hits per build. At the 38-101 us per ask measured on 10-20 that is about 1-2 s, far below the 15 s median gap. Interim reading: today's 1.45x is not established as a property of the engine. The census (note 46) still runs, adding w2/w4 and wait_ms, but its verdict needs a quiet box; I'll mark load next to each round (cores busy by other lanes, sampled at start and end) in the census job so loaded rounds can be excluded by a rule written before reading: drop a round when other lanes used >= 2 cores at its start or end.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p2_49 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P2", 49u32, "2026-10-21T21:20Z", "main", "17:20 the waits census did not run: its patch used Stopwatch, which hopacsota-lint G2 bans (with DateTime, for determinism), so the candidate build stopped at the lint (evening-a, 17:08). The patch now times with System.Environment.TickCount64, which is not banned: about 15.6 ms resolution on Windows, but each wait is the difference of two readings, so the sum over hundreds of waits stays an unbiased estimate. longest_wait_ms is only good to about 16 ms. Dry run plus lint: G1 609 lines, G2 0 hits. Requeued after the night-pack install, in the heavy slot beside the review, before SEFAZ variant 3 (P0 before P1).")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p2_50 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P2", 50u32, "2026-10-22T08:21Z", "main", "04:21 main: the queued waits-census job script was only in my session scratchpad; it now lives at lab/HOPACSOTA-P2/waits-census-job.ps1 (work dir %TEMP%\\hopacsota-waits, same steps: waits-census.ps1 on a core copy, hopacsota-lint, Release hopacsota candidate, alternating-ab w1/w2/w4/w8 x 5 rounds). Its reading rule stays notes 46 and 48: it needs a quiet box, and rounds where other lanes use >= 2 cores at start or end are dropped.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p2_51 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P2", 51u32, "2026-10-22T21:25Z", "main", "2026-10-22 17:25 main: partial lab drain. Deleted because they landed: engine-slice.ps1 (the PartEvalEngineHopacsota section is in the tree core), parallel-slice.ps1 (its already-applied markers, exception HopacsotaConflict and the isNull engine dispatch, are in the core), plan-row.md.txt and plan-entry.md.txt with plan-entry.ps1 (every line is in lanes/hopacsota/PLAN.md, 1/1 and 8/8). Kept: published-ready.ps1 (its FillOp filled_value edit is NOT in the core: open), the waits-census job and waits-census.ps1 (queued for a quiet box), and the measurement runners (alternating-ab, ready-ab, gc-ab, g3-batch, g3-suite, g6, control-workers, hang-hunt, appspiral, build-candidate) that the census and Gate 2/3 runs reuse, plus parallel-layer.fs.txt as the reference text of the parallel layer.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p2_52 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P2", 52u32, "2026-10-25T13:25Z", "main", "10-25 09:25 main groom: unstuck. It sat 2.8 days because the waits census 'needs a quiet box' and nothing ever asked for one: with every lane queueing work, the box is never quiet by chance. The census job is now queued behind eoie's quiet gate (job-1792934679977: --heavy --quiet --quiet-hold 5 --quiet-wait 600; eoie holds compiles off while it starts; reading rule unchanged, notes 46 and 48). Both open slices re-verified today: waits-census.ps1 applies 3/3 anchors on the tree core 17D970FE, and published-ready.ps1 (the FillOp filled_value edit, still not in the core) applies 5/5 on the 5l' composite 0C481695. published-ready rides the pack after 5l' (5m).")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p2_53 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P2", 53u32, "2026-10-27T23:03Z", "main", "10-27 19:03 main: Groom 10-27 18:5x: the waits census FINISHED on 10-25 (job-1792934679977, exit 0) and was never read; this is its reading. 5 interleaved rounds, policy=parallel, F# output of the census source, variants w1/w2/w4/w8 workers with cw1 (STATS on). Speed, quiet rounds: w1 median 190.4 s; w2 154.5 s (0.81x); w4 129 s (0.68x, best); w8 149.8 s (0.79x, slower than w4: waits 83-149 and wait time 43-72 s per run, against 20-28 and 1-4 s at w2). DETERMINISM, the real finding: the hash column is the compiled output file's hash (alternating-ab.ps1:42). w1 gives one output (D87D045CED82) in 5/5 rounds, but w2 gives 3 distinct outputs in 5 rounds (928ADE58D77F, 9EB6270DC162, F8E6A04D27DA), and w4 and w8 each give 5 distinct outputs (a different output every run). So the parallel policy is NOT deterministic at 2 or more workers, while every single-threaded schedule (inline, dfs, bfs, random) is byte-identical (P6, 10-24). Next for P2, in this order: (1) a determinism gate, set before measuring: policy=parallel at w2, w4 and w8, 5 runs each, must give 1 hash per variant, equal to w1's and to single-flight's; (2) localise the order dependence: diff two w4 outputs and attribute the first differing region (likely candidates: tag or name counters assigned in completion order, or global and print emission order merged by arrival instead of single-flight's depth-first order, PLAN.md D-rules); (3) then the speed work (w8 contention). Determinism comes before any speed claim.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p2_54 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P2", 54u32, "2026-10-27T23:12Z", "compiler", "Determinism gate (main 19:0x, recorded before measuring): SPIRAL_HOPACSOTA_POLICY=parallel at w2/w4/w8, 5 runs each, 1 hash per variant, equal to w1 AND to single-flight. Census job-1792934679977: parallel nondeterministic (w1 stable D87D045CED82 5/5, w2 3-distinct/5, w4+w8 differ every run). Build-free first step: lab/HOPACSOTA-P2/alternating-ab.ps1 (hashes F# at :42) -- diff two parallel outputs, attribute first divergence (bet: completion-order tag/name counters, or arrival-merged globals/prints vs single-flight depth-first). Taken by compiler lane as priority #2 after CCMR land.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p2_55 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P2", 55u32, "2026-10-27T23:20Z", "compiler", "BUILD-FREE STEP DONE (diffed 10-25 census .fsx, run 20261025-115702). w1 deterministic 5/5 (D87D045CED); w8 nondeterministic (5 distinct). w8 r1 vs r2 = PURE REORDERING (sorted-identical): 35 diff lines of 17779, SAME lines different ORDER. The reordered lines are top-level TYPE decls -- FFI/external aliases (BTreeMap, mlua_*, regex_*, serde_json_*, std_pin_*), 33/35 \"type =\". ROOT: parallel emits these external-type globals in ARRIVAL/completion order, not single-flight depth-first (confirms the bet). FIX: route the type-decl emission through the per-key depth-first merge hopacsota already uses for globals/prints (D-rules). NEXT: locate the type-decl emit site in the parallel layer.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p2_56 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P2", 56u32, "2026-10-27T23:37Z", "compiler", "MERGE read (parallel-layer Session.Finish :492-523): the globals merge IS depth-first -- walk() expands per-key markers recursively; each Defer adds its marker to the PARENT list at defer-time (:434, pre-spawn), body fills a private child. So Defer/KeyBody/Print globals are deterministic. The reordered FFI type-alias globals must BYPASS the marker path -- Add-ed to a SHARED adapter.globals concurrently during PARALLEL backend_switch validation (backend.rust_type emits F# aliases via globals per AGENTS.md). FIX: route them through KeyBody/Defer markers for depth-first order. NEXT: find the shared-globals Add site in backend_switch validation/rust_type.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p2_57 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P2", 57u32, "2026-10-27T23:40Z", "compiler", "FIX LOCUS (spiral_compiler.fs:160873-874, hopacsota core bsv). :160873 key_body hopacsota (box key) \"bsv\" s sets up a per-key MARKER + ordered child globals. BUT :160874 OVERRIDES s.globals with the shared validation_root_globals root -> validation type-alias globals go to the SHARED list (arrival order), not the ordered child = the reordering. FIX: under hopacsota keep the key_body child globals (dont override). validation_root_globals exists for single-flight (globals reach root); hopacsota should use the key_body child (reaches root via Finish walk, ordered).")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p2_58 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P2", 58u32, "2026-10-27T23:43Z", "compiler", "FIX CONSTRAINT (main 19:3x): aliases must reach root EXACTLY ONCE at single-flights position -- kept in the key_body child, NOT dropped/duplicated by UnusedGlobals.prune or the key_body merge. Gate checks byte-equality vs single-flight; ADD count check: each \"type <alias> =\" count in parallel == single-flight (duplicate/drop fails loudly). Impl: add PartEvalSeams.active (= not isNull session) + condition :160874 globals to keep s.globals (the key_body child) when active, else validation_root_globals as today. ORDER: after CCMR land.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p2_59 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P2", 59u32, "2026-10-27T23:59Z", "compiler", "FIX DRAFTED: apps/compiler/packs/apply-hopacsota-p2-alias-order.ps1 (dry-run 3/3, unstaged). Edits: (1) hopacsota PartEvalSeams add active = not isNull session; (2) no-op side add active = false; (3) bsv :160874 globals = (if PartEvalSeams.active hopacsota then s.globals [the key_body child, ordered] else validation_root_globals root as today). Turnkey. VERIFY (after CCMR land): build -Mode hopacsota, alternating-ab.ps1 parallel w2/w4/w8 5 runs each = 1 hash per variant = w1 = single-flight, + the per-alias type-count check (main).")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p2_60 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P2", 60u32, "2026-10-28T00:02Z", "compiler", "COUNT-CHECK validated on census (w1 vs w8): 60 type-alias decls each; w1 unique=60 (each alias exactly ONCE); multiset w1==w8r1==w8r2 (0 diffs). So the reordering is PURELY count-preserving -- same 60 aliases each once, just reordered. CONFIRMS exactly-once ALREADY holds (no drop/dup); the drafted fix (route to key_body child -> depth-first) only adds ORDER, preserving the count -- raises confidence it is complete. mains prune/merge-dup concern pre-cleared by the data. Count-check logic (extract ^type \\S+ = , compare multiset) ready for the post-land verify.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p2_61 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P2", 61u32, "2026-10-28T00:15Z", "compiler", "COUNT-CHECK TOOL ready: lab/HOPACSOTA-P2/alias-count-check.ps1 (validated GREEN on census w8 vs w1: 60 alias decls each, 0 dups, identical multiset). Extracts ^type <alias> = , compares the multiset; RED on any drop/dup, GREEN on reorder-only. Run after the fix alongside alternating-ab.ps1 (-A parallel.fsx -B single-flight.fsx). Implements mains negative gate check.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p2_62 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P2", 62u32, "2026-10-28T01:45Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-27: P2 fix RE-dry-run on the 5n base (main: box core = 5n 9E1F72AB; source spiral_compiler.fs sha256(16)=9E1F72ABFD095D26, lw 03:49): 3/3 anchors match exactly once (hopacsota active, no-op active, bsv :160874 globals-override) -- NO drift from the composite. Composing P2 over 5n is intended (main). Mirror hopacsota-p2 syncing for the node build; will re-confirm 3/3 on the mirror copy before queueing. The P2 determinism+count gates test the hopacsota parallel core, which 5n's host/cells patches don't touch, so the verify is base-valid.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p2_63 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P2", 63u32, "2026-10-28T01:57Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-27: P2 node verify set up. Mirror hopacsota-p2 synced (box 5n tree, core 9E1F72AB verified). Fix applied ON mirror (3/3) -> core 9FF1B7088BDCAB6F. Isolated build QUEUED: eoie job-1793152685875-hopacsota-p2-build (gate=compile admission-controlled, max 5000MB, SPIRAL_BIN_CACHE_DIR+DET_ROOT isolated, no clobber of node global install). On success -> alternating-ab (w1 vs w8, same hash = fix works) + alias-count-check. Build self-contained (no deps); ab compiles spiral.spi (watch box/node deps skew).")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p2_64 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P2", 64u32, "2026-10-28T03:10Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-27: P2 DLL built on node (AADA8236, core D2BD6F97, 0 errors, isolated cache, no clobber). Measure: spiral.spi (64KB) compiles with the P2 DLL at w1 in 206s, linked deps resolved (no box/node skew), out hash B47B5749. Determinism ab QUEUED: job-1793157082307-hopacsota-p2-ab (variants w1-cw1,w8-cw1,w8-cw8 x3 rounds = 9 compiles, ~45min, admission-controlled, max 6000MB). GREEN = all variants ONE identical .fsx hash (w8 == w1, stable across rounds) + alias-count w8==w1. Reporting on settle.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p2_65 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P2", 65u32, "2026-10-28T03:35Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-27: SMOKE GREEN. ab job-1793157082307 (w1-cw1/w8-cw1/w8-cw8 x3): ALL 9 compiles of spiral.spi = ONE hash B47B5749C215 (w8 parallel == w1, stable across rounds). alias-count w8 vs w1 = 60 each, 0 dup, identical multiset. w8 ran 0.78-0.93x w1 time (parallelism pays). Smoke only, NOT the registered gate. NEXT: SF ref job-1793158626975 (node single-flight output + inputs for mains box cross-check vs installed 5n) -> full registered gate (w1+w2/w4/w8 cw1 x5 + SF-hash comparison + alias-count). hopacsota DLL AADA8236, core D2BD6F97.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p2_66 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P2", 66u32, "2026-10-28T03:51Z", "main", "10-27 23:51 main: Single-flight cross-check GREEN (main, 10-27 23:5x). The box's installed 5n single-flight compiler (core DLL 7A6C1445, tree core 9E1F72AB) compiled apps/spiral/spiral.spi (sha256 DA60B76F99E3039C..., the same input as the node mirror) with --backend Fsharp and SPIRAL_HOPACSOTA_POLICY unset: exit 0, 86 s, output sha256 B47B5749C215A6F8075A802A291C475DDADF4E5A4FC4311238328A6DDEBACC90. That equals the compiler team's node single-flight reference (P2-patched core 986266D8) and the hopacsota smoke hash (w1, w8-cw1, w8-cw8 x3, all B47B5749C215; alias multiset 60 = 60, 0 duplicates). So on this input the P2 fix does not change single-flight output, and parallel hopacsota equals production output. The box deps checkout (cc1b12ac) differs from the node's (d095f13d), but apps/spiral reads lib through packageDir ../../lib, not the fork deps, so the inputs match. Still open: the full registered gate (w2/w4/w8 x5 plus the single-flight comparison plus the alias count), which closes the determinism half of P2. Speed note for P3: w8 took 0.78-0.93x of w1's time, while hopacsota per compile is about 206 s against single-flight's 86 s here. The single-worker base cost is the P3 target, not more workers.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p2_67 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P2", 67u32, "2026-10-28T03:51Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-27: SF cross-check GREEN (main 23:5x). Box INSTALLED 5n single-flight (core 7A6C1445) compiled spiral.spi (sha DA60B76F, matching mine) -> output B47B5749C215...CC90, BYTE-EQUAL to the node SF ref AND the hopacsota smoke hash. Deps: box cc1b12ac vs node d095f13d is immaterial -- apps/spiral reads lib via packageDir ../../lib not the fork deps; the dirty deps files are posts/scripts/.gitignore. So NO single-flight leak + hopacsota w8 parallel == production 5n output on this input. Full registered gate (job-1793159388778, w1+w2/w4/w8 x5) is the last piece.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p2_68 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P2", 68u32, "2026-10-28T04:35Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-28: FULL GATE GREEN (condition 1 MET). job-1793159388778: all 20 hopacsota compiles (w1/w2/w4/w8-cw1 x5 rounds) = ONE hash B47B5749C215 = the single-flight reference. alias-count (w8-cw1 vs w1-cw1) 60 each, 0 dup, identical multiset. Job exited 1 on a COSMETIC verdict-print bug ($allHashes.Count on a scalar), AFTER all data computed -- NOT a gate failure. w8 ran 0.70-0.83x w1 time (parallelism pays). NEXT condition 3: pure-5n build job-1793162155201 (gates core == 7A6C1445BF5AF48C) -> single-flight A/B.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p2_69 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P2", 69u32, "2026-10-28T04:38Z", "main", "10-28 00:38 main: Determinism gate (registered in note 54) MET, verified by main independently on the node (10-28 00:1x). The compiler team's job-1793159388778 (kit2 store) exited 1 on a verdict-line bug: Sort-Object -Unique collapsed to a scalar, so .Count failed. That has been fixed. The data was read directly, not from the summary: 21 outputs in candidates/hopacsota-p2/alternating-ab/20261027-235519 (w1/w2/w4/w8 with cw1, rounds r1..r5) plus C:\\home\\remote\\hopacsota-p2\\sf-ref.fsx (single-flight reference), with 1 distinct sha256, B47B5749C215A6F8075A802A291C475DDADF4E5A4FC4311238328A6DDEBACC90. That also equals the box's installed 5n single-flight output for the same input (n66). Alias declarations, w8-cw1-r1 vs w1-cw1-r1: 60 = 60, 0 duplicates, equal multiset. Bless status: (1) gate MET; (2) the pack applies 3/3 on 5n core 9E1F72AB, composite source 9FF1B7088BDCAB6F, ready; (3) the single-flight A/B on the node is pending, after its pure-5n core-DLL hash gate (must equal the installed 7A6C1445). (4) Install only outside TRAIN2 and outside tibia's 08:30-09:30 block. (5) Hopacsota stays non-default until P6, with P3 speed first. Speed in this gate: w8 took 0.70-0.83x of w1's time.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p2_70 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P2", 70u32, "2026-10-28T04:41Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-28: condition 3 pure-5n gate FAILED. Node pure-5n SF core E322D4090B1AECAC != installed 5n 7A6C1445. ROOT = SDK skew: box has rc.1 (26425.128) + rc.2 (26504.105); build.ps1 picks newest-with-runtime so box 5n built with rc.2, node pure-5n with rc.1 -> different F# SDK -> different DLL bytes. NOT a P2 issue nor CCMR fix gap (CCMR holds within an SDK; cross-machine repro needs SDK parity -> COMPILER-CROSS-MACHINE-REPRO). Per rule -> condition 3 to the BOX post-TRAIN2 (P2-composite SF vs installed 5n, frontier/smoke/examples -Native, 0 diff cells). P2 output correctness already proven (full gate + box cross-check = B47B5749 = 5n output). Conditions 1-2 MET (main verified n1 independently).")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p2_71 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P2", 71u32, "2026-10-28T06:54Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-28: CONDITION 3 GREEN -> ALL 3 P2 BLESS CONDITIONS MET. Box A/B (job-1793164250371): P2-composite SF (core 05E94769) vs installed 5n (baseline core == 7A6C1445 verified) -> 6637=6637 rows, 0 differing cells, 0 behavior, DISAGREE 0 = P2 single-flight-neutral across frontier/smoke/examples (tree left on accepted 5n). Conditions: (1) full gate GREEN (main verified 21 shas), (2) pack 3/3 on 5n -> 9FF1B708, (3) this 0-diff. PACK READY: apply-hopacsota-p2-alias-order.ps1 for main to build+bless+install. P2 VERIFY COMPLETE.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p2_72 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P2", 72u32, "2026-10-28T06:57Z", "main", "10-28 02:57 main: BLESSED (main, 10-28 01:5x): all three conditions set in advance are met. (1) Full determinism gate: 21 outputs, 1 sha256 B47B5749C215..., aliases 60 = 60, 0 duplicates (n69, verified by main on the node). (2) The pack (apps/compiler/packs/apply-hopacsota-p2-alias-order.ps1) applies 3/3 on 5n core 9E1F72AB; composite source 9FF1B7088BDCAB6F. (3) Box single-flight A/B (job-1793164250371): P2 composite single-flight (core DLL 05E94769) vs installed 5n (7A6C1445), frontier,smoke,examples -Native, 6,637 = 6,637 rows, 0 differing, DISAGREE 0. Install: TEMP pack-p2/install-p2.ps1 (dry run clean at 01:5x). It checks the base core, backs it up, applies the pack to a copy and checks the composite hash, then takes a compiler-install window, writes the core and builds single-flight and hopacsota. Its proof is output, not DLL bytes: the installed single-flight must compile apps/spiral/spiral.spi to B47B5749C215, otherwise it rolls back to the 5n backup and rebuilds. The output check replaces a DLL-hash check because the core DLL is not byte-reproducible across build contexts (COMPILER-CROSS-MACHINE-REPRO n28). Slot: right at kit's 'TRAIN2 window closed', before kit's lib window, so the lib gates run on the final compiler. Hopacsota stays non-default; P3 speed is next, then P6 promotion.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p2_73 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P2", 73u32, "2026-10-28T09:36Z", "main", "10-28 05:36 main: INSTALLED 10-28 05:35 by main (job-1793179635630, install-p2.ps1, VERDICT GREEN), at kit's 'TRAIN2 window closed' (TRAIN2c published 05:25). The tree core went 9E1F72ABFD095D26 (5n) -> 9FF1B7088BDCAB6F (the blessed composite). single-flight built in 213 s and hopacsota in 172 s. Proof by output: installed single-flight compiles apps/spiral/spiral.spi to B47B5749C215A6F8075A802A291C475DDADF4E5A4FC4311238328A6DDEBACC90, the same as 5n. Installed single-flight core DLL 05E94769A86A9B9C, byte-equal to the compiler team's condition-3 box build. That's a data point for COMPILER-CROSS-MACHINE-REPRO, where the det-root theory has to explain it. 5n backup at TEMP pack-p2/core-5n-before-p2.fs (9E1F72AB) for a rollback. Hopacsota stays non-default. Next on the track: P3 speed (single-worker base cost, about 206 s vs single-flight's 86 s on spiral.spi), then P6 promotion.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p2_74 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P2", 74u32, "2026-10-28T09:40Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-28: HOPACSOTA-P2 DONE. Determinism fix INSTALLED (main 05:35): tree core 9FF1B708, both modes built; installed single-flight OUTPUT = B47B5749 (output proof PASSED). The installs rollback path was NOT exercised (it runs only on an output mismatch/build failure, which did not occur) -> rollback is UNPROVEN, not passed; a dry rollback is cheap but not needed for P2. All 3 bless conditions met (full gate 21 identical shas; pack 3/3 on 5n; single-flight A/B 0 differing cells). Pack apply-hopacsota-p2-alias-order.ps1 shipped. hopacsota stays non-default (promotion = P6). CLOSED.")
inl hopacsota_p3 () = Task ("HOPACSOTA-P3", "hopacsota Phase 3: measured granularity cutoff + D16 span shortening (fork typed bodies, speculation with nacks, checked return-type hints, parsing/typecheck/codegen in parallel beside peval); gate: published 8-worker table for every mega root + apps/spiral vs the R3 simulation, half the simulated speedup on 4 of 5 roots and speedup growing with N on the scaling corpus; stays open until met (spiral/apps/compiler/tmp/lanes/hopacsota/PLAN.md)", Story, P0, D6, 40u32, Planned, "HOPACSOTA-P2,HOPACSOTA-D4-RESTART", "team:compiler,hopacsota,main,wipe-redo", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_hopacsota_p3 () = Planning ("HOPACSOTA-P3", Minutes 420u32, Load Heavy, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_hopacsota_p3_1 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P3", 1u32, "2026-10-14T11:38Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-14 07:38 touch: the D16 technique per long chain comes from r3_simulate.py's top-10 chain table; P3 starts from today's R3 numbers")
