inl note_of_hopacsota_p3_4 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P3", 4u32, "2026-10-19T12:17Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 08:17 inherited from HOPACSOTA-RESEARCH: the R3 instrumentation (lab/HOPACSOTA-RESEARCH/r3_instrument.ps1 + r3_simulate.py) no longer applies since pack-1v moved its PartEval anchor behind PartEvalSeams; re-home it into PartEvalSeamsHopacsota (it sees every deferred body) before measuring the cutoff.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p3_5 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P3", 5u32, "2026-10-19T14:22Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 10:22 lap 2 grain: blocked on P2's engine (no worklist trace hook yet); inputs ready: lab/HOPACSOTA-RESEARCH/r3_instrument.ps1 + r3_simulate.py and PLAN.md's LF result (longest remaining chain first within 4 percent) Next: -")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p3_6 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P3", 6u32, "2026-10-19T18:39Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 14:39 lap 4 grain: still blocked on the P2 engine slice; new tool for when it lands: packs can now be gated with pair-drift -Tracked on a candidate copy and reviewed on a private Debug build, so P2/P3 code is measured without touching the shared DLL Next: wait for P2")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p3_7 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P3", 7u32, "2026-10-23T14:16Z", "main", "2026-10-23 10:16 main: the R3 instrumentation is re-homed, since the old r3_instrument.ps1 and r3_simulate.py are gone with the drained lab. %TEMP%\\hopacsota-census\\census.ps1 patches PartEvalSeamsHopacsota's Session; it's inert unless SPIRAL_HOPACSOTA_CENSUS_OUT is set, and works under the queue policies (dfs, bfs, random), where a deferred body runs only after its parent returns, so its measured run time is its self time. Each body gets an id, its parent (the body running when Defer was called; 0 is the root), its spawn offset (the time since the parent's start) and its self time. The root is a row too, and the file is appended when the session ends. Timing uses System.TimeProvider.System.GetTimestamp (high resolution; lint G2 bans only the Stopwatch and DateTime tokens; the identifiers avoid G2's record/replay words). Lint: G1 619 lines, G2 0 hits, parse clean. simulate.py does list scheduling over the spawn tree (a child is ready at its parent's start plus its offset) and gives the critical path, the total/critical bound and simulated speedups at 2, 4 and 8 workers; checked on a hand-computed tree (4.3 ms path, 3.49x bound, reached at 4 workers). Limits of v1: only spawn edges, so claim waits are not edges yet, which makes it an upper bound. Rides pack-next8 (core BF7EE0AD76C18036). Measure after it builds: SPIRAL_HOPACSOTA_POLICY=bfs plus the census on the 5 mega roots and apps/spiral, which gives P3's starting table.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p3_8 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P3", 8u32, "2026-10-24T11:49Z", "main", "2026-10-19 laps folded from the deleted history ledger (docs/agents/history/2026-10-19-backlog-laps, HISTORY-BACKLOG-LAPS-DRIFT): lap 1 08:26 (main): inherited the R3 instrumentation re-home (lab/HOPACSOTA-RESEARCH scripts, anchor moved behind PartEvalSeams) from the closed research item -> next: re-home r3_instrument.ps1 into PartEvalSeamsHopacsota once P2's engine exists")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p3_9 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P3", 9u32, "2026-10-28T07:10Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-28: P3 build-free start (P2 done, unblocks P3). Read PartEvalSeams.Session.Defer (:54036): each deferred body allocates a child ResizeArray + a \"globals\" marker + Children registration + a globals append + with_globals env + a body closure -- a FIXED per-deferral overhead paid for EVERY type-known body under a deferring policy. At w1 (my measure 206s vs single-flight 86s) no parallelism amortizes it -> the ~2.4x base cost IS this overhead. So the granularity cutoff (P3 core) = defer a body only if its census self-time > this per-deferral cost; inline cheaper ones. The census hook is already built into the Session (:54024 census_out, SPIRAL_HOPACSOTA_CENSUS_OUT), so no patch -- the existing P2-composite hopacsota DLL (AADA8236) works. NEXT (build-bound run): SPIRAL_HOPACSOTA_CENSUS_OUT + bfs on the 5 mega roots + apps/spiral -> self-time table -> set the cutoff + the D16 span-shortening fork points.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p3_10 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P3", 10u32, "2026-10-28T09:46Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-28 P3 attribution RULE (pre-measure, main steer): target = the w1 SERIAL gap (206s vs 86s = 2.4x), NOT granularity (w8 only 0.70-0.83x w1, so parallelism isnt the gap). Profile w1 hopacsota vs single-flight on spiral.spi; attribute the ~120s delta across (A) per-body deferral alloc (Job/Ch/Session; engine bodies/spawned counts), (B) D4 wait-for (engine bridges/waits), (C) per-key globals merge (Session.Finish), (D) GC/alloc + MVar contention (sampled profile). RULE: lever = the largest single contributor >=40% of the delta; top two each 20-40% -> take both; ignore <15%. Engine stats first; add a dotnet-trace sampled profile if counts dont localize the TIME. Record attribution BEFORE picking the lever.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p3_11 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P3", 11u32, "2026-10-28T09:54Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-28 P3 w1-profile (node job-1793180917498): SF 120.2s, w1 hopacsota 180.2s, delta 60s (node under load; box was 206/86). Engine stats (LOAD-INDEPENDENT): bodies=1515, claims=372147, ready_hits=325376, bridges=80862, waits=0, conflicts=0, cycles=0, abandoned=0. RULED OUT at w1: bucket B (D4 wait-for)=0 (waits=0); MVar contention=0 (1 worker, conflicts=0). Overhead = the PER-OPERATION machinery: ~372k family/memo claims + ~325k ready-hits + ~80k Hopac Ch bridges, vs only 1515 bodies (bucket A per-body modest). At w1 every deferred eval still crosses the Ch worklist (pure overhead, no parallelism). PRELIM lever: a single-worker fast-path that skips the Ch bridge + direct-calls the claim path. NEXT: dotnet-trace sampled profile for the TIME split (Ch.give/take vs memo/family claim vs globals merge) to apply the >=40% rule.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p3_12 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P3", 12u32, "2026-10-28T09:58Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-28 P3 lever rule REFINED (main): DONE RULE = w8 vs single-flight (NOT w1) -- a w1-only bridge-skip cant earn P6 promotion (needs w4/w8 to beat SF, which still pay every claim+bridge). Lever must cut per-op cost at EVERY worker count. Candidates: (1) READY-HIT INLINE -- resolve a claim inline when immediately ready, no Ch round-trip (325k of 372k claims are ready-hits = 87%); (2) batch claims per body; (3) memoize family lookup per key. The dotnet-trace TIME split picks which per-op cost is biggest -> the lever. Measure EACH candidate at BOTH w1 AND w8; drop any that helps w1 but not w8.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p3_13 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P3", 13u32, "2026-10-28T10:00Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-28 P3 engine-code CONFIRMS mains arithmetic (Claim :53878-53906): a ready-hit resolves INLINE (ctx.Ready/published TryGetValue -> ValueSome, NO bridge); only the slow settle path (:53896) bridges. So 325k ready-hits DONT bridge; 80k bridges = ~47k non-ready claims + Family/Fill/Announce. ready-hit-inline lever MOOT. Real per-op overhead: EVERY claim (372k) does let id = box (table, key) (object ALLOCATION) + 1-2 dict lookups (Ready, published) -> 372k boxings+lookups at ALL N. Refined levers: (A) kill the per-claim box alloc (372k GC pressure, all N) via a struct/cached key; (B) settle/Ch bridge (80k). dotnet-trace TIME split picks A vs B (not installed on node; install or probe.ps1 -Profile + profile-thread.py, both present).")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p3_14 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P3", 14u32, "2026-10-28T10:04Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-28 P3 profiling setup: probe.ps1 -Profile is SAMPLES-only + shells to dotnet-trace via Get-DiagTool (:56, not installed -> auto-installs). BUT profile spiral.spi (machinery-exerciser: 1515 bodies, 372k claims), NOT a mega root -- mega roots are ~0-join-point (MEGA-PARALLEL n1), barely defer, wouldnt exercise Claim/box/Ch. So: install dotnet-trace + profile the spiral.spi w1 compile DIRECTLY (dotnet-trace collect -p <pid> --profile dotnet-sampled-thread-time --format Speedscope) -> profile-summary.py -> frames -> split box(table,key)/GC (lever A) vs Ch give/take (lever B). Gated on a quieter node window (clean frames; node ~99% CPU). Then prototype the winner, measure w1+w8 vs the w8-vs-SF done rule.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p3_15 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P3", 15u32, "2026-10-28T10:20Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-28 P3 bridge machinery (Engine :53786-53827): bridge = ctx.Bridges++ then Scheduler.run control op -- a SYNCHRONOUS hop onto the CONTROL scheduler (NumWorkers=1 :53775). ask = bridge + MVar.modifyFun on the Table guard. settle (non-ready claim path :53822) = ask ClaimOp. So the 80k bridges each = Scheduler.run through ONE control worker + an MVar modify -> the 1-worker control scheduler SERIALIZES all table ops. At w8, 8 body threads funnel table ops through 1 control worker = an Amdahl serial fraction = the likely reason hopacsota cant beat SF at w8 (invisible at w1). Lever B (batch/reduce bridges, or widen control) raises the w8 ceiling AND cuts per-op at all N. Lever A (372k box Tuple allocs) is per-op alloc only. Profile frames: Tuple..ctor/GC=>A; Scheduler.run/Hopac/MVar=>B.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p3_16 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P3", 16u32, "2026-10-28T10:24Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-28 P3 DONE RULE for any control-path lever (main): re-run P2s FULL determinism gate (w1/w2/w4/w8 x5 = the single-flight hash), NOT just the w8 speed check -- the 1-worker control scheduler (:53775) + single Table guard MVar (:53780, all ask/settle do MVar.modifyFun guard) may be WHAT makes per-key merges + claim decisions linearizable (deterministic); naively widening control could reopen the nondeterminism P2 closed. PREFER levers that do LESS control work while KEEPING it serial: (a) resolve more claims without settle (fewer bridges), (b) batch several ops per hop, (c) SHARD the Table/guard so independent keys dont share one guard (per-key serial -> still linearizable, cross-key parallel). TWO serial points: the 1-worker control SCHEDULER and the single guard MVar; the 2-point measurement (bridges/sec + control busy% at w1 vs w8, alternated; ~100% busy at w8 = the ceiling) says which saturates first.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p3_17 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P3", 17u32, "2026-10-28T10:36Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-28 P3 STATE (groom): Active, measurement stage (was mis-marked 0 Planned). Serial gap attributed (per-op claim/bridge machinery; wait-for=0). w1 profile: machinery NOT CPU-hot (compiler frames dominate) -> box-alloc lever A ruled OUT as CPU-dominant. bridge = Scheduler.run through a 1-worker control scheduler + MVar (lever B) is the likely cost AND the w8 Amdahl cap. Determinism gate: any control-path lever re-runs P2s full gate (w1/w2/w4/w8 x5 = SF hash). NEXT: contention job (SF/w1/w8 wall+CPU+bridges) decides bridge-latency vs redundant-work + the ceiling; then pick + prototype a determinism-safe lever (fewer bridges / batch per hop / shard the table guard). See notes 10-16.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p3_18 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P3", 18u32, "2026-10-28T10:53Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-28 P3 CONTENTION RESULT (clean, job-1793184486226): SF wall 26.5s cpu 42s; w1 hopacsota wall 26.4s cpu 43.6s (== SF! the earlier 120/180 +60s was NODE LOAD, not real -- w1 has NO overhead); w8 wall 30.8s cpu 76s (SLOWER than SF + ~1.8x CPU), bridges 103k vs w1 80k. bridges/sec w8/w1 = 1.1 (control worker SATURATED: 8 workers dont raise throughput); wall speedup w1->w8 = 0.86x (w8 slower than w1). DIAGNOSIS: the 1-worker control scheduler + single guard MVar cap throughput (Amdahl) AND w8 adds contention (more bridges, ~1.8x CPU, no speedup). LEVER = SHARD the Table guard (determinism-safe): per-shard serial -> still linearizable, independent keys parallel -> raises bridges/sec + cuts contention bridges. Done rule: w8 beats SF AND P2 full det gate (w1/w2/w4/w8 x5 = SF hash). Prototype next.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p3_19 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P3", 19u32, "2026-10-28T11:02Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-28 P3 w8 STATS (job-...486226) confirm SHARD. w1: ready_hits=325376 bridges=80862 waits=0 conflicts=0. w8: ready_hits=306234 (-19k) bridges=103002 (+22k) waits=903 conflicts=0. w8 slowdown is NOT key contention (waits=903 = 0.24% of claims, conflicts 0, DAG highly parallel) -- it is LOST READY-HITS: parallelism claims ~19k keys before their value is computed -> miss cache -> settle -> +22k bridges that serialize on the 1-worker control (bridges/sec capped ~3300). So the control IS the bottleneck + the DAG has the parallelism to beat SF. SHARD design (vs real Table :53637): shard entries by stableHash(id)%N (pure fn of id); per-shard named/published/conflicts/abandoned; widen control so per-shard Decides run parallel; per-shard markers re-ordered depth-first by Session.Finish (tags dont leak to output); graph (cross-key waits) stays global 1-worker (rare). Done rule: w8 beats SF + P2 full det gate. Prototype at the 11:30 box slot.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p3_20 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P3", 20u32, "2026-10-28T14:30Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-28 estimate 420m FROM MILESTONES (gate = lanes/hopacsota/PLAN.md P3): rebuild shard env + apply lever + build hopacsota 90m; determinism gate (w1/w2/w4/w8 x5 = single-flight hash) 60m; publish 8-worker speed table for 5 mega roots + apps/spiral via bisect.ps1 -GcServer 0 90m; reach half the R3-simulated speedup on 4/5 roots incl D16 span-shortening tuning 120m; speedup growing with N on the scaling corpus 60m. Sum = 420m.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p3_21 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P3", 21u32, "2026-10-28T14:41Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-28 D16.3 CHECKED RETURN-TYPE HINTS -- design (build-free; main 4-part spec). Unblocks this item + MEGA-PARALLEL lever B. The seam: a JP method defers to the worklist (PartEvalSeams.defer, 160498) ONLY when its return type is known UP FRONT (deferred=`annot`, 160495). D16.3 supplies that type as a HINT when there is no annotation, so type-known recursive bodies can defer too.\n\n(1) WHERE a return type is knowable before evaluating the body:\n  a. Annotated JPs -- annot already gives it (160495); they defer today, no hint needed.\n  b. Type-known recursive inl/methods with NO annot (the mega-sample case): HINT from (i) a prior build's recorded type for the same specialization key (QUERY-CACHE-COMPILER persists per-key results = the hint source), or (ii) a cheap static read of the body's NON-recursive (base-case) return arm, which for a well-founded recursion is the result type, or (iii) a fixpoint seed refined on disagreement.\n  c. Bodies whose result is a constructor application of a known union/nominal (type evident without evaluating the payload).\n\n(2) HOW the hint is CHECKED (body disagrees): the defer hands back the HINTED type; dependents proceed on it; the body evaluates in parallel; at the existing check (160501, today `if annot <> ty then raise_type_error`) compare the ACTUAL type to the hint. MATCH -> commit (dependents valid). DISAGREE -> the dependents computed on a wrong type, so their claims must be NACKED and RE-EVALUATED with the correct type (speculation-with-nacks). Unlike an annot mismatch (a user error -> raise), a hint mismatch is EXPECTED/recoverable -> restart, not error. DETERMINISM: the hint affects only timing/speculation, never the final value; a full discard+restart reproduces single-flight's result, so Gate G2 determinism holds IFF the nack discards ALL speculative work (no partial-state leak; Scheduler.create/kill per build closes the fix-57 leak class). \n\n(3) ANCHORED SITES (live core):\n  - 160495-160498 method-JP defer: when annot=None, look up/compute the hint and pass it as the deferred type; new hint-store lookup here.\n  - 160500-160501 fill+check: split the check -- annot-mismatch stays raise_type_error; HINT-mismatch triggers nack+restart.\n  - 53878 Claim / 53972 abandon_chain / 53976-53988 Check(): a wrong hint makes a consumer claim a key on a wrong type = exactly the HopacsotaConflict case, which TODAY RAISES (\"the canonical-order restart (D4) is not built yet\"). => D16.3 DEPENDS ON D4 (canonical-order restart): D4 must turn a conflict into a deterministic restart before D16.3 hints are safe. Hard dependency, call it out.\n  - 54036 Session.Defer / 54004 Policy: extend to carry (hint, check-fn) alongside the deferred option.\n\n(4) PRE-REGISTERED MEASURE (build-bound, after TRAIN3, lab/bisect.ps1 -GcServer 0): on runtime_native + the 5 mega roots at w8, report bridges(before/after), waits(before/after) -- expect bridges UP (more deferral) and waits DOWN (dependents proceed on the hint instead of blocking) -- plus the nack/restart count (wrong-hint overhead) and paired wall (single-flight vs w8, in-round A/A, per-round ratios). SUCCESS = w8 wall beats single-flight to the P3 gate (half the R3 speedup on 4/5 roots) AND determinism w1/w2/w4/w8 x5 = single-flight hash (DISAGREE 0) AND nack rate low enough not to eat the speedup. SEQUENCING: build D4 (canonical-order restart) first, then D16.3 hints, then MEGA-PARALLEL lever B rides it.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p3_22 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P3", 22u32, "2026-10-29T11:46Z", "compiler", "10-29 07:46 compiler: SHARD LEVER DRAFTED after the wipe (n19's prototype was a TEMP draft, never in the core). Design correction to n19: sharding by stableHash(id) is UNSAFE. Decide runs the caller's lookup/mark/fill_write closures, which do TryGetValue and index-set on the evaluator's shared Dictionary (the per-body inner dicts from family join_point_method/join_point_closure, plus backend_switch_validated). Two shards holding keys of one Dictionary would write it concurrently. publish (Volatile.Write of published.SetItem, a read-modify-write) and the named counter would race too. A lock is out (G2 bans lock/Monitor/Interlocked/ConcurrentDictionary in hopacsota code; PLAN D3 = sharded server-owned table). DRAFT apps/compiler/packs/apply-hopacsota-p3-shard.ps1, 8 anchors, each matching exactly once: N Tables, each behind its own MVar guard. The shard = PhysicalHash of the id's FIRST tuple item (the backing Dictionary; every id is box(store, key) via ITuple), so all writes to one Dictionary stay behind one guard while ~1.5k per-body dicts spread over the shards. Per-shard published and per-shard marker numbering (hopacsota-key-<shard>-<n>; markers are opaque and P2 already proved arrival order irrelevant to output). counts() sums shards via Job.seqCollect, and the stats line prints shards=. SPIRAL_HOPACSOTA_SHARDS (default 16; 1 = today's single-guard engine, the A/A control). Why it should help without widening control: Scheduler.run runs a non-blocking job on the calling thread (PLAN 888), so an uncontended MVar take completes on the body thread and only a contended one parks onto the 1-worker control. Less contention means fewer serialized resumes. On a scratch copy: hopacsota-lint G1 646 lines, G2 0 hits; pair-drift 0 touched. NEXT (build-bound, after pack 6's review frees the slot): build -Mode hopacsota from the scratch core; determinism gate w1/w2/w4/w8 x5 = single-flight hash at SHARDS=16; stats + paired wall SHARDS=1 vs 16 at w8 on the n18 corpus (in-job A/A, gcServer=0). Expect w8 bridges~equal and wall < SF. If contention moves to the control worker, widen control next (n19's second half).")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p3_23 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P3", 23u32, "2026-10-29T14:40Z", "compiler", "10-29 10:40 compiler: shard (n22) + IVar (recovered, WIPE-REDO-LEDGER n5) COMPOSE and keep determinism: node job-1793282628758 round 1, w8 shards16 and w8 shards1 (= IVar alone) both output B47B5749C215A6F8 = sf. Engine stats at w8: waits 166 (shards16) / 157 (shards1) vs 903 before IVar (n19), so IVar removes about 83% of the waits; bridges 100,916 / 100,561 vs 103,002, so shards neither add nor remove bridges (expected: a bridge is one ask; sharding cuts guard contention, not asks). Walls are NOT evidence (node at 100% CPU, sf 155 s vs 26.5 s quiet). The speed table and the done rule (w8 beats sf; half the R3 speedup on 4/5 roots) need a quiet slot, measured with packs/measure-arms.ps1 (rotated rounds, per-round ratios).")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p3_24 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P3", 24u32, "2026-10-29T15:01Z", "compiler", "10-29 11:01 compiler: proof-2 summary (packs/measure-arms.ps1, 2 rotated rounds, apps/spiral/spiral.spi, gcServer=0, node at 97-100% CPU): sf 154.9 s; w1 141.3 s (ratio 0.91); w8 shards16 219.3 s (1.41, per-round 1.41-1.75); w8 shards1 = IVar alone 230.2 s (1.48, 1.48-1.81); all outputs = sf. Reading: the direction matches n18 (w8 slower than sf with 1 control worker), and shards16 vs shards1 is a 5% wall gap inside the per-round spread, so not evidence. The determinism half of the done rule holds for shard + IVar + D4; the speed half needs a quiet slot (and probably n19's second half: widen the control scheduler, which my shard applier deliberately leaves at 1 worker).")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p3_25 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P3", 25u32, "2026-10-29T15:20Z", "compiler", "10-29 11:20 compiler: n19's second half DRAFTED: apps/compiler/packs/apply-hopacsota-p3-control-workers.ps1 (1 anchor). The control scheduler width comes from SPIRAL_HOPACSOTA_CONTROL_WORKERS (default 1 = today's behaviour). Determinism argument: Graph and Worklist stay single Job.foreverServer jobs (one message at a time wherever they run) and each table shard keeps its MVar, so the protocol order is unchanged; only parked bridge resumptions can run in parallel. Composes with shard + IVar + D4 (lint G1/G2 clean). SPEED RUN LAUNCHED anyway under node load (no-quiet-machine-wait): node job-1793287335290-compiler-p3-speed builds single-flight + hopacsota from one source (shard + IVar + D4 + control), then measure-arms 5 rotated rounds on apps/spiral/spiral.spi: sf, sfAA (in-job A/A = the noise floor), and w8 shards16 with control 1/4/8. Reading rule, fixed before the numbers: an arm counts as faster only if its median per-round ratio is below 1 by more than sfAA's spread, and determinism needs all outputs SAME as sf.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p3_26 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P3", 26u32, "2026-10-29T17:11Z", "compiler", "P3 speed run on the node (job-1793287335290, 5 rotated rounds, 6b core vs P3 shard+IVar candidate DC6A5710, entry apps/spiral/spiral.spi Fsharp, gcServer=0). Medians: sf 226.8 s wall / 125.0 s CPU; A/A sfAA ratio 0.91 (min 0.73, max 0.96) = wall noise floor ~25% under node load; w8 c1 ratio 1.20 cpu 182.8; w8 c4 1.23 cpu 179.6; w8 c8 1.23 cpu 179.7. Every round of every arm produced B47B5749C215A6F8 (determinism holds). waits 202-328 of 372312 claims (<0.1%), conflicts 0: contention is solved, and control workers 1/4/8 change nothing. Verdict: no wall win at w8; +45% CPU. Cost sits in per-claim bookkeeping (306k ready_hits, ~101k bridges over 1515 bodies), not in waiting. Next lever: cut bridge/claim cost (ready-hit fast path without a claim record; bridge only when the callee is unresolved), then re-measure w1/w4/w8 against an A/A row. Rows: node C:\\home\\remote\\p3speed-arms\\rows.tsv")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p3_27 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P3", 27u32, "2026-10-29T17:29Z", "compiler", "Where the +45% CPU at w8 comes from (re-read of the earlier p3d4 node run, 2 rounds): sf 122-124 s CPU; w1 102 s CPU with 80.9k bridges and 325.5k ready_hits, i.e. the engine at ONE worker is 17% CHEAPER than single-flight, so per-bridge/claim mechanics are NOT the cost (refutes n26's 'cut bridge cost' lever). w8 s1 and s16 both 180-184 s CPU with only ~+20k bridges (per-body Ready caches hit less: 307k vs 325k): the extra ~80 s comes from running 8 bodies at once, independent of sharding. Also ruled out by reading the code: deep structural hashing in box(table,key) (the hot tables join_point_closure/method key on hash-consed ConsedNode). Side finding: backend_switch_validated keys string * RData [] under HashIdentity.Structural, but ctx.Ready (default .NET equality, arrays by reference) can miss structurally equal keys and fall to settle; published/entries use HashIdentity.Structural, so correctness holds (all outputs B47B5749), only extra bridges. Candidates for the parallel cost: spinning while 8 bodies queue on the single control scheduler / the guard MVar, or GC suspensions across 8 allocating threads. Discriminating run queued on the node after the D4 matrix: job-1793295047297 (sf, w1, w2, w4, w8, w8+GCgen0size=256MB, w8+gcConcurrent=0; 3 rounds).")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p3_28 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P3", 28u32, "2026-10-29T17:30Z", "compiler", "RETRACTION of n27's side finding: BodyContext.Ready is Dictionary<obj, obj>(HashIdentity.Structural) (spiral_compiler.fs:53602, same as Table.entries and published), and F# structural equality compares arrays by content, so backend_switch_validated keys (string * RData []) do NOT miss in Ready. No extra bridges from that path; nothing to fix. n27's main finding stands (w1 CPU 102 s < sf 123 s; the w8 cost comes from parallel execution; discriminating run job-1793295047297 queued).")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p3_29 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P3", 29u32, "2026-10-29T18:58Z", "compiler", "10-29 15:0x compiler: two more candidates for the w8 +80 s CPU REFUTED by direct measurement (box, Hopac 0.5.1 netstandard2.0, the engine's shape: a 1-worker control Scheduler, callers on dedicated threads, Scheduler.run; scratchpad hopac-park/*.fsx). (1) Idle body threads spinning in bridge(Take) while waiting for work: N threads blocked in Scheduler.run on an unfilled IVar for 5 s cost about 0.2 s CPU each over a 1.1 s fsi baseline (8 threads: 2.83 s total), i.e. they PARK; spinning would be ~40 s. (2) Per-bridge cost under contention: N threads x 20k Scheduler.run(MVar.modifyFun) on one control worker cost 0.8-3.1 us CPU per bridge at 1/2/4/8 threads (two rounds), so ~100k bridges is ~0.3 s, over 250x too small. So neither waiting nor bridge traffic explains w8's cost (w1 is 17% under sf). Left: the evaluation work itself costing more when 8 bodies run at once, i.e. GC suspensions across allocating threads (tested by job-1793295047297's w8+GCgen0size=256MB and w8+gcConcurrent=0 arms) or cache/memory-bandwidth inflation (if GC is cleared, next is per-thread CPU sampling; dotnet-counters gc-heap and cpu would split it).")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p3_30 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P3", 30u32, "2026-10-29T20:09Z", "compiler", "10-29 16:1x compiler: THE w8 COST IS GEN0 GC (interim, 2 of 5 rounds agree; job-1793295047297, node, p3d4 build, apps/spiral to F#, gcServer=0). CPU s: round 1 sf 127 | w1 102, w2 119, w4 140, w8 171 | w8+DOTNET_GCgen0size=0x10000000 85 | w8+gcConcurrent=0 184; round 2 w1 99, w2 111, w4 143, w8 185 | w8gen0 90. Wall: w8 431/366 s vs w8gen0 141/146 s. CPU grows linearly with workers, a 256 MB gen0 budget HALVES w8's CPU and cuts wall ~3x, and disabling background GC changes nothing. Reading: every gen0 collection suspends all threads and scans all stacks, and each body runs on a dedicated thread with a deep evaluation stack, so collections get costlier as bodies run in parallel. This is the effect test.ps1 already guards with DOTNET_GCgen0size=0x10000000 for suite runs ('deep evaluation stacks make every gen0 collection costly', AGENTS.md); measure-arms runs, Cells, kit and direct CLI calls do not set it. n29's refutations (parked waits, bridge cost) are consistent with this. All outputs B47B5749C215A6F8 = sf. Queued for the fair comparison (sf WITH gen0 too, plus an A/A): job-1793303418017 (sfgen0, sfgen0aa, w1/w2/w4/w8 with gen0, 5 rounds). If the engine still wins at w8 there, the lever is to ship the gen0 budget with the compiler itself (runtimeconfig GC setting, if one exists) rather than relying on the caller's env.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p3_31 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P3", 31u32, "2026-10-29T21:04Z", "compiler", "10-29 17:0x compiler: CAN THE COMPILER CARRY THE GEN0 BUDGET ITSELF? NO, not via runtimeconfig. Empirical probe (box, bundled dotnet 11.0.0-rc.1; a console app making 20M escaping 64-byte allocations into a ring buffer, printing GC.CollectionCount(0)): default 561 gen0 GCs; env DOTNET_GCgen0size=0x10000000 13; runtimeconfig configProperties 'System.GC.Gen0Size' = 268435456: 561 (ignored); 'GCgen0size': 561 (ignored). (A first version without escaping allocations showed 0 GCs in every arm, because the JIT stack-allocates non-escaping objects; the control caught it.) The official docs page was not found by search, so this measurement is the evidence for rc.1. Levers that remain: (a) launchers set DOTNET_GCgen0size (Cells' compile path, kit's regen, the spiral CLI, test.ps1 already does); (b) the host re-execs itself once with the variable set when it is absent (one extra process start, against ~2x CPU at w8 and a large gen0 reduction for single-flight too). Decide after job-1793303418017 (sf vs engine, both with gen0), which also shows how much single-flight itself gains.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p3_32 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P3", 32u32, "2026-10-29T22:05Z", "compiler", "10-29 18:0x compiler: CURVE FINAL (job-1793295047297, 5 rotated rounds, node under other lanes' load; p3d4 build; apps/spiral to F#; wall ratio = arm / sf in the same round, median (min-max)): w1 1.13 (0.54-1.36) cpu 102 s; w2 1.07 (0.75-1.14) cpu 117; w4 1.39 (0.87-1.85) cpu 141; w8 1.83 (1.58-2.86) cpu 177; w8 + DOTNET_GCgen0size=0x10000000: 0.68 (0.63-0.82) cpu 86; w8 + gcConcurrent=0: 1.63 cpu 181; sf cpu 124. So with a 256 MB gen0 budget, w8 parallel evaluation beats single-flight by 32% wall and 30% CPU, in all 5 rounds; without it, w8 loses 1.8x. CAVEAT: sf here ran WITHOUT the larger gen0; the fair comparison (sf and w1/w2/w4/w8 all with gen0, plus an sf A/A) is job-1793303418017, running now. Levers if it holds: launchers set DOTNET_GCgen0size (runtimeconfig keys are ignored, n31), or the host re-execs itself with it.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p3_33 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P3", 33u32, "2026-10-29T22:06Z", "compiler", "10-29 18:1x compiler: SHIP-READINESS of the P3 engine appliers on the current pack stack (scratch: pack 7 core + pack 8's ureq move-only + rc_str opt-in): apply-hopacsota-p3-shard.ps1 (8 edits), apply-hopacsota-p3-ivar.ps1 (9 anchors), apply-hopacsota-p3-control-workers.ps1 (1 edit) all apply cleanly in that order; pair-drift 8 pairs, 0 one-sided; hopacsota-lint G1 660 lines (budget 5,934, largest section 429 of 1,000), G2 0 banned constructs outside the allowlist. Proposed as the pack after PACK-8, together with the gen0 lever, once the fair gen0 run (job-1793303418017) confirms engine vs sf with gen0 on both sides. The D4 restart is already in PACK-7.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p3_34 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P3", 34u32, "2026-10-29T22:07Z", "compiler", "10-29 18:1x compiler: TRADE-OFF to measure BEFORE shipping the gen0 lever: a 0x10000000 gen0 budget lets each compiler process allocate up to ~256 MB more before a collection (workstation GC), so peak private memory rises per process. The box often has only 3-5 GB free and runs several compiler processes at once (Cells cells, kit regen, test.ps1 lanes at -Parallel N), and memory caps already reap jobs (retry-after-memory-kills). measure-arms records wall and CPU, not memory, so the curve did not see this. Next measurement: peak private bytes per compile (kit job's peak_private_mb, or Measure-TreeCpu extended) for sf and w8, with and without gen0, on apps/spiral and on a small Cells-sized program; then choose the budget (e.g. 64 MB may keep most of the win). test.ps1 already uses 256 MB for suite runs, so suites carry that cost today without trouble.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p3_35 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P3", 35u32, "2026-10-29T22:19Z", "compiler", "10-29 18:3x compiler: THE RE-EXEC LEVER, MEASURED (main asked for its cost). Drafted apps/compiler/packs/apply-host-gen0-reexec.ps1 (host: a process started without DOTNET_GCgen0size re-execs itself once with 0x10000000; opt-out SPIRAL_GEN0_REEXEC=0; inert under test.ps1, which sets the variable). Candidate gen0h (pack 7 + it): host F02AA06C9FFD1052, core 9C899B30495F7DC5. Box, BelowNormal, under TRAIN4e load, 7 rotated rounds, medians: --check on a parse error: off 1,825 ms, off A/A 1,882, re-exec 1,783, env preset 1,601. Small compile (--backend C): off 1,912, off A/A 1,785, RE-EXEC 2,915 (+~1 s, a second runtime start), env preset 1,535. Passthrough is exact: exits 5/0 and all stdout/stderr text identical across every arm. VERDICT: unconditional re-exec costs ~1 s per one-shot compile, about +55% on small programs, so it eats the win where Cells and kit compile most often. PROPOSAL (hybrid): re-exec only in long-lived modes (--server, --server-tcp, --batch, build-file), where one extra start is amortized; Cells' compiler daemon runs --server, so it gets the budget. One-shot --backend/--check stay single-process; launchers that run big one-shot compiles (kit regen) set DOTNET_GCgen0size themselves. The applier will be narrowed to those modes before it goes in a pack. Memory cost per process: job-1793311763598 (queued on the node).")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p3_36 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P3", 36u32, "2026-10-29T22:20Z", "compiler", "10-29 18:3x compiler: apply-host-gen0-reexec.ps1 NARROWED (main agreed): it re-execs only when the arguments contain --server, --server-tcp, --batch or build-file AND DOTNET_GCgen0size is unset AND SPIRAL_GEN0_REEXEC is not 0. The child always gets DOTNET_GCgen0size set, so a re-exec loop is impossible, and a caller that already sets it (test.ps1, kit regen) never re-execs. Dry run: anchors match on the live host; parse-clean. Regression row to carry with it: the exit and stdio passthrough harness (durable copy C:\\home\\data\\pack8-drafts\\gen0-reexec-measure.ps1; one-shot arms gave identical exits 5/0 and identical stdout/stderr with re-exec forced on). Since the narrowed policy no longer re-execs one-shot modes, the row must drive a LONG-LIVED mode: --batch JOBS.tsv RESULTS.tsv with a passing and a failing job, checking the exit code and a byte-identical RESULTS.tsv with and without SPIRAL_GEN0_REEXEC=0. Built and measured together with the budget choice (256 vs 64 MB) once job-1793311763598 reports per-process memory.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p3_37 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P3", 37u32, "2026-10-29T22:29Z", "compiler", "10-29 18:3x compiler: FAIR RUN RESULT, which WITHDRAWS n32's '0.68 / beats single-flight' claim. job-1793303418017 (node, 5 rotated rounds, gcServer=0, DOTNET_GCgen0size=0x10000000 on EVERY arm including sf, in-job sf A/A): wall ratio vs sfgen0, median (min-max): sfgen0aa 0.95 (0.70-1.18), w1 1.24 (1.09-1.82), w2 1.07 (0.82-1.45), w4 1.08 (0.90-1.33), w8 1.25 (1.08-1.34). CPU: sf 77 s, A/A 75, w1 81, w2 83, w4 85, w8 85. All 30 runs B47B5749C215A6F8. With the same GC budget the engine shows NO speed win over single-flight: its ratios sit inside or just past the A/A band (+-30%) at about +10% CPU, so by the A/A rule (main 18:1x) no speed claim is made. The 0.68 came from sf lacking the budget. What DOES hold: the gen0 budget itself is a SINGLE-FLIGHT win on apps/spiral, sf CPU 124 s (curve job, default GC) -> 77 s (-38%), wall 209 -> 108 s (across two jobs and loads, so wall is directional; the CPU drop is large and consistent across rounds). So the lever is worth more for every large compile than for hopacsota. Hopacsota's parallel value stays its determinism (P6) and its future headroom; for speed, the next step is a profile of where the w8 CPU goes once GC is out of the way (cache and memory bandwidth inflation, n29).")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p3_38 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P3", 38u32, "2026-10-30T02:32Z", "compiler", "10-29 22:4x compiler: THE W8 CPU PROFILE (the next step named in n37). job-1793327075117-compiler-p3-profile, node, candidate p3d4 (sf and hopacsota DLLs), apps/spiral -> F#, DOTNET_GCgen0size=0x10000000 and gcServer=0 on both arms, dotnet-trace 10.0.750501 sampled-thread-time, whole run. Both outputs 98B5A7305CEB4164 (identical). Wall under trace: sf 137 s, w8 143 s. Busy cpu: sf 185.0 s, w8 239.7 s (+54.7 s). Innermost compiler frame by name+line (frame-hot.py), sf -> w8: HashConsTable.Add 6.2 -> 35.7 s; its bucket loop loop@7177 9.9 -> 4.9 s; data_to_rdata's node 9.0 -> 25.7 s; f@159248/f@159266 (data_to_rdata's f) 16.2 -> 24.2 s; everything else within a few s or lower (term@160170 7.7 -> 5.3). So the hash-cons path (Add + loop + node + f) is 41.3 s in sf and 90.4 s in w8: +49 s of the +55 s extra cpu. CAUSE (read from the source): HashConsTable.Add (spiral_compiler.fs:7170) runs everything inside lock sync_root, INCLUDING 'hash x', a structural hash of tuples/arrays/Maps of RData. Each join-point family's table (join_point_method/closure/type, one HashConsTable per family via PartEvalSeams.family) is shared by all 8 workers, so they queue on one lock and spin (CPU attributed to Add), and data_to_rdata's node/f run the hash-cons for every sub-node (cache-line traffic on the shared buckets, counter, total_size). Thread view: 9 engine workers, each 14-23 s busy and 65-75 s waiting. SIDE FINDING (latent bug, gpt5parallel side only): StripedHashConsTable (:7207) gives every shard its own counter, so tags repeat across shards, while ConsedNode.Equals compares tags only: two different values in different shards compare EQUAL. Used only for gpt5parallel's backend_strings (:137954); it must not be reused as-is. NEXT (D16, the chain the profile names): a decision change before code (PLAN rule 5): hash outside the lock, and stripe HashConsTable internally with globally unique tags (tag = local * shards + shard). HashConsTable is SHARED with single-flight, so G6 (single-flight projection untouched) needs an explicit decision change or a hopacsota-only table type. Scripts: C:\\home\\remote-stage\\p3-profile\\ (node-p3-profile.ps1, wait-summary.py, frame-hot.py; speedscope files kept there).")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p3_39 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P3", 39u32, "2026-10-30T04:07Z", "compiler", "10-30 00:0x compiler: STRIPED HASH-CONSING MEASURED, NOT ADOPTED (decision change and result in PLAN.md 'Decision changes', 2026-10-29 22:5x entry). apply-hashcons-striped.ps1 (10 anchored edits: 16 shards for hopacsota's 3 join-point family tables only, through a new PartEvalSeams.hash_cons seam; single-flight identical by construction). Built as p3hc on the pack7check mirror with p3d4's exact appliers + it (job-1793328321896, core 870AF34CC27CFD7C). A/B job-1793328542930, node, apps/spiral -> F#, w8, 5 rotated rounds: the smoke (small sample) gives equal outputs, all 20 big runs 791709BF6F5C5642. Cpu ratio vs p3d4, median (min-max): A/A 1.010 (0.988-1.073), B 1.028 (1.012-1.056), sf 0.877 (0.815-0.917); wall A/A 0.933, B 0.981 (node at 76-100% from other lanes). B is not below the A/A band, so by the pre-registered rule it is not adopted. WHAT THIS REFUTES: the n38 reading that the +49 s hash-cons cost was lock contention. 16x fewer contenders per lock moved nothing, so it is per-call work. NEXT (cheap first): count data_to_rdata calls per family at sf and w8 (an env-gated counter in the hopacsota layer, printed at the end like the engine's existing stats line), to see whether w8 builds join-point keys MORE OFTEN (repeated claims, abandoned or restarted bodies, Ready-cache misses) or the same number at higher cost per call (cache misses on shared nodes).")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p3_40 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P3", 40u32, "2026-10-30T04:20Z", "compiler", "10-30 00:2x compiler: ENGINE COUNTS, w1 vs w8 (job-1793333446082, node, p3d4 hopacsota, apps/spiral -> F#, SPIRAL_HOPACSOTA_STATS=1, gen0 256 MB, 2 runs each, all outputs 791709BF6F5C5642). bodies 1515 = 1515 = spawned; claims 372,788 at w1 vs 372,788/372,773 at w8; waits 0 vs 154/118; abandoned/conflicts/cycles 0. ready_hits 325,839 vs ~306,400 (-6%), so bridges 81,108 vs ~101,900 (+25%). cpu w1 78.9/80.6 s vs w8 90.9/83.0 s (sf 77.5 median in the A/B). CONCLUSION: w8 does NOT evaluate or claim more (no duplicated work, no restarts). The extra cpu is per-call cost plus ~20k more claims missing the per-thread Ready cache and taking the shared bridge. That fits n39 (striping the locks moved nothing). NEW HYPOTHESIS being measured (job-1793334098153, dotnet-counters time-in-gc + gen0/1/2 counts, sf/w1/w8 with and without the gen0 budget): n38's profile filed UNMANAGED_CODE_TIME as 'wait', but workstation GC runs in unmanaged code and suspends and scans EVERY thread's stack, and w8 has 9 deep peval stacks (1.5 GB reserved each) against single-flight's 1. Per-GC cost would then grow with the worker count, which is also why the gen0 budget helps w8 far more than sf (COMPILER-GEN0-BUDGET: -23% vs ~0 on the node).")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p3_41 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P3", 41u32, "2026-10-30T04:38Z", "compiler", "10-30 00:3x compiler: GC IS THE TAX, MEASURED (job-1793334098153, node at 76-100% from other lanes, p3d4, apps/spiral -> F#, dotnet-counters 10.0.750501 System.Runtime, gcServer 0; all 5 outputs 791709BF6F5C5642). Wall / gen0 GCs / gen1 / gen2 / total GC pause: sf default 197.1 s / 2,265 / 121 / 8 / 93.5 s; w1 default 260.3 / 2,155 / 231 / 9 / 53.2; w8 default 298.2 / 2,027 / 285 / 9 / 178.1; sf gen0 256 MB 117.7 / 35 / 33 / 4 / 14.3; w8 gen0 256 MB 146.4 / 37 / 39 / 5 / 22.4. READING: (1) with the default GC, pause is ~47% of sf's wall and ~60% of w8's; per GC, w8 pauses ~77 ms vs sf ~39 ms, so per-GC cost roughly doubles with 9 peval threads. That is the dominant w8 tax, not hash-cons locking (n39) and not extra work (n40: identical bodies and claims). (2) The pause includes suspension (waiting for every thread to reach a safepoint), which on an oversubscribed machine costs wall but little cpu. That explains why the gen0 budget cut sf wall 40% here while the node's cpu rows (COMPILER-GEN0-BUDGET n7) showed no sf cpu gain. (3) With gen0 256 MB, w8 still trails sf by 28.7 s wall, 8.1 s of it GC pause. NEXT P3 step (the D16 'chain the profile names', now GC): measure w8 with gcServer=1 (parallel marking across cores) and with a larger gen0 budget per worker, each against sf on the same settings, plus an in-job A/A. n38's profile reading is corrected: UNMANAGED_CODE_TIME there was largely GC, not idle waiting.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p3_42 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P3", 42u32, "2026-10-30T10:25Z", "compiler", "10-30 01:1x compiler (recorded in the 06:2x groom): GC SETTINGS A/B, job-1793335277435, node, p3d4, apps/spiral -> F#, gen0 256 MB, 3 rotated rounds, all outputs 791709BF6F5C5642. Wall ratio vs sf, median (min-max) / GC pause / cpu: sf A/A 1.005 (0.898-1.036) / 12.9 s / 78.0 s; w8 1.266 (0.933-1.333) / 21.8 s / 85.5 s; w8 gcServer=1 1.183 (1.041-1.230) / 8.0 s / 84.1 s; w8 gen0 1 GB 1.198 (1.173-1.205) / 21.6 s / 77.4 s (gen0 count unchanged at 33-41, so the runtime caps the budget); sf gcServer=1 0.941 (0.840-1.147) / 7.2 s / 72.2 s. READING: server GC halves the pause for both engines, but w8 stays ~18% SLOWER in wall than sf even with less GC pause than sf. So GC is a tax, but not why 8 workers give no speedup. n38's thread view (9 engine threads, each 14-23 s busy of a 143 s run) suggests the engine is effectively serial; a concurrency sweep of the w8 trace (concurrency.py, time at k busy engine threads) was cut off by the session restart. NEXT: run it (CPU-light, node), then attack the measured serial chain (D16).")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p3_43 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P3", 43u32, "2026-10-30T10:30Z", "compiler", "10-30 06:3x compiler: CONCURRENCY MEASURED (job-1793355992357, concurrency.py over n38's w8 speedscope: busy intervals per engine thread, swept; engine threads = 10). Share of the engine span with k engine threads computing: k=0 8.0%, k=1 46.6%, k=2 19.9%, k=3 13.0%, k=4 8.8%, k>=5 3.8%. Mean concurrency 1.81 over the span (1.96 while any engine thread runs). READING: the parallel frontier is NARROW. Eight workers seldom have more than two ready bodies, so the ceiling is ~1.8x before overhead. The engine's costs (bridges +25% from Ready-cache misses, n40; GC pause per GC ~2x with more deep stacks, n41-n42) eat that margin, so w8 nets out slower than single-flight. This matches R3's caveat (the span is dominated by a few big single bodies: root 8.6 s self, method#639 7.3 s, method#283 4.7 s). NEXT, by D16 order: technique 2 (speculate on unannotated bodies as soon as their key is first seen) and technique 4 (parse/typecheck/codegen in parallel beside peval) widen the frontier, while technique 1 (fork typed bodies) is already in. First a frontier census: per body, the time from key discovery to claim, to see whether ready work exists but is claimed late (a scheduling fix) or simply does not exist (needs speculation or D16.4).")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p3_44 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P3", 44u32, "2026-10-30T16:49Z", "compiler", "10-30 12:49 compiler: WORKLIST TRACE READ, the dispatch question is answered: the worklist is NOT the limit. PLAN.md decision entry 10-30 11:00 (one allowlisted clock, read only while SPIRAL_HOPACSOTA_TRACE is set) plus its result line. Two earlier node attempts died at the hopacsota lint G2 (Stopwatch); the fix funnels all clock reads through one allowlisted trace_clock line. Node job-1793372514310-compiler-p3tr3 on the pack7check mirror, candidate p3tr = p3-shard + p3-ivar + d4-restart + worklist-trace, built in 1,484 s under load. Gate: traced w8 = untraced w8 = 791709BF6F5C5642 (the trace changes nothing it observes). W8 states over the 388.1 s body span: all threads busy with work queued 90.9%, no work 9.0%, token-starved 0.0%, dispatchable-but-idle 0.02%. Mean outstanding 104.3 bodies; queue wait median 12.9 s, p90 65.8 s. So the frontier is wide and the 8 tokens are always handed out. 'Busy' includes D5 waits and GC freezes; with GC pause at about half of wall and mean cpu concurrency 1.81 (earlier notes), the bodies are held but mostly not running. That is consistent with n38's hash-cons result (a lock fix moved nothing). NEXT (a new PLAN decision, measurement first): allocation per body (data_to_rdata/ret_ty allocation counts at w1 vs w8 via dotnet-counters alloc-rate plus an EventPipe allocation sample), then a GC-side arm (gcServer=1 with per-heap gen0 budgets, or DOTNET_GCHeapCount) as an A/B with an in-job A/A. Reader: C:\\home\\remote-stage\\p3-profile\\worklist-states.py on node; trace C:\\home\\remote-stage\\p3tr\\w8.trace.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p3_45 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P3", 45u32, "2026-10-30T16:55Z", "compiler", "10-30 12:55 compiler, n44 addendum: the job's w4 arm agrees. Output 791709BF6F5C5642, wall 742.4 s; trace span 548.2 s: all busy with work queued 98.1%, no work 1.8%, token-starved 0.0%; mean outstanding 213.9; queue wait median 84.0 s. Both widths are saturated with held work and never token-starved, so dispatch is ruled out at w4 and w8 alike. Job exit 0 (job-1793372514310).")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p3_46 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P3", 46u32, "2026-10-30T21:16Z", "compiler", "10-30 17:16 compiler: BRIDGE 17:16 MEASURED (PLAN.md extension 15:1x, reading rule fixed before the run). Node job-1793387673117, candidate p3br = p3-shard + p3-ivar + d4-restart + worklist-trace + NEW apply-hopacsota-bridge-trace.ps1 (times bridge = Scheduler.run control with the one allowlisted trace_clock, only while tracing; lint 0 hits outside the allowlist). w8 on apps/spiral -> F#: output 791709BF6F5C5642 (unchanged), engine stats as before (1,515 bodies, 372,773 claims, 101,785 bridges). Body-held thread-time 3,875.7 s over a 570.6 s span; bridge time on body threads 1,243.9 s = 32.1% of it; mean 17.8 ms per bridge call vs lane B's 0.27 us uncontended (about 66,000x). The per-thread split is even (110-210 s each; the root's 572.9 s in 107 bridges is its Drain wait). READING by the rule: MIXED (10-50%), so both sides get measured. But this is the first direct serialization evidence: a third of every body thread's time is queued on the 1-worker control scheduler that D5 2b routes every claim through. NEXT: (1) the same job's w2 arm (if running out of cap, a w2-only rerun): per-call bridge time should drop sharply with 2 threads if this is contention; (2) a PLAN decision entry on D5 2b: decide claims on the caller under a striped or lock-free guard instead of Scheduler.run control, A/B against this trace (bridge share and w8 wall, with an in-job A/A). Reader: C:\\home\\remote-stage\\bridge-share.py (node). Relevant to HOPACSOTA-HOPAC-DEPTH's open adoptions.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p3_47 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P3", 47u32, "2026-10-30T21:30Z", "compiler", "10-30 17:30 compiler: CORRECTION to n46 plus the w2 arm. The reader assumed workers-1 body threads; the trace has `workers` body contexts (ctx 1..w) plus the root, so held time was undercounted. Corrected w8: held 4,445.9 s, bridge 1,243.9 s = 28.0% (not 32.1%), still MIXED. w2 (same job, exit 0, output 791709BF6F5C5642): held 507.0 s over 254.4 s, bridge 206.3 s = 40.7%, 86,469 calls at a mean of 5.3 ms, also MIXED. READING: per-call cost grows with threads (5.3 -> 17.8 ms, a contention part), but the share is HIGHER at w2, and even 2 threads pay ~20,000x lane B's 0.27 us uncontended. Each bridge hands work to the control scheduler's single worker thread, and on a node at 100% from other lanes, waking it costs an OS time slice. So these numbers are dominated by wakeup latency under oversubscription and overstate a quiet machine. NEXT: the same p3br w2/w8 traced runs in a short EXCLUSIVE quiet slot (box, per main's 10-30 policy for timing work), then the D5 2b decision entry from those numbers. The p3br candidate exists on the node; the box needs its own build (~10 min). PLAN.md has both result lines (w8 corrected, w2 added).")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p3_48 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P3", 48u32, "2026-10-30T22:02Z", "compiler", "10-30 18:02 compiler: QUIET-BOX BRIDGE NUMBERS (exclusive slot, pre-check 0 foreign jobs, cpu 7%; job-1793397619176; same p3br binaries, core 64D379AD60C15061, apps/spiral on the box tree, gen0 256 MB, gcServer 0; outputs A6119E31D4DF4400 x3). w8: 29.3 s wall / 61.6 s cpu, bridge 23.6% of body-held time, 607 us/call; w8 in-job A/A: 30.2 s / 63.9 s, 21.5%, 586 us/call (noise about ±2 pt); w2: 30.8 s / 60.8 s, 5.1%, 280 us/call. By the pre-registered rule: w2 CLEARS the bridge, w8 is MIXED. The per-call cost doubles from w2 to w8, so it is contention growing with threads; the node's 5.3-17.8 ms/call (n46-n47) was oversubscription. w8 has NO wall gain over w2, and at w8 the body threads hold work for ~167 thread-s while the process uses ~62 cpu-s, so ~105 thread-s are held but off-CPU: the bridge ~39 s, the rest D5 waits (948 vs 280) plus GC suspension. NEXT (PLAN.md 'Quiet result' entry): a decision entry before code on (a) taking the remaining Decide claims off Scheduler.run control (ready_hits already serve 306k of 372k claims lock-free) or striping the guard per shard, and (b) timing D5 waits per thread with the same trace clock (counted today, not timed). This is the first quiet-machine evidence for where w8's time goes.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p3_49 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P3", 49u32, "2026-10-30T22:54Z", "compiler", "10-30 18:54 compiler: BRIDGE SPLIT measured (new apply-hopacsota-bridge-split.ps1, stacked on bridge-trace; anchors re-fitted to the stacked candidate; lint 0 hits outside the allowlist; box candidate p3split core DB948637AA3B0468, job-1793398647216 in a drained bench slot, though the box was at 100% from non-kit work, so absolute times are inflated and only proportions are read). Body-thread time at w8 (A/A pair): D5 waits (await) 40.6 / 37.2 s; claim bridges 57.9 / 70.8 s, of which INSIDE table.Decide only 1.3 / 1.6 s and reaching it 56.6 / 69.2 s. At w2: waits 0.8 s, claim bridges 3.5 s (Decide 1.1 s). READING: the decision itself is nearly free; about 98% of claim-bridge time at w8 is the transport. The candidate already stripes the guard 16 ways (guards.[shard_of id], p3-shard), so the queue is Scheduler.run on the control scheduler created with NumWorkers = Some 1. D5 waits grow ~50x from w2 to w8 and are the second lever. DECISION ENTRY written before code (PLAN.md 2026-10-30 18:5x, proposed): control gets NumWorkers = Some workers; determinism argument recorded there. Applier apps/compiler/packs/apply-hopacsota-control-workers.ps1 (1 anchor, ok on the stacked core). Pre-registered gate: ABBA w8 A = p3split vs B = p3split + control-workers, outputs identical, ADOPT iff B's reaching-Decide time is at least halved AND B's wall beats A by more than A's A/A spread. Candidate B (p3cw) is building on the box; the A/B runs in the next bench window (box-p3cw-ab.ps1).")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p3_50 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P3", 50u32, "2026-10-30T23:03Z", "compiler", "10-30 19:03 compiler: CONTROL-WORKERS A/B, NOT ADOPTED by the pre-registered gate (PLAN.md 18:5x entry + its result line). Box job-1793401077046, bench + compile-hold, ABBA at w8, all 4 outputs A6119E31D4DF4400 (the box was at 100% from non-kit work; ABBA spreads it over both arms). A = p3split (DB948637AA3B0468): walls 68.6 / 66.3 s (A/A spread 2.3 s), reaching-Decide 74.7 / 69.9 s. B = A + apply-hopacsota-control-workers.ps1 (0DB15F6AC5ED1E3D, control NumWorkers = workers): walls 68.3 / 68.0 s, reaching-Decide 60.9 / 61.2 s. -16% on the transport, nothing on the wall, so the bridge's cost is Scheduler.run from a foreign thread itself plus the shard MVar queues, not the control worker count. The applier stays in packs/ as a record, in no pack. Today's P3 picture, all measured (n44-n49 + this): the worklist never starves; at w8 ~105 of ~167 body-thread-seconds are off-CPU; ~25% of held time is bridges, almost all transport (Decide itself ~1.5 s); D5 waits grow ~50x from w2 to w8. NEXT: D5 waits: which keys, and why w8 bodies need keys other bodies are still computing (trace per wait: key family, holder, wait time).")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p3_51 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P3", 51u32, "2026-10-30T23:28Z", "compiler", "10-30 19:28 compiler: D5 WAIT LOG measured (new apply-hopacsota-wait-log.ps1, stacked on bridge-split; lint 0 hits; box job-1793401646085, candidate p3wait core 9FA8B2FD93C35EFC, in a bench window, box at 100% from non-kit work; outputs w8 = w2 = 8740373223C658E6). w8: 439 waits, 41.7 s, median 6.3 ms, p90 152 ms, max 5.9 s; the 10 longest waits are 63% of wait time, the 50 longest 87%; waited-on holders skewed (t4 17.8 s, t1 9.0 s, t3 6.6 s, others at most 2.5 s). w2: 70 waits, 1.1 s. All keys are Tuple`2, so the type is uninformative. READING: w8's D5 time is a few LONG-POLE bodies that many waiters need, a critical-path shape, not a sync cost. The levers are scheduling (start long-pole bodies early) or granularity (split them). NEXT: name the 10 longest waits' keys (join-point name/arity; extend the wait log to print a key description), then a decision entry. TODAY'S P3 MAP (n44-n50 + this): the worklist never starves; at w8 ~105 of ~167 body-thread-s are off-CPU = bridge transport ~40-60 s (Decide itself ~1.5 s; more control workers -16%, not adopted) + D5 waits ~40 s (long poles) + GC. Readers: C:\\home\\data\\cuda-python\\bridge-share.py and wait-summary.py.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p3_52 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P3", 52u32, "2026-10-30T23:41Z", "compiler", "10-30 19:41 compiler: LONG POLES NAMED: they are !!!!BackendSwitch arm VALIDATION. New apply-hopacsota-wait-keys.ps1 (stacked on wait-log; lint 0 hits) prints %A of the key for waits over 50 ms. Box job-1793403051908 (p3keys core 829377FE243FCEF3, bench window, box at 100% from non-kit work), w8 output 8740373223C658E6: 260 waits, 41.7 s; the 10 longest waits are 60% of wait time (max 5.5 s), and ALL 10 wait on the same key shape, a seq of (backend, RData[]) -> YPrim BoolT pairs over Gleam/Lua/Python/Cpp/...: the backend_switch_validated table (every arm of a switch evaluated for every backend once per build, AGENTS.md BackendSwitch). One body validates a hot switch's arms for ~14 backends while every other body reaching that switch blocks on it (holders t1 21.5 s, t4 11.6 s). Only the current backend's arm produces the value; the others matter only for type errors. CANDIDATE DECISION recorded in PLAN.md (not adopted; to be written as its own entry with a measurement first): take validation off the requester's critical path (finish it later, or key it per backend so bodies split the work), keeping exactly single-flight's first error. Gate idea in PLAN.md (ABBA w8 with A/A, outputs identical, BackendSwitch-mismatch negatives keep their first error). This is the most specific P3 lever found so far; together with the bridge transport (n49-n50) it accounts for most of w8's off-CPU time.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p3_53 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P3", 53u32, "2026-10-30T23:42Z", "compiler", "10-30 19:42 compiler, code pointers for the validation decision (live tree, single-flight side): the BackendSwitch op validates each other arm at spiral_compiler.fs ~160930-160946. The key is (backend, data_to_rdata of the arm closure); PartEvalSeams.validated / key_hit / key_body 'bsv' / fill route it through the hopacsota table, so the first body to reach a (backend, arm) pair evaluates apply s (b, DB) for that backend (deep lib bodies take seconds) and every later body blocks on the claim. validate_type t' then compares against the current arm. The requester needs the validated type only to raise a mismatch error, so the candidate is to let a requester that finds the key in flight continue and check later at its join, keeping single-flight's first-error order; the gpt5parallel side (137966-137974, 153668, 155483) has the same table as a ConcurrentDictionary.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p3_54 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P3", 54u32, "2026-10-31T01:28Z", "compiler", "10-30 21:28 compiler: DECISION ENTRY WRITTEN (proposed, not adopted), PLAN.md 'BackendSwitch validation stops blocking its requesters'. Mechanism read in the tree: Engine.Validated (~53907) claims (table, (backend, arm RData)) via settle, and later bodies block in await while the owner runs apply s (b, DB). Constraints: re-evaluating in the requester would duplicate global registration (validation emits globals, e.g. rust_type's F# aliases); skipping loses the requester's own arm comparison. Proposal: a deferred check. Open item (a) is ANSWERED: the validated type feeds only validate_type, an equality chain seeded by the first arm in key order, so collecting arm types in key order and comparing once all fill gives the same first mismatch and the same message. Still open: (b) raising a deferred error at single-flight's position (D4 restart may serve); (c) a quiet-box upper bound on the gain (~40 s of D5 wait in ~167 body-thread-s at w8). Gate defined in the entry.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p3_55 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P3", 55u32, "2026-10-31T02:34Z", "compiler", "10-30 22:34 compiler: deferred BackendSwitch validation, DESIGN written (PLAN.md 'BackendSwitch validation stops blocking its requesters', Design 22:2x) and APPLIER drafted: packs/apply-hopacsota-deferred-validation.ps1 (11 anchors, stacked on p3keys; scratch apply ok; lint G1 754/5,934 lines, largest section 501/1,000, G2 0 hits). Single-flight changes by one seam line (validated_then replaces validated + key_hit + read, same calls in the same order on its side). A requester that meets an in-flight validation drops a 'deferred' placeholder marker at its position and continues; Drain re-claims each deferred key, appends the owner's marker to the placeholder (Finish then expands the validation's effects at the first single-flight position) and runs the check; an unfilled key or a failed check raises HopacsotaConflict; a failing build that deferred anything restarts canonically (D4), so the first error text is single-flight's. NEW negative sample samples/frontier_hopacsota_deferred_validation_mismatch (3 annotated join points reach one switch whose Bend arm spins at compile time then returns bool; ExpectedErrors 'The backend switch needs to have the same type for all of its branches.'): single-flight 'Got: bool / Expected: i32'; A = p3keys under parallel w8: same error 5/5 with waits=2, so the sample drives bodies into an in-flight validation (the deferral path). The existing oracle had NO BackendSwitch arm-mismatch negative, so without this sample the gate's error-order clause would have been vacuous. Running: job-1793412772270-compiler-p3defer-abba (box-p3-abba.ps1, a generic ABBA runner replacing the per-experiment copies: builds B = p3defer from p3keys + the applier, then A1 B1 B2 A2 on apps/spiral -> F# at w8 under bench + compile-hold; gate: outputs identical, no canonical restart, D5 wait total at least halved, B wall faster than A by more than the A/A spread). After it: the mismatch sample on B, 5 repeats, must print the same error.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p3_56 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P3", 56u32, "2026-10-31T04:09Z", "compiler", "10-31 00:09 compiler: p3defer BUILT (lint 755/5,934, G2 0; 4,662 s on a saturated box, BelowNormal starvation lifted mid-build) and its ERROR-ORDER clause is MET. frontier_hopacsota_deferred_validation_mismatch under parallel w8, 5 repeats: A = p3keys waits=2 -> 'Got: bool / Expected: i32' 5/5; B = p3defer waits=0 deferred=2..4 -> 'restarting the build in canonical single-flight order (D4)' then the same 'Got: bool / Expected: i32' 5/5. The deferred path, the placeholder markers and the failing-build restart all ran. Speed clauses (D5 wait total halved, wall beyond A's A/A spread, outputs identical with no restart) wait on job-1793412772270's ABBA, which now waits for bench + compile-hold.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p3_57 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P3", 57u32, "2026-10-31T05:41Z", "compiler", "10-31 01:41 compiler: the p3defer speed bench is DEFERRED, not run. Its bench window waited 50 min behind kit-train5e's heavy-build window (232 min left at 01:40), and its queued request blocked my own heavy builds (a waiting bench request holds back new heavy-build grants). Stopped job-1793422133040; the request was gone with it. Rerun when the box has no heavy window: pwsh C:\\home\\data\\cuda-python\\box-p3-abba.ps1 -A p3keys -B p3defer -Metric Waits (both builds exist, no -BuildFrom needed). The correctness clause is already met (n56).")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p3_58 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P3", 58u32, "2026-10-31T13:39Z", "compiler", "10-31 10-31 09:39 compiler: the deferred p3keys-vs-p3defer ABBA bench's script C:\\home\\data\\cuda-python\\box-p3-abba.ps1 is ARCHIVED with the C:\\home\\data drain (DATA-DIR-HOME), together with its two python helpers, bridge-share.py and wait-summary.py (no .py in repos, and C:\\home\\data is closed). When the bench runs: use apps/compiler/packs/measure-arms.ps1 (alternating arms over apps/spiral, -GcServer 0) for wall and pause, and port the Waits/SPLIT readers from the archived helpers to PowerShell inside measure-arms as a -Trace option rather than restoring a separate script. Restore the old one with scratch-archive.ps1 -Restore <manifest of the 10-31 data archive> -Only cuda-python.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p3_59 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P3", 59u32, "2026-10-31T14:40Z", "compiler", "10-31 10-31 10:40 compiler: BENCH TOOLING READY (no .py, no separate script). apps/compiler/packs/measure-arms.ps1 gained -Trace: each run sets SPIRAL_HOPACSOTA_TRACE=<out>\\<arm>-<round>.trace and SPIRAL_HOPACSOTA_STATS=1, and each row adds WaitsS (sum of .waits), ReachingS (claim-bridge time minus Decide minus D5 waits, i.e. pure transport) and BridgeShare (bridge time / body-held thread-time), plus a Restart flag (canonical restart seen in the log). The summary prints their medians per arm. Ported from the archived bridge-share.py / wait-summary.py and A/B-checked on 13 archived traces: identical to the python on every value it prints (p3-keys w8: waits 41.7 s, reaching 64.6 s, share 27.4%; p3-split w8a 56.6 s / 28.7%; p3-wait w2 waits 1.1 s, 5.0 s / 7.5%; p3cw-ab B1 60.9 s / 28.0%). Older traces without .waits or without the split columns now give null rather than a misleading 0. BENCH COMMAND (needs a bench window; measure-arms takes none itself): kit window request bench 60 --label compiler-p3defer --wait 120, then pwsh apps/compiler/packs/measure-arms.ps1 -Trace -Rounds 4 -Arms 'p3keys=<cand>\\p3keys\\cache\\bin\\hopacsota\\SpiralCompiler\\Release\\net11.0\\SpiralCompiler.dll|SPIRAL_HOPACSOTA_POLICY=parallel,SPIRAL_HOPACSOTA_SHARDS=16,SPIRAL_HOPACSOTA_WORKERS=8,DOTNET_GCgen0size=0x10000000;p3defer=<same with p3defer>|<same settings>' (<cand> = %LOCALAPPDATA%\\spiral-bin-candidates). Pre-registered reading unchanged (n55): ADOPT when outputs are identical, no canonical restart, median Waits at least halved, and the p3defer wall below p3keys by more than the p3keys round-to-round spread.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p3_60 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P3", 60u32, "2026-10-31T19:31Z", "compiler", "10-31 10-31 15:31 compiler: p3defer BENCH RAN on the node (box at 100% with train6's heavy window): node job job-1793474035968-compiler-p3defer-ab, measure-arms.ps1 -Trace -Rounds 4 over the node checkout's apps/spiral -> Fsharp, both arms SPIRAL_HOPACSOTA_POLICY=parallel, SHARDS=16, WORKERS=8, gen0 256 MB, gcServer 0; arms are the box-built candidates p3keys (core 829377FE243FCEF3) and p3defer (BB7AFC58173D0E7E), shipped to C:\\home\\remote-stage\\p3ab. Node load 95-100% throughout. RESULT: all 8 outputs 791709BF6F5C5642 (the canonical output), 0 canonical restarts. p3keys median wall 113.4 s (rounds 101.6/114.3/113.4/146.3, spread 44.8 s), waits 55.3 s median (240 waits), reaching 115.8 s, bridge share 0.31. p3defer median wall 117.8 s (117.8/126.5/123.3/104.8), waits 0.7 s (8 waits, 200 deferred validations), reaching 131.8 s, bridge share 0.20. VERDICT by the pre-registered rule (n55): NOT ADOPTED. Outputs identical, no restart and waits cut 99% all hold, but p3defer is not faster than p3keys by more than the p3keys spread (median ratio 1.09, min 0.72, max 1.16). Reading: deferring BackendSwitch validation removes the D5 waits, but the time reappears as claim-bridge transport (reaching +16 s median), confirming HOPACSOTA-HOPAC-DEPTH n(10-30 21:39): the w8 bottleneck is crossing from body threads to the control scheduler, not the waits. Next lever is design-level there (decide on the caller thread, i.e. a G2 decision entry, or CPS peval), not more wait removal. apply-hopacsota-deferred-validation.ps1 stays a pending candidate (correct, no speed win yet); it is worth re-measuring on a quiet node, since a 44.8 s A/A spread hides a <10% effect.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p3_61 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P3", 61u32, "2026-10-31T21:52Z", "compiler", "10-31 10-31 17:52 compiler: p3defer RE-MEASURE QUEUED on the node with an in-job A/A arm (n60's verdict was limited by a 44.8 s spread and no A/A): job-1793483387012-compiler-p3defer-aa, measure-arms -Trace -Rounds 6, arms p3keys, p3keysb (= the same p3keys DLL) and p3defer, each POLICY=parallel, SHARDS=16, WORKERS=8, gen0 256 MB; output C:\\home\\remote-stage\\p3ab\\out-aa. It shares the node with main's lib sweep (job-1793483260661), which the A/A arm accounts for. Reading: as n55, judged against the p3keys/p3keysb spread instead of p3keys' round spread. Queued after the user caught the node idle at 17:42 (NODE-ALWAYS-FED). NEXT node-runnable compiler step, ready to queue when this ends: MUTUAL-TAIL-LOOPS' native half (the P rows' Rust builds and runs vs C) once its box predict job-1793481217029 reads out.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p3_62 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P3", 62u32, "2026-10-31T22:26Z", "compiler", "10-31 10-31 18:26 compiler: p3defer RE-MEASURE with A/A (job-1793483387012, 6 rounds, node at 92-100% with main's lib sweep beside it): p3keys median wall 125.8 s; p3keysb (= the same p3keys DLL) 115.4 s, ratio 0.93 (0.76-1.15); p3defer 114.1 s, ratio 0.85 (0.78-1.30). CPU identical across arms (medians 101.6 / 103.4 / 102.6 s). Waits: p3keys 62.3 s (606 waits), p3keysb 54.3 s, p3defer 0.8 s (13 waits, 406 deferred validations), a 98-99% cut again. Reaching: 135.8 / 132.0 / 112.7 s (the reverse of n60's direction, so this metric is noise on a loaded node too). All 18 outputs 791709BF6F5C5642, 0 canonical restarts. VERDICT unchanged: NOT ADOPTED on speed. p3defer's wall sits inside the A/A spread, so there's no measurable wall win; it removes waits without changing CPU or wall. It stays a correct pending candidate. A speed claim either way needs a quiet node or cpu-only A/B; the evaluator's w8 limit lies elsewhere (HOPACSOTA-HOPAC-DEPTH: claim transport / design-level levers).")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p3_63 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P3", 63u32, "2026-11-01T17:14Z", "compiler", "11-01 13:12 compiler: APPLIERS MOVED OUT OF THE REPO (user 11-01 13:0x drafts rule). apply-hopacsota-p3-ivar.ps1 (sha256 7473979B2C40...) and apply-hopacsota-p3-shard.ps1 (85A66F68A56D...) now live at %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\spiral\\HOPACSOTA-P3\\. Byte-identical move, verified by hash. Before either rides a pack, re-dry-run it on the then-live core: the last ship-readiness run (n33, 10-29) predates packs 8-14a, so its anchors may be stale.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p3_64 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P3", 64u32, "2026-11-03T07:30Z", "compiler", "11-03 07:3x compiler GROOM (WIP cap: Active = touched in 12 h). Parked to Planned (38 h). Next: re-dry-run apply-hopacsota-p3-ivar/shard (kit drafts HOPACSOTA-P3) on the live pack23 core 28F212AEFACE68EC before any pack.")
