inl x_client_token_refresh () = Task ("X-CLIENT-TOKEN-REFRESH", "xc refresh ENV_FILE: trade X_OAUTH2_REFRESH_TOKEN for a new access and refresh token at POST /2/oauth2/token (confidential client Basic auth) and rewrite X_OAUTH2_ACCESS_TOKEN/X_OAUTH2_REFRESH_TOKEN in the env file in place without printing them", Story, P3, D2, 100u32, Done, "", "x,auth", "current", "current")
inl backend_switch_globals_leak () = Task ("BACKEND-SWITCH-GLOBALS-LEAK", "BackendSwitch validation is not hermetic for globals (found 23:58 2026-10-15 while migrating lib is_zig guards): evaluating a non-selected arm for its type runs global calls it reaches, so their text lands in the selected backend's output", TaskKind, P1, D4, 100u32, Done, "", "compiler,backend-switch,both-cores", "current", "current")
inl rust_union_impls () = Task ("RUST-UNION-IMPLS", "Rust backend emits an enum plus 'impl tag' and 'impl Default' (about 18 lines) for every union instantiation, and one array-collect loop per element type: x/apps/client/view/view.rs (1786 lines) spends 302 on 17 enum impls and 182 on 8 from_* loops; repro: any crate using list post, list link, list medium, option x -- emit Default/tag only when used, or generically", Story, P3, D4, 100u32, Done, "", "compiler,rust,code-shape", "current", "current")
inl rust_match_duplication () = Task ("RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION", "Pattern-match lowering duplicates arm bodies on every backend (core peval, not codegenRust): or-patterns and wildcard fallbacks reach one shared continuation through the prepass EPatternMemo node, which peval evaluates once per path, so the body is emitted per alternative (or as a nested-match staircase); plus aliasing specialization (SPIRAL-LITERAL-ALIAS-SPECIALIZATION) and Rust-only flattened-record moves through recursion (x posts.merge_newest 107 lines); measured in x/apps/client args (1243 before workarounds) and view", Story, P0, D4, 35u32, Planned, "PACK-7", "team:compiler,rust,code-shape,main,wipe-redo", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_rust_match_duplication () = Planning ("RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION", Minutes 480u32, Load Heavy, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_rust_match_duplication_1 () = Note ("RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION", 1u32, "2026-10-17T10:02Z", "main-thread", "06:02 main-thread night measurement (6-line repeated windows, variable numbers normalized): x view.rs 26 percent of 1,884 lines, top window x16 = runs of Rc str clone moves (flattened record fields moved one by one), fetch.rs 18, args.rs 11. The text metric also counts program shape (a mega sample scores 98 from unrolled straight-line code), so the next measure is by AST: clone-move runs and duplicated arm bodies.")
inl note_of_rust_match_duplication_2 () = Note ("RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION", 2u32, "2026-10-19T12:47Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 08:47 helper-lane lap 1: first AST-level measurement, and it moves the item: lab/RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION/or_pattern.spi (union of 3 payload cases + Dot, one or-pattern arm | Circle x | Square x | Triangle x => x * x + 7i32 * x - 3i32, inside a let function) compiled with the installed compiler (Invoke-Heavy, try.ps1) emits the arm body 3 times on every backend checked: C (v3/v8/v13 = 7l * v...), Zig (@as(i32, 7) *% ...) and Rust (7i32.wrapping_mul ...), all exit 41. So the or-pattern copy is made by partial evaluation, shared by all backends, not by codegenRust; the fix belongs in the core's or-pattern lowering (bind the matched payload to one variable and emit the body once, or wrap it in a join point), and its gain shows on C/Zig too. The other shapes in the title (_ staircases, aliasing specialization, flattened-record moves) still need their own probes.")
inl note_of_rust_match_duplication_3 () = Note ("RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION", 3u32, "2026-10-19T13:32Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 09:32 helper-lane lap 2: second shape probed (lab/RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION/wildcard_pair.spi: match a, b with | Red, Red | Green, Green | _ => 7 * 3 + 1, inside a let function; C and Rust exit 224): the wildcard arm becomes a staircase of nested matches with the fallback body written 3 times (once under each first-scrutinee case plus the outer _), on both C and Rust - here the body folded to 22, but a non-constant body is copied the same way, so like the or-pattern this is partial evaluation's multi-scrutinee lowering, not codegenRust. Same probe also shows the aliasing duplication from SPIRAL-LITERAL-ALIAS-SPECIALIZATION: pair_score a b and pair_score b b produce two functions, pair_score_0(v0, v1) and pair_score_1(v0), because the second call passes one variable twice. So 3 of the title's 4 shapes are core (peval) behaviours shared by every backend; only the flattened-record moves are Rust-specific.")
inl note_of_rust_match_duplication_4 () = Note ("RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION", 4u32, "2026-10-19T15:14Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 11:14 helper-lane lap 3: retitled to the core cause and located it. In the pattern prepass (spiral_compiler.fs, pattern compile around 53005-53040) PatOr(a, b) builds cp id a on_succ (cp id b on_succ on_fail) with on_succ wrapped as EPatternMemo, and every clause's failure path chains into the next, ending in EPatternMiss; EPatternMemo is only a memo of the prepassed term (Utils.memoize at 52386/52525/52706) and is gone before partial evaluation (157548/159834 fail if one survives), so peval sees the same E reached from several control paths and inlines it on each - hence one copy of the arm per or-alternative, and the wildcard staircase where the fallback is reached from under each first-scrutinee case. Fix direction (the standard ML or-pattern lowering): when a memoized continuation is reachable from more than one path and is not trivially small, lower it to a local join point whose parameters are the pattern's bound variables, and have each path call it; that turns N copies into one body plus N calls on every backend. Related: SPIRAL-LITERAL-ALIAS-SPECIALIZATION covers the aliasing shape.")
inl note_of_rust_match_duplication_5 () = Note ("RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION", 5u32, "2026-10-19T15:44Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 11:44 helper-lane lap 4: pinned the elimination point and the tool for the fix. The shared continuation dies in the prepass rewrite at spiral_compiler.fs 52706 (EPatternMemo x -> Utils.memoize dict f x: every reference gets the same rewritten E object, so peval later walks it once per path), with the free-variable pass doing the same at 52386. The prepass already has the node the fix needs: EJoinPoint of Range * E * TPrepass option * backend option * name option (51957), whose body becomes one method that every call site invokes with its free variables (the pattern-bound variables included) as arguments. Sketch: at 52706, when the memo is hit for the second time and the continuation is not trivially small (a literal, a variable, or a short op), emit it once as a local EJoinPoint bound by an ELet and replace each reference by an application of that binding; the first visit cannot know yet, so the rewrite needs a reference count of EPatternMemo nodes first (one cheap prior walk over the clause tree). Cost is one call per path instead of one body copy per path, on every backend. Next: a lab patch with the count + join-point lowering, probed on the or-pattern and wildcard samples via a Debug candidate.")
inl note_of_rust_match_duplication_6 () = Note ("RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION", 6u32, "2026-10-19T15:48Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 11:48 helper-lane: correction to the lap-4 sketch and a concrete first patch. Lowering at 52706 cannot add join points: lower (52580) turns each EJoinPoint into EJoinPoint' using scope.[x], a free-variable table computed earlier per node, so a node created during lowering has no scope entry. The join point must exist before that pass, i.e. at pattern compilation. Staged lab/RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION/or_join_patch.py (anchor --check OK, shared prepass so both cores): PatOr now memoizes EJoinPoint(range, on_succ, None, None, None) instead of the bare continuation, unless the continuation is a literal, unit, variable or symbol; both alternatives then call one join-point method whose arguments are the pattern-bound variables. Scope: or-patterns only (the wildcard/next-clause chain stays as is for now, it touches every multi-clause match). run.ps1 compiles or_pattern.spi before and after on C and Rust and counts the arm body's copies (expect exit 41, 3 copies -> 1). HEAVY QUEUE row added.")
inl note_of_rust_match_duplication_7 () = Note ("RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION", 7u32, "2026-10-19T18:27Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 14:27 helper-lane: pack 8 bisect (lab/BACKLOG-LAPS/pack8-bisect): or_join_patch.py alone breaks samples/frontier_format_any_union and frontier_join_format_any on every backend (TracedError: Expected a symbol as the index into the layout type. Got: %d, i32), while the positional-rename and Assign candidates compile both. lib's format code uses type-annotated or-patterns in real code (sm'_real.spir 660-689: | (_ : f32) | (_ : f64) => ...), and the prepass does not distinguish real code (RawReal(r,a) -> f a, 53272), so the join-point wrapping also hit compile-time, type-directed continuations. Dropped from pack 8. Redesign options: skip the wrapping when an alternative carries a type annotation or type test (the continuation is type-specialized anyway), or lower only in runtime code by threading a real flag through the prepass; whichever is chosen must pass the format_any samples and a lib Cells sweep before it rides a pack.")
inl note_of_rust_match_duplication_8 () = Note ("RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION", 8u32, "2026-10-22T07:35Z", "main", "03:35 main: v2 of the or-pattern join point drafted (lab/RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION/or_join_patch_v2.py; dry run by default; refuses if already applied). It applies the 10-19 14:27 redesign: wrap the shared continuation in EJoinPoint only when NEITHER alternative is type-directed. typeDirected walks the pattern and is true for PatAnnot, PatExists, PatFilledDefaultValue, PatDefaultValue and PatNominal (all compile-time type tests), recursing through Dyn/Unbox/When/Pair/Or/And/record members/arrays. So sm'_real.spir's '| (_ : f32) | (_ : f64)' keeps today's lowering, the case that broke frontier_format_any_union in pack 8. Trivial continuations (ELit, EB, EV, ESymbol) stay unwrapped as in v1. Checked without a build: every constructor and arity exists (E.EJoinPoint has 5 fields at core 52023; PatDefaultValue and PatRecordMembers* match the raw Pattern at 43910-43930), and the anchor matches once on both the tree core and the PACK-AFTER-NIGHT core. Gate (its own pack, not PACK-AFTER-NIGHT, because it changes peval for every or-pattern): Debug candidate; run.ps1 counts or_pattern.spi's arm body (3 copies -> 1 on C and Rust, exit 41); frontier_format_any_union and frontier_join_format_any compile; then a lib Cells sweep and -Suite all with DISAGREE 0, where only residuals with or-patterns change.")
inl note_of_rust_match_duplication_9 () = Note ("RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION", 9u32, "2026-10-22T13:38Z", "main", "2026-10-22 09:38 main: design notes for the clause-chain half (after v2 lands in PACK-NEXT-2), read from the tree core, no build. The clause chain is lowered at 53141/53144: List.mapFoldBack loop clauses (EPatternMiss (EV id)), where each clause's on_fail is EPatternMemo on_fail (the remaining clauses). The memo shares the E node in the prepass, but peval evaluates it once per failure path, which produces the nested-match staircase. A v3 would wrap that on_fail as v2 does for or-patterns: EJoinPoint (p r, on_fail, None, None, None) when on_fail is non-trivial (not EPatternMiss/ELit/EV), skipped when any remaining clause is type-directed (the same typeDirected walk). Better than the heuristic would be an explicit real-code flag: PartEvalPrepassEnv (52933) has only term and ty, and RawReal(r,a) -> f a (53350) drops the information. A field set by RawReal would let both v2 and v3 skip join points in compile-time code, but whole .spir modules (sm'_real.spir) may not pass through RawReal, so their entry path has to be traced first. The order is fixed: v2 has to prove itself in PACK-NEXT-2 (format_any samples, lib Cells sweep, DISAGREE 0). Then v3 is measured on x/apps/client args (1,243 lines before workarounds) as its own pack, because it touches every multi-clause match.")
inl note_of_rust_match_duplication_10 () = Note ("RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION", 10u32, "2026-10-22T21:09Z", "main", "2026-10-22 17:09 main: or-join v2 is RED in PACK-NEXT-3's review. On lab/RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION/or_pattern.spi (Circle x | Square x | Triangle x => x * x + 7 * x - 3) the candidate (core 95AF71D2FE8AE0E6) fails with FatalError: Index was outside the bounds of the array, exit 5, on C. The installed compiler compiles it and the program exits 41. v2 had only ever been anchor-checked, so this review is its first execution. Probable cause from the patch: the shared continuation is wrapped in EJoinPoint(p r, on_succ, None, None, None) inside the pattern compiler, but that body reads x, which each alternative binds on its own. A join point's captured environment is fixed where it is created, so the alternatives' different bindings index past it. Next design: the join point must take the alternatives' bound variables as parameters (bind once, call with each alternative's values), or wrap only bodies that use no pattern-bound variable. Gate for v3: or_pattern.spi exits 41 with one arm-body copy, plus the matrix.")
inl note_of_rust_match_duplication_11 () = Note ("RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION", 11u32, "2026-10-22T21:20Z", "main", "2026-10-22 17:20 main: correction to note 10's cause, from a code read (no build). Pattern variables come from one per-clause dictionary (cp's v x, core 53083: PatVar -> ELet(p r, v a, EV id, on_succ)), so x gets the SAME index in every alternative; the shared body does not see different bindings. A better fit for \"Index was outside the bounds of the array\": the lowering pass handles EJoinPoint(r,body,...) through scope env x (52735), and scope looks up per-node free-variable data computed by an earlier analysis (52466). v2 creates its EJoinPoint inside the pattern compiler (53130). If that analysis already ran, the new node has no scope entry, its var table is too small, and EV i -> env.term.var.[i] (52738) indexes past it. v3 options: (1) create the join point where the analysis sees it (wrap the or-pattern body before the free-variable pass, or run scope on the new node at creation); (2) reuse a construct the lowering already scopes, such as a let-bound closure called from each alternative. Next step, after the compile holds: a Debug candidate with v2 under SPIRAL_DIAG_QUIET=0 to get the stack, confirming 52738 before writing v3.")
inl note_of_rust_match_duplication_12 () = Note ("RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION", 12u32, "2026-10-22T21:44Z", "main", "2026-10-22 17:44 main: coverage gap. A census of or-pattern arms (| A | B => with an inner | and no ||; scratch or_pattern_census.py) finds 0 of 1,240 sample .spi files with an or-pattern arm, and 10 of 212 lib/spiral files, 2 of them binding variables. So the -Suite all matrix could not have caught v2's crash; only the dedicated probe did. Before v3: add a regression sample with a variable-binding or-pattern (or_pattern.spi's Circle x | Square x | Triangle x, exit 41), one sample per fixed bug, and gate v3 on lib Cells gates of the 2 binding files, since those are its real users.")
inl note_of_rust_match_duplication_13 () = Note ("RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION", 13u32, "2026-10-22T21:52Z", "main", "2026-10-22 17:52 main: v3's lib gate target, made concrete: the only variable-binding or-pattern in lib/spiral is state_core.spi:594, | Some value, _ | _, Some (Ok (Some value)) =>, which binds value in both alternatives (exactly v2's crash shape). stream.spi:249/257 are wildcard-only (StreamNil, _ | _, StreamNil), the non-binding case. v3 gates: or_pattern.spi exits 41 with one arm-body copy, lib_gate state_core.spi and stream.spi green, and the new regression sample.")
inl note_of_rust_match_duplication_14 () = Note ("RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION", 14u32, "2026-10-22T21:57Z", "main", "2026-10-22 17:57 main: regression sample added before next3b's matrix started: samples/or_pattern_bound_alternatives (or_pattern.spi: Circle x | Square x | Triangle x => x * x + 7 * x - 3, exits 41 from Square 4), the matrix's first variable-binding or-pattern. On next3b (no or-join) it is a plain new row expected to agree with exit 41; for v3 it is the guard that would have caught v2.")
inl note_of_rust_match_duplication_15 () = Note ("RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION", 15u32, "2026-10-22T22:08Z", "main", "2026-10-22 18:08 main: v3 option (2) ruled out by a code read. \"Wrap only bodies that use no pattern-bound variable\" cannot be decided where v2 inserts the join point: at cp time the arm body is still an EPatternRef that the clause loop fills only after every pattern is compiled (core 53141-53152: \"I need to deal with the patterns first before replacing the strings in the body\"). Any v3 must therefore create the shared join point after the refs are filled (a post-pass over the lowered clause), or teach propagate/lower to scope a join point created inside cp. Both start from the Debug stack trace (next-session step 4).")
inl note_of_rust_match_duplication_16 () = Note ("RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION", 16u32, "2026-10-23T13:45Z", "main", "2026-10-23 09:45 main: v3 design from a code read (no build), replacing both v2 placements. Facts: in compile_pattern (core ~53054-53153) the arm body is an EPatternRef that is filled only after every clause is compiled. A join point created inside cp has no scope entry from the earlier free-variable analysis, so v2's crash came from creating EJoinPoint there. Every pass downstream, type inference included, already handles join points that come from the RAW tree: let functions become RawJoinPoint through join_point and adjust_join_point (core ~44497-44527), and 'let x = e in b' is RawMatch(r, e, [PatVar x, b]), which pattern_match turns into ELet. v3 is therefore a raw rewrite before type inference. A clause | P1 | P2 => body whose top-level pattern is a PatOr, whose alternatives bind the same variable set V and are not type-directed (v2's typeDirected walk: PatAnnot, PatExists, PatNominal, PatDefaultValue, PatFilledDefaultValue), whose body is not trivial (a literal, a variable or a symbol), and which sits outside real code becomes: let k = fun V => join body in match s with | P1 => k V | P2 => k V | other clauses. k is a fresh uninterned name like ' arg' in adjust_join_point, and V is a tuple when there are several variables. Type inference sees an ordinary let-bound join point, so scoping, free variables and codegen need no change, and each backend emits one body plus N calls. The rewrite belongs where the parser builds RawMatch (or one raw-tree pass after parsing that also gets the RawReal flag). Gates as before: samples/or_pattern_bound_alternatives exits 41 with one body copy, frontier_format_any_union and frontier_join_format_any compile, lib_gate state_core.spi and stream.spi, then -Suite all with DISAGREE 0. The clause-chain (wildcard staircase) half is the same rewrite applied to the remaining-clauses continuation, as a later pack.")
inl note_of_rust_match_duplication_17 () = Note ("RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION", 17u32, "2026-10-23T13:49Z", "main", "2026-10-23 09:49 main: v3 implemented as %TEMP%\\pack-next7\\or-pattern-join-v3.ps1 and composed alone into pack-next8 (core 19B0B1D5F8629846 on next7 9CC2E91F). Two anchors: module OrPatternJoin before inl_or_let_process, and the 'match ... with' parser calling OrPatternJoin.lift instead of RawMatch. It parses under both defines with pair-drift clean. Shape: each clause whose top-level pattern is a PatOr, not type-directed, with a non-trivial body becomes 'let <uninterned ' or N'> = fun V => join body' around the match, and the clause body becomes the call. V is the first alternative's bound variables, which equal the second's (DisjointOrPatternVar enforces it), with unit for none and a pair chain for several. It is deliberately not the let-join form: let would PatDyn the arguments and stop static folding, while a plain fun whose body is a RawJoinPoint keeps static arguments specializing, and a dynamic scrutinee gives one body. The uninterned name can't shadow a user variable. Known risk for the gate: the rewrite runs at parse time without a real-code flag, so an unannotated or-pattern inside real code (.spir) would get a join point. The format_any samples and a lib Cells sweep (sm'_real.spir) are the check, and if they fail, the next step is the RawReal flag of note 9. Review rule (pack-next8, after next7): only outputs of samples containing an or-pattern arm may change; or_pattern_bound_alternatives exits 41 with one arm-body copy on C and Rust; frontier_format_any_union and frontier_join_format_any compile; lib_gate state_core.spi and stream.spi pass; DISAGREE 0.")
inl note_of_rust_match_duplication_18 () = Note ("RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION", 18u32, "2026-10-23T14:16Z", "main", "2026-10-23 10:16 main: pack-next8 now also carries the inert hopacsota census (HOPACSOTA-P3), so its core is BF7EE0AD76C18036. The census is off without its environment variable, so the review rule for or-pattern v3 is unchanged.")
inl note_of_rust_match_duplication_19 () = Note ("RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION", 19u32, "2026-10-24T06:32Z", "main", "2026-10-24 02:3x main: or-pattern join v3 reaches its first full gate. It came into pack 5g through the next8 base (5g = next9 stack + my fixes, core 1A297706E577BB03), so review-4 is its review. Its rule's parts the matrix cannot check are wired into review-4: %TEMP%\\pack-next5g\\orjoin-check.ps1 runs with the reviewed candidate DLL (SPIRAL_COMPILER_DLL, which try.ps1 and Cells' toolchain.ex both honor), compiling lab or_pattern.spi to C and Rust and counting arm-body copies (rule: exit 41, 1 copy), then lib_gate state_core.spi and stream.spi in process mode (lib's only or-pattern users). The matrix covers or_pattern_bound_alternatives and frontier_format_any_union / frontier_join_format_any. review-4 is in the requeue manifest for tibia's block end.")
inl note_of_rust_match_duplication_20 () = Note ("RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION", 20u32, "2026-10-24T11:49Z", "main", "2026-10-19 laps folded from the deleted history ledger (docs/agents/history/2026-10-19-backlog-laps, HISTORY-BACKLOG-LAPS-DRIFT): lap 5 11:48 (subagent): or-pattern join-point patch at pattern compile (lowering cannot add join points: scope table) + run.ps1 queued -> next: read the candidate run (3 copies -> 1) | lap 6 14:27 (subagent): bisect: or_join_patch is pack 8's regression (format_any samples; real-code type-annotated or-patterns); dropped from the pack -> next: redesign: skip annotated alternatives or thread a real flag; prove on format_any + lib sweep | lap 7 19:54 (subagent): re-read: or_join_patch dropped (format_any regression) -> next: redesign without type-annotated or-patterns")
inl note_of_rust_match_duplication_21 () = Note ("RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION", 21u32, "2026-10-24T11:49Z", "main", "2026-10-19 helper patch record folded from the deleted history folder: patch lab/RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION/or_join_patch.py, state 'candidate queued (run.ps1)', proof: or_pattern body 3 copies -> 1")
inl note_of_rust_match_duplication_22 () = Note ("RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION", 22u32, "2026-10-27T16:43Z", "main", "10-27 12:43 main: lab/RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION drained to its one live probe (untracked, so no git change). Archived by scratch-archive.ps1 (kit archive 20261027-124241, blob verify 0 failures; restore with -Restore that manifest.tsv) and removed: or_join_patch.py (v1, dropped from pack 8 on 10-19 for the format_any regression), or_join_patch_v2.py and run.ps1 (v1's candidate runner). The v3 design superseded both patches and landed as OrPatternJoin in the core through pack 5g: spiral_compiler.fs:44537 typeDirected, 44546 trivial, 44551 lifted, which lift a non-trivial, non-type-directed or-pattern body into a function of its bound variables. The patches' anchor line no longer exists in the core, so they can't apply anyway. Also removed: or_pattern.spi, byte-identical (271 B, over the census's 200-byte floor) to the landed regression sample samples/or_pattern_bound_alternatives/main.spi. By-stem search (RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION, or_join_patch, lab\\or_pattern) over 971 scripts in the spiral, compiler-tmp, packs and i574n.github script folders and TEMP to depth 1: the only references are spent pack scripts (pack-next2/3 compose.ps1, next3/next3b review.ps1, next5g orjoin-check.ps1, all superseded or installed; current packs are 5n and 5o) and two old backlog exports. The positive control hit inside the folder. Kept: wildcard_pair.spi, the item's open case (a wildcard arm after two pair arms on a 3-case union), not yet measured. Spiral lab folder count is unchanged at 49.")
inl note_of_rust_match_duplication_23 () = Note ("RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION", 23u32, "2026-10-27T16:50Z", "main", "10-27 12:50 main: Wildcard fallthrough duplication MEASURED (node job-1793119666554, compiler 4FD712E9, which already carries OrPatternJoin v3). Probe: lab wildcard_pair.spi, a match a, b over a 3-case union with arms Red, Red / Green, Green / _. As authored, its wildcard body is static (7 * 3 + 1) and folds to a literal, so it hides the copies. A dynamic twin (TEMP rmd-wildcard/wildcard_pair_dyn.spi, body k * 3 + 1 with k runtime) shows them: the body is emitted 3 times on BOTH backends. C: 'v2 * 3l' at lines 45, 59, 67 of pair_score. Rust: 'v2.wrapping_mul(3i32)' plus wrapping_add at lines 18/19, 30/31, 37/38. Cause, read from the output's shape: the pair match is lowered as a nested match on a, then on b, and the fallthrough continuation is copied into every failed inner branch (Green then not-Green, Red then not-Red) and into the outer default (Amber). OrPatternJoin does not apply because no clause is a PatOr. Design pointer for the core (compiler lane authors it): the same raw-level lift that OrPatternJoin does, applied to a clause that more than one failed path reaches. When compile_pattern would place a non-trivial, non-type-directed clause body on two or more failure paths, bind it once as 'let <uninterned> = fun V => join body' (V = the clause's bound variables; unit for a bare _), and make each path a call. The same trivial() and typeDirected() guards apply, so static bodies still fold. Regression sample when it lands: the dyn twin, rule 1 copy on C and Rust and the same exit (pair_score Red Green 7 + pair_score Green Green 7 = 22 + 202 = 224, exit 224 on C after the 255 mask, 224 on Rust). The measurement scripts and both outputs are in TEMP rmd-wildcard (node-measure.ps1, out/).")
inl note_of_rust_match_duplication_24 () = Note ("RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION", 24u32, "2026-10-27T17:06Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-27: wildcard/clause-chain half design v1 (build-independent; from reading cp/compile_pattern 53102-53191 + OrPatternJoin 44525-44574). MECHANISM confirmed: cp threads the SAME on_fail into every sub-test fail edge (EPairTest 53144 -> both a- and b- fail to on_fail), each clause's on_fail = the next clause wrapped in EPatternMemo (53188); EPatternMemo is eliminated before peval (158180) so peval re-inlines the fallthrough at every decision-tree leaf reaching it. A fully-discriminating clause lands at 1 leaf (body once); a NON-discriminating clause (catch-all / wildcard in a branched position) lands at >=2 leaves -> body copied (n23's `_` 3x). FIX = the SAME OrPatternJoin lift (raw rewrite, `let k = fun V => join body` + call, reusing boundVars/typeDirected/trivial/parameters), keyed on the clause BODY's multi-reachability instead of a PatOr pattern; peval then copies only the call, static scrutinees still fold. TARGETING (my call, decide-autonomously; objection welcome): rule (a) conservative syntactic -- only multi-scrutinee (tuple/PatPair) matches, non-first clause, non-trivial non-type-directed body, pattern non-discriminating at a branched position (bare catch-all or a wildcard tuple component). Sound (under-targeting only leaves some dup, never changes behavior); avoids the whole-match churn of lifting all bodies (keeps your \"only affected samples change\" review rule). Exact boundary validated on the build gate. GATE: dyn twin (TEMP rmd-wildcard, exit 224, 1 copy C+Rust) landed as a sample; frontier_format_any_union/_join compile; lib_gate state_core.spi + stream.spi (:249/257 wildcard-pair users); -Suite all DISAGREE 0, only wildcard-fallthrough residuals change. Same real-code risk as or-join (parse-time, no RawReal flag; typeDirected + format_any/lib sweeps are the check). Lands by extending OrPatternJoin (lifted gains a clause-body branch) at the same parser site; re-anchor only if that section moves (5n/5o don't). Build-bound; sequenced AFTER COMPILER-CROSS-MACHINE-REPRO. Full design: compiler scratchpad note-rust-match-dup-wildcard-design.txt.")
inl note_of_rust_match_duplication_25 () = Note ("RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION", 25u32, "2026-10-29T12:21Z", "main", "10-29 08:3x main: wipe recovery. wildcard_pair_dyn.spi (the dynamic sample showing the arm body copied 3 times on C and Rust) and node-measure.ps1 existed only in the node's %TEMP%\\rmd-wildcard. Both were rescued and copied into lab/RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION, untracked and hash-equal to the rescue, next to the static wildcard_pair.spi. Now n24's gate has its regression sample again. The generated rmd_wildcard_*.c/.rs were not copied, since the sample regenerates them.")
inl note_of_rust_match_duplication_26 () = Note ("RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION", 26u32, "2026-10-31T03:21Z", "main", "10-30 23:21 main: lab/RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION is ARCHIVED (user, 10-30 22:5x: the lab dir didn't work and drifted; drafts don't live outside the repo). Archive 20261030-232045 under %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\archive. Restore this folder with: pwsh i574n.github/scripts/scratch-archive.ps1 -Root C:\\home\\git\\spiral\\lab -Restore '%LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\archive\\20261030-232045\\manifest.tsv' -Only 'RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION\\*' -RestoreTo C:\\home\\git\\spiral\\lab. Resume this item's work in its real place in the repo, not in lab.")
inl note_of_rust_match_duplication_27 () = Note ("RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION", 27u32, "2026-10-31T20:02Z", "compiler", "10-31 10-31 16:01 compiler GROOM (user direction via /btw, 10-31 15:4x): the self-hosted rewrite (SELF-HOSTED-SPIRAL) starts only after EVERY backend is SOTA: no memory problems, no Rc slowdowns, no union hacks, no recursion limits, no missing arithmetic; GADT bugs and conversion gaps are 'unacceptable by now'; edition 3 finishes first; Gleam is a top test target (abyssal codebases) with F#, Rust and Wasm; Cells as fast as F# projects. This item is now on that gate (priority -> P0).")
inl note_of_rust_match_duplication_28 () = Note ("RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION", 28u32, "2026-11-01T04:34Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 00:34 compiler, round-2 groom (user 00:2x: backend codegen is CPU-light text work, write it while runs batch): NEXT CPU-LIGHT EDIT: draft the EPatternMemo shared-continuation as one join point (one body per arm, or-patterns jump to it) in core peval as an applier; measure with the duplicated-body census (arm bodies per match in samples/*/main.rs, build-free) before and after the batched build.")
inl note_of_rust_match_duplication_29 () = Note ("RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION", 29u32, "2026-11-01T04:51Z", "main", "main 00:5x LAB CODE IS SAFE (user 11-01 00:2x: 'check for all lab references in history... make sure we didn't lose that code... to repurpose them or groom their tasks'). This item's lab/RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION folder (3 files: node-measure.ps1, wildcard_pair.spi, wildcard_pair_dyn.spi) is archived in %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\archive\\20261030-232045 (blob-verified). Restore it into its proper repo place with: pwsh C:\\home\\git\\i574n.github\\scripts\\scratch-archive.ps1 -Restore '%LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\archive\\20261030-232045\\manifest.tsv' -RestoreTo <repo dir> -Only 'RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION/*'. Next step for the owner at its next pull: restore the useful files in place as incomplete drafts (Cells heals them in place), or record why it starts over.")
inl note_of_rust_match_duplication_30 () = Note ("RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION", 30u32, "2026-11-03T07:31Z", "compiler", "11-03 07:3x compiler GROOM (WIP cap: Active = touched in 12 h). Parked to Planned (51 h; last note main). Owner compiler. Next: restore the archived lab files (kit archive 20261030-232045) into tmp/samples and re-measure on pack23.")
inl note_of_rust_match_duplication_31 () = Note ("RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION", 31u32, "2026-11-05T08:38Z", "compiler", "11-05 04:3x compiler: RE-MEASURED on installed pack43 (the item's recorded next step). Restored the archived probes (kit archive 20261030-232045, -Only 'RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION\\*'; the manifest uses backslashes) to compiler scratch. wildcard_pair_dyn (match a, b | Red, Red => 101 | Green, Green => 202 | _ => k * 3 + 1), compiled to Rust: the wildcard body 'v2.wrapping_mul(3i32); wrapping_add(1i32)' is emitted THREE times (in the Green/_ and Red/_ inner fallbacks and the outer _ arm), i.e. once per path into the shared continuation, as the item says (55 Rust lines, 81 C). So the premise holds on today's core. Next step unchanged: share the continuation (EPatternMemo once per match, as a join point), then measure x's args/view units again.")
inl note_of_rust_match_duplication_32 () = Note ("RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION", 32u32, "2026-11-05T08:41Z", "compiler", "11-05 04:4x compiler: MECHANISM located for the fix. The prepass shares continuations by wrapping them in EPatternMemo (spiral_compiler.fs:7919 PatOr on_succ, :7920 PatAnd on_fail, :7930 each case's on_fail = the remaining cases) and memoizes the PE (Utils.memoize, :7249/:7388/:7599), so the TERM is shared. But peval evaluates the shared node once per path that reaches it, so the residual repeats it (the 3x wildcard body above). FIX SKETCH: in the prepass, when a memoized continuation is reachable from 2+ paths AND is larger than a small threshold, emit it as a local join point (a 'join' unit-arg function over its free vars, which peval already turns into one JPMethod per specialization) and call it per path. Or-pattern on_succ binds the same pattern vars from each alternative, so those vars become the join point's arguments. Below the threshold (a literal, a single op) keep today's duplication, which is cheaper than a call. Cost: every backend's residuals change, so the review needs -Suite all -Native with 0 behavior cells plus a size measure (generated lines per backend and x's args/view units). Not started tonight: it needs a threshold study first (count shared-continuation sizes over samples + x units). Sized D4 / 480 m as filed.")
inl note_of_rust_match_duplication_33 () = Note ("RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION", 33u32, "2026-11-05T08:43Z", "compiler", "11-05 04:4x compiler: TEXT-LEVEL UPPER BOUND for the threshold study (no build needed). Over the 502 sample main.rs on the box (41,897 lines), per function and with vN normalized to v#: 16,179 lines (38.6%) lie inside 3-line windows that occur more than once. CAVEAT: this counts ALL repetition, not only match continuations. The top files are runtime_native (4,588/4,638), a deliberate long let chain (typecheck_long_let_body 1,600/1,612), runtime_bench, the DFA megas, which repeat for their own reasons. Repeated-block length histogram (lines: blocks): 3:358 4:208 5:314 6:61 7:66 8-13: ~95 14-15: 94 16-25: ~105 26-39: 12 40+: 34. Reading for the threshold: most repeats are 3-5 lines, i.e. roughly the size of a call plus its argument binds. So a join point should only replace continuations of about 8+ residual lines, and a compiler-level count (the prepass knows which memoized nodes peval reaches twice) is still needed to separate match duplication from ordinary repetition.")
