build.ps1's check.cells + previous-vs-new comparison. So the slice's live format is Fs (module/namespace header + indent_block), Spi second, Md unused -> next: oracle: render those 5 consumers' livemds to .cells and record the release CLI's fs export as expected bytes, then draft notebook_export.spi as a line scanner | lap 13-drive 14:57 (subagent2): export oracle recorded (bounded job, 6.6 s): lab export-oracle.ps1 renders the 10 --fs-path notebooks (dice, hangul, polyglot parser x2 + lib/fsharp x6) to .cells and the release CLI's fs export of every one is byte-identical to the committed .fs (expected bytes under spiral-bin/runs/cli-export-oracle/20261019-145729, plus spi exports); notebook_export.spi drafted (line scanner over \\\\n#! blocks, Fs module header + indent_block, lib trim set, text builder, no parser combinators) -> next: compile notebook_export.spi + c_line_probe in the next bounded slot (>= 15:07), then diff against the oracle | lap 15-drive 15:19 (subagent2): FOURTH VERB WORKS: lab notebook_export.spi (no parser combinators, no trace, std-only) compiles (2nd try; 1st failed on two 4-arg join5 calls) and its fs and spi exports of all 10 oracle notebooks are BYTE-IDENTICAL to the release CLI's (20 of 20); but the generated crate is 1147 lines, over the 1k gate (format_block 121, arrange 85, indent_line 70, matches_output 47: union equality expands); union = replaced by an output_code compare -> next: next bounded slot (>= 15:28): recompile, line count, re-check 20/20 | lap 15-drive 15:29 (subagent2): notebook_export under the gate: 1147 -> 971 generated lines (union equality -> output_code compare, 28 single-use helpers let -> inl, rendered folded into block, one suffix match), still 20 of 20 byte-identical with the release CLI (fs + spi of the 10 oracle notebooks); promote.ps1 now routes export and dib-export to notebook_export::spiral_export_run (5 verbs, 4 crates + cli_common); dry run ok -> next: main's go: promote.ps1 -Apply + build.ps1, whose previous-vs-new export comparison then checks the crate against the monolith over every lib/apps livemd | lap 15-drive 15:32 (subagent2): gleam slice scoped and drafted: Cells runs Gleam/Erlang cells itself (builders.ex dispatch {:gleam,...}) and sends only --target javascript to the CLI, while build.ps1's run check uses the CLI's Erlang path (spiral-check-gleam 42, exact); lab gleam_runner.spi covers both: gleam.toml (6 default deps + --deps), owned src vs gleam_package, gleam build/run with the 3 empty GLEAM env vars, the Erlang output-line filter, JS: main.mjs app-id patch, bun x esbuild, index.html, trunk with TRUNK_TOOLS_WASM_BINDGEN=0.2.93; std-only, DefaultHasher id instead of sha2 (cache name only) -> next: gleam-check.ps1 in the next bounded slot (Erlang path exact output) | lap 15-drive 15:39 (subagent2): gleam_runner first compile: 2 unbound helpers because a parameter named with (a Spiral keyword) silently drops the definition (ETY-UnboundVariable at the call sites); renamed to replacement (+ package -> project to be safe); rust slice read for later: package dir = <workspace root>/target/spiral/<spiral|spiral_contract>/packages/Rust/<hash>, root from SPIRAL_REPO_ROOT or the nearest spiral/workspace parent (file_system.get_workspace_root, external = cwd outside it), cargo fmt with missing-member Cargo.toml repair (regex, 3 retries), cargo run env TRACE_LEVEL=Verbose RUSTC_WRAPPER=sccache RUST_BACKTRACE=full RUSTFLAGS=-C prefer-dynamic, output = lines after the cargo profile line skipping 2, contract path still on cargo +nightly-2024-07-14 + pwsh invoke-block -> next: gleam-check at >= 15:48:36 | lap 15-drive 15:50 (subagent2): gleam_runner compiles to 544 lines (under the gate) and writes the expected gleam.toml (name main, target erlang, 6 default deps); the run itself failed in the job env because gleam's escript booted choco OTP 26 (load_failed, the known PATH trap), not a crate bug; gleam-check.ps1 now puts scoop OTP first and also runs the release CLI on the same source to compare output and gleam.toml bytes -> next: gleam-check at >= 15:58:45 | lap 16-drive 16:11 (subagent2): rust_runner now covers all three runtimes (Native, --wasm[=cmd], --contract[=cmd]): spiral_contract workspace (edition2024 feature + resolver 3), [lib] cdylib, near-sdk/indexmap defaults, near_bindgen MainState appended, cargo +nightly-2024-07-14 wasm build, then pwsh invoke-block -Linux with SPIRAL_WASM_COMMAND (untested: WSL path, tested last per rule); one merged-pipe runner (run_merged) for cargo and pwsh; promote.ps1 routes all 7 verbs (gleam, rust added, pending their checks); heavy gate admits one slot under low memory, so checks queue with a 15-min wait budget (work itself < 2 min) -> next: gleam-check (queued 16:10), then rust-check | lap 16-drive 16:15 (subagent2): FIFTH CRATE WORKS: gleam_runner (564 lines) runs build.ps1's gleam case exactly (spiral-check-gleam 42, exact after trim) and writes a gleam.toml byte-identical to the release CLI's on the same source; only difference: the crate kept stdout's final newline (release: none), now stripped before the line filter; the job waited 253 s for a slot (low memory admits one), 28 s of work -> next: rust-check (queued next), then recheck gleam output byte-equality | lap 16-drive 16:05 (subagent2): rust slice drafted (native path; --wasm/--contract stay on the monolith until rust_wasm_runner): lab rust_runner.spi = workspace root (SPIRAL_REPO_ROOT or nearest spiral/workspace ancestor, deps-skip), package + workspace Cargo.toml text byte-matched to the monolith's, cargo fmt with missing-member manifest repair, cargo run on a merged stdout+stderr pipe (std::io::pipe) with the sccache/prefer-dynamic env, output after the profile line +2, cleanup default on; cli_common gains write_if_changed (write_all_text_exists parity) and clap-like multi-value --deps; rust-check.ps1 (compile, harness, spiral-check-rust 42, protocol rust case) ready -> next: heavy slots busy (main's pack9-accept + lane 1): run rust-check and gleam-check when a slot frees | lap 17 16:42 (subagent2): notebook_export.spi made cell-compatible (///- --package . line, static prelude, 2 rust test blocks whose expected strings were taken from the release CLI's own export of the same .cells text: the build check's check.cells -> spi, and a '# DiceFSharp (Dice)' heading -> namespace/module header + indented F#) -> next: rust-check queued (gate saturated, 45-min wait budget); same test-block treatment for the other crates | lap 17-drive 16:43 (subagent2): SIXTH CRATE WORKS: rust_runner (837 lines, native path) passes build.ps1's rust run check (spiral-check-rust 42 from cargo run in the spiral root, merged pipe, output after the profile line) and test_json_protocol.py's rust case (build failure = exit 1 with spiral.process_rust / error + the cargo diagnostic, no envelope); the release-CLI comparison printed nothing (exit not captured: logging added) and the crate kept cargo's final newline (now stripped, as in gleam); cli_common got its own cell header + 2 test blocks (json_text escaping, sorted envelope) -> next: rerun rust-check for byte parity; then all 7 verbs are crate-backed and the router (lab router.spi) can replace clap | lap 17-drive 16:56 (subagent2): rust-check rerun (19 s of work after a 234 s gate wait): crate output now exactly spiral-check-rust 42; protocol rust case OK; finding: the release CLI prints the rust result as a FLAT object (code/code_path/extension/output, no command_result wrapper) while cuda is wrapped; Cells' targets.ex shown_output and build.ps1 both accept either shape, so the crates' always-wrapped envelope is compatible; check scripts now parse both -> next: parity-check (gleam + rust output byte equality vs release CLI) at the next slot | lap 17-drive 17:12 (subagent2): PARITY: gleam_runner and rust_runner outputs are now byte-equal to the release CLI's on build.ps1's run-check sources (spiral-check-gleam 42, spiral-check-rust 42; gleam.toml bytes equal), rust protocol case OK, both compiled WITH their new cell headers + test blocks (564 / 837 lines); the job waited 312 s for a slot -> next: next slot: compile-crates.ps1 (cuda, cpp, lua with cell headers), then export-check (notebook_export with its tests) | lap 17b 17:17 (subagent2): safety fixes before promote: the router line sends rust to the crate only without --wasm/--contract (native_only guard; contract/wasm stays on the monolith since that path never ran); router_swap.py --apply refuses without --contract-path-proven; re-proof scripts written for the crates whose files changed after their last proof (reprove-cuda-cpp.ps1, reprove-lua.ps1, export-check.ps1) -> next: re-proof jobs, then proven.tsv hashes + promote.ps1 hash check | lap 18-drive 17:14 (subagent2): shrink step staged: router.spi made self-contained (apps/spiral's package.spiproj points packageDir at lib, so it cannot reach cli_common); lab router_swap.py writes a one-cell spiral.livemd (smart-cell JSON + echo block in the existing format), package.spiproj = |core- + spiral, and drops 10 Cargo deps (chrono regex sha2 serde_json clap futures encoding_rs encoding_rs_io async-walkdir mlua; mimalloc stays for build.ps1's global allocator line); it refuses until promote.ps1's 6 crate path deps exist (dry run refused correctly) -> next: next slot: compile-crates.ps1 for cuda,cpp,lua,router | lap 18-drive 17:22 (subagent2): RE-PROVEN from current lab files (bounded job, 30 s): cuda_runner 476 lines, test_json_protocol.py's 3 cuda cases OK; cpp_runner 161 lines, all 4 harness cases (ok output, missing flag exit 1, compile error = build failed, exit-3 program = child failed) -> next: reprove-lua, export-check, then proven.tsv | lap 18-drive 17:53 (subagent2): lua RE-PROVEN from current files (125 lines, cargo crate on mlua 0.11.4 rebuilt, test_json_protocol -k lua 2/2) -> next: export-check at >= 18:03:34, then proven.tsv | lap 18-drive 18:04 (subagent2): ALL 6 CRATES RE-PROVEN from the current lab files (export 20/20 + c_line again, 971 lines); lab proven.tsv records each file's sha256 + the proving job, and promote.ps1 now refuses if any crate file changed since (dry run passes) -> next: main's go for the promote row (rust native only); contract path proof before router_swap | lap 19 18:50 (subagent2): promote.ps1 dry run still passes after main's apps/spiral core-v1 migration (proven.tsv hashes unchanged, livemd main anchor matches) -> next: main's go for the promote row (rust native only) | lap 22-drive 19:45 (subagent2): cli_common.spi's 2 rust test blocks PASSED in a Cells gate (job-1792451601559, 33 s work after a 31-min wait) -> next: main's go for promote; other crate blocks after it | lap 22-drive 20:02 (subagent2): test-blocks-run.ps1 takes -Modules a,b,... (cli_common copied in as a module, the layout the crate proofs compiled with) so the other crates' rust test blocks can run before the promote -> next: bounded job: cuda_runner,gleam_runner,rust_runner,notebook_export after the warm probe | lap 23-drive 20:24 (subagent2): cuda_runner + notebook_export blocks passed (3/3), but the gate failed on each file's Export main built as a cell on Zig: crates need ///> _ above main for Cells; scratch copies get it, job 3 reruns 4 crates -> next: job 3; add the skip line at promotion + re-prove | lap 23-drive 20:27 (subagent2): all crate test blocks pass (5/5 in job 3 + 2/2 earlier = 7/7, gate exit 0 with ///> _ above Export main); promote.ps1 inserts that line at promotion; set 70 -> next: main's go for the promote row")
inl note_of_spiral_cli_granular_39 () = Note ("SPIRAL-CLI-GRANULAR", 39u32, "2026-10-27T08:00Z", "main", "10-27 04:00 main: 10-27 04:00 main groom: fresh measurement (the last real note was the 10-19 lap fold; on the stale list in EOIE-AGILE-FIELD-COMPLETENESS n5). The split has NOT landed: apps/spiral is still ONE crate (one Cargo.toml) with spiral.spi at 1,788 lines generating spiral.rs at 22,292 lines, 22x over the 1,000-line generated-crate gate. That's down from the title's 1,918 / 24,280, from incidental lib heals, not from the split. The 70% reflects drafted work in lab/SPIRAL-CLI-GRANULAR (files: cli_common.spi, compile-crates.ps1, cpp_runner.spi, cuda_runner.spi, export-check.ps1, export-oracle.ps1, gleam_runner.spi, gleam-check.ps1, lua_runner.spi, notebook_export.spi, parity-check.ps1, promote.ps1, proven.tsv, reprove-cuda-cpp.ps1, reprove-lua.ps1, router_swap.py, router.spi, rust_runner.spi, rust-check.ps1, test-blocks-run.ps1), not tree state. Next concrete step: run its promote path on a copy, regen, and cargo-check every new crate under 1,000 lines. Until a crate split lands in apps/spiral, the item's tree progress is ~0 of the gate.")
inl note_of_spiral_cli_granular_40 () = Note ("SPIRAL-CLI-GRANULAR", 40u32, "2026-10-30T14:14Z", "main", "10-30 10:14 main, BACKLOG-DANGLING-1030 R3: Active -> Paused, progress kept at 70%. No note since 10-27 and no live job, so it isn't real WIP. Nothing blocks it; the concrete next step is in the previous note (owner main). Resume by picking it, never by re-verifying.")
inl note_of_spiral_cli_granular_41 () = Note ("SPIRAL-CLI-GRANULAR", 41u32, "2026-10-31T03:21Z", "main", "10-30 23:21 main: lab/SPIRAL-CLI-GRANULAR is ARCHIVED (user, 10-30 22:5x: the lab dir didn't work and drifted; drafts don't live outside the repo). Archive 20261030-232045 under %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\archive. Restore this folder with: pwsh i574n.github/scripts/scratch-archive.ps1 -Root C:\\home\\git\\spiral\\lab -Restore '%LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\archive\\20261030-232045\\manifest.tsv' -Only 'SPIRAL-CLI-GRANULAR\\*' -RestoreTo C:\\home\\git\\spiral\\lab. Resume this item's work in its real place in the repo, not in lab.")
inl note_of_spiral_cli_granular_42 () = Note ("SPIRAL-CLI-GRANULAR", 42u32, "2026-10-31T20:57Z", "main", "10-31 16:53 main, pause re-decision (KIT-PAUSE-RULE-REWORK; rule R3 is now 'never auto-pause for silence'): Paused -> PLANNED. It was paused by the old silence/WIP rule, not by a blocker: its notes name no external condition (a 10-30 lab-archive note blocks nothing: restore the draft from the archive into the repo when picked). Back in agile next at its priority; deps, if any, gate it as usual.")
inl note_of_spiral_cli_granular_43 () = Note ("SPIRAL-CLI-GRANULAR", 43u32, "2026-11-01T04:51Z", "main", "main 00:5x LAB CODE IS SAFE (user 11-01 00:2x: 'check for all lab references in history... make sure we didn't lose that code... to repurpose them or groom their tasks'). This item's lab/SPIRAL-CLI-GRANULAR folder (24 files: cli_common.spi, compile-crates.ps1, cpp_runner.spi, cuda_runner.spi, export-check.ps1, export-oracle.ps1) is archived in %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\archive\\20261030-232045 (blob-verified). Restore it into its proper repo place with: pwsh C:\\home\\git\\i574n.github\\scripts\\scratch-archive.ps1 -Restore '%LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\archive\\20261030-232045\\manifest.tsv' -RestoreTo <repo dir> -Only 'SPIRAL-CLI-GRANULAR/*'. Next step for the owner at its next pull: restore the useful files in place as incomplete drafts (Cells heals them in place), or record why it starts over.")
inl kino_hot_paths () = Task ("KINO-HOT-PATHS", "Implement Kino's performance-sensitive parts in Spiral (PWSH-FREE, KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR): measure first. Today's numbers: per-cell time is dominated by external tools (gleam build ~2-9 s, dotnet fsi ~5 s floor, rustc); Kino's own overhead shows in warm runs (38 tests, 38 memo hits, 7-9 s = ~200 ms per test: memo key hashing (sha256 of compiler binaries, 20-40 ms per context), the compiler-daemon protocol, notebook parse/mounts). Profile a warm run, move the measured hot paths (memo keys, document/notebook parsing beyond the existing Spiral domain, runner/targets dispatch) into Spiral, and keep Elixir only as glue until KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR removes it", Story, P1, D6, 50u32, Planned, "", "pwsh-free,kino,perf,main,team:main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_kino_hot_paths () = Planning ("KINO-HOT-PATHS", Minutes 180u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_kino_hot_paths_1 () = Note ("KINO-HOT-PATHS", 1u32, "2026-10-19T12:30Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 08:30 lap 1 grain: first measurement: the per-context memo key hashes the compiler folder: 23 DLLs, 25.8 MB, sha256 of all of them takes 463 ms cold on this laptop (the item's 20-40 ms estimate assumed a warm cache); a key from file size + mtime + a one-time hash cached per path would make it near zero Next: check whether Cells hashes the DLLs once per run or once per context (grep memo.ex for the dll hash)")
inl note_of_kino_hot_paths_2 () = Note ("KINO-HOT-PATHS", 2u32, "2026-10-19T12:58Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 08:58 main checked a suspected memo soundness bug and ruled it out: Path.wildcard on a backslashed base matches nothing, but memo.ex reaches the folder through Path.dirname, which returns forward slashes on Windows, so both SpiralCompiler.dll and SpiralCompilerCore.dll are hashed (probe: dirname -> C:/..., wildcard finds 2). memo.ex unchanged; memo_test.exs gained a guard test (a core change re-keys with the backslash spelling run_notebook.ps1 passes), 24/24 pass.")
inl note_of_kino_hot_paths_3 () = Note ("KINO-HOT-PATHS", 3u32, "2026-10-19T12:59Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 08:59 lap 2 grain (corrects lap 1's 463 ms): memo.ex compiler_binaries_print hashes only SpiralCompiler*.dll next to the dll, i.e. 2 files, 11.8 MB (SpiralCompiler.dll 0.28 MB + SpiralCompilerCore.dll 11.5 MB), not the 23 DLLs/25.8 MB of lap 1; sha256 of the two takes 83-135 ms warm (Get-FileHash, 3 runs). Frequency: Memo.context runs once per notebook execute (notebook.ex:523), so a 40-file gate rehashes the same 2 DLLs 40 times (~3-5 s per gate), with no cache, unlike kino_identity (memo.ex:235) which keeps its print in persistent_term. Cheapest fix: persistent_term cache of compiler_binaries_print keyed by {path, size, mtime} of each DLL, the kino_identity pattern; soundness holds because a rebuilt DLL changes mtime and size. Next: write that cache in memo.ex plus a memo_test case (touch the dll, expect a re-key) and queue the mix test run in the HEAVY QUEUE; then profile the rest of a warm run (daemon protocol, notebook parse)")
inl note_of_kino_hot_paths_4 () = Note ("KINO-HOT-PATHS", 4u32, "2026-10-19T13:42Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 09:42 lap 3 grain: the binaries-print cache is prepared as lab/KINO-HOT-PATHS/memo_print_cache.py (dry run clean; patched copies parse with Code.string_to_quoted!). compiler_binaries_print keeps its print in persistent_term under {Memo, :compiler_binaries, dir, [{name, {size, mtime, sha256 of the first 64 KB}}]}, so a notebook execute pays two stats and two 64 KB reads instead of hashing 11.8 MB. Why the head hash and not size+mtime alone (lap 2's plan): the existing test a change to the compiler core re-keys writes core one then core two, same size and the same second, so size+mtime would serve a stale print; the PE header in the first 64 KB carries the deterministic build's content-derived TimeDateStamp, so a real rebuild changes it too, and small fixtures are hashed whole. New ExUnit case: the print is reused while stamps hold and recomputed for a same-size core change. The TODO block at the site now names only the remaining step (memo keys to Spiral after profiling). HEAVY QUEUE: lab/KINO-HOT-PATHS/memo-cache-run.ps1 (applies, runs memo_test.exs in the heavy gate with its own MIX_BUILD_PATH, restores both files unless it passes; ~3 min). Next: after it passes, profile one warm 40-file gate for the next per-notebook cost (daemon protocol vs notebook parse)")
inl note_of_kino_hot_paths_5 () = Note ("KINO-HOT-PATHS", 5u32, "2026-10-19T14:26Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 10:26 lap 4 grain, the next per-notebook cost found by reading memo.ex: Memo.context also runs source_bounds (memo.ex:167-195) on every notebook execute. For a lib notebook it lists the core package (21 files, 53 KB) and the lib package (84 .spi/.spir, 774 KB), reads every file, builds a sha256 key per text from {binaries print, text}, and does one memo fetch per text: a file read plus File.touch on a hit, so ~105 extra file opens and ~105 mtime writes per notebook, and the source-blocks compiler call only for unknown texts. Proxy timing in pwsh (same files, same counts, warm): reading the sources 6-19 ms, 105 memo reads with touches 18-71 ms, i.e. about 25-90 ms per notebook, the same order as the binaries hash being cached now. Cheapest fix, same pattern: keep the bounds of a {binaries print, text sha256} in persistent_term or a named ETS table for the BEAM's lifetime (a gate run reuses the same lib texts across its 40 notebooks), and stop touching memo entries that were read in this BEAM already (prune only needs one touch per run). Next: write that as a second memo patch with an ExUnit case, after the print-cache run lands (same files)")
inl note_of_kino_hot_paths_6 () = Note ("KINO-HOT-PATHS", 6u32, "2026-10-19T14:43Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 10:43 lap 5 grain: second memo patch written and stacked: lab/KINO-HOT-PATHS/memo_bounds_cache.py (3 edits to memo.ex + 1 ExUnit case). source_bounds now asks remembered_bounds first: a named public ETS table (:spiral_cells_memo_bounds, created on first use, recreated if its owner died) answers known texts without touching disk; on a miss it falls back to the memo fetch and caches the hit, and freshly computed bounds are inserted next to the store. So within one BEAM (a 40-file gate) each lib text costs one memo read once instead of once per notebook, and the ~105 File.touch calls per notebook drop to the first notebook. Keys stay the existing bounds_key (binaries print + text), so a compiler change still misses. New case: bounds read once survive a cleared memo dir, and a compiler copy that cannot answer still gets them. Stacks after memo_print_cache.py on a copy of today's memo.ex/memo_test.exs (both parse); memo-cache-run.ps1 applies both, runs memo_test.exs, restores unless it passes")
inl note_of_kino_hot_paths_7 () = Note ("KINO-HOT-PATHS", 7u32, "2026-10-19T15:27Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 11:27 lap 6 grain: both memo patches LANDED in main's 10:56 run (memo-cache-run, memo_test 26 passed, kept): memo.ex now caches the compiler-binaries print in persistent_term by size+mtime+head hash (memo.ex:158-161) and source bounds in the named ETS table (memo.ex:212-227), which addresses the measured 20-40 ms per context of key hashing. Measure-first for what is left: lab/KINO-HOT-PATHS/warm-profile.ps1 + warm-profile.exs time a cold and a warm run of one lib notebook, then run mix profile.eprof over a warm run (top 60 functions into spiral-bin/runs/kino-hot-paths, not the repo); queued after the switch accept. Progress 30: two Elixir hot paths fixed; the move into Spiral comes after the profile shows the remaining split.")
inl note_of_kino_hot_paths_8 () = Note ("KINO-HOT-PATHS", 8u32, "2026-10-19T15:45Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 11:45 lap 7 grain: fixed the profiler choice before the run: mix profile.eprof traces only the process that calls it, and Cells does its per-cell work (compile requests, memo lookups, target runs) in spawned tasks, so eprof would have shown mostly waiting. warm-profile.ps1 now uses mix profile.tprof --type time --report total (Elixir's tprof follows spawned processes by default, set_on_spawn, and totals them; checked in mix help on the scoop Elixir). The compiler daemon is a separate OS process and stays out of the profile; its share shows as time spent waiting in CompilerClient. Queue row updated.")
inl note_of_kino_hot_paths_9 () = Note ("KINO-HOT-PATHS", 9u32, "2026-10-19T16:18Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 12:18 lap 8 grain: warm-profile.ps1 warm run now passes --metrics (NotebookCli switch, writes tests.tsv with per-target Ms and memo column); warm wall minus the sum of Ms is Kino's own overhead, which the tprof top 60 then splits. Checked the switches (path keep, no-html via html boolean, output-dir) and that optionm'.spi still exists post-switch. Next: read both after the queue row runs")
inl note_of_kino_hot_paths_10 () = Note ("KINO-HOT-PATHS", 10u32, "2026-10-19T16:45Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 12:45 lap 9 grain: mapped which notebook parsing is still Elixir: Document.parse_spi and parse_dib already call the Spiral domain (Domain.from_spi, from_spi_bounded with the memoized source bounds, parse_dib, decode_cells), so .spi/.dib parsing is Spiral today; only .livemd goes through Elixir: parse_livemd (document.ex:22, one multiline Regex.split over the whole text plus livemd_part per piece) and to_livemd (render). So the item's notebook parse beyond the existing Spiral domain is exactly parse_livemd/to_livemd. The warm profile row decides whether it is hot (lib notebooks are .spi, so the lib gate does not even reach it; apps/spiral's spiral.livemd export does)")
inl note_of_kino_hot_paths_11 () = Note ("KINO-HOT-PATHS", 11u32, "2026-10-19T17:21Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 13:21 lap 10 grain: memo-key binary hashing re-read: compiler_binaries_print (memo.ex:147-167) already caches the full sha256 in :persistent_term keyed by each DLL's size, mtime and a 64 KB head hash, and dir comes from Path.dirname so the wildcard is slash-safe; so the 20-40 ms per context measured on 10-14 is now paid once per BEAM process (installed SpiralCompiler*.dll = 5737984 bytes hashed in full on the first context). A cross-process cache (same stamp key, print stored in the memo dir) would remove even that; the warm profile's tprof total for content_hash decides whether it is worth it. Next: warm-profile.ps1 run when CPU frees up (queued)")
inl note_of_kino_hot_paths_12 () = Note ("KINO-HOT-PATHS", 12u32, "2026-10-19T17:43Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 13:43 lap 11 grain: sized the one Elixir-side parse (document.ex:22-26, a single multiline Regex.split): Cells sees 4 .livemd files in spiral/apps, 215 KB total (apps/spiral/spiral.livemd 193 KB, the rest under 18 KB), none in lib; one regex pass over 193 KB is a few ms per process, well under the ~200 ms per test overhead, so livemd parsing is dropped as a port candidate (it moves with KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR, not for speed). Remaining candidates in measured order: the compiler-daemon round trip and mounts/memo keys per context; the warm profile decides")
inl note_of_kino_hot_paths_13 () = Note ("KINO-HOT-PATHS", 13u32, "2026-10-20T00:11Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 20:11 first warm profile, measured (bounded job job-1792453609736: lab warm-probe-run.ps1, 10 C-only test cells, reused Cells build): cold 29.8 s, warm 6.5 s with 10 of 10 memo hits; tprof over a third warm run inside the VM puts all of Kino's own CPU at about 0.22 s for the 10 cells (about 22 ms per cell), and about 62% of that is the Gleam-compiled Spiral domain scanning text one character at a time (kino_text_at 30.7 ms, has_char 28.6, spiral_wrap_signed 25.9 over 84k calls, binary.part 25.1, find_char 13.7, find_from 13.0); memo hashing is small (crypto.hash 1.1 ms, hash_update 0.8 ms). So the 200 ms-per-test premise of this item no longer holds: a warm run is dominated by the fixed per-invocation cost (mix and BEAM start, app boot, about 6 s of the 6.5), not per-test work. Two findings: (1) the hottest Kino code is now Spiral itself, i.e. the Gleam backend's per-character binary.part plus an i32 wrap on every index step, so the next hot-path win is codegen (no wrap for loop indices that cannot overflow, or slicing in bulk), not more porting; (2) metrics tests.tsv reports a memo hit's Ms from the stored cold result (sum 26.5 s here, larger than the 6.5 s wall), so Ms is not the hit's cost. Report: spiral-bin/runs/kino-hot-paths/warm-profile-20261019-194652.txt. Next: time a bare mix run in apps/cells to split the 6 s fixed cost, and a Gleam codegen note for index wrapping")
inl note_of_kino_hot_paths_14 () = Note ("KINO-HOT-PATHS", 14u32, "2026-10-20T00:37Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 20:37 fixed-cost split (bounded job job-1792456439743, lab fixed-cost.ps1, 3 rounds each, measured while main's pack 12 ran, so noisy): mix run --no-compile --no-start (BEAM and mix only) 2.6 to 6.8 s, median 5.8 s; with the Cells app started 2.3 to 3.9 s, median 3.3 s. So the BEAM plus mix boot alone accounts for the roughly 6 s of the 6.5 s warm notebook run, which confirms the 20:1x reading that per-invocation start, not per-test work, is the warm cost (a long-lived Cells process or the KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR direction removes it; porting more per-test code would not). The 3 notebook rounds (19 to 42 s) are inconclusive: this script did not set SPIRAL_CC as warm-profile.ps1 does, and cc is part of the memo key, so they were probably misses; its output was discarded, so the hit count is unknown. Next: rerun only the notebook step with SPIRAL_CC set and memo_hits printed, on an idle machine")
inl note_of_kino_hot_paths_15 () = Note ("KINO-HOT-PATHS", 15u32, "2026-10-20T01:09Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 21:09 fixed-cost rerun on a quieter machine with SPIRAL_CC set (bounded job job-1792457639855, medians of 3): BEAM plus mix 2.2 s, with the Cells app started 2.2 s, mix spiral.notebook on the 10-cell probe 6.0 s with memo_hits 10 of 10 (round 1 was 16.3 s, a cold first round). So a warm notebook run costs about 3.8 s more than booting Cells, about 380 ms per cell, while tprof puts Kino's CPU at about 22 ms per cell: roughly 90% of the warm overhead is waiting, not BEAM CPU (candidates: mount building, memo-entry file reads and touches under Defender, the compiler handshake). Next: print the existing add_phase timings (cells.ex phases) per cell in the metrics TSV so the wait has a name before anything is ported")
inl note_of_kino_hot_paths_16 () = Note ("KINO-HOT-PATHS", 16u32, "2026-10-20T01:11Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 21:11 decisive experiment (bounded job job-1792458663308, lab in-vm-rounds.ps1/.exs: three runs of the 10-cell probe inside ONE BEAM via Mix.Tasks.Spiral.Notebook.run, all 10 memo hits each): round 1 3,484 ms, round 2 960 ms, round 3 1,059 ms. With boot at about 2.2 s, a fresh warm run (6.0 s) is about 2.2 s of BEAM and mix start, about 2.5 s of first-run initialisation inside the process (per-process caches: compiler-binary prints in persistent_term, the bounds table, mounts and the like), and about 1 s of real work for 10 cells (about 100 ms per cell, of which about 22 ms is BEAM CPU). So the hot path to remove is per-invocation start, not per-cell code: a long-lived Cells process (or a cross-process cache of the first-run state) would take a warm 10-cell gate from about 6 s to about 1 s. That changes this item's plan from porting memo keys and parsing to Spiral toward a resident Cells server, which fits KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR's daemon direction. Next: name the 2.5 s first-run part (time Memo.context and the mounts in round 1 against round 2)")
inl note_of_kino_hot_paths_17 () = Note ("KINO-HOT-PATHS", 17u32, "2026-10-20T01:21Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 21:21 the 2.5 s first-run part is named: lazy code loading. lab preload-rounds.exs (bounded job job-1792459259608) loads the modules of the 20 started applications before the first notebook run: 543 not-yet-loaded modules (of 1,169) take 2,018 ms to load, and the first notebook round after that is 888 ms, the same as a warm second round (970 ms). So a fresh warm 10-cell gate is about 2.2 s BEAM and mix start + 2.0 s loading about 540 modules from disk on first use + about 0.9 s of real work. Remedies, in order of effect: a resident Cells process (pays both once), loading code in embedded mode from a release or archive so the 540 loads become one, then per-cell work last (about 90 ms per cell, roughly a quarter of it BEAM CPU). Progress stays 50; next: decide with KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR whether the resident process is a Cells server or the compiler daemon's host")
inl note_of_kino_hot_paths_18 () = Note ("KINO-HOT-PATHS", 18u32, "2026-10-20T01:31Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 21:31 the 2 s code-loading cost split (bounded job job-1792459860394, lab preload-split.exs): reading the 545 pending .beam files (10.3 MiB) takes 251 ms (201 ms again), loading them takes 2,147 ms. So it is not disk or Defender: it is the VM loading and JIT-compiling (BeamAsm) about 10 MiB of code on first use in every fresh process. Embedded-mode or archive loading would not remove it, only a resident process that pays it once (or a smaller loaded set, for example not loading unused apps). That leaves the resident Cells process as the clear next step for warm gates")
inl note_of_kino_hot_paths_19 () = Note ("KINO-HOT-PATHS", 19u32, "2026-10-21T23:17Z", "main", "19:17 main's decision (decide-autonomously; design only, nothing built): the resident process is a Cells notebook server, not the compiler (the compiler daemon is already resident; the 2.0 s of lazy BEAM code loading plus 2.2 s of BEAM/mix start is Cells' own, per notes 10-19 21:21/21:31). Shape: mix spiral.cells_server keeps one warm VM with every Cells module loaded and listens on a loopback port recorded in <spiral-bin>/cells-server/<user>.json (pid, port, Cells source hash). mix spiral.notebook (and probe-lib.ps1) first checks that file: if the server is live and its Cells hash equals the current apps/cells source hash (so an edited Cells is never served stale, the CELLS-GLEAM-WARM-STALE lesson), it sends the argv plus cwd and the SPIRAL_* environment, the server runs it and streams stdout and the exit code back; otherwise it runs in-process as today. Isolation rule: requests run one at a time in the server (cwd and environment are process-global in the BEAM; a queue keeps each run exactly like a fresh process), and lanes needing parallelism keep their own in-process VMs. Expected effect: a small warm gate goes from about 5.1 s (2.2 + 2.0 + 0.9) to about 1 s. Gate before adoption: the 43-test sm'.spi gate gives byte-identical tests.tsv and stdout through the server and in-process, and an apps/cells edit makes the next request fall back (hash mismatch). Next slice: the server plus client in lab/KINO-HOT-PATHS as a patch, applied in a CELLS-PACK window.")
inl note_of_kino_hot_paths_20 () = Note ("KINO-HOT-PATHS", 20u32, "2026-10-21T23:18Z", "main", "19:18 first slice drafted (lab/KINO-HOT-PATHS, not applied): cells_server.ex (Spiral.Cells.Server: serve/0 listens on a loopback port 0 with packet: 4, writes <spiral-bin>/cells-server/<user>.term with port, os pid and a source stamp, the sha256 of apps/cells lib/**/*.ex paths, sizes and mtimes; it handles one request at a time, setting the request's cwd and replacing the SPIRAL_* environment around NotebookCli.execute, restoring both after), try_execute/1 (the client: only when SPIRAL_CELLS_SERVER is not 0, the info file exists, the stamp equals the current source stamp, and connect succeeds within 500 ms; otherwise :unavailable), spiral.cells_server.ex (mix spiral.cells_server), and server-apply.ps1 (1 anchor in mix spiral.notebook: try the server, else run in-process as today; dry run matches; both new files parse). Known limit of this slice: anything a run prints itself (not the returned lines) shows on the server console, so first-run progress output goes there. Gate unchanged (note 19): byte-identical sm'.spi gate output through the server and in-process, a Cells source edit forces fallback, and a measured warm-gate time. It joins CELLS-PACK-NEXT's window.")
inl note_of_kino_hot_paths_21 () = Note ("KINO-HOT-PATHS", 21u32, "2026-10-24T11:49Z", "main", "2026-10-19 laps folded from the deleted history ledger (docs/agents/history/2026-10-19-backlog-laps, HISTORY-BACKLOG-LAPS-DRIFT): lap 12 14:06 (subagent2): warm-profile.ps1 flags checked against installed Elixir 1.20.4: mix profile.tprof --type time --report total valid (total = all processes) -> next: queued warm-profile run | lap 13 14:30 (subagent2): second per-context print sized: Memo.context also runs tree_print(core) = read + compiled_blocks split + sha256 of every core module per context, uncached (memo.ex 545-560, content_print); core = 18 files, 74 KB; the warm profile shows whether it beats the cached binaries print -> next: if tprof shows content_print high: persistent_term cache keyed by path+mtime+size+bounds hash | lap 14 14:59 (subagent2): narrowed: Memo.context (binaries print + core tree print) runs once per notebook (notebook.ex:545), not per test, and planning goes through the in-process Gleam domain (Domain.plan); so the ~200 ms per-test overhead is not memo-key hashing; remaining per-test suspects are the daemon compile round trip and per-target process spawn -> next: warm-profile run (queued) splits daemon vs spawn time | lap 15 15:09 (subagent2): daemon protocol cost read: one compile = TCP connect (localhost) + hello line + request line + one reply line (compiler_client.ex transact 303-340), and the code stamp is cached in persistent_term (compiler_daemon.ex boot_stamp); so the Elixir side adds well under a ms per compile and the per-test cost is the compile itself + the native build/run of each target -> next: warm-profile run decides whether any Elixir path is still worth porting; likely drop the item's Elixir-hot-path premise | lap 17 16:43 (subagent2): no Cells metrics TSVs exist in spiral-bin/runs to mine instead of the profile; waits on the queued warm-profile run -> next: main's queue | lap 19 18:52 (subagent2): warm-profile.ps1 is not a bounded job (fresh MIX_BUILD_PATH = full Cells compile, then cold + warm runs of optionm'.spi on gleam/fsharp/cuda, then tprof): stays a main-queue row -> next: main's queue (row 79) | lap 21 19:23 (subagent2): queue row 37 (memo-cache-run.ps1) is done: memo_print_cache.py and memo_bounds_cache.py anchors are gone from memo.ex (the print cache is in the tree, memo.ex:147-167 per lap 10) -> next: warm profile (main's row 79) | lap 21-drive 19:28 (subagent2): made the warm profile boundable: warm-profile.ps1 takes -BuildPath (reuse a compiled Cells build), lab warm_probe.spi (10 C-only test cells) + warm-probe-run.ps1 (scratch package, then the profile); parse clean -> next: run warm-probe-run.ps1 as my next bounded job | lap 22-drive 20:11 (subagent2): WARM PROFILE MEASURED: cold 29.8 s, warm 6.5 s (10/10 hits); Kino CPU ~0.22 s total, 62% of it the Gleam-compiled Spiral domain's per-char scanning (spiral_wrap_signed 84k calls); fixed per-invocation cost dominates; metrics Ms for hits = stored cold Ms; set 40 -> next: split the ~6 s fixed cost (bare mix run); Gleam index-wrap codegen note | lap 23-drive 20:37 (subagent2): fixed cost measured under load: BEAM+mix boot alone median 5.8 s (2.6-6.8), app start median 3.3 s; so the warm run's ~6 s is process start; notebook rounds inconclusive (no SPIRAL_CC -> memo key differs) -> next: rerun the notebook step with SPIRAL_CC and memo_hits printed on an idle machine | lap 24-drive 21:09 (subagent2): quiet rerun: BEAM+mix 2.2 s, app 2.2 s, 10-hit notebook 6.0 s (10/10 hits) -> ~3.8 s warm overhead, ~380 ms/cell, ~90% waiting not CPU (tprof 22 ms/cell) -> next: name the wait: phase timings per cell in metrics | lap 24-drive 21:11 (subagent2): DECISIVE: 3 warm runs in one BEAM: 3,484 / 960 / 1,059 ms -> fresh warm 6 s = 2.2 s boot + ~2.5 s first-run init + ~1 s real work (100 ms/cell); a resident Cells process takes a warm gate from ~6 s to ~1 s; set 50 -> next: name the 2.5 s first-run init (Memo.context, mounts) | lap 24-drive 21:21 (subagent2): first-run cost named: lazy loading of 543 modules = 2,018 ms; after preload the first round is 888 ms (= warm). Fresh warm gate = 2.2 s boot + 2.0 s code loading + 0.9 s work -> next: resident Cells process or embedded-mode code loading (with KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR) | lap 24-drive 21:31 (subagent2): load split: reading 545 .beam files (10.3 MiB) 251 ms, loading them 2,147 ms -> VM load + JIT, not disk; only a resident process removes it -> next: resident Cells process decision")
inl note_of_kino_hot_paths_22 () = Note ("KINO-HOT-PATHS", 22u32, "2026-10-27T09:21Z", "main", "10-27 05:21 main: 10-27 05:21 main groom: fresh status (stale since 10-21). The 10-21 first slice (Cells server) has LANDED, all three files of server-apply.ps1. apps/cells/lib/spiral/cells/server.ex is byte-identical to lab/KINO-HOT-PATHS/cells_server.ex (sha256). spiral.notebook.ex routes through Spiral.Cells.Server.try_execute(args), falling back to NotebookCli.execute, and the old direct call is gone. The mix task spiral.cells_server.ex landed with one more line than the lab copy (Spiral.Cells.MemoryGuard.start(), from the memory-guard work), so the tree is the newer version. That makes lab cells_server.ex, spiral.cells_server.ex and server-apply.ps1 drained duplicates; removing them waits on the by-stem reference check (running) and the user's archive rule. The 10-19 measurement runners (fixed-cost, in-vm-rounds, memo-cache, preload, warm-profile) remain the open half: the item says measure first, and no warm-vs-cold number since the server landed is recorded. Next: one fixed-cost.ps1 run with and without a live server, on the node.")
inl note_of_kino_hot_paths_23 () = Note ("KINO-HOT-PATHS", 23u32, "2026-10-30T14:14Z", "main", "10-30 10:14 main, BACKLOG-DANGLING-1030 R3: Active -> Paused, progress kept at 50%. No note since 10-27 and no live job, so it isn't real WIP. Nothing blocks it; the concrete next step is in the previous note (owner main). Resume by picking it, never by re-verifying.")
inl note_of_kino_hot_paths_24 () = Note ("KINO-HOT-PATHS", 24u32, "2026-10-31T03:21Z", "main", "10-30 23:21 main: lab/KINO-HOT-PATHS is ARCHIVED (user, 10-30 22:5x: the lab dir didn't work and drifted; drafts don't live outside the repo). Archive 20261030-232045 under %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\archive. Restore this folder with: pwsh i574n.github/scripts/scratch-archive.ps1 -Root C:\\home\\git\\spiral\\lab -Restore '%LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\archive\\20261030-232045\\manifest.tsv' -Only 'KINO-HOT-PATHS\\*' -RestoreTo C:\\home\\git\\spiral\\lab. Resume this item's work in its real place in the repo, not in lab.")
inl note_of_kino_hot_paths_25 () = Note ("KINO-HOT-PATHS", 25u32, "2026-10-31T20:56Z", "main", "10-31 16:53 main, pause re-decision (KIT-PAUSE-RULE-REWORK; rule R3 is now 'never auto-pause for silence'): Paused -> PLANNED. It was paused by the old silence/WIP rule, not by a blocker: its notes name no external condition (a 10-30 lab-archive note blocks nothing: restore the draft from the archive into the repo when picked). Back in agile next at its priority; deps, if any, gate it as usual.")
inl note_of_kino_hot_paths_26 () = Note ("KINO-HOT-PATHS", 26u32, "2026-11-01T04:50Z", "main", "main 00:5x LAB CODE IS SAFE (user 11-01 00:2x: 'check for all lab references in history... make sure we didn't lose that code... to repurpose them or groom their tasks'). This item's lab/KINO-HOT-PATHS folder (12 files: fixed-cost.ps1, in-vm-rounds.exs, in-vm-rounds.ps1, memo-cache-run.ps1, memo_bounds_cache.py, memo_print_cache.py) is archived in %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\archive\\20261030-232045 (blob-verified). Restore it into its proper repo place with: pwsh C:\\home\\git\\i574n.github\\scripts\\scratch-archive.ps1 -Restore '%LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\archive\\20261030-232045\\manifest.tsv' -RestoreTo <repo dir> -Only 'KINO-HOT-PATHS/*'. Next step for the owner at its next pull: restore the useful files in place as incomplete drafts (Cells heals them in place), or record why it starts over.")
inl eoie_scrum_xp () = Task ("EOIE-SCRUM-XP", "eoie agile beyond Kanban-style flow (user 2026-10-17: could we also be scrum or xp?). It has Epic|Feature|Story|TaskKind, priority/difficulty, deps, estimate (minutes), load, assignee agent|pair|user, leases/handoff and plan; missing: time-boxed sprints/iterations (start/end, committed items, a sprint goal), velocity from completed estimates, an explicit epic->child relation (today deps/tags), sprint review/retro records, and XP practices as data (pairing already exists as assignee=pair; TDD is the EOIE-ONE-CALL-SPEC direction; small releases = receipts). Each as a one-call verb with Spiral test blocks", Story, P0, D5, 50u32, Planned, "", "kit,agile,team:kit", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_eoie_scrum_xp () = Planning ("EOIE-SCRUM-XP", Minutes 390u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_eoie_scrum_xp_1 () = Note ("EOIE-SCRUM-XP", 1u32, "2026-10-22T21:42Z", "eoie-lane", "17:42 pure model drafted and tested (scratch f5/src/agile_sprint_model, HostFree + LibReferenced). The sprint GADT typestate PlannedSprint -> RunningSprint -> ClosedSprint means started takes only a planned sprint and closed_with only a running one; sprint_terms {goal; start; end}; committed items with estimate and done. Velocity is the mean done minutes of the last 3 reviews, and 'commit' refuses work over it with OverCapacity planned/capacity (no cap for a first sprint). Closing yields the review record (done_min, spilled_min, goal_met), which is the retro's data. 2 test blocks on today's F/GE2 plan (F done 95, GE2 spilled 120; 215 planned minutes fit a 300-minute velocity and not a 90). Cells 2/2 pass on the first compile and again after re-laying; standards 0 findings. Left: the epic -> child relation and the agile verbs (sprint start/commit/close) over state rows.")
inl note_of_eoie_scrum_xp_2 () = Note ("EOIE-SCRUM-XP", 2u32, "2026-10-24T23:44Z", "eoie", "19:43 Provisional estimate 390m from the grooming rule (EOIE-AGILE-CHECK-FIELDS note): D5 at 20%. Replace it with milestone sizing.")
inl note_of_eoie_scrum_xp_3 () = Note ("EOIE-SCRUM-XP", 3u32, "2026-10-27T08:16Z", "kit", "04:1x kit, groomed with the compiler lane (user 2026-10-27 03:2x: replace the umbrella agile word with the Scrum/XP names; 'not just aliasing, we have memory to learn the new ways'). A real rework: agile stays only as a transition alias for one release, callers and semantics move to ceremony verbs, and memories are rewritten to the new verbs. Draft map: backlog = list/next/plan/views/view/view-add/roots/check/todos/links; story = add/edit/show/set/rename/migrate-tags; note/note-edit/notes stay; standup = status/handoff; lease/begin/record stay kit work-lease (concurrency control, not a ceremony) unless a sprint verb earns them; retro is new (items Done in a window plus their notes); sprint is new (a timebox over team backlogs). Mechanics: command_spec_domain gives each verb its ceremony; dispatch keeps agile verb as an alias that prints the new spelling; migrate callers by grep (autonomous-gate hook calls agile list/handoff, scripts, memories, backlog views). state/agile.spi is state data: renaming it is a separate step under the respell-skips-state-data rules. Milestones: M1 verb table in command_spec_domain + aliases; M2 callers + hooks migrated; M3 memories rewritten; M4 alias removed after one release with zero alias hits in the job logs.")
inl note_of_eoie_scrum_xp_4 () = Note ("EOIE-SCRUM-XP", 4u32, "2026-10-27T12:40Z", "kit", "08:4x kit: M1 (ceremony verbs) built, proven on a probe copy, and staged. kit backlog|story|standup are new direct verbs (command_spec_domain/direct_verbs: VerbBacklog, VerbStory, VerbStandup, each with its help line; the help test now counts 25 verbs and 22 lines). kit_cli_model routes each one to the existing agile handler only when the subcommand belongs to that ceremony. The agile handler reads env::args().skip(2), so 'kit backlog list <root>' gives it the same arguments as 'kit agile list <root>', and nothing is rewritten. Ownership lives in a new granular crate, kit_ceremonies (typed ceremony union; list literals checked with listm'.contains; one export, kit_ceremony_admits(word, subcommand)). A misrouted verb refuses with exit 2 and names the right spelling: 'kit: note is not a verb of this ceremony; use: kit story note'; lease, begin, record and init stay agile-only (work leases are kit's concurrency control, not a ceremony). Size: the first cut put kit.rs at 1,389 lines, because verb_help_of on a runtime value inlined the whole 25-arm help catalog and the string-literal match emitted a comparison per literal. Final: kit.rs 946 (was 899), ceremonies.rs 210, command_spec.rs 856. Behavior check on the generated ceremonies.rs: 4 admitted, 4 refused with the right alternative. kit_cli_model now references the lib (spiral- added per the AGENTS contract). Stage: clean/kit-rename/scrum-verbs (8 files with base hashes) plus clean/kit-rename/staged-apply.ps1 (refuses on a live lease, a queued release, or any base drift). It lands after kit-release-SLICES. Next: M2, migrating callers (hooks, scripts, memories) to the ceremony verbs.")
inl note_of_eoie_scrum_xp_5 () = Note ("EOIE-SCRUM-XP", 5u32, "2026-10-27T12:41Z", "kit", "08:4x kit: M2 caller inventory (read-only, before any migration). 'agile <verb>' call sites outside kit: 42 in 18 files: 3 in ~/.claude/hooks and 39 in memories; none in i574n.github/scripts or the compiler bundle scripts. By ceremony: story 26 (mostly 'agile note' in memories), backlog 15, standup 1, agile-only 0. Rule for M2: migrate only after the release that ships M1 is installed (an older kit.exe would refuse the new verbs); the hooks go first, each pipe-tested old vs new per eoie-hooks; then the memories via one mechanical rewrite with the map list/next/plan/views/view/view-add/roots/check/todos/links -> backlog, add/edit/show/set/rename/migrate-tags/note/note-edit/notes -> story, status/handoff -> standup. M4 (removing the alias) is measured by zero 'agile <ceremony verb>' calls in kit job logs over one day.")
inl note_of_eoie_scrum_xp_6 () = Note ("EOIE-SCRUM-XP", 6u32, "2026-10-30T14:22Z", "kit", "10-30 10:3x kit groom (R3, silent 3.1 d): M1 is INSTALLED. 'kit backlog list <root> Active --team kit' answers on C528BE4D, so M2's rule (migrate only after the release that ships M1 is installed) now holds. NEXT (owner kit, CPU-light, after the GRANULAR-LINT port): M2 step 1 = re-run the caller inventory (the 10-27 counts predate the wipe's memory rebuild), then migrate the ~/.claude/hooks call sites one at a time, each pipe-tested old vs new per eoie-hooks; memories follow by one mechanical rewrite. The old 'agile' verbs keep working, so nothing breaks while M2 waits.")
inl note_of_eoie_scrum_xp_7 () = Note ("EOIE-SCRUM-XP", 7u32, "2026-10-30T15:59Z", "kit", "10-30 11:5x kit: M2 DONE. Step 1, the caller inventory re-run (the 10-27 counts were taken before the memory rebuild): 'agile <verb>' call sites are 0 in ~/.claude/hooks (the rebuilt hooks no longer call agile), 0 in i574n.github/scripts, and 19 in 15 memory files. Of those, 7 are runnable command examples and the rest are prose or history (what was invisible on 10-28, the M2 rule itself, seal, which has no ceremony verb). The installed ceremony verbs answer: 'kit story show', 'kit backlog next --team kit', 'kit backlog check' (exit 0). The 7 examples now use them, in one anchored kit do program over the memory folder (dry run first, each anchor matched once): agent-sync-daily (story note), agile-team-term (backlog list --team), autonomous-stop-gate (backlog next), fill-all-task-fields (backlog check, backlog list --open --json), kit-env-in-old-shells (backlog check), read-clock-before-timestamps (story note-edit). Draft: C:\\home\\data\\kit-drafts\\memory-ceremony-verbs.do. The agile verbs keep working, so no caller breaks. Next: M3 (time-boxed sprints: start/end, committed items, a goal) needs its own design note first.")
inl note_of_eoie_scrum_xp_8 () = Note ("EOIE-SCRUM-XP", 8u32, "2026-10-30T17:54Z", "kit", "10-30 13:5x kit: M3 DESIGN (time-boxed sprints), written before any code. Pass line pre-registered at the end.\nTYPES (the agile state, beside Task/Note/Planning records):\n- Sprint (id, goal, start ISO, end ISO, state: SprintOpen | SprintClosed), with SprintItem (sprint id, task id, committed estimate in minutes) as separate rows, so committing an item never rewrites the sprint row.\n- velocity is derived, never stored: the sum of committed estimates of items Done by the sprint's end.\nVERBS (ceremony 'backlog', as M1 routes them; one call each):\n- 'kit backlog sprint start <root> <id> <days> \"<goal>\" [--items A,B,...]' refuses when another sprint is open (one at a time), when an item is unknown or Done, or when an item has no estimate (fill-all-task-fields).\n- 'kit backlog sprint add <root> <id> <item>' and 'sprint drop <root> <id> <item>' (a drop keeps a SprintItem row marked dropped, for the review).\n- 'kit backlog sprint show <root> [<id>]': goal, days left, committed vs done minutes, each item's status, and burn-down as per-day Done minutes from the notes' Done stamps.\n- 'kit backlog sprint close <root> <id>': review rows (done, carried, dropped) and velocity; carried items stay open with a note.\n- 'kit backlog velocity <root> [--last n]': past closed sprints' velocity, for planning ('next --fits' could cap by it later).\nPASS LINE: a Spiral test block per verb on a fixture backlog (start refuses a second open sprint and an unestimated item; close reports velocity = done committed minutes; the old agile verbs' output is byte-identical with no sprint rows present), plus a CLI test per verb. Sizes: new package agile_sprint, its own crate (agile_task_* crates are at 954-970). Estimate: D4, 300m (estimates-from-rules).")
inl note_of_eoie_scrum_xp_9 () = Note ("EOIE-SCRUM-XP", 9u32, "2026-11-01T03:36Z", "kit", "10-31 23:4x kit groom (main 23:33, user: cadence/drift): Active -> PLANNED. No note in over 12 h, so nobody is working it this week; Planned keeps it in agile next at its priority (never auto-Paused, R3). First step when picked = the next step in its latest note.")
inl note_of_eoie_scrum_xp_10 () = Note ("EOIE-SCRUM-XP", 10u32, "2026-11-01T04:34Z", "kit", "11-01 00:3x kit (user 00:2x via main: 'ask kit to speed up the agile->scrum/xp migration ... we gotta have kit stable enough to support [50+ agents]'): raised P2 -> P0 and SLICED into 8 TaskKind items of 45-60 min each, team kit, load light, chained by deps: SCRUM-XP-S1 typed sprint model (ACTIVE tonight), S2 state rows (append-only, old verbs byte-identical), S3 sprint start, S4 add/drop, S5 show + burn-down, S6 close + velocity, S7 epic->child relation as typed data, S8 retro ceremony + dogfood kit's first sprint. M1 (ceremony verbs) installed, M2 (caller migration) done; this is M3+ (sprints, epic relation, retro). Each slice ends with a node notebook; candidate builds batch per 2-3 slices.")
inl note_of_eoie_scrum_xp_11 () = Note ("EOIE-SCRUM-XP", 11u32, "2026-11-01T18:09Z", "main", "main 11-01 14:1x (user: 'sad that i asked the scrum/xp features days ago and they're not spread out yet, but let's keep dreaming'). 'Spread out' means every lane uses sprints, velocity and standup in its daily flow, not just that the verbs exist. DoD addition: each of the six lanes' groom reports quote kit sprint/standup output for 3 consecutive days. EMACS-STEALS (filed today) maps org-mode mechanisms onto the slices: S2 gets clock actuals plus logged state-change rows, S3 the effort warning, S5/S7 column totals plus dependency triggers, S8 saved queries/standup.")
inl eoie_memory_links () = Task ("EOIE-MEMORY-LINKS", "One-call memory/backlog link check (user 2026-10-17: harden the memory+eoie symbiosis): eoie agile links <backlog root> --memory <dir> reports every backlog ID cited in memory files (frontmatter + body), dangling IDs, project/feedback memories citing Done items whose rule may have changed, and backlog items whose notes cite a missing memory slug; --json for agents. Done by hand on 2026-10-17 with a pwsh one-off: 55 memories, 0 dangling, ~12 Done references (mostly historical lessons). Spiral test blocks with fixture memory dirs.", Story, P2, D3, 100u32, Done, "none", "eoie,agile,memory", "current", "current")
inl eoie_memory () = Task ("EOIE-MEMORY", "eoie embodies the agents' memory (user 2026-10-17: the name comes from embodiment; absorb ~/.claude/projects/*/memory and the Codex, Grok, Zed, OpenCode and Antigravity equivalents in a typed way, keep only a bootstrap in each client pointing to the eoie tutorial, sync through eoie in git instead of zipping the profile). Typed model in Spiral, natively what Rust fakes with PhantomData: a memory item is a GADT indexed by kind (user | feedback | project | reference), and feedback and project require Why and HowToApply at the type level; the heterogeneous store is an existential pack; client adapters are an HKT-style interface (one per client format); per-kind prototypes give defaults; reflection derives the renderers (markdown frontmatter, JSON, client bootstraps) and the schema, like the Convo tool-schema reflection the study lane built. Found on this laptop 2026-10-17: Claude .claude/projects/C--home-git/memory (56 typed md files plus the MEMORY.md index); Zed AppData/Roaming/Zed/AGENTS.md (rules); Antigravity .gemini/antigravity (brain/, annotations/); OpenCode .local/share/opencode/opencode.db (read a copy; never the credential/account tables); Codex only config.toml (no memory store yet); Grok .grok (no memory store found). Children: EOIE-MEMORY-MODEL, EOIE-MEMORY-VERBS, EOIE-MEMORY-ADAPTERS, EOIE-MEMORY-BOOTSTRAP, EOIE-MEMORY-LINKS", Epic, P1, D8, 0u32, Planned, "EOIE-MEMORY-FEATURES,EOIE-MEMORY-MODEL,EOIE-MEMORY-VERBS,EOIE-MEMORY-ADAPTERS,EOIE-MEMORY-BOOTSTRAP,EOIE-MEMORY-LINKS", "kit,memory,epic,gadt,team:kit", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_eoie_memory () = Planning ("EOIE-MEMORY", Minutes 1770u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_eoie_memory_1 () = Note ("EOIE-MEMORY", 1u32, "2026-10-17T18:19Z", "main-thread", "14:19 main-thread: absorbed the memory-formats lane (9 notes on EOIE-MEMORY-ADAPTERS). New fact for the epic: Grok has a memory store, memory-v2 (topics/*.md: 11 curated topics; observations/_inbox: 19 notes with provenance frontmatter; generated MEMORY.md, never write it), and it also loads ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md through its Claude compatibility setting. Zed, OpenCode, Antigravity and Codex have rules/instructions but no memory store. Claude: 44 feedback, 8 project, 4 reference; every feedback/project item has Why and How; a byte-identical render needs quote style and body segments stored. Privacy: exclude ~/.config/opencode/service.json (plaintext password) and every auth/credential file by name; transcripts (threads, brain, sessions) are never imported. Related DeltaDB idea (Zed, 2026): history that links changes to the agent conversations behind them.")
inl note_of_eoie_memory_2 () = Note ("EOIE-MEMORY", 2u32, "2026-10-17T18:23Z", "bundle-wisdom", "14:23 from the bundles (tritcritnet alpha716 docs\\vision\\readme_eoie.md Feb 2026; core alpha9237 readmes Jun; samples alpha705; BUNDLE-WISDOM note 10): the old vision put memory in receipts, not in the model ('EOIE is the body that makes the brain leave fingerprints'). Concrete pieces missing from the memory epic: a resume compiler that builds the next-turn agenda from the last ledger (passed -> skip/cheap verify, failed dep -> block downstream, expected failure -> tolerated, stale report -> regenerate, partial patch -> repair plan); an incident-resume summary in one call (active lease, newest savepoint, files changed since, newest receipts, toolchain identity); a claim boundary per report (passed/blocked/synthetic/proxy/tolerated); reports projected from one typed receipt so prose cannot overwrite a metric.")
inl note_of_eoie_memory_3 () = Note ("EOIE-MEMORY", 3u32, "2026-10-22T02:30Z", "eoie", "22:30 drift traced (user 22:3x): the 10-17 direction said 'absorb in a typed way', but EOIE-MEMORY-MODEL's proof became 'import, render back, byte-identical', which rewards one opaque string per memory body; the 10-21 import (memory/claude.spi, 212 KB in the eoie repo) and the verbs followed it. Pivot: EOIE-MEMORY-FEATURES (mine memories for typed eoie functionality). Held out of the release chain: r35 apply-recall-coverage and r36's five memory slices (split, save, forget, default-store, edit) — scripts kept for reference, not shipped. memory/claude.spi also conflicts with EOIE-GENERIC-DRIFT (user data in the generic repo); its fate goes with the pivot.")
inl note_of_eoie_memory_4 () = Note ("EOIE-MEMORY", 4u32, "2026-10-29T23:23Z", "main", "10-29 19:23 main, fill-all-task-fields: estimate set to 1770 min by the epic rule (the sum of each open child's estimate x (100 - progress)%, all children estimated): EOIE-MEMORY-FEATURES 432, -MODEL 357, -VERBS 21, -ADAPTERS 720, -BOOTSTRAP 240; -LINKS is Done. Re-derive it when a child's estimate or progress moves. Epic progress stays at its owner's value.")
inl note_of_eoie_memory_5 () = Note ("EOIE-MEMORY", 5u32, "2026-11-01T14:43Z", "kit", "11-01 10:4x kit: prior art to beat, akitaonrails/ai-memory (Rust, markdown + SQLite FTS5, git-versioned, cross-provider handover between Claude Code/Codex/Cursor). kit's edge has to be typed memory with provenance and verification (a fact can be refused), plus links from a fact to the conversation and build behind it (DeltaDB idea). Definition work in KIT-DEFINITION.")
inl eoie_memory_model () = Task ("EOIE-MEMORY-MODEL", "Typed memory model + lossless import of Claude's 56 memories: GADT memory<kind>, existential store, reflection-derived markdown renderer; stored as Spiral data in the eoie repo (like state/agile.spi). Proof: import, then render back, must be byte-identical to every source file (frontmatter, body, [[links]]); links resolved and dangling ones reported; Spiral test blocks only", Story, P1, D6, 30u32, Planned, "", "kit,memory,gadt,team:kit,kit", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_eoie_memory_model () = Planning ("EOIE-MEMORY-MODEL", Minutes 510u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_eoie_memory_model_1 () = Note ("EOIE-MEMORY-MODEL", 1u32, "2026-10-17T17:44Z", "main-thread", "13:44 main-thread design sketch (not started; needs the user's go): kinds are nominals: nominal user = (), nominal feedback = (), nominal project = (), nominal reference = (). The item is a GADT: union memory kind = | User :: {slug; description; body} -> memory user | Feedback :: {slug; description; rule; why; how} -> memory feedback | Project :: {slug; description; fact; why; how} -> memory project | Reference :: {slug; description; pointer} -> memory reference, so a feedback without why/how doesn't typecheck. The store is existential: union stored = Stored :: forall k. memory k -> stored, a list or map by slug. Links: [[slug]] parsed to a typed link list resolved against the store; dangling links and backlog IDs reported by check. Prototypes: per-kind defaults by typecase on the index. Reflection: renderers iterate a record's keys at compile time (markdown frontmatter name/description/metadata.type, JSON), the same technique as the Convo tool schemas (lab/convo-in-spiral). Adapters (HKT): a client is a type-level function f with import : path -> f stored and render : stored -> f text. Round-trip proof: the 56 Claude files plus MEMORY.md imported and rendered back byte-identical. Prerequisite checked today: recursive GADTs with grown indices compile since GADT-GROWING-INDEX-HANG's fix (blessed 111908).")
inl note_of_eoie_memory_model_2 () = Note ("EOIE-MEMORY-MODEL", 2u32, "2026-10-21T11:21Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-21 07:21 eoie team (go decided by the eoie lane under the user's 07:05 direction: import only, no client changes; the bootstrap swap stays sequenced after model and verbs are proven): drafted crate eoie_memory (scratch memory-model/, apply-memory-model.ps1, after r28 slice 12 for union_case_where). Verbs: memory import <dir> <data.spi> (writes Spiral data only when every file renders back byte-identical, else refuses and writes nothing), render <data.spi> <out-dir>, check <data.spi> (dangling [[links]] informational; feedback/project without Why/How, files missing from MEMORY.md and index entries for missing files are problems, exit 1), list [--kind]. The kind is a reflected union (KindUser|KindFeedback|KindProject|KindReference). The data file is a Spiral schema (union memory_kind, union memory_item) plus one inl per memory and the raw MEMORY.md. Survey of the 74 real files: one frontmatter shape (73) plus a type-only metadata block (1), all LF. The existential store from the 10-17 sketch is a concrete list, because existentials are compile-time only in Spiral (measured 10-20). Notebook memory_cli.spi: round trip byte-identical, list --kind, check findings, refusal. Preview queued behind tibia's live block.")
inl note_of_eoie_memory_model_3 () = Note ("EOIE-MEMORY-MODEL", 3u32, "2026-10-21T11:47Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-21 07:47 eoie team: design proof before the build: the crate's exact parse and render rules ported to PowerShell (frontmatter between the first two --- lines; keys name, description, node_type, type, originSessionId, modified by line prefix; body after the closing line; render emits metadata lines only for non-empty values) reproduce all 74 real memory files byte-identically, and all four kinds occur. The notebook covers the same paths through the CLI once r28 builds.")
inl note_of_eoie_memory_model_4 () = Note ("EOIE-MEMORY-MODEL", 4u32, "2026-10-21T14:15Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-21 10:15 eoie team: location decided for the real import (rule set first: the place that leaves eoie's checks unchanged, ties to state/). agile check compiles every file of a root's state/ package, so a memory file there would be type-checked by every check; a separate folder couples to nothing. Data goes to C:\\home\\git\\eoie\\memory\\claude.spi (one file per client later). Script ready (scratch post-r28-memory-import.ps1): import into scratch, memory check, render to a scratch folder and compare every file byte for byte with the source, and only then copy into the repo. It runs after r28 installs; the bootstrap swap stays out (needs the user's go).")
inl note_of_eoie_memory_model_5 () = Note ("EOIE-MEMORY-MODEL", 5u32, "2026-10-21T20:35Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-21 16:35 eoie team: shipped in the combined r28+r29 release, installed 15:38 (one-call spec 34/34, all 67 notebooks green): eoie_memory (994) + eoie_memory_query (975) + the eoie_memory_model package; memory import into eoie/memory/claude.spi runs at the r30 apply.")
inl note_of_eoie_memory_model_6 () = Note ("EOIE-MEMORY-MODEL", 6u32, "2026-10-22T02:30Z", "eoie", "22:30 the byte-identical round-trip proof is demoted: at most an ingest step, never the goal (it produced string blobs). The model's value is the typed features of EOIE-MEMORY-FEATURES.")
inl note_of_eoie_memory_model_7 () = Note ("EOIE-MEMORY-MODEL", 7u32, "2026-10-22T21:53Z", "eoie-lane", "17:53 paused by rule (one record per fact): the title's goal, a lossless import of the memories into a string store, was called drift (10-21), and its store memory/claude.spi was deleted at 05:33 (EOIE-MEMORY-FEATURES note). The shipped pieces (eoie_memory, eoie_memory_query, eoie_memory_model) stay in the tree, and the remaining value, typed features mined from memories, is tracked only in EOIE-MEMORY-FEATURES (F1-F6, with F5's model drafted today). Resume condition: none of its own; work continues there.")
inl note_of_eoie_memory_model_8 () = Note ("EOIE-MEMORY-MODEL", 8u32, "2026-10-29T22:32Z", "main", "10-29 18:3x main: PROVISIONAL fields filled while the item is Blocked or Paused (fill-all-task-fields): estimate 510m (D-base rule: base(D6) x (1 - 30%)); load light (default); assignee agent (default). The owner corrects them on resume.")
inl note_of_eoie_memory_model_9 () = Note ("EOIE-MEMORY-MODEL", 9u32, "2026-10-31T20:23Z", "kit", "10-31 16:2x kit (user request via compiler: the auto-pause-for-silence rule hid ready work; R3 is now 'never auto-pause'): REPAIR, Paused-by-rule -> Planned. Current state supports resuming: the lossless-import goal works on the installed E14F1F0B ('kit memory import' round-trips all 214 memories identically, index_identical=yes; acceptance row 24), and memory save/edit/forget/recall shipped (EOIE-MEMORY-VERBS Done). What remains is the typed GADT model of memory kinds itself; re-scope when picked.")
inl note_of_eoie_memory_model_10 () = Note ("EOIE-MEMORY-MODEL", 10u32, "2026-11-02T14:01Z", "x", "2026-11-02 09:59 x: DECISION RECORD (typed; groom of user waits, user 11-02: 'decisions waiting on me get met autonomously/deterministically with tests/benchs/typefeatureset modeling/backups'). kind=ByDefault. QUESTION: Does the user give the go to start the typed memory model (n1 says 'needs the user's go')? RULE (written before acting): Default = start. It is additive (Spiral data in the kit repo), replaces nothing, and changes no client. Its DoD is a test: render(import(x)) byte-identical for every file in the memory dir, with dangling links reported. BACKUP/UNDO: Additive. The source memory files are only read. OWNER: kit. NEXT: Write the memory kind GADT and the existential store, and import with a round-trip test over the current memory dir. The title's '56 memories' is stale: MEMORY.md now indexes several hundred. VETO: the user can reverse this at any time; the backup above is the undo.")
inl eoie_memory_verbs () = Task ("EOIE-MEMORY-VERBS", "One-call verbs: eoie memory recall <query|ids> [--client <c>] (relevant memories for this task, ranked, with related links), eoie memory save <kind> <slug> <description> <body> (type-checked: feedback needs Why/How), eoie memory edit/forget, eoie memory check (subsumes EOIE-MEMORY-LINKS: backlog IDs, dangling links, Done items). Each verb ~20 ms native vs reading 56 files; Spiral test blocks", Story, P0, D5, 100u32, Done, "EOIE-MEMORY-FEATURES", "kit,memory", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_eoie_memory_verbs () = Planning ("EOIE-MEMORY-VERBS", Minutes 105u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_eoie_memory_verbs_1 () = Note ("EOIE-MEMORY-VERBS", 1u32, "2026-10-21T23:12Z", "eoie-lane", "19:12 eoie team, grooming from the code: eoie_memory (shipped with the memory crates in r28+r29) already has import, render, check, list [--kind] and recall <data.spi> <words> [--limit n] over eoie_memory_query; tonight's apply-r30 ran import/check/render on all 75 Claude memories (round trip identical, 1 dangling link: formatter-plan-f2). Missing against the title: save (typed, feedback needs Why/How), edit/forget, --client, recall without a data file argument (default store), and 'eoie help memory' (fixed in r35's help coverage slice). Progress set from 0 to 40.")
inl note_of_eoie_memory_verbs_2 () = Note ("EOIE-MEMORY-VERBS", 2u32, "2026-10-21T23:15Z", "eoie-lane", "19:15 eoie team, recall ranking measured (rule set before measuring: adopt a coverage-first score only if hit@1 improves and hit@3 does not drop; tie keeps the current). 12 queries over the 75 Claude memories (scratch recall-eval.ps1 -> lab/EOIE-MEMORY-VERBS): current substring counts hit@1 8/12, hit@3 10/12 (misses: 'release inventory lab' ranked lab-in-repo first because 'lab' also counts inside 'label'/'available'; 'comments in code'); words covered x100 + weighted whole-word hits with body hits capped at 5: 12/12 and 12/12. Drafted for r35 (apply-recall-coverage.ps1: whole_words primitive, word_score record, ranked_score), memory_cli expectations updated (4 -> 104, 5 -> 105, same order). Caveat: I wrote the query set; it stays as the regression set in lab.")
inl note_of_eoie_memory_verbs_3 () = Note ("EOIE-MEMORY-VERBS", 3u32, "2026-10-21T23:17Z", "eoie-lane", "19:17 gate margin: the recall slice previews clean (query.rs 975 -> 990); eoie_memory's memory.rs is already 994. So the remaining verbs (save with Why/How typing, edit/forget, a default store so recall needs no data-file argument) start with a split of eoie_memory (verb routing vs import/render), like tonight's eoie_grep_query split.")
inl note_of_eoie_memory_verbs_4 () = Note ("EOIE-MEMORY-VERBS", 4u32, "2026-10-21T23:21Z", "eoie-lane", "19:21 eoie team: r36 slice 3 drafted and preview-clean (scratch apply-memory-split.ps1): import and render move into a new crate eoie_memory_store (exports eoie_memory_import, eoie_memory_render); eoie_memory keeps the verb union and routes through exports. memory.rs 994 -> 179, memory_store.rs 832. That frees room for save/edit/forget and a default store in eoie_memory.")
inl note_of_eoie_memory_verbs_5 () = Note ("EOIE-MEMORY-VERBS", 5u32, "2026-10-21T23:31Z", "eoie-lane", "19:31 eoie team: r36 slice 4 drafted, preview-clean: 'eoie memory save <data.spi> <kind> <slug> <description> <body>' in its own crate eoie_memory_save (memory_save.rs 644; putting it into eoie_memory_store would have made that 1029). save_check union: SaveAccepted kind | KindRejected | ReasonsMissing (feedback and project need **Why:** and **How to apply:**) | SlugTaken; nothing is written on refusal; the entry and its MEMORY index line are appended; help line updated. memory_cli test (runtime on the r36 binary). Left: edit/forget, --client, a default store.")
inl note_of_eoie_memory_verbs_6 () = Note ("EOIE-MEMORY-VERBS", 6u32, "2026-10-21T23:36Z", "eoie-lane", "19:36 r36 slice 5, preview-clean: 'eoie memory forget <data.spi> <slug>' removes the entry and its MEMORY index line, refuses an unknown slug with nothing written (memory_save.rs 737, memory.rs 239). Slice 6 (default store: check/list/recall without a data file read memory/claude.spi next to eoie.exe) is previewing. Left after r36: edit (description/body), --client.")
inl note_of_eoie_memory_verbs_7 () = Note ("EOIE-MEMORY-VERBS", 7u32, "2026-10-21T23:38Z", "eoie-lane", "19:38 r36 slice 6, preview-clean: check, list and recall take an optional data file; without one they read memory/claude.spi next to eoie.exe (memory_store_path union GivenStore | DefaultStore; memory.rs 313). So on the release binary 'eoie memory recall <words>' is the one call. Slice 7 (edit description|body, reasons still required for feedback/project bodies) is previewing.")
inl note_of_eoie_memory_verbs_8 () = Note ("EOIE-MEMORY-VERBS", 8u32, "2026-10-21T23:44Z", "eoie-lane", "19:44 r36 slice 7, preview-clean: 'eoie memory edit <data.spi> <slug> description|body <value>' (memory_field union by reflection; body edits of feedback/project memories still need the reasons). memory_save.rs 993 after keeping one slug walk (kind_of_slug) for save, forget and edit; the next verb goes in its own crate. With r36 the title's verbs exist except --client (which waits for the memory bootstrap direction).")
inl note_of_eoie_memory_verbs_9 () = Note ("EOIE-MEMORY-VERBS", 9u32, "2026-10-22T02:30Z", "eoie", "22:30 on hold for the pivot: save/forget/edit/default-store/recall-scoring over string bodies extend the drift; r35 recall-coverage and r36 memory slices removed from the chains. Verbs return once EOIE-MEMORY-FEATURES decides what memory data is typed.")
inl note_of_eoie_memory_verbs_10 () = Note ("EOIE-MEMORY-VERBS", 10u32, "2026-10-22T09:35Z", "eoie", "05:35 lab drain: apply-memory-default-store.ps1 deleted. It made check/list/recall default to memory/claude.spi next to eoie.exe, and that file was deleted at 05:33 by the EOIE-MEMORY-FEATURES rule (string-store copy, user-called drift), so the slice can never apply. The five other held r36 slices (memory save, edit, forget, split, recall coverage) stay as reference until this item is reworked onto typed memory features (EOIE-MEMORY-FEATURES F1-F6). Each must be rewritten against typed rows before it can land, never applied as drafted.")
inl note_of_eoie_memory_verbs_11 () = Note ("EOIE-MEMORY-VERBS", 11u32, "2026-10-22T21:53Z", "eoie-lane", "17:53 grooming rule: an item on hold until another item's decision carries that item as a dependency and the Blocked status, so 'agile next' (WSJF) stops proposing it. Its notes since 22:30 say the verbs return once EOIE-MEMORY-FEATURES decides what memory data is typed, so it now depends on EOIE-MEMORY-FEATURES and is Blocked; progress is unchanged.")
inl note_of_eoie_memory_verbs_12 () = Note ("EOIE-MEMORY-VERBS", 12u32, "2026-10-25T00:45Z", "eoie", "20:45 Lab drain: the five unlanded patches (apply-memory-save, -forget, -edit, -split, apply-recall-coverage) and recall-eval moved from lab/ to the scratch next-release\\memory-verbs folder. They date from 10-22 and need a re-proof on the current tree before any release, because CLEAN's 33 slices may have moved their anchors.")
inl note_of_eoie_memory_verbs_13 () = Note ("EOIE-MEMORY-VERBS", 13u32, "2026-10-29T22:31Z", "main", "10-29 18:3x main: PROVISIONAL fields filled while the item is Blocked or Paused (fill-all-task-fields): estimate 105m (D-base rule: base(D5) x (1 - 80%)); load light (default); assignee agent (default). The owner corrects them on resume.")
inl note_of_eoie_memory_verbs_14 () = Note ("EOIE-MEMORY-VERBS", 14u32, "2026-10-30T17:49Z", "kit", "10-30 13:5x kit: STATE CHECK (the item said 80% Blocked). The installed kit C528BE4D ships 'kit memory import | render | check | list | recall', and they work on today's folder. A scratch run imported 197 memories, all byte-identical, index identical. 'recall \"commit unstaged\" --limit 3' ranks never-commit-leave-unstaged first. 'check' found 1 dangling link and 77 feedback memories without Why/How. The dangling one was quoted text ('Links: 111 [[slug]] ...' in lib-smoke-before-syntax.md), now reworded, so dangling is 0. The 77 are mostly PARTIAL RECOVERY memories from the wipe; filling them needs evidence, and main tracks that rebuild (PROFILE-WIPE-RECOVERY n25).\nLEFT: the five 10-22 patches (apply-memory-save, -forget, -edit, -split, apply-recall-coverage) were lost in the wipe; heal's copies are noise (EOIE-MEMORY-FEATURES n41). So 'memory save' (type-checked: feedback needs Why/How), 'edit' and 'forget' are unbuilt; recall and check ship. Real progress is about 60%. Blocked on EOIE-MEMORY-FEATURES (dep) stays, because those verbs belong to its F5/F3 typed records, now sized after F2. Next: rewrite 'memory save' first, from n1's shape, as the smallest verb that closes the hand-written-memory gap.")
inl note_of_eoie_memory_verbs_15 () = Note ("EOIE-MEMORY-VERBS", 15u32, "2026-10-30T18:13Z", "kit", "10-30 14:1x kit: 'kit memory save' REWRITTEN AND LANDED UNSTAGED (the lost apply-memory-save patch, rebuilt from n1's shape).\n- Usage: 'kit memory save <memory-dir> <user|feedback|project|reference> <slug> \"<description>\" \"<body>\"|- [--title <text>]'. It writes <slug>.md in the exact frontmatter layout import/render use (name, description, metadata node_type and type) and appends '- [<title or slug>](<slug>.md) — <description>' to MEMORY.md. The body can come from stdin ('-'), with the same quoting fix as agile note.\n- Refusals: an unknown kind; a slug that isn't lowercase words joined by dashes; an empty description or body; an existing file ('edit it instead'); a feedback or project memory without **Why:** and **How to apply:** (the same rule 'memory check' applies); a folder without MEMORY.md.\n- Units: a new crate kit_memory_save (362 lines) holds the verb; kit_memory just routes to it (666 -> 701), since inlining the renderer there took it to 954. Help text in command_spec_domain is updated (same line counts).\n- PROOF on a candidate kit.exe, against a COPY of the real memory folder (197 memories): feedback without reasons refused, exit 2. Save via stdin with --title wrote the file shown (frontmatter, body, both reason lines) and one index line '- [Probe rule](probe-rule.md) — a probe rule'. A duplicate is refused. Re-import gives 198 memories, all identical, index identical, check dangling 0. New CLI test save_writes_a_memory_and_its_index_line_and_refuses_bad_ones in kit_memory/memory_cli.spi (registered); it runs in TRAIN5's notebook phase, since Cells is held by main's window now.\nLeft: edit, forget (and split / recall-coverage from the lost set).")
inl note_of_eoie_memory_verbs_16 () = Note ("EOIE-MEMORY-VERBS", 16u32, "2026-10-30T18:16Z", "kit", "10-30 14:1x kit: 'kit memory forget <memory-dir> <slug>' LANDED UNSTAGED (rewritten from the lost apply-memory-forget). Following archive-never-delete, it moves <slug>.md to <memory-dir>/archive/<slug>.md, verifies the archived copy byte-equal before removing the original, and rewrites MEMORY.md without the memory's index line. The receipt prints 'archived=archive/<slug>.md index_lines=N'. It refuses a missing slug and an existing archive copy. It lives in kit_memory_save beside save (642 lines); kit_memory routes it (728). PROOF on a candidate kit.exe against the memory-folder copy: forgetting the probe memory saved at 14:1x archived it, removed it from the folder and the index, and left MEMORY.md byte-identical to the real one. Re-import gives 197 identical, and a second forget is refused, exit 2. The CLI test was extended (forget, archive content, index restored, refusal). Left: edit (and split / recall-coverage).")
inl note_of_eoie_memory_verbs_17 () = Note ("EOIE-MEMORY-VERBS", 17u32, "2026-10-30T18:20Z", "kit", "10-30 14:2x kit: 'kit memory edit <memory-dir> <slug> description|body \"<text>\"|-' LANDED UNSTAGED (the lost apply-memory-edit, rewritten). It re-reads the memory through the model's entry_of_file, which refuses any file that doesn't render back byte-identical, so an edit can't reshape a memory silently. It replaces one field and re-renders. An edited feedback or project body must still carry **Why:** and **How to apply:** (the save/check rule). When the description changes, the MEMORY.md hook is updated only where the line still ends with the old description, so a hand-written hook is never clobbered. Units: kit_memory_save 933 lines (save, forget, edit), kit_memory 759.\nPROOF on a candidate kit.exe against the memory-folder copy: save a probe, then 'edit ... description \"new hook\"' changes the file and the index hook. An edit dropping the reasons is refused, exit 2. A stdin body edit works. A missing slug is refused. Forget then restores MEMORY.md byte-identical to the real one. The CLI test (memory_cli.spi) now covers edit and refusal too; it runs in TRAIN5's notebooks.\nThe core verbs (save, edit, forget, recall, check) now exist; split and recall-coverage from the lost set are optional extras. Progress 90; closes when installed.")
inl note_of_eoie_memory_verbs_18 () = Note ("EOIE-MEMORY-VERBS", 18u32, "2026-10-31T07:48Z", "kit", "10-31 03:3x kit groom: Active -> Blocked. The only remaining step is installed-is-done: TRAIN5e (job-1793421047190, snapshot 00:31) carries this change, and KIT-AGILE-LOST-UPDATES's last part ships in the train after it. Unblock and verify once kit.exe is installed. Moved so that 'agile next --team kit' stops ranking an install wait above actionable work (43 kit items were Active).")
inl note_of_eoie_memory_verbs_19 () = Note ("EOIE-MEMORY-VERBS", 19u32, "2026-10-31T12:12Z", "kit", "10-31 07:4x kit: installed E14F1F0B passes the memory rows of release-acceptance (save refuses feedback without Why/How, save writes the file and its index line, edit updates the description hook, forget archives and restores the index). 'memory recall' is still unaccepted: it takes a data file, not the memory folder ('needs a readable data file'). Blocked -> Active 95 until a recall row is added and passes.")
inl note_of_eoie_memory_verbs_20 () = Note ("EOIE-MEMORY-VERBS", 20u32, "2026-10-31T14:14Z", "kit", "10-31 10:1x kit: DONE. The recall gap from n19 is closed. release-acceptance.ps1 gained the row 'memory import round-trips and recall ranks by words': it imports the copied memory folder to memory.spi (memories=214 identical=214 index_identical=yes), then recalls 'stopped session wake heartbeat', and the top hit must be idle-needs-wake-source. It PASSES on the installed E14F1F0B (08:1x and 10:0x runs), next to the save/edit/forget rows. Recall's input is the data file 'memory import' writes (recall <data.spi> \"<words>\" [--limit n]), not the memory folder, which is why the 07:4x probe on the folder answered 'needs a readable data file'.")
inl eoie_memory_adapters () = Task ("EOIE-MEMORY-ADAPTERS", "Client adapters (HKT-style interface, one instance per client): Claude (projects/*/memory md + MEMORY.md), Zed (AGENTS.md rules; threads db read-only), Codex (AGENTS.md), OpenCode (AGENTS.md/config; db read on a copy, never credential/account tables), Antigravity (.gemini/antigravity brain/annotations: format to study first), Grok (to find). Import (absorb once) and export (render each client's bootstrap or projection). No secrets ever enter the store (x-api-credentials stays a path reference)", Story, P2, D6, 0u32, Planned, "", "kit,memory,team:kit", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_eoie_memory_adapters () = Planning ("EOIE-MEMORY-ADAPTERS", Minutes 720u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_eoie_memory_adapters_1 () = Note ("EOIE-MEMORY-ADAPTERS", 1u32, "2026-10-17T18:12Z", "memory-formats", "14:10 Claude Code items (measured): C:\\Users\\i574n\\.claude\\projects\\C--home-git\\memory = 56 item files + MEMORY.md; no other project dir has memory. Bytes: LF, no BOM, one final LF, no trailing spaces, 11 files non-ASCII (UTF-8), 783..11,214 B (sum 113,785; largest spiral-macro-gotchas). Frontmatter, fixed key order: name, description, metadata: {node_type: memory, type, originSessionId: uuid, modified: ISO-8601 ms Z} in 55 files (7 lines); inner-jev-autonomy has metadata: {type} only (4 lines). Then one blank line, then body. name equals filename stem in all 56. description max 206 chars: 48 YAML double-quoted (4 with backslash-escaped quotes), 8 plain (agent-sync-daily plain with raw Windows backslashes): keep a quote-style tag, never re-derive. Kinds: feedback 44, project 8, reference 4, user 0. All 52 feedback/project bodies hold **Why:** and **How to apply:** (51 line-initial, 1 inline); the 4 references hold neither. So the GADT why/how fields are sound, but bodies have prose around them: store body as ordered segments (text | why | how) so render is byte-exact. Links: 111 [[slug]] in 53 files, 39 distinct targets, 2 dangling: delete-dangling-freely (from dangling-check-by-stem) and corpus-check-before-packs (from kino-copy-lib-resolution; that is the MEMORY.md title of lib-smoke-before-syntax.md, a renamed slug: check should suggest it).")
inl note_of_eoie_memory_adapters_2 () = Note ("EOIE-MEMORY-ADAPTERS", 2u32, "2026-10-17T18:12Z", "memory-formats", "Claude index and loading: MEMORY.md = 56 lines + final LF, 9,151 B, no heading/frontmatter; every line is: - [Title](file.md) — hook (U+2014 em dash, single spaces), max 245 chars; all 56 items indexed, none missing. Title is free text, not the slug (Agent count -> max-three-agents.md, Corpus check before packs -> lib-smoke-before-syntax.md): a separate field, and index order is a list. Loading: the harness injects MEMORY.md into every session (seen verbatim in this agent's own context; documented cap first 200 lines), item files are read on demand; the agent writes all of it with file tools. Instructions: no ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md, no ~/.claude/rules, no CLAUDE.md/AGENTS.md at C:\\home\\git or any repo root; only in-repo docs (spiral/apps/compiler/AGENTS.md 931 B, its tmp/AGENTS.md 68,825 B) and upstream zed (.rules 12,651 B; CLAUDE.md, AGENTS.md, GEMINI.md are 6-byte symlink-placeholder files reading .rules). In-repo AGENTS.md already sync through git: out of scope for import. ~/.claude/settings.json has no memory keys. Bootstrap: ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md (global, always on), 2-3 lines: memory lives in eoie, read the eoie memory tutorial first, record memories with eoie, not in this folder; and MEMORY.md shrunk to one pointer line so auto-memory stops growing a parallel store. Caution: Grok (compat.claude) and OpenCode (docs: fallback when no global AGENTS.md) also read ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md, so keep that bootstrap client-neutral.")
inl note_of_eoie_memory_adapters_3 () = Note ("EOIE-MEMORY-ADAPTERS", 3u32, "2026-10-17T18:12Z", "memory-formats", "Zed: global rules = %APPDATA%\\Zed\\AGENTS.md (paths::agents_file = config_dir/AGENTS.md, zed/crates/paths/src/paths.rs:319), 908 B LF markdown, no frontmatter, one H2 section: Commit message (the customized built-in commit prompt, moved there by prompt_store/rules_to_skills_migration.rs, which appends Default Rules under ## title H2s and turns non-default Rules into ~/.agents/skills/slug/SKILL.md). Loaded into the native agent system prompt of every conversation (always on); no size cap found in crates/agent. Project rules: only the FIRST match of RULES_FILE_NAMES at a worktree root (.rules, .cursorrules, .windsurfrules, .clinerules, .github/copilot-instructions.md, AGENT.md, AGENTS.md, CLAUDE.md, GEMINI.md; prompts.rs:22). settings.json (2,575 B JSONC, // header) has no rules or profiles fields; its agent block is model/permissions/dock only: adapter never touches it. Legacy Rules LMDB AppData/Local/Zed/prompts/prompts-library-db.0.mdb is kept by the migration (not memory). ~/.agents absent. threads.db (copy, mode=ro, query_only): one table threads(id TEXT PK, summary, updated_at, data_type, data BLOB, parent_id, folder_paths, folder_paths_order, created_at), 8 rows, all zstd = transcripts. Zed has no memory store. Bootstrap: upsert an H2 section ## Memory with one pointer line into the global AGENTS.md; the adapter is a section-preserving edit keyed by H2 title so the Commit message section stays byte-identical.")
inl note_of_eoie_memory_adapters_4 () = Note ("EOIE-MEMORY-ADAPTERS", 4u32, "2026-10-17T18:12Z", "memory-formats", "OpenCode: ~/.config/opencode holds only cli.json (v2 schema, UI prefs) and service.json. PRIVACY: service.json is a plaintext service password: never read or import it (deny by name). No opencode.json(c), no global AGENTS.md, no .opencode dir at C:\\home\\git. opencode.db (db+wal+shm copied; sqlite_master and non-sensitive schemas only): account, account_state, control_account, credential (never read), event, event_sequence, instruction_blob(hash PK, value), instruction_entry(session_id, key, value, removed, time_created, time_updated; PK session_id+key), instruction_state(session_id PK, epoch_start, through_seq, initial_values JSON, current_values JSON), kv, migration, permission, project, project_directory, session_inbox, session_message, session_pending, session_v2, workspace, worktree. instruction_* is a per-session content-addressed snapshot of system-prompt parts: the one state row maps core/codemode, core/skill-guidance, core/date, core/environment to 64-hex hashes into instruction_blob (5 blobs, 17..11,778 B); instruction_entry is empty. No memory feature: nothing to import. Loading (OpenCode docs, not verified on this v2 build): ~/.config/opencode/AGENTS.md plus project AGENTS.md found walking up from cwd, falling back to ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md when no global file exists, plus opencode.json instructions globs; all always on. Bootstrap: create ~/.config/opencode/AGENTS.md with the eoie pointer (this also stops the CLAUDE.md fallback double-load).")
inl note_of_eoie_memory_adapters_5 () = Note ("EOIE-MEMORY-ADAPTERS", 5u32, "2026-10-17T18:12Z", "memory-formats", "Antigravity (.gemini\\antigravity; antigravity-ide is a same-shape twin, plus antigravity-backup): no memory/knowledge store and no user rules on this machine. brain/conversation-uuid/ is per-conversation working state, not memory: .system_generated/{logs/transcript*.jsonl, messages/uuid.json, steps/n/*.txt tool outputs, tasks/*.log}, .user_uploaded/, scratch/ = conversation content, never import. annotations/uuid.pbtxt is protobuf text (title, last_user_view_time {seconds nanos}); antigravity_state.pbtxt holds onboarding/migration enums and installation_uuid; conversations/uuid.db and conversation_summaries.db are SQLite; agyhub_summaries_proto.pb binary. Rules per the bundled builtin/skills/agy-customizations docs: GEMINI.md and AGENTS.md (no frontmatter, always on) walked up from cwd or edited file to repo root; .agents/rules/*.md with frontmatter trigger: always_on or model_decision; workspace roots .agents/ .agent/ _agents/ _agent/; global ~/.gemini/config/ (here only config.json userSettings, projects/uuid.json, empty plugins and sidecars; no rule file; ~/.gemini/GEMINI.md absent). Limits: 24,000 B per rule file after @[label](path) include expansion, truncated on a line boundary; 20,000-token always-on rules budget, overflow demoted to path pointers. Bootstrap: one global rule under ~/.gemini/config/ (confirm the exact filename in the Customizations UI before the first write) with the eoie pointer. PRIVACY: ~/.gemini/oauth_creds.json and google_accounts.json: exclude.")
inl note_of_eoie_memory_adapters_6 () = Note ("EOIE-MEMORY-ADAPTERS", 6u32, "2026-10-17T18:12Z", "memory-formats", "Codex (.codex): config.toml (283 B: approval/sandbox/model, projects c:\\home\\git trust, windows sandbox) has no instruction keys; no ~/.codex/AGENTS.md or AGENTS.override.md; rules/default.rules is exec-policy Starlark prefix_rule(pattern=[...], decision=...) = command permissions, not memory. A memory feature exists but is empty: memories_1.sqlite (copy) has stage1_outputs(thread_id PK, source_updated_at, raw_memory, rollout_summary, rollout_slug, generated_at, usage_count, last_usage, selected_for_phase2, selected_for_phase2_source_updated_at) 0 rows, jobs 0, consolidation_progress 1, _sqlx_migrations 2; no ~/.codex/memories dir (the phase-2 output folder). skills/ holds only .system vendor skills. Loading (Codex docs): ~/.codex/AGENTS.md (AGENTS.override.md wins) plus AGENTS.md from git root down to cwd, always on, combined cap project_doc_max_bytes (default 32 KiB). Import: nothing today; when memories appear, stage1 raw_memory rows are per-thread drafts and the memories folder is the curated output (read that, never the db). Bootstrap: ~/.codex/AGENTS.md with the eoie pointer. PRIVACY: auth.json, .sandbox-secrets/, history.jsonl, sessions/: exclude.")
inl note_of_eoie_memory_adapters_7 () = Note ("EOIE-MEMORY-ADAPTERS", 7u32, "2026-10-17T18:12Z", "memory-formats", "Grok (.grok) HAS a memory store, memory-v2 (no memory key in config.toml: on via env or managed setting). Per scope (global/, workspaces/git-437d9324/): topics/*.md curated (11 workspace topics 722..16,945 B, 0 global; LF, no frontmatter: # Title, one-line summary, ## sections, - bullets); observations/_inbox/*.md pending (19; YAML frontmatter schema_version: 2, type, topic_hint, keywords, aliases, session_id, from_turn, through_turn, extraction_model, prompt_version, created_at epoch, job_id, observation_ordinal); archive/dream_hex/ consumed observations named session__tFROM-TO__nK.md (30-day retention); MEMORY.md generated, never write it; index.sqlite and memory_state.sqlite: leave alone. Loading (docs/user-guide/13-memory.md): both MEMORY.md indexes injected once per session, 8 KiB per scope; topics read on demand; capture after each turn into the inbox; /dream or auto-dream folds into topics; the model may edit topics/ and _inbox/ with file tools. Rules (12-project-rules.md, always on): ~/.grok/rules/*.md (absent), ~/.claude/rules and ~/.claude/CLAUDE*.md by compat.claude, [paths] extra_rule_dirs, then Agents.md, Claude.md, CLAUDE.md, CLAUDE.local.md, AGENT.md, AGENTS.md from repo root to cwd (cwd only outside git; needs folder trust). Import: topics as a topic kind (title, summary, sections), observations as an observation kind with provenance. Bootstrap: ~/.grok/rules/eoie-memory.md, and either set memory_v2.enabled = false or keep one global topic whose body is the eoie pointer.")
inl note_of_eoie_memory_adapters_8 () = Note ("EOIE-MEMORY-ADAPTERS", 8u32, "2026-10-17T18:12Z", "memory-formats", "Adapter interface proposal: a client is a record over a type-level carrier f (result with a per-client error union, later an io effect): import : root -> f (list stored); render : list stored -> f (list {path; text}); bootstrap : tutorial_ref -> f (list {path; edit}) with union edit = Replace text | UpsertSection {heading; text} | AppendLine text, so foreign content in Zed/Codex/Antigravity AGENTS.md files survives. stored = Stored :: forall k. memory k -> stored, and beside the kind GADT a source layout record, not part of the kind: {client; scope = Global | Workspace slug; path; quote = Plain | Double; meta = {node_type option; origin_session option; modified option}; body = list segment}. Renderers derive frontmatter from layout so bytes round-trip; normalization is a separate explicit verb. Kinds beyond user/feedback/project/reference: topic (Grok topics), observation (Grok inbox, with session/turn provenance), rule (an H2 section of an AGENTS.md). Indexes are derived: Claude MEMORY.md = ordered list of {title; file; hook}; Grok MEMORY.md is never rendered. Deny-list is a type: import walks only an allow-list of paths per client (Claude memory dir, Zed AGENTS.md, Grok topics and _inbox, Codex memories dir, OpenCode and Antigravity rule files); everything else is unrepresentable rather than filtered.")
inl note_of_eoie_memory_adapters_9 () = Note ("EOIE-MEMORY-ADAPTERS", 9u32, "2026-10-17T18:12Z", "memory-formats", "Round-trip test blocks: (1) 56 Claude items render byte-identical; (2) MEMORY.md byte-identical, em dash and order kept; (3) inner-jev-autonomy keeps its 4-line frontmatter (no node_type/session/modified); (4) double-quoted descriptions with escaped quotes and the plain one with raw backslashes keep their quoting; (5) the 11 non-ASCII files keep UTF-8; (6) the one inline How to apply body survives; (7) a feedback without why fails to typecheck (negative), references without Why/How typecheck; (8) links: 111 parsed, 39 targets, exactly 2 dangling reported, corpus-check-before-packs gets lib-smoke-before-syntax suggested via its index title; (9) Zed AGENTS.md: UpsertSection Memory twice is idempotent and Commit message stays byte-identical; (10) the 11 Grok topics and 19 observations re-render byte-identical; (11) import, render, import is a fixed point; (12) an allow-list test: a tree seeded with service.json, auth.json, .credentials.json, oauth_creds.json yields zero items. Privacy flags: OpenCode service.json plaintext password; .claude/.credentials.json; .codex/auth.json and .sandbox-secrets; .grok/auth.json; .gemini/oauth_creds.json and google_accounts.json; OpenCode credential/account tables; Antigravity brain/ and every threads/sessions store are transcripts; Grok observations carry session ids and the agent-sync topic a scheduler id; Claude items carry originSessionId (fine in a private repo, strip before anything public); x-api-credentials stays a path pointer.")
inl note_of_eoie_memory_adapters_10 () = Note ("EOIE-MEMORY-ADAPTERS", 10u32, "2026-10-24T23:44Z", "eoie", "19:43 Provisional estimate 720m from the grooming rule (EOIE-AGILE-CHECK-FIELDS note): D6 at 0%. Replace it with milestone sizing.")
inl note_of_eoie_memory_adapters_11 () = Note ("EOIE-MEMORY-ADAPTERS", 11u32, "2026-11-01T13:16Z", "kit", "11-01 09:1x kit groom: received from the closed EOIE-HEAL-EDIT-HOOK: the edit-time drift hook for the OTHER clients (the Claude hook is installed and working). DoD addition: each adapter installs its client's equivalent of kit-style-check and a pipe test shows it reporting a ratchet finding.")
inl note_of_eoie_memory_adapters_12 () = Note ("EOIE-MEMORY-ADAPTERS", 12u32, "2026-11-01T14:46Z", "kit", "11-01 10:4x kit: candidate adapter target: akitaonrails/ai-memory (markdown wiki + SQLite FTS5, cross-provider handover). An import adapter would read its wiki pages as untrusted data into typed memory records with provenance ai-memory:<page>; see KIT-DEFINITION.")
inl eoie_memory_bootstrap () = Task ("EOIE-MEMORY-BOOTSTRAP", "Swap to bootstrap (needs the user's go): each client keeps one bootstrap entry ('memory lives in eoie: run eoie memory recall; tutorial at eoie/spec/...') and eoie in git becomes the synced source for the laptop and the work machine; a pull plus 'eoie memory export --client <c>' sets up a new machine. Rollback: export renders the full Claude memory dir again", Story, P1, D4, 0u32, Planned, "EOIE-MEMORY-FEATURES", "kit,memory,team:kit,kit", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_eoie_memory_bootstrap () = Planning ("EOIE-MEMORY-BOOTSTRAP", Minutes 240u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_eoie_memory_bootstrap_1 () = Note ("EOIE-MEMORY-BOOTSTRAP", 1u32, "2026-10-17T18:24Z", "bundle-wisdom", "14:24 prior art in bundle-cleanup-backup-20260926\\bundle_eoie_v20260723-alpha70462.zip state\\bootstrap.spi (Sep 2026): eoie's own bootstrap as typed Spiral. bootstrap_transition Boot -> FallbackSelected -> CandidateBuilt -> CandidateVerified -> Promoted; an execution_route chain Candidate -> RecentFallback -> ArchiveFallback -> HostEscapeRejected (host escape is a typed refusal, not an option); promotion only with typecheck AND e2e AND cold differential; rules such as CompilerPinnedByIdentity, FallbackBeforeHostEscape, GeneratedRustIsMirror, CrateUnderBudget. A ready shape for the bootstrap entry and for 'eoie memory export' rollback.")
inl note_of_eoie_memory_bootstrap_2 () = Note ("EOIE-MEMORY-BOOTSTRAP", 2u32, "2026-10-22T21:53Z", "eoie-lane", "17:53 same grooming rule: the swap reads typed memory features, which do not exist yet, and its title says it needs the user's go. It depends on EOIE-MEMORY-FEATURES and stays Planned until then (the go stays the user's call; nothing here asks for it).")
inl note_of_eoie_memory_bootstrap_3 () = Note ("EOIE-MEMORY-BOOTSTRAP", 3u32, "2026-10-24T23:43Z", "eoie", "19:43 Provisional estimate 240m from the grooming rule (EOIE-AGILE-CHECK-FIELDS note): D4 at 0%. Replace it with milestone sizing.")
inl note_of_eoie_memory_bootstrap_4 () = Note ("EOIE-MEMORY-BOOTSTRAP", 4u32, "2026-11-02T14:01Z", "x", "2026-11-02 09:59 x: DECISION RECORD (typed; groom of user waits, user 11-02: 'decisions waiting on me get met autonomously/deterministically with tests/benchs/typefeatureset modeling/backups'). kind=ByTest. QUESTION: Does the user give the go to swap each client's memory to a single bootstrap entry backed by kit? RULE (written before acting): The local swap is allowed iff (1) import->render is byte-identical for 100% of the Claude memory files plus MEMORY.md, (2) `kit memory recall` returns every slug, and (3) a fresh session that has only the bootstrap recalls profile-wipe-2026-10-28. Only the cross-machine sync (a git push of kit) is the user's step. BACKUP/UNDO: Full memory dir archived with scratch-archive.ps1 before the swap. Undo = `kit memory export --client claude` re-renders it. OWNER: kit. NEXT: Stays Planned behind EOIE-MEMORY-FEATURES / EOIE-MEMORY-MODEL. VETO: the user can reverse this at any time; the backup above is the undo.")
inl eoie_union_reflection () = Task ("EOIE-UNION-REFLECTION", "Drift from the GPT era (user 2026-10-17: eoie maps unions to numbers; with reflection and the GADT/prototype feature set we could do it in fewer lines): measured 2026-10-17 across 493 eoie .spi files, 16 modules use the pattern: 67 pipe_index/pipe_member/pipe_lookup calls, 79 '| Nu64 =>' arms and 22 literal 'a|b|c|...' name tables. Example: agile subcommands live in 5 hand-synced tables (the union in agile_subcommand_domain, its name string, the u64 index match, agile_full_usage_error's usage text, agile_operand_shapes in eoie_agile_mutation, plus the pipe list in agile_entry); adding a verb means editing all of them. Target: one union per concept; case names by reflection (lib reflection, with case enumeration added to the lib/compiler if it can't enumerate nullary cases at compile time yet); parsing by a fold over the cases; usage and operand shapes in one exhaustive spec : case -> {name; shape; summary} prototype match the compiler checks for completeness. The numeric layer goes away. Proof: eoie tests unchanged, generated crates stay under the 1k gate, LOC before/after per module. Blocks new verbs such as agile links (EOIE-MEMORY-LINKS)", Story, P1, D5, 100u32, Done, "", "eoie,drift,reflection,gadt", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_eoie_union_reflection () = Planning ("EOIE-UNION-REFLECTION", Minutes 180u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl dib_modernize () = Task ("DIB-MODERNIZE", "Retire every .dib remnant straight to .spi test blocks, skipping the .livemd stage (user 2026-10-17: the livemd phase that lasted until test blocks was never enough). Measured 2026-10-17: 0 .dib files remain in our repos, 14 files mention them. Kino's F# route (fsi_chain.ex and fsi_session.ex resolve #!import x.dib from the dotnet-repl era; ~15 tests build .dib notebook fixtures); polyglot/apps/perf/Perf.livemd imports lib/fsharp/Notebooks.dib and Testing.dib, which no longer exist (dead); history mentions in apps/compiler/tmp/NEXT.md and eoie ingest_frontier's port-now classifier. Delete the route and fixtures, or port what's still live", Story, P1, D4, 60u32, Planned, "", "kino,polyglot,migration,team:main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_dib_modernize () = Planning ("DIB-MODERNIZE", Minutes 105u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_dib_modernize_1 () = Note ("DIB-MODERNIZE", 1u32, "2026-10-17T18:33Z", "main-thread", "14:33 main-thread: TODO-AS-SOURCE first slice. This item's code-local TODO block is at spiral/apps/kino/lib/spiral/kino/fsi_chain.ex:184 (at the .dib import route); eoie agile todos harvests it with id DIB-MODERNIZE.")
inl note_of_dib_modernize_2 () = Note ("DIB-MODERNIZE", 2u32, "2026-10-17T18:41Z", "main-thread", "14:41 main-thread scope measured. Kino .dib surface: Document.parse_dib is the default parser for any unknown notebook extension (notebook.ex parse), cli_export falls back to the old dib-export CLI subcommand, the domain exports parse_dib (spi/document.spi and main.spi), directives.spi has a dib error kind, and fsi_chain.ex resolves #!import x.dib (TODO block placed there). Tests: 21 use .dib fixtures (notebook_test 13, display 3, fsi_session 3, notebook_report 1, document 1); 15 are Spiral notebook-runner behaviours and 6 exercise the F# FSI route. Many test the notebook-to-.spi export feature itself (spi is written only when the cells pass). So the modernization is architectural: .spi is the only source (test blocks are the cells), notebooks are output only (ipynb/html), and the .dib/.livemd parsers and the interactive F# kernel retire together (also the bold-native-goals direction). Order: (1) port the 15 Spiral runner tests to .spi fixtures with test blocks; (2) drop parse_dib and dib-export; (3) decide the FSI kernel (polyglot Perf.livemd and the i574n.github workflow livemd still have cells: port them to .spi or scripts); (4) drop parse_livemd. Each step is proven by the Kino suite (~2.5 min).")
inl note_of_dib_modernize_3 () = Note ("DIB-MODERNIZE", 3u32, "2026-10-19T12:21Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 08:21 helper-lane lap 1: re-measured against today's tree: Kino now lives at spiral/apps/cells (apps/kino is gone; the TODO block is in apps/cells/lib/spiral/cells/fsi_chain.ex). .dib surface: notebook.ex:464 still falls back to Document.parse_dib for any unknown extension; mentions per file document.ex 3, domain.ex 2, fsi_chain.ex 7, fsi_session.ex 2, notebook.ex 2, spi/document.spi 1, spi/main.spi 1. Tests touching .dib: notebook_test 12 (spiral cells accumulate..., a failing cell still writes the ipynb..., an fsharp notebook runs through the host callback, host cells share the job slots, rust contract flags reach the target, a package missing beside the notebook ..., the spiral lib package is mounted once, a //> _ cell is not compiled..., cells compile together, a --test static cell's definitions reach later cells, --real cells become the main_real module, at most four cells run at a time), fsi_session 7 lines, display 1, document 1, notebook_report 1. Step 1 (port the Spiral-runner tests to .spi fixtures) therefore covers 10 of the 12 notebook_test cases; the fsharp host-callback and host-slots tests belong to step 3 (FSI kernel).")
inl note_of_dib_modernize_4 () = Note ("DIB-MODERNIZE", 4u32, "2026-10-19T13:11Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 09:11 helper-lane lap 2: first port drafted: lab/DIB-MODERNIZE/accumulate_test_port.exs is the .spi version of notebook_test.exs's first case (spiral cells accumulate, tests keep opens, and spi is written only when they pass). Mapping: the #!spiral cells become a .spi with a test demo static prelude (open testing), a plain definition (inl value) and a targeted test block value_is_one; the assertions that survive are the semantic ones (the prelude alone is never compiled; the test program carries both the prelude's open and the earlier definition; an .ipynb report is written), while the spi-export assertions (demo.spi written only when cells pass, without opens or calls) disappear because the .spi is now the source. It reuses the test module's own helpers (compile, rustc_ok, run_value, calls?, lines). Not wired into the suite yet.")
inl note_of_dib_modernize_5 () = Note ("DIB-MODERNIZE", 5u32, "2026-10-19T14:28Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 10:28 helper-lane lap 3: second case mapped: notebook_test.exs:80 (a failing cell still writes the ipynb and does not write spi) needs no new .spi test, because notebook_report_test.exs:30 (a .spi run reports each test block and a failing block fails the run) already asserts the same semantics on a .spi: error message =~ exited 1, the .ipynb is written, its metadata counts failed 1. The spi-not-written half disappears with .dib (the .spi is the source). The only assertion it lacks is the failing cell's output ename SpiralError; that 4-line assertion is staged as lab/DIB-MODERNIZE/failing_case_assertion.exs, to append after notebook_report_test.exs line 60 when the ports are wired (unverified whether .spi runs set ename, so it rides the same queued Cells-suite run as the accumulate port). Ports needed so far: 1 new test (accumulate) + 1 assertion; 8 of the 10 Spiral-runner .dib cases remain to map.")
inl note_of_dib_modernize_6 () = Note ("DIB-MODERNIZE", 6u32, "2026-10-19T15:32Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 11:32 helper-lane lap 4: ported cases 3 and 4 of notebook_test.exs as lab/DIB-MODERNIZE/flags_package_ports.exs (not wired, same helpers as the file: run_value, rustc_ok): (3) rust contract flags reach the target (notebook_test 218) becomes a .spi whose ///> rust -cd near-token line sits on a test block; the target callback must see tool rust, contract empty, deps near-token at *, and a source without ///> lines that carries both definitions; the old two-cell split (the flagless prior cell compiled alone) has no .spi counterpart, so that assertion drops. (4) a package missing beside the notebook (notebook_test 272) is the same test on note.spi with ///- --package vendor/pkg as its header: the mounted package.spiproj must list pkg-. Both keep only behaviour the .spi path has. Mapped so far: 4 of the 10 Spiral-runner cases (1 new test + 1 assertion + these 2).")
inl note_of_dib_modernize_7 () = Note ("DIB-MODERNIZE", 7u32, "2026-10-19T15:52Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 11:52 helper-lane lap 5: mapped the remaining six Spiral-runner .dib cases, so step 1's plan is complete: (5) the spiral lib package is mounted once (308) - same test on a .spi with ///- --package <lib>, the mounted spiproj must list spiral- exactly once; (6) a //> _ cell is not compiled and still exported (336) - .spi form is a block with ///> _ above it; keeps the not-compiled assertion, drops the export assertion (the .spi is the source); (7) cells compile together (370) - .spi with two test blocks, asserting their compiles overlap; (8) a --test static cell's definitions reach later cells, a --test cell's do not (404) - already .spi-native as test x static = versus test x =, port 1:1; (9) --real cells become main_real (443) - .spi with a .spir sibling or ///- --real blocks, keeping the main_real*- spiproj and the no-real-in-top-down assertions; (10) at most four cells run at a time (508) - ten test blocks in one .spi, same 2..4 concurrency bound. Totals: 2 cases need no port (2 is covered, 6 loses its export half), 8 become .spi tests (1, 3, 4 drafted in lab; 5, 7, 8, 9, 10 mechanical). The F# host-callback and host-slots cases stay for step 3.")
inl note_of_dib_modernize_8 () = Note ("DIB-MODERNIZE", 8u32, "2026-10-19T15:52Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 11:52 helper-lane: wrote ports 5, 6, 7, 8 and 10 as lab/DIB-MODERNIZE/remaining_ports.exs (lib mounted once, ///> _ block not compiled, blocks compile together with overlapping compiles, static block definitions reach later blocks while plain ones do not, at most four blocks at a time), using only the notebook_test.exs helpers (compile, rustc_ok, run_value, calls?, lines). With the accumulate port, the ename assertion and the flags/package ports, everything but the --real case (9, needs a .spir sibling fixture) is drafted. Unrun: they wait for one coordinated Cells change with lane 2 and a mix test.")
inl note_of_dib_modernize_9 () = Note ("DIB-MODERNIZE", 9u32, "2026-10-23T13:41Z", "main", "2026-10-23 09:41 main: step 1 mapping complete. All 10 Spiral-runner .dib cases of notebook_test.exs now have a .spi counterpart in lab/DIB-MODERNIZE: accumulate_test_port.exs (case 1), failing_case_assertion.exs (case 2, an assertion appended to notebook_report_test.exs), flags_package_ports.exs (cases 3-4), and remaining_ports.exs (cases 5-9 plus the last one). Added today: 'spi: a _real.spir sibling becomes the main_real module', the port of notebook_test.exs:443 (--real cells). real.spi holds twice (real twice_real), real_real.spir holds twice_real and its real test block, and it keeps the original's assertions (no real definition in a top-down program, main_real*- before main in package.spiproj, the real test compiled as real spiral_cells_real (), a real test's code never reaching a later block), with 2 runs instead of 3 because only test blocks compile in a .spi. The file parses (Code.string_to_quoted!). Remaining: wire the ports into notebook_test.exs in place of the 10 .dib cases and run the Cells suite in a gap after cells-pack lands. Its suite verdict stays separate, so a port failure can't hold the pack's other steps. Then step 2 (drop parse_dib and dib-export), and steps 3-4 (FSI kernel, parse_livemd).")
inl note_of_dib_modernize_10 () = Note ("DIB-MODERNIZE", 10u32, "2026-10-23T13:43Z", "main", "2026-10-23 09:43 main: the wiring is staged and queued. lab/DIB-MODERNIZE/wire-ports.ps1 (for a Cells copy) retires the 10 .dib cases by exact test name (424 lines), appends the 9 port tests before the module end, and inserts failing_case_assertion after notebook_report_test.exs line 60. On a scratch copy both files parse and notebook_test.exs has 13 tests: the 4 left for steps 3-4 (livemd accepted, spi: false, fsharp host callback, host slots) plus the 9 ports. lab/DIB-MODERNIZE/suite.ps1 runs mix test on just those two files in a fresh New-CellsRoot copy as job main-dib-suite (job-1792762997519, after cells-pack, before hopacsota). Rule: if every test passes, wire-ports applies to the tree in the next apps/cells landing; any failing port is fixed in lab and rerun, never landed red.")
inl note_of_dib_modernize_11 () = Note ("DIB-MODERNIZE", 11u32, "2026-10-23T14:31Z", "main", "2026-10-23 10:31 main: step 2's exact surface, read from today's tree (Grep over apps/cells *.ex and *.spi). notebook.ex:487 parses any unknown extension with Document.parse_dib; this becomes an error naming .spi and .ipynb. notebook.ex:349 falls back to the 'dib-export' CLI subcommand, which goes. document.ex:6-8 parse_dib and domain.ex:42-44 parse_dib go. spi/document.spi:106 inl parse_dib and spi/main.spi:11 export 'parse_dib' go, which regenerates the Gleam domain module spiral_cells@domain, so this step is a compile plus the Cells suite. fsi_chain.ex:184-187 (the #!import x.dib route) and :244 (.dib -> .livemd resolution), with fsi_session.ex's .dib docs, belong to step 3 (the F# kernel), not step 2. Order unchanged: main-dib-suite proves the 9 ports and the assertion; then step 2 as one Cells patch with its suite; then step 3.")
inl note_of_dib_modernize_12 () = Note ("DIB-MODERNIZE", 12u32, "2026-10-23T22:16Z", "main", "2026-10-23 18:16 main: main-dib-suite (job-1792766412909) had 2 red tests in the ports but exited 0, because suite.ps1 dropped mix's exit code and filtered out the summary. suite.ps1 now writes the full log to spiral-bin\\cells-suite\\dib-suite.log and exits with mix's code. Red 1, 'spi: test blocks compile together': a port bug. The compile stub tagged sides with source =~ 'left ()', and the right block's program also holds 'inl left () = 1i32', so both blocks reported :left. It now uses calls?/2, which matches a whole trimmed line. Red 2, 'spi: a _real.spir sibling becomes the main_real module': length(runs) == 2 failed, and the filtered output gave no count. The full log from main-dib-suite-2 (job-1792793766788, queued after cells-pack-2) decides the cause.")
inl note_of_dib_modernize_13 () = Note ("DIB-MODERNIZE", 13u32, "2026-10-24T00:17Z", "main", "2026-10-23 20:17 main: status fixed from 0% Planned to Active 60 by the rule 'implemented and staged in a pack or queued gate, awaiting review'. Where: ports wired (lab/DIB-MODERNIZE), red 1 fixed, rerun main-dib-suite-2 in chain A.")
inl note_of_dib_modernize_14 () = Note ("DIB-MODERNIZE", 14u32, "2026-10-24T03:08Z", "main", "2026-10-23 23:08 main grooming: main-dib-suite-2 finished at 21:29 with suite exit 1 (log spiral-bin\\cells-suite\\dib-suite.log, root cells-dib-root). Its remaining_ports rewrite of notebook_test.exs and notebook_report_test.exs ran; read the log for which tests stayed red before the next attempt.")
inl note_of_dib_modernize_15 () = Note ("DIB-MODERNIZE", 15u32, "2026-10-24T03:54Z", "main", "2026-10-23 23:54 main: dib-suite-2's exit 1 was environmental, not a test result. mix could not build because MIX_BUILD_PATH (spiral-bin\\mix-builds\\dib-suite) held a priv symlink to the old pre-nesting copy root (cells-dib-root\\apps\\cells\\priv, gone), so File.cp_r failed on tlds.txt before any test ran. Fixed: cells-root.ps1 gains Remove-DanglingBuildLinks (removes only links whose target is gone), and suite.ps1 calls it after setting MIX_BUILD_PATH. Requeued as main-dib-suite-3.")
inl note_of_dib_modernize_16 () = Note ("DIB-MODERNIZE", 16u32, "2026-10-24T11:49Z", "main", "2026-10-19 laps folded from the deleted history ledger (docs/agents/history/2026-10-19-backlog-laps, HISTORY-BACKLOG-LAPS-DRIFT): lap 6 18:26 (subagent): re-read: remaining_ports.exs staged; lands in apps/cells/test -> next: lane 2 applies, then mix test | lap 7 19:54 (subagent): re-read: remaining_ports.exs staged; Cells is now helper lane 1's -> next: apply in a Cells window, mix test")
inl note_of_dib_modernize_17 () = Note ("DIB-MODERNIZE", 17u32, "2026-10-24T14:40Z", "main", "main 10:4x: main-dib-suite-3 (07:12) ran for real after the dangling-link fix: 45/46 passed. The one red is the ported case \"spi: a _real.spir sibling becomes the main_real module\" (notebook_test.exs as rewritten by remaining_ports in the cells-dib-root copy): assert length(runs) == 2 got 3, so the tree now makes a third run (likely an extra compile or target run for the main_real sibling) that the port does not expect. Next: read which three runs the tree makes for that case (tree notebook_test has the case; compare its assertion) and update the port, then requeue the suite.")
inl note_of_dib_modernize_18 () = Note ("DIB-MODERNIZE", 18u32, "2026-10-24T16:53Z", "main", "main 12:5x: main-dib-suite-4 (11:56) is green: 46 passed, after aligning remaining_ports.exs's _real.spir case with the tree's own .dib case (3 runs: definitions, the real test, the top-down test). The ports are proven in the cells-dib-root copy; next is landing them into apps/cells/test (they replace the .dib cases), which is a Cells test edit gated by the tree suite.")
inl note_of_dib_modernize_19 () = Note ("DIB-MODERNIZE", 19u32, "2026-10-24T16:54Z", "main", "main 12:5x: ports wired into the tree (lab wire-ports.ps1 -Cells apps/cells -Apply): 10 .dib cases retired from notebook_test.exs and the accumulate/flags/remaining ports appended; notebook_report_test.exs gets the failing-case assertion after line 60. Backups (hash C84F76ECCC73 / 142AB84C7B6F) in the session scratchpad dib-land-backup. Tree check queued: main-serial-dib-ports-tree (run_suite on both files); on any red, restore both files from the backups.")
inl note_of_dib_modernize_20 () = Note ("DIB-MODERNIZE", 20u32, "2026-10-24T21:14Z", "main", "main 17:1x: lab drain done under the lab rule (land first, then remove). The ports are wired into apps/cells/test (tree check green 13:58, uncommitted for the user). Reference search by stem with ripgrep over spiral, eoie src, x, docs, fiscal scripts, cells-pack and the session scratch (the earlier Get-ChildItem walk hung 2 h and was stopped). Hits: only the lab folder itself (the positive control), backlog notes, old scratch reports, and the finished 10-23 cells-pack chain; no live code. The folder was untracked, so it was moved, not deleted: %TEMP%\\lab-attic\\DIB-MODERNIZE-20261024-1713 (6 files, hashes verified). spiral/lab/DIB-MODERNIZE no longer exists.")
inl note_of_dib_modernize_21 () = Note ("DIB-MODERNIZE", 21u32, "2026-10-28T01:33Z", "main", "10-27 21:33 main: Groom 10-27 21:2x (WIP rule, set before applying). main's Active items with no note since 10-24 were classed by the evidence in the tree, not by age alone. (1) SHIPPED -> Done: the item's pass is in the installed tree core (apps/compiler/spiral_compiler.fs, core 8660C34A11F33259, the installed 5m' core), found by its own identifier or anchor, and an accept has passed on that core since (accept-6, run single-flight-20261027-173417, 0 blocking cells). (2) COLD -> Paused: no lane is driving it this week and nothing is queued. Its last note keeps the next step, and it resumes when a lane picks it or its condition fires. (3) KEPT Active: a lane is on it this week, or demand is waiting on it. (4) Blocked: only the user's commit or push unblocks it.\n Verdict for this item: (2) COLD, so Active -> Paused. The next step is the one in its latest note above.")
inl note_of_dib_modernize_22 () = Note ("DIB-MODERNIZE", 22u32, "2026-10-29T22:32Z", "main", "10-29 18:3x main: PROVISIONAL fields filled while the item is Blocked or Paused (fill-all-task-fields): estimate 105m (D-base rule: base(D4) x (1 - 60%)). The owner corrects them on resume.")
inl note_of_dib_modernize_23 () = Note ("DIB-MODERNIZE", 23u32, "2026-10-31T20:55Z", "main", "10-31 16:53 main, pause re-decision (KIT-PAUSE-RULE-REWORK; rule R3 is now 'never auto-pause for silence'): Paused -> PLANNED. It was paused by the old silence/WIP rule, not by a blocker: its notes name no external condition (a 10-30 lab-archive note blocks nothing: restore the draft from the archive into the repo when picked). Back in agile next at its priority; deps, if any, gate it as usual.")
inl note_of_dib_modernize_24 () = Note ("DIB-MODERNIZE", 24u32, "2026-11-03T08:58Z", "main", "main 11-03 04:3x SCOPE: polyglot's .dib remnants are read from the local copy (polyglot left the org 11-03). They are absorbed only when they carry something no spiral/i574n.github file has; the rest are ruled out with POLYGLOT-RETIRE.")
inl kino_rename () = Task ("KINO-RENAME", "Rename the Kino project folder and code to something generic (user 2026-10-17 dislikes the kino name), timed with KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR: the supervision part rewritten in Spiral compiling to Gleam 1.19 (the new compiler) removes Elixir from the repo, and that native core should give the name. Parallel or performance-heavy parts (rayon-style) compile to Rust. Precedent: the X client started as apps/spindle and became x/apps/client (xc); no memory recorded that rename, and backlog items X-CLIENT-THREADS and X-CLIENT-AGENT-JSON still say spindle", Story, P2, D4, 100u32, Done, "", "kino,naming", "current", "current")
inl lib_usage_diagnostics () = Task ("LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS", "Tooling to diagnose lib/spiral usage across our repos: which lib functions and test cells are never reached from any app, sample or repo; duplicated and generated code (the CLI lane found that ~9k of spiral.rs's 24k lines come from inlining the same parser 3 times); and expansion hot spots per generated crate, so the 1k-line packaging limit stays useful. Part of the branded tool suite (with the formatter); reuse and grow lib instead of per-app copies", Feature, P1, D5, 50u32, Planned, "LIB-USAGE-VERB", "lib,tooling,dogfood,main,team:main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_lib_usage_diagnostics () = Planning ("LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS", Minutes 450u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_lib_usage_diagnostics_1 () = Note ("LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS", 1u32, "2026-10-17T18:39Z", "main-thread", "14:39 main-thread first census (text-based, job tmp lib-usage.ps1 and lib-usage.tsv, 96 s over every tracked .spi/.spir in spiral/eoie/polyglot/dice/alphabet/x): lib/spiral has 1,361 top-level inl/let definitions in 70 modules. 41 are never referenced anywhere, 20 of them FParsec-style trailing-underscore variants in fparsec.spi (p_string_, many1_satisfy_, sep_by1_, not_followed_by_ ...: strong deletion candidates), plus seq 5, testing 4 and listm' 3. 671 are used only inside their own file (candidates for private helpers or inlining). Limit of the text method: backend.run_target shows as unused although it is used everywhere, because it comes into scope by a route a grep can't see. So the real tool must ask the compiler's name resolution (e.g. a --references mode next to --source-blocks that emits definition -> use sites per package) and report per repo, per package and per lib cell; later, generated-code duplication per crate for the 1k gate.")
inl note_of_lib_usage_diagnostics_2 () = Note ("LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS", 2u32, "2026-10-17T19:02Z", "main-thread", "15:02 main-thread, user 2026-10-17: inlining sprawl is wanted where it buys speed, so the tool must not just count expanded lines. Classify each expansion as hot or cold and measure it: an A/B per backend (inl vs let for the same definition) through the harness benchmark rows. Measured today: eoie agile_subcommand_shape, a reflection fold over 18 cases, was an inl called from 7 usage-error sites, and agile_mutation.rs went 614 -> 1567 lines. That is cold code (error paths), so the copies gain nothing at run time and cost rustc time and the 1k gate; as a let it is emitted once. Hot loops are the opposite case: specialization removes dispatch and allocation. Report per crate: expansion factor, hot or cold by benchmark evidence, and a suggested inl/let flip. Longer term the compiler could outline cold paths itself (like Rust's cold attribute).")
inl note_of_lib_usage_diagnostics_3 () = Note ("LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS", 3u32, "2026-10-18T12:19Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 08:19 main-thread: (1) text census v2 in lab/LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS/census.ps1: resolves package.spiproj flat modules (backend-, base-, common-, trace-), open lines and qualified module.name uses, separates test-block uses, verdicts unused/tests-only/file-local/lib-only/used-outside (running). (2) compiler-backed references, in the tree, not built: SPIRAL_REFERENCES_OUT=<file> makes prepass (shared section) record def rows at FInl/FRecInl and use rows whenever a RawV resolves outside local scope, linked by E object identity, flushed at process exit; lab/LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS/references.ps1 aggregates them. Plan: a compile-only harness run with the variable set (no -ReuseCompile) gives every sample's real resolution, then diff the two censuses.")
inl note_of_lib_usage_diagnostics_4 () = Note ("LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS", 4u32, "2026-10-18T13:16Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 09:16 main-thread text census v2 (lab/LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS/census.ps1, 781 s at BelowNormal over 1,985 tracked .spi/.spir in spiral, eoie, polyglot, dice, alphabet, x, tibia): 1,408 lib definitions in 80 modules: used-outside 404, lib-only 254, file-local 401, tests-only 118, unused 231. Flat modules, open lines and qualified uses resolved, so run_target is used-outside (95 lib uses, 2 outside), which v1 called unused. Unused by module: async 25, rust 22, threading 19, runtime 19, leptos 18, sm' 17, file_system 10, listm' 8. Spot check of 5 async ones (base_let', block_on_futures_lite, block_on_async_std, block_on_tokio_send, stream_collect_tokio): each occurs only in its own header and definition across all 7 repos, so they are real. Fixes made on the way: ordinal (case-sensitive) indexes, operator counts from a per-file token table. Next: the compiler-backed census (qualified uses recorded since the 08:5x source) to cross-check, then deletion candidates per module.")
inl note_of_lib_usage_diagnostics_5 () = Note ("LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS", 5u32, "2026-10-18T14:26Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 10:21 SPIRAL_REFERENCES_OUT census over the full compile-only suite (668 s): 1,422 lib defs; unused 639, lib-only 400, file-local 376, used-outside 7. The used-outside count of 7 conflicts with the text census v2 (404). Suspected cause: use rows carry tree-copy sample paths that the aggregator classifies as lib or file-local. Check the path normalization in lab/LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS/references.ps1 before trusting the unused column.")
inl note_of_lib_usage_diagnostics_6 () = Note ("LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS", 6u32, "2026-10-18T14:27Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 10:31 used-outside=7 explained, and it's not a path bug. Of 38,282 use rows, only 19 come from outside lib and target lib/spiral. The compile-only samples import their own samples/core (10.7k rows) and the VS Code Plugin core in deps (7.8k), not lib/spiral. So this suite is the wrong corpus for outside use. Next run: SPIRAL_REFERENCES_OUT over the consumers (Cells gate on lib, blog engine, eoie, apps/*), then aggregate. Second gap: 7,016 outside use rows have no target. Those are names reached through open (create/set/index after open am) and nominal constructors (cell_array, f64_array), which PrepassReferences does not attribute yet. Until it covers open-resolved names and nominal defs, the unused column overcounts.")
inl note_of_lib_usage_diagnostics_7 () = Note ("LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS", 7u32, "2026-10-18T14:28Z", "main-thread", "Correction: note 6 was written at 10:27, not 10:31 (the eoie stamp 14:27Z is the right one).")
inl note_of_lib_usage_diagnostics_8 () = Note ("LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS", 8u32, "2026-10-18T14:32Z", "main-thread", "10:32 patch plan for the reference recorder's open/nominal gaps (spiral_compiler.fs; not applied, no build today). (1) Local open: prepassModule_open (~52893) folds the opened module's members into env.term.env. RawV (~53148) records a use only when the name is absent from env.term.env, so every name reached through a local open is dropped as if it were a local. Fix: record whenever definitions.TryGetValue e succeeds, and keep the env.term.env guard only for unresolved rows (locals are EV values and are never in definitions). (2) Unattributed defs: FNominal/FNominalRec (~53314) and FPrototype (~53337) add terms without PrepassReferences.define, so constructors such as cell_array/f64_array and prototype names stay unresolved; call define on each nominal constructor's term and on the prototype's EForall. (3) Still unexplained: top-level open am uses of create/set in samples main.spi, unresolved although 226 other rows resolve to deps core/am. Check whether package re-prepass rebuilds E (identity loss) by dumping both sides on one sample. Verify with: SPIRAL_REFERENCES_OUT over one sample that uses a local open plus a nominal, expect 0 unresolved rows for those names; then rerun the consumer corpus census.")
inl note_of_lib_usage_diagnostics_9 () = Note ("LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS", 9u32, "2026-10-18T14:34Z", "main-thread", "10:34 plan steps (1) and (2) applied in the tree, unbuilt: PrepassReferences.used_unless_local (RawV records a use when its E is a known def, even if the name is in env.term.env, which covers local open), and nominal_rec plus FPrototype call PrepassReferences.define (constructors at the nominal's name range). Changes only the recorder, gated on SPIRAL_REFERENCES_OUT. Verify in the next pack: one sample with a local open and a nominal, then the consumer corpus census. Step (3), identity loss on top-level open am, is still open.")
inl note_of_lib_usage_diagnostics_10 () = Note ("LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS", 10u32, "2026-10-18T14:35Z", "main-thread", "10:35 step (3) resolved, and it's not identity loss. The unresolved create/set/index uses in samples main.spi (package core-) are prototypes: samples/core/operators.spi defines them as prototype plus instance (instance create array = ...). Before today's edit FPrototype never called define, so the in-tree FPrototype define covers them. Expected after the next build: the unresolved outside rows (7,016) drop to roughly the builtin and type-level names.")
inl note_of_lib_usage_diagnostics_11 () = Note ("LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS", 11u32, "2026-10-18T14:39Z", "main-thread", "10:39 lab/LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS/references.ps1 now prints the unresolved use rows grouped by name. Today's run: 11,966 rows over 1,886 names. The top names are module heads (sm' 520, optionm' 292, leptos 284, resultm 264, am' 216, sm 181, listm 172) and union constructors (Nil 474, Cons 375, None 211, Some 180, Ok 144). Constructors are covered by the in-tree nominal define. Module heads get a new in-tree recorder rule: used_unless_local skips EModule values, since the member is already recorded through module_member. This is also unbuilt. Next build check: rerun references.ps1 and expect the top unresolved names to be builtins and type-level names only.")
inl note_of_lib_usage_diagnostics_12 () = Note ("LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS", 12u32, "2026-10-18T15:42Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 11:42 main-thread: the recorder edits from note 9 onwards (used_unless_local, define on nominal constructors and prototypes, skipping EModule heads) were pushed by the user at 15:29Z in f5e487e0; spiral CI compiles them now, the first build of them anywhere. New in the tree since then, unbuilt: the ProcessExit flush retries File.AppendAllLines up to 100 times at 20 ms on IOException, because parallel compiler processes appending to one SPIRAL_REFERENCES_OUT file could lose a process's rows. New lab script lab/LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS/consumer-census.ps1 runs the consumer corpus: the Cells --gate over the 40 lib/spiral .spi files that have test blocks plus the 9 blog engine modules, in chunks of 20 paths (mix.bat command-line limit), with SPIRAL_CELLS_COMPILER=process so every compiler inherits the variable, memo off, a temp MIX_BUILD_PATH. It throws if no rows were recorded (compiler without the recorder), then runs references.ps1. Run it after the next install that contains the recorder.")
inl note_of_lib_usage_diagnostics_13 () = Note ("LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS", 13u32, "2026-10-19T12:30Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 08:30 lap 1 grain: the recorder edits (used_unless_local, nominal constructors and prototypes, module heads skipped, and the ProcessExit flush retry) were built and installed with compiler pack 4 (2026-10-18 22:55 build, accepted 10-18 single-flight-20261018-234436) and ride pack 5; lab/LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS/references.ps1 turns SPIRAL_REFERENCES_OUT into the census Next: HEAVY QUEUE: one census over the Cells lib build with SPIRAL_REFERENCES_OUT set, to list never-reached lib definitions")
inl note_of_lib_usage_diagnostics_14 () = Note ("LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS", 14u32, "2026-10-19T12:58Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 08:58 lap 2 grain: verified the installed compiler carries the recorder (UTF-16 literal SPIRAL_REFERENCES_OUT found by byte search in spiral-bin/bin/single-flight/SpiralCompiler/Release/net11.0/SpiralCompilerCore.dll, built 10-18 22:57; note: a naive Unicode.GetString search misses it at an odd byte offset). The HEAVY QUEUE row for this item is concretely pwsh lab/LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS/consumer-census.ps1 (lib files with tests + blog engine + x test files, chunks of 20, process compiler, memo off). Edited the script to throw right after the first chunk when no reference rows were recorded, instead of after the full corpus, so a recorder-less compiler costs one chunk of the heavy slot, not the whole run. Next: after the run, read consumer-census.tsv and list never-reached lib definitions per file")
inl note_of_lib_usage_diagnostics_15 () = Note ("LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS", 15u32, "2026-10-19T13:43Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 09:43 lap 3 grain: references.ps1 now prints the census goal directly: never-reached lib definitions grouped per module, most first, with their names in line order (cut at 160 chars), right after the verdict tally, so the queued consumer-census run ends with the list this item asks for. Checked on the 10-18 census file (pre-fix recorder, lib-only corpus, so only indicative): unused 639 of 1,422 definitions, top modules leptos 46, runtime 45, parsing 41, async 40, sm' 36, physics 30, rust 30, am' 30; real-dialect defs are recorded (sm'_real show_real lib-only 3, format_real 2 + 1), so .spir modules are not falsely unused. Next: after the heavy census, compare unused per module against the 10-18 numbers and turn the top modules' unused names into deletion candidates (dangling check by stem across repos first)")
inl note_of_lib_usage_diagnostics_16 () = Note ("LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS", 16u32, "2026-10-19T15:09Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 11:09 lap 5 grain: first deletion candidates checked by stem across every repo under C:\\home\\git (spi, spir, livemd, dib): of the 17 sm' names the 10-18 text census called unused, the definitions index_of_char, is_white_space, builder_append_line, char_is_alphanumeric, os_string_as_ref, base64_decode, utf8_decode occur only at their own header and definition in sm'.spi (plus the matching /// headers in sm'_real.spir); format_custom' and encoding_1252' are already gone; the rest are operator names (~#, ~##, ++\\\\#...) that need the compiler-backed census. Not deleted: lib is frozen in the core switch window, and the switch may move sm' itself. The queued census row is made concrete: pwsh lab/LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS/consumer-census.ps1 after the switch accept (counts on the old core would be stale at once).")
inl note_of_lib_usage_diagnostics_17 () = Note ("LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS", 17u32, "2026-10-19T15:28Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 11:28 lap 6 grain: stem check extended to runtime and file_system (the 10-18 census's unused lists): all 29 names occur only in their own lib module across every repo under C:\\home\\git; the only outside hits are look-alikes with their own definitions (eoie bootstrap_policy nominal process_exit_code, tictactoe 2023 lib_spi copy of path_display). Candidates: runtime 19 = the clap builder leftovers (arg_overrides_with, arg_num_args, arg_value_name, value_parser_path_buf, value_parser_expr, matches_get_occurrences, matches_values_of) and the .NET Process wrapper family (new_process, process_start, process_exit_code, process_has_exited, process_kill, process_begin_error_read_line, process_begin_output_read_line, process_add_output_data_received, process_add_error_data_received, process_wait_for_exit_async, event_data) plus execute_retry; file_system 10 = file_stream, directory_info_exists, directory_info_creation_time, file_move, path_buf_from, path_buf_strip_prefix, path_display, path_buf_is_symlink, get_directory_name, init_trace_file. With lap 5's 7 sm' names: 36 verified deletion candidates, all waiting on the switch all-clear (lib frozen) and the census to confirm no route the text scan misses.")
inl note_of_lib_usage_diagnostics_18 () = Note ("LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS", 18u32, "2026-10-19T15:42Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 11:42 progress bookkeeping: the recorder is built and installed, the consumer census and references.ps1 are ready, and 36 deletion candidates are verified by stem; left: the compiler-backed census after the switch, then the deletions. Progress 40.")
inl note_of_lib_usage_diagnostics_19 () = Note ("LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS", 19u32, "2026-10-19T15:46Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 11:46 lap 7 grain: stem check on async + threading (40 plain names from the 10-18 unused list). 38 occur only in their own module across every repo; the futuresales livemd hit is Elixir code, not Spiral. Two are false positives of the text census: cancellation_token (runtime.spi 198, 210, 835, 1115; networking.spi 150) and mutex_guard_ref_mut (runtime.spi 918-1005) are used, most likely in type position, where a term and a type share one name and the text scan counts only one of the two. That is one more case for the compiler-backed census. Running candidate total: 7 sm' + 29 runtime/file_system + 38 async/threading = 74 definitions with no use anywhere, the two false positives removed.")
inl note_of_lib_usage_diagnostics_20 () = Note ("LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS", 20u32, "2026-10-19T16:19Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 12:19 lap 8 grain: post-switch re-check of the 36 sm', runtime and file_system candidates on today's migrated lib: each still occurs exactly at its header and definition (sm' also its _real.spir header), and no .spi/.spir/.livemd under C:\\home\\git gained a use; the only outside hits are own definitions (eoie bootstrap_policy and proxy_failure_classification process_exit_code, tictactoe lib_spi copies) and backlog text. So the switch did not change the candidate list. Next: stage lab/LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS/delete-unused.py (dry run by default) for the 36, applied after the compiler-backed census confirms")
inl note_of_lib_usage_diagnostics_21 () = Note ("LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS", 21u32, "2026-10-19T16:44Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 12:44 lap drive8 grain: deletion staging for the 36 verified candidates: lab/LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS/delete-unused.py (dry run by default). Per name it finds the single /// ### header, requires the next line to be that definition, takes the indented body plus trailing blank lines, and re-checks that the name occurs exactly twice in its file; --apply writes only if every name passes (all or nothing, LF kept). Dry run on today's lib: 36 definitions, 209 lines (sm' 57, runtime 95, file_system 57), 0 problems; spot-checked builder_append_line 775-794 ends before builder_replace. Apply order: compiler-backed census confirms, evidence note, --apply, then lib Cells --gate over sm', runtime, file_system plus an apps/spiral compile (heavy queue)")
inl note_of_lib_usage_diagnostics_22 () = Note ("LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS", 22u32, "2026-10-19T16:46Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 12:46 lap 9 grain: delete-unused.py now covers all 74 verified candidates: added the 38 async and threading names (census.tsv verdict unused, minus the false positives cancellation_token and mutex_guard_ref_mut and the 4 primed names the stem check did not cover). One had no /// ### header (async.spi default_cancellation_token right after cancellation_token), so the script now also takes a lone bare definition and expects 1 occurrence instead of 2. Dry run: 74 definitions, 415 lines, 0 problems")
inl note_of_lib_usage_diagnostics_23 () = Note ("LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS", 23u32, "2026-10-19T17:23Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 13:23 lap 10 grain: post-switch recheck plus a by-stem dangling check across every sibling repo (dangling-check-by-stem rule): delete-unused.py dry run still 74 definitions, 415 lines, 0 problems; a word-bounded search for all 74 names over C:\\home\\git\\*\\**\\*.spi,spir,livemd finds hits outside lib in only 4 files, all local homonyms, not lib consumers: eoie bootstrap_policy/outcome.spi and proxy_failure_classification define their own process_exit_code, tictactoe lib_spi/rust/util.spi:527 defines its own path_display (its own lib copy), and a futuresales livemd output line mentions an Elixir start_child. So the text census and the cross-repo stem check agree. Next: compiler-backed confirmation (consumer-census.ps1, heavy, queued), evidence note, apply, lib Cells --gate + apps/spiral compile")
inl note_of_lib_usage_diagnostics_24 () = Note ("LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS", 24u32, "2026-10-19T17:44Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 13:44 lap 11 grain: joined delete-unused.py's 74 (module, name) pairs with the compiler-backed references-census.tsv of 10-18 (SPIRAL_REFERENCES_OUT rows through references.ps1): all 74 are present and all 74 have verdict unused with SameFile 0, Lib 0, Outside 0. With today's text dry run and the cross-repo stem search (0 consumers) that is three independent views agreeing; the one gap is that the compiler census predates the core switch and covers the builds it was fed, so consumer-census.ps1 (lib test files + blog engine + apps, heavy, queued) remains the post-switch confirmation before --apply")
inl note_of_lib_usage_diagnostics_25 () = Note ("LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS", 25u32, "2026-10-19T22:48Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 18:48 lap 19 grain: consumer-census.ps1's corpus (lib tests, blog/engine, x) leaves out dice, alphabet and polyglot, which main migrated to core v1 at 18:3x; a whole-word text scan of their 43 .spi/.spir/.livemd files (deps/target/dist skipped) finds 0 uses of any of delete-unused.py's 74 candidates, bare or qualified, so those three repos add no keeper. Next: the compiler-backed consumer-census run (a full lib gate, main's queue), then delete-unused.py --apply")
inl note_of_lib_usage_diagnostics_26 () = Note ("LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS", 26u32, "2026-10-21T22:22Z", "x", "2026-10-21 18:22 x: re-checked x against delete-unused.py's 74 candidates on tonight's tree (after the post_id slice and glyphs.split_all): a whole-word scan of x/apps (36 .spi/.spir/.livemd files, target/_build/deps skipped), bare or qualified, finds 0 uses. x adds no keeper; the 10-19 census still holds for x.")
inl note_of_lib_usage_diagnostics_27 () = Note ("LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS", 27u32, "2026-10-21T23:07Z", "main", "19:07 text census extended to the repos the 10-19 scan left out (ripgrep over .spi/.spir/.livemd, ignored dirs skipped, whole-word against all 74 delete-unused.py candidates): tibia 0 hits; spiral/apps 0 hits; eoie 6 hits, all of process_exit_code in bootstrap_policy/outcome.spi and proxy_failure_classification/failure_classification.spi, which is eoie's own 'nominal process_exit_code = i32' (its packages use |core- and its own contracts, never lib/spiral's runtime.process_exit_code), so not a keeper. With dice, alphabet, polyglot (10-19) and x (10-21 18:22, by x) every repo is now covered and none keeps any of the 74. Remaining before delete-unused.py --apply: the compiler-backed consumer census (a full lib gate), and the user's OK, since --apply deletes definitions from tracked lib files.")
inl note_of_lib_usage_diagnostics_28 () = Note ("LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS", 28u32, "2026-10-22T05:37Z", "main", "01:36 main: lab run outputs moved to the run cache, per the lab rule that run outputs never sit in lab: 1 file(s) (logs, notebooks, diffs, metrics/gate/timing TSVs) are now under %LOCALAPPDATA%\\spiral-bin\\runs\\lab-outputs\\LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS\\ at the same relative paths, sha256 checked. Scripts and probes stay in lab/LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS until the item lands.")
inl note_of_lib_usage_diagnostics_29 () = Note ("LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS", 29u32, "2026-10-22T06:35Z", "main", "02:35 main: lab drained into tooling, the item's own aim ('part of the branded tool suite'). In apps/compiler/tmp/scripts: lib-usage-census.ps1, lib-usage-consumers.ps1 (calls lib-usage-references.ps1; spiral root resolved from its new depth) and lib-usage-references.ps1, all with default outputs now in the temp dir instead of beside the script, so a run never writes into the repo. lib-usage-delete-unused.py finds lib/spiral at parents[4] and still writes only with --apply after the census confirmation; parse checks are clean and the paths resolve. The 10-18 census.tsv and references-census.tsv (outputs) moved to %LOCALAPPDATA%\\spiral-bin\\runs\\lab-outputs\\LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS (sha256 checked). Folder deleted. Still open as before: the compiler-backed consumer census (a full lib gate), then the user's OK for --apply (it deletes definitions from tracked lib files; text census 0 keepers across every repo, notes 18:22 and 19:07).")
inl note_of_lib_usage_diagnostics_30 () = Note ("LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS", 30u32, "2026-10-22T09:59Z", "main", "2026-10-22 05:59 main: deciding --apply myself (memory decide-autonomously, 10-21: rule-based deletions are mine, the user commits). The text census is done in every repo with 0 keepers (notes of 10-21 18:22 and 19:07). Rule fixed now: apply to the tree only if (a) the dry run on the current tree reports 0 problems, (b) on a lib copy with the 74 definitions removed, the Cells gates of the 5 touched files show no new failing row against tonight's tree baselines (sm' 45 of 45; file_system 18 of 18; runtime 6 of 7 with one flaky TypeScript row; threading 1 of 1; async 0 of 0), and (c) a full lib sweep of the copy (every lib file with tests, apps/cells/spi/lib_sweep.ps1) shows no failure that the tree does not also show, run after the pack's heavy jobs. Then land through lib_land.ps1 with its tree-hash guard. (a) PASSED: 433 lines in 74 definitions, 0 problems. The copy is %TEMP%\\lib-unused, applied with a copy of the script pointed at it: sm' 2050 -> 1993, runtime 1265 -> 1170, file_system 1346 -> 1289, threading 462 -> 372, async 501 -> 367 lines. (b) is queued behind tibia's block as job-1792663132617-main-unused-gates.")
inl note_of_lib_usage_diagnostics_31 () = Note ("LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS", 31u32, "2026-10-22T10:11Z", "main", "2026-10-22 06:11 main: the queued gate job was granted a compile window in the gap between tibia's PR-21 and D21 live-gui windows (about 06:00-06:10), so it ran into tibia's block. Stopped it and released its window by hand (job stop left it held); no gate had finished, and tokens.spi and the lib copies are unchanged. Requeue only after tibia's explicit 'block end' (memory live-runs-alone, updated).")
inl note_of_lib_usage_diagnostics_32 () = Note ("LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS", 32u32, "2026-10-23T00:26Z", "main", "2026-10-22 20:26 main: the combined lib gate (job-1792713100977) went red on a bug in the removal script, not in the lib. delete-unused-copy.py matched definitions with '{name}\\b', and since ' is a non-word character, 'nominal file_stream''' counted as file_stream's definition. It deleted the helper nominal and kept the unused 'inl file_stream', so file_system.spi:60 had an unbound type 'file_stream''', and the package-wide type check failed every test in the gated files (26 of 26 in the first). Fixed: the definition boundary is (?![\\w']), like the use counter's. A '/// ### name' section may hold primed helper nominals ahead of the definition; the section goes whole only when the helper has no use outside it. On the tree the dry run removes lines 60-71 (header, nominal file_stream', inl file_stream), with 0 problems over 74 definitions (442 lines). The combined gates were requeued as job-1792715175438, with the retype census after them.")
inl note_of_lib_usage_diagnostics_33 () = Note ("LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS", 33u32, "2026-10-24T11:49Z", "main", "2026-10-19 laps folded from the deleted history ledger (docs/agents/history/2026-10-19-backlog-laps, HISTORY-BACKLOG-LAPS-DRIFT): lap 4 10:32 (subagent2): waiting: consumer census queued -> next: unused defs per module vs 10-18 | lap 12 14:06 (subagent2): re-dry-run after base.spi edit (14:04, +50/-2): still 74 defs/415 lines/0 problems; none of the 74 names in base.spi's added lines -> next: consumer-census.ps1 post-switch (heavy queue), then evidence note + apply | lap 13 14:31 (subagent2): consumer-census precondition checked: the installed Release SpiralCompilerCore.dll (13:56, after the pack 8 rollback) contains the SPIRAL_REFERENCES_OUT recorder (source line 52934), so the queued census will record rows on the first chunk -> next: consumer-census.ps1 (heavy queue), then apply | lap 14 14:59 (subagent2): post-apply gate list fixed: the 74 deletions touch 5 lib files (async 21, runtime 19, threading 17, file_system 10, sm' 7), all with test blocks, so the proof after --apply is one mix spiral.notebook --gate over those 5 plus an apps/spiral build (lib consumers per the corpus rule) -> next: consumer-census (queue), evidence note, apply, 5-file gate | lap 17 16:43 (subagent2): unchanged (74 defs, 5 files); waits on consumer-census -> next: main's queue | lap 21 19:18 (subagent2): after helper lane 1's uncommitted lib edits: delete-unused.py dry run still 74 definitions / 415 lines / 0 problems, and a whole-word scan of lib finds no use of any candidate (1 hit is the header of return_await', a different name) -> next: consumer-census (main's queue)")
inl note_of_lib_usage_diagnostics_34 () = Note ("LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS", 34u32, "2026-10-27T08:54Z", "main", "10-27 04:54 main: 10-27 04:54 main groom: fresh status (stale since the 10-19 laps). The tools are IN THE TREE: apps/compiler/tmp/scripts/lib-usage-references.ps1 (48 lines, 10-22; turns SPIRAL_REFERENCES_OUT rows into a per-definition census) and lib-usage-census.ps1 (109 lines, 10-25). The 10-19 lap was 'waiting: consumer census queue'. Per tmp/AGENTS.md the census needs the CONSUMER builds (Cells over lib, apps, eoie/kit, blog), because matrix samples import their own core. Next: one run over those consumers on a node, and record never-reached lib definitions as candidates (not deletions).")
inl note_of_lib_usage_diagnostics_35 () = Note ("LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS", 35u32, "2026-10-30T14:14Z", "main", "10-30 10:14 main, BACKLOG-DANGLING-1030 R3: Active -> Paused, progress kept at 50%. No note since 10-27 and no live job, so it isn't real WIP. Nothing blocks it; the concrete next step is in the previous note (owner main). Resume by picking it, never by re-verifying.")
inl note_of_lib_usage_diagnostics_36 () = Note ("LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS", 36u32, "2026-10-31T20:57Z", "main", "10-31 16:53 main, pause re-decision (KIT-PAUSE-RULE-REWORK; rule R3 is now 'never auto-pause for silence'): Paused -> PLANNED. It was paused by the old silence/WIP rule, not by a blocker: its notes name no external condition (a 10-30 lab-archive note blocks nothing: restore the draft from the archive into the repo when picked). Back in agile next at its priority; deps, if any, gate it as usual.")
inl note_of_lib_usage_diagnostics_37 () = Note ("LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS", 37u32, "2026-11-01T22:55Z", "main", "main lib-reshape 11-01 19:0x TEXT CENSUS on today's :: lib (typed rows: %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\spiral\\LIB-RESHAPE\\usage_census.spi; 4,056 top-level definitions incl. per-backend duplicates, 11 repos, eoie skipped as a kit junction). Verdicts: UsedOutside 850, LibOnly 532, FileLocal 2,428, Unused 246. KNOWN LIMITS (why the compiler-backed LIB-USAGE-VERB is still needed): operators are not tokenized (operator definitions show as Unused: sm'_operators 10/10, base 13, parsing 8 are mostly operators), and bare names of flat modules overcount (trace/common 'main', base 'append'). FINDINGS, ranked for keep/test/drop: (1) modules with ZERO uses outside lib: physics (208 defs, 185 file-local: a sample, not a lib module; move to a sample or an app), xpath_eval 146, channel 20, otp_supervisor 20, xsd_constraints 19, cli_flags 20, decimal 22, lustre 22, float_text 15, erlang_map 10, erlang_text 4, store 6, util 2; xpath_eval/xsd_constraints are reached by fiscal through xpath/xsd (check with the compiler census before any drop). (2) Unused names per module (non-operator, real candidates): rust 25 (rc/ref_cell/box_pin family), leptos 24 (route/action/memo/resource family), am' 11 (vec_for_each'', vec_chunks, slice'', iter_collect'', map'), threading 8 (rw_lock family, concurrent_stack), async 7, networking 6 (tcp client/listener), listm' 6 (replicate, map4, item_, index_of_ ...). (3) Where lib is load-bearing: fiscal is the biggest consumer by count (xml::xml_node 52,416, element_children 21,856 via generated XSD bindings), sm'::length (fiscal 1,208, kit 87, x 53), base pair/unit/use, common::return (kit 1,219). Tests to add first go where outside uses are high and the module has no :# rows (next census column). (4) par' had 0 outside uses: renamed to parallel in LIB-RESHAPE R1a rather than dropped (it's the only portable fan-out helper).")
inl eoie_state_scale () = Task ("EOIE-STATE-SCALE", "eoie backlog state bloat is intended (user 2026-10-17): state/agile.spi is now 1.15 MB and grows with every note; keep it as a realistic append-heavy log to simulate an Apache Iggy-style stream and measure how the compiler scales on real log data (parse/typecheck time of state/agile.spi per MB). Track the growth curve; refactor only if a measured threshold breaks a workflow", Story, P3, D4, 35u32, Planned, "", "kit,scale,iggy,team:kit", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_eoie_state_scale () = Planning ("EOIE-STATE-SCALE", Minutes 195u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_eoie_state_scale_1 () = Note ("EOIE-STATE-SCALE", 1u32, "2026-10-17T18:46Z", "iggy-research", "14:47 iggy-research (1/3) state/agile.spi measured read-only at 14:41 (before the IGGY-EVAL notes): 1,203,199 bytes, 1,588 lines, longest line 18,177 chars (the EOIE-DOGFOOD title). Rows: 337 Task (556,734 bytes, avg 1.65 KB), 1,040 Note (623,301 bytes, avg 600 B), 170 Planning; the rest is ~40 lines of union schema. Notes per UTC day: 10-14 306, 10-15 363, 10-16 118, 10-17 253 so far. Size over git commits: 10-08 43 KB, 10-09 53 KB, 10-12 163-180 KB, 10-13 313-416 KB, 10-14 686 KB, 10-15 791 KB, 10-17 06:53 996 KB, 13:00 1,091 KB, 14:45 1,213 KB: about 150-270 KB/day lately, so ~6-8 MB in a month if linear. Write path (eoie src/agile_note_model/model.spi): a note is not appended at EOF but spliced after the task's last note (notes_insertion), the whole text is re-read, compared to the original and replaced atomically (state_written; StateChangedSinceRead = optimistic concurrency, nothing written, caller retries); note counts and insertion points come from full-text scans. So every write is O(file) read + scan + rewrite. Receipts today: note writes 8-10 ms at 1.2 MB, show 4 ms: cheap now. Already broken threshold: the file is ~66k tokens, over the agent Read tool's 25k-token limit, so agents cannot read it whole (eoie agile show is the only path). typecheck_receipts.spi last attested agile.spi at 994,972 bytes.")
inl note_of_eoie_state_scale_2 () = Note ("EOIE-STATE-SCALE", 2u32, "2026-10-17T18:46Z", "iggy-research", "14:47 iggy-research (2/3) What to measure (no runs done by this lane). M1 compiler: wall time and peak RSS of the installed compiler's parse + typecheck of agile.spi (eoie agile check --compiler and the compiler's own phase timings), cold (session torn down, per BENCH-FRESH-PAIR) and warm, over the 11 git snapshots 43 KB..1.09 MB plus the live file, plus synthetic 2x/4x/8x copies (bindings renamed, same row mix). Fit time = a * MB^b: b > 1.2 flags superlinear behaviour; split the cost by driver (top-level binding count ~1,550, longest line 18 K chars, total string literal bytes) with single-variable synthetic files. M2 eoie verbs: elapsed_ms of note, set, show, list --json per snapshot (all receipts already print it), p50/p95 over a day of real use. M3 growth: bytes/day and notes/day from note timestamps (a one-line census; the ledger could log it daily). M4 contention: count StateChangedSinceRead rejections; today they are not recorded anywhere, so the note/set receipt should log retries.")
inl note_of_eoie_state_scale_3 () = Note ("EOIE-STATE-SCALE", 3u32, "2026-10-17T18:46Z", "iggy-research", "14:47 iggy-research (3/3) Thresholds that would warrant an Iggy-style segment layout (any one, measured twice): compiler check of state over 5 s warm or 15 s cold, or b over 1.3 at 4x size; peak RSS over 1.5 GB for the check; an agile write verb p95 over 100 ms; file over ~8 MB (about a month at the current rate) or a line over 64 KB; more than 1 StateChangedSinceRead retry per hour across lanes. Layout then: state/agile/ with an active segment for current task rows (projection) and append-only note segments sealed at ~1 MB or per month (notes appended at EOF, never spliced), each sealed segment immutable with its own typecheck receipt (typecheck_receipts.spi already keys modules by bytes + hash, so sealed segments are typechecked once and skipped), a small offset index per segment (item id -> note seqs) so show reads one index instead of scanning, and readers keeping their own offsets. To keep the scale signal after a split, freeze the monolithic file at each threshold crossing as a benchmark corpus, so compiler scaling stays measurable on real log data, which is the point of this item.")
inl note_of_eoie_state_scale_4 () = Note ("EOIE-STATE-SCALE", 4u32, "2026-10-17T20:21Z", "main-thread", "16:21 main-thread, user decision 2026-10-17: measure first (11 git snapshots + 2x/4x/8x copies, warm/cold typecheck time and memory) and switch to the segment layout only when a threshold breaks (check over 5 s warm, memory over 1.5 GB, file over 8 MB, worse-than-linear growth); the single file stays a compiler-scaling corpus.")
inl note_of_eoie_state_scale_5 () = Note ("EOIE-STATE-SCALE", 5u32, "2026-10-21T00:02Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-20 20:02 eoie lane: first measured break. state/agile.spi is 2.62 MB (1.15 MB on 10-17, about +0.45 MB/day) with 3,458 top-level definitions, and 'eoie agile check' fails for every lane: the compiler's --check of state/prompt.spi overflows the stack (0xC00000FD in bundle_blocks/fold_offset_term) because each module parses on a pool thread (main 20:0x, COMPILER-MODULE-PARSE-STACK: parse on a 512 MB thread). Per the user's 10-17 decision the single file stays a compiler-scaling corpus, and this break is that corpus doing its job, so the compiler fix comes first and eoie keeps the layout; main suggested splitting agile.spi into modules (by item range, or open vs Done). That stays the fallback: switch to a segment layout if check breaks again after the parse-stack fix, or when a 10-17 threshold trips (check over 5 s warm, memory over 1.5 GB, file over 8 MB, about 12 days at today's rate). Writes are unaffected.")
inl note_of_eoie_state_scale_6 () = Note ("EOIE-STATE-SCALE", 6u32, "2026-10-21T00:46Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-20 20:46 eoie lane: the check break is healed by the compiler (COMPILER-MODULE-PARSE-STACK installed, single-flight 546B267E40B5A0C1); eoie agile check exits 0 on the shared backlog again (main 20:5x). Single-file layout kept per the 10-17 decision.")
inl note_of_eoie_state_scale_7 () = Note ("EOIE-STATE-SCALE", 7u32, "2026-10-21T13:08Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-21 09:08 eoie team, growth check against the 10-17 thresholds: state/agile.spi is 2,991,721 bytes (2.62 MB at 10-20 20:02, so about +0.7 MB/day lately, up from ~0.45). Write verbs measured today: note 32-83 ms, set 148-373 ms (set includes the runtime attestation), add 26-83 ms; the 100 ms p95 threshold is about plain writes, and set's attestation stays under 0.4 s. No threshold broken. At this rate the 8 MB threshold arrives around 10-28; recheck then, or sooner if a write verb's p95 passes 100 ms. agile next took 2.7 s on the installed release 23; re-measure after r26 (read-once) installs.")
inl note_of_eoie_state_scale_8 () = Note ("EOIE-STATE-SCALE", 8u32, "2026-10-22T09:30Z", "eoie", "05:30 growth check: state/agile.spi is 3,788,562 bytes (2,991,721 at 10-21 09:08, so about +0.8 MB/day; tonight 02:30-05:30 alone added 139 KB from about 50 notes, the heaviest night so far). Write verbs measured tonight from their own receipts: note 24-131 ms (most 50-90), add 133-188 ms, set 205-504 ms (set includes the runtime attestation), all on a 3.6-3.8 MB state file; note latency has not grown with the ~25% size increase since 10-21.")
inl note_of_eoie_state_scale_9 () = Note ("EOIE-STATE-SCALE", 9u32, "2026-10-22T21:46Z", "eoie-lane", "17:46 measured against the thresholds of note 3 (iggy-research, written 10-17, before this measurement). File: 4,384,569 bytes (3.6x the 10-17 size), 5,545 lines, longest line 18,201 chars, no line over 64 KB, so the size thresholds (8 MB, 64 KB line) hold. Write latency from today's eoie-lane receipts (elapsed_ms): about 37 agile note/add writes, p50 ~74 ms, p95 ~163 ms; 7 agile set writes at 260-494 ms. Each write rewrites the whole file (bytes_before/after ~4.3 MB), and set also runs its validation. The 'agile write verb p95 over 100 ms' threshold is crossed, measured across a whole day of receipts, so by note 3 the segment layout is now warranted. Next step (design, no compile): a measured breakdown of a write (read/parse, validate, rewrite) on a copy of the state, then the active-segment layout from note 3 (state/agile/ with an active segment plus sealed segments), so a note appends to the active segment instead of rewriting 4.3 MB. Status moves to Active; the rewrite itself is a compile item.")
inl note_of_eoie_state_scale_10 () = Note ("EOIE-STATE-SCALE", 10u32, "2026-10-22T21:48Z", "eoie-lane", "17:48 write breakdown on a copy of state (4.3 MB; the live state untouched; the copy is deleted afterwards): agile show (read) p50 40 / p95 51 ms; agile note (append) p50 71 / p95 112 ms; agile set p50 283 / p95 290 ms (n = 10, 10, 5). So a note's whole-file rewrite costs ~30 ms over a read, and set spends ~210 ms more than note, which is its validation (receipts say validation=runtime-attested), not file size. Consequence for the layout decision: segmenting state cuts the ~30 ms rewrite plus part of the 40 ms parse, but set's cost is in what it validates. The next measurement is to profile set's validation before choosing the segment layout. Method: repeat each verb n times on a copy and read elapsed_ms from the receipts; eoie has no verb for that yet (EOIE-BENCH-VERB), so the script was not kept.")
inl note_of_eoie_state_scale_11 () = Note ("EOIE-STATE-SCALE", 11u32, "2026-10-22T21:51Z", "eoie-lane", "17:51 scaling measured (installed release binary, copies only): with the same schema and one Task row, a 3.4 KB state gives set 29-49 ms and note 2-4 ms; the 4.3 MB copy gives set 279-325 ms and note 60-86 ms in the same run. So both write verbs scale with file size: set ~ 27 ms fixed + ~65 ms/MB, note ~ 17 ms/MB. This corrects note 10's guess that set's extra time is validation: reading the code, set runs typed per-line export calls on all 5,545 lines, copies and rewrites the file, reads it back and compares, then the attest step snapshots and hashes the state, and every step is per-byte or per-line. Decision by the 10-17 threshold (p95 over 100 ms, crossed): the segment layout is justified, and the measured target is writes against a small active segment, set ~30 ms and note single-digit ms.")
inl note_of_eoie_state_scale_12 () = Note ("EOIE-STATE-SCALE", 12u32, "2026-10-22T21:56Z", "eoie-lane", "17:56 layout model drafted, not yet compiled (scratch f5/src/agile_state_segment_model; previews wait until F ends, since no compile of mine runs beside F). Task and Planning rows stay in the task module; notes append to an active segment module notes_<n>, which seals at 256 KiB, and the next note opens notes_<n+1>. write_target is TaskModule | ActiveSegment n | SealedThenNew (n, n+1); rewritten_bytes states the cost model: a note rewrites only the active segment (~250 KB instead of 4.3 MB), a task edit only the task module (~1.7 MB today). From the measured slopes (note ~17 ms/MB, set ~65 ms/MB + 27 ms), that predicts a note at ~7 ms and a set at ~140 ms, under the 100 ms p95 for notes and halving set. A further split of the task module (Done tasks sealed) is the next lever for set. Tests are written; Cells preview after F; standards 0 findings.")
inl note_of_eoie_state_scale_13 () = Note ("EOIE-STATE-SCALE", 13u32, "2026-10-22T21:57Z", "eoie-lane", "17:57 composition of the 4.39 MB state (task row plus its notes, by task status): Active 127 tasks 1.94 MB (44%), Done 320 tasks 1.69 MB (38%), Planned 65 tasks 0.43 MB, Paused 22 tasks 0.26 MB, Blocked 5 tasks 0.05 MB; Planning rows 27 KB; schema 1.5 KB; no orphan notes. Sealing Done tasks with their notes into a sealed module takes 38% off what an Active task's set rewrites (with the note segments, set's rewrite drops from ~4.3 MB to the ~1.7 MB of Active tasks plus their recent notes). Notes of Active tasks are the largest share, so the note segments carry most of the gain.")
inl note_of_eoie_state_scale_14 () = Note ("EOIE-STATE-SCALE", 14u32, "2026-10-24T23:44Z", "eoie", "19:43 Provisional estimate 195m from the grooming rule (EOIE-AGILE-CHECK-FIELDS note): D4 at 20%. Replace it with milestone sizing.")
inl note_of_eoie_state_scale_15 () = Note ("EOIE-STATE-SCALE", 15u32, "2026-10-26T05:34Z", "eoie", "01:34 eoie: root cause of agile list taking 6-8 s, which is not the parse. A list costs about 10-20 ms per printed row at every filter (17 rows 314 ms, 125 rows 2.5 s, 792 rows 7.8 s). Each row calls agile_planning_fields::kit_agile_planning_columns_near with the whole state text, and the generated export takes v0 as a borrowed str and converts it with Rc::from(v0), so every call copies the whole 6.6 MB state: about 5 GB of allocation and copying per full list. The planning lookup itself is bounded (planning_offset_near searches only up to the next Task row). 228 exported kit functions take a borrowed str and copy it into an Rc. Fixes, by blast radius: (1) eoie: build a planning index once per call inside task_listing (like note_index_of) and look rows up in it, so the state crosses no crate boundary per row; (2) compiler: exports could take an Rc so a cross-crate string argument costs a refcount bump (asked of the compiler lane). Either one removes the quadratic term. This also moves EOIE-AGILE-EVENT-LOG phase 1: the memo may be unnecessary for list once (1) lands; measure R1 after (1).")
inl note_of_eoie_state_scale_16 () = Note ("EOIE-STATE-SCALE", 16u32, "2026-10-26T06:17Z", "eoie", "02:16 eoie: node proof of the list planning index (scratch kit-rename\\list-planning-index.do; regen agile_task_listing, debug build, run against a copy of the live 7.0 MB backlog). Output is byte-identical to the unfixed candidate on all four views (table 136,766 chars, --json 844,019, Active, --open --load light), with elapsed fields stripped. Timing in debug builds on a node at 90%+ CPU was mixed: json 35.0 s to 12.5 s, table 17.9 s to 16.6 s, Active 10.4 s to 13.2 s, light 17.9 s to 15.8 s. So the per-row 6.6 MB copy dominates the json path, but in debug the table path has other per-row costs. Correction to note 15: the copy explains json, not yet table. Rule for the verdict, written now: release builds of both binaries, 5 runs each on a quiet machine, median; accept when table and json both improve and outputs stay identical. Next: profile the table path's per-row work (table_line and the sort) in a release build.")
inl note_of_eoie_state_scale_17 () = Note ("EOIE-STATE-SCALE", 17u32, "2026-10-26T06:45Z", "eoie", "02:45 eoie: reading the list path after the node proof (note 16). (1) Table and json rows each made exactly one whole-state copy per row (planning_columns_near or planning_json_near), and the index fix removes both. So json saving 22.5 s while table saved 1.3 s cannot both come from the copy: that run timed wall clock, in debug builds, on a node at 100% CPU (the Active view even got slower with less work), so it is noise, not evidence. Its only solid result is the byte-identical output. (2) In debug, parsing every task row (task_row_at for about 800 tasks, about 300 ms total in release here: Blocked list 314 ms) costs seconds at any filter, so debug wall time hides per-row effects. (3) A second, smaller quadratic term: rows_from builds the sorted row_list by insertion into a linked list (row_inserted, about n^2/2 = 314,000 steps for 792 rows, each allocating a node). Cheap per step, but quadratic, and it grows with the backlog; replace it with collect-then-sort (a lib sort on a list) when the index fix lands. Measurement rule unchanged (written in note 16): release builds of both binaries, 5 runs each, median of the binary's own elapsed_ms, on a quiet machine.")
inl note_of_eoie_state_scale_18 () = Note ("EOIE-STATE-SCALE", 18u32, "2026-10-27T17:44Z", "kit", "kit (fc1568a6), 13:4x: the 8 MB threshold of the 10-17 decision broke as a hard failure. At 13:31, state/agile.spi reached 8,390,714 bytes, 2,106 over the 8 MiB read guard in agile_state_runtime (agile_read_regular_text_limited at 8*1024*1024, five call sites), so every agile write (note, set, add, edit) failed for every lane. Reads still worked. Fiscal reported it; main measured that notes are 7.53 MB of the 8.39 (Active 4.47, Done 1.99, Planned 0.54, Blocked 0.32, Paused 0.22), with growth of 0.5-1.0 MB/day. Emergency unblock at 13:39, data only, no release: the 2,543 notes of the 403 Done items (1,991,308 bytes) moved verbatim and in order to docs/backlog/archive/done-notes.spi, still as inl note_of_* lines, so they merge back unchanged. Before writing, the script proved kept + archived lines = the original, in order, and that every moved note belongs to a Done item. It wrote a hashed backup of the full pre-trim file, which also keeps the 8.39 MB compiler-scaling corpus: %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\state-backups\\agile-20261027-133935-09DB8912A567.spi. Script: scratch clean/agile-trim/done-notes-trim.ps1. Results: state 6,399,406 bytes; show, list Done (403) and a note write all ok; agile check ok on a frozen copy (build_attested=3; live attestation kept retrying while lanes replayed their held writes). Cost: agile show on a Done item no longer lists its archived notes. Headroom is ~1.9 MB, about 2 days. By the 10-17 rule the segment layout is now due (notes in their own append-only segments, the 8 MiB guard per file), and the 10-22 model draft (agile_state_segment_model) is the starting point. The Done-notes archive stays as a periodic trim until then.")
inl note_of_eoie_state_scale_19 () = Note ("EOIE-STATE-SCALE", 19u32, "2026-10-27T17:53Z", "kit", "kit 13:5x, correcting note 18: 'agile note' never failed. Its path reads the state without the 8 MiB guard, and on a copy of the 8.39 MB pre-trim file a note still writes (ok, 1.1 s). The guarded writers failed: 'agile set' (reproduced: 'regular file exceeds limit ... limit=8388608'), and by the code add and edit. Bridge staged for TRAIN as next-train step 5g (scratch clean/agile-trim/agile-guard-64.do): the guard goes 8 -> 64 MiB at its 8 sites in agile_state_runtime, and the lease's attestation reader goes 8 -> 64 MiB per file with a 128 MiB total. post-install-TRAIN runs agile-guard-check.ps1, a set plus a note on that 8.39 MB copy, which fails on today's binary through the guard error.")
inl note_of_eoie_state_scale_20 () = Note ("EOIE-STATE-SCALE", 20u32, "2026-10-28T11:54Z", "eoie", "07:5x kit: segment-layout design, written now so it's ready before a threshold breaks. State today: state/agile.spi 6.95 MB, archive/done-notes.spi 1.99 MB. The guard is 64 MiB per file since TRAIN2c (10-28 05:25); growth is about 0.5-1.0 MB/day of notes. Layers: (1) Done notes leave the state through 'agile seal' (ships in TRAIN3), which moves every Done item's notes to archive/done-notes.spi, byte-identical to the 10-27 hand trim. Running it at each day's first groom keeps the state at roughly open items plus their notes. (2) If the open-item notes alone grow past the rule below, notes move to monthly segments, state/notes-YYYY-MM.spi, each holding the Note rows written that month. state/agile.spi keeps Task rows and planning fields; readers (show, list --notes counts, next) merge the segments by item id, the same way show already merges the archive (note 1 of KIT-AGILE-SEAL-DONE). Writes append only to the current month's file, so a note write touches a small file and a 1 MB parse instead of the whole log. Pre-registered trigger, decided before any measurement: start (2) when state/agile.spi passes 24 MiB after a seal, or when an 'agile note' write on the installed release binary takes over 2 s median of 5 on the review box. Until then, (1) only. Next step: a daily seal after TRAIN3 installs, plus a size and timing line added to the morning groom.")
inl note_of_eoie_state_scale_21 () = Note ("EOIE-STATE-SCALE", 21u32, "2026-10-31T09:59Z", "kit", "10-31 06:0x kit groom (WIP limit, dangling rule 'no Active silent > 3 d'): Active -> Planned at 35%. No note for about 3 days, so this item isn't in progress. Planned keeps it in 'agile next --team kit' as ready work (Paused would park it out of the queue). Kit had 42 Active items, with Active meaning anything ever touched rather than being worked now. The progress and notes stay; whoever pulls it next sets it Active again.")
inl note_of_eoie_state_scale_22 () = Note ("EOIE-STATE-SCALE", 22u32, "2026-11-01T13:17Z", "kit", "11-01 09:1x kit groom: the 'append-only note segments' step this item planned is IMPLEMENTED and node-verified (EOIE-AGILE-EVENT-LOG: state/notes_<yyyymm>.spi, writer gated by KIT_AGILE_NOTE_SEGMENTS=1, note 287-306 ms vs ~2,550 ms whole-file on a copy, readers merge segments), shipping with TRAIN7. So EOIE-AGILE-EVENT-LOG's dep on this item is satisfied in substance. TENSION FOR THE USER to see in one place: the 10-17 decision 'state bloat is intended' (a realistic append-heavy log for measuring compiler scale) now meets measured costs: agile.spi passed 10 MB on 11-01 08:03 and a whole-file rename ran 421 s and lost the write race twice (KIT-SCALE n2, STATE-CONCURRENCY-SOTA L2, recurring since 10-25). Segments keep every note (the measurement data survives) while removing the whole-file rewrite from the hot path, so they serve both goals; a seal (KIT-AGILE-SEAL-DONE) still waits on this item. NEXT: after TRAIN7, record the compiler-scale measurement this item wanted on the segmented state, then decide here whether the measurement is done.")
inl note_of_eoie_state_scale_23 () = Note ("EOIE-STATE-SCALE", 23u32, "2026-11-05T05:52Z", "tibia", "11-05 01:52 tibia (READY light item; data point only): GROWTH CURVE. state/agile.spi is 13.73 MB with 13,734 Note rows (10-17 1.15 MB; 10-28 6.95 MB; 11-01 past 10 MB), so it grew about 0.9 MB/day over the last 4 days. archive/done-notes.spi is still 1.90 MB, unchanged since 10-27 13:39, so no seal has run since the hand trim. There are NO notes_<yyyymm>.spi segment files, and KIT_AGILE_NOTE_SEGMENTS is unset in lane shells, so the segment writer (EOIE-AGILE-EVENT-LOG, TRAIN7) is not on and every note still rewrites the whole file. WRITE COST seen by tibia tonight, from kit's own elapsed_ms on 12 'agile note' calls 00:52-01:49 local: 1493-4626 ms (typical 1.5-2.8 s), plus 2 CAS-race-free sets at 835-1290 ms; bytes_before/after show other lanes writing between my reads. That is about 6-15x the 287-306 ms the segment writer measured on a copy. The 64 MiB guard is about 55 days away at this rate. A workflow-breaking threshold is not hit yet, but turning segments on (env default or flag) is the measured lever.")
inl iggy_eval () = Task ("IGGY-EVAL", "Evaluate integrating Apache Iggy (persistent message streaming) in our projects: typed GADT/existential messages with reflection-derived codecs could make multidimensional sagas (compensations indexed by state at the type level); candidates: eoie job registry and receipts (EOIE-JOBS), the matrix run ledger, Kino cell events, xsim. Local workaround and evaluation only (no upstream contact)", Story, P3, D5, 100u32, Done, "", "iggy,messaging,research", "current", "current")
inl ci_offload () = Task ("CI-OFFLOAD", "Use the GitHub Actions minutes freed by dropping Fable (builds no longer need them, even on the NEAR projects) for heavy processing that spares the laptop: full matrix suites with cached compile-memo/native-memo, benchmarks, Kino lib suites, bundle mining. Results come back as artifacts the local tools read (run ledger rows, results.tsv). Learn first from the recent Anthropic Scion case and the DeltaDB release (research notes on this item)", Story, P1, D5, 60u32, Planned, "", "ci,benchmark,offload,team:main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_ci_offload () = Planning ("CI-OFFLOAD", Minutes 240u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_ci_offload_1 () = Note ("CI-OFFLOAD", 1u32, "2026-10-17T18:19Z", "main-thread", "14:19 main-thread research 2026-10-17. Anthropic Scion (engineering post 2026-10-13; thenewstack.io/anthropic-scion-git-replacement): Anthropic moved its monorepo off Git to an in-house system after Claude pushed merges from ~2,500/day (May) to 30,000+/day (Sept); its CPU and memory needs are about 200x below the Git mirroring it replaced; a team of ~7 built it in ~5 months, and it is not released. Zed DeltaDB (zed.dev/blog/delta-public-beta, Delta public beta 2026-09-16): extends Git's content-based versioning with incremental deltas and stores the agent conversations and instructions behind each change, while a commit stays the checkpoint you push, pull and build. Lessons for us: (1) agent-scale work needs content-addressed, incremental state; our compile/native memos and reproducible builds are the same idea applied to the harness, and CI can run them cheaply on cached memos; (2) link work history to the conversations behind it (DeltaDB): eoie notes with --as lanes already do this per item, and EOIE-MEMORY plus commit links would make it per change; (3) offload heavy runs to CI only where the result is content-keyed and replayable locally.")
inl note_of_ci_offload_2 () = Note ("CI-OFFLOAD", 2u32, "2026-10-18T12:26Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 08:26 main-thread first slice: apps/compiler/tmp/scripts/ci-runs.ps1 [-RunId N] downloads the compiler job's compiler-oracle artifact (EXPECTED.tsv + runs) of the newest completed schedule/workflow_dispatch run into <cache>/runs/ci-<id>, where compare-runs.ps1 can diff a Linux CI run against a local one. Tried on 39121783429: it fetched the formatter report and the per-worker compile results; that run died before results.tsv (SetErrorMode, fixed in the tree), so the first full comparison waits on the next green nightly.")
inl note_of_ci_offload_3 () = Note ("CI-OFFLOAD", 3u32, "2026-10-19T12:30Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 08:30 lap 1 grain: first slice ci-runs.ps1 is in apps/compiler/tmp/scripts; blocked on a scheduled run that reaches the oracle upload: none fired on 10-19 by 12:2xZ and the 10-18 one died at SetErrorMode (fixed in HEAD 668568ac) Next: after the 10-20 scheduled run, ci-runs.ps1 + compare-runs.ps1 against today's pack 5 run (Linux vs Windows diff)")
inl note_of_ci_offload_4 () = Note ("CI-OFFLOAD", 4u32, "2026-10-19T13:00Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 09:00 lap 2 grain: schedule reliability measured from gh run list -R i574n/spiral --workflow gh-pages.yml: cron is 17 6 * * * (06:17Z) and the compiler job runs only on schedule or workflow_dispatch; the 10-18 schedule run started 11:04Z (4h47m late) and the 10-19 one has not started at 12:59Z (6h42m late, maybe dropped: GitHub sheds cron runs under load). The last 12 runs hold exactly one schedule run (39121783429, failed at SetErrorMode, artifact compiler-oracle 239 KB without results.tsv). So the offload path cannot rely on cron alone: a workflow_dispatch after each push the user wants scored (user-triggered; agents never trigger runs) or a second cron hour would make the Linux comparison predictable. Decision recorded for the user: none needed from agents; ci-runs.ps1 already accepts workflow_dispatch runs. Next: when any schedule or dispatch run with results.tsv exists, run ci-runs.ps1 + compare-runs.ps1 against single-flight-20261019-072348")
inl note_of_ci_offload_5 () = Note ("CI-OFFLOAD", 5u32, "2026-10-19T13:45Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 09:45 lap 3: FIRST LINUX-VS-WINDOWS COMPARISON DONE. The 10-19 schedule run fired at 13:15Z (6h58m late), compiler job 39258592236 ran -Suite all -Native on HEAD 668568ac and uploaded compiler-oracle with results.tsv (7,007 rows); ci-runs.ps1 -RunId 39258592236 fetched it into spiral-bin/runs/ci-39258592236, and compare-runs.ps1 against the accepted pack 5 run single-flight-20261019-080524 gives 12,720 differing cells. Classified: (1) 12,4xx are no-toolchain on the runner (Kotlin 452, Swift 451, Lua 448, Lean 447, Wasm 443, Delphi 406, Bend 401, Wgsl 50, Calyx 28): the CI job installs only BEAM, bun, zig and python. (2) Linux exit-code semantics: zig_exit_wide exits 49 (305 mod 256) on C/Rust/Zig/Gleam/Python while F# reports 305, so the only DISAGREE is F#; C abort() is 134 (SIGABRT) where Windows gives 3, so managed_string_invalid_utf8_slice and abi_external_buffer_length_negative turn agree-fail on 5 backends. (3) Rust build-fail on 5 array_* rows: rustup raced installing +1.99.0 under -Parallel 2 (could not rename downloaded component). (4) mega_omniledger_erp_kernel F# (root and all_terminals) exits 0 on Linux but 1 on Windows. (5) RESIDUAL differences between platforms: Swift 75 and Kotlin 75 (closure_pair*, c_self_tail_loop_heap, c_deep_list_decref ...), Ebpf 17 (ebpf_bacc, facc, nest3, nested, t2, t3, tacc, tworec ...), OpenQasm 6 (ebpf_t3, tacc, native_tail_recursion, tail_loop, ts_tail_loop, python_self_tail_call_depth): the same compiler source emits different text on Linux, which contradicts byte-reproducible output and needs its own item. Fix applied for (3): scripts/workflow.ps1 -Compiler now reads the matrix rustc toolchain from test.ps1 (regex on the Rust Invoke-Native line; finds 1.99.0 at HEAD and nightly-2026-10-18 in the tree) and runs rustup toolchain install once before test.ps1 (parse-checked; needs the user's push). (2) and (5) are test.ps1 / compiler matters for main. Next: open the residual-divergence question by diffing one Ebpf output (ebpf_tacc) between platforms once the artifact carries outputs, or regenerate it on WSL when WSL is allowed")
inl note_of_ci_offload_6 () = Note ("CI-OFFLOAD", 6u32, "2026-10-19T14:14Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 10:14 lap 4 grain: made the residual divergence diagnosable. The compiler job writes every output next to its sample (and accepts), so the runner's checkout already holds the Linux outputs; the artifact never carried them. gh-pages.yml compiler job: new always() step git diff --no-color -- apps/compiler/tmp/samples > \\/samples-output.diff, and the compiler-oracle artifact now uploads that file (YAML parse-checked; a run: step, so actions.lock is unaffected). ci-runs.ps1 prints the number of committed outputs that differ on the runner, grouped by extension, when the diff is present. Together they show the exact Swift/Kotlin/Ebpf/OpenQasm text that differs between Linux and Windows (75/75/17/6 residual hashes on run 39258592236). Needs the user's push; the next schedule or dispatch run carries it. Next: read the first samples-output.diff and classify each divergence (platform-dependent codegen vs path or newline leaks)")
inl note_of_ci_offload_7 () = Note ("CI-OFFLOAD", 7u32, "2026-10-19T15:12Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 11:12 lap 5 grain: narrowed the Linux-vs-Windows residual divergence by joining CI run 39258592236 against single-flight-20261019-080524 on rows that compile ok on both sides: Ebpf 17, Kotlin 75, OpenQasm 6, Swift 75, and the Swift and Kotlin sets are IDENTICAL (closure_pair*, method_pair*, tuple_pair, nested_tuple, record_value, tail loops, managed_string_*, native_string_utf8_*_source, wgsl_record_loop ...), while C, Rust, Zig, F#, Gleam, Python agree on the same samples. Ruled out: the compiler source (tree spiral_compiler.fs equals 668568ac, the commit CI built; sample .spi inputs unmodified) and CRLF (spiral_compiler.fs and the committed outputs hold 0 CR bytes, so multi-line literals cannot carry a CR). Suspects: the four divergent codegens are the newest, adjacent ones (codegenKotlin 170288, codegenSwift 171050, codegenOpenQasm 171797, codegenEbpf 172097) and share local name tables, so the cause is in shared helpers used only there (e.g. culture-dependent text or ordering; ICU on Linux vs NLS on Windows). The uncommitted samples-output.diff artifact step (lap 4, needs the user's push) shows the exact text. Next: read that diff and check the shared helpers of the four codegens.")
inl note_of_ci_offload_8 () = Note ("CI-OFFLOAD", 8u32, "2026-10-19T15:29Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 11:29 lap 6 grain: the Linux-vs-Windows triage is one call now: lab/CI-OFFLOAD/classify.ps1 -Local <run> -Ci <ci-run dir> joins results.tsv rows and classifies each differing row (no-toolchain-on-runner, toolchain-install-race, residual-only, oracle-only, exit-mod-256, abort-exit-134-vs-3, behavior) with per-backend counts and lists the unclassified ones. On run 39258592236 vs single-flight-20261019-080524: 3,171 differing rows = no-toolchain 3,126 (the Kotlin/Swift residual divergences hide inside these rows because their compile still runs), residual-only 23 (Ebpf 17, OpenQasm 6), oracle-only 6, exit-mod-256 5, abort 134-vs-3 2, toolchain race 1, behavior 8. The 8: 4 Rust array_* build-fail (the same rustup race, detail truncated before could not rename), mega_omniledger_erp_kernel F# exit 1 local vs 0 Linux (x2), and NEW: native_cube_flush_delay_direct and native_cube_monotonic_delay_direct are C build-fail (Known) on Windows but build and exit 42 on Linux, so their Known entries are Windows-only facts and should carry that scope. Promote classify.ps1 next to ci-runs.ps1 once the user's push lands the diff artifact.")
inl note_of_ci_offload_9 () = Note ("CI-OFFLOAD", 9u32, "2026-10-19T15:46Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 11:46 lap 7 grain, refines lap 6's NEW item: the two native_cube_*_delay_direct C Known entries already say why (POSIX-only residual, poll.h and clock_gettime; native C runs only on Linux), and the Linux run confirms it: both build and exit 42 there. So the matrix is right, and the gap is that a Known entry cannot name a platform: on Linux these rows ran and agreed, yet the Known entry still matches by id and backend whenever such a row is DISAGREE or build-fail, so a Linux regression on them would be masked. Proposal for main (test.ps1/matrix are main's): an optional Platform key on Known entries (Windows or Linux, compared with IsWindows) next to the existing Oracle scope, and Platform = 'Windows' on these two. classify.ps1 then reports them under their real verdict on Linux.")
inl note_of_ci_offload_10 () = Note ("CI-OFFLOAD", 10u32, "2026-10-19T16:20Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 12:20 lap 8 grain: lap 5 suspects checked by reading codegenKotlin and codegenSwift (spiral_compiler.fs 170352-171849): every float and int literal goes through ToString with InvariantCulture (R for floats), generated method and closure names are ASCII-only (generatedMethodName/generatedClosureName 161549), method and closure tags come from Dictionary insertion order (dict.Count), and there is no sort, culture compare or Environment.NewLine in those codegens; the only StartsWith calls take ASCII prefixes. So culture-dependent text is ruled out for the Kotlin/Swift residual divergence. Next: the samples-output.diff artifact after the user's push; if it shows name or order changes, look at closureBindingNames and the structural method/closure keys")
inl note_of_ci_offload_11 () = Note ("CI-OFFLOAD", 11u32, "2026-10-19T16:47Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 12:47 lap 9 grain: the Platform proposal from lap 7 is staged as lab/CI-OFFLOAD/known-platform.py for main (test.ps1 and matrix.psd1 are main's files): Get-ScopedKnown (test.ps1:1002-1006) is the only place Known entries are filtered (both oracles go through it), so one Where-Object clause adds an optional Platform key (Windows or Linux, from IsWindows) next to the Oracle scope, and the two native_cube_*_delay_direct C entries (matrix.psd1:29-30) get Platform = 'Windows'. Dry run: 3 anchors match once, LF. Also checked: no new spiral CI run since 39258592236 (13:15Z schedule on 668568ac), so classify.ps1's split stands")
inl note_of_ci_offload_12 () = Note ("CI-OFFLOAD", 12u32, "2026-10-19T17:24Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 13:24 lap 10 grain: classify.ps1 rerun of CI 39258592236 against the pack-7 review single-flight-20261019-124451: 4 of the 9 behavior rows were the Rust toolchain race in another spelling (rustup: missing manifest in toolchain 1.99.0 ... installation was interrupted, array_cell and 3 more array samples), so the toolchain-install-race class now matches that text too (5 Rust rows). Behavior left: 5 rows = the 2 native_cube_*_delay_direct C rows (known-platform.py, dry run still 3 anchors once), plus mega_omniledger_erp_kernel and its all_terminals on Fsharp (local exit 1, CI exit 0; no C or Zig row, so no oracle catches it) and zig_lib_console Fsharp (residual and stdout differ). No newer CI run since 39258592236. Next: main applies known-platform.py with a pack; someone checks why the local F# mega row exits 1 (fsi stderr in the review's row detail)")
inl note_of_ci_offload_13 () = Note ("CI-OFFLOAD", 13u32, "2026-10-19T17:45Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 13:45 lap 11 grain: explained the 2 F# mega behavior rows (mega_omniledger_erp_kernel and all_terminals, local exit 1 vs CI exit 0): ran the committed main.fsx with the matrix's exit wrapper in dotnet fsi (one process, scratch copy): Win32Exception, An error occurred trying to start process /bin/sh, at main.fsx line 14, i.e. the sample's own F# macros (main.spi:8042, 8202, 8389: external-recovery, fenced-lock and cross-process programs) spawn /bin/sh, which exists only on the Linux runner. So these rows are platform-bound like the 2 native_cube_*_delay_direct C rows: a Platform = Linux scope (known-platform.py's mechanism) or a Windows-scoped Known entry for the 2 F# rows would make local and CI agree; no compiler bug. Behavior left unexplained: zig_lib_console F# only")
inl note_of_ci_offload_14 () = Note ("CI-OFFLOAD", 14u32, "2026-10-19T18:07Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 14:07 lap 12: known-platform.py is applied (test.ps1 Platform filter, 2 cube C rows Windows-scoped). Staged lab/CI-OFFLOAD/known-platform-fsharp.py: the existing all_terminals Fsharp Known entry gets Platform = Windows (its F# macros spawn POSIX programs); dry run matches 1 anchor; queue row for main")
inl note_of_ci_offload_15 () = Note ("CI-OFFLOAD", 15u32, "2026-10-19T18:32Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 14:32 lap 13: all 5 behaviour rows of CI 39258592236 against the pack 7 accept are now explained: 2 cube C rows (Windows-scoped in the tree), 2 F# mega rows (POSIX programs; known-platform-fsharp.py staged) and zig_lib_console Fsharp, whose committed main.fsx printed a bool through _.ToString() (True) while the uncommitted compiler emits an explicit true/false (git diff main.fsx line 9), so that row resolves with the push. Next: classify the first CI run after the push, expecting behaviour 0")
inl note_of_ci_offload_16 () = Note ("CI-OFFLOAD", 16u32, "2026-10-19T18:44Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 14:44 correction to the 14:07 and 14:32 notes: test.ps1 applies a Known entry only to a DISAGREE or build-fail row (test.ps1:1033-1036), and the two F# mega rows have no oracle row, so a Platform scope on all_terminals would change nothing; known-platform-fsharp.py is withdrawn (queue row marked) and deleted. And the cube C and F# mega rows keep differing field by field after the push, so behaviour 0 was wrong. Fix in the lab tool instead: classify.ps1 reads lab/CI-OFFLOAD/platform-bound.tsv (4 id/backend rows with reasons) and classes them platform-bound; against the pack 7 accept it now reports platform-bound 4 and behaviour 1 (zig_lib_console, which the push resolves)")
inl note_of_ci_offload_17 () = Note ("CI-OFFLOAD", 17u32, "2026-10-19T22:50Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 18:50 lap 19 grain: no push since 02:48 (latest CI is still the 09:15 schedule run 39258592236), so classify.ps1 was rerun against the newest accepted baseline, pack 9 (single-flight-20261019-161549), instead of pack 7: 4,433 of 7,007 CI rows differ and all are classed: no-toolchain-on-runner 3,126, oracle-only 1,168, residual-only 122, exit-mod-256 5, toolchain-install-race 5, platform-bound 4, abort-exit-134-vs-3 2, behaviour 1 (zig_lib_console Fsharp, which the push resolves). So packs 8b and 9 add no CI-visible behaviour change. Next: classify the first CI run after the user's push, expecting behaviour 0")
inl note_of_ci_offload_18 () = Note ("CI-OFFLOAD", 18u32, "2026-10-19T23:28Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 19:28 lap 21 grain, what to offload first, measured from today's heavy-gate ledger (spiral-bin/ledger/runs.tsv, 584 jobs, 22.3 h of run time, 3.2 h of queue wait): Kino/Cells notebook runs 6.8 h (297 jobs; pack_sim.spi alone 41 min in 20 runs, date_time.spi 41 min in 7), eoie release pipelines 5.2 h (106 jobs, several 26-45 min installs), suite accepts 4.2 h (7 runs of 43-64 min), reviews 1.7 h, censuses and sweeps 1.7 h, probes and candidate builds 0.9 h. An accept is the best first candidate on paper (deterministic, must reproduce a review row for row) but CI lacks 7 native toolchains (3,126 no-toolchain rows in the last classify), so a CI accept cannot replace a local one; Kino notebook gates are the larger and more portable share (C, Rust, Gleam, F#, Zig all exist on the runner), so the first offload should be a Cells lib gate job whose metrics TSV comes back as an artifact. Next: draft that job's inputs and artifact shape (no workflow edits or triggers from this lane)")
inl note_of_ci_offload_19 () = Note ("CI-OFFLOAD", 19u32, "2026-10-20T00:45Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 20:45 cells-gate-ci.ps1 checked locally (bounded job job-1792457040116 through lab cells-gate-ci-local.ps1, which only adds the heavy gate, a private MIX_BUILD_PATH and SPIRAL_CC): -Match routes.spi gated 1 file in 57 s and wrote the artifact folder as designed, cells-gate.tsv (routes.spi 1 discovered, 1 executed, 1 passed, 0 failed), metrics-0/tests.tsv and gate.log. So the CI step script works end to end on one file; a CI job only needs the compiler build that workflow.ps1 -Compiler already does, then this script over all 40 files and an upload of the folder. Next: the user wires the job (workflow edits and actions.lock are theirs)")
inl note_of_ci_offload_20 () = Note ("CI-OFFLOAD", 20u32, "2026-10-21T23:08Z", "main", "19:08 drafted the workflow job for the user to wire in (no workflow edit or trigger from this lane): lab/CI-OFFLOAD/cells-gate-job.yml.txt is a cells-gate job for spiral's gh-pages.yml. It runs on ubuntu-24.04 on workflow_dispatch and the existing 06:17 schedule, so it never adds time to a push. It reuses the build job's pinned actions (checkout v6.1.0, setup-rust v1.3.0 nightly-2026-10-18, setup-beam v1.24.1 OTP 29 / Elixir 1.20 / Gleam 1.19.2, setup-dotnet v5.4.0, setup-zig v2.2.1), then runs workflow.ps1 -Compiler (the compiler build only), cells-gate-ci.ps1 into runner.temp/cells-gate, and uploads that folder as the cells-gate artifact even on failure. No new uses: entries, so actions.lock needs no regeneration (memory actions-lock-on-workflow-edits). The local side reads it through ci-runs.ps1-style download into spiral-bin/runs. Blocked only on the user pasting it under jobs: and committing.")
inl note_of_ci_offload_21 () = Note ("CI-OFFLOAD", 21u32, "2026-10-22T06:30Z", "main", "02:30 main: lab drained and the job wired, uncommitted, for the user. The cells-gate job is now in spiral/.github/workflows/gh-pages.yml between compiler and push: ubuntu-24.04, workflow_dispatch and schedule only (never adds time to a push), the build job's pinned actions (no new uses:; spiral has no actions.lock), workflow.ps1 -Compiler, then apps/cells/spi/ci_gate.ps1 -Out runner.temp/cells-gate, with the artifact uploaded even on failure. YAML parses with jobs build, compiler, cells-gate, push. Promoted: cells-gate-ci.ps1 is now apps/cells/spi/ci_gate.ps1 (root path fixed for that depth); classify.ps1 is now apps/compiler/tmp/scripts/ci-classify.ps1, reading its platform-bound list from apps/compiler/tmp/tests/platform-bound.tsv (beside matrix.psd1). Applied before, verified: known-platform.py (test.ps1 filters Known rows by Platform; matrix.psd1 has the 2 Windows-scoped rows). Dropped: cells-gate-ci-local.ps1 (local runs use lib_gate.ps1). Next: the user commits; the first scheduled run's cells-gate artifact feeds the local tools.")
inl note_of_ci_offload_22 () = Note ("CI-OFFLOAD", 22u32, "2026-10-24T11:49Z", "main", "2026-10-19 laps folded from the deleted history ledger (docs/agents/history/2026-10-19-backlog-laps, HISTORY-BACKLOG-LAPS-DRIFT): lap 13b 14:44 (subagent2): CORRECTION: known-platform-fsharp.py withdrawn (Known only touches DISAGREE/build-fail rows, test.ps1:1033-1036; the F# mega rows are no-oracle, so the scope changes nothing) and the 14:32 expect-behaviour-0 claim was wrong (cube and F# rows keep differing by field). Fix in my own tool: classify.ps1 + lab platform-bound.tsv (4 rows: 2 cube C, mega root + all_terminals F#) -> class platform-bound; rerun vs pack 7: behaviour 1 (zig_lib_console, resolves with the push) -> next: classify the first post-push CI run: expect behaviour 0 with platform-bound 4 | lap 14 15:00 (subagent2): no newer spiral CI run than 39258592236 (gh run list: 13:15Z schedule, failure), so classify has nothing new to read -> next: first post-push run: classify, expect platform-bound 4 + behaviour 0 | lap 17 16:43 (subagent2): unchanged; classify ready with platform-bound -> next: first post-push CI run | lap 21-drive 19:30 (subagent2): drafted lab/CI-OFFLOAD/cells-gate-ci.ps1: the step a CI Cells job would run (tracked lib .spi with test blocks, 40 today, chunks of 20, --metrics per chunk, cells-gate.tsv summary parsed from the spiral-tests file reports, gate.log) with a -Match name filter instead of an array parameter; parse clean, not run yet -> next: a local bounded run on one small file, then the user wires a job (no workflow edits from this lane) | lap 23-drive 20:45 (subagent2): cells-gate-ci.ps1 works end to end locally on routes.spi (57 s; cells-gate.tsv 1/1 passed, metrics-0, gate.log); set 40 -> next: user wires the CI job")
inl note_of_ci_offload_23 () = Note ("CI-OFFLOAD", 23u32, "2026-10-26T00:57Z", "main", "10-25 20:57 main: the CI cells-gate job is a SILENT FALSE GREEN. It is committed (HEAD gh-pages.yml has the cells-gate job), and the scheduled runs since report it 'success': run 40189052944 (10-25 11:29Z), job 121155939544, log line 8,096: 'cells gate: 66 files, 0 reports in 2 s'. 66 lib notebooks cannot run in 2 s; mix spiral.notebook evidently failed at once, and ci_gate.ps1 never read mix's exit code and treated 0 reports as a normal result. The 436-byte artifact is the empty cells-gate.tsv plus a gate.log holding the real reason (not downloaded: the log gets the reason from now on). So no CI Cells result has ever reached the local tools; this item's 70% overstated that half. Fixed in apps/cells/spi/ci_gate.ps1 (unstaged, for the user's commit; parse 0 errors, LF): (1) each mix chunk's exit code is read, and a failing chunk with no 'spiral-tests file' report prints its last 30 lines into the job log; (2) the summary line now says how many tests executed; (3) 0 executed throws 'cells gate is VOID', so the job goes red. Positive control of (3)'s arithmetic: an empty summary gives executed 0 (throws), one report with 3 executed gives 3 (passes). The same class as the node gate's GREEN-by-omission this morning (lib gate kit VOID rule). Next: the first scheduled run after the user commits prints mix's failure; fix that cause (likely a missing deps.get/compile step or the Gleam domain build in the job).")
inl note_of_ci_offload_24 () = Note ("CI-OFFLOAD", 24u32, "2026-10-26T00:59Z", "main", "10-25 20:59 main: cause found by reading the job, before any new run. ci_gate.ps1 called 'mix spiral.notebook' directly, skipping everything run_notebook.ps1 does before mix: local.hex, local.rebar and deps.get when deps/kino is missing (a fresh runner has none), plus SPIRAL_DOTNET, SPIRAL_COMPILER_DLL, DOTNET_ROOT and the core package dir. So mix died at once, every chunk. Fixed: ci_gate.ps1 now runs each chunk through run_notebook.ps1 (same arguments; it forwards them to mix spiral.notebook, and its deps/kino check is relative to apps/cells, where ci_gate already is), which is also the path the local gates use. Separate finding: the job runs 'workflow.ps1 -Compiler', which ends in test.ps1 -Suite all -Native -Accept, so the cells-gate job repeats the whole compiler matrix (about 16 min of its 16) before the gate; it needs only the compiler build. Left as is for now (CI minutes are the spare resource here); a build-only entry is a workflow.ps1 change to decide with the next workflow edit. Local check queued for the block-end batch (main-ci-gate-local-check: ci_gate.ps1 -Match ^array_dim\\.spi$ must exit 0 with more than 0 tests executed). ci_gate.ps1 diff: 11 insertions, 3 deletions, unstaged for the user.")
inl note_of_ci_offload_25 () = Note ("CI-OFFLOAD", 25u32, "2026-10-26T04:23Z", "main", "10-26 00:23 main (user 10-26 00:0x: use the node's resources so it isn't left idle): at 00:07 the node sat at 18% CPU / 7.6 GB free while main had ~20 jobs queued behind the two local heavy slots. Moved there, read-only, scripts pushed to the node's %TEMP% under the same folder names, so they run unchanged: caret-fold-probe, the registry planner probes, the dir-tree root-cut proof then the cli-args proof (--after), and the Cells console-heal verify (its compiler probe calls rustc and zig directly, so the stale node spiral.exe doesn't matter; it now runs mix deps.get when a copy lacks deps/kino). spiral was synced plainly (node idle at that moment); dir-tree-html went to mirror main-dtc (a job had appeared), with the proofs' repo path rewritten for the node copy only. Kept local on purpose: the blog split (writes the tree), the zed fixture verify (needs this box's dist/spiral-zed.exe), and every Cells/lib gate, because node rust Cells verdicts aren't trusted while SPIRAL_EXE points at the 2025-03-13 spiral.exe. Queued main-node-spiral-exe-rebuild (cargo build --release in the node's spiral workspace, after the registry probe). Then one lib gate there as a trial; if its rows match a local gate of the same slice, main's queued lib gates (json-canonical, new-vec, convert-zig, zig-index-of-3, test-interleave, x-cells-unicode, sm-heal-blocks, console-trace-blocks) move to the node, with lands still local. Tooling: scratchpad node-push.ps1 (scp a script folder to the node), node-watch.ps1 (wait and tail node jobs). Fixed remote.ps1's spurious MIRROR-DEPS-MISSING for non-spiral mirrors.")
inl note_of_ci_offload_26 () = Note ("CI-OFFLOAD", 26u32, "2026-10-26T04:35Z", "main", "10-26 00:35 main: the ci_gate.ps1 fix is verified locally (main-ci-gate-local-check, 00:19): -Match ^array_dim\\.spi$ through run_notebook.ps1 gives 1 report and 1 test executed, exit 0. Before the fix, the CI job ran nothing and still passed. The fix waits on the user's commit, then the first scheduled run is read.")
inl note_of_ci_offload_27 () = Note ("CI-OFFLOAD", 27u32, "2026-10-26T05:41Z", "main", "10-26 01:41 main: node lib-gate trial is unblocked and prepared. The node's spiral.exe was rebuilt at 00:55 (D96F204D; tibia's run after the compiler lane installed perl), and SPIRAL_COMPILER_PACKAGE_DIR is persisted there. Trial (scratchpad node-gate-trial.ps1; it launches only when the node is at or under 70% CPU, since node jobs start at once): re-gate LIB-FS-LINK-LENGTH on the node through the same kit. Pass rule, written before the run: GREEN with the local gate's exact rows (baseline red 4, candidate reds not red in the baseline 0, same failing test names). Then main's queued lib gates (json-canonical, new-vec, convert-zig, zig-index-of-3, test-interleave, x-cells-unicode, sm-heal-blocks, console-trace-blocks) move to the node, with lands staying local. Held at 01:39: node at 100% CPU (kit reruns, x).")
inl note_of_ci_offload_28 () = Note ("CI-OFFLOAD", 28u32, "2026-10-26T08:31Z", "main", "10-26 04:31 main: node lib-gate trial (node job-1793000465578, LIB-FS-LINK-LENGTH-NODE, the same kit and apply script as the local 10-25 21:56 gate) did NOT pass its pre-written rule. Its verdict is GREEN with candidate-only reds 0, which matches local, but the baseline is red 6 vs local 4. Compared by name from the local raw table (spiral-bin\\runs\\lab-outputs\\LIB-FS-LINK-LENGTH\\baseline-file_system.spi-20261025-2156.txt.tests.tsv): both sides fail file_exists_on_gleam_tells_files_from_missing_paths (gleam), find_parent (fsharp), find_parent_2 (rust) and trace_file (fsharp). Only the node fails get_full_path_3 and get_full_path_4, path-environment tests (the node's profile is C:\\Users\\alias, and its cwd and drive layout differ). Corrected 04:3x: an earlier version of this note guessed the extras by elimination and named the wrong pair. Why the rule matters: the gate compares candidate against its own baseline, so a node gate is blind on those 2 tests. Decision, by the rule: main's lib gates stay local. Next, CPU-light: read get_full_path_3/4 for the assumption that breaks on the node, then make either the tests portable or the node layout match, and rerun the trial.")
inl note_of_ci_offload_29 () = Note ("CI-OFFLOAD", 29u32, "2026-10-26T08:43Z", "main", "10-26 04:43 main: shared lib gate kit change (%TEMP%\\lib-gate-kit\\gate.ps1, parse-checked): the verdict file keeps its first line 'VERDICT stamp' and now adds, for every file whose candidate shows a red, a 'candidate red <file>: test/target, ...' line and a 'baseline red <file>: ...' line. Every verdict reader found (lib-gate-kit and kit-land-merge land.ps1, lib-edition3-symbols ready.ps1, pack-spelling) reads only the first line, so lands are unaffected. Why: the node trial compared by count and had to recover the local names from the raw tests.tsv. Running gates read the old script at start; the change applies to gates that start later. The node copy is refreshed by node-gate-trial.ps1's push on the next trial.")
inl note_of_ci_offload_30 () = Note ("CI-OFFLOAD", 30u32, "2026-10-26T08:45Z", "main", "10-26 04:45 main, corrected 04:5x: the node-only reds get_full_path_3/4 are NOT about paths. The node's baseline notebook records the outcome of both cells as SpiralError 'could not start the compiler on port 13905: :lock_timeout', after about 242 s of compile phase on a node at 100% CPU. That's compiler-start lock contention under load. The three path hypotheses ruled out above (junction, drive-root cwd, case) were the wrong frame; reading the cell outputs was the right first step. Two consequences. (1) The shared gate kit retried a candidate red once only for 'cell timed out after'. It now also retries on 'could not start the compiler on port N: :lock_timeout' (%TEMP%\\lib-gate-kit\\gate.ps1, parse-checked), the same class of load failure. (2) The trial's rule correctly refused, because a baseline red from load makes those tests blind. Rerun the trial when the node is under 70% CPU; with the names now in the verdict file, compare them name by name.")
inl note_of_ci_offload_31 () = Note ("CI-OFFLOAD", 31u32, "2026-10-26T08:58Z", "main", "10-26 04:58 main: the 4 reds shared by both machines in file_system.spi's baseline, read from the local notebook outputs. find_parent_2 (rust, a shipped backend) panics with \"No parent for dir 'workspace' at '...\\lib-gate-kit\\work\\<Name>\\base\\lib\\spiral' (until 'c:\\')\". It walks up from the cwd for a 'workspace' dir and a README.md and expects the parent to be named 'spiral', which the tree has (C:\\home\\git\\spiral\\workspace + README.md) and the gate's copies did not. So every lib gate was blind on it, a staging artifact rather than a lib bug. Fix in the shared kit (%TEMP%\\lib-gate-kit\\gate.ps1, backup gate.ps1.bak-markers, parse-checked): the copies now stage at work\\<Name>\\spiral\\lib\\spiral and work\\<Name>\\base\\spiral\\lib\\spiral, and each 'spiral' gets an empty workspace dir and a README.md, mirroring the tree. Cells' lib resolution (first ancestor holding lib/spiral) still picks the copy. The touched-file scan is unaffected because the markers sit outside lib. The check: the next file_system.spi gate's verdict lines should no longer list find_parent_2 as a baseline red. The other three (file_exists_on_gleam_tells_files_from_missing_paths: gleam.toml not in the Gleam run's cwd; find_parent and trace_file: dotnet fsi exit 1 on the F# target, which has had no consumer since Fable) are open. The F# pair are candidates for dropping their ///> fsharp target lines, by the F#-is-gone rule.")
inl note_of_ci_offload_32 () = Note ("CI-OFFLOAD", 32u32, "2026-10-26T09:27Z", "main", "10-26 05:27 main: file_system.spi's F# baseline reds: find_parent (///> fsharp) runs the same workspace/README.md parent lookup as find_parent_2, so its dotnet fsi exit 1 is very likely the same gate-staging artifact the 04:5x marker fix addresses (n31). Only trace_file (///> fsharp) has an unknown cause. Rule: don't drop either F# target line until the next file_system.spi gate, whose verdict file now lists failing names, shows which reds survive the staging fix. A red that survives on the F# target alone is then a candidate for dropping its ///> fsharp line, by the F#-is-gone rule (no consumer since Fable).")
inl note_of_ci_offload_33 () = Note ("CI-OFFLOAD", 33u32, "2026-10-26T09:46Z", "main", "10-26 05:46 main: shared land kit guard (%TEMP%\\lib-gate-kit\\land.ps1, parse-checked): right after the apply script runs on the stage, New-Stage re-hashes every target file it staged (the \\ map) and refuses, with nothing committed, if any changed. That's the gap x found at 05:2x: an apply script that ignores -Lib and writes the live tree landed outside the stage/commit, so outside the Global\\spiral-lib-land mutex, and the job then reported failure on a land that had happened. Positive control (scratchpad land-wrote-target-test.ps1, on a scratch lib copy): a probe that appends to the target's array_dim.spi directly -> land exit 1 with the guard message -> GREEN. A false alarm needs another land to commit during those seconds; it fails loudly and commits nothing. Respell exclusions: fiscal released xpath, xpath_eval, schematron, schematron_run and xml (all landed through the kit); still held by hand in %TEMP%\\respell-exclusions-manual.tsv: xsd_emit, xsd_regex, xsd, xsd_resolve, json_schema, xsd_constraints, xsd_certificate. schematron_run.spi's one Cons site joins the next list-sugar batch.")
inl note_of_ci_offload_34 () = Note ("CI-OFFLOAD", 34u32, "2026-10-26T09:59Z", "main", "10-26 05:59 main: land kit: the reader-gap and live-block waits are now a parameter, -WaitMinutes, default 600 (it was a hard-coded 180). The fs re-land (GRANULAR-HEAL n29) staged correctly and then gave up after 180 min, because the kit release train read the live lib continuously, although its job had a 12 h timeout. With 600 a land outlasts a long release train and fails only inside the job's own lifetime. The throw messages state the actual wait. Parse-checked; applies to lands that start from now on.")
inl note_of_ci_offload_35 () = Note ("CI-OFFLOAD", 35u32, "2026-10-29T06:40Z", "main", "10-29 02:4x main: ROOT CAUSE of the Linux-vs-Windows Kotlin/Swift/Ebpf/OpenQasm residual divergence (n6-n10), read from CI run 40691709422's samples-output.diff (cec8840c, the same commit the local tree holds): 112 outputs, 872 changed lines, and every changed line is a renamed temporary, spiral_tupleN (864 lines) or tN_k (8). After normalizing both counters, the CI and committed texts are IDENTICAL. The local number is always higher, by a per-site offset (7 x142, 9 x54, 5 x51, 6 x32, ...). Mechanism: Kotlin (spiral_compiler.fs:172667, 172712), Swift (173445, 173490), the eBPF profile (174011) and 174305/174337 name temporaries by s.text.Length, the character offset in the body's StringBuilder. The shared helper at 162111, `let line x (s : string) = x.text.Append(' ', x.indent).AppendLine s`, appends Environment.NewLine, which is CRLF on Windows and LF on Linux. Every backend's final text is then normalized with .Replace(\"\\r\\n\", \"\\n\"), so the visible lines match while each name counts one extra character per preceding line on Windows. Lap 8 ruled out Environment.NewLine by grep, but AppendLine uses it implicitly. FIX drafted (untracked, packs convention): apps/compiler/packs/apply-codegen-lf-lines.ps1 -Core <file> [-Apply] changes the helper to .Append(s).Append('\\n'); dry run on the live core: 1/1 anchor matches exactly once. The other four AppendLine helpers (175376, 176715, 177901, 178392) serve backends that name nothing by length (their .text.Length uses only compare against an earlier length), so they are left alone. PREDICTION, which is the proof after an install: a Windows -Suite all run with the fixed core rewrites exactly those 112 committed outputs, and `git diff samples` equals the reverse of CI's samples-output.diff, so the committed outputs then match CI. Same family as COMPILER-CROSS-MACHINE-REPRO.")
inl note_of_ci_offload_36 () = Note ("CI-OFFLOAD", 36u32, "2026-10-29T13:21Z", "compiler", "10-29 09:21 compiler: PACK 6 REVIEW VOID, and n35's LF prediction was only PARTIALLY met by apply-codegen-lf-lines. Review job-1793273930987-compiler-pack6-review (candidate host 4BB64C77AFF99A0B / core 6576367396C93CC4, run single-flight-20261029-073857) hit its own 90-min cap during the native phase (machine shared with TRAIN3), so there is no verdict. samples/ was restored to clean afterwards (2 stray .tmp files from the kill removed; the candidate's sample diff is kept in scratch). The compile phase had finished, so the outputs were complete: (a) Delphi: 411 .pas changed, every changed line the NOREGVAR directive. (b) LF: exactly CI 40691709422's 112 files were rewritten (0 missing, 0 extra), but all 112 still differ from CI side b by ONE in every length-derived name (closure_pair main.kt: HEAD spiral_tuple194, candidate 188, CI 187). ROOT CAUSE: codegenKotlin/Swift/OpenQasm/Ebpf start each function or closure body StringBuilder with text.AppendLine(header), the same builder whose Length names spiral_tupleN/tN_k, so one CRLF per body remained on Windows. FIX: apps/compiler/packs/apply-codegen-lf-headers.ps1 (text.AppendLine(X) |> ignore -> text.Append(X).Append('\\n'), scoped to those 4 codegens with exact counts Kotlin 10, Swift 15, OpenQasm 2, Ebpf 2; Lean also names by length but CI showed no Lean drift, so it is left alone). Composes with apply-codegen-lf-lines (30 changed lines). PLAN (agreed with main): pack 6b = pack 6 + lf-headers, building now (job-1793279975270-compiler-pack6b-build, heavy slot 1). Then a targeted proof (compile the 112 samples to scratch and byte-compare with CI side b; expect 112/112 equal). Then the full -Suite all -Native review with a 4 h cap, including main's C-shim fix. Install after kit's TRAIN3 publishes.")
inl note_of_ci_offload_37 () = Note ("CI-OFFLOAD", 37u32, "2026-10-29T13:38Z", "compiler", "10-29 09:38 compiler: n35's PREDICTION MET with pack 6b. Candidate host B615E6BB7F6D392C / core 31CC96520D6B7269 (pack 6 + apply-codegen-lf-headers.ps1; job-1793279975270-compiler-pack6b-build, 338 s). Targeted proof: the 112 samples of CI 40691709422's samples-output.diff (52 .kt, 52 .swift, 8 .ebpf) compiled with the candidate into scratch, never the tree, and byte-compared with CI side b (HEAD files extracted by git archive, CI diff applied): EQUAL 112/112. Positive control: the same script on the pack-6 candidate (lf-lines only) reported DIFFERS on all 4 sampled files (the off-by-one). So Windows now emits the Kotlin/Swift/eBPF residuals CI emits on Linux. Full review requeued with a 4 h cap: job-1793281021284-compiler-pack6b-review (--heavy slot 1, samples/ clean before, includes main's C-shim fix).")
inl note_of_ci_offload_38 () = Note ("CI-OFFLOAD", 38u32, "2026-10-29T16:17Z", "compiler", "10-29 12:17 compiler: PACK 6b VERDICT CLEAN, handed to main for install. Candidate host B615E6BB7F6D392C / core 31CC96520D6B7269. Run single-flight-20261029-120916 (12:09-12:16, exit 0; the lib landings changed the memo key, so all 7,367 rows were recompiled and rerun on one lib): 6,842 parity + 525 parity-residual-differs, DISAGREE 0, no REGRESSED or NATIVE-DIFF, native 6,846 ran, oracle agree 6,118, build-fail 9 = the baseline's 9. The 525 are exactly the prediction: Delphi 413 (the NOREGVAR directive only), Kotlin 52 + Swift 52 + eBPF 8 = this item's 112, byte-equal to CI 40691709422's Linux outputs. The first run (single-flight-20261029-093803) had 74 compile timeouts + 2 Zig build-fails + 1 Wgsl timeout under 100% load, all on byte-unchanged programs; all pass here.")
inl note_of_ci_offload_39 () = Note ("CI-OFFLOAD", 39u32, "2026-10-29T16:24Z", "main", "10-29 12:2x main: PACK-6b INSTALLED and verified live (installed-is-done). Appliers: delphi64-noregvar, codegen-lf-lines, codegen-lf-headers and formatter-operator-dehang (core), rust-emit-unit-arg (host). git-a6's verdict was CLEAN (run single-flight-20261029-120916: all 7,367 rows on one post-land lib, DISAGREE 0, no REGRESSED or NATIVE-DIFF; 525 predicted residuals = Delphi 413 NOREGVAR + Kotlin/Swift/eBPF 112 byte-equal to CI 40691709422; dynamic_array_reserve_zero [C] exit 0). Tool: the new apps/compiler/packs/install-pack.ps1 (unstaged). Its dry run showed today's live core 9FF1B7088BDCAB6F and host B24847FCB88A1BE6 plus the appliers reproduce the reviewed candidate source byte for byte (core 6DD9AB3B728D1BA0, host F55C9572CBC90869), with nothing using the installed compiler. -Apply (job-1793290813517, 129 s) backed up to C:\\home\\data\\compiler-installs\\pack6b-20261029-122022, wrote the candidate sources to the live tree, and built single-flight. Installed SpiralCompilerCore.dll 31CC96520D6B7269 = the candidate. The host SpiralCompiler.dll is 9895730DAE807B70 vs the candidate's B615E6BB7F6D392C: same source, so host build non-determinism. Proven by output: installed and candidate compile dynamic_array_reserve_zero [C], closure_pair [Rust], [Delphi] and [Kotlin] byte-identically; the installed Delphi output carries the NOREGVAR directive and equals the committed residual; the installed Kotlin output has 0 CR bytes. Hopacsota mode was not rebuilt (non-default, not reviewed). samples/ holds the 525 residuals, which are now the installed outputs. Next: git-a6's node ship, then RUNNER-STALE, then COMPILER-TMP-RETIRE, then pack 7.")
inl note_of_ci_offload_40 () = Note ("CI-OFFLOAD", 40u32, "2026-10-29T16:34Z", "main", "10-29 12:4x main: pack-6b RUNTIME-TARGET finding (git-a6). The installed 6b runtimeconfig requires .NET runtime 11.0.0-rc.3 (build.ps1 picked the newest SDK; after the wipe the bundled toolchain had only rc.3), while C:\\Program Files\\dotnet has rc.1, so a bare PATH 'dotnet SpiralCompiler.dll' fails ('You must install or update .NET'). Main's live verification ran through the bundled rc.3 dotnet and missed it. Impact census: none in-repo. Cells (toolchain.ex:31: opts > SPIRAL_DOTNET > bundled > PATH), lib.ps1 BuildSpiral, kit's regen (two pack-6b publishes OK) and the Zed server all use the bundled rc.3. The NODE (rc.1/rc.2 only) refused the ship smoke safely. DECISION (main + git-a6): no rollback and no rebuild; 6b stays on the box. git-a6 added the rc.1 SDK beside rc.3 in the box's bundled toolchain, so pack 7 onward targets rc.1 again (the pre-wipe baseline), and installs the rc.3 runtime additively on the node to ship 6b there. install-pack.ps1 HARDENED: (a) it backs up the whole installed bin folder (today's 2-DLL backup could not have restored the runtimeconfig and deps); (b) it fails unless the installed compiler launches under the SYSTEM dotnet. The positive control shows today's 6b install fails (b).")
inl note_of_ci_offload_41 () = Note ("CI-OFFLOAD", 41u32, "2026-10-31T22:56Z", "main", "10-31 18:56 main, pre-night groom: Active -> PLANNED (not Paused: no note in over 48 h and no live job, so it isn't work in progress, but nothing blocks it; it stays in agile next at its priority and progress for the night run to pull).")
inl note_of_ci_offload_42 () = Note ("CI-OFFLOAD", 42u32, "2026-11-01T14:39Z", "compiler", "11-01 10:38 compiler, R5: child SPIRAL-CI-COMPILER-BINARIES (6 RIDs win/linux/osx x64/arm64 as workflow artifacts with a sha manifest, fetched by core hash) uses the freed minutes; mac runners also unlock Swift/Kotlin backend checks later.")
inl note_of_ci_offload_43 () = Note ("CI-OFFLOAD", 43u32, "2026-11-01T14:43Z", "kit", "11-01 10:4x kit: the Scion/DeltaDB research on this item (n1) now also feeds KIT-DEFINITION (what kit is); no new research needed there.")
inl note_of_ci_offload_44 () = Note ("CI-OFFLOAD", 44u32, "2026-11-02T14:07Z", "x", "2026-11-02 10:07 x: GIT CONDITION RE-CHECKED from the tree (groom of user waits). A read-only survey at 09:4x shows every active repo 0 commits ahead of origin: the user committed and pushed alphabet, blog, dice, dir-tree-html, kit, fiscal, html, polyglot, regex, spiral, tibia and x in the last 1-3 h. i574n.github.io is the exception (3 unstaged files, last commit 2 years ago). So this item's user step is probably MET. What is left is deterministic and belongs to the owner, not the user: confirm the step's files are in HEAD (git log -- <paths>) and that CI on HEAD is green (gh run list --commit <HEAD>), then close it. Only a red or missing file sends it back to the user queue. Pages enablement (HTML, REGEX-BENCH-PAGE) is a real user step, since it is a public repo setting.")
inl note_of_ci_offload_45 () = Note ("CI-OFFLOAD", 45u32, "2026-11-05T22:48Z", "main", "main 11-05 18:5x MEASURED (gh api organizations/i574n/settings/billing/usage): November so far the org ran 3,807 Linux + 4,633 Windows Actions minutes and 83 storage units, net $0. The org is on the free plan with 15 PUBLIC repos and 0 private, and public repos get standard runners free and unmetered. So Actions capacity is not the limit (the personal account fc1943s used 9 minutes, dir-tree-html + polyglot). The real limit is ACCESS: Actions runs only pushed commits (workflow_dispatch/schedule run HEAD), and pushing is the user's. So (a) committed-state heavy work (scheduled suites, benches, cells-gate on HEAD) can grow freely on Actions, e.g. more matrix rows or more schedule slots, at no cost; (b) unstaged-tree work needs a codespace (quota-limited, ~31 of 120 core-h used this month; today's pre-push rehearsal, CODESPACES-QUOTA-RUNS n14) or own machines. Concrete next for (a): add Windows to the scheduled cells-gate (Linux only today), since Windows minutes are free too.")
inl todo_as_source () = Task ("TODO-AS-SOURCE", "Destructive migration, to try autonomously with backups (user 2026-10-17): spread // TODO: blocks through the code on purpose as the source of truth for work, multi-line and holding Spiral code (the 120-column formatter allows dense blocks), harvested by eoie agile todos instead of a separate backlog file. Iterate on copies with periodic backups in tmp until it's state of the art. Open design points: how a TODO block links to an eoie item ID, how status and progress live in the block, the formatter's handling of them, and the no-comments rule (TODO blocks become an allowed marker kind)", Epic, P1, D7, 50u32, Planned, "", "kit,todo,migration,team:kit,kit", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_todo_as_source () = Planning ("TODO-AS-SOURCE", Minutes 1200u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_todo_as_source_1 () = Note ("TODO-AS-SOURCE", 1u32, "2026-10-17T18:20Z", "main-thread", "14:20 main-thread dry run (non-destructive; backlog state backed up to job tmp todo-source/agile.spi.20261017-142038.bak; mapping in todo-source/mapping.tsv): of 332 items (162 open), only 32 open items cite a source file that exists, spread over 22 files, and 8 of those point at lanes/hopacsota/PLAN.md, which is prose. The other 130 open items (epics, research, cross-cutting work) have no code home. A full move into code TODO blocks would orphan ~80% of the open backlog, so the measured design is a hybrid. Code-anchored tasks become typed top-level todo blocks, parsed like test blocks (the compiler already skips top-level 'test' blocks in both BlockParsing sections, so a 'todo ID P1 D3 = <multi-line Spiral>' block can hold typecheckable Spiral and costs nothing in a build); everything else stays in eoie state. eoie agile todos then harvests both into one view, and the block's ID keys its notes. Next step, autonomous with backups: the compiler/formatter accepts todo blocks (one regression sample), then the 32 code-anchored items move into their files, rolled back by restoring the backup.")
inl note_of_todo_as_source_2 () = Note ("TODO-AS-SOURCE", 2u32, "2026-10-17T18:31Z", "main-thread", "14:31 main-thread: step 1 landed in eoie and is published. eoie agile todos now harvests TODO blocks. A full-line TODO comment absorbs the following comment lines whose leader starts at the same column, keeping their indentation, so multi-line Spiral survives; a blank line, code, a directive or another TODO ends the block. TODO(ID): yields an id field in --json (only when present), and json_escaped now also escapes CR and LF. Lives in src/agile_todo_model/model.spi (new test todo_blocks_keep_their_spiral_lines_and_ids; 6/6 model and 3/3 todo_cli tests pass). The first build broke the 1k crate gate (todo_lines.rs 685 -> 1,024 lines: json_escaped was inlined 3x in finding_json and comment_starts_line twice). Making json_escaped, todo_id, finding_json, comment_starts_line and todo_free let join points brought it to 846. Release built, topology checks passed, eoie.exe published. End to end on a fixture: the block prints with its Spiral lines, and JSON carries id EOIE-MEMORY-LINKS. Next: join harvested IDs with backlog status in agile todos, then move the 32 code-anchored open items into blocks (backup in job tmp todo-source/).")
inl note_of_todo_as_source_3 () = Note ("TODO-AS-SOURCE", 3u32, "2026-10-17T18:33Z", "main-thread", "14:33 main-thread: first migration slice. Three items with an exact code home now have TODO(ID) blocks: EOIE-UNION-REFLECTION (agile_subcommand_domain/subcommand.spi, target shape in Spiral), EOIE-MEMORY-LINKS (eoie_agile_mutation/agile_mutation.spi) and DIB-MODERNIZE (kino fsi_chain.ex). Backups are in job tmp todo-source/*.bak. The generated .rs is unchanged (comments). Finding: the automatic file mapping is weak (titles cite files in passing, e.g. SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM -> scripts/build.ps1), so placement needs per-item judgment; items in the Tibia lane and design-prose targets were left alone. Usage note: agile todos takes the backlog root first, then the paths.")
inl note_of_todo_as_source_4 () = Note ("TODO-AS-SOURCE", 4u32, "2026-10-17T19:19Z", "main-thread", "15:19 main-thread next grain, designed not built: agile todos joins each TODO(ID) block with the backlog row of that ID (the scan already gets the backlog root as its first operand) and prints the status after the id, plus tally stale=N (block whose item is Done) and unknown=M (no such item). Placement: a new small crate agile_todo_status (status lookup by the Task anchor + agile_task_text tuple_field 6, a typed todo_status union Open | Stale | Unknown), because agile_todo_lines is at 846 of the 1000-line gate and agile_todo_scan at 678. JSON adds a status field next to id.")
inl note_of_todo_as_source_5 () = Note ("TODO-AS-SOURCE", 5u32, "2026-10-17T19:52Z", "main-thread", "15:52 main-thread, user decision 2026-10-17 15:5x: tag format is // TODO: ID text (tag right after the colon, IDE TODO patterns respected; the ID is the leading upper-case token with a dash; TIBIA, HOPACSOTA and BLOG are the only dashless IDs, never used as tags). Done today: agile todos joins each tagged block with its backlog row and reports stale (item Done) and unknown (no item) blocks, via a new census export eoie_agile_task_status.")
inl note_of_todo_as_source_6 () = Note ("TODO-AS-SOURCE", 6u32, "2026-10-17T20:13Z", "main-thread", "16:12 main-thread, user 2026-10-17: tags also accept a single upper-case word (TODO: TIBIA ...), so dashless IDs work. Stale tags leave the code by a deterministic rule (Jira-style state change + comment): agile todos --apply removes a block whose item is Done and archives its text as a note on that item; an unknown tag creates the item from the block's first line instead of being deleted; open tags stay. Without --apply it only reports, exit 0.")
inl note_of_todo_as_source_7 () = Note ("TODO-AS-SOURCE", 7u32, "2026-10-17T20:21Z", "main-thread", "16:21 main-thread, user decision 2026-10-17: an item created by agile todos --apply for an unknown tag gets triage defaults: Story, P4, D3, Planned, tags todo,triage, title cut from the block's first line to 3-7 words; the TODO stays in code as an open tag.")
inl note_of_todo_as_source_8 () = Note ("TODO-AS-SOURCE", 8u32, "2026-10-17T20:47Z", "main-thread", "16:47 main-thread done (unstaged): tag format // TODO: ID text, with a single upper-case word accepted too; todo_id lives once in agile_todo_lines and is exported (eoie_agile_todo_id) so agile_todo_scan stays under the 1k gate (it hit 1,079 when the parser was copied into it); the status join prints stale/unknown lines and stale=N unknown=M; both existing tags migrated (agile_mutation EOIE-MEMORY-LINKS, cells fsi_chain DIB-MODERNIZE). Release build: 197 Rust + 12 Spiral tests, topology ok. Next: agile todos --apply (remove Done blocks into notes, create triage items for unknown tags).")
inl note_of_todo_as_source_9 () = Note ("TODO-AS-SOURCE", 9u32, "2026-10-17T23:28Z", "main-thread", "19:28 main-thread --apply deferred on evidence: the repos hold 2 tags today, both open, so it would do nothing yet. Design ready: agile_todo_scan is at 971 of the 1,000-line gate, so the standing join (status lookup, stale/unknown lines, tallies) moves into a new crate agile_todo_standing that scan calls once with (backlog text, rendered findings, apply flag, root). Apply only touches blocks whose TODO line starts with a comment (a trailing tag after code is reported, never cut); the block extent is the finding's line plus its continuation lines up to the next path:N prefix. Stale: cut the lines, then add a note via a new export of agile_task_notes note_added (author todos-apply, body = path:line + the block). Unknown: create the item via a new export of agile_task_creation agile_add with the triage defaults (Story, P4, D3, tags todo,triage, title = text after the id cut to 7 words). Build when the first stale or unknown tag appears.")
inl note_of_todo_as_source_10 () = Note ("TODO-AS-SOURCE", 10u32, "2026-10-18T13:08Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 09:08 main-thread migration slice: 3 open items now live as TODO blocks at their code sites, harvested by eoie agile todos (stale 0, unknown 0): FLOAT-TEXT-CANONICAL above lib/spiral/sm'_real.spir format_real, DATE-TIME-PORTABLE above lib/spiral/date_time.spi date_time_switch, KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR at the top of apps/cells/lib/spiral/cells/runner.ex (its first strangler slice). With DIB-MODERNIZE and EOIE-MEMORY-LINKS that is 5 tags across the repos; --apply becomes useful once some of them close.")
inl note_of_todo_as_source_11 () = Note ("TODO-AS-SOURCE", 11u32, "2026-10-19T12:30Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 08:30 lap 1 grain: harvest health: eoie agile todos over spiral/lib and apps/cells/lib finds 4 TODO blocks (FLOAT-TEXT-CANONICAL, DATE-TIME-PORTABLE, DIB-MODERNIZE in fsi_chain.ex:184, KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR in runner.ex:1), stale 0, unknown 0; note: todos with only the backlog root scans 4 files and finds 0, so callers must pass code paths Next: move one more open item to its code site (KINO-HOT-PATHS above memo.ex's key hashing)")
inl note_of_todo_as_source_12 () = Note ("TODO-AS-SOURCE", 12u32, "2026-10-19T13:01Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 09:01 lap 2 grain: KINO-HOT-PATHS now lives as a TODO block at its code site, above compiler_binaries_print in apps/cells/lib/spiral/cells/memo.ex:145 (comment only, 2 lines under 120 columns, carrying the measured cost and the cache plan). eoie agile todos over spiral/lib + apps/cells/lib: files=141 todos=5 stale=0 unknown=0 (FLOAT-TEXT-CANONICAL, DATE-TIME-PORTABLE, DIB-MODERNIZE, KINO-HOT-PATHS, KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR). Side finding: runner.ex:1's block line is longer than 120 columns; the formatter does not wrap comments, so the 120-column rule for TODO blocks is by hand. Next: move one more item whose work is a single code site (candidate: SPIRAL-DIAGNOSTIC-LOCATIONS at spiral_compiler.fs UnboundTypeName, about line 50208)")
inl note_of_todo_as_source_13 () = Note ("TODO-AS-SOURCE", 13u32, "2026-10-19T13:46Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 09:46 lap 3 grain: RUST-NIGHTLY-EVERYWHERE now has a TODO block at its single remaining code site, dice/contract/build.ps1:50 above the cargo +nightly-2024-07-14 wasm build (2 lines of 117 columns: why the pin exists and the lift being probed); eoie agile todos over dice/contract: todos=1 stale=0 unknown=0. First TODO block outside spiral, so a harvest over all items needs the dice root too: callers pass code roots explicitly (lap 1 note), and the set to pass is now spiral/lib, spiral/apps/cells/lib and dice/contract. SPIRAL-DIAGNOSTIC-LOCATIONS was skipped on purpose: its site is spiral_compiler.fs, and a comment there forces a compiler rebuild while main packs builds. Next: when CELLS-ACCEPT-TUPLES settles, move CELLS-GATE's failed_targets plan to summary_lines in notebook.ex")
inl note_of_todo_as_source_14 () = Note ("TODO-AS-SOURCE", 14u32, "2026-10-19T14:27Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 10:27 lap 4 grain: SPIRAL-INDEPENDENT moved to its code site, dice/scripts/init.ps1 above the polyglot link (2 lines, 117 and 120 columns: what still routes through polyglot and the prepared rewrite). eoie agile todos over dice/scripts + dice/contract: todos=2, stale=0, unknown=0; rewrite-deps.ps1's anchors still match (dry run unchanged at 22 files), since the block sits above, not on, the anchored line. Harvest roots in use now: spiral/lib, spiral/apps/cells/lib, dice/scripts, dice/contract. Open design point answered by practice: blocks for items whose fix is a pending lab patch go on the line the patch will change (here the link line the rewrite keeps and the deps paths it rewrites), so applying the patch is also the moment to cut the block. Next: CELLS-GATE and CELLS-REPIN are unblocked now that CELLS-ACCEPT-TUPLES is Done (helper lane 1, 100), but their patches are queued; no block until they land")
inl note_of_todo_as_source_15 () = Note ("TODO-AS-SOURCE", 15u32, "2026-10-19T14:52Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 10:52 lap 5 grain, one open design point answered by experiment (the formatter's handling of TODO blocks): SpiralCompiler format on a copy of lib date_time.spi re-wraps comments to 120 columns, and the DATE-TIME-PORTABLE block's first line is 125 columns, so it came out as // TODO: ... calendar / // fields and format / //     in plain Spiral...: the wrapped tail becomes an unindented comment line between the head and its indented continuation, which breaks the block shape the harvest relies on (head line plus indented continuation lines). The second lib block (sm'_real.spir FLOAT-TEXT-CANONICAL) has the same risk if over 120. Rule for TODO blocks, now with a reason: every line at most 120 columns, so the formatter leaves the block alone (my memo.ex, dice init and dice contract blocks are 113-120). Fix for the two lib blocks: rewrap them to 120 by hand in the next lib edit window (lib is frozen for the core switch until main's all-clear), or teach the formatter to treat // TODO: blocks as unwrappable (compiler lane, SPIRAL-FORMATTER). Next: after the all-clear, rewrap the two lib blocks and recheck the harvest")
inl note_of_todo_as_source_16 () = Note ("TODO-AS-SOURCE", 16u32, "2026-10-19T15:30Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 11:30 lap 6 grain, two more design points answered from the harvest itself: (1) link = the first token after TODO: is the eoie item ID, and eoie agile todos checks it against the backlog (stale=0 unknown=0 over cells, dice scripts and contract, lib: 7 blocks); (2) status and progress stay in the item, not in the block: the block holds where and what, and it is rewritten when the facts at that site change. Did that for dice/contract/build.ps1's RUST-NIGHTLY-EVERYWHERE block (now 3 lines, each at most 120 columns, harvest shows it whole): it records today's result (nightly-2026-10-18 + build-std + mvp builds once rust-lld gets --allow-undefined or near-sys >= 0.2.14 is locked). Open: the formatter wrap (two lib blocks, after the all-clear) and the no-comments rule wording for TODO blocks.")
inl note_of_todo_as_source_17 () = Note ("TODO-AS-SOURCE", 17u32, "2026-10-19T15:47Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 11:47 lap 7 grain, a full census of the migration: eoie agile todos over spiral, dice, alphabet, blog, dir-tree-html and polyglot (10,677 files, 2,690 scanned, 5.5 s) finds 12 TODO blocks, stale 0, unknown 0, so every block links to a live item. Width check: 3 blocks have lines over 120 columns. runner.ex (KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR, Elixir: mix format never rewraps comments, so harmless) and the 2 known lib blocks (date_time.spi DATE-TIME-PORTABLE, sm'_real.spir FLOAT-TEXT-CANONICAL), which are the only ones the Spiral formatter would break. So the post-switch rewrap list stays at exactly those 2. The harvest also reports skipped generated:5,619 and syntax:2,363 files, which keeps generated outputs from adding false TODOs.")
inl note_of_todo_as_source_18 () = Note ("TODO-AS-SOURCE", 18u32, "2026-10-19T16:22Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 12:22 lap 8 grain: rewrapped the two lib TODO blocks the formatter would break: date_time.spi:107 (DATE-TIME-PORTABLE) and sm'_real.spir:591 (FLOAT-TEXT-CANONICAL), each now 3 lines of at most 120 columns, same words, LF kept, comment-only hunks (the loop.while' hunks in sm'_real.spir are the switch migration, not mine). eoie agile todos over lib: todos=2 stale 0 unknown 0, both harvested whole. Helper lane 1: lib/spiral/date_time.spi and sm'_real.spir touched (comments only). Left: the no-comments rule wording for TODO blocks")
inl note_of_todo_as_source_19 () = Note ("TODO-AS-SOURCE", 19u32, "2026-10-19T16:48Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 12:48 lap 9 grain: the no-comments rule wording for TODO blocks was drifted at its source: the memory rule (no-comments-self-explanatory) told writers to link with TODO(BACKLOG-ID):, but eoie's harvester reads the ID only as the token right after TODO: (agile_todo_model/model.spi:328-339 todo_id: colon at marker+4, then a capitalized token closed by a space), so the documented spelling would give unlinked TODOs. All 12 blocks in the repos already use the harvested form. The rule text now says: // TODO: BACKLOG-ID what is left, continuation lines //     ..., each at most 120 columns, TODO(ID): yields no ID. Left in the epic: confirm the formatter keeps a rewrapped block byte-identical")
inl note_of_todo_as_source_20 () = Note ("TODO-AS-SOURCE", 20u32, "2026-10-19T16:50Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 12:50 lap 9b grain: formatter design point answered by running it (installed Release, format --stdin --path, two short processes): both rewrapped TODO blocks come back byte-identical (3 lines each in date_time.spi and sm'_real.spir), so a block of at most 120 columns is stable under the formatter. format --check still reports both files as would change, but for pre-existing code lines (sm'_real.spir over-120 at 178, 205 ...), the known lib drift (79 lib files), not the TODO blocks. All four open design points of this epic are now answered (link = first token after TODO:, status in the item, formatter-stable at 120, the rule wording)")
inl note_of_todo_as_source_21 () = Note ("TODO-AS-SOURCE", 21u32, "2026-10-19T17:25Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 13:25 lap 10 grain: post-switch harvest of spiral (eoie agile todos, 3.3 s): 10 blocks, stale 0, unknown 0. The 5 ID-linked blocks all point at open items (DATE-TIME-PORTABLE Active 50%, DIB-MODERNIZE Planned, FLOAT-TEXT-CANONICAL Planned, KINO-HOT-PATHS Active, KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR Active), so no block outlived its item. The other 5 are upstream core text in lab/CORE-LIB-CONSOLIDATE/candidate-v0 and candidate-v1 (TODO: This..., TODO: Put...; listm.spi:120, serializerm/utils.spir x3), counted as ID-less; they leave the harvest when those lab copies are promoted or deleted after the switch. Next: per-repo migration step 1 = a harvest rule check that a block whose item is Done is reported (stale today means unknown ID only?)")
inl note_of_todo_as_source_22 () = Note ("TODO-AS-SOURCE", 22u32, "2026-10-19T17:45Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 13:45 lap 11 grain: the stale rule answered by a probe (scratch file, two blocks): eoie agile todos reports stale (item is Done) for a block naming CORE-LIB-CONSOLIDATE (Done) and unknown (no backlog item) for NO-SUCH-ITEM-XYZ, counts stale=1 unknown=1. So stale means exactly the Done case and the harvest already enforces that a block dies with its item; today's stale=0 over spiral means no block outlived its item. All design points of the epic are now answered by experiment; what remains is the migration itself, which needs the user's go (destructive by the epic's own definition)")
inl note_of_todo_as_source_23 () = Note ("TODO-AS-SOURCE", 23u32, "2026-10-19T22:52Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 18:52 lap 19 grain: harvest after main's core-v1 migration of dice, alphabet and polyglot: spiral 10 blocks, dice 2, alphabet 0, polyglot 0, stale 0 and unknown 0 in all four. Spiral's 5 ID-less upstream blocks still live in lab/CORE-LIB-CONSOLIDATE/candidate-v0 and candidate-v1; CORE-LIB-CONSOLIDATE is Done now, so deleting those two lab copies (lab rule: promote or delete when Done) would drop them from the harvest, a call for main. Dice's SPIRAL-INDEPENDENT block (init.ps1:22) is replaced by rewrite-deps.ps1's own text when that lands. Next: the per-repo migration, on the user's go")
inl note_of_todo_as_source_24 () = Note ("TODO-AS-SOURCE", 24u32, "2026-10-19T23:31Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 19:31 lap 21 grain, migration prototype on a copy (the epic allows autonomous tries on copies): lab/TODO-AS-SOURCE/migrate-prototype.py writes 5 TODO blocks for compiler-side items (CALYX-BACKEND, OPENQASM3-BACKEND, EBPF-C-PROFILE, GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS, LEAN-BACKEND) at their codegen sites, each at most 120 columns, into a copy only (it refuses the tree file and a copy that already has them). Harvesting the copy found 0 blocks: eoie agile todos skips files over 1 MiB as large (agile_todo_model/model.spi:59 largest_scanned_bytes 1048576, :234 SkipLarge), and spiral_compiler.fs is 11 MB, which is also the large:1 in every spiral harvest so far. So the single largest code site cannot hold TODO blocks today; the migration needs either a size exemption or a bounded scan for that file in eoie (an eoie-lane change, recorded here for main to route), or compiler TODOs living next to the tests instead. Next: route the size limit to the eoie lane; then rerun the prototype harvest")
inl note_of_todo_as_source_25 () = Note ("TODO-AS-SOURCE", 25u32, "2026-10-20T00:00Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 20:00 the 1 MiB harvest limit was sent to the eoie lane (git-0e) as main asked: exempt the compiler path, raise the limit, or scan large files line by line")
inl note_of_todo_as_source_26 () = Note ("TODO-AS-SOURCE", 26u32, "2026-10-20T00:45Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-19 20:45 eoie-lane (request from helper lane 2 via main): agile todos skipped spiral_compiler.fs (11 MB) as large because agile_todo_model largest_scanned_bytes was 1 MiB; raised to 32 MiB (whole-file read with NUL check stays cheap at that size), boundary test block added (11 MB scanned, 32 MiB+1 skipped), the old 2 MB large probe moved to 40 MB; model.spi 7/7. Ships with release job-1792457142086 (the 20:10 run regenerated the owner on pack 11; its test stage failed only on a flaky port fixed since).")
inl note_of_todo_as_source_27 () = Note ("TODO-AS-SOURCE", 27u32, "2026-10-20T02:33Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 22:33 user 2026-10-19 21:4x: review how the spread TODO comments pay off and what more eoie can leverage (TODO-SOURCE-PAYOFF-REVIEW).")
inl note_of_todo_as_source_28 () = Note ("TODO-AS-SOURCE", 28u32, "2026-10-20T02:49Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-19 22:49 eoie lane queue for next run, from main's review (TODO-SOURCE-PAYOFF-REVIEW notes 2-3), in order: (1) todos --json emits key todos twice, list then count (todo_scan.spi 170-177); (2) single-word IDs only if they exist in the backlog, harvest only TODOs that start a comment (spiral_compiler.fs TODO in commented-out code reads as item I), before any --apply; (3) harvest roots stored in the backlog; (4) agile show lists live code sites; (5) agile check TODO rules; (6) Done refuses or folds blocks; (7) todos --record auto-notes; (8) --tagged/--id filters; (9) README still says 1 MiB cap (now 32 MiB). Queued after job stop (EOIE-JOBS note 25).")
inl note_of_todo_as_source_29 () = Note ("TODO-AS-SOURCE", 29u32, "2026-10-20T04:44Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-20 00:44 eoie lane, main's review items (note 28) in source, awaiting the full regen and release: (1) --json count key is todo_count; (2) a TODO counts only where it starts a comment (no earlier leader outside strings on the line), and a single-word ID only when the backlog declares Task (WORD,; dashed IDs always; (3) agile roots <root> [add|drop <path>...] keeps HarvestRoot rows under a harvest_root union in state/agile.spi and a bare agile todos <root> scans them; (6) agile set <id> <p> Done refuses and lists the blocks still naming the item (quiet eoie_agile_todo_sites export from the scan crate, no --apply cut yet); (8) --tagged and --id <ID>; (9) README 32 MiB plus the join rules. Decision on (4): no sites in agile show, because a show would pay the 10 s harvest; agile todos <root> --id <ID> over the stored roots is the one-call site lookup that notes should cite instead of line numbers. Left: (5) check rules and (7) --record auto-notes, which need recorded sites first.")
inl note_of_todo_as_source_30 () = Note ("TODO-AS-SOURCE", 30u32, "2026-10-20T12:20Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-20 08:20 eoie lane: (7) agile todos <root> --record in source (crate agile_todo_record, 861 lines): harvests tagged blocks over the stored roots, diffs them with TodoSite (ID, path:line) rows from the previous record, writes one note per task whose blocks appeared, moved or vanished (author todos), and rewrites the rows in one guarded write; a vanished block now leaves a note instead of going unnoticed (the EOIE-UNION-REFLECTION case). (5) agile todos prints wide=N and a line per TODO block over 120 columns in a Spiral file. Notebooks ledger.spi and record_cli.spi. Remaining from note 28: (5) check rules beyond width.")
inl note_of_todo_as_source_31 () = Note ("TODO-AS-SOURCE", 31u32, "2026-10-24T02:25Z", "main", "2026-10-23 22:2x main grooming: Paused by rule (Active, no note since 2026-10-20T12:20Z, no child item noted in 3 days). Resume when picked; progress kept at 50%.")
inl note_of_todo_as_source_32 () = Note ("TODO-AS-SOURCE", 32u32, "2026-10-24T11:49Z", "main", "2026-10-19 laps folded from the deleted history ledger (docs/agents/history/2026-10-19-backlog-laps, HISTORY-BACKLOG-LAPS-DRIFT): lap 12 14:10 (subagent2): sibling harvest: dice 2 blocks (contract/build.ps1:50 RUST-NIGHTLY-EVERYWHERE, scripts/init.ps1:22 SPIRAL-INDEPENDENT, both open), polyglot 0, alphabet 0; stale 0 unknown 0 in all three -> next: migration needs the user's go | lap 13 14:32 (subagent2): harvest coverage completed for the non-lane repos: i574n.github, blog, dir-tree-html 0 blocks each (with lap 12: dice 2, polyglot 0, alphabet 0; spiral 10); every linked block names an open item -> next: migration on the user's go | lap 14 15:00 (subagent2): blocked on the user's go for the migration (all design points answered; harvest stale 0 unknown 0 across 7 repos) -> next: user's go | lap 17 16:43 (subagent2): unchanged -> next: user's go | lap 21-drive 19:31 (subagent2): FINDING via a copy-only prototype (migrate-prototype.py, 5 compiler TODO blocks): eoie agile todos skips files over 1 MiB (model.spi:59), so spiral_compiler.fs (11 MB) can never hold harvested TODO blocks; eoie-lane change needed -> next: main routes the size limit to the eoie lane")
inl note_of_todo_as_source_33 () = Note ("TODO-AS-SOURCE", 33u32, "2026-10-25T12:14Z", "eoie", "08:14 eoie: the user's 10-25 08:0x request revisits this epic. EOIE-AGILE-PER-REPO (phase 1: per-repo agile files with CLI aggregation) is the step before it, and its pre-registered benchmark includes layout C (per-repo files plus TODO blocks) to judge this epic's migration on token, latency and contention numbers rather than design alone.")
inl note_of_todo_as_source_34 () = Note ("TODO-AS-SOURCE", 34u32, "2026-10-31T03:21Z", "main", "10-30 23:21 main: lab/TODO-AS-SOURCE is ARCHIVED (user, 10-30 22:5x: the lab dir didn't work and drifted; drafts don't live outside the repo). Archive 20261030-232045 under %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\archive. Restore this folder with: pwsh i574n.github/scripts/scratch-archive.ps1 -Root C:\\home\\git\\spiral\\lab -Restore '%LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\archive\\20261030-232045\\manifest.tsv' -Only 'TODO-AS-SOURCE\\*' -RestoreTo C:\\home\\git\\spiral\\lab. Resume this item's work in its real place in the repo, not in lab.")
inl note_of_todo_as_source_35 () = Note ("TODO-AS-SOURCE", 35u32, "2026-10-31T20:23Z", "kit", "10-31 16:2x kit (same repair): Paused-by-rule -> Planned. This is a deliberate destructive migration (the user, 10-17: try autonomously with backups), not abandoned work; silence was the only reason it was paused. It needs a fresh decision on scope when picked: which repos, with the backups and dry-run that EOIE-AGILE TODO markers now support (KIT-WIP-TODO-MARKERS).")
inl note_of_todo_as_source_36 () = Note ("TODO-AS-SOURCE", 36u32, "2026-11-01T04:51Z", "main", "main 00:5x LAB CODE IS SAFE (user 11-01 00:2x: 'check for all lab references in history... make sure we didn't lose that code... to repurpose them or groom their tasks'). This item's lab/TODO-AS-SOURCE folder (1 files: migrate-prototype.py) is archived in %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\archive\\20261030-232045 (blob-verified). Restore it into its proper repo place with: pwsh C:\\home\\git\\i574n.github\\scripts\\scratch-archive.ps1 -Restore '%LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\archive\\20261030-232045\\manifest.tsv' -RestoreTo <repo dir> -Only 'TODO-AS-SOURCE/*'. Next step for the owner at its next pull: restore the useful files in place as incomplete drafts (Cells heals them in place), or record why it starts over.")
inl note_of_todo_as_source_37 () = Note ("TODO-AS-SOURCE", 37u32, "2026-11-02T16:01Z", "main", "main 11-02: the scope decision this epic was waiting on now has a measurement plan, TODO-VS-AGILE-BENCH (pre-registered metrics and decision rule; run on the node). Parent of KIT-WIP-TODO-MARKERS and TODO-VS-AGILE-BENCH, named here because children no longer depend on parent epics.")
inl disk_cleanup () = Task ("DISK-CLEANUP", "Periodic disk cleanup for this machine: measure build outputs (spiral-bin bin/obj/runs/scratch/native, compile-memo, cargo target dirs, Kino builds, job tmp caches, Downloads bundles) and prune what's provably regenerable on a schedule, with a report of what was freed. The memo stores already prune themselves (compile 2 days, native 14 days)", Story, P1, D3, 70u32, Planned, "", "disk,ops,team:main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_disk_cleanup () = Planning ("DISK-CLEANUP", Minutes 60u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_1 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 1u32, "2026-10-17T19:13Z", "main-thread", "15:13 main-thread inventory (read-only, directory level; a full per-file walk of spiral-bin took over 25 min and was stopped, so the tool must use directory-level stamps or the ledger): spiral-bin has 12 bin-pack*-backup compiler snapshots from 10-15/10-16 holding 6.5 GB (only the newest blessed pack and its predecessor are useful); runs has 1,842 run folders since 09-26, 894 older than 7 days, about 2.1 GB (sampled 50 = 121 MB), and the fast-tier fold cache and cost-report read results.tsv, so prune keeps results.tsv and drops the rest; compile-memo (53k files) and native-memo prune themselves. Earlier totals: eoie/src/target 16.8 GB, spiral/target 10.1 GB, toolchains 5.1 GB. C: had 15.3 GB free at 14:54. Design: an eoie verb with a dry-run report (path, size, last use, rule that made it prunable), rules as a typed union, never deleting tracked files.")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_2 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 2u32, "2026-10-17T19:55Z", "main-thread", "15:55 main-thread, user decision 2026-10-17: pack backups keep the newest 2 (bin-pack1x/1y); the 10 older ones (1f..1w) were deleted with approval, freeing 5.35 GB (C: 19.8 GB free). That is the first retention rule for the tool: compiler pack backups keep 2.")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_3 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 3u32, "2026-10-18T13:24Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 09:24 main-thread: C: is down to 11.9 GB free (19.8 GB at 10-17 15:55). Runs: 1,890 folders, 1,676 older than 7 days; a 60-folder sample keeps 2 MB of results.tsv and could drop 50 MB of worker files and logs, about 1.4 GB over all old runs, so runs are not where the 8 GB went (likelier: cargo target dirs during today's eoie regeneration and the memo stores; a directory-level size pass of eoie/src/target, spiral/target and spiral-bin/compile-memo is next). Pack backups already at the keep-2 rule (bin-pack1x, bin-pack1y). No deletion done: only the pack-backup rule has the user's approval so far.")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_4 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 4u32, "2026-10-18T13:28Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 09:28 main-thread directory-level sizes (robocopy /L /BYTES /XJ, minutes not hours): spiral-bin 38.3 GB = bin 10.25, runs 7.68, eoie-regeneration 5.71 (today, the eoie regeneration in progress at 09:22), native 5.31, toolchains 4.99, scratch 1.04, the 2 kept pack backups 0.58 each, compile-memo 0.52, core-src 0.36, obj 0.32; outside it eoie/src/target 16.7 GB, spiral/target 9.9 GB (both unchanged since yesterday), Temp 2.0 GB. So the 8 GB drop is mostly today's eoie-regeneration. Candidate rules for the dry-run tool: eoie-regeneration keeps the last run, runs older than 7 days keep only results.tsv (about 1.4 GB), native build outputs older than the native memo's 14 days.")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_5 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 5u32, "2026-10-19T12:21Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 08:21 helper-lane lap 1: re-measured (directory listing plus robocopy /L byte totals, no walk of spiral-bin): C: has 20.4 GB free (11.9 GB on 10-18 09:24, so the eoie regeneration space came back); spiral-bin/eoie-regeneration is now 1.99 GB in 12 run folders from 10-17 14:48 to 10-18 19:32 (the oldest alone 92 MB), so the keep-last rule frees about 1.9 GB; runs has 1,908 folders, 1,708 older than 7 days (the keep-results.tsv rule, about 1.4 GB per the 10-18 sample); pack backups still at keep-2 (bin-pack1x 10-16 14:46, bin-pack1y 10-16 17:20). No deletion: only the pack-backup rule has user approval; the other two rules stay dry-run candidates.")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_6 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 6u32, "2026-10-19T13:11Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 09:11 helper-lane lap 2: wrote and ran the dry-run spec for the eoie verb: lab/DISK-CLEANUP/dry-run.ps1 (read-only; robocopy /L byte totals, one pass per rule, deletes nothing) with the three rules as data: pack backups keep the newest 2 (0 candidates now), eoie regeneration keeps the last run (11 folders, 1.50 GB), runs older than 7 days keep their tsv files (robocopy /MINAGE:7 excluding results.tsv, fast-tier.tsv, toolchain.tsv: 8.04 GB). Total 9.84 GB prunable today, far above the 10-18 sample estimate for runs (1.4 GB), so worker files and logs of old runs are the biggest win; the fold cache and cost reports read only results.tsv. Still no deletion: only the pack-backup rule has user approval.")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_7 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 7u32, "2026-10-19T14:29Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 10:29 helper-lane lap 3: typed the three rules for the future eoie verb as lab/DISK-CLEANUP/rules.spi (lab sketch, not eoie src): union retention = KeepNewest i32 | KeepLastRun | KeepTsvAfterDays i32, union approval = UserApproved | DryRunOnly, rule = root + retention + approval, and decide/applies that return Keep or Prune per entry (newest rank, age, results-tsv flag); a DryRunOnly rule always keeps, so only the approved pack-backup rule can prune. Probe via try.ps1 C (bounded, 6 s): 6 decisions over runtime values exit 13 as predicted (old pack backup pruned, regeneration and runs computed as prune but held by DryRunOnly, results.tsv kept). OPEN QUESTION for the user (recorded, not asked): approve KeepLastRun for spiral-bin/eoie-regeneration (1.50 GB) and KeepTsvAfterDays 7 for spiral-bin/runs (8.04 GB)? Flipping either is one approval field in the rule table.")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_8 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 8u32, "2026-10-19T22:24Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 18:24 helper-lane lap 6: measured one more root the rules sketch has to cover. spiral-bin/candidates held 19 private-cache compiler builds from today, about 0.7 GB: pack8b 107 MB, pack9/pack10 64 MB each, five 56 MB single-patch Debug candidates, and eleven 13 MB pack8 bisect and splice dirs. I removed my own ten whose patches have landed (nominal-with, export-walk, field-splice, and the seven pack8-<item> bisect dirs, about 240 MB), after checking that no process command line referenced them. That is the KeepLastRun-style rule a future verb should apply: a candidate dir can be pruned once its pack is accepted. Main's pack dirs and the two candidates still riding pack 11 (fsx-pointer, assign-delphi) stay.")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_9 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 9u32, "2026-10-21T22:31Z", "x", "2026-10-21 18:31 x: measured x's build outputs (all regenerable by pwsh scripts/build.ps1): x/target 583 MB in 3,607 files, of which release (cargo) is 570 MB and build, sim-mix-build and sim-mix-world about 6+3+3 MB; empty leftovers bless, determinism, e2e-eight, e2e-metrics. apps/sim/_build 7 MB, deps 5 MB, frames/build 3 MB. About 0.6 GB in total: x is not a disk-pressure source; C: has 75 GB free of 930, and the os_mon disk_almost_full alarm in every BEAM log comes from the default 80% threshold, not from x. Nothing pruned.")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_10 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 10u32, "2026-10-22T00:17Z", "main", "20:17 main re-measure (robocopy /L byte totals, read-only, job main-disk-sizes, 3 min): C: 69.5 GB free (x saw 75 GB at 18:31; the os_mon disk_almost_full alarm is its default 80% threshold, as x noted). Growth since 10-18 09:28: eoie/src/target 16.7 -> 26.4 GB, spiral/target 9.9 -> 15.8 GB, TEMP 2.0 -> 10.4 GB; spiral-bin: bin 10.4, native 5.4, toolchains 5.3, eoie-regeneration 4.2 (30 runs), runs 3.9, candidates 3.2 (tonight's night-release candidates included), scratch 1.4. dry-run.ps1 rules: 8.11 GB prunable (runs older than 7 days keeping tsv 3.92, eoie-regeneration keep-last 4.19); nothing deleted. Uncovered by any rule: the two cargo target dirs (42 GB together) and TEMP; next for this item: a cargo-target rule (profile dirs untouched for N days, cargo can rebuild them) and a TEMP owner census by top-level folder, both dry-run first.")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_11 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 11u32, "2026-10-22T15:59Z", "main", "2026-10-22 11:59 main: TEMP owner census (read-only Python scan of %TEMP%, the item's planned next step): 14.46 GB in 4,353 top-level entries. claude 7.4 GB = two session folders: fc1568a6-... 5.3 GB (another session, not touched) and this session 2.1 GB. Then spiral_cells 757 MB (live Cells runs), wsl.3.0.3.0.x64.msi 351 MB (157 h idle, no owner in the backlog), wctFB21.tmp 128 MB (190 h), main's lib copies lib-ns/lib-helpers/lib-dt/lib-concat 116 MB each, about 20 eoie-hidden-window-*/eoie-timeout-tree-* dirs of 70-99 MB each (41-78 h idle, eoie's), spiral_kino 89 MB, spiral-kino-compiler 81 MB (118 h). Pruned by a pre-written rule, own files only: in this session's scratch, every top-level item idle > 6 h, not used by today's live scripts and not named anywhere in the backlog text (352 items, 0.75 GB: old pack/candidate core copies, lua/calyx/census probes, tree-sitter cli...); 74 cited items kept; plus 1,232 finished task outputs idle > 6 h (0.77 GB, one 730 MB). Freed 1.5 GB in all. main's 4 lib copies are kept: all differ from today's tree, but without their bases that cannot tell unlanded work (lib-ns is the staged tree-lib move) from an older snapshot, so the delete-after-landing rule keeps them. Suggested owners for the rest: eoie for its hidden-window and timeout-tree dirs (about 1.6 GB, a KeepLast rule), and the user for the WSL msi.")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_12 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 12u32, "2026-10-23T15:09Z", "main", "2026-10-23 11:09 main: C: 62.0 GB free (69.5 at 10-22 20:17). Candidates rule run (scratch candidates-prune.ps1, rule fixed before the run): prune a spiral-bin/candidates dir only if written > 48 h ago, no live process command line or staged script (TEMP pack-*, lib-land, compiler tmp/scripts) names it, a note by main/main-thread/helper-lane ties it to us, and no open item or note from the last 48 h cites it. 2 dirs pruned (jp-debug, x-budget, 99 MB); 54 kept, mostly because an open item cites them (hopac-depth 627 MB via HOPACSOTA-HOPAC-DEPTH, speed-ab-old via COMPILER-PACK-SPEED-AB). So candidates frees space only as items close: the future verb should key candidate retention on the citing item's Done state. Biggest uncovered roots stay the two cargo target dirs (42 GB at 10-22).")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_13 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 13u32, "2026-10-24T00:25Z", "main", "2026-10-23 20:25 main: C: 58.6 GB free (62.0 at 11:09). Rerun of the candidates rule (dry run): would free 0 MB, since every dir was written within 48 h, is cited by an open item or a recent note, or isn't tied to main by a note. Space frees as items close, as note 3 predicted. Status corrected to Active (the rule exists and runs).")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_14 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 14u32, "2026-10-24T11:49Z", "main", "2026-10-19 laps folded from the deleted history ledger (docs/agents/history/2026-10-19-backlog-laps, HISTORY-BACKLOG-LAPS-DRIFT): lap 4 11:32 (subagent): blocked: user decision on the two dry-run rules (regeneration keep-last 1.50 GB, runs keep-tsv 8.04 GB); rules typed in lab rules.spi -> next: the user's approval of either rule | lap 5 12:19 (subagent): user decision pending -> next: the user's approval | lap 7 19:54 (subagent): re-read: C: at 9.4 GB free (Erlang raised disk_almost_full at 18:32) -> next: add a candidates rule; main's pack caches about 250 MB")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_15 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 15u32, "2026-10-24T21:54Z", "main", "main 17:5x measure (nothing deleted): today's gate and pack scratch in %TEMP% is 1.55 GB over 37 folders. lib-gate-kit is 1,035 MB (work\\<name> copies of lib, one per gate, now with a base snapshot each since 16:5x); pack-next* cores are 20-57 MB each. Rule proposal, to apply next block: lib-gate-kit\\work\\<name> may be removed once verdict-<name>.txt is GREEN and the slice has landed (the land log says 'landed <name>') or the item is withdrawn; a pack-next* folder may be removed once a later pack that composes it is installed, keeping the newest 2. The proof/e3 copies already clean themselves up.")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_16 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 16u32, "2026-10-25T09:21Z", "main", "10-25 05:21 main: applied note 10-24 17:54's rule for lib-gate-kit\\work: removed 34 folders (1,035 MB) whose verdict file is GREEN and whose name has a 'landed <name>' line in an eoie job log from the last 4 days. Kept: FISCAL-GENERIC, LIB-DICTM, LIB-GAPS-X, LIB-LISTM-RUNTIME-PATHS and LIB-UNICODE, GREEN but with no land line in range. Tool: scratchpad gate-work-sweep.ps1 (dry run by default). C: free 33.8 GB after.")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_17 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 17u32, "2026-10-25T20:41Z", "main", "10-25 16:41 main: measure (robocopy list mode, fast): C: free 24.9 GB (28.1 at 14:09). spiral-bin by folder: bin 10.36 GB, eoie-regeneration 6.61 (kit's), runs 6.58, native 5.65, toolchains 5.31, candidates 4.64, scratch 2.11, eoie-regen-target 1.75. The note 10-19 rules (regeneration keep-last, runs keep-tsv) still await their own measure-then-apply pass. The candidate rule to pre-register next: keep the candidate dirs named by any pending pack's dll.txt plus pack-spelling; remove the rest once their pack is installed. The old full-file disk walk took more than 3 h and was stopped as stale; use robocopy /L /BYTES for measures.")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_18 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 18u32, "2026-10-25T20:57Z", "main", "10-25 16:57 main: candidate rule, written before applying: a main pack candidate under spiral-bin\\candidates (pack-next*, pack11-13, combined-pack, pack-after-night, night-pack, night-release, pack-fmt-only, pack-fmt-next) is removed once its pack is installed or superseded and no pending pack's dll.txt names it. pack-next4 (the pending 5m review's candidate) and pack-spelling (the respell tool) stay. Other lanes' candidates (hopacsota-*, hopac-depth, eoie-*-measure, x-profile, lua-function-limits, waits-census, format probes) are not mine and stay. Applying to: combined-pack, night-pack, night-release, pack-after-night, pack-fmt-next, pack-fmt-only, pack-next3, pack-next3b, pack-next5, pack-next5b, pack-next5c, pack-next5d, pack-next5e, pack-next5f, pack-next5h, pack-next5i-hopacsota, pack-next5k, pack-next9-debug, pack11, pack12, pack13.")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_19 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 19u32, "2026-10-25T21:00Z", "main", "10-25 17:00 main: candidate rule APPLIED: 21 main pack candidate dirs removed (the list in n18), 1,236 MB freed, C: 25.3 GB free. Checked before: no process command line referenced any of them; pack-next4 and pack-spelling kept by name (the script refuses both); other lanes' candidates untouched.")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_20 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 20u32, "2026-10-26T00:09Z", "main", "10-25 20:09 main: runs keep-tsv rule (10-18 note), refined from its readers before applying. The readers are fast-tier.ps1 (results.tsv; its fold stamp is results.tsv size and mtime) and cost-report.ps1:63 (the earliest worker*.jobs.tsv's CreationTime as the run's start, else the folder's). Rule: in a matrix run folder (name single-flight|hopac|hopacsota|gpt5parallel-yyyyMMdd-HHmmss, holding a results.tsv, no subfolders, last written more than 7 days ago), remove the worker<n>-<n>.{jobs.tsv,results.tsv,log,log.out} files except the earliest worker*.jobs.tsv. Keep results.tsv, toolchain.tsv, fast-tier.tsv and every other file. Folders of other shapes (mesa and vulkan DLL runs, wgsl entries, about 0.6 GB) are other lanes' and stay. Refuse if any process command line names one of the folders. After: check every results.tsv stamp is unchanged. Dry run: 68 folders, 488 files, 364 MB (runs is 6.58 GB in 218 folders, already pruned from 1,890 by someone since 10-18). Tool: scratchpad runs-keep-tsv.ps1 (dry run by default, -Apply).")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_21 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 21u32, "2026-10-26T00:09Z", "main", "10-25 20:09 main: runs keep-tsv APPLIED: removed 488 files (364 MB) from 68 old matrix run folders; all 68 results.tsv stamps unchanged, so fast-tier's fold cache stays valid; C: 26.7 GB free. Still open from the 10-19 rules: eoie-regeneration keep-last (6.61 GB, kit's folder: ask kit after the rename) and native outputs older than the native memo's 14 days (5.65 GB, measure next).")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_22 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 22u32, "2026-10-26T00:17Z", "main", "10-25 20:17 main: native outputs measured. spiral-bin\\native is native/<mode>/samples/<sample>/<backend> build dirs that the native tier rewrites: single-flight 2,747 MB, hopac 2,103 MB, gpt5parallel 701 MB, hopacsota 236 MB. Rule, written before applying: a native/<mode> folder whose mode no script can produce is unreachable, regenerable build output and is removed. Evidence: every native path is built as native/<mode> (test.ps1:52, bench-native.ps1:14), mode comes from test.ps1's ValidateSet (single-flight|sf|gpt5parallel|gp|hopacsota|hs) or ConvertTo-SpiralMode (env.ps1:15, which throws on anything else), and no literal native\\hopac exists in spiral, eoie or i574n.github/scripts (a literal grep is no proof alone: native/single-flight isn't written literally either). native/hopac (last written 10-13, from the gpt5parallel mode's old name) matches; the other three are live modes and stay. Refuse if any process command line names the folder.")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_23 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 23u32, "2026-10-26T00:20Z", "main", "10-25 20:20 main: native\\hopac removed by the n22 rule: 2.07 GB freed, C: 28.8 GB free; no process named it. The same unreachable-mode rule covers 4 more spiral-bin folders: bin\\hopac 9,838 MB (an old compiler build from 10-13 plus about 4 GB of .r2r-old-* leftovers from 10-08), obj\\hopac 219 MB, core-src\\hopac 13 MB and scratch\\hopac 12 MB. Every bin/obj/core-src/scratch path is keyed by a mode that build.ps1:22 (ConvertTo-SpiralMode or a literal single-flight/gpt5parallel pair), env.ps1:125 or test.ps1:30 produces, never hopac. No env var, process command line or loaded dotnet module names bin\\hopac, and spiral/apps/compiler/tmp has no literal reference. Still required before removal: the stem search for hard-coded bin\\hopac DLL paths in other lanes' %TEMP% scripts. The Grep tool times out on %TEMP%, and the recursive Select-String version was stopped because it is a heavy scan inside tibia's PR-28 block (the positive control, a single-flight\\SpiralCompiler stem, does find 3 scripts). Runs after block end; if it finds 0, remove the 4 folders (about 10.1 GB).")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_24 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 24u32, "2026-10-26T01:10Z", "main", "10-25 21:10 main: eoie-regeneration rule, from kit (owner) at 21:0x. Nothing reads an older run: compiler-contracts/test-regeneration.ps1 gives each run its own GUID folder (line 40), uses only that one, deletes it on success (line 241) and prunes folders older than 2 days at startup (lines 37-38); the 6.61 GB is runs that threw before cleanup plus -KeepStaged runs, useful only to someone inspecting a failure. Rule: (now) keep only the newest run, delete only while no test-regeneration process runs; (after KIT-RENAME) the renamed script writes spiral-bin\\kit-regeneration, so all of eoie-regeneration is dead and is deleted whole on kit's 'rename finalized' message. Applying only the after-rename form, since the release with the rename starts around 21:17: one deletion instead of two.")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_25 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 25u32, "2026-10-26T08:10Z", "main", "10-26 04:10 main: retired hopac build mode removed: spiral-bin\\bin\\hopac 9.61 GB, obj\\hopac 0.21, core-src\\hopac 0.01, scratch\\hopac 0.01 (freed 9.86 GB; C: 46.7 GB free). The first stem search (recursive over all of %TEMP%) ran 3.5 h without output and was stopped; it was read-only, so there was nothing to restore. The rewrite does one read per file over 28,958 tracked repo files plus %TEMP% to depth 3, then 739 untracked repo files. Positive control: 54 files name single-flight\\SpiralCompiler. Retired-mode references: 1, the backlog note text in agile.spi (history, not a consumer); 0 untracked; 0 running processes with a hopac path. Deleted by the rule written before the search (CLEAR = no consumer).")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_26 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 26u32, "2026-10-26T15:18Z", "main", "10-26 11:18 main: 10-26 11:2x main pass: runs keep-tsv rule (n20) reapplied to the 16 matrix run folders that crossed 7 days since 10-25: 76 worker files removed (131 MB), results.tsv stamps changed 0, so fast-tier's fold cache stays valid. C: 47.1 GB free (46.9 before; 48.1 at 07:00). Earlier today, 06:5x, the measure gave spiral-bin candidates 3.43 GB (main's only remaining dir is pack-next4, whose kit the running 5n review reads, so it stays until install-5n; the rest are other lanes'), runs 6.27, compile-memo 0.48, r2r-cache 0.48, core-src 0.35, bin 0.75. Nothing else is due by a pre-registered rule; eoie-regeneration waits for kit's 'rename finalized' (n24).")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_27 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 27u32, "2026-10-26T19:20Z", "main", "10-26 15:20 main: 10-26 15:2x main: removed main's own finished scratch copies from today, 10 folders, freeing 509 MB: 4 lib-copy typecheck trees (sm-pack, parsing, runtime, testing; each verdict recorded on LIB-MACRO-HEAL), 2 blog diagnostic trees, the cube compile test, the cube proof's runaway reference copy, and 2 small ones. Reparse points were deleted first, deepest first, so no delete recursed through a link. After the removal the live trees count unchanged: spiral lib 76 .spi, polyglot lib 539 files, blog engine 8 .spi. Kept: every apply/proof script, pre-land backups (dir-tree-args-lib/pre-land), and the requeue plan. Finding: cube-diag (my compile test) was 229 MB, from the same robocopy-follows-the-lib-link trap that hung cube-proof-3. Rule recorded in memory: proof copies use /XJD.")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_28 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 28u32, "2026-10-26T22:39Z", "main", "10-26 18:39 main: 10-26 18:39 main: disk measurement, measured, not deleted (deletions only by pre-written rule). C: free 46 GB. Largest roots, files only, reparse points skipped so links aren't double-counted: %LOCALAPPDATA%\\spiral-bin 27.8 GB; %TEMP% 30.2 GB; spiral\\target 25.9 GB; %LOCALAPPDATA%\\eoie\\jobs 0.0 GB; polyglot\\target and eoie\\target do not exist. These three roots hold 83.9 GB, almost 2x the free space. Proposed rule, to pre-register before any sweep: (1) %TEMP% top-level dirs older than 48 h whose name matches a known scratch prefix (registry-execute-run-*, cube-*, lib-*-node-*, pause-requeue excluded while referenced), owner-agnostic only after a reader-lease check; (2) spiral\\target/test and per-run target dirs older than 7 days; (3) spiral-bin runs/ and scratch/ older than 7 days, never bin/ of the installed compiler. main's own registry run dirs are already cleaned after each probe (0 left).")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_29 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 29u32, "2026-10-26T23:23Z", "main", "10-26 19:23 main: 10-26 19:22 main: RULE for main's own %TEMP% share, registered before the per-folder sizes are read (the measurement is running). A path may be deleted only if ALL hold: (1) OWNER: it is a main-made run or proof COPY, never a slice: the registry-execute-run-* / registry-planner-run-* run dirs, '<slice>\\copy' subfolders made by main's proof-check.ps1 scripts, and lib-*-node-* staging copies made by main. Slice folders (apply.ps1, manifests, pre-land undo copies) are never deleted while their chain is queued or their item open. (2) NOT REFERENCED: the path does not appear in any queued or held job's manifest (TEMP pause-requeue plan.json, extras.json, *.json manifests) or in the argv of a running eoie/kit job (eoie job list). (3) IDLE: newest file older than 2 h, and no running job writes under it. (4) LINK-SAFE: reparse points under it are removed first (Delete() on the link, never recursing into a target), and read-only flags are cleared before removal. Everything else in %TEMP% (other teams' folders, unknown owners, toolchain caches) is out of scope for main; the report lists their sizes so their owners can act. Each deletion is logged with path and size on this item.")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_30 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 30u32, "2026-10-27T09:08Z", "main", "10-27 05:08 main: 10-27 05:08 main: per-owner disk measurement, measured (C: 36 GB free, down from 46 GB on 10-26). %TEMP% = 35.7 GB over 1,702 folders. The big ones: claude 16.2 GB (Claude Code session temp, all under C--home-git, broken down per session: fc1568a6 kit 10.65 GB, ac4b4e98 tibia 3.87 GB, 5a6e53fb main 0.58 GB, b7595ac3 x 0.55 GB, 84b53718 fiscal 0.45 GB, 88a146f8 compiler 0.14 GB), lib-gate-kit 4.75 GB (shared gate work folders, one per gate name), eoie-parked-train 2.6 GB (kit, last write 10-24), dice-menu-href 1.1 GB (main's proof copies, rebuilt per run), spiral_cells 0.86 GB. Under n29's rule main may remove only its own run and proof copies, which is about 2 GB here (dice-menu-href's reference/healed copies once its proof chain ends, plus main-named gate work folders); the large shares are other lanes' and were reported to them. No deletion tonight: dice-menu-href proof-3 is queued and reads that folder.")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_31 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 31u32, "2026-10-27T09:42Z", "main", "10-27 05:42 main: 10-27 05:42 main: progress after the per-owner measurement. tibia freed 2,912 MB from its session temp (41 finished proof copies: lib copies, bisect/probe roots and the stage roots of landed lib slices; drafts, scripts and notes kept), and C: went from 36 to 44 GB free. kit acked its 10.65 GB share plus eoie-parked-train with a plan: byte-redundant copies first, then a hash-verified zip of the text-only rest before the originals go, preceded by i574n.github/scripts/unstaged-backup.ps1 (KIT-SCRATCH-WATCH M2), which zips every repo's staged, unstaged and untracked changes with a hash check. kit relayed a user rule from about 10-27 04:2x, 'archive, never delete; delete only provably redundant throwaways after a backup'. main did not see it first-hand and follows it as relayed. main deleted nothing tonight.")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_32 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 32u32, "2026-10-27T11:31Z", "kit", "07:3x kit, archive pass done for kit's share, under the user's 04:2x archive-never-delete rule. (1) Idle cargo target dirs (CACHEDIR.TAG, no process naming them) freed directly as build output: candidate-target 2.77 GB, cargo-check-target 0.73, plus 12 nested targets in fsrc, ge3-fix, fmt-rebase and pending-heal (about 2.1 GB). (2) i574n.github/scripts/scratch-archive.ps1, a content-addressed archive verified before any removal: my scratchpad's 474 folders older than 10-26, minus every folder a live script references (tree-read, agile-records were kept for the running release), came to 345,711 files, 5.27 GB -> unique 0.52 GB -> blobs.zip 0.067 GB, verify failures 0, originals removed; restore with -Restore %LOCALAPPDATA%/kit/archive/20261027-054550/manifest.tsv. A second copy is on the node (C:/home/remote-stage/backups/archive/20261027-054550, both files hash-verified). A restore drill on 4,786 files was byte-identical. (3) %TEMP%/eoie-parked-train (10-24, no live references or processes) archived the same way to %LOCALAPPDATA%/kit/archive/20261027-064527 and removed. C: free went from 36 GB (main's 10-27 report) to 50 GB, with other lanes writing at the same time.")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_33 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 33u32, "2026-10-27T14:51Z", "main", "10-27 10:51 main: main's own temp cleanup (rule n29: own copies only; provably redundant, regenerable scratch), C: now 52.2 GB free. (1) TEMP dice-menu-href reference/, healed/ and normalized/ (2,263 MB): build copies of dice/ui made by the href proof; the heal is LANDED (GRANULAR-HEAL n64), the copies are regenerable by proof.ps1, and the scripts stay. Junctions were deleted first, and dice's deps link was checked intact. (2) Five lib-gate-kit work folders (1,327 MB): LIB-RUNTIME-CASTS, LIB-DATE-TIME-GRANULAR, LIB-CRYPTO-ED25519, LIB-CONSOLE-TRACE-BLOCKS and LIB-JSON-SCAN-ARRAYS. Each was proven landed by a strict byte test: every lib file its candidate copy changed relative to its base copy is now byte-identical in the live lib. Verdict files are kept, and a re-gate would regenerate the folder. Not removed: the other main gate folders whose changed files the live lib has since moved past (landed by the chain logs, but not provable by bytes any more), and every XSD-*, X-* and FISCAL-* folder, which belong to fiscal and x (the kit is shared).")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_34 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 34u32, "2026-10-27T15:28Z", "main", "10-27 11:28 main: main's health sentinel (session scratchpad health-sentinel.ps1, both boxes every 15 min until 21:00) fired twice at 11:23 and 11:2x on local free memory 1,461 and 1,528 MB against its 1,536 MB floor. Both were normal load (six Claude sessions at 6 GB, two heavy jobs; commit 30.7 of 94 GB), not a runaway, so the floor was miscalibrated for today's box. Restarted with -MinFreeMB 1024. The real danger this morning was 0.45 GB (tibia's three WGSL runners, 15 GB), which the 1 GB floor still catches with margin.")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_35 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 35u32, "2026-10-27T17:42Z", "main", "10-27 13:42 main: Disk pass 13:0x-13:3x (C: 50.7 GB free at the start). Freed: LOCALAPPDATA main-cargo-target (0.55 GB, a pure build output of the old export-migrate harness, which no longer sets CARGO_TARGET_DIR; no process named it), and chat-2's VOID copy TEMP main-export-migrate/copy-polyglot-20261027-110147 (0.77 GB) through scratch-archive.ps1, so it can be restored. Finding for a durable rule: the shared lib gate scratch TEMP lib-gate-kit/work holds 48 per-gate folders (3.46 GB, about 50 MB each, every lane's gates) that never expire. A byte-landing census (TEMP lib-gate-work-census.tsv: a folder counts as landed when every .spi its candidate changed against its base is byte-equal in the live lib) proves only 2 (LIB-NEAR-DRAIN landed, LIB-XML-BLANK-LINE no change). 34 are UNPROVEN only because the live lib moved on after they landed. 6 were excluded because a queued chain uses them (radix, nil, list-sugar). A byte proof can't age these folders out, so the fix belongs at write time: gate.ps1/land.ps1 drop a gate's work folder once its land succeeds, keeping only the verdict file it may reuse. Not applied: gate.ps1 and land.ps1 are shared by every lane and land.ps1 is in use now, so this waits for an idle queue and a heads-up to the lanes.")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_36 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 36u32, "2026-10-27T19:37Z", "main", "10-27 15:37 main: INCIDENT and FIX in i574n.github/scripts/scratch-archive.ps1 (unstaged). It followed directory links: EnumerateFiles(folder, '*', AllDirectories) walks through junctions and symlinks, so archiving a folder that holds a link into a live repo archived that repo too, and the removal step (Remove-Item -Recurse) then ran on a folder with live links inside. Found 15:2x: main's 13:15 archive of chat-2's 0.77 GB repo copy (574 own files) had grown blobs.zip to 14.3 GB with a 98 MB manifest. 366k of its entries came through the copy's deps\\spiral link to C:\\home\\git\\spiral, and C: free fell from 50.7 to 35.2 GB. Stopped (main's own task) before its removal step, so nothing outside the copy was touched; the partial run (kit archive 20261027-131545) was removed and C: returned to 49.2 GB. Fix: a stack walk (Get-OwnEntries) that prunes reparse points instead of entering them; each link is written to the run's links.tsv (relative path, Junction or SymbolicLink, target) and not archived as content; -Apply deletes each recorded link itself (FileSystemInfo.Delete) and refuses if new links appeared, then removes the folder; -Restore recreates the links after the files. Positive control (a junction from the fixture to an outside folder): the outside file survived, the manifest held only the 2 own files, links.tsv recorded the junction, the fixture was removed, and restore brought back both files plus the junction with its original target. Rerun on the chat-2 copy: 574 files, 6 links recorded (not followed), 0.77 GB, zip 0.17 GB, done in under a minute, spiral intact. All lanes were told at 15:3x to check any of today's archives of folders containing links. Earlier main archives today (lab folders, the stage folder) had no links.")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_37 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 37u32, "2026-10-27T20:33Z", "main", "10-27 16:33 main: Retention rule for lib gate work folders DRAFTED and proven on copies (TEMP lib-gate-retention/apply.ps1; not yet applied to the live lib-gate-kit/land.ps1, 329461A3). Reading the code: only gate.ps1 reads work\\<Name>, and it deletes and recreates that folder itself at its start (gate.ps1:17). The verdict that gate reuse depends on is the separate verdict-<Name>.txt in the kit root, so a landed gate's work folder is dead weight (about 50 MB each, 48 of them today, 3.46 GB). Change: after a successful land, land.ps1 drops work\\<Name>, unlinking its reparse points first, deepest first (LIB-FS-JUNCTION's test data holds a junction), and prints what it dropped. The verdict file is kept. Apply guards: the anchor (the end-of-land cleanup) must occur exactly once and be the file's last statement; refuse if already applied; refuse while any process runs land.ps1 via -File (matching the script argument, not any mention of the path); back up to land.ps1.pre-retention. Controls: (1) on a land.ps1 copy with a fixture work\\X holding a junction to an outside folder, the patched tail dropped work\\X, unlinked 1 link, the outside file survived, and verdict-X.txt was kept; (2) with a process holding '-File <copy>', the apply refused ('in use by 1 process') and the copy stayed byte-identical. My first two controls were flawed, both caught: the guard first matched the leaf name, then my own apply invocation's -Land argument, before being narrowed to a -File script argument. Apply window: after TRAIN publishes and the nil batch-2 chain's land finishes (it uses land.ps1). The existing 48 folders get a one-off sweep then, landed ones only: those whose names have a 'landed <Name>' line in a land log.")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_38 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 38u32, "2026-10-28T10:41Z", "main", "10-28 06:41 main: 10-28 06:3x-06:4x cleanup, main. (1) land.ps1 retention applied (TEMP lib-gate-retention/apply.ps1, controls GREEN on 10-27): after a successful land, land.ps1 now drops work\\<Name> (links unlinked first, deepest first); verdict-<Name>.txt is kept; backup land.ps1.pre-retention. (2) The existing gate work folders were swept by rule (lib-gate-retention/sweep.ps1: drop a work folder only when its verdict file exists, it is untouched for 1 h, and no gate.ps1 or land.ps1 is running; links unlinked first): 48 dropped, 4.17 GB freed, 3 kept. (3) TEMP apply.ps1 draft folders: 112 untouched since before 10-27 00:00 (and with no file inside newer, and no running process naming them) were ARCHIVED, not deleted, with i574n.github/scripts/scratch-archive.ps1: archive 20261028-063929 in LOCALAPPDATA kit\\archive, 7,691 files, 13 links recorded and not followed, blob verify failures 0. Restore any with -Restore <manifest.tsv> -RestoreTo <dir>. 16 current drafts remain. C: free 49 GB. Remaining scope: the periodic part (a scheduled run of the sweep and the age-rule archive) and spiral-bin bin/obj/runs measurement.")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_39 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 39u32, "2026-10-28T11:24Z", "main", "10-28 07:24 main: spiral-bin measured 10-28 07:2x (scratchpad measure-bin.ps1: an explicit stack walk that skips reparse points, with a 60 s and 300k-file cap per folder; no recursion through junctions), about 29 GB in all. Largest: runs 6.42 GB (246 run dirs), toolchains 5.31 (needed), native 3.46, candidates 3.43 (49 builds), eoie-regen-target 2.63, scratch 2.24 (264k files, last 10-26), probes 0.66, mix-builds 0.65, r2r-cache 0.60, compile-memo 0.42. Done now: eoie-regen-target (a cargo debug/tmp target last written 10-23, before the kit rename; 0 references in kit or spiral/apps, no process naming it, 0 reparse points) removed as a build output. C: free 51 GB. Rules proposed for the periodic part, to record before applying: (a) runs: keep every run id cited in a backlog note (grep agile.spi for single-flight-*), plus the newest 20; ARCHIVE the rest (they are evidence, so restorable, not deleted). (b) candidates: keep any build referenced by a dll.txt, an install script or a running job; remove the rest as build outputs. (c) scratch, probes, mix-builds, cells-results: entries untouched for 7+ days and named by no running process are build outputs to remove. Each rule runs as a dry list first, then a sweep while no gate, land, accept or train is running.")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_40 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 40u32, "2026-10-28T11:26Z", "main", "10-28 07:26 main: Rule (c) applied 10-28 07:2x (TEMP lib-gate-retention/bin-sweep.ps1; refuses while any gate, land, accept, train or matrix run is active). In spiral-bin scratch, probes and mix-builds, entries untouched 7+ days, named by no running process, and containing no reparse point: 175 removed as build outputs (probes 174 from 10-06 and the scratch gpt5parallel build from 10-14, plus mix-builds without links). 11 mix-builds entries containing links (Mix build trees link their deps) were skipped. They need an unlink-first pass, as land.ps1's retention does, before removal. C: free 52 GB, up from 45 GB at 06:15. Open: rule (a) (archive runs not cited by any note, keep the newest 20) and rule (b) (remove unreferenced candidates), the unlink-first pass for those 11, and the scheduling for 'periodic'.")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_41 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 41u32, "2026-10-28T13:35Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-28 09:4x: reclaimed 56 stale .d-bad/.r2r-old backups (33.8 MB) from %LOCALAPPDATA%\\spiral-bin\\bin\\single-flight\\SpiralCompiler\\Release\\net11.0 after compiler-lane confirmation that NO install/rollback reads them. Evidence: no script in spiral/eoie/kit/i574n.github creates or reads .d-bad (the 32 .d-bad-* are a 07-05..10-06 one-off from a since-removed op); kit bundle rollback is slot-based (backup_domain, restoration_* over i32 slots) with its own staging, zero `d-bad` strings; the 27 .r2r-old-* are build.ps1 Unlock-SpiralOutputs transients swept at the next build (build.ps1:148), only an immediate same-build restore (build.ps1:133), never a persistent rollback. Left 3 from today 05:30 for the next build sweep. Rest of DISK-CLEANUP scope (build bin/obj, runs, scratch) unchanged.")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_42 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 42u32, "2026-10-30T00:26Z", "main", "10-29 20:26 main, from x: the node's BEAM raised ':alarm_handler {:set, {{:disk_almost_full, C:\\}}}' at 20:26 during a mix run. The node's C: has 28.6 GB free. OTP's alarm is percentage-based, so builds still run, but space keeps shrinking. Tonight's archive-by-move of pre-retire mirrors (C:\\home\\remote-archive\\20261029-*: main 7, x 18, kit 8, fiscal 3, compiler 1) protected the data but freed NOTHING, because the moves stayed on the same volume. NEXT SESSION (main, after the pack-7 install, as its own job): inside the archived mirrors ONLY, free the regenerable build output (_build, target, node_modules, mix-build*, .cache; allowed directly by archive-never-delete). FIRST record and UNLINK every junction (each mirror's spiral\\deps -> C:\\home\\git\\spiral\\deps; memory unlink-mirror-junctions-first), because a recursive delete through a junction deletes the shared deps checkout. Dry-run the size first, guard every delete with safe-delete.ps1's Remove-TreeSafely, and keep the sources. Measure free space before and after. Not done tonight on purpose: there is no time for the junction-safety steps before 21:00.")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_43 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 43u32, "2026-10-30T00:30Z", "main", "10-29 20:30 main, SIZED (read-only walk of node C:\\home\\remote-archive at 20:3x, reparse points skipped; PowerShell 7 Get-ChildItem doesn't follow junctions without -FollowSymlink): C: has 28.6 GB free; tonight's archives total 11.2 GB, of which 8.2 GB is regenerable build output in 24 dirs. Two cargo target dirs hold 7.5 GB of it: 20261029-kit-preretire\\dryrun\\target 4.1 GB and 20261029-x-preretire\\xv29\\spiral\\target 3.38 GB. Then kit-libwin-a 0.25, xv29\\x 0.14, kit-libwin-b 0.10, main land1029\\fiscal 0.10 GB, and so on. Freeing all 24 would take C: to about 36.8 GB free. Plan as n42 (junctions recorded and unlinked first; Remove-TreeSafely; sources kept). The dryrun and xv29 target dirs alone give 7.5 GB, and their owners (kit, x) can free them. Note: fiscal's 8 newly attributed mirrors are not yet archived or measured.")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_44 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 44u32, "2026-10-30T01:14Z", "main", "10-29 21:14 main, from kit's candlib check (no live dependency: SPIRAL_LIB unset at User and Machine scope; 0 of 11 queued node jobs and no env var reference an archived mirror; 12 script hits, all finished one-offs). One stale DEFAULT is main's: node C:\\home\\remote-stage\\lib-perf\\node-lib-perf.ps1 (the 10-26 sm-linear perf experiment, job-1793048449505-main-sm-linear-ab) defaults -SpiralLib to C:\\home\\remote\\candlib\\spiral\\lib\\spiral, and its sm-linear-node-*.ps1 use candlib-smlinear. Both mirrors are archived tonight. To rerun it, pass -SpiralLib to a freshly synced mirror (REMOTE-SYNC-OK), or move candlib back from C:\\home\\remote-archive\\20261029-kit-preretire\\candlib. Archived mirrors aren't touched by tomorrow's build-output cleanup beyond their _build/target trees.")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_45 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 45u32, "2026-10-30T02:33Z", "main", "10-29 22:33 main, DONE for tonight's archives, brought forward from P5 because node C: was shrinking about 1.6 GB/h (28.6 GB at 20:26 -> 25.34 at 22:3x). Script: C:\\home\\data\\main-drafts\\night-1030\\node-archive-cleanup.ps1 (pushed via scp, hash-checked, run with -File). It frees a build dir (_build/target/node_modules/.cache/mix-build*) only when a walk proves it has NO reparse point inside, re-checks just before removal, and verifies the deps checkout afterwards. Dry run, then -Apply: 19 dirs removed, C: 25.05 -> 32.76 GB (+7.7 GB). Link targets that existed before still exist: 43 of 111, identical to the dry run (the other 68 are mirror-internal links to pre-move paths). Deps checkout intact. Manifest: node C:\\home\\remote-archive\\cleanup-manifest-20261029-2233.tsv. SKIPPED by design: 20 dirs with a link inside (Elixir _build trees holding mix's links); a link-aware pass would be needed to free those. GROWTH SOURCE: mirrors written in the last 3 h total only 0.8 GB, while the node's TEMP holds 9 GB, likely job scratch and build output. Not touched tonight (live jobs write there); owners are asked to size and free their own TEMP output.")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_46 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 46u32, "2026-10-30T02:52Z", "main", "10-29 22:52 main, ROOT CAUSE of the node disk growth, found via git-a6's TEMP census: the node's WSL Ubuntu VM (up since 10-25 15:16) has a WSLg compositor CRASH LOOP since 22:23. /usr/bin/weston dumps about 107 MB every 2-3 min into the node's %LOCALAPPDATA%\\Temp\\wsl-crashes (10 dumps, 1,068 MB by 22:39; msrdc /wslg keeps reconnecting), about 3 GB/h. A second source: 4.4 GB of spiral_cells per-cell dirs left by killed or capped runs (Cells removes them only on a clean finish, kit confirmed at targets.ex:62 / cells.ex:72). CONTAINED, not fixed: C:\\home\\data\\main-drafts\\night-1030\\node-temp-contain.ps1 zip-archives all but the newest dump (963 MB -> 9 MB, verified by entry size), and frees spiral_cells dirs older than the earlier of now-3h and the oldest live job start minus 30 min (link-free, named by no live process): 353 dirs, 4.36 GB. Node C: 32.49 -> 37.7 GB. Runs hourly overnight (cron). NOT done, the USER's call: stopping the loop itself. Options: (a) turn off WSLg via guiApplications=false in the node's .wslconfig (persistent config), then wsl --shutdown; (b) wsl --shutdown alone (the VM may hold the user's work); (c) find why weston started crashing at 22:23 (dumps kept in C:\\home\\remote-archive\\wsl-crash-dumps).")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_47 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 47u32, "2026-10-30T10:25Z", "main", "10-30 06:25 main: containment LAPSED 01:26-06:2x (the box crash killed main's session and its hourly cron); node C: 35.58 -> 30.9 GB, all from the still-running WSLg crash loop. Run again now and the cron restored. The loop itself still needs the user's call (.wslconfig guiApplications=false and/or wsl --shutdown).")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_48 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 48u32, "2026-10-30T10:38Z", "main", "10-30 06:38 main: fiscal archived 20 finished night mirrors to node C:\\home\\remote-archive\\20261030-fiscal-night (retire-mirrors.ps1, junctions intact 27/27). A link-safe dry run of node-archive-cleanup.ps1 -Match ^20261030- finds only 0.1 GB freeable (4 of 8 build dirs; the other 4 hold links), so it's not applied. Not worth it, and nothing was removed. The node's C: (30.9 GB) is driven by the WSLg dump loop, archived hourly; the loop is still the user's call.")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_49 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 49u32, "2026-10-30T23:02Z", "main", "10-30 19:02 main: node growth measured (node-growth2.ps1, new files in the last 3 h by top-level dir, low priority). C: free 33.27 GB (18:06) -> 29.80 GB (18:5x), about 4.8 GB/h. Almost all of it is x's queue mirrors: C:\\home\\remote\\xqueue1 3.34 GB, xqueue2 1.88 GB, xqueue3 0.19 GB; Temp wsl-crashes about 1 GB/3 h (archived hourly) and spiral_cells 0.43 GB. The archives under C:\\home\\remote-archive (20261030-*) hold only 0.1 GB of link-safe build output (dry run). x was asked to free each finished queue mirror's target/ and _build/ (link-safe). Method note: the first scan's top-folder-mtime filter missed nested writes; scan every top-level dir's new files instead.")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_50 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 50u32, "2026-10-31T00:41Z", "main", "10-30 20:41 main: node C: recovered 29.80 GB (18:5x) -> 38.86 GB (20:3x containment). x freed its queue mirrors as asked (xqueue1 3.34 -> 0.26 GB, xqueue2 1.88 -> 0.09, xqueue3 0.19 -> 0.09), and the 20:3x containment archived 9 WSL dumps and removed 23 stale spiral_cells dirs (+1.1 GB). Box C: 308.6 GB free, so no box pruning is needed. Box spiral-bin by size: toolchains 3.20 GB, kit-regeneration 1.99 (kit's), native 0.93 and compile-memo 0.62 (both self-pruning), scratch 0.50, runs 0.30, r2r-cache 0.24. The schedule part shares PROFILE-MIRROR-TO-NODE's open question (a lasting trigger is the user's call); until then the hourly node containment cron covers the node, which is the only disk under pressure.")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_51 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 51u32, "2026-10-31T03:21Z", "main", "10-30 23:21 main: lab/DISK-CLEANUP is ARCHIVED (user, 10-30 22:5x: the lab dir didn't work and drifted; drafts don't live outside the repo). Archive 20261030-232045 under %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\archive. Restore this folder with: pwsh i574n.github/scripts/scratch-archive.ps1 -Root C:\\home\\git\\spiral\\lab -Restore '%LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\archive\\20261030-232045\\manifest.tsv' -Only 'DISK-CLEANUP\\*' -RestoreTo C:\\home\\git\\spiral\\lab. Resume this item's work in its real place in the repo, not in lab.")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_52 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 52u32, "2026-10-31T12:21Z", "main", "10-31 08:21 main measurement: box C: 294.3 GB free of 930.8 GB (no pressure). spiral-bin: mix-builds 0.04 GB, scratch 0.51, runs 0.41, lib-land-undo 0.00; spiral-bin-candidates 1.97 GB (pack10 needed until its install is verified); kit\\archive 0.78 GB; %TEMP% 8.78 GB, mostly main's 10-31 scratch trees (headings-scratch, headings-step2, lp-land, union-reds, debris/pg-land): prune those after the queued lands and wave 2 finish. Node C: 38.3 GB free at 07:07 (containment hourly).")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_53 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 53u32, "2026-10-31T14:45Z", "main", "10-31 10:45 main: pruned main's finished scratch in %TEMP% (C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\Temp, checked before deleting): 10-31 trees (headings-scratch, headings-step2, lp-land, lp-sm, union-reds, debris-land, pg-land, land-0820, w6-scratch, spl-ab, logs; 0.11 GB) and 10-30 proof dirs (lambda-parens-prove 1.2 GB, backend-switch-strict, headings-proof, selector-control, otp-pg-control, otp-pg-proof, typed-call-probe, process-logs-proof; 1.16 GB freed). 31 junctions (to spiral/deps, apps/cells/deps, workspace) were unlinked one by one BEFORE the recursive deletes; the live targets were verified intact (deps, workspace, cells deps = mix.lock's 2). Everything pruned had landed or been recorded; each land keeps its own undo under spiral-bin\\lib-land-undo. Left in TEMP: claude (6.2 GB, the harness's session task outputs and scratchpads: not main's to prune), final-wave and scaffold-proof (in use).")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_54 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 54u32, "2026-10-31T21:16Z", "main", "10-31 17:16 main: afternoon prune of main's finished %TEMP% scratch (final-wave, paired-*, fsharp-*, land-0820, w7-scratch, lib-toml, scaffold-proof, strict-*, ...): 6 junctions unlinked first, live targets verified (spiral deps, workspace, cells deps 2, cells priv 8 files), 0.11 GB freed. NODE TREND: C: free fell from 38.3 GB (07:07) to 29.0 GB (17:13) despite hourly containment; main's own node work dirs are only ~1.4 GB (spiral mirror 0.97), so the decline is other lanes' work and WSL crash dumps (~9 archived per hour). Still above the 20 GB alert line; worth a look by the node's owners if it keeps falling.")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_55 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 55u32, "2026-11-01T03:40Z", "main", "main 23:4x WIP groom (user 23:3x: drift/stall, too much waiting): Active -> Planned. a periodic chore, not continuous WIP; it runs at each pre-night groom.")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_56 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 56u32, "2026-11-01T04:50Z", "main", "main 00:5x LAB CODE IS SAFE (user 11-01 00:2x: 'check for all lab references in history... make sure we didn't lose that code... to repurpose them or groom their tasks'). This item's lab/DISK-CLEANUP folder (2 files: dry-run.ps1, rules.spi) is archived in %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\archive\\20261030-232045 (blob-verified). Restore it into its proper repo place with: pwsh C:\\home\\git\\i574n.github\\scripts\\scratch-archive.ps1 -Restore '%LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\archive\\20261030-232045\\manifest.tsv' -RestoreTo <repo dir> -Only 'DISK-CLEANUP/*'. Next step for the owner at its next pull: restore the useful files in place as incomplete drafts (Cells heals them in place), or record why it starts over.")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_57 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 57u32, "2026-11-02T14:00Z", "x", "2026-11-02 09:59 x: DECISION RECORD (typed; groom of user waits, user 11-02: 'decisions waiting on me get met autonomously/deterministically with tests/benchs/typefeatureset modeling/backups'). kind=ByDefault. QUESTION: How should the node's WSLg weston crash loop be stopped: guiApplications=false + wsl --shutdown, wsl --shutdown alone, or investigate? RULE (written before acting): Autonomous part only: check whether the node's %LOCALAPPDATA%\\Temp\\wsl-crashes still gains dumps; if not, the loop ended and nothing changes. If it does, the .wslconfig edit and wsl --shutdown are a USER step, because they are node-wide config and the shutdown kills other lanes' WSL work (same as IGGY-MULTI-MACHINE). BACKUP/UNDO: .wslconfig copied to .wslconfig.bak. Dumps zip-archived (node-temp-contain.ps1), never deleted. OWNER: resilience. NEXT: Count dumps newer than 1 h in the node's wsl-crashes and apply steps 2-3 only if the count is above 0. VETO: the user can reverse this at any time; the backup above is the undo.")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_58 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 58u32, "2026-11-02T14:34Z", "x", "2026-11-02 10:28 x: DECISION RECORD v2 (user 11-02: 'why mine? try healing autonomously/deterministically with tests/benchs/typefeatureset/backups the pending decisions'). Only git commits (the user's standing rule) and the harness hook config (the harness itself says Claude must not change it) stay user-only. Everything else gets a precondition, a check and an undo; actions only the user's accounts can take get a non-blocking default. kind=ByTest. RULE: Reverses x's earlier override. Agent applies guiApplications=false iff dumps still grow in the node's wsl-crashes AND kit job list plus kit window list on the node show no WSL/k3s/iggy job running. Steps: back up .wslconfig, edit, wsl --shutdown, then PASS iff WSL starts, the k3s smoke passes, and no new dump appears in 30 min. BACKUP/UNDO: Undo: restore .wslconfig.bak and wsl --shutdown. OWNER: resilience.")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_59 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 59u32, "2026-11-03T02:40Z", "main", "main 11-02 22:4x NODE t1101 DISK (compiler flagged: Erlang raised disk_almost_full during a Cells mix run at 21:55; C: 226 GB used, 6.2-7.1 GB free). Freed: node-disk-cleanup.ps1 -Apply reclaimed 898 MB (Diag output; the 1 GB WSL crash dump was skipped, likely locked). Sized: C:\\home\\remote-archive 20.1 GB (the lanes' pre-retire backups of 10-29..10-31: x-groom 6.0 GB, tibia-stage 2.6, fiscal-night 2.4, kit-archive-seed.tar 1.6, tibia-pre-retire 1.5, fiscal-preretire2 1.4, x-preretire 1.3, kit/main/compiler/fiscal preretire 0.3-0.9 each, rescue-2026-10-28.tar 0.6, wsl-crash-dumps 0.3); %LOCALAPPDATA%\\wsl 15.8 GB (the Ubuntu for k3s + iggy: keep); scoop 13.5; C:\\home\\git 14.6; .nuget 6.8 (a cache, but live node builds restore from it); profile-mirror 4.5; .rustup 3.3; .cargo 1.6; remote mirrors ~12 (kit-verify 4.9 GB the largest); remote-stage 9.7 (many lanes' small stages); spiral-bin ~9 (kit-regeneration 2.0, toolchains 1.75). The ~80 GB here leaves the rest to Windows/Program Files. DECISION FOR THE USER (backups, so not main's to delete under archive-never-delete): keep remote-archive's 20 GB of pre-retire archives, or move them to vultr's larger disk (CLOUD-NODE-BACKUP) and free the node. Main deletes no backup without that.")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_60 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 60u32, "2026-11-03T19:42Z", "resilience", "11-03 15:3x resilience (main's node-disk heads-up): WSL/weston crash loop ACTIVE AGAIN (dumps 1068MB, written 15:29). CLEARED them (1068MB -> 0); node C: 19GB free after (eased from main's 6.2GB via main's archive cleanup). LOOP-SOURCE STOP (guiApplications=false + wsl --shutdown, n57/58) is BLOCKED: k3s server (pid 27541) + iggy-server (pid 8461) + k8s pods ARE running on the node WSL, so n58's precondition \"no WSL/k3s/iggy job running\" is NOT met - a shutdown would bounce active k3s/iggy. So the shutdown needs a quiet-k3s/iggy window or the user's call (node-wide + disrupts k3s/iggy). INTERIM: clear the dumps each heartbeat (the node-temp-contain hourly cron looks lapsed); flagged main to restore it. Per-heartbeat node-dump-clear added to my recurring durability tasks (the node disk feeds my mirrors, so keeping it clear is resilience's concern).")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_61 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 61u32, "2026-11-04T21:43Z", "kit", "11-04 23:4x kit: LIVE INCIDENT on the NODE (laptop-ts), not the box: C: hit 0 GB free about 17:28 and broke TRAIN24's regen (\"There is not enough space on the disk\" in agile_state_transient_schema_domain and kit_agile_policy) plus a size run. Cause: C:\\Users\\alias\\AppData\\Local\\spiral-bin\\kit-regeneration held 11 staged workspaces of 0.7-1.25 GB (trains that stop before cleanup, failed size runs, and two deliberate -Keep runs); test-regeneration.ps1 -KeepStaged leaves them by design and nothing ages them out. I stopped TRAIN24, removed all 11 (none in use), free went 0 -> 12.9 GB, and relaunched as TRAIN25. FIX TO ADD here: a retention rule for kit-regeneration on every machine (remove staged workspaces older than N hours that no running job names, run before each regen or train), and a free-space floor check at train start that refuses with a clear message instead of failing mid-regen.")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_62 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 62u32, "2026-11-04T22:16Z", "kit", "11-04 18:3x kit: the FREE-SPACE FLOOR half is BUILT (unstaged) in kit/scripts/launch-train.ps1, right after the toolchain parity check: one Invoke-NodePwsh call reads the node's C: free GB and counts kit-regeneration staged workspaces older than 6 h; under 5 GB the launcher throws \"node C: has N GB free (< 5 GB ...); K staged workspace(s) older than 6 h sit in ...; free space first, refusing a node train\", otherwise it logs \"node disk ok: N GB free, K stale staged workspace(s)\". So a full disk refuses in seconds at launch instead of failing 70 min into regen like TRAIN24. Parses (CRLF kept, 182); the probe ran live the launcher's way: \"10.7 0\" -> 10.7 GB, 0 stale. Deliberately NOT auto-deleting: the retention half (age out stale workspaces no running job names) stays open for this item.")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_63 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 63u32, "2026-11-05T02:20Z", "resilience", "11-04 star-exchange (hurdle C, resilience): the NODE (laptop) C: hit ~0GB ~17:28 (compiler remote-stage dirs + kit 11 regen workspaces; half-broke fiscal t1101 restore). Resilience already runs node-utilization-monitor.ps1 (DISK-LOW alarm at <15GB free on the mirror node). RULE: delete stage/regen dirs when the result is noted; check disk+RAM next to load. main is TAKING a unified node-disk-free alarm WITH resilience - the existing monitor + its 15GB floor is the mechanism to build on.")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_64 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 64u32, "2026-11-05T05:31Z", "main", "11-05 01:30 main NODE DISK: deleted the node's stale Visual Studio installer payload C:\\Users\\alias\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\bgnajmgc (1.46 GB: Win11 SDK + VC++ component packages from an 11-03 20:00 install; regenerable download cache; no VS installer process running, no process referencing it). Kept DiagOutputDir (the Remote Desktop client's own rotating trace, 40 MB, written now). With the polyglot mirror (4.31 GB) the node went 10.5 -> 16.6 GB free tonight, back above the 15 GB alarm.")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_65 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 65u32, "2026-11-05T19:57Z", "main", "main 11-05 15:5x NODE DISK ALARM (main's share, with resilience): the monitor DETECTS (node-utilization-monitor.last.log 15:45 'DISK-LOW: 13GB') but never DELIVERS. node-utilization-monitor.ps1:64 notes via 'kit story note' (not a kit verb, exit 2) to REMOTE-OFFLOAD-FIRST (no longer in the backlog), with *> $null, so every WASTE/DISK-LOW alarm since then was dropped silently. Routed to resilience with the fix (agile note to a live item, keep rc, log NOTE FAILED). Today's 13 GB came from 20 dead profile-mirror .tgz.part uploads (6.82 GB, MAIN-ORPHAN-SWEEP n33), now deleted (node 20 GB free); resilience landed the .part prune (unstaged). Box C: 183 GB free, no box cleanup needed this round.")
inl bundle_wisdom () = Task ("BUNDLE-WISDOM", "Wisdom mined from the 2026 zip bundles and bootstrap_llm files (user 2026-10-17)", Story, P2, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "eoie,history", "current", "current")
inl eoie_lease () = Task ("EOIE-LEASE", "From the January 2026 bootstrap bundles (BUNDLE-WISDOM note 4): one time-box authority for agile begin/lease; a write class allowed during WRAP so closeout writes are never blocked; lease rollover on every new user turn (a stale lease survived into the next turn 5+ times); start-at for resumed chats; status and mutation must agree", Story, P2, D4, 25u32, Planned, "", "kit,agile,lease,team:kit", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_eoie_lease () = Planning ("EOIE-LEASE", Minutes 180u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_eoie_lease_1 () = Note ("EOIE-LEASE", 1u32, "2026-10-22T09:29Z", "eoie", "05:29 link, not a duplicate: tonight added a second authority, the TREE lease (a release reading eoie holds it; the PreToolUse hook blocks writes under the leased root; D turns it into a typed tree-read window class with eoie window claim). This item's time-box lease (agile begin/lease) stays separate, but its 'a write class allowed during WRAP' point applies to both: the backlog lives outside eoie (i574n.github), so agile closeout writes are never blocked by an eoie tree lease. Keep that property when the tree lease moves into eoie (no backlog root under a leased tree).")
inl note_of_eoie_lease_2 () = Note ("EOIE-LEASE", 2u32, "2026-10-22T21:37Z", "eoie-lane", "17:37 pure model drafted and tested (scratch f5/src/agile_time_box_model, HostFree + LibReferenced), covering the bundle points against today's agile_lease_terms, which derives running/wrapping/expired from two booleans. (1) One time authority: phase_at (box, now) computes the phase from started/budget/wrap. (2) A write class for WRAP: write_class CodeWrite | CloseoutWrite | ReadOnly. Closeout writes are never refused, even after expiry; code writes need Running, and a refusal names the phase. (3) Rollover: 'rolled box turn_started' restarts the clock on a new user turn and never moves it back, the fix for the stale lease that survived into the next turn 5+ times. (4) Start-at: box_start StartNow | StartAt mark for resumed chats. (5) Status and mutation agree by construction: both go through 'with_phase forall result.', so they cannot read different clocks. 3 test blocks; Cells 3/3 pass on the first compile; standards 0 findings after re-laying one line. Next (compile): agile_lease_terms reads its phase through this model, and agile begin/lease write a box row with its start mark.")
inl note_of_eoie_lease_3 () = Note ("EOIE-LEASE", 3u32, "2026-10-24T02:23Z", "main", "2026-10-23 22:2x main: consolidated pendencies covered here (EOIE-CONSOLIDATED-INTAKE, class a): LEASE-1, LEASE-2, LEASE-3, LEASE-5, LEASE-6, LEASE-8 (one lease authority, adopt a clock, configurable budgets, a lease for any workspace, pause). Their 85-minute container parts are parked with EOIE-CONSOL-BUNDLE-ERA.. Spec text: Downloads/eoie-consolidated/agile_eoie.md.")
inl note_of_eoie_lease_4 () = Note ("EOIE-LEASE", 4u32, "2026-10-24T23:44Z", "eoie", "19:43 Provisional estimate 180m from the grooming rule (EOIE-AGILE-CHECK-FIELDS note): D4 at 25%. Replace it with milestone sizing.")
inl note_of_eoie_lease_5 () = Note ("EOIE-LEASE", 5u32, "2026-10-28T10:30Z", "eoie", "10-28 groom (user via main): Paused. Last note 2026-10-24, nothing queued. Next step: check whether its remaining milestone shipped in TRAIN2c (installed kit.exe A5B55974, 10-28 05:25). If it did, close it with that evidence; if not, size the remaining slice from its last milestone note and resume it through agile next.")
inl note_of_eoie_lease_6 () = Note ("EOIE-LEASE", 6u32, "2026-10-31T23:49Z", "kit", "10-31 19:5x kit, pause re-decision (main pre-night groom; R3 is now \"never auto-pause for silence\"): Paused -> PLANNED. It was paused by the 10-28 silence groom (\"nothing queued\"), not by a blocker, and no note names an external condition. Back in agile next at its priority; its first step stays the one in the 10-28 note: check whether the remaining milestone shipped in a later train (installed kit 4983F9C0, TRAIN6) and close it with that evidence, else do the next milestone.")
inl eoie_authority_audit () = Task ("EOIE-AUTHORITY-AUDIT", "Compilerless audit from the bundle era (BUNDLE-WISDOM note 9): a lint that catches fields or flags read with the wrong authority (a value trusted from a source that doesn't own it), as a one-call eoie verb with Spiral test blocks", Story, P3, D4, 0u32, Planned, "", "kit,audit,team:kit", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_eoie_authority_audit () = Planning ("EOIE-AUTHORITY-AUDIT", Minutes 240u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_eoie_authority_audit_1 () = Note ("EOIE-AUTHORITY-AUDIT", 1u32, "2026-10-24T23:44Z", "eoie", "19:43 Provisional estimate 240m from the grooming rule (EOIE-AGILE-CHECK-FIELDS note): D4 at 0%. Replace it with milestone sizing.")
inl bench_fresh_pair () = Task ("BENCH-FRESH-PAIR", "Honest benchmarking (BUNDLE-WISDOM note 6: stale warm compiler sessions and cache hits once faked regressions): one cold A/B command that tears down warm servers and memos before each compile, interleaves A and B, and rolls back automatically; fits the run ledger and CI-OFFLOAD", Story, P2, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "bench,harness", "current", "current")
inl eoie_resume_compiler () = Task ("EOIE-RESUME-COMPILER", "Continuity from the bundle era (BUNDLE-WISDOM note 10): a 'resume compiler' verb that builds the next turn's agenda from backlog state, recent receipts and open leases, with claim boundaries on reports and seals bound to the tree state; pairs with EOIE-MEMORY recall", Story, P2, D5, 40u32, Planned, "", "kit,continuity,team:kit", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_eoie_resume_compiler () = Planning ("EOIE-RESUME-COMPILER", Minutes 300u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_eoie_resume_compiler_1 () = Note ("EOIE-RESUME-COMPILER", 1u32, "2026-10-21T13:19Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-21 09:19 eoie team: v1 drafted for r30 (scratch apply-resume.ps1; crate eoie_resume): eoie resume <backlog-root> [--team t] prints the next work (agile next 8, WSJF, team filter), recent jobs (job list 12) and machine windows (window list) in one call, for an agent resuming after a context compaction. Each section is the eoie binary's own verb run as a child process (direct spawn), so its output matches the standalone verb exactly; sections come from a reflected union. CLI notebook resume_cli.spi with a two-item fixture backlog. v2: recent notes by author. Compile queued behind tibia's live blocks (detached job eoie-preview-r30).")
inl note_of_eoie_resume_compiler_2 () = Note ("EOIE-RESUME-COMPILER", 2u32, "2026-10-21T13:27Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-21 09:27 eoie team: v2 in the same r30 slice: --as <author> adds a fourth section, that author's 8 newest notes across all items (at, item id, first 160 chars on one line), read in-process through agile_note_model (note_at, declared_from) instead of a new agile verb, since agile_mutation sits near the 1000-line gate. Test resume_as_an_author_adds_that_authors_newest_notes_across_items checks newest-first order, author filtering, the empty case and that the section is absent without --as. Paren and concat5 scans clean; the queued preview compiles v2.")
inl note_of_eoie_resume_compiler_3 () = Note ("EOIE-RESUME-COMPILER", 3u32, "2026-10-22T09:29Z", "eoie", "05:29 status: the v2 slice (--as <author> section via agile_note_model) is part of r30, which is inside release ABC (regenerated and built 10-22, not yet installed: the R13 tail carries to the next session). Verify the fourth section on the installed binary after the tail, then decide the next increment.")
inl note_of_eoie_resume_compiler_4 () = Note ("EOIE-RESUME-COMPILER", 4u32, "2026-10-22T22:03Z", "eoie-lane", "18:03 'eoie resume' is in the tree (eoie_resume) but not in the installed binary ('unknown verb: resume'); it ships with F. after-install-F.ps1 now checks 'eoie resume <backlog> --as eoie-lane' for 'sections=4 author=eoie-lane', the v2 fourth section.")
