inl note_of_cells_everywhere_32 () = Note ("CELLS-EVERYWHERE", 32u32, "2026-11-01T04:50Z", "main", "main 00:5x LAB CODE IS SAFE (user 11-01 00:2x: 'check for all lab references in history... make sure we didn't lose that code... to repurpose them or groom their tasks'). This item's lab/CELLS-EVERYWHERE folder (16 files: backend-run.ps1, backend_test_block.spi, cells-cell-run.ps1, cells-directives-run.ps1, cells-targets-run.ps1, cells-text-run.ps1) is archived in %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\archive\\20261030-232045 (blob-verified). Restore it into its proper repo place with: pwsh C:\\home\\git\\i574n.github\\scripts\\scratch-archive.ps1 -Restore '%LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\archive\\20261030-232045\\manifest.tsv' -RestoreTo <repo dir> -Only 'CELLS-EVERYWHERE/*'. Next step for the owner at its next pull: restore the useful files in place as incomplete drafts (Cells heals them in place), or record why it starts over.")
inl note_of_cells_everywhere_33 () = Note ("CELLS-EVERYWHERE", 33u32, "2026-11-03T18:36Z", "tibia", "11-03 14:4x tibia (MAIN-ORPHAN-SWEEP, claimed by the gate): no team tag and no note since 11-01, so OWNER = main (cross-repo epic; team:main added, other tags kept). TIBIA SHARE MEASURED: 129 of 137 tibia .spi are cell files (a ///- header or static prelude, or ///> tests); the 8 that are not are thin host glue and data: apps/arena bot_dofile, bot_env, bot_prelude, hooks, parity (the Lua host shims the bots are loaded through), apps/arena/standards.spi and src/standards.spi (kit standards rule rows), apps/canary/canary.spi (the canary entry). NEXT STEP for main: a per-repo census with the same test (x, kit, fiscal, lib), then decide per glue file whether it gets a prelude-only test or stays declared glue; then restore the archived lab files per n-latest or record why it restarts.")
inl x_cells_split () = Task ("X-CELLS-SPLIT", "x: make all 22 client .spi files cell compatible by a destructive split: shared backend-neutral domain modules (protocol first: the cN k/n frame header and thread flaws, written by frames and read by assemble from one definition) plus thin Rust glue (serde_json, unicode crates, HTTP); every crate module gains test blocks on ///> rust -d ... and, where pure, gleam and zig, so the same source later serves SIM-IN-SPIRAL on BEAM. Baseline 2026-10-19 02:30: 6 of 22 files run under Cells (prelude, posts, cli, oauth1, wire, transport), the 16 crate files have 0 test blocks, 0 unions and 99 Rust blobs (xc 23, net 20, wire 14, frames 11, oauth1 7, token 6, args 6)", Story, P1, D5, 100u32, Done, "-", "x,cells,gleam,split,gadt,cells-everywhere", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_x_cells_split () = Planning ("X-CELLS-SPLIT", Minutes 600u32, Load Heavy, Assigned Agent)
inl lib_substring_gleam () = Task ("LIB-SUBSTRING-GLEAM", "lib sm'.substring has no Gleam arm: its fallback emits F# .Substring, so lib code using it cannot target Gleam (found by the x lane 2026-10-19, which works around it with its own backend-switched cut). Add a Gleam arm (string.slice on UTF-8 bytes, matching C's byte semantics) and a ///> gleam test block", TaskKind, P3, D2, 100u32, Done, "", "lib,gleam,cells", "current", "current")
inl eoie_job_stop () = Task ("EOIE-JOB-STOP", "eoie job has no stop verb, and a supervisor whose child tree is killed from outside keeps reporting state=running with no children until its timeout (seen 2026-10-19 06:02 main-thread: job-1792403971209-rust-nightly-verify, child tree stopped by command line at 06:00, status still running at 06:02, supervisor pid had no children; I stopped the supervisor by hand). Add job stop <id> (stops the tree, state=stopped) and detect child exit so status reflects it. Also worth a note in job start help: pwsh -File cannot take array args, so pass lists as one comma string", TaskKind, P2, D2, 100u32, Done, "", "eoie,job,dogfood", "current", "current")
inl backlog_laps () = Task ("BACKLOG-LAPS", "Backlog laps (user 2026-10-19 08:0x: iterate until 22:00, main lane plus one subagent; advance one grain of sand of every task, go through the whole backlog over and over, then pick one task and take it to the end, rinse and repeat). Ledger spiral/lab/BACKLOG-LAPS/ledger.tsv (lap, id, owner, at, grain, next); main owns the first half of the open main/shared list, the subagent the second half (CPU-light grains only, heavy runs queued to main as scripts). Other lanes' items (TIBIA, EOIE, X, DOTA) are skipped.", TaskKind, P1, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "backlog,process", "current", "current")
inl spiral_assign_primitive () = Task ("SPIRAL-ASSIGN-PRIMITIVE", "Portable assignment primitive !!!!Assign(target, value), checked like Conv (target a dyn local of the value's type), lowered per backend (a = b, Delphi :=, Lean := in let mut, Gleam/Bend rejected), F# marks targets let mutable; replaces BackendSwitch set' macros in while_loop/while_control (design: FSHARP-MACRO-MUTATION note 2)", Story, P1, D4, 100u32, Done, "", "compiler,portable,fsharp,main", "current", "current")
inl rust_export_types () = Task ("RUST-EXPORT-TYPES", "Rust Export across crates: unions and records reachable from an !!!!Export signature are emitted pub and named after their Spiral source (command, not US0) so a consumer crate can name and match them; today codegenRust emits private enum US0 named by a per-crate counter (probe lab/CLI-ARGS-STYLE/export_union.spi), which forces x args.rs to flatten command into kind i32 + 13 scalars", Story, P0, D4, 100u32, Done, "", "compiler,rust,export,main", "current", "current")
inl gadt_join_point_growing_index () = Task ("GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX", "Compiler hang: a let rec join point over a runtime GADT value whose recursion grows the type index (term n -> term (succ n)) never finishes compiling, instead of sharing one join point across phantom indices or reporting polymorphic recursion", TaskKind, P0, D4, 100u32, Done, "", "team:compiler,compiler,gadt,bug,mega,typefeatureset", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_gadt_join_point_growing_index () = Planning ("GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX", Minutes 480u32, Load Heavy, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_gadt_join_point_growing_index_1 () = Note ("GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX", 1u32, "2026-10-19T14:36Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 10:36 found by helper-lane-2 while sizing MEGA-SAMPLES-TOGETHER's shared goal (substitution and renaming under binders in lean_cic, spiral_proves, zeta). Repros in spiral/lab/GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX (installed single-flight compiler, try.ps1 -Backend C, bounded): debruijn_rename.spi, a well-scoped de Bruijn term union rec term n (Var of var n, Lam of term (succ n), App) with inl rec rename/lift and inl rec size over a static term, compiles and exits 5 (correct size after weakening). debruijn_size_runtime.spi, the same term made runtime (inl ~t) and size a let rec join point: TIMEOUT after 45 s (compiler killed; 60 s for the rename variant). gadt_same_index_runtime.spi, a let rec join point over a runtime union rec tree n whose recursion keeps the index: compiles, exits 3. So the hang needs both a runtime value and an index that grows in the recursive call; it looks like join-point specialization per index instantiation without a bound (succ n, succ (succ n), ...), although the index nominals are phantom (nominal succ n = ()) and the runtime layout is identical for every n. Expected: one join point for all n (erase phantom indices for specialization), or a fast EJP-style error naming the polymorphic recursion. Related but distinct: GADT-GROWING-INDEX-HANG (Done, the case-field type growth in ty_is_uninhabited). Owner: compiler lane (main)")
inl note_of_gadt_join_point_growing_index_2 () = Note ("GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX", 2u32, "2026-10-19T14:39Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 10:39 lap 2 grain: triage: the hang is unbounded specialization, not a wrong result. size is a let rec join point; join points specialize per argument type, and every recursive call on term (succ n) has a new type, so the specialization set never closes (the fixed-index runtime case compiles because its key repeats); rename is an inl rec over a runtime ~t, which inlines without end. The compiler bug is the missing guard: partial evaluation should stop like EJP0040 does for dynamic ifs, by counting distinct specializations of one join-point body (or index-type nesting depth) and reporting the growing index with a hint (erase the index at runtime through an existential such as a term_dyn wrapper, or keep the recursion static). The runtime remedy for the lean_cic, spiral_proves and zeta roots is that erasure, independent of the guard Next: find where single-flight memoizes join-point specializations (join point dictionary keyed by body + argument types) and add a per-body distinct-key counter with an EJP report, as a candidate patch in lab/GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX")
inl note_of_gadt_join_point_growing_index_3 () = Note ("GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX", 3u32, "2026-10-19T14:42Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 10:42 patch design located in single-flight PartEval (spiral_compiler.fs 158355-161335): (1) EUnbox's runtime case body (159931 run s a, and Unbox's 159962) is not wrapped in within_dynamic_if (159488, EJP0040, only if_'s two branches are, 159519-159520), so an inl rec recursing through a runtime match never reaches the nesting limit; wrap those calls so EJP0040 counts union-match branches too. (2) EJoinPoint' (159727-159764): dict is the per-(backend, body) specialization table; at the Owner branch add a distinct-key cap (SPIRAL_JP_SPECIALIZATION_LIMIT, default 1000) that raises RunawayUnrollDiagnostic.render with a new code and the hint erase the growing index at runtime (existential) or keep the recursion static. Constraint: PartEval is a SPIRAL_CORE_GPT5PARALLEL pair, and pair-drift.ps1 fails a build whose normal pair changed on one side only; routing the calls through PartEvalSeams would pass the check but spend hopacsota's G6 seam budget on a non-seam. So the patch either mirrors both guards on gpt5parallel's side (it already has jp_method_specialization_budget_exhausted at 41659) or waits for gpt5parallel's retirement (HOPACSOTA-P6).")
inl note_of_gadt_join_point_growing_index_4 () = Note ("GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX", 4u32, "2026-10-19T14:45Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 10:45 analysis note (helper-lane-2, for the compiler lane): why the runtime form cannot terminate today. A runtime value needs a residual layout for its type, and residual unions are monomorphized per type arguments; the type term zero has a Lam case whose field is term (succ zero), whose Lam field is term (succ (succ zero)), and so on, so the layout of term zero is an infinite family of union types. That is the same type-level growth GADT-GROWING-INDEX-HANG met (its fix capped nesting in ty_is_uninhabited for the inhabitedness check), but a runtime value additionally forces the layouts and a join point per instantiation, which the cap does not cover; the static repro avoids it because partial evaluation consumes the term before any layout exists. Two directions: (a) index erasure for runtime layouts when every index argument is a phantom nominal (nominal succ n = ()): one residual union per GADT head, cases tagged as usual, index checks already done by the type checker; (b) a bounded detection that reports EJP-style polymorphic recursion through a growing phantom index, naming the union and the field. (b) is the cheap correctness fix (no hang), (a) is what the mega roots' runtime substitution needs")
inl note_of_gadt_join_point_growing_index_5 () = Note ("GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX", 5u32, "2026-10-19T18:39Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 14:39 lap 4 grain: helper lane 2 analysis (10:45) explains the non-termination: a runtime value needs a residual layout per type argument, and term zero has a Lam field of term (succ zero) whose Lam field is term (succ (succ zero)), so monomorphization never closes; the guard (cap distinct index instantiations per union) belongs in the PartEval pair, blocked by pair-drift until gpt5parallel retires Next: none until HOPACSOTA-P6, unless the guard fits a shared pre-PartEval section")
inl note_of_gadt_join_point_growing_index_6 () = Note ("GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX", 6u32, "2026-10-21T22:23Z", "main", "18:23 code read (no compile): the non-termination is the eager layout recursion at PartEval's TUnion arm, single-flight spiral_compiler.fs:159909 (gpt5parallel's twin at 151407). Building YUnion maps every case payload through ty s v, and for term zero the Lam payload is term (succ zero), a new type at every level, so ty recurses through TUnion forever (recursive unions with fixed arguments terminate only because their instantiation repeats). Cheapest correct guard, mirrored on both sides of the pair, so pair-drift passes without spending a hopacsota seam: a union-nesting depth counter around the payload mapping at TUnion. On single-flight a plain ref local to the PartEval closure is enough, since its ty runs on one thread; on gpt5parallel the twin needs the depth carried in its environment record, because its ty runs on Hopac workers. Above SPIRAL_UNION_NESTING_LIMIT (default 256), raise a RunawayUnrollDiagnostic-style error naming the union, the case and the last two instantiations, with the hint from helper-lane-2 note 2 (erase the phantom index at runtime with an existential, or keep the recursion static). Unblocked enough to draft as lab/GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX/union-depth.ps1 with both anchors; gate: the static repro unchanged, the runtime repro errors in < 5 s instead of hanging, and -Suite all DISAGREE 0.")
inl note_of_gadt_join_point_growing_index_7 () = Note ("GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX", 7u32, "2026-10-21T22:31Z", "main", "18:31 guard drafted: lab/GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX/union-depth.ps1, 4 anchors, dry run matches each once; pair-drift on the patched copy is clean (both PartEval sides changed). Each side gets a UnionNesting module beside its DynamicIfNesting (SPIRAL_UNION_NESTING_LIMIT, default 256, ThreadLocal depth; ty recursion is synchronous per thread on both cores). At the TUnion arm, each case payload's ty s v runs inside the counter, and past the limit it raises EJP0041 through RunawayUnrollDiagnostic.render with the source frames, a headline naming the union case and the hint to keep the recursion static or erase the index with an existential. single-flight raises with raise_type_error and an empty trace, gpt5parallel with partEvalTypeError [], each as its EJP0040 does. Gate, fixed now: the lab repros debruijn_size_runtime.spi and debruijn_rename_runtime.spi end in EJP0041 within 30 s instead of hanging; gadt_same_index_runtime.spi and the static repros compile as before; -Suite all shows DISAGREE 0 with no residual change (256 levels is far above any legitimate union nesting in the samples, to be confirmed by the review). Follow-up: register EJP0041 in DiagnosticClassifier beside EJP0040 (7453/7480/7638/7661/7938) so a stall report classifies it. Rides the next pack.")
inl note_of_gadt_join_point_growing_index_8 () = Note ("GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX", 8u32, "2026-10-21T22:34Z", "main", "18:34 the classifier follow-up is now in the same patch: union-depth.ps1 has 9 anchors (EJP0041 literal, DiagnosticEJP0041 case, both code mappings, a classifyIngress branch as DiagnosticRunawayInlineUnroll + DiagnosticTerminalSemantic, then the 2 modules and 2 TUnion guards). Dry run matches each once; pair-drift on the patched copy is clean. Any other exhaustive match over DiagnosticCodeId that needs the new case shows up in the candidate build.")
inl note_of_gadt_join_point_growing_index_9 () = Note ("GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX", 9u32, "2026-10-22T00:24Z", "main", "20:24 pre-build check of the open question in note 18:34 (other exhaustive matches over DiagnosticCodeId): grep finds exactly 4 EJP0040 sites in spiral_compiler.fs (case 7480, code mapping 7638, text mapping 7661, classifyIngress 7938-7939) and union-depth.ps1 covers each with its EJP0041 twin, so no further match should break the candidate build; outside the core EJP0040 appears only in prose (FRONTIER.md, hopacsota PLAN.md, a mega sample README, NEXT.md, tmp/AGENTS.md:98). New companion lab/GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX/union-depth-doc.ps1 (dry-run, anchor unique) documents SPIRAL_UNION_NESTING_LIMIT and EJP0041 next to SPIRAL_IF_NESTING_LIMIT/EJP0040 in tmp/AGENTS.md; apply with the pack.")
inl note_of_gadt_join_point_growing_index_10 () = Note ("GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX", 10u32, "2026-10-22T06:05Z", "main", "02:05 main: union-depth.ps1 is fully in the PACK-AFTER-NIGHT core (pack-next carries it). All 5 EJP0041 blocks and both union-payload layout sites are present verbatim. The 2 counter blocks hold a __MODULE__ placeholder the script fills at run time, and module UnionNesting with SPIRAL_UNION_NESTING_LIMIT is at pack core 137748, used at 151463-151468. So the guard installs with that pack. At install: apply union-depth-doc.ps1 to tmp/AGENTS.md, then drain the folder. The debruijn_*/gadt_* repros: the hang case becomes an EJP0041 rejection, and adding it as a negative matrix row (ExpectedErrors pin) is the one regression sample this fix gets, after install.")
inl note_of_gadt_join_point_growing_index_11 () = Note ("GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX", 11u32, "2026-10-22T18:52Z", "main", "2026-10-22 14:52 main: install steps of the 02:05 rule applied. (1) union-depth-doc.ps1 applied to apps/compiler/tmp/AGENTS.md: SPIRAL_UNION_NESTING_LIMIT (default 256, checked in both cores at 137748 and 159661) and EJP0041 are documented beside EJP0040, as an unstaged edit over the user's staged file. (2) The one regression sample: samples/gadt_growing_index_runtime = debruijn_size_runtime.spi (a runtime term n with let rec size, the former 45 s hang), pinned in matrix.psd1 ExpectedErrors with Contains 'has no finite layout' (UnionNesting.headline). Its first row comes from PACK-NEXT-3's review, and that row is evidence for this item, not for the pack: expected = a compile error containing the pin within the 20 s timeout. A timeout or a different error reopens the item with that text. Lab drains after that row: the other 3 repros (static rename exit 5, same-index runtime) are recorded in notes 1-2.")
inl note_of_gadt_join_point_growing_index_12 () = Note ("GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX", 12u32, "2026-10-23T07:11Z", "main", "2026-10-23 03:11 main: lab drain: union-depth.ps1 and union-depth-doc.ps1 deleted, both landed (tree core has EJP0041 at 7455 and SPIRAL_UNION_NESTING_LIMIT in 4 places; apps/compiler/tmp/AGENTS.md documents the limit). No script reads them (by-stem search of spiral *.ps1 with a positive control; only two scratchpad pack scripts of installed packs name them). The 4 .spi probes stay as reference for the open scope (one join point shared across phantom indices); the regression sample gadt_growing_index_runtime is an adapted copy of debruijn_size_runtime, not byte-equal, so that probe stays too.")
inl note_of_gadt_join_point_growing_index_13 () = Note ("GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX", 13u32, "2026-10-23T18:06Z", "main", "2026-10-23 14:06 main: REOPENED by the 10-22 14:52 rule (a timeout reopens the item). The regression sample's first real rows (next4 review-2, run single-flight-20261023-135519): samples/gadt_growing_index_runtime times out at 20 s on C, Rust and Delphi ('no result within 20000 ms', one worker recycle each), so EJP0041's union-nesting cap does not catch this runtime-term shape (let rec size over a runtime term n), which is the former 45 s hang. next3b's earlier rows were a different error (a type error, from an older edit of the sample, which changed at 10-22 19:24). Per the rule this is evidence for this item, not for PACK-NEXT-4's verdict. Next: find where the runtime case grows (the join point for size specialized per index) with a bounded probe, then a cap there with the 'has no finite layout' headline.")
inl note_of_gadt_join_point_growing_index_14 () = Note ("GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX", 14u32, "2026-10-23T18:11Z", "main", "2026-10-23 14:11 main: bounded probe (installed single-flight compiler, --backend C, 120 s bound, compile window): no output and no EJP0041 within 120 s, killed. Depth 256 cannot take that long, so the growth is NOT inside one union layout (where UnionNesting counts nested ty calls): each step is a new join-point specialization of size at term (succ^k n), laid out one level at a time, so the union depth never passes 1. Fix direction, rule written before drafting: cap the type depth of a join point's specialization key (the nesting of nominal applications like succ (succ ...)) at SPIRAL_UNION_NESTING_LIMIT and raise EJP0041 with the existing 'has no finite layout' headline there; adopt only if the sample then fails within 20 s with that text and the matrix shows no other row change.")
inl note_of_gadt_join_point_growing_index_15 () = Note ("GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX", 15u32, "2026-10-23T18:20Z", "main", "2026-10-23 14:20 main: cap drafted per the rule above: %TEMP%\\pack-next9\\gadt-index-cap.ps1, in pack-next9 (core 2741D35BD11FF46B). Both UnionNesting modules (the pair stays symmetric; pair-drift 0 one-sided) gain applicationDepth (YApply nesting), tooDeep and indexHeadline ('specializing a join point at a type index nested more than N levels: ... has no finite layout', so the matrix.psd1 pin still matches). Each core's EJoinPoint' case checks its env_global_type before claiming the key and raises EJP0041 with the existing hint (SPIRAL_UNION_NESTING_LIMIT). TJoinPoint' (type-level join points) is left alone. Assumption to verify: the growing index reaches env_global_type (size's join point is built under its forall n); if it only travels in the argument's type, the review row stays a timeout and the check moves to the argument types. next9's review.ps1 prints the sample's rows with pinned=True/False; adopt per the rule (fails within 20 s with the pin, no other row change). The next9 Debug type-check build queued behind the 5f build covers the new code.")
inl note_of_gadt_join_point_growing_index_16 () = Note ("GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX", 16u32, "2026-10-23T19:27Z", "main", "2026-10-23 15:27 main: the cap WORKS. next9's Debug type-check build (core 1AF7692BCABBEEB8, build succeeded in 410 s) compiles samples/gadt_growing_index_runtime --backend C to 'error[EJP0041]: specializing a join point at a type index nested more than 256 levels: a type index that grows at every level (term n -> term (succ n)) has no finite layout', exit 5 after 10.9 s (Debug; Release is faster), against the installed compiler's 120 s with no result. So the growing index does reach env_global_type, and the matrix.psd1 pin ('has no finite layout') matches. Remaining per the rule: next9's review must show every backend row failing with the pin within 20 s and no other row change; it rides review-3's stack (next9 after next8).")
inl note_of_gadt_join_point_growing_index_17 () = Note ("GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX", 17u32, "2026-10-23T21:45Z", "main", "2026-10-23 17:45 main: every backend row now ends in the pinned EJP0041 on the next9 Debug build: C 10.9 s, Delphi 12.7 s, F# 10.8 s, Rust 21.5 s (exit 5, 'has no finite layout' in each). Rust is 1.5 s over the 20 s row timeout on a DEBUG build with cells-pack's suite running beside it; Release is faster, so the rule's 'within 20 s' is decided by review-3's Release rows, not by this probe. If Rust's Release row still times out, the cause is the depth (256 levels): the next lever is checking the specialization depth incrementally instead of recomputing applicationDepth per join point.")
inl note_of_gadt_join_point_growing_index_18 () = Note ("GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX", 18u32, "2026-10-24T03:49Z", "main", "2026-10-23 23:49 main, corrected 01:19: samples/gadt_growing_index_runtime still hangs on the installed single-flight (B553EF6B78E6EE29) and on the 5f candidate: both TIME OUT at 300 s (probe job-1792813100262). The reason is that the cap (pack-next9 gadt-index-cap.ps1, proven on a Debug build at 15:27/17:45) was never part of 5f, so no bisect is needed. It now rides pack 5g (compose adds it; review-4 job-1792813775220), where the rule of note 15 decides: every backend row fails with the pin 'has no finite layout' within 20 s, and no other row changes.")
inl note_of_gadt_join_point_growing_index_19 () = Note ("GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX", 19u32, "2026-10-24T11:49Z", "main", "2026-10-19 laps folded from the deleted history ledger (docs/agents/history/2026-10-19-backlog-laps, HISTORY-BACKLOG-LAPS-DRIFT): lap 5 10:45 (subagent2): analysis for main: runtime layout of term zero is an infinite union family (monomorphized per index); fix options: phantom-index erasure (a) or bounded polymorphic-recursion error (b) -> next: main's lane")
inl note_of_gadt_join_point_growing_index_20 () = Note ("GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX", 20u32, "2026-10-28T01:33Z", "main", "10-27 21:33 main: Groom 10-27 21:2x (WIP rule, set before applying). main's Active items with no note since 10-24 were classed by the evidence in the tree, not by age alone. (1) SHIPPED -> Done: the item's pass is in the installed tree core (apps/compiler/spiral_compiler.fs, core 8660C34A11F33259, the installed 5m' core), found by its own identifier or anchor, and an accept has passed on that core since (accept-6, run single-flight-20261027-173417, 0 blocking cells). (2) COLD -> Paused: no lane is driving it this week and nothing is queued. Its last note keeps the next step, and it resumes when a lane picks it or its condition fires. (3) KEPT Active: a lane is on it this week, or demand is waiting on it. (4) Blocked: only the user's commit or push unblocks it.\n Verdict for this item: (2) COLD, so Active -> Paused. The next step is the one in its latest note above.")
inl note_of_gadt_join_point_growing_index_21 () = Note ("GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX", 21u32, "2026-10-31T03:21Z", "main", "10-30 23:21 main: lab/GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX is ARCHIVED (user, 10-30 22:5x: the lab dir didn't work and drifted; drafts don't live outside the repo). Archive 20261030-232045 under %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\archive. Restore this folder with: pwsh i574n.github/scripts/scratch-archive.ps1 -Root C:\\home\\git\\spiral\\lab -Restore '%LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\archive\\20261030-232045\\manifest.tsv' -Only 'GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX\\*' -RestoreTo C:\\home\\git\\spiral\\lab. Resume this item's work in its real place in the repo, not in lab.")
inl note_of_gadt_join_point_growing_index_22 () = Note ("GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX", 22u32, "2026-10-31T20:01Z", "compiler", "10-31 10-31 16:01 compiler GROOM (user direction via /btw, 10-31 15:4x): the self-hosted rewrite (SELF-HOSTED-SPIRAL) starts only after EVERY backend is SOTA: no memory problems, no Rc slowdowns, no union hacks, no recursion limits, no missing arithmetic; GADT bugs and conversion gaps are 'unacceptable by now'; edition 3 finishes first; Gleam is a top test target (abyssal codebases) with F#, Rust and Wasm; Cells as fast as F# projects. This item is now on that gate (priority -> P0, status -> Active).")
inl note_of_gadt_join_point_growing_index_23 () = Note ("GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX", 23u32, "2026-10-31T20:05Z", "compiler", "10-31 10-31 16:05 compiler (groom): user (10-31 15:4x): 'we shouldn't have gadt bugs ... by now, unacceptable'. Stays P0 and is a gate for SELF-HOSTED-SPIRAL.")
inl note_of_gadt_join_point_growing_index_24 () = Note ("GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX", 24u32, "2026-10-31T20:33Z", "compiler", "10-31 10-31 16:33 compiler: STATE on the installed PACK-10 compiler: samples/gadt_growing_index_runtime no longer hangs. --backend C ends in 'error[EJP0041]: specializing a join point at a type index nested more than 256 levels ... has no finite layout' (main.spi:23:18, size's join point), exit 5 after 18.2 s on a loaded box. So the 10-23 cap shipped, and it is a STOPGAP: the program is valid (a de Bruijn-indexed lambda term; size should return 6 for 'Lam (App (Var VZ, Lam (Var (VS VZ))))'), and per the user (10-31) a GADT program that should compile but errors is a bug, unacceptable now. THE FIX (option (a) of the 10-19 analysis): PHANTOM-INDEX ERASURE. The index n is phantom: zero/succ are ':!' unit nominals, and no case of var n / term n stores a value whose representation depends on n (it only reappears as the index of the same family: var (succ n), term (succ n)). So every instantiation term zero, term (succ zero), ... has the same runtime layout. Plan: (1) a pass over each recursive union family that marks a type parameter phantom when it occurs only as a type argument of the family itself (or of other families where it is phantom: var n inside term n), never in a payload's primitive/layout position; (2) union layout keys and join-point specialization keys erase phantom arguments (one layout 'term _' and one size join point shared across indices); type checking keeps the full index (the GADT guarantee stays static). (3) Regression: gadt_growing_index_runtime flips from the pinned EJP0041 error to exit 6 (= size) on every backend; EJP0041 stays for a REAL growing layout (an index that does change the payload). Estimate: core work in PartEval's union/join-point keying, ~1-2 days with the matrix review; it's the item's remaining 60%. CORRECTION 16:34: 'size should return 6' was wrong. By hand, size of Lam (App (Var VZ, Lam (Var (VS VZ)))) = 1 + (1 + 1 + (1 + 1)) = 5, and the regression's expected exit is whatever main returns from it (read main's last lines when writing the sample's expectation), not a number from this note.")
inl note_of_gadt_join_point_growing_index_25 () = Note ("GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX", 25u32, "2026-11-01T04:50Z", "main", "main 00:5x LAB CODE IS SAFE (user 11-01 00:2x: 'check for all lab references in history... make sure we didn't lose that code... to repurpose them or groom their tasks'). This item's lab/GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX folder (4 files: debruijn_rename.spi, debruijn_rename_runtime.spi, debruijn_size_runtime.spi, gadt_same_index_runtime.spi) is archived in %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\archive\\20261030-232045 (blob-verified). Restore it into its proper repo place with: pwsh C:\\home\\git\\i574n.github\\scripts\\scratch-archive.ps1 -Restore '%LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\archive\\20261030-232045\\manifest.tsv' -RestoreTo <repo dir> -Only 'GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX/*'. Next step for the owner at its next pull: restore the useful files in place as incomplete drafts (Cells heals them in place), or record why it starts over.")
inl note_of_gadt_join_point_growing_index_26 () = Note ("GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX", 26u32, "2026-11-01T12:35Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 08:35 compiler, ROUND 4 QUALITY SPEC: FIELDS: est 120 -> 480 (erasure in PartEval is not a 2 h job); tag team:compiler. DEFINITION OF DONE: samples/gadt_growing_index_runtime returns 5 on C, Rust and F# (today EJP0041 after 18 s), and the 4 lab files restored from %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\archive\\20261030-232045 (debruijn_rename*.spi, debruijn_size*) become samples that compile and agree with C; EJP0041 still fires for a genuinely unbounded index (keep its negative sample). NEXT STEP: restore the 4 lab files into samples/ as drafts (scratch-archive.ps1 -Restore ... -Only 'GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX\\*'), run probe.ps1 on each with -Stacks 15 to see where the join point key keeps growing. TYPEFEATURESET PLAN: the fix ERASES the phantom index at the join point boundary: the join point key uses the GADT's erased type (its union without the index argument) and the index survives only as an existential witness in the body, so the key set is finite; the erasure rule is a predicate on Ty ('index position of a GADT union' marked by the union's declaration), not a name pattern. Model: the mega_zeta / brzozowski GADT samples and the debruijn lab files.")
inl note_of_gadt_join_point_growing_index_27 () = Note ("GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX", 27u32, "2026-11-01T14:37Z", "main", "main 10:4x: the browser research lists 'fix the GADT index bug at run time' as a prerequisite for everything (steal list #5 and the rogue route's M0). It points to this item, so no new item; keep it P0.")
inl note_of_gadt_join_point_growing_index_28 () = Note ("GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX", 28u32, "2026-11-01T19:23Z", "compiler", "11-01 15:35 compiler: STEP 1 DONE (lab files home). Restored the 4 lab files from %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\archive\\20261030-232045. debruijn_size_runtime is the existing samples/gadt_growing_index_runtime in edition-2 spelling, so it was not duplicated; debruijn_rename.spi is the static twin of the rename one and was not landed. Landed two new samples (unstaged), respelled with the installed `format --spelling symbols` and matched to the existing sample's `+.`: samples/gadt_debruijn_rename_runtime (rename with lift, weakening term zero -> term (succ zero), then size: expected 5, like gadt_growing_index_runtime) and samples/gadt_same_index_runtime (tree n with a constant index, count = 3: the control that must already compile). CODE READ (core F6F6879566C5A830), where the index grows: (1) a union instance is a nominal application YApply(term, arg), and nominal_type_apply lays out its cases (PartEval ty / TUnion, 9997-10022). Lam's payload is term (succ n), so each index is a distinct YUnion H, and the depth guard there gives EJP0041 'laying out union case'. (2) The join-point key carries env_global_type, the bindings of the body's free type variables. size's n is rebound to succ n at every recursive call, so the key grows, and UnionNesting.tooDeep (10384) gives EJP0041 'specializing a join point'. A fix must cut BOTH. DESIGN, sound by construction: (a) DECLARATION-LEVEL phantom parameters: a union parameter is phantom when it occurs only as an argument in the same parameter position of its own family (term n -> term (succ n)) or of another family where it is phantom (var n). That is a static property of the declarations alone. nominal_type_apply then lays the union out with phantom arguments replaced by unit, so every index shares one layout and one H. The nominal type keeps its real argument (type checking and show_ty are unchanged). (b) JOIN-POINT LEVEL: when a key's type-variable binding exceeds a small depth (e.g. 8) for the same jp_body, retry the body with that variable bound to an abstract rigid type that any typecase or layout inspection rejects. If the body evaluates, one join point serves every index. If it inspects the index (real type-level computation, e.g. a static vector length), the retry fails, the per-index specialization stands, and EJP0041 stays the honest error (its negative sample keeps passing). NEXT STEP: implement (a) first, alone; it is enough if the recursive call's type argument is inferred from the erased data type. Probe on gadt_growing_index_runtime with probe.ps1 -Stacks, on the node (the box is compile-quiet).")
inl note_of_gadt_join_point_growing_index_29 () = Note ("GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX", 29u32, "2026-11-02T13:16Z", "main", "main 11-02 USER: \"crazy that we haven't finished these gadt bugs yet, compiler lane gotta do better\". Status: this item P0 45% Active (28 notes) and SPIRAL-UNFORGEABLE-WITNESSES P0 80% Planned are the open GADT bugs; the rest of the family is Done (GADT-UNINHABITED-PAYLOAD-RUNTIME since 10-12; its sample and gadt_rank_matrix now pass on 4 and 7 backends). What this one blocks today: the zeta mega sample's 3 open proof goals (ZETA-TYPED-ARITH), runtime CIC terms (CIC-* items), REGEX-TYPED-CORES and LIB-VEC-FIN (length-growing runtime recursion), MEGA-SAMPLES-TOGETHER. Ask for the compiler lane: make this the next pack's top item with a dated plan (minimal repro, fix, one regression sample with a matrix row), ahead of the linked-units second cut.")
inl note_of_gadt_join_point_growing_index_30 () = Note ("GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX", 30u32, "2026-11-02T13:26Z", "compiler", "11-02 09:10 compiler: DATED PLAN (user 11-02 via main: top of the next pack, ahead of the linked-units follow-ups; pack16 was already built and proven, so it ships as is and costs this item nothing).\nMINIMAL REPRO (exists, pinned): samples/gadt_growing_index_runtime. A de Bruijn `term n` with `Lam :: term (succ n) -> term n` and runtime `size`; expected 5; today ExpectedErrors 'has no finite layout' (EJP0041). Twins: samples/gadt_debruijn_rename_runtime (rename + lift + size, expected 5) and the control samples/gadt_same_index_runtime (constant index, 3, compiles today).\nREFINEMENT FOUND TODAY, which changes n28's (a): plain erasure of the phantom index to () is UNSOUND for GADTs. TUnion layout (spiral_compiler.fs ~10007) evaluates each case's specialized destructor against the bound index, and a case whose destructor does not unify is dropped (VZ :: var (succ n) does not exist in var zero). Binding n := () would drop VZ from every instance. FIX (a'): bind a phantom parameter to one ERASED marker type that unifies with every index pattern in destructors. Then the erased layout holds ALL constructors, and nominal_type_apply keys every instance of the family to the same (nominal, Erased) application, so the layout is one finite union. The unboxed payload of Lam is then term (succ Erased) -> laid out as term Erased, so size's inferred n stays at the same key: one join point instead of an unbounded chain. Phantom = declaration-level: the parameter occurs only as a type argument of :! unit nominals or of the phantom positions of union families (term n, var n). Type checking runs before PartEval and keeps exact indices, so the GADT guarantee stays static. Anything that typecases on the index keeps per-index specialization (no erasure), and EJP0041 stays for real growth.\nSCHEDULE: 11-02 implement (a') as a kitdo program over the live core and probe the three samples locally (compile-only; the box is compile-quiet). 11-02/03, only if size's key still grows: n28's (b) rigid-index retry at the join point. 11-03: matrix: gadt_growing_index_runtime moves from ExpectedErrors to a passing row on C, Rust, F#, Zig, Gleam (exit 5), plus gadt_debruijn_rename_runtime (5) and gadt_same_index_runtime (3); a residual A/B over the examples suite must show 0 changed rows outside GADT samples; then the pack.\nDoD (n26 unchanged): exit 5 on C/Rust/F#, the lab twins agree with C, and EJP0041 still fires for a genuinely unbounded index.")
inl note_of_gadt_join_point_growing_index_31 () = Note ("GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX", 31u32, "2026-11-02T14:06Z", "compiler", "11-02 10:15 compiler: ITERATION LOG on program %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\spiral\\GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX\\1-phantom-index-erasure.kitdo. gadt1 (erase every unit-nominal index): the growing-index EJP0041 is GONE. gadt_growing_index_runtime and gadt_debruijn_rename_runtime got past layout and join-point specialization and stopped at union construction (\"For key Var ... Got: var (succ zero) Expected: var <mark>\"). BUT erasing every index broke constructor-specialized GADTs: gadt_rank_matrix and gadt_specialized_case_rank (Pattern miss: state_a raw_a laid out with DecidedA too), gadt_uninhabited_payload_runtime (Typecase miss) and wgsl_gadt_payload_union (15 backends). LESSON: a unit-nominal index is not automatically phantom; shallow indices select constructors, and that is the point of a GADT. gadt3 erases only on GROWTH: an index nested deeper than PhantomIndex.depth = 4, or one already holding the mark (term (succ mark) folds back to term mark, a fixed point), so constant and shallow indices keep their exact specialized layouts. Plus phantom_erase: union construction accepts a payload whose type equals the case type under canonical erasure (the same shared layout). Probing the whole `gadt` filter on gadt3 now; the control comparison follows.")
inl note_of_gadt_join_point_growing_index_32 () = Note ("GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX", 32u32, "2026-11-02T15:01Z", "compiler", "11-02 11:05 compiler: REPRO FIXED on candidate gadt4 (program GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX\\1, 6 replacements). samples/gadt_growing_index_runtime compiles and exits 5 on C, Rust, F# and Delphi, agreeing with the oracle (single-flight-20261102-105134); it had stopped at EJP0041 for weeks. The whole `gadt` filter: 103 jobs, 99 ok, DISAGREE 0, and every previously-green GADT sample is still green (rank_matrix 7/7, specialized_case_rank, uninhabited_payload, wgsl_gadt_payload_union on 15 backends, contract_gadt, frontier_gadt_growing_index). THE TWO CUTS: (a) layout: an index that grows past depth 4, or already holds the mark, is laid out once with the index bound to PhantomIndex.mark, and inside that layout a destructor pattern unifies with the mark, so all constructors stay; (b) join point: env_global_type maps such indices to the mark, so size's specializations reach a fixed point after 5 levels instead of hitting the 256 limit. Union construction accepts a payload equal to the case type under canonical erasure (phantom_erase). Shallow and constant indices keep exact constructor-specialized layouts, which is what the gadt1 regressions taught. LEFT: gadt_debruijn_rename_runtime fails \"The union does not have key VS. Got: var (succ zero)\". PE specializes `ren` (\\ v => VS v) on a var zero argument, and var (succ zero) prunes VS because its payload var zero is uninhabited (10022-10025). That is a separate dead-branch issue. NEXT: residual A/B of examples+contracts vs control, then fix the twin (build of an uninhabited-payload constructor in a dead branch: emit unreachable instead of a type error).")
inl note_of_gadt_join_point_growing_index_33 () = Note ("GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX", 33u32, "2026-11-02T15:41Z", "compiler", "11-02 11:45 compiler: gadt4 A/B. EXAMPLES (Rust/Gleam/Zig, 1,421 rows vs control 082202): only the two target samples change. CONTRACTS (728 rows, control 111742 vs candidate 112912): ONE regression, mega_brzozowski_derivatives/antimirov_typed_slot_bound on Zig, ok -> \"Typecase miss. Got: antimirov_position_tree bit_alphabet .phantom-index\". Its shape index is a STATIC type-level tree deeper than 4, and the depth threshold erased it, breaking a typecase on the shape. LESSON: depth is not growth. gadt5 replaces the depth rule with an ANCESTRY rule. PhantomIndex keeps a per-join-point stack of the keys being specialized (pushed around term_scope'' of the Owner body); a binding is erased only when it properly contains the binding at the same position of an ACTIVE ancestor specialization of the same join point (size@term zero -> size@term (succ zero)). A descent over a static tree passes ever-smaller subterms, and siblings are never on the stack, so those keep exact specializations. Layout erasure now fires only for an index holding the mark. Also added: constructing a case that the layout pruned because its payload is uninhabited, in a branch PE reaches but the program never runs at runtime (the rename twin), emits a typed failwith \"union case ruled out by partial evaluation\" instead of a compile error. A missing key with an inhabited payload still errors. gadt5 gadt filter + full A/B running.")
inl note_of_gadt_join_point_growing_index_34 () = Note ("GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX", 34u32, "2026-11-02T17:37Z", "compiler", "11-02 13:45 compiler: FIXED (candidate gadt7, built on the node 13:09). samples/gadt_growing_index_runtime compiles and RUNS exit 5 on Fsharp, C, Rust and Delphi, oracle agree (node job-1793639237695-compiler-gadt7). A/B on the node mirror (pinned lib snapshot; the box lib was mid-land): examples Rust/Gleam/Zig, 1,421 rows, ONLY that row changes (error -> ok); contracts, 728 rows, 0 differences, so antimirov stays green. FINAL DESIGN (program GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX\\1, 7 replacements): (1) DECLARATION-LEVEL growth. erased_families(env.nominals) marks a family whose constructor PAYLOAD (a GADT constructor's domain, not its result) applies the family itself to a non-variable index (term n holds term (succ n)), plus every family named in its payloads (var). Members are laid out at PhantomIndex.mark for every unit-nominal index, so all their values share one representation (gadt6 broke there: exact var layouts inside an erased term, so rustc E0308 Rc<UH0> vs Rc<UH2>). (2) A join-point key binding that grows past an ACTIVE ANCESTOR specialization of the same join point is mapped to the mark (the type-argument growth `size (succ n)`). (3) A mark layout lets destructor patterns unify with the mark, so all constructors stay. (4) Union construction accepts a payload equal under canonical erasure. (5) A pruned case with an uninhabited payload in a PE-only branch is a typed failwith, not an error. WRONG TURNS, kept for the record: erase every unit index (broke constructor-selecting GADTs), erase past depth 4 (broke static deep shapes), dynamic family detection (layout-order dependent). MATRIX: the ExpectedErrors row for gadt_growing_index_runtime is removed (it now runs on the default backends). gadt_debruijn_rename_runtime still TIMES OUT in both arms; that is the follow-up twin, not a regression. NEXT: a genuinely-unbounded negative sample (EJP0041 must still fire), then pack17.")
inl note_of_gadt_join_point_growing_index_35 () = Note ("GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX", 35u32, "2026-11-02T18:46Z", "compiler", "11-02 14:55 compiler: HANDED TO MAIN in pack17 (SpiralCompiler.dll 6B9374835A1BBACF, core R2R E556D773EB123175; box and node builds byte-identical; install-pack dry run clean on live 081180B0). Full review on the node mirror, control = installed: contracts 728 rows with 0 diffs; examples Rust/Gleam/Zig/C 1,981 rows, behavior changes only on gadt_growing_index_runtime (error -> ok) and zig_export_fn; Rust+C native 1,070 rows, the only behavior change is this sample, error -> exit 5, oracle agree on Rust. Matrix (unstaged): the ExpectedErrors row removed; samples/gadt_unbounded_index_negative added with an ExpectedErrors EJP0041 row and a 60 s timeout (nest t holding nest (box t) carries real data, so erasure must not apply; installed compiler errors in 14 s). DoD: met except the lab twin gadt_debruijn_rename_runtime (it times out in both arms; it is closure growth, `rename (lift ren)` nests a static closure per level, not index growth). Closing after install, with the twin split out as its own item.")
inl note_of_gadt_join_point_growing_index_36 () = Note ("GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX", 36u32, "2026-11-02T18:54Z", "compiler", "11-02 14:55 compiler: DONE. pack17 is installed on the box (14:50; SpiralCompiler.dll 6B9374835A1BBACF, core E556D773EB123175, undo compiler-installs\\pack17-20261102-144751). samples/gadt_growing_index_runtime runs exit 5 on C/Rust/F#/Delphi; EJP0041 still fires for the genuinely unbounded samples/gadt_unbounded_index_negative; contracts unchanged. The lab twin is split out as GADT-DEBRUIJN-RENAME-CLOSURE-GROWTH (closure growth, not index growth). Unblocks per n29: ZETA-TYPED-ARITH, the runtime CIC terms, REGEX-TYPED-CORES, LIB-VEC-FIN, MEGA-SAMPLES-TOGETHER; their owners can retry on the installed compiler.")
inl cells_gate_machine_window () = Task ("CELLS-GATE-MACHINE-WINDOW", "Cells --gate takes a test-suite machine window from EOIE-MACHINE-WINDOWS and waits while a live GUI run or a compiler install holds the machine, instead of lanes asking in chat (split from CELLS-GATE 2026-10-19)", Feature, P1, D2, 100u32, Done, "EOIE-MACHINE-WINDOWS", "cells,testing,gate,one-call,team:main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_cells_gate_machine_window () = Planning ("CELLS-GATE-MACHINE-WINDOW", Minutes 60u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_cells_gate_machine_window_1 () = Note ("CELLS-GATE-MACHINE-WINDOW", 1u32, "2026-10-19T15:42Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 11:42 first grain, the interface this item needs from EOIE-MACHINE-WINDOWS: one call to request a window of class test-suite with an expected duration, granted or queued, and a release in the gate's teardown (spiral.notebook.ex cond, next to CompilerClient.shutdown, which also runs when the gate fails). The gate can estimate the duration from the file and test count; main's 10:51 data point: sm'.spi with 42 tests took 217 s on the warm daemon and 537 s with a process per compile. The conflicts that matter here are declared once: test-suite vs live-gui (the 10-14 reaper incidents) and test-suite vs compiler-install. Until that call exists, nothing changes in Cells. Status: blocked on the eoie lane.")
inl note_of_cells_gate_machine_window_2 () = Note ("CELLS-GATE-MACHINE-WINDOW", 2u32, "2026-10-19T16:01Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 12:01 lap 7 status check: EOIE-MACHINE-WINDOWS is still (EOIE-MACHINE-WINDOWS  Feature  P1  D4  0%  Planned), so the Cells side stays unstarted on purpose; the interface from note 1 is unchanged.")
inl note_of_cells_gate_machine_window_3 () = Note ("CELLS-GATE-MACHINE-WINDOW", 3u32, "2026-10-19T16:43Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 12:43 lap 8 grain: placement decided while blocked: the machine-window acquire and release go into the wrappers that start a gate (the scaffold's scripts/gate.ps1 around its mix call, and Invoke-Heavy callers), not into mix spiral.notebook's gate branch (spiral.notebook.ex:10-19), because Cells does not call eoie (the same rule CELLS-NEW-PROJECT follows by printing the epic command instead of running it). The Cells side therefore needs no change; the wrapper needs one eoie call before mix (window test-suite with a duration) and one in its finally block. Still blocked on EOIE-MACHINE-WINDOWS (Planned, 0%)")
inl note_of_cells_gate_machine_window_4 () = Note ("CELLS-GATE-MACHINE-WINDOW", 4u32, "2026-10-19T17:19Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 13:20 lap 9 grain: planning set (estimate 60m, load light) and the duration input for the window request found: the gate CLI already writes --metrics <dir>/tests.tsv (notebook.ex:271-275, columns File Test Target Status Ms Memo), so the gate wrapper can request a test-suite window with expected duration = sum of Ms of the previous tests.tsv for the same paths (memo-hit rows count ~0), falling back to a fixed default on a first run; the wrapper passes --metrics into the per-run dir. Still blocked on EOIE-MACHINE-WINDOWS (Planned 0%): it unblocks with an eoie verb pair that grants or queues a class=test-suite window for N minutes and releases it")
inl note_of_cells_gate_machine_window_5 () = Note ("CELLS-GATE-MACHINE-WINDOW", 5u32, "2026-10-19T17:42Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 13:42 lap 10 grain: the window-duration half is written and self-tested: lab/CELLS-GATE-MACHINE-WINDOW/expected-minutes.ps1 -MetricsDir <previous run> reads the gate's --metrics tests.tsv, sums Ms of rows whose Memo is not hit (Memo hit is the only value Cells counts, notebook.ex:214), times a 1.5 margin, ceil to minutes, at least 1; no table = 10 min first-run default. Self-test on a synthetic table (30 s run + 50 s hit + 50 s run) gives 2 min; a missing dir gives 10. The acquire/release half still waits on EOIE-MACHINE-WINDOWS (Planned 0%): the gate wrapper will pass Minutes as the requested window length")
inl note_of_cells_gate_machine_window_6 () = Note ("CELLS-GATE-MACHINE-WINDOW", 6u32, "2026-10-19T17:58Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 13:58 lap 11 grain, an interim bridge until EOIE-MACHINE-WINDOWS exists: the scaffold's scripts/gate.ps1 (lab/CELLS-NEW-PROJECT/patch/priv/new_project/scripts/gate.ps1) now runs the mix gate through spiral's own heavy gate (dot-sources apps/compiler/tmp/scripts/heavy.ps1 when the spiral checkout has it, Invoke-Heavy -Label cells gate <project>), so a gate waits for a free heavy slot instead of competing with suites; without heavy.ps1 it runs directly. Invoke-Heavy has no reentrancy marker (heavy.ps1 reads no env var), so a nested call would take a second slot or wait on itself: gate.ps1 takes -InsideHeavySlot to skip it, and spiral-new-run.ps1 (which already holds a slot) passes it; both parse with 0 errors; the ExUnit assertion (gate.ps1 mentions spiral.notebook) still holds. When EOIE-MACHINE-WINDOWS lands, the Invoke-Heavy call becomes the window acquire with expected-minutes.ps1's length")
inl note_of_cells_gate_machine_window_7 () = Note ("CELLS-GATE-MACHINE-WINDOW", 7u32, "2026-10-22T05:51Z", "main", "01:51 main: unblocked. EOIE-MACHINE-WINDOWS exists now (eoie window request/run/release, classes live-gui|test-suite|heavy-build|compile|compiler-install). The duration half is promoted: lab expected-minutes.ps1 is now apps/cells/spi/expected_minutes.ps1, re-self-tested (a synthetic 30 s run + 50 s hit + 50 s run gives 2 min; a missing metrics dir gives 10), and the lab folder is deleted. Remaining: lib_gate.ps1 and the scaffold gate.ps1 take a test-suite window of expected_minutes around their mix call. A wrapper can't tell it already holds a window: there is no env marker (checked eoie src), and every main job wraps lib_gate in 'eoie window run compile', so a nested request could wait on itself. Asked eoie for EOIE_WINDOW_HELD=<class>:<id> on window run children (and EOIE_HEAVY_HELD for job start --heavy). The wrapper change lands once that exists, with no lib_gate run in flight.")
inl note_of_cells_gate_machine_window_8 () = Note ("CELLS-GATE-MACHINE-WINDOW", 8u32, "2026-10-23T21:32Z", "main", "2026-10-23 17:32 main: the marker exists in eoie source (src/eoie_window_run/run.spi child_status sets EOIE_WINDOW_HELD on the window-run child) but not in the installed eoie.exe yet: 'eoie window run compile 1 -- pwsh -Command $env:EOIE_WINDOW_HELD' printed it empty at 17:3x. The wrapper change (lib_gate.ps1 and the scaffold gate.ps1 take a test-suite window of expected_minutes around their mix call unless EOIE_WINDOW_HELD is set) waits for the eoie release that carries it; re-probe with that one-liner first.")
inl note_of_cells_gate_machine_window_9 () = Note ("CELLS-GATE-MACHINE-WINDOW", 9u32, "2026-10-24T11:49Z", "main", "2026-10-19 laps folded from the deleted history ledger (docs/agents/history/2026-10-19-backlog-laps, HISTORY-BACKLOG-LAPS-DRIFT): lap 6 11:42 (subagent2): interface spelled out (test-suite window acquire with duration, release in the gate teardown; conflicts live-gui, compiler-install) -> next: blocked on EOIE-MACHINE-WINDOWS | lap 12 14:29 (subagent2): scaffold gate.ps1: -Path is [string[]], which pwsh -File passes as one comma string (a.spi,b.spi -> Resolve-Path fails); now splits on commas like try.ps1 -Modules; parse 0, split check resolves both files. Dep EOIE-MACHINE-WINDOWS still Planned 0% -> next: swap Invoke-Heavy for the window acquire when EOIE-MACHINE-WINDOWS lands | lap 13 14:41 (subagent2): blocked on EOIE-MACHINE-WINDOWS (Planned 0%, EOIE lane); interim bridge in the scaffold gate.ps1 stays -> next: unblocks: EOIE-MACHINE-WINDOWS lands its window acquire verb | lap 14 15:03 (subagent2): blocked on EOIE-MACHINE-WINDOWS (Planned) -> next: EOIE lane lands the window verb | lap 16 16:06 (subagent2): blocked on EOIE-MACHINE-WINDOWS -> next: EOIE lane | lap 17 16:43 (subagent2): blocked -> next: EOIE-MACHINE-WINDOWS | lap 19 18:56 (subagent2): blocked -> next: EOIE-MACHINE-WINDOWS")
inl note_of_cells_gate_machine_window_10 () = Note ("CELLS-GATE-MACHINE-WINDOW", 10u32, "2026-10-25T00:01Z", "main", "2026-10-24 20:01 main groom: status Active -> Blocked. Since the 10-19 lap 6 note, the only next step has been 'blocked on EOIE-MACHINE-WINDOWS', and that item is itself Blocked at 95% (eoie team). This resumes when it is Done.")
inl note_of_cells_gate_machine_window_11 () = Note ("CELLS-GATE-MACHINE-WINDOW", 11u32, "2026-10-30T02:01Z", "main", "10-29 22:01 main, groom: re-verified Blocked: waits on EOIE-MACHINE-WINDOWS (kit's, 95%, Blocked on EOIE-RELEASE-TRAIN). kit published TRAIN4 today (C528BE4D), so ask kit next session whether TRAIN4 or TRAIN5 carries the machine-windows piece; kit's EOIE-WINDOW-ANNOUNCE window kinds (n7) are related.")
inl note_of_cells_gate_machine_window_12 () = Note ("CELLS-GATE-MACHINE-WINDOW", 12u32, "2026-11-02T12:24Z", "x", "2026-11-02 08:24 x: GROOM: UNBLOCKED. Its blocker EOIE-MACHINE-WINDOWS is Done (100%), and the windows are in daily use (kit window request/release/wait-clear with heavy-build and tree-write classes; this session used tree-write several times today). What is left is Cells taking a test-suite window itself in --gate. Blocked -> Planned.")
inl note_of_cells_gate_machine_window_13 () = Note ("CELLS-GATE-MACHINE-WINDOW", 13u32, "2026-11-05T06:23Z", "main", "11-05 02:23 main DONE in apps/cells/spi/run_notebook.ps1 (unstaged, existing script): every --gate run takes ONE kit test-suite window for its notebooks (label \"cells gate <files>\") and releases it on every exit path (warm and mix exits through Exit-Notebook, plus a trap for thrown mix steps). kit's model makes test-suite wait behind live-gui and compiler-install (exclusive) and bench, while sharing the machine with other suites and heavy builds. --min-free-mb 0 keeps the 3 GB RAM floor from stalling gates on a busy box; the wait is bounded (SPIRAL_CELLS_GATE_WAIT_MIN, default 30), after which the gate runs anyway with a stderr warning, so a stuck window can't deadlock every lane. Lease SPIRAL_CELLS_GATE_WINDOW_MIN (default 180); opt out with SPIRAL_CELLS_GATE_WINDOW=0; no kit on the machine = no window, unchanged behavior. PROOF on the box: the exact request call was granted (queued 9 s), listed as held with its label, and gone after release; a real gate (apps/kernel/src/env.spi) passed 2/2 through the edited script and left no window of its own; another lane's gate (date_time.spi) was already holding a test-suite window minutes later, picked up from the live tree.")
