inl planning_of_eoie_wsjf_lpt () = Planning ("EOIE-WSJF-LPT", Minutes 60u32, Load Light, Unassigned)
inl spiral_op_arg_name_words () = Task ("SPIRAL-OP-ARG-NAME-WORDS", "Compiler: a local inl named words used inside a !!!! op argument (ArrayCreate of u32 with rows * words) fails with TracedError: Cannot apply a forall with a term at that argument; renaming the binding (width) compiles; name-dependent, so find what the name resolves to", TaskKind, P1, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "compiler,bug,peval,main", "current", "current")
inl formatter_keep_breaks () = Task ("FORMATTER-KEEP-BREAKS", "Formatter keeps authored line breaks: single-valued habits enforced, mixed habits (line breaks) left as written, over-120 still broken; then redo the destructive run from HEAD", Story, P0, D5, 95u32, Blocked, "COMPILER-DEAD-ARM-GLOBALS,LIB-EDITION3-ROUND", "formatter,spiral,team:compiler,main,wipe-redo", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_formatter_keep_breaks () = Planning ("FORMATTER-KEEP-BREAKS", Minutes 120u32, Load Heavy, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_formatter_keep_breaks_1 () = Note ("FORMATTER-KEEP-BREAKS", 1u32, "2026-10-20T11:32Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-20 07:32 main: user 2026-10-20 07:2x: 'the formatter is keeping things in a single line where it had breaks before, the formatter also should enforce the way things were, try finding a generic balance between the hand written libs'. Traced: lanes/formatter/PLAN.md Q2/Q3 (user 2026-10-14) chose normalize, on condition that the canonical layout is built from the user's own multi-line templates. The implementation joined everything that fits (e.g. lib/spiral/rust/rust.spi nominal rust_type arguments, one per line in the source, became one line), which breaks that condition and F2 ('a rule with no single habit in the user corpus is left as written, never normalized'). The lane's own facts show line breaking is mixed: the user breaks well under 120 (353 of 498 multi-line pipelines and 24 of 44 template heads would fit on one line). Decision (main, the user left the call): single-valued habits stay canonical (spacing, +4, records, match arms, operator alignment); authored breaks are kept; over-120 lines still break by the F5 templates; nothing is joined. Mechanism: Flatten records which tokens began a source line; layoutFlatLine aligns them to the re-lexed tokens; inlineText puts an invisible marker before a node that began an authored line, which displayWidth counts as unfittable, so every fits check across an authored break fails and the packers flush there; the marker is stripped on output. Gate: G2 (the user corpus polyglot@bb856d13, 86 files, formats to itself except spacing and over-120 lines), G1, G3, then redo FORMATTER-DESTRUCTIVE-RUN from the HEAD text of each file whose only change was formatting.")
inl note_of_formatter_keep_breaks_2 () = Note ("FORMATTER-KEEP-BREAKS", 2u32, "2026-10-20T12:00Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-20 08:00 main: measured on the user corpus (polyglot@bb856d13, 86 files, 14,683 non-blank lines; lab/FORMATTER-KEEP-BREAKS/extract-corpus.ps1, measure.ps1, classify*.ps1). Current formatter: 77 of 86 files change, 3,102 lines removed and 3,218 added, 439 code lines lost to joins. Candidate 1 (authored-break marker): 7 lost, 2,427/3,548 (it still re-broke blocks that fit by its own templates). Candidate 2 (plus: a top-level block with no code line over 120 stays as written): 0 lost, 1,026/1,919, 35 files unchanged. What is left: 229 hunks of Q1 literal splits (decided), 96 blank-line insertions and 81 blank removals (blank lines between blocks normalized to one; the 91% habit is mixed, so F2 says keep as written), 61 spacing-only (F4 rules, decided), 54 line breaks added (mostly over-120 statements, G4), 7 indentation-only (F3 violations: whole-block re-layout re-indents untouched lines, e.g. cli.spi group contents moved from +4 to aligned). Candidate 3 is staged (keep-breaks.ps1 now 26 edits, applied to a copy of core E00C68FCDAA3E950, pair-drift clean): blank lines between blocks emitted as written, and statement-level as-written (inside a block that needs re-layout, a statement whose tokens are exactly whole source lines that fit at the new indent is emitted from the source lines, shifted uniformly). Build waits for tibia's live block (until about 09:25).")
inl note_of_formatter_keep_breaks_3 () = Note ("FORMATTER-KEEP-BREAKS", 3u32, "2026-10-20T13:44Z", "main", "2026-10-20 09:44 candidate 3 (core 848A9564CD62EB03): G2 user corpus joins lost 0, removed 881 / added 1760 (c2 1026/1919), 43 of 86 files unchanged (c2 35); lib-head joins 0, 422/1204, 37 of 85 unchanged; G1 second pass 0 changes; G4 over-120 lines user 334->22, lib-head 270->77. Remaining violations found: BracketInnerSpace pads ;[..] arrays (user writes tight 54 vs 7), and statements re-laid for one over-120 macro string lose their authored shape and inner blank lines (svg/toggle). Candidate 4 (core 911F5BAB1A0FE771) keeps ;[ ] gaps as written and keeps such statements as written with inner blank/comment lines, leaving the split to splitMacroLine.")
inl note_of_formatter_keep_breaks_4 () = Note ("FORMATTER-KEEP-BREAKS", 4u32, "2026-10-20T14:29Z", "main", "2026-10-20 10:29 candidate 5 (core A0B2EFEA0EAA96E4, lab/FORMATTER-KEEP-BREAKS/keep-breaks.ps1): G2 user joins lost 0, removed 737 / added 1576, 45 of 86 unchanged, spacing-only hunks 61->8, blank lines 2774->2763 (c3 2752), G4 334->22; lib-head joins 0, 365/1036 (c3 422/1204), 37 of 85 unchanged, G4 270->77; G1 second pass 86 of 86 unchanged. Changes over c3: ;[..] array gaps as written (F2: user corpus 54 tight vs 7 spaced); an overflowing statement keeps its authored lines, inner blank lines and // comments when splitMacroLine (moved before the layout) brings every over-120 line to <=120; trailing // remarks count as written. Candidate 4 had accepted any macro line and left 17 lib-head lines over 120 that the macro splitter cannot split; c5 asks the splitter. Remaining diffs read: Q1 literal splits of long #if globals, macro continuations, over-120 signatures. Combined pack core B9BE5388B7B31B61 (HOPACSOTA chain + c5) staged in the combined-pack candidate, pair-drift clean.")
inl note_of_formatter_keep_breaks_5 () = Note ("FORMATTER-KEEP-BREAKS", 5u32, "2026-10-20T15:29Z", "main", "2026-10-20 11:29 candidate 6 = c5 + blank runs inside a block as written (layout dropped a blank line after a blank line), measured on the combined pack's Release single-flight DLL (core E29ACF09A0CF43AD): user joins 0, removed 727 / added 1576, 46 of 86 unchanged, G4 334->22, blank lines lost only 1 (util.spi 29->28); lib-head joins 0, 361/1036, 38 of 85 unchanged, G4 270->77, blank losses accordion 7->5 and am'_prototypes 3->2; G1 second pass 86 of 86 unchanged. Matrix review of the pack running.")
inl note_of_formatter_keep_breaks_6 () = Note ("FORMATTER-KEEP-BREAKS", 6u32, "2026-10-20T15:32Z", "main", "2026-10-20 11:32 checked a suspected semantic change in lib/spiral/leptos/ui/accordion.spi: an authored two-line lambda body, (if right_element_none then '' else '[margin-right:40px]') then a line ++# ' ' ++# summary_class (), is laid out since c3 as if/then/else lines followed by ++# lines at the if's column, without the parentheses. Not a semantic change: an operator line aligned with if applies to the whole if (probe: if c / then 1i32 / else 2i32 / + 10i32 compiles to C and exits 11), and the parse guard compares full statement shapes. Cosmetic leftover: the operator-chain layout drops the parentheses of a first operand that is a round group (later operands keep theirs as op ( .. )). Combined pack E29ACF09A0CF43AD review single-flight-20261020-112256 vs single-flight-20261020-015844: 7,290 rows, missing 0, differing cells 0, behavior cells 0, DISAGREE 0.")
inl note_of_formatter_keep_breaks_7 () = Note ("FORMATTER-KEEP-BREAKS", 7u32, "2026-10-20T18:07Z", "main", "14:07 tibia repo evidence (tibia session): installed single-flight core EA7AC11D786629F2 formatted 42 files; second --check clean; over-120 lines 473 -> 23 (macro strings it cannot split); 15 compiled outputs (4 C probes, 1 WGSL, 9 Lua, map Rust) byte-identical before/after, including a Lua string.format literal split with backslash continuations. Redo apply: 1203 files rewritten (1261 formatting-only, 3 merged, 0 conflicts). The first entry baseline compiled 1 of 26 because the package dir was unset; entry-compare.ps1 now compiles a scratch mirror with old and new text at the same path.")
inl note_of_formatter_keep_breaks_8 () = Note ("FORMATTER-KEEP-BREAKS", 8u32, "2026-10-20T18:07Z", "main", "14:07 host usage line (Program.fs) now lists Wasm, Wgsl, Calyx, Kotlin, Swift, OpenQasm, Ebpf, Lean and Bend with their extensions; reported by tibia. Goes into the next host build.")
inl note_of_formatter_keep_breaks_9 () = Note ("FORMATTER-KEEP-BREAKS", 9u32, "2026-10-20T18:43Z", "main", "14:43 tibia leftovers (23 over-120 after the format): 4 are macro strings it cannot split. 19 are code the layout did not re-lay: (a) tuple bindings with if-expression values, about 9; (b) one-line record literals bound with inl, 3; (c) a pipeline call ending in a lambda, 1; (d) test names over 120. Pathological: a long one-line 'if a then x else if b then y ...' chain (30 branches) was laid as a staircase nesting each 'else if' one level deeper, until indentation alone passed 120. Next candidate: lay an 'else if' chain flat ('else if c then' at the if's column, as elif already is) and give F5 templates for (a), (b) and (c). Tibia now writes elif and pipes generated code through SpiralCompiler format.")
inl note_of_formatter_keep_breaks_10 () = Note ("FORMATTER-KEEP-BREAKS", 10u32, "2026-10-20T20:02Z", "main", "16:02 else-if staircase: Spiral rejects a flat 'else if c then' with an indented body (probe: parse error at the second else), so laying an else-if chain flat is impossible as written. The parser folds elif into the same nested RawIfThenElse, so candidate 7 (lab else-if-chain.ps1 over the tree core E29ACF09A0CF43AD) lays an else-if chain that must be re-laid as an elif chain. Only an over-120 statement is rewritten; one that fits keeps 'else if' on its line, and the sameParse guard still applies. Queued as heavy job else-if-c7: a 30-branch probe plus C byte-compare, G2/G4 on user and lib-head against c6, G1.")
inl note_of_formatter_keep_breaks_11 () = Note ("FORMATTER-KEEP-BREAKS", 11u32, "2026-10-20T22:44Z", "main", "18:44 tibia leftovers diagnosed (format-corpus listing): in isolation the record literal bound with inl and the pipeline ending in lambdas re-lay fine (record onto its own line, pipeline one stage per line). The tuple binding with an if value fails the parse guard, and because the guard rejects a whole top-level block, that one statement keeps every other long line of its function verbatim. Three hand layouts of the tuple (one item per line; inline; the if split over lines) compile to identical C, so the rejected candidate is something else (likely the comma path dropping the if's authored parentheses). Candidate 8 (heavy job formatter-c8: prune-walk.ps1 + guard-dump.ps1) adds SPIRAL_FORMAT_GUARD_DUMP=<dir>, which writes each rejected candidate with its shapes, and the reparse-point-pruning walk. Next design: when a block fails the guard, retry it with each over-120 statement laid alone.")
inl note_of_formatter_keep_breaks_12 () = Note ("FORMATTER-KEEP-BREAKS", 12u32, "2026-10-20T22:46Z", "main", "18:46 candidate 8 (lab guard-dump.ps1 + prune-walk.ps1, heavy job formatter-c8) adds a per-statement retry when a block fails the parse guard. The first layout records the source-line span of every statement asWrittenStatement declined as too wide. The block is then re-laid once per innermost span, with only that span, its enclosing statements and the spans already accepted allowed to change; every other statement is kept exactly as written. Each retry must pass sameParse to be accepted, a partial result is tallied as parse-guard-partial, and only a block with no acceptable retry falls back to verbatim (and is dumped with SPIRAL_FORMAT_GUARD_DUMP). Gates in the job: guard dump of the tibia probes, the combined probe (record and pipeline beside the guarded tuple), G1 on probes and the user corpus, G2 user/lib-head against c7, and format --check walks of dice/alphabet/polyglot/dir-tree-html within 3 min each.")
inl note_of_formatter_keep_breaks_13 () = Note ("FORMATTER-KEEP-BREAKS", 13u32, "2026-10-20T23:16Z", "main", "19:16 candidate 7 installed and reviewed: single-flight core 91FFA26142D63D73 (tree 1486B2B06DF3B3B5) and hopacsota core 2F2586DB0277DEA2 built at 17:2x. Review single-flight-20261020-190616 vs single-flight-20261020-141656: 7,290 rows, 0 missing, 0 differing cells, 0 behavior cells, DISAGREE 0. Over the redone repos it would change one file, apps/cells/spi/notebook.spi: the over-120 scoped_test_body line from CELLS-WGSL-TEST-STATUS gets its if laid as if / then / else inside the call's parentheses. Formatted with the next Cells edit.")
inl note_of_formatter_keep_breaks_14 () = Note ("FORMATTER-KEEP-BREAKS", 14u32, "2026-10-20T23:49Z", "main", "19:49 candidate 8 results (formatter-c8): the per-statement retry works. The tibia probe function is kept as parse-guard-partial, with the record and pipeline re-laid and only the tuple statement as written; G1 clean on the probes and the user corpus; user corpus identical to c7. The symlink walks finish: dice 36 s, alphabet 121 s, polyglot 20 s, dir-tree-html 10 s. The guard dump names the tuple bug: the comma path lays a parenthesized if as '(if c' with then/else at the parenthesis column, one left of the if, which Spiral rejects (ExpectedEob). One caution: lib-head differs from c7 in one file, sm'_real.spir (+101/-45). The retry re-laid a block c7 kept verbatim, and that block lost authored parentheses ('(fun () => ...) : () -> string' became 'fun () => ... : () -> string'). The AST is the same, but it isn't as written. Candidate 9 (queued as formatter-c9): a retry is accepted only when it keeps the original's parenthesis count, and a parenthesized if in a comma list stays on one line when it fits (tuple-if.ps1). Gates: probes, G1, user and lib-head vs c8 and c7. The evening pack now carries tuple-if.ps1 too.")
inl note_of_formatter_keep_breaks_15 () = Note ("FORMATTER-KEEP-BREAKS", 15u32, "2026-10-21T00:51Z", "main", "20:51 formatter-c9 was lost to the idle memory reaper at about 20:50 (attached 'eoie job run', kill-on-close). It had built and passed the probe stage and the symlink walks (alphabet 134 s, polyglot 11 s, dir-tree-html 9 s) before it died in G2. Requeue detached when the user asks; the evening pack (core 197AB83568E512E0 on tree 5AA4938BE6339F4A, dry run clean) waits on c9's gates.")
inl note_of_formatter_keep_breaks_16 () = Note ("FORMATTER-KEEP-BREAKS", 16u32, "2026-10-21T05:19Z", "main", "2026-10-21 01:19 c9 walk: alphabet --check did not finish in 180 s. Its Spiral sources are 4 files; the cost is apps/hangul/hanja.spi (and its dist copy), one 465 KB table literal of ~27k i32 pairs on 4586 lines, all under 120 columns, so layout takes the AsWritten path; the eager 'expected = shapes original' parse and flatten still run on the whole block. A slice-scaling probe (scratchpad hanja-scaling.ps1, 250/500/1000/2000 rows on the c9 DLL) could not start: 2.6 GB free with 3 heavy jobs, the process sat at 6 MB and no CPU for 90 s. Rerun when the box is quiet; if superlinear, make 'expected' lazy for AsWritten blocks first.")
inl note_of_formatter_keep_breaks_17 () = Note ("FORMATTER-KEEP-BREAKS", 17u32, "2026-10-21T05:26Z", "main", "2026-10-21 01:26 candidate 9 (paren-count guard on retries + tuple-if layout) passes: G2 user 0 lines lost to joins, 46/86 unchanged, output identical to c7 and c8; G2 lib-head identical to c7, and the only c8 difference (1 file, the dropped authored parens in sm'_real.spir) is gone; G1 second pass 86/86 unchanged; probe second pass identical; walks dice 176 s, polyglot 57 s, dir-tree-html 54 s, alphabet over 180 s (note 16). Goes in the evening pack with lazy-expected (job formatter-c9b).")
inl note_of_formatter_keep_breaks_18 () = Note ("FORMATTER-KEEP-BREAKS", 18u32, "2026-10-21T05:52Z", "main", "2026-10-21 01:52 main: lazy-expected gated on the exact evening pack: formatted output byte-identical to c9 on the user corpus (86 files). alphabet/apps/hangul/hanja.spi --check: c9 still running after 240 s (stopped), pack exit 0 in 139 s. Most of the remaining 139 s is outside the shape parse; next suspect is flatten/markAuthoredBreaks on one 27k-pair block (to profile when the box is quiet).")
inl note_of_formatter_keep_breaks_19 () = Note ("FORMATTER-KEEP-BREAKS", 19u32, "2026-10-21T07:20Z", "main", "2026-10-21 03:20 main: evening pack INSTALLED 03:19 (job pack-evening-install, compiler-install window 03:05-03:19): tree core A9A71772B249FB8B = candidates 8-9 (retry with paren-count guard, tuple-if layout, guard dump), prune-walk, lazy-expected, plus the X-COMPAT and diagnostic changes; Release SpiralCompilerCore single-flight BCFA535906B978C8, hopacsota EFC325C8FB23A9B5; pair-drift clean, hopacsota lint G1 596 lines, G2 0. Matrix review (-Suite all -Native -ReuseNative vs single-flight-20261020-141656) running in heavy slot 1.")
inl note_of_formatter_keep_breaks_20 () = Note ("FORMATTER-KEEP-BREAKS", 20u32, "2026-10-21T08:24Z", "main", "2026-10-21 04:24 main: evening pack REVIEW PASSED. single-flight-20261021-031858 (-Suite all -Native -ReuseNative, SPIRAL_CC pinned) vs single-flight-20261020-141656: 7,290 rows each, missing 0, DISAGREE 0, residual differences 0, behavior cells 2 = samples/conversation_negative_orphan_result Fsharp and C, compile error -> timeout (20 s). Explained as load: the review ran beside x's round4 (Cells + cargo), tibia's R56 suites and eoie's r26 quick compile with about 3 GB free, and summed compile time was 1,940 s against 398-757 s in the five previous reviews. Rerun single-flight-20261021-042025 (-Filter that sample): C 796 ms, Rust 817 ms, Delphi 783 ms, F# 7.0 s first-job warm-up, all 'error' as expected (baseline C 762 ms, F# 2,434 ms). Checked and ruled out a host cause: the fingerprint walk covers 31 files in the package dir (31-43 ms either way). No residual moved, so no -Accept is needed.")
inl note_of_formatter_keep_breaks_21 () = Note ("FORMATTER-KEEP-BREAKS", 21u32, "2026-10-21T11:15Z", "main", "2026-10-21 07:15 main: diagnostic patch for the hanja.spi cost (139 s for a file kept as written): lab/FORMATTER-KEEP-BREAKS/stage-timings.ps1 (dry run 2/2 anchors on the installed core A9A71772B249FB8B). With SPIRAL_FORMAT_TIMINGS=1 it prints 'format-timing <path> <stage> <ms>' for join-macros, lex-1, respace-1, render-1, lex-2, layout (with flatten separately), lex-3, respace-2, render-2. Diagnostic candidate only, never installed. Plan after tibia's live block: Debug candidate in candidates/format-timings, then format --check on hanja.spi and on its 250/500/1000/2000-row slices (scratchpad hanja-scaling.ps1), and fix the stage that grows faster than linear.")
inl note_of_formatter_keep_breaks_22 () = Note ("FORMATTER-KEEP-BREAKS", 22u32, "2026-10-21T12:21Z", "main", "2026-10-21 08:21 main: stage timings measured (Debug candidate format-timings = installed core + stage-timings.ps1, SPIRAL_FORMAT_PARALLEL=1, machine busy): hanja.spi --check 18 s total; ms join-macros 3007, lex-1 2691, respace-1 1981, render-1 27, lex-2 2652, flatten 399, layout 531, lex-3 2385, respace-2 2938, render-2 72. Slices 250/500/1000/2000 rows: 2/3/12/18 s; per stage 8x rows gives 8-19x time, with noise from load, so nothing grows clearly worse than linear. Verdict: last night's 139 s (and >240 s on c9) was mostly machine load; the real cost is the pipeline lexing the whole file 3 times plus respace twice and joinMacroContinuations (about 2-3 s each on 465 KB). Cheapest next win: lex once and reuse when layout keeps every block as written (hanja's case). Not urgent.")
inl note_of_formatter_keep_breaks_23 () = Note ("FORMATTER-KEEP-BREAKS", 23u32, "2026-10-21T20:39Z", "main", "16:39 staged the cheapest win from the 08:21 stage timings: lab/FORMATTER-KEEP-BREAKS/skip-unchanged-respace.ps1 (1 anchor, dry run matches once on the tree core A9A71772B249FB8B). When layout returns its input unchanged (laid = prespaced, the common case for files kept as written such as hanja.spi), formatText returns prespaced and skips lex-3, respace-2 and render-2 (about 2.4 + 2.9 + 0.1 s of hanja's 18 s). This is safe only if respace and render are idempotent on their own output, so the gate proves it with one binary: skip-respace-gate.ps1 builds a Debug candidate (candidates/format-skip), then formats the user corpus (polyglot@bb856d13), lib-head and alphabet hanja.spi twice, with SPIRAL_FORMAT_FULL_SECOND_PASS=1 (old path) and without (shortcut). Rule, fixed before measuring: 0 differing files everywhere, and the shortcut no slower on hanja; then it joins the next pack. Runs after tonight's install, never during a live block.")
inl note_of_formatter_keep_breaks_24 () = Note ("FORMATTER-KEEP-BREAKS", 24u32, "2026-10-21T21:19Z", "x", "2026-10-21 17:19 x review (read-only, at main's request) of lab/FORMATTER-KEEP-BREAKS/skip-unchanged-respace.ps1. Question: with f = joinLines . renderAll . respace . lexText, is f(f(y)) = f(y), where y = joinMacroContinuations text? Verdict: yes by construction; no counterexample found. Reasons: (1) joinMacroContinuations feeds only the first pass's input, and the old code didn't run it in the second pass either. (2) splitLines/joinLines are a fixed pair after one pass: CRLF becomes LF, exactly one trailing newline, one trailing empty piece dropped, and respace trims trailing Blank lines (empty file gives '\\n', stable). (3) Tabs never reach respace: measured with the installed compiler's 'tokens' verb, tokenize_line stops at a tab (leading tab, 'f<TAB>-1', 'x<TAB>-y' and 'x<TAB>- y' give no tokens after it), so lexLine makes any line with a tab outside strings/comments Opaque Unlexable, and it is rendered verbatim in both passes. render writes indent and gaps as spaces and trimmedTail is TrimEnd, so trailing whitespace is normalized in pass 1. (4) gapRule depends only on tokens, InsideMacro, constraint and array marks, except ApplicationSpace, which needs left.NearTo = right.From; pass 1 widens that gap to 1, so pass 2 reads it as AsWritten and keeps 1. (5) The respaceLine guard (relexed tokenKinds = original) is a deterministic function of the line text, so a GuardKept line yields the same decision again. (6) No side effects are lost: formatText discards respace's changes, and layoutTally is only touched by layout, which still runs. Inputs worth adding to skip-respace-gate's corpora anyway: f(x) application spacing on a line that also trips the guard; CRLF with a lone CR; no trailing newline; trailing blank lines; a macro continuation ending in '\\'; an empty file; a Code line with a tab (expected verbatim).")
inl note_of_formatter_keep_breaks_25 () = Note ("FORMATTER-KEEP-BREAKS", 25u32, "2026-10-21T21:20Z", "main", "17:20 thanks to x's review (note 24): its 8 inputs are now lab/FORMATTER-KEEP-BREAKS/synthetic/*.spi: a tab before '-1' and '- x' and before a unary '-' (the one theoretical gap: lexing after a tab vs a space); f(x) application spacing next to g (y)-1; CRLF plus a lone CR; no trailing newline; trailing blank and whitespace-only lines; a macro continuation ending in '\\'; an empty file; trailing spaces and tabs. skip-respace-gate.ps1 now formats that corpus as well. Tonight's running gate started before the edit, so synthetic-check.ps1 runs it separately on the same candidate. The pre-written rule applies to it too: 0 differing files.")
inl note_of_formatter_keep_breaks_26 () = Note ("FORMATTER-KEEP-BREAKS", 26u32, "2026-10-21T21:51Z", "main", "17:51 skip-respace gate PASSED (job main-evening-a; Debug candidate format-skip = tree core plus skip-unchanged-respace.ps1, core 34CB1982CFDB2CD3, built in 801 s on a loaded box). Byte comparison, full second pass vs shortcut, same binary: user 86 files 0 differing (155.6 s -> 123.5 s); lib-head 85 files 0 differing (229 s -> 548.3 s: that run overlapped the 17:27 memory squeeze, so it is load, not a slowdown; the rule checks speed on hanja only); hanja.spi 0 differing (80.7 s -> 30.0 s, 2.7x faster); x's synthetic corpus (synthetic-check.ps1) 8/8 identical, with the tab and lone-CR files kept verbatim as x measured. Accepted by the pre-written rule. It joins the next pack, after tonight's night pack, whose core 9E336ED57C8ACFE5 is already under pre-install review and stays as is. Peak memory 641 MB.")
inl note_of_formatter_keep_breaks_27 () = Note ("FORMATTER-KEEP-BREAKS", 27u32, "2026-10-22T03:08Z", "main", "23:08 user decisions from the dice and alphabet unstaged diffs (rounds of 23:0x-23:2x): (1) one-line if that fits stays one line, then stays where written, else+if still becomes elif; (2) no synthetic parentheses in output (join heading a pipeline stays bare); (3) a lambda that is not the last argument stays bare with its head on the call line (|> listm.fold fun acc (k, v) =>), body 8 past the pipe, remaining arguments 4 past it (parse checked: fold_head probe exits 57 as intended); multi-line stage arguments 4 past the pipe, not aligned under the callee; (4) one over-long line re-lays only that line (fun x => { x with stays); (5) macro strings break at the last fitting space else anywhere, never inside a {!expr} hole, continuation 4 past the statement; (6) long heads keep one part per line. Causes found (candidate builds with layout dumps, TEMP copy): formatter-made parentheses lost their synthetic mark in about 69 layout sites that write opening/closing text directly, so dropSyntheticParentheses never saw them; if layout always split keywords because opensLayout is true for every if; the macro splitter hard-cut between a {!expr} unit and its closing brace. Fix in progress in a TEMP candidate (markedText plus liftSyntheticGroups, authored-break-aware if, elif precedence, glued holes, middle-lambda stage layout); round 1 gate: fix 1 alone byte-identical on user, lib, synthetic, only dice join lines changed; round 2 gate running. Not yet done: (4) minimal re-lay for records.")
inl note_of_formatter_keep_breaks_28 () = Note ("FORMATTER-KEEP-BREAKS", 28u32, "2026-10-22T03:12Z", "main", "23:12 night rule for landing the formatter fixes (fixed before measuring): the round-2 gate must show no token changes, and every hunk in user, lib and eoie must sit in a block that had a line over 120 or be a then-placement or synthetic-parenthesis repair of that kind; then the fixes are written into the tree spiral_compiler.fs (user commits) and ride PACK-AFTER-NIGHT's review. A failing part is dropped, the rest land.")
inl note_of_formatter_keep_breaks_29 () = Note ("FORMATTER-KEEP-BREAKS", 29u32, "2026-10-22T05:30Z", "main", "01:30 main: drain plan for lab/FORMATTER-KEEP-BREAKS (26 files). Promoted into apps/compiler/tmp/formatter: extract-corpus.ps1, synthetic/ (8 edge-case files), and candidate-gate.ps1. That's my fmt-fix gate made general: -Dll and -Work, corpora user (polyglot@bb856d13) + lib HEAD + synthetic + dice/documents + eoie HEAD, review-*.diff per corpus. Rule fix: the candidate's exit must EQUAL the installed exit; it used to require 0, but eoie exits 2 on both sides. Patch scripts were checked by AST (each New block searched in the cores): lazy-expected 2/2 in tree; guard-dump 3/4 in tree (the 4th landed revised); skip-unchanged-respace 1/1 in the PACK-AFTER-NIGHT core, lands with it; stage-timings 0/0 (instrument, numbers in notes). The rest are runners and classifiers whose results are in notes. The folder is deleted when PACK-AFTER-NIGHT installs; the lab copies stay until main-fmt-fix4 ends, since it reads them.")
inl note_of_formatter_keep_breaks_30 () = Note ("FORMATTER-KEEP-BREAKS", 30u32, "2026-10-22T05:47Z", "main", "01:47 main: round 4 (job main-fmt-fix4) hit its wall clock after the user and lib corpora; the Debug build alone took 1,371 s on the loaded machine. user: 84 files, 0 differing. lib: 9 differing, 1 flagged token change (runtime.spi). Read by hand, the flag is legitimate: the source writes 'execution_options fun x => { x with' on one line. The installed formatter moved '{ x with' down; the candidate keeps it (the user's re-lay-only-the-long-line decision). The 4-column shift then moves where the over-long single source string is split. Both outputs split a string the source wrote whole, so concatenated text is equal. candidate-gate.ps1 now drops formatter-made quote-^^-quote joins before comparing tokens. OPEN, blocks the pack: lib took 970.6 s on the candidate against 239.7 s installed (4x; round 3 was 1.4x, Debug vs Release both times). Rule, fixed before measuring (job main-fmt-timing, per-file, min of 2 interleaved runs per side): files at more than 3x their installed time get their round-4 path found and fixed; if the total ratio stays above 2x, round 4's slow change leaves the pack and round 3's behaviour ships for that construct. The 8 other lib hunks are still to be read.")
inl note_of_formatter_keep_breaks_31 () = Note ("FORMATTER-KEEP-BREAKS", 31u32, "2026-10-22T05:49Z", "main", "01:49 main: hand review of all 9 round-4 lib hunks against the lib HEAD source. Every one is legitimate and moves the output toward the source. (1) conversation, file_system, state_core, rust/testing: authored 'if ... then' and one-line ifs restored; file_system's lambda body sits 4 past the pipe, as written. (2) json_scan: the source's 2-line elif chain was over 120, so it is re-laid one elif per line with 'then' kept inline. (3) listm' cons': the 124-column line breaks only after '=', and the one-line if is kept. (4) sm' obj_to_string_zig: only the elif lines over 120 move their 'then' down. (5) accordion: the parentheses around the if were AUTHORED; the installed formatter dropped them and split the if. (6) runtime: the record body stays on the lambda line, as written (note 30). Output quality passes on lib; user 0 diffs. Remaining blocker: speed (main-fmt-timing); the synthetic, dice and eoie corpora still need a run (candidate-gate.ps1 after the speed fix).")
inl note_of_formatter_keep_breaks_32 () = Note ("FORMATTER-KEEP-BREAKS", 32u32, "2026-10-22T05:55Z", "main", "01:55 main: round 5 queued (heavy job main-fmt-fix5, own cache candidates/fmt-fix5, so the timing job's DLL stays intact). apply.ps1 changes: (1) elseIfChain lays the body out at indent+2 again ONLY when the first layout's head is an 'if' line too wide for the 'el' prefix (before, any failed elif form re-laid the whole remaining chain, up to 2^depth in nested chains); (2) middleLambda is lazy (Option.orElseWith after lastArgumentLambda), so a stage whose last-argument form succeeds never lays out the middle-lambda body. Applies on tree core 9E336ED57C8ACFE5: 12 edits + 70 marked sites. compose.ps1 picks it up (same apply.ps1). Gate: apps/compiler/tmp/formatter/candidate-gate.ps1 (tokens equal modulo formatter-made string joins, candidate exits equal installed exits) on user, lib, synthetic, dice, eoie, plus the speed rule of note 30 on its per-corpus seconds. Hard stop: any of my compile jobs still running at 05:25 is stopped (tibia live block 05:30).")
inl note_of_formatter_keep_breaks_33 () = Note ("FORMATTER-KEEP-BREAKS", 33u32, "2026-10-22T06:11Z", "main", "02:11 main: speed rule of note 30 applied to job main-fmt-timing (per-file, min of 2 interleaved runs per side, lib HEAD): total installed 305 s, candidate 433 s = 1.42x, the same as round 3 (1.4x, Debug vs Release). Largest per-file gaps are about +3 s on every file (parsing 5.6 -> 9.8, base 2.3 -> 5.1): constant Debug start-up, no layout blow-up, no file at 3x or more. Rule met; the 4x in main-fmt-fix4 was load (the candidate ran in the busiest stretch). Round 5 (main-fmt-fix5) stays queued: it does strictly less layout work (the narrower elif retry, the lazy middle lambda) and is the run that covers synthetic, dice and eoie under candidate-gate.ps1. Its outputs must equal round 4's on user and lib, where round 4 is already reviewed.")
inl note_of_formatter_keep_breaks_34 () = Note ("FORMATTER-KEEP-BREAKS", 34u32, "2026-10-22T06:15Z", "main", "02:15 main: main-fmt-fix5 (round 5 build + candidate-gate, queued since 01:5x) stopped while still queued, at eoie's request. eoie's release ABC regeneration was crawling on a saturated machine (8 cores busy), and an install by about 05:00 needs the CPU before tibia's 05:30 live block. No window leaked. It reruns when eoie reports ABC past regeneration, if it can end before 05:25 by its estimate; otherwise after 06:30 or next session. The script is unchanged.")
inl note_of_formatter_keep_breaks_35 () = Note ("FORMATTER-KEEP-BREAKS", 35u32, "2026-10-22T06:52Z", "main", "02:52 main: design for the 'general minimal re-lay' (splice mode), grounded in today's formatter layout loop (spiral_compiler.fs ~181309-181394, read only). The parts exist: (a) fitsAsWritten keeps any block whose lines all fit, byte for byte (AsWritten); (b) relayOnly plus relaidSpans let layoutFlatLine re-lay only chosen statement spans and keep every other span as written; retried() already uses that, leaf span by leaf span, with the parse guard and the authored parenthesis count. But retried() runs only as a FALLBACK, after the full layout of an over-long block fails the parse guard. Splice mode makes it the FIRST attempt for any block with an over-long line: (1) lay the block once to fill relaidSpans; (2) choose the leaf spans whose source lines include one over 120 (sourceLines gives each span's original lines); (3) set relayOnly to those spans plus their enclosing spans, as retried does with 'around', and re-lay; (4) accept when chosen() passes (same parse shapes) and every output line fits; otherwise grow to the parent span, and finally fall back to today's full layout. Effect: a block with one long statement changes only that statement, the user's 'fun x => { x with' case without a special rule. Cost: one extra layoutFlatLine per long block (round-4 timing shows layout is not the bottleneck: 1.42x is Debug start-up). Gate: candidate-gate.ps1; expect fewer differing lines than round 5 on lib and eoie, 0 token changes, user corpus 0 diffs. Not started; it goes in a later round after round 5 ships.")
inl note_of_formatter_keep_breaks_36 () = Note ("FORMATTER-KEEP-BREAKS", 36u32, "2026-10-22T07:15Z", "main", "03:15 main: start rule for main-fmt-fix5b, fixed now: it needs about 80 min (Debug build about 23 min on this load, then 5 corpora) and must end before 05:25 (tibia's live block at 05:30). If it has not started by 04:00, I stop it (my own queued job) and requeue it after 06:30 or for the next session. If it starts by 04:00 and is still running at 05:20, the 05:18 guard stops it.")
inl note_of_formatter_keep_breaks_37 () = Note ("FORMATTER-KEEP-BREAKS", 37u32, "2026-10-22T07:58Z", "main", "03:58 main: correction to note 35 after reading asWrittenStatement (core ~180101-180144). The normal layout ALREADY keeps any statement whose source lines all fit, as written, at statement granularity, and records each statement it cannot keep in relaidSpans (first, last source line). What a full re-lay still changes are the ENCLOSING statement's own lines (head, closing) whenever a nested statement is too long; that is the 'fun x => { x with' case. retried() already re-lays only the innermost unkeepable spans (relayOnly) and keeps every enclosing line as written, but runs only as the parse-guard FALLBACK. So splice mode is a 1-anchor reorder, drafted as %TEMP%\\fmt-fix\\splice-first.ps1 (dry run; anchor matches once on the tree core; meant to apply after apply.ps1). In the LaidOut branch, 'marked' is replaced by retried()'s lines when they exist and every line fits 120 (comments exempt), and the unchanged 'chosen marked' path continues. chosen on already-chosen, mark-free text is the identity, and if it fails the old fallback still runs. Expected effect: fewer changed lines than round 5 wherever only a nested statement was long, and the round-4 record-on-lambda rule becomes a special case. It goes to a later round, after round 5's gate, so the two effects stay separable. Gate: candidate-gate.ps1 compared with round 5 and with installed: 0 token changes, fewer differing lines, user corpus still 0.")
inl note_of_formatter_keep_breaks_38 () = Note ("FORMATTER-KEEP-BREAKS", 38u32, "2026-10-22T08:17Z", "main", "04:17 main: round 5 (main-fmt-fix5b; candidate built in 169 s once load dropped) so far: user 84 files, 0 differing; lib 82 files, 9 differing, 0 beyond parentheses/whitespace/string joins. And lib's 82 outputs are BYTE-IDENTICAL to round 4's hand-reviewed outputs (0 of 82 differ), so the narrower elif retry and the lazy middle lambda changed no output, only work. Remaining corpora: synthetic (now with heap_pair_types and mut_heap_pair_types), dice+documents, eoie.")
inl note_of_formatter_keep_breaks_39 () = Note ("FORMATTER-KEEP-BREAKS", 39u32, "2026-10-22T09:01Z", "main", "2026-10-22 05:02 main: rule fixed before the outcome. If main-fmt-fix5b has not printed its eoie corpus row by 05:18, the guard stops it. Round 5 then stays in the 06:31 compose on the corpora already measured: user 84 files differing 0; lib 9 differing, byte-identical to round 4's reviewed outputs; synthetic 10 differing 0; dice 2 intended. The eoie corpus check (candidate-gate.ps1 -Corpus eoie against the composed pack DLL) becomes a precondition of install.ps1: no install while any eoie file differs beyond parentheses and whitespace. If the row arrives before 05:18 and shows such a difference, the formatter step leaves compose before 06:31 instead.")
inl note_of_formatter_keep_breaks_40 () = Note ("FORMATTER-KEEP-BREAKS", 40u32, "2026-10-22T09:06Z", "main", "2026-10-22 05:06 main: round 5 (main-fmt-fix5b, core 8C9B7BFBAB54AB88) finished all corpora; automatic part PASS. Corpora: user 84 files differing 0; lib 82 differing 9, byte-identical to round 4's reviewed outputs; synthetic 10 differing 0; dice 6 differing 2 (intended); eoie 560 differing 86. Every corpus has 0 files differing beyond parentheses and whitespace, and installed and candidate exit the same (eoie 2 and 2). eoie hunks reviewed against the gate's input snapshot (corpus-eoie), not the moving tree. Totals vs the authored input: installed +9093 -2283 lines, candidate +8242 -1962, so round 5 is closer to the authored text on every count. Of the 478 candidate lines in the review diff, 335 occur verbatim in the input: the installed core split authored one-line 'if c then x' / 'elif c then' and round 5 keeps them. The other 143 are authored lines over 120: round 5 breaks once after '=' and keeps a fitting 'if .. then .. else' on one line, or keeps 'then' on the elif head with the call below; the installed core exploded them into if / then / else lines. Both classes match the decided rules (re-lay only the long line; then stays where authored). Verdict: round 5 stays in the 06:31 compose, and the 05:02 fallback rule is moot.")
inl note_of_formatter_keep_breaks_41 () = Note ("FORMATTER-KEEP-BREAKS", 41u32, "2026-10-22T09:35Z", "main", "2026-10-22 05:35 main: round 5 against the AUTHORED eoie input (corpus-eoie snapshot, not installed vs candidate), 1,327 hunks. Hunks whose authored lines are over 120 columns are re-lays the rules require: 815 pure splits, plus most of the 427 'other', which are \\$'...' macros split with backslash continuations ('break anywhere, last space first'). Only 56 hunks touch lines that already fit: 22 remove a leading blank line at file start (single-valued habit), 2 fold 'else' + nested 'if' into 'elif', about 27 are parenthesis or whitespace normalisation, and 5 VIOLATE keep-breaks by moving an authored 'elif c then' + body to 'elif c' / 'then body'. Sites: agile_mutation_policy/model.spi:119, state_receipt_codec_model/common.spi:335 and :341, state_receipt_codec_model/sm.spi:31, toolchain_argument_domain/bridge.spi:18. Cause: a sibling branch of the same if chain holds an over-long line (model.spi: 131 columns), so the whole statement is re-laid and fitting branches are re-laid too. That is the case splice-first (%TEMP%\\fmt-fix\\splice-first.ps1: try retried(), the innermost over-long statements, before the full re-lay) targets. Rule fixed now for round 6: splice-first goes in only if (1) the 5 sites keep their authored lines, (2) user/lib/synthetic/dice show no new differing file vs round 5, and (3) eoie's fitting-line hunks drop from 56 toward the 22+2 habit-only ones. Not part of the 06:31 pack; it is a candidate for the next pack.")
inl note_of_formatter_keep_breaks_42 () = Note ("FORMATTER-KEEP-BREAKS", 42u32, "2026-10-22T09:37Z", "main", "2026-10-22 05:37 main: root cause of the 5 'then moved down' sites, from reading round 5's layout code. mergeThen lifts 'then' back onto the condition line only when the branch was laid as a bare 'then', or as 'then body' with the body NOT authored on its own line. An authored 'elif c then' / body branch is laid as 'then body' (it fits), and the merge refuses because bodyOnOwnLine is true, so 'then' stays under the condition. Splice-first alone would not fix it: retried() re-lays the innermost over-long statement together with all its ancestors, and the if chain is an ancestor. Fix staged: %TEMP%\\fmt-fix\\then-on-head.ps1 adds a third mergeThen case; when the body was authored on its own line, it keeps 'cond then' on the head and puts the body back on its own line, 4 columns in. Dry run: matches round 5's core (8C9B7BFBAB54AB88) once. %TEMP%\\fmt-fix\\round6.ps1 builds round 5 + then-on-head (cache fmt-fix6), formats the 4 site files and prints the lines that start with 'then' (authored vs round 5 vs round 6), then runs the full candidate gate in formatter-gate6. Start rule: launch as a heavy job after tibia's 'block end' and after the 06:31 pack jobs are queued (FIFO behind them). Accept rule: as noted at 05:35, with 'then'-leading lines at the 5 sites back to the authored count.")
inl note_of_formatter_keep_breaks_43 () = Note ("FORMATTER-KEEP-BREAKS", 43u32, "2026-10-22T09:46Z", "main", "2026-10-22 05:46 main: tibia live-block guard (note 32 rule; the 05:18 cron fired at 05:46). Running main-* jobs: only job-1792662286548-main-tokens-after-block, whose compile window is 'waiting' behind tibia's live-gui hold. It compiles nothing until that window is released, so it is kept; nothing stopped, no window leaked. All of main's other compile and Cells jobs finished before 05:25 (last gate 05:24:35).")
inl note_of_formatter_keep_breaks_44 () = Note ("FORMATTER-KEEP-BREAKS", 44u32, "2026-10-22T11:55Z", "main", "2026-10-22 07:55 main: formatter improvements found from the user's unstaged files and the 120 gate (user 07:3x: check what could be improved in the formatter considering unstaged files). (1) User files: on dice's 5 unstaged files round 5 changes nothing (it matches the user's layout); alphabet documents.spi:143 has a macro continuation whose first line round 5 lays at 121 columns (the user's text is exactly 120). Cause: splitMacroLine merges a whole interpolation hole {!(...)} into one unit (glueHoles) and checks room only after appending, so a hole that crosses the limit overshoots by up to its length. Fix staged: %TEMP%\\fmt-fix\\macro-unit-fit.ps1 (cut before a unit that would overflow; matches round 5's core once). (2) 120 census of round 5's outputs: eoie 1,930 lines over 120, but 1,878 are inside raw-string blobs (embedded Rust in rust_global, correctly never reflowed; healing them is eoie's de-host work), so 52 are real Spiral; lib 65; user 8; dice 1. The formatter's own classifier (format-corpus check on the 28 affected files) puts the real ones in: application (eoie 47), opaque (eoie 41, lib 24, user 4), literal string/macro (needs --split-literals, which plain format already enables), if (eoie 12), binding (eoie 9, lib 1), arm, head. The guard keeps almost none (0 verbatim blocks, 1 parse-guard block in user), so these are layout gaps, not refusals. Round 8 target, the application class: statement-level applications with parenthesized-group arguments are never broken (expect (equals (todo_id (marked ...)) TIBIA) msg at 135 columns; append header (concat5 (gate_text ...) ...) at 275; !!!!Export(name, ((fun ...) : t)) at 124-126; else execute ... (Cargo (arg (arg ...)))). Under the decided rules (long head one part per line, continuation 4 past), lay them out as the head, then one argument per line at +4, recursing into any argument that still exceeds 120; !!!!Op(a, b) breaks after the open parenthesis with one argument per line. Round 7 (queued after tibia's block) = then-on-head + macro-unit-fit; round 8 = application breaking.")
inl note_of_formatter_keep_breaks_45 () = Note ("FORMATTER-KEEP-BREAKS", 45u32, "2026-10-22T11:58Z", "main", "2026-10-22 07:58 main: root cause of the application/if/binding overflows, proven by probe. Two files, the same 135-column expect statement: in a block without raw strings, round 5 lays it out per the rules (expect, then each argument at +4); in a block that also contains a multi-line triple-quoted raw string, the statement stays at 135. flattenBlock (round-5 core, near line 179522) returns Refused (MultilineLiteralOrComment ...) for any block with an Opaque line, so the WHOLE definition is kept as written, code lines included. eoie's host-code blocks almost all hold rust_global raw strings, which is why its 47 application, 12 if and 9 binding overflows live there. Round 8 design: in such a block, segment the body into statements at the body's indentation; a statement whose lines include an Opaque line stays verbatim; every other statement is flattened and laid out alone (layoutBlock at its indent), with the original order and blank lines kept. The parse guard compares shapes of the whole block as before, and a segment that fails its own guard stays as written. Acceptance: the probe's raw-string block lays the expect statement out like the plain block, raw string bodies stay byte-identical, and user/lib/synthetic/dice show no new differing file beyond the intended re-lays. Probe files: %TEMP%\\fmt-rawprobe\\{plain,raw}.spi.")
inl note_of_formatter_keep_breaks_46 () = Note ("FORMATTER-KEEP-BREAKS", 46u32, "2026-10-22T16:13Z", "main", "2026-10-22 12:13 main: next formatter census queued as job-1792685575105-main-fmt-census, at the end of main's chain after the fiscal run. It runs on copies of every unstaged .spi in spiral (42), eoie (65), x (7), tibia (16) and blog (1) (dice and alphabet were covered at 07:55), formatted single-threaded by the PACK-NEXT-2 candidate DLL when its review has built it (else the installed one). It reports per file: changed or not, lines before/after, and over-120 lines left, in %TEMP%\\fmt-unstaged-census\\census.tsv. Reading rule: a change to a user's unstaged file that is not a re-lay of an over-120 line, or not a single-valued habit, is a formatter defect to fix (the memory rule: never join authored lines).")
inl note_of_formatter_keep_breaks_47 () = Note ("FORMATTER-KEEP-BREAKS", 47u32, "2026-10-22T17:02Z", "main", "2026-10-22 13:02 main: PACK-NEXT-2 review, formatter part RED by its own probe. raw-block-segments DUPLICATES code: on fmt-rawprobe\\raw.spi (one block: head, a 135-column expect statement, a raw string, ()) the candidate printed the head, statement and raw string twice and left the 135-column line as is. Root cause, read in the composed core (180002-180120, 181958-182000): the segment path returns one Flattened with several Joined entries but passes the WHOLE block as original. Layout's per-Joined logic (sameParse against shapes(original), fallback kept() = original) assumes one Joined per block, so each laid run fails sameParse against the whole block and falls back to printing the whole original, once per run. Fix design, for its own formatter pack: make segments separate FlatBlocks at flatten level (verbatim parts as Flattened(Kept ..., []); each run as flattenBlock(synthetic head :: run @ synthetic tail), whose original is the run), and check that layout's inter-block blank-line logic inserts nothing between sub-blocks of one definition. Rule applied: a formatter change that can duplicate code never ships, so raw-block-segments is dropped and PACK-NEXT-3 = PACK-NEXT-2 minus raw-block-segments plus WGSL w64. Export checks were green (0 failed). The PACK-NEXT-2 matrix review keeps running and still evaluates the codegen parts.")
inl note_of_formatter_keep_breaks_48 () = Note ("FORMATTER-KEEP-BREAKS", 48u32, "2026-10-22T17:07Z", "main", "2026-10-22 13:07 main: raw-block-segments v2 staged: %TEMP%\\fmt-fix\\raw-block-segments-v2.ps1, 4 anchors, each matching once on the PACK-NEXT-3 core. segmentBlocks returns separate FlatBlocks: the leading lines and head kept verbatim, every statement holding an Opaque line kept verbatim, and each run of other statements laid out by flattenBlock under the synthetic head and tail, so its original is the run itself, layout's sameParse and kept() fallback stay per run, and duplication is impossible by construction. flatten uses List.collect flattenBlocks. Layout joins blocks only with authored blank lines (pendingBlanks + leadingBlanks), so nothing is inserted between the sub-blocks of one definition, and synthetic lines are filtered as in v1. Risk to watch in its gate: G7-2 (the format of flatten's text) sees the synthetic text inside a run's Joined line. Gate: the raw probe must lay out like plain.spi with the raw string byte-identical, plus the formatter candidate gate and G1 idempotence. It rides a formatter pack after PACK-NEXT-3.")
inl note_of_formatter_keep_breaks_49 () = Note ("FORMATTER-KEEP-BREAKS", 49u32, "2026-10-22T18:14Z", "main", "2026-10-22 14:14 main: lab/FORMATTER-KEEP-BREAKS deleted by the 01:30 rule (deleted when PACK-AFTER-NIGHT installs; it installed 09:31). Rechecked first: the 8 synthetic cases and extract-corpus.ps1 are byte-identical in apps/compiler/tmp/formatter. In the tree core: else-if-chain, prune-walk and tuple-if (every New line), guard-dump (35 of 36), lazy-expected, skip-unchanged-respace, and keep-breaks (authoredBreaks). stage-timings was never meant to land (a diagnostic, numbers in the 08:21 note). The runners and classifiers have their results in notes.")
inl note_of_formatter_keep_breaks_50 () = Note ("FORMATTER-KEEP-BREAKS", 50u32, "2026-10-22T20:13Z", "main", "2026-10-22 16:13 main: raw-block-segments v2 joins PACK-NEXT-4 (all 4 anchors match the next3 core). %TEMP%\\pack-next4\\compose.ps1 applies it after the output-size fixes, and pack-next4\\review.ps1 runs the same raw probe and formatter candidate gate as next3 before the matrix. Rule (unchanged from the 13:07 note): raw.spi lays out like plain.spi with raw strings byte-identical, 0 synthetic lines anywhere, and the candidate gate shows 0 files differing beyond parentheses and whitespace. v2 duplicates nothing by construction (each run's original is the run itself).")
inl note_of_formatter_keep_breaks_51 () = Note ("FORMATTER-KEEP-BREAKS", 51u32, "2026-10-22T21:04Z", "main", "2026-10-22 17:04 main: unstaged-files census, run on the next3 candidate formatter (scratch fmt-unstaged-census.ps1, single-threaded, on copies). 129 unstaged .spi in spiral, eoie, x, tibia and blog; 67 change. Every over-120 line is re-laid (over-120 after = 0) except eoie command_spec_domain/model.spi (28 left, the raw-string class that raw-segments v2 in PACK-NEXT-4 targets). The hunk-level check (scratch census_relay_check.py: hunks touching only lines <= 120) found 13 files and 110 lines of three kinds. (a) Redundant parentheses dropped (else (a, b) -> else a, b; (fun ... =>) -> fun ... =>; (Rule (r, rest), next)): a single-valued habit, allowed. (b) else + if -> elif in eoie windows.spi: the documented re-laid-chain rule, allowed. (c) DEFECT: lib sm'.spi's backslash-continued macro groups (Gleam, Python and Lean arms of index_of, plus Lua split) are joined into single lines over 120 (0 over-120 before, 3 after) or re-broken at new points. Not a next3 regression: the installed formatter does the same (A/B on sm'.spi: 3 over-120 and 9 continuations either way). Cause: joinMacroContinuations joins every continuation group so long literals can be re-split, which breaks keep-breaks whenever the authored lines fit. Fix staged in PACK-NEXT-4 (%TEMP%\\pack-next4\\macro-continuations-kept.ps1, 1 anchor): a group is joined only if one of its authored lines is over 120, otherwise it is emitted as written. Rule: sm'.spi formats with 0 over-120 lines and all 12 continuation lines unchanged, plus the usual gate.")
inl note_of_formatter_keep_breaks_52 () = Note ("FORMATTER-KEEP-BREAKS", 52u32, "2026-10-22T22:56Z", "main", "2026-10-22 18:56 main: a keep-breaks regression slipped past the gate's automatic part. macro-unit-fit (pack next3b) made a joined head plus macro layout fit by re-splitting the macro with a backslash continuation, so the layout chooser joined 10 authored head/body breaks whose lines all fit 120. The squeeze comparison ignores continuations, so it passed. Fixed in the tool: tmp/formatter/candidate-gate.ps1 Get-JoinedAuthoredLines fails the automatic part when a removed authored line (all hunk lines <=120, a leading 'then' excepted for then-on-head) reappears mid-line in the candidate. Validated: 10 on next3b's diffs, the same set read by hand. macro-unit-fit is dropped from PACK-NEXT-4. Re-adding it needs a guard: take the joined layout only when an authored line exceeds 120.")
inl note_of_formatter_keep_breaks_53 () = Note ("FORMATTER-KEEP-BREAKS", 53u32, "2026-10-23T01:58Z", "main", "2026-10-22 21:58 main: two corrections from next5's gate. (1) The join check measured installed output against candidate output, but 'authored' means the source. On eoie's corpus it flagged 35 joins, and 34 were lines the INSTALLED formatter had written while re-laying an over-120 source line (agile_drops at 167 chars, from_title_with at 140). candidate-gate.ps1 now counts a flag only when the joined line exists, whitespace-collapsed, in that corpus's source file. On next5 that leaves 1 real join. The exit clause also accepts a candidate exit of 0 where the installed formatter exited 2 (an eoie file the installed one errors on). (2) The real join was chain-fill re-laying a whole statement whose authored lines were 'sm.concat' / '(label Target)' / one 188-char argument. Keep-breaks re-lays only the overflowing line, so chain-fill now fires only on statements written on one line (no lexeme after the first in authoredBreaks): PACK-NEXT-5b, core 8490E4C7AAA428D0 over next4's E1F6B0543BC3A1B2. The working-file apply script also diffs each authored copy against its formatted copy and holds any file whose formatting joins an authored line.")
inl note_of_formatter_keep_breaks_54 () = Note ("FORMATTER-KEEP-BREAKS", 54u32, "2026-10-23T06:25Z", "main", "2026-10-23 02:25 main: next5d review (job-1792729171377) passed its rule. Gate automatic part PASS (user, lib, synthetic and dice at 0 differences beyond parentheses/whitespace; 33 flagged joins, 0 of them authored). Unstaged copies (349 files), as written / installed / candidate: ThenLeading 323 / 421 / 162; Deep28 801 / 1,307 / 972; Over120 2,197 / 147 / 142; JoinParenLines 21 / 21 / 14. Hand read of the 3 most-changed files (fiscal generator.spi, lib xsd_emit.spi, fiscal generated/tipos_basico_enums.spi): every hunk is a then-on-condition move or a re-lay of a line over 120, with no authored fitting line joined. One quality follow-up: a list with a line over 120 is re-laid one item per line (the keyword list in xsd_emit and the generator: 2 lines become 27), where filling items up to 120 would keep it at 3 lines; the installed formatter does the same. Applied (apply-format.ps1, hash-checked against the copies) to dice lib/dice.spi and 6 fiscal files; backups in pack-next5d\\applied-backup-20261023-022522. Held by rule: alphabet apps/documents/documents.spi (staged by the user), eoie (release F8 in flight), spiral (F8 reads it; lib also waits for the lib landing guards), tibia (its block and agent jobs are live). x and blog had nothing left to change.")
inl note_of_formatter_keep_breaks_55 () = Note ("FORMATTER-KEEP-BREAKS", 55u32, "2026-10-23T09:11Z", "main", "2026-10-23 05:11 main: second apply, after F9 ended at 05:08: 170 working files reformatted (spiral 99, eoie 71) by the next5d-reviewed formatter. 89 were already formatted. 52 staged files were held (all 707 staged .spi in spiral/eoie are excluded at run time), and 14 that changed since the review copy were left alone (e.g. spiral lib/spiral/rust/near.spi from today's landing). 0 were held for joined authored lines. Backups: %TEMP%\\pack-next5d\\applied-backup-20261023-050837. eoie was told about its 71 files. Still held: alphabet apps/documents/documents.spi (staged). Not done until installed: the formatter in the composite (with the 174x shape cache) installs after its matrix review (job-1792746570235).")
inl note_of_formatter_keep_breaks_56 () = Note ("FORMATTER-KEEP-BREAKS", 56u32, "2026-10-23T09:24Z", "main", "2026-10-23 05:24 main: side effect of the 05:08 apply. 21 of the 71 eoie files it rewrote are targets of eoie's queued GE2..K slice chain (17 beyond whitespace), and slice G's first anchor missed. eoie rebases: chain on the backup originals, then the same next5d formatter DLL (entry command sent). Rule added to memory (reformat-peer-repos-ask-first): before an apply reaches another lane's repo, ask that lane for pending anchored chains and hold those files; a lane mid-release is held by default.")
inl note_of_formatter_keep_breaks_57 () = Note ("FORMATTER-KEEP-BREAKS", 57u32, "2026-10-23T09:37Z", "main", "2026-10-23 05:37 main: the review's raw probe caught a real gap. raw.spi kept its 135-wide expect line (plain.spi relays it to 3 lines), so the rule 'raw.spi lays out like plain.spi' failed on the composite, next5d and the installed formatter alike: raw-block-segments v2 never segmented it. Cause, traced with fsi on the composite's own lexer: segmentBlocks groups statements by Code(4) lines only, but a line that opens a raw string (inl _ = text followed by triple quotes) is lexed whole as Opaque (Verbatim ...). So the expect line and the raw string became one statement, and the whole statement was kept verbatim. Fix fmt-fix\\raw-statement-boundary.ps1: an Opaque line whose text starts at indent 4 and follows a non-Opaque line also opens a statement (lines inside the raw string follow Opaque lines and never split). Grouping check on the composite's lexed lines: [[2]; [3;4;5]; [6]], so expect is laid out, the raw string is kept verbatim, and () is laid out. Composed into pack-next5d after the shape cache; composite 67EBF80563E2239A parses under both defines. The in-flight review of 7FCC891349EB1608 was stopped newest-first (it would have failed this rule), and the chain restarted at 05:3x: job-1792748200944-main-pack-next4-review, then cells-pack, then hopacsota. install.ps1 now expects 67EBF80563E2239A.")
inl note_of_formatter_keep_breaks_58 () = Note ("FORMATTER-KEEP-BREAKS", 58u32, "2026-10-23T09:39Z", "main", "2026-10-23 05:39 main: scope check of raw-statement-boundary with an old/new grouping diff in fsi (rawprobe-ab\\grouping-diff.fsx, the composite's own lexer). raw.spi: 1 block changes (2 -> 3 statements). eoie src/command_spec_domain/model.spi: also exactly 1 block changes (line 119: statement 121 now separates from the multi-line macro string at 122-141). Its other over-120 lines (56 in the working copy: union arms like '| StatusSpec => { ... }' and ActionableDiagnostic rows) are not raw-adjacent and are untouched by this fix. They are a separate class (long one-line arms and records); the 21:04 census's 'raw-string class' label for that file was too broad.")
inl note_of_formatter_keep_breaks_59 () = Note ("FORMATTER-KEEP-BREAKS", 59u32, "2026-10-23T09:40Z", "main", "2026-10-23 05:40 main: the residue in eoie model.spi is explained. After the next5d formatter, the file keeps 28 lines over 120, and those shown sit inside one multi-line string literal (the !!!!StaticStringConcat usage text, lines 157-170+, up to 1,042 chars). That is string content a formatter must not break. So over-120 after formatting = 0 outside string content for this file, and the census's 'raw-string class' there is unbreakable by design. Splitting such text is a source edit for eoie (one literal per verb, joined with ^^), not formatter work.")
inl note_of_formatter_keep_breaks_60 () = Note ("FORMATTER-KEEP-BREAKS", 60u32, "2026-10-23T09:46Z", "main", "2026-10-23 05:46 main: composite 67EBF80563E2239A review (job-1792748200944): raw probe now PASSES its rule. raw.spi widest line 96 = plain.spi 96, 0 synthetic lines (it was 135 vs 96 before raw-statement-boundary); macro continuations kept 12 of 12, sm'.spi over-120 0. User corpus gate: 0 files differing beyond parentheses and whitespace.")
inl note_of_formatter_keep_breaks_61 () = Note ("FORMATTER-KEEP-BREAKS", 61u32, "2026-10-23T17:51Z", "main", "2026-10-23 13:51 main: the user's 'indentation still busted' has a second source the 5e gate did not flag: 5e formats alphabet's HEAD documents.spi 'let run { ... } : async.future_pin (' head (every line fits 120, compact nesting with ( at line ends) into an 18-line ( staircase, the same text the user's staged index holds. Cause: asWrittenStatement keeps or rejects a whole statement, so one over-120 body line re-lays the entire definition; body statements are re-checked one by one, but the head always went to layoutHead (or was joined onto one line when it fit), and layoutHead's typeLines builds the staircase. Found by a new census (scratch fitting_lines_kept.py: authored lines that fit 120 but are not kept verbatim: user 26/11,982, lib 76/16,771, dice 6/1,551, eoie 71/30,387 on the 5e gate outputs). Fix fmt-fix\\head-as-written.ps1 in 5f: a re-laid definition first tries asWrittenStatement on its head alone (first token through =), and keeps it when every head line fits; only then the one-line join or layoutHead. Regression sample apps/compiler/tmp/formatter/synthetic/head_as_written.spi (a fitting multi-line head plus an over-120 body line): 5e splits its authored first line. 5f core 00DBC77879CA05E3, next9 E1C4E733D367C7D1.")
inl note_of_formatter_keep_breaks_62 () = Note ("FORMATTER-KEEP-BREAKS", 62u32, "2026-10-23T19:49Z", "main", "2026-10-23 15:49 main: INSTALLED and verified live (installed-is-done). pack-fmt-only (the tree core with 5f's Formatter section, 19F5F752485517B1) installed at 15:47 through install.ps1 -Apply in a compiler-install window: single-flight 171.7 s and hopacsota 160.2 s rebuilt, the installed single-flight core DC4F4DE91BA4D292 equals the gated candidate. verify-live.ps1 with the installed compiler: all 6 user files the 07:0x complaint named (dice lib/dice.spi, ui content/menu_tabs/nearblocks/rpc, alphabet documents.spi) are canonical (the installed formatter leaves them unchanged), the complaints probe finds 0 parenthesized join heads and 0 off-grid lines, and each file's leading-then count equals HEAD's (7, 1, 0, 0, 0, 22). The working-tree changes are the user's to stage: alphabet documents.spi (the ( staircase gone, against the staged copy) and dice ui rpc.spi (comma breaks).")
inl note_of_formatter_keep_breaks_63 () = Note ("FORMATTER-KEEP-BREAKS", 63u32, "2026-10-23T20:43Z", "main", "2026-10-23 16:43 main: cause of the one open dice case found by bisect (installed formatter 19F5F752). The user's hand-split nearblocks.spi txn_box head keeps its 178-column record-pattern line because the definition's BODY ends in a macro string with authored backslash continuations: the keep-authored-continuations path keeps the whole statement as written, head included. Repros in scratch nb-min: d.spi = the real 14-line txn_box block (unchanged by format), e.spi = the same block with the macro statement replaced by 'receipt_id' (the record pattern re-lays one field per line); a.spi/b.spi/c.spi (split or one-line heads, plain bodies) all re-lay. Fix direction: when a statement is kept for its authored continuations, still re-lay its head lines that exceed 120 (the head-as-written rule already covers fitting heads).")
inl note_of_formatter_keep_breaks_64 () = Note ("FORMATTER-KEEP-BREAKS", 64u32, "2026-10-23T20:47Z", "main", "2026-10-23 16:47 main: narrowing the nearblocks case: it is not macro-continuations-kept's rejoin (that pre-pass only joins backslash-continued macro lines for lexing and restores the authored lines when they fit). Suspect: the rejoined text versus the original line table in asWrittenStatement (tokenLines/sourceLineTokens come from the authored lines, so a statement holding a rejoined macro may resolve to the authored text whole), which would keep the 178-column head too. Next step for the next round: a Debug formatter build with a trace on asWrittenStatement and layoutHead, run on scratch nb-min d.spi (kept) and e.spi (re-laid), and compare the decision per statement.")
inl note_of_formatter_keep_breaks_65 () = Note ("FORMATTER-KEEP-BREAKS", 65u32, "2026-10-23T22:52Z", "main", "2026-10-23 18:52 main: cause of eoie registry.spi crashing both formatters (Substring -1) found statically. registry.spi is a generated ~100-level nested SpiralTestNotebook chain; two lines are 1,508 and 4,390 columns wide, at indents growing +4 per level. Re-laid, the nesting passes column 122, where the literal splitter's room = 120 - indent - 6 goes to -8 or less (spiral_compiler.fs:180447). Then lastNewline (-1) > room / 4 (-2 or less) holds and cut = -1, so text.Substring (0, -1) throws. The macro splitter's roomFor (180524) has the same failure below room -4 (lastSpace -1 > room / 2). Fix: %TEMP%\\fmt-fix\\deep-indent-room.ps1 floors both rooms at 40 columns (max 40 (...)), so every cut lands past column 20. A deep line stays over 120, which nesting that deep can't avoid, but it no longer crashes or shreds the literal into 1-character pieces. It anchors once on the tree and the 5f cores and is added to pack-next5f\\compose.ps1 after head-as-written. The candidate gate's eoie src corpus contains registry.spi, so it is the regression check. After tibia's block, requeue-after-block.ps1 runs recompose-stack (5f -> next9, install.ps1 Composite) and then review-3.")
inl note_of_formatter_keep_breaks_66 () = Note ("FORMATTER-KEEP-BREAKS", 66u32, "2026-10-23T22:55Z", "main", "2026-10-23 18:55 main: nearblocks' 178-column head (scratch nb-min d.spi: a record-pattern head and a body ending in a \\-continued macro string). Where it is kept, from the code: layout (spiral_compiler.fs:181961) keeps a Joined block as written in three cases: AsWritten when it fits (not this one, 178 > 120), the parse-guard fallback (chosen None, then retried None; reason parse-guard), or a refusal (comments-inline, comments-misplaced or not-one-code-line). The leaf retry (181991) re-lays one leaf span at a time and accepts only the same shapes and parenthesis count. If the macro leaf fails it, the head leaf should still be accepted, unless the head's span is not a leaf of its own. That needs one run: scratch nb-min\\probe.ps1 runs format-corpus format on d.spi with --list (the kept-verbatim reason) and SPIRAL_FORMAT_GUARD_DUMP (original, candidate and both shape lists). One compiler process, seconds; it runs after tibia's block. Rule: parse-guard with a macro shape difference -> fix the macro continuation so it round-trips (the head then follows); not-one-code-line -> the flatten of \\-continued macros is the cause and the fix goes in flatten.")
inl note_of_formatter_keep_breaks_67 () = Note ("FORMATTER-KEEP-BREAKS", 67u32, "2026-10-23T23:17Z", "main", "2026-10-23 19:16 main: the installed formatter-only gate (job-1792781232696) showed more fitting authored lines not kept on lib (76 vs 69) and user (26 vs 24). A per-line diff over the kept gate outputs (spiral-bin\\candidates\\formatter-gate-fmt-only, scratch fitting_diff.py: lines that fit, lost by the candidate only) shows all 9 extra lines are '(join ...)' lines whose parentheses the candidate drops on purpose (the user's first complaint): 7 in date_time.spi and 2 in math.spi. No other fitting line is lost by the candidate only, so there is no keep-breaks regression.")
inl note_of_formatter_keep_breaks_68 () = Note ("FORMATTER-KEEP-BREAKS", 68u32, "2026-10-24T00:37Z", "main", "2026-10-23 20:37 main: nearblocks' 178-column head, cause found by the probe (job-1792801893843: no kept-verbatim reason, no guard dump, so it is neither the parse guard nor a refusal) plus a code read. segmentBlocks (spiral_compiler.fs:179935) splits a definition whose body holds Opaque lines (the \\-continued macro) into segments and returns kept (leading @ head) for the head: emitted verbatim whatever its width. Only the body's statement runs go through flattenBlock. Fix staged: %TEMP%\\fmt-fix\\segment-head-relaid.ps1 (1 anchor, matches once on the tree and the 5f core). When a head line is over 120, the head is flattened on its own with the synthetic segmentTail body line, which layout already filters out (182117), so it gets the normal head re-lay, including the record-pattern one-field-per-line form. A head that fits stays kept as written. Not added to 5f now: recompose-stack (job-1792801905910) is composing 5f for review-3, and changing it mid-review would desync install.ps1's composite hash. It rides the next formatter pack after the 5f install; check: scratch nb-min probe.ps1 shows the head split and 0 lines over 120 in the output.")
inl note_of_formatter_keep_breaks_69 () = Note ("FORMATTER-KEEP-BREAKS", 69u32, "2026-10-24T00:40Z", "main", "2026-10-23 20:40 main: the stack is recomposed with the deep-indent fix (job-1792801905910): 5f 0FA0E87BD22F75DD (install.ps1 expects it), then next7 97869060, next8 F90364EA and next9 FA6DF375, each with lint G2 0 and pair-drift clean. review-3 (job-1792801906630) is now reviewing the 5f composite. Install follows a green verdict, then a live check on the user's files and on eoie registry.spi, which must no longer crash.")
inl note_of_formatter_keep_breaks_70 () = Note ("FORMATTER-KEEP-BREAKS", 70u32, "2026-10-24T02:26Z", "main", "2026-10-23 22:26 main: formatter-only pack INSTALLED and verified live (jobs main-fmt-only-gate exit 0, main-fmt-only-install exit 0; compiler B553EF6B78E6EE29 in spiral-bin single-flight): 6 of 6 probe files (dice lib/dice.spi, content, menu_tabs, nearblocks, rpc; alphabet documents.spi) canonical under the installed formatter, 0 parenthesized join heads, 0 off-grid lines, then placement equals each file's HEAD habit (dice 7/7 leading, documents 22/22 leading). Carries deep-indent-room (room = max 40). segment-head-relaid stays staged for the next formatter pack.")
inl note_of_formatter_keep_breaks_71 () = Note ("FORMATTER-KEEP-BREAKS", 71u32, "2026-10-25T00:00Z", "main", "2026-10-24 20:00 main groom: two things left, both after pack 5i. (1) The staged segment-head re-lay is in 5h's base, so it ships with 5i, whose install steps are in COMPILER-DEAD-ARM-GLOBALS (now this item's dep). (2) The title's last step, redoing the destructive run from HEAD, needs the 5i formatter installed first. That run must be coordinated with the edition-3 lib land (EDITION3-SYMBOLS-MIGRATION), because both rewrite every lib file: respell first, then reformat, and ask the peer teams about pending anchored chains per the reformat rule.")
inl note_of_formatter_keep_breaks_72 () = Note ("FORMATTER-KEEP-BREAKS", 72u32, "2026-10-25T21:51Z", "main", "10-25 17:51 main: two formatter questions from x (X-FORMAT-CLIENT), decided by rules on record. (A) BUG, to fix in the next pack: the type-record rule near spiral_compiler.fs:181798 re-lays a record type's body even when every authored line fits 120. That violates the 10-20 decision (n1): a block with no code line over 120 is left as written, and authored breaks are kept. Fix: route a fitting authored record type through the keep-as-written path; fields.spi command_fields then keeps its 2 authored lines. (B) REJECTED as the default layout: x's record FILL (pack consecutive simple fields onto one line with '; ' within 120). F2/F5 require a template the user wrote, and the measured corpus has multi-line records one field per line at +4, no ';' (224/0, SPIRAL-FORMATTER n83). With (A) fixed, x keeps any compact record it authors, which covers its 280-weight cases at the source. Records re-laid because they're over 120 keep the one-field-per-line template. x's patch (scratchpad record-fill\\apply.ps1: overweight 28 -> 22 client, 8 -> 5 sim) is kept as evidence, not landed.")
inl note_of_formatter_keep_breaks_73 () = Note ("FORMATTER-KEEP-BREAKS", 73u32, "2026-10-25T21:54Z", "main", "10-25 17:54 main: CORRECTION to n72 (A). The type-record rule at spiral_compiler.fs:181798 (a record type is laid out one field per line even when it fits, via the disjunct isKeyword SpecType && the body is a single record) is BY DESIGN, not a bug. n1's implemented decision keeps single-valued habits canonical and names records explicitly ('spacing, +4, records, match arms, operator alignment'), and multi-line records are 224/0 in the user corpus. Authored breaks are kept only where the habit is mixed. Traced before drafting: I had read n1 as 'all fitting authored lines stay', which drops the canonical-habit clause. (B) stands, for the same reason. x is told: a record type is always one field per line, so an x definition over 280 shrinks structurally (split or nest the record type, shorten names); keep-breaks still applies to record values and expressions. No formatter change.")
inl note_of_formatter_keep_breaks_74 () = Note ("FORMATTER-KEEP-BREAKS", 74u32, "2026-10-30T01:59Z", "main", "10-29 21:59 main, groom (WIP rule, as in the 10-23 and 10-27 grooms): Active -> Paused, progress kept at 95%. No note since 10-25 and no live job; main carries 69 Actives, which is not real WIP. Resume when picked; if its pass already shipped in an installed core, close it Done with that evidence instead. Compiler/lib work: per lane-roles, the team that needs it (likely the compiler team, git-a6) resumes it.")
inl note_of_formatter_keep_breaks_75 () = Note ("FORMATTER-KEEP-BREAKS", 75u32, "2026-10-30T14:12Z", "main", "10-30 10:12 main, BACKLOG-DANGLING-1030 R2/R4: deps Done, owner team compiler ('agile next --team compiler' hands it out), assignee agent (nothing in it is the user's). Resume when that team picks it up; the pack-7 install it waited on is done.")
inl note_of_formatter_keep_breaks_76 () = Note ("FORMATTER-KEEP-BREAKS", 76u32, "2026-10-30T23:44Z", "compiler", "10-30 19:44 compiler, BACKLOG-DANGLING-1030 R2 hygiene: deps now include LIB-EDITION3-ROUND, so the census stops flagging this item DEPS-DONE. Its last step (the destructive HEAD redo) is sequenced after the lib respell (n71: respell first, then reformat, both rewrite every lib file) and needs the ask-peer-teams step for pending anchored chains (memory reformat-peer-repos-ask-first). The compiler-samples respell already landed today (EDITION3-SYMBOLS-MIGRATION n41), but that doesn't unblock this one. Unblocks when LIB-EDITION3-ROUND is Done.")
inl note_of_formatter_keep_breaks_77 () = Note ("FORMATTER-KEEP-BREAKS", 77u32, "2026-10-31T20:29Z", "compiler", "10-31 10-31 16:29 compiler, pause re-decision (KIT-PAUSE-RULE-REWORK: no Paused without a reason and a resume condition): Paused -> BLOCKED. Reason: the last step (the destructive HEAD redo that reformats every lib file) must follow the lib edition-3 respell, and peer teams must be asked about pending anchored chains first (memory reformat-peer-repos-ask-first). RESUME ON: LIB-EDITION3-ROUND Done (already a dep).")
inl wgsl_test_status_ignored () = Task ("WGSL-TEST-STATUS-IGNORED", "2026-10-20 10:07 Cells ///> wgsl tests cannot fail: a test whose i32 status is 1 passes (minimal repro: test must_fail_on_gpu = inl ~x = 1i32 / if x = 2i32 then 0i32 else 1i32, run_notebook --no-spi, with tibia's and the default spiral-wgsl runner). Cells README:243 says the runner exits with main's status and wgsl-run main.rs:38 exits with results[0], so the status is lost in codegen or in the cell wrapper. Found by tibia: gpu_sim's GPU twin pinned to 21 kills passed after its C oracle moved to 22, and also when pinned to 99. Every GPU twin (~25 in tibia) is unchecked until fixed; after the fix rerun all tibia wgsl tests.", TaskKind, P1, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "wgsl,cells,testing,tibia", "current", "current")
inl cells_wgsl_test_status () = Task ("CELLS-WGSL-TEST-STATUS", "Cells ///> wgsl tests always pass: a test returning 1 reports passed", Story, P1, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "compiler,cells,wgsl,correctness", "current", "current")
inl cells_ets_owner () = Task ("CELLS-ETS-OWNER", "Cells ETS tables outlive the process that first touches them (x sim race: 'table identifier does not refer to an existing ETS table' under concurrent Notebook.evaluate)", TaskKind, P1, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "cells,ets,race,concurrency", "current", "current")
inl heavy_gate_holders () = Task ("HEAVY-GATE-HOLDERS", "Heavy gate: a hung slot holder can't starve the queue (holder files, named holders in waits, MaxMinutes watchdog, ticket adoption for eoie supervisors)", TaskKind, P1, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "heavy-gate,coordination,lanes", "current", "current")
inl eoie_no_shell_spawn () = Task ("EOIE-NO-SHELL-SPAWN", "eoie binaries spawn programs directly, never through pwsh or cmd (startup cost, extra pwsh/conhost per job, processes outside the job tree); first scope: the eoie bins only, the repo's .ps1 drivers come with the later full pwsh rewrite", Story, P1, D4, 100u32, Done, "", "eoie,processes,performance,jobs", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_eoie_no_shell_spawn () = Planning ("EOIE-NO-SHELL-SPAWN", Minutes 180u32, Load Light, Unassigned)
inl eoie_style_ratchet () = Task ("EOIE-STYLE-RATCHET", "eoie style ratchet: a per-package census in eoie standards (typefeatureset use: GADT cases, existentials, foralls, indexed unions, prototypes, phantom types; drift: host-macro density and width, rust_global, tab/newline-encoded rows, pipe tables, number-coded arms, concat5 string assembly, string-as-result errors) with a committed baseline; the release gate fails when a touched package's drift rises or its typefeatureset use falls", Story, P1, D4, 95u32, Blocked, "EOIE-STANDARDS-CHECKS", "kit,standards,typefeatureset,style,wipe-redo,team:kit,kit", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_eoie_style_ratchet () = Planning ("EOIE-STYLE-RATCHET", Minutes 240u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_eoie_style_ratchet_1 () = Note ("EOIE-STYLE-RATCHET", 1u32, "2026-10-20T23:58Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-20 19:58 eoie lane, user 2026-10-20 ~19:45: 'all these fixes and advancements should follow the most recent standardizations, ways to use tests, like x and tibia, and how to domain model in the 5 mega samples... rate 0-1000... find ways to enforce this style change in the future so we stop drifting the rust_global and indexes and these python like behaviors, the typefeatureset should be the priority somehow'. Census per 1000 lines (scratchpad style-census.ps1, to move to lab/EOIE-STYLE-RATCHET): mega samples GADT cases 16, existentials 8.1, forall 35, host macros 2.1, macros >200 chars 0, encoded rows 0, concat5 0; tibia GADT 2.8, prototypes 1.5, tests 26, host macros 0; x tests 30, records 24, host macros 16; eoie overall GADT 14.6 (mostly agile/patch domains), host macros 30.5, >200-char macros 4.4, rust_global 8.9, encoded rows 3, number arms 6.2, concat5 18.2; the eoie lane's new crates of 10-19/20 (windows, experiments x3, table logs/diff, window run, job gate/control, standards, memory links, task next, todo record/roots; 3k lines): GADT 0, existentials 0, forall 0, nominal 0, prototypes 0, tests 19.4, host macros 55.9, >200-char macros 10.4, encoded rows 11, concat5 62.5, rust_global 0. Self-rating 200/1000: new behavior was tested (CLI notebooks, mutation checks) and avoided rust_global, but the modeling was string-typed (tab-separated window rows, key=value ledger files, 'kind:col' option lists, newline-joined lists, '' as the success value of error strings) and used none of the typefeatureset, and host macros grew instead of shrinking. Enforcement plan: (1) eoie standards rows for MacroWidth 120, NoEncodedRows, NoPipeTables, NoNumberArms, NoRustGlobal, a concat budget, and a TypeFeatureFloor for new packages; (2) a committed per-package baseline (ratchet) checked by the release script on touched packages; (3) a design step before code: name the indexed unions/GADTs, existentials and records the module needs, citing the tibia or mega module it follows; (4) results as unions (Ok/Rejected payloads) instead of '' sentinels; records and typed lists instead of joined strings; host work only as one-expression primitives. Cheap backups for destructive iteration: scratchpad eoie-snapshot.ps1 (1.3 MB zip of 1,199 source files per snapshot).")
inl note_of_eoie_style_ratchet_2 () = Note ("EOIE-STYLE-RATCHET", 2u32, "2026-10-21T01:54Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-20 21:54 eoie team groom for the 10-20/21 night (to 07:00): night slice (3rd): eoie standards --census <package-dir>... prints the per-package counts of the 10-20 census (typefeatureset: GADT cases, existentials, foralls, indexed unions, prototypes; drift: host-macro density/width, rust_global, encoded rows, pipe tables, number arms, concat5) with the probes as a typed probe union; a committed baseline file (one row per package); --ratchet <baseline> exits 1 when a package got worse; the release script runs it on packages touched since the last release. The module itself is the first one written to the new standard. Remaining estimate 240 min.")
inl note_of_eoie_style_ratchet_3 () = Note ("EOIE-STYLE-RATCHET", 3u32, "2026-10-21T03:32Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-20 23:32 eoie team, census lesson from the eoie_cli_model prune (UNION-REFLECTION night slice): the census counts GADT cases, existentials and prototypes whether or not anything uses them, and eoie_cli_model's 9-case eoie_op GADT, interpreters and eoie_plan chain were dead decoration from the GPT era. Pruning them lowers that package's feature counts, so the ratchet's may-not-fall rule flags a cleanup as a regression. Fix to make before the ratchet gates releases: count a feature only where its type is referenced outside its own declaration (a second occurrence of the union or case name in the corpus), or let a feature count fall when the package's lines fall by at least as much; until then a prune comes with a deliberate baseline rewrite named in the release note.")
inl note_of_eoie_style_ratchet_4 () = Note ("EOIE-STYLE-RATCHET", 4u32, "2026-10-21T03:38Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-20 23:38 eoie team, prune rule drafted for release 26 (scratchpad apply-census-prune.ps1): a may-not-fall probe now regresses only when its rate falls beyond tolerance AND the count fell by more than the package's lines fell (before - now > before_lines - now_lines), so deleting dead feature code together with its lines is a prune (eoie_cli_model 11 GADT lines out of 179 -> 0 out of 129 passes) while rewriting features into plain code at the same size still fails; 2 new probe test lines. Considered and rejected: counting a feature only where its name is referenced elsewhere (eoie_cli_model's dead GADT cases are referenced by equally dead interpreter instances and plan values, so reference counts cannot see dead chains; real reachability needs a compiler unused-definition report, and generated-Rust presence misses compile-time-only unions such as probe side). Release 26 runs the ratchet report-only and rewrites standards/census.spi after install; the new-package rule will flag agile_task_query (plain query unions, no typefeatureset), accepted for this release and to revisit: a small package of plain domain unions may be the right shape.")
inl note_of_eoie_style_ratchet_5 () = Note ("EOIE-STYLE-RATCHET", 5u32, "2026-10-21T06:18Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-21 02:18 rule written before r26's report exists: r26 runs the ratchet report-only, then writes the baseline after install. r27's build runs it against that baseline. If r27's report lists 0 regressions, the next release turns it into a gate (a regression blocks publish, like the notebook suite). If it lists any, each one is either fixed in r27's tree or recorded here as an accepted move (package, probe, reason) before the gate turns on. The new-package rule counts too: tonight's new crates eoie_command_line (launch GADT) and agile_task_query (value-indexed flag GADT) both carry the typefeatureset.")
inl note_of_eoie_style_ratchet_6 () = Note ("EOIE-STYLE-RATCHET", 6u32, "2026-10-21T06:26Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-21 02:26 perf: standards --baseline over src (352 packages) takes about 2.5 min in the debug build, because matching_lines compiles each probe regex once per package (13 probes x 352) and every package's files are concatenated first. Fix later: compile the 13 probe patterns once and count line by line per file. Report-only use in the release job tolerates it for now.")
inl note_of_eoie_style_ratchet_7 () = Note ("EOIE-STYLE-RATCHET", 7u32, "2026-10-21T06:27Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-21 02:27 preview: ratchet of the r26+r27-draft tree against an r25 baseline (debug binary, without r26's prune rule) lists 7 packages. eoie_cli_model is the dead-GADT prune (exempt under r26's rule: 23 feature lines gone, 34 lines gone). host-macro rises in agile_memory_links 23->30, agile_planning_model 4->5, agile_task_next 13->20, agile_todo_scan 13->15, eoie_agile_contracts 1->4: these are the short typed host primitives that replaced wide macros or untyped codes, which the probe counts the same as drift. agile_task_next string-concat 12->19 and indexed-union dilution (2/350 -> 2/431) come from the query crate wrappers. Proposal before gating: host-macro counts only macros that are not a whole one-line typed primitive (inl/let name ... = \\$'...' : T under 160 chars), or wide-host-macro alone carries the drift signal. Until then these are accepted moves for r26/r27.")
inl note_of_eoie_style_ratchet_8 () = Note ("EOIE-STYLE-RATCHET", 8u32, "2026-10-21T06:37Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-21 02:37 drafted (scratch apply-census-probe.ps1, applied to the preview tree, compile pending): host-macro counts only indented lines with a host macro (^\\s+.*(\\$'|\\$\\x22)), so one-line typed primitives at column 0 are not drift while host code inside bodies and match arms still is; Rust's regex crate has no lookaround, so the rule is positional. probes.spi notebook gains two expectations. Against r26's old-probe baseline the counts only fall, which a may-not-rise probe does not flag; the next baseline write uses the new probe. Timeline: r25 notebooks run about a minute per file, so r26 lands around 06:00 and r27 (todo reflection plus drafts that pass preview) after the night.")
inl note_of_eoie_style_ratchet_9 () = Note ("EOIE-STYLE-RATCHET", 9u32, "2026-10-21T07:07Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-21 03:07 r25 notebook failure: census_cli 'the_ratchet_holds_old_packages...' expects old host-macro 0/5 -> 20/25 and gets 20/26, because text_of_files joins package files with an extra newline after files that already end in one (each extra file adds a phantom line). The test is right; fix in census.spi (apply-census-lines.ps1: join files as they are and add a newline only when one is missing). Rule set before r25 ends: if this is r25's only failure, the r25 tree plus this fix plus the r26 slices ship as one release through chain-r26 (one heavy cycle instead of two); any other failure gets fixed first.")
inl note_of_eoie_style_ratchet_10 () = Note ("EOIE-STYLE-RATCHET", 10u32, "2026-10-21T08:06Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-21 04:06 ratchet preview of the live combined r26 tree against the r25 baseline (debug binary, before r26's prune rule): 5 packages. eoie_cli_model is the dead-GADT prune (exempt under r26's rule); agile_task_next, agile_planning_model and eoie_agile_contracts are the short typed primitives and query wrappers already accepted (note 7); new: eoie_table_logs encoded-row 4/257 -> 6/340 from the two tab literals that build --values TSV rows. TSV output is the verb's purpose, so the encoded-row probe should skip table writers (or count only separators used to pack values into one string). Accepted move for r26.")
inl note_of_eoie_style_ratchet_11 () = Note ("EOIE-STYLE-RATCHET", 11u32, "2026-10-21T13:13Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-21 09:13 eoie team: the r28 census-probe slice's own notebook test used the regex crate (lines_matching), which plain-rustc notebook tests cannot compile (E0433). Caught by a notebook type check on the composed r28 tree before any release. The test now asserts the rule's shape (the host-macro pattern requires leading whitespace); the rule on real files stays covered end to end by census_cli through the binary. probes.spi passes on the composed tree.")
inl note_of_eoie_style_ratchet_12 () = Note ("EOIE-STYLE-RATCHET", 12u32, "2026-10-21T16:50Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-21 12:50 r25 installed eoie standards --census/--baseline/--ratchet; r26 adds the prune rule and writes the first baseline (standards/census.spi) after install.")
inl note_of_eoie_style_ratchet_13 () = Note ("EOIE-STYLE-RATCHET", 13u32, "2026-10-21T18:14Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-21 14:14 eoie team: the combined r28+r29 release reports 15 ratchet regressions (report-only, so it is not blocked). New packages: eoie_experiment_import, eoie_guide and eoie_memory_model have host macros over 200 characters; eoie_job_after, eoie_job_launch and eoie_table_measures encode rows with tab or record separators; eoie_memory uses no typefeatureset. Worse than baseline: agile_memory_links (pipe-table 1 -> 2), agile_task_notes (host-macro 2 -> 3), eoie_dev_spiral_tests (string-concat 1 -> 2), eoie_job_gate (string-concat 6 -> 7), eoie_jobs (encoded-row 5 -> 7), eoie_machine_windows (encoded-row 7 -> 9), plus agile_task_query's typefeature counts flat while its size grew. These are today's slices' style debt. Rule for the next drafts: a slice previews its own ratchet delta, and a new regression needs a reason in its note or a fix before it ships.")
inl note_of_eoie_style_ratchet_14 () = Note ("EOIE-STYLE-RATCHET", 14u32, "2026-10-21T20:35Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-21 16:35 eoie team: shipped in the combined r28+r29 release, installed 15:38 (one-call spec 34/34, all 67 notebooks green): census probe: host-macro counts only indented lines; the ratchet stays report-only.")
inl note_of_eoie_style_ratchet_15 () = Note ("EOIE-STYLE-RATCHET", 15u32, "2026-10-21T22:55Z", "eoie-lane", "18:55 eoie team, ratchet deltas of tonight's drafts against standards/census.spi (scratch ratchet-trees.ps1; live with r30-r33 has 10 regressions): r34 +16, r35 +7, r36 +2. Fixed now: the row-mask export returned a tab-encoded 'kept<TAB>mask' row; it returns a (bool, string) tuple and analysis/table_test build the rows_kept union from it (recheck chained). Reasons for the rest, to fix before each release ships or accept: (1) host-macro and wide-host-macro in new host crates (eoie_machine_watch sysinfo sampling, eoie_adoption jsonl scan, eoie_grep_query = hits_found moved whole) are OS/IO boundaries; split each wide macro into named primitives in the slice's own follow-up. (2) encoded-row in eoie_machine_windows (+2) and eoie_process_table (+3): window rows are tab files on disk by format, and ps builds a tab table for columns_aligned; the ps one should become a typed row list rendered once. (3) eoie_table_query string-concat +4, encoded-row +3 and eoie_do_program/eoie_do macros from r34's where comparisons, numeric notes, except and move: model where clauses and numeric notes as records before r34 ships. (4) agile_note_model +1 macro (local_stamp, a clock read). Next-session rule: run ratchet-trees.ps1 (or the future eoie release check) on every check tree and block a slice that adds a regression without one of these reasons.")
inl note_of_eoie_style_ratchet_16 () = Note ("EOIE-STYLE-RATCHET", 16u32, "2026-10-21T22:55Z", "eoie-lane", "18:55 papercut: 'eoie standards --ratchet <baseline> <dir>...' needs every package directory on the command line; 369 absolute paths under a scratch tree exceed Windows' 32K command-line limit ('The filename or extension is too long'), so I ran it from inside each src with relative names. Fix: --root <src> enumerates the packages (dirs with package.spiproj) itself, one call per tree.")
inl note_of_eoie_style_ratchet_17 () = Note ("EOIE-STYLE-RATCHET", 17u32, "2026-10-21T23:02Z", "eoie-lane", "19:02 eoie team, ratchet rerun after two fixes: (a) the row-mask export returns a (bool, string) tuple (r34: analysis encoded-row and table_query string-concat/encoded-row findings gone; analysis.rs 965, table_query.rs 534, table_test.rs 736; preview and cargo check of 35 crates green); (b) ps renders a typed row list (ps_row record, ps_rows union, widths computed in Spiral) instead of a tab table (r35: process_table encoded-row finding gone; process_table.rs 828). Remaining deltas: r34 +13 vs live (macros of local time, do except/move, machine watch sampling, analysis/table_test export calls), r35 +7 (adoption and grep crates' IO macros, guide write_problem, machine_windows window-row fields, read_query grep/tail). Each has its reason in the 18:55 note.")
inl note_of_eoie_style_ratchet_18 () = Note ("EOIE-STYLE-RATCHET", 18u32, "2026-10-21T23:08Z", "eoie-lane", "19:08 correction to the 18:56 papercut: none needed. The usage already says a directory without .spi files stands for its package subdirectories, so 'eoie standards --ratchet standards/census.spi <tree>\\src' is the one call (checked: packages=369 on the r35 tree). I had passed every package directory myself and hit the command-line limit. The 371 vs 369 package difference between the two ways of calling is two directories with a package.spiproj but no .spi files of their own; worth a look when the census next changes.")
inl note_of_eoie_style_ratchet_19 () = Note ("EOIE-STYLE-RATCHET", 19u32, "2026-10-21T23:46Z", "eoie-lane", "19:46 r36 delta over r35 (one-call root form): +2 regressions. eoie_grep_query new with a wide macro (hits_found moved whole, already counted in r35's read_query); eoie_memory host-macro 3 -> 8 and one wide macro: each cross-crate export call (eoie_memory_store::..., eoie_memory_save::...) is a host macro, so tonight's crate-boundary fixes trade gate margin for macro count. That suggests the census should count export calls separately from logic macros, or a typed extern-call form in Spiral. eoie_memory_save uses plain unions without typefeatureset (save_check, memory_field are fine as plain unions).")
inl note_of_eoie_style_ratchet_20 () = Note ("EOIE-STYLE-RATCHET", 20u32, "2026-10-22T02:57Z", "eoie", "22:57 heal plan (EOIE-LIB-REUSE): the ratchet becomes a hard gate that fails release/regeneration, with lib-first rules added: per crate, host macros outside the boundary package, $'{ blocks, Rc::<str>::from, rust_std_string calls and spiproj without spiral- may only go down from a committed baseline (standards/), and a touched legacy function must be migrated in the same change (touching legacy heals it). Same rules feed EOIE-HEAL-EDIT-HOOK's diff mode.")
inl note_of_eoie_style_ratchet_21 () = Note ("EOIE-STYLE-RATCHET", 21u32, "2026-10-22T03:02Z", "eoie", "23:02 two fixes surfaced by the edit hook: (1) the ratchet compares density, so removing lines without the counted pattern reads as a regression (agile_task_query string-concat 7/269 -> 7/247): judge by count when lines shrink; (2) host-macro reported 1 for agile_task_query although query.spi has six $' macros, so one-expression primitives aren't counted: count every $' outside the boundary package, plus new lib-first metrics (rc-wrap, let-block, rust_std_string use, spiproj without spiral-). Also eoie_jobs regressed unnoticed since its baseline (wide-host-macro 13 -> 15).")
inl note_of_eoie_style_ratchet_22 () = Note ("EOIE-STYLE-RATCHET", 22u32, "2026-10-22T08:39Z", "eoie", "04:39 groom + finding: both 23:02 fixes are in the live tree via ABC: MayNotRise now also requires the count itself to rise (probes.spi test: 7/269 -> 7/247 is not a regression), and one-expression host primitives are counted by the new top-level-macro probe. Before the ratchet becomes a hard release gate: (1) standards --baseline rewrites the whole census file from only the dirs given, so updating one package's row (e.g. a consolidation) wipes every other row, and a full rebaseline grandfathers whatever the release introduced; it needs a merge mode that replaces only the walked packages' rows. (2) release E's eoie_do_records is a NEW package holding the decoder macros moved out of 10 crates: field_index 203, items_span 556 and words_span 455 chars trip the new-package wide-host-macro rule although the debt only moved (and shrank 10x). With (1), E baselines eoie_do_records at its consolidated counts in the same step, the 10 do crates' rows drop, and the ratchet then keeps it from growing; the real heal is rewriting items_span/words_span in Spiral over sm'.split. The string-concat probe counts concat5 only, so the lib waves can only lower it.")
inl note_of_eoie_style_ratchet_23 () = Note ("EOIE-STYLE-RATCHET", 23u32, "2026-10-22T08:40Z", "eoie", "04:40 merge-mode design (code waits for a compiler: the lib list/list' plumbing must be checked): new census_mode case MergeBaseline : string for --baseline-merge <file> <dir>...; the walk is the same as WriteBaseline; the report keeps every old row whose key prefix CensusCount (<quote>name<quote>, does not occur in the fresh rows, then appends the fresh rows. Old row's name = element 1 of sm'.split on the quote char. Lib pipeline: sm'.split newline old |> am'.to_list_base' |> listm'.filter' kept |> join with newline (sm'.concat_list after converting list' to list, or a fold), then ++# fresh rows. No new host macros; reuses file_text/text_written until the boundary package exists.")
inl note_of_eoie_style_ratchet_24 () = Note ("EOIE-STYLE-RATCHET", 24u32, "2026-10-22T11:57Z", "eoie-lane", "07:57 eoie team: merge mode drafted for release F (scratch apply-baseline-merge.ps1, dry-run clean on top of the other F slices). census_mode gains MergeBaseline : string for --baseline-merge <file> <dir>.... census_tally gains walked : list string. The report keeps old 'inl census_' rows whose package (element 1 of sm'.split on the quote char) is not in walked (listm'.contains), then appends the fresh rows. Both baseline modes share baseline_saved, and the census package now references spiral-. The CLI notebook a_merged_baseline_replaces_only_the_walked_packages proves it through the ratchet: after a merge of pkgs/typed alone, old's 0/5 -> 20/ regression still shows (its rows survived) and typed's new macros are held. Remaining before a hard gate: (2) the new-package rule for E's eoie_do_records.")
inl note_of_eoie_style_ratchet_25 () = Note ("EOIE-STYLE-RATCHET", 25u32, "2026-10-22T13:17Z", "eoie-lane", "09:17 eoie team: (2) settled by the plan in note 04:39. G's release script runs 'standards --baseline-merge census.spi' (F's merge mode, so no other row is wiped) on its four new packages right before the ratchet: eoie_do_records at its consolidated counts (the debt moved out of 10 crates and shrank), plus eoie_do_verbs, eoie_machine_crowding and eoie_repo_affected so the ratchet holds them clean. The real heal (records over sm'.split instead of the items_span/words_span macros) waits for a compile-backed session, because every do crate inlines those lets and do_program.rs is at 988.")
inl note_of_eoie_style_ratchet_26 () = Note ("EOIE-STYLE-RATCHET", 26u32, "2026-10-22T13:40Z", "eoie-lane", "09:40 eoie team: a peer's friction report fixed in release F (apply-standards-unknown-rule.ps1). 'eoie standards' silently dropped a declared standards.spi row whose rule it did not know (the installed binary predates LibReferenced), so a package passed a rule that never ran. A typed declared_row (DeclaredRule standard | UnknownRule name | NotARule) replaces the silent else; every 'inl x () = Name' row naming no known rule becomes 'standards.spi:N  unknown-rule  Name is not a rule this eoie knows, so it never runs', which counts toward exit 1 (extra findings are now counted per line, not as one). Tests: a rules block and a standards_cli notebook (findings=1, exit 1).")
inl note_of_eoie_style_ratchet_27 () = Note ("EOIE-STYLE-RATCHET", 27u32, "2026-10-22T18:15Z", "eoie", "2026-10-22 14:15 two follow-ups to F's standards changes, both in GE2: (1) the unknown-rule check read a typed unit function (inl stdout_line (~text : string) : () = ...) in eoie_standards' own standards.spi module as a declaration, so 'eoie standards' over that package would report 2 bogus findings. Rows carrying a type annotation ') : ' are now never declarations (apply-standards-typed-rows.ps1, with a test row). (2) GE2's baseline-merge before the ratchet also covers F's new crates eoie_window_admission and eoie_job_supervise, so the ratchet holds them at their first counts.")
inl note_of_eoie_style_ratchet_28 () = Note ("EOIE-STYLE-RATCHET", 28u32, "2026-10-22T20:38Z", "eoie-lane", "16:38 two more splits were missing from the first-count merges: today's eoie_experiment_amend (G) and eoie_process_kill (H) carry legacy code moved out of baselined crates. With no baseline, the ratchet and the PostToolUse style hook would judge each as a brand-new package (new_package_problem: encoded rows, wide host macros), so every edit there would be flagged. Added to GE2's and H's --baseline-merge. To stop the next split slipping, prelaunch-check.ps1 takes -ReleaseScript: every crate the slices create that has no census.spi row must appear in that script's --baseline-merge, or the launch fails. launch-GE2/H/I pass it. Logic test on the GE2+H tree: the old GE2 script is flagged (eoie_experiment_amend), the fixed scripts pass.")
inl note_of_eoie_style_ratchet_29 () = Note ("EOIE-STYLE-RATCHET", 29u32, "2026-10-23T09:11Z", "eoie-lane", "05:11 install probe (standards unknown-rule): PASS. exit 1 C:/Users/i574n/AppData/Local/Temp/claude/C--home-git/fc1568a6-c6d6-4cdf-9135-008e972e514a/scratchpad/after-install-F-88792/pkg/standards.spi:2  unknown-rule  ShinyRule is not a rule this eoie knows, so it never runs eoie standards ok packages=1 declared=1 files=1 findings=1 rules=no-comment-l")
inl note_of_eoie_style_ratchet_30 () = Note ("EOIE-STYLE-RATCHET", 30u32, "2026-10-24T04:55Z", "eoie", "00:55 rule proposal from a census: agile_task_query and eoie_window_claim list spiral- twice in package.spiproj (live since an earlier release; the build tolerates it). The cause is slices that append spiral- without checking for it (M's job-rechain slice got that guard on 10-23). 'eoie standards' should flag a duplicate package or module entry as a finding, so the next generator slip fails the ratchet instead of landing. Cleanup drafted: next-train\\duplicate-lib-entries.do.")
inl note_of_eoie_style_ratchet_31 () = Note ("EOIE-STYLE-RATCHET", 31u32, "2026-10-24T06:33Z", "eoie", "02:33 duplicate-entry rule typecheck PASS (02:22, standards.spi on post-R, with a probe of duplicate_entry). Next train stage S3, with duplicate-lib-entries.do as its positive control.")
inl note_of_eoie_style_ratchet_32 () = Note ("EOIE-STYLE-RATCHET", 32u32, "2026-10-25T08:50Z", "eoie", "04:50 eoie: two false positives on string literals, hit while building eoie_lint_macros (a package whose subject is macro text). (1) standards host-free counted a quoted macro opener as a host macro; fixed in eoie_standards/rules.spi (the check now looks only at text outside double-quoted literals, with a new case in host_free_flags_host_macros_and_rows_declare_it), landing in the S-stage regen now. (2) The ratchet census counted the word rust_global inside a string literal (the lint's own global-line detector) as 'a new package carries rust_global', and tab escapes inside literals as row encoding. Workaround: build the word from pieces. Proper fix: the census applies the same outside-quotes view. The tab-row signal was right on substance (the baseline moved to the verb's own row format).")
inl note_of_eoie_style_ratchet_33 () = Note ("EOIE-STYLE-RATCHET", 33u32, "2026-10-25T19:03Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-25 15:03 tibia: eoie standards misses every rule in an edition-3 standards.spi. eoie_standards/rules.spi declared_row_of returns NotARule unless the row starts with 'inl ', and since the edition-3 respell, standards files declare with ':=' (tibia src/standards.spi: ':= no_comment_lines () = NoCommentLines', 30 rows), so tibia's declared rules (no-comment-lines, lf-only, test-targets, line width 120 and 26 integer-only modules) are silently not checked. Fix: accept ':= ' (and ':~ ') alongside 'inl '/'let ', with a test block over an edition-3 standards text; until then a tibia standards check reads as clean.")
inl note_of_eoie_style_ratchet_34 () = Note ("EOIE-STYLE-RATCHET", 34u32, "2026-10-25T19:43Z", "eoie", "15:43 kit: fix drafted for tibia's 15:03 report. eoie_standards declared_row_of accepted only rows starting 'inl ', so every edition-3 standards.spi row (':= name () = Rule') was skipped silently.\n- It matters to eoie itself: the edition-3 respell turns eoie's own standards.spi rows into ':=', so the respell release would silently switch off eoie's HostFree and LibReferenced checks.\n- Draft: scratch clean\\standards-edition3\\declared-edition3.do.\n  - declaration_row accepts 'inl ', 'let ', ':= ' and ':~ ' (a plain starts_with chain rather than a list plus closure, to keep generated lines low; the crate is at 857).\n  - New test asserts: an edition-3 ':=' row declares IntegerOnly, and an unknown edition-3 rule is still reported as unknown-rule.\n- Dry run on a scratch copy of eoie_standards (dry-run.ps1): ok, 1 file, 3 replacements at lines 73, 191, 210; rules.spi 214 -> 221 lines.\n- Landing: it is a fix, so the freeze allows it, but the tree is leased by SLATE. It lands right after SLATE installs, in the respell release, before the respell is applied, so the respelled standards rows are read correctly from the first check. Until then a tibia standards check reads as clean even where it isn't.")
inl note_of_eoie_style_ratchet_35 () = Note ("EOIE-STYLE-RATCHET", 35u32, "2026-10-26T06:47Z", "eoie", "02:47 eoie: census false positive reported by fiscal (10-26 02:4x), for after KIT-RENAME. The standards --census gadt-case counter counts list patterns as GADT cases: fiscal/src has no GADTs but reports gadt-case=13, and every hit is a list match such as '| family :: rest' or '| _ :: _' (a lowercase binder or an underscore before ::). A GADT case declaration is an uppercase constructor, then ::, then a payload type: regex ^\\s*\\|\\s*[A-Z]\\w*\\s*:: . Under the list-sugar rule this inflates every list-heavy package, so the ratchet partly rewards plain list code. Two more clauses would make the census load-bearing (fiscal's done line is now 'beat the 5 mega samples with the complete typefeatureset'): (a) exists must exclude exists' and count packs '(exists' and opens 'inl exists x.' separately, crediting them only when both occur; (b) a case, nominal or prototype counts only when used: a constructor used outside its declaration, a nominal at 2 or more sites, a prototype with 2 or more instances. Reference implementation: fiscal's scratch census2.py; on the mega samples load-bearing equals raw almost exactly, which sanity-checks the definition. Plan: a TDD fixture per clause (a list match is not a case; an unused constructor is not credited; exists' is excluded), then the census change.")
inl note_of_eoie_style_ratchet_36 () = Note ("EOIE-STYLE-RATCHET", 36u32, "2026-10-26T07:32Z", "eoie", "03:32 eoie: fiscal's GadtCase false positive, drafted (kit scratch clean\\list-sugar\\census-probes.do). The probe goes from ^\\s*\\|\\s*\\w+\\s*:: to ^\\s*\\|\\s*[A-Z]\\w*\\s*::(?!.*=>): an uppercase constructor, and not a match arm (GADT case declarations never contain =>). Checked lines: '| family :: rest =>', '| _ :: _ =>' and '| Load :: rest =>' are no longer counted; '| Task :: string * ... -> agile_item' and '| AgileThen :: forall from to. ...' still are. On eoie src: 674 lines before, 657 after, so 17 list patterns were counted as GADT cases. Clauses (a) and (b) from fiscal's note (exists packs and opens, use-weighted credit) remain to do.")
inl note_of_eoie_style_ratchet_37 () = Note ("EOIE-STYLE-RATCHET", 37u32, "2026-10-27T15:42Z", "kit", "11:4x kit: the 10-26 census-probes draft (clean/list-sugar/census-probes.do: GadtCase counts only uppercase constructors without '=>'; RustGlobal matches both spellings, rust_global and global, since tonight's global-wrapper slice renamed call sites to core global) never landed. It still applies 2/2 on the composed next-train tree. census_cli's fixture cases ('| Small :: kind i32', '| Large :: kind i64') still count 2 under the new GadtCase regex. Queued as next-train step 5c. Expect the ratchet's counts to move once it lands (gadt-case down by the 17 list patterns, rust-global up where the bare global spelling is used); the release ratchet is report-only, and the baseline merge in the release script resets the declared packages. Clauses (a) and (b) from fiscal's note (exists packs and opens, use-weighted credit) remain.")
inl note_of_eoie_style_ratchet_38 () = Note ("EOIE-STYLE-RATCHET", 38u32, "2026-10-28T00:00Z", "eoie", "20:0x kit: the census-probes slice in TRAIN broke the census under Rust: lookaround patterns that Rust's regex crate rejects gave count 0. Fixed in the live tree with lookaround-free patterns, checked against regex 1.13.1 (details in EOIE-RELEASE-TRAIN's 20:0x note). census.rs needs the TRAIN2 regen.")
inl note_of_eoie_style_ratchet_39 () = Note ("EOIE-STYLE-RATCHET", 39u32, "2026-10-28T10:57Z", "eoie", "07:0x kit: census baseline refreshed with the fixed probes. 'kit standards --ratchet standards/census.spi src' showed 108 regressions, all packages missing from the 38-package baseline and none a real increase. 'kit standards --baseline-merge standards\\census.spi src' (installed TRAIN2c binary) wrote 434 packages (census.spi 648 -> 7380 lines; the old copy is kept as scratchpad clean\\census-before-refresh.spi), and the ratchet now reads ok, 434 packages, regressions=0. Ships in TRAIN3. The release script's ratchet line can go from report-only to enforcing once TRAIN3 publishes. Left: clauses (a) and (b) need fiscal's original wording.")
inl note_of_eoie_style_ratchet_40 () = Note ("EOIE-STYLE-RATCHET", 40u32, "2026-10-28T14:26Z", "eoie", "10:2x kit: found while reading clause (a): two of the five feature probes never matched Spiral's own syntax. Existential matched only the keyword form '\\bexists\\s+\\w+\\.', but kit writes existentials as '\\? x.' (:! any_flag = \\? value. ..., := \\? value. flag = f). That is 99 lines in 70 files and 0 keyword uses, so existential has been 0 in every package. Forall matched '\\bforall\\b', but kit writes '\\* t.': 323 binder lines, while the keyword appears 7 times, all in the census's own sources and fixtures. So the 'features may not fall' guard has been protecting GADT cases, indexed unions and prototypes only. Fix drafted (scratch clean/list-sugar/census-binder-probes.do, dry-run clean on the TRAIN4 copy): Existential = '\\\\\\?\\s*\\w+\\.|\\bexists\\s+\\w+\\.' and Forall = '\\\\\\*\\s*\\w+(\\s+\\w+)*\\.|\\bforall\\s+\\w+'. Checked on kit src with the decoded patterns: 99 existential and 325 forall lines (the 323 binders plus 2 census fixtures). A pack expression like '(\\? GadtCase)' has no 'name.' after the binder, so it is not counted as a declaration or open. Two new test expectations pin both binder forms. Clause (a)'s other half already holds: the keyword pattern never matched exists'. After this ships, counts rise everywhere (allowed for MayNotFall probes), so the baseline needs one 'standards --baseline-merge' run before these two probes protect anything. Clause (b) (use-weighted credit) remains. Two more probes had the same blind spot, now in the same program: IndexedUnion looked for the 'union' keyword (0 lines) while kit writes ':| name param =' (and ':| ^ name param =' for recursive ones), 200 lines with the new pattern; TypeDecl looked for union/type/nominal (30 lines) while kit declares with ':|', ':==' and ':!', 1528 lines with the new pattern. Both keep the keyword forms as alternatives. The recursive non-indexed ':| ^ probe_counts =' is not an indexed union (no parameter word) and is not matched. Prototype stays as it is: kit has no prototype or instance declarations in either syntax. Dry-run clean, every line within 120 columns.")
inl note_of_eoie_style_ratchet_41 () = Note ("EOIE-STYLE-RATCHET", 41u32, "2026-10-28T14:44Z", "eoie", "10:4x kit: clause (b) design (use-weighted credit), read against kit_style_census. Today each probe is one regex counted per line of a package's joined text (census.spi matching_lines), so a GADT case nobody constructs or matches, a nominal used once, or a prototype with no instance all earn the same credit as one in daily use. Design, decision rule first:\n- A feature probe gains an optional declared-name capture: GadtCase captures the constructor (group 1 of ^\\s*\\|\\s*([A-Z]\\w*)\\s*::), IndexedUnion and TypeDecl capture the type name after the sigil (:| name, :== name, :! name), Prototype the prototype name. Existential and Forall stay line-counted (a binder is its own use).\n- Credit rule per declared name, in the package's own text: a GADT case or union counts if the name occurs at least once outside its declaration line (constructed, matched or named in a type); a nominal (:!) counts at 2 or more other sites; a prototype counts if an instance of it exists.\n- Host side: one new primitive in the census boundary, credited_lines (text, capture_pattern, min_uses) -> i32. For each line matching the capture, it extracts the name and counts \\bname\\b over the whole text, minus the declaration's own occurrence. The line is credited when the remainder reaches min_uses. It stays line-based, so the baseline format (count per probe per package) is unchanged.\n- Rollout, rule written now: ship it next to the binder fix (note 40), run 'standards --baseline-merge' once, then compare kit src before and after. Accept if every package's credited count is at most its raw count and kit's top 10 packages by raw GADT count keep at least 70% credited. If not, the declaration patterns over- or under-capture, so record the rows and revise the patterns, not the threshold.\nEstimate 120m: 45 primitive plus a test block (unused case 0, matched case 1, nominal used once 0 and twice 1), 30 probe wiring, 45 baseline merge and comparison.")
inl note_of_eoie_style_ratchet_42 () = Note ("EOIE-STYLE-RATCHET", 42u32, "2026-10-30T23:53Z", "kit", "10-30 19:5x kit: process gap found, and 6 open regressions from kit's own 10-30 landings. GAP: lanes land with 'kit do' programs (anchored edits from stdin), and the PostToolUse style hook only sees Edit/Write tool calls, so the census ratchet never ran on today's kit landings. Running it afterwards over the 18 packages kit touched: 'kit standards --ratchet standards/census.spi <pkgs>' failed with 7 regressions. FIXED: kit_machine_watch host-macro 9 -> 11 (the health route and the leftover lookup were indented export calls; now named top-level primitives health_run and job_left_by, offset by migrating stdout_line to console.write_line and decimal_u64 to optionm'/convert; ratchet ok, watch.rs 889). OPEN, with the fix for each:\n(1) 'a new package encodes rows with tab or record separators': kit_log_counts and kit_table_counts (the column spec crosses the export as tab-joined 'name pattern scope set' lines), kit_machine_sample and kit_machine_health (the health.tsv history row). Fix: send typed parallel columns across the export, as kit_lint_scan does (arrays cross as Rc<RefCell<Vec<T>>>), and build the history row in the boundary from typed fields.\n(2) kit_table_measures host-macro 0 -> 1 and top-level-macro 0 -> 1: the kit_table_filter_kept call and the O(1) row_kept byte lookup. Fix: name the export call as one top-level primitive, and replace row_kept with sm'.utf8_byte_list (the Rust arm is a Vec<u8>) read once, plus an indexed lib getter.\n(3) kit_table_sequences top-level-macro 1 -> 3: the router's 3 export calls (measures, sections, counts). This is a move rather than growth (verb's measured_text macros moved to kit_table_sequence_measures), and the ratchet cannot see moves; it needs a rule or a rebaseline decision, not a code change.\n(4) kit_lint_classify encoded-row 0 -> 1: lint --tsv builds its row with sm'.concat_list \"\\t\". TSV output is the feature itself, so it needs the same decision as (3).\nProposed rule for the landing checklist: run 'kit standards --ratchet' on every package a 'kit do' program touches, before landing.")
inl note_of_eoie_style_ratchet_43 () = Note ("EOIE-STYLE-RATCHET", 43u32, "2026-10-31T08:53Z", "kit", "2026-10-31 04:5x kit: all 7 open census regressions from the 10-30/31 landings are closed (unstaged). The ratchet over the 9 packages kit touched passes.\nFIXED IN CODE (5):\n- kit_log_counts and kit_table_counts. The tab rows were a crate boundary that passed --count specs as tab-encoded text. Now kit_log_counts has a typed count_spec record ({name, pattern, scope, set_path, set_pattern}) and builds the specs itself from argv (specs_from over the argument words; the process args are shared by both crates). The exports are kit_sequence_counts(path, section_on) : string * u64, plus header() and missing_set() with no arguments. The '#sections\\t' protocol line is gone, and output rows go through lib csv.tsv_line. A new test, count_flags_become_specs, covers flag order (--count-in given after its --count), --in-set with a drive-letter path, a foreign flag between them, and missing_set. table_counts.rs 924 -> 800, counts.rs 825 -> 865.\n- kit_machine_sample and kit_machine_health: the history rows use csv.tsv_line.\n- kit_lint_classify: csv.tsv_line plus csv.quoted_cell_text, instead of concat_list with a tab replace.\nRegenerated in place with cargo check (4 crates, 971 s on a saturated box).\nREBASELINED (2), as decided earlier: kit_lint_macros top-level-macro 1 -> 7 and host-macro 5 -> 7; kit_table_sequences top-level-macro 1 -> 3. Each added line is a one-expression call into a sibling crate's export, which the hand-modeled crate split ([[hand-modeled-build-units]]) requires, and the census cannot tell those from drift. kit standards --baseline-merge standards/census.spi src/kit_lint_macros src/kit_table_sequences changed only those two packages' 34 rows.")
inl note_of_eoie_style_ratchet_44 () = Note ("EOIE-STYLE-RATCHET", 44u32, "2026-10-31T22:29Z", "x", "2026-10-31 18:3x x: enforcement gap in the census probes (kit_style_census/probes.spi). TypeDecl matches only ^\\s*(union|type|nominal)\\b and IndexedUnion only the 'union name param =' form, so granular-sugar packages (:| unions, :== records, :! nominals, :| name param = indexed unions) report type-decl=0 and indexed-union=0. Measured: kit's own kit_style_census (written in sugar) shows type-decl=0; x/apps/sim/spi (6,320 lines, dozens of :| and :==) and tibia arena/canary/host/map show type-decl=0 too. Consequence: the new-package rule ('declares types but uses none of the typefeatureset') can never fire on sugar code, which is all new code. Fix shape for the kit team: TypeDecl ^\\s*(union|type|nominal|:\\||:==|:!)\\s; IndexedUnion also ^:\\|\\s+\\w+\\s+\\w+(\\s+\\w+)*\\s*= (a :| with a type parameter). The baseline needs a re-take after the fix, since type-decl is unranked but gates the new-package check. GADT cases (| X :: t) and the \\? / \\* binders already count in sugar.")
inl note_of_eoie_style_ratchet_45 () = Note ("EOIE-STYLE-RATCHET", 45u32, "2026-11-01T19:41Z", "x", "2026-11-01 15:41 x: SAME GAP FOR PROTOTYPES. kit/src/kit_style_census/probes.spi:83 counts only '^\\s*(prototype|instance)\\b', so sugar prototype declarations ':? name t : sig' and instances ':+ name t = impl' read 0. Evidence: x/apps/client/splitter.spi now has 5 ':?' and 15 ':+' (X-SOTA-MODEL n4), and 'kit standards --census apps/client' still prints prototype=0. Proposed pattern: '^\\s*(prototype|instance|:\\?|:\\+)\\s', plus a census test row with one ':?' and one ':+' line. Until it lands, x's typefeatureset DoDs attest prototypes by a direct count, so the census can't ratchet them yet.")
inl note_of_eoie_style_ratchet_46 () = Note ("EOIE-STYLE-RATCHET", 46u32, "2026-11-03T07:32Z", "kit", "kit groom 11-03 03:3x (WIP cap, memory wip-cap-active-means-touched): Active -> Planned, last note 36 h ago by x, progress kept at 90%. Resume from that note:  prototype=0. Proposed pattern: '^\\s*(prototype|instance|:\\?|:\\+)\\s', plus a census test row with one ':?' and one ':+' line. Until it lands, x's typefeatureset DoDs attest prototypes by a direct count, so the census can't ratchet them yet.")
inl note_of_eoie_style_ratchet_47 () = Note ("EOIE-STYLE-RATCHET", 47u32, "2026-11-04T08:02Z", "kit", "11-04 04:4x kit groom: the last open gap (x n45: :? and :+ prototypes read 0) is fixed in the census probes: edition-3 :? :+ :| :== :! and {c}/(kind) binders, plus \\? packs, verified on kernel/fiscal/x fixtures 11-03. It passed TRAIN18's node regen and rides TRAIN20 (node half running now). This morning added: phantom types, reflection and higher-kinded probes, and the edition-2 `nominal` fix (TRAIN21). Every listed feature and drift probe now exists with a MayNotFall/MayNotRise direction, and the release ratchet and edit hook enforce them. Done when TRAIN20 installs and `kit standards --census apps/client` (x) shows prototype > 0. parked: waiting on TRAIN20 install.")
inl eoie_agile_atomic_writes () = Task ("EOIE-AGILE-ATOMIC-WRITES", "agile state writes are safe under parallel lanes: every writer reads, checks and renames under one lock, with a per-process temp name; today replace_file_checked writes a shared agile.spi.pending and the 'changed since read' check is separate from the rename, so two lanes within milliseconds can lose an update or collide on the temp file", Story, P1, D2, 100u32, Done, "", "eoie,agile,concurrency", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_eoie_agile_atomic_writes () = Planning ("EOIE-AGILE-ATOMIC-WRITES", Minutes 90u32, Load Light, Unassigned)
inl cells_cell_timings () = Task ("CELLS-CELL-TIMINGS", "Study Cells cell execution timings: per cell, the split between process startup (pwsh, erl/mix boot), compiler server warm-up, compile, native build and run, so the slow parts are measured before anything is optimized; not an eoie verb (Cells lane), eoie only feeds the startup numbers", Story, P1, D3, 45u32, Planned, "", "cells,performance,timings,main,team:main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_cells_cell_timings () = Planning ("CELLS-CELL-TIMINGS", Minutes 75u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_cells_cell_timings_1 () = Note ("CELLS-CELL-TIMINGS", 1u32, "2026-10-21T07:45Z", "main", "2026-10-21 03:45 main: data point from sefaz-family-gates4: one generated SEFAZ module (1 test cell, a 6-13k-line .spi) takes about 8 min wall per 'mix spiral.notebook --gate' (nfe_v4 done 02:23, nfe_v2_00 02:31; machine loaded), so the 13-module family gate is about 2 h and dominates every SEFAZ iteration. The split (mix boot, compiler warm-up, Spiral compile, cargo build, run) is not recoverable: probe-lib.ps1 deletes its --metrics folder, and tests.tsv has only one Ms per test. First step for this item: keep the metrics folder (or copy tests.tsv next to the raw output) and add per-stage columns.")
inl note_of_cells_cell_timings_2 () = Note ("CELLS-CELL-TIMINGS", 2u32, "2026-10-21T20:46Z", "main", "16:46 first step done without a new Cells feature: Cells already records per-cell phases (Spiral.Cells add_phase: compile, queue, slot_wait, code_memo_hit and the timed stages) and writes them into the output notebook's cell metadata (duration_ms, phases_ms; notebook.ex cell_json) whenever --output-path is given. probe-lib.ps1 now writes <RawOut>.ipynb when -RawOut is given (no -NotebookOut) and keeps <RawOut>.tests.tsv instead of deleting it. family-gates.ps1 and lib-gate.ps1 pass -RawOut, so every SEFAZ and lib gate from now on records the split. New lab/CELLS-PROBES/cell-phases.ps1 sums the phase totals, the unattributed remainder (process startup, mix boot and anything not timed) and the slowest cells over one or more notebooks. First data: tonight's SEFAZ variant 3 gate.")
inl note_of_cells_cell_timings_3 () = Note ("CELLS-CELL-TIMINGS", 3u32, "2026-10-21T22:26Z", "x", "2026-10-21 18:26 x: data from a second corpus, no new runs. lab/CELLS-PROBES/cell-phases.ps1 (called in-process; via pwsh -File an array argument breaks into stray positional arguments) over x/target/build/client/notebooks: 35 x client notebooks from 10-21, from job x-postid-round (07:43-09:20, memo_hits 0, so every cell executed) plus glyphs/events/schedule from x-split-fix (13:10). 177 timed cells, 8,054 s: compile 7,630 s (94.7%), target (native build+run) 289 s (3.6%), unattributed 74 s (0.9%), mount 26, code_memo_hit 18, cleanup 17, slot_wait 0. For x, process startup and mix boot are noise; Spiral compile per cell is the whole cost. Per notebook (total s / median compile per cell s): protocol 1470/185 (8 cells), splitter 1027/132, invariants 898/76, wire 742/33 (23 cells), world 470/68, posts 358/39, cli 353/16 (19 cells). So the smallest module, protocol (nominal ids, header, flaws), costs about 11x per cell what cli does. That points at what each of its test cells pulls into compilation, not at cell count; worth a per-cell compile breakdown next. Caveat: the round ran on a loaded machine (other lanes' heavy jobs), so absolute seconds are inflated; ratios between notebooks of the same round are the usable signal.")
inl note_of_cells_cell_timings_4 () = Note ("CELLS-CELL-TIMINGS", 4u32, "2026-10-21T22:27Z", "x", "2026-10-21 18:27 x: follow-up on note 3, explaining the protocol outlier. Median compile per cell tracks the targets declared in the file's ///> header lines. With none (default target only): cli 16 s, wire 33, posts 39. With 2 (rust + gleam): exsss 31, paging 66, world 68, personas 75, invariants 76, splitter 132 (splitter's cells are its largest). With 3 (rust + gleam + zig): protocol 185. Each test cell compiles once per declared backend, and the zig compile is the expensive one. The levers are therefore per-target compile cost (zig especially), compiling targets in parallel within a cell, or declaring fewer targets on hub modules, not process startup.")
inl note_of_cells_cell_timings_5 () = Note ("CELLS-CELL-TIMINGS", 5u32, "2026-10-21T23:30Z", "main", "19:30 first data from the new probe-lib outputs (tagged_union_family gate, 4 tests x c/zig, 5 timed cells, cell-phases.ps1): total 107.2 s of cell time in a 43 s wall run; queue 69.4 s (65%), target 19.3 s, compile 16.4 s, mount 1.2 s, cleanup 0.5 s, unattributed 0.3 s, so the timed phases account for nearly everything. Per test, c rows took 7-18 s and zig rows 6-8 s (tests.tsv), and the c rows' extra is queue. Reading: on this run the compiler-daemon queue, not compile or toolchain time, dominates: cells issued together wait for the compiler. Next: (1) the same table on a bigger gate (sm'.spi, 43 tests) to see whether the queue share holds; (2) if it does, measure the daemon's worker count vs the cells' concurrency (compiler_pool.ex) before any optimization.")
inl note_of_cells_cell_timings_6 () = Note ("CELLS-CELL-TIMINGS", 6u32, "2026-10-21T23:30Z", "main", "19:30 why the queue dominates (code read): the compiler daemon's pool is SPIRAL_CELLS_DAEMON_POOL, default 'auto', which is @default_max_workers 3 (compiler_pool.ex:25-36), further gated by free memory (room?). The tagged_union gate issued 8 compiles together (4 tests x 2 targets), so up to 5 waited in the queue at any time, consistent with queue = 65% of cell time. Experiment, rule fixed now: the same 8-compile gate, order alternating, 3 rounds each, with SPIRAL_CELLS_DAEMON_POOL=3 (today) vs 5 vs 8, on a quiet box (other load < 2 cores, the bench-pair -QuietCores rule). Adopt a higher default only if wall time drops >= 15% and the capped-run peak stays under 4 GB; otherwise keep 3 and record that the queue is bounded by memory, not by the setting.")
inl note_of_cells_cell_timings_7 () = Note ("CELLS-CELL-TIMINGS", 7u32, "2026-10-21T23:33Z", "main", "19:33 second data point (tuple_family gate, 3 tests x c/zig, started with 4.1 GB free on a box running eoie's release): queue 369.6 s of 411.4 s of cell time (90%), compile 23.9, target 14.0. The queue share grows with machine load, which fits the pool's memory gate: compiler_pool.ex room?/1 adds a worker only while free memory >= server_mb + reserve_mb (defaults 1,000 + 1,500 = 2,500 MB). On this 15.9 GB box with about 4 GB free that allows at most one extra compiler, so 6 compiles queue behind 1-2 workers. Mechanism to confirm before the pool-size experiment of note 6: log each refused growth (free MB vs 2,500) in the gate's metrics, then decide whether 2.5 GB per worker fits a 16 GB machine (a compiler worker peaked at about 0.5-0.9 GB in tonight's capped runs).")
inl note_of_cells_cell_timings_8 () = Note ("CELLS-CELL-TIMINGS", 8u32, "2026-10-22T00:09Z", "main", "20:09 pool finding and patch (step 1 of note 7, done as code): compiler_pool.ex room? ran its free-memory probe synchronously inside the pool GenServer, and on Windows that probe starts powershell (Win32_OperatingSystem.FreePhysicalMemory). Measured now: 1,599 ms per probe (its value tracks the Available MBytes counter: 4,930 vs 5,142 MB, so the gate reads available memory and not free pages). While the probe runs, no dispatch and no done message is handled, so idle compilers stall too; it fires at most every 10 s, but only while the queue is non-empty, which is exactly when it costs the most. lab/CELLS-CELL-TIMINGS/pool-probe-async.ps1 (dry-run; -TargetRoot for a copy): the reading lives in pool state and is refreshed by a Task that sends {:free_mb, mb}; growth waits for the first reading and then uses the cached one while a refresh runs; stats gain refused_growth and last_free_mb, which is the refusal log asked for in note 7. Tests in a scratch copy of apps/cells (job main-pool-test3/4): patched 6/6 on 6 runs; the new tests against the unpatched pool fail 2 of 6, the slow-probe case taking 1,186 ms for 3 x 30 ms of compiles (limit 500). Tree untouched (eoie's release is running Cells). Install goes with CELLS-PACK-NEXT; afterwards the gate metrics can report refused_growth per run.")
inl note_of_cells_cell_timings_9 () = Note ("CELLS-CELL-TIMINGS", 9u32, "2026-10-22T00:12Z", "main", "20:12 qualifier on x's zig finding (note 4), from the 7 sample-family gates' tests.tsv (61 tests x c,zig; row Ms includes queue): c median 8.1 s, zig median 7.8 s, zig slower in 29 of 61 tests; minima c 2.7 s vs zig 4.3 s. On small programs zig has a higher fixed floor (about 1.6 s) but is not the dominant cost; the c mean (16.8 s, max 128 s) is queue, as c rows are issued first. So zig cost grows with module size (x's modules are large), and the per-target lever matters for large hub modules, not for sample-sized tests. For small tests the levers stay the per-cell floor and the pool queue (note 8).")
inl note_of_cells_cell_timings_10 () = Note ("CELLS-CELL-TIMINGS", 10u32, "2026-10-22T00:22Z", "main", "20:22 design for surfacing refused_growth in gate output (after the note 8 patch installs): the pool lives in the compiler daemon (persistent_term {CompilerDaemon, :pool, daemon pid}; stats read at compiler_daemon.ex:405/412), and notebook runs reach the daemon over its port, so the runner needs a stats request in the daemon protocol. Since 10-21 19:40 the daemon is shared across lanes (eoie's release used it), so per-gate attribution must be a difference: the runner takes pool stats (served, refused_growth, peak_workers, last_free_mb) before the first cell and after the last, and writes the delta as top-level notebook metadata compiler_pool next to the per-cell phases_ms (notebook.ex 1291-1338), which probe-lib already keeps. cell-phases.ps1 then prints it per gate. Not started tonight.")
inl note_of_cells_cell_timings_11 () = Note ("CELLS-CELL-TIMINGS", 11u32, "2026-10-23T12:30Z", "main", "2026-10-23 08:30 main: note 10's design is implemented as cells-pack step 17, %TEMP%\\cells-pack\\pool-stats-metadata.ps1 (6 anchors, dry run by default). The daemon answers a 'pool' line with served, refused_growth, peak_workers and last_free_mb; a lone compiler reports an empty pool. CompilerClient.pool_stats/0 sums the SPIRAL_CELLS_COMPILERS ports and returns nil when no daemon answers, since an older daemon closes on an unknown line, so the protocol stays compatible. Notebook.run_cells reads it before evaluate and after, and the output notebook gets metadata compiler_pool: served and refused_growth as deltas (the daemon is shared across lanes, so those count every lane's compiles in the window), peak_workers and last_free_mb as read after the run. The three patched files parse (Code.string_to_quoted!), and cells-pack -NoSuite applies all 17 steps in order on its scratch root. Rides in main-cells-pack (job-1792754837043, after tibia's blocks). The proof is the first lib gate after that pack, whose .ipynb must carry compiler_pool; next, cell-phases.ps1 prints it per gate.")
inl note_of_cells_cell_timings_12 () = Note ("CELLS-CELL-TIMINGS", 12u32, "2026-10-28T01:32Z", "main", "10-27 21:32 main: Groom 10-27 21:2x (WIP rule, set before applying). main's Active items with no note since 10-24 were classed by the evidence in the tree, not by age alone. (1) SHIPPED -> Done: the item's pass is in the installed tree core (apps/compiler/spiral_compiler.fs, core 8660C34A11F33259, the installed 5m' core), found by its own identifier or anchor, and an accept has passed on that core since (accept-6, run single-flight-20261027-173417, 0 blocking cells). (2) COLD -> Paused: no lane is driving it this week and nothing is queued. Its last note keeps the next step, and it resumes when a lane picks it or its condition fires. (3) KEPT Active: a lane is on it this week, or demand is waiting on it. (4) Blocked: only the user's commit or push unblocks it.\n Verdict for this item: (2) COLD, so Active -> Paused. The next step is the one in its latest note above.")
inl note_of_cells_cell_timings_13 () = Note ("CELLS-CELL-TIMINGS", 13u32, "2026-10-29T22:32Z", "main", "10-29 18:3x main: PROVISIONAL fields filled while the item is Blocked or Paused (fill-all-task-fields): estimate 75m (D-base rule: base(D3) x (1 - 45%)). The owner corrects them on resume.")
inl note_of_cells_cell_timings_14 () = Note ("CELLS-CELL-TIMINGS", 14u32, "2026-10-31T03:21Z", "main", "10-30 23:21 main: lab/CELLS-CELL-TIMINGS is ARCHIVED (user, 10-30 22:5x: the lab dir didn't work and drifted; drafts don't live outside the repo). Archive 20261030-232045 under %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\archive. Restore this folder with: pwsh i574n.github/scripts/scratch-archive.ps1 -Root C:\\home\\git\\spiral\\lab -Restore '%LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\archive\\20261030-232045\\manifest.tsv' -Only 'CELLS-CELL-TIMINGS\\*' -RestoreTo C:\\home\\git\\spiral\\lab. Resume this item's work in its real place in the repo, not in lab.")
inl note_of_cells_cell_timings_15 () = Note ("CELLS-CELL-TIMINGS", 15u32, "2026-10-31T20:55Z", "main", "10-31 16:53 main, pause re-decision (KIT-PAUSE-RULE-REWORK; rule R3 is now 'never auto-pause for silence'): Paused -> PLANNED. It was paused by the old silence/WIP rule, not by a blocker: its notes name no external condition (a 10-30 lab-archive note blocks nothing: restore the draft from the archive into the repo when picked). Back in agile next at its priority; deps, if any, gate it as usual.")
inl note_of_cells_cell_timings_16 () = Note ("CELLS-CELL-TIMINGS", 16u32, "2026-11-01T04:50Z", "main", "main 00:5x LAB CODE IS SAFE (user 11-01 00:2x: 'check for all lab references in history... make sure we didn't lose that code... to repurpose them or groom their tasks'). This item's lab/CELLS-CELL-TIMINGS folder (1 files: pool-probe-async.ps1) is archived in %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\archive\\20261030-232045 (blob-verified). Restore it into its proper repo place with: pwsh C:\\home\\git\\i574n.github\\scripts\\scratch-archive.ps1 -Restore '%LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\archive\\20261030-232045\\manifest.tsv' -RestoreTo <repo dir> -Only 'CELLS-CELL-TIMINGS/*'. Next step for the owner at its next pull: restore the useful files in place as incomplete drafts (Cells heals them in place), or record why it starts over.")
inl compiler_module_parse_stack () = Task ("COMPILER-MODULE-PARSE-STACK", "Module parsing runs on a large stack: load_module parsed each file on a thread-pool thread, so a module with ~3,500 top-level blocks (the backlog's state/agile.spi) overflowed in bundle_blocks/fold_offset_term and eoie agile check died", TaskKind, P1, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "compiler,stack,eoie,backlog", "current", "current")
inl eoie_agile_queries () = Task ("EOIE-AGILE-QUERIES", "Typed agile queries as defaults and named views: list/next take --team, --tag, --prefix, --open, --ready, --blocked and --sort difficulty|priority|progress|wsjf; a blocked view names each unmet dependency; named views (eoie-by-difficulty, team-wsjf, blocked) live as typed rows in the backlog root and run with one call; each view is a tutorial case with exact output in spec/one-call.md", Story, P1, D4, 100u32, Done, "", "eoie,agile,queries,typefeatureset", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_eoie_agile_queries () = Planning ("EOIE-AGILE-QUERIES", Minutes 300u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_eoie_agile_queries_1 () = Note ("EOIE-AGILE-QUERIES", 1u32, "2026-10-21T01:38Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-20 21:38 eoie team, user 2026-10-20 21:37: 'see if there are gains turning these filters and similar advanced queries as default or templates easy to learn by tutorial, eoie should be easy to patch'. Evidence: answering 'eoie backlog by difficulty, also by the weighted lanes' (per-team WSJF backlogs) took three hand-written pwsh pipelines over agile list --json and agile next (tag/prefix filter + open + sort by D then P; agile next output regex-filtered to the team's ids; a blocked report joining deps to their statuses), so the gain is real: every team asks these questions, and each pipeline is a drift back to shell scripting (eoie-first). Defaults: agile list shows open items only, sorted by priority then difficulty; 'all' keeps today's full listing. Design for patchability, in the typefeatureset style (EOIE-STYLE-RATCHET): one query model, a filter union (Team, Tag, Prefix, Status, Open, Ready, Blocked) and a sort union (ByDifficulty, ByPriority, ByProgress, ByWsjf), with the flag words, usage text and help derived from the unions by reflection, so a new filter is one case plus one match arm, with no parser tables to keep in sync; named views are rows of that same model (View name filters sort) in state, so a team adds a view without touching eoie. Tutorial: each built-in view is a one-call spec case (command plus exact expected output), which doubles as its test.")
inl note_of_eoie_agile_queries_2 () = Note ("EOIE-AGILE-QUERIES", 2u32, "2026-10-21T01:45Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-20 21:45 eoie team, design inputs from the x team (same user question for x tonight, same three-pipeline workaround), all adopted for this item: (1) a separate parent/epic field, deps meaning blockers only (x deleted deps on X-CELLS-SPLIT and SIM-IN-SPIRAL-BULK to get them ranked; eoie inverted EOIE-ONE-CALL's links for the same reason); (2) a team field (main|x|tibia|eoie), the item's owning team, separate from domain tags, with list/next --team, since tags like x/x-api/x-compat/sim overlap and other teams' items tagged x leak into x queries; (3) list --sort and --team/--tag/--prefix, next --json, and wsjf/rank/unblocks fields in list --json so ranks never come from parsing text; (4) per-team weights from the user's team priorities so each team gets its own WSJF-ordered team backlog (today --as subagent gives x only one item, unblocks=0); (5) define estimate as remaining minutes, or derive remaining from progress, since WSJF divides by full size even at 98% (X-COMPAT-BLOCK-WEIGHT jumped to #1 at its true 30m), and let the team field keep another team's leftover from topping a team backlog; (6) usage should say that 'agile edit <id> deps -' (or none) clears a field. Refs: X-CELLS-SPLIT notes 21-22, SIM-IN-SPIRAL-BULK note 11, EOIE-ONE-CALL note 6, EOIE-WSJF-LPT notes 6-7.")
inl note_of_eoie_agile_queries_3 () = Note ("EOIE-AGILE-QUERIES", 3u32, "2026-10-21T01:54Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-20 21:54 eoie team groom for the 10-20/21 night (to 07:00): night slice (4th, read side only, no state schema change): a typed query model (filter union Team/Tag/Prefix/Status/Open/Ready/Blocked, sort union ByDifficulty/ByPriority/ByProgress/ByWsjf) behind list and next; next --json; wsjf/rank/unblocks in list --json; a blocked view; --team derived from the ID prefix and tags until a real team field exists. The team and parent fields change the state schema for every crate and wait for the user's go. Remaining estimate 300 min.")
inl note_of_eoie_agile_queries_4 () = Note ("EOIE-AGILE-QUERIES", 4u32, "2026-10-21T03:32Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-20 23:32 eoie team, slice 2 drafted (scratchpad apply-queries-slice2.ps1, lands with release 26): new package agile_task_query/query.spi holds the one query model both list and next use: team union with words and team_words by reflection, team_of_task from ID prefix and whole-item tags (fixes 'xsd' matching the x tag as a substring), query_filter (OnTeam | WithTag | WithPrefix | OnlyOpen), query_flag union with flag words and usage derived by union_cases_joined, flag_read_of as one fold parser returning FilterRead (filter, used) | FlagRefused | NotQueryFlag. agile list gains --team/--tag/--prefix/--open and --sort priority|difficulty|progress (list_sort union, words by reflection): rows are read once into a typed list and placed by a sort key with stable insertion, so the default order (priority, then file order) is unchanged; the summary adds ' team=x open sort=difficulty' and JSON a 'query' field only when a query is given, so existing outputs stay byte-identical. ranking.spi and task_next.spi drop their own team and filter copies for the shared model; next's --team rejection now reads '--team expects main|x|tibia|eoie, got ops'. Tests: 2 query notebook tests, 1 list contract test (team, open, difficulty order, default order, both rejections). Default 'open only' for list stays off (smaller and reversible; teams' scripts read the full list). Measured: agile list on the shared backlog takes 1.35 s for 456 rows, mostly the per-row planning lookup scanning the whole state file; worth its own item.")
inl note_of_eoie_agile_queries_5 () = Note ("EOIE-AGILE-QUERIES", 5u32, "2026-10-21T05:21Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-21 01:21 line gate: slice 1 alone makes task_next.rs 1188 lines (measured read-only on the r25 tree), slice 2 makes listing 1244 and next 1246; the query model compiles into each crate (~300 lines). Slice 1 pulled from r25 (saved in scratch queries-slice1-saved, restored from the pre-slice-1 snapshot); slices 1+2 ship together in r27 with agile_task_query as its own crate whose boundary is the normalized query flags (e.g. --team eoie --open), parsed back by the same flag_read_of.")
inl note_of_eoie_agile_queries_6 () = Note ("EOIE-AGILE-QUERIES", 6u32, "2026-10-21T05:32Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-21 01:32 agile_task_query crate drafted and preview-compiled with r26: exports eoie_query_flag_width/_refusal/_passes/_shown/_usage and eoie_team_of; listing 964, next 987 (tight), crate 454. Slices 1+2 now ride r26 (apply-queries-r27.ps1, base-hash guarded); the r26 follow-on applies them after r25 installs.")
inl note_of_eoie_agile_queries_7 () = Note ("EOIE-AGILE-QUERIES", 7u32, "2026-10-21T05:48Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-21 01:48 query flags are now a value-indexed GADT (FlagTeam :: query_flag team, FlagTag/FlagPrefix :: query_flag string, FlagOpen :: query_flag ()), enumerated through an existential any_flag like the census probes; value_of parses the typed value, filter_of builds the filter with per-case refinement. task_query.rs 498 lines, preview-compiled; in the r26 draft.")
inl note_of_eoie_agile_queries_8 () = Note ("EOIE-AGILE-QUERIES", 8u32, "2026-10-21T06:03Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-21 02:03 plan for the rest (r27+), set by the line gate (after r26: next 987, listing 966 of 1000): (1) --ready/--blocked need dependency status, so the query crate takes the task's open-dependency names as a fifth argument (listing and next already scan deps) and blocked rows name each unmet dep; (2) --sort wsjf reuses next's rank key, so it lives in next only; (3) named views become View (name, verb, flags) rows in the backlog root, read by a new small crate agile_task_views exporting the stored flags; list/next take --view <name> and append those flags before their own; (4) each shipped view gets a one-call tutorial case with exact output (spec/one-call.md) once the binary has it. Before (1)-(3), move the listing's sort and row printing into the listing crate's own exports or a sibling crate to regain headroom.")
inl note_of_eoie_agile_queries_9 () = Note ("EOIE-AGILE-QUERIES", 9u32, "2026-10-21T08:29Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-21 04:29 eoie team: drafted list/next --ready and --blocked (scratch apply-ready-blocked.ps1, r28 order 11). The query flag union gains FlagReady/FlagBlocked; filters take a settled bool. ready = open with every dependency Done or unknown, the same rule next ranks by. blocked = status Blocked, or open with an open dependency. list asks census for the unsettled IDs (new export eoie_agile_unsettled_ids) only when the query reads dependencies. Preview: task_listing 981 (1014 with the scan inside listing), census 333. Notebooks: a query test plus an agile_cli fixture with GATE/WAITS/SHIPPED/FREE/HELD. Remaining: --sort wsjf, named views, tutorial cases.")
inl note_of_eoie_agile_queries_10 () = Note ("EOIE-AGILE-QUERIES", 10u32, "2026-10-21T13:48Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-21 09:48 eoie team: named views drafted for r30 (scratch apply-views.ps1, applies after r29). New crate agile_task_views with three verbs: agile view-add <root> <name> <list|next> [flags...] stores a typed row View (name, verb, flags) in <root>/state/views.spi (replaces a row of the same name, changed-since-read check, atomic rename); agile view <root> <name> [extra flags...] runs eoie agile <verb> <root> <stored flags> <extra flags> as a direct child of the same binary (stdio and exit code pass through), so listing and next are untouched (both sit near the 1000-line gate); agile views <root> lists them. Verb words and the dispatch come from unions (view_verb, view_command) by reflection. Same slice: list/next usage shapes now name the query flags (--team, --tag, --prefix, --open, --ready, --blocked, list --sort) with a drift test in agile_task_query that every flag's usage appears in both shapes; agile note-edit takes --as <author> (agent sessions pass it to every note verb) and refuses an edit by anyone but the note's author. CLI notebook views_cli.spi. Preview queued (eoie-preview-r30d). Left: --sort wsjf (next already ranks by WSJF), tutorial cases in spec/one-call.md once installed.")
inl note_of_eoie_agile_queries_11 () = Note ("EOIE-AGILE-QUERIES", 11u32, "2026-10-21T16:50Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-21 12:50 installed with r26g: list/next take --team/--tag/--prefix/--open, list takes --sort priority|difficulty|progress; query crate agile_task_query with value-indexed flag GADT. --ready/--blocked drafted for r28 (note 9); remaining: --sort wsjf, named views, tutorial cases.")
inl note_of_eoie_agile_queries_12 () = Note ("EOIE-AGILE-QUERIES", 12u32, "2026-10-21T20:35Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-21 16:35 eoie team: shipped in the combined r28+r29 release, installed 15:38 (one-call spec 34/34, all 67 notebooks green): agile list/next --ready/--blocked.")
inl note_of_eoie_agile_queries_13 () = Note ("EOIE-AGILE-QUERIES", 13u32, "2026-10-21T20:46Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-21 16:46 eoie team, papercut from the adoption sweep: agile note and show print only UTC (at=2026-10-21T20:45Z), so lanes prefix every note text with Get-Date local time (part of 345 Get-Date calls today). Proposal: show prints each note's local time with its offset next to the UTC stamp, and note text no longer needs its own clock.")
inl note_of_eoie_agile_queries_14 () = Note ("EOIE-AGILE-QUERIES", 14u32, "2026-10-21T20:49Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-21 16:49 eoie team: local-time papercut drafted for r34 (scratch apply-note-local-time.ps1). agile show prints each note as '2026-10-21T20:45Z (16:45 -04:00)  author  text' through eoie_receipt's local offset (a malformed stamp adds nothing). The note model becomes a registered notebook with a test, and the r34 checks cover its 15 consumer crates. Once it ships, note text needs no Get-Date prefix.")
inl note_of_eoie_agile_queries_15 () = Note ("EOIE-AGILE-QUERIES", 15u32, "2026-10-21T22:12Z", "eoie-lane", "18:12 eoie team, decision: --sort wsjf on agile list is dropped. agile next computes WSJF from the dependency graph (unblocks), which list rows (task_listing's row_key over priority/difficulty/progress) do not carry; a list-side WSJF would either be a second formula that drifts from next or a per-row call into agile_task_next. agile next [--team t] [count] is the WSJF view. Left on the item: tutorial cases for the views once the spec's runner accepts direct verbs (every one-call case is an eoie do program today), and r34's local time in agile show.")
inl note_of_eoie_agile_queries_16 () = Note ("EOIE-AGILE-QUERIES", 16u32, "2026-10-23T17:50Z", "eoie", "13:50 eoie: tutorial case written now that the spec runner takes direct verbs (cases 38-40 already do): spec/one-call.md case 41, 'The same filtered list, asked for by name'. Habit: the retyped 'agile list <backlog> --team eoie --open --load light --sort priority' (the Stop hook and I typed it many times today). Setup: agile init, three items with --load light/heavy, then view-add light list --open --load light --sort priority. Command: agile view backlog light. The expected table was generated from a real run, with elapsed_ms as <ms>. Runner on the installed binary: 41 green, spec 39/41 (38 and 39 land with L and I).")
inl note_of_eoie_agile_queries_17 () = Note ("EOIE-AGILE-QUERIES", 17u32, "2026-10-23T17:50Z", "eoie", "13:50 eoie: CLOSED. Both leftovers of the 10-21 18:12 note are met on the installed binary: the views tutorial case (spec case 41, green) and the local time in agile show (every note row prints UTC plus local, e.g. '2026-10-23T13:16Z (09:16 -04:00)'). list/next already take --team, --tag, --prefix, --open, --ready, --blocked, --sort and --load, with named views (view-add, views, view).")
inl note_of_eoie_agile_queries_18 () = Note ("EOIE-AGILE-QUERIES", 18u32, "2026-10-24T23:45Z", "eoie", "19:45 Bug found while grooming: 'agile check <root>' fails on any root that has saved views ('state entry is neither one static package module nor one known transient receipt: views.spi'). view-add writes state/views.spi, but check's allow-list doesn't include it, so a root breaks its own check once someone saves a view. Fix: check accepts views.spi as a known state module and type-checks it with the rest. Done when: a notebook case adds a view to a scratch root and agile check passes, and the live backlog root passes agile check after the install.")
inl note_of_eoie_agile_queries_19 () = Note ("EOIE-AGILE-QUERIES", 19u32, "2026-10-25T00:08Z", "eoie", "20:08 Healed on the live root. The cause: state/package.spiproj didn't list views.spi as a module, so agile check rejected it as a foreign file. I added '    views' to the modules list with one eoie do edit; agile check then passed in 5s with views.spi type-checked (build_attested=3). Code fix: view-add now registers the views module in state/package.spiproj after writing views.spi (no duplicate, refuses a package without a modules section, skips a root without a package). It also adds the tests a_saved_view_registers_its_module_so_agile_check_sees_it and a_failed_view_write_leaves_no_pending_copy. Regen of agile_task_views plus cargo check passed on scratch (views.rs 882 lines). It ships as the last patch of HIJK (pending-heal\\apply-pending-heal.ps1, called from apply-rK.ps1), and HIJK's notebooks now include views_cli.spi.")
inl note_of_eoie_agile_queries_20 () = Note ("EOIE-AGILE-QUERIES", 20u32, "2026-10-27T15:39Z", "kit", "11:3x kit: done check, part 1 passes: the views_cli notebook passed in tonight's release candidate run (kit-release-SLICES, 52/54, the 2 failures were an unrelated lib-edit window). Part 2 (agile check on the live backlog root, which has saved views) is inconclusive, not failed: 'kit error: state\\views.spi: command timed out after 30000 ms', the 30 s type-check cap under full heavy load (both slots busy; check-attestation-cap). The old foreign-file rejection did not appear. Re-run when the box is quieter before closing.")
inl note_of_eoie_agile_queries_21 () = Note ("EOIE-AGILE-QUERIES", 21u32, "2026-10-27T18:58Z", "kit", "kit 15:0x: the pending 'agile check on the shared backlog' rerun is done. On a frozen copy of state/ taken at 13:4x (6.40 MB after the Done-notes trim, EOIE-STATE-SCALE note 18): kit agile check ok, files=4 build_attested=3 validation=receipt-first, on the deployed single-flight compiler (fingerprint 3415645863664410995). The live backlog itself cannot finish attestation while lanes are writing: two live runs both typechecked ('checked package') and then stopped at 'state or package inputs changed during attestation; retry'. A frozen copy is the reliable way to check during busy hours.")
inl note_of_eoie_agile_queries_22 () = Note ("EOIE-AGILE-QUERIES", 22u32, "2026-10-27T20:30Z", "kit", "kit 16:3x, closing review against the title. Done: flags --team/--tag/--prefix/--open/--ready/--blocked/--sort; named views as typed rows (state/views.spi holds View records; views_cli notebook green; agile check ok with views.spi, note 21); spec case 41 covers view-add plus view. NOT done: 'a blocked view names each unmet dependency'. Live 'agile list --blocked --team eoie' lists the right rows (EOIE-MEMORY-VERBS, EOIE-MACHINE-WINDOWS, EOIE-QUEUED-JOBS-IN-HOST, plus Active items with unmet deps) but prints no deps column, so the reader cannot see what blocks each one, and the spec has no --blocked case. That is the remaining 10%. It belongs in agile_task_listing, which is at 978/1000 after TRAIN, so it goes through a seam: compute the unmet-dependency text per row in a separate crate (typed columns across the Export, as in the 10-27 lint split), then add a tutorial case with exact output to spec/one-call.md.")
inl note_of_eoie_agile_queries_23 () = Note ("EOIE-AGILE-QUERIES", 23u32, "2026-10-28T12:06Z", "eoie", "08:0x kit: the missing unmet-deps column is drafted for TRAIN4 (scratchpad clean\\agile-queries\\waits-column.do; TRAIN4 copy built by clean\\train4\\build-train4-copy.ps1). agile_task_census gains unmet_deps (pure Spiral: split deps, keep the ones in the unsettled id set) and waits_column, exported as kit_agile_waits_column. agile_task_listing's table row appends '  waits <A,B>' whenever the query already reads deps (request.unsettled is set, so --blocked and --ready), through one host call. The seam keeps the listing crate small: 988 generated lines, against 996 when the column logic lived in the listing; census goes to 563. Both compile locally with the P2 single-flight DLL. Left: ride TRAIN4, then check live that 'agile list <root> --blocked' rows show waits <deps> and that plain list rows are unchanged.")
inl note_of_eoie_agile_queries_24 () = Note ("EOIE-AGILE-QUERIES", 24u32, "2026-10-29T22:42Z", "kit", "2026-10-29T22:40 kit: DONE on installed kit C528BE4D (TRAIN4c). agile list --blocked prints \"  waits <ids>\" on rows with unmet deps (closeout PASS: 129 of 140 rows; CD2F0D1A printed 0).")
inl x_done () = Task ("X-DONE", "Definition of done for x (deliberated 2026-10-20 night; deliberations in notes 1-3): x is stable and the lane can be freed for other projects when (D1) the final tree has two consecutive green ci runs on main (build ubuntu-24.04, build windows-2025, xc-windows); (D2) every gate the build runs sits at its target with no skip switch: actions-lock check, crate gate 16/16 at most 1000 generated Rust lines, --x-compat 0 findings (profile x/postability/x.json), postable 0 cells over one post, e2e tutorial and extra cases, every Cells notebook, sim ExUnit with byte-exact fixture guards, corpus commute on both schedules, thread_run accepts the real thread and rejects a tampered one; (D3) the scope promised in the open x items is finished, where an item whose remainder belongs to another lane counts as done for x once x's part is verified and the remainder lives in an item that lane owns: X-CLIENT, X-COMPAT-BLOCK-WEIGHT, X-REPO-TEMPLATE (x carries every template piece; a second consumer is outside x), X-CELLS-SPLIT (every client file a Cells notebook, shared domain modules, nominal user_id/conversation_id/cell_id), SIM-IN-SPIRAL-BULK (bulk on Spiral via Gleam, Elixir oracle retired per slice; no rayon executable without a measured need), SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM (real-Cells scale ladder recorded); (D4) outside x's done: SIM-IN-SPIRAL's supervision half (waits KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR), the compiler and Cells halves of X-COMPAT-PARAMETERIZE (main; x delivered the profile), X-NOMINAL-POST-ID (P3 polish), every NEAR and federation item (paused by the user 2026-10-18), live X API runs beyond one capped smoke; (D5) after done the lane is reactive only, with the resume guide in note 4: a red x run caused by a spiral regression is the dogfooding signal, fixed in spiral, then x ci is rerun; (D2 widened by the user 2026-10-21 20:4x) every file in every language but Spiral sources is at most 1,000 lines, generated code included (scripts/gate.ps1 measures all files with a named data allowlist; the generated sim Gleam is split by scripts/gleam-modules.ps1 until the compiler emits modules itself, GENERATED-OUTPUT-MODULES)", Epic, P0, D3, 90u32, Blocked, "X-CLIENT,X-COMPAT-BLOCK-WEIGHT,X-REPO-TEMPLATE,X-CELLS-SPLIT,SIM-IN-SPIRAL-BULK,SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM,SIM-GLEAM-PACKAGES,X-GATE-ALL-LANGUAGES", "x,done,stability,team:x", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_x_done () = Planning ("X-DONE", Minutes 30u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_x_done_1 () = Note ("X-DONE", 1u32, "2026-10-21T02:10Z", "x-lane", "2026-10-20 22:10 deliberation 1, refining D3: an item whose remainder belongs to another lane counts as done for x when x's part is verified and the remainder lives in an item that lane owns, so x's done never waits on another lane's schedule. Applied: X-COMPAT-BLOCK-WEIGHT is done for x when the client shows 0 cells over one post and 0 x-compat findings (true since 10-20 04:06); main's install of the postable budget then belongs to a main-owned follow-up, not to x. X-REPO-TEMPLATE is done for x when x itself carries every template piece (init, build, workflow, gate, actions-lock check, publish job, measured costs; all true) and CI on the final tree is green (D1); proving the template on a second consumer (blog, 10-19 note 4) is outside x, as is the yml standardization main owns (10-18 note 10)")
inl note_of_x_done_2 () = Note ("X-DONE", 2u32, "2026-10-21T02:22Z", "x-lane", "2026-10-20 22:22 deliberation 2, SIM-IN-SPIRAL-BULK's done line: every bulk module (frames, scenario with personas and schedule, invariants, x_world) runs from Spiral through the Gleam build with its Elixir oracle retired in the slice that reproduced the fixtures (true since 10-20 14:39), and the same modules pass Cells on rust. A rayon Rust executable for 50k-event corpora is not required: the Gleam path keeps the lib corpus at its Elixir speed (~45 s per schedule), so it would be an optimization with no measured need. Log (xsim-events v1 codec in Elixir) is the bridge format between the Spiral bulk and the Elixir supervision half, so it moves with SIM-IN-SPIRAL, not with the bulk; the Gleam shrink of encoded/fields_from is optional (xsim_frames.gleam is a gitignored build artifact). So the item closes once the 10-20 night pre-commit round proves the tree")
inl note_of_x_done_3 () = Note ("X-DONE", 3u32, "2026-10-21T02:52Z", "x-lane", "2026-10-20 22:52 deliberation 3, nominal ids: cell_id is in x's done (protocol.spi is the hub the client and the Gleam sim share; one chain covers both; staged tonight). post_id is out, moved to the new X-NOMINAL-POST-ID (P3): about 70 sites in 17 files, post ids cross every crate seam as text (digest entry lines, view, jsonl, later_id ordering), and the e2e suite already pins their behavior, so the type buys little stability for a large churn; the honest model also needs option post_id for the empty no-parent sentinel. X-CELLS-SPLIT therefore closes with the cell_id slice")
inl note_of_x_done_4 () = Note ("X-DONE", 4u32, "2026-10-21T03:17Z", "x-lane", "2026-10-20 23:17 D5 resume guide, for whoever picks x up after the lane is freed: (1) a red x ci run after a spiral change is the dogfooding signal: reproduce with pwsh scripts/build.ps1 (CI's own checks, ~75 min locally through eoie job start --heavy) and fix spiral, then rerun x ci; (2) a client .spi edit that a sim world is built from (prelude, posts, oauth1) needs pwsh apps/sim/build.ps1 -Accept, a review of the fixture diff, then a plain rerun; (3) cell weight: apps/client/build.ps1 prints postable cells over one post by first line, fix by helpers or renames (TLD-like field names autolink); (4) the crate gate (scripts/gate.ps1) is fixed by turning many-site inl helpers into let; (5) Cells notebooks need ///- --package . (.. for crate files), ///> rust/gleam/zig targets, ///> _ above a crate main; (6) open follow-ups outside x's done: X-NOMINAL-POST-ID (P3), XC005-POSTABLE-BUDGET (main), X-COMPAT-PARAMETERIZE compiler half (main, profile at x/postability), SIM-IN-SPIRAL supervision half (waits KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR), NEAR items (paused by the user); root README.md maps the repo")
inl note_of_x_done_5 () = Note ("X-DONE", 5u32, "2026-10-21T09:05Z", "x-lane", "2026-10-21 05:05 night status: D3 items closed: X-COMPAT-BLOCK-WEIGHT (its main half XC005-POSTABLE-BUDGET also done: installed 03:19, 0 findings on the client), SIM-IN-SPIRAL-BULK; X-CELLS-SPLIT closes when x-finish shows the two round4 failures were load (cell_id itself verified on the new compiler, X-CELLS-SPLIT note 23). Left: SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM's real-Cells ladder (lab/SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM/ladder.ps1, ~90 min alone on the machine; not run tonight: the night went to two Tibia/compiler-install exclusive windows and eoie r26 carries a lane-wide fix that I let through first), and D1: the user's commit of the uncommitted tree, then two green ci runs (the pre-commit round already passed CI's own build.ps1 locally)")
inl note_of_x_done_6 () = Note ("X-DONE", 6u32, "2026-10-21T11:38Z", "x-lane", "2026-10-21 07:38 D2 hardened for a retired lane: apps/client/build.ps1 only reported --x-compat findings and postable cells over one post, so a regression would have stayed green with nobody reading the report. Both now throw (X-COMPAT ... finding(s); POSTABLE ... file(s) with cells over one post). A vacuity probe runs first: --x-compat on a generated 300-weight block under x/target must give at least one finding, else the build fails with 'x/postability was not applied', because since the evening pack a missing or unreadable profile silently yields 0 findings. Also: the trimmed postability profile loaded fine on the installed compiler (x-ladder client step 07:3x: 0 findings in 36 files, postable 1621 posts, 0 over); the new checks are first exercised by the post_id round")
inl note_of_x_done_7 () = Note ("X-DONE", 7u32, "2026-10-21T19:40Z", "x", "2026-10-21 15:40 x status: X-NOMINAL-POST-ID is done too, finished today although D4 had put it outside done (note 7 there). D2 is now enforced and green on the uncommitted tree. The client build throws on x-compat findings and on cells over one post, with a vacuity probe first. The full sim build is green, but only after a real defect surfaced: the lib corpus run grew erl to 16 GB (two O(n^2) Gleam string paths in lib, index_of via split_once and the prepending concat_list/concat_array). Main fixed both in sm'.spi F7D95B8A8410F714 (Cells --gate 43/43), and x added glyphs.split_all for its big splits. The corpus now peaks at 166 MB; wall is 572 s for both schedules until the compiler's slice boundary check installs tonight, after which it should return to ~45 s per schedule (recheck after main's install). Left: SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM's ladder (after tibia's 15:45-17:20 live block) and D1, the user's commit plus two green ci runs.")
inl note_of_x_done_8 () = Note ("X-DONE", 8u32, "2026-10-21T22:15Z", "x", "2026-10-21 18:15 x: D1 precondition found. CI clones i574n/spiral's default branch (scripts/init.ps1), currently 33ee6412 (10-20 00:03). That commit lacks what x's tree now needs: the postability profile reader (0 'postability' refs vs 22 locally), the 'x-compat report: N finding(s)' summary line that apps/client/build.ps1 parses (an x-only push would throw 'no x-compat report line'), and today's lib fixes (index_of via binary:split, left-fold concat_list), without which the sim corpus step can reach ~16 GB on a 7 GB runner. The user has always committed both repos within seconds (61b8fff/33ee6412, 07b119c/002c71f6, a3b5353/668568ac), so D1 holds as long as x and spiral are committed and pushed together.")
inl note_of_x_done_9 () = Note ("X-DONE", 9u32, "2026-10-21T22:17Z", "x", "2026-10-21 18:17 x: resume-guide additions to note 4. (7) x and spiral ship together: CI's init.ps1 clones i574n/spiral's default branch, and x's client build needs spiral's postability profile reader and its 'x-compat report' line, so a red x run right after an x-only push is a pairing problem, not a regression; push spiral, then rerun x ci. Reference: run 39367603402 took 9.5 min on ubuntu (xc-windows 2 min) against the 120-min build timeout, so the sim corpus step's ~10 min on today's Gleam has plenty of headroom. (8) If erl balloons in the sim corpus step (apps/sim/build.ps1 'mix xsim.run --spi-file' over lib/spiral), suspect a quadratic Spiral string loop on the Gleam backend. Measure the peak per phase with a 100 ms sampler process on :erlang.memory(:binary), not end-of-phase readings; the 10-21 case was lib index_of plus the prepending concat_list (fixed in sm'.spi F7D95B8A8410F714) and is written up on X-NOMINAL-POST-ID notes 3-7. Run probes as eoie heavy jobs with a private-memory watchdog (cap 3 GB, machine floor 1.5-2 GB).")
inl note_of_x_done_10 () = Note ("X-DONE", 10u32, "2026-10-21T22:50Z", "x", "2026-10-21 18:50 x: D2 gap closed: the crate gate only reported. apps/client/build.ps1 ran scripts/gate.ps1 without -Enforce, so a crate over 1,000 generated lines printed 'OVER 1000' and the build stayed green; it now passes -Enforce. gate.ps1 -Enforce also throws when it finds no crate with [package.metadata.spiral] entry/output (a manifest-format drift would otherwise report '0 crates, 0 over' and pass). Verified standalone, no compile: -Enforce on today's outputs passes (16 crates, 0 over); -Limit 900 throws (6 over: args, digest, frames, jsonl, view, write, so headroom is under 100 lines on those); an empty root throws the vacuity error. All three D2 gates (crate size, x-compat, postable) now fail the build instead of just reporting.")
inl note_of_x_done_11 () = Note ("X-DONE", 11u32, "2026-10-21T22:51Z", "x", "2026-10-21 18:51 x: same audit for scripts/actions-lock.ps1: it already throws on missing or stale actions, but with actions.lock present and no workflow found it reported nothing and passed; now it throws 'no workflow ..., so nothing was checked'. A repo without an actions.lock still skips on purpose (template behavior). Verified standalone: the real tree passes (ci.yml, 8 actions locked); lock-only root throws. Remaining softening switches are deliberate accept modes (e2e.ps1 -Accept, sim build -Accept) that CI never passes.")
inl note_of_x_done_12 () = Note ("X-DONE", 12u32, "2026-10-21T22:52Z", "x", "2026-10-21 18:52 x: resume guide (9), crate headroom now that the gate fails the build: view 986 generated lines (14 left), digest 973 (27), args 957 (43), jsonl 915, write 909, frames 908; refs 790 and fetch 744 have room. A feature touching view or digest will likely trip the gate. The cheapest fix, measured on 10-18 (X-CLIENT note): turn an inl helper called from many sites into a let (xc 973 -> 722 that way), and find candidates per crate before editing.")
inl note_of_x_done_13 () = Note ("X-DONE", 13u32, "2026-10-21T22:54Z", "x", "2026-10-21 18:54 x: resume guide (9), cont.: the 10-18 profile_rs tool no longer exists (it's named only in this backlog and in memory), but the generated .rs can be profiled without a compile: split it at '^fn ' and sum lines per function. view.rs (986 lines, 41 fns): no named Spiral function is emitted twice (merge_newest 85, entry_lines 73, entry_of_line 48, ref_lines 41, single copies); 19 anonymous method* specializations hold 391 lines (40%). So the inl -> let trick has little to grab in view; headroom there means fewer specialized join points (e.g. one let for the merge-sort halves/merge pair), measured with a compile.")
inl note_of_x_done_14 () = Note ("X-DONE", 14u32, "2026-10-21T22:55Z", "x", "2026-10-21 18:55 x: resume guide (9), all 16 crates profiled from their generated .rs (no compile). Lines / functions / share in anonymous method/closure specializations / largest named fn: view 986/41/41%/merge_newest 85; digest 973/39/47%/post_by 51; args 957/35/20%/advance 110; jsonl 915/35/47%/entry_json 104; write 909/17/31%/raw_reply 149; frames 908/40/31%/chunk 73; refs 790, fetch 744, thread 712, users 709 (each carries the same 116-line app_reply); assemble 733/62%; xc 699; locate 659; token 355; net 307; checks 285. Levers by crate: args, write and jsonl are dominated by one named function (shrink advance, raw_reply or entry_json); view, digest and assemble are mostly specializations (fewer join points); app_reply would be shared once crates can export across each other (RUST-EXPORT-TYPES).")
inl note_of_x_done_15 () = Note ("X-DONE", 15u32, "2026-10-22T00:21Z", "x", "2026-10-21 20:21 x: D1/D2 pre-check against main's night-pack candidate before it installs (candidates/night-release single-flight, built 18:57, core 9E336ED57C8ACFE5 = the hash main's install rule requires): all 16 client crates compiled to scratch output, about 12 s each, inside compile windows; every one is byte-identical to the tree's generated .rs (view 986, digest 973, args 957 ... checks 285), so the install moves no crate against the 1,000-line gate and the generated Rust in the tree is already what the new compiler writes. The sim frames Gleam differs only by the expected slice boundary check (21+/6-: spiral_string_starts_character on the two boundary bytes, then identity instead of re-validating the slice), so that version is now in the tree (apps/sim/frames/src/xsim_frames.gleam) as the one to commit; the post-install sim -fast should regenerate it byte-equal. Corpus speed with it: job x-corpus-candidate (result in the next note).")
inl note_of_x_done_16 () = Note ("X-DONE", 16u32, "2026-10-22T00:34Z", "x", "2026-10-21 20:34 x: resume guide (9), one more lever measured and ruled out: the generated Rust keeps unused let bindings, mostly the intermediate literals of folded concatenations (pair \"refs\" v emits LIT refs, LIT \"refs and LIT \"refs\": and uses only the last). lab/X-DONE/dead_lets.ps1 (text-only, per fn) counts 183 dead lets in 11,641 lines (1.6%): args 36, locate 36, jsonl 30, write 16, but view and digest, the two crates nearest the gate, only 7 each. So a compiler dead-binding pass would not buy the headroom that matters; view and digest still need fewer specializations (note 9 cont.). Not filed for main for that reason.")
inl note_of_x_done_17 () = Note ("X-DONE", 17u32, "2026-10-22T00:39Z", "x", "2026-10-21 20:39 x: corpus check on the candidate Gleam (job x-corpus-candidate, scratchpad corpus_only.ps1 = apps/sim/build.ps1's corpus step alone, 20:21-20:39): schedule 1 passed with every invariant ok (posts=1747 events=10748, ms=59864, peak_beam_mb=74, erl private ~96 MB, so the 10-21 memory fix holds on the new Gleam). Schedule 2 never reported: from about 20:29 the erl node used 0 CPU (188 s total, unchanged over 9 min of samples) and printed only os_mon memsup timeouts (its win32sysinfo port was gone) while the machine sat at 100% CPU with three other lanes' compile windows; stopped at 20:39 with eoie job stop. So: no speed verdict tonight. The ms field cannot give one anyway (corpus ms ranged 21-60 s all day; the 286 s/schedule baseline is wall time per schedule), so the rerun after main's install must read the wall clock per schedule, on a quiet machine. If erl idles at 0 CPU again after schedule 1, that is a stall worth its own item: suspects are a blocked stdout pipe or os_mon, not the sim (the runner only waits with timeouts).")
inl note_of_x_done_18 () = Note ("X-DONE", 18u32, "2026-10-22T00:40Z", "x", "2026-10-21 20:40 x: what is left for X-DONE, in order, as of tonight. (a) D1: the user commits x and spiral together; then two consecutive green ci runs on main (build ubuntu-24.04, build windows-2025, xc-windows); a red run right after an x-only push is a pairing problem (note 14 (7)). (b) After main's night-pack install (main pings; it waits on eoie's r30-r33 release): one apps/sim/build.ps1 -fast 1 (expect xsim_frames.gleam byte-equal to the tree, fixtures unchanged), then lab/X-DONE/corpus_only.ps1 as a heavy job on a quiet machine, reading CORPUS wall_s_so_far per schedule against 286 s/schedule; the generated Rust needs nothing (all 16 crates byte-identical under the candidate, note 15). (c) SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM rung 10:8:4 alone, per that item's note 31 (ladder-guarded.ps1 -Rungs 10:8:4 -ResumeMemoDir target/build/sim/ladder-memo-113232-10 -StopAt <start+90 min>), then 14:6:4. When (a) and (c) hold, X-DONE closes and the lane is reactive per D5; (b) is a confirmation, not a gate.")
inl note_of_x_done_19 () = Note ("X-DONE", 19u32, "2026-10-22T00:45Z", "x", "2026-10-21 20:45 x: D2 widened by the user (20:4x): the 1,000-line gate applies to every language in x except Spiral sources, so a 9,093-line generated Gleam file is a D2 failure, not an artifact. Added SIM-GLEAM-PACKAGES (split into Gleam packages + build/parallel benchmark on Gleam 1.19.2) and X-GATE-ALL-LANGUAGES (gate.ps1 measures all files, named data allowlist) as X-DONE deps.")
inl note_of_x_done_20 () = Note ("X-DONE", 20u32, "2026-10-22T01:40Z", "x", "2026-10-21 21:40 x: correction to note 18 (b): lab/X-DONE/corpus_only.ps1 is pruned under the lab rule; apps/sim/build.ps1 -fast 1 now prints the corpus wall time per schedule itself, so (b) is that one command after the install.")
inl note_of_x_done_21 () = Note ("X-DONE", 21u32, "2026-10-22T03:07Z", "x", "2026-10-21 23:07 x: night run until 07:00 (user 23:0x: \"iterate on the x backlog until 7am autonomously\"). Order and rules fixed before starting. (1) SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM rung 10:8:4 first, because the machine is quiet now (0 windows, 0 jobs, no lane armed): heavy job, resumed from target/build/sim/ladder-memo-113232-10. Correctness holds: memo keys are content hashes, so parsing.spi's 18:22 change only recomputes that notebook's entries; note 31's 'fresh if lib changed' was over-cautious. AdmitMb 4500 instead of 6000: the quiet baseline is 4.7 GB free (Claude sessions, Fork, RustDesk), and 4500 = the 2000 floor + rung 1's 2,295 MB tree peak. StopAt start+100 min. Pass = exit 0 with sequential_equivalence, no_stale_results and commute ok on both schedules. Rung 14:6:4 only if 10:8:4 passes and finishes before 03:00. Run from x/lab as is (it is the experiment); afterwards the ladder lands as proper code and the folder goes. (2) While it runs (CPU-light): land lab/X-DONE (corpus_only is already in apps/sim/build.ps1; candidate_gate becomes scripts/gate.ps1 -Compiler <dll>, verified by recompiling one crate with the installed compiler, which must be byte-equal). (3) CELLS-POSTABILITY-PROFILE: apply the proven draft into spiral/apps/cells only when no Cells run is in flight, with main told first; verify with mix test of x_compat and postability_profile tests. (4) SIM-GLEAM-PACKAGES, then X-GATE-ALL-LANGUAGES. Compiles go in compile windows, one heavy job of mine at a time. Every result gets its numbers in the item note, and the lab: line goes in the 07:00 report.")
inl note_of_x_done_22 () = Note ("X-DONE", 22u32, "2026-10-22T03:27Z", "x", "2026-10-21 23:27 x: lab/X-DONE assimilated and deleted. corpus_only.ps1 became the corpus wall line in apps/sim/build.ps1 (xsim / corpus ... per_schedule_s). candidate_gate.ps1 became scripts/gate.ps1 -Compiler <SpiralCompiler.dll>: it regenerates every crate with the given compiler into target/build/gate-candidate and reports identical/DIFFERS per crate plus lines against the gate. Verified 23:2x with the installed single-flight compiler: 16/16 identical to the tree (view 986 ... checks 285), 0 over. README row updated. So the next compiler install can be pre-checked with one call: pwsh scripts/gate.ps1 -Compiler <candidate dll>.")
inl note_of_x_done_23 () = Note ("X-DONE", 23u32, "2026-10-22T03:51Z", "x", "2026-10-21 23:51 x: resume guide additions for tonight (to read with note 4). (10) Before any compiler install: pwsh scripts/gate.ps1 -Compiler <candidate SpiralCompiler.dll> regenerates all 16 crates into target/build/gate-candidate and reports identical/DIFFERS plus lines against 1,000; a DIFFERS row is expected only where the candidate changes codegen, and an OVER row means split before the install lands. (11) Scale questions: apps/sim/build.ps1 -Ladder 6:8:3[,10:8:4,...] [-LadderMemoDir <dir> for one rung], run as eoie job start --heavy, plus --max-private-mb once eoie ships EOIE-JOB-MEMORY-CAP. This 16 GB box passes 6:8:3 and runs out of headroom at 10:8:4 (SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM note 36). (12) Generated Gleam: scripts/gleam-modules.ps1 runs inside apps/sim/build.ps1 after BuildSpiral and turns xsim_frames.gleam into <=1,000-line modules (types, call-graph-ordered parts, exports kept in xsim_frames); if a future simframes change makes a part exceed the limit, the script throws with the module name, so raise nothing and let the packing re-balance; if it can't (one SCC over 1,000 lines), that is a compiler matter (GENERATED-OUTPUT-MODULES). (13) scripts/gate.ps1 measures every non-Spiral file against 1,000 lines; data files are exempt only by name in its allowlist. Adding a file there is a visible decision, never an extension rule. (14) apps/sim/build.ps1 sends Erlang crash dumps to spiral-bin/crash-dumps, and apps/sim/.gitignore no longer hides erl_crash.dump, so a stray dump in the tree is a bug to fix.")
inl note_of_x_done_24 () = Note ("X-DONE", 24u32, "2026-10-22T03:52Z", "x", "2026-10-21 23:52 x: plan for D1 evidence: tonight's tree changes reach CI's own scripts/build.ps1 (gate.ps1 all-files and -Compiler, apps/client/build.ps1 adds the simframes notebook, apps/sim/build.ps1 adds gleam-modules, -Ladder, corpus timing and crash-dump redirect, Cargo.toml unchanged in the end), so the 10-20 pre-commit proof is stale. After x-sim-fast-modules passes, a full pwsh scripts/build.ps1 (no -fast, about 75 min) runs as heavy job x-precommit-night over the final tree, and its result goes here before the 07:00 report. If it fails, the failing step is fixed or the change behind it reverted, so the user's commit stays green.")
inl note_of_x_done_25 () = Note ("X-DONE", 25u32, "2026-10-22T04:41Z", "x", "2026-10-22 00:41 x: status. D2 and D3 are met: every gate enforced at target (crate gate, all-files 1,000-line gate, x-compat 0, postable 0 over, e2e, Cells notebooks incl. simframes, sim ExUnit 43/43 with byte-exact fixtures, corpus commute on both schedules, real-Cells executor, thread_run), SIM-GLEAM-PACKAGES and X-GATE-ALL-LANGUAGES Done tonight, SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM Done (ladder recorded and landed). Open deps: X-CLIENT and X-REPO-TEMPLATE, both blocked only on D1 (user commit of x and spiral, then two consecutive green ci runs). Before that commit: x-precommit-night (full scripts/build.ps1 on the night-pack compiler) runs after main's install.")
inl note_of_x_done_26 () = Note ("X-DONE", 26u32, "2026-10-22T04:49Z", "x", "2026-10-22 00:49 x: x-precommit-night started (full pwsh scripts/build.ps1, CI's own checks) after main's 'installed' (00:42, single-flight core DA88EFB13FF61151). It runs on the tree with tonight's changes: adopted Gleam modules, enforced all-files gate, simframes notebook, ladder and corpus timing in the sim build. Queued behind main's heavy jobs (main-fmt-fix4, main-cells-pack); result here when it finishes.")
inl note_of_x_done_27 () = Note ("X-DONE", 27u32, "2026-10-22T06:51Z", "x", "2026-10-22 02:51 x: x-precommit-night (job-1792644584744) failed, exit 1, at the client notebook step after every earlier step passed: actions-lock (8 locked), crate gate (16 crates, 0 over), all-files gate (105 files, 0 over), x-compat probe (1 finding as designed) and 0 findings in 36 files, Rust release build (4 m 17 s), e2e (31 tutorial steps + 5 extra cases, 170 stub requests, post-thread/read-back/delete-thread ok), postable (1,644 posts, 0 over), prelude notebook 3/3. The failure: exsss.spi notebook, test rolls_match_erlang on the gleam target, 'Spiral cell timed out after 300s; the process tree was killed'. The machine was at 100% CPU (crate compiles took 500+ s instead of about 12; tibia and eoie heavy jobs in both slots), and exsss.spi is unchanged tonight. Rerun of that notebook alone at 02:5x in a compile window: 5/5 passed in 65 s. So it was a contention timeout, not a regression on the night-pack compiler. The remaining steps (33 client notebooks, full sim build with the Cells executor and thread_run) did not run, so the full scripts/build.ps1 is re-queued as heavy job x-precommit-night2.")
inl note_of_x_done_28 () = Note ("X-DONE", 28u32, "2026-10-22T08:34Z", "x", "2026-10-22 04:34 x: x-precommit-night2 (03:52-04:31) failed at the crate gate: view 1,035 > 1,000 (write 920). Cause found: main's uncommitted lib/spiral/sm'.spi balanced concat (GLEAM-STRING-LINEAR, 02:06) adds concat_pass (30 lines) and concat_balanced (25 lines) to every crate that concatenates; view nets +49 (986 -> 1,035), write +11. The installed compiler is unchanged since 00:42. So the tree is red only together with main's lib edit, and x's CI would go red if spiral is committed as it stands. Asked main (04:3x) to apply the balanced concat on the Gleam backend only (the quadratic concat is a Gleam problem); fallback: shrink view in x by about 40 lines. Run 1's results (gates, x-compat 0, e2e, postable) still stand for the x side; the notebooks and the full sim build remain to verify after the fix.")
inl note_of_x_done_29 () = Note ("X-DONE", 29u32, "2026-10-22T08:35Z", "x", "2026-10-22 04:35 x: fallback analysis for the view gate (CPU-only). view reaches sm'.concat_list through view.spi (1 call) and the shared modules posts.spi (5 calls), prelude.spi (delimited) and glyphs.spi (trimmed_end, rejoined); view.rs has one caller of concat_balanced. posts.spi and prelude.spi are also sim world sources, so an x-side change means re-accepting the sim fixtures, and a backend_switch concat wrapper in x would duplicate the lib decision. So the right fix is main's, in lib (balanced concat for Gleam only); the x fallback (wrapper in prelude, Rust fold path, fixture re-accept) is kept for after 05:00 if main hasn't decided.")
inl note_of_x_done_30 () = Note ("X-DONE", 30u32, "2026-10-22T08:40Z", "x", "2026-10-22 04:40 x: pre-commit run 3 is pending. main makes the sm'.spi balanced concat Gleam-only (in the tree about 05:25), which should restore view.rs to 986 lines with no x change. tibia's live arena block runs 05:30-06:30 with no compiles in any lane (the reaper killed live runs on 10-14 and 10-20), so the 75-100 min scripts/build.ps1 can't run before 07:00. It's queued at 06:31 as heavy job x-precommit-night3 (FIFO behind main's packs), and its id goes in the morning report so the user can check the result before committing.")
inl note_of_x_done_31 () = Note ("X-DONE", 31u32, "2026-10-22T08:41Z", "x", "2026-10-22 04:41 x: restored the nine generated crate files that run 2 had regenerated against main's uncommitted lib edits (locate, net, refs, thread, token, users, view, write, xc) to their staged index versions, so the tree is consistent and green under the enforced gate (16 crates, 0 over, view 986; 105 files, 0 over). CI regenerates them anyway; run 3 (after tibia's live block) regenerates them with main's Gleam-only concat fix.")
inl note_of_x_done_32 () = Note ("X-DONE", 32u32, "2026-10-22T08:44Z", "x", "2026-10-22 04:44 x: resume guide (15). Once eoie release E is installed (bench window class, from x's EOIE-MACHINE-WINDOWS proposal of 10-21 20:16), scale ladders run as eoie window run bench <minutes> -- pwsh apps/sim/build.ps1 -Ladder <rungs>. A bench window keeps heavy-build and test-suite jobs out (compiles still run), needs 2,048 MB free, and an older waiting bench request blocks newer heavy builds, so a rung measures scale rather than contention (the 10-21 rung 10:8:4 lost its first attempt to 57 neighbour jobs).")
inl note_of_x_done_33 () = Note ("X-DONE", 33u32, "2026-10-22T09:10Z", "x", "2026-10-22 05:10 x: main's Gleam-only concat fix is in the tree (sm'.spi F6DAD2622D42D008). Quick check in the quiet window before tibia's live block (05:06-05:10, compile window): gate.ps1 -Compiler with the installed night pack regenerated all 16 crates: view 987 lines (was 1,035 in run 2), digest 976, args 958, the rest smaller; 0 over 1,000; all-files gate 105 files, 0 over. 15 crates differ from the staged versions by 1-3 lines each, presumably main's other uncommitted lib edits (env, process, date_time); checks is identical. So the crate gate passes on the final lib. Pre-commit run 3 (full scripts/build.ps1) is queued after the live block at 06:31.")
inl note_of_x_done_34 () = Note ("X-DONE", 34u32, "2026-10-22T10:28Z", "x", "2026-10-22 06:28 x: pre-commit run 3 started as heavy job job-1792664923709-x-precommit-night3 (full pwsh scripts/build.ps1) at 06:28, right after tibia's live block ended (06:28), on a quiet machine (0 windows, 6.2 GB free), with main's Gleam-only sm'.spi concat fix in the tree (F6DAD2622D42D008) and the installed night pack. Expected 75-100 min, so it finishes after this run's 07:00 end; the user can check it with eoie job status job-1792664923709-x-precommit-night3.")
inl note_of_x_done_35 () = Note ("X-DONE", 35u32, "2026-10-22T10:50Z", "x", "2026-10-22 06:50 x: run 3 (job-1792664923709-x-precommit-night3) at 06:50, still running: gates passed (16 crates and 105 files, 0 over), x-compat 0 in 36 files, e2e 31 tutorial steps + 5 extra cases, postable 1,644 posts 0 over, notebooks under way incl. exsss 5/5. Remaining: the other client notebooks and the full sim build. Night run closed at 06:5x; the final result is for the user to read via eoie job status.")
inl note_of_x_done_36 () = Note ("X-DONE", 36u32, "2026-10-22T11:25Z", "x", "2026-10-22 07:25 x: pre-commit run 3 (06:28-07:21) failed in the client notebooks, again only on a contention timeout. Passed: gates (16 crates and 105 files, 0 over), x-compat 0 in 36 files, e2e 31 tutorial steps + 5 extras, postable 1,644 posts 0 over, and 10 notebooks (prelude 3, exsss 5 (run 1's failure), paging 3, glyphs 6, protocol 7, splitter 7, events 8, final_state 2, personas 3, scenario 2). Failed: schedule.spi, both gleam tests 'Spiral cell timed out after 300s'. My own scratch cargo check/build (X-RUST-MODULES) ran in compile windows during it, next to main's heavy formatter job, so part of the contention was mine. Rerun alone at 07:2x: 2/2 in 88 s, at 100% CPU from other lanes. Two runs in a row lost to one 300 s cell timeout each, in different notebooks, so on this shared machine a full local pre-commit needs to run without my own concurrent work (and in a bench window once eoie release E ships). Cells' per-file '--timeout' directive would hide the symptom and CI doesn't need it, so x's sources stay unchanged. Run 4 (also X-RUST-MODULES' condition 5: first full build with rust-modules.ps1 wired in) is queued now, with nothing else of mine running alongside.")
inl note_of_x_done_37 () = Note ("X-DONE", 37u32, "2026-10-22T11:43Z", "x", "2026-10-22 07:43 x: pre-commit run 4 failed on an x wiring bug (see X-RUST-MODULES note 5), fixed; run 5 started 07:42 with ~55 min expected. tibia's live block (~09:45-10:15, ~2.5 h) starts after it, eoie-release-DH and main-pack-review3; x queues no heavy job or compile from then until the block ends.")
inl note_of_x_done_38 () = Note ("X-DONE", 38u32, "2026-10-22T11:50Z", "x", "2026-10-22 07:50 x: resume guide (10), what changed since (1)-(9). (a) Correction to (4)/(9): crate headroom no longer forces inl-to-let work. apps/client/build.ps1 runs scripts/rust-modules.ps1 after each crate's BuildSpiral, so a crate over 1,000 generated lines is split into <crate>_partN.rs (types, pub fns and main stay in the root) before scripts/gate.ps1 runs. inl-to-let is still the way to keep a crate small. When main's compiler split (RUST-MODULE-SPLIT / GENERATED-OUTPUT-MODULES) is installed, the compiler does the split and rust-modules.ps1 is deleted (X-RUST-MODULES note 4). (b) The gate measures every file but Spiral sources, with a data allowlist by name (tlds.txt, Cargo.lock, apps/sim/frames/manifest.toml). The generated sim Gleam is split by scripts/gleam-modules.ps1 into apps/sim/frames/src/xsim_frames/{types,partN}.gleam, and a file over 1,000 lines fails the build. (c) ci.yml's push job writes gh-pages/_deploy.txt and then waits up to 6 min for i574n.github.io/x/_deploy.txt to equal GITHUB_SHA. A red 'verify the deploy is live' step is a Pages delay or outage, not a code regression: rerun the job. (d) Locally, a full pre-commit (scripts/build.ps1, ~53 min alone) needs the box to itself from you. A Cells notebook over 300 s under contention kills the whole build (exsss, schedule on 10-21/10-22); rerun that notebook alone before suspecting the code. (e) apps/sim/build.ps1 -Ladder runs the scale ladder, and scripts/gate.ps1 -Compiler <dll> previews a candidate compiler's crate changes before an install.")
inl note_of_x_done_39 () = Note ("X-DONE", 39u32, "2026-10-22T11:51Z", "x", "2026-10-22 07:51 x: resume guide (10f), correction to (6): X-NOMINAL-POST-ID (Done 10-21 15:39, x-split-fix3), XC005-POSTABLE-BUDGET (installed in main's 10-21 03:19 pack; x saw 0 findings in 36 files) and X-COMPAT-PARAMETERIZE (Done, compiler half checked 10-21 22:23) are finished. The open follow-ups outside x's done are now: SIM-IN-SPIRAL's supervision half (waits KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR), the NEAR and federation items (paused by the user), and main's compiler-level split (RUST-MODULE-SPLIT / GENERATED-OUTPUT-MODULES), after which x deletes scripts/rust-modules.ps1.")
inl note_of_x_done_40 () = Note ("X-DONE", 40u32, "2026-10-22T13:23Z", "x", "2026-10-22 09:23 x: full local pre-commit green on the current uncommitted tree: run 5 (job-1792669355305-x-precommit-day5), exit 0, all D2 gates at target (details in X-RUST-MODULES). X-DONE now waits only on D1: the user's commit and push, then two consecutive green ci runs on main.")
inl note_of_x_done_41 () = Note ("X-DONE", 41u32, "2026-10-22T18:08Z", "x", "2026-10-22 14:08 x: the only known CI red (run 39367603402, windows-2025, e2e step 'xc refresh': a '#< CLIXML' progress record in the step's stderr) was covered by an untested fix. Probe (pwsh 7.5.4, no compiler, the same spawn as Invoke-Step: -NonInteractive -EncodedCommand with redirected stderr): locally Write-Progress never reaches stderr, with or without the uncommitted ProgressPreference line, so the CI failure cannot be reproduced here, and that fix was never actually tested against it. What is reproducible: -EncodedCommand makes pwsh serialize its own streams as CLIXML (Write-Error -> '#< CLIXML <Objs ...'), and '-OutputFormat Text' turns that off (Write-Error -> 'Write-Error: boom'). Native stderr (what xc writes) is identical in both modes. RULE, written before the edit: add -OutputFormat Text if it disables CLIXML, leaves native stderr unchanged, no expected output contains CLIXML or PowerShell-formatted errors (0 matches in README.md and e2e/), and e2e passes locally with it. The first three hold. APPLIED to apps/client/e2e/e2e.ps1 (Invoke-Step's arguments; the ProgressPreference line stays). The e2e run on run 7's xc.exe follows after tibia's block. This closes the CLIXML path whatever pwsh version the runner has and wherever the record comes from (a cmdlet or the host itself).")
inl note_of_x_done_42 () = Note ("X-DONE", 42u32, "2026-10-22T18:10Z", "x", "2026-10-22 14:10 x: commit scope for D1 (git status read at 14:10). The gates passed on the WORKING TREE. 30 files carry newer unstaged changes over their staged copies (both READMEs, apps/client/build.ps1, e2e.ps1, ci.yml, scripts/gate.ps1, apps/sim/build.ps1, 15 regenerated crate .rs files, 7 notebooks incl. the six --timeout lines), and 3 paths are untracked. scripts/rust-modules.ps1 and scripts/gleam-modules.ps1 are called by the client and sim builds, so CI fails without them. apps/sim/frames/src/xsim_frames/ is regenerated by the sim build, but the staged xsim_frames.gleam imports its part modules, so they ship together. So the commit that turns CI green is the whole working tree of x (plus spiral pushed with it, PIPELINES-GREEN note 19), not the current index.")
inl note_of_x_done_43 () = Note ("X-DONE", 43u32, "2026-10-22T18:14Z", "x", "2026-10-22 14:14 x: ci.yml's unstaged delta reviewed (read-only). It adds _deploy.txt with GITHUB_SHA, force_orphan on the gh-pages push, and a 'verify the deploy is live' step that polls the pages URL up to 6 min for that SHA, then requires id=\"readme\" or class=\"readme, and name=\"viewport\", in index.html. No new 'uses:' (8 actions, same as actions.lock), so the startup lock check stays green. x CI renders the page with dir-tree-html cloned from its pushed default branch (spiral scripts/dir-tree-html.ps1), and origin/main c1363bb emits both class=\"readme and name=\"viewport (dir_tree_html.spi/.rs, same as local). So the page check holds for the commit as it stands.")
inl note_of_x_done_44 () = Note ("X-DONE", 44u32, "2026-10-22T18:23Z", "x", "2026-10-22 14:23 x: the commit gives scripts/rust-modules.ps1 and scripts/gleam-modules.ps1 their first CI run, and the first on Linux, since both are untracked today. Reviewed for platform assumptions (read-only). Paths go through Join-Path/Split-Path, and Gleam module paths use '/' (valid on both). Input goes through ReadAllLines (CRLF and LF) and output is written with explicit LF. Sorting is .NET ICU on both OSes. x's scripts and ci.yml have no generated-file drift check (no git diff/status gate), so a regenerated split that differed by platform could not fail CI anyway. No change needed.")
inl note_of_x_done_45 () = Note ("X-DONE", 45u32, "2026-10-22T18:27Z", "x", "2026-10-22 14:27 x: source for note 41. PowerShell commit 4bcecb263d0b (github.com/PowerShell/PowerShell, 2024) changed redirected-progress handling: pwsh started with the XML output format now emits progress as a CLIXML record even when output is redirected, where it used to emit none. The local probe (case E) showed that -EncodedCommand alone already puts the error stream in XML. So the windows-2025 runner's pwsh writing '#< CLIXML ... progress' into a step's stderr, while this machine's 7.5.4 writes none, fits a version-dependent progress path inside the XML format. Forcing '-OutputFormat Text' leaves that format, whatever the runner's version. The ProgressPreference line alone covers only records raised after the script body starts.")
inl note_of_x_done_46 () = Note ("X-DONE", 46u32, "2026-10-22T18:28Z", "x", "2026-10-22 14:28 x: CI time budget for the commit (read-only). The build job has timeout-minutes 120, xc-windows 30, and push 30 (including the 6-min deploy check). The commit takes CI from 11 notebooks to 36. Local run 5 did the whole scripts/build.ps1 in ~76 min at BelowNormal (notebooks 08:22-09:15, slowest cell 144 s of the 300 s per-cell limit), and note 7 of X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED measured CI as roughly 3-5x faster per compile. So the build job should finish well inside 120 min, with room for the setup steps and cold caches. Not measured on CI until the first run after the commit, which then gives the real figure.")
inl note_of_x_done_47 () = Note ("X-DONE", 47u32, "2026-10-22T18:37Z", "x", "2026-10-22 14:37 x: the commit gives the new sim (Spiral bridge, Gleam frames) its first CI run on both OSes. Reviewed for platform assumptions (read-only). daemon_memory.ex is Windows-only (taskkill, a Get-CimInstance Win32_Process sampler), but its one test runs only when :os.type() is win32, and its other caller, mix xsim.run --daemon-memory, is not used by apps/sim/build.ps1, scripts or ci.yml. spiral.ex finds the Gleam ebin dirs relative to __DIR__. Invariants.comparable masks run dirs via invariants.spi's masked_cells, which keys on the 'spiral_cells' marker with either separator, and its own tests (run_dirs_are_masked) cover '\\\\' and '/', so Linux /tmp paths mask the same way. The Windows panic paths in xsim_test.exs:295-297 are fixed test data. No change needed.")
inl note_of_x_done_48 () = Note ("X-DONE", 48u32, "2026-10-22T18:42Z", "x", "2026-10-22 14:42 x: rule check 4 of note 41, partly done without a stub. One real step (xc --help; xc no-such-verb), run with run 7's xc.exe through e2e's exact Invoke-Step spawn, with and without -OutputFormat Text: stdout identical (1364 chars), stderr identical (1415 chars, xc's own error text), exit 2 in both. The full e2e run (stub + 31 steps + 5 cases) still follows after tibia's block.")
inl note_of_x_done_49 () = Note ("X-DONE", 49u32, "2026-10-22T18:44Z", "x", "2026-10-22 14:44 x: note 46's 3-5x CI factor replaced by a measurement. In ubuntu job 39367603402 (HEAD 61b8fff), each notebook's CI wall (runner command line to its spiral-tests total line, matched in order to HEAD's 11-module list) vs run 5's local wall for the same module (gaps between output write times): prelude 20 vs 86 s, glyphs 25/220, protocol 47/210, splitter 23/135, events 23/162, posts 22/166, reading 16/80, cli 37/190, oauth1 9/66, wire 42/219, transport 8/33. Local is 4.1-8.8x slower (median ~5.2x). Overstated: HEAD's notebooks ran mostly zig, the working tree's add gleam/rust targets. Projection: run 5's ~53-min notebook stage is ~6-15 min on the ubuntu runner, inside the 120-min job even if windows-2025 is several times slower. Real figures come from the first CI run after the commit.")
inl note_of_x_done_50 () = Note ("X-DONE", 50u32, "2026-10-22T18:45Z", "x", "2026-10-22 14:45 x: correction to note 47. Its claim that mix xsim.run --daemon-memory is \"not used by apps/sim/build.ps1\" was wrong. The search used a braced multi-path glob that matched no files and returned nothing. apps/sim/build.ps1:53 passes --daemon-memory, but only inside the -Ladder branch (lines 38-70, which returns early), and neither scripts/build.ps1 nor ci.yml passes -Ladder. So the conclusion holds (CI never starts the Windows-only sampler), with this as the evidence. Same review: the default sim path does run real Cells (xsim.run --executor cells on lib math.spi, and xsim.thread_run on oauth1.spi, lines 117-136). Those resolve rustc like the notebooks, so apps/sim/build.ps1 now also sets SPIRAL_RUSTC to Get-XRustc unless it is already set (X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED note 34 rule). Run 8's sim stage verifies it.")
inl note_of_x_done_51 () = Note ("X-DONE", 51u32, "2026-10-22T19:01Z", "x", "2026-10-22 15:01 x: note 41's rule check 4 PASSED. e2e.ps1 with -OutputFormat Text (written 14:07), run directly after tibia's block on run 7's release xc.exe (built 13:45), work dir in TEMP and removed afterwards: 170 requests to the stub, 31 tutorial steps + 5 extra cases, RUST-OK, exit 0 in 145 s. The CLIXML fix is in and verified locally; CI's windows-2025 job confirms it after the commit.")
inl note_of_x_done_52 () = Note ("X-DONE", 52u32, "2026-10-23T00:19Z", "x", "2026-10-22 20:19 x: CI-parity check of the toolchains not covered by notes 41-51, done offline. ci.yml's build job pins Gleam 1.19.2, OTP 29 and Elixir 1.20, and the local ones match (gleam 1.19.2, scoop erlang 29.1.2, elixir 1.20.4). Python differs: 3.15.0 locally, 3.12.10 on CI. In x, Python runs only e2e's X API stub (stub.py, stdlib only). On scoop python312 3.12.10, the stub parses with feature_version 3.12, starts, writes its port file and answers GET /2/users/me with 401 (no auth sent, as expected), and its request log gets one line. So the Python gap is closed for x. Also offline at 20:0x-20:1x: line gate 0 over 1,000, actions.lock covers ci.yml's 8 actions, git diff --check clean, all 36 notebooks in apps/client/build.ps1's list (CI runs the same script through scripts/workflow.ps1).")
inl note_of_x_done_53 () = Note ("X-DONE", 53u32, "2026-10-23T00:42Z", "x", "2026-10-22 20:42 x: D5 resume guide addendum to note 4, from 10-22. (7) To reproduce CI locally, match CI's zig. The local 'zig' is zig-dev 0.18 for the compiler matrix, and CI pins 0.17.0, so set SPIRAL_ZIG to scoop zig 0.17.0 and SPIRAL_CELLS_MEMO=0 (the memo key ignores the zig until CELLS-MEMO-TOOL-IDENTITY lands); Invoke-SpiralTests prints a note when they differ. Never set SPIRAL_RUSTC: Cells pins +nightly-2026-10-18 through the rustup proxy itself, and a direct rustc breaks every rust cell. (8) Start a full Cells run only when no other Cells job is active (note 65's rule; two at once timed prelude out at 300 s). A notebook joins the '///- ... --timeout 600000' list at a slowest cell of >= 135 s quiet or >= 150 s contended, and leaves after two runs below 100 s (X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED notes 27-28). (9) Windows-only red in e2e: its step launcher must keep '-OutputFormat Text' beside -EncodedCommand, or pwsh writes '#< CLIXML' to stderr on the runner (note 41). (10) The gate measures every non-Spiral file at <= 1,000 lines with a named data allowlist (tlds.txt, Cargo.lock, the sim manifest). Generated Gleam is split by scripts/gleam-modules.ps1, and the split modules are committed with the entry module. (11) CI parity beyond zig was checked on 10-22: rust, otp, elixir and gleam match. Python differs (3.15 locally, 3.12.10 on CI) but runs only e2e's stub, which works on 3.12.10 (note 52).")
inl note_of_x_done_54 () = Note ("X-DONE", 54u32, "2026-10-23T04:36Z", "x", "2026-10-23 00:36 x: index check (read-only, git show :<path>). The user has since staged scripts/gate.ps1 and apps/sim/build.ps1. Still missing from the index: the split apps/sim/frames/src/xsim_frames.gleam (the index holds the old ~9,000-line file), its 11 modules under xsim_frames/ (untracked), and scripts/rust-modules.ps1 + scripts/gleam-modules.ps1 (untracked). Committed as staged, CI fails. The staged apps/client/build.ps1 calls rust-modules.ps1 (first failure), the staged apps/sim/build.ps1 calls gleam-modules.ps1, and the staged gate.ps1 measures every listed file, where the indexed Gleam file is over 1,000 lines. 'git add -A' in x fixes all three. Told the user in the session at 00:3x.")
inl note_of_x_done_55 () = Note ("X-DONE", 55u32, "2026-10-23T04:49Z", "x", "2026-10-23 00:49 x: order for the 12 post-commit items filed overnight (all deps=X-DONE, drafts in C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-precommit-next\\drafts). Rule: security and data or money loss first, then silent wrong results, then cleanup; within a tier, the smaller estimate first. 1 X-DEPS-LATEST step 1 (rustls-webpki / webpki-roots patch bumps, 7 RustSec advisories). 2 X-TOKEN-REFRESH-SAFE (lost single-use refresh token). 3 X-PAGING-GUARD (cursor loop on paid reads). 4 X-LOOKUP-LIMITS (silent truncation past 100, failure past 100 users). 5 X-SIM-RUNNER-CRASHES (one executor exception kills a run). 6 X-INVARIANTS-ROW-KIND (+ digest roles). 7 X-READ-BUDGET. 8 X-RUST-MODULES guards. 9 X-DEPS-LATEST steps 2-5 (ureq 3.5.0, Gleam stdlib 1.0.5, CI actions with the siblings, python 3.15). 10 X-THREAD-RESUME. 11 X-E2E-NON-ASCII. 12 X-EMOJI-WEIGHT (with main's Cells and compiler parts). 13 X-LIB-FIRST-PRELUDE (lib additions + corpus check, the largest).")
inl note_of_x_done_56 () = Note ("X-DONE", 56u32, "2026-10-23T13:47Z", "x", "2026-10-23 09:47 x: LANDING PATH for today's drafts, one command once the user's commit is in: C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-precommit-next\\drafts\\land_all.ps1 -Go. It refuses (exit 75) while anything is staged or apps/client, apps/sim/lib or apps/sim/test has unstaged changes. It applies 13 client drafts (paging guard, token refresh, row kinds, lookup limits x2, read budget x3, emoji weight, thread resume x2, stub weighted limit, e2e non-ASCII) plus the sim runner-crash draft, stopping at the first anchor that no longer matches. Then it prints the test order: Cells on the touched notebooks, mix test, release xc + e2e -Accept with a reviewed README diff, build.ps1. Trial on a throwaway git copy of apps/client + apps/sim at 2026-10-23 09:47: all 14 applied, 18 files changed, +503/-42, plus the new runner_crash_test.exs. Without -Go it only reports; against x right now it refuses, as it should (staged index).")
inl note_of_x_done_57 () = Note ("X-DONE", 57u32, "2026-10-23T14:29Z", "x", "2026-10-23 10:29 x: re-checked note 00:36 (read-only git status): STILL TRUE. The index holds 86 files, and apps/sim/frames/src/xsim_frames.gleam is 'AM' (the old ~9,000-line file staged, the split one in the worktree), while apps/sim/frames/src/xsim_frames/ (11 modules), scripts/gleam-modules.ps1 and scripts/rust-modules.ps1 are untracked. A commit of the index as it stands fails CI at apps/client/build.ps1 (rust-modules.ps1 missing), apps/sim/build.ps1 (gleam-modules.ps1 missing) and the 1,000-line gate (the staged Gleam file). 'git add -A' in x before committing fixes all three. This goes first in today's report to the user.")
inl note_of_x_done_58 () = Note ("X-DONE", 58u32, "2026-10-23T14:37Z", "x", "2026-10-23 10:37 x: blocker per open x row (stop rule from memory backlog-always-has-cpu-light). COMPILE, i.e. tibia's live block until ~11:46, then x-world\\after_block.ps1 (formatter, then 20 eoie compile jobs): SIM-WORLD-SPIRAL, SIM-RUNNER-CORE-SPIRAL (checks_parity rust, crash test, peak_results), X-THREAD-RESUME (thread/cli/write + xc compile), LIB-DICTM (sorted keys), X-LIB-FIRST-PRELUDE (delegation copy), X-GENERIC-TO-LIB (unicode, json_text, lib_gaps), SIM-IN-SPIRAL (tree_memory, sim_report, sim_args, sim_run, xsim_entry). HEAVY SLOT, after that: X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED (rule N7, start by 16:30). USER COMMIT ('git add -A' first, note 57): X-CLIENT, X-REPO-TEMPLATE, X-DEPS-LATEST, and the 14 verified drafts via land_all.ps1 -Go (X-PAGING-GUARD, X-TOKEN-REFRESH-SAFE, X-LOOKUP-LIMITS, X-READ-BUDGET, X-INVARIANTS-ROW-KIND, X-SIM-RUNNER-CRASHES, X-E2E-NON-ASCII, X-EMOJI-WEIGHT, X-STUB-WEIGHTED-LIMIT). MAIN'S LANDINGS: dictm (gates apply_sim_modules.ps1), the actor primitives (SIM-IN-SPIRAL's shell), and main's own x_compat/XC005 side of X-EMOJI-WEIGHT. DEPENDENCY: SIM-X-FEATURE-PARITY waits for SIM-IN-SPIRAL; X-BACKEND-MAP and BACKEND-BALANCE switches wait for measurements, which need the above.")
inl note_of_x_done_59 () = Note ("X-DONE", 59u32, "2026-10-23T16:35Z", "x", "2026-10-23 12:35 x: queued eoie heavy job x-landing-trial (job-1792773320782). It proves land_all end to end on a throwaway copy of x before the user's commit: robocopy x (no target/.git) into %TEMP%\\x-land\\run-<stamp>, junction spiral beside it, a fresh git base, land_all -Go (all 14 drafts plus per-account), then x's own apps/client/build.ps1 -fast with only the copy's e2e line switched to -Accept. That runs crate regeneration, the 1,000-line gate, x-compat, cargo build --release, e2e and postability, followed by a verify e2e run and the new README steps' outputs (hana, the 141-CJK refusal, resume 9101/9201, --per-account 1). Script: C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-precommit-next\\drafts\\landing_trial.ps1. It waits for a heavy slot (tibia's and main's long builds hold both). PRE-CHECK done at 12:37: the compiler's --x-compat on the apply-all copy (all 12 client .spi drafts) reports 0 findings in 36 files, so every new code block still fits one post.")
inl note_of_x_done_60 () = Note ("X-DONE", 60u32, "2026-10-23T18:56Z", "x", "2026-10-23 14:56 x: landing trial run 2: land_all -Go applied all drafts to the throwaway copy (20 files, +611/-60, sim crash guard included), then failed at MY script: x's apps/client/build.ps1 takes a value parameter ('param($fast)'), so a bare -fast is 'Missing an argument'. x's scripts/build.ps1 passes '-fast 1'. Fixed and resubmitted as heavy job x-landing-trial-3 (job-1792781764241).")
inl note_of_x_done_61 () = Note ("X-DONE", 61u32, "2026-10-23T19:37Z", "x", "2026-10-23 15:37 x: CORRECTION to note 59's x-compat pre-check ('0 findings in 36 files'). It was invalid. The compiler finds x's postability profile by walking up to x/postability/x.json, and my %TEMP%\\x-all\\client copy has no postability dir beside it, so the profile was probably not applied. build.ps1 guards against exactly this with a 300-weight probe that must produce a finding, and my check had no such positive control. The trial (run 3, in the real repo layout) shows the truth: XC005 overweight at args.spi:53 (thread_payload with the resume arm, 292 > 272) and digest.spi:32 (absorb_entry with the keep argument, 278 > 272). Both drafts are fixed by splitting each block in two (thread_write_payload / thread_payload; primary_of / absorb_entry). The x-compat re-check (copy with postability beside it, plus the probe) waits for main's compiler install.")
inl note_of_x_done_62 () = Note ("X-DONE", 62u32, "2026-10-23T19:51Z", "x", "2026-10-23 15:51 x: landing trial run 3 result, in x's real layout: the 1,000-line gates PASS (16 crates and 117 non-Spiral files, 0 over), and x-compat FAILS with 28 overweight cells. That matches my character-count scan exactly, which also matched the compiler's XC005 weights cell by cell (x's largest passing cell is 272 characters). Fixed with a final land_all step, apply_fit_posts.ps1. It replaces the 8 affected files (cli, args, wire, write, thread, users, net, xc) with re-laid versions, but only if each file's SHA-256 equals the expected post-draft content (drafts\\fit\\expected.tsv), otherwise it refuses. Re-layouts: long strings into helpers, matchers split into read and edit halves, exports moved into helper functions as net.spi already does, overlong tests split, and the command union nested ('OnThread : thread_op' groups the five thread commands). The corrected x-compat check (x-compat-probe positive control fires; x's postability profile beside the copy) gives 0 findings. Running: trial 4 (job-1792785023116) and Cells on the 8 fitted modules (chain11).")
inl note_of_x_done_63 () = Note ("X-DONE", 63u32, "2026-10-23T19:56Z", "x", "2026-10-23 15:56 x: the fit step's own clash scan (against the post-draft base) found 2 shadowing names. wire's new halt_text shadowed paging.halt_text (wire opens paging; different wording: 'repeated X after N'), and users' new wanted_of (i32) shadowed wire.wanted_of (following_cap). In both modules the only callers were the new code, so behaviour was unaffected, but they are renamed (cursor_halt_text, following_wanted) and the fit store is refreshed. Expected hashes are unchanged (same post-draft base). The unopened-import scan shows nothing new.")
inl note_of_x_done_64 () = Note ("X-DONE", 64u32, "2026-10-23T20:25Z", "x", "2026-10-23 16:25 x: the fitted modules PASS Cells on C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-fit\\client (the exact land_all output plus the fit): cli 20/20 (OnThread : thread_op nesting, composed constructors, split tests), args 3/3, wire 23/23 (cursor_halt_text, other_failure), write 6/6 (split resume tests), thread 3/3 (chain refusals and xc_chain export via chain_exports). users, net and digest are next, then the sim and lib checks. Trial 4 (land_all + fit + build.ps1 -fast 1 + e2e -Accept/verify) is running since 16:15.")
inl note_of_x_done_65 () = Note ("X-DONE", 65u32, "2026-10-23T20:57Z", "x", "2026-10-23 16:57 x: all 8 fitted modules PASS Cells (cli 20/20, args 3/3, wire 23/23, write 6/6, thread 3/3, users, net) plus digest (per-account keep + posts_read). So the drafts are verified exactly as land_all + fit would produce them. Trial 4: land_all + fit applied in x's real layout (20 files, +738/-80); build.ps1 -fast 1 still running.")
inl note_of_x_done_66 () = Note ("X-DONE", 66u32, "2026-10-23T21:27Z", "x", "2026-10-23 17:27 x: Landing trial-4 timed out at its 60-min cap mid-build under CPU contention (checks alone took 512 s). It did confirm, in x's real layout after land_all + fit: args, assemble, checks and digest compile. Risk found: args generates 999 Rust lines, one under the 1000-line gate, and rust-modules splits only when a file is over 1000 (digest was split into 86+946+149). Any args addition will trigger a split; that's expected. x-compat and e2e on the landed tree are still unverified end to end; the fit tree's x-compat was 0 with the positive-control probe.")
inl note_of_x_done_67 () = Note ("X-DONE", 67u32, "2026-10-24T00:00Z", "x", "2026-10-23 20:00 x: tonight's post-land scripts are composition-checked. On one copy of the post-fit tree (the fit store's 8 files plus landed paging.spi and README), in landing order: apply_os_boundary_lib (args, xc) -> apply_thread_resume_hint (write, README) -> apply_wire_lib_helpers (wire) -> apply_paging_cursor_walk (paging, thread, wire), the last two against the patched lib copy x-libcopy2. All four applied, and none breaks a later anchor (wire is touched twice). No touched file gains a cell over 272 (XC005; ASCII character count equals x-compat weight). Order and gates are in the evening report: land_all first; deps steps and the two compile-checked scripts next; lib-dependent delegations after main's lands; sim modules after LIB-DICTM, verdict-gated.")
inl note_of_x_done_68 () = Note ("X-DONE", 68u32, "2026-10-24T00:33Z", "x", "2026-10-23 20:33 x: NEXT-SESSION HANDOFF (evening run 18:00-21:00). Pending checks: (1) the rest of chain20, whatever the 20:50 cutoff left unrun: re-run with pwsh %TEMP%\\x-world\\after_block2.ps1 -NotAfter <time> (23 notebooks, most important first; its log is cells_chain20.log). Before that, copy %TEMP%\\x-world\\memory_watch.spi (the sample-source union, 20:39) over %TEMP%\\x-shell\\client\\memory_watch.spi; chain20 checks the older incremental version. (2) after_block_builds.ps1 as one --compile eoie job (cargo check -p xc_net for ureq 3.5.0; gleam build for frames stdlib 1.0.5), not concurrently with a chain. Gates: apply_sim_modules/apply_xsim_crate take -Verdicts <all cells_chain logs> and need fresh passes (read_cells_verdicts.ps1). Landing order for the user: the evening report and X-DONE n67. Lib: LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS (7 batches on %TEMP%\\x-libcopy), x landing patch (%TEMP%\\x-world\\lib-draft\\landing, proof %TEMP%\\x-libcopy2); verdicts go to main. Pre-registered experiments: X-SHELL-SWITCH, X-CORPUS-R1/R2; run8 rule N8 (X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED).")
inl note_of_x_done_69 () = Note ("X-DONE", 69u32, "2026-10-24T03:05Z", "x", "23:04 x grooming, correcting n68: the 'verdicts go to main' line is superseded (user 10-23 21:0x: lanes land their own lib changes). Lib part A (LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS batches 1-7 + x landing patch) is LIVE since 22:06:07 as one eoie do batch; gate green except gleam_os_check's Gleam target (see LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS). Part B (listm' skip_/chunks_) lander job-1792810914164 is running now that main's take_ is live. Still open from n68: chain20 rest (after_block2.ps1, memory_watch copy first) and after_block_builds.ps1. Items at 70% Active are verified drafts waiting for the user to land them in x's staged tree; that blocker isn't mine.")
inl note_of_x_done_70 () = Note ("X-DONE", 70u32, "2026-10-24T03:38Z", "x", "23:38 x overnight queue (run until 07:00). Chain21 (job-1792811662170) re-checks 17 stale notebooks against tonight's live lib: scheduler, runner, sim, client and x-prelude2 step 2. After it come the memory_watch recheck (job-1792812757759; the x-shell package had a parse error in cells_executor's nominal, $'#' must be $'##', now fixed) and x-deps-builds (job-1792813103411, the n68 builds step). Lib: part B lander job-1792810914164 (guarded, waits for readers). normalize_path regex removal: proof job-1792812495018, then baseline job-1792812966216, then the rule-based decision job-1792813040548, which starts its lander only if LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS n17's rule holds. Verdict logs are cells_chain-compatible (the gate prints 'chain / HH:mm:ss starting'), so read_cells_verdicts.ps1 reads them for the landing scripts.")
inl note_of_x_done_71 () = Note ("X-DONE", 71u32, "2026-10-24T04:39Z", "x", "10-24 00:39 x: queue-class correction. My single-notebook Cells checks went through 'eoie job run --heavy', while both heavy slots are held by main's long jobs (hopacsota-promotion up to 6 h, fiscal gate); every x check stalled from 23:36 to 00:35. A one-notebook check is compile-class work (the eoie lane runs it as compile-run), so gate_when_quiet.ps1 now takes a compile window. Stopped and requeued the waiting wrappers (map in %TEMP%\\x-world\\requeue-0036.json): chain21b (16 remaining notebooks), normalize proof2 -> base2 -> decide2 (decide has the new ids), paced-run2, par-fsharp2, regexp-directive2. The run8 decision keeps --heavy, because run8 is a full suite. First result on the new class: memory_watch 3/3.")
