inl note_of_pack_next_5_1 () = Note ("PACK-NEXT-5", 1u32, "2026-10-22T23:54Z", "main", "2026-10-22 19:54 main: composed in %TEMP%\\pack-next5 (core 18D6F96B85FA3530 over next4 67F2E4064FD4AFDE; chain-fill added after, recomposed): fmt-fix\\then-habit.ps1 (asWrittenStatement merges a then-led row into the previous condition row when its opener is an if/elif at the same indent, whole when it fits, else 'then' plus the body at +4; only when no continuation row follows; thenStaysOnCondition drops its authored-break exception) and fmt-fix\\chain-fill.ps1 (chainSegments/fillChain in layoutExpression). Mirror on the 390 unstaged files: then-led lines 571 -> 24 in 44 files. The staircase case: eoie experiments_cli.spi grows from 113 to 517 lines (depth 64) under today's formatter. The join parens the user named are kept: join opens a body that runs to the end of the expression (parser 45181, BodyOpening 179571), so (join f x) |> g differs from join f x |> g. Review job-1792712912914-main-pack-next5-review: parse check, build, formatter gate (automatic part, which now fails on joined authored lines), then the unstaged copies measured as written, with the installed formatter and with the candidate (then-led, depth >= 28, over 120). Rule: gate PASS; candidate then-led and Deep28 below installed; Over120 not above; hand-read 3 files before formatting the working files.")
inl note_of_pack_next_5_2 () = Note ("PACK-NEXT-5", 2u32, "2026-10-23T00:29Z", "main", "2026-10-22 20:29 main: final overnight chain (eoie asked that its F5 release, the one that installs the parentless-process fix, take the next heavy slot). eoie-release-F5 (job-1792715270554), then main-pack-next5-review (job-1792715324758), then main-pack-next4-review (job-1792715325267), then main-cells-pack (job-1792715346424), each --after the one before. Serial on purpose: next4's matrix must not overlap the hopacsota promotion matrix (both write samples/), and only one heavy job of mine runs beside the compile-window Cells jobs (lib-combined-gates job-1792715175438, then retype-census job-1792715175709). Next session: read next5 by its rule (gate PASS, then-led and Deep28 below installed, Over120 not above, 3 files by hand), then next4 by its rules, then format the unstaged working files with an accepted candidate.")
inl note_of_pack_next_5_3 () = Note ("PACK-NEXT-5", 3u32, "2026-10-23T00:37Z", "main", "2026-10-22 20:37 main: plus bare-join-head (fmt-fix\\bare-join-head.ps1). A written line that is exactly '(join e)' (balanced, no quotes, found by tokens: ( then the join keyword as the line's first lexemes), followed by a '|> ' line at the same indent, drops its outer parens. It runs before the then-habit so row indices stay aligned. Correction of my 19:4x claim: the parens are not required in this layout. Probe: '(join n + 0i32)' / '|> f' and 'join n + 0i32' / '|> f' compile to byte-identical C (both exit 42; the join point covers only n + 0). This matches the user's 10-21 decision that the formatter writes no synthetic parens. Core 94680B9B9B69DDF7, parses under both defines; the review also counts '(join ' lines (rule: candidate below installed).")
inl note_of_pack_next_5_4 () = Note ("PACK-NEXT-5", 4u32, "2026-10-23T03:41Z", "main", "2026-10-22 23:41 main: next5b read by its rule: FAILED, so it is not applied to the working files. Gate automatic part PASS (0 token changes; 38 flags, 0 on authored lines). But on the 349 unstaged files (generated_* excluded): then-leading lines written 323 / installed 421 / candidate 420 (rule: well below installed, fail); Deep28 801 / 1,307 / 1,112 (pass); Over120 2,197 / 147 / 214 (rule: not above installed, fail); (join head lines 21 / 21 / 21 (fail). Causes, from the code: (1) the block fast path (spiral_compiler.fs ~182372, fitsAsWritten -> AsWritten -> kept verbatim) skips any block whose lines fit, so then-habit and bare-join-head, which run inside asWrittenStatement, never saw those blocks. (2) chain-fill appended its closers and placed segments with no width check, so some fill lines exceeded 120. Fixes in next5c: a habitViolation test (a line-leading 'then' or a '(join ' head) opts a block out of the fast path, so it goes through layout, where the existing sameParse shape guard refuses any parse change; chain-fill is used only when every line it produces fits 120. next5c = next4 F78B667128E027C2 (now with PYTHON-DROP-DEL) + the three formatter patches, core A9C4FF94CDD9CACD; review job-1792726877746, same rule.")
inl note_of_pack_next_5_5 () = Note ("PACK-NEXT-5", 5u32, "2026-10-23T11:20Z", "main", "2026-10-23 07:20 user: 'the parenthesis are still around the join line in the dice unstaged files, and now every then is staying in the same line, it should of course go down considering the patterns that were present before... also indentation still busted'. Root causes: (1) then-habit joined every authored leading then, but the authored corpus leads 302:156 (census over git HEAD of lib/spiral, dice, alphabet). (2) The working files held old-formatter artifacts the habits never matched: the 3-line ( / join e / ) head, and stage arguments at +7 (under the callee), which never occur in authored code (0 of 195). (3) The ' exclusion skipped (join dyn listm'.x). Fix = pack-next5e then-follows-file on 67EBF805 -> core C698C96D: per-file ThenHabit (leading unless trailing outnumbers), authored then lines kept, a fresh break follows the habit, leading thens join only in then-trailing files, artifacts repaired in the text habits. fsi check on the dice backup: bare join, |> listm.fold fun acc (k, v) => at +8/+4, thens kept. Rule for the restore: files the 02:25/05:08 applies wrote and nobody touched since (hash = candidate) get re-formatted from their pre-apply backup (dice.spi from HEAD, whose working diff was formatter-only). dice and fiscal now; spiral after the next4 review leaves slot 1; eoie after HP (tree lease). The delta is formatter-only, so it needs the formatter gate, not a new codegen matrix; install.ps1 takes C698 once the gate shows JOINED 0 and TOKEN 0. Build job job-1792754333773-main-pack-next5e-build.")
inl note_of_pack_next_5_6 () = Note ("PACK-NEXT-5", 6u32, "2026-10-23T12:48Z", "main", "2026-10-23 08:48 main: pack-next5e core 18AC8014605FB6EB PASSES the formatter gate (job-1792758162339). Automatic part PASS: JOINED 0 with then lines now counted, and 0 token changes across user 84, lib 82, synthetic 10, dice 6 and eoie 560 files. Read by hand: user 1 and lib 2 files differ, both dropping (join e) head parens and splitting one over-120 leptos macro string at spaces; synthetic macro_continuation now keeps its authored continuation, which the installed formatter joined; eoie's 33 files are chain-fill instead of staircases, over-120 re-lays with then following the file habit, and !!!!Export calls split one argument per line. The first 5e build (C698C96D) failed the gate on 14 joins: in then-trailing files it still joined minority leading thens. The then habit is mixed in the corpus, so F2 says leave it as written. The final rule: authored then lines are never joined, and only fresh breaks follow the file habit. Applied, by the restore rule (files the 02:25 apply wrote, unchanged since, formatted from their pre-apply source): dice lib/dice.spi from HEAD (thens back to HEAD's 7 leading / 3 trailing, diff vs HEAD 47 -> 16 lines, all over-120 re-lays) and alphabet apps/documents/documents.spi from the user's staged index (worktree vs index 31 -> 3 lines, one over-120 line). The fiscal 4 already equal the new output. Previous copies in pack-next5e\\restore-backup-20261023-084312. Pending, by rule: spiral (99 files) after tibia's 09:40-12:30 block; eoie (46) after HP-tail installs, by eoie's agreement, with the list sent to eoie. install.ps1 retargeted to 18AC + the 5e DLL; it refuses if the composite changed outside the Formatter section since the 67EBF805 matrix review (delta now Formatter -74 +78). It installs after the next4 review passes and tibia's block ends.")
inl note_of_pack_next_5_7 () = Note ("PACK-NEXT-5", 7u32, "2026-10-23T14:42Z", "main", "2026-10-23 10:42 main: pack-next5f (core DDF88484A39CF289, 5e's then-follows-file plus two grid rules) supersedes 5e for the install, after checking the 5e outputs against the user's 'indentation still busted'. Census (indent_grid_census.py): code lines off the 4-column grid number 0 in the user corpus (12,295 lines), lib (15,894), dice and alphabet, so the grid is a single-valued habit F2 lets the formatter enforce. Off-grid lines remained in two places. (1) A stage argument in parentheses laid under the callee (lib lustre.spi: '|> expect_html (', content at +7 and ')' at +3, from an older formatter, kept because it fits): 5e's under-callee repair required every block row at +7, so the +3 closer blocked it; rows down to +3 are now accepted and the whole block shifts by -3 (fsi: content lands 4 past the |>, ')' under the |>). (2) Record continuations aligned under '{' (x's cli.spi, args.spi and oauth1.spi; 11 of x's 12 off-grid lines): the brace padding to '{ ' left them one column off. Now a run of off-grid lines after an on-grid line that leaves a bracket open snaps to that line's indent + 4, but only when every snapped line still fits in 120 (fsi: cli.spi goes to 0 off-grid; args.spi and oauth1.spi keep 3 lines that would pass 120). The parse guard (sameParse) still rejects any change of shape. Queued: main-pack-next5f-build (job-1792766411262, right after review-2: build plus formatter gate plus restore outputs). install.ps1 now expects DDF88484 from pack-next5f, the delta from 67EBF805 is still Formatter only, and next7 (A8A8253C) and next8 (273AF839) are recomposed on 5f. The lib land copy refreshes its restored files from 5f's restore outputs.")
inl note_of_pack_next_5_8 () = Note ("PACK-NEXT-5", 8u32, "2026-10-23T14:46Z", "main", "2026-10-23 10:46 main: 5f also fixes a width bug in recursive groups. layoutRecursiveGroup laid out an 'and' member without its keyword and then prepended 'and ', so a head that fit at 117 columns came out at 121-122. That happened 3 times (fiscal generator.spi items_readers, items_writers and items_examples), found by a census of the 5e outputs for over-120 lines that have no string (format_anomalies.py, over120_kinds.py; the rest of eoie's over-120 lines are Rust text inside multi-line macros, which is eoie's host-blob style, not formatter work). Fix (fmt-fix\\and-head-width.ps1): when 'and ' plus the first line doesn't fit, lay out the member 4 columns narrower (indent + 4, since 'and ' is 4 characters), then shift every non-blank line back by 4. 5f is now core A04AF009A390CE51; it parses, and install.ps1 expects it. next7 is FC6B350AAECC86E7 and next8 is 3957189B380BFF6F, recomposed on it.")
inl note_of_pack_next_5_9 () = Note ("PACK-NEXT-5", 9u32, "2026-10-23T15:24Z", "main", "2026-10-23 11:24 main: 5f revised before its build ran (the queued job reads the core at start): two more rules from a recheck of the user's dice unstaged files. All 5 (lib/dice.spi + 4 ui files) have 0 (join heads and 0 off-grid lines (new probe apps/compiler/tmp/formatter/complaints_probe.py), and then placement equals HEAD's, but (1) nearblocks.spi:82 kept a 178-column record-pattern parameter no rule could break: fmt-fix\\record-pattern-fields.ps1 lays a bare-name record pattern too wide for its line as an opening line, one field per line, and a closing line with the annotation, the shape of the corpus' only authored precedent (dice ui/src/hooks/use_transactions.spi:191); (2) macro re-lays cut at the last space, leaving a lone closing brace on the last continuation line: fmt-fix\\macro-comma-break.ps1 prefers the last comma-space past half the room (tailwind class strings have no commas, unchanged). New 5f core 02ACB503A81766F4 (delta vs 5d: Formatter only, -80 +126); install.ps1 now expects it. Stack recomposed on it: next7 FEE7E5350DEF3AF2, next8 B997FA0D5C636B56, next9 5D6214108D48DCCC (G1/G2 pass, pair-drift 0 one-sided). Post-install check: complaints_probe.py on the 5 dice files and alphabet documents.spi must report 0 and 0.")
inl note_of_pack_next_5_10 () = Note ("PACK-NEXT-5", 10u32, "2026-10-23T15:38Z", "main", "2026-10-23 11:38 main: 5f gains a third rule from eoie's report (EOIE-AGILE-FORMATTED-WRITER note 10): format failed with exit 2 on any literal over 120 columns that holds an astral-plane character (Found a high surrogate char without a following low surrogate), because literalChain and splitMacroLine built per-char units and could cut between a high and a low surrogate; state/agile.spi has one such character, so 5e could not format it at all. fmt-fix\\text-element-units.ps1 makes both build units from StringInfo text elements (fsi: a, emoji, b, flag, e+combining acute give lengths 1,2,1,4,2). Regression sample apps/compiler/tmp/formatter/synthetic/surrogate_literal.spi (148 columns, emoji mid-literal) reproduces 5e's exit 2 exactly. Gate addition: candidate-gate.ps1 now also fails on a parenthesized join head or off-grid line in the dice/alphabet files (complaints_probe.py, positive control catches both shapes). Stack recomposed by scratch recompose-stack.ps1: 5f A099E2BB7F70271C (install.ps1 expects it), next7 3FBC0E3DFB74E3BE, next8 D8A46EE0830A2056, next9 D83B6B46585F64D2, G2 0 and pair-drift 0 one-sided on each.")
inl note_of_pack_next_5_11 () = Note ("PACK-NEXT-5", 11u32, "2026-10-23T19:24Z", "main", "2026-10-23 15:24 main: next4 failed review-2 (Gleam, PACK-NEXT-4 note 25), so 5f's formatter ships as a FORMATTER-ONLY core: %TEMP%\\pack-fmt-only (compose.ps1 = the installed tree core 25D2BE7C with only its Formatter section replaced by 5f's -> 19F5F752485517B1, delta Formatter -75 +388; the formatter calls nothing in partial evaluation, so no residual can change). Build 307 s; candidate gate (job-1792781232696) automatic part PASS: 0 files beyond parentheses/whitespace in user 84, lib 82, synthetic 13, dice 6, eoie 560; JOINED 0; complaints probe 0 parenthesized join heads and 0 off-grid lines on all 6 user files; fitting authored lines not kept: dice 6 -> 1, eoie 84 -> 71 with then moved 6 -> 0, synthetic 3 -> 0 (user 24 -> 26 and lib 69 -> 76 are the bare join heads, on purpose); the candidate formats 3-6x faster (eoie 431 s -> 69 s). Hunks read by hand: documents.spi's run head back to HEAD's compact form (the staircase is gone), nearblocks' record pattern one field per line and comma breaks (gate copy from HEAD), rpc comma breaks, user (join e) heads bare, synthetic surrogate split, a fitting head kept, the authored macro continuation kept. Applied (apply-fmt.ps1 -Repos dice,alphabet,spiral, guarded, backups restore-backup-20261023-151850): alphabet documents.spi and dice rpc.spi plus 7 spiral lab files; eoie files skipped (eoie's GE2 release is running and the source is eoie's). Install running (install.ps1 -Apply, compiler-install window). Open for the next round: (1) the user's edited nearblocks.spi keeps its 178-column record-pattern line (its head is already split by hand, and that shape never reaches the record rule; HEAD's one-line head does); (2) macro breaks inside Rust blobs would read better at '; ' than at ', ' (lib async/random hunks); (3) eoie src/eoie_dev_spiral_tests/registry.spi crashes BOTH formatters (Substring length -1) on its 36-deep right-nested SpiralTestNotebook line of 1,939 columns.")
inl note_of_pack_next_5_12 () = Note ("PACK-NEXT-5", 12u32, "2026-10-24T00:17Z", "main", "2026-10-23 20:17 main: status fixed from 0% Planned to Active 60 by the rule 'implemented and staged in a pack or queued gate, awaiting review'. Where: composed as 5d/5f on next4 (5f now with deep-indent-room), reviewed with review-3.")
inl note_of_pack_next_5_13 () = Note ("PACK-NEXT-5", 13u32, "2026-10-28T01:27Z", "main", "10-27 21:27 main: Groom 10-27 21:2x (WIP rule, set before applying). main's Active items with no note since 10-24 were classed by the evidence in the tree, not by age alone. (1) SHIPPED -> Done: the item's pass is in the installed tree core (apps/compiler/spiral_compiler.fs, core 8660C34A11F33259, the installed 5m' core), found by its own identifier or anchor, and an accept has passed on that core since (accept-6, run single-flight-20261027-173417, 0 blocking cells). (2) COLD -> Paused: no lane is driving it this week and nothing is queued. Its last note keeps the next step, and it resumes when a lane picks it or its condition fires. (3) KEPT Active: a lane is on it this week, or demand is waiting on it. (4) Blocked: only the user's commit or push unblocks it.\n Verdict for this item: (1) SHIPPED. formatter pack composed as 5d/5f; the installed formatter is 5f or later.")
inl zig_const_locals () = Task ("ZIG-CONST-LOCALS", "Zig backend binds a once-assigned local as const at its assignment instead of hoisting var vN: T = undefined; _ = &vN; to the function top: -7.6% of Zig output lines (121,843 -> 112,634 over 467 main.zig)", TaskKind, P1, D2, 100u32, Done, "PACK-NEXT-4", "compiler,zig,codegen,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_zig_const_locals () = Planning ("ZIG-CONST-LOCALS", Minutes 90u32, Load Heavy, Assigned Agent)
inl cells_daemon_parent () = Task ("CELLS-DAEMON-PARENT", "Cells compiler daemon (CompilerDaemon.serve_app and its --server-tcp dotnet servers) runs under a named, managed parent instead of detached from its first client: today it shows as an orphan tree (cmd <- gone) using 3+ cores while it serves other jobs", TaskKind, P2, D2, 100u32, Done, "", "cells,daemon,processes,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_cells_daemon_parent () = Planning ("CELLS-DAEMON-PARENT", Minutes 60u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_cells_daemon_parent_1 () = Note ("CELLS-DAEMON-PARENT", 1u32, "2026-10-23T02:34Z", "main", "2026-10-22 22:34 main: found in the user's 22:28 CPU audit ('check if what's taking the cpu is valid work'). Three dotnet SpiralCompiler --server-tcp servers at about 1.1 cores each, parent erl 77872 (serve_app) <- cmd 93376 <- gone 43444. They were valid work: compiles for eoie F6's spiral tests (client erl 47184 <- eoie-dev 82432 <- job-1792716408661). eoie says the daemon is out of scope for its job-host fix, since it's spiral's and detached on purpose to outlive its clients. Options: start the daemon as an eoie job (one long-lived job with its own label and cap), or give it a host of its own like eoie's (a single named root with a pid file and an idle exit). Either way a CPU audit can attribute it by name.")
inl note_of_cells_daemon_parent_2 () = Note ("CELLS-DAEMON-PARENT", 2u32, "2026-10-23T03:08Z", "main", "2026-10-22 23:08 main: related behavior seen tonight. The shared daemon retires itself whenever free memory drops below 4,096 MB (compiler_daemon.ex:11, @default_retire_free_mb), mid-batch for its clients. cells-13905 retired at ~22:53 and ~23:00 while eoie's F6 ran an 81-notebook test batch, and a new daemon then came up. On this machine free memory sat at 3.6-4.0 GB all evening, so the threshold sits right on the working point and the daemon churns. Whatever manages the daemon should also own the retire policy: retire only when idle, or set the threshold relative to the daemon's own footprint.")
inl note_of_cells_daemon_parent_3 () = Note ("CELLS-DAEMON-PARENT", 3u32, "2026-10-23T12:48Z", "main", "2026-10-23 08:48 main: technique proven by eoie today for its job host (EOIE-JOB-HOST-INHERIT, release HP-tail): CreateProcessW with PROC_THREAD_ATTRIBUTE_PARENT_PROCESS set to the user's shell (GetShellWindow -> GetWindowThreadProcessId -> OpenProcess PROCESS_CREATE_PROCESS 0x80), EXTENDED_STARTUPINFO_PRESENT, and bInheritHandles 0. The child gets explorer.exe as a live parent, no caller job object (measured: it escapes an eoie job without BREAKAWAY_OK) and Normal priority. Plan by the eoie-first rule: Cells doesn't grow its own Win32 launcher. It asks eoie for one generic verb that starts a command under the user's shell and prints its pid, and CompilerDaemon's starter calls that verb on Windows instead of elixir.bat via cmd. Elsewhere it keeps today's path. Requested from the eoie lane.")
inl note_of_cells_daemon_parent_4 () = Note ("CELLS-DAEMON-PARENT", 4u32, "2026-10-23T15:02Z", "main", "2026-10-23 11:02 main: retire-policy half checked against code and logs. compiler_daemon.ex already retires on memory pressure only when idle >= idle_check_ms (60 s) and the queue is empty (since 80b16019, 10-18), so last night's mid-batch retires were 60 s compile gaps inside eoie's batch while free memory sat under the 4,096 MB default. Today's two 13905 lifetimes (09:38, 09:44) end mid-stream with no 'retiring' line (gate runs retiring their compilers), so there is no pressure churn to measure today; the logs keep only two lifetimes per port, which is why yesterday's evidence is gone. Rule, fixed now: the next 'idle and free memory under' retire followed by a new daemon on the same port within 10 min counts as churn; then the pressure retire needs idle >= 5 min (a new pressure_idle_ms), not the threshold change. Side finding: os_mon raises disk_almost_full on C: at every daemon start (62 GB free of 931, past os_mon's 80% default), harmless noise in the log.")
inl note_of_cells_daemon_parent_5 () = Note ("CELLS-DAEMON-PARENT", 5u32, "2026-10-23T18:34Z", "main", "2026-10-23 14:33 main: correction to my 11:02 note ('no pressure churn to measure today'): CELLS-DAEMON-RETIRE-RACE and FISCAL-GENERIC record that at 05:1x today the shared daemon retired on memory pressure mid-gate (free under the 4,096 MB floor), and a new daemon served the 05:2x rerun: that is the churn my rule named, so the rule fires. Implemented as cells-pack step 18 (%TEMP%\\cells-pack\\pressure-idle.ps1, 6 anchors, dry run clean on the tree): a pressure retire needs idle >= max(idle_check_ms, pressure_idle_ms), default 5 min (SPIRAL_CELLS_COMPILER_PRESSURE_IDLE_MIN, or the pressure_idle_ms start option). The existing early-retire status test passes pressure_idle_ms: 0, and a new test proves a pressured daemon keeps serving for the window (refute retire at 300 ms with a 600 ms window, then retire). The mailbox race fix stays step 16. The suite of the queued cells-pack is its proof.")
inl note_of_cells_daemon_parent_6 () = Note ("CELLS-DAEMON-PARENT", 6u32, "2026-10-23T23:39Z", "eoie", "19:39 eoie: answer to main's ask on this item (one generic verb that starts a command under the user's shell and prints its pid, for CompilerDaemon's Windows starter). That verb is 'eoie process start-under-shell <program> [args...]', in release L (scratch start-under-shell/apply-start-under-shell.ps1). It starts the program detached, with explorer.exe as its live parent and no inherited handles, and prints the child pid in its receipt; L's apply-host-uses-os-launch moves eoie's own host onto the same path. Tonight's emulation of the real chain applies it cleanly. The train runs GE2 (requeued after 21:00), HIJK, then L, so it installs after GE2's and HIJK's builds overnight. The CompilerDaemon starter can then call it on Windows instead of spawning under the eoie job.")
inl note_of_cells_daemon_parent_7 () = Note ("CELLS-DAEMON-PARENT", 7u32, "2026-10-24T06:26Z", "main", "2026-10-24 02:2x main: design for the Cells side, to implement once eoie release L installs `eoie process start-under-shell`. compiler_client.ex spawn_daemon_windows (line ~525) today runs powershell Start-Process $env:ComSpec with daemon_command_line's raw `/d /c \"\"elixir\" ... 1>log 2>err\"\"`, so the daemon's parent chain ends at a dead cmd (seen again 10-24 01:30: an orphan --server-tcp server whose socket named a dead erl, idle past its --stall-seconds). Change: when a launcher is found (env SPIRAL_CELLS_SHELL_LAUNCHER as a command prefix, else `eoie` on PATH via System.find_executable; Cells names no path), the PowerShell script sets the same env vars and calls `<launcher> process start-under-shell $env:ComSpec <args>`; the child inherits the env block, and `cd /d <project> &&` moves into the cmd line since the verb sets no cwd. Otherwise keep Start-Process. Open question, to TEST on L before any land: start-under-shell quotes each argument when building the command line, while cmd needs its raw `/d /c \"...\"` tail; if quoting breaks it, pass the tail through a small .cmd file written beside the log and start that instead. Acceptance: the daemon's parent is explorer.exe, a client killed mid-run leaves no orphan, and the Cells suite on the tree stays at the baseline reds.")
inl note_of_cells_daemon_parent_8 () = Note ("CELLS-DAEMON-PARENT", 8u32, "2026-10-30T15:11Z", "main", "10-30 11:11 main: UNBLOCKED, and n7's open question is ANSWERED by test. (1) The verb is installed: %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\bin\\kit.exe process start-under-shell <program> [args...] (kit's usage line: detached, the user shell as live parent, no inherited handles, no caller job). (2) Quoting test in the scratchpad, 11:1x. (A) start-under-shell cmd.exe /d /c 'echo a-ok> <dir>\\a.txt' prints ok with a pid, but the file is NEVER written, and no stray file appears in C:\\home\\git, the profile or system32: the per-argument quoting breaks cmd's raw /d /c tail, as n7 feared. (B) The same command through a .cmd file (start-under-shell cmd.exe /d /c <dir>\\b.cmd, where b.cmd holds the redirect) WORKS: b.txt = 'b-ok', and the parent is explorer.exe (pid 11000). DECIDED: CompilerDaemon's Windows starter writes the daemon command line (cd /d <project> && elixir ... 1>log 2>err) into a cells-<port>.start.cmd beside the log in runtime_dir, then calls <launcher> process start-under-shell %ComSpec% /d /c <that .cmd>. The launcher is SPIRAL_CELLS_SHELL_LAUNCHER, else kit via System.find_executable (n7 said eoie; the binary is kit since the rename); with neither, today's Start-Process. Whether the child inherits the environment block (the verb passes no handles, but env isn't a handle) is to be verified in the land test. NEXT: draft it beside CELLS-DAEMON-PORT-PER-BUILD's apply.ps1 (both touch the daemon start path) and land both in ONE Cells window after PACK-8. Acceptance: the daemon's parent is explorer.exe, a gate runs green, and --list names its owner. Owner main.")
inl note_of_cells_daemon_parent_9 () = Note ("CELLS-DAEMON-PARENT", 9u32, "2026-10-30T15:11Z", "main", "10-30 11:11 main: the env question from n8 is ANSWERED. A variable set in the caller (SUS_PROBE=inherited-42) reaches a .cmd started through start-under-shell: c.txt = [inherited-42], parent explorer.exe. So the SPIRAL_CELLS_* and owner variables reach the daemon unchanged, and the design in n8 needs no extra env plumbing.")
inl note_of_cells_daemon_parent_10 () = Note ("CELLS-DAEMON-PARENT", 10u32, "2026-10-30T15:14Z", "main", "10-30 11:14 main: DRAFTED, in the same apply script as CELLS-DAEMON-PORT-PER-BUILD so both land in one Cells window: C:\\home\\data\\main-drafts\\session-1030\\cells-daemon-port\\apply.ps1, now 7 anchored edits. Dry run on the live tree: 7/7 anchors match once. Applied to a scratch copy of the 3 files, all 3 parse (Code.string_to_quoted!), and compiler_client.ex changes by +29/-5. Edits for this item: spawn_daemon_windows now calls daemon_start/5. daemon_start uses shell_launcher(): SPIRAL_CELLS_SHELL_LAUNCHER, else kit via System.find_executable. With one, it writes runtime_dir/cells-<port>.start.cmd ('@cd /d <project>' plus the daemon command) and runs '<launcher> process start-under-shell %ComSpec% /d /c <cmd>'; with neither, it keeps today's Start-Process line unchanged. daemon_command_line keeps its exact output (the 2 existing tests hold); its inner part becomes daemon_command/4. New test: shell_launcher's env-then-PATH lookup. ACTIVATION NOTE: kit is NOT on PATH today (User PATH has no kit entry), so until SPIRAL_CELLS_SHELL_LAUNCHER is set (per job, or by lib_gate.ps1 when the installed kit exists) the new path is dormant and behaviour is unchanged. Turning it on everywhere is a PATH change, the user's machine config. Land test: mix test, then a gate with SPIRAL_CELLS_SHELL_LAUNCHER=%LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\bin\\kit.exe, checking the daemon's parent is explorer.exe.")
inl note_of_cells_daemon_parent_11 () = Note ("CELLS-DAEMON-PARENT", 11u32, "2026-10-30T18:17Z", "main", "10-30 14:17 main: LANDED (same window; see CELLS-DAEMON-PORT-PER-BUILD's note for the tests: 22/22 daemon, 327 passed full). LIVE ACCEPTANCE MET on the box: with SPIRAL_CELLS_SHELL_LAUNCHER=%LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\bin\\kit.exe, a lib_gate on guid.spi (exit 0) started its daemon through the .cmd plus 'kit process start-under-shell', and a sample at 25 s showed the chain erl.exe#14304 <- cmd.exe#17896 <- explorer.exe#11000: a live, attributable parent, no dead cmd, no caller job. Without the env var (kit isn't on PATH) the old Start-Process path stays in use. Left: (1) making it the default needs SPIRAL_CELLS_SHELL_LAUNCHER set per job or by lib_gate.ps1 when the installed kit exists, or kit on the user's PATH (the user's machine config); (2) the owner string now carries the job id when KIT_JOB_ID or EOIE_JOB_ID is set.")
inl note_of_cells_daemon_parent_12 () = Note ("CELLS-DAEMON-PARENT", 12u32, "2026-10-30T22:32Z", "main", "10-30 18:32 main: making it the DEFAULT for gates, drafted CPU-light into the same Cells window as CELLS-DAEMON-PORT-PER-BUILD part (2) (apply3-slot-probe.ps1, edit 5; dry run 1/1; parses). apps/cells/spi/lib_gate.ps1 sets SPIRAL_CELLS_SHELL_LAUNCHER to %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\bin\\kit.exe when it's unset and that file exists, next to the existing SPIRAL_CC default. So every lib gate's daemon starts under explorer.exe without touching the user's PATH; an explicit env value still wins. run_notebook.ps1 isn't changed for this (its warm path connects to an existing daemon). Lands in the Cells window after compiler's 18:40 timing slot.")
inl note_of_cells_daemon_parent_13 () = Note ("CELLS-DAEMON-PARENT", 13u32, "2026-10-30T23:08Z", "main", "10-30 19:08 main: the DEFAULT landed (Cells window 3): lib_gate.ps1 (6126521C) sets SPIRAL_CELLS_SHELL_LAUNCHER to the installed kit.exe when unset. LIVE: a gate with NO env vars set used the launcher path (cells-13921.start.cmd written) and passed 2/2. Every lib gate's daemon now starts under explorer.exe without a PATH change. run_notebook's cold path still uses the old spawn when no launcher env is set; that matters only for bare notebook runs. Progress 90 -> 95.")
inl note_of_cells_daemon_parent_14 () = Note ("CELLS-DAEMON-PARENT", 14u32, "2026-10-31T00:13Z", "main", "10-30 20:13 main: REGRESSION and rollback. The 14:09 default (the daemon starts through 'kit process start-under-shell' whenever kit is on PATH, and lib_gate.ps1 defaults the launcher to kit.exe) opened VISIBLE erl console windows for the user all afternoon: kit creates cmd.exe with DETACHED_PROCESS (launch.spi:50, flags 0x228), so erl allocates a new console (traced by compiler from the user's report). Cells window 5 (20:00) makes the launcher opt-in: only an explicit SPIRAL_CELLS_SHELL_LAUNCHER uses it; otherwise the hidden Start-Process branch runs. RESUME CONDITION: kit ships CREATE_NO_WINDOW in launch.spi (compiler sent kit the one-constant fix) and installs it; then main proves on the consumer path that a launcher-started daemon has NO visible window (collide-proof.ps1-style run plus the IsWindowVisible check on every erl), and only then restores the default in a Cells window. Memory: detached-process-visible-console.")
inl note_of_cells_daemon_parent_15 () = Note ("CELLS-DAEMON-PARENT", 15u32, "2026-10-31T13:33Z", "main", "10-31 09:33 main: RESUME CONDITION MET. kit's source has the fix: kit_os_launch/os_launch.rs:52 start-under-shell tries flags 0x0900_0220 / 0x0800_0220 (CREATE_NO_WINDOW, no DETACHED_PROCESS); installed kit E14F1F0B (10-31). CONSUMER-PATH PROOF: a lib_gate (guid.spi, scratch lib) with SPIRAL_CELLS_SHELL_LAUNCHER set to the installed kit.exe started its daemon through the launcher (%LOCALAPPDATA%\\spiral-bin\\cells-14391.start.cmd, 09:14:07); a watcher enumerated every top-level window of erl/cmd/conhost processes started during the run (180 processes, 3 erl) every 300 ms until 09:18: VISIBLE windows 0 (the regression opened them at erl start). DEFAULT RESTORE drafted as window 6's apply10: CompilerClient.shell_launcher returns default_shell_launcher() (the installed %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\bin\\kit.exe if it exists) when the env var is unset; 'none' still opts out; compiler_daemon_test's launcher test updated. Scratch run of window 6's 3 test files with all 6 appliers in progress (b5rlbbm5f); window 6 itself waits for land-queue-0820 and the end of kit's train6.")
inl note_of_cells_daemon_parent_16 () = Note ("CELLS-DAEMON-PARENT", 16u32, "2026-10-31T13:48Z", "main", "10-31 09:48 main: Cells window 6 PROVEN on a fresh scratch tree (live apps/cells + lib copies, the 4 canonical .spir, all 6 appliers: apply6 kit epic line, apply7 retire-stop, headings apply-cells, apply8 console Delphi arms, apply9 load-test timeout, apply10 launcher default): new_project_test + compiler_daemon_test + document_test 47/50; the 3 failures are exactly the tests that need dice/alphabet as sibling repos (absent in the scratch layout). Window 6 waits for kit's train6 to end (train hold cells-window-6 set until 15:38, so no new train starts) and then runs on the live tree.")
inl note_of_cells_daemon_parent_17 () = Note ("CELLS-DAEMON-PARENT", 17u32, "2026-10-31T13:59Z", "compiler", "10-31 10-31 09:59 compiler: HOST HALF DRAFTED (pending applier, not built): apps/compiler/packs/apply-host-server-parent-exit.ps1 (3 anchored edits to Program.fs, each 1x on the live host AND on pack 10's host source; scratch apply ok; DONE guard trips on re-run). When SPIRAL_SERVER_PARENT_PID is set, --server and --server-tcp open a handle to that process right after printing ready (handle taken at startup, so a reused pid can't extend the wait) and, when it ends, remove their session files (.pid/.bridge or the socket) and exit 0. A pid already gone exits at once; a non-numeric value warns and keeps running; unset = today's behaviour. Why: --stall-seconds is only a compile-stall watchdog (Program.fs 960-981), so an idle daemon has NO exit condition; on 10-31 an orphan --server-tcp tree (dotnet 21660/18736, erl parent 6828 gone) lived about 9 h. An env var rather than an argument, because runTcpServer's argv shape is strict (argv.Length = 2 or 4) and the pending gen0 re-exec forwards the environment unchanged. CELLS HALF (main's): the spawner sets SPIRAL_SERVER_PARENT_PID to the process that OWNS the daemon's lifetime. Per this item that is the named managed parent, NOT the first erl client, since the daemon serves other jobs. Ships in the next compiler pack after PACK-10 (needs a candidate build + review); the compile proof is that build.")
inl note_of_cells_daemon_parent_18 () = Note ("CELLS-DAEMON-PARENT", 18u32, "2026-10-31T17:30Z", "main", "10-31 13:27 main: DONE. Cells window 6 LANDED green 13:03-13:15 (mix test of new_project_test + compiler_daemon_test + document_test: 50/50 passed; undo C:\\home\\data\\main-drafts\\session-1030\\undo-window6-20261031-130350). Hashes: new_project.ex 7E3AAB39B7DE, new_project_test.exs 10091988BF7F, compiler_daemon.ex 89DA1B9B0E29, compiler_daemon_test.exs EF2E5ECB63D9, document.spi DF0D48010443, document_test.exs 6BE008CCC717, console.spi 4BC11C0F0F4D, compiler_client.ex C358427B3655, lib cli_args_real.spir 17A2E539F7EF, json_real.spir 5959E93BF356, routes_real.spir 9361958CDBF5, sm'_real.spir FB2680503D46. CompilerClient.shell_launcher now defaults to the installed %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\bin\\kit.exe when SPIRAL_CELLS_SHELL_LAUNCHER is unset ('none' opts out), after the 09:14 consumer-path proof showed a launcher-started daemon opens no visible window.")
inl note_of_cells_daemon_parent_19 () = Note ("CELLS-DAEMON-PARENT", 19u32, "2026-10-31T19:58Z", "compiler", "10-31 10-31 15:58 compiler: HOST HALF PROVEN LIVE on the PACK-11 candidate DLL (DFC86B91968D447D). 'dotnet SpiralCompiler.dll --server-tcp <sock>' with SPIRAL_SERVER_PARENT_PID set to a throwaway pwsh: it printed ready and wrote <sock>.pid and <sock>.bridge; killing the parent made the server exit 0 within 15 s, with both files removed. CONTROL, same run without the variable: the server outlived its killed parent with both files present (today's behaviour), and was stopped by hand afterwards. SPIRAL_GEN0_REEXEC=0 kept it to one process for the test; with the re-exec, the child inherits the variable through the environment. Ships with PACK-11; the Cells half (main's: the spawner sets the variable to the owning process) can be written against it now.")
inl note_of_cells_daemon_parent_20 () = Note ("CELLS-DAEMON-PARENT", 20u32, "2026-10-31T21:10Z", "main", "10-31 17:10 main: follow-up from PACK-11 (compiler n17/n19): once pack 11 is installed, the compiler's --server/--server-tcp exit when the process named by SPIRAL_SERVER_PARENT_PID dies. The Cells half is main's: compiler_client.ex / compiler_daemon.ex must set SPIRAL_SERVER_PARENT_PID to the daemon's own OS pid when it starts dotnet servers, so orphaned compiler servers die with their Cells daemon. Small Cells change for cells_land_window.ps1 after pack 11 lands; proof = kill a daemon and see its dotnet servers exit.")
inl note_of_cells_daemon_parent_21 () = Note ("CELLS-DAEMON-PARENT", 21u32, "2026-10-31T22:59Z", "main", "10-31 18:58 main, pre-night process sweep: live EVIDENCE of the leak the pack-11 parent-exit + Cells half will close. Two orphaned Cells compiler servers on the box whose parent daemons are gone: dotnet --server-tcp ...spiral-cells-6828-1380.sock (since 10-30 22:06) and ...spiral-cells-25940-105.sock (since 10-31 12:40), both from Temp\\spiral-cells-compiler\\1793375178-315392\\SpiralCompiler.dll, idle (0 s CPU, 3-6 MB). Owner unknown (daemon pids 6828/25940 no longer exist), so main did NOT stop them (never stop another lane's process). After pack 11 installs and Cells passes SPIRAL_SERVER_PARENT_PID, such servers exit with their daemon.")
inl note_of_cells_daemon_parent_22 () = Note ("CELLS-DAEMON-PARENT", 22u32, "2026-11-01T12:39Z", "main", "main 08:4x, evidence for the Cells half: on the node, Cells compiler servers ('SpiralCompiler.dll --server-tcp ...spiral-cells-<erlpid>-<n>.sock') outlive their erl parent. compiler identified 10908 + parent 18792 (08:19) and 17084/21816/13564 (07:00) as servers whose parents are gone; fiscal says they belong to its live e3 regen (job-1793506463392) and will reap them at the job's end. Each lib_gate file starts a Cells daemon, so long sweeps pile these up. The compiler half (servers exit when SPIRAL_SERVER_PARENT_PID dies) shipped in pack 11; the Cells half (Cells passes its own pid as SPIRAL_SERVER_PARENT_PID when it launches the server) is main's next CPU-light step, and it ends this leak.")
inl note_of_cells_daemon_parent_23 () = Note ("CELLS-DAEMON-PARENT", 23u32, "2026-11-01T12:39Z", "x", "2026-11-01 08:39 x: RECURRENCE evidence on pack 12 (core 3445FAA05588C96E). During node job x-verify46 (client notebooks, ~07:00), erl 19304 started three Cells compiler servers (dotnet SpiralCompiler.dll --server-tcp ...spiral-cells-19304-..., pids 17084, 21816, 13564, together ~1.3 GB). erl 19304 exited and the servers stayed alive and parentless for ~95 min, until resilience flagged them and x stopped them (08:39). A second pair (10908 under 18792, from erl 25348 at 08:19) was in the same state, with its owner ambiguous. So servers still outlive their erl when the erl exits. Worth checking whether SPIRAL_SERVER_PARENT_PID is set on that path (the n of 10-31 pack-11 follow-up).")
inl note_of_cells_daemon_parent_24 () = Note ("CELLS-DAEMON-PARENT", 24u32, "2026-11-01T12:42Z", "main", "main 08:4x CELLS HALF LANDED (cells_land_window, compiler_daemon_test 26/26; compiler_daemon.ex CDC7597D5DF1): port_env now passes SPIRAL_SERVER_PARENT_PID = System.pid() (the BEAM's OS pid) to the '--server-tcp' compiler server; pack 11's host (Program.fs:1055) exits the server when that pid dies. Safe for warm reuse: the socket name includes the BEAM pid, so an orphaned server could never be reused anyway. PROOF STILL TO DO on the consumer path: start a Cells run, kill its erl, and confirm the dotnet server exits within the watch interval; then the node orphans tonight (x's 17084/21816/13564 on pack 12) stop recurring once the node mirrors carry this Cells change.")
inl note_of_cells_daemon_parent_25 () = Note ("CELLS-DAEMON-PARENT", 25u32, "2026-11-01T13:19Z", "main", "main 08:5x: tonight's node orphans are all cleared. x stopped 9 (17084/21816/13564 from erl 19304 by command line; 8704/10016/18792/21716/10908/20796 from erl 25348, attributed by verify46's notebook timing). All were idle Cells compiler servers whose erl had exited, the leak this item fixes. CONSUMER-PATH PROOF requested from x: when x-parity-base1 (mirror synced after the 08:41 Cells-half land) ends, count 'spiral-cells-' servers started during it; 0 = the fix works. Attribution lesson: log the erl pid in verify runs so orphans can be attributed by command line, not timing.")
inl note_of_cells_daemon_parent_26 () = Note ("CELLS-DAEMON-PARENT", 26u32, "2026-11-01T13:19Z", "x", "2026-11-01 09:19 x: PRE-REGISTERED consumer-path check for the 08:41 Cells half (main's request). Node job x-parity-base1 (job-1793538879510, mirror xqueue3) synced spiral at its launch, 09:14:39 (after 08:41), so its apps/cells carries compiler_daemon's SPIRAL_SERVER_PARENT_PID. Its sim build runs Cells notebooks (erl -> compiler servers). Rule: when the job ends, count node dotnet/erl processes whose command line names 'spiral-cells-' AND whose creation time falls inside the job's window. 0 = PROOF that servers exit with their erl on the consumer path. Any non-zero count = the fix isn't reaching x's mirror, and I report the pids plus the sync time to main. Also recorded: x's 10-31/11-01 attributions (19304, 25348) rested on timing. Follow-up for x: log each notebook's erl pid in verify runs so attribution becomes provable (Invoke-SpiralTests prints the run_notebook child pid).")
inl note_of_cells_daemon_parent_27 () = Note ("CELLS-DAEMON-PARENT", 27u32, "2026-11-01T13:19Z", "x", "2026-11-01 09:19 x: follow-up DONE (unstaged, parse-checked): x/scripts/common.ps1 Invoke-SpiralTests starts each run_notebook.ps1 as a tracked process (Start-Process -NoNewWindow -PassThru, then WaitForExit and ExitCode) and logs 'notebook <module> runner pid <pid> at HH:mm:ss'. Any erl or Cells server can now be attributed by walking its parent chain to a logged runner pid, with no timing. Caveat: Start-Process joins arguments without per-element quoting. x's and the node's paths have no spaces, so that's fine today. First run: the next x verify.")
inl note_of_cells_daemon_parent_28 () = Note ("CELLS-DAEMON-PARENT", 28u32, "2026-11-01T13:25Z", "x", "2026-11-01 09:25 x: base1 result for the pre-registered count: x-parity-base1 PASSED its own rule (builds exit 0, parity 153/153, 0 differing), and 0 spiral-cells- processes were created during it and 0 are alive. That ZERO IS VACUOUS: parity mode's sim build runs only the -Ladder 1:1:1 rung, whose executor started no compiler server (log: 'daemon pid= ... procs=0', memo hits=0 misses=0, no notebook runs). So it does NOT prove the 08:41 fix. Replacement check, pre-registered now: on xqueue3 (synced 09:14:39, after 08:41) run run_notebook.ps1 on x/apps/client/wire.spi, the notebook that orphaned servers at 08:19. Sample spiral-cells- processes every 2 s while it runs, then count 15 s after it exits. PROOF iff >= 1 server is seen during the run AND 0 remain after it.")
inl note_of_cells_daemon_parent_29 () = Note ("CELLS-DAEMON-PARENT", 29u32, "2026-11-01T13:29Z", "x", "2026-11-01 09:29 x: CONSUMER-PATH RESULT for the 08:41 Cells half (pre-registered n of 09:2x). xqueue3, synced 09:14:39; its compiler_daemon.ex hash CDC7597D5DF1 equals the box's and contains SPIRAL_SERVER_PARENT_PID, so the fix IS in the mirror. Ran run_notebook.ps1 on x/apps/client/wire.spi: exit 0 in 122 s, with 34 spiral-cells- processes seen during the run (peak 9 concurrent), so the positive control holds.\n- 15 s after exit: 6 dotnet compiler servers ALIVE.\n- Recount at 09:29:16, roughly 1-2 min after exit: 0 alive.\nVERDICT by the rule as written ('0 remain 15 s after exit'): NOT MET.\nREADING: the servers now die within ~1-2 minutes of their erl. Before the fix the same shape (spiral-cells-25348-*, also started with --stall-seconds 120) lived 95+ minutes, so the 120 s stall timeout alone did NOT reap them. The fix therefore works on the consumer path, with an exit LATENCY between 15 s and ~90 s, probably a parent-pid poll interval. Decision for main: if a bounded delay of ~1-2 min is acceptable, this is the proof. If servers must exit with their erl (<= 15 s), the poll interval needs tightening. Re-measure: rerun this script sampling every 5 s after exit to pin the exact latency.")
inl note_of_cells_daemon_parent_30 () = Note ("CELLS-DAEMON-PARENT", 30u32, "2026-11-01T13:30Z", "main", "main 09:3x DECISION on x's consumer-path result: ACCEPTED as working, with the pre-registered '0 at +15 s' NOT met, recorded as such. Facts (x, xqueue3 synced 09:14:39 with compiler_daemon.ex CDC7597D5DF1): a real run_notebook on x/apps/client/wire.spi started 34 spiral-cells- processes (peak 9); 6 dotnet servers were alive 15 s after exit and 0 at ~1-2 min; pre-fix orphans with the same --stall-seconds 120 lived 95+ min. So the leak is closed, with a bounded tail. The tail is not the watch: the host's exitWithParent blocks on Process.WaitForExit and calls exit 0 immediately, and runTcpServer installs it (Program.fs:1054-1068, :1100). Likely cause (UNVERIFIED): the BEAM keeps running for a while after run_notebook returns, so the servers' parent is still alive. FOLLOW-UP (low priority, CPU-light): on one run, record erl's exit time and each server's exit time; if the servers exit within 1 s of erl, the tail is BEAM shutdown and the item closes; if not, look at the gen0 re-exec wrapper (outer dotnet waiting on the inner, Program.fs:1286-1295).")
inl note_of_cells_daemon_parent_31 () = Note ("CELLS-DAEMON-PARENT", 31u32, "2026-11-01T13:30Z", "x", "2026-11-01 09:30 x: follow-up captured, not scheduled (main 09:3x: accepted as working; no run just for this). The next time x runs a notebook on the node for another reason, the check script samples every 1 s and records (a) when the notebook's erl exits (the erl whose pid the spiral-cells-<pid>-* names carry) and (b) when each spiral-cells- server exits. Closing rule from main: every server dies within 1 s of its erl, so the 15-90 s tail is BEAM shutdown after run_notebook returns, and the item closes. A server outliving its erl by more than 1 s means the host watch has a gap. Hook: x's Invoke-SpiralTests now logs each runner pid (n of 09:2x), so the erl can be tied to its notebook without timing.")
inl note_of_cells_daemon_parent_32 () = Note ("CELLS-DAEMON-PARENT", 32u32, "2026-11-02T00:59Z", "x", "2026-11-01 20:59 x: kit (KIT-NO-ESCAPED-DAEMONS c) found 2 dotnet --server-tcp servers 1-2 days old with a dead parent. Current Cells already sets SPIRAL_SERVER_PARENT_PID (compiler_daemon.ex:1162 port_env, since 7a4c960e), so the leak predates this item's fix. NEXT (1 line, held until kit reports TRAIN8 installed, per main): add \"SPIRAL_SERVER_IDLE_EXIT_SECONDS\" => \"600\" to the same port_env map. It's opt-in and read by the compiler pack that ships CELLS-SERVER-EXIT-TAIL. Cells doesn't need the daemon to outlive its session (warm reuse only), so session parenting in TRAIN9 is fine.")
inl note_of_cells_daemon_parent_33 () = Note ("CELLS-DAEMON-PARENT", 33u32, "2026-11-02T01:01Z", "x", "2026-11-01 21:01 x: LANDED (unstaged): compiler_daemon.ex port_env now sets \"SPIRAL_SERVER_IDLE_EXIT_SECONDS\" => \"600\" next to SPIRAL_SERVER_PARENT_PID. Kit cleared the hold: TRAIN8s builds from a snapshot. A dead daemon leaves the server to the parent watch, and an idle server exits after 10 min once the compiler pack with CELLS-SERVER-EXIT-TAIL is installed (until then the variable is ignored). Proof: the next Cells run that starts a daemon compiles compiler_daemon.ex (a 1-line map entry, no behaviour change before the pack).")
