inl note_of_eoie_heavy_one_limit_1 () = Note ("EOIE-HEAVY-ONE-LIMIT", 1u32, "2026-10-23T03:56Z", "eoie", "23:56 eoie: found when F7 started (23:51): 'Invoke-Heavy: acquired slot 2 ... (held at most 240 min)'. eoie_machine_windows heavy_wrapper takes the job timeout and discards it (inl _ = timeout), so every --heavy job gets the gate's default MaxMinutes 240 (SPIRAL_HEAVY_MAX_MINUTES unset). The heavy-queue watchdog kills the job's whole process tree at that deadline, whatever eoie's limit says. F7 (limit 6 h) would have been killed at 03:52, possibly mid release build. Done tonight: stopped F7's own watchdog, pid 46596, verified as a child of F7's heavy wrapper 120228 with deadline 03:51:58; F7's eoie supervisor still enforces its 6 h limit. Fix drafted for release H: tree-read/apply-heavy-one-limit.ps1 (wrapper passes -MaxMinutes timeout/60000+1, the test's fake gate takes the parameter, and the heavy-job CLI test asserts '-MaxMinutes 1 {' and 'held at most 1 min'). Dry run on the GE2+H copy is clean; chained into apply-rH.")
inl note_of_eoie_heavy_one_limit_2 () = Note ("EOIE-HEAVY-ONE-LIMIT", 2u32, "2026-10-24T03:06Z", "eoie", "23:06 groom: the slice tree-read\\apply-heavy-one-limit.ps1 (run by HIJK's apply-rH) left heavy_wrapper's concat5 with 4 arguments; the Q typecheck caught it 22:41 in crowding.spi. Fixed on the slice (backup .before-arity-fix); the Q typecheck passes 10/10 since. Ships with HIJK.")
inl note_of_eoie_heavy_one_limit_3 () = Note ("EOIE-HEAVY-ONE-LIMIT", 3u32, "2026-10-24T11:23Z", "eoie", "07:23 groom: blocked on the train. The only work left is to install stage HIJK and run its after-install check, so 'agile next' must not hand this out as ready work. Back to Active when stage HIJK installs.")
inl note_of_eoie_heavy_one_limit_4 () = Note ("EOIE-HEAVY-ONE-LIMIT", 4u32, "2026-10-29T06:57Z", "main", "10-29 02:5x main: SHIPPED and checked after install. The installed kit.exe (10-28 05:25) passes the job timeout through. Both of main's --heavy jobs tonight, job-1793253922878-main-oracle-review-1 and job-1793254803476-main-oracle-review-1b, were started with timeout_ms=10800000 (180 min), and each printed 'Invoke-Heavy: acquired slot 1 after 0 s (held at most 181 min)'. That is n1's -MaxMinutes timeout/60000+1, not the gate's default 240, so the two limits are one. Done.")
inl x_paging_guard () = Task ("X-PAGING-GUARD", "x client: pagination stops on a repeated next_token and at a page cap, so an API cursor loop cannot spin xc or burn paid reads", Story, P2, D2, 100u32, Done, "X-WIP-VERIFY", "x,client,api", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_x_paging_guard () = Planning ("X-PAGING-GUARD", Minutes 40u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_x_paging_guard_1 () = Note ("X-PAGING-GUARD", 1u32, "2026-10-23T03:58Z", "x", "2026-10-22 23:58 x: found by a read-only review. paging.spi:14-17 'paged' calls fetch with each next_token until the token is empty, with no guard. A cursor that repeats (the API returning a token already seen, or a cycle) makes xc thread and every paged read loop forever, spending paid X API reads on each request. The tests (paging.spi:24-38) cover two pages and an error, not a loop. Fix in the domain: keep the seen tokens; a repeat returns a typed error (e.g. CursorRepeated token) naming the page count; add a page cap (a named constant or a parameter, a few hundred for xc's thread reads) that returns CursorBudget. Tests: a pager that returns 't1' forever must stop with CursorRepeated after 2 fetches; a pager with an endless fresh token must stop at the cap. Also minor: 'listm.append acc [p.body]' is O(n^2) over pages; cons and reverse once.")
inl note_of_x_paging_guard_2 () = Note ("X-PAGING-GUARD", 2u32, "2026-10-23T04:06Z", "x", "2026-10-23 00:06 x: a draft fix is at C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-precommit-next\\drafts\\X-PAGING-GUARD.md (not compiled). It adds a cursor_halt union (CursorRepeated / CursorBudget), a walk state record, a 500-page budget and a 'walker' record so paging stays generic over the caller's error, plus 4 tests with hand traces. Its post weights were checked without a compiler via 'xc post-thread --dry-run' on paging_draft.spi: 22 posts, max weight 248, no cell split (the first version had one 301-weight definition and was split). Measured constraint for wire.spi: union failure (224 chars) and describe_failure (263) have no room for an inline-record case or one more arm, so the draft names a halted_cursor record and suggests passing whole records to the describers. Open at compile time: top-level 'let rec ... and ...' and mixed record punning.")
inl note_of_x_paging_guard_3 () = Note ("X-PAGING-GUARD", 3u32, "2026-10-23T04:33Z", "x", "2026-10-23 00:33 x: the three Spiral drafts (paging, token, invariants) pass x-compat with 0 findings ('mix xsim.x_compat' on the extracted .spi files, Cells' checker for hashtag, mention, cashtag and autolink hazards). Their post weights were checked before with xc post-thread --dry-run. Cost note: a fresh MIX_BUILD_PATH made mix compile kino, spiral_cells and xsim (about 107 .ex files) first; that ran outside any live block or compile window.")
inl note_of_x_paging_guard_4 () = Note ("X-PAGING-GUARD", 4u32, "2026-10-23T09:05Z", "x", "2026-10-23 05:05 x: the draft now COMPILES, partly verified. C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-precommit-next\\drafts\\apply_paging_guard.ps1 applies it to a client package (each anchor asserted exactly once). On a scratch copy (C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-draftcheck\\client, packageDir made absolute; x's tree untouched), the Spiral compiler built thread/thread.spi to Rust in 15 s (49 KB) under 'eoie window run compile' (a window granted at 4.1 GB free, then released). The cursor messages, the conversation resource and the 500 budget appear in the output, so the new paging walk, wire's CursorHalted / cursor_text and thread's halted_in were type-checked. Two fixes came from the run-up: the names stepped / finished / continued clashed with exsss.spi:54 and splitter.spi:104/109 and are now walk_step / walk_bodies / walk_on. NOT yet verified: the 4 new paging test blocks (Cells only) and cargo build of the generated crate. Both go in the first pre-commit after applying.")
inl note_of_x_paging_guard_5 () = Note ("X-PAGING-GUARD", 5u32, "2026-10-24T07:24Z", "x", "10-24 03:24 x: LANDED in C:\\home\\git\\x's working tree (unstaged layer over the user's staged commit; X-DONE n74). Left: run8 on the real tree after tibia's block (N8), then the user commits.")
inl note_of_x_paging_guard_6 () = Note ("X-PAGING-GUARD", 6u32, "2026-10-24T11:19Z", "x", "10-24 07:19 x: in HEAD 6ef9c48 (user commit 05:20). x-verify-landing2 (07:08) compiled all 16 crates and passed the line gate, then failed only on x-compat. The 3 findings came from my prelude step 1 and the heal: autolinks listm.map/resultm.map in prelude.spi, and wire.spi replied_to_in at weight 290 > 271. All 3 were fixed unstaged at 07:05, and x-compat now finds 0 in 36 files. Done = job-1792840102746-x-verify-landing3 exits 0 (regen, gate, x-compat, release build, e2e -Accept, scripts/build.ps1).")
inl note_of_x_paging_guard_7 () = Note ("X-PAGING-GUARD", 7u32, "2026-10-28T10:59Z", "x", "2026-10-28 06:50 x: CLOSED by X-WIP-VERIFY's pre-registered rule. verify25 (node job-1793174907446, pinned compiler 4FD712E9E357) GREEN on every clause of X-WIP-VERIFY note 6: exit 0; sim accept and plain sim build exit 0 (ExUnit 45/45, corpus commute ok, thread_run pass, tamper rejected with same_cells=false); client gate 29 crates 0 over 1000; x-compat 0 in 43 client and 24 sim files; all 42 client and 23 sim notebooks passed, 0 failing totals; plain-pass e2e 37 of 37 tutorial steps same, 0 FAILED, 153 stub requests. Outputs landed three-way (node-proof -Land x-verify25: 21 files plus 19 Gleam modules, 0 conflicts; net.rs held back because its source changed after the sync, so verify26 lands it).")
inl x_token_refresh_safe () = Task ("X-TOKEN-REFRESH-SAFE", "x client: xc refresh never loses the rotated refresh token (atomic replace of the env file; new tokens kept somewhere on write failure)", Story, P2, D2, 100u32, Done, "X-WIP-VERIFY", "x,client,auth", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_x_token_refresh_safe () = Planning ("X-TOKEN-REFRESH-SAFE", Minutes 45u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_x_token_refresh_safe_1 () = Note ("X-TOKEN-REFRESH-SAFE", 1u32, "2026-10-23T04:00Z", "x", "2026-10-23 00:00 x: found by a read-only review of token/token.spi. 'refreshed' reads the env file, then 'exchanged' posts the refresh token to X, which rotates it server-side (X's OAuth 2.0 refresh tokens are single-use), then 'saved' writes the file. write_text (line 20) is std::fs::write: it truncates and then writes, with no temp file and rename. On failure, line 72 returns only 'could not write <path>' (exit 4), and the new access and refresh tokens are dropped. After that, the file holds the now-invalid old token (if the open failed) or a truncated file (if the write failed midway), and only a fresh browser OAuth authorization recovers. Fix: write to <path>.tmp in the same folder, flush, then std::fs::rename over the original (rename replaces an existing target on Windows too). If that fails, write the new pair to <path>.renewed next to it and name that file in the exit-4 message, never print secrets to the console (memory x-api-credentials). Minor: split_on on '\\n' keeps '\\r' on CRLF env files and rewritten() rejoins with '\\n', so a CRLF file comes back mixed. Keep the file's line ending. Tests: a read-only target path keeps the old file intact and writes .renewed; a CRLF file round-trips.")
inl note_of_x_token_refresh_safe_2 () = Note ("X-TOKEN-REFRESH-SAFE", 2u32, "2026-10-23T04:08Z", "x", "2026-10-23 00:08 x: correction to note 1's CRLF point. key_line (token.spi:45-47) keeps a trailing '\\r' on the lines it replaces, so an existing CRLF file keeps its endings for the two token keys. Only a key that with_key APPENDS (the file had no X_OAUTH2_REFRESH_TOKEN line yet) gets a bare '\\n'. So it is minor: append with the file's own line ending. The lost-token issue stands unchanged.")
inl note_of_x_token_refresh_safe_3 () = Note ("X-TOKEN-REFRESH-SAFE", 3u32, "2026-10-23T04:09Z", "x", "2026-10-23 00:09 x: a draft fix is at C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-precommit-next\\drafts\\X-TOKEN-REFRESH-SAFE.md (not compiled). It adds boundary primitives synced_write (File::create + write_all + sync_all) and renamed (std::fs::rename, which replaces on Windows and Unix); 'replaced' writes <path>.tmp and renames it over; on failure, saved -> kept_aside writes <path>.renewed and names it, never printing secrets. Appended keys keep the file's line ending, and a test plan is included. Post weights checked with 'xc post-thread --dry-run': 11 posts, max 267, no cell split (the first version's kept_aside spilled and was split into set_aside / token_lost). Open at compile time: '&&' short-circuit on the Rust backend, and the '?' inside the closure of the one-expression primitive.")
inl note_of_x_token_refresh_safe_4 () = Note ("X-TOKEN-REFRESH-SAFE", 4u32, "2026-10-23T09:07Z", "x", "2026-10-23 05:07 x: the draft now COMPILES, partly verified. C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-precommit-next\\drafts\\apply_token_refresh_safe.ps1 applies it with asserted anchors; on a scratch copy (C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-draftcheck2, x untouched) the Spiral compiler built token/token.spi to Rust in 15 s (26 KB) under an eoie compile window. sync_all, std::fs::rename, the .renewed sidecar and the 'authorize again' message are in the output, so synced_write / renamed / file_replaced / kept_aside / line_end were type-checked. A pre-run name check renamed 'replaced' to 'file_replaced' (world.spi:113 has a 'replaced'; token.spi does not open world, but the clash was a trap). NOT yet verified: rustc on the generated code (the '?' inside the closure of synced_write) and a test of the failure path. Both go in the first pre-commit after applying.")
inl note_of_x_token_refresh_safe_5 () = Note ("X-TOKEN-REFRESH-SAFE", 5u32, "2026-10-24T07:07Z", "x", "10-24 03:07 x review (CPU-free): the atomic replace holds on Windows. file_replaced = synced_write(path.tmp) (File::create + write_all + sync_all) and then std::fs::rename(tmp, path). On Windows, std's rename is MoveFileExW with MOVEFILE_REPLACE_EXISTING, and the temp file sits in the same directory, so it's a same-volume replace; a crash leaves either the old file or the complete new one. The known failure (the destination opened without FILE_SHARE_DELETE, e.g. by an editor) makes rename return Err, and the draft then keeps the renewed tokens in the aside file and prints where (line 34), so the rotated token is never lost. Nothing to change. Directory fsync doesn't apply on NTFS.")
inl note_of_x_token_refresh_safe_6 () = Note ("X-TOKEN-REFRESH-SAFE", 6u32, "2026-10-24T07:24Z", "x", "10-24 03:24 x: LANDED in C:\\home\\git\\x's working tree (unstaged layer over the user's staged commit; X-DONE n74). Left: run8 on the real tree after tibia's block (N8), then the user commits.")
inl note_of_x_token_refresh_safe_7 () = Note ("X-TOKEN-REFRESH-SAFE", 7u32, "2026-10-24T11:19Z", "x", "10-24 07:19 x: in HEAD 6ef9c48 (user commit 05:20). x-verify-landing2 (07:08) compiled all 16 crates and passed the line gate, then failed only on x-compat. The 3 findings came from my prelude step 1 and the heal: autolinks listm.map/resultm.map in prelude.spi, and wire.spi replied_to_in at weight 290 > 271. All 3 were fixed unstaged at 07:05, and x-compat now finds 0 in 36 files. Done = job-1792840102746-x-verify-landing3 exits 0 (regen, gate, x-compat, release build, e2e -Accept, scripts/build.ps1).")
