inl formatter_speed () = Task ("FORMATTER-SPEED", "SpiralCompiler format runs in time linear in the file size: a 287 KB generated module takes 555 s today (about 90 ms per line)", Story, P1, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "team:main,formatter,performance", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_formatter_speed () = Planning ("FORMATTER-SPEED", Minutes 120u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_formatter_speed_1 () = Note ("FORMATTER-SPEED", 1u32, "2026-10-23T06:12Z", "main", "2026-10-23 02:12 measured on generated_mdfe_v1_00.spi (FISCAL-GENERATOR-FORMATS note 2). Shapes are parsed per block (layout -> Joined -> blockShapes over the block's own lines), so a per-file quadratic is not obvious from the code; each block may parse up to 4 candidate layouts (preferred, plain, habits, chain fill). Next: a scaling probe on the first quarter of the same file (about 140 s means linear with a large constant, about 35 s means quadratic in file size), then a sampled profile of one run.")
inl note_of_formatter_speed_2 () = Note ("FORMATTER-SPEED", 2u32, "2026-10-23T06:15Z", "main", "2026-10-23 02:15 main: scaling probe: the first quarter of generated_mdfe_v1_00.spi (1,600 of 6,216 lines, 70 KB) took 169 s wall and 37 s CPU, against 555 s wall for the whole file, so time grows linearly (3.3x for 4x the lines). Wall time is mostly BelowNormal starvation on the loaded box (4.6x the CPU time). The real cost is a constant of about 23 ms of CPU per line, roughly 150 s of CPU for this module. Next: a sampled profile on a quiet box (dotnet-trace or a stopwatch build) to find which of flatten / blockShapes re-parses / layout candidates dominates.")
inl note_of_formatter_speed_3 () = Note ("FORMATTER-SPEED", 3u32, "2026-10-23T06:47Z", "main", "2026-10-23 02:47 main: profiled (dotnet-trace, scratch tool path, on the quarter file): 94% of the worker thread is sameParse -> blockShapes -> shapeOf -> shapeSteps, which renders each parsed block to a shape string by F# reflection. Per node it ran isPosition (IsRecord + GetRecordFields + sort) and up to 3 GetUnionFields (foldedConcat, rawSeqHalves, then the node itself). Fix: fmt-fix\\shape-cache.ps1 classifies each runtime type once (ShapeKind in a ConcurrentDictionary: position, enum, union with PreComputeUnionTagReader + per-case PreComputeUnionReader, tuple/record precomputed readers, pair, array, enumerable) and the helpers match on it. The type-test order is unchanged. Equivalence and speed (fsi harness over the installed SpiralCompilerCore's own parse results: 60 lib/spiral files + the fiscal quarter, 2,252 blocks): 2,252 shapes byte-equal, rendering 378,818 ms -> 2,175 ms (174x). Composed into pack-next5d after chain-fill; composite 372640E632978A3E parses under both defines. The next4-composite review's formatter gate is the end-to-end check.")
inl note_of_formatter_speed_4 () = Note ("FORMATTER-SPEED", 4u32, "2026-10-23T09:46Z", "main", "2026-10-23 05:46 main: first end-to-end number from the composite's formatter gate (same box, same run, user corpus 84 files): installed 87.5 s, candidate with the shape cache 55.8 s (-36% wall, 1.57x). Smaller than the 174x on shape rendering alone, because a whole run also pays process start, lexing, parsing and layout per file, and the user files are small. On the large generated module (where shapes were 94% of the profile) the gain should be close to that share; a timed rerun of generated_mdfe_v1_00.spi with the installed composite measures it.")
inl note_of_formatter_speed_5 () = Note ("FORMATTER-SPEED", 5u32, "2026-10-23T09:49Z", "main", "2026-10-23 05:49 main: end-to-end on the large generated file, same box, same minute, BelowNormal, first 1,600 lines of the saved original generated_mdfe_v1_00.spi. Installed formatter: 100 s wall, 34.1 s CPU. Composite candidate (67EBF80563E2239A, shape cache + the reviewed habits): 10 s wall, 5.0 s CPU. That is 6.8x less CPU and 10x less wall time, with byte-identical output. Per line: 21 ms down to 3.1 ms of CPU, so the 300k generated lines drop from about 1.8 h to about 16 min of CPU, which makes FISCAL-GENERATOR-FORMATS' format-after-generate step viable once the composite installs. (A first attempt timed a 3-line placeholder: the running fiscal family gate had replaced the live module, so that number was discarded.)")
inl note_of_formatter_speed_6 () = Note ("FORMATTER-SPEED", 6u32, "2026-10-23T09:51Z", "main", "2026-10-23 05:51 main: composite review's formatter gate (job-1792748200944, same run, same box), installed vs candidate. user 84 files: 87.5 s vs 55.8 s. lib 82 files: 274.5 s vs 44.5 s (6.2x). synthetic 10 files: 2.7 s vs 3.5 s (tiny files, process start dominates). Every corpus is at 0 files differing beyond parentheses and whitespace. The lib figure matches the 6.8x CPU on the generated module: larger files gain the most.")
inl note_of_formatter_speed_7 () = Note ("FORMATTER-SPEED", 7u32, "2026-10-30T14:17Z", "compiler", "10-30 10:1x compiler: DONE, measured on the installed compiler (core 9C899B30495F7DC5, the same hash on box and node; the shape cache is in the live core at spiral_compiler.fs:180175-180187). Node job job-1793369749168-compiler-fmt-full, format --check, SPIRAL_FORMAT_PARALLEL=1, generated_mdfe_v1_00.spi: the first quarter (101,098 B, 2,367 lines) took 2.0 s CPU / 3.9 s wall; the whole module (419,911 B, 9,467 lines; it has grown from the title's 287 KB) took 7.8 s CPU / 10.4 s wall. 4.0x the input for 3.9x the CPU: linear, as the title asks. Before the fix the module took 555 s. Both runs exit 0; the over-120 lines it lists are --check findings, not failures (layout stays FORMATTER-KEEP-BREAKS's concern). Script: scratchpad night\\fmt-full.ps1.")
inl matrix_rustc_codegen_units () = Task ("MATRIX-RUSTC-CODEGEN-UNITS", "Matrix Rust rows compile with -C codegen-units=1 if a quiet-box A/B confirms it is faster for single-file programs", TaskKind, P2, D1, 0u32, Planned, "", "team:main,matrix,rust,performance,main", "current", "current")
