inl note_of_eoie_crate_gate_6 () = Note ("EOIE-CRATE-GATE", 6u32, "2026-10-31T23:48Z", "kit", "10-31 19:5x kit, pause re-decision (main pre-night groom; R3 is now \"never auto-pause for silence\"): Paused -> PLANNED. It was paused by the 10-28 silence groom (\"nothing queued\"), not by a blocker, and no note names an external condition. Back in agile next at its priority; its first step stays the one in the 10-28 note: check whether the remaining milestone shipped in a later train (installed kit 4983F9C0, TRAIN6) and close it with that evidence, else do the next milestone.")
inl note_of_eoie_crate_gate_7 () = Note ("EOIE-CRATE-GATE", 7u32, "2026-11-04T20:18Z", "kit", "11-04 20:5x kit: DONE, verified on the INSTALLED kit (TRAIN14c): `kit crate gate <root> [--enforce] [--limit n]` reports one \"gate / <crate> <file> N lines ok|OVER largest <fn> M\" row per Cargo.toml with [package.metadata.spiral] found anywhere under the root (repo root C:\\home\\git\\kit -> 336 crates, not only src); with --enforce: an empty root refuses (\"no crate with [package.metadata.spiral] under the root, so nothing was measured\", exit 1), --limit 500 refuses on 182 over (exit 1), and crates without generated output refuse too (44 new unregenerated crates in today's tree, exit 1). Release trains already gate on it (TRAIN22 stopped there).")
inl backend_balance () = Task ("BACKEND-BALANCE", "Every project compiles each component to the backend that suits it: Gleam (BEAM) where Erlang's runtime pays (supervision, many concurrent long-lived actors, timeouts, monitors, hot reload, distribution), Rust where native speed pays (data-parallel work with rayon, short-lived CLIs, native libs, tight memory)", Epic, P1, D7, 80u32, Planned, "LIB-PAR-MAP,LIB-OTP-ACTORS,X-BACKEND-MAP,EOIE-BACKEND-MAP,TIBIA-BACKEND-MAP", "spiral,lib,x,kit,tibia,backends,team:main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_backend_balance () = Planning ("BACKEND-BALANCE", Minutes 2400u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_backend_balance_1 () = Note ("BACKEND-BALANCE", 1u32, "2026-10-23T10:59Z", "x", "2026-10-23 06:59 x: user request 2026-10-23 06:5x: 'everything that can leverage erlang goodies compile to gleam and the rest rayon etc compiles to rust, all projects gotta reach the perfect balance between gleam and rust compilations; x is a good playground, tibia and eoie we haven't thought about'. RULE, written before any classification. A component goes to GLEAM when two or more hold: (G1) it lives for minutes or longer and holds state; (G2) it runs many independent activities at once (clients, jobs, fetchers, simulated users); (G3) it must supervise, restart, time out or monitor other processes or OS children; (G4) its parts talk by messages; (G5) hot code reload or multi-node distribution would remove real pain. A component goes to RUST when one holds: (R1) CPU-bound work over independent items that rayon can split (seeds, files, schedules, cells); (R2) a CLI invoked per command, where BEAM's cold start (measure it; ~0.5-1 s) is too slow; (R3) it needs native libraries (TLS, GPU, SIMD, compression); (R4) peak memory matters more than throughput. TIES and everything else: keep the current backend. Domain code stays backend-neutral (backend_switch only at boundaries), so a component can be compiled both ways and its Cells test blocks must agree on rust and gleam. Every switch lands only with a measurement before and after (wall time, cold start, peak memory; an existing benchmark if there is one) and the result in the item. GPU work stays WGSL and C stays where the matrix needs it (tibia). Children: LIB-PAR-MAP and LIB-OTP-ACTORS (lib groundwork), then X-BACKEND-MAP (playground first), EOIE-BACKEND-MAP and TIBIA-BACKEND-MAP.")
inl note_of_backend_balance_2 () = Note ("BACKEND-BALANCE", 2u32, "2026-10-23T11:00Z", "x", "2026-10-23 07:00 x: R2 measured, 5 runs each, on this box while eoie's F9 and main's corpus checks ran. 'erl -noshell -eval halt().' (OTP 29) took 2430, 2603, 2056, 1913 and 1110 ms. Rust CLIs: 'xc --help' 145, 34, 87, 71, 53 ms; 'eoie agile status' 45, 51, 73, 70, 160 ms. BEAM cold start is 15-50x a Rust CLI, so per-command tools stay Rust, and BEAM components must be long-lived services (a host or daemon) that the Rust CLI talks to, the way Cells keeps a warm compiler daemon (KINO-WARM-RUNNER). Re-measure on a quiet box before any decision rests on the exact factor.")
inl note_of_backend_balance_3 () = Note ("BACKEND-BALANCE", 3u32, "2026-10-24T00:42Z", "x", "2026-10-23 20:42 x: progress bookkeeping by rule, not a new estimate. The epic's progress is the mean of its five deps' recorded progress: X-BACKEND-MAP 30, EOIE-BACKEND-MAP 15, TIBIA-BACKEND-MAP 80, LIB-PAR-MAP 50, LIB-OTP-ACTORS 50 -> 45. Status Planned -> Active, because all five are Active. Recompute it the same way when a child moves.")
inl note_of_backend_balance_4 () = Note ("BACKEND-BALANCE", 4u32, "2026-10-24T03:36Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-23 23:36 tibia: recomputed by note 3's rule after TIBIA-BACKEND-MAP moved to 90 (Blocked on KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR's runtime-process arm; its classification, host core, executor, actor wrapper and Rust launcher are landed, TIBIA-BACKEND-MAP note 16): mean of 30, 15, 90, 50, 50 = 47.")
inl note_of_backend_balance_5 () = Note ("BACKEND-BALANCE", 5u32, "2026-10-24T11:21Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-24 07:21 tibia: recomputed by note 3's rule after TIBIA-BACKEND-MAP was re-sized to 60 (its OS-port arm entered scope, TIBIA-BACKEND-MAP note 19): mean of LIB-PAR-MAP 50, LIB-OTP-ACTORS 50, X-BACKEND-MAP 30, EOIE-BACKEND-MAP 15, TIBIA-BACKEND-MAP 60 = 41.")
inl note_of_backend_balance_6 () = Note ("BACKEND-BALANCE", 6u32, "2026-10-26T21:51Z", "tibia-lane", "21:51Z tibia: recomputed by note 3's rule after TIBIA-BACKEND-MAP returned to 90 (its 10-26 12:19Z note: the joint land of LIB-PROCESS-HANDLE-SPLIT, world_port and world_executor). Mean of LIB-PAR-MAP 50, LIB-OTP-ACTORS 50, X-BACKEND-MAP 30, EOIE-BACKEND-MAP 15 and TIBIA-BACKEND-MAP 90 is 47.")
inl note_of_backend_balance_7 () = Note ("BACKEND-BALANCE", 7u32, "2026-10-27T06:47Z", "tibia-lane", "06:47Z tibia measurement for the rule (GLEAM-RUST-SPLIT notes 10-12): on hunting_pack's 100-hunt sweep, Spiral's C output is 5.8x faster than its Rust output single-threaded (1.05 s vs 6.09 s, rows identical), and Rust's par'.map scales 5.2x on 8 cores but still lands 1.55x behind C seq. Consequence by the pre-registered rule: CPU-bound simulation sweeps stay on C, scaled by OS processes. Rust is chosen where its libraries or memory model pay, not for raw simulation speed, until the Rust codegen gap (likely Rc boxing) closes.")
inl note_of_backend_balance_8 () = Note ("BACKEND-BALANCE", 8u32, "2026-10-27T23:50Z", "tibia-lane", "23:5xZ tibia: recomputed by note 3's rule. X-BACKEND-MAP is now Done (100) and TIBIA-BACKEND-MAP is at 95 (its only step left is the first live world_executor run). Mean of LIB-PAR-MAP 50, LIB-OTP-ACTORS 50, X-BACKEND-MAP 100, EOIE-BACKEND-MAP 15 and TIBIA-BACKEND-MAP 95 is 62.")
inl note_of_backend_balance_9 () = Note ("BACKEND-BALANCE", 9u32, "2026-10-28T12:59Z", "tibia-lane", "10-28 08:5x tibia: TIBIA-BACKEND-MAP is Done. P-EXEC1 passed: the Gleam arena host (world_executor over real canary, mariadb and a pwsh client) ran a live block end to end, counted 4 of 4 with the exact trace and released the world (TIBIA-BACKEND-MAP notes 28-30). Cross-lane finding for every BEAM component that spawns PowerShell: on Windows, an Erlang port without the hide option makes pwsh/powershell exit 0 without running anything. LIB-PORT-HIDE fixes lib process.port_for and gates for the 14:00 window, with a real-pwsh row on both spawn paths.")
inl note_of_backend_balance_10 () = Note ("BACKEND-BALANCE", 10u32, "2026-10-30T16:01Z", "tibia-lane", "10-30 12:0x tibia: recomputed by note 3's rule (mean of the children). LIB-PAR-MAP 100 (Done), LIB-OTP-ACTORS 50 (Paused), X-BACKEND-MAP 100, EOIE-BACKEND-MAP 15 (Paused), TIBIA-BACKEND-MAP 100: mean 73, up from 62. The two open children are Paused: LIB-OTP-ACTORS and EOIE-BACKEND-MAP.")
inl note_of_backend_balance_11 () = Note ("BACKEND-BALANCE", 11u32, "2026-10-30T16:08Z", "tibia-lane", "10-30 12:1x tibia: recomputed by note 3's rule after main re-scored LIB-OTP-ACTORS to 85: step_runtime already is the deterministic non-BEAM arm, so the channel-based arm was dropped as a duplicate. Mean of 100, 85, 100, 15, 100 = 80. Open: LIB-OTP-ACTORS (a non-BEAM supervisor nobody needs yet) and EOIE-BACKEND-MAP (Paused).")
inl note_of_backend_balance_12 () = Note ("BACKEND-BALANCE", 12u32, "2026-10-31T11:28Z", "tibia-lane", "10-31 07:3x tibia (cleanup round): Active -> Paused at 80. Every open child is Paused (LIB-OTP-ACTORS 85, a non-BEAM supervisor nobody needs yet; EOIE-BACKEND-MAP 15, kit adoption), so no tibia, x or lib work can move this epic. Resume condition: either child resumes. Then recompute by note 3's rule (mean of the children).")
inl note_of_backend_balance_13 () = Note ("BACKEND-BALANCE", 13u32, "2026-10-31T23:03Z", "x", "2026-10-31 19:03 x groom: Paused -> Active. Its resume condition fired in a new form: the user asked on 10-31 ~18:05 for 'the perfect gleam rust balance' (TYPEFEATURESET-ENFORCE n6), and x is driving a new child map now. The Cells backend map in CELLS-CENSUS n2 makes services Gleam, CLIs Rust over one GADT message family (the iggy split), and keeps the domain neutral. Progress is kept at 80 until the children's mean is recomputed.")
inl note_of_backend_balance_14 () = Note ("BACKEND-BALANCE", 14u32, "2026-11-01T13:06Z", "kit", "11-01 09:0x kit: this item is now a FEEDER of CODEBASE-DIAMOND (the deterministic rewrite-distance gauge, user 09:0x): its counts become Mechanical evidence in DIAMOND-S2. Keep building it here; the gauge consumes its rows, it does not re-measure them.")
inl note_of_backend_balance_15 () = Note ("BACKEND-BALANCE", 15u32, "2026-11-03T18:36Z", "tibia", "11-03 14:4x tibia (MAIN-ORPHAN-SWEEP): no team tag and no note since 11-01, so OWNER = main (org epic over the per-project maps; team:main added, other tags kept). Dep status from the tibia side: TIBIA-BACKEND-MAP is Done. NEXT STEP for main: read LIB-PAR-MAP, LIB-OTP-ACTORS, X-BACKEND-MAP and EOIE-BACKEND-MAP, close this epic when the open ones close (it is 80%), and keep feeding DIAMOND-S2 rows as kit n-latest says.")
inl lib_par_map () = Task ("LIB-PAR-MAP", "lib/spiral: a backend-neutral parallel map (par'.map / par'.each over independent items) with a Rust arm on rayon and a Gleam arm on BEAM processes, so data-parallel code is written once and compiled to either", Story, P1, D4, 100u32, Done, "GLEAM-ERLANG-1X", "lib,spiral,main,backends", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_lib_par_map () = Planning ("LIB-PAR-MAP", Minutes 240u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_lib_par_map_1 () = Note ("LIB-PAR-MAP", 1u32, "2026-10-23T11:00Z", "x", "2026-10-23 07:00 x: BACKEND-BALANCE groundwork. Today lib/spiral has no parallel primitive and no rayon arm: eoie reaches rayon only through raw host-code blobs (eoie_do_read/host.spi:36, eoie_grep_query/search.spi:51, eoie_bundle_retention/retention_terminal.spi:7), which eoie's own style contract asks to remove. Shape: backend_switch with Rust = rayon par_iter over a Vec, results in input order; Gleam = one process per chunk (chunk count = schedulers), monitored, results collected in order, the first crash surfaced as a typed error; any other backend = the sequential map (same results, so tests agree everywhere). Test blocks on rust + gleam: order kept, a raising element is reported, an empty list works. Benchmark both arms on one CPU-bound workload (e.g. x's splitter over every client cell) against the sequential map.")
inl note_of_lib_par_map_2 () = Note ("LIB-PAR-MAP", 2u32, "2026-10-23T11:30Z", "main", "2026-10-23 07:30 main: design read (code + probe). The Rust blocker is Send. Spiral's Rust output is Rc everywhere: strings are Rc<str>, closures are Rc<dyn Fn> built by a per-closure factory 'fn name(free vars) -> Rc<dyn Fn(..)>' (codegenRust closure, core ~164974). A closure with no free vars keeps one copy per thread in a thread_local. rayon needs Send + Sync, so today's eoie blobs convert to String/Vec inside host code. Probe (scratchpad par-probe/main.rs, rustc -O, std::thread::scope with 4 and 2 chunks): calling the FACTORY fn pointer inside each worker (fn() -> Rc<dyn Fn> is Send + Sync) type-checks and runs. Results come back in input order: i32 squares, Rc<str> strings deep-copied at the boundary as String, and empty input. Plan, decided by rule: (1) compiler, Rust only: a macro escape that renders a closure term as its factory call text (name plus captured args) when every free var is a Send type (primitive scalar or string-by-copy), and refuses at compile time otherwise. That puts soundness in the compiler instead of an unsafe Send wrapper, which would be UB once a capture is an Rc cloned on two threads. (2) lib par'.map/par'.each: Rust arm = rayon par_iter over chunks, each worker calls the factory once; element and result types limited to scalars and strings, copied at the boundary. Gleam arm = one process per chunk (chunks = schedulers_online), each sends back its index with list.map(chunk, f), collected in order under a monitor, so a crash surfaces as Error. Every other backend = the sequential map. (3) Test blocks rust + gleam: order kept, empty list, crash reported; then the benchmark from the first note. Step 1 is a pack item (codegenRust only, matrix review); the Gleam arm can draft now. Not started in lib/spiral: tibia's live block 09:40-12:30, and lib lands only in gaps.")
inl note_of_lib_par_map_3 () = Note ("LIB-PAR-MAP", 3u32, "2026-10-23T11:43Z", "main", "2026-10-23 07:43 main: Gleam arm reworked onto the user's granular direction (LIB-OTP-ACTORS note 2): scratchpad par-lab/par'.spi is now pure Spiral over par-lab/erlang_process.spi primitives (new_subject, spawn_unlinked, monitor, select_map, select_specific_monitor, selector_receive_forever, exited_normally, schedulers_online). Typed union par_event = ChunkDone (index, values) | WorkerEnded exit_reason. One worker per chunk (chunk count = schedulers_online). It collects until every result and every Down has arrived, so no stray monitor message is left in the caller's mailbox, then concatenates in index order, and an abnormal Down fails with failwith. No global blob: the only globals are imports and one @external line per BIF. Tests: chunks_split_evenly, chunks_empty (all backends), map_keeps_order (gleam/rust/fsharp), map_empty, map_strings. Not compiled yet; it needs GLEAM-ERLANG-1X (the 1.x API names) and a compile gap. Arrays live in the process dictionary on Gleam, so items and captures must not hold arrays; this mirrors the Rust arm's Send rule.")
inl note_of_lib_par_map_4 () = Note ("LIB-PAR-MAP", 4u32, "2026-10-23T11:48Z", "tibia-lane", "Use case from tibia-lane (07:5x, TIBIA-BACKEND-MAP note 5: tibia's largest R1 win). (1) Trainer inner loop: network.evolve (src/network.spi 162-181) scores 64 offspring per generation, each fitness candidate independent; fitness = worth o camp (network_tactic w) first count (hunting_pack), and each call runs about 50 training clears. Shape: par'.map over offspring indices 0..63 giving i32 scores, then the existing sequential max (keeps the first best on ties, so the result stays bit-identical to the sequential evolve). The closure captures camp (a record of unions, i32 and nested arrays such as kinds and layout), o (a union) and the parent weights (array i32); it returns i32. (2) Held-out scoring: clear_ledger_of over 100 seeds is a fold of per-seed ledgers, so par'.map per seed plus a sequential sum. Today: single-threaded C, about 43-67 min per M1 arm on this 8-thread laptop. Constraints tibia needs: results in input order; deterministic (no shared RNG; tibia keys every roll by seed, tick, slot and purpose); captured values that are arrays of i32 and records of unions crossing the thread boundary; the C oracle and the Rust arm agreeing in Cells. A first consumer test: evolve on a 4-gene toy fitness, par versus sequential, bit-identical best genes.")
inl note_of_lib_par_map_5 () = Note ("LIB-PAR-MAP", 5u32, "2026-10-23T12:06Z", "main", "2026-10-23 08:06 main: Rust half drafted. (1) Compiler: %TEMP%\\pack-next6\\rust-closure-factory.ps1. Two anchors, each matching once on the next5e core, which parses under both defines. A macro term preceded by the text '__spiral_closure_factory ' renders as its closure's factory fn name (closure_names via closure_of_var, the same map the Export op uses for 'a closure without captures'). The marker text is stripped, and a captured or unknown closure is a codegen error naming the reason. (2) lib arm, par-lab/par'.spi map_on_threads: one expression on std::thread::scope with chunks = len.div_ceil(available_parallelism). Each worker calls (factory())(x.clone()) and the results are flattened in chunk order. It's std-only because Cells builds Rust cells with bare rustc (cells.ex rustc_args, no crates), so rayon can't link in test blocks; a rayon arm waits for a cargo-built consumer. rustc enforces Sync on items and Send on results, so Rc<str> items are refused at compile time (sound). Strings therefore stay gleam-only in the tests until a String boundary copy exists. The pack carrying (1) goes into the next codegen review (matrix: no residual changes, since no sample uses the marker), and lib par'.spi lands with GLEAM-ERLANG-1X in a gap.")
inl note_of_lib_par_map_6 () = Note ("LIB-PAR-MAP", 6u32, "2026-10-23T14:06Z", "x", "2026-10-23 10:06 x: (1) CAVEAT for the Rust arm in note 5, from today's measurement (LIB-DICTM note 5): Spiral compiles a closure's record, tuple and unit parameters FLATTENED into separate Rust arguments, while map_on_threads calls (factory())(x.clone()) with one value. So par'.map over items that are tuples or records (tibia's use case passes offspring indices, fine; a (seed, schedule) pair would not be) fails with E0057 'this function takes N arguments but 1 was supplied'. Either restrict the item type to single-value types at the type level, or have the arm call through a Spiral-side adapter closure that takes the whole item as one i32 index and looks it up in an array captured by the factory. The index form also suits the 'no captures' factory rule, because the array becomes the map's own argument. UNVERIFIED: whether a closure RETURNING a record or tuple also changes shape on Rust. Today's evidence covers parameters only (dictm's folds returned lists), so probe a record-returning closure in the arm's first test. (2) x's first consumer, for the measured switch in X-BACKEND-MAP note 2: replaying N schedules of one corpus is N independent scheduler.sync_run calls. Item = schedule seed (i32), result = a scheduler record (or its XSIM summary line, a string), order kept, 2 to ~20 items of seconds each. The commute check over consecutive results stays sequential after the map.")
inl note_of_lib_par_map_7 () = Note ("LIB-PAR-MAP", 7u32, "2026-10-23T18:30Z", "main", "2026-10-23 14:30 main: on rayon (BACKEND-BALANCE R1 names it): par'.map's Rust arm uses std::thread::scope with chunking, not rayon, because Cells compiles Rust cells with bare rustc and no crates, so a rayon arm could not be gated by the lib's own test blocks. A rayon variant fits cargo consumers only: a separate par'.map_rayon whose arm is '(*!native).par_iter().map(|x| (__spiral_closure_factory !fn())(x.clone())).collect()' (the closure-factory marker gives each worker its own closure, since Spiral closures are Rc and not Send), proven through a consumer's cargo build (x is the playground per BACKEND-BALANCE). Not drafted today; the std arm lands first with today's lib landing.")
inl note_of_lib_par_map_8 () = Note ("LIB-PAR-MAP", 8u32, "2026-10-23T20:37Z", "main", "2026-10-23 16:37 main: par'.spi NOT landed: its Rust arm needs the __spiral_closure_factory marker that pack-next7's codegen implements (the installed compiler emits it verbatim: E0425 + a parse error), and F# also fails map_keeps_order; Gleam passes now. Kept in %TEMP%\\lib-land-1023\\lib\\spiral\\par'.spi (out of the land copy's package.spiproj). Next: land with next7's install, after an F# look.")
inl note_of_lib_par_map_9 () = Note ("LIB-PAR-MAP", 9u32, "2026-10-24T00:17Z", "main", "2026-10-23 20:17 main: status fixed from 0% Planned to Active 50. par' is drafted (lib-land-1023), and its Rust arm waits for pack-next7's closure factory (rust-closure-factory in pack-next6/7). It lands after that install.")
inl note_of_lib_par_map_10 () = Note ("LIB-PAR-MAP", 10u32, "2026-10-24T04:04Z", "x", "10-24 00:04 x: for main's 'F# look' (n 16:37): queued a Cells run of the draft par'.spi on a live-lib copy (%TEMP%\\x-par-fsharp, par' listed last) as job-1792814654580-x-par-fsharp-probe. Its F# arm is the plain listm.map fallback, so the failing map_keeps_order output should show whether F# trips on the module (Gleam-only union par_event over erlang_process types) or on the arm. The Rust failure there is the known missing closure-factory marker and isn't news. The result goes into this note's follow-up.")
inl note_of_lib_par_map_11 () = Note ("LIB-PAR-MAP", 11u32, "2026-10-24T05:36Z", "x", "10-24 01:36 x: F# look ANSWERED (n10's probe, rerun as job-1792817012060, 01:19). The draft par'.spi on a live-lib copy (par' listed last) ran 5 tests and failed 2: map_empty and map_keeps_order, both only on rust (the known missing __spiral_closure_factory marker). F# map_keeps_order PASSES on today's lib and compiler (single-flight B553EF6B78E6EE29), and gleam passes everything. So 16:37's F# failure doesn't reproduce; the only blocker left is pack-next7's closure factory for the Rust arm.")
inl note_of_lib_par_map_12 () = Note ("LIB-PAR-MAP", 12u32, "2026-10-24T05:40Z", "main", "2026-10-24 01:40 main: thanks to x's n11 (F# passes; only the Rust closure-factory marker is missing). pack-next6's rust-closure-factory.ps1 was in no composed stack (next4 took other next6 slices, not this one); it composes cleanly on 5g and is now in pack 5g's compose (core AEC3FF009A141AB8). It rides review-4 (job-1792813775220), and after the install par'.spi lands through the gate kit with its rust tests.")
inl note_of_lib_par_map_13 () = Note ("LIB-PAR-MAP", 13u32, "2026-10-26T09:44Z", "tibia-lane", "09:44Z 09:5xZ State check (tibia; GLEAM-RUST-SPLIT note 3, part 4, the Rust+rayon predict sweep, waits on this item). par'.spi never landed: lib/spiral has no par' and package.spiproj doesn't list it. The 10-24 05:40 plan was to land it through the gate kit after pack 5g's install. 5g and later installed, and the blocker is gone: the installed compiler has the closure-factory marker (apps/compiler/spiral_compiler.fs:163852, rustClosureFactoryMarker = \"__spiral_closure_factory\"). Nobody picked the draft up afterwards.\nThe newest draft is %TEMP%\\lib-land-1023\\lib\\spiral\\par'.spi (4,682 bytes, 10-23 10:50; x-par-fsharp holds the same copy). It can't land as written:\n(1) Edition: inl, let rec, match, if/then and `open` throughout. Lib is edition 3 now, so it needs conversion to := / :~ ^ / ?= / ? ?> |: / :<.\n(2) Granular: the Gleam arm is already Spiral over erlang_process primitives. The Rust arm is not. map_on_threads is one host program: thread::scope, then chunks(...).map(|chunk| scope.spawn(move || chunk.iter().map(|x| (__spiral_closure_factory !fn())(x.clone())).collect())), then join and flat_map, with `as usize`/`as i32` casts. available_parallelism also casts in host text.\nPre-written rule for the Rust redesign: spawned closures borrow a scope lifetime Spiral can't name, so the scope-plus-spawn-plus-join triple counts as one host primitive taking a Spiral chunk list and the closure-factory fn. Chunking, chunk_size arithmetic and casts (through convert.unativeint and conv) move to Spiral, reusing par's own chunks. Before slicing, check with main's GRANULAR-LINT 'block' class that a scope primitive counts as one host expression; this is the same question as the trace and crypto cfg cases (GRANULAR-HEAL note 27).\nProof shape: map_keeps_order (gleam, rust, fsharp), map_empty (gleam, rust) and map_strings (gleam), plus a Rust consumer that sums squares over 100,000 items with both par'.map and listm.map: equal results and fewer wall-clock seconds on this box's cores.\nLib order: par' after erlang_process (lib module 111).\nThe tibia sweep measurement (GLEAM-RUST-SPLIT note 3, rule: byte-identical rows on 100 held-out seeds and at most 1/3 of C's wall time) starts once par' lands.")
inl note_of_lib_par_map_14 () = Note ("LIB-PAR-MAP", 14u32, "2026-10-26T09:53Z", "tibia-lane", "09:53Z 10:0xZ First par' land drafted: LIB-PAR-GLEAM, the Gleam arm in edition 3, and sequential listm.map on every other backend. x's 10-23 draft is converted to edition 3 (:| par_event, := and :~ ^ join points, ?= and ? ?> |:, list sugar, typed literals). The Gleam arm was already granular Spiral over erlang_process primitives, and all of them exist in today's lib: new_subject, spawn_unlinked, monitor, select_specific_monitor, down_reason, new_selector, select_map, selector_receive_forever, exited_normally, schedulers_online, send. One fix: listm.collect became listm'.collect. The Rust arm stays out until its rule (note 13) is decided, so Rust takes the sequential fallback. Tests: chunks_split_evenly, chunks_empty, map_keeps_order (gleam, fsharp), map_empty and map_strings (gleam). Placement: package.spiproj gains `par'` right after erlang_process. Formatter clean, 0 lines over 120.\nProof on the consumer path, against a lib copy with the slice applied: a probe program sums par'.map (\\ x => x * x) over 1..100.\n- Compiled to Gleam and run with `gleam run` inside a copy of Cells' warm Gleam package (gleam_erlang and gleam_otp 1.3), it prints 338350 through the process arm. The first run printed a stale cached module ('__assert_eq { expected = 3 }') because the copied source was older than the warm build; touching the source fixed it.\n- Compiled to F# and run with dotnet fsi, it prints 338350 (the sequential arm).\nGate on the remote kit worker node: job-1793008453212-tibia-par-gleam-gate (scratch lib-par-gleam/lib-par-gleam.ps1 plus par'.spi; spiproj base D6395F2028FC6A5A, identical on both boxes). The land is packed into tibia-serial-gaps. Undo: the spiproj undo.do, plus removing par'.spi by literal path.")
inl note_of_lib_par_map_15 () = Note ("LIB-PAR-MAP", 15u32, "2026-10-26T12:35Z", "tibia-lane", "12:35Z 12:4xZ LIB-PAR-RUST drafted under GRANULAR-LINT rule (8) as accepted (A1 scope class, A2 spiral_scope_ prefix, A3 tests on its backend). It stacks on LIB-PAR-GLEAM. One shim in global, spiral_scope_map<T: Sync + Clone, U: Send>(items: &[T], chunk_size: usize, make: fn() -> Rc<dyn Fn(T) -> U>) -> Vec<U>, whose body is one thread::scope, one spawning iterator map over items.chunks(chunk_size), where each worker calls make() once and maps f, and one joining flat_map. No arithmetic, casts or branches. In Spiral: rust_available_parallelism (one call, as usize), map_on_threads (workers via convert.convert, the length <= workers ? 1 : ceil-division chunk size, usize via convert.convert) and rust_scoped_map, whose single host expression passes __spiral_closure_factory !fn. The compiler emits the closure's capture-free factory function name there (spiral_compiler.fs: a capturing closure is a codegen error, 'A closure that crosses threads must capture nothing'). map gains | Rust => map_on_threads; map_keeps_order and map_empty gain ///> rust. Proof (scratch lib-par-rust/probe-run.ps1, a copy with par-gleam and par-rust applied): par'.map over 1..200000 squares (i64) sums to 2666686666700000, equal to listm.map's sum, on rustc -O; an empty list gives 0; the output holds exactly one spiral_scope_map and one thread::scope. Formatter clean, 0 lines over 120. Land: tibia-serial-gaps-lib, gate-then-land right after par-gleam lands (local gate, since par'.spi must exist). GLEAM-RUST-SPLIT part 4 (the predict sweep on Rust + par'.map against C, byte-identical rows at most 1/3 of the time) can start once it lands.")
inl note_of_lib_par_map_16 () = Note ("LIB-PAR-MAP", 16u32, "2026-10-30T02:00Z", "main", "10-29 22:00 main, groom (WIP rule, 3 days with no note): Active -> Paused, progress kept at 50%. Last note 2026-10-26, before the 10-28 profile wipe interrupted every lane, and no live job. Resume when picked; it is not abandoned, and it keeps its priority P1.")
inl note_of_lib_par_map_17 () = Note ("LIB-PAR-MAP", 17u32, "2026-10-30T02:04Z", "main", "10-29 22:04 main, groom: RESUMABLE. Every dep is Done (GLEAM-ERLANG-1X), so nothing blocks it; it's Paused only for want of a picker (P1, 50%). Pick-up: the team that needs it (lane-roles), after the pack-7 install.")
inl note_of_lib_par_map_18 () = Note ("LIB-PAR-MAP", 18u32, "2026-10-30T02:05Z", "main", "10-29 22:05 main, groom follow-up: paused tonight by the WIP rule, but an ACTIVE item depends on it: BACKEND-BALANCE (Active). Resume it FIRST among the paused ones, since it gates live work.")
inl note_of_lib_par_map_19 () = Note ("LIB-PAR-MAP", 19u32, "2026-10-30T14:13Z", "main", "10-30 10:12 main, BACKLOG-DANGLING-1030 R2/R4: deps Done, owner team main ('agile next --team main' hands it out), assignee agent (nothing in it is the user's). Resume when that team picks it up; the pack-7 install it waited on is done.")
inl note_of_lib_par_map_20 () = Note ("LIB-PAR-MAP", 20u32, "2026-10-30T14:24Z", "main", "10-30 10:24 main: DONE, verified on the live tree, not from notes. tibia's two drafts (the Gleam arm, then the Rust arm under GRANULAR-LINT rule 8) LANDED on 10-26 22:11 but were never recorded here: lib/spiral/par'.spi (5,554 B, sha256 5453657581BF, committed in spiral 3f1b7588) is listed in package.spiproj. Rust uses std thread::scope through the one spiral_scope_map shim and __spiral_closure_factory, not rayon (the accepted granular rule). Gleam uses erlang_process primitives; other backends fall back to sequential listm.map. Proof, through the consumer path: apps/cells/spi/lib_gate.ps1 -File par'.spi on the box at 10:2x, 'tests: passed 6 failed 0 of 6', type errors 0, discovered 6 = executed 6, failed_targets {}, slowest 51 s (the Rust cell). The tests are chunks_split_evenly, chunks_empty, map_workers_have_room_for_deep_recursion [rust], map_keeps_order [gleam fsharp rust], map_empty [gleam rust] and map_strings [gleam]. This unblocks Active BACKEND-BALANCE's LIB-PAR-MAP dep. Left elsewhere: GLEAM-RUST-SPLIT part 4 (the Rust predict sweep over par'.map) is that item's work, now unblocked.")
inl lib_otp_actors () = Task ("LIB-OTP-ACTORS", "lib/spiral: typed actors and supervisors with a Gleam arm on gleam_otp (actor, supervisor, subject, monitor, timeouts) and a deterministic single-threaded arm elsewhere, so long-lived concurrent components are modeled once in Spiral", Story, P1, D5, 85u32, Planned, "GLEAM-ERLANG-1X", "lib,spiral,main,backends,team:main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_lib_otp_actors () = Planning ("LIB-OTP-ACTORS", Minutes 360u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_lib_otp_actors_1 () = Note ("LIB-OTP-ACTORS", 1u32, "2026-10-23T11:00Z", "x", "2026-10-23 07:00 x: BACKEND-BALANCE groundwork, the G-side of the rule. lib/spiral/process.spi covers only OS-process capture on Gleam (gleam/erlang/process); there is no actor, supervisor or monitor primitive. Shape: a typed message protocol per actor (a GADT of requests with their reply types), start / call / cast / stop, supervisors with restart strategies (one_for_one first), timeouts as values. The Gleam arm uses gleam_otp. The deterministic arm (Rust and the rest) runs actors as a step loop with a mailbox, so the same actor code is testable without BEAM and agrees on test blocks. Relate to LIB-REACTORS (reactors map onto actors on BEAM) and KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR (Kino's Elixir supervision is the first big consumer). Check gleam_otp's current release and API before designing (memory latest-of-everything).")
inl note_of_lib_otp_actors_2 () = Note ("LIB-OTP-ACTORS", 2u32, "2026-10-23T11:07Z", "x", "2026-10-23 07:07 x: user direction 2026-10-23 07:0x: 'we gotta master gleam's subject api granular modeling from spiral though, to not do mess like rust_global that eoie indulged in'. Design constraint: model gleam/erlang/process and gleam/otp granularly in Spiral, one nominal per Gleam type (Subject(msg) as a phantom-typed nominal over the message type, Pid, Monitor, Selector(payload), Timer, ExitReason, actor Started/Next) and one named one-expression primitive per Gleam function (process.new_subject, send, receive with a timeout, call, try_call, selector select/select_map, monitor, link/unlink, send_after, cancel_timer). No $'{...}' or multi-statement host blocks and no global blobs beyond the imports. The typed layer on top (actor protocols as GADTs of request -> reply, supervisors) is pure Spiral built from those primitives. Mirror Gleam's real API: read gleam_erlang's and gleam_otp's current docs and releases first. Test blocks on gleam for each primitive, so the binding is proven granular, not just compiled.")
inl note_of_lib_otp_actors_3 () = Note ("LIB-OTP-ACTORS", 3u32, "2026-10-23T11:47Z", "main", "2026-10-23 07:47 main: API read from hexdocs (current: gleam_erlang 1.3.0, gleam_otp 1.3.0). process: Subject, Pid, Monitor, Selector, Timer, Name, Down = ProcessDown(monitor, pid, reason) | PortDown, ExitReason = Normal | Killed | Abnormal(dynamic), 44 functions (spawn, spawn_unlinked, new_subject, send, receive(from, within), receive_forever, call, monitor, select_map, select_specific_monitor, selector_receive(_forever), send_after, cancel_timer, link, kill, trap_exits, named subjects). actor: Builder(state, message, return), Next, Started(pid, data), StartError = InitTimeout | InitFailed(String) | InitExited(ExitReason); new, new_with_initialiser, on_message, start, continue, stop, stop_abnormal, with_selector, named, call, send. static_supervisor: new(OneForOne | OneForAll | RestForOne), add(ChildSpecification), restart_tolerance(intensity 2, period 5 by default), auto_shutdown, start, supervised. supervision.ChildSpecification(start, restart Permanent | Transient | Temporary, significant, child_type Worker(shutdown_ms) | Supervisor) with worker, supervisor, restart, significant, timeout, map_data. Drafts in scratchpad par-lab, granular per the user's direction: erlang_process.spi (8 nominals, 22 one-expression primitives, 4 gleam tests) and otp_actor.spi (4 nominals; new, on_message, continue, stop, stop_abnormal, start, started_data, call, send; counter_actor_counts gleam test). Next, in order: static_supervisor + supervision bindings, then the typed layer in pure Spiral (a protocol = GADT of request -> reply, start, call, cast, a supervised child), then the deterministic arm (mailbox queue + step loop, call = enqueue + run until the reply). Blocked only on compiling: GLEAM-ERLANG-1X (dep) moves the cells to 1.x. Open question for the compile check: whether a curried Spiral closure is fn(a) -> fn(b) -> c in Gleam; the drafts assume curried (!handler(state)(message)).")
inl note_of_lib_otp_actors_4 () = Note ("LIB-OTP-ACTORS", 4u32, "2026-10-23T11:49Z", "main", "2026-10-23 07:49 main: drafted par-lab/otp_supervisor.spi: nominals supervisor_builder, supervisor, child d; Spiral unions strategy (OneForOne | OneForAll | RestForOne) and restart (Permanent | Transient | Temporary), each mapped to its Gleam constructor by a compile-time match. Primitives new, restart_tolerance, add, start, supervised, worker, with_restart, with_shutdown, plus the gleam test supervisor_starts_a_worker. Typed-layer design question for the first compile experiment: BackendSwitch types every arm and requires them equal, so a reply handle inside a message type has to be ONE Spiral type on every backend. The candidate is nominal reply_to r = backend_switch { Gleam: process.Subject(r); others: a Spiral ref (option r) }, as listm' list'_switch does for List(t). The experiment settles whether a switch field may name a Spiral type rather than host text. If it can't, reply handles become an index into an actor-local reply table, a pure Spiral i32 on every backend, with the Gleam arm keeping a Subject per pending call.")
inl note_of_lib_otp_actors_5 () = Note ("LIB-OTP-ACTORS", 5u32, "2026-10-23T12:37Z", "x", "2026-10-23 08:38 x: evidence for main's open question in note 3. Yes, a Spiral closure reaches Gleam curried: fn(a) -> fn(b) -> c, applied as f(a)(b). The draft neutral map C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-world\\lib-draft\\dictm.spi folds with the Gleam arm 'dict.fold(!d, !init, fn(acc, k, v) { !f(acc)(k)(v) })', and its tests passed 3/3 on gleam (plus 6/6 for inbox.spi on top of it), so the curried shape your drafts assume (!handler(state)(message)) is right. RUST CAVEAT for the deterministic arm: in a closure that reaches a Rust host splice, a tuple parameter is flattened into separate arguments and a unit () parameter is dropped. dictm's fold over set k = dict k () therefore broke on Rust with 12x E0057 (wrong argument count) when the splice called (!f)(acc)(k)(v) with v : (). The fix was a separate fold_keys primitive whose closure never takes the unit. For otp_actor: a handler or message that is () or a tuple must not be called positionally from a Rust splice; keep the splice's closure types free of () and tuples, or wrap them in a record or nominal.")
inl note_of_lib_otp_actors_6 () = Note ("LIB-OTP-ACTORS", 6u32, "2026-10-23T13:06Z", "x", "2026-10-23 09:06 x: widening note 5's Rust caveat. Not only () and tuples: RECORDS are flattened too. On Rust every compound parameter of a Spiral closure becomes separate arguments, so a host splice that calls (!handler)(state)(message) breaks as soon as state or message is a record, tuple or unit. Hit today through dictm.fold over record values (LIB-DICTM note 5). For the deterministic arm, keep the actor step loop in pure Spiral (mailbox list + a match on the message), and keep $'...' to primitives that never call back into Spiral closures. On the Gleam arm, actor.on_message(!handler) with curried whole values is fine.")
inl note_of_lib_otp_actors_7 () = Note ("LIB-OTP-ACTORS", 7u32, "2026-10-23T13:13Z", "main", "2026-10-23 09:13 main: typed-layer design, settled with x's 09:xx finding (Rust flattens a closure's record, tuple and unit parameters, so no host splice may call a closure with user-chosen types; Gleam closures are curried f(a)(b), which confirms the drafts' !handler(state)(message)). Decision: no uniform handle type across backends. That would need the reply_to switch experiment of note 4, and on Rust it would put closures with user types behind splices. Instead, domain code is generic over a runtime record: an actor is { init : s; handle : s -> m -> step s } with union step s = Continue : s | Stop, and a runtime h r is a record of Spiral functions { spawn; send; call } over a handle type h and a reply type r. Domain functions are written forall h r against it, and the entry picks the runtime in one run_target: Gleam passes the otp_actor-backed runtime (h = process.Subject(m), r = process.Subject(reply)), and every other backend the deterministic step runtime (pure Spiral: mailbox list and state in Spiral refs, call = enqueue plus drain to the reply, no splice calls a closure). Each arm returns the domain result, so BackendSwitch's equal-type rule holds on the result, not on the handles. Messages that carry replies are parameterized by r (union counter_message r = Add : i32 | Read : r). Next: draft step_runtime.spi and a counter domain test that runs on gleam, rust and c with the same expected value.")
inl note_of_lib_otp_actors_8 () = Note ("LIB-OTP-ACTORS", 8u32, "2026-10-23T13:15Z", "main", "2026-10-23 09:15 main: drafted scratchpad par-lab/step_runtime.spi following note 7. Types: union step s = Continue : s | Stop; type actor s m = { init; handle : s -> m -> step s }. The deterministic runtime is pure Spiral, with mut cells for state, mailbox and a draining flag: spawn; drain (a join-point loop; a send from inside a handler only enqueues, so there is no reentrancy); send; reply; call, which enqueues a request carrying a reply cell, drains, and fails if the actor stopped without replying. otp_start adapts the same actor spec to gleam_otp (on_message, Continue -> continue, Stop -> stop). The counter domain counter_total forall h r takes start, post, ask and answer, and the test counter_counts_on_every_backend picks the otp runtime on Gleam and the step runtime elsewhere, with ///> gleam rust c and expected 7. stopped_actor_ignores_later_messages runs on the default target. Not compiled: it waits for GLEAM-ERLANG-1X and a compile gap after tibia's block. The first compile also answers whether a record type with mut fields can be built with { state = mut ...; ... }.")
inl note_of_lib_otp_actors_9 () = Note ("LIB-OTP-ACTORS", 9u32, "2026-10-23T13:20Z", "x", "2026-10-23 09:20 x: requirements from the first consumer, the xsim runner shell (SIM-IN-SPIRAL note 20), mapped onto main's par-lab drafts (otp_actor, step_runtime: actor s m = {init; handle}). The pure scheduler (x-world scheduler.spi) is the actor STATE. It already emits StartTask {key; sources} / CancelTask key commands and takes finished_task key result, so the shell is: union runner_message = Delivered delivery | Finished {key; result} | Crashed {key; reason} | Settle (reply to scheduler). Primitives it needs beyond the current drafts: (1) the actor's own subject inside handle, so an executor can send Finished back: gleam_otp actor.new_with_initialiser (the draft has only new); (2) spawn_unlinked + monitor, with a selector mapping the Down message to Crashed for the task's key: runner.ex turns a Task exit into status :crash (select_specific_monitor or select_map in the draft's erlang_process); (3) kill(pid) for CancelTask (runner.ex uses Task.shutdown(task, :brutal_kill)), so the shell keeps key -> pid in its state; (4) send_after for pace_ms and per-task timeouts. Deterministic arm: run the executor inline and feed Finished immediately, which is exactly scheduler.sync_run (parity with the Elixir Runner already passes), so step_runtime's counter shape fits as is. Test plan when it compiles: the same prelude stream through the Gleam shell and through sync_run must settle to the same visible map.")
inl note_of_lib_otp_actors_10 () = Note ("LIB-OTP-ACTORS", 10u32, "2026-10-23T13:44Z", "tibia-lane", "tibia-lane 09:4x: requirements from the second consumer, the tibia arena host (TIBIA-BACKEND-MAP note 6), mapped onto main's step_runtime draft (actor s m = {init; handle}). The pure core is tested and landed: apps/host/arena_host.spi, step : host -> host_event -> host * list host_command (events WindowGranted, WindowLost, WorldReady, WorldCrashed, RunEnded outcome, RunTimedOut; commands StartWorld, RestartWorld, ResetCharacter, StartRun stage run, StopRun, Record fate, ReleaseWindow), over block_plan's crash rule. Four traces are Cells tests on lua and gleam that replay an event list, which is exactly what the deterministic arm should run. Needs beyond the drafts: (1) handle emits commands, so either handle returns step s plus a command list that the runtime hands to an executor, or the executor is a second actor the handler sends to (x's StartTask/CancelTask is the same shape, so one pattern would serve both). (2) OS processes as ports with an exit status delivered as a message: canary.exe and mariadbd for the world actor, and target/offscreen/offscreen.exe (apps/offscreen, landed today: one OTClient run on a hidden desktop, exit 0 with a pid/exit line, 124 on timeout) for the run actor. A port exit maps to WorldCrashed or RunEnded. (3) A timer (send_after) for RunTimedOut, cancellable when the run ends first. (4) A monitor on the block actor, so the eoie window is released when the block ends however it ends (WindowLost covers revocation). (5) The window request itself is an eoie CLI call, so a port with a typed reply parsed from its key=value line. No BEAM-only types in the domain: the pure step stays as it is, and only the executor is per-backend.")
inl note_of_lib_otp_actors_11 () = Note ("LIB-OTP-ACTORS", 11u32, "2026-10-23T20:37Z", "main", "2026-10-23 16:37 main: LANDED 16:36: erlang_process.spi (4/4 on gleam), otp_actor.spi (1/1), otp_supervisor.spi (1/1), step_runtime.spi (2/2, the cross-backend step runtime), listed in lib package.spiproj. Defects the gate found in my drafts, fixed before landing: step_runtime assigned mut fields through a non-mut record (it type-failed the whole package), erlang_process.down_reason used field access on the 1.x Down union (now a case), and the supervisor test left the message type open.")
inl note_of_lib_otp_actors_12 () = Note ("LIB-OTP-ACTORS", 12u32, "2026-10-23T21:00Z", "tibia-lane", "Finding from the first consumer, tibia-lane 17:0x: on the Gleam backend a Spiral array created in the caller cannot be read inside an otp_actor. tibia's scratch arena-actor/arena_host.spi wraps the arena host's step function as a step_runtime.actor (state host * trace, messages Event or Trace r, read back with call), modelled on counter_total. Gleam via step_runtime.otp_start fails with: exception error: no function clause matching array:is_fix(undefined) (array.erl:463), then a perform_call panic (gleam/erlang/process.gleam:626). The host state holds runs : array i32 (block_plan.stages_of), built in the test process as spec.init, and Spiral arrays on Gleam live in the creating process's dictionary, so the actor process sees undefined. The 6 non-actor tests pass on lua and gleam (Cells job tibia-host-actor). Options as I see them: (1) document that actor state must hold no Spiral arrays or refs (consumers use lists or records), or (2) have otp_start take an init thunk evaluated inside the actor (new_with_initialiser, which x also asked for), or (3) copy arrays across the spawn. tibia will move the host's run plan to a list next session unless (2) lands first. Repro: tibia scratch arena-actor (package with block_plan and arena_host, test the_host_step_runs_inside_an_actor_and_gives_the_same_trace_on_every_backend). Update 17:1x: building the array inside the actor works. tibia's host_actor keeps its run plan as a list and builds the host on the first message, 7 of 7 green on gleam and lua (job tibia-host-actor2), landed in apps/host/arena_host.spi. main landed option (2) as otp_actor.new_with_init at 17:1x, and tibia moves to it next session.")
inl note_of_lib_otp_actors_13 () = Note ("LIB-OTP-ACTORS", 13u32, "2026-10-23T21:01Z", "main", "2026-10-23 17:01 main: accepted tibia's first-consumer finding (array state crosses the actor's process boundary: Gleam arrays live in the creating process's dictionary, array:is_fix(undefined)). Fix chosen: an init-thunk constructor, otp_actor.new_with_init (fun () => state) over gleam_otp actor.new_with_initialiser, so the state is built inside the actor process (x asked for the same); plus a test whose state holds an array, gated on gleam. First lib item of the next round.")
inl note_of_lib_otp_actors_14 () = Note ("LIB-OTP-ACTORS", 14u32, "2026-10-23T21:15Z", "main", "2026-10-23 17:15 main: tibia's array-state finding FIXED the same day: otp_actor.new_with_init (timeout_ms : i32) (init : () -> s) over gleam_otp actor.new_with_initialiser runs init inside the actor process and returns its subject. Test array_state_is_built_inside_the_actor (state ;[4; 5; 6], length back through call = 3) passes on gleam; the file's gate is 2/2 (lesson: a Cells test sees only the definitions above it, so the test sits at the end of the file). Landed 17:14 as otp_actor.spi 200738F70462FE88; tibia and x told.")
inl note_of_lib_otp_actors_15 () = Note ("LIB-OTP-ACTORS", 15u32, "2026-10-23T21:16Z", "x", "2026-10-23 17:15 x: consumer note after main's new_with_init (state built in the actor process). The sim shell (x-world runner_shell.spi) still needs the actor's OWN subject in its handler, for workers to send Finished back. Today an Adopt message right after start delivers it, which leaves a short window where state.me is None (dispatched skips spawning then). Proposed extension: the init thunk receives the subject, as gleam_otp's initialiser does (fun (me : subject m) => state). Then Adopt and the None case disappear.")
inl note_of_lib_otp_actors_16 () = Note ("LIB-OTP-ACTORS", 16u32, "2026-10-23T21:25Z", "main", "2026-10-23 17:25 main: x's follow-up landed too (17:21, otp_actor.spi 6525F5CCD4F86A0F): new_with_self (timeout_ms : i32) (init : erlang_process.subject m -> s) gives the initialiser the actor's own subject (gleam_otp new_with_initialiser), so workers can report back without an Adopt message. Test the_initialiser_receives_the_actors_own_subject; gate 3/3 on gleam. tibia's executor will use it for OS port exits.")
inl note_of_lib_otp_actors_17 () = Note ("LIB-OTP-ACTORS", 17u32, "2026-10-23T21:27Z", "x", "2026-10-23 17:27 x: note 15's request is done: main's new_with_self (6525F5CC) gives the initialiser the actor's own subject. runner_shell has switched; its gleam check is queued.")
inl note_of_lib_otp_actors_18 () = Note ("LIB-OTP-ACTORS", 18u32, "2026-10-23T23:55Z", "main", "2026-10-23 19:55 main: status fixed by the item's own two parts. The Gleam arm landed on 10-23 (erlang_process, otp_actor with new/new_with_init/new_with_self/on_message/call/send, otp_supervisor; all gates green), so this is Active 50, not Planned 0. Left: the deterministic single-threaded arm for the other backends (Rust first, the end-product target): the same builder API over an in-process mailbox queue run to quiescence, plus tests that pass on rust and gleam with the same expected text.")
inl note_of_lib_otp_actors_19 () = Note ("LIB-OTP-ACTORS", 19u32, "2026-10-23T23:56Z", "main", "2026-10-23 19:56 main: design for the single-threaded arm (no code yet; it needs compile iterations). (1) Types: erlang_process' nominals are bare Gleam types (subject t = process.Subject); each becomes a backend_switch type record, as dictm does, with a Rust arm. Rust subject t = Rc<RefCell<SpiralMailbox<T>>>, where SpiralMailbox holds a VecDeque<T> queue, an Option<Rc<dyn Fn(T)>> deliver hook and a delivering flag, emitted once through global. (2) Semantics, deterministic run to quiescence: send with no deliver hook pushes to the queue; with a hook it calls deliver unless delivering is already set, in which case it pushes, and after the handler returns it drains the queue in FIFO order, so a handler that sends to itself never recurses. receive pops the front or returns Error at once: timeouts are meaningless in one thread, and an empty mailbox after quiescence is a definite timeout. receive_forever on an empty mailbox fails with a deadlock message instead of hanging. (3) otp_actor on Rust: builder = the initial-state thunk plus the handler. start creates the subject and installs deliver = (state in Rc<RefCell<s>>, handler) mapping Next to Continue(s') or Stop. After Stop the hook is removed and later sends just queue, matching a dead actor's mailbox. call builds a reply subject, sends request reply_subject, and pops the reply, which the synchronous delivery has already put there; an empty reply mailbox is the timeout outcome. (4) spawn on Rust runs the work closure to completion at once, so a spawned process that blocks in receive is the deadlock failure above; monitors report Normal when the work returns and Abnormal(text) on failwith (catch_unwind). (5) Test plan: the existing gleam tests (counter, array state, new_with_self, supervisor restart) gain '///> rust' with the same expected values; supervisor restart on Rust re-runs the child init after a failwith inside the handler. Rust is first because it is the end-product target; other backends follow the same mailbox shape.")
inl note_of_lib_otp_actors_20 () = Note ("LIB-OTP-ACTORS", 20u32, "2026-10-23T23:57Z", "main", "2026-10-23 19:57 main: first step of the single-threaded arm drafted as a standalone Rust probe (scratch mailbox-probe: main.spi plus run.ps1, queued in requeue-after-block.ps1 as main-probe-rust-mailbox). It contains the SpiralMailbox struct, spiral_mailbox_new and spiral_mailbox_send. send pushes, then delivers through the hook unless delivering is already set, and drains FIFO with the borrow released before each handler call. new_subject, send, receive_or and on_deliver exercise it. The main checks FIFO receive (3 then 4), the empty mailbox, and a handler that sends to itself 3 times before replying elsewhere, which needs the reentrancy guard. Expected exit 30143. If it holds, the mailbox becomes erlang_process' Rust subject arm (backend_switch type record), and otp_actor's Rust start installs on_deliver over Rc<RefCell<state>>.")
inl note_of_lib_otp_actors_21 () = Note ("LIB-OTP-ACTORS", 21u32, "2026-10-24T00:32Z", "main", "2026-10-23 20:32 main: next step staged after the probe. %TEMP%\\lib-otp-single\\apply.ps1 (6 anchors, dry run clean on the tree) gives erlang_process' subject and received backend_switch type records: Rust subject = Rc<RefCell<SpiralMailbox<T>>> and received = Option<T>, with the mailbox helpers emitted through global, byte-identical to the probe's. new_subject, send, receive (an empty mailbox after quiescence is the timeout outcome) and received_value get Rust arms; their Gleam arms are unchanged. It applies only if main-probe-rust-mailbox (job-1792801895047) exits 730143, and then goes through lib-gate-kit on erlang_process.spi with '///> rust' added to send_then_receive and receive_times_out.")
inl note_of_lib_otp_actors_22 () = Note ("LIB-OTP-ACTORS", 22u32, "2026-10-24T00:40Z", "main", "2026-10-23 20:40 main: the Rust mailbox probe passed on its first real run (job-1792802071939, exit 730143): FIFO receive, the empty mailbox, a handler sending to itself 3 times drained without recursion, and a counter actor answering call with 7 after Add 3 and Add 4. So %TEMP%\\lib-otp-single\\apply.ps1 (8 anchors now, with '///> rust' on send_then_receive and receive_times_out) is queued: main-lib-otp-single-gate (job-1792802374879) after chain A's fiscal gate, then main-lib-otp-single-land (--after-ok). Next after it lands: otp_actor's Rust start over on_deliver and Rc<RefCell<state>>, as the probe's start_actor did.")
inl note_of_lib_otp_actors_23 () = Note ("LIB-OTP-ACTORS", 23u32, "2026-10-24T03:37Z", "main", "2026-10-23 23:37 main: tibia's 23:3x finding closes a gap I left at 17:15. new_with_init fixed array state for otp_actor callers, but step_runtime.otp_start still called otp_actor.new spec.init, so state built in the caller died at the actor boundary for every step_runtime user. Fix drafted as %TEMP%\\lib-step-init\\apply.ps1 (dry run clean on the live lib, 7 anchors): step_runtime.actor's init becomes () -> s, spawn runs it, otp_start uses new_with_init 10000i32 spec.init, and the lib call/init test timeouts go from 1000 to 10000 ms (tibia's arena_host timed out at 1000 ms under load). Breaking for callers: tibia's arena_host.spi:216 needs init = fun () => (plan, None, ''); tibia was told. Queued: job-1792812964392-main-lib-step-init-gate then job-1792812965354-main-lib-step-init-land, at the end of main's lib chain.")
inl note_of_lib_otp_actors_24 () = Note ("LIB-OTP-ACTORS", 24u32, "2026-10-28T01:33Z", "main", "10-27 21:33 main: Groom 10-27 21:2x (WIP rule, set before applying). main's Active items with no note since 10-24 were classed by the evidence in the tree, not by age alone. (1) SHIPPED -> Done: the item's pass is in the installed tree core (apps/compiler/spiral_compiler.fs, core 8660C34A11F33259, the installed 5m' core), found by its own identifier or anchor, and an accept has passed on that core since (accept-6, run single-flight-20261027-173417, 0 blocking cells). (2) COLD -> Paused: no lane is driving it this week and nothing is queued. Its last note keeps the next step, and it resumes when a lane picks it or its condition fires. (3) KEPT Active: a lane is on it this week, or demand is waiting on it. (4) Blocked: only the user's commit or push unblocks it.\n Verdict for this item: (2) COLD, so Active -> Paused. The next step is the one in its latest note above.")
inl note_of_lib_otp_actors_25 () = Note ("LIB-OTP-ACTORS", 25u32, "2026-10-30T02:04Z", "main", "10-29 22:04 main, groom: RESUMABLE. Every dep is Done (GLEAM-ERLANG-1X), so nothing blocks it; it's Paused only for want of a picker (P1, 50%). Pick-up: the team that needs it (lane-roles), after the pack-7 install.")
inl note_of_lib_otp_actors_26 () = Note ("LIB-OTP-ACTORS", 26u32, "2026-10-30T14:12Z", "main", "10-30 10:12 main, BACKLOG-DANGLING-1030 R2/R4: deps Done, owner team main ('agile next --team main' hands it out), assignee agent (nothing in it is the user's). Resume when that team picks it up; the pack-7 install it waited on is done.")
inl note_of_lib_otp_actors_27 () = Note ("LIB-OTP-ACTORS", 27u32, "2026-10-30T14:25Z", "main", "10-30 10:25 main, resume check on the live tree (not from notes): the Rust arm NEVER LANDED. erlang_process.spi has no SpiralMailbox and no '///> rust' rows; otp_actor.spi (4,904 B, 10-29) is Gleam-only (4 tests, all '///> gleam'). The step-init fix DID land: step_runtime.spi uses new_with_init. Why it didn't land, from healing's restored kit job records (C:\\home\\recovery\\wipe-20261028\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\kit\\jobs): main-lib-otp-single-gate was stopped on request twice (job-1792802374879 and job-1792810375742, exit 143), so both land jobs skipped or stopped. The draft %TEMP%\\lib-otp-single\\apply.ps1 and the probe's sources are LOST in the wipe: they were not MFT-resident, and healing's triage holds only the job metadata files. The probe's RESULT survives in n22 (exit 730143: FIFO receive, empty mailbox = timeout, a 3x self-send drained without recursion, counter actor call = 7). NEXT, owner main: rewrite from n21/n22. (1) Records: Rust subject = Rc<RefCell<SpiralMailbox<T>>>, received = Option<T>, mailbox helpers through global. (2) Rust arms for new_subject, send, receive and received_value. (3) '///> rust' on send_then_receive and receive_times_out. (4) otp_actor's Rust start over on_deliver + Rc<RefCell<state>>. Prove it on a scratch lib copy with lib_gate, then land through lib-guarded-do in a window.")
inl note_of_lib_otp_actors_28 () = Note ("LIB-OTP-ACTORS", 28u32, "2026-10-30T14:26Z", "main", "10-30 10:26 main, CORRECTION to n27's NEXT: don't rewrite the lost lib-otp-single draft. lib/spiral/channel.spi landed since then and already IS the cross-backend mailbox: sender/receiver backend_switch types (Gleam process.Subject, Rust std::sync::mpsc), with channel, send, receive_within (returning erlang_process.receipt) and spawn (Gleam process, Rust threading.spawn). Two '///> rust ///> gleam' tests cover it (a sent message received plus an empty channel timing out; a spawned worker sending across threads). The 10-23 design (a Rust erlang_process.subject over Rc<RefCell<SpiralMailbox>>) would duplicate it. NEXT, owner main: give otp_actor a Rust arm built on channel.spi, not on erlang_process. That needs one design call first, the title's 'deterministic single-threaded arm elsewhere' versus threaded mpsc. Rule: the title is the spec (gate-met-is-not-goal-met), so the default arm is single-threaded deterministic (a run-to-quiescence loop over a channel the caller drains), and a threaded arm via channel.spawn is opt-in. Before drafting, check whether BACKEND-BALANCE's consumers (x, kit, tibia) need the threaded form.")
inl note_of_lib_otp_actors_29 () = Note ("LIB-OTP-ACTORS", 29u32, "2026-10-30T16:08Z", "main", "10-30 12:08 main: CORRECTION to n28, and the item re-scored from the tree. The 'deterministic single-threaded arm elsewhere' in the title ALREADY EXISTS as lib/spiral/step_runtime.spi (172 lines, last change cec8840c on 10-28). One typed actor spec (step_runtime.actor s m) runs two ways: spawn/drain/send/reply/call deterministically on every backend, and otp_start over otp_actor.new_with_init on BEAM. Its test counter_counts_on_every_backend runs on gleam, rust and c, and reactor_actor_steps_each_message_at_the_clock_tag on c and rust. tibia's arena host (apps/host/arena_host.spi:281-297) drives the SAME host_actor both ways. So n28's next step (an otp_actor arm on channel.spi) is DROPPED: it would duplicate step_runtime. Consumer census (10 repos): otp_actor is used only by step_runtime (lib) and tibia's arena host (BEAM by design, tibia confirmed); x and kit use neither; tibia's XCOMPAT-ACTOR-2 slice reshapes Gleam macros only. LEFT: supervisors. otp_supervisor.spi is Gleam-only, and no deterministic supervision exists for the other backends. No consumer needs it today, so it's a feature to file only when a caller appears (the all_digits precedent, LIB-MACRO-HEAL n71). Progress 50 -> 85; Paused until a non-BEAM caller needs supervision. The remaining 15% is that arm.")
inl note_of_lib_otp_actors_30 () = Note ("LIB-OTP-ACTORS", 30u32, "2026-10-31T21:28Z", "main", "10-31 17:28 main, pause re-decision (KIT-PAUSE-RULE-REWORK): Paused -> PLANNED. Its notes name no blocker beyond its dependency GLEAM-ERLANG-1X, which agile next already gates; the 10-30 re-score (85%) and the concrete next step (n28/n29: otp_actor's Rust arm over channel.spi/step_runtime.spi, no rewrite of the lost draft) stand.")
