inl c_nullary_case_singleton () = Task ("C-NULLARY-CASE-SINGLETON", "C heap unions build each nullary case (UH0_Nil()) by malloc + refcount instead of sharing one static immortal object", TaskKind, P1, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "compiler,c,codegen,performance,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_c_nullary_case_singleton () = Planning ("C-NULLARY-CASE-SINGLETON", Minutes 120u32, Load Heavy, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_c_nullary_case_singleton_1 () = Note ("C-NULLARY-CASE-SINGLETON", 1u32, "2026-10-23T14:30Z", "main", "2026-10-23 10:30 main: found reading samples/native_closure_union_rec/main.c. UH0 * UH0_Nil() { malloc; tag = 1; refc = 1 }, so every list end and every payload-free case pays a malloc and a free through UHDecref0. A census of the tree main.c files finds 46 nullary heap-union constructors in 19 files. The design must keep the decref path branch-light: (a) a static object per nullary case with an immortal refcount, where UHDecref0 never frees an object whose refc is above a sentinel bound, so the static's refc starts at INT32_MAX / 2 and increments wrap safely below the sentinel; or (b) the tag lives in the pointer for nullary cases (a tagged-pointer null per case), which changes every switch on x->tag. Rule, written before measuring: pick (a) unless the benchmark shows decref overhead above 2% on a refcount-heavy sample (bench-pair -Native on mega_brzozowski_derivatives runtime_native and native_closure_union_rec, 5 rounds, QuietCores 2); adopt only if allocation-heavy samples speed up by 5% or more and every C row stays bit-identical in exit and stdout. Same question for Zig, which allocates per box.")
inl note_of_c_nullary_case_singleton_2 () = Note ("C-NULLARY-CASE-SINGLETON", 2u32, "2026-10-23T14:58Z", "main", "2026-10-23 10:58 main: option (a) drafted, %TEMP%\\pack-next9\\c-nullary-singleton.ps1 (1 anchor in union_tmpl's heap constructor), composed as pack-next9 on next8 (3957189B) -> core 86448170CAD44C75, hopacsota lint G1/G2 pass. A nullary UH case emits 'static UHi x = { .refc = INT32_MAX/2, .tag = t }; return &x;'. No decref sentinel is needed: C refcounts are single-threaded and balanced, so the static's count cannot reach 0; the code read found no refc == 1 reuse path and free only inside UHDecref. Pending (CPU, after tibia's block): fsc --parseonly, then the pre-written rule from the 10:30 note (bench-pair -Native on mega_brzozowski_derivatives and native_closure_union_rec, 5 rounds, QuietCores 2; adopt on >=5% on allocation-heavy samples with every C row bit-identical). Zig question still open.")
inl note_of_c_nullary_case_singleton_3 () = Note ("C-NULLARY-CASE-SINGLETON", 3u32, "2026-10-23T23:42Z", "main", "2026-10-23 19:42 main: mirror survey across backends on samples/native_closure_union_rec. Rust already shares nullary cases (a thread_local Rc, cloned), and F#, Lean, Gleam and Python use plain constructors or values. Swift's case is a struct, so no allocation. Kotlin (class UH0_Nil instantiated per use) and Lua (function Uh0_Nil() return { tag = ... } end, a new table per call) allocate per nullary case like C did. Mirror drafted as pack-next10 on next9: %TEMP%\\pack-next10\\nullary-mirrors.ps1 has 4 anchors, each matching once on the tree and next9. Kotlin: object instead of class, and the zero and box sites name the object without (). Lua: the heap case becomes NAME = (function() local shared = {...} return function() return shared end end)(), so no new chunk-level local, staying under Lua 5.1's 200-local limit, and the call sites are unchanged. Lua stack unions (Us) and TypeScript are left as they are: whether anything mutates or compares those tables or objects by identity is unverified. Review rule (next10_rules.py, checked with positive and negative controls): a changed main.kt or main.lua must equal its snapshot with exactly these rewrites applied, no other file changes, and native results and DISAGREE 0 as baseline. Compose and review wait for the next9 review.")
inl note_of_c_nullary_case_singleton_4 () = Note ("C-NULLARY-CASE-SINGLETON", 4u32, "2026-10-23T23:48Z", "main", "2026-10-23 19:48 main: Wasm added to the pack-next10 mirror. Its bump allocator never frees, so a nullary case allocated per use (alloc 8, store the tag) was also an unbounded leak in loops. Now each distinct tag gets one 8-byte static data segment [tag][pad], interned like string literals at data_end before heap_start is computed. Objects with the same tag are byte-identical and immutable, so tag alone is the key across unions. Boxing a nullary case returns (i32.const at). The patch has 6 anchors (Kotlin 3, Lua 1, Wasm 2), each matching once on the tree. The patched F# was read on a scratch copy: the arm is if/else with the allocation path under else, and nullary_object sits after intern. The rule for main.wat is that allocation calls must not increase, with the matrix's native check (DISAGREE 0) deciding.")
inl note_of_c_nullary_case_singleton_5 () = Note ("C-NULLARY-CASE-SINGLETON", 5u32, "2026-10-23T23:51Z", "main", "2026-10-23 19:51 main: Delphi added to pack-next10 (7 anchors now). Its nullary heap constructor did Result := TUH0.Create per call, and none of the 412 generated main.pas files frees anything, so every Nil leaked an object. Now: var <case>_shared: TUHn = nil; plus a constructor that creates it on first use and returns it. FPC/Delphi allow an initialized global var between routines. Stack unions (TUS, records) are unchanged. next10_rules.py predicts the main.pas rewrite exactly and was checked on the real sample text (1 file within the rule, 0 violations). Order: next10's review compares against the installed outputs, so it runs only after next9 is reviewed and installed; review.ps1 and compose.ps1 are in %TEMP%\\pack-next10.")
inl note_of_c_nullary_case_singleton_6 () = Note ("C-NULLARY-CASE-SINGLETON", 6u32, "2026-10-24T00:00Z", "main", "2026-10-23 20:00 main: TypeScript added to pack-next10 (8 anchors). It was held back on an identity risk, now settled by a code read: TS's EQ lowering (a === b, core 174081) serves primitives only. Union equality reaches codegen as a multi-scrutinee unbox comparing tags (v{a}.tag === v{b}.tag at 173971), so no union object is ever compared by identity. A nullary case now emits const <case>_shared: U = { tag: i }; and the function returns it; the types are readonly already. next10_rules.py predicts the main.ts rewrite and was checked on the real sample (1 file within the rule). Python is skipped on purpose: a fieldless NamedTuple per call is cheap, and Python is the slowest target anyway. Swift's nullary case is a struct (no allocation), and Rust already shares a thread_local Rc.")
inl note_of_c_nullary_case_singleton_7 () = Note ("C-NULLARY-CASE-SINGLETON", 7u32, "2026-10-29T17:52Z", "compiler", "10-29 13:5x compiler: SHIPPED, but the item still showed 0% Planned. Committed C output at HEAD, samples/native_closure_union_rec/main.c:47-50: 'UH0 * UH0_Nil() { static UH0 x = { .refc = 1073741823, .tag = 1 }; return &x; }' (1073741823 = INT32_MAX/2, written as a literal), i.e. option (a) from n1/n2. The next10 mirrors are in the core as well (Kotlin object, Delphi '<case>_shared' first-use global at spiral_compiler.fs:165680, plus the Lua/Wasm/TS rewrites per n3-n6). Bit-identity half of the n1 rule: met by every accepted review since (DISAGREE 0). NOT met: the speed half. No note records the pre-registered bench (bench-pair -Native on mega_brzozowski_derivatives runtime_native and native_closure_union_rec, 5 rounds, QuietCores 2; adopt only at >=5% on allocation-heavy samples). Remaining = that bench, run after the fact: installed core vs the same core with the singleton anchor reverted. If it is under 5%, the decision goes back to main (the Delphi/Wasm halves fix unbounded leaks regardless, so they stay). Progress 90.")
inl note_of_c_nullary_case_singleton_8 () = Note ("C-NULLARY-CASE-SINGLETON", 8u32, "2026-10-29T18:27Z", "compiler", "10-29 14:3x compiler: the pre-registered bench, run after the fact (node, gcc 15.2, matrix C flags -std=c11 -O2 -fwrapv -w; committed HEAD main.c = arm S, the static singleton; arm M = the same file with each nullary constructor rewritten back to the old malloc form, i.e. the pre-change output byte for byte; arm Saa = an A/A copy of S; 15 rotated rounds x 15 runs, verdict = median of per-round ratios). brz runtime_native (6 nullary ctors): S 15.75 ms, A/A 0.924, M/S 1.095. native_closure_union_rec (1 ctor): S 15.81 ms, A/A 1.194, M/S 1.167. Exits and stdout identical in every arm. A first 5-round run under heavier node load gave A/A 1.04/1.19 and M/S 0.78/1.36 (opposite directions). VERDICT: INCONCLUSIVE, not a win or a loss. Both samples the rule names run ~15 ms, mostly process start, so the A/A spread (up to 19%) exceeds any allocation effect and the >=5% threshold cannot be decided on them. What would decide it: an allocation-heavy micro-sample (build and drop millions of list ends) with in-process timing. Not worth a pack slot on its own: the change is correct (every accepted review since, DISAGREE 0) and its Delphi/Wasm halves fix unbounded leaks. Recommendation: keep it shipped, close on correctness, and drop the speed claim from the title's motivation. Main decides. Bench script: compiler scratchpad nullary-bench/bench.ps1.")
inl note_of_c_nullary_case_singleton_9 () = Note ("C-NULLARY-CASE-SINGLETON", 9u32, "2026-10-29T18:33Z", "compiler", "10-29 14:3x compiler: DONE on correctness (main agreed 14:3x). The change shipped (committed C output uses the static singleton; every accepted review since has DISAGREE 0), and the Delphi/Wasm mirrors fix unbounded leaks. The n1 speed claim is withdrawn, not refuted: the two samples the rule names run ~15 ms each, mostly process start, and the A/A spread measured in the same job (0.924 and 1.194) is wider than any >=5% effect could be, so they cannot carry the claim (n8). A future speed claim needs an allocation-heavy sample with in-process timing.")
