inl note_of_x_per_account_min_1_1 () = Note ("X-PER-ACCOUNT-MIN-1", 1u32, "2026-10-23T16:22Z", "x", "2026-10-23 12:22 x: user request 2026-10-23 ~11:58: 'make --per-account have a minimum of 1 instead of 5'. Today cli.spi validates 5..100 (usage line :81 '5..100 (default 5)'; the test at :422 per_account_below_the_api_minimum_is_rejected expects 'rejected: --per-account expects a number from 5 to 100, got 4'), because X's GET /2/users/:id/tweets takes max_results 5..100, and args.spi:38 passes per_account straight through as max_results. DESIGN: (1) cli accepts 1..100, with the usage line and rejection text saying 1 to 100; (2) the request asks for max(5, N) (a wire-side constant named for the API floor); (3) after fetching, each account's posts are cut to its newest N before merging, so --limit and --per-author keep their meaning; (4) tests: the cli test pins 1 accepted and 0 rejected, and a unit test checks the per-account cut (N=1 out of 5 fetched keeps the newest). e2e gets one tutorial step, 'xc timeline --per-account 1 alice bob', showing one post each. COST NOTE for the README line: below 5, X still bills the 5 posts it returns, so --per-account 1 trims what is shown but doesn't cut paid reads (X-READ-BUDGET's cost line would show it). Lands after X-DONE with the other client drafts; the draft and verification go on a scratch copy while the tree is staged.")
inl note_of_x_per_account_min_1_2 () = Note ("X-PER-ACCOUNT-MIN-1", 2u32, "2026-10-23T16:30Z", "x", "2026-10-23 12:30 x: drafted as C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-precommit-next\\drafts\\apply_per_account_min_1.ps1 (in land_all and apply_all_check: 12 of 12 drafts apply together, no new name clashes). cli: range 1..100, the usage line says 'posts shown per account, 1..100 (default 5; X sends at least 5)', and the test now rejects 0 and accepts 1 ('accounts alice per=1 ...'). wire: requested_posts n = max(timeline_floor () = 5, n) in posts_path, plus a test that n=1 asks for max_results=5. fetch: each primary entry carries 'keep' = N, and refs are taken only from the kept posts, so a smaller N also means fewer reference lookups. digest: kept_posts cuts each account's primary posts to the first N (X and the e2e stub both return newest first; the stub sorts by created_at descending), and a test checks keep '1' -> 1 of 2, '' -> all. Negative control already in hand: today's stub answers 400 to max_results < 5, so without the floor the e2e step would fail. Cells on cli, wire, fetch, digest queued (self-gating chain8).")
