inl note_of_x_per_account_min_1_11 () = Note ("X-PER-ACCOUNT-MIN-1", 11u32, "2026-10-27T07:20Z", "x", "2026-10-27 03:20 x: UNBLOCKED. Main's Zig index_of fix LANDED in lib, unstaged (chain main-serial-zig-index-of-6, gate and land exit 0 under the fixed runner). The whole-repo verify is ready but NOT started: it arrived after the standing autonomous window ended (10-26 21:00), so it waits for the next session. To run: sync spiral and x to node mirror xv11, push %TEMP%\\x-verify-full\\verify11.ps1 to C:\\home\\remote-stage\\xv11, start it (compiler pinned; re-stage the pinned DLL if 5n is installed by then), and run actions-lock locally. On exit 0, close this item, X-COMPAT-VS-POSTABLE and X-STUB-WEIGHTED-LIMIT.")
inl note_of_x_per_account_min_1_12 () = Note ("X-PER-ACCOUNT-MIN-1", 12u32, "2026-10-28T10:43Z", "x", "2026-10-28 06:39 x: CLOSED by X-WIP-VERIFY's pre-registered rule. verify25 (node job-1793174907446, pinned compiler 4FD712E9E357) GREEN on every clause of X-WIP-VERIFY note 6: exit 0; sim accept and plain sim build exit 0 (ExUnit 45/45, corpus commute ok, thread_run pass, tamper rejected with same_cells=false); client gate 29 crates 0 over 1000; x-compat 0 in 43 client and 24 sim files; all 42 client and 23 sim notebooks passed, 0 failing totals; plain-pass e2e 37 of 37 tutorial steps same, 0 FAILED, 153 stub requests. Outputs landed three-way (node-proof -Land x-verify25: 21 files plus 19 Gleam modules, 0 conflicts; net.rs held back because its source changed after the sync, so verify26 lands it).")
inl eoie_queued_jobs_in_host () = Task ("EOIE-QUEUED-JOBS-IN-HOST", "queued jobs wait as job files held by the host, not as one resident supervisor each; the supervisor starts when the job's after-chain clears and its gate grants", Story, P0, D4, 100u32, Done, "EOIE-RELEASE-TRAIN", "kit,jobs,process", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_eoie_queued_jobs_in_host () = Planning ("EOIE-QUEUED-JOBS-IN-HOST", Minutes 30u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_eoie_queued_jobs_in_host_1 () = Note ("EOIE-QUEUED-JOBS-IN-HOST", 1u32, "2026-10-23T16:30Z", "eoie", "12:30 eoie, from the user's 12:2x question ('a shit ton of eoie processes, is our product stable?'). Census 12:25 (scratch eoie-process-census.ps1): 26 eoie processes = 2 hosts (the pre-install one draining) + 18 supervisors + 6 job waits; 12 of the 18 supervisors serve QUEUED jobs (main's 9-job serial chain, my GE2 chain, a raw gate run, a typecheck), each polling its after file every 250 ms or its gate every 5 s. Measured over 10 s: all 25 used 672 ms CPU (0.84% of the box) and 161 MB. No leak: every waiter and supervisor targets a live job, every after-chain points at a live job, and the only dead parent is the draining host. Design: the host already runs; it can hold the queue (after-waits and gate polls in one loop) and spawn a job's supervisor at grant, so idle queued jobs cost no process. Rule before building: adopt it if a 12-job queue drops eoie processes by 12 and CPU by at least half, with the stop_cli, after and windows CLI tests unchanged.")
inl note_of_eoie_queued_jobs_in_host_2 () = Note ("EOIE-QUEUED-JOBS-IN-HOST", 2u32, "2026-10-23T17:05Z", "eoie", "13:05 eoie: decision model drafted and passing (scratch f6/src/eoie_job_queue, HostFree, LibReferenced): a queued_job (stop request, NoChain or Chained predecessor, mode, predecessor state) maps to KeepWaiting | StartSupervisor | SettleStopped | SettleSkipped detail, with the same rules and skip detail as eoie_job_after's after_outcome_of today ('--after-ok: <id> ended exit N'). The host's loop would fold this over its queued job files every beat and spawn a supervisor only on StartSupervisor; a serial chain behind a running job costs 0 processes (test). Gate waits (slot and window) stay in the supervisor for now, since they need a live process holding the ticket. Next: the host loop (eoie_job_host host_run), after release M.")
inl note_of_eoie_queued_jobs_in_host_3 () = Note ("EOIE-QUEUED-JOBS-IN-HOST", 3u32, "2026-10-23T17:12Z", "eoie", "13:12 eoie: host design, read from eoie_job_host/host_run.spi (host_served drains request files and starts a supervisor per request; supervisor_requested waits 5 s for the supervisor pid, then falls back to a detached start). (1) On a request whose job has an after file, the host evaluates eoie_job_queue's step_of from the job files: KeepWaiting writes shell-host/held/<id> and starts nothing; StartSupervisor starts the supervisor as today; SettleStopped and SettleSkipped write the job's exit line exactly as eoie_job_after does (143 stopped, 125 skipped with the same detail). (2) Each 500 ms beat the host re-evaluates only shell-host/held/ (no scan of the whole jobs root), so the held set lives in files and survives a host restart or upgrade; the host is not idle while held/ is non-empty. (3) supervisor_requested treats a held marker as taken and returns 'held', so job start prints supervisor_pid=held and never falls back to a detached supervisor for a held job. (4) job stop needs no change: its stop file is read by step_of. eoie_job_after stays for the fallback path and older hosts. Tests: a three-job serial chain shows one supervisor process at a time; stop of a held job settles 143; --after-ok skip detail unchanged; a host restart re-holds from held/. Risk: host_run is what HP fixed today, so this lands only after GE2's acceptance and with HP's 6 checks rerun.")
inl note_of_eoie_queued_jobs_in_host_4 () = Note ("EOIE-QUEUED-JOBS-IN-HOST", 4u32, "2026-10-23T20:25Z", "eoie", "16:25 eoie: host loop drafted and typechecked; it ships as release P (after O, before N), its own release because it changes the host every job depends on. Slice: scratch queued-in-host/apply-queued-in-host.ps1 + blocks/queue.spi. eoie_job_host gains queue: step_of over a queued_job read from the job files (after = predecessor TAB mode; predecessor state via job_state_now, so a missing predecessor reads as lost, exactly like eoie_job_after). host_served: a request whose predecessor is live is recorded as <jobs>/held/<id> (its executable) instead of starting a supervisor. Each 500 ms beat, held_served re-evaluates held/: KeepWaiting writes the job's heartbeat_ms, which keeps readers from declaring it lost after 15 s; StartSupervisor starts it; a stop writes 143 and an --after-ok on a failed predecessor writes 125, with eoie_job_after's exact details. Held jobs count as live, so the host does not idle-exit. supervisor_awaited returns SupervisorHeld 'held' (or 'settled'), so job start prints supervisor_pid=held. Notebooks on the post-O tree pass (16:13 and 16:17): queue (4 model tests plus a read-only IO probe), host_run (probe reaching host_run and supervisor_requested), host_model. after-install-P on today's eoie: the held check fails as it must (supervisor_pid=131976, 1 supervisor); run-after-head, skip detail ('--after-ok: <id> ended finished exit 3'), stop 143 ('stopped on request while waiting for <id>') and HP 6/6 pass, and P must keep them passing. Today's details match the model's strings exactly. The train order is now GE2, HIJK, L, M, O, P, N.")
inl note_of_eoie_queued_jobs_in_host_5 () = Note ("EOIE-QUEUED-JOBS-IN-HOST", 5u32, "2026-10-23T21:05Z", "eoie", "17:05 eoie: review of P against every verb that touches a queued job, read in the post-O tree. job stop writes the stop file and waits 15 s for a settle (eoie_job_control control.spi:47-53), and the host settles a held job within one 500 ms beat, so it works unchanged. job amend refuses only once a pid file exists, and a held job has none, so the new limit is read when its supervisor starts. job after (M) rewrites the after file, which the host re-reads every beat. job wait and status read state, and the beaten heartbeat keeps the job queued. Known risk, accepted for P with a follow-up before P installs: if the host process dies while jobs are held, nobody beats them and the first reader marks them lost after 15 s, whereas today each queued job's own supervisor would outlive a host crash. Follow-up slice: job status, list and wait call the existing host-start path (supervisor_requested's HostMissing branch) when held/ is non-empty and host.pid is dead, so a reader revives the queue instead of burying it. P's acceptance gains a probe: kill the isolated-home host with a held job, run job status, and the job must still finish.")
inl note_of_eoie_queued_jobs_in_host_6 () = Note ("EOIE-QUEUED-JOBS-IN-HOST", 6u32, "2026-10-23T21:07Z", "eoie", "17:07 eoie: enforced mechanically: launch-P refuses until queue.spi carries the revival follow-up (marker held_revived), and P moved to the end of the train (GE2, HIJK, L, M, O, N, P; N rebases on O again, P on N), so P's refusal ends the train after N instead of stranding N. P can't reach install tonight in any case (GE2's regen alone runs past 18:00).")
inl note_of_eoie_queued_jobs_in_host_7 () = Note ("EOIE-QUEUED-JOBS-IN-HOST", 7u32, "2026-10-23T21:26Z", "eoie", "17:26 eoie: the follow-up from note 5 is now in P's slice, so launch-P's gate (held_revived) passes. (1) A held job is never declared lost by silence: in job_state_now's lost branch, a job with a held/<id> file reads Live JobPending, since it has no process that could be lost. This removes the 15 s race in which a reader would bury a held job before a revived host beats it. (2) eoie_job_host exports eoie_job_host_revive (held_revived: idle | running | revived | error). It starts a host through the existing detached path when held/ is non-empty and host.pid is dead, and job status and job wait call it first (eoie_jobs already depends on the eoie-job-host crate, so no cycle). Checks on a tree rebuilt as post-M plus O plus P (17:12 and 17:23): host_run and queue notebooks pass, and the jobs.spi probe reaches job_status and job_waited with only the standalone link error (eoie_job_host crate). after-install-P gains 'held: survives a host crash': kill the isolated home's host while a job is held, wait 20 s (past the old silence limit), run job status, and the job must finish exit 0. A slip caught in my own slice: a here-string without its trailing newline merged two statements into one line (it parsed, but the second swap never ran). Found by checking the dry-run output, not the parse.")
inl note_of_eoie_queued_jobs_in_host_8 () = Note ("EOIE-QUEUED-JOBS-IN-HOST", 8u32, "2026-10-24T09:20Z", "eoie", "05:1x eoie, user asked again ('damn we have a lot of eoie processes up, is this drift or intended?'), the second time after 10-23 12:2x. Census: 61 eoie processes = 1 host + 56 supervisors + 3 caller waiters + 1 window run. 8 jobs run; 52 are queued, 34 on --after (release P's held queue removes these) and 18 on a slot or window gate (P keeps those in the supervisor). Each idle supervisor costs ~7 MB plus a ~7.5 MB conhost (stage R removes the conhost): ~800 MB in all on a memory-tight box. Priority raised P2 -> P0 because the user repeated the request; the gate-wait remainder is EOIE-QUEUED-JOBS-NO-PROCESS.")
inl note_of_eoie_queued_jobs_in_host_9 () = Note ("EOIE-QUEUED-JOBS-IN-HOST", 9u32, "2026-10-24T11:22Z", "eoie", "07:2x groom: estimate set from what remains, not from the item text. Release P carries the held queue in tonight's train, behind GE3 -> HIJK -> L -> M -> O -> N -> Q. Left: P's install and its after-install check (a queued --after job shows no supervisor; job stop, amend and after on a held job keep their receipts), about 30 min of check time once P lands.")
inl note_of_eoie_queued_jobs_in_host_10 () = Note ("EOIE-QUEUED-JOBS-IN-HOST", 10u32, "2026-10-24T11:23Z", "eoie", "07:23 groom: blocked on the train. The only work left is to install release P and run its after-install check, so 'agile next' must not hand this out as ready work. Back to Active when release P installs.")
inl note_of_eoie_queued_jobs_in_host_11 () = Note ("EOIE-QUEUED-JOBS-IN-HOST", 11u32, "2026-10-30T14:11Z", "kit", "10-30 10:1x kit: after-install check PASSED on the installed kit C528BE4D. A queued --after job is held by the host (supervisor_pid=held, 0 kit processes for it); job amend and job stop on the held job both return receipts. This was the only remaining work (07:2x groom note), so the item closes.")
inl eoie_jobs_doctor () = Task ("EOIE-JOBS-DOCTOR", "eoie jobs doctor: one call that audits every eoie process and job (host liveness and parent, supervisors whose job settled, job waits on settled or missing jobs, after-chains to missing jobs, window rows of dead holders, detached environment vs caller) and exits 1 with one row per finding", Story, P1, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "eoie,jobs,process", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_eoie_jobs_doctor () = Planning ("EOIE-JOBS-DOCTOR", Minutes 60u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_eoie_jobs_doctor_1 () = Note ("EOIE-JOBS-DOCTOR", 1u32, "2026-10-23T16:30Z", "eoie", "12:30 eoie: today's checks were hand-rolled scripts (scratch eoie-process-census.ps1 for the user's 12:2x stability question; the detached 'window check' and 'cmd /c set' probes that found the EOIE_WINDOWS_HOME leak at 10:4x). By the generic-tools rule each method must be an eoie command, not a scratch script. Typed findings: DeadParent pid | SettledButAlive job | WaitOnSettled job | ChainToMissing job | OrphanWindow id | HostEnvDiffers names (the detached environment probe; today it would have shown EOIE_WINDOWS_HOME, CARGO_BUILD_JOBS and a missing EOIE_LEASE_POLICY). The census today: 0 findings except the draining host. Spec examples: today's 10:4x leak (HostEnvDiffers EOIE_WINDOWS_HOME) and 12:25 census.")
inl note_of_eoie_jobs_doctor_2 () = Note ("EOIE-JOBS-DOCTOR", 2u32, "2026-10-23T16:38Z", "eoie", "12:38 eoie: pure model drafted and passing (scratch f6/src/eoie_jobs_doctor, HostFree, LibReferenced). role_of reads a command line into JobHost | JobSupervisor job | JobWaiter job | WindowHolder | OtherCall. process_findings: a dead-parent host counts only when it serves no job (IdleOrphanHost), because a host draining after an install is by design; a supervisor of a settled job is SettledButSupervised, and a waiter on a settled or missing job is WaitOnSettled/WaitOnMissing. chain_findings: ChainToMissing. drift compares a detached job's environment with the caller's over the steering prefixes (EOIE_, SPIRAL_, CARGO_, RUSTUP_; EOIE_JOB_* excepted) and reads today's leak as +EOIE_WINDOWS_HOME +CARGO_BUILD_JOBS -EOIE_LEASE_POLICY. Tests: the 12:25 census (draining host, no finding), an idle orphan host, stale waiters, roles, chains, drift. Next: the verb crate, with snapshots through eoie_machine_watch's sysinfo rows, job state from the job files, and the detached env probe as a 'cmd /c set' job.")
inl note_of_eoie_jobs_doctor_3 () = Note ("EOIE-JOBS-DOCTOR", 3u32, "2026-10-23T16:59Z", "eoie", "12:59 eoie: verb drafted as 'eoie job doctor [--env]' (scratch job-doctor/apply-job-doctor.ps1; crate eoie_jobs_doctor: model + check + entry). check.spi reads the eoie processes through eoie_machine_watch's sysinfo snapshot (role and parent liveness), job standing from the job files, and the after-chains from every job folder. --env runs the same 'cmd /c set' (or 'env') attached and as a detached job and diffs the variable names, which avoids parsing the NUL-separated snapshot. Notebooks: model and check pass on f6 with the real legacy packages copied in. The slice's anchors are order-independent with job-rechain (both orders tried: union tail JobStop, JobAfter, JobChain, JobDoctor, JobUnknown); job-rechain's case anchor was made order-independent for this. In release M: 8 candidate notebooks, baseline merge, and acceptance 'job doctor --env' must print ok, which proves the env-leak class fixed end to end once GE2's caller environment is in.")
inl note_of_eoie_jobs_doctor_4 () = Note ("EOIE-JOBS-DOCTOR", 4u32, "2026-10-23T17:25Z", "eoie", "13:25 eoie: companion slice from the same question (scratch waiting-for/apply-waiting-for.ps1, in release M): job status/list/wait print waiting_for=after:<id> | window:<class> | heavy-slot after state=queued. A queued job no longer looks the same whether it is stuck or correctly waiting on its predecessor. gate_queued became a typed gate_wait union over the same job files (lib sm' calls instead of rust_std_string); the stop_cli after test asserts the predecessor. eoie_jobs model notebook and the doctor's check notebook pass with it; all four M slices applied together on a src copy (union and routes consistent). after-install-M asserts 'state=queued waiting_for=after:<slow id>' for a chained job.")
inl note_of_eoie_jobs_doctor_5 () = Note ("EOIE-JOBS-DOCTOR", 5u32, "2026-10-24T02:23Z", "main", "2026-10-23 22:2x main: consolidated pendencies covered here (EOIE-CONSOLIDATED-INTAKE, class a): TOOL-4 (helper and daemon lifecycle: stale compiler servers reaped by identity, with a leak receipt). Spec text: Downloads/eoie-consolidated/agile_eoie.md.")
inl note_of_eoie_jobs_doctor_6 () = Note ("EOIE-JOBS-DOCTOR", 6u32, "2026-10-24T03:06Z", "eoie", "23:06 groom: add the unwatched-jobs check from the 22:14-22:45 audit (EOIE-JOBS note 49). A live job is unwatched when no job wait/run process waits on it or on any successor reachable through after links. Print the sample time. Also flag supervisors whose conhost exists (until EOIE-SUPERVISOR-NO-CONSOLE ships) and hosts anchored outside their owner session (until EOIE-JOB-OWNER-PARENT).")
inl note_of_eoie_jobs_doctor_7 () = Note ("EOIE-JOBS-DOCTOR", 7u32, "2026-10-24T05:59Z", "eoie", "01:59 case from main (01:5x): a Spiral compiler server (dotnet SpiralCompiler.dll --server-tcp ...spiral-cells-<erlpid>-<n>.sock --stall-seconds 120) outlived its parent and its erl. The pid in the socket name no longer existed, and it sat at 0 CPU for 25+ min despite --stall-seconds 120 (it later exited by itself). Doctor should flag compiler servers whose socket's erl pid is dead and that have idled past their own stall limit, suggest 'quit' on the socket, and attribute them by start time against the job registry.")
inl note_of_eoie_jobs_doctor_8 () = Note ("EOIE-JOBS-DOCTOR", 8u32, "2026-10-24T23:43Z", "eoie", "19:43 Provisional estimate 60m from the grooming rule (EOIE-AGILE-CHECK-FIELDS note): D3 at 50%. Replace it with milestone sizing.")
inl note_of_eoie_jobs_doctor_9 () = Note ("EOIE-JOBS-DOCTOR", 9u32, "2026-10-27T23:20Z", "eoie", "19:2x eoie: audit of the live doctor against the title. 'kit job doctor' ships, and today it reads 'ok processes=6 findings=0'. It covers idle orphan hosts, settled-but-supervised, waits on settled or missing jobs, chains to missing jobs, and --env drift. Window rows of dead holders don't need a doctor finding: kit_machine_windows prunes them on every window operation (live_rows -> orphaned_window: a ProcessHolder whose pid in the row id is dead gets WindowPruned). So the open gap is note 7's orphaned Cells compiler servers. Draft: scratchpad clean\\jobs-doctor\\orphan-servers.do, 7/7 on package copies. The model gains server_fact {pid, owner, owner_alive} and the finding OrphanCompilerServer (pid, owner). serves_cells means a command with --server-tcp and spiral-cells-; cells_owner_of reads the erl pid from the socket name spiral-cells-<erlpid>-<n>.sock. server_findings ignores an owner that doesn't parse. The finding line is 'orphan-compiler-server pid=.. owner=.. (the erl named in its socket is gone; send quit on the socket or stop the pid)'. It has a rust test block. check.spi scans every process row, not only kit*. Node compile (job-1793143001848-kit-doctor-regen, box DLL 4FD712E9E357): it typechecks, but kit_jobs_doctor goes from 887 to 1091 generated lines, over the 1,000-line gate. The growth comes from the inlined word_after, split and try_convert (servers_from is 89 lines) plus the new finding case. The control crates are byte-identical. Next: split the server scan into its own crate (hand-modeled unit, kit_jobs_doctor_servers exporting the findings text), so the doctor crate only calls it; or parse the owner with a lighter lib primitive. Both go in the release after TRAIN.")
inl note_of_eoie_jobs_doctor_10 () = Note ("EOIE-JOBS-DOCTOR", 10u32, "2026-10-27T23:35Z", "eoie", "19:3x eoie: split done and proven on the node (job-1793143757656, box DLL 4FD712E9E357). The new crate kit_jobs_doctor_servers (HostFree + LibReferenced standards; packages spiral-, kit_machine_watch, kit_jobs_doctor_model; sysinfo dep) holds the process scan and exports kit_job_doctor_server_report : () -> i32 * string. doctor_run takes it the way it takes env_drift, prints the lines, and counts them in findings=. Generated sizes: kit_jobs_doctor 887 -> 917, kit_jobs_doctor_servers 502, kit_jobs_doctor_env byte-identical. The model keeps server_fact, OrphanCompilerServer, serves_cells, cells_owner_of, server_findings and the rust test block. Land as: scratchpad clean\\jobs-doctor\\stage (manifest.tsv, 5 new files) plus orphan-servers.do (11 edits: model, check, entry, the doctor's Cargo dep, the workspace member). Release after TRAIN, then run 'kit job doctor' against a real orphaned server: start Cells, kill its erl, expect orphan-compiler-server.")
inl note_of_eoie_jobs_doctor_11 () = Note ("EOIE-JOBS-DOCTOR", 11u32, "2026-10-29T15:38Z", "kit", "10-29 11:3x kit: shipped in TRAIN3 (kit.exe CD2F0D1A, 11:35) with the kit_jobs_doctor_servers crate; live: 'kit job doctor' -> 'ok processes=9 findings=0'. That is a smoke check (the old binary also printed ok), and the crate's own notebooks passed in the candidate run (kit_jobs_doctor_servers/servers.spi, kit_jobs_doctor/check.spi). Done.")
inl x_format_client () = Task ("X-FORMAT-CLIENT", "x: format apps/client and apps/sim's Spiral with the canonical formatter after the drafts land, so eoie standards' line-width 120 passes without an x override", Story, P3, D2, 100u32, Done, "X-DONE,X-SIM-FRAMES-GLEAM-IMPORTS", "x,format,standards", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_x_format_client () = Planning ("X-FORMAT-CLIENT", Minutes 45u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_x_format_client_1 () = Note ("X-FORMAT-CLIENT", 1u32, "2026-10-23T18:21Z", "x", "2026-10-23 14:21 x: decision on eoie's question (EOIE-STANDARDS-CHECKS note 4 follow-up): x declares NO LineWidth override. 120 is the formatter's own limit (memory formatter-keep-breaks: it re-lays only what exceeds 120), so x's 136 over-wide client lines are unformatted code, not a different standard. ORDER: after land_all.ps1 -Go, because the 14 drafts are anchored on today's exact text and formatting first would break their anchors (the reformat-peer-repos lesson, applied to my own patch chain). Steps: SpiralCompiler format on each .spi (copy first, read 3 files by hand), byte-compare the generated Rust and Gleam before and after (tibia did that for 15 outputs), then Cells and eoie standards (expect 0 line-width findings, and 0 test-targets findings once eoie's release M ships).")
inl note_of_x_format_client_2 () = Note ("X-FORMAT-CLIENT", 2u32, "2026-10-24T00:43Z", "x", "2026-10-23 20:43 x: scope update from the 10-23 evening drafts, so the formatting pass covers everything that lands. Post-land scripts touch apps/client args/args.spi, xc/xc.spi, write/write.spi (+ README), wire.spi, thread/thread.spi, paging.spi, prelude.spi. Lib-dependent delegations touch the same files. apply_sim_modules adds 17 sim modules (inbox ... xsim_shell_entry) and apply_xsim_crate adds xsim/xsim.spi. Order: format only after all of them land and pass, then x-compat (XC005) on the formatted cells, because re-laying can move cell weights. Rules from memory formatter-keep-breaks: never join authored lines, re-lay only lines over 120, and apply to unstaged files only.")
inl note_of_x_format_client_3 () = Note ("X-FORMAT-CLIENT", 3u32, "2026-10-24T03:08Z", "x", "23:08 x grooming: main installed a formatter-only compiler pack at 22:18 (B553EF6B78E6EE29, single-flight): no parens around join heads, 'then' placement follows each file's habit, deep-indent room max 40. Run this item with that compiler (or newer), and compare against a format of the same tree with it, not against earlier 10-23 formats. Still waits for the user to land x's queued drafts.")
inl note_of_x_format_client_4 () = Note ("X-FORMAT-CLIENT", 4u32, "2026-10-24T23:45Z", "x", "10-24 19:45 x: grooming: the condition 'after the drafts land' is MET (HEAD 6ef9c48, user commit 05:20). What's left is the unstaged fix layer: x-compat fixes, chain_json, e2e expectations, plural, and the frames imports. Start when verify-landing5 passes and the user commits that layer. Its anchors are the last chain this pass could break. Use main's newest formatter pack (n3).")
inl note_of_x_format_client_5 () = Note ("X-FORMAT-CLIENT", 5u32, "2026-10-25T03:53Z", "x", "10-24 23:53 x: measured input for this item. The installed formatter (10:08 build) on x's edition-3 text changes 40 of 44 files and makes 28 cells overweight, mostly records exploded to one field per line and long signatures re-laid with deep indentation. So the format pass here must come with per-cell splits until x-compat and postable are both 0, plus a check that no authored line is joined (0 files lost lines in the measured run).")
inl note_of_x_format_client_6 () = Note ("X-FORMAT-CLIENT", 6u32, "2026-10-25T23:41Z", "x", "2026-10-25 19:41 -04:00 (written earlier under the eoie lease) x: MEASURED on a copy (scratch x-simland-0214\\format_measure.ps1; installed 5l'' formatter over apps/client and the new apps/sim/spi). Before: client 35 files / 4,619 lines, 133 lines over 120, 0 cells over one post, x-compat 0; sim/spi 23 files / 4,550 lines, 65 over 120, 0 spilled, x-compat 0. The formatter changes 51 of 58 files. After: client 4,991 lines, 1 over 120, 21 cells over one post (x-compat 21 overweight); sim/spi 4,752 lines, 0 over 120, 8 spilled (x-compat 8). So formatting fixes 197 long lines and creates 29 overweight definitions in 17 files: wire 5, glyphs 3, fields 2, posts 2, assemble 2, personas 2, scenario 2, world 2, and 1 each in api, digest, fetch, locate, refs, token, xc, runner_shell, schedule. Plan, by the formatter-keeps-breaks rule (re-lay only >120): (1) land the formatter result file by file; (2) split each of the 29 by hand into named helpers, as the batch dry-run patch did, gated on x-compat 0 + dry-run 0 spilled + --check per package; (3) glyphs.spi waits for main's job-1792944559767 (it lands the glyphs heal), and the sim/spi files go last, after the parity landing. Size: 29 splits at ~5-10 min each, 3-5 h, light load. || 2026-10-25 17:19 -04:00 x: classified the 29 (scratch classify_overweight.ps1): 5 are RECORD LAYOUT (fields command_fields + filter_fields, assemble.frame_of, posts.at, personas.acted_in), where the formatter puts one field per line (EDITION3-280-SPLIT n2). Those need a formatter record-fill layout (fields filled within 120 columns), which is compiler work x now owns, not hand splits. 24 are code: 16 split by hand on formatted copies (%TEMP%\\fmt-bisect client, %TEMP%\\fmt-measure sim), each with a named helper: wire x5 (post_extras, included_posts, edit_history_in, root_fields, ghost_error), api classified_export, assemble frame_members, digest primary_entry, fetch fetch_filters, locate frame_members, posts loud_posts, refs refs_of + refs_args (the generated Rust is byte-identical, pub fn xc_refs unchanged), token with_tokens, xc credentials_hint, and in sim personas fresh_cast, runner_shell withdrawn_demand, scenario rooted_post + merged_posts, schedule corpus_pacing, world metrics_pair + child_cell. Every split file: x-compat clean except its record-layout def, 0 lines over 120, --check exit 0 (one wrong guess, ghost_error typed json instead of string, was caught by --check and bisected). The 3 glyphs defs wait for job-1792944559767, and gleam_weight goes away with that heal. Tooling gap seen: '--check' rejection prints only 'Package typecheck rejected' with no error, so finding the failing definition took a per-edit bisect; worth an eoie or compiler item (print the first type errors). Landing: after node verify5b, copy the 46 formatted files that carry no record-layout def into the tree and verify again; fields, assemble, posts, personas and glyphs wait for the record-fill layout. || 2026-10-25 17:45 -04:00 x: RECORD FILL drafted and measured (scratchpad\\record-fill\\apply.ps1, compiler formatter). layoutFields packs consecutive simple fields onto one line, separated by '; ', within the width; a field the author started on a new line still starts one (authoredBreaks), so no authored line is joined; fields whose body needs its own layout keep their lines; the formatter's parse guard still falls back to as-written if a layout changes meaning. Proof 2 on the node (job-1792964325355, candidate = tree core + patch, installed vs candidate on the same x copy): overweight client 28 -> 22, sim 8 -> 5, only 12 files differ. Fixed: assemble.frame_of, fields.filter_fields, posts.at, personas.acted_in. NOT fixed: fields.command_fields, a record TYPE: type definitions go through a separate path (3 sites near spiral_compiler.fs:181804, 181811, 181891) that maps each field to its own line. The patch now routes those 3 through layoutFields as well; proof 3 = job-1792964722843. With all 5 records fixed, the 16 hand splits plus the record fill leave only glyphs (main's landing) before x formats cleanly. || 2026-10-25 17:57 -04:00 x: DECIDED by main on FORMATTER-KEEP-BREAKS (rules on record, user 10-20 n1): record fill as a default is rejected (the user corpus template is one field per line at +4, no ';', 224/0), and record TYPES are one field per line by design. Record VALUES keep their authored breaks. So x fixes it at the source: the 4 over-120 record values (fields.filter_fields, assemble.frame_of, posts.at, personas.acted_in) are re-authored as 2 lines within 120 (scratch rewrap.ps1, applied to the tree; x-compat 0 in client and sim/spi). The installed formatter keeps those lines; it only normalizes brace spacing and the +4 continuation, and none is overweight after formatting. Left: fields.command_fields, a 15-field record TYPE, 376 > 273 after formatting by design. It needs a structural change: split it into two record types or nest a sub-record (it carries the CLI parse fields; check its consumers: fields.spi helpers, args, the xc_args export). That's its own small design step before fields.spi can be formatted. The record-fill patch stays as evidence only (4 of 5 fit). || 2026-10-25 18:07 -04:00 x: command_fields SPLIT, applied to the tree (scratch cfsplit.ps1): command_fields = {kind; payload; selection : selection_fields; window : window_fields}, with selection_fields (following, usernames, max_results, limit, per_author, cap) and window_fields (since, until, no_replies, no_reposts, depth, hints, json), written in the canonical one-field-per-line form; blank, source_fields, counts_fields, filter_fields and output_fields go through per-sub-record helpers (record updates on a bound variable); 2 args tests now read f.selection.usernames, f.window.since and f.window.no_replies/no_reposts. Because Spiral exports flatten a record into its leaves in declaration order, the boundary is unchanged: pub fn xc_parse_os_args and xc_timeline_fields keep the same flat 15-tuple (byte-identical signatures), and the xc crate's generated Rust is byte-identical. Checked: --check exit 0; fields.spi 0 lines over 120; formatted with the installed formatter, 0 overweight and 0 long lines; x-compat client 0. So all 5 record definitions are now within 280 after formatting. Landing the format: re-run the formatter on the tree files, then apply the 16 code splits as anchored edits against the formatted text (the scratch copies are older than the tree for fields, posts, assemble and personas). || 2026-10-25 19:37 -04:00 x: LANDED in C:\\home\\git\\x (unstaged): the batch dry-run patch (POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS M1) and the formatting of 57 files (installed 5l'' formatter, glyphs.spi skipped) plus 23 anchored hand splits (scratch format_land.ps1, rehearsed stacked twice on copies). After landing: x-compat 0 in apps/client (35 files) and apps/sim/spi (23); --check exit 0 for xc and scenario_checks; the only lines over 120 are glyphs.spi's 9. verify7b on the node (pre-format tree) showed e2e 36/36 + 5 extra after accepting the one stale tutorial output (xc thread 3000001, the respelled prelude), postable 1301 posts / 35 files / 0 spilled, and the client notebooks green up to protocol.spi's known zig failure (LIB-ZIG-INDEX-OF). The README accept is copied into the tree (3 lines, the respelled prelude in the thread output). The formatting changes prelude/posts text again, which the sim worlds embed, so verify8 (job-1792971438120) runs the sim build -Accept, then e2e -Accept, then both full builds; the accepted fixture and README diffs come back for review before they enter the tree. Node gaps reported to kit2 and fixed on the way: rename-folded moves, empty dirs, mirror deps, the stale compiler; the node's spiral rust runner is missing, so Cells rust-with-deps cells are 'unsupported' there (oauth1 thread_run).")
inl note_of_x_format_client_7 () = Note ("X-FORMAT-CLIENT", 7u32, "2026-10-26T04:23Z", "x", "2026-10-26 00:23 -04:00 x: verify8 on the node (job-1792971438120, after the format + batch landing): sim -Accept regenerated 11 fixtures; e2e -Accept updated 3 of 36 tutorial outputs (the new 'post-thread --dry-run FILE...' usage line in xc --help and in the --per-account usage refusal, plus the thread output of the formatted prelude); then e2e 36/36 + 5 extra; postable 1325 posts / 35 files / 0 spilled; x-compat 0. Reviewed and copied into the tree: the README (only those 3 tutorial blocks beyond my earlier move edits) and the fixtures, which are the formatted sources as the sim renders them (the sim-small program matches the formatted prelude line for line after the notebook header rewrite). Two failures, both fixed or explained. (1) mix test: ':xsim_frames.split_cell/3 is undefined'. The formatter wrapped !!!!Export( so the name sits on the next line, and scripts/gleam-modules.ps1 found exports with a per-line regex, so it dropped split_cell from xsim_frames' kept surface (14 of 15). FIXED: it matches '!!!!Export\\(\\s*\"(\\w+)\"' over the whole file (15 of 15); nothing else in x or lib parses exports per line. (2) Cells glyphs rust -d cells: the node's spiral runner rejected --rs-path (stale 2025-03-13 build). It is being rebuilt as node upkeep (job-1792988618221), with verify9 chained after it (job-1792988648856: full client + sim builds in a fresh mirror). Expected result: green up to protocol.spi's known zig failure (LIB-ZIG-INDEX-OF).")
