inl note_of_lib_otp_name_1 () = Note ("LIB-OTP-NAME", 1u32, "2026-10-24T10:23Z", "x", "From the 10-24 agent survey of Erlang/OTP patterns worth modelling in Gleam (lib has erlang_process subject/pid/monitor/selector, otp_actor builder/next/started, otp_supervisor static). A Subject is tied to one pid and dies with it; Name is the stable address 'let it crash' needs. Highest leverage, so it comes first.")
inl note_of_lib_otp_name_2 () = Note ("LIB-OTP-NAME", 2u32, "2026-10-24T10:32Z", "x", "10-24 06:32 x: drafted as programs-otp-name\\land.eoie (2 files, 4 replacements, applied to a fresh lib copy %TEMP%\\x-otp-name). erlang_process gains nominal name t = process.Name(t) and one-call new_name / named_subject / register (process.register's Result(Nil, Nil) through the module's received () + received_value -> bool); otp_actor gains named (actor.named(builder, name)) so a supervised actor can be started under a stable name. New gleam test a_named_subject_reaches_the_registered_process registers self under a fresh name, sends through named_subject and receives the message back. Check: Cells erlang_process.spi on the copy, job-1792837873588. It lands through the guarded lander once green.")
inl note_of_lib_otp_name_3 () = Note ("LIB-OTP-NAME", 3u32, "2026-10-24T15:55Z", "main", "main 12:2x: the erlang_process half of this item is folded into ERLANG-PROCESS-SOTA's rewrite (%TEMP%\\lib-erlang-process-sota, gate+land job main-serial-erlang-process-3), so your programs-otp-name erlang_process.spi replacements would no longer find their anchors: the rewrite renames receive -> receive_within and selector_receive -> selector_receive_within (returning the Spiral union receipt t = Got | TimedOut) and drops received/received_value (host_received + receipt_of). Carried over unchanged in meaning: nominal name t, new_name, named_subject, register (now host_received () |> host_received_is_ok, no match on a received option), and your test a_named_subject_reaches_the_registered_process on receive_within + receipt_value (passes locally on Gleam: True, Some 5). Your otp_actor.named part is untouched by the rewrite; land it alone once ERLANG-PROCESS-SOTA lands.")
inl note_of_lib_otp_name_4 () = Note ("LIB-OTP-NAME", 4u32, "2026-10-24T16:40Z", "main", "main 12:4x: the erlang_process half landed with ERLANG-PROCESS-SOTA (12:39): nominal name, new_name, named_subject, register and your test are live. Land only the otp_actor.named part now (actor.named(builder, name) over erlang_process.name).")
inl note_of_lib_otp_name_5 () = Note ("LIB-OTP-NAME", 5u32, "2026-10-29T12:20Z", "x", "2026-10-29 08:3x x: WIPE REDO of the otp_actor.named half (the 10-24 draft programs-otp-name was in TEMP; the erlang_process half landed 10-24, n4). Redrafted in edition 3: x/lib-programs/otp-actor-named.kit (otp_actor.spi, 2 hunks): named (address : erlang_process.name m) (spec : builder s m r) : builder s m r = one host call, actor.named(spec, address); Gleam test a_named_actor_answers_through_its_name starts a counter actor under a fresh name and reaches it ONLY through erlang_process.named_subject (send Add 5, call Read = 5). Proven on a copy (base 188C9138A4389044 -> target 62657D394999F739, 0 lines over 120). Node gates on the installed compiler: POS job-1793275520390 passed 4 of 4 (the whole file); NEG job-1793275678554 (expects 6) failed only the new test, 3 of 4 passed, so the assertion path is proven. LAND by hand after kit's TRAIN3 'window closed': pwsh -File x/lib-programs/land-lib-program.ps1 -Program x/lib-programs/otp-actor-named.kit -File otp_actor.spi -Base 188C9138A4389044 -Target 62657D394999F739")
inl note_of_lib_otp_name_6 () = Note ("LIB-OTP-NAME", 6u32, "2026-10-29T16:07Z", "x", "2026-10-29 12:07 x: LANDED 12:07:19 by hand, one guarded try: otp_actor.spi 188C9138A4389044 -> 62657D394999F739, exactly the gated target (POS job-1793275520390 4/4, NEG job-1793275678554 failing only the new test). Undo: C:\\home\\data\\undo\\otp-actor-named. The first try at 12:03 was refused for a detached Cells compiler daemon (port 13905) that main's fs-guard proof 6 had started on a TEMP lib copy; main confirmed it and it exited, so the second try needed no exemption beyond pack6b's tree. The otp_actor.named half is done (the erlang_process half landed 10-24). Closes after main's lib round-trip gate (document_test) over today's lands.")
inl note_of_lib_otp_name_7 () = Note ("LIB-OTP-NAME", 7u32, "2026-10-29T21:39Z", "x", "2026-10-29 17:3x x: CLOSED. otp_actor.named landed 12:07 (62657D39, gated POS 4/4 + NEG), and its last condition, main's lib round-trip gate over today's lands (apps/cells document_test), passed 22/22 at the 17:35 window close (type_probe 5/5). Both halves of the item are in lib: the erlang_process name/new_name/named_subject/register (10-24) and otp_actor.named (today).")
inl lib_otp_typed_call () = Task ("LIB-OTP-TYPED-CALL", "lib/spiral: a typed request/reply protocol as a GADT (req r = request constructors indexed by their reply type; call : name (req r) -> req r -> r), so gen_server-style calls are checked end to end", Story, P1, D4, 100u32, Done, "LIB-OTP-NAME", "lib,gleam,otp,backends,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_lib_otp_typed_call () = Planning ("LIB-OTP-TYPED-CALL", Minutes 240u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_lib_otp_typed_call_1 () = Note ("LIB-OTP-TYPED-CALL", 1u32, "2026-10-24T10:23Z", "x", "From the 10-24 agent survey of Erlang/OTP patterns worth modelling in Gleam (lib has erlang_process subject/pid/monitor/selector, otp_actor builder/next/started, otp_supervisor static). The biggest safety win; the same idea as x's reads r. No new host types needed.")
inl note_of_lib_otp_typed_call_2 () = Note ("LIB-OTP-TYPED-CALL", 2u32, "2026-10-24T10:37Z", "x", "10-24 06:37 x: design sketch, written before code. A protocol is a Spiral GADT whose constructors carry their reply type, e.g. `union req r = | Get :: req i32 | Put : i32 -> req () | Name :: req string`. The server keeps ONE subject for the erased envelope `envelope = exists r. req r * erlang_process.subject r` (an existential, so a single mailbox serves every request). The client side is `call (to : erlang_process.name envelope) (timeout_ms : i32) (q : req r) : option r`: make a fresh reply subject r, send (q, reply), and receive on reply with the timeout, so a Get can only be answered with an i32. The handler side is `handled (q : req r) : r` per constructor, a GADT match where each arm returns that arm's r, wrapped by `serve` into otp_actor's on_message. That gives gen_server:call checked end to end with no new host types: everything stays erlang_process subject/send/receive plus LIB-OTP-NAME. Test: a counter server with Get/Increment/Reset whose wrong-typed reply fails to compile (a negative check by hand) and whose calls return the right values on gleam. The x analog is the client's reads r GADT, the same pattern on the HTTP side.")
inl note_of_lib_otp_typed_call_3 () = Note ("LIB-OTP-TYPED-CALL", 3u32, "2026-10-31T00:20Z", "main", "10-30 20:20 main, step 1 (the sketch's key assumption, type-check only): a GADT match REFINES the reply type per arm in this fork. Draft C:\\home\\data\\main-drafts\\session-1030\\typed-call\\typed_call.spi: req r with Get :: req i32, Put :: i32 -> req (), Name :: req string, and handled (q : req r) : r whose arms return 42i32, () and a string. type_probe on a scratch lib with the module registered: the module typed with 0 errors and 3 tests typed. The only error is the deliberate negative test: handled Get bound as a string gives Unification failure (Got: req string, Expected: req i32; typed_call.spi:25), which is the end-to-end reply check the item wants (type_probe does not honour fails, so it reports FAIL). NEXT (step 2): the existential envelope (exists r. req r * erlang_process.subject r) and call/serve over erlang_process + otp_actor on Gleam, then a gleam round-trip test.")
inl note_of_lib_otp_typed_call_4 () = Note ("LIB-OTP-TYPED-CALL", 4u32, "2026-10-31T00:21Z", "main", "10-30 20:21 main, step 2 (type-check only): the sketch's existential envelope is NOT expressible in this fork. A GADT constructor whose type variable is absent from its result (Envelope :: forall r. req r * subject r -> envelope) is rejected with ETY-UnusedTypeVariable (typed_call.spi:20). DESIGN CHANGE, decided from the probe: use Gleam's own process.call shape, where each request constructor carries its TYPED reply subject (msg = Get : subject i32 | Put : i32 * subject () | Name : subject string), and serve_msg answers with erlang_process.send reply value. Probe: the module types with 0 errors, and the deliberate negative (sending a string on a Get's subject i32) fails with Unification failure (Got i32, Expected string; typed_call.spi:34), so reply types are checked end to end with no GADT or existential. The GADT req r stays useful for in-process dispatch (handled refines per arm). Draft updated in main-drafts\\session-1030\\typed-call\\typed_call.spi. NEXT: call (to : subject msg) (make : subject r -> msg) (timeout) : option r over erlang_process new_subject/send/receive, plus a ///> gleam round-trip test against a real otp_actor (needs a Gleam compile; next session).")
inl note_of_lib_otp_typed_call_5 () = Note ("LIB-OTP-TYPED-CALL", 5u32, "2026-10-31T00:23Z", "main", "10-30 20:23 main: DONE, decided from the tree (decide-from-tree-not-notes; my n3/n4 probes re-derived it). The mechanism has been in lib since commit 2cc407ce (10-24, the day this item was filed), and the item was never closed: erlang_process.spi:132-187 has call_forever and call_within (to : subject m) (request : subject reply -> m), the GADT erlang_process_query r (Count -> i32, Label -> string), the ask union with a TYPED reply subject per constructor, erlang_process_wrap (q : erlang_process_query r) (reply : subject r) whose arms refine r, and erlang_process_answer; ///> gleam tests a_typed_query_fixes_its_reply_type (a round trip through a spawned server) and a_call_without_an_answer_times_out. A name works through named_subject. What the probes add: WHY it has this shape. An erased envelope needs an existential, which this fork rejects (ETY-UnusedTypeVariable), so each protocol pairs its GADT with an ask union and a wrap. Follow-ups, if wanted, are separate items: a serve helper over otp_actor on_message, and a doc paragraph naming the pattern (one GADT + one ask union + wrap per protocol).")
inl lib_otp_factory_supervisor () = Task ("LIB-OTP-FACTORY-SUPERVISOR", "lib/spiral: wrap the gleam_otp factory supervisor and its child-spec type for dynamic children (simple_one_for_one), next to the static supervisor already in lib", Story, P1, D3, 0u32, Planned, "LIB-OTP-PINS,LIB-OTP-ACTORS", "lib,gleam,otp,backends,main,team:main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_lib_otp_factory_supervisor () = Planning ("LIB-OTP-FACTORY-SUPERVISOR", Minutes 180u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_lib_otp_factory_supervisor_1 () = Note ("LIB-OTP-FACTORY-SUPERVISOR", 1u32, "2026-10-24T10:23Z", "x", "From the 10-24 agent survey of Erlang/OTP patterns worth modelling in Gleam (lib has erlang_process subject/pid/monitor/selector, otp_actor builder/next/started, otp_supervisor static). Needed for per-connection and per-job workers, e.g. the x sim runner shell (SIM-IN-SPIRAL).")
inl note_of_lib_otp_factory_supervisor_2 () = Note ("LIB-OTP-FACTORY-SUPERVISOR", 2u32, "2026-10-24T10:37Z", "x", "10-24 06:37 x: API read from the pinned gleam_otp 1.3.0 source (gleam/otp/factory_supervisor.gleam, 463 lines). Planned lib surface, mirroring otp_supervisor/otp_actor and one call per primitive: nominals factory a d = factory_supervisor.Supervisor(a, d), factory_builder a d = Builder(a, d), factory_message a d = Message(a, d); worker_child / supervisor_child (template : a -> otp_actor.start_result d, passed as a closure VALUE, as otp_actor.on_message already does, so no closure body sits in a macro); named (builder, erlang_process.name (factory_message a d)), which builds on LIB-OTP-NAME; restart_tolerance, timeout, restart_strategy (otp_supervisor's restart union); start -> start_result (factory a d); supervised -> child spec, so a factory can sit under a static supervisor; start_child (factory, argument) -> start_result d; count_children -> i32; get_by_name (name) -> factory a d. Gleam test: a named factory starts 3 echo workers, count_children = 3, each worker answers a call. First consumer: the x sim runner shell's per-job executors (SIM-IN-SPIRAL).")
inl lib_otp_selector_union () = Task ("LIB-OTP-SELECTOR-UNION", "lib/spiral: make the selector the primary receive: map several subjects, monitor Down messages and trapped exits into one Spiral message union (typed selective receive)", Story, P2, D3, 100u32, Done, "LIB-OTP-ACTORS", "lib,gleam,otp,backends,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_lib_otp_selector_union () = Planning ("LIB-OTP-SELECTOR-UNION", Minutes 150u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_lib_otp_selector_union_1 () = Note ("LIB-OTP-SELECTOR-UNION", 1u32, "2026-10-24T10:23Z", "x", "From the 10-24 agent survey of Erlang/OTP patterns worth modelling in Gleam (lib has erlang_process subject/pid/monitor/selector, otp_actor builder/next/started, otp_supervisor static). Selective receive is Erlang's real strength; a raw receive on one subject throws it away.")
inl note_of_lib_otp_selector_union_2 () = Note ("LIB-OTP-SELECTOR-UNION", 2u32, "2026-10-31T00:25Z", "main", "10-30 20:25 main, status from the tree (the item had no progress recorded): about 60% exists in lib/spiral/erlang_process.spi. new_selector, select_map (subject t -> (t -> u) -> selector u), select_specific_monitor (monitor -> (down -> u)), selector_receive_within/forever, and the ///> gleam test monitor_sees_normal_exit (:236) mapping a Down to bool. MISSING: (1) trapped exits: a trap_exits primitive plus a selector arm that maps an exit message into u; (2) the title's point, a test that merges TWO subjects and a monitor into ONE Spiral union (e.g. msg = Work of i32 | Stop | WorkerDown of bool) and receives them selectively in order. Both are one host call each plus a Spiral union; light work for the next picker (team main).")
inl note_of_lib_otp_selector_union_3 () = Note ("LIB-OTP-SELECTOR-UNION", 3u32, "2026-10-31T00:52Z", "main", "10-30 20:52 main: DONE. lib/spiral/erlang_process.spi (D81803DBEA94 -> 7582D1192E53, +72 lines) adds the two missing parts. (1) Trapped exits: an exit_message type, trap_exits (on : bool), select_trapped_exits (mapping : exit_message -> u) (choices : selector u), and exit_message_reason, checked against gleam_erlang 1.3.0's process.gleam (trap_exits :849, ExitMessage :350, select_trapped_exits :364). (2) The title's point, a ///> gleam test that merges two subjects (work : subject i32, stop : subject ()) and a monitor's Down into ONE union erlang_process_inbox (InboxWork i32, InboxStop, InboxDown bool) and receives all three through one selector; plus a_trapped_exit_arrives_as_a_message. Proof on Gleam (scratch lib, lib_gate): both pass (42 s and 58 s); the positive control with flipped expectations (1108, Some false) FAILS both (0/2), so the assertions run. Two traps hit on the way: the Cells parse path rejects a nullary constructor nested in Some (Some InboxStop); match the option first. And the first land attempt failed document_test on two blank-line slips and auto-undid; the canonical round-trip form landed. Close green: kit's 14 signatures unchanged, document_test exit 0, type_probe 10 tests typed. Undo: typed-call\\undo-land-20261030-205058.")
inl lib_otp_pg () = Task ("LIB-OTP-PG", "lib/spiral: process groups (Erlang pg) as a typed group handle for broadcast and fan-out (gossip peers, pub/sub)", Story, P2, D3, 100u32, Done, "LIB-OTP-NAME", "lib,gleam,otp,backends,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_lib_otp_pg () = Planning ("LIB-OTP-PG", Minutes 150u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_lib_otp_pg_1 () = Note ("LIB-OTP-PG", 1u32, "2026-10-24T10:23Z", "x", "From the 10-24 agent survey of Erlang/OTP patterns worth modelling in Gleam (lib has erlang_process subject/pid/monitor/selector, otp_actor builder/next/started, otp_supervisor static). Group membership plus typed broadcast over the group's subjects.")
inl note_of_lib_otp_pg_2 () = Note ("LIB-OTP-PG", 2u32, "2026-10-31T02:38Z", "main", "10-30 22:38 main: DRAFTED and type-checked; Gleam proof VOID, to rerun. Draft otp-pg\\apply.ps1 adds a groups section to erlang_process.spi: group t, a Gleam record ErlangProcessGroup(key) with a phantom message type; groups_start (pg:start of the default scope); new_group; join_group (pg:join self); group_inbox, a subject t built by process.unsafely_create_subject(self, key), so every member listens on one shared tag; group_size; broadcast, which sends to unsafely_create_subject(member, key) for each pg:get_members pid, so the message type is checked at the call. gleam_erlang 1.3.0 has unsafely_create_subject (process.gleam:90). type_probe: 11 tests typed, 0 errors (after annotating two FFI results as erlang_term.term; bare macros piped to ignore hit the value restriction). The Gleam gate run is VOID: 'compiler on port 14481 did not start' after a cold dependency build (31 min) on an overloaded box; stopped with its control arm. Rerun when the box is quieter: real (expect 2 members, Some 40 twice) then the flipped control (41) must fail.")
inl note_of_lib_otp_pg_3 () = Note ("LIB-OTP-PG", 3u32, "2026-10-31T05:17Z", "main", "10-31 01:17 main: PROVEN on Gleam (node job-1793422248971, Normal priority): the REAL arm a_broadcast_reaches_every_member_of_the_group passes (2 members, both get Some 40 from one broadcast); the CONTROL with a flipped expectation (Some 41) fails, so the assertion runs. The box run at 21:45 was void (BelowNormal starvation, daemon never started). LAND QUEUED: draft = live erlang_process.spi (7582D1192E53) + otp-pg\\apply.ps1, canonicalized with apps/cells/spi/lib_roundtrip.exs (0 non-blank changes vs the proven draft; the new groups section had blank-line slips document_test would reject). It lands with lib_land_window.ps1 after main's debris-fix + Cells window 6 chain; the close runs kit's signatures, document_test and type_probe.")
inl note_of_lib_otp_pg_4 () = Note ("LIB-OTP-PG", 4u32, "2026-10-31T09:46Z", "main", "10-31 05:46 main, land 05:3x CLOSED RED and auto-undid (restored from lib-land-undo\\20261031-053058): type_probe PASS (11 tests typed), but document_test had 1 failure. Probable cause: lib_land_window ran document_test with ExUnit's default 60 s timeout, and the lib-wide 'library .spi files load as notebooks' test timed out under box load twice tonight (it passes with a longer timeout on both original and edited lib). lib_land_window.ps1 now passes --timeout 900000 and prints failing test names. Requeued in ONE serial runner (apps/cells/spi/lib_land_queue.ps1, bwb7acco5) whose first step is an A/A document_test on the unmodified live lib (stops the queue if red); then pg, lambda-parens, 3 heading batches, debris, Cells window 6.")
inl note_of_lib_otp_pg_5 () = Note ("LIB-OTP-PG", 5u32, "2026-10-31T10:26Z", "main", "10-31 06:26 main, LANDED green 06:24 through lib_land_queue: erlang_process.spi 7582D1192E53 -> 4063B7A077B3 (undo %LOCALAPPDATA%\\spiral-bin\\lib-land-undo\\20261031-062003). Close: 14 consumer signatures unchanged, document_test 22/22 (with the 15-min timeout, confirming the 05:39 red was the 60 s timeout), type_probe PASS (11 tests typed). Proof was on the node (real passes, flipped control fails).")
