inl note_of_typefeatureset_enforce_1 () = Note ("TYPEFEATURESET-ENFORCE", 1u32, "2026-10-24T13:17Z", "main", "2026-10-24 09:17 main: first census and metric. Typefeatureset constructs (indexed union cases `| C : ... -> ...`, prototype/instance, exists, plus plain `union` for the denominator side). lib/spiral: 29,377 lines, 48 unions, 71 indexed cases (17 files), 56 prototype/instance (10 files), 25 exists (10 files): about 200 constructs, 6.8 per 1k lines. The omniledger mega sample: 276 in 12,030 lines, 22.9 per 1k lines, 3.4x denser. That confirms the user's point that hand-written and agent-era lib barely uses the typefeatureset. Metric for the review rule and for later tooling (MEGA-SAMPLES-AS-MODELS, NESTED-IF-TOOL): typefeature density per 1k lines per file, with the mega samples as the reference band (about 20+); a NEW lib module below 10/1k needs a one-line reason in review (e.g. host primitives only). Review rule from now on: data whose invariants depend on an index or state is an indexed union (GADT), not a union plus runtime checks; open dispatch over types is a prototype with instances, not a match on a tag; values hidden behind an interface are existentials. Lib rewrite plan: by usage weight, starting with the modules every product calls (am', listm', sm', resultm, optionm'), done as GRANULAR-HEAL slices so each file is rewritten once for both reasons.")
inl note_of_typefeatureset_enforce_2 () = Note ("TYPEFEATURESET-ENFORCE", 2u32, "2026-10-25T19:49Z", "fiscal", "15:4x fiscal: the fiscal path measured with a calibrated census (omniledger 22.6/1k reproduces main's 22.9 within 1.5%): fiscal/src and lib xsd, xsd_resolve, xsd_emit, xsd_regex, xsd_constraints, xml all at 0-0.5/1k, generated modules 0/1k. Findings and the ordered remodel plan with gates are in FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT (R1 the flat xsd union as an indexed union by sort; R2 tuple payloads with -1/'' sentinels; R3 the string-encoded enumeration_key; R4 failwith readers; R5 stringly names; G1 string aliases instead of validator-only nominals; G2 the unused versioned-family GADT).")
inl note_of_typefeatureset_enforce_3 () = Note ("TYPEFEATURESET-ENFORCE", 3u32, "2026-10-30T01:59Z", "main", "10-29 21:59 main, groom (WIP rule, as in the 10-23 and 10-27 grooms): Active -> Paused, progress kept at 15%. No note since 10-25 and no live job; main carries 69 Actives, which is not real WIP. Resume when picked; if its pass already shipped in an installed core, close it Done with that evidence instead. Compiler/lib work: per lane-roles, the team that needs it (likely the compiler team, git-a6) resumes it.")
inl note_of_typefeatureset_enforce_4 () = Note ("TYPEFEATURESET-ENFORCE", 4u32, "2026-10-30T02:05Z", "main", "10-29 22:05 main, groom follow-up: paused tonight by the WIP rule, but an ACTIVE item depends on it: FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT (fiscal, Active, worked tonight). Resume it FIRST among the paused ones, since it gates live work.")
inl note_of_typefeatureset_enforce_5 () = Note ("TYPEFEATURESET-ENFORCE", 5u32, "2026-10-31T20:59Z", "main", "10-31 16:53 main, pause re-decision (KIT-PAUSE-RULE-REWORK; rule R3 is now 'never auto-pause for silence'): Paused -> PLANNED. It was paused by the old silence/WIP rule, not by a blocker: its notes name no external condition (a 10-30 lab-archive note blocks nothing: restore the draft from the archive into the repo when picked). Back in agile next at its priority; deps, if any, gate it as usual.")
inl note_of_typefeatureset_enforce_6 () = Note ("TYPEFEATURESET-ENFORCE", 6u32, "2026-10-31T22:29Z", "x", "2026-10-31 18:3x x: user 10-31 ~18:05, verbatim: \"also all new code should use the typefeatureset gadts/existentials/hkts/prototypes/reflection as much as possible like they do with phantomdata in rust, make sure old code is enforced with that also, even if we advance in a slower manner in the process ... also having the perfect gleam rust balance also, we had experiments with iggy, gotta reread the history\". x adopts this for every x and Cells slice: new code models with GADTs, existentials, foralls and phantom indices first, and old code is migrated in the same change that touches it, even when that slows the slice. First slice: x/apps/sim/spi/sim_serve.spi. auth_kind returned the strings app/user/client/oauth1/\"\" and write_reply checked them. It now builds a caller GADT indexed by the CLIENT's own phantom contexts (wire::app_context, wire::user_context, plus client_context and no_context) behind an existential any_caller, and post_or_delete demands a caller wire::user_context witness, so an app bearer can't reach a write by construction. The string survives only at the log boundary (caller_word). kit census on x/apps/sim/spi: gadt-case 0 -> 5, existential 0 -> 2, forall 8 -> 11, 10 fewer lines. Proof: node job x-parity-typed1 (e2e-parity: stub vs sim request logs, rule 0 differing rows of 153). Enforcement gap found on the way: the census probes miss the granular sugar (noted on EOIE-STYLE-RATCHET). CORRECTION 18:33: proof x-parity-typed1 failed at compile: 'Cannot dyn an existential into a runtime var' (caller_of returned a different any_caller pack per runtime branch). Spiral existentials are compile-time packs, so they cannot carry an index chosen from runtime data. Reworked: no existential; replied tests the credentials and each branch calls the forall caller_reply with a concrete constructor (caller_reply w log r AppBearer, ...). That is fiscal's model_census pattern: concrete instantiations of a \\* function that matches mixed-index arms. Census is now gadt-case 5, existential 0, forall 12. Rule for reviewers: an existential is right for compile-time heterogeneity (kit's any_probe over a static probe list). For a runtime decode boundary, the branch-per-constructor dispatch into a \\* function is the staged equivalent. Re-proof: x-parity-typed2.")
inl note_of_typefeatureset_enforce_7 () = Note ("TYPEFEATURESET-ENFORCE", 7u32, "2026-10-31T23:21Z", "x", "2026-10-31 19:19 x: the typed-caller slice of sim_serve.spi (n6) is PROVEN. x-parity-typed2 passed with request logs byte-equal on method, path, query, authorized, context and body over 153 requests, so the GADT dispatch changed no wire behaviour. The next x enforcement target is the Cells Spiral domain (CELLS-IN-SPIRAL n2), starting with CELLS-SPI-LIB.")
