inl note_of_codegen_readable_2_1 () = Note ("CODEGEN-READABLE-2", 1u32, "2026-10-30T02:00Z", "main", "10-29 22:00 main, groom: READY TO START. Every dep is Done (READABLE-GENERATED-NAMES), so the item is unblocked though still Planned (P1, est 1440 m). Pick-up: the compiler team (git-a6), per lane-roles, after the pack-7 install (next session).")
inl note_of_codegen_readable_2_2 () = Note ("CODEGEN-READABLE-2", 2u32, "2026-10-30T14:12Z", "main", "10-30 10:12 main, BACKLOG-DANGLING-1030 R2/R4: deps Done, owner team compiler ('agile next --team compiler' hands it out), assignee agent (nothing in it is the user's). Resume when that team picks it up; the pack-7 install it waited on is done.")
inl note_of_codegen_readable_2_3 () = Note ("CODEGEN-READABLE-2", 3u32, "2026-10-30T17:00Z", "compiler", "10-30 13:00 compiler: P0 CENSUS (read-only, committed sample outputs under apps/compiler/tmp/samples, occurrences/distinct names). Locals v<n>: C 62,340/3,435 (520 files), Rust 39,813/3,458 (482), F# 75,491/6,738 (576), Zig 108,204/3,613 (680). Union types US<n>/UH<n>: C 4,728/15, Rust 5,509/15, F# 11,833/32, Zig 20,013/32. Heap<n>: 2,967/5 C, 1,743/5 Rust, 1,555/5 F#, 2,063/5 Zig. Mut<n>: 417/3, 200/3, 174/3, 232/6. method<n>: 511/15 C, 388/12 Rust, 230/12 F#, 384/12 Zig. closure<n>: 1,888/80 C, 198/80 Rust, 32/8 F#, 214/80 Zig. <stem>_c<n> (named closures since READABLE-GENERATED-NAMES): about 180/12 per backend. Reading: (1) the v<n> locals are almost all of the noise, so the 'preserve names from inner code' ask is the big lever: a local bound by a named Spiral let should emit '<name>_<n>' (the closure precedent) and keep v<n> only for compiler temporaries; (2) union types are about 15-32 distinct names with thousands of uses each, so naming them from the source union/nominal ('option_u<n>') is cheap and highly visible; (3) Heap/Mut are 5 and 3-6 distinct names, so dropping the heap/stack distinction from names is small and can ride with (2); (4) method<n> are few, and readable method names are already stem-based where a source name exists. Proposed order: (2)+(3) as one applier with a residual-only review (names change, behaviour can't: the native column must be byte-identical in exit/stdout), then (1) behind the same gate. The binding name is already carried to codegen for closures (closureSourceName), and locals need the same at the let-emission sites. Not started beyond this census; first pick-up = locate the union/heap/mut naming helpers in codegenC/Rust/Fsharp/Zig.")
inl note_of_codegen_readable_2_4 () = Note ("CODEGEN-READABLE-2", 4u32, "2026-10-30T17:01Z", "compiler", "10-30 13:01 compiler, pick-up pointers for (2): union names are minted from the layout tag at many per-backend sites (spiral_compiler.fs on the post-pack-8 tree: Rust 165046-165047 and 165733/166451, C 176502-176564, Lua 169164-170118 'Uh/Us', Python 178365-178385), plus Heap{tag}/Mut{tag} (Rust defaults 164951-165012). The source-name data already exists: unionSourceNames (Rust section, used at about 165180 by import_rust_unions), and exported_union_names feeds rename_exported_unions (165167), a TEXT post-pass that regex-renames US<n>/UH<n> to the export name. So (2) = one shared helper 'unionTypeName u' returning '<source>_<tag>' when unionSourceNames has the node (else today's US/UH<tag>), called at every mint site. That replaces the regex post-pass instead of adding a second one. Line numbers move with every pack; re-grep 'UH{' / 'US{' before drafting.")
inl note_of_codegen_readable_2_5 () = Note ("CODEGEN-READABLE-2", 5u32, "2026-10-31T01:31Z", "compiler", "10-30 21:31 compiler, sizing for step (2): 76 union-name mint sites ($'U[HhSs]{...}' and the Incref/Decref helper names): codegenC 16, codegenGleam 16, CodegenPython 12, codegenLua 12, codegenFsharp 11 (both cores), codegenRust 4, TypeScript 2, Zig 2, Delphi 1 (Kotlin/Swift/Lean/Wasm name unions through other helpers; census those separately). Most sites see only the integer tag, not the Union, so a central rename can't work: each backend's uheap/ustack memo (where the tag is minted for a Union) has to record tag -> source name (unionSourceNames.TryGetValue(box u)), and every mint site reads that table. Pack-sized: do it one backend per applier, C first (it is the oracle; its 16 sites cover type, case, incref/decref names), with a residual-only review (native exit/stdout must be byte-identical). Not started.")
inl conhost_audit () = Task ("CONHOST-AUDIT", "Find where conhost gets spawned by our work and how much pwsh we rely on; plan rewrites (with PWSH-FREE) (user 2026-10-24 06:56 review list)", TaskKind, P2, D3, 75u32, Planned, "", "processes,pwsh,windows,team:main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_conhost_audit () = Planning ("CONHOST-AUDIT", Minutes 120u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_conhost_audit_1 () = Note ("CONHOST-AUDIT", 1u32, "2026-10-24T13:04Z", "main", "2026-10-24 09:04 main: census at 09:00: 38 conhost.exe, 308 MB. By parent: 14 eoie job supervisors (the installed host, one console per job until eoie stage R's no-conhost lands), 13 my serial-runner/heavy-wrapper pwsh (each started detached, so it gets a console; merged at 09:04 into fewer runners), 5 cmd.exe (Cells runs mix.bat through `cmd /c`, which allocates a console; calling erl/elixir directly would not), 2 Invoke-Heavy watchdog pwsh (heavy.ps1 Start-HeavyWatchdog starts a whole pwsh per heavy job; a .NET timer thread in the wrapper would do), and 4 others (mariadbd, wlanext, a user pwsh, 1 with a dead parent). Fix plan, in order of count: (1) eoie stage R (eoie team), (2) fewer runners: one runner per independent chain, never one per land (memory serial-runner-not-job-chains), (3) the Cells mix launch without cmd (EXS-TO-SPIRAL or a direct `erl` launch), (4) the in-process watchdog in heavy.ps1. Related fix made now: land.ps1's reader guard counted the long-lived shared Cells CompilerDaemon (serve_app) as a running Cells run, so no land could ever fire while the daemon lived (step-init waited 2.5 h). The guard now ignores serve_app; notebook runs remain readers.")
inl note_of_conhost_audit_2 () = Note ("CONHOST-AUDIT", 2u32, "2026-10-24T13:06Z", "main", "2026-10-24 09:06 main: step (4) done: heavy.ps1 Start-HeavyWatchdog now runs the watchdog loop as an in-process Start-ThreadJob (fallback: the old detached pwsh), so a heavy job no longer spawns a second pwsh plus conhost. Probe (scratch watchdog-probe.ps1): 0 watchdog processes, 1 thread job, and the probe's child process was stopped at its 1-minute deadline (65 s). This applies to heavy jobs started from now on.")
inl note_of_conhost_audit_3 () = Note ("CONHOST-AUDIT", 3u32, "2026-10-25T17:08Z", "eoie", "13:08 kit (user 10-25: 'study why these parentless pwsh and conhost processes keep spawning and dying, every minute or so').\n- Probe: scratch spawn-probe\\probe.ps1 ran 4 min and recorded 86 births of pwsh/conhost/cmd. All the short-lived ones sit under the 5 claude.exe sessions; none come from eoie. eoie only owns the long-lived supervisor conhosts (see EOIE-SUPERVISOR-NO-CONSOLE).\n- What CC spawns for each PowerShell tool call:\n  (a) a command parser: scoop shim pwsh -EncodedCommand, running a 29 KB AST script, which starts a real pwsh plus conhost. The timeout is CLAUDE_CODE_PWSH_PARSE_TIMEOUT_MS (default 5000, retried);\n  (b) the PreToolUse hook: shim pwsh -Command (a CC wrapper with verdict logic) -> real pwsh -> cmd /c;\n  (c) the call itself: cmd /c chcp & shim pwsh -> real pwsh.\n  Edits add the PostToolUse hook, and every turn end adds the Stop hook.\n- Why they are parentless: at 100% CPU a bare pwsh start takes 2.0-4.7 s (measured). The 5 s parse times out, CC kills the shim it spawned, and the shim's real pwsh child plus its conhost keep running a few more seconds with a dead parent. The retry spawns the next pair.\n- Kept: CLAUDE_CODE_PWSH_PARSE_TIMEOUT_MS=15000 (~/.claude/settings.json env).\n- Tried and reverted: running the 3 hooks inside CC's hook pwsh (& 'hook.ps1'; exit $LASTEXITCODE). It passed a 7/7 pipe test, but live, one PreToolUse run timed out at 45 s with no lease present. Without Git Bash, CC treats a hook timeout as a block, so the old commands were restored at 13:10.\n- Observed live: the no-op hook wrapper itself took 6-12 s under load. A hook timeout blocks tool calls in every session, whatever the hook form, so the real lever is fewer pwsh starts per call. Options left: CC hook 'args' form with an exe in 'command' (no shell wrapper), or Git Bash; CC's error text says the user decides that.")
inl note_of_conhost_audit_4 () = Note ("CONHOST-AUDIT", 4u32, "2026-10-25T17:34Z", "eoie", "13:34 kit (user 10-25 'try healing from this'): the 3 hooks now use Claude Code's args form, which its own error text names: an exe in 'command', arguments in 'args', no shell wrapper.\n- Lease hook: C:\\Windows\\System32\\cmd.exe /d /c if exist %LOCALAPPDATA%\\eoie\\tree-leases\\*.lease <real pwsh> -NoProfile -NonInteractive -File eoie-tree-lease.ps1.\n- Style and Stop hooks: <real pwsh> -NoProfile -NonInteractive -File <script>. The real pwsh path skips the scoop shim.\n- Default onFailure is kept: a failed or timed-out hook no longer blocks every session. The release publish hash gate stays the backstop for the lease.\n- Pipe test (scratch spawn-probe\\hook-args-test.ps1, old shell form vs args form, at 100% CPU): 8/8 identical exit codes, stdout and stderr (lease hit by edit and by command, miss, none; style finding and elsewhere; gate no-session and block).\n  Times old -> new: lease none 4588 -> 65 ms; lease hit 13.6 -> 10.0 s; style finding 13.4 -> 10.5 s; style elsewhere 7.4 -> 3.2 s; gate block 23.6 -> 12.4 s. The old gate was close to its 30 s timeout.\n- Live checks:\n  - an ordinary tool call passes;\n  - a test lease on C:\\probe-root made the live hook refuse a Write there, with the lease message (lease removed after);\n  - gate.log shows a Stop decision at 13:30:29 (block, stop_hook_active True) from another session after the switch at 13:26.\n- 3-min birth probe after the switch: 69 of 102 pwsh/conhost/cmd births exited before the probe could read them (hooks now live ms, not s), so attribution by source is inconclusive. Queried births with a dead parent: 13 in the 4 min before, 1 after.\n- Kept: CLAUDE_CODE_PWSH_PARSE_TIMEOUT_MS=15000. Not done: per-call parser cost itself (CC internal).")
inl note_of_conhost_audit_5 () = Note ("CONHOST-AUDIT", 5u32, "2026-10-30T01:59Z", "main", "10-29 21:59 main, groom (WIP rule, as in the 10-23 and 10-27 grooms): Active -> Paused, progress kept at 75%. No note since 10-25 and no live job; main carries 69 Actives, which is not real WIP. Resume when picked; if its pass already shipped in an installed core, close it Done with that evidence instead.")
inl note_of_conhost_audit_6 () = Note ("CONHOST-AUDIT", 6u32, "2026-10-31T20:59Z", "main", "10-31 16:53 main, pause re-decision (KIT-PAUSE-RULE-REWORK; rule R3 is now 'never auto-pause for silence'): Paused -> PLANNED. It was paused by the old silence/WIP rule, not by a blocker: its notes name no external condition (a 10-30 lab-archive note blocks nothing: restore the draft from the archive into the repo when picked). Back in agile next at its priority; deps, if any, gate it as usual.")
inl note_of_conhost_audit_7 () = Note ("CONHOST-AUDIT", 7u32, "2026-11-02T14:41Z", "main", "main 11-02: parent epic is PWSH-FREE; recorded here instead of as a dep (sole dep on an unfinished parent epic hid it from 'agile next'; AGILE-EPIC-DEP-LINT).")
inl list_sugar_scan () = Task ("LIST-SUGAR-SCAN", "Find verbose smells: Cons/Nil where :: and list sugar exist, and similar ones (learn the catalogue first), then heal them across repos (user 2026-10-24 06:56 review list)", TaskKind, P2, D3, 55u32, Planned, "", "spiral,style,lint,main,team:main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_list_sugar_scan () = Planning ("LIST-SUGAR-SCAN", Minutes 180u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_list_sugar_scan_1 () = Note ("LIST-SUGAR-SCAN", 1u32, "2026-10-24T12:45Z", "main", "2026-10-24 08:45 main: catalogue and first heal. Sugar the core already has: `::` (precedence 50, right associative; in patterns it binds looser than `,`, so `a, b :: c` is `(a, b) :: c`), `| x :: xs =>` patterns, `;[ ... ]` array literals; there is no empty-list literal, so `Nil` stays. Counts: 4,046 `Cons (` in 68 files over all repos; lib has 585 in 20 files, 540 of them in my XSD/XPath/XML/JSON-schema modules. Heal slice %TEMP%\\lib-list-sugar\\apply.ps1, a mechanical rewrite with balanced-paren parsing, strings/macros/comments skipped and multi-comma tuples left alone. Rules: the head is parenthesized when its top level holds an operator binding at 50 or looser, a keyword or a comma; the tail likewise, except for `::`; a cons is wrapped when it is only part of a pattern or of a larger expression; a pattern head that is a constructor application is parenthesized ((Enumeration value) :: rest). Result: 636 sites in 9 files, every file strictly shorter. Gate on all 9 files, then land (runner main-serial-list-sugar). Other repos follow per team once the lib gate proves the rules: fiscal generator.spi alone has 189.")
inl note_of_list_sugar_scan_2 () = Note ("LIST-SUGAR-SCAN", 2u32, "2026-10-24T16:31Z", "main", "main 12:4x: pass 1 (9 XSD/XML lib files, 636 Cons sites) GREEN 11:08 after the pattern-slot fix (a cons chain inside a constructor's tuple slot needs parens in patterns; constructor heads do not) and landed (live: 0 sites left in those files). Pass 2 queued as job main-serial-list-sugar-2b: 43 sites in schematron, schematron_run, base, cursor_walk, oauth, resultm, sm', testing (++# binds tighter than ::, probed; composes with base-try, sm-lines, sm-index in either order). Left in lib after pass 2: listm'.spi 24 (the list module itself, by design), process.spi 4 (tibia's heal), unicode.spi 2 (pending slice), xsd/xsd_emit 9 inside strings or macros the converter skips. Other repos and the similar-smell catalogue are still open.")
inl note_of_list_sugar_scan_3 () = Note ("LIST-SUGAR-SCAN", 3u32, "2026-10-25T07:00Z", "x", "10-25 03:00 x: x HEALED (user 02:2x: 'haven't i asked to use things like :: instead of cons? we did this refactor before, now we're drifting all over. heal then continue'). 148 Cons sites in apps/client rewritten to :: (0 left). Tool: %TEMP%\\x-list-sugar\\apply.ps1, main's apply.ps1 extended for edition-3 spelling. Parens are needed around ?=, ?|, ?>, |:, ?&, a bare ?, \\ lambdas, \\*, :=, :~ and :&. A '|' line is a pattern arm unless it starts '|:', '||' or '|>'. ?& acts like 'when', and ?>/|: like then/else for standalone positions. ';' and '}' end a standalone expression (no redundant parens in record fields). Gate: all 23 crates' Rust plus simframes' Gleam regenerated before and after on a scratch copy, 24 outputs, 0 differ (job-1792909991197). The tree then matched the gated scratch file for file. Also healed: the 17 staged sim modules (38 sites; the 7 left are inside fixture strings quoting old prelude.spi source). Memory list-sugar-not-cons records the rule, with a grep of every diff for 'Cons (' before landing.")
inl note_of_list_sugar_scan_4 () = Note ("LIST-SUGAR-SCAN", 4u32, "2026-10-26T04:50Z", "main", "10-26 00:50 main: CORRECTION to the 10-24 08:45 catalogue note's claim 'there is no empty-list literal, so Nil stays'. It is false: lib already uses [] as an empty list (listm'.spi:251 in choose, :115, :304, :317, am'.spi:82, leptos.spi:870), and a compile probe under edition 3 (packageDir lib, |core- and spiral-) matched '| [] =>' in a pattern and bound ':= empty : list i32 = []' in an expression; the C build exited 3 = count [] * 10 + count [1;2;3], as predicted. So [] is Nil's sugar exactly as :: is Cons's, and EOIE-DO-SLOP-GATE's 'Cons/Nil for ::/[]' was right. The memory list-sugar-not-cons was corrected. Next: a Nil -> [] pass over lib with the same mechanical tool and the same byte-identical-output gate (the sugar is syntax only). The heal must skip Nil where it is a constructor name of a user union rather than the list case (grep each union's cases first), and parenthesize [] in tuple pattern slots like other list patterns. Lanes' code from 10-24 to 10-26 followed the false rule, mine included (tonight's planner.spi and the dir-tree tree heal).")
inl note_of_list_sugar_scan_5 () = Note ("LIST-SUGAR-SCAN", 5u32, "2026-10-26T05:20Z", "main", "10-26 01:19 main: Nil census of lib for the [] heal: 789 Nil tokens outside string literals, in 29 files (xsd_emit 278, xpath_eval 96, xsd_regex 66, xsd 62, xml 50, json_schema 47, xsd_resolve 43, xpath 24, ...). No union in lib has a case named Nil (a '| Nil' line without '=>': 0), so every Nil token is the list case and the heal is mechanical. The xsd*/xpath* files carry the fiscal team's pending slices, so they get the rewrite inside those slices (the n6 rule of SPIRAL-NOTEBOOK-FILES); the rest go in one batch slice with the byte-identical-output gate.")
inl note_of_list_sugar_scan_6 () = Note ("LIST-SUGAR-SCAN", 6u32, "2026-10-26T10:24Z", "eoie", "06:24 eoie: a gap in the earlier codegen-neutral proof of the Cons sugar conversion ('0 of 260 generated files changed', scratch node-sugar.ps1). It regenerated only the touched packages that own a Cargo.toml. A Spiral-only package (no crate of its own) is generated into each consumer crate, so its consumers were never regenerated: the proof covered the crates it named and not the ones that inline them. New generic proof (scratch clean\\respell\\node-neutral.ps1 -Tree -Fix <program.do> -OutName -ExpectSources) reads the touched packages from the program's edit paths, follows package.spiproj packages lists recursively, and regenerates every crate whose closure reaches a touched package. The tree is asserted by source digest first. Gate: 0 generated files changed. Example: the 41 packages of the expect heal reach 85 of 234 crates. The sugar conversion needs the same rerun (generate its .do from a converted copy, then node-neutral) before it lands post-rename.")
inl note_of_list_sugar_scan_7 () = Note ("LIST-SUGAR-SCAN", 7u32, "2026-10-26T13:38Z", "eoie", "09:38 eoie: the Cons sugar conversion is now proven with the closure-aware proof (node-neutral.ps1 -Converter cons-to-sugar.ps1, tree asserted 1207 B92853CE28A241F6): 113 sites in 19 files, the 15 touched packages reach 14 crates, regen exit 0, 0 of 260 generated files changed. Codegen-neutral for every crate that can see it, so the earlier proof's gap (Spiral-only packages' consumers never regenerated) is closed, and the conversion lands post-rename. The converter's string scan also had the escaped-backslash bug (a string ending in one escaped backslash never closed), fixed before this run, with a regression case in cons-to-sugar-test (10/10).")
inl note_of_list_sugar_scan_8 () = Note ("LIST-SUGAR-SCAN", 8u32, "2026-10-30T02:00Z", "main", "10-29 22:00 main, groom (WIP rule, 3 days with no note): Active -> Paused, progress kept at 55%. Last note 2026-10-26, before the 10-28 profile wipe interrupted every lane, and no live job. Resume when picked; it is not abandoned, and it keeps its priority P2.")
inl note_of_list_sugar_scan_9 () = Note ("LIST-SUGAR-SCAN", 9u32, "2026-10-31T21:00Z", "main", "10-31 16:53 main, pause re-decision (KIT-PAUSE-RULE-REWORK; rule R3 is now 'never auto-pause for silence'): Paused -> PLANNED. It was paused by the old silence/WIP rule, not by a blocker: its notes name no external condition (a 10-30 lab-archive note blocks nothing: restore the draft from the archive into the repo when picked). Back in agile next at its priority; deps, if any, gate it as usual.")
inl lambda_parens () = Task ("LAMBDA-PARENS", "Find F#-style parenthesis abuse in Spiral (lambdas need none): remove, compile in a fast loop, restore where it breaks, or teach the formatter (user 2026-10-24 06:56 review list)", TaskKind, P2, D3, 90u32, Planned, "LIB-UNION-CASE-AMBIGUOUS-REDS", "spiral,style,formatter,main,team:main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_lambda_parens () = Planning ("LAMBDA-PARENS", Minutes 180u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_lambda_parens_1 () = Note ("LAMBDA-PARENS", 1u32, "2026-10-30T15:39Z", "main", "10-30 11:39 main: step 1 CENSUS (read-only, tracked .spi in 10 repos; deps/target/_build/lab and /// lines excluded). The removable shape is '(\\ ...)' in LAST-argument position: the matching ')' is the line's last token, so the bare lambda's body extent is the same. Parenthesised lambdas with more after the ')' NEED their parens and are counted separately, not as abuse. Counts, tail (removable candidates) / inner: spiral 98 / 242, kit 13 / 653, x 51 / 183, tibia 35 / 56, dice 5 / 6, i574n.github 1 / 1, fiscal 0 / 2, dir-tree-html 0 / 7, alphabet 0, blog 0. TOTAL 203 candidates. The bare form is valid and already used: lib has 77 bare trailing lambdas (benchmark.spi:142 '|> listm.map \\ row => row, None'). Examples: dice lib/dice.spi:271 '|> listm.map (\\ _ => roll_progressively roll_dice reroll 10i32)'; i574n.github scripts/pins/pins.spi:28; tokens.spi:382 'at |> skip_while (\\ c => c = ' ')'. RISK, why each needs a compile: a candidate inside a multi-line '? ... ?>' condition (tokens.spi:391 '? peek past |> glyph_is (\\ c => c = ':')') may let the bare lambda swallow the next line's ?>, so the title's method (remove, compile, restore where it breaks) applies per file. PLAN: (2) a fast loop per file on a scratch copy: strip the tail parens, compile (--check, or the repo's own build), restore any file that fails or whose generated output changes; (3) the formatter rule (team compiler) once (2) shows which shapes are always safe. Teams own their repos: kit 13, x 51, tibia 35; main does spiral 98, dice 5 and i574n.github 1. Step (2) is compile-bound, so it waits for a quiet box after PACK-8 and the lib windows.")
inl note_of_lambda_parens_2 () = Note ("LAMBDA-PARENS", 2u32, "2026-10-30T18:59Z", "main", "10-30 14:59 main: step (2) METHOD proven on the first site, i574n.github scripts/pins/pins.spi:28 '|> listm.map (\\ t => { ... })' -> '|> listm.map \\ t => { ... }' (APPLIED, unstaged). Proof by output identity on scratch copies laid out like the repo (i574n.github/scripts/pins with a spiral junction, so the package.spiproj ../../../spiral reference resolves), compiling the consumer entry pins_check.spi through BuildSpiral: original and edited both give pins_check.rs 42,281 B D44F445BE0D2E27F, byte-identical; a POSITIVE CONTROL that changes the same lambda's body (version = 'control') gives 41,476 B 910AB4660D2C8B8F, so the edited line is in that compile. Traps hit and recorded: BuildSpiral returns False instead of throwing (check its RUST-COMPILED line and the new .rs mtime, not 'no exception'); it needs spiral/scripts/core.ps1 dot-sourced first (ResolveLink); and a flat scratch copy breaks the relative package path. Generalised loop for the rest (main: spiral 98, dice 5): per file, an edited copy, compile its entry, compare the generated output hash with the original, then a control. Left 103 for main; x 51, tibia 35, kit 13 for their teams.")
inl note_of_lambda_parens_3 () = Note ("LAMBDA-PARENS", 3u32, "2026-10-30T19:10Z", "main", "10-30 15:10 main: dice's 5 sites (lib/dice.spi:13, 271, 273, 295, 297) NOT changed. The identity loop ran with all 5 edits, and original and edited compile to the same Rust (476,460 B, 07F2A90E9CADB091), but the POSITIVE CONTROL (take_while bound 7 -> 6) ALSO gave 07F2A90E, so those functions aren't reachable from dice.spi's Rust main, and identity proves nothing there. The script restored the original byte for byte (git diff empty). LESSON for the loop: the compile must go through an entry that USES the edited code, and the control is what shows that; for dice, that means its test blocks or the notebook build (lib/build.ps1's asserted runs), not dice.spi's main alone. Next for dice: find the consumer entry per function, or prove these through Cells test blocks with a control. pins.spi stays the one applied site.")
inl note_of_lambda_parens_4 () = Note ("LAMBDA-PARENS", 4u32, "2026-10-30T22:33Z", "main", "10-30 18:33 main: consumer map for spiral's sites (re-census on today's tree): 111 trailing-lambda-paren sites in 31 files (up from 98 after today's lib lands and the e3 respell). LIB files with their own test blocks: physics 16 (44 tests), base 9 (27), seq 8 (27), listm' 6 (33), sm' 6 (72), stream 5 (17), am' 3, par' 3, plus 1-2 each in trace, benchmark, otp_actor, json_scan, reactors, testing, reflection, env, file_system, oauth, erlang_port, xml, async; leptos 4 has 0 tests (needs an app consumer). NON-LIB: apps/spiral/spiral.spi 13 (consumer: the spiral CLI crate compile), apps/compiler/spi/tokens.spi 5 (compiler self-host tokens), samples 11 (consumer: the matrix row for each sample). PROOF per lib file: on a scratch lib, edit that file's sites, then lib_gate -File <file> (all its targets) must stay at the same pass count; plus a positive control that flips one edited lambda body and must turn a test red. That shows the test blocks reach the edited code (the dice lesson: identity without a control proves nothing). Batch it as one scratch job per file at BelowNormal once CPU frees; samples ride the next matrix run.")
inl note_of_lambda_parens_5 () = Note ("LAMBDA-PARENS", 5u32, "2026-10-30T22:57Z", "main", "10-30 18:57 main: the lib proof METHOD WORKS (prove-lib-file.ps1 in main-drafts\\session-1030\\lambda-parens). json_scan.spi: base 2/2, edit 2/2 (0 type errors), control (an unbound name spliced into the edited lambdas) 0/2 with 2 type errors -> PROVEN: the edit keeps every result and the tests provably reach the edited code. The batch over the other 20 lib files with sites is running now at BelowNormal (results in batch-1030.txt; edited copies in %TEMP%\\lambda-parens-prove). PROVEN files then land together in one lib window, each re-based against the live file at land time.")
inl note_of_lambda_parens_6 () = Note ("LAMBDA-PARENS", 6u32, "2026-10-31T00:06Z", "main", "10-30 20:06 main: FIRST LIB LAND of the batch proof. json_scan.spi (2 sites) and physics.spi (16 sites) were each PROVEN by prove-lib-file.ps1 (3 arms on scratch lib copies: base = edit on every test, and a control that splices an unbound name into the edited lambdas fails its tests, so the tests reach the edits; physics took 2,624 s under load). Landed with land-proven.ps1 in a tree-write window at 20:03: it waited for no live lib readers, checked that each proof's base copy is byte-identical to the live file, and diffed each edited copy line by line (every changed line drops exactly one paren pair). Packed in the same window: platform.spi:38's failwith label 'runtime.run_platform /' -> 'platform.run_platform /' (LIB-CANON-NAMES leftover). Close GREEN: kit's 14 signatures unchanged, document_test exit 0, type_probe 3/3 (json_scan 2 typed, physics 43, platform 2; controls ok). Hashes: json_scan C4DA61798533, physics D8652A79FC7F, platform 8D3F4B298341. Undo: lambda-parens\\undo-land-20261030-200309. The batch (18 files left, ~40 min each under load) keeps running; land the next PROVEN files the same way.")
inl note_of_lambda_parens_7 () = Note ("LAMBDA-PARENS", 7u32, "2026-10-31T00:56Z", "main", "10-30 20:56 main: base.spi (9 sites) is BREAKS and stays as is. In the edit arm, join_body_unit_6 and join_body_unit_8 FAIL on all 5 targets where base passes them, and most later tests went not_run (the arm stopped after them). A five-target consistent failure is not load. The base arm itself had 2 flaky rows on the loaded box (from_pair_twice failed on one target, from_pair_3 failed once), so treat single-row differences with care. Next for base.spi: find which of the 9 paren removals sits in the join_body_unit_6/8 path (likely a lambda whose parens group an application, i.e. not a pure tail lambda) and exclude that site; the remaining sites can be re-proven. The batch continues (seq.spi now).")
inl note_of_lambda_parens_8 () = Note ("LAMBDA-PARENS", 8u32, "2026-10-31T01:36Z", "main", "10-30 21:36 main, base.spi BREAKS narrowed (read-only). The finder (prove-lib-file.ps1 Get-TailSites) only takes lines where a '(\\ ' lambda's matching ')' ends the line, so the failing tests' own comparison parens were never edited; the break comes through a definition. Of the 9 sites, 2 are not plain tail lambdas: line 819 in try_unit, ex_fn (\\ () => $'nil' : exn), where the parens scope the ': exn' annotation to the lambda body, and without them it can bind to the whole application (a typing change, not spacing); and line 503 in defaultof, (\\ k => \\* v. => ...), a lambda whose body is a forall-lambda. The other 7 (try, try_2, try_unit:804, and the 4 test-only try_unit/try_finally calls) are plain. NEXT: (1) teach Get-TailSites to skip a lambda whose body ends in a top-level ': T' annotation or starts with a forall '\\*', a general rule since the same shapes exist in other files; (2) re-prove base.spi with those 2 excluded (about 67 min under load, so after the batch).")
inl note_of_lambda_parens_9 () = Note ("LAMBDA-PARENS", 9u32, "2026-10-31T02:20Z", "main", "10-30 22:20 main: batch PAUSED to cut box load (2 GB free, 7 main jobs plus kit's TRAIN5; Cells daemons were retiring under memory pressure). Done: json_scan and physics PROVEN and landed; base.spi (9 sites) and seq.spi (8 sites) BREAKS, both measured with the OLD finder, before the 21:3x rule that skips lambdas whose body ends in a ': T' annotation or starts with a forall. Resume from listm'.spi with the new finder, and re-prove base.spi (now 7 sites) and seq.spi. Remaining files: listm', sm', stream, am', par', trace, benchmark, otp_actor, reactors, testing, reflection, env, file_system, oauth, erlang_port, xml, async.")
inl note_of_lambda_parens_10 () = Note ("LAMBDA-PARENS", 10u32, "2026-10-31T05:21Z", "main", "10-31 01:20 main: batch RESUMED on the node (job-1793423997747, Normal priority, mirror main-manifest) with the prover now in the repo at apps/cells/spi/lambda_parens_prove.ps1 (paths relative to its folder; finder skips annotated-body and forall-body lambdas). Order: base.spi and seq.spi re-proved first (both BREAKS under the old finder), then listm', sm', stream, am', par', trace, benchmark, otp_actor, reactors, testing, reflection, env, file_system, oauth, erlang_port, xml, async. One verdict line per file goes to C:\\home\\remote\\main-manifest\\lambda-parens-results.txt on the node, and edited copies are kept under lp-work. PROVEN files land on the box with lib_land_window.ps1.")
inl note_of_lambda_parens_11 () = Note ("LAMBDA-PARENS", 11u32, "2026-10-31T06:42Z", "main", "10-31 02:42 base.spi (7), seq.spi (8), listm'.spi (6) PROVEN on the node (edit keeps every test result; control reaches the edits). Canonical round-trip: 0 non-blank diff. One land queued behind the pg land (bgm53gg1d, lib_land_window, bases 3B198B46C303/D4959BC04EAB/23A5559D4D25). Node batch job-1793423997747 continues; results in C:\\home\\remote\\main-manifest\\lambda-parens-results.txt")
inl note_of_lambda_parens_12 () = Note ("LAMBDA-PARENS", 12u32, "2026-10-31T09:53Z", "main", "10-31 05:53 main, node batch: 14 more PROVEN (stream 5, am' 3, par' 3, trace 2, otp_actor 2, reactors 2, testing 2, reflection 2, env 1, file_system 1, oauth 1, erlang_port 1, xml 1, async 1); sm'.spi BREAKS (6 sites; the edit changes test results). Hypothesis 'a following |> line gets swallowed by the paren-less lambda' REFUTED by evidence: listm'.spi:123 '|> listm.map (\\ x => x, x)' + next '|> unzip' would not type-check if swallowed, yet listm'/seq (same shape) keep every test result with the control reaching the edits; Spiral's layout ends the lambda at a new line at the outer indent. sm' suspects: the sites before '///> gleam' / '///> lua' target lines (sm' :68 annotated '\\ (i : i64)', :80 am'.init_base) or the backend arms at :824/:911. NEXT: per-site bisection of sm' on the node (prove each of the 6 sites alone). The 14 new PROVEN files mostly overlap the heading-cleanup batches: after those land, re-apply each lambda edit with git merge-file (heading removal touches only comment lines) and land as a second lambda wave.")
inl note_of_lambda_parens_13 () = Note ("LAMBDA-PARENS", 13u32, "2026-10-31T10:11Z", "main", "10-31 06:11 main, sm' BISECTION started on the node: job-1793441483178-main-lp-bisect-sm (two halves: sites 1,2,6 = lines 68/80/1691, the sites before ///> target lines; sites 3,4,5 = lines 678/824/911, the pipeline sites), results in C:\\home\\remote\\main-manifest\\lambda-parens-bisect-sm.txt. lambda_parens_prove.ps1 gained -Site (comma-joined 1-based indices in file order; work dir suffixed -sites-...) and no longer forces BelowNormal priority (it starves on a loaded box).")
inl note_of_lambda_parens_14 () = Note ("LAMBDA-PARENS", 14u32, "2026-10-31T10:22Z", "main", "10-31 06:22 main, WAVE 2 PREPARED: the 14 newly PROVEN edited copies fetched to %TEMP%\\lp-land\\wave2\\edited; every mirror base equals live. git merge-file onto the heading-cleanup outputs (ours = headings-step2\\out, base = live, theirs = lambda edit): 0 conflicts in all 14, every lambda line carried (stream 5, am' 3, par' 3, otp_actor 2, reactors 2, testing 2, reflection 2, trace 2, env/file_system/oauth/erlang_port/xml/async 1). Canonical forms in wave2\\canon (bic7oarwa). Land after the 3 heading batches, with bases = the heading outputs' hashes. Also 06:2x: kit's trains now run on a lib snapshot (launch-train.ps1 copies spiral/lib/spiral, release-train passes -Lib and SPIRAL_CELLS_LIB); lib_land_window.ps1 Get-LiveReaders treats release-train.ps1 ancestors with a non-live -Lib as scratch, so trains no longer hold lib lands (verified: 0 live readers with train5e running).")
inl note_of_lambda_parens_15 () = Note ("LAMBDA-PARENS", 15u32, "2026-10-31T11:03Z", "main", "10-31 07:03 main, LANDED green 06:33 (lib_land_queue): base.spi 9398472528DE, seq.spi 7617B92B3DCC, listm'.spi 007CEAD42CAD (21 sites; undo lib-land-undo\\20261031-062412); close: 14 signatures unchanged, document_test 22/22, type_probe 3/3 (25+26+32 tests typed). sm' BISECTION: sites 1,2,6 PROVEN (975 s) and sites 3,4,5 PROVEN (1163 s), yet all 6 together gave BREAKS at 03:10. Either an interaction between the halves or a flaky test result in the 03:10 run: NEXT rerun all 6 on the node as an A/A for repeatability before deciding. Wave 2 (14 files) queued as bauk7x67c will REFUSE 9 of its files' bases (heading batches 2-3 were undone), by design; re-merge after those batches land.")
inl note_of_lambda_parens_16 () = Note ("LAMBDA-PARENS", 16u32, "2026-10-31T12:25Z", "main", "10-31 08:25 main: sm' A/A on the node (job-1793447291260, all 6 sites): PROVEN (edit keeps every test result, control reaches the edits), as were both halves. The 03:10 BREAKS did not repeat: a flaky test result under that night's load. sm'.spi edit (base A683500F9D51 = live, canonical with its _real.spir) queued last in land-queue-0820.")
inl note_of_lambda_parens_17 () = Note ("LAMBDA-PARENS", 17u32, "2026-10-31T13:42Z", "main", "10-31 09:42 main: WAVE 2 LANDED green 09:21 (13 files merged onto the heading-cleaned lib: am' 3B9CBDF05159, async 2D924212CEEB, env C6E4B205A466, erlang_port 70BEB3B54573, file_system 22ECEEC5773F, oauth 363E05FDFFA2, otp_actor F20775D10318, par' C7D74049D149, reactors 76A453E0B86E, stream FC90147ED0B4, testing 254D923ACEB9, trace B32A35D33460, xml 8DCCACD535ED; undo lib-land-undo\\20261031-090630) and sm'.spi LANDED 09:31 (6 sites, 489262328AFA; undo ...-093151). Each close: signatures unchanged, document_test, type_probe. Landed so far: 3 + 13 + 1 = 17 files. LEFT: reflection.spi's proven edit (re-merge onto its renamed live text, rides the final heading wave).")
inl note_of_lambda_parens_18 () = Note ("LAMBDA-PARENS", 18u32, "2026-10-31T15:18Z", "main", "10-31 11:18 main: reflection.spi's proven edit LANDED 11:06 with the final heading wave (reflection.spi 26C999761970). Every file the node batch PROVEN is now in lib: base, seq, listm' (06:33), wave 2's 13 (09:21), sm' (09:31), reflection (11:06): 18 files, 50 sites. lib/spiral is DONE for this item. Left: consumer repos (each team runs lambda_parens_prove.ps1 on its own sources).")
inl note_of_lambda_parens_19 () = Note ("LAMBDA-PARENS", 19u32, "2026-10-31T15:22Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-31 11:21 fiscal: fiscal/src has 7 parenthesized lambdas (corpus_checks 432/465/477/493, generator 8/557/690) and none is trailing: each is followed by another argument (listm.map (\\\\ x => ..) xs, listm'.try_find_ (\\\\ .. ) rows), so the parens are required and no prover run is needed.")
inl note_of_lambda_parens_20 () = Note ("LAMBDA-PARENS", 20u32, "2026-10-31T15:29Z", "main", "10-31 11:29 main: consumers: dice lib/dice.spi has 5 tail-paren sites (e.g. :13 stream.take_while, :271/:273/:295 listm.map); dice_contract.spi and alphabet's documents.spi have 0. lambda_parens_prove.ps1 can't prove dice (lib_gate needs a lib/spiral layout); the proof for dice is its own build.ps1 (4 asserted backends) base vs edit, a node job. kit, x, fiscal and tibia were sent the prover for their sources.")
inl note_of_lambda_parens_21 () = Note ("LAMBDA-PARENS", 21u32, "2026-10-31T18:25Z", "kit", "10-31 14:3x kit: checked for kit's Spiral sources. (1) Notebook-era headings: kit/src has 0 '/// #' lines, so nothing to drop. (2) Trailing-lambda parens: a rough scan finds about 15 sites in 14 kit files. lambda_parens_prove.ps1 proves through lib_gate.ps1, which needs files under a lib/spiral tree, so kit would need a variant that proves via a package notebook. At about 15 sites the value doesn't justify it now; kit leaves its parens until the prover takes a -Notebook/-Package mode.")
inl note_of_lambda_parens_22 () = Note ("LAMBDA-PARENS", 22u32, "2026-11-01T03:40Z", "main", "main 23:4x WIP groom (user 23:3x: drift/stall, too much waiting): Active -> Planned. lib waves landed; kit's ~15 sites are kit's to land (n21).")
inl note_of_lambda_parens_23 () = Note ("LAMBDA-PARENS", 23u32, "2026-11-01T14:39Z", "compiler", "11-01 10:38 compiler, R5: the user asks for smarter paren removal beyond trailing lambdas; the measurement of what the formatter still misses is split to FORMATTER-PARENS-MISSES (parse-tree-equality census per ParenShape, then one formatter rule per shape proven safe). This item keeps the trailing-lambda proof batch.")
inl nested_if_tool () = Task ("NESTED-IF-TOOL", "Tooling to detect overly nested ifs as candidates for typefeatureset modeling (user 2026-10-24 06:56 review list)", Feature, P2, D3, 60u32, Planned, "", "tooling,typefeatures,lint,main,team:main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_nested_if_tool () = Planning ("NESTED-IF-TOOL", Minutes 180u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_nested_if_tool_1 () = Note ("NESTED-IF-TOOL", 1u32, "2026-10-24T13:22Z", "main", "2026-10-24 09:22 main: prototype (scratch nested-if-census.ps1: an indentation stack per definition; elif/else/then continue a chain, so flat elif chains count as one level). With threshold 4 over lib, x, tibia and fiscal: 11 definitions, which is little, because elif chains dominate. Deepest: tibia map.spi ids_with (7), tibia map walk_tiles, competitor tick, random tick (5), lib decimal.spi rec, parsing.spi many_chars_till (5), unicode.char_of (5, all inside the old C macro text the unicode heal removes), xsd_regex.spi let (4), tibia steps/observer/step_away (4). x and fiscal have none at 4+. Candidates for typefeatureset modeling: lib decimal.rec and parsing.many_chars_till (main; the parsing one with FPARSEC-RETIRE), xsd_regex (main), the tibia ones (tibia team, TIBIA-GRANULAR-HEAL). As a lint, depth >= 4 belongs in GRANULAR-LINT's eoie verb next to the macro rules, with the census as its baseline.")
inl note_of_nested_if_tool_2 () = Note ("NESTED-IF-TOOL", 2u32, "2026-10-28T01:33Z", "main", "10-27 21:33 main: Groom 10-27 21:2x (WIP rule, set before applying). main's Active items with no note since 10-24 were classed by the evidence in the tree, not by age alone. (1) SHIPPED -> Done: the item's pass is in the installed tree core (apps/compiler/spiral_compiler.fs, core 8660C34A11F33259, the installed 5m' core), found by its own identifier or anchor, and an accept has passed on that core since (accept-6, run single-flight-20261027-173417, 0 blocking cells). (2) COLD -> Paused: no lane is driving it this week and nothing is queued. Its last note keeps the next step, and it resumes when a lane picks it or its condition fires. (3) KEPT Active: a lane is on it this week, or demand is waiting on it. (4) Blocked: only the user's commit or push unblocks it.\n Verdict for this item: (2) COLD, so Active -> Paused. The next step is the one in its latest note above.")
inl note_of_nested_if_tool_3 () = Note ("NESTED-IF-TOOL", 3u32, "2026-10-31T21:00Z", "main", "10-31 16:53 main, pause re-decision (KIT-PAUSE-RULE-REWORK; rule R3 is now 'never auto-pause for silence'): Paused -> PLANNED. It was paused by the old silence/WIP rule, not by a blocker: its notes name no external condition (a 10-30 lab-archive note blocks nothing: restore the draft from the archive into the repo when picked). Back in agile next at its priority; deps, if any, gate it as usual.")
inl mega_parallel_join_points () = Task ("MEGA-PARALLEL-JOIN-POINTS", "Research why the 5 mega samples cannot use parallel compilation (no join points?): check whether join points can be introduced or the limits are purely type-based (user 2026-10-24 06:56 review list)", TaskKind, P2, D4, 70u32, Planned, "HOPACSOTA-P3", "compiler,hopacsota,parallel,main,team:compiler", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_mega_parallel_join_points () = Planning ("MEGA-PARALLEL-JOIN-POINTS", Minutes 240u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_mega_parallel_join_points_1 () = Note ("MEGA-PARALLEL-JOIN-POINTS", 1u32, "2026-10-25T23:38Z", "compiler", "Question (user 2026-10-24 06:56): why can't the 5 mega samples use parallel compilation (no join points?);\ncan join points be introduced, or are the limits purely type-based?\n\nEvidence (source structure of all 5 mega samples, apps/compiler/tmp/samples/mega_*/main.spi):\n  sample                                          join  inl   let rec/and  annot\n  mega_brzozowski_derivatives            (3219 L) 0     435   92           304\n  mega_lean_cic_bottom_up_kernel         (9295 L) 3     1823  221          427\n  mega_omniledger_erp_kernel            (11256 L) 2     2344  229          497\n  mega_spiral_proves_spiral_rel_consist (11121 L) 0     1342  273          457\n  mega_zeta_structural_laboratory        (7096 L) 0     1146  84           311\n\nFinding: the mega samples lack join points by AUTHORING STYLE, not by type-impossibility.\n- ~0 `join` keyword across all five (0,3,2,0,0 vs 435-2344 `inl`). They are written in Spiral's inline idiom:\n  an `inl` binding partial-evaluates INTO the call site, producing no separate evaluation task. Join points\n  (the units hopacsota's parallel worklist distributes) come from `join` or non-inlined type-known methods.\n  So partial evaluation collapses each mega program into one giant inlined expression tree -> nothing to\n  distribute -> sequential -> the documented 10-30x \"mega roots\" slowness in gpt5parallel/hopacsota.\n- NOT purely type-based: the samples are heavily type-annotated (304-497 each), so the \"type-known body\"\n  precondition for deferral is already met in most places. JPs CAN be introduced.\n\nTwo paths to introduce join points (each needs a build to measure speedup AND oracle parity):\n1. Author-side: mark the recursive/expensive methods (the 84-273 `let rec`/`and` per sample, e.g. brzozowski's\n   derivative recursion) with `join`. Direct but edits the samples.\n2. Evaluator-side (more valuable, generic): a hopacsota heuristic that treats a type-known non-inline recursive\n   method as an IMPLICIT join point, so existing samples parallelize without edits. This is the real SOTA lever.\nCatch: `join` memoizes + evaluates a body as a unit instead of inlining, so it CHANGES the residual. Any JP\nintroduction must keep -Suite all oracle parity (DISAGREE 0, residuals explained) while cutting mega wall-clock.\n\nConfirmation (deferred, needs the compiler + a run under 100% CPU):\n- Build hopacsota, compile a mega root, read the eval_worklist JP counters (spiral_compiler.fs ~19198+\n  \"eval_worklist_*\" snapshot kinds) to confirm the live JP count is ~0 for these samples.\n- Add `join` to one recursive method in mega_brzozowski_derivatives, rebuild, measure JP count + wall-clock +\n  residual vs the single-flight oracle. If parity holds and wall-clock drops, pursue path 2 (the heuristic).")
inl note_of_mega_parallel_join_points_2 () = Note ("MEGA-PARALLEL-JOIN-POINTS", 2u32, "2026-10-26T20:35Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-26 (build-independent source-read: located the EXACT deferral predicate; sharpens path 2 and corrects two guesses in note 1). Source: spiral_compiler.fs PartEvalSeamsHopacsota module, 53966-54046 (the Session.Defer member + policy parse), and the method-JP seam 160416-160445.\n\nTHE DEFER PREDICATE (Session.Defer, 54011-54046):\n  match policy, deferred with\n  | (BreadthFirst | Seeded _ | Parallel), Some result when active -> <spawn/queue the body as a task>; result\n  | _ -> evaluate s            // INLINE, no deferral\nTwo gates, both in this one match:\n1. POLICY gate: `SPIRAL_HOPACSOTA_POLICY=inline` parses to None (53986), and None is NOT in the deferring arm -> every body evaluates inline. CRUCIAL: DepthFirst (dfs) is ALSO not in the deferring arm, so `dfs` inlines too. Only bfs / random:<seed> / parallel actually enqueue. So with the DEFAULT policy, NOTHING defers, for ANY sample -- parallelism is off by default.\n2. ANNOTATION gate: deferral fires only when `deferred = Some result`. The method seam passes the method's `annot` option (160443: `PartEvalSeams.defer hopacsota s {...} annot ...`). An unannotated method body (annot=None) falls to `| _ -> evaluate s` = inline. Recursive JPs must be annotated (160423 raises \"Recursive join points must be annotated\"), so the recursive JPs that EXIST are deferrable; but a body must first BECOME a join_point_method (not inline) to reach this seam at all.\n\nWHY THE MEGA SAMPLES HAVE ~0 LIVE JPs -- now three compounding gates, not one:\n(a) inl-authoring (note 1's finding): `inl` inlines into the call site, so few bodies ever become join_point_method -> few JP SITES.\n(b) annotation gate: only annotated method JPs defer (recursive ones are always annotated, so this gate mostly passes WHERE a JP site exists).\n(c) policy gate: the default `inline` (and `dfs`) defer nothing regardless.\nSo \"no join points\" = (few JP sites) x (must be annotated) x (default policy defers nothing).\n\nCONSEQUENCE FOR PATH 2 (the implicit-JP heuristic, the SOTA lever): marking a type-known recursive method as an implicit JP is necessary but NOT sufficient -- it only helps when (i) the body is actually routed through the method seam as a join_point_method instead of inlining, (ii) it carries an annotation so `deferred = Some` (the samples are heavily annotated, so usually available), and (iii) the run uses bfs/random/parallel, NOT the default inline. Any speedup measurement MUST set SPIRAL_HOPACSOTA_POLICY to a deferring value; under the default it is a no-op by construction.\n\nCORRECTION to note 1's confirmation step: the live-JP instrument is NOT \"eval_worklist_* snapshot kinds\" (my earlier guess) -- it is the census: `SPIRAL_HOPACSOTA_CENSUS_OUT=<file>` (53999-54043) makes Session emit one TSV row per DEFERRED body (census_id, parent, offset_us, duration_us). So the confirmation is: build hopacsota, compile a mega root under SPIRAL_HOPACSOTA_POLICY=parallel (or bfs) WITH SPIRAL_HOPACSOTA_CENSUS_OUT set, and count rows -> ~0 rows confirms ~0 deferrable JPs for that sample. (Under the default inline policy the census is trivially empty, which would be a false confirmation -- must use a deferring policy.)\n\nSTILL BUILD-BOUND (unchanged): the actual JP-count + wall-clock + oracle-parity measurement needs a hopacsota build + a run under 100% CPU. This read removes the guesswork about WHAT to set and WHERE the gate is.")
inl note_of_mega_parallel_join_points_3 () = Note ("MEGA-PARALLEL-JOIN-POINTS", 3u32, "2026-10-27T01:00Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-26 build-independent follow-up to the Defer-predicate note: located WHERE the inline-vs-join-point routing happens -- it is PURELY SYNTACTIC, no heuristic. The term match (spiral_compiler.fs:160393+): EJoinPoint' (:160408) reaches the deferrable method/closure/type JP seam (:160416, from a `join`/`:~`); EFun'/ERecursiveFun' (:160400-401) go to `efun` and are INLINED (from `inl`); ERecBlock (:160406) fails with \"Recursive blocks should be inlined and eliminated during the prepass\" (mutual recursive inl groups are prepass-inlined). So the mega samples' inl-authored recursive methods are inlined/prepass-eliminated and NEVER reach the JP seam -> ~0 JPs, confirmed at the routing level (completes note 1+2's \"by authoring style\"). PATH 2 HOOK POINT = the PREPASS (where ERecBlock is eliminated :160406 and raw EJoinPoint transformed :160407): an implicit-JP heuristic must convert a TYPE-KNOWN RECURSIVE method into an EJoinPoint' (with an inferred annotation so Defer's deferred=Some holds) INSTEAD of inlining -- a NEW prepass rule, not a tweak. Full path 2 = (prepass) type-known-recursive-inl -> EJoinPoint'+annot AND (runtime) a deferring policy (bfs/parallel, per note 2); either alone is a no-op. Confirm the ~0-JP claim via SPIRAL_HOPACSOTA_CENSUS_OUT under a deferring policy (not the guessed eval_worklist counters). Impl + measure still build-bound. (Routing line refs re-confirmed in the unshifted 160k region 10-26.)")
inl note_of_mega_parallel_join_points_4 () = Note ("MEGA-PARALLEL-JOIN-POINTS", 4u32, "2026-10-28T14:33Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-28 PATH-2 DESIGN (build-independent source read; exact deferral mechanism). The parallelism seam is PartEvalSeams.defer, two call sites both INSIDE the EJoinPoint arm: 159875 (JP closures, deferred=Some() always) and 160498 (JP methods, deferred=`annot` -- deferred to the worklist ONLY if the join point carries an up-front return-type annotation; term_scope'' then runs the body while dependents proceed on annot, checked against the body type at 160501). So deferral (hence parallelism) happens ONLY for JOIN POINTS. The mega samples have ~0 `join` and inl everything, so no EJoinPoint reaches 160498 -> nothing to defer -> sequential (the 10-30x). Two preconditions to parallelize a body: (1) it is a JOIN POINT (`join` or a non-inlined typed method); (2) the JP is annotated (the annot gate).\nLEVERS: A (author, direct) add `join`+return annotation to the mega recursive methods (e.g. brzozowski derivative) -> deferrable JPs; CHANGES the residual (recursion becomes an emitted annotated function, not an inlined tree). B (evaluator, SOTA, no sample edits) promote a type-known recursive inl/method to an implicit annotated JP; the hard part is precondition (2) -- get the return type up-front WITHOUT evaluating -> this IS HOPACSOTA-P3 D16.3 `checked return-type hints` (guess from prior build / cheap inference, defer speculatively, check at 160501, restart on mismatch). MEGA-PARALLEL-JOIN-POINTS thus CONVERGES with HOPACSOTA-P3 D16.\nPARITY (the tradeoff, note 1 confirmed): introducing JPs is NOT residual-preserving -- a JP emits a function (TyJoinPoint, 160506) where inl emitted an inlined tree, so residual hashes change and output grows; the Defer seam preserves residuals only for JPs that ALREADY exist (single-flight side is a no-op), and the mega samples have none. Path-2 must keep BEHAVIOR identical (exit/stdout), re-accept the oracle with the JP-structured output, and justify the speedup vs the larger output.\nMEASURE (build-bound): apply lever A to ONE brzozowski method, build hopacsota, read eval_worklist JP counters (JP count rises from ~0), measure mega wall-clock vs single-flight, diff residual (expect change -> re-accept), verify exit/stdout unchanged + -Suite all no DISAGREE/REGRESSED; if parity+speedup hold, pursue lever B via D16.3. Research question answered: JPs CAN be introduced (not purely type-based); the limit is authoring style (inl) + the annot precondition, both addressable.")
inl note_of_mega_parallel_join_points_5 () = Note ("MEGA-PARALLEL-JOIN-POINTS", 5u32, "2026-10-28T14:38Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-28 (main go/no-go method): lever B (implicit annotated JPs from return-type hints) is the PRODUCT, sequenced AFTER HOPACSOTA-P3 D16.3 hints (where the up-front-type precondition is solved). Lever A is ONLY a MEASUREMENT PROBE, never a shipped sample edit. Steps (build-bound, run after TRAIN3 when a slot frees): copy runtime_native to a SCRATCH dir (NEVER edit the real mega sample -- it is the benchmark); hand-add `join` + return annotations to its recursive methods (brzozowski derivative recursion); build hopacsota; measure single-flight vs 8 workers with the paired bisect method (lab/bisect.ps1 -GcServer 0, in-round A/A, per COMPILER-PACK-SPEED-AB); read eval_worklist JP counters to confirm JPs formed (rise from ~0). Output = lever A's speedup CEILING + whether the residual change + oracle re-accept are worth it. Record that ceiling as B's GO/NO-GO input before anyone builds B. main's call 10-28.")
inl note_of_mega_parallel_join_points_6 () = Note ("MEGA-PARALLEL-JOIN-POINTS", 6u32, "2026-10-31T22:57Z", "main", "10-31 18:56 main, pre-night groom: Active -> PLANNED (not Paused: no note in over 48 h and no live job, so it isn't work in progress, but nothing blocks it; it stays in agile next at its priority and progress for the night run to pull).")
inl gleam_rust_split () = Task ("GLEAM-RUST-SPLIT", "Master the split between Gleam (subject or better) and Rust (rayon/crossbeam) for runnable projects: find improvements in x and tibia and where eoie/kit, which predates the Gleam backend, should adopt it (user 2026-10-24 06:56 review list)", Story, P0, D5, 55u32, Planned, "X-BACKEND-MAP", "gleam,rust,concurrency,x,tibia,kit,team:main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_gleam_rust_split () = Planning ("GLEAM-RUST-SPLIT", Minutes 480u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_gleam_rust_split_1 () = Note ("GLEAM-RUST-SPLIT", 1u32, "2026-10-24T11:22Z", "x", "10-24 07:22 x: grooming de-dup. The x part of this item is X-BACKEND-MAP, which maps each x component by BACKEND-BALANCE's rule with one measurement per switch; x is the playground for the rule. This item reuses that map and its measurements for x and adds the tibia and eoie/kit parts, so the x mapping is not done twice. Dep added: X-BACKEND-MAP.")
inl note_of_gleam_rust_split_2 () = Note ("GLEAM-RUST-SPLIT", 2u32, "2026-10-25T15:05Z", "x", "2026-10-25 11:05 -04:00 x: 'subject or better', read in gleam_otp 1.3.0 (Cells' warm package, %LOCALAPPDATA%\\spiral-bin\\cells-gleam-warm\\*\\build\\packages\\gleam_otp) and gleam_erlang 1.3. 1.x has actor, static_supervisor, factory_supervisor (Erlang simple_one_for_one: children from a template, started on demand), supervision (Restart Permanent/Transient/Temporary, significant) and port. There is NO Task module any more (0.16 had task.gleam). Finding for x's sim shell: runner_guard runs each executor as spawn_unlinked + monitor + a selector with a deadline. That is the right primitive for one-shot work whose crash or timeout must be REPORTED: it is Elixir's Task.Supervisor.async_nolink + yield/shutdown, and a supervisor would restart or log instead of reporting. Its gap is ownership. Workers and guards are unlinked, so when the shell dies (crash, or a test ending), a stalled worker lives until the VM exits. That's harmless for a batch replay or a CLI (the VM exits) and a leak for a long-lived service. Rule, applied mechanically by BACKEND-BALANCE's G1/G3. (a) Batch replay and Cells tests keep today's monitor guard. (b) When the shell becomes a service (X-BACKEND-MAP component 4, the X API server; 5, xc watch), guards go under a factory_supervisor with Temporary children owned by the shell's supervisor, so shutting the shell kills its executors, and memory_watch goes under a static_supervisor one_for_one with Transient restart. (c) Named processes (process.new_name / named_subject) replace passing subjects around only where a restarted actor must stay reachable, which is exactly (b). Rust side: rayon via LIB-PAR-MAP for R1 work. crossbeam only where threads must hand items through bounded channels (a streaming pipeline); x has none today, so no crossbeam work for x. Measurements stay with X-BACKEND-MAP's registered experiments (quiet box).")
inl note_of_gleam_rust_split_3 () = Note ("GLEAM-RUST-SPLIT", 3u32, "2026-10-25T21:19Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-25 17:19 tibia part, classified by BACKEND-BALANCE's rule (x's half is X-BACKEND-MAP). (1) Live arena orchestration (apps/host: host loop, world_port, executor) is BEAM/Gleam: supervising canary, mariadb and OTClient processes with deadlines and crash restarts is what OTP pays for; landing in TIBIA-BACKEND-MAP. (2) Probes inside the client stay Lua (OTClient runs Lua 5.1), compiled from Spiral (TIBIA-NO-HAND-LUA). (3) Simulators (hunting_pack, pack_sim, hunt_sim) keep the C oracle and WGSL GPU twins, which the tests compare. (4) Batch sweeps over independent seeds (scripts/predict.ps1, the duo predictions, saturation sweeps) run today as one single-threaded C exe (PR-28's 200 hunts took 236 s); this is the Rust + rayon candidate through lib par'.map (LIB-PAR-MAP, 50%). Rule written before measuring: once LIB-PAR-MAP's Rust arm lands, rebuild predict on the Rust backend with par'.map over the seed block; adopt Rust for sweeps iff its rows are byte-identical to the C rows on the same 100 held-out seeds and wall time is at most 1/3 of C on this box's cores; otherwise C stays and the measurement goes on this item. (5) Offline tools (apps/map generator, predict tables) follow (4)'s result. Deps: LIB-PAR-MAP for (4); nothing else blocks the tibia part.")
inl note_of_gleam_rust_split_4 () = Note ("GLEAM-RUST-SPLIT", 4u32, "2026-10-26T08:46Z", "x", "2026-10-26 03:57 x: eoie/kit part, classified by BACKEND-BALANCE's rule from the source (read-only, renamed tree src/kit_*). Concurrency lives in four places. (1) Job supervision (kit_jobs, kit_job_supervise, kit_job_host): detached per-job supervisor processes with file heartbeats (heartbeat_ms) and std::thread::sleep polling. That supervision must outlive the caller and belong to the caller's session (memory rule: parent = the caller's nearest non-shell ancestor, one host per owner), which a resident BEAM node or a VM per job would break, and a BEAM start per job is slow (measured 03:5x: bare erl -noshell -eval 'halt().' took 3.2/6.8/3.2 s on this loaded box). Verdict: stays Rust; the gain to look for is OS waits (process handles) instead of sleep polling, not Gleam. (2) kit_process_streaming: std::thread::spawn + mpsc to drain child stdout/stderr together, the textbook Rust shape; stays. (3) parallel_search_contract: a hand-built rayon ThreadPoolBuilder(num_threads(workers)); lib-first candidate for lib par'.map once LIB-PAR-MAP's Rust arm lands (same move as tibia's sweeps, n3), with the rule: switch iff output is identical and wall time is not worse at the same worker count. (4) Process tables and observation (kit_process_table, kit_process_observation, kit_machine_windows): native OS APIs, Rust by definition. Nothing in kit is an actor tree with restarts, the shape where BEAM pays, so kit adopts no Gleam component; the eoie part of this item reduces to (3) behind LIB-PAR-MAP and (1)'s polling note for the kit team.")
inl note_of_gleam_rust_split_5 () = Note ("GLEAM-RUST-SPLIT", 5u32, "2026-10-26T13:03Z", "tibia-lane", "13:03Z 12:5xZ Part 4 set up (the rule of note 3 is unchanged). What I found: par'.map's Rust arm (LIB-PAR-RUST) needs closures that capture nothing (a compiler error otherwise), and Spiral's Rust values are Rc (not Sync), so camps and tactics can't cross threads. The sweep therefore maps plain seed indices, and each worker builds its row from top-level functions only. Scratch sweep-rust: sweep_rows (hunt_row i: simulate (arena_hunt, mirroring, respawning 60000) with cavebot_with_heal 160 on held-out seed first+i, giving index, kills, hits_taken, damage_taken, potions), sweep_par (par'.map, Rust) and sweep_seq (listm.map, C). Same rows module, so only the map and the backend differ. Compiling hunting_pack to Rust took over 520 s CPU and 4.5 GB here, so it moved to the remote kit worker node: job tibia-sweep-rust, chained after my last node gate to avoid stacking memory. Its rule, pre-registered now: the rows must be byte-identical (sequential C against parallel Rust, same 100 held-out seeds), and Rust is adopted for sweeps iff identical and par/seq wall time <= 1/3. Added before any result: the timing counts only if CPU load before both runs is under 50%. At 100% load the ratio is VOID, the identity verdict still stands, and the timing reruns in a quiet window.")
inl note_of_gleam_rust_split_6 () = Note ("GLEAM-RUST-SPLIT", 6u32, "2026-10-26T18:42Z", "tibia-lane", "18:42Z Part 4 measurement (tibia-sweep-rust on the node) is VOID by its pre-registered rule: it never produced two runnable builds. The seq arm compiled to C (710 s). The par arm compiled to Rust (2790 s) but rustc failed with E0277 'Rc<str> cannot be sent between threads safely': hunt_row returns a formatted string, and Spiral's Rust strings are Rc<str> (not Send), so par'.map cannot move them across rayon workers. Then the job hit its 5.4 h cap, which counts queue wait. Rule for the retry, written before rerunning: workers return plain numeric tuples (kills, hits, damage, potions as i32), and the main thread formats the rows after the join; this matches LIB-PAR-RUST's capture-free closure constraint. Acceptance unchanged: rows identical to the C seq build, and par/seq <= 1/3 counts only at CPU load under 50%. The 2790 s Rust compile of hunting_pack is the bigger practical cost and is noted for BACKEND-BALANCE.")
inl note_of_gleam_rust_split_7 () = Note ("GLEAM-RUST-SPLIT", 7u32, "2026-10-26T21:46Z", "tibia-lane", "21:46Z Part 4 retry running on the remote kit worker node (job-1793051191332, tibia-sweep-rust2), built to the retry rule written at 18:42Z. hunt_row now returns hunt_tally = i32 * i32 * i32 * i32 * i32 (index, kills, hits_taken, damage_taken value, potions: all i32, so Send), and row_text formats after the join on the main thread. Both arms share the rows module, so only the map differs: par'.map for Rust, listm.map for C. The lib snapshot is from 17:42 local with LIB-PAR-GLEAM and LIB-PAR-RUST applied. Node load at start: cpu 54%, 10.6 GB free. Acceptance is unchanged: rows identical to the C seq build, and par/seq <= 1/3 counts only when CPU load is under 50%. The script prints the load before each run.")
inl note_of_gleam_rust_split_8 () = Note ("GLEAM-RUST-SPLIT", 8u32, "2026-10-26T23:50Z", "tibia-lane", "23:50Z Part 4 retry 2 result (node job-1793051191332). Both arms built for the first time: Rust par in 772 s plus rustc; C seq emitted in 336 s, then gcc -O2 needed about 2,600 CPU-s at 100% node load (a BACKEND-BALANCE cost).\n- seq (C): 100 rows in 3.2 s.\n- par (Rust): exit -1073741571 = 0xC00000FD STATUS_STACK_OVERFLOW after 16 rows, 0.5 s.\nVerdict by the rule: not adopted (rows not identical). The 0.158 ratio means nothing with a crash at 100% load.\nCause: LIB-PAR-RUST's spiral_scope_map spawns workers with scope.spawn, i.e. Rust's default 2 MiB thread stack, and hunting_pack's simulate recurses deeper than that. The numeric-tuple fix did remove the Send error.\nRule for retry 3, written before any rerun:\n- Workers spawn through std::thread::Builder::new().stack_size(64 MiB).spawn_scoped(scope, ...). This is a follow-up slice on LIB-PAR-RUST after it lands from serial-gaps-lib4, proven by a par' test whose worker recurses past 2 MiB.\n- If 64 MiB still overflows, raise once to 256 MiB, then stop and report.\n- Acceptance unchanged: rows identical to the C seq build, and par/seq <= 1/3 counts only when node load is under 50% (the script prints the load).\n- Noted: the seq rows print in descending index order (99, 98, ...); both arms share the rows module, so identity still compares like with like.")
inl note_of_gleam_rust_split_9 () = Note ("GLEAM-RUST-SPLIT", 9u32, "2026-10-27T00:55Z", "tibia-lane", "00:55Z Retry 3 started on the node (job-1793062222773, tibia-sweep-rust3), built per the 23:50Z rule: setup3 = setup2 plus LIB-PAR-RUST-STACK, so workers spawn with stack_size(64 << 20). The bundle check confirmed stack_size in par'.spi and hunt_tally rows. Node load at start: 100% (other lanes). By the rule this run decides only row identity. If rows match, the built exes stay in C:\\home\\remote-stage\\tibia-sweep-rust3, and the timing half reruns those binaries alone once node load is under 50% (seconds; no rebuild).")
inl note_of_gleam_rust_split_10 () = Note ("GLEAM-RUST-SPLIT", 10u32, "2026-10-27T02:44Z", "tibia-lane", "02:44Z Retry 3 result (node job-1793062222773).\n- Build: Rust par in 1592 s plus rustc; C seq emitted in 336 s plus gcc.\n- Runs: both exit 0, 100 rows each. Rows IDENTICAL (0 differing lines).\n- With 64 MiB worker stacks (LIB-PAR-RUST-STACK) the stack overflow is gone. That settles the identity half of the acceptance: par'.map on Rust gives the same rows as the C seq build.\n- Timing half: VOID for this run by the rule (100% node load before both runs; the observed ratio of 1.453 means nothing under load).\n- The load-gated rerun is queued as node job-1793069128718. It waits for 3 consecutive one-minute samples under 50%, then reruns the built exes (seconds) and reports the ratio; adopt iff <= 0.333. It gives up after 6 h and reports VOID.")
inl note_of_gleam_rust_split_11 () = Note ("GLEAM-RUST-SPLIT", 11u32, "2026-10-27T05:55Z", "tibia-lane", "05:55Z Part 4 verdict (node job-1793069128718; load-gated, ran at 01:56 local with load 15-27%):\n- seq (C) 100 rows in 0.76 s; par (Rust, par'.map over 8 workers with 64 MiB stacks) 100 rows in 1.17 s. Rows identical.\n- Ratio par/seq 1.554, against the bar of <= 0.333: NOT ADOPTED by the pre-registered rule.\nReading, not yet a finding:\n- If all 8 workers were busy, Rust's per-hunt CPU is about 1.17 x 8 / 100 = 94 ms, against 7.6 ms per hunt in C. That suggests Spiral's Rust output for hunting_pack (Rc-boxed values) runs roughly 12x slower than its C output, which parallelism can't recover.\n- It could also be thread or Rc-clone overhead.\nRule for the discriminating measurement, written before running it: build sweep_seq once more for Rust (listm.map, same rows module) and time it single-threaded at load under 50%.\n- If Rust seq / C seq >= 3, codegen dominates. BACKEND-BALANCE records that batch sweeps over hunting_pack stay on C (scaled by OS processes), not Rust threads, until the Rust codegen gap closes.\n- If it is < 3, the thread path is the cost, and par'.map's chunking (chunk size, per-worker closure factory) gets profiled next.")
inl note_of_gleam_rust_split_12 () = Note ("GLEAM-RUST-SPLIT", 12u32, "2026-10-27T06:47Z", "tibia-lane", "06:47Z Discriminating measurement (node job-1793080696010; load 30-45%, under the 50% rule), same rows module, 100 hunts:\n- C seq: 1.05 s.\n- Rust seq (listm.map): 6.09 s. Rows identical.\n- Ratio Rust seq / C seq = 5.8, past the >= 3 threshold, so codegen dominates by the rule written at 05:55Z.\n- Threads aren't the problem: Rust par'.map on 8 workers (1.17 s, retry 3) is 5.2x faster than Rust seq, so LIB-PAR-RUST plus the 64 MiB stacks scale well.\n- The gap is Spiral's Rust output for hunting_pack, about 5.8x slower per hunt than its C output (Rc-boxed values and clones are the likely suspects; not profiled yet).\nApplied by the rule: batch sweeps over hunting_pack stay on C, scaled by OS processes, until the Rust codegen gap closes.\nNext lever, recorded here and not started: profile one hunt in the Rust build (where the time goes: Rc clone/drop vs arithmetic). If closing the gap to under 3x is feasible, the Rust par path (5.2x thread scaling) would beat C seq.")
inl note_of_gleam_rust_split_13 () = Note ("GLEAM-RUST-SPLIT", 13u32, "2026-10-30T11:24Z", "tibia-lane", "10-30 07:5x tibia groom (R3): tibia's part is complete (the 10-27 discriminating measurement, note 12). tibia parks after TIBIA-PARK, so it adds nothing more here. Next for the item: the Rust thread::scope granular rule (LIB-PAR-MAP part 4), owner unassigned; x and kit should state theirs.")
inl note_of_gleam_rust_split_14 () = Note ("GLEAM-RUST-SPLIT", 14u32, "2026-10-30T14:14Z", "main", "10-30 10:14 main, BACKLOG-DANGLING-1030 R3: the next step named in tibia's 07:5x note (the Rust thread::scope granular rule, LIB-PAR-MAP part 4) now has an owner: LIB-PAR-MAP is team main as of 10:13 and is resumed FIRST among main's paused items, since Active BACKEND-BALANCE depends on it.")
inl note_of_gleam_rust_split_15 () = Note ("GLEAM-RUST-SPLIT", 15u32, "2026-10-30T15:05Z", "x", "2026-10-30 11:1x x states its part (answers tibia's 07:5x note). GLEAM side: x's concurrency work is the SIM-IN-SPIRAL S3c async driver (s3c.cells_executor.spi: a BEAM process per run via erlang_process spawn_unlinked / receive_within, cancel-on-supersede, timed_out handling), which replaces the sequential sync_run_with that x-s3-p7b showed cannot overlap or cancel runs (cancels 0 vs 1, peak_inflight 1 vs 3 against the Elixir engine). Its discriminating measurement is the parity run x-s3c-2 (job-1793372043910, running): the pass line is 0 stable-field differences against Elixir on cells and memo, plus matching fake-leak behaviour. RUST side: the client crates (29, xc 960 the largest) have no runtime concurrency need; each command is one HTTP exchange or a local file walk. Rust parallelism matters to x only at BUILD time (X-CARGO-BUILD-PROFILE thin16: -23%/-45% release builds; X-PARALLEL-BUILD), not at run time. So x adds no thread::scope requirement to LIB-PAR-MAP part 4. x's part here closes when S3c parity passes and the s3cset lands (SIM-IN-SPIRAL n103).")
inl note_of_gleam_rust_split_16 () = Note ("GLEAM-RUST-SPLIT", 16u32, "2026-10-31T00:05Z", "x", "2026-10-30 20:0x x: x's part is DONE (n15's condition): the S3 slice landed in live x (SIM-IN-SPIRAL n117). The Spiral engine now runs executor calls in spawned BEAM processes with cancel-on-supersede and Elixir's harvest semantics, and is at parity with the Elixir engine (cells/memo 0 stable-field differences; paced fake/leak match). x adds no Rust runtime-concurrency requirement.")
inl note_of_gleam_rust_split_17 () = Note ("GLEAM-RUST-SPLIT", 17u32, "2026-10-31T11:28Z", "tibia-lane", "10-31 07:3x tibia (cleanup round): Active -> Paused at 55. Parts done: x (n16, the S3 executor at parity), tibia (TIBIA-BACKEND-MAP Done; C beats Rust 5.8x on simulation sweeps, so sweeps stay C plus OS processes, BACKEND-BALANCE n7), lib (LIB-PAR-MAP Done). The only part left is kit adopting Gleam/Rust where it pays, which is EOIE-BACKEND-MAP (Paused, 15). Resume condition: EOIE-BACKEND-MAP resumes.")
inl note_of_gleam_rust_split_18 () = Note ("GLEAM-RUST-SPLIT", 18u32, "2026-10-31T20:05Z", "compiler", "10-31 10-31 16:05 compiler (groom): raised P1 -> P0 and named the concurrency DESIGN item. User (10-31 15:4x): 'it'll be a challenge making a design that reuses hopac crossbeam and gleam's subject at the same time under the hood'. Scope: one Spiral concurrency interface (spawn, fill-once cell, request/reply channel, choice) lowered to Hopac on F#, crossbeam (+ rayon) on Rust and Subject/OTP on Gleam; HOPACSOTA-P7 (engine interface) is the compiler-side consumer. Today's p3defer bench (HOPACSOTA-P3 n60) is evidence that the transport between workers is the cost to design around. Gate for SELF-HOSTED-SPIRAL.")
inl note_of_gleam_rust_split_19 () = Note ("GLEAM-RUST-SPLIT", 19u32, "2026-11-03T09:33Z", "tibia", "11-03 05:3x tibia, EVIDENCE UPDATE for the tibia part (n-latest cites 'C beats Rust 5.8x on simulation sweeps'): measured again 11-03 on quiet vultr with an A/A control (TIBIA-RUN-METRICS n10): the same node-generated sweep sources, C 47.7 s vs Rust 126.4 s for 4000 hunts, i.e. C/Rust = 2.65x (A/A spread 3.3%). The decision stands (sweeps stay C plus OS processes, since C is still 2.65x ahead), but the Rust gap more than halved since 10-27, so revisit if COMPILER-RUST-ARRAY-HOT-LOOP lands its next step: at parity, Rust's rayon would make in-process parallel sweeps an option that C needs OpenMP plus atomic refcounts (pack19) for.")
