inl resource_reports () = Task ("RESOURCE-REPORTS", "Detailed day/night reports of what takes CPU and memory (per lane, per job, dangling processes) to iterate on what slows us down (user 2026-10-24 06:56 review list)", Story, P1, D4, 90u32, Planned, "", "processes,metrics,reporting,team:main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_resource_reports () = Planning ("RESOURCE-REPORTS", Minutes 240u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_resource_reports_1 () = Note ("RESOURCE-REPORTS", 1u32, "2026-10-30T14:57Z", "main", "10-30 10:57 main: FIRST SLICE, a read-only prototype: i574n.github/scripts/resource-report.ps1 [-Hours 24] [-Node] (new, unstaged). It needs no new data, since it parses 'kit job list' rows (the same format as the node's eoie.exe). (1) Jobs started in the window, by machine and team (the label prefix): count, wall hours, running, stopped, non-zero exits, max peak_private_mb. (2) The 10 longest jobs. (3) Box processes of at least 200 MB outside every live kit job's tree (walking parents up to each job's pid/supervisor), grouped by name: the dangling candidates. FIRST READING, 10:5x, 24 h: the node carries most of the wall time: fiscal 62.4 h over 119 jobs (peak 5.6 GB), x 40.6 h, compiler 30.8 h, tibia 5.1 h, kit 3.9 h; box kit 8.1 h, compiler 3.3 h, main 1.2 h. Longest: x-verify32 6.8 h (stopped), x-verify33 5.7 h, x-s3-p7b 5.3 h (exit 1). Wall time isn't CPU, and the CPU column needs per-job sampling the job records don't keep. Outside jobs on the box: 7 claude.exe (2.6 GB, the sessions) and canary.exe 1.4 GB (started 10:46, parent gone; tibia was asked, not touched). NEXT: (a) a CPU column, which needs kit to sample job-tree CPU into the job dir, so it's a kit verb (team kit, after TRAIN5; generic-tools-not-per-experiment); (b) a night/day split by start hour; (c) per-team failure triage links. Owner main for the script; kit for the sampling.")
inl note_of_resource_reports_2 () = Note ("RESOURCE-REPORTS", 2u32, "2026-10-30T16:34Z", "main", "10-30 12:34 main: input for n1's NEXT (a): the CPU column must NOT use the installed kit's cores. kit found that sysinfo 0.38.4's per-process cpu_usage reads lifetime averages on the box: a busy loop at 1.45 real cores reads 0.0, and a box at 7.9/8 cores reads 0.44 total (EOIE-MACHINE-HEALTH n9; memory sysinfo-cpu-usage-unreliable). kit's fix, the kit_machine_sample crate (CPU-time delta over the sample window; 1.47 against Windows' 1.46), is unstaged for TRAIN5. So the report's CPU column reads kit_machine_sample once TRAIN5 installs it; until then, Windows counters (Get-Counter '\\Process(*)\\% Processor Time' over a window, or a CPU-time delta from Get-Process .TotalProcessorTime) and never 'kit ps' or doctor cores.")
inl note_of_resource_reports_3 () = Note ("RESOURCE-REPORTS", 3u32, "2026-10-30T22:44Z", "main", "10-30 18:44 main: CPU column step DONE without waiting for kit's sampler: resource-report.ps1 gains -SampleSec (default 5) and a 'box CPU right now' section (per-process TotalProcessorTime delta over the window, grouped by name, as a percentage of all logical cores; Windows counters only, so the sysinfo lifetime-average trap doesn't apply). First reading, 18:4x: box 63.4% busy = rustc 27.7%, claude.exe x14 13.6%, pwsh x28 12.1%, the rest under 3%. FINDING: the 28 pwsh processes burn about 1 core, which is lane overhead (background waits, polling loops, hook runs), not work. NEXT: a per-pwsh breakdown by command line, to name which waits poll instead of blocking on kit job wait or window wait-clear; then a node run of the same section (-Node).")
inl note_of_resource_reports_4 () = Note ("RESOURCE-REPORTS", 4u32, "2026-10-30T22:44Z", "main", "10-30 18:44 main: CORRECTION to n3's finding, measured right after: the busy pwsh processes are Claude Code tool-call shells (command line starts with the CLAUDE_CODE script wrapper; 3 shells at 18.8%, 13.1% and 9.1% over 5 s), i.e. lanes' own active commands. The background waits (remote.ps1 node job wait, lib_gate, kit window wait-clear) read 0.0%. So it isn't polling overhead, and nothing needs fixing there; the report's next step is the node run (-Node).")
inl note_of_resource_reports_5 () = Note ("RESOURCE-REPORTS", 5u32, "2026-10-31T00:15Z", "main", "10-30 20:15 main: node run DONE (resource-report.ps1 -Node). 24 h, node: fiscal 125 jobs / 141.9 wall-h (25 non-zero exits, peak 5.6 GB), x 34 / 33.9 h, compiler 26 / 15.1 h, tibia 18 / 15.0 h, kit 3; box: compiler 40, fiscal 37, kit 62 (14 non-zero). Node now: CPU 58%, 8.5 GB free. Longest: fiscal-p7-e3lv-set-grptcr running 7.98 h with 83 B of stdout. Busy-but-silent, so I checked liveness: its tree's two rustc processes did +14.6 s and +46.9 s CPU in 15 s (about 4 cores), so it is ALIVE (a long Rust build), not hung. fiscal-p7-e3lv-set-brsigned has been QUEUED 7.96 h and set-signed2 36 min, each asking max_private_mb 9000 against 8.5 GB free, so the admission cap holds them. That is fiscal's queue design; no action taken (fiscal's processes). Report gaps found: 'f' and 'p' rows are labels without a team prefix (team = label up to the first '-'); and the box section lists 6 claude.exe (2.5 GB) as 'outside any job tree', which are sessions, not dangling processes; exclude claude/explorer next. Next step: those two fixes, then make it a scheduled day/night report.")
inl note_of_resource_reports_6 () = Note ("RESOURCE-REPORTS", 6u32, "2026-10-31T00:17Z", "main", "10-30 20:17 main: both n5 fixes are in scripts/resource-report.ps1 (i574n.github, unstaged), checked on a rerun. (1) -Teams (main, x, kit, eoie, fiscal, compiler, tibia, heal, healing): a label whose prefix is not a team groups as '(no team)', which replaces the 'f', 'p' and 'qjh' rows. (2) The dangling section skips claude.exe and its descendants (sessions and their tool shells). It now shows 6 dotnet.exe (2.5 GB) and taskhostw. Next: attribute dotnet.exe under detached Cells daemons to the daemon's owner (the daemon's 'status' verb returns 'os_pid N argv ...'), so they read as owned, not dangling; then schedule the day/night report. Seen: main has 0 kit jobs in 24 h (main runs its proofs as session background shells), so the per-team table under-counts main.")
inl note_of_resource_reports_7 () = Note ("RESOURCE-REPORTS", 7u32, "2026-10-31T00:17Z", "main", "10-30 20:17 main: attribution is in. For each process outside job trees and sessions, the report walks up to an ancestor listening on a Cells daemon port (13905-14999), asks it 'status', and prints the owner (the notebook's --path leaf, or the owner text). Rerun: the 6 dotnet.exe (2.5 GB) are 'cells:14661 edit' (3, 1.4 GB) and 'cells:14215 strict' (3, 1.1 GB), i.e. main's own LAMBDA-PARENS and strict-blast proofs. Nothing on the box is truly dangling (taskhostw is Windows). Next: schedule the day/night report (the kit schedule due/ran pattern, like profile-mirror-if-due).")
inl note_of_resource_reports_8 () = Note ("RESOURCE-REPORTS", 8u32, "2026-10-31T13:00Z", "main", "10-31 09:00 main: SCHEDULING READY, registration left to the user (persistent machine config). New i574n.github/scripts/resource-report-save.ps1 runs resource-report.ps1 -Hours 12 -Node and saves %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\reports\\resource-<yyyyMMdd-HHmm>.md (never under C:\\home\\data). register-scheduled-tasks.ps1 gained resource-report-morning (06:55) and resource-report-evening (18:55); -DryRun shows only those two as new, the other 3 unchanged. To enable: pwsh i574n.github/scripts/register-scheduled-tasks.ps1. First saved report: resource-20261031-0858.md (59 lines). Readings: kit-train5e timed out (exit 124) after 5.00 h; node x 21 jobs, 14 non-zero; box fiscal 13 jobs 14.4 wall-h; main shows 1 box job because main's proofs run as session shells, which the report excludes (known undercount).")
inl note_of_resource_reports_9 () = Note ("RESOURCE-REPORTS", 9u32, "2026-10-31T15:48Z", "main", "10-31 11:48 main: the known undercount is FIXED. resource-report.ps1 has a new section, 'Claude session tool shells by lane': each live process is walked up to its session's claude.exe, whose --resume names the lane (i574n = main, no --resume shown as such), and processes, working set and CPU seconds (user + kernel time from CIM) are totalled per lane. First reading at 11:4x: compiler 11 processes 372 MB 2,643 CPU-s, kit 20 / 1,426 MB / 330, fiscal 50 / 2,401 MB / 195, tibia 18 / 823 MB / 95, main 16 / 702 MB / 87, x 5 / 211 MB / 16; box 84.9% of all cores. Live processes only: finished session shells are not counted (that needs a sampler). Left: the user's registration of the two daily tasks.")
inl note_of_resource_reports_10 () = Note ("RESOURCE-REPORTS", 10u32, "2026-11-01T03:40Z", "main", "main 23:4x WIP groom (user 23:3x: drift/stall, too much waiting): Active -> Planned. undercount fixed (n9); scheduled-task registration is the user's call.")
inl note_of_resource_reports_11 () = Note ("RESOURCE-REPORTS", 11u32, "2026-11-02T14:02Z", "x", "2026-11-02 09:59 x: DECISION RECORD (typed; groom of user waits, user 11-02: 'decisions waiting on me get met autonomously/deterministically with tests/benchs/typefeatureset modeling/backups'). kind=ByDefault. QUESTION: Should the day/night resource report run as a new scheduled task? RULE (written before acting): Default: do not register a new scheduled task. resource-report.ps1 runs on demand at each groom round and session start (the existing cadence), writing a timestamped report under %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit. This is safe because it changes nothing on the machine and is fully reversible. A scheduled registration line goes into register-scheduled-tasks.ps1 only as a commented candidate, listed with SCHEDULED-TASKS-CONFIRM's veto batch. BACKUP/UNDO: Nothing machine-level changes. The registrar edit is left unstaged in i574n.github for the user's review. OWNER: main. NEXT: Wire resource-report.ps1 into the groom-round cadence, record the first day and night readings, and close at 100% once two readings name the top CPU and memory consumers per lane. VETO: the user can reverse this at any time; the backup above is the undo.")
