inl edition3_test_symbol () = Task ("EDITION3-TEST-SYMBOL", "Edition-3 symbol for test headers: :# (the scheme puts : before every top-level form; echoes Rust #[test]; 0 uses of :# in spiral, x and tibia; # is an operator char, so :# lexes as one operator token). The lexer maps :# to TokVar (\"test\", variable), so blockParsingIsTest, the header check and the test modes work unchanged. Rollout: (1) 5j lexer arm (test-symbol-lexer.ps1, staged); (2) Cells reads and writes :# headers keeping each header's spelling across the .livemd round trip (document.spi 374/533/574, memo.ex @test_header, accept.ex header regex, document_test.exs ^test regex) and tree-sitter recognizes :#; (3) only then format --spelling writes :# for column-0 test headers (convertLine), and a mechanical pass converts every repo", Story, P0, D4, 100u32, Done, "", "edition3,cells,tree-sitter,team:compiler,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_edition3_test_symbol () = Planning ("EDITION3-TEST-SYMBOL", Minutes 300u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_edition3_test_symbol_1 () = Note ("EDITION3-TEST-SYMBOL", 1u32, "2026-10-25T06:34Z", "main", "2026-10-25 02:34 main: stage 1 shipped (5j installed: :# lexes as TokVar test, checked with the installed tokens command). Stage 2, the tree-sitter half: done in the tree, uncommitted for the user. apps/zed/tree-sitter-spiral/grammar.js test_keyword is now /(test|:#)[ \\t]+/, regenerated with tree-sitter 0.27.1, and the new corpus test test/corpus/test_symbol.txt parses a :# header and a test header to the same tree; tree-sitter test passes 6/6, and the HEAD grammar has no :# arm. The Zed extension needs the user's commit plus an extension.toml rev bump. Left in stage 2: the Cells half (document.spi 374/533/574, memo.ex @test_header, accept.ex header regex, document_test.exs), including keeping each header's spelling through the .livemd round trip. Then stage 3: convertLine writes :# and one mechanical pass runs over every repo.")
inl note_of_edition3_test_symbol_2 () = Note ("EDITION3-TEST-SYMBOL", 2u32, "2026-10-25T06:38Z", "main", "2026-10-25 02:38 main: stage 2's Cells half DRAFTED: %TEMP%\\cells-test-symbol\\apply.ps1, 10 anchored edits, dry run 10/10. The spelling rides the test cell's own ///- options line as --symbol, a no-op flag, so the .livemd round trip keeps it with no new wire format. header_of accepts \":# \" and adds a \" symbol\" pseudo-mode; test_options turns that into --symbol; test_block_go writes \":# \" back when the options carry --symbol; test_like and malformed_name know :#; directives.spi passes --symbol through (its parser rejects unknown flags); memo.ex @test_header and accept.ex's header regex accept :#; DocumentTest counts :# headers. Apply together with option B (%TEMP%\\cells-document-tight) once no Cells run reads the tree. Then add one DocumentTest case (a :# file round-trips byte for byte), run the Cells suite, and rerun the lib-trip-all scan. After that, stage 3: convertLine writes :#.")
inl note_of_edition3_test_symbol_3 () = Note ("EDITION3-TEST-SYMBOL", 3u32, "2026-10-25T07:24Z", "main", "10-25 03:24 main: stage 3 drafted for pack 5l: %TEMP%\\pack-next5l\\test-header-symbol.ps1, dry run anchored 1/1 on the 5k core. convertLine replaces 5j's Unchanged arm. On a column-0 'test' or ':#' line, only lexeme 0 is respelled (headerSpelling: Symbols test->:#, Words :#->test), and the name and modes stay as written, so 'test forall =' keeps its name. keepsTokens still guards it: ':#' lexes as TokVar test, the same token as the word. Next: compose 5l = 5k core + this + host-no-parts + LUA-FUNCTION-LIMITS once 5k installs; then a mechanical format --spelling symbols pass over the repos (ask peer lanes about pending anchored chains first).")
inl note_of_edition3_test_symbol_4 () = Note ("EDITION3-TEST-SYMBOL", 4u32, "2026-10-25T14:23Z", "main", "10-25 10:23 main: format --spelling gap reported by kit (eoie respell dry run on 5l'): eoie/src/eoie_tree_copy/terminal.spi line 3 'inl emit_runtime () : () = rust_global \"\"\"' keeps 'inl' in symbols mode while line 1's 'inl rust_global' becomes ':='. Probable cause, from reading convertLine on the 5k core: keepsTokens re-lexes the attempted line alone with lexLine lex_state_init. A line that ends by OPENING a multi-line string (triple quote continuing on later lines) lexes differently in isolation than in its file, so the respelled line is Rejected and kept verbatim. The round trip holds, so it's cosmetic. Fix for a later pack: compare tokens only up to the first lexeme that starts a string still open at end of line, or relex with the line's actual start state. Positive control: that line respelled, plus a one-line triple-quoted string that still respells today.")
inl note_of_edition3_test_symbol_5 () = Note ("EDITION3-TEST-SYMBOL", 5u32, "2026-10-30T01:59Z", "main", "10-29 21:59 main, groom (WIP rule, as in the 10-23 and 10-27 grooms): Active -> Paused, progress kept at 70%. No note since 10-25 and no live job; main carries 69 Actives, which is not real WIP. Resume when picked; if its pass already shipped in an installed core, close it Done with that evidence instead. Compiler/lib work: per lane-roles, the team that needs it (likely the compiler team, git-a6) resumes it.")
inl note_of_edition3_test_symbol_6 () = Note ("EDITION3-TEST-SYMBOL", 6u32, "2026-11-01T12:03Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 08:02 compiler, pre-influx groom: compiler + Cells halves are SHIPPED. The installed formatter respells test <-> :# (FormatterConvert.headerSpelling, spiral_compiler.fs ~30050, and the ':#' token at ~1275), and Cells' parser accepts both (main, 11-01 respell-trap sweep, which also fixed the '^test ' filters in apps/cells/spi and compiler scripts). Left: the tree-sitter grammar (tag tree-sitter). Close when that lands, or split it off.")
inl note_of_edition3_test_symbol_7 () = Note ("EDITION3-TEST-SYMBOL", 7u32, "2026-11-01T14:39Z", "compiler", "11-01 10:38 compiler, R5: the destructive respell of compiler samples/tests to :: and :# is filed as EDITION3-SAMPLES-RESPELL (trap checks: keepsTokens, predict P = 0 on the matrix, diff classifier Semantic = 0). This item stays for the tree-sitter half only.")
inl note_of_edition3_test_symbol_8 () = Note ("EDITION3-TEST-SYMBOL", 8u32, "2026-11-02T14:07Z", "x", "2026-11-02 10:07 x: GIT CONDITION RE-CHECKED from the tree (groom of user waits). A read-only survey at 09:4x shows every active repo 0 commits ahead of origin: the user committed and pushed alphabet, blog, dice, dir-tree-html, kit, fiscal, html, polyglot, regex, spiral, tibia and x in the last 1-3 h. i574n.github.io is the exception (3 unstaged files, last commit 2 years ago). So this item's user step is probably MET. What is left is deterministic and belongs to the owner, not the user: confirm the step's files are in HEAD (git log -- <paths>) and that CI on HEAD is green (gh run list --commit <HEAD>), then close it. Only a red or missing file sends it back to the user queue. Pages enablement (HTML, REGEX-BENCH-PAGE) is a real user step, since it is a public repo setting.")
inl note_of_edition3_test_symbol_9 () = Note ("EDITION3-TEST-SYMBOL", 9u32, "2026-11-04T11:59Z", "compiler", "11-04 08:0x compiler: CLOSE-OUT CHECK (x's n8 owner step). (1) The tree-sitter half is in HEAD: grammar.js test_keyword /(test|:#)[ \\t]+/ and test/corpus/test_symbol.txt, both since d095f13d (10-25). (2) CI on spiral HEAD 98e51d32 (i574n/spiral, = github-i574n/main per ls-remote): gh-pages success x2. (3) One gap found: apps/zed/extension.toml still pinned the grammar at rev 3c76d3ed (10-14), whose grammar has no :# arm, so the Zed extension would not highlight :# headers. Bumped rev -> 98e51d32 (pushed, contains the :# arm), unstaged in the tree. Compiler and Cells halves shipped earlier (n6), and the respell pass is EDITION3-SAMPLES-RESPELL. Closing: done but for the one-line rev bump, which rides the user's next commit.")
inl lib_generated_cost () = Task ("LIB-GENERATED-COST", "Shrink the Rust that two generic lib helpers generate per crate: file_system.file_delete's traced error path and union_reflection's per-case closures", Story, P2, D4, 40u32, Planned, "", "lib,rust,crates,kit,team:kit", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_lib_generated_cost () = Planning ("LIB-GENERATED-COST", Minutes 240u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_lib_generated_cost_1 () = Note ("LIB-GENERATED-COST", 1u32, "2026-10-25T05:59Z", "eoie", "01:59 Measured while hand-splitting eoie crates (EOIE-HAND-CRATE-UNITS). (1) file_system.file_delete: on Rust, the Error arm calls trace Critical, which pulls in the trace state machinery. One call cost about 200 generated lines in eoie_job_host (held_released 117 + new_trace_state 87); a one-call std::fs::remove_file primitive removed them. (2) union_reflection.cases.union_case_where and union_cases_fold build one closure per union case: dropping one small union_case_where lookup saved 46 lines in eoie_ci_probe, and union_cases_fold over the 25 agile subcommands costs 202 lines (agile_subcommand_of) plus 347 (agile_subcommand_shape) in every crate that inlines them. Rule, written before changing anything: keep the APIs and their meaning (one record per fact stays: the union is the table); a change lands only if the lib tests and Cells pass and the generated size of a probe crate drops by at least 25% for that helper, measured on the same compiler. Options to measure: file_delete returns or ignores the error without a trace on Rust (the caller decides); union reflection generates a single match or if-chain instead of nested closures.")
inl note_of_lib_generated_cost_2 () = Note ("LIB-GENERATED-COST", 2u32, "2026-10-25T06:15Z", "main", "2026-10-25 02:15 main: (1) file_delete MEASURED by the item's rule on a one-call probe (main calls file_system.file_delete once; installed 5j, Rust): live lib 517 generated lines with 13 trace mentions; patched 78 lines with 0, an 85% drop against the 25% bar. The patch: the Rust Error arm uses console.write_error_line (sm'.(++#) \"file_system.file_delete: \" error') instead of trace Critical. console comes before file_system in lib's module order. The other backends already report nothing (Zig catch {}, Gleam, Lua). The binary was checked: a missing file deletes nothing and exits 0; a file locked with no sharing prints \"file_system.file_delete: Os { code: 32 ... }\" to stderr and exits 0, as before (non-fatal). The slice is %TEMP%\\lib-file-delete-cheap\\apply.ps1 (anchor 1/1), gated by the lib kit on file_system.spi, then landed, as job-1792908815992. (2) union_reflection's per-case closures are next, by the same rule.")
inl note_of_lib_generated_cost_3 () = Note ("LIB-GENERATED-COST", 3u32, "2026-10-25T06:23Z", "main", "2026-10-25 02:23 main: (2) union_reflection probe (installed 5j, Rust; a 25-case union plus a spec function returning (name, shape), in eoie's agile_subcommand_shape form). Variant A, union_cases_fold with the step inlined per case: 335 generated lines, and the binary prints the right shape. Variant B, cases folded into a list at compile time, made runtime with dyn, then listm'.try_find: EJP0040 (more than 1,000 nested dynamic ifs). try_find is a recursive inl, so it still unrolls over a dyn list; B needs a real runtime loop (a let rec or join point over the list, or an array with one indexed loop) so that spec compiles to one 25-arm match instead of 25 inlined copies. Next: write that variant and measure it against A's 335 by the 25% rule. Probes are in %TEMP%\\lib-generated-cost\\union-*.")
inl note_of_lib_generated_cost_4 () = Note ("LIB-GENERATED-COST", 4u32, "2026-10-25T06:26Z", "main", "2026-10-25 02:26 main: (2) MEASURED, union lookup on 25 cases (installed 5j, Rust, the binary prints the right shape): union_cases_fold with the step inlined per case: 335 lines. Cases folded once into a list, made runtime with dyn, searched by a let rec loop (spec becomes one runtime match): 248 lines, a 26% drop, so it clears the 25% bar. The listm'.try_find variant hits EJP0040, because the recursive inl unrolls even over a dyn list. The helper this points to: lib reflection gets union_cases_find (a runtime loop over the dyn case list, written with list sugar per memory list-sugar-not-cons: | v :: rest =>, never Cons). Callers like eoie's agile_subcommand_of and agile_subcommand_shape switch to it. Expected gain per crate is about 26% of those helpers' 202 + 347 lines. Not landed yet; the next step is the helper plus a lib test, gated by the kit.")
inl note_of_lib_generated_cost_5 () = Note ("LIB-GENERATED-COST", 5u32, "2026-10-25T07:15Z", "eoie", "03:15 eoie measurement for the union_cases_find design, on 5k with baseline and trial in one job: making agile_subcommand_shape go through agile_subcommand_of (union-returning fold plus a match), instead of its own string fold, moved eoie_agile_routes from 692 to 816 (+124). No other consumer of agile_subcommand_domain changed (agile_task_query 685, command_spec_domain 693, eoie_agile_mutation 723, eoie_command_help 865). Reverted under the prewritten rule. Read: per-case unrolling costs more when the fold's accumulator is a union than when it is a string, so a find helper returning option t may pay that cost per call site. Measure union_cases_find on routes (shape only) as well as on the of form before switching both.")
inl note_of_lib_generated_cost_6 () = Note ("LIB-GENERATED-COST", 6u32, "2026-10-25T07:40Z", "main", "10-25 03:40 main: both slices from the cost probes LANDED in the tree lib, uncommitted. file_system.file_delete's Rust error arm now prints one stderr line (one-call probe: 517 -> 78 generated Rust lines). Gate job-1792908815992 GREEN: file_system.spi 19/22, the same 3 reds as the unchanged tree lib. reflection.union_cases_find (fold-backed): gate job-1792909796496 GREEN 10/10. Its first land hit a transient 'file in use' on reflection.spi; the retry, job-1792913813496, landed. eoie told.")
inl note_of_lib_generated_cost_7 () = Note ("LIB-GENERATED-COST", 7u32, "2026-10-25T22:52Z", "eoie", "18:52 kit: the lib batch for S1 owner-parent (scratch lib-gaps-3\\lib-batch.do: env.command_line_arguments_from plus env.process_id, sm'.char_code/of_char, console.read_to_end, file_system.try_append) still applies to the live lib after the day's lib lands by other lanes. do-dry on copies, root lib\\spiral: ok, 4 files, 5 replacements (env.spi lines 205 and 241, sm'.spi 1063, console.spi 116, file_system.spi 1645). It lands through the lib gate right after the rename, then S1 owner-parent builds on it (EOIE-JOB-OWNER-PARENT).")
inl note_of_lib_generated_cost_8 () = Note ("LIB-GENERATED-COST", 8u32, "2026-10-30T23:42Z", "main", "10-30 19:4x main R3 groom (BACKLOG-DANGLING-1030): owner kit (its last notes are kit's lib batch for S1 owner-parent; tag kit added 19:4x). Next step per that note: land the 4-file batch (env/sm'/console/file_system) in a lib window with the standard close.")
inl note_of_lib_generated_cost_9 () = Note ("LIB-GENERATED-COST", 9u32, "2026-11-01T03:36Z", "kit", "10-31 23:4x kit groom (main 23:33, user: cadence/drift): Active -> PLANNED. No note in over 12 h, so nobody is working it this week; Planned keeps it in agile next at its priority (never auto-Paused, R3). First step when picked = the next step in its latest note.")
inl x_compat_vs_postable () = Task ("X-COMPAT-VS-POSTABLE", "x: x-compat (compiler --x-compat) and the build's postable gate (xc post-thread --dry-run) disagree. On the 17 staged sim modules x-compat says 0 findings while the dry run spills 121 cells (e.g. tree_memory sample_of: weight 278 + 15 over 2 posts). Find which counts what (cell boundaries, defused dots, record-brace spaces, header budget) and make them one rule.", TaskKind, P2, D2, 100u32, Done, "X-WIP-VERIFY", "x,x-compat,postable,compiler", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_x_compat_vs_postable () = Planning ("X-COMPAT-VS-POSTABLE", Minutes 60u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_x_compat_vs_postable_1 () = Note ("X-COMPAT-VS-POSTABLE", 1u32, "2026-10-25T06:20Z", "x", "10-25 02:20 x: found while staging the sim modules (SIM-IN-SPIRAL). On x's own respell-with-formatter tree the two checks reported the same count (28/28), so the gap is specific to something these modules contain. Start from one cell, tree_memory.spi sample_of in %TEMP%\\x-simland-0214\\stage: compare x-compat's weight for it with the dry run's 278+15.")
inl note_of_x_compat_vs_postable_2 () = Note ("X-COMPAT-VS-POSTABLE", 2u32, "2026-10-25T08:11Z", "x", "2026-10-25 04:11 -04:00 x: root cause. The two checks don't count differently. The compiler's --x-compat finds its profile by walking up to the nearest postability/*.json (or SPIRAL_POSTABILITY_PROFILE). A file with no profile was skipped silently but still counted in 'N file(s)'. The staged sim modules live in %TEMP%\\x-simland-0214, with no x/postability above them, so their run reported '0 finding(s)'. Proof: the same 300-a probe p.spi gives 0 findings in %TEMP% and 1 under C:\\home\\git\\x\\target. Cells' Elixir copy (Spiral.Cells.XCompat.profile_for) falls back to a built-in default_profile, so the two implementations also disagree there. Fix (host Program.fs runXCompatReport, unstaged): each file without a profile prints '<file>: no postability profile (...)', the summary adds ', N unchecked without a profile', and the exit code is 2. No fallback profile in the compiler, because the profile is x's data. Proof job: job-1792915766485-x-compat-unprofiled, a private Debug build plus both probes. Once that binary is installed, x build.ps1's x-compat-probe guard is dead code (it only caught this silent skip) and goes.")
inl note_of_x_compat_vs_postable_3 () = Note ("X-COMPAT-VS-POSTABLE", 3u32, "2026-10-25T08:22Z", "x", "2026-10-25 04:22 -04:00 x: the one rule, written before measuring. x-compat weighs the text the poster sends, as splitter.spi builds it: each dot with a label char before and a letter after becomes ZWSP+dot (extra_weight 1), and the poster detects no links. So in x-compat a domain match is a link (link_weight, an autolink finding) only when one of its dots stays undefused. With x's profile every domain dot is defusable, so posted x code never autolinks: the 'TLD-like names' renames (x-compat-naming) were extra work for a link that defusing already prevents. Before this, 'optionm.map' weighed 23+1 in x-compat and 11+1 on the poster. Gate: (1) a probe 'optionm.map' gives 0 autolink findings; (2) for every .spi in x/apps/client and the 17 staged sim modules, x-compat's overweight set equals the dry run's spilled cells (xc post-thread --dry-run), compared cell by cell; (3) the 300-a probe is still flagged.")
inl note_of_x_compat_vs_postable_4 () = Note ("X-COMPAT-VS-POSTABLE", 4u32, "2026-10-25T09:27Z", "x", "2026-10-25 05:26 -04:00 x: gate run job-1792916934610-x-compat-posted-rule (baseline = host fix only; candidate = posted-link rule). Gate 1: the 'optionm.map' probe gave 1 autolink before and 0 after. Gate 3: the 300-a probe is flagged in both. Gate 2 (overweight cells vs the dry run's spills, 61 files): 3 mismatches in both runs, all from cell NUMBERING (x-compat c14,c15 vs dry run c4,c5). Cause, the third half of the rule: x-compat began a block at every column-0 line (each ///> directive and each :< open was its own cell), while the poster (splitter.spi file_cells) starts a cell at an unindented line after at least one blank line, keeps blanks beyond the first at the cell head, and trims the end. The cell count feeds the header weight and groups lines differently, so a check could pass in one and spill in the other. Fix (core XCompat xCompatTopLevelBlocks, unstaged): the same state machine as file_cells. Remaining known difference: the poster NFC-normalizes before weighing and x-compat doesn't (ASCII is the same). Gate rerun: job-1792920150483-x-compat-cell-rule (the same 3 gates; want 0 mismatches).")
inl note_of_x_compat_vs_postable_5 () = Note ("X-COMPAT-VS-POSTABLE", 5u32, "2026-10-25T09:43Z", "x", "2026-10-25 05:43 -04:00 x: ONE RULE, gate met (job-1792920150483-x-compat-cell-rule, a private Debug build of the tree core with the host fix, posted-link rule and file_cells boundaries). Gate 1: optionm.map probe 0 findings. Gate 3: the 300-a probe flagged. Gate 2: 61 files (x/apps/client + the 17 staged sim modules), overweight cell set == dry-run spill set for every file, 0 mismatches. Left: (a) ship it in the next compiler pack (x's, after main's pack5k install); (b) after install, delete x build.ps1's x-compat-probe guard (dead: it only caught the silent no-profile skip, which is now exit 2) and add a build line running x-compat with the profile on apps/client; (c) Cells' Elixir copy gets the same defusing rule when a profile has posting.defused_dot (patch drafted, applied after the Cells job ends; the default profile keeps flagging links).")
inl note_of_x_compat_vs_postable_6 () = Note ("X-COMPAT-VS-POSTABLE", 6u32, "2026-10-25T16:10Z", "x", "2026-10-25 12:10 -04:00 x: field evidence for this item. On x's 17 staged sim modules (SIM-IN-SPIRAL n67) the installed compiler's --x-compat gives 18 autolink findings, and a candidate built from the tree core gives 0 (same files, SPIRAL_POSTABILITY_PROFILE = x/postability/x.json). All 18 are identifier dots (otp_actor.call, optionm.map, ...) that splitter.spi defuses. So the tree's x-compat change is what unblocks the sim landing, and getting it installed now gates more than x-compat hygiene.")
inl note_of_x_compat_vs_postable_7 () = Note ("X-COMPAT-VS-POSTABLE", 7u32, "2026-10-25T17:22Z", "x", "2026-10-25 13:22 -04:00 x: user question 10-25 (how far from running the splitter on every code file, so e2e runs outside GitHub Actions and off paid X, e.g. a local Bluesky/Mastodon). MEASURED with the release xc (post-thread --dry-run) over every 8th tracked .spi per repo (scratchpad split-census.ps1/.tsv). Cells over one post: x/apps 0 of 352 (6/6 files clean); dice 8/35 (22.9%); tibia 41/166 (24.7%); spiral/lib 147/399 (36.8%); eoie/src 390/973 (40.1%); 0 nonzero exits. Worst files: lib xsd_emit 96/198, eoie command_spec_domain/model 20/59, eoie_standards/rules 16/34. Cost: median 925 ms per file (one process each; process start dominates, X-BACKEND-MAP n4), so ~2,400 .spi files take ~37 min serially; the drafted batch form (X-BACKEND-MAP n5, x-precommit-next apply_batch_dry_run.ps1) would make it one process. Other languages: xc runs on any text, but file_cells is Spiral's top-level-block rule, so .rs/.ex/.fs come out as 1-4 giant cells (cells.rs 1 cell = 59 posts; Program.fs 4 cells = 296 posts). Platforms: xc and e2e/stub.py speak only X API v2, and the only profile is postability/x.json. Facts for the other two, checked 10-25: Mastodon's default limit is 500 characters and every link counts as 23 (docs.joinmastodon.org/user/posting). Bluesky's cap is 300 graphemes plus 3,000 bytes, and whether link text counts is disputed by third-party sources, so a test post against a local PDS settles it. GoToSocial is a single binary on SQLite but documented for Linux only, so on this box it needs WSL, which is held until the workflow passes. The existing item is X-FEDERATION-BRIDGES (P4, Paused under the deferred X-ON-NEAR).")
inl note_of_x_compat_vs_postable_8 () = Note ("X-COMPAT-VS-POSTABLE", 8u32, "2026-10-25T18:20Z", "x", "2026-10-25 14:20 -04:00 x: SHIPPED in 5l'' (installed 14:13, MVID 7fd00bff). Installed x-compat: 0 findings on x/apps/client (44 files) and on the 17 staged sim modules (18 on 5k); the no-profile host exits 2 with 'N unchecked without a profile'; a 300-weight block with the profile is 1 overweight finding. Follow-ups done in x (unstaged): apps/client/build.ps1 drops the 300-weight probe guard and fails the build on a nonzero x-compat exit instead, so an unprofiled run can't pass as 0. The new Get-XCompatFindings is tested from the file's own AST: client 0, no-profile dir throws, overweight-with-profile 1. Left: the Cells defused patch (job-1792944559767) and a green x verify.")
inl note_of_x_compat_vs_postable_9 () = Note ("X-COMPAT-VS-POSTABLE", 9u32, "2026-10-26T08:46Z", "x", "2026-10-26 01:5x x: the Cells defused patch never landed: main's job-1792944559767 (X-CELLS-UNICODE gate + land, which runs land_cells_lib.ps1) was stopped while waiting (exit 143). Rehearsed on copies of today's files (5 of 5 change, -Undo restores hashes) and re-queued as job-1792993843563-x-serial-x-cells-unicode (gate on unicode.spi, then land.ps1 with its reader guard and x_compat_test). Then: x verify on the real tree closes this item.")
inl note_of_x_compat_vs_postable_10 () = Note ("X-COMPAT-VS-POSTABLE", 10u32, "2026-10-26T08:46Z", "x", "2026-10-26 03:35 x: node verify11 (job-1792997006033, restored runner): client e2e 36/36 + 5 extras and postable 1,325 posts / 35 files / 0 over one post, all green; the client build's only red is protocol.spi's 5 zig tests (headers_* etc.), the known lib Zig arm that main's queued main-serial-zig-index-of-3 fixes. The sim build stopped on a stale fixture: sim-stress is generated from posts.spi's own text, which changed with the (~!) p edit. Reviewed (the diff is exactly that line plus the cell boundaries it shifts; sim-small byte-identical) and accepted sim-stress.world.json + .expected.json into apps/client/e2e. One more verify after main's zig fix closes the 85% x items.")
inl note_of_x_compat_vs_postable_11 () = Note ("X-COMPAT-VS-POSTABLE", 11u32, "2026-10-26T08:46Z", "x", "2026-10-26 04:1x x: main's 00:35 X-CELLS-UNICODE gate (job-1792976677986) was RED, 16 unicode.spi rows on rust and gleam plus the livemd round trip, and its land refused. Cause: x's code-points apply script (scratchpad code-points\\apply.ps1) joined '/// ### nfc' and ':= nfc' (a here-string drops its last newline) and left an untyped conv in code_points' Rust arm (ETY-ValueRestriction). Both fixed; a fresh candidate compiles unicode.spi on Rust and Gleam (exit 0), and the healed x glyphs.spi and splitter.spi compile against it on both. The re-queued job-1792993843563 runs the fixed script.")
inl note_of_x_compat_vs_postable_12 () = Note ("X-COMPAT-VS-POSTABLE", 12u32, "2026-10-26T09:46Z", "x", "2026-10-26 05:46 x: Cells defused-dot rule LANDED with X-CELLS-UNICODE (job-1792993843563): gate GREEN 05:18 on unicode.spi, x_compat_test 4/4 on the landed Cells, files audited against the land backup (only the patch hunks differ: x_compat.ex +28/-3, targets.ex +1, unicode.spi +39, glyphs.spi heal +6/-62). The job reported exit 1 only because main's land-apply.ps1 calls land_cells_lib.ps1 with its live defaults, so the land happened on the first call and the second (stage) call hit 'backup exists'; main told. Left on this item: one fully green x verify (needs main's zig index_of fix for protocol.spi).")
inl note_of_x_compat_vs_postable_13 () = Note ("X-COMPAT-VS-POSTABLE", 13u32, "2026-10-28T10:48Z", "x", "2026-10-28 06:39 x: CLOSED by X-WIP-VERIFY's pre-registered rule. verify25 (node job-1793174907446, pinned compiler 4FD712E9E357) GREEN on every clause of X-WIP-VERIFY note 6: exit 0; sim accept and plain sim build exit 0 (ExUnit 45/45, corpus commute ok, thread_run pass, tamper rejected with same_cells=false); client gate 29 crates 0 over 1000; x-compat 0 in 43 client and 24 sim files; all 42 client and 23 sim notebooks passed, 0 failing totals; plain-pass e2e 37 of 37 tutorial steps same, 0 FAILED, 153 stub requests. Outputs landed three-way (node-proof -Land x-verify25: 21 files plus 19 Gleam modules, 0 conflicts; net.rs held back because its source changed after the sync, so verify26 lands it). main told: this unblocks tibia.")
inl lua_function_limits () = Task ("LUA-FUNCTION-LIMITS", "Lua backend: keep every emitted function within Lua 5.1's 200 active locals and 60 upvalues (do-block scoping, spill table, env tables for large capture sets), so large modules such as tibia's competitor load", Story, P1, D5, 100u32, Done, "", "compiler,lua,backends,tibia", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_lua_function_limits () = Planning ("LUA-FUNCTION-LIMITS", Minutes 240u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_lua_function_limits_1 () = Note ("LUA-FUNCTION-LIMITS", 1u32, "2026-10-25T06:30Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-25 02:30 Found by tibia's client heal under pack 5j. Lua 5.1 caps a function at 200 active locals and 60 upvalues, and the Lua backend emits functions past both. Evidence on apps/arena/competitor.spi with the healed client.spi (scratch heal-e3 and tibia-scratch): (a) composites as inl: 'competitor.lua:3596: function at line 1943 has more than 200 local variables' (go_4, a 26-parameter loop body with 1031 local statements over 1734 lines); (b) the same composites as let/:~ (emitted as Lua functions): 'competitor-let.lua:2785: function at line 2184 has more than 60 upvalues'. measure, steps, scan and path load fine either way. Pre-heal competitor loads, so the module sits at both limits and any growth trips one. CELLS-APPS note 19's red (1), competitor's generated test main.lua over 200 locals, is the same limit. Rule: generated Lua must load under Lua 5.1 for any program the compiler accepts (Lua is the WoW/Dota/OTClient target, backend-targets memory). Approach to measure first, cheapest first: (1) locals: scope single-use temporaries in do ... end blocks so slots free; if still over, spill the overflow into one local table; (2) upvalues: functions past 60 captures take their captured set through an env table, the way closures already do (closure1({ v6, v0, v3 })). Accept when competitor with the healed client loads under lua 5.1 in both forms, and the Lua rows of the compiler matrix and the arena LUA-OK checks stay green. LUA-SINGLE-SCRUTINEE (main) reduces local pressure but doesn't bound it.")
inl note_of_lua_function_limits_2 () = Note ("LUA-FUNCTION-LIMITS", 2u32, "2026-10-25T06:45Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-25 02:45 Root cause in codegenLua (5k core, spiral_compiler.fs ~169530): complex emits every value-producing if/match as 'local getvN = function() <branches that return> end; local vN = getvN()'. That thunk keeps temporaries out of the parent's 200 slots but captures every outer variable its branches name as an upvalue (the 60-upvalue red: a getv769 thunk in go_9, which has 26 parameters plus 90 locals at that depth), and allocates a closure per evaluation. Slice 1 (scratch lua-function-limits/lua-do-blocks.ps1, 5 anchors, all inside codegenLua, verified by line range): an assign_tail mode beside discard_tail, reset in print for every function body; tail_value emits 'names = x' (or table.unpack for tuples) in assign mode and 'return x' otherwise; TyLocalReturnData and simple tails go through it; complex emits 'local d' plus 'do ... assigns ... end'. Safe because tails are always last in their branch (while bodies already discard tails). Expected: no upvalues and no closure allocation from value-producing branches. The 200-locals case is unproven: temporaries move from a thunk frame into the parent's do-scope, so peak locals could rise; measure. Candidate build plus tests queued as heavy job-1792910627931-tibia-lua-function-limits: competitor and 8 probes under 3 client variants (healed inl, healed let, tree) through lua loadfile, then the lua.spi and file_system.spi Cells runs on the candidate. Applied on top of the 5k core as main asked (not in 5k).")
inl note_of_lua_function_limits_3 () = Note ("LUA-FUNCTION-LIMITS", 3u32, "2026-10-25T07:36Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-25 03:36 Slice 1 measured (candidate build 259 s, job-1792910627931, stopped after its probe pass). The upvalue red is gone: the let variant no longer trips 60 upvalues. As expected, temporaries moved into the parent frame, so competitor now exceeds 200 locals in all three client variants, including the unhealed tree (function at line 1800), a regression slice 1 would bring alone. measure, nexbot, path, random, scan, steps, summon and vbot load in all three. So slice 1 never ships without slice 2. Slice 2 (scratch lua-function-limits/lua-spill-locals.ps1, 2 anchors made unique by slice 1's lines, inside codegenLua): luaSpillLocals runs in print on every emitted function body. When the body declares more than 180 'local vN' names, they live in one per-call table: 'local __spill = {}' goes right after the header of the function that owns them (the inner function for closures), so recursive join points each get their own; declarations become '__spill[N] = ...', bare 'local vN' lines are dropped, and every reference outside string literals becomes __spill[N]. Parameters stay parameters, and nested closures capture one upvalue. Functions under the budget are untouched, so most outputs stay byte-identical. Combined candidate (slices 1 and 2 on the 5k core) queued as heavy job-1792913736301-tibia-lua-function-limits-2 with the same probe matrix plus the lua.spi and file_system.spi Cells runs.")
inl note_of_lua_function_limits_4 () = Note ("LUA-FUNCTION-LIMITS", 4u32, "2026-10-25T10:26Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-25 06:26 Combined candidate (5k plus both slices; job-1792913736301, 735 s build): all 27 probe builds load under lua 5.1 (competitor, measure, nexbot, path, random, scan, steps, summon, vbot with the healed inl, healed let and tree clients). Spill triggers in exactly one function each in competitor, measure and random, and no getter thunks remain. Behaviour (job-1792920152425): ground_trace, lead_trace and parity compiled by the candidate match their hand-written Lua under stubs (9/15/16 lines). Cells on the candidate: tibia lua.spi 5/5, lib file_system.spi 21/22; the 1 failure is the Gleam gleam.toml cwd test, and Gleam codegen is untouched. The sm'.spi run hit the 60-min job cap, so the full lib Lua matrix rows are left to main's pack review. Handed to main at 2026-10-25 06:26: entry scratch lua-function-limits/lua-function-limits.ps1 (pair-only, dry run clean on 5k). Next here: re-run the heal land (client-let-b4.spi) and CELLS-APPS's competitor test once a pack with these slices installs.")
inl note_of_lua_function_limits_5 () = Note ("LUA-FUNCTION-LIMITS", 5u32, "2026-10-25T14:39Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-25 10:39 main's 5l review (single-flight-20261025-081136, 7,352 rows, DISAGREE 0) found one regression: mega_omniledger_erp_kernel/runtime_native on Lua went from ran/agree to: main function has more than 200 local variables. Cause: the do-block's bare local vN at chunk level wasn't counted by spillChunkLocals (it matched only local names =), while the getter form it replaced emitted two counted lines per bind. Fix: lua-spill-locals.ps1 gains 2 edits (count bare declarations; drop bare local spilled.vN lines after the rename, since a spilled bare declaration isn't a Lua statement). Proof on lua5.1 with chunk-spill-check.ps1 (210 do-block binds): the old regex reproduces the error, counting without the drop fails to parse (= expected near do), the fix runs with the right total. Main dropped the pair from 5l so SLATE isn't blocked; it rides the next pack with that row in the full matrix (scripts sent to main 10:4x).")
inl note_of_lua_function_limits_6 () = Note ("LUA-FUNCTION-LIMITS", 6u32, "2026-10-26T05:26Z", "tibia-lane", "05:26Z Pack 5m dropped the pair again (main 01:0x): omniledger runtime_native on Lua still fails 'main function has more than 200 local variables'. Findings: (1) the committed tmp/samples main.lua (175,865 bytes, from 10-25 00:25) loads fine on lua5.1; the review's failing output was 158,405 bytes, and 'native-reused' means the memo replayed by content. So the candidate emits 5l's regressed bytes, and the fix changed nothing for this program. (2) In the committed file only 4 locals are at column 0 against 238 at indent 8, mostly inside spilled.getvN getter functions. So main's second hypothesis is the likely one: the regressed output's chunk-level locals sit indented inside do or if blocks, where the column-0 '^local' count can't see them. (3) A second requirement surfaced: tibia competitor.lua with the healed client fails 'function at line 2449 has more than 60 upvalues', so the pair must bound upvalues too, not only main-chunk locals. Reproduction running on the remote node: job-1792992453345-tibia-luafix-repro, mirror tibia-luafix (spiral d095f13d, digest-verified), builds main's composite (A29F241986A441F3) into an isolated SPIRAL_BIN_CACHE_DIR, compiles omniledger runtime_native to Lua and load-checks it. The fix gets drafted against that exact output.")
inl note_of_lua_function_limits_7 () = Note ("LUA-FUNCTION-LIMITS", 7u32, "2026-10-26T05:28Z", "tibia-lane", "05:28Z Correction to note 6 (3): the competitor 60-upvalue failure came from the node's current compiler, which has no pair. The overflowing function (competitor.lua:2449) is a getv769 getter thunk, and note 4 already showed the pair removes the thunks and loads competitor with the healed let client. So omniledger's chunk locals are the only open failure. Main was told the same.")
inl note_of_lua_function_limits_8 () = Note ("LUA-FUNCTION-LIMITS", 8u32, "2026-10-26T05:49Z", "main", "10-26 01:49 main: root cause found by tibia (10-26 01:4x): CRLF. Lua's shared line helper (x.text.Append(' ', x.indent).AppendLine s, core ~162056) writes CRLF on Windows, so the main chunk holds 'local v58' plus CR LF in memory and the file becomes LF only on write. spillChunkLocals' declared regex [ \\t]*(?:=|$) can't match a bare declaration across the CR, so 290 bare 'local vN' were never counted or renamed ('local x = ...' lines were, through the '='). tibia's 5l proof ran on LF text, which is why it passed. Reproduced on the node from 5m's composite: 158,405 bytes, main.lua:3196, identical to the review. Fix: one line at the top of spillChunkLocals that turns CR LF into LF. Plan: once tibia's node verification (omniledger plus the tibia arena entries) is green, the pair plus this line joins pack 5n over 5m', with the omniledger runtime_native Lua row back to ran/agree as 5n's hard requirement.")
inl note_of_lua_function_limits_9 () = Note ("LUA-FUNCTION-LIMITS", 9u32, "2026-10-26T05:59Z", "tibia-lane", "05:59Z Root cause found and fixed (node-proven). Lua's shared helper `let line x s = x.text.Append(' ', x.indent).AppendLine s` writes CRLF on Windows, so the main chunk holds `local vN\\r\\n` in memory; output is normalized to LF only on write. spillChunkLocals' declared regex `[ \\t]*(?:=|$)` can't match a bare declaration across the \\r, so omniledger's 290 bare `local vN` were never counted or renamed (my 5l proof ran on LF text, which is why it passed). Fix: `let code = code.Replace(\"\\r\\n\", \"\\n\")` as the first line of spillChunkLocals (scratch luafix/spill-lf-apply.ps1, shared with main at %TEMP%\\tibia-luafix; base A29F241986A441F3 -> 9C3FD752D0770582). Node proof, job-1792993429268, isolated cache, core 5AB0EA0547624D29: omniledger runtime_native Lua loads (162,787 bytes) and runs to exit 0 with stdout identical to the committed main.lua's; all 14 tibia arena entries load, including competitor with the healed client. The before-repro (job-1792992453345) reproduced the review exactly. Main takes it into pack 5n; 5n's review must bring the omniledger row back to ran/agree. The client heal (22 to 7 sites) lands after that install.")
inl note_of_lua_function_limits_10 () = Note ("LUA-FUNCTION-LIMITS", 10u32, "2026-10-26T06:03Z", "main", "10-26 02:03 main: tibia's CRLF fix (%TEMP%\\tibia-luafix\\spill-lf-apply.ps1, one anchor at the top of spillChunkLocals, keeps the target's line endings) applies cleanly on the 5m' composite too: 8660C34A11F33259 -> BC8145A1DAA91953 (dry run on a copy). Tibia's own spiral_compiler-lf.fs sits on 5m (A29F2419), so 5n takes the apply script, not that file. Node proof by tibia (job-1792993429268): omniledger runtime_native Lua loads (162,787 bytes, was main.lua:3196 over 200 locals), exit 0, stdout identical to the committed main.lua; all 14 arena entries load. 5n's matrix review is the ran/agree check.")
inl note_of_lua_function_limits_11 () = Note ("LUA-FUNCTION-LIMITS", 11u32, "2026-10-28T03:02Z", "tibia-lane", "02:5xZ tibia: the 5n re-check is DONE and passes. 5n is the installed compiler (core 7A6C1445BF5A, cleared by main at 22:49 after its A/B). The live tibia tree's 9 apps/arena entry modules, the same set as this item's original check, compiled to Lua and loaded with lua loadfile: competitor 161,255 bytes LUA-OK (it was the over-200-locals main chunk), plus measure, nexbot, path, random, scan, steps, summon and vbot, all LUA-OK. Method note: non-entry library modules (client, hooks, observer and others) have no main of their own, so compiling them alone emits common.spi's F#-only export main and fails to parse; that's an artifact of compiling a module without an entry, not a defect. Done.")
