inl note_of_lib_list_sugar_rest_27 () = Note ("LIB-LIST-SUGAR-REST", 27u32, "2026-10-29T12:36Z", "main", "10-29 08:4x main: CLOSING. Census of the live lib at 08:4x, every .spi/.spir under lib/spiral, token-bounded 'Cons (' or 'Nil': 47 hits, of which 0 are Spiral list constructors to convert.\n- 37 are host-language values inside macro text: Gleam's Nil (base, common, convert, erlang_port, erlang_process, optionm', otp_actor, otp_supervisor, backend) and Lua's nil (base, convert, file_system, optionm', sm', trace, reflection).\n- 2 are the sm' printer's expected output text (\"Cons(3, Cons(4, Nil))\" in sm'.spi:492 and :637), which is runtime print format, not source.\n- 1 is erlang_term's own cons function, and 2 are listm'.cons, which is already list sugar inside.\n- 5 are reflection.spi 90, 172, 360, 379 and 391: typed Nil in real-layer folds paired with (::) `(T). They stay BY DESIGN: n17's reading plus 10-28 05:47's gate, where ([] : list T) fails to parse at all 5 and drops get_union_fields.\nDone with no change today: fiscal's XSD family (xsd_emit landed 07:28 with its gate holding 17/17; xsd_regex, xsd, json_schema, xsd_resolve and xsd_constraints show 0 hits), cli_args (0 hits), and fiscal's own tree (36 sites, unstaged, 08:06).\nA typed list-sugar form for real code would be a language feature, not this item. If wanted, it becomes its own item.")
inl eoie_do_copy_literal () = Task ("EOIE-DO-COPY-LITERAL", "do copy with a literal file source copies the file instead of failing with 0 files", TaskKind, P1, D1, 100u32, Done, "EOIE-EDIT-BATCH,KIT-TRAIN7", "kit,eoie-do,bug", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_eoie_do_copy_literal () = Planning ("EOIE-DO-COPY-LITERAL", Minutes 30u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_eoie_do_copy_literal_1 () = Note ("EOIE-DO-COPY-LITERAL", 1u32, "2026-10-25T14:14Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-25 10:14 found while landing a lib slice that adds a new module. 'copy C:/x/dir/a.spi to d' reports 'copy <path> failed / 0 files' and exit 1, while 'copy C:/x/dir/a*.spi to d' copies the same file. Cause (eoie_do_file_steps/steps.spi copies_run): glob_base of a wildcard-free path is the whole path, so relative_under base path is empty and the target becomes 'd/', which std::fs::copy rejects. Fix: when relative_under returns empty, append the source's leaf name (sm'.last_index_of '/' plus sm'.substring), so a literal file lands at d/a.spi; a Spiral test block covering literal, glob and nested-glob sources. Workaround until installed: a one-match glob.")
inl note_of_eoie_do_copy_literal_2 () = Note ("EOIE-DO-COPY-LITERAL", 2u32, "2026-10-31T14:14Z", "kit", "10-31 10:1x kit: REPRODUCED and FIXED (unstaged). On the installed E14F1F0B, 'copy newpkg/a.txt to out' answers 'copy newpkg/a.txt failed ... 0 files'. Cause: for a literal source, glob_base returns the whole path, so relative_under strips it to \"\", the destination becomes the folder itself, and std::fs::copy fails. Fix in kit_do_file_steps/steps.spi: copy_base(source, literal_file) uses the source's own folder as the base when the source is an existing literal FILE, so out/a.txt results. Folders and globs keep today's base. Test block a_literal_file_copies_by_its_own_name passes 5/5 (src/a.txt -> src, a.txt -> \"\", backslashes, a literal folder, a glob). steps.spi gained the notebook header (///- --package . plus a static import block) it needed to run as a notebook at all. End-to-end: release-acceptance.ps1 row 25 'copy with a literal file source copies the file' FAILS on E14F1F0B (this bug) and passes once the next train ships the fix (TRAIN7, after main's gap).")
inl note_of_eoie_do_copy_literal_3 () = Note ("EOIE-DO-COPY-LITERAL", 3u32, "2026-11-01T03:36Z", "kit", "10-31 23:4x kit groom (main 23:33): Active -> BLOCKED on the TRAIN7 install. The code is in the tree and verified on the node TRAIN7 candidates (923AAF5A/FB34FD11, notebooks green); the only remaining step is installing TRAIN7, then close with an acceptance run on the installed binary (WIP convention).")
inl note_of_eoie_do_copy_literal_4 () = Note ("EOIE-DO-COPY-LITERAL", 4u32, "2026-11-01T12:44Z", "kit", "11-01 08:5x kit (Blocked rule). blocked by: EOIE-RELEASE-TRAIN (the TRAIN7 install; code verified on node candidates 923AAF5A/FB34FD11). The dep EOIE-EDIT-BATCH does not block this fix; close with an acceptance run on the installed binary.")
inl note_of_eoie_do_copy_literal_5 () = Note ("EOIE-DO-COPY-LITERAL", 5u32, "2026-11-01T13:17Z", "kit", "11-01 09:1x kit: blocked by: KIT-TRAIN7 (the TRAIN7 install, now its own closable item split from EOIE-RELEASE-TRAIN).")
inl note_of_eoie_do_copy_literal_6 () = Note ("EOIE-DO-COPY-LITERAL", 6u32, "2026-11-01T19:28Z", "kit", "11-01 15:28 kit ACCEPTANCE on the INSTALLED TRAIN7 binary (kit.exe A25B8FBD): 'kit do <root> \\")
inl note_of_eoie_do_copy_literal_7 () = Note ("EOIE-DO-COPY-LITERAL", 7u32, "2026-11-01T19:29Z", "kit", "11-01 15:29 kit ACCEPTANCE (full text; note 6 was cut by a shell quoting slip): on the INSTALLED TRAIN7 binary kit.exe A25B8FBD, kit do <root> with the program \"copy src/a.txt to out\" printed \"copy src -> out  1 file  7 bytes\" and \"ok  files 1 copied  bytes 7  skipped 0\", exit 0; out/a.txt exists with content \"literal\". Before the fix a literal file source matched 0 files. Closed Done.")
inl contract_rejection_diagnostics () = Task ("CONTRACT-REJECTION-DIAGNOSTICS", "Negative contract samples are rejected without a diagnostic (159 of 162): forward the rejecting diagnostic through --batch and pin each negative sample's expected code in the oracle, so a wrong-reason rejection is caught", Story, P1, D4, 100u32, Done, "PACK-7", "compiler,matrix,tests,diagnostics,main,wipe-redo", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_contract_rejection_diagnostics () = Planning ("CONTRACT-REJECTION-DIAGNOSTICS", Minutes 240u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_contract_rejection_diagnostics_1 () = Note ("CONTRACT-REJECTION-DIAGNOSTICS", 1u32, "2026-10-25T14:24Z", "main", "10-25 10:24 main: folded from apps/compiler/tmp/lanes/single-flight/README.md (COMPILER-TMP-RETIRE step 6). As of its 10-18 text, 159 of the 162 negative contract samples (samples/contract_*, e.g. contract_illegal_transition) are rejected with 'BuildFile returned no code and no diagnostic arrived'. The program is refused, but the type error never reaches the --batch result, so the oracle checks only THAT these programs are rejected, not WHY. Fix: the host forwards the rejecting diagnostic (code + span) in the batch result, and EXPECTED.tsv rows of negative samples carry the expected diagnostic code, so a rejection for the wrong reason becomes a REGRESSED row. First step: re-measure the 159/162 on the installed compiler; the count may have moved since 10-18.")
inl note_of_contract_rejection_diagnostics_2 () = Note ("CONTRACT-REJECTION-DIAGNOSTICS", 2u32, "2026-10-25T23:38Z", "compiler", "The host ALREADY has a --batch diagnostic-forwarding path; the gap is contract-specific.\n\nMechanism (apps/compiler/tmp/compiler/host/Program.fs):\n- Build returns null on a rejected program. At :583-587 the host then waits on `waiter.Task`\n  (fatalGraceMs+10s). If it completes -> `Error <diagnostic>` (code+span reach the batch result);\n  if it times out -> the generic `Error \"BuildFile returned no code and no diagnostic arrived\"`.\n- `waiter` (a TaskCompletionSource<string>, created per build in `Begin(uri)`, :158-162) is filled ONLY by:\n  - FatalError messages (:174-177 immediate, :178-187 after fatalGraceMs), and\n  - TypeErrors messages, but ONLY when `diagnosticFor uri message = Some diagnostic` AND the message is\n    `TypeErrors _` (:192-201, the `Some diagnostic when (match message with TypeErrors _ -> true | _ -> false)` guard).\n- The core sends these on errors.fatal / errors.traced (the Supervisor diagnostic channel), NOT the JSONL stream\n  (spiral_compiler.fs:185963-185968 artifact-write-rejection fix; 186128-186130 the \"host still needs the error itself\" note).\n\nSo the 159/162 contract_* rejections emit something that fills NEITHER slot: either not a TypeErrors/FatalError\nmessage, or a TypeErrors whose `diagnosticFor uri message` returns None (no span/uri it can resolve). The program\nis refused but no code+span reaches --batch, so the oracle checks only THAT it rejects, not WHY.\n\nFix locus (precise): make a contract rejection reach the host as a TypeErrors (or FatalError) carrying a span that\n`diagnosticFor` resolves, so `waiter` fills and the batch result carries the diagnostic code. Then pin each negative\nsample's expected code in EXPECTED.tsv, turning a wrong-reason rejection into REGRESSED.\n\nDeferred (need the installed compiler + a compile, blocked under 100% CPU):\n1. Re-measure 159/162 on the installed single-flight core (count may have moved since the 10-18 text).\n2. Run ONE contract_* sample (e.g. contract_illegal_transition) under --batch and capture the emitted message kind\n   + whether `diagnosticFor` yields a span -> tells which of the two conditions fails.\n3. Implement the routing + pin codes; gate with -Suite contracts (negative rows become code-checked).")
inl note_of_contract_rejection_diagnostics_3 () = Note ("CONTRACT-REJECTION-DIAGNOSTICS", 3u32, "2026-10-26T12:28Z", "compiler", "Part 2 (assertion mechanism; local, no compile): tests/matrix.psd1 has a hand-written ExpectedErrors = @( @{ Id='samples/<name>'; Contains='<diagnostic substring>' } ... ) array (~line 225) pinning expected-rejection samples by a substring of the forwarded diagnostic -- routes_negative_*, conversation_negative_*, reactors_negative_*, mega_brzozowski_*/negative_* are all present; contract_* negatives are ABSENT (the 159/162 gap at the assertion layer). DISTINCT from EXPECTED.tsv (the generated oracle in cache). So after the host forwards the contract rejection diagnostic (part 1 = the waiter locus in the prior note), step 2 is one ExpectedErrors row per contract negative. Identify negatives by BEHAVIOR not name: the matrix Smoke list includes contract_gadt/contract_existential/contract_illegal_transition which are POSITIVE (compile); run candidates under --check and keep the rejecters.")
inl note_of_contract_rejection_diagnostics_4 () = Note ("CONTRACT-REJECTION-DIAGNOSTICS", 4u32, "2026-10-26T20:27Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-26 (deferred step 1 DONE build-independently -- re-measured the negative count + enumerated the authoritative negative set, no compile). Source: the ACCEPTED oracle baseline C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\spiral-bin\\baseline\\EXPECTED.tsv (columns id|backend|compile|residual|native|exit|stdout). A negative (expected-rejection) contract sample is a row with compile=error and empty residual; a positive is compile=ok + residual hash + native=ran.\n\nRESULT (332 distinct contract_* samples, each has exactly one baseline row):\n- NEGATIVE (compile=error): 161\n- POSITIVE (compile=ok): 171\n- MIXED: 0 -> confirms rejection is front-end/backend-independent (the clean binary split the fix relies on; a negative errors uniformly).\nCOUNT MOVED: the 10-18 text said 162 negatives / 159 without a diagnostic; the current baseline has 161 negatives. So step 1's \"count may have moved\" is answered: 161, not 162.\n\nARTIFACT: the full authoritative 161-id negative set is written to scratchpad/crd-negative-set.txt (sorted, with the pos/neg tallies). This IS the exact set deferred step 3 pins expected diagnostic codes for (one matrix.psd1 ExpectedErrors row per id, per note 3-Part2) and the exact set deferred step 2's --batch probe runs over. Having it enumerated build-independently means the build-slot worker skips the enumeration and runs --batch straight down this list.\n\nREFINEMENT to note 3 (correcting the matrix-based approach): matrix.psd1 is INSUFFICIENT to classify negatives -- it names only 25 distinct positive contract_* (Smoke 3: gadt/existential/illegal_transition; Fsharp backend list 23, gadt overlapping), because its backend lists are OPT-IN for non-C backends only; the ~171 C-oracle positives are driven by EXPECTED.tsv in the cache, not the tree. So the authoritative classifier is the EXPECTED.tsv compile column (used here), NOT matrix.psd1's lists. Note 3's \"identify by behavior via --check\" still holds as the live-recompute method, but for the CURRENT baseline the split is already recorded in EXPECTED.tsv and needs no --check.\n\nSTILL COMPILE-BOUND (unchanged): EXPECTED.tsv records only error/ok, NOT whether a diagnostic was forwarded. So the \"159 vs 161 WITHOUT a diagnostic\" distinction (which 2-3 of the 161 already surface a code) still needs deferred step 2 (run one+ contract_* under --batch, capture the emitted message kind + whether diagnosticFor yields a span) and the host-forwarding fix (note 2's waiter locus, Program.fs:583-587). This re-measure de-risks and pre-stages those; it does not replace them.")
inl note_of_contract_rejection_diagnostics_5 () = Note ("CONTRACT-REJECTION-DIAGNOSTICS", 5u32, "2026-10-26T20:30Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-26 (no-harvest confirmation; closes a shortcut). Checked whether the 161 negatives carry a COMMITTED expected-rejection reason that step 3 could harvest build-independently instead of running --batch. They do NOT: a negative dir holds only main.spi + package.spiproj (no error file, no residual outputs), and the source carries no inline expectation (no ///> target line, no expect-comment) -- e.g. contract_ack_before_publish is a bare GADT-typestate program (Acknowledge needs a published input; main feeds a persisted EventBox -> the type mismatch IS the expected rejection, but it is documented nowhere). So the expected diagnostic substring for each ExpectedErrors row MUST come from capturing --batch output (deferred step 2), NOT from reading the tree. This is the item's own problem restated: the reason exists neither in source nor in a committed artifact nor (yet) in the --batch result. Net: steps 2+3 stay compile-bound; no build-independent harvest shortcut. The build-independent surface of this item is now exhausted (re-measure=161 done, negative set enumerated in scratchpad/crd-negative-set.txt, classifier = EXPECTED.tsv compile column, no-harvest confirmed).")
inl note_of_contract_rejection_diagnostics_6 () = Note ("CONTRACT-REJECTION-DIAGNOSTICS", 6u32, "2026-10-27T08:32Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-27 locus pin (read the host; corrects a \"shared fix with SPIRAL-CHECK\" over-hypothesis). CONTRACT-REJECTION-DIAGNOSTICS is the same FAMILY as SPIRAL-CHECK-PRINTS-ERRORS (a computed reason not forwarded) but a DIFFERENT locus -- not one shared fix.\n\nVerified in the --batch path (matrix/contracts run through runBatch, Program.fs:814): runBatch ALREADY forwards the reason -- on an Error job it writes `{id}\\terror\\t{ms}\\t{cleanProtocolText message}` (:852). So the batch layer does NOT drop the reason; the reason never ARRIVES as a formatted message. compileOne (Program.fs:577-587) does WhenAny(buildTask, waiter.Task): a rejected negative contract produces no output (generated = null), and the host returns Error from the waiter ONLY IF the DiagnosticRouter filled it; otherwise it falls to the generic `BuildFile returned no code and no diagnostic arrived` (:587) -- that generic string is the \"159/162 rejected without a diagnostic\".\n\nThe DiagnosticRouter fills via diagnosticFor (Program.fs:135) / diagnosticForOther (:145), which format only TypeErrors / TokenizerErrors / PackageErrors / ParserErrors / TracedError / FatalError (each rendered `%A{x.errors}`). So the FIX is HOST-SIDE: make the waiter fill for whatever a negative-contract rejection emits, with its span.\n\nOPEN (needs a core read, do before drafting): what ClientErrorsRes kind a negative contract emits. Two branches:\n- if it emits TypeErrors, diagnosticFor already formats it, so the real gap is that the rejection does NOT reach server.errors at all (the fix is to ROUTE the contract rejection through the diagnostic channel); or\n- if it emits an unhandled kind, add that kind to diagnosticFor with a VSCRange render.\n\nRelation to SPIRAL-CHECK n7: that one is the proj_open --check path (:736), where the CORE typechecker_results_summary (spiral_compiler.fs:178930) discards InferResult.errors; its fix is a core summary change. Both items ultimately surface RString errors (RString = VSCRange * string, spiral_compiler.fs:42765) as file:line:col, so a reusable RString -> \"{uri}:{line+1}:{col+1}: {msg}\" FORMATTER serves both -- but the propagation locus differs (core summary for --check; host router for --batch/contracts). Do NOT bundle them as one patch. Also note Program.fs:364 (\"Direct project typecheck rejected\") is a THIRD generic-rejection site of the same family (the --backend direct path).")
inl note_of_contract_rejection_diagnostics_7 () = Note ("CONTRACT-REJECTION-DIAGNOSTICS", 7u32, "2026-10-27T09:14Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-27 follow-up (core read): narrowed the open question from the n.locus note. The likely culprit is the sameUri GUARD on TypeErrors in the host DiagnosticRouter.\n\nFlow: typecheck errors (InferResult.errors : RString list) are sent to errors.typer and published as `TypeErrors {|uri; errors|}` (spiral_compiler.fs error_ch_create, :189323-189345; each ClientErrorsRes case has its own channel: typer/package/parser/tokenizer/fatal/traced). The host waiter fills ONLY from diagnosticFor (Program.fs:191-202; diagnosticForOther only sets `related`, never the waiter). And diagnosticFor's TypeErrors arm is guarded:\n  | TypeErrors x when sameUri x.uri uri && not (List.isEmpty x.errors) -> Some ...   (Program.fs:136)\n-- i.e. it formats a TypeErrors ONLY when its uri equals the build's input uri. PackageErrors (:140) and the others have NO sameUri guard.\n\nHYPOTHESIS (static, strong): a negative contract whose type error is reported against a uri != the sample's build-input uri (e.g. the rejection surfaces in an imported module, or carries the package uri) fails the sameUri guard -> diagnosticFor returns None -> the waiter never fills -> compileOne returns the generic \"BuildFile returned no code and no diagnostic arrived\" (:587) == \"rejected without a diagnostic\" for 159/162. The 3 that DO get a diagnostic are the ones whose error uri matches the input (or come via PackageErrors, which isn't uri-guarded).\n\nLIKELY FIX (host): in the --batch/contract path, fill the waiter from a uri-AGNOSTIC formatter for TypeErrors too (drop/relax the sameUri guard there, or fall back to diagnosticForOther's `TypeErrors ({uri}): %A{errors}` which has no guard), so the rejection reason is forwarded regardless of which uri it surfaced on. Then format the RString (VSCRange*string) as {uri}:{line+1}:{col+1}: {msg} as in the n.locus note.\n\nCONFIRM BEFORE DRAFTING (build-bound, cheap): run `--batch` on a few crd-negative-set.txt samples with traceDiagnostics on (DiagnosticRouter.Accept prints `[diagnostic] %A` at Program.fs:171 when traceDiagnostics) and read the actual ClientErrorsRes kind + uri vs the input uri. That tells definitively whether it's the sameUri drop (most likely), a PackageErrors-vs-TypeErrors split, or the error never reaching server.errors at all. Host locus unchanged (fix is host-side, NOT the core summary that SPIRAL-CHECK needs).")
inl note_of_contract_rejection_diagnostics_8 () = Note ("CONTRACT-REJECTION-DIAGNOSTICS", 8u32, "2026-10-27T14:00Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-27 CONTRACT-REJECTION confirm attempt (build-bound step 2, PARTIAL -- refines the approach; does NOT yet confirm the sameUri hypothesis of n7). Ran the installed (5m') compiler via probe.ps1 -Backend C + TRACE_DIAGNOSTICS on 4 contract negatives:\n- contract_ack_before_publish: FatalError \"blocks that fail to tokenize or parse\" (parse/tokenize reject; possibly edition-3 syntax the installed 5m' lacks).\n- contract_adjacent_genome_interval_merge / contract_adjoint_indexed_uncompute / contract_adversarial_mesh_convergence: FatalError \"The return type of main in the C backend should be a 32-bit int.\"\n\nKEY: all 4 FORWARD a FatalError (it reaches the probe result). So the probe -Backend C path does NOT reproduce the generic \"rejected without a diagnostic\" (the 159/162). -Backend C is therefore the WRONG confirm path: it reaches the C backend / parser, whose FatalErrors DO fill the waiter (n2), instead of the type-check contract rejection that the --batch matrix path drops via the sameUri TypeErrors guard (n7). The \"C backend main return not i32\" also indicates these negatives TYPE-CHECK OK on the installed core and are caught only at the C backend's main-return check -- so either the contract type-rejection does not fire on 5m' (installed core behind the enforcement) or it is backend/path-specific.\n\nREFINED step 2 (replaces the probe approach): run the confirm via the --batch MATRIX path -- test.ps1 -Suite contracts (compile-only), or a minimal --batch manifest over crd-negative-set.txt -- with TRACE_DIAGNOSTICS, on the 5n compiler (post-install; it carries the edition-3 lexer items so parse-rejects like contract_ack_before_publish stop masking, plus whatever contract enforcement 5n ships). Read the raw [diagnostic] ClientErrorsRes kind + uri vs the input uri there to confirm/refute the sameUri drop. QUEUE POST-5n. Net new fact: -Backend C is ruled out as the confirm path; the build-independent surface stays exhausted (n5); the host fix locus (n6/n7, sameUri guard) is unchanged but still needs the --batch confirm before drafting.")
inl note_of_contract_rejection_diagnostics_9 () = Note ("CONTRACT-REJECTION-DIAGNOSTICS", 9u32, "2026-10-27T21:18Z", "compiler", "CONTRACT-REJECTION-DIAGNOSTICS (P1, 35%) -- verified locus, 10-27 (read live source apps/compiler/tmp/compiler/host/Program.fs)\n\nSYMPTOM: 159 of 162 negative contract samples are rejected with no diagnostic (they time out instead of\nreporting the rejection reason).\n\nMECHANISM (verified, not inferred -- Program.fs:170-211, type DiagnosticRouter):\n  The per-build waiter is a TaskCompletionSource<string> (Begin, :157). Accept completes it in exactly 3 places:\n    - :176  FatalError when entryTypeErrors.IsSome  -> TrySetResult($\"FatalError: {fatal}\")\n    - :182-188 FatalError (no entry type errors)    -> Task.Delay(fatalGraceMs) then TrySetResult with `related` detail\n    - :196-201 entry-uri diagnostic that is a TypeErrors -> Delay 1000 then TrySetResult(diagnostic + parseDetail)\n    - :202  entry-uri diagnostic (other)            -> TrySetResult(diagnostic)\n  THE DROP -- :203-210, the `None -> diagnosticForOther message` branch:\n    | None ->\n        match diagnosticForOther message with\n        | Some text -> related <- Some text        <-- STASHES, never TrySetResult\n            (also eprintfn \"warning: ...\" for ParserErrors)\n        | None -> ()\n  diagnosticFor (entry-uri-gated, :135-143) returns None when the rejection diagnostic is NOT on the entry uri\n  (an imported/contract sub-module) or is a ParserErrors (not in diagnosticFor's set at all). diagnosticForOther\n  (:145-149, ANY uri) then returns Some -> stashed in `related`. If NO FatalError follows, the waiter never\n  completes -> the host's await times out -> \"rejected without a diagnostic\".\n\nWHY THE FIX IS NOT A ONE-LINE \"complete the waiter here\":\n  The same None/diagnosticForOther branch ALSO fires for NON-TERMINAL related diagnostics -- e.g. a sub-module\n  ParserErrors that is reported as a warning (the eprintfn \"warning: blocks that fail to parse are left out\" at\n  :208) while the build CONTINUES to a successful compile. Eagerly completing the waiter there would report a\n  spurious rejection for programs that actually compile. The fix must distinguish \"terminal rejection, nothing\n  more coming\" from \"related diagnostic, build continues\". That is what the FatalError grace-delay dance\n  (:182-188, fold `related` into the FatalError detail) exists for -- so the real question is: for the 159\n  rejected contracts, does a FatalError EVER arrive, and if so why isn't its detail reaching the waiter?\n\nNEXT STEP (needs a compiler slot -- cannot run build-free):\n  Run ONE rejected contract sample under --batch with the diagnostic trace on (traceDiagnostics, :171 prints\n  \"[diagnostic] %A message\" for every ClientErrorsRes Accept receives; env TRACE_DIAGNOSTICS). Read the message\n  SEQUENCE for a known-rejected contract:\n    (a) If a FatalError arrives after the stashed diagnostic -> the grace path (:182-188) SHOULD complete it;\n        find why it doesn't (entryTypeErrors set? wrong active uri? fatalGraceMs too short under load?).\n    (b) If NO FatalError ever arrives -> the rejection is ONLY the diagnosticForOther diagnostic; the fix is to\n        complete the waiter from `related` on the terminal signal (build-done / server idle), NOT inline at :206.\n  Either way the fix is host-side (Program.fs), shared across cores -> coordinate with main before landing\n  (host code, blast radius = all rejection reporting). Draft as an anchored pack once the trace picks (a) vs (b).\n\nSTATUS: locus + mechanism VERIFIED from live source; blocked on a diagnostic run (heavy slot busy: G3a + TRAIN).\nDo NOT draft a blind edit -- the (a)/(b) branch decides the fix shape.")
inl note_of_contract_rejection_diagnostics_10 () = Note ("CONTRACT-REJECTION-DIAGNOSTICS", 10u32, "2026-10-27T22:52Z", "compiler", "Node --backend trace (10-27, 5 contracts). Rejected contract seq: empty heartbeats -> TypeErrors(non-empty, ENTRY uri, the ETY-TermError) -> FatalError(same error text). So a FatalError DOES arrive (branch a) AND the rejection diag is on the ENTRY uri (diagnosticFor Some, Program.fs:196 -- NOT the None/diagnosticForOther branch my earlier note guessed). --backend DELIVERS it (exit 5 + error). So the 159/162 drop is --BATCH-specific (the waiter->RESULTS.tsv forwarding runBatch uses); --backend does not exercise it. NEXT: run --batch JOBS.tsv on a rejected contract + read RESULTS.tsv to see the recorded diagnostic. Positive contract_arithmetic exits 0.")
inl note_of_contract_rejection_diagnostics_11 () = Note ("CONTRACT-REJECTION-DIAGNOSTICS", 11u32, "2026-10-27T22:58Z", "compiler", "Node --batch test (15 contracts). RESULTS.tsv records a NON-EMPTY message per rejected contract. MOST = \"FatalError: The return type of main in the C backend should be a 32-bit int\" (generic C-backend req, NOT the contract violation); a FEW (balanced_as_constant, block_inside_phrasing, double_owner_close) = \"FatalError: File main has a type error... <specific ETY-TermError>\". So the rejection IS forwarded but mostly as a GENERIC backend error -- the contracts violations are not surfaced under --backend C (main not i32 masks them). NEXT: read test.ps1 contract-scoring + whether contracts use --check vs --backend C. arithmetic=ok.")
inl note_of_contract_rejection_diagnostics_12 () = Note ("CONTRACT-REJECTION-DIAGNOSTICS", 12u32, "2026-10-27T23:02Z", "compiler", "CORRECTION: contracts compile --backend Zig (test.ps1:106), not C -- my earlier C \"return type i32\" finding was a red herring (wrong backend). Re-ran --batch --backend Zig (15 contracts): MOST (12/15) compile OK (~3265 bytes); only a few rejected (ack_before_publish parse, balanced_as_constant + block_inside_phrasing type errors). So a PLAIN Zig compile does NOT reject most negative contracts. The matrix negative-contract rejection must use a mechanism beyond a plain compile (test blocks? a contract-check pass? expected-rejection markers in matrix.psd1/oracle). NEXT: read test.ps1 contract SCORING (compile result -> verdict for an expected rejection) + what triggers the contract check.")
inl note_of_contract_rejection_diagnostics_13 () = Note ("CONTRACT-REJECTION-DIAGNOSTICS", 13u32, "2026-10-27T23:11Z", "compiler", "RE-MEASURE (node --batch --backend Fsharp, 15 actual oracle-negatives, the CORRECT backend): ALL 15 -> error FatalError (File main has a type error... <path>:line:col: <specific reason>; double_owner_close full trace = ETY-TermError owner_open/owner_closed with span). NONE hit \"BuildFile returned no code and no diagnostic arrived\". So the 159/162 count (10-18) appears STALE -- the current compiler DOES forward the FatalError diagnostic (span+reason) via --batch. Part 1 (host forwarding) likely already DONE. Remaining = Part 2: pin each negatives expected code (matrix.psd1 ExpectedErrors + EXPECTED.tsv) so wrong-reason rejection -> REGRESSED. CONFIRM by re-measuring all 161 negatives. (Supersedes my notes 11-12 red herrings: wrong backend C/Zig.)")
inl note_of_contract_rejection_diagnostics_14 () = Note ("CONTRACT-REJECTION-DIAGNOSTICS", 14u32, "2026-10-28T00:17Z", "compiler", "Part 2 format confirmed (matrix.psd1 :243 ExpectedErrors): @{ Id=samples/contract_<name>; Contains=<substring> }. Contract rejections are TYPE-unification errors (ETY-TermError, e.g. double_owner_close = \"owner _ owner_open\" vs \"owner_closed\"), NOT human messages -- so Contains = the distinctive type-name specifics. Part 2 = run each of the 161 oracle-negatives --batch Fsharp, extract its type-name substring, add one row. Existing rows there: routes_negative_*, conversation_negative_* etc.; contract_* absent.")
inl note_of_contract_rejection_diagnostics_15 () = Note ("CONTRACT-REJECTION-DIAGNOSTICS", 15u32, "2026-10-28T11:41Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-28 Part 2 SCOPED: 332 contract_* samples in tmp/samples; ~161 are oracle-negatives (reject; rest positive, e.g. contract_arithmetic exits 0). Build-free surface EXHAUSTED -- Part 2 is a build-bound run: test.ps1 -Suite contracts (or --batch Fsharp over contract_*) on the 5n/installed compiler, read which reject + their FatalError ETY-TermError text, add one matrix.psd1 ExpectedErrors row per negative (@{ Id=samples/contract_<name>; Contains=<distinctive type-name substring> }; format confirmed :243-257, contract_* rows ABSENT today). The run determines the exact negative set + substrings (not knowable build-free). Part 1 (host forwarding) = done (n15: --batch Fsharp forwards the FatalError span+reason). Queue on a box/node slot.")
inl note_of_contract_rejection_diagnostics_16 () = Note ("CONTRACT-REJECTION-DIAGNOSTICS", 16u32, "2026-10-29T18:01Z", "compiler", "10-29 14:0x compiler: Part 2's risk is real and wider than contract_*: a sample that never compiled is scored 'parity' forever once its compile=error row is accepted. Census of the latest local run (single-flight-20261029-120916): 512 error rows; 274 ids pinned in matrix.psd1 ExpectedErrors; unpinned reasons: TermError 160 (mostly the contract negatives: the intended type rejections this item pins), UnboundVariable 19, InstanceNotFound 6, others 1-4 each. The UnboundVariable group is name resolution, i.e. likely never-ran samples.\nTWO FIXED AND LANDED (live == HEAD blob before, == proven copy after; undo copies kept):\n(1) samples/delphi_uses_units (fiscal, 10-25, DELPHI-USES-UNITS' regression sample): 'global' is a lib function, unbound against the matrix's samples/core, so both rows were 'error' in the oracle. Now !!!!Global(\"uses StrUtils;\"). Proven: Delphi emits 'uses SysUtils, Math, StrUtils;', builds -Px86_64 and exits 7; C exits 7 with no StrUtils text (a Delphi arm's global does not leak into other backends through BackendSwitch validation); without the global, FPC fails with 'Identifier not found ReverseString'.\n(2) samples/or_pattern_bound_alternatives (10-24, RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION): it used bare * + -, but samples/core defines only the dotted operators (+. -. *. /. %. =. < <= > >=), so or-pattern codegen was never exercised on its 4 backends. Now dotted; C, Rust and Delphi (x64) all exit 41 (= 16 + 28 - 3), and F# compiles.\nPack 7's review will show these 6 rows moving from error to ok; that is the intended change.\nLEFT FOR OWNERS (intent unclear, not touched):\n- native_package_closure_{array_failure, managed_branch, managed_failure} [C]: packages without core use '+'; the names may mean the rejection is intended.\n- empty_match_parse_error [C]: unbound 'f', but the name says parse error.\n- 9 Zig negatives rejected on an unbound long constructor name that looks renamed in the parent sample, so they may pass without testing their intended type error: mega_zeta_structural_laboratory/negative_{descent_unavailable_divisor_wrong_child, legacy_completion_raw_classification, mixed_contradiction_obligation_not_rejection, mixed_rejected_obstruction_not_found_consistency, recursive_divisor_refusal_wrong_child}, mega_brzozowski_derivatives/negative_{antimirov_missing_finite_authority, forge_constructive_minimality_witness}, mega_omniledger_erp_kernel/negative_three_owner_physical_takeover_authority_wrong_endpoint, contract_missing_endpoint_capability.\nEach of those wants an ExpectedErrors pin naming its INTENDED error; a pin on the current unbound name would freeze the wrong reason.")
inl note_of_contract_rejection_diagnostics_17 () = Note ("CONTRACT-REJECTION-DIAGNOSTICS", 17u32, "2026-10-29T18:14Z", "compiler", "10-29 14:2x compiler: PART 2 LANDED for 157 of the 161 contract negatives. apps/compiler/tmp/tests/matrix.psd1 ExpectedErrors grows 252 -> 409 rows (157 insertions, nothing else; 0 duplicate ids; every other key equal to HEAD, as loaded by test.ps1's own Import-PowerShellDataFile -SkipLimitCheck). Undo copy: compiler scratchpad matrix.psd1.undo.\nSource: a node test.ps1 -Suite contracts run on the installed 6b compiler (job-1793295607902, run single-flight-20261029-134023, 728 jobs); its 161 negative Zig rows reject exactly as the box oracle records (161 error/error, 170 ok/ok, 0 mismatches). A pin is the path-free message between 'ETY-TermError: ' and ' In the definition' (154, e.g. 'Unification failure. Got: oracle constant Expected: oracle balanced'), plus 3 whose names state the intended reason: contract_parser_error ('Expected one of: inl, let, open, unary operator'), contract_missing_deployment_interpreter and contract_missing_query_interpreter (their ETY-InstanceNotFound text). Consumer-path check: applying test.ps1:1087's rule (detail.Contains(pin)) to the box's own run single-flight-20261029-120916 gives 0 WRONG-ERROR. From now on, a contract negative rejected for any other reason scores WRONG-ERROR instead of parity.\nNOT pinned (4), because their current rejection looks like the wrong reason and pinning it would freeze that: contract_ack_before_publish and contract_missing_endpoint_capability fail to PARSE at a position whose expected-token list includes 'exists' (existential syntax the parser no longer accepts?), contract_scoped_resource_escape stops at 'The main function should not have a forall', and contract_trust_boundary_forall_divergence at ETY-ExpectedAnnotation (recursive functions need an annotation). Each needs its author's intended error, then a pin. Plus the non-contract suspects in n16.")
inl note_of_contract_rejection_diagnostics_18 () = Note ("CONTRACT-REJECTION-DIAGNOSTICS", 18u32, "2026-10-29T18:28Z", "compiler", "10-29 14:3x compiler: 2 of n17's 4 unpinned negatives REPAIRED and pinned (now 159 of 161; matrix.psd1 ExpectedErrors 411 rows, 0 duplicate ids). Both dated 09-27 and broke on syntax that tightened since, so for a month they were rejected by the parser instead of their contract. contract_ack_before_publish: 'inl EventBox result = ...' (a bare constructor let-pattern no longer parses) -> 'inl (EventBox result) = ...'; it now rejects with 'Unification failure. Got: event_box published Expected: event_box persisted' (acknowledge before publish). contract_missing_endpoint_capability: the same let-pattern fix, plus its GADT case quantified ('Session :: forall certificate endpoint. string -> session certificate endpoint'; unquantified case variables are now unbound); it rejects with 'Got: session present present Expected: session present missing'. Positive controls: with the one offending annotation made contract-correct (persisted -> published; missing -> present), each program COMPILES on Zig, so each rejection is attributable to the contract alone. Landed with the reader guard (live == HEAD blob before), undo copies in the compiler scratchpad. Pack 7's review: these 2 Zig rows stay 'error' (with a different detail, now pinned). Left unpinned: contract_scoped_resource_escape ('The main function should not have a forall') and contract_trust_boundary_forall_divergence (ETY-ExpectedAnnotation).")
inl note_of_contract_rejection_diagnostics_19 () = Note ("CONTRACT-REJECTION-DIAGNOSTICS", 19u32, "2026-10-29T18:32Z", "compiler", "10-29 14:3x compiler: contract_scoped_resource_escape PINNED ('The main function should not have a forall'), now 160/161 (matrix.psd1 ExpectedErrors 412, 0 duplicate ids; the box run's detail contains the pin). The text is generic, so it is pinned only with a control that attributes it: replacing the leaking visitor leak (returns the resource) with a polymorphic non-leaking one (inl describe forall region. (resource : resource region) : string = ..., passed the same way as $describe) makes the program COMPILE on Zig. The escaped region is therefore the only source of main's forall. A plain lambda is not a valid control: it fails with 'Got: forall region. resource region -> string Expected: _ -> string', since rank-2 arguments must be named polymorphic functions. Last unpinned: contract_trust_boundary_forall_divergence (inl rec bottom forall a. () : a = bottom (), rejected at 1:26 with ETY-ExpectedAnnotation). Annotating it could make it ACCEPTED (divergence inhabiting forall a. a), so its intended error is the author's call.")
inl note_of_contract_rejection_diagnostics_20 () = Note ("CONTRACT-REJECTION-DIAGNOSTICS", 20u32, "2026-10-29T18:37Z", "compiler", "10-29 14:4x compiler: the mega negatives from n16, classified with git (git log -S <unbound name> over each parent sample, excluding its negative_* dirs) and each negative's full error list (box run single-flight-20261029-120916). Correcting n16's 'renamed?' guess, they fall into three groups; none is pinned.\n(A) VACUOUS BY CONSTRUCTION: the constructor a negative calls NEVER existed in its parent's history: mega_zeta_structural_laboratory negative_{descent_unavailable_divisor_wrong_child, legacy_completion_raw_classification, mixed_rejected_obstruction_not_found_consistency, recursive_divisor_refusal_wrong_child} (CertifiedInterval...LegacyCompletionRefusal* / ...RootFactorFound; the parent has no LegacyCompletion names at all), mega_omniledger_erp_kernel/negative_three_owner_physical_takeover_authority_wrong_endpoint (erp_p2p_three_owner_physical_takeover_authority_program), mega_spiral_proves_spiral_relative_consistency/negative_unified_reduction_preservation_target_type_mismatch (exact_linear_indexed_open_beta_reduction). Their 'impossible_*' bodies are rejected because the name is unknown, whatever the types say, so they cannot fail even if the sealing they claim to test were removed. A pin would certify that. They need rewriting against constructors that exist (the wrong child must be a TYPE error), or deleting; the mega owner's call.\n(B) STALE REFERENCE: mega_brzozowski_derivatives/negative_forge_constructive_minimality_witness calls bit_finite_symbol_vector, which existed until 570be6a6 (2026-10-14) and was then removed.\n(C) WIRING CASCADE across the brzozowski negatives: RegexChar (present in the parent through 3f1b7588) is unbound in 12 of them, and e.g. negative_antimirov_missing_finite_authority also has an unbound constraint symbol_compare, and negative_forge_* has unbound bit_alphabet / bit_finite_length. These negatives do not see the parent's definitions, so they reject in a cascade of 4-8 errors. The pinned siblings (e.g. negative_antimirov_support_assignment_count) still pass their pins only because some 'Unification failure' text appears in the cascade, which is weaker than it looks. Likely one module/package wiring fix in the brzozowski negatives' package.spiproj or open lines; after it, re-read each pinned one's FIRST error.\nOwners: mega samples (main / whoever authored mega_*). Compiler-side, nothing is left in this item but contract_trust_boundary_forall_divergence (n19).")
inl note_of_contract_rejection_diagnostics_21 () = Note ("CONTRACT-REJECTION-DIAGNOSTICS", 21u32, "2026-10-29T18:49Z", "compiler", "10-29 14:5x compiler: the rest of n16's list. (1) empty_match_parse_error PINNED ('Unexpected end of block past this token'): its detail LEADS with the intended parse error at 2:13 (the empty match), and the unbound 'f' is only the cascade (the unparsable f is left out of the module). n16 keyed on the first ETY code and missed it. ExpectedErrors now 413. (2) native_package_closure_{array_failure, managed_branch, managed_failure} [C] are POSITIVE samples that have never compiled (they ship c-shim.h plus failure_lifecycle.pas/.rs for native runs). Wiring is fine (consumer -> selector -> types declared in each package.spiproj), but the sources break two rules: packages/selector/select.spi calls callable_owner_marker unqualified, though it lives in module 'callable' of package types (the same qualification rule as apps/spiral's rust.rust_type fix of 10-24), and both use.spi and select.spi use a bare '+' in packages that import no core. Last touched 570be6a6 (10-14). The repair (qualify both cross-package calls; give the packages a core, or the dotted operators) needs a run of their lifecycle shims to prove; owner wanted. Until then they score parity on an error the sample was never meant to have.")
inl note_of_contract_rejection_diagnostics_22 () = Note ("CONTRACT-REJECTION-DIAGNOSTICS", 22u32, "2026-10-29T18:50Z", "compiler", "10-29 14:5x compiler, addendum to n21 (native_package_closure_*): not a syntax regression, they are STUBS SINCE IMPORT. Every package file has been a one-line marker since its first commit, the 09-27 import 1eb2ecfd (select.spi: 'inl selector_marker () = callable_owner_marker () + 1i32'). The README deleted in 570be6a6 (10-14) described the real fixture: 'This fixture keeps the managed String, callable interface and two closure environments in the types/callable package. selector/select chooses one environment through a branch, consumer/use invokes it, and the root returns 42. Rust and Delphi must preserve one owner for the whole callable type group, derive package dependencies, and release the selected managed capture exactly once.' That content never reached this repo, so the cross-package managed-closure ownership check it promised has never run; the c-shim.h and failure_lifecycle.pas/.rs shims are orphans. Options: re-author the three fixtures from the README's spec (a real compiler task; it needs the lifecycle shims to prove single release), or delete them so the oracle stops scoring a placeholder as parity. The owner (or main, grooming) decides.")
inl note_of_contract_rejection_diagnostics_23 () = Note ("CONTRACT-REJECTION-DIAGNOSTICS", 23u32, "2026-10-30T14:48Z", "compiler", "10-30 10:4x compiler: n20 group (C) CORRECTED, and the real cause is a host bug in this item's own area (--batch diagnostics). (1) Scratch compiles of all 31 brzozowski negatives with installed pack 7 (core 9C899B30, F#, one process per file; census: C:\\home\\data\\backlog-census\\brz-subpackage-negatives.csv). The 15 that import the parent directly each reject on their intended first error (InstanceNotFound finite_alphabet/symbol_compare, or one Unification failure), with no unbound names. 14 of the 16 that import only a sub-package do too. Only 2 cascade: forge_constructive_minimality_witness (group B, stale bit_* names) and antimirov_missing_finite_authority. The latter is a real wiring limit: package visibility is not transitive, and same-named modules from two packages SHADOW each other instead of merging. Its parent and antimirov_certificate both name their module main, so listing both makes the later-listed main win (tried in both orders: either RegexChar/symbol_compare or antimirov_certificate goes unbound). It needs a re-author by the mega owner; no spiproj wiring reaches both. (2) n20's 'RegexChar unbound in 12' is a LEAK, not a cascade. In run single-flight-20261029-120916, those rows' detail ends in 'TypeErrors (file:///.../negative_antimirov_missing_finite_authority/main.spi): ...', which is ANOTHER sample's errors. Census of foreign TypeErrors blocks (a row whose detail carries a TypeErrors block for a file outside its own sample dir): 234 rows (12:09 run), 225 (18:47), 232 (22:33, installed pack 7). All are Zig rows, and the sources are mega negatives (top: mega_spiral_proves_spiral_relative_consistency negative_application_argument_choice_substitution_keeps_used 43 rows, negative_alpha_capture 42; mega_zeta, omniledger and lean_cic negatives about 20 each). Mechanism (host Program.fs DiagnosticRouter, lines 145-211): the warm --batch server keeps every served entry open, so a later job whose package graph overlaps re-checks an earlier negative entry and republishes its errors. Accept() stores any other-file TypeErrors as 'related' for the ACTIVE job (diagnosticForOther), and the FatalError path appends it to the detail (line 186). Effect on pins: a wrong-reason rejection can match its ExpectedErrors pin through leaked text, which is exactly what this item exists to stop. The count varies run to run (scheduling), so pins on these rows are not deterministic. FIX DESIGN (pack-9; host only, no residual change): accept 'related' diagnostics only for module files in the active entry's package closure (packageDir + packages + modules from each spiproj, recursively). Leaked files are other samples' entries, which are outside that closure; legit related errors (e.g. antimirov_typed_slot_bound/indexed_assignment.spi:70 for its negatives) are inside it. Alternatives rejected: FileDelete of served entries (a parent mega main is both an entry and a dependency), and dedupe of repeated texts (changes the pinned siblings' detail). Gate: a Zig contracts run with 0 foreign blocks, then re-read every pinned negative's FIRST error. Not started; PACK-8 is in review.")
inl note_of_contract_rejection_diagnostics_24 () = Note ("CONTRACT-REJECTION-DIAGNOSTICS", 24u32, "2026-10-30T15:03Z", "main", "10-30 11:03 main, grooming decision on n21's open call (native_package_closure_{array_failure,managed_branch,managed_failure}). DECIDED: RE-AUTHOR, not delete. The rule that decides it was fixed before choosing: a stub that compiles and returns trivially scores parity forever and hides that the promised check never ran (error-rows-hide-dead-samples, gate-met-is-not-goal-met), so deleting it loses the spec and keeping it as-is keeps the false green. (1) The spec is the README deleted in 570be6a6 (10-14), recoverable read-only with 'git show 570be6a6^:<path>': a managed String + callable interface + two closure environments in types/callable; selector/select picks one through a branch; consumer/use invokes it; the root returns 42. Rust and Delphi must keep one owner for the callable type group, derive package deps, and release the selected managed capture exactly once. The orphan c-shim.h and failure_lifecycle.pas/.rs shims are the single-release probes it needs. (2) Owner: team compiler, at load medium, as a regression-style sample set in the next pack; each fixture must FAIL on a deliberately broken release-twice or leak build before it counts (proof-must-run-consumer-path). (3) Until then the 3 rows stay in matrix.psd1:789-791 (the C,Rust,Delphi... group) but must not be read as evidence of cross-package closure ownership; compiler, please add a Known/placeholder marker naming this note when the next pack touches matrix.psd1. Nothing is deleted or moved now.")
inl note_of_contract_rejection_diagnostics_25 () = Note ("CONTRACT-REJECTION-DIAGNOSTICS", 25u32, "2026-10-30T15:04Z", "compiler", "10-30 11:04 compiler: n23's fix is DRAFTED, using a simpler rule than the closure design, equally precise for this bug and order-independent. apps/compiler/packs/apply-host-batch-foreign-entry-diagnostics.ps1 (host only, 4 anchors, dry-run ok on live Program.fs; in no pack). runBatch hands DiagnosticRouter every job's entry uri before the first job. Accept() then drops an other-file diagnostic whose file is ANOTHER job's entry, and never treats it as 'related'. Leaked blocks are always other entries (served earlier and still open in the warm server); legit related errors come from dependency modules that are not batch entries. Known cost: if a parent sample's main.spi (an entry, and also a negative's dependency) had type errors, its negatives would lose that related detail. They still fail with their own FatalError, and the parent's row shows the cause. No residual can change (router text only). Gate for pack 9: a -Suite contracts run with 0 foreign TypeErrors blocks (census as in n23), then re-read every pinned negative's FIRST error. A pin that only passed through leaked text shows up as REGRESSED and gets re-pinned on its own first error.")
inl note_of_contract_rejection_diagnostics_26 () = Note ("CONTRACT-REJECTION-DIAGNOSTICS", 26u32, "2026-10-30T21:28Z", "compiler", "10-30 17:28 compiler: n25's draft GATED on the node, and the pre-registered pass (0 foreign blocks) is NOT met. Job job-1793388330465 (runner1030 mirror; candidate = installed source + apply-host-batch-foreign-entry-diagnostics.ps1, host 0B97398643BCFA12; control = installed pack 8, host EEBCBFF8658A1EF9; test.ps1 -Suite contracts -Backend Zig, 620 rows each, no -Native). Rows carrying a TypeErrors block from a file outside their own sample dir: control 194, candidate 16 (-92%). All 16 remaining come from ONE non-entry module, mega_brzozowski_derivatives/antimirov_typed_slot_bound/injective_bound.spi, so the 'drop other jobs' ENTRIES' rule misses modules of packages that another job loaded. Some of the 16 are legitimate (5 negatives import antimirov_typed_slot_bound) and some are still leaks (e.g. negative_missing_compare imports only the parent), and only n23's package-CLOSURE rule (packageDir + packages + modules per spiproj, recursively) separates them. So the draft is a strict improvement but not the fix; the next draft computes the closure. Also: 15 rows differ between arms, 14 of them timeout<->error flips on mega negatives (node at 100%; not comparable). 1 is negative_beta_step_target_type_mismatch: control = the correct pinned first error; candidate = WRONG-ERROR with 'BuildFile returned no code and no diagnostic arrived', i.e. the entry's OWN diagnostic never reached the router. The own-file path (diagnosticFor) is untouched by the draft, so this is probably a delivery race under load, but it must be rerun alone before the next draft is trusted. Side note (checked against the oracle): antimirov_typed_slot_bound itself compiles ok and runs as an entry (EXPECTED Zig ok), so its modules' type errors appear only when it is checked in some negatives' context; that is the error text the closure rule must attribute correctly.")
inl note_of_contract_rejection_diagnostics_27 () = Note ("CONTRACT-REJECTION-DIAGNOSTICS", 27u32, "2026-10-30T21:39Z", "compiler", "10-30 17:39 compiler: CLOSURE DRAFT (the n23 design) written and COMPILED. apps/compiler/packs/apply-host-related-diagnostics-closure.ps1 (host only, 4 anchors, dry-run ok on the live Program.fs; replaces n25's entry-only draft, which stays as a record). relatedClosure(inputPath) finds the entry's package.spiproj and walks the package graph with the core's own `schema` (so packageDir and its '..' default, './' names and the compiler dir resolve exactly as the compiler does), collecting every module file of every reachable package. compileOne hands that set to the router before each job (every host mode, not only --batch), and the router accepts an other-file diagnostic as 'related' only if its file is in the set. Box candidate crd2 built clean in 234 s (job-1793396087538). Gate queued for after the 18:00 timing slot: C:\\home\\data\\backlog-census\\box-crd2-gate.ps1, Zig contracts on the installed control vs crd2, SPIRAL_CC pinned. Pass line unchanged: 0 rows with a TypeErrors block from a file outside the row's closure; and since the census can't see closures, the 16 brzozowski rows from n26 get checked by hand: the 5 that import antimirov_typed_slot_bound keep their related block and the others lose it. Plus negative_beta_step_target_type_mismatch rerun alone.")
inl note_of_contract_rejection_diagnostics_28 () = Note ("CONTRACT-REJECTION-DIAGNOSTICS", 28u32, "2026-10-30T22:16Z", "compiler", "10-30 18:16 compiler: CLOSURE FIX PASSES its pre-registered gate. Box job job-1793397761450 (candidate crd2 = installed source + apply-host-related-diagnostics-closure.ps1, host 0325FE6A063C28DA; control = installed pack 8, host EEBCBFF8658A1EF9; test.ps1 -Suite contracts -Backend Zig, 620 rows each, SPIRAL_CC pinned). Rows with a TypeErrors block from a file outside their own sample dir: control 226, candidate 4. Read by hand, all 4 remaining are LEGITIMATE closure members: negative_antimirov_slot_shape, negative_forge_structural_support_bound, negative_raw_indexed_not_structural_support_bound and negative_raw_regex_not_positioned_bound each list antimirov_typed_slot_bound- in their package.spiproj, and the block comes from that package's injective_bound.spi. So foreign blocks outside the closure: 0 of 226. Rows whose compile/residual/oracle/baseline differ between arms: 0. The node's single WRONG-ERROR (n26, negative_beta_step_target_type_mismatch) did not recur on the quieter box, consistent with a load race, not the fix. READY FOR PACK 9: apps/compiler/packs/apply-host-related-diagnostics-closure.ps1 (host only, no residual can change). Pack-9 review rule: a -Suite all review with the residual set unchanged, then re-read every pinned negative whose detail loses a foreign block (any pin that passed only through leaked text must show up and be re-pinned on its own first error). n25's entry-only draft (apply-host-batch-foreign-entry-diagnostics.ps1) is superseded and stays as a record.")
inl note_of_contract_rejection_diagnostics_29 () = Note ("CONTRACT-REJECTION-DIAGNOSTICS", 29u32, "2026-10-31T18:36Z", "main", "10-31 14:36 main: INSTALLED. The closure fix (apps/compiler/packs/apply-host-related-diagnostics-closure.ps1, which passed its pre-registered gate 10-30: foreign TypeErrors blocks outside the closure 0 of 226, 0 arm differences) shipped in pack 10, installed and verified 14:27-14:30 (SpiralCompiler.dll 7827E9406813BB24 = the reviewed candidate). Done under installed-is-done; compiler re-creates its guard samples as a pack-10 follow-up.")
inl note_of_contract_rejection_diagnostics_30 () = Note ("CONTRACT-REJECTION-DIAGNOSTICS", 30u32, "2026-11-05T02:19Z", "fiscal", "2026-11-04 22:19 fiscal (star exchange 11-04 ask): another field case of 'BuildFile returned no code and no diagnostic arrived': node job-1793829209251-fiscal-decode-units, unit generated_gtve_v4_00 (1.6 MB generated Spiral, node at 100% load, compiler-process mode, pinned core 21F39A40A549E6E6). Cells then marks BOTH tests failed (one is a 'fails Unification failure' negative), so a silent compiler-process failure reads as a red until the raw output is opened. Ask: any diagnostic at that point (exit code / last stderr of the compiler process / memory at exit) so the gate can mark the cell VOID instead of failed. Its sibling gtve_v3_00 passed; a one-unit re-gate is queued (job-1793838041480).")
inl mutual_tail_loops () = Task ("MUTUAL-TAIL-LOOPS", "Native backends turn a strongly connected group of mutually tail-calling methods into one looping function with a state tag, as self tail calls already are; today they recurse on a 1 GB (Rust, Zig, Kotlin, Swift) or 256 MB (FPC) stack", Story, P0, D5, 100u32, Done, "PACK-7", "compiler,codegen,backends,main,wipe-redo", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_mutual_tail_loops () = Planning ("MUTUAL-TAIL-LOOPS", Minutes 360u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_mutual_tail_loops_1 () = Note ("MUTUAL-TAIL-LOOPS", 1u32, "2026-10-25T15:25Z", "main", "10-25 11:25 main: folded from apps/compiler/tmp/NEXT.md 'Open, in order' entry 5 (COMPILER-TMP-RETIRE step 6). Mentioned in BACKEND-COVERAGE-GAPS note 15 and KOTLIN-BACKEND note 1, but it had no item of its own. Probe first: a 3-method mutual tail recursion at depth 10^7 on each native backend, to show which still overflow without the big stack.")
inl note_of_mutual_tail_loops_2 () = Note ("MUTUAL-TAIL-LOOPS", 2u32, "2026-10-25T23:38Z", "compiler", "Current mechanism (spiral_compiler.fs): each native backend has its OWN `self_tail tag body` predicate (Rust ~165024,\nand the repeats at 165646/166362/166908/170858/... per backend). It tests whether a method's body tail-calls ITSELF\n(the same `tag`), descending through TyIf / TyUnionUnbox / TyIntSwitch / TyDo/TyIndent. When true (e.g. :165012,\n:165320, :166036, :166665, :170513), the backend emits the body as a LOOP (while(true)/continue, repeat, loop/br)\ninstead of a recursive self-call. So self-recursion is stack-free; MUTUAL recursion is not.\n\nGap: a strongly-connected group of methods that tail-call EACH OTHER (A->B->A, an SCC in the tail-call graph) is\nNOT merged -- each inter-method tail call is a real recursive call, so the group recurses and relies on the big\nstack (1 GB Rust/Zig/Kotlin/Swift, 256 MB FPC) to not overflow.\n\nDirection to implement (needs builds):\n1. SHARED analysis (core, once): build the tail-call graph among the method group and find SCCs whose inter-member\n   edges are all TAIL calls. This generalizes the per-backend `self_tail` (self-edge) to an SCC over members.\n2. Per-backend merged emission: emit each non-trivial SCC as ONE state-tagged looping function -- a selector tag\n   picks which member's body runs; a tail call to another member sets the selector and `continue`s (no recursion);\n   locals/params unify across members (a union/record of the members' parameter sets, as self-tail already copies\n   params into loop vars). Lands in each native backend's codegen (they aren't translated from each other).\n3. The existing single-method self-loop becomes the 1-member SCC case, so the two paths unify.\n\nProbe first (per the item, build-blocked under 100% CPU): a 3-method mutual tail recursion at depth 10^7 on each\nnative backend, to show which overflow today without the big stack (expected: all, since only self-tail loops).\nThen implement + gate with -Suite all -Native (oracle parity) and a shrunk stack to prove no overflow.\nEstimate D5/360m: this touches every native backend's method emission; the shared SCC analysis is the leverage.")
inl note_of_mutual_tail_loops_3 () = Note ("MUTUAL-TAIL-LOOPS", 3u32, "2026-10-26T15:58Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-26 (build-independent, probe-prep): checked the existing sample suite before authoring the probe. The samples whose names suggest SCC coverage -- native_managed_scc_tail_recursion and native_managed_array_scc3_tail_recursion -- are NOT multi-method mutual recursion: each is a SINGLE inl rec keep that tail-calls ITSELF with ROTATING arguments (left/right, or first/second/third via join keep (n-.1) second third first). self_tail already loops those. The \"scc\"/\"scc3\" naming refers to the rotating args + union phase, not a multi-METHOD SCC, so they do NOT cover this item's gap. Confirmed: no existing sample exercises >=2 DISTINCT methods in a tail-call SCC (A->B->A), so the probe is genuinely new, not a duplicate.\n\nIdiom learned for the probe: join <fn> <args> is the tail-call form; ~n keeps the counter dynamic (a static n would peval-unroll into EJP0040 runaway inline); operators =. -. %.; if/then/else. Existing self-tail samples run at depth 1e6; this item wants 1e7.\n\nDrafted a 3-method probe (phase_a->phase_b->phase_c->phase_a, acc, depth 1e7) at compiler scratchpad mutual-tail-loops-probe.spi, ready to drop into samples/native_mutual_tail_recursion when a slot frees. ONE syntax unknown: mutual-FUNCTION inl rec f ... and g ... is not shown in any sample (and appears only for union rec ... and union ... in recursive_union_mutual), so the probe's first build also confirms the compiler ACCEPTS mutual tail functions at all -- a prerequisite for the whole item; adjust the chainer if and is wrong for functions. The sample is a FAILING probe by design today (expected to overflow on every native backend, since only self-tail loops), so it is NOT a matrix row until the fix lands. Next build-step: build it, confirm it compiles + overflows at 1e7 across Rust/Zig/Kotlin/Swift (1GB) and FPC (256MB), then implement the shared SCC analysis + per-backend merged emission and re-gate with a shrunk stack.")
inl note_of_mutual_tail_loops_4 () = Note ("MUTUAL-TAIL-LOOPS", 4u32, "2026-10-26T20:39Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-26 (build-independent; CORRECTS note 3's \"no existing sample\" claim + resolves the syntax prerequisite + finds a free all-backend parity anchor). Grepped the .spi corpus for mutual-function recursion (^\\s*and \\w+).\n\nCORRECTION to note 3: a 2-METHOD mutual tail-call SCC sample ALREADY EXISTS and passes everywhere:\n  samples/frontier_hopacsota_mutual_pending/main.spi --\n    let rec is_even (n:i32):bool = if n=0 then true  else is_odd  (n-1)\n    and let is_odd  (n:i32):bool = if n=0 then false else is_even (n-1)\n  is_even tail-calls is_odd, is_odd tail-calls is_even = exactly the A->B->A tail SCC this item targets. Baseline EXPECTED.tsv: it runs compile=ok / native=ran / exit=0 on ALL 15 backends (Bend,C,Delphi,Fsharp,Gleam,Kotlin,Lean,Lua,OpenQasm,Python,Rust,Swift,TypeScript,Wasm,Zig) at n=10. So note 3's \"no existing sample exercises >=2 distinct methods in a tail-call SCC\" was WRONG -- it missed this one (it only checked the native_managed_scc* names). The gap is therefore NOT correctness (the SCC compiles+runs on every backend today) -- it is STACK USAGE AT DEPTH: today both members recurse (inter-method tail calls are real calls), so at high depth they overflow the 1GB/256MB stack; the fix makes the SCC loop.\n\nSYNTAX PREREQUISITE RESOLVED (note 3's one open unknown): mutual FUNCTIONS are accepted. Form: `let rec f ... and let g ...` (and `inl rec ... and inl ...`). Three samples use it: frontier_hopacsota_mutual_pending (let), gadt_growing_index_runtime:18 (`and inl lift`), mega_brzozowski_derivatives:1810/1817 (`and inl static_language_consume_right`/`star_closure`). So the drafted probe's `and`-chainer form is CORRECT; its first build no longer needs to confirm \"does the compiler accept mutual functions\" -- it does.\n\nPROBE SIMPLIFICATION: the minimal overflow probe is a HIGH-DEPTH (1e7) is_even/is_odd -- i.e. frontier_hopacsota_mutual_pending with ~n=1e7 instead of 10 -- cleaner than the drafted 3-method phase_a/b/c version. Keep the 3-method probe (scratchpad/mutual-tail-loops-probe.spi) as the RICHER case (it stresses the SCC selector with >2 members, exercising the merged-emission selector tag beyond a 2-way toggle), but the 2-method structure + syntax are already proven by the existing sample.\n\nFREE PARITY ANCHOR for the fix: because frontier_hopacsota_mutual_pending already has a green baseline row on all 15 backends, the SCC-merge fix has a ready-made all-backend regression anchor -- after the fix it must stay compile=ok/exit=0 on all 15 (now recursing, then looping). No NEW sample is needed to catch a correctness regression; only the high-depth variant is needed to prove no overflow under a shrunk stack.\n\nBONUS (MEGA-PARALLEL-JOIN-POINTS cross-link): mega_brzozowski_derivatives's mutual functions (static_language_consume_right/star_closure) are `inl`, so they inline into the call site (no JP) -- consistent with MEGA-PARALLEL note 2's finding that the mega samples lack join points by inl-authoring.\n\nSTILL BUILD-BOUND (unchanged): building the high-depth probe to confirm overflow on each native backend, then the shared SCC analysis + per-backend merged emission, then -Suite all -Native parity under a shrunk stack. This read removes the syntax risk and gives the fix an existing all-backend anchor.")
inl note_of_mutual_tail_loops_5 () = Note ("MUTUAL-TAIL-LOOPS", 5u32, "2026-10-27T09:27Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-27 build-independent design (hook points pinned by reading the codegen). The existing self-tail-loop machinery is an SCC-OF-1; mutual-tail just needs it generalized to an SCC-of-N. Read from the Rust codegen (same shape in every native backend's section -- C/Zig/Delphi/Lean/Wasm/Kotlin/Swift all have a `self_loop` mutable + the same `when (method (a,c)).tag = tag` guard):\n\n- self_loop decl: `let mutable self_loop : (int * L<Tag,Ty> []) option = None` (spiral_compiler.fs:164252 Rust; :165228 next backend; etc.) -- holds (current method tag, its params).\n- SET at method emission: `self_loop <- Some (x.tag, x.free_vars)` around the body (:164968-164978), cleared by without_self_loop inside nested constructs (:164253).\n- USED: `| TyJoinPoint(JPMethod(a,c),args) when d.IsNone && (match self_loop with Some (tag,_) -> (method (a,c)).tag = tag | None -> false) ->` (:164557) -- a tail call to the SAME method tag emits parallelMoves of the args into the params + `continue` (the loop), instead of a real `jp` call.\n\nWHY MUTUAL OVERFLOWS: for a↔b (is_even/is_odd), a's tail call to b has `(method b).tag <> a.tag`, so the :164557 guard FAILS -> it falls to the generic `| TyJoinPoint(JPMethod(a,c),args) -> ... jp ...` arm -> a REAL call. Each a->b->a is a stack frame -> overflow at high depth (the frontier_hopacsota_mutual_pending sample is is_even/is_odd, green at n=10 only because shallow; the probe is n=1e7).\n\nDESIGN (generalize SCC-of-1 -> SCC-of-N):\n(1) CORE (shared analysis, new pass): Tarjan's SCC over the tail-call graph of JPMethods (edge A->B when A tail-calls B in return position). A non-trivial SCC (>1 member, or a self-loop) is a merge group. This is a shared core pass (like the method/closure memo), computed once, keyed by method tag -> its SCC id + members.\n(2) PER-BACKEND emission: when emitting a method that heads a non-trivial SCC, emit the WHOLE SCC as ONE function with a dispatch loop: a `which : i32` discriminator selecting the member, unified params (the union of members' params, or a tagged payload), and `while true { match which { | 0 -> <memberA body> | 1 -> <memberB body> } }`. A tail call to an SCC member sets `which` + the params and `continue`; a tail call OUT of the SCC stays a real call; a non-tail call stays a call. self_loop generalizes from `Some (tag, params)` to `Some (sccId, members, dispatchVar, unifiedParams)`, and the :164557 guard becomes \"(method (a,c)).tag is in self_loop's SCC members\" -> set dispatchVar + parallelMoves + continue. The current self-tail case is the SCC-of-1 specialization (dispatchVar trivial).\n(3) Non-native backends that already trampoline (BEAM/Gleam/Lua tail calls) need no merge; scope the pass to the backends that emit real calls (C/Rust/Delphi/Zig/Lean/Wasm/Kotlin/Swift/F#).\n\nNEXT (build-bound): implement the SCC pass + the Rust merged-loop first (prove on a high-depth is_even/is_odd that currently overflows -> runs), then mirror per backend (the self_loop shape is identical across them, so the per-backend diff is mechanical once Rust is proven). Keep the byte-for-byte gate: an SCC-of-1 must emit EXACTLY the current self-tail loop (no churn on existing rows).")
inl note_of_mutual_tail_loops_6 () = Note ("MUTUAL-TAIL-LOOPS", 6u32, "2026-10-30T14:16Z", "compiler", "10-30 10:1x compiler, BACKLOG-DANGLING-1030 R3 (Active, no note since 10-27 09:27): Active -> Paused at 25%. Reason: nobody is on it today. The compiler team is on HOPACSOTA-P3 and the PACK-8 review. Resumable from the notes alone: note 4 names the anchor sample (frontier_hopacsota_mutual_pending, passes on all 15 backends) and note 5 pins the hook points (self_loop SCC-of-1 -> SCC-of-N). The scratch probe mutual-tail-loops-probe.spi was lost in the 10-28 wipe and was not recovered (checked 10-30 against C:\\home\\recovery\\wipe-20261028); the anchor sample replaces it. Next step when picked: an applier in apps/compiler/packs that generalizes self_loop on the Rust section, proven on the anchor sample.")
inl note_of_mutual_tail_loops_7 () = Note ("MUTUAL-TAIL-LOOPS", 7u32, "2026-10-31T04:35Z", "compiler", "10-31 00:34 compiler: DESIGN for the Rust half (read from codegenRust; no code yet, parked for the manifest-model pack). Facts: method bodies are emitted by the jp memo at first reference, and a record is memoized BEFORE its show runs, so a member reached by a tail call can be created and printed mid-emission without looping. Plan, one applier: (1) method_records: Dictionary<int, MethodRecFsharp> filled in method's f; emitted_groups: HashSet<int>; group_loop: (string * HashSet<int>) option beside self_loop, and without_self_loop clears both. (2) tail_callees body: the self_tail walk, collecting JPMethod tags in tail position. tail_group tag: the members that reach tag and that tag reaches over tail edges (BFS per member, sorted). (3) In method's show, when the group has more than one member, the fn becomes a wrapper 'group_<min>(Group<min>::M<tag>(v..))', and the first member shown emits 'enum Group<min> { M<t>(param types).. }' plus 'fn group_<min>(mut st: Group<min>) -> R { loop { match st { Group<min>::M<t>(mut v..) => { live_binds; self_loop <- Some t; binds body; unit: return (); } .. } } }'. (4) A new TyJoinPoint case BEFORE the self-tail case: a tail call to any member becomes 'st = Group<min>::M<tag>(values); continue;' (values via var, as the self case does), so self calls inside a group reassign st too. A group of one keeps today's loop. Anchor: frontier_hopacsota_mutual_pending (is_even/is_odd) emits one group. Regression sample: a deep mutual recursion that overflows a 1 GB stack today, in the Rust and C lists only, until the other native backends get the same treatment.")
inl note_of_mutual_tail_loops_8 () = Note ("MUTUAL-TAIL-LOOPS", 8u32, "2026-10-31T07:38Z", "compiler", "10-31 03:38 compiler: Rust half BUILT and SMOKED. packs/apply-rust-mutual-tail-loops.ps1 (3 anchors) -> candidate mutualtail, core 8F7F8374F7C6F0BD (495 s). frontier_hopacsota_mutual_pending (is_even/is_odd): exactly one group, emitted as 'enum Group0 { M0(i32), M1(i32) }' + 'fn group_0(mut st: Group0) -> bool { loop { match st { Group0::M0(mut v0) => { ... st = Group0::M1(v2); continue; } ... } } }', both fns one-line wrappers; rustc -O build exits 0 like the installed output. Deep sample (the same pair at n = 100,000,000; draft in my scratchpad, not in the repo): UNOPTIMIZED rustc, installed output -> 'thread has overflowed its stack' (exit 0xC00000FD); candidate -> exit 0. With -O the installed output also passes, because LLVM turns this simple pair into a sibling call itself, so a matrix sample built with -O would not discriminate. The change makes the loop a guarantee instead of an optimizer outcome, and the regression sample needs a shape LLVM cannot sibling-call (a pair holding an Rc local live across the call), proven on both builds before it lands. Next: the -Suite all -Native review on the C,Rust rows, AFTER pack 9 is installed and accepted (the review rewrites samples/ in place, and main's accept run needs the tree as is).")
inl note_of_mutual_tail_loops_9 () = Note ("MUTUAL-TAIL-LOOPS", 9u32, "2026-10-31T14:18Z", "compiler", "10-31 10-31 10:18 compiler: -O DISCRIMINATING SHAPE FOUND AND PROVEN. A mutual pair where each step allocates a one-case heap union from the runtime n and matches on it keeps an Rc local live ACROSS the tail call; its drop runs after the call, so LLVM can't sibling-call. Installed output (rustc -O, edition 2021): is_even_0 'let v1: Rc<UH0> = Rc::new(UH0::UH0_Cell(v0)); match &*v1 { ... is_odd_1(v4) }'. At n = 100,000,000: installed -> 'thread has overflowed its stack', exit 0xC00000FD after 33.5 s; candidate mutualtail (core 8F7F8374F7C6F0BD) -> 'enum Group0' + 'fn group_0' with 'st = Group0::M1(v4); continue', exit 0 in 50 s (loaded box). At n = 20,000,000 both exit 0 (installed 23.1 s, candidate 10.7 s): the -O frames are small, so overflow needs 20M-100M steps, too slow for a matrix row. REGRESSION SAMPLE PLAN: land this shape with a SMALL n (e.g. 1000) as samples/mutual_tail_rc_live; the matrix pins its Rust residual hash, so losing the group loop shows as a residual change in review; the deep run above is the one-off semantic proof, not a matrix row. Sample text (packages: |core- / modules: main): ':| ^ cell = / | Cell : i32 // :~ ^ is_even (n : i32) : bool = / := ~c = Cell n / ?= c ?| / | Cell m => / ? m = 0i32 ?> !+ / |: is_odd (m - 1i32) // :& :~ is_odd (n : i32) : bool = <same with !- and is_even> // := main () : i32 = / := ~n = 1000i32 / ? is_even n ?> 0i32 |: 1i32'. Next unchanged: re-dry-run apply-rust-mutual-tail-loops.ps1 on the PACK-10 tree, then its own -Suite all -Native review (C,Rust rows), with the sample added.")
inl note_of_mutual_tail_loops_10 () = Note ("MUTUAL-TAIL-LOOPS", 10u32, "2026-10-31T19:40Z", "compiler", "10-31 10-31 15:40 compiler: applier re-checked on the NEW live core (PACK-10 installed: 35,582 lines, gpt5parallel gone): packs/apply-rust-mutual-tail-loops.ps1 3/3 anchors 1x, and still 3/3 after PACK-11's two core appliers are stacked on a copy, so it can follow PACK-11 or ride with the next pack. Its review is a -Suite all -Native run that rewrites the Rust outputs, so it runs after PACK-11's predict job finishes (one test.ps1 at a time, mutex per cache). The other pending core appliers also anchor on PACK-10's core: delphi-arc-records 17/17, delphi-string-literal-index 1/1, hopacsota-p3-shard 8/8, hopacsota-p3-ivar 9/9 (deferred-validation stacks on p3keys, so it fails on the bare core by design).")
inl note_of_mutual_tail_loops_11 () = Note ("MUTUAL-TAIL-LOOPS", 11u32, "2026-10-31T20:02Z", "compiler", "10-31 10-31 16:01 compiler GROOM (user direction via /btw, 10-31 15:4x): the self-hosted rewrite (SELF-HOSTED-SPIRAL) starts only after EVERY backend is SOTA: no memory problems, no Rc slowdowns, no union hacks, no recursion limits, no missing arithmetic; GADT bugs and conversion gaps are 'unacceptable by now'; edition 3 finishes first; Gleam is a top test target (abyssal codebases) with F#, Rust and Wasm; Cells as fast as F# projects. This item is now on that gate (priority -> P0).")
inl note_of_mutual_tail_loops_12 () = Note ("MUTUAL-TAIL-LOOPS", 12u32, "2026-10-31T21:14Z", "compiler", "10-31 10-31 17:14 compiler: review STARTED (P0 gate item). Candidate mutualtail2 = live PACK-10 core + PACK-11's 2 core appliers + apply-rust-mutual-tail-loops.ps1 (host: PACK-11's 2): job-1793481212017-compiler-mutualtail-build (--heavy), then job-1793481217029-compiler-mutualtail-predict (after-ok), predict-residuals.ps1 -Candidate mutualtail2 -InstalledRun single-flight-20261031-154548 (PACK-11's own run), so P measures ONLY the mutual-tail change. PRE-REGISTERED reading: P must contain only Rust rows (codegenRust is the only edited emitter), each of which has at least one mutual tail group (fn group_<n> appears in the candidate's main.rs); compile-status diffs 0; verdict diffs 0. Then the native half: those P rows built with rustc and run, every exit and stdout agreeing with C, plus samples/mutual_tail_rc_live (n9's sample text, n = 1000) added with its Rust row. If P holds a non-Rust row, or a Rust row without a group, the applier is wrong and gets fixed before any native run.")
inl note_of_mutual_tail_loops_13 () = Note ("MUTUAL-TAIL-LOOPS", 13u32, "2026-10-31T21:36Z", "compiler", "10-31 10-31 17:36 compiler: candidate mutualtail2 BUILT (job-1793481212017, 669 s, heavy slot granted beside train6): == single-flight built in 577.2 s -> C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\spiral-bin-candidates\\mutualtail2\\cache\\bin\\single-flight\\SpiralCompiler\\Release\\net11.0\\SpiralCompiler.dll; SpiralCompiler.dll DFC86B91968D447D; SpiralCompilerCore.dll 458FCE37F10A1FCB; warnings: the same 7-line set as PACK-11 (FS0026,FS0046,FS0760), so the group-loop codegen adds none. Predict job-1793481217029 running (P vs PACK-11's run; pre-registered reading in n12). Next session: read it, then the native half.")
inl note_of_mutual_tail_loops_14 () = Note ("MUTUAL-TAIL-LOOPS", 14u32, "2026-10-31T22:06Z", "compiler", "10-31 10-31 18:06 compiler: REVIEW GATE MET (n12's pre-registered reading). Predict job-1793481217029 (mutualtail2 = PACK-10 + PACK-11 + apply-rust-mutual-tail-loops; core 458FCE37F10A1FCB) vs PACK-11's run single-flight-20261031-154548, 7,401 rows each, 0 timeouts: P = 3, ALL Rust (native_recursive_tail_recursion, frontier_hopacsota_mutual_pending, native_managed_scc_tail_recursion); compile-status diffs 0; verdict diffs = the same 3 (parity -> parity-residual-differs, the intended residual change); each of the 3 candidate main.rs has exactly one 'fn group_<n>' (Group1, Group0, Group1). NATIVE half on those 3: rustc -O vs gcc 15.2 -O2 of the committed main.c: all build, all exit 0, stdout identical 3/3. NOTE: that compile-only run wrote the candidate's output next to the sources, so those 3 samples' main.rs in the tree now hold the group-loop Rust (uncommitted) until it ships or an installed-compiler run rewrites them. NEXT: ship with the next compiler pack (PACK-12, or ride PACK-11 if main hasn't installed it yet) with samples/mutual_tail_rc_live (n9's text, n = 1000) added and the 3 + 1 Rust rows accepted at install.")
inl note_of_mutual_tail_loops_15 () = Note ("MUTUAL-TAIL-LOOPS", 15u32, "2026-11-01T03:41Z", "compiler", "10-31 10-31 23:41 compiler, cadence groom: TOP-3 CPU-LIGHT #2. Next concrete step: add samples/mutual_tail_rc_live (an Rc local live across the mutual call, n=1000 so the old code still passes at -O0 and the gate shows only the shape) with an inline expectation, then hand the group-loop applier plus the sample to the next compiler pack. Writing the sample is CPU-light; the pack build waits for a window.")
inl note_of_mutual_tail_loops_16 () = Note ("MUTUAL-TAIL-LOOPS", 16u32, "2026-11-01T06:09Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 02:09 compiler, round 3: SAMPLE ADDED (unstaged): apps/compiler/tmp/samples/mutual_tail_rc_live/{main.spi,package.spiproj}, n9's proven shape verbatim (one-case heap union 'cell' allocated from the runtime n in each step, so an Rc local stays live across the mutual tail call and LLVM can't sibling-call it), n = 1000, main returns 0 when is_even 1000 holds. That exit 0 is the inline expectation, checked against C. Installed formatter: 'format --check' exit 0 (parses, formatter-stable). No matrix.psd1 entry needed: C and Rust rows are default-on; the other backends' lists (Backends = @{...}) take it only after a measured run. It ships with apply-rust-mutual-tail-loops in PACK-12. Its first compile happens in BACKEND-BATCH's matrix pass on the stacked candidate: the Rust residual must contain 'fn group_0', and C must exit 0.")
inl note_of_mutual_tail_loops_17 () = Note ("MUTUAL-TAIL-LOOPS", 17u32, "2026-11-01T06:51Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 02:51 compiler: sample FIX: '=' / '-' -> '=.' / '-.' (the samples' core defines only the dotted forms; the first matrix pass showed ETY-UnboundVariable on all 4 rows). Re-proven: installed C + Rust compile; pack12a Rust has 1 group fn; native C and candidate Rust both exit 0.")
