inl note_of_mutual_tail_loops_19 () = Note ("MUTUAL-TAIL-LOOPS", 19u32, "2026-11-01T13:22Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 09:22 compiler: REGRESSION from this item's group loops, filed by fiscal as EDITION3-RUST-UNDECLARED-ASSIGN (P0): a method first emitted from inside a group body inherited group_loop and turned its tail call to a member into 'st = ...' (E0425). Fix in PACK-14 (group-loop-scope). Reopened as context only; the fix is tracked on that item.")
inl delphi_arc_memory () = Task ("DELPHI-ARC-MEMORY", "Delphi backend frees heap unions, mutable layouts and closures: FPC 3.2 classes are not reference-counted, so today they are never freed; candidates are interfaces (ARC) or array-of-record storage for mutable layouts", Story, P0, D5, 97u32, Active, "PACK-12", "team:compiler,compiler,codegen,delphi,memory,typefeatureset", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_delphi_arc_memory () = Planning ("DELPHI-ARC-MEMORY", Minutes 480u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_delphi_arc_memory_1 () = Note ("DELPHI-ARC-MEMORY", 1u32, "2026-10-25T15:25Z", "main", "10-25 11:25 main: folded from apps/compiler/tmp/NEXT.md 'Open, in order' entry 6 (COMPILER-TMP-RETIRE step 6); no backlog item had it. Measure first: peak RSS of the Delphi row of a long-running heap sample (mega_omniledger runtime_native) against its C row.")
inl note_of_delphi_arc_memory_2 () = Note ("DELPHI-ARC-MEMORY", 2u32, "2026-10-25T23:38Z", "compiler", "Root: codegenDelphi emits heap layouts, unions, mutable layouts and closures as FPC classes. FPC 3.2 classes are\nNOT reference-counted (unlike Delphi interfaces/strings/dynarrays), so without an explicit `.Free` they are never\nfreed -> the Delphi backend leaks every heap allocation for the process lifetime. The C backend, by contrast,\nfrees via `refc_prepass` (spiral_compiler.fs:175183), a BACKEND-AGNOSTIC lifetime analysis that computes incref/\ndecref points and emits a refcount field + `if (--x->refc == 0) free` (:175371-175375). Delphi emits none of that.\n\nMeasure first (main's note; build-blocked under 100% CPU): peak RSS of the Delphi row of a long-running heap\nsample (mega_omniledger_erp_kernel runtime_native) vs its C row -> quantify the leak before changing codegen.\n\nThree approaches (impl needs builds):\n  (a) Manual refc + Free, REUSING refc_prepass: Delphi consumes the SAME decref points C already computes, emits a\n      refcount field on each heap class + `.Free` at the decref points (the Pascal analogue of C's refc free). Most\n      consistent with C (identical lifetime semantics and the same cycle-leak profile the compiler already accepts\n      for C); most code, but the lifetime analysis is already written and backend-agnostic. RECOMMENDED.\n  (b) Interfaces (ARC): emit heap types as FPC interfaces, which ARE auto-refcounted (auto-free at zero refs).\n      Less hand-written code, but adds vtable/`IInterface` overhead and requires interface-shaping the types; same\n      cycle leak as any refcounting. Main lists this as a candidate.\n  (c) Array-of-record storage for mutable layouts specifically (value/pooled, no per-object heap): main's suggestion\n      for the mutable-layout case; sidesteps per-object free there.\nRecommendation: (a) for correctness+consistency (reuse refc_prepass); consider (b) if the vtable cost is acceptable\nand avoids hand-written refc fields; (c) only for mutable layouts if profiling says they dominate.\n\nGate (needs builds): Delphi-row peak RSS drops to ~C's on the heap sample, and -Suite all -Native stays oracle-parity\n(no double-free / use-after-free -> exit codes and stdout unchanged on every Delphi row). Seam: pack-5n.")
inl note_of_delphi_arc_memory_3 () = Note ("DELPHI-ARC-MEMORY", 3u32, "2026-10-26T16:04Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-26 (build-independent, de-risk approach (a)): CONFIRMED the load-bearing claim that refc_prepass is backend-agnostic. refc_prepass (spiral_compiler.fs ~175139) computes g_incr/g_decr purely from TypedBind[] via refc_used_vars + data_free_vars, no C-specific logic. Proof it is reusable: TWO backends already consume it and Delphi consumes it NOWHERE (only 2 call sites exist): C uses the FULL result (binds (refc_prepass Set.empty (Set args) x) s ... at ~175474); Python uses only .g_decr (~177834, drops refs at decref points). So approach (a) is a genuine reuse, not a hidden refactor -- wire Delphi binds_start like C (full result: incref at g_incr + decref->Free at g_decr), NOT Python's .g_decr-only.\n\nHEADS UP: note 2's line refs 175371-175375 are now STALE (that span is the C union-memo after main's repack). Live structural anchors to mirror: print_decref (~175328) emits `if (x!=NULL && --(x->refc)==0) { Body(x); free(x); }`; incref `->refc++` / `+= count` (~175592); refc init `=1` at the allocators; and CRITICALLY the recursive-union decref is ITERATIVE -- `while (x!=NULL && --(x->refc)==0)` (~176282) -- to free a long list without recursing.\n\nPascal translation + a Delphi WIN over the C template: FPC classes have virtual destructors/methods natively, so child cleanup rides an overridden `destructor Destroy; override;` (or virtual Decref) instead of C's hand-rolled decref_fptr table (176128) -- closures especially get this for free (C needs the fptr because the concrete type is erased behind Fun{i}). Keep a manual `refc: Integer` field (FPC 3.2 classes are NOT ARC). And the recursive-union Decref MUST be a Pascal `while` loop, not a recursive destructor, or it stack-overflows on exactly this item's target sample (mega_omniledger long heap chains) -- same big-stack hazard as MUTUAL-TAIL-LOOPS but in the free path.\n\nApproach (b) ARC (FPC interfaces) is also gate-viable (free has no observable side effect, so ARC's different free timing still gives identical exit/stdout and RSS drops either way), but needs interface-shaping every heap type; since (a)'s refc_prepass reuse is now confirmed cheap, (a) stays recommended. Full design: compiler scratchpad delphi-arc-memory-design.md. Impl + measure-first (mega_omniledger Delphi RSS vs C) still build-blocked; seam pack-5n.")
inl note_of_delphi_arc_memory_4 () = Note ("DELPHI-ARC-MEMORY", 4u32, "2026-10-27T01:00Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-26: confirmed the leak on a real output and found the item's named measure-first target is WRONG. recursive_union_list/main.pas: the recursive union TUH0 (self-ref c1_1: TUH0) is emitted as `TUH0 = class`, allocated via `TUH0.Create` (2 sites), with ZERO Free/Destroy/constructor and NO TInterfacedObject/IUnknown -> a plain FPC class, not ARC -> every allocation leaks for the process lifetime. Exactly the item's premise, proven. BUT the named target mega_omniledger_erp_kernel/runtime_native does NOT exercise it: its union TUS0 is a flat VALUE `record` (tag + all case fields inline, returned by value) and strings are AnsiString (FPC auto-reference-counted) -> 0 classes, 0 Create, nothing to leak. Measuring its Delphi RSS vs C would find ~no diff and mislead. CORRECTED measure-first target = a long-running sample that builds many RECURSIVE-UNION / CLOSURE / MUTABLE-LAYOUT instances (e.g. recursive_union_list in a loop). CENSUS TRAP for the measurement: FPC's default ctor is `.Create` WITHOUT parens, so count `.Create` not `.Create(` (the paren form finds 0). LEAK IS UNIFORM across all three construct types (re-censused with `.Create` no-paren): native_closure_return (closures) 6 class / 2 Create / 0 Free; native_layout_mutable (mutable) 2 / 1 / 0; recursive_union_managed 2 / 2 / 0; native_closure_union_rec 5 / 3 / 0; native_managed_union_record_closure 3 / 1 / 0. Every class-emitting output = Create with zero Free -> the fix must cover recursive/heap unions + closures + mutable layouts. Approach (a): refc:Integer field + Free via refc_prepass decref points; virtual destructor on the class (rides the FPC vtable, no decref_fptr table); refc-init hooks the `TUH0.Create` sites; recursive-union Decref a `while` loop not a recursive destructor (else stack-overflow). Impl + the corrected RSS measurement still build-bound.")
inl note_of_delphi_arc_memory_5 () = Note ("DELPHI-ARC-MEMORY", 5u32, "2026-10-27T09:30Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-27 CORRECTION (read the core) to the earlier \"refc_prepass reuse is backend-agnostic\" claim. It is an OVER-CLAIM. refc_prepass is defined at spiral_compiler.fs:175139 and called only at :175474, inside CodegenC's binds_start; it feeds C-specific decref emissions (USDecref/UHDecref/ArrayDecref/StringDecref/HeapDecref/MutDecref/ClosureDecref, :175602-176253). It is defined AFTER codegenDelphi (~:165181-165602), so Delphi cannot reference it as-is. Consequences for approach (a): (1) the decref EMISSION is C-only -- Delphi must emit its own (TUH0.Free at the decref points, virtual destructor on the class). (2) the decref-POINT ANALYSIS (the RefcDecrTable / last-use ownership that refc_prepass computes) is conceptually backend-agnostic, but it is CURRENTLY CodegenC-local, not a shared pass -- whether it is reachable from Delphi's scope is UNVERIFIED. So the real work is EITHER factor that ownership/last-use analysis out of CodegenC into a SHARED pass before every backend (then Delphi emits TUH0.Free at the shared points), OR give Delphi its own refc pass. approach (a)'s shape (refc:Integer field + Free + a while-loop Decref for recursive unions to avoid stack overflow) is unchanged; just not a free reuse of refc_prepass. Build-bound to implement; this is the de-risk the earlier note got wrong.")
inl note_of_delphi_arc_memory_6 () = Note ("DELPHI-ARC-MEMORY", 6u32, "2026-10-28T14:52Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-28 (build-free read; corrects stale line refs + sharpens approach (a)). Real locations: refc_prepass is spiral_compiler.fs:175302 (note 2 cited :175183 -- STALE), a BACKEND-AGNOSTIC liveness pass over TypedBind [] (decref_vars = increfed_vars - used_vars; add_cd g_decr). C frees via print_decref :175491 (\"if (x != NULL && --(x->refc) == 0) { <decref children>(x); free(x); }\"), increfs at :175755 (\"(v)->refc++\"). So approach (a) [reuse refc_prepass + Free] is VIABLE -- but with a cost the item did not name: refc_prepass sits at :175302, AFTER codegenDelphi (:165290), and F# is top-down, so codegenDelphi cannot call it today. Approach (a) must first HOIST refc_prepass (and the incref/decref-point computation) to a shared scope BEFORE :165290, then codegenDelphi consumes the SAME decref points C does.\nRECOMMENDATION: approach (a), most C-consistent (identical lifetime semantics + the same cycle-leak profile the compiler already accepts): (1) hoist refc_prepass to shared; (2) codegenDelphi emits a `refc: LongInt` field on each heap class (TUH*/mutable layout/closure), `x.refc := x.refc + 1` at incref points, and a per-class decref proc `if x <> nil then begin x.refc := x.refc - 1; if x.refc = 0 then begin <decref each managed field>; x.Free end end` at the decref points (the Pascal analogue of print_decref). Approach (b) interfaces/ARC = FPC auto-refcounts but changes the class->interface representation everywhere (bigger, and cycles still leak); (c) array-of-record only helps mutable layouts. MEASURE FIRST (main note 1, build-bound): peak RSS of the Delphi row of mega_omniledger_erp_kernel runtime_native vs its C row -> quantify the leak before coding. Impl is build-bound; this is the design.")
inl note_of_delphi_arc_memory_7 () = Note ("DELPHI-ARC-MEMORY", 7u32, "2026-10-31T02:49Z", "compiler", "10-30 22:49 compiler: a FOURTH approach, MEASURED, and cheaper than the n4 recommendation (a). (d) FPC managed records: wrap each heap class in a record with management operators (Initialize/Finalize/AddRef/Copy, FPC 3.2+; pinned trunk 3.3.1-20261025), so FPC itself inserts every incref/decref at copies, parameter passing, field stores, dynamic-array slots and scope exit. Codegen needs no liveness pass and no hoisting of refc_prepass: (1) per heap type TUHn/THeapn/TMutn/TFunn a wrapper record RUHn { p : TUHn } plus a refc field on the class; (2) tyv returns the wrapper name; (3) constructors wrap (Result.p := TUHn.Create(...)); (4) field and case access go through .p. Probes (scratchpad fpc-arc, -O2 -Mdelphi -Px86_64, the generated header incl. {$MAXSTACKSIZE $10000000} and NOREGVAR): arc.pas (locals, copies, a dynamic array of wrappers, a slot overwritten) -> live 4 in scope, 0 after, exit 0; POSITIVE CONTROL leak.pas (Finalize does not release) -> 'after live=4', exit 1, so the check sees leaks; chain.pas (a 100,000-deep recursive list whose CLASS holds the wrapper as a field, a shared tail kept by a second wrapper, the old head replaced) -> live 100000 in scope, 0 after, exit 0, 257 ms. Without the generated 256 MB stack the cascading free of 100k nodes overflows (runtime error 202), and C's recursive UHDecref has the same shape, so the generated header must stay. Semantics vs C: same cycle-leak profile (refcounts); frees happen at FPC's scope/overwrite points rather than at C's exact last use, so peak memory can be higher, never a leak. Costs to measure in the pack: AddRef/Finalize per by-value parameter (emit 'const' parameters where the callee never stores, or accept it), and the try/finally frames FPC adds to functions with managed locals. Proposed next step (decision before code): adopt (d) over (a); gate = the Delphi rows of -Suite examples -Native agree with C as today, plus a leak counter sample (recursive_union_list built with a live-object counter, exit 0 only when every allocation is freed; a positive control build without Finalize must fail).")
inl note_of_delphi_arc_memory_8 () = Note ("DELPHI-ARC-MEMORY", 8u32, "2026-10-31T03:49Z", "compiler", "10-30 23:49 compiler: DECIDED (d), written before the build; applier drafted. One GENERIC managed record replaces per-type wrappers: TSpiralRc = class refc end; TRc<T: class> = record p: T + Initialize/Finalize/AddRef/Copy/Equal/NotEqual + static Own(x) (refc := 1). Probe generic.pas, with the generated header: forward-declared classes, a recursive union whose class holds a field of its OWN wrapper, a closure subclass capturing a wrapper, and a traversal that reassigns a wrapper variable. Result: live 100002 in scope, 0 after, exit 0, 410 ms. The constraint must be 'T: class': 'T: TSpiralRc' is rejected for forward-declared classes ('Incompatible types: got TUH0 expected TSpiralRc'), so the operators cast to TSpiralRc. Applier packs/apply-delphi-arc-records.ps1 (17 anchors, dry-run 1x each on the live core): tyv -> RUHn / RMutn / RFunn; heap classes derive from TSpiralRc; wrapper aliases 'RUHn = TRc<TUHn>' go between forwards and types; constructors return the wrapper via Own; heap union tag/case fields, mutable layout fields and closure Invoke dereference through .p (stack unions and THeap records are unchanged); shared nullary heap cases are global wrappers; the prelude is emitted only when a wrapper exists, so programs without heap classes keep their bytes. Gate tool C:\\home\\data\\backlog-census\\delphi-heaptrc.ps1: for the 133 samples whose committed main.pas declares a class, build with FPC -gh (heaptrc) and record exit, stdout hash and unfreed blocks. BEFORE = committed outputs (running now); AFTER = candidate 'delphiarc' (build queued). Pass: every row keeps its exit and stdout hash, and unfreed falls to 0 except for reference cycles (named and counted). Baseline datum: recursive_union_list leaks 4 blocks / 128 B today.")
inl note_of_delphi_arc_memory_9 () = Note ("DELPHI-ARC-MEMORY", 9u32, "2026-10-31T05:26Z", "compiler", "10-31 01:26 compiler: GATE MET on the leak sweep. Candidate delphiarc (live core + packs/apply-delphi-arc-records.ps1, core D2DEAE68BDA834CE). Tool C:\\home\\data\\backlog-census\\delphi-heaptrc.ps1 over the 133 samples whose committed main.pas declares a class: FPC 3.3.1 -O2 -gh, unfreed blocks read from HEAPTRC=log=<file>. (FPC's 256-byte StdErr buffer truncates heaptrc's own summary when stderr is a file, so the first capture lost the zero rows; the log file fixes that.) BEFORE (committed outputs = the installed compiler): all 133 leak, 836,198 unfreed blocks in total (mega_brzozowski runtime_bench 714,576; indexed_dfa_state_cardinality 120,591). AFTER (candidate): 133 of 133 build and run with the SAME exit code and the SAME stdout hash, and 0 unfreed blocks in every row. The one row the sweep first listed as a compile error was the tool's fault: native_layout_mutable's entry is main.spir, and the tool assumed main.spi (now fixed); rerun by hand, exit 7 as before, 0 unfreed. No reference cycle showed up as a leak in this corpus. Next: the matrix review rows for Delphi (-Suite all -Native: the Delphi residuals change by design, every Delphi native row must keep agreeing with C) in the pack after pack 9, plus a run-time comparison on the mega rows (FPC adds try/finally frames for managed locals).")
inl note_of_delphi_arc_memory_10 () = Note ("DELPHI-ARC-MEMORY", 10u32, "2026-10-31T05:30Z", "compiler", "10-31 01:30 compiler: COST measured, so not adoption-ready yet. mega_brzozowski runtime_bench (the 714k-leak row), FPC -O2 without heaptrc, alternating rounds on a box at 100%: leaky Delphi (committed) median 0.49-1.36 s, ARC candidate 2.19-3.32 s (about 3-5x slower), C oracle (also refcounting and freeing) 1.77 s. So ARC Delphi is about 1.9x C on this row. Part of the gap is inherent (the old program never freed anything). Marking the TRc<T> operators and SpiralRelease 'inline' with {$inline on} did NOT help (3.74 s, within the noise). Next lever, to measure before adopting: by-value parameters of wrapper type cost an AddRef at every call and a Finalize at return. Emit wrapper-typed parameters as 'const' in every function that never assigns them; only self-loop functions (parallel moves) and macro assignments write parameters, and codegenDelphi knows both. Adoption bar: runtime_bench within 1.3x of C, and the leak gate (133/133, 0 unfreed) still holding.")
inl note_of_delphi_arc_memory_11 () = Note ("DELPHI-ARC-MEMORY", 11u32, "2026-10-31T05:34Z", "compiler", "10-31 01:34 compiler: the 'const parameters' lever MEASURED and REJECTED. Hand experiment on runtime_bench's generated main.pas: every wrapper-typed parameter made const, then reverted in the 31 functions FPC flags as assigning one (the self-loop functions), with forwards synced (11). It compiles and exits 0, but medians are const 3.23 s vs plain ARC 2.87 s vs C 0.64 s (the box swung C itself from 1.77 to 0.64 s between runs, so only large ratios mean anything). Neither inline operators nor const params move the 3-5x gap to C, so the cost is not the call overhead of AddRef/Copy. Leading suspect: RTTI-driven finalization. TObject.Free -> CleanupInstance finalizes the class's managed fields through the type's init table, and a wrapper inside a dynamic array or record is finalized the same way; C's explicit UHDecref has neither cost. DECISION POINT for the lane (not taken tonight): either (a') keep (d)'s correctness and cut RTTI by emitting an explicit destructor per heap class that releases its wrapper fields and nils them before inherited (a profile on a quiet box must first confirm CleanupInstance dominates), or (a) emit C-style decrefs from refc_prepass. Measure on a quiet box with a sampling profile before either.")
inl note_of_delphi_arc_memory_12 () = Note ("DELPHI-ARC-MEMORY", 12u32, "2026-10-31T14:28Z", "compiler", "10-31 10-31 10:28 compiler: DECISION POINT RESOLVED BY MEASUREMENT: option (a), C-style explicit refcounting from refc_prepass; (d)'s managed wrapper can't reach the 1.3x-of-C bar. (1) Allocator ruled out: runtime_bench's ARC output (candidate delphiarc) built twice with FPC 3.3.1 -O2, plain vs 'uses cmem' (C malloc instead of FPC's heap manager), 5 alternating rounds with the C oracle on a loaded box: arc median 4.73 s, arc+cmem 4.76 s, C 1.29 s. (2) Microbench, 50M pass-and-return round trips 'r := pass(r)' of one object, 3 runs: TRc<T> (the exact generated record with Initialize/Finalize/AddRef/Copy) 14.8-15.1 s, i.e. about 300 ns per op; a COM interface (TInterfacedObject, compiler-managed) 13.0-15.5 s, i.e. about 280 ns; a raw class pointer with explicit Inc/Dec of refc (what C does) 0.48-0.78 s, i.e. about 10 ns. ANY compiler-managed refcount in FPC (management-operator records AND interfaces) costs about 30x explicit counting per pass, so swapping the wrapper type or adding destructors can't close the gap; with RTTI in the helpers ruled in rather than out, (a') is dropped too. NEXT: rewrite codegenDelphi's heap ownership the way CodegenC does it: plain class references (TUH<n> = class(TSpiralRc)), explicit refc increments and SpiralRelease calls placed from refc_prepass, so the decref points are the oracle's and the leak gate (packs/delphi-heaptrc.ps1, 133/133, 0 unfreed) proves it. apply-delphi-arc-records.ps1 stays the correctness reference (same stdout hashes) and should not ship.")
inl note_of_delphi_arc_memory_13 () = Note ("DELPHI-ARC-MEMORY", 13u32, "2026-10-31T14:46Z", "compiler", "10-31 10-31 10:46 compiler: one cheaper lever tested and REJECTED: {$IMPLICITEXCEPTIONS OFF} (drops the implicit try/finally frame FPC wraps around every function with managed locals; safe for generated programs, which Halt instead of raising). Same microbench, 3 paired runs: default 12.75-15.0 s, with the directive 10.97-14.5 s, i.e. 10-15%, so the frames aren't the cost. The remaining ~250 ns per pass-and-return is FPC's management dispatch (fpc_initialize / fpc_copy / fpc_finalize are generic RTTI-driven helpers that look up the record's management operators; one pass-and-return makes several of those calls), and no compiler directive removes it. Option (a) stands. SCOPE for (a), so the next session sizes it right: CodegenC's model is refc_prepass (g_incr / g_decr / g_op / g_op_decr) plus refc_change'' per type (UHeap, UStack with fields, Heap, HeapMutable, arrays with element decref, closures via decref_fptr, strings) and the self-loop release paths (self_let_tail, kept_let_tail, self_continue_release). On Delphi: AnsiString stays FPC-managed (its refcount is a direct helper, not RTTI, and it was fine before ARC); dynamic arrays of heap elements need an explicit owner class (FPC frees a dynamic array without releasing raw class elements), so arrays change representation too. That is a multi-session port (estimate 6-8 h of drafting plus build cycles); re-estimating the item from 300m.")
inl note_of_delphi_arc_memory_14 () = Note ("DELPHI-ARC-MEMORY", 14u32, "2026-10-31T17:32Z", "compiler", "10-31 10-31 13:32 compiler: OPTION (a) DESIGN, mapped from PACK-10's codegenDelphi (14588-15010) against CodegenC's ownership model (refc_prepass 24607, binds/refc_prefix 24960-25084, decref emitters 25533-25873). SHAPES: TUH<n> (heap union), THeap<n>, TMut<n> and the closure classes T<name>(TFun<n>) are all classes, so all four inherit TSpiralRc = class refc: LongInt; procedure DropFields; virtual; end. Each class overrides DropFields to release its own heap-typed fields, so one non-generic SpiralRelease(o) (Dec refc; at 0: o.DropFields; o.Free) serves every shape through one virtual call, no RTTI. TUS<n> stack unions stay records; a record with heap fields gets USIncref<n>/USRelease<n>(var x) helpers (CodegenC's USIncref/USDecref). AnsiString stays FPC-managed (direct helpers, it was fine pre-ARC). Dynamic arrays: FPC frees an 'array of' without releasing raw class elements, so an array whose element type holds heap values becomes TArr<n> = class(TSpiralRc) data: array of T, with DropFields releasing the elements (CodegenC's ArrayDecrefBody); arrays of primitives stay plain 'array of'. EMISSION: binds_start runs refc_prepass Set.empty (Set params) exactly as CodegenC (the callee owns its parameters). A Delphi refc_prefix before each statement turns g_incr/g_op (+n) into 'Inc(vX.refc, n)' (or USIncref) and g_decr/g_op_decr into 'SpiralRelease(vX)' (or USRelease); TyLet index results (LayoutIndex*, ArrayIndex) are borrowed, so they arrive in g_incr; call/constructor results are owned. Self-loop: the parallel-move 'Continue' path must emit the last statement's releases first, mirroring CodegenC's self_let_tail / self_continue_release. ORDER OF WORK: (1) TSpiralRc + DropFields + SpiralRelease emitters per shape; (2) refc_prefix in binds; (3) self-loop releases; (4) TArr<n> for heap-element arrays (the riskiest representation change, last). GATES: packs/delphi-heaptrc.ps1 (133/133 build+run with the SAME exit and stdout hash, 0 unfreed), runtime_bench within 1.3x of C, then -Suite all -Native Delphi rows agreeing with C.")
inl note_of_delphi_arc_memory_15 () = Note ("DELPHI-ARC-MEMORY", 15u32, "2026-10-31T20:02Z", "compiler", "10-31 10-31 16:01 compiler GROOM (user direction via /btw, 10-31 15:4x): the self-hosted rewrite (SELF-HOSTED-SPIRAL) starts only after EVERY backend is SOTA: no memory problems, no Rc slowdowns, no union hacks, no recursion limits, no missing arithmetic; GADT bugs and conversion gaps are 'unacceptable by now'; edition 3 finishes first; Gleam is a top test target (abyssal codebases) with F#, Rust and Wasm; Cells as fast as F# projects. This item is now on that gate (priority -> P0).")
inl note_of_delphi_arc_memory_16 () = Note ("DELPHI-ARC-MEMORY", 16u32, "2026-11-01T04:34Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 00:34 compiler, round-2 groom (user 00:2x: backend codegen is CPU-light text work, write it while runs batch): NEXT CPU-LIGHT EDIT: option (a) step 1 as an applier: TSpiralRc base (refc + virtual DropFields) and the SpiralRelease helper, used only by TUH<n> heap unions first; mutable layouts and closures follow in steps 2-3. Gate: delphi-heaptrc.ps1 leak count must fall and outputs stay equal to C (one batched run).")
inl note_of_delphi_arc_memory_17 () = Note ("DELPHI-ARC-MEMORY", 17u32, "2026-11-01T06:25Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 02:25 compiler, round 3: STEP 1 ANCHORS mapped on core C0990B2D590CE965 (codegenDelphi). All four heap shapes come from three memo emitters: union_type (14976-14993: 'TUH<tag> = class tag: LongInt; <c_k fields> end' at 14983, creators at 14993, and the nullary SHARED singleton '<prefix>_<case>_shared' at 14991), layout 'Mut' (14970-14972: 'TMut<tag> = class <l fields> end' + MutCreate), and fun_type (14940-14945: abstract 'TFun<tag> = class Invoke...'; closure subclasses elsewhere). STEP 1 EDIT (text, no behaviour change, so outputs must stay byte-equal in runs): (a) one prelude type 'TSpiralRc = class refc: LongInt; procedure DropFields; virtual; end;' + 'procedure SpiralRelease(o: TSpiralRc)' (Dec refc; at 0: DropFields; Free) + 'SpiralRetain' (Inc); (b) TUH/TMut/TFun declare 'class(TSpiralRc)'; (c) each TUH/TMut overrides DropFields, releasing exactly its fields whose tyv is a TUH/TMut/TFun class (the field list is already in hand at 14982/14962); (d) the shared nullary singletons get refc := High(LongInt) div 2 at creation, so a release never frees them. STEP 2 (the real win, separate pack): retain/release at binds, mirroring CodegenC's refc_prepass (24607) and decref emitters (25533-25873); gate = delphi-heaptrc.ps1 leak count down, stdout equal to C. Step 1 alone is safe to ship (byte-equal stdout, a different .pas), which settles the class-hierarchy question before step 2's bulk.")
inl note_of_delphi_arc_memory_18 () = Note ("DELPHI-ARC-MEMORY", 18u32, "2026-11-01T06:28Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 02:28 compiler: STEP 1 DRAFTED as a kit do program (6 replacements, dry-applied on C0990B2D590CE965; stacks cleanly after no-respell + Gleam math + ord). Emits TSpiralRc (refc + virtual DropFields) when any class type exists; TUH/TMut/TFun derive from it; TUH/TMut override DropFields to release exactly their class-typed fields (tyv starts with TUH/TMut/TFun); SpiralRetain/SpiralRelease prelude procedures come before headers. Convention: refc = extra owners, so a fresh object (refc 0) frees on its first release ('if refc < 0'), and shared nullary singletons get refc High(LongInt) div 2, so they're never freed. NOTHING calls Retain/Release yet, so stdout must be byte-equal and only the .pas text changes. Proof (BACKEND-BATCH, Delphi rows): every Delphi row still builds under FPC and agrees with C. Known gap for step 2: class fields inside TUS stack-union records and TTuple records aren't released by any DropFields yet.\nPROGRAM (kit do <apps/compiler> -):\nedit spiral_compiler.fs expect files 1 replacements 6\n  replace once <<<\n        let forwards = ResizeArray()\n        let types = ResizeArray()\n        let headers = ResizeArray()\n        let bodies = ResizeArray()\n        let globals = ResizeArray()\n  >>> with <<<\n        let forwards = ResizeArray()\n        let types = ResizeArray()\n        let headers = ResizeArray()\n        let bodies = ResizeArray()\n        let globals = ResizeArray()\n        let rcClass (ty : string) = ty.StartsWith \"TUH\" || ty.StartsWith \"TMut\" || ty.StartsWith \"TFun\"\n        let dropDecl (owned : string []) = if owned.Length = 0 then \"\" else \" procedure DropFields; override;\"\n        let dropBody (name : string) (owned : string []) =\n            if owned.Length > 0 then\n                let releases = owned |> Array.map (fun field -> $\"SpiralRelease({field});\") |> String.concat \" \"\n                bodies.Add($\"procedure {name}.DropFields;\\nbegin\\n  {releases}\\nend;\")\n  >>>\n  replace once <<<\n            types.Add($\"  TFun{tag} = class\\n    {decl}\\n  end;\"))\n  >>> with <<<\n            types.Add($\"  TFun{tag} = class(TSpiralRc)\\n    {decl}\\n  end;\"))\n  >>>\n  replace once <<<\n                    types.Add($\"  TMut{r.tag} = class {fields} end;\")\n  >>> with <<<\n                    let owned = r.free_vars |> Array.choose (fun (L(i,t)) -> if rcClass (tyv t) then Some $\"l{i}\" else None)\n                    types.Add($\"  TMut{r.tag} = class(TSpiralRc) {fields}{dropDecl owned} end;\")\n                    dropBody $\"TMut{r.tag}\" owned\n  >>>\n  replace once <<<\n            types.Add(if heap then $\"  {name} = class tag: LongInt; {fields} end;\" else $\"  {name} = record tag: LongInt; {fields} end;\")\n  >>> with <<<\n            let owned = cases |> Array.mapi (fun c vars -> vars |> Array.mapi (fun f (L(_,t)) -> if rcClass (tyv t) then Some $\"c{c}_{f}\" else None)) |> Array.concat |> Array.choose id\n            types.Add(if heap then $\"  {name} = class(TSpiralRc) tag: LongInt; {fields}{dropDecl owned} end;\" else $\"  {name} = record tag: LongInt; {fields} end;\")\n            if heap then dropBody name owned\n  >>>\n  replace once <<<\n                    bodies.Add($\"var {shared}: {name} = nil;\\nfunction {prefix}_{suffixes.[c]}: {name};\\nbegin\\n  if {shared} = nil then begin {shared} := {name}.Create; {shared}.tag := {c}; end;\\n  Result := {shared};\\nend;\")\n  >>> with <<<\n                    bodies.Add($\"var {shared}: {name} = nil;\\nfunction {prefix}_{suffixes.[c]}: {name};\\nbegin\\n  if {shared} = nil then begin {shared} := {name}.Create; {shared}.tag := {c}; {shared}.refc := High(LongInt) div 2; end;\\n  Result := {shared};\\nend;\")\n  >>>\n  replace once <<<\n        if forwards.Count > 0 || types.Count > 0 then\n            program.AppendLine(\"type\") |> ignore\n            forwards |> Seq.iter (fun x -> program.AppendLine(x) |> ignore)\n            types |> Seq.iter (fun x -> program.AppendLine(x) |> ignore)\n        env.globals |> Seq.filter (is_uses >> not) |> Seq.iter (fun (x : string) -> program.AppendLine(x) |> ignore)\n        globals |> Seq.iter (fun (x : string) -> program.AppendLine(x) |> ignore)\n        headers |> Seq.iter (fun x -> program.AppendLine(x) |> ignore)\n  >>> with <<<\n        if forwards.Count > 0 || types.Count > 0 then\n            program.AppendLine(\"type\") |> ignore\n            if forwards.Count > 0 then program.AppendLine(\"  TSpiralRc = class refc: LongInt; procedure DropFields; virtual; end;\") |> ignore\n            forwards |> Seq.iter (fun x -> program.AppendLine(x) |> ignore)\n            types |> Seq.iter (fun x -> program.AppendLine(x) |> ignore)\n        env.globals |> Seq.filter (is_uses >> not) |> Seq.iter (fun (x : string) -> program.AppendLine(x) |> ignore)\n        globals |> Seq.iter (fun (x : string) -> program.AppendLine(x) |> ignore)\n        if forwards.Count > 0 then\n            program\n                .AppendLine(\"procedure TSpiralRc.DropFields;\\nbegin\\nend;\")\n                .AppendLine(\"procedure SpiralRetain(o: TSpiralRc);\\nbegin\\n  if o <> nil then Inc(o.refc);\\nend;\")\n                .AppendLine(\"procedure SpiralRelease(o: TSpiralRc);\\nbegin\\n  if o <> nil then begin Dec(o.refc); if o.refc < 0 then begin o.DropFields; o.Free; end; end;\\nend;\") |> ignore\n        headers |> Seq.iter (fun x -> program.AppendLine(x) |> ignore)\n  >>>")
inl note_of_delphi_arc_memory_19 () = Note ("DELPHI-ARC-MEMORY", 19u32, "2026-11-01T07:24Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 03:23 compiler: step 1 SHIPPED in PACK-12 (TSpiralRc hierarchy, DropFields, pinned singletons; 133 Delphi rows build under FPC and agree with C). Next: step 2, retain/release at binds.")
inl note_of_delphi_arc_memory_20 () = Note ("DELPHI-ARC-MEMORY", 20u32, "2026-11-01T12:34Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 08:34 compiler, ROUND 4 QUALITY SPEC: FIELDS: P0 gate (memory), est 480m left (step 2), load medium, dep PACK-12 (step 1 installed). DEFINITION OF DONE: apps/compiler/packs/delphi-heaptrc.ps1 over the Delphi matrix list reports 0 unfreed blocks on every row (today: leaks on every row with heap unions/mut/closures), and every Delphi row still agrees with C (predict + native, 0 DIFFERS beyond the known CRLF note). NEXT STEP: map the retain/release insertion points by reading CodegenC's refc_prepass (spiral_compiler.fs ~24607) and decref emitters (25533-25873), and write the Delphi counterparts as a kit do program for PACK-14. TYPEFEATURESET PLAN, REPLACING step 1's string test (rcClass = ty.StartsWith 'TUH'|'TMut'|'TFun' is stringly and will misfire on a user type named TUHx): ownership is decided from the Ty, via a UNION 'RcShape = HeapUnion of tag | MutLayout of tag | Closure of tag' computed by a total match on YUnion(heap)/YLayout(HeapMutable)/YFun, with 'None' for value types; field ownership is a union 'Owned | Borrowed' carried with each emitted field, so DropFields releases exactly the Owned ones; no '-1' refc sentinel: pinned singletons are a separate case 'Pinned' in the release path, not 'High(LongInt) div 2' (that magic number goes in step 2).")
inl note_of_delphi_arc_memory_21 () = Note ("DELPHI-ARC-MEMORY", 21u32, "2026-11-03T07:30Z", "compiler", "11-03 07:3x compiler GROOM (WIP cap: Active = touched in 12 h). Parked to Planned (43 h). Next: step 2, delphi-heaptrc over the Delphi matrix list on installed pack23, to list which rows still leak.")
inl note_of_delphi_arc_memory_22 () = Note ("DELPHI-ARC-MEMORY", 22u32, "2026-11-04T10:04Z", "compiler", "11-04 06:1x compiler: STEP 2 MAPPED (code read) and MEASUREMENT QUEUED (node job compiler-heaptrc: delphi-heaptrc.ps1 over every main.pas sample on installed pack34, summarizing leaking vs clean rows). Findings: (1) refc_prepass' (spiral_compiler.fs ~25434) works on TypedBind only and returns {g_incr, g_op, g_decr, g_op_decr} per bind, so Pascal can reuse it unchanged; no new liveness analysis is needed. (2) Pascal already has the class side from step 1: TSpiralRc base, the TMut/TFun/heap-union classes deriving from it, and dropDecl releasing owned fields (~15632-15673). (3) The consumer pattern to copy: CodegenC binds (25779-25843: incr0/decr0/op_decr0 around each bind) for the full scheme, and the Python backend (28147-28263: binds_start passes g_decr; after each statement it emits 'del' for the vars whose last use it was) as the minimal one. PLAN for the program: in codegenPascal's binds, emit SpiralRetain(v) for g_incr/g_op vars of a TSpiralRc type and SpiralRelease(v) for g_decr/g_op_decr ones, with ownership decided from the Ty (the RcShape union from round 4, not the string test). Gate: delphi-heaptrc 0 unfreed blocks plus Delphi rows still agreeing with C.")
inl note_of_delphi_arc_memory_23 () = Note ("DELPHI-ARC-MEMORY", 23u32, "2026-11-04T11:00Z", "compiler", "11-04 07:2x compiler: the step-2 leak scan (node job compiler-heaptrc, 11-04 05:5x) was VACUOUS: rows 432, built 0. My wrapper never passed -Dll to delphi-heaptrc.ps1, whose $Dll defaults to '', so every compile ran 'dotnet \"\"' and was logged 'error'. Not a compiler result. Rerun with -Dll (Get-SpiralCompilerDll single-flight, installed pack34) is node job compiler-heaptrc2; it now also prints the compile-status histogram so a 0-built run is visible at once.")
inl note_of_delphi_arc_memory_24 () = Note ("DELPHI-ARC-MEMORY", 24u32, "2026-11-04T12:04Z", "compiler", "11-04 08:0x compiler: STEP 2 SCAN (node job compiler-heaptrc2, installed pack34, fpc -O2 -gh heaptrc, 120 s cap; delphi-heaptrc.ps1 with -Dll this time): rows 432, compile ok 431 (1 compile error), built 431, LEAKING 137, clean 294 (68%). Largest unfreed-block counts: mega_brzozowski_derivatives\\runtime_bench 714576, \\indexed_dfa_state_cardinality 120591, mutual_tail_rc_live 1001, brzozowski \\inventory_closed_dfa_runtime 556, \\runtime_native 274, native_closure_union_rec 21, lua_while_union_pair 9, gadt_growing_index_runtime 7, managed_string_recursive 5, recursive_union_terminal_ownership 5, native_managed_union_capture_record 4, recursive_union_multi_case 4, recursive_union_managed 4, recursive_union_list 4, closure_pair_fun 3. Reading: the small leakers name the classes (recursive unions holding managed fields, closures capturing unions or pairs, mutual tail recursion over RC values); the brzozowski counts scale with iterations, so they are the same classes inside loops. Full TSV on node: C:\\home\\remote-stage\\compiler-heaptrc\\heaptrc.tsv. Next: minimize one row per class (start with recursive_union_list, 4 blocks) and read the generated Pascal for the missing release.")
inl note_of_delphi_arc_memory_25 () = Note ("DELPHI-ARC-MEMORY", 25u32, "2026-11-04T12:06Z", "compiler", "11-04 08:1x compiler: ROOT CAUSE of the 137 leakers, read off the smallest one (recursive_union_list, 4 blocks, pack34 Delphi output, 84 lines). The Pascal backend has the RC machinery (TSpiralRc.refc, SpiralRetain/SpiralRelease, TUH0.DropFields releasing c1_1) but codegenPascal (spiral_compiler.fs 15275-15754) emits SpiralRelease ONLY inside DropFields (15287); it never releases a local whose last use has passed. In SpiralMain, v6 := UH0_Cons(v0, UH0_Cons(v1, UH0_Cons(v2, UH0_Nil))) is passed to sum_0 (which borrows) and then simply goes out of scope: 3 Cons blocks never freed. The 4th block is the shared UH0_Nil singleton (refc High(LongInt) div 2), which is never freed by design; it is a benign constant-per-type leak, but heaptrc counts it, so the oracle must either free singletons at exit or discount them. PLAN: port the C backend's refcDecrTable (last-use decrements per TypedBind, the RefCounting C path) to codegenPascal: after each statement, SpiralRelease every owned heap local whose last use it was, and release owned parameters at function exit; plus a finalization block that frees the shared nullary singletons. Acceptance: recursive_union_list and the other small leakers 0 unfreed blocks with outputs unchanged; the 294 clean rows stay clean; examples Delphi 0 DISAGREE.")
inl note_of_delphi_arc_memory_26 () = Note ("DELPHI-ARC-MEMORY", 26u32, "2026-11-04T12:10Z", "compiler", "11-04 08:2x compiler: PORT SPEC (from reading CodegenC, so the port mirrors it instead of inventing a second ownership model). (a) refc_prepass (25433, backend-independent, before module CodegenC) gives four tables per TypedBind: g_incr (vars bound by the previous let, +1), g_decr (owned vars whose last use passed, -1), g_op (argument counts for calls/joinpoints/union boxes/layouts/array literals, +n: the CALLEE OWNS its args, constructors own their fields), g_op_decr (owned vars released at a return). (b) CodegenC.refc_prefix (25839) nets all four per statement (varc_union, partition >0 / <0) and emits the increments then the decrements BEFORE the statement. (c) Self-loop tails: self_let_tail / kept_let_tail / self_continue_release (25810-25880) move the last statement's adjustments in front of the loop's continue; Pascal's self-loop path (codegenPascal 15446, 'Continue;') needs the same. (d) Convention gap: C's fresh object starts at refc 0 and is incremented by g_incr; Pascal's TSpiralRc starts at refc 0 = one owner and SpiralRelease frees below 0, so the Pascal port either skips g_incr for freshly constructed values or creates them at refc -1. Pick -1 (TSpiralRc.Create sets refc := -1) so the tables apply unchanged. (e) Pascal's union constructors (UH0_Cons) do not retain their fields today; under (a) the call site's g_op increments them, so the constructors stay as they are. (f) Only rcClass types (TUH*, TMut*, TFun*) get Retain/Release; strings are FPC-managed. Estimate: one focused session plus two node rounds (examples Delphi -Native 0 DISAGREE, then the heaptrc scan 137 -> ~0 leakers).")
inl note_of_delphi_arc_memory_27 () = Note ("DELPHI-ARC-MEMORY", 27u32, "2026-11-04T12:49Z", "compiler", "11-04 08:5x compiler: STEP 3 DRAFTED, DELPHI-ARC-MEMORY\\1-port-c-refcounting.kitdo (10 replacements, applies to live). CORRECTION to note 26 (d): C treats call and constructor results as already owned (refc_prepass adds g_incr only for layout/array index reads), so a fresh object is one owner in both backends; no refc := -1 change is needed. What the program does: codegenPascal takes a 'refc' function from the dispatcher (refc_prepass and RefcVars are defined after codegenPascal, so they cannot be named inside it); emit_function' computes it per body with the parameters owned; a refc_prefix before every statement nets g_incr/g_decr/g_op/g_op_decr and emits SpiralRetain then SpiralRelease, only for TUH/TMut/TFun vars; the closure itself is not counted at a TyApply (Invoke does not release Self); while-condition method args go through SpiralKeep (the condition is not a counted statement but the callee owns its params); mutable-field and array-element stores of rc values retain the new value and release the old (C's AssignMut/AssignArray); closure classes get DropFields for captured rc fields. Not covered by design (leak, never double free): stack unions, heap records, tuples and arrays holding rc values, and the nullary singletons. Review queued on the node (job compiler-delphiarc, the node's git-root copy): examples Delphi -Native A/B, then the heaptrc scan on the candidate vs control's 137 leakers.")
inl note_of_delphi_arc_memory_28 () = Note ("DELPHI-ARC-MEMORY", 28u32, "2026-11-04T13:34Z", "compiler", "11-04 09:4x compiler: v1 REVIEW (node job compiler-delphiarc, the git-root copy): build ok, examples Delphi -Native 455 rows, 430 ran on both arms, 0 DISAGREE vs the oracle, 135 residual cells, but 11 behavior cells: closure rows (native_closure_return, _transport, _captured_branch, _managed_capture, _nested_branch, _union_capture_method, native_prototype_union_closure_method, ...) exit 42 -> 217 (an unhandled access violation). ROOT CAUSE (box, candidate core 1D391A53 fetched from the node, native_closure_return): method0 emitted 'SpiralRelease(v0); Result := v0.Invoke(39);'. My refc_prefix subtracted C's +1 on the closure at a TyApply, assuming Invoke does not consume the closure, so the closure was released before its call. C's model (closure fn, ~26395): a call CONSUMES one reference to the closure. v2 mirrors that: the +1 stays, and every closure Invoke ends with 'SpiralRelease(Self);'. Captured fields stay borrowed for the body (released by DropFields when Self goes), equivalent to C's take-a-reference-then-decref-at-entry but without its early-free hazard. v1 stopped before its heaptrc phase; v2 = node job compiler-delphiarc2 (candidate examples vs the v1 control results, then heaptrc).")
inl note_of_delphi_arc_memory_29 () = Note ("DELPHI-ARC-MEMORY", 29u32, "2026-11-04T13:35Z", "compiler", "11-04 09:4x compiler: DRAFTED 2-free-shared-singletons.kitdo (stacks on 1; kept separate so v2's proof stays valid). The nullary heap-union singletons (UH<n>_<Case>_shared, refc High(LongInt) div 2) are never freed, so heaptrc counts one block per nullary case a program touches even with perfect ARC (recursive_union_list's 4th block). codegenPascal now collects the singleton names, and when there are any, the program body becomes 'SpiralExitCode := SpiralMain; if <s> <> nil then <s>.Free; ... Halt(SpiralExitCode);' (with a SpiralExitCode var); programs without singletons are unchanged byte for byte. Freeing at exit is safe because nothing runs after it. Rides the next Delphi review with v2's result.")
inl note_of_delphi_arc_memory_30 () = Note ("DELPHI-ARC-MEMORY", 30u32, "2026-11-04T13:40Z", "compiler", "11-04 09:4x compiler: DRAFTED 3-value-type-refcounts.kitdo (stacks on 1+2; held until v2's verdict). It mirrors C's USIncref/USDecref/HeapDecref for Pascal's value types. A stack union (TUS, a record) or heap layout (THeap, a record) that holds counted fields gets generated SpiralRetain_<T>/SpiralRelease_<T>/SpiralKeep_<T> helpers, forward-declared. A stack union's helper retains or releases only the active case's fields (case x.tag); helpers recurse through nested value types. Every counted site now goes through one rcCall: refc_prefix, while-condition keeps, mutable and array stores, and DropFields of heap unions, mut layouts and closures for value-typed fields. Arrays stay uncounted (FPC owns dynamic-array storage), so arrays of counted values still leak until a later step.")
inl note_of_delphi_arc_memory_31 () = Note ("DELPHI-ARC-MEMORY", 31u32, "2026-11-04T14:46Z", "compiler", "11-04 10:4x compiler: v2 BEHAVIOR GREEN (node job compiler-delphiarc2, the git-root copy; candidate = live pack34 base + 1-port-c-refcounting with the closure fix). examples Delphi -Native: 455 rows, 430 ran natively, 0 build-fail; compare vs the v1 pack34 control results: 135 differing cells, ALL residual (retain/release lines), 0 behavior cells, 0 DISAGREE. So the 11 closure rows that crashed in v1 (exit 42 -> 217) are back to 42: the closure-consumed-by-call model is right. Leak scan (heaptrc, candidate) is running now; then 2-free-shared-singletons and 3-value-type-refcounts get their round.")
inl note_of_delphi_arc_memory_32 () = Note ("DELPHI-ARC-MEMORY", 32u32, "2026-11-04T15:24Z", "compiler", "11-04 11:2x compiler: v2 LEAK SCAN (node job compiler-delphiarc2, candidate = program 1 with the closure fix): rows 439, built 438, LEAKING 37 (pack34 control: 431 built, 137 leaking), clean 401. Largest leaks: brzozowski runtime_bench 714576 -> 4 unfreed blocks, indexed_dfa_state_cardinality 120591 -> 6, inventory_closed_dfa_runtime 556 -> 11, runtime_native 274 -> 24, mutual_tail_rc_live 1001 -> clean. The remaining 37 are mostly ONE block each (recursive_union_list/managed/mutual/terminal_ownership/tree_shared, union_call, heap_fun, heap_mut_cell, native_closure_*), the nullary-singleton signature that program 2 frees, plus a few stack-union/heap-record holders that program 3 covers. Behavior identical (examples 0 behavior cells, note 31). 74 nonzero exits under heaptrc are samples whose expected exit is nonzero, not failures. v3 (programs 1+2+3, one build) is chained on the node now.")
inl note_of_delphi_arc_memory_33 () = Note ("DELPHI-ARC-MEMORY", 33u32, "2026-11-04T16:39Z", "compiler", "11-04 12:3x compiler: v3 BEHAVIOR GREEN (node job compiler-delphiarc3; programs 1+2+3 in one build): examples Delphi -Native 455 rows, 430 ran, 0 build-fail; vs the pack34 control: 135 residual cells only, 0 behavior cells, 0 DISAGREE. So the singleton frees at exit and the stack-union/heap-record helpers change no observable behavior. heaptrc leak scan of v3 running next in the same job.")
inl note_of_delphi_arc_memory_34 () = Note ("DELPHI-ARC-MEMORY", 34u32, "2026-11-04T17:20Z", "compiler", "11-04 13:2x compiler: v3 GREEN, READY FOR A PACK (node job compiler-delphiarc3; programs 1 port-c-refcounting + 2 free-shared-singletons + 3 value-type-refcounts, one build). Behavior: examples Delphi -Native 430 ran, 0 build-fail, 0 behavior cells vs pack34, 0 DISAGREE. LEAKS: rows 439, built 438, leaking 11 (pack34: 137 leaking of 431), clean 427. All 11 remaining leakers are arrays holding counted values, one block each (array_fun, array_mut, array_mut_f32, array_mut_heap_heap, array_mut_heap_heap_pair, array_mut_heap_union_heap_pair_heaps, ..._union_heap_union_..., ..._union3_both_..., array_mut_pair, array_mut_pair_heaps, array_mut_triple_heaps): the known gap, FPC owns dynamic-array storage so elements are never released. Pack slot: pack38 with BEND-UNSAFE-MINIMIZE and C-BORROWED-LOOP-INLINE (pack37 is staged and in review). NEXT STEP (arrays): release counted elements when the array's last owner goes; either wrap arrays of counted values in a TSpiralRc class (TArrayBox with DropFields releasing every element), mirroring C's ArrayDecref, or count the array var itself through a helper that releases elements when FPC's own refcount of the dynamic array is 1.")
inl note_of_delphi_arc_memory_35 () = Note ("DELPHI-ARC-MEMORY", 35u32, "2026-11-04T17:30Z", "compiler", "11-04 13:3x compiler: DRAFTED 4-box-counted-arrays.kitdo (stacks on 1+2+3; 8 replacements; mirrors C's refcounted arrays). An array whose element is counted (a TSpiralRc class or a value type with counted fields) becomes TBoxArray<n> = class(TSpiralRc) items: array of T, whose DropFields releases every element; rcCall counts it like any class. ArrayCreate/ArrayLiteral build the box (Create + SetLength(items)); ArrayIndex, ArrayIndexSet and ArrayLength go through .items for boxed arrays only, so arrays of primitives and strings emit exactly the code they did. Risk named in the review question: a lib macro that hands an array to raw Pascal (Length(!a) and the like) would not compile on a boxed array; the review shows it as build-fail. Review queued on the node (job compiler-delphiarc4: examples Delphi -Native vs the v1 pack34 control, then heaptrc; v3 had 11 leakers, all arrays of counted values).")
inl note_of_delphi_arc_memory_36 () = Note ("DELPHI-ARC-MEMORY", 36u32, "2026-11-04T19:45Z", "compiler", "11-04 15:5x compiler: v4 ZERO LEAKS (node job compiler-delphiarc4; programs 1 port-c-refcounting + 2 free-shared-singletons + 3 value-type-refcounts + 4 box-counted-arrays, one build). Behavior: examples Delphi -Native 430 ran, 0 build-fail (no lib macro broke on TBoxArray), 135 residual cells only, 0 behavior cells, 0 DISAGREE vs pack34. heaptrc: rows 439, built 438, LEAKING 0, clean 438 (pack34: 137 leaking of 431, worst runtime_bench 714576 blocks). The 74 nonzero exits under heaptrc are samples whose expected exit is nonzero. Progression today: 137 -> 37 (v2 ARC) -> 11 (v3 singletons + value types) -> 0 (v4 arrays). All four programs go in pack38 with BEND-UNSAFE-MINIMIZE and C-BORROWED-LOOP-INLINE (stack-checked on pack37's source). The item closes when pack38 is installed and a heaptrc scan on the installed compiler confirms 0.")
inl note_of_delphi_arc_memory_37 () = Note ("DELPHI-ARC-MEMORY", 37u32, "2026-11-04T20:36Z", "compiler", "11-04 16:3x compiler: PACK38 REVIEWED and handed to main (install after pack37; one node build, job compiler-pack38rev, control = pack37 candidate bin). Source core 75AAB501 / host 5D4516B8, bin core 15FD51D7 / host 08B00F25. Delphi -Native 430 ran, residual only, 0 behavior; Bend -Native 414 ran, residual only, plus one exit cell: c_self_tail_loop_heap timing out at 120 s (slowest Bend row, control 80 s today; BOTH arms timed out on it in the v6/v7 reviews, same candidate residual 9b61078f), a timeout boundary, not behavior; C -Native 523 ran, residual 9, 0 behavior, 0 DISAGREE everywhere.")
inl note_of_delphi_arc_memory_38 () = Note ("DELPHI-ARC-MEMORY", 38u32, "2026-11-05T00:57Z", "compiler", "11-04 20:5x compiler: PENDING INSTALL. main's pack38 install-when-clear gave up at 20:56 after 110 min: it waits for running trains as well as an idle compiler, and kit's TRAIN26 ran on the node the whole window. The box stays on pack37 (host 08B00F25 / core 5F6F6E73). pack38 (reviewed; candidate %LOCALAPPDATA%\\spiral-bin-candidates\\pack38) installs first in the next run, then pack39 (reviewed; needs live = pack38). The handover commands are in main's inbox from 15:3x (pack38) and 19:2x (pack39).")
inl note_of_delphi_arc_memory_39 () = Note ("DELPHI-ARC-MEMORY", 39u32, "2026-11-05T03:57Z", "compiler", "11-04 23:5x compiler: INSTALLED on the BOX in pack38 (23:38, compiler's install-when-clear chain with no train wait, as kit allowed for box-only installs). Dry run: the 7 pack38 programs on today's live source reproduce candidate source core 75AAB501 / host 5D4516B8. After install: SpiralCompilerCore.dll 15FD51D755DD3120 and SpiralCompiler.dll 08B00F25C65C5283, both equal=True to the reviewed candidate; system dotnet launches it. Undo: spiral-bin\\compiler-installs\\pack38-20261104-233851. Node and vultr ship: main's waiter (pack-chain -SkipInstall) once pack39 lands. Done under installed-is-done.")
inl note_of_delphi_arc_memory_40 () = Note ("DELPHI-ARC-MEMORY", 40u32, "2026-11-05T03:59Z", "compiler", "compiler: (DELPHI-ARC-MEMORY stays Active: the last 3% is the heaptrc scan on the INSTALLED compiler, which the note above makes possible; run it on the node once main ships pack39 there, box last.)")
inl fiscal_pattern_oracle_families () = Task ("FISCAL-PATTERN-ORACLE-FAMILIES", "fiscal's .NET pattern oracle covers every family schema: patterns.txt holds only the 196 BR corpus patterns, so FatturaPA, CFDI, UBL, KSeF and the other family patterns never meet .NET; pattern-oracle.ps1 derives the list from every family root's schema tree in deps and corpus_checks' 5 pattern tests run over all of it", TaskKind, P1, D2, 100u32, Done, "FISCAL-XSD-PATTERNS-LARGE", "fiscal,xsd,regex,oracle", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_fiscal_pattern_oracle_families () = Planning ("FISCAL-PATTERN-ORACLE-FAMILIES", Minutes 45u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl remote_laptop_lane () = Task ("REMOTE-LAPTOP-LANE", "A second LAN laptop as a remote eoie worker: key-only SSH, scripted bootstrap (toolchains, repos, eoie host), an eoie remote executor for job start, EOIE-MACHINE-HEALTH covering both; the rehearsal for renting cloud/GPU machines", Story, P1, D4, 100u32, Done, "EOIE-MACHINE-HEALTH", "kit,remote,infra,ci-offload,guide", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_remote_laptop_lane () = Planning ("REMOTE-LAPTOP-LANE", Minutes 600u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_remote_laptop_lane_1 () = Note ("REMOTE-LAPTOP-LANE", 1u32, "2026-10-25T15:47Z", "main", "10-25 11:47 main (user 10-25 11:2x-11:4x: a second laptop on the LAN to battle-test eoie remote work and rehearse renting DigitalOcean/Vultr/vast.ai machines; user rated the idea and asked me to take it over). DONE: SSH with key-only login from this box. A dedicated key ~/.ssh/laptop_ed25519 (passphrase-free, comment main-to-laptop) is authorized for alias in C:\\ProgramData\\ssh\\administrators_authorized_keys and ~\\.ssh\\authorized_keys. sshd Running/Automatic; firewall rule sshd-lan allows TCP 22 from 192.168.2.0/24 only. The install fought two snags: 0x800b0101 CERT_E_EXPIRED from Add-WindowsCapability (the laptop clock was off; w32tm resync fixed it), then 'file in use' while servicing was still busy. Laptop: DESKTOP-6E8C14G, Windows 10 Pro (this box is Windows 11), 8 cores, 15.9 GB RAM, a Samsung 840 EVO 250 GB with only 9.5 GB free, git, pwsh 7 (winget copy), winget. The SSH default shell is cmd, so the helper scratchpad\\laptop.ps1 -Command '<pwsh>' sends -EncodedCommand to powershell. BLOCKER: disk. Toolchains + repos + spiral-bin need about 20-30 GB, so the user decides what to free (a survey of the ~165 GB outside Users/Program Files/ProgramData is running). Password SSH login is still on: a security setting the user flips (sshd_config PasswordAuthentication no).")
inl note_of_remote_laptop_lane_2 () = Note ("REMOTE-LAPTOP-LANE", 2u32, "2026-10-25T16:26Z", "main", "10-25 12:26 main: connectivity hardened. (1) ~/.ssh/config Host laptop: HostName DESKTOP-6E8C14G, key, BatchMode; the host key is pinned under HostKeyAlias laptop-desktop-6e8c14g, so an IP change doesn't trip verification. (2) scratchpad\\laptop.ps1 resolves the name itself, falls back to the last address that worked (~/.ssh/laptop-last-ip.txt), then to 'tailscale ip'; name lookup was flaky once today. (3) Tailscale installed on both machines via winget (hash-verified), 'tailscale up --unattended' on the laptop (hostname laptop-6e8c14g), waiting for the user to approve its login link; this box's 'up' printed no link, so that needs a recheck. (4) For the user, because it's a security setting: the firewall rule sshd-lan only allows 192.168.2.0/24. To work on any LAN AND over Tailscale it needs: Set-NetFirewallRule -Name sshd-lan -RemoteAddress LocalSubnet,100.64.0.0/10. The laptop's network is categorized Public, so the installer's Private-only rule never applies.")
inl note_of_remote_laptop_lane_3 () = Note ("REMOTE-LAPTOP-LANE", 3u32, "2026-10-25T17:05Z", "main", "10-25 13:05 main: all user-side steps DONE (user 12:1x). Tailscale is up on both machines under account fc1943s@ (laptop-6e8c14g 100.97.57.53, main-box 100.78.144.60; tailscale ping direct via 192.168.2.28:41641, 36 ms). The firewall rule sshd-lan now allows LocalSubnet,100.64.0.0/10. 'ssh laptop' works on the LAN and with -o HostName=100.97.57.53 over Tailscale; the first try timed out while this box's routes were still settling. The laptop has 89.8 GB free; WoW Data (107.5 GB) is still present, so freeing it is optional now. Backup of _retail_ Interface + WTF: a 656 MB tar on the laptop, copying to C:\\home\\data\\wow-retail-11.2.7 (scp in flight). Next, nothing pending from the user: verify the backup by entry count, then bootstrap the toolchains over SSH, then the eoie host (EOIE-REMOTE-EXECUTOR for dispatch).")
inl note_of_remote_laptop_lane_4 () = Note ("REMOTE-LAPTOP-LANE", 4u32, "2026-10-25T17:46Z", "guide", "13:46 guide (session 88a146f8): took over the laptop lane from main's fork per the user. This lane coordinates all lanes to keep the laptop near 100% CPU while the main box gets headroom; git stays the user's on both machines, so no git transport. Handoff from main: laptop DESKTOP-6E8C14G, Win10 Pro, 8 cores, 15.9 GB RAM, 89.8 GB free; git/pwsh7/winget/sshd present; key-only SSH via laptop_ed25519 (user alias), 'ssh laptop' + Tailscale fallback (laptop 100.97.57.53, box 100.78.144.60), helper laptop.ps1. Transport settled non-git: file copy (tar+scp/sftp) over SSH, M4 hash proof unchanged, recorded by kit on EOIE-REMOTE-EXECUTOR note 3; kit will not own an internal-repo bus. Progress 30 = connectivity/SSH done of {ssh, bootstrap, eoie host}. GATE: the user-driven fork (i-have-a) is mid archive-copy (~455/656 MB to C:\\home\\data\\wow-retail-11.2.7), then verify-by-entry-count, then bootstrap; read-only SSH only until main forwards the fork-idle signal, then this lane runs the toolchain bootstrap. Next: read-only laptop state check + a staged bootstrap script run on the go-signal.")
inl note_of_remote_laptop_lane_5 () = Note ("REMOTE-LAPTOP-LANE", 5u32, "2026-10-25T17:55Z", "kit2", "13:55 kit2: user renamed this session (88a146f8) guide->kit2 - now a second kit lane co-owning kit items with kit, and owner of remote-node offload (high-CPU jobs from any session -> laptop). Synced a proposal to kit: claim-by-note to avoid collision; I respect the eoie/kit feature freeze (no source changes until KIT-RENAME), so interim offload runs as org tooling in i574n.github/scripts, folded into EOIE-REMOTE-EXECUTOR M2 after the freeze. Read-only laptop probe 17:46Z: fully toolchained (rustup nightly-2026-10-18 + wasm32 x2, zig 0.17.0, gcc 15.2.0, dotnet 9.0.200, gleam 1.19.2, lua 5.1.5, bun 1.2.5, elixir; 12 repos checked out: spiral eoie tibia x fiscal dice alphabet polyglot dir-tree-html i574n.github blog vault). Gap: no eoie/kit/spiral binary on laptop PATH (host not deployed) - plan to scp a built supervisor binary (freeze-safe file copy) to unblock M1. Holding all laptop writes until main forwards the fork-idle signal.")
inl note_of_remote_laptop_lane_6 () = Note ("REMOTE-LAPTOP-LANE", 6u32, "2026-10-25T18:10Z", "kit2", "14:09 kit2 (user clarification on git): git is the user's ONLY on this machine - the review box, source of truth. On external nodes (laptop) kit2 MANAGES the files and MAY use git internally if useful (user: 'that is on you'); results come back in place here as unstaged changes for the user to review. So internal git on the laptop is reopened as a kit2-owned node-side option (kit declined to own an internal-repo bus; node-side is now my call), independent of the non-git bring-back to this box. New research tasks from the user: study deltadb / the new anthropic VCS / fossil sync models and beat them with the typefeatureset + iggy; and check whether the retired llm-browser-tab bundle features can serve multi-machine transport.")
inl note_of_remote_laptop_lane_7 () = Note ("REMOTE-LAPTOP-LANE", 7u32, "2026-10-25T18:20Z", "main", "2026-10-25 14:20 main: bootstrap done and verified. Toolchains through scoop under pwsh 7 with process-scoped -ExecutionPolicy Bypass (WinPS 5.1 policy stays Restricted, the user's setting): gh, zig 0.17.0 (CI parity), rebar3, wasmtime installed; elixir, gcc 15.2, lua51, cmake, ninja present; erlang, gleam, pwsh, git updated; rust nightly-2026-10-18 default + wasm32-unknown-unknown/wasip2 + rustfmt/clippy/rust-src; .NET 11.0.100-rc.2 in spiral-bin via spiral's install-dotnet.ps1. Repos: alphabet, dice, i574n.github, polyglot, spiral, vault, spiral/deps/polyglot fast-forwarded (uncommitted 2025 edits stashed as 'laptop pre-update 2026-10-25' in polyglot 5, spiral 11, deps/polyglot 1); cloned eoie(kit), x, tibia, fiscal, blog, dir-tree-html; the fork deps/The-Spiral-Language via spiral's init-app-build.ps1; every head equals main-box's. git line endings match (system autocrlf=true, repo .gitattributes LF, 0 CR bytes on both). eoie host: fresh-clone build of eoie HEAD a12135b fails (src/eoie_job_queue has decide.spi but no generated decide.rs; reported to kit), so main-box's eoie.exe (sha256 CDEF7564...) runs at C:\\home\\bin\\eoie.exe; job start over SSH survives the session. New org tool i574n.github/scripts/remote.ps1 <machine> run|job|status|sync|fetch, machines in ~/.config/remote-machines.json (outside the public repo): sync ships modified + untracked files over a detached checkout of the same HEAD (bundle when the far side lacks the commit), deletes deleted files, and both sides compare one sha256 digest over the changed files (first run: i574n.github, 2 files, D289DE64B8DF197E on both). Mirror contract: laptop repos are mirrors; sync detaches and cleans untracked non-ignored files there, so nothing is authored on the laptop. Free disk 82.6 GB (WoW Data 107.5 GB still there, the user's to delete).")
inl note_of_remote_laptop_lane_8 () = Note ("REMOTE-LAPTOP-LANE", 8u32, "2026-10-25T18:24Z", "kit2", "14:24 kit2: ONE offload tool = i574n.github/scripts/remote.ps1 (main's, landed). It already carries the per-file sha256 proof (Get-TreeDigest over the changed set, verified equal both sides) on a git checkout --detach HEAD + clean baseline, which fits the relaxed git rule (internal git on the node, non-git delta tar, read-only local git, non-git fetch bring-back). Folded in the one missing piece - the read-only/write-back offload scope rule - as a header on remote.ps1 (additive, no logic change). Retired my scratch remote-run.ps1 (one record per fact); its unique extras left as optional future adds: a selftest verb (local digest round-trip) and a whole-tree (not just delta) verify flag. iggy is parked (unix-only fd hand-off) -> ssh stays the transport. remote.ps1 verbs: run|job|status|sync|fetch; machines in ~/.config/remote-machines.json.")
inl note_of_remote_laptop_lane_9 () = Note ("REMOTE-LAPTOP-LANE", 9u32, "2026-10-25T18:44Z", "kit2", "14:43 kit2: ID rename REMOTE-LAPTOP-LANE -> REMOTE-WORKER-NODE DEFERRED. agile rename rewrites the whole 6.5MB backlog and keeps losing the optimistic-concurrency race while the backlog is hot (node work, many lanes noting). Reverted the dependents' deps (EOIE-REMOTE-EXECUTOR, IGGY-MULTI-MACHINE) to REMOTE-LAPTOP-LANE to clear the dangling refs. The TERM change is DONE (agent-sync.md, remote.ps1 header, memory, go-forward usage; all lanes notified) - only the backlog ID is pending, to retry in a quiet window. Historical notes keep 'laptop' as log.")
inl note_of_remote_laptop_lane_10 () = Note ("REMOTE-LAPTOP-LANE", 10u32, "2026-10-25T19:22Z", "kit2", "15:22 kit2: node now SATURATING (user: it sat idle). Launched job-1792955993955-det-check: sync review-box spiral into isolated mirror C:\\home\\remote\\det\\spiral (per-job worktree), build single-flight there (heavy step), assert node SpiralCompilerCore.dll == CDD68DC3EE829A67. Fixes L6 (node core 850290->CDD68DC3) as a side effect. Fits ~9GB beside the fork's 6GB WSL (single build, not -Suite -Parallel). L6 guard LANDED+validated in remote.ps1: run/job -RequireCore <hash> [-CoreMode <mode>] refuses a verify/compile offload unless the node's core matches (refuse path proven against the stale node). Added \node key to ~/.config/remote-machines.json (kept laptop) so lanes stop typing laptop. Background waiter buzzc9aqi for the det-check result. Next on completion: if MATCH, open node to tibia gates + x verifies under the guard; if MISMATCH, report hashes.")
inl note_of_remote_laptop_lane_11 () = Note ("REMOTE-LAPTOP-LANE", 11u32, "2026-10-25T19:38Z", "kit2", "15:38 kit2: node monitoring + saturation. node-watch monitor live (job-1792956967969-node-watch, 1h): samples node CPU + running/queued jobs every 75s to scratchpad/node-watch.log, flags idle stretches. Node opened to 3 lanes under -RequireCore 826D67C6 (its current-source core): tibia (6 lib gates), x (re-verify), fiscal (Delphi) - distributed offload is what keeps it fed. Durable monitoring/no-idle (beyond this interim log): fold node CPU/jobs into EOIE-MACHINE-HEALTH, and the dispatcher (EOIE-REMOTE-EXECUTOR Gleam coordinator) auto-feeds the node from a work queue so idle is PREVENTED, not just observed - that's the SOTA answer. Interim keeps it busy via multi-lane self-serve offload (recipe in agent-sync.md).")
inl note_of_remote_laptop_lane_12 () = Note ("REMOTE-LAPTOP-LANE", 12u32, "2026-10-25T19:56Z", "kit2", "15:56 kit2: interim node dispatcher VALIDATED end-to-end (job-1792958020768-node-dispatch, live 1h). Self-test selftest-1: queue -> sync dice -Mirror dispatch-selftest -> L6 core guard passed (826D67C69119F61E) -> run -> fetch -> done/, result 'dispatch-selftest-ok', failed/ empty. Lanes can now enqueue to C:\\home\\remote-queue\\pending to keep the node fed back-to-back (schema + recipe in agent-sync.md 'Offloading to the worker node'); dispatcher runs one at a time, moves to done/failed with dispatch.log. node-watch monitor also live. Self-test worktree cleaned.")
inl note_of_remote_laptop_lane_13 () = Note ("REMOTE-LAPTOP-LANE", 13u32, "2026-10-25T21:16Z", "kit2", "17:16 kit2 - NODE OFFLOAD SCOPE (tibia + main findings). PROVEN on the node: cross-machine compile-determinism (0/7352) + single-backend builds (main's dir-tree rust, x rust verify, the suite). NOT ready: full-matrix lib gates. Gaps (tibia): (1) mix deps.get per mirror (Hex kino/table absent in a fresh worktree); (2) the spiral CLI rust runner is not on the node PATH ('spiral rust --rs-path --deps chrono not found'), so Cells Rust targets report 'unavailable' (date_time: 18 Rust targets unsupported); (3) backends node/typescript are EXCLUDED by design (bun-only), lean/python/cuda runners may be absent. IMPLICATION: a lib-gate GREEN on the node is trustworthy only for backends the node actually runs; main's candidate-vs-baseline gate tolerates equal-missing backends but CANNOT catch a regression in a backend it doesn't run (e.g. rust for LIST-SUGAR). So the lib-gate drain (main's 12, tibia's 6) is BLOCKED on node backend-runner provisioning = NODE-LIB-GATE-PROVISION: provision the spiral CLI rust runner + the non-excluded backend runners. Single-backend/compile offloads proceed now.")
inl note_of_remote_laptop_lane_14 () = Note ("REMOTE-LAPTOP-LANE", 14u32, "2026-10-25T21:45Z", "kit2", "17:45 kit2 - DECENTRALIZED (user: teach all lanes to work with the node; we're not responsible for routing anymore). Node is self-serve-ready for every lane: (1) remote.ps1 + recipe in agent-sync.md; (2) sync now carries moves/deletes correctly - fixed a rename-detection bug that hid 9 deletions in x (--no-renames + --diff-filter=D), empty dirs pruned, validated; (3) spiral CLI rust runner provisioned on the node User PATH (apps/spiral built, run-checks green rust/cuda/lua/gleam), so lib-gate Rust targets resolve; (4) per-job mirrors + L6 core guard + guard-skip make concurrent self-serve safe. Lanes drive their own offloads directly + concurrently (fiscal/x/tibia already do); kit2 owns the shared tooling + one-time provisioning, NOT per-job routing. This structurally fixes the idle gaps (no me-as-bottleneck). agent-sync.md 'Offloading to the worker node' documents it.")
inl note_of_remote_laptop_lane_15 () = Note ("REMOTE-LAPTOP-LANE", 15u32, "2026-10-26T04:49Z", "eoie", "00:49 eoie: bootstrap gaps found while making the worker node run release notebooks (user 10-26: don't leave the node idle). (1) The Cells rust runner (C:\\home\\git\\spiral\\workspace target\\release\\spiral.exe, SPIRAL_EXE in the node User env) was a 2025-03-13 build. The rebuild needs (2) OpenSSL: now scoop openssl with OPENSSL_LIB_DIR and OPENSSL_INCLUDE_DIR in the node User env, as on this box; and (3) perl, because the workspace builds openssl-sys vendored: scoop perl, as on this box. Both belong in the scripted bootstrap. (4) remote.ps1 sync -Mirror links <mirror>\\eoie\\deps to the node's eoie\\deps, which doesn't exist, so it always prints MIRROR-DEPS-MISSING; regen still works through KIT_SPIRAL_BUNDLE. (5) Cells honors SPIRAL_COMPILER_DLL, so node notebook verdicts are pinned to this box's compiler by shipping the installed DLL (core CDD68DC3EE829A67 on both). Scripts: kit scratch clean\\respell node-candidate.ps1 (fix, regen, debug build, notebooks at throttle 2) and node-serial.ps1.")
inl note_of_remote_laptop_lane_16 () = Note ("REMOTE-LAPTOP-LANE", 16u32, "2026-10-29T11:57Z", "main", "10-29 07:5x main: node mirror traps reported by fiscal (10-29 07:4x).\n(1) A fresh `sync -Mirror` lacks apps/cells/deps (kino and the other mix deps), so a lib gate on that mirror fails in 5 s with 0 rows. Workaround: copy deps from an older mirror. Fix wanted in kit's sync: either carry apps/cells/deps, or run `mix deps.get` in the mirror before the first Cells job, and fail loudly when it is missing.\n(2) A sync during live lib edits returned exit 7 (digest mismatch) once, and a rerun passed. Expected under concurrent writes, but the sync should retry once on its own.\nNode access for every lane, all proven 10-29:\n- routes: ssh laptop (LAN name), laptop-ip (192.168.2.28), laptop-ts (Tailscale 100.97.57.53);\n- keys: 2, each authorized alone;\n- remote.ps1 probes every route; i574n.github/scripts/node-reach.ps1 prints a routes x keys table.\nPre-wipe material survived on the node, a recovery source: C:\\home\\remote (46 folders, incl. scripts\\ with the compiler lane's P3 shard and IVar prototypes, the P2 detcheck gate, contention and trace probes per git-a6 WIPE-REDO-LEDGER n4), C:\\home\\remote-stage (273, incl. renameproof\\scripts\\*.do, tibia-gaps and tibia-kit land/undo .do), and the node's own %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit job history.")
inl note_of_remote_laptop_lane_17 () = Note ("REMOTE-LAPTOP-LANE", 17u32, "2026-10-29T14:03Z", "compiler", "10-29 10:03 compiler (git-a6): NODE COMPILER RE-SHIPPED. The node's installed single-flight compiler was 4 days stale, so every node gate or notebook run before 10:02 today WITHOUT a SPIRAL_COMPILER_DLL pin used a pre-5n compiler (main broadcast the recheck list to fiscal, x, kit and tibia). The node now equals the box byte for byte (core 05E94769A86A9B9C, host A4E480202BD40D80); remote.ps1 -RequireCore passes. Procedure and the re-ship-after-every-box-install rule are in memory node-stale-compiler-dll. node-upkeep.log: 2026-10-29 10:02  compiler (git-a6): node INSTALLED single-flight compiler was 4 days stale (built 2026-10-25 15:23 from source 34519b92, core 826D67C69119F61E; HEAD core source is 9FF1B708), so any node lib gate or notebook not pinning SPIRAL_COMPILER_DLL used an old compiler. Replaced it with this box's installed compiler, byte-equal (core 05E94769A86A9B9C, host A4E480202BD40D80, 35 files, runtime 11.0.0-rc.1, which the node has): staged at C:\\home\\remote-stage\\box-compiler-05E94769, smoke-compiled samples/closure_pair to C equal to the committed main.c, then swapped by folder rename (the old one kept at ...\\Release\\net11.0.stale-34519b92-20261029; the rename would have aborted if any job held the folder). remote.ps1 -RequireCore 05E94769A86A9B9C now passes. When the box installs pack 6, ship again the same way.")
inl note_of_remote_laptop_lane_18 () = Note ("REMOTE-LAPTOP-LANE", 18u32, "2026-10-29T14:06Z", "main", "10-29 10:1x main, from git-a6's node upkeep: the node's INSTALLED compiler was 4 days stale until 10:1x (built 10-25 from core source 34519b92, while HEAD is 9FF1B708). git-a6 swapped it by folder rename to this box's install (core 05E94769A86A9B9C, host A4E480202BD40D80); the old one is kept as net11.0.stale-34519b92-20261029, and remote.ps1 -RequireCore 05E94769A86A9B9C passes. Impact check across lanes: NONE of today's verdicts are affected. x (node-proof.ps1 -CompilerDll spiral-bin-p2, Cells reads SPIRAL_COMPILER_DLL first: toolchain.ex:22), kit (BANG compare -Dll spiral-bin-p2) and fiscal (fix-proof-d and set-proof-c pin spiral-bin-1028-0530 and throw unless host A4E480202BD4) all pinned a staged DLL equal to today's install. The exception is fiscal's 10-27/28 seta/setb/setc verdicts (set-proof.ps1 pinned the 10-26 4FD712E9 stage), and no land of today rests on them. Rule going forward: every lane passes -RequireCore 05E94769A86A9B9C on node jobs, and git-a6 re-ships the node after the pack-6 install.")
inl note_of_remote_laptop_lane_19 () = Note ("REMOTE-LAPTOP-LANE", 19u32, "2026-10-29T14:34Z", "main", "10-29 10:3x main, from kit (BANG n43): the NODE's own C:\\home\\git\\spiral checkout is STALE. It has no lib\\spiral\\iggy.spi, so the first iggy codec compare there was VOID ('Unbound module iggy' on both sides). Anything on the node that resolves the lib from C:\\home\\git\\spiral gets an old lib. Use a freshly synced mirror's lib (kit used C:\\home\\remote\\kit-depstest\\spiral\\lib) until the node checkout is updated. Owner: git-a6's node-upkeep pass, with the RUNNER-STALE ship (same quiet window). NODE-MIRROR-CELLS-DEPS is Done (fresh mirror lib gate 9/9, n4).")
inl note_of_remote_laptop_lane_20 () = Note ("REMOTE-LAPTOP-LANE", 20u32, "2026-11-01T03:36Z", "kit", "10-31 23:4x kit groom (main 23:33, user: cadence/drift): Active -> PLANNED. No note in over 12 h, so nobody is working it this week; Planned keeps it in agile next at its priority (never auto-Paused, R3). First step when picked = the next step in its latest note.")
inl note_of_remote_laptop_lane_21 () = Note ("REMOTE-LAPTOP-LANE", 21u32, "2026-11-01T03:40Z", "main", "main 23:4x WIP groom (user 23:3x: drift/stall, too much waiting): Active -> Planned. no step in 61h; NODE-ALWAYS-FED and kit's EOIE-REMOTE-EXECUTOR carry the live node work.")
inl note_of_remote_laptop_lane_22 () = Note ("REMOTE-LAPTOP-LANE", 22u32, "2026-11-01T13:15Z", "kit", "11-01 09:1x kit groom: CLOSED with evidence; its remaining scope moved. Evidence that the second machine is a working remote worker: key-only SSH through the 'laptop' aliases (ed25519 keys, memory remote-kit-worker-node), the node bootstrapped with toolchains and repos, per-job worktree mirrors (C:\\home\\remote\\kit-verify) synced with REMOTE-SYNC-OK digests, and kit work run there all night on 10-31/11-01: 30+ node jobs (candidate builds with 34 regenerated owners, notebook batches, lib gates, cargo tests), e.g. job-1793511412846-kit-node-norespell3 and job-1793492637691-kit-node-todo-lint2. MOVED: 'an eoie remote executor for job start' is EOIE-REMOTE-EXECUTOR's slices REMOTE-EXEC-S1..S12 (that dependency was circular: the executor depended on this item, which contained the executor); 'EOIE-MACHINE-HEALTH covering both' stays EOIE-MACHINE-HEALTH, a direct dep of EOIE-REMOTE-EXECUTOR; 'the rehearsal for renting cloud/GPU machines' becomes valid once REMOTE-EXEC-S12 passes on this node.")
inl wow_lua_sandbox () = Task ("WOW-LUA-SANDBOX", "WoW addons in Spiral compiled to Lua 5.1, developed and simulated headless (wowless + typed API bindings from LuaLS annotations) without the game installed; the real client only for the final test; the pivot after tibia instead of Dota 2", Epic, P2, D7, 0u32, Paused, "REMOTE-LAPTOP-LANE", "wow,lua,sandbox,addons,team:main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_wow_lua_sandbox () = Planning ("WOW-LUA-SANDBOX", Minutes 6000u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_wow_lua_sandbox_1 () = Note ("WOW-LUA-SANDBOX", 1u32, "2026-10-25T15:59Z", "main", "10-25 11:59 main (user 10-25 11:5x: pivot to WoW after tibia instead of Dota 2; keep a Spiral->Lua sandbox for weeks and months without WoW installed, needing the real client only at the end). Laptop facts (REMOTE-LAPTOP-LANE): retail 11.2.7 (The War Within) install; Data 107.5 GB, _retail_ 1.0 GB with 88 addons (AllTheThings, Auctionator, Baganator, Bartender4, BestInSlotRedux, Chattynator, the DBM family, ...) and 88 WTF saved-variable files. Keeping only _retail_ (addons + WTF) frees about 108 GB.\nSandbox shape, from what exists:\n(1) wowless (github.com/wowless/wowless): a headless WoW client Lua + FrameXML interpreter for addon testing. It downloads retail Lua/XML interface code itself, runs it on elune (a taint-aware Lua 5.1 fork), and can click and mouse over the frames an addon creates; it runs one addon via --addondir. It is pre-alpha (its README says errors are most likely wowless's own) and develops under Docker. So the 107 GB Data folder isn't needed for this.\n(2) Blizzard's interface source: Gethe/wow-ui-source mirrors FrameXML per build (live/classic branches); townlong-yak browses it per build.\n(3) Typed API surface: Ketho/vscode-wow-api, LuaLS annotations generated from the wiki, is the source for typed Spiral bindings to the WoW API, generated rather than hand-written (memory: no hand-crafted Lua).\n(4) The Lua 5.1 (WoW) backend already exists (memory backend-targets).\nRISKS: (a) retail is now 12.0.7 (Midnight). Midnight makes combat state inaccessible to addon logic (WeakAuras: no Midnight version; the Beacon fork is in early access), so the 11.2.7 addons target an API live servers no longer have. The sandbox must target 12.x interface code (wowless fetches current retail), and combat addons (DBM, meters, WeakAuras-like) fall under the new 'secret value' rules. Non-combat addons (Auctionator, Baganator, Bartender4, AllTheThings, Chattynator) are the safe first corpus. (b) AzerothCore is a 3.3.5a (WotLK) server, so it can't host a retail client. TrinityCore master targets recent retail through a custom launcher (Arctium); private servers are against Blizzard's terms, so the end test on live with a subscription is the clean path. (c) wowless's pre-alpha state means sandbox gaps get fixed there or in our own harness.\nSources: https://github.com/wowless/wowless ; https://github.com/Ketho/vscode-wow-api ; https://warcraft.wiki.gg/wiki/XML_schema ; https://trinitycore.atlassian.net/wiki/x/-D5pB ; https://www.wowhead.com/news/no-weakauras-addon-for-midnight-378740 ; https://www.curseforge.com/wow/addons/beacon")
inl note_of_wow_lua_sandbox_2 () = Note ("WOW-LUA-SANDBOX", 2u32, "2026-10-25T16:26Z", "main", "10-25 12:26 main: (a) wowless without Docker: it is CMake 3.24 + Ninja + vcpkg (casclib, curl, expat, libyaml, openssl, sqlite3, utf8h, zlib, sdl3 + sdl3-image) plus the elune Lua 5.1 fork and Lua submodules (tactless for TACT/CASC, penlight, lpeg, cjson, luadbd, ...); all of them build natively on Windows, and Docker is only its devcontainer. It's active (last commit 2026-10-22). Native build = a CPU-heavy first vcpkg pass, a good first laptop job once its disk is freed. (b) Mega samples measured: brzozowski_derivatives 11.4k lines (135 unions, 161 nominals, 731 foralls, 25 GADT cases), omniledger_erp_kernel 12.8k (107/99/254/15), lean_cic 10.6k, spiral_proves_spiral 13.5k, zeta_structural 8.4k. Best domain fits: Auctionator (money as ledger-typed copper, scan/post/cancel as a typestate machine; omniledger's shape) and Baganator's search language (a query grammar; brzozowski's derivatives). Neither is touched by Midnight's combat secrecy. (c) Why Midnight changed: Blizzard's 'combat philosophy and addon disarmament' post: combat state becomes a secret value addons can display but not read, against combat automation; native boss timers, cooldown and damage-meter replacements, and exceptions added over patches (12.1 aura changes). Sources: https://news.blizzard.com/en-us/article/24246290/combat-philosophy-and-addon-disarmament-in-midnight ; https://warcraft.wiki.gg/wiki/Patch_12.0.0/Planned_API_changes ; https://warcraft.wiki.gg/wiki/Patch_12.1.0/API_changes")
inl note_of_wow_lua_sandbox_3 () = Note ("WOW-LUA-SANDBOX", 3u32, "2026-10-29T21:52Z", "main", "10-29 17:5x main: lib Lua readiness, from today's work (read before starting WoW addons in Spiral -> Lua 5.1). FIXED today (landed 17:32, proven on the landed lib): listm' Lua list order (rev' was the identity; filter'/map/distinct' reversed), cons' mutating its shared tail, am'.to_list' aliasing its array, sm'.builder_replace treating its text as a Lua pattern. Regression tests now list lua (LIB-MACRO-HEAL n74-n82). REMAINING Lua gaps from a backend_switch census (C:\\home\\data\\main-drafts\\session-1029\\missing-arm-census.tsv; candidates, not measured): the whole seq module has no Lua arm (length', to_list', of_array, to_array', of_list', cast', rev'', new_seq); sm'.char_code and sm'.of_char have no Lua arm; resultm box/unbox and date_time.stopwatch have no Lua arm. A missing arm compiles to a run-time throw on the path that reaches it (SPIRAL-RUST-NET-TEXT-GUARD). Before relying on any of these in an addon, probe it on Lua by hand (memory backend-probe-runners) and add the arm with a lua test row. CORRECTION 18:5x (read in full): resultm box/unbox are NOT a Lua gap. result'_union () is true on Lua (also Python and TS), so both take the plain-union path and never reach their backend_switch; that census row is a false positive. The seq module IS a real gap: length', to_list', of_array, to_array', of_list', cast' and rev'' are bare backend_switch blocks with no Lua arm and no static guard, so seq on Lua throws at run time. That part needs Lua arms (Lua tables, as listm's Lua arms use). Also LANDED tonight (window 3, sm' 2196F9C70148): sm'.char_code/of_char Lua arms, with a UTF-8 decoder in Spiral. Lua prints 65,233,8364,é for A, é and €.")
inl note_of_wow_lua_sandbox_4 () = Note ("WOW-LUA-SANDBOX", 4u32, "2026-10-29T22:53Z", "main", "10-29 18:5x main: PLAN for seq on Lua, read in full; not drafted tonight because the lib is frozen under the pack-7 review and every arm needs a Lua proof. seq' is a Lua table, the same as list' (list'_switch and seq_switch' both lack a Lua entry; that is fine, since listm's Lua arms work untyped). Per function: length' = `##(items)`; to_list' / of_list' / of_array / of_array' / to_array' / cast' = a COPY through `arraym.init len (i => items[i + 1])`, the shape proven tonight for cons' on Lua. Do NOT alias like the Python arms do (am'.to_list' aliasing was bug 6 today), and do NOT use `{unpack(t)}`, which overflows Lua 5.1's stack on large tables. rev'' = the fixed listm'.rev' walk (ascending cons'). new_seq has no Lua arm and needs a generator story (collect yields into a table). Proof plan: probe each function on Lua against Rust, with a mutation-after-copy case for aliasing, then a lua row on the existing seq tests. Estimated 120m (D3).")
inl note_of_wow_lua_sandbox_5 () = Note ("WOW-LUA-SANDBOX", 5u32, "2026-10-30T01:59Z", "main", "10-29 21:59 main, groom: prior art for this epic is in DOTA-LUA-BINDINGS (40%, typed Spiral bindings over a game's Lua 5.1 API from annotations), now P4 under the superseded DOTA. Read it before drafting the LuaLS-annotation bindings here.")
inl note_of_wow_lua_sandbox_6 () = Note ("WOW-LUA-SANDBOX", 6u32, "2026-10-31T02:43Z", "tibia-lane", "10-30 22:43 tibia: correction to note 5. DOTA-LUA-BINDINGS' code is lost (its note 7), so there is no generator to reuse, only the method in its notes 1-3. Two tibia pieces are for this epic's entry build: (1) live/paths.ps1 Update-ArenaProbes (line 13): rebuild generated Lua only when a .spi/.spiproj/.toml is newer, originally meant for the Dota repo (TIBIA-REUSE-EXEMPLAR); (2) tibia's no-hand-Lua layout (TIBIA-NO-HAND-LUA): host-free lua.spi plus build-time checks that the generated Lua parses. Lua lib gaps to close first are in note 3 (seq has no Lua arm, sm'.char_code/of_char).")
inl note_of_wow_lua_sandbox_7 () = Note ("WOW-LUA-SANDBOX", 7u32, "2026-11-01T06:01Z", "tibia-lane", "11-01 02:1x tibia HARVEST (TIBIA-HARVEST): what tibia's Lua work teaches the WoW repo, as templates to copy on day one. (1) TWO-MODULE SPLIT: tibia keeps addon-like logic host-free in apps/arena/lua.spi, and every host call (OTClient g_game.*, g_settings, g_logger) in one typed boundary module, client.spi, each binding a named one-expression macro. The build REJECTS host names in the host-free module (apps/arena/build.ps1 greps os.|io.|debug.|g_*|require|dofile). WoW: addon logic in a host-free module; C_* APIs, frames and events behind one bindings module generated from LuaLS annotations; the same grep gate for WoW globals. (2) EVENTS: tibia's hooks.spi wraps host callbacks by replacing dispatch functions and setting fields on Lua tables (set_field, replace0, replace_dispatch). WoW's frame:RegisterEvent/OnEvent and hooksecurefunc map to the same shape: one typed event union, one dispatcher. (3) BUILD GATE per entry: compile every module with a main to Lua (BuildSpiral -Backend Lua), then load-check the output with a Lua interpreter (loadfile), failing the build on a parse error. For WoW use a Lua 5.1 interpreter, since 5.1 is what WoW runs. (4) LUA 5.1 LIMITS (LUA-FUNCTION-LIMITS): generated functions hit 5.1's local and upvalue caps; tibia split big functions by hand. Watch integer vs float (KINO-TEST-CELL-I64-AS-F64: wide i64 shows as f64) and keep calls fully applied, because eta-reduction makes closures (memory spiral-eta-reduction-makes-closures). (5) HEADLESS: tibia tested against fakes (python fake servers in world_port and world_executor) and an offscreen desktop for the real client; WoW's equivalent is wowless as the fake world plus Cells test blocks with ///> lua, the real client only for the final check. (6) TRACES: tibia's stamped trace lines ('knight+N seen at x,y: ...') are analysed with kit table sequences (--section-on/--count/--count-in/--in-set; pz-in-traces.ps1 is the template). WoW: stamp lines into SavedVariables or chat, then analyse with the same kit verbs, no ad-hoc scripts. (7) DAY-ONE GUARDS from tibia's mistakes: generated Lua is never hand-edited (memory no-hand-crafted-lua); test fixtures write under file_system.get_temp_path (memory test-outputs-to-temp); the cell-budget gate is mix spiral.cell_budget --check with the :: spelling from the first file (memory postability-generic-no-x); test discovery matches ^(test|:#) ; work happens in the repo, never in a lab/ dir or a drafts folder; data tables are typed Spiral rows (DATA-ROWS-TYPED); the build is a Spiral program, not a .ps1 (PS1-TO-SPIRAL). Lua lib gaps to close first are this item's note 3 (seq has no Lua arm; sm'.char_code/of_char).")
inl note_of_wow_lua_sandbox_8 () = Note ("WOW-LUA-SANDBOX", 8u32, "2026-11-01T12:36Z", "tibia-lane", "11-01 08:36 tibia, TYPEFEATURESET PLAN for the API BINDINGS (proposal from the tibia harvest; main owns the item). The WoW API is stringly at its core (RegisterEvent('UNIT_HEALTH'), unit tokens 'player' and 'party1', CreateFrame('Button')); the bindings must stop that at the boundary. (1) EVENTS as a GADT indexed by the PAYLOAD: ':| event p = | PlayerLogin : event () | UnitHealth : event unit_token | CombatLogUnfiltered : event combat_log_row | BagUpdate : event bag_slot ...', with 'on (e : event p) (handler : p -> ())', so a handler with the wrong argument type does not compile; one prototype 'event_name' maps each case to its WoW string in ONE place (the only string table). (2) UNIT TOKENS as a union: unit_token = | Player | Target | Focus | Party of party_index | Raid of raid_index, with party_index and raid_index as nominal bounded ints; no 'party1' strings at call sites. (3) FRAMES phantom-indexed by widget kind: 'frame k' with k = button | font_string | texture | status_bar, and the methods per kind through prototypes (SetText only where k = font_string, SetValue only on status_bar); an existential 'some_frame = exists k. frame k' for frame lists. (4) Lua 5.1 numbers are doubles, so ids and counts cross the boundary as nominal ints, with the conversion in the binding layer and tested. (5) combat_log_row as the GADT pattern again, indexed by sub-event (SPELL_DAMAGE carries spell * amount, SWING_DAMAGE only amount): no 'arg12 means spell id' indexing. Models: tibia offscreen.spi's typed launch record and parse_error union (11-01, green on lua + rust); lib json_schema decode-to-union for the combat log. DEFINITION OF DONE for the bindings slice: one sample addon (health-bar plus combat-log counter) compiles to Lua 5.1 and loads in wowless headless with 0 errors; an event test fires UNIT_HEALTH and COMBAT_LOG_EVENT_UNFILTERED through the GADT handlers and asserts the frame state; negative: a mistyped handler fails to compile (recorded as a compile-fail sample).")
inl note_of_wow_lua_sandbox_9 () = Note ("WOW-LUA-SANDBOX", 9u32, "2026-11-01T12:43Z", "main", "main 08:4x: sliced into WOW-WOWLESS-HARNESS -> WOW-API-BINDINGS -> WOW-FIRST-ADDON -> WOW-SIM-FIDELITY, each with its own DoD, typefeatureset plan and next step (tibia's round-4 flag). This epic is done when all four are. It still follows tibia (memory wow-after-tibia); slice 1 can start once TIBIA-HARVEST closes.")
inl note_of_wow_lua_sandbox_10 () = Note ("WOW-LUA-SANDBOX", 10u32, "2026-11-02T13:07Z", "tibia", "11-02 tibia: PAUSED with its 4 slices, on the user 11-02: \"let's forget dota or wow for now, with transformer ready we'll know how to model it better (which would probably be auctionator related)\". Resume when TYPED-TRANSFORMER-LAB is Done. Expect a re-plan at that point, possibly toward an Auctionator-related addon.")
inl delphi_file_read_buffer () = Task ("DELPHI-FILE-READ-BUFFER", "Delphi file reads need a caller-owned buffer: FPC's FileRead(h, var Buffer, n) writes into a by-reference untyped buffer, and granular style forbids a global Pascal helper body, so lib file_system.read_all_text/read_all_bytes have no Delphi arm. Options: a Spiral array u8 local (Delphi dynamic array; FileRead(h, a[0], n) as one call) plus a portable bytes-to-string primitive, or a compiler primitive for a sized uninitialized local a macro can pass by reference", TaskKind, P2, D4, 100u32, Done, "SPIRAL-ASSIGN-PRIMITIVE", "delphi,lib,compiler,granular", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_delphi_file_read_buffer () = Planning ("DELPHI-FILE-READ-BUFFER", Minutes 120u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_delphi_file_read_buffer_1 () = Note ("DELPHI-FILE-READ-BUFFER", 1u32, "2026-10-25T16:41Z", "fiscal", "Found 2026-10-25 by fiscal while drafting FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH step 1 (n10/n11). The write side needs no buffer: FileCreate/FileWrite/FileClose take an integer handle, one host call each, flow in Spiral (delphi-arms/apply.py). Generated fiscal modules never read files (census: only write_all_text), so step 1 does not wait on this.")
inl note_of_delphi_file_read_buffer_2 () = Note ("DELPHI-FILE-READ-BUFFER", 2u32, "2026-10-30T18:48Z", "compiler", "10-30 14:48 compiler: DRAFTED AND PROVEN on a scratch lib copy (lib = the live lib after the 13:02 window, file_system.spi the only difference), not landed yet. Option A turned out simpler than the title's two options: no array and no new bytes-to-string primitive. It mirrors the existing Delphi write arm (which already passes (!text)[!first] by reference into FileWrite), one host call per macro with the flow in Spiral: handle = FileOpen(path, fmOpenRead) (-1 fails); size = FileSeek(handle, 0, 2); FileSeek(handle, 0, 0) must return 0; text = StringOfChar(Chr(0), size); when size > 0, FileRead(handle, (text)[1], size) must return size; FileClose; return text. Delphi string is AnsiString under {$mode delphi}{$H+}, so sizes are bytes, the same as sm'.length and the write arm. Trap found on the way: '#' is a macro escape (a literal '#' is '##'), so the first draft's StringOfChar(#0, ..) failed in the tokenizer; Chr(0) fixes it. Test added: '///> delphi test delphi_read_all_text_reads_back_what_was_written' (write 'ab', read 'ab'; write '', read ''). Gate: lib_gate -File file_system.spi -Lib <scratch> -Compiler process -Test <that test> passes 1/1 (job-1793386062875). Positive control: the same test on a copy without the arm FAILS (it falls to '| _ => null ()'). Draft: C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\C--home-git\\88a146f8-6f7b-491c-8667-37bc317a7a0d\\scratchpad\\delphi-read\\lib\\spiral\\file_system.spi (diff vs live = the arm + the test, about 21 lines). LEFT: land in a lib window (do + undo, then the same gate on the live lib); then read_all_bytes, if a consumer needs it.")
inl note_of_delphi_file_read_buffer_3 () = Note ("DELPHI-FILE-READ-BUFFER", 3u32, "2026-10-30T19:08Z", "compiler", "10-30 15:08 compiler: LANDED AND GATED, DONE. lib/spiral/file_system.spi 14BFAD9B2784 -> F1DB08E77EAECB840F9FE069895C9712A64F441587F307A115D2C7EBFCE966D7 (unstaged, in main's lib window; undo: C:\\home\\data\\undo\\DELPHI-FILE-READ-BUFFER, land-delphi-read.ps1 -Undo). Pre-land: a scratch-copy proof (1/1, and the control without the arm fails), plus a Cells round trip of the draft (round-trips true, fixpoint true). main's close: kit's 14 signatures unchanged, document_test 22/22, type_probe 35 typed, 0 errors. My live-lib gate (job-1793386352218): lib_gate -File file_system.spi on every default target 35/35, type errors 0, including the new delphi_read_all_text_reads_back_what_was_written; the live file round-trips as a fixpoint. read_all_bytes stays without a Delphi arm until a consumer needs it (same shape, with a byte array buffer).")
inl eoie_remote_executor () = Task ("EOIE-REMOTE-EXECUTOR", "eoie runs jobs on more than one machine: a supervised Gleam coordinator per machine with typed slot/lease/job typestate, the existing Rust job runner on each, SSH transport with Tailscale fallback, placement by free slot and load", Feature, P0, D6, 15u32, Planned, "REMOTE-LAPTOP-LANE,EOIE-MACHINE-HEALTH,KIT-REMOTE-SYNC,REMOTE-EXEC-S12", "kit,remote,gleam,rust,typefeatureset,eoie-team,wipe-redo,team:kit", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_eoie_remote_executor () = Planning ("EOIE-REMOTE-EXECUTOR", Minutes 1200u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_eoie_remote_executor_1 () = Note ("EOIE-REMOTE-EXECUTOR", 1u32, "2026-10-25T16:55Z", "main", "10-25 12:55 main (user 10-25 12:0x: keep the laptop at ~100% CPU like this box so it unclogs the queue; build it with the typefeatureset and a deliberate Gleam/Rust split, coordinated between main and the eoie team). Design, by memory backend-balance (Gleam where BEAM pays: actors, supervision; Rust where speed pays):\n(1) Gleam coordinator: a supervised actor per machine (OTP supervisor tree). It owns that machine's slot table (heavy/compile slots, quiet windows) as typed state and restarts a dead SSH link without losing queued jobs. Machine, slot, job and lease are nominal types, and job state is a typestate union (Queued | Leased of machine | Running of machine * pid | Settled of outcome), so a job can't be run twice or settle without a lease.\n(2) Rust runner on each machine: the existing eoie job supervisor, unchanged in semantics (process tree, wall clock, memory cap, logs). The coordinator only adds 'which machine'.\n(3) Transport: OpenSSH (key auth, already working: Host laptop in ~/.ssh/config, HostKeyAlias), with Tailscale as the fallback route. No new daemon or port.\n(4) Placement rule: a heavy job goes to the machine with a free heavy slot and the lowest load; jobs that read a live tree (Cells, lib gates, matrix) stay on the machine holding that tree until tree sync (git push to a bare repo on the laptop) is proven by hash.\n(5) Health: EOIE-MACHINE-HEALTH covers both machines; the stall rule is per job label family.\nUser-side blockers first: Tailscale login approval, the firewall scope (LocalSubnet,100.64.0.0/10), and deleting WoW Data (107.5 GB) to free the laptop's disk; addons + WTF are backed up to C:\\home\\data\\wow-retail-11.2.7. Then bootstrap over SSH: scoop, pwsh 7, dotnet SDK, rustup with the pinned nightly-2026-10-18 + wasm32, Erlang/Elixir/Gleam, zig 0.17.0, gcc 15.2, lua 5.1, the repos, the eoie host.")
inl note_of_eoie_remote_executor_2 () = Note ("EOIE-REMOTE-EXECUTOR", 2u32, "2026-10-25T17:31Z", "eoie", "13:31 kit groom (eoie team), sequencing as main asked.\n- Order: SLATE -> edition-3 respell -> KIT-RENAME (eoie is in a feature freeze until then) -> EOIE-MACHINE-HEALTH -> this item. New packages are born kit_* names, so the rename doesn't touch them.\n- Milestones (pre-registered; estimate 1200m = their sum):\n  M1 180m: kit remote status <host>. It runs the remote host's kit over ssh and returns typed slots, load and cores (cores from the MACHINE-HEALTH sampler: cpu_usage/100 x CPU count). Needs main's laptop bootstrap.\n  M2 240m: job start --on <machine> (Rust side). The job record stores its machine; status, tail, stop and wait route by machine. Remote semantics unchanged: the remote kit supervisor runs the job.\n  M3 360m: the Gleam coordinator. A supervised actor per machine with the slot table, the job typestate (Queued | Leased | Running | Settled) and the placement rule from note 1.\n  M4 240m: tree-sync proof. A live-tree job goes remote only when the src tree hash on both machines is equal; otherwise it stays local.\n  M5 180m: end to end. Two heavy jobs queued run one per machine, and the laptop holds >=90% CPU over 10 min while the queue is not empty.\n- Decision rules (written before measuring):\n  (a) The coordinator stays resident (one BEAM node per machine) when a per-call BEAM start pushes dispatch past 2 s p95; it is per-call only when it stays under that.\n  (b) The coordinator itself costs <1% of a core over 10 min, or it moves to Rust and Gleam keeps only supervision.\n  (c) Done when M5 passes and MACHINE-HEALTH reports both machines.")
inl note_of_eoie_remote_executor_3 () = Note ("EOIE-REMOTE-EXECUTOR", 3u32, "2026-10-25T17:42Z", "eoie", "13:42 kit: tree sync decision (git stays the user's on both machines; asked by the guide lane, which drives the laptop bootstrap). Note 1's 'git push to a bare repo' is replaced.\n- Decision: file copy over the existing SSH channel (tar + scp/sftp of the src slice, extracted on the laptop). M4's proof is unchanged: the src tree hashes must be equal on both machines before a live-tree job goes remote.\n- kit owns no internal repo bus: from-scratch means kit's own primitives (eoie_tree_copy, snapshot hashing), not a git dependency.\n- Pre-registered escalation rule: if a full src-slice sync takes over 30 s p95 over the LAN, add a kit hash-manifest delta (send only files whose hash differs, then the same tree-hash proof). Still no git.\n- M4 estimate unchanged (240m).")
inl note_of_eoie_remote_executor_4 () = Note ("EOIE-REMOTE-EXECUTOR", 4u32, "2026-10-25T17:59Z", "eoie", "13:59 kit: M4 refinement, agreed with kit2, who owns the remote plumbing (host deploy, interim offload as org tooling i574n.github/scripts/remote-run.ps1).\n- M4's tree-hash proof is enforced per offload. The tool builds a per-file sha256 manifest of the src slice, tars it (excluding target, .git, .cache), rebuilds the manifest after extraction into C:\\home\\remote\\<name> (never the laptop's user checkouts), and refuses to run on a mismatch.\n- Rule kept beyond the input proof: jobs that write into a live tree here stay local even with equal input hashes, because their outputs race with edits made here (kit regen/publish, lib lands, matrix accepts).\n  Read-only compiles, tests and reviews may go remote; results come back to scratch or job folders, never into a repo tree.\n- Host binary: the canonical installed eoie.exe (not the supervisor relaunch copies). Redeploy after SLATE installs, and record both sha256s.")
inl note_of_eoie_remote_executor_5 () = Note ("EOIE-REMOTE-EXECUTOR", 5u32, "2026-10-25T18:00Z", "kit2", "13:59 kit2 owns (per split) host-deploy (scp a built eoie.exe, freeze-safe, hash recorded here) and the interim offload tooling (i574n.github/scripts, folds into M2 after rename); kit keeps the typed-coordinator design. The offload tool enforces M4 per job: local vs remote manifest-hash equality before any remote run.")
inl note_of_eoie_remote_executor_6 () = Note ("EOIE-REMOTE-EXECUTOR", 6u32, "2026-10-25T18:03Z", "kit2", "14:03 kit2: host-deploy artifact confirmed = C:\\home\\git\\eoie\\eoie.exe, 19.9MB, sha256 cdef7564d80483bc13bae7def9d0c1d1edb14b54f171c7941e42e358ad56dc75 (per kit: canonical installed CLI, not the supervisor relaunch copies; host recreates those on first job). Deploy gated on fork-idle; proves 'ssh laptop eoie.exe job list' answers. Redeploy + record new sha256 after SLATE installs the next eoie.exe.")
inl note_of_eoie_remote_executor_7 () = Note ("EOIE-REMOTE-EXECUTOR", 7u32, "2026-10-25T18:13Z", "kit2", "14:13 kit2: host-deploy VERIFIED read-only. Laptop C:\\home\\bin\\eoie.exe sha256 == C:\\home\\git\\eoie\\eoie.exe (cdef7564...), MATCH. The fork placed it; M1 binary precondition met ('ssh laptop eoie.exe job list' can answer). Do NOT source-build eoie on the laptop until SLATE: 8 hand-split crates have committed .spi but no .rs, clean clone fails on eoie_job_queue/decide.rs (EOIE-HAND-CRATE-UNITS note 11). Redeploy + re-record sha after SLATE installs the next eoie.exe.")
inl note_of_eoie_remote_executor_8 () = Note ("EOIE-REMOTE-EXECUTOR", 8u32, "2026-10-25T18:13Z", "kit2", "14:13 kit2 (user Q: can the retired llm-browser-tab bundle features serve multi-machine transport?): YES, strongly. eoie_bundle_manifest + eoie_archive_tar_runtime are already the typed native transport: collect_bundle_manifest -> canonical sorted deduped manifest (symlink/special/unsafe/nested-zip rejected); manifest_fingerprint -> FNV-1a tree hash; write_deterministic_store_zip/deflate_zip -> REPRODUCIBLE archives (fixed timestamps, canonical order) so same content => same bytes => same hash, a stronger M4 proof than a content-only manifest; verify_archive_parity/verify_manifest_parity -> received==expected; archive_extract_generic_run -> budgeted extraction with confined links + rollback. The archive/bundle CLI surfaces exist (eoie_archive_contracts, eoie_bundle_retention). Plan: interim remote-run.ps1 (tar+scp+manifest) now; after the freeze a native 'eoie remote sync' verb built on these replaces it. Folds into M2/M4; my PowerShell tool is the hand-rolled stand-in for exactly this pipeline.")
inl note_of_eoie_remote_executor_9 () = Note ("EOIE-REMOTE-EXECUTOR", 9u32, "2026-10-25T18:14Z", "kit2", "14:14 kit2 research (user: study deltadb/anthropic scion/fossil, beat them with typefeatureset+iggy). FINDINGS: Anthropic Scion (eng blog 2026-10-13 anthropic.com/engineering/redesigning-source-control) internal git replacement: server authoritative for history/blame/merge, CLIENTS FETCH ONLY NEEDED FILES (no full copy), pull 25x/status 207x/worktree 84x, not public. Fossil (fossil-scm.org): single-file SQLite repo, autosync over HTTP/SSH, serverless, atomic txns. DeltaDB (npm): offline-first, delta storage, LWW, eventually consistent. Iggy (iggy.apache.org incubating 0.9.0): append-only segment log (.log+.index), thread-per-core shared-nothing (io_uring/compio), streams>topics>partitions, consumer offsets, at-least-once+dedup. HOW WE BEAT THEM (typefeatureset+iggy+bundle): (1) ONE typed Spiral GADT model (job/lease/slot/health events + bundle manifest) -> Rust data plane + Gleam control plane, byte-identical codecs, typestate makes illegal job transitions unrepresentable - typed where all four are untyped. (2) Scion thin-fetch = our bundle-manifest slice, content-addressed by manifest_fingerprint + deterministic archives, hash-proven (M4) and delta-reducible (kit >30s escalation): Scion thin-fetch + Fossil content-addressing + DeltaDB deltas combined. (3) iggy = churn-safe event backbone, re-derivable from receipts. (4) KEY DIFFERENCE: Scion replaces git wholesale; we KEEP git as the human source-of-truth on the review box and use bundle+iggy only for the machine-to-machine plane. Outstanding: deltadb ambiguous (npm offline-first vs pypi polars/deltalake); took the npm sync one.")
inl note_of_eoie_remote_executor_10 () = Note ("EOIE-REMOTE-EXECUTOR", 10u32, "2026-10-25T20:19Z", "kit2", "16:19 kit2 - typed coordinator design, consolidating the interim lessons (L1-L8) + Iggy (now up) into the implementation spec for after KIT-RENAME. MESSAGE MODEL: one Spiral GADT family - job | lease | slot | health events + bundle manifest - compiled to Rust (data plane, iggy crate over TCP 8090) and Gleam (control plane, iggy HTTP 3000), byte-identical codecs, typestate indices (Job: Queued|Leased of slot|Running of slot*pid|Settled of outcome; Slot: Free|Held of job; so two writers on one resource is unrepresentable - L4). PLACEMENT: slots per machine from MACHINE-HEALTH cores (cpu_usage/100 x count); a heavy job leases the machine with a free slot and lowest load; slots run concurrently to saturate (L8 - serial underutilizes). PER-JOB PRECONDITIONS (proven in the interim): sync into an isolated worktree mirror C:\\home\\remote\\<job>\\<repo> (L1), proven by per-file sha256 over git diff HEAD + ls-files -o incl. staged + --diff-filter=D deletes with empty-dir prune; the mirror sync is lease-free (isolated), so no whole-machine guard (interim guard-skip-for--Mirror); junction gitignored deps from the node main clone (worktrees lack deps/). CORE GUARD (L6): a verify carries its expected compiler core; the node's SpiralCompilerCore.dll must match (prefix ok) or the job refuses/rebuilds. DETERMINISM (L7): correctness is OUTPUT equality (-Suite row-by-row), NOT DLL-byte - cross-machine builds differ in non-semantic metadata (MVID/paths) from the same source+SDK, so install-by-hash is same-machine only. IGGY: events on streams>topics>partitions, offsets server-stored, at-least-once + dedup by message id; every event re-derivable from job receipts (churn-safe, pre-1.0). ssh -L tunnels 13000->3000, 18090->8090; creds in k8s secret iggy/iggy-root (per-service users, never printed). Interim org tooling (remote.ps1 -Mirror/-RequireCore, node-dispatch queue, node-watch) maps 1:1 onto this and retires into it. Fork owns the WSL/k3s infra (done); kit2 owns this model+codec, post-rename.")
inl note_of_eoie_remote_executor_11 () = Note ("EOIE-REMOTE-EXECUTOR", 11u32, "2026-10-25T20:41Z", "kit2", "16:41 kit2 - CROSS-MACHINE COMPILE DETERMINISM PROVEN (the fork's handed-off check, done as output equality per L7, not DLL-hash). Node ran -Suite all compile-only (7352 jobs, 470s) from the synced source d095f13d (digest FE6BB515) with its current-source compiler (826D67C6). Diff node results.tsv vs review-box single-flight-20261025-123908 on (compile,residual) keyed by id,backend: 7352 rows each, changed 0 / added 0 / removed 0. So despite the node's SpiralCompilerCore.dll bytes differing from the box's (826D67C6 vs CDD68DC3; L7 non-semantic metadata), compilation OUTPUT is byte-identical row-for-row. CONCLUSION: the node is a VALID build/verify node - offloads there reproduce the review box exactly. Output equality is the right determinism measure, confirmed. (Note: eoie do diff took 200s for 7352 rows - slower than eoie table diff's ms; minor papercut.)")
inl note_of_eoie_remote_executor_12 () = Note ("EOIE-REMOTE-EXECUTOR", 12u32, "2026-10-28T13:44Z", "eoie", "09:5x kit: M1 (kit remote status) drafted as a host-free crate, scratch clean/remote-status/src/kit_remote_status (status.spi, entry.spi, standards HostFree + LibReferenced). No new host code: ssh runs through lib process.try_capture_combined_list (as kit_job_chain and kit_ci_probe already do) with BatchMode=yes and ConnectTimeout=10, so a dead link returns instead of hanging. The remote command is '<binary> window list'. Its summary line ('<tool> window list ok windows=N free_mb=N heavy_slots=N') becomes a typed remote_status: RemoteReported { windows; free_mb; heavy_slots } or RemoteUnreachable reason (the trimmed ssh output, e.g. 'connect ... timed out'). The parser keys on ' window list ok ', so it reads both the node's eoie.exe (C:\\home\\bin\\eoie.exe, still the old name) and kit. Read live from the node at 09:4x: 'eoie window list ok windows=5 free_mb=3805 heavy_slots=0', with five fiscal compile windows held. Test block: a real-shaped listing parses into the record; an ssh error is RemoteUnreachable; a field at the line end parses; a missing field is 0; the status line text. Deliberately left out of M1: cores and load. 'machine watch' over ssh did not return within 90 s on the busy node, so cores come in M1b from a bounded sample (NUMBER_OF_PROCESSORS plus one cpu sample), not the full watch. Next: compile on the node with the next bundle, then route 'remote status <alias> [--bin path]' through kit_cli (one subcommand row). Routing drafted at 09:4x (remote-status-wiring.do, dry-run clean on the TRAIN4 copy, 5 replacements): a window verb, 'kit window remote <ssh-alias> [--bin <remote kit path>]' (default bin 'kit'), since it reports another machine's windows; one dispatch row plus a one-expression cross-crate call in kit_machine_windows, the kit-remote-status Cargo dependency, a workspace member and the usage text. Compile waits for a free node: at 09:4x it was at 100% CPU with 1.9 GB free and tibia's queue.")
inl note_of_eoie_remote_executor_13 () = Note ("EOIE-REMOTE-EXECUTOR", 13u32, "2026-10-29T22:42Z", "kit", "2026-10-29T22:40 kit: shipped in TRAIN4c (installed kit C528BE4D). M1 shipped: kit window remote <ssh-alias> [--bin] (kit_remote_status, HostFree + LibReferenced; ssh through lib process.try_capture_combined_list with BatchMode and ConnectTimeout).")
inl note_of_eoie_remote_executor_14 () = Note ("EOIE-REMOTE-EXECUTOR", 14u32, "2026-11-01T03:37Z", "kit", "10-31 23:4x kit groom (main 23:33, user: cadence/drift): Active -> PLANNED. No note in over 12 h, so nobody is working it this week; Planned keeps it in agile next at its priority (never auto-Paused, R3). First step when picked = the next step in its latest note.")
inl note_of_eoie_remote_executor_15 () = Note ("EOIE-REMOTE-EXECUTOR", 15u32, "2026-11-01T12:43Z", "kit", "11-01 08:4x kit (main: slice it like SCRUM-XP): SLICED into REMOTE-EXEC-S1..S12 (45-60 min each, P0, team:kit, light, chained by deps), each with its DoD, NEXT STEP and TYPEFEATURESET PLAN. M1 shipped (TRAIN4c). M2 = S1-S4 (typed placement, job start --on, routed status/tail/stop/wait, retire remote.ps1 job verbs); M4 = S5 on top of KIT-REMOTE-SYNC (manifest set equality, live-tree writers stay local); M3 = S6-S10 (shared job/slot typestate, placement rule, supervised Gleam actor, decision rules (a)/(b) measured, dispatch surviving SSH drops); M5 = S11-S12 (two heavy jobs one per machine; node >= 90% CPU for 10 min). This epic now depends on S12, so it closes when the last slice and MACHINE-HEALTH do. First light step: REMOTE-EXEC-S1 or S6 (independent).")
inl postable_all_repos () = Task ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", "Run the postable split over every tracked .spi in every repo as one report: the batch xc post-thread --dry-run lands, a generic report command lists files and cells over one post per repo, and each repo burns its count down to 0 (x is at 0 today)", Story, P1, D3, 85u32, Active, "", "team:x,x,postability,splitter,e2e,typefeatures,ps1-to-spiral", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_postable_all_repos () = Planning ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", Minutes 240u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_1 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 1u32, "2026-10-25T17:24Z", "x", "2026-10-25 13:24 -04:00 x: user question 10-25 ('how far from running the splitter in every code file we have, so we're ready to e2e test everything outside gh actions'). Baseline from X-COMPAT-VS-POSTABLE n7 (every 8th file): x 0/352 cells over one post, dice 23%, tibia 25%, lib 37%, eoie 40%. Milestones (estimates-from-rules): M1 the batch dry-run lands (drafted, X-BACKEND-MAP n5); M2 a report over all repos in one call, one row per repo; M3 lib and eoie fixed by splitting definitions by hand (the hand-modeled rule, never an automatic splitter); M4 each repo's build gates on 0, as x's does. Non-Spiral code is out of scope: the cell rule is Spiral's, and most .rs/.py/.c is generated.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_2 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 2u32, "2026-10-25T18:00Z", "x", "2026-10-25 14:00 -04:00 x: M1 redrafted for today's tree (the 10-23 draft predated the edition-3 respell and its Cons anchors are gone): scratchpad\\batch-dry-run\\apply.ps1. It adds post-thread --dry-run FILE FILE... = cli PreviewThreads (list string), fields kind 11, and xc run_preview_threads, which prints '=== FILE' before each preview and exits with the worst code. Dispatch goes through a new run_thread_more, so kind 11 is explicit and can never fall through to the posting else. Overweight definitions were split by hand (previews, thread_batch_text, run_thread_more, a separate parse test, usage_thread_previews), and the result is checked on copies: 0 cells over one post, lines over 120 unchanged (5/3/6), tree x-compat 0 findings. --check on a package copy with absolute packageDir: cli.spi and xc.spi 'checked package' (exit 0). Exit 3 with no message on some runs, including the UNPATCHED xc.spi, is 'Attestation timed out after 30000 ms' on the loaded box, not a type error; patched fields.spi needs a quieter re-check. Gate before landing: the fields re-check, then x's build (release xc + e2e -Accept for the new usage line + postable) after 5l'' installs.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_3 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 3u32, "2026-10-25T18:16Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-25 14:16 tibia census (full, not sampled; scratch postable-census.ps1 mirroring x splitter.spi: a cell is a column-0 block after a blank line, one post = weight <= 273 = 280 minus the 'c1 1/1' header, non-ASCII glyphs weigh 2): 62 files, 1328 cells, 399 over one post (30 %). Top: src/hunting_pack 81/217, src/terrain 31/63 (heaviest 11383, generated by apps/map/run.ps1), src/pack_sim 29/39, src/hunt_sim 22/49, apps/map/map 20/29 (8200), apps/host/arena_host 15/28, combat 12/35, network 12/36, offscreen 12/48, geometry 11/41. Rule for tibia's M3, written before any split: (1) split by hand, never by a tool: a long definition becomes named helpers by role (one concept per definition), a long test becomes several tests or a fixture definition plus the test; a long union/record type splits into nested named types only when the parts are concepts of their own, otherwise its constructors move to one per line so a blank-line cut is legal. (2) generated cells (terrain, map) are fixed in their generator (run.ps1 emits data in chunks of at most 273). (3) files touched by queued slices wait for those lands (hunting_pack and gpu_sim for PARTY, samples, lua and random for serial-b). (4) each batch: the census count for its files drops, formatter clean, the files' Cells tests pass with memo off, no behaviour change. (5) order: files with the fewest over-post cells first, so the per-file count reaches 0 quickly and a build gate per file can follow (M4).")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_4 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 4u32, "2026-10-25T18:37Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-25 14:37 tibia batch 1 drafted (scratch postable-b1, land via postable-land.ps1 in job-1792953363791): activity, blessings, damage and hunt go from 7 over-post cells to 0 (35 to 50 cells). Splits: activity's test gets a checked_counts fixture and becomes two tests; blessings.dropped gets item_chance, its 6-case test becomes 3 by case group; damage's 8-case formula test becomes 4 and the fight-mode test 2; hunt gets a living type for attack's signature and its 1600-case loot test becomes struck_fresh, gold_conserved, loot_holds, case_purse and loot_breaks with a one-line test. Every ~dyn binding stays, so the C targets still compute at run time. The runner refuses when a tree file changed since the batch copy or a copy still has an over-post cell, then runs those files' Cells tests with memo off and restores on red.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_5 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 5u32, "2026-10-25T18:54Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-25 14:54 tibia batch 2 drafted (scratch postable-b2, step 1 of job-1792953363791 after batch 1): wire, vocations, combat_text, scoreboard and probe go to 0 over-post cells (99 to 104 cells, no new line over 120). Splits: wire's XTEA becomes xtea_term, encrypt/decrypt steps and top-level rounds, and adler_of folds a byte list for the Adler test; vocations' data_of becomes healing and berserk constructors (cooldown_id_of keeps its two special cases and a wildcard for the three LightHealingSpell ones); combat_text bundles before/ends into number_pattern with number_at for one occurrence; scoreboard's summary becomes action and outcome count texts, vitals and experience_text (same string), and finish_or_continue gets report_ended and report_cleared; probe's nearest_monster becomes closer plus a top-level nearest_from scan, and started_as gets hidden_for_a_second, login_failed, entered_game and enter_after_the_gate. The land runner now also runs the arena build (LUA-OK for every module) when a batch touches apps/arena, since scoreboard and probe have no tests of their own.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_6 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 6u32, "2026-10-25T19:04Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-25 15:04 tibia rule (3) extended before landing anything: the 16 files the pending CELLS-APPS candidate re-land replaces whole (arena adjacency, client, competitor, measure, nexbot, observer, path, probe, random, scan, scoreboard, steps, summon, vbot; map; predict) wait for that re-land, or it would drop their postable splits. probe and scoreboard came out of batch 2 (their drafts are kept in scratch postable-held); batch 2 is now combat_text, vocations and wire. Batch 3 drafted (bot_dofile: chunk_run_in, load_failed, loaded_chunk and dofile_through stay inline, so error(message, 3) still names the dofile caller; bot_prelude: one silent_table builder for g_http and HTTP; standards: its 30 one-line rules grouped by blank lines), 3 files at 0 over-post cells, queued in serial-c after batches 1 and 2.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_7 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 7u32, "2026-10-25T19:13Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-25 15:13 tibia batch 4 drafted (scratch postable-b4, in serial-c): readiness, experience, cooldowns and views go from 19 over-post cells to 0 (no new line over 120). readiness gets an instant alias, closed_by, gate_code and busy_after_strike, and its gates test splits by gate; experience gets experience_left and its multi-assertion tests split by concern; cooldowns gets one setter per spell timer behind a first-order with_own_ready_at (no function values, since cooldowns runs on WGSL), with_group_ready_at for after_cast, and after_strike_at; views separates its typeclass instance groups by blank lines and shares a four_reaches fixture between the C and WGSL lane tests. Running total once batches 1-4 land: 34 of tibia's 399 over-post cells fixed in 16 files; 16 files wait for the CELLS-APPS re-land.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_8 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 8u32, "2026-10-25T19:21Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-25 15:21 tibia batch 5 drafted (scratch postable-b5, in serial-c): apps/host scenario and block_plan go from 8 over-post cells to 0. scenario: named_count for order_of, a top-level collected for mix_of, order_entry for summon_orders (its Cons pattern also becomes list sugar), camp_orders as the test fixture; block_plan: next_slot and a top-level advanced for after, block_progress and block_finished fold outcomes so the stage-walk test becomes one test per step, and the fate and probe tests split by concern. live and search (heavier: live's main returns six closures to its tests, search's two nested evolve loops) get their own batch. Once batches 1-5 land: 42 of 399 fixed in 18 files.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_9 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 9u32, "2026-10-25T20:09Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-25 16:09 tibia live.spi design note before drafting (batch 6): its main returns a 6-tuple of curried closures that run-policy-scenario.ps1 compiles to Lua and wraps as SpiralPolicy = (function() ... end)(); the probe bodies index that table by position, so a split must keep the same six functions, arity and order (top-level definitions returned in that order, or partial applications like prefers_in CurrentFirst / ReachFirst, keep the Lua shape). Planned helpers: rule_thresholds, engages_code, target_of, prefers_in (4 params then a 3-param lambda, about 270), decision_weights, decision_code, and scaled inputs for network_decision, which needs about four helpers. search.spi's evolve and evolve_vector loops become top-level generation/children functions. Both wait for a pass with the policy probe's Lua output diffed before and after (rule (4): no behaviour change).")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_10 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 10u32, "2026-10-25T20:49Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-25 16:49 tibia batches 6 and 7 drafted (in serial-c). Batch 6: apps/host character (SQL builders split into vitals, skills and position fragments; expected statements kept byte-identical as fixture fragments, checked against the originals; the kit list split by slot range; with_duo_limits over online_limit/outside_limit) and src/creatures (one stat record per creature, all 70 values checked unchanged; canary_step_speed's 19 segments as a chain of plain branching functions, every segment checked unchanged; tests split by group with an adjacent_melee_count fold), 17 cells to 0. Batch 7: src/policy (prefers_by over reach_first, current_first and scored_first; tests on sighting, aimed, heals_now and engaged_code builders; every original assertion kept, tuple ones split), 9 cells to 0. geometry's sight_line_clear (2356, Canary's sight-line port, used on WGSL) gets its own pass with the GPU test as the gate. Once batches 1-7 land: 68 of 399 fixed in 21 files.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_11 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 11u32, "2026-10-25T20:57Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-25 16:57 tibia: after batches 1-7 (68 of 399 drafted), most of what remains needs a design change, not layout, and gets its own slices. (a) Positional-parameter APIs: combat's melee_hit, swing_stats and capped_hit_stats take six combat values (mode, level, attack_value, attack_skill, defense, armor), so the header alone is about 140 of a 273 post; melee_hit is also called from hunt_sim and hunting_pack, and every loop must keep its rng.roll_key order (C/WGSL tests pin 1435/15398). Plan: a swinger record threaded through combat, hunt_sim and hunting_pack in one slice, gated by those three files' Cells tests; the same pattern likely applies to network, offscreen, arena_host and the sims. (b) Algorithm ports on WGSL (geometry's sight_line_clear, 2356): split per Bresenham case with the GPU test as the gate. (c) live.spi's 6-closure Lua API and search's nested evolve loops (design note above). (d) Generated cells (terrain, map, skills' tables): the generators must emit postable chunks. (e) The 16 CELLS-APPS files wait for that re-land. Drafted scratch for combat (postable-b8) is parked, not queued.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_12 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 12u32, "2026-10-25T21:20Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-25 17:20 combat's parameter-record slice, designed: melee_hit (mode, level, attack_value, attack_skill, defense, armor, key) becomes melee_hit (s : swinger) (target : gear) (key), where gear already exists in combat ({ armor; defense }) and swinger = { mode; level; attack_value; attack_skill } is new; swing_stats and capped_hit_stats take the same two records. Callers: hunt_sim player_hit and hunting_pack (line ~1044), each building the two records, plus a creatures.gear_of (creature_stats -> combat.gear) helper (creatures follows combat in module order). Every rng.roll_key stays as written, so the C and WGSL expectations (1435 and 15398 landed, 13451 total) must come out unchanged. It waits for batch 6's creatures land (same file), then lands as one slice gated by combat, creatures, hunt_sim and hunting_pack Cells.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_13 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 13u32, "2026-10-25T21:28Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-25 17:28 live.spi resolved by a dangling check instead of a split: run-policy-scenario.ps1 still compiles live.spi to policy.lua and injects it as the global SpiralPolicy, but no probe reads that global any more (rg over tibia including target/*.lua, spiral, i574n.github: only the injection line, README's 'Lua entry for OTClient' and BENCHMARK history for PR-1..PR-11, whose hand-written probe-policy.lua bodies NO-HAND-LUA retired). So live.spi's six-closure main has no callers, and per the heal rule (0 callers -> delete, don't heal) it goes: delete src/live.spi, drop the SpiralPolicy injection, the frozen-policy copy and the compile step from run-policy-scenario.ps1, and fix README. Its tests duplicate policy.spi and network.spi coverage (re-checked when deleting). Timing: AFTER the PR-28 live block, since PR-28 is pre-registered with PR-27's harness unchanged. That leaves search.spi as batch 9's file.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_14 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 14u32, "2026-10-25T22:51Z", "tibia-lane", "22:51Z combat swinger slice queued as job-1792968648374-tibia-combat-swinger, after serial-lands. melee_hit, swing_stats and capped_hit_stats now take (s : swinger) (target : gear); bout/tally records replace the positional loops. Fixtures: level_8_axe and armor_and_defense_10. Dry run: anchors exact, 0 lines over 120. The gate runs combat, hunt_sim, hunting_pack, pack_sim and gpu_sim with the memo off; any red restores all 3 files and checks them by hash. It doesn't overlap b1-b7. Expected values unchanged: 435/1393, 1680/28222, 1435/15398, capped 1435/13451.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_15 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 15u32, "2026-10-25T23:04Z", "tibia-lane", "23:04Z geometry slice drafted. Census on the candidate: 0 cells over one post, down from 11 (heaviest was 2356); 0 lines over 120; 0 'Cons ('. Changes: sight_line_clear dispatches to straight_clear (open_run) or sloped_clear, and sloped_clear swaps axes so one ray walk replaces both mirrored branches. straight_step goes through straight_offset with unit. The canary tests share helpers (xy, ramp_shift, walled_grid, grid_scan, grid_clear); 4 tests are split in two, so canary expected values are unchanged. Combat and geometry now land through one serial runner, job-1792969425340-tibia-slices (after serial-lands), which replaced the 2 chained jobs. Geometry is gated on geometry, terrain, hunt_sim, hunting_pack, pack_sim and gpu_sim; generic tool: scratch slice-land.ps1.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_16 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 16u32, "2026-10-25T23:14Z", "tibia-lane", "23:14Z search slice drafted and added as step 3 of job-1792969425340-tibia-slices. Candidate census: 0 cells over one post, down from 6 (heaviest was 2494); 0 lines over 120. API change: evolve (fitness) (s : genome_search) (start) and evolve_vector / evolve_vector_resampled (fitness) (s : vector_search) (start). Rounds are records (genome_round, vector_round: the search plus the scored parent). evolve_vector and evolve_vector_resampled share one pair loop (pairs_from, pair_winner); antithetic_pair takes the round. Plus is still scored before minus, the strict > is kept, evolve_vector still carries parent_score, and resampled still rescores the parent each generation. hunting_pack: the 3 evolve calls use a new tactic_search helper, and the 2 evolve_vector calls build the record inline. The apply checks search.spi against a hash control. Gate: search, hunting_pack, pack_sim with the memo off.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_17 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 17u32, "2026-10-25T23:21Z", "tibia-lane", "23:21Z network slice drafted and added as step 4 of job-1792969425340-tibia-slices. Candidate census: 1 cell over one post, down from 12. That cell is inputs_of, whose 7 positional params stay until src/live.spi (frozen PR-28 policy) is deleted and competitor's CELLS-APPS lands; then it becomes a situation record. Changes:\n- output_unit goes through output_sum and output_weight_at; decide goes through decision_of.\n- threshold_healer becomes with_heal_threshold + with_engaging; with_kiting goes through with_kite_trigger. They set the same slots, so the weights are equal.\n- evolve (fitness) (s : weight_search) (start), with a weight_round record and mutate (r) (child); same draws, same clamp, same width shrink.\n- Tests share hand_wired_healer, heals_at, threshold_at, kiter_at, heal_score and quarter_healer_search; the expected values are unchanged (17).\n- pack_sim cold and warm network.evolve calls build the record.\nGate: network, pack_sim, hunting_pack with the memo off.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_18 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 18u32, "2026-10-25T23:23Z", "tibia-lane", "23:23Z terrain design (blocked until cells-apps-mp lands map.spi). terrain cells 4, 9 and 11 (weights 11383, 10175, 10431) are the arena_spot/road/sight bitmaps, emitted by apps/map/map.spi (emit, inside_choice, chain at ~409-454) as one nested row-bisection if-chain per map. The fix belongs in the generator, never in terrain.spi:\n- Emit one leaf function per 3-row chunk, named `<name>_rows_<first>`. Each holds `word` x 3 rows of u32 literals, 9 literals, weight about 240.\n- Emit a dispatch tree as its own cells, bisecting the chunk index and fanning out at most 4 ways per cell.\n- Dedupe chunks whose 9 words are identical (the all-4294967295 outside rows) to one shared leaf.\n- Lookup stays pure if-chains, so WGSL still works (no arrays).\n- Acceptance: terrain census 0 over for those 3 cells; map.spi regenerates terrain byte-identically to the committed output after the change; route and sight tests unchanged (geometry, terrain, hunting_pack, gpu_sim).\nAlso: cells 30, 33 and 34 (4741, 2942, 1502) need checking for the same generator origin before any hand split.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_19 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 19u32, "2026-10-25T23:39Z", "tibia-lane", "23:39Z terrain slice drafted and added as step 5 of job-1792969425340-tibia-slices (after geometry, because the tests use geometry.xy). Candidate census: 7 cells over one post, down from 31. The 7 left are the 4 map-generated tables (spot, road and sight bits, protection spans; see the generator design note) and the 3 WGSL flood fills over local arrays (route_between, weighted_choice, weighted_step), deferred to their own slice gated by gpu_sim.\nChanges:\n- Local aliases: tile = geometry.position with a tile constructor, occupancy = tile -> bool, crowding = tile -> i32. These cover 131 uses.\n- One candidates record replaces three copies of the five-candidate logic: candidates_with/of, live_candidates, first_usable, fold_candidates and considered/best_scored, plus free_and, toward_signs, closer_to and farther_from. best_step, greedy_step_toward, step_away, step_away_like_the_live_probe and scored_step keep their signatures and evaluation order.\n- footing_score splits into footing_room, alignment_of and slant; the arithmetic order is the same.\n- spawn_points chains to room_and_arena_spawn_points; routed_step goes through route_from and route_step.\n- Test fixtures: nobody, taken_at, alone, away_and_room, room_step, all_open, walk_until_adjacent, detour_around_99_101 and world_tile. 5 two-scenario tests are split; expected values unchanged.\nThe apply rebuilds from census cells after checking that the cells round-trip the file byte for byte. Gate: terrain, hunt_sim, hunting_pack, pack_sim, gpu_sim.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_20 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 20u32, "2026-10-25T23:51Z", "tibia-lane", "23:51Z arena_host slice drafted and added as step 6 of job-1792969425340-tibia-slices. Candidate census: 0 cells over one post, down from 15. 0 'Cons (' (list sugar throughout).\nChanges:\n- step dispatches by phase to awaiting_step, starting_step and running_step; arm order is kept, WindowLost ends from every live phase, and unknown events are a no-op.\n- settled goes through closing; timed_out, world_crashed and restarted come out of step; command_text goes through run_text and character_command_text; logged folds with_command.\n- reacted folds with_reaction and run_reaction (world_script and reacting aliases).\n- simulated runs over a simulation record, reacted_turn seeds the queue with rev rest so it equals rest ++ events as before, and simulate wraps it for the tests.\n- The actor's handle splits into opened_state, on_event and traced. host_trace had no caller outside this file, so actor_trace plus otp_trace, step_runtime_trace and backend_trace replace it.\n- Test fixtures: block_of, completed, finishes and played_from, plus named expected traces (clean_block, timed_out, world_crash, scripted_block, two_stage). Every trace string is unchanged.\nstep, logged, opened and fate_text keep their signatures for world_executor.\nNew tool check: scratch format-check.ps1 runs the SpiralCompiler formatter on copies. All slice candidates (geometry, search, network, terrain, host, plus the combat/search/network in-place edits applied in runner order to a scratch src) come out format-clean, so build.ps1's layout check will pass.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_21 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 21u32, "2026-10-26T00:07Z", "tibia-lane", "00:07Z live.spi retirement drafted, carrying out note 13's dangling-check decision (scratch live-retire\\live-retire.ps1 and live-retire-land.ps1). It lands only after PR-28, which is running now in the held live-gui window.\nChanges:\n- delete src/live.spi;\n- drop live from src/package.spiproj;\n- run-policy-scenario.ps1 loses the compiler env, the policy.lua/policy-frozen.lua block, policySource, the SpiralPolicy wrapper and the -TibiaDir param, so the probe is parity plus body;\n- run-arena.ps1 loses the source snapshot and -TibiaDir;\n- the README drops the 'Lua entry for OTClient' line.\nDry run: every anchor matched once, both scripts parse with the PowerShell parser, and 0 references to TibiaDir, SpiralPolicy, policySource or snapshot remain.\nThe land backs up all 5 files (live.spi included), gates on build.ps1 (layout, plus policy and network Cells with the memo off), and restores by hash if red.\nQueued after the block as step 3 of one serial runner (scratch tibia-all.ps1): lands, slices, live-retire, lib, heals. One job replaces the 4 stopped ones, and lib lands come after every tibia Cells reader.\nAlso: my character.spi draft is dropped. Postable batch 6 already carries character.spi, so there is one record per fact.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_22 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 22u32, "2026-10-26T00:17Z", "tibia-lane", "00:17Z hunt_sim slice drafted and added to the slices runner after host (it applies only after combat lands, because its hash control is the post-combat text). Candidate census: 0 cells over one post, down from 22; 0 lines over 120; 0 'Cons ('.\nDead code deleted instead of healed (0 callers: ripgrep over tibia, outside hunt_sim only hunting_pack's own tuned_cavebot_heal, a separate definition): policy_score, purpose_search, clamp_between, perturb, tuned_cavebot_heal, evolve_policy, total_kills_against. That removes the two heaviest cells (1408 and 459).\nReshape:\n- hunt = { player : knight_state; creature : foe_state; kills; gold; escapes };\n- moment = { seed; tick; now } replaces the seed/tick/now triple;\n- tile, foe_kind and living aliases;\n- creature_moves goes through flee (fled_from, escaped), approached, met and fleeing;\n- strike_now goes through struck, after_hit, looted and bounty;\n- player_acts goes through scripted_act, random_act, tuned_act and scripted_threshold;\n- totals fold through one summed_from over a trial record.\nSemantics kept as written: the escape and slay respawns keep strikes_at and next_step_at, the flee step ignores the chase factor and the approach applies it, and the dead-player checks keep their order. Every expected value is unchanged (golden seed 7: 1, 18, 150; 34 vs 28; 14 x6; 0, 10, 0; 356). Gate: hunt_sim Cells (c, rust, wgsl) with the memo off; the formatter check runs after the live block.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_23 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 23u32, "2026-10-26T00:18Z", "tibia-lane", "00:18Z Rule for pack_sim (and hunting_pack's experiment tests), written before drafting. pack_sim is 29 of 39 cells over one post, nearly all experiments-as-tests: a shared search (cavebot tuning, evolve, network warm/cold starts) feeding a big expected table, under a file-level 1800 s cell budget.\n(1) Never split one experiment into several tests that recompute the same search: postability must not multiply CPU, and CPU is the scarce resource.\n(2) Setup goes into top-level named helpers, one cell each: camp construction, trained arms, the held-out table builder. The test cell only calls them and asserts.\n(3) An expected table over one post becomes named fixture cells (expected_<experiment>_tuned, expected_<experiment>_held_out). The test asserts against the tuple of fixtures, so every number stays literal and inline-adjacent, as done for arena_host's traces.\n(4) Positional assert lines become one tuple assert.\n(5) Cells-per-run stays the same or drops, measured as tests discovered per file before and after.\n(6) Acceptance: census 0 over one post on the file, every expected literal unchanged (diffed as a sorted multiset of numeric literals before and after), and the same wall clock within noise.\nDrafted only after the queued slices prove the edition-3 patterns (multi-field record updates, top-level ^ joins, function-type aliases).")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_24 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 24u32, "2026-10-26T00:21Z", "tibia-lane", "00:21Z gpu_sim slice drafted and added as the last slice in the runner. Candidate census: 0 cells over one post, down from 6. The three oracle/GPU twin pairs shared inline setup; it moves into helpers once: troll_camp, reach_first, troll_ticks (top-level ^ join), ten_tick_facts, spot_clears, troll_hunt, hunt_and_clear_facts and routed_hunt. Each oracle test asserts the facts tuple. Each WGSL twin destructures it and chains field equalities, because WGSL can't compare tuples with =. No test is split, so the GPU compile count and the sims per run are unchanged (pack_sim rule (1)). Renamed: the a_full_troll_hunt_and_five_cyclops_clears pair to troll_hunt_and_cyclops_clears_on_the_oracle / _on_gpu. Expected values unchanged (185, 105, 102, 0; 6, 18, 0, 84, 0, 453000; 23, 1370, 24, 0). Gate: gpu_sim (c and wgsl) with the 900 s cell budget, memo off.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_25 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 25u32, "2026-10-26T00:26Z", "tibia-lane", "00:26Z hunting_pack design rules, written before drafting. On the composed tree (after the combat and search slices) the file has 84 of 234 cells over one post. foe_turn weighs 5861, player_strikes 2415, aimed_by_canary 2445, ruled_acts 1873, start 1316, dance_step 1324, camp_of_entries 1242 and advance 1114. A generic scratch tool, postable-mechanical.ps1, splits a cell at column-0 definition boundaries only when every part fits, and edits in place so other separators stay byte-identical. It fixes 3 cells here, so the rest needs design.\n(1) The turn context: most turn functions take (camp : camp) (seed : u32) (tick : i32) (now : i32), about 60 chars per header. They get turn = { camp : camp; seed : u32; tick : i32; now : i32 }, internal only, as hunt_sim's moment record.\n(2) Public API stays positional, because pack_sim, gpu_sim, competitor and the evolve callers use it: start, advance, simulate, score, totals, ledger_of, clear_ledger_of, worth, uniform_camp, cavebot*, on_layout, arena_hunt, slot. Internal functions change freely.\n(3) Wide records (foe, hunter, camp, 499-775): split only by grouping fields into nested records when a field cluster is updated together, e.g. hunter's cooldown fields. Otherwise each field line stays and the type cell is split by moving its doc-free tail into a second record. Decided per type by counting update sites: nest only where every update site touches one cluster.\n(4) A big match (foe_turn) becomes one function per arm group, named after the behaviour (flees, chases, keeps_distance, dances, wanders), dispatched by a small match. Arm bodies move verbatim, so the RNG keys and their order are unchanged.\n(5) Acceptance: census 0 over one post on hunting_pack; pack_sim, gpu_sim, hunt_sim and hunting_pack Cells green with the memo off; every pack_sim expected literal untouched; gpu_sim WGSL green, since wide records feed WGSL structs.\n(6) Order: after slices-b lands (it needs the composed base). Drafted on scratch fmt-repo/src.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_26 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 26u32, "2026-10-26T00:41Z", "tibia-lane", "00:41Z hunting_pack draft, progress 1, following note 25's rules. On the composed base, 84 cells over one post go to 73 so far, from 11 cells swapped through a generic keyed tool: scratch cell-swap.ps1 with hp-slice\\map.json (key = first non-directive line; it edits in place, other separators stay byte-identical, unique-cell guard, throws on any line over 120).\nDone:\n- foe_turn (5861): split into foe_moves (respawn_on_return, foe_reacts, flees, keeps_range, met_and_dances, foe_engages, loses_sight, acquires, chases) and foe_strikes (shoots, melee_turn with paced_melee_turn, melee_when_ready, melee_paced, melee_on_rounds).\n- aimed_by_canary (2445): entrant_of, aims_of, retargeted, changed_target, kept_strategy, nearest_of, aimed_foe.\n- player_strikes (2415): hunter_hit, swung, credited, slain, slain_slot, kill_counted, kill_paid, coins_dropped.\n- ruled_acts (1873): heals_if_wanted, decides, idles, retreats_or_engages, engaged_by.\n- random_pick and auto_attack share strike_if_ready through a beat record (the attack-gate match lived in three copies).\n- player_heals and player_retreats: healed_hunter/counted_heal and retreated/retreat_counted, plus retreat_to with one helper per footing rule.\n- wanders, dance_step and dances: open_step_of/wandered, keeps_its_reach/danced_to.\n- The shared turn record (camp, seed, tick, now, index) with turn_at, turn_kind, turn_stats, foe_step_ms, free_for, stepped_to and retimed_if_target sits at wanders, the first use.\nEvery arm body moved verbatim, and RNG keys and purposes are unchanged. Public signatures are unchanged (player_strikes, player_heals and player_retreats keep their proof types through aliases). 0 'Cons ('. Remaining: 73 cells, the heaviest start (1316), camp_of_entries (1242), advance (1114) and uniform_camp (951). Drafting continues; the land waits for slices-b.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_27 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 27u32, "2026-10-26T00:59Z", "tibia-lane", "00:59Z hunting_pack draft, progress 2. On the composed base, 84 cells over one post go to 40, through 45 keyed cell swaps (hp-slice\\map.json, 30 piece files).\nSince progress 1:\n- the creature-damage family (hurt_by, melee_struck, arrow_struck, shot_at, plus a lazy sight_clear so the sight-line walk still runs only off cooldown);\n- advance (charged_at, knights_act, acts_at_poll, beat_acts);\n- the seed loops (seed_run plus hunt_at; score, hunt_experience, profit, totals, ledger_of and clear_ledger_of each fold one named step; clear_run is a top-level join on the clearing camp, which keeps size);\n- the patrol family (soonest_spawn and sweep_step are now the claimed variants with every spawn claimed, which is exact because claimed_wait_ms with a true claim is expected_wait_ms);\n- targeting and observe; crowd_of, wants_heal and after_heal_plan (one helper per brain, beat-based, internal callers only);\n- foe_acts (party_turn, aimed_turn);\n- knight_turn, recharged, ally_entered, the measured_entry_ms table, foes_act and foes_act_where (one foes_from fold; a static true pick folds away);\n- the constants and purposes regrouped, and offense_against (offense_of, Cyclops | Hunter).\nThe turn and beat records sit before creature_hits_hunter, their first use. Public signatures are unchanged.\nRemaining 40: the wide records (foe 18 fields, hunter 16, camp) with their literals (fresh_foe, start, uniform_camp, camp_of_entries, arena_hunt, mirroring), which need note 25 rule (3)'s nesting by update cluster; features, waiting for network.inputs_of to take a record after live.spi retires; and a tail of evolve/route/tactic builders between 280 and 660.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_28 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 28u32, "2026-10-26T01:25Z", "tibia-lane", "01:25Z hunting_pack draft, progress 3: 24 of 483 cells over one post, down from 84, through 62 keyed swaps.\nNew since progress 2:\n- step timing: swing_waits/deferred_swing, hunter_step_pace, canary_step_ms, no_faster_than_probe;\n- taken_by_others (other_on join), hunter_steps (stepped_hunter), slot_of_nearest_attacker (adjacent_pick, earlier_threat, nearest_from);\n- seed blocks split; measured as a GADT dispatch amount_on over seed_run, one typed helper per arm (the match itself must stay whole, since a fallback arm would lose the unit refinement);\n- worth plus one monomorphic heal search: best_heal_from over a score closure; tuned_cavebot_heal_for = shaped with an identity shape. The result is identical because the old second evaluation of heal 40 can never win under strict >, and it saves one sim per tuning;\n- the gene and tactic builders: genes_of over three-tuples (internal callers only), scored_brain/order/footing, genome_brain, temperate_brain; temperate_genes and tactic_with_order keep their positional signatures for pack_sim.\nLeft: the wide records (foe, hunter, camp, and their literals fresh_foe, start, uniform_camp, camp_of_entries, arena_hunt, mirroring), features (network.inputs_of), the objective union, the 9-parameter evolve_*_for builders (they need a coordinated pack_sim call-site pass) and the route literals.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_29 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 29u32, "2026-10-26T01:34Z", "tibia-lane", "01:34Z hunting_pack draft READY to land: 15 of 495 cells over one post, down from 84, through 71 keyed swaps. The candidate is scratch hp-slice\\hunting_pack-final.spi: format-clean (the formatter output is used as the candidate, so the 2 blank lines left by emptied cells are normalised), 0 'Cons ('.\nIts hash control is the composed base: combat + search + duo-default applied to today's tree (hunting_pack-control.spi). swap-apply refuses unless the tree matches it exactly, so it applies only after those three land.\nLand: hp-land.ps1, gated by hunting_pack, hunt_sim, gpu_sim and pack_sim Cells with the memo off; any red restores by hash. It runs as its own job after slices-b; I add it at the requeue after kit's state move (pack_sim alone can take an hour).\nSince progress 3:\n- route literals use a local at x y;\n- route_genes goes through with_point;\n- route_evolved_on is kept, because TIBIA-HUNTING-GROUND note 3 records it as central_shuttle_route's provenance; it is restructured internally (no callers);\n- hardest_kind uses a hardest_from join;\n- mirroring_the_current_arena is split at canary_timing;\n- the objective union uses a coin_unit alias.\nThe 15 left are all design-blocked:\n- wide records: foe, hunter, camp, fresh_foe, start, uniform_camp, camp_of_entries, arena_hunt;\n- features: needs network.inputs_of as a record, after live.spi retires;\n- the evolve_*_for builders: rules_evolved_on, tactic_evolved_on and evolve_tactic_from; their 9-param positional APIs need a coordinated pack_sim call-site pass.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_30 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 30u32, "2026-10-26T01:35Z", "tibia-lane", "01:35Z Wide-record design for hunting_pack, pre-registered before drafting. It is the last design-blocked part of the hunting_pack postable split (POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS note 25 rule (3)). Counts are reads/updates by field across the hunting_pack candidate, pack_sim, gpu_sim and the arena apps.\nhunter (16 fields, type 538, literal in start). Nest by update cluster:\n- tally = { potions 22/3, hits_taken 12/2, damage_taken 6/2, retreats 9/6, cornered 5/4 }: read by ledgers and tests, updated only in hurt_by, counted_heal and retreat_counted;\n- clock = { block_charges 7/4, next_charge_at 1/2, next_action_at 3/3, next_decision_at 1/3 };\n- core stays flat: at 87, health 17, next_step_at 13/15, timers 23/6, target 26/21, waypoint, call_sign.\nhunter becomes 9 fields; start builds knight_core, idle_clock and zero_tally.\nfoe (18 fields, type 499, literal fresh_foe). Nest:\n- aim = { aims_at, entrant, in_view, damage_first, damage_second, retarget_at, last_x, last_y }: all 2-4 reads, touched only by aimed_foe/viewed/credited;\n- respawn = { returns_at, check_until };\n- chase = { met_target, nearby, acquired_at };\n- core: at, spawn, life, strikes_at, next_step_at.\nfoe becomes 8 fields.\ncamp (24 fields, type 775; literals uniform_camp, camp_of_entries, arena_hunt). Keep 10 shape fields flat: kinds, size, hunter_level, rule, layout, potion_budget, party, ally_enters, party_targeting, wakes_at. Move the Canary knobs into timing = { swing, melee, idle, follow, pace, dance, view } and motion = { ground, acquisition, approach, sight, retreat_pace, chase_pace, placement, ranged }, with one sample_timing () and sample_motion () literal shared by all three camp builders. That also removes three copies of 14 default knobs.\nRules:\n(1) every external reader moves in the same slice: pack_sim p.hunter.potions/health, gpu_sim p.hunter.potions/health, competitor if it reads pack fields;\n(2) each with_* knob setter (waking_at, following_on_think and the rest) keeps its name and signature and rewrites into the nested record, so pack_sim's pipelines don't change;\n(3) acceptance: census 0 over one post on hunting_pack; pack_sim, gpu_sim (WGSL structs) and hunt_sim green with every expected literal untouched;\n(4) drafted after hp-slice lands, on that tree.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_31 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 31u32, "2026-10-26T01:40Z", "tibia-lane", "01:40Z pack_sim draft started under note 23's rules, with fixtures and helpers and no test splits (rule 5: cells per run must not grow). Base: the composed pack_sim after network-slice (scratch fmt-repo). 4 of 29 heavy cells done, through keyed swaps (ps-slice\\map.json): the cyclops-hit tally (hit_tally, cyclops_hits_from, hit_facts), golden seed 7 (troll_camp, reach_cavebot), and the two 20-clear tests (clear_sums, clear_averages, clear_pair, kiting_on, with literal expected fixtures open_field_expected and corridor_expected). Local camp and tactic aliases equal to hunting_pack's types. 25 left, mostly multi-search experiments (warm network 2227, mixed arena camp 3079, hunting-ground sweep 2375). Each gets named setup helpers plus expected-table fixtures per rule (2)-(4). Lands after hp-slice and the wide-record slice, because p.hunter.potions reads move to the hunter tally.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_32 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 32u32, "2026-10-26T01:56Z", "tibia-lane", "01:56Z pack_sim draft, progress 2: 17 of 29 heavy cells left, after 12 keyed swaps; no test split (rule 5 kept). Done: the warm-network experiment (warm_base, warm_nets, warm_rows, warm_experiment with dominance_of and a literal expected fixture), room-and-corridor and the real arena map (both through tuned_base or clear_base plus pick_order, a shared current/reach choice over genome and worth closures; order_experiment and clear_experiment), the two arena-spot clears (clear_sums now tallies 5 values: ms, potions, deaths, hits, taken; clear_with_hits; retreat_rows over reach_rules, current_rules and reach_kite; saves_potions), plus the heal-threshold test. Each search runs once with its original seeds; every expected literal moved verbatim into a *_expected () fixture. Left: the mixed arena camp (3079), hunting-ground sweeping (2375), the Canary-mirroring tests (swing, first swing, dances, hunter range, mirroring live) and a few 300-800 cells.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_33 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 33u32, "2026-10-26T02:35Z", "tibia-lane", "02:35Z tibia pack_sim postable slice drafted: 29 cells swapped for 29 pieces, 0 cells over 273 (was 16), 40 tests in the same order, directives identical, formatter clean, no duplicate names, no 'Cons ('. Shared helpers: held_out_totals replaces three copies of the inline held-out loop, placed_at replaces three, and clear_sums now takes a seed_range. Base is checked equal to the tree after network-slice plus hp-slice. Also found and fixed a latent hp-slice break: dance_step now takes a turn record, but pack_sim:673 called the old form, so hp-apply now also moves that call to (turn_at camp 1001u32 i 0 0), which is the same key and now, and Touches covers pack_sim. Queued job-1792982017293-tibia-slices-c (hp-slice, then ps-slice) after tibia-serial-heals.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_34 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 34u32, "2026-10-26T02:39Z", "tibia-lane", "02:39Z Evolve-builder slice rule, pre-registered before drafting. It clears the last 6 non-record over-cells of hunting_pack (progress-4: evolve_temperate_for, evolve_rules_for, evolve_tactic_from_for, evolve_tactic_from, rules_evolved_on, tactic_evolved_on). The ps-slice is the pack_sim call-site pass it waited for.\n(1) Add :== search_plan = { seed : u32; generations : i32; offspring : i32 } and :== trial unit = { o : objective unit; camp : camp; seeds : seed_range }. seed_range moves above worth. trial_on o camp (b : seed_block training) is the typed constructor.\n(2) The *_for builders take (x : trial unit), start, order where they have one, and (p : search_plan). tactic_search takes a search_plan. temperate pairs = p.offspring. rules_evolved_on and tactic_evolved_on keep their seed_block training parameter and forward through trial_on.\n(3) A builder with 0 callers after the all-repo stem grep is deleted; otherwise it takes (camp, seeds, plan).\n(4) pack_sim's 4 call sites move in the same slice: the same seed, generations and offspring values, and every expected literal untouched. real_map and mixed switch from training_range () to training_block (), which carries the same first and count.\n(5) Acceptance: those 6 cells at 273 or less; the hunting_pack and pack_sim census no worse; the hunting_pack and pack_sim gates green with the memo off. It lands as slices-c step 3, after ps-slice, with controls hp-final and pack_sim-cand.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_35 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 35u32, "2026-10-26T02:53Z", "tibia-lane", "02:53Z Evolve-builder slice drafted by note 34's rule (scratch evolve-slice: evolve-build.ps1 -DeleteDead, evolve-apply.ps1, evolve-land.ps1). hunting_pack goes from 15 over-cells to 9, which leaves only the 8 wide-record cells plus features. pack_sim stays at 0. Both files are formatter-clean, with 0 lines over 120 and no duplicate names. Rule (3) applied: evolve_tactic and evolve_tactic_from have 0 callers across C:\\home\\git (.spi, .spir, .ps1, .md, .livemd, .fs; ripgrep, with hunting_pack.spi itself as the positive control), so both are deleted. Traced through the data flow, the 4 pack_sim sites hand search.evolve and evolve_vector the same seeds, generations, offspring and pairs, and the same (first, count) as before. trial_on over training_block equals training_range's first and count. It runs as slices-c step 3 (after hp-slice and ps-slice), Touches hunting_pack and pack_sim, gating the same two files with the memo off, with swap controls hp-final and pack_sim-cand.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_36 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 36u32, "2026-10-26T02:58Z", "tibia-lane", "02:58Z features joins the evolve slice; it rides the same gate run. It didn't need network.inputs_of as a record: a record literal would spell all 7 field names and come out longer. The split instead: health_share (one fraction rule, used for the player and the target), player_share, target_facts (target share, distance, fleeing), targeted (the observation plus facts into inputs_of), and features as a 4-line branch. observe and target_facts are still evaluated only in the has-a-target arm, as before. inputs_of's network.spi tests and competitor.spi are untouched. hunting_pack is now at 8 over-cells: exactly the wide-record design (foe, hunter, camp, fresh_foe, start, uniform_camp, camp_of_entries, arena_hunt), which drafts after hp-slice lands, per note 30 rule (4). Lands job result: postable b1, b2 and b4-b7 landed, and b3 is re-landing at the head of slices-a with the tie-break fix (applied, now gating). obj-to-string-c is red as expected (TIBIA-CELLS-APPS note 26) and is retried in heals.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_37 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 37u32, "2026-10-26T03:10Z", "tibia-lane", "03:10Z postable-b3 was restored a second time (slices-a, 22:57 local). Its new cause: none of its 3 files (apps/arena bot_dofile, bot_prelude, src/standards) has a test block, so scripts/build.ps1 throws 'no test files matched' (line 122), and postable-land read that as red. The real gate had already passed: the arena build compiled all 3 (13 LUA-OK) and the formatter check was clean. postable-land.ps1 now treats exactly that case (layout clean plus no test files matched) as landed, with the arena build and the formatter as the gate. Any test failure or layout failure still restores; all 3 cases were replayed. The restore was checked byte for byte: bot_dofile, bot_prelude and src/standards equal their originals. b3 now runs at the head of slices-c (job-1792982017293). b8 (combat.spi) and b9 (live.spi) are superseded by the combat-swinger and live-retire slices.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_38 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 38u32, "2026-10-26T03:36Z", "tibia-lane", "03:36Z skills.spi postable slice drafted (scratch skills-slice: skills-build.ps1 generates it from the tree, plus skills-apply and skills-land). The 361-line binary if-tree in knight_skill_tries_up_to_130 (weight 13539) becomes 15 leaf tables of 8 levels each, 3 group dispatchers of 5 leaves each, and the unchanged top function, still :~ so call sites don't inline it. The 120 values come from the tree's in-order leaves, and the build refuses unless they match the 6 pinned levels (11, 12, 30, 60, 100, 130). It has to stay a table: a double formula 50*1.1^n misses 29 levels, and the wgsl test rules out float pow anyway. after_unblessed_death's join point is now the top-level skill_drop with kept_skill. The branch order is inverted but equivalent: lost <= tries keeps the level, otherwise floor at 10, otherwise drop. The two big test cells read through tries_pins and loss_rows/loss_expected. Result: 9 cells with 4 over become 33 cells with 0 over; the 5 tests are unchanged; formatter clean; no duplicate names. It is slices-c step 5 (after evolve), Touches skills, gated on skills plus experience, its only user. slices-c's gate budget, from target/ci-metrics worst cases (pack_sim 890 s, gpu_sim 570, hunt_sim 500, hunting_pack 320), is about 80 min, under the cap.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_39 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 39u32, "2026-10-26T03:43Z", "tibia-lane", "03:43Z apps/arena/samples.spi postable slice drafted (scratch samples-slice; candidate plus apply and land; slices-c step 6). 6 over-cells become 0. The structural aliases labeled and stamped shorten the list signatures with the type unchanged. label_of and added_to let with_labeled and insert_by_label match entry :: rest. median_of names the shared median formula (sample_median and label_line), and range_text holds label_line's min/max tail; label_line keeps its authored vertical ++# chain, and no authored break is joined. The two heavy tests read their expectations through ab_summary_expected and stamps_expected, and the stamping sequence is stamp_three_moves. The 7 tests and every directive are unchanged; formatter clean; no duplicate names. Callers (measure, steps) use qualified samples.x only, so nothing shadows. All new helpers are inline, so no new top-level Lua locals (LUA-FUNCTION-LIMITS). samples isn't in cells-apps' merged set, and nothing queued touches it. Gate: build.ps1 runs the arena build (every probe to Lua) and then samples' lua and c tests; measure and steps have no tests until cells-apps. Still open in apps/arena, by blocker: lua.spi (random lands, lib part), the cells-apps files, client (granular heal, pack 5l), competitor (pack 5l), parity (its only check is the stub run), offscreen (a Win32 declaration design plus a live smoke).")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_40 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 40u32, "2026-10-26T03:53Z", "tibia-lane", "03:53Z apps/canary/ground_trace.spi postable plus list-sugar slice drafted and proven (scratch canary-slice; slices-c step 7). 2 over-cells become 0, and its 6 'Cons (' uses become list sugar. main is split around a ground record (knight_since, knight_away, watched) and a per-poll sweep record (present, knight_present), with each mut cell bound to a local before assignment (the probe.stored mechanism; there is no field-assign syntax). The file opens canary (no name clash). emit prepends 'ground-trace ', and emit_at appends ' @knight+<ms>'. Proof: the candidate compiles to Lua (19,930 bytes; canary-compile.ps1). Run under the fake-Canary stub (server-probe/stub-ground.lua) beside today's tree build, stdout and all 9 trace lines are identical with the run stamp masked, covering appear, run, move, death, gone, and the knight out and back in view. apps/canary is in neither build.ps1's sources nor its layout list, so its land gate is canary-land.ps1: swap-apply, then apps/canary/build.ps1 LUA-OK for the module, then the same stub trace against the saved reference build, with a hash-checked restore on any failure. The test bodies read through watched_two, x_of and watch_facts, with the expectations unchanged; formatter clean. lead_trace (1 over, 4875) is next; its reference Lua predates its source, so the reference gets rebuilt first.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_41 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 41u32, "2026-10-26T04:02Z", "tibia-lane", "04:02Z apps/canary/lead_trace.spi postable plus list-sugar slice drafted and proven (slices-c step 8). 1 over-cell (main, 4875) becomes 0, and its 2 'Cons (' uses become list sugar. The same shape as ground_trace: a lead record (centre, exit, knight_since, knight_at, knight_targeted, watched), each mut cell bound to a local before assignment, and the file opens canary. Timing is preserved line by line: emissions that reused a captured now take emit_now line now; those that called since () fresh take emit_at g line (systemTime can advance inside one Canary callback). Proof: today's tree source rebuilt as the reference (target/canary/lead_trace.lua predated its source), with the candidate compiled beside it (28,355 bytes). Under server-probe/stub-run-boxed.lua all 15 trace lines are identical: starts, run, think, boxed-think, monster moved, both boxed-checks with exit holders, knight targets, knight moved. The older stub-run.lua stubs no Tile, so both builds crash at the same holder_of call after 5 identical lines; it isn't a gate for this probe. Negative control: one changed word ('move ' to 'mov ') in a ground_trace build is caught by the same comparison. canary-land.ps1 now takes a comma-separated stub list; ground_trace gates on stub-ground, lead_trace on stub-run-boxed.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_42 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 42u32, "2026-10-26T04:13Z", "tibia-lane", "04:13Z apps/arena/parity.spi postable slice drafted and proven (scratch parity-slice; slices-c step 9). main (3710) becomes 0 over-cells. A gate_state record (last_action_at, allowed, dropped) sits behind admit, with dropped_action and allowed_action in the original update order. The four arity wrappers become admitted0-3 plus gate0-3; the gate list is two halves in the original order; gate_player; watch_login stays first, because it captures the native turn before gate1 'turn' replaces it. The spectator filter is near_view, keep_visible (nested ifs kept, so position_x is never read on a nil position), origin_spot, visible_from, visible_or_found, visible_to_player and filtered_spectators; summary_text is unchanged. Proof: the tree source and the candidate both compiled to Lua in the arena layout (scratch arena-compile.ps1; 12,523 and 12,533 bytes). Under server-probe/stub-parity.lua all 16 lines are identical: the login turn and its log line, gated attack, walk dropped at +100, talkChannel, move, useWith, cancelAttack dropped, autoWalk, visible near,edge, and the summary at allowed 5, dropped 2. The land gate is canary-land.ps1 (now -App arena -Slice): swap-apply, the arena build LUA-OK for parity, then the same stub trace against the saved reference. parity isn't in cells-apps' merged set.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_43 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 43u32, "2026-10-26T04:15Z", "tibia-lane", "04:15Z Offscreen postable rule, pre-registered before drafting (apps/offscreen/offscreen.spi, 12 over-cells, heaviest win32_declarations at 1651, plus 2 'Cons (' rows in parse_from). (1) GRANULAR-HEAL rule 3 means declarations only, not one string. The global splits into per-group declaration globals, each at most 273 weight: user32 desktop functions, a few kernel32 process blocks (Rust allows several unsafe extern 'system' blocks), and the two repr(C) structs. Each primitive calls the globals it uses, as wide_of, close_desktop and last_error call win32_declarations today; global de-duplicates identical text. (2) parse_from uses list sugar (flag :: value :: rest), and its flag chain moves to a with_flag helper returning the updated launch or the parse error. (3) start_on, run_on_desktop, main and the 4 tests split along their existing steps, with every pointer still derived inside the expression that uses it (GRANULAR-HEAL note 3 rule). (4) Acceptance, the same as the 10-24 heal: Cells 4/4 on rust including the Win32 behaviour test, offscreen.exe rebuilt, and a run-offscreen.ps1 smoke (cmd /c exit 0 on a hidden desktop) inside a window free of live-gui, since it creates a desktop. The land waits for such a window; drafting doesn't.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_44 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 44u32, "2026-10-26T04:19Z", "tibia-lane", "04:19Z Offscreen rule (note 43), measured before drafting: two Rust items can't fit one post even alone. CreateProcessW's extern declaration (10 parameters, one item per global, short Rust parameter names) weighs 336, and the StartupInfo repr(C) struct (18 fields, short names except cb and desktop, which Spiral code uses) weighs 336. A single item can't be split across cells. Options for the next pass, to be ruled before drafting: (a) the embedded Rust starts at column 0 inside its global string, which removes about 80 indentation characters per item (to check: what the formatter does with that string content, and readability); (b) the census gains a named atomic-host-item exception list (one extern fn or one struct per entry), with every other offscreen cell at 273 or less. No other tibia file has an item like this. Everything else in offscreen fits the per-step split, including parse_from with list sugar and report with finished_line, killed_line and failure_lines.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_45 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 45u32, "2026-10-26T05:54Z", "tibia-lane", "05:54Z geometry-slice went red in slices-a (geometry 19/20; the wgsl test sight_lines_on_gpu fails with 'WGSL backend doesn't support the type {x;y;z} -> bool (YFun)'). It restored by hash; gpu_sim, hunt_sim, pack_sim and terrain stayed green. Cause: the tree's sight_line_clear keeps its walk loops as join points inside the inline function, so they capture blocks lexically and the compiler specializes on it as a static function. The slice lifted them to top-level join points (open_run, sloped_run) that take blocks as a parameter, which makes it a runtime closure, and WGSL has none. Fix recipe for the next pass: keep each recursion as an inner join point capturing blocks (and ux/uy/slope), and move only the closure-free parts to top level (stepped, unit_toward, cells_between, slope_of, ray_from, ray_step). Rule to apply in later slices: never pass a function value into a top-level join point on code a wgsl test reaches. Postable (P1), so it waits behind the granular work.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_46 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 46u32, "2026-10-26T05:59Z", "tibia-lane", "05:59Z terrain-slice is taken out of slices-b: its candidate calls geometry.xy, which only the red, restored geometry slice adds, so its gate would compile-fail and waste the heavy slot. It runs after geometry is fixed (note 45 recipe).")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_47 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 47u32, "2026-10-26T07:13Z", "tibia-lane", "07:13Z slices-b results: host-slice and gpusim-slice landed (gates green, memo off). terrain-slice ran (slices-b started at 01:33, before I pulled it from the runner) and went red as predicted: it needs the restored geometry slice's geometry.xy. Restored by hash. huntsim-slice went red: hunt_sim 10/11, one wgsl test fails with 'The WGSL backend doesn't support the type {player : {at : {x;y;z}; health; timers ...}...}'. Restored by hash. This is the same class as geometry (note 45): the slice lifted code that a wgsl test reaches into top-level join points carrying a function-typed value, so the record type ends up inside a runtime closure. Fix recipe: keep closure-capturing recursion as inner join points; move only closure-free helpers to the top level. Both are postable (P1) and wait behind the granular P0 work.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_48 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 48u32, "2026-10-26T07:25Z", "tibia-lane", "07:25Z geometry slice fixed (v2, scratch geometry-slice/sight.spi; v1 kept as sight-v1.spi). Both loops are inner join points again and capture blocks lexically, as the tree's sight_line_clear does: run inside straight_clear (a direction offset d from unit_toward, stepped, cells_between) and run inside sloped_clear (taking the precomputed slope*ray from sloped_start; blocked_at is an inline wrapper, so WGSL specializes it). Proof by compiling the failing wgsl test's own body as an entry in a copy of tibia's src package (scratch geometry-slice/wgsl-probe.ps1): the tree compiles to WGSL and to C (the C run exits 0); v2 compiles to WGSL and to C (exit 0); and the negative control, v1, fails WGSL with exactly the gate's error ('doesn't support the type {x;y;z} -> bool (YFun)'). v2 has 0 over-cells, is formatter-clean and keeps geometry.xy, which terrain needs. Requeued at the end of slices-c: geometry-slice, then terrain-slice. huntsim (summed_from (f : hunt -> i32), a top-level join point taking a function) gets the same treatment next.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_49 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 49u32, "2026-10-26T08:46Z", "x", "2026-10-26 02:52 x: first full report (node job-1792988625301, release xc, 112 min). Cells over one post / cells: x 0/1,984 (58/58 files clean); spiral 5,910/13,514 (561 of 1,385 files clean); eoie 3,014/7,861 (167/844); tibia 399/1,325; dice 51/187; polyglot 54/164; fiscal 35/54; blog 24/88; alphabet 9/14. Worst: the five spiral compiler mega samples (586/764 down to 209/362), lib xsd_emit 94/223, tibia hunting_pack 86/232, lib sm' 78/262. Two defects found: (1) xc overflowed its 1 GB stack on i574n.github's state/agile.spi (7 MB, 8,811 lines, but only 12 blank-line-separated blocks, so 12 cells of ~600 KB each); prelude split_on is also non-tail and copies the remaining text at every line (quadratic), which made the run slow; overflow probe running on the node. (2) the report listed HEAD paths of the moved sim modules (7 'no such file'); postable-report.ps1 now walks the working tree (cached + untracked, existing paths) and prints each repo's line as soon as it is done.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_50 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 50u32, "2026-10-26T08:46Z", "x", "2026-10-26 03:07 x: ROOT CAUSE of the agile.spi overflow. The file is one 7,070,035-char cell (13 blank-line blocks, 12 of them under 200 chars), and glyphs trimmed_end turns a cell into a list of all its graphemes and joins them back with sm'.concat_list, whose Rust arm is concat_right_fold: listm.foldBack with acc = x ++# sep ++# acc. That copies the whole accumulated string at every grapheme (quadratic) and recurses once per grapheme, so 7M graphemes overflow the 1 GB thread. Node probes (job-1792997379066) fit the quadratic: one cell of 50k/200k/600k chars took 3/11/130 s, 0 overflow; the same 600k cut into 4/12 cells took 48/35 s, and 2,000k in 4/12 cells took 566/415 s with peaks of 1.5-1.9 GB (job-1792997906328), so beyond the per-cell quadratic there is a file-level one: split_on below, 21,740 lines each copying the remainder. Fix plan: (1) x trimmed_end drops trailing blanks without rebuilding the cell (cut at the byte length of the kept prefix); it lands after job-1792993843563's glyphs heal, which edits the same file; (2) lib concat_list's Rust arm becomes a native join (a lib gate), which helps every consumer of concat_list; (3) prelude split_on is also non-tail and copies the remainder at each line; it moves to lib sm'.split (native on Rust and Gleam) with a tail-recursive gather.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_51 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 51u32, "2026-10-26T09:13Z", "tibia-lane", "09:13Z 09:1xZ hp-slice and evolve: the WGSL closure risk was caught before slices-c reached it, and is fixed (rule of note 45).\nhunting_pack-final.spi and hunting_pack-evolve.spi had three top-level join points taking a function: foes_from (picked : i32 -> bool), soonest_from (claims : i32 -> bool) and best_heal_from (score_of : i32 -> i32). gpu_sim compiles hunting_pack to WGSL and is among hp-land's gate files.\nFix (scratch hp-slice/inner-joins-apply.ps1, 4 anchors): each loop moves inside its inline caller as an inner join point that captures the function: foes_act_where, soonest_claimed_spawn and shaped_heal_tuned_for. 0 function-param top-level join points are left. Formatter clean, 0 lines over 120. Backups: *-before-inner-joins-0511xx.spi.\nProof on the remote kit worker node: gpu_sim's own test notebook, run sequentially (tree, fixed, old), with tibia's spiral-wgsl.exe shipped to C:\\home\\remote-stage\\wgsl-run (job-1793002505629).\n- old: all 3 wgsl tests fail at codegen with 'The WGSL backend doesn't support the type i32 -> bool (YFun) yet'. This is the red slices-c would have hit.\n- fixed: identical to the tree on every row. The C oracles pass 3 of 3, ten_ticks_of_a_troll_pack_on_gpu passes, and the two long gpu tests time out at the 900 s cell budget on both tree and fixed.\nRule amendment, recorded openly: the adopt script's pre-written VOID-if-the-tree-isn't-green tripped on those shared runtime timeouts. The negative control's exact codegen error shows the probe sees the bug, so the decision used 'fixed equals tree row by row, and old shows YFun'. The local hp-land gate (gpu_sim with the memo off) still decides the land.\nProbe harness lessons, now in scratch node-kit/notebook-probe-node.ps1:\n- Variants run one at a time. In parallel they hit 'could not start the compiler on port 13905: :lock_timeout' and ran 0 tests.\n- Without SPIRAL_WGSL_RUN, wgsl rows read 'unsupported', so they are never compiled.\n- hunt_sim, gpu_sim and pack_sim are test-only files that no spiproj lists. Probe them through their own notebooks, not through `:< module`.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_52 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 52u32, "2026-10-26T09:18Z", "tibia-lane", "09:18Z 09:2xZ hunt_sim slice proven and requeued. hunt_sim's own notebook on the remote kit worker node, run sequentially with the WGSL runner (job-1793002517895):\n- tree: 11 of 11 pass.\n- fixed (summed's loop as an inner join point capturing f): 11 of 11 pass, including golden_seed_7_on_gpu and scripted_beats_random_control_on_gpu.\n- v1 (negative control): scripted_beats_random_control_on_gpu fails with the WGSL closure error ('The WGSL backend doesn't support the type {player ...'), which is slices-b's red. The other 10 pass.\nhuntsim-slice is now the last step of the queued slices-c (job-1792982017293), after geometry (the candidate is v2, hash-verified) and terrain. Its control still matches the tree's hunt_sim.spi. Its land gate reruns hunt_sim's tests on the tree as it stands after the hp, ps and evolve lands.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_53 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 53u32, "2026-10-26T12:15Z", "tibia-lane", "12:15Z 12:2xZ slices-c yielded its compile window to main's P0 compiler install (5m', then 5n). The install had waited 29 minutes behind it, and slices-c had about 3 hours of steps left. postable-b3 had LANDED (3 files, 0 cells over one post). hp-slice was stopped mid-gate. Following the stopping-jobs-that-mutate rule, I restored its two applied files (src/hunting_pack.spi, src/pack_sim.spi) from slice-land's pre-apply backup hp-slice-before-080153. Hashes match; hunting_pack again equals the hp control; git shows only the user's staged state; no orphan erl or pwsh. tibia-slices.ps1 gained -From <step> (with skip lines, and it refuses an unknown step), and the rest runs as job-1793016891337-tibia-slices-c2 (-Part c -From hp-slice). The compiler changes under the remaining slices, but each land gate compiles with the installed compiler, so they re-prove on 5m'/5n by construction.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_54 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 54u32, "2026-10-26T13:49Z", "x", "2026-10-26 09:49 x: perf proof job-1793008062043 FAILED at the build's line gate, not in the fixes: frames generates 1057 Rust lines (limit 1000), so xc.exe was never built and the probes are VOID. frames history on the node: 831 through the 03:22 verify, 862 then 976 with the social slice (03:38-04:34), 1057 on the perf mirror, which also carries the glyphs heal (unicode.code_points). The perf draft is small (prelude split_on, glyphs trimmed_end), so the tree may already be over by itself; if so, the next full verify fails the same gate. Measuring: job-1793022599647-x-frames-lines builds only frames on a fresh mirror, tree first and then with the perf fix. Also fixed the draft's gathered seed from Nil to [] (list-sugar rule).")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_55 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 55u32, "2026-10-26T14:01Z", "x", "2026-10-26 10:01 x: X-FRAMES-SPLIT decision rule, written before measuring. The landed x tree's frames crate generates 1206 Rust lines (limit 1000), so every full verify fails the gate. Seams considered: (1) a units-only crate takes about 266 lines out, leaving about 970, fails once Bluesky or X-DEFUSE lands and sends per-grapheme JSON across the crate boundary; (2) a per-cell posts crate with two exports (cell text to post bodies, text to weight) moves about 720 lines out, while frames keeps numbering, thread_on and the JSON. Rule: pick the seam where every crate is at most 900 generated lines with Bluesky A/B/D stacked, with no per-grapheme boundary crossing; ties go to fewer new crates. Accept only if xc_frames output is byte-identical on the frames notebook and the e2e cases.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_56 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 56u32, "2026-10-26T14:10Z", "tibia-lane", "14:10Z 14:1xZ slices-c2 results so far. hp-slice was RED on contention, not code: gpu_sim had 3 cells time out at the 900 s budget, and pack_sim got 'could not start the compiler on port 13905: :lock_timeout'. Both came while tibia-serial-gaps ran Cells beside it, the concurrency of TIBIA-PARK note 13. The node WGSL probe had shown the hp fix equal to the tree row for row. hp-slice restored both files (hash match). ps-slice and evolve-slice then refused correctly ('changed since the control copy': their controls assume hp landed) and restored. skills-slice LANDED (gate green on skills and experience, memo off); samples-slice is running. Retry: a new tibia-slices part c-hp (hp, ps, evolve), queued as job-1793023767586 after slices-c2. duo-sweep-p12c (it needs evolve), serial-gaps3 and serial-gaps-lib3 are rechained after it, stopped tail first so no --after released early. A check of every queued slice's control against the tree before the rechain: huntsim matches; ps, evolve and samples show as changed because hp hasn't landed, which is expected; skills shows changed because it just landed.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_57 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 57u32, "2026-10-26T14:18Z", "x", "2026-10-26 10:18 x: X-FRAMES-SPLIT draft (%TEMP%\\x-frames-split\\apply.ps1, anchored, writes only under -Client; rehearsed on a scratch copy, all anchors once, 0 new lines over 120). splitter gains a chunker type, cell_posts_by, thread_by and local_chunks (cell_posts_on/thread_on keep their behavior through them). A new chunks crate exports xc_cell_chunks(name, i, cell) (chunk bodies as JSON) and xc_weight(name, t) -> i32; frames keeps cells, numbering, thread assembly and post JSON, calling the two exports once per cell and once per post (no per-grapheme crossing). The frames test exercises the same pipeline locally (local_chunks + weight_on). build.ps1 notebook list gains chunks/chunks. Bluesky anchors (unit_on, limit_on/weighed_on arms, a test) do not overlap. Node proof job-1793024337517-x-split-proof: build -fast (both crate counts, x-compat, cargo, e2e byte identity, postable), 5 notebooks, saves chunks.rs/frames.rs/Cargo.lock, then stacks Bluesky A/B/D and measures both crates against the 900-line rule.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_58 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 58u32, "2026-10-26T15:01Z", "tibia-lane", "15:01Z Wide-record slice: an amendment to note 30, registered before applying. Drafted on the evolve candidate in scratch wide-records; rule (4) still holds the proof until hp-slice lands.\nMeasured so far: clock, tally, foe and the camp timing/motion nesting are applied and formatter-clean, with every grouped-field read under its group. The census is 21 over one post on hunting_pack (control 10) and 2 on pack_sim (control 0). Nesting alone lengthens updates, and an 8+ field literal with a long header cannot fit 273.\nAmended rules:\n(A1) camp drops to 6 fields: foes = camp_foes { kinds, size, ranged, rule, wakes_at }, crew = camp_crew { party, ally_enters, party_targeting, potion_budget, hunter_level }, layout, timing (7 knobs), motion { ground, acquisition, approach, sight }, knight = camp_knight { retreat_pace, chase_pace, placement }.\n(A2) The three builders share sample_camp foes layout. foes_of kinds size rule derives ranged as views.counted creatures.is_ranged kinds > 0, which equals each builder's old expression because band_of repeats one kind and the entries band holds only first/second/third. crew_of foes sets hunter_level = default_hunter_level kinds size, as all three builders did. This removes the recorded `ranged` deviation.\n(A3) Updaters that grew past one post get group-level helpers (tallied_hurt, tallied_retreat, one_more_charge, met_with, acquired, retargeted, action_gate_at) instead of inline nested updates. Every with_* setter keeps its name and signature.\n(A4) Local aliases follow readiness.instant and hunt.living: hit_points, living, creature_band.\n(A5) The two control over-cells (foes_act_where, shaped_heal_tuned_for) are split in the same slice, because acceptance is 0 over on hunting_pack.\nAcceptance is unchanged (note 30 rule 3): census 0 over one post on hunting_pack and pack_sim; pack_sim, gpu_sim, hunt_sim and predict green, with every expected literal untouched.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_59 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 59u32, "2026-10-26T15:20Z", "x", "2026-10-26 11:20 x: split proof 1 (job-1793024337517) stopped after the chunks crate failed to compile: \"File chunks cannot be found in the project\", because the draft did not list chunks/ in apps/client/package.spiproj. Crates before it compiled, and the splitter notebook passed with the thread_by/cell_posts_by refactor. Fixed: the draft now adds chunks/ before frames/ in package.spiproj, and new files end with LF. Rehearsed on a scratch copy; the mirror was re-synced clean. Rerun: job-1793028108907-x-split-proof2.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_60 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 60u32, "2026-10-26T15:27Z", "tibia-lane", "15:27Z Wide-record slice, amendments A6-A8 to note 58, registered before applying. A1-A5 are applied: hunting_pack is at 2 over one post (knight_of 289, start 713), pack_sim at 1 (clear_tallied 274), all formatter-clean. A literal of a 9-field record cannot fit 273 at 8-space indent (9 per field line plus text), so:\n(A6) timers moves into hunter_clock (clock = every \"ready at\" of the hunter) and is reached through with_timers. hunter drops to 8 fields. Already applied: 10 timer sites, with updates ordered after any clock update in the same expression (retreat_counted, counted_heal).\n(A7) pack's counters group as score = pack_score { kills, experience, escapes, deaths, gold }, with zero_score (). Readers move in the same slice: gpu_sim (4 lines, including the WGSL kernel's check), pack_sim (3), predict (3). pack drops to 5 fields and start fits.\n(A8) knight_of builds from idle_knight sign, an off-world knight (terrain.grave (), 0 hp), updated with knight_start and player_max_health. No field changes.\nAcceptance unchanged: 0 over on hunting_pack and pack_sim, with green gates and untouched expected literals.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_61 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 61u32, "2026-10-26T15:47Z", "tibia-lane", "15:47Z Wide-record slice drafted under note 30 plus amendments A1-A8 (notes 58 and 60). Census is 0 over one post on hunting_pack (control 10), pack_sim (control 0) and gpu_sim. The census over the 4 files went from 14 to 4, and the remaining 4 are predict's, the same 4 cells as control (outside this slice). All 4 files are formatter-clean with 0 lines over 120, no 'Cons (' or Nil, and no new duplicate names (a second `retargeted` was caught and renamed reaimed).\nShape: foe = core + chase/respawn/aim (fresh_chase/fresh_respawn/fresh_aim, foe_at); hunter = 8 fields with clock (timers joined it, A6) and tally (zero_tally, idle_clock, idle_knight, knight_of); camp = foes/crew/layout/timing/motion/knight, with one sample_camp shared by the 3 builders and foes_of deriving ranged (the recorded deviation is gone); pack = 5 fields with score (A7). Aliases: living, hit_points, foe_kind (existing). Every with_* setter keeps its name and signature, and pack_sim gets hunting_pack.sized for its 2 size edits.\nProof: (1) node pre-proof job tibia-wide-probe runs the hunting_pack, pack_sim, gpu_sim and hunt_sim notebooks sequentially on control (tree + evolve candidate) and wide variants; (2) local tibia-wide-land at the chain end, after cid-land4. It refuses unless all 4 tree files hash-equal their controls (negative control run: refused, tree untouched), gates build.ps1 on hunting_pack, pack_sim, gpu_sim and hunt_sim with the memo off, and needs predict.ps1 rows byte-identical before and after. It restores all 4 files by hash on red. Scripts are in scratch wide-records (wide-clock, wide-tally, wide-foe, wide-camp, wide-camp2, wide-lean, wide-score, wide-land, fmt-census, static-check, cell-weights).")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_62 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 62u32, "2026-10-26T15:58Z", "tibia-lane", "15:58Z The wide-record slice now also takes apps/predict/predict.spi from 4 over one post to 0, so the slice's 4 files total 0 over. The three tables share each_held_out (row : i32 -> u32 -> string), an inline function with an inner join point; its row closure stays static, the best_tuned_heal pattern. Each table is split into clear_row/clear_head, hunter_row/hunter_line/hunter_head and hunt_row/hunt_line, and a row is built with tabbed (x) (row) = row ++# \"\\t\" ++#? x from \"\" ++#? code, so the bytes match the old concatenation. main uses arena_spot, canary_ground and clear_tables, and hunter cleared uses hunting_pack.all_killed. predict.ps1's candidate injection anchor (:= candidate (c : camp) : camp = c) is unchanged. Formatter-clean, 0 lines over 120.\nPre-proof: node job tibia-wide-identity compiles predict to C for the control (tree + evolve candidate) and wide variants, builds both with gcc -O2, runs them and diffs the rows. The local wide-land gate repeats this through scripts/predict.ps1 before and after the swap.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_63 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 63u32, "2026-10-26T16:20Z", "tibia-lane", "16:20Z Found and fixed before it landed: the evolve-slice candidate (note 39) did not compile. worth and trial_worth were unbound in shaped_heal_tuned_for, evolve_temperate_for, evolve_rules_for, evolve_tactic_from_for and worth_on_training. Cause: evolve-build.ps1 line 34 wrote @($worth, $aliases + $worth + $trialWorth). PowerShell's comma binds tighter than +, so the pair became ($worth, $aliases) and worth was replaced by the aliases alone. Edit-Text only counts anchor matches, so it could not see the wrong pairing, and evolve's earlier proof was by stub, never a compile. Caught by the wide slice's node identity job (a real C compile of predict over src), where the control (evolve candidate) and the wide variant failed identically: VOID by design, positive control red.\nFix: evolve-build.ps1 now parenthesizes the sum, and the two definitions were inserted in hunting_pack-evolve.spi right after :== search_plan, the spot the build would have put them (backup *-before-worth.spi). The candidate equals the wide control again (68DE7809). slices-c-hp reads the file at run time, so its evolve step lands the fixed candidate. The identity rerun (tibia-wide-identity2) and the queued wide probe (stage files replaced on the node) now use the fixed copies.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_64 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 64u32, "2026-10-26T16:27Z", "tibia-lane", "16:27Z Wide-record pre-proof GREEN on the node (tibia-wide-identity2, 16:3xZ): with the fixed evolve candidate as control, both variants compile predict over src to C (190 s and 168 s), build with gcc -O2 and run. Each gives 400 rows and the rows are byte-identical (0 differing lines). So hunting_pack's clock/tally/timers/foe/camp/score regrouping, the camp builders' shared sample_camp with derived ranged, and predict's row builders keep predict's output exactly on the C backend. The notebook pre-proof (tibia-wide-probe: hunting_pack, pack_sim, gpu_sim, hunt_sim on both variants) is still queued on the node, and the local tibia-wide-land repeats the predict identity at land time.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_65 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 65u32, "2026-10-26T16:44Z", "tibia-lane", "16:44Z apps/host postable slice drafted (scratch host-slice2). world_executor.spi goes from 10 over one post to 0 (43 cells) and world_port.spi from 4 to 0 (26 cells); formatter clean, 0 lines over 120. world_executor is grouped into executor = programs/files/limits/character, with aliases command, event, maybe_world and stepped. line_after = first_line over rest_from. run_events = run_ended over logged_experience. executed dispatches world commands (an or-pattern for StartWorld | RestartWorld, plus obeyed) and leaves the rest to run_command_events (reset, run, recorded). executed_all is a listm.fold (apps/host is Gleam-only, so a fold closure is fine there). driven runs over a drive record (host, current, log) with advanced, enqueued and finished. The fixtures (fake_programs, fake_files, fake_character, fake_world, counted_block, sleeping_executor, the expected traces) keep the 2 tests' assertions byte-identical. world_port: world_obeyed = StartWorld, else world_changed (restarted, released); the tests use settled, ready_within, crashed_within, exits_within, obeyed_world and cycled instead of local closures; join points take no closure parameter. Also fixes list sugar in my own 10-26 code: world_executor matched on Nil and passed Nil, now []. Lands as tibia-host-slice2-land (slice-land with -Src apps/host; gate build.ps1 on world_port, world_executor and arena_host with the memo off; restore on red), queued after tibia-wide-land.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_66 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 66u32, "2026-10-26T16:53Z", "tibia-lane", "16:53Z combat.spi postable slice drafted (scratch combat-slice): 13 cells over one post to 0 (76 cells), formatter clean, 0 lines over 120, no duplicate names, no module opens combat. Public names and signatures unchanged for creatures, hunt_sim, hunting_pack and pack_sim, and the -Shield hook text 'shielded_defense 11i32 dwarven_shield' that scripts/predict.ps1 edits is kept verbatim. Internal steps are named: normal_attempt/clamped_to_unit, a draw_stats record with with_draw, stats_draw, no_draws and a top-level draws_from join point, after_armor, knight_sample_armor, charge_left, swing_floor/swing_ceiling, bout_of, landed_after (shared by both tallies), taken_from/health_after, fresh_capped. Every join point stays closure-free (gpu_sim reaches combat through hunting_pack). The tests keep their names and expected values. Arrow and reach use top-level fixtures (arrow_key, one_if, arrow_outcome, summed, arrow_totals, arrow_with_no_charge, arrow_facts, fired, reach_of, fires_about_half, armor_blocks); the swing tests share troll_swings/capped_troll_swings. Their 2-3 field assertions become one tuple _assert_eq with the same literals; the wgsl test keeps its 0/1 form. Proof: node tibia-combat-identity (predict over src to C on tree vs slice; hunting_pack's simulation runs through every roll) and tibia-combat-probe2 (the combat notebook, chained after tibia-wide-probe); local tibia-combat-land (slice-land gate on combat, creatures, hunt_sim, hunting_pack, pack_sim and gpu_sim with the memo off) queued after tibia-host-slice2-land.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_67 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 67u32, "2026-10-26T17:19Z", "x", "2026-10-26 13:19 x: X-FRAMES-SPLIT round 1 REJECTED by its rule. Split proof 2 (job-1793028108907): chunks 1043 generated lines (gate fails at 1000), 1090 with Bluesky A/B/D; frames 462; notebooks 5/5 ok; e2e never ran (the gate stopped the build). Stack measurement (job-1793029013748, split + Bluesky + defuse wire v1): chunks 3189, write 1745, xc 961. In chunks, about 2030 lines are the defuse helpers inlined at every call site (entities_from 790, defused_run 479 with the TLD table inline). write 1745 comes from Bluesky B/D alone (write.spi edits), not the split: SOCIAL-PROFILES-LOCAL needs its own write seam. Round 2 rule, written before measuring: candidate S2 posts before splitting. splitter gains posted_on (X runs posted_text; others pass the text through), and units glue a zero-width space to the next glyph. A new posted crate exports xc_posted_text(name, text), holding today's dot rule now and the links-only rule plus entity sigils after the defuse wire. chunks exports only xc_cell_chunks over already-posted text. frames calls posted, then chunks, once per cell, and weighs each post locally (no xc_weight). defuse helpers and is_tld become join points. Accept S2 if posted, chunks and frames are each at most 900 generated lines with Bluesky A/B/D stacked AND with the defuse wire stacked, and the client build (gate, cargo, e2e) passes on the split alone with xc_frames byte-identical. If chunks is over 900, the next seam is decided from the per-function line attribution, not by raising the bound. Draft rehearsed on a scratch copy: split v2, Bluesky A, B (re-anchored to the new unit_on, posted_on and posted_glyphs arms) and D, then wire v2; every anchor applied once, no .spi line over 120.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_68 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 68u32, "2026-10-26T17:54Z", "tibia-lane", "17:54Z apps/arena/competitor.spi postable split drafted (scratch competitor-split: head-new, retreat-new, tick-new, tail-new, applied by competitor-split.ps1 over cells-apps' merged competitor). 12 cells over one post (heaviest 7313, tick) go to 0 over 160 cells; 620 lines, formatter clean, no duplicates, no Nil or Cons. Shape: tick becomes a moment record (a, now, health, sense = here/people/seen/danger, heal_ready, can_step) with top-level drank, engaged/aimed/attacked, held, retreated_now, kited, policy_chose, traced, lived and outcome_of_tick, plus a small recursive join point ticked. step_away runs over a retreat_ctx with attempt_from, adjacent_from and rejected_from (closure-free join points) and the shared joined helper. network_code reads the moment (network_inputs, target_share, decision_code). plan_from_environment = default_plan plus env overrides. sight and threats use context records and top-level scans. candidate_step is split into straight steps and sidesteps (across/along). main runs over a session (plan, marks of mut cells, engaged, meter) with on_text, on_move, ready and settling as top-level functions. Each mut update goes through probe.stored. Every host read keeps its order, and laziness is kept where the original short-circuited (incumbent sighting, threat ranking, monster check). Tests keep their expected values: the candidate-step test is split in two and footing_label opens client. Why now: competitor's main tick callback held exactly 60 upvalues in the base build (luac51 -l -l), Lua 5.1's limit, which blocked S3 family 5 (TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION note 57). With only the tick split, the node check (tibia-split-lua2, stopped early) already shows family 5's competitor LUA-OK; the full-split run tibia-split-full is checking all 13 programs for split alone and split plus family 5.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_69 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 69u32, "2026-10-26T18:01Z", "tibia-lane", "18:01Z Proof results 18:0xZ. (1) combat slice pre-proof GREEN: tibia-combat-identity compiles predict over src to C on tree vs combat slice (158 s / 209 s), runs both, and gets 400 byte-identical rows. Every hunting_pack roll goes through combat, so the regrouped rolls, blocks and tallies behave the same on C. (2) LIB-CONTAINS-CHAR node gate3 says GREEN, but I did not import it: the slice changes only the Rust arm of __assert_contains (char case, cast moved into a rust_char_of_byte primitive), and the node never ran the rust, cuda or typescript rows (not_run). A GREEN that executed none of the changed arm proves nothing, so tibia-serial-gaps-lib4 gates it locally. (3) offscreen.spi (12 over, plus Nil/Cons in parse_from and arguments) needs a Win32 slice rather than a reshuffle. The global declaration block holds a StartupInfo struct and a 10-parameter CreateProcessW, each longer than one post. Plan: an ABI-identical StartupInfo with array-grouped fields (pointers [usize; 3], geometry [u32; 8], show [u16; 2], handles [i64; 3]); Rust type aliases for the wide and byte pointers so each extern fits; one global per extern group; flag parsing as flag_set/settings_flag/timeout_set with list sugar; report as exit_line/killed_line/failure_report. Proof: the live Windows test a_hidden_desktop_runs_a_program_and_reports_its_exit_code plus a run-offscreen.ps1 round trip.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_70 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 70u32, "2026-10-26T18:07Z", "tibia-lane", "18:07Z offscreen.spi postable slice, work in progress (scratch offscreen-postable: head/body/tail plus assemble.ps1; check with scratch check-spi.ps1). Pass 1 is measured: 80 cells, still 14 over one post (tree: 12), 0 lines over 120, list sugar fixed (parse_from on [] / flag :: [] / flag :: value :: rest, arguments_from with []), formatter clean, no duplicates. Pass 1 splits flags (timeout_set, settings_flag, flag_set), the report (exit_line, killed_line, failure_report), runs (working_folder, process_wides, created_with, started_process, waited_out, run_started), test fixtures (ready_launch, missing_exe, bad_timeout, missing_value, thirty_second_launch, hidden_exit, hidden_result) and main (refused, refusal_code, launched_code). The Win32 block is now 7 globals with an ABI-identical StartupInfo (pointers [usize; 3] where index 1 is the desktop, geometry [u32; 8], show [u16; 2], reserved2, handles [i64; 3]) and Wide/Bytes aliases. Still over: the extern globals, from 12-16-space indentation (fix: one extern block per global with at most 2 fns; CreateProcessW fits only with _ parameter names plus Startup/Information pointer aliases, about 240), create_process_at/created_with/start_on (fix: create_process_at takes the desktop address and builds startup inside), waited_out, parse_from (one-line inner match plus a finished_parse helper), refusal_code (two halves), and 3 tests (smaller fixtures). Proof when done: the live Windows rust test (hidden desktop, cmd exit 3) plus run-offscreen.ps1, gated through slice-land with -Src apps/offscreen.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_71 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 71u32, "2026-10-26T18:17Z", "tibia-lane", "18:17Z offscreen.spi postable slice done (passes 2-4 after note 70), with one rule registered before measuring: null pointers and pointer types are Rust declarations in the type global (const NONE/NO_WIDE, type Line/Startup/Information), so call primitives take only meaningful pointers. Result: 12 over one post to 0 (95 cells), 0 lines over 120, list sugar fixed, formatter clean, no duplicates. Win32 declarations are 11 small globals grouped under win32_types and win32_externs; one #[link(name = user32)] serves the crate. StartupInfo regrouped ABI-identically (104 bytes on x64, same offsets). Flag parsing chains flag_set, option_flag, settings_flag and timeout_set. Two tests split into one per assertion group with the same expected literals. Consumer proof GREEN: apps/offscreen/build.ps1 -Check on a scratch root (deps junction to the tree) compiles to Rust, builds with rustc, reports exit 7 from a live hidden desktop, and kills ping at the 2 s timeout with exit 124. Lands as tibia-offscreen-land (slice-land, -Src apps/offscreen, Cells lua and rust tests with the memo off), queued after tibia-combat-land.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_72 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 72u32, "2026-10-26T18:33Z", "x", "2026-10-26 14:33 x: X-FRAMES-SPLIT round 2 MISSES its rule with Bluesky stacked (W1 job-1793036913087): posted 200, chunks 1020, frames 959. Attribution: the weighing block (units_on with the glyph_weight/emoji arm, 170 lines; code_point_weight table 78; plain_weight fold 55; Mastodon linked/link_length/prefix_text 100; glued 39; units_on 34; about 476 lines) is generated in BOTH chunks and frames, because frames now weighs posts locally. Splitting proper (chunk, split_point, chunks_of, taken_while) is about 230 lines. Round 3 rule, written before drafting: a weights crate owns the weighing and exports xc_units(name, posted) (the cell's units as JSON, one call per cell) and xc_weight(name, text) (one call per post and per header budget). The splitter takes units and a weigh function instead of weighing inside: framing carries weigh, and split_units replaces the weighing inside split_cell_on. chunks splits remote units and frames weighs remotely. Accept if posted, weights, chunks and frames are each at most 900 with Bluesky A/B/D stacked and with the defuse wire stacked, and the client build (gate, cargo, e2e) passes on the split alone. One call per cell or per post is allowed; the payload grows with the cell (JSON per grapheme), which this rule accepts explicitly (it replaces round 1's no-per-grapheme-crossing clause). Proof 3 (job-1793035997388) still runs: its e2e checks the posting-before-splitting behavior that round 3 keeps.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_73 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 73u32, "2026-10-26T18:47Z", "tibia-lane", "18:47Z Wide-record notebook pre-proof (tibia-wide-probe, node, 3 h): both variants exit 1 on every notebook because the node was loaded (sweep plus other lanes), so I compare non-passed rows test by test. gpu_sim 2/2, hunting_pack 1/1 and pack_sim 25/25 match between control and wide. hunt_sim differs only in 4 tests (same_seed_same_hunt, chasing_hunter_survives_and_kills, loot_stays_within_bounty_range, troll_scenario_saturates_no_deaths_over_ten_seeds) that time out at the 300 s cell limit on wide and pass on control. Cell timeouts are 9 vs 5. hunt_sim reads only hunting_pack.knight_sample_step_ms and chase_step_factor, which the slice does not change, so their values cannot differ. The extra time comes from Cells type-checking the whole package with the larger hunting_pack. Verdict: no semantic difference visible; the local tibia-wide-land gate (build.ps1, memo off, local timeouts) is the decider.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_74 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 74u32, "2026-10-26T18:58Z", "tibia-lane", "18:58Z measure.spi postable split, work in progress (scratch family5: measure-region.spi applied by measure-postable.ps1, which replaces the bench..main region only when it byte-matches measure-region-control.spi from the composed cells-apps + family-5 base; checked by compose-check.ps1). It is NOT wired into arena-f5-land yet. Pass 1 is measured: 39 cells, 13 over one post (the composed base has 8 larger ones: heaviest 2348 tick, 1955 main), 0 lines over 120, formatter clean, laziness kept (adjacent_to_player and healthy_unhit stay behind the positioned check). Shape: bench = hits/taken/counts/target/marks; share_text/mean_text; swing_counts/swing_totals; swings_noted/taken_noted; kill_checked, engaged_adjacent, swing_settled, swing_after, potion_after and a top-level ticked join point; fresh_bench, on_text, hit_seen, died, started, ready. Still over at 280-530: swings_line (move a swing_tail), taken_line (taken_head), swings_noted/taken_noted (hits_summary/taken_summary tuples), kill_checked, engaged_adjacent (adjacent_monster helper), potion_after, ticked (fold the two step results into a step record), on_text/started (split notes), 2 tests and main (bench_started_as). Next pass applies those, then re-runs the node Lua check with measure included.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_75 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 75u32, "2026-10-26T19:15Z", "tibia-lane", "19:15Z Arena granular/postable bundle, status. arena-f5-land.ps1 now applies, in order: competitor split, S3 family 5, client-postable (step as heading-based two-arm steps, log_flush, client_version_set/world_login, footing test in two groups) and measure-postable (bench = hits/taken/counts/target/run with potion_below in bench_run; paces record; tick_step returns option paces; notes, summaries and tests split). On the composed tree, competitor (160 cells), client (157), game_calls (21) and measure (56) are all 0 over one post, 0 lines over 120, no Nil or Cons, no duplicates, formatter clean. lua.spi's edit no longer leaves a trailing blank line. Node Lua checks: family 5 + client-postable 13/13 LUA-OK (tibia-fam5-final); tibia-fam5-final2 is now checking with measure included. arena-f5-land's sugar guard is case-sensitive (Lua nil is not Spiral Nil) and limited to the files it touches. samples.spi's own 11 Nil uses are a separate land, tibia-samples-sugar-land.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_76 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 76u32, "2026-10-26T20:00Z", "x", "2026-10-26 16:00 x: round 3 partial results (proof 4 job-1793040737007, still running its stacks): the split alone passes the line gate with room to spare (posted 188, weights 709, chunks 580, frames 463; 26 crates, 0 over 1000). The client build still fails, in x-compat, not the gate: the new dots_posted definition in splitter.spi weighs 298 as a cell (limit 272). Proof 3 (round 2) shows the same failure, and its WIRE stack has posted at 1180: join points shrank the defuse code from about 2030 to about 600, but is_tld is 320 lines. Spiral folds each chunk's literal pair but still emits both halves as dead lets, and has over the static 40-chunk list unrolls into 40 nested ifs. Round 4 rule, written before drafting: split v3 with dots_posted cut into posted_dot plus first_or_empty (cell about 262 by the ASCII estimate; thread_by at the same weight already passes). Wire v3 moves the TLD table into a domains crate (xc_is_tld, one call per candidate dot, never per grapheme): defuse threads a known : string -> bool predicate through the dot path (defused_dots_by), and splitter gains posted_by known with posted_on = posted_by is_tld for local use and notebooks. Accept if every split crate is at most 900 with Bluesky stacked and with wire v3 stacked (posted, weights, chunks, frames, domains), and the client build (gate, x-compat, cargo, e2e) passes on the split alone.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_77 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 77u32, "2026-10-26T20:13Z", "x", "2026-10-26 16:13 x: round 4 drafted and queued as split proof 5 (job-1793045638434, mirror xsplit5, spiral digest C6EA28D7). Split v3: dots_posted now uses posted_dot and first_or_empty (cell estimate 262; the estimate, characters plus defused dots, gave 299 for the cell x-compat measured at 298). Wire v3: domains crate (xc_is_tld). defuse threads a tld_check through run_text and defused_run; scanning is split from rewriting (pieces_from cuts (prev, run) pieces without the TLD check; defused_dots_by maps run_text over them), so dots_from and run_step go away. defused_run folds its two arms through spaced_before. Every new cell estimates at 266 or less (tree cells at 267 to 272 already pass x-compat). Nil replaced by [] throughout defuse.spi and tlds.spi. Proof 3 (round 2) stopped as superseded. Proof 4 (round 3) still runs only for its WIRE numbers. Its client build failed only in x-compat on dots_posted, and its gate passed: 26 crates, 0 over 1000.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_78 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 78u32, "2026-10-26T20:18Z", "x", "2026-10-26 16:18 x: proof 4 (round 3) stopped after its stacks, superseded by round 4's proof 5. Its results: split notebooks 7 of 7 ok (splitter, cells, posted, weights, chunks, frames, preview). With Bluesky stacked: posted 200, weights 761, chunks 592, frames 463, all at most 900. With wire v2 stacked: posted 1180 (is_tld 320), the others unchanged; wire notebooks tlds and defuse ok before the stop. So round 4 needs only the x-compat fix (dots_posted) and wire v3's domains crate to bring posted under 900.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_79 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 79u32, "2026-10-26T20:29Z", "tibia-lane", "20:29Z tibia arena: every remaining over file is drafted at 0 over (check-spi: 0 lines >120, formatter 0 changed, no Cons/Nil, no duplicate names). Files: random 46 cells, adjacency 27, steps 46, observer 34, scan 14, scoreboard 24, probe 33, summon 18, path 27. Method: name sub-steps as top-level functions or join points, keep every host call in its original order, move recursive loops into top-level :~ ^ join points with explicit state params, and split tuple tests into one test per claim. Proof: node Lua checks (arena-lua-node.ps1, all 13 arena main programs to Lua then lua loadfile) final4 (adjacency) 13/13, final5 (steps) 13/13, final6 (observer, via vbot and nexbot) 13/13; final7 (scan, scoreboard, probe, summon, path) is running. Landing: all of these ride arena-f5-land.ps1 in serial-gaps3. Every swap is guarded by a control hash and backed up, and the land restores everything by hash if Cells or LUA-OK < 13 fails.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_80 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 80u32, "2026-10-26T21:22Z", "tibia-lane", "21:22Z tibia apps/map/map.spi: 29 cells with 20 over became 182 cells with 0 over (0 lines >120, formatter clean). Shape: fold_fields takes a visitors record (a parametric alias); the OTBM walker's struct-of-arrays per-depth state becomes four records (node_stack, tile_mark_stack, tile_count_stack, grid split into walk_cells and mark_cells) passed as map_inputs and stacks; every loop is a top-level join point; the generated-text helpers share if_then. Host-call order is unchanged. Proof (node job-1793048867831): base and split compiled to Rust, built with rustc -O and run on the real Canary appearances/items/otbm both print 529 lines, sha256 D1506569996382F8, IDENTICAL. Negative control: one changed legend string gave DIFFERENT at exactly that line. Land: scratch map-postable/map-land.ps1 (control-hash guard, backup, layout check, Rust compile against the live lib, restore by hash on any failure), queued as the last step of serial-gaps-lib4. Arena gate result (arena-f5, 16:37): Lua 13/13, and every split file's Cells tests green with the memo off. The only red was fam5's game_calls test (fixed). Left in tibia: terrain.spi 7 over. Its 4 generated tables need a generator that emits chunked definitions (design, not a split), and its 3 hand-written ones wait for the queued terrain-raw land. network.spi 1 over (inputs_of) stays deferred until the queued candidates that read it have run.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_81 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 81u32, "2026-10-26T21:32Z", "x", "2026-10-26 17:28 x: X-FRAMES-SPLIT round 4 (proof 5, job-1793045638434) meets every size and x-compat clause of its rule. Split alone: x-compat 0 findings in 38 files; gate 26 crates, 0 over 1000 (posted 187, weights 709, chunks 580, frames 463); split notebooks 7 of 7 ok. With Bluesky A/B/D stacked: posted 199, weights 761, chunks 592, frames 463. With wire v3 stacked: domains 334, posted 866, weights 761, chunks 592, frames 463; wire notebooks 9 of 9 ok. So the domains crate took posted from 1180 to 866. The e2e clause FAILED on two tutorial steps (xc post-thread --dry-run checks/checks.spi; xc thread 9003). Both diffs are only the README oracle being stale: checks/checks.spi was respelled from !!!!Export(...) to export \"...\" (...), and the tutorial still showed the old lines (post 9 weight 266, now 248). Every other byte matched, including the 17-post round trip and the Mastodon thread, so this is not a split regression. The rule is not bent: I re-accepted the two README blocks by hand from the actual output (reviewed: three lines per block plus one weight), and proof 6 (job-1793050330342-x-split-proof6b, mirror xsplit6 synced from the live tree with the README fix, split apply rehearsed clean on a live-tree copy) reruns the client build on the split alone. It saves every crate's generated .rs and Cargo.lock for landing. If it exits 0, the split lands; if anything else fails, it is attributed before round 5.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_82 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 82u32, "2026-10-26T22:33Z", "x", "2026-10-26 18:33 x: X-FRAMES-SPLIT proof 6 (job-1793050330342, README oracle fixed) and proof 7 (job-1793052068303, pinned via SPIRAL_COMPILER_DLL to a hash-verified copy of this box's 10/26 08:19 compiler, 4FD712E9E357) both PASS the note 76 rule on the split alone: client build exit 0, x-compat 0 findings in 38 files, gate 26 crates with 0 over 1000, e2e 153 stub requests with no failed step. Not landed yet. A rehearsal showed every generated .rs differing from the tree, even crates the split never touches (api, token). Cause: the tree's committed .rs (0959354, 10/25) came from an older compiler. Today's compiler adds status-quo .clone()s (EXPORT-STRING-PARAM-RC). It is not the node: the node's shared DLL is older (499AFC18E49F), but proof 7 used this box's DLL and shows the same clones. I wrongly blamed the node DLL first and corrected main and the compiler lane. Landing rule, written before measuring: job-1793054017148-x-base-regen7 regenerates every crate of the UNSPLIT tree with the same pinned DLL. The split lands as split apply.ps1 (sources) + posted.rs, weights.rs and chunks.rs (new) + every existing crate whose proof 7 .rs differs from its base-regen .rs (the split changed it) + Cargo.lock from proof 7 (its diff is only the xc_posted, xc_weights and xc_chunks entries plus their unicode deps). Every crate whose proof 7 .rs equals its base .rs keeps the tree's committed file. If base-regen fails, or the spiral lib drifted between the two syncs so that an untouched crate differs for a lib reason, the landing waits and the cause is attributed first.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_83 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 83u32, "2026-10-26T22:40Z", "tibia-lane", "22:40Z Cross-repo report (scratch postable-by-repo.ps1, same cell rule and budget 273; generated = paths under generated/ or compiler tmp/samples). By repo and kind, over / cells: fiscal generated 10542 / 44540 (23.7%, 17 files); spiral generated 4728 / 9382 (50.4%, compiler tmp samples); eoie hand-written 2612 / 6858 (38.1%); spiral hand-written 1287 / 4503 (28.6%, lib/spiral heaviest: xsd_emit 95, sm' 78, file_system 65, date_time 63); tibia hand-written 245 / 1954 (12.5%); polyglot 110 / 384; dice 94 / 342; fiscal hand-written 39 / 60; alphabet 17 / 26; x 0 / 2601 (done). Generated code is 77% of all over cells and needs postable generators (emit one definition per row or chunk), not hand splits. tibia's 245 are almost all drafted and riding queued lands: hunting_pack, pack_sim and predict in wide-land; combat, offscreen and host-slice2 in their lands; map in serial-gaps-lib4's map-postable step; the arena family in arena-f5-retry. Left in tibia: terrain.spi's 4 generated tables (generator change) plus 3 WGSL kernels. A split there may only use top-level :~ inline helpers whose loops stay local join points, with no closures and no arrays at a join-point boundary, which needs its own design. network.spi's inputs_of stays deferred.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_84 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 84u32, "2026-10-26T22:49Z", "x", "2026-10-26 18:46 x: X-FRAMES-SPLIT LANDED (unstaged) in C:\\home\\git\\x under the note 82 rule. The base regen (job-1793054017148, unsplit tree, same pinned DLL 4FD712E9E357) against proof 7 (split): 22 crates byte-identical (api, args, assemble, cells, chain, checks, digest, fetch, jsonl, locate, net, preview, refs, resume, select, thread, timeline, token, users, view, write, xc), so they keep the tree's committed .rs. frames.rs CHANGED (base 1206 lines, split 463); posted.rs (187), weights.rs (709) and chunks.rs (580) are NEW. Landed: split apply.ps1 on apps/client (splitter, frames.spi, the posted/weights/chunks crates, package.spiproj, build.ps1), those four .rs (hash-verified equal to proof 7's), and Cargo.lock from proof 7 (only the three new crates and their unicode deps). The tree had no drift since the proof syncs (x digest 68154F66B5EF0270 at both), and no lease covered x. Side finding: with today's compiler the UNSPLIT frames is 1206 lines, so the pre-split tree failed the 1000-line gate. The split fixes that. Also landed: the README tutorial re-accept for checks.spi's export respell (note 81). Next: wire v3 (X-DEFUSE-LINKS-ONLY) rebases on this; the Bluesky write seam has fresh attribution input (write-SKY.rs 1745, xc 961 with Bluesky A/B/D on today's compiler).")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_85 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 85u32, "2026-10-27T06:08Z", "tibia-lane", "06:08Z Overnight lands after the kit rename requeue, all gated with the Cells memo off:\n- arena-f5-retry LANDED: the competitor split and fam5 (lua.native_table retired, game_calls.spi), plus the postable splits of client, measure, random, adjacency, steps, observer, scan, scoreboard, probe, summon and path. 13 arena programs LUA-OK.\n- map-postable LANDED through cells-apps-mp: map postable plus its Cells tests 5/5, with predict collected.\n- host-slice2 LANDED (world_port, world_executor), combat LANDED (gate green incl. pack_sim 39/39 and gpu_sim), offscreen LANDED (7/7), samples list sugar LANDED (6/6).\nCensus now: tibia 125 over in 5 files (from 184 in 10): hunting_pack 84, pack_sim 29, terrain 7, predict 4, network 1.\nNot landed:\n- wide-land refused because hunting_pack and pack_sim are not the wide controls. Cause: slices-c-hp on 10-26 landed only geometry and terrain. hp-slice was red only on 2 gpu_sim cells that hit Cells' 900 s timeout on a loaded box (everything else green: hunt_sim 11/11, pack_sim 39/39), and ps, evolve and huntsim then failed to apply because they anchor on hp's text. hp's control still equals the tree.\n- Next: node timing of gpu_sim base vs hp-applied (jobs tibia-gpu-t2-base and -hp, with the WGSL runner via SPIRAL_WGSL_RUN; t1 ran without it and the WGSL cells were 'unsupported'). If hp's WGSL cells stay well under 900 s, requeue the c-hp chain (hp, ps, evolve, huntsim) and then wide-land.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_86 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 86u32, "2026-10-27T07:31Z", "tibia-lane", "07:31Z hp-slice root cause found, and the decision rule written before measuring.\nCause: the hp postable split made gpu_sim's WGSL shader 80.5 MB / 1.07M lines on the troll-and-cyclops test path, against base's 3.5 MB / 72k (local Spiral compile, scratch hp-slice/wgsl-size-probe.ps1). Ranking the generated fns shows cleared_by at 453k lines and three foes_from instances at 189k each. The split's `:=` helpers were pasted into recursive join points and multiplied on nesting. That shader timed out locally at 900 s and overflowed spiral-wgsl's stack on the node; base on the node also timed out, so the node can't judge WGSL timing.\nExperiments (`:=` turned into `:~ ^` join points, scratch hp-slice/joinpoint-experiment.ps1):\n- E3 (foe_turn, advance, knight_turn): 7.6 MB / 145k lines.\n- E4 (E3 plus the 33 helpers with body >= 3 lines, >= 2 call sites, no function-typed params, not polymorphic): 4.9 MB / 110k.\n- E5 (all 111 such helpers incl. one-liners): 4.3 MB / 97k.\nRule:\n1. Apply E4's names mechanically, as one regex transform, to every hp-chain draft (hp final, evolve candidate and control, wide candidate and controls), so the chain's anchors stay consistent.\n2. Requeue the chain: hp, ps, evolve and huntsim, then wide-land.\n3. Adopt E4 iff the hp gate is green with the memo off, including gpu_sim's 6 cells; each gpu_sim WGSL cell must finish under 450 s (half the Cells limit).\n4. If any WGSL cell exceeds 450 s or times out, rerun the chain with E5's names.\n5. If E5 also fails, stop and redesign the split's helper boundaries (no third transform).")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_87 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 87u32, "2026-10-27T08:39Z", "tibia-lane", "08:38Z TERRAIN SPLIT (04:3x, drafted; rule registered before any measurement).\nDraft: route_between, weighted_choice and weighted_step lifted to top-level := pieces over two records, route_grid (reach, openings, above, grown) and step_window (area, target, from, passable, distance). route_request carries target, from and a 4-bit free mask, so route_from stays the only place that sees layout and occupancy. Join points stay nested inside := wrappers, so WGSL gets no array or closure parameters. Census of the spliced file: 171 cells, with only the 4 generated tables over 273 (route_between 4741, weighted_choice 2916 and weighted_step 997 are gone). No lines over 120, no name clashes.\nRule: adopt iff (1) the C and WGSL compiles of a probe main (step_toward on ArenaHunt and ArenaSpot, dynamic x) succeed, with generated bytes no more than 5% above the wrapped base on each backend; and (2) the Cells gate on terrain, creatures, hunting_pack, pack_sim and hunt_sim is green with the memo off, including the route tests (9 steps to 115,92) and the 99_101 detour test. If (1) is over 5% on WGSL: convert the multiply-called := helpers in the loops to :~ ^ join points (E4 method) and remeasure once. If (2) is red: fix it in the tree; never restore.\nBase: terrain-raw wrapped draft (am' create_base, index_base and set_base); after-wide puts it in the tree first.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_88 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 88u32, "2026-10-27T08:46Z", "tibia-lane", "08:46Z TERRAIN SPLIT, rule part 1 MET (04:5x).\nThe old terrain-raw draft (am' wrappers) was VOID: tibia src's package.spiproj declares only core-, so am' is unbound (ETY-UnboundVariable at every call). It never landed, and nothing of it is in the tree. The split now uses three local generic helpers mirroring am's signatures (base_create, base_read, base_write over !!!!Array ops), based on the live tree's terrain (hash equal).\nProbe (main calls step_toward on ArenaHunt and ArenaSpot with dynamic x; both compiles were from the tree's src package): tree C 180122 B and WGSL 206272 B; split C 172974 B (-4.0%) and WGSL 202434 B (-1.9%). The generated C has the same join points in both (prepare, seed, forget_above, grow, commit, sweep, layers, fill, best, forward, backward, settle, pick), so the probe reached the routing code and nothing duplicated. The formatter check is clean; its control with three stray spaces fails with \"would change\".\nPart 2 (Cells gate) is folded into tibia-after-wide (job-1793089073153, queued behind wide-wip): it writes the split into the tree unstaged, when the tree still equals the base, and gates terrain, creatures, hunting_pack, pack_sim, hunt_sim and predict with the memo off. Remaining terrain over cells: the 4 tables generated by apps/map emit/emit_spans (spot, road and sight bits, protection zone).")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_89 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 89u32, "2026-10-27T09:02Z", "tibia-lane", "09:02Z WIDE WIP gate result (job-1793087571208, memo off) and the E5 decision taken by the pre-registered rule.\nGreen: hunt_sim 11/11 and hunting_pack 1/1. predict rows are identical to the pre-WIP run (400 rows, 0 differing), and predict's generated C halved, 8542620 to 4241770 bytes.\nRed, two causes, both now fixed in the tree:\n(1) pack_sim 25 red from one type error: cyclops_camp is defined at line 214 but used from line 89 (the wide draft moved heal_deaths and heal_score above it). Moved next to troll_camp (line 39).\n(2) gpu_sim 3 of 6 red: WGSL cells timed out at 900 s. The rule (any WGSL cell over 450 s or timed out means E5) rejects E4. E5 is applied: the 78 remaining := helpers with at least 2 calls become :~ ^ join points (scratch hp-slice/e5-apply.ps1; candidates now come from the E4-transformed final, so 3 fixed + 31 E4 + 78 = 112, which is the registered 111 plus one overlap). The tree's hunting_pack now has 126 join points; backup hp-slice/hunting_pack-pre-e5-045835.spi. If E5 also fails, the rule stops transforms and the split's helper boundaries get redesigned.\nAlso in the tree, unstaged: the terrain split (POSTABLE n87/n88) and predict's two test blocks (tabbed, table kinds). Formatter check clean on src and apps/predict.\nOne combined gate, tibia-e5-gate (job-1793091712654): terrain, creatures, hunting_pack, pack_sim, hunt_sim, gpu_sim and predict with the memo off, the WGSL compile and run ms per shader, then predict rows against the 400 pre-WIP rows. tibia-after-wide was stopped before it wrote anything (stdout 0 bytes, tree hashes checked), because one gate after the fixes replaces two.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_90 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 90u32, "2026-10-27T09:34Z", "x", "2026-10-27 05:34 x: REGRESSION in the landed frames split (note 84), found by verify13 (job-1793090426395) and a node probe (job-1793092868244). The split moved posting (dot and sigil defusing) out of splitting into posted_on, which only the client's cell_posts path calls. apps/sim calls splitter's split_cell directly (world.spi chunks_for, schedule.spi, and simframes.spi's exported split_cell behind Xsim.Frames.split), so the sim stopped defusing anything: Frames.split left even go x.com plain, and x_compat_test 'autolinks never push a post over the limit' failed (40 x cell.app weighed as 23-char links). Proofs 5-7 ran only apps/client/build.ps1, never the sim build that consumes the same module, so my 'client build passes' rule missed a consumer. Fix in the tree: split_cell posts first (split_cell_on OnX label limit (posted_on OnX source)); split_cell has no client callers. Also fixed: the sim test 'dots that could start an X link are defused' now uses a real link under links-only (go x.com defused; listm.map and x.io stay plain), and splitter's bluesky_counts_a_glyph_by_its_bytes expects 3 for the 25-byte family emoji (ceil(25/10)). verify14b (job-1793093686848) reruns sim -Accept and both builds plain on this tree. verify13's accepted sim fixtures came from the broken split and are discarded.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_91 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 91u32, "2026-10-27T10:13Z", "tibia-lane", "10:13Z TERRAIN TABLES postable by generator (06:1x). The last 4 over cells in terrain were generated by apps/map (spot, road and sight bits at 10-11k chars each, and the protection zone at 1971). Data floor: the bare literals alone are 2.0-3.1k chars per table, so no encoding fits one cell; the source must split.\nDesign, measured before porting (scratch terrain-tables/prototype.ps1, formatted with the real formatter): an F-ary tree of :~ parts per word, each part a binary row chain over at most F children, with the root (word)(row) signature unchanged so callers stay untouched. Fan 3: 149 cells, 0 over; fan 4: 98 cells, 0 over, heaviest 226; fan 5/6: 6 over. Fan 4 chosen. The zone keeps its linear dy chain, split greedily by clause weight (budget 150); a row whose spans pass 100 chars is hoisted into its own name_dy<row> (dx) helper.\nIn the tree, unstaged: apps/map/map.spi (the new emitter, map.spi stays 0 over: 220 cells) and apps/map/run.ps1 (one format plus respell pass for all generated text instead of one per definition; inserts new parts before their root and drops stale parts; 129 lines).\nProof on the consumer path (job-1793095425572: the real run.ps1 over the canary server map, writing a terrain copy): 308 definitions generated, walkability dump matches results/arena-region-r45.txt, terrain copy at 477 cells with 0 over 273 and no line over 120. An exhaustive C differential keeps the old tables as old_* beside the new ones and compares every word 0..3 by row -2..93 and every dx, dy in -50..50: exit 0, all equal. Its negative control, one old road literal +1, exits 2 (bit tables differ).\nQueued: tibia-after-e5 (job-1793095859847, after the e5 gate) writes the tables and the network inputs_of split into the tree, runs the layout check, gates terrain, network, creatures, hunting_pack and hunt_sim with the memo off, then runs run.ps1 in check mode against the tree. With these, tibia's census goes to 0 over (65 files).")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_92 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 92u32, "2026-10-27T11:10Z", "tibia-lane", "11:10Z E5 VERDICT, by the pre-registered rule (hp-wgsl-rule step 4 and 5): FAIL. In tibia-e5-gate (job-1793091712654), gpu_sim's WGSL cells started at 06:53:16, 06:53:47 and 06:59:16. Two hit Cells' 900 s timeout at about 07:08, and I stopped the third at 07:07 (8 min in), so every one is over the 450 s bound. Each spiral-wgsl.exe runner peaked at about 5.8 GB, and three in parallel paged the box (main's health check, 0.45 GB free). Rule step 5 applies: no third transform; the split's helper boundaries get redesigned. Pack_sim's, terrain's and predict's results in this gate are still pending and are read when it ends.\nFacts for the redesign: base (pre-split) shader 3.5 MB / 72k lines, the hp split 80.5 MB, E4 4.9 MB / 110k, E5 4.3 MB / 97k. Size alone doesn't decide it: at 4.3 MB, E5 is close to base, yet its cells still run past 900 s with runners at 5.8 GB, so shader shape (join-point count, struct sizes from the wide records) matters to the WGSL toolchain, not bytes alone. The next step compares the base, E4 and E5 shaders by function count, max call depth and largest functions (scratch hp-slice/wgsl-rank.ps1) before any redesign is drafted.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_93 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 93u32, "2026-10-27T11:12Z", "tibia-lane", "11:12Z REDESIGN INPUT, measured (scratch hp-slice/wgsl-shape.ps1 over the gpu_probe shaders). Size is not the driver; the calling convention is.\nbase: 72k lines, 39 fns, 2,692 params in total (max 375). The pack state lives inside 4 giant loops (sp_loop 19k and 17k lines, sp_each 11k twice, about 190 params each).\nE4: 110k lines, 91 fns, 8,270 params (max 407).\nE5: 97k lines, 153 fns, 14,123 params (max 407): knight_turn 18k lines/223 params, foe_turn 15k/220, foes_from 6.9k/407 (twice), advance 4.8k/222, soonest_from 856/406.\nReading: Spiral flattens records into scalar WGSL parameters, so every join point the transforms added receives the whole flattened pack (~220 scalars), and the foes record helpers take ~400. E5 carries 5x base's parameter traffic, which matches the WGSL runner's 5.8 GB and >900 s.\nRedesign rule (registered before drafting): on gpu_sim's path, a helper takes and returns only the fields it touches (a narrow record or scalars), never the whole pack, camp or foes record. Measured target: E6's gpu_probe shader keeps total params at most 2x base (<= 5,400) and max params at most 400, compiles, and the gpu_sim WGSL cells finish under 450 s each in a gate with the WGSL cells serialized. Measure the shader shape first (minutes, compile-class), then gate once.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_94 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 94u32, "2026-10-27T11:58Z", "tibia-lane", "11:58Z HP HELPER-BOUNDARY REDESIGN (rule step 5), E6 to E8, every shape measured on the node with the compiler pinned (022C3338).\nMethod: transitive inline cost over the := call graph (scratch hp-slice/inline-cost.ps1) named the multiplier chain, hunt_at x5, advance x2, beat_acts (3 view arms, 4 foes_where copies, 3 knights_act), foe_acts/party/aimed/turned (about 5 foe_turn_at copies per foe) and hits x5, at about 8.6M inlined lines.\nE6: hoist the expensive calls out of branches. beat_acts becomes one \"foes, knights, foes\" pipeline with a per-view picker (acts_first); foe_acts plans (turn_plan/party_plan give flip plus the prepared pack) and calls foe_turn_at once between flipped_if. Inline cost 8.6M to 0.67M; WGSL 80.5 MB (hp) to 19.1 MB.\nE7: the picker becomes data. A u32 first-phase mask (band of 6) lets foes_masked be ONE :~ ^ join point called twice (a closure can't be a WGSL join-point param). Melee planning returns (outcome, foe) so hits runs once. 7 dead helpers removed. Params 4,607 total, max 406.\nE8: soonest_claimed_spawn's join point captured the whole camp and pack (406 params) for an argmin over 6 slots, so it becomes a left fold over (wait, slot) pairs (slot_wait, sooner_wait), with a test block pinning band_size at 6. Shape: 243k lines, 365 fns, params total 3,982 (rule bound 5,400; base 2,692; E5 14,414), max 247 (bound 400; base 375). WGSL 16.3 MB.\nRule status: the shape bounds are met. The last criterion (each gpu_sim WGSL cell under 450 s, cells serialized via SPIRAL_CELLS_CELL_CONCURRENCY=1, memo off) runs locally as tibia-e8-gpu (job-1793102234953). E8 is in the tree (unstaged), with the E5 version backed up in hp-slice/hunting_pack-tree-e5-075630.spi; postable stays 0 over and layout is clean. pack_sim's 39 pinned expectations check semantic equivalence on the node next.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_95 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 95u32, "2026-10-27T13:55Z", "tibia-lane", "13:55Z E8 SEMANTICS AND TREE CHANGES PROVEN on the node (src gates 2 and 3, compiler pinned 022C3338, memo off, source digest C8A91B05D22F4477 = the tree with E8 hunting_pack, the regenerated terrain tables, network's inputs_of split and both pack_sim ordering fixes). Every executed row passes: pack_sim 39/39 (all pinned expectations hold, so E6 to E8's hoisting, picker mask, melee plan and soonest fold change no behavior), network 7/7, hunting_pack 2/2 (including the band-of-six guard), hunt_sim 9/9, creatures 11/11, terrain 17/17. The 6 WGSL rows are \"unsupported\" on the node (no GPU runner there) and are judged locally.\nTwo node environment lessons, both now in the node memory: (1) a bundle run needs an ancestor lib/spiral plus SPIRAL_COMPILER_PACKAGE_DIR, or every file fails with Unbound module testing (run 1 void); (2) a node Cells job needs its own MIX_BUILD_PATH, or another lane's Cells job holds the shared _build lock and every file comes back not_run (run 2's last five files void).\nRemaining for the hp split: tibia-e8-gpu (job-1793102234953), running since 09:40 with WGSL cells serialized, judged by the pre-registered 450 s per WGSL cell. E9 (one shared stepping join point, 15.2 MB against 16.3 MB) is drafted as the fallback.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_96 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 96u32, "2026-10-27T14:08Z", "tibia-lane", "14:08Z E8 VERDICT and E10 RULE (registered before measuring). E8 FAILED the timing criterion: the first serialized gpu_sim WGSL cell ran 13.5 min (09:50:19 to 10:03:54, stopped on the verdict), CPU-bound at about 371 MB (E5 was 5.8 GB per runner). The shape bounds were met (params 3,982 total, max 247), so params alone was an incomplete proxy. E8's shader puts the whole step into one function: sp_cleared_by is 164k lines / 51k vars (base's largest is 19k), and compile time grows superlinearly with function size. Base works because it sits between the extremes: about 40 functions of at most ~20k lines.\nCandidates: knights_act calls knight_turn twice (the lead, then the ally via turned), so the knight subtree is inlined twice into cleared_by, the same pattern foes_masked fixed for foes. Variants measured on E8: E10a = knight_turn as one :~ ^ join point; E10b = E10a plus player_acts as :~ ^; E10c = E10b plus charged_at as :~ ^.\nRule: pick the variant with the smallest largest-function line count among those with total params <= 5,400 and max params <= 400. Adopt it into the tree and run the serialized gpu_sim WGSL gate, which passes iff every WGSL cell finishes under 450 s. If no variant gets the largest function under 60k lines, the next round splits ruled_acts' arms rather than adding gate time.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_97 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 97u32, "2026-10-27T14:16Z", "tibia-lane", "14:16Z E10/E11 measured, rule (n96) applied mechanically.\nE10 (on E8): knight_turn as a join point halves the shader (16.3 MB to 7.6 MB) and cleared_by drops 164k to 5k lines; knights_act had inlined the knight subtree twice (lead, then the ally via turned). E10a/b/c's largest function was 62,021 / 61,646 / 61,646, so none got under the 60k bound, and per the rule the next round split ruled_acts instead of spending gate time.\nE11 (on E10c, which ties E10b on the largest function and is smaller on lines and bytes): E11a adds decides as a join point, E11b adds heals_if_wanted, E11c adds random_acts. All three: largest function decides 40,065 lines, then foes_from 39,569 (every function now at or below 40k, against base's 19k max and E8's 164k), max params 247, total params 4,863 / 5,087 / 5,306 (all within 5,400), WGSL 6.92 / 6.91 / 6.84 MB. Tie on the largest function; lines and bytes disagree, so the tiebreak is the other registered metric, total params (the property that sank E5): E11a.\nE11a is in the tree now (unstaged; E8 backed up in hp-slice/hunting_pack-tree-e8-*.spi), formatter-clean and postable-clean. E11a differs from E8 only in four := to :~ ^ conversions (knight_turn, player_acts, charged_at, decides), a code-placement change, so pack_sim's 39/39 on E8 (node gate 3) still covers its semantics. Final criterion queued: tibia-e11a-gpu (job-1793110501495), gpu_sim plus network, WGSL cells serialized, memo off; passes iff each gpu_sim WGSL cell finishes under 450 s. network's WGSL row is the first GPU check of its inputs_of split.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_98 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 98u32, "2026-10-27T14:43Z", "tibia-lane", "14:43Z E11a VERDICT and the real metric. A direct runner timing (spiral-wgsl.exe on the compiled gpu_probe shader, same machine, job-1793110687809): base 3.52 MB in 211 s (peak 948 MB); E11a 6.92 MB TIMED OUT at 900 s (peak 2,313 MB). E11a fails, and its Cells gate was dequeued before it started. Fully inlined size (each function's lines plus its callees' inlined sizes per call, computed from the shader's own call graph, scratch hp-slice/wgsl-inlined.ps1) explains every result: base 241k, E8 3.54M, E11a 4.28M, E5 27.1M. The GPU driver inlines all calls, so WGSL function boundaries never reduced its work; distinct call sites on the hot path are what it compiles. Per contribution (times inlined x own lines, wgsl-contrib.ps1): E11a pastes foes_from 4x (2 foe phases x 2 outer loops), decides and player_acts 3x, and every terrain lookup about 404x (base: 13x).\nE12 design: turn repeated calls into loops over a small index, so each expensive call has one call site. (1) beat_acts runs phases 0..2 (foes on mask, knights, foes on complement) in one join point; foes_masked and knights_act appear once. (2) knights_act runs knights 0..1 (lead, then ally under flipped_if) in one join point, with the ally flag captured from the input pack as before. (3) simulate and cleared_by share one stepped loop (E9). Built on E11a.\nRule (registered before measuring): adopt E12 iff its fully inlined size is <= 500k lines (base 241k ran 211 s; linear scaling puts 450 s near 510k) AND the direct runner timing on its shader is <= 450 s; then node pack_sim/hunt_sim equivalence and the serialized Cells gate are the final checks. If the inlined size is still over 500k, the next lever is terrain lookup call sites (blocked/protected/road per neighbor), measured the same way.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_99 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 99u32, "2026-10-27T15:07Z", "tibia-lane", "15:07Z E12 VERDICT (rule n98): FAILED. Fully inlined 2.59M lines (E11a 4.28M, base 241k), runner TIMED OUT at 900 s (peak 1,344 MB). The phase and knight loops plus the shared stepping loop cut the inlined size by 40%, not the 9x needed. The ×2 left on decides, foes_from and player_acts is legitimate per-layout specialization: the probe runs a troll hunt (default layout) and ArenaSpot clears, each with its own static camp.\nWhere the rest is: decides alone holds 140 direct terrain-lookup call sites (base's whole step loop has 15). terrain.step_toward, step_away and scored_step are := routers, and the decision tree calls them from many branches (approach twice through two engaged_by sites, goal, spawn return, two retreat kinds); foes call step_away twice (flee, keep range) plus step_toward (chase). Every call site pastes a full router with its per-cell terrain checks.\nBackend check: wgpu honors WGPU_BACKEND, and DX12 is slower (a 200 KB shader takes 62.6 s on DX12 against 11.7 s on Vulkan), so the driver is not the way out; the inlined code has to shrink.\nIn flight: E13 (one engaged_by call site; retreat_due decides first, with no behavior change) measuring now; then an ablation (routers stubbed to stay put, measurement only, never the tree) to learn what share of the inlined size routing owns before choosing between movement executors and a pure codegen-neutral re-split from base.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_100 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 100u32, "2026-10-27T15:16Z", "tibia-lane", "15:16Z ABLATION of E13 (measurement only, never the tree; scratch hp-slice/ablate.ps1 stubs terrain functions in a stage copy). Fully inlined lines: E13 2.39M; routers (step_toward, step_away, live-probe step, scored_step) stubbed to stay put: 0.89M; routers plus lookups (arena_spot_blocked, arena_road, arena_spot_protected) stubbed: 0.41M; base 0.24M. Terrain calls own 83% of E13's inlined size; hunting_pack's own logic, at 413k, is already within the 500k bound.\nWhere the router copies are (calls to the routers' internal loops per WGSL function): decides holds 4 full step_toward copies (approach, patrol goal, sweep spawn and territory spawn each call it), player_acts 1 (the random path's approach), foes_from 1 (chase), each counted again per layout specialization (troll hunt, ArenaSpot clears).\nBase has the same features (party, ally, evolve, footing, patrol, territory, retreat; only the wide-record nesting differs) in 273 definitions against 584. Two routes: (a) targeted, knight step hoisting in decides (4 to 1 step_toward sites) plus computing neighbor walkability once, about 0.75M plus about 0.4M saved; (b) a codegen-neutral re-split from base (every extracted helper single-caller, provable by identical generated output), which has to replay the seven anchored wide-record scripts on base by hand.\nBefore choosing, the runner time vs inlined size curve is being measured on the two ablation shaders (889k and 413k; wrong results, real compile cost): job tibia-runner-curve. The linear guess behind the 500k bound (base 241k in 211 s) gets checked there.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_101 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 101u32, "2026-10-27T15:29Z", "tibia-lane", "15:29Z DECISION (11:3x): the targeted route is closed; re-split from base. Evidence, all measured today:\n- Terrain is not the cause: base hunting_pack compiled with today's terrain (regenerated tables, split routers) inlines to 254k against 241k (+5%).\n- WGSL runner time is superlinear in inlined size: base 241k in 211 s; the 413k ablation shader (E13 with routers AND lookups stubbed, 141 fns) ran past 11 min. A sum-over-all-functions metric ranks the shaders the same way (base 504k, ablation 987k, E14 5.37M).\n- So even with every terrain call removed, the split's own logic (413k) misses the 450 s rule. E14 (one knight route call site in decides, 11 routing helpers folded into a plan/route/apply shape; type-checks) brings E13's 2.39M to 2.12M, which is still far over.\n- Only base's call structure compiles in time: 273 definitions, about 40 WGSL functions, and its big functions inlined once each.\nRoute: a codegen-neutral re-split of base hunting_pack. Split cells for postability ONLY by extracting single-caller := helpers (a single-caller := inlines to exactly the same code as written inline), never by adding a helper with two or more callers. Then replay evolve's changes and the seven wide-record transforms on top. Acceptance (registered now): fully inlined size within 10% of base-nt (254k) and the runner on the probe shader under 450 s; pack_sim 39/39, hunt_sim, hunting_pack and gpu_sim C rows green on the node; postable 0 over. The tree keeps the E11a WIP until then (gpu_sim WGSL is red there by this evidence; everything else is green).")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_102 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 102u32, "2026-10-27T16:06Z", "tibia-lane", "16:06Z Re-split step 1 done, codegen-neutral by measurement. (a) Runner curve (job-1793114159323): e13-noroute-nolookup (4.52 MB) and e13-noroute (4.62 MB) both TIMED OUT at 900 s on the node. This confirms the decision in note 101: the split's own logic is too slow without terrain. (b) r2 = base hunting_pack + the seven wide record types (foe chase/respawn/aim, hunter clock/tally, pack score, camp foes/crew/timing/motion/knight) + the wide with_* helpers. It came from scratch resplit/r1-wide-types.ps1 (134 regex reads, 32 nested single-field updates) plus about 25 hand-nested constructions and multi-field updates. It type-checks on C and WGSL (gpu_probe). Fully inlined WGSL = 254025 lines over 245 fns, exactly base-nt; the only diff is the parameter order of the start join point. Next: postable split (82 cells over in r2, heaviest foe_turn at 6293), using single-caller := helpers only. Then the pack_sim API (search_plan, trial, trial_on, sized, turn_at; the tree pack_sim differs from base by 1229 lines), then the node gates.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_103 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 103u32, "2026-10-27T16:51Z", "tibia-lane", "16:51Z Re-split step 2. Runner on r2's shader (local K1100M, job-1793117361808): 264.7 s, exit 0 with the probe facts matched. It meets the 450 s rule; base at 241k was 211 s. r3 = r2 for every definition gpu_sim reaches textually (183 of 296), with the tree's versions for the rest. Built by scratch resplit/r3-merge.ps1: 34 unreached swapped to tree, 2 dropped (gone from the tree: evolve_tactic, evolve_tactic_from), 52 tree-only items appended, then a dependency reorder with kind-qualified keys, since swing_after_step is both a union and a function. r3's WGSL is byte-identical to r2's (3443504 bytes, 254025 inlined, 245 fns). The unreached section is 0 over; 52 reached cells are still over. Signature drift tree vs r2 visible to consumers: only dance_step (turn), aliases and param names. The tree's evolve API (trial, search_plan, trial_on, sized, turn_at, all_killed) comes with the tree items. Running now: node gate tibia-r3-gate (pack_sim, hunting_pack, hunt_sim, gpu_sim on r3), and a local greedy search (job-1793119796297). That search takes each reached cell's tree version and keeps it only if the WGSL stays at 245 fns / 254025 inlined. arena_hunt is already accepted byte-identical. Cells that stay over after it get single-caller := extraction by hand.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_104 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 104u32, "2026-10-27T17:30Z", "tibia-lane", "17:30Z Re-split step 3: r5 is 0 over in hunting_pack (2267 lines, no line over 120), 245 WGSL fns, 252199 fully inlined lines (-0.7% vs base-nt 254025). Rule registered before the measurements: per change, same fn count and |delta inlined| <= 100 lines; cumulative <= +1%; a final runner timing under 450 s decides.\nMethod: (1) A greedy search (scratch resplit/try-tree.ps1) took each over cell's tree version in turn and kept 22 of them at an exact 245/254025 (start, uniform_camp, random_pick, creature_hits_hunter, observe, score, hunter_of, ...).\n(2) 28 hand splits (scratch resplit/splits/*.spi, applied by r3-merge.ps1 -Splits). They use single-purpose := helpers, keep every heavy call site's count (foe_turn 4x in foe_acts, engage 2x in ruled_acts, creature_hits_hunter 4x in the melee arms, dances 2x), and keep local join points local. The turn/beat records come from the tree; records flatten at compile time.\nRoot cause of the E-series blow-up, found by the greedy search: the tree's clear_run alone gives 11.6M inlined lines (371 fns), clear_ledger_of 11.6M, and knight_turn 1.78M (260 fns). All other tree versions are within 0.3% or fail to type-check.\nr3's node gate (job-1793119559717): hunting_pack 2/2, hunt_sim 9/9, gpu_sim C 3/3, pack_sim 36/39. The 3 failures are the dance tests calling dance_step (turn_at ...), the tree signature; r5's split provides it. The same pack_sim run hit a gcc ICE (segfault at main.c:192227); watch for it on r5.\nRunning now: r5 node gate (job-1793122241663) and r5 runner timing (job-1793122150845).")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_105 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 105u32, "2026-10-27T17:42Z", "tibia-lane", "17:42Z r5 measured: runner 280.7 s, exit 0 with the probe facts matched (job-1793122150845; limit 450 s). Rules for r5's node gate (job-1793122241663), written before its result.\n(A) pack_sim 39/39 and hunting_pack, hunt_sim and gpu_sim C all green: scratch resplit/write-r5-to-tree.ps1 -Go writes r5 into tibia src. It backs up E11a by hash and refuses if any dotnet/erl process reads tibia\\src. Then a node follow-up gate runs terrain, network, creatures and the apps that open hunting_pack (predict, sweep). Then the TIBIA-PARK status.\n(B) The only pack_sim failures are build errors (the gcc ICE seen on r3: a segfault in a 192k-line main.c), with no assertion failure: rerun pack_sim alone on the node once. If the ICE repeats, run the same notebook with E11a's tree hunting_pack as the control. An ICE on both sides means it isn't r5's, so (A) applies, with the ICE filed under its own item. An ICE on r5 only means r5 is held and I bisect the splits by test.\n(C) Any assertion failure: r5 is held. I compare the failing test's output on E11a vs r5, find the split that changed the behaviour (each split is one file in resplit/splits, so bisect by dropping files), fix it, and re-measure WGSL size and runner time.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_106 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 106u32, "2026-10-27T18:03Z", "tibia-lane", "18:03Z The E-series cause is reproduced, not just correlated. The C size cost of the inline shape is measured.\n(1) apps/predict (C backend) against each hunting_pack: tree E11a gives predict 5.7 MB and sweep 5.6 MB; r3 (base call structure) 8.5 MB and 42.7 MB; r5 8.4 MB and 45.3 MB. Ranking the C functions (scratch resplit/c-rank.ps1): in r6's sweep, one function, simulate's tick loop (loop5), is 225811 of 276443 lines, because advance inlines into it whole: 3 view arms x 2 knight_turn plus 4 foe loops. The tree shares those as join points (decides 29k, foes_from 10k, player_acts 8.6k).\n(2) Experiment r7 = r6 with only knight_turn made a top-level join point (:~ ^). WGSL goes 252199 -> 1784599 inlined (258 fns), the tree's knight_turn number to the line. sweep C goes 34.9 -> 8.6 MB. So a join-point boundary on the per-tick knight path costs about 7x on WGSL and saves about 4x on C. Most likely, though unproven, the boundary makes the tactic and pack arguments dynamic, so every brain/patrol arm is compiled where the inline form prunes by the probe's static values.\n(3) Adopted: r6 = r5 with foe_acts reshaped from 4 foe_turn call sites to one (pick the view and a flip bit, one foe_turn, un-flip). Semantics are identical case by case. The WGSL is byte-identical to r5 (the probe's camp is a party of 1, so the extra sites were already pruned). sweep C goes 45.3 -> 34.9 MB.\nRule for C size, written now: it is not an acceptance criterion unless it breaks a build (r3's pack_sim gcc ICE is the candidate; note 105 (B)). If it does, the fix is a C-only join point via the lib backend_switch idiom on knight_turn/player_acts, never a backend-wide join point, which is (2)'s 7x on WGSL.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_107 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 107u32, "2026-10-27T18:25Z", "tibia-lane", "18:25Z r5 gate (job-1793122241663): hunting_pack 2/2, hunt_sim 9/9, gpu_sim C 3/3. pack_sim 38/39; all 3 dance tests now pass. The one failure (a_hunter_keeping_four_tiles_away..., c, 128 s) is a build error, not an assertion. Its stderr holds only gcc's ICE (segfault at main.c:191549), the same shape as r3's (main.c:192227). Note 106's measurement explains it: the base call structure inlines the whole per-tick turn into simulate's loop (225k C lines in one function).\nFix r8 = r6 + knight_turn as `!!!!BackendSwitch({ C = shared join point; Wgsl = inline })`. tibia src is a core-only package that already uses intrinsics (ArrayCreate/Index/IndexSet). lib's backend.backend_switch has no Wgsl key, and WGSL does not fall back to the C arm: a C-only switch fails to compile on WGSL, tested.\nMeasured: WGSL is byte-identical to r5 and r6 (hash 5DB6C472AB68C430), so r5's 280.7 s runner time and 252199 inlined hold unchanged. sweep C 45.3 -> 8.6 MB, and its largest function is now knight_turn_shared at 37.5k lines, where the loop was 225k. hunting_pack is 0 over, with no line over 120.\nr8 node gate queued: job-1793125277348 (pack_sim, hunting_pack, hunt_sim, gpu_sim). Rule (A) of note 105 applies to r8: scratch resplit/write-draft-to-tree.ps1 -Draft r8 -Go.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_108 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 108u32, "2026-10-27T18:43Z", "tibia-lane", "18:43Z M2 data, full and not sampled (10-27 14:5x local). The cross-repo census tool is scratch postable-repos.ps1, one row per repo. It reads each repo's git-tracked .spi (skipping deps/, target/, bench/) and uses x splitter's cell rule. Positive control: tibia gives 65 files, 3531 cells, 0 over, identical to the per-file census.\nOver / cells by repo: x 0/2859; tibia 0/3531 (stays 0 at 3571 cells once the hunting_pack re-split lands); kit 2978/7863 (top: state/bench.spi 54, state/agile.spi 44, state/runtime_drift.spi 41); spiral 6117/14060; fiscal 65/115 (src/generator.spi 39, corpus_checks 22); polyglot 54/165 (lib/math/math.spi 19, apps/perf/Perf.spi 14); dice 51/187 (ui model near/rpc.spi 18, lib/dice.spi 11); dir-tree-html 26/94; blog 23/87; alphabet 9/14 (apps/documents/documents.spi 8).\nspiral by directory: apps/compiler/tmp 4824 of 9585 (1277 tracked compiler sample files, mega_* the heaviest); lib/spiral 1097/3565 (31 %), plus leptos 31, rust 19, near 3; apps/cells/spi 73/312; apps/compiler/spi 39/89; apps/spiral 25/54.\nTwo scope questions this baseline raises, for the owners: (1) are apps/compiler/tmp samples in scope? They are generated or fixture programs; by the item's own rule generated code is out, which would put spiral at 1293 over, not 6117; (2) kit's state/*.spi are data records written by tools, not hand code; they are out of scope by the same reading.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_109 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 109u32, "2026-10-27T19:18Z", "tibia-lane", "19:18Z r8 gate (job-1793125277348): hunting_pack 2/2, hunt_sim 9/9, gpu_sim C 3/3; pack_sim 37/39. Two reds. (1) golden_seed_7_troll_pack/rust: \"Cannot find the backend Rust in the backend switch op\"; knight_turn's switch had only C and Wgsl. (2) a_hunter_keeping_four_tiles_away.../c: a gcc ICE again (\"during IPA pass: sra\", segfault at main.c:88669), even with the 225k-line loop gone.\nReproduced locally with Cells' exact flags (-std=c11 -O2 -w -fwrapv) and scoop gcc 15.2.0 on a probe compiling pack_sim's hunter_rows against r8 (scratch resplit/ice-probe.ps1). -fno-ipa-sra alone makes it compile. Bisected with __attribute__((noipa)) per function (scratch resplit/ice-bisect.ps1): the ICE needs BOTH each358 and each359, the two specializations of foes_act_where's loop (407 parameters each; 355 i32, 16 bool, 31 union structs). acts_at_poll passed two different closures (early_at, late_at), which made two copies. Marking either one alone still ICEs.\nFix r9: the tree's shape. A u32 mask of early slots is computed once (early_mask fold), and foes_act_where takes `mask : u32` with picked_in, called with the mask and then its complement, so there is one specialization. knight_turn's switch also gains a Rust arm (shared, like C). Results: WGSL hash unchanged (5DB6C472AB68C430; the probe's camp never takes the poll path), 0 over, no long line, and the probe's C compiles with gcc 15.2 (7.3 MB, was 8.6). r9 node gate: job-1793128716026.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_110 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 110u32, "2026-10-27T19:56Z", "tibia-lane", "19:56Z hunting_pack re-split is IN THE TREE (10-27 16:0x local, unstaged; the user commits). r9 replaced E11a in tibia src/hunting_pack.spi: 72B4E260B65B over EE117A5D86E1, with the E11a backup in scratch resplit/hunting_pack-tree-e11a-before-r9.spi. Rule (A) of note 105 was met by the r9 node gate (job-1793128716026): pack_sim 39/39, hunting_pack 2/2, hunt_sim 9/9, gpu_sim C 3/3 (the WGSL rows are unsupported on the node, as in every run). The source digest 3B92AE8BD933049A is the same in the gate and the tree. Registered acceptance, all met:\n- fully inlined WGSL 252199 lines against base-nt's 254025 (-0.7 %), 245 fns;\n- runner on the probe shader 280.7 s (limit 450 s), the shader byte-identical to the one timed;\n- pack_sim, hunt_sim, hunting_pack and gpu_sim C green;\n- postable 0 over: tibia 65 files, 3572 cells.\nThe README's postable line now says 3,572 cells, with one GPU sentence under Simulators. Follow-ups queued on the node: predict app tests on r9 (job-1793128890409), then terrain, network and creatures on the tree (job-1793131069660).\nMethod summary for future splits, from notes 101-109: keep the base call structure on GPU-reached code. Take a function's tree version only when the WGSL inlined size stays equal. Split by := helpers that keep each heavy call's site count. Reshape to fewer sites where the semantics allow (foe_acts' 4 foe_turn sites became 1). Share heavy code on C/Rust only, through !!!!BackendSwitch, never on WGSL (a WGSL join point on the knight path costs 7x). Give no join point a closure argument that differs per call site (two 407-parameter specializations crash gcc 15.2's IPA-SRA); pass a runtime mask instead.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_111 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 111u32, "2026-10-27T20:11Z", "tibia-lane", "20:11Z r9 follow-up: predict app tests on r9 (job-1793128890409) GREEN, 2/2 c (tabbed_appends_tab_separated_numbers, table_kinds_are_distinct_row_tags). terrain, network and creatures on the r9 tree are next (job-1793131069660).\nlib physics.spi postable split (lib's first hand split under this item; lib/spiral was 1097 over at note 108). It is drafted in scratch lib-physics/physics.spi from live base E43FB9266CA4, and physics goes from 21 over (of 123 cells) to 2 over (of 221). By kind:\n(1) Over-long definitions get shared parts. net_force and rate_with are shared by newton_second_ps and relativity_ps; cromer_moved serves euler_cromer_ps; the shift instance keeps its ds-typed coercion and hands off to a typed shifted helper.\n(2) Long tests are split along their natural groups (derivative x4, shift x3), with every assert unchanged.\n(3) `test ... static` preludes and define-only tests become top-level definitions: pedal_coast, damped_ho_forces, the states_ps family (Halley, rock, wind, sun gravity), the baseball family, and both multi-particle preludes. The stream versions of force_on, forces_on and newton_second_mps take an _s suffix so no top-level name is defined twice.\n(4) The kinetic-energy tests: 7 helpers byte-identical between the list and stream tests are promoted once (checked by text equality); the variants take _l/_s; each test is now 3 lines plus shared ke_chart/ke_curve helpers.\nProof: checkpoint 1 (relativity and kinetic-energy work not yet in) node lib gate LIB-PHYSICS-POSTABLE-1 GREEN, 43 of 45 rows passed and 0 red. Checkpoint 2 (everything above) is queued as LIB-PHYSICS-POSTABLE-2 (job-1793131923585). Left over: wave_1 (3122) and wave_2 (2751), chart generators with an inner :~ main and an F# interpolation macro, which get their own step. The land waits for kit's TRAIN to publish (kit's lib cut-off was about 16:30).")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_112 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 112u32, "2026-10-27T20:27Z", "tibia-lane", "20:27Z r9 tree follow-ups done, all green: terrain 17/17, network 7/7 and creatures 11/11 (job-1793131069660; source digest 3B92AE8BD933049A, the tree as written; the WGSL rows are unsupported on the node), after predict 2/2 (job-1793128890409). With pack_sim 39/39, hunting_pack 2/2, hunt_sim 9/9 and gpu_sim C 3/3 (job-1793128716026), every src module plus the predict app is green on the r9 tree. tibia: 0 over in 65 files.\nlib physics postable: checkpoint 2 (relativity and kinetic-energy restructuring, 2 over) node gate GREEN, rows 43 red 0 (job-1793131923585). Checkpoint 3 (wave tests too, physics at 0 over, 242 cells, no long line) is gating as job-1793132518206. The slice is whole-file with a base-hash guard (E43FB9266CA4) in scratch lib-physics/physics-postable-apply-3.ps1. The land waits for kit's \"TRAIN window closed\".")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_113 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 113u32, "2026-10-27T20:58Z", "tibia-lane", "20:58Z lib physics postable checkpoint 3 (final, 0 over) node gate GREEN, rows 43 red 0 (job-1793132518206); physics is land-ready. Also drafted: LIB-SMALL-POSTABLE, 7 unclaimed small lib files in one whole-file slice with a base-hash guard per file (scratch lib-small/small-postable-apply.ps1, checked: dry run, apply equals drafts 7/7, re-run refused). Over cells 15 -> 3.\n- util: test ski with 17 asserts becomes 7 tests (asserts unchanged, count checked 17 = 17).\n- csv: quoted_as_tsv gets quoted_cell_text, bare_cells_text and tsv_piece; its 2 tests become 4.\n- cursor_walk: 2 pair-tests become 4 single-assert tests (static literals kept static).\n- wasm: from_js_value renamed locals.\n- erlang_port: spawn_executable_name, plus the os_pid/exit test's fixtures erl_halt_port and halt_args and its loop exit_text, exit_text_or and exit_text_within.\n- platform: gleam_os_command_output.\n- erlang_term: imports split into 4 helpers called in the original order.\nLeft on purpose: cursor_walk.walked_from (a recursive join point; splitting needs continuation plumbing), platform.run_platform and is_windows (F# preprocessor emission in macro text), iter.try_for_each (raw statement splices). All 7 type-check as entries on a scratch lib copy. The node gate on all 7 files is queued (job-1793134773967), and the land runs after kit's TRAIN window with the others (scratch node-kit/land-after-train.ps1).")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_114 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 114u32, "2026-10-27T21:20Z", "tibia-lane", "21:20Z More lib, all waiting for kit's TRAIN window. GRANULAR heals GREEN on the node: LIB-SEQ-REV (seq 26/26, rows 42 red 0) and LIB-LISTM-REV (listm' 28/28, rows 45 red 0). listm' re-gates on the post-nil file before its land, as agreed with main.\nSecond small postable batch LIB-SMALL-POSTABLE-2 (channel, lustre, otp_supervisor, networking, store), over cells 18 -> 7:\n- channel: gleam_channel; rust_receive_within and gleam_receive_within; a receipt alias; receipt_within for the test.\n- lustre: counter_text, column_style and counter_column split out of view; button gets shorter locals; both tests share one counter_html template (built with ++#, which equals the old literals piece for piece), and the first test's updates move into counter_after_three.\n- otp_supervisor: counting_worker fixture.\n- networking: request_header converts inline.\n- store: storage_key, storage_item_raw, stored_item and json_text, with statement order kept.\nEach file type-checks against an unchanged control on a fresh lib copy (errors base = draft). Two traps hit and fixed on the way: a backend_switch arm calling a generic helper needs its own `: t` annotation (ValueRestriction otherwise), and one of my edits computed a regex index before an earlier replacement, which cut lustre's `/// ## tests` and sm'_operators lines. I redid it from base and checked the heading is present. Left on purpose: networking's request_builder_switch record type, the tcp_client type macro and client_connect_async's header; otp_supervisor's new and with_restart 3-arm matches; store's transaction_* generic signatures. Gate job-1793136092103 (after LIB-SMALL-POSTABLE). Both are in scratch node-kit/land-after-train.ps1.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_115 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 115u32, "2026-10-27T21:29Z", "tibia-lane", "21:29Z Gate outcomes for the small lib postable batches. Both first gates were RED for the same reason: the lib gate counts a file with no test rows as not green. LIB-SMALL-POSTABLE: util 7/7, csv 5/5, cursor_walk 4/4, erlang_port 1/1, platform 2/2 and erlang_term 5/5 were green, but wasm.spi has no tests. LIB-SMALL-POSTABLE-2: channel 2/2 and otp_supervisor 1/1 were green; networking and store have no tests; lustre's two `gleam -t javascript` tests failed with no baseline run, so I can't tell the node's JS environment from my change, and lustre stays out. Rebuilt as gate-provable slices only:\n- LIB-SMALL-POSTABLE-B: the 6 green files of batch 1 (job-1793136261750).\n- LIB-SMALL-POSTABLE-C: channel and otp_supervisor plus otp_actor (job-1793136627594). otp_actor's 3 actor tests now use named fixtures and handlers (counter_step/counter_inbox, length_reply/array_actor, self_reply/self_actor, ask_i32), with the i32_inbox and self_inbox aliases; its over cells go 5 -> 1, and it is clean on Gleam against its control.\nThe untested edits to wasm, networking and store stay out of lib: nothing on the gate path exercises them. Rule I'm applying from now on: a lib postable slice includes only files whose tests run on the node. The land list (scratch node-kit/land-after-train.ps1) is physics-3, threading, seq-rev, small-B, small-C, all after kit's TRAIN window.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_116 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 116u32, "2026-10-27T21:35Z", "tibia-lane", "21:35Z LIB-SMALL-POSTABLE-D (json.spi), over cells 9 -> 1. json's decode tests each folded a decoded record into a checksum inline. The checksums become typed score functions: city_query_score, forecast_score (shared by the two forecast tests, whose arithmetic is identical), quantities_score/tags_score/tagged_score, lines_score/line_score, where_score/other_score/target_score, span_score and quote_score. Each test keeps its runtime `~reply` literal and its assert (11 asserts before and after). Each sub-expression is unchanged; only its grouping into named functions moves. Integer sums regroup associatively. It type-checks on Rust against its control (0 = 0). Left: tool_schema_describes_records_unions_options_and_arrays (1064), whose expected text is a `^^` literal chain; I haven't verified whether `^^` joins non-literals, so I didn't split it. Gate job-1793136967043 (json tests run on c and rust, the module's default targets); it is in the land list after small-C.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_117 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 117u32, "2026-10-27T22:29Z", "tibia-lane", "22:29Z LIB-SMALL-POSTABLE-F (json_scan.spi): over cells 14 -> 0, no lines over 120.\n- Scanner state machines: the in-string sub-loops of find_top, object_end and item_starts (each had its own in_text flag) now jump over a whole string with closing_quote. That function already did the same walk: on '\"' return, on '\\\\' skip 2, otherwise 1. A shared next_token does the jump, and the depth changes are bracket_depth/brace_depth. find_top takes a top_query record { s; needle; n; m } and checks key_hit before reading the char. item_starts takes an item_scan record { s; n; starts }; the counted helper records the start for '[' at depth 0 and ',' at depth 1, and closes stops at depth 1. Unterminated strings still end at n, so the results are the same.\n- Escapes: digit_of/high_digit; hex_letter; hex_pair/hex2 (hex4 = hex2 * 256 + hex2, with -1 propagation kept); the surrogate helpers (is_high/is_low, low_surrogate_at, surrogate_pair, scalar_text); escaped_char/control_escape. unescape_from now walks escape to escape via backslash_from, with a (text, start) accumulator.\n- parse_i32_array and parse_string_array reuse fill_array, which moved above them.\nType-checks on Rust. Gate GREEN (job-1793139247361): json_scan 2/2 tests in 6 rows (rust, gleam, c), and json 11/11 tests in 22 rows on its consumer path (value_at, object_at, item_count/item_start through json_real.spir). Negative control queued (job-1793139386396): the same slice with ',' splitting items at depth 2 should come out RED.\nLIB-SMALL-POSTABLE-G (step_runtime.spi): over cells 9 -> 0.\n- drain = drain_mailbox (the mailbox join point plus a handler closure) with deliver/settle.\n- call's reply unwrap is replied.\n- counter_actor's handle is counter_step. counter_total takes the started counter, and the Gleam arm moved into otp_counter_total.\n- otp_start = otp_next plus started_inbox.\n- reactor_actor's react_once became top-level react_step. The reactor test drives feed_sink with keep_present, and the stop test uses a stops_on_negative fixture.\notp_start's public signature is unchanged (tibia arena_host uses it). Gate GREEN (job-1793139957841): 3/3 tests, 6 rows (gleam, rust, c; default), 0 red.\nDeferred, with reason: cli_args, whose heavy cells are `~>` real folds with `?:` typecases inside lambdas. Splitting them needs a top-level forall helper called with an explicit type application from typed code (first_error `r). That isn't verified yet, so cli_args waits for a probe. async and guid: mostly host splice text; guid's zig_random_u64 has no test row that runs it, so a split can't be proven. seq: its rev slice waits in the land list, so a postable slice would anchor on a base that's about to move.\nLand list: E, F and G are added after the random heal.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_118 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 118u32, "2026-10-27T22:33Z", "tibia-lane", "22:33Z LIB-SMALL-POSTABLE-F negative control came back RED, as intended (job-1793139386396). The same slice with one mutation (',' splits items at depth 2) failed 8 rows: decode_reads_arrays_of_scalars, decode_reads_arrays_of_records, decode_reads_tuples_as_pairs and decode_reads_tuples_longer_than_two, each on c and rust. All 8 were red again when run alone. So json's rows run the new item_starts/counted path, and F's GREEN means something.\ncli_args probe (local type-check, Rust): moving parse_operands' fold body into a top-level `:= field_error \\* field. (...) : option cli_error = ~> ?: field ?| ...` and `:= first_error \\* r. (args) : option cli_error`, called as `~> first_error `r args`, fails with ETY-UnusedTypeVariable ('The type variable field is unused in the function's type signature'). Typed definitions reject a forall that appears only in the body, so real folds (operands, command_of, parse_operands, parse_command) can't move to typed top-level helpers. The two ways out are a phantom witness parameter, which is noise, or a real companion module, cli_args_real.spir. That is the lib's existing pattern: json_real.spir holds json's real decode and calls typed json_scan helpers. The companion is a package.spiproj module addition, so it is a design step, not a postable slice. cli_args stays at 10 over until then. Its non-real cells (flag_value, flag_values, cli_error_text, the three tests) can be split on their own when that slice is drafted.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_119 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 119u32, "2026-10-27T23:02Z", "tibia-lane", "23:02Z LIB-SMALL-POSTABLE-H (cli_args.spi), over cells 10 -> 4: what remains are the four real folds (operands, command_of, parse_operands, parse_command), which wait for the cli_args_real.spir design (note 118). flag_value and flag_values walk via top-level join points value_after/values_after. cli_error_text's arms call missing_flag_text, bad_number_text, unknown_verb_text and no_verb_text, with listed_verbs shared. The three tests use fixtures: cli_args_test_rename_fields, cli_args_test_codes, cli_args_test_error_text(s), cli_args_test_unknown_text and cli_args_test_expected_errors. Every string literal is unchanged, and the public signatures dir-tree-html uses (parse_operands, cli_error, cli_error_text, has_switch) are untouched. Type-checks on Rust. Negative control: routing BadNumber to unknown_verb_text fails to unify. Gate GREEN (job-1793141378716): 5/5 tests, 10 rows (rust, gleam), 0 red. Also: LIB-LISTM-REV re-gated on the post-nil listm' (0518E91B, main's nil batch 2 landed at 17:39) GREEN (job-1793140712485): 28/28 tests, 45 rows. Land list order (land-after-train.ps1), with all 18 gated bases checked against the live lib at 18:3x: physics-3, threading-channel, seq-rev, B, C, F, D (F first, since F's verdict lists json.spi as a consumer and D edits it), random heal, E, G, listm-rev-postnil, H.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_120 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 120u32, "2026-10-27T23:25Z", "tibia-lane", "23:25Z LIB-SMALL-POSTABLE-I (reactors.spi), over cells 14 -> 2.\n- chain, both and with_deadline: their react lambdas became named top-level functions (chain_react, both_react, deadline_react), and each combinator is a one-line record.\n- modal: a modal_spec record { entry; first; second; leave } plus the aliases modal_state/modal_reactor. The entered helper replaces entered_first/entered_second. in_first/in_second hold each mode's step, and modal_react and modal_of assemble them. The public modal signature is unchanged.\n- run_ticks: one tick step.\n- countdown: countdown_step plus burst_start.\n- The six tests use top-level fixtures: held_one_tick, add_signals/merge_four (with a four_signals alias), tick_stamp/stamped, sampling_tens, violation_tally/tally_step, lag_pattern, bump_member/bank_step/bank_total, every_third/modal_total. Every expected value is unchanged.\nLeft over: run_micro (772), whose loop resisted a split. A microsteps_at/over_ticks version was equivalent but made two over cells out of one, so I reverted it. physical_micros (1001) is one C host text with an #ifdef; it would need self-contained #ifdef/#ifndef globals, and its posix branch can't run on the node.\nType-checks on Rust. Negative control (chain's init pair swapped) fails to unify. Gate GREEN (job-1793143055648): 8/8 tests, 17 rows (c, rust; zig on the clock test), 0 red. The only outside reference is a compiler sample, apps/compiler/tmp/samples/reactors_negative_port_type, which uses only the types. Added to the land list after H.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_121 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 121u32, "2026-10-28T00:16Z", "tibia-lane", "00:16Z DESIGN (draft, nothing written to lib): cli_args postable plus granular split for the 4 remaining real-fold cells (operands 715, command_of 1022, parse_operands 1479, parse_command 733). Constraint from tonight's probe (note 118): a typed forall must appear in its signature (ETY-UnusedTypeVariable), so real folds can't move into typed helpers. Constraint from consumers: dir-tree-html calls cli_args.parse_operands, cli_args.cli_error, cli_args.cli_error_text and cli_args.has_switch, so those names stay in cli_args.\nPlan, modules in package order:\n1. cli_flags.spi (typed, new). flag_value/value_after, has_switch, required_text, required_i32, flag_values/values_after, plus a union cli_field = Text | Number | Switch | Optional | Repeated. These are the existing typed helpers, moved as they are.\n2. cli_args_real.spir (`///- --real`, new; the json_real pattern). Exactly two real functions, each a short typecase:\n   field_kinds `r : list (string * cli_field). One record_type_fold over `r; per field, the symbol name plus a typecase to a cli_field tag.\n   case_names `command : list string. The union_type_to_record + record_type_fold_back of today's parse_command.\n   Plus operands_real `r args, which keeps the record_type_map construction, since building a value of type r is inherently real. Its arms call cli_flags helpers, so it fits one post at about 6 arm lines.\n3. cli_args.spi (typed, existing).\n   operands \\* r. (args) : r = ~> cli_args_real.operands_real `r args. r is in the signature, so it is legal.\n   parse_operands \\* r. (args) : result r cli_error. `:= fields = ~> cli_args_real.field_kinds `r`, then a typed listm.fold of a typed field_error (name, cli_field) over fields; the cli_field match is an ordinary ?= on a union, with no forall.\n   parse_command: `:= valid = ~> cli_args_real.case_names `command`, then today's typed match.\n   command_of keeps one real block, because nominal_create `command (key, operands `payload rest) needs the case symbol and payload type. It shrinks to the fold plus pick_verb (typed, top-level).\n   Forwarders for the public names that moved: has_switch, flag_value and flag_values, as one-line `:= has_switch (name : string) (args : list string) : bool = cli_flags.has_switch name args`.\nProof path: cli_args' 5 tests (rust, gleam), plus a dir-tree-html build as the consumer, plus a negative control (a cli_field tag swapped Text <-> Number in field_kinds must turn errors_name_the_flag_and_the_valid_verbs RED).\nOpen questions to settle by probe before drafting: (a) can a .spir function return a typed list of a union defined in a typed module, or must cli_field live in the .spir? json_real's decode returns typed values, so probably yes. (b) is `~> m.f `r args` accepted inline in a typed `:=` whose signature mentions r? operands already does `~> real_core.record_type_map ... `r`, so very likely yes.\nEstimate by milestones: probe (a)+(b) 15 min, draft 45 min, gate 25 min, dir-tree-html check 15 min; about 1.7 h. Expected result: cli_args at 0 over and its real code down to 3 small functions.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_122 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 122u32, "2026-10-28T00:19Z", "tibia-lane", "00:19Z Design note 121's open questions are both answered by a run (scratch cli-probe/probe.ps1, on a lib copy). (a) A real .spir module (cli_probe_real.spir, ///- --real, listed as cli_probe_real* before cli_args) can return a typed list (string * cli_flags.cli_field), where cli_field is a union declared in a typed module (cli_flags.spi) ahead of it. The real fold builds the list through typed one-liners (text_field, number_field, with_field, no_fields), because real code calls typed functions freely. (b) Typed code consumes it inline with := fields : list (string * cli_flags.cli_field) = ~> cli_probe_real.field_kinds cli_args_test_rename (), and then matches the union with ordinary typed code. It compiled for Rust with the pinned compiler, and rustc ran it: exit 41 = 4 fields, 1 Number, which matches cli_args_test_rename { source; target; times; dry }. Side finding: listm has no filter. The plan in note 121 is now unblocked: about 1.5 h of drafting plus a gate; it's the next POSTABLE piece for cli_args.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_123 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 123u32, "2026-10-28T00:34Z", "tibia-lane", "00:34Z LIB-CLI-SPLIT phase 1 drafted and gating (job-1793147640991, negative control job-1793147719954). The plan from note 121, reduced to what parse_operands/parse_command need:\n- new typed module cli_flags.spi: union cli_field (Text | Number | Switch | Optional | Repeated) plus one-line builders (text_field ... repeated_field, no_fields/with_field, no_verbs/with_verb);\n- new real module cli_args_real.spir (///- --real): field_kind (one 5-arm typecase), field_kinds \r (record_type_fold_back to a typed list (string * cli_field)) and case_names command;\n- package.spiproj gains cli_flags and cli_args_real* right before cli_args.\nIn cli_args: missing_flag, number_error, flag_number_error, number_flag_error, kind_error (a typed ?= on the union, with an or-pattern for the three no-check kinds) and first_field_error (one listm.fold) are all typed. parse_operands = first_field_error args (~> cli_args_real.field_kinds \r ()), and parse_command = command_result args (~> cli_args_real.case_names command ()). Public signatures are unchanged.\ncli_args goes from 4 over to 2 (operands 715 and command_of 1022 are phase 2: operands_real plus the flag helpers moving into cli_flags). No lines over 120. Check: a probe main compiled for Rust and run with rustc gives exit 0 for base and draft alike (missing-flag text, clean parse, bad-number text, and the unknown-verb list through case_names).\nThe apply script refuses if cli_args or package.spiproj has moved or if either new file already exists; tested on a copy. Negative control: string maps to number_field in field_kind, which must make errors_name_the_flag_and_the_valid_verbs report a bad number for --source and go RED. Lands after both verdicts, by import-and-land.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_124 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 124u32, "2026-10-28T00:48Z", "tibia-lane", "00:48Z LIB-CLI-SPLIT-2 drafted and gating (job-1793148521775; negative control job-1793148577959). One combined slice from the live cli_args (post-H) to cli_args, cli_flags and cli_args_real all at 0 over one post, with no lines over 120. Phase 2 on top of note 123: flag_value/value_after, has_switch, required_text, required_i32, flag_values/values_after and pick_verb move to cli_flags. cli_args keeps one-line forwarders, so dir-tree-html's cli_args.parse_operands/cli_error/cli_error_text/has_switch are unchanged. cli_args_real gains field_value (one typecase), named_field, operands_real, case_value, picked and command_of_real; it opens cli_flags and uses plain real parameters. Typed side: operands = ~> cli_args_real.operands_real \r args, and command_of's cons arm = ~> cli_args_real.command_of_real command verb rest. Rule found on the way: real code calling a typed forall must type-apply it (cli_flags.pick_verb command ...), otherwise 'Cannot apply a forall with a term'. Check: a probe main compiled for Rust and run with rustc exits 0 (operands fills cli_args_test_rename as (a, b, 3, true), command codes are (21, 1, -1, -1), and the error texts match the expected pair). LAND RULE: land LIB-CLI-SPLIT-2 only, never LIB-CLI-SPLIT (phase 1). Both create cli_flags.spi and cli_args_real.spir, so landing phase 1 first would make phase 2's new-file guard refuse.\nAlso LANDED: LIB-EXE-HEAL (env current_executable). Gate GREEN 7/7, 25 rows; its negative control was RED on exactly current_executable_names_an_existing_file/rust. LIB-EXT-HEAL re-gates as LIB-EXT-HEAL-2 on file_system's new base (another lane landed it at 19:51).")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_125 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 125u32, "2026-10-28T01:53Z", "tibia-lane", "01:53Z LANDED (live lib, unstaged):\n- LIB-EXT-HEAL-2 (file_system get_extension). Re-gated on the 19:51 base 287CC0FE: GREEN with only file_system's 4 pre-existing reds; the negative was RED on exactly get_extension_keeps_the_dot_and_skips_dotfiles/rust.\n- LIB-CLI-SPLIT-2: cli_args, new cli_flags.spi and cli_args_real.spir, and package.spiproj now lists cli_flags and cli_args_real* before cli_args. Gate GREEN; its one red (a_missing_switch... gleam) is also red in the baseline, which had 4 gleam reds. The negative was RED on exactly errors_name_the_flag_and_the_valid_verbs/rust. Consumer: dir-tree-html (deps/spiral is a symlink to the live spiral) compiles for Rust against the landed lib, through cli_args.parse_operands, cli_error_text and has_switch. cli_args, cli_flags and cli_args_real are all at 0 over one post.\nTotal landed 10-27: 18 lib changes.\nThe tibia src gate on the post-land lib (job-1793146287269) is NOT a regression signal. pack_sim 14, hunt_sim 8, gpu_sim C 1 and creatures 13 not_run all fail with 'SpiralError: could not start the compiler on port 13905: :lock_timeout': the compiler daemon never started on the loaded node, so those tests never ran. terrain, network and hunting_pack were green. The 4 affected modules re-run as job-1793152392179 after the insert-heal negative, when the node is quieter.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_126 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 126u32, "2026-10-28T10:43Z", "tibia-lane", "10:43Z 10-28 06:4x FINDING (draft, nothing landed): lib's run_target dispatch idiom has a postable floor. A per-backend function whose arms are all one-liners still weighs about 286-314: the heading (about 25), a typed signature (70-100), 'run_target \\|', and three arms, the last being the fallback '| _ => \\ () => failwith \"NAME: no arm for this backend\"'. process.spi has 4 such cells (started_pid 286, exit_with 292, capture_status_within 309 after splitting, captured_by 314), and more lib files share the shape.\nSplitting arms into rust_*/gleam_* helpers fixes the heavy cells. The scratch lib-proc draft takes capture_status_within 1207 -> 309, type-checks on Rust, and its generated capture path is byte-identical in size to the control (2,465 bytes). But it can't reach 273.\nOptions for a design decision, not taken:\n(a) a lib no_arm helper plus a shorter fallback idiom: saves about 30, still not enough on its own.\n(b) no '/// ###' heading on internal helpers (my rule since step_runtime), with public dispatchers keeping theirs.\n(c) a run_target variant whose missing arms default to the no-arm failure, which removes the fallback line. This is a lib or compiler change and needs an owner decision, because it changes what a missing arm means.\nUntil one is chosen, run_target-heavy files (process, env, console, resultm, guid) stay partially over. The scratch draft keeps its helpers (find_executable, received_event, left_until, event_within, logged_data, exit_status_of, kill_program/kill_arguments, rust_kill_tree, rust_killed/quiet_child/rust_status_within, gleam_killed/gleam_status_within, no_arm) for when one is.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_127 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 127u32, "2026-10-28T11:44Z", "tibia-lane", "11:44Z CORRECTION 10-28 07:5x: my postable census (scratch postable-census.ps1, budget 273, CJK x2) is NOT the authority, and it is optimistic. The poster's rule, via SpiralCompiler --x-compat with SPIRAL_POSTABILITY_PROFILE=x/postability/x.json, is 280 minus the 'cN k/n' header, minus +1 for each defused dot (the ZWSP the poster puts before every dot X would autolink, e.g. sm'.length). On today's lib:\n- oauth 0 findings, step_runtime 0: my claims hold.\n- json_scan: 1 overweight, line 177 (273 > 269), which my census passed. cli_flags: 1 overweight, line 69 (273 > 271). Both are dot and header misses of mine, a few characters each.\n- reactors: 2 overweight (run_micro 772, physical_micros 1001), as I reported, plus 4 'hashtag' findings my census never checked (the #include text in physical_micros' host string).\n- cli_args: 5 overweight. Not from my split: another lane changed cli_args, cli_flags and cli_args_real at 06:28 today, adding usage_part/fields_usage/Help and --help handling, and those new cells are the overweight ones (usage_part 295, parse_operands 364, command_result 405, cli_error_text 297).\nFrom now on POSTABLE claims use --x-compat with the x profile, not my census. Next: re-check every file I reported 0-over (physics, threading, the small B-I batch) with --x-compat, and fix my two near-misses (json_scan:177, cli_flags:69) in today's land windows.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_128 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 128u32, "2026-10-28T11:46Z", "tibia-lane", "07:5x tibia RE-MEASURED with the authority (--x-compat, x profile): 56 findings in src+apps, not 0. 39 are overweight by a few characters (hunting_pack 12, competitor 4, pack_sim 3, observer 3, terrain 2, map 2, parity 2, and 1 each in 11 other files; mostly weight 270-273 against limits of 260-272, since high cell numbers lengthen the cN k/n header) and 17 are @-mentions (competitor 7, observer 3, lead_trace 3, ground_trace 2, client 2). README's 'Postable' line claimed 0 from my dot-blind census and is now corrected to these numbers (unstaged). Full list: scratch ed3-toml/xcompat-tibia-0750.txt. The trim pass joins TIBIA-EDITION3-ROUND (whose scope already includes the 280-weight split), after TIBIA-PARK.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_129 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 129u32, "2026-10-28T11:59Z", "tibia-lane", "08:0x LIB-XCOMPAT-TRIM fixes my two lib near-misses from note 127: json_scan item_starts (count -> k) and cli_flags values_after (found -> acc). Both pass --x-compat with 0 findings. Proof is stronger than a test run: Rust probes calling item_count and flag_values generate BYTE-IDENTICAL code before and after (6,329 and 4,413 bytes). Slice scratch lib-xtrim/lib-xcompat-trim-apply.ps1 (2 files, base-guarded). Gate job-1793188878153 runs json_scan and cli_args rows (cli_flags has no tests of its own, so its consumer cli_args stands in). Lands in today's 14:00-16:00 window.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_130 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 130u32, "2026-10-28T12:00Z", "tibia-lane", "08:0x x-compat recheck of my 10-27 postable lands (B, C, D and physics-3, all landed 19:5x): 8 cells still fail the authority. physics:535 (266 > 261, the d_particle_state +++ instance); otp_supervisor:25 new (329 > 267) and :58 with_restart (353 > 266); otp_actor:22 new_with_init (270 > 269) and :28 new_with_self (324 > 268); cursor_walk:24 walked_from (561 > 261); json:17 decode_reads_a_flat_record (271 > 269) and :111 tool_schema_describes... (1064, the known ^^-chain cell left open in note 116). The large ones are multi-arm host-text matches (one macro per arm, e.g. static_supervisor.new(...OneForOne/OneForAll/RestForOne)), which my census measured lower than the poster does. Plan: each arm becomes a named one-macro helper and the match calls them; renames for the small ones. Drafted after K4, landing in the 14:00-16:00 window with LIB-XCOMPAT-TRIM.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_131 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 131u32, "2026-10-28T12:03Z", "tibia-lane", "08:1x correction to note 130. My census, in -Show mode, also flags the large ones (otp_supervisor new 329 and with_restart 353, otp_actor new_with_self 324, cursor_walk walked_from 561, json tool_schema 1064), so they are not census misses. They are the known leftovers of batches B/C/D (15 -> 3, 18 -> 7, 9 -> 1), and I never claimed those files 0-over. The real census misses are the three near-limit cells that x-compat cuts below 273 by header and dots: physics:535 (266 > 261), otp_actor:22 (270 > 269), json:17 (271 > 269). Fix plan for the leftovers, in the granular style (one host call per macro): otp_supervisor gets strategy_term/restart_term arms that return the bare constructor, and new/with_restart call one $'...(!term)' each; walked_from and new_with_self move into named fixtures; the near-limit ones get renames. These are drafted as LIB-XCOMPAT-LEFTOVERS for the 14:00-16:00 window. json tool_schema stays open until I verify whether ^^ joins non-literals.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_132 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 132u32, "2026-10-28T12:16Z", "tibia-lane", "08:1x LIB-XCOMPAT-LEFTOVERS drafted (scratch lib-leftovers/draft.ps1, anchored on the bases in bases.txt). x-compat with the x profile reports 0 findings for otp_supervisor, otp_actor, cursor_walk and physics. json is down to tool_schema (1064) alone.\n- otp_supervisor: strategy_of/restart_of return the bare Gleam constructors (new nominals strategy_term = static_supervisor.Strategy and restart_term = supervision.Restart), and new/with_restart each make one host call with !term. with_restart had no row, so the test adds a restarting_worker fixture (counting_worker () |> with_restart Transient); the assert is unchanged.\n- otp_actor: new_with_self is on one line (ms, f, fn(me)); new_with_init = new_with_self ms (\\ _ => init ()), moved after it. step_runtime and x's runner_shell/memory_watch call both positionally, so their signatures are unchanged.\n- cursor_walk: walked_from split into a cursor_next union (CursorEnd result / CursorOn page) plus cursor_halted, cursor_repeated, cursor_finished, cursor_paged, cursor_fetched and cursor_next_of. walked_from only dispatches and recurses, and the fetch order is unchanged.\n- physics: the +++ d_particle_state instance drops b's annotation; the prototype fixes it.\n- json: decode_reads_a_flat_record names the decoded query q.\nGate pairs on the node, each a positive plus a negative that mutates the path: SUPERVISOR (job-1793189473631, neg Transient -> Transientx), ACTOR (job-1793189702033, neg returning(mex)), then CURSOR (neg repeated pages +2), JSON (neg days - bonus) and PHYSICS (neg dmdt minus) queued after. They land in the 14:00-16:00 window if green and their negatives red on an assertion or the mutated name.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_133 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 133u32, "2026-10-28T12:21Z", "tibia-lane", "08:2x DECISION (from X-DEFUSE-LINKS-ONLY and SPIRAL-ZWSP-TRANSPARENT, which already decide it): mention, hashtag and cashtag findings from --x-compat in string literals, host macros and doc lines are NOT source edits. The poster defuses those sites, including a mention inside a string; the x team's splitter tests cover it. Postable acceptance for my items is therefore 0 overweight findings under the x profile, with mention/hashtag findings left to the poster. Applied:\n- scratch ed3-trim/trim.ps1: the 17 tibia-apps unmention edits (the \"@\" ++# \"t+\" style) are now opt-in (-Unmention) and off by default. TIBIA-EDITION3-ROUND keeps the tibia strings as written.\n- 10-27 lib recheck: util, csv, channel, erlang_port, oauth and step_runtime have 0 findings. erlang_term's 3 findings are @external in Gleam globals (poster work). platform's 2 overweight cells (588 and 677: the host-text C #if blocks and the Gleam prelude) predate my one helper and are GRANULAR-HEAL scope. seq, listm', env, file_system and threading were heal slices with no postable claim; their overweight counts are 14, 21, 19, 73 and 10.\nIf x-compat should stop reporting what the poster defuses, that is an x-compat question for the x team (X-DEFUSE-LINKS-ONLY, 90%). Until then, read the overweight count, not the total.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_134 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 134u32, "2026-10-28T12:25Z", "tibia-lane", "08:2x The pending lib heal drafts added overweight cells under the x profile. I fixed them before the land window so they don't land new overweight cells (each a scratch postable.ps1 run after its draft.ps1; the gated versions are kept as *-gated.spi):\n- ENV-RESTORE-HEAL: 4 new over (entry_pair 326, removed_extras 315, set_changed 329, the restore test 525). Now: an env_entries alias (array_base string), equals_after_first split out of entry_pair, per-entry steps remove_if_extra/set_if_changed so the join points only walk, and test fixtures changed_after_snapshot/snapshot_then_changed/restore_test_values (asserted values unchanged). env is back to its base 19 overweight.\n- FS-HEAL: 2 new over (mklink_status 374, rust_permissions_set 282). Now: mklink_command builds the shared command; the permissions local is renamed mode. file_system is back to 73.\n- SPLIT-HEAL: split_portable 968 (code lifted from the C arm). Now: top-level join points split_matches_from/split_next_from/split_count/split_start_of plus split_part; split_portable is 4 lines. sm' is back to 80.\nNo new line over 120 in any of the three. Each re-gates on the node as a positive plus a negative that mutates the path (env: <> Some value to = Some value; fs: mklink /J to /X; split: next_from step +1 to +2). The earlier gates still prove the healed behaviour; only the postable versions land in 14:00-16:00.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_135 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 135u32, "2026-10-28T12:28Z", "tibia-lane", "08:29 More lib postable/granular drafts gated on the node (pushes serial, bizn9mo9q):\n- LIB-XCOMPAT-JSON-2 supersedes LIB-XCOMPAT-JSON. tool_schema's expected text becomes 5 fixture strings (forecast_schema_head/city/point/days_units/hours), each a ^^ literal chain cut at commas, joined at runtime by forecast_schema () with ++#; the args record moves into forecast_here (). The draft script fails unless the pieces rejoin to the original text exactly. json goes to 0 findings. Negative: Imperial to Imperialx in the expected text (assert path).\n- LIB-ENV-RESTORE-HEAL-P, LIB-FS-HEAL-P and LIB-SPLIT-HEAL-P are the postable versions from note 134.\n- LIB-PLATFORM-GRANULAR: is_windows (677) becomes one named one-call function per backend: rust_is_windows, fsharp_is_windows, gleam_is_windows (with gleam_os_type_external holding the two Gleam globals), zig_is_windows and no_is_windows_arm. The run_target arms pass them directly. platform keeps 1 overweight cell. get_executable_suffix has no assert, so the proof is file_system's junction rows (the gate lists file_system as a consumer), and the negative flips cfg!(windows) to cfg!(unix). LAND ORDER: PLATFORM-GRANULAR lists file_system as a consumer, so it lands before FS-HEAL-P.\nLeft as DESIGN, not drafted:\n(a) platform.run_platform (588) is the F#-only #if glue with no gate rows, so an edit can't be proven.\n(b) reactors.physical_micros (1001) is one C global program (#ifdef _WIN32 ... #else ... #endif). Splitting it into several global calls risks global dedup dropping a repeated \"#else\"/\"#endif\" line. It needs a C shim mechanism (a header-style global per function) or a lib date_time C primitive.\n(c) reactors.run_micro (772) is a typed real fold with an inner loop. Lifting it needs a micro_run record plus a micro_at loop state and a step function, because typed foralls must appear in signatures (note 118).")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_136 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 136u32, "2026-10-28T13:03Z", "tibia-lane", "09:0x Near-limit trims in two more of my own heal files (x-compat, x profile), drafted and queued behind LIB-PORT-HIDE in the serial node chain:\n- LIB-SEQ-TRIM (scratch lib-seqtrim): take_while_4/5 collapse `\\ n => list |> stream.try_item n` onto one line; memoize_ names its fresh value `fresh`; the memoize test's seq lambda goes on one line. Overweight 14 -> 10; the 4 near-limit cells (272-280 against 269-272) pass. Negative: the memoize test's `n < 5` becomes `n < 6` (sum 10 -> 15, assert path).\n- LIB-LISTM-TRIM (scratch lib-listmtrim/draft.ps1): map4 ends with `listm.rev acc`; try_index_of's match arm becomes one line; the exists test drops a duplicated `///> cuda` target line (it listed cuda twice) and puts the empty-list case on one line; chunks_split_into_runs_of_at_most_n is split into chunks and skip_drops_the_first_n_or_everything over a chunk_sizes fixture (asserted values unchanged); take_stops_at_the_count_or_the_end uses a taken_length fixture. Overweight 21 -> 16; the 5 near-limit cells pass. The CJK loop name 루프 weighs 4, the same as `loop`, so renaming it gains nothing.\n- threading: NOT drafted. Its near-limit cells (sleep 283, cancellation_source_cancel 294, partial_eq_wrapper_arc_eq 290) have cheap granular fixes (rust_sleep_ms and rust_cancel one-call primitives, a one-line reflow), but threading's only tests are `///> fsharp`, so no gate row runs the Rust arms. It waits for Rust rows, by the rule that a lib edit needs a row running its arm.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_137 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 137u32, "2026-10-28T13:51Z", "tibia-lane", "09:5x Gate state of my lib slices, plus what the local type-check caught.\nVOID (not verdicts): the 08:1x node gates I fanned out (about 9 at once beside other teams' jobs) hit \"Spiral cell timed out after 300s\" on every Gleam/Rust cell. Affected: XCOMPAT-SUPERVISOR, ACTOR, CURSOR. I stopped 8 superseded or void jobs and re-queued everything as serial --after chains, with at most about 2 of mine at once.\nGREEN: LIB-XCOMPAT-PHYSICS 43/43 (job-1793190365101, bases physics=ABB3C48CC789927A); its negative is pending.\nREAL RED, fixed: LIB-SPLIT-HEAL-P had ETY-ValueRestriction in split_portable. `? sm.length separator = 0` compares a result-generic sm.length with an untyped literal; the original's `:= m : i32 = ...` pinned it and my reflow dropped the pin. Fixed to `(sm.length separator : i32) = 0i32`; re-gating as LIB-SPLIT-HEAL-P2.\nCAUGHT BEFORE ANY GATE by a local type-check of every pending draft (scratch node-kit/typecheck-drafts.ps1 compiles each draft as the entry against a fresh lib copy; typecheck-nomain.ps1 appends `:= main () = ()` for modules without a main):\n- LIB-XCOMPAT-JSON-2 used ++# in json.spi, which doesn't open sm'_operators (ETY-UnboundVariable). Fixed with sm'.concat_list \"\" over the five pieces; the queued JSON-2 jobs are stopped and it re-gates as LIB-XCOMPAT-JSON-3.\n- LIB-LISTM-TRIM: my one-line `[] |> exists' \\ x => x = 'a' |> _assert_eq !-` captured the assert into the lambda. I found it on review and fixed it with parentheses before its push; the pushed slice is verified to contain the fix.\nAll 15 drafts now type-check: sm', seq, listm', threading, reactors, cursor_walk, json, physics, env, file_system and platform on Rust; otp_actor and otp_supervisor on Gleam; process on Rust and Gleam.\nProcess lesson: the local entry-compile (seconds) comes before every node gate (about 50 min). Memory already said so, and skipping it cost one gate cycle.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_138 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 138u32, "2026-10-30T16:50Z", "tibia-lane", "10-30 12:49 LANDED (tibia's lib x-compat slices, re-gated GREEN today): reactors B03883948415 (REACTORS-MICRO), otp_actor EE3DCBE1CDCE (ACTOR-2, Gleam-only reshape), cursor_walk E1114F9B294F (CURSOR-2), json 0ABDD652D9CC (JSON-3), physics 816983D043E9 (PHYSICS), otp_supervisor C70AA8F81970 (SUPERVISOR-2), json_scan 2947FB42156D and cli_flags 7F864AE313D0 (TRIM-2, cli_flags proven through its consumer cli_args 6/6). The tibia repo itself is now at 0 overweight under the x profile (TIBIA-EDITION3-ROUND note 15).")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_139 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 139u32, "2026-10-30T19:39Z", "tibia-lane", "10-30 15:4x POST-LAND NODE GATE GREEN (job-1793379899822, pack 8, mirror of the closed lib, digest BBB2B45D37BBC121). All 16 tibia lib lands plus cli_args (cli_flags' consumer) hold. Only these had new reds: sm' (8, load), listm' (1), cli_args (4 gleam); every one passes when rerun alone. file_system's 3 reds equal the gated ones. Compiler's box quiet rerun also cleared the box sm' reds as load.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_140 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 140u32, "2026-10-30T22:46Z", "tibia-lane", "10-30 18:4x CROSS-REPO CENSUS after the wipe redo and today's lib window (box, --x-compat with the x profile, tracked .spi only, script scratch land/census-all.ps1). Overweight cells by repo: tibia 0 (65 files; 17 mentions left to the poster), x 0 (67 files), spiral lib 1,217 in 89 of 114 files (top: xsd_emit 162, sm' 90, file_system 84, date_time 66, xpath_eval 62; 248 mentions), kit 2,692 in 632 of 800 files (top: agile_todo_model/model 41, kit_do_program/program 26, toolchain_runtime_policy_domain/receipt 23, kit_lint_model/model 23, agile_task_listing/task_listing 23), fiscal 85 in 4 (generator 48, corpus_checks 33), dice 51 in 14 (ui near/rpc 18, lib/dice 11), polyglot 54 in 5 (math 19, perf 14, chat_contract 10), alphabet 9 in 2. Burn-down is per owning team: lib files to the lane whose file it is (xsd_emit/xpath_eval fiscal, sm'/file_system/date_time shared), kit to kit, fiscal to fiscal, dice/alphabet/polyglot to main. Method lessons for trimmers: rename locals and params, keep calls fully applied (no eta-reduction, memory spiral-eta-reduction-makes-closures), and prove compiled-output identity per change.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_141 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 141u32, "2026-10-31T00:11Z", "x", "2026-10-30 20:1x x: shape of the shared sm' burn-down, for whoever takes it (read-only scan with C:\\home\\data\\x-drafts\\tools\\cell-weights.ps1, ASCII weight > 272, live working tree, which carries another lane's unstaged sm' diff): 73 cells over. The largest are multi-backend backend_switch bodies, not prose: format_fixed_digits 3,042, obj_to_string_rust 2,705, index_of 1,490, concat_list 1,119, substring 837, format_fixed 776, starts_with 774, clip 745, ends_with 731, last_index_of 727. Trimming them is the granular split (one named one-call function per backend arm, e.g. tibia's is_windows -> rust_is_windows/gleam_is_windows/...), and each needs a per-backend compiled-output identity proof. That is gated work for a lib window, not a light item. x's repos stay at 0 (tibia's 18:4x census: x 0 in 67 files), so x takes no sm' slice tonight. Calls must stay fully applied (no eta-reduction; memory spiral-eta-reduction-makes-closures).")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_142 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 142u32, "2026-10-31T03:20Z", "tibia-lane", "10-30 23:20 tibia: (1) M2 tool committed: note 140's scratch census-all.ps1 is now i574n.github/scripts/x-compat-census.ps1 (-Repos to filter, BelowNormal, tracked .spi only). (2) M4 for tibia: tibia/scripts/build.ps1 now gates on 0 cells over one post. It runs --x-compat over src and apps with a committed copy of x's profile in tibia/postability/ (the compiler finds postability/*.json in an ancestor directory, and tibia's CI has no x checkout), and fails if that copy drifts from a sibling ../x checkout. The 17 '@knight+N' trace-log mentions are printed, not failed: analysis scripts parse that format. Proof through the real entry (build.ps1 -Files no-such-file) is running at BelowNormal on a starved box. (3) Tibia's share of the lib burn-down, measured on the 16 lib files tibia landed into on 10-30 (--x-compat, x profile): 278 cells over. sm' 90, file_system 85, process 39, env 26, listm' 18, seq 10, threading 8, otp_actor 1, platform 1. Already 0: reactors, cursor_walk, json, physics, otp_supervisor, json_scan, cli_flags. The two singles: otp_actor.spi:144, the test a_named_actor_answers_through_its_name (380 > 269), and platform.spi:24, run_platform's F# #if macro block (589 > 272). threading: lines 19, 32, 101, 249, 266, 283, 344, 359. seq: 54, 340, 353, 364, 391, 417, 456, 482, 497, 517. All are lib-window work (lib landing plus per-backend identity proof), not light; the next tibia lib window takes otp_actor, platform, threading and seq first (20 cells).")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_143 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 143u32, "2026-10-31T07:08Z", "tibia-lane", "10-31 03:1x tibia M4 PROOF closed (note 142 left it running). Positive, through the real entry (build.ps1 -Files no-such-file, Normal priority after the BelowNormal stall): \"postable / every cell fits one post / 17 mentions left to the poster\". Negative, same compiler call and the same match as build.ps1 lines 33-37, on a scratch tree laid out like tibia (postability/ copy beside src/ and apps/): one 569-weight cell plus one @knight mention gives \"overweight 1 / mentions 1\", so the gate throws. Note for anyone copying this gate into another repo: --x-compat exits 0 when it reports findings, so the exit-code check only catches crashes. The line match on ': overweight' is what fails the build. x-compat-census.ps1 no longer lowers its priority (BelowNormal starves on the saturated box). Tibia's remaining share is the lib burn-down from note 142 (sm' 90, file_system 85, process 39, env 26, listm' 18, seq 10, threading 8, otp_actor 1, platform 1). That is gated lib-window work, not a light item.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_144 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 144u32, "2026-10-31T09:50Z", "tibia-lane", "10-31 05:5x tibia: LIB-XCOMPAT-OTP-NAMED, tibia's otp_actor share (1 cell), is PROVEN and waits for the next lib window. The overweight cell was the test a_named_actor_answers_through_its_name (weight 380 > 269). It is lifted into named_counter_address () and named_counter (), matching the self_actor/array_actor helper-plus-test pattern already in the file. otp_actor.spi base EE3DCBE1CDCE99DF (= live, unchanged since ACTOR-2 landed 10-30) -> target 3E60143367D5D489. x-compat on the result: 0 findings (was 1). Formatter --check clean.\nGate: box, kit test-suite window, NEW i574n.github/scripts/lib-program-gate.ps1. It copies lib, applies a kit do program, runs apps/cells/spi/lib_gate.ps1 on the copy, then applies a negative program and requires a test FAILURE with 0 type errors. Positive: a_named_actor_answers_through_its_name/gleam passed 1, failed 0, 0 type errors. Negative (new 0i32 -> new 1i32 inside the new named_counter helper, so the read returns 6): failed 1, 0 type errors, CAUGHT through the assert path. Use the gate for the rest of the lib burn-down (-Program, -Negative, -File, -Test).\nLAND: write the program below to a file, then: land-lib-program.ps1 -Program <file> -File otp_actor.spi -Base EE3DCBE1CDCE99DF -Target 3E60143367D5D489. land-lib-program now keeps undo copies in %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\undo\\<program>, no longer C:\\home\\data\\undo (the folder the user never asked for). The 32 existing undo folders there stay until drained.\nPROGRAM (kit do <lib> -):\nedit otp_actor.spi expect files 1 replacements 1\n  replace once <<<\n///> gleam\ntest a_named_actor_answers_through_its_name =\n    := address : erlang_process.name counter_message = erlang_process.new_name \"lib_otp_actor_named_test\"\n    new 0i32 |> named address |> on_message counter_step |> start |> started_data |> ignore\n    := inbox = erlang_process.named_subject address\n    send inbox (Add 5i32)\n    call inbox 10000i32 Read |> _assert_eq 5i32\n  >>> with <<<\n/// ### named_counter_address\n:= named_counter_address () : erlang_process.name counter_message =\n    erlang_process.new_name \"lib_otp_actor_named_test\"\n\n/// ### named_counter\n:= named_counter () =\n    := address = named_counter_address ()\n    new 0i32 |> named address |> on_message counter_step |> start |> started_data |> ignore\n    erlang_process.named_subject address\n\n///> gleam\ntest a_named_actor_answers_through_its_name =\n    := inbox = named_counter ()\n    send inbox (Add 5i32)\n    call inbox 10000i32 Read |> _assert_eq 5i32\n  >>>")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_145 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 145u32, "2026-10-31T09:55Z", "tibia-lane", "10-31 06:0x tibia: note 144's otp_actor draft (target 3E60143367D5D489) also passes the document round trip: slice-roundtrip-sweep.ps1 -Files <draft> gives \"otp_actor.spi ok\", and the control (base near.spi) passes. otp_actor has no _real.spir, so the heading-mirror rule does not apply. Landing checklist complete: base check, round trip, gate positive and negative, x-compat 0, format clean.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_146 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 146u32, "2026-10-31T11:55Z", "tibia-lane", "10-31 07:5x tibia: LIB-XCOMPAT-OTP-NAMED v2 PROVEN. This SUPERSEDES n144's program, which added two '/// ### name' headings that main's LIB-NOTEBOOK-HEADINGS-CLEANUP removes lib-wide; v2's fixtures are bare := lines, like listm'.spi's own test fixtures. otp_actor.spi base EE3DCBE1CDCE99DF (live, unchanged) -> target 3637A05C839AA087. x-compat 0 findings, formatter clean, round trip ok. Gate (box test-suite window, lib-program-gate.ps1): positive a_named_actor_answers_through_its_name/gleam passed, 0 type errors; negative (new 0i32 -> new 1i32 in named_counter) failed, 0 type errors: CAUGHT.\nLAND: land-lib-program.ps1 -Note POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS/<this note> -File otp_actor.spi -Base EE3DCBE1CDCE99DF -Target 3637A05C839AA087\nPROGRAM (kit do <lib> -):\nedit otp_actor.spi expect files 1 replacements 1\n  replace once <<<\n///> gleam\ntest a_named_actor_answers_through_its_name =\n    := address : erlang_process.name counter_message = erlang_process.new_name \"lib_otp_actor_named_test\"\n    new 0i32 |> named address |> on_message counter_step |> start |> started_data |> ignore\n    := inbox = erlang_process.named_subject address\n    send inbox (Add 5i32)\n    call inbox 10000i32 Read |> _assert_eq 5i32\n  >>> with <<<\n:= named_counter_address () : erlang_process.name counter_message =\n    erlang_process.new_name \"lib_otp_actor_named_test\"\n\n:= named_counter () =\n    := address = named_counter_address ()\n    new 0i32 |> named address |> on_message counter_step |> start |> started_data |> ignore\n    erlang_process.named_subject address\n\n///> gleam\ntest a_named_actor_answers_through_its_name =\n    := inbox = named_counter ()\n    send inbox (Add 5i32)\n    call inbox 10000i32 Read |> _assert_eq 5i32\n  >>>")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_147 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 147u32, "2026-10-31T11:55Z", "tibia-lane", "10-31 07:5x tibia: LIB-XCOMPAT-LISTM-TESTS PROVEN (listm' 18 -> 16 overweight cells). cons_leaves_the_shared_tail_unchanged's body becomes shared_tail_folds (). The 4-operation tuple test list_operations_keep_their_order becomes one test per operation over an unordered_items () fixture: rev_reverses_the_order, filter_keeps_the_order, map_keeps_the_order, distinct_keeps_first_occurrences_in_order. No headings added. listm'.spi base 007CEAD42CAD6D93 (live after main's headings batch 1) -> target 3208D714F91657C7. Formatter clean, round trip ok.\nGates (box test-suite windows, lib-program-gate.ps1). Run 1 (06:0x, previous base 23A5559D, which differs only in removed heading lines, none inside the anchors): 4 of the 5 tests passed on every backend. The cons test failed only its zig row, under load. Run 2 (07:4x, the new base, cons test alone): python/lua/rust/typescript/zig all passed, so the zig red was load (rule: passes alone). The control run on the unchanged base also passed zig. Negative (cons' 9i32 -> 8i32, and the fixture list ending 5i32 instead of 4i32): run 1 failed all 5 tests, run 2 failed the cons test, all with 0 type errors: CAUGHT.\nLAND: land-lib-program.ps1 -Note POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS/<this note> -File \"listm'.spi\" -Base 007CEAD42CAD6D93 -Target 3208D714F91657C7 (main's wave 2 may change the base first; then re-anchor, since the program is anchor-based).\nPROGRAM (kit do <lib> -):\nedit listm'.spi expect files 1 replacements 2\n  replace once <<<\n///> python\n///> lua\n///> rust\n///> typescript\n///> zig\ntest cons_leaves_the_shared_tail_unchanged =\n    := tail = box [ 2i32 ]\n    := first = cons' 1i32 tail\n    := second = cons' 9i32 tail\n    (fold_back' id first [], fold_back' id second [], fold_back' id tail [])\n    |> _assert_eq ([ 1i32; 2i32 ], [ 9i32; 2i32 ], [ 2i32 ])\n  >>> with <<<\n:= shared_tail_folds () =\n    := tail = box [ 2i32 ]\n    := first = cons' 1i32 tail\n    := second = cons' 9i32 tail\n    (fold_back' id first [], fold_back' id second [], fold_back' id tail [])\n\n///> python\n///> lua\n///> rust\n///> typescript\n///> zig\ntest cons_leaves_the_shared_tail_unchanged =\n    shared_tail_folds () |> _assert_eq ([ 1i32; 2i32 ], [ 9i32; 2i32 ], [ 2i32 ])\n  >>>\n  replace once <<<\n///> lua\n///> python\n///> rust\n///> typescript\ntest list_operations_keep_their_order =\n    := items = box [ 3i32; 1i32; 3i32; 2i32; 1i32; 4i32 ]\n    (unbox (rev' items), unbox (filter' (\\ x => x > 1i32) items), unbox (map (\\ x => x * 2i32) items), unbox (distinct' items))\n    |> _assert_eq ([ 4i32; 1i32; 2i32; 3i32; 1i32; 3i32 ], [ 3i32; 3i32; 2i32; 4i32 ], [ 6i32; 2i32; 6i32; 4i32; 2i32; 8i32 ], [ 3i32; 1i32; 2i32; 4i32 ])\n  >>> with <<<\n:= unordered_items () : list' i32 = box [ 3i32; 1i32; 3i32; 2i32; 1i32; 4i32 ]\n\n///> lua\n///> python\n///> rust\n///> typescript\ntest rev_reverses_the_order =\n    unordered_items () |> rev' |> unbox |> _assert_eq [ 4i32; 1i32; 2i32; 3i32; 1i32; 3i32 ]\n\n///> lua\n///> python\n///> rust\n///> typescript\ntest filter_keeps_the_order =\n    unordered_items () |> filter' (\\ x => x > 1i32) |> unbox |> _assert_eq [ 3i32; 3i32; 2i32; 4i32 ]\n\n///> lua\n///> python\n///> rust\n///> typescript\ntest map_keeps_the_order =\n    unordered_items () |> map (\\ x => x * 2i32) |> unbox |> _assert_eq [ 6i32; 2i32; 6i32; 4i32; 2i32; 8i32 ]\n\n///> lua\n///> python\n///> rust\n///> typescript\ntest distinct_keeps_first_occurrences_in_order =\n    unordered_items () |> distinct' |> unbox |> _assert_eq [ 3i32; 1i32; 2i32; 4i32 ]\n  >>>")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_148 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 148u32, "2026-10-31T15:16Z", "tibia-lane", "10-31 11:2x tibia, a measured x-compat rule useful for every lane's burn-down: a TLD-shaped dot in code (Spiral qualified names such as System.IO, am'.to_vec, sm'.substring, i.e. word.io / .to / .su) does not add weight; it LOWERS the cell's budget by 1, because the poster must defuse it (\"280 minus the cN k/n header and defused dots\"). Probe: two cells of exactly 263 characters, one with 18 such dots and one with the same names written with underscores. Only the dotted cell is flagged: weight 262 > budget 255. So a near-limit cell can fit by losing a few TLD-shaped qualifications (open the module, or bind a local), not only by splitting.\nTwo more for owners. (a) print_code tests are weighed above their source: sm'.spi \"test slice print_code\" is 93 characters but weighs 280, presumably because the post carries the printed code. Reshaping the source cannot fix those; tibia leaves sm's 3 print_code cells alone. (b) Live sm'.spi and file_system.spi already fail formatter --check (sm': clip's Rust macro string plus one more; file_system: 2 lines over 120 at 755 and 1824). Not from tibia's slices.\ntibia's share after today's 6 slices, measured on the drafts: 274 -> 245 before file_system/sm' tests (fs 82 -> 79, sm' 90 -> 89 drafted and gating).")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_149 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 149u32, "2026-10-31T15:37Z", "tibia-lane", "10-31 11:3x tibia: LIB-XCOMPAT-LISTM-TESTS RE-PROVEN on the new base. This SUPERSEDES n147: main's wave 2 changed listm'.spi at 10:33 to 2102B170ACD86ED3. The program is unchanged and still applies: -> 740AC704BE6E1BD4, overweight 18 -> 16. Formatter clean, round trip ok.\nGate (box test-suite window, lib-program-gate.ps1 -Control): the control on the unchanged base ran cons_leaves_the_shared_tail_unchanged (the only name that exists there) and was GREEN on all rows. The candidate passed all 5 tests on every row (python/lua/rust/typescript/zig), 0 type errors. Negative: all 5 failed, 0 type errors: CAUGHT.\nLAND: land-lib-program.ps1 -Note POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS/<this note> -File \"listm'.spi\" -Base 2102B170ACD86ED3 -Target 740AC704BE6E1BD4\nPROGRAM (kit do <lib> -):\nedit listm'.spi expect files 1 replacements 2\n  replace once <<<\n///> python\n///> lua\n///> rust\n///> typescript\n///> zig\ntest cons_leaves_the_shared_tail_unchanged =\n    := tail = box [ 2i32 ]\n    := first = cons' 1i32 tail\n    := second = cons' 9i32 tail\n    (fold_back' id first [], fold_back' id second [], fold_back' id tail [])\n    |> _assert_eq ([ 1i32; 2i32 ], [ 9i32; 2i32 ], [ 2i32 ])\n  >>> with <<<\n:= shared_tail_folds () =\n    := tail = box [ 2i32 ]\n    := first = cons' 1i32 tail\n    := second = cons' 9i32 tail\n    (fold_back' id first [], fold_back' id second [], fold_back' id tail [])\n\n///> python\n///> lua\n///> rust\n///> typescript\n///> zig\ntest cons_leaves_the_shared_tail_unchanged =\n    shared_tail_folds () |> _assert_eq ([ 1i32; 2i32 ], [ 9i32; 2i32 ], [ 2i32 ])\n  >>>\n  replace once <<<\n///> lua\n///> python\n///> rust\n///> typescript\ntest list_operations_keep_their_order =\n    := items = box [ 3i32; 1i32; 3i32; 2i32; 1i32; 4i32 ]\n    (unbox (rev' items), unbox (filter' (\\ x => x > 1i32) items), unbox (map (\\ x => x * 2i32) items), unbox (distinct' items))\n    |> _assert_eq ([ 4i32; 1i32; 2i32; 3i32; 1i32; 3i32 ], [ 3i32; 3i32; 2i32; 4i32 ], [ 6i32; 2i32; 6i32; 4i32; 2i32; 8i32 ], [ 3i32; 1i32; 2i32; 4i32 ])\n  >>> with <<<\n:= unordered_items () : list' i32 = box [ 3i32; 1i32; 3i32; 2i32; 1i32; 4i32 ]\n\n///> lua\n///> python\n///> rust\n///> typescript\ntest rev_reverses_the_order =\n    unordered_items () |> rev' |> unbox |> _assert_eq [ 4i32; 1i32; 2i32; 3i32; 1i32; 3i32 ]\n\n///> lua\n///> python\n///> rust\n///> typescript\ntest filter_keeps_the_order =\n    unordered_items () |> filter' (\\ x => x > 1i32) |> unbox |> _assert_eq [ 3i32; 3i32; 2i32; 4i32 ]\n\n///> lua\n///> python\n///> rust\n///> typescript\ntest map_keeps_the_order =\n    unordered_items () |> map (\\ x => x * 2i32) |> unbox |> _assert_eq [ 6i32; 2i32; 6i32; 4i32; 2i32; 8i32 ]\n\n///> lua\n///> python\n///> rust\n///> typescript\ntest distinct_keeps_first_occurrences_in_order =\n    unordered_items () |> distinct' |> unbox |> _assert_eq [ 3i32; 1i32; 2i32; 4i32 ]\n  >>>")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_150 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 150u32, "2026-10-31T16:22Z", "tibia-lane", "10-31 12:2x tibia: otp_actor slice HELD (n146's v2 program, re-anchored onto main's wave 2: base 9CBE041A30815924 -> 7082AC9725088575). Re-gate 11:40-12:08: the unchanged base FAILS a_named_actor_answers_through_its_name/gleam, and so does the candidate. The same test passed on base EE3DCBE1 at 05:11 and 07:33. Main's wave-2 diff to otp_actor.spi is heading removals plus one lambda-parens edit (new_with_init), which should not touch named actors. The rule (candidate red = base red) is met, but it is vacuous while the test cannot pass, so this slice does not land until the base red is explained. Diagnostic queued (lib_gate -Detail on the live file); lib-program-gate.ps1 now keeps each run's raw lib_gate output (<Work>\\<label>.raw.txt), so later reds carry their error text.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_151 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 151u32, "2026-10-31T17:14Z", "tibia-lane", "10-31 13:1x tibia, scoping n148's defused-dot rule for tibia's share. Across the 26 cells of tibia's share that are 30 or less over (measured on the post-slice drafts, against x's tlds.txt with 1286 entries), each has only 0-2 TLD-shaped dots, and none can fit by dequalifying alone (best case read_all_bytes: 2 dots against +6). The lever is real but small here; these cells need reshaping (LIB-XCOMPAT-TIBIA-DEFS).")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_152 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 152u32, "2026-10-31T17:59Z", "tibia-lane", "10-31 13:5x tibia: LIB-XCOMPAT-OTP-NAMED PROVEN on the NODE, and n150's hold is cleared. Gate: node mirror tibia-gates (spiral synced 13:2x, REMOTE-SYNC-OK digest BEC35FF2108C2C55, head fd70dccf plus the box's unstaged tree), lib-program-gate.ps1 -Control with -Kit eoie.exe. The control on the unchanged base 9CBE041A30815924 PASSED a_named_actor_answers_through_its_name/gleam, so the box's 11:40 base red was load (the box was at 100% CPU, with other cells timing out at 300 s the same hour). Candidate -> 7082AC9725088575: passed, 0 type errors. Negative (new 0i32 -> new 1i32 in named_counter): failed, CAUGHT. Formatter clean, round trip ok, x-compat 0. Supersedes n144 and n146.\nLAND: land-lib-program.ps1 -Note POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS/<this note> -File otp_actor.spi -Base 9CBE041A30815924 -Target 7082AC9725088575\nPROGRAM (kit do <lib> -):\nedit otp_actor.spi expect files 1 replacements 1\n  replace once <<<\n///> gleam\ntest a_named_actor_answers_through_its_name =\n    := address : erlang_process.name counter_message = erlang_process.new_name \"lib_otp_actor_named_test\"\n    new 0i32 |> named address |> on_message counter_step |> start |> started_data |> ignore\n    := inbox = erlang_process.named_subject address\n    send inbox (Add 5i32)\n    call inbox 10000i32 Read |> _assert_eq 5i32\n  >>> with <<<\n:= named_counter_address () : erlang_process.name counter_message =\n    erlang_process.new_name \"lib_otp_actor_named_test\"\n\n:= named_counter () =\n    := address = named_counter_address ()\n    new 0i32 |> named address |> on_message counter_step |> start |> started_data |> ignore\n    erlang_process.named_subject address\n\n///> gleam\ntest a_named_actor_answers_through_its_name =\n    := inbox = named_counter ()\n    send inbox (Add 5i32)\n    call inbox 10000i32 Read |> _assert_eq 5i32\n  >>>")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_153 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 153u32, "2026-10-31T18:10Z", "tibia-lane", "10-31 14:1x tibia, LANDING BLOCKED by base churn, not by proofs. Proven programs live in notes: env (DEFS n4), listm' (n149), otp_actor (n152), process (TESTS n3, re-anchored onto DAC63B6A, node re-gate pending), file_system (re-anchored after main's F# retire, node re-gate queued). At 14:07, env (now D6A498FC) and listm' (now 3A6A2BDE) had moved again under main's FSHARP-OUTSIDE-COMPILER retire (13:15-13:4x, 19 lib files). otp_actor's land was refused by other lanes' live readers. The landing script (scratch land-1031.ps1, land-lib-program.ps1 -Note per slice) exempts fiscal's box gates by ancestry: their -Lib is a TEMP copy whose deps\\spiral junction points back into the copy, so a lib land cannot affect them. Asked main for a quiet point, or for main to land the 5 programs in its own window.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_154 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 154u32, "2026-10-31T18:29Z", "tibia-lane", "10-31 14:28 tibia: LANDED otp_actor.spi 9CBE041A30815924 -> 7082AC9725088575 (n152's program), in main's freeze window, tree-write window, land-lib-program.ps1 -Note, applied with no live lib reader. Undo: %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\undo\\POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS-n152. Post-land on the live file: round trip ok, x-compat 0 overweight (was 1). tibia's otp_actor share is done.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_155 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 155u32, "2026-10-31T18:55Z", "tibia-lane", "10-31 14:5x tibia: LISTM' TEST SLICE RE-PROVEN on the frozen base (supersedes n149; the program is unchanged). Control GREEN; candidate 5/5 on every row (python/lua/rust/typescript/zig); negative 5 failed, CAUGHT; overweight 16 after. Gate: node mirror tibia-gates re-synced 14:2x (REMOTE-SYNC-OK digest A6842A82921F0194), lib-program-gate.ps1 -Control, eoie.exe, inside main's 14:12-16:30 freeze. Formatter clean, round trip ok.\nLAND: land-lib-program.ps1 -Note POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS/<this note> -File \"listm'.spi\" -Base 3A6A2BDED0617936 -Target 4262C51D58E3C6C7\nPROGRAM (kit do <lib> -):\n\nedit listm'.spi expect files 1 replacements 2\n  replace once <<<\n///> python\n///> lua\n///> rust\n///> typescript\n///> zig\ntest cons_leaves_the_shared_tail_unchanged =\n    := tail = box [ 2i32 ]\n    := first = cons' 1i32 tail\n    := second = cons' 9i32 tail\n    (fold_back' id first [], fold_back' id second [], fold_back' id tail [])\n    |> _assert_eq ([ 1i32; 2i32 ], [ 9i32; 2i32 ], [ 2i32 ])\n  >>> with <<<\n:= shared_tail_folds () =\n    := tail = box [ 2i32 ]\n    := first = cons' 1i32 tail\n    := second = cons' 9i32 tail\n    (fold_back' id first [], fold_back' id second [], fold_back' id tail [])\n\n///> python\n///> lua\n///> rust\n///> typescript\n///> zig\ntest cons_leaves_the_shared_tail_unchanged =\n    shared_tail_folds () |> _assert_eq ([ 1i32; 2i32 ], [ 9i32; 2i32 ], [ 2i32 ])\n  >>>\n  replace once <<<\n///> lua\n///> python\n///> rust\n///> typescript\ntest list_operations_keep_their_order =\n    := items = box [ 3i32; 1i32; 3i32; 2i32; 1i32; 4i32 ]\n    (unbox (rev' items), unbox (filter' (\\ x => x > 1i32) items), unbox (map (\\ x => x * 2i32) items), unbox (distinct' items))\n    |> _assert_eq ([ 4i32; 1i32; 2i32; 3i32; 1i32; 3i32 ], [ 3i32; 3i32; 2i32; 4i32 ], [ 6i32; 2i32; 6i32; 4i32; 2i32; 8i32 ], [ 3i32; 1i32; 2i32; 4i32 ])\n  >>> with <<<\n:= unordered_items () : list' i32 = box [ 3i32; 1i32; 3i32; 2i32; 1i32; 4i32 ]\n\n///> lua\n///> python\n///> rust\n///> typescript\ntest rev_reverses_the_order =\n    unordered_items () |> rev' |> unbox |> _assert_eq [ 4i32; 1i32; 2i32; 3i32; 1i32; 3i32 ]\n\n///> lua\n///> python\n///> rust\n///> typescript\ntest filter_keeps_the_order =\n    unordered_items () |> filter' (\\ x => x > 1i32) |> unbox |> _assert_eq [ 3i32; 3i32; 2i32; 4i32 ]\n\n///> lua\n///> python\n///> rust\n///> typescript\ntest map_keeps_the_order =\n    unordered_items () |> map (\\ x => x * 2i32) |> unbox |> _assert_eq [ 6i32; 2i32; 6i32; 4i32; 2i32; 8i32 ]\n\n///> lua\n///> python\n///> rust\n///> typescript\ntest distinct_keeps_first_occurrences_in_order =\n    unordered_items () |> distinct' |> unbox |> _assert_eq [ 3i32; 1i32; 2i32; 4i32 ]\n  >>>")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_156 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 156u32, "2026-10-31T18:56Z", "tibia-lane", "10-31 14:5x tibia: SM' TEST SLICE PROVEN on the frozen base (split_joined fixture for split_list_uses_the_whole_separator). Control GREEN; candidate GREEN on rust and gleam; negative failed, CAUGHT; sm'_real.spir pair round trip ok; overweight 89 after (90). Gate: node mirror tibia-gates re-synced 14:2x (REMOTE-SYNC-OK digest A6842A82921F0194), lib-program-gate.ps1 -Control, eoie.exe, inside main's 14:12-16:30 freeze. Formatter clean, round trip ok.\nLAND: land-lib-program.ps1 -Note POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS/<this note> -File \"sm'.spi\" -Base D3FCCD791E27FED1 -Target 94A51BB514CE0C58\nPROGRAM (kit do <lib> -):\n\nedit sm'.spi expect files 1 replacements 1\n  replace once <<<\n///> rust\n///> gleam\ntest split_list_uses_the_whole_separator =\n    := ~s = \"a::b::::c\"\n    (\n        split_list \"::\" s |> concat_list \"|\",\n        split_list \",\" \"x\" |> concat_list \"|\",\n        split_list \",\" \"a,\" |> concat_list \"|\"\n    )\n    |> _assert_eq (\"a|b||c\", \"x\", \"a|\")\n  >>> with <<<\n:= split_joined (separator : string) (s : string) : string = split_list separator s |> concat_list \"|\"\n\n///> rust\n///> gleam\ntest split_list_uses_the_whole_separator =\n    := ~s = \"a::b::::c\"\n    (split_joined \"::\" s, split_joined \",\" \"x\", split_joined \",\" \"a,\") |> _assert_eq (\"a|b||c\", \"x\", \"a|\")\n  >>>")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_157 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 157u32, "2026-10-31T20:34Z", "tibia-lane", "10-31 16:3x tibia: ALL 6 tibia x-compat slices LANDED inside main's freeze (14:12 onward), each via land-lib-program.ps1 -Note against its frozen base, with no live lib reader at apply time. otp_actor 7082AC97 (n152) and process BE258A03 (TESTS n5) at 14:28; file_system 7F2BD058 (TESTS n7) and sm' 94A51BB5 (n156) at about 16:2x; env C66DDE65 (DEFS n6) and listm' 4262C51D (n155) at 16:28. Undo copies in %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\undo\\<ITEM>-n<note>. Post-land: every live file round-trips (sm' with its _real.spir). The land was held from 14:5x by live readers. Exempted, after checking each one's lib path: fiscal box gates (their own TEMP lib copy), kit-train6 (its .cache\\release-tree lib copy), and x's node-proof once past its sync (it reads the box tree only in remote.ps1 node sync). Never exempted: compiler's live sm' cuda gate (15:18-16:2x), which the loop waited out (compiler was told the sm' base moves). tibia's lib share: 274 -> 233 overweight cells today (sm' 82, file_system 79, process 23, listm' 16, env 15, seq 10, threading 7, platform 1, otp_actor 0).")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_158 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 158u32, "2026-10-31T21:54Z", "tibia-lane", "10-31 17:5x tibia: POST-LAND GATE queued on the node (main's ask: the node was idle at 17:42): job-1793483617612-tibia-postland-gate, a full lib_gate (every test, every backend) of the 6 landed files on mirror tibia-gates (REMOTE-SYNC-OK digest 070CB91953285E7E = the box's post-land lib), with its own BuildPath and Cells port 14777 beside main's lib sweep. The next node-ready item is TIBIA-LIB-CONSUMER-REGATE (tibia's full build on the post-land lib).")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_159 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 159u32, "2026-10-31T23:07Z", "tibia-lane", "10-31 19:0x tibia: POST-LAND GATE GREEN (job-1793483617612-tibia-postland-gate, node mirror tibia-gates digest 070CB91953285E7E, every test on every backend of each landed file, 17:53-19:05). env C66DDE65 15/15; listm' 455791B7 35/35 (main's follow-up changed it after tibia's land); otp_actor 7082AC97 4/4; file_system 7F2BD058 35/35; sm' BB51848F 72/72 (also moved after tibia's land); process BE258A03 16/18. The 2 process reds are split_args/rust and split_command/typescript, the same rows that were red on the UNCHANGED-base controls earlier today (box 09:4x and node 14:07), so they predate tibia's lands; they are the owners' pre-existing reds. 0 type errors anywhere. tibia's 6 lands hold on the post-land lib.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_160 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 160u32, "2026-11-01T06:28Z", "x", "2026-11-01 02:28 x: DESIGN for PS1-TO-SPIRAL port 1 ('xc postable census', which replaces scripts/postable-report.ps1 and i574n.github's x-compat-census.ps1), read from the live client:\n- Hooks: xc.spi:343 'preview' already calls xc_preview::xc_preview(site, text) and prints each post's 'cK i/n' header. run_preview_threads (xc.spi:359) loops files after '=== FILE'. cli.spi:326 parses 'post-thread --dry-run FILE...' into PreviewThreads.\n- New typed surface (typefeatureset): in cli.spi, a command case PostableCensus of a list of paths ('xc postable census FILE...'). In a small census module, ':== file_census = { path : string; cells : i32; spilled : i32; posts : i32 }', a header parser over preview's lines (posts = header count, cells = headers with i = 1, spilled = i = 1 and n > 1), and ':== census_total' summed by listm::fold. Output: one 'FILE cells=.. spilled=.. posts=..' row per file, a total row, the 10 worst files, and Prometheus rows 'x_postable_cells_total' / 'x_postable_spilled_total' (X-RUN-METRICS' fourth number).\n- File discovery stays OUT of xc: the caller passes paths. postable-report.ps1's git ls-files plus the target/e2e skip become one line of glue in verify, until kit grows a file-walk verb.\n- Pure parts carry test blocks on rust and gleam (header parsing, summing, worst-10 ordering).\n- Proof: a node verify. Pass rule: the new subcommand's totals over x equal postable-report.ps1's for the same tree. Then both scripts archive (ORG-SCRIPTS-HOME n5 item 2).\nNEXT STEP: draft the census module and cli case as a note-stored draft, then prove through verify (Rust client build on the node).")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_161 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 161u32, "2026-11-01T06:30Z", "x", "2026-11-01 02:30 x: DRAFT census.spi (new client module; register it after protocol in the client package). It reuses protocol::parsed, the client's own typed header reader (header_reading = result frame_header header_rejection), so no new parser. Remaining for the port: a cli.spi PostableCensus case, an xc.spi loop calling 'preview' per file, then census_of and census_row plus the total and worst-10 rows. Equality with postable-report.ps1 over the same tree is the gate: the script's regex '^c\\d+ (\\d+)/(\\d+)$' is stricter about the first word than protocol::parsed, and the equality run will show whether body lines ever parse as headers.\nDRAFT:\n///- --package . --timeout 600000\n///> rust\n///> gleam\ntest census static =\n    :< testing\n\n:< sm'_operators\n:< prelude\n\n:== file_census = { path : string; cells : i32; spilled : i32; posts : i32 }\n\n:= blank_census (path : string) : file_census = { path; cells = 0i32; spilled = 0i32; posts = 0i32 }\n\n:= one_if (b : bool) : i32 = ? b ?> 1i32 |: 0i32\n\n:= with_header (c : file_census) (h : protocol::frame_header) : file_census =\n    := first = h::part = 1i32\n    := spills = one_if (first && h::part_count > 1i32)\n    { c ?| posts = c::posts + 1i32; cells = c::cells + one_if first; spilled = c::spilled + spills }\n\n:= counted_line (c : file_census) (line : string) : file_census =\n    ?= protocol::parsed line ?| Ok h => with_header c h | Error _ => c\n\n:= census_of (path : string) (preview : string) : file_census =\n    pieces \"\\n\" preview |> listm::fold counted_line (blank_census path)\n\n:= count_field (name : string) (n : i32) : string = \" \" ++# name ++# \"=\" ++# sm'::obj_to_string n\n\n:= census_row (c : file_census) : string =\n    c::path ++# count_field \"cells\" c::cells ++# count_field \"spilled\" c::spilled ++# count_field \"posts\" c::posts\n\ntest a_preview_counts_cells_spills_and_posts =\n    census_of \"a.spi\" \"c1 1/1\\nbody\\nc2 1/2\\nmore\\nc2 2/2\\nend\" |> census_row\n    |> _assert_eq \"a.spi cells=2 spilled=1 posts=3\"")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_162 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 162u32, "2026-11-01T12:34Z", "x", "2026-11-01 08:34 x: ITEM UPGRADE (round 4). Fields: tags gain team:x; the stale dep X-COMPAT-VS-POSTABLE (Done 10-28) is dropped; estimate 240 / light / agent stand. P1 is justified: it retires 2 .ps1 (postable-report.ps1 and i574n.github's x-compat-census.ps1, PS1-TO-SPIRAL port 1) and is the x half of the 280-char synergy with Cells.\nDEFINITION OF DONE (verifiable):\n(1) 'xc postable census FILE...' exists in the client.\n(2) Over x's tracked .spi (the same list postable-report.ps1 builds), its total row equals postable-report.ps1's line for x exactly: files, files with every cell in one post, cells, cells over one post, posts. Command: run both on one tree and diff the numbers. Threshold: 0 differences.\n(3) census.spi's test blocks pass on rust and gleam (Cells notebook in the client build).\n(4) Artifacts: the README command row, x_postable_cells_total / x_postable_spilled_total rows (X-RUN-METRICS), and both scripts archived with scratch-archive.ps1.\nNEXT CONCRETE STEP: new crate apps/client/census/ (Cargo.toml with [package.metadata.spiral] entry = \"census.spi\", output = \"census.rs\", which apps/client/build.ps1:13 picks up) holding n161's census.spi draft. Then cli.spi gains the PostableCensus command case next to PreviewThreads (cli.spi:326/394), and xc.spi gets run_postable_census mirroring run_preview_threads (xc.spi:359): 'preview' per file, then census_of, census_row, total, worst 10.\nTYPEFEATURESET PLAN (before any code):\n- Headers stay typed end to end. Parse with protocol::parsed : string -> header_reading (= result frame_header header_rejection). A non-header line is an Error case that the fold skips by pattern, never a -1, an empty string or a regex match.\n- Scope as a phantom index: ':! per_file = ()' and ':! per_repo = ()'. GADT ':| census_row scope = | FileRow :: file_census -> census_row per_file | TotalRow :: census_total -> census_row per_repo'. worst_files takes 'list (census_row per_file)', so a total can never be ranked as a file, and the total is built only by folding FileRows.\n- Counts are i32 fields of records (file_census { path; cells; spilled; posts }), never a tab or pipe row. The text exists only in census_row's printer and in X-RUN-METRICS' 'metric count' exposition (sim_report.spi's ':! metric u' pattern, the model to copy).\n- File discovery stays outside xc (caller-passed paths), so the census itself is pure and backend-neutral, the BACKEND-BALANCE R2 CLI.\n- Models: x's own wire.spi (reads r / writes r GADTs, phantom app_context/user_context) and sim_report.spi's phantom-unit metric.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_163 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 163u32, "2026-11-03T04:43Z", "x", "2026-11-03 00:43 x: DRAFT MOVED to the store: the 11-01 census.spi draft lived only in a backlog note. It's now %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\x\\POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS\\census.spi (34 lines), per the drafts-store rule. Before use it needs the symbols respell ('test census static =' -> ':# census static =', 'test a_preview_...' -> ':# a_preview_...'), as the client tree was respelled since then. NEXT (one client-build proof): register census after protocol in apps/client/package.spiproj, add the cli PostableCensus case and the xc loop over 'preview', and run the DoD equality against postable-report.ps1 on one tree (0 differences), with the -Accept sim build in the proof (memory x-sim-cell-budget-in-accept-build). Not started tonight because a client-build proof doesn't fit before the 02:00 window end.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_164 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 164u32, "2026-11-03T04:45Z", "x", "2026-11-03 00:45 x: census phase A started. The draft is respelled (':#' blocks; the explicit 'path = path' field) and registered after protocol in a package draft (base 5C471A179D53). PRE-REGISTERED RULE (x-census1): census.spi blocks: a_preview_counts_cells_spills_and_posts passes on rust and gleam (spiral-tests total failed 0, executed >= 1, exit 0). That proves only the census model. The CLI case, the xc loop and the DoD equality with postable-report.ps1 stay as the next step.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_165 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 165u32, "2026-11-03T05:02Z", "x", "2026-11-03 01:02 x: census phase A GREEN by its rule (x-census1 on core 43CAC3DC4EFD7CA3): census.spi block a_preview_counts_cells_spills_and_posts executed 1, failed 0, no rust/gleam mismatches, exit 0. NOT LANDED on purpose: registering census in apps/client/package.spiproj changes the client build, the consumer path, which this proof didn't run. So the green model stays in kit drafts x\\POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS (census.spi plus package.spiproj on base 5C471A179D53) and lands with the CLI slice: the cli PostableCensus case, the xc loop over 'preview', the client build plus sim -Accept build, and the DoD equality against postable-report.ps1.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_166 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 166u32, "2026-11-03T05:26Z", "x", "2026-11-03 01:26 x: census draft EXTENDED (unproven; kit drafts x\\POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS\\census.spi, 2 blocks): repo_census record, added, repo_total and repo_line, which renders postable-report.ps1's EXACT line ('x: files N, files with every cell in one post M, cells C, cells over one post S, posts P'), so the DoD equality is a string compare of one line per repo. Block a_repo_total_reads_like_the_report_line pins the format. Every definition is sized under the 272 cell budget (largest raw 263, the 11-01 with_header). WIRING DESIGN, read from the tree: (1) cli.spi thread_op gains 'CensusThreads : list string', parsed from 'postable census FILE...', and thread_batch_text renders it; (2) fields.spi thread_op_payload maps it to kind 12i32 with delimited newline paths; (3) xc.spi run_thread_more routes kind 12 to run_census: for each path, census::census_of path (preview (file_text path)), then print census_row per file and repo_line (repo_total ...) last; (4) usage_thread_previews gains the usage line, and the e2e tutorial's expected help text must be regenerated with -Accept (memory x-sim-fixtures-embed-client-sources); (5) one proof: client build, sim -Accept build (cell budget), e2e RUST-OK, then postable-report.ps1 and 'xc postable census' over the same tree with an identical x line. Note the regex gap from n-?: the script requires '^c\\d+ (\\d+)/(\\d+)$' while protocol::parsed reads cell_text forms, so the equality run decides.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_167 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 167u32, "2026-11-03T05:28Z", "x", "2026-11-03 01:28 x: PRE-REGISTERED RULE (x-census2, the extended draft): census.spi blocks: both a_preview_counts_cells_spills_and_posts and a_repo_total_reads_like_the_report_line pass on rust and gleam (spiral-tests total discovered 2, executed 2, failed 0, exit 0). Still the model only; the land waits for the CLI slice's client-build proof (n-2).")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_168 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 168u32, "2026-11-03T05:35Z", "x", "2026-11-03 01:35 x: x-census2 GREEN by its rule (core 43CAC3DC4EFD7CA3): discovered 2, executed 2, passed 2, failed 0, exit 0, so the repo_line format is pinned on rust and gleam. Still held in the drafts store (census.spi now holds the file and repo totals); it lands with the CLI slice per the n-3 design.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_169 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 169u32, "2026-11-03T07:23Z", "x", "2026-11-03 03:23 x: CLI SLICE drafted (kit drafts x\\POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS, on bases cli 1DC30BC36F27, fields C474A16F9F16, xc D82237F1C898 and package 5C471A179D53). cli.spi: thread_op CensusThreads, 'postable census FILE...' parsed by postable_command, describe text, and a zig block postable_census_takes_one_or_more_files. fields.spi: kind 12. xc.spi: run_census previews each existing file, prints census_row per file and repo_line of repo_total 'total' last. Usage text is deliberately NOT changed in this slice, because the e2e tutorial compares help output; it follows with an e2e -Accept. PRE-REGISTERED RULE (x-census-cli1, core 489EA99474EDE920): sim accept exit 0, sim build 0, client build 0, x e2e RUST-OK with no FAILED line, and 'census equality SAME': postable-report.ps1's x line and the census total line agree after the colon, over the same file list (the script's own git ls-files filter). Any fixture -Accept changes is listed, and must be explained by a client source this slice touched.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_170 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 170u32, "2026-11-03T07:32Z", "x", "2026-11-03 03:32 x: x-census-cli1 NOT GREEN at the sim -Accept build: xc.spi's census::... references were unbound. Client subpackage modules reach root client modules ONLY through ':<' opens, never qualified (checked: no subpackage uses root::name), so xc.spi now opens census and uses the names unqualified, with no clash against xc, prelude, posts or transport (checked). Rerun x-census-cli2, rule unchanged (n-1), on core 489EA99474EDE920 with the pinned compiler copy.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_171 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 171u32, "2026-11-03T07:44Z", "x", "2026-11-03 03:44 x: x-census-cli2 NOT GREEN, 'Unbound module census'. Root cause: x/apps/client has BOTH spiral.toml and package.spiproj, and the manifest lookup is toml-first (names = spiral.toml, package.spiproj; the same rule Cells' manifest port carries), so my package.spiproj edit was ignored. The draft now adds census after protocol in spiral.toml too (base 6021BD986304), keeping both manifests in sync. Rerun x-census-cli3, rule unchanged. Lesson for x: any client module registration edits spiral.toml, and package.spiproj only as its mirror.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_172 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 172u32, "2026-11-03T08:00Z", "x", "2026-11-03 03:59 x: STATE at pause. x-census-cli3 (job-1793692201820, core 489EA99474EDE920) is RUNNING; it's not killed, and its result is recorded on x's next turn. Drafts are in kit drafts x\\POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS (census.spi, spiral.toml, package.spiproj, cli.spi, fields.spi, xc.spi on recorded bases). Its rule is n-4. NEXT STEP if green: land all six through scratchpad land-x-census (to write; bases 5C471A179D53 package, 6021BD986304 toml, 1DC30BC36F27 cli, C474A16F9F16 fields, D82237F1C898 xc) plus any fixture its -Accept regenerated, then the usage-text follow-up with an e2e -Accept. If red: read the tail, fix, rerun when the run restarts. Waits on: the job, then the next run.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_173 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 173u32, "2026-11-03T08:22Z", "x", "2026-11-03 04:22 x: RESULT recorded at pause, as main asked: x-census-cli3 NOT GREEN, at a third gate. It ran wholly on pinned core 489EA99474EDE920. Passed: sim accept exit 0 (cell budget clean, no fixture changed) and sim build exit 0, so the toml registration fix worked. Failed: client build at gate.ps1 -Enforce, 'gate / 1 crate(s) over 1000 lines: xc', because the census code compiles into the xc crate and pushes it past the 1k line gate (memory line-gate-all-languages). The equality step didn't run. NEXT STEP when the run restarts: make census its own client sub-crate like preview/ and frames/ (move census.spi to apps/client/census/census.spi, register it as 'census/' then '    census' in spiral.toml and package.spiproj, and give it a Cargo.toml matching preview's), so xc only calls into it. Then rerun under the same n-5 rule. Drafts stay in kit drafts x\\POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS. Nothing landed.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_174 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 174u32, "2026-11-03T09:05Z", "x", "2026-11-03 05:05 x: CENSUS AS A SUB-CRATE (the fix for cli3's 'xc over 1000 lines'). Drafts in kit drafts x\\POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS: census\\census.spi (///- --package .., opens protocol; model plus rows_back and total_of; exports xc_census_row (path, preview) and xc_census_total rows; blocks for the file count, the report line and rows read back), census\\Cargo.toml (xc_census, like preview), spiral.toml and package.spiproj (census/ census after preview; re-derived from live, so no root census), xc\\xc.spi (run_census calls the two crate functions, so xc only loops and prints), xc\\Cargo.toml (xc_census path dependency), plus cli.spi and fields.spi as before. Launcher: unique staging names and an scp exit check (compiler n8), and the pinned compiler copy (fiscal n1). RULE for x-census-cli4, same as n-5: sim accept exit 0, sim build 0, client build 0 (now including the 1k crate gate), e2e RUST-OK with no FAILED line, census equality SAME. Fixtures changed by -Accept are listed and must come from a touched client source.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_175 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 175u32, "2026-11-03T09:38Z", "x", "2026-11-03 05:38 x: x-census-cli4 NOT GREEN, at the same 1k gate. Sim accept 0 and sim build 0, but xc grew 975 -> 1044 generated lines: even the thin wiring (row loop, fold closures, two crate calls) inlined about 69 lines into xc, which was already 25 short of the gate. FIX: the census crate now owns the whole file loop, xc_census_files (site, paths) (file_system file_exists and read_all_text, then xc_preview via a crate dependency added to census/Cargo.toml, then rows and the total), and xc's run_census is one write_line of that call. Rerun x-census-cli5, same rule (n-5), on core 489EA99474EDE920.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_176 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 176u32, "2026-11-03T10:42Z", "x", "2026-11-03 06:42 x: x-census-cli5 NOT GREEN, but further: sim accept 0, sim build 0, and the 1k crate gate PASSED (the census crate fix worked). It then failed the client CELL-BUDGET with 3 over 272: census.spi value_of 300 (its long /// heading counts toward the cell), cli.spi thread_command 277 (one more dispatch line), fields.spi thread_op_payload 320 (one more payload arm). FIXES: the heading is shortened; 'postable' moves into other_command (refresh/thread/postable); CensusThreads moves into thread_edit_payload, so thread_op_payload is back to its live shape. Rerun x-census-cli6 on core 21D5992EDB80D270, same rule. LESSON (already in memory x-sim-cell-budget-in-accept-build, now hit on the client): adding one arm or one dispatch line to an existing near-full definition overflows it, so put new cases in the smallest sibling.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_177 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 177u32, "2026-11-03T12:14Z", "x", "2026-11-03 08:14 x: x-census-cli6 GREEN on every pre-registered condition (job-1793702714184, pinned core 21D5992EDB80D270 at both ends): sim accept 0 with no fixture changed, sim build 0, client build 0 (1k crate gate and CELL-BUDGET pass), e2e 141 requests RUST-OK 37 steps + 5 cases, and census equality SAME over 74 files (postable-report and xc postable census both say files 74, cells 3562, over one post 0, posts 3562). LANDED, unstaged: sources via the hash-guarded land script (census/census.spi 8471108A06A6, census/Cargo.toml 4244A8EA7046, spiral.toml 343D66DA2811, package.spiproj E8E743313695, cli.spi 1DEF3FB997C7, fields.spi F91F4D8F7006, xc/xc.spi D2A31DF9633F, xc/Cargo.toml 08E150A139E9); 65 generated outputs via node-proof -Land (0 conflicts: 29 client crates regenerated by the newer core including census.rs and xc.rs, sim frames that embed client sources); Cargo.lock 9C93CE2E6237 -> 75F8A1CDEAD3 (only adds the xc_census package, taken from the node build). NEXT: other repos' census and burn-down via the same command; the xc usage text for 'postable census' needs an e2e -Accept round.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_178 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 178u32, "2026-11-03T12:26Z", "x", "2026-11-03 08:26 x: USAGE FOLLOW-UP drafted, kit drafts x\\POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS\\usage\\cli.spi on live base 1DEF3FB997C7: usage_thread_previews gains '       xc postable census FILE...   a row of cells and posts per FILE, then their total' (cell 239 chars, under 272 - 15 headroom). No fixture embeds the usage (cli6's -Accept changed none); README.md and args.rs regenerate in the build. RULE for x-usage1 (cli6 recipe, pinned core at both ends): sim accept 0, sim build 0, client build 0 (CELL-BUDGET), e2e RUST-OK with no FAILED, census equality SAME, and xc's usage prints the postable census line. Queued after x-sim-maps1 and x-html-gleam2 on the node.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_179 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 179u32, "2026-11-03T12:55Z", "x", "2026-11-03 08:55 x: x-usage1 is FOLDED into x-events1 (X-SOTA-MODEL n20). Both edit cli.spi on base 1DEF3FB997C7, so the events draft starts from the usage draft and one ~75 min node run proves both; x-events1 checks that usage prints the postable census line. CROSS-REPO BURN-DOWN next: ~5,000 tracked .spi over 21 repos; the box xc is from 10-25, too old for splitter numbers, so the report runs with the fresh xc from the node after the queue.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_180 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 180u32, "2026-11-03T16:30Z", "x", "2026-11-03 12:30 x: CROSS-REPO BURN-DOWN prepared (scratchpad census-all.ps1): fetch the node's fresh xc.exe (C:\\home\\remote\\xqueue3\\x\\target\\release, rebuilt by x-events3) into temp on the box, then run x/scripts/postable-report.ps1 over every repo under C:\\home\\git that tracks .spi (21 repos, ~5,000 files), writing one line per repo plus a per-file TSV to scratch. CPU-light (a Rust dry-run split, no compile). Runs right after x-events3's verdict, so its numbers come from the binary that verdict proves. Output: per-repo 'files, files with every cell in one post, cells, cells over one post, posts' as the item's burn-down table, in chat and on this item.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_181 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 181u32, "2026-11-03T17:22Z", "x", "2026-11-03 13:22 x: USAGE FOLLOW-UP LANDED via x-events3 (n178/179 rule held): 'xc postable census FILE...' is in the usage, README's 2 tutorial outputs carry it (the -Accept round changed exactly those), and x census equality SAME over 75 files (3617 cells, 0 over one post). Cross-repo burn-down runs next with this binary (census-all.ps1).")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_182 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 182u32, "2026-11-03T18:49Z", "x", "2026-11-03 14:49 x: CROSS-REPO BURN-DOWN BASELINE (census-all.ps1 on the box, xc 45DE17A7ACF6 built by x-events3; the same postable-report line as x's DoD; 21 repos, 5,041 files; per-file TSV in x's scratchpad). Repo: files, files fully postable, cells, cells over one post, posts. x 75/75, 3617, 0, 3617 | singularity 4/4, 11, 0, 11 | html 17/12, 349, 9, 358 | tibia 137/71, 3994, 67, 4524 | regex 17/1, 619, 111, 761 | tictactoe 50/9, 647, 135, 1213 | dice 16/2, 191, 52, 580 | blog 10/2, 87, 23, 123 | dir-tree-html 7/0, 136, 43, 222 | polyglot 5/0, 164, 54, 345 | alphabet 2/0, 14, 9, 1948 | fork-spiral-patch 3/0, 16, 4, 35 | i574n.github 9/1, 189, 67, 8168 | Spiral-s-ML-Library 169/34, 1337, 567, 3456 | The-Spiral-Language 857/210, 3671, 1529, 8850 | kit 1042/270, 9580, 3504, 27271 (eoie is the same tree; count once) | spiral 1485/648, 16353, 6295, 33138 | fiscal 93/0, 123212, 35018, 277846. WORST FILE per repo: fiscal generated_ubl_receiptadvice_2_1 / invoice_2_1 1809 each; spiral apps/compiler/tmp/samples mega_omniledger_erp_kernel 576 and mega_lean_cic 554; kit state/bench.spi 54, state/agile.spi 44; Spiral-s-ML ml/primitives 52; i574n.github apps/graphs/compiler_model 30; tictactoe lib_spi/ui/db 24; dice ui/src/model/near/rpc 18; regex src/certificate 16; polyglot lib/math 19; html cli/html 3, html_pages/tree 3; tibia results/map_grids 2. READING: most spill mass is NOT hand-written code: fiscal's GENERATED output (35,018, an emitter fix: one cell per record), kit's agile STATE data files, and spiral's compiler tmp SAMPLES. PROPOSAL for the DoD: report three classes per repo (hand-written, generated, data/state), with the burn-down to 0 applying to hand-written; generated burns down through its emitter, and data/state is excluded from postability. The owners' burn-down: html 9, tibia 67, regex 111, tictactoe 135, dice 52, blog 23 are small hand-code targets.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_183 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 183u32, "2026-11-03T18:57Z", "x", "2026-11-03 14:57 x: n182's CLASS PROPOSAL implemented as a draft (kit drafts x\\POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS\\classes\\census.spi, base 8471108A06A6 -> FCDED2D2ADC1), x's own decision recorded here: a closed union file_class = Hand | Generated | Data. class_named reads the path: '/generated/' is Generated, '/state/', '/tmp/' or '/results/' is Data, anything else Hand. Each census row carries ' class=<name>' before its counts (row_back still reads the last three words). 'xc postable census' prints a hand / generated / data line in the report format, then the unchanged overall total as the LAST line, so the DoD equality with postable-report.ps1 still compares the same string. Rust blocks: the row test is updated to class=hand, and a new block sorts 4 paths (hand, generated, data, data). Cells in budget. DoD reading from now on: each repo's HAND line burns down to 0; generated burns down through its emitter; data is excluded. Rides x-next3, which prints the three class lines for x (expect hand = total, generated 0, data 0).")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_184 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 184u32, "2026-11-03T21:59Z", "x", "2026-11-03 17:59 x: x-next3 NOT GREEN in 50 s, caused by MY census draft: the split 'added' used '{ added_counts r c ?| ... }', a record update on an application, which the formatter round-tripped but the parser rejects ('Expected: }' at census.spi:54), so 'added' was left out and repo_total failed. Fixed: ':= base = added_counts r c' then '{ base ?| ... }' (C10FA3E6E688, formatted, max cell 263). Lesson added to memory format-drafts-before-proof: a clean format round trip is not a parse check. Relaunched as x-next3b on the newest core 2ABF22D79E6B6943 (the node's core changed twice in an hour: pack27, then D1854B99FD3FA940, then 2ABF22D79E6B6943).")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_185 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 185u32, "2026-11-03T22:43Z", "x", "2026-11-03 18:42 x: FILE CLASSES LANDED, green in x-next3b (job-1793743112574, pinned core 2ABF22D79E6B6943): sim accept 0 (sim-stress fixtures regenerated), sim build 0, client build 0 (CELL-BUDGET, 1k gate), e2e RUST-OK 37 + 5, census SAME over 76 files: census.spi C10FA3E6E688 prints a hand / generated / data line before the unchanged total. x reads hand 76 files / 3,659 cells / 0 over one post, generated 0, data 0 (as predicted), and census equality still SAME. The DoD per n182/n183 is now measurable per class; the next cross-repo run uses the classifying xc.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_186 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 186u32, "2026-11-03T22:56Z", "x", "2026-11-03 18:56 x: CLASS-SPLIT BASELINE (the n182 per-file TSV re-read with the landed class_named rule: '/generated/' is generated; '/state/', '/tmp/' or '/results/' is data; else hand; eoie counted once as kit). HAND cells over one post, the DoD target: x 0, singularity 0, tibia 1 (its 66 others are results data), html 9, alphabet 9, blog 23, dir-tree-html 43, dice 52, polyglot 54, i574n.github 67, regex 111, tictactoe 135, fiscal 136 (of 317 hand cells; 34,882 are GENERATED, the emitter's fix), Spiral-s-ML-Library 567, The-Spiral-Language 1,529, spiral 1,536 (another 4,759 are DATA, mostly apps/compiler/tmp samples), kit 3,147 (plus 357 data). READING: excluding generated output and data cuts the burn-down from about 51k to about 7.3k hand cells, and more than half of that is kit (3,147) plus spiral and The-Spiral-Language hand code. tibia and html are one small heal from 0.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_187 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 187u32, "2026-11-04T05:50Z", "x", "2026-11-04 01:50 x: the next measurement is queued. The 2 repos one small heal from 0 (html 9, tibia 1 hand cells over one post, per n-last) get a fresh census on the node in x-next6, with the classifying xc that run builds; the box's xc is from 10-25 and predates the postable verb. The step is informational only: hand files = tracked .spi minus generated/state/tmp/results/target, and it doesn't touch x-next6's verdict. Next: with the per-cell list in hand, heal html's 9 and tibia's 1 (or hand them to their lanes if the cells are theirs).")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_188 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 188u32, "2026-11-04T11:12Z", "x", "2026-11-04 07:12 x: FRESH MEASUREMENT (claimed by x; a current xc with the postable verb, built on the node by x-next5c at 06:57 and copied to the box, run per file over the box's LIVE trees; hand = tracked .spi minus generated/state/tmp/results/target/deps). html: 3 hand cells over one post, all in cli/html.spi (node_at L44 302, render_tree L86 401, self_test L129 453); down from 9 after main's block move to lib. tibia: 1, src/hunting_pack.spi aim_seen L1210 290 (unchanged from the n182 baseline of 1). Both owners messaged with the exact cells. CAVEATS: (1) the node's C:\\home\\git\\tibia and html copies are STALE or unsynced (hashes differ; html has no .spi there), so node-side census of non-mirror repos is invalid: the census step in x-next6 reads C:\\home\\git\\<repo> on the node and should be ignored; (2) xc's batch total classifies some files out (html batch counted 2 of 9 files), so the per-file sweep is the authoritative over-count.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_189 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 189u32, "2026-11-04T11:22Z", "x", "2026-11-04 07:22 x: html re-measured after main's split, INCLUDING main's unstaged/new files (24 hand .spi, not 9): 8 cells over in 5 files (cli/html.spi 2, html_pages/tree.spi 3, html_model/unit.spi 1 and html_pages/unit.spi 1 (unit main() export lists, maybe classifiable as generated), html_parse/markdown.spi 1). The tracked-only 3 -> 2 in cli/html.spi is real, but the block move added cells elsewhere. Exact cells sent to main. Lesson for this item: measure tracked AND untracked (git ls-files --cached --others --exclude-standard), since a mid-refactor repo's new files count too.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_190 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 190u32, "2026-11-04T12:25Z", "x", "2026-11-04 08:25 x: REGEX MEASURED (main's ask): xc postable census (node build 11-04 07:07) over C:\\home\\git\\regex\\src\\*.spi = 15 hand files (0 untracked), 548 cells, 96 over 272, 0 files fully postable, 672 posts. Bands: 273-320 = 33 (trims), 321-544 = 51 (one split each), >544 = 12 (language ends 1522; nullable evidence 1266 / nullability_of 1378 / accept_reason 1298; xsd class_body 1075 / alternation 1043; syntax order_re 969; semantics bt 905; dfa build_rows 661; lazy empty_store 646 / grow_to 548; certificate columns_sound 573); 17 of 96 are '///> rust' test cells. Per-file cell list (line:length) sent to main, from a blank-line split whose per-file counts equal xc's for all 15 files. So far per repo: x 0 over (77 files), tibia 1, html 8 in 5 files, regex 96 in 15.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_191 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 191u32, "2026-11-04T13:17Z", "x", "2026-11-04 09:17 x: LIB HEAL of x's own lands (drafts kit drafts spiral\\LIB-HEADING-STRIP, land queued as x-libheal1 after x-httpwire1 releases lib). (1) 9 explanatory '/// ##' lines that x added today removed: list_sort 1, doc_blocks 1, doc_render 1, erlang_file 3, erlang_port 3 (the erlang_http/tcp/text ones are in x-next6's frozen drafts, after it lands). (2) 4 over-272 cells that x's own lands left in lib, now 0: erlang_file file_appended (append_mode split out) and its append test (test_cache_dir/test_cache_file helpers); erlang_port erl_eval_port (erl_eval_args, erl_eval_options) and run_collect (next_event, plus the Quiet arm as a blank-line-separated ':& :~ after_quiet' member, main's finding of today; quiet_left renamed quiet). xc census over the 5 drafts: 122 cells, 0 over; format clean; vultr --check 0 for all 5. No behavior change: pure extraction and renaming.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_192 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 192u32, "2026-11-04T13:21Z", "x", "2026-11-04 09:21 x: LIB MEASURED (xc postable census, node build 11-04 07:07, over spiral/lib/spiral/*.spi): 116 hand files, 40 fully postable, 5388 cells, 1255 over 272, 8229 posts; 76 files spill. Largest: xsd_emit 175, sm' 91, file_system 85, xpath_eval 72, date_time 65, xsd_regex 49, xml 41, parsing 40, xsd_resolve 34, json_schema 33, xpath 27, schematron 26, convert 25, xsd 25, process/metrics/base 21, resultm 20, iggy 19, console 18, listm'/am' 16, reflection/crypto 15, env 14. Erlang modules (LIB-ERLANG-TYPED-STDLIB): 24 over in 9 files. x heals its own now: erlang_term 1 -> 0 (test split in two, 5 heading lines removed; 98863DCD2A5E -> 8226C671D1EF, vultr --check 0), added to the x-libheal1 batch (now 6 files). After x-next6/x-next8 land: erlang_tcp 6, erlang_http 3, erlang_text 1. LEFT FOR ITS OWNER: erlang_process 9, all long concurrency tests (the biggest 1313 chars, a selector over two subjects and a monitor) plus a 10-line type-alias block and the pg import block; splitting needs shared spawn/ready helpers.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_193 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 193u32, "2026-11-04T13:42Z", "x", "2026-11-04 09:42 x: x-libheal1 LANDED green (lib_land_window: consumers compile, type_probe 6/6 PASS with 26 tests typed, respell 0; lib digest 4272FFF88CF0E607; undo lib-land-undo\\20261104-092405): list_sort A080A4EEDF82, doc_blocks A7B00A03CE59, doc_render 1CE2E168678D, erlang_file 07D8AC591A8E, erlang_port B76AF2420D24, erlang_term 8226C671D1EF. 15 explanatory heading lines gone; x's 5 over-272 cells in those files now 0. LIMIT: the gate TYPES tests and does not run them; these edits are pure extraction, renames and a ':&' split, and their first execution is the next node run that compiles them (x-next6's land onward). erlang_process (9 -> 0) is NOT in this land: its concurrency-test rewrite runs on the node first (x-proc1: live control and draft arm, each must report 14/14). regex is at 1 over (main): the evidence GADT, a recorded residual, and the class rule for indexed types is in main's report to the user.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_194 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 194u32, "2026-11-04T14:17Z", "x", "2026-11-04 10:17 x: MORE REPOS MEASURED (xc postable census over tracked+untracked .spi, hand class): alphabet 33 over of 107 cells (8 files, 0 fully postable); blog 23 of 87 (10 files, 2 postable); dice 52 of 191 (16, 2); dir-tree-html 43 of 136 (7, 0). Earlier: x 0, tibia 1, html 0 (main healed), regex 1 (the evidence GADT residual, main), lib 1255 of 5388 (x healed its own 15 lines and 5 cells today; erlang_process 9 -> 0 pending x-proc1). NOT YET MEASURED: eoie and kit (1026 files each overflow one command line; batch them), fiscal (exclude generated/: 59 MB of UBL bindings, ~70k lines each), i574n.github (exclude docs/backlog/state: agile.spi is 13 MB of data), Spiral-s-ML-Library, tictactoe, polyglot, singularity, spiral apps. They need batching and the node: xc costs ~26 ms per post (filed XC-CENSUS-SPEED), so the big repos are minutes of CPU, not for the box.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_195 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 195u32, "2026-11-04T15:51Z", "x", "2026-11-04 11:51 x: x-census1 DONE on the node (job-1793805147716, xc 06595421CEB6, box trees synced to mirror xcensus at 11:0x; generated/ and docs/backlog/state/ excluded; hand class only). kit: 1054 hand of 1117 files, 301 fully postable, 9057 cells, 3309 over 272, 24971 posts (914 s); worst agile_todo_model/model 42, kit_lint_model/model 31, kit_do_program/program 26, agile_task_listing/task_listing 23, and 22 each in toolchain_runtime_policy_domain/receipt, agile_note_model/model, state_receipt_codec_model/common, kit_machine_windows/windows_cli. fiscal (hand, src/): 14 files, 1 postable, 498 cells, 178 over; worst corpus_checks 41, families 28, generator 25, sunat 20, canon 16. i574n.github: 19 files, 1 postable, 354 cells, 119 over (8518 posts, one huge cell, likely a docs data file outside state/); worst apps/graphs/compiler_model 30, examples/perf/Perf 14, apps/graphs/emit 13, scripts/pins/workflows 12. polyglot: 5 files, 0 postable, 164 cells, 54 over; worst lib/math/math 19. NOTE eoie is the SAME repo as kit (C:\\home\\git\\eoie resolves to kit's toplevel), counted once. FULL PICTURE of over-272 cells: x 0, tibia 1, html 0, regex 1, lib 1255 -> lower after today's lands, kit 3309, fiscal 178, i574n.github 119, polyglot 54, dice 52, dir-tree-html 43, alphabet 33, blog 23. Not measured: Spiral-s-ML-Library, tictactoe, singularity, spiral apps (outside lib).")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_196 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 196u32, "2026-11-04T15:57Z", "x", "2026-11-04 11:57 x: LAST SMALL REPOS measured on the box (~1.5 min of xc, under the load rule; hand class, tracked+untracked .spi): Spiral-s-ML-Library 169 files, 34 postable, 1337 cells, 567 over 272 (worst ml/primitives 52, corebase/tensorm/tensor_main 30, ml/layers 28); tictactoe 50 files, 9 postable, 647 cells, 135 over (worst lib_spi/ui/db 24, lib_spi/ui/hooks/use_fetch 22, lib_spi/rust/util 22); singularity 4 files, all postable, 0 over. Only spiral's apps/ remain unmeasured (1383 .spi, 5 MB: minutes of xc, so a node job, not the box).")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_197 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 197u32, "2026-11-04T23:35Z", "x", "2026-11-04 19:35 x: SPIRAL apps/ MEASURED (the last unmeasured tree in the census; on VULTR, not the node, while the node runs x-next6d; vultr load 3.5, under the cap; xc 5d8d0ac1621c, lever-1 splitter, whose output is md5-identical to the box's). 1390 .spi under spiral/apps (same skip rule as x-census1: target, e2e, deps, node_modules, _build, .git, generated) -> 47 hand files (the rest classify as generated), fully postable 9, cells 1779, OVER 272: 475, posts 2882, wall 15 s. By app: kernel 205 (14 files), cells 172 (18), compiler 39 (1 file), spiral 25 (1), transformer 20 (2), lisp 10 (1), wasm 4 (1). Full census now (over 272): kit 3309, lib 1255 (before today's heals), Spiral-s-ML-Library 567, spiral apps 475, fiscal 178, tictactoe 135, i574n.github 119, polyglot 54, dice 52, dir-tree-html 43, alphabet 33, blog 23, tibia 1, regex 1, x 0, html 0, singularity 0. Every tree is measured now; what's left is healing, repo by repo, owned by each repo's lane. Stage removed from vultr.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_198 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 198u32, "2026-11-04T23:40Z", "x", "2026-11-04 19:40 x: CONFIRMED + LIB RE-MEASURED (vultr, xc 5d8d0ac1621c, ONE xc call per file). spiral apps/: 475 over 272 is confirmed per file (1390 .spi: 47 hand, 1343 classify as data; same per-app split). spiral lib (live, 147 hand .spi): fully postable 47, cells 5916, OVER 272: 1296 (was 1255 at the earlier count; new lib code since), posts 8839. Top: xsd_emit 175, sm' 90, xpath_eval 89, file_system 85, date_time 66, xsd_regex 49, xml 41, parsing 40, xsd_resolve 34, json_schema 33. TRAP: a batched 'xargs ... xc postable census' read ZERO lib rows, because xargs treats the apostrophe in sm'.spi / am'.spi as an opening quote and aborts the batch. Census on Linux: one call per file (lib about 5 s, apps 47 s) or xargs -d with a newline delimiter.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_199 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 199u32, "2026-11-05T12:00Z", "x", "2026-11-05 08:0x x: HEAL ATTEMPT on lib date_time.spi STOPPED before drafting. Live ECE2A9DD6806 has 67 cells over 271 (mix spiral.cell_budget --check; weights 272 to 2765), and 21 of them are backend_switch dispatchers with 5 to 7 arms, which no split can bring under 271 (measured: one-line arms weigh about 45 each). The lib as a whole has 1315 over-budget cells, 125 of them such switches. Filed BACKEND-SWITCH-POSTABLE (team compiler) for a dispatch shape that fits one cell. Until it lands, a date_time heal would only move code into helpers without lowering the count. Also measured: lib erlang_*.spi has 0 over-budget cells (11 advisory findings: autolink 4, mention 7).")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_200 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 200u32, "2026-11-05T12:33Z", "x", "2026-11-05 08:5x x: LIB HEAL DRAFTED, lib float_text.spi (3 cells over 271 -> 0; consumer float_native.spi only). Draft %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\spiral\\POSTABLE-FLOAT-TEXT\\float_text.spi 2A4FD0168CF6 on live 8A8ACC4FA293. laid_out splits at its own branches: fixed_digits (leading_point, trailing_zeros, inner_point) and scientific_digits (exponent_text, padded_exponent). float_text_repr becomes is_special, then signed_repr, unsigned_repr, mantissa_digits and trimmed_text (nonzero_span), all := and inlined, with the same evaluation order. The 23-check test splits into 6 named tests (fixed point, long fixed point, exponents, small exponents padded, long exponents, signs/zeros/specials) over a top-level repr_check. Every check is kept, and each test keeps the original directive block (rust, fsharp, typescript, gleam, blank-separated as in live). Cell budget 0 findings, round trip canonical + fixpoint + byte-exact, 0 comment lines. PROOF rides x-next10 on the node (copied as SPIRAL:lib/spiral/float_text.spi, base 8A8ACC4FA293): lib_gate float_text.spi and float_native.spi, reported on their own lines, outside the x exit code. Then a lib_land_window land. Not on the box (100% CPU, 2 GB free at 08:4x), not on vultr (no node.js for the typescript target). Skipped decimal.spi (7 over): it is the fiscal lane's XPath/EN 16931 decimal.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_201 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 201u32, "2026-11-05T15:05Z", "x", "2026-11-05 11:1x x: LIB date_time.spi HEALED in a draft, unblocked by kit's BACKEND-SWITCH-POSTABLE land (flat backend_switch measured per arm). Draft %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\spiral\\POSTABLE-DATE-TIME\\date_time.spi D5807B495FD5 on live ECE2A9DD6806. --granular over-budget cells: 56 -> 12, and all 12 left are TEST blocks, deliberately unchanged: all 48 test blocks are byte-identical to live, so the same tests passing on the new code is the equivalence proof. Splitting tests is a later, separate change. SHAPES: (1) every multi-line backend arm became a typed := helper (inlined, e.g. format_gleam, format_lua, min_value_lua, date_time_milliseconds_rust, gleam_add_days), so every switch is flat ('<Backend> = \\ () => <call>', <=120 chars). (2) Shared pieces factored once: gleam_date_time (date, time_of_day, duration -> date_time, was repeated 5x), gleam_month, gleam_date, gleam_stamp, gleam_calendar_imports, gleam_duration_imports, span_seconds_float (the identical Gleam/Lua arms of hours/minutes/seconds/milliseconds), lua_midnight, millis_suffix (Lua + Python). (3) ONE RECORD PER FACT: civil_of_micros no longer repeats the days->civil math; it calls civil_of_days, whose helpers (civil_era, civil_year_of_era, civil_day_of_year, civil_month, civil_month_day) moved above it. (4) GRANULAR heals in passing: monotonic_nanoseconds' Rust arm was an inline '{ static START ...; ... }' block macro; now a global fn spiral_monotonic_nanoseconds plus a one-call macro. zig_micros_now's single global is split into a Windows fn, a Linux fn and a chooser (the comptime os.tag if stays in Zig). (5) Kept: run_target dispatchers whose arms are now one-line calls (new_time_span, span_ticks, time_span_format, guid_time_zone, get_utc_offset); get_utc_offset's F#-only interpolation of target stays inside an untyped-target helper so it renders only on F#. Checks: round trip canonical + fixpoint + byte-exact, 0 comment lines, vultr --check exit 0 on core dc49ffdc170199df (vultr's own compiler; early evidence only). PROOF (pre-registered): x-next10 on the node runs lib_gate date_time.spi twice in one job, A = live and B = the draft (RawOut rows compared). Pass = B's per-test rows identical to A's (same tests, same targets, same verdicts) and B exit = A exit. Then a lib_land_window land, base ECE2A9DD6806.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_202 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 202u32, "2026-11-05T19:46Z", "x", "2026-11-05 15:3x x: LANDED lib date_time.spi ECE2A9DD6806 -> D5807B495FD5 (the postable heal, 56 -> 12 over-budget cells; the 12 left are unchanged test blocks) and float_text.spi 8A8ACC4FA293 -> 2A4FD0168CF6 (3 -> 0). lib_land_window closed green (3 drafts round-trip, 2 consumer-used signatures unchanged, apps/spiral compiles, document_test 26 passed, type_probe 3/3 PASS, 0 respell drift; lib digest F0EC3B7F2A44E351; undo lib-land-undo\\20261105-152932). Proof: x-next10 lib gates on the node (job-1793894849167, pinned pack51 core E190545B5F30A7B4). Pre-registered date_time rule MET: A (live) and B (draft) in the same job, 103 test-target rows each, 0 differing on (file, test, target, status), all passed, both exit 0. float_text 6/6, consumer float_native 2/2.")
inl social_profiles_local () = Task ("SOCIAL-PROFILES-LOCAL", "x: Mastodon and Bluesky postability profiles plus a local stub for each in e2e, so threads are tested for free against both rule sets with no paid X calls", Story, P1, D4, 100u32, Done, "-", "team:x,x,postability,e2e,mastodon,bluesky,social", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_social_profiles_local () = Planning ("SOCIAL-PROFILES-LOCAL", Minutes 480u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_social_profiles_local_1 () = Note ("SOCIAL-PROFILES-LOCAL", 1u32, "2026-10-25T17:24Z", "x", "2026-10-25 13:24 -04:00 x: from the same user question ('x without having to pay it, like a bootstrapped bluesky/mastodon instance'). Facts checked 10-25: Mastodon's default limit is 500 characters and every link counts as 23 (docs.joinmastodon.org/user/posting). Bluesky's cap is 300 graphemes plus 3,000 bytes, and the link counting is disputed by third parties, so it is pinned by a test against a real PDS before the profile is final. Plan: postability/mastodon.json and bluesky.json next to x.json (the profile already parameterizes weights, links and limits); stubs beside e2e/stub.py speaking the Mastodon statuses API and atproto createRecord; xc gains a target per site. A real self-hosted server (GoToSocial is Linux-only and needs WSL; a Bluesky PDS is a Node service) stays under X-FEDERATION-BRIDGES (Paused), so this item needs no WSL.")
inl note_of_social_profiles_local_2 () = Note ("SOCIAL-PROFILES-LOCAL", 2u32, "2026-10-25T19:23Z", "x", "2026-10-25 15:23 -04:00 x: DESIGN (CPU-free). Today's profile format (postability/x.json) can't express either site, so a profile file alone would be silently wrong. x.json has three X-specific rules: (1) per-code-point weights by range (CJK 2); (2) X's autolinker, where BARE domains (cell.ai) become 23-weight links, hence defused dots; (3) one limit, 280. Mastodon (docs.joinmastodon.org/user/posting): 500 'characters'; a link counts 23 only when it starts with http(s)://, so bare domains are plain text and need no defusing. Bluesky: 300 graphemes plus 3,000 UTF-8 bytes; a link is a facet the poster sends, so with xc sending no facets nothing is a link and raw URL text counts by its graphemes. Proposed schema extension, with x.json unchanged in meaning: 'count': 'code_point_weight' (x) | 'grapheme' (mastodon, bluesky); 'links': {'kind': 'x_autolink' | 'scheme_only', 'weight': 23} or absent (bluesky); 'byte_limit': 3000 (bluesky); 'posting.defused_dot' absent where links need a scheme. Readers to extend, both of them: splitter.spi's weight (glyphs.plain_weight, now lib unicode.code_points; graphemes come from lib unicode.graphemes), and the compiler's XCompat section (same profile JSON), so x-compat and the dry run keep agreeing (X-COMPAT-VS-POSTABLE's rule). To confirm before the profiles are final: whether Mastodon's 500 counts graphemes or code points, and whether a mention '@user@domain' counts only '@user'. The local stubs check what xc sends; a real instance or PDS (X-FEDERATION-BRIDGES, WSL) is what confirms the counting. Order: schema + x.json round-trip test first (every x file must give identical posts), then mastodon.json + its stub, then bluesky.json + its stub.")
inl note_of_social_profiles_local_3 () = Note ("SOCIAL-PROFILES-LOCAL", 3u32, "2026-10-25T23:41Z", "x", "2026-10-25 19:41 -04:00 (written earlier under the eoie lease) x: found while sizing step 1. The splitter does NOT read postability/x.json; X's rules are hard-coded in Spiral: splitter.spi:81 post_limit () = 280, glyphs' light ranges (0-4351, 8192-8205, 8208-8223, 8242-8247 weigh 1), and the defused-dot classes at splitter.spi:22 (label_char before, letter after = the profile's before/after). Only the compiler's x-compat reads the JSON, so today the two agree because they were written to match, not because one follows the other. A second site multiplies that. So the real step 0 is ONE profile value: a Spiral record site_profile {limit : i32; count : Weighted (ranges) | Graphemes; links : XAutolink {weight; defuse_before; defuse_after} | SchemeOnly {weight} | NoLinks; byte_limit : option i32}, with x_profile (), mastodon_profile (), bluesky_profile () as Spiral constants. split_cell, posted_unit and plain_weight take the profile instead of reading globals. A Rust test then reads postability/x.json (serde_json, ///> rust -d serde_json, as wire's tests do) and asserts it equals x_profile (), field by field, so the JSON the compiler reads and the Spiral the poster runs can't drift. Gate for step 0: every x file's dry-run posts byte-identical before and after (POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS's batch dry run makes that one call), x-compat unchanged. Then each site is a constant plus a JSON file plus a stub.")
inl note_of_social_profiles_local_4 () = Note ("SOCIAL-PROFILES-LOCAL", 4u32, "2026-10-25T23:41Z", "x", "2026-10-25 19:41 -04:00 (written earlier under the eoie lease) x: step 0 DRAFTED: scratch x-simland-0214\\site-draft\\site.spi (client package, after wire). One Spiral value per site: site_profile = {name; limit; counting : CodePointWeights (ranges, default) | Graphemes; links : XAutolink (weight, defusing) | SchemeOnly weight | NoLinks; byte_limit : option i32}; x_profile () (280, the 4 light ranges else 2, XAutolink 23 with the [A-Za-z0-9_-]/[A-Za-z] defusing), mastodon_profile () (500, graphemes, scheme-only 23) and bluesky_profile () (300 graphemes, no links, 3000 bytes). Test the_x_profile_is_the_one_the_compiler_reads parses postability/x.json through wire's serde_json decoder and asserts it renders the same as x_profile (), so the compiler's JSON and the poster's Spiral rules can't drift; a second test pins the other two sites. Checked: --check exit 0 in a client package copy, x-compat 0, 0 lines over 120. Its Cells run needs the cwd fix (a rust -d cell reading ../../postability/x.json), so it waits for main's job-1792944559767. Next after landing: splitter.spi's post_limit and glyphs' light ranges read x_profile () instead of their own constants (glyphs after main's landing), then mastodon.json + a stub.")
inl note_of_social_profiles_local_5 () = Note ("SOCIAL-PROFILES-LOCAL", 5u32, "2026-10-26T08:46Z", "x", "2026-10-26 03:12 x: step 1 (stubs) LANDED unstaged: x/apps/client/e2e/social_stub.py, stdlib only (python -I), same port-file/JSONL-log contract as stub.py. mastodon: POST /api/v1/statuses (status, in_reply_to_id; JSON or form), GET/DELETE /api/v1/statuses/:id, GET .../context; 500 graphemes with every http(s) URL at 23 and bare domains as text; 422 'Validation failed: Text character limit of 500 exceeded'. bluesky: createSession (e2e.bsky.social / e2e-app-password), createRecord app.bsky.feed.post with reply root/parent, deleteRecord, getPostThread; 300 graphemes and 3,000 UTF-8 bytes, no link weighting; 400 InvalidRequest. Its grapheme counter is an independent oracle (NFC, combining marks, ZWJ/VS/skin tone/tag joins, regional-indicator pairs, CRLF, Hangul LV): --self-test 17/17, and a code-point mutant fails 7 of them. HTTP smoke (scratch social_stub_smoke.ps1): both sites post a 3-post thread, refuse 501/301 chars and a missing token, read the thread back and delete. Error texts are from the services' docs and source, not yet checked against a live server. Next: xc targets per site (post-thread --site mastodon|bluesky over site.spi's profiles), then e2e steps against these stubs.")
inl note_of_social_profiles_local_6 () = Note ("SOCIAL-PROFILES-LOCAL", 6u32, "2026-10-26T08:46Z", "x", "2026-10-26 03:35 x: step 2 (xc writes to Mastodon) DRAFTED as an anchored apply (scratch x-social-draft\\apply.ps1, rehearsed: every anchor once, no line over 120). protocol gets platform = OnX | OnMastodon; wire adds status_path/status_verb/decode_status plus path_on/verb_on/decoded_on, and X's own functions stay unchanged (tests: mastodon paths, reply body {in_reply_to_id,status}, a status reads back as created_reply); splitter adds unit_on/units_on/split_cell_on/limit_on/cell_posts_on/thread_on/weight_on, with Mastodon units weighing 1 per grapheme and never defused, limit 500, and the X names kept as OnX wrappers (test: a 308-grapheme cell with x.io is one post with no U+200B); frames exports xc_frames(name, text); write threads a target {on; via} with write_args + platform name; xc reads XC_SITE (x|mastodon, anything else exit 2), and only writes switch base (MASTODON_BASE_URL, MASTODON_ACCESS_TOKEN). Mastodon links: every http(s):// run up to the next blank weighs at least 23 (its first unit carries the shortfall; longer links keep 1 per grapheme, which overcounts safely), tests 23 for http://a.co and 31 for 'see http://a.co now'. Rehearsed copy: x-compat 0 findings in 35 files after splitting five new cells (side_of via keys_of, order carries site, oauth2_token_name, unusable, shorter tests); first node try stopped at that gate, which caught them. Proof running: node job-1793000862185 (full client build + Mastodon dry run + post-thread against social_stub.py). The draft also adds an e2e case after X's post-thread: social_stub.py mastodon on its own port, xc post-thread oauth1.spi with XC_SITE=mastodon, checks every status accepted, each replying to the previous id, no U+200B in any body, no request to the X stub, and no more posts than X's 280 split (rehearsed: e2e.ps1 parses, x-compat 0).")
inl note_of_social_profiles_local_7 () = Note ("SOCIAL-PROFILES-LOCAL", 7u32, "2026-10-26T08:46Z", "x", "2026-10-26 03:57 x: step 3 DESIGN (Bluesky), drafted only after Mastodon's proof lands, on its seams. (a) xc opens the session once: POST /xrpc/com.atproto.server.createSession with BLUESKY_IDENTIFIER and BLUESKY_APP_PASSWORD through transport, keeping accessJwt as the bearer and did as the repo; BLUESKY_BASE_URL defaults to https://bsky.social. write_args gains the repo DID, so the write crate never holds the password. (b) created_reply gains cid (X and Mastodon decode it as ''), and posting gains root : chain, because every atproto reply names root and parent {uri, cid}; a Bluesky-only writes case ThreadReply :: string * created_reply * created_reply keeps X's ReplyPost unchanged. (c) createdAt comes from the order's stamp, rendered as RFC 3339 by one host call in the crate that already has jiff (net), passed in rather than read from the clock in write, so e2e stays deterministic. (d) DeletePost on Bluesky is POST com.atproto.repo.deleteRecord {repo, collection, rkey = last uri segment}. (e) Splitter: limit 300 graphemes plus a 3,000-byte cap per post (site.spi byte_limit), no link weighting and no defusing; the byte cap needs a second budget in chunk (fits checks both). (f) e2e: the same case as Mastodon against social_stub.py bluesky, which already speaks createSession/createRecord/deleteRecord/getPostThread.")
inl note_of_social_profiles_local_8 () = Note ("SOCIAL-PROFILES-LOCAL", 8u32, "2026-10-26T08:46Z", "x", "2026-10-26 04:5x x: Mastodon PROVEN on the node (job-1793001519584, social draft applied): e2e 36/36 tutorial steps unchanged ('same', so X's behavior holds) + 5 extras, and the new case 'mastodon post-thread / 17 statuses / exit 0' passed every check (all accepted, each replying to the previous id, no U+200B, nothing sent to the X stub, no more posts than X's split). Bluesky stage A DRAFTED on top (scratch x-bluesky-draft\\apply.ps1, rehearsed with the social draft beneath): wire gains Anonymous :: \\* c. authorizer c (header_kind 'none'), anonymous_api, session {token; account}, session_body and session_of (+2 tests); transport maps Anonymous to no header; net's POST omits an empty Authorization (open_post_builder), so createSession sends no credentials header. Rehearsal: x-compat 0 in 35 files, no line over 120, wire/transport/net/write compile to Rust (exit 0). Next stages: B records (createRecord/deleteRecord paths and bodies, cid + root on created_reply, ThreadReply), C splitter limit 300 + byte weight, D xc session + env, E e2e against social_stub.py bluesky.")
inl note_of_social_profiles_local_9 () = Note ("SOCIAL-PROFILES-LOCAL", 9u32, "2026-10-26T09:16Z", "x", "2026-10-26 05:16 x: Bluesky stages B-E DRAFTED on the Mastodon seams (scratch x-bluesky-draft\\apply_b.ps1 and apply_d.ps1, stacked on social + stage A). B: protocol platform gains OnBluesky : bluesky_writer {repo; at}; writes gains ThreadReply (text, root, parent), which X and Mastodon map to their reply; created_reply gains cid (ref_uri/ref_cid next to it); createRecord/deleteRecord bodies are built in Spiral from json_string/member/object (wire's helpers moved up to one home before first use), and the record-type key is assembled at runtime, which keeps a dollar-type literal (an X cashtag) out of the source. C: splitter weighs a Bluesky unit max(1, ceil(bytes/10)) against 300, so a full post is at most 300 graphemes and 3,000 bytes. D: the write crate opens one session per thread (createSession with BLUESKY_IDENTIFIER/BLUESKY_APP_PASSWORD read from the environment, so the secret never crosses write_args), each post's createdAt is stamp+k rendered RFC 3339 in pure Spiral (civil-from-days; test: 0, 1792999879 -> 2026-10-26T07:31:19Z, 951782400 -> 2000-02-29), xc accepts XC_SITE=bluesky with BLUESKY_BASE_URL (default https://bsky.social) and requires the app password. preview became platform-aware too (xc_preview(site, text); the price line only for x, so --dry-run shows each site's own split). E: e2e Bluesky case against social_stub.py bluesky (one session, all records 200, every reply names its parent and the thread root, no U+200B, nothing sent to the X stub). Rehearsed stacked: x-compat 0 in 35 files, no line over 120, wire/splitter/write/frames/preview/xc compile to Rust. Node proof queued: job-1793006265288 (all layers + net heal, full client build with both social e2e cases, then every notebook but protocol).")
inl note_of_social_profiles_local_10 () = Note ("SOCIAL-PROFILES-LOCAL", 10u32, "2026-10-26T10:29Z", "x", "2026-10-26 06:29 x: Mastodon slice PROVEN on the node: notebooks job-1793004081457 passed 30 of 30 (every client notebook but protocol, whose 5 zig rows are main's lib fix), with the social draft and the net.spi granular heal applied. Together with proof5's e2e (36/36 tutorial steps 'same', mastodon post-thread 17 statuses with every check green), the slice is ready to land. It lands with the Bluesky layers through scratch x-bluesky-draft\\land_social.ps1 (7 layers, 12 files, undo-able) once Bluesky's proof job-1793006265288 is green.")
inl note_of_social_profiles_local_11 () = Note ("SOCIAL-PROFILES-LOCAL", 11u32, "2026-10-26T10:30Z", "x", "2026-10-26 06:30 x: Mastodon slice + net.spi granular heal LANDED unstaged in x/apps/client (scratch x-bluesky-draft\\land_mastodon.ps1, 9 files changed: protocol, wire, splitter, net, frames, write, preview, xc, e2e/e2e.ps1; backup with recorded hashes, -Undo restores). Proven first: node e2e 36/36 + mastodon post-thread (proof5) and notebooks 30/30 (job-1793004081457). The Bluesky layers (A, B, D, usage, README) apply on top of this text after Bluesky's proof job-1793006265288.")
inl note_of_social_profiles_local_12 () = Note ("SOCIAL-PROFILES-LOCAL", 12u32, "2026-10-26T11:41Z", "x", "2026-10-26 07:41 x: Bluesky proof 1 (job-1793006265288) caught a real break that my rehearsal missed: RUST-FAILED x/apps/client/fetch (and refs, thread, users), TracedError 'Typecase miss' at their export lines. Cause: stage A rewrote transport bearer_of with an or-pattern mixing a payload GADT case and a nullary one (Signed _ | Anonymous); it type-checks but partial evaluation misses in every crate that reads through app_reply. Fixed with separate arms. Fetch/refs/thread/users/write/xc now compile on the stacked rehearsal, x-compat 0. The rehearsal had only compiled the edited modules; it now compiles every crate entry (lesson in memory). Rerun: job-1793014774212-x-sky-proof2, applying only Bluesky A/B/D on top of the tree, since Mastodon and the net heal are landed.")
inl note_of_social_profiles_local_13 () = Note ("SOCIAL-PROFILES-LOCAL", 13u32, "2026-10-26T15:00Z", "x", "2026-10-26 11:00 x: Bluesky proof 2 (job-1793014774212): all three layers (A, B+C, D+E) applied; notebooks 30 of 30 passed with the stack, including fetch/refs/thread/users, so the Typecase-miss fix holds. The build is VOID, not red: RUST-FAILED api with ETY-ModuleIndexFailed for unicode.code_points/is_white_space in glyphs.spi. The xsky mirror's spiral lib predated the lib unicode landing (Cells resolved a newer lib, which is why the notebooks passed). Also, the landed tree's frames crate is over the line gate (1206), so any build would fail there next. Plan: rerun after X-FRAMES-SPLIT lands, on a freshly synced mirror (spiral and x both), with sky_proof.ps1 now exiting non-zero on a failed build. land_bluesky.ps1 waits for a green build, not just notebooks.")
inl note_of_social_profiles_local_14 () = Note ("SOCIAL-PROFILES-LOCAL", 14u32, "2026-10-26T17:36Z", "x", "2026-10-26 13:36 x: Bluesky A/B/D push write to 1745 generated lines (stack measurement job-1793029013748; the gate fails at 1000), so the Bluesky landing needs a write seam no matter how X-FRAMES-SPLIT ends. Where the growth is, per function in the stacked write.rs: send_write_5 grew 56 -> 410 and send_write_19 39 -> 147 (the two writes instantiations; the Bluesky arm inlines record_verb, reply_write twice, record_of, post_fields and the member/object string builders); closure0 grew 78 -> 171 (bluesky_thread, opened, iso_of); closure4 adds 137; raw_reply gained a second instance (raw_reply_23, 50, the session request). chained stays a single Rc<dyn Fn> instance, so the posting loop is not duplicated. Step W1, already in the draft (apply_b.ps1, rehearsed): ref_json, post_fields, reply_member, record_of, post_write, reply_write, delete_write and record_verb become join points (polymorphic join points exist in lib, 10 sites). Rule, written before measuring: if W1 brings write to at most 900 with the split landed, Bluesky lands with W1. Otherwise the next seam is a bluesky crate exporting the record bodies (xc_bluesky_record(kind, repo, at, text, root, parent) -> string), called once per post, so write keeps one host call per Bluesky write. Measure write in the next node job after split proof 3 (job-1793035997388); proof 3 was already running, so its inputs were left alone.")
inl note_of_social_profiles_local_15 () = Note ("SOCIAL-PROFILES-LOCAL", 15u32, "2026-10-26T18:33Z", "x", "2026-10-26 14:33 x: W1 measured (job-1793036913087, split v2 + Bluesky A/B/D, notebooks wire/splitter/write/xc 4 of 4 ok): write 1760, xc 961. Join points on the record builders made write 15 lines larger, so W1 fails its rule. The next seam per note 14: a bluesky crate that builds the createRecord/deleteRecord bodies, one host call per write.")
inl note_of_social_profiles_local_16 () = Note ("SOCIAL-PROFILES-LOCAL", 16u32, "2026-10-26T18:53Z", "x", "2026-10-26 14:53 x: rule for W1b, written before measuring. In the stacked write.rs, send_write_5 repeats the platform-by-write matrix (3 platforms x 3 created kinds) about four times, because path_on, verb_on and decoded_on in wire.spi are := and each use pastes its own match. W1b (apply_w.ps1, after B) makes those three join points. Accept W1b as part of Bluesky if write is at most 900 with Bluesky stacked. If it shrinks write but stays over 900, keep W1b (it also helps the tree's own write, 964) and take the crate seam for Bluesky. If it does not shrink write, drop it. Measured on mirror xskyw (split v2 + Bluesky A/B/D already applied).")
inl note_of_social_profiles_local_17 () = Note ("SOCIAL-PROFILES-LOCAL", 17u32, "2026-10-26T19:52Z", "x", "2026-10-26 15:52 x: W1b measured (job-1793040979807): write 1778, 18 lines more than W1's 1760; wire and write/write notebooks ok. Under its rule W1b is DROPPED (join points do not shrink the platform-by-write matrix, because each instance still carries all of it). By function, against the committed write.rs: closures +250 (bluesky_thread, opened, iso_of date math, the session request), the three matchers about +340 (path_on 125, decoded_on 122, verb_on 90 across the created/deleted instances, since ThreadReply and cid widened every arm), method +124, a second raw_reply instance +54, the record builders about +170. Only 103 lines sit inside OnBluesky arms. Note: the committed write.rs (837) is older than its source; the gate built 964 from the same tree, so the deltas are about 127 too high, but the gap to 900 stands (about 800 lines). Next round needs a crate seam. Rule, written before drafting: candidate S-A, a requests crate exporting xc_write_request (platform, write kind and fields -> path, method and body as JSON) and xc_write_decoded (platform, kind, reply doc -> created/deleted JSON), called once per post, takes the matrix and builders out of write. Candidate S-B, a bluesky crate for session, createdAt and thread start. Pick the seam where write and every new crate are at most 900 with the split landed; ties go to fewer crates.")
inl note_of_social_profiles_local_18 () = Note ("SOCIAL-PROFILES-LOCAL", 18u32, "2026-10-26T20:16Z", "x", "2026-10-26 16:16 x: write seam analysis, CPU-free, from the stacked write.rs (W1+W1b, 1778 lines, fetched from xskyw). Line budget: the created and deleted request matrices (path_on 90+35, verb_on 65+25, decoded_on 83+39), Bluesky record builders about 150, a second raw_reply instance for the session request 50, chained 136, the removal fold (method30) 62, resume_written 31, describe_failure 48, lib string_slice 88, and the dispatcher closures 171+137 (written with every mode inlined). Unused pattern-binding clones (let vN = vN.clone() for fields an arm never reads, e.g. ThreadReply's 7 fields in each of 9 arms per matrix) are 112 lines (6.3%). That's a Rust codegen item, but not enough on its own; X-DONE note 16 measured dead lets at 1.6% before the wider arms. Conclusion: write needs a seam on its own anyway. The tree's write is 964 and over 900 before Bluesky; Bluesky adds about 800. Plan, in order of fewest crates: (1) collapse the platform-by-kind matrix in wire.spi into one platform match that yields a site record (paths and decode shape), plus one builder per kind, so ThreadReply's fields bind once and not once per platform arm; (2) a requests crate (xc_write_request, xc_write_reply) holding the builders and decoders, one call per write; (3) split write by export mode (single write, thread and resume, removal) if (1)+(2) still leave write over 900. Rule for that round: write and every new crate at most 900 with the split landed and Bluesky stacked, e2e green including the Bluesky case, fewest crates wins. Blocked on a design slot after X-FRAMES-SPLIT lands, since both edit splitter-adjacent wire.spi paths.")
inl note_of_social_profiles_local_19 () = Note ("SOCIAL-PROFILES-LOCAL", 19u32, "2026-10-26T22:55Z", "x", "2026-10-26 18:54 x: fresh attribution of write with Bluesky A/B/D stacked on the landed split, today's compiler pinned (job-1793052772540): write 1745 lines, xc 961. Largest fns: send_write_5 410 and send_write_19 147 (the two result-type instances of the platform x write-kind matrix); closure0 171 (mode dispatcher); closure4 137 (RFC 3339 createdAt day arithmetic inlined in the side closure); chained_3 136; raw_reply_6/23 60+50. Inside send_write_5, each Bluesky arm is about 40 lines of nested format! calls, because the record JSON is concatenated in Spiral (member/object/quoted_text are := and inline everywhere). Mastodon's bodies are already one serde_json::json! host call each (status_body). Round B' (rule written before drafting or measuring): the Bluesky bodies become json! host calls over json values (ref_value, post_record, replying, record_of, delete_body), with the \"$type\" key kept as record_type_key () through a parenthesized json! key. member/object/quoted_text stay test-only where they were (B no longer moves them). One new test: a created post's record has $type app.bsky.feed.post and its text. Accept B' if write-SKY is at most 1595 lines (at least 150 fewer than 1745) on the same pinned compiler, AND the wire notebook passes (the two existing Bluesky tests plus the new one), AND every applied cell estimates at most 272 with 0 lines over 120. B' is step 0 of note 18's plan; the at-most-900 goal for write stays with steps 1 to 3 (site record, requests crate, split by mode). If B' misses, attribute by function before changing it.")
inl note_of_social_profiles_local_20 () = Note ("SOCIAL-PROFILES-LOCAL", 20u32, "2026-10-26T23:32Z", "x", "2026-10-26 19:31 x: B' MISSES its note 19 rule. job-1793056344554 (pinned compiler 4FD712E9E357, A + B' + D on the landed split): write 1607 lines (needed at most 1595; 138 fewer, not 150), xc 961, notebooks wire and write/write ok. Attribution: send_write is the only function that changed (557 to 419). The Bluesky bodies shrank from about 40 lines to about 10 per arm, so the json! lever is used up. What remains is structural. Each of send_write's two result-type instances runs three separate platform x write-kind matches (created instance: verb 108, path 87, decode 91; deleted instance: verb 42, path 32, decode 47), and every arm re-destructures and clones the GADT payload (seven fields for ThreadReply) even when it only picks a path string. The bound is not raised. B' stays in the draft as the body shape the next round builds on. Round S1 (note 18 step 1, rule written before drafting or measuring): destructure writes r ONCE into a plain union, outgoing = Fresh text | Answer (text, root, parent) | Removal id, via outgoing_of. X, Mastodon and Bluesky path and verb take outgoing (write_path/write_verb, status_path/status_verb, record_path/record_verb), and the platform dispatch becomes the r-free join points outgoing_path and outgoing_verb, emitted once and shared by both instances. Decoding matches the GADT once and calls the platform-only join points created_on and deleted_on (decode_write, decode_status and decode_record go away). send_write computes outgoing_of q once. Accept S1 if write is at most 1450 lines (at least 157 fewer than 1607; predicted about 1385) on the same pinned compiler, AND notebooks wire and write/write pass (tests move to Removal/Answer or the GADT entry points path_on/verb_on), AND every cell estimates at most 272 with 0 lines over 120, AND xc stays at most 1000. If S1 misses, attribute by function first. The at-most-900 goal remains with steps 2 and 3 (requests crate, split by mode).")
inl note_of_social_profiles_local_21 () = Note ("SOCIAL-PROFILES-LOCAL", 21u32, "2026-10-27T00:13Z", "x", "2026-10-26 20:13 x: S1 MISSES its note 20 rule. job-1793058365291 (pinned compiler 4FD712E9E357, A + B' + D + S1): write 1473 lines (needed at most 1450; 134 fewer than B' instead of the predicted ~220), xc 961, notebooks wire and write/write ok, every cell at most 272, 0 lines over 120. The bound is not raised. Attribution against B': send_write 419 to 91 (-328) plus method18/21/22/11/14 and smaller (-214) went away, as designed. The new shared join points cost +215: outgoing_verb 106, outgoing_path 47, created_on 38, deleted_on 24, plus renumbered methods. outgoing_path is already minimal at 47 lines (six arms of a literal plus a clone), so the Spiral-level lever is used up. Calibration: destructuring a GADT once saves the per-instance copies, but the shared r-free join points cost back about 60% of that saving. Use that ratio in the next estimate. Top functions now: closure0 171 (mode dispatcher), closure4 137 (side closure with RFC 3339 createdAt arithmetic), chained 136, raw_reply 110, outgoing_verb 106, string_slice 94, send_write 91. closure0 by kind: kinds 7/8 (post-thread) ~90 lines building sides, kind 1/2 (post/delete) ~45, kind 9 (delete-thread) ~12, kind 10 (resume) ~15. Projection for note 18's steps 2 and 3: the code every mode needs (send_write, outgoing_*, created_on/deleted_on, raw_reply, describe_failure, closure4, string_slice, the JSON list loops and their methods) is about 900 lines. So a split by mode without a requests crate would copy about 900 lines into each mode crate. Step 2 has to come first, and the requests crate itself sits near the bound unless closure4's date arithmetic moves out too (it belongs with the session/side setup, not the request path). B' and S1 both stay in the draft (apply_b.ps1 v2, apply_s1.ps1), since they are correct and smaller; the next round's rule is written against the S1 baseline of 1473.")
inl note_of_social_profiles_local_22 () = Note ("SOCIAL-PROFILES-LOCAL", 22u32, "2026-10-27T07:26Z", "x", "2026-10-27 03:26 x: round R1 (note 18 step 2, requests crate), rule written before drafting or measuring. A new crate requests (xc_requests; deps serde_json, xc_net, xc_api) owns everything that talks to a site. That is the target from primitive site args: name, base, token, OAuth keys, nonce and k, stamp, Bluesky repo, plus iso_of for createdAt; outgoing from primitive ask args: kind fresh/answer/removal, text, root and parent id+cid; outgoing_verb/outgoing_path, raw_reply, created_on/deleted_on, describe_failure (and string_slice through clipped), and the Bluesky createSession. It exports three primitive-tuple functions in the existing pages_reply style: xc_create -> (code, id, text, cid, failure), xc_delete -> (code, deleted, failure), xc_session -> (code, token, account, failure). write keeps the modes, chained posting, removal, resume and the CLI glue, and calls those exports. Its side becomes i32 -> site args, and its failures become text. Why: S1 attribution (note 21) puts about 900 lines of request code in every mode, and today's census on the base regen shows the same transport code re-emitted across crates (describe_failure 48 lines in each of 5 crates, string_slice in 11). Accept R1 if, with Bluesky A + B' + D + S1 stacked on the pinned compiler 4FD712E9E357: write <= 900 AND requests <= 900, xc <= 1000, notebooks wire, write/write and requests/requests pass, every cell estimates <= 272, and 0 lines are over 120. Landing additionally needs the full client build (gate, x-compat, cargo, e2e including the Bluesky stub). If R1 misses, attribute by function first.")
inl note_of_social_profiles_local_23 () = Note ("SOCIAL-PROFILES-LOCAL", 23u32, "2026-10-27T08:18Z", "x", "2026-10-27 04:18 x: R1 MEETS the line clauses of its note 22 rule (job-1793087640410, pinned 4FD712E9E357, A + B' + D + S1 + R1): requests 795, write 716 (from 1745), xc 962. Rehearsal: 0 cells over 272, 0 lines over 120. CORRECTION, the notebook clause is VOID for R1 and also for B' and S1 (notes 20/21): my measure scripts ran 'try { Invoke-SpiralTests ... *> $null }' and printed ok whenever nothing threw, but a failing test does not throw there. The full verify of the pre-WIP tree (verify11, job-1793085906859) proves it: wire.spi's Mastodon test a_status_reads_back_as_a_post failed (ParserErrors at wire.spi:669, a two-field record pattern 'created_reply { id; text }', which does not parse), and that test was in all three stacks. Fixed in the tree with single-field helpers reply_text/id_and_text. Per the user's 03:2x rule (WIP lives in its proper place in the repo, unstaged, from the start), the whole x WIP stack is now in C:\\home\\git\\x unstaged, rehearsed byte-identical first: Bluesky A, B', D, S1, R1 (new crate apps/client/requests), defuse wire v3 (tlds, defuse, domains), perf fixes (prelude split_on, glyphs trimmed_end) and the README section on Mastodon/Bluesky threads. Backup: %TEMP%\\x-backup-1027-0355. Generated .rs are not regenerated yet. verify12 (job-1793089097830) runs the full client and sim builds on that tree with e2e -Accept and sim -Accept first (the sim fixtures and the tutorial still show the old any-dot defusing; Cells and the client now defuse only links), then both builds plain. Its verdict decides R1's notebook clause and the reviewed accepts.")
inl note_of_social_profiles_local_24 () = Note ("SOCIAL-PROFILES-LOCAL", 24u32, "2026-10-27T12:24Z", "x", "2026-10-27 08:24 x: BUG found in review (X-SITE-E2E-REVIEW), present since stage D. xc's run_write sends every write kind through write.spi, but only post-thread (bluesky_thread) opened a Bluesky session. With XC_SITE=bluesky, a single xc post or xc delete, delete-thread and resume ran on own_side: the X user token as the Bluesky bearer, an empty repo in the record, and a 401 or a bad record. Fixed in the tree (unstaged): write.spi gets with_session (open the session once, then run the mode on that account's side) and with_site (Bluesky -> with_session, every other site -> own_side); written, thread_written and resume_of all go through with_site, and bluesky_thread is gone. e2e.ps1 gains two Bluesky checks: a refused sign-in (exit 4, no record created, the stub's 'Invalid identifier or password' named, the password never echoed, the output in the leak check) and a single post followed by xc delete of its at:// uri (both exit 0, exactly one deleteRecord, no X-stub requests). Proof: the next X-WIP-VERIFY.")
inl note_of_social_profiles_local_25 () = Note ("SOCIAL-PROFILES-LOCAL", 25u32, "2026-10-27T13:40Z", "x", "2026-10-27 09:40 x: second site gap closed (tree, unstaged). xc delete-thread (kind 9) and post-thread --resume (kind 10) read the thread through X's API (xc_thread with X_BEARER_TOKEN and the X base URL) and then write through run_write, which follows XC_SITE. Under Mastodon or Bluesky that read X for a post that does not live there. xc.spi now refuses both when XC_SITE is not x, with exit 2 and 'xc: delete-thread reads the thread through X's API, so it needs XC_SITE=x, not bluesky'. xc thread (kind 6) stays a documented X-only read. e2e checks that delete-thread under XC_SITE=bluesky exits 2 without a single X request. Reading threads per site is future work, if wanted.")
inl note_of_social_profiles_local_26 () = Note ("SOCIAL-PROFILES-LOCAL", 26u32, "2026-10-28T12:21Z", "x", "2026-10-28 08:21 x: progress corrected 55 -> 95. Everything in this item's scope is in the tree and proven by verify25 (job-1793174907446, GREEN): per-site rules in the splitter (OnMastodon, OnBluesky; a profile file alone could not express either site, note 2), the local Mastodon and Bluesky stub (apps/client/e2e/social_stub.py) started by e2e, threads posted to both and read back, the Bluesky session for every write kind, the zero-width-space check on Bluesky records, and the X-only refusals for delete-thread and resume (exit 2, no X request). The one step left is the 'for free' part in CI: social_stub.py is untracked, so CI's e2e fails at 'the Mastodon stub did not write its port' (run 40677686892). Closes at the first green CI run after the user commits apps/client/e2e/social_stub.py. Optional future work, not in scope: reading threads per site (xc thread stays X-only).")
inl note_of_social_profiles_local_27 () = Note ("SOCIAL-PROFILES-LOCAL", 27u32, "2026-10-30T02:00Z", "x", "2026-10-30T02:00Z groom: dropped the stale dep X-COMPAT-VS-POSTABLE (Done since 10-28T10:48Z) and set assignee=user, since n26's only remaining step is the user's commit of apps/client/e2e/social_stub.py, then the first green CI run. Stays Blocked 95.")
inl note_of_social_profiles_local_28 () = Note ("SOCIAL-PROFILES-LOCAL", 28u32, "2026-11-01T12:03Z", "x", "2026-11-01 08:03 x groom: the user step in n26 is MET in its ported form. social_stub.py was ported to apps/client/e2e/social_stub.ps1 (the 10-31 .py-to-.ps1 port) and the user committed it in af62b77 (10-31). Remaining: the first green CI with it. On 48bdcb2 ubuntu is green, and windows failed only on a hex.pm outage (X-DONE n). Blocked -> Active and assignee user -> agent: it closes on the next all-green CI.")
inl note_of_social_profiles_local_29 () = Note ("SOCIAL-PROFILES-LOCAL", 29u32, "2026-11-01T12:36Z", "x", "2026-11-01 08:36 x: ITEM UPGRADE (round 4). Fields: tags gain team:x; the user-batch tag is dropped, since its user step is met (af62b77). Estimate 480 stays for the record; remaining work is ~0 agent minutes. DEFINITION OF DONE: an x CI run on main where ALL jobs succeed with apps/client/e2e/social_stub.ps1 in the tree (the Mastodon and Bluesky tutorial steps pass inside the client e2e). Artifact: the run id on this item. NEXT STEP: watch the next push's ci run (48bdcb2's windows red was a hex.pm outage). TYPEFEATURESET PLAN: n/a for the stub (PowerShell test double). The client side already models platforms as a union (splitter.spi platform = OnX | OnMastodon | OnBluesky with per-platform limits), which the profiles feed.")
inl note_of_social_profiles_local_30 () = Note ("SOCIAL-PROFILES-LOCAL", 30u32, "2026-11-01T20:56Z", "x", "2026-11-01 16:56 x: still 95%. The next all-green CI depends on X-DONE n86's fix: CI on 08a49e2 and bcbca2d is red on both OSes with 'No .NET 11 SDK found', because sim builds before client and didn't provision .NET. The fix is apps/sim/build.ps1 calling Ensure-SpiralCompiler, unstaged and waiting for the user's commit and push. Those reds stop before the e2e, so they say nothing about social_stub.ps1. The client's Mastodon and Bluesky steps passed in x-verify47's e2e today (17 Bluesky records, post/delete 0/0, refused cases 4 and 2), and 'accepted 0 of 37'. Typefeatureset update: the platforms are now prototypes on nominals (on_x, on_mastodon, on_bluesky: cell_limit 280/500/300) with one runtime match in rules_of (X-SOTA-MODEL n4).")
inl note_of_social_profiles_local_31 () = Note ("SOCIAL-PROFILES-LOCAL", 31u32, "2026-11-02T12:24Z", "x", "2026-11-02 08:24 x: GROOM: the last 5% has a home here, CELLS-IN-SPIRAL n5's S4: the sim serves Mastodon statuses (social_stub.ps1's first family) through sim_serve, typed by a 'platform p' prototype, with parity against social_stub. Then apps/client/e2e/stub_http.ps1 and social_stub.ps1 retire (stub_http stays only because social_stub dot-sources it). Proof: the x verify rule plus a parity record against the stub.")
inl note_of_social_profiles_local_32 () = Note ("SOCIAL-PROFILES-LOCAL", 32u32, "2026-11-03T12:39Z", "x", "2026-11-03 08:39 x: DONE per the n29 definition. ARTIFACT: x CI run 41598090220 on main 2fe525a (11-03 07:42Z), all 4 jobs success (build ubuntu-24.04, build windows-2025, xc-windows, push). apps/client/e2e/social_stub.ps1 is in that tree (blob d06ebbac). Both OS e2e logs show: mastodon post-thread 11 statuses exit 0, bluesky post-thread 11 records exit 0, bluesky refused sign-in exit 4, bluesky post and delete 0/0, bluesky delete-thread refused exit 2, then RUST-OK 37 tutorial steps and 5 extra cases. The sim-served Mastodon follow-up (the stubs retire into sim_serve) is tracked as CELLS-IN-SPIRAL n5 S4, not here.")
inl eoie_backend_reach () = Task ("EOIE-BACKEND-REACH", "eoie reach <file.spi> --backend <name> [--lib <dir>]: the lib definitions a Spiral file reaches through the arms that backend specializes (the Delphi arm, else _, of a run_target; that key of a backend_switch), listing each one with no arm for the backend and the call chain to it; a non-unit backend_switch without the key emits a backend_switch_has_no_<B>_arm placeholder that fails the build, so this finds build breaks before a compile", TaskKind, P2, D3, 0u32, Planned, "EOIE-AFFECTED-CRATES", "kit,spiral,backends,tooling,team:kit", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_eoie_backend_reach () = Planning ("EOIE-BACKEND-REACH", Minutes 120u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_eoie_backend_reach_1 () = Note ("EOIE-BACKEND-REACH", 1u32, "2026-10-25T17:29Z", "fiscal", "Prototype measured 2026-10-25 by fiscal (session scratchpad delphi-survey/reach.py, about 100 lines of Python): on fiscal's generated_ksef_fa_3 against the Delphi backend it reached 45 lib definitions and found 14 gaps on the tree lib, 0 with the drafted Delphi slices, each with its chain (e.g. sm'.replace <- xml.escape_text_xml <- xml.write_node). Limits of the prototype: name resolution is textual (qualified names plus :< opens), and the |core- package is not walked. A typed version in eoie would read the package graph like EOIE-AFFECTED-CRATES does.")
inl note_of_eoie_backend_reach_2 () = Note ("EOIE-BACKEND-REACH", 2u32, "2026-10-25T22:20Z", "fiscal", "19:1x fiscal: a blind spot measured. The prototype missed sm'.ends_with reached through file_system's (</>) operator: its reference regex is word-based, so operator definitions and uses (</>, ++#, |>) are never followed. The real Delphi build found it (backend_switch_has_no_Delphi_arm_in_lib_spiral in the path join). A typed eoie reach must resolve operator names as references.")
inl iggy_multi_machine () = Task ("IGGY-MULTI-MACHINE", "Apache Iggy as the multi-machine event log of EOIE-REMOTE-EXECUTOR: one Spiral GADT message model (job, lease, slot, health events) compiled to Rust and Gleam, Rust as the data plane (iggy SDK producer over TCP, the job runner on each machine), Gleam as the control plane (supervised coordinator consuming over Iggy HTTP, placement), byte-identical codecs from both backends; reopened from IGGY-EVAL because its first revisit trigger fired (a second machine consuming events live)", Story, P0, D6, 40u32, Planned, "REMOTE-LAPTOP-LANE", "iggy,remote,gleam,rust,typefeatureset,eoie-team,kit,team:kit,kit", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_iggy_multi_machine () = Planning ("IGGY-MULTI-MACHINE", Minutes 900u32, Load Heavy, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_iggy_multi_machine_1 () = Note ("IGGY-MULTI-MACHINE", 1u32, "2026-10-25T17:52Z", "main", "2026-10-25 13:52 main: plan with pre-written rules. Facts now: latest is still server-0.9.0 (2026-09-18, gh release list), no 1.0; the release has no binary assets; core/server builds on compio, which has an IOCP driver on Windows, so native Windows is unverified, not ruled out (IGGY-EVAL note 2). No Erlang/Elixir SDK: Gleam talks HTTP (port 3000), Rust uses the iggy crate over TCP 8090. M1 (120 m, heavy, on the laptop): cargo build --release of tag server-0.9.0 natively on Windows, start it with a disposable data dir, round-trip 10,000 messages from main-box over Tailscale with the iggy CLI or bench. Rule M1: build ok AND server up AND 10,000/10,000 received in order with 0 errors -> the server lives on the laptop, single node, and M2-M4 proceed; build or run fails -> record the error, no WSL (no-wsl rule), keep the SSH transport of EOIE-REMOTE-EXECUTOR and park this item until a Linux node exists (CI-OFFLOAD or a rented VM). M2 (300 m): the message model as one Spiral union family (GADT-first, typestate indices on lease/slot), codecs derived once; Rust producer in the job runner. M3 (300 m): Gleam consumer over HTTP inside the coordinator's supervision tree, offsets server-stored, at-least-once with dedup by message id. M4 (180 m): a self-checking parity sample, the same values encoded by the Rust and Gleam outputs must be byte-identical and decode back. Churn guard: nothing stored only in Iggy; every event is re-derivable from job receipts, so a pre-1.0 format break costs a fresh data dir. Split rule (Gleam/Rust balance): Rust gets the code on the per-message hot path, Gleam gets supervision, placement and retries (backend-balance).")
inl note_of_iggy_multi_machine_2 () = Note ("IGGY-MULTI-MACHINE", 2u32, "2026-10-25T18:01Z", "main", "2026-10-25 14:01 main: rule added before the M1 build runs. A source scan of server-0.9.0 predicts a Windows build failure: core/server/src/segment_recovery.rs:41 imports std::os::unix::fs::FileExt unguarded in non-test code, and core/server_common/src/fs_utils.rs:24 uses nix::fcntl::fallocate (guard not yet read). Rule M1b: run the unmodified build first and record the real errors. If every error is a unix-only std/nix call with a direct Windows equivalent (FileExt::read_at/write_at -> std::os::windows::fs::FileExt::seek_read/seek_write, fallocate -> File::set_len, getrlimit/uname diagnostics -> cfg-out) and they total at most 10 sites, carry them as one local patch file outside the repos (no upstream contact) and continue M1 with it; the round-trip then must also pass a restart-recovery check (stop the server mid-stream, restart, consumer resumes from its stored offset with 0 lost and 0 duplicated past dedup). More than 10 sites, or any error inside compio's driver or the shard runtime -> park as in rule M1.")
inl note_of_iggy_multi_machine_3 () = Note ("IGGY-MULTI-MACHINE", 3u32, "2026-10-25T18:20Z", "main", "2026-10-25 14:20 main: M1 PARKED by rule M1b (applied as written). Native Windows build of tag server-0.9.0 on the laptop (rust 1.98.0 from its rust-toolchain.toml, VS 2022 linker), three runs: (1) unmodified, 560 s: hwlocality-sys build script needs pkg-config + a system hwloc; (2) local patch 1 site, core/server/Cargo.toml routes windows to the existing musl branch (hwlocality features=[vendored], cmake build): passes; journal fails on std::os::fd (file_storage.rs:24,82, durable_storage.rs:328); patched 2 sites with std::os::windows::io::AsHandle::as_handle(..).try_clone_to_owned() -> File::from(OwnedHandle); (3) message_bus fails with 10 errors: fd_transfer.rs (std::os::unix, libc::fcntl F_DUPFD_CLOEXEC, TcpStream::as_raw_fd/from_raw_fd), transports/tcp.rs (std::os::fd, libc::shutdown SHUT_RD, PollFd as_raw_fd), socket_opts.rs (libc::SOMAXCONN). fd_transfer is the shard runtime's accepted-socket hand-off between thread-per-core shards, which the rule names as an automatic park; the count also passes 10 (3 patched + 8 here, before the server crate's known segment_recovery FileExt, fs_utils fallocate, diagnostics getrlimit/uname). Kept for a revisit: laptop C:\\home\\ext\\iggy (shallow clone of the tag) and C:\\home\\ext\\iggy-windows.patch (the 3 working sites). Consequence: the laptop transport stays SSH (i574n.github/scripts/remote.ps1, eoie job start over SSH survives the session, checked with a 45 s probe). Iggy waits for a Linux node (CI-OFFLOAD runner or a rented VM, where io_uring is native); M2 (message model) and M4 (Rust/Gleam codec parity) are transport-independent and continue inside EOIE-REMOTE-EXECUTOR, so the Iggy adapter becomes one more transport case later. Revisit triggers: a Linux node in the pool, or an iggy release whose message_bus builds on windows.")
inl note_of_iggy_multi_machine_4 () = Note ("IGGY-MULTI-MACHINE", 4u32, "2026-10-25T18:46Z", "laptop-fork", "2026-10-25 14:46 laptop fork: user asked (14:4x) whether the Linux-only Iggy calls for a SOTA WSL bootstrap on the worker hosting k3s + latest Iggy. Facts: Iggy publishes no Windows server and, since server-0.5.0, no GitHub binaries at all (gh release list; _build_rust_artifacts.yml targets x86_64/aarch64 linux gnu/musl only; Windows zips only for iggy-cli in 2025). The laptop already has WSL 3.0.3 (microsoft/WSL Latest 2026-10-09; 3.0.4 pre-release 10-20), features Microsoft-Windows-Subsystem-Linux + VirtualMachinePlatform Enabled, hypervisor present, distro 'Ubuntu' (WSL 2 format, stopped, from the 2025 setup), no .wslconfig. k3s latest v1.37.1+k3s1 (2026-09-30); it bundles containerd, so it runs the official Iggy image with no Docker. GATE: the standing rule no-wsl-until-workflow-passes holds; this plan starts only when i574n.github/scripts/workflow.ps1 passes or the user lifts the rule for the worker node. Plan M1-WSL (laptop, heavy): (0) upgrade the Ubuntu distro to its current LTS, .wslconfig memory cap 6 GB (16 GB host), systemd=true in /etc/wsl.conf; reach ports through ssh -L from main-box (no Windows firewall change: that is the user's setting). (a) native: cargo build of the latest iggy server tag inside WSL (io_uring native), systemd unit, disposable data dir. (b) k3s v1.37.x single node + the apache/iggy image of the same version. Measure both the same way: 10,000-message round trip from main-box through the forward (0 errors, in order), restart-recovery (stop mid-stream, consumer resumes from its stored offset, 0 lost, 0 duplicated past dedup), idle RSS of the whole stack, p99 latency. Rule: choose (b) k3s when its extra idle RSS over (a) is <= 1 GB and its p99 is <= 1.2x (a)'s, because the same manifests then run unchanged on rented Linux nodes joining as k3s agents (the multi-machine goal); otherwise choose (a) and keep k3s for when a second Linux node exists. Either way M2-M4 (Spiral message model, Rust producer over TCP, Gleam consumer over HTTP, byte-identical codec parity) proceed unchanged on top. Owner after this fork retires: kit2 (remote plumbing) with the eoie team.")
inl note_of_iggy_multi_machine_5 () = Note ("IGGY-MULTI-MACHINE", 5u32, "2026-10-25T18:57Z", "kit2", "14:57 kit2: user LIFTED the no-WSL gate for the worker node (via the fork, 2026-10-25). Fork (i-have-a) owns M1-WSL: iterate until Iggy runs on k3s in the node's WSL (WSL 3.0.3 + Ubuntu present); scripts + k8s manifests into C:\\home\\git\\eoie (new folder, unstaged, user commits). Fork hands kit2 the endpoints (TCP 8090, HTTP 3000 via ssh -L), the credentials location (a k8s Secret, never printed) and the verify script when up. kit2 / EOIE-REMOTE-EXECUTOR owns the transport-independent typed message model + Rust/Gleam codec parity to consume it; SSH stays the job-dispatch transport, iggy becomes the event log once k3s is up.")
inl note_of_iggy_multi_machine_6 () = Note ("IGGY-MULTI-MACHINE", 6u32, "2026-10-25T20:07Z", "laptop-fork", "2026-10-25 16:07 laptop-fork: M1-WSL DONE (user lifted the WSL gate for worker nodes, 14:5x). Apache Iggy 0.9.0 (apache/iggy:0.9.0, newest stable; 0.9.1-edge.3 is pre-release) runs on k3s v1.37.1+k3s1 in the worker's WSL 3.0.3 Ubuntu 24.04.2 (kernel 6.18.54.1). Recipe: kit repo infra/k3s-iggy/ (bootstrap.sh, iggy.yaml, node-wsl.ps1, verify.ps1; staged in the session scratchpad k3s-iggy/ and applied once kit's SLATE release lease ends). Acceptance from main-box through ssh -L 127.0.0.1:13000:127.0.0.1:3000: VERIFY-OK 10,000 sent, 10,000 received, 0 duplicates, 0 missing, ordered, with a kubectl rollout restart mid-stream (consumer resumed from its server-stored offset on the local-path PVC). On-node: 10,000 sent in 0.6 s (17,253 msg/s over HTTP JSON), read back in 6.4 s. Footprint: iggy pod 132 MiB, 54m CPU idle; WSL VM 1.2 GB used of the 6 GB hard cap (.wslconfig memory=6GB swap=0); Windows sees vmmem 2.6 GB. Iggy booted 4 io_uring shards pinned to CPUs 0/2/4/6. Pitfalls fixed on the way, each now in the recipe: (1) wsl.exe passes args through a shell, so Windows backslash paths get eaten: give wslpath forward slashes; (2) a Service named iggy makes the kubelet inject IGGY_SERVICE_*/IGGY_PORT_* env vars and iggy-server refuses unknown IGGY_* vars: enableServiceLinks false; (3) 0.9 refuses wildcard binds without node.advertised_address: IGGY_NODE_ADVERTISED_ADDRESS=127.0.0.1 (clients reach the node through forwards; change it when in-cluster or LAN clients need metadata addresses); (4) hostNetwork true, because WSL localhost forwarding only sees real listening sockets, not ServiceLB iptables DNAT (servicelb and traefik disabled); (5) seccompProfile Unconfined + SYS_NICE as Iggy's compose requires for io_uring; (6) on Windows, localhost may resolve to ::1 while WSL forwards IPv4: use 127.0.0.1 everywhere; (7) a bare jsonpath={...} in PowerShell becomes a scriptblock (-encodedCommand): quote it. WSL stays up across SSH sessions with vmIdleTimeout=-1 and [general] instanceIdleTimeout=-1 (checked: Running and /ping 200 after the bring-up session ended). Open for kit2: (a) node reboot: nothing starts WSL at logon, so after a reboot run node-wsl.ps1 again (idempotent, about 12 s once installed); (b) JWT secrets are random per start (server WARN), so HTTP clients re-login after a restart; persistent secrets via config if wanted; (c) root credentials live only in secret iggy/iggy-root (48-hex password, never printed); create per-service users instead of using root in the implementation; (d) the Rust producer should use TCP 8090 through a forward (iggy crate matching server 0.9.0); the Gleam consumer uses HTTP 3000 (server.http in the Iggy repo documents every body).")
inl note_of_iggy_multi_machine_7 () = Note ("IGGY-MULTI-MACHINE", 7u32, "2026-10-25T20:26Z", "kit2", "16:26 kit2 (fork final handoff; fork now stopped): node + iggy fully mine. Iggy HTTP tokens survive restarts via k8s secret iggy/iggy-jwt (IGGY_HTTP_JWT_ENCODING/DECODING_SECRET); the Gleam consumer keeps its token across restarts (1h expiry). RISK for the codec work (not fixed, see IGGY note 7): iggy memory_pool defaults to 4 GiB > the pod's 3Gi limit - size memory_pool down or raise the pod limit before any load test. Recipe lands in kit repo infra/k3s-iggy at SLATE; tested copy C:\\home\\remote-stage\\k3s-iggy. All iggy wiring (typed codec, EOIE-REMOTE-EXECUTOR M2-M4) is post-rename.")
inl note_of_iggy_multi_machine_8 () = Note ("IGGY-MULTI-MACHINE", 8u32, "2026-10-25T20:26Z", "laptop-fork", "2026-10-25 16:26 laptop-fork: open point (b) DONE: persistent JWT. node-wsl.ps1 now creates secret iggy/iggy-jwt (64 hex, never printed) when absent; iggy.yaml feeds it to IGGY_HTTP_JWT_ENCODING_SECRET and IGGY_HTTP_JWT_DECODING_SECRET (accepted by the typed env provider: log 'value changed ... from environment variable'). verify.ps1 now fails if the token issued before the mid-stream restart is rejected after it. On-node run: VERIFY-OK 10,000 sent in 1.3 s, 10,000 received, 0 duplicates, 0 missing, ordered, 'restarted mid-stream, token survived'. New risk seen in the boot config dump, not fixed: memory_pool enabled with size 4 GiB (bucket_capacity 8192) against the pod's 3Gi memory limit, so heavy load could OOM-kill the pod. Size the pool under the limit (env name per the IGGY_<config path> rule; check typed_env_provider) or raise the limit inside the 6 GB WSL cap. Final recipe hashes (session scratchpad k3s-iggy/, identical on the node in C:\\home\\remote-stage\\k3s-iggy): bootstrap.sh 547FD2FD6ACE, iggy.yaml FCAA2FA4D793, node-wsl.ps1 F90BBEC9969F, verify.ps1 70F9B8D3F1B9. kit takes them into infra/k3s-iggy/ of its repo when its SLATE release lease ends (the folder holds first drafts until then). This fork stops here.")
inl note_of_iggy_multi_machine_9 () = Note ("IGGY-MULTI-MACHINE", 9u32, "2026-10-25T20:29Z", "eoie", "16:29 kit: handoff received from the retired worker-node fork. The repo folder infra/k3s-iggy in the kit tree holds its FIRST drafts (untracked, unstaged), written before SLATE's lease at 15:19. The tested finals are in that fork's scratchpad (session 5a6e53fb, folder k3s-iggy), with sha256 prefixes bootstrap.sh 547FD2FD6ACE, iggy.yaml FCAA2FA4D793, node-wsl.ps1 F90BBEC9969F, verify.ps1 70F9B8D3F1B9.\n- Proven per the fork: Iggy 0.9.0 on k3s v1.37.1 in WSL on the worker node, VERIFY-OK across a mid-stream restart with a persistent JWT.\n- kit's action after SLATE installs (no live lease):\n  - copy the 4 finals over the drafts and check each sha256 prefix after the copy;\n  - read each file in full first: generic (no machine names, user paths, IPs or lane names), LF, no comments;\n  - leave the folder unstaged for the user to commit.\n- If the fork's scratchpad is gone by then, the drafts stay and this note records the gap.")
inl note_of_iggy_multi_machine_10 () = Note ("IGGY-MULTI-MACHINE", 10u32, "2026-10-25T20:31Z", "eoie", "16:31 kit: review of the 4 finals (read in full; copied to kit scratch clean\\k3s-iggy-finals, sha256 prefixes matched). They meet the rules: generic, no machine names, user paths, IPs beyond loopback, or lane names; no comments beyond the sh shebang; root password and JWT secret generated on the node into k8s secrets, never stored.\nPoints the user should know before committing:\n(1) node-wsl.ps1 edits the user's global ~/.wslconfig: memory cap, swap=0, vmIdleTimeout=-1 and instanceIdleTimeout=-1. This affects every WSL distro on that machine, not just this recipe, and it runs wsl --shutdown when anything changed.\n(2) iggy.yaml runs the pod with hostNetwork true, seccompProfile Unconfined and the SYS_NICE capability: a looser sandbox, likely for Iggy's io_uring/thread affinity. Worth a deliberate yes.\n(3) bootstrap.sh checks the k3s binary's sha256 against the release's sha256sum file, but install.sh is fetched from raw.githubusercontent at the tag without a checksum.\n(4) Deviations from the proposal, fine as built: the Service is ClusterIP with hostNetwork rather than a LoadBalancer (servicelb disabled), and websocket 8092 was added.\nLands as an unstaged layer after SLATE installs.")
inl note_of_iggy_multi_machine_11 () = Note ("IGGY-MULTI-MACHINE", 11u32, "2026-10-26T05:12Z", "eoie", "01:12 eoie: lessons L9-L15 from tonight's node work (continuing kit2's L1-L8 in EOIE-REMOTE-EXECUTOR note 10), then where node plus iggy pays per project. User 10-26 01:0x: gather insights on leveraging iggy and prioritize it asap, across every project and lib.\nL9 remote proof, local accept: a deterministic heavy step proven on the node becomes a hash check here. The rename release's 234-crate regen (about 2 h under load here, 19 min on the node) became a 1-minute manifest check (260/260 sha256). Two guards made it sound: freshness (no module source newer than the regen) and a full-run fallback on any mismatch.\nL10 toolchain parity is found one failure at a time: a stale spiral.exe (2025-03-13), then no OpenSSL, then no perl for vendored openssl-sys, then the mirror deps junction. Wanted: a typed toolchain inventory per machine (versions, binary dates, env vars such as OPENSSL_*), diffed against this box before an offload: a health event on iggy.\nL11 pin verdicts to one compiler: Cells and regen honor SPIRAL_COMPILER_DLL, so shipping this box's installed DLL (core hash equal on both) makes node notebook verdicts comparable with local ones. Pairs with L7.\nL12 feedback is ssh polling today: every watcher runs remote.ps1 node run Get-Content every 2-3 min. This is the clearest iggy job: job and step events (started, step result, DONE or STOP with job id) on a topic, with watchers as consumers.\nL13 file markers are ambiguous: a watcher matched an old run's STOP line and reported a finish that had not happened. Event ids and consumer offsets remove that class.\nL14 stringly remote args break: pwsh -File turned kit_adoption,kit_agile_mutation into one string and the regen filter matched nothing. A typed message (the GADT job model) instead of a joined command line fixes the class.\nL15 one serial runner per node keeps it fed without a human between steps (markers gate each step), but it serializes; L8's concurrent slots apply here too.\nWhere it pays, by workload (from tonight's numbers plus kit2's notes):\n- kit/eoie releases: regen manifest (done), candidate notebooks on the node (in progress); release gates take node verdicts as typed events, not log greps.\n- spiral compiler matrix: -Suite all compile-only is output-identical across machines (7352 rows, kit2 16:41), so it can shard by backend over both machines, with results as events.\n- lib gates (every project): Cells notebooks once the rust runner rebuild lands (tonight).\n- tibia: Cells-app lands and scoring jobs off-box; live GUI windows stay here.\n- x and fiscal: verifies already self-serve; job events remove the polling.\nDecision (rule: the user's explicit 'prioritize asap'): IGGY-MULTI-MACHINE, EOIE-REMOTE-EXECUTOR and KIT-REMOTE-SYNC move P1 to P0. The kit-side wiring stays after KIT-RENAME (feature freeze; that release is running now). The typed message model, codecs and iggy client are generic, so they go to lib/spiral, which the freeze doesn't cover: new item LIB-IGGY-CLIENT starts now.")
inl note_of_iggy_multi_machine_12 () = Note ("IGGY-MULTI-MACHINE", 12u32, "2026-10-26T05:34Z", "eoie", "01:34 eoie: L16-L17 from the node notebook runs.\nL16 Cells resolves the spiral- lib from an ancestor folder holding spiral/lib/spiral (or deps/spiral/lib/spiral and three more forms) and only then from SPIRAL_LIB (apps/cells notebook.ex find_spiral_lib). A mirror under C:\\home\\remote has no such ancestor, so every notebook failed with 'Package .../cells-mounts/<id>/spiral is not loaded'. Also, this box's lib/spiral carries 12 uncommitted files, so the node's own checkout is not the same lib. Fix: sync spiral into a per-job mirror and set SPIRAL_LIB to its lib/spiral (node-candidate.ps1 -SpiralLib). This belongs in remote.ps1's mirror setup next to the deps junction.\nL17 a node verdict needs a positive control. The first full run reported 0/53 pass in 6 minutes, including pure model notebooks that pass here: an environment failure that looked like a verdict. Rule for node notebook runs: one notebook known to pass here runs first, and a failure stops the run as environment (proof-must-run-consumer-path).")
inl note_of_iggy_multi_machine_13 () = Note ("IGGY-MULTI-MACHINE", 13u32, "2026-10-26T06:16Z", "eoie", "02:16 eoie: L18-L19, two false verdicts caught tonight.\nL18 exit 0 is not a pass. From 00:55 to 02:07 the node's Cells rust runner crashed at startup (a -p spiral build, x's report). Every rust cell came back 'unsupported', yet the notebook runs still exited 0: my positive control passed and the tablecsv proof reported 4/4. Both are void. A node verdict now reads the run's own spiral-tests total: executed above 0 (or no tests discovered), passed equal to executed, unsupported 0; anything else is NOT-RUN. This is exactly what a typed JobStep event should carry (executed, passed, unsupported as fields), so a watcher can't confuse 'nothing ran' with 'passed'.\nL19 cargo exit 0 is not a run check either: a full-workspace build kept the crashing binary as fresh. A rebuilt tool counts only after it runs (memory one-entry-no-native-wrappers: staged build with a run check). A Toolchain event should carry the run check's result, not just version and mtime.")
inl note_of_iggy_multi_machine_14 () = Note ("IGGY-MULTI-MACHINE", 14u32, "2026-10-26T07:10Z", "eoie", "03:10 eoie: L20 the release's notebook step belongs on the node. Tonight the same RESUME candidate's 53 release notebooks ran in 29 min on the node (strict verdict, throttle 2) against about 20 in 82 min on the saturated review box, where the step holds a heavy slot for 2 h or more. Like L9's regen manifest: the node runs the notebooks against a candidate built from the same digest-verified sources with this box's compiler (core hash checked) and posts a typed verdict (per notebook: executed, passed, unsupported, failed test names). The local release accepts it after checking the candidate's source digest and the known-failures rule, and runs locally only what must touch this box (the install, live-tree probes). With iggy, the verdict is a JobStep event stream rather than a file the release has to poll.")
inl note_of_iggy_multi_machine_15 () = Note ("IGGY-MULTI-MACHINE", 15u32, "2026-10-26T08:15Z", "eoie", "04:15 eoie: L21 a re-synced mirror can keep a stale build. remote.ps1 sync extracts the tar with each file's original mtime; after a re-sync that restores older sources over a newer build, cargo's mtime fingerprint calls the old binary fresh and does not relink. Caught tonight: M4c's mirror reported 10/10 on an unfixed tree because its kit.exe was still the 01:30 build that had the route fix. Audit of tonight's other node verdicts: the arity, tablecsv and candidate strict runs never re-synced after their builds, the sugar proof compares regenerated .rs without building, and the list proof's regen changed the crate (forcing a rebuild), so all of them stand. Fix in kit's node-candidate.ps1: touch every .rs and .toml before building, and STOP if the candidate binary is older than the run's start. Proper fix belongs in remote.ps1 sync (stamp extracted files with the extraction time), owned by the compiler lane (kit2 tooling).")
inl note_of_iggy_multi_machine_16 () = Note ("IGGY-MULTI-MACHINE", 16u32, "2026-10-26T09:09Z", "eoie", "05:09 eoie: lesson L22: build on the node, fetch the binary. A review-box client needs no local build: both boxes are x86_64 Windows, so the node's target\\release exe runs here unchanged (the iggy SDK tree is ~400 crates; building it here would take a heavy slot from every lane). Applies to any node-built tool that this box only runs: build there, remote.ps1 fetch, run here. Lesson L23: a follower must tolerate the topic not existing yet; starting the watcher before the job is now the normal order, and the deadline exit (3) doubles as the negative control.")
inl note_of_iggy_multi_machine_17 () = Note ("IGGY-MULTI-MACHINE", 17u32, "2026-10-26T09:52Z", "eoie", "05:52 eoie: lesson L24: a node proof must assert the tree it ran on. The 05:0x preflight of all 111 test-phase notebooks ran 50 min on the wrong mirror. remote.ps1 declares its own -Mirror, so '-Mirror C:\\home\\remote\\preflight\\eoie' after 'job start ... pwsh -File node-candidate.ps1' bound to remote.ps1 and never reached the script, which fell back to its default mirror: a stale tree with none of the four gate fixes. Caught by the script's own first log line ('candidate on C:\\home\\remote\\candidate\\eoie'). A per-file source-hash diff then showed the stale mirror differing from this tree in exactly the four fixed files. Fixes: remote.ps1 throws when -Mirror or -Force comes with any verb but sync (tested: exit 1 with the reason; plain run unaffected). Node scripts take the path as -Tree. node-candidate gained -ExpectSources '<count> <digest>' over the src .spi files (scratch clean\\respell\\source-hashes.ps1 -Digest) and stops on a mismatch. Controls: this tree and the synced preflight and listspeed mirrors all 1207 B92853CE28A241F6; the stale mirror 1207 19E94994061E75F3. Rerun job-1793008419585-kit-preflight-055143 is the first proof run wrapped by node-publish-job.ps1, watched from this box by iggy events (review-box-follow.ps1), with the log watcher kept as a cross-check.")
inl note_of_iggy_multi_machine_18 () = Note ("IGGY-MULTI-MACHINE", 18u32, "2026-10-26T13:03Z", "eoie", "09:03 eoie: lesson L25: on the node, chain proofs in one serial-runner job, not --after links. I had queued 6 proofs as an --after chain, each with a 2-3 h cap. kit's cap counts queue time, so heal-wave2 reached 2.1 h of its 2 h while still queued and lint-verb 1.6 h, and neutral-expect was running with 30 min left of a 3 h cap that its queue wait had mostly used. Replaced by job-1793019819577-kit-proof-runner: one job (12 h cap) running scratch clean\\iggy-m2\\node-serial-runner.ps1 over a JSON plan (proof-steps.json). Each step still goes through node-publish-job.ps1 under its old label, so the review-box followers already waiting on those topics needed no change: the event layer decouples watchers from how jobs are grouped. Also: a node script value that starts with '-' (e.g. a smoke command line) can be read as a parameter name by pwsh -File; node-candidate now takes ~ for a leading dash.")
inl note_of_iggy_multi_machine_19 () = Note ("IGGY-MULTI-MACHINE", 19u32, "2026-10-26T13:44Z", "tibia-lane", "13:44Z 13:4xZ tibia: today's node lessons, L26 to L31.\nL26 A gate mirror's lib is refreshed whole, never one file. Copying one live date_time.spi (post-process-split, uses erlang_term) into a pre-split mirror made every Cells run that loaded date_time fail with 'ETY-UnboundVariable: erlang_term', and 3 gates came back void. Fix: zip lib/spiral without target, _build, .cache and node_modules (3 MB, against 1,147 files of slow per-file scp -r), Expand-Archive on the node, robocopy /MIR, then hash-compare key files with the source box.\nL27 A node GREEN is imported only with its bases. Lib files moved under queued slices (listm', file_system), so a stale GREEN would have landed on a different base. The verdict file now carries `bases file=hash;...`, and the gate kit re-gates when a base moved.\nL28 Weak GREEN: when the node baseline fails the very tests a slice touches (rust -d chrono hours, minutes, seconds and time_span round trips; broad zig and gleam rows), the rule 'every candidate red is red on the baseline' passes trivially. Read the baseline rows and re-gate where the toolchain works. Node toolchain gaps today: rust -d chrono time tests, several zig and gleam rows, sm' and date_time gates with 0 test rows (still undiagnosed).\nL29 Notebook probes of variants run one at a time on the node. Three parallel run_notebook calls collided on the Cells compiler daemon ('could not start the compiler on port 13905: :lock_timeout'), and 2 of 3 ran 0 tests.\nL30 WGSL on the node needs tibia's spiral-wgsl.exe plus the DX dlls; scoped by SPIRAL_WGSL_RUN per job, not installed under the shared toolchain path. Without it wgsl rows read 'unsupported' and nothing compiles, so a probe that can't see a codegen bug looks green. Long gpu_sim tests time out at the 900 s cell budget there, but codegen errors such as YFun still surface, and a negative control proves it.\nL31 Two tree-writing chains need `--after` on the job that actually produces their precondition. A predecessor that exits on its own guard ('evolve slice has not landed') releases its dependents at once, and compile windows admit 2 jobs, so they don't serialize writers (same root as L25).\nPer-project map (note 11), tibia line: lib gates (one at a time, whole-lib mirror tibia-gaps), WGSL notebook probes (tree, candidate and negative control), and the Rust par'.map sweep (GLEAM-RUST-SPLIT part 4) run on the node. Tree writes and lands stay on the review box. Candidates for iggy events: gate verdicts (name, verdict, bases, rows) as typed events, so gate-then-land consumes them instead of verdict files copied by hand.")
inl note_of_iggy_multi_machine_20 () = Note ("IGGY-MULTI-MACHINE", 20u32, "2026-10-26T15:46Z", "main", "10-26 11:46 main: L32 (main, 11:4x): per-job -Mirror copies are left on the node, and they drain its disk. Free space: 52.4 GB at 07:00, 44.2, 39.0, then 31.4 GB at 11:44, about 5 GB/h, with 13+ mirrors of about 3.2 GB each under C:\\home\\remote from jobs that had finished hours earlier (preflight, heal1/3, lint1, ctrl1, stopcli, listidx, tablecsv, arity, candidate). Removing a mirror isn't enough while new ones keep arriving: main deleted its own main-dtc (3.86 GB, after checking that no node process or job named it) and free space still fell 0.5 GB in that minute. Rule: a node job removes its own mirror when it ends; before syncing a new one, read free disk in node status. A delete-on-finish step in the per-job mirror runner, or one reused mirror per repo per lane, is the structural fix (owners told: kit, compiler). Logged in C:\\home\\remote-queue\\node-status.txt.")
inl note_of_iggy_multi_machine_21 () = Note ("IGGY-MULTI-MACHINE", 21u32, "2026-10-26T15:47Z", "main", "10-26 11:47 main: Per-project map (note 11), main line, 11:4x. On the node: read-only proofs of main's repos (dir-tree-html's root-cut and cli-args proofs ran there 10-26 00:xx, GREEN), the polyglot contract and dice contract wasm proofs once the node has the nightly-2024-07-14 wasm toolchain, and upstream-fork driver builds plus repros (posts/*), which read nothing but the fork. Kept on the review box: lib lands, installs, the samples respell apply and the accept matrix (tree writers and single-flight accepts). Candidates for iggy events: pins_check and workflows results (repo, file, identical/differs, first line), so a ref bump's propagation across the 11 org repos is one event stream instead of 11 file reads; and matrix row verdicts (id, backend, oracle agree/DISAGREE) as the accept input, replacing results.tsv file shipping between boxes.")
inl note_of_iggy_multi_machine_22 () = Note ("IGGY-MULTI-MACHINE", 22u32, "2026-10-26T15:51Z", "main", "10-26 11:51 main: correction to main's map line (n21), checked 11:5x: the node has only nightly-2026-10-18 (rustup toolchain list), not the nightly-2024-07-14 that the dice and polyglot contract builds pin for wasm32 (nearcore 1.79 compatibility, dice build.ps1 RUST-NIGHTLY-EVERYWHERE note). So contract proofs stay on the review box for now. Provisioning (rustup toolchain install nightly-2024-07-14 plus the wasm32-unknown-unknown target, about 1 GB) waits until the node's mirror pruning (L32) brings free disk back above 40 GB; it is node upkeep and the provisioner records it in node-status.txt.")
inl note_of_iggy_multi_machine_23 () = Note ("IGGY-MULTI-MACHINE", 23u32, "2026-10-26T15:52Z", "main", "10-26 11:52 main: correction to L32 (n20), from the compiler lane's check against the node job list at 11:5x: not all the listed mirrors are finished. heal*, arith*, stdstr1 and global2 map to kit's still-queued jobs (kit-heal-e3, kit-arith-literals, kit-neutral-stdstr, kit-neutral-global2), so deleting them would destroy in-flight work. The orphans with no current job are candidate (00:22), arity (00:35), listidx (01:51) and ctrl1 (07:42). The compiler lane holds only edition3-lexer (0.1 GB). The rule stands, sharpened: a mirror is removed only by its owning job (or owner), or after a check that no queued or running job, as well as no process, names it.")
inl note_of_iggy_multi_machine_24 () = Note ("IGGY-MULTI-MACHINE", 24u32, "2026-10-26T16:04Z", "main", "10-26 12:04 main: L32 resolved 12:0x: kit removed 13 finished mirrors after a node-side check (no process, no running or queued job, no runner plan names them): 32.7 GB freed, node C: 54.2 GB free. Structural fix in kit's node-serial-runner: a step's mirror is deleted right after the step when no later step uses it, and standalone jobs prune theirs when their runs end. Kept: mirrors named by live plans (sugar2, lint1, heal3) and candlib (the shared SPIRAL_LIB mirror). With disk back above 40 GB, main is provisioning nightly-2024-07-14 + wasm32 on the node for the contract proofs (n22).")
inl note_of_iggy_multi_machine_25 () = Note ("IGGY-MULTI-MACHINE", 25u32, "2026-10-26T22:04Z", "kit", "18:04 kit: four lessons from today's node proofs. L33: a 'sync -Mirror' can fail and still leave a usable-looking mirror. lint3's sync printed 'being used by another process' on a line I didn't read, kept pre-rename folders, and its proof stopped on a missing file; three queued proofs pointed at 0.01 GB mirrors left by earlier failed syncs. Rule: read the whole sync output for REMOTE-SYNC-OK, check the mirror with source-hashes -Digest on the node before queueing, and pass -ExpectSources to every proof. L34: mirrors carry the committed generated Rust, which can lag its sources. Proofs that do not regenerate a crate then fail its notebooks for reasons unrelated to the slice (table_query.rs and agile_mutation.rs before FOLLOWUP's regen: 4 identical false failures in two different slices). Rule: after a release regen, re-sync every queued mirror, and check one regenerated .rs hash against the live tree (B6DBE935438C89A3 here). L35: the node serial runner silently ran one empty step twice. A local '$steps' collided with the [string] '$Steps' parameter (PowerShell names are case-insensitive), so the plan was flattened to one string. Rule: dry-run a runner change locally with a stand-in step before shipping it. L36: slices proven one at a time can still collide when landed in sequence. A composition pass on a copy (every slice in landing order, markers ignored) found a local-only path defect in one applier. Rule: run compose-slices before any multi-slice landing.")
inl note_of_iggy_multi_machine_26 () = Note ("IGGY-MULTI-MACHINE", 26u32, "2026-10-30T23:42Z", "main", "10-30 19:4x main R3 groom (BACKLOG-DANGLING-1030): owner kit. It was invisible to 'agile next --team kit' (tagged only eoie-team, which kit's team filter does not match); tag kit added 19:4x, so kit's gate and next now hand it out. P0 per the 10-26 iggy priority.")
inl note_of_iggy_multi_machine_27 () = Note ("IGGY-MULTI-MACHINE", 27u32, "2026-10-31T04:08Z", "main", "10-31 00:08 main: heal recovered main's iggy k3s drafts from a node job (main 10-30 scratchpad, not pre-wipe): C:\\home\\recovery\\wipe-20261028\\from-node\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\C--home-git\\5a6e53fb-7ff8-4098-adad-000b9fa45742\\scratchpad\\k3s-iggy (iggy.yaml: namespace, local-path PVC, server; node-wsl.ps1; verify.ps1, a send/receive count check over HTTP with a restart option). An identical copy is under kit's scratchpad clean\\k3s-iggy-finals. iggy is kit's broker, not one of the spiral services that spiral/deploy/k3s was decided for (CI-K3S R4), so it lands in kit's repo at kit's choice.")
inl note_of_iggy_multi_machine_28 () = Note ("IGGY-MULTI-MACHINE", 28u32, "2026-11-01T03:36Z", "kit", "10-31 23:4x kit groom (main 23:33, user: cadence/drift): Active -> PLANNED. No note in over 12 h, so nobody is working it this week; Planned keeps it in agile next at its priority (never auto-Paused, R3). First step when picked = the next step in its latest note.")
inl note_of_iggy_multi_machine_29 () = Note ("IGGY-MULTI-MACHINE", 29u32, "2026-11-01T18:09Z", "main", "main 11-01 14:1x (user: 'it seems the wipe made our iggy experiments stall, that should be a core piece for our sota ambitions considering all projects, another chance to test the current state of iggy multi node capabilities (beta/nightly builds etc)'). Back to the top of kit's queue after the SCRUM-XP slices. First step: test the newest iggy beta or nightly multi-node and cluster state on two machines (node plus Vultr VM), with the result noted here. Then IGGY-PUBLIC-CLOUD (filed today) puts it on the cloud k3s, with the WSL instance as fallback.")
inl note_of_iggy_multi_machine_30 () = Note ("IGGY-MULTI-MACHINE", 30u32, "2026-11-01T18:15Z", "kit", "11-01 14:15 kit: queued AFTER the SCRUM-XP slices (user 14:0x via main: iggy is a core piece that stalled with the wipe). Next run: the newest iggy beta/nightly in multi-node mode across the Windows node + the Linux worker vultr (remote.ps1 now drives both, REMOTE-CLOUD-LINUX-WORKER), then IGGY-PUBLIC-CLOUD (iggy on cloud k3s, outbound-only workers, WSL as fallback).")
inl note_of_iggy_multi_machine_31 () = Note ("IGGY-MULTI-MACHINE", 31u32, "2026-11-02T14:34Z", "x", "2026-11-02 10:28 x: DECISION RECORD v2 (user 11-02: 'why mine? try healing autonomously/deterministically with tests/benchs/typefeatureset/backups the pending decisions'). Only git commits (the user's standing rule) and the harness hook config (the harness itself says Claude must not change it) stay user-only. Everything else gets a precondition, a check and an undo; actions only the user's accounts can take get a non-blocking default. kind=ByTest. RULE: Accepting the finals is the agent's call under tests. PASS iff: the install.sh checksum is pinned and verified; a strict-profile smoke (no hostNetwork, default seccomp) runs, or each loosened setting has a recorded reason and a test showing the smoke fails without it; and the .wslconfig change follows DISK-CLEANUP's idle precondition. The commit itself stays the user's, as usual (leave it unstaged). BACKUP/UNDO: Undo: .wslconfig.bak; manifests unstaged. OWNER: kit.")
inl lib_gate_kit_staged_land () = Task ("LIB-GATE-KIT-STAGED-LAND", "lib-gate-kit land.ps1 lands through a stage: copy the tree lib, run the slice's ApplyScript and the formatter on the copy, then commit only the changed files with lock retries and roll back the committed ones from saved originals if any stays locked, so no apply script can leave the live lib half-landed; plus the -Target/-Live switch the landing rule asks for, so the rollback can be proven on a scratch target", TaskKind, P1, D2, 100u32, Done, "-", "lib,gates,landing,safety", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_lib_gate_kit_staged_land () = Planning ("LIB-GATE-KIT-STAGED-LAND", Minutes 90u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_lib_gate_kit_staged_land_1 () = Note ("LIB-GATE-KIT-STAGED-LAND", 1u32, "2026-10-25T17:58Z", "fiscal", "Found 2026-10-25 13:33 by fiscal: kit land.ps1 ran xsd-slices/apply.ps1 on the tree, 2 of 4 files were written before a sharing lock (PermissionError) on the 3rd, and the job exited 1 with the tree half-landed (healed by hand from work\\XSD-SLICES\\base; XSD-NAMESPACE-NAMES n6). The fiscal apply script now stages itself and its rollback is proven, but every other team's apply script still writes the tree directly through the kit. The reader guard is not enough: it sees Cells, live-gui and release readers, not every process holding a lib file open.")
inl note_of_lib_gate_kit_staged_land_2 () = Note ("LIB-GATE-KIT-STAGED-LAND", 2u32, "2026-10-25T18:08Z", "main", "10-25 14:08 main: LANDED in the kit's land.ps1 (%TEMP%\\lib-gate-kit), so every team's apply is covered with no change to their scripts. Before the apply, land.ps1 snapshots every lib .spi/.spir and package.spiproj (land-snapshot\\<name>-<stamp>) with its hash. If the apply exits non-zero, Restore-Lib puts back every changed or deleted file, removes the files the apply added, and re-hashes everything. It prints 'every lib file matches its pre-land hash: True', or names the files to restore by hand. New -LibRoot (default: the tree lib; the reader guard runs only for the tree) makes it testable. Positive control on a scratch lib: an apply that rewrote a.spi, added new.spi and exited 1 left the lib byte-identical (a.spi 702895F0 before and after, new.spi removed, the other 2 files unchanged). Not covered: an apply that skips a locked file and still exits 0 (fiscal's catch [IO.IOException] trap); that needs the apply to exit non-zero. Next: DISK-CLEANUP's gate-work rule also covers land-snapshot (delete after the land's verdict file and a 'landed <name>' log line).")
inl note_of_lib_gate_kit_staged_land_3 () = Note ("LIB-GATE-KIT-STAGED-LAND", 3u32, "2026-10-25T18:10Z", "fiscal", "14:1x fiscal: main landed this in the kit's land.ps1 at 14:06 (snapshot of every lib .spi/.spir/package.spiproj with its hash; restore on a non-zero apply exit; prints whether every file matches its pre-land hash). One record per fact: my stage-then-commit draft is NOT landed over it. It stays as proven merge material for the two gaps snapshot-and-restore leaves, and main has the path: (1) an apply that skips a locked write and exits 0, which a staged apply plus a kit-side commit closes by construction; (2) restore copies without lock retries. The draft is session scratchpad kit-staged-land/land.ps1 with prove.ps1, taking -Target (default the tree; reader guard only for the tree). Proofs on scratch targets: (a) normal land, 3 files incl. a new one; (b) a lock mid-commit rolls back the earlier commits, exit 1, hashes identical before and after; (c) a failing apply leaves the target untouched; (d) a new file committed before a locked one is deleted on rollback.")
inl note_of_lib_gate_kit_staged_land_4 () = Note ("LIB-GATE-KIT-STAGED-LAND", 4u32, "2026-10-25T18:14Z", "main", "10-25 14:14 main: REPLACED n2's snapshot-and-restore with fiscal's stage-then-commit land.ps1, merged: kit land.ps1 1A5257FCB99DFA36 (previous kept as %TEMP%\\kit-land-merge\\land.ps1.snapshot-restore-version). The apply runs on a robocopy stage of the lib, so it never touches the tree; the kit formats the changed files on the stage and commits them with 20 x 3 s retries per copy, rolling back earlier commits if one stays locked. That closes both of n2's gaps: a skipped write that exits 0 can't half-land, and a restore no longer fails on a still-held lock. Merge additions: package.spiproj is staged and committed (fiscal's filter took only .spi/.spir, so a new module's registration would not have landed), and an apply that deletes a lib file is refused with the target untouched. Proofs on scratch targets (%TEMP%\\kit-land-merge, prove.ps1 + prove-extra.ps1): (a) normal 3 files incl. a new one: exit 0; (b) locked file mid-commit: exit 1, hashes identical, the new file absent; (c) failing apply: exit 1, untouched; (e) spiproj registration lands; (f) deleting apply refused, untouched. Callers unchanged (-Name -ApplyScript). Note: an apply that ignores -Lib and writes the tree itself (x's land-apply.ps1 wrapper) still lands, but outside the stage, so the kit neither formats nor rolls it back; it relies on its own -Undo.")
inl note_of_lib_gate_kit_staged_land_5 () = Note ("LIB-GATE-KIT-STAGED-LAND", 5u32, "2026-10-25T21:10Z", "main", "10-25 17:10 main: next step proposed to kit2 (it owns the node): split the kit's serial chains into a NODE gate plus a LOCAL land. gate.ps1 is read-only (it works on a lib copy in work\\<name> and writes verdict-<name>.txt); land.ps1 is the only tree writer and gates on that file. So gates can run on the output-validated node (kit2's -Suite equality: 7,352 rows, 0 changed) against a per-job spiral mirror, and only the verdict and logs come back. Trial: LIB-LIST-SUGAR-REST. Validation condition before trusting a node verdict: Cells resolves lib from the first ancestor holding deps/spiral/lib/spiral, so the gate must run inside the mirror (fiscal's trap), and the trial's verdict must match a local gate of the same slice.")
inl note_of_lib_gate_kit_staged_land_6 () = Note ("LIB-GATE-KIT-STAGED-LAND", 6u32, "2026-10-25T21:17Z", "main", "10-25 17:17 main: gate.ps1 gains -SpiralRoot (default C:\\home\\git\\spiral, so local runs are unchanged; backup gate.ps1.bak-spiralroot). It hardcoded that root in 3 places: the source lib it copies into work\\<name>, apps\\cells\\spi\\lib_gate.ps1, and apps\\cells for the livemd round trip. A node run in a per-job mirror would have silently tested the node's main clone. kit2 runs the LIB-LIST-SUGAR-REST trial with -SpiralRoot at the mirror, after mix deps.get there.")
inl note_of_lib_gate_kit_staged_land_7 () = Note ("LIB-GATE-KIT-STAGED-LAND", 7u32, "2026-10-25T21:18Z", "main", "10-25 17:18 main: node-gate trial deferred by its own validity rule. kit2 (with tibia's run) found the node's spiral CLI Rust runner missing (Cells Rust targets 'unavailable', 18 unsupported), plus mix deps.get needed per mirror. The kit's candidate-vs-baseline rule tolerates rows missing on both sides, so a node GREEN would skip Rust, the backend list-sugar touches most. Condition added before any node verdict counts: for every target in the touched files' ///> lines, the node run's unsupported/not-run count is 0; any 'unavailable' candidate row of a touched file voids the verdict. Next: kit2 provisions the node's spiral CLI Rust runner (NODE-LIB-GATE-PROVISION), then the LIB-LIST-SUGAR-REST trial.")
inl note_of_lib_gate_kit_staged_land_8 () = Note ("LIB-GATE-KIT-STAGED-LAND", 8u32, "2026-10-25T21:36Z", "main", "10-25 17:36 main: the kit's land.ps1 (shared by main, fiscal and tibia) is made rename-proof ahead of kit's respell+rename release. It resolves the CLI as the first existing of kit\\kit.exe, eoie\\kit.exe and eoie\\eoie.exe, the jobs folder as LOCALAPPDATA kit\\jobs or eoie\\jobs, and the release label as (eoie|kit)-release-. kit confirmed the receipt fields keep their names (state=, label=, id=). The same lookup went into main's cpu-check.ps1 (supervisors matched as (eoie|kit)-*.exe) and hold-queue.ps1. Smoke-tested on today's paths. Backups: *.bak-rename.")
inl note_of_lib_gate_kit_staged_land_9 () = Note ("LIB-GATE-KIT-STAGED-LAND", 9u32, "2026-10-25T21:49Z", "main", "10-25 17:49 main: node gate trial QUEUED, self-served through the dispatcher (C:\\home\\remote-queue\\pending\\main-lsr-gate-trial.json; the node is decentralized now and its Rust runner is provisioned). It stages gate.ps1, roundtrip.exs and list-sugar's apply.ps1 by scp to C:\\home\\remote-stage\\main-gate, syncs spiral into mirror main-lsr, runs under -RequireCore 826D67C69119F61E (the node's own build of 34519B92), then mix deps.get in the mirror's apps\\cells and gate.ps1 -SpiralRoot <mirror>. Results are pulled to %TEMP%\\node-gate-trial, NOT into the local kit, so no node verdict can feed a local land before validation. A node GREEN counts only if (a) the candidate logs show 0 unavailable or not-run rows for every ///> target of the 5 touched files (n7), and (b) it matches a local gate of the same slice.")
inl note_of_lib_gate_kit_staged_land_10 () = Note ("LIB-GATE-KIT-STAGED-LAND", 10u32, "2026-10-25T22:05Z", "main", "10-25 18:05 main: the node dispatcher has been down since 15:55 (dispatch.log), so the trial JSON was withdrawn and the trial self-served: remote.ps1 node sync spiral -Mirror main-lsr (REMOTE-SYNC-OK, digest 687088DB4DB8D876, 309 changed), runner staged at C:\\home\\remote-stage\\main-gate\\run.ps1, node job job-1792965902428-main-lsr-gate under -RequireCore 826D67C69119F61E. Plain job start: --heavy needs heavy.ps1 inside a spiral tree. Fetch C:\\home\\remote-stage\\main-gate when it settles; apply n7's 0-unavailable check and compare with a local gate.")
inl note_of_lib_gate_kit_staged_land_11 () = Note ("LIB-GATE-KIT-STAGED-LAND", 11u32, "2026-10-25T22:21Z", "main", "10-25 18:21 main: node trial result: GREEN in 3.7 min, and VOID by n7's rule. Every touched file printed 'no test rows ... on the tree lib either: the same red', so NO test executed on either side; only the type checks ran (0 type errors in all 5 files, a useful fact in itself). That exposed a kit bug independent of the node: when a touched file gives no rows on both sides, gate.ps1 treated it as an equal red and passed it, so a broken test environment GREENs every slice. FIXED in gate.ps1: a touched file with N > 0 test blocks (non-static) and no rows on either side makes the verdict VOID (land.ps1 needs 'GREEN '); files with no test blocks keep the old 'nothing to compare'. Audit: 2 local GREENs in the last 4 days went through that branch (bench_kit.spi, rust.spi), and both files have 0 test blocks, so they stand; no local verdict passed through the hole. Next: the node's Cells test execution produces no rows even with the Rust runner on PATH, reported to the compiler lane (node upkeep is owner-less). The trial reruns after that, with this gate.ps1.")
inl note_of_lib_gate_kit_staged_land_12 () = Note ("LIB-GATE-KIT-STAGED-LAND", 12u32, "2026-10-25T23:18Z", "main", "10-25 19:18 main: land STARVATION observed. CLI-ARGS-STYLE's gate was GREEN at 15:18, but its land waited the full 180 min on the reader guard (Cells runs in flight from several lanes, e.g. erl 62088/135512, gone since) and threw at 18:19. With 4-5 lanes gating lib through Cells all day, 2 clear samples 20 s apart rarely happen. Requeued as main-land-cli-args-3 behind the float_text land. Proposed fix, to decide by measurement: the staged commit now copies the changed files within seconds, so the guard could use a reader/writer window instead of polling. Cells gate runs hold a shared 'tree-read' window (eoie already has the class); a land takes it exclusively, waiting until-granted, and new readers queue behind it. That needs the Cells runners (lib_gate.ps1, run_notebook) to request the shared window, which touches every lane's gates: a kit/Cells change to agree with tibia, x and fiscal.")
inl note_of_lib_gate_kit_staged_land_13 () = Note ("LIB-GATE-KIT-STAGED-LAND", 13u32, "2026-10-25T23:21Z", "main", "10-25 19:21 main: read the window model (eoie_machine_windows/window_model.spi). TreeRead is lease_only: it never clashes with any window, and the PreToolUse hook blocks writes under a leased path, which is the reader half of a reader/writer lock. Missing: a writer intent that keeps NEW readers out while it waits, so a writer can't starve. Requested from kit for after KIT-RENAME (eoie source is frozen until then): a 'tree-write <path>' class. Waiting, it queues new tree-read claims on that path behind it (the existing 'an older exclusive waiter keeps newcomers out' rule); granted once current leases drain; the land commits its staged files and releases. Adoption is then: Cells gate runners claim tree-read on lib, and land.ps1 requests tree-write instead of polling erl processes. Until then, lands retry under the 180 min guard.")
inl note_of_lib_gate_kit_staged_land_14 () = Note ("LIB-GATE-KIT-STAGED-LAND", 14u32, "2026-10-25T23:40Z", "main", "10-25 19:40 main: land GAP-CATCHING rework (land.ps1), after the float_text land starved a second time (job-1792959121132, 208 min waiting on Cells runs from fiscal's test-suite and the eoie SLATE notebook checks, all reading the real lib through deps junctions). Before: wait for 2 clear samples 20 s apart, THEN robocopy+apply+format (about a minute) and commit, so both the gap needed and the race window were about a minute. Now: stage, apply, format and diff first; poll the readers every 2 s; on the first clear sample, check that every file to commit still hashes as it did at stage time (else restage, max 3 rounds), then commit (a few file copies). The race window shrinks to the commit itself, and a gap of a few seconds suffices. Waiting lines are throttled to one a minute or on a reason change. Proofs on a %TEMP% lib copy: unguarded target commits (land_probe.spi landed, exit 0, apply output kept out of the stage object); the same copy guarded via -GuardedTree stages and then waits on a real Cells reader (erl 116164) with the target untouched. The drift-restage path is not exercised by a test yet. The stopped float_text land is requeued as main-land-float-text-tight-4 behind cli-args-3, which now runs on the new land. The tree-write window from kit stays the real fix (writer preference).")
inl note_of_lib_gate_kit_staged_land_15 () = Note ("LIB-GATE-KIT-STAGED-LAND", 15u32, "2026-10-25T23:48Z", "main", "10-25 19:48 main: fixed a lost-update bug in the land, older than today's rework. The diff compared the stage with the CURRENT target after the apply and format minute, so a file another lane changed in that minute showed up as 'changed' with the stage's OLD content, and the commit would have reverted that lane's work. Now the stage-time baseline is the hash of each file as robocopy copied it (before the apply), so any target change since the copy, to a file being committed, restages (max 3 rounds). Positive control (scratchpad land-kit-drift-test.ps1, %TEMP% lib copy): a writer appends to array_dim.spi in the target during round 1's apply; round 1 reports 2 files to commit (the probe plus array_dim.spi, the revert) and restages; round 2 commits 1 file; the other writer's change is kept; exit 0. The earlier proofs (unguarded commit, guarded wait on a real reader) still hold. Exposure: main-land-cli-args-3 staged at 19:40 on the pre-fix land; its landed list must be exactly cli_args.spi + package.spiproj, else a revert happened and gets repaired from its originals folder.")
inl note_of_lib_gate_kit_staged_land_16 () = Note ("LIB-GATE-KIT-STAGED-LAND", 16u32, "2026-10-26T02:05Z", "main", "10-25 22:05 main: queue lesson (no item of its own): a job's timeout_ms counts its wait for a heavy slot too. Four post-block checks queued with 30-min caps (ci-gate-local-check, registry-planner-probe, caret-fold-probe, zed-fixtures-lib-verify) timed out at exit 124 without running a step, because both slots were held by long jobs (pack5m review, list-sugar gate). Worse, the blog split rule (90-min cap) had waited 54 min: a cap landing after its apply but before its VERDICT would kill it mid-move, skipping the revert. All waiting jobs were stopped while still slotless (no step had started: verified by the absence of 'acquired slot' in each tail) and requeued with long caps: the 5 checks at 4 h, and the blog split, console heal verify, root-cut proof and cli-args proof at 12 h, with cli-args re-chained to the new root-cut id. Rule for queueing: cap = expected run plus the queue ahead; a tree-writing job gets a cap that cannot fire inside its write window.")
inl note_of_lib_gate_kit_staged_land_17 () = Note ("LIB-GATE-KIT-STAGED-LAND", 17u32, "2026-10-26T05:37Z", "main", "10-26 01:37 main: LOST UPDATE between two of main's own lands, caught by the dir-tree heal proof's RED, now fixed with a lock. Timeline: main-land-list-sugar-rest staged at 21:56 and LIB-FS-LINK-LENGTH's land at 22:07, both from the old file_system.spi (neither had committed); both waited on the reader guard, and with 2-second polling both saw the same gap and committed at 22:11:30. Each drift check passed (the other had not written yet), both wrote file_system.spi, list-sugar wrote last, and fs-link-length's rust_is_symlink/rust_file_length vanished from the tree (the proof failed with ETY-ModuleIndexFailed: the module does not have the key rust_is_symlink). The drift check was right but unguarded: check-then-act across two processes. Fix in land.ps1: a machine-wide named mutex Global\\spiral-lib-land, taken after the reader gap and held across the drift check and the commit, released after the commit (an abandoned mutex counts as acquired); a land that waits on the lock re-checks drift once it holds it, so it restages on top of the other land. Positive control (scratchpad land-race-test.ps1): two lands of the same scratch file launched together, each appending its own marker. B committed first; A, holding the lock, reported 'the target changed under the stage: restaging', restaged and committed. Both markers survive: GREEN. Audit of every lib land since 10-25 12:00 (9 landed-file records): only file_system.spi lost an update; the other 7 files match their land hash. Repair queued: main-serial-reland-fs (the gate verdict is still GREEN; the land restages from the current file with list-sugar's change), then the dir-tree heal proof rerun. Lanes running their own land copies should take the same mutex name.")
inl note_of_lib_gate_kit_staged_land_18 () = Note ("LIB-GATE-KIT-STAGED-LAND", 18u32, "2026-10-27T23:12Z", "main", "10-27 19:11 main: Groom 10-27 19:1x: status was stale (Planned 0%) although the work landed on 10-25 (n4: main merged fiscal's stage-then-commit design into the kit's land.ps1) and was hardened since (10-26 lost-update lock fix). Every clause of the title is in the current TEMP lib-gate-kit/land.ps1 (read today): robocopy stage of the lib (New-Stage :50-54); the apply runs on the stage, never the tree (:57-58); the formatter runs on the stage's changed files (:74); commit copies only changed files with 20 retries each (:119-132); a locked file rolls back every already-committed file from the saved originals, and the target is left as before (:133-138); and a -Target parameter (default the live lib) for scratch-target proofs. Today it ran 2 clean lands (LIB-CONVERT-RADIX-REBASE 15:40, LIB-NIL-BATCH-2 17:39). Remaining before Done: a recorded rollback proof on a scratch -Target (a locked file mid-commit that leaves the target hash-equal). If one is already among n5-n17, close it; otherwise it's 15 minutes of light work. The dep on XSD-NAMESPACE-NAMES is history (fiscal's half-land found it) and no longer gates anything.")
inl note_of_lib_gate_kit_staged_land_19 () = Note ("LIB-GATE-KIT-STAGED-LAND", 19u32, "2026-10-28T11:00Z", "main", "10-28 07:00 main: DONE (main, 10-28 06:59): the rollback proof n18 asked for, run on a scratch -Target (TEMP lib-gate-retention/rollback-proof.ps1; the live lib untouched, checked). Negative: a 2-file land (cli_flags.spi and env.spi) while env.spi is held open by another process (read share, so the stage copy works but the commit write cannot): land.ps1 exited 1 with 'env.spi stayed locked: rolled back 1 committed files; the target is as before', and every target file hash-equals its pre-land snapshot. Positive control, the same land with no lock: exit 0, exactly the 2 files changed. A first attempt that held the file with FileShare.None was a flaw in the proof (it blocked the stage copy's read too, and robocopy's default retries waited indefinitely), not a land.ps1 bug, so it was stopped and redone. All parts of the title are now proven: stage copy, apply and formatter on the stage, commit only changed files with retries, rollback from saved originals, and -Target. Note: robocopy's default /R:1000000 /W:30 means a stage copy of an unreadable file hangs for days; worth bounding (/R:3 /W:2) in New-Stage as a separate small fix.")
inl note_of_lib_gate_kit_staged_land_20 () = Note ("LIB-GATE-KIT-STAGED-LAND", 20u32, "2026-10-29T12:21Z", "main", "10-29 08:3x main: DECIDED, RETIRED. The staged-land kit (gate.ps1 + land.ps1, only ever in %TEMP%) was rescued from the node's TEMP as a 10-26 03:40 copy, now in C:\\home\\data\\rescue-2026-10-28\\node-temp\\extracted\\lib-gate-kit. It lacks its 3 later changes (-Compiler process|daemon, the roundtrip.exs missing-file filter, the work-folder cleanup). Since the wipe, every lane lands through apps/cells/spi/lib_gate.ps1 (proof on a lib copy; it now exits 2 'nothing ran' on a depless mirror, per x) plus i574n.github/scripts/lib-guarded-do.ps1 (reader guard, undo by hash, gate as its own job). Rebuilding a second landing tool would break one-record-per-fact, so the rescued copy stays archived as reference only. If a feature is missing from lib-guarded-do (the -Compiler switch, the post-land cleanup), it gets added there.")
inl delphi_uses_units () = Task ("DELPHI-USES-UNITS", "codegenDelphi hardcodes the program's uses clause (uses SysUtils, Math; at spiral_compiler.fs:165652), so lib or app code cannot bring in another unit (Classes, StrUtils, or a third-party library such as ACBrNFe): collect units from a lib-side marker the way Gleam arms emit global \"import ...\" (e.g. a global whose text is \"uses <Unit>\" folds into the one uses clause, deduplicated), so a Spiral program compiled to Delphi can call a Pascal library with one host call per macro", TaskKind, P1, D3, 100u32, Done, "-", "delphi,compiler,codegen,interop", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_delphi_uses_units () = Planning ("DELPHI-USES-UNITS", Minutes 120u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_delphi_uses_units_1 () = Note ("DELPHI-USES-UNITS", 1u32, "2026-10-25T18:12Z", "fiscal", "Found 2026-10-25 by fiscal designing FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH step 3: a Spiral-generated Delphi harness calling ACBr (TACBrNFe.NotasFiscais.LoadFromString / Validar / GerarXML) needs ACBrNFe and its units in uses. Today globals land after the type section (:165657), where a uses clause is not legal Pascal.")
inl note_of_delphi_uses_units_2 () = Note ("DELPHI-USES-UNITS", 2u32, "2026-10-25T18:14Z", "fiscal", "14:2x fiscal: draft in landing form, session scratchpad delphi-uses-units/apply.py <spiral_compiler.fs> [--apply] (anchored on text, newline-aware; dry run ok on base 34519B923366CCEC at line 165665, the single Delphi program-assembly site, so there is no core-pair twin). In codegenDelphi's program text, every env global whose text starts with 'uses ' is folded into the one uses clause after SysUtils, Math (trimmed, trailing ';' dropped, deduplicated, SysUtils/Math not repeated), and those globals are not emitted again in the declaration section; every other global is untouched. A lib or app Delphi arm then brings in a unit with global \"uses StrUtils\" and calls it with one host macro. Gate when packed: the compiler's Delphi matrix rows (unchanged output for every sample, since none emits a 'uses ' global) plus one new sample, a Delphi program whose global \"uses StrUtils\" lets $'ReverseString(...)' run and print, which fails today ('identifier not found') and passes after (the regression-sample rule: one minimal sample per fixed gap). Not queued: main's PACK-5L2 compiler install holds the compiler now; this rides the next pack.")
inl note_of_delphi_uses_units_3 () = Note ("DELPHI-USES-UNITS", 3u32, "2026-10-28T14:36Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-28 (build-free read): the CODEGEN side is ALREADY IMPLEMENTED; the item's \"hardcodes uses SysUtils, Math at :165652\" claim is STALE. At spiral_compiler.fs:165734-165736 codegenDelphi collects every env.global whose text starts with \"uses \" (is_uses), strips/trims/dedups (Seq.distinct, excluding SysUtils/Math), and folds them into the single uses clause: `uses SysUtils, Math, <units>;`; non-uses globals still emit normally (:165741). So the exact mechanism the item wants (a lib-side `uses <Unit>` global folds into the one uses clause, deduplicated, the way Gleam arms emit global \"import ...\") EXISTS. Remaining (the real 2nd half): a lib-side convention to EMIT a `uses <Unit>` global from a Delphi BackendSwitch arm (so e.g. `uses ACBrNFe` reaches env.globals) + an interop sample proving a Pascal-library call compiles and runs. Progress bumped to reflect codegen-done; owner to confirm the lib-side state.")
inl note_of_delphi_uses_units_4 () = Note ("DELPHI-USES-UNITS", 4u32, "2026-10-29T18:01Z", "compiler", "10-29 14:0x compiler: codegen half was already done (n3); the regression sample fiscal landed 10-25 never compiled ('global' is unbound against the matrix's samples/core) and was scored parity on its oracle 'error' rows. Fixed and landed: !!!!Global(\"uses StrUtils;\"). Proven: Delphi 'uses SysUtils, Math, StrUtils;', -Px86_64 build, exit 7; C exit 7 with no leak; without the global FPC fails 'Identifier not found ReverseString' (the gap's own path). Pack 7's review records the 2 rows as error -> ok. Left: the lib-side convention for a Delphi BackendSwitch arm to emit 'uses <Unit>' (e.g. ACBrNFe) from lib, the fiscal harness's real use. Details: CONTRACT-REJECTION-DIAGNOSTICS census note.")
inl note_of_delphi_uses_units_5 () = Note ("DELPHI-USES-UNITS", 5u32, "2026-11-01T03:34Z", "compiler", "10-31 10-31 23:34 compiler, cadence groom (user 23:33: drift/stall check; no Active item silent >12 h): DONE: codegen half done (n3) and the regression sample fixed and landed 10-29 with !!!!Global('uses StrUtils;'); proven Delphi 'uses SysUtils, Math, StrUtils;' build exit 7 = C exit 7. Nothing left in scope.")
inl kit_remote_sync () = Task ("KIT-REMOTE-SYNC", "kit remote sync <host>: native non-git cross-machine code transport on the deterministic bundle pipeline (eoie_bundle_manifest + eoie_archive_tar_runtime); replaces the interim remote-run.ps1 after KIT-RENAME. M4 proof = byte-identical deterministic bundle with matching manifest_fingerprint on both sides. FNV-1a is accidental-drift detection only (no adversary between our own machines), not tamper-proofing. Lib-first: generic parts (deterministic archive, manifest fingerprint, budgeted extract) go into lib/spiral; kit keeps only the remote/host part.", Story, P0, D5, 0u32, Planned, "", "kit,remote,transport,bundle,lib-first,typefeatureset,wipe-redo,team:kit", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_kit_remote_sync () = Planning ("KIT-REMOTE-SYNC", Minutes 300u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_kit_remote_sync_1 () = Note ("KIT-REMOTE-SYNC", 1u32, "2026-10-25T18:15Z", "kit2", "14:15 kit2 created per kit 5 directives (kit/kit2 sync): (1) ships after KIT-RENAME; (2) FNV-1a = accidental-drift only, not tamper-proof; (3) lib-first - deterministic archive + manifest fingerprint + budgeted extract into lib/spiral, kit keeps remote/host; (4) dep of EOIE-REMOTE-EXECUTOR M4, which drops to proof wiring; (5) 950-margin gate on eoie_bundle_*/eoie_archive_* below.")
inl note_of_kit_remote_sync_2 () = Note ("KIT-REMOTE-SYNC", 2u32, "2026-10-25T18:19Z", "kit2", "14:19 kit2 lib-first partition (kit directive 3), grounded in source. ALREADY IN lib/spiral (reuse, do not duplicate): crypto.sha256 / sha256_compute_hash / hash_text for content+manifest hashing; FNV-1a exists as xsd_emit.fnv_from (offset 2166136261) with hex_text/int_text. RECOMMENDATION: the M4 proof hashes the canonical manifest text with lib crypto.sha256 (already generic, stronger, matches the validated PowerShell M4) - FNV only as an optional cheap pre-check, so bundle's 64-bit manifest_fingerprint is NOT the proof hash. MOVE TO lib/spiral (generic, new to lib; backend_switch shape + lib test blocks): canonical manifest collector (walk+normalize+dedup+reject symlink/special/unsafe/nested-zip) from eoie_bundle_manifest.collect_bundle_manifest; deterministic archive writer (canonical order, fixed timestamps) from write_deterministic_store_zip/deflate_zip; budgeted confined-link extractor with rollback from eoie_archive_tar_runtime + archive_deflate_domain.archive_extract_with_limit; verify_manifest_parity. KIT KEEPS (remote/host, not generic): ssh transport over Host laptop + Tailscale fallback, node mirror-dir lifecycle, placement/offload orchestration, the kit remote sync <host> CLI verb. So KIT-REMOTE-SYNC = lib extraction above + a thin kit remote/host crate; EOIE-REMOTE-EXECUTOR M4 just wires the proof from lib. Reuse-crate margins already clear (bundle/archive generated .rs all <= 764).")
inl note_of_kit_remote_sync_3 () = Note ("KIT-REMOTE-SYNC", 3u32, "2026-10-25T18:55Z", "kit2", "14:55 kit2: per-job-mirror design VALIDATED locally (scratchpad/mirror-test.ps1, no node). git worktree add --detach gives an isolated working tree sharing the source object store (no clone/copy), so checkout+clean+delta-apply happen in C:\\home\\remote\\<job>\\<repo> and cannot lock files in another job's tree (fixes L1 Argu.dll collision). Collector git diff --name-only HEAD (staged+unstaged) + ls-files -o --exclude-standard, deletes --diff-filter=D. Result over a repo with a modified tracked file, a STAGE-ONLY new file (git add no commit), an untracked file, and a deleted tracked file: changed set includes the staged file (x positive control PASS), src digest == mirror digest (8090D79D), staged file present in mirror, modify applied, delete gone. Next: implement -Mirror in remote.ps1 (additive, default unchanged), validate on the free node, then coordinate making it default.")
inl note_of_kit_remote_sync_4 () = Note ("KIT-REMOTE-SYNC", 4u32, "2026-10-25T19:05Z", "kit2", "15:05 kit2: per-job mirror -Mirror LANDED in remote.ps1 and VALIDATED on the node (L1 fix). sync <repo> -Mirror <job> builds an isolated git worktree at C:\\home\\remote\\<job>\\<repo> (HEAD-ensure on the source; worktree add --detach --force; checkout+clean+delta+digest in the mirror), so concurrent offloads cannot lock each other's trees. Node test: sync dice -Mirror kit2test -> worktree created, 8 changed, src digest == mirror digest 303B9BD0, REMOTE-SYNC-OK, worktree removed clean. Parse-checked. Default unchanged (no -Mirror = shared clone, back-compat); flip to always-mirror after coordinating with main. TODO: L6 compiler-core guard (verify jobs must match the synced core) lands with the determinism check that builds the correct node compiler; run/job -Mirror cwd not added yet (callers cd explicitly).")
inl note_of_kit_remote_sync_5 () = Note ("KIT-REMOTE-SYNC", 5u32, "2026-10-25T19:48Z", "kit2", "15:48 kit2: L6 guard usability fix (x feedback). -RequireCore now prefix-matches (826D67C6 or the full 826D67C69119F61E both pass; StartsWith, case-insensitive) and a real mismatch error names the full node core so it is copy-pasteable and does not read like a false staleness failure. parse-checked. x verify-remote3 passed the guard and is running on the node; tibia + fiscal re-pinged with the full core.")
inl note_of_kit_remote_sync_6 () = Note ("KIT-REMOTE-SYNC", 6u32, "2026-10-25T20:07Z", "kit2", "16:06 kit2: two sync bugs from lane offloads. (1) x: deletions left empty dirs -> cargo workspace glob apps/client/* choked on a leftover empty dir. FIXED in remote.ps1 sync: after removing deleted files, prune now-empty ancestor dirs deepest-first up to the repo root (parse-checked). (2) tibia: worktree mirrors lack gitignored deps (apps/cells/deps Hex kino+table; also deps/The-Spiral-Language for the matrix |core-), so mix/Cells fail 'Unchecked dependencies' and the matrix would miss its core. INTERIM: the offload command provisions its own deps first (mix deps.get / init) - tibia did this per mirror. PROPER follow-up: a shared node deps cache + junction into each mirror after worktree-create (fetch once, share), as a dispatcher per-job prep step or a sync -Deps <paths> junction option. Holding my -Suite saturation run until the matrix |core- deps are provisioned in the mirror (same gap).")
inl note_of_kit_remote_sync_7 () = Note ("KIT-REMOTE-SYNC", 7u32, "2026-10-30T01:59Z", "kit", "2026-10-30T01:59 kit groom: unchanged at 0% (deps KIT-RENAME). Interim path still works: i574n.github/scripts/remote.ps1 'sync ... -Mirror <name>' with REMOTE-SYNC-OK served every kit node proof on 10-29, and kit's 9 stale pre-retire mirrors are archived (WIPE-REDO-LEDGER n59). A native 'kit remote sync' stays the goal.")
inl note_of_kit_remote_sync_8 () = Note ("KIT-REMOTE-SYNC", 8u32, "2026-11-01T13:16Z", "kit", "11-01 09:1x kit groom: dropped the KIT-RENAME dep (a P0 waiting on a P1 at 75%). kit2's directive (1) 'ships after KIT-RENAME' meant the eoie->kit source freeze, which is over: new crates are born kit_* and kit_bundle_manifest / kit_archive_tar_runtime already carry kit names. The rename's remaining tail (the node's binary switch in the user's quiet window) does not touch this code. Directives (2)-(5) stand. First slice: REMOTE-EXEC-S5 builds on this item's deterministic manifest.")
inl note_of_kit_remote_sync_9 () = Note ("KIT-REMOTE-SYNC", 9u32, "2026-11-05T11:40Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-05 07:4x kit: SLICE 1 (lib-first manifest) DRAFTED, not landed: new lib module file_manifest.spi in the kit drafts store (%LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\spiral\\KIT-REMOTE-SYNC). Typed: manifest_entry {path; bytes; sha256}; manifest_change = Added|Changed|Removed of path. file_manifest root skipped = sorted relative '/' paths under root (recursive; skips directories named in skipped), each hashed with lib crypto.get_file_hash (sha256 hex, per kit2's 10-25 partition: sha256 is the proof hash, not FNV). manifest_text = '<sha256> <bytes> <path>\\n' per entry (canonical). manifest_fingerprint = crypto.hash_text of that text (the M4 'byte-identical on both sides' check). manifest_changes base target = one merge walk over two sorted manifests (ordinal compare via comp/lt_is, same order as am'.sort). Tests: a merge walk (removed a / added c / changed d), the canonical text, and a temp-dir tree (b.txt + sub/a.txt, target/ skipped, sha256 of 'x' and 'y', fingerprint stable). PROOF so far: box --check of the whole lib scratch copy with the module = 'checked package'; lib_roundtrip.exs canonical true fixpoint true (bytes unchanged). NOT YET: node lib_gate + mutant (queued behind TRAIN29, one Cells job per node), then lib_land_window -Draft file_manifest.spi=<draft> -Apply. Symlink/special-file rejection is a later slice (lib has no symlink probe yet). Slice 2 after the land: kit's thin remote crate (kit remote manifest <root> prints the fingerprint; kit remote sync <host> diffs box vs node manifests and ships Added/Changed, removes Removed).")
inl note_of_kit_remote_sync_10 () = Note ("KIT-REMOTE-SYNC", 10u32, "2026-11-05T12:02Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-05 08:0x kit: lib draft grew the reader (still unlanded): entry_of_line / manifest_of_text parse the canonical text back (path may hold spaces; any bad line -> None for the whole manifest, never a silent drop; CRLF tolerated). Test the_canonical_text_reads_back_and_rejects_a_bad_line. Box --check of the whole lib copy ok; round trip canonical true fixpoint true. Mutant for the node gate saved beside it (file_manifest.mutant.spi: lt_is -> gt_is in the merge, which must fail changes_walk_two_sorted_manifests_once). SLICE 2 DRAFTED (kit drafts store %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\kit\\KIT-REMOTE-SYNC, applies only AFTER the lib land): placement = kit_mirror, which already owns local tree comparison (mirror check). New linked crate kit_mirror_manifest exports manifest_printed ((root, skip_csv)): stdout is exactly the canonical manifest text (so two machines' outputs diff byte for byte), stderr 'kit mirror manifest ok files=N fingerprint=<sha256>'; default skips .git target node_modules _build .cache deps. kit_mirror gains CommandManifest and a typed command_of/command_ran dispatch (replaces is_check): 'kit mirror manifest <root> [--skip a,b]'. Box --check of both crates against the draft lib = checked package. CLI test a_manifest_prints_sorted_hash_size_path_lines_and_a_fingerprint (exact digests for the mirror fixture's left tree, --skip scripts -> files=2) rides the first train after the land. Apply also needs: workspace member kit_mirror_manifest, its Cargo.lock entry, kit-mirror's dependency. Slice 3: kit mirror sync <host> = run 'kit mirror manifest' on the node over ssh, manifest_of_text it, manifest_changes against the local one, ship Added/Changed, delete Removed, compare fingerprints (M4).")
inl note_of_kit_remote_sync_11 () = Note ("KIT-REMOTE-SYNC", 11u32, "2026-11-05T13:04Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-05 09:3x kit: SLICE 3a DRAFTED (drafts store, applies after the lib land with slice 2): 'kit mirror plan <ssh-alias> <local-root> <remote-root> [--skip a,b]', a dry run. It runs 'kit mirror manifest <remote-root>' on the remote over ssh (BatchMode, ConnectTimeout 12) through lib process.captured_by with stdout ONLY: the stderr summary must not reach the parser, or manifest_of_text rightly rejects the whole text. It parses the text with manifest_of_text; a typed remote_manifest = RemoteListed entries | RemoteRefused reason (ssh/remote exit, or a bad line). Then manifest_changes theirs ours prints 'add|change|remove<TAB>path' (what would make the remote match local) and the summary 'kit mirror plan in-step|drift machine=<alias> changes=N local=<fingerprint>', exit 1 on drift. kit_mirror gains CommandPlan. Box --check of both crates against the draft lib = checked package; unit tests for the ssh words and the plan line. SLICE 3b (transport, not started): tar of Added+Changed (bsdtar from System32, never Git Bash GNU tar, see memory launch-train-from-powershell-not-bash), scp, remote extract, remote removals, then re-plan must print in-step (the M4 fingerprint equality). LIB GATE (node job-1793883219578, fm1 mirror): real draft 4EC1AFBA tests 4/4, type errors 0; mutant run in progress.")
inl note_of_kit_remote_sync_12 () = Note ("KIT-REMOTE-SYNC", 12u32, "2026-11-05T13:30Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-05 09:3x kit: SLICE 1 LANDED (lib file_manifest.spi, closed green 09:15: consumer compiles, document_test 26, type_probe 4 tests, respell 0; live hash E9FC997A = proven draft; undo spiral-bin\\lib-land-undo\\20261105-091523). The first land was undone only by respell_drift: lib spells fields e::path, not e.path (memory updated). SLICES 2-3 APPLIED to the kit tree (unstaged): kit_mirror (CommandManifest|Plan|Sync|Unpack, typed command_of/skip_of dispatch, usage) + NEW linked crate kit_mirror_manifest (manifest.spi: manifest/plan; sync.spi: sync + remote unpack with removals refused outside the root, stage dir cleaned) + workspace member + Cargo.lock entries + kit-mirror's dependency. Box --check against the live lib: kit_mirror_manifest, kit_mirror, kit_cli all checked; lint 0, ratchet ok. Proofs running: node kept size run job-1793885307933 (size12 mirror, kit + spiral with the landed module), box notebooks job-1793885336127. CLI test (manifest exact digests + --skip) rides TRAIN30. DoD M4 = a real 'kit mirror sync node <box root> <node root>' ending 'kit mirror sync ok ... fingerprint=' after TRAIN30 installs on both machines.")
inl note_of_kit_remote_sync_13 () = Note ("KIT-REMOTE-SYNC", 13u32, "2026-11-05T13:33Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-05 09:3x kit: slices 2-3 PULLED BACK OUT of the tree before TRAIN30 (node size run job-1793885307933: kit_mirror_manifest sync.rs 2710 lines, kit_mirror check.rs 1099, both over the 1000 gate). Box notebooks were green (manifest.spi 1/1, sync.spi 1/1), so the logic stands, but the shape does not fit. kit_mirror restored to HEAD; the applied copy is archived at %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\archive\\kit_mirror_manifest-applied-20261105-0931; the drafts store keeps the sources. The lib module stays landed (unused, so it generates nothing). Profile: sync.rs has 122 fns / 14 enums; the lib file_system.file_delete guard alone inlines ~230 lines (delete_shape_protected 122 + get_full_path 106); unpack_printed 157, sync_printed 93, changes_from 88, packed 81. kit_mirror links fine (7 calls into the new crate) but was already near the gate, and 4 new dispatch arms tip it over. REDESIGN: (1) kit_mirror changes one line: a non-check command word goes to ONE export (kit_mirror_remote_run (), which reads argv itself), so kit_mirror grows by ~3 lines; (2) split the remote side into three linked crates under 1000: kit_mirror_manifest (manifest + plan + remote manifest read), kit_mirror_sync (pack/ship/re-plan), kit_mirror_unpack (the remote half, the only one that deletes files, so the only one paying the file_delete guard). The crossing type is an array of manifest rows (the shape kit_proof_scope_manifests proved), never the remote_manifest union.")
inl note_of_kit_remote_sync_14 () = Note ("KIT-REMOTE-SYNC", 14u32, "2026-11-05T14:22Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-05 10:2x kit: v2 APPLIED to the tree (unstaged), all six crates under the gate on node AND box (box --backend Rust single-package compiles match the node regen line for line, so box sizing is now a valid pre-check for crates without links): kit_mirror 890 (one new arm forwards any non-check command to kit_mirror_remote_run), kit_mirror_remote 499 (remote_command union, reflected words, argv dispatch, manifest/plan printing; links local/plan/unpack/sync), kit_mirror_local 694 (walk + hash + canonical text + fingerprint, export listing_of -> array_base listing {text; fingerprint; files}), kit_mirror_plan 730 (ssh read of the remote manifest + both parses + changes, export plan_of -> array_base shipment {refused; added; changed; removed; found}), kit_mirror_sync 534 (pack via System32 bsdtar with pid-named temp files and no stage directory, scp, remote unpack, re-plan; cleanup via rust_std_fs.model.remove_file, because the lib's guarded file/directory_delete alone inlined ~410 lines), kit_mirror_unpack 531 (the remote half; removal list name passed as an argument and checked as a plain relative name; removals outside the root refused). Also LANDED a lib improvement: file_manifest hashes bytes directly (read_all_bytes -> stream cursor -> crypto.hash_read) instead of crypto.get_file_hash, whose normalize_path/read_link chain cost ~200 lines per consumer (closed green 09:58, live 9FD1F34E). Node probe #1 found 15x E0433 missing sha2 in local/plan Cargo.toml (fixed: sha2 0.11.1 + lock deps; memory new-crate-external-deps-cargo-only); probe #2 job-1793888353021 running; box notebooks for the five crates job-1793887997701 running. Rides TRAIN30.")
inl note_of_kit_remote_sync_15 () = Note ("KIT-REMOTE-SYNC", 15u32, "2026-11-06T00:29Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-05 20:2x kit: TRAIN32 notebook RED on kit_mirror/mirror_cli.spi (a_manifest_prints_sorted_hash_size_path_lines_and_a_fingerprint; 2 of 3 passed): kit mirror manifest <root> answered 'mirror unknown command left'. kit_mirror_remote remote_run matched argv as _ :: _ :: word :: words, but env.command_line_arguments () is [mirror; <command>; ...] (check.spi already matches _ :: word :: ...), so word was the root. Fixed (unstaged, TRAIN33): _ :: word :: words. Same mistake as S7's argv; a scan of every crate new since the last install found no other. Vultr proof refused by its gate at 20:26 (387 MB free, load 4.8, another lane); one-token pattern fix, regen/cargo pending.")
inl note_of_kit_remote_sync_16 () = Note ("KIT-REMOTE-SYNC", 16u32, "2026-11-06T00:36Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-05 20:3x kit: the remote_run argv fix PROVEN on the box (pack52, heavy line, job-1793925012993-kit-box-probe-mirror): kit_mirror_remote 489, kit_mirror 890, unpack 531, sync 534, plan 730, local 694, cargo check ERRORS=0. Rides TRAIN33; mirror_cli runs in its notebooks.")
inl state_concurrency_sota () = Task ("STATE-CONCURRENCY-SOTA", "SOTA state concurrency for the multi-machine system via the typefeatureset (user 2026-10-25: things will only get more chaotic). Model every shared resource - node mirror, compile slot, lease, job, backlog transaction - as a typed GADT with typestate so illegal concurrent states are unrepresentable; replace coarse guards and optimistic whole-file rewrites with fine-grained typed leases. Lessons from the 2026-10-25 multi-writer episode in note 1. Governs EOIE-REMOTE-EXECUTOR and KIT-REMOTE-SYNC.", Epic, P1, D7, 0u32, Planned, "", "concurrency,typefeatureset,remote,kit,kit2,team:kit", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_state_concurrency_sota () = Planning ("STATE-CONCURRENCY-SOTA", Minutes 2040u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_state_concurrency_sota_1 () = Note ("STATE-CONCURRENCY-SOTA", 1u32, "2026-10-25T18:49Z", "kit2", "14:47 kit2 - lessons from the 2026-10-25 multi-writer node episode, each with its SOTA/typefeatureset response. L1 SHARED-CLONE COLLISION: the fork's single-flight build (job-1792953108714) failed in 10s because ReadyToRun could not delete Argu.dll.r2r, held by x's verify on the SAME C:\\home\\git clone. Fix: per-job mirror C:\\home\\remote\\<job>\\<repo>, one writer per mirror; a typed Mirror held by a Lease, never the shared clone for builds. L2 OPTIMISTIC WHOLE-FILE STARVATION: agile rename rewrites the whole 6.5MB backlog, so on a hot backlog it keeps losing the read-modify-write race to quick note-appends (state/agile.spi changed since read). Fix: fine-grained backlog transactions (per-item or append-structured) so a rename/edit does not contend with unrelated notes; a typed Txn scoped to the item it touches, not the whole file. L3 DIGEST FALSE-MATCH: sync collected files with git ls-files -m -o (-m misses STAGED files) so staged files were silently dropped AND the digest still matched because it hashed only the files it chose. Fix: the proof hashes exactly the set sent; collector = git diff --name-only HEAD (staged+unstaged) + ls-files -o + --diff-filter=D deletes; positive control: a stage-only file (git add, no worktree change) must arrive on the remote. L4 COARSE -Force GUARD: sync's running-jobs guard is per-whole-machine and -Force bypasses it. Fix: a typed per-mirror Lease with typestate (Free | Held of owner) acquired before any write, so two writers on one resource is unrepresentable; no -Force over a held lease. L5 MULTI-EDITOR THRASH: remote.ps1 edited by fork, x and kit2 within minutes. Fix: single-writer ownership + claim note now; a typed edit-lease on shared org tooling later (the eoie tree lease already does this for repos). COMMON THREAD: every failure was an UNTYPED shared resource mutated without a typed lease/typestate. typefeatureset answer: Mirror, Slot, Lease, Job, BacklogTxn as nominal types with typestate indices (Queued|Leased|Running|Settled; Free|Held), codecs derived once, illegal transitions uncompilable. Implementers: EOIE-REMOTE-EXECUTOR (Gleam coordinator slot/lease typestate), KIT-REMOTE-SYNC (mirror + exact-set digest); L2 warrants a child item for the eoie agile transaction model (eoie core, after KIT-RENAME).")
inl note_of_state_concurrency_sota_2 () = Note ("STATE-CONCURRENCY-SOTA", 2u32, "2026-10-25T18:57Z", "kit2", "14:57 kit2 - L6 STALE-COMPILER STATE (x, 2026-10-25): node Ensure-SpiralCompiler used an older INSTALLED compiler, not one built from the synced tree core (34519B92), so verifies there reported pre-5l'' findings (18 on x) while the review box 5l'' reports 0 on the same files - a FALSE result from a mismatched compiler, even though the src sync digest matched (spiral d095f13d 939FF982). The compiler is ANOTHER piece of state that must match the synced core; src-tree equality is necessary but not sufficient. Fix (typefeatureset): a verify job carries its expected core hash as a typed precondition; the node's effective compiler core is checked == expected before the run (a typed CompilerCore guard), else build from the synced core or refuse. Ties to the determinism check (build single-flight from the synced core) and the offload flow; add the guard to remote.ps1 / the offload wrapper.")
inl note_of_state_concurrency_sota_3 () = Note ("STATE-CONCURRENCY-SOTA", 3u32, "2026-10-25T19:05Z", "kit2", "15:05 kit2: L1 (shared-clone collision) ADDRESSED - per-job worktree mirror landed + validated on the node via remote.ps1 -Mirror (KIT-REMOTE-SYNC note 4). L6 (compiler-core guard) next, with the determinism check. L2 (backlog whole-file optimistic starvation) still open - needs the eoie agile transaction model after KIT-RENAME.")
inl note_of_state_concurrency_sota_4 () = Note ("STATE-CONCURRENCY-SOTA", 4u32, "2026-10-25T19:07Z", "kit2", "15:07 kit2 - L6 guard spec (from spiral apps/compiler/tmp/scripts/env.ps1 + apps/compiler/tmp/AGENTS.md: Release builds are byte-reproducible across cache folders, so a compiler built from the same source has the same SpiralCompilerCore.dll sha256 = install-by-hash). Guard: the node's EFFECTIVE compiler core = sha256 of its SpiralCompilerCore.dll (Get-SpiralCompilerDll <mode> Release on the node, Get-FileHash); the EXPECTED core = the same on the review box for the synced HEAD. A verify/compile offload runs only if node-core == review-box-core; else build the compiler on the node from the synced core (the determinism check's step 1) or refuse. Implement as a typed precondition carried by the verify job (expected core hash), checked in the offload flow before the run. This is why x's node verify was wrong (18 findings): the node's Ensure-SpiralCompiler picked a stale installed core, not one from the synced tree. Lands with the determinism check (which builds single-flight from the synced core d095f13d and compares vs the review box CDD68DC3), schedule-gated on the fork's Iggy-up signal.")
inl note_of_state_concurrency_sota_5 () = Note ("STATE-CONCURRENCY-SOTA", 5u32, "2026-10-25T19:12Z", "kit2", "15:11 kit2 - L6 CONFIRMED with hashes (read-only): node SpiralCompilerCore.dll = 850290573E560143 (C:\\Users\\alias\\AppData\\Local\\spiral-bin\\...\\single-flight\\...\\Release\\net11.0), review box = CDD68DC3EE829A67. They DIFFER, so the node's installed compiler is NOT built from the current synced core - x's 18 x-compat findings were the stale compiler's. Guard values now known: EXPECTED core = CDD68DC3EE829A67 (review box, synced HEAD); the node must reach it (build single-flight from the synced core) before any verify/compile offload is trusted. The determinism check's step-1 build produces exactly that; until then, no node verifies.")
inl note_of_state_concurrency_sota_6 () = Note ("STATE-CONCURRENCY-SOTA", 6u32, "2026-10-25T19:36Z", "kit2", "15:36 kit2 - L7 CROSS-MACHINE BUILD NON-REPRODUCIBILITY (determinism check result). Node built single-flight from the current synced source 34519b92 -> SpiralCompilerCore.dll 826D67C6; review box's installed core from the SAME source 34519b92 (installed 14:14, between the 14:09 stage and 14:20 f9f5d288) = CDD68DC3. SAME source + SAME SDK (both rc.2 11.0.100-rc.2.26504.105) -> different DLL bytes across machines. So 'Release builds byte-reproducible across cache folders' holds same-machine, not cross-machine (non-semantic metadata: assembly MVID, embedded user/paths). CONSEQUENCE: install-by-hash does NOT hold cross-machine; the determinism/L6 guard must NOT key on DLL-byte equality. REFRAME: (a) the REAL determinism proof is OUTPUT equality (-Suite compile-only row-by-row vs the review-box run), not DLL hash; (b) a verify is valid when the node's compiler is built from the CURRENT source (same F# source+SDK => same IL => same Spiral output); (c) the L6 guard keys on source-provenance - the node's current-source core 826D67C6 is its expected value for this source, caught on drift. Node compiler IS now current-source, so node verifies are valid. The byte-diff root cause (MVID/path) is compiler-lane territory; flagged to main; not a blocker.")
inl note_of_state_concurrency_sota_7 () = Note ("STATE-CONCURRENCY-SOTA", 7u32, "2026-10-25T20:08Z", "kit2", "16:08 kit2 - L8 UNDERUTILIZATION (user: node idle for minutes). node-watch shows the node at 8-32% CPU - not idle, but far from saturated: offloads are SERIAL and LIGHT (one Cells gate is ~25% of 8 cores), and node-jobs counts only eoie jobs so remote.ps1-run work is invisible (CPU is the truth signal). Saturating 8 cores needs PARALLELISM: one self-parallelizing heavy job (-Suite -Parallel sizes to cores/mem) OR concurrent dispatch up to a core/slot budget. The interim dispatcher is SERIAL -> underutilizes by design. Fix: add slots (concurrent dispatch) to the interim dispatcher, and the typed coordinator's slot/placement model (EOIE-REMOTE-EXECUTOR M3) for the durable version. node-watch should also flag UNDERUTILIZED (<~50% = cores idle), not just fully idle (<15%).")
inl note_of_state_concurrency_sota_8 () = Note ("STATE-CONCURRENCY-SOTA", 8u32, "2026-10-25T20:38Z", "kit2", "16:38 kit2 - L8 fix VALIDATED in production: concurrent offloads saturate. fiscal's GADT Cells compile (core guard matched 826D67C6, 126s) ran ALONGSIDE kit2's determinism -Suite (7352 jobs, compiled 470s) on the node - both in isolated per-job mirrors, both succeeded, node at 100% throughout (monitor confirmed). Proves per-job worktree mirrors + guard-skip-for--Mirror let multiple lanes build concurrently = the parallelism that saturates 8 cores (vs the earlier serial ~25%). The interim mechanism delivers the saturation now; the typed slot coordinator formalizes it post-rename. (Determinism output-diff of the suite vs single-flight-20261025-123908 running.)")
inl note_of_state_concurrency_sota_9 () = Note ("STATE-CONCURRENCY-SOTA", 9u32, "2026-10-25T21:24Z", "kit2", "17:24 kit2 - L9 OPERATIONAL (user: 'node idle again, sign of mismanagement'). The node idled in the GAPS between serial offloads - while kit2 read a result and wrote the next runner, the node waited (my setup latency = idle). This is L8 (serial underutilizes) recurring at the ORCHESTRATION level, not just within one run. FIX: (1) immediate - keep MULTIPLE offloads concurrent (per-job mirrors/stages + the guard-skip make it safe); node now runs rust-provision + fiscal's type-check at once. (2) structural - the dispatcher auto-feeds from a queue so the node never waits for kit2's per-job setup; lanes enqueue, slots run N concurrently. The manual one-job-at-a-time loop with setup gaps is the anti-pattern; continuous concurrent feeding (lanes self-serve in parallel + the dispatcher queue) is the discipline.")
inl note_of_state_concurrency_sota_10 () = Note ("STATE-CONCURRENCY-SOTA", 10u32, "2026-10-29T23:23Z", "main", "10-29 19:23 main, fill-all-task-fields: NO estimate basis. There are no child deps and no pre-registered milestones (notes L1-L9 are lessons, not milestones), so the estimate stays blank rather than guessed. Sizing question for the owner (kit/kit2, pair): name the first 3 typed resources to model (e.g. node mirror, compile slot, lease) with a milestone each; the estimate then follows from those. EOIE-WINDOW-ANNOUNCE n7's three window kinds (box-exclusive, compile-quiet, lib-stable) are a natural first milestone.")
inl note_of_state_concurrency_sota_11 () = Note ("STATE-CONCURRENCY-SOTA", 11u32, "2026-10-30T10:26Z", "kit", "2026-10-30T10:26 kit groom, answering main's 10-29 sizing question with pre-registered milestones, each from a real failure on 10-29/30 (estimate = sum): M1 node mirror typestate Free -> Syncing -> Ready -> InUse -> Retired, with 'retire = same-volume move' and REMOTE-SYNC-OK as transitions (tonight's 9 stale mirrors, the candlib default) - 480m. M2 compile/lib windows with reader classes, including 'copy-read' (EOIE-WINDOW-ANNOUNCE n8: a copy-only gate held fiscal's land) - 480m. M3 job lifecycle with host-held vs detached supervisor as distinct types (TRAIN5 was LOST at 01:26 because its host-held supervisor died with the caller session) - 600m. M4 backlog transaction as compare-and-set on expected old values (the guarded eoie->kit retag shape) - 480m. Total 2040m. Each milestone is done when an illegal transition fails to typecheck in a Spiral test block.")
inl note_of_state_concurrency_sota_12 () = Note ("STATE-CONCURRENCY-SOTA", 12u32, "2026-10-30T10:27Z", "kit", "2026-10-30T10:27 kit: correction to n11's M3 rationale. TRAIN5 was lost because the BOX crashed at 01:26, not because a host-held supervisor died with a healthy caller. M3 still stands (host-held vs detached supervisors are distinct lifecycles), and it gains a case: a job whose machine rebooted must surface as 'lost, tree state unverified' until a post-crash check runs. Estimate unchanged at 2040m.")
inl note_of_state_concurrency_sota_13 () = Note ("STATE-CONCURRENCY-SOTA", 13u32, "2026-11-01T13:11Z", "kit", "11-01 09:1x kit: L2 (whole-file optimistic starvation) recurred on 11-01: a rename ran 421 s on the 9.7 MB state and lost the race twice to groom-round writes (KIT-SCALE n2, escalated there as the first deliverable). L1-L6 also become the state-concurrency rubric of CODEBASE-DIAMOND: each lesson is a gauge question with a known proof shape (typed Mirror lease, per-item transactions, CompilerCore precondition).")
inl note_of_state_concurrency_sota_14 () = Note ("STATE-CONCURRENCY-SOTA", 14u32, "2026-11-01T17:20Z", "main", "main 13:3x (00:26 coverage pass): the user's scale bar lives here: 'even though we're not 50+ agent level that rewrote bun from zig to rust, we gotta have kit stable enough to support that, and we'll need it' (11-01 00:2x). Measurable DoD to add: a kit load test with 50 concurrent writers (agile note/add/set, window request/release, job start) for 10 min on one box. It passes with zero lost updates (AGILE-STATUS-LOST-UPDATES class), zero torn state files, p99 agile write latency under 5 s, and kit check green after. Run it on the node or a codespace so the box isn't saturated.")
inl fpc_trunk_everywhere () = Task ("FPC-TRUNK-EVERYWHERE", "FPC 3.3 (trunk) on every machine (user 2026-10-25 14:4x: 'shouldn't we update fpc to 3.3 everywhere?'): FPC has no 3.3 release (3.2.2 is the last stable; 3.3.1 is trunk, where new language work lands), so the toolchain is the official trunk snapshot from downloads.freepascal.org/fpc/snapshot/trunk (i386-win32 driver + utils, x86_64-win64 cross compiler + units), installed beside scoop's 3.2.2 with scoop's current junction repointed; no CI workflow installs fpc (every .github grepped), so 'everywhere' = this machine + the remote kit worker node", TaskKind, P1, D2, 100u32, Done, "-", "toolchain,delphi,fpc,upkeep", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_fpc_trunk_everywhere () = Planning ("FPC-TRUNK-EVERYWHERE", Minutes 60u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl compiler_cross_machine_repro () = Task ("COMPILER-CROSS-MACHINE-REPRO", "Compiler builds byte-identical across machines: the core DLL embeds per-user absolute paths (staged core source, PDB), so the node and this box differ (826D67C6 vs CDD68DC3) on the same source and SDK", Story, P2, D4, 100u32, Done, "PACK-7", "compiler,determinism,build,main,wipe-redo", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_compiler_cross_machine_repro () = Planning ("COMPILER-CROSS-MACHINE-REPRO", Minutes 120u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_compiler_cross_machine_repro_1 () = Note ("COMPILER-CROSS-MACHINE-REPRO", 1u32, "2026-10-25T19:43Z", "main", "10-25 15:43 main: root cause of kit2's cross-machine result (STATE-CONCURRENCY-SOTA L7: the node built 34519B92 into SpiralCompilerCore 826D67C6, this box into CDD68DC3, same source and SDK). The installed core DLL embeds exactly 2 absolute paths, both under the user profile: the staged core source C:\\Users\\<user>\\AppData\\Local\\spiral-bin\\core-src\\single-flight\\<sha16>\\spiral_compiler.fs (from F#'s signature data, which --pathmap does not rewrite; tmp/AGENTS.md says so) and the PDB path under spiral-bin\\obj. build.ps1:170 maps the private cache to the shared cache (-p:PathMap=$cache=$sharedCache), and both are per-user absolute paths. So the bytes are reproducible only within one user profile: the node's user is 'alias', this box's 'i574n'. MVID is a content hash, so it differs too. Fix options, to measure, not chosen: (1) stage the core source and the obj dir under a machine-independent root that both machines create the same (C:\\home\\git is identical on both), and PathMap that root to a fixed token like /_/spiral; (2) a fixed-path junction (C:\\spiral-cache pointing at the shared cache) used for staging and obj, plus PathMap to /_/. Gate for either: a fresh build on this box keeps install-by-hash (same hash twice), and a node build of the same source gives the identical SpiralCompilerCore sha256. Until then, cross-machine identity is proven by output equality (-Suite rows), as kit2 does.")
inl note_of_compiler_cross_machine_repro_2 () = Note ("COMPILER-CROSS-MACHINE-REPRO", 2u32, "2026-10-25T20:42Z", "main", "10-25 16:42 main: kit2's output-equality proof PASSED. The node ran -Suite all compile-only (7,352 jobs) from synced source d095f13d with its own build of core 34519B92. Against single-flight-20261025-123908, compile + residual: 0 changed, 0 added, 0 removed. So the node's compiler is behaviourally identical to this box's, and the per-user embedded paths affect only DLL bytes and the MVID, not output. This item therefore stays P2: it matters only for install-by-hash across machines, such as shipping one reviewed DLL to the node without a rebuild. The fix options in note 1 are unchanged.")
inl note_of_compiler_cross_machine_repro_3 () = Note ("COMPILER-CROSS-MACHINE-REPRO", 3u32, "2026-10-25T22:05Z", "main", "10-25 18:05 main: handed to the compiler lane (session 88a146f8, formerly kit2), agreed in the lane split: it owns the compiler core, determinism and the L7 gap; main stays the release/accept integrator. Notes 1-2 carry the diagnosis and the gate.")
inl note_of_compiler_cross_machine_repro_4 () = Note ("COMPILER-CROSS-MACHINE-REPRO", 4u32, "2026-10-25T22:41Z", "compiler", "compiler lane: airtight root cause + decision-ruled fix (diagnosis only; build/land pending a heavy slot).\n\nPROOF the sole cross-machine input is the username in the staged source path:\n- Byte-diffed node 826D67C6 vs box CDD68DC3 (host, both 12,152,832 B, same length so no shift): 277,072 differing bytes in 1,558 ranges. That looked alarming but decompresses to nothing.\n- The scattered high-entropy ranges are F# compressed metadata resources: FSharpSignatureCompressedData/B and FSharpOptimizationCompressedData/B (deflate). Decompressed FSharpOptimizationCompressedData (491,840 B) differs cross-machine by EXACTLY 5 bytes, one range, at the source-path occurrence \"unit.H`1.abC:\\Users\\{i574n|alias}\\AppData\\Local\\spiral-bin\\\". A path-free resource (OptimizationDataB) is byte-identical across machines. So deflate merely amplifies a single 5-byte username diff file-wide.\n- Only two absolute C:\\Users strings exist in the binary (strings scan): the staged .fs (F# signature/optimization data, build.ps1:158 -p:SpiralCoreSource) and the output PDB. Both vary only in the username. R2R=False on both installed artifacts, so crossgen is not involved.\n- Consistent with main notes 1-2: same-machine builds are byte-identical across cache folders (staged source lives under $sharedCache=LOCALAPPDATA\\spiral-bin, same across caches on one machine); only the username changes cross-machine.\n\nWHY pathmap alone cannot fix it:\n- F# signature/optimization metadata is NOT pathmapped; it embeds the literal path fsc was given (-p:SpiralCoreSource=$staged).\n- In a default build Get-SpiralCacheDir == $sharedCache (both LOCALAPPDATA\\spiral-bin unless SPIRAL_BIN_CACHE_DIR is set), so build.ps1:170 -p:PathMap=$cache=$sharedCache is a no-op; even when active its target is still per-user.\n\nFIX (decision rule, pre-registered):\n1. Stage the compiled .fs under a machine-independent, username-free root identical on both machines. Rule: not the per-user profile, not the git working tree (cache != repo; avoids reader-guard/Cells-live-tree hazards). Use C:\\home\\spiral-cache (sibling of C:\\home\\git, which main confirms identical on both and present on the node), env-overridable via a new SPIRAL_DET_ROOT defaulting to it. Content-addressed by sha16, so sharing across concurrent builds is safe.\n2. Do NOT relocate obj (concurrent builds from different private caches must not share one obj dir -- that is what -p:PathMap=$cache=$sharedCache protects). Instead change the pathmap TARGET to a fixed token: -p:PathMap=$cache=/_/ so the embedded PDB output path is machine-independent. PDB GUID/checksum and the PE timestamp are content-derived under deterministic build and fall into line once source path + PDB path match.\n3. Consumers to move together: build.ps1:158 ($staged root), $lastStaged, build.ps1:168 (prune), test.ps1:62 and rebuild-determinism.ps1:10 (these walk Get-SpiralCacheDir core-src -- reconcile to the new root). Grep eoie, i574n.github scripts and remote.ps1 core guard for core-src assumptions, one path at a time (brace globs fail silently).\n\nGATE (all must hold):\n- G1 pre-gate (no build): candidate DLL has zero C:\\Users strings.\n- G2: two fresh builds on this box give identical sha256 (install-by-hash preserved same-machine).\n- G3: a node build of the same synced source gives identical sha256 to this box, for BOTH artifacts (host 12MB and core 5.7MB).\n- G4: full byte-diff shows zero differing ranges.\n\nSEAM: lands as an anchored apply script + composite core hash on a stated base core into pack 5n (not a live-tree edit, not a prebuilt DLL). Lives in apps/compiler/tmp/scripts/build.ps1 -> coordinate with COMPILER-TMP-RETIRE so the fix moves with that relocation. Payoff: once landed, a blessed single-flight DLL installs on the node by hash with no rebuild, removing the apply-script-only constraint for node-bound work.\nEvidence fetched to scratch: node-826D67C6.dll (host), node-core-6C0A22AB.dll (core).")
inl note_of_compiler_cross_machine_repro_5 () = Note ("COMPILER-CROSS-MACHINE-REPRO", 5u32, "2026-10-25T23:38Z", "compiler", "Diagnosis (airtight, see eoie note 4 + the obj-shared correction below): the entire\ncross-machine byte difference of SpiralCompilerCore.dll reduces to per-user absolute\npaths embedded in F# compressed signature/optimization metadata. Proven: decompressed\nFSharpOptimizationCompressedData (491,840 B) differs node-vs-box in exactly 5 bytes =\nthe username (i574n|alias) at the staged-core path; a path-free resource is byte-identical.\nDeflate amplifies those bytes into 277 KB of DLL diff. Same-machine builds already\nbyte-reproducible across caches (AGENTS.md), so the only cross-machine input is the username.\n\n## Two embedded-path sources (both under per-user $(LOCALAPPDATA)/spiral-bin)\n1. staged core:  build.ps1:158  core-src/<mode>/<sha16>/spiral_compiler.fs  (-p:SpiralCoreSource)\n2. SDK attrs:    Directory.Build.props:15  $(SpiralSharedCacheDir)/obj-shared/<proj>/<tfm>.AssemblyAttributes.fs\n   (obj at props:16 = private $(SpiralCacheDir)/obj/... -> pathmapped, not a sig-data source)\n\n## The det root\nFixed, username-free, present on both machines, outside the git tree: `C:\\home\\spiral-cache`\n(both machines have C:\\home). Env-overridable: new `SPIRAL_DET_ROOT`. Add `Get-SpiralDetRoot`\nto spiral/apps/compiler/tmp/scripts/env.ps1 (mirror Get-SpiralCacheDir shape; default the fixed path).\n\n## Edits (3 files)\n- build.ps1:158  `$staged = Join-Path $sharedCache \"core-src/...\"`  -> Join-Path $detRoot \"core-src/...\"\n  (define `$detRoot = Get-SpiralDetRoot` near $sharedCache at the top; prune at :168 uses $detRoot too)\n- build.ps1:170  `-p:PathMap=$cache=$sharedCache`  -> `-p:PathMap=$cache=/_/`\n  (today $cache==$sharedCache so the current pathmap is a no-op; /_/ makes the raw PDB path machine-independent)\n- Directory.Build.props:15  `$(SpiralSharedCacheDir)/obj-shared/...`  -> a det-root property\n  (add `<SpiralDetRoot Condition=\"'$(SPIRAL_DET_ROOT)'!=''\">$(SPIRAL_DET_ROOT)</SpiralDetRoot>` +\n   default `C:\\home\\spiral-cache`; point TargetFrameworkMonikerAssemblyAttributesPath at $(SpiralDetRoot)/obj-shared/...)\n- test.ps1:62 + rebuild-determinism.ps1:10 walk core-src under Get-SpiralCacheDir -> reconcile to $detRoot.\n\nNOTE: lives in apps/compiler/tmp/scripts + tmp/compiler — coordinate with COMPILER-TMP-RETIRE.\nSeam: ship as an anchored apply script + composite core hash on a stated base core into pack 5n (not a DLL).\n\n## Gate (all must hold; needs a heavy slot)\n- G1 (cheap, pre-build): candidate DLL has ZERO `C:\\Users` strings (ascii+utf16 scan).\n- G2: two fresh builds on this box -> identical sha256 (install-by-hash preserved).\n- G3: a node build of the same synced source -> identical sha256, for BOTH host (12MB) and core (5.7MB).\n- G4: full byte-diff zero differing ranges, INCLUDING inside the decompressed FSharpSignatureCompressedData.\n\nThe note text with the obj-shared correction + complete fix is the message I tried to post as\nnote 5 (blocked by the tree-lease hook). Re-run `eoie agile note <backlog> COMPILER-CROSS-MACHINE-REPRO <text> --as compiler`.")
inl note_of_compiler_cross_machine_repro_6 () = Note ("COMPILER-CROSS-MACHINE-REPRO", 6u32, "2026-10-26T04:25Z", "compiler", "compiler lane: FIRST NODE PROOF (G1 PASS). Built the repro fix on the worker node (build.ps1 edits: staged source -> det root C:\\home\\spiral-cache, pathmap target -> /_/) in a private cache; job repro-build exit 0, 283s. The node-built single-flight core (sha DF98A378, 12,152,832 B) has ZERO C:\\Users strings -- the two previously-embedded user paths are gone. Positive controls confirm the edits took effect: staged source is now C:\\home\\spiral-cache\\core-src\\single-flight\\34519b923366ccec\\spiral_compiler.fs (no username), and the PDB path is now /_//obj/single-flight/.../SpiralCompilerCore.pdb (pathmap /_/). The one remaining cross-machine variable is the COMPRESSED AssemblyAttributes path (still under the node's per-user $sharedCache in build 1, not a visible string). Build 2 (with the Directory.Build.props obj-shared -> $(SpiralDetRoot) edit) is running on the node now to close it. Next: G2 (two node builds identical sha) and, when local box headroom allows, G3/G4 (node core == box core, byte-identical) with crossmachine-gate.ps1.")
inl note_of_compiler_cross_machine_repro_7 () = Note ("COMPILER-CROSS-MACHINE-REPRO", 7u32, "2026-10-26T04:37Z", "compiler", "compiler lane: decompress analysis refines the G4 criterion (decompress is reliable via the file-offset-to-EOF method; the exact-length-copy method flaked earlier). Decompressed build 1's node core (build.ps1 edits only, props NOT yet edited):\n- FSharpSignatureCompressedData (699,353 B): contains C:\\Users\\alias AND C:\\home\\spiral-cache AND obj-shared. The C:\\Users\\alias is the UNFIXED AssemblyAttributes path (node's per-user $sharedCache/obj-shared); build 2's Directory.Build.props edit ($(SpiralDetRoot)/obj-shared) replaces it.\n- FSharpOptimizationCompressedData (491,821 B): contains C:\\home\\spiral-cache (staged .fs, FIXED) plus C:\\Users\\h and C:\\Users\\stephan. Those two are CONSTANT dependency-package paths (FSharp.Core / NuGet optimization data baked at the package's build), identical on every machine -- proven by the earlier box-vs-node OptimizationData byte-diff being EXACTLY 5 bytes (the local username at the staged path): if stephan/h varied, more bytes would differ. They do NOT break cross-machine byte-identity.\nREFINED G4: the fix must eliminate every MACHINE-VARYING path (the local username in the staged .fs, the PDB, and AssemblyAttributes), NOT every C:\\Users string -- the constant dep paths (C:\\Users\\h, C:\\Users\\stephan) are byte-identical on all machines and are expected to remain. So the decompressed-resource check is \"no C:\\Users\\<localuser>\", not \"no C:\\Users\". build 1 fixed staged+PDB (G1 PASS: no visible C:\\Users). build 2 (props edit) targets the last machine-varying path (AssemblyAttributes C:\\Users\\alias) and built OK (exit 0, 438s). Its decompress check -- expect no C:\\Users\\alias, only the constant stephan/h -- is pending: the node's ssh is timing out under concurrent-build load (4 cargo spiral.exe rebuilds + builds), so the fetch waits for the node to shed load. TODO: crossmachine-gate.ps1 should add this decompressed machine-varying-path check (local username only), not just the visible-strings G1.")
inl note_of_compiler_cross_machine_repro_8 () = Note ("COMPILER-CROSS-MACHINE-REPRO", 8u32, "2026-10-26T04:44Z", "compiler", "compiler lane: FIX VALIDATED on the node (G4 by construction). Built the FULL fix on the node (build.ps1 det-root + /_/ pathmap edits AND Directory.Build.props obj-shared -> $(SpiralDetRoot) edit; build 2 exit 0, core sha F7F387E5) and ran the enhanced crossmachine-gate.ps1 (now with a decompressed machine-varying-path check, positive-controlled on build 1, which correctly FAILS on C:\\Users\\alias):\n- G1 (visible ASCII/UTF16 strings): PASS, 0 C:\\Users.\n- DECOMP (decompressed FSharp Signature/Optimization CompressedData): only C:\\Users\\h and C:\\Users\\stephan remain -- the CONSTANT FSharp.Core/NuGet dependency paths, byte-identical on every machine. NO C:\\Users\\alias (the node builder's local user) anywhere, including the compressed AssemblyAttributes path (now C:\\home\\spiral-cache/obj-shared).\n- machine-varying (C:\\Users\\alias): PASS (0).\nTherefore build 2's core embeds ONLY machine-independent paths (C:\\home\\spiral-cache staged source + AssemblyAttributes, /_/ PDB) plus the constant deps. A box build of the same source with the same fix embeds the IDENTICAL paths (det root is the same string on both machines; deps are constant) under a deterministic build -> byte-identical core. G4 holds by construction; the literal node-core == box-core comparison (G3) only confirms it and needs a box build (box was saturated; run on headroom).\nApply-script (verified on the node): build.ps1 (4 edits: $detRoot def, staged path, prune path, PathMap target /_/) + Directory.Build.props (SpiralDetRoot property + AssemblyAttributes -> $(SpiralDetRoot)/obj-shared). Still to add for the landed form (not needed for the core build/gate): reconcile test.ps1:62 and rebuild-determinism.ps1:10 (they walk core-src under Get-SpiralCacheDir) to the det root. Reusable gate: crossmachine-gate.ps1 (G1 strings + G4 block-diff + DECOMP machine-varying). Ready for pack 5n once G3 box build confirms.")
inl note_of_compiler_cross_machine_repro_9 () = Note ("COMPILER-CROSS-MACHINE-REPRO", 9u32, "2026-10-26T16:45Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-26 (build-independent consumer census -- the \"grep eoie/i574n.github/remote.ps1 for core-src assumptions, one path at a time\" sub-task note 4 flagged as risky; done with explicit patterns, no brace globs). Result DE-RISKS the fix: blast radius is CONTAINED to the compiler tree; zero sibling-repo ripple.\n\nSTAGED core-src path consumers (these move to SPIRAL_DET_ROOT per the fix) -- all in apps/compiler/tmp/scripts:\n- build.ps1:156 (resolves the source to stage), :158 (the staged path `core-src/$m/<sha16>/spiral_compiler.fs`), :168 (prune keeps newest 8), :170 (-p:SpiralCoreSource=$staged + -p:PathMap=$cache=$sharedCache).\n- test.ps1:62 (walks `core-src` for *.jsonl core logs).\n- rebuild-determinism.ps1:10 (touches staged spiral_compiler.fs under core-src/$mode).\n2nd embedded-path source (the obj-shared SDK attrs, note 4 #2): apps/compiler/tmp/compiler/Directory.Build.props (writes $(SpiralSharedCacheDir)/obj-shared/<proj>/<tfm>.AssemblyAttributes.fs -- the F# source-path embed --pathmap can't rewrite).\n\nUNAFFECTED (do NOT touch): Get-SpiralCoreSource (env.ps1:24) returns the AUTHORITATIVE TREE source (apps/compiler/spiral_compiler.fs), not the staged copy; its consumers projection-hash.ps1:13, hopacsota-lint.ps1:4 and build.ps1:156 (as the stage INPUT) read the tree, so the staging-root change does not touch them.\n\nSIBLING REPOS -- ZERO staged-core-src consumers (all spiral-bin refs are the INSTALLED bin / job queue, not staged source):\n- eoie: kit_compiler_discovery (main.spi:165-166 / compiler_discovery.rs:164-165) finds the INSTALLED compiler under LOCALAPPDATA/spiral-bin; kit_job_slot/kit_window_run use spiral-bin/heavy-queue. None read core-src.\n- i574n.github: respell-repo.ps1:31-32 uses spiral-bin\\candidates + \\bin (installed DLL); pins/workflows.spi:107 sets CI SPIRAL_BIN_CACHE_DIR=RUNNER_TEMP/spiral-bin (cache dir, not core-src -- CI determinism is not this item's G1-G4 gate); remote.ps1 has NO core-src/staged reference.\n\nCONCLUSION: the atomic fix touches exactly build.ps1 + test.ps1 + rebuild-determinism.ps1 + tmp/compiler/Directory.Build.props -- all inside apps/compiler/tmp; no cross-repo change. This is the full consumer list for the anchored apply (still needs a build for G1-G4: zero C:\\Users strings, same-machine sha256 stable, node==box sha256, zero byte-diff ranges). Coordinate-with-COMPILER-TMP-RETIRE caveat stands (these scripts relocate under that item). Fix itself (SPIRAL_DET_ROOT staging + -p:PathMap=$cache=/_/ token) is a build-slot job, not clean prep (multi-file, must land atomically; a staging-root change without its 3 consumers breaks the build).")
inl note_of_compiler_cross_machine_repro_10 () = Note ("COMPILER-CROSS-MACHINE-REPRO", 10u32, "2026-10-26T20:55Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-26 (the SEAM is now a VERIFIED anchored apply, build-independent). Produced scratchpad/apply-ccmr-det-root.ps1 (the item's \"anchored apply script\" seam) and proved it on scratch copies per verify-drafts-on-scratch-copies.\n\nVERIFICATION (no compiler build): all 8 anchors match EXACTLY ONCE on the LIVE tree (apps/compiler/tmp), and the script applies cleanly on fresh scratch copies to the exact intended result. The 8 edits (5 files):\n  1. scripts/env.ps1       : add Get-SpiralDetRoot (mirrors Get-SpiralCacheDir; env SPIRAL_DET_ROOT, default C:\\home\\spiral-cache on Windows else $HOME/spiral-cache; creates the dir).\n  2. scripts/build.ps1      : define `$detRoot = Get-SpiralDetRoot` right after the `$sharedCache = ...` line.\n  3. scripts/build.ps1      : stage the .fs under $detRoot (was $sharedCache).\n  4. scripts/build.ps1      : prune core-src under $detRoot (was $sharedCache).\n  5. scripts/build.ps1      : `-p:PathMap=$cache=$sharedCache` -> `-p:PathMap=$cache=/_/`.\n  6. compiler/Directory.Build.props : add 3 SpiralDetRoot property rows + point TargetFrameworkMonikerAssemblyAttributesPath at $(SpiralDetRoot) (was $(SpiralSharedCacheDir)).\n  7. scripts/test.ps1       : clean stale *.jsonl under Get-SpiralDetRoot/core-src (was $cache) -- the core writes those logs next to the staged .fs, which now lives under the det root.\n  8. scripts/rebuild-determinism.ps1 : touch staged .fs under Get-SpiralDetRoot/core-src (was Get-SpiralCacheDir).\n\nCORRECTNESS CHECKS CONFIRMED on the applied scratch copy:\n- `$sharedCache` is PRESERVED at its ONLY non-determinism use (build.ps1 r2r-cache imageCache, the machine-local ReadyToRun image store -- NOT embedded in the DLL, must not move). Only the three determinism sites (staged/prune/pathmap) changed.\n- core-src consumer set is COMPLETE: grep core-src over all scripts = exactly 4 lines (build.ps1 x2, test.ps1, rebuild-determinism.ps1), all handled; matches the earlier ccmr consumer census (scratchpad/ccmr-consumer-census.txt). No sibling-repo consumer (census: eoie/i574n.github/remote.ps1 read the installed bin/job queue, never staged core-src).\n\nREMAINING = build-bound only (the G1-G4 gate, needs a heavy slot):\n- G1 (cheap, pre-build but needs the built candidate DLL): zero C:\\Users strings (ascii+utf16 scan).\n- G2: two fresh builds on this box -> identical sha256 (install-by-hash preserved same-machine).\n- G3: a node build of the same synced source -> identical sha256 for BOTH host (12MB) and core (5.7MB).\n- G4: full byte-diff zero differing ranges, including inside decompressed FSharpSignatureCompressedData.\nRun on this box: `pwsh scratchpad/apply-ccmr-det-root.ps1 -Root <apps/compiler/tmp> -Apply` then build x2 + node build + diff.\n\nCOORDINATION: these files relocate under COMPILER-TMP-RETIRE (main's). Anchors are TEXT (not line numbers) so they survive a repack as long as the lines' text is unchanged; if the retire MOVES the files, point the apply's -Root at the new location. Lands into pack 5n as main's note says. Order: main sequenced this to land BEFORE RUST-DERIVE-INFERENCE (WSJF 40%, unblocks ship-DLL-by-hash). This completes the build-independent half; the apply is ready to run the moment a heavy slot frees.")
inl note_of_compiler_cross_machine_repro_11 () = Note ("COMPILER-CROSS-MACHINE-REPRO", 11u32, "2026-10-27T12:46Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-27 CCMR det-root + Get-FileSha256-dedup apply is NON-BREAKING (node job-1793102876807-ccmr-proof, exit 0, 1721s, on a ccmr-proof mirror with per-job SPIRAL_BIN_CACHE_DIR + SPIRAL_DET_ROOT):\n- apply-ccmr-det-root.ps1: all 9 anchors matched exactly once + APPLIED (8 det-root edits + the test.ps1 Get-FileSha256 dedup drop).\n- pair-drift clean (8 pairs, 0 touched). Debug build 0 errors (489s, DLL 92776AD6) with the core staged under SPIRAL_DET_ROOT (.node-det-ccmr-...\\core-src\\single-flight\\<sha>) -- det-root edits 1-5 all exercised (build.ps1's staging + pathmap are config-independent, so Debug runs them, confirmed by the warning paths).\n- test.ps1 -Suite frontier -Native exit 0: 174 jobs, 92 native ran, 0 build-fail, oracle agree 84, DISAGREE 0. build.ps1 + test.ps1 both call the deduped Get-FileSha256 (env.ps1's kept copy); native tier exercised the native-memo key path.\n- SAMPLES-DIFF-UNCHANGED before/after (CCMR does not touch the core, so no residual drift).\n- compare-runs.ps1 self-diff: 0 differing cells (exercises the shared results-path.ps1 Resolve-Results dedup).\n\nNOT a determinism claim. A single-machine two-cache comparison proves nothing (builds are already byte-identical across cache folders via the shared-cache PathMap since 2026-10-17); CCMR fixes per-USER paths (i574n vs alias), which only a box-vs-node G3 test settles, and that stays parked behind G2 (the two-cache-root redesign). rebuild-determinism.ps1 was NOT exercised (determinism half).\n\nLANDING the apply to the live tree remains gated on G2 + coordination: it rewrites shared build scripts (env.ps1/build.ps1/Directory.Build.props/test.ps1/rebuild-determinism.ps1) other lanes use. The apply pack + the Resolve-Results dedup (results-path.ps1 + ci-classify/compare-runs repoint) are already landed unstaged in the tree. Both proof mirrors removed after the run (node disk).")
inl note_of_compiler_cross_machine_repro_12 () = Note ("COMPILER-CROSS-MACHINE-REPRO", 12u32, "2026-10-27T13:13Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-27 CCMR path forward (I own CCMR after deciding route (b): 5n strips its repro-*, my apply-ccmr-det-root.ps1 lands later). Re-examined what G2 actually needs:\n\nThe apply's Get-SpiralDetRoot defaults to C:\\home\\spiral-cache -- a STABLE, user-independent absolute path present on BOTH box and node (both have C:\\home\\). That is exactly the fix for the original bug (the core DLL embedded per-USER LOCALAPPDATA paths, i574n vs alias). Staging the core-src .fs and the SDK AssemblyAttributes under C:\\home\\spiral-cache makes their embedded paths identical on every machine, so the determinism that the $cache=/_/ pathmap could not reach (the .fs lives under $detRoot, not $cache) is covered by the det-root being the same absolute path everywhere.\n\nSo the \"G2 two-cache-root redesign\" likely DE-SCOPES to running the G3 proof, not a redesign: build the SAME composite on the box and on the node, both with the default det-root C:\\home\\spiral-cache, and compare SpiralCompilerCore.dll sha256 -- equal => determinism holds => land apply-ccmr-det-root.ps1 (det-root + Get-FileSha256 dedup). My non-breaking node-proof (n11) used a PER-JOB SPIRAL_DET_ROOT only for isolation; that per-job root is what made that run's embedded path per-job and so not determinism-testable -- the G3 run must use the stable default instead.\n\nOpen question to settle during the G3 run (don't assume): the prune in build.ps1 (Get-ChildItem $detRoot/core-src/$m -Directory | Sort LastWriteTime -Desc | Select -Skip 8 | Delete) runs against the now-SHARED C:\\home\\spiral-cache. It is sha-keyed (distinct cores -> distinct dirs) and keeps the latest 8, so concurrent same-core jobs share an identical dir (safe) and a prune deleting an in-use dir needs >8 concurrent distinct cores -- low risk, but confirm no concurrent box build loses its staged .fs during the G3 run; if it bites, gate prune behind a lock or skip it when another build is live (that, not a two-cache split, is the minimal hardening). Sequence: after 5n installs, G3 on the installed base, then land.")
inl note_of_compiler_cross_machine_repro_13 () = Note ("COMPILER-CROSS-MACHINE-REPRO", 13u32, "2026-10-27T16:18Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-27 ~12:0x: G3 toolchain-identity precondition CONFIRMED SATISFIED + symmetric (build-independent; corrects an intra-session misread, caught before it stood).\n\nThe node CAN build net11 and already did (note 9: core F7F387E5, 10-26). The build dotnet is CACHE-STAGED, resolved by Resolve-SpiralDotnet (apps/compiler/tmp/scripts/env.ps1:61) in order: $SPIRAL_DOTNET, <cache>/toolchains/dotnet, <sharedCache>/toolchains/dotnet, then the system PATH dotnet (each candidate must pass Test-DotnetHasSdk11). Both machines resolve to the cache-staged dotnet, with an IDENTICAL SDK pair:\n  box  C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\spiral-bin\\toolchains\\dotnet\\sdk : 11.0.100-rc.1.26425.128 + 11.0.100-rc.2.26504.105\n  node C:\\Users\\alias\\AppData\\Local\\spiral-bin\\toolchains\\dotnet\\sdk : 11.0.100-rc.1.26425.128 + 11.0.100-rc.2.26504.105\nBoth have ONLY the net11 rc.1 runtime (Microsoft.NETCore.App 11.0.0-rc.1.26425.128), no rc.2 runtime. build.ps1 builds with \"the newest installed SDK whose bundled runtime the system dotnet also has\" (temp global.json, tmp/AGENTS.md) => rc.2 is skipped on both (no rc.2 runtime) => both select rc.1.26425.128 => SAME compiler toolchain on both machines. (The SYSTEM dotnet at C:\\Program Files is net9-only on the node / rc.1 on the box, but Resolve-SpiralDotnet never reaches it, so it is a red herring -- an earlier intra-session query hit it and wrongly concluded \"node needs a net11 SDK\"; retracted.)\n\n=> G3's toolchain-identity precondition HOLDS today; NO SDK provisioning is needed. The only remaining gate is the BUILD half the box never ran (box was saturated 10-26, note 9): G2 (two box builds -> identical sha, install-by-hash preserved) + G3 (box build of the SAME source as the node == node's sha256 for host+core). Run on the POST-5n core, since the pack re-anchors after 5n changes the core. Node side is already proven buildable.\n\nNEW G3 CAVEAT for crossmachine-gate.ps1: assert the RESOLVED SDK build is identical on both machines (Resolve-SpiralDotnet result + build.ps1's global.json SDK pick), not merely \"a net11 SDK is present\". Both machines carry an extra rc.2 SDK; if an rc.2 RUNTIME were later installed on only one machine, that machine's build.ps1 would flip to the rc.2 SDK and the core sha would diverge for a NON-SOURCE reason. Currently mitigated symmetrically (neither has the rc.2 runtime). Also still TODO for the landed form (note 9): reconcile test.ps1 + rebuild-determinism.ps1 core-src walks to the det root.")
inl note_of_compiler_cross_machine_repro_14 () = Note ("COMPILER-CROSS-MACHINE-REPRO", 14u32, "2026-10-27T17:06Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-27 12:1x: implemented note 13's caveat. crossmachine-gate.ps1 (scratchpad, the reusable G3 gate) now has an optional PRE-G check: pass -BoxSdk/-NodeSdk (each = the SDK version build.ps1 resolved via Resolve-SpiralDotnet on that machine) and it asserts they are identical, printing that a G4 diff under SDK skew is a toolchain artifact, not a pack path leak. Parse-verified (no execution; runs at G3 post-5n). Both machines resolve the cache-staged <LOCALAPPDATA>\\spiral-bin\\toolchains\\dotnet (rc.1.26425.128) today, so PRE-G passes; it guards against a future rc.2-runtime asymmetry.")
inl note_of_compiler_cross_machine_repro_15 () = Note ("COMPILER-CROSS-MACHINE-REPRO", 15u32, "2026-10-27T20:52Z", "compiler", "CCMR box-half COMPLETE (G1+G2+G3a all GREEN), 10-27, compiler lane.\n\nG1 (no machine-varying path) + G2 (two Release builds from one checkout identical: core 1B66E4E3 / host D9F9CD3F)\nwere already proven on scratch copy C:\\home\\ccmr-box, det-root C:\\home\\spiral-cache.\n\nNEW gap found and fixed: the PROJECT-DIR path (apps/compiler/tmp/compiler/core) is embedded in F# compressed\nsignature data, and --pathmap over the cache dir alone does NOT rewrite it, so the core was still\ncheckout-location-DEPENDENT. Fix = extend build.ps1's PathMap to also map $BundleRoot (the bundle/project root)\nto a fixed token /_b/.\n\nG3a PROOF (box-local stand-in for cross-machine): built the SAME core from TWO different checkout roots\n(C:\\home\\ccmr-box and C:\\home\\ccmr-alt) with the extended PathMap + same det-root. Result: BYTE-IDENTICAL,\nsha EEDEE7927F608AF3. So the core is now checkout-location-independent.\n\n*** SHIPPABLE REFERENCE SHA IS NOW EEDEE792, NOT 1B66E4E3. *** 1B66E4E3 was G2 WITHOUT the project-dir pathmap\n(embedded the real project path -> location-dependent). The project-dir pathmap rewrites that path to /_b/,\nchanging the bytes by design to the location-independent form. Any prior G3b reference of 1B66E4E3 is superseded.\n\nLANDED INTO THE PACK: folded the extension into apps/compiler/packs/apply-ccmr-det-root.ps1 edit 5; its new value\nis \"-p:PathMap=$cache=/_/%2c$BundleRoot=/_b/\". Whole pack re-verified DRY-RUN 9/9 anchors match exactly once.\nGOTCHA: MSBuild -p: splits the value on ',', so the 2nd PathMap entry's comma MUST be %2c-escaped (raw comma =>\nMSB4177, parses the path as a property name). $BundleRoot is already defined in build.ps1 above the PathMap line.\n\nREMAINING: G3b (true cross-machine) = node builds the same core with det-root C:\\home\\spiral-cache + the extended\nPathMap; its core sha must equal EEDEE792. Needs node capacity (node saturated; TRAIN running). CCMR land =\napply-ccmr-det-root.ps1 (9/9) after the full gate; results-path.ps1 stays separate (n21). Pack edit is unstaged\nin C:\\home\\git\\spiral\\apps\\compiler\\packs.")
inl note_of_compiler_cross_machine_repro_16 () = Note ("COMPILER-CROSS-MACHINE-REPRO", 16u32, "2026-10-27T21:37Z", "compiler", "CCMR G3b (cross-machine) = DLL CONTENT IDENTICAL; residual is the PDB debug-directory only. 10-27.\n\nG3b: node (user alias) core 88FD12AC vs box EEDEE792. BUT the DLLs are the SAME length and differ in only 56 bytes,\n4 regions, ALL in the debug directory that POINTS AT THE PDB (advisor-confirmed reading of the hex):\n  - 0xF18D8 \"SHA256\\0\" = the PdbChecksum entry (hash of the .pdb, not the dll).\n  - 0xF1870 \"RSDS\" = the PDB ID GUID = first 16 bytes of that pdb-hash with the GUID v4 nibble set (byte7 04->44).\n  - 0x2D354 + 0x2D370 = two IMAGE_DEBUG_DIRECTORY TimeDateStamps (0x2D370-0x2D354 = 0x1C = one 28-byte entry).\nThe DLL PROPER IS CROSS-MACHINE IDENTICAL: MVID 5c49e447-f508-b0bb-4b8f-70c71ef404a3 on BOTH, IL + metadata + all\n22,165 printable strings byte-identical, decomp C:\\Users = h/stephan only on both. So core determinism WORKS; only\nthe PDB (and the dll's pointer to it) differs.\n\nWHY THE PDBs DIFFER (CustomDebugInformation dump): the NODE pdb has 2 CDI, the BOX pdb has 0:\n  - NODE CDI[0] = Source Link (GUID cc110556-...): {\"documents\":{\"C:\\\\home\\\\remote\\\\ccmr-g3b\\\\spiral\\\\*\":\n       \"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/i574n/spiral/3f1b7588.../*\"}}  <- MACHINE-LOCAL repo path embedded.\n  - NODE CDI[1] = Embedded Source (GUID 0e8a571b-...), 2.18 MB of embedded source.\n  - BOX = 0 CDI.\nROOT: the node built from a GIT WORKTREE mirror (.git present -> SourceLink + source-embedding activate -> the 2 CDI,\none carrying the machine-local repo root C:\\home\\remote\\ccmr-g3b\\spiral), while box EEDEE792 was built from a NON-GIT\nrobocopy scratch (ccmr-box, no .git -> SourceLink inactive -> 0 CDI). The pathmaps cover $cache, $BundleRoot\n(=...\\spiral\\apps\\compiler\\tmp) and the det-rooted core-src, but NOT the git REPO ROOT (...\\spiral, one level above\n$BundleRoot) that SourceLink uses.\n\n=> G3b WAS CONFOUNDED (git node vs non-git box). It is NOT a compiler determinism failure -- the DLL content matches.\n\nFIX DIRECTIONS for a reproducible SHIP dll (build-bound to verify):\n (a) SIMPLEST: for the CCMR reproducible build, disable the env-specific PDB content:\n     <EnableSourceLink>false</EnableSourceLink> + <EmbedUntrackedSources>false</EmbedUntrackedSources>\n     (+ keep <DebugType>portable</DebugType>). The pdb then holds only the already-pathmapped doc names (which\n     ALREADY match cross-machine) -> deterministic pdb -> deterministic dll debug-directory -> dll hash matches.\n (b) OR <ContinuousIntegrationBuild>true</ContinuousIntegrationBuild> + a PathMap entry for the git REPO ROOT\n     (one level above $BundleRoot) so SourceLink emits the normalized path. More fragile (repo root differs per\n     checkout; must map each to the same token).\n (c) OR ship-by-MVID / by a debug-directory-stripped dll hash instead of the raw dll hash (the content is identical).\nCLEAN RE-TEST: build BOTH machines in the SAME environment (both from a git worktree, or both non-git) with the fix,\nthen compare. My G3b node=git vs box=non-git is not apples-to-apples.\n\nSTATUS: box-half (G1/G2/G3a) STANDS; the folded det-root+project-PathMap pack is correct + still needed. The\ncross-machine goal needs this ADDITIONAL small build-config step (a), verified by a same-env re-test. The core's own\nbytes (MVID/IL) are already cross-machine identical -- the hardest part is DONE. Node pdb/core kept at C:\\home\\g3b-bin,\nbox at C:\\home\\g3a-bin-box.")
inl note_of_compiler_cross_machine_repro_17 () = Note ("COMPILER-CROSS-MACHINE-REPRO", 17u32, "2026-10-27T22:11Z", "compiler", "Phase-2 fix DRAFTED: apps/compiler/packs/apply-ccmr-sourcelink.ps1 (dry-run 1/1, unstaged). Adds to compiler/Directory.Build.props: EnableSourceLink=false, EnableSourceControlManagerQueries=false, EmbedUntrackedSources=false, EmbedAllSources=false, IncludeSourceRevisionInInformationalVersion=false. Independent anchor (GenerateAssemblyInfo), composes with apply-ccmr-det-root.ps1. First-attempt property set. VERIFY (build-bound, needs a heavy slot): build Release single-flight on two machines SAME-ENV (both git worktrees, same det-root + %2c PathMap), decomp both PDBs (expect 0 CustomDebugInformation blobs), confirm core sha256 matches. If a CDI survives, add the missing property (DebugType / a SourceLink package PrivateAssets).")
inl note_of_compiler_cross_machine_repro_18 () = Note ("COMPILER-CROSS-MACHINE-REPRO", 18u32, "2026-10-27T22:29Z", "compiler", "Phase-2 PASS + CROSS-MACHINE MATCH. Node (machine 2, git worktree) with det-root + %2c PathMap + apply-ccmr-sourcelink.ps1 -> PDB 0 CDI, core sha EEDEE792 = box reference EXACTLY. Two machines/checkout mechanisms -> byte-identical core. ship-DLL-by-hash cross-machine ACHIEVED (3 packs). Land both after gate.")
inl note_of_compiler_cross_machine_repro_19 () = Note ("COMPILER-CROSS-MACHINE-REPRO", 19u32, "2026-10-27T22:30Z", "main", "10-27 18:30 main: main BLESS (18:0x) for landing CCMR's 3 composable packs (apps/compiler/packs: apply-ccmr-det-root.ps1 with the project-dir PathMap folded in, and apply-ccmr-sourcelink.ps1), after the compiler lane's cross-machine proof: the node (git worktree, SourceLink off) and the box (non-git) built byte-identical cores, sha EEDEE7927F608AF3...964B, a full 64-char match. Conditions: (1) land only after bundle accept-6 (job-1793136844465) finishes; no apps/compiler/tmp build-file edits while test.ps1 runs. (2) Gate rule, set before measuring: a candidate built with both packs, run as -Suite frontier,smoke,examples -Native via SPIRAL_COMPILER_DLL, shows 0 residual/exit/stdout differences against the accepted baseline (DISAGREE 0); the packs change only build metadata, so any differing cell is RED. (3) CCMR lands before the pack-5n install, so 5n's installed DLL is the first reproducible one. (4) No box git-worktree confirmation: git is the user's here, and the node-to-box match already proves machine independence.")
inl note_of_compiler_cross_machine_repro_20 () = Note ("COMPILER-CROSS-MACHINE-REPRO", 20u32, "2026-10-27T22:34Z", "compiler", "LAND GATE (main 18:0x, recorded before run): candidate built with BOTH packs (det-root+folded %2c PathMap, sourcelink) runs test.ps1 -Suite frontier,smoke,examples -Native via SPIRAL_COMPILER_DLL; must be DISAGREE 0 (0 residual/exit/stdout diffs vs accepted baseline). Packs change build metadata only -> codegen byte-identical; any differing cell = RED = abort. Land AFTER accept-6 (job-1793136844465), no tmp build-file edits during it, BEFORE pack-5n install. Skip box git confirm.")
inl note_of_compiler_cross_machine_repro_21 () = Note ("COMPILER-CROSS-MACHINE-REPRO", 21u32, "2026-10-27T22:36Z", "main", "10-27 18:36 main: Correction to n19 condition (2), main's own error: a gate against the accepted baseline can't isolate CCMR, because lib moved after that baseline (convert radix 15:40, fiscal's 8 modules 16:04, nil batch 2 17:39), so residual cells differ regardless of the packs. Replacement rule, set before any run: an A/B on the same tree in the same window. -Suite frontier,smoke,examples -Native runs once with the installed compiler and once with the CCMR candidate (SPIRAL_COMPILER_DLL), then compare-runs installed vs candidate: GREEN iff 0 differing cells (compile, residual, native, exit, stdout, oracle), row for row. Nothing is allowed through, since the packs change only build metadata. Sequencing: accept-6 finishes and TRAIN publishes, then the compiler lane runs ccmr-land.ps1, then main starts the pack-5n install once CCMR is reported landed.")
inl note_of_compiler_cross_machine_repro_22 () = Note ("COMPILER-CROSS-MACHINE-REPRO", 22u32, "2026-10-27T22:38Z", "compiler", "LAND GATE v2 (main 18:3x, corrects note 20: baseline compare cant isolate CCMR -- lib moved today). A/B on the SAME tree same window: run test.ps1 -Suite frontier,smoke,examples -Native TWICE (once INSTALLED, once CANDIDATE via SPIRAL_COMPILER_DLL built with both packs), then compare-runs. GREEN iff 0 differing cells (compile/residual/native/exit/stdout/oracle, row for row). Packs change build metadata only. Run AFTER accept-6 AND TRAIN publish; land before 5n (main holds 5n until I report landed).")
inl note_of_compiler_cross_machine_repro_23 () = Note ("COMPILER-CROSS-MACHINE-REPRO", 23u32, "2026-10-28T04:41Z", "main", "10-28 00:41 main: Qualification found 10-28 00:4x by P2's bless condition 3. A pure-5n single-flight build on the node hashed to core E322D4090B1AECAC; the box's installed 5n is 7A6C1445. The compiler team traced it to .NET SDK skew: the box has SDK rc.1 (26425.128) and rc.2 (26504.105), and build.ps1 picks the newest with a matching runtime, so tonight's 5n install built with rc.2. The node has only rc.1. So CCMR's byte-identity holds per SDK, and cross-machine equality needs SDK parity. The 10-27 proof (EEDEE792 on both machines) must have had matching SDKs. Follow-ups: (1) the node moves to rc.2 (compiler team, machine upkeep). (2) The SDK version becomes part of the build's identity: build.ps1 prints it, and any cross-machine comparison refuses (VOID, not RED) when the SDKs differ, so skew can't look like a codegen change. A global.json pin per checkout is the stronger form. (3) Once nothing pins it, the box's stale rc.1 is removed (archive-first rules don't apply to an SDK; uninstall through its installer).")
inl note_of_compiler_cross_machine_repro_24 () = Note ("COMPILER-CROSS-MACHINE-REPRO", 24u32, "2026-10-28T04:48Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-28: SDK-in-build-identity design (main; after the P2 cond-3 SDK skew, node rc.1 vs box rc.2 -> core E322D409 != 7A6C1445). SDK skew changes DLL BYTES not OUTPUT (compare-runs over residuals never REDs on it); only the DLL-HASH cross-machine compare must VOID. PLAN: build identity = {coreHash, sdk}; build.ps1 already prints the SDK -> record it into the attestation/manifest beside the hash. remote.ps1 Assert-NodeCore (:49) throws on a core-sha mismatch -> change to take {expectedHash, expectedSdk}, read the node {hash, sdk}, VOID if sdk differs, RED only if sdk matches AND hash differs. global.json pin is stronger (forces parity) but gated on eoie dotnet (C:\\Program Files\\dotnet) supporting the pinned SDK -- an rc.2 compiler demands the rc.2 runtime (AGENTS.md 10-17). VOID-on-skew first (safe), pin a gated follow-up.")
inl note_of_compiler_cross_machine_repro_25 () = Note ("COMPILER-CROSS-MACHINE-REPRO", 25u32, "2026-10-28T04:52Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-28: SKEW-INVARIANCE MEASURED = True (main rider). Same pure-5n core (9E1F72AB) built rc.1 (core E322D4090B1AECAC) vs rc.2 (7A6C1445BF5AF48C) -> compiling spiral.spi (DA60B76F) gives IDENTICAL output B47B5749C215...CC90 on both. So SDK skew changes DLL BYTES only, NOT output. Validates VOID-on-skew: skew -> hash differs -> VOID (not RED); output identical so no false codegen regression. Cross-machine here (node rc.1 vs box rc.2) but output is source-determined/machine-invariant; the formal same-box rc.1-vs-rc.2 check rides the VOID-path impl as the committed reproducible test.")
inl note_of_compiler_cross_machine_repro_26 () = Note ("COMPILER-CROSS-MACHINE-REPRO", 26u32, "2026-10-28T04:55Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-28: VOID-on-skew REFINED (n24 soundness gap). Identity = {sourceHash, sdk, coreHash}, not {coreHash, sdk} -- else a source regression masked by an sdk diff would wrongly VOID. Assert-NodeCore (remote.ps1:49, called from run/job :80-81 on -RequireCore): node.sourceHash != expected -> THROW (codegen/source mismatch, always); elif node.sdk != expected.sdk -> VOID (SDK skew, output-invariant per n25); elif coreHash differs -> THROW (build nondeterminism). build.ps1 writes a manifest {sourceHash,sdk,coreHash} beside the DLL at install; Assert-NodeCore reads the node manifest + caller passes -RequireSourceHash/-RequireSdk. Focused impl task (build.ps1 + remote.ps1 edits).")
inl note_of_compiler_cross_machine_repro_27 () = Note ("COMPILER-CROSS-MACHINE-REPRO", 27u32, "2026-10-28T06:54Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-28 CORRECTION to n24/n70: my SDK-skew diagnosis was WRONG. Node + box build.ps1 BOTH use rc.1 (both logs: rc.1.26425.128, newest the system dotnet can run). Yet cross-machine cores DIFFER for the SAME source+SDK: P2-composite SF (9FF1B708, rc.1) = node 986266D8 vs box 05E94769; pure-5n (9E1F72AB) = node E322D409 vs box 7A6C1445. So CCMR byte-identity is NOT holding node-vs-box despite the landed packs -- a determinism gap (hypothesis: pathmap / obj-shared AssemblyAttributes / shared-cache path not normalized cross-machine), NOT SDK. Output-invariant (both B47B5749). INVESTIGATE: diff the two builds pathmap + obj-shared + R2R inputs. P2 bless unaffected (output-neutral).")
inl note_of_compiler_cross_machine_repro_28 () = Note ("COMPILER-CROSS-MACHINE-REPRO", 28u32, "2026-10-28T06:55Z", "main", "10-28 02:55 main: CORRECTION to n23 (main, 10-28 01:5x): the SDK-skew cause is WRONG. The compiler team re-read both build logs: node and box both build with SDK rc.1.26425.128 ('the newest the system dotnet can run'), not rc.1 vs rc.2. Yet the same source with the same SDK gives different core DLLs on the two machines after the CCMR land: pure 5n is node E322D409 vs box 7A6C1445, and the P2-composite single-flight is node 986266D8 vs box 05E94769. So CCMR's cross-machine byte-identity does NOT hold for the landed packs. The 10-27 16:xx proof (EEDEE792 on both machines) was a candidate built under conditions the landed scripts don't reproduce, which is a real determinism gap. Likely areas (compiler team investigating): PathMap coverage, obj-shared AssemblyAttributes, shared-cache or R2R input paths not normalized across machines. What does hold, measured: the compiler's OUTPUT is machine-invariant (spiral.spi -> B47B5749C215 from all four DLLs), so correctness, the 5n accept and P2 are unaffected. The n23 follow-ups change: the node SDK upgrade is moot; VOID-on-skew aimed at the wrong cause and is revised once the real cause is found. The item stays open until a box build and a node build of one source hash equal again.")
inl note_of_compiler_cross_machine_repro_29 () = Note ("COMPILER-CROSS-MACHINE-REPRO", 29u32, "2026-10-28T07:06Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-28: CCMR gap DIAGNOSED (Directory.Build.props:18 + AGENTS.md). AssemblyAttributesPath = $(SpiralDetRoot)/obj-shared/<proj>/<TF>.AssemblyAttributes.fs; --pathmap does NOT rewrite it, so the det-root path embeds in the F# sig data. My P2 builds used ISOLATED SPIRAL_DET_ROOTs (node vs box) -> different embedded path -> different cores. NOT a CCMR fix bug, NOT SDK: the DEFAULT det-root (props:16 C:\\home\\spiral-cache, machine-INDEPENDENT) is the SAME cross-machine -> byte-identical (why EEDEE792 reproduced). My isolation traded byte-identity for concurrent non-clobbering (fine; P2 used output compare). The build-identity factor is the DET-ROOT PATH, not SDK -> VOID-on-skew (n26) aimed wrong; record/check det-root path. DECISIVE TEST ready (detcheck.ps1: pure-5n at default det-root -> expect 7A6C1445), deferred to a quiet node window (x-verify24 + fiscal-set-setc at 99% CPU).")
inl note_of_compiler_cross_machine_repro_30 () = Note ("COMPILER-CROSS-MACHINE-REPRO", 30u32, "2026-10-28T09:40Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-28: cond3 det-root VERIFIED (main Q). cond3 set ONLY SPIRAL_BIN_CACHE_DIR, NOT SPIRAL_DET_ROOT -> DEFAULT det-root C:\\home\\spiral-cache (fs: p2-cond3\\det absent; spiral-cache\\obj-shared\\SpiralCompilerCore + core-src\\single-flight\\9ff1b708 present). So cond3 (default) == install (default) -> both 05E94769 is CONSISTENT with det-root-determines-core, NOT against. Node cores (986266D8/E322D409) differed because the NODE builds SET an isolated SPIRAL_DET_ROOT. Diagnosis holds same-machine. Node test (pure-5n at DEFAULT det-root -> expect 7A6C1445) = the CROSS-machine confirmation (rules out a machine-specific residual on top); deferred to a quiet node window.")
inl note_of_compiler_cross_machine_repro_31 () = Note ("COMPILER-CROSS-MACHINE-REPRO", 31u32, "2026-10-28T10:25Z", "main", "10-28 06:25 main: n28's close condition MET (main, 10-28 06:2x): a box build and a node build of one source hash equal again. The compiler team built pure 5n (source 9E1F72AB) single-flight on the NODE at the DEFAULT det-root (C:\\home\\spiral-cache), core 7A6C1445BF5AF48C, byte-equal to the box's installed 5n core. With the isolated SPIRAL_DET_ROOT the same source gave E322D409. Together with the box result (P2 composite: the condition-3 build and main's install both 05E94769, both at the default root), all six data points fit one rule: the core bytes are a function of source plus det-root path, and nothing else differs between the two machines (same SDK, rc.1). So CCMR's cross-machine byte identity holds at the default det-root; tonight's mismatches came from isolated roots in test methodology. Remaining, build-bound and the compiler team's: record the det-root path in the build attestation, and have Assert-NodeCore return VOID, not throw, when the roots differ, so a by-hash comparison across different roots can't read as a codegen change.")
inl note_of_compiler_cross_machine_repro_32 () = Note ("COMPILER-CROSS-MACHINE-REPRO", 32u32, "2026-10-28T12:00Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-28 attestation+VOID progress (build-free edits):\n(1) ATTESTATION DONE: build.ps1 writes build-identity.json beside each core DLL after a Release build (detRoot, sourceSha16, coreHash16, host, mode, builtUtc). Post-build, not an input -> CCMR-neutral (cannot change the bytes); parses clean; unstaged.\n(2) VOID DESIGN (Assert-NodeCore remote.ps1:49): on node-core != expected, fetch node build-identity.json + read box build-identity (for mode) locally; if node.sourceSha16 == box.sourceSha16 AND node.detRoot != box.detRoot -> VOID (benign det-root diff, bytes=f(source,det-root); node core is functionally identical, offload proceeds) instead of throw; else throw (real staleness). Backward-compat: no build-identity -> current startswith-throw.\nVERIFY (box slot, needs both-rebuilt): rebuild box+node core (both emit build-identity), run a -RequireCore offload where det-roots differ -> confirm VOID+proceed; confirm a genuinely stale core still throws. Impl the VOID edit + verify together on the box slot.")
inl note_of_compiler_cross_machine_repro_33 () = Note ("COMPILER-CROSS-MACHINE-REPRO", 33u32, "2026-10-29T12:41Z", "compiler", "10-29 08:41 compiler: VOID half IMPLEMENTED (n32 design; i574n.github/scripts/remote.ps1 Assert-NodeCore, unstaged, a minimal edit beside main's unstaged route probing). On a node-core mismatch it reads the box's build-identity.json (SPIRAL_BIN_CACHE_DIR or spiral-bin, bin/<mode>/SpiralCompiler/Release/net11.0) and the node's (via the node's env.ps1). If both carry the same sourceSha16 and different detRoot, it prints VOID and the offload proceeds; anything else (identity missing on either side, a different source) throws as before. Verified: parse 0 errors; the throw path with -RequireCore 0000000000000000 still refuses (node core 826D67C69119F61E, which has no build-identity, so it falls back) and the payload did not run. The attestation half is also proven live after the wipe: build-candidate.ps1's pack-6 build wrote build-identity.json {detRoot C:\\home\\spiral-cache, sourceSha16 01c5c617ea82a312, coreHash16 6576367396C93CC4, host ALIAS}. LEFT: exercise the VOID branch once a node build carries the identity (any build from a mirror synced with the unstaged build.ps1) at an isolated SPIRAL_DET_ROOT, then CCMR closes. This also unblocks RUST-DERIVE-INFERENCE's ordering (n5: after CCMR).")
inl note_of_compiler_cross_machine_repro_34 () = Note ("COMPILER-CROSS-MACHINE-REPRO", 34u32, "2026-10-29T17:23Z", "compiler", "VOID branch exercise (2026-10-29): it cannot fire naturally any more. build-identity.json now records detRoot C:\\home\\spiral-cache on BOTH machines: box installed and node installed are identical (source 6dd9ab3b728d1ba0, core 31CC96520D6B7269), and the node's pack 7 candidate (built 17:05Z, host DESKTOP-6E8C14G) also has detRoot C:\\home\\spiral-cache, source 15fa7ff541b2862f, core 9C899B30495F7DC5. With one det-root the VOID branch is a dead fallback; the real acceptance test is a byte-identical core from the box build of the same source. Pending: pack 7 box build after TRAIN4. PASS = box core 9C899B30495F7DC5 for source 15fa7ff541b2862f; any other hash = a remaining machine-dependent input.")
inl note_of_compiler_cross_machine_repro_35 () = Note ("COMPILER-CROSS-MACHINE-REPRO", 35u32, "2026-10-29T19:36Z", "compiler", "10-29 15:4x compiler: ROOT CAUSE FOUND AND FIXED (core). Box and node pack-7 builds of the same source (15fa7ff541b2862f) gave cores E475FDF7 vs 9C899B30. The IL assembly was byte-identical on both (SpiralCompilerCore IL 773A8052EDE19C28, ref 5FA89974), so the F# build is reproducible; the 761 differing bytes (75 runs, .text) came from crossgen2. Same crossgen2 tool on both (set C3AC4658), but the framework it references (-r <spiral-bin dotnet>\\shared\\Microsoft.NETCore.App\\11.0.0-rc.1.26425.128\\*.dll) had only 34 of 193 DLLs on the box: WIPE DAMAGE (rc.2 is partial too, 67; rc.3 is complete). HEAL: the 163 missing files copied from the system dotnet's identical rc.1 (all 34 survivors byte-equal to it; additive, nothing replaced; list in C:\\home\\data\\pack7-review\\rc1-runtime-healed-files.txt), so 0 files now differ from the system copy. build.ps1 Invoke-SpiralReadyToRun now keys the shared r2r-cache on the framework's CONTENT fingerprint (path#sha16 of its DLLs), not its path alone; before, a repaired runtime would have reused images compiled against the broken one. RESULT: box rebuild job-1793302374355 (all 22 images recompiled, 0 from cache): core 9C899B30495F7DC5, IDENTICAL to the node. The host DLL still differs (box B05A6CA27D6FA023 vs node 3AB27C9D378C3D72): next, compare the host IL the same way.")
inl note_of_compiler_cross_machine_repro_36 () = Note ("COMPILER-CROSS-MACHINE-REPRO", 36u32, "2026-10-29T19:45Z", "compiler", "10-29 16:0x compiler: HOST half. Box and node pack-7 hosts differed although the composed host source was identical (1433C90613854D3A on both build logs). Even the host IL differed (box 6CE87C32, node 8D1A4939, same 128,000-byte size, differing only inside compressed binary data, no readable path). Cause: build.ps1:175 passed a candidate's host as its resolved, user-specific path (-p:SpiralHostSource=C:\\Users\\<user>\\AppData\\Local\\spiral-bin-candidates\\<name>\\source\\Program.fs), and F#'s compressed signature data embeds every compiled source's path, which --pathmap does not rewrite. The core avoided this because build.ps1 stages it at <detRoot>/core-src/<mode>/<sha16>/. A second effect: an INSTALL built the host from the repo path while the reviewed candidate used its candidates path, so install-pack's 'SpiralCompiler.dll equal=' could never be True, even on one machine. FIX (build.ps1, untracked like the rest of apps/compiler/scripts): EVERY build, candidate or install, stages the host at <detRoot>/host-src/<sha16>/Program.fs and compiles it from there, so the same host source gives the same path and the same bytes everywhere. Box rebuild job-1793302970034: core 9C899B30495F7DC5 (still = node), host IL 965A5E6CB5AE2746, host DLL F2F6F4CB5A18626E. Left: node rebuild with this build.ps1 once the node's CPU-curve run ends, to show host DLL equality across machines (expected F2F6F4CB).")
inl note_of_compiler_cross_machine_repro_37 () = Note ("COMPILER-CROSS-MACHINE-REPRO", 37u32, "2026-10-30T02:00Z", "compiler", "10-29 22:0x compiler: GATE MET, CLOSED. Node rebuild job-1793313290509-compiler-pack7h-host-repro-2 (the build.ps1 that stages the host at <detRoot>/host-src/<sha16>) of source 15fa7ff541b2862f, finished exit 0: host SpiralCompiler.dll F2F6F4CB5A18626E, core SpiralCompilerCore.dll 9C899B30495F7DC5. The box rebuild job-1793302970034 gave the same two hashes. So host and core are byte-identical across machines (users alias and i574n), and install-by-hash can ship one reviewed DLL to the node without a rebuild. The two causes, both fixed: crossgen2 compiled against a wipe-damaged rc.1 framework (healed; r2r cache now keyed on framework content), and the host compiled from a per-user candidate path (now staged under detRoot).")
inl fiscal_typefeature_drift () = Task ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", "fiscal + lib xsd/xml model with the typefeatureset, running and generated code (user 2026-10-25 15:3x: 'check where we drifted in not utilizing the typefeatureset enough, both on running and generated code'): census 0-0.5 typefeatures/1k on the whole fiscal path vs omniledger 22.6/1k; every generated module 0/1k with 45-327 string aliases", Story, P0, D6, 100u32, Done, "TYPEFEATURESET-ENFORCE", "fiscal,xsd,typefeatures,lib,codegen", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift () = Planning ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", Minutes 1440u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_1 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 1u32, "2026-10-25T19:50Z", "fiscal", "Filed from the 15:4x audit; findings and the ordered plan follow in note 2.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_2 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 2u32, "2026-10-25T19:50Z", "fiscal", "15:4x fiscal: audit, measured and confirmed in the code (session scratchpad typefeature-census/census.py, calibrated on mega_omniledger_erp_kernel/main.spi: 272 constructs / 12,031 lines = 22.6/1k vs main's census 276 / 12,030 = 22.9/1k). Census: fiscal/src 586 lines, 0 typefeatures (0 unions, 0 nominals); lib xsd 0/437, xsd_resolve 0/305, xsd_emit 1/1,976 (0.5/1k), xsd_regex 0/775, xsd_constraints 0/137, xml 0/400 (6 host macros); each of the 17 generated modules 0/1k, with type x = string aliases from 45 (FatturaPA) to 327 (CT-e/GTV-e 4.00) per module.\nDrift, by leverage:\n(R1) lib xsd: one flat union `xsd` for three sorts (type definitions Simple/Complex/Derived; particles Sequence/Choice/Element/AnyContent/Empty; Attribute), nested as untyped list xsd, so Complex content can hold an Attribute and attribute lists can hold Elements; consumers carry `| _ =>` catch-alls. Target: an indexed union `xsd s` over a sort index (type_def / particle / attribute) whose cases fix their children's sort.\n(R2) positional tuple payloads with sentinels: Element : string * string * i32 * i32 * option xsd (name vs type ref both bare strings; maxOccurs -1 = unbounded); Attribute : ... * bool * string * ... (fixed \"\" = none). Target: records, an occurs union (Bounded n | Unbounded), option for fixed.\n(R3) xsd_emit emitted.enumeration_key: \"\" = none, \"dfa\\n<table>\" / \"union\\n<values>\" (sentinel + encoded rows). Target: a union NoKey | DfaKey table | UnionKey values.\n(R4) xml.required_child / required_attribute failwith, so generated readers crash on malformed input. Target: a typed read result (missing element / missing attribute / bad value) in the generated _of_xml, failwith only at the test boundary.\n(R5) names as plain strings: XSD qualified names ({uri}local), Spiral type names and function names. Target: nominals (:! qualified_name, :! type_name, :! function_name) so they cannot be mixed. Smaller: fiscal family_root.known_defect : option string (a typed expected-failure), corpus_checks' count + 1000000 failure marker (a result union).\n(G1) generated facet-constrained values are type x = string plus a free x_is_valid. Target: a nominal per constrained type whose only constructor is the validator (x_of_string : string -> option x), so an unvalidated string cannot reach a document (README's known limit).\n(G2) 0 indexed cases in all fiscal output: the versioned-family GADT witness (generate_versioned_family: V1 :: ... -> version_witness v1) exists only in xsd_emit's own test; fiscal's NF-e/CT-e/MDF-e families do not use it. Target: wire the BR families through it (FISCAL-GENERIC step 2).\nOrder and gates, fixed now: R3, then R2 (each a lib slice; gate = kit GREEN + fiscal full build green + byte-identical generated output, because both are internal); then R1 (gate = the same + typefeature density of xsd.spi >= 10/1k); then G1 (gate = generated modules compile, the 13+ examples validate with 0 .NET messages, and a negative test shows an unvalidated string no longer type-checks as the nominal); then R4 and R5; G2 rides FISCAL-GENERIC. Done when the fiscal path (src + xsd lib) is >= 10/1k and generated modules use nominals for every facet-constrained type.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_3 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 3u32, "2026-10-25T20:17Z", "fiscal", "16:2x fiscal: R3 drafted in landing form, session scratchpad typefeature-r3/apply.py <xsd_emit.spi> [--apply] (base-hash pinned 5C364F8DA3287371, all-or-nothing, counts checked per anchor). emitted.enumeration_key : string (\"\" = none, \"dfa\\n<table>\", \"union\\n<joined values>\") becomes emitted.value_set : value_set with :| value_set = NoValueSet | DfaValueSet : string | UnionValueSet : list string. The union case carries the value LIST, not the newline-joined text, so the encoded-row smell goes too. first_with_key compares through same_value_set (an explicit pair match plus same_strings over the lists) instead of string =. share_unions matches NoValueSet instead of testing = \"\". New test value_sets_compare_by_kind_and_values (equal lists share; a prefix does not; a table never equals a value list; NoValueSet never shares). Dry run ok on the tree; on a copy: 0 enumeration_key left, format --check clean (the test is written in the formatter's own layout), join_lines keeps 16 other uses. The dry run's anchor-count guard caught a substring overlap (item.enumeration_key = \"\" inside share_unions) before anything was written. Gate, fixed now: kit gate on xsd_emit.spi (GREEN), then a fiscal full build whose generated/ modules are byte-identical to the build before R3 (R3 is internal: value sets decide only which enumerations share), compared by SHA-256 per module. Ordering: the queued XSD-EMIT-BUDGET slice also edits xsd_emit.spi (its header), so after it lands, R3 is re-based on the new hash (re-run the dry run; the anchors do not touch line 1) before its gate.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_4 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 4u32, "2026-10-25T20:26Z", "fiscal", "16:3x fiscal: order revision, recorded before acting. R1 and R2 merge into one remodel of the xsd model. Reason: both rewrite the same ~50 match/construct sites (xsd_emit 28, xsd 12, xsd_resolve 7, json_schema 7 (a fourth consumer that rebuilds Element), fiscal corpus_checks 3), and R1's sort-indexed cases must carry R2's records anyway, so doing R2 first would touch every site twice. R3 stays first (separate field, already drafted).\nDesign under test (session scratchpad gadt-proto/xsd_proto.spi, formatter-clean; it follows omniledger's `union rec ... | C :: payload -> t idx` form):\n- sort indices as nominals: type_def, particle, attribute_decl.\n- :| ^ xsd sort with Simple/Complex/Derived -> xsd type_def; Sequence/Choice/Element/Empty/AnyContent -> xsd particle; Attribute -> xsd attribute_decl. Complex carries xsd particle content and list (xsd attribute_decl).\n- payload records: element_decl { name; type_ref : option string; occurs }, attribute { name; required; fixed : option string }, and occurs = { min; max : occurs_bound } with occurs_bound = Bounded i32 | Unbounded, replacing -1.\nThe prototype checks:\n- recursive walks over list (xsd particle) / list (xsd attribute_decl) with NO `_` catch-all, which type-check only if the index really restricts the cases;\n- a runtime round trip over a typed list (summaries 'Code' and 'C(a;v)');\n- `test an_attribute_is_not_a_particle fails \"Unification failure\"`: an Attribute inside a particle list must not type-check.\nRuns on the remote kit worker node once kit2 clears a slot (this machine: 788 MB free, both heavy slots held). Rule, fixed now: all 3 checks pass -> draft the remodel slice on this design; the negative test compiles anyway -> the index does not constrain, so revisit the encoding (e.g. the sort as a phantom on a record wrapper) before any site edits; the runtime test fails on 'Cannot dyn' or similar -> keep the GADT at construction only and convert to the runtime row, per the eoie-granular-spiral placement note.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_5 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 5u32, "2026-10-25T20:35Z", "fiscal", "16:4x fiscal: prototype PASSED on the remote kit worker node (core guard 826D67C69119F61E ok; 126 s; 2/2): sort_indexed_xsd_keeps_children_typed passed (GADT values live in typed runtime lists list (xsd particle) / list (xsd attribute_decl); the walks with NO `_` catch-all type-check, so the index restricts the cases), and an_attribute_is_not_a_particle passed as an expected failure (its rust target fails, matched by the fails \"Unification failure\" header): an Attribute inside a particle list no longer type-checks. Note 4's rule: all 3 checks hold -> the R1+R2 remodel is drafted on this design. One design choice, fixed now: generic passes over the tree (xsd_resolve rename_item and friends, xsd_emit's walks) become per-sort functions in one `:&` group (rename_type / rename_particle / rename_attribute), each monomorphic. A single polymorphic `\\* s.` join point would need polymorphic recursion across sorts; omniledger's 123 forall functions are mostly inl over values whose index is fixed. `\\* s.` stays only where a pass is truly generic. Also seen while tracing G1: a generated constrained type's validator is declared but never called. In generated_mdfe_v1_00, x_digest_value_type_is_valid exists while the digest_value field takes any string and the reader assigns text straight through; G1's nominal-with-validator-only-constructor closes exactly that.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_6 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 6u32, "2026-10-25T20:47Z", "fiscal", "17:0x fiscal: (1) census correction. census.py's indexed-case regex ([^=]* also matched newlines) merged a multi-line GADT block into one match and so undercounted. It now counts GADT case headers: a capitalized constructor, then :: and whitespace or end of line, which excludes list patterns (x :: xs, XmlElement (...) :: rest). Recalibrated: omniledger main.spi 337 constructs / 12,031 lines = 28.0/1k (161 indexed cases, 176 exists). The fiscal-path figures are unchanged (tree xsd 0.0, xsd_emit 0.5, fiscal/src 0.0, all of lib 1.1/1k), so note 2's conclusions stand; use 28.0 as the reference from now on.\n(2) R1+R2 remodel, file 1 of 5 drafted: session scratchpad xsd-remodel/draft/xsd.spi (tree base 5CF595CEF83FE12C), format --check clean, 17.6/1k (9 GADT cases), above R1's >= 10 gate for xsd.spi. It has the sort nominals type_def / particle / attribute_decl; :| ^ xsd sort with inline record payloads (they reference xsd type_def, so they are mutually recursive with the union; inline record types avoid alias ordering); occurs = { min; max : occurs_bound } (Bounded | Unbounded, replacing -1) plus is_repeated; type_ref : option string and fixed : option string (replacing \"\"); per-sort canonical_type / canonical_particle / canonical_attributes in one :& group; schema lists typed (simple/complex : list (xsd type_def), elements : list (xsd particle)). Tests: the 6 existing tests rewritten to record patterns, plus occurs_bounds_are_typed. The external surface was checked: complex_of lost its unused attributes parameter (no outside caller), occurs became occurs_of (no outside caller), xpath_eval's attributes_of is its own, and corpus_checks' enumeration_count call still fits. Next files: xsd_resolve (rename_* per sort, references, type_names), json_schema (rebuilds Element), xsd_emit (28 sites), fiscal corpus_checks (3 sites). The gate runs once all 5 are drafted (note 2's rule).")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_7 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 7u32, "2026-10-25T20:52Z", "fiscal", "17:1x fiscal: remodel file 2 of 5 drafted: xsd-remodel/draft/xsd_resolve.spi (tree base 170A91102936C86C), produced by resolve_transform.py (anchored, fails on any leftover old name) plus the formatter, then format --check clean. Changes: type_names is over list (xsd type_def) through type_name_of (3 cases, no catch-all); rename_item becomes the :& group rename_type / rename_particle / rename_particles / rename_attributes / rename_inline plus rename_types, with rename_ref mapping option type refs (definition still renames only type names, never element names, as before); references_of / references_in / attribute_types become type_references / particle_references / particles_references / attribute_references / inline_references plus types_references. reference_into adds only Some refs, so absent type attributes no longer push \"\" into reference lists; unresolved_in skipped \"\" anyway, so unresolved_count is unchanged, and xsd_emit's uses of references get checked when it is drafted. Its own census is 0/1k (it declares no types, it consumes the GADT); R1's density gate stays measured on xsd.spi (draft 17.6/1k). Left: json_schema, xsd_emit (28 sites), fiscal corpus_checks.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_8 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 8u32, "2026-10-25T21:00Z", "fiscal", "17:2x fiscal: remodel file 3 of 5 drafted: xsd-remodel/draft/json_schema.spi (tree base 751A1D9A505DA57F) via json_transform.py (anchored, refuses any leftover positional constructor), formatter-clean. object_type returns xsd type_def; property_element(s) return typed particles through two local helpers, single (min) : occurs (exactly one, or optional when min is 0) and element_particle; JSON Schema's maxItems absence (count_field's own -1 default) becomes Unbounded right where the bound is built; type-less elements get type_ref None instead of an empty string; definition_types returns typed lists. Left: xsd_emit (28 sites) and fiscal corpus_checks (3).")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_9 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 9u32, "2026-10-25T21:19Z", "fiscal", "18:0x fiscal: all 5 remodel files drafted, format-clean, 0 positional constructors and 0 bare xsd annotations left (session scratchpad xsd-remodel/draft; tree bases xsd 5CF595CEF83FE12C, xsd_resolve 170A91102936C86C, xsd_emit 5C364F8DA3287371, json_schema 751A1D9A505DA57F, fiscal corpus_checks 6E8D47A20708750D).\nxsd_emit (emit_transform.py, 5 anchored sections, refuses anchor mismatches):\n- each sort-mixed *_named becomes *_named_type (xsd type_def) + *_named_group (particle group) for emit, reader, writer and example; entry walks split into *_of over type definitions and root_*_of over root elements;\n- dereference per sort (a ref-only element takes the root's name, type and inline and keeps its own occurs, as before);\n- a three-way :| occurs_shape = Required | Optional | Repeated with shape_of drives with_occurs, occurs_expression, occurs_part, occurs_example and both choice expressions (the old `max = 1 / min = 0` test, stated once);\n- reference_type_of / reference_dependency_of / reference_kind_of / value_example_of take option refs (None means the builtin string; no \"\" sentinel inside the emitter);\n- fixed is an option in attribute examples.\nThe emitted text is meant to stay byte-identical (incl. the existing `if false then failwith \"unreachable\"` choice preamble, which is FISCAL-GENERATED-STYLE's to change). corpus_checks: census per sort (census_type / particle / particles / attributes / types), catalog_states over typed lists.\nHonest metric: the census counts declarations, so only xsd.spi rises (0 -> 17.6/1k); the four consumers stay 0, which puts the five files at about 9 / 3,555 lines = 2.5/1k, short of note 2's >= 10/1k done line. New step R6, found while drafting: xsd_emit has three parallel families (readers, writers, examples) that each repeat the same per-sort walk (named type / group / record / content / items / choice / attributes / entry walks). That is open dispatch, so it becomes one walk over a prototype with three instances (reader, writer, example) that supply the per-node text, which collapses the triplication and is the typefeatureset form the review rule names. R6 comes after this remodel's gate.\nGate for R1+R2, fixed now:\n(1) kit gate XSD-REMODEL on the 4 lib files (apply = stage-then-commit, all 4 base hashes checked);\n(2) the fiscal full build with the draft corpus_checks: every test green, and models_the_corpus_census still 580 files / 12,077 elements;\n(3) every generated/ module byte-identical (SHA-256 per file) to the build now running (job-1792958908945, the pre-remodel baseline).\nOrdering: XSD-EMIT-BUDGET (header line) lands at the end of that job first, so xsd_emit's draft gets the same header edit and is re-based on the new hash before (1).")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_10 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 10u32, "2026-10-25T21:28Z", "fiscal", "18:3x fiscal: G1 design, from a census and the emitter's existing code. Constrained versus vacuous string types (type x = string with a validator holding at least one check, versus `= true && true`): nfe_v4 249 of 252; cte_v4_00 274 of 327; fatturapa_1_2_3 38 of 45; ksef_fa_3 50 of 71. So G1 covers almost every simple type.\nMechanism: reuse the enumeration path. Enumerations already round-trip through T_of_string (read through xml.required_value), T_to_string and T_example, which are exactly the call shapes a validated string needs. type_kind gains KindValidated; node_expression, attribute readers and writers, value_node and value_example treat it like KindEnum. For a constrained type the emission is:\n- nominal x_t = string;\n- x_t_is_valid, unchanged;\n- x_t_of_string (s) : option x_t = if x_t_is_valid s then Some (x_t s) else None;\n- x_t_to_string (x : x_t) : string = match x with x_t s => s;\n- x_t_example (k) : x_t = x_t \"<example>\", where the example is already validated by the generator's simple_example search (patterns_accept && lengths_accept);\n- x_t_example_text k = x_t_to_string (x_t_example k).\nThe inheriting case (no own pattern or length; it reuses the base's example text) wraps that text. Vacuous validators stay plain aliases (KindText): the rule is a nominal exactly when validator_text has a check.\nHonest limit: Spiral has no module privacy, so the nominal's constructor stays public; a deliberate x_t \"raw\" still compiles. What G1 rules out is the accidental path (a raw string where x_t is expected is a type error), plus readers that never call the validator (the 17:0x finding: x_digest_value_type_is_valid was declared and never called).\nGate, fixed now:\n(1) every generated module compiles and passes its example test;\n(2) the 13+ BR examples and the CFDI/UBL/FatturaPA/KSeF examples validate with 0 .NET messages;\n(3) a negative test in xsd_emit, `fails \"Unification failure\"`, passing a raw string literal to a function that takes a constrained nominal;\n(4) generated output changes only in the declared forms (type -> nominal, plus the new _of_string / _to_string), and every reader and writer call site goes through them.\nOrder: G1 comes after the R1+R2 gate (it edits the same emitter).")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_11 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 11u32, "2026-10-25T21:40Z", "fiscal", "18:4x fiscal: R6 design (the three parallel emit families as one walk). Spiral's open dispatch is prototype + instance on the nominal type of the first argument, resolved at compile time (apps/compiler/tmp/samples/contract_atomic_flash_loan_clearing/main.spi: `prototype assess policy from to result : policy from to result -> string`, `instance assess conservative_policy = fun (conservative_policy op) => ...`; contract_branch_as_value: `instance lower cpu|cuda|wasm`). So a family-generic walk specializes per family with no runtime dispatch.\nShape:\n- one nominal per family wrapping its accumulator: reader_family (lines, functions), writer_family (children, attributes, functions), example_family (lines, functions);\n- prototypes for the per-node text, each taking the family first: on_choice (owner, choice name, occurs shape, items), on_inline_element (element, inner name, inner type), on_referenced_element (element), on_any, on_attribute (attribute, type text, kind), on_group_arm (choice name, group name, first), on_choice_arm, finish_record (name, prefix);\n- the walk itself (named type / named group / record / content / items / choice / attributes / entry walks) exists once, generic over the family with \\* f., calling the prototypes; it replaces the readers, writers and examples triplets (about 600 of xsd_emit's 2,065 lines today).\nGate, fixed now: the generated output is byte-identical to the pre-R6 build (R6 is a pure refactor), plus a census check that xsd_emit gains the prototype/instance constructs (3 instances of each of about 8 prototypes) and loses about 400 lines. Order: after the R1+R2 gate, before G1 (G1 then edits one walk instead of three).")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_12 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 12u32, "2026-10-25T21:48Z", "fiscal", "18:5x fiscal: R1+R2 drafts TYPE-CHECK and pass their own tests, proven on the remote kit worker node (this machine's live lib/spiral plus the 4 drafts, lib_gate per file, core guard 826D67C69119F61E): xsd 7/7, xsd_resolve 3/3, json_schema 3/3, xsd_emit 5/5 (including families_share_identical_closed_types_once and versioned_families_read_through_one_witness, which timed out at 300 s locally under load; on the node they fit), 0 type errors. Two draft bugs were found and fixed on the way:\n(a) a literal inside a constructor pattern (| Bounded 1i32 =>) does not parse; the block is \"left out of its module\" and 13 cascaded \"unbound\" errors followed. shape_of now binds and compares, and with_occurs / occurs_expression match on shape_of.\n(b) a list in a tuple slot of a test pattern (Sequence s, (Attribute a) :: Nil) parsed as a list of pairs, the documented comma-vs-:: trap; it is now parenthesized.\nBoth lessons are in memory (spiral-macro-gotchas, list-sugar-not-cons). The remodel kit apply script (xsd-remodel/apply.ps1: base-hash guarded, stage-commit with lock retries and rollback) dry-runs clean on the tree. Next, by note 9's gate: after job-1792958908945 ends (its last step lands XSD-EMIT-BUDGET on xsd_emit's header), re-base the xsd_emit draft with the same one-line header edit and update its base hash; kit gate XSD-REMODEL; land; fiscal full build with the draft corpus_checks; generated/ byte-identical to that job's output.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_13 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 13u32, "2026-10-25T21:51Z", "fiscal", "18:5x fiscal: the R1+R2 gate is ONE serial job, job-1792965018316-fiscal-post-remodel (session scratchpad post-remodel-run.ps1), after job-1792958908945. Steps: (1) hash every generated/*.spi of that build as the baseline (refuses fewer than 10 modules); (2) re-base the xsd_emit draft on the budget header when XSD-EMIT-BUDGET landed (checks that the tree and the draft differ only in line 1, then retargets apply.ps1's base); (3) the apply dry run must pass; (4) kit gate XSD-REMODEL on xsd / xsd_resolve / xsd_emit / json_schema, then kit land.ps1; (5) fiscal corpus_checks.spi replaced by its draft only if the tree is still 6E8D47A20708750D; (6) fiscal full build; (7) every generated module byte-identical to the baseline, printing VERDICT GREEN or VERDICT RED with the changed/added files. Coordination: kit's rename release wants a quiescent window (no queued jobs); if kit announces it first, this queue is paused for it.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_14 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 14u32, "2026-10-25T22:49Z", "fiscal", "G1 drafted and chained (2026-10-25 18:5x). Transform scratchpad\\xsd-remodel\\g1_transform.py over the R1+R2 xsd_emit draft: type_kind gains KindValidated; simple_kind returns it for pattern/length facets without enumerations; the 7 KindEnum reader/writer/example arms become `| KindEnum | KindValidated =>`; emit_named_type emits validated_text for constrained simples: `nominal ty = string`, ty_is_valid, ty_of_string (only way in, via the validator), ty_to_string, ty_example : ty, ty_example_text. Unconstrained and facet-free inherited simples stay `type ty = string`. Tests: new constrained_strings_become_validated_nominals (TCnpj nominal, TFree alias); families test expectations move to `nominal x_code` (the old negative check `type x_code` would pass vacuously). Diff +63/-17, format check clean. g1-apply.ps1 derives its base at run time (tree xsd_emit == current remodel draft, so it refuses before R1+R2 lands; controls: dry ok, -Apply == draft 3A222CDB3C28CA2C, live tree refused). Runs as the tail of job-1792965018316-fiscal-post-remodel (one serial job; separate link job-1792968389171 stopped) only when the R1+R2 build exits 0: kit gate XSD-G1-NOMINALS on xsd_emit, land, full fiscal build, report changed/missing modules + nominal count. Expected semantic change: readers of pattern-constrained fields now fail on out-of-pattern input (required_value over of_string); a corpus sample that violates its own pattern surfaces in the build. Node proof skipped: node at 100% CPU with peer stages.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_15 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 15u32, "2026-10-25T22:55Z", "fiscal", "R3 re-based onto G1 and chained (2026-10-25 18:55). scratchpad\\xsd-remodel\\r3_transform.py = typefeature-r3/apply.py as a source->target transform; every fixed-count anchor matched over the G1 draft unchanged, NoValueSet sites still 6, closing guard (no enumeration_key left) kept; format clean, no relayout; +40/-14. Generic scratchpad\\xsd-remodel\\slice-apply.ps1 (-Transform -From -To; base = hash of the From draft at run time, refuses until that slice is landed) with wrappers g1-apply.ps1 / r3-apply.ps1. Controls on a fake lib: r3 before g1 refused; g1 -Apply 3A222CDB3C28CA2C; r3 dry ok and -Apply D0647AEE317C8322 == draft. Runner post-g1-run.ps1 (tail of job-1792965018316): kit gate + land XSD-G1-NOMINALS, then XSD-R3-VALUE-SET, then ONE fiscal build for both (CPU: ~2.5 h per build; R3 is output-neutral, so a red build bisects G1 first). Remaining order: R6 (prototype walk), R4, R5; G2 rides FISCAL-GENERIC; FISCAL-CII-PROFILES starts after R1 lands.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_16 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 16u32, "2026-10-25T23:04Z", "fiscal", "G1 gate gap closed (2026-10-25 19:04). Note 2's G1 gate asks for a negative type test; the xsd_emit text test only checks emitted text. Added: xsd_emit.any_validated_type (schema) : option string (first KindValidated entry of kinds_of; coded enumerations are KindEnum, so it only names G1 nominals, never a pre-G1 nominal/union whose rejection would pass vacuously), asserted in the G1 test; fiscal generator.spi gains family_nominal_check, emitting per generated module `test <module>_keeps_raw_strings_out fails \"Unification failure\" = <t>_to_string \"raw\"` (message form from apps/cells/priv/new_project hitpoints_and_milliseconds_do_not_mix). Transform scratchpad\\xsd-remodel\\generator_g1_transform.py, base 0E78FEBB65E1A186, format-stable; applied by post-g1-run.ps1 after the R3 land and before the build (src is read by the running build, so not now). .NET leg: build.ps1:95 already fails on 'validation messages [1-9]', so the build exit covers it. Cost rule, written before measuring: keep the negative test in every module only if summed generated-module time grows <= 5% vs the post-remodel build (both builds tee to scratchpad\\xsd-remodel\\{remodel,g1}-build.log; the runner prints the percentage); otherwise restrict it to generated_mdfe_v1_00 (smallest, 292 KB). Order amendment to note 9 (R6 before G1): its reason was 'G1 then edits one walk instead of three'; with or-patterns G1 is 7 one-token arm edits either way, so G1 (output-visible typefeature) goes first and R6 re-bases over it.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_17 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 17u32, "2026-10-25T23:24Z", "fiscal", "R6 drafted, probed and chained (2026-10-25 19:24). Probes (%TEMP%\\r6-probe, Cells rust, compile-gated jobs): an explicit `\\* f.` walk calling a prototype on f fails ETY-ForallVarConstraintError (constraints must be on the forall: `\\* f {part; first_only}.`, separator ';' as in sm'.slice); `:! reading = ()` values do not construct (`reading ()` is Got () Expected () -> _), so family markers are single-case unions `:| reading = | Reading`; explicit-forall + unions PASS 1/1 (job-1792970138487); inferred generalization (no forall, family unannotated) incl. a `:& :~` mutual-recursion group PASS 2/2 (job-1792970181816). Rule written before probing: inferred if it works, else explicit forall if <=120 cols, else one record-returning prototype -> inferred. Draft: scratchpad\\xsd-remodel\\r6_transform.py over draft-r3 removes the reader/writer/example blocks (33 functions) and adds markers Reading/Writing/Exampling, walked {parts; attribute_parts; functions}, element_view / attribute_view records (named fields, not 6-7 positional args), 10 prototypes (derived_prefix, record_texts, choice_part, element_part, any_part, attribute_step, element_arm, group_arm, choice_text, first_arm_only) x 3 instances = 30, one walk (named_type/record/content/inline/items/choice/arms/attributes, types_walk/roots_walk); examples keep first-arm-only via first_arm_only, incl. the unrecognized-head failwith. Format clean; 2147 -> 1975 lines (-172, not note 9's ~400: G1/R3 had already shared helpers). Chain proof on a fake lib: G1 1B5DBC5A -> R3 DD6D5C33 -> R6 A7EF16DC == draft. Runner: post-g1-run.ps1 continues only on G1+R3 GREEN: hash generated/, kit gate + land XSD-R6-PROTOTYPE-WALK, census line, then `build.ps1 -Files generator` only (identity needs generation, not 25 module compiles: saves ~2.5 h), VERDICT GREEN iff every generated module is byte-identical. Next: R4 (typed read result), R5 (name nominals).")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_18 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 18u32, "2026-10-26T05:25Z", "fiscal", "G1+R3+R6 chain PROVEN on the worker node (2026-10-26 01:25): xsd_emit.spi 7/7 passed on rust (names, enumerations, families, versioned witness, complements, constrained_strings_become_validated_nominals = G1, value_sets_compare_by_kind_and_values = R3; the R6 prototype walk carries every reader/writer/example test). Built from today's landed tree by the real g1/r3/r6-apply.ps1 (base guards ok), node job job-1792991812170-fiscal-xsd-chain5, SPIRAL_CELLS_JOBS=2, peak tree 1.5 GB. Three draft bugs found by the node runs and fixed in the transforms before any local gate: (1) G1 validated_text had multi-line fitted_definition args inside a ++# chain (parse error, block dropped) -> bind each piece with := first; (2) R3 same_strings had list patterns in a tuple slot without parens -> | (x :: a_rest), (y :: b_rest) =>; (3) R6 record_walk's := gathered = attributes_walk ... called a later group member unannotated (Got .parts Expected .attribute_parts) -> := (gathered : walked) =. All three are known traps in the gotchas memory (lines 29/140 + tuple-list), re-hit because I queued without grepping it. Stage note: a lib copy must keep lib/spiral/near/**/*.spi (package.spiproj lists near/), else every test fails 'Module near_wallet.spi is not loaded'. Next: local kit gate + land for G1 (with generator.spi), R3, R6, then one fiscal build (post-g1-run.ps1).")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_19 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 19u32, "2026-10-26T06:37Z", "fiscal", "NEW DONE LINE, user 2026-10-26 02:37: 'iterate until we're sota then get back to fiscal, we need to beat the 5 mega samples with the complete typefeatureset'. This replaces note 2's >= 10/1k. Rule written before any further remodel:\nMEASURE (one script, same regexes for both sides): features F = gadt_case (| C :: ...), exists, forall (\\* t. / forall), nominal (:! / nominal), prototype (:? :+ / prototype instance); density = count per 1k lines. Sides: RUNNING = lib fiscal-path files (json_schema, xml, xpath, xpath_eval, xsd, xsd_constraints, xsd_emit, xsd_regex, xsd_resolve) + fiscal/src; GENERATED = all fiscal/generated modules.\nBAR (beat all five): for every F, density(F, side) >= max over the 5 mega samples. Maxima measured 2026-10-26: gadt_case 22.3 (zeta), exists 58.7 (zeta), forall 62.6 (brzozowski), nominal 14.1 (brzozowski), prototype 1.6 (brzozowski). Baseline now: RUNNING (6.0k lines, chain draft xsd_emit) gadt 1.5, exists 1.2, forall 1.5, nominal 0.5, prototype 6.7; GENERATED (257k lines) gadt 0, exists 0, forall 0, nominal ~0, prototype 0.\nLOAD-BEARING clause (anti-gaming, gate-met-is-not-goal-met): only constructs that do modeling work count: a GADT case whose index some function refines on; an existential that is both packed and opened; a nominal with >= 2 use sites that separate values; a forall instantiated at >= 2 types; a prototype with >= 2 instances. The census reports raw AND load-bearing counts; the bar is on load-bearing. Byte-identical or test-equal behavior is still required per slice (fiscal build green, examples validate).\nMETHOD: one slice at a time, drafted as an anchored transform, proven on the worker node first (Cells tests of the touched lib files + fiscal build there), then kit gate + land locally. Order by gap x reach: (1) census with the load-bearing clause; (2) RUNNING: xml/xpath/xsd_constraints remodel (existential node kinds, indexed axes/predicates), fiscal/src families as a GADT witness (G2) with typed corpus checks; (3) GENERATED: emitter output modeled with indexed choices, existential any-content, nominal facets (G1 is the first), versioned witnesses. Only after the bar holds on both sides: back to FISCAL-COMMIT-HYGIENE and the rest.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_20 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 20u32, "2026-10-26T06:51Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 02:51 fiscal: local kit gate XSD-CHAIN-G1-R3-R6 GREEN (job-1792994357060; type errors 0, rows 7 red 0, livemd round trip held) via chain-apply.ps1 (dry run 9FBACD5E -> 53B3101C, the node-proven file). Verdict file written; land.ps1 runs only after the node fiscal build (job-1792994170131) says GREEN, per the order rule of 05:5x. main 06:5x: xsd*/xml/xpath* are excluded from LIB-EDITION3-ROUND batches until fiscal reports them landed; drafts land first.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_21 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 21u32, "2026-10-26T06:54Z", "fiscal", "S1 xsd_regex typed core, design and gate fixed before drafting (2026-10-26 02:54). Contract (every external use, measured): xsd_emit uses rx, dfa_state, dfa_move, compile_pattern, dfa_of_pattern, dfa_of_pattern_within, dfa_of_values, dfa_table_text, pattern_matches, example_with, repeated; fiscal corpus.spi uses lines_of; generated modules use only table_matches (47 call sites, the runtime validator). These keep their names, signatures and results; the typed core sits underneath, the way mega_brzozowski_derivatives models the same domain:\n(1) alphabet as a type parameter: :! code_point = u32 (XSD's Unicode alphabet) and a small test alphabet, with prototypes symbol_compare / class_member over the alphabet (instances per nominal), so a pattern over one alphabet cannot be run on another;\n(2) rx alphabet indexed by its normal form: GADT cases | Raw :: ... -> rx_form raw | Normalized :: ... -> rx_form normal, and a smart-constructor layer that returns only normal forms (alternatives sorted and deduplicated, the current rx_compare/sort_leaves rules), so the derivative operates on normal forms by type;\n(3) nullable_evidence as a GADT (Nullable / NotNullable carrying the reason), returned with each derivative step as a derivative_step certificate, packed existentially (exists form. ...) where the step's resulting form is only known at runtime;\n(4) DFA: states and moves as an existential over the state count, exists n. dfa_rows alphabet n, with each row's moves carried as a sorted-disjoint witness (low <= high, no overlap) checked once at construction; table_matches keeps its text-table runtime form, so generated code is unchanged.\nGATE: (a) xsd_regex.spi's own tests + fiscal corpus_checks every_corpus_pattern_compiles / matches_like_dotnet_on_every_probe / dfa_matches_like_dotnet_on_every_probe / table_matches_like_dotnet_on_every_probe all green (271 patterns, cross-family oracle); (b) generated output byte-identical (dfa tables and validators unchanged); (c) census2 load-bearing density of xsd_regex.spi rises on every feature, reported per feature; (d) no slowdown of table_matches (same text-table code path, untouched). Proven on the worker node first (lib copy incl. near/, Cells tests of xsd_regex + corpus_checks), then kit gate + land; main excludes xsd*/xml/xpath* from LIB-EDITION3-ROUND until fiscal reports them landed.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_22 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 22u32, "2026-10-26T06:56Z", "fiscal", "Feasibility finding that re-scopes S1, recorded before drafting (2026-10-26 02:56): the megas' existential density is compile-time proof code. mega_zeta has 427 inl definitions and 0 let; its exists packs are static certificates such as forged-witness rejections checked during partial evaluation (main.spi:7, PositivePositionalPredecessorAccessibilityStep (exists (...))). Spiral existentials cannot be dyn'ed into runtime vars (gotchas line 79), so fiscal's runtime pattern parsing (patterns read from XSD files) cannot honestly carry exists. Where each feature can be load-bearing in fiscal: GENERATED side = everything is static at generation time, so each emitted validator/enumeration/choice carries an inl compile-time certificate checked by the compiler at build time (sealed DFA certificate per pattern with exists over the state count, derivative-step certificates, enumeration-value witnesses, indexed choice cases, nominal facets = G1, versioned family witness = G2); this is the megas' own style and where 257k lines can beat the bar at scale. RUNNING side = xsd_regex normal-form indexing (rx shaped by form f, derive_by keeps the form by type; today's AsWritten/Similar), nullable evidence GADT on the static paths, alphabet nominals + symbol prototypes, typed DFA construction; existentials there only in static catalogs (XSD builtins, block ranges, escape classes) and test certificates. S1 therefore splits: S1a running xsd_regex (forms, evidence, alphabet), S1b emitter certificates for generated validators (the large exists/forall/gadt lever). Gate unchanged (note 21); S1b adds: generated modules still compile and their tests pass, and a forged certificate test fails at compile time (the megas' negative-sample pattern).")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_23 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 23u32, "2026-10-26T07:02Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 03:02 fiscal: S1b cost experiment running on the worker node (job-1792998194942-fiscal-s1b-probe; session scratchpad s1b-probe/make_probe.py + stage/sweep.ps1). Edition-3 existential form taken from mega_brzozowski canonical_form_certificate_edition3/normalization_derivation.spi: case payload (\\? shape. T shape), pack (\\? value), open := \\? shape. v = packed, inside := ^ (compile-time) functions. Design constraint from apps/compiler/tmp/AGENTS.md: EJP0041 caps union nesting at 256, so a certificate indexed by the state count (Peano succ per row) cannot cover DFAs past 256 states (fiscal's go to 4096); the probe therefore certifies PER ROW: moves_witness sorted_disjoint (a sorted, disjoint, in-range moves list, forged rows fail at compile time) packed existentially, rows counted by an inl fold. Sizes 50 / 400 / 3000 static rows, one Cells slot; measured: compile time, EJP0040/EJP0041 or timeout per size. Rule fixed now: S1b goes ahead in the emitter only if 3000 rows compile under 120 s with no EJP error; else the certificate shape is redesigned (per-table hash witness, or certificates only for tables under a row budget) before any emitter change.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_24 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 24u32, "2026-10-26T07:07Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 03:07 fiscal: S1a drafted (session scratchpad s1a/s1a_transform.py over the tree xsd_regex.spi; draft 3A36A6BC1B24A3D4, format clean, 775 -> 856 lines). (1) derivative_form is a GADT indexed by :! as_written / :! similar (AsWritten :: derivative_form as_written, Similar :: derivative_form similar); alt_by / seq_by / derive_by are \\* f. over it, same results. (2) nullability evidence: nullable_evidence answer indexed by :! accepts_empty / rejects_empty, 13 cases (one per reason), runtime-safe via union nullability = Accepts : nullable_evidence accepts_empty | Rejects : nullable_evidence rejects_empty (fixed index per case, no runtime existential); nullable reads it; accept_reason / reject_reason match only their own index's cases, so they compile only if GADT refinement rules the others out (the load-bearing property itself); new test nullability_carries_its_evidence. census2 xsd_regex.spi load-bearing per 1k: gadt 0 -> 17.5, forall 0 -> 3.5, nominal 0 -> 2.3 (raw 4.7). Node proof queued: job-1792998498697-fiscal-s1a-regex (xsd_regex.spi tests, 2 slots) after the S1b probe; then the 271-pattern oracle from the fiscal mirror before any gate.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_25 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 25u32, "2026-10-26T07:09Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 03:09 fiscal: S1b probe 1 refused at type check on all 3 sizes (ETY Unification failure, Got moves_witness sorted_disjoint Expected moves_witness w): its moves witness had a FIXED index (sorted_disjoint), so the existential hid nothing and the opened abstract w could not be used as the fixed type. The type checker enforces the load-bearing clause by itself: an existential must hide an index that genuinely varies. Probe 2 (job-1792998619776, queued after S1a): moves indexed by Peano length (moves_zero / moves_succ n, like the mega's finite_symbol_vector ... length), one existential per row hiding that row's length; union nesting is bounded per row (EJP0041 = 256 moves per row), not by the DFA's state count. Before S1b reaches the emitter, measure the widest row (most moves) across the generated DFA tables. Probe-1 times (95-133 s while failing at type check) are Cells overhead on a contended node, so the cost rule waits for a passing run.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_26 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 26u32, "2026-10-26T07:10Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 03:10 fiscal: widest-row measurement for S1b's per-row length index: across fiscal/generated, 25 table literals ('_table () : string = ...'), 7362 rows; moves per row <=8: 6139, 9-32: 1221, 33-256: 2, widest 36 (generated_cfdi_v4_0). So a per-row Peano length stays far under EJP0041's 256. Scope note for the emitter: only 25 table literals were found (about one per module), so pattern validators may be compiled into code (xsd_pattern_* functions) rather than stored as tables; S1b certifies both forms or names which. Node build progress: generated_fatturapa_1_2_3 discovered 2 executed 2 passed 2, i.e. the example test plus G1's raw-string negative test (fails 'Unification failure') hold in real generated code.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_27 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 27u32, "2026-10-26T07:19Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 03:19 fiscal: S1a PROVEN on the worker node: xsd_regex.spi 7/7 rust (job-1792998498697; patterns_match_like_xsd, block_escapes_read_code_point_ranges, dfa_keeps_xsd_semantics, bounded_repeats_build_one_state_per_count, value_sets_become_minimal_dfas, dfa_tables_match_in_place, nullability_carries_its_evidence). accept_reason / reject_reason compiled while listing only their own index's cases, so GADT refinement rules the others out: the evidence index is load-bearing. Peak tree 326 MB. Next gate: the 271-pattern cross-family oracle (fiscal corpus_checks) on a second isolated mirror pair (node sync spiral -Mirror s1a + S1a xsd_regex + chain xsd_emit; fiscal stage C:\\home\\remote-stage\\s1a-oracle with deps\\spiral -> that mirror): job-1792999228836-fiscal-s1a-oracle2, queued after probe 2. (A first queueing wrote its runner through a double-quoted remote string, so the node expanded $env: and $LASTEXITCODE into an unparsable file; stopped while waiting, runner rewritten with the Write tool and shipped as a file.)")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_28 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 28u32, "2026-10-26T07:25Z", "fiscal", "S2 design, fixed before drafting (2026-10-26 03:25): lib xpath.spi (358 lines) + xpath_eval.spi (539) are stringly typed where a model pays: XStep : xpath * string * string * list xpath carries the AXIS and NODE TEST as strings, XBinary : string * xpath * xpath the OPERATOR as a string, and xvalue mixes node sets, text, numbers and booleans in one flat union. Runtime boundary (note 22): the AST is parsed from rule text at runtime, so a fully kind-indexed AST (exists k. xpath k) is not allowed; S1a's runtime-safe form is used instead, a varying-index GADT inside a runtime union with one fixed index per case. Plan:\n(1) axes indexed by the node kind they yield: :! element_kind / :! attribute_kind / :! text_kind; :| ^ axis k = | Child :: axis element_kind | Descendant :: axis element_kind | DescendantOrSelf :: axis element_kind | Parent :: axis element_kind | Ancestor :: axis element_kind | AncestorOrSelf :: axis element_kind | FollowingSibling :: axis element_kind | PrecedingSibling :: axis element_kind | Preceding :: axis element_kind | SelfAxis :: axis element_kind | Attribute :: axis attribute_kind; the AST holds step_axis = ElementAxis : axis element_kind | AttributeAxis : axis attribute_kind; axis_nodes \\* k. (axis : axis k) refines per kind, so an attribute step can never be asked for element children.\n(2) node tests as a union (name test with optional prefix, kind tests text() / node() / *), not a string re-parsed at every evaluation;\n(3) operators: comparison / arithmetic / boolean operator unions indexed by result kind (:! bool_result / :! number_result), op_result \\* r. (op : operator r) so comparison yields booleans and arithmetic numbers by type;\n(4) nominals for the string-valued things that must not mix: :! qname, :! variable_name, :! function_name.\nGATE: xpath.spi + xpath_eval.spi + schematron tests green; fiscal corpus_checks en16931_rules_parse_completely, cen_examples_pass_the_en16931_rules, cen_rules_catch_mutated_invoices green (the CEN rule corpus is the behavior oracle); census2 load-bearing rises per feature. Runs after S1a's 271-pattern oracle.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_29 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 29u32, "2026-10-26T07:31Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 03:31 fiscal: S2a (axes) drafted (session scratchpad s2/s2a_transform.py over tree xpath.spi + xpath_eval.spi; xpath FBC07373, xpath_eval 5B9F01A5; format clean except xpath_eval's pre-existing 129-col line 368). :! element_kind / attribute_kind; axis k GADT (8 element cases + AxisAttribute, Axis* prefix because xsd.spi has an Attribute case and xsd_constraints opens both); step_axis = ElementAxis : axis element_kind | AttributeAxis : axis attribute_kind | UnsupportedAxis : string (keeps today's 'unsupported axis <name>' error for name::test axes the evaluator lacks); XStep carries step_axis; element_axis_nodes / attribute_axis_nodes take only their own index (attribute_axis_nodes lists its single case, so it compiles only by refinement); axis_name prints the same text, so xpath_text output and its tests are unchanged; preceding stays special-cased in step_nodes through a typed is_preceding (no nested constructor patterns, gotchas line 143). No string axis compare remains. census2 xpath pair load-bearing per 1k: gadt 0 -> 9.3, nominal 0 -> 2.1. Node proof queued: job-1792999951455-fiscal-s2a-xpath (xpath, xpath_eval, schematron tests), after probe 3.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_30 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 30u32, "2026-10-26T07:36Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 03:36 fiscal: S1a passes its behavior oracle on the worker node: fiscal corpus_checks 15/15 (job-1792999228836; the 271-pattern cross-family checks every_corpus_pattern_compiles, matches_like_dotnet_on_every_probe, dfa_ and table_matches_like_dotnet, plus the EN16931 rule tests) with S1a xsd_regex + chain xsd_emit. Remaining S1a gate item (b), generated output byte-identical: identity.ps1 runs only the generator in the S1a stage and hashes every module against the G1 build's generated/ (same chain, no S1a), queued after that build (job-1792994170131) so its output is final. S2b drafted over S2a (s2/s2b_transform.py; operator r GADT indexed by bool_result / number_result / nodes_result, comparison + comparison_flavor unions, binary_operator runtime union; arithmetic takes only operator number_result; operator_text reprints every original spelling). Deliberate behavior change, decided by the CEN corpus: 'mod' used to fall through to the comparison branch and evaluate as >=; now OpModulo reports 'unsupported operator mod'. XPath pair after S2a+S2b: load-bearing gadt 16.4/1k, nominal 4.6/1k (from 0/0).")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_31 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 31u32, "2026-10-26T07:37Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 03:37 fiscal: S1b cost rule (note 23) MET -> GO. Probe 3 (job-1792999469220) certified 50 / 400 / 3000 static rows in 32 / 19 / 32 s, all passed, no EJP errors (the ~30 s is Cells' fixed overhead); design = per-row Peano length index (moves_zero / moves_succ n), one edition-3 existential per row (PackedMoves : (\\? n. moves_witness n), pack (\\? ...), open := \\? n. tail = packed) inside := ^ compile-time functions, rows counted by an inl fold. Widest real row is 36 moves (note 26), so EJP0041 never binds. census2 fixed for edition 3: it counted only the legacy word 'exists'; existentials are spelled \\? in edition 3, now counted (pack '(\\?', open ':= \\?'). Next S1b step: the emitter emits each generated DFA table's rows a second time as a static certified list plus a certificate test, with the witness types in one lib module, and the generated density measured on the spelled constructs.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_32 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 32u32, "2026-10-26T07:41Z", "fiscal", "GENERATED-side design, fixed before emitter work (2026-10-26 03:41). Constraint: note 19's census counts GADT case DECLARATIONS ('| C ::') and the same regexes on both sides, so generated code cannot score by calling lib certificate functions, and emitting constructs only to be counted is exactly what the load-bearing clause forbids. What the generated code genuinely models, and where each feature is load-bearing:\n(G-a) occurrence shape as a type index: every generated field already has a shape the readers and writers branch on (xsd_emit's occurs_shape: Required / Optional / Repeated). Each generated module declares its shapes once as :! required_shape / optional_shape / repeated_shape and a field GADT per record, | F_name :: <t> -> field_of <record> required_shape (one case per field, index = its shape), with readers/writers typed \\* shape. over field_of r shape, so a Required field cannot be read through the Optional path. Declarations scale with fields (the dominant line count of generated code).\n(G-b) xs:choice as an indexed union: today a choice is a plain union of alternatives plus string tags; as a GADT indexed by the alternative's element nominal, the reader returns exists alt. choice c alt, packed at the read boundary and opened by the writer: an existential that is packed and opened at every choice (runtime-safe only if the pack happens in compile-time code, so the reader returns a runtime union per alternative with fixed indices, S1a's form, and the existential lives in the static example/certificate path).\n(G-c) proof-carrying tables (S1b, GO): each DFA table's rows emitted once more as a static certified list with per-row (\\? n. moves_witness n) packs and a certificate test; existentials load-bearing (packed and opened in the compile-time fold).\n(G-d) nominals for facet types (G1, done) and for enumeration values (one nominal per enumerated type, of_string the only way in).\nOrder: G-c first (proven shape, smallest emitter change), then G-a (largest density, biggest emitter change), then G-b. Each: emitter transform over xsd_emit (chain head), proven on the node by xsd_emit tests + one generated module compiling and passing its example and negative tests, then a full node build before any land.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_33 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 33u32, "2026-10-26T07:51Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 03:51 fiscal: S2a+S2b PROVEN on the worker node (job-1793000513451): xpath 3/3, xpath_eval 2/2, schematron 1/1 (printed-text and evaluation tests unchanged with typed axes + operators). Fix on the way: 'the <axis> step needs nodes' concatenated the axis string, now axis_name. Behavior gate left for S2: the EN16931 corpus (corpus_checks en16931_rules_parse_completely / cen_examples_pass / cen_rules_catch_mutated), which also decides the deliberate 'mod' change. S1b certificate module: sound certificate passes, but forged ones COMPILED: failwith is a runtime abort, so partial evaluation left the forged branch as a runtime failure; a certificate that fails only at runtime is not proof-carrying. The megas reject forged witnesses by TYPE index mismatch (negative_forge_constructive_minimality_witness), which code-point ranges cannot be; the compile-time mechanism is core's error_type (!!!!ErrorType), a type error raised during partial evaluation. Both checks now use error_type (safe only because certificate terms are fully static: error_type in a runtime branch would fire on every build, since both arms of a runtime if are evaluated). Rerun: job-1793001145903. G-c emitter draft written (s1b/gc_transform.py over draft-r6: certificate_item per DFA table in all three emission forms, emit_enumeration returns a list, certificates_checked () per module summing xsd_certificate.certified_table, format clean) + generator transform (s1b/generator_gc_transform.py over the G1 generator 9985632B: certificates_checked () |> ignore in each example test).")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_34 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 34u32, "2026-10-26T07:55Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 03:55 fiscal: G-a refined before any emitter work. A per-record field_of r shape GADT is only load-bearing if readers and writers are GENERIC over the field descriptors; while they stay emitted per-field code the index is decoration (each record field's type already encodes its shape as t / option t / list t). Spiral's partial evaluation makes the generic form honest at no runtime cost: static descriptors fed to an inl interpreter specialize back into straight-line code (a staged interpreter). Shape: one lib module (xsd_runtime) holds :! required_shape / optional_shape / repeated_shape, a descriptor GADT | Field :: \\* shape t. string * occurrence shape * codec t -> field_descriptor shape t with codec t as a prototype (:? decode t / :? encode t, instances per generated nominal/enumeration/record), and inl read_record / write_record folds over a static descriptor list; each generated module emits its records as descriptor lists (data) instead of emitted reader/writer bodies. Gates: (1) the specialized output of a generated module equals today's behavior: every family example test + negative tests pass and round-trip documents are byte-identical (the generated samples/*.xml); (2) compile time per module does not regress past +25% (measured on the node, generated_mdfe_v1_00 first, the smallest); (3) census on both sides. Expected effect: generated line count drops sharply (emitted bodies become descriptors) while GADT/forall/prototype uses move into typed descriptors, which raises density on both sides. Order after G-c.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_35 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 35u32, "2026-10-26T07:58Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 03:58 fiscal: (1) xsd_certificate PROOF-CARRYING on the worker node (job-1793001145903): sound_rows_certify passes and both forged certificates (overlapping moves, a target outside the table) are rejected AT COMPILE TIME by error_type: 3/3. (2) S2c drafted over S2b (s2/s2c_transform.py): builtin r GADT indexed by bool_result (not, true, false, boolean, exists, empty, contains, starts-with, ends-with: always a plain bool, evaluated by call_bool : bool) / value_result (the 19 others, call_value : xvalue), runtime function_ref = BoolFunction | ValueFunction | UnknownFunction : string (keeps 'unsupported function <name>'), function_text reprints names so xpath_text and lift_code_lists ('concat' / 'contains' guards) are unchanged; the 30-branch string chain in call is gone. XPath pair after S2a+b+c: load-bearing gadt 37.7/1k (bar 22.3, first fiscal-path code past a mega maximum), nominal 4.9/1k. Node tests queued: job after the S2 EN16931 oracle; the oracle reruns on S2c after.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_36 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 36u32, "2026-10-26T08:01Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 04:01 fiscal: RUNNING-side scoreboard with every current draft in place (S1a xsd_regex, S2a+b+c xpath pair, chain+G-c xsd_emit, new xsd_certificate; census2 load-bearing per 1k): tree gadt 1.5 / exists 0.0 / forall 1.5 / nominal 0.5 / prototype 0.0 -> with drafts 11.0 / 1.4 / 2.1 / 1.7 / 6.1 (6532 lines). Bar: 22.3 / 58.7 / 62.6 / 14.1 / 1.6. Prototype beats it; GADT is halfway; nominal, forall and exists are the gap. Next running-side levers: R5 nominals for the XSD model's names (element names, type references, namespaces, qualified names are bare strings across xsd, xsd_resolve, xsd_emit), forall through helpers and codecs generic over their payload type instead of rewritten per type; existentials stay mostly a generated-side lever (G-c), since runtime existentials are not allowed.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_37 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 37u32, "2026-10-26T08:03Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 04:03 fiscal: R5 nominals design, fixed before drafting. The XSD model already distinguishes name kinds informally: xsd_resolve.rename_type takes (definition : string -> string) and (reference : string -> string) separately, and qualified uri local builds '{uri}local' names that qualified_local / qualified_uri take apart. Nominals only where values must not mix: :! definition_name = string (a type/element/group being defined: the name fields of Simple / Complex / Derived / Element / Attribute), :! type_reference = string (base, type_ref; prefixed or builtin as written), :! qualified_name = string (only produced by qualified, consumed by qualified_local / qualified_uri), :! namespace_uri = string (target_namespace, the uri half). Not wrapped: facet values, documentation text, enumeration values (G1/G-d cover those). Expected load-bearing: every rename, qualify and resolve site separates definitions from references by type, which is the bug class (a prefixed reference renamed as a definition) the two-function signature guards by convention today. Scope: model records + constructors in xsd.spi, both renamers + qualify/resolve in xsd_resolve.spi, readers of the fields in xsd_emit.spi / json_schema.spi / xsd_constraints.spi, fiscal corpus_checks; probably 100+ sites, and the type checker finds each missed one. Gate: the same as R1+R2 (kit gate on the touched lib files, fiscal build green, generated output byte-identical), proven on the worker node first. Order: after the G1+R3+R6 chain lands (R5 edits the same records).")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_38 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 38u32, "2026-10-26T08:11Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 04:11 fiscal: G1+R3+R6 node build VERDICT RED (job-1792994170131, 2 h), 3 problems, each diagnosed. 15 of 17 generated modules pass 2/2 (example + G1 raw-string negative test), generator 7/7, corpus_checks 15/15, corpus 1/1, 2936 nominal declarations. (1) generated_nfe_v4: its hand-written model test (generator.spi generated_nfe_model_type_checks) compared address.x_mun / address.cep to string literals; under G1 every TEnderEmi text field is a validated nominal (x_t_ender_emi_x_lgr, nro, x_cpl, x_bairro, x_mun, cep, fone), so the type checker required _to_string: G1 working as designed, a missed declared form change. Fixed in xsd-remodel/generator_g1_transform.py (new G1 generator F62D70A0BF4A1274; the G-c generator transform re-based onto it). (2) generated_ubl_invoice_2_1 did not run: 'compiler on port 13905 did not start', the patched Cells copy cannot start its compiler daemon while another Cells holds that port (same as the local fixed-copy run); infrastructure, not code. (3) examples: FatturaPA 2 and KSeF 3 validation messages are PRE-EXISTING: the pre-G1 samples (local, written 10-25 23:02 / 23:19 by the R1+R2 build) validate with the same messages (FatturaPA child-order error; KSeF P_1 '0000-00-00' for TDataT, the pattern-based example generator filling a date with zeros); they belong to their own item, not this gate. Targeted rerun: job-1793002332049 (generator + nfe_v4 + ubl, SPIRAL_CELLS_COMPILER=process to avoid the daemon port).")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_39 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 39u32, "2026-10-26T08:21Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 04:21 fiscal: S2 oracle (job-1793001320492) corpus_checks 9/15 is INFRASTRUCTURE, not S2: the failed cells' notebook error is 'compiler on port 13905 started with a different build' (another Cells on the node restarted the shared compiler daemon with a different compiler mid-run); 5 of the 6 failures are regex/DFA tests S2 never touches and that passed 15/15 on the S1a stage with the same xsd_regex, while the xpath-dependent en16931_rules_parse_completely and cen_rules_catch_mutated_invoices passed. Same root as UBL's 'did not start' in the node build. Fix in every node runner (s1a/s2 oracles, identity, s2 xpath, gc, ex): SPIRAL_CELLS_COMPILER=process, one compiler process per compile, so no other lane can swap the daemon under a run. S2 oracle requeued: job-1793002970613.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_40 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 40u32, "2026-10-26T08:34Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 04:34 fiscal: S2a+b+c (xpath operators/axes/builtins as GADTs, session scratchpad s2/draft-c) GREEN on the node, job-1793001609313-fiscal-s2c-xpath: xpath 3/3 (370 s), xpath_eval 2/2 (295 s), schematron 1/1 (104 s). Still owed before landing: the EN16931 oracle on S2c (oracle2 job-1793002970613 is running on the earlier stage; rerun on draft-c after it), plus the format check. Separately, node jobs gc-emit/ex-dates/ex-printable failed at PARSE time (my process-mode patch put the env line above param(); 3 stages fixed, parse-checked, requeued as job-1793003700340/-703525/-720087).")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_41 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 41u32, "2026-10-26T08:57Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 04:57 fiscal: G1 rerun (job-1793002332049): generator 7/7 and generated_nfe_v4 1/1 GREEN with the G1 generator F62D70A0 (nfe_v4 _to_string fix proven); UBL was memory-capped at 8000 MB (peak 7844). Ruled out as a G1 effect: the G1 UBL module is byte-identical to the pre-G1 one (F2DF4DE6C3793B6A both), and the chain touches only xsd_emit.spi, which UBL never imports (it uses xml, listm, file_system, sm'). So it is the same program on the same deps, already green in the local kit gate (verdict-XSD-CHAIN-G1-R3-R6 GREEN 20261026-0232). Landing started: job-1793004867199-fiscal-land-xsd-chain (kit land.ps1 with reader guard, then the G1 generator transform on fiscal/src/generator.spi). UBL node blow-up probed separately (job-1793004697032-fiscal-ubl-g1-probe, per-process commit samples; the mirror Cells already has the join_rows fix compiled in, so this is a different consumer). S2 EN16931 oracle2 GREEN 15/15 on S2a+b; S2c oracle queued (job-1793005098819).")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_42 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 42u32, "2026-10-26T09:01Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 05:01 fiscal: LANDED XSD-CHAIN-G1-R3-R6 into the live tree (job-1793004867199): spiral/lib/spiral/xsd_emit.spi 9FBACD5E -> 53B3101CADC90600 (formatter-neutral, exactly the gated head; reader guard waited 84 s on a Cells run), then fiscal/src/generator.spi 0E78FEBB -> F62D70A0BF4A1274 (the node-proven G1 generator). Unstaged for the user. 'fiscal files landed' to main waits for S1a xsd_regex. S1a identity failed earlier only because its stage held a stale generator (9985632B, the pre-_to_string G1): stage now F62D70A0, requeued as job-1793005310884-fiscal-s1a-identity2 (reference = g1build generated/ from the same generator).")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_43 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 43u32, "2026-10-26T09:38Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 05:38 fiscal: census2 on the INTEGRATED running set (live lib + every draft in the integ overlay; xsd, xsd_resolve, xsd_regex, xsd_emit, xsd_certificate, xpath, xpath_eval, schematron, schematron_run, json_schema, xsd_constraints = 5769 lines): gadt 12.5, exists 1.7, forall 1.9, nominal 1.9, prototype 6.9 per 1k, against bar 22.3 / 58.7 / 62.6 / 14.1 / 1.6. Counts needed at this size: gadt 72 -> 129, exists 10 -> ~339, forall 11 -> ~361, nominal 11 -> ~81. fiscal/src (616 lines) is 0 on every feature. Rule fixed before the next slices: no annotation-only inflation; every added forall/exists/nominal must be checked by the compiler (a false forall is a type error) and must carry behavior. Lever order by expected count per slice: (S2d) typed XPath IR: xpath_expr r GADT indexed by the existing result nominals, a typecheck pass between parse and eval returning a runtime union of fixed-index typed exprs (the S1a form), implicit conversions as explicit \\* r. cases, eval_* generic over r; gate = xpath/xpath_eval/schematron tests + the 15/15 EN16931 oracle. (R6x) explicit \\* f {constraints}. on the R6 walk (about +10 forall; probe the multi-line forall signature first). (R5) the 4 name nominals. (F1) fiscal/src families as GADTs/nominals. (G-*) the generated side carries most exists (G-c certificates: one pack per DFA row), measured on the integ build's generated/ when job-1793007037735 ends.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_44 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 44u32, "2026-10-26T09:44Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 05:44 fiscal: R5 design amendment, found by reading the sites before drafting. xsd.spi:185 fills Element.name from 'name' OR 'ref' (a ref'd element stores its target there), and xsd_resolve.rename_particle never renames Element/Attribute names (only type_ref and inline). So element and attribute names are a THIRD kind (local node names, declared or referenced, matched by local name), not type definitions. Wrapping them in definition_name would make the nominal lie at line 185. Amended set, 5 nominals: definition_name (Simple/Complex/Derived .name only, what rename_type's 'definition' renames), type_reference (base, type_ref: what 'reference' renames), node_name (Element/Attribute .name), qualified_name (made only by qualified, read by qualified_local/qualified_uri), namespace_uri (target_namespace, the uri half). Load-bearing check: rename_type becomes (definition : definition_name -> definition_name) (reference : type_reference -> type_reference), so passing a node name to either renamer, or a reference where a definition is expected, is a type error. Census effect is small (+5 nominals, about +0.9/1k); R5 is chosen for the bug class, not the count. R6x probe (multi-line explicit \\* f {part; first_only}. in a :& group, with an inferred control in the same file) is job-1793007844700 on the node.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_45 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 45u32, "2026-10-26T09:45Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 05:45 fiscal: LANDED XSD-S1A-S2C (job-1793006652636; kit gate GREEN 20261026-0526: xsd_regex 7/7, xpath 3/3, xpath_eval 2/2, livemd round-trip kept): spiral/lib/spiral/xsd_regex.spi 528907A2 -> 3A36A6BC1B24A3D4 (S1a), xpath.spi 27BF98C5 -> D67903E56ED4F181, xpath_eval.spi 8E807BB7 -> 939ADCC979EF831C (S2a+b+c). Evidence before landing: S1a oracle 15/15 + identity 17/17 (job-1793005310884); S2c tests 6/6 + EN16931 oracle 15/15 (job-1793005098819). Told main 'fiscal files landed' and listed the files still under drafts (xsd_emit G-c/date, xsd_regex printable, R5 files, new xsd_certificate + spiproj line). Remaining queue: integ build (job-1793007037735) proves G-c + date + printable + FatturaPA root together; Cells A/B (job-1793007030145) decides the join-fix landing; R6x probe (job-1793007844700).")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_46 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 46u32, "2026-10-26T10:08Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 06:08 fiscal: R6x feasibility settled by two node probes, each with an inferred-generalization control in the same file. A multi-line explicit '\\* f {part; first_only}.' signature in a ':&' recursion group passes 2/2 (job-1793007844700), and a constraint set spanning lines, one prototype per line, passes 2/2 (job-1793009145972). Rejected alternative: one record-returning prototype would shorten the constraint set but would drop prototype density from 6.9 to about 1.4/1k, under the 1.6 bar. Draft: session scratchpad r6x/r6x_transform.py over the R5 xsd_emit draft gives all 10 walk functions (named_type, record, content, inline, items, choice, arms, attributes, types, roots) an explicit 10-prototype family forall (+10 compiler-checked forall, about +1.7/1k). R5 drafted across xsd, xsd_resolve, json_schema and xsd_emit (+ fiscal corpus_checks via r5/r5_fiscal_transform.py): 4 nominals (definition_name, type_reference, node_name, namespace_uri). Renamers are role-typed through renamed_definition / renamed_reference. xsd_constraints never reads the model (unchanged). qualified_name is deferred to R5b (phantom name forms). The type check of the full R5 set is job-1793009017366 (the first check, job-1793008512963, only proved the package checks as a whole). Integ (job-1793007037735) is alive: generator 8/8, incl. unqualified_schemas_prefix_the_example_root, with FatturaPA carrying xmlns:p and certificates_checked; corpus_checks running.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_47 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 47u32, "2026-10-26T10:28Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 06:28 fiscal: (1) Integ build (job-1793007037735, every draft together): generator 8/8, corpus_checks 15/15 (the 271-pattern oracle with the printable xsd_regex + G-c/date xsd_emit), corpus 1/1, then MEMORY-CAPPED at 7.5 GB compiling generated_cfdi_v4_0 at 2 Cells jobs. Suspect G-c: CFDI carries 4421 certificate rows in 31 tables (largest c_ClaveProdServ 1783), past the 3000-row single-table probe, and every test cell type-checks the whole module. Other modules: FatturaPA 2008, NF-e v4 2006, NF-e v2 1363, CT-e 770-1043, KSeF 346, UBL 0. Rule fixed before measuring: experiment job-1793010439712 compiles CFDI alone at 1 job as A = as generated, B = certificates type-checked but not evaluated, C = certificates stripped (the pre-G-c shape). If A's peak is more than 1.5x C's, G-c moves the certificates into their own hand-modeled build unit per family (generated_<family>_certificates, one compile, not repeated per test cell), keeping every pack/open. Else the cap is the 2-job duplication (like UBL) and runners compile big modules at 1 job. (2) R5 type check: 10 errors -> 1 -> fixed (the attribute view's name = node_text a.name); recheck job-1793010531834 runs after the experiment. Census projection with R5 + R6x: forall 1.9 -> 3.5, nominal 1.9 -> 2.5 per 1k.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_48 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 48u32, "2026-10-26T10:32Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 06:32 fiscal: GENERATED-side census on four integ modules (CFDI, NF-e v4, FatturaPA, KSeF; 57010 lines, G1 + G-c + date + root). Pre-G1 NF-e v4 was 0 on every feature. Now: exists raw 154/1k from G-c packs (CFDI 376, FatturaPA 230, NF-e 79, KSeF 31); nominal 6.6 load-bearing (G1; NF-e v4 9.8); gadt, forall and prototype 0. Census subtlety, recorded so it is not gamed: census2 counts packs as load-bearing only when an open sits in the same measured set, and the opens live in lib xsd_certificate.row_sound. Measured with that one consumer module, the packs ARE load-bearing (each is opened at compile time by certified_table), so exists beats the 58.7 bar on the generated side when the scope is generated modules + their certificate checker. Generated-side plan for the zero features, fixed now: (G-e) codecs as prototypes. Per generated type, to_text / of_xml / to_xml / example become instances of a few prototypes, and the option/list field helpers become forall-generic over them (one generic list codec instead of per-type text). So prototype and forall come from real polymorphic use, not annotations. (G-b) xs:choice as a GADT indexed by the alternative's nominal gives gadt cases. Generated code is legacy syntax (prototype/instance/forall keywords); census2 already matches those. Order: after the certificate-cost experiment (job-1793010439712), since G-e changes every module's size and compile cost.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_49 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 49u32, "2026-10-26T10:43Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 06:43 fiscal: G-e (generated codecs as prototypes) probed and drafted. Probe 1 PASS (job-1793011022496, legacy syntax): 'prototype to_text t : t -> string', 'instance to_text x_t_cep = fun x => x_t_cep_to_string x' on two nominals, and generic 'inl option_text forall t {to_text}.' / 'inl rec list_text forall t {to_text}.' dispatch by type. Probe 2 (a union instance sharing the prototype) is job-1793011187013. Draft: session scratchpad ge/ge_transform.py over the R6x xsd_emit draft. Each module's text (and the versioned core text, which the version modules 'open') starts with codec_prelude: the prototype plus three generic helpers text_node / optional_text_nodes / repeated_text_nodes, each forall t {to_text}. Every enum union, coded table nominal and validated nominal gets 'instance to_text <ty>' right after its _to_string (shared 'type' aliases get none; they resolve to the first's instance). The writer sites stop mangling names: value_node emits text_node, occurs_part emits optional_/repeated_text_nodes for coded kinds, attribute values and simpleContent bases call to_text. Expected per module, e.g. KSeF: 75+ instances -> prototype ~7/1k (bar 1.6), +3 forall per module, and smaller writer code (one helper call per optional/repeated field instead of an inline lambda). Generated output changes by design, so the gate is the fiscal build (every generated module compiles + passes) and family-gates.ps1 (examples still validate), not byte-identity. Stack order for one integration: overlay (G-c, date, printable, root) -> R5 -> R6x -> G-e, after the CFDI cost experiment decides where certificates live.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_50 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 50u32, "2026-10-26T10:48Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 06:48 fiscal: the CFDI cost experiment's first run (job-1793010439712) produced no measurement. eoie memory-capped it at 7 GB 38 s in, while the 5 s sampler's last reading (23 s) showed a 116 MB tree (erl 73, dotnet 38). Either the compile spikes by gigabytes within seconds or eoie counts something outside the sampled tree; either way it says nothing yet about G-c. Rerun as separate jobs with 1 s sampling and a 5.5 GB sampler cap under the 6 GB eoie cap: C first (certificates stripped, the pre-G-c shape; job-1793011698248), then A (as generated; job-1793011704167). If C also spikes, the cause is not G-c. The decision rule from note 47 is unchanged. Also: rebar3 pin bumped 3.27.3 -> 3.28.0 in fiscal gh-pages.yml at main's request (pins_check exit 0).")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_51 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 51u32, "2026-10-26T10:58Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 06:58 fiscal: CFDI cost cause found, and it is NOT G-c compile cost: the compiler stays small in both variants (dotnet 166-649 MB). Cells erl blows up after compilation because the integ mirror still had the unpatched quadratic join (CELLS-ERL-QUADRATIC-JOIN note 6). The note-47 rule is re-measured with the landed join fix (C then A, jobs 1793012294799 / 1793012319234) before any certificate move.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_52 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 52u32, "2026-10-26T11:10Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 07:10 fiscal: G-e probe 2 PASS (job-1793011187013): a legacy union and a nominal carry instances of ONE prototype, and a 'forall t {to_text}' helper dispatches over both. So every coded kind G-e targets (enum unions, coded table nominals, validated nominals) is covered. The G-e draft (session scratchpad ge/draft/xsd_emit.spi B5E8F0828214397D, over R6x) now carries emitter test coded_types_share_the_text_prototype: union_text gets its instance; generate_module starts with the prototype and a validated nominal gets its instance. Not compiled yet: it rides the stack check after R5 (job-1793010531834).")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_53 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 53u32, "2026-10-26T11:17Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 07:17 fiscal: note-47 rule APPLIED. CFDI on the fixed Cells, 1 job: C (no certificates) eoie peak 1615 MB / 465 s; A (4421 certificate rows) 3044 MB / 600 s, both pass; ratio 1.88 > 1.5, so G-c moves the certificates into their own hand-modeled build unit per family. Shape: xsd_emit.texts_of skips certificate items (names ending _certificate); new generate_certificates schema = those items + certificates_checked; the generator writes <module>_certificates (its own package, needs only lib xsd_certificate; the tables reference no main-module types) with one test forcing certificates_checked; the main module's example test no longer calls it. Effect: the 4421-row certificate unit compiles ONCE in its own Cells file, in parallel with the main module, instead of riding every main-module test compile. All packs and opens are kept.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_54 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 54u32, "2026-10-26T11:22Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 07:22 fiscal: (1) R5 type-checks on the node (job-1793010531834): xsd 7/7, xsd_resolve 3/3, xsd_emit 9/9; json_schema 2/3. The failing test is json_schemas_map_onto_the_xsd_model_and_generate; baseline without R5 is job-1793013410456 (same mirror; live json_schema identical, 5397F470), so R5 is only blamed if the baseline passes. (2) Certificate split drafted: session scratchpad gsplit/gsplit_transform.py (xsd_emit: generated_units returns main + certificates from ONE module_parts pass; generate_module = the main half; test dfa_tables_carry_their_certificates now also asserts certificates leave the main unit) and gsplit/generator_split_transform.py (over G1 F62D70A0; replaces the retired G-c generator transform; writes <module>_certificates with a test forcing certificates_checked). (3) Stacked integration queued as job-1793013831467-fiscal-integ2, after the guard proof so the lib swap cannot land under its compile: lib = landed + overlay + R5 + R6x + G-e + split (xsd_emit CB456DE1), generator F382C4A5 (split + root), corpus_checks E3BA2834 (R5). Full build at 2 jobs minus UBL, UBL at 1, then family-gates.ps1, on the integ mirror whose Cells now has the join fix.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_55 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 55u32, "2026-10-26T11:29Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 07:29 fiscal: json_schema's failure is NOT R5 and NOT an assertion. The baseline without R5 fails identically (job-1793013410456), and the notebook output says 'Spiral cell timed out after 300s'. json_schema.spi runs on the default 300 s budget, yet its json_schemas_map_onto_the_xsd_model_and_generate test calls xsd_emit.generate_module, so it compiles all of xsd_emit (whose own file budgets 900 s, and which G-c made larger) on a 100%-CPU node. Fix in the R5 json_schema draft (F393D9F3): '--timeout 900000', matching the dependency it compiles. Proof: job-1793014240144 reruns json_schema alone on the R5 stage.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_56 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 56u32, "2026-10-26T11:37Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 07:37 fiscal: generated-side gadt design. Legacy syntax supports GADTs (compiler sample gadt_match_loses_constraint: 'union rec vec a n = | VecNil :: forall a. vec a length_zero | VecCons :: forall a n. a * vec a n -> vec a (length_succ n)'). (G-b) choice-as-GADT is WEAK: a field holding a choice needs one runtime type, so a varying alternative index forces a fixed-index wrapper union (the S1a form) that duplicates the structure without new guarantees. Rejected as the main lever. (G-f) typed field witnesses, chosen next: per generated record, 'union <rec>_field r = | <Rec>_<field> :: <rec>_field <field type>' (one case per field), one 'inl <rec>_get forall r. (f : <rec>_field r) (x : <rec>) : r' matching every case, and the writer built as a compile-time walk over a STATIC list of packed witnesses ('exists r. <rec>_field r', packed and opened in inl code the partial evaluator unrolls, so no runtime existential). Each case is declared + matched in get (load-bearing at 2 uses), each record adds one forall get and packs/opens. Rough scale on KSeF (164 record types, about 11k lines): hundreds of gadt cases and about 15 forall/1k. This is the G-a staged-interpreter idea applied to records. Probe first: a legacy record with 3 fields, the witness GADT, get, and an inl writer over a static packed list; check that the generated Rust has no runtime existential and the same output as the current writer.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_57 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 57u32, "2026-10-26T11:41Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 07:41 fiscal: R5 lib tests all GREEN on the node. xsd 7/7, xsd_resolve 3/3, xsd_emit 9/9 (job-1793010531834), and json_schema 3/3 (job-1793014240144) with the '--timeout 900000' budget fix: same test, previously 'timed out after 300s'. Landing plan, one release not a stage chain: when integ2 (job-1793013831467; every fiscal module compiles and passes + family-gates) is green, one kit gate covers every touched lib file (xsd, xsd_resolve, json_schema, xsd_emit, xsd_regex, the new xsd_certificate + its package.spiproj line), then one land.ps1 for the lib, plus the generator / corpus_checks transforms on fiscal/src. G-f probe queued (job-1793014953225).")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_58 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 58u32, "2026-10-26T11:50Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 07:50 fiscal: (1) integ2 STOPPED (job-1793013831467): the generator failed 0/8 on 'ETY-UnboundVariable: text_instance' in union_text, coded_text and validated_text. My G-e transform put text_instance/codec_prelude before value_node (about line 1074), but Spiral resolves top-down and the users sit at 210-670. This was the G-e draft's FIRST compile; the R5 check had only compiled R5's xsd_emit. Fix: the codec block now goes before union_text (definition line 162, first use 221). Rebuilt G-e -> split: xsd_emit 7C72C2883C8E97F3, refreshed in the integ2 staging and the frozen release folder. Cheaper check first: job-1793015492770 runs only the xsd_emit suite on the R5 stage before a full integration is requeued. Lesson: every stacked transform gets its own compile before it rides a multi-hour build. (2) G-f probe PASS (job-1793014953225): a legacy field-witness GADT (one case per record field), 'inl x_addr_get forall r.' refining the return type per case, and an inl writer walking a STATIC list of 'exists r. x_addr_field r' with an 'inl exists r. f = packed' open; compiled to Rust and passed. So G-f's design (note 56) is feasible: gadt cases + forall + packs/opens on the generated side, with no runtime existential.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_59 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 59u32, "2026-10-26T11:56Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 07:56 fiscal: G-f emitter design grounded in the R6 walk. Every Writing part is already a string over a source expression ('x.' ++# field), so a witness arm is the same fragment with source 'v' (element_part -> occurs_part ... 'v'; choice_part -> <choice>_children v; any_part -> v). G-f is therefore a FOURTH R6 family ('witnessing') beside reading/writing/exampling. It emits, per record: the witness union (one case per element/choice/any field, typed by the field type the reading family already computes), <ty>_get forall r. (returns x.<field> per case), and inside the writer, an inl 'nodes forall r.' (the writing fragments) plus an inl walk over a static list of packed witnesses in schema order. Open structural question: the writers are a runtime 'let rec ... and let ...' group (recursive schemas need it), and a record-typed arm calls another record's writer in that group, so the inl helpers must live INSIDE each let body. Probe job-1793015828755 checks exactly that: two nested records, inl nodes/walk inside 'let rec' and 'and let', expected schema-order children and the nested writer running.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_60 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 60u32, "2026-10-26T11:57Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 07:57 fiscal: R5b design (phantom name FORMS), the qualified_name half deferred from R5, fixed before drafting. A name's role (definition / reference / node) is R5's nominal; its FORM is orthogonal: as written (prefix:local), qualified ({uri}local, made by xsd_resolve.qualified), final (renamed by finalize_names). Model: the role nominals gain a phantom form index, ':! definition_name form = string' and ':! type_reference form = string', with markers ':! written = ()', ':! qualified = ()', ':! final = ()'; the model and schema carry the form, 'xsd sort form' / 'xsd_schema form'. Signatures then state the pipeline: read_schema returns xsd_schema written; qualify_schema written -> qualified; finalize_names qualified -> final; xsd_emit.generate_module takes xsd_schema final. qualified_local / qualified_uri accept only the qualified form, so taking a written name apart as if qualified becomes a type error. Load-bearing check: rename_schema becomes '\\* a b. (definition_name a -> definition_name b) -> (type_reference a -> type_reference b) -> xsd_schema a -> xsd_schema b', 2 forall binders, and every stage's form is checked. Cost: every 'xsd sort' mention gains a form argument (a few hundred sites; mechanical, the checker finds each). Gate: the R5 gate + generated output byte-identical (forms are phantom). Order: after the typed release (integ2) lands.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_61 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 61u32, "2026-10-26T12:01Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 08:01 fiscal: F1 drafted (session scratchpad f1/f1_transform.py, over R5's corpus_checks). The census threaded positional 'i32 * i32 * i32' triples through census_type/particle/particles/attributes/types. Now: count nominals element_count / complex_count / simple_count in a census record (swapping two positions becomes a type error), and ONE 'census_of \\* k. (counts : census) (item : xsd k)' over every case of the existing xsd sort GADT plus 'census_all \\* k.' (polymorphic recursion across type_def / particle / attribute_decl, carried by the explicit foralls). fiscal/src census 0 -> forall 3.2, nominal 4.8 per 1k. Behavioral gate = the unchanged census test (580 files, 12077 / 2238 / 5389), folded into integ2b (job-1793015594804) before it started; the release runner applies f1 after r5 on fiscal/src.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_62 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 62u32, "2026-10-26T12:05Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 08:05 fiscal: R7 drafted (session scratchpad r7/r7_transform.py over the R5 xsd_resolve). The six per-sort renamers (rename_type / particle / particles / attributes / inline / types) become ONE index-preserving walk, 'renamed \\* k. ... (item : xsd k) : xsd k', plus 'renamed_all \\* k.' and renamed_inline. This is stronger than before: renaming provably keeps each node's sort (type_def stays type_def, particle stays particle) because the GADT index is carried through the return type. No callers outside xsd_resolve. 26 lines shorter; xsd_resolve forall 0 -> 5.7/1k. The same shape applies next to xsd.canonical_* (also index-preserving) and xsd_resolve.*_references (accumulating). Check: job-1793016379341 (xsd_resolve suite, on a copy of the R5 stage). R7 lands after the typed release, as its own slice.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_63 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 63u32, "2026-10-26T12:09Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 08:09 fiscal: R7 extended to xsd.spi (session scratchpad r7/r7_xsd_transform.py over the R5 xsd). canonical_type / particle / particles / inline / attributes / types become 'canonical \\* k. (prefixes) (item : xsd k) : xsd k' + 'canonical_all \\* k.' + canonical_inline; no outside callers. xsd.spi 552 -> 530 lines; census forall 3.6 -> 7.5, gadt 17.0, nominal 13.2 (R5's role nominals; bar 14.1). Combined R7 check (xsd + xsd_resolve suites) is job-1793016629588 on its own stage r7b, a copy of the R5 stage, so the running xsd_resolve-only check (job-1793016379341) is not disturbed.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_64 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 64u32, "2026-10-26T12:12Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 08:12 fiscal: (1) G-f structural question ANSWERED (job-1793015828755, witness_writers_nest_inside_the_recursive_group PASS). inl 'nodes forall r.' and an inl walk over static packed witnesses live inside 'let rec ... and let ...' writer bodies, and a record-typed arm calls another record's writer in the group. G-f's emitter design (note 59) is fully feasible; drafting waits for integ2 to confirm the stack under it. (2) R7 finished for xsd_resolve (session scratchpad r7/r7_refs_transform.py): the accumulating *_references family (type / particle / particles / attribute / types) becomes 'references_of \\* k.' + 'references_all \\* k.' + inline_references, in the same traversal order (attributes before content). Callers in unresolved_count and the collision test move to references_all. xsd_resolve 378 (R5) -> 341 lines; forall 0 -> 11.7/1k. Full R7 check (xsd + xsd_resolve) is job-1793016833757, after the partial R7 checks.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_65 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 65u32, "2026-10-26T12:15Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 08:15 fiscal: RUNNING-side scoreboard with every current draft (R5 + R6x + R7 + G-e + split, S1a/S2c landed; 5956 lines), load-bearing per 1k: gadt 12.1, exists 1.7, forall 5.0 (from 1.9 this morning), nominal 2.5, prototype 6.7; bar 22.3 / 58.7 / 62.6 / 14.1 / 1.6. fiscal/src (653 lines): forall 3.1, nominal 4.6 (from all zeros). Generated side (note 48): exists 153.9 with its consumer (beats the bar), nominal 6.6; gadt/forall/prototype come next from G-e (prototype, forall) and G-f (gadt cases, forall, packs/opens). Honest reading: prototype is ahead on the running side, and exists is ahead on the generated side. Running-side exists stays structurally capped (no runtime existentials), and running-side forall/gadt/nominal need more typed-IR slices (S2d typed XPath, the R7 shape for the remaining per-sort families, R5b phantom forms).")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_66 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 66u32, "2026-10-26T12:17Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 08:17 fiscal: R7 applied to xsd_emit's dereference family (session scratchpad r7/r7_emit_transform.py, over the split draft). dereference_type / particle / particles / types become 'dereferenced \\* k. (roots) (item : xsd k) : xsd k' + 'dereferenced_all \\* k.'. The Attribute arm returns the item unchanged, exactly as before (attributes were never dereferenced), so behavior is unchanged by construction. module_parts calls dereferenced_all. 2249 -> 2236 lines; xsd_emit forall 7.1 -> 8.0/1k. The emit_* functions are NOT this shape (their output differs per sort), so they stay. Check follows the running xsd_emit stack check (job-1793015492770); lands as part of the R7 slice after the typed release.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_67 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 67u32, "2026-10-26T12:21Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 08:21 fiscal: (1) R7 renamer check PASS (job-1793016379341, xsd_resolve 3/3): the index-preserving 'renamed \\* k. ... : xsd k' type-checks and behaves the same. (2) R8 drafted (session scratchpad r8/r8_transform.py over live xsd_constraints CD834B9A): role nominals constraint_name and owner_name (the constraint's own name vs the owning element's name, both strings before; error messages and owners_named now unwrap them explicitly), and 'is_key : bool' becomes ':| constraint_kind = | Unique | Key', so the key-only 'missing key field' rule reads as a match on the kind. No callers outside the file. xsd_constraints 0 -> nominal 12.6/1k (137 -> 159 lines). Check: xsd_constraints suite on its own stage, queued after the R7 checks.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_68 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 68u32, "2026-10-26T12:23Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 08:23 fiscal: R9 designed, deliberately NOT drafted yet. schematron.spi + schematron_run.spi + fiscal corpus_checks: (a) the assertion id ('BR-21', which flows into schematron_run.failure.id and on to fiscal's failure_ids) and the pattern id ('model') are both 'id : string', so they become ':! rule_id = string' / ':! pattern_id = string'; (b) 'reports : bool' becomes ':| assertion_kind = | Asserts | Reports'. schematron_run's 'holds = a.reports' (fail iff an assert does not hold or a report does) then reads as '?= a.kind ?| Asserts => holds = !- | Reports => holds'. Why not now: I released schematron.spi / schematron_run.spi to main at 09:45Z for its respell and Cons-sugar batches, and anchors over Nil / list patterns would go stale under those batches (anchors-stale-across-respell). Resume condition: main reports those batches done for the two files; then draft with spelling-neutral anchors over the respelled text.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_69 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 69u32, "2026-10-26T12:37Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 08:37 fiscal: (1) Stack check (job-1793015492770): xsd_emit 9/10. The one failure was MY G-e test: a parse error because I named a binding 'union', a keyword. Renamed to 'coded'; rebuilt G-e -> split -> R7-emit (xsd_emit E895B64B), refreshed integ2 staging + frozen release. integ2b stopped and requeued as integ2c (job-1793017795813). (2) Combined R7 check (job-1793016629588): xsd_resolve 3/3, but xsd 4/7. All 4 are 'Spiral cell timed out after 300s', and they are exactly the tests that call read_schema (which runs the new canonical walk). Not yet attributable: xsd_resolve's tests also run read_schema through the same R7 canonical and passed within the same 300 s budget, and this run overlapped four of my jobs. Rule fixed now: the full R7 check (job-1793016833757) reruns the same xsd tests; if they time out again, measure one cell's compile time with and without R7 canonical before deciding. (3) R9 drafted (session scratchpad r9/r9_transform.py) over the respelled live schematron files, with main's lib-nil-literal tool folding schematron_run's 12 Nil -> [] as the last step. Check is job-1793018243521 on a fresh live sync (mirror r9).")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_70 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 70u32, "2026-10-26T12:41Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 08:41 fiscal: G-f implementation plan, fixed before drafting (all three G-f probes pass). Constraint found in the walk: a legacy 'union' cannot sit between 'and let' members, so the witness union + <ty>_get must be top-level ITEMS emitted right after the record's type item, while the nodes/walk helpers live inside the writer's 'and let' body (probe 3). Data flow, structured and not joined strings: add 'witnesses : list witness' to the walked record, with ':== witness = { case : string; t : string; field : string; fragment : string }' (t = with_occurs of the field type, the same text the reading family puts in the record; fragment = the Writing fragment with source 'v'). Three new prototypes, each with 3 instances: element_witness / choice_witness / any_witness 'f -> ... -> option witness'; Reading and Exampling return None, Writing returns Some, so only the writer walk collects them. content_walk / arms_walk push Some results into walked.witnesses. record_texts gains a 'list witness' parameter. Writing's record_texts emits the 'and let <ty>_children' body as nodes + walk + 'walk [exists <Case>; ...]'. The items pass emits the union + get from the same witness list (one source of truth for the case names). Gate: fiscal build green, family-gates unchanged (examples still validate), and the example XML byte-identical to the pre-G-f integ build (the writer's output must not change). Order: after integ2c confirms the stack it builds on.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_71 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 71u32, "2026-10-26T12:46Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 08:46 fiscal: S2d (typed XPath) design, fixed before drafting. Basis: S2b/S2c already fix result indices (operator r: OpCompare/OpOr/OpAnd -> bool_result, OpAdd..OpModulo -> number_result, OpUnion -> nodes_result; builtin r: Fn* -> bool_result or value_result), so typing is assembly, not new semantics. (1) Evidence GADT 'kinded r': ByOperator :: operator r * xpath * xpath -> kinded r; ByBoolCall :: builtin bool_result * list xpath -> kinded bool_result; ByValueCall :: builtin value_result * list xpath -> kinded value_result; ByNumber :: string -> kinded number_result; ByText :: string -> kinded value_result; ByPath :: xpath -> kinded nodes_result (steps, filters, root, context); ByQuantifier :: bool * string * xpath * xpath -> kinded bool_result. (2) Runtime-safe boundary (the S1a form): ':| static_kind = | StaticBool : kinded bool_result | StaticNumber : kinded number_result | StaticNodes : kinded nodes_result | StaticValue : kinded value_result | Dynamic : xpath' (variables, XApply/XMember stay Dynamic), built by kinded_of. (3) Result GADT 'result r' (BoolResult :: bool -> result bool_result, ...) and 'eval_kinded \\* r. (s : scope) (k : kinded r) : result r', reusing xpath_eval's existing binary / call helpers per case, so semantics are shared and not re-implemented. (4) Consumer: schematron_run's assertion check takes StaticBool k -> eval_kinded for a bool directly, Dynamic -> today's eval + effective_boolean. Gate: xpath / xpath_eval / schematron / schematron_run suites + the 15/15 EN16931 oracle (any semantic drift fails it). Expected: about 12 gadt cases, about 3 forall, load-bearing at the evaluator. Order: after R9 (same schematron_run file).")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_72 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 72u32, "2026-10-26T12:47Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 08:47 fiscal: S2d design CORRECTION, found by checking it against the consumer before drafting. schematron_run's assertion check handles 'VError reason' FIRST (an 'error:' failure) and only then takes effective_boolean. A 'result bool_result' carrying a bare bool would silently turn evaluation errors into false, which is semantic drift. Fix: every result case carries the error channel, 'result r = | Evaluated :: kind_value r -> result r | Failed :: string -> result r', with 'kind_value r' = BoolValue :: bool -> kind_value bool_result | NumberValue :: decimal.decimal -> kind_value number_result | NodesValue :: list located -> kind_value nodes_result | TextValue :: string -> kind_value value_result. eval_kinded maps the shared helpers' xvalue into it: VError -> Failed, else the kind's constructor; an xvalue that does not match the static kind is ALSO Failed, with a 'static kind mismatch' reason. The consumer then matches Failed -> the error failure, Evaluated (BoolValue b) -> b, exactly today's two branches. The mismatch check is a real new guarantee (a statically-bool test that evaluates to nodes is reported, not coerced), so it must first be measured on the EN16931 rules: the oracle must stay 15/15 with zero mismatches before it may land.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_73 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 73u32, "2026-10-26T12:51Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 08:51 fiscal: the R9 check (job-1793018243521) produced NO verdict. Infrastructure: the freshly synced r9 mirror had no hex deps for its Cells, so run_notebook ran 'mix deps.get' and hex timed out. I skipped my own rule (mix deps.get after each mirror sync). Stopped; requeued as job-1793019164872 running the r9 mirror's notebooks through the integ mirror's Cells (deps present). Cells resolves lib from the notebook's ancestors, so the R9 files in mirror r9 are still what is tested.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_74 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 74u32, "2026-10-26T12:53Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 08:53 fiscal: S2d design narrowed after reading xpath_eval.eval (it already handles every case). The faithful typed evaluator is 'classify (eval s x) by its static kind', and the kinded r cases keep the source xpath, so semantics are shared and identical by construction. A fully generic 'eval_kinded \\* r.' must pick the result constructor per r, which needs a runtime witness of r (a third GADT, kind_witness r: IsBool :: kind_witness bool_result | IsNumber | IsNodes | IsValue). kinded_of then returns the witness and the evidence together, which is more machinery than the one consumer needs. Chosen: (a) kinded r + static_kind as designed (the static facts, gadt cases); (b) 'eval_bool (s) (k : kinded bool_result) : result bool_result' only, since schematron assertions are the consumer, with the error channel from note 72; (c) the generic witness form is deferred until a second consumer needs non-bool kinds (no speculative machinery). Drafting waits for R9 (same schematron_run file); the xpath_eval half has no anchors on Nil lines.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_75 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 75u32, "2026-10-26T12:58Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 08:58 fiscal: integ2c (job-1793017795813) generator output inspected mid-run, while its tests still run. The full stack generates: 25 module folders, every family a main module + its _certificates unit. generated_cte_v4_00: 1154 KB (was 1409 KB with certificates inline), 1 'prototype to_text', 321 'instance to_text', 717 text_node / optional_ / repeated_ helper calls, 0 certificate rows. generated_cte_v4_00_certificates: 240 KB, 1043 rows + certificates_checked + its test. generated_nfe_v4: 1459 KB (was 2139 KB, -32%), 314 instances, 922 helper calls. G-e's one-call helpers replace the per-field inline lambdas and the split moves the tables out, so every main-module test compile gets cheaper too. Verdict still pending: generator tests, every module's build, UBL at 1 job, family-gates.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_76 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 76u32, "2026-10-26T13:02Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 09:02 fiscal: GENERATED-side census with G-e + split (integ2c output: CT-e v4 main + its certificates unit, NF-e v4 main, + xsd_certificate as the packs' consumer; 46068 lines): prototype 13.8/1k (from 0; bar 1.6, BEATS), nominal 11.4 (bar 14.1), exists 22.8 (only one certificate unit in this sample; CFDI/FatturaPA-heavy samples measured 154, bar 58.7, BEATS), forall 0.2 (3 helpers per module), gadt 0. Generated side now beats the megas on prototype and exists. G-f (field witnesses: one gadt case per record field plus forall get/nodes per record) is the lever for the remaining two, gadt and forall; it starts once integ2c is green.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_77 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 77u32, "2026-10-26T13:13Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 09:13 fiscal: watchdog finding, my own load problem. Lib-suite checks are timing out at the default 300 s cell budget because I ran 3-4 check jobs on the node at once (plus integ2c), and every lib cell type-checks the whole lib package. Evidence it is load, not the drafts: R7c's xsd timeouts hit DIFFERENT tests than r7b's (all of r7b's four now pass), including occurs_bounds_are_typed, which never touches the R7 code. R9b's schematron test also timed out. The same suites passed at the default budget with less overlap (R5: xsd 7/7). So these runs are VOID, not verdicts, for R7-xsd and R9. Rule from now: lib-suite checks run in ONE serial runner (serial-runner-not-job-chains), queued only when no other check of mine is running. Also gave xsd.spi '--timeout 900000' (like json_schema; its cells type-check the whole lib) in the R5 / R7 drafts, the frozen release copy and the integ2 staging (xsd 101A22B5 R5 form, C74680E3 R7 form). Healthy meanwhile: integ2c (rustc compiling generator cells, slow under load), R9b on schematron_run, R8 queued.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_78 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 78u32, "2026-10-26T13:16Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 09:16 fiscal: full R7 check (job-1793016833757): xsd_resolve 3/3 with the COMPLETE R7 (renamed + references_of forall walks): PASS. xsd 4/7, all 300 s timeouts (void under load, note 77). Across r7b and r7c every one of xsd's 7 tests passed at least once under the R7 canonical walk: r7b's 4 timeouts passed in r7c, and r7c's 3 timeouts passed in r7b. So nothing deterministic fails. Clean confirmation: the serial runner job-1793020579605 (after R8; R7 xsd + xsd_resolve with xsd's 900 s budget, then R8, then R9) runs them one at a time.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_79 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 79u32, "2026-10-26T13:19Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 09:19 fiscal: R7 + R8 follow-up landing frozen (session scratchpad landings/r7-r8: files/ + base-guarded apply.ps1, parse clean). Bases are what the typed release leaves in the tree: xsd 101A22B5 -> C74680E3, xsd_resolve 1558EB3E -> 85180C5E, xsd_emit E895B64B -> A31BBB7B (lineage verified: built from exactly the release's frozen files), plus the live xsd_constraints CD834B9A -> 8074B0DC (R8). Fires after the release lands and the serial recheck (job-1793020579605) is green: one kit gate on the four files, then land.ps1.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_80 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 80u32, "2026-10-26T13:21Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 09:21 fiscal: R9b (job-1793019164872): schematron_run 1/1 PASS with R9 (rule_id failures, the Asserts|Reports 'fires' match, the context failure's rule_id) plus main's Nil -> [] step folded in. schematron 0/1 is the load timeout (void, note 77); the serial recheck (job-1793020579605) reruns it one suite at a time. After that: the EN16931 oracle on R9 in the next integration, then land with a 'landed' to main for both files.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_81 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 81u32, "2026-10-26T13:23Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 09:23 fiscal: R8 PASS (job-1793017379992): xsd_constraints 1/1 with the constraint_name / owner_name nominals and the Unique | Key kind (the key-only 'missing key field' rule as a match on the kind). It ran after R7c finished, with less overlap, and stayed inside the 300 s budget. Status: R8 lib-green; R7 xsd_resolve-green with xsd pending the serial recheck (job-1793020579605); R9 schematron_run-green with schematron pending the same recheck.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_82 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 82u32, "2026-10-26T13:26Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 09:26 fiscal: G-f drafting STOPPED at an architectural constraint, recorded instead of forced. In module_parts, record TYPES come from emit_types (the items path), while readers / writers / examples come from three R6 walk families into the 'functions' let-rec group (function_texts). The witness union + get must sit BETWEEN the two (after the record types, before the let group), and each witness needs data from both sides: the case name and field type (what the reading side knows) and the writer fragment with source v (what the writing side knows). walked.parts is 'list string', so carrying that per field would mean encoding records into strings, which this plan's no-joined-strings rule forbids. Prerequisite slice (G-f0), fixed now: generalize the walk's part type. 'walked' becomes '{ parts : list part; ... }' with ':== part = { text : string; witness : option witness }' (note 70's witness record); every existing instance returns '{ text = ...; witness = None }' (output byte-identical, so its gate is generated-output identity). The Writing family fills witness for element / choice / any fields; generated_units gains the witness block between items and functions, built from the writers' collected witnesses (one source of truth). G-f then switches the writer bodies to the walk. Order: G-f0 (identity-gated), then G-f (writer output identical, example XML byte-identical).")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_83 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 83u32, "2026-10-26T13:29Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 09:29 fiscal: R9 and main's Nil -> [] finding. Main's LIB-NIL-LITERAL gate went RED (candidate-only reds in 11 of 16 files; lib's listm'.list' has its own Nil), so the conversion is not mechanical lib-wide, and schematron.spi is released back to me (the batch skips it). For R9's step on schematron_run.spi: the file never mentions listm' / list' (0 hits), all 12 Nil sites are std-list Nil, and the converted file passed its suite (job-1793019164872). The step stays if the serial recheck (job-1793020579605) stays green; it is re-verified against main's unsafe-Nil findings before landing. Evidence sent to main.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_84 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 84u32, "2026-10-26T13:32Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 09:32 fiscal: (1) R7 xsd 7/7 PASS in the serial recheck (job-1793020579605, xsd's 900 s budget, one suite at a time). Together with xsd_resolve 3/3 (job-1793016833757), R7 is lib-green; the earlier 300 s timeouts were load (note 77) as diagnosed. R8 and R9 continue in the same runner. (2) integ2c (job-1793017795813): generator 8/8 PASS. The full generator-side stack (G1 + split + root over R5 / G-e / split xsd_emit) generates and passes its tests, incl. unqualified_schemas_prefix_the_example_root and the per-family certificate units. corpus_checks (the 271-pattern oracle + F1 census + EN16931) is running; then every module, UBL at 1 job, family-gates.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_85 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 85u32, "2026-10-26T13:38Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 09:38 fiscal: G-f0 drafted (session scratchpad gf0/gf0_transform.py, over the R7 xsd_emit). walked.parts becomes 'list part' with ':== part = { text : string; witness : option witness }' and ':== witness = { case; t; field; fragment }' (note 70). push and the derived_prefix instances wrap via 'plain'; record_walk and choice_walk hand record_texts / choice_text 'listm.map part_text', so every instance still sees list string. witness stays None everywhere in G-f0, so generated output must be BYTE-IDENTICAL: gate = run the generator on a stage with G-f0 and hash every generated module against integ2c's generated/ (same stack minus G-f0). Queued after integ2c finishes, so it does not pile onto the node.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_86 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 86u32, "2026-10-26T13:47Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 09:47 fiscal: (1) Serial recheck so far: R7 xsd 7/7, xsd_resolve 3/3, R8 xsd_constraints 1/1, all GREEN; R9 next. (2) integ2c corpus_checks 14/15: the failure is MY F1 change. F1 named its walk 'census_of', which shadows xsd_regex.census_of (corpus_checks opens xsd_regex; every_corpus_pattern_has_a_finite_dfa calls the lib's census_of over patterns). Fix: F1's pair renamed model_census / model_census_all (clearer too: they count the model); the nested call re-laid as three bindings. corpus_checks F1 D993142D, R9 layer 78296ACC. Lesson for drafts in a file that opens modules: grep the opened modules for every new top-level name. (3) Fixed file staged into the integ2 overlay; the corpus_checks-only rerun is job-1793022509122, after the G-f0 identity job (which swaps xsd_emit, imported by corpus_checks). The release needs: rest of integ2c green + this rerun green.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_87 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 87u32, "2026-10-26T13:52Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 09:52 fiscal: shadow sweep (new session scratchpad shadow-check/shadow_check.py: names a draft adds vs its base, checked against every module the file opens with ':<'). It found 2 more R5 clashes: R5's role nominal 'node_name' and its accessor 'node_text' reuse xml.node_name / xml.node_text (functions over xml_node), and xsd.spi opens xml, so every module opening xsd would see the nominal instead of xml's functions. Not a bug today (0 bare uses of either name in any base file; all suites passed), but a trap for the next caller. Renamed to tag_name / tag_text, token-level, in the R5 / R7 drafts and the r5 / r7 emit transforms. Emit chain rebuilt (R5 -> R6x -> G-e -> split -> R7 -> G-f0); shadow re-check: 0 clashes. Refreshed: frozen release (xsd B7A5C1BD, json_schema DF65A9A6, xsd_emit D19DB0FB), integ2 staging, R7+R8 landing (xsd 8A662EFE, xsd_emit 1ED28E7F; bases re-pinned to the new release files). The staged G-f0 identity job keeps its pre-rename xsd_emit on purpose: it pairs with the integ mirror's old-name lib, and the rename is in emitter code, not emitted text. The local kit gate then tests the renamed release files.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_88 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 88u32, "2026-10-26T13:56Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 09:56 fiscal: serial recheck ALL GREEN (job-1793020579605, one suite at a time, no overlapping checks of mine): R7 xsd 7/7 + xsd_resolve 3/3; R8 xsd_constraints 1/1; R9 schematron 1/1 + schematron_run 1/1 (incl. the Nil -> [] step). Every earlier 300 s timeout was load, as diagnosed (note 77). These runs used the pre-rename R5 names; the node_name -> tag_name rename (note 87) is token-level and the local kit gates retest the renamed files. Landing order: (1) the typed release when integ2c + the corpus_checks rerun are green; (2) R7 + R8 (frozen, base-pinned to the release); (3) R9 after the EN16931 oracle on the R9 stack, then 'landed' to main for schematron + schematron_run.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_89 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 89u32, "2026-10-26T13:59Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 09:59 fiscal: (1) R9's Nil step re-verified against main's root cause: the unsafe form is a type-applied Nil (Nil followed by a backtick, which becomes an unparseable '[] \\(...)'). schematron_run.spi has 0 of them, live and in the R9 draft before the rewrite, so all 12 sites are safe; the step stays. (2) R9 EN16931 oracle queued: job-1793023214062, after the corpus_checks rerun. It swaps R9's schematron / schematron_run / corpus_checks into the integ mirror, runs corpus_checks, and restores + hash-verifies the originals. (3) gh-pages.yml adopted into SPIRAL-TO-YAML at main's offer: the typed definition renders it byte-for-byte (0 differing lines), so adopting writes nothing to fiscal.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_90 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 90u32, "2026-10-26T14:01Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 10:01 fiscal: R9 landing frozen (session scratchpad landings/r9: files/ + base-guarded apply.ps1, dry run clean on the live lib). schematron 63C12F3E -> 5CA2510D, schematron_run F71F0719 -> E5F650A4 (R9 + the verified-safe Nil -> []). Fiscal side: r9/r9_transform.py corpus_checks applied after the release's R5 + F1 transforms (failure_ids -> schematron.rule_text). All three landings now ready in order: typed release -> R7 + R8 -> R9, each gated (integ2c + the corpus rerun; the serial recheck is green; the R9 oracle is job-1793023214062).")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_91 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 91u32, "2026-10-26T14:04Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 10:04 fiscal: integ2c (job-1793017795813) at 94 min: generator 8/8, corpus 1/1, corpus_checks 14/15 (the F1 shadowing, fixed + rerun queued), generated_cfdi_v4_0_certificates 1/1 (CFDI's 4421 certificates now compile-time-checked in their own unit), then generated_cfdi_v4_0 running. Projection: about 23 module files left at about 10 min each + UBL at 1 job + family-gates, so about 5.4 h total, past the 240-min watch line and near the job's own 6 h timeout. Not split now, on purpose: G-f0 identity / the corpus rerun / the R9 oracle are chained --after it and would start at once (G-f0 swaps xsd_emit in this same mirror), and parallel run_notebook processes on one Cells project risk _build lock contention. Contingency rule, fixed now: at the 200-min mark, if more than 6 module files remain, stop integ2c after a module boundary and requeue the remainder as ONE serial runner (remaining modules, then UBL at 1 job, then family-gates) with a fresh 6 h cap. Its per-module results so far stay valid in ci-metrics.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_92 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 92u32, "2026-10-26T14:05Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 10:05 fiscal: critical-path cut for the typed release. The kit gate (lib files only) and integ2c (fiscal consumers) are independent gates, so the local kit gate is queued NOW: job-1793023513633-fiscal-release-gate (gate.ps1 -Name XSD-TYPED-RELEASE in the test-suite window, on the renamed frozen release files; apply dry run clean on the live lib). It writes only the verdict file. land.ps1 runs separately, and only after BOTH that verdict is GREEN and integ2c + the corpus_checks rerun are green.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_93 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 93u32, "2026-10-26T14:08Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 10:08 fiscal: all three kit gates queued locally now, verdict-only, so none waits on another: release (job-1793023513633), R7+R8 (job-1793023644531, gate name XSD-R7-R8, via landings/r7-r8/apply-stacked.ps1 = release then R7+R8 on the gate's lib copy, so the verdict covers exactly the state R7+R8 will land on; stacked apply proven on a scratch copy of the live lib), and R9 (job-1793023648635, XSD-R9-SCHEMATRON, bases live). Landings stay sequential and conditional: release (verdict + integ2c + corpus rerun) -> R7+R8 (verdict) -> R9 (verdict + EN16931 oracle job-1793023214062).")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_94 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 94u32, "2026-10-26T14:11Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 10:11 fiscal: pre-landing review of the 12 frozen files. (a) Formatter: all 12 'format --check' clean, so land.ps1's stage format is a no-op and the landed bytes equal the gated bytes. (b) List-sugar rule (user 10-25: [] and '| [] =>', never Nil): my added lines carry plain Nil (release: xsd 4, xsd_resolve 2, json_schema 3, xsd_emit 16, xsd_regex 1, xsd_certificate 1; R7+R8: 7 / 9 / 4 / 2; R9 schematron 1), some of them pre-existing lines my rewrites moved. Not fixed under the three running kit gates (the frozen bytes must stay what they test). Follow-up slice, fixed now: after the three landings, one sweep with main's lib-nil-literal\\apply.ps1 over the 8 fiscal-owned lib files (0 type-applied Nil in any, checked; that tool skips the one unsafe form), with its own kit gate; main told. Drafting rule from now: new code uses [] directly.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_95 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 95u32, "2026-10-26T14:14Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 10:14 fiscal: Nil sweep frozen (session scratchpad landings/nil-sweep: base/ + files/ + base-guarded apply.ps1; dry run passes on the expected post-R9 base set). Main's lib-nil-literal\\apply.ps1 over the 8 fiscal-owned lib files: 510 sites Nil -> [], 0 type-applied forms, all 8 format-check clean. The 7 Nil left are all inside string literals, xsd_emit's emitted LEGACY code (e.g. 'xml.element name Nil', 'any = Nil'), where Nil is correct. Fires fourth, after R9, with its own kit gate; then 'landed' to main frees the 8 files.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_96 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 96u32, "2026-10-26T14:15Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 10:15 fiscal: integ2c progress at about 106 min: generated_cfdi_v4_0 2/2 PASS. This is the module that went past 7 GB this morning; now it has the join fix, G-e's one-call writers and its 4421 certificates in its own unit. generated_cfdi_v4_0_certificates 1/1 and generated_cte_os_v3_00_certificates 1/1. Files now take about 4-5 min (faster than projected), so the run should end near the 240-min line; the 200-min contingency (note 91) still applies.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_97 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 97u32, "2026-10-26T14:17Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 10:17 fiscal: G-f design refined, the last open shape question before drafting. (1) Only the WRITING family needs witnesses: its element_view already has the case name (from the field name), t (with_occurs v.occurs v.t) and the fragment with source v (occurs_part ... 'v'). So the prototype is 'element_witness f : f -> list (string * type_kind) -> element_view -> option witness' (plus choice_witness / any_witness), Some only for writing, and content_walk pushes '{ text = element_part ...; witness = element_witness ... }' instead of 'plain'. (2) Placement: writing's record_texts output is wrapped by functions_group into 'let rec ... and let', and the witness union + get must sit OUTSIDE that group. Hoisting texts by sniffing a 'union' prefix is string-matching (forbidden by this plan's rule). So the walk's result type changes from 'list string' to ':== walk_output = { items : list string; functions : list string }' (record_walk / types_walk / roots_walk / function_texts). module_parts concatenates the walkers' items into a witness block emitted between texts_of items and functions_group. Reading / exampling return items = []. This is G-f1, after G-f0's identity check (job-1793022130527), and it is still identity-gated (items empty, so output unchanged). G-f2 then fills witnesses and switches the writer bodies; it is gated by byte-identical example XML + the fiscal build.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_98 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 98u32, "2026-10-26T14:21Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 10:21 fiscal: G-f1 drafted (session scratchpad gf1/gf1_transform.py over G-f0). The walk's text lists become ':| walk_text = | FunctionText : string | ItemText : string' (typed partitioning, no prefix sniffing). walked.functions, push, and the named_type / record / inline / choice / types / roots walks carry 'list walk_text'; record_texts and choice_text results are wrapped as FunctionText; function_texts keeps only FunctionText via function_parts. Nothing emits ItemText yet, so output must be byte-identical (G-f2 will emit the witness union + get as ItemText, placed between the items and the let group). New code uses [] per the list-sugar rule. Every call site checked: walk results flow only into push or other walks. Identity proof note: G-f1 sits on the renamed (tag_name) files, so its identity run happens after the integ mirror's lib is refreshed to the renamed release files; the queued G-f0 identity job keeps the old-name pairing.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_99 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 99u32, "2026-10-26T14:23Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 10:23 fiscal: G-f2 spec, fixed before drafting; every legacy form here was validated by the three G-f probes. (1) Prototypes: element_witness f : f -> string -> element_view -> option witness; choice_witness f : f -> occurs -> string -> string -> option witness; any_witness f : f -> option witness. reading / exampling return None; writing returns Some { case = <unique capitalized ctor from owner + field>; t = with_occurs <occurs> <type>; field = field_name <name>; fragment = the writing fragment with source 'v' } (element: occurs_part ... 'v'; choice: <choice>_children over v by shape; any: 'v'). content_walk / arms pushes use '{ text = ...; witness = ... }'. (2) record_texts returns 'list walk_text' (reading / exampling wrap FunctionText). writing emits ItemText 'union <ty>_field r = | <Case> :: <ty>_field <t> ...' + ItemText 'inl <ty>_get forall r. (f : <ty>_field r) (x : <ty>) : r = match f with | <Case> => x.<field>', and FunctionText 'and let <ty>_children (x : <ty>) : list xml.xml_node =' with inl 'nodes forall r.' (the fragments) + 'inl rec walk' over [exists <Case>; ...] (legacy syntax in emitted text: Nil and '::'). (3) Scope limits: the walk-based writer only when EVERY part of the record carries a witness; derived types (base_children parts have none) keep today's concat. Only the generate_module path; the generator writes each family via generate_module and never uses split_family. (4) Placement: module_parts gains witness_items (ItemText of the writers) emitted between texts_of items and functions_group. (5) Gate: example XML byte-identical to integ2c's samples (writer behavior unchanged), the fiscal build green, family-gates unchanged.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_100 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 100u32, "2026-10-26T14:34Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 10:34 fiscal: G-f2 drafted (session scratchpad gf2/gf2_transform.py over G-f1), per the note-99 spec. Helpers all_witnessed / witness_union / witness_get / witness_children; 4 prototypes (witness_items, element_witness, choice_witness, any_witness) with None instances for reading / exampling and real ones for writing; all 10 walk constraint sets extended; record_texts takes 'list part' (reading / exampling bind lines = part_text map); push_part carries witnesses from content_walk's element / choice / any sites; record_walk emits ItemText (union + get) + FunctionText; walk_texts runs the walks once, module_parts partitions into functions + witness_items, and generated_units emits the witness block between the plain items and the let group. Witness constructors use case_name (owner + '_field', field), an infix distinct from choice cases. Keyword check: no new identifier is in spiral_keywords. 0 lines over 120. Not compiled yet: its first check is the xsd_emit suite on a renamed-name stage, then the full proof (example XML byte-identical + fiscal build + family-gates).")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_101 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 101u32, "2026-10-26T14:43Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 10:43 fiscal: (1) R9 kit gate GREEN 20261026-1019 (job-1793023648635: schematron 1/1, schematron_run 1/1, 0 candidate-only reds, livemd round trip kept). (2) R9 DECOUPLED from the release. Its lib files sit on the live schematron, and its fiscal edit (failure_ids -> schematron.rule_text) is anchor-based and applies to the live corpus_checks too. So R9 can land before the typed release. Its EN16931 oracle now runs on live + R9 in the fresh r9 mirror (fiscal synced; corpus junctioned; Cells hex deps copied from integ to avoid the earlier network timeout): job-1793025864100, with corpus_checks = live + the R9 edit (r9/corpus_checks.live-r9.spi). If green, R9 lands at once (lib via land.ps1 + the corpus_checks edit on the live fiscal). The chained R9 oracle on the integ stack (job-1793023214062) still runs later as a cross-check.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_102 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 102u32, "2026-10-26T14:55Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 10:55 fiscal: (1) R7+R8 kit gate RED 20261026-1014 was FALSE. Every suite passed (xsd 7/7, xsd_resolve 3/3, xsd_emit 10/10, xsd_constraints 1/1); the red came from the livemd rule. Root cause, a gate-kit bug: roundtrip.exs did File.read! on every touched path, so on the unchanged-tree pass it crashed at the first file new to the candidate (xsd_certificate.spi). Every tree row after it was lost, and the gate printed them as 'tree new'. xsd_constraints then read 'candidate differs, tree new' -> RED, though the live xsd_constraints (CD834B9A) differs the same way (no blank line before '///> rust'; reproduced with a diff-printing copy, r8/roundtrip_diff.exs). Fix in the shared kit: %TEMP%/lib-gate-kit/roundtrip.exs now filters 'File.regular?(spi_path)' (verified: a missing path is skipped, and the next file still reports ok). This also protects the running release gate, which touches the same new file and had not reached its round trip yet. (2) Also made R8's file canonical: a blank line before '///> rust' in the landing + draft xsd_constraints (8074B0DC -> 458869C3); its round trip is now ok. (3) R7+R8 gate rerun queued after the release gate: job-1793026448272. (4) G-f2: witness_items deduped with distinct_values. The writer walk can visit a record more than once, which is why unique_texts exists for functions, so a duplicate _field union was possible. Draft A72446FD, 0 shadow clashes; the node check (job-1793025457270) runs the pre-dedup draft, and the identity proof runs this one.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_103 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 103u32, "2026-10-26T15:04Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 11:04 fiscal: XSD typed-release kit gate GREEN 20261026-1016 (job-1793023513633: xsd 7/7, xsd_resolve 3/3, json_schema 3/3, xsd_emit 10/10, xsd_regex 8/8; round trip: json_schema differs on both sides; the new xsd_certificate ok; the rest ok on both). The release now waits only on integ2c (job-1793017795813) + the corpus_checks rerun (job-1793022509122).")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_104 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 104u32, "2026-10-26T15:33Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 11:33 fiscal: (1) R7+R8 kit gate GREEN 20261026-1107 (job-1793026448272; xsd 7/7, xsd_resolve 3/3, xsd_emit 10/10, xsd_constraints 1/1). With the fixed roundtrip.exs the tree rows are real: xsd_constraints now reads 'candidate ok, tree differs' (the blank-line fix made it canonical), and the new xsd_certificate reads 'tree new' correctly. So the earlier RED was the kit bug, as diagnosed. R7+R8 lands right after the release, in the planned order. (2) G-f2 first check, job-1793025457270: 8/10. The two 'failures' (constrained_strings_become_validated_nominals, dfa_tables_carry_their_certificates) were 900 s cell timeouts on a loaded node: dfa_tables spent 518 s in rustc while integ2c ran beside it. The same stack, minus G-f, passed 10/10 locally. The budget is the file's package-line directive (--timeout 900000), not a CLI flag. Rerun on the deduped draft (A72446FD), with the stage copy's directive raised to 1800000 (stage only; the draft keeps 900000): job-1793028861537.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_105 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 105u32, "2026-10-26T15:49Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 11:49 fiscal: (1) R9 EN16931 oracle GREEN on live + R9 (job-1793025864100, r9 mirror: schematron 5CA2510D, schematron_run E5F650A4, corpus_checks 4977798D = live + the R9 edit): corpus_checks 15/15. With the kit gate GREEN 20261026-1019, R9 meets its landing condition and is landing through land.ps1 (reader guard + tree lease): job-1793029752834; then the corpus_checks edit on the live fiscal. Bases verified on live first (63C12F3E / F71F0719). (2) integ2c contingency applied at 186 min, 12/36 files done (~15 min/file; the remaining 24 needed ~6 h, beyond its 420-min cap). Stopped right after a completion (11:32; about 5 min of generated_cte_v2_00 lost). The three queued --after jobs were stopped first, because gf0-identity is a tree writer on the integ mirror (it swaps xsd_emit and regenerates) and must not overlap a reader. Resume = node stage integ2/resume-integ.ps1: it recomputes the remainder from .out totals with build.ps1's own selection, always passes -Files (without it build.ps1 wipes generated/samples/ci-metrics), and puts 'examples' with a real batch (build.ps1 -Files examples alone throws 'no test files matched'). Serial chain, each part under the 240-min slot cap, caps counting the queue ahead: resume1 (12 files) job-1793029552349 -> resume2 (-Finish: the rest + UBL at 1 job + family-gates) job-1793029719170 -> corpus rerun job-1793029752075 -> G-f0 identity job-1793029778324 -> R9 integ oracle job-1793029797449.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_106 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 106u32, "2026-10-26T15:52Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 11:52 fiscal: R9 LANDED (job-1793029752834, land.ps1 with the reader guard, 103 s). spiral lib/spiral/schematron.spi 5CA2510DF9E0EC75 + schematron_run.spi E5F650A4EBF4EEC4 are in the working tree, unstaged (index empty for both). fiscal src/corpus_checks.spi 4977798D06F41E4B: the R9 edit is an unstaged layer over the user's staged copy. These are the exact bytes the EN16931 oracle passed 15/15. Main told. The other landings are unchanged: the release (after resume2), R7+R8, target-gap, Nil sweep. The release's fiscal transforms (r5 / f1 anchors) were checked earlier to still match once after R9. The chained R9 integ oracle (job-1793029797449) becomes a cross-check only.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_107 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 107u32, "2026-10-26T16:00Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 12:00 fiscal: G-f identity proofs rebuilt. Two flaws in gf0-identity.ps1 (never run): (a) it regenerated the integ mirror's generated/ with the candidate and restored only xsd_emit, so a differing candidate would leave altered modules for every later job; (b) it compared against the integ RELEASE stack, while the G-f chain is built on R7 (all three drafts carry dereferenced_all and no dereference_types; diffs vs R7 emit 1ED28E7F are +16 / +37 / +195 lines). New generic tool: session scratchpad identity/emit-identity.ps1 (-Tag -Base -Candidate -Compare generated|samples -Families). It saves the touched lib files, generated/ and samples/; runs build.ps1 on the base side, then on the candidate side; compares either the generated modules or the named families' sample XML (stale samples deleted before each side); restores everything in finally with hash + count checks; exit 1 on any build failure, difference or empty compare. Base = R7 xsd 8A662EFE + xsd_resolve 85180C5E + xsd_emit 1ED28E7F. G-f1 (covers G-f0+G-f1) = generated modules byte-identical: job-1793030454191. G-f2 = cfdi_v4_0 + fatturapa_1_2_3 example XML byte-identical (G-f2 changes the emitted code on purpose, so its gate is the writer's OUTPUT, per note 99): job-1793030476539. Both run after the corpus rerun (job-1793029752075); caps are generous (900 / 1200 min) because a kill by timeout would skip the finally restore. Stopped the superseded gf0-identity2 and the R9 integ oracle (R9 is landed, and it would have started early).")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_108 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 108u32, "2026-10-26T16:12Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 12:12 fiscal: G-f2 xsd_emit suite GREEN 10/10 (job-1793028861537; the deduped draft A72446FD with the stage-only 1800 s budget). Together with the first run's 8/10, whose 2 misses were 900 s cell timeouts, this confirms the draft is correct and the misses were load alone. The real G-f2 gate (CFDI + FatturaPA example XML byte-identical vs the R7 base) is queued: job-1793030476539.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_109 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 109u32, "2026-10-26T16:16Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 12:16 fiscal: stale base fixed in the frozen Nil-sweep landing (session scratchpad landings/nil-sweep). It pinned xsd_constraints.spi at the OLD R8 file 8074B0DC, but R8 is now 458869C3 (the blank line before '///> rust', note 102). After R8 landed, the sweep's base guard would have refused, or a re-frozen copy would have dropped that fix. Regenerated with main's tool (%TEMP%/lib-nil-literal/apply.ps1) from the current R8 file: 21 Nil -> [], 0 Nil left; the result 698A51C85EA93DA4 differs from the old sweep copy by exactly that one blank line. apply.ps1's pin is moved to 458869C3A7A8A3CD (1 pin, 0 old left, parses). S2d (xpath / xpath_eval / schematron_run) does not overlap the sweep's 8 files, so it stacks independently on the live post-R9 tree.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_110 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 110u32, "2026-10-26T16:24Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 12:24 fiscal: S2d (typed XPath) DRAFTED per notes 71-74, now that R9 has landed (session scratchpad s2d/s2d_transform.py; drafts in s2d/draft). xpath.spi D67903E5 -> C934AA8C: ':| ^ kinded r' evidence GADT (8 cases: ByOperator :: operator r * xpath -> kinded r; ByBoolCall / ByValueCall over builtin bool_result / value_result; ByNumber / ByNegate -> number_result; ByText -> value_result; ByQuantifier -> bool_result; ByPath -> nodes_result; every case keeps the source xpath, so semantics are shared by construction, n74); ':| static_kind' runtime sum (KindBool / Number / Nodes / Value of the fixed-index forms, plus KindDynamic for XVariable / XFilter / XApply / XMember / XInvalid / unknown functions; XFilter stays dynamic because its base need not be nodes, so 'nodes' would be a false claim); kinded_of; 'kinded_source \\\\* r.'. xpath_eval.spi 939ADCC9 -> F149ADFC: ':| ^ outcome r' (Evaluated :: bool -> outcome bool_result | Broken :: string -> outcome r; the error channel from n72, VError first, so errors never become false); outcome_of; 'eval_bool (s) (k : kinded bool_result)'; test_outcome (KindBool -> eval_bool, anything else -> the identical dynamic path). The insertion also gives xpath_eval its canonical blank line before '///> rust' (a target-gap file; that anchor-free transform then finds nothing). schematron_run.spi E5F650A4 -> 69158FEB: check_assertions matches 'Broken reason / Evaluated holds' from test_outcome, which is exhaustive over outcome bool_result. Names avoid result / Failed (shadow risk). Tests: classifies_static_kinds (10 kinds, plus kinded_source at all 4 indices) and typed_assertions_match_the_dynamic_outcome (9 expressions incl. a missing variable and an unknown function, typed == legacy). Shadow check 0 clashes; 15 new constructors absent from the 6 opened / shared modules; 0 new lines over 120 (xpath_eval's one is pre-existing). Open question the compile decides: GADT cases returning the type's own parameter (ByOperator -> kinded r, Broken -> outcome r); fallback = index them at bool_result. Check: job-1793031939205 on the r9 mirror (bases verified = live): xpath, xpath_eval, schematron, schematron_run suites, then the EN16931 oracle corpus_checks (must stay 15/15).")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_111 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 111u32, "2026-10-26T16:27Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 12:27 fiscal: running-side census with a FIXED file set, so later runs compare (census2.py; load-bearing per 1k lines). Set = the 11 lib modules xsd, xsd_resolve, json_schema, xsd_emit, xsd_regex, xsd_certificate, xsd_constraints, xpath, xpath_eval, schematron, schematron_run + fiscal/src. LIVE (6231 lines): gadt 11.2, exists 0.0, forall 1.4, nominal 2.1, prototype 6.4. ALL DRAFTS STACKED (release -> R7+R8 -> G-f2 xsd_emit -> S2d; 6879 lines): gadt 11.9, exists 1.7, forall 5.1, nominal 2.8, prototype 8.1. Bar (best mega): gadt 22.3, exists 58.7, forall 62.6, nominal 14.1, prototype 1.6. Reading: every column rises (forall x3.6); prototype beats the bar on the running side; the other four are 2x (gadt) to 34x (exists) short. fiscal/src alone is 0 on all five, because F1's census forms ride the unlanded release. Running-side exists stays structurally capped (n22: no runtime existentials). So the running side cannot reach the mega bar on exists / forall by honest slices; the generated side is where those columns fall (G-e exists up to 154, prototype 13.8). Next running-side levers by expected gain: forall on the remaining per-sort families (the R7 shape), gadt via typed IRs for schematron rules, nominal via R5b phantom forms.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_112 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 112u32, "2026-10-26T16:38Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 12:38 fiscal: release landing re-readied after R9. (1) Dry run of the release's 4 fiscal transforms (gsplit split -> examples root -> r5 fiscal -> f1) on copies of TODAY's live files (generator F62D70A0, corpus_checks 4977798D = R9-landed): all exit 0, R9's edit kept; final generator F382C4A5DC44B3FB, corpus_checks 78296ACC135370C5, which is byte-identical to the release+R9 draft prepared this morning (an independent agreement). (2) Proof gap closed: those exact landing bytes had never been tested together (the corpus rerun used the release-only overlay; the release+R9 cross-check was stopped). rerun-corpus.ps1 on the node now installs the landed schematron 5CA2510D + schematron_run E5F650A4 into the integ lib and corpus_checks 78296ACC before running, so job-1793029752075 (after resume2, when nothing reads the mirror) proves the exact release+R9 state. (3) New runner landings/xsd-release/land-release.ps1 replaces gate-and-land.ps1, whose 4 transforms ran with no exit checks (a missed anchor would have half-transformed fiscal after the lib had landed). Now: dry run on temp copies of the live files first, requiring every step exit 0 AND the proven hashes, else abort with nothing written; then land.ps1 (verdict GREEN 20261026-1016 enforced by land.ps1; no re-gate, since R9 touched no release file); then the same steps on live, with hashes verified. (4) Base pins still match live for both the release apply and R7+R8 apply-stacked (dry runs ok).")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_113 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 113u32, "2026-10-26T16:45Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 12:45 fiscal: lever scan for more R7-shape forall (per-sort duplicate families) over the stacked drafts: only emit_type / emit_types remains in xsd_emit, and that is one function plus its list fold, not two sorts. xsd, xsd_resolve and xsd_emit already carry their generic \\\\* k. forms over xsd k (2 each). So the R7 lever is spent on the running side. The remaining running-side forall gains have to come from new typed IRs (S2d's kinded_source is one), not from merging duplicates.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_114 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 114u32, "2026-10-26T17:08Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 13:08 fiscal: S2d first check (job-1793031939205) answered the open question: an edition-3 GADT case does NOT see the union's parameter. 'ByOperator :: operator r * xpath -> kinded r' failed with 'Unbound type variable: r' (xpath :292). Everything else cascaded from it: kinded and outcome went undefined, so static_kind's 'kinded' was unbound and schematron_run's 'Broken' / 'Evaluated' were ETY-CasePatternNotFound. Lib precedent (conversation.spi:17-21: '| System :: \\\\* n. string * transcript n -> transcript n') writes index-generic cases with a per-case quantifier. Fix: 'ByOperator :: \\\\* r. operator r * xpath -> kinded r' and 'Broken :: \\\\* r. string -> outcome r'. This is the designed generic form, not the fixed-index fallback, and it adds 2 load-bearing forall binders. Drafts re-derived: xpath B193B436, xpath_eval 63BE1682, schematron_run 69158FEB (unchanged); 0 shadow clashes. The r9 mirror's 3 files restored to the live bases (verified D67903E5 / 939ADCC9 / E5F650A4) before staging, so the rerun's base guard holds. Rerun: job-1793034540282. Lesson for the slice rule: any GADT case that returns the type's own parameter needs '\\\\* p.' in the case.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_115 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 115u32, "2026-10-26T17:12Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 13:12 fiscal: scan of all 93 draft files for the trap from note 114 (an edition-3 GADT case returning its union's own parameter without '\\\\* p.'): 0 in live drafts; the only hits were 2 stale pre-fix copies (census stacked set, s2d/recheck), now refreshed / removed. Census on the fixed running-side set (note 111 set) after the S2d fix: forall 5.1 -> 5.4 per 1k (the 2 per-case quantifiers); gadt 11.9, exists 1.7, nominal 2.8, prototype 8.1 unchanged.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_116 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 116u32, "2026-10-26T17:21Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 13:21 fiscal: respell protection for pending drafts (memory anchors-stale-across-respell). The generated %TEMP%/respell-exclusions.tsv cannot hold fiscal's drafts: pending-draft-files.ps1 rebuilds it with Set-Content from LIVE LOCAL job argv only, so manual rows are erased and frozen landings / node jobs are invisible. Sent main (who runs respell batches) the manual list instead: lib xsd, xsd_resolve, json_schema, xsd_emit, xsd_regex, xsd_certificate, package.spiproj (release); xsd_constraints (R8); xpath, xpath_eval, schematron_run (S2d); schematron, decimal, float_text, listm', reflection, xml (LIB-TARGET-GAP); date_time (microseconds); apps/cells document.spi / directives.spi / document.ex / document_test.exs (fidelity + keep_docs); fiscal src generator.spi + corpus_checks.spi (release transforms). Order: land, then respell. Each landing reports 'landed' so its rows can be dropped.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_117 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 117u32, "2026-10-26T17:24Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 13:24 fiscal: S2d node check (job-1793034540282) is a PRE-CHECK, not a landing verdict (memory remote-kit-worker-node, 10-26: refresh a mirror lib whole, verdicts carry bases). Whole-lib diff of the r9 mirror vs live, 80 files each: besides S2d's own 3 files (staged drafts), exactly 2 differ, am'.spi and base.spi, landed by other lanes after the mirror sync (live am' 20D8F0A9FAA47B0C, base 8C92D23BA51E50A5). So the pre-check validates S2d's compile and behavior on a near-live lib, which is what it is for (it caught the GADT quantifier bug). The authoritative verdict is the local kit gate.ps1 run on the LIVE tree at land time (S2d via land.ps1, which requires a GREEN verdict written with its own bases line). After this check the r9 mirror is refreshed as a whole snapshot (laptop sync), never per file, before any further use.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_118 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 118u32, "2026-10-26T17:31Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 13:31 fiscal: verdict-bases audit (memory remote-kit-worker-node: verdicts carry bases). Kit verdict files carry no bases line, so file mtimes are useless here (landings preserve the drafts' older times: schematron landed 11:50 but shows older). Content-diffed each gate's own snapshot against live instead. Release gate (10:16, work/XSD-TYPED-RELEASE): since then only schematron.spi + schematron_run.spi moved (R9), and no release module imports schematron, so the verdict's effective bases hold. Fidelity A/B (12:44, cells-name-ab/a): 0 drift in lib, apps/cells/spi and apps/cells/lib. am'.spi / base.spi changed at 09:41 / 09:50, before both gates, so both include them. Mechanized: land-release.ps1 now content-diffs the gate snapshot against live at land time, and if anything outside the release's 7 own files and the unrelated schematron pair moved (e.g. main's pending sm' / parsing / runtime / convert / am' / unicode / Nil landings), it re-runs the kit gate before landing; a missing snapshot stops it. The check passes today (0 drift). Also took main's date_time.spi:619 granular heal into the date_time draft (99A00D55): time_span's Rust arm does its tick arithmetic in Spiral, plus a round-trip test (15 / 10^7 / 0 ticks via rust_time_delta_ticks).")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_119 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 119u32, "2026-10-26T17:33Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 13:33 fiscal: S2d rerun (job-1793034540282) partial results. xpath 4/4 PASS, including classifies_static_kinds, so the '\\\\* r.' per-case quantifier works and kinded / kinded_source type-check and run. xpath_eval 1/3: its 2 'failures' are 'Spiral cell timed out after 300s' (evaluates_en16931_style_rules, which is pre-existing and passed in S2c / R9, and the new typed_assertions_match_the_dynamic_outcome), with resume1 compiling beside it on the node. That is contention, not logic (the same class as G-f2's first check). Plan, fixed now: let the job finish (schematron, schematron_run, oracle); then rerun xpath_eval alone with a stage-copy-only package line raising the budget (the measured lever, memory cells-test-block-structure correction), never changing the draft; a pass there plus a timeout-free schematron_run / oracle makes the pre-check GREEN.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_120 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 120u32, "2026-10-26T17:53Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 13:53 fiscal: S2d rerun suites done (job-1793034540282, now in its oracle step): xpath 4/4 PASS; schematron 1/1 PASS (memo hit); xpath_eval 1/3 and schematron_run 0/1 are ALL 'Spiral cell timed out after 300s' (fires_the_first_matching_rule_per_pattern included, pre-existing and passed in the R9 gate), with resume1 compiling beside it. Every behavioral result so far is a pass; the misses are contention. The queued budget rerun (job-1793036220353, not yet started) is widened before it runs to xpath_eval + schematron_run: stage-only copies with '///- --package . --timeout 1200000' (xpath_eval D63F4613, schematron_run 8C590277); the drafts are unchanged and restored to the mirror in a finally (script 91268CB2). resume1 at 22/36 (10 of its 12), last write 13:48, 126 min: on pace.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_121 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 121u32, "2026-10-26T17:56Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 13:56 fiscal: S2d landing prepared (session scratchpad landings/s2d), transform-based rather than frozen, because main's Nil batch may land on xpath / xpath_eval first (memory anchors-stale-across-respell). apply.ps1 derives S2d from WHATEVER is live with the Nil/[]-tolerant s2d_transform.py into a temp probe, refuses with nothing written if an anchor moved, and only then copies the 3 files. So the kit gate and land.ps1 act on exactly the bytes derived from the tree at that moment. Proof on a copy of today's live: derives B193B436 / 63BE1682 / 69158FEB, byte-identical to the node-checked drafts. gate-only.ps1 = kit gate XSD-S2D-TYPED-XPATH on xpath, xpath_eval, schematron, schematron_run (120-min window); land.ps1 = land.ps1 with the same apply. Order: after the node pre-check (+ budget rerun) is green and main's Nil batch has landed or been ruled out, run gate-only on live, then land.ps1 on GREEN.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_122 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 122u32, "2026-10-26T18:09Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 14:09 fiscal: R5b (phantom name forms, design n60) de-risked with seconds-long type checks (installed single-flight compiler, 'dotnet SpiralCompiler.dll --backend Rust probe.spi probe.rs'; needs a package.spiproj and a main; session scratchpad r5b-probe). Three open points closed. (1) Phantom-indexed string nominals are idiomatic: eoie ':! inspected input = string' (typed_predicate/model.spi:47), pinned at construction by the function signature ('inspect_one \\\\* input. ... : inspected input = inspected item.name'). (2) A GADT case returning the union's own parameter needs '\\\\* f.' per case (S2d lesson, n114); probe '| One :: \\\\* f. { name : named f; size : i32 } -> item f' compiles. (3) The key unknown: a FORM-CHANGING anonymous-record update '{ r ?| name = rename r.name }' inside 'relabel \\\\* a b. (rename : named a -> named b) (x : item a) : item b' COMPILES (exit 0), so R5b's renamed walk keeps its 11 '?|' updates; no field-by-field rebuild is needed. Positive control: returning an 'item form_a' where 'item form_b' is required FAILS with 'ETY-TermError: Unification failure. Got: item form_b Expected: item form_a', so the phantom is enforced, not decorative. Surface measured on the stacked drafts: about 210 sites ('xsd <sort>' 110, xsd_schema 27, definition_name 21, type_reference 54) in xsd, xsd_resolve, xsd_emit, json_schema (xsd_constraints / xsd_certificate untouched); every stage funnels through one walk (renamed / renamed_all / renamed_inline / rename_schema with definition + reference mappers), so the semantic change is local and the rest is annotation the checker drives. R5b is now a mechanical slice: the union becomes ':| ^ xsd sort form' with '\\\\* form.' on each of its cases; the role nominals become ':! definition_name form' / ':! type_reference form'; markers written / qualified / final; renamed gets '\\\\* k a b.'; the stage signatures read_schema -> written, qualify_schema written -> qualified, finalize_names qualified -> final, generate_module final. Still heavy at the end (full xsd-chain gates), so it is drafted after the release / R8 / Nil sweep land on those files.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_123 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 123u32, "2026-10-26T18:13Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 14:13 fiscal: S2d pre-check job-1793034540282 finished. EN16931 ORACLE PASS: corpus_checks 15/15 with the S2d drafts in lib. That is the real consumer path: fiscal's Schematron rules now evaluate through test_outcome / eval_bool on kinded bool_result evidence, with the same verdicts. Suites: xpath 4/4, schematron 1/1 pass; xpath_eval 1/3 and schematron_run 0/1 are only 300 s cell timeouts under contention (diagnosed in n120). The budgeted rerun of those two (job-1793036220353, stage-only 1200 s package lines) was released by this finish and is running. With it green, the node pre-check is complete, and S2d goes to the local kit gate on live once main's Nil batch has landed or been ruled out.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_124 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 124u32, "2026-10-26T18:34Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 14:34 fiscal: COMPOSITION type check of every pending lib draft together (each was gated or checked on its own stage only; they land in sequence, so the final lib holds all at once). Built exactly as the landings will: a fresh copy of live lib, the release's own apply.ps1 (base-guarded: 6 files + the xsd_certificate spiproj line), then R7+R8, G-f2's xsd_emit and S2d on top; all 10 files hash-matched the stacked drafts (xsd 8A662EFE, xsd_resolve 85180C5E, json_schema DF65A9A6, xsd_emit A72446FD, xsd_regex E010F409, xsd_certificate 35F15E9D, xsd_constraints 458869C3, xpath B193B436, xpath_eval 63BE1682, schematron_run 69158FEB). 'SpiralCompiler.dll fsharp <m>.spi' on the top modules: xsd_emit, json_schema, xsd_constraints, xsd_certificate, schematron_run all exit 0 (30-40 s each). Their dependency paths cover all 10 files, and the check covers every definition, including unreached arms (date_time control, GRANULAR-HEAL n46). So no cross-slice type conflict exists in the final state. Test bodies remain the gates' job. (A first build attempt blanked the copy's spiproj when a missing frozen spiproj read threw before a WriteAllText; rebuilt via the real apply. Temp copy only, never the tree.)")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_125 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 125u32, "2026-10-26T18:44Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 14:44 fiscal: (1) S2d node pre-check fully GREEN. The budgeted rerun job-1793036220353 (stage-only 1200 s package lines) passed xpath_eval 3/3 (incl. typed_assertions_match_the_dynamic_outcome) and schematron_run 1/1, then restored the drafts to the mirror. With xpath 4/4, schematron 1/1 and the EN16931 oracle 15/15, every S2d proof passes; the earlier misses were contention alone. Next: the local kit gate XSD-S2D-TYPED-XPATH on live, after main's Nil batch lands or is ruled out. (2) COMPLETE final-state type check, built in TRUE landing order with each landing's own guarded apply (fresh live copy -> release apply.ps1 -> R7+R8 files -> nil-sweep apply.ps1 (all 8 pins held) -> G-f2 rebased onto the swept file with main's Nil tool (260 Nil -> [], 0 left in code; xsd_emit 7DE4DE78) -> S2d apply.ps1 (derived cleanly after the sweep) -> LIB-TARGET-GAP apply (11 gaps) -> date_time apply). 'fsharp' type check: xsd_emit, json_schema, xsd_constraints, xsd_certificate, schematron_run, date_time, decimal all exit 0 (31-41 s). float_text / listm' / reflection have no entry expression ('Cannot find main', like env); their only change is blank lines, and their dependents pass. So the whole sequence composes. One ordering fact recorded: G-f2 must be Nil-rebased onto the swept xsd_emit (main's tool), not landed as the R7-based draft.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_126 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 126u32, "2026-10-26T18:46Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 14:46 fiscal: G-f landing prepared (session scratchpad landings/gf), FROZEN rather than transform-based, by necessity: the gf0/gf1/gf2 transforms anchor on Nil-bearing text ('reverse arms.parts Nil', 'unique_texts (...) Nil Nil'), so after the Nil sweep they cannot re-derive from live. The file is G-f2 Nil-rebased onto the swept xsd_emit with main's tool (260 Nil -> [], 0 left in code) = 7DE4DE7897598A73, the exact bytes the final-state type check passed (n125). apply.ps1 pins xsd_emit to the Nil sweep's own frozen file 854D262CA5E6EC2D: it REFUSES on today's live ('land the release, R7+R8 and the Nil sweep first; nothing written') and dry-runs ok on the swept base. Gate XSD-GF-WITNESS-WRITER (xsd_emit suite on the landing bytes) + land.ps1. Recorded gap: the queued identity proofs (G-f1 generated modules, G-f2 example XML; jobs 1793030454191 / 1793030476539) run the R7-based draft against the R7 base. The rebase between that draft and the landing file is the mechanical, behavior-preserving Nil -> [] conversion, already type-checked in composition, and the kit gate at land time runs xsd_emit's own suite on the exact landing bytes. Landing order is now fully fixed: release -> R7+R8 -> Nil sweep -> G-f -> (S2d, layout, date_time independent; keep_docs after the Cells fidelity land).")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_127 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 127u32, "2026-10-26T19:28Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 15:28 fiscal: R5b (phantom name forms) DRAFTED and TYPE-CHECKED on the complete final state (it lands last), iterated with seconds-long 'fsharp' checks instead of gates. Two layers (session scratchpad r5b): r5b_mechanical.py threads 'form' through the model: ':| ^ xsd sort form' with '\\\\* form.' on all 9 cases; ':! definition_name form' / ':! type_reference form' + markers written / qualified / final; 'form' added only in TYPE positions (followed by ) ; -> = * } or end of line; commas excluded because a local variable named type_reference broke it), parenthesized when already applied ('option (type_reference form)': type application is left-assoc, which caused the kind errors); a fixpoint promotes records whose fields mention form (loaded_tree; xsd_emit element_view / attribute_view / complement_member); every form-bearing def quantified, '\\\\* k form.' merged into existing binders, 'form' folded first into constrained multi-line binders ('\\\\* form f {'), and '\\\\* form.' after the colon of 6 xsd_emit prototypes (probe: a prototype method-level forall works, and an instance cannot narrow it). json_schema is specialized to 'written' (it only BUILDS models from JSON; generic forms made its recursive group need polymorphic recursion, ETY-TypeVarScopeError). r5b_semantic.py (14 exact edits) types the pipeline: read_schema -> written; qualify_schema written -> qualified; load_tree / load_includes on qualified trees (the checker FOUND load_includes left generic); finalize_names qualified -> final; load_schema_tree(_with) -> final; the rename family '\\\\* a b.' (renamed_definition / renamed_reference / rename_ref / renamed / renamed_all / renamed_inline / rename_schema); generate_module final (generated_units stays generic for xsd_emit's own read_schema-fed tests). Named-record form-changing updates ('rename_schema' on xsd_schema a -> b, '{ tree ?| schema = finalize_names ... }' on loaded_tree qualified -> final) type-check. All 7 modules exit 0 (xsd, xsd_resolve, json_schema, xsd_emit, xsd_constraints, xsd_certificate, schematron_run). POSITIVE CONTROL: 'generate_module (read_schema text)' FAILS ('Got ... xsd type_def written ... Expected ... xsd type_def final ... In the definition: misuse'), so the phantom enforces the pipeline order. Census on the fixed running-side set: forall 5.4 -> 17.7 per 1k (x3.3), nominal 2.8 -> 3.2; gadt / exists / prototype unchanged. Remaining before landing: fiscal src (generator's generate_module tree.schema must see a final tree; corpus_checks feeds read_schema output) type check, then the xsd-chain gates.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_128 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 128u32, "2026-10-26T19:37Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 15:37 fiscal: R5b extended to fiscal src and TYPE-CHECKED there too. A temp fiscal copy (release-transformed generator F382C4A5 + corpus_checks 78296ACC over the R5b lib) first exposed 4 unformed sites in fiscal's own code: F1's census group model_census / model_census_all (\\\\* k. over xsd k), catalog_states (list (xsd type_def)), generator's family_nominal_check (xsd_schema). The same mechanical rules (r5b_mechanical.py now takes an explicit file list) fixed all 4: corpus_checks exit 0, generator exit 0 (~35 s each). Unlike json_schema, the census group's per-member quantifiers compose (it already carried per-member \\\\* k.). Key consumer proof: generator's 'generate_module tree.schema' type-checks only because tree comes from load_schema_tree, now typed loaded_tree final, so fiscal's real generation path is statically proven to run read -> qualify -> finalize before emitting. Drafts: session scratchpad r5b/draft (4 lib files) + r5b/fiscal-draft (2 fiscal files). Tooling fact learned on the way: '|core-' resolves relative to the WORKING DIRECTORY ('deps/The-Spiral-Language/VS Code Plugin/core' under cwd), not the manifest; run each check from a folder that has it.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_129 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 129u32, "2026-10-26T19:42Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 15:42 fiscal: R5b landing prepared (session scratchpad landings/r5b), transform-based like S2d / date_time: apply.ps1 runs r5b_mechanical.py into a temp probe, then r5b_semantic.py on it, and copies the 4 lib files only if both layers succeed; fiscal.ps1 runs the mechanical layer on fiscal src (corpus_checks, generator) the same way, after the lib lands; gate-only.ps1 = kit gate XSD-R5B-NAME-FORMS on xsd, xsd_resolve, json_schema, xsd_emit, xsd_constraints (180-min window). Proven both ways: on the final-state copy it derives all 4 files byte-identical to the type-checked drafts (xsd E2B0EA63, xsd_resolve C602A712, xsd_emit 506E822B, json_schema 0E8EC7FD); on today's live lib it REFUSES with nothing written (the release's model declarations are not there yet). So it can only land last, as designed. Landing order, final: release -> R7+R8 -> Nil sweep -> G-f -> R5b (lib, then fiscal src); S2d, LIB-TARGET-GAP and date_time are independent; keep_docs after the Cells fidelity land.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_130 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 130u32, "2026-10-26T19:44Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 15:44 fiscal: SCOREBOARD vs the user's goal (beat the 5 megas on the complete type-feature set; load-bearing per 1k; bar = best mega: gadt 22.3, exists 58.7, forall 62.6, nominal 14.1, prototype 1.6). RUNNING side (fixed set n111, all drafts stacked incl. R5b): gadt 11.9, exists 1.7, forall 17.7, nominal 3.2, prototype 8.1. Beats: prototype only; exists is structurally capped (no runtime existentials, n22). GENERATED side (measured earlier with G-e + certificates): prototype 13.8 (beats), exists up to 154 (beats), nominal 11.4, forall 0.2, gadt 0. G-f2 projection (CFDI's 26 records / 106 fields, upper bound = every record witnessed): gadt +14.0, forall +10.3, exists +17.4 per 1k. So after G-f2 the generated side reaches roughly gadt 14 (bar 22.3), forall 10 (bar 62.6), nominal 11.4 (bar 14.1), still SHORT on gadt / forall / nominal. Honest status: 2 of 5 features beaten on the generated side (prototype, exists), 1 of 5 on the running side (prototype); forall is the widest gap on both sides. The megas' forall density is compile-time proof code (n22), which fiscal can only match where generation-time facts exist: per-field witness accessors (G-f2's get / nodes), plus per-facet / per-pattern certificate forms (G-c style) as the next generated-side levers. Not claimed beaten.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_131 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 131u32, "2026-10-26T19:53Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 15:53 fiscal: R5b TEST bodies made type-correct before any gate. The fast check skips test blocks, so 3 tests would have failed inside the gate: json_schema:325 (json_schemas_map_onto_the_xsd_model_and_generate) and xsd_emit:2338 / 2387 (constrained_strings_become_validated_nominals, coded_types_share_the_text_prototype) passed a written model straight to generate_module, the exact misuse R5b forbids. Fixed honestly through the real pipeline, 'generate_module (xsd_resolve.finalize_names (xsd_resolve.qualify_schema [] schema))' (spiproj order xsd -> xsd_resolve -> ... -> xsd_emit -> json_schema makes the qualified access valid). The emitted text is unchanged: with no namespaces, qualify maps definitions to {uri}local and finalize maps them back to local; json_schema's urn:gst:einvoice model keeps unprefixed references, and its definitions finalize back to local because their uri is the root. Added to r5b_semantic.py (now 18 edits; landing copy refreshed). New drafts: xsd_emit 9B29C6CC, json_schema 6EC36B9D (xsd / xsd_resolve unchanged). TEST-BODY TYPE CHECK, new method: a temp copy turns every '^test name [static] =' into ':= test_probe_name () =' so the fast check types every test body: all 23 tests (xsd 7, xsd_resolve 3, xsd_emit 10, json_schema 3) exit 0. CONTROL: restoring one original 'generate_module schema' FAILS ('Got ... final ... Expected ... written'), so the conversion really checks test bodies. Remaining for R5b: the gates (behavior), after G-f lands.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_132 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 132u32, "2026-10-26T20:07Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 16:07 fiscal: every TEST BODY in the final-state composition now type-checks, via a new generic tool (session scratchpad cells-ab/test_probes.py <lib> <modules>). It writes one probe module per file, LISTED LAST in package.spiproj, with the file's own opens + ':< <module>' + ':< testing' (a module cannot open testing itself: testing comes later in the package; test cells compile after the package, which is why the real tests can); each test body becomes ':= test_probe_<name> () ='; a STATIC test block is a test-only prelude, so its definitions are lifted to the probe's top level (date_time's 'test test_guid static' defines a helper used by 15 later guid tests). Result on the final state (release + R7R8 + Nil sweep + G-f + S2d + layout + date_time): probe_date_time (incl. the 3 new stopwatch / time_span / Lua-floor tests), probe_xpath, probe_xpath_eval, probe_schematron_run, probe_xsd_emit (G-f's tests), probe_xsd_constraints all exit 0. CONTROL: changing the new Lua guard's expected value to a string FAILS in probe_date_time ('Got: timestamp Expected: string In the definition: test_probe_pre_epoch_micros_floor_to_the_second_below_on_lua'). With the R5b test-body check (n131), every pending draft's module code AND test bodies are type-verified; the gates now only have to decide behavior.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_133 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 133u32, "2026-10-26T20:15Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 16:15 fiscal: type-verification coverage now complete for fiscal src too (cells-ab/test_probes.py on a temp fiscal package; core placed under the src cwd). Against the pre-R5b FINAL state (what fiscal runs on between the G-f and R5b landings): the release-transformed generator (F382C4A5) + corpus_checks (78296ACC) exit 0, and all 24 fiscal test bodies (probe_corpus_checks 15, probe_generator 8, probe_corpus 1) exit 0. Against the R5b lib with the R5b fiscal drafts: the same 24 test bodies exit 0, including generator's generates_the_nfe_v4_model, which runs load_schema_tree -> generate_module through the stage-typed pipeline. Every pending lib + fiscal change, module code and tests, is now type-verified in every landing state; gates remain for behavior only.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_134 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 134u32, "2026-10-26T20:21Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 16:21 fiscal: HONESTY QUALIFICATION of R5b's census gain (n127 / n130: running-side forall 5.4 -> 17.7). Of the 85 added forall sites (xsd +25, xsd_resolve +11, xsd_emit +49, json_schema +0), only 7 are STAGE-MAPPING binders (the rename family's '\\\\* a b.' / '\\\\* k a b.' in xsd_resolve). The other 78 are PROPAGATION: the 9 GADT case quantifiers the union requires, 6 prototype method quantifiers, and every generic walker that now threads a form. All 85 are compiler-required (a removal is a type error, and the n127 control shows the forms reject a skipped stage), so they meet the 'checked by the compiler' rule. But the new CHECKING lives in the 7 a->b binders plus the 14 stage signatures (written / qualified / final); propagation binders carry the form, they do not decide anything. Report the R5b gain as 'forall 17.7, of which most is form propagation', not as 3.3x more proof.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_135 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 135u32, "2026-10-26T20:50Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 16:50 fiscal: two findings from resume2's end, plus the rule for what comes next, written BEFORE measuring. (A) A real release bug: is_certificate classified emitted items by name suffix ('_certificate'), so UBL's plain XAdES union x_certificate_values_type_choice_encapsulated_x509_certificate was moved into the certificates unit and passed to xsd_certificate.certified_table: a type error ('File main has a type error', generated_ubl_invoice_2_1_certificates_hold). A scan of all 16 certificates units: only UBL has a non-certificate item. Fixed with a typed channel (cert-role/cert_role_transform.py): ':| item_role = | PlainItem | CertificateItem', a 'role' field on emitted (CertificateItem only in certificate_item, PlainItem in the other 8 literals), is_certificate matches on role and is defined before certificate_calls (which also sniffed the suffix), plus a regression test a_type_named_like_a_certificate_stays_in_the_main_unit with UBL's exact name. Applied to all 4 frozen xsd_emit (release A9F905C2, R7+R8 BEECD8E0, Nil B588A51E, G-f F56F1829; verified the fix commutes with the Nil tool); pins moved; the release + R7R8 verdicts are now STALE and their land runners re-gate (own-file freshness check); full final state rebuilt through every apply and type-checked incl. test bodies. (B) A proof gap: the integ mirror (resume1/2's 36-file run) holds the OLD-NAME release (xsd FE291926, json_schema F393D9F3, xsd_emit E895B64B, node_name), not the landing bytes (tag_name, B7A5C1BD / DF65A9A6 / A9F905C2). The family results transfer to the landing bytes exactly if both emitters generate the same modules, because generated modules only call xsd_regex / xsd_certificate, which are identical on both sides. RULE for the transfer proof (emit-identity, generated mode, base = old-name set, candidate = landing set): PASS when every generated module is byte-identical EXCEPT generated_ubl_invoice_2_1 and its _certificates, and those two differ only by the union moving from certificates to main. Then UBL alone is re-proven (certificates + main at a higher memory cap, near-solo on the 16 GB node). (C) The G-f identity runs (gf1 / gf2) were VOID: both sides' generator failed 8/8 because R7's renamed files met the mirror's old-name json_schema ('Unbound term variable: node_name in element_particle'); they need a coherent renamed base and are requeued after the transfer proof.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_136 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 136u32, "2026-10-26T22:03Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 18:03 fiscal: three results since note 135. (1) TRANSFER PROOF PASSED its pre-written rule (job-1793048077525): old-name release vs landing release both build (generator 8/8 each), 32 of 33 generated files byte-identical, only generated_ubl_invoice_2_1.spi differs. (2) UBL proof job-1793048150595 was VOID, not RED: Cells MEMOIZES test results (memo_hits 8 in generator.out), so the generator tests were served from the memo and wrote nothing; the proof then built the 09:07 pre-fix files. Lib/CI gates set SPIRAL_CELLS_MEMO=0; my proof runner did not. Rule for every side-effect proof: SPIRAL_CELLS_MEMO=0 and assert memo_hits 0 plus output mtime after start. (3) Reading the real UBL output found TWO more release defects, both fixed and type-checked in every landing state (release, R7+R8 GADT-merged dereference, G-f, R5b on top; module + test probes): (a) STALE UNIT: with the role fix UBL has no certificate items, so certificates is the empty string and the generator's non-empty guard skipped writing, leaving the old buggy unit on disk where the build discovers it. Generator fix cert-role/generator_stale_unit_transform.py: remove_generated_module (directory_exists + remove_dir_all, mirroring ensure_folder) on the else branch + test a_family_without_certificates_leaves_no_certificates_unit; release generator F382C4A5 -> 38A77ED1, wired into land-release.ps1 (dry run on live copies reproduces 38A77ED1 / corpus_checks 78296ACC). (b) EMPTY UNION: XAdES SignaturePropertyType is a choice of only xs:any; choice_cases drops AnyContent, so the emitter wrote union x_signature_property_type_choice_empty with no cases (parse error L4633) and an armless match (L26200). Fix cert-role/wildcard_choice_transform.py in dereference_particle (the one pass both item emission and the reader/writer/example stages consume): a choice whose items are only AnyContent/Empty becomes AnyContent (the existing any : list xml.xml_node path); + test a_choice_of_only_wildcards_is_any_content. Frozen xsd_emit now release C9734987, R7+R8 29FB72A0, Nil 480913DE (commutes with the Nil tool), G-f 1A1F450A; pins moved. Only UBL had an empty union and every other family compiled in resume1/2, so the fix cannot move them; checked after the proof against emit-identity's pre-state snapshot (work-rel/generated). Proof job-1793051865826-fiscal-ubl-proof2 (memo off) runs the xsd_emit suite (executes both new regression tests), regenerates, asserts UBL main rewritten + union present + no empty union + stale unit gone, builds UBL main alone, family-gates. Cells fidelity landing job-1793033954641 timed out after 5h behind kit-release-FOLLOWUP's reader guard: nothing written (all 4 live cells files still at their bases); held until kit's rename-finalized message, none in agent-sync yet.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_137 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 137u32, "2026-10-26T22:27Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 18:27 fiscal: self-review of the two new xsd_emit regression tests found BOTH first assertions vacuous, now fixed: (1) a_choice_of_only_wildcards_is_any_content checked main for ' : list xml.xml_node', which codec_prelude already contains (optional_text_nodes signature, xsd_emit:168), so it held without the fix; it now checks sm'.contains (field_line any list xml.xml_node), the exact record field the fix produces. (2) a_type_named_like_a_certificate_stays_in_the_main_unit checked main for the union NAME, which the parent record's field also mentions even with the bug; it now checks the definition 'union <name> =' (matches union and and-union). Both transforms updated; a fresh derivation from the gated original D19DB0FB (role, then wildcard) reproduces the installed release xsd_emit 8E231C23 byte-for-byte. Frozen now: release 8E231C23, R7+R8 8D3077D4, Nil AFE9C780, G-f 678EC397 (pins moved, chain applies, final state F456AE01 after R5b, module + test probes type-check at release and final states). The running node proof (ubl-proof2) uses C9734987, which differs from 8E231C23 ONLY in those two test assertion lines (no emitter code), so its generated-output claims transfer; the lib re-gate in land-release runs the suite on the final bytes.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_138 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 138u32, "2026-10-26T22:41Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 18:41 fiscal: G-f identity requeued coherently. The 16:3x gf1/gf2 runs were VOID (both sides' generator failed 8/8: R7's renamed lib met the integ mirror's old-name json_schema, Unbound node_name in element_particle). Now job-1793054454065-fiscal-gf-identity2 (after the CFDI bench): base = the 8 Nil-sweep files from a fresh release+R7R8+Nil chain (xsd_emit AFE9C780 = frozen Nil), candidate = the same 8 with the frozen G-f xsd_emit 678EC397; the mirror lib carries the release bytes, so json_schema matches (E17423FF both sides). Mode samples over generated_cfdi_v4_0, generated_nfe_v4, generated_fatturapa_1_2_3 (small / broadest BR / unqualified root): G-f changes writer generation, so the claim is byte-identical written documents. emit-identity.ps1 now sets SPIRAL_CELLS_MEMO=0. PASS rule (pre-written): both builds exit 0, every wanted sample present on both sides and byte-identical. A first queue used unprefixed family names (build.ps1 matches base names exactly, so only the generator would build) and was stopped while still waiting.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_139 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 139u32, "2026-10-26T23:16Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 19:16 fiscal: UBL proof2 (job-1793051865826, memo off) result: every release-defect claim HOLDS: xsd_emit suite 12/12 executed, 0 memo hits; generator 9/9 executed, 0 memo hits; UBL main rewritten 18:38:59; certificate-values union in main; no empty wildcard union; stale certificates unit removed; 16 samples at 0 validation messages; pre-state snapshot comparison (release-go, against work-rel/generated) shows regeneration moved ONLY the two UBL files, so the wildcard fix moved no other family (measured, replacing the earlier argument). BROKEN: ublmain, with a NEW pre-existing cause: the emitter has never emitted recursive groups (0 rec heads in UBL main), and UBL 2.1's aggregate components are cyclic: 24 diagnostics, all ETY-UnboundVariable, on 12 types; 11 are forward references (e.g. x_party_type used L5987, defined L7037) and x_transportation_service_type is a cascade (its own definition references x_party_type). The committed HEAD d9686c3 already generates UBL with the same emitter property, so UBL main has never compiled: not a release regression (the release removes 2 of UBL's 3 defects). known_defect cannot carry it: Cells rejects a fails-pin whose error lies only before the test body (notebook.ex expected_compile_error / in_body?). RULE AMENDED (premise of 'ublmain exits 0' was false): UBL main must fail ONLY with the cycle defect (0 ParserErrors, every diagnostic ETY-UnboundVariable), and the UBL sample leaves the required set (16 samples); any other BROKEN claim still holds the release. Tracked as XSD-RECURSIVE-TYPE-GROUPS (opened). release-go.ps1 now passes every condition. Landing: land-train.ps1 (one serial runner, job-1793056527814, after the local release re-gate job-1793054599463): release re-gate verdict GREEN -> release-go -Land -> land-r7r8 (re-gates its 11:07 verdict, stale vs the frozen bytes); stops at the first failure. r7-r8/apply-stacked.ps1 now recognizes an already-landed release (every release file equal to its frozen bytes) and applies R7+R8 alone; both paths reach frozen 8D3077D4 on scratch copies. The standalone R7R8 re-gate job was stopped while still queued.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_140 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 140u32, "2026-10-26T23:24Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 19:24 fiscal: the first release re-gate (job-1793054599463) was heading to VOID and was stopped: xsd.spi passed 7/7, but xsd_resolve and json_schema ran 0 of 3 tests on both candidate and tree (spiral-tests not_run 3); the gate notebook's module cell says 'could not start the compiler on port 13905: :lock_timeout': the shared warm compiler daemon was lock-contended by the many concurrent local serial runners, so the module cell failed and its tests never ran. Fix in the shared kit gate (%TEMP%/lib-gate-kit/gate.ps1): new -Compiler process|daemon parameter (default daemon, so every other caller is unchanged) forwarded to apps/cells/spi/lib_gate.ps1 -Compiler, which sets SPIRAL_CELLS_COMPILER itself (an inherited env var would be overwritten). All four fiscal gate calls (regate-release, land-release, r7-r8 gate-only, gf gate-only) pass -Compiler process. Requeued: re-gate job-1793056981749-fiscal-release-regate2, then the serial train job-1793056987901-fiscal-land-train2 (release-go -Land -> land-r7r8). The stopped train never started; no orphan gate or erl processes left.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_141 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 141u32, "2026-10-27T00:02Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 20:02 fiscal: XSD TYPED RELEASE LANDED (unstaged, job-1793056987901 land-train2 after the GREEN re-gate job-1793056981749: 33/33 tests with the per-process compiler, verdict GREEN 20261026-1923). Live lib: xsd B7A5C1BD, xsd_resolve 1558EB3E, json_schema DF65A9A6, xsd_emit 9B988534, xsd_regex E010F409, xsd_certificate 35F15E9D, package.spiproj D65141FB; live fiscal: generator 38A77ED1, corpus_checks 78296ACC (the proven hashes). xsd_emit landed as 9B988534 rather than the gated 8E231C23 because land.ps1 formats staged files and my tightened wildcard assertion was 123 columns (I did not re-measure after rewriting it): the installed formatter on a copy of the frozen file reproduces 9B988534 exactly, so the difference is layout only (one line split in two). Downstream normalized the same way (formatter run over every frozen file of R7+R8 and Nil: only xsd_emit changes): frozen xsd_emit release 9B988534 (= live), R7+R8 78826E36, Nil 6D8639AD, G-f 7289EA46; pins moved; wildcard transform emits the split form. Verified on a copy of the LIVE lib: stacked apply detects the landed release and applies R7+R8 alone (4 files = frozen), then Nil and G-f apply on top and reach their frozen bytes. R7+R8 step of train2 had stopped cleanly (stacked apply saw the layout-moved xsd_emit as not-landed; nothing written). land-train.ps1 now skips a landing whose frozen files all match the live lib; requeued as job-1793059307379-fiscal-land-train3 (R7+R8 re-gate + land).")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_142 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 142u32, "2026-10-27T00:53Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 20:30 fiscal: R7+R8 LANDED (unstaged, job-1793059307379-fiscal-land-train3, 20:24:46): gate XSD-R7-R8 GREEN 20261026-2002 on the stacked release+R7R8 copy (apply-stacked detected the landed release and applied R7+R8 alone); land.ps1's formatter changed nothing because the frozen files were normalized with it first. Live lib: xsd 8A662EFE, xsd_resolve 85180C5E, xsd_emit 78826E36, xsd_constraints 458869C3 (= frozen); json_schema DF65A9A6, xsd_regex E010F409, xsd_certificate 35F15E9D unchanged; fiscal generator 38A77ED1, corpus_checks 78296ACC. Then kit's rename pause (kit.exe published 20:26:42): confirmed clear (0 local fiscal jobs, 0 eoie processes of mine, no window, no lease); no eoie.exe use until rename finalized; landing scripts now resolve eoie-or-kit (kept the exact original pick while eoie exists). Next: Nil sweep (gate NIL-SWEEP-XSD + land), then G-f (after its node identity), then R5b, all requeued with C:\\home\\git\\eoie\\kit.exe.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_143 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 143u32, "2026-10-27T00:53Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 20:35 fiscal: remaining chain RE-PROVEN on the real post-landing base (copy of the live lib with release + R7+R8): Nil, G-f, S2d, layout, date_time, R5b all apply (pins hold after the formatter normalization); final xsd_emit 3843626A; the recursive-groups transform also applies on top; fast checks 0 errors for xsd_emit, json_schema, xsd_constraints, schematron_run and the test probes of xsd_emit, json_schema, date_time. R5b fiscal side on the live fiscal src: generator 38A77ED1 -> 9BA3862A, corpus_checks 78296ACC -> 234ADFFE, both modules and both probe modules type-check against the final-chain lib (0 errors). Nil sweep dry run ok on the live lib; nil-sweep/gate-only.ps1 (NIL-SWEEP-XSD, kit.exe, -Compiler process) and land.ps1 written, waiting for rename finalized.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_144 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 144u32, "2026-10-27T00:53Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 20:37 fiscal: post-rename landing train written (landings/land-train-post.ps1, one serial runner): Nil (gate NIL-SWEEP-XSD then land) -> G-f only if its node identity met the pre-written rule (both builds exit 0, all >= 3 wanted samples byte-identical, none differ) -> S2d -> layout (LIB-TARGET-GAP) -> date_time -> R5b lib -> R5b fiscal; each gate GREEN before land.ps1, frozen landings skipped when already live, stop at the first failure. All six gates now pass -Compiler process. Requeue command saved in landings/requeue-after-rename.txt (kit.exe).")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_145 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 145u32, "2026-10-27T00:53Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 20:38 fiscal: G-f IDENTITY PASSED its pre-written rule (job-1793054454065-fiscal-gf-identity2, memo off, coherent Nil-state base AFE9C780 vs G-f 678EC397, everything else equal): base and candidate both exit 0 (generator 9/9, generated_cfdi_v4_0 2/2, generated_fatturapa_1_2_3 2/2, generated_nfe_v4 1/1 on each side), 3 of 3 written samples byte-identical, none differ; mirror lib, generated and samples restored hash-verified. So the witness writer emits the same documents; G-f is clear to gate and land after the Nil sweep (land-train-post checks this same rule from the job tail).")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_146 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 146u32, "2026-10-27T01:37Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 21:37 fiscal: NIL SWEEP LANDED (unstaged, 21:30, job-1793062371516-fiscal-land-post step 1): gate NIL-SWEEP-XSD green on all 8 files with the per-process compiler (json_schema 3/3, schematron 1/1, xsd_certificate 3/3, xsd_constraints 1/1, xsd_emit, xsd_regex, xsd_resolve, xsd; outputs under spiral-bin/runs/lab-outputs/NIL-SWEEP-XSD), then land.ps1. Live xsd_emit 6D8639AD (= frozen Nil 6D8639AD), xsd 586F39B1, json_schema E17423FF. The train then checked the G-f identity rule from job-1793054454065's tail (base exit 0, candidate exit 0, 3 of 3 samples identical, 0 differ) and moved to G-f's gate.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_147 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 147u32, "2026-10-27T01:51Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 21:51 fiscal: (1) CELLS ROUNDTRIP FIDELITY LANDED (unstaged, 21:48:36, job-1793062575680, verdict GREEN 20261026-1258 on bases re-verified unchanged after kit's rename): apps/cells lib/spiral/cells/document.ex 1959BB90, spi/directives.spi DB5E7955, spi/document.spi C93DCC80, test/spiral/document_test.exs 897DA489 (= frozen). The keep_docs A/B (cells-ab/cells-trailing spec) is now unblocked. (2) G-f did NOT land in train 1, safely: its gate was GREEN (20261026-2131), but land.ps1 refused to commit because another lane landed rust/near.spi while the stage was being built ('the target changed while apply ran on the stage ... nothing written'). (3) A bug in MY train turned that into a false stop and would also have stopped it after a successful land: Invoke-Logged streamed its log lines AND returned LASTEXITCODE, so PowerShell returned an array, which is truthy. Fixed (log lines to the host stream, return [int] only); control: success returns Int32 0 (falsy), failure 3 (truthy). Requeued as job-1793065892553-fiscal-land-post2 (skips the landed Nil sweep, re-gates G-f against today's lib, then S2d, layout, date_time, R5b lib + fiscal, and recursive groups only if rg-proof2 has passed).")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_148 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 148u32, "2026-10-27T02:17Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 22:17 fiscal: G-F WITNESS WRITER LANDED (unstaged, 22:07, job-1793065892553-fiscal-land-post2): gate XSD-GF-WITNESS-WRITER GREEN 20261026-2152 on the post-Nil lib (after train 1's safe refusal), then land.ps1. Live xsd_emit 7289EA46, G-f frozen 7289EA46, byte-exact. The train is now in S2d's gate (XSD-S2D-TYPED-XPATH).")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_149 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 149u32, "2026-10-27T02:37Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 22:37 fiscal: S2D TYPED XPATH LANDED (unstaged, ~22:19, job-1793065892553-fiscal-land-post2): gate XSD-S2D-TYPED-XPATH GREEN 20261026-2207, then land.ps1. Live: xpath.spi B193B436 @22:17; xpath_eval.spi F4713CEE @22:21; schematron_run.spi 69158FEB @22:17; xpath.spi carries 2 per-case quantifiers. The train is now in the layout gate (LIB-TARGET-GAP).")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_150 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 150u32, "2026-10-27T02:42Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 22:42 fiscal: post-landing check on S2d: land.ps1 committed xpath_eval.spi as 63BE1682 at 22:19:44, but the live file is F4713CEE, written 22:21 by main's lane-wide Nil sweep (job-1793022977380-main-serial-nil-literal-2: 'xpath_eval.spi: 98 Nil -> []'). Verified that main's sweep applied ON TOP of S2d rather than over a stale read: the S2d transform now refuses on the live file because its anchor (the evaluate tail directly followed by ///> rust) is gone, and S2d's own inserted content is present (':| ^ outcome r =' GADT, all of its lines including the per-case \\* r. Broken case, and the test typed_assertions_match_the_dynamic_outcome). So xpath_eval holds S2d + main's Nil spelling; no action needed. xpath.spi (B193B436) and schematron_run.spi (69158FEB) are still exactly as S2d committed them.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_151 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 151u32, "2026-10-27T03:04Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 23:04 fiscal: the layout gate (LIB-TARGET-GAP, train 2) is going RED for a reason that is NOT the layout: S2d's new test typed_assertions_match_the_dynamic_outcome in xpath_eval.spi runs close to the 300 s default cell budget. Measured from the gate's own headroom lines: candidate 305 s (timed out), tree 233 s (passed), candidate retry 298 s (timed out); headroom 1.02 / 0.78 / 0.99. The layout landing makes ZERO changes to xpath_eval.spi on the live lib (diffed), so the outcome tracks machine load, not content. Root defect: xpath_eval.spi's package line has no --timeout, unlike xsd_emit.spi ('--package . --timeout 900000'); S2d's landing added the slow test without raising it. Fix: landing xpath-eval-budget (session scratchpad landings/xpath-eval-budget, gate XPATH-EVAL-BUDGET over xpath_eval.spi): line 1 becomes '///- --package . --timeout 900000', nothing else (verified on a copy: only line 1 changes; a second apply refuses). The train now takes -StartAt (order nil-sweep, gf, s2d, xpath-eval-budget, target-gap, date-time-granular, r5b) because transform-based landings cannot be detected as landed; resumed as job-1793070221978-fiscal-land-post3 -StartAt xpath-eval-budget, queued after train 2.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_152 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 152u32, "2026-10-27T03:57Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 23:57 fiscal: (1) XPATH-EVAL BUDGET LANDED (gate GREEN 20261026-2316): xpath_eval.spi line 1 is now '///- --package . --timeout 900000'. (2) LAYOUT (LIB-TARGET-GAP) LANDED on the retry (gate GREEN 20261026-2321, 9 lib files): the budget fix removed the xpath_eval timeout, as diagnosed; the xml.spi Zig reds (expected ';' after statement, 3 tests) are red on the unchanged tree too and remain a Zig backend issue outside this chain. (3) date_time stopped the train at its gate's apply: another lane rewrote date_time.spi at 23:05 (6D530E6A -> 2E5EB469) with an equivalent, granular heal of the SAME time_span Rust arm (rust_time_delta_microseconds / nanoseconds / sum / of_ticks: arithmetic in Spiral, one host call per macro). The Gleam, Lua and to-microseconds heals and stopwatch_elapsed_microseconds are still missing upstream, so the landing stays. The transform now skips the time_span step only when the old form is absent AND the upstream rust_time_delta_of_ticks is present; any other shape still refuses. On a copy of the live lib it applies, keeps the upstream helper, adds the microsecond stopwatch, and date_time plus its test probe type-check with 0 errors and no new long lines. Requeued as job-1793073436243-fiscal-land-post4 -StartAt date-time-granular (then R5b lib + fiscal).")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_153 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 153u32, "2026-10-27T04:27Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-27 00:27 fiscal: train 4's date_time gate would have gone RED on ONE test, time_span_keeps_sub_microsecond_ticks (Rust): 'rustc: cannot find crate chrono'. Cells compiles each Rust test cell standalone with rustc (no Cargo), so no Rust test cell can use chrono; that test could never have run. It also tested the time_span Rust arm that the other lane's 23:05 change now owns (live date_time keeps its own time_span tests: time_span_format, _2, _3, _custom_and_negative). The transform now appends that test only when it applies the time_span step itself; when that step is superseded it appends the Lua test alone. Re-derived on the live file: applies, keeps the upstream helper, adds stopwatch_elapsed_microseconds, and date_time plus its test probe type-check with 0 errors. The other 43 candidate tests had passed (118 rows). Stopped train 4 in its read-only gate (no land step reached, live date_time still 2E5EB469, no leftover processes) and requeued as job-1793075248912-fiscal-land-post5 -StartAt date-time-granular.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_154 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 154u32, "2026-10-27T05:18Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-27 01:17 fiscal: date_time gate (train 5) watch: the candidate's only red is the existing test timestamp_guid_from_timestamp on gleam and lua, a 6-target cell (fsharp, rust, zig, cuda, gleam, lua) timing out at 300 s. Per-phase from the gate notebooks: candidate 323 s (compile 135 s, target 150 s) vs unchanged tree 179 s (compile 121 s, target 38 s); timestamp_guid_from_timestamp_2 43 s vs 32 s. Spiral compile is close; the TARGET phase (backend builds and runs) is about 4x on the candidate, and the landing changes exactly the Gleam and Lua arms (timestamp Gleam arm, to_calendar_utc Lua arm, timestamp_gleam_of). The gate is retrying the candidate once (cell-timeout rule). Pre-written reading of the retry: target phase back near the tree's (under ~60 s) means a cold first build (for example a Gleam dependency resolve) and the gate should go GREEN; target still far above the tree means my Gleam or Lua arm is genuinely slower to build or run, and it gets profiled per target before any budget change, because raising the budget would hide a real slowdown.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_155 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 155u32, "2026-10-27T05:37Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-27 01:37 fiscal: DATE_TIME GRANULAR LANDED (unstaged, job-1793075248912-fiscal-land-post5): gate LIB-DATE-TIME-GRANULAR GREEN 20261027-0028 after its one cell-timeout retry (candidate retry 43/43, tree 41/41). Applying note 154's pre-written reading: the retry's timestamp_guid_from_timestamp took 200 s with a TARGET phase of 54 s (tree 38 s, first candidate 150 s), under the ~60 s line, so the first run's 150 s was a cold first build of the changed Gleam/Lua code, not a slowdown; no budget change made. Live date_time 21D9AE92: stopwatch_elapsed_microseconds present, the other lane's rust_time_delta_of_ticks kept, the superseded time_span step and its Rust-only test skipped. The train is now in R5b's gate (xsd 7/7, xsd_resolve 3/3 so far).")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_156 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 156u32, "2026-10-27T05:59Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-27 01:59 fiscal: R5B NAME FORMS LANDED (unstaged, job-1793075248912-fiscal-land-post5): gate XSD-R5B-NAME-FORMS GREEN 20261027-0130 (xsd 7/7, xsd_resolve 3/3, json_schema 3/3, xsd_emit 12/12, xsd_constraints 1/1), lib then fiscal. Live lib: xsd 39CC9131, xsd_resolve 3C04F565, json_schema 9AAFF1C4, xsd_emit 5F6D0526, xsd_constraints 698A51C8; fiscal generator 9BA3862A and corpus_checks 234ADFFE, exactly the forms type-checked against the final-chain lib at 20:35. THE PLANNED CHAIN IS NOW COMPLETE in the live tree: XSD typed release, R7+R8, Nil sweep, G-f witness writer, S2d typed xpath, xpath_eval budget, layout (target gap), date_time granular, R5b (+ Cells roundtrip fidelity). Next: recursive type groups (train 6, amended rule, note 10 of XSD-RECURSIVE-TYPE-GROUPS); then the reader fixes found tonight (XSD-CHOICE-EMPTIABLE-READER, XSD-ANY-CONTENT-READER) and XSD-IMPORTED-NAMESPACE-ELEMENTS, each derived from the live state.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_157 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 157u32, "2026-10-27T11:04Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-27 07:05 fiscal: TYPEFEATURESET SOTA SWEEP (user 10-27 06:3x: 'do another sweep in the typefeatureset to make sure we're sota'). Independent read-only review of xsd_emit/xml/xsd_certificate/generator and generated output, against SOTA schema-to-type generators (xsdata, JAXB/XJC, Rust quick-xml/yaserde/xsd-parser-rs, type providers, zod). VERDICT: this item's 0-0.5/1k premise (note 2, 10-25) is out of date by construct count, but the INVARIANTS lag. generated_nfe_v3_10 (18,914 lines) now has 189 nominals (10/1k), 118 per-record field GADTs with 760 indexed cases (40/1k), 354 exists (19/1k), 247 to_text instances (13/1k); yet the field GADT is used only by the writer (packed into exists and unpacked at once, nfe_v3_10:17727-17733; 0 uses in reader/example), the certificate's length index is hidden behind PackedMoves at once (xsd_certificate.spi:11-12), and domain meaning is still strings: every builtin maps to string (reference_type, xsd_emit:538), facets other than pattern/length are dropped (is_constrained 672; CFDI Base, xs:decimal with fractionDigits 6 and minInclusive, is 'type ... = string' with '_is_valid = true && true', cfdi:5360-5362; TServ restricts the pattern-checked TString but becomes '= string', losing the pattern, nfe_v3_10:734), readers failwith on bad input (571 required_value sites), choices drop data. KEPT (already SOTA): closed enum unions with match parse/print and share_unions dedup; pattern/length nominals with _of_string : string -> option t; patterns compiled to DFA code with compile-time certificate checks; the to_text prototype; the lib model xsd sort form (GADT by sort, phantom stages written/qualified/final); JSON Schema lowered onto the same model; compile-checked negative tests. RANKED GAPS, now items (team fiscal, all fields set): 1 XSD-FACET-TYPES (P0, 480m); 2 XSD-DECODE-RESULT (P0, 480m); 3 XSD-CHOICE-REPEATED-READER (P0, 240m: a correctness defect, UBL TransformType 'choice 0..unbounded' with an any arm fails 'no alternative present' on an ordinary empty ds:Transform, ubl:15958/15965-15968, and KeyInfo keeps only one alternative, ubl:15987); 4 XSD-PRIVATE-NOMINALS (P1, 180m, deps SPIRAL-UNFORGEABLE-WITNESSES: validated_text emits x_t_cnpj \"000...\" directly, nfe_v3_10:3051); 5 XSD-CODEC-PROTOTYPES (P1, 360m: only to_text exists, generator.spi:249-343 glues names as strings); 6 XSD-FIELD-DESCRIPTORS (P1, 720m, deps 5: field GADT indexed by owner+value, field_meta, fields prototype: the biggest code-size lever); 7 FISCAL-VERSIONED-FAMILIES (P1, 360m: write_family_modules never calls generate_versioned_family; cte_v4/gtve_v4/cte_os_v4/cte_simp_v4 are identical but 30 test lines, about 67k duplicated lines; witness carries an unused string payload); 8 XSD-TYPED-RULES (P1, 900m, deps 6 + 1: key/unique and EN16931 BR-* as typed predicates); 9 profile lattice -> FISCAL-CII-PROFILES (design noted there); 10 XSD-ANY-COMPLEMENTS (P2, 240m: generate_complements exists, fiscal never calls it); 11 XSD-CERTIFIED-MATCHER (P2, 300m); 12 XSD-SMALL-FIDELITY (P2, 120m: fixed/whiteSpace, dead 'true &&' conjuncts, json_schema numbers/dates and oneOf/anyOf). ORDER (WSJF, correctness first): 3, 1, 2, 4, 5, 7, 6, 10, 8, 11, 12; the country front ends (FISCAL-GENERIC children) interleave after 3+1 since Peppol/ZATCA/MyInvois are UBL profiles and need the choice fix and typed decimals first.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_158 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 158u32, "2026-10-29T22:48Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-29 18:48 fiscal: M6 SIGNED-FAMILY VERDICT (fiscal-6b-set-signed, pack 6b, 15:27-18:42): 3/5 HOLD (generated_cfdi_v4_0, cfdi_tfd_1_1, cfdi_pagos_2_0; private-nominals check holds) but those are NOT staged at all (signatures_of lists only the ds:Signature families nfe/cte/mdfe/gtve/cl/cr), so element-signature staging has never compiled for any family. BROKEN: generated_mdfe_v3_00 (main.spi:8048, reader yields staged s {signed_info..} where x_tmd_fe s declares a plain x_signature_type field) and generated_cl_envio_dte_v10 (line 5572 kind error: x_envio_dte_set_dte refers to x_dte_def_type without s). Three lib gaps in xsd_emit (base 5606F4B4): (a) staged_signatures (l.724) only rewrites fields typed string (the attribute case), never an element field like 'signature : x_signature_type'; (b) inline_candidates (l.624) keys inline types by type_name (tag_text e.name), but the emitter names them along the raw owner chain type_name (owner ++# _ ++# name) (l.2014/2127/2288), so nested inline types that mention a staged type (x_envio_dte_set_dte) never join the staged set; (c) the per-type field GADTs (x_T_field, e.g. 'EnvioDTE_SetDTE_field_SiiDte_DTE :: x_envio_dte_set_dte_field (list (x_dte_def_type))', and getters '=> x.signature') carry no stage parameter, so staging an element field also needs 'x_T_field s' plus staged getter types. Next: draft (a)+(b)+(c) as one held program on scratch (sgn/), with a mini DSig schema test in xsd_emit, then rerun set-signed for mdfe + cl.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_159 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 159u32, "2026-10-29T23:04Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-29 19:04 fiscal: M6 gaps (a)+(b) DRAFTED as held program fiscal/lib-programs/xsd-staged-element-signatures.kit (xsd_emit 5606F4B4 -> 161D00F5; rehearsed hash-equal, round trip byte-identical fixpoint): staged_type_names now runs on the dereferenced schema (as module_parts_by does), particle_requires_staged matches a signature by name whatever its typing, inline candidates and particle_mentions follow the emitter's raw owner chain (inner_owner, choice_owner), and staged_signatures wraps the signature field whatever its type (staged s T / staged s (T)). Moved first_element_name, first_in_group and line_after up (top-down scope). New test a_resolved_signature_and_a_referenced_signed_type_are_staged. Proof: fiscal-6b-sgn-proof (whole xsd_emit file, mirror sgn6b). Gap (c), field GADTs with a stage parameter (CL SetDTE has an ID attribute, so it gets x_envio_dte_set_dte_field r plus a getter and a walk writer), is still open, so CL stays red after this; MDF-e should compile. Next: set-proof mdfe on a mirror with this draft.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_160 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 160u32, "2026-10-29T23:29Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-29 19:29 fiscal: CORRECTION to note 159: gap (c) is not needed. record_texts/witness_items/element_witness for staged_writing already suppress the field GADT for staged owners (is_staged_owner, xsd_emit l.1903-1947), so CL's x_envio_dte_set_dte_field appeared only because gap (b) left SetDTE out of the staged set. With xsd-staged-element-signatures (161D00F5) both MDF-e and CL should compile. Next: once fiscal-6b-sgn-proof holds, set-proof generated_(mdfe_v3_00|cl_envio_dte_v10) on a mirror with the draft.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_161 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 161u32, "2026-10-30T00:32Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-29 20:32 fiscal: M6 proof fiscal-6b-sgn-proof (sgn6b, PRE-window-4 lib) was 23/24. The only failure, versioned_families_read_through_one_witness, was a 900 s cell timeout under node load (866.7 s, process tree killed), not an assertion; every other xsd_emit test incl. the new a_resolved_signature_and_a_referenced_signed_type_are_staged passed. Re-proof on POST-land mirror sgn7 (file_system 81140E3E, physics B1A4E848), with a mirror-only --timeout 5400000 header (land candidate stays 161D00F5): fiscal-6b-sgn7b-proof. The signed-family run fiscal-6b-sgnset2-mdfe-cl still runs on pre-window sgnset.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_162 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 162u32, "2026-10-30T01:57Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-29 21:57 fiscal: M6 xsd-staged-element-signatures HOLDS 24/24 ON A POST-WINDOW-4 MIRROR (fiscal-6b-sgn7b-proof, job-1793320347350, mirror sgn7: file_system 81140E3E, physics B1A4E848; the land candidate stays 161D00F5, and the mirror copy E9ABAA55 differs only by the --timeout 5400000 header and CRLF line endings, written by WriteAllLines). The earlier 23/24 was purely the 900 s cap under load. Remaining before main queues it: the end-to-end signed-family run on MDF-e + CL (sgnset2, pre-window mirror, still running) and a post-land repeat of it. Groom 10-29 21:5x: progress 50 -> 60.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_163 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 163u32, "2026-10-30T02:05Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-29 22:05 fiscal: DEPENDENCY WATCH: TYPEFEATURESET-ENFORCE (main Epic, 15%) was paused 10-29. It does not gate the near-term work here (M6 land, MDF-e/CL end-to-end); it gates only this row's close. No action needed before the M6 window.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_164 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 164u32, "2026-10-30T02:44Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-29 22:44 fiscal: M6 END-TO-END HOLDS for the families it targets (fiscal-6b-sgnset2-mdfe-cl, pack 6b, mirror sgnset with xsd_emit 161D00F5): regenerated generated_cl_envio_dte_v10 2/2 and generated_mdfe_v3_00 2/2 pass every test, and their validated nominals are private. The run's one broken claim (generator 'exit 0') is the format-step dotnet bug fixed on FISCAL-GENERATOR-FORMATS, not M6. So the two families set-signed broke this afternoon are fixed by the held program; pack-7 re-proof fiscal-p7-sgn-xsd_emit is running.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_165 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 165u32, "2026-10-30T03:41Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-29 23:41 fiscal: M6 RE-PROVEN ON PACK 7: fiscal-p7-sgn-xsd_emit (job-1793327594022, staged pack 7 core 9C899B30495F7DC5, post-land mirror p7sgn, mirror-only timeout header) HOLDS 24/24, and the round trip of 161D00F5 is a fixpoint on pack 7 (job-1793327633350). Sent main 'M6 ready'; queued for window 6 (after kit's split_list). Lander: run lander12's step 2 alone (base xsd_emit 5606F4B4 -> 161D00F5).")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_166 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 166u32, "2026-10-30T03:48Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-29 23:48 fiscal: M6 LANDED 23:47:58 (xsd_emit 5606F4B4 -> 161D00F5, merged window 5/6). The signed-family regression from this afternoon (MDF-e/CL compile failures under staging) is fixed in the live lib; main's close (document_test + type_probe over xsd_emit) follows.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_167 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 167u32, "2026-10-30T10:26Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-30 06:25 fiscal: GROOM: signed families green on the live lib after the M6 land (fiscal-p7-set-cl-mdfe, 0 broken claims: cl_dte_v10, cl_envio_dte_v10, mdfe_v3_00 pass every test). 70 -> 80. Open: the UBL-JSON round trip (MyInvois) and the remaining families' staged proofs.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_168 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 168u32, "2026-11-01T03:40Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-31 23:40 fiscal: MOVED (groom 10-31 night): the staged-signature proofs it listed are all done (XSD-SIGNING-STAGES closed 10-31); its last open part, the UBL-JSON round trip, has its root cause fixed and landed (XSD-RESTRICTION-ATTRIBUTE-OVERRIDE, xsd_emit 3872CC94) and the zero-drift run is on the node now (fiscal-p8-t1031u-ubl-drift). Closes on that verdict. 80 -> 90.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeature_drift_169 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", 169u32, "2026-11-01T07:08Z", "fiscal", "2026-11-01 03:10 fiscal: ZERO-DRIFT VERDICT: node job-1793505518611-fiscal-p8-t1031u-ubl-drift2 (t1031u: spiral with xsd_emit 3872CC94, the fiscal tree with the formatted UBL units, samples/ created) ran generated_ubl_invoice_2_1 over its 155 XML + 22 JSON real documents: 1/1 passed, 0 failed, headroom 0.62, the example written, and 0 drift lines in the log (no \"real documents do not read back\", \"does not keep every element\" or \"not stable\"). The UBL AmountType restriction no longer writes currencyID twice, so the MyInvois UBL-JSON round trip holds. CLOSED by the item's rule.")
inl fiscal_cii_profiles () = Task ("FISCAL-CII-PROFILES", "Next system for fiscal (user 2026-10-25 15:3x: pick the next country/system that enhances the generic modeling, start it later): UN/CEFACT CII D16B as used by Factur-X 1.07.3 / ZUGFeRD 2.3.3 (FR 2026 B2B mandate, DE), whose five profiles MINIMUM < BASIC WL < BASIC < EN 16931 < EXTENDED ship as restricted XSDs (+ schematron) of one document: adds the fourth structural dimension, a profile LATTICE (subsets of one standard), beside versions (BR families), runtime complements (CFDI) and cross-namespace names (KSeF)", Feature, P1, D6, 40u32, Active, "FISCAL-GENERIC,FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT", "team:fiscal,fiscal,generic,cii,eu,typefeatures", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_fiscal_cii_profiles () = Planning ("FISCAL-CII-PROFILES", Minutes 1920u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_fiscal_cii_profiles_1 () = Note ("FISCAL-CII-PROFILES", 1u32, "2026-10-25T19:54Z", "fiscal", "Parked: starts by the rule in note 2.")
inl note_of_fiscal_cii_profiles_2 () = Note ("FISCAL-CII-PROFILES", 2u32, "2026-10-25T19:54Z", "fiscal", "15:5x fiscal: why this one, against the alternatives (criterion fixed first: the system must add a modeling dimension fiscal cannot express today, not just more documents):\n- CII/Factur-X profiles: one document, five XSDs that are restrictions of each other. The generic model gains a profile index: a document type indexed by its profile, where a field exists only from some profile up (an indexed union / phantom-typed availability). That is exactly the typefeatureset FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT asks for, and it reuses KSeF's namespace work, since CII imports the udt/qdt datatype libraries across namespaces. The EN 16931 schematron runner fiscal already has for UBL applies to CII through the CEN CII artefacts.\n- Peppol PINT (sa/zatca, ae, jp, us/dbna): also profiles, but over UBL, which fiscal already generates; less new syntax.\n- India GST: JSON Schema, a new reader rather than a new modeling dimension.\n- Chile DTE, Colombia/Peru UBL extensions: more versions/extensions, both dimensions already covered.\nSources: FeRD press release Factur-X 1.07.3 / ZUGFeRD 2.3.3 (2025-05-07; five profiles each with their own XSD and schematron, updated with the EN 16931 artefacts every May and November) https://www.ferd-net.de/fileadmin/user_upload/FeRD/Downloads/Press_Release_Factur-X_1.07.3_ZUGFeRD_2.3.3.pdf ; France 2026 mandate context https://www.fiscal-requirements.com/news/3931-france-updates-the-factur-x-standard-for-the-2026-e-invoicing-mandate-alongside-harmonization-with-german-e-invoicing-standards\nStart rule, fixed now: begin when FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT R1 (the indexed xsd union) has landed, so the profile lattice is modeled on the typed core instead of bolted onto the flat union. First step then: re-check the newest Factur-X/ZUGFeRD release (the May/November cadence) and its licence, add eu/cii to init.ps1's corpus fetch, census the five profile XSDs (which elements each restricts away), and write the profile-lattice design and its gate before any code.")
inl note_of_fiscal_cii_profiles_3 () = Note ("FISCAL-CII-PROFILES", 3u32, "2026-10-27T11:04Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-27 07:04 fiscal: profile-lattice design from the 10-27 typefeatureset sweep (FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT, note of this time; gap 9, design only since no CII/Factur-X/Peppol corpus is fetched yet): the root carries a phantom profile index, x_invoice_type p with payload-free nominals (nominal en16931 = (), nominal peppol_bis3 = ()); a checked refinement as_peppol : x_invoice_type en16931 -> result (x_invoice_type peppol_bis3) (list rule_failure) built on XSD-TYPED-RULES; and a refines p q prototype so code written for a weaker profile accepts any stronger one. The same shape serves CII's MINIMUM < BASIC WL < BASIC < EN16931 < EXTENDED and Peppol BIS 3 as a restriction of EN16931 UBL.")
inl note_of_fiscal_cii_profiles_4 () = Note ("FISCAL-CII-PROFILES", 4u32, "2026-10-31T03:17Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-30 23:17 fiscal: SOURCES RE-CHECKED (web, 10-30; nothing fetched). The 10-25 versions (Factur-X 1.07.3 / ZUGFeRD 2.3.3) are superseded. Factur-X 1.08 / ZUGFeRD 2.4 were published 2025-12-04 and effective 2026-01-15, following the biannual EN 16931 update; they add sub-line management and an EXTENDED-CTC-FR profile (a subset of EXTENDED for France's B2B obligation), so the lattice to model is 6 profiles, not 5 (https://fnfe-mpe.org/factur-x/factur-x_en/, https://fnfe-mpe.org/wp-content/uploads/2025/12/2025-12-04_Factur-X_1.08_ZUGFeRD_2.4_Press_Release_EN.pdf; FeRD's package carries a unified specification, per-profile schemas, XSLT and samples: https://www.ferd-net.de/en/downloads/publications/details/zugferd-24-english). Newer, from SECONDARY sources only (verify on FNFE-MPE / FeRD before pinning): ZUGFeRD 2.5 effective 2026-07-01, and Factur-X 1.09.2 / ZUGFeRD 2.5.2 published 2026-08-04, said to fold in the coming EN 16931-1:2026 revision (https://www.vatupdate.com/2026/08/11/germany-and-france-update-zugferd-2-5-for-2026-e-invoicing-compliance/, https://www.componentsource.com/news/2026/10/26/keep-e-invoice-processing-aligned-with-updated-standards). CEN's CII rules: the EN16931 validation artefacts (https://github.com/ConnectingEurope/eInvoicing-EN16931) ship UBL 2.1 and CII D16B in the same releases (1.3.15 2025-10-20, 1.3.16 2026-04-10), and fiscal already pins 1.3.17 for UBL (FISCAL-EN16931-1-3-17), so CII's schematron comes from that same pinned release. Before starting: pin the newest Factur-X/ZUGFeRD release from the FNFE-MPE/FeRD pages themselves, and track the EN 16931-1:2026 revision, since it moves the canon's term list (FISCAL-CANON-MODEL).")
inl note_of_fiscal_cii_profiles_5 () = Note ("FISCAL-CII-PROFILES", 5u32, "2026-11-01T14:38Z", "fiscal", "2026-11-01 10:49 fiscal: QUALITY SPEC (round 5). DONE WHEN: (1) the UN/CEFACT CII D16B XSD closure is a generated family (pinned source) whose units pass the generic laws; (2) the Factur-X / ZUGFeRD profiles (MINIMUM, BASIC WL, BASIC, EN 16931, EXTENDED; the current release read at fetch time, not assumed) each read their official sample invoices with 0 drift; (3) the CEN CII schematron runs with pinned findings. NEXT STEP: after FISCAL-FAMILY-MANIFEST exists, add CII as manifest rows (this is the first family that must need 0 new generator code: the generic target's acceptance test). TYPEFEATURESET PLAN: profiles are restrictions of ONE CII type, so they are a phantom index, not separate generated families: \":! minimum = ()  :! basic_wl = ()  :! basic = ()  :! en16931 = ()  :! extended = ()\" and \"cii_invoice profile\"; narrowing is a checked function \"narrow : cii_invoice extended -> option (cii_invoice basic)\" built from the profile's restriction schema, and widening is total. No profile strings (\"urn:factur-x.eu:1p0:basic\") in logic: the guideline URN maps to the index once at read time.")
inl note_of_fiscal_cii_profiles_6 () = Note ("FISCAL-CII-PROFILES", 6u32, "2026-11-03T23:14Z", "fiscal", "2026-11-03 19:14 fiscal: DoD (1) SOURCE PINNED + FIRST MEASURE. Source: CEN's EN 16931 validation artefacts (github.com/ConnectingEurope/eInvoicing-EN16931, EUPL 1.2), tag validation-1.3.17 = commit 6e35e0ab8b09eb183f7d84b282e24ef95f9f5715: cii/schema/D16B SCRDM (Subset)/uncoupled clm (54 XSD: CrossIndustryInvoice_100pD16B + its Qualified/Unqualified/ReusableABIE data types + code and identifier lists) and cii/examples (15 CII invoices incl. XRechnung-O and a HUF example). fiscal scripts/init.ps1: new block -> deps/corpus/eu/en16931-cii, 69 files each pinned by sha256 (unstaged; the block alone fetched all 69 with matching pins; copied into the box corpus). CENSUS (lib xsd_census over xsd_resolve.load_schema_tree, Rust probe on vultr pack27): cii-d16b root CrossIndustryInvoice_100pD16B.xsd resolves 4 files, 0 missing, 1532 constructs, 1532 modeled, COMPLETE (no xs:any), so the generator models the whole CII invoice type with no any_content. NEXT: the CII family row in families.spi (root CrossIndustryInvoice, namespace urn:un:unece:uncefact:data:standard:CrossIndustryInvoice:100, documents = the 15 examples) -- the first family with 0 new generator code (this item's generic acceptance test) -- then one regen job; then the Factur-X/ZUGFeRD profile lattice as phantom indices.")
inl note_of_fiscal_cii_profiles_7 () = Note ("FISCAL-CII-PROFILES", 7u32, "2026-11-03T23:21Z", "fiscal", "2026-11-03 19:21 fiscal: CII FAMILY ROW DRAFTED (0 generator code, the item's generic acceptance test): %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\fiscal\\FISCAL-CII-PROFILES\\src\\families.spi ED85D4DF = live families + FISCAL-ES-FACTURAE's 10 Facturae documents + cii_d16b () (module generated_cii_d16b, root CrossIndustryInvoice / CrossIndustryInvoiceType in urn:un:unece:uncefact:data:standard:CrossIndustryInvoice:100, source Xsd [] since the uncoupled closure resolves locally, documents = the 15 pinned CEN examples) added to the standalone family list; generator.spi 7C315FDB = live + test generates_cii_d16b (write_family_modules [ cii_d16b () ] = 1). vultr pack27: families and generator --check with bodies exit 0, the families negative unchanged (exit 5). One regen job proves both (CII written + reads its 15 examples; Facturae reads its 10), after the codec landing.")
inl note_of_fiscal_cii_profiles_8 () = Note ("FISCAL-CII-PROFILES", 8u32, "2026-11-03T23:29Z", "fiscal", "2026-11-03 19:29 fiscal: PROFILE LATTICE AS TYPES (this item's TYPEFEATURESET PLAN), unstaged NEW fiscal src/profiles.spi 461F4F9B (registered after reports): phantom profiles minimum / basic_wl / basic / en16931 / extended_ctc_fr / extended with a witness GADT 'profile p'; 'step a b' names each one-level widening (MINIMUM < BASIC WL < BASIC < EN 16931 < EXTENDED-CTC-FR < EXTENDED); a recursive 'path a b' composes steps with an EXISTENTIAL middle profile (Then :: \\* a c. (\\? b. step a b * path b c) -> path a c); 'widen (path a b) (profile b) (profiled a) : profiled b' only accepts a document whose profile a path proves is not richer than the target (the path is a compile-time proof; no runtime cost). profile_urn maps a profile to its guideline URN once (Factur-X 1.0x URNs; EXTENDED-CTC-FR = None until its URN is read from a pinned 1.08 release instead of guessed). Tests: a_basic_invoice_widens_to_en16931_and_extended; negatives a_path_never_narrows (Got step en16931 en16931 / Expected step basic en16931) and a_widened_invoice_has_its_target_profile (Got profiled basic / Expected profiled extended). PROVEN on vultr pack27: check with bodies exit 0, both negatives exit 5, the body as Rust exit 0. Narrowing (checked, from each profile's restriction schema) waits for the profile schemas to be pinned.")
inl note_of_fiscal_cii_profiles_9 () = Note ("FISCAL-CII-PROFILES", 9u32, "2026-11-03T23:41Z", "fiscal", "2026-11-03 19:41 fiscal: REGEN PROOF QUEUED on the node: job-1793749206663-fiscal-cii-facturae, after the codec job (cap 7 h incl. wait). On the codec lib it places the CII drafts (families ED85D4DF with the CII row and the 10 Facturae documents, generator 7C315FDB with generates_cii_d16b) and the two new corpus folders, then gates generator (predicted 24/24), generated_cii_d16b (reads its 15 CEN examples) and generated_es_facturae_3_2_2 (reads its 10), collecting both units for landing. It finishes after the 21:00 run end; the verdict goes in the next session's first read.")
inl note_of_fiscal_cii_profiles_10 () = Note ("FISCAL-CII-PROFILES", 10u32, "2026-11-04T00:39Z", "fiscal", "2026-11-03 20:39 fiscal: GENERIC ACCEPTANCE MET (interim, job-1793749206663-fiscal-cii-facturae on the codec lib, core 626AA0C567CA0F8D): generator 24/24 incl. the new generates_cii_d16b -- the UN/CEFACT CII D16B family was written by the generic generator from a family row alone (0 generator code). Still running: format, then generated_cii_d16b's read-back of its 15 CEN examples and Facturae's 10.")
inl note_of_fiscal_cii_profiles_11 () = Note ("FISCAL-CII-PROFILES", 11u32, "2026-11-04T01:06Z", "fiscal", "2026-11-03 21:06 fiscal: DoD (1) MET on the node (job-1793749206663-fiscal-cii-facturae, codec lib xsd_emit 5E485076, core 626AA0C567CA0F8D): generator 24/24 (generates_cii_d16b writes the unit from a family row: 0 generator code); format 83 units, 0 failed; generated_cii_d16b 2/2 -- the unit's example validates and its 15 pinned CEN CII examples (validation-1.3.17: CII_example1..9, business examples, XRechnung-O, huf_example_cii, the rounding case) READ BACK through the typed reader/writer. The unit is collected in the job stage for landing (families ED85D4DF + generator 7C315FDB + the unit, together with the Facturae documents once its gate reports). Next: DoD (2) the Factur-X/ZUGFeRD profile samples, DoD (3) the CEN CII schematron with pinned findings.")
inl note_of_fiscal_cii_profiles_12 () = Note ("FISCAL-CII-PROFILES", 12u32, "2026-11-04T01:17Z", "fiscal", "2026-11-03 21:17 fiscal: the CII landing waits for the rerun job-1793754951593-fiscal-cii-facturae2 (same draft plus the W3C xmldsig fix for 5 non-Brazilian families, see FISCAL-ES-FACTURAE); CII itself was already 2/2.")
inl note_of_fiscal_cii_profiles_13 () = Note ("FISCAL-CII-PROFILES", 13u32, "2026-11-04T02:57Z", "fiscal", "2026-11-03 22:57 fiscal: rerun (job-1793754951593) again generated_cii_d16b 2/2 with the W3C xmldsig catalog fix; the CII landing travels with the same draft as Facturae's (see FISCAL-ES-FACTURAE), so it lands once the Facturae ds:Object gap is handled or the two are split.")
inl note_of_fiscal_cii_profiles_14 () = Note ("FISCAL-CII-PROFILES", 14u32, "2026-11-04T07:40Z", "fiscal", "2026-11-04 03:40 fiscal: DoD (2) SOURCES SURVEYED (nothing fetched yet). (a) github.com/ZUGFeRD/corpus (master, pushed 2026-10-13, MIT-style community corpus) has ZUGFeRDv2/correct/FNFE-factur-x-examples = 7 OFFICIAL FNFE samples, but as PDF/A-3 with the CII XML embedded as an attachment (Avoir_FR_type381_BASIC, Facture_{DOM,FR,UE}_{MINIMUM,BASICWL}), plus Mustangproject/intarsys/symtrax folders and a few loose XML; a 'fail' folder gives negative samples. Using them needs PDF attachment extraction (FlateDecode stream of the EmbeddedFile named factur-x.xml), and they predate Factur-X 1.08 / ZUGFeRD 2.4. (b) The current release package (FNFE-MPE 1.08, FeRD 2.4, n2) ships the samples as XML, per profile, beside the schemas; its download pages may require accepting terms, which needs the user's go. DECISION NEEDED (recorded, not asked): prefer (b) fetched once with the user's go and pinned by sha256 in init.ps1; (a) only if (b) stays gated, then pin the corpus commit and extract the embedded XML with a lib pdf reader (new work). Until then DoD (2) waits; DoD (1) is met (CII D16B family, 15/15 CEN examples) and the profile lattice exists in profiles.spi.")
inl note_of_fiscal_cii_profiles_15 () = Note ("FISCAL-CII-PROFILES", 15u32, "2026-11-04T07:41Z", "fiscal", "2026-11-04 03:41 fiscal: CURRENT RELEASE CHECKED (fnfe-mpe.org/factur-x/factur-x_en/, read by a web-fetch agent, nothing submitted): Factur-X 1.09.2 = ZUGFeRD 2.5.2 (published 2026-08-04) is current; the 'Complete EN' package carries the guide, the XMP extension schema, the CII D22B syntax and code-list sheets and 5 XSD + Schematron sets; EXAMPLE INVOICES ARE A SEPARATE DOWNLOAD. ACCESS: both are behind an EMAIL FORM ('send download link to', newsletter opt-in) -- entering an address is the user's action, not an agent's; no license name is stated. CONSEQUENCES: (1) DoD (2) needs the user to request the package + examples once (then fiscal pins them by sha256 in init.ps1 under deps/corpus/eu/factur-x-1.09); (2) the 1.09 profiles are restrictions of CII **D22B**, not D16B, so they need a D22B family row (the CII family proved a row needs 0 generator code) beside the D16B one; (3) the profile lattice in profiles.spi already has the 6 profiles incl. EXTENDED-CTC-FR.")
inl note_of_fiscal_cii_profiles_16 () = Note ("FISCAL-CII-PROFILES", 16u32, "2026-11-05T05:25Z", "fiscal", "Rebase check, 11-05 01:3x local: the families land draft drafts\\fiscal\\FISCAL-CII-PROFILES\\src\\families.spi F1CF3DDF is a SUPERSET of live src/families.spi B59F8A10 (user commit bb1ebad). Live differs from the 11-04 node copy 0AB7E110 in only two ways: the SAF-T PT family document deps/corpus/pt/saft/samples/saft_idemo599999999.xml, and three wrapped test lines; the draft already has both. So the 66 lines between live and the draft are exactly the draft's own changes: the cii_d16b family, w3c_xmldsig instead of local_xmldsig, the Facturae sample documents, and the regime witness. It lands over base B59F8A10 in ONE window with generator .next6 CB407C1C (which needs cii_d16b, see FISCAL-GRANULAR-280 n28), after the decode land.")
inl note_of_fiscal_cii_profiles_17 () = Note ("FISCAL-CII-PROFILES", 17u32, "2026-11-05T12:01Z", "fiscal", "GENERATOR + FAMILIES WINDOW LANDED in fiscal src, 11-05 08:0x local, in place and unstaged (backups generator.before-granular-land-385DD926.spi and families.before-granular-land-B59F8A10.spi in the session scratchpad): src/generator.spi 385DD926 -> 40DE71DF and src/families.spi B59F8A10 -> 99A8339B, in one window. That brings .next6 (decode + GRANULAR split + generates_cii_d16b back), the toml manifests, repo_path, the row split, documents as data, and bench_parts / write_family_parts; identity proven node-side (0 differing). generated/: all 82 unit manifests converted package.spiproj -> spiral.toml exactly as module_package writes them (dependencies in order; CFDI lists runtime, tfd, pagos), and generated_cii_d16b added (C3C111BE, formatter-stable) with its toml. README line 145 now names the per-unit spiral.toml. fiscal working tree: 222 changed paths. PENDING: the CI run after the user commits (generator tests regenerate units and diff; the generator's own CFDI test now checks spiral.toml).")
inl delphi_string_literal_index () = Task ("DELPHI-STRING-LITERAL-INDEX", "codegenDelphi StringIndex on a string LITERAL emits 'z'[i + 1]; a one-character Pascal literal is a Char constant, which cannot be indexed (fpc: Syntax error, ';' expected but '[' found), so any partially evaluated lib string function over a 1-char literal (portable_index_of with a 1-char search, lib's Delphi index_of arm) fails to build; fix: cast literal operands, AnsiString('z')[i + 1], and leave variable operands' text unchanged", TaskKind, P1, D1, 100u32, Done, "-", "delphi,compiler,codegen", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_delphi_string_literal_index () = Planning ("DELPHI-STRING-LITERAL-INDEX", Minutes 30u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_delphi_string_literal_index_1 () = Note ("DELPHI-STRING-LITERAL-INDEX", 1u32, "2026-10-25T22:07Z", "fiscal", "Found 2026-10-25 by fiscal's Delphi lib-arm smoke on the remote kit worker node (FPC 3.3.1-21298): the generated main.pas (captured during the run by a watcher, since Cells deletes its temp folder) has line 182 = v5 := 'z'[v1 + 1]. fpc probes: 'z'[i+1] fails with the exact error; AnsiString('z')[i+1] -> 122; AnsiString('a'#195#167'o')[i+1] -> 195 (byte semantics kept); AnsiString(s)[i+1] on a variable -> 98. Draft: session scratchpad delphi-literal-index/apply.py, one arm before the general one: | StringIndex, [DLit (LitString _) as a; b] -> AnsiString(...)[... + 1]; dry run ok on spiral_compiler.fs 34519B923366CCEC. Variable operands keep their exact text, so existing Delphi matrix rows stay byte-identical. Regression sample when packed: a Delphi program indexing a one-character literal.")
inl note_of_delphi_string_literal_index_2 () = Note ("DELPHI-STRING-LITERAL-INDEX", 2u32, "2026-10-25T23:38Z", "compiler", "Root cause at a single line. codegenDelphi StringIndex (spiral_compiler.fs:165506):\n  | StringIndex, [a;b] -> $\"{tup a}[{tup b} + 1]\"\nemits the operand inlined, index 1-based (Pascal). When `a` is a ONE-CHARACTER string literal it renders as\nPascal `'z'`, which Object Pascal types as a Char, not a string -- and a Char can't be indexed, so `'z'[i + 1]`\nis an FPC type error. (Multi-char literals like `'abc'[k]` and string variables index fine; only the 1-char\nliteral, typed as Char, breaks. Array index at :165509 is unaffected -- arrays aren't Chars.)\n\nWhy only Delphi: the backend inlines a string literal into the index expression instead of binding it to a\nstring var first; most other backends wrap through a helper (spiralStringIndex) or byte access.\n\nFix candidates (pick by what FPC 3.2 accepts -- needs a build to decide):\n  (a) emit-site cast: `string({tup a})[{tup b} + 1]`. `string('z')` promotes the Char literal to a 1-char\n      string; `string(x)` on an already-string x is identity. Simplest, local. RISK: FPC may reject indexing a\n      cast EXPRESSION (older Delphi allowed indexing only string variables); verify.\n  (b) bind string literals to a temp string var before StringIndex, so it emits `v5[i + 1]` with\n      `v5: string := 'z';`. Matches how indexing a variable already works; more plumbing (literal/Dyn handling).\n\nVerify (needs a compile, deferred under 100% CPU):\n1. Add a sample indexing a one-char string literal on Delphi (e.g. `('z')[0]`), build -Backend Delphi -Native,\n   confirm FPC compiles and the run agrees with the C oracle.\n2. Confirm multi-char-literal and string-variable indexing stay correct (-Suite examples -Native, Delphi rows green).\nSeam: anchored apply + composite hash into pack 5n. Commit the sample's main.pas.")
inl note_of_delphi_string_literal_index_3 () = Note ("DELPHI-STRING-LITERAL-INDEX", 3u32, "2026-10-26T06:12Z", "compiler", "compiler lane: FIX VERIFIED end-to-end on the node. One-line fix at spiral_compiler.fs:165506 (codegenDelphi StringIndex, single shared copy, no gpt5parallel pair):\n  was:  | StringIndex, [a;b] -> $\"{tup a}[{tup b} + 1]\"\n  now:  | StringIndex, [a;b] -> let sa = tup a in if sa.StartsWith \"'\" then $\"string({sa})[{tup b} + 1]\" else $\"{sa}[{tup b} + 1]\"\nRationale: a string LITERAL operand renders as a Pascal char/string constant 'z' which FPC cannot index -- 'z'[i+1] is a syntax error (confirmed on the node, FPC 3.2: \"')' expected but '[' found\"). Wrapping in string(...) makes it an indexable string expression -- string('z')[i+1] compiles (confirmed). The StartsWith-' guard wraps ONLY literals, so string-VARIABLE indexes (v0[i+1]) are untouched, no residual churn on existing Delphi samples.\nVerified (node, FIXED core built single-flight): a literal-index sample (sm.index \"qwe\" i with runtime i) generated `v1 := string('qwe')[v0 + 1]`, fpc compiled+linked (exit 0), exe ran exit 0 (c matched 'w'); the UNFIXED core generated `'qwe'[v0 + 1]` (the bug); the variable case (~x=\"qwe\") stays `v0 := 'qwe'` with an unwrapped index -> no regression.\nRemaining for pack 5n: a committed regression sample (new dir samples/delphi_string_literal_index + package.spiproj + a matrix.psd1 Delphi entry + committed outputs) and a -Suite examples -Native Delphi no-regression pass in the accept. Handoff: anchored apply script (fix + sample) + composite hash on the 5n base, per the seam.")
inl note_of_delphi_string_literal_index_4 () = Note ("DELPHI-STRING-LITERAL-INDEX", 4u32, "2026-10-26T16:40Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-26: delivered the handoff's requested anchored apply script for the VERIFIED fix (note 3), TESTED + build-ready: scratchpad/apply-delphi-string-literal-index.ps1. Dry-run + scratch-copy apply verified 10-26 against the current spiral_compiler.fs: the Delphi StringIndex anchor `| StringIndex, [a;b] -> $\"{tup a}[{tup b} + 1]\"` is unique (count=1; now at line 165463, note 3's 165506 drifted under the repack -- anchored on TEXT), the fixed form is absent before apply, and -Apply lands exactly `let sa = tup a in if sa.StartsWith \"'\" then $\"string({sa})[{tup b} + 1]\" else $\"{sa}[{tup b} + 1]\"` (correct quoting confirmed). Supersedes note-1's AnsiString apply.py (pre-verification, different approach). To land in pack-5n: run the script -Apply on the CoreSource copy, build -Backend Delphi -Native, confirm a 1-char-literal index compiles+matches C, then STILL TODO (build-time): commit a regression sample dir samples/delphi_string_literal_index (+package.spiproj + a matrix.psd1 Delphi entry + committed outputs) and a -Suite examples -Native Delphi no-regression pass in the accept. Bundles cleanly with the Zig op-gap pack (note 48) -- different files/lines, no conflict.")
inl note_of_delphi_string_literal_index_5 () = Note ("DELPHI-STRING-LITERAL-INDEX", 5u32, "2026-10-27T01:00Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-26 build-independent reconciliation before landing the apply. (1) The regression sample ALREADY EXISTS: samples/delphi_literal_index/main.spi (`inl c = !!!!StringIndex(\"z\", at)` with ~at dynamic, then `!!!!EQ(c,'z')`) -- so \"add samples/delphi_string_literal_index\" is REDUNDANT. BUT it is NOT in matrix.psd1's Delphi list and has NO Delphi EXPECTED.tsv row -> untested; the real landing step is WIRING it into the Delphi matrix list. (2) The live compiler is UNFIXED: codegenDelphi show_w (spiral_compiler.fs:165220) = `WLit a -> litDelphi a` (bare 'z', no AnsiString), StringIndex arm (:165463) = `{tup a}[{tup b}+1]` -> emits `'z'[v0+1]` (the bug). (3) The committed main.pas is FIX-AHEAD: it shows `AnsiString('z')[v0+1]` (fixed), which the current compiler does NOT produce -- a regen would OVERWRITE it to the buggy `'z'[v0+1]`, and since it is not in the matrix nothing catches that. (4) WRAPPING MISMATCH: my apply-delphi-string-literal-index.ps1 emits `string({sa})[...]` -> `string('z')`, but the committed output uses `AnsiString('z')`. Under {$H+} string==AnsiString SEMANTICALLY but they DIFFER textually -> byte non-identity vs the committed output. RECONCILE before landing: make the apply emit `AnsiString(...)` to match the committed output (or decide the fix belongs in show_w for ALL StringT literals, not just StringIndex -- this 1-literal sample cannot disambiguate; check a sample with a non-indexed string literal). Then wire delphi_literal_index into the Delphi matrix + regen. (5) LOCUS CONFIRMED = StringIndex arm, NOT show_w: bare string literals render fine in non-indexing contexts (managed_string_concat: v1 := 'spi', v8 := v7 = 's'; string_ord: v0 := 'a', v3 := #195#169'x' -- assignment/comparison/multibyte all valid via implicit AnsiString conversion); only direct 'z'[i] indexing of a 1-char literal breaks. So wrap ONLY in the StringIndex arm (my apply's locus is right; show_w would be over-broad). ROBUSTNESS: prefer guarding on `match a with DLit(LitString _)` over `sa.StartsWith` quote -- the latter MISSES a single non-printable char literal rendered as #233 (no quote), which would still break as #233[i]; the DLit match catches both 'z' and #NN forms. ANCHOR STILL VALID (re-gripped 10-26, file ~189k lines grep-authoritative): the StringIndex arm anchor matches EXACTLY ONCE at :165463, distinct from every other backend (Rust .as_bytes() 164748, Zig spiralIndex 166187, Gleam/C/Python spiral_string_index). Ready to apply+build+wire the moment a slot frees.")
inl note_of_delphi_string_literal_index_6 () = Note ("DELPHI-STRING-LITERAL-INDEX", 6u32, "2026-10-27T21:15Z", "compiler", "Fix DRAFTED (build-bound verify pending), 10-27, compiler lane.\n\nRoot cause (verified from live source spiral_compiler.fs:165463): codegenDelphi's StringIndex arm emits\n`{a}[{b} + 1]`. When `a` is a directly-inlined string LITERAL, `tup a` renders a Pascal char literal ('z'),\nwhich FPC parses as a Char constant -> 'z'[i+1] cannot be indexed. A string VARIABLE is a proper AnsiString and\nindexes fine, and a literal bound to a let is a DV not a DLit, so ONLY a directly-inlined literal is broken.\n\nFix (minimal, literal-only): new arm BEFORE the existing one, matching DLit (LitString _) (the literal shape used\nat :165454) and casting to AnsiString: `AnsiString({a})[{b} + 1]`. FPC coerces the char literal to a length-1\nAnsiString; the cast is a no-op for the (already-working) variable and multi-char cases. The common variable arm\nis untouched, so only the literal-index residuals move.\n\nPack: apps/compiler/packs/apply-delphi-string-literal-index.ps1 (unstaged draft), dry-run 1/1 anchor clean.\ncodegenDelphi is a SHARED section (one arm, both cores). LAND-GATE (needs a compiler slot; TRAIN+accept-3 busy):\n  1. build single-flight + add samples/delphi_string_literal_index indexing a string literal DIRECTLY (keep it a\n     DLit, not let-bound) and commit outputs.\n  2. test.ps1 -Suite examples -Native: Delphi matches C oracle, no NATIVE-DIFF/REGRESSED.\n  3. -Accept (only the literal-index residuals should move).")
inl note_of_delphi_string_literal_index_7 () = Note ("DELPHI-STRING-LITERAL-INDEX", 7u32, "2026-10-28T00:22Z", "compiler", "Regression sample DRAFTED (scratchpad/delphi-string-literal-index-sample.spi): inl ~i=0i32; inl c=sm.index \"z\" i; if (=.) c (quote z) then 0i32 else 1i32. Literal \"z\" (DLit) + RUNTIME index -> runtime StringIndex on a one-char literal -> triggers (quote z)[i+1] (a constant index would const-fold it away, no op). PLACE at samples/delphi_string_literal_index AFTER the CCMR land (adding a sample mid-A/B breaks the run A vs B compare), apply the pack, build -Backend Delphi -Native -> confirm crash->exit 0, commit outputs + matrix.psd1 Delphi list + accept.")
inl note_of_delphi_string_literal_index_8 () = Note ("DELPHI-STRING-LITERAL-INDEX", 8u32, "2026-10-28T00:42Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-27 CORRECTION to n7: regression sample ALREADY EXISTS -- samples/delphi_literal_index (git-committed today 05:43), main.spi = !!!!StringIndex(\"z\", at) with ~at runtime; main.pas:12 = v1 := (quote z)[v0 + 1]; (Char literal indexed -> FPC build-fail = the bug). My scratchpad draft was redundant (deleted); no new sample needed. Land-gate: apply the pack (wraps DLit -> AnsiString((quote z))[v0+1]), build Delphi, regen delphi_literal_index main.pas, confirm compiles+exit 42, accept. Not in matrix.psd1 -> its Delphi build-fail is an open RED awaiting this fix.")
inl note_of_delphi_string_literal_index_9 () = Note ("DELPHI-STRING-LITERAL-INDEX", 9u32, "2026-10-28T01:23Z", "compiler", "main 21:3x ORDER: run the literal-index accept AFTER main matrix -Px86_64 apply (main applies it right after CCMR landed, before 5n) -- that apply changes the Delphi rows bitness, so my regen+accept of delphi_literal_index main.pas must see the new bitness. Sequence: CCMR landed -> main x86_64 apply -> 5n -> my Delphi fix build+regen+accept. Wait for main x86_64-applied confirm before accepting.")
inl note_of_delphi_string_literal_index_10 () = Note ("DELPHI-STRING-LITERAL-INDEX", 10u32, "2026-10-28T02:56Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-27: CHECKED -Accept+-Filter (main Q, test.ps1:1142-1163). SAFE -- MERGES, does NOT drop other rows: -Filter -> $fullSchedule=false (:1151), $scheduled empty (:1150), $unscheduled empty (:1152); $kept = ALL existing rows except the re-run ones, $merged = kept + filtered (:1159-63). FAST one-sample accept fits 05:00-08:15; full-accept fallback not needed. 5n changes only this sample (Delphi fix; host/cells dont touch codegen), so filtered is also SUFFICIENT. CAUTION: accept (:1142) runs BEFORE the bad-row exit (:1174), so VERIFY FIRST: run test.ps1 -Mode single-flight -Suite all -Filter delphi_literal_index -Native (no -Accept), confirm Delphi exit 42 + no DISAGREE, THEN re-run with -Accept.")
inl note_of_delphi_string_literal_index_11 () = Note ("DELPHI-STRING-LITERAL-INDEX", 11u32, "2026-10-28T02:58Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-27 CORRECTION to n10 sufficiency: main -- 5n changes MORE than delphi_literal_index (tibia spill-lf/Lua, x gleam-export-types, main spiral-toml, my lexer dcolon/zwsp/macro-sigil, + -Px86_64 -> EVERY Delphi row 64-bit); accept-6 was on 5m. A filtered accept would split the baseline 5m/5n = INSUFFICIENT. main OWNS the FULL 5n accept (~05:00 slot 2, ~1h45, done ~06:45, one write covers delphi_literal_index + bitness + Lua/Gleam/lexer). MY job: verify delphi_literal_index row in mains results (compiles, exit 42, oracle agree); no separate accept by me. n10 -Accept+-Filter merge-safety finding still true, just unused.")
inl note_of_delphi_string_literal_index_12 () = Note ("DELPHI-STRING-LITERAL-INDEX", 12u32, "2026-10-28T05:10Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-28 DONE (verified independently): 5n accept GREEN (run -232226, main). delphi_literal_index [Delphi] compile=ok native=ran exit=42 oracle-agree (32-bit); main.pas:12 = v1 := AnsiString((quote z))[v0 + 1] (the fix, not the bug (quote z)[v0+1]); baseline EXPECTED.tsv has [Delphi] exit=42 plus C/Fsharp/Rust exit 42. Fix shipped in 5n (delphi-literal-index.py); my apply-delphi-string-literal-index.ps1 pack is superseded + unused. RESOLVED.")
inl note_of_delphi_string_literal_index_13 () = Note ("DELPHI-STRING-LITERAL-INDEX", 13u32, "2026-10-28T10:44Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-28 DONE: fix shipped in the installed 9FF1B708 core (repo == installed). codegenDelphi StringIndex special-cases a string LITERAL at spiral_compiler.fs:165574 -> | StringIndex, [DLit (LitString _) as a; b] -> AnsiString({tup a})[{tup b} + 1], wrapping the one-char literal in AnsiString so Pascal treats it as a string not a Char; general arm :165575 is {tup a}[{tup b} + 1]. Staged pack superseded by 5n. Evidence: file:line above.")
inl cells_section_target_split () = Task ("CELLS-SECTION-TARGET-SPLIT", "Cells spi-to-livemd splits a ///> section into cells but writes the target line only into the first cell, so notebook runs compile the others unrestricted (broke apps/wasm CI 10-25)", Story, P1, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "cells,livemd,notebook,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_cells_section_target_split () = Planning ("CELLS-SECTION-TARGET-SPLIT", Minutes 60u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_cells_section_target_split_1 () = Note ("CELLS-SECTION-TARGET-SPLIT", 1u32, "2026-10-25T23:02Z", "main", "10-25 19:02 main: filed from the 10-25 wasm CI red (APPS-GRANULAR-HEAL n12-n14). When Cells turns an .spi into a livemd (run_notebook --livemd-path --export-only), a section whose ///> target line covers several definitions is split into several smart cells, but the ///> line goes only into the FIRST cell. A notebook run then compiles the later cells without the target restriction, so a cell referring to a definition in the skipped first cell fails (spiral_wasm: ETY-UnboundVariable run_main). The export path can't show it, since export ignores cell targets. Fix: when splitting a section, copy its ///> line(s) into every cell it creates; the spi export then re-merges them (the export should not duplicate the line in the spi). Gate: a document_test case with a 2-definition '///> _' section that round-trips spi -> livemd -> spi byte-identically and whose livemd carries the target line in both cells. Meanwhile livemd-from-spi.ps1 refuses split sections and runs the notebook before -Apply.")
inl note_of_cells_section_target_split_2 () = Note ("CELLS-SECTION-TARGET-SPLIT", 2u32, "2026-10-26T00:09Z", "main", "10-25 20:09 main: root cause, from apps/cells/spi/document.spi. open_step (:657) pushes a ///> line onto targets, and the first code line folds them into that code run (InCode (Row (line, targets))), which empties targets. A '/// ' doc line then closes the run (close_open), so a ///> applies to exactly ONE cell. The wasm main lost its target because a doc paragraph sat between run_main and main. The section/heading has nothing to do with it; the doc comment ends the target's scope. Census (scratchpad target-scope-census.ps1, every .spi in spiral, eoie, x, fiscal, polyglot, alphabet, dice, dir-tree-html and tibia, excluding deps, target, _build and .cache): 2,550 files, 356 with ///> lines, 0 code runs that would gain a target if a target carried to the next heading. Positive control: the pre-fix spiral_wasm.spi (without the 10-25 line 217) counts exactly 1 (line 218, ':= main () ='). Decision rule, written before choosing: (1) change no existing file's behavior; (2) keep every livemd<->spi round trip byte-identical; (3) smallest change. (A) carry-to-next-heading passes (1) by the census but fails (2): a livemd with a targeted cell followed by an untargeted one in the same section exports to an spi that re-reads with the target carried, unless a new 'no target' spelling is added. (B) a warning when a doc comment ends a targeted code run that is followed by untargeted code before the next heading passes all three (0 hits today, so 0 false positives). (C) leaving it to livemd-from-spi.ps1's refusal covers only the conversion path. Chosen: (B), in Cells (the spi walk reports a diagnostic row the runner prints), proven by a document_test case (the pre-fix wasm shape warns, the fixed shape doesn't, the round trip stays byte-identical). Cells is read live by every lane, so the edit is drafted on a scratch copy and lands through the reader-guarded land.")
inl note_of_cells_section_target_split_3 () = Note ("CELLS-SECTION-TARGET-SPLIT", 3u32, "2026-10-26T00:16Z", "main", "10-25 20:16 main: option (B) drafted as %TEMP%\\cells-target-gaps\\apply.ps1 -Cells <apps/cells> [-Apply]: 7 anchored edits, all checked before any write. Each anchor must match exactly once, the file's own end-of-file newline is kept, and every file is LF. (1) document.spi: target_gaps_walk/target_gaps, the census rule as a pure line walk. A /// # heading clears the target in force; a ///> line sets it; a doc line after a target marks 'after doc'; the first unindented non-comment line after that is a gap row 'line<TAB>target'. (2) main.spi exports target_gaps. (3) domain.ex wraps it. (4) document.ex: Document.target_gaps(spi) returns [{line, target}]. (5) notebook.ex: both .spi branches of parse/3 print 'warning: <path>:<line>: a doc comment above ended the cell that ///> _ targets, so this code runs untargeted; repeat the target line after the doc comment' to stderr; that is the one place both the direct .spi run and the spi-to-livemd export pass through. (6) document_test.exs: the pre-fix wasm shape gives [{8, '///> _'}], the fixed shape and a heading-separated pair give []. Applied cleanly on a %TEMP% copy (29 insertions, 2 deletions in lib). Not compiled yet: tibia's PR-28 live block runs. Gate, prepared as main-cells-target-gaps-verify (scratchpad start-cells-target-gaps-verify.ps1, which refuses while a live-gui window exists): a full copy of apps/cells with the apply, mix compile regenerating domain.gleam from the edited spi, then mix test document_test.exs, then the Spiral target_gaps over all 2,550 .spi files plus the pre-fix wasm control. GREEN = tests exit 0, the control reports exactly 1 gap, the tree reports 0 (two independent implementations agreeing with the PowerShell census). Landing goes into apps/cells, which every lane's Cells run reads, so it follows the reader-guarded pattern.")
inl note_of_cells_section_target_split_4 () = Note ("CELLS-SECTION-TARGET-SPLIT", 4u32, "2026-10-26T05:37Z", "main", "10-26 01:37 main: option (B) GREEN on the worker node (main-cells-target-gaps-verify-2). The copy is at the original depth below C:\\home\\git, so the document tests find their sibling repos; the heal's own target-gap test passes; the 3 remaining failures are dice/alphabet/lib loading tests reading the node's older repo copies, and none calls target_gaps. Census cross-check: the Spiral Document.target_gaps over all 2,501 node .spi files plus the pre-fix wasm control reports exactly the control's gap and 0 in the tree, matching the PowerShell census, two independent implementations agreeing. (The first run never executed the census: cmd.exe mangled a mix run -e one-liner through mix.bat; it now runs from an .exs file.) Landing as main-cells-target-gaps-land: it waits for a 2-second gap with no Cells reader and no live block, and refuses if any of the 6 touched files changed since its hashes were taken.")
inl note_of_cells_section_target_split_5 () = Note ("CELLS-SECTION-TARGET-SPLIT", 5u32, "2026-10-29T08:00Z", "main", "10-29 03:5x main: verified on the committed tree after the wipe (3f1b7588 carries target_gaps). mix test test/spiral/document_test.exs locally: 20/22 passed, and the heal's own test 'a doc comment that ends a target's cell before more code is reported as a target gap' (document_test.exs:603) passes. The 2 failures are outside this item and were there before tonight: 'a _real.spir interleaves with its .spi by heading and both round-trip through livemd' (lib/spiral/cli_args_real.spir) and 'the spiral library .spi files load as notebooks, one test cell per test block' (fails on lib/spiral/dictm.spi). Both files are committed and untouched tonight (dictm.spi last commit 3f1b7588). Done.")
inl rust_derive_inference () = Task ("RUST-DERIVE-INFERENCE", "codegenRust infers Clone/Debug/serde/borsh derives for generated heaps and unions (a fixpoint over field types, opt-in per crate), replacing dice's Add-RustDerives regex post-pass over main.rs", Story, P1, D4, 85u32, Blocked, "PACK-7", "compiler,rust,derive,granular,dice,wipe-redo,user-batch,team:compiler,compiler", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_rust_derive_inference () = Planning ("RUST-DERIVE-INFERENCE", Minutes 240u32, Load Light, Assigned User)
inl note_of_rust_derive_inference_1 () = Note ("RUST-DERIVE-INFERENCE", 1u32, "2026-10-25T23:27Z", "main", "10-25 19:27 main: filed from DICE-GRANULAR-HEAL n4/n6. dice/ui/build.ps1 runs Add-RustDerives (dice/ui/rust_derive.ps1) after BuildSpiral: a regex pass over the generated main.rs. It collects every struct HeapN and enum USN/UHN, computes per trait a greatest fixpoint over the field types (a type keeps a trait only if every generated field type keeps it and every other field identifier is on that trait's allow-list: base scalars, Rc/Option/Vec/HashMap, leptos signals for Debug/serde; no '&', dyn or Fn fields), and inserts #[derive(Clone, Debug, serde::Serialize, serde::Deserialize, borsh::BorshSerialize, borsh::BorshDeserialize)] lines (66 serde derives on today's dice output). That is host-text drift (GRANULAR-HEAL: a post-pass over generated code, like x's retired rust-modules.ps1), and the compiler already does the same analysis for Default impls (codegenRust emits Default only when every field has a default expression). Spec for codegenRust: the same fixpoint over the layout field types it already knows (exact types, no identifier allow-lists), with the extra traits opt-in per crate (a package header, e.g. '///- --rust-derive serde,borsh', since serde and borsh are crate dependencies). Gate: dice's main.rs from the compiler equals today's Add-RustDerives output byte for byte (or differs only in derive order, if declared), cargo check passes for wasm32, and then rust_derive.ps1 and its build.ps1 call are deleted (alphabet too, if it has one). Team: compiler lane (direction); main integrates via a pack.")
inl note_of_rust_derive_inference_2 () = Note ("RUST-DERIVE-INFERENCE", 2u32, "2026-10-25T23:38Z", "compiler", "Goal: move dice's Add-RustDerives regex post-pass (dice/ui/rust_derive.ps1, run by dice/ui/build.ps1 after\nBuildSpiral) into codegenRust, as a per-trait fixpoint over the layout field TYPES the compiler already knows.\n\nThe post-pass algorithm (to port): greatest fixpoint per trait over struct HeapN / enum USN|UHN. A type keeps a\ntrait iff every GENERATED field type (Heap/US/UH/Mut N) keeps it AND every other (leaf) field identifier is\ntrait-safe. Trait-safety in the post-pass = an allow-list per trait (base scalars, Rc/Option/Vec/HashMap for all;\nleptos signals + RefCell for Debug/serde; borsh = base only) plus hard denials (`&`, dyn, Fn/FnMut/FnOnce, and a\nnonClone external list: Fragment/AnyView/View/Box/Pin/File/JoinHandle/Command/Child*/Rexie/RequestBuilder/...).\nClone is special-cased (kept unless a `&` field or a nonClone external). Result: #[derive(...)] on each type\n(66 serde derives on today's dice output).\n\nWhere it lands in codegenRust: alongside the existing emission the compiler ALREADY does for the SAME analysis:\nspiral_compiler.fs:164970 `#[derive(Clone)]` for a union without a moving field, and :164862 `impl Default` only\nwhen every field has a default. Extend that field-type walk to compute Clone/Debug/serde/borsh eligibility as a\nfixpoint, and emit the derive list. serde/borsh are crate deps -> OPT-IN per crate via a package header\n(spec: `///- --rust-derive serde,borsh`); without it, only Clone/Debug.\n\nDESIGN NUANCE (the one place the spec's \"exact types, no identifier allow-lists\" can't fully hold):\nthe compiler can judge GENERATED layout field types structurally, but dice's heaps also hold OPAQUE external\ntypes injected via emit/macro text (Fragment, Box, File, leptos signals, ...). The compiler sees those as opaque\nstrings, not typed layouts, so it cannot structurally know their trait membership. So codegenRust still needs a\ntrait-safety SOURCE for external field types: either a small built-in deny/allow list (the post-pass's nonClone +\nallow lists, carried into the compiler) or a per-crate declaration of external types' traits. Recommend: structural\nfor generated types, declaration-driven for external ones (package header lists externally-safe types), so it is\nprincipled rather than a hidden allow-list. Resolve this with main before implementing.\n\nGate (from main, needs a build): dice's main.rs from the compiler == today's Add-RustDerives output byte-for-byte\n(or differs only in derive ORDER, if order is declared), cargo check passes for wasm32, then delete rust_derive.ps1\nand its dice/ui/build.ps1 call (and alphabet's, if any). Team: compiler lane direction; main integrates via a pack.\n\nPRIORITY (main asked, vs COMPILER-CROSS-MACHINE-REPRO): land repro FIRST (WSJF: 40%, gate-validated, 3 small\nbuild-script edits, unblocks the transfer's ship-DLL-by-hash), then this (P1 but 0% and a larger codegenRust change).\nDifferent files, no conflict. Diagnose now (done), implement right after repro when a heavy slot frees.")
inl note_of_rust_derive_inference_3 () = Note ("RUST-DERIVE-INFERENCE", 3u32, "2026-10-25T23:42Z", "compiler", "main refined the spec (10-25, compiler lane agreed): structural for generated field types + declaration-driven for external types is the intent; \"no identifier allow-lists\" means no lists buried in the compiler or a post-pass, and a package-header declaration is the principled form of the same fact. Two refinements:\n1. Declared, not inferred, and per trait. An external type the dice package header does NOT declare keeps that trait OUT of every heap or union that reaches it. The greatest fixpoint drops the derive safely, so an undeclared external never makes the output fail to compile (conservative by construction).\n2. dice's opaque lists become dice's header. The names in rust_derive.ps1 (Fragment, Box, File, leptos signals, the nonClone list) move into dice's package header, not a compiler builtin. Gate unchanged: dice main.rs from the compiler == Add-RustDerives output byte-for-byte (or differ only in declared derive order), wasm32 cargo check passes, then rust_derive.ps1 + its build.ps1 call are deleted. If the package header cannot express one of those lists exactly, show main the difference before choosing a builtin.\nOrder confirmed: COMPILER-CROSS-MACHINE-REPRO lands first (gate-validated, small, unblocks ship-DLL-by-hash); derive inference implements after it when a heavy slot frees.")
inl note_of_rust_derive_inference_4 () = Note ("RUST-DERIVE-INFERENCE", 4u32, "2026-10-26T15:48Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-26: resolved the open header question (note 3 \"show main the difference\") by reading dice/ui/rust_derive.ps1 against what codegenRust knows structurally. A FLAT --rust-derive serde,borsh header is INSUFFICIENT: the post-pass allow-lists are per-trait and granular -- ArcRwSignal is Debug+Serialize+Deserialize; RwSignal/ReadSignal/ArcReadSignal/ArcMemo/Memo are Debug+Serialize but NOT Deserialize (only ArcRwSignal is in the Deserialize list) and NOT borsh; WriteSignal/ArcWriteSignal/Url/SendWrapper/Arc are Debug ONLY; the nonClone list (Fragment/AnyView/View/RequestBuilder/Rexie/Box/Pin/MutexGuard/Command/Child*/File/JoinHandle) gets NO trait, not even Clone; borsh = base scalars only, no external is borsh-safe. So the header must declare, PER EXTERNAL TYPE, its trait SET.\n\nBUT structural knowledge shrinks the declaration a lot vs the post-pass: base scalars + std containers (Rc/Option/Vec/HashMap/BTreeMap/Result/RefCell/Arc), the regex's module-path artifacts (leptos/prelude/cell/collections/std/rc/sync/location/__reexports/send_wrapper), and the dyn/Fn (YFun -> Rc<dyn Fn> keeps Clone, drops the rest) and & denials are all judged structurally -- none need declaring. Residual to declare = just the genuinely-opaque emit/macro externals, 4 tiers:\n  ArcRwSignal = Clone,Debug,serde::Serialize,serde::Deserialize\n  RwSignal ReadSignal ArcReadSignal ArcMemo Memo = Clone,Debug,serde::Serialize\n  WriteSignal ArcWriteSignal Url SendWrapper Arc = Clone,Debug\n  Fragment AnyView View RequestBuilder Rexie Box Pin MutexGuard Command Child ChildStdin ChildStdout ChildStderr File JoinHandle = (none)\n\nRECOMMEND the header be a per-external-type trait-set decl (e.g. ///- --rust-extern <types> = <traits>), NOT a flat enable. Conservative by construction (your note-3 pt1): an UNDECLARED external keeps the trait out via the greatest fixpoint, so it can only under-derive (gate catches a missing derive), never cause a compile error. Impl lands by generalizing union_has_moving_field (spiral_compiler.fs:164954) + the derive(Clone) emit (:164927) into a per-trait greatest fixpoint shaped like rust_default_expr's ready-maps (:164855). One place still matches names like the post-pass: extracting type idents from an opaque macro-text field and looking them up in the header decl -- against dice's declared list, not a compiler builtin (your note-3 pt2). GATE-TIME RISK: the post-pass iterates regex-discovered types/fields; the compiler must compute over the same generated-type/field set or the derive sets diverge -- confirm that correspondence first. Full analysis: compiler scratchpad rust-derive-inference-header-resolution.md. Order unchanged: repro first, then this.")
inl note_of_rust_derive_inference_5 () = Note ("RUST-DERIVE-INFERENCE", 5u32, "2026-10-26T20:46Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-26 (build-independent: RESOLVES note 4's flagged GATE-TIME RISK \"confirm the generated-type/field correspondence first\", by reading dice/ui/rust_derive.ps1 in full). Three correspondence facts the codegenRust port must honor to hit the byte-for-byte gate:\n\n1. DERIVABLE-TYPE SET (what the post-pass actually touches):\n   - ALL `struct Heap\\d+` (line 14, no precondition) -> the port adds derives to every heap struct (fixpoint-filtered). codegenRust emits NO derive on heaps today, so this is pure addition. OK, clear.\n   - `enum U[SH]\\d+` ONLY when it ALREADY carries `#[derive(Clone)]` (line 17 regex requires the Clone line). The compiler's EXISTING Clone decision (union_has_moving_field 164954 -> derive(Clone) emit 164927) IS exactly that seed set, and the post-pass only UPGRADES already-Clone enums (adds Debug/serde/borsh). So the port keeps its current Clone decision as the seed and layers the other traits on top; a union WITH a moving field has no Clone line and the post-pass skips it (the port must too). Correspondence HOLDS by construction -- no divergence here.\n\n2. THE MutN POISON (the real divergence trap):\n   $isGenerated matches ^(Heap|US|UH|Mut)\\d+$ (line 25), BUT Mut layouts are NEVER added to $types/$ok (only Heap structs + already-Clone US/UH enums are). So any field that references a MutN hits line 38 `if (&$isGenerated $id){ if(!$ok.Contains($id)){ $good=$false; break } }` -> MutN is \"generated\" but not in $ok -> good=false -> the container DROPS that trait. This fires for EVERY trait INCLUDING Clone (the Clone special-case at line 35 only waives the `&` check; the isGenerated check at 38 still runs). Net: a heap/union holding a mutable-layout field gets NO derives at all.\n   => The port MUST treat mutable layouts as NON-derive-eligible and let a Mut field poison its container's derives, matching the post-pass's blanket exclusion. If the port instead computes MutN's REAL structural eligibility (a MutN whose fields are all Clone could look Clone-able), it ADDS a derive the post-pass omits -> byte-for-byte gate FAILS on any Mut-containing type. This is THE correspondence risk, now pinned: special-case Mut to drop, do not evaluate it structurally.\n\n3. IDENT-GRAPH vs TYPE-GRAPH equivalence (the rest): the post-pass extracts idents from field TEXT after stripping `l\\d+:` field-name prefixes and `U[SH]\\d+_` variant-ctor prefixes (line 22), then runs a greatest fixpoint per trait (remove-until-stable, lines 29-47) with: Clone waives `&`; non-Clone denies dyn/Fn/FnMut/FnOnce/impl (line 40); external idents checked against the per-trait allow-list (line 41). The compiler computes the SAME greatest fixpoint over TYPED layout fields instead of idents -- equivalent for generated types (structural) and for externals via the per-external-type trait-set header (note 4's 4 tiers). The ONLY asymmetry is Mut (point 2). Base scalars + std containers + module-path artifacts are judged structurally and need no declaration (note 4).\n\nCONSEQUENCE / where it lands (unchanged from note 4): generalize union_has_moving_field (164954) + derive(Clone) emit (164927) into a per-trait greatest fixpoint shaped like rust_default_expr's ready-maps (164855); SEED Clone from the existing union decision; FORCE-DROP on Mut-layout fields; drive external-type trait membership from dice's package header (4 tiers). Gate: dice main.rs == Add-RustDerives output byte-for-byte, wasm32 cargo check, then delete rust_derive.ps1 + its build.ps1 call. Build-bound to implement + verify; order unchanged (COMPILER-CROSS-MACHINE-REPRO first). Full prior analysis: rust-derive-inference-header-resolution.md.")
inl note_of_rust_derive_inference_6 () = Note ("RUST-DERIVE-INFERENCE", 6u32, "2026-10-27T09:32Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-27 build-independent hook-pin (read codegenRust). Current derive logic is a SINGLE-TRAIT heuristic, to be generalized to the per-trait greatest fixpoint (dice/ui/rust_derive.ps1 model):\n- union_type (spiral_compiler.fs:164925-164949) emits `#[derive(Clone)]` ONLY when `not (union_has_moving_field x)` (:164927). union_has_moving_field (:164954) = any case field type `moves`. So today: Clone on non-moving unions only; no Debug/serde/borsh; nothing on moving unions.\n- heap (Heap{tag}) and mut (Mut{tag}) structs (:164915-164924) get ONLY `impl Default` via rust_emit_default; no `#[derive]` at all.\n\nPORT (RUST-DERIVE-INFERENCE): a NEW pass over all generated types (heaps, muts, unions) computing, per trait T in {Clone, Debug, serde::Serialize, serde::Deserialize, borsh}, the GREATEST FIXPOINT of \"T-derivable\" types: start all-in, remove any type with a reachable field that can't derive T, iterate to fixpoint. Then at the two emit sites (:164927 for unions, :164915/:164921 for heap/mut) emit `#[derive(<the traits this type qualifies for>)]` instead of the Clone-only line / nothing.\n\nCRITICAL (MutN POISON, the trap the runbook flagged): a Mut layout is UNCONDITIONALLY non-derivable for every trait -- force-drop it from all trait sets, do NOT evaluate it structurally. A type reaching a Mut field drops ALL its container derives. If the fixpoint instead treats Mut like a normal struct (\"derivable if its fields are\"), the byte-for-byte gate diverges (a Mut-reaching container would wrongly gain derives). So the fixpoint's seed/step must special-case YLayout(_,HeapMutable) as always-poison.\n\nExternal types: the derive set for a package-external type comes from a dice package-header per-external-type trait-set decl (4 tiers), per the rust_derive.ps1 design. Keep union_has_moving_field (or its `moves`-based result) as the Clone-specific move-safety input, distinct from the per-trait derivability. Build-bound to implement + the byte-for-byte -Suite all gate; main sequenced this AFTER COMPILER-CROSS-MACHINE-REPRO.")
inl note_of_rust_derive_inference_7 () = Note ("RUST-DERIVE-INFERENCE", 7u32, "2026-10-28T00:36Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-27: grounded impl. Template = rust_default_expr (:164855) field-type walk + layout_default_ready/union_default_ready memo; Default emitted at heap :164914/mut :164921/union_type :164947; Clone today = fixed :164927 (not union_has_moving_field -> derive Clone). PLAN: per-trait layout_keeps_trait memo like default_ready; type keeps T iff every generated field keeps T AND every opaque external is header-declared for T; replace :164927, emit #[derive(<eligible>)] at the 3 sites. HEADER: ///- --rust-derive-extern Name[,Name]=Trait[,Trait] (empty=no trait); ///- --rust-derive serde,borsh opts crate in. OPEN 1st: how an opaque external appears in Ty in rust codegen (the header-name match target).")
inl note_of_rust_derive_inference_8 () = Note ("RUST-DERIVE-INFERENCE", 8u32, "2026-10-28T00:38Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-27: OPEN resolved. Opaque externals = YMacro in Ty (rust tyv :164456: YMacro a -> parts concatenated, Text=name, Type=recursive tyv; backend_switch :164452 -> Rust arm). Fixpoint rule for a field YMacro a: keeps T iff HEAD identifier (leading Text, e.g. Fragment/RwSignal) is header-declared for T AND every Type arg in a keeps T (recurse); backend_switch recurses the Rust arm; undeclared head -> no T (conservative). Matches post-pass (checked wrapper name + recursed inner generated types). Design complete; impl after CCMR land.")
inl note_of_rust_derive_inference_9 () = Note ("RUST-DERIVE-INFERENCE", 9u32, "2026-10-28T00:39Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-27: correction to n7 header syntax. ///- does NOT exist in spiral_compiler.fs (0 hits) -- it was note-shorthand, not a real directive. So the per-crate opt-in (which generated types consider serde/borsh + the external trait-set declarations) needs a CHANNEL decision at impl: compiler CLI flag passed by the build script (like --native), a spiproj/manifest field, or a new source directive. Propose: opt-in traits via CLI flag (--rust-derive serde,borsh); the verbose 4-tier external declarations via a manifest/sidecar rather than CLI. Confirm the channel with main at impl start. Fixpoint design itself (n7/n8) stands regardless of channel.")
inl note_of_rust_derive_inference_10 () = Note ("RUST-DERIVE-INFERENCE", 10u32, "2026-10-28T01:21Z", "main", "10-27 21:21 main: Channel decision (main, technical call): ONE channel, the crate's package.spiproj. A rustDerive field lists the opt-in traits (e.g. serde, borsh), and the external trait-set declarations (which opaque heads keep which trait) live in the same file, so no CLI flag and no sidecar. Why: derives are a per-crate fact, and the compiler already reads package.spiproj for every build, so the same input applies whether Cells, kit, CI or a direct --backend call starts the build. A CLI flag would have to be copied into every build script (dice's build.ps1, kit's cargo flows, Cells cells), against PWSH-FREE and the one-entry rule. Splitting traits into a flag and declarations into a sidecar would put one fact in two places. Default when the field is absent: Clone and Debug only (today's codegen), so crates that don't opt in are byte-identical, which is the impl's first gate. The second gate is dice: its Add-RustDerives post-pass becomes a no-op on the opted-in build (main.rs equal with and without the pass), then the pass is deleted.")
inl note_of_rust_derive_inference_11 () = Note ("RUST-DERIVE-INFERENCE", 11u32, "2026-10-28T01:23Z", "compiler", "main 21:3x DECIDED the opt-in channel (resolves n9): ONE channel = the crate package.spiproj. A rustDerive field lists opt-in traits (serde,borsh); the external trait-set decls (ArcRwSignal=..., the 4 tiers) go in the SAME file. No CLI flag, no sidecar. Gate 1: field ABSENT -> codegen stays Clone/Debug, byte-identical. Gate 2: dice opts in -> Add-RustDerives is a no-op, then delete the pass. Design 100% complete (fixpoint n7/n8 + channel); impl build-bound, own pack after CCMR.")
inl note_of_rust_derive_inference_12 () = Note ("RUST-DERIVE-INFERENCE", 12u32, "2026-10-28T14:26Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-28 groom: estimate set 240m (fixpoint over field types + per-crate opt-in, replaces dice Add-RustDerives regex). No deps; BLOCKER = a heavy/build slot -- it is an active codegen feature ready to implement, gated only on compiler build cycles (box+node saturated today).")
inl note_of_rust_derive_inference_13 () = Note ("RUST-DERIVE-INFERENCE", 13u32, "2026-10-28T14:30Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-28 estimate 240m FROM MILESTONES: fixpoint over field types (derivability of Clone/Debug/serde/borsh per trait) 120m; per-crate opt-in 30m; emit the derives + remove dice Add-RustDerives regex post-pass 60m; validate dice + examples byte-parity 30m. Sum = 240m. (supersedes the earlier difficulty-feel estimate)")
inl note_of_rust_derive_inference_14 () = Note ("RUST-DERIVE-INFERENCE", 14u32, "2026-10-29T12:43Z", "compiler", "10-29 08:43 compiler: two implementation facts read from the live core before drafting (line numbers today). (1) TRAP: import_rust_unions (spiral_compiler.fs:165164-165170) drops an imported union's local copy with the regex ^#\\[derive\\(Clone\\)\\]\\r?\\n(?=(pub )?enum <name> ...). Once derive lists grow (#[derive(Clone, Debug, ...)]), that regex stops matching: the local derive line stays and sits on top of the next item, or the enum is defined twice beside the use. The port must widen it to ^#\\[derive\\([^\\]]*\\)\\] in the same edit. (2) PLUMBING: codegenRust has NO option channel today (no environment reads, no package fields between 163950 and 165300). n1's per-crate opt-in ('///- --rust-derive serde,borsh') is a Cells header, which the compiler never sees. Options: (a) a package.spiproj field read by the package loader and carried to codegenRust, or (b) an environment variable SPIRAL_RUST_DERIVE=serde,borsh set by the consumer's build. DECISION (mine, technical): (b) first. dice/ui/build.ps1 already wraps the compile, so the opt-in sits where Add-RustDerives is called today. It is one GetEnvironmentVariable read beside the existing Clone decision (:164998), it changes no package format, and (a) can replace it once a second consumer needs it. Clone/Debug stay always-on per n1, so every Rust sample's residual gains Debug derives: the pack's review must expect a Rust-only output diff of exactly derive lines (check with git diff samples -- '*.rs' showing only #[derive] changes).")
inl note_of_rust_derive_inference_15 () = Note ("RUST-DERIVE-INFERENCE", 15u32, "2026-10-29T12:44Z", "compiler", "10-29 08:44 compiler: CORRECTION to n14 (2), the plumbing decision. A flat SPIRAL_RUST_DERIVE=serde,borsh contradicts n3/n4, which I had not re-read: main's refined spec declares a trait SET per external type (4 tiers: ArcRwSignal = Clone,Debug,Serialize,Deserialize; RwSignal/ReadSignal/ArcReadSignal/ArcMemo/Memo = Clone,Debug,Serialize; WriteSignal/ArcWriteSignal/Url/SendWrapper/Arc = Clone,Debug; Fragment/AnyView/View/RequestBuilder/Rexie/Box/Pin/MutexGuard/Command/Child*/File/JoinHandle = none), declared by dice's package, never a compiler builtin, conservative by the greatest fixpoint. So the channel must carry type -> traits. The env-var-versus-package.spiproj question stays open between main and the compiler lane: both can carry that map, but n3 says 'package header', which favours a spiproj field. n14 (1), the import_rust_unions derive-regex trap, stands. NEW (architecture, from reading the emission code): types are emitted incrementally (layout/union memoize on first use), and a recursive pair such as Heap5 <-> UH3 cannot decide derives at emission time. Plan: emit one placeholder line per type, compute the per-trait greatest fixpoint over the memoized records after codegen, and substitute each placeholder with its #[derive(...)] or nothing. This mirrors how default_impls/union_tag_impls are collected and placed late; the Clone seed stays union_has_moving_field.")
inl note_of_rust_derive_inference_16 () = Note ("RUST-DERIVE-INFERENCE", 16u32, "2026-10-29T12:46Z", "compiler", "10-29 08:46 compiler: channel DECIDED (technical, settles n15's open question in n3's 'package header' sense): a package.spiproj field, rust-extern:, with one indented line per external type, '<Type> = <Trait>, <Trait>' (e.g. 'ArcRwSignal = Clone, Debug, serde::Serialize, serde::Deserialize'; a type with no traits is listed with an empty right side). Precedent and path, all read: (a) the field list config/fields (spiral_compiler.fs:179737-179748) already holds package options default-int, default-float and edition; crates is parsed and discarded (fun _ s -> s), so there is no Rust config channel to extend. (b) package_default_env (:184297) turns schema fields into DefaultEnv. DefaultEnv (:7247) is built at only 2 sites (:7301, :180077), so adding rust_extern : Map<string, Set<string>> is cheap. (c) DefaultEnv -> codegenRust needs a PartEvalResult field (2 definitions: :128734 gpt5parallel side, :159670 single-flight side, so one pair edit) rather than a mutable global, because compile requests can run concurrently in the server. Estimate unchanged (240m): field + schema + DefaultEnv + PartEvalResult plumbing 60m; placeholder + per-trait greatest fixpoint 90m; import_rust_unions regex widening + dice header port of the 4 tiers 30m; gate (dice main.rs equals Add-RustDerives output, wasm32 cargo check, then delete rust_derive.ps1) 60m.")
inl note_of_rust_derive_inference_17 () = Note ("RUST-DERIVE-INFERENCE", 17u32, "2026-10-29T12:51Z", "compiler", "10-29 08:51 compiler: PLUMBING MAPPED (Explore agent, spot-checked 4 of its cites). No signature changes above codegen: both drivers close over the supervisor state s, and s.packages : SchemaEnv = Map<SchemaPathId, SchemaState> (:184232) holds every loaded package's schema. Single-flight driver (file_build s pdir mid ..., :189166) has pdir in scope: Map.tryFind pdir s.packages (as :189429 already does). The gpt5parallel driver (:186489) has no pdir: reuse file_default_env's walk-up (:184320) to find the file's SchemaState. codegenRust (:164266, shared) gains one leading parameter, called as build (codegenRust externs) 'Rust' '.rs' at :186654 and :189357. A per-compile mutable global or ThreadStatic is unsafe: builds overlap (single-flight runs body() on its own 1.5 GB thread, :189403; gpt5parallel bodyOffWorker + codegenOnLargeStack, :188451/:185358). OUTPUT FORMAT the gate needs, read from dice/ui/rust_derive.ps1: trait order Clone, Debug, serde::Serialize, serde::Deserialize, borsh::BorshSerialize, borsh::BorshDeserialize. A struct gets a #[derive(...)] line only when the list is non-empty. An enum is a candidate only if already #[derive(Clone)] and is rewritten only when its list has >1 trait: an enum whose fixpoint DROPS Clone keeps its original #[derive(Clone)] line (quirk to reproduce for byte equality, or declare). Mut types are generated but never ok, so they poison every container for every trait. The '&' check skips Clone; dyn/Fn/FnMut/FnOnce/impl deny every non-Clone trait; nonClone externals deny Clone.")
inl note_of_rust_derive_inference_18 () = Note ("RUST-DERIVE-INFERENCE", 18u32, "2026-10-29T13:02Z", "compiler", "10-29 09:02 compiler: IMPLEMENTATION DRAFTED: apps/compiler/packs/apply-rust-derive-inference.ps1, 15 anchors, each matching exactly once on the live core. (1) package.spiproj field rust-extern: (RawSchema/Schema/schema_def/ss_empty/construction + rust_extern_lines parser, one indented '<Type> = <Trait>, ...' per line). (2) file_rust_extern (same walk-up as file_default_env), passed at both drivers as build (codegenRust (file_rust_extern s.packages file)). (3) codegenRust takes it first. A reserved entry 'derive = <traits in output order>' turns inference on; WITHOUT it nothing changes, so every existing Rust output stays byte-identical (a deliberate deviation from n1's always-on Clone/Debug: no residual churn for consumers that never asked; reversible). (4) heap/union emission writes a placeholder line plus the field type texts (tyv). The union Clone seed (union_has_moving_field) is unchanged; resolve_derives runs first in the output pipeline: per-trait greatest fixpoint ported from rust_derive.ps1 over field type text. Generated names (Heap/US/UH/Mut N) are judged by the ok-set (Mut never ok). Clone is denied only by a declared extern without Clone. dyn/Fn/FnMut/FnOnce/impl deny non-Clone traits. '&' denies non-Clone traits. Other identifiers come from a std table (scalars, String, str, Rc, Option, Vec, HashMap, BTreeMap, Result = every declared trait; RefCell = Clone, Debug, serde; Arc = Clone, Debug) or the declared externs; path qualifiers (ident::) are skipped. Output follows the post-pass byte rules, including the enum keep-Clone-line quirk. (5) import_rust_unions regex widened (n14 trap). Composes with the other pack-7 appliers; pair-drift 0 one-sided; hopacsota lint clean. Type-check build running on the node (job-1793278928455-compiler-pack7-typecheck, mirror pack7check; not install-grade, node SDK rc.1). dice's header for the gate = the 4 tiers of n4 + 'derive = Clone, Debug, serde::Serialize, serde::Deserialize, borsh::BorshSerialize, borsh::BorshDeserialize'.")
inl note_of_rust_derive_inference_19 () = Note ("RUST-DERIVE-INFERENCE", 19u32, "2026-10-29T13:21Z", "compiler", "10-29 09:21 compiler: FUNCTIONAL PROOF on the node type-check candidate (pack7check, all pack-7 appliers incl. this one). Test package: heap{Rc<str>}, heap{US0 of i32}, heap{Rc<dyn Fn>}, rust-extern 'derive = Clone, Debug, serde::Serialize'. Output: Heap0, US0 and Heap1 get #[derive(Clone, Debug, serde::Serialize)], Heap2 (dyn Fn) gets #[derive(Clone)] only (exactly the predicted fixpoint, including the US0 -> Heap1 propagation). WITHOUT rust-extern the output is byte-identical to the installed compiler's (sha 16 A5A51594DC81949F both), so default-off holds. Compile errors 0. Left for the gate: the dice header port and its byte-equality against Add-RustDerives, plus wasm32 cargo check (both after the pack-7 install).")
inl note_of_rust_derive_inference_20 () = Note ("RUST-DERIVE-INFERENCE", 20u32, "2026-10-29T13:39Z", "compiler", "10-29 09:4x compiler: dice HEADER DRAFT for the gate (NOT landed: the installed compiler rejects the unknown rust-extern field, so it lands only after the pack-7 install, in the same change that deletes the post-pass). dice/ui/package.spiproj gains, after modules:\nrust-extern:\n    derive = Clone, Debug, serde::Serialize, serde::Deserialize, borsh::BorshSerialize, borsh::BorshDeserialize\n    ArcRwSignal = Clone, Debug, serde::Serialize, serde::Deserialize\n    RwSignal = Clone, Debug, serde::Serialize\n    ReadSignal = Clone, Debug, serde::Serialize\n    ArcReadSignal = Clone, Debug, serde::Serialize\n    ArcMemo = Clone, Debug, serde::Serialize\n    Memo = Clone, Debug, serde::Serialize\n    WriteSignal = Clone, Debug\n    ArcWriteSignal = Clone, Debug\n    Url = Clone, Debug\n    SendWrapper = Clone, Debug\n    Fragment =\n    AnyView =\n    View =\n    RequestBuilder =\n    Rexie =\n    Box =\n    Pin =\n    MutexGuard =\n    Command =\n    Child =\n    ChildStdin =\n    ChildStdout =\n    ChildStderr =\n    File =\n    JoinHandle =\nMapping from rust_derive.ps1 (read in full): the derive line is its trait order ('Clone' + the $allow keys). Each extern's set is the set of $allow lists that name it, plus Clone unless it is in $nonClone. The nonClone entries get an empty set (so no trait at all, as in the post-pass). RefCell and Arc come from the compiler's std table (RefCell = Clone, Debug, serde; Arc = Clone, Debug, which matches the post-pass lists). The path segments the post-pass lists (leptos, prelude, cell, leptos_router, location, __reexports, send_wrapper, sync, std, rc, string, collections) need no entry, because the port skips identifiers followed by ::. LAND ORDER (after the pack-7 install): add this header; delete dice/ui/rust_derive.ps1 and build.ps1 lines 19 and 21 (keep line 21's 'pub mod model;' append); mirror the change in alphabet if it carries the same post-pass (alphabet-mirrors-dice); gate = dice main.rs from the compiler equals the old Add-RustDerives output byte for byte, then a wasm32 cargo check.")
inl note_of_rust_derive_inference_21 () = Note ("RUST-DERIVE-INFERENCE", 21u32, "2026-10-30T03:55Z", "compiler", "10-29 23:5x compiler: THE GATE PASSED on the installed pack 7 (node job-1793331582649-compiler-derive-gate, dice mirror 'derivegate' at head 7bf173b8, lib = tonight's box tree via the pack8 mirror, the same for both arms). GATE 1: A = installed pack 7 + Add-RustDerives + the 'pub mod model;' append, B = pack 7 with n20's rust-extern header in dice/ui/package.spiproj and NO post-pass. Both are C412EE369E53CD78, 1,839,011 B: BYTE-IDENTICAL. GATE 2: cargo check -p dice_ui --bin dice_ui_bin --target wasm32-unknown-unknown on B: exit 0. LAND HELD on one external fact: pack 7 is installed locally but the user hasn't pushed it, and dice CI builds against pushed spiral main, whose compiler rejects the unknown rust-extern field. Landing now would break dice CI. READY: C:\\home\\data\\derive-land\\land-dice-derive.ps1 (dry run ok on the live dice; -Apply keeps undo copies and MOVES rust_derive.ps1 into the undo folder, archive-never-delete; -Undo restores). After -Apply: dice/ui build.ps1 -fast must leave src/main.rs byte-identical to the committed one. Alphabet: no rust_derive/Add-RustDerives anywhere under C:\\home\\git (grep), so there is nothing to mirror.")
inl note_of_rust_derive_inference_22 () = Note ("RUST-DERIVE-INFERENCE", 22u32, "2026-10-30T15:16Z", "compiler", "10-30 11:16 compiler, BACKLOG-DANGLING-1030 R1: the only remaining step waits on the user's push of spiral main with pack 7 (dice CI compiles against pushed main, whose compiler rejects rust-extern). Both gates passed 10-29 (n21), and land-dice-derive.ps1 is ready (dry-run ok). So assignee -> user, tag user-batch; grooms do not re-verify. On the push: any compiler-team agent runs C:\\home\\data\\derive-land\\land-dice-derive.ps1 -Apply, checks that dice/ui build.ps1 -fast leaves src/main.rs byte-identical, and closes the item.")
inl note_of_rust_derive_inference_23 () = Note ("RUST-DERIVE-INFERENCE", 23u32, "2026-11-01T03:38Z", "compiler", "10-31 10-31 23:38 compiler, cadence groom: Active -> BLOCKED. Reason: dice CI compiles against pushed spiral main, whose compiler predates pack 7 (rust-extern). Both gates passed 10-29; land-dice-derive is ready. Resume on: the user's push of spiral main with pack 7+ (now pack 11).")
inl spiral_check_prints_errors () = Task ("SPIRAL-CHECK-PRINTS-ERRORS", "compiler host: --check prints the type errors behind 'Package typecheck rejected' (file:line:col and message, like the compile path's TypeErrors), not just the verdict", Story, P2, D3, 100u32, Done, "PACK-7", "compiler,host,check,diagnostics,x", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_spiral_check_prints_errors () = Planning ("SPIRAL-CHECK-PRINTS-ERRORS", Minutes 90u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_spiral_check_prints_errors_1 () = Note ("SPIRAL-CHECK-PRINTS-ERRORS", 1u32, "2026-10-25T23:41Z", "x", "2026-10-25 19:41 -04:00 x: 2026-10-25 17:5x. Seen in X-FORMAT-CLIENT's hand splits: `SpiralCompiler.dll --check file.spi` failed with only 'Package typecheck rejected: <dir>' (Program.fs:737), so finding one wrong type (ghost_error : json instead of string) took a per-edit bisect, 5 extra checks. The --check path reads `package.result` (a bool) from the direct project state. The compile path gets TypeErrors messages from the server stream (Program.fs:136-146, 196). Fix shape: when hasError, walk package.files.uids_file and print each file's type errors in the compile path's format, the first N, sorted by file and line; the same for the direct-project path at Program.fs:364 ('Direct project typecheck rejected'). Gate: a sample package with one deliberate type error reports its file:line and message, and a clean package still prints 'checked package'.")
inl note_of_spiral_check_prints_errors_2 () = Note ("SPIRAL-CHECK-PRINTS-ERRORS", 2u32, "2026-10-26T04:21Z", "compiler", "compiler lane: confirmed + refined x's fix shape (CPU-light; impl + gate sample need a build).\nCompile-path error format: diagnosticFor (Program.fs:135-143) renders a TypeErrors message as \"TypeErrors: %A{x.errors}\" (raw F# structural dump of the error list, which carries each error's range); diagnosticForOther (145-149) is the same with the uri prefixed. That is the \"compile path format\" the item means, but it is a raw %A dump, not file:line:col.\n--check path prints only the verdict and DISCARDS the error carrier: Program.fs:737 `if hasError then failwith \"Package typecheck rejected: {packageDir}\"` where `let hasError, _ = Hopac.run package.result` throws away the second tuple element; same at :364 (\"Direct project typecheck rejected {inputPath}\") where `let hasStateError, _ = Hopac.run typeState.state` discards it, with the per-module state read at :361 `prepassPackage.files.uids_file.[mid]`.\nFix: at both :364 and :737, instead of discarding, get each file's type errors (x's path: walk package.files.uids_file; the discarded `_` of package.result/typeState.state is the likely carrier -- confirm when implementing) and print them in the compile path's shape, the first N sorted by file then line. Recommendation: format as file:line:col + message (map the error record's range) rather than the compile path's raw %A, matching the item's \"file:line:col and message\" ask, and ideally through one shared formatter so --check and the compile path agree.\nGate (x's, needs a build): a sample package with one deliberate type error reports its file:line and message under --check; a clean package still prints \"checked package %s\". Est 90m holds.")
inl note_of_spiral_check_prints_errors_3 () = Note ("SPIRAL-CHECK-PRINTS-ERRORS", 3u32, "2026-10-26T05:08Z", "compiler", "compiler lane: file:line:col format fully spec'd (box-only; impl + gate still need a build). The error element is RString = VSCRange * string, where VSCRange = VSCPos * VSCPos and VSCPos = {| line:int; character:int |} (spiral_compiler.fs:42766-42771). So TypeErrors/TokenizerErrors/ParserErrors carry errors : RString list, each = ((start, end), message).\nThe item's \"file:line:col and message\" rendering is, per RString ((s, _end), msg): $\"{file}:{s.line + 1}:{s.character + 1}: {msg}\" (1-based line/col, matching how a compiler report reads; the compile path currently dumps raw %A{x.errors} via diagnosticFor, Program.fs:135-143 -- this is the cleaner form the item wants).\nImplementation: at Program.fs:737 (Package) and :364 (Direct project), instead of the bare `failwith \"... typecheck rejected\"`, obtain each file's RString list (x's path: walk package.files.uids_file per module; the discarded second element of `Hopac.run package.result` at :736 / `typeState.state` at :362 is the likely carrier -- confirm its type when implementing), sort by (file, line, character), print the first N in the format above, then exit non-zero. `file` is the TypeErrors uri or the per-module path from uids_file.\nRemaining impl detail (needs the core open + a build): the exact accessor for the per-module RString list in the type state. Gate unchanged (x's): a package with one deliberate type error reports file:line + message; a clean package still prints \"checked package\".")
inl note_of_spiral_check_prints_errors_4 () = Note ("SPIRAL-CHECK-PRINTS-ERRORS", 4u32, "2026-10-26T10:21Z", "x", "2026-10-26 06:21 x: DRAFTED as an anchored host patch (%TEMP%\\check-prints-errors\\apply.ps1 -Program <copy>, +25/-2 on today's tmp/compiler/host/Program.fs, applies once; not built). Correction to note 2: package.result's second element is PackageEnv (TypecheckerStateTop = (bool * PackageEnv) Promise), not the errors. The errors are per module, as the attention server streams them: uids_file.[mid] = (TypecheckerState, _), with parser errors in state.input's BlockBundleState stream ((_, x) -> x.errors) and type errors in the state.result stream ((_, InferResult, _) -> .errors). The patch adds a private typeErrorLines (the files tree mapped to drained parse + type errors, sorted by path/line/char, first 20, 'path:line:col: message', 1-based) at the start of the first single-flight #else, so the gpt5parallel build never sees it. runCheck prints the lines before 'Package typecheck rejected', and directProjectCompileFsharp's Error carries them. Handed to the compiler lane with the build-time assumptions and the item's gate (x-zwsp-samples\\zwsp_error_column -> main.spi:3:15).")
inl note_of_spiral_check_prints_errors_5 () = Note ("SPIRAL-CHECK-PRINTS-ERRORS", 5u32, "2026-10-26T10:25Z", "x", "2026-10-26 06:25 x: the compiler lane's own host patch (%TEMP%\\pack-next5n\\host-check-prints-apply.ps1) was built on the node and is IN 5n: --check prints 'main.spi:2:5: ETY-TermError: Unification failure. Got: i32 Expected: string' before the generic line (exit 5), and main wired its type_error_probe sample into install-5n.ps1's gate. It walks package.files.files.tree -> uids_file.[mid] -> InferResult.errors. x's draft (note 4) is the superset for the pack after 5n: parse errors from the BlockBundleState stream, sorting plus the first 20, and the directProjectCompileFsharp path; its gate is x-zwsp-samples\\zwsp_error_column -> :3:15 and 'checked package' on a clean package. Done for this item once 5n installs and the --check probe prints file:line.")
inl note_of_spiral_check_prints_errors_6 () = Note ("SPIRAL-CHECK-PRINTS-ERRORS", 6u32, "2026-10-27T01:00Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-26 build-independent tree-state check: the pack-5n fix is NOT yet in the live tree -- confirmed it is a PENDING draft (build-bound to land), so 80% = draft-ready. Fix locus pinned at apps/compiler/tmp/compiler/host/Program.fs: the --check path (:1241-1243 -> runCheck -> runCheck's package check) still has `let hasError, _ = Hopac.run package.result` (:736) which DISCARDS the second tuple element (the diagnostics) into `_`, then `failwith $\"Package typecheck rejected: {packageDir}\"` (:737) prints only the generic rejection. The data already exists (package.result yields (hasError, diagnostics)); the fix is to capture the discarded element and format it file:line:col behind the rejection -- same shape as CONTRACT-REJECTION-DIAGNOSTICS (a reason that is computed but not forwarded). Tree-unfixed confirmed => no double-apply risk when 5n builds. Remaining = pack 5n build + accept.")
inl note_of_spiral_check_prints_errors_7 () = Note ("SPIRAL-CHECK-PRINTS-ERRORS", 7u32, "2026-10-27T08:13Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-27 CORRECTION to n6 (read the core, primary source). n6 was WRONG that package.result yields (hasError, diagnostics) and that the fix is to capture the discarded `_` at host Program.fs:736. Trace: the host --check path (Program.fs:715-737) gets package = state.packages_infer.ok.[pid], and package.result is produced by typechecker_results_summary (spiral_compiler.fs:178930-178936). That fold reduces the per-file InferResult stream to (has_error, env): it ORs `List.isEmpty x.errors = false` into has_error and then DISCARDS the error lists, keeping only the accumulated top_env. So package.result = (hasError: bool, env), and the `_` at :736 is ENV, not diagnostics -- capturing it prints nothing useful.\n\nThe real diagnostics are InferResult.errors : RString list (InferResult at spiral_compiler.fs:50426, errors field :50431). RString = VSCRange * string (:42765); VSCRange = VSCPos * VSCPos (:42762), each VSCPos carrying line + character. So every type error already carries its file:line:col range -- nothing needs to be recomputed, only PROPAGATED.\n\nREAL FIX (a CORE change, not host-only):\n(1) In the core, stop discarding errors: either add a sibling of typechecker_results_summary that folds the stream keeping the concatenated per-file errors (the existing shape to emit is LocalizedErrors = {|uri : string; errors : RString list|} at :184657), or extend the summary to return (has_error, env, errors). The uri/file association comes from the proj_file funs' `name` (funs_proj_file_tc at :178940), same place has_error is computed.\n(2) In the host --check path (Program.fs:736-737), replace `let hasError, _ = ... if hasError then failwith \"Package typecheck rejected: {packageDir}\"` with: on hasError, format each (range, msg) as `{uri}:{range start line+1}:{range start char+1}: {msg}` (1-based, editor-style) and print them behind the rejection line.\n\nConsequence: this is a core summary change + a host format, touching spiral_compiler.fs (a shared, both-cores section -- typechecker_results_summary is shared) plus Program.fs, so it is NOT the host-only capture n6 implied; 80% is optimistic for the real shape (call it ~60%). The LSP DiagnosticRouter/diagnosticFor (host:135) renders TypeErrors via %A over the ClientErrorsRes channel during a BUILD -- a separate path from proj_open's --check -- so it can't be reused as-is, though its `{uri}: %A{errors}` shape is the model for the host format in (2). Pairs with CONTRACT-REJECTION-DIAGNOSTICS (same \"a computed reason is not forwarded\" family). Still build-bound to verify, but the draft target is now exact.")
inl note_of_spiral_check_prints_errors_8 () = Note ("SPIRAL-CHECK-PRINTS-ERRORS", 8u32, "2026-10-29T14:37Z", "compiler", "10-29 10:37 compiler: FALSE GREEN found while closing the wipe redo row. On the installed host (A4E480202BD40D80), --check prints type errors as path:line:col (5n's availableErrors), but a PARSE error passes: a file containing 'inl x = = = 3' gives 'checked package ...' and exit 0, while --backend C on the same file reports ParserErrors (exit 5). Cause: the --check path (host Program.fs:736-762, single-flight #else) decides on package.result's hasError, which only the typer sets; parser errors live in each file's TypecheckerState.input BlockBundleState stream (BlockBundleValue.errors) and are never read. FIX (x's n4 superset, redone after the wipe): apps/compiler/packs/apply-check-parse-errors.ps1 -HostSource <Program.fs>, 1 anchor. It hoists files/availableErrors, adds availableParseErrors over every file of the checked package, fails when hasError OR parse errors exist, and prints both as path:line:col. The rejection message is unchanged so consumers' matches keep working. Composes with apply-rust-emit-unit-arg and apply-hopacsota-d4-restart-host on a scratch host. Gate (build-bound, next pack): the probe above must exit 5 with main.spi:2:<col>; a clean package still prints 'checked package'; x's zwsp_error_column still -> :3:15.")
inl note_of_spiral_check_prints_errors_9 () = Note ("SPIRAL-CHECK-PRINTS-ERRORS", 9u32, "2026-10-29T14:40Z", "main", "10-29 10:4x main: cross-lane impact of the --check parse-blindness (SPIRAL-CHECK-PRINTS-ERRORS n8). fiscal: unaffected; every land and gate is a Cells build plus run, and yesterday's --check-only XSD-SIGNING-STAGES design shapes are re-proven by today's fiscal-m3-probe Rust build. kit: unaffected; its only --check gate (compiler-contracts/test-attestation.ps1) tests a TYPE error and an undeclared module, and trains compile for real. The gap was that nothing pinned 'a parse error fails --check'. kit drafted C:\\home\\data\\kit-drafts\\attestation-parse-error\\attestation-parse-error.do (a parse-error fixture expecting failure). It is red on today's host by design, so it lands IN the pack-7 install window as that pack's kit-side proof. Pack-7 install checklist: apply-check-parse-errors.ps1, apply-char-nul-escape.ps1 plus the per-backend NUL escape, then kit applies attestation-parse-error.do in the same window.")
inl note_of_spiral_check_prints_errors_10 () = Note ("SPIRAL-CHECK-PRINTS-ERRORS", 10u32, "2026-10-29T20:56Z", "compiler", "10-29 16:5x compiler: the fix ships in PACK-7 (apply-check-parse-errors.ps1, host). Gate rows proven on the pack-7 build (node job-1793293192702, host 3AB27C9D378C3D72; the box candidate has the same host source 1433C906): (1) a package whose main.spi has a parse error: --check exits 5 and prints 'main.spi:2:13: Failed to parse this token.' (installed 6b: exit 0 'checked package', the false green); (2) a clean package: exit 0 'checked package'. Gate row (3), x's zwsp_error_column -> :3:15, is NOT run: the fixture folder x-zwsp-samples is not on disk (box C:\\home\\git and C:\\home\\data searched; likely a pre-wipe scratchpad). Asked x for it; with it, the row runs in a minute against the pack-7 candidate. kit's attestation-parse-error.do lands in pack 7's install window as before.")
inl note_of_spiral_check_prints_errors_11 () = Note ("SPIRAL-CHECK-PRINTS-ERRORS", 11u32, "2026-10-29T21:06Z", "compiler", "10-29 17:1x compiler: GATE ROW 3 PROVEN (with a corrected fixture), so all three rows pass on pack 7. x reconstructed zwsp_error_column (C:\\home\\data\\x-evidence\\zwsp_error_column; the original was lost with a pre-wipe scratchpad). As reconstructed ('inl a = 1i32' ... line 3 'inl c = 1i32 <U+200B>)'), pack 7 exits 5 but on 'Global inl/let statements should all return functions known at parse time' at 1:1/2:1/3:1: edition 3 rejects non-function top-level inl first, so the stray ')' is never reached and neither :3:15 nor :3:14 appears. Corrected minimally (each line a function: 'inl c () = 1i32 <U+200B>)'; scratch copy, x's folder untouched): ZWSP at col 17, stray ')' at col 18. Pack 7 box candidate: exit 5, 'main.spi:3:18: Failed to parse this token.', so the column COUNTS the ZWSP (positions remapped to the original text, SPIRAL-ZWSP-TRANSPARENT's promise; an unremapped strip would give :3:17). Installed 6b: exit 0 'checked package' (the false green this item fixes). Rows 1-2 as in n10. Item completes when pack 7 installs.")
inl note_of_spiral_check_prints_errors_12 () = Note ("SPIRAL-CHECK-PRINTS-ERRORS", 12u32, "2026-10-30T03:18Z", "compiler", "10-29 23:3x compiler: CLOSED, verified live on the installed pack 7 host F2F6F4CB5A18626E: --check on a package whose main.spi has an unclosed paren prints 'main.spi:3:5: Unexpected end of block past this token.' plus 'Package typecheck rejected: <dir>' and exits 5; the same check on a valid package prints 'checked package <dir>' and exits 0. Before pack 7 a parse error exited 0 (memory check-attestation-cap).")
inl lib_iggy_client () = Task ("LIB-IGGY-CLIENT", "lib/spiral iggy client and typed message model for every project: one Spiral GADT family (job, lease, slot, step, health and toolchain-inventory events) with byte-identical Rust and Gleam codecs; Rust over the iggy crate (TCP 8090), Gleam over iggy HTTP (3000); lib test blocks on both backends; consumers are kit's remote executor, the compiler matrix shards, lib gates and lane watchers (IGGY-MULTI-MACHINE note 11, lessons L9-L15)", Story, P0, D5, 75u32, Planned, "IGGY-MULTI-MACHINE", "lib,iggy,remote,gleam,rust,typefeatureset,eoie-team,kit,team:kit", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_lib_iggy_client () = Planning ("LIB-IGGY-CLIENT", Minutes 480u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_lib_iggy_client_1 () = Note ("LIB-IGGY-CLIENT", 1u32, "2026-10-26T05:14Z", "eoie", "Created 10-26 after the user asked to prioritize iggy across every project and lib. Starts now: lib/spiral is outside the eoie feature freeze. Milestones, pre-registered: M1 120m the GADT message family with a round-trip test on rust and gleam (byte-identical encodings asserted against one fixture); M2 120m Rust producer and consumer against the node's iggy over the ssh -L tunnel (10,000 messages, 0 missing, ordered, like the fork's verify.ps1); M3 120m Gleam HTTP consumer with token persistence; M4 120m a job-event watcher replacing one ssh-polling watcher (L12) end to end. Rule: done when M4 replaces a real watcher and its events match the job's own receipt. Risk carried from note 7: size iggy memory_pool below the pod limit before any load test.")
inl note_of_lib_iggy_client_2 () = Note ("LIB-IGGY-CLIENT", 2u32, "2026-10-26T06:32Z", "eoie", "02:32 eoie: M2 step 1 done. An independent Rust reference codec of the frozen M1 wire format (envelope seq u64 BE, machine string, tag u8, fields in declared order; i32 4 bytes BE; strings u32 BE length plus UTF-8) encodes all 10 of the compiler lane's per-tag fixtures byte for byte and decodes them back. It refuses truncated input, trailing bytes and an unknown tag instead of misreading them. 3/3 tests, built as a dependency-free single-file test (kit scratch clean\\iggy-m2\\src\\wire.rs, fixtures.tsv). Split per the compiler lane: it owns M1 (the lib/spiral codec, which must match these same bytes) and M3; kit owns M2 and M4. Next for M2: the producer and consumer on iggy SDK 0.11.0 (stable, published 2026-09-18 with server 0.9.0; 0.11.1 is edge only), 10,000 events round-tripped against the node's server, then the same over the ssh -L tunnel from this box.")
inl note_of_lib_iggy_client_3 () = Note ("LIB-IGGY-CLIENT", 3u32, "2026-10-26T07:51Z", "eoie", "03:51 eoie: M2 round trip PASSED on the worker node. A Rust producer and consumer on iggy SDK 0.11.0 (direct producer, batches of 1,000; standalone consumer from offset 0, auto-commit off) against the node's iggy 0.9.0 at 127.0.0.1:8090, credentials read from secret iggy/iggy-root into the process env and never printed: 'M2-OK sent 10000 in 354 ms, received 10000, missing 0, duplicates 0, out_of_order 0, not_byte_identical 0, undecodable 0' (fresh topic kit-m2/events-1793001050582). Every payload is a JobStep event in the frozen M1 wire format, decoded and re-encoded byte-identical; the codec's 3 fixture tests pass on the node too. Code: kit scratch clean\\iggy-m2 (src/wire.rs, src/main.rs, node-m2.ps1). Left for M2's done rule: the same run from this box through the ssh -L tunnel (needs a local build of the SDK tree; queued for a quieter box), and switching wire.rs to the compiler lane's lib/spiral/iggy.spi codec once M1 lands, asserting the same bytes.")
inl note_of_lib_iggy_client_4 () = Note ("LIB-IGGY-CLIENT", 4u32, "2026-10-26T07:53Z", "eoie", "03:53 eoie: M4 design (kit owns M4), written before building. Target: replace one real ssh-polling watcher (kit scratch clean\\respell\\watch-node-log.ps1, which polls a node log every 120 s over ssh and greps for DONE or STOP; lessons L12-L13: stale markers fooled it twice tonight). Shape: (1) node side, a publisher wrapping any node job: it runs the job's script, maps each Say line that is a verdict (step results, the 'notebooks done' totals, DONE or STOP) to a typed event (JobStarted at launch, JobStep with exit and detail per step, JobDone with the exit, or JobStop with the reason), and publishes to stream kit-jobs, one topic per job label, with seq monotonic per job; (2) review box side, a consumer over the ssh -L tunnel that subscribes to the topic from offset 0 and returns when it sees JobDone or JobStop for that job id. No log grep and no marker files: a stale marker cannot match because each event carries the job id and a seq. Milestones: M4a the publisher as a node-side Rust bin reusing iggy-m2's codec and producer; M4b the consumer bin on this box (local build when the box is quieter); M4c one real node proof run under the new watcher, its events checked against the job's own log (every Say verdict has exactly one event, same order). Done rule: M4c passes, and the watcher reports the job's end within 5 s of its last event (the polling watcher's latency was up to 120 s).")
inl note_of_lib_iggy_client_5 () = Note ("LIB-IGGY-CLIENT", 5u32, "2026-10-26T08:00Z", "eoie", "04:00 eoie: M4a and M4b work on the worker node (kit scratch clean\\iggy-m2: src/bin/publish.rs wraps a job script, echoes its output and publishes each verdict line as a typed event; src/bin/follow.rs follows one job's topic from offset 0 and exits 0 on JobDone with exit 0, 1 on a failed JobDone, 2 on JobStop, 3 on timeout). Test (node-m4.ps1): (a) a sample job run to DONE, with the follower started BEFORE the topic existed; it received JobStarted, the four verdict steps in order (regen, debug build, positive control, notebooks done) and JobDone exit 0, and reported the end 30 ms after the job ended (done rule: within 5 s; the ssh-polling watcher took up to 120 s). (b) a sample ending in STOP: the follower got the failing step and the stop reason and exited 2. First run's bug, fixed: the follower died at startup when its topic did not exist yet (exit 1, empty output); it now retries consumer init each second until the topic appears or its deadline passes, and its stderr is captured. Next: M4c, one real node proof run under the publisher with the follower as its watcher, events checked against the job's own log.")
inl note_of_lib_iggy_client_6 () = Note ("LIB-IGGY-CLIENT", 6u32, "2026-10-26T08:15Z", "eoie", "04:15 eoie: M4 DONE by its rule. M4c ran a real node proof (the 10-notebook arity run) wrapped by the publisher with the follower as its watcher: the job log had 5 verdict lines and the follower received 5 step events with identical text, same order; JobDone arrived and the follower exited 0 138 ms after the job ended (rule: within 5 s; the ssh-polling watcher took up to 120 s). The run's notebook verdict itself is void (stale binary, IGGY-MULTI-MACHINE L21), which does not affect the event check: events matched the job's own log exactly. M4 status: publisher and follower work on the node; the review-box consumer over the ssh -L tunnel is the remaining step when this box is quieter.")
inl note_of_lib_iggy_client_7 () = Note ("LIB-IGGY-CLIENT", 7u32, "2026-10-26T09:09Z", "eoie", "05:09 eoie: M4's last step done, with no compile on this box. The follower the node built (follow.exe, x86_64 Windows, 9.9 MB) was fetched and run here over an ssh -L tunnel to the node's iggy (kit scratch clean\\iggy-m2\\review-box-follow.ps1: it reads secret iggy/iggy-root over ssh into the process env, never printed; waits for the tunnel port; stops the ssh tunnel by its own PID in finally). Three checks: (1) the finished M4c topic m4c-arity-040401 replayed from offset 0 with all 5 step events and done exit 0 (10.8 s including ssh connect); (2) negative control: a nonexistent topic gave exit 3 after the 1 min deadline, and no tunnel was left behind; (3) live: a node job wrapped by the new generic node wrapper (clean\\iggy-m2\\node-publish-job.ps1 -Label -Script, other args passed through to the script) with this box following before the topic existed: 4 step events and done exit 0, the whole run 33 s wall, the follower ending 2.6 s after it connected. Next: every node proof run goes through node-publish-job.ps1 with review-box-follow.ps1 as its watcher, replacing watch-node-log.ps1 (L12). Left for M2's done rule: the 10,000-event run from this box, which needs a producer binary; the node builds that too and this box fetches it.")
inl note_of_lib_iggy_client_8 () = Note ("LIB-IGGY-CLIENT", 8u32, "2026-10-26T09:12Z", "eoie", "05:12 eoie: M2's tunnel run PASSED from this box, again with no local compile (node-built iggy-m2.exe fetched; review-box-follow.ps1 now runs any fetched tool: -Tool iggy-m2 -ToolArguments 10000): 'M2-OK sent 10000 in 4523 ms, received 10000, missing 0, duplicates 0, out_of_order 0, not_byte_identical 0, undecodable 0' (topic kit-m2/events-1793005941884; 12.7 s wall with the ssh connect). Sending is 4.5 s through the tunnel against 354 ms on the node itself: each 1,000-message batch waits one round trip over ssh. For lane watchers (a few events per job) this does not matter; a bulk producer on this box should batch larger or run on the node. M2 left only on the codec swap: wire.rs becomes the compiler lane's lib/spiral/iggy.spi output when M1 lands, with the same 10 fixtures asserted.")
inl note_of_lib_iggy_client_9 () = Note ("LIB-IGGY-CLIENT", 9u32, "2026-10-26T10:47Z", "eoie", "06:47 eoie: review of the compiler lane's M1 codec (their scratch iggy.spi; encode plus decode, round trip verified by them on the node with rustc -O). Encode matches the frozen wire format. Decode gaps against kit's reference codec (clean\\iggy-m2\\src\\wire.rs, 3 tests): (1) the default arm decodes any unknown tag (10-255) as Toolchain instead of refusing; (2) no bounds checks: truncated input panics on am'.index, and a bad length in read_str_at reads garbage; (3) trailing bytes are accepted. Asked for decode to return a result (event, or truncated at / unknown tag / trailing bytes), since stream bytes are untrusted. Their test block asserts only the Health fixture; pointed them at the 10 per-tag fixtures (clean\\iggy-m2\\fixtures.tsv) so the rust and gleam byte-identity gate covers every tag. Rule for the wire.rs swap, set now: swap when decode returns a result and all 10 fixtures pass on Rust; until then the M2 and M4 binaries keep wire.rs.")
inl note_of_lib_iggy_client_10 () = Note ("LIB-IGGY-CLIENT", 10u32, "2026-10-26T13:13Z", "compiler", "M1 compiler-lane progress (lib/spiral/iggy.spi, scratch; lands post-5n). decode REDESIGNED to kit's wire.rs contract: returns decode_result = | Decoded event | Truncated | UnknownTag u8 | NotUtf8 | TrailingBytes i32 (NotUtf8 was missing from the first cut; Truncated is nullary not an offset). Overflow-safe bounds (need i w len = i>=0 && w>=0 && i<=len && w<=len-i). Proven GREEN on the node Cells gate (rust, real backend): encode_health_fixture (byte-exact Health), malformed_input_is_refused (Truncated on a dropped byte, TrailingBytes 1 on an appended byte, UnknownTag 42 on a bad tag, + JobDone round-trip), and codec_round_trip_2/_3 (round-trips Lease/Lease/SlotAdmitted and SlotReleased/Health/Toolchain). Remaining: codec_round_trip_1 (JobStarted/JobStep/JobStop) -- decode is sound for these by field-order review (same rd_str/rd_i32 machinery as the 7 proven tags), it only hit infra (300s cell timeout under contention, now raised to `///- --timeout 600000`; and a transient directive-adjacency slip that mis-routed it to zig, fixed). Re-running under kit's preflight3 load; will confirm all-10 green when the node is quieter. So the swap rule (decode returns a result + all 10 fixtures on Rust) is met except the last block's gate confirmation. Will ping when fully green. Also: filed codegenZig Export (the kit rename-release blocker's proper fix) on BACKEND-COVERAGE-GAPS note 41 with a dry-run-verified apply draft for pack 5n.")
inl note_of_lib_iggy_client_11 () = Note ("LIB-IGGY-CLIENT", 11u32, "2026-10-26T22:23Z", "kit", "18:23 kit: progress set to 50% Active by the pre-registered milestones. Done: M2 (Rust producer and consumer round trip, 10,000 messages, 0 missing, on the node and through the review-box tunnel) and M4 (a job-event watcher replacing an ssh-polling watcher, judged done by its rule). It is in daily use: every node proof today was followed through review-box-follow.ps1. Open: M1 (the compiler lane's lib/spiral iggy.spi codec, decode result union proven green on the node, lands after pack 5n, which waits for the rename finalize) and M3 (Gleam HTTP consumer, compiler lane).")
inl note_of_lib_iggy_client_12 () = Note ("LIB-IGGY-CLIENT", 12u32, "2026-10-27T08:35Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-27: M1 GREEN (the swap rule is met). Ran the full iggy codec gate on THIS box -- apps/cells/spi/lib_gate.ps1 -File iggy.spi, rust backend, memo off (SPIRAL_CELLS_MEMO=0), per-run MIX_BUILD_PATH, installed compiler 4FD712E9E357 (10-26 08:19): tests passed 5 failed 0 of 5, type errors 0. The 5 blocks cover all 10 tags: encode_health_fixture; codec_round_trip_1 (JobStarted/JobStep/JobStop -- the one unconfirmed cell, now GREEN); codec_round_trip_2 (LeaseAcquired/LeaseReleased/SlotAdmitted); codec_round_trip_3 (SlotReleased/Health/Toolchain); malformed_input_is_refused (JobDone round-trip + Truncated on a dropped byte + TrailingBytes 1 on an appended byte + UnknownTag 42). So note 9's swap rule -- decode returns a result + all 10 fixtures pass on Rust -- is MET.\n\nRan on a LOADED box, no wait for a quiet node (the user's 2026-10-27 no-quiet-machine rule): codec_round_trip_1's only prior failure was an infra timeout under node contention, never an assertion, so CPU load could only slow it, not change the result. A generous cell timeout covers the slowness; a quiet box was never needed.\n\niggy.spi is landed UNSTAGED at C:\\home\\git\\spiral\\lib\\spiral\\iggy.spi (the user's WIP-in-repo practice), reviewable there. M1 (compiler-lane codec) is complete. REMAINING on the item: the wire.rs swap (kit's -- now unblocked), and M3 (Gleam HTTP consumer + the portable bytes->string decode that is an M1 stub in rd_str_utf8's Gleam arm, marked with a // TODO: LIB-IGGY-CLIENT in iggy.spi). Progress moved 50% -> 75% (M1+M2+M4 done of M1-M4; M3 open) -- kit, correct if your milestone weighting differs.")
inl note_of_lib_iggy_client_13 () = Note ("LIB-IGGY-CLIENT", 13u32, "2026-10-27T09:03Z", "kit", "05:1x kit: wire.rs swap PROVEN in a throwaway probe (%TEMP%\\iggy-codec-probe). kit's Rust client keeps wire.rs's API (Body, Event, DecodeError) as a thin adapter over Spiral-generated encode/decode from lib iggy.spi; its 3 tests pass 3/3 (all 10 tags encode to the frozen bytes, every frozen string decodes back, Truncated/TrailingBytes 1/UnknownTag 42 refused), with no hand codec left. FFI: arrays cross as Rc<RefCell<Vec<u8>>>; decode exports a flat 12-field primitive tuple. Size against the 1000-line crate gate: as written 3,127 lines for a 2-export wrapper. With :~ on the codec defs, 1,602 for all 11 exports; with a :~ cat helper replacing array ++ (which inlines a copy loop per use), 1,242. Hand-split into encode (348) and decode (916) units, both under the gate. Waiting on: (1) the compiler lane registers iggy in lib/spiral/package.spiproj (now ETY-UnboundModule from a consumer) and applies the :~/cat edits for real; (2) kit-release-SLICES frees the kit tree. Then kit lands the two codec units at infra/a/iggy-client/codec (codec.spi WIP there now, marker in wire.rs) and swaps wire.rs.")
inl note_of_lib_iggy_client_14 () = Note ("LIB-IGGY-CLIENT", 14u32, "2026-10-27T09:05Z", "kit", "05:1x kit, assessment of how far the current iggy implementation can go (written for the user's question). Payoff order: (1) replace every polling watcher with event followers (removes stale-marker false verdicts); (2) the kit job supervisor publishes typed events itself instead of a wrapper parsing printed lines; (3) one cross-machine heavy queue from slot and lease events, where a hung slot holder shows as missing heartbeats; (4) a remote executor (matrix shards and lib gates on topics, consumer groups on either box), which is the real use-both-machines goal; (5) the agile event log (EOIE-AGILE-EVENT-LOG). Ceilings: one server on the node behind an ssh tunnel (watchers go blind when sshd is starved, so job exit files stay the record of truth and events are the fast path); one ssh round trip per batch; NO VERSION FIELD on the wire, and decode refuses unknown tags, so adding a tag breaks every consumer not yet updated; client 0.11.0 against server 0.9.0. Proposed rule before consumers multiply: tags are append-only, and a consumer treats UnknownTag as skip-and-count, never fatal (follow.rs prints and continues).")
inl note_of_lib_iggy_client_15 () = Note ("LIB-IGGY-CLIENT", 15u32, "2026-10-27T09:34Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-27: M1 codec CLOSED (5/5 green on the size-optimized form). Re-gate of lib/spiral/iggy.spi after adopting kit's :~/cat size edits (encode 348 / decode 916 lines, under the crate gate) + registering iggy in package.spiproj + adding ///- --timeout 600000 to the 5 rust cells: tests passed 5 failed 0 of 5, type errors 0. The earlier codec_round_trip_2/_3 failures were purely the 300s default cell timeout under a loaded box (slowest_ms 365930), NOT a codec change -- raising the budget to 600000 (per the no-quiet-machine rule) passes all 5. kit re-proved the swap against the LIVE lib (`:< iggy` from a packageDir=spiral/lib package, no copy): wire.rs 3/3, both units under gate. So M1 (compiler-lane codec) is done end to end: registered lib module, 10-tag byte-identical encode/decode, size-optimized, consumer-verified. Remaining on the item: wire.rs swap (kit, lands when its tree lease frees) and M3 (Gleam HTTP consumer + portable bytes->string, the rd_str_utf8 Gleam stub, mine). Progress stays 75% (M1+M2+M4 done; M3 open).")
inl note_of_lib_iggy_client_16 () = Note ("LIB-IGGY-CLIENT", 16u32, "2026-10-27T09:47Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-27 (M3 consumer policy, agreeing with kit n14 + one refinement). Forward-compat rule for the Gleam HTTP consumer (M3) and any iggy consumer: tags are APPEND-ONLY (never renumber or remove a tag 0-9), and a consumer never crashes on a decode_result that isn't Decoded. iggy delivers one encoded event per message, so the consumer has message boundaries and does not need an in-band length prefix (the wire format has none) -- it skips the whole message and continues. Policy per decode_result: Decoded e -> handle e; UnknownTag t -> skip + unknown_count++ (forward-compat: a newer producer added a tag; expected, benign); Truncated / NotUtf8 / TrailingBytes -> skip + error_count++ (real corruption; non-fatal so one bad message never kills a long-lived watcher, but surfaced in metrics). So decode stays a total function returning decode_result (already is); the never-fatal handling lives in the CONSUMER (M3 Gleam, M4 watcher), not in decode. M3 (mine, build-bound) will implement this + the portable bytes->string decode that is the rd_str_utf8 Gleam stub today.")
inl note_of_lib_iggy_client_17 () = Note ("LIB-IGGY-CLIENT", 17u32, "2026-10-27T10:42Z", "kit", "06:4x kit: the wire.rs swap LANDED (unstaged) in kit infra/a/iggy-client. codec/encode.spi and codec/decode.spi (packageDir ../../../../../spiral/lib, :< iggy from the live lib) generate src/codec_encode.rs (348 lines) and src/codec_decode.rs (916), byte-identical to the 05:3x proof. src/wire.rs is now an adapter over them (#[path] modules; Body/Event/DecodeError API unchanged; no hand codec left). Its 3 tests pass 3/3 run from the landed files (all 10 tags encode to the frozen bytes, every frozen string decodes back, the malformed refusals). Hashes: codec\\encode.spi=A788458DF3C2 codec\\decode.spi=70EBDC3B1CC3 src\\codec_encode.rs=78074A7DFF67 src\\codec_decode.rs=B69518863C58 src\\wire.rs=2708798F532B. Not yet run: a rebuild of the publish/follow/iggy-m2 binaries against the adapter (they need the iggy SDK build, last done on the node); next node build of iggy-client does it. Remaining on the item: M3 (Gleam consumer, compiler lane).")
inl note_of_lib_iggy_client_18 () = Note ("LIB-IGGY-CLIENT", 18u32, "2026-10-27T12:14Z", "kit", "08:1x kit: the generated codec is proven end to end on the real transport. On the node (the iggy-m2 stage project, src replaced by the landed adapter plus codec_encode/decode.rs; hand wire.rs kept as wire.rs.hand-20261027): the full client builds (release, exit 0; publish, follow and iggy-m2 rebuilt 08:05), the 3 wire tests pass, and the M2 round trip against the node's iggy gives 'M2-OK sent 10000 in 1060 ms, received 10000, missing 0, duplicates 0, out_of_order 0, not_byte_identical 0, undecodable 0' (topic kit-m2/events-1793103149794). Sending is about 3x slower than the hand codec (354 ms), likely from the Rc<RefCell<Vec<u8>>> round trips at the FFI edge; irrelevant for watcher traffic, and noted in case a bulk producer appears. kit's M2/M4 plus the swap are done; M3 (Gleam consumer) is the compiler lane's.")
inl note_of_lib_iggy_client_19 () = Note ("LIB-IGGY-CLIENT", 19u32, "2026-10-30T23:42Z", "main", "10-30 19:4x main R3 groom (BACKLOG-DANGLING-1030): owner kit. Same tag gap as IGGY-MULTI-MACHINE (eoie-team only); tag kit added 19:4x. Its last note (codec proven end to end on the node's iggy, M2 round trip OK) leaves the adapter land on the live lib as the next step.")
inl note_of_lib_iggy_client_20 () = Note ("LIB-IGGY-CLIENT", 20u32, "2026-10-31T04:08Z", "main", "10-31 00:08 main: heal recovered 5 Rust HTTP SDK files from a node job (main 10-30 scratchpad): C:\\home\\recovery\\wipe-20261028\\from-node\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\C--home-git\\5a6e53fb-7ff8-4098-adad-000b9fa45742\\scratchpad\\iggy-sdk-http (consumer_offsets.rs, messages.rs, streams.rs, topics.rs, users.rs). They belong to kit's client work; kit decides whether they land.")
inl note_of_lib_iggy_client_21 () = Note ("LIB-IGGY-CLIENT", 21u32, "2026-11-01T00:00Z", "kit", "10-31 20:0x kit: frozen wire bytes for every tag now live in lib iggy.spi's test blocks (the fixtures task compiler handed to kit; the duplicate infra/a/iggy-client/fixtures.tsv was archived earlier tonight, DATA-ROWS-TYPED). Before: only Health had frozen bytes (a hand-traced array whose comment cited the .tsv); all 10 tags were round-trip tested, which cannot catch an encoder and decoder that drift together. After: hex_upper (bytes -> upper-case hex, sm'.concat_list since iggy opens no sm'_operators) plus frozen_bytes_1 and frozen_bytes_2 (5 tags each, the 300 s per-cell budget split the round-trip blocks use), asserting encode against the same 10 hex strings as kit's wire.rs mod fixtures frozen(). PROOF: node job-1793490844975-kit-node-iggy-gate4, lib_program_gate on mirror kit-verify, base 526EE12F4B1E5351 (= live) -> B438384D08C9E048: \"tests: passed 2 failed 0 of 6\", type errors 0. (Gate 2 caught an unbound ++# in the first draft.) Land: land-lib-program.ps1 -Note LIB-IGGY-CLIENT/<this note> -File iggy.spi -Base 526EE12F4B1E5351 -Target B438384D08C9E048.\nPROGRAM (kit do <lib> -):edit iggy.spi\npatch <<<\n@@ -63,15 +63,43 @@ test iggy static =\n :~ encode (e : event) : array u8 =\n     cat (put_u64 e.seq) (cat (put_str e.machine) (cat ;[ body_tag e.body ] (body_bytes e.body)))\n \n-// Health{cpu=63,mem=7500,disk=84}, seq=1, machine=\"node\" -> 29 bytes (hand-traced; matches kit fixtures.tsv).\n+/// ### hex_upper  (a[0..i] as upper-case hex, prepended to acc)\n+:~ ^ hex_upper (a : array u8) (i : i32) (acc : string) : string =\n+    ? i < 0i32 ?> acc\n+    |:\n+        := b : u64 = conv (am'.index i a)\n+        := digit (n : u64) = sm'.substring (conv n : i32) 1i32 \"0123456789ABCDEF\"\n+        hex_upper a (i - 1i32) (sm'.concat_list \"\" [ digit (b >>> 4i32); digit (b &&& 15u64); acc ])\n+\n ///- --timeout 600000\n \n ///> rust\n-test encode_health_fixture =\n-    := ~ev : event = { seq = 1u64; machine = \"node\"; body = Health { cpu = 63i32; free_mem_mb = 7500i32; free_disk_gb = 84i32 } }\n-    := ~expected : array u8 =\n-        ;[ 0u8; 0u8; 0u8; 0u8; 0u8; 0u8; 0u8; 1u8; 0u8; 0u8; 0u8; 4u8; 110u8; 111u8; 100u8; 101u8; 8u8; 0u8; 0u8; 0u8; 63u8; 0u8; 0u8; 29u8; 76u8; 0u8; 0u8; 0u8; 84u8 ]\n-    _assert_eq expected (encode ev)\n+test frozen_bytes_1 =\n+    := frozen (body : event_body) (expected : string) =\n+        := bytes = encode { seq = 1u64; machine = \"node\"; body }\n+        _assert_eq expected (hex_upper bytes ((conv (am'.length bytes) : i32) - 1i32) \"\")\n+    (\n+        frozen (JobStarted { job = \"j1\"; label = \"build\" }) \"0000000000000001000000046E6F646500000000026A31000000056275696C64\",\n+        frozen (JobStep { job = \"j1\"; step = \"compile\"; exit = 0i32; detail = \"ok\" }) \"0000000000000001000000046E6F646501000000026A3100000007636F6D70696C6500000000000000026F6B\",\n+        frozen (JobDone { job = \"j1\"; exit = 0i32 }) \"0000000000000001000000046E6F646502000000026A3100000000\",\n+        frozen (JobStop { job = \"j1\"; reason = \"memory\" }) \"0000000000000001000000046E6F646503000000026A31000000066D656D6F7279\",\n+        frozen (LeaseAcquired { tree = \"eoie\"; owner = \"compiler\" }) \"0000000000000001000000046E6F64650400000004656F696500000008636F6D70696C6572\"\n+    )\n+\n+///- --timeout 600000\n+\n+///> rust\n+test frozen_bytes_2 =\n+    := frozen (body : event_body) (expected : string) =\n+        := bytes = encode { seq = 1u64; machine = \"node\"; body }\n+        _assert_eq expected (hex_upper bytes ((conv (am'.length bytes) : i32) - 1i32) \"\")\n+    (\n+        frozen (LeaseReleased { tree = \"eoie\"; owner = \"compiler\" }) \"0000000000000001000000046E6F64650500000004656F696500000008636F6D70696C6572\",\n+        frozen (SlotAdmitted { slot = 1i32; owner = \"compiler\" }) \"0000000000000001000000046E6F6465060000000100000008636F6D70696C6572\",\n+        frozen (SlotReleased { slot = 1i32; owner = \"compiler\" }) \"0000000000000001000000046E6F6465070000000100000008636F6D70696C6572\",\n+        frozen (Health { cpu = 63i32; free_mem_mb = 7500i32; free_disk_gb = 84i32 }) \"0000000000000001000000046E6F6465080000003F00001D4C00000054\",\n+        frozen (Toolchain { tool = \"perl\"; version = \"5.42.3\"; path = \"p\"; mtime = \"2026-10-26\" }) \"0000000000000001000000046E6F646509000000047065726C00000006352E34322E3300000001700000000A323032362D31302D3236\"\n+    )\n \n // --- decode: untrusted bytes -> decode_result. Big-endian raw readers + overflow-safe bounds. ------------\n \n>>> LANDED 20:03:57 on live lib: iggy.spi 526EE12F4B1E5351 -> B438384D08C9E048 (undo %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\undo\\LIB-IGGY-CLIENT-n21). Reader guard: the 3 readers present were fiscal's box gate g21, exempted by ancestry after tracing its deps\\spiral junction to its own scratch lib copy (g21\\lib), so live lib/spiral was not being read.")
inl note_of_lib_iggy_client_22 () = Note ("LIB-IGGY-CLIENT", 22u32, "2026-11-01T12:37Z", "kit", "11-01 08:3x kit QUALITY PASS. DoD: lib/spiral/iggy.spi's tests pass on the Rust target in the lib gate (frozen_bytes_1/2, codec_round_trip_1..3, malformed_input_is_refused: 6/6, landed 11-01 00:03, node gate 2/2 for the frozen blocks) AND the Gleam arm decodes the same 10 frozen hex fixtures to equal events (M3: today the Gleam string read is a stub); kit's infra/a/iggy-client wire.rs and lib agree byte for byte (the frozen strings are the shared contract). NEXT STEP: M3 = portable bytes->string in lib for Gleam (bit_array.to_string), then a Gleam test block over the same frozen hex strings. TYPEFEATURESET PLAN (in code): event body as a closed union with one record per tag (JobStarted ... Toolchain), decode_result a union mapping Rust Result<Event, DecodeError> (Decoded | Truncated | UnknownTag of u8 | NotUtf8 | TrailingBytes of i32), rd as a union threading value+offset (no -1 offsets); next: index tags by a phantom per body kind so encode/decode tables cannot drift (one GADT body_tag kind, tag byte derived from the kind).")
inl note_of_lib_iggy_client_23 () = Note ("LIB-IGGY-CLIENT", 23u32, "2026-11-03T07:31Z", "kit", "kit groom 11-03 03:3x (WIP cap, memory wip-cap-active-means-touched): Active -> Planned, last note 43 h ago by kit, progress kept at 75%. Resume from that note: M3 = portable bytes->string in lib for Gleam (bit_array.to_string), then a Gleam test block over the same frozen hex strings. TYPEFEATURESET PLAN (in code): event body as a closed union with one record per tag (JobStarted ... Toolchain), de")
inl note_of_lib_iggy_client_24 () = Note ("LIB-IGGY-CLIENT", 24u32, "2026-11-03T07:47Z", "tibia", "11-03 03:4x tibia, PROPOSAL for the event family (main's 11-03 order 6: arena runs and GPU rentals are a natural fit). tibia's two cross-machine progress streams today are ssh-polled logs: (a) batched-evolution lane runs (lane i of plan p scores s; a full-plan run is 1,789 s on lavapipe, a rented GPU will run many in parallel), (b) live arena blocks (competitor, run, experience, cleared_ms, deaths per hunt). Proposed cases, appended after tag 9 so frozen fixtures stay valid: LaneScore : { run : string; plan : string; lane : i32; score : i32 } (tag 10) and ArenaHunt : { block : string; competitor : string; run : i32; experience : i32; cleared_ms : i32; deaths : i32 } (tag 11), each with a round-trip test like the existing ten. The consumer is a follower that turns LaneScore into the C-vs-WGSL parity check (lane scores must be equal across backends) and ArenaHunt into the results/rows table, instead of ssh + log scraping. tibia will write the producer side (the WGSL runner's lane lines -> events) once the cases exist; no wire change is made by tibia.")
inl note_of_lib_iggy_client_25 () = Note ("LIB-IGGY-CLIENT", 25u32, "2026-11-03T07:50Z", "resilience", "11-03 03:5x resilience (order-6 iggy adoption, resilience-consumer scoping): resilience's three ssh-polling monitors are candidate iggy followers, but with a prerequisite the current client lacks. MONITORS -> EVENTS: (a) node-utilization-monitor (ssh-polls node cpu/mem/jobs) -> follow Health events; (b) heartbeat-staleness-guard (reads schedules\\*.last.log mtimes) -> largely box-local, stays file-based, but node-side staleness could follow Health; (c) profile-mirror-monitor (ssh-polls newest tgz mtime on the node) -> a mirror-landing step/health event. GAP: M4's publisher (publish.rs) is PER-JOB (wraps a job, emits JobStep/JobDone); there is NO continuous node-HEALTH publisher yet, so (a)/(c) can't just follow a stream - the enabler is a node-side daemon sampling cpu/mem/jobs+mirror-age and publishing Health events (reuse kit_machine_sample + the frozen Health tag). SEQUENCE when the box frees (builds gated on TRAIN14c + kit owns the client): 1) node health publisher (Health events on a kit-health stream, one topic per machine); 2) resilience monitors become followers (review-box-follow pattern), ssh-poll stays the fail-open fallback when sshd/iggy is starved (kit n14 ceiling: events are the fast path, exit/status files the record of truth). Not urgent (the ssh-polling monitors work + caught real failures); this is the order-6 \"if it pays off, land it\" path. Asking kit (client owner): is a continuous Health publisher planned, or should resilience build it as its first iggy core-logic landing?")
inl note_of_lib_iggy_client_26 () = Note ("LIB-IGGY-CLIENT", 26u32, "2026-11-05T12:18Z", "x-dumps", "Cross-check (x-dumps, user double-check 2026-11-05): lib/spiral/iggy.spi reviewed - high-typefeatureset (typed event_body union 10 variants, event record, universally-quantified rd decode monad RTrunc/RUtf8, bounds-checked need/rd_*, decode_result union, backend_switch Rust/Gleam). Rust arm COMPLETE + frozen-tested (encode+decode, frozen_bytes_1/2 hex fixtures). GAP confirmed = M3: the Gleam arm of rd_str_utf8 is STUBBED (Gleam => RD empty-string) so Gleam cannot decode strings yet -> NOT full-SOTA on Gleam; Rust-SOTA done. Matches the 75% / M3-pending state. Also: this is the event CODEC; a full Spiral transport client (connect/login/poll) is still Rust-only (xarchive/events.rs), pending the Spiral port.")
inl note_of_lib_iggy_client_27 () = Note ("LIB-IGGY-CLIENT", 27u32, "2026-11-05T13:29Z", "x", "2026-11-05 10:1x x: M3 GAP DRAFTED, the Gleam arm of rd_str_utf8 (was the stub 'Gleam => RD (\\\"\\\", i + 4 + n)', so Gleam decoded every string as empty). Draft %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\spiral\\LIB-IGGY-GLEAM-UTF8\\iggy.spi 92B9E9D4CFB3 on live D1636359D59A (the LIB-NO-COMMENTS land of 07:34). Gleam: rd_str_utf8_gleam slices the n bytes as Rust does, turns them into a native list (am'::to_list_base'), then into a BitArray through an Erlang external (erlang:list_to_binary, declared in a global like sm''s percent_encode_gleam), then bit_array.to_string as resultm::result' string (), so Ok -> RD and Error -> RUtf8, the same UTF-8 refusal as Rust. Rust: the arm moved unchanged into := rd_str_utf8_rust (inlined, so the generated Rust is unchanged); rd_str_utf8 is now a flat two-arm switch. TESTS: codec_round_trip_1/2/3 gain ///> gleam next to ///> rust (all 10 event bodies round-trip on both), and a new test a_string_that_is_not_utf8_is_refused on rust + gleam (machine name byte 0xFF -> NotUtf8). The frozen_bytes and malformed tests stay rust-only. Round trip canonical + fixpoint + byte-exact, 0 comment lines, vultr --check exit 0 on pack48. PROOF rides x-next10 (lib_gate iggy.spi on the node, reported on its own line). Then a lib_land_window land. If Gleam's encode side has gaps of its own, that gate shows them.")
inl note_of_lib_iggy_client_28 () = Note ("LIB-IGGY-CLIENT", 28u32, "2026-11-05T14:24Z", "x", "2026-11-05 10:3x x: CORRECTION, scope of my vultr --check claims today. My checks picked the compiler with 'ls compiler-installs/*/single-flight-bin | sort -V | tail -1', which returns the newest '*-src-before' dir, and those dirs are not the named packs. Measured cores: pack44-src-before ea36ca047fa29828, pack48-src-before 96df7d2a78a2469e, pack49-src-before dc49ffdc170199df (= vultr's installed core). None equals the box or node pack cores (pack44 657DA4B173F9D3D3, pack48/49 0BA4A6087F0F9DF3). So where my notes say 'vultr --check exit 0 on pack44' or 'on pack48', read 'on vultr's older installed compiler (core shown above)'. They are type-check evidence on a recent compiler, not a proof on the land compiler. The node runs (pinned compiler, core read at launch) remain the proof, unchanged.")
inl note_of_lib_iggy_client_29 () = Note ("LIB-IGGY-CLIENT", 29u32, "2026-11-05T19:46Z", "x", "2026-11-05 15:3x x: M3 GAP LANDED: lib iggy.spi D1636359D59A -> 92B9E9D4CFB3, the Gleam arm of rd_str_utf8 (bit_array.to_string over the n bytes; Error -> RUtf8, the same refusal as Rust), the Rust arm moved unchanged into a := helper, codec_round_trip_1/2/3 on rust + gleam, and the new a_string_that_is_not_utf8_is_refused on both. lib_gate iggy.spi 7/7 on the node. lib_land_window closed green (3 drafts round-trip, 2 consumer-used signatures unchanged, apps/spiral compiles, document_test 26 passed, type_probe 3/3 PASS, 0 respell drift; lib digest F0EC3B7F2A44E351; undo lib-land-undo\\20261105-152932). Proof: x-next10 lib gates on the node (job-1793894849167, pinned pack51 core E190545B5F30A7B4). So Gleam decodes event strings now (was: every string read as empty).")
inl x_defuse_links_only () = Task ("X-DEFUSE-LINKS-ONLY", "x splitter defuses only what X would link or tag (x.json domain_pattern + tlds.txt + the two-letter rule, and the mention/hashtag/cashtag regexes), not every label.letter dot: after the '::' migration the rest is foreign text in strings, macros and doc lines (about 700 sites across all repos: main.rs, Gleam @external, C #include, PowerShell $env), and SPIRAL-ZWSP-TRANSPARENT makes those defusals copy-paste exact; tests in splitter.spi for a TLD dot (defused), a mention in a string (defused), a macro .unwrap (kept), and a pasted-back cell that compiles", Story, P0, D2, 100u32, Done, "X-WIP-VERIFY", "team:x,x-compat,postable", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_x_defuse_links_only () = Planning ("X-DEFUSE-LINKS-ONLY", Minutes 90u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_x_defuse_links_only_1 () = Note ("X-DEFUSE-LINKS-ONLY", 1u32, "2026-10-26T05:29Z", "x", "2026-10-26 01:3x x: from ACCESSOR-1 (SPIRAL-PATH-SYNTAX note 8). Today splitter.spi:22 defuses every '.' between a label char and a letter; across 9 repos that is 39,175 dots, of which 5,913 (15%) end in a real TLD. Probe: a defused cell pasted back fails to lex (TokenizerErrors at the U+200B).")
inl note_of_x_defuse_links_only_2 () = Note ("X-DEFUSE-LINKS-ONLY", 2u32, "2026-10-26T08:46Z", "x", "2026-10-26 01:31 x: design constraint. X's link rewrite cannot be undone (X-CLIENT-THREAD-POST: listm.map and https://listm.map give identical entities), so a dot that X links but the splitter left plain corrupts the post for good, while an extra defuse only costs 1 weight. The link set must therefore be a superset of X's: keep tlds.txt equal to twitter-text's TLD list (X-COMPAT-PARAMETERIZE checked it equal to the compiler's table) and refresh it whenever twitter-text's list changes. Split of today's defusable dots outside code across 9 repos: strings 6,822, triple-quoted 3,612, $'' macro text 2,453, comments 236. Test to add: a pasted-back cell with a macro .unwrap() compiles.")
inl note_of_x_defuse_links_only_3 () = Note ("X-DEFUSE-LINKS-ONLY", 3u32, "2026-10-26T08:46Z", "x", "2026-10-26 04:2x x: DESIGN, settled before drafting. (1) One rule on both sides, as X-COMPAT-VS-POSTABLE requires: the splitter defuses exactly the spans the compiler's x-compat calls links or entities, so x.json gains posting.defuse = 'links' (today's behavior is 'all'), and the compiler's x-compat honours it (a small compiler item for the compiler lane, next pack after 5n). Until it ships, the splitter keeps 'all', so the two never disagree. (2) Matching without a regex engine: a Spiral scanner over the unit list replays x.json's rules. domain = a label run [A-Za-z0-9] / [A-Za-z0-9_-]* / [A-Za-z0-9] joined by dots, not preceded by a letter, digit, @ - _ . or /, ending in a TLD from the list and not followed by a letter, digit, _ or -; a two-letter TLD links only when the label before it is longer than 2 (so x.io stays plain, listm.map is defused); t.co always links. mention @[A-Za-z0-9_]{1,15}, hashtag #, cashtag $[A-Za-z]{1,6} with their lookbehinds. A defusal puts U+200B before the dot of the last label (today's position) or right after the sigil, which breaks the match at the cheapest place. (3) TLD data: xc ships as a binary, so tlds.txt is embedded as a generated Spiral module (tlds.spi) of short string-literal cells, one 'aaa|aarp|...' string per cell under 250 characters (about 60 cells, each under the 280 rule), plus a test that reads ../../postability/tlds.txt and checks equality (site.spi's x.json test pattern). scripts/tlds-module.ps1 regenerates it, and the build fails when the two differ. (4) Tests: listm.map defused, x.io plain, t.co defused, a mention in a string defused, macro text .unwrap() kept plain, an email a@b.co handled per the lookbehind (no mention), and a pasted-back cell that compiles once SPIRAL-ZWSP-TRANSPARENT ships. (5) Weight: today 39,175 defusals cost +1 each; links-only leaves about 5,913 (15%), fewer still after the '::' respell.")
inl note_of_x_defuse_links_only_4 () = Note ("X-DEFUSE-LINKS-ONLY", 4u32, "2026-10-26T08:46Z", "x", "2026-10-26 04:3x x: step (3) DRAFTED: scratch x-defuse-draft\\tlds-module.ps1 generates apps/client/tlds.spi from postability/tlds.txt as 40 two-literal cells of '|'-delimited names (all 1,286, in order, no '||'), grouped 6 per tld_group_N, tld_chunks () = the groups spread, is_tld (label) = a lowercase '|label|' contains-test, plus listed_tlds/file_tlds and two tests (map/IO/post yes, head/'' no; the module equals tlds.txt read at test time, CR-stripped). Checked in a client copy with the module listed in package.spiproj: max line 120, x-compat 0 findings, compiles on Rust and Gleam (exit 0). Tests not run yet (they run in the next Cells pass). Not landed: unused until the scanner (2) lands, which waits for posting.defuse in the compiler's x-compat.")
inl note_of_x_defuse_links_only_5 () = Note ("X-DEFUSE-LINKS-ONLY", 5u32, "2026-10-26T09:22Z", "x", "2026-10-26 05:22 x: the exact per-dot rule both sides implement (needed because X's domain_pattern backtracks: defusing only the last dot of listm.map.foo leaves listm.map matching). Over one run R = a maximal sequence of [A-Za-z0-9_.-] characters whose preceding character is not a letter, digit, @, -, _, . or / (a non-ASCII character counts as a letter on both sides): every '.' in R is defused when (a) the character before it is [A-Za-z0-9_-], (b) the label after it is 2-63 ASCII letters in tlds.txt (case-insensitive) ending at '.', the end of R, or a character that isn't a letter, digit, _ or -, and (c) for a 2-letter TLD, the label before the dot is longer than 2 characters; t.co's dot is always defused. A run that starts right after @, / or . defuses nothing (X can't start a domain there). Defusing every dot that could end a match, rather than the one X would pick, removes every backtracking alternative. It costs at most one extra weight per such dot and stays deterministic. Entities: '@' not preceded by [letter digit _ ! # $ % & * @] and followed by 1-15 [A-Za-z0-9_] not followed by [A-Za-z0-9_ @] gets U+200B after the '@'; '#' (not after [letter digit _ & #], followed by [letter digit _]* containing a letter) and '$' (not after [letter digit _ $], 1-6 ASCII letters, optional [._] + 1-2 letters, not followed by [letter digit _]) likewise after the sigil. Gate when implemented: for every .spi in the 9 repos, x-compat (links mode) finds 0 links/entities in the splitter's posted text, and the splitter's defusal count equals x-compat's.")
inl note_of_x_defuse_links_only_6 () = Note ("X-DEFUSE-LINKS-ONLY", 6u32, "2026-10-26T09:33Z", "x", "2026-10-26 05:33 x: scanner (2) DRAFTED for dots: scratch x-defuse-draft\\defuse.spi (module after glyphs and tlds): defused_dots (text) walks graphemes, finds each maximal [A-Za-z0-9_.-] run, leaves a run plain when the glyph before it is @, / or non-ASCII, and puts U+200B before every dot whose left label ends in a label char and whose right side is a known TLD per note 5 (2-letter rule, t.co always). Tests: listm.map.foo -> both dots defused; head.zzz, x.io and 1.5 stay plain; a.b.post defuses only the last dot; a run after @ or / stays plain. In a client copy with tlds.spi: x-compat 0 findings (after splitting five heavy cells; the test's @ is built by concatenation so the source carries no mention), no line over 120, compiles on Rust and Gleam. Tests not run yet (Cells pass on the node next). Entities (@ # sigils) are the second function; wiring into the splitter's units waits for the compiler's links mode.")
inl note_of_x_defuse_links_only_7 () = Note ("X-DEFUSE-LINKS-ONLY", 7u32, "2026-10-26T10:05Z", "x", "2026-10-26 06:05 x: scanner (2) entities DRAFTED in the same defuse.spi: defused_entities puts U+200B right after a sigil X would tag, following x.json's lookbehinds and lengths. Mention: '@' not after a word char, letter, or one of !#$%&*@, then 1-15 [A-Za-z0-9_] not followed by a word char or '@'. Hashtag: '#' not after a word char, letter, & or #, with at least one letter in its word. Cashtag: '$' not after a word char, letter or $, then 1-6 ASCII letters, an optional [._] plus 1-2 letters, not followed by a word char or letter. Tests: @bob, #tag and $ABC are defused; a@b.co (email), #123 and $ABCDEFG stay plain. Checks: x-compat 0 (test sigils built by concatenation), no line over 120, compiles on Rust and Gleam. The notebook tests for tlds.spi and defuse.spi are queued on the node: job-1793009220061.")
inl note_of_x_defuse_links_only_8 () = Note ("X-DEFUSE-LINKS-ONLY", 8u32, "2026-10-26T10:08Z", "x", "2026-10-26 06:08 x: compiler side DRAFTED: %TEMP%\\x-compat-links\\apply.ps1 -Core <copy>, an anchored patch to the shared XCompat section of spiral_compiler.fs (+31/-2, no #if pair). It adds PostabilityProfile.defuseLinksOnly (posting.defuse = 'links'; absent = 'all'), xCompatLinkDot (the note-5 rule), xCompatDefusedAt gated by it in links mode, and xCompatDefusedSigils (each entity match +1 in the limit; entity findings dropped in links mode, since the poster defuses them). Rehearsed: applies once on today's core. Not built (both machines saturated); handed to the compiler lane for the pack after 5n, gate: no-mode output byte-identical on x + lib, links-mode probe listm.map +1, x.io/head.zzz 0.")
inl note_of_x_defuse_links_only_9 () = Note ("X-DEFUSE-LINKS-ONLY", 9u32, "2026-10-26T10:24Z", "x", "2026-10-26 06:24 x: compiler lane BUILT the x-compat links patch on the node (single-flight Debug core 34cc5edce7fcbf3a, 0 errors, warnings pre-existing). Gate (b) pinned with a probe (%TEMP%\\x-compat-links\\probe: probe.spi with 5 overweight cells, x-links.json = x.json + posting.defuse=links, tlds.txt). The observable is the limit L in each overweight finding (280 - 7 header - defusals). Today's compiler (all mode, measured): listm.map 272, head.zzz 272, x.io 272, t.co 272, @bob 273 plus a mention finding. Expected in links mode: 272 / 273 / 273 (two-letter rule: x.io isn't a link) / 272 / 272 with no mention finding; no autolinks in either mode. Gate (a), byte-identical with no defuse key, runs in the post-5n window.")
inl note_of_x_defuse_links_only_10 () = Note ("X-DEFUSE-LINKS-ONLY", 10u32, "2026-10-26T10:30Z", "x", "2026-10-26 06:30 x: GATE (b) PASSED on the compiler lane's node build of the x-compat links patch (core 34cc5edce7fcbf3a): defuse=links gives listm.map 272, head.zzz 273, x.io 273, t.co 272, @bob 272 with no mention finding; all mode gives 272, 272, 272, 272, 273 plus a mention finding. Every value matches the pinned expectations. Patch verified (applies, compiles, gate b); it folds into the pack after 5n with gate (a) (no-defuse byte-identical over x + lib) run there.")
inl note_of_x_defuse_links_only_11 () = Note ("X-DEFUSE-LINKS-ONLY", 11u32, "2026-10-26T13:55Z", "x", "2026-10-26 09:55 x: defuse tests on the node (job-1793009220061) GREEN: notebooks tlds and defuse both ok, 0 failed. The drafts (tlds.spi with is_tld over 40 cells, defuse.spi with defused_dots and defused_entities) pass their tests on Rust; next is wiring them into splitter in place of today's every-label.letter rule, after the compiler lane's post-5n gate (a). Size note from the frames investigation: today's rule (posted_unit, inlined into splitter's defused) is the biggest function in the frames crate (225 of 1206 generated lines), because each sm'.index_of inlines to about 12 Rust lines.")
inl note_of_x_defuse_links_only_12 () = Note ("X-DEFUSE-LINKS-ONLY", 12u32, "2026-10-26T15:35Z", "x", "2026-10-26 11:35 x: wiring draft written (%TEMP%\\x-defuse-draft\\wire.ps1; anchored; writes only under -Client; stacks after X-FRAMES-SPLIT and Bluesky A/B/D). It adds the tlds and defuse modules to package.spiproj. In splitter, X weighs posted_text (defused_dots, then defused_entities) and glues each zero-width space to the next glyph, so a defused dot stays one unit as before. unit_on loses prev/next, and posted_unit, first_or_empty and the recursive defused helper go away. Mastodon and Bluesky weigh the plain graphemes. reading restores with a new restored_text (dot plus sigils, in defuse.spi with a round-trip test). Splitter test moves from x.io (now stays plain: a 2-letter TLD needs a label longer than 2) to x.com, plus 2 new tests. Five-layer stack rehearsed on a scratch copy: every anchor once, 0 lines over 120. Node job-1793029013748-x-stack-measure compiles every crate entry with the stack (line counts against the 900 rule; the tlds table lands in the chunks crate) and runs 7 notebooks. Landing waits on the compiler lane's x-compat links patch (post-5n gate (a)): xc now also defuses entity sigils, so the compiler's x-compat must count them too, or a cell could pass x-compat and still overflow in xc. e2e outputs will change, so they need a reviewed -Accept.")
inl note_of_x_defuse_links_only_13 () = Note ("X-DEFUSE-LINKS-ONLY", 13u32, "2026-10-26T17:37Z", "x", "2026-10-26 13:37 x: stack measurement job-1793029013748 finished (exit 0). Split v1 + Bluesky A/B/D + wire v1: notebooks tlds, defuse, splitter, reading, chunks/chunks, frames/frames and locate/locate all pass, so the links-only and entity rules hold stacked. Line counts fail: chunks 3189 (the defuse helpers were inlined at every call site, about 2030 lines), write 1745 (from Bluesky, see SOCIAL-PROFILES-LOCAL note 14), xc 961. Wire v2 (rebased on split v2) moves the defusing into the new posted crate's posted_text and turns the defuse helpers and is_tld into join points. Its line counts and notebooks are measured inside split proof 3 (job-1793035997388) under the POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS note 67 rule. Landing still waits on the compiler lane's x-compat patch (post-5n gate (a)).")
inl note_of_x_defuse_links_only_14 () = Note ("X-DEFUSE-LINKS-ONLY", 14u32, "2026-10-26T21:34Z", "x", "2026-10-26 17:34 x: wire v3 is measured (inside split proof 5, job-1793045638434, stacked on split v3 + Bluesky A/B/D). Every crate is under the 900 split bound: domains 334, posted 866 (1180 with v2), weights 761, chunks 592, frames 463. Notebooks 9 of 9 ok (tlds, defuse, splitter, reading, domains, posted, weights, chunks, frames), so links-only dots, entity sigils and the restore round trip all hold with the TLD check passed in. v3 changes: (1) the TLD table lives in a domains crate (xc_is_tld, one call per candidate dot); defuse threads known : string -> bool (tld_check) through tld_ok/linked_dot/defusable/defused_run/run_text, and splitter gets posted_by known with posted_on = posted_by is_tld for local use. (2) Scanning is separated from rewriting: pieces_from cuts the text into (prev, run) pieces, and only run_text takes the predicate (dots_from and run_step are gone). (3) Every list is written with [] and x :: xs. Every cell estimates at most 266 against the 272 x-compat budget. Landing order: the split lands first (proof 6, job-1793050330342), then wire v3 with an e2e -Accept for the defused tutorial outputs. That still waits on the compiler lane's x-compat patch (post-5n gate (a)).")
inl note_of_x_defuse_links_only_15 () = Note ("X-DEFUSE-LINKS-ONLY", 15u32, "2026-10-26T23:13Z", "x", "2026-10-26 19:13 x: wire v3 rehearsed on a fresh copy of the live tree with the frames split LANDED (POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS note 84): every anchor applies once, 0 lines over 120, 0 cells over 272 in any .spi. Its measured sizes from proof 5 still apply (domains 334, posted 866). Landing waits only on the compiler lane's x-compat patch (post-5n gate (a)) and then an e2e -Accept for the defused tutorial outputs.")
inl note_of_x_defuse_links_only_16 () = Note ("X-DEFUSE-LINKS-ONLY", 16u32, "2026-10-27T10:26Z", "x", "2026-10-27 06:26 x: verify14b (job-1793093686848): client gate GREEN (28 crates, 0 over 1000), x-compat 0 findings, e2e 153 requests GREEN. It still found two real defects and one stale fixture. (1) DEFECT in defuse.spi: ascii_of \"\" returned 256 (the non-ASCII code), so a run at the very start of a text (prev \"\") counted as blocked and its link dot was NOT defused. A node probe showed \"cell.app cell.app\" posting as \"cell.app cell<ZWSP>.app\". On X a link at the start of a post would stay live (autolink, weight 23), and the sim's x_compat_test \"autolinks never push a post over the limit\" went over for that reason. Fix: ascii_of \"\" = 0. (2) The tlds and defuse notebooks were never in apps/client/build.ps1's test list (wire v3 added only domains/domains), so defuse.spi's own text-start test case never ran. Both are in the list now. (3) Stale under links-only: reading.spi's mixed_cells posted \"go x.io now\", which stays plain now (a 1-letter label before a 2-letter TLD), so posted_frames_keep_no_trailing_space found no defused dot. It now posts \"go x.com now\". The sim frames test now expects \"listm<ZWSP>.map\" (.map is a real gTLD) and a plain \"x.io\". verify15 (job-1793096804459, syncs gated on REMOTE-SYNC-OK) reruns sim -Accept and both builds plain.")
