inl export_string_param_rc () = Task ("EXPORT-STRING-PARAM-RC", "codegenRust: an export taking a string param lowers to pub fn x(v0: &str){closure(Rc::from(v0))}, copying the arg on every cross-crate call; emit an Rc param (caller clones = refcount bump) for hot cross-crate exports to remove the copy", Story, P1, D3, 95u32, Blocked, "", "compiler,rust,codegen,export,performance,team:compiler,compiler", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_export_string_param_rc () = Planning ("EXPORT-STRING-PARAM-RC", Minutes 150u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_export_string_param_rc_1 () = Note ("EXPORT-STRING-PARAM-RC", 1u32, "2026-10-26T05:38Z", "compiler", "filed from kit's measurement 10-26: kit agile list calls one export per printed row with the whole 6.6 MB state, ~5 GB copying per full list (6-8s), 228 kit exports have this shape. Pairs with RUST-DERIVE-INFERENCE (both codegenRust export-signature). kit interim: index once per call (EOIE-STATE-SCALE note 15).")
inl note_of_export_string_param_rc_2 () = Note ("EXPORT-STRING-PARAM-RC", 2u32, "2026-10-26T16:50Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-26 (decision-prep; the DECISION is the user's/main's to commit -- this is the pre-registered rule + recommendation, build-independent). Evidence: codegenRust export lowering, spiral_compiler.fs:164721 (decl) + 164722 (call):\n  decl: `v{k}: &str`                      (for a YPrim StringT param)\n  call: `Rc::<str>::from(v{k})`           -> a FULL BYTE COPY of the string into a fresh Rc<str> on EVERY call.\nkit cost (note 1): agile list calls one export per printed row with the whole 6.6 MB state -> ~5 GB copied per full list (6-8 s), 228 kit exports have this shape.\n\nDECISION 1 -- param type (how the caller hands over the Rc without re-copying the bytes; the wrapped closure's domain is Spiral's Rc<str>):\n  (a) &Rc<str>  : caller passes a free BORROW of the one Rc it holds; export does `v{k}.clone()` (one refcount bump) to give the closure an owned Rc<str>. Zero byte copy. RECOMMENDED.\n  (b) Rc<str>   : caller passes an owned Rc -> the bump moves to EVERY call site (`export(state.clone())`); same copy elimination, worse ergonomics.\n  (c) keep &str : status-quo byte copy.\n  Recommend (a): for kit's hot pattern (one 6.6 MB state Rc borrowed into 228 exports per row) -> 0 byte copies, 228 pointer-sized bumps; if kit currently holds a String not an Rc, it pays ONE copy to build the Rc then borrows it N times (1 vs 228 copies). Cleanest call site: `export(&state)`.\n\nDECISION 2 -- scope:\n  HOT-ONLY (opt-in annotation), NOT all exports. Flipping every string-param export &str->&Rc<str> is a breaking API change for every consumer and hurts COLD callers passing a string literal (would need `&Rc::<str>::from(\"x\")` instead of `\"x\"`). So: keep `&str` default; an opt-in marker switches a hot export to `&Rc<str>`. The marker rides the SAME export-signature annotation mechanism as RUST-DERIVE-INFERENCE (note 1 pairs them; one per-crate/per-export marker drives both derive inference and the Rc-param opt-in).\n\nCHANGE LOCUS (when decided + a build slot): codegenRust 164721/164722, the `YPrim StringT ->` branch of the None (non-record) decl/call map -- for an annotated-hot export emit `v{k}: &Rc<str>` / `v{k}.clone()` instead of `v{k}: &str` / `Rc::<str>::from(v{k})`. Record-param exports (164714-719) and non-string params are unaffected. GATE: kit's agile-list full run drops from ~5 GB/6-8 s copying to refcount bumps (measure peak alloc / wall-clock); -Suite examples -Native exports stay green; the annotated hot exports compile at their call sites with `&state`.\nINTERIM (kit-side, already noted): index once per call (EOIE-STATE-SCALE note 15) -- a workaround; the compiler fix is the real removal. Est 150m holds. Pairs with RUST-DERIVE-INFERENCE for the shared annotation design -- decide both together.")
inl note_of_export_string_param_rc_3 () = Note ("EXPORT-STRING-PARAM-RC", 3u32, "2026-10-27T14:06Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-27 diagnosis (0% -> locus + fix pinned, build-independent; read codegenRust's Export builder, spiral_compiler.fs:164698-164742, the | None non-record param path).\n\nSTRING PARAM (the item's title): decl at :164721 emits `v{k}: &str`; call at :164722 emits `Rc::<str>::from(v{k})`. So `pub fn {name}(v0: &str) ... { closure()(Rc::<str>::from(v0)) }`. Rc::<str>::from(&str) HEAP-COPIES the string bytes into a new Rc<str> every call; a Rust consumer that already holds an Rc<str> (Spiral's internal string type) pays a needless re-allocation.\nFIX (recommended, low-risk): decl `v{k}: impl Into<Rc<str>>`, call `v{k}.into()`. An Rc<str>-holding caller passes it for free (Into<Rc<str>> for Rc<str> is identity); a &str/literal caller still works (allocates only when it must). codegenRust change at :164721-164722 only.\n\nRELATED (kit's ~5GB-per-list, note 1): the EXPORTED-RECORD path (:164714-164719) takes the record BY VALUE (`v: {record_name}` at :164719) -> a full record copy per call. If kit's 6.6 MB state is exported as a record, THAT by-value copy is the ~5GB, DISTINCT from the string param. A by-ref decl (`v: &{record_name}`, read fields through the ref) would avoid it, but the closure call (:164719 passes `v.{k}` by value) would then need each field borrowed/cloned -- confirm the closure's param ownership before changing (it may clone inside anyway, moving the cost not removing it).\n\nBoth are codegenRust export-signature (pairs with RUST-DERIVE-INFERENCE at the same emit site, per note 1). Build-bound to verify (wasm32/host cargo check + a kit consumer build + a copy-count or bench). DESIGN NOD NEEDED (item is main-tagged): the `impl Into<Rc<str>>` string fix is low-risk and self-contained; the record by-ref is a bigger signature change touching every record-export caller -- flag to main before drafting that half. The string half can draft + land independently once a slot frees.")
inl note_of_export_string_param_rc_4 () = Note ("EXPORT-STRING-PARAM-RC", 4u32, "2026-10-27T17:06Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-27 12:2x: STRING HALF drafted as a verified anchored pack (build-independent). apps/compiler/packs/apply-export-string-rc.ps1, 2 edits at the codegenRust export builder (| None non-record param path, spiral_compiler.fs:164721-164722): decl `v{k}: &str` -> `v{k}: impl Into<Rc<str>>`; call `Rc::<str>::from(v{k})` -> `v{k}.into()`. Both anchors match exactly-once on the live core; apply-proven on a scratch copy (new text in, old &str arm gone, 0 residue), scratch removed. Strictly more general than &str: identity for an Rc<str> caller, still accepts &str/String/Box<str>. codegenRust emit-site only; non-string params and the record-by-value path untouched. The RECORD-BY-REF half (v: {record_name} by value, :164719) stays main-tagged and is NOT in this pack. LAND-GATE (build-bound, NOT matrix-covered — Rust Export emits a crate with no main, E0601): host + wasm32 cargo check of an exported crate + a kit consumer build + a copy/bench count showing an Rc<str> caller no longer re-allocates. Re-anchor only if the codegenRust export builder changes (5n/5o do not touch it). Pairs with RUST-DERIVE-INFERENCE at the same emit site.")
inl note_of_export_string_param_rc_5 () = Note ("EXPORT-STRING-PARAM-RC", 5u32, "2026-10-29T14:07Z", "compiler", "10-29 10:07 compiler: DO NOT SHIP apps/compiler/packs/apply-export-string-rc.ps1 as drafted (n4: every string-param export &str -> impl Into<Rc<str>>). Measured with rustc 1.101.0-nightly on a 6-shape probe: impl Into<Rc<str>> accepts a &str literal, String, Rc<str>, &*String and Rc::clone(&rc), but REJECTS &String (E0277: Rc<str>: From<&String> not satisfied) and &Rc<str> (E0277: Rc<str>: From<&Rc<str>> not satisfied). Deref coercion does not apply through a generic bound, while today's &str parameter takes both silently. Spiral-generated Rust holds strings as Rc<str>, so a caller passing &rc, and any hand-written caller passing &string, would stop compiling. That is a breaking change for all 228 kit exports (n1). This vindicates n2's design: keep &str as the default and switch only annotated hot exports, where the caller is known to hold an Rc<str> (the &Rc<str> + clone form, option a). The applier stays unstaged and out of every pack until it is opt-in.")
inl note_of_export_string_param_rc_6 () = Note ("EXPORT-STRING-PARAM-RC", 6u32, "2026-10-29T17:45Z", "compiler", "10-29 13:5x compiler: OPT-IN DRAFTED, the replacement for the do-not-ship applier (n5). apps/compiler/packs/apply-export-rc-str-opt-in.ps1 (unstaged, -Core <file> [-Apply]), 2 exact anchors in codegenRust (shared section, so both cores). It stacks AFTER apply-rust-derive-inference.ps1 (pack 7): on the live 6b core it refuses. (1) A package's existing \rust-extern: field takes an entry \rc_str = <export>, <export>, ... (the same field and parser as RUST-DERIVE's derive = ...); it is excluded from derive_externs so it is never taken as an extern type. (2) The export builder's non-record param path: a listed export emits {k}: &Rc<str> and calls with {k}.clone(); every other export keeps &str + Rc::<str>::from(v{k}), so the 228 existing kit exports are byte-identical. Proof on a scratch copy of pack 7 (recomposed core 15FA7FF541B2862F = the node pack7 build's sourceSha16): 5 insertions / 3 deletions, pair-drift 8 pairs, 0 one-sided. Shape proof (rustc nightly-2026-10-18 -O2, scratchpad pack8-draft/rc-probe): a listed export called with &state hands the closure the SAME allocation (Rc::ptr_eq true, 0 bytes copied); an unlisted export still copies and still accepts a literal, &String and &Rc<str>; negative control: &String passed to a listed export is rejected (E0308), which is the documented contract: list only exports whose callers hold an Rc<str>. Left (build-bound, pack 8): a Spiral sample with an exported string fn plus rust-extern rc_str, compiled with the pack-8 compiler and cargo-checked from a caller crate (Export crates have no main, so the matrix does not cover them); then kit lists its hot exports in its spiproj and measures agile list (target: ~5 GB copied to 0).")
inl note_of_export_string_param_rc_7 () = Note ("EXPORT-STRING-PARAM-RC", 7u32, "2026-10-29T20:09Z", "compiler", "10-29 16:3x compiler: END-TO-END PROVEN, after the proof caught a real defect. Sample (C:\\home\\data\\pack8-drafts\\rust-export-rc-str): one string fn exported twice, hot_len listed in package.spiproj 'rust-extern: rc_str = hot_len', cold_len unlisted; a caller crate passes &state (Rc<str>), a literal and &String. FIRST RUN FAILED: the opt-in emitted 'v0.clone()', and the rustMoveOnLastUse post-pass stripped it as a last-use clone (it assumes vN owns its value), leaving 'closure0()(v0)' with v0: &Rc<str> -> rustc E0308. The earlier hand-written rustc shape proof (n6) could not see this: only the real pipeline output shows it. FIX in the applier: emit 'Rc::clone(v0)' (the same refcount bump; the post-pass only matches the vN.clone() suffix). SECOND RUN on candidate pack8a (pack 7 + ureq move-only + this, core 180F8E78208FAB8C): hot_len(v0: &Rc<str>) -> closure0()(Rc::clone(v0)); cold_len(v0: &str) -> closure1()(Rc::<str>::from(v0)), byte-identical to today; rustc --crate-type lib ok; the caller crate builds and exits 0. Control: the installed 6b rejects the unknown rust-extern field (PackageErrors), as expected before pack 7. Export crates have no main, so the matrix cannot carry this: the lib + caller harness in that folder IS the regression check (run it in pack 8's review). Left: kit lists its hot exports and measures agile list (target ~5 GB copied -> 0).")
inl note_of_export_string_param_rc_8 () = Note ("EXPORT-STRING-PARAM-RC", 8u32, "2026-10-30T16:58Z", "compiler", "10-30 12:56 compiler: INSTALLED AND ACCEPTED in pack 8 (main installed 11:46, core 17DDB2DCEF2A2D52 host EEBCBFF8658A1EF9, equal=True, node shipped; the live tree core 4191A77FB3D3A335 carries it; accept run single-flight-20261030-120324, PACK-8 n13). The compiler half is DONE: a rust-extern 'rc_str = <export>, ...' entry makes the listed exports take &Rc<str> and pass Rc::clone (n-proof 10-29: hot_len vs cold_len end to end). LEFT, owner kit: list the hot exports (the agile list row printers that receive the 6.6 MB state, n1/n2) under rust-extern rc_str in their package.spiproj, regen, and measure the copy drop. That adoption is this item's done rule; the compiler needs nothing more.")
inl note_of_export_string_param_rc_9 () = Note ("EXPORT-STRING-PARAM-RC", 9u32, "2026-10-31T00:21Z", "kit", "10-30 20:2x kit: ADOPTED and MEASURED (unstaged in kit/src); this was the item's done rule. The rust-extern rc_str entries: agile_planning_filters 'rc_str = kit_agile_planning_selected, kit_agile_planning_selected_near', agile_planning_fields 'rc_str = kit_agile_planning_minutes_near'. Every string parameter of a listed export becomes &Rc<str>, so all 3 call sites changed from &*!x to &!x (task_next.spi x2, task_listing.spi x1), and task_next's literal \"all\" got a binding, because a listed export rejects a &str literal. Generated line counts unchanged except task_next 967 -> 968. A/B on the live 8.5 MB backlog, with a control build identical except the adoption reverted, 3 runs each, outputs byte-identical: 'agile next 50' control 8.4/11.4/9.0 s vs rc_str 1.58/1.57/1.41 s, about 6x faster; 'agile list all --load light' unchanged at about 0.9 s (that path was already indexed per call, EOIE-STATE-SCALE n15). TRAIN5 note: the drafted agile_planning_gaps crate inlines task_next's macro; regenerated from its .spi it compiles (the draft holds no .rs, so the train regenerates it). Closes when the next train installs it.")
