inl eoie_table_empty_cells () = Task ("EOIE-TABLE-EMPTY-CELLS", "one rule for empty and non-numeric cells in numeric where comparisons, shared by table query, table test and the measured path; settled with tibia (its 10-24 report: an empty cell passes delay<=1000 in table query), then applied to every path with a flag if both readings are needed", Story, P1, D3, 100u32, Done, "LIB-TEXT-ORDER,LIB-TRY-PARSE-F64", "kit,table,tibia", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_eoie_table_empty_cells () = Planning ("EOIE-TABLE-EMPTY-CELLS", Minutes 120u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_eoie_table_empty_cells_1 () = Note ("EOIE-TABLE-EMPTY-CELLS", 1u32, "2026-10-26T12:28Z", "eoie", "08:28 eoie: opened from the rename preflight. Release-train stage S2 (10-24) meant 'table query --where numeric comparisons fail on cells that are not numbers' (tibia's report), but its program changed only cell_passes, which table test uses (rows_kept), not the host closure plain table query filters with. So tibia's query case is still unfixed, and table test silently flipped against EOIE-TABLE-TEST-WHERE #1 (10-21: foreign_potions<=0 keeps empty cells as tibia expects). table_test_cli caught it: n=a 1 against the expected n=a 2 (candidate and control both; installed eoie gives a 2). Decided for the release: revert S2's cell_passes change (scratch clean\\kit-rename\\table-empty-cells-revert.do), keeping the test block as documentation of the installed behaviour (an empty cell compares as text). Next, with tibia: pick one rule (likely: non-numeric cells fail numeric comparisons everywhere, with --keep-empty for the potions case), apply it to query_rows, rows_kept and measures in one change, and update both CLI notebooks.")
inl note_of_eoie_table_empty_cells_2 () = Note ("EOIE-TABLE-EMPTY-CELLS", 2u32, "2026-10-26T12:30Z", "eoie", "08:30 eoie: tibia answered (10-26 08:3x). The rule fits: every one of its table query filters (at<200, run>1, run>2, gated>0, delay<=1000) wants an empty cell to FAIL; its table test calls take no --where; the 10-21 foreign_potions<=0 ask has no caller left (live/ retired 10-25), so --keep-empty needs no caller and is optional. Revert for the release accepted. Rule for the follow-up, settled: a non-numeric or empty cell fails every numeric comparison (>, >=, <, <=) in table query's plain path (query_rows host closure), table test (cell_passes via rows_kept) and the measured path, in one change; = and != stay text comparisons; table_test_cli's where fixture gets a second kept a row so its n stays meaningful, and table_cli gains an empty-cell case for delay<=1000.")
inl note_of_eoie_table_empty_cells_3 () = Note ("EOIE-TABLE-EMPTY-CELLS", 3u32, "2026-10-26T22:54Z", "tibia-lane", "22:54Z tibia design for the settled rule, written before drafting (read from eoie src at 19:0x local; the release holds the tree lease, so nothing is edited there).\n\nSites: there are three comparison stacks, not one.\n(a) table.spi query_rows: one host block. Its own compare closure falls back to text order for non-numbers.\n(b) table.spi cell_passes, used through rows_kept by table test and analysis: numbers_ordered, then texts_ordered.\n(c) kit_table_measures/measures.spi: a second copy of the whole stack (compare union, filters_from, row_passes, ordered with partial_cmp and a text fallback).\n\nAGENTS.md says touching a legacy function migrates it, so (a) moves to Spiral rather than getting a patched closure.\n\nPlan, in one change: a new module kit_table_query/wheres.spi holds where_op, where_op_of, the clause split, the single cell_passes (rule: >, >=, <, <= hold only when both sides parse as finite numbers; = and != stay text) and filters/filters_from/row_passes. table.spi (rows_kept and the migrated plain query) and measures.spi import it, and measures' copy is deleted.\n\nBehaviour kept byte-identical: header line, projection, limit, the 'kit table query ok total= matched= [groups=] shown=' lines, --quiet, the unknown-columns text and its order (filters, cols, group), and the read-error text (check kit_table_text::kit_table_problem against the old '{path}: {error}').\n\nKnown deltas, to be stated in the change:\n- A ragged row's missing cell now reads as \"\" in plain query, as table test already does. Before, every filter failed on it; now only = against a non-empty value and the numeric operators fail.\n- A no-operator clause can't reach any path: option parsing rejects it ('--where expects col=value').\n\nBlocker found: grouped output sorts by count descending, then value ascending. Spiral has no string ordering (`<` on strings is ETY-ConstraintError; tibia hit it today in a test), and lib's only code-point order is private to xpath_eval (text_order_from). So a lib slice comes first: a general text_order (a, b) : i32, either moved out of xpath_eval or added as a backend_switch with Rust str::cmp, plus a lib test.\n\nTests: table.spi's documented test becomes 'an empty or text cell fails every numeric comparison', expecting (!-, !-, !-, !+, !-) on (\"\" <= 1000, \"\" < 1001, \"\" > 1000, 999 <= 1000, abc < abd). table_cli gains latency_ms<=50000 on runs.tsv (row 2's latency is empty): matched=1, where the installed binary gives matched=2. table_test_cli's where fixture gets a second kept row.\n\nProof path: build the candidate kit on the remote kit worker node from a src copy (copy-eoie-src-only), run table_cli and table_test_cli against it, and hand the anchored apply script to the eoie lane after the release.")
inl note_of_eoie_table_empty_cells_4 () = Note ("EOIE-TABLE-EMPTY-CELLS", 4u32, "2026-10-26T23:44Z", "tibia-lane", "23:44Z tibia, design addendum (19:4x local).\nkit confirmed nothing of theirs has this in flight. The apply script anchors on the tree after kit's land-post-rename slices (several touch kit_table_query) and builds on the quoted-CSV fix; kit messages \"slices landed\".\n\nPrerequisites found while drafting the wheres module (lib-first per AGENTS.md: the touched host one-liners migrate to lib):\n1. LIB-TEXT-ORDER, for grouped output's value order: proven, gating as job-1793057877440.\n2. An optional float parse, for cell_passes' numeric test. No lib function returns option f64: float_native.parse assumes valid text, and convert.try_convert parses integers only. The Rust arm must match today's trim().parse::<f64>().ok(), including inf/NaN, which parse as numbers (NaN then fails every comparison, as now). Shape: float_native.try_parse_f64 (s : string) : option f64, a backend_switch with Rust one-call plus Some/None in Spiral, and a rust test over \"12.5\", \" 3 \", \"\", \"abc\", \"inf\".\n3. The clause split (operator_at, operator_width) and substrings come from sm' (index_of, substring), not host macros.\n\nUnchanged: --where without an operator is rejected at option parsing, so the paths' differing no-operator handling never matters. Order of work: lib slices 1-2 gate, then the eoie change against the post-slice tree, proven by table_cli and table_test_cli run against a candidate kit built on the node.")
inl note_of_eoie_table_empty_cells_5 () = Note ("EOIE-TABLE-EMPTY-CELLS", 5u32, "2026-10-27T00:17Z", "tibia-lane", "00:17Z tibia: code drafted in scratch eoie-table-empty-cells/draft. It is unproven, waits for kit's \"slices landed\", and needs the rebase anchors.\n- wheres.spi (new module, listed before table in kit_table_query's spiproj): where_op, the clause split over sm' (operator_from as a join point, operator_width, clause_of returning option clause), and cell_passes. The ordered ops compare float_native.try_parse_f64 results directly, and a non-number on either side fails. There is a NaN fix in passing: query_rows made every NaN comparison false, but cell_passes mapped NaN to 'equal' so <= passed; one rule now. Two rust tests, then filters/filters_from/row_passes.\n- plain-query.spi (replaces query_rows): projection (all columns or named, missing names appended in order), row_text, listed_rows counting total/matched/shown under below_limit, and grouped counting (key_rows counts plus a first-seen list, ranked by count descending then xml.text_order ascending, the same order as the old BTreeMap sort by byte).\nOpen before the rebase:\n(1) a cells.spi boundary primitive row_width (one host call) for all-columns projection;\n(2) whether ?= takes 'when' guards, or printed_groups needs an if;\n(3) for groups, matched = the sum of counts;\n(4) check that kit_table_problem's text equals the old '{path}: {error}', so 'kit error: table ...' is byte-identical;\n(5) measures.spi drops its compare/filters copy and imports wheres;\n(6) re-lay the three lines over 120 at the rebase.\nProof: table_cli and table_test_cli on a candidate kit built on the node, plus the new empty-cell case (latency_ms<=50000 gives matched=1).")
inl note_of_eoie_table_empty_cells_6 () = Note ("EOIE-TABLE-EMPTY-CELLS", 6u32, "2026-10-27T00:55Z", "tibia-lane", "00:55Z tibia: the draft is proven behaviourally before the rebase (scratch eoie-table-empty-cells/probe; compiler only, no kit build, done during kit's rename pause).\nThe probe package holds a lib copy with LIB-TEXT-ORDER and LIB-TRY-PARSE-F64 applied, copies of rust_std_string, union_reflection, query.spi and cells.spi (plus the new row_width primitive), the draft wheres.spi and plain.spi, and two probe mains, compiled to Rust and built with rustc. kit_table_text is stubbed with a plain file read.\n- wheres: printed '11 00 11 latency_ms|50000'. Numeric ops hold on numbers; empty and text cells fail <= and <; = and != stay text; the clause split works. Under the old text-fallback rule the middle pair would print 11.\n- plain query on table_cli's runs.tsv:\n  - latency_ms<=50000 with cols variant,latency_ms prints 'fast<TAB>40000', group '1<TAB>fast', matched=1 (the empty latency now fails; the installed binary gives matched=2).\n  - With all columns, '3<TAB>fast<TAB>9<TAB>40000'.\n  - With no filter, 4 rows, groups '3 fast' then '1 base', matched=4.\n  - Tie fixture b,z,a,z,B: groups '2 z', then '1 B', '1 a', '1 b', i.e. byte order like the old BTreeMap sort.\n- Partial evaluation pruned the all-columns branch while cols was a literal. Reading cols from the environment forced it in, and the generated Rust contains row_width.\nTop-level plain_query is now drafted (header, 'kit table query ok' lines with and without groups, unknown-columns error, --quiet) and run through query-probe.ps1 on table_cli's assertions. All match byte for byte: 'variant TAB retries / fast 5 / fast 9 / total=4 matched=2 shown=2'; variant!=base grouped gives matched=3; group variant gives 'count TAB variant / 3 fast / 1 base / total=4 matched=4 groups=2 shown=2'; '--cols nope' gives 'kit error: table unknown columns: nope (header: timestamp,variant,retries,latency_ms)' exit 2; --quiet hides the summary. New: latency_ms<=50000 gives matched=1. measures.spi with its private compare/filters/row_passes stack deleted and wheres imported (measures-probe.ps1) matches table_cli's measured rows byte for byte: '3 fast 5.333 46120 3.512 92240' and '1 base 4 60000 0 60000'; retries>=4 gives '2 fast 7' and '1 base 4'. New: latency_ms<=50000 with --count gives '1 fast 1', matched=1. Its read errors already go through table_read and rejected, like plain_query. Read errors: kit_table_text's table_problem only checks existence ('{path}: file not found', with its own rust test), while old query_rows printed the OS error text ('{path}: The system cannot find the file specified. (os error 2)'). Plain query now shares table_problem with the measured path and table test: a stated delta that no CLI notebook pins. Still open: then the rebase onto kit's post-slice tree and the CLI notebooks on a candidate kit.\nCLI notebook edits drafted (draft/cli-test-edits.ps1; anchors match once on today's tree, re-check after the rebase). table_test_cli's where fixture gains 'a 13 0 4', so errors<=0 keeps 'n=a 2, b 2' through a numeric row; the installed binary would show n=a 3 there, because the empty cell compares as text. Its passed-metric case becomes 'n=a 4 (3 numeric), b 3'. table_cli gains latency_ms<=50000 with expected 'kit table query ok total=4 matched=1 shown=1'.")
inl note_of_eoie_table_empty_cells_7 () = Note ("EOIE-TABLE-EMPTY-CELLS", 7u32, "2026-10-27T08:03Z", "tibia-lane", "08:03Z IN THE TREE (unstaged, about 03:55 local), on kit's post-slice src, per the user's WIP-in-place rule.\nFiles: kit_table_query/wheres.spi (new), table.spi (query_rows' host block replaced by a Spiral plain_query; the private where stack moved to wheres), cells.spi (+ row_width), package.spiproj (wheres), kit_table_measures/measures.spi (its private stack removed; imports wheres), table_cli.spi and table_test_cli.spi (the new cases).\nChecks done: layout clean after a formatter re-lay of my 6 long lines; table and measures compile to Rust from the tree files (probe package with the live lib), and the generated code carries row_width, xml.text_order and try_parse_f64, with no text-fallback compare left. Earlier probes matched every table_cli assertion byte for byte and showed the new rule (matched=1).\nOpen: table_cli and table_test_cli on a candidate kit built from this tree, asked of kit's build path. Prerequisites LIB-TEXT-ORDER and LIB-TRY-PARSE-F64 are landed (Done). Backup: tibia scratch eoie-table-empty-cells/tree-before-035516.")
inl note_of_eoie_table_empty_cells_8 () = Note ("EOIE-TABLE-EMPTY-CELLS", 8u32, "2026-10-27T11:01Z", "tibia-lane", "11:01Z LINE GATE FIX (07:0x). kit-release-SLICES refused kit_table_query/table_query.rs at 1,182 lines (limit 1,000), regenerated from this layer. Ranked: table_run_0 252 (the inlined plain path), filters_from 114, option_applied 100, row_passes 83, with no duplicated copies, so the crate was simply too big. A Spiral-package split wouldn't help (imported packages compile into the consumer); a crate boundary does.\nDone in kit's tree, unstaged: new crate src/kit_table_plain (plain.spi, package.spiproj, Cargo.toml modeled on kit_table_measures) holding the plain query path, migrated off concat5 to sm'_operators ++#, exported as kit_table_plain(&str x4, u64, bool) -> i32. table.spi calls it with a one-expression macro like measured_query. Tab-joined output lines live in a kit_table_query module, lines.spi, so the new package has no encoded rows. Wiring: lines module in the kit_table_query spiproj, the kit-table-plain Cargo dep, and the src/Cargo.toml member.\nProof (Spiral to Rust on a fresh copy of the live lib): table_query.rs 708 lines, plain.rs 769 lines, and table_query calls kit_table_plain. Release requeue asked of kit; its table_cli/table_test_cli run is the behavior check.\nFollow-up drafted (scratch lib-tsv-line/lib-tsv-line.ps1, dry-run clean): lib csv.tsv_line (fields : list string) over sm'.concat_list, with a test block. Once landed, lines.spi drops its 2 bare tab literals (kit_table_query has no census baseline, so the edit hook calls it a new package with encoded rows).")
inl note_of_eoie_table_empty_cells_9 () = Note ("EOIE-TABLE-EMPTY-CELLS", 9u32, "2026-10-27T19:51Z", "tibia-lane", "19:51Z Follow-up applied after kit's window closed (10-27 15:50 local): kit src/kit_table_query/lines.spi now uses lib csv.tsv_line for row_text, group_line and group_header. The 2 bare tab literals and the sm'_operators import are gone; the file goes F0BBA368A7FF -> 756CA76CC2B8, unstaged. The output text is unchanged (\"\" for no fields, the cell alone for one, tab-joined otherwise). It type-checks on Rust in a scratch copy of kit_table_query, rust_std_string and union_reflection with lib staged as src/spiral; the live file passes the same check as the control. Applied with scratch eoie-table-empty-cells/apply-lines-tsv.ps1, a live-hash guard that refuses a second run (checked). Kit has been told; behaviour is checked by kit's table_cli/table_test_cli in its next release run.")
inl note_of_eoie_table_empty_cells_10 () = Note ("EOIE-TABLE-EMPTY-CELLS", 10u32, "2026-10-30T01:58Z", "kit", "2026-10-30T01:58 kit groom: deps settled: LIB-TEXT-ORDER and LIB-TRY-PARSE-F64 are both Done, so this item is unblocked at 80%. The last follow-up (tibia, 10-27 15:50) applied the kit_table_query/lines.spi change. Next: verify the one empty/non-numeric cell rule on the installed kit (C528BE4D), then close; not run in this groom.")
inl note_of_eoie_table_empty_cells_11 () = Note ("EOIE-TABLE-EMPTY-CELLS", 11u32, "2026-10-30T11:12Z", "tibia-lane", "10-30 07:3x LIVE CHECK on the installed kit C528BE4D (scratch empty-cells.tsv: a=500, b='', c=abc, d=2000, e=1000). query: delay<=1000 -> a,e (matched=2); delay>0 -> a,d,e; delay<1001 -> a,e; delay= -> b; name!=a -> b,c,d,e (text). measured: --where delay<=5000 --mean delay -> n 3, mean 1166.667. test: one-sample --where delay<=5000 -> n=all 3. One rule on all three paths: an empty or non-numeric cell fails every numeric comparison, and = / != stay text. Closing.")
inl eoie_open_shadow_lint () = Task ("EOIE-OPEN-SHADOW-LINT", "eoie: lint for top-level names that shadow a name from a module the file opens with ':<'", Story, P2, D2, 80u32, Planned, "", "kit,lint,spiral,team:kit", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_eoie_open_shadow_lint () = Planning ("EOIE-OPEN-SHADOW-LINT", Minutes 90u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_eoie_open_shadow_lint_1 () = Note ("EOIE-OPEN-SHADOW-LINT", 1u32, "2026-10-26T13:54Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 09:54 fiscal: request from the field. Shadowing a name from an opened module is a recurring silent bug class: fiscal F1 'census_of' shadowed xsd_regex.census_of (an existing caller broke, far from the edit); R5's 'node_name' / 'node_text' shadowed xml's functions (latent trap); TIBIA-AGENT's 'index' counter shadowed the array index function; PACKAGE-REGISTRY's ':< materialize' resolved to a type alias. Stopgap used today: session scratchpad shadow-check/shadow_check.py <base>=<draft> ... (names the draft adds vs its base; each checked against every ':<' module's top-level definitions; 0 false positives across 10 drafts). Wanted: one eoie verb, e.g. 'eoie spiral shadows <file> [--base <file>]', exit non-zero on a clash, usable in the PostToolUse style hook. Done = the verb + a Spiral test block per clash kind (value, nominal, union case, alias).")
inl note_of_eoie_open_shadow_lint_2 () = Note ("EOIE-OPEN-SHADOW-LINT", 2u32, "2026-10-31T15:29Z", "kit", "10-31 11:2x kit: BUILT (unstaged), shipping in TRAIN7. Verb: 'kit lint shadows <root> [<path>...] [--baseline <rows>] --opens [--lib <dir>]' adds rows 'file:line  name  open-shadow  from <module>' for a top-level definition whose name, in the same namespace (term or type), is also defined by a module the file opens with a top-level ':<'. 'main' is exempt. Module resolution: 'pkg.mod' -> <root>/pkg/mod.spi; a bare 'mod' -> the file's own folder, else <lib>/mod.spi, where lib comes from --lib or SPIRAL_LIB (no project paths baked in). Unresolved modules contribute nothing. Rows honour --baseline like the other kinds and count toward 'new', but only when --opens is given, so existing runs are unchanged. Code: pure clash logic in kit_lint_shadows_core (opened_modules, open_clashes, test a_definition_named_like_an_opened_member_is_a_finding: term and type clashes found, a different namespace and main not), plus a NEW crate kit_lint_opens (resolution and rows; test an_opened_package_module_is_resolved_and_its_names_checked, with a temp-folder fixture, a missing module and a baseline-suppressed rerun), called once from kit_lint_shadows. The first attempt put everything in the two existing crates and blew the gate (core 731 -> 1180, runner 777 -> 1037), hence the hand-modeled split. Now: core 731, kit_lint_opens 922, runner 847; regen plus cargo check pass; notebooks shadows.spi 6/6 and opens.spi 1/1. PROTOTYPE census before building (PowerShell): kit/src has 13 findings in 7 files (e.g. kit_agile_mutation and kit_agile_routes redefine i32_eq/i32_le/i32_nonnegative from agile_mutation_policy.model; kit_log_counts' test fixture 'trace' shadows section_scan.trace; kit_cli_model's expect shadows command_spec_domain's), lib/spiral has 5 in 1 file, and fiscal/tibia 0 (their layouts may not resolve with the src/<pkg> rule). ACCEPTANCE: release-acceptance.ps1 row 'lint shadows --opens names a shadowed opened member' (fixture pkg/a.spi opens pkg.b and redefines helper) FAILS on the installed E14F1F0B as expected and must pass on TRAIN7. Done once installed plus fiscal's original repro.")
inl note_of_eoie_open_shadow_lint_3 () = Note ("EOIE-OPEN-SHADOW-LINT", 3u32, "2026-10-31T15:55Z", "kit", "10-31 12:0x kit: first fixes from the prototype census, ahead of the verb shipping. kit_agile_mutation/agile_mutation.spi and kit_agile_routes/routes.spi each carried byte-identical copies of i32_eq, i32_le and i32_nonnegative, which they also open from agile_mutation_policy.model. The local copies are removed (i32_positive, which is unique, stays). Behaviour is unchanged, and each package loses one host macro ($\"0 <= !value\"). Census: kit_agile_routes ratchets ok. kit_agile_mutation was rebaselined, and the reason was checked: plain 'git diff' compares against the INDEX, and the user's staged kit changes already raise its indented host macros 18 -> 20 against HEAD, while this edit took its top-level macros 17 -> 14 and left the indented count alone. So the rebaseline records drift already staged, plus a decrease. Verification (notebooks/regen) is queued with the respell batch until TRAIN6 frees the CPU. Remaining findings, left for review: kit_cli_model's expect (a test helper over command_spec_domain's), kit_log_counts' test fixture 'trace' over section_scan.trace (a test-only name), agile_task_census i32_text, agile_task_revision task_arguments_offset, and bundle_verify_contract preflight_binding/complete_binding.")
inl note_of_eoie_open_shadow_lint_4 () = Note ("EOIE-OPEN-SHADOW-LINT", 4u32, "2026-11-03T07:30Z", "kit", "kit groom 11-03 03:3x (WIP cap, memory wip-cap-active-means-touched): Active -> Planned, last note 64 h ago by kit, progress kept at 80%. Resume from that note: r command_spec_domain's), kit_log_counts' test fixture 'trace' over section_scan.trace (a test-only name), agile_task_census i32_text, agile_task_revision task_arguments_offset, and bundle_verify_contract preflight_binding/complete_binding.")
inl rust_string_wrapper_inline () = Task ("RUST-STRING-WRAPPER-INLINE", "lib string wrappers (trim/substring) inline per callsite in codegenRust, bloating generated Rust past the 1k gate; make hot wrappers join-point methods so each emits one shared fn called per use instead of inlining", Story, P1, D3, 0u32, Planned, "RUST-INLINE-SINGLE-USE", "compiler,rust,codegen,size,lib,wipe-redo,team:compiler", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_rust_string_wrapper_inline () = Planning ("RUST-STRING-WRAPPER-INLINE", Minutes 150u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_rust_string_wrapper_inline_1 () = Note ("RUST-STRING-WRAPPER-INLINE", 1u32, "2026-10-26T15:37Z", "compiler", "filed from kit's measurement 10-26: kit_lint_macros 461 Spiral -> 2,056 Rust (gate 1,000). Mechanism (spiral_compiler.fs): the StringSlice intrinsic is ALREADY one shared fn string_slice (164434/164749); the bloat is lib WRAPPERS (trim/substring, sm'.spi:1321) being plain := functions that Spiral inlines whole (bound casts, Rc clones, empty-string thread_local) at every callsite. codegenRust already has the shared-helper mechanism: a METHOD join point emits one top-level fn method_N(...) called per use (164966, self-tail -> loop). Fix is LIB-LEVEL + no compiler build: put :~/join on the big, frequently-called wrappers so they land as methods. VERIFY the regen shows top-level fn <name>(...) {} (method, win) NOT Rc::new(move|...| ) / fn ->Rc<dyn Fn> (closure path at 165003, heavy: box + thread_local, WORSE). kit's :~ experiment grew because it was applied to 11 tiny 1-3 line helpers (inlining those is cheaper) and/or they became closures not methods. operator_between is kit-local, not lib. Distinct from EXPORT-STRING-PARAM-RC (that's export signatures). Reject: making trim/substring F# intrinsics (duplicates logic across ~15 backends, violates granular-style). Full analysis: scratchpad/lib-string-op-codegen-size.md.")
inl note_of_rust_string_wrapper_inline_2 () = Note ("RUST-STRING-WRAPPER-INLINE", 2u32, "2026-10-26T17:09Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-26 (concrete lib edit for the codegen-size win, build-independent analysis of lib/spiral/sm'.spi). The bloaty string wrappers kit measured are OUTER `:=` functions that inline their whole chain per callsite:\n- trim (sm'.spi:1321): `:= trim (input:string):string = input |> trim_with []`\n- trim_with (:1289): `:= trim_with (chars)(input):string = input |> trim_start chars |> trim_end chars`\n- trim_start (:1217) / trim_end (:1253): `:=` outer; their INNER recursive loops are ALREADY `:~ ^ 루프` (so the loops already emit as shared methods -- good). Only the outer wrappers inline.\n- substring (:988): `:= substring (start:i32)(len:i32)(str:string):string = run_target \\| | Rust => \\ () => ... slice ...` (a backend_switch via run_target).\n\nEDIT (draft for kit to try, NOT yet applied -- lib is live-tree, Cells reads it, so land as a separate gated change): change the OUTER wrappers from `:=` to `:~` so each emits ONCE as a shared method called per use, instead of inlining the chain at every callsite: trim, trim_with, trim_start, trim_end, substring. All are monomorphic (string->string; substring i32->i32->string->string) and called in statement position, so they should land as METHODS (shared `fn`), not boxed closures. VERIFY on regen: `fn <name>(...)` emitted once + `<name>(...)` at each call (WIN); if you see `Rc::new(move|...)` / `fn ... -> Rc<dyn Fn>` it became a closure (misfire -- back it out). substring caveat: its body is a `run_target` backend_switch with `\\ () =>` arms; a `:~` wraps the resolved dispatch as a method -- should be fine but is the one most likely to misfire, check it first.\n\nSCOPE CAVEAT (important): sm'.spi is SHARED lib -- making these `:~` changes codegen for EVERY consumer across ALL backends, not just kit_lint_macros. So this is NOT a kit-crate-local change: it needs the full `-Suite all -Native` oracle-parity gate + a corpus check (lib + tibia + Kino compile, per lib-smoke-before-syntax) and must accept the perf trade-off (a method call vs inlining) on any HOT trim/substring use elsewhere. For cold lint/verb crates the size win dominates; for a hot inner loop the call overhead is a (tiny) regression. If the all-consumers trade-off is unwanted, the alternative is a crate-local wrapper kit marks `:~` rather than changing lib. Build-blocked (node committed ~8h to kit-release-FOLLOWUP); kit owns the call on whether the lib-wide change is worth it vs a crate-local wrapper.")
inl note_of_rust_string_wrapper_inline_3 () = Note ("RUST-STRING-WRAPPER-INLINE", 3u32, "2026-10-26T20:15Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-26 (DECISION recorded; item parked). kit decided: NO lib-wide :~ change to sm'.trim/trim_with/trim_start/trim_end/substring for kit's sake. kit measured the crate-local alternative on the node (kit-lint-wrappers2, lint4 mirror): lint's OWN trim/substring/starts_with/replace put through four local :~ wrappers -> each emitted as ONE fn called 6-25x, NOT a boxed closure (confirms this item's note-1/note-2 method-vs-closure prediction empirically). But lint.rs went 2,056 -> 2,005 lines = only 2.5%. So the structural bloat is not wrapper-inlining; kit splits the lint crate into hand-modeled build units instead (GRANULAR-LINT note 17), which is the real size fix.\n\nCompiler CONCURS with parking the lib-wide change: >=2.5% best case (crate-local ceiling; the lib change can't beat it inside one crate) does NOT justify a full -Suite all -Native oracle-parity gate + a per-consumer hot-path call-overhead trade-off on every trim/substring use across all backends. The draft (scratchpad/rswi-lib-draft.txt) is PARKED, not deleted: the lib-wide :~ edit is proven-sound and ready, but is NOT blocked by kit and should only be revived if a DIFFERENT consumer wants the size win for its own reasons and accepts the all-consumers perf trade-off. Status -> parked (resolve-as-wont-do for the lib-wide scope; crate-local/build-unit split is the chosen path). Pairs still with EXPORT-STRING-PARAM-RC only on the shared annotation mechanism, which is a separate open item.")
inl note_of_rust_string_wrapper_inline_4 () = Note ("RUST-STRING-WRAPPER-INLINE", 4u32, "2026-10-31T20:29Z", "compiler", "10-31 10-31 16:29 compiler, pause re-decision (KIT-PAUSE-RULE-REWORK): Paused -> PLANNED, back in agile next. It was paused by the 3-day silence rule, not by a decision; per-callsite inlining of lib string wrappers bloats Rust output; it belongs with the Rust-cost gate items (RUST-MOVE-ON-LAST-USE, RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION).")
inl kit_job_front_ticket () = Task ("KIT-JOB-FRONT-TICKET", "job start --heavy --front: queue a job first in the heavy line with a ticket owned by its own supervisor", Story, P2, D3, 50u32, Planned, "KIT-RENAME,EOIE-RELEASE-TRAIN", "kit,jobs,slots,team:kit", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_kit_job_front_ticket () = Planning ("KIT-JOB-FRONT-TICKET", Minutes 300u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_kit_job_front_ticket_1 () = Note ("KIT-JOB-FRONT-TICKET", 1u32, "2026-10-26T17:05Z", "kit", "13:05 kit: Found while launching kit-release-FOLLOWUP (KIT-RENAME note 44). The coordinator agreed it should go first, but job start --heavy can't do that. The supervisor's slot gate (kit_job_slot slot_gate) always stamps its ticket with the current ticks and overwrites SPIRAL_HEAVY_TICKET. A pre-made ticket owned by another live pid would be first forever and stall the box. The workaround was a plain job plus a hand-written heavy-wrapper.ps1 (scratch clean\\respell\\launch-FOLLOWUP.ps1 -Ticket), which also skips the window gate's bookkeeping. Shape: job start --heavy --front. ticket_created takes a stamp one tick before the current live head, still owned by the supervisor's pid, so the ticket dies with the job. Every other gate step is unchanged. The job's slot file and job list say 'front'. Milestones: (1) slot.spi stamp parameter, with a test block where a front ticket sorts first and a dead-owner front ticket is skipped; (2) the --front flag through job_start_args and start_shape; (3) one-call spec case + release. Rule for using it: only after the lane coordinator agrees the order, never by default.")
inl note_of_kit_job_front_ticket_2 () = Note ("KIT-JOB-FRONT-TICKET", 2u32, "2026-10-26T19:42Z", "kit", "15:42 kit: design read against the tree (no edits; the tree is held by FOLLOWUP). The options travel as positional values through four packages: kit_job_start_args start_args (a 14-tuple Export), kit_job_launch (re-splats them into kit_job_create's 14-argument Export), kit_job_create (writes job files such as slot_by_ms), then kit_job_slot/kit_job_gate read the files. The style contract says touching a legacy function means migrating it, so --front is not a one-flag change. Plan: (1) carry start_options as one typed record from start_args through launch into create (a record Export instead of 14 positionals), with a front field set by a width-1 flag FrontStart, refused unless the shape is HeavyStart; (2) create writes job file 'front'; (3) slot_gate stamps the ticket one tick before the live head when 'front' exists, owner still the supervisor pid; (4) tests: a windows_cli fixture with two queued heavy jobs where the --front one is admitted first, and a dead-owner front ticket that gets skipped. Re-estimated from these milestones: 300m (the record migration of the four Export boundaries is most of it).")
inl note_of_kit_job_front_ticket_3 () = Note ("KIT-JOB-FRONT-TICKET", 3u32, "2026-10-27T13:55Z", "kit", "09:5x kit, design correction before building (learned tonight from three crate splits): note 2's 'carry start_options as one typed record ... a record Export instead of 14 positionals' cannot work. A !!!!Export signature carries only FFI shapes: scalars, strings, arrays (Rc<RefCell<Vec<T>>>) and tuples of them. Spiral records and unions are private generated types (US0, UH1 ...). Revised plan: inside each of start_args, launch and create, migrate the legacy positional handling to one local typed record (start_options) and keep the Export boundary as the positional tuple, widened by one bool 'front' (the boundary stays scalar, as kit's style contract asks of boundaries). create writes job file 'front'; slot_gate stamps the ticket one tick before the live head when 'front' exists; the owner is still the supervisor pid. The tests are unchanged from note 2. Current sizes (headroom for the record and one field): start_args.rs 578, launch.rs 593, create.rs 341, slot.rs 358. Starts after kit-release-SLICES lands, so the four crates rebuild once with the other staged changes.")
inl note_of_kit_job_front_ticket_4 () = Note ("KIT-JOB-FRONT-TICKET", 4u32, "2026-10-27T14:06Z", "kit", "10:1x kit: built on a probe copy and staged (clean/kit-rename/job-front-ticket, 4 files with base hashes; applied by staged-apply.ps1 after kit-release-SLICES). start_args: FrontStart is a width-1 flag '--front' that sets start_options.front (already a typed record inside the package); it is refused unless the shape is HeavyStart ('--front applies to --heavy jobs: it queues the job first in the heavy line'); the Export tuple gains one bool. launch threads front into create through both job_launched callers (launched_text passes false). create takes front and writes job file 'front' (folded into the existing write-error refusal). slot: front_name (pure) builds {head_ticks-1:020}-{pid}.t from the live head ticket, so it sorts first; one-expression primitives ticket_ticks, own_pid and ticket_written; slot_gate takes front_ticket when the job's 'front' file exists, and falls back to a normal ticket when the queue has no live head. A dead-owner front ticket is skipped by the existing first_live_ticket (pid liveness). The owner stays the supervisor pid. Test block: a_front_ticket_sorts_one_tick_before_the_head. Sizes: start_args 616 (was 578), launch 594 (593), create 356 (341), slot 448 (358). Not yet proven: the live two-job ordering (note 2's windows_cli fixture) needs the built candidate; it runs after the stage lands and the next release builds.")
inl note_of_kit_job_front_ticket_5 () = Note ("KIT-JOB-FRONT-TICKET", 5u32, "2026-10-30T01:58Z", "kit", "2026-10-30T01:58 kit groom (user, via main 21:54): STATE CHECK after the wipe. The source has it (kit_job_start_args/start_args.spi:28 FrontStart '--front', :179 refusal '--front applies to --heavy jobs...'; kit_job_slot/slot.spi also references it), but installed kit C528BE4D answers 'kit job start --front' with the generic job usage, not that refusal, and its usage text lists no --front. So it is in the tree, not in the shipped binary (the usage/dispatch path is not wired or not regenerated). Progress stays 50. Next train: wire it, and accept when 'kit job start --front' returns the --heavy refusal and a --heavy --front job is queued first.")
inl note_of_kit_job_front_ticket_6 () = Note ("KIT-JOB-FRONT-TICKET", 6u32, "2026-11-01T03:37Z", "kit", "10-31 23:4x kit groom (main 23:33, user: cadence/drift): Active -> PLANNED. No note in over 12 h, so nobody is working it this week; Planned keeps it in agile next at its priority (never auto-Paused, R3). First step when picked = the next step in its latest note.")
inl rust_unused_pattern_clones () = Task ("RUST-UNUSED-PATTERN-CLONES", "Rust codegen: skip the let vN = vN.clone() for union-arm fields the arm body never reads (bind them as _ in the match pattern)", TaskKind, P3, D3, 0u32, Planned, "", "compiler,rust,codegen,line-gate,x,team:compiler", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_rust_unused_pattern_clones () = Planning ("RUST-UNUSED-PATTERN-CLONES", Minutes 120u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_rust_unused_pattern_clones_1 () = Note ("RUST-UNUSED-PATTERN-CLONES", 1u32, "2026-10-26T20:16Z", "x", "2026-10-26 x: measured in the Bluesky-stacked write.rs: 112 of 1778 lines (6.3%) are clones of pattern fields that the arm never uses. Most come from ThreadReply's 7 fields bound in each platform arm of path_on, verb_on and decoded_on. Count: a let vN = vN.clone() whose vN appears at most three times in its fn (pattern, binding, clone). This differs from X-DONE note 16's dead literal lets (1.6%). Done when: codegenRust emits _ for unread arm fields, the corpus agrees, and x's generated line counts drop with no rustc warnings added.")
inl note_of_rust_unused_pattern_clones_2 () = Note ("RUST-UNUSED-PATTERN-CLONES", 2u32, "2026-10-27T12:02Z", "x", "2026-10-27 08:02 x: sized on today's split-tree client outputs (proof 7, pinned compiler 4FD712E9E357, 23 crates, 16,279 lines). The generated code has 935 match-arm re-binding clones (let vN: T = vN.clone();), but only 102 are never read in their arm (a brace-depth scan to the end of the arm), i.e. 0.6% of all lines. Worst crates: write 29 (pre-R1), chain 9, users/locate/thread 7 each. The 6.3% measured earlier was the Bluesky-stacked write before R1. For x this fix is a polish item, not a line-gate lever; P3 stands. The remaining arm clones are read; they are the &-match-then-own pattern, which only a borrow-aware codegen could drop.")
inl note_of_rust_unused_pattern_clones_3 () = Note ("RUST-UNUSED-PATTERN-CLONES", 3u32, "2026-10-29T21:31Z", "compiler", "10-29 17:3x compiler: checked as a possible line-gate lever for kit's TRAIN4c red (kit_standards/standards.rs 1,024 > 1,000): that file has 12 self-rebind clones (let vN: T = vN.clone();), and only 2 are never read again in their fn. So the fix would save 2 lines there, not the 24 needed. It stays P3 polish, as x concluded in n2.")
inl note_of_rust_unused_pattern_clones_4 () = Note ("RUST-UNUSED-PATTERN-CLONES", 4u32, "2026-11-04T01:12Z", "x", "2026-11-03 21:11 x: ORPHAN SWEEP (MAIN-ORPHAN-SWEEP, x's corner: open x items with no note in 48 h). Owner is team:compiler (x is a consumer only); next step compiler's, per its last note (only 2 of 12 self-rebind clones were dead, a small lever).")
inl note_of_rust_unused_pattern_clones_5 () = Note ("RUST-UNUSED-PATTERN-CLONES", 5u32, "2026-11-04T11:21Z", "compiler", "11-04 07:2x compiler GROOM: priority P1 -> P3 to match the notes (x n2: 102 dead arm clones = 0.6% of x client lines; compiler 10-29: 2 of 12 in kit_standards). Not a line-gate lever; polish only.")
inl kit_evidence_refresh () = Task ("KIT-EVIDENCE-REFRESH", "kit evidence refresh: rewrite one EvidenceRef row from the file on disk (sha256, size), so binary evidence never needs a hand edit", Story, P2, D3, 100u32, Done, "KIT-RENAME", "kit,evidence,release", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_kit_evidence_refresh () = Planning ("KIT-EVIDENCE-REFRESH", Minutes 120u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_kit_evidence_refresh_1 () = Note ("KIT-EVIDENCE-REFRESH", 1u32, "2026-10-26T21:02Z", "kit", "17:02 kit: Found while preparing KIT-RENAME's post-install step (note 40: 'refresh evidence.spi's binary row for kit.exe with the closeout tool, never by hand-editing a hash'). No such verb exists. state/evidence.spi:47 is core.EvidenceRef ('eoie.exe', sha256, 8424960u64, core.BinaryMedia, core.BundlePath), which evidence_currentness_domain's closeout check reads and also requires a root binary named kit or kit.exe (terminal.spi 265-268). The only writers in src are register_coverage_evidence (coverage rows only, terminal.spi:297) and kit_toolchain_proxy's identity_line (portable toolchain rows, ExternalRequired). So after install, the check will truthfully report the binary evidence stale, and nothing can refresh it without a hand edit. Shape: 'kit evidence refresh <root> <relative path>' rewrites one EvidenceRef row from the file on disk (name, sha256 via the existing portable_sha256, byte length), keeps the media and custody fields, refuses a path outside the root or not already listed, and supports a dry run. Renaming a row (eoie.exe to kit.exe) is the same verb with --as <new relative path>. Test block: a fixture root with a two-row evidence file; refresh one, the other stays byte-identical, and the closeout check passes.")
inl note_of_kit_evidence_refresh_2 () = Note ("KIT-EVIDENCE-REFRESH", 2u32, "2026-10-27T12:55Z", "kit", "09:0x kit: built and proven on a probe copy, staged with the ceremony verbs (one CLI stage: clean/kit-rename/cli-ceremonies-evidence, 11 files with base hashes, applied by staged-apply.ps1 after kit-release-SLICES). 'kit evidence refresh <root> <relative path> [--as <new relative path>] [--dry-run]' lives in a new granular crate, kit_evidence (evidence.rs 428 lines): a typed refresh_outcome; pure rewritten_row and refreshed_text; one-expression host primitives for args, the existing kit_proxy_search::inspection_file_sha256 and the file length; the write goes through workspace_primitives' replace_file_checked. It is a direct verb (VerbEvidence, help line; kit.rs 958, command_spec.rs 862). Test block: one row rewritten (rename), the other byte-identical; an unlisted path refused; a path escaping the root refused. End-to-end on the generated Rust (fixture root with a two-row evidence.spi; sha256 via a stub with the same signature): --dry-run writes nothing; refresh --as kit.exe rewrites only that row, with sha256 equal to Get-FileHash and length 12, and the other row byte-identical; a missing file and ../escape exit 2 with their reasons. After the next install, 'kit evidence refresh <kit root> eoie.exe --as kit.exe' fixes evidence.spi:47 without a hand edit.")
inl note_of_kit_evidence_refresh_3 () = Note ("KIT-EVIDENCE-REFRESH", 3u32, "2026-10-30T01:58Z", "kit", "2026-10-30T01:58 kit groom (user, via main 21:54): SHIPPED: installed kit C528BE4D lists 'kit evidence refresh <root> <relative path> [--as <new relative path>] [--dry-run]'. Progress 70 -> 90. Close after one --dry-run against a real EvidenceRef row shows the rewritten row (not run in this groom).")
inl note_of_kit_evidence_refresh_4 () = Note ("KIT-EVIDENCE-REFRESH", 4u32, "2026-10-31T07:42Z", "kit", "10-31 03:3x kit: DONE line met. On the installed kit C528BE4D, 'kit evidence refresh C:\\home\\git\\kit evidence/coverage/current.lcov.receipt --dry-run' exits 0 and prints the rewritten row, core.EvidenceRef (\"evidence/coverage/current.lcov.receipt\", \"225416fa…426e\", 133u64, ...). Get-FileHash and the file length agree (sha256 225416fa…, 133 bytes).")
