inl note_of_node_mirror_cells_deps_1 () = Note ("NODE-MIRROR-CELLS-DEPS", 1u32, "2026-10-29T11:59Z", "main", "10-29 07:5x main, from fiscal's report (fiscal 07:4x and 07:5x): `remote.ps1 node sync spiral -Mirror X` prints MIRROR-DEPS-OK, which covers spiral/deps only. X\\spiral\\apps\\cells\\deps is absent, so lib_gate dies at 'kino ... run mix deps.get' with 0 rows and still exits as a test failure ('BROKEN 0 rows'), which reads as red instead of void. fiscal's workaround: copy apps\\cells\\deps from an older mirror (a copy, not a junction; see unlink-mirror-junctions-first).\nFix, two halves:\n(1) The sync either carries apps/cells/deps or runs `mix deps.get` in the mirror (MIX_ENV=test) before the first Cells job, and MIRROR-DEPS-OK checks apps/cells/deps\\kino too.\n(2) lib_gate.ps1 maps 'no test rows plus a deps error' to a distinct VOID exit and label, never to a red result.\nAlso seen once: a sync during live lib edits returned exit 7 (digest mismatch); a rerun passed. The sync should retry once by itself.\nDone when a fresh mirror runs a lib gate with nonzero rows with no manual copy, and a deliberately depless mirror prints VOID.")
inl note_of_node_mirror_cells_deps_2 () = Note ("NODE-MIRROR-CELLS-DEPS", 2u32, "2026-10-29T12:09Z", "main", "10-29 08:2x main: half (2) is done by x (unstaged): apps/cells/spi/lib_gate.ps1 now exits 2 with 'nothing ran' when no spiral-tests total appears; before, a cold mirror printed 'type errors: 0' and exit 0, a silent green. It was proven through that failing path. Left for kit: half (1), the sync carrying or fetching apps/cells/deps, plus the exit-7 retry.")
inl note_of_node_mirror_cells_deps_3 () = Note ("NODE-MIRROR-CELLS-DEPS", 3u32, "2026-10-29T12:16Z", "kit", "10-29 08:1x kit: half (1) done, unstaged in i574n.github/scripts/remote.ps1 (the 'sync -Mirror' post-step). For repo spiral, when the mirror's apps\\cells\\deps lacks kino and the node's shared checkout has it, robocopy /E copies it (a copy, not a junction, per unlink-mirror-junctions-first; 194 files, 4.8 MB). MIRROR-DEPS-OK now requires both deps\\The-Spiral-Language and apps\\cells\\deps\\kino and says 'apps\\cells\\deps copied'; a mirror without kino prints MIRROR-DEPS-MISSING naming mix deps.get. Exit 7 (digest mismatch, tree changed while packed) now retries the whole sync once (REMOTE_SYNC_RETRIED guard). Proven through the real path: 'remote.ps1 laptop sync spiral -Mirror kit-depstest' on a fresh mirror printed MIRROR-DEPS-OK ... apps\\cells\\deps copied, REMOTE-SYNC-OK (852 changed, digest E9AEBB727319E703), and the mirror's apps\\cells\\deps\\kino exists as a real folder (LinkType empty). The exit-7 retry is reasoned, not provoked. Not committed: remote.ps1 also holds main's unstaged route-probing work (Test-TcpPort, multi-route Resolve-MachineAddress, dual env.ps1 path), so whoever commits that commits this too. Left: a lib gate with nonzero rows on a fresh mirror (the done rule); I'll run it after TRAIN3, since the node is busy with verify29.")
inl note_of_node_mirror_cells_deps_4 () = Note ("NODE-MIRROR-CELLS-DEPS", 4u32, "2026-10-29T14:24Z", "kit", "10-29 10:2x kit: DONE. Half 1 meets the done rule: a fresh mirror runs a lib gate with nonzero rows and no manual copy. The kit-depstest mirror, created by 'remote.ps1 laptop sync spiral -Mirror kit-depstest' (which copied apps\\cells\\deps, a real folder, not a junction), ran node job job-1793283280092 (-RequireCore 05E94769A86A9B9C, L6 guard ok; lib_gate.ps1 -File unicode.spi -Compiler process -BuildPath per run): 'tests: passed 9 failed 0 of 9', 'type errors: 0', exit 0. Half 2 (a depless mirror reports VOID/exit 2 instead of a silent green) was done and proven by x (n2). The exit-7 digest retry is in place, reasoned rather than provoked. remote.ps1 is used straight from the repo, so the fix is live as it stands. It's still unstaged beside main's route-probing work, so whoever commits that file commits both.")
inl note_of_node_mirror_cells_deps_5 () = Note ("NODE-MIRROR-CELLS-DEPS", 5u32, "2026-10-29T16:11Z", "x", "2026-10-29 12:1x x: COPY-ONLY CELLS DAEMONS, at main's request (so the lib reader guard can attribute a lib_gate daemon that serves a TEMP lib copy without anyone vouching for it). Two halves. (A) i574n.github/scripts/lib-guarded-do.ps1 (applied, unstaged): Test-CopyDaemon skips an erl whose own command line is Spiral.Cells.CompilerDaemon.serve_app with -pa '<root>/mix-build-lib-gate/lib/...' where <root> is under AppData\\Local\\Temp and <root>\\lib\\spiral is a real directory with no reparse point on the way (Test-RealCopy). Tested on the shipped functions with real dirs: real TEMP copy True; junction copy, copy without lib, the live mix-builds/lib-gate daemon, and a non-daemon erl all False. (B) spiral apps/cells/spi/lib_gate.ps1, DRAFT x/lib-programs/lib-gate-copy-daemon.kit (base 483AB0E3411DCAEF -> target 62CE6CBF94F29ED6, proven byte-exact on a copy, target parses): Get-CopyGateSettings returns nothing for the live tree (behavior unchanged), and for a copy root a build path <root>\\mix-build-lib-gate plus a stable private port 14000-14999 (SHA-256 of the root, not GetHashCode, which .NET randomizes per process; verified equal across processes). The copy run then starts its own daemon whose command line names the copy, and it never joins a shared 13905 daemon built for the live lib. Explicit -BuildPath and an already-set SPIRAL_CELLS_COMPILER_PORT are respected. Landing (B) waits for the reader guard: refused 12:1x by compiler-pack6b-review-reuse's window; undo copy saved in C:\\home\\data\\undo\\lib-gate-copy-daemon.")
inl note_of_node_mirror_cells_deps_6 () = Note ("NODE-MIRROR-CELLS-DEPS", 6u32, "2026-10-29T16:26Z", "x", "2026-10-29 12:2x x: (B) LANDED 12:24:10 under the reader guard (spiral apps/cells/spi/lib_gate.ps1 483AB0E3411DCAEF -> 62CE6CBF94F29ED6; undo C:\\home\\data\\undo\\lib-gate-copy-daemon), after main's pack-6b install. PROVEN ON THE CONSUMER PATH: a real lib_gate run, in daemon mode, on a real robocopy of lib under TEMP (971 files, 0 reparse points). The copy run started its OWN daemon on private port 14892 (cells-14892.log, not the shared 13905), and lib-guarded-do -DryRun skipped both of its erl processes as 'reads a lib copy under TEMP' with no vouching or exemption. The gate itself passed (common.spi -Test process_counters_add_up_per_name: 1 of 1, 0 type errors, on the newly installed pack-6b compiler, so it is also LIB-PROCESS-COUNTER's test passing on pack-6b) in 33 s total, including the cold Cells build into <copy>\\mix-build-lib-gate. So a fresh copy root costs ~30 s once; repeat runs reuse it. The daemon exited after the run.")
inl near_wallet_lustre5 () = Task ("NEAR-WALLET-LUSTRE5", "near/wallet standalone build on lustre 5 + gleam_stdlib 1.x to match the migrated lib lustre.spi (drop lustre_ui 0.4 and its stylesheet link)", TaskKind, P2, D3, 100u32, Done, "GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS", "gleam,lustre,near,lib,deps", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_near_wallet_lustre5 () = Planning ("NEAR-WALLET-LUSTRE5", Minutes 120u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_near_wallet_lustre5_1 () = Note ("NEAR-WALLET-LUSTRE5", 1u32, "2026-10-29T12:04Z", "main", "10-29 08:1x main: opened from GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS. lib/spiral/near/wallet/gleam.toml still pins lustre >= 4.6.1 and < 5.0.0 and lustre_ui >= 0.4.0 and < 1.0.0 (the manifest locks lustre 4.6.4, lustre_ui 0.4.0 and gleam_stdlib 0.57.0), and index.html links build/dev/javascript/lustre_ui/priv/styles.css. The committed src/near_wallet.gleam (80af8e2f, 10-07) imports lustre/internals/vdom and lustre/ui/button, so it builds on its lock today, but a regeneration from the migrated lib emits lustre 5 code.\nSteps:\n1. Bump gleam.toml to lustre >= 5.7.0 and gleam_stdlib >= 1.0.5. Check that modem, decipher and gleam_javascript have stdlib-1.x releases; decipher may be dead, so replace it with gleam/dynamic/decode if so.\n2. Regenerate near_wallet.gleam, then gleam build -t javascript.\n3. Replace the lustre_ui stylesheet with the few lustre-ui-button rules it needs, or drop it.\n4. Run the e2e (package.json now uses @playwright/test 1.64.1).\nDone when gleam build exits 0 and the e2e passes.")
inl note_of_near_wallet_lustre5_2 () = Note ("NEAR-WALLET-LUSTRE5", 2u32, "2026-10-29T13:09Z", "main", "10-29 09:1x main: PROGRESS, not done. (1) Deps: in a scratch project, wallet deps resolve on stdlib 1.x once decipher is dropped. decipher pins gleam_json < 3, and the generated code imports only lustre and gleam/string, so decipher was unused. Resolved: lustre 5.7.1, modem 2.1.3, gleam_javascript 0.13.0, gleam_json 3.1.0, gleam_stdlib 1.0.5. lustre_ui is dropped as well. (2) BuildSpiral from the migrated lib writes near_wallet.gleam, and gleam build --target javascript exits 0 (warnings only, from generated code). (3) Live edits, unstaged, with undo copies in main scratchpad wallet-lustre5\\undo: gleam.toml (as in 1), manifest.toml (the lock resolved from that gleam.toml), and index.html (drops the lustre_ui styles.css inline link, a file that no longer exists). (4) The real build.ps1 (job-1793278798897) got through the notebook (first run), BuildSpiral, gleam build and esbuild (dist\\near_wallet.js 44.8 kb). Main STOPPED it inside the trunk step at 09:08 because kit's TRAIN3 notebook freeze bars writes under spiral/lib/spiral. Left after 'TRAIN3 notebooks end': rerun build.ps1 -SkipNotebook 1 from the trunk step to the end (trunk + e2e on @playwright/test 1.64.1); delete the 2 stray renders src/near_wallet.spi.html and .spi.ipynb (untracked, not ignored, 0 tracked anywhere in spiral) that the first run's notebook wrote. Note: trunk reports 0.22.1 is available (init.ps1 pins 0.21.14).")
inl note_of_near_wallet_lustre5_3 () = Note ("NEAR-WALLET-LUSTRE5", 3u32, "2026-10-29T14:13Z", "main", "10-29 10:2x main: DONE (all unstaged). The real build.ps1 (job-1793282547135, -SkipNotebook after the first run's notebook passed) runs end to end on lustre 5: BuildSpiral, gleam build, esbuild (dist\\near_wallet.js 44.8 kb), trunk --release (near_wallet release build 16.8 s), then the e2e: 1 passed (27.5 s) on @playwright/test 1.64.1, WALLET-BUILD exit 0. Changed: gleam.toml (stdlib 1.x, lustre 5.7, gleam_json 3; decipher and lustre_ui dropped, since the generated code imports only lustre and gleam/string), manifest.toml (re-resolved), index.html (the lustre_ui stylesheet link dropped), src/near_wallet.gleam (regenerated from the migrated lib), package.json (Playwright). The 2 stray notebook renders the first run wrote are removed (untracked, not ignored, and no .spi.ipynb or .spi.html tracked anywhere in spiral). Undo copies are in main scratchpad wallet-lustre5\\undo. Not done (not needed for green): buttons lost the lustre_ui stylesheet. The class names are kept, so a stylesheet can be added back later. The build also flags trunk 0.22.1 as available (init.ps1 pins 0.21.14), for TOOLCHAIN-LATEST.")
inl profile_mirror_to_node () = Task ("PROFILE-MIRROR-TO-NODE", "Mirror the profile state a wipe destroys (Claude memory, transcripts, settings, hooks, Stop gate, kit jobs/backups, every session scratchpad's source files) to the node hourly, restore-proven", TaskKind, P1, D2, 100u32, Done, "WIPE-REDO-LEDGER", "wipe,recovery,node,durability,backup,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_profile_mirror_to_node () = Planning ("PROFILE-MIRROR-TO-NODE", Minutes 90u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_profile_mirror_to_node_1 () = Note ("PROFILE-MIRROR-TO-NODE", 1u32, "2026-10-29T12:16Z", "main", "10-29 08:1x main: i574n.github/scripts/profile-mirror.ps1 is new and unstaged.\nWhat it collects:\n- ~/.claude/projects, with every memory file and all lane transcripts;\n- ~/.claude/hooks and ~/.claude/autonomous;\n- %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\{backups, jobs, windows} (lease files skipped);\n- settings.json, .gitconfig, .config/remote-machines.json, .ssh/config and known_hosts (NEVER private keys), and the rescue loss-map;\n- every session scratchpad's small source files under %TEMP%\\claude: scripts, drafts, ledgers and notes under 2 MB. Lib copies, mix builds, deps, targets and task outputs are excluded.\nHow it ships: tar.gz, then scp to the node's C:/home/profile-mirror over the first route that answers (laptop, laptop-ts, laptop-ip), then a sha256 check on the node; it keeps the newest 48. One archive is 3,286 files, 84 MB.\nProven 08:13: profile-mirror-20261029-081328.tgz, hash-equal on the node. Restore probe: pulled back and extracted, 164 of 164 memory files with 0 hash differences, 6 transcripts, settings.json and hooks present.\nSchedule: hourly at :52 today from main's session cron. Durable scheduling needs either a Windows scheduled task (persistent config, so the user's call) or kit's own timer. Done when it runs without a live Claude session and one restore drill passes.")
inl note_of_profile_mirror_to_node_2 () = Note ("PROFILE-MIRROR-TO-NODE", 2u32, "2026-10-29T12:22Z", "kit", "10-29 08:2x kit: design, written before any code. The ask was a kit timer that runs without a live Claude session.\nConstraint (user rule, EOIE-JOB-OWNER-PARENT n1, 10-24): a job's processes belong to the session that started them. Each owner session gets its own job host, which drains when the owner ends; parenting jobs under explorer to outlive sessions was explicitly rejected. So a kit job can't be a session-independent timer without breaking that rule. Two shapes fit:\n(A) Windows scheduled task: truly session-free. It's persistent machine config, so the user decides.\n(B) Relay through whichever session is alive, which needs no new parent model. kit gets 'kit schedule due <name> <minutes>' (exit 0 when the named timer is due, from a stamp file under %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\schedules) and 'kit schedule ran <name>'. A cheap hook step already present in every session (the Stop gate or the PreToolUse lease hook) calls 'kit schedule due profile-mirror 60' and, when due, starts 'kit job start profile-mirror ...', owned by that session as the rule wants. The mirror then runs hourly as long as ANY Claude session is active: always true during autonomous runs, never true when the box is idle, and nothing changes while it's idle anyway. The hook edit is user config (~/.claude/settings.json / hooks), so it's the user's call too; kit's half (the schedule verbs plus the stamp file) is mine and ships in a release.\nGap in (B): a wipe while no session runs. But a wipe needs a running process, and every wipe candidate so far ran inside a session.\nRecommendation: (B) now, since it needs only kit code plus a one-line hook call, and (A) if the user wants coverage while idle. Nothing gets built until main or the user picks; kit's half starts after TRAIN3.")
inl note_of_profile_mirror_to_node_3 () = Note ("PROFILE-MIRROR-TO-NODE", 3u32, "2026-10-29T12:37Z", "kit", "10-29 08:3x kit: option B's kit half (main's pick, 08:3x) is drafted, not compiled, outside the profile and the leased tree: C:\\home\\data\\kit-drafts\\kit_schedule (schedule.spi: schedule_verdict ScheduleDue | ScheduleWaits ms, schedule_request AskDue | MarkRan | ListSchedules | ScheduleUsage, request_of over the argument list, a name check refusing path characters, two rust test blocks; run.spi: schedules_root = KIT_SCHEDULES_HOME, else LOCALAPPDATA </> kit </> schedules, else REFUSE (never a relative folder, the wipe lesson from jobs_root's '.kit-jobs' fallback); stamps hold unix ms; due exits 0 when due and 1 when not; entry.spi exports kit_schedule_run; HostFree + LibReferenced standards) plus wire-schedule.do (VerbSchedule in direct_verbs, the dispatch arm in kit_cli_model, the kit_cli dependency, the workspace member). Dry run and regen after TRAIN3 releases the tree lease; it then ships in TRAIN4. The hook line that calls 'kit schedule due profile-mirror 60' stays the user's call.")
inl note_of_profile_mirror_to_node_4 () = Note ("PROFILE-MIRROR-TO-NODE", 4u32, "2026-10-29T14:52Z", "kit", "10-29 10:5x kit: the kit_schedule draft compiles. In the same node compare (job-1793285455384) the new crate kit_schedule generates 808 lines, 0 compile failures. Not yet run: its two rust test blocks (verdict, request parsing), cargo check, and the CLI wiring (wire-schedule.do, which dry-runs only on the live tree). Those come after TRAIN3, together with the hook decision (the user's call).")
inl note_of_profile_mirror_to_node_5 () = Note ("PROFILE-MIRROR-TO-NODE", 5u32, "2026-10-29T18:23Z", "kit", "2026-10-29T18:23 kit: closeout on the TRAIN4 candidate (AECDD318) found that kit schedule refuses every call with its usage line. Cause: run.spi reads env.command_line_arguments_from 2i32, but the lib already skips the exe (argument_offset 1 on Rust), so index 0 is 'schedule' and 2 drops the sub-verb. The 2 was copied from kit_machine_windows, whose own argument helper counts raw argv. The unit tests cover request_of only, not the argv slice. Fix drafted: C:\\home\\data\\kit-drafts\\schedule-args\\schedule-args-offset.do (from 1i32; dry run ok). It lands in TRAIN5. TRAIN4 publishes with the bug because nothing calls kit schedule yet (the hook line is the user's call). Done = train4-closeout.ps1 row PROFILE-MIRROR passes on the installed binary (due 0, ran, due 1 due=no).")
inl note_of_profile_mirror_to_node_6 () = Note ("PROFILE-MIRROR-TO-NODE", 6u32, "2026-10-29T21:48Z", "main", "10-29 17:4x main: the hourly mirror had grown to 24,379 files / 262.7 MB (morning: 3,286 / 84 MB). Archive 17:40:31 sha256 422E87D2... is OK on the node, but 48 kept at that size is about 12.6 GB. Cause: node-compile BUNDLES in session scratchpads (kit fc1568a6: guard-compile, gaps-compile, std-split, win-split, t4-precheck, drafts-compile; main: bang\\bang-bundle), each a bundle\\base plus bundle\\cand copy of the kit tree (about 1,670 files per side) that is regenerated per compile, and so not drafts. Fix in i574n.github/scripts/profile-mirror.ps1 (unstaged): the heavy-folder exclusion also skips `*bundle\\base` and `*bundle\\cand`. Dry run: 7,425 files / 202.1 MB, of which ~/.claude/projects (transcripts) is 140.6 MB, the part the mirror exists for. kit's real drafts live durably in C:\\home\\data\\kit-drafts and are unaffected.")
inl note_of_profile_mirror_to_node_7 () = Note ("PROFILE-MIRROR-TO-NODE", 7u32, "2026-10-29T21:58Z", "main", "10-29 18:0x main: the durable-schedule decision is now a one-liner for the user. New wrapper i574n.github/scripts/profile-mirror-if-due.ps1 (unstaged): asks `kit schedule due profile-mirror 60`, runs profile-mirror.ps1 DETACHED (a hook never blocks), and calls `kit schedule ran profile-mirror` only when the log shows the node sha256 line. It needs the kit.exe that TRAIN4e installs (the installed CD2F0D1A answers \"unknown verb: schedule\"); main tests it after \"TRAIN4 published\". USER CHOICE: (A) a Windows scheduled task every 15 min running `pwsh -NoProfile -File C:\\home\\git\\i574n.github\\scripts\\profile-mirror-if-due.ps1`. It runs with no Claude session, so it meets this item's done rule. (B) a Claude Code hook (for example on Stop) running the same command. It runs only while a session is live. Both are persistent config, so they are not applied without the user's yes.")
inl note_of_profile_mirror_to_node_8 () = Note ("PROFILE-MIRROR-TO-NODE", 8u32, "2026-10-29T22:42Z", "kit", "2026-10-29T22:40 kit: shipped in TRAIN4c (installed kit C528BE4D). kit schedule due/ran/list works end to end (closeout PASS: due 0, ran, due 1 due=no). The argv off-by-one TRAIN4b found is fixed. What is left is the user's call: the hook line that runs the mirror when kit schedule says due.")
inl note_of_profile_mirror_to_node_9 () = Note ("PROFILE-MIRROR-TO-NODE", 9u32, "2026-10-29T22:50Z", "main", "10-29 18:4x main: wrapper PROVEN on the installed kit C528BE4D (TRAIN4e). `kit schedule due profile-mirror 60` returned due=yes (never ran); profile-mirror-if-due.ps1 -Wait ran the mirror (7,578 files, 212.6 MB, node archive 184731 sha256 D21364AF...) and marked it ran; `due` then returned due=no next_in_s=3599; a second wrapper call was a no-op. Only the USER's trigger choice is left: (A) a scheduled task every 15 min running `pwsh -NoProfile -File C:\\home\\git\\i574n.github\\scripts\\profile-mirror-if-due.ps1` (works with no session), or (B) a Stop hook running the same command.")
inl note_of_profile_mirror_to_node_10 () = Note ("PROFILE-MIRROR-TO-NODE", 10u32, "2026-10-29T23:43Z", "main", "10-29 19:43 main, FOUND AND FIXED: the mirror's green line hid broken archives. It printed 'tar.exe: (null)' at 19:34. A tar -tzf check of the 8 newest node archives found 3 TRUNCATED (15:46, 16:45, 19:34), each holding exactly 1 entry: this session's live transcript (5a6e53fb...jsonl, 47-61 MB), which grew while tar read it. tar wrote its header and then got a short read. The script ignored tar's exit code, and the sha256 check passed because it compares the node copy with the local file, not with the sources. So about 1 in 3 hourly archives was unusable while the newest looked fine. FIX, profile-mirror.ps1 68AB020BAD51 -> 904250655ECB (scratch-proven, then installed with a base pin, LF and parse checks): (1) copy each file into a snapshot folder first, and tar the snapshot; unreadable files are skipped and counted; (2) require tar exit 0 AND tar -tzf entries == files copied, or throw before scp, leaving the archive local. Proof: the 19:39 run's node archive lists 7937 of 7937 entries, 0 errors, including the live transcript. Positive control: a tgz cut to 1/3 lists 1 of 3 entries, so the check throws. Cost: about 240 s per run, against about 60 s. The broken 3 remain among the 48 kept and age out; restore must tar -tzf-check its candidate (memory profile-mirror-to-node updated).")
inl note_of_profile_mirror_to_node_11 () = Note ("PROFILE-MIRROR-TO-NODE", 11u32, "2026-10-30T10:34Z", "git-51", "10-30 06:3x git-51: GAP. Newest node archive is profile-mirror-20261030-011525.tgz; nothing since, so 5h+ unmirrored at 06:35. The hourly trigger was main's session cron, which ended with the ~01:26 kill (TRAIN5 lost the same minute) and the 06:19 restarts. The kit schedule stamp (%LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\schedules\\profile-mirror) is from 10-29 18:49, so the cron ran profile-mirror.ps1 directly, not the if-due wrapper. Integrity of the newest 3 (tar -tzf on the node): 011525 8,882 entries/185 memory files, 002520 8,854/184, 230852 8,755/183, all exit 0, so the 19:43 truncation fix holds. This is the case the durable trigger choice (A scheduled task / B Stop-hook relay, n7-n8) exists for: a session-cron trigger dies with its session. Not run by git-51 (main's script; main re-arms).")
inl note_of_profile_mirror_to_node_12 () = Note ("PROFILE-MIRROR-TO-NODE", 12u32, "2026-10-30T23:52Z", "main", "10-30 19:52 main: the n11 gap is closed: the mirror runs again (node archives 14:04, 15:21, 16:53, 18:22, and one in progress at 19:50 via profile-mirror-if-due.ps1; the caller is detached, so its parent is not traceable). But the cadence was 77-92 min, not hourly: the wrapper stamps 'kit schedule ran' only AFTER a successful run (about 4-5 min in) and called the mirror due at 60 min, so a poll about an hour after the last start sees about 55 min and skips. FIX: profile-mirror-if-due.ps1 default -EveryMinutes 60 -> 50 (i574n.github, unstaged), so an hourly poll always runs, and faster polls run at most every 50 min. Check at the next groom: node archive gaps <= 65 min.")
inl note_of_profile_mirror_to_node_13 () = Note ("PROFILE-MIRROR-TO-NODE", 13u32, "2026-10-31T00:00Z", "heal", "10-30 20:0x heal (git-51): RESTORE DRILL on today's format (snapshot + tar entry check, n10). Node archive profile-mirror-20261030-195237.tgz: tar lists 27,566 entries. Opened on the node with Python tarfile, 199 files under projects/C--home-git/memory/ sha256'd and compared with the box's live copies: 197 hash-equal, 0 differ, 2 edited after 19:52 (live 201 = 199 + 2 newer). The archive restores. Bridge log 10:13-19:52: 9 hourly runs from this session's cron, all tar-verified, so no gap since 10:13. Trap for future drills: match the project path, not '/memory/' (the archive holds several projects' memory folders whose same-named files collide). Still the user's: the lasting trigger (A scheduled task / B Stop hook). This bridge ends with this session.")
inl note_of_profile_mirror_to_node_14 () = Note ("PROFILE-MIRROR-TO-NODE", 14u32, "2026-10-31T00:22Z", "main", "10-30 20:22 main: TRIGGER HANDOVER. heal's bridge (its session cron at :17/:47 running profile-mirror-if-due.ps1 since 10:13; 10 verified archives, the last 19:52) ends at 21:00. main armed the same poll in its own session cron (17,47 * * * *, job c812c203, first run about 20:59; it expires after 7 days or with this session). This also explains the n12 cadence: 30-min polls against a 60-min threshold stamped after a ~5-min run gave the 77-92 min gaps; with the new 50-min threshold each run lands at about 60 min. heal's restore drill on the 19:52 archive passed (197/199 memory files hash-equal, 2 newer; n13). Still the user's call: the lasting trigger (scheduled task or Stop hook, n7/n13); a session cron dies with its session, as on 10-30 01:26.")
inl note_of_profile_mirror_to_node_15 () = Note ("PROFILE-MIRROR-TO-NODE", 15u32, "2026-10-31T05:33Z", "main", "10-31 01:33 main: the 00:53 run uploaded its archive at 00:58:45 (profile-mirror-20261031-005845.tgz; 28,750 files, 669 MB), then spent 30+ min in its last step, Remove-TreeSafely of the snapshot folder (guarded per-file delete, slow on a saturated box). Until that ends, the wrapper doesn't stamp 'ran', so a poll past 50 min could start a second run. kit's schedule did not start one at 01:29, but profile-mirror-if-due.ps1 now also returns early when a profile-mirror.ps1 process is alive (i574n.github, unstaged; checked against the live run, 1 match, never itself). Mirror health: newest node archive 00:58, so no gap. A possible follow-up for the script owner: snapshot to a folder deleted in the background, or reuse it.")
inl note_of_profile_mirror_to_node_16 () = Note ("PROFILE-MIRROR-TO-NODE", 16u32, "2026-10-31T08:16Z", "heal", "10-31 04:1x heal (git-51): DONE. The durable trigger is installed on the user's 'heal what's pending autonomously' (10-31): Task Scheduler task profile-mirror-to-node, every 15 min, Interactive logon (the user's ssh config), runs 'conhost.exe --headless pwsh -NoProfile -NonInteractive -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File profile-mirror-if-due.ps1' (no console flash), MultipleInstances IgnoreNew, 45 min limit. The wrapper's 50-min due stamp and live-run guard prevent double runs with the session crons. Registered reproducibly in i574n.github/scripts/register-scheduled-tasks.ps1 (unstaged; new optional Every field for repeating tasks; -DryRun reports 'unchanged'). Proven: the 04:05 task run started the mirror (pid 16164), the 04:07 run stood down by the guard, and the task result is 0. The restore drill passed earlier (n13). Session crons can now be dropped.")
inl note_of_profile_mirror_to_node_17 () = Note ("PROFILE-MIRROR-TO-NODE", 17u32, "2026-10-31T08:59Z", "heal", "10-31 04:5x heal (git-51): FIX after the first scheduled runs. Task Scheduler's default task priority is 7 (BelowNormal), so the mirror inherited BelowNormal and, on the box at 100% CPU, STARVED: 23.6 s of CPU in 49 min, stuck in file collection (the heavy-gate-lessons rule). The task is set to Priority 5 (Normal), and the live run was raised to Normal (CPU resumed at ~0.5 s/s). register-scheduled-tasks.ps1 now passes -Priority 5 for repeating tasks. Lesson for any scheduled job on this box: set the priority explicitly; the default is BelowNormal.")
inl note_of_profile_mirror_to_node_18 () = Note ("PROFILE-MIRROR-TO-NODE", 18u32, "2026-10-31T15:23Z", "resilience", "10-31 11:2x resilience (git-51): FIXED a silent backup stall. Newest node archive was 09:11 at 11:18 (2h gap) though the task fires every 15 min. Cause: the task's ExecutionTimeLimit was 45 min, but a mirror run (765 MB, 100%-CPU box, wifi scp) takes 45-70 min, so Task Scheduler killed each run before it wrote the archive, and 'due' never cleared. Fix: raised the live task's limit to PT2H and launched a run now (the 11:20 task run is in flight with the new limit). register-scheduled-tasks.ps1 Every-branch now uses -ExecutionTimeLimit 2h (was 45m). Lesson: a repeating backup task's time limit must exceed its slowest run under load, not its quiet-box time; too tight = silent no-op, not an error. The restore drill (05:40) and config-baseline (06:10) are quick and keep their defaults.")
inl note_of_profile_mirror_to_node_19 () = Note ("PROFILE-MIRROR-TO-NODE", 19u32, "2026-10-31T16:24Z", "resilience", "10-31 12:2x resilience (git-51): resolved a wedged run and found a follow-up. The 11:20 run (pid 19020) collected+tarred+scp'd a COMPLETE archive (profile-mirror-20261031-112657.tgz, 29650 entries, tar -tzf verified on the node) but then hung 60 min in its final bookkeeping with no child process, never printing the sha256 line, so schedule ran never fired and due stayed yes. I verified the archive intact, stopped the wedged wrapper, and ran kit schedule ran (due now clears, next ~50 min). Backup goal met for this cycle. FOLLOW-UP (file if it recurs): the mirror's hot-path verification is a REMOTE Get-FileHash over ~774 MB via ssh (profile-mirror.ps1 line ~79-84) — slow and the likely wedge point. Since profile-restore-drill now independently verifies node archives (tar + entry count + memory hashes), replace the mirror's remote full-hash with a fast remote \tar -tzf | count sanity check after scp; keep the local hash. Smaller hot path, same safety.")
inl note_of_profile_mirror_to_node_20 () = Note ("PROFILE-MIRROR-TO-NODE", 20u32, "2026-10-31T17:19Z", "resilience", "10-31 13:1x resilience (git-51): the n19 follow-up is DONE and PROVEN, not deferred. Replaced the mirror's hot-path remote Get-FileHash (~800 MB over ssh, the wedge point) with a remote size + 'tar -tzf | count' check (ConnectTimeout 8); local hash kept for the log; deeper hash verification stays in the daily restore drill. Proof run (direct, -Wait) completed cleanly in ~38 min: printed its final sha256/keeping line, exited with NO lingering process (the wedge symptom gone), empty stderr, landed profile-mirror-20261031-125408.tgz (independently tar-verified on the node). A truncated/corrupt scp still fails the size-or-entry-count check. profile-mirror.ps1 edited unstaged, parse clean; the scheduled task picks it up automatically.")
inl note_of_profile_mirror_to_node_21 () = Note ("PROFILE-MIRROR-TO-NODE", 21u32, "2026-10-31T21:02Z", "resilience", "10-31 16:5x resilience (git-51): CORRECTION + real follow-up. Re-diagnosed the apparent 'wedge': the proof run has a live scp.exe child and its node archive is a partial file (tar can't open it yet), so it is a genuinely SLOW scp of the 808 MB archive over the contended wifi link (~40 min), not a hang. Runs ARE completing and landing fresh archives (~hourly); the 83-min gap at 16:18 was within the 3h freshness bar. So my 2h-limit fix (n18) is the right call and sufficient; I was too quick to kill pid 19020 earlier (that one WAS a true post-scp verify hang: archive complete at 29650 entries, no scp child). TWO distinct, real issues to design (not hot-patch): (a) the archive is 808 MB and growing (transcripts dominate ~360 MB); a full tar+scp every 50 min over ~0.3-2 MB/s is wasteful and causes run overlap -> consider incremental/dedup transfer, or drop the cadence to every 3-4h (the daily restore drill already deep-verifies), or split transcripts into their own less-frequent archive; (b) the post-scp remote-verify ssh can stall under node load with no hard timeout -> bound it (job + 60s) and treat a verify timeout as 'archive landed, verify deferred to the drill'. Filed for a proper change with a proof; backups are landing meanwhile.")
inl global_fragment_dedup () = Task ("GLOBAL-FRAGMENT-DEDUP", "Compiler: a multi-line global whose closing triple quote is outdented is emitted once per call site (rustc E0428); the duplicate-fragment exception must apply to single-line fragments only, on both PartEval sides", Story, P2, D2, 100u32, Done, "PACK-7", "compiler,codegen,rust,globals,bug", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_global_fragment_dedup () = Planning ("GLOBAL-FRAGMENT-DEDUP", Minutes 90u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_global_fragment_dedup_1 () = Note ("GLOBAL-FRAGMENT-DEDUP", 1u32, "2026-10-29T12:24Z", "compiler", "10-29 08:3x compiler: reported by x (LIB-PROCESS-COUNTER n7; memory rust-global-closing-quote). ROOT CAUSE and fix drafted.\nMinimal repro, no lib (session scratch global-quote/{aligned,outdented}): one inl whose body is global with a triple-quoted Rust fn plus a $'' call, called from 3 sites. With the closing triple quote at the content's column -> 1 copy. With the closing quote 4 columns left (at global's column) -> 3 copies, byte-identical (no CR, same tails), i.e. rustc E0428.\nCause: the triple-quoted literal is dedented against the closing quote's column, so the outdented form keeps 4 leading spaces. Globals are collected in partial evaluation (spiral_compiler.fs:160156, single-flight PartEval side) through preserves_duplicate_fragment, which KEEPS duplicates of any text starting with whitespace or a closing brace. That exception exists for block assembly from fragments (kit: global \"    use std::io::{Read, Write};\" inside an earlier-opened module, and repeated global \"}\" closers; all 4 such uses in lib/samples/apps/dice/x/kit are single-line). So a complete multi-line block that merely starts indented is mistaken for a fragment and never deduped. The Rust backend then emits env.globals as-is (its own global' set is built from the already-duplicated list). The gpt5parallel side (globals_add, :128609) has the opposite gap: it dedups EVERY fragment, so a second global \"}\" would be dropped.\nFIX: apps/compiler/packs/apply-global-fragment-single-line.ps1 -Core <file> [-Apply], 6 anchored edits. In the 5 copies of the predicate (single-flight peval, shared preservesDuplicateGlobalFragment, F#, and two more backends), a fragment is kept duplicated only when it is ONE line: (IsWhiteSpace x.[0] || x.[0] = '}') && not (x.TrimEnd().Contains '\\n'). gpt5parallel globals_add gets the same rule. Scratch copy: 6/6 anchors; pair-drift 8 pairs, 0 one-sided.\nNEXT (build-bound, rides the next single-flight install pack): one regression sample (the 3-site outdented repro, Rust-only list entry in tests/matrix.psd1), full -Suite all -Native review DISAGREE 0, and the x repro (process-counter.kit on lib common.spi, outdented) emitting 1 copy.")
inl note_of_global_fragment_dedup_2 () = Note ("GLOBAL-FRAGMENT-DEDUP", 2u32, "2026-10-29T12:38Z", "compiler", "10-29 08:38 compiler: regression sample added to the applier (-Samples <dir> writes samples/global_multiline_outdented_dedup: one inl whose !!!!Global triple-quoted // comment pair has its closing quote LEFT of the content, called 3 times; default backends; main returns 0). Positive control on the INSTALLED compiler, copies of the comment per output: C 1 (its global' has no fragment exception), Rust 3, Delphi 3, F# 6, TypeScript 6 (6 = the peval duplicates times the backend's own fragment-preserving list). The candidate must give 1 on every backend; the committed outputs are the check. C native exit 0.")
inl note_of_global_fragment_dedup_3 () = Note ("GLOBAL-FRAGMENT-DEDUP", 3u32, "2026-10-30T02:01Z", "compiler", "10-29 22:0x compiler (groom): progress 60 -> 85. apply-global-fragment-single-line.ps1 and its sample global_multiline_outdented_dedup ride PACK-7 (a PACK-7 dep; candidate built and reviewed today). Left: main's PACK-7 install, then 1 copy per backend in the sample's committed outputs and x's process-counter repro emitting 1 copy.")
inl note_of_global_fragment_dedup_4 () = Note ("GLOBAL-FRAGMENT-DEDUP", 4u32, "2026-10-30T03:18Z", "compiler", "10-29 23:3x compiler: CLOSED. Installed with pack 7. run single-flight-20261029-223356 (pack 7 -Accept, installed core 9C899B30495F7DC5): samples/global_multiline_outdented_dedup exits 0 on Fsharp, C, Rust and Delphi, and every committed output (main.c/.fsx/.pas/.rs) carries exactly 1 copy of the outdented multi-line global (before the fix, on the installed 6b: C 1, Rust 3, Delphi 3, F# 6). x's process-counter repro goes through the same predicate; x can confirm on its next Rust build.")
inl lib_tests_not_run () = Task ("LIB-TESTS-NOT-RUN", "lib: 73 of 516 discovered tests never execute (sm'.spi 0 of 65, step_runtime 0 of 3, one each in base, benchmark, date_time, parsing, rust/testing); classify why, then run them or mark them", TaskKind, P1, D2, 100u32, Done, "LIB-PACKAGE-DOT-LISTED-TWICE", "lib,cells,lib-gate,tests", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_lib_tests_not_run () = Planning ("LIB-TESTS-NOT-RUN", Minutes 90u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_lib_tests_not_run_1 () = Note ("LIB-TESTS-NOT-RUN", 1u32, "2026-10-29T12:45Z", "main", "10-29 08:5x main: after today's lustre5 land, the lib is at 443 passed, 0 failed, 516 discovered (the 06:39 sweep was lib-sweep-20261029-libroot.tsv, plus 4 lustre tests now passing). The gap is NOT failures: lib_sweep.ps1 reads 'tests: passed P failed F of D', and in 9 files D > P + F. Biggest: sm'.spi 0 of 65 in 189 s (the same budget as files that pass, so not a timeout) and step_runtime 0 of 3 in 187 s; then base 25/26, benchmark 2/3, date_time 43/44, parsing 39/40, rust/testing 3/4.\nsm'.spi starts with 'test sm static =' (a static block). Its 65 tests carry fsharp/gleam/rust/cuda/typescript/lua target lines, so the candidates are: (a) every target reports as accepted, static or memo-only, so none counts as passed; (b) the targets are unsupported in this Cells, e.g. cuda or fsharp after the F# retirement; (c) a file-level static marker suppresses the run.\nA main probe at 08:20 used a cold -BuildPath and was stopped by main at 08:43 so it would not block fiscal's lands. Next: one WARM lib_gate -File \"sm'.spi\" -Show 70 -RawOut, then group its tests.tsv by Status. It is a Cells reader, so it waits for kit's TRAIN3 notebooks to end.\nDone when every discovered test either runs on at least one available backend or is explicitly marked with its reason, and the sweep's D equals P + F + marked.")
inl note_of_lib_tests_not_run_2 () = Note ("LIB-TESTS-NOT-RUN", 2u32, "2026-10-29T13:17Z", "main", "10-29 09:2x main, static reading (no run).\n(1) sm'.spi's 65 = (c) a file-level static marker. Line 8 '///- --static' (since fd6311b4, 2025-11-21; the ONLY lib file with it) gives every test mode static: Cells notebook.ex:261 test_state(%{mode: 'static'}) -> 'static', so each is type-checked only and counted under 'static', neither passed nor failed.\n(2) Ruled out for the 1-test gaps in base, benchmark, date_time, parsing and rust/testing:\n- the 'test X static' header (55 files have one, and 48 of those show gap 0);\n- untargeted test blocks (parsing has 12 and a gap of only 1, so untargeted tests run on the default).\nstep_runtime's 3 include one untargeted test (:135 stopped_actor_ignores_later_messages).\nNext, after the TRAIN3 freeze: one warm lib_gate per gap file with -RawOut, grouped by Status, for the per-test reasons. For sm', a measured run of its 65 tests without --static (on a lib copy: pass count and time) decides whether to lift the directive or keep it as a deliberate static file, then document it.")
inl note_of_lib_tests_not_run_3 () = Note ("LIB-TESTS-NOT-RUN", 3u32, "2026-10-29T17:03Z", "main", "10-29 12:5x main: MEASURED (job-1793292616415, sm'.spi copy under TEMP with the file-level '///- --static' removed and a 1500 s cell budget in the copy only): 70 of 70 tests PASS, 224 target runs, 0 failures, 0 type errors, 749 s wall under TRAIN4 load. Per target: c 9, cuda 31, delphi 2, fsharp 48, gleam 41 (+1 with gleam_regexp), lean 2, lua 16, python 1, rust 43 (+4 with regex), typescript 16, zig 10. DECISION: lift --static. sm' (the most-used string module) goes from type-check-only to real execution on 13 backends. Cost: a gate of sm'.spi itself takes about 12 min under load (less when idle, memo hits on reruns); other files are unaffected. Land: main-drafts land-unstatic.ps1 (reader guard, the one-line directive removal, a canonical blank-only fixpoint, undo copy) AFTER 'TRAIN4 published', since TRAIN4 compiles against the live sm'.spi. That also settles the 65-test gap; the small 1-test gaps in base, benchmark, date_time, parsing and rust/testing and step_runtime's 2 untargeted tests remain for a warm gate each.")
inl note_of_lib_tests_not_run_4 () = Note ("LIB-TESTS-NOT-RUN", 4u32, "2026-10-29T17:35Z", "main", "10-29 13:31 main: small-gap probe (job-1793293456944-main-lib-gaps-probe, lib_gate on a fresh lib copy, scoop gcc pinned) ran every test in the six files that had none recorded: step_runtime 6/6, base 107/107, benchmark 2/2, date_time 117/117, parsing 89/89, rust/testing 3/3, type errors 0 in each, no NOT-RUN rows. These six are closed as \"run and green\"; the remaining not-run set is the sm' --static case (measured 70/70 with the directive removed, landing queued behind TRAIN4).")
inl note_of_lib_tests_not_run_5 () = Note ("LIB-TESTS-NOT-RUN", 5u32, "2026-10-29T19:22Z", "main", "10-29 15:20 main: sm'.spi LANDED without its '///- --static' file header, 4B7C5CDB0476 -> E56D63DE1C1F (measured 70/70 passing with the directive removed, job-1793292616415-main-sm-static-measured-2, all 13 target rows green). The 65 previously static-only tests now run as normal test cells. Landed in the 10-29 15:19 lib gap (after kit's TRAIN4 notebooks end, after fiscal's 4), through main's window sequence: reader + held-window check, proven base hashes asserted, canonical round trip (fixpoint true, blank-only diff), private MIX_BUILD_PATH. document_test after the gap lands: 22 passed (15:22, private MIX_BUILD_PATH), closing run after git-a6 console.spi follows. Undo copies under main scratchpad land-*/ (durable scripts C:\\home\\data\\main-drafts\\window-1029).")
inl note_of_lib_tests_not_run_6 () = Note ("LIB-TESTS-NOT-RUN", 6u32, "2026-10-29T19:22Z", "main", "10-29 15:2x main: at 95%. Closes when the next live lib_gate over sm'.spi (or the nightly sweep) shows its formerly static-only tests as executed rows on the live tree, not only on the 12:50 measured copy.")
inl note_of_lib_tests_not_run_7 () = Note ("LIB-TESTS-NOT-RUN", 7u32, "2026-10-30T00:10Z", "main", "10-29 20:10 main, CLOSED by its n-last criterion: a live lib_gate over the LIVE sm'.spi (2196F9C70148, post-window-4 tree; lib_gate.ps1 with a private BuildPath, mix-builds\\main-libgate; scoop gcc pinned; run 19:53-20:06 after window 4 closed) reports 'tests: passed 72 failed 0 of 72; type errors: 0'. The per-test TSV holds 227 EXECUTED rows (72 tests x their target backends), all Status=passed with real run times (e.g. symbol_to_string: gleam 133.5 s, lua 57.6 s, fsharp 12.3 s). Before the 15:20 land of the header-less sm', the gate ran 0 of its 65 tests ('--static'). Evidence: C:\\home\\data\\main-drafts\\session-1029\\lib-gate-sm-live.{log,raw,raw.tests.tsv,raw.ipynb}. All 73 originally not-run tests (sm' 65 plus 8 in six files, n-1) now execute and pass.")
inl delete_root_guard () = Task ("DELETE-ROOT-GUARD", "Every recursive delete refuses an empty, relative, drive-root, profile or temp-root target (shared Assert-SafeDeleteRoot for scripts; the same rule in lib file_system.directory_delete and kit remove_dir_all callers)", Story, P1, D3, 85u32, Planned, "PROFILE-WIPE-RECOVERY,EOIE-RELEASE-TRAIN", "wipe,safety,lib,kit,scripts,file-system,team:resilience,resilience", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_delete_root_guard () = Planning ("DELETE-ROOT-GUARD", Minutes 240u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_delete_root_guard_1 () = Note ("DELETE-ROOT-GUARD", 1u32, "2026-10-29T13:21Z", "main", "10-29 09:2x main, from an audit of env-rooted recursive deletes (main scratchpad env-delete-audit.tsv; a read-only subagent; behaviour tested in child shells under pwsh 7.6.9 and Windows PowerShell 5.1, with no deletes):\nFacts that shape the rule:\n- With TEMP and TMP stripped, [IO.Path]::GetTempPath() AND Rust std::env::temp_dir() return C:\\Users\\i574n\\. With USERPROFILE also stripped, it is C:\\WINDOWS\\.\n- Elixir System.tmp_dir!() can fall back to the current directory.\n- \"$env:TEMP\\x\" with TEMP unset is C:\\x.\n- Join-Path $env:X 'y' with X unset THROWS in both PowerShell editions, so it fails closed.\n- Get-ChildItem \"\" and Get-ChildItem $null list the CURRENT directory.\nResult: 72 sites; 1 RISKY, 3 UNCLEAR, 68 SAFE.\n- RISKY: polyglot\\deps\\dotnet-interactive\\eng\\DownLoadSqlToolsServiceLayerPackage.ps1:76,99. $workdir = GetTempPath(), then [IO.Directory]::Delete($workdir, $true) deletes the temp ROOT itself, which is the whole profile when TEMP and TMP are stripped. It is vendored upstream, its only caller buildSqlTools.cmd is never invoked, and the backlog never mentions it.\n- UNCLEAR: kit compiler-contracts\\test-native-probes.ps1:22,27 sets the cwd to GetTempPath(), so probes run in the profile when TEMP is stripped. Lib env.spi environment_restored strips the environment on an empty snapshot; main's guard draft is proven next and lands after TRAIN3. spiral scripts\\core.ps1:407-422: an empty $path targets C:\\target\\...\n- Latent, SAFE today: formatter\\candidate-gate.ps1:10 and tibia run-policy-scenario.ps1:86. If their root were ever \"\" instead of throwing, they would delete folders in the cwd.\nThe shared guard, kept here and not as a file, since it has no home yet:\nfunction Assert-SafeDeleteRoot([string] $Path) {\n    if ([string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($Path) -or -not [IO.Path]::IsPathFullyQualified($Path)) { throw \"refuse delete: '$Path' is empty or relative\" }\n    $full = [IO.Path]::TrimEndingDirectorySeparator([IO.Path]::GetFullPath($Path))\n    $forbidden = @([IO.Path]::GetPathRoot($full), $env:USERPROFILE, $HOME, [Environment]::GetFolderPath('UserProfile'), [IO.Path]::GetTempPath(), $env:LOCALAPPDATA, $env:APPDATA, $env:SystemRoot) | Where-Object { $_ } | ForEach-Object { [IO.Path]::TrimEndingDirectorySeparator([IO.Path]::GetFullPath($_)) }\n    if ($forbidden -contains $full) { throw \"refuse delete: '$full' is a root, profile or temp root\" }\n    $full\n}\nPlan:\n(1) Place it in i574n.github/scripts as a dot-sourced module (org tooling home) and adopt it at every RISKY, UNCLEAR and latent site. The vendored polyglot deps file is upstream: leave it unexecuted, and record it.\n(2) Lib: file_system's directory delete refuses the same set, with a Rust row and a negative.\n(3) kit: its rooted tree removal already refuses its own root; add the forbidden set to remove_dir_all callers. That is kit's slice, after TRAIN3.\nPart 2 of the audit (enumerate-then-delete under a temp-derived root, the shape that would empty the profile entry by entry and skip locked files) is running; its rows append to the same TSV.")
inl note_of_delete_root_guard_2 () = Note ("DELETE-ROOT-GUARD", 2u32, "2026-10-29T13:24Z", "main", "10-29 09:3x main: STEP 1 DONE (unstaged). i574n.github/scripts/safe-delete.ps1 is dot-sourceable and defines Get-ForbiddenDeleteRoots (every drive root, USERPROFILE, HOME, the profile, GetTempPath, TEMP, TMP, LOCALAPPDATA, APPDATA, SystemRoot, ProgramData, Program Files x64 and x86), Assert-SafeDeleteRoot <path> [-Under <base>] (refuses an empty or relative path, any forbidden root after GetFullPath, and anything outside -Under; prefix-safe) and Remove-TreeSafely. Proof (main scratchpad safe-delete-test.ps1): 15/15 PASS. It refuses empty, null, relative, C:\\, the profile with and without a trailing slash, the temp root, LOCALAPPDATA, Windows, a ..-path back to the profile, outside -Under, and the 'data-evil' prefix trick. THE WIPE SCENARIO: in a child shell with TEMP and TMP removed, GetTempPath() = C:\\Users\\i574n and the guard refuses it. Allowed child and scratch deletes still work. Adopted: spiral scripts/core.ps1 ClearCargoTarget refuses an empty path (it would clear \\target at the drive root); callers pass relative paths ('.', '../..'), so only the empty case is refused. Test: refused on '', and clears a real target\\debug\\deps; parse 0 errors. Handed to owners at 09:3x: kit (compiler-contracts test-native-probes.ps1 cwd = GetTempPath; remove_dir_all callers), git-a6 (formatter candidate-gate.ps1:10), tibia (live/run-policy-scenario.ps1:86).")
inl note_of_delete_root_guard_3 () = Note ("DELETE-ROOT-GUARD", 3u32, "2026-10-29T13:33Z", "main", "10-29 09:3x main: audit part 2 (enumerate-then-delete under temp-derived roots; 26 more rows, 98 in env-delete-audit.tsv). No surviving PowerShell, Rust or Elixir site lists the temp root itself and deletes from it, apart from the vendored SqlTools root delete and Zed's filtered non-Windows updater. Nothing in the backlog shows a cleanup in 10:30-11:10 local on 10-28. The scratch-archive runs were at 06:39, 06:42 and 07:11, the lib-gate sweep at 06:3x and bin-sweep at 07:2x. Two of that day's cleanup scripts (TEMP lib-gate-retention\\bin-sweep.ps1 and sweep.ps1) were lost and can't be audited. The cause stays UNPROVEN. ONE RISKY site, in org tooling, FIXED (unstaged): i574n.github/scripts/scratch-archive.ps1 never checked -Root, so a profile or temp root would archive and then recursively delete every top-level folder. It now dot-sources safe-delete.ps1, guards Root, validates every name -Under Root BEFORE archiving, and re-guards each folder at delete. Tests (main scratchpad scratch-archive-guard-test.ps1, own -Store): the profile root, the temp root with TEMP and TMP stripped, and C:\\ are refused; a '..' list name is refused before any archive, and the sibling file survives; a normal dry run keeps originals; -Apply archives and removes 2 folders; -Restore brings them back byte-equal; parse 0 errors. kit is running its own audit of 74 recursive-delete files and drafted test-native-probes' unique-child cwd (C:\\home\\data\\kit-drafts\\delete-root-guard), both after TRAIN3.")
inl note_of_delete_root_guard_4 () = Note ("DELETE-ROOT-GUARD", 4u32, "2026-10-29T13:37Z", "kit", "10-29 09:3x kit: kit's part of the audit, merged with main's part 2 (main scratchpad env-delete-audit.tsv, enumerate-then-delete: none surviving under temp roots). Kit's recursive deletes (74 files: remove_dir_all, Remove-Item -Recurse, rd /s, the Spiral wrappers and the rooted walkers) were classified by where each root comes from. Result: no kit delete can remove the profile root, the temp root or a bare environment root. Every TEMP- or env-rooted delete appends a PID-named leaf (most also nanoseconds or a GUID), so with TEMP stripped the worst case is a folder like C:\\Users\\i574n\\kit-launch-<pid>. The ~190 fixture_root_remove calls are safe by type: fixture_root has one constructor, the unique 'kit spiral test <label> <pid> <nanos>' child.\nFixes, ranked (all after TRAIN3):\n(1) PRODUCT, the important one: 'kit proxy fs-remove-tree <root> <relative>' (legacy_operations_entry/legacy_terminal.spi:66-77) checks only that relative is non-empty and confined under root; root is unchecked, so 'fs-remove-tree C:\\Users\\i574n AppData' passes. Add: refuse when the target equals OR CONTAINS a forbidden root. The forbidden set must hold even with a stripped environment: drive roots, C:\\Users and every folder directly under it, the Windows folder, plus USERPROFILE, TEMP/TMP, LOCALAPPDATA, APPDATA and SystemRoot when set. Lib-first: a file_system.delete_target_allowed (target) primitive with a test, used by rust_std_fs's rooted_remove_tree_within, so every rooted removal inherits it.\n(2) compiler-contracts/test-job-orphan-wait.ps1:23 'Remove-Item -Recurse -Force $env:KIT_JOBS_HOME' reads the variable at delete time, without -LiteralPath: keep the path in a local variable, use -LiteralPath, and call Assert-SafeDeleteRoot (i574n.github/scripts/safe-delete.ps1).\n(3) test-native-probes.ps1:22: the cwd is the temp root itself; drafted as kit-drafts\\delete-root-guard\\native-probes-cwd.do (refuse when TEMP/TMP is unset; use a unique child).\n(4) test-dev-workflow.ps1:194 and test-binary-publication.ps1:94-95 guard only with 'is under the temp root', which means the profile when TEMP is gone: add the TEMP/TMP-set check there too.\n(5) Linux only: kit_process/process.spi:388 remove_dir_all on a socket path taken from another process's /proc/<pid>/cmdline. Use remove_file, and require the path under the compiler's own runtime folder.\n(6) Hygiene: bundle_output_contract and filesystem_directory_contract delete literal '/tmp/kit-...' paths, which on Windows resolve to C:\\tmp\\...; harmless leaves, but give them the unique-child shape.\nOut of scope, already verified by main: test-regeneration.ps1:41 stale-workspace sweep, release hygiene's per-entry removal, bundle retention's per-file removal.")
inl note_of_delete_root_guard_5 () = Note ("DELETE-ROOT-GUARD", 5u32, "2026-10-29T13:39Z", "kit", "10-29 09:3x kit: the lib half of fix (1) is drafted, not compiled: C:\\home\\data\\kit-drafts\\delete-root-guard\\lib-delete-guard.spi, for lib file_system.spi before directory_delete, whose recursive Rust arm today is a bare std::fs::remove_dir_all with no checks. delete_refusal (target) (protected) refuses: a relative or empty path; a structural root that holds even with an empty environment (a drive root, X:\\Users and any single profile under it found by path depth without listing, X:\\Windows, /, /home and one home under it); and any target that IS or CONTAINS a protected root (delete_covers: the protected path starts with the target, compared segment-wise and ignoring case and slash direction). delete_protected_roots adds USERPROFILE, HOME, TEMP, TMP, TMPDIR, LOCALAPPDATA, APPDATA, SystemRoot and ProgramData when they're set. The test covers 11 refusals and 2 allowed paths (a unique child under TEMP; a repo .cache child). Still to check with the compiler: 'when' guards in matches, sm'.ends_with, listm'.length, and the listm'.fold argument order. Wiring: directory_delete recursive and kit's rooted_remove_tree_within both call delete_refusal and fail with its reason. Lands through the lib window after TRAIN3, with the lib gate's negative control (guard disabled -> the test is red).")
inl note_of_delete_root_guard_6 () = Note ("DELETE-ROOT-GUARD", 6u32, "2026-10-29T13:41Z", "main", "10-29 09:4x main: safe-delete.ps1 FIXED per kit's merge note n4. It refused only targets EQUAL to a forbidden root, so 'C:\\Users' or '...\\AppData' passed and would have taken the profile or LOCALAPPDATA with them. Now a target that EQUALS OR CONTAINS any forbidden root is refused. The set holds with a stripped env: known-folder APIs (UserProfile, LocalApplicationData, ApplicationData, Windows, System, ProgramFiles x64/x86, CommonApplicationData), the Users folder and EVERY profile under it, every drive root, GetTempPath, plus USERPROFILE, HOME, TEMP, TMP, LOCALAPPDATA, APPDATA, SystemRoot and ProgramData when set. -Under now also refuses the base itself. Proof: 20/20 PASS, including C:\\Users, AppData, C:\\Users\\Public, Under-equal-base, and the profile, LocalAppData and Users with USERPROFILE, LOCALAPPDATA, APPDATA, TEMP and TMP ALL stripped. scratch-archive's tests still pass (a scratch folder under TEMP is allowed, since a child of the temp root contains no forbidden root). kit's product gap (kit proxy fs-remove-tree never checks root) gets the same equals-or-contains rule in rust_std_fs rooted_remove_tree_within, after TRAIN3 (kit).")
inl note_of_delete_root_guard_7 () = Note ("DELETE-ROOT-GUARD", 7u32, "2026-10-29T14:48Z", "kit", "10-29 10:4x kit: the lib delete guard is PROVEN on the node, in a fresh spiral mirror kit-delguard (synced during the TRAIN3 freeze; -RequireCore 05E94769A86A9B9C). The first gate (job-1793284669264) answered the open syntax questions: 8 type errors, because listm'.fold and listm'.length don't exist (the core listm has them) and listm'.map works on list', not list. The 'when' guards and sm'.ends_with were fine. Fixed draft: positive job-1793285007688 'tests: passed 1 failed 0', type errors 0; negative control job-1793285239910 (delete_covers forced to false) 'failed: a_recursive_delete_refuses_roots_profiles_and_their_ancestors' on rust, type errors 0, so the test catches a guard that no longer protects ancestors. Files: kit-drafts\\delete-root-guard\\lib-delete-guard.spi (the slice) and file_system.with-guard.spi (the patched file, made against the live lib at 10:4x). Still to do at the lib window: re-merge against the live file_system.spi, wire directory_delete recursive to fail with delete_refusal's reason, then kit's rooted_remove_tree_within (fix 1).")
inl note_of_delete_root_guard_8 () = Note ("DELETE-ROOT-GUARD", 8u32, "2026-10-29T15:06Z", "main", "10-29 11:1x main, step 2 (lib file_system) PROOF STATE. Draft: C:\\home\\data\\main-drafts\\fs-guard\\apply.ps1. It adds delete_target_is_protected (drive roots, '/', /home/<x>, <drive>:\\Users and each profile under it, <drive>:\\Windows, and equals-or-contains any of USERPROFILE/HOME/TEMP/TMP/TMPDIR/LOCALAPPDATA/APPDATA/SystemRoot/ProgramData). directory_delete (its F# and Rust arms) and remove_dir_all refuse such targets with failwith. directory_delete moves below get_full_path, since Spiral resolves top-down; every in-file user comes after get_full_path anyway. SAFE TEST DESIGN: no test ever deletes a real protected root. The negative uses a SURROGATE: a TEMP scratch folder set as LOCALAPPDATA inside the test process through environment_restored (real NUL via sm'.char_text 0u8, then restored). Proof fs-guard-proof-2: control (unguarded) deletes the surrogate, so the test FAILS through the real delete path; the guard arm REFUSES it, so the test PASSES; no surrogate left behind. The other 4 guard-arm tests (predicate, scratch delete, get_full_path_2 and _keeps_the_drive) are VOID: 'Spiral cell timed out after 300s' (slowest 816 s under the TRAIN3 rerun plus pack-6b load). The same timeout hit the control's get_full_path test. Rerun when the box is quiet, then land after the env guard.")
inl note_of_delete_root_guard_9 () = Note ("DELETE-ROOT-GUARD", 9u32, "2026-10-29T15:34Z", "main", "10-29 11:3x main: the env half is landed (GRANULAR-HEAL note, env.spi BB39D5562692). The delete half: safe-delete.ps1 in use; the lib file_system guard is drafted, with its surrogate negative proven (refused under the guard, deleted without it), and 4 tests VOID by load timeouts awaiting a quiet rerun.")
inl note_of_delete_root_guard_10 () = Note ("DELETE-ROOT-GUARD", 10u32, "2026-10-29T15:46Z", "kit", "10-29 11:4x kit: kit-side fixes (2), (3) and (4) are APPLIED in kit/compiler-contracts (unstaged). The guards are inline: kit's AGENTS.md says kit knows no external project, so it doesn't dot-source i574n.github's safe-delete.ps1. (3) test-native-probes.ps1: refuses when TEMP or TMP is unset; the isolated cwd is a fresh GetTempPath()\\kit-native-probes-<guid> child, never the temp root. (2) test-job-orphan-wait.ps1: the same TEMP/TMP refusal; the jobs home sits in a local variable; the delete is -LiteralPath and only runs when the leaf is its own kit-orphan-wait-<pid> (before, it read the env var at delete time, without -LiteralPath). (4) test-dev-workflow.ps1 and test-binary-publication.ps1: the TEMP/TMP refusal before the fixture, because their 'under the temp root' cleanup check means 'under the profile' when TEMP is gone. Proof through the real path: all 4 parse; test-job-orphan-wait.ps1 and test-native-probes.ps1 run in a child with TEMP and TMP REMOVED throw at their new guard line (4 and 22) before any job, folder or delete. Remaining: (1) kit's fs-remove-tree calls main's delete_target_is_protected after main's lib slice lands, with kit's wrapper still refusing relative paths; (5) Linux socket remove_dir_all -> remove_file; (6) the literal /tmp leaves. Lib half: main's slice lands; kit's draft is archived (kit-drafts\\archive\\20261029-delete-guard-superseded).")
inl note_of_delete_root_guard_11 () = Note ("DELETE-ROOT-GUARD", 11u32, "2026-10-29T16:07Z", "main", "10-29 12:0x main: step 2 (lib file_system guard) PROVEN, job-1793289735035-main-fs-guard-proof-6. Guard arm 6/6: kit's Windows case list (refused C:\\, C:\\Users, C:\\Users\\me, c:/users/someone, C:\\Users\\me\\AppData, C:\\Users\\me\\AppData\\Local\\Temp, C:\\Windows, ''; allowed C:\\home\\git\\kit\\.cache\\x and TEMP\\kit-run-42), the profile/temp predicate, a real scratch-tree delete, the surrogate refusal, and both get_full_path tests. Control (tests only): the surrogate test FAILS (the unguarded delete removes the TEMP surrogate set as LOCALAPPDATA), so the guard is what makes it pass; no surrogate left behind. Changes from earlier drafts: an empty path is refused always; a relative path is resolved and then checked (kit's wrapper stays strict); STRUCTURAL rules for every profile's AppData, its Local/Roaming/LocalLow and Local\\Temp (kit's 'C:\\Users\\me\\AppData' case showed env containment only covers the current user); real NUL via sm'.char_text 0u8. safe-delete.ps1 got the same per-profile AppData rule (22/22). Pre-land round trip found 3 blank-line slips (0 non-blank); fs-guard\\land.ps1 re-applies onto the live file at land time, canonicalizes, and refuses unless the result is a blank-only fixpoint. It lands LAST in today's lib window, after kit's merged-lib, which also touches file_system.")
inl note_of_delete_root_guard_12 () = Note ("DELETE-ROOT-GUARD", 12u32, "2026-10-29T16:44Z", "kit", "10-29 12:4x kit: fix (1) path settled. Measured by main with a 1-line probe calling delete_target_is_protected, built to Rust: the landed predicate gives 827 lines (main_0 297: the 9-variable fold unrolled plus the nested list-pattern match, 25 match levels); a ':~' join-point pass gives 888 (worse: specialized per static name, and the decision tree stays); kit's flat version (C:\\home\\data\\kit-drafts\\delete-root-guard\\compact-predicate.spi: a segments array plus count, all shape rules OR'ed, a recursive join point over the env names) gives 690 (delete_shape_protected 122, delete_variable_protected 40; the rest is get_full_path 106, normalize_path 62 and boilerplate every file_system caller pays). Earlier, the landed predicate pushed kit_legacy_operations 868 -> 2067 and a standalone kit_delete_guard crate to 1200 lines, over the 1000 gate. Plan: main lands the flat text as the lib predicate with its 6 tests unchanged, AFTER 'TRAIN4 published' (so TRAIN4 compiles against one lib); then kit re-wires the parked kit_delete_guard crate (C:\\home\\data\\kit-drafts\\delete-root-guard\\kit_delete_guard-crate, notebook 1/1) into fs-remove-tree in TRAIN5, re-measuring the crate against the gate.")
inl note_of_delete_root_guard_13 () = Note ("DELETE-ROOT-GUARD", 13u32, "2026-10-29T16:49Z", "main", "10-29 12:4x main: PREDICATE SIZE FIX, measured and proven, landing after 'TRAIN4 published'. kit measured the landed predicate at about 600 generated Rust lines per caller crate, which pushes kit's crates over the 1000-line gate. Main's measurement: a probe whose 1-line main calls file_system.delete_target_is_protected, built to Rust. Landed: 827 lines; main_0 is 297 (the 9-variable fold UNROLLED at compile time, plus the 10 list patterns compiled into a 25-level nested match). Main's first fix, turning the helpers into ':~' join points: 888, WORSE (join points specialize per static name literal; the decision tree stays). kit's flat draft (C:\\home\\data\\kit-drafts\\delete-root-guard\\compact-predicate.spi: a segments array plus count and flat OR'ed rules, and a recursive variable scan): 690 lines (delete_shape_protected 122 + delete_variable_protected 40; the rest is get_full_path 106, normalize_path 62 and runtime that any file_system caller already pays). The semantics were checked rule by rule against the landed version. Proof job-1793292275945: 6/6 pass, 0 type errors (kit's Windows cases, the profile/temp predicate, a scratch delete, the surrogate refusal, get_full_path x2). Land: main-drafts fs-guard\\land-flat.ps1 (reader guard, re-apply onto the live file, canonical blank-only fixpoint, undo copy) after TRAIN4 publishes, since TRAIN4's regen compiles kit crates against the live lib. Lesson: in Spiral, static lists in a fold and nested list patterns unroll in generated code; for size-sensitive lib helpers, use flat boolean rules over an array.")
inl note_of_delete_root_guard_14 () = Note ("DELETE-ROOT-GUARD", 14u32, "2026-10-29T17:43Z", "main", "10-29 13:42 main: scratch-archive.ps1 trap closed (found by git-a6 at 13:40 and hit by main at 13:39). -Only filtered RESTORES only, so `-Root <dir> -Only <name>` archived EVERY folder under Root, and with -Apply would have removed them all after verify. Main's dry run `-Root <scratchpad> -Only kitbin` swept 50 folders (24075 files, 0.97 GB). git-a6's `-Root spiral-bin-candidates -Only pack6c` would have taken the staged runner. Fix (i574n.github/scripts/scratch-archive.ps1, unstaged): in archive mode -Only now filters the folder names (-like) and throws \"-Only '<p>' matches no folder under <Root>; nothing archived\" on an empty match. Proven: -Only kitbin -> 1 folder, 1 file (dry); -Only no-such-folder -> exit 1. The applied run 20261029-134246 archived only kitbin (the stale A5B55974/CD2F0D1A scratch kit copy; sweep-4 row 7 closed, and the hourly sweep cron now names %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\bin\\kit.exe). Lesson: a filter parameter must narrow every mode it appears in, or refuse.")
inl note_of_delete_root_guard_15 () = Note ("DELETE-ROOT-GUARD", 15u32, "2026-10-29T19:22Z", "main", "10-29 15:20 main: kit's flat delete_target_is_protected predicate LANDED in lib file_system.spi, B5339FEFFC2E -> 633EE8887B75 (proven 6/6 on 6b, job-1793292275945-main-fs-guard-flat-proof; generated Rust 690 vs 827 lines). Landed in the 10-29 15:19 lib gap (after kit's TRAIN4 notebooks end, after fiscal's 4), through main's window sequence: reader + held-window check, proven base hashes asserted, canonical round trip (fixpoint true, blank-only diff), private MIX_BUILD_PATH. document_test after the gap lands: 22 passed (15:22, private MIX_BUILD_PATH), closing run after git-a6 console.spi follows. Undo copies under main scratchpad land-*/ (durable scripts C:\\home\\data\\main-drafts\\window-1029). x was sent the new hash for its fs-heal-4 rebase. kit's kit_delete_guard re-measure (1000 lines) now has its base.")
inl note_of_delete_root_guard_16 () = Note ("DELETE-ROOT-GUARD", 16u32, "2026-10-29T19:33Z", "kit", "2026-10-29T19:33 kit: kit_delete_guard re-measured on main's flat predicate (lib file_system 633EE8887B75). Node job job-1793302219789-kit-guard-compile-6b ran under -RequireCore 31CC96520D6B7269 (L6 guard ok); node-regen-compare printed core 31CC96520D6B7269. Result: kit_delete_guard 802 generated lines (under 1000), 0 compile failures, 0 missing backend arms. The parked draft had two test lines joined into one; it is fixed and the test gained 3 cases: C:\\Users refused, C:\\ refused, a kit .cache folder allowed. Next (TRAIN5): wire kit's fs-remove-tree through kit_delete_target_refusal, then cargo check and the notebook run the test.")
inl note_of_delete_root_guard_17 () = Note ("DELETE-ROOT-GUARD", 17u32, "2026-10-29T22:35Z", "main", "10-29 18:3x main: self-audit of the 36 scripts main wrote or changed today (org scripts, install-pack, type_probe, every window and heal script). Parse errors: 0. \"$var:\" interpolation traps: 0. Recursive deletes: 5. scratch-archive and safe-delete were already guarded. profile-mirror's cleanup of its TEMP work folder now goes through safe-delete's Remove-TreeSafely; tested to remove a scratch folder and to REFUSE C:\\Users\\i574n (\"is or contains ... a drive root, profile\"). install-pack's two removals of its own PID-unique stage folder stay as they are: by construction the leaf is one it created, and dot-sourcing org tooling into a spiral-repo script would add a cross-repo dependency the node may not have. PSScriptAnalyzer is not installed; installing it means a download, which is left for the user to decide.")
inl note_of_delete_root_guard_18 () = Note ("DELETE-ROOT-GUARD", 18u32, "2026-10-29T23:45Z", "main", "10-29 19:45 main: scratch-archive.ps1 had a write-after-archive gap, now closed (51DEC6551474 -> 4A73EC186D9A). Its blob verification was sound: each file is read once, stored by sha256, and the zip re-hashed. But -Apply removed each folder without checking that it was unchanged since the read, so a file appended, rewritten or created in between was deleted, with only its older content archived. FIX: stat before reading, and skip a file as '(changed while reading)' if its size moved (originals kept, exit 1). Then record length plus mtime ticks per file, and before removing a folder, re-walk it and throw if any file changed or appeared. PROOF on scratch Roots: quiet folders archive, remove and restore byte-identically on both versions. A live writer (a child pwsh appending every 20 ms; mine, stopped by command line afterwards) gave: OLD removed the busy folder, losing the appends after its read (the positive control); NEW exited 1 and kept it. Found while auditing archive validity after the profile-mirror truncation (PROFILE-MIRROR-TO-NODE n10).")
inl note_of_delete_root_guard_19 () = Note ("DELETE-ROOT-GUARD", 19u32, "2026-10-30T04:05Z", "main", "10-30 00:05 main: FIXED a dry-run that writes, in the shared i574n.github/scripts/lib-guarded-do.ps1 (2F79F05C4265 -> 5F913C088DED; found by fiscal). Its dry pass added 'dry-run' only to edit statements, so write <<< >>> blocks REALLY wrote during a dry pass, even with -DryRun. Reproduced on a scratch root (positive control: the old -DryRun created made-by-write.txt). kit do --dry-run REFUSES programs that write ('covers edit, read and fact programs'), so the fix strips write blocks from the dry pass and refuses up front (exit 4) if any write target already exists, so nothing half-applies (fiscal's apply pass had hit 'already exists'). PROOF: (a) -DryRun writes nothing and leaves edits unapplied; (b) apply writes the file and applies the edit; (c) an existing target exits 4 with nothing changed. Edit-only programs are unaffected. Durable: C:\\home\\data\\main-drafts\\night-1030\\lgd-fix.")
inl note_of_delete_root_guard_20 () = Note ("DELETE-ROOT-GUARD", 20u32, "2026-10-30T11:24Z", "tibia-lane", "10-30 07:4x tibia: FIXED (unstaged in tibia), live/run-policy-scenario.ps1. The client folder now comes from [Environment]::GetFolderPath('ApplicationData'), the known-folder API, so it holds even with APPDATA stripped (checked: a child shell without APPDATA still gets C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Roaming). The script throws if that path isn't fully qualified, and both deletes take -LiteralPath under bot\\ only. Parse 0 errors. A standalone guard in place of dot-sourcing i574n.github, because tibia is a public repo whose scripts must run without the org checkout.")
inl note_of_delete_root_guard_21 () = Note ("DELETE-ROOT-GUARD", 21u32, "2026-11-01T03:36Z", "kit", "10-31 23:4x kit groom (main 23:33, user: cadence/drift): Active -> PLANNED. No note in over 12 h, so nobody is working it this week; Planned keeps it in agile next at its priority (never auto-Paused, R3). First step when picked = the next step in its latest note.")
inl note_of_delete_root_guard_22 () = Note ("DELETE-ROOT-GUARD", 22u32, "2026-11-01T04:41Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 00:41 compiler (FYI for kit, per main 00:5x): CI ubuntu has been red since 10-31 on this item's test file_system directory_delete_refuses_a_protected_surrogate_root. Cause: env.environment_snapshot_usable refuses to restore 'before' once the test has added LOCALAPPDATA, because the variable is set now but missing from the snapshot. Compiler is landing a one-line fix in lib/spiral/env.spi (the Linux critical list becomes [PATH; HOME]); no change to file_system.spi. Details, program and gates are on CI-LINUX-ENV-RESTORE-GUARD.")
inl note_of_delete_root_guard_23 () = Note ("DELETE-ROOT-GUARD", 23u32, "2026-11-01T06:24Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 02:24 compiler: FYI the env.spi guard fix for this item's Linux CI red is LANDED (02:24, one line, see CI-LINUX-ENV-RESTORE-GUARD). No file_system.spi change.")
inl note_of_delete_root_guard_24 () = Note ("DELETE-ROOT-GUARD", 24u32, "2026-11-02T14:00Z", "x", "2026-11-02 09:59 x: DECISION RECORD (typed; groom of user waits, user 11-02: 'decisions waiting on me get met autonomously/deterministically with tests/benchs/typefeatureset modeling/backups'). kind=ByDefault. QUESTION: May PSScriptAnalyzer be downloaded/installed to lint the scripts' recursive deletes? RULE (written before acting): Default = install it with Install-Module PSScriptAnalyzer -Scope CurrentUser. The downloads-standing-ok and latest-of-everything memories already allow it, it stays in the user profile, and Uninstall-Module undoes it. BACKUP/UNDO: Undo with Uninstall-Module PSScriptAnalyzer. No system scope. OWNER: main. NEXT: Install PSScriptAnalyzer at user scope and run it over the 36 audited scripts plus the lib/kit delete callers. VETO: the user can reverse this at any time; the backup above is the undo.")
inl note_of_delete_root_guard_25 () = Note ("DELETE-ROOT-GUARD", 25u32, "2026-11-05T04:17Z", "resilience", "AUDIT (resilience): both guards already EXIST; the gap is uniform application, not absence. SCRIPT side - Assert-SafeDeleteRoot is defined in i574n.github\\scripts\\safe-delete.ps1 (+ safe-delete-test.ps1) but CONSUMED by only ONE script, scratch-archive.ps1. The other recursive-delete scripts do Remove-Item -Recurse / Remove-TreeSafely WITHOUT the shared guard: node-ship, node-proof, node-disk-cleanup, node-archive-cleanup, profile-mirror, remote, respell-repo, lib-reanchor, node-temp-contain, node-regen-compare (verify each; some route through Remove-TreeSafely which may or may not assert the safe-root rule). LIB side - kit\\src\\rust_std_fs has a rooted delete-policy family (rooted_domain/effects/negative/policy_probe/terminal.spi) = the file_system.directory_delete guard; confirm every remove_dir_all caller (5 in rust_std_fs_mutation\\mutation.rs, 3 in rust_std_fs_runtime.rs) routes through the rooted policy rather than calling std fs directly. Un-gated next step: wire Assert-SafeDeleteRoot into the ~10 unguarded scripts and verify against safe-delete-test.ps1 patterns (empty/relative/drive-root/profile/temp-root must all refuse) - deferred to a pass with box headroom so the guard test can run. No blind mass edits on a saturated box. resilience.")
inl note_of_delete_root_guard_26 () = Note ("DELETE-ROOT-GUARD", 26u32, "2026-11-05T04:42Z", "resilience", "LIB half CONFIRMED GAP (resilience, code read): in kit\\src\\rust_std_fs the only PRODUCTION remove_dir_all is cleanup_tree (rooted_effects.spi:41) = raw std::fs::remove_dir_all(path).is_ok(), with NO safe-root assertion; all other remove_dir_all are test teardown. The rooted_* family (rooted_entry_exists/rooted_list_names/read_regular_limited) is READ/LIST sandboxing to a root, NOT delete-target safety. So the lib guard this item wants (file_system.directory_delete refuses empty/relative/drive-root/profile/temp-root) is NOT present - cleanup_tree is an unguarded recursive delete. Fix: route cleanup_tree (and any directory_delete) through a shared assert mirroring Assert-SafeDeleteRoot before remove_dir_all. resilience.")
inl note_of_delete_root_guard_27 () = Note ("DELETE-ROOT-GUARD", 27u32, "2026-11-05T04:51Z", "resilience", "CRITICAL REFINEMENT (resilience, verified against script delete targets) - do NOT blind-wire the current guard. Assert-SafeDeleteRoot refuses any path that is OR IS INSIDE a forbidden root (Test-PathContains matches startsWith(root+sep)), and forbidden roots include the temp path, LOCALAPPDATA, APPDATA and every profile dir. But the unguarded scripts legitimately delete INSIDE those roots: node-disk-cleanup ($temp=$env:TEMP, deletes temp subdirs), node-temp-contain, node-archive-cleanup (LOCALAPPDATA\\kit), and the critical-mirror/profile-mirror staging dirs. So wiring the current Assert-SafeDeleteRoot into them would BREAK 5+ cleanup scripts (guard refuses their valid temp/cache subdir deletes). The current over-refusal is the SAFE fail-closed direction (good) but unusable for cleanup. Real fix = TWO-TIER guard: ALWAYS hard-refuse the catastrophic set (empty/relative/drive-root, a profile-or-temp ROOT itself, an ANCESTOR of a forbidden root like C:\\Users), but ALLOW a vetted deep descendant via -Under + a min-depth (e.g. >=2 levels below the forbidden root AND inside the declared base). Relaxing a safety guard moves toward fail-open, so this needs careful test coverage (extend safe-delete-test.ps1 with allow-cases for temp subdirs under a base), NOT a rush. Scripts that delete OUTSIDE forbidden roots (node-ship/node-proof/node-regen-compare/draft-regen-bundle work dirs if under C:\\home\\git) can adopt the current guard as-is. resilience.")
inl kit_path_tag_parse () = Task ("KIT-PATH-TAG-PARSE", "path_tag_contract/model.spi fails to parse: a record brace at column 0", TaskKind, P2, D1, 100u32, Done, "", "kit,heal,codegen", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_kit_path_tag_parse () = Planning ("KIT-PATH-TAG-PARSE", Minutes 30u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_kit_path_tag_parse_1 () = Note ("KIT-PATH-TAG-PARSE", 1u32, "2026-10-29T14:10Z", "kit", "10-29 10:1x kit: path_tag_contract/model.spi does not parse ('ParserErrors' at 0-based line 15: 'Expected one of: inl, let, union, nominal, prototype, type, instance, and, open'), seen identically on base and candidate in two regen-compare proofs (GRANULAR-HEAL n87 on 10-28, BANG-MACRO-CLEANUP n42 today). Cause: the record path_tag_indices is written ':== path_tag_indices = {' with its closing '}' at column 0 (line 16), so the brace reads as a new top-level statement. Every other kit record uses the indented form (':== x =' then an indented '{ ... }'). Fix: re-lay that record, regen the crate, and check whether anything consumes it (a crate that never compiled has no consumers that built).")
inl note_of_kit_path_tag_parse_2 () = Note ("KIT-PATH-TAG-PARSE", 2u32, "2026-10-29T14:16Z", "kit", "10-29 10:1x kit: drafted C:\\home\\data\\kit-drafts\\path-tag-parse\\path-tag-record.do (2 replacements): there are TWO column-0 record braces in model.spi, the record type at :12 and the record value at :51; both get the indented form used everywhere else. Context: path_tag_contract and its consumers (path_tag_negative_probe, path_tag_policy_probe, proxy_search_entry) are not workspace members and have no Cargo.toml. They are Spiral-only contract/probe packages that kit's regen never compiles, which is why the parse error only appears when a whole-tree compare touches them. Applying the draft is cheap and keeps them parseable; if nothing is meant to run them, archive them instead (archive-never-delete), but not both. Apply after TRAIN3 (the rerun's notebooks read the live src now).")
inl note_of_kit_path_tag_parse_3 () = Note ("KIT-PATH-TAG-PARSE", 3u32, "2026-10-29T22:52Z", "kit", "2026-10-29T22:52 kit: status for next session. The two column-0 record braces (model.spi :12 type, :51 value) were re-laid in the tree before TRAIN4 and are installed with C528BE4D. They are not yet PROVEN to parse: path_tag_contract has no Cargo.toml, so neither regen nor draft-regen-bundle compiles it, and today's BANG compare (job-1793303438170) did not include it. This morning's failure (ParserErrors at model.spi:15, 'Expected one of: inl, let, union ...') came from a compare whose bundle did include it. Proof recipe: compile src/path_tag_contract/path_tag_terminal.spi (or a consumer probe such as path_tag_policy_probe/main.spi) with the installed compiler (core 31CC96520D6B7269) to Rust; the ParserErrors line must be gone. Use a real compile, not 'spiral --check', which caps at 30 s and exits 0 on parse errors until pack 7. Not run tonight: git-a6's pack-7 review holds the machine (~5 h).")
inl note_of_kit_path_tag_parse_4 () = Note ("KIT-PATH-TAG-PARSE", 4u32, "2026-10-29T23:04Z", "kit", "2026-10-29T23:04 kit: DONE, proven on the node at core 31CC96520D6B7269 (node-regen-compare on the node's installed compiler, -RequireCore, L6 guard ok). path_tag_contract has no Cargo.toml and no main, so the proof compiles its consumer path_tag_policy_probe (':< path_tag_contract.model'), given a throwaway Cargo.toml in the bundle only (nothing added to the tree). Control run job-1793315020873: base = HEAD model.spi (column-0 braces) -> FAILED with this morning's exact 'ParserErrors ... line = 15 ... Expected one of: inl, let, union, nominal, prototype, type, instance, and, open'; cand = the re-laid model.spi installed with C528BE4D -> compiles, 152 lines, 0 failures, 0 missing arms. The fix is shipped with TRAIN4.")
inl runner_stale () = Task ("RUNNER-STALE", "Cells runner spiral.exe is behind apps/spiral HEAD on box and node, and differs between them; rebuild once after TRAIN3 + the pack-6 install, swap safely, ship to the node", Story, P1, D2, 90u32, Planned, "EOIE-RELEASE-TRAIN", "node,cells,runner,upkeep,wipe-hiccup,team:main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_runner_stale () = Planning ("RUNNER-STALE", Minutes 75u32, Load Heavy, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_runner_stale_1 () = Note ("RUNNER-STALE", 1u32, "2026-10-29T14:13Z", "compiler", "10-29 10:1x compiler: filed from the compiler lane's node upkeep (assigned by main 10:1x). Cells resolves the runner as opts[:spiral] || SPIRAL_EXE || default_spiral() (apps/cells/lib/spiral/cells/toolchain.ex:42). Both machines run a runner older than apps/spiral HEAD, and the two differ from each other: box workspace\\target\\release\\spiral.exe built 2026-10-26 06:28, sha16 7CEC582FD5E29728 (no SPIRAL_EXE, default path); node SPIRAL_EXE = the same path, built 2026-10-25 17:28, sha16 04C10A363AC987E1. apps/spiral changed at 3f1b7588 (2026-10-27 05:43): spiral.spi +/-200 lines, spiral.rs regenerated (10,061+/13,309-), spiral.livemd removed, plus an unstaged apps/spiral/build.ps1 edit. So box and node Cells verdicts can differ by runner, and neither runs HEAD's runner. Visibility added now: i574n.github/scripts/remote.ps1 status prints node runner/compiler and box runner/compiler sha16 + build time side by side (unstaged; verified: compilers match 05E94769A86A9B9C, runners differ). SEQUENCE (main): (1) kit's 'TRAIN3 published'; (2) the pack-6(b) install; (3) broadcast to all lanes and check for live Cells readers (lib-guarded-do's reader rule), then rebuild the box runner as a singleton (cargo --release in workspace), smoke one cell, rename-swap the old exe aside, log; (4) ship to the node by stage + smoke + rename-swap, then verify with remote.ps1 status (runner lines equal) and -RequireCore; (5) then COMPILER-TMP-RETIRE. A swap under a running notebook is the failure mode to avoid.")
inl note_of_runner_stale_2 () = Note ("RUNNER-STALE", 2u32, "2026-10-29T14:37Z", "compiler", "10-29 10:37 compiler: scope widened (main, kit's REMOTE-LAPTOP-LANE n19): the node's own C:\\home\\git\\spiral checkout is stale too, at d095f13d with 112 lib .spi/.spir files vs the box's cec8840c with 236 (no lib\\spiral\\iggy.spi), so anything on the node resolving the lib from its checkout gets an old lib. remote.ps1 status now prints a checkout line (HEAD + lib file count + names/sizes digest) for node and box beside the runner and compiler lines. The quiet-window pass refreshes the node checkout too: mirrors link deps\\ from it (agent-sync.md), so check for live mirror jobs first (memory unlink-mirror-junctions-first).")
inl note_of_runner_stale_3 () = Note ("RUNNER-STALE", 3u32, "2026-10-29T16:52Z", "compiler", "10-29 12:52 compiler: STAGED, not swapped (main: no swap until kit's 'TRAIN4 published', so the whole train sees one runner). Built HEAD's committed apps/spiral/spiral.rs (3f1b7588, which already carries the mimalloc allocator line) with cargo +nightly-2026-10-18 --profile release-unwind --package spiral into a SEPARATE CARGO_TARGET_DIR (no tree write, no contention with TRAIN4's workspace target): %LOCALAPPDATA%\\spiral-bin-candidates\\runner-3f1b7588\\target\\release-unwind\\spiral.exe, sha16 2E5F532E73EEAF14, 5m38s (job-1793292311432). Smoke: --help exit 0 with the same 6 commands as the live 7CEC582F; a real Rust cell (samples/closure_pair/main.rs via 'spiral.exe rust --rs-path') exit 0 on both, identical output. Deliberately NOT regenerated spiral.rs with the pack-6b compiler: RUNNER-STALE's goal is the runner = HEAD's apps/spiral; regenerating belongs to the normal apps/spiral build. SWAP PLAN after TRAIN4: broadcast + live Cells reader check, then move the live workspace\\target\\release\\spiral.exe aside (a running exe can be renamed on Windows, so in-flight cells keep the old one), copy the staged exe in, smoke again; then node-ship.ps1 the same exe to the node's SPIRAL_EXE path, then the node checkout update.")
inl note_of_runner_stale_4 () = Note ("RUNNER-STALE", 4u32, "2026-10-29T22:42Z", "compiler", "10-29 18:4x compiler: SWAPPED on kit's 'TRAIN4 published' (18:35:54, kit confirmed nothing of its own was using the old runner). BOX workspace\\target\\release\\spiral.exe 7CEC582FD5E29728 (10-26, stale) -> 2E5F532E73EEAF14 (HEAD 3f1b7588 build, release-unwind); old archived hash-verified at C:\\home\\data\\runner-archive-2026-10-29\\spiral.exe.7CEC582FD5E29728. NODE: node-ship 04C10A363AC987E1 (10-25) -> 2E5F532E73EEAF14, digest verified, previous kept as .replaced-runner-2E5F532E. SMOKE NOTE: the swap script's check (stdout must contain the program's 'runner-smoke 42') FAILED on the new runner, and its auto-restore could not run (the file was briefly held). Running the archived OLD runner on the identical 'rust --rs-path' smoke gave the same behavior (exit 0, the same 3 trace lines, no program text), so the criterion was wrong for both runners and the new one has behavioral parity; kept. Next swap: parity with the old runner is the criterion. 'runner swapped' sent to main and kit. Left: node checkout sync, deferred (other lanes' jobs on the node; 14 node-only files archived).")
inl note_of_runner_stale_5 () = Note ("RUNNER-STALE", 5u32, "2026-10-30T16:55Z", "compiler", "10-30 12:55 compiler: RUNNER READY TO SWAP (staged, not swapped). Built on the BOX from C:\\home\\git\\spiral\\workspace with the landed spiral.spi + spiral.rs (5ECE8F9946287738), cargo +nightly-2026-10-18 release-unwind in a private target seeded from the 10-29 warm target: 4 min, exe F1DD2E34F1E816C8, staged at C:\\home\\data\\cuda-python\\runner-F1DD2E34.exe. Why the box and not the node proof's exe (EB706BDE, built from the identical spiral.rs and Cargo.lock): spiral.rs reads env!(CARGO_MANIFEST_DIR) at 4 sites, so the proof exe's cells report code_path under c:/home/remote/pack8 (a mirror). A swap runner must be built from the real checkout path (memory runner-bakes-manifest-dir). The node's checkout has the same path, so this box exe ships to the node as on 10-29. Smoke: --help exit 0 (13 lines, same as live). A rust --rs-path cell (closure_pair) exits 0 with output byte-identical to the live 2E5F532E (1,437 B, 46ED123B03DE33A7, code_path included). Cuda: SPIRAL_PYTHON = scoop python312 (numpy 2.5.4) exit 0 with the ok line; SPIRAL_PYTHON = a nonexistent path exits 1 'cannot find the path', so the runner takes its interpreter from SPIRAL_PYTHON. The no-numpy control ran on the node (n5). Swap script C:\\home\\data\\cuda-python\\runner-swap.ps1 (10-29's, re-parameterized): dry run ok, live 2E5F532E, 0 users. Waiting on main + kit's go, then node-ship.ps1 for the node, then CUDA-CELLS-FLEET-PARITY's 5 sm' cuda rows on both machines.")
inl note_of_runner_stale_6 () = Note ("RUNNER-STALE", 6u32, "2026-10-30T18:04Z", "compiler", "10-30 14:04 compiler: SWAP REVERTED by a pre-registered rule; the box is back on 2E5F532E73EEAF14 (hash-verified, smoke exit 0); the node was never shipped. Sequence: box swap to F1DD2E34 at 13:0x (main + kit go), then kit's sm' gate on the new lib under kit's failing condition (numpy-less python 3.15 first on PATH, SPIRAL_PYTHON = python312): 63/72. Kit's 5 cuda failures (to_char_array, format, show, to_char_array_2, concat_array) PASS, so the cuda fix works. But 9 others failed. A quiet-box rerun of the 9 alone (job-1793382555570), same lib (sm' A683500F), per arm: NEW F1DD2E34 7/9, failing show_floats [zig] and show_floats_in_python_repr_layout [delphi] with 'Spiral cell timed out after 300s; the process tree was killed' (the slowest passing cell took 79 s, so this is a hang); OLD 2E5F532E 9/9. The other 7 were load (tibia saw the same file's load reds on the node this morning). The hang's target moved between runs (loaded: delphi/zig the other way round), so it's nondeterministic. Rule (agreed with main before the read): new fails and old passes = runner, so restore. F1DD2E34 is kept at C:\\home\\data\\runner-archive-2026-10-30\\spiral.exe.F1DD2E34F1E816C8. Checked so far: the child-process pipe sites (wait/BufReader) have the same shape in the committed (old) and regen (new) spiral.rs, so it's not an obvious pipe-order change. NEXT (box time needed, queued behind tibia's smoke and main's Cells window): (1) repeat the 2 tests 3x per arm to rule out a flake; (2) if it holds, run show_floats on the new exe under a CPU sampler (busy hang vs blocked wait); (3) build a runner from HEAD spiral.spi (no cuda edit) with the installed compiler. If that hangs too, it's a pack-7 Rust codegen regression in the regen, not the cuda edit; then bisect the 13,926-line regen diff by function. 2) Workaround for kit's cuda cells: scoop python312 first on PATH (72/72 this morning).")
inl note_of_runner_stale_7 () = Note ("RUNNER-STALE", 7u32, "2026-10-30T18:48Z", "compiler", "10-30 14:48 compiler: RUNNER CLEARED on follow-up, re-swap deferred to after TRAIN5 (kit's call). After n6's revert: (1) flake check, show_floats + show_floats_in_python_repr_layout ALONE, 3 rounds alternating new/old (job-1793384471396, quiet box, CPU sampler log C:\\home\\data\\cuda-python\\flake\\cpu-samples.tsv): all 6 runs 2/2, no timeout. (2) the original 9 together with the order REVERSED, old first (job-1793385454471): old 9/9, new 9/9, 4.1 min each. Tally for the two show_floats tests: new runner 1 failure in 8 runs (its first cold run, 9 cells in flight), old 0 in 5. The daemon-port confound is ruled out: lib_gate -Compiler process sets SPIRAL_CELLS_COMPILER=process, which skips the warm daemon (spiral.notebook.ex:26, notebook.ex:776, cells.ex:765). main's caveat stands: the old runner also passed cold and first, so the new runner's first-run path stays slightly suspect at n=8. PLAN: after TRAIN5 publishes (TRAIN5's launcher pins 2E5F532E and puts python312 first, so its cuda cells pass without the swap), re-swap the box to F1DD2E34 with runner-swap.ps1, then node-ship.ps1. Guard: one show_floats warm-up before a train's notebooks; on a no-load recurrence, revert and bisect (HEAD-spi pack-7 regen on the node, job-1793383855225, for the churn diff).")
inl note_of_runner_stale_8 () = Note ("RUNNER-STALE", 8u32, "2026-10-30T19:52Z", "compiler", "10-30 15:52 compiler: NODE RUNNER SWAPPED to F1DD2E34F1E816C8 (kit confirmed TRAIN5 is box-only, so the node could move now; the box stays on 2E5F532E until TRAIN5 publishes). node-ship.ps1 -Label runner-F1DD2E34. Pre-swap smoke on the node (the swap runs only on SMOKE-OK): a closure_pair rust --rs-path cell's JSON output from the staged new exe is byte-identical to the node's live old runner's, code_path included (the exe was built from C:\\home\\git\\spiral on the box, the same path as the node checkout). Digest 4DAB82B4CC4A96DF verified after the copy; the previous runner is kept as spiral.exe.replaced-runner-F1DD2E34. Post-swap cuda test on the node through the live runner, with numpy-less Programs\\Python312 FIRST on PATH: control (SPIRAL_PYTHON unset) exit 1 'No module named numpy' (the bug's own path); fixed (the node's User SPIRAL_PYTHON = scoop python312) exit 0 with the ok line. So the node half of CUDA-CELLS-FLEET-PARITY is done. LEFT: the box swap after TRAIN5 publishes (runner-swap.ps1, byte-equal smoke + auto-restore), then the box sm' cuda cells under kit's failing PATH (72/72 bar; the 5 cuda cells already passed on F1DD2E34 on the box at 13:3x).")
inl note_of_runner_stale_9 () = Note ("RUNNER-STALE", 9u32, "2026-10-30T23:10Z", "compiler", "10-30 19:10 compiler: DRIFT NOTE for the post-TRAIN5 box swap. main's LIB-CANON-NAMES (19:05, runtime.spi retired, consumers renamed to process / rust/clap) edited apps/spiral/spiral.spi again (now blob 8028a925; the cuda_python edit is intact; 0 'runtime.' refs left), but the landed apps/spiral/spiral.rs is still the pre-rename regen 5ECE8F9946287738. So the tree's .rs no longer matches its .spi. The rename is behaviour-neutral, so the node's F1DD2E34 stays fine, but the BOX swap after TRAIN5 must use a runner regenerated from the CURRENT spiral.spi: regen spiral.rs with the installed compiler (the mimalloc tail appended as build.ps1 does), cargo release-unwind from C:\\home\\git\\spiral\\workspace (private target, warm seed runner-cuda1030), the same byte-equal smoke vs the live runner, then swap box and node to that one hash so both machines match again. Land the new spiral.rs with it.")
inl note_of_runner_stale_10 () = Note ("RUNNER-STALE", 10u32, "2026-11-02T23:54Z", "main", "main 11-02 19:5x orphan sweep round 4: Active with no note for over 48 h and no lane driving it this week, so Active -> Planned at 90% (an Active status that nobody touches lies to agile wip). The next step stays the one in the latest note; it is back in agile next at its priority.")
inl note_of_runner_stale_11 () = Note ("RUNNER-STALE", 11u32, "2026-11-05T07:43Z", "compiler", "11-05 03:5x compiler: a concrete reason to rebuild: the box and node spiral.exe (box built 11-03 09:08) do not contain SPIRAL_PYTHON (byte scan), although spiral.spi has had it since 11-02 00:38; see SPIRAL-CUDA-RUNNER-PYTHON's 11-05 note. A rebuild via apps/spiral/build.ps1 (box, then ship to the node) unblocks CUDA-CELLS-FLEET-PARITY.")
inl spiral_char_nul_escape () = Task ("SPIRAL-CHAR-NUL-ESCAPE", "Spiral char literal '\\0' compiles to the digit '0' on every backend: the lexer drops unknown escapes, and 6 char renderers would write a raw NUL; fix both, one regression sample", Story, P1, D2, 100u32, Done, "PACK-7", "compiler,lexer,codegen,bug", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_spiral_char_nul_escape () = Planning ("SPIRAL-CHAR-NUL-ESCAPE", Minutes 60u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_spiral_char_nul_escape_1 () = Note ("SPIRAL-CHAR-NUL-ESCAPE", 1u32, "2026-10-29T14:46Z", "compiler", "10-29 10:4x compiler: reported by main 10:2x (lib env-snapshot guard; memory spiral-nul-literal-is-zero). CONFIRMED on every backend: nul = '\\0' and zero = '0' emit b'0'/b'0' (Rust), '0'/'0' (C) and '0'/'0' (F#). ROOT CAUSE: the lexer's char_quoted escape table (spiral_compiler.fs:43229) maps only \\n \\r \\t \\b, and any other escape falls through 'x -> x'. The string table (special_char :43247) has the same shape. FIX: apps/compiler/packs/apply-char-nul-escape.ps1 -Core <fs> [-Samples <dir>] [-Apply]. (1) Lexer: '0' -> NUL in char_quoted. (2) A real NUL must be ESCAPED by every renderer that wrote the raw char ('x -> string x'): litFsharp '\\000', litGleam \\u{0}, litLua \\0, codegenC \\0, C++ lit_string \\0, Python \\x00. The applier finds the 6 fallthroughs, checks each sits under a LitChar arm, and inserts the arm by file order. Already NUL-safe: Rust (control chars -> 0u8), Lean ('\\x00'), TypeScript and every numeric backend (Bend, Wasm, Calyx, Kotlin, Swift, OpenQasm, eBPF, Delphi #0, Zig @as(u8, 0)). All shared sections: pair-drift 0 touched pairs. Regression sample (one per bug): samples/char_nul_literal, written by -Samples ('\\0' =. '0' -> exit 1, else 0). Positive control on the INSTALLED compiler: C native exit 1 (the bug). STRINGS: \"\\0\" inside a string has the same bug, but a ripgrep census of every *.spi/*.spir under C:\\home\\git found NO string literal using \\0 (only backlog prose); its positive control found the 3 char uses main cited (base.spi:497, reflection.spi:57/:83; kit_cold_rebuild_matrix's '\\0' is raw macro text and stays '\\0' in matrix.rs). The string half is a separate edit (special_char plus each backend's string-literal escaper), left out of this pack because nothing uses it.")
inl note_of_spiral_char_nul_escape_2 () = Note ("SPIRAL-CHAR-NUL-ESCAPE", 2u32, "2026-10-30T02:01Z", "compiler", "10-29 22:0x compiler (groom): progress 0 Planned -> 85 Active. The applier apply-char-nul-escape.ps1 rides PACK-7 (a PACK-7 dep), whose candidate was built and reviewed today; the remaining 15% is main's PACK-7 install, then the char_nul_literal sample exiting 0 on the installed compiler.")
inl note_of_spiral_char_nul_escape_3 () = Note ("SPIRAL-CHAR-NUL-ESCAPE", 3u32, "2026-10-30T03:17Z", "compiler", "10-29 23:3x compiler: CLOSED. Installed with pack 7 (main 22:2x). run single-flight-20261029-223356 (pack 7 -Accept, installed core 9C899B30495F7DC5): samples/char_nul_literal exits 0 on Fsharp, C, Rust and Delphi (the sample exits 1 when '\\0' equals '0'); main's live probe also has Lua emitting '\\0', exit 0 (the 6b control emits '0', exit 1). The string half (special_char) stays out: no string literal in the corpus uses \\0 (n1 census).")
inl spiral_rust_net_text_guard () = Task ("SPIRAL-RUST-NET-TEXT-GUARD", "compiler: a Rust build that reaches a lib body with no Rust-capable arm fails in Spiral with the definition's location, not later at cargo; covers both a .NET-only text body (no run_target/backend_switch) and a backend_switch missing its Rust arm (today: compile_error! in the generated .rs)", TaskKind, P2, D3, 95u32, Planned, "LIB-DOTNET-ONLY-BODIES,PACK-10", "compiler,rust,backends,lib,team:compiler,compiler", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_spiral_rust_net_text_guard () = Planning ("SPIRAL-RUST-NET-TEXT-GUARD", Minutes 120u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_spiral_rust_net_text_guard_1 () = Note ("SPIRAL-RUST-NET-TEXT-GUARD", 1u32, "2026-10-29T17:55Z", "main", "10-29 13:5x main: split from LIB-DOTNET-ONLY-BODIES (closed today; its n11 left \"the compile-time guard ... compiler lane\"). Two shapes reach cargo today. (1) A general function whose only body is .NET text compiles silently into Rust as F# text (n1 census: am'.map', average, distinct, skip_while, parallel_map, transpose, sm'.join'; all Rust-reachable ones now have arms). (2) A backend_switch without a Rust arm emits compile_error!(\"backend_switch: ...no Rust arm yet\"), so Spiral->Rust passes and only cargo fails. kit hit this at 13:21 in TRAIN4 (file_system.create_directory from kit_schedule), and a node \"compiles\" verdict missed it. Interim safety net: i574n.github/scripts/node-regen-compare.ps1 now lists \"candidate crates with a missing backend arm\" (kit, positive-controlled), and the backend-switch-idiom memory says to grep the generated .rs. Done when a Rust build reaching either shape exits non-zero in Spiral naming the def and its file:line, with one regression sample per shape (regression-samples-allowed), shipped in a pack.")
inl note_of_spiral_rust_net_text_guard_2 () = Note ("SPIRAL-RUST-NET-TEXT-GUARD", 2u32, "2026-10-29T19:13Z", "main", "10-29 15:1x main: third shape for this guard, on TypeScript. A backend_switch without a TS arm compiles to `(() => { throw new Error(\"backend_switch: this lib/spiral function has no TypeScript arm yet\") })()`, so the program builds and fails only at RUN time, and only on the path that reaches it. Found with convert.i64 / u64 / i32, which have no TS arm (LIB-MACRO-HEAL n61). The guard should report a reached missing arm at Spiral compile time on every backend, not just Rust's compile_error!.")
inl note_of_spiral_rust_net_text_guard_3 () = Note ("SPIRAL-RUST-NET-TEXT-GUARD", 3u32, "2026-10-29T19:51Z", "main", "10-29 15:5x main: the convert.i32/i64/u64 TS missing arms named in n2 are filled by a lib draft (LIB-MACRO-HEAL n72, landing next window). The guard itself is still needed for the other missing arms.")
inl note_of_spiral_rust_net_text_guard_4 () = Note ("SPIRAL-RUST-NET-TEXT-GUARD", 4u32, "2026-10-29T20:59Z", "compiler", "10-29 17:0x compiler: DESIGN (read-only; finishing it needs a build). Why lib cannot do this alone: !!!!BackendSwitch evaluates EVERY arm on every backend for type validation, and the validation pass (spiral_compiler.fs:153661) runs the other arms with {s with backend = <that arm's backend>; seq = a fresh ResizeArray; i = backend_switch_validation_tags () (a counter from 1<<<30)}. Inside an arm, s.backend names the arm's own backend, not the build target, so a compile-time error in lib's fallback would fire on every backend. That is why lib/spiral/backend.spi:19-60 emits TEXT instead (Rust compile_error!, Zig @compileError, TypeScript throw, bare identifiers on C/Delphi/...), and the failure only surfaces at cargo/zig/run time.\nFix shape: (1) core, both cores as a section pair: an explicit 'validating' flag in the env, set at the validation site next to the fresh seq and tag counter (plus single-flight's twin of :153661). (2) A new intrinsic, e.g. !!!!MissingBackendArm('<msg>'): under validation it returns a placeholder of the arm's type t (what the text arms do today); on the emitting path it raises a Spiral type error naming the definition and its file:line. (3) lib backend.spi's `_ =>` fallback arms (and the .NET-only general-body case this item lists) switch from compile_error!/throw text to the intrinsic. That covers Rust, TypeScript (n2) and every other backend at once.\nGate: two regression samples (a backend_switch missing the built backend's arm; a .NET-only text body reached from Rust) must fail in Spiral with file:line, while a program that only VALIDATES those arms (built for a backend that has its arm) still compiles. Order: the compiler install first, then the lib change (the old compiler does not know the intrinsic).")
inl note_of_spiral_rust_net_text_guard_5 () = Note ("SPIRAL-RUST-NET-TEXT-GUARD", 5u32, "2026-10-29T21:49Z", "main", "10-29 17:5x main: MEASURED missing-arm census of the live lib (after window 2), a target list for this guard. Tool: C:\\home\\data\\main-drafts\\session-1029\\missing-arm-census.ps1; rows in missing-arm-census.tsv beside it (file, line, owner def, arms present, arms missing). 243 backend_switch blocks; missing TypeScript 144, Zig 132, Lua 106, Python 84, Fsharp 76, Gleam 65, Rust 47. Many are deliberately single-backend helpers (the am' Rust vec/slice family). The general-purpose ones are what matter. convert.try_convert, unbox, f32 and f64 have NO TypeScript arm, so convert.convert (= try_convert |> optionm.value) THROWS at run time on TS; today's i32/i64/u64 TS arms fixed only those three. Most of date_time (get_year, format, format_iso8601, min/max_value, to_calendar_utc, unix_epoch, specify_date_kind, to_universal_time, time_span, date_time_utc) is also missing on TS. Every one of those is a silent runtime throw today. With this guard each would become a compile-time error naming the def, but only if the guard ignores helpers that are intentionally single-backend, so it must fire only on a REACHED missing arm. CORRECTION 17:5x (measured right after posting): convert.convert does NOT throw on TS for an integer case. `convert.convert 5i32 : i64` compiles and prints 5 under bun, so try_convert's numeric cases resolve before its backend_switch is reached, and the census counts blocks, not reachable throws. The census is a list of CANDIDATE sites to probe per backend, not of confirmed bugs; only measured cases count as bugs (convert.i32/i64/u64 on TS were measured).")
inl note_of_spiral_rust_net_text_guard_6 () = Note ("SPIRAL-RUST-NET-TEXT-GUARD", 6u32, "2026-10-31T01:47Z", "compiler", "10-30 21:47 compiler: DECISION before code. The n4 design is narrowed: no new env field and no new intrinsic are needed. The single-flight core (shared with hopacsota) already carries the exact flag n4 asked for: RustGapTrace.validating, a ThreadLocal depth that BackendSwitch raises around every non-current arm (spiral_compiler.fs:160941-160946) and that hopacsota hands to its body threads through the validating/set_validating seams (159801). lib's fallbacks already emit a fixed marker on every native/TS backend. Change, one applier (packs/apply-missing-arm-guard.ps1, core only, lib untouched): (1) RustGapTrace gets `missing_arm`, one regex for lib's markers: `backend_switch[^\\n]*?\\bno\\s+\\w+\\s+arm` (Rust 'has no Rust arm yet' / 'no Rust arm (lib/spiral)', Zig, TypeScript incl. its line-continued text) and `backend_switch_has_no_\\w+_arm_in_lib_spiral` (Lean, C, Delphi, Wasm, Bend, Kotlin, Swift). (2) In EMacro, when validating = 0 (the build's own path) and an MText matches, raise_type_error with the macro text; the error carries s.trace, so it names the calling defs with file:line. (3) SPIRAL_RUST_GAP_TRACE=1 keeps its survey role: it reports every reached gap and does NOT raise, so one compile still lists all of them. Scope: shape 2 (backend_switch without the build's arm) on all 12 marked backends, including the unit-typed Rust/TS fallbacks, which today compile to a silent no-op comment. Not covered: shape 1 (a general function whose only body is .NET text; there is no marker to key on), and the Gleam/Lua/Fsharp/Python/Cpp fallbacks, which emit placeholders without a marker (a lib follow-up can add one). gpt5parallel core: not touched (no RustGapTrace there). Regression samples (one per shape covered): backend_switch_missing_arm_negative (a marker on the build path -> ExpectedErrors 'no C arm' style Contains) and backend_switch_validated_gap (all 16 arms, only Bend's is a marker -> compiles everywhere the matrix builds it, since Bend is only validated). Gate (status-changing pack): paired predict run (pack9-predict method) -> residual set P must be EMPTY, and every ok->error row must have the new error text; that error set is the list of reached lib gaps, each answered by a lib arm (lib first) or accepted as a real gap before the pack lands. Corpus check (tibia + kit + Kino lib tests) before landing, because unit-typed Rust fallbacks may be reached there today.")
inl note_of_spiral_rust_net_text_guard_7 () = Note ("SPIRAL-RUST-NET-TEXT-GUARD", 7u32, "2026-10-31T01:53Z", "compiler", "10-30 21:53 compiler: AMENDS n6 before any build. BACKEND-SWITCH-PROTOTYPES n2 (main) shows the UNIT fallbacks are load-bearing: date_time's 45 type-level calls rely on a missing arm being a silent () (Gleam-only imports). So the guard no longer matches the unit markers ('backend_switch: no Rust arm (lib/spiral)', 'no TypeScript arm (lib/spiral)'); regex now: 'backend_switch: this lib/spiral function has no[\\s\\\\]+\\w+\\s+arm|backend_switch_has_no_\\w+_arm_in_lib_spiral' (non-unit Rust/Zig/TS text, the unbound-identifier arms, and console/convert/sm' per-type gap markers such as console.spi:33 C printf of an unsupported type). Drafts written, unstaged, not built: apps/compiler/packs/apply-missing-arm-guard.ps1 (2 anchors, dry-run 1x each on today's core), samples/backend_switch_missing_arm_negative (+ matrix.psd1 ExpectedErrors 'reaches a lib/spiral backend_switch that has no arm for the') and samples/backend_switch_validated_gap (16 arms, only Bend's is a marker). Relation to main's strict-blast: strict makes a missing NON-UNIT arm an error on EVERY build (validation included); this guard fires only on the arm the build actually emits, in core, lib untouched. If strict lands, this guard still covers the per-type gap markers and any switch written directly with !!!!BackendSwitch. Next: Debug candidate build in a free heavy slot (kit-train5 holds it now), then the paired predict run.")
inl note_of_spiral_rust_net_text_guard_8 () = Note ("SPIRAL-RUST-NET-TEXT-GUARD", 8u32, "2026-10-31T02:19Z", "compiler", "10-30 22:19 compiler: BUILT and SMOKED. Candidate 'guard' (live core 4191A77F + packs/apply-missing-arm-guard.ps1 -> core BEFEDEC7AA8EB280, host EEBCBFF8658A1EF9 = installed host; Release, 359 s). probe.ps1: backend_switch_missing_arm_negative -> exit 5 'TracedError: This program reaches a lib/spiral backend_switch that has no arm for the Rust backend ... Macro: backend_switch_has_no_C_arm_in_lib_spiral', trace innermost-first with file:line:col and caret (main.spi:1:9 and 2:5), same on C; backend_switch_validated_gap -> ok on C (133 B) and Rust (504 B). CONTROL: the installed compiler compiles the negative sample ok (exit 0, 193 B), so the error is the guard's. BLAST RADIUS, reasoned from the markers: every matched non-unit marker except TypeScript's already fails the NATIVE build today (Rust compile_error!, Zig @compileError, unbound identifiers on Lean/C/Delphi/Wasm/Bend/Kotlin/Swift, and console/convert/sm' per-type markers are the same kind), so a test or consumer that builds green today cannot reach one; the guard only moves those failures earlier. New failures are possible only where a reached marker never ran: TypeScript (run-time throw on an unexecuted path) and compile-only flows. Corpus check, therefore: lib_gate with SPIRAL_COMPILER_DLL=<guard> on the lib files whose tests target TypeScript (18 files, 114 TS target lines; base 20, sm' 17, parsing 17, date_time 8, reflection 7, env 6, listm' 5, seq 5, ...). main's strict-blast (BACKEND-SWITCH-PROTOTYPES) already measures a superset for sm'/date_time/base/file_system/am' (any reached non-unit missing arm on any target), so those 5 read from its strict-blast.txt instead of a second run. Queued: job-1793412988469-compiler-guard-predict (matrix predict, after PACK-9's; the matrix imports samples/core, not lib, so expect P empty and 0 status diffs).")
inl note_of_spiral_rust_net_text_guard_9 () = Note ("SPIRAL-RUST-NET-TEXT-GUARD", 9u32, "2026-10-31T04:55Z", "compiler", "10-31 00:55 compiler: NOT in pack 9, and two open points before it can ship. (1) pair-drift refuses a single-flight-only PartEval change ('PartEval changed only on the single-flight side'), so the guard needs a gpt5parallel side. gpt5parallel has no RustGapTrace and runs evaluation on Hopac jobs, so a ThreadLocal depth is not reliable there. The stateless test 's.i.Value >= 1 <<< 30' (validation uses backend_switch_validation_tags) is WRONG: join point bodies reset i = ref 0 (spiral_compiler.fs:160066, 160497), so a body first evaluated inside a validation would look non-validating. The gpt5parallel side needs a real 'validating' flag on its env, set at its two validation sites (153672 replay, 155487 validation). (2) A hopacsota hazard in the single-flight side as drafted: Session.Defer's body runs with adapter.set_validating 0 (~54059). A join point body spawned DURING another backend's arm validation therefore runs as not-validating, and under the parallel policy the guard could fire on that other backend's marker. RustGapTrace.report has the same blind spot today. Fix: capture adapter.validating () at spawn time and restore THAT value in the body, not 0 (a hopacsota-layer edit; written as its own decision before code). Neither affects the single-flight results measured in n8.")
inl note_of_spiral_rust_net_text_guard_10 () = Note ("SPIRAL-RUST-NET-TEXT-GUARD", 10u32, "2026-10-31T07:18Z", "compiler", "10-31 03:18 compiler: the guard's samples are OUT of the repo (user 01:5x: repo drift). Their whole content, so this note is the record: samples/backend_switch_missing_arm_negative/main.spi = ':= main () : i32 = /     $'backend_switch_has_no_C_arm_in_lib_spiral' : i32' with the usual 'packages: core- / modules: main' spiproj and the matrix ExpectedErrors line Contains 'reaches a lib/spiral backend_switch that has no arm for the'. samples/backend_switch_validated_gap/main.spi = a !!!!BackendSwitch over all 16 keys returning 0i32, where only Bend's arm is $'backend_switch_has_no_Bend_arm_in_lib_spiral' : i32. Both are re-created in samples/ when the guard ships. Copies: C:\\home\\data\\rescue-2026-10-31\\spiral-packs\\missing-arm-guard.")
inl note_of_spiral_rust_net_text_guard_11 () = Note ("SPIRAL-RUST-NET-TEXT-GUARD", 11u32, "2026-10-31T07:38Z", "compiler", "10-31 03:38 compiler: the gpt5parallel side COMPILES. Candidate guard2 = live core + packs/apply-missing-arm-guard.ps1 (7 edits: RustGapTrace regex + EMacro guard on single-flight; validating field, 5 carries, root 0, both validation sites +1, EMacro guard on gpt5parallel), Debug gpt5parallel build in a private cache: 'Build succeeded' (1,166 s). pair-drift on that core: 1 pair touched, 0 one-sided. Lib corpus over the 14 TS-target files (Normal priority): no line in any run matched the guard's error text; the failing tests also fail with the installed compiler and vary between runs in both directions (reflection: installed 6 failures, guard 3; listm': 'append' vs 'distinct'), i.e. load flakiness, not the guard. Ready for the pack after 9, with its two samples re-created from n10.")
inl note_of_spiral_rust_net_text_guard_12 () = Note ("SPIRAL-RUST-NET-TEXT-GUARD", 12u32, "2026-10-31T11:35Z", "compiler", "10-31 10-31 07:35 compiler: STALE note fixed (groom): the 03:38 gpt5parallel side of the guard is DROPPED; after the retirement only the single-flight half exists (2 edits: RustGapTrace.missing_arm regex + the EMacro raise when validating = 0), and it ships in PACK-10. At the PACK-10 install the two samples and the ExpectedErrors line come back from n10. Open hazard: Session.Defer runs 'adapter.set_validating 0', so a deferred hopacsota body can raise the guard where single-flight would not; checked on the corpus only for single-flight.")
inl note_of_spiral_rust_net_text_guard_13 () = Note ("SPIRAL-RUST-NET-TEXT-GUARD", 13u32, "2026-10-31T11:40Z", "compiler", "10-31 10-31 07:40 compiler: Defer HAZARD ANALYSED (read only, live core). Session.Defer (spiral_compiler.fs 54036-54069) runs every deferred body with adapter.set_validating 0 (54059), and the single-flight adapter maps that to RustGapTrace.validating (159801). Single-flight never resets the counter at a join point: a body first reached while backend_switch validation type-checks another backend's arm (the counter is raised at 160941-160946) runs with validating > 0, so the guard stays silent there. Hopacsota resets it to 0 on purpose, because under parallel the first claimer of a join-point key is racy, so inheriting the caller's context would make the result schedule-dependent. CONSEQUENCE: under SPIRAL_HOPACSOTA_POLICY bfs, random:<seed> or parallel (NOT the default 'inline', which never defers; 54011), a join point reached only from a non-selected arm, whose body hits a lib backend_switch with no arm for the target, raises the guard in hopacsota where single-flight accepts. FIX DESIGN (context-free, so deterministic on both engines): drop the validating test from the EMacro raise and check the OUTPUT instead: after codegen, fail iff the missing-arm marker survived into the emitted program (that is exactly 'the output would not build'). Report the macro's first source location, collected at EMacro time without raising. This is also stricter than today, since a validation-time body that does get emitted still yields compile_error! in the .rs with the current guard. NEEDS: one discriminating sample (non-selected C arm -> join point -> lib switch with no Rust arm) on a hopacsota build with the guard. Waits for the PACK-10 install (main's install-when-clear) plus a heavy window for the hopacsota candidate.")
inl note_of_spiral_rust_net_text_guard_14 () = Note ("SPIRAL-RUST-NET-TEXT-GUARD", 14u32, "2026-10-31T14:06Z", "compiler", "10-31 10-31 10:06 compiler: CONTEXT-FREE GUARD DRAFTED (pending, PACK-11): apps/compiler/packs/apply-missing-arm-guard-output.ps1, 4 edits stacked on PACK-10's guard; every anchor 1x on PACK-10's candidate core source (core-src\\single-flight\\5b4655ec4bf5990b), scratch apply ok, DONE guard trips on re-run. (1) RustGapTrace gains missing_arm_sites, a ConcurrentDictionary from marker to call chain (the ordinally smallest chain wins, so the report doesn't depend on evaluation order), plus clear_missing_arm / note_missing_arm / missing_arm_error. (2) EMacro no longer raises: it records the site (SPIRAL_RUST_GAP_TRACE=1 still prints every one). (3) build_many clears the sites before peval. (4) After codegen, a build is BuildFatalError exactly when a missing-arm marker is in the EMITTED code. The message keeps 'This program reaches a lib/spiral backend_switch that has no arm for the <backend> backend ...' (so n10's ExpectedErrors Contains still holds) and adds 'Reached from: <chain>'. Effect: the verdict is the same on single-flight and every hopacsota policy (Defer's validating = 0 no longer matters), and a body first reached during validation but later emitted is now caught in Spiral instead of at cargo. Corpus: 0 generated outputs in any repo contain a marker (only lib backend/console/convert/sm' sources do), so no current row should flip. Proof still owed: candidate build, the two guard samples on single-flight AND SPIRAL_HOPACSOTA_POLICY=parallel, and the predicted set (expected P = 0, verdict diffs only the guard samples).")
inl note_of_spiral_rust_net_text_guard_15 () = Note ("SPIRAL-RUST-NET-TEXT-GUARD", 15u32, "2026-10-31T19:11Z", "compiler", "10-31 10-31 14:59 compiler: SHIPPED in PACK-10 (installed 14:30). Its samples are back in apps/compiler/tmp/samples with the ExpectedErrors line, accepted (PACK-10 n3): the negative errors with 'This program reaches a lib/spiral backend_switch that has no arm for the <backend> backend' on all 4 backends, validated_gap compiles. LEFT: the context-free v2 (packs/apply-missing-arm-guard-output.ps1, PACK-11), whose 4 anchors now match the live core.")
inl note_of_spiral_rust_net_text_guard_16 () = Note ("SPIRAL-RUST-NET-TEXT-GUARD", 16u32, "2026-11-01T00:33Z", "compiler", "10-31 10-31 20:32 compiler: v2 SHIPPED in PACK-11 (installed 20:29): the guard now decides from the emitted output and names the call chain ('Reached from: ...'); the negative sample errors that way on all 4 backends at parity. Remaining proof for Done: the two guard samples under SPIRAL_HOPACSOTA_POLICY=parallel on a hopacsota build (the agreement v2 exists for).")
inl note_of_spiral_rust_net_text_guard_17 () = Note ("SPIRAL-RUST-NET-TEXT-GUARD", 17u32, "2026-11-03T07:31Z", "compiler", "11-03 07:3x compiler GROOM (WIP cap: Active = touched in 12 h). Parked to Planned (55 h). Next: the two guard samples under SPIRAL_HOPACSOTA_POLICY=parallel on a hopacsota build, then Done.")
inl kit_unbuilt_probe_crates () = Task ("KIT-UNBUILT-PROBE-CRATES", "kit: 89 src/*_probe crates are outside the Cargo workspace and no runner builds them; archive the unreached ones and drop their census rows", TaskKind, P3, D2, 45u32, Planned, "EOIE-RELEASE-TRAIN", "kit,cleanup,probes,team:kit", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_kit_unbuilt_probe_crates () = Planning ("KIT-UNBUILT-PROBE-CRATES", Minutes 90u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_kit_unbuilt_probe_crates_1 () = Note ("KIT-UNBUILT-PROBE-CRATES", 1u32, "2026-10-29T18:00Z", "kit", "2026-10-29T18:00 kit: measured on the TRAIN4 tree. 89 src/*_probe dirs are not members of src/Cargo.toml. kit_dev_spiral_tests/registry.spi names none of the sampled ones (filesystem_copy_policy_probe, bundle_verify_policy_probe, rust_std_fs_strict_read_probe); only standards/census.spi baselines count them. They carry most of kit's /tmp literals (Linux era; on Windows they write C:\\tmp). Plan: for each dir, grep its stem in kit and sibling repos (dangling-check-by-stem). Archive the unreached ones with scratch-archive.ps1 (verified, restorable), drop their census rows in the same change, and land it in a train after TRAIN4.")
inl note_of_kit_unbuilt_probe_crates_2 () = Note ("KIT-UNBUILT-PROBE-CRATES", 2u32, "2026-10-29T18:27Z", "kit", "2026-10-29T18:27 kit: stem check (dangling-check-by-stem) done for all 89. Sibling repos: none in spiral (apps, lib, scripts; deps excluded). i574n.github has only backlog prose (agile.spi x5, done-notes x1). Inside kit, KEEP 2: kit_coverage_smoke (a workspace member) reads src/filesystem_batch_write_plan_probe/{main,rollback_plan}.spi and src/filesystem_action_batch_plan_probe/main.spi as fixture text. Leave kit/state data untouched (migration.spi, consolidation_template.spi and consolidation_fixtures.spi name some stems). state/migration.spi is a hashed gate input in contracts_entry and cold_proof_domain, so it is never respelled or edited by this cleanup. Archive set = the other 87. standards/census.spi drops their rows (1513 refs) in the same change, then census --ratchet re-proves. Stem list: C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\C--home-git\\fc1568a6-c6d6-4cdf-9135-008e972e514a\\scratchpad\\probe-stems.txt Durable copy of the stem list: C:\\home\\data\\kit-drafts\\tmp-leaves\\probe-stems.txt (the scratchpad copy does not survive this session).")
inl note_of_kit_unbuilt_probe_crates_3 () = Note ("KIT-UNBUILT-PROBE-CRATES", 3u32, "2026-10-29T23:20Z", "kit", "2026-10-29T23:20 kit: PLAN CORRECTION. The 89 probes are not dead weight. Many are the only compile path for a contract package. Of 45 *_contract/*_bindings packages without a Cargo.toml, 26 are imported by a workspace member (live; regen compiles their reachable parts), and 19 are imported ONLY by these probes: bootstrap_experiment_contract, cold_proof_contract, differential_catalog_bindings, differential_catalog_contract, differential_normalization_contract, dogfood_shim_contract, exact_replace_contract, family_contract_bindings, family_contract_contract, filesystem_copy_contract, filesystem_move_contract, filesystem_roundtrip_contract, hash_integrity_contract, parallel_search_contract, parallel_workstream_contract, patch_mutation_contract, patch_preflight_contract, path_tag_contract, portable_toolchain_contract. Archiving the probes alone would leave those 19 uncompiled. That is how path_tag_contract's column-0 parse error survived until today (KIT-PATH-TAG-PARSE was proven only by compiling path_tag_policy_probe with a bundle-only Cargo.toml). New rule per family: (a) if a probe-only contract is unreferenced elsewhere (dangling-check-by-stem across repos), archive the probes AND the contract together; (b) if it is still wanted, give the contract its own test block (notebook) so it compiles without the probe, then archive the probe. Probes whose contracts all have member importers can go as planned. The 2 fixture dirs read by kit_coverage_smoke stay.")
inl note_of_kit_unbuilt_probe_crates_4 () = Note ("KIT-UNBUILT-PROBE-CRATES", 4u32, "2026-10-30T00:13Z", "kit", "2026-10-30T00:13 kit: CORRECTION to n3's count (19 -> 12), now TRANSITIVE. A breadth-first walk of package.spiproj imports from the 256 workspace members reaches 341 of 447 packages. Of the 45 *_contract/*_bindings packages without a Cargo.toml, 33 are reachable (n3 missed intermediate importers such as legacy_operations_entry, proxy_search_entry, patch_*_app, product_diff_contract) and 12 are UNREACHABLE from any crate: bootstrap_experiment_contract, cold_proof_contract, differential_catalog_bindings, differential_catalog_contract, differential_normalization_contract, family_contract_bindings, family_contract_contract, filesystem_copy_contract, filesystem_move_contract, filesystem_roundtrip_contract, parallel_workstream_contract, portable_toolchain_contract. None has a test module, and the only importers are their own *_negative_probe/*_policy_probe dirs. Cross-repo: 0 references in spiral (deps excluded; the glob is positive-controlled) and only backlog prose in i574n.github. So these 12 families (each contract plus its probes) are the archive set, to move together with scratch-archive.ps1 (verified, restorable). Every other probe stays until its contract gets its own test block. Controls: kit_standards and path_tag_contract both show reachable.")
inl note_of_kit_unbuilt_probe_crates_5 () = Note ("KIT-UNBUILT-PROBE-CRATES", 5u32, "2026-10-30T01:58Z", "kit", "2026-10-30T01:58 kit groom (user, via main 21:54): analysis complete (n2-n4: stem check, keep the 2 kit_coverage_smoke fixture dirs, transitive set of 12 dead families); progress 0 -> 30, Planned -> Active. Estimate 60 -> 90m (12 families: archive with verification, drop their census rows, census --ratchet). Deps EOIE-RELEASE-TRAIN: it edits src, so it lands in a train after TRAIN5.")
inl note_of_kit_unbuilt_probe_crates_6 () = Note ("KIT-UNBUILT-PROBE-CRATES", 6u32, "2026-10-30T02:17Z", "kit", "2026-10-30T02:17 kit: archive step ready. The set is C:\\home\\data\\kit-drafts\\tmp-leaves\\dead-families.txt: 27 dirs, the 12 unreachable contracts plus their 15 importing probes. Closure checks: 0 of the 15 are crates, and 0 packages import them. archive-dead-families.ps1 (same folder) is dry-run by default (set 27; census rows dropped 459 of 7380). With -Apply it refuses on a tree lease or any import from outside the set, copies each dir to kit-drafts\\archive\\<stamp>-dead-probe-families, checks the copy with per-file sha256, removes the source only after a match, and rewrites standards/census.spi without the 459 rows. After apply: 'kit standards --ratchet standards/census.spi' and a regen must stay green; ships in a train after TRAIN5. Progress 30 -> 45.")
inl note_of_kit_unbuilt_probe_crates_7 () = Note ("KIT-UNBUILT-PROBE-CRATES", 7u32, "2026-11-01T03:36Z", "kit", "10-31 23:4x kit groom (main 23:33, user: cadence/drift): Active -> PLANNED. No note in over 12 h, so nobody is working it this week; Planned keeps it in agile next at its priority (never auto-Paused, R3). First step when picked = the next step in its latest note.")
inl pack_7 () = Task ("PACK-7", "Compiler pack 7: global-fragment dedup, formatter re-pad closer, Bend f32 math, OpenQasm tanh, Rust derive inference, Gleam compact arms, char NUL escape, hopacsota D4 restart (core + host), --check parse errors; box build, full review against a pre-registered reading rule, install by main", Story, P1, D4, 100u32, Done, "HOPACSOTA-D4-RESTART", "compiler,pack,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_pack_7 () = Planning ("PACK-7", Minutes 360u32, Load Heavy, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_pack_7_1 () = Note ("PACK-7", 1u32, "2026-10-29T18:42Z", "compiler", "10-29 14:4x compiler: PACK 7 COMPOSITION (all appliers in apps/compiler/packs, unstaged). Core, in this order: apply-global-fragment-single-line, apply-formatter-repad-closer, apply-bend-float-math, apply-openqasm-tanh, apply-rust-derive-inference, apply-gleam-compact-arms, apply-char-nul-escape, apply-hopacsota-d4-restart. Host: apply-hopacsota-d4-restart-host, apply-check-parse-errors. Composed core source sha16 15FA7FF541B2862F (= build-identity sourceSha16 15fa7ff541b2862f of the node build). pair-drift: 8 pairs, 0 one-sided. Node build job-1793293192702: SpiralCompiler.dll 3AB27C9D378C3D72, SpiralCompilerCore.dll 9C899B30495F7DC5, runtimeconfig 11.0.0-rc.1 (launches under the system dotnet), detRoot C:\\home\\spiral-cache (so a box build of the same source should reproduce 9C899B30495F7DC5: COMPILER-CROSS-MACHINE-REPRO n34).\nFunctional probes on the node build: --check on a parse error exits 5 with path:2:13 (installed: exit 0 'checked package', the false green); global_multiline_outdented_dedup 1 copy on F#/C/Rust/Delphi/TS; char_nul_literal C native exit 0 and no raw NUL bytes on F#/Rust/Python/Lua/Gleam; native_float_math_f32 Bend compiles. D4 restart: forced-conflict matrix job-1793294062916 (w1/w2/w4/w8 x5, output == sf) running.\nLand steps after install (scratchpad pack7-land.ps1, dry run green): the 3 regression samples written from a scratch copy of the PRE-install core (global_multiline_outdented_dedup, char_nul_literal, native_float_math_f32), the run.py tanh edit, matrix-add-to-list OpenQasm native_float_math_family and Bend native_float_math_f32.\nSequence (main 14:3x): box build in the 16:00-18:00 gap as a kit job (private cache, BelowNormal); full review after 'TRAIN4 published' (~18:00) with review-candidate.ps1 (no -Reuse), the 525 6b outputs snapshotted first (C:\\home\\data\\samples-6b-snapshot-2026-10-29, manifest-verified). Reading rule and expected-change list: next note.")
inl note_of_pack_7_2 () = Note ("PACK-7", 2u32, "2026-10-29T19:04Z", "compiler", "PACK 7 REVIEW: PRE-REGISTERED READING RULE AND HAND-OFF (compiler, 10-29, written before the review runs)\nRUN: pwsh apps/compiler/packs/review-candidate.ps1 -Dll %LOCALAPPDATA%\\spiral-bin-candidates\\pack7\\cache\\bin\\single-flight\\SpiralCompiler\\Release\\net11.0\\SpiralCompiler.dll -Suite all (native, NO -Reuse; SPIRAL_CC = scoop gcc 15.2, set by the script). Before it starts: the 525 6b outputs are snapshotted at C:\\home\\data\\samples-6b-snapshot-2026-10-29 (manifest.tsv; re-verify the hashes first).\nPASS only if ALL of these hold:\n1. Blocking verdicts: REGRESSED 0, DISAGREE 0, NATIVE-DIFF 0, WRONG-ERROR 0. A timeout or crash row counts only if the same row is not a timeout in the installed oracle under load (rerun that one row alone before calling it).\n2. Residuals: the set of rows whose residual hash changed EQUALS the predicted set P below (measured before the review by compiling the same tree with the installed compiler and with the node's pack 7 build). A changed row outside P, or a P row that did not change, fails the rule until explained row by row.\n3. Expected verdict changes, outside P's codegen effect (tree edits of 10-29, compiler lane):\n   - samples/delphi_uses_units [C, Delphi]: error -> ok; native exit 7 on both.\n   - samples/or_pattern_bound_alternatives [Fsharp, C, Rust, Delphi]: error -> ok; native exit 41 on all four.\n   These 6 rows show as new/FIXED or UNEXPECTED-OUTPUT against the old oracle; that is the intended change.\n   - samples/contract_ack_before_publish, samples/contract_missing_endpoint_capability [Zig]: stay compile=error with the pinned details ('Got: event_box published Expected: event_box persisted' / 'Got: session present present Expected: session present missing'); contract_scoped_resource_escape stays 'The main function should not have a forall'. matrix.psd1 ExpectedErrors has 413 rows (160 of 161 contract negatives pinned, plus empty_match_parse_error on its intended 'Unexpected end of block past this token'); any WRONG-ERROR is blocking.\n4. Delphi: every Delphi native row now builds -Px86_64 (test.ps1:754). Every Delphi row must agree with the oracle on exit and stdout; samples/mega_brzozowski_derivatives/inventory_closed_dfa_runtime [Delphi] exits 0 (the 64-bit regression check: with the NOREGVAR directive removed it exits 1).\n5. Not in the review (written at land, after this rule passes): the 3 new regression samples and the 2 new matrix rows.\nIF PASS (main, next session; nothing is installed tonight if the review ends after 21:00):\n  a. pwsh C:\\home\\data\\pack7-review\\pack7-land.ps1 (dry run), then -Apply. It must run BEFORE the install: it writes the 3 regression samples from a scratch copy of the PRE-install core (it refuses if the live core already carries pack 7), edits run.py (tanh) and adds the OpenQasm native_float_math_family and Bend native_float_math_f32 matrix rows.\n  b. pwsh apps/compiler/packs/install-pack.ps1 -Name pack7 -CoreAppliers apply-global-fragment-single-line.ps1,apply-formatter-repad-closer.ps1,apply-bend-float-math.ps1,apply-openqasm-tanh.ps1,apply-rust-derive-inference.ps1,apply-gleam-compact-arms.ps1,apply-char-nul-escape.ps1,apply-hopacsota-d4-restart.ps1 -HostAppliers apply-hopacsota-d4-restart-host.ps1,apply-check-parse-errors.ps1 (dry run first; it refuses unless the appliers reproduce the reviewed source and the system dotnet launches the result).\n  c. Broadcast the new core (installed SpiralCompilerCore.dll hash); re-ship the node install (node-stale-compiler-dll); then an -Accept run.\nIF FAIL: no install. List the failing rows with their details on PACK-7; the compiler lane triages.\nPREDICTED SET P (measured 10-29 14:43-15:11, job-1793299401404: test.ps1 -Suite all compile-only on a node mirror of the box tree, digest 12374A7BE1DA1ED9, installed 31CC9652 vs node pack7 9C899B30; 7367 rows each, same keys): EXACTLY 359 rows change, ALL Gleam, ALL residual-only; 0 compile-status changes; 0 rows on any other backend. List: C:\\home\\data\\pack7-review\\predicted-set.tsv. Cross-check: brace-bodied Gleam case arms (the shape gleam-compact-arms rewrites) occur in 363 committed main.gleam files. The other appliers are opt-in or only reach the new regression samples (rust-extern derive, NUL chars, outdented multi-line globals, Bend f32, OpenQasm tanh), so they change no existing row. A non-Gleam residual change in the review fails rule 2.")
inl note_of_pack_7_3 () = Note ("PACK-7", 3u32, "2026-10-29T19:43Z", "main", "10-29 15:4x main (installer owner): verified git-a6's change to apps/compiler/packs/install-pack.ps1's launch probe (--help -> --capabilities, line 73). On the installed 6b: `--help` exits 2 by design under the bundled dotnet, so the old probe would have refused every install. `--capabilities` exits 0 under the bundled dotnet and fails under the system dotnet (C:\\Program Files\\dotnet, exit -2147450730, with the \"install or update .NET\" text), which is exactly the runtime-mismatch case the probe exists for. Kept as is. Install hand-off is unchanged: pack7-land.ps1 before install-pack, then broadcast the core, re-ship the node, -Accept (n2).")
inl note_of_pack_7_4 () = Note ("PACK-7", 4u32, "2026-10-29T19:45Z", "compiler", "10-29 16:0x compiler: CANDIDATE REBUILT (the review uses this one): box pack7 core 9C899B30495F7DC5 (= the node build, so the predicted set P measured with the node build applies byte for byte to the core), host F2F6F4CB5A18626E (host IL 965A5E6C; it differs from the node's host only by the source path baked into F#'s signature data, not behavior). The box's rc.1 runtime was healed first (it was partial: wipe damage; COMPILER-CROSS-MACHINE-REPRO n35/n36). build.ps1 now stages the host source at a normalized path for EVERY build, so install-pack's final check should print equal=True for BOTH SpiralCompiler.dll and SpiralCompilerCore.dll. install-pack.ps1's launch probe is now --capabilities (exit 0 on pack 7; it fails with 'install or update .NET' on the rc.3-built 6b). The old --help probe exits 2 by design and would have failed this install. Ordering with kit (TRAIN5): no install tonight; the runner swap goes first on 'TRAIN4 published'; the review runs as a --heavy kit job.")
inl note_of_pack_7_5 () = Note ("PACK-7", 5u32, "2026-10-29T21:42Z", "compiler", "10-29 17:4x compiler: REVIEW QUEUED: job-1793310125389-compiler-pack7-review, a --heavy kit job behind kit's TRAIN4d (17:35, publish ~18:30), cap 5 h; it runs C:\\home\\data\\pack7-review\\pack7-review-job.ps1 (re-verifies the 6b snapshot manifest and the candidate core 9C899B30495F7DC5 / host F2F6F4CB5A18626E, then review-candidate -Suite all, no -Reuse). Lib under review: main's 17:35 window (sm' 9B2BCC159225, listm' DC9551655B26 with the Zig empty' fix, convert 210D27203B5C, common E981D750A532, am' 87168AA1D752, file_system 1F37E39FF3595D80); reading rule 2b covers the 13 lib-reading samples. Expected end ~21:30-22:00. Main reads it next session against n2's rule (+2b) and installs with the hand-off order there.")
inl note_of_pack_7_6 () = Note ("PACK-7", 6u32, "2026-10-29T21:45Z", "compiler", "10-29 17:5x compiler: CORRECTION to n5. The --heavy review (job-1793310125389) did NOT wait behind TRAIN4d (the train holds no heavy slot), so it ran beside it and was STOPPED after 61 s (exit 143). Restore check against the known pre-run state: 525/525 6b snapshot files hash-equal, no clean output modified, no new output; it stopped before test.ps1 wrote anything. Also fixed while there: review-candidate.ps1 ran apps/compiler/tmp/scripts/test.ps1 through the COMPILER-TMP-RETIRE forwarding stub; it now runs apps/compiler/scripts/test.ps1 from apps/compiler. New start rule: by hand, right after the runner swap that follows kit's 'TRAIN4 published' (runbook step 9).")
inl note_of_pack_7_7 () = Note ("PACK-7", 7u32, "2026-10-29T22:48Z", "compiler", "10-29 18:4x compiler: REVIEW STARTED by hand after main's window 3 closed (18:46) and the runner swap: job-1793314021578-compiler-pack7-review (kit, --heavy, cap 5 h; started 18:47:07). Preflight inside the job: 6b snapshot 525/525 hash-equal; candidate core 9C899B30495F7DC5 / host F2F6F4CB5A18626E as expected; samples dirty before = 529 (the 525 6b outputs + the 4 deliberate sample .spi fixes); test.ps1 now runs from apps/compiler/scripts (the remaining stub warning is kit's heavy-gate lookup of heavy.ps1, whose fix rides TRAIN5). Lib under review: windows 1-3 (window 3: file_system 8661852BC5D5, testing 20C1C824AF64, sm' 2196F9C70148). Expected end ~21:30-22:00. Read it against n2's rule (+2b for the 13 lib-reading samples); the run dir and the 'samples changed by the run' stat are printed at the job's end.")
inl note_of_pack_7_8 () = Note ("PACK-7", 8u32, "2026-10-29T23:29Z", "compiler", "10-29 19:3x compiler (main's sweep 8). (1) OVERLAP: other lanes compiled on the box during this review's first ~27 min (main 18:50 type_probe; x 18:50-18:51 fs4c: 2 Spiral->Rust, 2 rustc -O, 1 wasm32 check; x 18:52/18:53 type_probe; fiscal ~18:55/19:00 two cells round trips; x 19:00-19:02, 19:05-19:06, 19:13-19:14 Spiral->Gleam). The review's compile phase ran 18:47:07-~18:51:47 (280 s), and its native phase from then on. Effect on the reading rule: none on verdicts. Rule 1-4 judge exits, stdout, residual hashes and pinned error text, none of which depend on load; test.ps1 records no memory rows, and per-row ms are informational, never gated. The one load-sensitive outcome is a TIMEOUT: any timeout row (compile or native) is re-run ALONE before it counts (rule 1), and for this run a timeout whose row falls in the windows above is presumed load-induced. (2) HOST-REPRO: job-1793309629205's 1 h timeout was starvation (BelowNormal at 100% node load, overlapping the 42-min gen0-memory spin), NOT a slow-build finding; requeued as job-1793313290509 with a 4 h cap. (3) Node mirrors: the queued jobs' env.ps1 files define every function they call (positive-controlled: pack7b and pack7pred; a negative-control name is absent). 55 node mirrors are still on the pre-retire layout; the only one of mine, pack7check, is resynced to the box tree (digest 7F8CBA85A3CEF54A, deps junction intact); the other 54 belong to other lanes (list sent to main). (4) node-pack7h.ps1 copied to C:\\home\\data\\pack7-review.")
inl note_of_pack_7_9 () = Note ("PACK-7", 9u32, "2026-10-29T23:47Z", "compiler", "PACK 7 REVIEW VERDICT: PASS (read 10-29 19:5x by the compiler lane against the pre-registered rule, PACK-7 n2 + 2b)\nRun: job-1793314021578-compiler-pack7-review, run dir C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\spiral-bin\\runs\\single-flight-20261029-184709 (18:47:07-19:38:36; compile 280 s, native tier 2,713 s, 6 lanes). Candidate core 9C899B30495F7DC5, host F2F6F4CB5A18626E. test.ps1 exit 1 only because of the 6 pre-announced UNEXPECTED-OUTPUT rows below.\nRULE 1, blocking verdicts: DISAGREE 0, REGRESSED 0, WRONG-ERROR 0, NATIVE-DIFF 0, compile timeouts 0, crashes 0. Oracle column: agree 6,122, known 10, no-zig 43, agree-fail 1 (samples/dynamic_array_bounds_negative [Rust], its documented Known entry, unchanged). Contracts 728/728 parity.\nRULE 2, residual set: 884 rows differ from the accepted oracle = 359 in P (ALL 359 predicted Gleam rows changed; 0 predicted rows unchanged) + 525 that are EXACTLY the 6b snapshot's rows (Delphi 413 incl. the 6 lib-reading Delphi rows, Kotlin 52, Swift 52, Ebpf 8: the installed 6b's own delta against the pre-6b oracle, never accepted). So pack 7's residual change equals P exactly. Rule 2b: the 13 lib-reading samples show no residual change beyond 6b's Delphi lines, so tonight's three lib windows moved no residual.\nRULE 3, expected verdict changes: UNEXPECTED-OUTPUT = exactly the 6 sample-fix rows: delphi_uses_units [C] exit 7, [Delphi] exit 7; or_pattern_bound_alternatives [Fsharp] [C] [Rust] [Delphi] exit 41 each. contract_ack_before_publish / contract_missing_endpoint_capability / contract_scoped_resource_escape: compile=error, parity, no WRONG-ERROR (the pins hold).\nRULE 4, Delphi -Px86_64: 437 Delphi rows, 0 not agreeing; mega_brzozowski_derivatives/inventory_closed_dfa_runtime [Delphi] ran, exit 0, agree.\nNEXT (main, next session), per n2: (a) C:\\home\\data\\pack7-review\\pack7-land.ps1 BEFORE the install; (b) install-pack.ps1 -Name pack7 with n2's applier lists (expect equal=True for BOTH DLLs, since build.ps1 now stages the host; launch probe --capabilities); (c) broadcast the core, re-ship the node, then -Accept (it absorbs the 525 6b rows + the 359 Gleam rows + the 6 sample-fix rows + the new regression samples). Reading scripts: read-review.ps1 / read-review-2.ps1 in this folder.")
inl note_of_pack_7_10 () = Note ("PACK-7", 10u32, "2026-10-30T00:34Z", "main", "10-29 20:34 main: next session's ordered start list is C:\\home\\data\\main-drafts\\next-session-1030.md: install pack 7 first (git-a6's n2/n9 order: pack7-land, install-pack with equal=True expected, broadcast, node re-ship, then -Accept regenerated against the live lib), then lanes recompile their drafts, then the first lib window.")
inl note_of_pack_7_11 () = Note ("PACK-7", 11u32, "2026-10-30T02:02Z", "compiler", "10-29 22:0x compiler (groom): deps RUST-DERIVE-INFERENCE,SPIRAL-CHAR-NUL-ESCAPE,GLOBAL-FRAGMENT-DEDUP,HOPACSOTA-D4-RESTART -> HOPACSOTA-D4-RESTART. RUST-DERIVE-INFERENCE already depended on PACK-7 (a cycle), and the other riders (SPIRAL-CHECK-PRINTS-ERRORS) point rider -> pack: a rider closes only after the pack installs. SPIRAL-CHAR-NUL-ESCAPE and GLOBAL-FRAGMENT-DEDUP now depend on PACK-7 the same way. Node cross-check of the candidate: job-1793313290509 rebuilt pack 7 on the node and got host F2F6F4CB5A18626E / core 9C899B30495F7DC5, the reviewed box hashes. Left: main's install.")
inl note_of_pack_7_12 () = Note ("PACK-7", 12u32, "2026-10-30T02:22Z", "main", "10-29 22:22 main: PACK 7 INSTALLED AND VERIFIED LIVE (installed-is-done). Under an exclusive compiler-install window (win-1793326554936, 22:15-22:2x; no other job was running). (a) pack7-land.ps1 dry run, then -Apply: 3 regression samples (global_multiline_outdented_dedup, char_nul_literal, native_float_math_f32), run.py tanh, and the OpenQasm + Bend matrix rows, each verified on disk. (b) install-pack.ps1 -Name pack7 with n2's 8 core and 2 host appliers. The dry run reproduced the reviewed source exactly (core src 15FA7FF541B2862F, host src 1433C90613854D3A, from live 6DD9AB3B / F55C9572). -Apply: 0 build errors; SpiralCompiler.dll F2F6F4CB5A18626E equal=True and SpiralCompilerCore.dll 9C899B30495F7DC5 equal=True (exactly git-a6's expected hashes); the system dotnet launches it. Backup: C:\\home\\data\\compiler-installs\\pack7-20261029-221710. (c) LIVE PROBE through the installed compiler: char_nul_literal -> Lua emits '\\0' and exits 0. CONTROL with the 6b backup (core 31CC96520D6B7269): emits '0' and exits 1. (d) Node re-shipped with node-ship.ps1 (digest 64E9B013870A6A4C, smoke ok, old 6b kept as net11.0.replaced-pack7); node core and host now equal the box's; logged in node-upkeep.log. Left: git-a6's -Accept (regenerated against the live lib, listm' 220ACFE33776 included), then Done.")
inl note_of_pack_7_13 () = Note ("PACK-7", 13u32, "2026-10-30T03:20Z", "main", "10-29 23:20 main: -ACCEPT DONE (git-a6, 23:13; run single-flight-20261029-223356). New baseline EXPECTED.tsv 64752BD4FD115CE5 (pre-accept copy C:\\home\\data\\pack7-review\\EXPECTED-pre-accept.tsv). Residual set = expected, read with read-accept.ps1 (positive control: it reproduces the review's 890): 904 changed = 890 (359 P + 525 6b + 6 sample-fix) + 14 added (the 3 regression samples, plus native_float_math_family [OpenQasm]); 0 lib-reading changes; 0 timeouts or crashes. ONE CAVEAT holds PACK-7 at 98%: native_float_math_f32 [Delphi] DISAGREEs (exit 217, FPC traps inf-inf), and [Fsharp] is a native build-fail that the new baseline records as expected. Both are pre-existing bugs the new sample is the first to reach, proven by hand and filed as PACK-8 riders (DELPHI-FPU-EXCEPTIONS-UNMASKED, FSHARP-ISNAN-UNQUALIFIED). Main asked git-a6 to record both rows as explicit Known entries naming those items, so the oracle doesn't read them as parity; PACK-7 closes on that. Also VERIFIED LIVE and CLOSED by git-a6: SPIRAL-CHAR-NUL-ESCAPE, GLOBAL-FRAGMENT-DEDUP, GLEAM-COMPACT-OUTPUT, SPIRAL-CHECK-PRINTS-ERRORS.")
inl note_of_pack_7_14 () = Note ("PACK-7", 14u32, "2026-10-30T03:22Z", "main", "10-29 23:22 main: CLOSED, Done. Installed with exact hashes (equal=True, core 9C899B30495F7DC5, host F2F6F4CB5A18626E), live-probed with a control (char_nul_literal: pack 7 exits 0, 6b exits 1), re-shipped to the node, and ACCEPTED (residual set equals the expected one, positive-controlled). The two pre-existing bug rows the new sample reached (native_float_math_f32 [Delphi] exit 217, [Fsharp] build-fail) are now explicit Known entries in matrix.psd1, each naming its PACK-8 fix item and removed by that fix's done rule. Verified with test.ps1 -Filter native_float_math_f32 (run single-flight-20261029-232113): both oracle=known, C/Rust/Bend exit 0, native DISAGREE 0. The user's commit of the accepted tree (samples, matrix.psd1, the pack-7 sources) is listed in C:\\home\\data\\main-drafts\\session-1029\\commit-suggestion.md.")
inl zig_empty_array_index () = Task ("ZIG-EMPTY-ARRAY-INDEX", "compiler Zig backend: an empty array literal is emitted as a comptime-known empty slice (`const v13: []const T = &[_]T{};`), and the prepend loop that copies it indexes `v13[i - 1]` in a branch never taken at runtime; Zig rejects that at compile time (\"indexing into empty slice is not allowed\"), so every Zig program reaching reflection.union_try_pick (trace's TRACE_LEVEL read, so any trace call) fails to build", TaskKind, P1, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "compiler,zig,backends,lib,trace", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_zig_empty_array_index () = Planning ("ZIG-EMPTY-ARRAY-INDEX", Minutes 90u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_zig_empty_array_index_1 () = Note ("ZIG-EMPTY-ARRAY-INDEX", 1u32, "2026-10-29T18:54Z", "main", "10-29 14:5x main: found while proving LIB-MACRO-HEAL's trace slice C on Zig. A probe main whose only statement is `trace Info (\\ () => \"clock-probe\") (\\ () => \"\")`, compiled with the installed 6b (core 31CC9652) via `--backend Zig` and run with `zig run` (local zig 0.18.0-dev.738), fails to BUILD on the UNCHANGED live lib (trace.spi 16BCF8E2B4D4) as well as on the healed draft: clock.zig:353:24 \"indexing into empty slice is not allowed\" at `tmp24 = v13[@intCast(v19 - 1)];`, where `const v13: []const Tuple1 = &[_]Tuple1{};` and the loop's `v19 == 0` branch takes the new element. Source: trace.read_state's Zig arm, `\"TRACE_LEVEL\" |> env.get_environment_variable |> reflection.union_try_pick`, which builds the case-name table by prepending onto an empty array. trace's tests list no Zig target, so no gate saw it. Untargeted notebook mains default to Zig (notebook-main-default-zig), so a Zig trace is not exotic. Fix direction (compiler, not lib): emit empty array values as runtime-length slices (e.g. a `var` backing array sliced at runtime, or a length the optimizer cannot fold), or let the prepend specialization skip the copy arm when the source length is statically 0. Done when the clock probe above builds and prints an HH:MM:SS line on Zig, with one regression sample (array prepend onto an empty static array, Zig target) per regression-samples-allowed, shipped in a pack. Repro files: main scratchpad macro-heal/run-trace-clock.ps1 (durable copy C:\\home\\data\\main-drafts\\macro-heal).")
inl note_of_zig_empty_array_index_2 () = Note ("ZIG-EMPTY-ARRAY-INDEX", 2u32, "2026-10-29T20:53Z", "compiler", "10-29 16:5x compiler: ROOT CAUSE IS LIB, NOT THE COMPILER (correcting n1's fix direction). The compiler never emits '&[_]T{}' (codegenZig array literals go through spiralNewArray, spiral_compiler.fs:166273-166279). The text is lib's: lib/spiral/listm'.spi:443, empty' Zig arm &[_]\t{}, a COMPTIME-known zero-length slice. cons'' Zig arm (:459-464) then indexes !tail[i - 1] under a runtime i = 0 branch, and Zig rejects any index into a comptime-known empty slice at compile time. FIX (one line, drafted, NOT landed: lib is frozen until the window): empty' Zig arm -> $'spiralNewArray(\t, 0)', a runtime-length empty slice from the Zig prelude's own allocator helper. PROOF with main's harness (run-trace-clock.ps1, copied into the compiler scratchpad so main's folder is untouched) on two lib COPIES, installed 6b: control (unchanged lib): Zig BAD, build fails at clock.zig:626 (the bug); Rust OK '16:40:51 1i clock-probe'. Fix copy: Zig OK '16:46:53 1i clock-probe' (an HH:MM:SS line, the item's done rule), Rust OK. Landing: listm'.spi is already in main's next lib window, so the one-liner rides in that land to avoid colliding anchors (exact old/new lines sent to main). Regression test (a ///> zig test block for cons' onto empty') to be written in the same window, proven through Cells on the landed lib.")
inl note_of_zig_empty_array_index_3 () = Note ("ZIG-EMPTY-ARRAY-INDEX", 3u32, "2026-10-29T21:20Z", "main", "10-29 17:1x main: ROOT CAUSE IS LIB, not the compiler (git-a6, ZIG-EMPTY-ARRAY-INDEX n2). listm'.empty''s Zig arm emitted `&[_]t{}`, a comptime-known empty slice, and cons''s Zig arm indexes it in its never-taken copy branch. Fix: `spiralNewArray(t, 0)`. Proven by git-a6 and re-proven by main on a fresh copy (Zig trace clock probe OK, \"17:17:11 1i clock-probe\"; Rust unchanged). Lands in main's listm' land in the next lib window; closes when that land and its document_test are green. No compiler change and no regression sample are needed: the lib test list_operations_keep_their_order covers cons' and empty', and a Zig row can be added to it after this land.")
inl note_of_zig_empty_array_index_4 () = Note ("ZIG-EMPTY-ARRAY-INDEX", 4u32, "2026-10-29T21:34Z", "main", "10-29 17:3x main: fix LANDED with listm' DC9551655B26 (empty''s Zig arm is spiralNewArray(t, 0)). Landed 10-29 17:32-17:33 in the post-TRAIN4c lib window (kit GO at 17:31, no train reading lib), through next-window-main.ps1: reader and window check, base hash pinned per file, canonical round trip, private MIX_BUILD_PATH. Hashes: sm' E56D63DE1C1F -> 9B2BCC159225, listm' 1719B5658442 -> DC9551655B26, convert 96F6D2E1B22C -> 210D27203B5C, common 258BE24CE842 -> E981D750A532, am' 638D7AA2FA7E -> 87168AA1D752; apps/cells/spi/type_probe.ps1 CD11C86BFBB1 -> B343B4F24D7A. type_probe from the landed path PASS 4/4; document_test 22/22. Closing at 100 after the closing document_test of this window.")
inl note_of_zig_empty_array_index_5 () = Note ("ZIG-EMPTY-ARRAY-INDEX", 5u32, "2026-10-29T21:36Z", "main", "10-29 17:35 main: CLOSED. The fix landed in listm' DC9551655B26, and the closing document_test after the window passed 22/22. The root cause was lib (empty's Zig arm), found by git-a6; no compiler change was needed.")
inl note_of_zig_empty_array_index_6 () = Note ("ZIG-EMPTY-ARRAY-INDEX", 6u32, "2026-10-30T02:00Z", "compiler", "10-29 20:5x compiler (recorded 22:0x; kit refused it at 20:5x because this shell lacked KIT_LEASE_POLICY=renewing): REGRESSION ROW LANDED: listm'.spi DC9551655B26 -> 220ACFE33776 via land-anchored.ps1 (RT fixpoint). cons_leaves_the_shared_tail_unchanged gains ///> zig (its box on Zig folds cons' onto empty', the exact bug path). Live listm' was RED 29/31 (job-1793320528501): two tests sat above their deps (box/fold_back', unbox/distinct'), so they never ran in Cells. Once running, Lua printed stray lines: list'_types had no Lua arm, and the missing-arm fallback is a runtime print on Lua. The applier (C:\\home\\data\\lib-drafts\\zig-empty\\apply-listm-cons-zig-target.ps1) fixes all three. Gate job-1793321172171: fixed 31/31; control 30/31, red only on the cons zig row. Closing checks job-1793321418458: document_test 0, type_probe PASS. Trap: lib_gate.ps1 exits 0 on failing tests. Census: 103 blocks lack a Lua arm; listm' was the only live unit-prelude case. Main, x and tibia told. The item is already Done; this adds the Zig regression row main n3 asked for.")
