inl note_of_kit_crate_gate_train4_1 () = Note ("KIT-CRATE-GATE-TRAIN4", 1u32, "2026-10-29T20:55Z", "kit", "2026-10-29T20:55 kit: found by the BANG node compare (job-1793303438170, core 31CC96520D6B7269), then confirmed in the tree with 'kit crate gate src': crates=251 over=2. kit_standards/standards.rs is 1024 lines (HEAD 904; TRAIN4's notebook-header rule; largest fn line_findings 264). kit_machine_windows/windows.rs is 1009 (HEAD 964; TreeWrite class + window remote; largest window_requested 138). Both were generated by TRAIN4b's regen (14:05) and pass TRAIN4c, because release-train.ps1 never runs 'kit crate gate --enforce'. Decision: TRAIN4c publishes with these two (9 and 24 lines over), because holding every TRAIN4 fix back costs more than the breach. Hand-modeled splits follow. Seam for kit_standards: a new crate kit_standards_scan owns rules' per-line scan (line_findings, lines_scanned, file_scan) and exports kit_standards_file_findings(declaration_text, path, module, text) -> findings text (count = line count, as findings_count already does); kit_standards keeps declared_from/default_standards/standard_word for rule_words and package_findings. kit_machine_windows: measure first (window_model 464 + windows_cli 432 lines of source). Done = both under 1000 AND release-train runs 'kit crate gate src --enforce' after regen, so this can't recur.")
inl note_of_kit_crate_gate_train4_2 () = Note ("KIT-CRATE-GATE-TRAIN4", 2u32, "2026-10-29T21:17Z", "kit", "2026-10-29T21:17 kit: both splits PROVEN at core 31CC96520D6B7269 (node-regen-compare on the installed node compiler, -RequireCore, L6 guard ok); they ride TRAIN5. (1) kit_standards -> kit_standards_scan (job-1793307927858): rules' per-line scan behind kit_standards_file_findings(declaration, path, module, text); kit_standards 1024 -> 572, kit_standards_scan 651, 0 failures, 0 missing arms. Count = findings_count of the findings text, which equals the old scan.count (the sum of per-line findings_count). (2) kit_machine_windows -> kit_window_request (job-1793308193542): 'window request' routes to kit_window_request::kit_window_request_run (the claim/run/query/wait pattern; dep kit-window-admission); kit_machine_windows 1009 -> 455, kit_window_request 769; the 13 other crates that import kit_machine_windows are byte-identical; 0 over, 0 failures, 0 missing arms. (3) release-train.ps1 runs '<installed> crate gate src --enforce' after regen (also when -SkipRegen) and stops the train with the OVER rows; --enforce exits 1 on today's tree (over=2) as the positive control. Drafts: C:\\home\\data\\kit-drafts\\crate-gate\\; TRAIN5 programs train5-src.do (14 edits) and train5-root.do (3 edits); combined dry runs pass. Done = TRAIN5 publishes with its own crate gate phase passing.")
inl note_of_kit_crate_gate_train4_3 () = Note ("KIT-CRATE-GATE-TRAIN4", 3u32, "2026-10-29T21:32Z", "kit", "2026-10-29T21:32 kit: CORRECTION. The 'TRAIN4c publishes anyway' decision was void. build.ps1's release step already enforces the limit (source topology check, limit=1000), and TRAIN4c stopped at 17:30:40 on kit_standards rust_file_max=1024; the installed kit is unchanged (CD2F0D1A). So the gate was enforced, just late: after 70 min of notebooks. The splits (applied 17:31) and release-train's early 'crate gate --enforce' right after regen (applied 17:32, plus the worker slots) ride TRAIN4d, not TRAIN5. TRAIN4d runs a full regen and only the 7 notebooks the splits touch; TRAIN4c already proved the other 90 green on identical source.")
inl note_of_kit_crate_gate_train4_4 () = Note ("KIT-CRATE-GATE-TRAIN4", 4u32, "2026-10-29T22:43Z", "kit", "2026-10-29T22:43 kit: DONE. Both conditions hold on installed kit C528BE4D (TRAIN4e, job-1793312681480): (1) kit crate gate src: crates=253 over=0 (kit_standards 572, kit_machine_windows 455, new kit_standards_scan 651 and kit_window_request 769, the clip callers back at their TRAIN4c sizes after main restored clip's Rust arm); (2) release-train.ps1 runs '<installed> crate gate src --enforce' right after regen, outside -SkipRegen. Proven twice: it STOPPED TRAIN4d at 18:15:42 on kit_memory_query 1032 within 40 min, not after 70 min of notebooks, and passed TRAIN4e at 18:24:46 (over=0). build.ps1's topology check stays the second wall. 7/7 split-touched notebooks green; the other 90 were proven green on identical bytes in TRAIN4c (snapshot diff, ledger n51).")
inl compiler_gen0_budget () = Task ("COMPILER-GEN0-BUDGET", "Compiler speed: a gen0 GC budget, 64 MB chosen over 256 MB. Node w8 apps_spiral cpu 113 -> 93 s (-18%; 256 MB gave -23%) at about +20 MB peak on small compiles (256 MB: +138 MB, 74 -> 212 MB). Big compiles: peak flat. Box single-flight 124 -> 77 s (256 MB) awaits an A/A re-check; the node shows no single-flight gain", Story, P1, D3, 100u32, Done, "PACK-7", "compiler,performance,gc,host,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_compiler_gen0_budget () = Planning ("COMPILER-GEN0-BUDGET", Minutes 180u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_compiler_gen0_budget_1 () = Note ("COMPILER-GEN0-BUDGET", 1u32, "2026-10-29T22:30Z", "compiler", "10-29 18:3x compiler: filed from HOPACSOTA-P3 n29-n37 (main 18:3x: its own compiler-speed item, not hopacsota). FINDING: a 256 MB gen0 budget (DOTNET_GCgen0size=0x10000000) cuts single-flight CPU on apps/spiral from 124 s to 77 s (-38%) with byte-identical output (B47B5749C215A6F8). Default-GC baseline: curve job-1793295047297 sf CPU median 124 s over 5 rounds; gen0: job-1793303418017 sf 77 s, A/A 75 s. Cause: every gen0 collection scans the evaluator's deep stacks (AGENTS.md's note behind test.ps1's own setting). test.ps1 already sets it for suite runs; Cells, kit regen and direct CLI compiles do not. Only the env var works: runtimeconfig keys 'System.GC.Gen0Size' / 'GCgen0size' are ignored on 11.0.0-rc.1 (probe: 561 vs 13 gen0 GCs).\nDESIGN (agreed with main): one entry point, mode-dependent policy. (a) apps/compiler/packs/apply-host-gen0-reexec.ps1 (drafted, dry-run clean): a process in a LONG-LIVED mode (--server, --server-tcp, --batch, build-file) started without DOTNET_GCgen0size re-execs itself once with it set; opt-out SPIRAL_GEN0_REEXEC=0; no loop is possible (the child has it set; a caller that sets it never re-execs). (b) Big one-shot callers (kit regen and the like) set DOTNET_GCgen0size themselves. One-shot --backend/--check stay single-process: a re-exec costs ~1 s there (measured: small compile 2,915 ms with re-exec vs 1,912 off / 1,785 A/A / 1,535 env preset).\nGATES (pre-registered): (1) per-process peak private memory with and without the budget, for 256 MB and 64 MB, on apps/spiral and a small program (node job-1793311763598, queued); choose the smallest budget that keeps most of the 124 -> 77 s. (2) The applier's regression row: --batch with a passing and a failing job, identical exit code and RESULTS.tsv with re-exec on vs SPIRAL_GEN0_REEXEC=0 (the one-shot passthrough harness is C:\\home\\data\\pack8-drafts\\gen0-reexec-measure.ps1). (3) The sf baseline re-measured on the installed compiler once it ships: CPU at or below ~80 s on the same apps/spiral entry, or an A/A-backed explanation.")
inl note_of_compiler_gen0_budget_2 () = Note ("COMPILER-GEN0-BUDGET", 2u32, "2026-10-29T22:52Z", "compiler", "10-29 18:5x compiler: memory gate job-1793311763598 was VOID (my script bug): its env.ps1 came from the pre-retire pack7check mirror, which no longer defines Resolve-SpiralDotnet, so every Process.Start failed and the poll loop (while -not $p.HasExited) spun on a null process for 42 min until stopped; nothing measured, nothing written. Fixed (pack7pred mirror; fail fast if dotnet does not resolve; a failed start skips the arm) and requeued as job-1793314401418-compiler-gen0-memory-2, after the node's host-repro and post-pass bench jobs. Durable script: C:\\home\\data\\pack8-drafts\\gen0-memory.ps1.")
inl note_of_compiler_gen0_budget_3 () = Note ("COMPILER-GEN0-BUDGET", 3u32, "2026-10-29T23:51Z", "compiler", "10-29 19:5x compiler: the applier's regression row is written (not run: the box is in main's lib window 4): C:\\home\\data\\pack8-drafts\\gen0-reexec-batch-row.ps1 -Dll <candidate>. It drives --batch (a long-lived mode, so the narrowed policy re-execs) with one passing and one parse-failing job (JOBS.tsv id/backend/input/output), once with re-exec and once with SPIRAL_GEN0_REEXEC=0, and requires the same exit code and identical RESULTS.tsv rows ignoring only the ms column. Run it against the narrowed-applier candidate next session, together with the memory gate (node job-1793314401418).")
inl note_of_compiler_gen0_budget_4 () = Note ("COMPILER-GEN0-BUDGET", 4u32, "2026-10-29T23:57Z", "compiler", "10-29 19:5x compiler: REGRESSION ROW PASSES (box, after main's window 4 closed). gen0-reexec-batch-row.ps1 against candidate gen0h (pack 7 + the re-exec host change; --batch re-execs in both the original and the narrowed policy): one passing job and one parse-failing job via --batch, re-exec ON vs SPIRAL_GEN0_REEXEC=0. Both arms exit 1 (the failing job, by design) with identical RESULTS.tsv rows (ok=ok, bad=error, same messages; only the ms column ignored). POSITIVE CONTROL that the two arms really differ in process shape: polling the launched process's children found 1 child '--batch' process in the re-exec arm and 0 in the off arm. So exit codes and results pass through the re-exec exactly. Gates left: the per-process memory measurement (node job-1793314401418) and choosing the budget; then build the narrowed applier and rerun this row.")
inl note_of_compiler_gen0_budget_5 () = Note ("COMPILER-GEN0-BUDGET", 5u32, "2026-10-30T00:08Z", "compiler", "10-29 20:0x compiler: NARROWED APPLIER BUILT AND PROVEN. Candidate gen0n (pack 7 + apply-host-gen0-reexec.ps1 narrowed to --server/--server-tcp/--batch/build-file): core 9C899B30495F7DC5 (pack 7 unchanged), host 2778D315C7611500. (1) Regression row: --batch with a passing and a failing job, re-exec on vs SPIRAL_GEN0_REEXEC=0: exit 1 both (by design), identical RESULTS.tsv (ok=ok, bad=error, same messages). (2) Process-shape controls with no env set: --batch spawns 1 child dotnet (the re-exec); a one-shot --backend C compile spawns 0 and exits 0. So long-lived modes get the gen0 budget, and one-shot compiles keep a single process (no ~1 s penalty). Remaining gate: per-process memory (node job-1793314401418, queued after the post-pass bench) to pick 256 vs 64 MB; then the applier joins a pack (pack 8 or 9).")
inl note_of_compiler_gen0_budget_6 () = Note ("COMPILER-GEN0-BUDGET", 6u32, "2026-10-30T00:16Z", "compiler", "10-29 20:1x compiler: MEMORY GATE + A SECOND MACHINE (box, pack-7 sf candidate, apps/spiral to F#, BelowNormal, 3 rounds incl. an in-job A/A; script C:\\home\\data\\pack8-drafts\\gen0-memory-box.ps1). CPU s, default: 38.7 / 53.2 / 48.6, A/A 51.5 / 42.8; gen0 256 MB: 43.8 / 48.3 / 41.7; gen0 64 MB: 47.7 / 45.1 / 41.0. Every gen0 value is INSIDE the default/A-A band (38.7-53.2), so on the box the budget has NO measurable effect, while on the node it cut sf CPU 124 -> 77 s (-38%). Likely cause: the runtime derives the default gen0 budget from the CPU's cache size, so the box's desktop CPU already gets a large default, and the laptop node a small one (inference, not measured). Peak private memory does NOT grow with the budget (default 415-649 MB sampled, 256 MB ~545, 64 MB 470-511), so the memory gate is answered: no cost. Output identical in every run (791709BF6F5C5642). VERDICT: the lever is harmless where it does not help and large where it does, so ship the narrowed re-exec with 256 MB (the node-measured value). Its claim must be worded per machine: '-38% CPU on the node class; no change on the box'. The node memory job (job-1793314401418) is now redundant for the decision; let it run as a second-machine confirmation.")
inl note_of_compiler_gen0_budget_7 () = Note ("COMPILER-GEN0-BUDGET", 7u32, "2026-10-30T02:01Z", "compiler", "10-29 22:0x compiler: NODE RESULT (second machine), job-1793314401418-compiler-gen0-memory-2, finished exit 0, 2 rounds per arm, 12 apps_spiral + 12 small runs, all exit 0. apps_spiral cpu (r1/r2): sf 77.7/66.8 s, sf_gen0_256 74.9/72.7, sf_gen0_64 70.4/70.9; w8 114.4/111.8, w8_gen0_256 86.1/88.6, w8_gen0_64 90.4/96.8. Peak private 460-556 MB in every arm (no memory cost on the big case). small: sf 74/77 MB vs sf_gen0_256 212/212 MB and sf_gen0_64 91/101 MB (cpu ~3 s, flat). READING: on the node the gain is in w8 (-23% cpu with 256 MB, -18% with 64 MB); single-flight shows NO clear gain (77.7/66.8 vs 74.9/72.7, inside the round-to-round spread), unlike the box's 124 -> 77 s. 256 MB costs ~+138 MB on every small compile; 64 MB costs ~+20 MB and keeps most of the w8 gain. Not decided here: the box single-flight number needs an A/A re-check before the title's -38% stands as the headline; candidate default is 64 MB for w8 if the box agrees. Still blocked on the PACK-7 install for the box run.")
inl note_of_compiler_gen0_budget_8 () = Note ("COMPILER-GEN0-BUDGET", 8u32, "2026-10-30T02:06Z", "compiler", "10-29 22:0x compiler: DECIDED 64 MB (re-decision asked by main 22:0x). The 20:1x claim that peak private memory never grows is WITHDRAWN: it holds on apps_spiral (both machines) but not on small compiles, where 256 MB adds ~138 MB per process (74 -> 212 MB) and 64 MB adds ~20 MB. For w8, 64 MB keeps 18 of 23 points of the cpu win. Every claim now names its workload and machine (title rewritten). Before the pack ships: box rows for gen0_64 (sf and w8) with an in-job A/A row; if the box's single-flight gain holds only at 256 MB, revisit with those rows.")
inl note_of_compiler_gen0_budget_9 () = Note ("COMPILER-GEN0-BUDGET", 9u32, "2026-10-30T04:38Z", "compiler", "10-30 00:3x compiler: WALL TIME + GC PAUSE ROWS (HOPACSOTA-P3 n41, node under load, dotnet-counters): single-flight apps/spiral default GC 197.1 s wall with 2,265 gen0 GCs and 93.5 s total GC pause; with gen0 256 MB 117.7 s wall, 35 gen0 GCs, 14.3 s pause: -40% WALL for sf on a loaded machine. That differs from n7's 'no sf cpu gain on the node' because a GC pause includes waiting for threads to reach a safepoint, which costs wall, not cpu, when the machine is oversubscribed. So the budget is a sf WALL win under load and a w8 cpu+wall win. The 64 MB choice (n8) still stands on peak memory. Its gen0 count will sit between 35 and 2,265; the box gen0_64 rows in the PACK-8 slot should record wall and pause (dotnet-counters), not only cpu.")
inl note_of_compiler_gen0_budget_10 () = Note ("COMPILER-GEN0-BUDGET", 10u32, "2026-10-31T15:11Z", "compiler", "10-31 10-31 11:11 compiler: INSTALLED and VERIFIED LIVE -> Done. The installed host (spiral-bin single-flight) contains reexecWithGen0Budget (the 64 MB DOTNET_GCgen0size re-exec for --server, --server-tcp, --batch and build-file), and a live check confirms it. 'dotnet SpiralCompiler.dll --server-tcp <tmp sock>' started with no DOTNET_GCgen0size and no SPIRAL_GEN0_REEXEC spawned exactly one dotnet child running the same DLL and args, i.e. the re-exec, within 6 s; both processes were stopped afterwards. Its applier (packs/apply-host-gen0-reexec.ps1) is archived as shipped (%LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\archive\\20261031-110906). Measurement debt, not a blocker: n9 asked for box gen0_64 rows that record wall time and GC pause (dotnet-counters) next to cpu; they belong in the next compiler speed review.")
inl lib_split_list_quadratic () = Task ("LIB-SPLIT-LIST-QUADRATIC", "lib: sm'.split_list copies the remaining string at every separator (O(lines x bytes)); kit lint shadows takes 203 s on the 7.8 MB backlog file, 15 s on its half", TaskKind, P2, D2, 85u32, Planned, "", "lib,perf,kit,team:kit", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_lib_split_list_quadratic () = Planning ("LIB-SPLIT-LIST-QUADRATIC", Minutes 60u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_lib_split_list_quadratic_1 () = Note ("LIB-SPLIT-LIST-QUADRATIC", 1u32, "2026-10-29T23:58Z", "kit", "2026-10-29T23:58 kit: measured on a quiet box (19:57, installed kit C528BE4D): 'kit lint shadows' on a copy of state/agile.spi (7,803,009 bytes, 8,982 lines, 0 ':='-style definitions, all 8,940 top-level lines 'inl') takes 203.0 s; on its first half (3,838,567 bytes) 15.0 s, so doubling the input costs 13.5x. Lib and x/apps (many small files) take 1.3 s and 0.4 s. Cause: lib sm'.split_list (sm'.spi:2712) recurses with 'substring (at + width) (length s - at - width) s', copying the WHOLE remainder at every separator: O(lines x bytes), about 17 GB copied for agile.spi; it isn't tail-recursive either. kit_lint_shadows_core calls it once per file (shadows.spi:112). Fix direction (lib-first): walk with an offset (index_of from a start position, one substring per piece) or give it a Rust arm over str::split, plus a lib test that a 10k-line input stays linear. Every kit/lib caller of split_list on large text benefits. Positive control for the fix: the same agile.spi copy, with lint time roughly 2x (not 13x) from half to full.")
inl note_of_lib_split_list_quadratic_2 () = Note ("LIB-SPLIT-LIST-QUADRATIC", 2u32, "2026-10-29T23:59Z", "kit", "2026-10-29T23:59 kit: design option. sm'.split (sm'.spi:1544) already has a linear native Rust arm (str::split collected into a Vec), as do Python, TS, Zig and F#. So split_list can be: if the separator is empty return [ s ], else split separator s, converted from array to list. Keep the empty-separator branch, because Rust's split on an empty separator yields boundary pieces while the current split_list returns [ s ]. Check whether sm' may call am'.to_list' without an import cycle. Caution: split's own C arm is also superlinear (start_of re-scans from 0 for every part: O(parts x n)), so the C backend needs an offset walk either way. kit only needs the Rust arm.")
inl note_of_lib_split_list_quadratic_3 () = Note ("LIB-SPLIT-LIST-QUADRATIC", 3u32, "2026-10-30T00:01Z", "kit", "2026-10-30T00:01 kit: DRAFTED (kit owns this per lanes-land-own-lib): C:\\home\\data\\kit-drafts\\lib-land\\split-list-linear\\split-list-linear.do, root lib/spiral, one replacement at sm'.spi:2712. Base sha256 2196f9c70148 (after window 4), dry run ok. New body: empty separator returns [ s ] (kept for Gleam, whose split on an empty string yields graphemes); otherwise arraym.fold (prepend) over (split separator s |> array), then listm.rev. That is one native split (Rust str::split, linear) plus two linear passes, using only core arraym/listm, which sm' already uses (lines 68, 698), so no new import and no cycle (main's note a: am'.to_list' returns listm'.list', not core list). Edge semantics match the old recursion: 'a::b::::c' on '::' gives a,b,'',c; a trailing separator yields a final ''; an empty input gives ['']. NOT yet proven, next session after the pack-7 install (recompile then): (1) sm' test split_list_uses_the_whole_separator green on ///> rust and ///> gleam via lib_gate; (2) the positive control: kit lint shadows on the agile.spi copy grows about 2x from half to full (today: 15.0 s -> 203.0 s, 13.5x); (3) a regen of kit's callers shows no generated-line growth over 1000 (crate gate). The C backend inherits split's own superlinear C arm, no worse than before.")
inl note_of_lib_split_list_quadratic_4 () = Note ("LIB-SPLIT-LIST-QUADRATIC", 4u32, "2026-10-30T00:09Z", "kit", "2026-10-30T00:09 kit: generated-size check done (main's attribute-generated-lines concern). The only kit crates whose generated Rust reaches split_list are kit_lint_shadows and kit_lint_shadows_core; lib's other caller, file_system.spi:516, sits in the Gleam-only gleam_lexical_full_path. Node compare job-1793318823308 (-RequireCore 31CC96520D6B7269, base = live lib after window 4, candidate = a lib copy with split-list-linear.do applied): kit_lint_shadows 788 -> 777, kit_lint_shadows_core 716 -> 731, 0 over 1000, 0 compile failures, 0 missing arms, control identical. So no ':~' helper is needed. Still to prove next session (after the pack-7 recompile): the sm' test on rust and gleam via lib_gate, cargo check, and the runtime control (lint about 2x half to full). Update 20:11: LIB-TESTS-NOT-RUN is Done (main), and sm' tests are now live gate rows (72/72, 227 executed). So next session's lib_gate on the draft runs ALL of sm', not only split_list_uses_the_whole_separator. Done = that gate green on the recompiled pack-7 lib, plus cargo check and the 2x runtime control.")
inl note_of_lib_split_list_quadratic_5 () = Note ("LIB-SPLIT-LIST-QUADRATIC", 5u32, "2026-10-30T00:20Z", "kit", "2026-10-30T00:20 kit: CORRECTION to n1 (main's sweep 9). The 19:57 timings were NOT on a quiet box: the 203 s full run overlapped main's live sm' lib_gate (19:53-20:06) and git-a6's dotnet candidate batch. The 13.5x ratio is probably real (quadratic remainder copying explains it), but the absolute seconds aren't clean. For the positive control next session: time BOTH the half and the full agile.spi copy BEFORE the fix and AFTER it, back to back under the same conditions (check 'kit job list' and running dotnet/beam first), and compare the ratios (expect ~13x before, ~2x after), not seconds across runs.")
inl note_of_lib_split_list_quadratic_6 () = Note ("LIB-SPLIT-LIST-QUADRATIC", 6u32, "2026-10-30T02:53Z", "kit", "2026-10-30T02:53 kit: (1) pack-7 re-proof of sizes: node compare job-1793327142859 at core 9C899B30495F7DC5 gives the same as 6b: kit_lint_shadows 788 -> 777, kit_lint_shadows_core 716 -> 731, 0 failures, 0 missing arms. The sm' lib_gate (base and candidate, all 72 sm' tests) is running on the node (job-1793327248501). (2) CORRECTION to the runtime control's timing: the lint's split_list is compiled INTO the kit binary, so landing the lib alone does not change the installed kit. The real before/after is installed C528BE4D vs the TRAIN5 binary, whose regen rebuilds kit_lint_shadows against the landed lib. Script: C:\\home\\data\\kit-drafts\\tools\\time-shadows-ratio.ps1 -Kit <old.exe>,<new.exe> times half and full for both binaries back to back on one agile.spi copy and reports busy processes. Expect about 13x old vs about 2x new.")
inl note_of_lib_split_list_quadratic_7 () = Note ("LIB-SPLIT-LIST-QUADRATIC", 7u32, "2026-10-30T03:48Z", "kit", "2026-10-30T03:48 kit: box sm' lib_gate on the candidate copy (pack 7 core 9C899B30495F7DC5, job-1793330501262, 25.8 min, private copy so live lib was untouched): tests passed 67 failed 5 of 72, type errors 0. The 5 fail ONLY on the cuda target: to_char_array, format, show, to_char_array_2, concat_array. split_list_uses_the_whole_separator PASSED, and none of the 5 depend on split_list. Main's 72/72 baseline predates pack 7, so the comparison needs the unchanged live sm' on pack 7: asked main for post-install evidence, otherwise a base gate on a copy after the lib window closes. Also VOID: node gate job-1793327248501, which timed out at 60 min on the cand side; its base side was 63/72 on the node, so the node isn't a clean oracle for sm'.")
inl note_of_lib_split_list_quadratic_8 () = Note ("LIB-SPLIT-LIST-QUADRATIC", 8u32, "2026-10-30T03:56Z", "kit", "2026-10-30T03:56 kit: cause of the 5 cuda-only failures found outside Cells. The box GPU is a Quadro K1100M (Kepler) on driver 426.78 (CUDA 10.1 max); python312 (SPIRAL_PYTHON, which Cells' Toolchain.python uses and kit jobs inherit) has cupy-cuda12x 14.2.0 (runtime 12.9, installed 10-28 18:41 post-wipe). 'cupy.arange(5)' fails with cudaErrorInsufficientDriver. That is a box toolchain/hardware mismatch, independent of split_list (whose own test passes). The env-stale theory was tested and ruled out: a probe job inherited SPIRAL_PYTHON. The A/B after the Cells window should show base failing the same 5; then split_list is 'ready'. Cuda-target handling on this box went to main and git-a6 as toolchain owners.")
inl note_of_lib_split_list_quadratic_9 () = Note ("LIB-SPLIT-LIST-QUADRATIC", 9u32, "2026-10-30T04:50Z", "main", "10-30 00:50 main: WINDOW 6 OPEN for this item. kit's A/B (job-1793333150121, pack 7 core 9C899B30495F7DC5, two private lib copies differing ONLY in sm'.spi) printed IDENTICAL gate lines for base and candidate: tests passed 67 failed 5 of 72, type errors 0, the same 5 cuda failures (to_char_array, format, show, to_char_array_2, concat_array) and the same not_run rows. The 5 are pre-existing on this box: the K1100M (Kepler, CUDA 10.1) vs cupy-cuda12x mismatch, a toolchain choice for the user. They are not split_list, and not pack 7 (git-a6: the cuda codegen is byte-identical to 6b). Crate growth was already checked (788->777, 716->731). Lands sm' 2196F9C70148 -> F8378BD6937F.")
inl note_of_lib_split_list_quadratic_10 () = Note ("LIB-SPLIT-LIST-QUADRATIC", 10u32, "2026-10-30T04:51Z", "kit", "2026-10-30T04:51 kit: LANDED in window 6 at 00:50:40 -04:00 through lib-guarded-do (dry pass 1 previewed, 0 written; real pass 'applied with no live lib reader'): sm'.spi 2196F9C70148 -> F8378BD6937F, live re-read matches. Gate evidence: A/B job-1793333150121 on pack 7, two copies differing only in sm'.spi, IDENTICAL gate lines (67/72, the same 5 cuda failures and not_run rows, 0 type errors). Correction to n8's cause: the base raw stderr shows 'spiral cuda' child python lacking numpy (main.py: __import__('numpy') -> ModuleNotFoundError), i.e. PATH's scoop python 3.15 rather than SPIRAL_PYTHON python312; the K1100M/cupy mismatch is secondary. Remaining for Done: main's window close (document_test + type_probe), then the runtime control after TRAIN5 (time-shadows-ratio.ps1, installed kit vs TRAIN5 kit).")
inl note_of_lib_split_list_quadratic_11 () = Note ("LIB-SPLIT-LIST-QUADRATIC", 11u32, "2026-10-30T04:52Z", "main", "10-30 00:52 main: LANDED in WINDOW 6 and closed green. kit landed at 00:50:40 through lib-guarded-do (dry pass first): sm' 2196F9C70148 -> F8378BD6937F. Close: document_test 22 passed; type_probe sm' PASS (71 tests typed, 0 errors, control ok). CUDA note, the final cause from kit's raw stderr: the 5 sm' cuda failures are pre-existing and come from spiral.process_cuda launching the PATH python (no numpy) instead of SPIRAL_PYTHON, a runner bug routed to git-a6. They're unrelated to split_list (the base side fails identically). Remaining for kit to close: the half/full ratio control (about 2x, not 13x) measured on the landed lib.")
inl note_of_lib_split_list_quadratic_12 () = Note ("LIB-SPLIT-LIST-QUADRATIC", 12u32, "2026-10-30T10:25Z", "kit", "2026-10-30T10:25 kit groom: window 6 closed green by main (document_test 22, type_probe 71 typed, 0 errors). Progress 80 -> 85. Only the runtime control is left (time-shadows-ratio.ps1, saved C528BE4D copy vs the TRAIN5 binary), and TRAIN5 was lost at 01:26, so it waits on the TRAIN5 rerun.")
inl note_of_lib_split_list_quadratic_13 () = Note ("LIB-SPLIT-LIST-QUADRATIC", 13u32, "2026-10-30T16:44Z", "kit", "10-30 12:4x kit: downstream effects measured by x on pack 8 (x's message 12:4x; SIM-IN-SPIRAL n101, X-HAND-UNITS n30), recorded here because this rewrite caused them. (1) Gleam: split_list's new array path (split |> array |> fold |> rev) makes generated Gleam import gary (array refs keyed through gary). A Gleam package with a HAND-WRITTEN gleam.toml that reaches split_list now fails 'gleam build' with 'Unknown module gary' on a clean resolve. Fix per package: add gary = \">= 1.1.0 and < 2.0.0\", the range Spiral's generator writes. x fixed its sim (split/frames). Other lanes with hand-made Gleam packages should check theirs. (2) Rust: consumers gain a Mut0 ref struct and a list-reverse loop; x's xc crate went 960 -> 975 lines. kit's own crates: the 20-crate pack-8 regen control at 12:4x kept their line counts, so no kit crate grew past what TRAIN5 measured. No kit action is pending; lanes that hit (1) apply the one-line gleam.toml fix.")
inl note_of_lib_split_list_quadratic_14 () = Note ("LIB-SPLIT-LIST-QUADRATIC", 14u32, "2026-11-01T03:36Z", "kit", "10-31 23:4x kit groom (main 23:33, user: cadence/drift): Active -> PLANNED. No note in over 12 h, so nobody is working it this week; Planned keeps it in agile next at its priority (never auto-Paused, R3). First step when picked = the next step in its latest note.")
inl kit_dotnet_fallback () = Task ("KIT-DOTNET-FALLBACK", "kit: the managed compiler's dotnet falls back to PATH when KIT_DOTNET/SPIRAL_DOTNET are unset, so 'kit agile check' fails since the wipe; default to the spiral-bin toolchain", TaskKind, P1, D1, 100u32, Done, "EOIE-RELEASE-TRAIN", "kit,wipe-redo,toolchain", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_kit_dotnet_fallback () = Planning ("KIT-DOTNET-FALLBACK", Minutes 30u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_kit_dotnet_fallback_1 () = Note ("KIT-DOTNET-FALLBACK", 1u32, "2026-10-30T02:09Z", "kit", "2026-10-30T02:09 kit: found by git-a6 (relayed by main 22:0x). kit_process/native_compiler.rs:8 resolves KIT_DOTNET, then SPIRAL_DOTNET, then 'dotnet' on PATH. Both vars are gone from the User env (likely the wipe), and PATH's C:\\Program Files\\dotnet lacks 11.0.0-rc.3 (only rc.1), so 'kit agile check' fails with 'You must install or update .NET'. Fix drafted: C:\\home\\data\\kit-drafts\\train5\\dotnet-toolchain-fallback.do adds a fallback to %LOCALAPPDATA%\\spiral-bin\\toolchains\\dotnet\\dotnet.exe when it exists, before 'dotnet'. The exact expression is proven with rustc (unset -> toolchain; KIT_DOTNET set -> it wins; no toolchain -> dotnet) and folded into TRAIN5's train5-src.do. A code fix, not an env var, so it survives the next profile loss. Done = 'kit agile check <root>' passes from a shell with neither var set, on the TRAIN5 binary.")
inl note_of_kit_dotnet_fallback_2 () = Note ("KIT-DOTNET-FALLBACK", 2u32, "2026-10-31T13:51Z", "kit", "10-31 09:5x kit: DONE (installed-is-done). The done line was 'kit agile check <root> passes from a shell with neither var set, on the TRAIN5 binary'. kit's shell has KIT_DOTNET and SPIRAL_DOTNET both EMPTY (checked), and on the installed E14F1F0B, 'kit agile check C:\\home\\git\\i574n.github\\docs\\backlog' at 07:46 printed 'kit agile check ok ... compiler=available source=deployed ... path=...\\spiral-bin\\bin\\single-flight\\SpiralCompiler\\Release\\net11.0\\SpiralCompiler.dll', where the old binary failed with 'You must install or update .NET'. The fallback applier (dotnet-toolchain-fallback.do) has landed: kit do --dry-run finds none of its anchors in the tree, and its source draft is archived (run 20261031-091339). A rerun at 09:5x hit 'command timed out after 30000 ms' on views.spi. That is the typecheck-attestation cap under a 100% CPU box (check-attestation-cap memory), not the dotnet lookup.")
