inl kit_zero_downtime_release () = Task ("KIT-ZERO-DOWNTIME-RELEASE", "kit releases must not take tools away from lanes: agile and every read verb keep working while a train regenerates, publishes and installs (user 10-30: \"we should have zero downtime deployments here\")", Story, P1, D2, 100u32, Done, "", "kit,release,downtime", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_kit_zero_downtime_release () = Planning ("KIT-ZERO-DOWNTIME-RELEASE", Minutes 60u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_kit_zero_downtime_release_1 () = Note ("KIT-ZERO-DOWNTIME-RELEASE", 1u32, "2026-10-31T01:33Z", "kit", "10-30 21:3x kit: filed from the user's report (via compiler): tibia could not use agile during TRAIN5 (a lease since 20:53). (1) FIXED: the block was the PreToolUse hook ~/.claude/hooks/eoie-tree-lease.ps1, not kit (kit never reads tree leases). While a release leased C:\\home\\git\\kit, the hook refused any shell command that named that path together with a write-verb pattern, and its redirection pattern matched '2>&1', so 'C:\\home\\git\\kit\\kit.exe agile ... 2>&1' was refused even though agile writes only to the backlog. The hook now, before matching: replaces the repo kit.exe invocation with 'kit.exe', blanks here-string bodies (note prose), and blanks stream merges or discards (N>&M, N>$null, *> $null, > NUL). 10/10 cases against the live lease: 5 agile/read/note commands allowed, 5 real writes into the tree still blocked (including a write whose value is a here-string). Backup eoie-tree-lease.ps1.before-zero-downtime-20261030. Tibia and compiler told. Known remaining false positive: a write-verb word inside a file name (lane-copy-swap.do matched \\bcopy\\b). (2) DRAFTED, applies when TRAIN5 releases the lease: release-train.ps1's lane-copy step did 'Move-Item .next -> kit.exe -Force', which fails while any lane runs kit.exe ('lane copy busy', install skipped). The draft C:\\home\\data\\kit-drafts\\train5\\lane-copy-swap.do renames the running binary into %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\retired first (Windows allows renaming a running exe), then moves the new one in, the way Publish-KitBinary already does for the repo copy.")
inl note_of_kit_zero_downtime_release_2 () = Note ("KIT-ZERO-DOWNTIME-RELEASE", 2u32, "2026-10-31T02:01Z", "kit", "10-30 21:5x kit: hook text changed at the user's request (relayed by tibia, memory work-in-the-repo): the refusal now says to keep a held change in C:\\home\\data\\<team>-drafts, never TEMP or a scratchpad. The old scratchpad advice was one traced cause of drafts lost in the 10-28 wipe. The hook test moved out of my scratchpad to i574n.github/scripts/tree-lease-hook-test.ps1 (10/10 pass).")
inl note_of_kit_zero_downtime_release_3 () = Note ("KIT-ZERO-DOWNTIME-RELEASE", 3u32, "2026-10-31T02:21Z", "kit", "probe: agile write check 22:0x")
inl note_of_kit_zero_downtime_release_4 () = Note ("KIT-ZERO-DOWNTIME-RELEASE", 4u32, "2026-10-31T02:50Z", "kit", "10-30 22:5x kit: release-train.ps1 gains -Snapshot (unstaged in kit/scripts; parses). It mirrors the live repo (robocopy /MIR, without target, .cache or .git: 66 MB, 2,946 files) into kit/.cache/release-tree/<name> and runs every phase there: regen, crate gate, candidate, notebooks, release build. Results still go to the live .cache/release-train/<name>. Any regen lease now holds the snapshot, so the live tree stays writable for lanes and for in-repo work during a 2-hour train. After regen, changed generated outputs are copied back to the live tree only where the live file is unchanged since the snapshot; conflicts are reported for the next in-place regen. After release, the built kit.exe replaces the live repo copy and the lane copy, both by retire-then-move. Also landed: the lane-copy swap from n1 (2). Not yet run end to end; first use is the train after TRAIN5b (TRAIN5b started 22:47 the old way with -SkipRegen, after in-place regens).")
inl note_of_kit_zero_downtime_release_5 () = Note ("KIT-ZERO-DOWNTIME-RELEASE", 5u32, "2026-10-31T03:38Z", "kit", "10-30 23:4x kit: first snapshot train, TRAIN5c (job kit-train5c-20261030, launched with kit/scripts/launch-train.ps1 -Snapshot -SkipRegen), found three snapshot-mode bugs, now fixed in release-train.ps1: (1) the spiral repo was resolved as <snapshot>/../spiral; now from the live root; (2) KIT_SPIRAL_BUNDLE is set from the live root when unset, because workspace.ps1 defaults it relative to its own folder; (3) changed packages came from 'git status' inside the snapshot, which has no .git, so TRAIN5c ran 39 binary notebooks and 0 module notebooks; now it asks the live repo. TRAIN5c keeps going to install the hidden-console fix the lanes need tonight; the follow-up train runs the full module set. Confirmed working: the live tree stayed writable the whole time. I landed and regenerated 7 crates in place during TRAIN5c with no lease in the way. Also fixed: user-env.ps1's loop variable $name clobbered callers' $Name (case-insensitive), which gave one launch the label 'kit-MSBuildSDKsPath'.")
inl note_of_kit_zero_downtime_release_6 () = Note ("KIT-ZERO-DOWNTIME-RELEASE", 6u32, "2026-10-31T08:35Z", "kit", "10-31 04:3x kit: TWO more downtime sources found tonight. (1) An in-place regen, test-regeneration.ps1 -Package <4 crates> -Publish, leases the WHOLE kit root (regeneration-<pid>.lease root=C:\\home\\git\\kit) for about 5 minutes. That blocked an unrelated edit to kit_lint_classify and scripts/release-train.ps1. Fix shape: lease only src/<package> for each selected package, plus the root Cargo files when members change. (2) Train notebooks that time out under load sink the whole train. TRAIN5e has 6 reds, all 'Spiral cell timed out after 300s' (kit_experiment_stats, kit_experiments x2, kit_handoff_render, kit_job_slot, kit_jobs model), while fiscal, compiler and a regen shared the box. A starved run is VOID, not a failure (memory below-normal-starves-on-saturated-box). Fix in release-train.ps1: timed-out notebooks rerun once, serially, after the parallel phase. A -Resume switch reuses the snapshot, candidate and passing verdicts of an earlier run with the same -Name and reruns only the non-passing notebooks, so a load-starved train finishes without another 4-hour cycle.")
inl note_of_kit_zero_downtime_release_7 () = Note ("KIT-ZERO-DOWNTIME-RELEASE", 7u32, "2026-10-31T09:05Z", "kit", "2026-10-31 05:1x kit: FIX (1) of n6 LANDED (unstaged), compiler-contracts/test-regeneration.ps1. A -Publish regen no longer leases the whole kit root. It walks the selected owners' package.spiproj 'packages:' lists and Cargo path deps transitively, and leases only those package folders, compiler-contracts, and the root-level workspace files (kit/* and kit/src/*). The publish-time inventory and hash check covers exactly that set, so an edit elsewhere in kit no longer voids the publish. PROOF, live: a regen of kit-lint-classify printed 'Leased 7 package folders and 17 workspace files; the rest of C:\\home\\git\\kit stays writable' (kit_lint_classify plus its transitive kit_lint_model, kit_lint_flow, kit_lint_flow_constructs, kit_lint_flow_idioms, kit_lint_pattern, plus compiler-contracts), then published 1 owner after 234 s. While it ran, the lease hook was pipe-tested: an Edit to scripts/launch-train.ps1 and to src/kit_log_counts/count_scan.spi got exit 0, and an Edit to src/kit_lint_classify/classify.spi and to src/Cargo.toml got exit 2. Earlier tonight the same kind of regen held the whole root for about 16 minutes. FIX (2) of n6 (train timeout retry and -Resume) is in scripts/release-train.ps1 and launch-train.ps1 (unstaged, parse-checked). Its first real use is resuming TRAIN5e once it stops on the timeout reds.")
inl note_of_kit_zero_downtime_release_8 () = Note ("KIT-ZERO-DOWNTIME-RELEASE", 8u32, "2026-10-31T10:22Z", "kit", "2026-10-31 06:2x kit: trains no longer hold lib lands (main's report: TRAIN5e held main's debris land for its full 180-min wait, and pg plus 6 more lands queued behind it). launch-train -Snapshot/-Resume now robocopies spiral/lib/spiral to .cache/release-tree/<name>-lib/lib/spiral (excluding bench and target) and passes -Lib <copy> on release-train's command line. release-train sets SPIRAL_CELLS_LIB and SPIRAL_LIB for the control and every notebook, and logs 'notebooks read the lib at <path>'. FINDING: SPIRAL_LIB alone never took effect in trains. notebook.ex resolve_lib tries SPIRAL_CELLS_LIB first, then walks the notebook's ancestors, and from kit/.cache/release-tree/... the walk reaches C:\\home\\git, where 'spiral/lib/spiral' is the live lib, so every train notebook ran against the live lib whatever SPIRAL_LIB said. Main owns the guard half: Get-LiveReaders in apps/cells/spi/lib_land_window.ps1 must treat a release-train.ps1 ancestor with a non-live -Lib like lib_gate's (the one-line regex is sent). LIVE: TRAIN5e restarted at 06:20 through the chained gate (job-1793441998142); its command line shows '-Resume -Lib C:\\home\\git\\kit\\.cache\\release-tree\\train5e-20261031-lib\\lib\\spiral'. gate3 is chained after it.")
inl note_of_kit_zero_downtime_release_9 () = Note ("KIT-ZERO-DOWNTIME-RELEASE", 9u32, "2026-10-31T10:40Z", "kit", "2026-10-31 06:5x kit: n1's known remaining false positive is FIXED. The lease hook matched the command-word verbs (copy, move, del, rd, rmdir, mkdir, cp, mv, rm, tee) with \\b boundaries, and '-' is a boundary, so file names such as lane-copy-swap.do or rm-notes.md counted as writes. They now match only when not glued to a word character, '.', '/', '\\' or '-'. i574n.github/scripts/tree-lease-hook-test.ps1 grew from 10 to 15 cases, all OK: reading lane-copy-swap.do and rm-notes.md under a leased root is allowed, and 'cp ...', 'copy ...' and 'rm ...' into it are still blocked, as are the original 10. Backup kit-tree-lease.ps1.before-filename-verbs-20261031.bak. With tonight's narrower regen lease (n7), the train's lib snapshot (n8, which unblocks lib lands) and the resume path, what remains for this item is accepting the retire-then-move lane-copy swap on TRAIN5e's install.")
inl note_of_kit_zero_downtime_release_10 () = Note ("KIT-ZERO-DOWNTIME-RELEASE", 10u32, "2026-10-31T13:39Z", "kit", "10-31 09:4x kit: DONE. The last open check (n9) passed on TRAIN5e's install at 07:43. train.log: 'live repo kit.exe refreshed: E14F1F0B' and 'lane copy ... refreshed: E14F1F0B (the previous build was renamed into ...\\kit\\retired, so running lanes kept working)'. %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\retired holds kit-/lane-kit-/repo-kit-20261031074316..20 exes, and no 'lane copy busy' or 'swap failed' line appeared (before n2, Move-Item -Force failed while any lane ran kit.exe and the install was skipped). Agile stayed writable throughout: kit and other lanes wrote notes during the train (EOIE-RELEASE-TRAIN n200-n201, written while TRAIN5e/6 ran). Delivered on this item: the lease hook's false positives (n1, n9), retire-then-move binary swaps (n2), snapshot trains so the live tree stays writable (n3/n4), the narrowed regen lease (n7), the train lib snapshot so lib lands proceed (n8), and resume after a cap or starvation (EOIE-RELEASE-TRAIN n196-n198). Follow-ups live elsewhere: the Done-path TODO scan (KIT-WIP-TODO-MARKERS n17) and incremental regen plus train holds (EOIE-RELEASE-TRAIN n203).")
inl cells_daemon_retire_stop () = Task ("CELLS-DAEMON-RETIRE-STOP", "Cells compiler daemon crashes instead of retiring when a pool worker does not stop within GenServer.stop's 30 s: kill the stuck worker and finish the retire", Story, P1, D2, 100u32, Done, "", "cells,daemon,reliability,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_cells_daemon_retire_stop () = Planning ("CELLS-DAEMON-RETIRE-STOP", Minutes 45u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_cells_daemon_retire_stop_1 () = Note ("CELLS-DAEMON-RETIRE-STOP", 1u32, "2026-10-31T01:43Z", "main", "10-30 21:43 main, found while a lib_gate looked stuck (the process-test proof, daemon port 14747). cells-14747.prev.log: 21:35:30 'idle and free memory under 4096 MB; retiring' (by design: the daemon idles while the gate runs the compiled tests, and the box had under 4 GB free), then 'retired; stopping', then at 21:38:56 the daemon CRASHED in retire_when_idle (compiler_daemon.ex:452): stop_pool_workers called GenServer.stop(worker, :normal, 30_000) on an extra pool worker that did not stop, and the :timeout exit killed the daemon instead of letting it retire. The client started a fresh daemon at 21:39:41, so the gate went on with a cold start (time, not correctness). The :memsup errors in the same log (os_mon_sysinfo get_mem_info timeouts at 21:27/29/31 under load) are noise: system_memory_data already reads through a task with a 2 s timeout. FIX DRAFTED: cells-daemon-port\\apply7-retire-stop.ps1 (2 anchors). stop_worker/2 catches the :exit and falls back to Process.exit(worker, :kill). Test: a GenServer that traps exits and hangs in terminate is gone within 2 s after stop_worker(worker, 200). Scratch proof running; land in the next Cells window with apply6 (spiral.new's 'eoie' epic line).")
inl note_of_cells_daemon_retire_stop_2 () = Note ("CELLS-DAEMON-RETIRE-STOP", 2u32, "2026-10-31T02:48Z", "main", "10-30 22:48 main: scratch proof. compiler_daemon_test.exs with apply7: the new test (a stuck worker that traps exits and hangs in terminate is gone within 2 s after stop_worker(worker, 200)) PASSES when run alone (1 passed). The full file was 22/26 under the overloaded box; the 4 failures are real-compiler daemon round trips timing out on :gen_tcp.recv (120 s and 60 s), not this change. Land in the next Cells window, whose full mix test reruns them.")
inl note_of_cells_daemon_retire_stop_3 () = Note ("CELLS-DAEMON-RETIRE-STOP", 3u32, "2026-10-31T11:27Z", "main", "10-31 07:27 main (backlog pass): the fix (stop_worker catches :exit and falls back to Process.exit(worker, :kill), plus a test) rides Cells window 6 as apply7-retire-stop. Window 6 failed at 04:59 from stale --failed reruns and a 60 s test timeout (both fixed in the script) and is running again now as the last step of the land queue; close this item when window 6 lands.")
inl note_of_cells_daemon_retire_stop_4 () = Note ("CELLS-DAEMON-RETIRE-STOP", 4u32, "2026-10-31T13:48Z", "main", "10-31 09:48 main: Cells window 6 PROVEN on a fresh scratch tree (live apps/cells + lib copies, the 4 canonical .spir, all 6 appliers: apply6 kit epic line, apply7 retire-stop, headings apply-cells, apply8 console Delphi arms, apply9 load-test timeout, apply10 launcher default): new_project_test + compiler_daemon_test + document_test 47/50; the 3 failures are exactly the tests that need dice/alphabet as sibling repos (absent in the scratch layout). Window 6 waits for kit's train6 to end (train hold cells-window-6 set until 15:38, so no new train starts) and then runs on the live tree.")
inl note_of_cells_daemon_retire_stop_5 () = Note ("CELLS-DAEMON-RETIRE-STOP", 5u32, "2026-10-31T17:27Z", "main", "10-31 13:27 main: DONE. Cells window 6 LANDED green 13:03-13:15 (mix test of new_project_test + compiler_daemon_test + document_test: 50/50 passed; undo C:\\home\\data\\main-drafts\\session-1030\\undo-window6-20261031-130350). Hashes: new_project.ex 7E3AAB39B7DE, new_project_test.exs 10091988BF7F, compiler_daemon.ex 89DA1B9B0E29, compiler_daemon_test.exs EF2E5ECB63D9, document.spi DF0D48010443, document_test.exs 6BE008CCC717, console.spi 4BC11C0F0F4D, compiler_client.ex C358427B3655, lib cli_args_real.spir 17A2E539F7EF, json_real.spir 5959E93BF356, routes_real.spir 9361958CDBF5, sm'_real.spir FB2680503D46. The retire-stop fallback (stop_worker catches :exit, then Process.exit(worker, :kill)) and its test are live.")
inl edition3_manifest_model () = Task ("EDITION3-MANIFEST-MODEL", "spiral.toml modules model: replace the multi-line string holding the old spiproj tree DSL with a SOTA model (an import-graph-derived order and an explicit prelude, no module list), decided by experiment before the edition-3 rounds convert the remaining manifests (user question 2026-10-30 evening)", Story, P1, D5, 95u32, Done, "", "edition3,compiler,cells,manifest,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_edition3_manifest_model () = Planning ("EDITION3-MANIFEST-MODEL", Minutes 480u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_edition3_manifest_model_1 () = Note ("EDITION3-MANIFEST-MODEL", 1u32, "2026-10-31T02:00Z", "main", "10-30 22:00 main, from the user's question (was a multi-line string the best choice for modules in spiral.toml? where was it decided? a more SOTA model?). WHERE: not by the user. The user chose the FILE (SPIRAL3-EDITIONS n10: spiral.toml; 'edition 3 to the end', 10-24). The modules ENCODING was main's own design note, EDITION3-SPIRAL-TOML n1 (10-24 17:0x local): modules = a TOML multi-line literal holding the existing spiproj indentation tree, parsed by the same file_hierarchy parser, 'so module order and flags need no new encoding'. It optimised for a zero-risk migration (read-back proven on 1,643 manifests, 0 differ) and compared NO alternative. COST of that choice: TOML gives the modules nothing (no structure, no schema or editor validation, diagnostics point into an opaque string); the sigil DSL survives inside strings (packages = ['|core-', 'spiral-'], module flags '-' include and '*' real, 'dir/' nesting); and a second translator now exists (Cells' Elixir port of tomlManifestText), so two parsers can drift. WHAT THE DSL MEANS (it is semantic, not cosmetic): order = each module sees only the earlier ones (F#-style); '-' = auto-include the module's names unqualified into every later module; '*' = real-mode .spir; 'dir/' = nesting. CENSUS (lib/spiral/package.spiproj): 124 module lines, 10 dirs, only 7 auto-includes (am'_prototypes, backend, base, iter_prototypes, sm'_prototypes, trace, common), 4 real; files already declare 241 explicit ':<' opens. OPTIONS: (A) structured TOML now: modules = an ordered array, flags as inline tables or a prelude = [...] key, packages as tables ({ name, path, include }); mechanical, reuses the converter and its read-back check. (B) the SOTA end state, as in Gleam, Cargo, Lean and Go: NO module list. Order derived from the import graph (qualified references + ':<' opens, topologically sorted; cycles already illegal); the 7 auto-includes become an explicit prelude key (or per-file ':<'); '*' from the .spir extension; dirs from the filesystem. The manifest keeps only name, edition, packages and crates. (C) a typed manifest written in Spiral (like Lake's lakefile.lean and build.zig): most expressive, but bootstrapping cost. RECOMMENDATION: B, reached through A's schema. TIMING: switching is cheap NOW (spiral.toml on disk: tibia 7, fiscal 0/33, x 0; the rounds are about to convert the rest). EXPERIMENT FIRST (experiment until SOTA): (1) derive lib's module order from references and check the declared order is a valid topological order of it; (2) count unqualified uses of the 7 auto-included modules' names (the cost of an explicit prelude); (3) census every repo's manifests for flag use.")
inl note_of_edition3_manifest_model_2 () = Note ("EDITION3-MANIFEST-MODEL", 2u32, "2026-10-31T02:02Z", "main", "10-30 22:02 main, EXPERIMENT 1 DONE (manifest-model\\order-check.ps1, read-only): over lib/spiral's 114 declared modules (all files found), the script collected 428 module references in code (':<' opens plus qualified 'module.name' uses, with test blocks, string literals and macro text excluded, since tests compile separately). 427 point to an EARLIER module; the one 'later' hit is a doc heading ('/// ### rust.rust_type' in backend.spi), not code. So the declared order is exactly a topological order of the code's reference graph: the hand-kept list holds no order the code doesn't already state, which supports option B (derive order; no module list). What references cannot show is unqualified use of the 7 auto-included modules, and those are listed first, i.e. they are a prelude. NEXT: experiment 2 (count unqualified uses of the prelude modules' names per file, the cost of making the prelude explicit) and experiment 3 (the cross-repo flag census, running).")
inl note_of_edition3_manifest_model_3 () = Note ("EDITION3-MANIFEST-MODEL", 3u32, "2026-10-31T02:35Z", "main", "10-30 22:35 main, user decision: 'make edition 3 toml work without the module list while retaining all the gains'. MODEL (prototype manifest-model\\derive-modules.ps1, F# port derive.fs.txt): a spiral.toml with NO modules key derives the module tree from moduleDir. (a) Files: every .spi/.spir under moduleDir, skipping generated dirs, links and nested packages (dirs with their own manifest); '*' comes from the .spir extension and 'dir/' from the filesystem. (b) Order: a stable topological sort (alphabetical ties) of the code's module references (':<' opens and qualified uses; test blocks, strings and macro text excluded). (c) A new key prelude = [...] replaces the '-' include flags: prelude modules keep their listed relative order, and every other module goes after each prelude module unless it is in the CUMULATIVE dependency closure of the prelude so far (this keeps unqualified prelude names visible; the non-cumulative first version made a cycle). On lib: 114 of 114 modules, no cycle. Visibility gains vs declared are inert: among the 7 prelude modules only 'main' (trace, common; entry points) is defined twice. 55 PROTOTYPE-INSTANCE inversions (a module now before an instance-defining module: convert, listm', am', sm', physics, leptos, xsd_emit) are the open risk; job main-manifest-order-compare type-probes every lib file under both orders to settle it. WIRING (one owner, the compiler): schema (pdir, text) calls withDerivedModules, which builds the modules block for a TOML without one and blanks the prelude lines (ranges unchanged), so config, the '-'/'*' flags and Directory nesting are untouched (apply-core.ps1, shared section, both cores). Host verb --resolve-manifest <dir> prints the effective manifest, with capability 'resolve-manifest' (apply-host.ps1). Cells' Manifest.read asks the compiler for a modules-free TOML (capability-gated) and falls back to from_toml (apply-cells.ps1, plus a test with an injected resolver). PROOFS RUNNING: node job job-1793413868544-main-manifest-candidate (Debug candidate in a private cache; probes: capability listed, --resolve-manifest equals the prototype's block, --check old vs derived on 5 lib files); Cells scratch manifest_test. Open follow-ups: LSP watchers do not re-derive when a .spi is added (CLI builds read fresh); cache the resolve call in Cells.")
inl note_of_edition3_manifest_model_4 () = Note ("EDITION3-MANIFEST-MODEL", 4u32, "2026-10-31T02:52Z", "main", "10-30 22:52 main, CANDIDATE PROBE 1 (node, job-1793413868544): the Debug candidate built in a private cache (0 errors, 262 s) and lists the capability 'resolve-manifest'. With the module list removed from a converted lib spiral.toml, --resolve-manifest printed a spiproj-shaped manifest whose modules block is derived (prelude first, with '-'). TWO FINDINGS: (1) module names containing an apostrophe (am'_prototypes, sm'_prototypes) cannot be written as TOML single-quoted literal strings (TOML has no escape inside '...'), so prelude entries need double quotes (\\\"am'_prototypes\\\"); the converter and docs must say so. (2) A REAL DERIVATION BUG, caught by --check: process.spi failed with 'Unbound type variable: threading' because the reference scanner stripped string literals over the WHOLE file, and a quote inside a char literal ('\\\"', process.spi:16, 28, 32, 34, 112) paired with a quote many lines later and erased the code between them, including process.spi:199's threading.cancellation_token. Experiment 1's '427 of 428' over-counted for the same reason: a missing edge never shows up as an order violation. FIX (prototype and F#): strip per line, char literals first, then macro text, then strings, then trailing // comments. Re-derived: threading now precedes process. Probe 2 (job-1793414868770) rebuilds the candidate and runs --check on EVERY lib .spi under both manifests, since only the compiler's own checker catches missing edges. Note: the box kit job that ran the type_probe comparison stalled silently (compiler's finding: the installed kit's DETACHED_PROCESS launch) and was stopped.")
inl note_of_edition3_manifest_model_5 () = Note ("EDITION3-MANIFEST-MODEL", 5u32, "2026-10-31T02:58Z", "main", "10-30 22:58 main: (1) PROBE 2 so far: rebuilt candidate (107 s); --resolve-manifest on the modules-free lib gives 123 module lines, IDENTICAL to the PowerShell prototype (after the line-local stripping fix); --check over every lib .spi under both manifests is running. (2) CONVERTER drafted (apply-core.ps1, 3 more anchors): 'format --manifest toml --derive <dirs>' writes prelude = [\\\"a\\\", ...] (DOUBLE-quoted, so names with an apostrophe work) from the spiproj's '-' entries instead of the modules block, and accepts only when the derived manifest names exactly the same module entries (path plus flags) as the listed ones (order may differ). Without --derive the converter is unchanged. --derive joins positional()'s bare-flag exceptions, so it doesn't swallow the directory. Dry-applied to a copy: all anchors match. (3) LSP GAP, precise: deletion already re-derives (file_delete marks the owning package dirty, revalidate_parent at spiral_compiler.fs:185202, then proj_graph_update re-reads the manifest). CREATION does not: the supervisor has no file-created request, and opening an unlisted file reports 'File X cannot be found in the project' (:188679). Follow-up: on that path, if the owning package's spiral.toml has no modules key, re-run proj_open for the package and retry once.")
inl note_of_edition3_manifest_model_6 () = Note ("EDITION3-MANIFEST-MODEL", 6u32, "2026-10-31T03:28Z", "main", "10-30 23:28 main: the pack draft now lives IN THE REPO at spiral/apps/compiler/packs/manifest-model/ (moved from main-drafts per the user's in-repo rule): apply-core.ps1 (schema derivation + --derive converter), apply-host.ps1 (--resolve-manifest + capability), apply-cells.ps1 (Cells Manifest.read asks the compiler), derive.fs.txt (the F# inserted by apply-core), derive-modules-oracle.ps1 (the PowerShell oracle; its lib output must equal the F# one), lib-derived-modules.txt (expected lib block), node-proof.ps1 (build + probes), order-check.ps1. Also fixed since the last note: both scanners understand edition-3 paths (a::b and multi-segment opens like ':< lib::extra'), checked on samples/folder_index_module and with the lib unchanged. Edit only the repo copy.")
inl note_of_edition3_manifest_model_7 () = Note ("EDITION3-MANIFEST-MODEL", 7u32, "2026-10-31T03:59Z", "main", "10-30 23:59 main, PROBE 2 GREEN (node job-1793414868770, Debug candidate in a private cache): --resolve-manifest on the modules-free lib gives 123 lines, identical to the PowerShell oracle; and --check on all 110 lib .spi files under the hand-written list vs the derived manifest: SAME exit on 110, DIFFERENT 0. That settles the 55 prototype-instance order inversions: no file's check depends on them. CELLS side green on a scratch copy: manifest_test.exs 4/4 in 4.7 s (new test: a modules-free TOML takes the compiler's derived manifest, falls back without a capable compiler, and never calls the resolver when a list is declared). Its first two runs crawled because main ran them at BelowNormal on a saturated box (compiler's 23:4x diagnosis; memory belownormal-starves-on-saturated-box). PROBE 3 starting (job main-manifest-probe3, built from the repo pack folder): the ::-aware scanner, P4 the regression sample manifest_derived_order (derived manifest passes; a hand-written alphabetical list, alpha before zeta, must fail: the positive control), P5 the --derive converter on a spiproj-only lib copy (must write a double-quoted prelude, no modules key, and resolve to the oracle block).")
inl note_of_edition3_manifest_model_8 () = Note ("EDITION3-MANIFEST-MODEL", 8u32, "2026-10-31T04:30Z", "main", "10-31 00:30 main, PROBE 3 GREEN (node job-1793420257179, Debug candidate built from spiral/apps/compiler/packs/manifest-model): build exit 0 (251 s); P2 --resolve-manifest on the modules-free lib equals the oracle (123 lines; ::-aware scanner); P4 regression sample manifest_derived_order: the derived manifest --check exit 0, and the positive control with a hand-written ALPHABETICAL list (alpha before zeta) exit 5, so order matters and the derivation gets it right; P5 converter 'format --manifest toml --derive' on a spiproj-only lib copy wrote BOTH packages (lib and the nested bench/parsing) as 'module list derived': prelude = [double-quoted names], no modules key, and the converted lib resolves to the oracle block (its own read-back accepts only identical module entry sets). Earlier: probe 2, --check on all 110 lib files, same result under both manifests. Bug fixed between 3 and 3b: positional() still collected the bare '--derive' as a directory (the second match case needed it too). STATUS: implementation proven; what remains is the compiler team's pack process (pair-drift check, -Suite all review and accept, install), and after install, the sample manifest_derived_order joins samples/ (it would fail every lane's matrix on today's installed compiler, so it waits in the pack). Follow-ups: LSP re-resolve on file creation (spiral_compiler.fs:188679, both cores); the rounds then convert with --derive (fiscal 33, x 2, kit, lib).")
