inl note_of_x_node_verify_script_1 () = Note ("X-NODE-VERIFY-SCRIPT", 1u32, "2026-10-31T11:48Z", "x", "2026-10-31 x: filed from the drift cleanup (SIM-IN-SPIRAL n146): the launcher builds node-proof's -Command for 'verify.ps1 -Part all -Accept -Out', hardcodes node paths (C:\\Users\\alias\\...\\SpiralCompiler.dll, dotnet), and lives in the data folder the user rejected. Done = the script in i574n.github/scripts with the node paths read from remote-machines.json or node-proof's own defaults, one verify launched through it, the old launcher archived with scratch-archive.ps1.")
inl note_of_x_node_verify_script_2 () = Note ("X-NODE-VERIFY-SCRIPT", 2u32, "2026-10-31T11:59Z", "x", "2026-10-31 07:59 x: WRITTEN, unstaged: i574n.github/scripts/x-node-verify.ps1 (-Label mandatory, -MirrorName default xqueue1, -Core optional -> -RequireCore, -TimeoutMs, -Part all|client|sim, -NoAccept). It builds the same node command as launch-x-verify33.ps1 (x-compat gate on client + sim spi, verify.ps1 -Part <p> [-Accept] -Out <stage>\\xvNN-out, free the mirror's target dirs), with no hardcoded node paths: inside the remote command the compiler and dotnet come from the node's own $env:LOCALAPPDATA\\spiral-bin, and node-proof's -CompilerDll (pasted into the remote script in single quotes, so it must be literal) is derived from one 'remote.ps1 node run $env:LOCALAPPDATA' call; the stage dir comes from remote-machines.json. Checks: parse 0 errors; the derived DLL path equals the old literal byte for byte. It prints the land command at the end. LEFT for Done: the next x verify launches through it (verify41 was already running on the old launcher), then the old launcher is archived with scratch-archive.ps1.")
