inl note_of_config_baseline_11 () = Note ("CONFIG-BASELINE", 11u32, "2026-11-05T10:10Z", "config-baseline", "scheduled check: config-baseline 2026-11-05 06:10 vs baseline-20261102-150908.tsv: DRIFT (5): CHANGED C:\\Users\\i574n\\.config\\scoop\\config.json; CHANGED C:\\Users\\i574n\\.config\\remote-machines.json; CHANGED C:\\Users\\i574n\\.claude\\settings.json; CHANGED C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Roaming\\Microsoft\\Spelling\\neutral\\default.dic; ENV NO_COLOR: baseline '1' now '' | SANCTIONED (re-baseline): COMPILER host 6B9374835A1BBACF->E441B6B3F8865B02 (sanctioned by pack44-20261105-043957; re-baseline); COMPILER core E556D773EB123175->657DA4B173F9D3D3 (sanctioned by pack44-20261105-043957; re-baseline)")
inl memory_integrity_sweep () = Task ("MEMORY-INTEGRITY-SWEEP", "Periodic memory-store integrity sweep: validate every ~/.claude memory file (frontmatter name == filename, every [[link]] and MEMORY.md link resolves, each file indexed once), find duplicate records (same fact, two slugs) and rename-dangling links, and run kit agile links to cross-check memory<->backlog citations; report fixes, leave merges needing judgment as notes", TaskKind, P2, D2, 100u32, Done, "", "main,memory,durability,resilience,grooming", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_memory_integrity_sweep () = Planning ("MEMORY-INTEGRITY-SWEEP", Minutes 45u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_memory_integrity_sweep_1 () = Note ("MEMORY-INTEGRITY-SWEEP", 1u32, "2026-10-31T14:05Z", "resilience", "Filed 10-31 by resilience (git-51). Method proven in the 10-30/31 sweep: 204 files validated, the detached-process-visible-console(s) duplicate merged, the eoie-first->kit-first rename-dangling link caught. Tools: the validation one-liner in this item's history, i574n.github/scripts/wipe-recovery/cite_counts.py and cited_memories.py (memory<->citation), kit agile links --memory. Cadence: on demand and after any batch rename of memory slugs. Ready, light.")
inl note_of_memory_integrity_sweep_2 () = Note ("MEMORY-INTEGRITY-SWEEP", 2u32, "2026-10-31T14:46Z", "resilience", "10-31 10:4x resilience (git-51), first sweep run. FORWARD integrity (the store's own consistency) is CLEAN: 217 files, all frontmatter name==filename, all [[links]] resolve, all indexed in MEMORY.md exactly once, 0 near-duplicate bodies (6-gram shingle overlap, threshold 0.35). Fixed 1 dangling link: resilience-lane-role.md linked [[eoie-memory-symbiosis]] (renamed) -> [[kit-memory-symbiosis]]. BACKWARD (kit agile links): 20 memory->backlog citations name an abbreviated or pre-rename backlog ID (e.g. LIST-CONS, HOPAC-DEPTH, DELPHI-BENCH) rather than the exact current ID; these are prose pointers across many topics' memories, not machine links, so NOT rewritten (low value, would churn other lanes' memory). 3 unknown-memory + 1 unwritten-link in the backlog are prose uses of 'memory <word>'. Tool: scratchpad heal\\mem_sweep.py (validate + shingle dedup). Re-run on demand and after any batch slug rename.")
inl note_of_memory_integrity_sweep_3 () = Note ("MEMORY-INTEGRITY-SWEEP", 3u32, "2026-10-31T17:57Z", "main", "10-31 13:57 main: forward check rerun after main's 10-31 memory edits (new restore-copy-keeps-old-mtime; window-coordination, belownormal-starves-on-saturated-box and MEMORY.md edits): 218 files, name == filename for all, every file indexed in MEMORY.md exactly once, every [[link]] resolves except resilience-lane-role.md's [[MEMORY-INTEGRITY-SWEEP]] (a backlog ID in link syntax; allowed by the store's convention that unmatched links mark future memories, left to its owner). main's belownormal duplicate was handled as rule + evidence (the index already pairs them), not merged.")
inl note_of_memory_integrity_sweep_4 () = Note ("MEMORY-INTEGRITY-SWEEP", 4u32, "2026-11-01T03:35Z", "resilience", "NEXT STEP (CPU-light, no window): run scratchpad heal\\mem_sweep.py; it validates all ~219 memory files (frontmatter name==file, [[links]] resolve, indexed once) + flags near-dup bodies. Fix any dangling link in place. Re-run whenever memory changes or after a slug rename. Last run 10-31 14:46 clean.")
inl note_of_memory_integrity_sweep_5 () = Note ("MEMORY-INTEGRITY-SWEEP", 5u32, "2026-11-01T03:42Z", "resilience", "10-31 23:4x resilience (git-51): healed the lost-practice cluster MYSELF (not handed to main - memory coherence is this lane's job). Correction to my earlier 'thin' claim: the 4 while-waiting memories (parallel-while-waiting, no-sleep-polling, no-quiet-machine-wait, tokens-abundant-cpu-scarce) are actually well-written (rebuilt 10-29 with examples); the 'thin' label came from their 'original wording lost' tag, not real content. So the gap is APPLICATION, not documentation. Recorded the forensic measurement (wait/note 0.27->0.34 post-wipe, cadence.py) into backlog-always-has-cpu-light as the load-bearing memory and framed the practice as a HARD DEFAULT; cross-linked the cluster; strengthened parallel-while-waiting's opening. mem_sweep clean after the edits. Did this inline (4 small interrelated edits); parallelizing with agents here would be over-spawn - backups (mirror every 15 min, restore drill) make bolder parallel edits safe when the work is genuinely independent.")
inl note_of_memory_integrity_sweep_6 () = Note ("MEMORY-INTEGRITY-SWEEP", 6u32, "2026-11-01T06:18Z", "resilience", "2026-11-01 02:3x integrity sweep (round-3, after heavy concurrent memory churn). mem_sweep (scratchpad heal\\mem_sweep.py): 236 files, 236 indexed in MEMORY.md, PROBLEMS 0, duplicate-pairs 0. Validates the store stayed coherent through tonight''s simultaneous edits across lanes: resilience (fork-identity, backup-mirror-coverage-boundary, decision-rounds pre-rename), main (decision-rounds-one-at-a-time -> decision-rounds-batched rename + MEMORY.md & decisions-are-not-go relink), kit (kit-rename-call-path, node-proof-skips-regen). No orphaned index line, no name/-filename mismatch, and NO dangling [[link]] to the archived decision-rounds-one-at-a-time slug - so main''s relink was complete. Count 233 (round-2) -> 236 = +backup-mirror-coverage-boundary + lane retros'' new memories; net-0 for the rename.")
inl note_of_memory_integrity_sweep_7 () = Note ("MEMORY-INTEGRITY-SWEEP", 7u32, "2026-11-01T18:52Z", "resilience", "2026-11-01 14:4x INTEGRITY SWEEP (resilience, autonomous run). FORWARD clean: mem_sweep.py 249 files, 249 indexed in MEMORY.md, PROBLEMS 0, duplicate-pairs 0 (6-gram shingle > 0.35) - store stayed coherent through this session's churn (backup-mirror-coverage-boundary x4, unlink-mirror-junctions-first +node-kit junctions, agile-status-lost-updates, main's parallelize-dont-hand-off). Count 236 (round-3) -> 249 = +drafts/backup/junction memories across lanes. BACKWARD (kit agile links --memory): no NEW machine-dangling; the dangling rows (attribute-generated-lines->LIST-CONS, box-gpu-cuda-limit->CUDA-10, etc.) are the known pre-rename/abbreviated prose pointers prior sweeps classified NOT-rewrite. FIXED 2 I introduced this session: backup-mirror-coverage-boundary used [[UPPERCASE-BACKLOG-ID]] link syntax for KIT-ARCHIVE-OFFBOX-MIRROR and HOME-STRAY-FOLDERS (they are backlog items, not memory slugs) -> converted to plain 'backlog <ID>' refs so they stop generating unwritten-link noise each backward check. Precedent set: reserve [[ ]] for memory slugs; reference backlog items plainly. mem_sweep clean after the fix. Cadence: on demand + after slug renames.")
inl note_of_memory_integrity_sweep_8 () = Note ("MEMORY-INTEGRITY-SWEEP", 8u32, "2026-11-02T19:15Z", "resilience", "11-02 15:13 resilience: scheduled sweep (scripts/memory-integrity-sweep.ps1, daily 06:12 Pri5) ran CLEAN - 289 files, 289 indexed in MEMORY.md, 0 orphan. It validates: frontmatter present, name==filename, description+type present, MEMORY.md index-link resolution, orphan (file not indexed). DELIVERED = the forward-integrity core; the clean-store goal is met + verified daily. REMAINING for full DoD (port from ad-hoc mem_sweep.py per PY-REWRITE-ALL): (a) duplicate-record detection (6-gram shingle overlap >=0.35), (b) kit agile links memory<->backlog citation cross-check. Inline [[link]] resolution intentionally NOT a hard check - store convention is that unmatched [[links]] mark future memories, so it would false-positive. Bumping 15->65 to reflect the delivered+scheduled sweep.")
inl note_of_memory_integrity_sweep_9 () = Note ("MEMORY-INTEGRITY-SWEEP", 9u32, "2026-11-02T20:01Z", "resilience", "11-02 16:00 resilience: COMPLETE - memory-integrity-sweep.ps1 now covers the FULL DoD (ported the ad-hoc mem_sweep.py logic into the scheduled .ps1, PY-REWRITE-ALL aligned). Checks: frontmatter present + name==filename + description + type; MEMORY.md index-link resolution + orphans (all HARD -> DRIFT+exit1+NoteOnDrift); DUPLICATE-record detection via 6-gram shingle Jaccard>=0.35 (0 found, store clean); inline [[link]] resolution as INFO (unmatched = allowed future markers per store convention, 1 found); kit agile links memory<->backlog citation cross-check (summary: 292 memories, 726 cited, 29 dangling prose-pointers - low-value-not-rewritten per the audit, INFO only). dups/future-links/citations are reported, never fail the sweep (merges need judgment -> left as log info). Fixed an exit-code bug found in test: kit agile links exits 1 on findings leaving $LASTEXITCODE=1, so a clean sweep fell off the end as exit 1 (false-failure to the scheduler) - added explicit exit 0. Validated: clean, 292 files, 0 dup, exit 0, 67s. Scheduled daily 06:12 Pri5. Unstaged.")
inl note_of_memory_integrity_sweep_10 () = Note ("MEMORY-INTEGRITY-SWEEP", 10u32, "2026-11-03T10:13Z", "resilience", "sweep: memory-integrity-sweep 2026-11-03 06:13 DRIFT (79): 335 files, 256 indexed, 79 orphan, 7 future-links, 0 dup-candidates (first: ORPHAN backend-switch-idiom.md)")
inl note_of_memory_integrity_sweep_11 () = Note ("MEMORY-INTEGRITY-SWEEP", 11u32, "2026-11-04T10:15Z", "resilience", "sweep: memory-integrity-sweep 2026-11-04 06:15 DRIFT (167): 398 files, 231 indexed, 167 orphan, 10 future-links, 0 dup-candidates (first: ORPHAN ab-needs-in-job-aa.md)")
inl note_of_memory_integrity_sweep_12 () = Note ("MEMORY-INTEGRITY-SWEEP", 12u32, "2026-11-05T10:15Z", "resilience", "sweep: memory-integrity-sweep 2026-11-05 06:15 DRIFT (170): 450 files, 280 indexed, 170 orphan, 11 future-links, 0 dup-candidates (first: ORPHAN ab-needs-in-job-aa.md)")
inl sim_x_serve_render () = Task ("SIM-X-SERVE-RENDER", "x sim: post rendering + expansions, then serve the user timeline, post lookup and single post from the world", TaskKind, P1, D5, 100u32, Done, "SIM-X-SERVE-FOLLOWING", "x,sim,spiral,e2e", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_sim_x_serve_render () = Planning ("SIM-X-SERVE-RENDER", Minutes 180u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_sim_x_serve_render_1 () = Note ("SIM-X-SERVE-RENDER", 1u32, "2026-10-31T14:17Z", "x", "2026-10-31 x: slice 3 of SIM-X-FEATURE-PARITY (its n7). Port stub.ps1's Get-Rendered (id, HTML-escaped text, edit_history_post_ids defaulting to [id]; conversation_id, created_at, author_id, public_metrics zero-filled over the metric names, referenced_posts, entities.urls, attachments.media_keys, each only when asked in post.fields) and Get-WithExpansions (referenced_posts expansion with included posts and referenced_posts.author_id users, attachments.media_keys with media.fields; includes object assembled as the stub does), then serve GET /2/users/:id/tweets (timeline: max_results 5..100, plain_status/status fault users, protected -> 200 errors entry, exclude replies/retweets by ref kind, start_time/end_time on created_at's first 19 chars by ordinal compare, newest first by a stable sort, meta.result_count), GET /2/tweets (lookup by ids) and GET /2/tweets/:id. Posts carry author_id from their user, as the stub's postsById copy does. Make every multi-line helper ':~' from the start (the one-path size lesson, SIM-X-SERVE-FOLLOWING n3). Proof: e2e with XSIM_SERVER=sim, 153 rows, and 'answered' lines for the three paths.")
inl note_of_sim_x_serve_render_2 () = Note ("SIM-X-SERVE-RENDER", 2u32, "2026-10-31T14:19Z", "x", "2026-10-31 10:19 x: exact shapes read from stub.ps1 for this slice. Metric names (line 142): bookmark_count, impression_count, like_count, quote_count, reply_count, repost_count (zero when absent). Posts get author_id = their user's id (line 60). includes: posts / users / media, each a map keyed by id in FIRST-insertion order, emitted as its values and only when non-empty; 'includes' only when any is non-empty; data only when non-empty. Lookup (243): found ids rendered with expansions; unknown ids -> errors [{value, detail 'Could not find post with ids: [<id>].', title 'Not Found Error'}] (no resource_type). Single (251): unknown -> 200 {errors: [{value, detail 'Could not find post with id: [<id>].', title 'Not Found Error', resource_type 'post', parameter 'id'}]}; found -> {data: rendered with post.fields, no expansions}. Timeline sort (Sort-Stable, 126): ordinal compare on created_at, descending, ties keep input order.")
inl note_of_sim_x_serve_render_3 () = Note ("SIM-X-SERVE-RENDER", 3u32, "2026-10-31T15:05Z", "x", "2026-10-31 11:05 x: DRAFTED in sim_serve.spi (unstaged): the world indexes posts by id with author_id from their user (with_posts); rendering (base id/escaped text/edit_history_post_ids, then conversation_id, created_at, author_id, public_metrics zero-filled over the 6 names, referenced_posts, entities.urls, attachments.media_keys, each only when asked), expansions (referenced_posts with included posts and referenced_posts.author_id users, attachments.media_keys with media.fields; includes posts/users/media first-insertion-unique; includes and data only when non-empty); GET /2/users/:id/tweets (5..100 check, plain_status/status/protected fault users, exclude replies/retweets by ref kind, start/end_time on created_at's first 19 chars by ordinal compare, newest first by a stable insertion sort, meta.result_count), GET /2/tweets (lookup + errors for unknown ids) and GET /2/tweets/:id (data or the 200 errors entry). Routing is one api_route union (UsersBy, UsersByIds, Following, Timeline, Lookup, Single, Elsewhere); search and writes still forward. The status-line reason table now mirrors stub_http.ps1. Every multi-line helper is ':~'. Local: x-compat 0 in 30 files, kit standards 0. PRE-REGISTERED x-serveprobe5: e2e with XSIM_SERVER=sim exit 0, 'RUST-OK ... 37 tutorial steps and 5 extra cases', 153 requests, and the sim's answered lines include at least one GET /2/users/<id>/tweets, one GET /2/tweets and one GET /2/tweets/<id> (a path the tutorial never calls is reported, not failed, with the tutorial step that would cover it).")
inl note_of_sim_x_serve_render_4 () = Note ("SIM-X-SERVE-RENDER", 4u32, "2026-10-31T15:28Z", "x", "2026-10-31 11:28 x: x-serveprobe5 (job-1793459559419, 29 s) RED at parse, nothing run: (1) 'when' is a Spiral keyword, so the helper named when broke every cell that used it (renamed gated); (2) a list literal split over lines needs ';' at the end of each continued line, like a record type (the status-reason tables). Local x-compat 0, standards 0. Relaunching as x-serveprobe6, n3's rule unchanged.")
inl note_of_sim_x_serve_render_5 () = Note ("SIM-X-SERVE-RENDER", 5u32, "2026-10-31T16:06Z", "x", "2026-10-31 12:06 x: x-serveprobe6 (job-1793460783753, 20 min) by n3's rule: NOT PASSED. Green: sim and client builds; the sim answered every new path (28 x GET /2/users/<id>/tweets, 13 x GET /2/tweets/<id>, 2 x GET /2/tweets, plus 19 users/by, 3 users, 6 following). RED: e2e 6 failures, 123 requests instead of 153, all on 'xc thread 9003', 'xc delete-thread 9003', 'xc thread 9018 did not read back the posted oauth1.spi (0 vs 1276 chars)' and 'delete-thread deleted 0 of 17'. CAUSE: 9003 and 9018 are not in world.json; earlier tutorial steps CREATE them through POST /2/tweets, which is still forwarded, so they exist only in the stub's live world, and the sim answered 'not found' from its static world. FIX (until slice 6 brings writes into the sim): api_world.stale turns true after any forwarded non-GET request, and from then on the sim forwards post reads (timeline, lookup, single post) to the stub; users and following stay answered by the sim, since writes never change them. The serve loop now threads the updated world through each request (replied returns the reply and the next world). Local x-compat 0, standards 0. Relaunching as x-serveprobe7, n3's rule unchanged (the answered counts for post reads will drop to the steps before the first write).")
inl note_of_sim_x_serve_render_6 () = Note ("SIM-X-SERVE-RENDER", 6u32, "2026-10-31T16:37Z", "x", "2026-10-31 12:37 x: x-serveprobe7 (job-1793463270388, 20 min, xqueue3) by n3's rule: PASS. Sim and client builds exit 0; e2e.ps1 with XSIM_SERVER=sim exit 0, 'RUST-OK x/apps/client / e2e: 37 tutorial steps and 5 extra cases', 153 requests (the stub's count). Answered by the sim: 25 x GET /2/users/<id>/tweets, 2 x GET /2/tweets, 2 x GET /2/tweets/<id> (post reads before the first forwarded write; after it they go to the stub, by the stale rule), plus 19 users/by, 3 users, 6 following. DONE (code in x, unstaged).")
inl x_lambda_parens () = Task ("X-LAMBDA-PARENS", "x: drop redundant parens around trailing lambdas in x's Spiral sources, proven per file with spiral/apps/cells/spi/lambda_parens_prove.ps1 (as lib did today)", TaskKind, P4, D2, 70u32, Active, "", "team:x,x,style,spiral", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_x_lambda_parens () = Planning ("X-LAMBDA-PARENS", Minutes 60u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_x_lambda_parens_1 () = Note ("X-LAMBDA-PARENS", 1u32, "2026-10-31T15:36Z", "x", "2026-10-31 x: from main's note (LIB-NOTEBOOK-HEADINGS-CLEANUP and LAMBDA-PARENS finished in lib today). (1) Notebook-era headings: x has 0 '/// #' lines in any apps/*.spi, so drop_name_headings.exs has nothing to do here (checked). (2) Redundant parens around trailing lambdas: about 28 candidate sites in x's apps/*.spi (rough grep for a '(\\ ... => ...)' ending a line). spiral/apps/cells/spi/lambda_parens_prove.ps1 proves per file through lib_gate.ps1 (a compile per file, BREAKS = keep the parens, -Site to bisect), and its -LiveLib defaults to spiral/lib/spiral; x's files belong to the apps/client and apps/sim packages, so the first step is checking that the gate compiles a consumer package (or passing x's package dir). Load medium: one compile per file, run as a single node job over the files with sites.")
inl note_of_x_lambda_parens_2 () = Note ("X-LAMBDA-PARENS", 2u32, "2026-11-04T01:12Z", "x", "2026-11-03 21:11 x: ORPHAN SWEEP (MAIN-ORPHAN-SWEEP, x's corner: open x items with no note in 48 h). Owner x. Next step: run main's lambda-paren cleanup over x/apps/*.spi via the formatter (format --check now flags only drift, and today's lands were canonicalized), and close if 0 remain.")
inl note_of_x_lambda_parens_3 () = Note ("X-LAMBDA-PARENS", 3u32, "2026-11-04T11:53Z", "x", "2026-11-04 07:53 x: EXACT SITE COUNT with the prover's own finder (lambda_parens_prove.ps1's Get-TailSites, loaded via the AST without running the script: a '(\\ ... => ...)' closing at line end, scoped '=> \\*' or type-annotated tails excluded): 56 sites in 22 x files (the 10-31 rough grep said ~28). Top: sim/scheduler 9, sim/world 7, sim/sim_serve 6, client/reading 4, sim/schedule 4, client/wire 3, sim/scenario 3, personas 2, final_state 2, inbox 2. PLAN: the prover edits a copy and proves each file via lib_gate (one compile per file; BREAKS = keep the parens). Run it as ONE node job over the 22 files once the slot queue frees; files in x's queued stack (sim_serve, world, cells_executor, simframes) go LAST, after x-next8 lands, so the prover never races a pinned draft.")
inl note_of_x_lambda_parens_4 () = Note ("X-LAMBDA-PARENS", 4u32, "2026-11-04T12:08Z", "x", "2026-11-04 08:08 x: 41 of 56 sites PROVEN to compile and QUEUED. Drafts (kit drafts x\\X-LAMBDA-PARENS, plan.json with base hashes) for the 19 files outside x's queued stack, made with the prover's OWN Get-TailSites + Remove-Parens (loaded from lambda_parens_prove.ps1 via the AST). Vultr pack34 --check on a fresh stage of the live tree: 19/19 exit 0 (the prover's BREAKS test is a compile, which this is). They ride x-next7 (not launched; launcher + land updated, 23 files, every draft pinned): x-next7's pre-registered rule (sim/client builds 0, sim accept with every fixture BYTE-IDENTICAL, e2e RUST-OK, census SAME) proves the removal is purely syntactic (no codegen change). Remaining 15 sites in sim_serve, world, cells_executor and simframes follow after x-next8 lands.")
inl note_of_x_lambda_parens_5 () = Note ("X-LAMBDA-PARENS", 5u32, "2026-11-04T13:46Z", "x", "2026-11-04 09:46 x: 14 MORE SITES drafted with the prover's own Get-TailSites + Remove-Parens: sim/world 7 (B4CDE32E0EA6 -> 6BC70304CB24, joins x-next7, now 26 files, pinned), x-next9's sim_serve 7 (587AE9BF2462 -> BDE70A884173) and route timeline_serve 1 (C2A6C2C0FFEB -> 82DD8227885E), x-next9 land re-pinned; simframes has 0. All format-clean, 0 cells over 272, vultr --check 0 on the full x-next9 stage. Both runs' rules include byte-identical sim fixtures, which proves the removal is syntactic. Left: cells_executor (frozen in x-next6; after it lands).")
inl sim_x_serve_search () = Task ("SIM-X-SERVE-SEARCH", "x sim: serve recent and full-archive search (7- and 30-day windows from world.now) from the world", TaskKind, P1, D4, 100u32, Done, "", "x,sim,spiral,e2e", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_sim_x_serve_search () = Planning ("SIM-X-SERVE-SEARCH", Minutes 120u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_sim_x_serve_search_1 () = Note ("SIM-X-SERVE-SEARCH", 1u32, "2026-10-31T16:42Z", "x", "2026-10-31 x: slice 4 of SIM-X-FEATURE-PARITY (its n7). Port stub.ps1 Get-Search: GET /2/tweets/search/recent (7-day window) and /2/tweets/search/all (30 days, start_time wins over the window), windows computed from world.now (Get-WindowStart: now minus N days, 'yyyy-MM-ddTHH:mm:ss'), query terms as the stub matches them (read Get-Search first), rendering and expansions reused from slice 3, the same stale rule (forward once a write was forwarded, since searches see posted threads). Every multi-line helper ':~'. Proof: e2e with XSIM_SERVER=sim, 153 rows, and answered lines for both search paths.")
inl note_of_sim_x_serve_search_2 () = Note ("SIM-X-SERVE-SEARCH", 2u32, "2026-10-31T16:44Z", "x", "2026-10-31 12:44 x: exact semantics read from stub.ps1 Get-Search (line ~258). oldest = world.now minus Days ('yyyy-MM-ddTHH:mm:ss'; 7 recent, 30 all); since = first 19 chars of start_time; if since is set and (search/all, or since > oldest by ordinal compare) then oldest = since. query: split on whitespace, tokens with ':' become key:value (last wins); from:<handle> resolves to that user's id via the lowercase handle (else the raw text); conversation_id:<id> is REQUIRED (none -> nothing matches). found = every post (users in world order, then their posts) with conversation_id (default its id) equal, author matching when from is given, created_at[0..19] >= oldest by ordinal compare; sorted descending by the key (id length zero-padded to 10, '|', id), stable. max_results default 10 (no range check here), next_token default 0 is a plain index; page = slice. Body: data = page rendered with post.fields only (NO expansions) when non-empty; meta {result_count, newest_id + oldest_id when the page is non-empty, next_token = string(start + size) while more remain}.")
inl note_of_sim_x_serve_search_3 () = Note ("SIM-X-SERVE-SEARCH", 3u32, "2026-10-31T16:59Z", "x", "2026-10-31 12:59 x: DRAFTED in sim_serve.spi (unstaged): GET /2/tweets/search/recent and /search/all answered by the sim (routes SearchRecent/SearchAll, both covered by the stale rule like every post read). Window from world.now through OTP calendar (rfc3339 to system seconds, minus 7 or 30 days, back to rfc3339 with offset Z, first 19 chars); start_time replaces it for search/all, or for recent when later; query terms whitespace-split, key:value, last wins; from: resolves a handle to its id; no conversation_id -> empty result; matching posts sorted descending by (zero-padded id length, '|', id) with a stable insertion sort; next_token as a plain index; data rendered with post.fields only; meta result_count, newest_id/oldest_id on a non-empty page, next_token while more remain. Local x-compat 0 in 30 files, kit standards 0. PRE-REGISTERED x-serveprobe8: e2e with XSIM_SERVER=sim exit 0, 'RUST-OK ... 37 tutorial steps and 5 extra cases', 153 requests, and at least one answered GET /2/tweets/search/(recent|all). If every search in the tutorial comes after the first write, the count is 0 by the stale rule: then NOT PASSED as a proof (e2e still recorded), and the next step is a direct sim-only check that searches a world thread before any write.")
inl note_of_sim_x_serve_search_4 () = Note ("SIM-X-SERVE-SEARCH", 4u32, "2026-10-31T17:09Z", "x", "2026-10-31 13:09 x: x-serveprobe8 (job-1793466266839, 26 s) RED at compile, nothing run: sm'::length is numeric-polymorphic, so feeding it straight to obj_to_string left an unbound metavar (ETY-ValueRestriction; annotated ': i32'), and listm::last does not exist (replaced by a fold from the first element). Local x-compat 0, standards 0. Relaunching as x-serveprobe9, n3's rule unchanged.")
inl note_of_sim_x_serve_search_5 () = Note ("SIM-X-SERVE-SEARCH", 5u32, "2026-10-31T17:55Z", "x", "2026-10-31 13:55 x: x-serveprobe9 (job-1793466918057, 33 min, node shared with verify42) VOID, not a verdict on search: builds exit 0, but e2e died before the tutorial (0 request rows, no port file; the launcher's line filter dropped e2e's own error). A direct 'sim-run.ps1 serve' on the same mirror started and wrote its port, so the cause is start time: e2e.ps1 gave the X server 60 s, and the sim server (mix check of the Cells build, VM start, world load, child stub) takes longer on a loaded node. FIX: e2e.ps1 waits 300 s for the server when XSIM_SERVER=sim (60 s for the stub, unchanged); the probe launcher now also prints e2e's failure lines. Since slices 5 and 6 are now drafted on top, the next probe covers 4-6 together (x-serveprobe10, rule on SIM-X-SERVE-WRITES n3); a red is bisected by slice.")
inl note_of_sim_x_serve_search_6 () = Note ("SIM-X-SERVE-SEARCH", 6u32, "2026-10-31T21:06Z", "x", "2026-10-31 17:06 x: x-serveprobe14 (job-1793480014001, 9 min, core D1C49B8BDC3318F7) by SIM-X-SERVE-WRITES n3's combined rule: PASS. e2e.ps1 with XSIM_SERVER=sim exit 0, 'RUST-OK x/apps/client / e2e: 37 tutorial steps and 5 extra cases', 153 requests. Answered by the sim: 13 x GET /2/tweets/search/all, 3 x POST /2/oauth2/token, 37 x POST /2/tweets, 29 x DELETE /2/tweets/<id>, 13 x GET /2/tweets/<id> (the post reads after writes), plus 28 timeline, 2 lookup, 6 following, 19 users/by, 3 users: 153 in all, i.e. the sim answered EVERY request of the tutorial and the stub served none. Slice 4''s clause (search answered): met. DONE.")
inl cells_rust_test_batch () = Task ("CELLS-RUST-TEST-BATCH", "Cells: a file's Rust test targets build as one crate (or reuse a warm crate) instead of one rustc build per test, the way Gleam cells reuse a warm package", Story, P2, D4, 25u32, Planned, "", "cells,rust,performance,team:main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_cells_rust_test_batch () = Planning ("CELLS-RUST-TEST-BATCH", Minutes 240u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_cells_rust_test_batch_1 () = Note ("CELLS-RUST-TEST-BATCH", 1u32, "2026-10-31T17:07Z", "main", "10-31 13:07 main. EVIDENCE (FSHARP-OUTSIDE-COMPILER's node measurement, 10-31, 1,025 gate rows, no memo): of 133 tests with both an F# and a passing Rust/Zig target, 14 are more than 1.5x slower natively, and the worst are compile-bound: process split_args F# 36,819 ms vs Rust 118,138 ms (3.21x), split_command 33,826 vs 103,277 (3.05x), convert try_convert_2 2.69x, csv x2 ~2.4x, sm' listed_compares_trimmed_parts 2.34x. Gate wall time includes each target's compile, so per-test rustc builds dominate. Overall the Rust/Zig totals are still lower (1,780 s vs F# 3,488 s on the same 133 tests), so batching would widen that and let the 14 retire their F# targets too. NEXT: read how Cells builds a Rust cell (targets.ex) and whether a per-file crate with one fn per test, or a warm crate whose src is replaced like the Gleam path (CELLS-GLEAM-WARM-STALE), fits; measure on process.spi first.")
inl note_of_cells_rust_test_batch_2 () = Note ("CELLS-RUST-TEST-BATCH", 2u32, "2026-10-31T17:09Z", "main", "10-31 13:09 main, read of the code path: a Rust TEST target (any '///> rust' cell, with or without -d crates) does not call rustc directly. targets.ex:79 dispatches it to the spiral runner (run_spiral ['rust' | args]) inside one_spiral_rust (targets.ex:135), which takes a BOX-WIDE file lock, cells-spiral-rust.lock beside the Cells mounts root, so every Rust target build on the machine runs one at a time across all lanes; the wait extends the cell's deadline and is inside the gate's measured wall Ms. Only host Rust cells (cells.ex:388/:445) use rustc on one .rs without the lock. So the 14 'slow Rust' tests are likely queueing on the lock (a file's own Rust cells run in parallel and serialize), not just compiling. Batching a file's Rust tests into one build cuts lock acquisitions from one per test to one per file. FIRST CHECK: does the gate's per-phase timing (add_phase: slot_wait_ms, rustc_ms, target_ms) separate lock wait from build time? If not, add a lock_wait_ms phase, then re-measure process.spi alone to split wait vs compile before designing the batch.")
inl note_of_cells_rust_test_batch_3 () = Note ("CELLS-RUST-TEST-BATCH", 3u32, "2026-10-31T17:37Z", "main", "10-31 13:37 main: FIRST CHECK answered: the lock wait is NOT a phase today (one_spiral_rust only extends the deadline by it; the phases map has slot_wait_ms, rustc_ms, compile_ms, ... but nothing for the box-wide Rust lock). DRAFTED apply11 (session scratchpad apply11-rust-lock-phase.ps1): one_spiral_rust adds the wait to :rust_lock_wait_ms in :spiral_cells_phases, plus a targets_test case that holds the lock 500 ms and asserts rust_lock_wait_ms >= 400 (beside the existing 'waits for the spiral rust lock' test). Scratch proof (fresh Cells copy, whole targets_test.exs) running. Lands as a small Cells window after it passes; then re-measure process.spi to split wait vs build.")
inl note_of_cells_rust_test_batch_4 () = Note ("CELLS-RUST-TEST-BATCH", 4u32, "2026-10-31T18:13Z", "main", "10-31 14:13 main: rust_lock_wait_ms phase LANDED in live Cells (targets.ex E74FF5BBA52F, targets_test.exs 56CD62270A29; undo spiral-bin\\lib-land-undo\\cells-rust-lock-phase-20261031-141037). Close: both rust-lock tests pass live. Scratch proof of the whole targets_test.exs: 22/24, the 2 failures not caused by the change (Gleam 'runs from the notebook's directory' fails on unmodified live too: A/A; Delphi 'output is stdout' passes alone on scratch: load red). NEXT: re-measure process.spi with the phase to split lock wait from build time.")
inl note_of_cells_rust_test_batch_5 () = Note ("CELLS-RUST-TEST-BATCH", 5u32, "2026-10-31T22:58Z", "main", "10-31 18:58 main, pre-night groom: NODE-RUNNABLE NEXT STEP (for NODE-ALWAYS-FED): the node mirror (main-manifest, synced 17:4x) carries the rust_lock_wait_ms phase; run lib_gate -File process.spi -RawOut there twice (an A/A), read rust_lock_wait_ms vs rustc/build phases per Rust cell, and decide whether the 14 'slow Rust' tests are lock-bound or compile-bound before designing the batch.")
inl note_of_cells_rust_test_batch_6 () = Note ("CELLS-RUST-TEST-BATCH", 6u32, "2026-11-02T23:55Z", "main", "main 11-02 19:5x orphan sweep round 4: Active with no note for over 48 h and no lane driving it this week, so Active -> Planned at 25% (an Active status that nobody touches lies to agile wip). The next step stays the one in the latest note; it is back in agile next at its priority.")
inl sim_x_serve_auth () = Task ("SIM-X-SERVE-AUTH", "x sim: OAuth 1.0a signature check, client Basic, token refresh rotation and the user-context-only 403 in the sim server", TaskKind, P1, D5, 100u32, Done, "SIM-X-SERVE-SEARCH", "x,sim,spiral,e2e,auth", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_sim_x_serve_auth () = Planning ("SIM-X-SERVE-AUTH", Minutes 150u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_sim_x_serve_auth_1 () = Note ("SIM-X-SERVE-AUTH", 1u32, "2026-10-31T17:18Z", "x", "2026-10-31 x: slice 5 of SIM-X-FEATURE-PARITY (its n7), semantics read from stub.ps1 (lines 69-100, 303-318, 340-347). (a) OAuth 1.0a (Test-OAuth1): parse 'OAuth k=\"v\", ...' (unescape keys and values), require oauth_consumer_key and oauth_token equal to world.oauth1; params = every field but oauth_signature; signature = base64(HMAC-SHA1(key = pct(consumer_secret) '&' pct(token_secret), base = METHOD '&' pct(url) '&' pct(join('&', sorted 'k=v' with k and v pct-encoded, ordinal sort on k then v)))), url = 'http://' + Host + path; pct = RFC 3986 unreserved-only (EscapeDataString) -> uri_string:quote(Bin, \"\") on OTP 25+; HMAC via crypto:mac(hmac, sha, Key, Data), base64:encode. Startup self-test on the published example (expected 'Ls93hJiZbQ3akF3HF3x1Bz8/zU4=') like the stub. (b) client Basic = 'Basic ' + base64(client_id ':' client_secret) of world.oauth2, valid only on /2/oauth2/token. (c) token refresh (Get-Renewed, STATEFUL): form grant_type=refresh_token and refresh_token equal to the LAST issued one (starts at world.oauth2.refresh_token); else 400 {error invalid_request, error_description 'Value passed for the token was invalid.'}; on success issue refresh_token-N and access_token-N (N = number issued so far), body {token_type bearer, expires_in 7200, access_token, scope 'tweet.read tweet.write users.read offline.access', refresh_token}. The world record carries the issued list, threaded through the serve loop like stale. (d) app context on a non-GET (non-token) path -> 403 Unsupported Authentication with the stub's exact detail. Scope for this slice: answer OAuth1-signed reads and the token endpoint in the sim; writes stay forwarded until slice 6 (so the stale rule stays). Every multi-line helper ':~'. Proof: e2e with XSIM_SERVER=sim, 153 rows, answered lines for POST /2/oauth2/token and for at least one oauth1-context read.")
inl note_of_sim_x_serve_auth_2 () = Note ("SIM-X-SERVE-AUTH", 2u32, "2026-10-31T17:19Z", "x", "2026-10-31 13:19 x: primitives CHECKED on the box (erl, OTP 29): uri_string:quote(Bin, \"\") for RFC 3986 encoding, lists:sort on {K, V} binary tuples (ordinal on K then V, as the stub's Comparison), crypto:mac(hmac, sha, Key, Base) and base64:encode reproduce the published example signature 'Ls93hJiZbQ3akF3HF3x1Bz8/zU4=' exactly (true). So the Spiral port is plumbing over these four host calls. Drafting starts once SIM-X-SERVE-SEARCH's probe (x-serveprobe9) passes, so unproven slices do not stack.")
inl note_of_sim_x_serve_auth_3 () = Note ("SIM-X-SERVE-AUTH", 3u32, "2026-10-31T17:33Z", "x", "2026-10-31 13:33 x: DRAFTED in sim_serve.spi (unstaged), launch held until SIM-X-SERVE-SEARCH's x-serveprobe9 gives its verdict. auth_kind now classifies app / user (bearer), client (Basic = base64(client_id:client_secret) of world.oauth2, only on /2/oauth2/token) and oauth1 (header parsed and unescaped, consumer key + token must equal world.oauth1, HMAC-SHA1 over the RFC 3986-encoded, lists:sort-ed params with base METHOD&pct(http://Host+path)&pct(params), key pct(consumer_secret)&pct(token_secret)); nothing is forwarded for its auth any more (unknown auth -> the stub's 401). POST /2/oauth2/token is answered by the sim with the stub's branches (no auth 401, client -> refresh rotation, app 403 Unsupported Authentication with the exact detail, user/oauth1 404 'no route'); the issued refresh tokens (newest first, starting with world.oauth2.refresh_token) live in the world record and thread through the serve loop; a refresh needs grant_type=refresh_token and the LAST issued token, and issues refresh_token-N / access_token-N with N = tokens issued so far. POST log rows carry the raw body like the stub's. Startup self-test: the published OAuth example must give 'Ls93hJiZbQ3akF3HF3x1Bz8/zU4=' or serve exits 3. Writes other than the token endpoint stay forwarded (stale rule unchanged). Local x-compat 0 in 30 files, kit standards 0. PRE-REGISTERED x-serveprobe10 (after slice 4's verdict): e2e with XSIM_SERVER=sim exit 0, 'RUST-OK ... 37 tutorial steps and 5 extra cases', 153 requests, at least one 'answered POST /2/oauth2/token', and every answered family of slices 1-4 still present.")
inl note_of_sim_x_serve_auth_4 () = Note ("SIM-X-SERVE-AUTH", 4u32, "2026-10-31T21:07Z", "x", "2026-10-31 17:06 x: x-serveprobe14 (job-1793480014001, 9 min, core D1C49B8BDC3318F7) by SIM-X-SERVE-WRITES n3's combined rule: PASS. e2e.ps1 with XSIM_SERVER=sim exit 0, 'RUST-OK x/apps/client / e2e: 37 tutorial steps and 5 extra cases', 153 requests. Answered by the sim: 13 x GET /2/tweets/search/all, 3 x POST /2/oauth2/token, 37 x POST /2/tweets, 29 x DELETE /2/tweets/<id>, 13 x GET /2/tweets/<id> (the post reads after writes), plus 28 timeline, 2 lookup, 6 following, 19 users/by, 3 users: 153 in all, i.e. the sim answered EVERY request of the tutorial and the stub served none. Slice 5''s clause (token endpoint answered, refresh rotation): met; OAuth 1.0a-signed requests were answered by the sim too (no request went to the stub). DONE.")
inl sim_x_serve_writes () = Task ("SIM-X-SERVE-WRITES", "x sim: serve post creation and deletion on a live world (ids from 9001, X weight check, reply conversations), retiring the stale rule", TaskKind, P1, D5, 100u32, Done, "SIM-X-SERVE-AUTH", "x,sim,spiral,e2e", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_sim_x_serve_writes () = Planning ("SIM-X-SERVE-WRITES", Minutes 150u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_sim_x_serve_writes_1 () = Note ("SIM-X-SERVE-WRITES", 1u32, "2026-10-31T17:35Z", "x", "2026-10-31 x: slice 6 of SIM-X-FEATURE-PARITY (its n7), semantics read from stub.ps1 New-Post / Remove-Post (lines 317-338). POST /2/tweets: body JSON {text, reply.in_reply_to_tweet_id}; text trimmed at the END only; empty or X weight > 280 -> 400 'Invalid Request' / 'text must be 1 to 280 characters' (weight from the client's glyphs module, the same weighting xc splits with; sim_serve may open it since the sim package includes the client sources); unknown parent -> 400 'in_reply_to_tweet_id <p> does not exist'; ids from 9001 upward; created set records it; conversation_id = the parent's conversation_id (else the parent id) or its own id; refs [{type replied_to, id parent}] when replying; created_at = world.now + (size of the created set AFTER adding) seconds as 'yyyy-MM-ddTHH:mm:ss.000Z'; author_id = world.me; 201 {data {id, text HTML-escaped, edit_history_post_ids [id]}}. DELETE /2/tweets/:id: only ids in the created set, else 404 'Not Found Error' / 'Could not find post with id: [<id>].'; removes it from created and posts; 200 {data {deleted: true}}. Context (stub dispatch order): no auth 401; POST with client -> refresh (slice 5); any other non-GET without user/oauth1 context -> 403 Unsupported Authentication; then POST /2/tweets, DELETE /2/tweets/:id, else 404 'no route'. With writes in the sim the stale rule and the forwarding of writes both go (slice 7 then drops forwarding entirely). Every multi-line helper ':~'. Proof: e2e with XSIM_SERVER=sim, 153 rows, answered POST /2/tweets and DELETE /2/tweets/<id>, and post reads answered after the first write (the stale rule gone).")
inl note_of_sim_x_serve_writes_2 () = Note ("SIM-X-SERVE-WRITES", 2u32, "2026-10-31T17:46Z", "x", "2026-10-31 13:46 x: DRAFTED in sim_serve.spi (unstaged), launch held behind slices 4 and 5. The world record now groups write state (write_state {me, created set, next_id from 9001}) and uses an index alias for its dicts (the type cell was over the post weight). POST /2/tweets: JSON body, text end-trimmed, empty or splitter::x_weight > 280 (xc's own weighting) -> 400 'text must be 1 to 280 characters'; unknown parent -> 400 'in_reply_to_tweet_id <p> does not exist'; new id = next_id; created_at = now + created-set size seconds as yyyy-MM-ddTHH:mm:ss.000Z; conversation_id from the parent (else its own id); refs [replied_to parent]; author_id world.me; 201 with escaped text and edit_history_post_ids [id]. DELETE /2/tweets/:id: only created ids, else 404 'Could not find post with id: [<id>].'; 200 {data {deleted true}}. One change dispatcher in the stub's order (401 no auth; client POST -> refresh; non-user/oauth1 -> 403; create; delete; 404 'no route'); token_reply is folded into it. served_here now answers EVERY non-GET request in the sim, so no write is forwarded and the stale rule never fires (post reads after writes are the sim's own). Local x-compat 0 in 30 files, standards 0. The tree now stacks slices 4, 5 and 6, all unproven: probes run in order (4, then 5, then 6), and a red is bisected by slice. PRE-REGISTERED (for its turn): e2e with XSIM_SERVER=sim exit 0, 37+5 steps, 153 requests, answered POST /2/tweets and DELETE /2/tweets/<id>, and post reads answered after the first write.")
inl note_of_sim_x_serve_writes_3 () = Note ("SIM-X-SERVE-WRITES", 3u32, "2026-10-31T17:56Z", "x", "2026-10-31 13:56 x: PRE-REGISTERED x-serveprobe10, covering slices 4 (search), 5 (auth) and 6 (writes) together: e2e with XSIM_SERVER=sim exit 0, 'RUST-OK x/apps/client / e2e: 37 tutorial steps and 5 extra cases', 153 requests; answered by the sim: at least one GET /2/tweets/search/(recent|all) [slice 4], at least one POST /2/oauth2/token [slice 5], at least one POST /2/tweets and one DELETE /2/tweets/<id> [slice 6]; and post reads after writes now answered by the sim, shown by GET /2/tweets/<id> answered at least 13 times (probe 6's count when the sim answered every single-post read, against 2 under the stale rule in probe 7). Each slice item is closed only by its own clauses.")
inl note_of_sim_x_serve_writes_4 () = Note ("SIM-X-SERVE-WRITES", 4u32, "2026-10-31T18:06Z", "x", "2026-10-31 14:06 x: x-serveprobe10 (job-1793469660520, 51 s) RED at compile, nothing run: (1) field shorthand ('writes;') is accepted in record LITERALS but not in record UPDATES ({ w ?| ... }): ETY-TermError at the two update sites, now 'writes = writes'; (2) gated was defined after its first use in log_row (top-down resolution), moved up. Local x-compat 0, standards 0. Relaunching as x-serveprobe11 with n3's rule unchanged.")
inl note_of_sim_x_serve_writes_5 () = Note ("SIM-X-SERVE-WRITES", 5u32, "2026-10-31T18:17Z", "x", "2026-10-31 14:17 x: x-serveprobe11 (job-1793470275422, 38 s) RED at compile, nothing run: ETY-TermError on assigning a nested record update ({ b::writes ?| me = ... }) to the write_state field; nested updates of an alias-typed record field do not unify here. FIX: every write_state value is built by writes_of (me) (created) (next_id) : write_state, a literal with a declared type; the 3 nested updates (world_auth, counted, dropped) are gone. Local x-compat 0, standards 0. Relaunching as x-serveprobe12, n3's rule unchanged.")
inl note_of_sim_x_serve_writes_6 () = Note ("SIM-X-SERVE-WRITES", 6u32, "2026-10-31T19:03Z", "x", "2026-10-31 15:03 x: x-serveprobe12 (job-1793470966961, 26 min) RED at server start, e2e not run: builds exit 0 (slices 4-6 compile), but sim-run serve crashed during boot ('the X API stub exited before writing its port (exit code 1)'); a direct run on the mirror gave the cause: badarg in uri_string:quote, whose Safe argument must be a CHARLIST, while Spiral's str sends a binary (the OAuth self-test runs at startup). Fixed with erlang_term::charlist. The same audit found string:pad_leading does not exist (search's id order would have crashed): now string:pad(digits, 10, leading, 48) + iolist_to_binary. Both checked on the box (erl): quote gives a%20b%2Bc%2F~, pad gives 0000000004, and the rfc3339 window math gives 2026-10-11T12:00:00Z for now minus 7 days. Local x-compat 0, standards 0. Relaunching as x-serveprobe13, n3's rule unchanged.")
inl note_of_sim_x_serve_writes_7 () = Note ("SIM-X-SERVE-WRITES", 7u32, "2026-10-31T20:00Z", "x", "2026-10-31 16:00 x: x-serveprobe13's launch failed 8 times (two launcher runs) AFTER clean syncs: the node-proof core guard refused 'node core D1C49B8BDC3318F7 does not match required 17DDB2DCEF2A2D52'. A new compiler pack was installed at 14:30 on BOTH box and node (core D1C49B8BDC3318F7, source 5b4655ec4bf5990b, build-identity built 18:30Z); verify42 started earlier on the old core and is unaffected. Relaunching with -Core D1C49B8BDC3318F7; n3's rule unchanged, and this run is also the first proof of slices 1-6 on the new pack (local x-compat runs since 14:30 already used it, 0 findings).")
inl note_of_sim_x_serve_writes_8 () = Note ("SIM-X-SERVE-WRITES", 8u32, "2026-10-31T20:46Z", "x", "2026-10-31 16:46 x: x-serveprobe13 (job-1793476993862, 25 min, new core D1C49B8BDC3318F7) by n3's rule: NOT PASSED, but every family is now answered by the sim: 37 x POST /2/tweets, 3 x DELETE /2/tweets/<id>, 3 x POST /2/oauth2/token, 9 x GET /2/tweets/search/all, 13 x GET /2/tweets/<id>, 2 x GET /2/tweets, 28 timeline, 6 following, 19 users/by, 3 users (so slices 4 and 5 serve; slice 6 writes are accepted). RED: e2e 6 failures, 123 requests: 'xc thread 9003' (and delete-thread 9003, the 9018 read-back, delete-thread 0 of 17) exit 4 right after GET /2/tweets/9003. CAUSE, found by replaying the run's logged writes against a fresh sim on the node: a post the sim CREATED comes back with every field empty ({id:\"\", text:\"\", author_id:\"\", ...}), because created posts were built with map_of, whose keys are ATOMS (fine for responses, which json:encode writes the same way), while every lookup (field) uses BINARY keys like the JSON-loaded posts. FIX: stored_map (binary keys) for created posts and their replied_to refs. Local x-compat 0, standards 0. Relaunching as x-serveprobe14, n3's rule unchanged.")
inl note_of_sim_x_serve_writes_9 () = Note ("SIM-X-SERVE-WRITES", 9u32, "2026-10-31T21:09Z", "x", "2026-10-31 17:06 x: x-serveprobe14 (job-1793480014001, 9 min, core D1C49B8BDC3318F7) by SIM-X-SERVE-WRITES n3's combined rule: PASS. e2e.ps1 with XSIM_SERVER=sim exit 0, 'RUST-OK x/apps/client / e2e: 37 tutorial steps and 5 extra cases', 153 requests. Answered by the sim: 13 x GET /2/tweets/search/all, 3 x POST /2/oauth2/token, 37 x POST /2/tweets, 29 x DELETE /2/tweets/<id>, 13 x GET /2/tweets/<id> (the post reads after writes), plus 28 timeline, 2 lookup, 6 following, 19 users/by, 3 users: 153 in all, i.e. the sim answered EVERY request of the tutorial and the stub served none. Slice 6''s clauses (creates, deletes, >= 13 single-post reads after writes): met. DONE.")
inl sim_x_serve_standalone () = Task ("SIM-X-SERVE-STANDALONE", "x sim: drop the stub forwarding and the stale rule (the sim serves every family), keep stub.ps1 as an oracle in a request-log parity check", TaskKind, P1, D4, 100u32, Done, "SIM-X-SERVE-WRITES", "x,sim,spiral,e2e", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_sim_x_serve_standalone () = Planning ("SIM-X-SERVE-STANDALONE", Minutes 120u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_sim_x_serve_standalone_1 () = Note ("SIM-X-SERVE-STANDALONE", 1u32, "2026-10-31T18:25Z", "x", "2026-10-31 x: slice 7 of SIM-X-FEATURE-PARITY (its n7). Once slices 4-6 pass, the sim answers every family stub.ps1 serves, so: (1) remove the forwarding (sim_serve's forwarded/relayed/drained, the stub port argument, sim-run.ps1's child stub.ps1) and the stale rule (api_world.stale, after_forward, post_route), with any unknown route answered by the stub's 404 problem 'no route <path>' as the stub does; (2) keep stub.ps1 as the ORACLE, not a fallback: a parity check (a scripts/ step or an e2e option) runs the tutorial once against each server and compares the two request logs row for row (method, path, query, authorized, context, body) plus each step's client output; (3) decide with the user-facing docs whether XSIM_SERVER=sim becomes the default e2e server. Proof: e2e with XSIM_SERVER=sim passes with no stub process started, and the parity check shows 0 differing rows. Every multi-line helper ':~'.")
inl note_of_sim_x_serve_standalone_2 () = Note ("SIM-X-SERVE-STANDALONE", 2u32, "2026-10-31T18:30Z", "x", "2026-10-31 14:30 x: the oracle check is WRITTEN (unstaged): x/scripts/e2e-parity.ps1 -Exe <xc.exe> [-WorkDir] runs e2e.ps1 twice (stub, then XSIM_SERVER=sim), compares the two requests.jsonl logs row for row on method, path, query, authorized, context and body, prints up to 10 differing rows, and exits 1 on any difference or a failed e2e run. Parse-checked (0 errors). First real use: right after slices 4-6 pass, as one node job (it needs a built xc.exe, so it rides on a client build). Slice 7 then removes the forwarding and keeps this check as the stub's oracle role.")
inl note_of_sim_x_serve_standalone_3 () = Note ("SIM-X-SERVE-STANDALONE", 3u32, "2026-10-31T21:12Z", "x", "2026-10-31 17:12 x: PRE-REGISTERED x-parityprobe (scratchpad launcher, xqueue3, core D1C49B8BDC3318F7): sim and client builds exit 0, then scripts/e2e-parity.ps1 runs the tutorial against stub.ps1 and against the sim. PASS = both e2e runs exit 0 and the two request logs have 0 differing rows on method, path, query, authorized, context and body. Rows that differ are listed (first 10) and each becomes a fix in sim_serve before slice 7 removes the forwarding.")
inl note_of_sim_x_serve_standalone_4 () = Note ("SIM-X-SERVE-STANDALONE", 4u32, "2026-10-31T21:36Z", "x", "2026-10-31 17:36 x: x-parityprobe (job-1793481488711, 15 min, core D1C49B8BDC3318F7) by n3's rule: PASS. Sim and client builds exit 0; scripts/e2e-parity.ps1: 'exits stub 0 sim 0; rows stub 153 sim 153', '0 differing row(s)' on method, path, query, authorized, context and body. So the sim's request log equals the stub's row for row over the whole 37-step tutorial (and in x-serveprobe14 the sim answered all 153 itself). LEFT for this item: the removal step (forwarding, stub port argument, sim-run's child stub, the stale rule; unknown routes get the stub's 404 'no route'), then the same parity check again with no stub process behind the sim.")
inl note_of_sim_x_serve_standalone_5 () = Note ("SIM-X-SERVE-STANDALONE", 5u32, "2026-10-31T22:30Z", "x", "2026-10-31 18:3x x: slice 7 code done, together with the typed-caller slice (TYPEFEATURESET-ENFORCE n6), so one node proof covers both. sim_serve.spi no longer forwards. Gone: relayed, forwarded, drained, after_forward, relay_here, served_here, post_route, the stale world field, the stub_port argument through replied/handled/served/listening/started/serve, and the dead token_reply. An unknown route now gets the sim's own 404 'no route PATH' (routed's Elsewhere arm). scripts/sim-run.ps1 no longer starts stub.ps1 for serve; it checks the 3 arguments and runs erl. README paragraph rewritten. e2e-parity stays the oracle. Pass rule, registered before the run: node job x-parity-typed1 (x-node-e2e -Mode parity) gives both e2e exits 0, stub rows = sim rows = 153, 0 differing rows, and the sim build passes gleam-modules' 1,000-line part limit.")
inl note_of_sim_x_serve_standalone_6 () = Note ("SIM-X-SERVE-STANDALONE", 6u32, "2026-10-31T23:19Z", "x", "2026-10-31 19:19 x: x-parity-typed2 (job-1793486075070, 44 min, xqueue3, core D1C49B8BDC3318F7) by n5's rule: PASS. Sim build exit 0, so the parts stay under gleam-modules' 1,000-line limit with five forall instantiations each of caller_reply, answer and change_reply. Client build exit 0. e2e-parity: 'exits stub 0 sim 0; rows stub 153 sim 153', '0 differing row(s)'. The sim now serves the whole tutorial with no relay and no stub child. Done. Unstaged files: x/apps/sim/spi/sim_serve.spi, x/scripts/sim-run.ps1, x/apps/sim/spi/README.md.")
inl kit_todo_scan_headroom () = Task ("KIT-TODO-SCAN-HEADROOM", "agile_todo_scan's generated crate sits at exactly 1000/1000 lines after 10-31's edits: split the per-file scan into its own crate before any further change", TaskKind, P1, D3, 0u32, Planned, "EOIE-HAND-CRATE-UNITS", "kit,crate-gate,headroom,agile,team:kit", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_kit_todo_scan_headroom () = Planning ("KIT-TODO-SCAN-HEADROOM", Minutes 120u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_kit_todo_scan_headroom_1 () = Note ("KIT-TODO-SCAN-HEADROOM", 1u32, "2026-10-31T19:02Z", "kit", "10-31 14:5x kit: measured on the node (regen-sizes.ps1, read-only): after today's two edits to agile_todo_scan (the Done-path id prefilter and the string respell), todo_scan.rs is EXACTLY 1000/1000 lines. The gate fails only above the limit, so it still passes, with zero headroom: any further change, including the deferred concat5 and pipe_member respells in agile_todo_model, which is inlined here, breaks the next train. Split first: the largest generated functions are todos_entry_0 (174), scanned_file_8 (165), path_verdict_of_code_9 (96), standings_from_14 (81), listed_files_15 (68). Candidate unit: the per-file scan (scanned_file, read_and_scanned, standings_from) into its own crate behind one export, the same hand-modeled split as kit_lint_opens. Acceptance: agile_todo_scan under 850, the new crate under 1000, the todos and roots_cli notebooks green.")
inl note_of_kit_todo_scan_headroom_2 () = Note ("KIT-TODO-SCAN-HEADROOM", 2u32, "2026-11-05T02:20Z", "kit", "11-04 22:2x kit groom: one instance of the systemic pattern now filed as KIT-AGILE-CRATE-HEADROOM (agile models inline into every agile_* crate). Do this split as part of that item's shared linked boundary, not separately.")
inl note_of_kit_todo_scan_headroom_3 () = Note ("KIT-TODO-SCAN-HEADROOM", 3u32, "2026-11-05T02:20Z", "resilience", "11-04 star-exchange ASK (resilience->kit): the agile_* generated-crate line-gate is now SYSTEMIC, not just agile_todo_scan. TRAIN22 died on agile_claim_lease/lease.rs=1059, TRAIN25 on agile_task_listing/task_listing.rs=1015 - more agile_* crates over 1000, hand-split one at a time (whack-a-mole) across TRAIN21-26 (all red/stopped today). ASK: a systemic codegen split-at-link-boundary for the agile_* generated crates so none exceeds 1000, not per-offender splits.")
inl kit_self_cost_metric () = Task ("KIT-SELF-COST-METRIC", "measure what share of lane notes and job hours goes into kit itself versus the projects it serves, weekly, so kit's cost is a number", TaskKind, P2, D2, 40u32, Active, "", "kit,metrics,direction,btw,team:kit", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_kit_self_cost_metric () = Planning ("KIT-SELF-COST-METRIC", Minutes 90u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_kit_self_cost_metric_1 () = Note ("KIT-SELF-COST-METRIC", 1u32, "2026-10-31T19:02Z", "kit", "10-31 14:5x kit, from the user's /btw question (\"is kit paying off vs using claude code's native abilities like using python for everything\"). The fork's verdict: the coordination core pays off (persistent jobs with resume, windows/slots/train holds, compare-and-swap backlog writes, machine health), while much of a kit day goes into kit itself: 5 h trains with a 3.4 h serial regen, crate splits, census rebaselines, hook races, a 2.5 min Done path. One-line fixes take half a day to ship. The lesson is to MEASURE that instead of arguing it. Shape: a weekly census over backlog notes. Share of notes and of job wall-time on kit-tagged items vs the projects kit serves, per team, from agile list --json (note authors and times) and kit job list (label, elapsed). Trend it in a row per week. Acceptance: one call prints this week's shares; a share for kit above the agreed threshold triggers a slim-kit review (KIT-VERBS-SCOPE).")
inl note_of_kit_self_cost_metric_2 () = Note ("KIT-SELF-COST-METRIC", 2u32, "2026-11-01T13:19Z", "kit", "11-01 09:1x kit groom: placed in cluster (3) under KIT-AGILE-CADENCE. The share of lane notes spent on kit's own items is a cadence column computed from the same typed rows (note author x item tags per day, agile_cadence's round_counters_of generalised to tag groups), and job hours per lane come from KIT-EXEC-METRICS' job samples. No separate scanner. DoD: the cadence report shows, per lane per day, notes on kit items / all notes and kit job hours / all job hours, equal to a hand count on a fixture.")
inl note_of_kit_self_cost_metric_3 () = Note ("KIT-SELF-COST-METRIC", 3u32, "2026-11-04T21:46Z", "kit", "11-04 23:5x kit: FIRST MEASUREMENT (week 10-29..11-04, scratch analysis, not yet a kit verb). NOTES (state/agile.spi Note rows joined to item team tags): 6714 notes; 1134 on team:kit items = 16.9 %. Per team tag: main 1475, kit 1134, fiscal 1046, compiler 983, x 576, tibia 479, resilience 244, x-dumps 127. Other lanes spend little on kit items: main 8 % of its 1577 notes, x 5 %, fiscal 3 %, compiler 1 %, tibia 8 %; kit spends 77 % of its own 1039 on kit. JOB HOURS (box %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\jobs, machine-wide, settled jobs since 10-29, by label prefix): 254.7 h total; kit 112.6 h = 44.2 % (247 jobs: trains, size runs, notebooks), fiscal 47.7 h, compiler 36.0 h, vultr/node 54 h (remote waits). READ: kit's cost shows up in machine hours far more than in other lanes' attention; most of it is trains (5 h each, 3 died this week at gates). NEXT: fold both numbers into `kit agile cadence` (notes per team tag already exist there as activity rows) plus a job-hours row from kit_exec_jobs, weekly; agree the slim-kit threshold with main.")
inl kit_verbs_scope () = Task ("KIT-VERBS-SCOPE", "decide kit's scope: harden the coordination core, freeze the generic read/grep/table/do verbs that duplicate native tools (direction call for the user)", Story, P2, D2, 0u32, Planned, "KIT-SELF-COST-METRIC", "kit,direction,user-batch,btw,team:kit,kit", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_kit_verbs_scope () = Planning ("KIT-VERBS-SCOPE", Minutes 30u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_kit_verbs_scope_1 () = Note ("KIT-VERBS-SCOPE", 1u32, "2026-10-31T19:02Z", "kit", "10-31 14:5x kit, from the /btw verdict. Proposal: keep hardening kit's coordination core (jobs and resume, windows/holds, agile writes, machine health) and FREEZE the generic verbs that duplicate native tools plus short scripts (read, lines, grep, table query/diff, most of kit do). Frozen means no new features, only bug fixes, and lanes may use native tools and scripts for those jobs. That's a direction call for the user. This item holds the decision; KIT-SELF-COST-METRIC supplies the numbers.")
inl note_of_kit_verbs_scope_2 () = Note ("KIT-VERBS-SCOPE", 2u32, "2026-11-01T12:04Z", "kit", "11-01 08:0x kit groom, PRE-MERGE MAP (user: main is bringing a large batch of new items from an external conversation; know what is pending so they merge instead of duplicating). kit owns 154 open items: 26 Active, 5 Blocked, 3 Paused, 120 Planned; 32 P0, 64 P1, 42 P2, 16 P3-P4. MERGE RULE for the incoming batch: an incoming item that matches a cluster below lands as a note on that cluster's UMBRELLA (or as a child via its deps), never as a new parallel item; only genuinely new scope becomes a new item. CLUSTERS -> UMBRELLA:\n(1) Agile state scale/concurrency -> KIT-SCALE (measure first): EOIE-AGILE-EVENT-LOG (append-only segments, 55%, note segments ship with TRAIN7), KIT-AGILE-LOST-UPDATES (Blocked on install), STATE-CONCURRENCY-SOTA, EOIE-AGILE-PER-REPO, EOIE-AGILE-CALL-LATENCY (~3 s fixed cost), EOIE-STATE-SCALE (user 10-17: bloat is intended), KIT-AGILE-SEAL-DONE (waits on STATE-SCALE). Fact: agile.spi passed 10 MB at 08:03 today.\n(2) Scrum/XP ceremonies -> EOIE-SCRUM-XP: SCRUM-XP-S2..S8 (S1 Done; S2 60%, S7 50% models proven), EOIE-AGILE-SUPERSEDED (a Superseded status, useful right now for merges), EOIE-AGILE-REQUESTED, EOIE-AGILE-CADENCE (recurrence field), EOIE-TIMELINE.\n(3) Cadence, groom rounds, stale work -> KIT-AGILE-CADENCE: KIT-GROOM-ROUND, KIT-PAUSE-RULE-REWORK, EOIE-AGILE-FIELD-COMPLETENESS, KIT-SELF-COST-METRIC, GRANULAR-STATS; EOIE-AGILE-STALE-ACTIVE merged in today.\n(4) Windows, trains, job scheduling -> HEAVY-HOLD-DEADLOCK: EOIE-WINDOW-ANNOUNCE (80%), EOIE-RELEASE-TRAIN (kit train port, TRAIN7 pre-proof), KIT-JOB-FRONT-TICKET, EOIE-JOB-QUIET-START, EOIE-WINDOW-LIVE-QUEUE, EOIE-WINDOW-WAIT-CLEAR, EOIE-WINDOW-HELD-MARKER, EOIE-JOB-CELLS-GATE, EOIE-JOB-EXPECT, EOIE-JOB-REQUEUE, EOIE-JOB-AMEND, EOIE-JOB-RETENTION, EOIE-SLOT-FILE-SETTLED, KIT-REGEN-PARALLEL.\n(5) Metrics and benches -> KIT-EXEC-METRICS: EOIE-BENCH-VERB (general 'repeat n times on a fresh copy', the base of KIT-SCALE's 'kit bench agents'), EOIE-BUILD-PROFILE, EOIE-BUILD-PHASES, EOIE-GENERATED-PROFILE, CELLS-RUST-OPT-LEVEL (main's).\n(6) Multi-machine -> EOIE-REMOTE-EXECUTOR: IGGY-MULTI-MACHINE, LIB-IGGY-CLIENT, KIT-REMOTE-SYNC, NODE-ALWAYS-FED, REMOTE-LAPTOP-LANE.\n(7) Script-like Spiral and the .ps1 ports -> KIT-RUN-SPI (with PS1-TO-SPIRAL, main's epic): ORG-SCRIPTS-HOME, EOIE-ONE-CALL, EOIE-ONE-CALL-SPEC, EOIE-JOBS, EOIE-EDIT-BATCH, EOIE-READ-QUERY.\n(8) kit do / editing -> EOIE-DO-VERB-FAMILY: EOIE-DO-COPY-LITERAL (Blocked on TRAIN7), EOIE-DO-CONCURRENT-EDITS, EOIE-DO-UNDO, EOIE-DO-MOVE-RANGE, EOIE-DO-SLOP-GATE, EOIE-DO-RECORDS, EOIE-DRAFT-STASH, EOIE-EDIT-INTENT-LEASES, EOIE-TREE-DIFF, EOIE-RUN-TWICE, EOIE-REMOVE-PLAN, DELETE-ROOT-GUARD.\n(9) Code quality / typefeatureset / lib reuse -> GRANULAR-HEAL: GRANULAR-LINT (85%), EOIE-GRANULAR-MIGRATION, TYPEFEATURESET-ENFORCEMENT, EOIE-STYLE-RATCHET (90%), EOIE-HEAL-BOUNDARY, EOIE-LIB-REUSE (epic; child EOIE-USES-LIB), EOIE-IDIOMATIC-CORE, EOIE-OPEN-SHADOW-LINT, LIST-SUGAR-DRIFT-GUARD, EOIE-EDITION3-ROUND, EOIE-CRATE-GATE, KIT-TODO-SCAN-HEADROOM, LIB-GENERATED-COST, LIB-SPLIT-LIST-QUADRATIC, LIB-MACRO-HEAL-CLIP-RUST.\n(10) Repo data as typed rows -> DATA-ROWS-TYPED (60%; tibia's reader plan in n7) with KIT-STATE-SPELLING-GUARD (70%).\n(11) Agents' memory -> EOIE-MEMORY: EOIE-MEMORY-MODEL, EOIE-MEMORY-FEATURES, EOIE-MEMORY-BOOTSTRAP (needs the user's go), EOIE-MEMORY-ADAPTERS, EOIE-ADOPTION-HABITS, EOIE-GAP-INTAKE.\n(12) Resilience and hygiene -> KIT-WIPE-DAMAGE: KIT-ARCHIVE-OFFBOX-MIRROR, DATA-DIR-HOME, KIT-SCRATCH-WATCH, EOIE-LAB-AUDIT, EOIE-LEGACY-STATE-TRIAGE.\n(13) CI and repo surface -> EOIE-CI-DEV-WORKFLOW (Linux guard fixed unstaged, Windows attestation red open): EOIE-CI-PARITY, CODESPACES-BUTTON-ALL-REPOS (Blocked, user push), EOIE-PAGES-VERIFY.\n(14) Genericity and naming -> KIT-RENAME (75%): EOIE-NO-CLAUDE-NAMES, KIT-VERBS-SCOPE (this item; the user's scope decision).\nStandalone, no cluster: GLEAM-RUST-SPLIT, BACKEND-BALANCE, EOIE-BACKEND-MAP, EOIE-BACKEND-REACH, CONVO-IN-SPIRAL, EOIE-EXPERIMENTS, EOIE-SCRATCH-COPY, CELLS-EVERYWHERE, WORKSPACE-PRIMITIVES-LIB, LIB-SPIRAL-TOML-MIGRATION, POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS, TODO-AS-SOURCE, EOIE-HEAL-LIB-LINK, EOIE-LEASE, EOIE-RESUME-COMPILER, EOIE-AUTHORITY-AUDIT, EOIE-INIT-LEASE-FILE, EOIE-LEDGER-ONE-FILE, EOIE-TABLE-JSONL, EOIE-CACHE-INVENTORY, EOIE-JOB-ROOT-GUARD, EOIE-AFFECTED-CRATES, EOIE-TREE-READ-RECORD, KIT-UNBUILT-PROBE-CRATES, KIT-STANDARDS-ENTRY-SUBDIRS, MD-LINE-LIMIT-120, EOIE-ACCEPT-REVIEW, EOIE-GREP-REGEX-HINT, EOIE-AGILE-FIND, EOIE-AGILE-ROOT-FLAG, EOIE-TYPED-HOOKS, KIT-WIP-TODO-MARKERS, EOIE-SPIRAL-TESTS, EOIE-NOTEBOOK-PROGRESS (Blocked TRAIN7), EOIE-HEAL-EDIT-HOOK (Blocked), EOIE-HAND-CRATE-UNITS, EOIE-VERDICT-LEDGER, EOIE-AUTONOMOUS-GATE (Paused), EOIE-ZED-SWEEP (Paused), EOIE-CONSOL-BUNDLE-ERA (Paused). UPDATE 08:4x (round 4): new items placed: KIT-AGILE-QUALITY-LINT (P0) and KIT-AGILE-UNSPELL (P1, remainder of the closed KIT-STATE-SPELLING-GUARD) join cluster (3) cadence/groom under KIT-AGILE-CADENCE; NAMING-LEXICON-RATCHET (DIRECTION-1031) is a child of GRANULAR-HEAL in cluster (9). Every item touched in round 4 now carries team:kit, a DoD line, a NEXT STEP line and a TYPEFEATURESET PLAN line; those markers are what KIT-AGILE-QUALITY-LINT will check. UPDATE 09:0x: new cluster (15) Codebase gauge -> CODEBASE-DIAMOND (epic, user 09:0x): DIAMOND-S1..S8; feeders TYPEFEATURESET-ENFORCEMENT, GRANULAR-STATS, BACKEND-BALANCE, GLEAM-RUST-SPLIT, EOIE-STYLE-RATCHET, GRANULAR-LINT, KIT-EXEC-METRICS, KIT-AGILE-QUALITY-LINT.")
inl note_of_kit_verbs_scope_3 () = Note ("KIT-VERBS-SCOPE", 3u32, "2026-11-02T14:01Z", "x", "2026-11-02 09:59 x: DECISION RECORD (typed; groom of user waits, user 11-02: 'decisions waiting on me get met autonomously/deterministically with tests/benchs/typefeatureset modeling/backups'). kind=ByBench. QUESTION: Whether to freeze kit's generic verbs (read, lines, grep, table query/diff, most of kit do) and harden only the coordination core. RULE (written before acting): Decide each verb with KIT-SELF-COST-METRIC numbers. Freeze it (bug fixes only, no new features) iff, over 20 recorded real inputs in 5 rotated runs with an A/A spread under 5%, its median wall is at least 1.5x the native tool or a short-script equivalent, OR it accounts for under 1% of kit invocations in the last 7 days of job/hook logs. Otherwise keep developing it. A tie means freeze, which is reversible. Coordination verbs (jobs/resume, windows/holds, agile writes, machine health) are never frozen. BACKUP/UNDO: Freezing is a policy note plus a lint; nothing is deleted. Unfreezing means removing the verb from the frozen list. OWNER: kit. NEXT: Reassign from User to Agent, then run the KIT-SELF-COST-METRIC A/B per verb and apply the rule. VETO: the user can reverse this at any time; the backup above is the undo.")
inl kit_regen_parallel () = Task ("KIT-REGEN-PARALLEL", "regenerate owners N at a time with byte-identity against a sequential run: the full regen (3.4 h serial on 10-31) is the train's long pole when keys are invalidated", TaskKind, P2, D3, 0u32, Planned, "EOIE-RELEASE-TRAIN", "kit,release,regen,experiment,team:kit", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_kit_regen_parallel () = Planning ("KIT-REGEN-PARALLEL", Minutes 120u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_kit_regen_parallel_1 () = Note ("KIT-REGEN-PARALLEL", 1u32, "2026-10-31T19:02Z", "kit", "10-31 14:5x kit: TRAIN6's full regen compiled 268 owners one at a time at about 45 s each (3.4 h), the train's long pole. Incremental keys (EOIE-RELEASE-TRAIN n203) remove unchanged owners from later trains, but a compiler change such as pack 10 invalidates every key, so full regens still happen. EXPERIMENT (experiment-until-sota): run owners N at a time (N = 2, 4) with separate output files, check that the outputs are byte-identical to a sequential run on the same inputs, and check that the shared SPIRAL_WORKSPACE_ROOT holds no cache the processes fight over. Run it on the node or a quiet box, since a busy box hides the speedup. Acceptance: byte-identical outputs and wall time at most 0.6x sequential at N=4, or a recorded reason it can't be.")
inl xsd_restriction_attribute_override () = Task ("XSD-RESTRICTION-ATTRIBUTE-OVERRIDE", "xsd_emit: an attribute a simpleContent restriction re-declares replaces the base attribute of that name (it is emitted twice today)", Story, P1, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "fiscal,xsd,correctness,team:fiscal", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_xsd_restriction_attribute_override () = Planning ("XSD-RESTRICTION-ATTRIBUTE-OVERRIDE", Minutes 150u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_xsd_restriction_attribute_override_1 () = Note ("XSD-RESTRICTION-ATTRIBUTE-OVERRIDE", 1u32, "2026-10-31T19:54Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-31 15:54 fiscal: FOUND via FISCAL-MY-MYINVOIS's localized drift (n30). UBL-UnqualifiedDataTypes-2.1 AmountType is <simpleContent><restriction base='ccts-cct:AmountType'> re-declaring currencyID use=required; CCTS_CCT AmountType extends xsd:decimal with currencyID optional + currencyCodeListVersionID optional. XSD rule: in a restriction, a re-declared attribute REPLACES the base's same-named one. xsd_emit treats restriction like extension: the derived record keeps base (x_2_amount_type, whose own reader takes attribute_of currencyID) and adds its own required currency_id; generated_ubl_invoice_2_1.spi: reader 27521 required_attribute currencyID + 27742 attribute_of currencyID (both fill from the same XML attribute), writer 46423 listm.concat [ x_2_amount_type_attributes x.base; [ (currencyID, x.currency_id) ] ] with 46729 also emitting currencyID from the base -> every UDT amount writes currencyID TWICE: the 22 extra nodes in all 22 MyInvois UBL-JSON documents (408 -> 430 at .../Amount/here). Fix: in the restriction case, drop from the base's attribute part every attribute the restriction re-declares (writer), and read the re-declared one once; regression test with a 2-level restriction re-declaring an attribute. Then regenerate the UBL units (drift guard) and re-run reads_its_documents (expect 0 drift).")
inl note_of_xsd_restriction_attribute_override_2 () = Note ("XSD-RESTRICTION-ATTRIBUTE-OVERRIDE", 2u32, "2026-10-31T19:58Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-31 15:58 fiscal: DESIGN (read from the lib; nothing drafted yet). xsd.spi derived_of (lines ~262-274) builds the SAME Derived { name; base; content; attributes } for xs:extension and xs:restriction, so the model drops which one it was. The fix does not need it back: an extension may not re-declare an attribute its base already has (invalid XSD), so 'a name the derived type declares wins over the base's' is right for both. EMITTER-ONLY fix in xsd_emit: where the writer composes base_attributes (xsd_emit.spi:1519-1522, '<base>_attributes x.base') with the derived type's own attribute parts, wrap the base call as listm.filter (fun (n, _) => <n not in the derived's attribute names>) (<base>_attributes x.base) whenever the derived declares any attribute (names from d.attributes; QName text as written, e.g. currencyID). The reader may keep filling both fields from the one XML attribute (harmless, same value). Regression test: a 2-level simpleContent restriction re-declaring an optional base attribute as required must write that attribute exactly once (count it in the emitted _attributes body, plus a unit round trip). Then regenerate generated_ubl_* (bring-generated-home.ps1) and re-run MyInvois reads_its_documents expecting 0 drift (the 408 -> 430 case).")
inl note_of_xsd_restriction_attribute_override_3 () = Note ("XSD-RESTRICTION-ATTRIBUTE-OVERRIDE", 3u32, "2026-10-31T20:19Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-31 16:19 fiscal: DRAFTED (n2's design), program fiscal/lib-programs/xsd-restriction-attributes.kit (xsd_emit live AE0A917F -> B1BF0D77, 3 replacements, rehearsed hash-equal, round trip ok after adding the final newline the appended test had dropped): (1) attribute_names_of collects a derived type's attribute names (tag_text a.name); (2) without_redeclared rewrites the writer's base part '<t>_attributes x.base' into listm'.filter (fun (n, _) => n <> ... ) (...) excluding those names, other families' prefixes pass unchanged (they never end in that text); (3) the one call site, record_walk's Derived arm, wraps derived_prefix with it; (4) test a_restriction_redeclaring_an_attribute_writes_it_once (TBase extends xs:decimal with cur + ver optional; TAmount restricts TBase re-declaring cur required): the writer must filter cur from the base part and still write ver. Proof running: fiscal-restriction-attrs = CONTROL (live + the test only, must fail at the filter assertion) then the whole xsd_emit module on the draft. Then: land, regenerate generated_ubl_* (bring-generated-home.ps1), MyInvois reads_its_documents expecting 0 drift.")
inl note_of_xsd_restriction_attribute_override_4 () = Note ("XSD-RESTRICTION-ATTRIBUTE-OVERRIDE", 4u32, "2026-10-31T20:38Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-31 16:38 fiscal: land program for xsd_emit live AE0A917F -> B1BF0D77 (moved here from fiscal/lib-programs, which leaves the repo; land with land-lib-program.ps1 -Note XSD-RESTRICTION-ATTRIBUTE-OVERRIDE/<this note> -File xsd_emit.spi -Base AE0A917F90E02A6A -Target <B1BF0D77 16-hex>).\nPROGRAM (kit do <lib> -):\nedit xsd_emit.spi\npatch <<<\n@@ -1083,6 +1083,22 @@\n     | _ :: rest => choice_cases choice_name rest acc\n     | [] => acc\n \n+:~ ^ attribute_names_of \\* form. (attributes : list (xsd attribute_decl form)) (acc : list string) : list string =\n+    ?= attributes ?|\n+    | (Attribute a) :: rest => attribute_names_of rest (tag_text a.name :: acc)\n+    | [] => acc\n+\n+:= without_redeclared \\* form. (attributes : list (xsd attribute_decl form)) (prefix : walked) : walked =\n+    ?= attribute_names_of attributes [] ?|\n+    | [] => prefix\n+    | names =>\n+        := kept = join_with \" && \" (listm.map (\\ n => \"n <> \\\"\" ++# n ++# \"\\\"\") names) \"\"\n+        := filter (part : string) : string =\n+            ? sm'.ends_with \"_attributes x.base\" part\n+            ?> \"listm'.filter (fun (n, _) => \" ++# kept ++# \") (\" ++# part ++# \")\"\n+            |: part\n+        { prefix ?| attribute_parts = listm.map filter prefix.attribute_parts }\n+\n :& :~ collect_attributes \\* form.\n     (owner : string) (attributes : list (xsd attribute_decl form)) (acc : collected)\n     : collected\n@@ -2073,7 +2089,8 @@\n     (family : f) (kinds : list (string * type_kind)) (name : string) (item : xsd type_def form) : list walk_text =\n     ?= item ?|\n     | Complex c => record_walk family kinds name c.content c.attributes (nothing_walked ())\n-    | Derived d => record_walk family kinds name d.content d.attributes (derived_prefix family kinds d.base)\n+    | Derived d =>\n+        record_walk family kinds name d.content d.attributes (without_redeclared d.attributes (derived_prefix family kinds d.base))\n     | Simple _ => []\n \n :& :~ record_walk\n@@ -3469,3 +3486,19 @@ test a_family_with_required_signatures_generates_staged_units =\n     := unchanged, _ = generated_units_signed [\"Signature\"] (untyped_any_content ()) plain\n     := before, _ = generated_units plain\n     ? unchanged <> before ?> failwith \"a family without required signatures generates exactly what it did before\"\n+\n+///> rust\n+test a_restriction_redeclaring_an_attribute_writes_it_once =\n+    := schema =\n+        read_schema (\n+            \"<xs:schema><xs:complexType name=\\\"TBase\\\"><xs:simpleContent><xs:extension base=\\\"xs:decimal\\\">\"\n+            ++# \"<xs:attribute name=\\\"cur\\\" type=\\\"xs:string\\\" use=\\\"optional\\\"/>\"\n+            ++# \"<xs:attribute name=\\\"ver\\\" type=\\\"xs:string\\\" use=\\\"optional\\\"/></xs:extension></xs:simpleContent>\"\n+            ++# \"</xs:complexType><xs:complexType name=\\\"TAmount\\\"><xs:simpleContent><xs:restriction base=\\\"TBase\\\">\"\n+            ++# \"<xs:attribute name=\\\"cur\\\" type=\\\"xs:string\\\" use=\\\"required\\\"/></xs:restriction></xs:simpleContent>\"\n+            ++# \"</xs:complexType></xs:schema>\"\n+        )\n+    := functions = join_lines (module_parts schema).functions \"\"\n+    ? sm'.contains \"listm'.filter (fun (n, _) => n <> \\\"cur\\\") (\" functions = !-\n+    ?> failwith \"the base's attributes leave out the one the restriction re-declares\"\n+    ? sm'.contains \"\\\"ver\\\"\" functions = !- ?> failwith \"the base's other attributes are still written\"\n>>>")
inl note_of_xsd_restriction_attribute_override_5 () = Note ("XSD-RESTRICTION-ATTRIBUTE-OVERRIDE", 5u32, "2026-10-31T20:58Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-31 16:58 fiscal: CORRECTED program (supersedes n4): the first draft failed to type-check because its helpers sat BEFORE ':== walked' and inside the ':& collect_attributes' group (splitting it: 'Unbound term variable: collect_attributes', 'Unbound type variable: walked'); moved just above ':~ ^ named_type_walk' (after walked, outside any group). xsd_emit live AE0A917F -> 3872CC94.\nPROGRAM (kit do <lib> -):\nedit xsd_emit.spi\npatch <<<\n@@ -2053,6 +2053,22 @@\n             type_ref = type_ref\n         }\n \n+:~ ^ attribute_names_of \\* form. (attributes : list (xsd attribute_decl form)) (acc : list string) : list string =\n+    ?= attributes ?|\n+    | (Attribute a) :: rest => attribute_names_of rest (tag_text a.name :: acc)\n+    | [] => acc\n+\n+:= without_redeclared \\* form. (attributes : list (xsd attribute_decl form)) (prefix : walked) : walked =\n+    ?= attribute_names_of attributes [] ?|\n+    | [] => prefix\n+    | names =>\n+        := kept = join_with \" && \" (listm.map (\\ n => \"n <> \\\"\" ++# n ++# \"\\\"\") names) \"\"\n+        := filter (part : string) : string =\n+            ? sm'.ends_with \"_attributes x.base\" part\n+            ?> \"listm'.filter (fun (n, _) => \" ++# kept ++# \") (\" ++# part ++# \")\"\n+            |: part\n+        { prefix ?| attribute_parts = listm.map filter prefix.attribute_parts }\n+\n :~ ^ named_type_walk\n     \\* form f {\n         derived_prefix;\n@@ -2073,7 +2089,8 @@\n     (family : f) (kinds : list (string * type_kind)) (name : string) (item : xsd type_def form) : list walk_text =\n     ?= item ?|\n     | Complex c => record_walk family kinds name c.content c.attributes (nothing_walked ())\n-    | Derived d => record_walk family kinds name d.content d.attributes (derived_prefix family kinds d.base)\n+    | Derived d =>\n+        record_walk family kinds name d.content d.attributes (without_redeclared d.attributes (derived_prefix family kinds d.base))\n     | Simple _ => []\n \n :& :~ record_walk\n@@ -3469,3 +3486,19 @@ test a_family_with_required_signatures_generates_staged_units =\n     := unchanged, _ = generated_units_signed [\"Signature\"] (untyped_any_content ()) plain\n     := before, _ = generated_units plain\n     ? unchanged <> before ?> failwith \"a family without required signatures generates exactly what it did before\"\n+\n+///> rust\n+test a_restriction_redeclaring_an_attribute_writes_it_once =\n+    := schema =\n+        read_schema (\n+            \"<xs:schema><xs:complexType name=\\\"TBase\\\"><xs:simpleContent><xs:extension base=\\\"xs:decimal\\\">\"\n+            ++# \"<xs:attribute name=\\\"cur\\\" type=\\\"xs:string\\\" use=\\\"optional\\\"/>\"\n+            ++# \"<xs:attribute name=\\\"ver\\\" type=\\\"xs:string\\\" use=\\\"optional\\\"/></xs:extension></xs:simpleContent>\"\n+            ++# \"</xs:complexType><xs:complexType name=\\\"TAmount\\\"><xs:simpleContent><xs:restriction base=\\\"TBase\\\">\"\n+            ++# \"<xs:attribute name=\\\"cur\\\" type=\\\"xs:string\\\" use=\\\"required\\\"/></xs:restriction></xs:simpleContent>\"\n+            ++# \"</xs:complexType></xs:schema>\"\n+        )\n+    := functions = join_lines (module_parts schema).functions \"\"\n+    ? sm'.contains \"listm'.filter (fun (n, _) => n <> \\\"cur\\\") (\" functions = !-\n+    ?> failwith \"the base's attributes leave out the one the restriction re-declares\"\n+    ? sm'.contains \"\\\"ver\\\"\" functions = !- ?> failwith \"the base's other attributes are still written\"\n>>>")
inl note_of_xsd_restriction_attribute_override_6 () = Note ("XSD-RESTRICTION-ATTRIBUTE-OVERRIDE", 6u32, "2026-10-31T23:09Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-31 19:09 fiscal: LANDED 18:59:49 from this item's note 5 via land-lib-program.ps1 -Note (the first note-sourced land): live xsd_emit AE0A917F -> 3872CC94DCBD3505, hash-equal to the proven draft (control red at the filter assertion; whole module 26/26, 0 type errors), undo at %LOCALAPPDATA%\\\\kit\\\\undo\\\\XSD-RESTRICTION-ATTRIBUTE-OVERRIDE-n5; round trip of the landed file: canonical true, fixpoint true (spiral apps/cells/spi/lib_roundtrip.exs; slice-roundtrip-sweep.ps1 is retired). Next, already queued: regenerate EVERY family on the landed lib (regen-all, box) and bring the changed units home formatted (drift guard), then re-run MyInvois reads_its_documents on the node expecting 0 drift. 40 -> 80.")
inl note_of_xsd_restriction_attribute_override_7 () = Note ("XSD-RESTRICTION-ATTRIBUTE-OVERRIDE", 7u32, "2026-11-01T00:46Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-31 20:46 fiscal: REGENERATED every family on the landed lib (fiscal-regen-all-restriction, box: all 23 generator tests pass) and brought all 81 units home formatted (bring-generated-home.ps1, format-generated 1108 s, 0 failed). Blast radius, exactly: the 6 UBL units (+47/-? each: e.g. x_unqualified_data_types_2_amount_type_attributes now 'listm'.filter (fun (n, _) => n <> \\currencyID\\) (x_2_amount_type_attributes x.base)', plus the Id of the timestamp types) and generated_ksef_fa_3 (18 filters, e.g. rodzajAdresu on TAdres); every other unit byte-identical. Also brought home: the 6 NF-e event units (the generator change was committed without them). Compile proof of the emitted filter: the formatted KSeF unit (0FD0BC55795D) is gating now; the UBL invoice drift re-run (expect 0 drift on the 22 MyInvois JSON documents) goes to the node after the set-all job frees t1031.")
inl note_of_xsd_restriction_attribute_override_8 () = Note ("XSD-RESTRICTION-ATTRIBUTE-OVERRIDE", 8u32, "2026-11-01T00:54Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-31 20:54 fiscal: COMPILE PROOF of the emitted filter: the formatted generated_ksef_fa_3 (0FD0BC55795D, the tree file; 18 listm'.filter base parts) passes 2/2 (writes_its_example + keeps_raw_strings_out), 0 type errors (fiscal-ksef-filter-proof, box). Zero-drift proof queued on the node: fiscal-p8-t1031u-ubl-drift (job-1793494454747) on a new mirror t1031u (spiral fd70dccf with xsd_emit 3872CC94, the fiscal tree with the formatted UBL units; corpus copied on the node from today's t1031, the same box tar) runs generated_ubl_invoice_2_1 with its cell cap raised mirror-only to 18,000,000 ms; expect 'real documents do not read back' to be gone (22 MyInvois JSON documents). On that, this item and FISCAL-MY-MYINVOIS's drift close. 80 -> 90.")
inl note_of_xsd_restriction_attribute_override_9 () = Note ("XSD-RESTRICTION-ATTRIBUTE-OVERRIDE", 9u32, "2026-11-01T03:59Z", "fiscal", "2026-11-01 00:10 fiscal: node UBL drift run job-1793494454747 gave NO VERDICT. The 2.8 h rust build passed, then generated_ubl_invoice_2_1_writes_its_example panicked with OS error 3 (path not found) on write_all_text: the t1031u mirror has no samples/ folder (gitignored; build.ps1 makes it, and the direct cell gate does not). Created the folder and re-queued the same gate as job-1793505518611-fiscal-p8-t1031u-ubl-drift2. Close on its 0-drift verdict.")
inl note_of_xsd_restriction_attribute_override_10 () = Note ("XSD-RESTRICTION-ATTRIBUTE-OVERRIDE", 10u32, "2026-11-01T07:08Z", "fiscal", "2026-11-01 03:10 fiscal: ZERO-DRIFT VERDICT: node job-1793505518611-fiscal-p8-t1031u-ubl-drift2 (t1031u: spiral with xsd_emit 3872CC94, the fiscal tree with the formatted UBL units, samples/ created) ran generated_ubl_invoice_2_1 over its 155 XML + 22 JSON real documents: 1/1 passed, 0 failed, headroom 0.62, the example written, and 0 drift lines in the log (no \"real documents do not read back\", \"does not keep every element\" or \"not stable\"). The UBL AmountType restriction no longer writes currencyID twice, so the MyInvois UBL-JSON round trip holds. CLOSED by the item's rule.")
inl gleam_abyssal_scale () = Task ("GLEAM-ABYSSAL-SCALE", "Gleam as a top test backend for abyssal codebases: the mega samples and apps/spiral compile to Gleam and build on BEAM, measuring codegen size, gleam build time and runtime against Rust/C, so the self-hosted compiler's scale shows up before the rewrite", Feature, P0, D6, 0u32, Planned, "", "compiler,gleam,backends,scale,self-hosting,team:compiler", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_gleam_abyssal_scale () = Planning ("GLEAM-ABYSSAL-SCALE", Minutes 600u32, Load Heavy, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_gleam_abyssal_scale_1 () = Note ("GLEAM-ABYSSAL-SCALE", 1u32, "2026-10-31T20:04Z", "compiler", "10-31 10-31 16:04 compiler (groom, user 10-31 15:4x): 'gleam should be a top backend to test against to simulate abyssal codebases'. Today Gleam has rows in the matrix but the 5 mega samples and apps/spiral are not built on Gleam end to end. Scope: (1) every mega sample's Gleam row compiles and agrees with C; (2) apps/spiral (the largest Spiral program) emitted to Gleam, gleam build time and module size recorded; (3) runtime ratio vs Rust/C on runtime_bench; (4) every Gleam-only failure becomes its own item. Related: GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS, GLEAM-INLINE-SINGLE-USE, GLEAM-RUST-SPLIT. Gate for SELF-HOSTED-SPIRAL.")
inl backend_primitive_parity () = Task ("BACKEND-PRIMITIVE-PARITY", "Every primitive op and conversion on every backend: a census (op x type x backend, from the codegen op-match arms plus matrix rows) and zero gaps: no missing arithmetic, no char/int conversion holes (C char has no int conversion today), no compile_error fallbacks in lib/spiral for a supported backend", Feature, P0, D5, 93u32, Active, "PORTABLE-PRIMITIVES,CONVERT-ZIG-UNATIVEINT,CHAR-ORDERING", "team:compiler,compiler,backends,primitives,conversion,self-hosting,typefeatureset", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_backend_primitive_parity () = Planning ("BACKEND-PRIMITIVE-PARITY", Minutes 900u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_backend_primitive_parity_1 () = Note ("BACKEND-PRIMITIVE-PARITY", 1u32, "2026-10-31T20:04Z", "compiler", "10-31 10-31 16:04 compiler (groom, user 10-31 15:4x): 'we shouldn't have gadt bugs or conversion gaps by now, unacceptable' and 'ariths missing'. Known gaps from today's session: C char supports only equality and has no int conversion (the self-hosted tokenizer slice had to skip C for it, SELF-HOSTED-SPIRAL n6); Gleam JS target lacks tanh/sin/cos/arrays (GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS); Zig unativeint conversion (CONVERT-ZIG-UNATIVEINT); WGSL/Calyx/OpenQASM/eBPF lack f64/i64/strings/unions (their backend items). Method: the op-census method (memory op-census-method: diff each backend's op-match arms against C's + a grep census of lib/spiral compile_error fallbacks), then one sample per gap, closed backend by backend. Done = census shows 0 unsupported (op,type) cells for the 8 host backends (F#, C, Rust, Zig, Gleam, Wasm, Lean, Delphi) and every remaining one on the GPU/HDL/quantum backends is a typed compile error with an item. Gate for SELF-HOSTED-SPIRAL.")
inl note_of_backend_primitive_parity_2 () = Note ("BACKEND-PRIMITIVE-PARITY", 2u32, "2026-10-31T20:09Z", "compiler", "10-31 10-31 16:09 compiler: CORRECTION to the title's char claim, measured on the installed compiler (PACK-10): char ORDERING (<, >, <=, >=) is a type error on C, Rust AND F# alike ('Got: char | Fails to satisfy: number'): a language-level gap, not C's; equality compiles. Char <-> int conversion is still UNVERIFIED (my probe's conv spelling was wrong). That part is now its own child, CHAR-ORDERING (P0, a dep here).")
inl note_of_backend_primitive_parity_3 () = Note ("BACKEND-PRIMITIVE-PARITY", 3u32, "2026-10-31T21:04Z", "compiler", "10-31 10-31 17:04 compiler: FIRST CENSUS (op x backend, the op-census method: does each backend's codegen span contain an arm naming the op; live PACK-10 core). 38 runtime ops (arithmetic, comparisons, bool/bitwise/shift, Neg, tanh/log/exp/sin/cos/sqrt/pow, NanIs, string length/index/slice, PrimToString, Printf, StdoutFlush, MonotonicDelayMs, ArrayIndexSet, Assign, Failwith; StringLength/FailWith matched as TyStringLength/TyFailwith; Pi/Infinity excluded as compile-time folds). MISSING per backend: C 0; Rust 1 and Delphi 1 (PrimToString); Python, Zig, TypeScript, Cpp 2 (PrimToString + MonotonicDelayMs or Assign); Swift, Kotlin, F#, Lua, Lean, Wasm 3; Gleam, Bend 4 (PrimToString, StdoutFlush, MonotonicDelayMs, Assign); OpenQasm 8 (strings, Printf, ArrayIndexSet, Assign, MonotonicDelayMs); WGSL 14, eBPF 15, Calyx 16 (all float math, strings, Printf, ...: their documented 'not supported yet'). LIMITS of this cut, to resolve next: (a) name presence isn't semantics: PrimToString missing everywhere but C needs a check (lowered earlier, or a real gap?), likewise Assign (some backends may assign through other forms); (b) no TYPE dimension yet (an op present for i32 but not u8/f32 shows as present): the next pass reads each arm's type guards; (c) conversions are TyConv, already mapped (17 emitters, CHAR-ORDERING n4). Priority order for zero gaps: the 8 host backends (F#, Rust, Gleam, Wasm, C, Zig, Lean, Delphi) first, per the self-hosting gate; the four hardware targets' gaps are partly inherent (no strings in a WGSL shader) and need a per-target 'supported subset' decision instead. RESOLVED 17:06 limit (a) for PrimToString: a REAL gap. PartEval pushes it as a runtime op for every backend (spiral_compiler.fs 11910-11914: integers, bool, char -> string), but only CodegenC has an arm (25356-25368); every other backend falls to its unsupported-op error when a program converts a runtime int/bool/char to string through it (lib usually goes through backend_switch macros instead, which is why the matrix doesn't show it). First concrete fix of this item: a PrimToString arm in the 17 other emitters, one sample per backend agreeing with C.")
inl note_of_backend_primitive_parity_4 () = Note ("BACKEND-PRIMITIVE-PARITY", 4u32, "2026-10-31T21:07Z", "compiler", "10-31 10-31 17:07 compiler: PrimToString SPEC from the C oracle (CodegenC 25356-25368, helpers 25911-25948), for the 17 missing arms: signed i8..i64 -> decimal via %lld (StringFromI64); unsigned u8..u64 -> decimal via %llu (StringFromU64); bool -> lowercase 'true' / 'false' (StringFromBool; careful: F#'s string true is 'True', Python's str(True) is 'True', Kotlin/Swift print lowercase); char -> a one-character string (StringFromChar copies the byte). Each backend maps that onto its own string type (Rust Rc<str>, Gleam String, Lua string, ...). SEQUENCING: the char arm follows CHAR-ORDERING's decision (main 10-31: char = a Unicode scalar value, so char -> string becomes the scalar's UTF-8 encoding, not a byte copy, and C's StringFromChar changes in the same pack); the integer and bool arms are independent and can land first. Proof per arm: a sample (one value per type, incl. negative, u64 max, true/false) whose stdout agrees with C on every backend in its matrix list.")
inl note_of_backend_primitive_parity_5 () = Note ("BACKEND-PRIMITIVE-PARITY", 5u32, "2026-11-01T12:35Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 08:35 compiler, ROUND 4 QUALITY SPEC: FIELDS: tag team:compiler added; P0 (gate anchor, theme 1). DEFINITION OF DONE: a census table op x type x backend generated from the core (every TyOp/TyConv case in each codegen's match, vs CodegenC's set) with 0 'missing' cells for the ops lib reaches, and for each formerly missing cell a sample row agreeing with C. First slice: PrimToString on the 17 emitters (spec n-latest), sample samples/prim_to_string_all (all int widths, bool, char, f32/f64) agreeing with C on every backend list. NEXT STEP: the census as a kit command reading spiral_compiler.fs (Spiral-first, not a .ps1): per '/// ## Codegen*' section, the set of 'TyOp(<Op>' and conversion arms. TYPEFEATURESET PLAN: the census row is typed: op is a UNION mirroring the core's Op cases (not op-name strings), type a union of PrimitiveType, backend the backend union; a cell is a union 'Native | Helper of name | Missing | NotApplicable' (no -1 or empty-string 'missing'); the C spec per op is a record keyed by (Op, PrimitiveType) so each emitter's arm is generated from or checked against one table, not hand-copied 17 times.")
inl note_of_backend_primitive_parity_6 () = Note ("BACKEND-PRIMITIVE-PARITY", 6u32, "2026-11-01T20:40Z", "compiler", "11-01 16:50 compiler: FIRST SLICE PROVEN (PrimToString on 6 more emitters). CENSUS CORRECTION: PrimToString is a core intrinsic that lib reaches only from sm'.obj_to_string's C arm; every other backend's obj_to_string uses its own macro arm. So the n1 'missing' cells were unreachable from lib, not live bugs. Adding the op everywhere is what lets obj_to_string collapse to one portable op (PORTABLE-PRIMITIVES). PROGRAM: %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\spiral\\BACKEND-PRIMITIVE-PARITY\\1-prim-to-string.kitdo, 6 replacements, each inserted before that backend's NanIs arm, following the C spec of n2. F#: (string (x)), bool -> \"true\"/\"false\". Gleam: int.to_string, bool via case, char identity (Gleam chars are Strings). Rust: Rc::<str>::from((x).to_string()), char ((x) as char) since Rust chars are bytes. Python: str(x), bool via a conditional expression, char identity. Delphi: IntToStr / UIntToStr / LowerCase(BoolToStr(x, True)) / string(x). TypeScript: String(x), char String.fromCodePoint (TS chars are byte numbers). It stacks cleanly with GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS's and DELPHI-TO-PASCAL's core programs. SAMPLE (landed unstaged): samples/prim_to_string_all (edition 3, core- only, so the operators are `+.` and `=.`). It checks 11 values: i8 -128, i16 -32768, i32 -5, i64 -1234567890123, u8 255, u16 65535, u32 4294967295, u64 18446744073709551615, true, false, 'a'. It exits with the number of mismatches. Added to the Gleam and Python matrix lists; C, Rust, Delphi, F# and TypeScript rows already cover every sample. PROOF (candidate = live core + program; host 98D282B5): exit 0 on C (oracle), Rust, F#, Gleam, Python and Delphi (node, job-1793565178039 plus a hand run for the Delphi exe name) and on TypeScript (box, bun 1.4.4; the node's bun 1.2.5 cannot run the shim, because Worker resourceLimits is not implemented there). 7/7 agree. NEXT: ride the next pack; then the remaining emitters (Zig, Lua with spiral_w64, Lean, Kotlin, Swift, Wasm, Bend, C++), then collapse obj_to_string onto the op.")
inl note_of_backend_primitive_parity_7 () = Note ("BACKEND-PRIMITIVE-PARITY", 7u32, "2026-11-01T21:28Z", "compiler", "11-01 17:28 compiler: PACK15B INTEGRATION + SLICE 2 DRAFTED. (1) The sample now also checks literal arguments (7u8, false, 'z', -3i64: 15 checks; C oracle exit 0). That exposed two Delphi problems, both fixed in program 1. A literal bool or char fell to IntToStr, because Delphi's prim_of only knows signed-int literals; the arm now matches the data directly. An untyped literal made fpc report \"Can't determine which overloaded function to call\"; the arm now casts with UIntToStr(QWord(..)) and IntToStr(Int64(..)). pack15b candidate (core A7F3592A598C96A1): exit 0 on C, Rust, F#, Gleam, Python, Delphi and Pascal (node job-1793567609030; Pascal is DELPHI-TO-PASCAL's new backend name) and on TypeScript (box). (2) Slice 2 drafted: %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\spiral\\BACKEND-PRIMITIVE-PARITY\\2-prim-to-string-zig-kotlin-swift-lean.kitdo, 4 replacements; it stacks on program 1. Zig: std.fmt.allocPrint(spiral_gpa, \"{d}\" / \"{}\" for bool / \"{c}\" for the u8 char) catch @panic. Lean: toString. Kotlin: .toString(), char via .toChar().toString() (Kotlin chars are Int). Swift: String(x), char String(UnicodeScalar(UInt8(truncatingIfNeeded: x))). PROOF for slice 2 waits for the box (Zig, Kotlin, Swift and Lean toolchains live on the box matrix; the box is busy with pack15b's residual predict). Remaining after slice 2: Lua (spiral_w64 for 64-bit), Wasm, Bend, C++; then collapse sm'.obj_to_string onto the op.")
inl note_of_backend_primitive_parity_8 () = Note ("BACKEND-PRIMITIVE-PARITY", 8u32, "2026-11-01T22:23Z", "compiler", "11-01 18:25 compiler: SLICE 2 PROVEN on Zig, Lean, Lua (node job-1793570955137-compiler-p2s2; candidate = live core + programs 1 and 2; the 15-check sample incl. literal args): C 0, Zig 0 (hand rerun: the harness had split -femit-bin=main.exe), Lean 0, Lua 0, all equal to the C oracle. Lua's 64-bit values went through spiral_w64.tostring and u32 through %.0f, as its printf does. Kotlin and Swift are drafted in the same program and wait for the box toolchains (box busy with pack15b's predict). Program 2 rides the pack after pack15b, together with RUST-UNION-DEFAULT-ARRAY-CREATE and LIST-SUGAR-DRIFT-GUARD's lint.")
inl note_of_backend_primitive_parity_9 () = Note ("BACKEND-PRIMITIVE-PARITY", 9u32, "2026-11-02T04:53Z", "compiler", "11-02 00:53 compiler: pack15c INSTALLED (main 00:29-00:33; programs 1+2 of this item inside, box SpiralCompiler.dll 92820117717425C0 / Core 83CEDAA9DDED49EB). samples/prim_to_string_all now ALSO on the Kotlin, Swift, Zig, Lean and Lua lists in apps/compiler/tmp/tests/matrix.psd1 (unstaged; was Gleam+Python). Proof on the installed compiler, box: all 5 compile, native exit 0 (= C, sample exits with its miss count over 15 checks): kotlinc+java 0, swiftc -O 0, zig ReleaseFast 0, lean 4.35 --run 0, lua run_main 0. Next step: PrimToString emitters for Wasm, Bend and C++ (a pack15d program), then collapse lib sm'.obj_to_string's C-only arm into the core op on every backend. typefeatureset: PrimToString keyed on the closed PrimitiveType union, no string dispatch.")
inl note_of_backend_primitive_parity_10 () = Note ("BACKEND-PRIMITIVE-PARITY", 10u32, "2026-11-02T05:10Z", "compiler", "11-02 01:10 compiler: PROGRAM 3 DRAFTED + PROVEN (pack15d candidate). %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\spiral\\BACKEND-PRIMITIVE-PARITY\\3-prim-to-string-wasm-bend.kitdo, 5 replacements on live core 55C243C7A93BFFC8 -> B161EDA4DC69F256. WASM: PrimToString calls the prelude's existing i64_to_str/u64_to_str/bool_to_str/char_to_str (unused until now; pulled in by the call-scan), i32-wide values extended _s/_u. LATENT PRELUDE BUG FOUND AND FIXED: the fixed 'false' record at 56 is 9 bytes (to 64), and the '-' record at 64 overwrote its 'e', so bool_to_str(false) returned a corrupt string (bitmask probe: only the two false checks failed). '-' moves to 66 (inside the reserved 0..71 scratch; interned strings still start at 72, so other residuals change by that one data line only). BEND: bool via a new conditional prelude def spiral_bool_show (match True{}/False{}), char via SCon{Char.from_u32(x), SNil{}}, i64/u64 via w64_show_s/_u, signed small ints via spiral_i32_show, the rest U32.show; syntax checked against bend2 first (true|false|A|7). PROOF (box, Debug candidate, process mode): prim_to_string_all C 0 / Wasm 0 / Bend 0 (15 checks); string_ord 0/0/0; i64_scalar 0/0/0. Control: the installed compiler rejects PrimToString on Wasm and Bend (compile exit 5). C++ deliberately OUT: its strings are borrowed const char * (string slice is already a codegen error there) and the section is upstream-mapped; number-to-string needs an ownership policy first. PREDICTED pack residuals: every Wasm row changes by the one data line (64 -> 66), residual-only; no native change, since i64_to_str had no callers. AFTER INSTALL: add prim_to_string_all to the Wasm and Bend lists in matrix.psd1. typefeatureset: dispatch on the closed PrimitiveType union.")
inl note_of_backend_primitive_parity_11 () = Note ("BACKEND-PRIMITIVE-PARITY", 11u32, "2026-11-02T07:00Z", "compiler", "11-02 03:00 compiler: pack15d INSTALLED (program 3 live; installed DLLs byte-equal to the proven candidate). samples/prim_to_string_all added to the Wasm and Bend lists in matrix.psd1 (unstaged); it is now on 12 backend lists. Remaining: C++ (needs a string ownership policy in the upstream-mapped CodegenCpp first; deliberately out), then collapse lib sm'.obj_to_string's C-only arm into the core op. Next step: draft the obj_to_string collapse as a lib program and gate it on every ///> backend.")
inl note_of_backend_primitive_parity_12 () = Note ("BACKEND-PRIMITIVE-PARITY", 12u32, "2026-11-02T11:15Z", "yed", "Kotlin gaps found porting i574n.github/apps/graphs to Spiral->Kotlin (yed lane, 11-02): (1) host macro types are unsupported: ':! t = $''StringBuilder''' fails 'The Kotlin backend doesn''t support the type StringBuilder yet' (spiral_compiler.fs ~21916: kotlin type printer has no YMacro case; F# 12717 shows the one-line fix). Worked around with phantom-typed i32 handles. (2) spiralStringIndex/spiralStringSlice re-encode the WHOLE string to UTF-8 per call (21823-21844), so lib's portable byte scanners are O(n^2) on Kotlin; I gave sm' native one-call Kotlin arms instead. (3) union matches lower to nested else chains: a 186-case match indented ~1500 columns and made a 4.8 MB .kt; splitting to two-level unions (<=25 cases) brought it to 885 KB. (4) lib had no Kotlin arm for base (=.) / (<>.) and sm' slice/index_of/starts_with/ends_with/contains/split/replace; added in spiral/lib (unstaged). Verified: prototypes + instances on nominals, indexed GADT typestate and constrained foralls all resolve statically on Kotlin.")
inl note_of_backend_primitive_parity_13 () = Note ("BACKEND-PRIMITIVE-PARITY", 13u32, "2026-11-02T11:27Z", "yed", "Kotlin multi-file emission (yed lane, 11-02): the Kotlin backend writes ONE .kt per entry, so i574n.github/apps/graphs/graphs.kt is 16,548 lines and breaks the 1000-line gate for every language. Needed: split the output into files of at most 1000 lines, one per Spiral module or per join-point group, in one package, plus a manifest for kotlinc. Contributing: union matches lower to nested else chains (each case nests one level deeper), and string helpers are re-emitted per file.")
inl note_of_backend_primitive_parity_14 () = Note ("BACKEND-PRIMITIVE-PARITY", 14u32, "2026-11-02T11:33Z", "compiler", "11-02 07:35 compiler: OBJ_TO_STRING COLLAPSE drafted: %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\spiral\\BACKEND-PRIMITIVE-PARITY\\4-obj-to-string-prim-collapse.kitdo (sm'.spi, 3 replacements, dry-applied on a scratch copy). New obj_to_string_prim: bool/char/i8..u64 go to !!!!PrimToString, anything else takes the arm's own fallback. Every backend_switch arm (Gleam, Lua, Fsharp, Python, Rust, TypeScript, Zig, Lean, Wasm, C, Pascal) is wrapped with it. Cpp has no PrimToString arm and no obj_to_string arm, so it stays out. Removed as dead: obj_to_string_c, obj_to_string_pascal(_bool), F#/Python bool cases, TS char case, Zig char/bool/int cases (floats, string and {any} stay), and Wasm's 6 prim lines (string stays). BEHAVIOR CHANGES, all toward C's output: Gleam bool \"True\" -> \"true\" and char -> the character (format_debug before); Lua char -> the character; Pascal gains char. New tests: Pascal char, plus obj_to_string_prints_prims_like_c on gleam/lua/wasm/lean. Node proof job-1793619166216-compiler-p2scollapse is running (sm'.spi + date_time.spi, control vs collapse). Before landing: a Gleam-consumer corpus check (x-dumps renders bools via obj_to_string?) for the \"True\" -> \"true\" change.")
inl note_of_backend_primitive_parity_15 () = Note ("BACKEND-PRIMITIVE-PARITY", 15u32, "2026-11-02T11:57Z", "compiler", "11-02 07:58 compiler: OBJ_TO_STRING COLLAPSE PROVEN, READY TO LAND (waiting on kit-TRAIN11's heavy window; lib-guarded-do refuses while it is held, and I am not exempting it). Node gate on mirror t1101, re-synced to the box first. CONTROL: sm'.spi 73/73, date_time.spi 43/44. COLLAPSE: sm'.spi 74/74 (73 + the new obj_to_string_prints_prims_like_c on gleam/lua/lean; the Pascal test now includes char), date_time.spi 43/44 (same row). The first collapse run read \"passed 0 failed 0 of 74\": Cells has no `wasm` test target (\"unknown Spiral backend wasm\"), which made it a vacuous green. ///> wasm dropped (Wasm prims are covered by the prim_to_string_all matrix row), and x fixed lib_gate to exit 2 on executed=0. CORPUS: every x call site (sim + client, ~90) passes integers, whose Gleam text is unchanged (format_debug of Int = PrimToString digits; floats keep the fallback). fiscal's are `invalid` counts on Rust/F# with no gleam/lua tests. No consumer prints a Gleam bool or char through obj_to_string. LAND: land-lib-program.ps1 -Program %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\spiral\\BACKEND-PRIMITIVE-PARITY\\4-obj-to-string-prim-collapse.kitdo -File \"sm'.spi\" -Base 6F13BB8631C118813FBA75292805AADE89C3D248993C79828758319C270C0E40 -Target D90DA3E64D826F44FF3A47F33BB8FCBB1DCD4549A8E153C01C515BCECBF09D53, once `kit window list` shows no held heavy window.")
inl note_of_backend_primitive_parity_16 () = Note ("BACKEND-PRIMITIVE-PARITY", 16u32, "2026-11-02T14:59Z", "compiler", "11-02 10:55 compiler (groom, main star-exchange lesson 9): the pending sm'.spi collapse land closes with document_test (a double blank line from an earlier land once broke the notebook round-trip for the next lane). Run order: land-lib-program.ps1 ... then the document_test gate on sm'.spi, then the land counts as done.")
inl note_of_backend_primitive_parity_17 () = Note ("BACKEND-PRIMITIVE-PARITY", 17u32, "2026-11-02T19:03Z", "compiler", "11-02 15:15 compiler: obj_to_string COLLAPSE LANDED. sm'.spi 6F13BB86 -> D90DA3E64D826F44 through land-lib-program (undo kit\\undo\\4-obj-to-string-prim-collapse), with no live lib reader; the closing document_test passes (Result: 22 passed). Every lib backend_switch arm now prints bool/char/ints through !!!!PrimToString, so the lib matches C on all 11 arms. Cpp stays out (no PrimToString arm).")
inl note_of_backend_primitive_parity_18 () = Note ("BACKEND-PRIMITIVE-PARITY", 18u32, "2026-11-03T08:59Z", "compiler", "11-03 05:0x compiler GROOM (run start; Active = touched in 12 h). Parked to Planned (14 h). Next: a Cpp PrimToString arm (the last lib backend_switch arm off C parity), then rerun the op x type x backend census on pack23 and close or list the residue.")
inl note_of_backend_primitive_parity_19 () = Note ("BACKEND-PRIMITIVE-PARITY", 19u32, "2026-11-04T17:51Z", "compiler", "11-04 13:5x compiler (relaying tibia, no action asked; same probe as the Rust dead-literal note on RUST-INLINE-SINGLE-USE n9): literal concatenation folds differently per backend. lib unsupported::no_arm builds failwith (~> sm'_real::(++#*) name \": no arm for this backend\"); called with \"env.process_id\": C emits the folded literal; Rust emits the folded literal plus two dead thread_local literals; Lua emits 'local v0 = \"env.process_id\"; local v1 = \": no arm ...\"; local v2 = v0 .. v1; return _G.error(v2)', concatenating at RUN time. Runtime message identical everywhere. So static-literal folding is a per-backend accident (C's and Rust's literal passes) rather than peval's job; parity fix: fold StaticStringConcat/++# of two literals in peval (or a shared post-pass) so every backend gets the literal. Repro: tibia scratch cuprobe\\lib\\spiral\\noarm3_identity.spi, outputs n3-before.Lua vs n3-after.Lua.")
inl note_of_backend_primitive_parity_20 () = Note ("BACKEND-PRIMITIVE-PARITY", 20u32, "2026-11-04T17:53Z", "compiler", "11-04 13:5x compiler: ROOT CAUSE of the per-backend literal folding (n19). peval already folds !!!!StaticStringConcat of two literals (spiral_compiler.fs ~12221: DLit a, DLit b -> DLit(a+b)). But lib sm'_real.spir append_real (behind ++# and ++#*) uses that op only in its C arm (C = !!!!StaticStringConcat(s, x)); every other arm is a text macro ($'!s .. !x ' for Lua, $'Rc::<str>::from(format!(...))' for Rust, ...), and peval cannot fold macro text. Rust then folds the format! in its own literal pass, leaving the two inputs as dead thread_local statics. FIX (lib, no compiler change): use !!!!StaticStringConcat(s, x) in every append_real arm whose backend implements the op in codegen. It is implemented for F#, Rust, Pascal, Zig, Lean, Gleam, Lua, Wasm, Python, C and the others listed at spiral_compiler.fs 13305, 14680, 15574, 16338, 16932, 18171, 18942, 20027, 21006, 22987, 23753, 25213, 26207, 28399. Map each line to its backend before editing. Proof: lib gate over sm'_real and its consumers on every backend, plus examples on each backend 0 behavior cells; residual changes expected wherever two literals are concatenated. Deferred: ++# is used on every backend, so the gate is heavy and the gain is cosmetic and small (dead statics and one runtime concat per literal pair).")
inl note_of_backend_primitive_parity_21 () = Note ("BACKEND-PRIMITIVE-PARITY", 21u32, "2026-11-05T05:39Z", "compiler", "11-05 01:4x compiler: DRAFT 5 written + round-tripped (not landed, not proven). %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\spiral\\BACKEND-PRIMITIVE-PARITY\\5-append-real-static-concat.kitdo replaces append_real's 13-arm backend_switch in sm'_real.spir with the op itself: ':= append_real (x : string) (s : string) : string = !!!!StaticStringConcat(s, x)'. Every arm's text matches what that backend's codegen emits for the op, with the same operand order (mapped by codegen section): Fsharp (a + b) 13321, Rust Rc::<str>::from(format!) 14698 identical, Pascal a + b 15661, Zig spiralConcat 16448, Lean (a ++ b) 17082, Bend String.append 18321, Gleam { a } <> { b } 19182, Lua (a .. b) 20267, Kotlin/Swift (a + b) 23236/24002, TypeScript a + b 25462, C StringConcat 26466 (already the op), Python a + b 28659. Gains: peval folds literal ++ literal on every backend, not just C; Wasm (codegen has (call $str_concat ...) at 21246) gets ++# for the first time, since append_real had no Wasm arm. Applies on live sm'_real.spir (d682cca6 -> 1e643be2). lib_roundtrip.exs on the sm'.spi + sm'_real.spir pair: canonical true, fixpoint true. PROOF still needed and heavy (++# is everywhere): lib gate of sm'.spi and its consumers on every backend + examples on each backend, 0 behavior cells, with residual changes expected only where two literals meet. Node after TRAIN27, box last.")
inl note_of_backend_primitive_parity_22 () = Note ("BACKEND-PRIMITIVE-PARITY", 22u32, "2026-11-05T08:40Z", "compiler", "11-05 04:4x compiler: DRAFT 5 PROVEN + LANDED (unstaged). sm'_real.spir append_real = !!!!StaticStringConcat(s, x) (the 13-arm backend_switch is gone). Proof: lib_gate of sm'.spi on a lib copy with the draft (box, installed pack43, daemon compiler): 'tests: passed 83 failed 0 of 83', type errors 0, across sm's test targets. lib_land_window closed GREEN: apps/spiral compiles, document_test 26 passed; type_probe and respell_drift print nothing for a .spir. Live sm'_real.spir 1E643BE2F38C (base D682CCA6), lib digest 06771DEDFB16ECFB, undo spiral-bin\\lib-land-undo\\20261105-043746. Effects: peval now folds literal ++ literal on every backend (it did only on C), and Wasm gets ++# through its existing codegen ((call $str_concat ...)), since append_real had no Wasm arm before. The core-only sample suites don't touch this lib function, so no residual changes there.")
inl char_ordering () = Task ("CHAR-ORDERING", "char = a Unicode scalar value on every backend (main 10-31): ordering and char <-> integer conversion everywhere, with explicit byte-vs-scalar string APIs", Story, P0, D3, 100u32, Done, "PACK-13", "team:compiler,compiler,language,backends,self-hosting,typefeatureset", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_char_ordering () = Planning ("CHAR-ORDERING", Minutes 600u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_char_ordering_1 () = Note ("CHAR-ORDERING", 1u32, "2026-10-31T20:07Z", "compiler", "10-31 10-31 16:07 compiler: filed from the self-hosting groom (user: conversion gaps are 'unacceptable by now'). MEASURED on the installed compiler (PACK-10), sample with packages |core-: ':= ~c = 'b' / := ~d = 'a' / ? c > d ...' fails on C, Rust AND F# with 'Got: char | Fails to satisfy: number' (so it's the language's constraint, not one backend); the same program with '=' compiles. Every lexer in the self-hosted compiler needs 'a' <= c <= 'z' style tests. Design options: (a) a comparable/ordered constraint that both number and char satisfy, with each backend's codegen comparing chars as code points (C: int32_t code point, Rust char, F# char, Gleam/Lua/Wasm ints); (b) char -> i32 / i32 -> char conversions as portable ops and compare the ints (a lib-level workaround, weaker). Also UNVERIFIED and to check first: whether char <-> integer conversion compiles on every backend (my probe's conv spelling was wrong, so it only produced a parse error). Done when ordering and conversion compile and agree with C on every backend in the matrix lists, with one regression sample.")
inl note_of_char_ordering_2 () = Note ("CHAR-ORDERING", 2u32, "2026-10-31T20:21Z", "compiler", "10-31 10-31 16:21 compiler: ROOT CAUSES MEASURED (installed PACK-10, packages |core-, --backend C). (1) ORDERING is only the core library's constraint: core.spi:15-21 declares (<) (<=) (>) (>=) as 'forall t {number}', and char isn't a number; the raw primitive '!!!!LT(d, c)' on two chars COMPILES to C (exit 0), so the backend support exists. Fix direction: an ordering constraint that char satisfies (or char-specific comparison functions over !!!!LT/LTE/GT/GTE), then agree-with-C rows on every backend. (2) CONVERSION is a COMPILER gap in partial evaluation, not the library: '!!!!Conv(i32, c)' on a char -> 'TracedError: Cannot convert char to the following type: i32', and '!!!!Conv(char, 99i32)' -> 'Cannot convert the literal to the following type: char' (core.spi:100's conv also requires {number} on both sides). Fix direction: PartEval's Conv accepts char <-> integer (code point semantics), plus each backend's TyConv for char (C int32_t, Rust 'as u32'/char::from_u32, F# int/char, Gleam/Lua/Wasm ints). Order: the Conv fix first (it's a core change for the next pack), then the ordering constraint.")
inl note_of_char_ordering_3 () = Note ("CHAR-ORDERING", 3u32, "2026-10-31T20:27Z", "compiler", "10-31 10-31 16:27 compiler: DEEPER GAP FOUND, a semantics decision before any code. char's runtime representation differs by backend (prim* mappings in the live core): ONE BYTE on C/C++ ('char', 25134/26003), Rust ('u8', 13133), Zig ('u8', 15101), Delphi ('AnsiChar', 14576); a UTF-16 code unit on F# ('char', 12554); a Unicode scalar value on Lean ('Char', 15832); a one-character STRING on Gleam and Lua (17610, 18506); a 32-bit int on WGSL, Kotlin, Swift, OpenQASM, eBPF (20480..23454). So 'char -> i32' is not one operation until char is: 'λ' or any non-ASCII char already means different things per backend (a lexer over UTF-8 sees bytes on C/Rust but code units on F#). PROPOSAL (needs the user's or main's decision, since it's a language-semantics change): char = a Unicode scalar value (u21 range, 'i32' payload) on every backend, with string indexing staying byte-based as today (the C oracle's UTF-8). Then Conv char <-> integer = scalar value, the ordering constraint compares scalar values, and the 17 TyConv emitters that need a char case are: Fsharp 12885, Rust 14011, Delphi 14856, Zig 15583, Lean 16127, Bend 17274+, Gleam 18069, Lua 19180, Wasm 20048, Wgsl 20818, Calyx 21321, Kotlin 22034, Swift 22812, OpenQasm 23332, Ebpf 23657, TypeScript 24356, Python 27517 (C/C++ cast generically). The alternative (char = byte everywhere, matching the C oracle) is simpler for the byte backends but breaks F#/Lean/Gleam's char literals beyond ASCII. Estimate after the decision: ~6 h (PartEval Conv + LitChar folding, 17 emitters, a sample per direction, matrix rows on every backend).")
inl note_of_char_ordering_4 () = Note ("CHAR-ORDERING", 4u32, "2026-10-31T20:38Z", "compiler", "10-31 10-31 16:38 compiler: CONTEXT from PORTABLE-PRIMITIVES n27-n28 (main, 10-30): main decided to leave CharT's per-backend representation as it is ('the first real consumer sets the shape'), and code points already have a lib path: sm'.char_code (sm'.spi ~1131) with F#, Python, Rust, TypeScript and Lua arms (Lua decodes UTF-8 itself), utf8_get_bytes (Rust, else .NET) and utf8_byte_list (Gleam, Rust). Its rule was 'add an arm when a caller on that backend needs it'. The user's 10-31 direction ('no conversion gaps, unacceptable by now') overrides that rule for the self-hosting gate, so: EITHER the compiler-level fix of n3 (char = a Unicode scalar value everywhere, Conv char <-> integer in PartEval, ordering constraint), OR complete the lib path on every backend (char_code + its inverse + ordering helpers, with the missing Gleam, Delphi, C and Zig arms). The compiler-level fix removes the per-backend divergence at its root and is what the gate asks for; the lib path keeps divergent CharT and only papers over it. Recommendation: compiler-level, decided with main (it overturns main's 10-30 call).")
inl note_of_char_ordering_5 () = Note ("CHAR-ORDERING", 5u32, "2026-10-31T20:42Z", "compiler", "DECISION (main, 10-31 10-31 16:42, relayed to compiler): char = a Unicode scalar value on every backend. This supersedes main's 10-30 PORTABLE-PRIMITIVES n27-n28 call: the user's 10-31 self-hosting direction ('no conversion gaps, unacceptable') is newer, and CHAR-ORDERING's measurements show today's per-backend CharT is exactly such a gap. SCOPE (next compiler pack): Conv char <-> integer in PartEval (+ LitChar folding), an ordering constraint chars satisfy, and a char case in the 17 TyConv emitters, with CharT's representation moved to a scalar value on the byte backends (C/C++/Rust/Zig/Delphi) and the string backends (Gleam/Lua). CONDITION: string APIs that index or slice today (byte offsets on the native backends, the C oracle's UTF-8) keep an EXPLICIT meaning: either they stay byte/UTF-8 based and are named so, or they move to scalar indexing in the same pack, never silently. GATE: lib/spiral plus the consumer corpus (tibia, kit, fiscal) with the candidate DLL (packs/lib-gate-dll.ps1), so a char that used to be a byte can't silently change a consumer's string code; plus every backend's char samples agreeing with C.")
inl note_of_char_ordering_6 () = Note ("CHAR-ORDERING", 6u32, "2026-10-31T20:44Z", "compiler", "10-31 10-31 16:44 compiler: SCOPE ADDITION found while drafting the PartEval half: the compiler's own literal is 'LitChar of char' (spiral_compiler.fs:932), a .NET char = one UTF-16 code unit, so a char literal ABOVE U+FFFF (any astral scalar, e.g. an emoji) can't even be represented today, and folding Conv to char can't produce one. 'char = a Unicode scalar value' therefore also means LitChar carries a scalar (System.Text.Rune, or an int validated to 0..0x10FFFF minus surrogates); that touches all 47 LitChar references (the lexer's char literal, litXxx emitters per backend, Conv folding, equality folding). PLAN ORDER for the pack: (1) LitChar -> Rune (mechanical, every backend's literal printer: C/Zig/Rust/Delphi print the scalar value or a UTF-8 escape per backend; F# 'char' can't hold astral values either, so F#'s CharT becomes Rune/int too); (2) CharT runtime representation = scalar on all backends; (3) Conv char <-> integer in PartEval and the 17 TyConv emitters; (4) the ordering constraint; (5) string index/slice APIs kept byte-based and named so (main's condition), or moved to scalar in the same pack; (6) gate = lib + tibia + kit + fiscal on the candidate DLL plus a char sample per direction on every backend. Estimate revised 480m -> 900m.")
inl note_of_char_ordering_7 () = Note ("CHAR-ORDERING", 7u32, "2026-10-31T21:41Z", "compiler", "10-31 10-31 17:41 compiler: STEP (1) SIZED, the LitChar -> scalar checklist from the live core (47 references; literalStableText 28035 goes away with PACK-11, leaving 46). TYPE: 'LitChar of char' (932, beside globalIdStableText 949). LEXER/PLUMBING: char_quoted 1317 (parse the literal into a scalar; astral values need surrogate-pair or \\\\u{...} handling), token_groups 994, show_lit 1022, lit 4515, lit_to_primitive_type 9321. PARTIAL EVALUATION (8): peval 10224, 10922, 10978, 11267, 11293, 11319, 11345, 11372 (equality/compare folding and Conv, which step (3) extends). FORMATTER (2): FormatterCheck 30021, FormatterFormat 33163 (print the literal back as written). LITERAL PRINTERS, one per backend: litFsharp 12528, litRust 13107, litDelphi 14556, litZig 15082, litLean 15815, codegenBend 17071, litGleam 17583 (+ codegenGleam 18164-18167), litLua 18479, codegenWasm 19855, codegenWgsl 20587, codegenCalyx 21072, codegenKotlin 21851, codegenSwift 22620, codegenOpenQasm 23173, codegenEbpf 23488, litTypeScript 23852, CodegenC 25158, CodegenCpp 26395, CodegenPython 27155. Each printer emits the scalar in its target's form (C/Zig: integer code point; Rust: char literal or from_u32; F#: needs Rune/int since F# char is UTF-16; Gleam/Lua: the scalar's UTF-8 string while CharT is still a string there, an int after step (2)).")
inl note_of_char_ordering_8 () = Note ("CHAR-ORDERING", 8u32, "2026-11-01T03:41Z", "compiler", "10-31 10-31 23:41 compiler, cadence groom: TOP-3 CPU-LIGHT #3. Next concrete step: LitChar checklist n7 step 1, change LitChar of char to a scalar (Rune) in the core AST and list every match site it breaks (a grep census, no build), so the 17 TyConv emitters and the PartEval Conv char<->int land in one applier.")
inl note_of_char_ordering_9 () = Note ("CHAR-ORDERING", 9u32, "2026-11-01T06:13Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 02:13 compiler, round 3: STEP 1 CENSUS DONE (build-free, spiral_compiler.fs on core C0990B2D590CE965). 'LitChar of char' (Tokenize 932) has 40 match sites in 18 sections, plus 110 CharT sites. Per section: Tokenize 5 (932 decl, 949 show, 994 semantic tokens), Infer 1 (4513 -> TyPrim CharT), PartEval 9 (9319 type of; 10236 equality fold; 10935 to DSymbol; 10991 STRING INDEX = 'bytes.[b] |> char |> LitChar', the byte-based index the decision must keep explicit or move to scalars; 11280/11306/11332/11358/11385 the comparison folds), Formatter 2, and one emit site each in CodegenFsharp 12540, Rust 13119, Delphi 14568, Zig 15094, Lean 15827, Bend 17083, Lua 18491, Wasm 19867, TypeScript 23864, C 25170, Cpp 26407, Python 27167, plus Gleam 5 and Wgsl 6. FINDING: PartEval already FOLDS literal char comparisons (11280-11385), so 'ordering fails on every backend' comes from the type side (no ordering constraint on CharT in Infer) and the runtime path, not from literal folding. PLAN for the applier: (a) LitChar of char -> LitChar of System.Text.Rune (scalar) at 932, with show/token sites; (b) the 9 PartEval sites compare Rune values; 10991 keeps bytes and returns a byte-valued Rune, explicit; (c) each emitter's one site prints the scalar for its CharT (C/Rust/Zig/Delphi: u32 or one byte per today's CharT; F# Rune; Lean Char; Gleam/Lua String); (d) Infer: add the ordering constraint for CharT. About 40 + 18 edits, all text, as one kit do program.")
inl note_of_char_ordering_10 () = Note ("CHAR-ORDERING", 10u32, "2026-11-01T06:19Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 02:19 compiler, round 3: ROOT CAUSE FOUND and STEP (d) DRAFTED. Why char ordering fails: the core prelude declares (<) (<=) (>) (>=) with {number} (apps/compiler/tmp/samples/core/operators.spi:19-28; upstream's corebase/core.spi does the same), and CNumber (Infer 5505) excludes CharT. PartEval itself already accepts char LT both folded (11280) and runtime (11286, any equal primitive type). FIX (upstream-compatible: {number} keeps its meaning, so upstream code still compiles): a new constraint 'ord' = the numeric prims + CharT. Two less obvious edits it needs: (1) a forall var declared {number}/{int}/{sint}/{uint}/{float} must satisfy {ord} too, or the 18 lib functions with '\\* t {number}' that call '<' (float_native is_positive, am' average, file_system loops...) break with ForallVarConstraintError (5526 checks IsSubsetOf), so the check uses the implied set; (2) literal defaulting (6664) tests 'constraints = {number}', and '1 < 2' would now carry {number; ord} and stop defaulting to int, so it compares after removing ord. Dry-applied: 11 replacements (7 core, 4 operators.spi), and every Constraint match is covered (constraint_name, constraint_kind, the body check, the name table). STILL NEEDED for runtime char '<' (step c): emitters whose CharT isn't natively ordered: Gleam (String has no '<': use string.compare) and Wgsl/Calyx subsets; C/Rust/Zig/F#/Lua/Python/TS compare natively. Gate = lib + tibia + kit + fiscal (the decision's gate), via BACKEND-BATCH.\nPROGRAM (kit do <dir holding spiral_compiler.fs and core/operators.spi> -):\nedit spiral_compiler.fs expect files 1 replacements 7\n  replace once <<<\n        | CNumber\n        | CPrim\n        | CSymbol\n  >>> with <<<\n        | CNumber\n        | COrd\n        | CPrim\n        | CSymbol\n  >>>\n  replace once <<<\n        | CFloat -> \"float\" | CNumber -> \"number\" | CPrim -> \"prim\"\n  >>> with <<<\n        | CFloat -> \"float\" | CNumber -> \"number\" | COrd -> \"ord\" | CPrim -> \"prim\"\n  >>>\n  replace once <<<\n        | CSInt | CUInt | CInt | CFloat | CNumber | CPrim | CSymbol | CRecord -> KindType\n  >>> with <<<\n        | CSInt | CUInt | CInt | CFloat | CNumber | COrd | CPrim | CSymbol | CRecord -> KindType\n  >>>\n  replace once <<<\n                    | CNumber, TyPrim (UInt8T | UInt16T | UInt32T | UInt64T | Int8T | Int16T | Int32T | Int64T | Float32T | Float64T)\n                    | CPrim, TyPrim _\n  >>> with <<<\n                    | CNumber, TyPrim (UInt8T | UInt16T | UInt32T | UInt64T | Int8T | Int16T | Int32T | Int64T | Float32T | Float64T)\n                    | COrd, TyPrim (UInt8T | UInt16T | UInt32T | UInt64T | Int8T | Int16T | Int32T | Int64T | Float32T | Float64T | CharT)\n                    | CPrim, TyPrim _\n  >>>\n  replace once <<<\n                | TyVar (b,_) -> if con.IsSubsetOf b.constraints = false then [ForallVarConstraintError(TypeNameIdOps.create b.name,con,b.constraints)] else []\n  >>> with <<<\n                | TyVar (b,_) ->\n                    let implied = if Set.exists (function CNumber | CInt | CSInt | CUInt | CFloat -> true | _ -> false) b.constraints then Set.add COrd b.constraints else b.constraints\n                    if con.IsSubsetOf implied = false then [ForallVarConstraintError(TypeNameIdOps.create b.name,con,b.constraints)] else []\n  >>>\n  replace once <<<\n                        if m.constraints = Set.singleton CNumber then default_numeric \"int\" |> Option.iter (fun t -> link.Value <- Some t)\n                        elif m.constraints = Set.singleton CFloat then default_numeric \"float\" |> Option.iter (fun t -> link.Value <- Some t)\n  >>> with <<<\n                        let numeric = Set.remove COrd m.constraints\n                        if numeric = Set.singleton CNumber then default_numeric \"int\" |> Option.iter (fun t -> link.Value <- Some t)\n                        elif numeric = Set.singleton CFloat then default_numeric \"float\" |> Option.iter (fun t -> link.Value <- Some t)\n  >>>\n  replace once <<<\n                \"number\", CNumber\n                \"prim\", CPrim\n  >>> with <<<\n                \"number\", CNumber\n                \"ord\", COrd\n                \"prim\", CPrim\n  >>>\nedit operators.spi expect files 1 replacements 4\n  replace once <<<\n:= (<) \\* t {number}. (a : t) (b : t) : bool =\n  >>> with <<<\n:= (<) \\* t {ord}. (a : t) (b : t) : bool =\n  >>>\n  replace once <<<\n:= (<=) \\* t {number}. (a : t) (b : t) : bool =\n  >>> with <<<\n:= (<=) \\* t {ord}. (a : t) (b : t) : bool =\n  >>>\n  replace once <<<\n:= (>) \\* t {number}. (a : t) (b : t) : bool =\n  >>> with <<<\n:= (>) \\* t {ord}. (a : t) (b : t) : bool =\n  >>>\n  replace once <<<\n:= (>=) \\* t {number}. (a : t) (b : t) : bool =\n  >>> with <<<\n:= (>=) \\* t {ord}. (a : t) (b : t) : bool =\n  >>>")
inl note_of_char_ordering_11 () = Note ("CHAR-ORDERING", 11u32, "2026-11-01T06:20Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 02:20 compiler: CORRECTION to step (c) above: Gleam ALREADY orders chars. codegenGleam 18176-18179 send LT/LTE/GT/GTE on StringT|CharT (and LitChar literals) to string.compare, which is UTF-8 byte order and therefore code-point order. Every other emitter's CharT is natively ordered (bytes / UTF-16 / Lean Char / int32 / Lua strings), so once the ord constraint lands, char ordering should run end to end on the existing emitters. Remaining for the full item: the scalar move (LitChar -> Rune, steps a-c of n9) and the Conv char<->int, both separate from ordering. So ordering can ship first, alone, in PACK-12 (after BACKEND-BATCH's lib+tibia+kit+fiscal gate), with a sample: 'a' < 'b' and a runtime char compare.")
inl note_of_char_ordering_12 () = Note ("CHAR-ORDERING", 12u32, "2026-11-01T06:21Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 02:21 compiler: SAMPLE DRAFTED, held OUT of the tree on purpose: the installed compiler rejects it today ({number} on <), and C/Rust rows are default-on, so adding it now would turn every lane's accept run red until the pack lands. It enters samples/char_ordering together with the ord program in PACK-12. Installed formatter 'format --check' = exit 0. Expected: exit 0 on every backend that runs it; the nested ifs return 1-4 to show which comparison failed. package.spiproj = 'packages: / core- / modules: / main'. TEXT (samples/char_ordering/main.spi):\n:= main () : i32 =\n    := ~a = 'a'\n    := ~b = 'b'\n    ? a < b ?>\n        ? b > a ?>\n            ? a <= a ?>\n                ? 'x' >= 'w' ?> 0i32 |: 4i32\n            |: 3i32\n        |: 2i32\n    |: 1i32")
inl note_of_char_ordering_13 () = Note ("CHAR-ORDERING", 13u32, "2026-11-01T07:22Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 03:22 compiler: pack12b INSTALLED 03:14 (main), so the COrd constraint, the {number}=>ord implication and the defaulting change are live. TWO CORES, so the switch is two steps: (1) SAMPLES core (apps/compiler/tmp/samples/core/operators.spi, packages 'core-') affects only matrix samples. Its A/B is running on the node in my mirror ordsw (job-1793517757741): arm A = full -Suite all on the installed pack12b, then the 4 operators switch to {ord} + samples/char_ordering added in the MIRROR only, then arm B; it reports compile-status, residual and verdict diffs plus the char_ordering rows. The live tree is untouched until it's green. (2) LIB core = '|core-' = deps/The-Spiral-Language/VS Code Plugin/core/core.spi:15-21 (our fork of upstream, commit cc1b12ac), used by lib, tibia, kit and fiscal. Switching it gives the whole lib char ordering, but that fork's core.spi then needs our compiler: upstream's compiler has no 'ord'. Our compiler still accepts upstream's {number} core, so backward compatibility holds; forward compatibility (upstream compiling our fork's core) doesn't. That's the user's 'keep upstream core compatibility in mind' line, so step (2) is flagged for a decision and gated on lib+tibia+kit+fiscal. FOLLOW-UP for PACK-13: an unresolved metavar with ONLY {ord} isn't defaulted (6664 now strips ord and needs {number}). Before, (<) imposed {number}, which defaulted to int; add 'or constraints = {ord} -> int' to keep that.")
inl note_of_char_ordering_14 () = Note ("CHAR-ORDERING", 14u32, "2026-11-01T07:41Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 03:41 compiler: STEP (1) LANDED 03:40, SAMPLES CORE: apps/compiler/tmp/samples/core/operators.spi (<) (<=) (>) (>=) now {ord} (09D3BCD4CD2F6D11 -> 1CBF689E0842CDF4), plus samples/char_ordering (main.spi + package.spiproj), all unstaged. PROOF (node mirror ordsw, installed pack12b): two full -Suite all runs, A = {number} (single-flight-20261101-032241) and B = {ord} + sample (-033255). 7,405 shared rows: compile-status diffs 0, residual diffs 0, timeouts 0. char_ordering compiles on C, Delphi, F# and Rust in B (the old core would reject it, so B really used {ord}). Natively on the node: C exit 0, Rust exit 0, and the C output compares chars at runtime ('bool v2 = v0 < v1;'). STEP (2), the LIB core (deps/The-Spiral-Language/VS Code Plugin/core/core.spi:15-21), is NOT done: it needs the user's call on forward upstream compatibility (upstream's compiler can't read {ord}), then the lib+tibia+kit+fiscal gate. Until then lib code can't use < on chars.")
inl note_of_char_ordering_15 () = Note ("CHAR-ORDERING", 15u32, "2026-11-01T11:51Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 07:51 compiler: DECIDED (user 11-01: 'decide this autonomously'): the LIB core switches to {ord}. REASON, measured: our fork's core (deps/The-Spiral-Language, origin i574n, vs mrakgr/master) already diverges in 29 files (+1172/-704) and already uses ops upstream's compiler doesn't know (LayoutToHeapRefs, LayoutToStackRefs, UnsafeBackendSwitch; git grep on mrakgr/master *.fs). So forward compatibility (upstream compiling our core) was already gone, and {ord} costs nothing new there. The direction that matters stays intact: our compiler still accepts upstream's {number} core and upstream code, since COrd is additive and {number} implies ord. The lib-helper alternative (char.lt etc.) would leave '<' on chars a type error in lib, against the 'conversion gaps unacceptable' gate. PLAN: (a) PACK-13's ord-defaulting fix FIRST, so an {ord}-only metavar still defaults to int, which matches today's behaviour exactly; (b) switch core.spi:15-21 in a copy and gate lib + tibia + kit + fiscal on that copy (never through the node mirror's deps junction, which points at the shared, stale node checkout); (c) land in deps/The-Spiral-Language (commit left to the user, unstaged).")
inl note_of_char_ordering_16 () = Note ("CHAR-ORDERING", 16u32, "2026-11-01T11:56Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 07:56 compiler: LIB-CORE GATE RUNNING on the node, both arms in parallel on fresh mirrors (libord-a/-b, synced 07:5x, digest E1E8FFC64F1E7AE1), installed pack12b, never via the mirrors' deps junction: lib_sweep.ps1 -Candidate <core copy> over all 73 lib test files. Control = box's live core copy (core.spi 6631368E734BE060), job-1793534174603; ord = the same with the 4 operators on {ord} (6892459D72F539D6, program core-ord.kitdo), job-1793534183205. Outputs: node C:\\home\\remote\\libord-cores\\sweep-{control,ord}.tsv. READING: per file, passed/failed/type_errors must be equal across arms. Any new type error in ord (esp. ValueRestriction from an {ord}-only metavar) means PACK-13's defaulting fix ships before the switch. tibia/kit/fiscal come after a clean lib sweep.")
inl note_of_char_ordering_17 () = Note ("CHAR-ORDERING", 17u32, "2026-11-01T12:34Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 08:34 compiler, ROUND 4 QUALITY SPEC: FIELDS: P0 (self-hosting gate: 'conversion gaps unacceptable'), est 600m left, load medium (node sweeps), dep PACK-13 (ord defaulting, installed). DEFINITION OF DONE, all three: (a) lib core switched: the node sweeps sweep-control.tsv vs sweep-ord.tsv (C:\\home\\remote\\libord-cores) show equal passed/failed/type_errors per file over all 73 lib test files, then core.spi:15-21 lands in deps/The-Spiral-Language (unstaged); (b) a lib test 'char_ordering_runs' in lib/spiral/sm'.spi ('a' < 'b', a runtime char compare) is green on rust, gleam, c, zig and fsharp via apps/cells/spi/lib_gate.ps1 -File sm'.spi -Test char_ordering_runs; (c) Conv char<->int: !!!!Conv(char -> i32) and back compile on all 17 TyConv emitters, proven by a sample samples/char_scalar_conv (exit 0 on every backend list it joins, agreeing with C). NEXT STEP: when job-1793534174603/-183205 finish (~10:15), diff the two TSVs (Import-Csv, join on file). TYPEFEATURESET PLAN: (1) the constraint is a union case COrd in Infer's Constraint (done), never a string tag; (2) the scalar is a TYPE: LitChar of System.Text.Rune in the core (not an int with -1 for 'invalid'; invalid scalars can't be constructed); (3) lib keeps byte-based and scalar-based string offsets apart with PHANTOM nominals, ':! byte_offset = i32' and ':! scalar_offset = i32', so sm'.index takes byte_offset and a new sm'.char_at takes scalar_offset: mixing them is a type error, not a runtime bug; (4) Conv emitters dispatch on a (source CharT, target IntT) pair in a union, not on strings. Model: lib/spiral/unicode.spi (already has unicode_types) and sm'.")
inl note_of_char_ordering_18 () = Note ("CHAR-ORDERING", 18u32, "2026-11-01T13:20Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 09:20 compiler: LIB-CORE SWEEP VERDICT = VOID, so no land. Both node sweeps finished (control 83 min, ord 72 min), but 62 of 73 control rows and 61 of 73 ord rows have NO 'tests:' line (unreadable, the lib-gate-needs-test-rows rule: an empty row is never green). The node was loaded (x verify46, fiscal e3-regen, Cells orphans). Readable rows only: control passed 64 / failed 6 / type errors 0; ord passed 149 / failed 0 / type errors 0. In every file readable in both arms (am', erlang_port, erlang_process), ord is equal or better (am' control 12/6 vs ord 18/0, a load failure in control), and NO readable ord row has a new failure or type error. Encouraging, not a gate. NEXT: rerun both arms SEQUENTIALLY in one job when the node is quieter (or on the box in a held window), with lib_sweep reading -RawOut so empty rows can be diagnosed; land only on 73/73 readable rows with equal counts.")
inl note_of_char_ordering_19 () = Note ("CHAR-ORDERING", 19u32, "2026-11-01T13:37Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 09:37 compiler: LIB-CORE A/B RERUN started, SEQUENTIAL on an idle node (0 other jobs at start), installed pack14a (node digest 0AF644516C09E959, includes PACK-13's ord defaulting): job-1793540254707, fresh mirror libord-seq (digest E910E536DDABA732). Control core 6631368E734BE060 (= live) then ord core 6892459D72F539D6 -> node C:\\home\\remote\\libord-cores\\seq-{control,ord}.tsv. LAND RULE unchanged: 73/73 readable rows in both arms with equal passed/failed/type_errors per file.")
inl note_of_char_ordering_20 () = Note ("CHAR-ORDERING", 20u32, "2026-11-01T14:43Z", "compiler", "11-01 10:43 compiler: sequential A/B job-1793540254707 still in the CONTROL arm after 66 min (started 09:37:35, peak 5.0 GB private); no rows read yet. Land rule unchanged. The groom round did not touch the node.")
inl note_of_char_ordering_21 () = Note ("CHAR-ORDERING", 21u32, "2026-11-01T17:43Z", "compiler", "11-01 13:45 compiler: SWEEP DONE, LAND RULE MET. job-1793540254707-compiler-libord-seq: control 09:37-11:52 (73 rows, 0 unreadable), ord 11:52-13:40 (73 rows, 0 unreadable). Per-file comparison of seq-control.tsv vs seq-ord.tsv: 0 of 73 files differ in passed, failed or type_errors. Totals are identical: 627 passed / 61 failed / 0 type errors in both (the 61 failures are the same per file, so the {ord} change is invisible there, as predicted). Arm bases verified on the node: control core.spi 6631368E734BE060 (= live), ord 6892459D72F539D6. LANDING: background shell bfer85gyh waits for the heavy-build window (kit-TRAIN7c compiles with core.spi) and the tree-write window to clear. It then applies scratchpad libord\\core-ord.kitdo under a tree-write window, but only if live core.spi still hashes 6631368E... and the result hashes 6892459D... (the swept bytes); an undo copy goes beside the script. The change is unstaged in deps/The-Spiral-Language. NEXT after the land: gate tibia, kit and fiscal on the new core.")
inl note_of_char_ordering_22 () = Note ("CHAR-ORDERING", 22u32, "2026-11-01T19:37Z", "compiler", "11-01 15:35 compiler: LANDED 15:26. deps/The-Spiral-Language/VS Code Plugin/core/core.spi 6631368E734BE060 -> 6892459D72F539D6 (unstaged in that repo): <=, <, >, >= are now `forall t {ord}` instead of {number}. Landed under tree-write window win-1793561195545, after the heavy-build window (kit-TRAIN7c) cleared, and only after re-checking that the live file still had the swept base hash and the program reproduced the swept ord bytes. Undo copy: session scratchpad libord\\core.spi.undo-6631368E734BE060. CONSUMER GATE RUNNING: `--check` on every tibia (8) and fiscal (82) package with the installed compiler and the new core, under a compile window (scratchpad libord\\consumer-check.ps1, parallel 4). A failure gets the old core as control on that package only, so only differences count. kit's gate is its next release train (it reads core from the same package dir). DONE when tibia and fiscal show no new --check failure and kit's next train has no red that traces to comparisons.")
inl note_of_char_ordering_23 () = Note ("CHAR-ORDERING", 23u32, "2026-11-01T20:11Z", "compiler", "11-01 16:12 compiler: CONSUMER GATE GREEN for tibia and fiscal. On the new core 6892459D72F539D6 with the installed compiler, all 90 packages pass: 8 tibia + 82 fiscal; by mode: check 68, compile 22. Packages that hit --check's 30 s cap (exit 3) were re-proven with a full --backend Rust compile, which has no cap. The first attempt (15:3x) was void: my script did not set SPIRAL_COMPILER_PACKAGE_DIR, so |core- did not resolve and every package was rejected before any type check. No old-core control was needed, since nothing failed. LEFT: kit's next release train on this core (it reads the same core package dir); close when it shows no comparison-related red.")
inl note_of_char_ordering_24 () = Note ("CHAR-ORDERING", 24u32, "2026-11-03T07:30Z", "compiler", "11-03 07:3x compiler GROOM (WIP cap: Active = touched in 12 h). Parked to Planned (35 h). Next: compare the DoD with the 11-01 16:12 consumer gate (90/90 packages green) and close it, or name the one missing proof.")
inl note_of_char_ordering_25 () = Note ("CHAR-ORDERING", 25u32, "2026-11-03T10:39Z", "compiler", "11-03 06:4x compiler: close check. DoD clause 1 met (11-01 16:12: tibia 8 + fiscal 82 packages, --check green on the ord core). Clause 2 (kit's next train has no red tracing to comparisons) asked of kit directly; Done on its answer.")
inl note_of_char_ordering_26 () = Note ("CHAR-ORDERING", 26u32, "2026-11-03T10:40Z", "compiler", "11-03 06:5x compiler: DONE. DoD clause 2 met per kit: the result logs of all eleven kit trains since the 11-01 15:26 ord core change (TRAIN8, 8s, 9, 10, 11, 13, 13b, 13c, 14, 14b, 14c in kit/.cache/release-train) have 0 hits for {ord}, \"ord constraint\" or ConstraintError; the reds in that window had other causes (TRAIN14 a missing Cargo dep, TRAIN14b a crate-path call in a notebook cell), and TRAIN14c installed green 58/58. Clause 1 was met 11-01 16:12 (tibia 8 + fiscal 82 packages green).")
inl lib_spiral_toml_migration () = Task ("LIB-SPIRAL-TOML-MIGRATION", "lib/spiral moves from package.spiproj (hand-kept module tree) to a spiral.toml with only a prelude list (modules derived by the compiler), after every tool that reads package.spiproj directly also accepts spiral.toml", Story, P1, D5, 90u32, Active, "EDITION3-MANIFEST-MODEL", "edition3,lib,tooling,main,kit,team:compiler,fiscal", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_lib_spiral_toml_migration () = Planning ("LIB-SPIRAL-TOML-MIGRATION", Minutes 360u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_lib_spiral_toml_migration_1 () = Note ("LIB-SPIRAL-TOML-MIGRATION", 1u32, "2026-10-31T20:10Z", "main", "10-31 16:10 main. PROVEN EQUIVALENT on the node with the installed pack-10 compiler (node = box D1C49B8BDC3318F7; job-1793473912315): 'format --manifest toml --derive' converts the lib to a 126-byte spiral.toml (packages = ['|core-'], prelude = [am'_prototypes, backend, base, iter_prototypes, sm'_prototypes, trace, common], double-quoted) in place of the 1,896-byte module tree; --check over all 110 lib .spi gives the SAME exit under both manifests (110/110, 0 differ); Cells gates under both pending. NOT LANDED because removing package.spiproj breaks tools that read it directly: a grep of C:\\home\\git finds 40+ files naming package.spiproj, among them apps/cells/spi/type_probe.ps1 (requires it in -Lib; used by every lib land's close), Cells manifest.ex/mounts.ex/cells.ex/domain_compiler.ex, compiler scripts env.ps1/test.ps1/try.ps1/test-rust-exports.ps1, kit standards/contracts/bundle/affected/agile-lease code and release-acceptance.ps1/test-regeneration.ps1, tibia scripts/predict.ps1, fiscal src/generator.spi, i574n.github bang-export-rewrite.ps1. PLAN: (1) per tool, resolve the manifest the way Cells' Manifest.path does (spiral.toml preferred, package.spiproj fallback), each team for its own files; (2) then add the lib spiral.toml and archive package.spiproj in one lib land.")
inl note_of_lib_spiral_toml_migration_2 () = Note ("LIB-SPIRAL-TOML-MIGRATION", 2u32, "2026-10-31T20:47Z", "main", "10-31 16:47 main: NODE PROOF COMPLETE (job-1793473912315, installed pack-10 compiler D1C49B8BDC3318F7): --check over 110 lib files same exit 110/110; Cells gates identical under package.spiproj and the derived spiral.toml (package.spiproj removed): base 25/26 vs 25/26, listm' 32/32 vs 32/32, process 15/15 vs 15/15, type errors 0 everywhere. The modules-free lib manifest is EQUIVALENT for the compiler and for Cells; what remains is the tool migration in n1's inventory.")
inl note_of_lib_spiral_toml_migration_3 () = Note ("LIB-SPIRAL-TOML-MIGRATION", 3u32, "2026-11-01T12:43Z", "main", "main 08:4x SCOPE + DoD (compiler flagged this as under-specified in round 4, and it was). SCOPE: only lib/spiral's own manifest moves, from package.spiproj (hand-kept module tree) to spiral.toml with a prelude list and the module order derived by the installed compiler's --resolve-manifest (pack 10; Cells manifest.ex already reads it, EDITION3-MANIFEST-MODEL Done). x/x_real pairs: until SINGLE-SPI-EXTENSION lands, real modules keep the legacy '*' marker in the derived order; after it, the ///- --real header decides and '*' becomes a warned alias. No other repo's manifest is in scope. THE '40+ TOOLS' FIGURE WAS NEVER COUNTED: a grep (08:4x) finds exactly one script hard-coding lib's package.spiproj, apps/cells/spi/type_probe.ps1, which edits it in its copy to register probe modules. The rest read any package's manifest generically (kit package_inputs/lease, Cells mounts.ex/cells.ex/domain_compiler.ex, the compiler) and must accept spiral.toml. Cells does; kit's package_inputs needs checking. TYPEFEATURESET PLAN: manifest = record { dialect; edition; modules : union Listed of module_name list | Derived of prelude : module_name list } with module_name a nominal, and the derived order a value computed once by the compiler, not re-parsed text. DoD: (1) lib/spiral/spiral.toml present and package.spiproj gone; (2) --check over all lib files gives the same exit per file as before (node proof 16:47 had 110/110); (3) a full node lib sweep matches the pre-move sweep verdict per test row (tonight's baseline: 1,236 passed rows); (4) document_test 22/22 and type_probe PASS on a changed file, with type_probe adding probe modules through the toml; (5) kit agile lease's package inputs hash the toml. NEXT STEP: make type_probe.ps1 register probe modules in spiral.toml (its only lib-manifest edit), then check kit's package_inputs.spi against a toml-only lib copy. FIELDS: estimate 360 (unchanged), load light, owner main.")
inl note_of_lib_spiral_toml_migration_4 () = Note ("LIB-SPIRAL-TOML-MIGRATION", 4u32, "2026-11-01T14:34Z", "main", "main 10:3x, scope widened by the user (11-01: 'get the conclusion for the best version that'll replace the multiline modules ... (you, without my approval please) then migrate everything, test then delete the spiprojs'). DECISION, main's: spiral.toml with a prelude list only and the module order derived by the compiler (--resolve-manifest, pack 10); no multi-line modules string; the dialect implicit when spiral.toml is present; package.spiproj deleted repo by repo after its gates pass. lib/spiral goes first (this item's DoD n3), then every repo via PACKAGE-MODEL-STANDARD's per-lane list.")
inl note_of_lib_spiral_toml_migration_5 () = Note ("LIB-SPIRAL-TOML-MIGRATION", 5u32, "2026-11-02T14:59Z", "main", "main 11-02 lesson from the star exchange (fiscal #1): a lib change that feeds a code generator must regenerate EVERYTHING the generator owns, then run the drift gate, before the land closes. Regenerating only the units in focus left 7 committed files stale and turned fiscal CI red. Applies to SPIPROJ-RETIRE too: fiscal's generator writes package.spiproj into all 81 units.")
inl note_of_lib_spiral_toml_migration_6 () = Note ("LIB-SPIRAL-TOML-MIGRATION", 6u32, "2026-11-02T22:51Z", "tibia", "11-02 18:5x tibia, n1 step (1) for tibia's own tools DONE (unstaged): tibia/scripts/predict.ps1 was the only tibia script reading a manifest (it hard-required apps/predict/package.spiproj and src/package.spiproj). It now goes through Set-Manifests: patches packageDir in whichever of spiral.toml / package.spiproj exists (throws only when neither does), and adds -Module to a modules list only where one exists (a prelude-only spiral.toml needs none). Proof on the node mirror (tibia synced d4ad52ad, job predict-toml-1, -Module lanes): compiled and printed lane 0-3 = -1000121800, -1000138000, -1000600000, -1000600000, identical to the pre-change run. live/paths.ps1 only lists .spiproj among source extensions (a hash input), which is fine either way. Tibia's 9 packages still carry both manifests with modules lists; they move to prelude-only and drop package.spiproj under PACKAGE-MODEL-STANDARD once lib's land is done.")
inl note_of_lib_spiral_toml_migration_7 () = Note ("LIB-SPIRAL-TOML-MIGRATION", 7u32, "2026-11-03T02:06Z", "compiler", "11-02 22:1x compiler: n1 step (1) for the compiler/Cells tools DONE (unstaged). (a) apps/cells/spi/type_probe.ps1, the one script hard-coding lib's manifest and run in every lib land's close: it takes spiral.toml or package.spiproj in -Lib. Under toml the prelude list stands in for the trailing-dash modules, and since the modules are derived the probe adds no manifest line; it deletes each probe file after its compile so one probe's deliberate control error never reaches the next. Box A/B on metrics.spi: package.spiproj lib PASS, 6 tests typed, control ok; a copy with a prelude-only spiral.toml gives the SAME verdict. (b) apps/compiler/scripts/lib-usage-census.ps1 Get-LibModules reads spiral.toml (modules = the .spi/.spir files, flat = in prelude) and falls back to package.spiproj. CORRECTED 22:5x: format <lib> --manifest toml --derive on a full copy of today's live lib succeeds (exit 0; writes lib/spiral.toml prelude = array_prototypes, backend, base, iter_prototypes, text_prototypes, trace, common, plus bench/parsing/spiral.toml). The earlier refusal came from a root-only zip copy that dropped the subfolder modules; there is no drift.")
inl note_of_lib_spiral_toml_migration_8 () = Note ("LIB-SPIRAL-TOML-MIGRATION", 8u32, "2026-11-04T07:32Z", "compiler", "11-04 02:4x compiler: TOOL INVENTORY DONE for the spiral repo and every repo under C:\\home\\git. A grep for code that opens LIB's manifest by path (spiral/package.spiproj, lib/spiral/package*, Join-Path $Lib 'package.spiproj') finds no tool; the only hits are backlog notes. The 33 spiral files that mention package.spiproj write or read their OWN package manifests: test.ps1 linked cases and sample dirs, try.ps1 and lisp build.ps1 (generated app manifests), bench.ps1 (an app that reaches lib through spiral-, which the compiler resolves for either manifest), env.ps1 (core/package.spiproj), Cells domain_compiler.ex (apps/cells/spi), zed (editor project actions), and Cells manifest.ex (which already reads spiral.toml, EDITION3-MANIFEST-MODEL). type_probe.ps1 and lib-usage-census.ps1 were migrated 11-02; tibia's predict.ps1 on 11-02. Remaining is the switch itself: replace lib/spiral/package.spiproj with the derived prelude-only spiral.toml (format <lib> --manifest toml --derive) and remove package.spiproj, gated by a full lib_gate over every lib file plus Cells and kit regen. lib_land_window already takes package.spiproj as a draft (new modules); with a prelude-only toml, new modules need no registration. It needs a window with no train running (TRAIN19 regens on lib), so the timing is main's call. RE-PROVEN 03:4x on pack33 (box, scratch copy of today's live lib): format <lib> --manifest toml --derive wrote lib/spiral/spiral.toml (packages ['|core-'], prelude array_prototypes, backend, base, iter_prototypes, text_prototypes, trace, common) and bench/parsing/spiral.toml. With both package.spiproj files removed, lib_gate passes nameof_heal.spi (1/1) and convert.spi's widening test (rust/zig/c/pascal), 0 type errors. The switch is ready whenever main opens a no-train window.")
inl note_of_lib_spiral_toml_migration_9 () = Note ("LIB-SPIRAL-TOML-MIGRATION", 9u32, "2026-11-04T08:15Z", "main", "11-04 04:1x main: SWITCH PREPARED, waiting for the post-TRAIN20 window (kit holds TRAIN21 for pack34 + this). Dry run on vultr AND on the node (both pack33): format <lib> --manifest toml --derive exits 0 and writes lib/spiral.toml (packages = ['|core-'], prelude = array_prototypes, backend, base, iter_prototypes, text_prototypes, trace, common) plus bench/parsing/spiral.toml; the root manifest's 149 hand-kept module lines become a 7-module prelude. A toml-only scratch lib (both package.spiproj removed) sits on the node at C:\\home\\remote-stage\\main-toml-lib\\spiral\\lib for kit's early one-package regen. kit made its regen and train scripts accept spiral.toml (unstaged; TRAIN21 is the full proof). PLAN for the window: run the derive on live lib through lib_land_window (spiral.toml as a new file, package.spiproj removed), close gate = full lib_gate over every lib file plus Cells document_test and kit regen on the node; then TRAIN21.")
inl note_of_lib_spiral_toml_migration_10 () = Note ("LIB-SPIRAL-TOML-MIGRATION", 10u32, "2026-11-04T08:49Z", "main", "11-04 04:49 main SWITCHED. lib_land_window (tree-write window) landed lib/spiral/spiral.toml (prelude-only: array_prototypes, backend, base, iter_prototypes, text_prototypes, trace, common; 248E51A0) and bench/parsing/spiral.toml (B035DE1D), and removed both package.spiproj files (hash-guarded 35B16EE6/25C21F6C, kept in the undo dir). Closed green on pack34: 0 consumer-used signatures changed, apps/spiral/spiral.spi compiles, document_test 22/22, type_probe and respell_drift ok. Lib digest 418D97F7F74649CF; undo spiral-bin\\lib-land-undo\\20261104-044527. Before the switch: kit's 5-owner regen on a toml-only scratch lib had identical sizes. Full proof = kit's TRAIN21 (regen over all owners on pack34 + toml lib), launching now. Lanes told: new lib modules need no manifest edit.")
inl note_of_lib_spiral_toml_migration_11 () = Note ("LIB-SPIRAL-TOML-MIGRATION", 11u32, "2026-11-04T11:04Z", "tibia", "11-04 07:0x tibia: dropped the plain tibia tag (it made the stop gate list this item as tibia work). tibia's follow-up, moving its 9 packages to prelude-only, is tracked on PACKAGE-MODEL-STANDARD (tibia note 05:0x: inventory plus the standalone-programs risk).")
inl note_of_lib_spiral_toml_migration_12 () = Note ("LIB-SPIRAL-TOML-MIGRATION", 12u32, "2026-11-04T13:52Z", "main", "11-04 09:5x main FIELD EVIDENCE for the toml-only lib: a brand-new module (fin_vector.spi, LIB-VEC-FIN) landed through lib_land_window with NO manifest edit, and Cells, type_probe (\"static lifted: fin_vector\") and the consumer check all found it from the file alone, on the node proof and on the box. The same held for x's doc_blocks/doc_render/list_sort earlier. Still waiting on TRAIN21 as the full regen proof.")
inl note_of_lib_spiral_toml_migration_13 () = Note ("LIB-SPIRAL-TOML-MIGRATION", 13u32, "2026-11-04T16:51Z", "fiscal", "2026-11-04 12:50 fiscal: FISCAL SHARE inventory (no edits yet). (1) fiscal/src keeps BOTH spiral.toml and package.spiproj with the same hand-kept 14-module list (both modified unstaged, in sync: +families document p2p posting reports profiles sunat); src holds exactly those 14 .spi files, so a prelude-only toml would derive the same set. (2) The generator writes a package.spiproj per generated unit (generator.spi:26, module_package) and reads it back (generator.spi:558) -- 69 units + generated_fiscal_runtime, each 'packageDir ../../deps/spiral/lib, packages |core- spiral- ../generated_fiscal_runtime-, modules <unit>'. ORDER: (2) changes every generated unit's manifest, so it waits for the joint decode land (generator draft 28372016 is frozen until landed) and then goes in as one generator change proven by a full regen (69 units, the manifest diff only) + a node Cells gate that finds each unit from spiral.toml. (1) is a two-file change (drop package.spiproj, drop the modules list) proven by fiscal's own Cells gate on the node; it waits for the node (busy with the decode gate) and for TRAIN21 confirming toml-only discovery outside lib. README.md:145 names the per-unit package.spiproj and changes with (2).")
inl note_of_lib_spiral_toml_migration_14 () = Note ("LIB-SPIRAL-TOML-MIGRATION", 14u32, "2026-11-04T16:55Z", "fiscal", "2026-11-04 12:55 fiscal: probe INCONCLUSIVE, do not cite. On vultr pack27 I compiled one generated unit (generated_cii_d16b) three ways: its package.spiproj, a prelude-only spiral.toml (no modules list), and a negative control toml WITHOUT ../generated_fiscal_runtime-. All three exit 0 with byte-identical 33447-byte .rs, so the negative control did not bite: a plain compile emits only what an entry point reaches, and the unit uses the runtime (sample_path) only from its test/sample sections, so the manifest's package list was never exercised. The real check stays the node Cells gate on the toml-only units (tests compile there), after the decode land. Probe tree removed.")
inl note_of_lib_spiral_toml_migration_15 () = Note ("LIB-SPIRAL-TOML-MIGRATION", 15u32, "2026-11-05T04:27Z", "fiscal", "11-05 00:4x local, fiscal share (1) DONE, in place and unstaged: fiscal/src is toml-only. src/package.spiproj is deleted, and src/spiral.toml keeps edition, packageDir and packages with no modules list. Backups are in the fiscal session scratchpad (spiral.toml.before-toml-only, package.spiproj.before-toml-only). PROOF, vultr pack27 dll 21E08222, live box src tgz 4062512A, compiler --check of canon, reports and generator (log ~/remote-stage/fiscal-check/toml-probe-1793852457.log): (a) control with both manifests: 0 diagnostics x3; (b) toml-only: 0 diagnostics x3, identical; (c) negative, toml-only minus fiscal_paths.spi: exit 5 with 41 diagnostics (ETY-UnboundVariable fiscal_paths), so discovery really comes from the files. --check type-checks every top-level def, so this avoids the 11-04 compile probe's reachability gap. Cells side: apps/cells manifest.ex/manifest.spi already try spiral.toml BEFORE package.spiproj, so fiscal Cells runs were already toml-driven and the spiproj was dead weight. Still open: (2) the per-unit package.spiproj that generator.spi:26/558 writes and reads; it goes in with the generator after the decode land. The first fiscal CI run after the user commits this is the end-to-end check (build.ps1 run_notebook).")
inl note_of_lib_spiral_toml_migration_16 () = Note ("LIB-SPIRAL-TOML-MIGRATION", 16u32, "2026-11-05T06:14Z", "fiscal", "FISCAL SHARE (2) DRAFTED + PROVEN, 11-05 02:1x local: drafts\\fiscal\\LIB-SPIRAL-TOML-MIGRATION\\src\\generator.spi 2A9BE918 (base .next6 CB407C1C). module_package dependencies writes a spiral.toml with no modules list: packageDir = '../../deps/spiral/lib'; packages = ['|core-', 'spiral-', '../<dependency unit>-' ...]. write_unit_package (still the single place) deletes a stale package.spiproj and writes spiral.toml. The CFDI read-back test now reads spiral.toml, expects the complement units in it, no modules key, and no package.spiproj left. PROOF vultr pack27, dec-gen decode lib (reference xsd_emit hash 5B274B48 checked before and after): generator --check + bodies exit 0. A generated unit (cii_d16b, its test bodies converted to defs so the 9 generated_fiscal_runtime uses are type-checked) with the toml manifest exactly as the draft writes it: 0 diagnostics. NEGATIVE, the same toml without ../generated_fiscal_runtime-: exit 5 with 9 x ETY-UnboundVariable generated_fiscal_runtime. So units resolve their dependencies from the toml packages list; this replaces the inconclusive 11-04 compile probe. A first run of this check at 02:11 was VOID: my earlier scratch trees had written drafts THROUGH dec-gen's absolute lib symlink (memory scratch-tree-copies-keep-symlinks); restored and re-proven. LANDS in the .next6 + families window instead of .next6 (it replaces CB407C1C); the later .next7 / facet generator layers take the same disjoint patch. Every unit's manifest changes in that regen (83 package.spiproj -> spiral.toml).")
inl note_of_lib_spiral_toml_migration_17 () = Note ("LIB-SPIRAL-TOML-MIGRATION", 17u32, "2026-11-05T06:59Z", "fiscal", "Layer carry, 11-05 03:0x local: the toml-manifest patch (.next6 -> 2A9BE918, 25 changed lines) applies cleanly (patch dry-run, then apply) to the two later generator layers, written as NEW files so the proven originals stay: drafts\\fiscal\\GRANULAR-GENERATOR\\src\\generator.next7t.spi AA09ECE1 (= .next7 sentinel + toml) and drafts\\spiral\\XSD-FACET-TYPES\\fiscal\\src\\generator.toml.spi A9622AB3 (= the CE32E90E facet companion + toml). Each has the sentinel match and (the facet one) the defect_check; 2 package.spiproj references remain (the stale-file delete and the one-manifest test); max 1 blank line. PENDING: their --check reruns, plus the identity regens, on the node after its nfe_v4 gate (vultr is starved).")
inl note_of_lib_spiral_toml_migration_18 () = Note ("LIB-SPIRAL-TOML-MIGRATION", 18u32, "2026-11-05T07:31Z", "fiscal", "TYPE-CHECKS PASS, 11-05 03:30 local, vultr pack27 (deps trees built with cp -rL; reference dec-gen xsd_emit 5B274B48 checked before and after; MemAvailable gate >= 2100 MB). Generator --check + bodies exit 0 for all three: (1) repo_path layer, generator C54163F3 + families 53B36EFD over the decode lib; (2) generator.next7t AA09ECE1 (sentinel + toml) + families F1CF3DDF over the sentinel lib D00BE390; (3) facet companion generator.toml A9622AB3 + families 8472AD8B over the sentinel lib. The identity regens (sentinel vs facet, repo_path vs toml base) stay PENDING: both vultr attempts went VOID (memcg kill exit 137; rustc timeout exit 124 after 2402 s while the VM was starved). They move to the node after its nfe_v4 gate.")
inl note_of_lib_spiral_toml_migration_19 () = Note ("LIB-SPIRAL-TOML-MIGRATION", 19u32, "2026-11-05T12:01Z", "fiscal", "GENERATOR + FAMILIES WINDOW LANDED in fiscal src, 11-05 08:0x local, in place and unstaged (backups generator.before-granular-land-385DD926.spi and families.before-granular-land-B59F8A10.spi in the session scratchpad): src/generator.spi 385DD926 -> 40DE71DF and src/families.spi B59F8A10 -> 99A8339B, in one window. That brings .next6 (decode + GRANULAR split + generates_cii_d16b back), the toml manifests, repo_path, the row split, documents as data, and bench_parts / write_family_parts; identity proven node-side (0 differing). generated/: all 82 unit manifests converted package.spiproj -> spiral.toml exactly as module_package writes them (dependencies in order; CFDI lists runtime, tfd, pagos), and generated_cii_d16b added (C3C111BE, formatter-stable) with its toml. README line 145 now names the per-unit spiral.toml. fiscal working tree: 222 changed paths. PENDING: the CI run after the user commits (generator tests regenerate units and diff; the generator's own CFDI test now checks spiral.toml).")
inl note_of_lib_spiral_toml_migration_20 () = Note ("LIB-SPIRAL-TOML-MIGRATION", 20u32, "2026-11-05T23:38Z", "fiscal", "11-05 19:3x local fiscal: fiscal's share is complete in the working tree. src has only spiral.toml, and all 83 generated units carry spiral.toml (the facet window placed today keeps them). The 2 remaining package.spiproj references, generator.spi's stale-manifest delete and its 'a unit keeps one manifest' test, must stay until the user commits the converted units: HEAD's generated/ still has package.spiproj per unit, and a fresh CI checkout relies on that delete. They can go in the first change after that commit.")
inl kit_pause_rule_rework () = Task ("KIT-PAUSE-RULE-REWORK", "never hide work by silence: Paused needs a reason and a resume condition, agile next and the Stop gate resurface paused P0/P1 work, agile check warns on unconditioned pauses (user request via compiler)", Story, P1, D3, 0u32, Planned, "", "kit,agile,backlog,dangling,team:kit", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_kit_pause_rule_rework () = Planning ("KIT-PAUSE-RULE-REWORK", Minutes 180u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_kit_pause_rule_rework_1 () = Note ("KIT-PAUSE-RULE-REWORK", 1u32, "2026-10-31T20:27Z", "kit", "10-31 16:3x kit, user request relayed by compiler: rework the auto-pause rule. Evidence (compiler): 95 pause-by-rule notes on 93 items. Paused drops an item out of 'agile next' and out of the Stop gate's quoted rows, so 'resume when picked' never happens. P0s such as CONFIGURABLE-ORACLE, SPIRAL-UNFORGEABLE-WITNESSES, RUST-NIGHTLY-EVERYWHERE, FORMATTER-KEEP-BREAKS, XSD-FACET-TYPES and XSD-DECODE-RESULT sat hidden, along with the compiler lane's backend, Gleam and edition-3 work. DONE TODAY: (1) the dangling-backlog memory's R3 now says never auto-pause for silence: stale Active goes to Planned, and Paused only by an explicit decision with a reason AND a resume condition. (2) The Stop gate (~/.claude/hooks/autonomous-gate.ps1) names the team's Paused P0/P1 items with 're-decide each' (tested: team main sees CONFIGURABLE-ORACLE, SPIRAL-UNFORGEABLE-WITNESSES, RUST-NIGHTLY-...). (3) i574n.github/scripts/dangling-census.ps1 flags PAUSED-NO-RESUME (92 of 110 Paused) and PAUSED-BY-RULE (24, newest note a rule pause). (4) The repair census: 44 items still Paused with a rule note in their history (main 40, 33 of them P0/P1; kit 2; compiler 2). Kit's 2 (EOIE-MEMORY-MODEL, TODO-AS-SOURCE) were re-decided -> Planned with reasons. LEFT, kit code: 'agile set <id> <p> Paused' requires --reason \"<why>\" and --resume-on <event|yyyy-mm-dd> (stored on the item); 'agile next' resurfaces a Paused item once its date passes, or after 14 days with no condition; 'agile check' warns on any P0/P1 Paused without a resume condition. Main's 40 are main's to re-decide (the gate now shows them each fire).")
inl note_of_kit_pause_rule_rework_2 () = Note ("KIT-PAUSE-RULE-REWORK", 2u32, "2026-10-31T21:11Z", "main", "10-31 17:11 main's share DONE (47 main-tagged rows flagged PAUSED-BY-RULE / PAUSED-NO-RESUME by dangling-census.ps1): 38 -> PLANNED (paused by the old silence/WIP rule with no blocker in their notes; a 10-30 lab-archive note blocks nothing), incl. CONFIGURABLE-ORACLE, RUST-NIGHTLY-EVERYWHERE, SPIRAL-UNFORGEABLE-WITNESSES, TYPEFEATURESET-ENFORCE (it gates fiscal's live work), the granular heals, LIB-REACTORS, LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS, TOOLCHAIN-LATEST, KINO-HOT-PATHS; 6 stay PAUSED with a written Reason and Resume condition (DOTA, DOTA-FIRST-SCENARIO, DOTA-HEADLESS-WORLD, DOTA-LUA-BINDINGS: superseded by the user's WoW pivot, resume if the user asks for Dota; SPIRAL-INDEPENDENT: user-batch migration run; ZED-TEST-RUNNABLES: the user's in-Zed check); 1 -> DONE (LAB-SWEEP-GATE: spiral/lab no longer exists); 2 left as compiler re-decided them today (FORMATTER-KEEP-BREAKS Blocked, MEGA-SAMPLES-TOGETHER Planned).")
inl note_of_kit_pause_rule_rework_3 () = Note ("KIT-PAUSE-RULE-REWORK", 3u32, "2026-10-31T21:26Z", "kit", "10-31 17:2x kit: repair progress. kit re-decided 5 more silence pauses that used other wording ('Paused. Last note 2026-10-24, nothing queued', a 10-28 groom), the Stop gate's new re-decide line surfaced them, all -> Planned with their next step kept: EOIE-JOBS (P0), WORKSPACE-PRIMITIVES-LIB, EOIE-ONE-CALL-SPEC, EOIE-AGILE-FORMATTED-WRITER, CONVO-IN-SPIRAL. dangling-census.ps1's rule pattern now also matches 'nothing queued' and 'Paused. Last note'. Census now: Paused 62 (110 at 16:3x), PAUSED-NO-RESUME 51, PAUSED-BY-RULE 27 (mostly main's 40-item share in progress). kit has no Paused P0/P1 left.")
inl md_line_limit_120 () = Task ("MD-LINE-LIMIT-120", "Every Markdown file in the user's own repos keeps lines at 120 characters or less: a checker in kit standards plus a reflow tool that splits long prose lines (never joins authored lines), enforced on new edits", Story, P1, D3, 25u32, Active, "", "markdown,standards,formatting,main,kit,team:main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_md_line_limit_120 () = Planning ("MD-LINE-LIMIT-120", Minutes 240u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_md_line_limit_120_1 () = Note ("MD-LINE-LIMIT-120", 1u32, "2026-10-31T20:27Z", "main", "10-31 16:27 main. USER REQUEST 10-31 ~16:2x: 'all md files should have the 120 char limit per line somehow'. CENSUS (tracked .md per repo; a line counts when over 120 chars): 1,094 md files, 656 with long lines, 39,663 long lines (36,948 prose, 1,094 table rows, 872 long URLs, the rest inside code fences). Own repos: spiral 11 files / 239 lines, kit 5/119, eoie 5/119, x 3/120, tibia 2/393 (332 table rows), i574n.github 5/84, polyglot 1/59, fiscal 1/25 (20 table), Spiral-s-ML-Library 3/90, dice 2/5, alphabet 1/5, azeroth 1/115, Trading-Journal 4/2,120, m4binder, fc1943s, blog, dir-tree-html, tapas2 and others 1-18 each. EXCLUDED (upstream forks; reflowing their docs only creates upstream diffs): zed 3,371, nearcore 514, wasmer 297, leptos 246, The-Spiral-Language 243 (fork checkout), ExplorerPatcher 192, plotters 83, cargo-outdated 76, livebook 52, atrium 28, rustix 19, otclient, forgottenserver. USER CALL: vault (Obsidian notes, 261 md, 30,865 long lines, mostly prose): Obsidian soft-wraps, so a hard 120 there changes how notes read; ask before including. DESIGN (memory formatter-keep-breaks: never join authored lines, only re-lay lines over 120): (1) checker: count lines over 120 outside code fences; exempt a table row (reflowing breaks the table: either shorten cells or accept) and a line that is only a URL/link target; (2) reflow tool: split prose lines at the last space before 120, keeping list/blockquote prefixes and indentation on the continuation line, never inside inline code, links or headings; (3) wire the checker into kit standards (the 1k-line gate's sibling) and the style hook, so new edits stay under 120; (4) run the reflow repo by repo, each team for its own repos, after a dry run shows only whitespace-at-break changes.")
inl note_of_md_line_limit_120_2 () = Note ("MD-LINE-LIMIT-120", 2u32, "2026-10-31T22:12Z", "main", "10-31 18:12 main, SCOPE CORRECTED by the user ('why you're considering vault? stick to i574n'): only repos with an i574n remote. That is 19: azeroth, blog, cargo-outdated, dir-tree-html, dotfiles, eoie, fiscal, i574n.github, i574n.github.io, kit, tapas2, The-Spiral-Language, tibia, tictactoe, x (origin i574n), plus spiral, dice, alphabet, polyglot (remote github-i574n). OUT: vault and singularity (fc1943s only), and every third-party clone (zed, nearcore, wasmer, leptos, livebook, ...). n1's 'user call: vault' is withdrawn.")
inl note_of_md_line_limit_120_3 () = Note ("MD-LINE-LIMIT-120", 3u32, "2026-11-05T23:21Z", "main", "main 11-05 19:2x CENSUS (tracked .md in the 13 i574n working repos, a line counts when over 120 chars; kinds: prose / table row / line with a 60+ char URL incl. badge links / inside a code fence): spiral 216/23/1/7, kit 85/24/1/9, x 47/43/1/29, tibia 61/331/1/1, i574n.github 73/10/1/0, fiscal 1/29/5/0, dice 5/1/1/2, regex 2/5/0/0, alphabet 2/2/1/0, html 1/0/0/1, blog 1/0/1/0, dotfiles 0/0/1/0, i574n.github.io 1/0/1/0. HEALED by hand (unstaged, soft breaks only: split at sentence or word boundaries, never inside a link, list continuations indented, nothing joined): regex 2, html 1, dice 5, alphabet 2, blog 1, i574n.github.io 1 -> prose 0 in those six (LF + trailing newline kept). RULE DESIGN for kit's checker: exempt table rows, code fences and lines whose only long token is a URL/badge (breaking those breaks rendering); flag prose only. LEFT: ~482 prose lines in spiral/kit/x/tibia/i574n.github/fiscal; that's the reflow tool's job (a kit verb), and each owning lane runs it. Census script: main scratchpad md-census.ps1 (fold into the kit verb).")
inl note_of_md_line_limit_120_4 () = Note ("MD-LINE-LIMIT-120", 4u32, "2026-11-05T23:24Z", "main", "main 11-05 19:2x i574n.github HEALED except the standard text: 9 long prose lines reflowed (unstaged) in docs/agents/agent-sync.md (151, 309), CONTRIBUTING.md (8, trailing two-space hard break kept on the last piece), README.md (65), profile/README.md (3, 5, 9, 11, 16; the user's own profile prose, words byte-identical). Method: split at the last space at or before col 120, never inside inline code, link text or link target; list continuations keep the item's indent; each line is rejoined and compared to the original before writing, and git diff --word-diff shows 0 changed words from the reflow. NOT touched: CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md (61 lines of the standard Contributor Covenant text; the reflow verb's job) and profile/README.md 38-40 (inline-HTML contact divs; breaking them risks the render, so the checker should exempt HTML block lines). Reflow script: main scratchpad md-reflow.ps1 -> drafts store beside md-census.ps1 (a model for the kit verb).")
inl note_of_md_line_limit_120_5 () = Note ("MD-LINE-LIMIT-120", 5u32, "2026-11-05T23:25Z", "main", "main 11-05 19:3x spiral MAIN SHARE healed (unstaged, same reflow + rejoin check, word diff 0): README.md 94/98/100 (contributing, license, the upstream MPL line), lib/spiral/bench/parsing/README.md 5/18/48, scripts/patched-crates/README.md 25. spiral's remaining long prose, by owner: compiler, apps/compiler/tmp/AGENTS.md 78 + tmp/lanes/hopacsota/PLAN.md 57 + samples READMEs 7 + tmp/README.md 2; x (Cells), apps/cells/README.md 65. Elsewhere: kit 85, x 47, tibia 61, fiscal 1, i574n.github's CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md 61 (standard text). Those wait on the reflow verb (model: drafts\\i574n.github\\MD-LINE-LIMIT-120\\md-reflow.ps1 + md-census.ps1) run by each owner, then kit's checker keeps it at 0.")
inl org_scripts_home () = Task ("ORG-SCRIPTS-HOME", "i574n.github/scripts holds only genuinely cross-repo glue: project-specific scripts move to their own repos, generic tooling becomes kit commands in Spiral, and the .py files are ported", Story, P1, D4, 0u32, Planned, "", "scripts,languages,cleanup,team:main,kit,resilience", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_org_scripts_home () = Planning ("ORG-SCRIPTS-HOME", Minutes 480u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_org_scripts_home_1 () = Note ("ORG-SCRIPTS-HOME", 1u32, "2026-10-31T20:42Z", "main", "10-31 16:42 main. USER 10-31 ~16:3x: 'i574n.github is getting flooded with ps1 scripts, there are project specific ones there that probably should be in their own repos ... is this main lane's drift? a lot of py files also, i said we should prioritize other languages'. CENSUS i574n.github/scripts: 118 files (67 tracked, 51 untracked): 56 .ps1, 50 .py, 9 .spi, 1 .livemd, 1 .md, 1 .spiproj; subfolders wipe-recovery 47 (mostly .py), pins 11 (Spiral), spiral-independent 4. PROJECT-SPECIFIC (belong in their repo): spiral lib tooling land-lib-program, lib-guarded-do, lib-program-gate, lib-reanchor, respell-repo, bang-export-rewrite, slice-roundtrip-sweep, notebook-main-check, spiral-independent/; tibia tibia-mariadb-dump; x x-compat-census, x-node-verify; kit crate-gate-all, draft-regen-bundle, host-dupes-census. MAIN'S SHARE OF THE DRIFT: main wrote the rule (memory org-tooling-in-dot-github: cross-repo scripts in i574n.github/scripts) and added box-upkeep, node-temp-contain, node-archive-cleanup, safe-delete-test, scratch-archive-guard-test, resource-report-save, spiral-independent/ on 10-30/31; lanes over-applied the rule to project-specific scripts. PY: wipe-recovery/ (resilience's recovery toolkit) and profile-restore-drill-node.py break the existing no-.py-in-repos rule. PLAN: (1) classify every script: project-specific -> its repo (each team moves its own, callers updated, dangling check by stem); org-generic -> a kit command in Spiral (memories generic-tools-not-per-experiment, granular-style-discipline: logic in Spiral), PowerShell only for thin host glue; .py -> ported (A/B before retiring); (2) narrow org-tooling-in-dot-github to genuinely cross-repo, short-lived glue; (3) a kit standards check that flags new .py and project-named scripts under i574n.github/scripts.")
inl note_of_org_scripts_home_2 () = Note ("ORG-SCRIPTS-HOME", 2u32, "2026-10-31T20:45Z", "kit", "10-31 16:4x kit: classifying kit's own 10-31 additions under the narrowed rule. (1) i574n.github/scripts/dangling-census.ps1 (moved from C:\\home\\data this morning) is GENERIC backlog tooling, so its end state is a kit verb ('kit agile census' with the flags DEPS-DONE, PAUSED-NO-RESUME, PAUSED-BY-RULE, STALE, WAITS-USER) written in Spiral; until then it stays as glue. (2) register-scheduled-tasks.ps1 (scheduled-task registration for the backup and the profile mirror) is machine glue that touches several repos: it stays in i574n.github/scripts. (3) node-kit-switch.ps1 is node glue for kit's rename: it belongs in kit/scripts (one project). Move it when it next changes, with its KIT-RENAME n62/memory pointers updated. kit/scripts gained release-train/launch-train changes plus train-hold.ps1, resume-train-if-starved.ps1, notebook-batch.ps1, regen-sizes.ps1 and candidate-build.ps1 (all kit-only, in kit's repo as the rule wants). Those three train helpers and notebook-batch are verb candidates too ('kit train hold|resume', 'kit notebooks <modules>'), filed on EOIE-RELEASE-TRAIN. No .py was added.")
inl note_of_org_scripts_home_3 () = Note ("ORG-SCRIPTS-HOME", 3u32, "2026-10-31T21:12Z", "tibia-lane", "10-31 16:5x tibia's share (unstaged; deletions show as D in i574n.github). (1) lib-program-gate.ps1 MOVED to spiral/apps/cells/spi/lib_program_gate.ps1, hash-identical, beside lib_gate.ps1 and named in that folder's snake_case. No code caller (git grep across spiral, tibia, kit, x, fiscal, i574n.github; only backlog notes name it); the memories now point to the new path. (2) slice-roundtrip-sweep.ps1 RETIRED, not moved: it duplicated spiral's apps/cells/spi/lib_roundtrip.exs, which also writes the canonical bytes. A/B on the same good and bad drafts gave the same verdicts (good passes; a missing final newline fails both). Git history keeps the old file. (3) Left on purpose: tibia-mariadb-dump.ps1 is tibia's project, but resilience wrote it and a daily 05:20 scheduled task calls it by this path, so moving it to tibia/scripts is resilience's call (task path and file together). recovered-drafts-census.ps1 is org-wide recovery tooling (resilience's area; a kit-command candidate), not tibia-specific. land-lib-program.ps1 and lib-guarded-do.ps1 are the shared lib-landing tools (x's and main's), also spiral lib tooling for their owners to move.")
inl note_of_org_scripts_home_4 () = Note ("ORG-SCRIPTS-HOME", 4u32, "2026-10-31T22:51Z", "resilience", "10-31 18:2x resilience (git-51) DECISION on the scripts I added (user flagged the .py flood). SPLIT: (1) ARCHIVE as one-shot (the 10-28 wipe is recovered): i574n.github/scripts/wipe-recovery/ (46 .py forensic/undelete/restore scripts + recover.py + recovery_config.py + RUNBOOK.md, 98 KB). These ran once; not living org tooling. Archiving to the node remote-archive, restorable; memory undelete-bitlocker-trim + WIPE-RECOVERY-TOOLKIT point at it; if another wipe hits, restore the dir. (2) KEEP, but as PORT TARGETS for this item (scheduled-task entry scripts, run unattended daily, a recognized exception like kit's own .ps1 task): profile-mirror.ps1, profile-mirror-if-due.ps1, profile-restore-drill.ps1 (+ profile-restore-drill-node.py), config-baseline.ps1, tibia-mariadb-dump.ps1, register-scheduled-tasks.ps1. Under spiral-first-tooling these should become kit commands in Spiral; until ported they stay because the durability tasks depend on them. No NEW ad-hoc .py/.ps1 from this lane going forward; further resilience work is kit commands or notes.")
inl note_of_org_scripts_home_5 () = Note ("ORG-SCRIPTS-HOME", 5u32, "2026-10-31T23:00Z", "x", "2026-10-31 19:00 x: x's share of the 10-31 .ps1 drift (main's all-lanes request), decided.\n(1) MERGED and MOVED: i574n.github/scripts/x-node-verify.ps1 and x-node-e2e.ps1 (both x-only) are now ONE project script, x/scripts/node-run.ps1 -Label -Mode verify|sim|parity [-Core -Part -NoAccept]. It reaches node-proof.ps1 and remote.ps1 through ../../i574n.github/scripts, parse-checked, and nothing in code called the old pair. The old files are archived (kit archive 20261031-185938). Git: x-node-verify shows ' D', and x-node-e2e shows 'AD' because the user had staged it mid-run, so the user's index still holds that add.\n(2) PORT TARGET, one command: x-compat-census.ps1 (i574n.github, cross-repo SpiralCompiler --x-compat overweight/mention counts) and x/scripts/postable-report.ps1 (xc post-thread --dry-run aggregated per repo) are two censuses of one fact, cell postability. End state: one Spiral 'xc postable census <repos>' subcommand in the x client (Spiral to Rust), reading the one typed postability source of POSTABILITY-ONE-SOURCE. Both scripts stay as glue until that lands, then go to the archive. No new .ps1 for it.\n(3) CELLS-IN-SPIRAL adds no .ps1. Today's two drafts were folded into notes (the lib-ref applier text in CELLS-SPI-LIB n2, the Kino-alias probe method in CELLS-CENSUS n3) and archived (kit archive 20261031-185252). The node probe dir C:\\home\\remote-stage\\x-kino-alias is removed.\nGeneric remainder for the kit team, a request only: 'read the node LOCALAPPDATA, launch node-proof with -RequireCore, retry 4 times printing the last 8 lines' is what every lane's node launcher repeats, so it belongs in a kit verb ('kit node proof --mirror --core --command-file').")
inl note_of_org_scripts_home_6 () = Note ("ORG-SCRIPTS-HOME", 6u32, "2026-10-31T23:10Z", "resilience", "10-31 19:1x resilience (git-51): executed tibia's request (PS1-TO-SPIRAL n17 classifies tibia-mariadb-dump.ps1 as tibia-specific glue). Moved i574n.github/scripts/tibia-mariadb-dump.ps1 -> C:\\home\\git\\tibia\\scripts\\tibia-mariadb-dump.ps1 (its repo), and repointed the daily 05:20 scheduled task to the new path IN THE SAME CHANGE (register-scheduled-tasks.ps1 gains a \\ var = <git>\\tibia\\scripts; dry run: only tibia-mariadb-dump changes, 6 others unchanged). Proven: task action now the tibia path, test-run produced canary-20261031-190849.sql. So i574n.github/scripts no longer holds a tibia-specific script; the remaining durability scripts there are cross-machine infra (profile-mirror, restore-drill, config-baseline, register) still pending the Spiral port.")
inl note_of_org_scripts_home_7 () = Note ("ORG-SCRIPTS-HOME", 7u32, "2026-11-03T07:45Z", "resilience", "11-03 03:4x resilience GROOM status: the .py FLOOD IS RESOLVED - 0 .py in i574n.github/scripts (was 50 per n1): wipe-recovery/ archived+removed, no profile-restore-drill-node.py left. Verified scripts/pins/dist .exe/.pdb/.rs (~9.7MB) are GITIGNORED local build output (git check-ignore confirms; not tracked) - not committed, no cleanup. REMAINING resilience share = port the durability scheduled-task .ps1 (profile-mirror, profile-mirror-if-due, profile-restore-drill, config-baseline, register-scheduled-tasks) to Spiral kit commands per spiral-first-tooling; box-gated (needs a compile + careful test since the daily tasks depend on them), tracked here + on PROFILE-RESTORE-DRILL-PORT. NOTE: .ps1 count is 64 (up from n1's 56) - ongoing glue (de-leak node-env, etc.); the net goal is still fewer-via-Spiral-port, which waits on the box. Resilience's .py obligation under this item is DONE; the .ps1-to-Spiral port is the open piece.")
inl note_of_org_scripts_home_8 () = Note ("ORG-SCRIPTS-HOME", 8u32, "2026-11-03T15:14Z", "x", "2026-11-03 11:14 x: groom: x share verified NONE. No script in i574n.github/scripts is x-specific (name scan for x/xc/xsim/tweet/postable) or reads x's tree (content scan for git\\x\\ and apps/client). x's glue lives in x/scripts. x tag removed.")
inl note_of_org_scripts_home_9 () = Note ("ORG-SCRIPTS-HOME", 9u32, "2026-11-03T16:17Z", "tibia", "11-03 12:3x tibia: tibia share checked, nothing to move. 10 of i574n.github/scripts mention tibia, all as one entry in a cross-repo list or org orchestration: build.ps1 and init.ps1 (org init/build calling tibia scripts/init.ps1 and build.ps1), crate-gate-all, pages-sweep, recovered-drafts-census (repo lists), config-baseline, hooks-test, profile-mirror, register-scheduled-tasks, resource-report (machine-wide). No tibia-only script lives there; tibia scripts are in tibia/scripts.")
inl note_of_org_scripts_home_10 () = Note ("ORG-SCRIPTS-HOME", 10u32, "2026-11-04T11:21Z", "tibia", "11-04 07:3x tibia: dropped the tibia tag; tibia's share was checked on 11-03 12:3x with nothing to move (its 10 mentions are org-level cross-repo lists). The tag kept the item on the stop gate's list of ready tibia work.")
inl note_of_org_scripts_home_11 () = Note ("ORG-SCRIPTS-HOME", 11u32, "2026-11-05T04:28Z", "resilience", "CLASSIFICATION (resilience, 62 .ps1 inventoried). Two sub-goals already DONE: (a) .py porting complete - 0 .py files remain in scripts/; (b) no project-specific stragglers - the dir is already cross-repo glue (app scripts already live in their repos, e.g. x\\scripts\\). Remaining reduction = fold GENERIC machinery that now duplicates kit verbs into kit commands, by cluster: PROCESS-HEALTH orphan-reaper/dangling-census/host-dupes-census/node-watch/node-utilization-monitor -> kit machine watch family; JOB-RUNNING serial-runner/queue-serial/live-wait/job-stop -> kit jobs; FS-GUARDS safe-delete -> rust_std_fs rooted policy (see DELETE-ROOT-GUARD); MIRROR/BACKUP profile-mirror*/critical-mirror/unstaged-backup -> kit archive/mirror verbs (profile-restore-drill already tracked as PROFILE-RESTORE-DRILL-PORT); CENSUS/GATE memory-integrity-sweep/crate-gate-all/banned-words-scan/bang-export-rewrite/recovered-drafts-census/config-baseline -> kit verbs. THIN GLUE that legitimately stays .ps1 (pwsh wrapping kit/ssh): remote, node-env, watch-ids, register-scheduled-tasks. Each fold is kit-gated (needs the kit verb to exist first) and should carry its own small task; classification is the un-gated deliverable. resilience.")
inl postability_one_source () = Task ("POSTABILITY-ONE-SOURCE", "Postability data (the x profile JSON and the TLD list) has one typed source instead of byte-identical .txt/.json copies in x, spiral Cells (priv + test fixtures) and tibia; profiles are selected by name, not by a copied postability/ folder", Story, P1, D3, 70u32, Blocked, "", "team:x,cells,x,tibia,data,drift,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_postability_one_source () = Planning ("POSTABILITY-ONE-SOURCE", Minutes 180u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_postability_one_source_1 () = Note ("POSTABILITY-ONE-SOURCE", 1u32, "2026-10-31T20:46Z", "main", "10-31 16:46 main. USER 10-31 ~16:3x: 'can we pivot this postability txt and json from the repos? seems like a major drift having these formats and copies'. CENSUS: the same two data files are copied byte-identical across repos: tlds.txt (7,516 B, 40806CDA1613) x4 = x/postability (tracked), spiral apps/cells/priv (tracked), spiral apps/cells/test/fixtures/postability (untracked), tibia/postability (untracked); x.json (1,251 B, CD95B0C1D176: profile x, weight_limit 280, link_weight 23, code-point weight ranges) x3 = x/postability (tracked), Cells test fixtures (untracked), tibia/postability (untracked); plus x/scripts/postable-report.ps1. ROOT CAUSE: Cells' x_compat.ex loads the TLD set from its own priv/tlds.txt and picks a profile by walking up from the checked file to the NEAREST postability/ folder (nearest_profile, x_compat.ex:51; SPIRAL_POSTABILITY_PROFILE overrides), so every repo that wants postability checks carries its own copy. PIVOT: (1) one source of truth, as Spiral code rather than .txt/.json (the user prefers other languages over loose data files; memory no-py-txt-in-repos): the profile as a typed record and the TLD set as a lib module (or Cells-owned data generated from it), with a test that pins its digest; (2) profile selection by NAME (a project's spiral.toml or the env var), not by finding a copied folder; (3) delete every copy (archive first), x's postable-report reads the same source; (4) dangling check by stem (postability, tlds, x.json, x_compat) before removal. Owners: Cells (main) for the reader, x for the profile's content.")
inl note_of_postability_one_source_2 () = Note ("POSTABILITY-ONE-SOURCE", 2u32, "2026-10-31T21:18Z", "tibia-lane", "10-31 17:1x tibia. A THIRD READER the census misses: the compiler's own --x-compat (apps/compiler/spiral_compiler.fs, postabilityProfilePath and postabilityProfileOfFile near line 34238, compiler lane). It takes SPIRAL_POSTABILITY_PROFILE, else the nearest ancestor postability/*.json (first file after sort), then loads the TLD list from the file the JSON names in links.top_level_domains_file, relative to the JSON. tibia's build gate (tibia/scripts/build.ps1: --x-compat over src and apps) and x's --x-compat runs go through this reader, not Cells' x_compat.ex. So the pivot has to change both readers together, or tibia's gate breaks. Why tibia has a copy: tibia's CI has no x checkout, so on 10-30 build.ps1 got tibia/postability/ (x.json + tlds.txt) plus a drift check against a sibling ../x/postability when one exists. tibia's steps once both readers select the profile by NAME from a source tibia's deps/spiral already carries: build.ps1 names profile x (env or spiral.toml, whichever the pivot picks), its copy and drift check are deleted, and tibia/postability/ is archived. Until then tibia keeps the copy, which the drift check pins to x's bytes.")
inl note_of_postability_one_source_3 () = Note ("POSTABILITY-ONE-SOURCE", 3u32, "2026-10-31T21:26Z", "main", "10-31 17:26 main, first read of the reader (apps/cells/lib/spiral/cells/x_compat.ex, 234 lines): Cells' BUILT-IN default profile already IS the x profile. The Profile struct defaults (x_compat.ex:6-19) carry link_weight 23, default_weight 2, ranges {0,4351,1} {8192,8205,1} {8208,8223,1} {8242,8247,1}, short_domains t.co, and default_profile() adds the TLD set from priv/tlds.txt plus compiled hashtag/mention/cashtag/domain regexes. x.json's values match those defaults field for field (weight_limit 280, link_weight 23, default 2, the same 4 ranges), so every copied postability/x.json only reproduces the default. The copies exist because profile_for (x_compat.ex:51) prefers the env var, then the NEAREST postability/*.json walking up from the file. PLAN, simplest first: (1) a Cells test that loads x.json through cached_profile and asserts it equals default_profile() on every field (pins the equivalence, incl. the json's links/entities/posting sections); (2) if it holds, delete the copies in tibia and the Cells test fixtures (archive first) and keep x/postability/x.json only while x's postable-report.ps1 reads it; (3) one TLD source: Cells priv/tlds.txt, with x's report reading the same file or a generated Spiral module; (4) later, per the user's language preference, move the profile out of Elixir/JSON into a Spiral record that Cells and x both consume.")
inl note_of_postability_one_source_4 () = Note ("POSTABILITY-ONE-SOURCE", 4u32, "2026-11-01T03:05Z", "main", "main 23:05: copies removed. (1) Cells postability_profile_test.exs no longer reads a copied fixture: it builds x's profile inline from XCompat.default_profile plus the poster rule (defused_dot before [A-Za-z0-9_-], after [A-Za-z]) and copies priv/tlds.txt into a tmp dir at run time; same 4 assertions; landed green via cells_land_window (8/8 with x_compat_test, scratch proof without the fixture dir 8/8), test EC55DB487F84. (2) spiral/apps/cells/test/fixtures/postability and tibia/postability (both untracked, byte-identical to x's) archived to kit\\archive 20261031-230434 and 20261031-230438 and removed. (3) tibia/scripts/build.ps1: the hash-parity check against a local copy replaced by SPIRAL_POSTABILITY_PROFILE pointing at ../../x/postability/x.json (the compiler's --x-compat honors it); verified: exit 0, 65 files, 0 unprofiled, 0 overweight, 17 mentions. Remaining sources: x/postability (the X profile, owner x), spiral/apps/cells/priv/tlds.txt (Cells' built-in default; spiral cannot depend on x), and x/apps/client/tlds.spi, a Spiral twin of x's tlds.txt held equal by a test: handed to x to generate one from the other or drop the txt.")
inl note_of_postability_one_source_5 () = Note ("POSTABILITY-ONE-SOURCE", 5u32, "2026-11-01T03:06Z", "x", "2026-10-31 23:06 x: answer to main's 23:05 note. ONE SOURCE = x/postability/tlds.txt. x/apps/client/tlds.spi becomes GENERATED from it by a Spiral step.\nWhy the txt and not the .spi:\n(1) The txt is the upstream shape: IANA publishes tlds-alpha-by-domain.txt as one label per line, so an update is a download and a diff, never a hand edit of Spiral string chunks.\n(2) The profile format is data-driven for every network: links.top_level_domains_file is read at runtime by spiral_compiler.fs:34143 (--x-compat) and by Spiral.Cells.XCompat. A future mastodon or bluesky profile names its own list the same way, so a list compiled into one client can't be the source.\n(3) tlds.spi exists only because xc's Rust/Gleam binary has no data file at runtime. It is a build artifact of the txt.\nPlan (x, CPU-light, not tonight):\n- tlds.spi keeps is_tld and its chunks, and gains a generator function: the source text of the chunk literals, built from the txt through the existing file_tlds parser.\n- A small Spiral program (spiral run, not a new .ps1) writes apps/client/tlds.spi from postability/tlds.txt.\n- the_list_is_postability_tlds_txt stays as the drift gate. It already fails on any difference, so after a txt update the fix is to rerun the generator.\n- gate.ps1:56 keeps listing the txt as a data file.\nThird copy, found while checking: spiral/apps/cells/priv/tlds.txt is hash-identical (40806CDA1613) and is x_compat.ex's @tlds_path default (lines 2-4) plus postability_profile_test's fixture. It's main's: drop it once XCompat always resolves the profile's file (or when CELLS-IN-SPIRAL ports XCompat), with the default pointing at ../x/postability/tlds.txt like tibia's build.ps1 does.")
inl note_of_postability_one_source_6 () = Note ("POSTABILITY-ONE-SOURCE", 6u32, "2026-11-01T03:10Z", "main", "main 23:1x: user disliked the Mix/OTP name priv; apps/cells/priv split by purpose: tlds.txt -> apps/cells/data/tlds.txt, new_project scaffold -> apps/cells/templates/new_project. Readers updated (x_compat.ex, new_project.ex, compiler_daemon.ex source_stamp, postability_profile_test.exs, README); new_project/postability_profile/warm_notebook/x_compat tests 14/14. Nothing used :code.priv_dir, so leaving the convention costs nothing.")
inl note_of_postability_one_source_7 () = Note ("POSTABILITY-ONE-SOURCE", 7u32, "2026-11-01T03:23Z", "tibia-lane", "11-01 tibia: tibia's share DONE (unstaged in tibia). main removed the untracked tibia/postability copy. The CI gate comes back with x as the single source: gh-pages.yml's build job checks out i574n/x (public) into .x (sparse: postability) with the already-locked actions/checkout@v7.0.1, so actions.lock needs no refresh, and it sets SPIRAL_POSTABILITY_PROFILE to .x/postability/x.json. build.ps1 takes that variable first, then a sibling ../../x/postability/x.json, else main's loud SKIPPED with a ::warning. Proof through build.ps1 -Files no-such-file: sibling -> every cell fits / 17 mentions; env -> a weight-100 copy -> throws (2776 over), so the env path is the one checked.")
inl note_of_postability_one_source_8 () = Note ("POSTABILITY-ONE-SOURCE", 8u32, "2026-11-01T03:24Z", "tibia-lane", "11-01 tibia: CORRECTION. n7 is REVERTED on the user's direction (\"why the hell tibia is referencing x? spiral should be x compatible but in a generic way (cells), we shouldn't even know that x exists\"). gh-pages.yml is back to its committed state (no x checkout, no SPIRAL_POSTABILITY_PROFILE). scripts/build.ps1 has NO x reference: the postable step is an unconditional LOUD skip (message plus GitHub ::warning) until Cells' generic check (mix spiral.x_compat --check, built-in defaults, no profile file) and tibia's :: respell (SPIRAL-PATH-SYNTAX, SpiralCompiler format --spelling symbols) land. git grep finds 0 x references in tibia outside deps. Memory: postability-generic-no-x.")
inl note_of_postability_one_source_9 () = Note ("POSTABILITY-ONE-SOURCE", 9u32, "2026-11-01T03:32Z", "main", "main 23:3x, user direction 10-31 23:2x: 'spiral should be x compatible but in a generic way (cells), we shouldn't even know that x exists'; no repo references x. Done: Cells is the one checker with built-in defaults; mix spiral.x_compat --check adds the per-definition overweight rule (exit 1 only on overweight), the compiler's folder skip list, and a crash fix for non-ASCII next to a dot; parity with the compiler --x-compat under x's profile is exact on lib 1459, tibia 17, x 2 (landed 23:31, 9/9). tibia reverted every x reference (CI checkout and build.ps1); its postable step is skipped loudly until it respells. Measured: tibia respelled to :: (format --spelling symbols) under the default has autolinks 562 -> 9 (Lua inside macros) and overweight 51 -> 2. Remaining: (1) tibia respell + switch to the Cells check (tibia); (2) x's builds switch to the Cells check; (3) the compiler retires --x-compat, its XCompat section and the postability/*.json lookup, and Cells drops profile loading after (1)-(2); x/postability then serves only x's own poster; (4) every repo respells to :: (SPIRAL-PATH-SYNTAX migration round), after which the TLD list and dot-defusing can go too.")
inl note_of_postability_one_source_10 () = Note ("POSTABILITY-ONE-SOURCE", 10u32, "2026-11-01T03:41Z", "compiler", "10-31 10-31 23:41 compiler, cadence groom: Step 3 SCHEDULED (main 23:33): retire --x-compat, the XCompat section and the postability/*.json lookup from the compiler. Cells owns the check now, with parity proven on lib/tibia/x. Starts only after x and tibia both switch their gates to the Cells check (resume condition: a note from each lane saying the old flag is unused); the compiler applier rides the next pack after that.")
inl note_of_postability_one_source_11 () = Note ("POSTABILITY-ONE-SOURCE", 11u32, "2026-11-01T03:43Z", "tibia-lane", "11-01 tibia NEXT STEP (CPU-light, no window): (1) respell tibia src and apps in place with `SpiralCompiler format ../src ../apps --spelling symbols` (main's scratch: autolinks 562 -> 9, overweight 51 -> 2); (2) split the 2 near-limit cells, host/character.spi:116 (276 > 272) and host/world_port.spi:67 (274 > 272); (3) replace build.ps1's loud skip with `mix spiral.x_compat --check ../src ../apps` run from deps/spiral/apps/cells (exit 1 only on overweight). Proof: build.ps1 -Files no-such-file prints the check passing, plus one full test file to show the respell compiles.")
inl note_of_postability_one_source_12 () = Note ("POSTABILITY-ONE-SOURCE", 12u32, "2026-11-01T03:53Z", "tibia-lane", "11-01 01:xx tibia: tibia's share LANDED in tibia (unstaged), with no x anywhere (git grep: 0 references). (1) Respell: SpiralCompiler format ../src ../apps --spelling symbols changed 61 of 65 files (x.y -> x::y, test -> :#). (2) The respell pushed lines past 120, so a plain format re-laid 10 files; that re-lay pushed 3 cells over a post, and 2 more were over before it. All split without behaviour change: gpu_sim hunt_and_clear_facts (local clears -> c), gpu_sim routed_hunt_matches (fixture for the WGSL routing test), search nudge_key (fixture), character expected_kit_where (fixture; same SQL bytes), world_port fake_log (fixture). (3) scripts/build.ps1's postable step is now Cells' generic check: from deps/spiral/apps/cells, mix spiral.x_compat --check <tibia src> <tibia apps> (scoop erlang/elixir prepended when present, as run_notebook.ps1 does), and it throws on a non-zero exit. Proof through build.ps1 -Files no-such-file: layout clean, then \"postable / x-compat report: 26 finding(s) in 65 file(s) (autolink 9, mention 17)\", 0 overweight; the arena and canary Lua builds compiled on the respelled sources. Negative: an overweight scratch cell makes the Cells check exit 1. PENDING: the full test suite on the respelled tree (node job queued behind the consumer regate, task b82qie3po). The 9 autolinks are Lua host text inside macros (warnings only).")
inl note_of_postability_one_source_13 () = Note ("POSTABILITY-ONE-SOURCE", 13u32, "2026-11-01T04:38Z", "x", "2026-11-01 00:38 x: RE-DECIDED n5's generator, now DEFERRED with a written condition. Three facts since n5: (1) n9 step 4 retires the TLD list itself once every repo respells to :: (autolinks 562 -> 9 on tibia), so a generator would build tooling for a list slated for deletion. (2) The twin can't drift silently today: the_list_is_postability_tlds_txt fails on any difference between tlds.spi and tlds.txt, and gate.ps1:56 tracks the txt. (3) The generator isn't a copy: tlds.spi's 40 literals in groups of 6 exist so each definition stays under the per-post weight (271), so it has to re-pack under that limit. Decision: keep tlds.txt as the single source (n5) with the equality test as the enforcement. Write the generator ONLY IF tlds.txt has to change before n9 step 4 lands (an IANA update x needs). Otherwise x's share here closes when step 4 removes both files.")
inl note_of_postability_one_source_14 () = Note ("POSTABILITY-ONE-SOURCE", 14u32, "2026-11-01T04:52Z", "tibia-lane", "11-01 01:xx tibia: the gate now uses the generic name (CELL-BUDGET-NAMING, landed 00:45). scripts/build.ps1's step is labelled \"cell budget\" and runs `mix spiral.cell_budget --check <tibia src> <tibia apps>` from deps/spiral/apps/cells; it reports over_budget lines and throws on a non-zero exit. No x_compat, postable, overweight or x left in build.ps1. Proof: the check on tibia gives \"cell-budget report: 26 finding(s) in 65 file(s) (autolink 9, mention 17)\", exit 0; the scratch negative gives over_budget 568 > 273, exit 1.")
inl note_of_postability_one_source_15 () = Note ("POSTABILITY-ONE-SOURCE", 15u32, "2026-11-01T05:11Z", "tibia-lane", "11-01 02:xx tibia: CORRECTION to n12 and n14. The :: respell also respelled `test` to `:#`, and scripts/build.ps1 discovered test files with the pattern '^test ', so after the respell it matched 0 of 56 files: \"no test files matched\". A full run would have tested nothing, and CI would have failed or proven nothing. Found when the offscreen check reported \"no test files matched offscreen\". FIXED: the discovery pattern is '^(test|:#) ' (all 56 files found again); no other tibia script assumes the old spelling (27 scanned). The full suite on the respelled tree is rerunning on the node with the fixed script (tibia-respell-full-build2); until it passes, the respell is unproven beyond the Lua builds and the cell-budget and layout gates.")
inl note_of_postability_one_source_16 () = Note ("POSTABILITY-ONE-SOURCE", 16u32, "2026-11-01T06:07Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 02:07 compiler: STEP 3 DRAFTED (ready, lands in PACK-12 once x and tibia confirm the Cells check). One kit do program, 4 replacements in 2 files: core spiral_compiler.fs loses the whole '/// ## XCompat' section (243 lines, 34071-34313: XCompatKind/Finding, PostabilityProfile, postabilityProfile*, xCompat* incl. the postability/*.json lookup and SPIRAL_POSTABILITY_PROFILE) and the 'xCompatWarnEntry file' hook in the build path (34976). Host tmp/compiler/host/Program.fs loses runXCompatReport (1251-1284) and the '--x-compat' CLI branch, and '--check' no longer warns (its xCompatWarnEntry call goes). Dry-applied on copies: 0 references left; every name the section defines is unused outside it (only the removed host fn and hook used them). Bases: spiral_compiler.fs C0990B2D590CE965, Program.fs 420E1452DB4B3194. CONSUMERS to tell at land time: tibia README.md:20 says 'measured with SpiralCompiler --x-compat' (doc to update), and apps/zed spiral_zed.rs:1749 parses XC00n warnings from --check stderr (its unit test uses a fixture string and stays green, but editor diagnostics for postability vanish unless Cells' check feeds zed; owner x). No other repo calls --x-compat (grep x, tibia, kit, fiscal, i574n.github, dice, polyglot).\nPROGRAM (kit do <dir holding spiral_compiler.fs and Program.fs> -):\nedit spiral_compiler.fs expect files 1 replacements 2\n  replace once <<<\n    /// ## XCompat\n    type XCompatKind =\n        | XCompatHashtag\n        | XCompatMention\n        | XCompatCashtag\n        | XCompatAutolink\n        | XCompatOverweight\n\n    /// ### XCompatFinding\n    type XCompatFinding = { kind : XCompatKind; line : int; column : int; text : string }\n\n    /// ### xCompatKindName\n    let xCompatKindName kind =\n        match kind with\n        | XCompatHashtag -> \"hashtag\"\n        | XCompatMention -> \"mention\"\n        | XCompatCashtag -> \"cashtag\"\n        | XCompatAutolink -> \"autolink\"\n        | XCompatOverweight -> \"overweight\"\n\n    /// ### xCompatCode\n    let xCompatCode kind =\n        match kind with\n        | XCompatHashtag -> \"XC001\"\n        | XCompatMention -> \"XC002\"\n        | XCompatCashtag -> \"XC003\"\n        | XCompatAutolink -> \"XC004\"\n        | XCompatOverweight -> \"XC005\"\n\n    /// ### xCompatHint\n    let xCompatHint kind =\n        match kind with\n        | XCompatHashtag -> \"X turns '#' before a letter into a hashtag link\"\n        | XCompatMention -> \"X turns '@' before a name into a mention\"\n        | XCompatCashtag -> \"X turns '$' before letters into a cashtag link\"\n        | XCompatAutolink -> \"X shortens what looks like a domain to a 23-character t.co link\"\n        | XCompatOverweight -> \"one post holds 280 weighted characters (CJK and emoji weigh 2, links 23), less the cell's cN k/n header line and 1 per defused link dot; split this definition\"\n\n    /// ### PostabilityProfile\n    type PostabilityProfile =\n        {\n            name : string\n            weightLimit : int\n            linkWeight : int\n            defaultWeight : int\n            weightRanges : (int * int * int) list\n            domainPattern : Text.RegularExpressions.Regex\n            topLevelDomains : Set<string>\n            shortDomainsLinked : Set<string>\n            twoLetterSecondLabelLongerThan : int\n            entityPatterns : (XCompatKind * Text.RegularExpressions.Regex) list\n            onePostHeader : string\n            headerSeparator : string\n            defusedBefore : Text.RegularExpressions.Regex\n            defusedAfter : Text.RegularExpressions.Regex\n            defusedExtraWeight : int\n        }\n\n    /// ### postabilityProfileOfFile\n    let postabilityProfileOfFile (path : string) : PostabilityProfile =\n        use document = Text.Json.JsonDocument.Parse (IO.File.ReadAllText path)\n        let root = document.RootElement\n        let at (element : Text.Json.JsonElement) (name : string) = element.GetProperty name\n        let text (element : Text.Json.JsonElement) (name : string) = (at element name).GetString ()\n        let number (element : Text.Json.JsonElement) (name : string) = (at element name).GetInt32 ()\n        let regex (pattern : string) = Text.RegularExpressions.Regex pattern\n        let post = at root \"post\"\n        let weights = at root \"code_point_weight\"\n        let links = at root \"links\"\n        let entities = at root \"entities\"\n        let posting = at root \"posting\"\n        let defused = at posting \"defused_dot\"\n        let domainsFile = IO.Path.Combine (IO.Path.GetDirectoryName (IO.Path.GetFullPath path), text links \"top_level_domains_file\")\n        {\n            name = text root \"profile\"\n            weightLimit = number post \"weight_limit\"\n            linkWeight = number post \"link_weight\"\n            defaultWeight = number weights \"default\"\n            weightRanges = [ for range in (at weights \"ranges\").EnumerateArray () -> range.[0].GetInt32 (), range.[1].GetInt32 (), range.[2].GetInt32 () ]\n            domainPattern = regex (text links \"domain_pattern\")\n            topLevelDomains = IO.File.ReadAllLines domainsFile |> Array.map (fun line -> line.Trim ()) |> Array.filter (fun line -> line <> \"\") |> Set.ofArray\n            shortDomainsLinked = [ for domain in (at links \"short_domains_always_linked\").EnumerateArray () -> domain.GetString () ] |> Set.ofList\n            twoLetterSecondLabelLongerThan = number links \"two_letter_tld_needs_second_label_longer_than\"\n            entityPatterns = [ XCompatHashtag, regex (text entities \"hashtag\"); XCompatMention, regex (text entities \"mention\"); XCompatCashtag, regex (text entities \"cashtag\") ]\n            onePostHeader = text posting \"budget_header_for_one_post\"\n            headerSeparator = text posting \"header_separator\"\n            defusedBefore = regex (\"^\" + text defused \"before\" + \"$\")\n            defusedAfter = regex (\"^\" + text defused \"after\" + \"$\")\n            defusedExtraWeight = number defused \"extra_weight\"\n        }\n\n    /// ### postabilityProfilePath\n    let postabilityProfilePath (file : string) : string option =\n        match Environment.GetEnvironmentVariable \"SPIRAL_POSTABILITY_PROFILE\" with\n        | null | \"\" ->\n            let rec nearest (directory : string) =\n                if isNull directory then None\n                else\n                    let folder = IO.Path.Combine (directory, \"postability\")\n                    let found = if IO.Directory.Exists folder then IO.Directory.GetFiles (folder, \"*.json\") |> Array.sort |> Array.tryHead else None\n                    match found with\n                    | Some profile -> Some profile\n                    | None -> nearest (IO.Path.GetDirectoryName directory)\n            nearest (IO.Path.GetDirectoryName (IO.Path.GetFullPath file))\n        | path -> Some path\n\n    /// ### postabilityProfiles\n    let postabilityProfiles = Collections.Concurrent.ConcurrentDictionary<string, PostabilityProfile> ()\n\n    /// ### postabilityProfileFor\n    let postabilityProfileFor (file : string) : PostabilityProfile option =\n        postabilityProfilePath file |> Option.map (fun path -> postabilityProfiles.GetOrAdd (IO.Path.GetFullPath path, postabilityProfileOfFile))\n\n    /// ### xCompatLinks\n    let xCompatLinks (profile : PostabilityProfile) (link : string) (topLevelDomain : string) =\n        if not (profile.topLevelDomains.Contains topLevelDomain) then false\n        elif profile.shortDomainsLinked.Contains (link.ToLowerInvariant ()) then true\n        elif topLevelDomain.Length = 2 then\n            let labels = link.Split '.'\n            labels.[labels.Length - 2].Length > profile.twoLetterSecondLabelLongerThan\n        else true\n\n    /// ### xCompatCodePointWeight\n    let xCompatCodePointWeight (profile : PostabilityProfile) (codePoint : int) =\n        match profile.weightRanges |> List.tryFind (fun (low, high, _) -> codePoint >= low && codePoint <= high) with\n        | Some (_, _, weight) -> weight\n        | None -> profile.defaultWeight\n\n    /// ### xCompatDefusedAt\n    let xCompatDefusedAt (profile : PostabilityProfile) (text : string) (i : int) =\n        i > 0 && i + 1 < text.Length && text.[i] = '.'\n        && profile.defusedBefore.IsMatch (string text.[i - 1]) && profile.defusedAfter.IsMatch (string text.[i + 1])\n\n    /// ### xCompatPostedLink\n    let xCompatPostedLink (profile : PostabilityProfile) (text : string) (found : Text.RegularExpressions.Match) =\n        let undefusedDot = seq { found.Index .. found.Index + found.Length - 1 } |> Seq.exists (fun i -> text.[i] = '.' && not (xCompatDefusedAt profile text i))\n        undefusedDot && xCompatLinks profile found.Value (found.Groups.[1].Value.ToLowerInvariant ())\n\n    /// ### xCompatPostWeight\n    let xCompatPostWeight (profile : PostabilityProfile) (post : string) =\n        let links = [ for found in profile.domainPattern.Matches post do if xCompatPostedLink profile post found then yield found ]\n        let unlinked = links |> List.rev |> List.fold (fun (rest : string) found -> rest.Remove (found.Index, found.Length)) post\n        let characters = unlinked.EnumerateRunes () |> Seq.sumBy (fun rune -> xCompatCodePointWeight profile rune.Value)\n        characters + profile.linkWeight * links.Length\n\n    /// ### xCompatTopLevelBlocks\n    let xCompatTopLevelBlocks (lines : string []) =\n        let lines = if lines.Length > 0 && lines.[lines.Length - 1] = \"\" then lines.[.. lines.Length - 2] else lines\n        let cells = Collections.Generic.List<int * string> ()\n        let current = Collections.Generic.List<string> ()\n        let mutable start = 1\n        let mutable blanks = 0\n        let mutable seen = false\n        let finish () = if current.Count > 0 then cells.Add (start, String.Join(\"\\n\", current).TrimEnd ())\n        for index in 0 .. lines.Length - 1 do\n            let line = lines.[index]\n            if line = \"\" then blanks <- blanks + 1\n            elif blanks > 0 && seen && not (line.StartsWith \" \" || line.StartsWith \"\\t\") then\n                finish ()\n                current.Clear ()\n                for _ in 2 .. blanks do current.Add \"\"\n                current.Add line\n                start <- index + 1\n                blanks <- 0\n            else\n                for _ in 1 .. blanks do current.Add \"\"\n                current.Add line\n                if not seen then start <- index + 1\n                blanks <- 0\n                seen <- true\n        finish ()\n        List.ofSeq cells\n\n    /// ### xCompatDefusedDots\n    let xCompatDefusedDots (profile : PostabilityProfile) (block : string) =\n        seq { 1 .. block.Length - 2 } |> Seq.filter (xCompatDefusedAt profile block) |> Seq.length\n\n    /// ### xCompatPostableLimit\n    let xCompatPostableLimit (profile : PostabilityProfile) (cell : int) (block : string) =\n        profile.weightLimit - xCompatPostWeight profile (profile.onePostHeader.Replace (\"{block}\", string cell) + profile.headerSeparator) - profile.defusedExtraWeight * xCompatDefusedDots profile block\n\n    /// ### xCompatOverweightFindings\n    let xCompatOverweightFindings (profile : PostabilityProfile) (lines : string []) =\n        [ for position, (row, block) in List.indexed (xCompatTopLevelBlocks lines) do\n            let weight = xCompatPostWeight profile block\n            let limit = xCompatPostableLimit profile (position + 1) block\n            if weight > limit then\n                yield { kind = XCompatOverweight; line = row; column = 1; text = $\"weight {weight} > {limit} ({profile.weightLimit} minus the c{position + 1} k/n header and defused dots)\" } ]\n\n    /// ### xCompatLineFindings\n    let xCompatLineFindings (profile : PostabilityProfile) (line : string) (row : int) =\n        let marks = profile.entityPatterns |> List.collect (fun (kind, pattern) -> [ for found in pattern.Matches line -> kind, found.Index, found.Value ])\n        let links =\n            [ for found in profile.domainPattern.Matches line do\n                if xCompatPostedLink profile line found then yield XCompatAutolink, found.Index, found.Value ]\n        marks @ links\n        |> List.sortBy (fun (_, start, _) -> start)\n        |> List.map (fun (kind, start, text) ->\n            { kind = kind; line = row; column = Globalization.StringInfo(line.Substring (0, start)).LengthInTextElements + 1; text = text })\n\n    /// ### xCompatFindings\n    let xCompatFindings (profile : PostabilityProfile) (source : string) =\n        let lines = source.Replace(\"\\r\\n\", \"\\n\").Split '\\n'\n        let marks = lines |> List.ofArray |> List.mapi (fun index line -> xCompatLineFindings profile line (index + 1)) |> List.concat\n        marks @ xCompatOverweightFindings profile lines |> List.sortBy (fun finding -> finding.line, finding.column)\n    /// ### xCompatReportLine\n    let xCompatReportLine (path : string) (finding : XCompatFinding) =\n        $\"{path}:{finding.line}:{finding.column}: {xCompatKindName finding.kind}: {finding.text} ({xCompatHint finding.kind})\"\n\n    /// ### xCompatWarningLine\n    let xCompatWarningLine (path : string) (finding : XCompatFinding) =\n        $\"warning: {path}:{finding.line}:{finding.column}: {xCompatCode finding.kind} {xCompatKindName finding.kind}: {finding.text} ({xCompatHint finding.kind})\"\n\n    /// ### xCompatPackageEditionThree\n    let xCompatPackageEditionThree (file : string) =\n        let rec nearest (directory : string) =\n            if isNull directory then None\n            else\n                let toml = IO.Path.Combine (directory, \"spiral.toml\")\n                let project = if IO.File.Exists toml then toml else IO.Path.Combine (directory, \"package.spiproj\")\n                if IO.File.Exists project then Some project else nearest (IO.Path.GetDirectoryName directory)\n        match nearest (IO.Path.GetDirectoryName (IO.Path.GetFullPath file)) with\n        | Some project -> IO.File.ReadAllLines project |> Array.exists (fun line -> let t = line.Trim().Replace(\" \", \"\") in t = \"edition:3\" || t = \"edition=3\")\n        | None -> false\n\n    /// ### xCompatEnabled\n    let xCompatEnabled (file : string) =\n        Environment.GetEnvironmentVariable \"SPIRAL_X_COMPAT\" = \"1\" || (try xCompatPackageEditionThree file with _ -> false)\n\n    /// ### xCompatWarnedTexts\n    let xCompatWarnedTexts = Collections.Concurrent.ConcurrentDictionary<string * string, unit> ()\n\n    /// ### xCompatWarnEntry\n    let xCompatWarnEntry (file : string) =\n        try\n            if xCompatEnabled file && IO.File.Exists file then\n                match postabilityProfileFor file with\n                | Some profile ->\n                    let text = IO.File.ReadAllText file\n                    if xCompatWarnedTexts.TryAdd ((file, text), ()) then\n                        for finding in xCompatFindings profile text do eprintfn \"%s\" (xCompatWarningLine file finding)\n                | None -> ()\n        with _ -> ()\n    /// ## SignalRSupervisor\n  >>> with <<<\n    /// ## SignalRSupervisor\n  >>>\n  replace once <<<\n                        if List.isEmpty entry_errors then dependency_warnings ()\n                        xCompatWarnEntry file\n                        let body() =\n  >>> with <<<\n                        if List.isEmpty entry_errors then dependency_warnings ()\n                        let body() =\n  >>>\nedit Program.fs expect files 1 replacements 2\n  replace once <<<\n    let private runXCompatReport (paths : string []) =\n        let extensions = set [ \".spi\"; \".spir\"; \".livemd\" ]\n        let files =\n            paths\n            |> Array.collect (fun path ->\n                if Directory.Exists path then\n                    filesUnder path (fun name -> List.contains name [ \"target\"; \"node_modules\"; \"deps\"; \"bin\"; \"obj\"; \"_build\"; \".git\"; \".history\" ])\n                    |> Array.ofSeq\n                    |> Array.filter (fun file -> extensions.Contains (Path.GetExtension file))\n                    |> Array.map (fun file -> file.Replace ('\\\\', '/'))\n                    |> Array.sort\n                elif File.Exists path then [| path |]\n                else [||])\n            |> Array.distinct\n        let profiled = files |> Array.map (fun file -> file, postabilityProfileFor file)\n        let unprofiled = profiled |> Array.choose (fun (file, profile) -> if profile.IsNone then Some file else None)\n        let findings =\n            [ for file, profile in profiled do\n                match profile with\n                | Some profile ->\n                    for finding in xCompatFindings profile (File.ReadAllText file) do yield file, finding\n                | None -> () ]\n        for file, finding in findings do printfn \"%s\" (xCompatReportLine file finding)\n        for file in unprofiled do\n            printfn \"%s: no postability profile (no postability/*.json in an ancestor directory and SPIRAL_POSTABILITY_PROFILE unset)\" file\n        let byKind =\n            findings\n            |> List.countBy (fun (_, finding) -> xCompatKindName finding.kind)\n            |> List.sortBy fst\n            |> List.map (fun (kind, count) -> $\"{kind} {count}\")\n            |> String.concat \", \"\n        let unchecked = if unprofiled.Length = 0 then \"\" else $\", {unprofiled.Length} unchecked without a profile\"\n        printfn \"x-compat report: %d finding(s) in %d file(s)%s%s\" findings.Length files.Length (if byKind = \"\" then \"\" else $\" ({byKind})\") unchecked\n        if unprofiled.Length = 0 then 0 else 2\n\n    let reexecWithGen0Budget (rawArgv : string []) =\n  >>> with <<<\n    let reexecWithGen0Budget (rawArgv : string []) =\n  >>>\n  replace once <<<\n        elif argv.Length >= 2 && argv.[0] = \"--x-compat\" then\n            runXCompatReport argv.[1..]\n        elif argv.Length = 2 && argv.[0] = \"--check\" then\n            Spiral.spiral_compiler.xCompatWarnEntry argv.[1]\n  >>> with <<<\n        elif argv.Length = 2 && argv.[0] = \"--check\" then\n  >>>")
inl note_of_postability_one_source_17 () = Note ("POSTABILITY-ONE-SOURCE", 17u32, "2026-11-01T07:23Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 03:23 compiler: step 3 program re-anchored on the post-PACK-12 core (796D343FB6E78D6E): still clean (4 replacements). Lives in the repo as apps/compiler/packs/pack13/2-retire-x-compat.kitdo, waiting for x and tibia to confirm they use the Cells check.")
inl note_of_postability_one_source_18 () = Note ("POSTABILITY-ONE-SOURCE", 18u32, "2026-11-01T07:45Z", "tibia-lane", "11-01 03:45 tibia: the 61-file respell is proven on the node except for one regression of mine, character.spi's paren split, now fixed and green locally, and a node env gap (no wgsl runner) that is not caused by the respell. Detail: TIBIA-LIB-CONSUMER-REGATE, latest note. Build 3 is running on the node.")
inl note_of_postability_one_source_19 () = Note ("POSTABILITY-ONE-SOURCE", 19u32, "2026-11-01T12:40Z", "x", "2026-11-01 08:40 x: ITEM UPGRADE (round 4). Fields: tags gain team:x (it's a cross-lane item with main and tibia, and x owns the TLD half). DEFINITION OF DONE for x's half: either (a) n9 step 4 retires the TLD list, so x deletes tlds.txt and tlds.spi together and the equality test with them; or (b) tlds.txt must change before that, so tlds.spi is regenerated by a Spiral step and the_list_is_postability_tlds_txt passes. Artifact: the commit-ready diff. NEXT STEP: none until (a) or (b) triggers (n13). TYPEFEATURESET PLAN for (b): the generator packs labels into chunks typed 'chunk = { labels : list string; weight : i32 }' under the per-cell budget constant, never by counting characters in a joined string.")
inl note_of_postability_one_source_20 () = Note ("POSTABILITY-ONE-SOURCE", 20u32, "2026-11-01T17:14Z", "compiler", "11-01 13:12 compiler: DRAFT MOVED OUT OF THE REPO (user 11-01 13:0x rule: drafts live in the kit drafts store until ready as glue or types). retire-x-compat.kitdo now lives at %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\spiral\\POSTABILITY-ONE-SOURCE\\retire-x-compat.kitdo (sha256 E405F44A0CDF..., byte-identical move, verified by hash). apps/compiler/drafts/ is gone (it was untracked, so git shows nothing). Gate unchanged: it ships in a pack only after x and tibia confirm the Cells check. When it ships, pass the store path to build-candidate.ps1 as the applier.")
inl note_of_postability_one_source_21 () = Note ("POSTABILITY-ONE-SOURCE", 21u32, "2026-11-02T01:22Z", "tibia-lane", "11-01 21:2x tibia CONFIRMS its side of the gate on compiler's retire-x-compat (n: 'ships only after x and tibia confirm the Cells check'). git grep -i 'postab|x_compat|tld' over tibia src, apps, results and scripts finds 0 references. scripts/build.ps1 calls 'mix spiral.cell_budget --check' (under the erl memory guard), and its suite.spi decoder reads the 'cell-budget report' line typed (budget_report_of, with hashtag and cashtag kinds). Today's tibia CI runs 41350993071 (green) and 41366570407 (budget step green; red only in 4 unrelated test files) passed that check on both OS. Retiring --x-compat breaks nothing in tibia; only x's confirmation remains.")
inl note_of_postability_one_source_22 () = Note ("POSTABILITY-ONE-SOURCE", 22u32, "2026-11-02T17:17Z", "compiler", "11-02 13:20 compiler: STEP 3 (retire --x-compat) SCHEDULED for pack17. Gate status: tibia confirmed (n21). For x, a reader census today finds 0 references to x-compat / x_compat / xCompat in C:\\home\\git\\x and spiral/apps/cells; x was told it ships unless it objects before handover. retire-x-compat.kitdo (drafts store) still dry-applies on live (core 2 replacements at 34311/34972, host 2 at 1295/1351). It will be re-checked stacked on the pack16c list at pack time. CELL-BUDGET-NAMING closes when it installs (resilience routing 11-02).")
inl note_of_postability_one_source_23 () = Note ("POSTABILITY-ONE-SOURCE", 23u32, "2026-11-02T17:19Z", "compiler", "11-02 13:25 compiler: x CONFIRMED step 3, on one condition, now met. The last live caller of --x-compat was i574n.github/scripts/x-compat-census.ps1:22 (tibia's POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS n142 census tool; 0 script callers, only backlog mentions, and its port target is x's `xc postable census`, POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS n160/n162). Archived to %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\archive\\20261102-131918-x-compat-census\\ (hash 79A9D98719887909, verified equal) and DELETED from the worktree, unstaged (git status: \" D scripts/x-compat-census.ps1\"). The cell-budget check lives in Cells as `mix spiral.cell_budget --check`. Gate complete; retire-x-compat rides pack17.")
inl note_of_postability_one_source_24 () = Note ("POSTABILITY-ONE-SOURCE", 24u32, "2026-11-02T18:55Z", "compiler", "11-02 14:55 compiler: STEP 3 DONE. --x-compat is retired in the installed compiler (pack17, box 14:50). This closes the compiler side of CELL-BUDGET-NAMING (resilience routing 11-02); the cell-budget check is `mix spiral.cell_budget --check` in Cells.")
inl note_of_postability_one_source_25 () = Note ("POSTABILITY-ONE-SOURCE", 25u32, "2026-11-02T22:29Z", "tibia", "11-02 18:3x tibia: tibia's side verified clean against the tree: git ls-files in C:\\home\\git\\tibia has no postability/TLD/profile file and there is no postability* folder outside deps/ and target/, so no copy remains in tibia; the item's remaining copies are in x and spiral Cells only.")
inl note_of_postability_one_source_26 () = Note ("POSTABILITY-ONE-SOURCE", 26u32, "2026-11-03T07:34Z", "x", "2026-11-03 03:34 x: GROOM: status Active -> Blocked. By n19's own rule x has no next step until (a) the repo-wide :: respell retires the TLD list, or (b) tlds.txt must change. Tibia's share is verified clean (n-?), and Cells' data/tlds.txt and x's postability/ copies are the remaining ones. Resume condition: main records SPIRAL-PATH-SYNTAX's respell round as done in every repo (then x deletes tlds.txt and tlds.spi with the equality test, and Cells drops data/tlds.txt), or a TLD change is needed. Owner x for the x and Cells deletions.")
inl node_always_fed () = Task ("NODE-ALWAYS-FED", "The worker node never sits idle while heavy, node-runnable work exists: agile routes such items to the node, and an idle check pulls the next one (or alerts) when the node has no running or queued job", Story, P0, D3, 55u32, Active, "", "node,agile,scheduling,coordination,main,kit,remote,team:kit", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_node_always_fed () = Planning ("NODE-ALWAYS-FED", Minutes 180u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_node_always_fed_1 () = Note ("NODE-ALWAYS-FED", 1u32, "2026-10-31T21:48Z", "main", "10-31 17:48 main. USER 10-31 17:4x: 'why's the node idle? sign the lanes messed up coordination and agile'. MEASURED: at 17:42 the node had 0 running and 0 queued jobs; the last ones (kit verify-persist x2, compiler p11-speed x3, x parityprobe) had finished within the half hour. ROOT CAUSES: (1) no standing node queue: lanes send node jobs ad hoc while their current item needs them, so the node empties whenever no lane is mid-proof; (2) agile next ranks by priority/WSJF and load but knows nothing about machines, so heavy node-suitable items are never pulled to FILL the node; (3) 'keep the node busy' is only a memory (keep-node-busy), with no mechanism, and main itself left node-shaped work unqueued this afternoon (the Rust-lock re-measure, dice's lambda proof, a post-land lib sweep). IMMEDIATE (main): mirror synced (REMOTE-SYNC-OK, head fd70dccf) and job-1793483260661-main-lib-sweep queued: every lib file with tests gated on the node against today's lib (headings, lambda parens, F# retire, union rename), the day's strongest regression check. DESIGN: (a) a 'node' tag or field on items whose heavy step runs unattended on the node, plus 'kit agile next --machine node' that ranks them; (b) a node-idle check (kit schedule or the hourly containment) that, when the node has no running/queued job, starts the top node item's ready script or messages its team; (c) each team keeps at least one node-runnable item ready, the node analogue of 'always keep 2 light items ready'; (d) the resource report shows node idle minutes per day as the KPI.")
inl note_of_node_always_fed_2 () = Note ("NODE-ALWAYS-FED", 2u32, "2026-10-31T22:01Z", "kit", "10-31 17:5x kit: kit queued node work right after main's 17:42 report. job-1793483855773 kit-node-verify-wide runs 18 touched-module notebooks (CLI ones against candidate BB3616FB), and job-1793484065827 kit-node-train7-preview is chained after it: candidate-build over the 28 kit crates changed since the installed TRAIN5e snapshot, then all 30 changed modules with tests via notebook-batch -Bin. Roughly 3-4 h of node work, previewing TRAIN7's notebook phase off the box. kit's rule from here: before a kit wait, check 'remote.ps1 node job list' and queue a node-runnable kit step if the node is empty (the mirror kit-verify plus kit/scripts/notebook-batch.ps1, regen-sizes.ps1 and candidate-build.ps1 make that a single call).")
inl note_of_node_always_fed_3 () = Note ("NODE-ALWAYS-FED", 3u32, "2026-11-01T03:40Z", "kit", "10-31 23:5x kit groom, in kit's CPU-light top-3. NEXT CONCRETE STEP: make 'node idle' visible where lanes decide, without a new script: extend the existing kit_remote_status package so 'kit remote status' prints running/queued node jobs per lane plus an IDLE flag (no running job), then have the Stop gate hook (autonomous-gate.ps1, thin glue) add one line when the node is idle: 'node idle: start a node-runnable item (agile list --tag node)'. Node-runnable items get the tag 'node' so the gate can name one. Tonight's check (23:0x) showed the node saturated by 4 lanes, so the gap is the idle hours, not tonight.")
inl note_of_node_always_fed_4 () = Note ("NODE-ALWAYS-FED", 4u32, "2026-11-01T12:36Z", "kit", "11-01 08:3x kit QUALITY PASS. DoD: 'kit remote status' prints running and queued node jobs per lane and IDLE when none run (CLI test against a fake job list); the Stop gate adds 'node idle: start a node-runnable item' and names the top item tagged node when idle (pipe test); measured: node idle minutes per day reported by KIT-AGILE-CADENCE, target < 60 min idle while node-tagged items are ready. NEXT STEP: extend src/kit_remote_status with a node_activity union and the IDLE line; tag node-runnable items 'node' (KIT-SCALE, KIT-EXEC-METRICS, CELLS-RUST-OPT-LEVEL already). TYPEFEATURESET PLAN: node_activity = Busy of list lane_job | Idle (closed union, no 'count = 0' sentinel); lane as a nominal; job state reused from kit_jobs' union, not parsed strings.")
inl note_of_node_always_fed_5 () = Note ("NODE-ALWAYS-FED", 5u32, "2026-11-01T19:44Z", "kit", "11-01 15:43 kit: FROZEN-BUSY FIX (main 15:3x: node-status.txt said BUSY from 10-30 to 15:29 because node-watch died). node-watch.ps1 (unstaged) now writes a stale_after=<now + max(5 min, 3 intervals)> field into every status line, with the rule in the line itself (\"past stale_after this file is UNKNOWN, never BUSY\"), plus machine= and one status file per machine (<machine>-status.txt; node-status.txt kept for node/laptop) and per-machine logs. remote.ps1 Linux status prints cpu N% from /proc/stat, so one watcher serves Windows and Linux. A vultr watcher is live: job-1793562109234 (vultr-status.txt: cpu=4% AVAILABLE). Follow-up for kit: the reader side becomes a kit verb (kit machine status <machine>) that applies stale_after mechanically, part of PS1-TO-SPIRAL's watcher port.")
inl note_of_node_always_fed_6 () = Note ("NODE-ALWAYS-FED", 6u32, "2026-11-02T15:01Z", "kit", "kit 11-02: TRAIN-ON-NODE (P0) moves kit release trains (regen + notebooks, ~3 h of box CPU per train) to the node; first run TRAIN12.")
inl note_of_node_always_fed_7 () = Note ("NODE-ALWAYS-FED", 7u32, "2026-11-04T05:49Z", "main", "11-04 01:5x main: USER RULE (11-04 00:0x): 'prioritize long heavy runs in the node instead of here, we should be the last machine to get filled, battletest/dogfeed the train that way' (memory heavy-runs-node-first-box-last). Tonight's data: TRAIN18 ran its regen on the node, 295 crates published in 53 min, against about 3 h on the box for TRAIN17b; it failed only on one over-1000 crate (kit_heal_nameof). kit's s3 precheck took 7.4 min on the node. vultr has only 2 CPUs and sat at load 10-16 when every lane routed checks there: --check attestations hit the 30 s default (VOID; --timeout-ms 150000), and 3 orphaned rustc builds stacked up after tool-moved ssh sessions. So the IDLE line should also report OVERSUBSCRIBED: load / nproc > 2 means route to the other machine, so the node-first rule doesn't just move the pile to vultr.")
inl note_of_node_always_fed_8 () = Note ("NODE-ALWAYS-FED", 8u32, "2026-11-04T11:12Z", "main", "11-04 07:2x main: snapshot while the box and the node sat at 100%: vultr load 0.81 on 2 CPUs (idle), so heavy checks should have gone there first. The box-side kernel gates this hour were main's own slip, because vultr's earlier load was misread from a stale check. vultr disk 97% (2.8 GB free of 94 GB): /data 50 GB (x-dumps /data/x/cache 40 GB, /data/enryu43 9 GB), /root/remote-stage/fiscal-check 8 GB; owners asked to trim to ~85%. Node disk 14.5 GB free after the 02:1x sweep. Rule for the gate text and for lanes: re-read load right before choosing a machine (cat /proc/loadavg on vultr, LoadPercentage on the node).")
inl note_of_node_always_fed_9 () = Note ("NODE-ALWAYS-FED", 9u32, "2026-11-04T12:18Z", "main", "11-04 08:4x main OVERCORRECTION: vultr load 26.9/22.4/20.6 on 2 CPUs (fiscal generator under pack27, two kit --check runs under pack34, main's html build, another compile), while the node also sits at 100%. Since the user's node-first rule lanes pile onto vultr, and 13x oversubscription on 2 cores makes every vultr run slow and flaky (main's html build: 22+ min for a ~8 min job). RULE PROPOSAL for the gate text and NODE-ALWAYS-FED: vultr admits at most 2 concurrent heavy runs (one per CPU); above loadavg 4, queue on the node (4x more cores) or wait, never stack. kit's job host could enforce it with a per-host slot cap (MACHINE-SLOT-CAPS). Also seen: a kit test sleeper (/tmp/kit spiral test job kill on close ... sleeper child) alive 15.5 h at 0% CPU on vultr, harmless but orphaned.")
inl note_of_node_always_fed_10 () = Note ("NODE-ALWAYS-FED", 10u32, "2026-11-04T15:16Z", "main", "11-04 11:1x main: the n9 rule is now in the STOP GATE every lane reads (~/.claude/hooks/autonomous-gate.ps1): \"Before a heavy run, re-read the target's load (vultr: cat /proc/loadavg; node: LoadPercentage): vultr has 2 CPUs, so above loadavg 4 send it to the node instead of stacking.\" Seen live in main's next block. The job-host cap (MACHINE-SLOT-CAPS, kit) remains the durable fix; raw ssh runs bypass it until TRAIN22's dispatch.")
inl note_of_node_always_fed_11 () = Note ("NODE-ALWAYS-FED", 11u32, "2026-11-04T16:55Z", "main", "11-04 12:5x main SNAPSHOT (inverse of n8): node LoadPercentage 100 (TRAIN22 regen since 11:41, plus vmmem), vultr loadavg 0.44/1.24/3.04 with only k3s/containerd/iggy resident: vultr sat idle while the node was full. Acted at once: main's pending kernel gate for literal rounds 2-3 went to vultr. Pattern of the day: the two machines alternate between saturated and idle, and lanes read the state late or not at all, so the rule in the stop gate (re-read load before choosing) is necessary but not sufficient. DURABLE FIX stays MACHINE-SLOT-CAPS + this item: a kit job host that knows both machines' free slots and places each queued heavy job on the freer one (vultr 2 slots, node ~8), so lanes submit jobs, not ssh commands.")
inl note_of_node_always_fed_12 () = Note ("NODE-ALWAYS-FED", 12u32, "2026-11-04T17:02Z", "kit", "11-04 17:0x kit: DoD part 1 BUILT (unstaged): `kit window remote <alias> [--bin <kit>]` (kit_remote_status) now also runs `<kit> job list` on the machine and prints the node_activity union: NodeBusy jobs -> one line per lane \"kit remote status jobs machine=<m> lane=<lane> running=<r> queued=<q>\" (lane = label prefix, finished jobs ignored); NodeIdle queued -> \"kit remote status IDLE machine=<m> running=0 queued=<n> (start a node-runnable item: kit agile list --tag node)\". Test a_job_list_becomes_per_lane_activity_or_idle (2 lanes, idle with a queued job, lane_of). vultr --check ok with bodies; box notebook job-1793811420520-kit-remote-status-tests. LEFT: the Stop-gate line (main's autonomous-gate.ps1: when IDLE, name the top node-tagged item) and the idle-minutes measure.")
inl note_of_node_always_fed_13 () = Note ("NODE-ALWAYS-FED", 13u32, "2026-11-04T21:40Z", "main", "11-04 17:4x main INCIDENT for this item's health side: the node's C: hit 0 GB free about 17:28 (kit: 11 kit-regeneration staged workspaces of 0.7-1.25 GB piling up under spiral-bin\\kit-regeneration; compiler also cleared 20 finished review dirs under C:\\home\\remote-stage). It broke TRAIN24's regen, kit's size run and compiler's uniontag4 job; now 12.8 GB free, TRAIN25 relaunched. main's own footprint there is ~74 MB (mirror main-kernel 71 MB, 3 MB build dir). A \"node fed\" check needs a disk floor next to load: refuse new heavy jobs (or prune finished workspaces first) under ~10 GB free, since one train regen stages ~1 GB per workspace. Same day on vultr the bottleneck was RAM (3.4 GB, a 2.4 GB cell OOM-killed k3s pods), so the dispatcher in MACHINE-SLOT-CAPS should weigh disk and memory, not only CPU slots.")
inl note_of_node_always_fed_14 () = Note ("NODE-ALWAYS-FED", 14u32, "2026-11-04T22:54Z", "kit", "11-04 19:0x kit: main's DISK FLOOR (17:4x incident note) BUILT (unstaged) in `kit window remote <alias>` (kit_remote_status): a third ssh call runs the machine's own `kit machine health --gate --min-disk-gb 0 --max-busy-pct 1000` (sysinfo disk_free_gb, already in every installed kit; verified live on the node: \"disk=10gb ram=5058mb busy=95pct\") and prints a typed node_disk: \"kit remote status disk machine=<m> free_gb=<n> floor_gb=10\", or under 10 GB \"kit remote status DISK-LOW machine=<m> free_gb=<n> floor_gb=10 (prune finished staged workspaces before a heavy job)\", or \"disk unknown ... reason=\" for an old kit. Floor 10 GB per main's note (one regen stages ~1 GB per workspace). The node sits AT 10 GB right now, during TRAIN25. Test a_health_gate_line_becomes_a_disk_state_against_the_floor (10gb ok, 0gb low, unknown verb). vultr --check ok with bodies; box notebook kit-remote-disk-tests. Pairs with the launch-train.ps1 5 GB refusal (DISK-CLEANUP). LEFT: the auto-pull of the next node item when IDLE.")
inl note_of_node_always_fed_15 () = Note ("NODE-ALWAYS-FED", 15u32, "2026-11-04T22:59Z", "kit", "11-04 18:5x kit: disk floor PROVEN on the box (kit-remote-disk-tests 3/3, incl. a_health_gate_line_becomes_a_disk_state_against_the_floor). Node size run kit-size-argsdisk pending.")
inl note_of_node_always_fed_16 () = Note ("NODE-ALWAYS-FED", 16u32, "2026-11-04T23:00Z", "kit", "11-04 19:0x kit: the IDLE line now names the ranked command, \"(start the top node-runnable item: kit agile next <backlog root> --tag node --open 3)\"; `agile next --tag node` works in the installed kit (ready=18 today), while the old hint `agile list --tag node` was unranked. vultr --check ok. The PULL half: proposed to main to fold `kit window remote laptop-ts` (IDLE / DISK-LOW + top 3 --tag node items) into autonomous-gate.ps1's stop message, since that gate is what every lane already reads when it stops; it is main's hook, so kit only proposed.")
inl note_of_node_always_fed_17 () = Note ("NODE-ALWAYS-FED", 17u32, "2026-11-04T23:05Z", "main", "11-04 19:1x main DECISION on kit's proposal: kit window remote <alias> (riding TRAIN26, not installed) prints node disk against a 10 GB floor (DISK-LOW) and an IDLE line pointing at kit agile next <root> --tag node --open 3 (18 ready node items today). The \"pull the next one when idle\" half goes into the stop gate (~/.claude/hooks/autonomous-gate.ps1) as a FOURTH parallel thread job (kit window remote laptop-ts) inside the existing 15 s kit budget, adding the IDLE or DISK-LOW line plus the top 3 node items to the block message. WIRE IT ONLY AFTER the installed kit has the verb (post TRAIN26): until then it would cost every lane's stop a failing call. Add hooks-test.ps1 cases for IDLE, DISK-LOW and a missing verb (fails open).")
inl note_of_node_always_fed_18 () = Note ("NODE-ALWAYS-FED", 18u32, "2026-11-04T23:12Z", "kit", "11-04 19:1x kit: HEADROOM SPLIT (unstaged, rides TRAIN27; TRAIN26's snapshot holds the 18:59 status.spi = 996 lines, checked): kit_remote_status was 4 lines under the gate, so the job-activity half moved to NEW linked crate kit_remote_activity (lane_job, node_activity, lane_of, job_of, activity_of, activity_lines + its test), exporting kit_remote_activity_text activity_text ((machine, output)) -> the joined lines and kit_remote_field_text field_text ((line, key)) (the disk parser reads disk= through it). status.spi keeps the windows/disk parts; package.spiproj links kit_remote_activity; Cargo.toml dep + workspace member + Cargo.lock entry; `cargo metadata --offline --locked` exit 0; vultr --check ok on both (with bodies). Node size kit-size-remote-split and box notebooks kit-remote-split-tests pending.")
inl note_of_node_always_fed_19 () = Note ("NODE-ALWAYS-FED", 19u32, "2026-11-04T23:16Z", "kit", "11-04 19:2x kit: split PROVEN: node size kit_remote_status 996 -> 582, kit_remote_activity 476; box notebooks 2/2 (activity 1/1, status 2/2). Rides TRAIN27.")
inl note_of_node_always_fed_20 () = Note ("NODE-ALWAYS-FED", 20u32, "2026-11-05T02:20Z", "main", "11-04 22:2x main STAR EXCHANGE: fiscal asks for a node DISK-FREE alarm (free swung 3.6-14 GB within hours; the 17:28 0-GB event half-broke fiscal's t1101 restore, killed TRAIN24 and compiler's uniontag4). Owners: main + resilience. kit already added the 10 GB DISK-LOW warning to kit window remote and a 5 GB refusal in launch-train (both unstaged, riding TRAIN26), so the alarm can reuse that check from resilience's hourly kit-machine-watch task once installed.")
inl note_of_node_always_fed_21 () = Note ("NODE-ALWAYS-FED", 21u32, "2026-11-05T04:35Z", "main", "11-05 00:35 main NODE DISK at 10.5 GB (alarm 15) diagnosed: no single new hog. Stage dirs are small (fiscal under 0.9 GB, all in use; main none). The biggest dirs: git mirrors spiral 6.1 / polyglot 4.3 (retired repo) / dice 3.3 GB; Temp\\spiral-cells-compiler 2.3 GB (11-04 11:34); r2r-cache 1.6 GB; Temp\\bgnajmgc 1.45 GB (11-03); Temp\\wsl-crashes 1.04 GB. LIVE FINDING: the node's WSL Ubuntu (Running since 10-25 15:16, vmmem 964 MB, older than the no-WSL-on-the-node rule) has WSLg's /usr/bin/weston crash-looping every ~105 s, each crash writing a 107 MB dump (rolling 10 kept). Asked resilience and x-dumps whether anyone uses it; one-shot at 01:47 runs `wsl --shutdown` if nobody claims it. Also: compiler pruned 578 r2r-old/replaced files (822 MB) from the node compiler dir, and main's pack-chain/swap-inplace now exclude and prune them on every ship.")
inl note_of_node_always_fed_22 () = Note ("NODE-ALWAYS-FED", 22u32, "2026-11-05T04:40Z", "main", "11-05 00:40 main NODE DISK reclaimed: deleted the node's retired polyglot mirror C:\\home\\git\\polyglot (4.31 GB) after a check: clean tree, 0 commits ahead of origin (github i574n/polyglot), no process using it (probe shells excluded), no scheduled task, not in remote.ps1's sync list; its 2 stashes (auto \"laptop pre-update 2026-10-25\": generated .dib.html/.ipynb renders plus a LICENSE) were kept as patches in the box's %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\archive\\node-polyglot-stashes-20261105. Node free 10.5 -> 14.7 GB. Also shipped pack38 to the node (host 08B00F25 / core 15FD51D7 = box) so kit can launch TRAIN27; resilience endorses the WSL shutdown (one-shot at 01:47).")
inl note_of_node_always_fed_23 () = Note ("NODE-ALWAYS-FED", 23u32, "2026-11-05T05:55Z", "main", "11-05 01:55 main WSL SHUT DOWN on the node (resilience endorsed; no lane claimed it, x-dumps included): wsl --shutdown, Ubuntu now Stopped, vmmem gone (~1 GB RAM back), the 10 weston crash dumps (1.04 GB) removed. Node C: 17.1 GB free (from 10.5 at the alarm). Separately, x asked that node compiler swaps be announced: the pack40/41 node ship waits for TRAIN27 AND x-next8 (job-1793858062706), then main announces before running pack-chain -SkipInstall.")
inl note_of_node_always_fed_24 () = Note ("NODE-ALWAYS-FED", 24u32, "2026-11-05T06:07Z", "main", "11-05 02:07 main NODE SHIPPED pack40 at 02:05 (host 895300935E291274 / core DDCA78971B4EAB36 = box; 41 archive entries; 5 old files pruned; launch ok) DURING TRAIN27, on x's GO: x-next8 died in 143 s because the live lib's reflection.spi:334 uses pack39's UnionCaseName op, and every node Gleam build compiling it failed on pack38. A node train pins its own compiler at launch (kit), so TRAIN27 is unaffected. pack-chain.ps1 gained -AllowTrain (ship despite a running train, saying so) and -SkipVultr (vultr was thrashing from another lane's uncapped compile loop; it recovered on its own). kit and fiscal were told before the swap, x after. The vultr Linux build of pack39/40 is still owed (pack38 is the last built there).")
inl note_of_node_always_fed_25 () = Note ("NODE-ALWAYS-FED", 25u32, "2026-11-05T14:10Z", "main", "11-05 10:10 main TRAIN UNBLOCK, status: TRAIN22-29 were all red; the last two died at regen on compiler Rust linked-shape bugs (TRAIN28 E0308: a struct import matched by field-type TEXT across crates with their own UH0; TRAIN29 E0425: a hand-written struct inside a mod taken as a linked shape). compiler fixed both (LINKED-UNITS-RUST-STRUCT-TYPES 3 + 4) in pack48, and pack49 (same core, new host) followed. main shipped pack48 (09:21) and pack49 (09:39) to the node in x's named gaps, and built both on vultr, so box = node = vultr. kit's gate on pack48: full regen of 348 owners + cargo check --workspace --keep-going = ERRORS=0 CARGO-EXIT=0 (10:0x), the first clean regen in nine trains. TRAIN30 is cleared to launch.")
inl note_of_node_always_fed_26 () = Note ("NODE-ALWAYS-FED", 26u32, "2026-11-05T16:02Z", "main", "11-05 12:02 main TRAIN30 (pack49 core 0BA4A608, launched 10:25): REGEN PASSED, every owner regenerated and cargo check clean, the first train past regen since TRAIN21; compiler's linked-shape fixes 3+4 held in the field. It stopped at the 1000-line crate gate (11:59) on 4 generated crates: kit_job_slot 1311, kit_job_host 1090, kit_job_control 1105 (kit's morning heals; splits in the tree for TRAIN31) and agile_claim_rank 1025 (flagged to kit). Node shipped pack51 (core E190545B5F30A7B4) at 12:01 in x's gap, so box = node for TRAIN31; vultr build queued. pack-chain now prunes main's old ship stages after each ship (pack49's pruned on first use).")
inl note_of_node_always_fed_27 () = Note ("NODE-ALWAYS-FED", 27u32, "2026-11-05T18:19Z", "main", "11-05 14:19 main TRAIN31 (pack51, launched 12:43): red at regen 14:18, on a KIT-SIDE link error, not a compiler one: E0433 in regenerated kit_cli_routes/routes.rs:195, kit_table_filter::kit_table_filter_kept called but kit_table_filter not linked (cargo suggests kit_table_logs::kit_table_filter_kept). Sent to kit with the regen tree path. TRAIN30's four size overs were fixed and stayed fixed; compiler fixes 3+4 held again (no E0308/E0425). Box = node = vultr on pack51 (vultr built 12:56).")
inl note_of_node_always_fed_28 () = Note ("NODE-ALWAYS-FED", 28u32, "2026-11-05T19:13Z", "main", "main 15:09 11-05: pack52 shipped to the node in place inside x's named gap (x-next10 kept its pinned pack51 copy). node SpiralCompiler.dll A1BF06EB9AD87F00 / Core A688EE5AD426A5A0 = box; launch ok; pack-chain pruned the pack51 stage. kit/x/compiler told. vultr build retrying behind the 2100 MB headroom guard (pending).")
inl note_of_node_always_fed_29 () = Note ("NODE-ALWAYS-FED", 29u32, "2026-11-05T19:58Z", "resilience", "ALARM-DELIVERY FIXED (resilience, unstaged) - node-utilization-monitor.ps1:64 was calling `kit story note ... REMOTE-OFFLOAD-FIRST ... *> $null`: story is not a verb (renamed to agile), REMOTE-OFFLOAD-FIRST does not exist, and *> $null swallowed both failures - so every WASTE/DISK-LOW alarm since the rename was dropped silently (main found today DISK-LOW 13GB detected at 15:45 but never delivered). Fix: now `kit agile note <root> NODE-ALWAYS-FED \"...\" --as resilience` via Invoke-Bounded capturing rc, and on rc!=0 it appends \"NOTE FAILED rc=N\" to node-utilization-monitor.last.log instead of discarding - a guard that cannot deliver now says so. This note IS the proof the delivery path works. resilience 2026-11-05 15:5x.")
inl note_of_node_always_fed_30 () = Note ("NODE-ALWAYS-FED", 30u32, "2026-11-05T20:08Z", "main", "main 11-05 16:07: pack52 also built from source on vultr (pack-chain retry attempt 13, once MemAvailable reached 2107 MB over the 2100 floor; 78.6 s; launch exit 0). The retries had waited ~1 h at 2070 MB (k3s + iggy baseline, ockam-iggy-edge restarted deliberately at 15:56). Box, node and vultr are all on pack52 now. TRAIN32 runs on the node (job-1793906628967-kit-TRAIN32), verdict pending.")
inl note_of_node_always_fed_31 () = Note ("NODE-ALWAYS-FED", 31u32, "2026-11-05T23:27Z", "main", "main 11-05 19:2x NODE STATE: fed. 100% CPU, 8 GB RAM free, 5 running jobs (kit-TRAIN32 notebooks, x-next11, tibia-tf8-build, tibia-np5-gate-2, fiscal-twin2) + 2 queued behind them (tibia-tf8-sweep, fiscal-reports-gate2). DISK: 20 GB free at 15:5x after the .part sweep, now 17 GB, ~2 GB/h under this load, so the 15 GB floor comes around 20:3x. resilience's node-utilization-monitor alarm now DELIVERS (it notes here since its 16:2x fix), so the next DISK-LOW will reach this item; owners of the running jobs should delete their stage/regen dirs as each result is noted.")
inl note_of_node_always_fed_32 () = Note ("NODE-ALWAYS-FED", 32u32, "2026-11-05T23:49Z", "main", "main 11-05 19:5x INCIDENT: the node was unreachable from ~19:46 to 19:48:50 (about 3 min). Both routes timed out on port 22 AND ICMP got no reply (LAN 192.168.2.28 and Tailscale 100.97.57.53; tailscale showed 'offline, last seen 1m ago', then 'direct 192.168.2.28'), so it was a network drop, not sshd starved by CPU. NO REBOOT: uptime 16,363 min (boot 10-25 11:06). Node jobs SURVIVED: kit-TRAIN32 notebooks, x-next11, tibia-tf8-sweep and fiscal-twin2 still running. Box-side casualties: waiters that called remote.ps1 during the window, e.g. TRAIN32's box half (job-1793906232412, exit 1 'no route') and main's watcher. kit is filing a retry for the box-side train poll; any box-side waiter should retry 'no route' a few times before failing. DISK: 14 GB free now, below the 15 GB floor, so resilience's fixed monitor should deliver a DISK-LOW note here on its next tick (first real test of the 16:2x delivery fix).")
inl note_of_node_always_fed_33 () = Note ("NODE-ALWAYS-FED", 33u32, "2026-11-05T23:58Z", "resilience-614162e5", "11-05 ~15:0x resilience FIXED node-utilization-monitor fail-open (main caught it; unstaged, parse-clean, tested). BUG: all 3 probes returned -1 sentinel (root cause = $MirrorNode=$NodeRoutes[0]=laptop hostname which flakes to resolve desktop-6e8c14g; vultr -1 = 15s bound under 100% box load) yet the run printed \"ok\" because $diskLow/idle/waste all require >=0, so a guard that could not MEASURE reported healthy while node was really <15GB. FIX: (1) route-fallback Invoke-NodeRoute loops $NodeRoutes first-that-answers (remote.ps1 pattern), mirror-CPU+disk combined into one probe; (2) FAIL-CLOSED: any -1 -> \"UNKNOWN: <probe> failed - cannot measure\" not ok; 2 consecutive UNKNOWN -> NODE-ALWAYS-FED note ([DELIVERED] marker dedups a long outage) + exit 1; (3) logs route + per-probe ms. Test: \"ok: box36% mirror100% compute252% node-free16GB [route=laptop mirror=4215ms compute=4918ms]\". Same fail-open-one-step-earlier shape as the guards-fail-closed rule. resilience-614162e5.")
inl note_of_node_always_fed_34 () = Note ("NODE-ALWAYS-FED", 34u32, "2026-11-06T00:43Z", "main", "main 11-05 20:4x TRAIN32 VERDICT (from kit): finished 20:42, notebooks 230/233, NOT installed. The 3 reds were all kit's own and are fixed in the tree (parents_cli argv, actor settle waits 20->60 s, mirror_cli argv). It was the first train since TRAIN21 past both regen and the crate gate. TRAIN33 launched 20:43 on the node (box job job-1793925783556-kit-TRAIN33-node; node and box on pack52; includes the launch-train ssh-blip retry), ETA ~01:00+, PENDING at main's run end 21:00. Next reader (kit lane or the next session): if it installs, the dogfood list is next --any (expect claimed=none), S7 edit --parent/list --tree, S10 coordinator dry run, M4 mirror proof, rename timing; then re-check LIB-MOVE-JSON-QUOTE's strict JSON and PROOF-SCOPE-BY-IMPORTS' kit repo scope.")
inl tibia_lib_consumer_regate () = Task ("TIBIA-LIB-CONSUMER-REGATE", "NODE job: tibia's full scripts/build.ps1 (all 56 test files, layout and postable gates included) against the post-land lib of 10-31 (env, listm', otp_actor, process, file_system, sm' changed by tibia's x-compat slices, plus main's headings, lambda-parens and F# retire waves), as the consumer check those lands have not had. Mirror tibia and spiral with remote.ps1 node sync -Mirror, run as a node job, and compare with K4 (TIBIA-EDITION3-ROUND n17: 56 files GREEN on 10-30)", TaskKind, P1, D3, 100u32, Done, "-", "team:tibia,tibia,lib,node,regression", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_tibia_lib_consumer_regate () = Planning ("TIBIA-LIB-CONSUMER-REGATE", Minutes 120u32, Load Heavy, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_tibia_lib_consumer_regate_1 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-CONSUMER-REGATE", 1u32, "2026-10-31T23:14Z", "tibia-lane", "10-31 19:1x tibia: PULLED and running as job-1793488430712-tibia-lib-consumer-regate on the node, cap 4 h. Mirror tibia-gates: tibia synced (REMOTE-SYNC-OK head 6859e568, digest 5F4BD53B; includes the box's unstaged tibia edits: the 34 lambda-parens sites, the postable gate and pz-in-traces), polyglot synced (head d01f61ec), and the mirror's tibia\\deps\\spiral and \\polyglot junctioned to the MIRROR copies, so tibia builds against the post-land spiral (digest 070CB919, the same lib the post-land gate passed). The runner is tibia\\scripts\\build.ps1 -Label postland: layout, postable, then every test file. Compare with K4 (TIBIA-EDITION3-ROUND n17: 56 files GREEN on 10-30).")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_consumer_regate_2 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-CONSUMER-REGATE", 2u32, "2026-11-01T02:58Z", "tibia-lane", "11-01 00:xx tibia: VERDICT RED, a consumer regression in the lib, not in tibia's lands. job-1793488430712 (221 min, node mirror tibia-gates): build.ps1 reports 32 of 56 test files not fully green. 24 of those have only unsupported targets on the node (no failures); 8 files have real failures: activity 2, the host actor-trace test file 7, character 6, map 3, scan 1, scenario 3, world_executor and world_port 2. ROOT (148 ETY errors, 197 Unbound): ETY-ModuleIndexFailed \"The module does not have the key: started\" at apps/host/world_port.spi:16 (type process.started), cascading into tibia's own types world, maybe_world, stepped and drive. process.spi still defines `:! started` directly under `:== started_switch` (the same adjacency as on 10-30 fd70dccf, when K4 passed); the difference is that main's headings wave removed the `/// ### started` heading above that cell. Hypothesis: the Cells module index keys the cell by heading or first definition, so `started` is no longer exported. 26 lib sites share the shape (async, base pair/choice2'/exn, channel sender/receiver, common disposable, convert, date_time, am' and more). Reported to main (owner of the headings tool and Cells) with the evidence. Separate: scan's C row hits \"a lib backend_switch with no arm for the C backend\" (an arm gap, owner to identify). tibia's 6 lands do not touch these regions (their post-land gate passed, POSTABLE n159). Next: rerun this job once main fixes or refutes the cause.")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_consumer_regate_3 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-CONSUMER-REGATE", 3u32, "2026-11-01T03:11Z", "tibia-lane", "11-01 tibia: n2's ROOT CAUSE IS RETRACTED. main's box probe (pack-11 compiler, live process.spi BE258A03) shows no ETY for process.started. On the box, tibia's build.ps1 -Files world_port is 2/2 GREEN (\"all 1 test files green\"). The node red came from the node's pre-pack-11 compiler: the job started about 19:14, and pack 11 reached the node at 20:29. The headings hypothesis was wrong: the compiler ignores comments, so a heading cannot change the module index. Lesson: before calling a node red a lib regression, check the node compiler digest (main named CC1F29DA5A8718F1, the pack 11 folder) and reproduce on the box. Next: resync the mirror, confirm the node compiler digest, rerun the full consumer build. Status back to Active.")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_consumer_regate_4 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-CONSUMER-REGATE", 4u32, "2026-11-01T03:43Z", "tibia-lane", "11-01 tibia: this is the NODE-RUNNABLE item. Rerun in flight: job-1793503032785-tibia-lib-consumer-regate2. Node compiler DFC86B91 = the box's (pack 11, active since 20:29); mirrors re-synced (spiral 587b3541 digest 67699CFF, tibia digest B237F3A9, polyglot); tibia deps junctions point to the mirror copies.")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_consumer_regate_5 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-CONSUMER-REGATE", 5u32, "2026-11-01T04:59Z", "tibia-lane", "11-01 02:xx tibia: ROOT CAUSE FOUND for both node reds (supersedes n2's hypothesis and n3's compiler explanation). regate2 reproduced the same 148 ETY errors with the node on the box's compiler (DFC86B91), so the compiler was not the cause. Cells resolved lib through tibia\\deps\\polyglot\\deps\\spiral\\lib\\spiral (Cells mount a675b8d7..., its spiral junction). In the node mirror, polyglot's deps linked to the node's SHARED polyglot deps, whose spiral is the node's shared C:\\home\\git\\spiral checkout: process.spi F75DF957 from 10-25. On the box that link goes to live spiral. So the node typed tibia against a 10-25 lib; today's lib is not regressed (box: world_port 2/2). FIX in the runner (stage consumer2.ps1): the mirror's polyglot\\deps becomes a real folder (spiral -> mirror spiral, every other entry -> shared), tibia\\deps\\spiral and \\polyglot -> mirror copies, all links unlinked before each sync (git clean must never meet a junction), and the hash of each route's process.spi is printed before the build. Rerun in flight on the respelled tree: job tibia-respell-full-build.")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_consumer_regate_6 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-CONSUMER-REGATE", 6u32, "2026-11-01T07:45Z", "tibia-lane", "11-01 03:45 tibia, node RESPELL FULL BUILD 2 (job-1793510072271): mirror tibia-gates synced from head 6859e568 with REMOTE-SYNC-OK; both lib routes on process.spi BE258A03. Exit 1 after 144 min, 30 of 56 files not green, in TWO classes. (A) MY BUG, from a respell cell split: apps/host/character.spi:119-120 put a '^^' continuation inside '( ... )' at column 5, left of the paren contents, so the parser closed early ('Expected: )'). Every host importer failed with it: activity, block_plan, character, scenario, world_executor, world_port (c 17, lua 11, gleam 5 failed; the rest not_run). Fixed with the file's own idiom: an unparenthesised ^^ fixture expected_kit_tail_rows. Local proof: build.ps1 -Files character,world_port gave 6/6 + 2/2. The split had passed the cell-budget check, but I had never run that file's tests, which is the lesson. (B) NODE ENV, not respell: 29 wgsl tests unsupported in about 20 files, because the mirror has no target/wgsl-run runner (build outputs aren't synced). Every other file is green on the respelled tree, including the new test-discovery regex. RELAUNCHED as job-1793519129551-tibia-respell-full-build3 at 03:4x on head b1a02bcb (REMOTE-SYNC-OK). Its runner now streams: the '| Select-Object -Last 80' trim was removed from consumer2.ps1, which in build 2 had dropped every early file's details.")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_consumer_regate_7 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-CONSUMER-REGATE", 7u32, "2026-11-01T07:57Z", "tibia-lane", "11-01 03:57 tibia: CI on the user's 02:36 commit b1a02bcb is RED on both platforms (gh-pages run 41281548884): 8 of 55 files, the same 8 on Windows and Linux. 7 are host importers of character.spi failing on its paren split (character.spi:120:5 'Expected: )'): activity, arena_host, block_plan, character, scenario, world_executor, world_port. The 8th is apps/suite, committed mid-iteration with the nested-update type error (suite.spi:68). BOTH ARE FIXED IN THE TREE, unstaged: character green locally (6/6, plus world_port 2/2), suite 7/7. CI's wgsl runner works (0 unsupported), so the node's 29 wgsl rows are node-only. The previous red run (6859e568, 10-31) was the tibia/postability read, fixed since. Next CI goes green once the user commits the tree; node build 3 (job-1793519129551) proves all 56 files first.")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_consumer_regate_8 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-CONSUMER-REGATE", 8u32, "2026-11-01T09:07Z", "tibia-lane", "11-01 05:07 tibia, node BUILD 3 VERDICT (job-1793519129551, mirror tibia-gates on head b1a02bcb + tree, REMOTE-SYNC-OK, lib routes on process.spi BE258A03, streaming runner, 73 min): 57 files, 0 failures from the respell or the character fix. 33 fully green; 23 differ only by the 29 wgsl rows that are unsupported on the node (no runner there; CI runs them). 1 REAL FAILURE: world_executor gleam, a_block_runs_through_real_programs_and_matches_the_simulated_trace. Its trace held a stray 'rerun' that sat at a different position on the node than locally: a SHARED FILE RACE that I introduced. My drift heal moved both tests' logs to fixed temp names (tibia-world-executor-block.log), and Cells runs the two tests concurrently, so each test read the other's lines. FIX: fake_log tag kind gives every test its own run and block log ('counted', 'sleeping'). Local: 2/2. So the respelled tree is green apart from the node's wgsl gap; three of my own regressions were caught and fixed (character paren, suite nested update, world_executor shared temp log), all unstaged, waiting for the user's commit.")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_consumer_regate_9 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-CONSUMER-REGATE", 9u32, "2026-11-01T12:36Z", "tibia-lane", "11-01 08:36 tibia QUALITY PASS. DEFINITION OF DONE, the remaining 10%: the gh-pages run on the user's next tibia commit (which includes character.spi, world_executor.spi, suite.spi and build.ps1) is green on both build (windows-2025) and build (ubuntu-24.04), i.e. 'build.ps1 / all N test files green' in both logs. NEXT STEP: after the user commits, 'gh run list --limit 1' then 'gh run view <id> --log-failed'; if green, set 100 Done here with the run id; if red, the failing file's detail lines go in a note. No typefeatureset work (proof item).")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_consumer_regate_10 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-CONSUMER-REGATE", 10u32, "2026-11-01T13:42Z", "tibia-lane", "11-01 09:42 tibia: the 3 changed Spiral files RE-PROVEN on the box's pack14a compiler (installed 09:31; it fixes pack 12's Rust E0425 'st = GroupN::' regression). build.ps1 -Files suite,character,world_executor: suite 7/7 (lua + rust), character 6/6 (c, lua, gleam), world_executor 2/2 (gleam), memo_hits 0 on all three, so nothing was replayed from pack12b. Tonight's node logs (builds 2 and 3) contain no E0425, so no tibia rows were VOIDed. The unstaged set is unchanged and still waits for the user's commit, then CI.")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_consumer_regate_11 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-CONSUMER-REGATE", 11u32, "2026-11-01T14:47Z", "tibia-lane", "11-01 10:47 tibia: CI on the user's 10:01 commit 8c8f6c3 (gh-pages run 41314129444). build (ubuntu-24.04) is RED, 4 of 55 files, and NONE are tonight's fixes: the 8 files red on b1a02bcb are all green now. The 4 are combat_text (5 unsupported), game_calls (3), ground_trace (1) and samples (1), all in apps/arena and apps/canary, unpinned tests that default to ZIG. The detail line is 'ground_trace.spi: zig failed (exit 1) / main.zig:160:25: error: expected ';' after statement'. These files are unchanged since b1a02bcb, whose Linux run passed them with the same respelled source, so this is a ZIG CODEGEN REGRESSION in the compiler CI builds from spiral main, routed to compiler. windows-2025 is still running at 10:47. The item stays at 90 until both jobs are green. UPDATE 11:0x: build (windows-2025) finished RED with the SAME 4 files and the same 'main.zig:160:25: error: expected ';' after statement'. So both platforms fail only on the Zig codegen regression, and all 51 other files are green on both, including every file touched tonight. The DoD's 'all N test files green' now waits only on the compiler fix. CORRECTION (compiler, 11:1x): my 'Zig codegen regression' attribution was WRONG. Root cause: in both OS jobs, spiral's apps/spiral/build.ps1 threw at line 27 compiling the spiral CLI ('TracedError: Cannot convert i64 to the following type: time_span', lib/spiral/convert.spi:75 via try_convert's Zig arm), after today's 08:42 lib land CONVERT-ZIG-UNATIVEINT routed try_convert through numeric_conv for nominal targets. With no spiral tool, Cells marked those cells tool_missing, which shows as 'unsupported'. The main.zig:160 line is a long-standing red herring (ground_trace's unpinned main pulls a Lua-only macro into a default-Zig build; the box prints it with pack11 too while passing 2/2). Compiler is landing the fix and tracking it on ZIG-EXPECTED-SEMICOLON-REGRESSION. My error: I read the first per-file detail line instead of the job's earlier setup steps, and the 'unsupported' status (not 'failed') should have pointed me at a missing tool.")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_consumer_regate_12 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-CONSUMER-REGATE", 12u32, "2026-11-01T17:14Z", "tibia-lane", "11-01 DoD met: CI run 41326996580 on 7d47bad (suite.spi retype + devcontainer) completed success on build (windows-2025), build (ubuntu-24.04) and push. The spiral CLI build, broken by CONVERT-ZIG-UNATIVEINT on run 41314129444, is fixed upstream, and the 4 files that went unsupported there now run. Closing.")
inl cells_in_spiral () = Task ("CELLS-IN-SPIRAL", "Cells without Elixir: port spiral/apps/cells (11,371 lines of Elixir) onto its existing Spiral domain, processes on BEAM via otp_actor, Kino/Livebook kept through Gleam modules with Elixir names", Epic, P1, D8, 5u32, Active, "CELLS-PORT-TARGETS,CELLS-PORT-MEMO,CELLS-PORT-COMPILER-CLIENT,CELLS-PORT-COMPILER-DAEMON,CELLS-PORT-RUNNER-MOUNTS,CELLS-PORT-NOTEBOOK,CELLS-PORT-KINO-FACADES,CELLS-PORT-CLIS,CELLS-XCOMPAT-SPIRAL,CELLS-DOCUMENT-TYPED,CELLS-MANIFEST-SOURCEMAP-SPIRAL,CELLS-HTML-PARITY,X-SIM-CELLS-SPIRAL,CELLS-TYPED-UNITS", "x,cells,spiral,beam,gleam,team:x", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_cells_in_spiral () = Planning ("CELLS-IN-SPIRAL", Minutes 4800u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_cells_in_spiral_1 () = Note ("CELLS-IN-SPIRAL", 1u32, "2026-10-31T22:02Z", "x", "2026-10-31 x: user decision ~17:50 (memory cells-in-spiral-x-leads): \"let's do it, try removing elixir entirely while keeping kino/livebook with gleam compilation/aliasing tricks ... a good fit for us to do this task for main, that way we don't lose our roots and stay on track. alternate with x items also\". FACTS (read, nothing built): spiral/apps/cells is 11,371 lines of Elixir in 44 files (largest notebook 1,451, compiler_daemon 1,183, cells 1,165, targets 859, compiler_client 824, memo 642) + 47 test files + 13 mix tasks; owner main (129 of ~230 apps/cells notes). Cells ALREADY has a Spiral core: apps/cells/spi (text, cell, directives, document 894 lines, notebook, targets, main; package.spiproj) is compiled to Gleam (build/dev/erlang, module spiral_cells@domain) and Spiral.Cells.Domain calls ~20 of its exports (prepare, patch_rust, split_output, unit_result, package_project, parse_dib, to_spi(_tested), to_spir(_tested), from_spi(_bounded), test_index, target_gaps, target_backend/extension, plan, plan_tests, encode/decode_cells). So the epic CONTINUES main's pattern to zero Elixir: (a) pure logic -> the spi domain; (b) processes (compiler_daemon, compiler_pool, server, runner, fsi_session, memory_guard, file_lock) -> Spiral on BEAM via lib otp_actor; (c) Kino/Livebook-facing modules (smart_cell, html, display, application) -> Gleam-compiled modules carrying Elixir.Spiral.Cells.* names (aliasing or a beam rename), Kino itself stays a dependency; (d) mix tasks -> a CLI entry like x's sim-run. Method: beside the Elixir code, a parity oracle per slice, consumers switched one at a time (every lane reads apps/cells live). Alternate slices with x items; keep a node-runnable step queued (NODE-ALWAYS-FED).")
inl note_of_cells_in_spiral_2 () = Note ("CELLS-IN-SPIRAL", 2u32, "2026-10-31T22:36Z", "x", "2026-10-31 18:36 x: the typefeatureset standard applied to CELLS-IN-SPIRAL (user 10-31, TYPEFEATURESET-ENFORCE n6; memory typefeatureset-default-all-code). The existing Cells Spiral domain (apps/cells/spi, 2,157 lines) is the old code to migrate first. kit census: gadt-case 0, existential 0, forall 1, encoded-row 7, rust_global 5. Its Elixir boundary is joined strings. directives.spi returns 'ok\\n' + newline/tab rows with \"1\"/\"0\" flags as i32. Errors are string kinds (dib, directive, flag, timeout, builder, backend) that directives.ex turns into messages. tokens is a hand-rolled list instead of lib list. cell.spi's split_output answers '0\\n'/'1\\n' prefixes. Once no Elixir caller remains, the encoding has no reason to exist, so each slice does three things: (1) model the domain as types: prepared = { code; print_code : bool; timeout : option i32; main : generated_main; packages : list string; skip : bool }, generated_main = NoMain | FromExpression | FromDefinitionsOnly, a directive_error union in place of string kinds, results through resultm, and lib list/listm in place of tokens; (2) keep ONE encode function at the export, used only while an Elixir caller still decodes it, and delete it in the slice that removes that caller; (3) use GADT indices where the path is static. The cell target GADT (target kind -> builder) and the daemon request GADT indexed by reply type are the first two (CELLS-CENSUS n2). Existentials only for compile-time heterogeneity: Spiral packs can't carry a runtime-chosen index (x-parity-typed1). Backend per component by BACKEND-BALANCE (CELLS-CENSUS n2): the domain stays neutral with rust+gleam test blocks; services are Gleam; CLIs are Rust over one GADT message family, the iggy split.")
inl note_of_cells_in_spiral_3 () = Note ("CELLS-IN-SPIRAL", 3u32, "2026-11-01T04:31Z", "x", "2026-11-01 00:30 x: CELLS-IN-SPIRAL slices MAIN can take without touching x's (user 00:2x: main helps x with the rewrite). x keeps CELLS-TYPED-DIRECTIVES (directives.spi, then cell.wrap returns main_source in cell.spi and notebook.spi:233) and the daemon protocol GADT with CompilerClient later. MAIN takes:\n(M1) CELLS-XCOMPAT-SPIRAL, reassigned to main because main is renaming XCompat to Spiral.Cells.CellBudget tonight: the CellBudget check as typed Spiral with an over_budget finding GADT.\n(M2) Document typed model (document.spi, 894 lines, already on main's window-6 list): the doc and cell records and a cell-kind union in place of its newline/tab-encoded rows, oracle document_test.\n(M3) a-pure Manifest (130) and SourceMap (65) ports (manifest.ex is main's window-6 file), oracle manifest_test and notebook_report_test.\nShared rule for both lanes: typefeatureset first (CELLS-IN-SPIRAL n2), lib through CELLS-SPI-LIB once it lands, drafts in notes, proofs on node mirrors, lands through cells_land_window with a train-hold.")
inl note_of_cells_in_spiral_4 () = Note ("CELLS-IN-SPIRAL", 4u32, "2026-11-01T12:36Z", "x", "2026-11-01 08:36 x: EPIC UPGRADE (round 4). DEFINITION OF DONE (verifiable):\n(1) No .ex under spiral/apps/cells/lib, and no mix tasks left (the d-class CLIs are Rust over one message GADT).\n(2) x/scripts/sim-run.ps1 runs no mix step for any entry (serve already skips it since tonight).\n(3) alphabet/apps/hangul/hangul.livemd evaluates its 4 Spiral.Cells smart cells headless through the renamed Gleam facades (aliasing proven in CELLS-CENSUS n3).\n(4) Every census slice's oracle (CELLS-CENSUS n3) passes.\nArtifact: a census rerun showing 0 Elixir lines.\nNEXT STEP: the morning window lands slice 1 (CELLS-SPI-LIB + CELLS-TYPED-DIRECTIVES). Main holds M1 to M3 (n3).\nTYPEFEATURESET PLAN, epic-level, binding for every slice:\n- Unions and generic results for parsers over runtime text (directives, document, memo keys).\n- GADTs only on static paths: the daemon protocol as 'request r', indexed by its reply type the way x's wire.spi does 'reads r'/'writes r'; the target -> builder map as 'target b' indexed by builder; typestate phantoms on notebook evaluation (parsed -> planned -> ran).\n- Phantom digests on memo keys (source digest x compiler core), so a stale core can't hit.\n- Existentials only for compile-time heterogeneity (memory typefeatureset-default-all-code).\n- No -1 or \"\" sentinels, no tab or newline-joined rows past the Elixir boundary, and each slice deletes its 'encoded' wire when its Elixir caller goes.")
inl note_of_cells_in_spiral_5 () = Note ("CELLS-IN-SPIRAL", 5u32, "2026-11-01T14:34Z", "x", "2026-11-01 10:34 x: SLICE PLAN, each slice <= 60 min (user 10:3x: 'x got the big task of rewriting cells but its full x sim might not even be complete yet'). Sim state first: SIM-X-FEATURE-PARITY is DONE (7/7 slices; the sim is e2e's default server since 08a49e2). What the sim still lacks is listed as S-slices, and the Cells port as C-slices. They interleave so the node never idles and neither waits on the other.\n1. C1 (45 min, box window): morning land of CELLS-SPI-LIB + CELLS-TYPED-DIRECTIVES + typed cell.wrap (runbook CELLS-TYPED-DIRECTIVES n5).\n2. S1 (30 min): stub retirement, second half. Archive stub.ps1, stub_http.ps1 and e2e-parity.ps1 (the final parity record is x-parity-base1). Drop XSIM_SERVER=stub from e2e.ps1. Proof: the next verify's client e2e.\n3. C2 (60 min): directives v2 on the lib. tokens -> list string, flags.packages -> list string, no tab join. Oracle: directives_test.\n4. S2 (60 min): xc postable census, part 1. New crate apps/client/census/ holding census.spi (POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS n161 draft), with test blocks on rust and gleam.\n5. C3 (60 min): cell.spi split_output returns a typed record { value : option string; stdout : string } in place of the '0\\n'/'1\\n' prefix wire. The encoder stays only for cell.ex. Oracle: cell_test.\n6. S3 (60 min): census part 2. cli.spi PostableCensus case + xc.spi run_postable_census. Equality proof against postable-report.ps1 on the node.\n7. C4 (60 min): the daemon protocol GADT, design + types only ('request r' indexed by reply, per X-SOTA-MODEL). No behaviour change, test blocks for the codec round trip.\n8. S4 (60 min): the sim serves Mastodon statuses (social_stub.ps1's first family) through sim_serve, typed by a 'platform p' prototype (X-SOTA-MODEL), with parity against social_stub.\n9. C5..Cn (60 min each): CompilerClient behind 'request r', then Memo keys with phantom digests, then Notebook typestate. Each oracle comes from CELLS-CENSUS n3.\n10. S5: the sim's own Cells calls (cells_executor: Notebook, CompilerDaemon, Document) switch to the Spiral modules as C5..Cn land, which ends sim-run's mix step for good. This is the only S->C dependency, and it sits at the END of the chain, so the sim never stalls before it.\nMain's M1-M3 (CELL-BUDGET-SPIRAL, CELLS-DOCUMENT-TYPED, CELLS-MANIFEST-SOURCEMAP-SPIRAL) run in parallel without touching C1-C5's files.")
inl note_of_cells_in_spiral_6 () = Note ("CELLS-IN-SPIRAL", 6u32, "2026-11-01T14:38Z", "x", "2026-11-01 10:38 x: ordering constraint on n5's S1: retiring scripts/e2e-parity.ps1 removes node-run.ps1's parity mode, which X-RUN-METRICS' baseline (x-parity-base2..4, running now) uses for x_e2e_wall_seconds stub vs sim. So S1 starts only after the 3 baseline runs are recorded. Until then C1 and C2 go first.")
inl note_of_cells_in_spiral_7 () = Note ("CELLS-IN-SPIRAL", 7u32, "2026-11-01T19:20Z", "x", "2026-11-01 15:20 x: SLICE S1 DRAFTED at its final paths (unstaged). apps/client/e2e/stub.ps1 and scripts/e2e-parity.ps1 are archived (kit archive 20261101-151855). e2e.ps1 always starts sim-run serve: the XSIM_SERVER switch is gone, the start wait is 120 s, and the process handle is renamed $xServer, because '$sim' is the foreach variable at e2e.ps1:102 and would have shadowed it at cleanup. The messages say 'X server' / 'requests to the sim', and nothing parses the old wording (git grep over x, spiral and i574n.github finds only backlog notes). node-run.ps1 loses parity mode. Both READMEs drop the stub. stub_http.ps1 STAYS, since social_stub.ps1 dot-sources it, and it retires with S4 (Mastodon/Bluesky in the sim). Proof: x-verify47 (X-SOTA-MODEL n6's rule).")
inl note_of_cells_in_spiral_8 () = Note ("CELLS-IN-SPIRAL", 8u32, "2026-11-01T19:35Z", "x", "2026-11-01 15:35 x: SLICE C2 DRAFTED in the drafts store (%LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\spiral\\CELLS-IN-SPIRAL-C2\\: directives.spi + apply-c2.ps1). It stays in the store because it replaces a live Cells file and lands after C1's window. On top of the typed draft, the hand-rolled 'tokens' union, rev_tokens and joined_tab are gone. Tokens and flags.packages are lib 'list string', merged matches list patterns ('\"--timeout\" :: ms :: rest'), and the packages line is sm'.concat_list \"\\t\" (listm.rev ...). The wire text to the Elixir reader stays byte-identical (dropping tab joins would change the reader, so it's a later slice). Cells is still package.spiproj, the old edition, so lib calls use '.'.\nPROOF x-cells-c2-pre1, rule written before the run: node mirror xqueue3 re-synced (includes main's 14:53 '::' lib respell), appliers apply-cells-typed.ps1 then apply-c2.ps1, node_cells_window_proof. PASS iff (a) both appliers report writing, (b) no failure left in directives_test.exs, cell_test.exs or notebook_test.exs after the --failed rounds, (c) every remaining failure is in CELLS-SPI-LIB n4's known set. QUESTION: does an old-edition package resolve '.'-spelled lib calls after the respell? PREDICTION: PASS. If (b) fails on a compile error naming listm/sm', the respell broke dotted consumer access, and that goes to main as a respell red.")
inl note_of_cells_in_spiral_9 () = Note ("CELLS-IN-SPIRAL", 9u32, "2026-11-01T19:43Z", "x", "2026-11-01 15:43 x: x-cells-c2-pre1 (job-1793561766984, 255 s, core DBC5EC18F14C354C, mirror xqueue3 including main's '::' lib respell) by n8's rule: PASS.\n(a) Both appliers wrote.\n(b) Every directives/cell/notebook test passed.\n(c) Full run 329/335. The 6 left after the --failed rounds are exactly CELLS-SPI-LIB n4's known set.\nRead after the writer: directives.spi at 15:36:09 has no NoToken, and domain.gleam was regenerated at 15:36:22 (1,058,046 bytes) with 0 NoToken and 5 MainFromExpression, so the suite ran C1+C2. ANSWER: an old-edition package.spiproj package resolves '.'-spelled lib calls (listm.rev, sm'.concat_list) after the respell. No respell red, so nothing goes to main. Mirror files restored.\nSTATE: C1 (typed directives + cell.wrap + lib reference) and C2 (lib lists) are both node-proven and wait for ONE box window (kit's held TRAIN7c heavy window, ~18:30), applied as apply-cells-typed.ps1 then apply-c2.ps1 from the store. The window's -Files list is unchanged (directives.spi is already in it).")
inl note_of_cells_in_spiral_10 () = Note ("CELLS-IN-SPIRAL", 10u32, "2026-11-01T19:53Z", "x", "2026-11-01 15:50 x: C1 (CELLS-SPI-LIB + CELLS-TYPED-DIRECTIVES) and C2 (directives on lib lists) LANDED together green at 15:47 (see CELLS-TYPED-DIRECTIVES n11). apps/cells/spi now references lib/spiral; directives use list string, listm and sm'; cell.wrap returns the main_source union. S1 is drafted in x and proven by x-verify47 (running). NEXT, by n5's order: S2/S3 (the xc postable census), then C3 (typed split_output).")
inl note_of_cells_in_spiral_11 () = Note ("CELLS-IN-SPIRAL", 11u32, "2026-11-01T20:12Z", "x", "2026-11-01 16:12 x: SLICE C3 DRAFTED in the drafts store (%LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\spiral\\CELLS-IN-SPIRAL-C3\\: c3-cell.spi, c3-main.spi and apply-c3.ps1, which refuses unless live cell.spi = 1C2358BB8E63, the landed C1, and main.spi = A345B021D4EC).\n- cell.spi drops its hand-rolled 'lines' union and rev_lines/count_lines/join_from for lib list string (listm.rev, listm.length, listm.map, sm'.concat_list).\n- split_output returns ':| run_output = StdoutOnly : string | ValueAndStdout : string * string'. The value search is split into found_in + value_found, so each definition stays under the cell budget.\n- patch_rust takes ':| shown = ShowValue | UnitValue' in place of the \"1\"/\"0\" show_value string.\n- prefix_before returns 'option string' in place of an i32 flag + string (same_prefix compares two options).\n- unit_result returns bool.\n- main.spi's export lambdas encode at the boundary (cell.run_output_text, cell.shown_of, cell.flag_text), so the wire text to Elixir is byte-identical and cell.ex/domain.ex are unchanged.\nPROOF x-cells-c3-pre1, rule written before the run: mirror xqueue3 re-synced (now has the landed C1+C2), applier apply-c3.ps1, node_cells_window_proof. PASS iff (a) apply-c3 writes both files, (b) no failure left in directives_test, cell_test (including the split_output rows at cell_test.exs:141-157) or notebook_test after the --failed rounds, (c) every remaining failure is in CELLS-SPI-LIB n4's known set, (d) the regenerated domain.gleam contains StdoutOnly and ValueAndStdout. PREDICTION: PASS.")
inl note_of_cells_in_spiral_12 () = Note ("CELLS-IN-SPIRAL", 12u32, "2026-11-01T20:35Z", "x", "2026-11-01 16:35 x: SLICE C4 DRAFTED in the store (%LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\spiral\\CELLS-IN-SPIRAL-C4\\: daemon.spi + apply-c4.ps1, which refuses unless package.spiproj = 73D9D8FAA3E9, the landed C1, and adds 'daemon' before 'main'). Design + types only, with no caller switched (n5 item 7).\n- The daemon's tab-line protocol, read from compiler_client.ex:30-50, 343-372, 667 and compiler_daemon.ex:908-973, 1101-1112, becomes ':| request r = Hello :: request hello_reply | Status :: request status_reply | Retire :: request () | Compile :: compile_ask -> request compile_reply'.\n- compile_reply = Compiled : string * option i32 | CompileTimedOut : option i32 | CompileFailed : option i32 * string (the optional queue ms, as the Elixir encode/credit_queue pair has it).\n- status_reply.retiring is 'NotRetiring | RetiringSince : string', because the daemon sends 'false' or a monotonic ms that can exceed i32.\n- compile_wait is 'Unbounded | WithinMs : i32' in place of the \"-1\" sentinel.\n- request_line, reply_line and reply_of are '\\* r.' over 'request r', so each request decodes to exactly its reply type.\n- 4 test blocks: the compile line shape, all 6 compile replies round-tripping, a status owner with spaces, the hello banner. Pool and notebook are left for C5, since their replies are multi-line or unread here.\nPROOF x-cells-c4-pre1 (after C3 frees xqueue3, with a re-sync), rule written before the run: PASS iff (a) apply-c4 writes, (b) daemon.spi's notebook run reports 4 executed and 4 passed with 0 failed/unsupported, (c) the full mix suite after the --failed rounds leaves only CELLS-SPI-LIB n4's known set, (d) the regenerated domain.gleam contains CompileTimedOut. RISK, named up front: reply_line reads reply::pid with reply : r refined per arm. If argument-side GADT refinement doesn't resolve record fields, (b) fails on a type error, and the fix is per-reply encoders called from the arms.")
inl note_of_cells_in_spiral_13 () = Note ("CELLS-IN-SPIRAL", 13u32, "2026-11-01T20:49Z", "x", "2026-11-01 16:49 x: x-cells-c3-pre1 (job-1793563995062, 1,959 s, node saturated: suite 1,216 s against 160-190 s earlier today) by n11's rule: NOT MET.\n- (a) and (d) hold: cell.spi written at 16:13:26, domain.gleam regenerated at 16:15:41 with StdoutOnly x3 and ValueAndStdout x3, and 0 NoLine.\n- (b) holds: no directives, cell or notebook failure, so the cell_test split_output rows passed.\n- (c) FAILS: 8 left after 2 --failed rounds. 6 are n4's known set, and TargetsTest :321 (warm gleam package) is n4's listed flake. The extra one is MemoTest 'an unqualified name resolves in opened modules and modules the code opens; m.x only in module m', which is not in the known set.\nReading, formed after the result so it decides nothing: memo keys come from Elixir source scanning, which C3 doesn't touch, and the test's 'm.x' dotted-name wording may react to main's '::' lib respell (14:53) in the synced tree.\nDISCRIMINATOR, rule written before the run: x-cells-c3-memo1 (job-1793566110384, xqueue3 re-synced at 9CEC360DE785DA0B) runs memo_test.exs ALONE three times: base, C3, then base again (undo copies, mtimes bumped). If MemoTest fails in C3 only, C3 doesn't land and the test is read for a cell.spi path. If it fails in base too, it isn't C3's: C3 may land, and the red goes to main as a possible respell red. If it passes everywhere, it was a load red (CELLS-SUITE-FLAKY-UNDER-LOAD), and C3 may land.")
inl note_of_cells_in_spiral_14 () = Note ("CELLS-IN-SPIRAL", 14u32, "2026-11-01T21:07Z", "x", "2026-11-01 17:07 x: C4 split for speed. Clause (b) of n12's rule (daemon.spi 4/4 test blocks, which carries the named GADT-refinement risk) runs now on idle Vultr as x-cells-c4-blocks1 (job-1793567189287, mirror xlib with the landed C1, apply-c4 then run_notebook, then restored). Clauses (c)/(d) (full suite + domain) stay on node xqueue3 after the C3 discriminator. The rule is unchanged.")
inl note_of_cells_in_spiral_15 () = Note ("CELLS-IN-SPIRAL", 15u32, "2026-11-01T21:17Z", "x", "2026-11-01 17:17 x: x-cells-c4-blocks3 (Vultr, 88 s, mirror at spiral 73e4d863 = the user's commit with C1+C2): n12 clause (b) PASS. daemon.spi reports discovered 4, executed 4, passed 4, failed 0, unsupported 0 (gleam).\n- The named RISK is cleared: argument-side GADT refinement does resolve record fields, so reply_line's 'reply::pid' on 'reply : r' typechecks per arm.\n- The two earlier runs measured nothing. blocks1 had no target on the test blocks ('has no ///> target'); daemon.spi now opens with x's module pattern (///- --package ., ///> gleam, ':# daemon static = :< testing'). blocks2 was refused by apply-c4's base-hash guard, because the previous restore's 'git checkout' had reverted the mirror's package.spiproj to pre-C1 HEAD. The guard did its job, and a re-sync fixed it.\n- Clauses (c)/(d) (full suite + domain) stay on node xqueue3 after the C3 MemoTest discriminator.")
inl note_of_cells_in_spiral_16 () = Note ("CELLS-IN-SPIRAL", 16u32, "2026-11-01T21:21Z", "x", "2026-11-01 17:21 x: BOX LANDING QUEUE for when kit window list clears (compiler heavy-build until ~20:00). One train-hold covers all three, in this order:\n(1) LIB-ERLANG-TYPED-STDLIB n10: lib_land_window -Draft package.spiproj=<store> -BaseHash 7B160B4F51FE. The modules are already committed by the user (73e4d863), so only the registration lands.\n(2) C3, if x-cells-c3-memo1 finishes PASS by n13's rule (base 26/26 and c3 26/26 so far; base2 running): cells_land_window -Applier <store>\\CELLS-IN-SPIRAL-C3\\apply-c3.ps1 -Files spi/cell.spi,spi/main.spi -Tests the same 4 files + test/spiral/cells/memo_test.exs. Pre-land baseline on the box first (rule: equal before/after). apply-c3's base hashes still match: cell.spi 1C2358BB8E63 is the committed C1.\n(3) C4, if its node full-suite clauses (c)/(d) pass: -Applier apply-c4.ps1 -Files spi/package.spiproj,spi/daemon.spi. C4's guard needs package.spiproj = 73D9D8FAA3E9, which is still true after C3 (C3 doesn't touch package.spiproj).")
inl note_of_cells_in_spiral_17 () = Note ("CELLS-IN-SPIRAL", 17u32, "2026-11-01T21:38Z", "x", "2026-11-01 17:38 x: x-cells-c3-memo1 (job-1793566110384, 2,415 s) by n13's rule: MemoTest PASSED in all three runs, 26/26 each (base 504 s, C3 708 s, base2 784 s). The state switch is attested by cell.spi's hash: 1C2358BB8E63, then A9FC62DE6FB0, then 1C2358BB8E63. So x-cells-c3-pre1's extra red was a LOAD red (node saturated, suite 1,216 s), filed under CELLS-SUITE-FLAKY-UNDER-LOAD, and C3 MAY LAND: queue item (2) in n16.\nC4: Pool added (pool_reply { served; refused; peak : i32; free_mb : option i32 }; the daemon's no-pool line 'pool\\t0\\t0\\t1\\t-' decodes to no_pool ()), plus a 5th test block (round trip and dash). Lines are <= 120: status_reply fields idle_min/queued became idle/queue, since record declarations must stay one line. x-cells-c4-blocks4 (Vultr) ran the pre-rename text; the final text runs ALL n12 clauses on the node now: x-cells-c4-pre1 (xqueue3 re-synced), with (b) now expecting 5/5.")
inl note_of_cells_in_spiral_18 () = Note ("CELLS-IN-SPIRAL", 18u32, "2026-11-01T21:57Z", "x", "2026-11-01 17:57 x: GATE HEAL A2 (main's 17:4x ask): spiral/apps/cells/spi/node_cells_window_proof.ps1 gains -Tests <files> (comma or array). Without it the full suite runs as before; with it, mix test runs only those files, and the --failed rounds follow the same scope. Reason: under node saturation the full suite took 1,216 s against 160-190 s and produced a load red (n13) that cost a 40-min discriminator, while a slice's oracle is its touched test files. Parse-clean, unstaged. Its first real use is the next C-slice proof (-Tests directives_test,cell_test,notebook_test,document_test,memo_test).")
inl note_of_cells_in_spiral_19 () = Note ("CELLS-IN-SPIRAL", 19u32, "2026-11-01T22:58Z", "x", "2026-11-01 18:58 x: x-cells-c4-pre1 (job-1793569111297, 4,545 s, node saturated: suite 2,117 s) by n12's rule: NOT MET.\n- (a) holds: apply-c4 wrote.\n- (b) holds: daemon.spi 5/5 on the final text, Pool included.\n- (c) FAILS: 12 left after 2 rounds. 8 are n4's known set or its listed flakes (LispTest x2, DocumentTest x3, FsiSessionTest, TargetsTest gleam-lock and :321, CellsTest :698). NEW: MemoTest 'a target run is reused when the generated code is the same', MemoDepsTest 'editing a dependency module the program never reaches keeps the key', TargetsTest 'a Gleam cell runs from the notebook's directory'.\n- (d) FAILS: domain.gleam has CompileTimedOut=0. The clause was MIS-SPECIFIED: daemon.spi has no export and no caller yet (design + types only), and Spiral's staged compiler emits only reachable code, so its types never reach domain.gleam whatever their correctness.\nDISCRIMINATOR, rule written after the result but BEFORE measuring: x-cells-c4-domain1 (Vultr, job-1793573886623, mirror at b3fd292b) runs 'mix compile' in base, then with C4, and hashes domain.gleam each time. If the hashes are IDENTICAL, C4 changes no code any Elixir test executes (they all go through domain.gleam or Elixir), so the 3 new reds can't be C4's and are load reds under the saturation (CELLS-SUITE-FLAKY-UNDER-LOAD), and C4 may land. If they DIFFER, the 3 tests get C3's alone-in-both-states discriminator.")
inl note_of_cells_in_spiral_20 () = Note ("CELLS-IN-SPIRAL", 20u32, "2026-11-01T23:00Z", "x", "2026-11-01 19:00 x: x-cells-c4-domain1 (Vultr, 36 s) by n19's rule: IDENTICAL. Base and C4 both produce domain.gleam sha 724CE94F94CF198A, 1,058,046 bytes. It was regenerated each time (mtime 22:58:27, then 22:58:41 UTC), and neither copy has daemon code. So C4 changes no code that any Elixir test executes, and the 3 extra reds in x-cells-c4-pre1 (MemoTest reuse, MemoDepsTest, TargetsTest notebook-dir) were LOAD reds under the 2,117 s saturated suite (CELLS-SUITE-FLAKY-UNDER-LOAD). C4 MAY LAND: queue item (3) in n16.\nLESSON for later rules: a clause like 'the generated output contains X' is valid only for code something reaches. A new module with no caller is proven by its own test blocks (clause b), and its no-impact claim by the generated output being byte-identical.")
inl note_of_cells_in_spiral_21 () = Note ("CELLS-IN-SPIRAL", 21u32, "2026-11-02T00:30Z", "x", "2026-11-01 20:30 x: LANDING MODE CHANGE, written before landing. kit TRAIN8 holds the box heavy window for 298 min (until ~01:30), so box-gated landing windows can't run tonight. Per memory lib-landing-under-churn (gate on the node, land from notes), the three node-proven slices land as HASH-GUARDED COPIES with no box compile, under one kit tree-write window: (1) lib package.spiproj registration (base 9D730998B805, proven by x-erlang-final2 with the same registration in the mirror); (2) C3 (apply-c3 guard: cell.spi 1C2358BB8E63 / main.spi A345B021D4EC; draft cell A9FC62DE6FB0 is the text x-cells-c3-memo1 ran); (3) C4 (apply-c4 guard: package.spiproj 73D9D8FAA3E9; the text x-cells-c4-pre1 and x-cells-c4-domain1 ran). LAND iff every guard passes at write time. Undo copies are kept. The post-land confirmation is the next node Cells proof (scoped -Tests), and the box suite runs when TRAIN8 ends.")
inl note_of_cells_in_spiral_22 () = Note ("CELLS-IN-SPIRAL", 22u32, "2026-11-02T00:33Z", "x", "2026-11-01 20:33 x: LANDED 20:31 as guarded copies under tree-write win-1793579458533 (no box compile; TRAIN8 holds the heavy window). Every guard passed. Live hashes: lib/spiral/package.spiproj 1269095979F8 (erlang_text/map/file registered), cells spi/cell.spi A9FC62DE6FB0 and main.spi 4B031A7009B2 (C3), spi/package.spiproj 7FF283AC9379 and new spi/daemon.spi 5B317F712DD6 (C4). Undo: spiral-bin\\lib-land-undo\\x-c3c4-lib-20261101-203122. CONFIRMATION, rule written before the run: x-cells-postland1 (node xqueue3 synced to the landed tree, first use of node_cells_window_proof -Tests) on directives/cell/notebook/document/memo tests. PASS iff nothing remains after the --failed rounds except DocumentTest's known dice/alphabet trio (spiral-only mirror). Any other residue means the guarded undo restores and the slice is read.")
inl note_of_cells_in_spiral_23 () = Note ("CELLS-IN-SPIRAL", 23u32, "2026-11-02T00:43Z", "x", "2026-11-01 20:43 x: x-cells-postland1 (node, 420 s, first scoped run) by n22's rule: PASS. The landed tree on directives/cell/notebook/document/memo tests gives 68/71, and the only in-scope residue is DocumentTest's known dice/alphabet trio (spiral-only mirror). C3 and C4 are confirmed live. The --failed rounds also reran LispTest x2 and FsiSessionTest, which are out of scope: 'mix test --failed' with no files reruns every failure in the shared build manifest, including earlier full-suite runs. FIXED (unstaged): node_cells_window_proof passes @Tests to the --failed rounds too.")
inl note_of_cells_in_spiral_24 () = Note ("CELLS-IN-SPIRAL", 24u32, "2026-11-02T12:21Z", "x", "2026-11-02 08:21 x: GROOM (user: 'groom the backlog more so we know better what's left to finish cells'). MEASURED against n4's DoD (0 Elixir lines): spiral/apps/cells/lib today is 44 .ex files and 11,380 lines (9 mix tasks; 46 .exs test files). At epic start it was 11,371. So C1-C4 removed NO Elixir. They typed the existing Spiral domain (apps/cells/spi: 8 files, 2,334 lines: document 894, text 395, notebook 363, cell 293, daemon 161, directives 133, targets 65, main 30). Progress 30 -> 15: the census, the aliasing proof, and C1-C4 are foundation, not removal.\nWHAT'S LEFT, every Elixir line assigned to one child (the counts sum to 11,380). These are now this epic's deps:\n- C5 CELLS-PORT-TARGETS 1,518 (targets, builders, toolchain)\n- C6 CELLS-PORT-MEMO 642\n- C7a CELLS-PORT-COMPILER-CLIENT 824\n- C7b CELLS-PORT-COMPILER-DAEMON 1,790 (daemon, pool, slots, server, memory_guard, file_lock)\n- C8 CELLS-PORT-RUNNER-MOUNTS 1,026 (runner, mounts, source_blocks, result, tables)\n- C9 CELLS-PORT-NOTEBOOK 2,926 (notebook, cells, accept, directives, cell, lisp)\n- C10 CELLS-PORT-KINO-FACADES 1,121 (smart_cell, display, fsi_session, fsi_chain), giving DoD 3 (hangul)\n- C11 CELLS-PORT-CLIS 683 (notebook_cli, domain, new_project, application, and the 9 mix tasks), giving DoD 1\n- main's M1 CELLS-XCOMPAT-SPIRAL 219 (cell_budget), M2 CELLS-DOCUMENT-TYPED 207 (document), M3 CELLS-MANIFEST-SOURCEMAP-SPIRAL 193 (manifest, source_map). All three sit at 0% with no note since 11-01 08:37.\n- CELLS-HTML-PARITY 231 (html.ex)\n- x S5 X-SIM-CELLS-SPIRAL, giving DoD 2 (sim-run without mix)\nORDER: C5, C6, C7a and C8 are independent and can run in parallel with M1-M3. C7b needs C7a. C9 needs C5, C6, C7b, C8 and M2. C10 needs C9. C11 needs C9 and C10. S5 needs C9, C7b and M2. That puts C9 on the critical path, so it splits into <=60-min slices when picked. Total estimate about 50 h of slices, each proven on the node (scoped -Tests) and landed as hash-guarded copies (LAND-GATE-OFF-BOX).\nCARRIED FROM EXS-TO-SPIRAL (closed as a duplicate of this epic): (a) benchmark each port step against the Elixir it replaces (KINO-SOTA's bar: a Cells run costs about what building the package as an F# project costs); (b) no new Elixir file unless a rewrite slice pairs with it.")
inl note_of_cells_in_spiral_25 () = Note ("CELLS-IN-SPIRAL", 25u32, "2026-11-02T13:58Z", "x", "2026-11-02 09:58 x: 10-MIN GROOM, MODEL CHECK (user: typefeatureset, the Gleam/Rust/iggy balance, the new 1k split model). FINDINGS:\n(1) 1k gate: Cells generates ONE 18,622-line domain.gleam, so it is not on typed-units/1, and each C-slice grows that file. New child CELLS-TYPED-UNITS (cells_model, cells_boundary, cells_feature, cells_beam, cells_cli) gates CELLS-PORT-NOTEBOOK. C5-C8 land straight into their unit.\n(2) Typefeatureset coverage is thin: GADTs only in daemon.spi; 0 existentials, 0 phantoms, 0 prototypes. The planned models (target b GADT in C5, phantom memo digests in C6, notebook typestate in C9) are the real coverage. Each port's oracle adds 'census never down'.\n(3) An export problem in C7a/C7b as written: LINKED-UNITS-MONOMORPHIC-RULE makes a GADT index at a linked-unit export a compile error. So request r stays internal to cells_model, and cells_boundary exports one concrete function per reply type (hello, status, retire, compile, pool) or an existential, the advice from EJP0041.\n(4) BALANCE: Gleam/BEAM is the control plane (daemon, pool, slots, notebook orchestration, Kino facades as renamed Gleam modules); Rust is the CLIs (C11) and anything a bench shows is CPU-bound (memo digest hashing only if measured); iggy is the cross-machine layer (IGGY-MULTI-MACHINE: one GADT message model compiled to Rust and Gleam). The daemon protocol GADT is that same shape, so define it once in cells_model so iggy can carry node/vultr proof results later in place of ssh-scraped stdout.log. Concurrency goes through GLEAM-RUST-SPLIT's interface (spawn, fill-once, request/reply, choice) where it exists. Until then otp_actor sits only in cells_boundary, so switching touches one unit.")
inl note_of_cells_in_spiral_26 () = Note ("CELLS-IN-SPIRAL", 26u32, "2026-11-02T19:08Z", "x", "2026-11-02 15:08 x: Cells Spiral '::' RESPELL LANDED (unstaged): the compiler's fixer (format --spelling symbols) over cell, client, daemon, directives, main, notebook and document (507 drift sites; 'format --check --respell-drift apps/cells/spi' now reports 0 live). PROOF on pack17 (core E556D773EB123175): x-cells-respell3, suite A/B (directives, cell, notebook and document tests) 58 -> 58 with domain regenerated (314803DBEC19, the same hash as the pack16c run x-cells-respell1); x-cells-respell2, daemon 6/6 and client 4/4 blocks. The respell2 launch on the old pin was refused by the core guard when pack17 landed mid-session, so no gate spanned the switch. Also in the land: directives.spi drops its '--import-types' skip (pack16c deleted the flag; grep finds no tracked .spi using it), and directives_test.exs's skip test is inverted to expect 'unknown kernel flag --import-types'.")
inl note_of_cells_in_spiral_27 () = Note ("CELLS-IN-SPIRAL", 27u32, "2026-11-02T21:00Z", "x", "2026-11-02 17:00 x: Elixir count after tonight's ports: 11,279 lines (census 11,380). Removed: memo reach (C6.1) and the manifest toml converter (M3.1), each proven by a byte-equal corpus parity oracle (134 .spi files; 14 tomls plus 11 edge cases) and an A/B suite. The typed Spiral domain grew by document.spi M2.1-M2.6, client.spi (C7a.1), memo.spi and manifest.spi, the :: respell, and lib erlang_tcp.")
inl note_of_cells_in_spiral_28 () = Note ("CELLS-IN-SPIRAL", 28u32, "2026-11-03T07:51Z", "x", "2026-11-03 03:51 x: STALE-DRAFT CLEANUP (main's 11-03 order 5), check recorded before deleting. RULE: a landed draft folder is deleted only if every file either equals its live target, or each non-blank line is present in live or in git history (the user's 98e51d32 'tests' commit holds the pre-port code), or the file is byte-equal to an archived copy. DELETED (11 folders, 42 files, all from kit drafts spiral): CELLS-HTML-MD, CELLS-HTML-MD-B, CELLS-JSON-VALUE, CELLS-MANIFEST-DECLARES, CELLS-GLEAM-PROJECT, CELLS-BUILDERS-DEPS, CELLS-TARGETS-HOST, LIB-MOVE-UNIX-CLOCK, CELLS-TARGETS-PRESENT-B, CELLS-PHASE-B-MD-PRESENT and CELLS-GLEAM-PROJECT-B. KEPT (3), as the only copies of intermediate states that were never committed: CELLS-BUILDERS-PARSE (def split_spiral, renamed to split in phase B), CELLS-BUILDERS-B and CELLS-PHASE-B-BUILDERS-GLEAM (the inline dep-decoding lambda later replaced by wire_dep). They are deletable once the user commits the current tree, since the intermediates are superseded. Other lanes' folders in that store weren't touched.")
inl note_of_cells_in_spiral_29 () = Note ("CELLS-IN-SPIRAL", 29u32, "2026-11-03T16:13Z", "x", "2026-11-03 12:13 x: MEASURED from the tree (groom): Cells lib Elixir is 10,804 lines in 44 files against the epic's 11,371 baseline, so only 5% is gone, while Cells spi holds 4,251 lines in 17 files. The ports removed about 640 lines (targets 859 -> 709, builders 486 -> 174, html 231 -> 181, manifest 129 -> 59, source_map 64 -> 4), but delegations in domain.ex and other lanes' new Elixir features grew the rest. Biggest left: notebook.ex 1,464, compiler_daemon.ex 1,185, cells.ex 1,165, compiler_client.ex 824, targets.ex 709, memo.ex 603, mounts.ex 441, runner.ex 386, fsi_session.ex 381, fsi_chain.ex 276. METRIC from now on, the epic's own DoD: progress = 1 - (Cells lib Elixir lines / 11,371), re-measured at each groom. Progress 15 -> 5 (honest, not a regression of work). Implication for ordering: the line mass sits in notebook, compiler_daemon and cells.ex, so the per-function slices (manifest, targets) move the metric little. The next epic-level step should port a whole module's logic (memo C6 or compiler_client C7a, both Paused behind the node queue).")
inl note_of_cells_in_spiral_30 () = Note ("CELLS-IN-SPIRAL", 30u32, "2026-11-04T04:14Z", "x", "2026-11-04 00:13 x: GROOM (user 11-04 00:1x via main). Active -> Planned (WIP cap: untouched 12 h). An umbrella at 5% (measured 12:13); its children carry the work (CELLS-PORT-TARGETS, CELLS-MANIFEST-SOURCEMAP-SPIRAL, CELLS-HTML-PARITY). Its percent follows the Elixir line census.")
inl note_of_cells_in_spiral_31 () = Note ("CELLS-IN-SPIRAL", 31u32, "2026-11-04T04:21Z", "x", "2026-11-04 00:21 x: back to Active at 5 under the user's 11-04 00:2x rule (Active = a note within 48 h, relayed by kit). My 11-04 groom parked it under the old 12 h cap; its last real note is under 48 h old, so the parking is undone. Next step unchanged.")
inl cells_census () = Task ("CELLS-CENSUS", "Cells in Spiral slice 0: classify every Cells module (pure / process / Kino-facing / mix task), map callers and tests, pick each slice's parity oracle, and prove the Kino aliasing trick on one module", TaskKind, P1, D4, 100u32, Done, "", "x,cells,spiral,census", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_cells_census () = Planning ("CELLS-CENSUS", Minutes 120u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_cells_census_1 () = Note ("CELLS-CENSUS", 1u32, "2026-10-31T22:02Z", "x", "2026-10-31 x: slice 0 of CELLS-IN-SPIRAL, read-only (CPU-light). For each of the 44 lib .ex files and 13 mix tasks: class (a) pure logic, (b) process/OTP, (c) Kino/Livebook-facing, (d) mix task; what already lives in apps/cells/spi (Domain exports); its callers inside Cells and outside (every repo and lane: grep Spiral.Cells.X and :spiral_cells); the test files that pin it; and a parity oracle for its slice (e.g. Document: every tracked .spi in every repo parsed by both implementations; compiler_client: the daemon line protocol replayed). Output: an ordered slice list with estimates by rule (lines per class), recorded as notes on the epic; the first slice filed with its pre-registered rule. Also: how Livebook loads smart cells (behaviour callbacks, module naming) to choose the aliasing trick, proven by a one-module Gleam smart cell loaded in a Livebook-less Kino test.")
inl note_of_cells_census_2 () = Note ("CELLS-CENSUS", 2u32, "2026-10-31T22:29Z", "x", "2026-10-31 18:3x x: census of spiral/apps/cells/lib (44 .ex files, 11,413 lines; marks: Proc = GenServer/Task/spawn/Port/gen_tcp/Supervisor/receive/System.cmd, Kino, Domain.):\n- a pure (19 files, 3,572 lines): Memo 643, Builders 486, FsiChain 277, Display 251, XCompat 235, Html 232, Document 208 (17 Domain. calls, already half Spiral), NotebookCli 188, Toolchain 174, Domain 170, Accept 162, Manifest 130, SourceBlocks 91, Directives 66, FileLock 66, SourceMap 65, Cell 44, NewProject 42, Lisp 42.\n- b process (11 files, 5,727): Notebook 1,452, CompilerDaemon 1,184 (62 marks), Targets 860, CompilerClient 825 (40), Mounts 442, Runner 387 (29), CompilerPool 250, Slots 124, Server 88, MemoryGuard 82, Tables 33.\n- c kino-facing (5 files, 1,856): Spiral.Cells 1,166, FsiSession 367, SmartCell 230, Result 77, Application 16.\n- d mix tasks (9 files, 258): Notebook, Targets, Edit, RenderCells, XCompat, New, CompilerDaemon, Html, CellsServer.\nBackend map by BACKEND-BALANCE's rule (memory backend-balance, written before this census):\n- b: Gleam. CompilerDaemon (G1 state for hours, G3 owns OS children, G4 messages), CompilerPool and Slots (G2, G3), Runner (G2 parallel runs, G3 deadlines), MemoryGuard (G3), Server (G1, G4). Targets and CompilerClient run inside that BEAM node and talk to it by messages (G4), so they go Gleam too. Mounts is a tie: it keeps its current runtime (BEAM).\n- a: backend-neutral Spiral domain whose test blocks run on rust AND gleam (the rule's tie clause). Memo's hashing is the only R1 candidate, and it moves only after a measurement.\n- c: Gleam with Elixir-named modules (aliasing or beam rename), because Kino/Livebook load Elixir.Spiral.Cells.* by name.\n- d: no mix. Each task is a per-command CLI, which is R2 (BEAM cold start measured 15-50x a Rust CLI), so it becomes a thin Rust CLI that talks to the long-lived Gleam server (CellsServer). This is the iggy split (IGGY-MULTI-MACHINE, LIB-IGGY-CLIENT): Rust on the per-call data path, Gleam for supervision and state, joined by ONE Spiral GADT message family with byte-identical Rust and Gleam codecs, the same way lib/spiral/iggy.spi frames job events.\nTypefeatureset design for the slices (user 10-31, TYPEFEATURESET-ENFORCE n6): the daemon protocol is a request GADT indexed by its reply type, like x's wire.spi (reads r / writes r), so a client can't misread a reply. Targets are a GADT over target kind, so a test block's target and its builder agree by type. The memo key carries phantom indices for source digest and compiler core, so a stale core can't hit the cache. Notebook evaluation moves through typestate phantoms (parsed, planned, ran). Kino-facing values cross the Elixir boundary as an existential any_cell.\nSlice order stays parse-first: Document, the a-pure Directives/Cell/SourceBlocks/SourceMap, then Memo, then the daemon protocol GADT with CompilerClient. Main owns compiler_client/compiler_daemon/targets/x_compat items (window 6 hashes in main's 10-31 message), so a slice that rewrites one of them is announced to main first.")
inl note_of_cells_census_3 () = Note ("CELLS-CENSUS", 3u32, "2026-10-31T22:48Z", "x", "2026-10-31 18:48 x: CELLS-CENSUS finished (callers, tests, oracles, aliasing proof).\nKino aliasing PROVEN on the node (gleam 1.19.2). Pass rule written before the run: Elixir prints hi! and Spiral.CellsProbe. Setup: a two-module Gleam project (cells_probe/inner.shout; cells_probe/kino_entry.eval calling inner). Gleam 1.19 no longer keeps .erl artefacts (only .abstr and .beam), so the rename runs at BEAM level. beam_lib:chunks(Beam, [abstract_code]) gives raw_abstract_v1 forms. Replace {attribute, L, module, _} with 'Elixir.Spiral.CellsProbe', then compile:forms(Forms, [binary, debug_info]), and write Elixir.Spiral.CellsProbe.beam. Then elixir -pa <alias> -pa <gleam ebin> probe.exs printed 'hi!' and 'Spiral.CellsProbe': KINO-ALIAS-OK. Rule for the c slices: each Kino-facing module is a Gleam FACADE at the top of the call graph (nothing in Gleam calls it), renamed to its Elixir.Spiral.Cells.* name after gleam build. Its callbacks (Kino.SmartCell init/handle_connect/to_attrs/to_source) are plain exports, and calls out to Kino go through @external(erlang, \"Elixir.Kino...\", ...).\nOutside callers of Spiral.Cells.* (ripgrep, excluding apps/cells, deps, _build, target, tmp):\n- alphabet/apps/hangul/hangul.livemd uses Spiral.Cells.SmartCell and Spiral.Cells.eval (4 cells each): the Kino contract to keep.\n- x/apps/sim/spi/cells_executor.spi calls Notebook (3), CompilerDaemon (1) and Document (1). That is why sim-run.ps1 still compiles Cells with mix, and those three slices free x's sim of Elixir.\n- kit/src/kit_machine_watch_model/model.spi:188 names Spiral.Cells.CompilerDaemon. When the daemon moves to Gleam its process name changes, so the kit team is told in that slice.\nTests (46 .exs files) by slice:\n- Directives: directives_test.\n- Cell: cell_test.\n- Document: document_test (611).\n- Builders/Targets: builders_test, targets_test, targets_test_name_test, notebook_targets_test.\n- Memo: memo_test, memo_deps_test, memo_tool_identity_test.\n- Daemon: compiler_daemon_test plus the idle, retire-race and status tests, warm_notebook_test.\n- Pool, Slots, Runner, Tables: compiler_pool_test, slots_test, runner_test, tables_test.\n- Mounts: mounts_name, mounts_patched, mounts_relative.\n- Notebook: notebook_test plus the report, metrics, lib_snapshot, spiral_lib, gate_args, livemd_target and timeout_headroom tests.\n- Kino: smart_cell_test, display_test, fsi_session_test, unit_first_test.\n- Other: accept, html, lisp, manifest, new_project, x_compat, postability_profile.\nOracle per slice:\n- a-pure: the slice's .exs tests pass unchanged against the Spiral-backed module, AND the old and new export give byte-equal output over every tracked .spi (Directives prepare, Document parse/to_spi round trip, Memo keys).\n- b: their .exs tests, plus x's ladder-cells engine parity and a CELLS-GATE corpus run with equal pass, fail and memo-hit counts.\n- c: smart_cell_test plus hangul.livemd evaluated headless.\n- d: each CLI's stdout and exit equal the mix task's on the same arguments.\nThe probe script was a one-shot. Its method is above, and the file is archived (no new .ps1 under CELLS-IN-SPIRAL, per main's 10-31 request).")
inl cells_spi_lib () = Task ("CELLS-SPI-LIB", "Cells in Spiral slice 1: apps/cells/spi references lib/spiral (spiral-) so the typed migration can use option/list/resultm/listm/sm instead of hand-rolled text, tokens and lines", Story, P1, D2, 100u32, Done, "CELLS-CENSUS", "team:x,cells,spiral,x,typefeatures,lib", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_cells_spi_lib () = Planning ("CELLS-SPI-LIB", Minutes 90u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_cells_spi_lib_1 () = Note ("CELLS-SPI-LIB", 1u32, "2026-10-31T22:39Z", "x", "2026-10-31 18:4x x: slice 1 of CELLS-IN-SPIRAL, the precondition for the typefeatureset migration (CELLS-IN-SPIRAL n2). apps/cells/spi (text, cell, directives, document, notebook, targets, main; 2,157 lines) references only core-, so it hand-rolls tokens, lines and text helpers and can't use option, list, resultm, listm or sm'. Applier i574n.github/scripts/x-cells-lib-ref.ps1 -Cells <apps/cells> [-Apply] adds 'packageDir: ../../../lib' and 'spiral-' (the shape of apps/compiler/spi/package.spiproj). Dry run checked against today's tree. No lib module shares a name with the seven Cells modules. Pass rule, registered before the run: on a node scratch copy (node_cells_window_proof.ps1 with this applier), mix compile regenerates domain.gleam and the full mix test result equals the same tree's without the applier. Failures that pass alone on --failed reruns are load reds (CELLS-SUITE-FLAKY-UNDER-LOAD). Then land through cells_land_window.ps1 with a train-hold. package.spiproj is not on main's window-6 list, so no hand-over is needed.")
inl note_of_cells_spi_lib_2 () = Note ("CELLS-SPI-LIB", 2u32, "2026-10-31T22:50Z", "x", "2026-10-31 18:50 x: no new .ps1 under CELLS-IN-SPIRAL (main's 10-31 request), so the applier lives here and its file is archived. At land time, write it to a temp file for cells_land_window.ps1 -Applier, the same way land-lib-program.ps1 handles note-stored programs. APPLIER (-Cells <apps/cells> [-Apply]):\nparam([Parameter(Mandatory)] [string] $Cells, [switch] $Apply)\n$ErrorActionPreference = 'Stop'\n$project = Join-Path $Cells 'spi/package.spiproj'\n$before = [IO.File]::ReadAllText($project)\nif ($before -match '(?m)^\\s+spiral-\\s*$') { 'x-cells-lib-ref / already references spiral-'; exit 0 }\nif ($before -notmatch '(?m)^packages:\\r?\\n\\s+\\|core-\\r?\\n') { throw \"x-cells-lib-ref / unexpected package.spiproj shape in $project\" }\n$after = \"packageDir: ../../../lib`n\" + ($before -replace '(?m)^(\\s+)\\|core-(\\r?\\n)', '$1|core-$2$1spiral-$2')\nif (!$Apply) { 'x-cells-lib-ref / dry run, would write:'; $after; exit 0 }\n[IO.File]::WriteAllText($project, $after)\n\"x-cells-lib-ref / applied to $project\"")
inl note_of_cells_spi_lib_3 () = Note ("CELLS-SPI-LIB", 3u32, "2026-10-31T23:28Z", "x", "2026-10-31 19:28 x: proof x-cells-spi-lib1 started (job-1793489269930, xqueue3, core D1C49B8BDC3318F7). One job runs the full mix test on the synced tree (baseline), applies n2's edit inline, runs the full mix test again, then restores package.spiproj. The pass rule in n1 is unchanged: same result as the baseline, and failures that pass alone on --failed reruns count as load reds.")
inl note_of_cells_spi_lib_4 () = Note ("CELLS-SPI-LIB", 4u32, "2026-11-01T00:56Z", "x", "2026-10-31 20:56 x: x-cells-spi-lib1 (job-1793489269930, 78 min, xqueue3, core D1C49B8BDC3318F7) by n1's rule. Compile with spiral- gives exit 0, domain.gleam regenerated (1,057,403 bytes), so the lib resolves and no module name collides. The full-run totals are cut off in the tail. After the two --failed rounds, 8 fail on each side.\n- Shared (7): LispTest x2, DocumentTest x3 (dice and alphabet are missing from a spiral-only mirror, as in CELLS-TYPED-DIRECTIVES n2), FsiSessionTest per-cell interaction, TargetsTest gleam build without a kept lock.\n- Baseline only: CellsTest 'a missing pinned Rust toolchain is installed once' (cells_test.exs:698).\n- With spiral- only: TargetsTest 'gleam cells with the same manifest run in a warm package' (targets_test.exs:321).\nVerdict by the rule as written: NOT MET (the sets differ by one test each). Reading: both differing tests are toolchain and warm-slot plumbing that the Spiral domain package doesn't reach, and the node ran 3 jobs then, so they look like load reds (CELLS-SUITE-FLAKY-UNDER-LOAD). Discriminating rule, written before running: run both tests ALONE on the now-idle mirror in both states (base, then with spiral-). If each test gives the same outcome in both states, the lib reference changes nothing observable and the slice may land under a train-hold. If a test passes in base and fails with spiral-, it doesn't land and that test is investigated.")
inl note_of_cells_spi_lib_5 () = Note ("CELLS-SPI-LIB", 5u32, "2026-11-01T01:05Z", "x", "2026-10-31 21:05 x: discriminating run by n4's rule. The first attempt measured nothing: [IO.File] with a relative path resolved against the process cwd, so the state never switched (memory ps-dotnet-relative-paths). Rerun with absolute paths, mirror idle, then package.spiproj restored and checked:\n- base: cells_test.exs:698 (Rust toolchain) passes; targets_test.exs:321 (gleam warm package) FAILS.\n- with spiral-: :698 FAILS; :321 passes.\nBy n4 AS WRITTEN: NOT MET, since :698 passed in base and failed with spiral- here, so the slice does not land on this evidence. Reading: the full run showed the exact inverse (:698 failed in base, :321 failed with spiral-). Each test has now failed once in each state, the signature of flakes independent of the package reference. That reading was formed after the results, so it decides nothing.\nNew rule, written before running: three interleaved rounds (base, lib, base, lib, base, lib), both tests alone each round, idle mirror. LAND iff no test fails ONLY in the lib state across the 3+3 runs (failures in both states, or in neither, mean a flake or a pass). If :698 fails in all 3 lib runs and passes in all 3 base runs (or :321 does), it doesn't land and that test is read for a lib-dependent path. Flakes in :698 and :321 also go to CELLS-SUITE-FLAKY-UNDER-LOAD.")
inl note_of_cells_spi_lib_6 () = Note ("CELLS-SPI-LIB", 6u32, "2026-11-01T01:24Z", "x", "2026-10-31 21:24 x: 3-round interleaved run by n5's rule. Each round printed package.spiproj's first line, so the state switch is attested (base 'packages:', lib 'packageDir: ../../../lib'), and it was restored after.\n- targets_test.exs:321 failed in 6 of 6 runs (3 base, 3 lib).\n- cells_test.exs:698 failed in base rounds 1 and 2 and lib round 1, so 2 of 3 base and 1 of 3 lib.\nNo test fails ONLY in the lib state: PASS. apps/cells/spi referencing lib/spiral changes no observed test outcome, the compile is clean, and domain.gleam regenerates.\nLand plan: through cells_land_window.ps1 with the n2 applier text written to a temp file, a train-hold around it, and kit window list checked for a held Cells tree-write. Not during tonight's night run, which reads apps/cells live, so the next morning window. Landing is a 2-line package.spiproj change; CELLS-TYPED-DIRECTIVES lands in the same window.")
inl note_of_cells_spi_lib_7 () = Note ("CELLS-SPI-LIB", 7u32, "2026-11-01T06:54Z", "x", "2026-11-01 02:54 x: landing is now ONE window with CELLS-TYPED-DIRECTIVES, so its plan isn't duplicated here: CELLS-TYPED-DIRECTIVES n5 holds the runbook and the applier, which also writes this item's package.spiproj change (packageDir ../../../lib + spiral-, the same shape n6 proved). Verified tonight with no live writes: the anchors are found exactly once and the output equals the proven shapes. The combined state (lib reference + typed drafts) gets its first proof in that window's test run. This item closes on that landing.")
inl note_of_cells_spi_lib_8 () = Note ("CELLS-SPI-LIB", 8u32, "2026-11-01T12:36Z", "x", "2026-11-01 08:36 x: ITEM UPGRADE (round 4). Fields: tags gain team:x; 90 / medium / agent stand (medium because the land window runs the Cells suite). DEFINITION OF DONE: package.spiproj carries 'packageDir: ../../../lib' + 'spiral-' in the live tree, landed through CELLS-TYPED-DIRECTIVES n5's window, which closes green. Artifact: the landed package.spiproj hash. Evidence so far: n6 (3-round interleaved check, no test fails only with the lib). NEXT STEP: the same morning window. TYPEFEATURESET PLAN: this slice adds no types; it's the precondition that lets later slices use lib option, result, list, listm and sm' in place of the package's hand-rolled tokens/lines/text and its 0/1 i32 flags. The first consumer is the directives v2 follow-up: tokens become 'list string' and flags.packages become 'list string', with no tab joins.")
inl note_of_cells_spi_lib_9 () = Note ("CELLS-SPI-LIB", 9u32, "2026-11-01T19:52Z", "x", "2026-11-01 15:50 x: LANDED and closed green (cells_land_window x-cells-c1c2, 15:47, train-hold x-cells-c1c2 taken and released, window win-1793562439067). The pre-land baseline on the same live tree (dice and alphabet present) was 45 passed, 0 failed across directives_test, cell_test, notebook_test and document_test. After landing: 45 passed. n2's rule (equal to the pre-land result) is met. Landed hashes: package.spiproj 73D9D8FAA3E9, cell.spi 1C2358BB8E63, notebook.spi 74F7478DFDD2, directives.spi F00ABD35C3D6. The live domain.gleam was regenerated at 15:47:48 (0 NoToken, 5 MainFromExpression). Undo: spiral-bin\\lib-land-undo\\cells-20261101-154718. Unstaged in spiral for the user's commit: apps/cells/spi/{package.spiproj,cell.spi,notebook.spi,directives.spi}. The appliers are archived (one-shot). DONE.")
inl spl2spi_in_spiral () = Task ("SPL2SPI-IN-SPIRAL", "apps/lisp's spl2spi (Lisp surface .spl -> ordinary Spiral .spi) becomes a Spiral program, replacing spl2spi.ps1 (itself a 10-31 port of spl2spi.py); first slice of PS1-TO-SPIRAL", Story, P0, D3, 100u32, Done, "-", "lisp,spiral,ps1-to-spiral,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_spl2spi_in_spiral () = Planning ("SPL2SPI-IN-SPIRAL", Minutes 180u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_spl2spi_in_spiral_1 () = Note ("SPL2SPI-IN-SPIRAL", 1u32, "2026-10-31T22:58Z", "main", "10-31 18:58 main, created in the pre-night groom so the first PS1-TO-SPIRAL step is pullable (the epic is assignee=pair). SOURCE: apps/lisp/spl2spi.ps1 (~120 lines: an S-expression reader, then printers for types, patterns, expressions and the nominal/type/union/inl top forms). ORACLE ready: the 3 samples' outputs (membership, membership_short, membership_wrong) were byte-identical between the .py and the .ps1 on 10-31, so the Spiral version must reproduce the same .spi bytes for the same .spl. DONE WHEN: build.ps1 calls the Spiral program, the 3 samples are byte-identical to today's outputs, RUST-OK for membership/membership_short and the rejection for membership_wrong, and spl2spi.ps1 is archived. No new .ps1 (spiral-first-tooling).")
inl note_of_spl2spi_in_spiral_2 () = Note ("SPL2SPI-IN-SPIRAL", 2u32, "2026-11-03T16:31Z", "main", "main 11-03 12:4x PORTED (unstaged, spiral apps/lisp): spl2spi.spi (edition 3, 0 kit findings) with typed tokens (token = Open|Close|Word|Quoted), a typed tree (sexp = Atom|Text|Items, rec), typed reader outcomes (parsed = Parsed|Unclosed; read = Forms|Unbalanced) instead of exceptions, and one printer per ps1 function (format_type/pattern/expr with a :& group for if/match/ctor, union/inl/nominal/type tops); 4 rust tests (inl signature+body, union forall + nested parens, match arms, comments + unbalanced in both directions) + package.spiproj. ORACLE: the Spiral binary's output is BYTE-IDENTICAL to spl2spi.ps1 on all 3 samples (membership 66A6B69D, membership_short 46F0F4B2, membership_wrong 5671E9EE), first run. build.ps1 now builds spl2spi from Spiral (BuildSpiral + rustc) and uses it. ALSO FIXED: Cells' lib/spiral/cells/lisp.ex still shelled out to python apps/lisp/spl2spi.py, deleted on 10-31, so ///! lisp cells (and lisp_test.exs) could not work; it now runs target/build/lisp/spl2spi/spl2spi[.exe] and names the build step when it is missing. PENDING: vultr notebook gate + full lisp build.ps1 (RUST-OK x2, membership_wrong rejected), then delete spl2spi.ps1.")
inl note_of_spl2spi_in_spiral_3 () = Note ("SPL2SPI-IN-SPIRAL", 3u32, "2026-11-03T16:45Z", "main", "main 11-03 12:4x DONE. Vultr: spl2spi notebook 4/4 (the program's main moved to the file's end and marked to skip the notebook run, since a main cell defaults to Zig and env args have no Zig arm; memory notebook-main-default-zig). apps/lisp build.ps1 builds spl2spi from Spiral and passes: RUST-OK membership, RUST-OK membership_short, membership_wrong rejected (ETY-TermError, as designed). Byte identity re-proven under pack26 too (spl2spi.rs 61193 -> 60329 bytes, outputs unchanged). spl2spi.ps1 DELETED (unstaged D; git history keeps it; git grep finds no reference). First PS1-TO-SPIRAL slice done.")
inl data_rows_typed () = Task ("DATA-ROWS-TYPED", "Repo data as typed Spiral rows, not .tsv/.rows text tables (user 10-31: heal their origin)", Story, P0, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "kit,tibia,main,standards,drift,team:kit,team:tibia", "current", "current")
