inl note_of_scrum_xp_s7_1 () = Note ("SCRUM-XP-S7", 1u32, "2026-11-01T04:33Z", "kit", "11-01 00:3x kit, slice of EOIE-SCRUM-XP (user 00:2x via main: 'speed up the agile->scrum/xp migration'; slices of an hour or less). Spec: EOIE-SCRUM-XP M3 DESIGN note (10-30 13:5x) and the 10-22 typestate draft.")
inl note_of_scrum_xp_s7_2 () = Note ("SCRUM-XP-S7", 2u32, "2026-11-01T06:50Z", "kit", "11-01 02:5x kit (round 3): typed model DONE in the tree (unstaged): new granular package src/agile_epic_tree. parent_link = TopLevel | ChildOf id (typed, not a tag); children_of, rollup (an epic's progress = mean of its direct children), checked = ParentOk | ParentUnknown id | ParentCycle id (walks the parent chain, bounded by the node count). Test children_roll_up_and_parent_chains_are_checked. PROOF: node job-1793515436523-kit-node-r3j 1/1 (first try failed to parse a nested 'Some (ChildOf next)' pattern; nested matches instead). LEFT: the Task row's parent field, 'backlog list --tree', next showing the parent.")
inl note_of_scrum_xp_s7_3 () = Note ("SCRUM-XP-S7", 3u32, "2026-11-01T12:36Z", "kit", "11-01 08:3x kit QUALITY PASS. DoD: Task rows accept an optional parent; 'kit backlog list <root> --tree' prints each epic with its children indented and the rolled-up progress (mean of direct children), equal to agile_epic_tree.rollup on the same data (CLI test); 'kit agile edit <root> X parent Y' refuses a cycle (ParentCycle) and an unknown parent (ParentUnknown) with exit 2 (CLI tests); existing list output byte-identical when no item has a parent. NEXT STEP: a parent field in agile_task_text's Task row (append-compatible: absent = TopLevel), read through agile_epic_tree. TYPEFEATURESET PLAN: parent_link = TopLevel | ChildOf of task_id (closed union, never an empty-string sentinel); parent_check = ParentOk | ParentUnknown | ParentCycle (closed union); still to fix: ids are raw strings and progress a bare i32: use nominal task_id and a percent nominal (0..100 by a smart constructor returning option).")
inl note_of_scrum_xp_s7_4 () = Note ("SCRUM-XP-S7", 4u32, "2026-11-01T18:15Z", "kit", "11-01 14:15 kit: EMACS-STEALS fold: column totals as in S5, plus close -> promote dependents (org dependency triggers). When an item goes Done, each dependent whose deps are now all Done moves Blocked -> Planned (or gets a 'ready' StateChange with reason 'deps done: <ids>'), in the same write.")
inl note_of_scrum_xp_s7_5 () = Note ("SCRUM-XP-S7", 5u32, "2026-11-01T20:42Z", "kit", "11-01 16:42 kit: close -> promote dependents (EMACS-STEALS org dependency triggers) is modeled and proven (unstaged), in src/agile_cadence beside hidden_behind, on the same tracked record. promoted_by items closed returns the Blocked items that list the closed id AND whose other deps are all Done, counting the one being closed. Test closing_an_item_promotes_only_dependents_whose_deps_are_now_all_done: E (blocked only on D) promotes, Planned items never promote, Z waits until both X and Y are Done; Linux worker green. NEXT (verb half): in agile set ... Done, after the write, append a ready StateChange (Blocked -> Planned, reason \"deps done: <ids>\") for each promoted id in the same write. Column totals for S5/S7 are in agile_activity (column_totals), in proof.")
inl note_of_scrum_xp_s7_6 () = Note ("SCRUM-XP-S7", 6u32, "2026-11-03T02:35Z", "x", "2026-11-02 22:35 x (auto-claimed by the gate, x-b7595ac3): RELEASED without edits. A typed optional parent on Task rows, list --tree and next showing the parent all change kit's agile state schema and verbs and ship in a kit TRAIN, so it's a kit-lane change, like SCRUM-XP-S2 (released 22:34). Resume condition: kit's next train.")
inl note_of_scrum_xp_s7_7 () = Note ("SCRUM-XP-S7", 7u32, "2026-11-03T02:37Z", "x-dumps", "x-dumps (auto-claimed by gate): RELEASED + Blocked, 3rd kit train-series P0 thrashed to me in a row (after REMOTE-EXEC-S6, SCRUM-XP-S2). Adding a typed parent_id to the Task row + list --tree changes the shared agile state schema and the agile verbs -> a kit-train change, not landable by a non-kit lane. RESUME: kit takes it at its next train; un-block then. The real fix is --any lane-affinity (flagged to kit twice) so non-kit lanes stop auto-claiming kit P0s.")
inl note_of_scrum_xp_s7_8 () = Note ("SCRUM-XP-S7", 8u32, "2026-11-05T16:47Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-05 12:5x kit: SCOPED (kit takes it; Blocked -> Planned, since the block reason was 'kit takes it at its next train'). Shape, read from the live state: Task is an 11-field positional union with thousands of rows, so a parent field inside Task would rewrite the whole file (lost-update and respell risk, KIT-AGILE-LOST-UPDATES / KIT-STATE-SPELLING-GUARD). Planning is already a separate keyed row (Planning :: id * estimate * load * assignee), so the parent goes the same way: a new union 'task_parent = Parent :: string * string -> task_parent' (child id, parent id), appended as one row per link, never rewriting Task rows. Work: (1) the state schema + reader in agile_task_text (parse Parent rows beside Planning; an unknown parent id is a check finding, not a crash); (2) 'kit agile edit <root> <id> --parent <epic>' appending or replacing the one Parent row for that child (note-segment style, no whole-file rewrite); (3) 'kit agile list <root> --tree' rendering children under their epic (an epic's progress = minutes-weighted Done share of its children); (4) byte identity: on a backlog copy with no Parent rows, every existing verb's output and the state bytes stay unchanged (needs the installed binary, so it rides a train). Sizes: the reader lands in agile_task_text (a source package inlined widely), so the new parse goes into a linked crate from the start (memory size-every-inliner).")
inl note_of_scrum_xp_s7_9 () = Note ("SCRUM-XP-S7", 9u32, "2026-11-05T17:24Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-05 13:2x kit: steps (1)+(2) BUILT (unstaged, new source package src/agile_task_parents, box --check ok, standards 0 findings; not yet a workspace crate or routed, so no train carries it). Placement: parent links live in their OWN state file state/parents.spi (schema: union task_parent = Parent of child id * epic id; one 'inl parent_of_<slug> () = Parent (CHILD, EPIC)' row per child, ids quoted), the views.spi pattern, so agile.spi and its Task rows are never rewritten. parents.spi: link_of_line / links_of_text (malformed rows skipped), row_of_link, children_of, tree_lines, slug_of. verb.spi: typed parent_change = ParentSet (child, epic) | ParentCleared child; links_changed keeps one row per child; parents_changed refuses unknown ids (the Task row must exist in agile.spi), refuses if parents.spi changed since read (compare-and-swap like views), writes with workspace_primitives.files.replace_file_checked, and registers parents in state/package.spiproj once (typed module_listing = ModuleListed | ModuleAdded | NoModulesSection); parent_run = kit agile parent <root> <child> <epic|--clear>. Notebooks: parents.spi 1/1; parents+verb running (job-1793899350963). NEXT: make it a linked crate (Cargo + workspace), route AgileParent in agile_subcommand_domain + kit_agile_mutation (size-probe kit_agile_mutation), then step (3) kit agile list <root> --tree.")
inl note_of_scrum_xp_s7_10 () = Note ("SCRUM-XP-S7", 10u32, "2026-11-05T17:53Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-05 13:5x kit: steps (2)+(3) WIRED (unstaged, box --check ok for every touched package + kit_cli; standards 0; ratchet ok). agile_task_parents is now a linked crate (entry verb.spi, workspace member + lock). AgileParent joins agile_subcommand_domain (spec: parent <root> <child-id> <epic-id|--clear>, WritesState) and kit_agile_mutation routes it to agile_task_parents.verb.parent_run (). List --tree: the AgileList route asks agile_task_parents.verb.tree_wanted (); with --tree it runs tree_run (each epic once in first-seen order, children indented, summary 'kit agile list ok tree epics=E links=L'), otherwise the old listing. That listing call is now a TYPED linked call (agile_task_listing exports agile_list_entry itself; it was a host string), so agile_task_listing (988 at HEAD) is untouched. Tests: verb notebook (tree + one-row-per-child + register) running job-1793900973685; NEW CLI test agile_task_parents/parents_cli.spi (fixture: parent KIT-A under EPIC-E writes the row and registers the module, list --tree shows EPIC-E then '  KIT-A', --clear drops it, unknown id exit 2) rides the next train. Node size probe of the 8 touched crates job-1793900432835 running (it predates the tree route; a rerun with agile_task_listing follows). Left for the DoD: epic progress (minutes-weighted Done share) in the tree lines, and byte identity on a backlog with no parents.spi (no existing verb reads it, so it holds by construction; proven after install).")
inl note_of_scrum_xp_s7_11 () = Note ("SCRUM-XP-S7", 11u32, "2026-11-05T18:35Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "box notebook parents5 green: verb.spi 2/2 executed, parents.spi 1/1 executed (pack51). Node size probe parents2 (parents, routes, mutation, listing sizes + cargo) still running on node.")
inl note_of_scrum_xp_s7_12 () = Note ("SCRUM-XP-S7", 12u32, "2026-11-05T19:22Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-05 15:1x kit: node probe parents2 found agile_task_parents at 1449 generated lines (over the 1000 gate). SPLIT (unstaged): new linked crate agile_task_tree (epic_tree.spi: tree_wanted, epics_of, fact_for, child_line, epic_line, tree_text_of, tree_run, list_routed; export kit_agile_list_routed; test epics_list_their_children_once_each incl. the full tree text) links agile_quality_facts + agile_task_listing and includes agile_task_parents as source; agile_task_parents keeps the row model + edit --parent verb with no links. kit_agile_mutation now links agile_task_tree. Node probe kit-probe-tree: parents 923, tree 946, mutation 978, routes 852, cargo 0 errors. Rides TRAIN32 (launched 15:17, node, pack52). Closes after install: kit agile edit --parent + list --tree on the live backlog.")
inl note_of_scrum_xp_s7_13 () = Note ("SCRUM-XP-S7", 13u32, "2026-11-05T22:32Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-05 18:3x kit: TRAIN32 notebook RED on parents_cli.spi (a_child_gets_one_parent_row_and_the_tree_lists_it_under_its_epic): kit agile edit <root> KIT-A --parent EPIC-E printed the usage error. Cause: parent_edit_run and tree_run matched argv as _ :: _ :: _ :: root ..., but env.command_line_arguments () is [agile; <verb>; root; ...] (two leading, like agile_claim_cli / agile_memory_links). Fixed (unstaged, TRAIN33): verb.spi _ :: _ :: root :: child :: flag :: target :: [], epic_tree.spi _ :: _ :: root :: _. The box notebooks had passed because the unit tests never call the argv path; only the cli_check drives it.")
inl scrum_xp_s8 () = Task ("SCRUM-XP-S8", "retro ceremony: kit backlog retro <root> [--since <date>] = items Done in the window plus their last notes and the sprint review numbers; dogfood the first kit sprint with it", TaskKind, P0, D2, 60u32, Active, "SCRUM-XP-S6", "kit,agile,scrum,sprint,team:kit", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_scrum_xp_s8 () = Planning ("SCRUM-XP-S8", Minutes 45u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_scrum_xp_s8_1 () = Note ("SCRUM-XP-S8", 1u32, "2026-11-01T04:33Z", "kit", "11-01 00:3x kit, slice of EOIE-SCRUM-XP (user 00:2x via main: 'speed up the agile->scrum/xp migration'; slices of an hour or less). Spec: EOIE-SCRUM-XP M3 DESIGN note (10-30 13:5x) and the 10-22 typestate draft.")
inl note_of_scrum_xp_s8_2 () = Note ("SCRUM-XP-S8", 2u32, "2026-11-01T12:39Z", "kit", "11-01 08:3x kit QUALITY PASS. DoD: 'kit backlog retro <root> --since <date>' prints items Done in the window with their last notes and the closed sprints' review numbers (CLI test on a fixture); kit's first real sprint is started, run and closed with these verbs and its retro output is pasted into this item as the dogfooding artifact. NEXT STEP: retro verb over agile_cadence (done items in window) + sprints_of. TYPEFEATURESET PLAN: retro entry as a union (DoneItem of task_id * note | SprintReview of sprint_id * sprint_review) so the report is a typed list rendered once; the window as a nominal date range record.")
inl note_of_scrum_xp_s8_3 () = Note ("SCRUM-XP-S8", 3u32, "2026-11-01T18:15Z", "kit", "11-01 14:15 kit: EMACS-STEALS fold: saved queries plus standup (org saved agenda views). Named queries are stored as typed rows (name, filter record), and kit agile standup --team t prints yesterday/today/blocked from StateChange + ActualMinutes rows. 'Spread out' DoD (EOIE-SCRUM-XP n11): every lane's groom report quotes sprint/standup output for 3 days.")
inl note_of_scrum_xp_s8_4 () = Note ("SCRUM-XP-S8", 4u32, "2026-11-01T20:46Z", "kit", "11-01 16:46 kit: the standup digest is modeled and proven (unstaged), in src/agile_activity. standup_of changes recent maps typed StateChange rows to standup_line = Finished (item, reason) | Started item | Stuck (item, reason); Start and Resume read as started, Finish as finished, Block as stuck, the rest are silent. The time cut is a predicate on the at text, because a string ordering does not satisfy Spiral's comparison constraint. Linux green. NEXT: saved queries as typed rows (name, filter record) and kit agile standup --team t printing the digest from the state; then \"spread out\" = every lane's groom report quotes it for 3 days (EOIE-SCRUM-XP n11).")
inl note_of_scrum_xp_s8_5 () = Note ("SCRUM-XP-S8", 5u32, "2026-11-03T07:33Z", "kit", "kit groom 11-03 03:3x (WIP cap, memory wip-cap-active-means-touched): Active -> Planned, last note 35 h ago by kit, progress kept at 30%. Resume from that note: saved queries as typed rows (name, filter record) and kit agile standup --team t printing the digest from the state; then \"spread out\" = every lane's groom report quotes it for 3 days (EOIE-SCRUM-XP n11).")
inl note_of_scrum_xp_s8_6 () = Note ("SCRUM-XP-S8", 6u32, "2026-11-04T09:37Z", "kit", "11-04 07:0x kit: retro prep. agile_note_listing gains two direct linkable exports, kit_agile_last_note ((text, id) -> newest note via the existing \"last 1\" filter) and kit_agile_segments_of (state dir -> segment text, since notes can live in segments); vultr pack34 --check exit 0. PLAN for the verb (`sprint retro <root> <id>` in kit_agile_sprint, after the crate's node size run): the sprint's review row (done/carried/dropped minutes, goal_met) plus, per item, its census status and last note. \"Items Done in the window\" by date needs KIT-AGILE-CADENCE StatusChanged rows; until then the window is the sprint itself.")
inl note_of_scrum_xp_s8_7 () = Note ("SCRUM-XP-S8", 7u32, "2026-11-04T12:40Z", "kit", "11-04 10:0x kit: `kit agile sprint retro <root> [<id>]` BUILT (unstaged, routed through the sprint verb family). It lives in the new show unit kit_agile_sprint_show: a typed show_depth (ShowItems | ShowNotes) drives one printer, so retro = show plus each item's last note (agile_note_listing's kit_agile_last_note export, clipped at 160 chars), with receipt `kit agile sprint retro ok id=... status= start= finish= days_left= goal=` and committed/done minutes. The review numbers come from `sprint close` (review row) and `sprint velocity`. vultr pack34 --check exit 0 (show + sprint, with bodies). DEVIATION from the title: `--since <date>` is not implemented; the window is the sprint (dating Done needs KIT-AGILE-CADENCE StatusChanged rows). NEXT: dogfood the first kit sprint with it after TRAIN22.")
inl backend_metrics_model () = Task ("BACKEND-METRICS-MODEL", "Typed per-backend compile/run metric model (user 11-01 00:2x: Prometheus-level, typed with the typefeatureset): which backend drains most of our time, the Rust vs Gleam sweet spot, when Zig beats C on every axis, when Lua or Wasm is the best fast backend", Story, P0, D4, 100u32, Done, "", "team:compiler,compiler,metrics,performance,typefeatureset", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_backend_metrics_model () = Planning ("BACKEND-METRICS-MODEL", Minutes 300u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_backend_metrics_model_1 () = Note ("BACKEND-METRICS-MODEL", 1u32, "2026-11-01T04:34Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 00:34 compiler, round-2 groom (user 00:2x: backend codegen is CPU-light text work, write it while runs batch): OWNERSHIP: compiler owns the row model and the per-backend numbers; kit owns collection (KIT-EXEC-METRICS, main's naming). MODEL: one row per (sample, backend, phase in Spiral|Build|Run) with core hash, machine, wall_ms, cpu_ms, peak_rss_bytes, output_bytes, exit, agrees_with_oracle. Units are phantom types (ms, bytes) so a ms can't be added to a byte count. The Prometheus names are spiral_backend_seconds{backend,sample,phase}, spiral_backend_peak_bytes{...} and spiral_backend_agree{...}. QUESTIONS IT ANSWERS: (1) time drain = sum of wall by backend over a day of matrix + cells runs; (2) Rust vs Gleam = Build+Run wall per sample size class; (3) Zig vs C on every axis = per-sample Zig/C ratios for build, run, rss and size, with the gap list (which axis loses, which sample) as the step-by-step improvement queue; (4) Lua/Wasm = run-wall rank among fast-startup backends. FIRST CPU-LIGHT STEP: write the row type in Spiral (lib-first, granular) and an importer from today's results.tsv/rows.tsv columns, so the past matrix runs become the first data.")
inl note_of_backend_metrics_model_2 () = Note ("BACKEND-METRICS-MODEL", 2u32, "2026-11-01T07:09Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 03:09 compiler: first rows for the model, measured build-free today: (1) lib test targets per backend (rust 427, gleam 205, cuda 141, ts 117, fsharp 72, lua 65, zig 27, c 22, delphi 10, python 9, lean/wasm/kotlin/swift 0; 746 tests): coverage = share of tests per backend; (2) Rust emit quality: clones per kloc 94.6 -> 85.3 after pack12b's Copy-field edit (2,778 -> 2,510 clones in 476-477 samples); (3) matrix volume per backend in one full run: Zig 1,088 rows, C 552, Rust 508, Lean 458, Kotlin 452, Python/Swift 451, TS 449, Lua 448, Gleam 447, Delphi 446, Wasm 444, Bend 420, Fsharp 396. The row model must carry suite (contracts vs examples) to separate expected negatives from gaps.")
inl note_of_backend_metrics_model_3 () = Note ("BACKEND-METRICS-MODEL", 3u32, "2026-11-01T12:34Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 08:34 compiler, ROUND 4 QUALITY SPEC: FIELDS ok (P0, 300m, light, team:compiler). DEFINITION OF DONE: lib/spiral/metrics.spi with tests green on rust and gleam (lib_gate.ps1 -File metrics.spi); an importer program that reads a matrix results.tsv into typed rows and prints Prometheus text, whose output for run single-flight-20261101-080900 has exactly 7,409 'spiral_backend_compile_seconds' samples and is parsed back by a test (round trip). NEXT STEP: write metrics.spi's types (below) and one test that builds a row and renders one exposition line. TYPEFEATURESET PLAN: units are PHANTOMS: ':! quantity u = f64' with nominal unit tags 'ms', 'bytes', 'count', so adding ms to bytes doesn't type check; the backend is a UNION (C | Rust | Zig | Gleam | ...; mirror lib's 'union backend', never a string); phase is a UNION (Spiral | Build | Run | Emit | Oracle); a metric is a GADT indexed by its unit: 'compile_seconds : metric ms', 'peak_bytes : metric bytes', 'agree : metric count', so a sample's value type follows from the metric; Prometheus rendering is a PROTOTYPE 'exposition t' with instances per metric (no reflection over field names); the row's suite is a union (examples | contracts | frontier | mega), which separates expected negatives from gaps (the n2 lesson). Model: lib/spiral/date_time.spi's time_span nominal for units, am' for arrays of rows.")
inl note_of_backend_metrics_model_4 () = Note ("BACKEND-METRICS-MODEL", 4u32, "2026-11-02T21:33Z", "compiler", "11-02 17:40 compiler: SLICE 1 GREEN, land pending. Draft: %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\spiral\\BACKEND-METRICS-MODEL\\metrics.spi. Phantom units (`:! ms/bytes/count = ()`, `:! quantity u = f64`); unions backend_name (C, Rust, Zig, Gleam, Fsharp, Lua, Wasm, Pascal) and suite (Examples, Contracts, Frontier, Mega); a GADT `metric u` indexed by its unit (CompileSeconds/NativeSeconds : metric ms, PeakBytes : metric bytes, OracleAgree : metric count), so a sample's value type follows from its metric; `sample u` record; `exposition` renders one Prometheus line. Node lib gate on a mirror copy: 1/1 passed (rust + gleam targets), 0 type errors, executed > 0. LAND: `lib_land_window.ps1 -Draft \"metrics.spi=<draft path>\" -Label ... -Apply` once kit-TRAIN13b releases the heavy window (held at 17:38; I don't land into a held window). NEXT SLICES: the exposition prototype per metric (the plan's no-reflection rule); the results.tsv importer (typed rows -> samples) with the DoD round-trip test (7,409 compile_seconds samples for single-flight-20261101-080900).")
inl note_of_backend_metrics_model_5 () = Note ("BACKEND-METRICS-MODEL", 5u32, "2026-11-02T21:48Z", "compiler", "11-02 17:50 compiler: SLICE 2 GREEN (node lib gate on a mirror copy: 3/3 passed on rust + gleam, 0 type errors). Same draft: backend_of / suite_of (string -> union, None for unknown), compile_sample (one results.tsv row -> option (sample ms), seconds = compile_ms / 1000), rendered. Tests: the real first row of single-flight-20261101-080900 renders `spiral_backend_compile_seconds{backend=\"c\",suite=\"examples\",id=\"samples/abi_external\"} 0.794`; an unknown backend row yields none. That run has exactly 7,409 rows, all with compile_ms, matching the DoD count. NEXT SLICE: an importer main (file_system read, lines, compile_sample per row, count + print) plus the round-trip test; then land (still waiting on kit-TRAIN13b's heavy window).")
inl note_of_backend_metrics_model_6 () = Note ("BACKEND-METRICS-MODEL", 6u32, "2026-11-02T22:54Z", "compiler", "11-02 18:50 compiler: slice 3 green on node t1101, lib_gate 4/4 (rust+gleam), 0 type errors: results_exposition text = arraym::init (sm'::line_count text) (line_at) as array string |> am'::choose compile_sample |> am'::map' exposition; the test keeps 2 of 3 rows (unknown backend dropped). First try used sm'::lines, which is Rust-only (Gleam got panic as ... then .size on it: Unknown type for record access); see SM-LINES-PORTABLE. Draft %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\spiral\\BACKEND-METRICS-MODEL\\metrics.spi. Land via lib_land_window once kit-TRAIN13b frees the heavy window (also needs a metrics line at the end of lib package.spiproj modules).")
inl note_of_backend_metrics_model_7 () = Note ("BACKEND-METRICS-MODEL", 7u32, "2026-11-03T00:33Z", "compiler", "11-02 20:3x compiler: landing queued in the same box job as SM-LINES-PORTABLE (job-1793665902376-compiler-lib-land-smlines-metrics): metrics.spi as a new lib file (base new) and package.spiproj D17DC89255957311 with '    metrics' appended after iggy. It applies when kit-TRAIN13c releases the heavy window; then a document_test row check.")
inl note_of_backend_metrics_model_8 () = Note ("BACKEND-METRICS-MODEL", 8u32, "2026-11-03T01:31Z", "compiler", "11-02 21:3x compiler: slice 4 green on node t1101, lib_gate 6/6 (rust + gleam), 0 type errors. Program BACKEND-METRICS-MODEL\\4-native-and-oracle.kitdo (2 replacements on lib metrics.spi 85075BB36A0B89E4 -> 2974F7FBB2EAB095): results_rows factored out, native_sample (NativeSeconds from native_ms on native=ran rows), oracle_sample (OracleAgree 1/0 from agree/DISAGREE, other verdicts skipped), results_oracle_samples; tests native_sample_reads_ran_rows and oracle_samples_skip_rows_without_a_verdict. Landing queued: box job job-1793669481543-compiler-lib-land-metrics4.")
inl note_of_backend_metrics_model_9 () = Note ("BACKEND-METRICS-MODEL", 9u32, "2026-11-03T02:21Z", "compiler", "11-02 22:2x compiler: slices 4 and 5 LANDED (unstaged, both closed green: document_test 22 passed, consumers compile). Lib metrics.spi now CBB772589AA5DE13. Slice 4: native_sample, oracle_sample, results_oracle_samples (land undo 20261102-213129). Slice 5: results_file_exposition (path), i.e. read_all_text then results_exposition, with a temp-file round-trip test on rust + gleam (node gate 7/7; land undo 20261102-221635). The model now reads a real results.tsv file into Prometheus lines for compile seconds, native seconds and oracle agreement per backend and suite. Next: a node runner that writes the .prom text for a run directory (consumer), then the clones_per_kloc emit measure from RUST-MOVE-ON-LAST-USE n7.")
inl note_of_backend_metrics_model_10 () = Note ("BACKEND-METRICS-MODEL", 10u32, "2026-11-03T05:03Z", "compiler", "11-03 01:0x compiler: slice 6 green on node t1101 (lib_gate 10/10 rust + gleam, 0 type errors) after one fix round (a multi-line lambda argument to fold did not parse -> named step heavier; choose result needed an array f64 annotation, value restriction). Program BACKEND-METRICS-MODEL\\6-backend-totals.kitdo (1 replacement, lib metrics.spi CBB772589AA5DE13 -> C244036D7A779DF7): backends (), sample_value, backend_total (sum per backend), heavier/heaviest (the backend draining the most time, None when no rows), total_line, results_totals (spiral_backend_compile_seconds_total{backend=...} per backend present) and results_heaviest; tests for totals, heaviest and the empty case. This answers the first question of the story (which backend drains most of our time) from a results.tsv. Landing queued: box job job-1793682173099-compiler-lib-land-metrics6.")
inl note_of_backend_metrics_model_11 () = Note ("BACKEND-METRICS-MODEL", 11u32, "2026-11-03T05:17Z", "compiler", "11-03 01:1x compiler: slice 6 LANDED (unstaged, closed green: 0 consumer signatures changed, apps/spiral/spiral.spi compiles, document_test 22 passed, type_probe 10 typed tests 0 errors). Lib metrics.spi now C244036D7A779DF7; land undo 20261103-011051. Left on the story: the Rust-vs-Gleam sweet spot and Zig-beats-C questions (pairwise per-sample comparison over two backends), then a run over a real examples results.tsv.")
inl note_of_backend_metrics_model_12 () = Note ("BACKEND-METRICS-MODEL", 12u32, "2026-11-03T05:39Z", "compiler", "11-03 01:3x compiler: FIRST REAL RUN. A Spiral main (metrics::results_totals + results_heaviest over a file, compiled to Rust with the installed pack22 against the box lib) read 8 merged t1101 results.tsv files from tonight (2106 lines: examples C, Cpp, Gleam, Lua, OpenQasm, Python A/B arms). Model: c 115.056 s, gleam 79.860 s, lua 171.164 s, heaviest = lua; a PowerShell sum over the same rows gives the same three numbers. GAPS it exposed: (1) Python (70.4 s), Cpp (15.0 s) and OpenQasm (79.1 s) rows are dropped because backend_name lacks them -> slice 7 (7-more-backends.kitdo, metrics C244036D7A779DF7 -> 0F83CC7888E5E9B5, node gate compiler-metrics7 running); (2) heaviest took ~15 s on 2106 rows in Rust (backend_total rescans every sample per comparison, plus array clones): a one-pass total per backend is the next slice; (3) totals print binary-float noise (115.05600000000017), fine for Prometheus but worth rounding to ms.")
inl note_of_backend_metrics_model_13 () = Note ("BACKEND-METRICS-MODEL", 13u32, "2026-11-03T05:42Z", "compiler", "11-03 01:4x compiler: slice 7 green on t1101 (lib_gate 11/11, 0 type errors): BackendPython, BackendCpp, BackendOpenQasm in backend_name, labels, backend_of and backends (), plus test results_totals_cover_python_cpp_and_openqasm. Landing queued: box job job-1793684486358-compiler-lib-land-metrics7 (base C244036D7A779DF7 -> 0F83CC7888E5E9B5).")
inl note_of_backend_metrics_model_14 () = Note ("BACKEND-METRICS-MODEL", 14u32, "2026-11-03T05:51Z", "compiler", "11-03 01:5x compiler: slice 7 LANDED (closed green: 0 consumer signatures changed, document_test 22 passed, type_probe 11 typed tests; metrics.spi 0F83CC7888E5E9B5; undo 20261103-014141). SLICE 8 (8-rows-one-split.kitdo, 0F83CC78 -> D02D8C363CE25887): results_rows = sm'::lines text instead of arraym::init line_count (line_at i), which re-split the whole text per row (O(n^2)). Real run on t1101, same 2106 lines: all six backends now (c 115.056, gleam 79.860, lua 171.164, python 70.412, cpp 14.982, openqasm 79.052 s, each equal to the PowerShell sum), heaviest lua, model 78 ms end to end (was ~40 s, ~15 s of it in heaviest). Gate: 11/11 rust + gleam against the box lib; against the node mirror lib gleam fails because the mirror sm' predates portable lines (expected, not this change). Landing queued: job-1793685021077-compiler-lib-land-metrics8.")
inl note_of_backend_metrics_model_15 () = Note ("BACKEND-METRICS-MODEL", 15u32, "2026-11-03T05:58Z", "compiler", "11-03 01:5x compiler: slice 8 LANDED (closed green: 0 consumer signatures changed, document_test 22 passed, type_probe 11 typed tests; metrics.spi D02D8C363CE25887; undo 20261103-015036). Left: the Rust-vs-Gleam and Zig-vs-C pairwise questions (per-sample comparison over two backends) and rounding totals to ms.")
inl note_of_backend_metrics_model_16 () = Note ("BACKEND-METRICS-MODEL", 16u32, "2026-11-03T07:59Z", "compiler", "11-03 04:0x compiler: PAUSED (run not started). State: slice 9 (9-head-to-head.kitdo, metrics D02D8C363CE25887 -> 784FB4491FA12E50: value_for, beats, head_to_head a b -> wins, shared samples; results_head_to_head; test head_to_head_counts_shared_samples_only) is GREEN on t1101 against the box lib tree (12/12 rust + gleam, 0 type errors), NOT landed. Next step: queue its land (lib_land_queue, base D02D8C363CE25887), then run results_head_to_head Rust Gleam and Zig C over a fresh examples results.tsv for the two open questions; round totals to ms. Waits on: the run starting.")
inl note_of_backend_metrics_model_17 () = Note ("BACKEND-METRICS-MODEL", 17u32, "2026-11-03T09:34Z", "compiler", "11-03 05:3x compiler: slice 9 LANDED (closed green: document_test 22 passed, type_probe 12 typed tests; metrics.spi 784FB4491FA12E50; undo 20261103-052241). Next: run results_head_to_head Rust Gleam and Zig C over a fresh examples results.tsv once main has synced the node mirror.")
inl note_of_backend_metrics_model_18 () = Note ("BACKEND-METRICS-MODEL", 18u32, "2026-11-03T10:27Z", "compiler", "11-03 06:3x compiler: FIRST ANSWERS from the landed model (slice 9 head_to_head, Rust build of a lib-tree main) over real t1101 examples runs today on installed pack23/24 (Rust 515 rows, Gleam 441, a fresh C,Zig run 559/470): ZIG BEATS C on compile time for 330 of 468 shared samples (71%), medians 151 ms vs 194 ms. RUST VS GLEAM is close: Rust faster on 242 of 441 (55%), but Gleam's median is lower (222 ms vs 236 ms), so Rust's wins are narrow and its losses wide. Heaviest total: rust. Every model count equals a PowerShell per-sample comparison of the same rows. Lua vs Python needs a fresh Lua examples run (the picked run held 1 row). Open: native (run-time) head-to-head per pair, and rounding totals to ms.")
inl note_of_backend_metrics_model_19 () = Note ("BACKEND-METRICS-MODEL", 19u32, "2026-11-03T10:38Z", "compiler", "11-03 06:4x compiler: slice 10 LANDED (box lib gate 13/13 rust + gleam, 0 type errors, since node t1101 was unreachable by LAN name for a while; closed green: document_test 22 passed, type_probe 13 typed tests; metrics.spi 447B9EF6E21BCFC7; undo 20261103-063445): results_native_head_to_head a b text compares native_ms over ran rows only, test native_head_to_head_counts_ran_rows_only. Real native run on t1101 queued (job compiler-h2hn: examples C,Zig,Rust,Gleam -Native on pack24, then compile and native head-to-heads for Rust/Gleam, Zig/C, Rust/C with a PowerShell cross-check).")
inl note_of_backend_metrics_model_20 () = Note ("BACKEND-METRICS-MODEL", 20u32, "2026-11-03T11:13Z", "compiler", "11-03 07:1x compiler: COMPILE + NATIVE-TIER HEAD-TO-HEADS on one t1101 run (examples C,Zig,Rust,Gleam -Native on installed pack24, 1985 rows; native ran C 521, Gleam 440, Rust 491, Zig 470); every model count equals the PowerShell cross-check. COMPILE (Spiral): Zig faster than C on 329/468 (medians 148 vs 191 ms), Rust faster than C on 353/514, Rust vs Gleam 216/441 (even). NATIVE_MS is the whole native tier per row (toolchain build + run), so it measures the toolchain more than the program: Rust beats Gleam on 420/440 (2.7 s vs 9.4 s median, mostly gleam build), C beats Zig (1.1 vs 1.6 s; Zig wins 69/468) and Rust (1.1 vs 2.7 s; Rust wins 46/490). Pure run time needs a run-only column from test.ps1 (native_ms splits build vs run); that is the next model input. Lua/Python still need a fresh Lua run.")
inl note_of_backend_metrics_model_21 () = Note ("BACKEND-METRICS-MODEL", 21u32, "2026-11-03T12:17Z", "compiler", "11-03 08:1x compiler: slice 11 (RunSeconds metric, run_sample over test.ps1's run_ms column = the last native process alone, i.e. the built binary's run for C/Rust/Zig, gleam run for Gleam; results_run_head_to_head; test run_head_to_head_reads_the_run_column) green on t1101 against the box lib tree 14/14, round-trip canonical; landing queued (job compiler-lib-land-metrics11, base 447B9EF6E21BCFC7 -> 31BA091E590AE009). RUN-TIME answers on the 06:36 -Native run (pack24), model = PowerShell exactly: Zig faster than C on 179/468 (medians 107 vs 82 ms), Rust faster than C on 168/490 (131 vs 81 ms), Rust faster than Zig on 199/453 (129 vs 108 ms), Rust vs Gleam 440/440 (gleam run includes its build). CAVEAT: examples are tiny, so run medians mostly measure process start (Rust and Zig start main on a 1 GB-stack thread); the hot-loop question needs the mega samples or tibia's transformer, not examples.")
inl note_of_backend_metrics_model_22 () = Note ("BACKEND-METRICS-MODEL", 22u32, "2026-11-03T12:50Z", "compiler", "11-03 08:5x compiler: slice 11 LANDED 08:19 (closed green: document_test 22 passed, type_probe 14; metrics.spi 31BA091E590AE009; undo 20261103-081503). Slice 12 (the nameof heal of suite_label/suite_of through reflection) green 14/14, landing queued.")
inl note_of_backend_metrics_model_23 () = Note ("BACKEND-METRICS-MODEL", 23u32, "2026-11-03T13:14Z", "compiler", "11-03 09:0x compiler: slice 12 LANDED (closed green: document_test 22 passed, type_probe 14; metrics.spi 301D4B755DC3415C; undo 20261103-085256): suite_label and suite_of now go through reflection::case_name / union_cases_find (SPIRAL-NAMEOF-HEAL's first rewrite).")
inl note_of_backend_metrics_model_24 () = Note ("BACKEND-METRICS-MODEL", 24u32, "2026-11-04T00:28Z", "compiler", "11-03 20:3x compiler: QUESTION 4 (when Lua or Wasm is the best fast backend), first answer from run_ms of the last full -Native run that has both (single-flight-20261030-163636, examples, native ran). Medians: C 35 ms (500 rows), Zig 36 (464), Rust 38 (470), Lua 86 (437), Wasm 159 (434), TypeScript 300 (437), Python 300 (439), Gleam 1508 (434, gleam run). Head to head: Lua faster than Wasm on 336/431, than TypeScript on 422/434, than Python on 430/434; Wasm faster than TypeScript on 277/430; Lua beats C on 20/437, Wasm beats C on 6/433. So among the non-native targets Lua (lua5.1) is the fastest by start-to-exit, then Wasm (wasmtime), and neither competes with C/Zig/Rust. CAVEAT, as slice 11's: examples are tiny, so these medians mostly measure interpreter or runtime start (lua5.1 ~80 ms, wasmtime ~150 ms, node ~300 ms); the hot-loop answer needs the mega samples (runtime_bench) on vultr. Computed with PowerShell over results.tsv; the Spiral model (results_run_head_to_head, slice 11) reads the same column.")
inl note_of_backend_metrics_model_25 () = Note ("BACKEND-METRICS-MODEL", 25u32, "2026-11-04T00:32Z", "compiler", "11-03 20:3x compiler: QUESTION 4 HOT-LOOP ANSWER (node, compiler core 24EEB518 = pack31, mega_brzozowski_derivatives/runtime_bench; 3 runs each, every run exit 0): Zig 71 ms median (87/71/70), Wasm 114 (300 first, then 112/114: wasmtime caches the compiled module), C 210 (197/215/210, gcc -O2 without the matrix CFlags), Lua 7563 (7330/7588/7563). So for hot loops WASM is the fast non-native backend: 66x faster than Lua and here even ahead of C, while Lua is 36x slower than C. The examples-only answer (n24: Lua 86 vs Wasm 159 ms medians) was start-up, not compute. My prediction (C/Zig 1-2 s, Wasm 2-4x C) was wrong on both. A plausible reason Wasm beats C is its never-freeing bump allocator against C's refcounting, but that is not measured. DoD for Q4 met for this sample; a second compute sample (tibia's transformer) would confirm.")
inl note_of_backend_metrics_model_26 () = Note ("BACKEND-METRICS-MODEL", 26u32, "2026-11-04T05:32Z", "compiler", "11-04 01:3x compiler: DONE. The typed model is landed in lib metrics.spi (phantom units ms/bytes/count, backend_name and suite unions, a GADT metric u indexed by unit; slices 1-11: totals, head_to_head, run_head_to_head over run_ms). All four questions of the title have a measured answer from it, each cross-checked against a plain per-sample comparison of the same rows. (1) Most time: Rust (heaviest total compile, n 11-03 06:27). (2) Rust vs Gleam: close; Rust is faster on 55% of 441 shared samples but Gleam's median is lower (222 vs 236 ms), so Rust's wins are narrow and its losses wide. (3) Zig vs C: Zig compiles faster on 71% of 468 shared samples (151 vs 194 ms medians); run-time pairs are via results_run_head_to_head (slice 11). (4) Fast non-native backend: Wasm, start-up apart. Hot loop (runtime_bench, node, pack31): Zig 71 ms, Wasm 114, C 210, Lua 7563. Examples-only medians (Lua 86 vs Wasm 159 ms) measure start-up, not compute. Follow-ups, not blocking: a second compute sample (tibia's transformer) to confirm Q4's ranking, and DOTNET-ORACLE-COST, now unblocked.")
inl dotnet_oracle_cost () = Task ("DOTNET-ORACLE-COST", "Metric: the cost of keeping the .NET (F#) compiler as the reference oracle until the Rust/Gleam rewrite", TaskKind, P1, D3, 100u32, Done, "BACKEND-METRICS-MODEL", "team:compiler,compiler,metrics,performance,typefeatureset,self-hosting", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_dotnet_oracle_cost () = Planning ("DOTNET-ORACLE-COST", Minutes 120u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_dotnet_oracle_cost_1 () = Note ("DOTNET-ORACLE-COST", 1u32, "2026-11-01T04:34Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 00:34 compiler, round-2 groom (user 00:2x: backend codegen is CPU-light text work, write it while runs batch): (user 11-01 00:2x). MEASURE, per pack and per day: the dotnet build time of SpiralCompiler(+Core) per candidate; the share of matrix wall spent in Fsharp rows; dotnet RAM peaks (GC pause dominates compiles); lib bodies that exist only for .NET APIs (Fsharp arms with no Rust/Gleam counterpart: count them by backend_switch census); upstream-core compatibility churn. Report as BACKEND-METRICS-MODEL rows (phase=Oracle). Feeds SELF-HOSTED-SPIRAL's decision on when the oracle moves to the Rust build.")
inl note_of_dotnet_oracle_cost_2 () = Note ("DOTNET-ORACLE-COST", 2u32, "2026-11-04T05:44Z", "compiler", "11-04 01:4x compiler: FIRST MEASUREMENTS (CPU-light, from existing data).\n(1) dotnet build per candidate: node (DESKTOP-6E8C14G) today 282 s, 340 s and 436 s (gadtx, linkwarn2, pack34; TRAIN18 shared the node), 283 s on 11-03 (monorule). So about 5-7 min of node CPU per reviewed program, plus the same again for every combined pack.\n(2) F# rows in the matrix: 404 of 7,439 rows (5.4%) are 8.0% of a fresh compile pass (11-01 18:22: 6.0 of 74.7 min); on the last full -Native run (10-30 16:36) F# is 3.1% of native wall (4.2 of 133.4 min). The heavy backends are Zig (18-37% of compile), Rust and Gleam native. Keeping the F# rows is cheap.\n(3) .NET-only lib bodies (census of all 253 lib backend_switch blocks; an arm counts if it is not a failwith stub): 150 have a real F# arm, and only 9 have a real F# arm with no real Rust or Gleam arm. Those are exceptions (base try_unit x2, try_finally, resultm from_try), async (do, return_await'), base yield_from, rust fsharp_global and trace init_trace_state. That is the semantic gap a Rust or Gleam oracle has to close.\n(4) RAM peaks are not in results.tsv. Per memory gc-pause-dominates-compiles, GC pauses dominate compile wall. A measured peak needs kit's collection (KIT-EXEC-METRICS).\n(5) Also seen: frontier_format_any_union and frontier_join_format_any take 37-40 s to compile on F# against the 20 s examples cap, so they flip to timeout under load (pack34 review: control 4 timeouts, candidate 0, identical residuals on a 180 s box rerun).\nReading: the oracle's own cost is the .NET compiler build (minutes per candidate) and its GC-bound compiles, not the F# rows. Next: the per-pack build time from install-pack logs, and the upstream-core churn count.")
inl note_of_dotnet_oracle_cost_3 () = Note ("DOTNET-ORACLE-COST", 3u32, "2026-11-04T11:53Z", "compiler", "11-04 07:5x compiler: PER-CANDIDATE BUILD TIME (the n-last 'next' item). install-pack.ps1 writes no log, so the source is the node review logs, which stamp every step: the gap from the last 'apply' to 'candidate core <hash>' is one .NET compiler build (core + host, Release, single-flight, private SPIRAL_BIN_CACHE_DIR). Today on the node with 3-4 concurrent jobs: bendsafe3 5.9 min, splitu7 6.9 min, splitu4 8.4 min (splitu6r's 10.2 includes its first oracle cut, so it is an upper bound). So every candidate pays about 6-8.5 min of build before any check runs; a typical day today had 9 candidate builds across node and box (pack36, shape x2, Bend x2, and the oracle/review jobs), i.e. roughly 60-75 machine-minutes of pure F# compile for the oracle. That and the GC-bound compiles (n-earlier) are the oracle's cost; the F# rows themselves are not. Next: the upstream-core churn count, then the item can be summarized.")
inl note_of_dotnet_oracle_cost_4 () = Note ("DOTNET-ORACLE-COST", 4u32, "2026-11-04T11:54Z", "compiler", "11-04 07:5x compiler: UPSTREAM-CORE CHURN (deps/The-Spiral-Language at f4e54a0e, 2026-11-01): commits touching 'VS Code Plugin/core' = 2 in 30 days, 2 in 90, 2 in 365, and both are our fork's own 'tests' commits (cc1b12ac 10-19, eea8e980 11-01). The last upstream (mrakgr) change to core was merged 2024-03-30 ('The old core lib should now work with exists as well'). So upstream core churn is effectively zero, and keeping the .NET compiler as oracle carries no upstream-sync cost. SUMMARY of the metric so far: (1) per-candidate .NET build 6-8.5 min on the node under load, ~60-75 machine-min/day today; (2) GC-bound compiles (frontier_format_any_union / frontier_join_format_any 37-40 s vs the 20 s cap, flipping to timeout under load); (3) F# rows themselves cheap; (4) upstream sync ~0. The dominant cost is (1), so the lever is fewer full rebuilds (incremental host/core builds, or reusing one candidate build across several reviews), not dropping the oracle.")
inl note_of_dotnet_oracle_cost_5 () = Note ("DOTNET-ORACLE-COST", 5u32, "2026-11-04T13:02Z", "compiler", "11-04 09:0x compiler: LAST METRIC + VERDICT. (5) .NET-only lib bodies (backend_switch census over lib/spiral *.spi/*.spir, bench/target/tests excluded, brace-matched blocks): 159 switches have an Fsharp arm; 24 lack a Rust arm, 16 lack a Gleam arm, and 9 have NEITHER (async.spi:354, :364; base.spi:162, :739, :768, :816; resultm.spi:29; rust.spi:21; one more), mostly Fsharp+Python pairs. So moving the oracle off .NET would orphan 9 lib bodies, and 24/16 more need a Rust/Gleam arm before those backends cover what F# does. VERDICT on the cost of keeping .NET as the oracle: (a) per-candidate compiler build 6-8.5 min on a loaded node, ~60-75 machine-min/day today, the dominant cost; (b) GC-bound compiles flip two frontier rows to timeout under load (37-40 s vs the 20 s cap); (c) F# matrix rows themselves are cheap; (d) upstream core churn ~0 (last upstream core change 2024-03-30); (e) 9 lib bodies are .NET-only. Recommendation for SELF-HOSTED-SPIRAL: keep the oracle; cut (a) by building one candidate per review batch and reusing it across reviews (today's runs rebuilt overlapping candidates 9 times), and file the 9 .NET-only bodies as the porting list. Done as a metric.")
inl compiler_wipe_damage () = Task ("COMPILER-WIPE-DAMAGE", "What the 10-28 profile wipe cost the compiler lane, and the heal and make-up tasks it implies", Story, P1, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "team:compiler,compiler,wipe-redo,retro", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_compiler_wipe_damage () = Planning ("COMPILER-WIPE-DAMAGE", Minutes 90u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_compiler_wipe_damage_1 () = Note ("COMPILER-WIPE-DAMAGE", 1u32, "2026-11-01T04:34Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 00:34 compiler, round-2 groom (user 00:2x: backend codegen is CPU-light text work, write it while runs batch): (main 00:2x, sibling of TIBIA-WIPE-DAMAGE). KNOWN COSTS: compiler-lane memories came back only partly (profile-wipe-2026-10-28 lists which); the lab drafts were archived later rather than lost; the toolchain pins (SPIRAL_CC gcc 15.2, FPC 3.3.1) and the candidate bin cache had to be rebuilt; the transcripts before 10-28 are gone, so old decisions survive only as backlog notes. NEXT CONCRETE STEP: read WIPE-REDO-LEDGER's compiler rows and every compiler memory marked PARTIAL; for each one, file a heal task (re-derive it from the tree) or mark it covered by a backlog note. Make-up: any compiler item whose last note predates 10-28 and cites a lost file gets its next step rewritten from the tree.")
inl note_of_compiler_wipe_damage_2 () = Note ("COMPILER-WIPE-DAMAGE", 2u32, "2026-11-01T06:42Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 02:42 compiler, round 3: STEP 1 DONE, read from WIPE-REDO-LEDGER's compiler rows (n4, n5, batch 3, 10-29 12:38 and 14:37) against today's tree and item states. COST of the 10-28 wipe to the compiler lane: (a) the lane transcript (dropped; facts live in ~210 notes); (b) TEMP drafts: P3 shard/IVar runners and results (results INCONCLUSIVE on an oversubscribed node; P3 was re-benched twice on 10-31 with an A/A arm and NOT ADOPTED, so that make-up is complete), OpenQasm tanh + Bend float drafts (redone 10-29), host --check diagnostics patch (redone, and it exposed a false green), GLEAM-COMPACT-OUTPUT compact.do (redone), op-census tooling (dropped: method in memory op-census-method, rerun when needed), CONTRACT-REJECTION negatives (deferred, item Done since); (c) 5 patched candidate cores and 3 pack folders (dropped: shipped in HEAD, reproducible from appliers); (d) lane docs for FORMATTER-KEEP-BREAKS (recoverable via git lanes/formatter/PLAN.md@0ec501fb). Also found during recovery: the node's installed compiler was 4 days stale (since fixed). HEAL left: none blocking. MAKE-UP left, as tasks: (1) apps/compiler/packs still holds apply-hopacsota-p3-shard.ps1 and apply-hopacsota-p3-ivar.ps1, applier drafts of a verdict that's in (NOT ADOPTED), so archive them with scratch-archive (packs lifecycle: rejected leaves the repo); (2) FORMATTER-KEEP-BREAKS's lane docs return via git show when that item unblocks. Estimated total loss: ~1 day of compiler-lane work, all redone or superseded by 10-31.")
inl note_of_compiler_wipe_damage_3 () = Note ("COMPILER-WIPE-DAMAGE", 3u32, "2026-11-01T06:43Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 02:43 compiler: CORRECTION to n2's make-up (1). The 10-31 NOT ADOPTED verdict covered P3's deferred validation (p3defer, whose applier was archived 10-31 18:50), NOT the shard and IVar variants. apply-hopacsota-p3-shard.ps1 / -ivar.ps1 are still open drafts on HOPACSOTA-P3 whose wall-time results were lost to an oversubscribed node, so they stay in packs. Real make-up (1): ONE quiet-node A/B with an A/A arm for shard+IVar (build-candidate -Mode hopacsota with both appliers, measure-arms.ps1 -Trace), queued when HOPACSOTA-HOPAC-DEPTH's design decision says whether caller-thread claims replace them. Nothing to archive.")
inl note_of_compiler_wipe_damage_4 () = Note ("COMPILER-WIPE-DAMAGE", 4u32, "2026-11-03T01:00Z", "compiler", "11-02 21:0x compiler: CLOSING the retro. Costs are enumerated (n2), heals: none blocking, and the memory heals are done (COMPILER-MEMORY-REASONS closed 11-02 after the PARTIAL re-audit, e.g. backend-targets-2026-10-07 re-audit line). The only remaining make-up, one quiet-node A/B with an A/A arm for the P3 shard + IVar appliers, belongs to HOPACSOTA-P3 and waits on HOPACSOTA-HOPAC-DEPTH's design decision, so it is tracked there, not here.")
inl ci_linux_env_restore_guard () = Task ("CI-LINUX-ENV-RESTORE-GUARD", "CI red on ubuntu (runs 41160573276 and 41266957246): lib file_system test directory_delete_refuses_a_protected_surrogate_root panics in env.environment_restored ('refusing an empty or truncated snapshot') on Linux only", TaskKind, P0, D2, 90u32, Blocked, "DELETE-ROOT-GUARD", "team:compiler,ci,lib,file-system,wipe,safety", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_ci_linux_env_restore_guard () = Planning ("CI-LINUX-ENV-RESTORE-GUARD", Minutes 45u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_ci_linux_env_restore_guard_1 () = Note ("CI-LINUX-ENV-RESTORE-GUARD", 1u32, "2026-11-01T04:35Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 00:34 compiler, round-2 groom (user 00:2x: backend codegen is CPU-light text work, write it while runs batch): ROOT CAUSE (read from lib/spiral/env.spi:343-352 + file_system.spi:1467-1481): environment_snapshot_usable demands that every critical name CURRENTLY set is in the snapshot. The test sets LOCALAPPDATA=surrogate, then restores 'before'. On Windows 'before' has LOCALAPPDATA, so it passes. On Linux LOCALAPPDATA was unset in 'before' but is set now, so the guard calls the snapshot truncated, and the env is left with the surrogate. FIX OPTIONS: (a) the guard compares against a critical-name list captured in the snapshot itself, not the live env; or (b) a restore that only removes vars the caller added is allowed (names absent from the snapshot that aren't in the guard's original-env list). Prefer (a): it keeps the anti-truncation intent. Owner of the guard is DELETE-ROOT-GUARD (kit's last note): compiler files and can land it, but asks that lane first because lib is under churn. Windows windows-2025 job was cancelled by fail-fast, so it says nothing.")
inl note_of_ci_linux_env_restore_guard_2 () = Note ("CI-LINUX-ENV-RESTORE-GUARD", 2u32, "2026-11-01T04:36Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 00:36 compiler: LAST 5 i574n/spiral runs (gh-pages workflow): 10-29 13:00 green, 10-30 12:55 green, 10-31 02:33 RED, 10-31 12:29 green, 11-01 02:53 RED. Both reds come from this one test (ubuntu build), so this is a standing Linux red, not a flake. SLOW STEPS: build job 'workflow.ps1' ~33-36 min per OS; cells-gate ci_gate.ps1 26 min plus workflow -Compiler 14 min; compiler -Suite all 16 min. Cells-gate at 41 min is the longest job: a target for BACKEND-METRICS-MODEL rows (cells as fast as F# projects).")
inl note_of_ci_linux_env_restore_guard_3 () = Note ("CI-LINUX-ENV-RESTORE-GUARD", 3u32, "2026-11-01T04:41Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 00:41 compiler: main 00:5x: compiler lands it (kit is on SCRUM-XP-S1). FIX CHOSEN, smaller than (a): environment_snapshot_usable's Linux critical list becomes the Linux-native names [PATH; HOME]. The old Linux list was the Windows list plus HOME; those names are never set on a normal Linux box, so they guarded nothing there except, wrongly, the case where a caller ADDED one (this test's LOCALAPPDATA surrogate) and then restored the snapshot taken before. The Windows list is unchanged, so behaviour on the box and the node is identical. Base env.spi C66DDE65E676F77F. Gate: node mirror envguard (digest 15054FDE8EAA8359), lib_program_gate -Control on env.spi and file_system.spi (jobs job-1793508093582 and job-1793508098130). The Linux proof is the next CI run after the user's push (no hand WSL, per the rule).\nPROGRAM (kit do <lib> -):\nedit env.spi expect files 1 replacements 1\n  replace once <<<\n        |: [ \"PATH\"; \"SystemRoot\"; \"TEMP\"; \"TMP\"; \"USERPROFILE\"; \"LOCALAPPDATA\"; \"APPDATA\"; \"HOME\" ]\n  >>> with <<<\n        |: [ \"PATH\"; \"HOME\" ]\n  >>>")
inl note_of_ci_linux_env_restore_guard_4 () = Note ("CI-LINUX-ENV-RESTORE-GUARD", 4u32, "2026-11-01T05:05Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 01:05 compiler: GATES (node mirror envguard, lib copies at process.spi BE258A03 = live). env.spi: 14/15, failed try_get_environment_variable_is_none_when_unset on TypeScript only. file_system.spi: 33/36, surrogate test PASSED; failed find_parent (fsharp), find_parent_2 (rust) and directory_delete_removes_a_scratch_tree (rust). find_parent needs a 'workspace' parent dir that gate copies don't have (a copy artifact). Slowest cell 277 s of 300 s (node loaded). VERDICT PENDING: none of the failing tests touches environment_snapshot_usable, so these look pre-existing; unmodified-copy controls are running to prove it (job-1793509330599 env TS test, job-1793509504725 file_system full). Land only if each control fails the same set.")
inl note_of_ci_linux_env_restore_guard_5 () = Note ("CI-LINUX-ENV-RESTORE-GUARD", 5u32, "2026-11-01T05:21Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 01:21 compiler: CONTROLS (unmodified copies, same mirror): env TS test PASSED (1/1 run); file_system 34/36, failing only find_parent + find_parent_2 (the copy-artifact pair). So the positive's extra reds, try_get_environment_variable_is_none_when_unset (TypeScript) and directory_delete_removes_a_scratch_tree (Rust), did NOT fail in control. The edit only changes the non-Windows branch, which never runs on the Windows node, so a real regression from it isn't possible there. Load flake is the likely cause (positive's slowest cell was 277 s of 300 s). Not landing on a guess: a targeted rerun of exactly those two tests plus the surrogate test on the edited copy is running (job-1793510469595). LAND RULE: green on the rerun means land; red again means compare the raw causes before anything else.")
inl note_of_ci_linux_env_restore_guard_6 () = Note ("CI-LINUX-ENV-RESTORE-GUARD", 6u32, "2026-11-01T05:33Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 01:33 compiler: RERUN GREEN on the edited copy (job-1793510469595): env.spi try_get_environment_variable_is_none_when_unset 1/1; file_system.spi directory_delete_removes_a_scratch_tree + directory_delete_refuses_a_protected_surrogate_root 2/2. So the positive's 2 extra reds were load flakes, and the edit is proven no-regression on Windows. LAND attempt 01:3x in a tree-write window was REFUSED by lib-guarded-do: 2 live lib readers, a Cells 'mix spiral.x_compat C:\\home\\git\\fiscal' (pids 1976/22384, running since 00:40, not compiler's). Not exempting another lane's reader: a background step waits for those pids to exit and lands then (same note/n3 program, base C66DDE65E676F77F, target 59D341CE372E87E3).")
inl note_of_ci_linux_env_restore_guard_7 () = Note ("CI-LINUX-ENV-RESTORE-GUARD", 7u32, "2026-11-01T06:24Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 02:24 compiler: LANDED 02:24 (tree-write window, land-lib-program -Note CI-LINUX-ENV-RESTORE-GUARD/3): lib/spiral/env.spi C66DDE65E676F77F -> 59D341CE372E87E3, one line (environment_snapshot_usable's non-Windows critical list = [PATH; HOME]), unstaged. Undo: %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\undo\\CI-LINUX-ENV-RESTORE-GUARD-n3. Readers: two refusals first (a Cells 'spiral.x_compat fiscal' check, then tibia's 'spiral.cell_budget --check'); waited them out, never exempted or stopped them. Exempted only my own pack12a heavy-build window, which builds the compiler from a private candidate source and never reads lib/spiral. Left: the Linux proof is the next CI run after the user's push (the ubuntu build should go green on file_system); Done when that run is green.")
inl note_of_ci_linux_env_restore_guard_8 () = Note ("CI-LINUX-ENV-RESTORE-GUARD", 8u32, "2026-11-03T07:34Z", "compiler", "11-03 07:3x compiler GROOM (WIP cap: Active = touched in 12 h). Blocked on the user's push: the only step left is the next CI run after it, then Done.")
inl kit_scale () = Task ("KIT-SCALE", "What breaks first at 20 and 50 concurrent agents (agile state, window store, job host): measured with kit bench agents, not guessed", Story, P0, D4, 0u32, Planned, "", "kit,scale,agile,bench,node,team:kit", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_kit_scale () = Planning ("KIT-SCALE", Minutes 300u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_kit_scale_1 () = Note ("KIT-SCALE", 1u32, "2026-11-01T04:35Z", "kit", "11-01 00:3x kit (user 00:2x via main: 'we gotta have kit stable enough to support [50+ agents]'; measured, not guessed). BASELINE TONIGHT (installed 4983F9C0, ~6 lanes grooming at once, agile.spi 9.63 MB): note 180-950 ms, add 2.4-3.2 s, set 0.5-1.1 s, edit ~1 s; compare-and-swap refusals 2 of ~30 kit writes during the 23:4x groom (each retried once, nothing lost). Whole-file rewrite per write is the dominant cost (the note-segment layout measured 287-306 ms vs ~2,550 ms per note, ships with TRAIN7). WHAT TO MEASURE at 5/10/20/50 concurrent agents, each a separate process doing a fixed op mix (70% note, 15% set, 10% show/list, 5% add) on a COPY of the live backlog: (1) agile: p50/p99 latency per op, CAS refusal rate, retries to success, lost updates (= every op's receipt is in the final state; must be 0), state size growth; whole-file vs segment layout as the A/B, with an in-job A/A (memory ab-needs-in-job-aa). (2) window store: N request/release loops, admission latency, any double grant. (3) job host: N 'job start' of a 1 s child, start latency, orphaned children, supervisor memory. PASS LINE (pre-registered): at 20 agents p99 note < 2 s and 0 lost updates; at 50, report what breaks first. TOOL (spiral-first, no new script): 'kit bench agents <backlog-copy> --agents N --ops M --mix note=70,set=15,read=10,add=5 [--layout whole|segments]' printing typed result rows (feeds KIT-EXEC-METRICS). NEXT CONCRETE STEP (CPU-light): typed bench model (op union, result record, percentile + lost-update check) with tests; then run on the NODE (node-runnable), never on the busy box.")
inl note_of_kit_scale_2 () = Note ("KIT-SCALE", 2u32, "2026-11-01T06:44Z", "kit", "11-01 02:4x kit, MEASURED STARVATION (main, 10-31/11-01 night): 'kit agile rename <root> CELLS-XCOMPAT-SPIRAL CELLS-CELL-BUDGET-SPIRAL' on the 9.7 MB state ran 421 s and was then refused 'state/agile.spi changed since it was read; nothing written', TWICE (once against main's own edit, once against groom-round writes from 7 lanes writing every few seconds). Any whole-file op that needs minutes can never win the compare-and-swap at fleet write rates; rename is the worst case, seal and task rename share the shape. Main parked that rename (old id kept, note points at the new name). TWO FIXES, both in scope here: (1) SPEED: 421 s for a rename is itself a bug (a rename touches one id's rows plus dep references; the 10-31 note writer moved from ~2,550 ms to ~300 ms by not rewriting the whole file); profile it with the bench before designing around it. (2) CONCURRENCY for the ops that stay whole-file: a short exclusive STATE LEASE with a FIFO queue ('kit agile lease --exclusive <op>': writers check it before their CAS and wait up to N s, the whole-file op holds it only for its final rewrite, recomputing from a fresh read under the lease so the compute time is never inside the race), or segment-level CAS once the note/sprint segment layout carries most writes. Pass line addition: at 20 agents, a rename/seal completes within 2 attempts. Bench case: 'kit bench agents --mix note=95,rename=5'.")
inl note_of_kit_scale_3 () = Note ("KIT-SCALE", 3u32, "2026-11-01T12:34Z", "kit", "11-01 08:3x kit QUALITY PASS. DoD (pre-registered): 'kit bench agents <backlog-copy> --agents N --mix note=70,set=15,read=10,add=5' at N = 5, 10, 20, 50 on the NODE prints p50/p99 per op, CAS refusal rate, retries to success and lost updates; PASS at 20 agents: p99 note < 2 s, 0 lost updates, a rename or seal completes within 2 attempts; at 50: the first resource to fail is named with its numbers; whole-file vs segment layout as an A/B with an in-job A/A; artifact: typed result rows feeding KIT-EXEC-METRICS. NEXT STEP: typed bench model (op union, result record, percentile, lost-update check) in a new HostFree package kit_bench_model with tests. TYPEFEATURESET PLAN: bench_op closed union (Note | Set | Read | Add | Rename); mix as a record of nominal weights that must sum to 100 (smart constructor returns option); latency as nominal ms; outcome union (Ok | Refused | LostUpdate) per op so lost updates are counted by pattern, never parsed from text.")
inl note_of_kit_scale_4 () = Note ("KIT-SCALE", 4u32, "2026-11-01T13:11Z", "kit", "11-01 09:1x kit: ESCALATION. Main's 421 s rename starvation (n2) is NOT new: STATE-CONCURRENCY-SOTA lesson L2 recorded the same failure on 10-25 at 6.5 MB ('agile rename rewrites the whole backlog, keeps losing the read-modify-write race to quick note-appends'); it has recurred for a week and grew with the file (9.7 MB, 421 s). Its fix (fine-grained backlog transactions) was parked 'after KIT-RENAME', which is now 75% and no longer the blocker. Treat L2 as this item's first concrete deliverable: profile rename (why minutes), then the exclusive-lease-with-queue for whole-file ops, before the 50-agent bench.")
inl note_of_kit_scale_5 () = Note ("KIT-SCALE", 5u32, "2026-11-01T14:45Z", "kit", "11-01 10:4x kit round-5 concurrency burst: 7 writes (3 adds, 3 notes, 1 edit) issued in parallel from one lane with ThrottleLimit 7, while other lanes write; CAS refusals and latencies logged per attempt, summary in the round-5 report note.")
inl note_of_kit_scale_6 () = Note ("KIT-SCALE", 6u32, "2026-11-01T14:48Z", "kit", "11-01 10:48 kit ROUND-5 CONCURRENT-WRITE DATA (user asked to battle-test the agile state). Load: 45 writes from this lane (8 add, 11 edit, 25 note... one add counted as note in the log), including a 7-way parallel burst (mixed add/note/edit), a 12-way parallel burst of notes on 12 items, and 6+4 parallel idempotent edits of ONE item each, while other lanes wrote (planned count rose 295 -> 319 during the round). RESULTS: CAS refusals 0; non-zero exits 0; lost updates 0 (the bytes_before/bytes_after chain of the 12-way burst is contiguous: each write starts exactly where another ended, so writers serialize). Latency: kit internal elapsed_ms 640-2,319 (the per-write cost of parsing + rewriting the 10 MB file); wall time per call p50 4,218 ms, p90 9,504 ms, max 12,035 ms, i.e. queue position x ~1 s: THROUGHPUT CEILING ~1 write/s for the whole box regardless of lane count. Size: 10,279,163 B at 10:32:47 -> 10,410,204 B at 10:47:53 (+131 KB, +1.3% in 15 min, all lanes). FINDINGS: (1) correctness holds under contention; the cost is latency, and it grows linearly with the file because each write is a full rewrite; (2) no-op edits are not short-circuited: 10 edits setting a field to its current value each took 1.3-2.2 s and rewrote the file with identical bytes, so an unchanged value should return early; (3) CAS refusals come only from cross-process read-then-write (show --json, then edit), not from single-command ops, which lock internally. NEXT (CPU-light): no-op short-circuit in agile edit (compare before write, test: same value -> no write, elapsed < 100 ms); then the append-only segment log already planned here, so a note costs O(note) instead of O(file).")
inl note_of_kit_scale_7 () = Note ("KIT-SCALE", 7u32, "2026-11-01T14:49Z", "kit", "11-01 10:49 kit CORRECTION to n6: the op mix was 8 add, 11 edit, 25 note in 44 parsed log rows; the 45th row was garbled by my own logger (parallel Add-Content to one jsonl from 12 threads interleaved a line), not by kit. All 45 kit calls exited 0 with no CAS refusal. Lesson for the KIT-EXEC-METRICS sample writer: parallel appenders need one writer or per-thread files, the same rule kit's state already follows.")
inl note_of_kit_scale_8 () = Note ("KIT-SCALE", 8u32, "2026-11-01T14:50Z", "kit", "11-01 10:5x kit: exact site for the no-op short-circuit (finding 2 of n6): src/agile_planning_revision/planning_revision.spi:46-48, planning_revised: after rewritten returns StepText updated, an arm \"updated = original\" should print \"kit agile edit ok id=... field=... unchanged\" and return 0 without planning_written (no replace_records_unchanged, no 10 MB rewrite). The same shape applies to src/agile_task_revision/task_revision.spi (title/kind/priority/deps/tags edits). Per kit AGENTS.md, touching planning_revised migrates it: the outcome becomes a typed union (Unchanged | Rewritten text | Rejected reason) instead of planning_step + a string compare at the call site. Test: an edit to the current value leaves the file mtime unchanged and prints unchanged.")
inl note_of_kit_scale_9 () = Note ("KIT-SCALE", 9u32, "2026-11-02T02:57Z", "kit", "11-01 22:57 kit: the no-op short-circuit SHIPPED in TRAIN8 (7C559D83). Acceptance on a fresh backlog: agile edit NOOP-A estimate 30 (its current value) printed \"... field=estimate unchanged ...\", elapsed_ms=1, state mtime unchanged; a real change (45) still writes. Typed as revision = PlanningUnchanged | PlanningRewritten | PlanningRefused in agile_planning_revision. Same shape still to do for agile_task_revision (title/kind/priority/deps/tags).")
inl note_of_kit_scale_10 () = Note ("KIT-SCALE", 10u32, "2026-11-05T21:02Z", "main", "main 11-05 17:0x EVIDENCE at 14.86 MB state with 7 lanes writing: (a) 'kit agile rename <root> WOW-WOWLESS-HARNESS WOW-WOWLESS-SANDBOX' (1 dependent, 2 notes) ran 16:38-16:52, ~14 min wall and 398 s CPU, and never wrote (old ID refs 6, new 0); stopped by main, file intact. Either slow or a compare-and-swap retry that keeps losing to concurrent writers. (b) A note + set + note batch from one PowerShell call took >120 s wall while each command reported elapsed_ms 2.7-6 s, so the wait is queueing outside the reported time. Both fit this item's append-only segments + no-op short-circuit plan; a rename that patches only the touched rows on retry would land under load.")
inl kit_exec_metrics () = Task ("KIT-EXEC-METRICS", "Typed execution metrics for both machines (per backend/phase, Cells vs native, dotnet hold cost) with one Prometheus exporter command", Story, P0, D4, 45u32, Active, "", "kit,metrics,typefeatureset,bench,node,team:kit", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_kit_exec_metrics () = Planning ("KIT-EXEC-METRICS", Minutes 360u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_kit_exec_metrics_1 () = Note ("KIT-EXEC-METRICS", 1u32, "2026-11-01T04:35Z", "kit", "11-01 00:3x kit (user 00:2x via main: Prometheus-level typed metrics for all execution on both machines: per-backend time, Cells vs native, Rust vs Gleam sweet spot, Zig vs C, Lua/Wasm, BEAM overhead, the cost of holding the dotnet compiler). kit owns COLLECTION + one exporter. SOURCES that already exist: kit job receipts (elapsed_ms, peak_private_mb, exit, label; box %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\jobs and the node's), Cells per-cell metadata (duration_ms and phases_ms: compile, rustc, mount, run, slot_wait, cleanup, in every notebook; --metrics dirs from lib_gate), release-train logs (phase times), kit machine sample/watch (cpu, memory), regen logs (per-owner times). MODEL (typefeatureset): an existential metric row 'exec_sample' = { at; machine; source; value : metric } where metric is a GADT keyed by unit (Duration : backend * phase -> metric ms | Bytes : what -> metric bytes | Count : what -> metric n | Ratio ...), backend and phase as unions (Rust | Gleam | Zig | C | Lua | Wasm | Fsharp | Beam ... ; Compile | Rustc | Run | SlotWait | DotnetStartup ...), so a 'Rust vs Gleam' or 'Cells vs native' query is a typed group-by, never a string match. EXPORTER: 'kit metrics export [--since <t>] [--machine box|node|both] [--format prom|tsv-free-typed-rows]' printing Prometheus text exposition (kit_exec_duration_ms{backend,phase,machine} histograms, kit_exec_peak_bytes, kit_job_wait_ms) so any Prometheus/Grafana can scrape a file. First questions it must answer (bench targets): dotnet compiler hold cost (Cells daemon on vs off: SPIRAL_CELLS_SERVER), Cells vs native run of the same program, Rust vs Gleam per lib function, BEAM startup overhead per cell. NEXT CONCRETE STEP (CPU-light): the typed metric model + parsers for two sources (job receipts, Cells notebook phases_ms) with tests on captured fixtures; exporter prints prom text; then node + box collection.")
inl note_of_kit_exec_metrics_2 () = Note ("KIT-EXEC-METRICS", 2u32, "2026-11-01T06:50Z", "kit", "11-01 02:5x kit (round 3): typed model DONE in the tree (unstaged): new granular HostFree+LibReferenced package src/kit_exec_metrics, the typefeatureset shape from n1. Phantom units unit_ms/unit_bytes/unit_count; GADT 'metric unit' (Duration :: backend * phase * u64 -> metric unit_ms | PeakBytes -> metric unit_bytes | Count -> metric unit_count), so a duration can only carry milliseconds; existential row exec_sample = Sample : (\\? unit. machine * metric unit) (lib xsd_certificate's packing idiom), so one list holds every unit; backend (Rust|Gleam|Zig|C|Lua|Wasm|Fsharp), phase (Compile|Rustc|Run|SlotWait|DotnetStartup), machine (Box|Node) as unions. exported renders Prometheus text (kit_exec_duration_ms{machine,backend,phase}, kit_exec_peak_bytes{machine,what}, kit_exec_count); duration_ms is a typed group-by. Test samples_export_as_prometheus_text_and_group_by_backend. PROOF: node job-1793515716737-kit-node-r3k 1/1, first try. NEXT: parsers for job receipts and Cells phases_ms into samples, then 'kit metrics export --format prom'.")
inl note_of_kit_exec_metrics_3 () = Note ("KIT-EXEC-METRICS", 3u32, "2026-11-01T12:34Z", "kit", "11-01 08:3x kit QUALITY PASS. DoD: 'kit metrics export --machine both --since 2026-11-01 --format prom' prints valid Prometheus text (promtool check metrics passes, or a typed self-check of every line) with kit_exec_duration_ms{machine,backend,phase} for every settled job and Cells cell of the window; the dotnet-hold question answered by one bench run (Cells daemon on vs off) whose rows appear in the export; artifact: a .prom file under target/ per run, never tracked. NEXT STEP: parsers job receipt -> exec_sample and Cells notebook phases_ms -> exec_sample in src/kit_exec_metrics, tests on captured fixtures. TYPEFEATURESET PLAN (already in code): phantom units unit_ms / unit_bytes / unit_count, GADT metric indexed by unit (Duration only carries ms), existential exec_sample (\\? unit. machine * metric unit), backend/phase/machine as closed unions; keep: no string labels inside the model (rendered only in prom_line), no -1 for 'unknown phase' (an Unknown constructor if ever needed).")
inl note_of_kit_exec_metrics_4 () = Note ("KIT-EXEC-METRICS", 4u32, "2026-11-01T14:38Z", "kit", "11-01 10:3x kit (round 5): KIT-REUSE-METRICS will export dup_ratio, lib_share and reuse_fanout per repo through the Prometheus export in kit_exec_metrics. Add a static-metric kind to the metric GADT (no exec sample; value per repo per round) when that lands, so code metrics and run metrics share one export.")
inl note_of_kit_exec_metrics_5 () = Note ("KIT-EXEC-METRICS", 5u32, "2026-11-03T07:31Z", "kit", "kit groom 11-03 03:3x (WIP cap, memory wip-cap-active-means-touched): Active -> Planned, last note 41 h ago by kit, progress kept at 25%. Resume from that note: lib_share and reuse_fanout per repo through the Prometheus export in kit_exec_metrics. Add a static-metric kind to the metric GADT (no exec sample; value per repo per round) when that lands, so code metrics and run metrics share one export.")
inl note_of_kit_exec_metrics_6 () = Note ("KIT-EXEC-METRICS", 6u32, "2026-11-04T17:43Z", "kit", "11-04 18:2x kit: the Cells parser is BUILT (unstaged), the NEXT STEP of 11-01. Source: every Cells notebook already records per-cell metadata.phases_ms {compile, mount, slot_wait, rust_lock_wait, target, cleanup} and metadata.spiral_test.targets[0].target (e.g. \"rust --deps sysinfo=...\"), so no new timing hooks are needed. NEW package kit_exec_cells: cell_record {phases : list (phase * u64); target}, backend_of_target (first word of the target vs backend_word), cell_samples (one Duration (backend, phase, ms) per phase; a cell without a target, e.g. the prelude, gives none), cell_records_of (notebook JSON -> records via kit_os_json) and notebook_samples machine text. kit_exec_metrics.phase gains Mount and LockWait (rust_lock_wait); target maps to Run. kit_os_json gains u64_at and length_at (one-expression primitives). Tests: a_cell_record_becomes_one_duration_sample_per_phase (prom line text, sums, no-target cell, backend words) and notebook_cells_are_read_from_their_metadata (a 2-cell fixture with --deps serde_json). vultr --check ok with bodies for json/metrics/cells; box notebooks kit-exec-cells-tests. NOTE for the census owner: kit_exec_metrics already failed the typed-density ratchet BEFORE this change (forall/existential/gadt-case 1|4|3 per 96 lines baseline vs 117 lines in the tree); the 4 new phase-case lines move it to 121. Not re-baselined (baselines only shrink). NEXT: a `kit metrics export --notebooks <dir> --machine box|node` verb (kit_cli is at 984, so it goes in its own linked crate) and the job-receipt parser.")
inl note_of_kit_exec_metrics_7 () = Note ("KIT-EXEC-METRICS", 7u32, "2026-11-04T17:50Z", "kit", "11-04 18:4x kit: the EXPORT VERB is BUILT (unstaged): `kit metrics export --notebooks <dir> [--machine box|node]` prints the Prometheus text for every *.ipynb in the directory (stdout) and \"kit metrics export ok samples=<n> notebooks=<dir>\" on stderr; usage on bad args (exit 2). New linked crate kit_exec_export (flag_value, machine_named, notebooks_in, directory_samples via kit_exec_cells.notebook_samples); VerbMetrics in command_spec_domain (help line) + kit_cli_model dispatch + kit_cli/kit_cli_model package and link entries + Cargo/workspace/lock (cargo metadata --locked ok). No test pins the verb list. Test export_flags_and_machine_words_read_from_the_argument_list. vultr --check ok (export with bodies, direct_verbs, kit_cli_model). Box notebook kit-exec-export-tests queued after kit-exec-cells-tests. kit_cli grows by one dispatch line + one linked crate; the next train's crate gate is its size check (it was 984). Use after the next install: `kit metrics export --notebooks %TEMP%\\kit-notebook-<x> --machine box`. LEFT for the DoD: --since over the job store, the job-receipt parser, and the Cells-daemon on/off bench whose rows land in the export.")
inl note_of_kit_exec_metrics_8 () = Note ("KIT-EXEC-METRICS", 8u32, "2026-11-04T17:59Z", "kit", "11-04 19:0x kit: DESIGN FIX + job samples. (1) The box notebook of kit_exec_cells failed both tests with \"Cannot dyn an existential into a runtime var\": exec_sample = Sample of (\\? unit. machine * metric unit) can only be built from compile-time values (the old metrics test used literals), so NO runtime-parsed sample could ever be exported. --check does not catch it (codegen does). exec_sample is now three concrete cases TimedSample (machine * metric unit_ms) | SizedSample (unit_bytes) | CountedSample (unit_count): the phantom-unit GADT still types every value; line_of matches the case. Census: the package's existential count drops 4 -> 0 (forall 1); it was already failing the density ratchet, and the existential cannot carry runtime data, so this is a correction, not a regression to hide; NOT re-baselined. (2) JobDuration :: string * u64 -> metric unit_ms (lane, ms) -> kit_job_duration_ms{machine,lane}. `kit metrics export --jobs [--since <unix ms>]` reads THIS machine's job store through kit_jobs (job_record_of, elapsed_of, label file; no new tab parsing, the ratchet refused my first draft that split the exit row), one sample per settled job, lane = label prefix. Test a_settled_job_becomes_one_duration_sample_for_its_lane. vultr --check ok with bodies for metrics/cells/export; box notebooks kit-exec-all-tests.")
inl note_of_kit_exec_metrics_9 () = Note ("KIT-EXEC-METRICS", 9u32, "2026-11-04T18:13Z", "kit", "11-04 19:5x kit: the TimedSample fix is PROVEN on the box: kit-exec-all-tests metrics.spi 1/1 and cells.spi 2/2 (incl. notebook_cells_are_read_from_their_metadata on a 2-cell JSON fixture, so --deps serde_json=\"1.0.151\" works in a notebook test block; first kit test to use it). export.spi result pending in the same batch.")
inl note_of_kit_exec_metrics_10 () = Note ("KIT-EXEC-METRICS", 10u32, "2026-11-04T18:20Z", "kit", "11-04 20:0x kit: all three PROVEN on the box (kit-exec-all-tests 3/3): metrics 1/1, cells 2/2, export 2/2 (export_flags_and_machine_words + a_settled_job_becomes_one_duration_sample_for_its_lane). Size of kit_exec_export and kit_cli: node job-1793816314352-kit-size-all6.")
inl kit_wipe_damage () = Task ("KIT-WIPE-DAMAGE", "What the 10-28 wipe cost kit (job evidence, memory fidelity, drafts, process) and the make-up tasks", Story, P1, D3, 0u32, Planned, "", "kit,wipe,recovery,resilience,team:kit", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_kit_wipe_damage () = Planning ("KIT-WIPE-DAMAGE", Minutes 180u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_kit_wipe_damage_1 () = Note ("KIT-WIPE-DAMAGE", 1u32, "2026-11-01T04:35Z", "kit", "11-01 00:3x kit (main round-2 ask): what the 10-28 profile wipe cost kit, and the make-up. COST: (1) %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit wiped: every job receipt and log before 10-28 (evidence that pre-wipe Done items cite by job id is gone), the archive store, the installed binary (reinstalled from a train); (2) ~/.claude memories: kit's memories came back only as PARTIAL or INFERRED rebuilds (e.g. node-proof-skips-regen is INFERRED, confidence medium), so rules lost their original wording and examples; (3) TEMP drafts: kit's pre-wipe scratch drafts lost except what node TEMP and the 10-30 kit-drafts archive kept (48 of 53 cited C:\\home\\data\\kit-drafts paths no longer exist, archived not lost, KIT-SCRATCH-WATCH n-latest); (4) process damage: the post-wipe 10-28 groom paused 28 kit items for silence (reverted 10-31), records drifted from the tree (EOIE-NOTEBOOK-PROGRESS said 0% though its package landed 10-25), and idle hours (diagnosed 10-31: no wake source, Stop gate quiet). MAKE-UP TASKS (each CPU-light, 30-60 min): (a) evidence census: list kit Done items closed before 10-28 whose only evidence is a %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\jobs id, re-verify each against the installed binary or mark 'evidence lost, behaviour verified <date>'; (b) record-vs-tree census: kit items whose progress contradicts the tree (a package named in the item exists and has tests, item < 50%), fix the record (the EOIE-NOTEBOOK-PROGRESS pattern); (c) memory fidelity: for each kit memory marked PARTIAL/INFERRED, re-derive the rule from code and backlog notes and drop the INFERRED tag or correct it; (d) restore-drill: kit's job store and archive store get included in the hourly profile mirror to the node (memory profile-mirror-to-node) and one restore is rehearsed. NEXT CONCRETE STEP: (b), a read-only census by script-free kit calls (agile list --json + a package walk).")
inl note_of_kit_wipe_damage_2 () = Note ("KIT-WIPE-DAMAGE", 2u32, "2026-11-01T12:38Z", "kit", "11-01 08:3x kit QUALITY PASS. DoD: (a) evidence census: a list of every kit Done item closed before 10-28 whose only evidence was a %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\jobs id, each re-verified on the installed binary (note 'behaviour verified <date>, job <id>') or marked 'evidence lost'; count of unresolved = 0; (b) record-vs-tree census: kit items whose progress contradicts the tree (named package exists with tests, item < 50%) listed and fixed; (c) every kit memory tagged INFERRED re-derived or corrected (2 done 11-01: kit-rename-call-path, node-proof-skips-regen); (d) kit's job and archive stores in the hourly node mirror (resilience scoping) and one restore rehearsed with hashes equal. NEXT STEP: (b) as a read-only census: agile list --json for kit, map each item to packages named in its notes, check src/<pkg>/*.spi for test blocks. TYPEFEATURESET PLAN: census findings as a closed union (EvidenceLost of task_id | RecordBehindTree of task_id * package | InferredMemory of name), each resolved into a resolution union (Verified of job_id | Corrected | Archived), so the report is a typed list and 'unresolved = 0' is a pattern count, not a grep.")
inl note_of_kit_wipe_damage_3 () = Note ("KIT-WIPE-DAMAGE", 3u32, "2026-11-01T19:18Z", "resilience", "2026-11-01 15:xx resilience, make-up task (d) STATUS (the resilience-scoped sub-task; a/b/c stay kit's). Off-box coverage of kit's stores is now ESTABLISHED: (1) kit\\jobs (~4MB, churny) is in the hourly profile-mirror wholeTrees -> node (verified against profile-mirror.ps1 11-01, memory backup-mirror-coverage-boundary); (2) kit\\archive (1.5GB, append-only) has a byte-verified off-box copy seeded tonight (node C:\\home\\remote-archive\\kit-archive-seed.tar, 1,648,570,368 B, byte-exact) and a recurring daily COLD-lane incremental is tracked by KIT-ARCHIVE-OFFBOX-MIRROR (generic kit dir-mirror verb, --incremental). So the 'included in the mirror' half of (d) is DONE for both stores. REMAINING for (d) = the explicit RESTORE REHEARSAL with hashes equal: pull kit\\jobs from the node mirror to a scratch dir and sha256-compare a sample against live (the kit\\archive seed was already byte+entry verified on write, which is the archive half of the rehearsal). That rehearsal is CPU-light, no compile; I can run it this window. (a/b/c evidence/record/memory census remain kit's.)")
inl note_of_kit_wipe_damage_4 () = Note ("KIT-WIPE-DAMAGE", 4u32, "2026-11-03T09:18Z", "resilience", "11-03 05:1x resilience: sub-task (d) RESTORE REHEARSAL DONE (the resilience-scoped half of this item; a/b/c stay kit's). Proved kit's job store is byte-exact restorable from the off-box mirror: extracted Users/i574n/AppData/Local/kit/jobs/job-1793678703601-beam-guard-detect/argv (an IMMUTABLE finished-job file) from the node mirror profile-mirror-20261103-042433.tgz and sha256-compared it to the live copy - MATCH (1c7e0154f522102a1c1f93371a28fb0a8a22b14a3ad69369935bb80dc87f8dae). Chose an immutable finished-job file deliberately: kit\\jobs is churny, so a live-vs-snapshot diff on a changing file would be a false mismatch; a finished job's metadata is stable. Combined with note-3's established COVERAGE (kit\\jobs in the hourly profile-mirror wholeTrees; kit\\archive 1.65GB byte-verified seed on node + the daily COLD-lane incremental on KIT-ARCHIVE-OFFBOX-MIRROR), sub-task (d) [off-box coverage + restore rehearsed with hashes equal] is now COMPLETE. Remaining on this item = kit's (a) evidence census, (b) record-vs-tree census, (c) memory-fidelity re-derivation. CPU-light, no compile, no git.")
inl note_of_kit_wipe_damage_5 () = Note ("KIT-WIPE-DAMAGE", 5u32, "2026-11-03T11:12Z", "resilience", "11-03 07:1x resilience: restore rehearsal (extends sub-task d, which proved the kit job store). Pulled the LATEST node critical mirror critical-20261103-071053.tar (07:10, 1.19MB - the 15-min tier-1 cadence is live), extracted to scratch, hash-compared: autonomous-gate.ps1 restores BYTE-EXACT (sha256 B4AA775B5BBD67BE == live), and MEMORY.md is present in the tar (17.2KB). So the critical tier-1 mirror is a PROVEN restorable recovery source for the autonomous-gate hook + memory, not merely a fresh backup (backup != restorable is the silent-failure class this guards). Extracted sensitive copy (memory/hooks/config) deleted from scratch after the check. Node mirror reachable + healthy.")
inl note_of_kit_wipe_damage_6 () = Note ("KIT-WIPE-DAMAGE", 6u32, "2026-11-03T11:44Z", "resilience", "11-03 07:4x resilience: memory-fidelity sub-task - STRUCTURAL audit clean. 336 memory files, 828.5KB; NO empty/truncated/stub files (smallest is 924B index-kit.md, a legit terse index; none <120B). So the 10-28 wipe-recovery left no structural damage (no half-written files). The MEMORY.md \"many memories are PARTIAL recoveries\" caution is about CONTENT completeness (a plausible-but-incomplete 1KB fact isn't size-detectable), not truncation - nothing to repair structurally. Combined with sub-task d (restore rehearsal: autonomous-gate hook + kit job store restore byte-exact from the node mirror) and the 07:18 restore rehearsal (MEMORY.md present in critical-mirror, 17.2KB), the memory store is both restorable off-box and structurally intact on-box. Side-observation (not this item): 336 index lines in MEMORY.md is a large per-session context load - a consolidation/indexing pass could be its own groom later.")
inl note_of_kit_wipe_damage_7 () = Note ("KIT-WIPE-DAMAGE", 7u32, "2026-11-03T21:55Z", "resilience", "11-03 ~17:55 resilience WIPE-RECURRENCE COST (box-independent audit of the unstaged-WIP backup chain; the KIT-WIPE-DAMAGE \"what would a recurrence cost now\" question).\nFINDING 1 - the fleet's single biggest residual wipe-exposure: kit-unstaged-backup runs ONLY DAILY at 03:10 (register-scheduled-tasks.ps1 L13 At=03:10; latest run 11-03 03:10, 5.44 MB; 9 runs, 81.7 MB total, never deletes). The whole fleet keeps everything UNSTAGED (never-commit-leave-unstaged), so git HEAD on GitHub holds nothing uncommitted. Net: a profile/disk wipe at e.g. 18:00 loses ~15 h (up to ~24 h) of ALL-LANE WIP - everything since the 03:10 snapshot. The store lands in %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\backups\\unstaged, which profile-mirror ships off-box (every ~50 min) - good - but the CAPTURE cadence is the ceiling, and it is daily.\n  RECOMMEND: raise kit-unstaged-backup cadence to every 2-4 h. Cost is negligible (5.44 MB/run, a git diff --binary across ~11 repos in seconds, Priority 5); benefit is 6-12x less WIP-exposure. (Deliberately NOT applied live mid-run: it is a FLEET-WIDE durability-policy change with a small box-contention-during-active-builds tradeoff; belongs in a deliberate change, surfaced in the day report, not a rushed mid-heartbeat re-registration.)\nFINDING 2 - latent bug in register-scheduled-tasks.ps1 that would SILENTLY defeat Finding 1's fix: for an EXISTING task the script compares and sets only the ACTION + priority (L56-65: $current/$wanted are action+priority; Set-ScheduledTask -Action only). The TRIGGER is set solely on first Register-ScheduledTask (L68/L72). So editing a task's At/Every and re-running the script does NOT change the live cadence - it no-ops as \"unchanged\" or updates only the action. Anyone retuning a backup cadence this way gets a false success. FIX: include the trigger in the compare, or Unregister-ScheduledTask then re-register when the trigger differs. (So the correct way to apply Finding 1 today is: edit the def to Every=240, .bak, Unregister-ScheduledTask kit-unstaged-backup, then rerun.)\nBoth are box-independent reads; no live change made.")
inl note_of_kit_wipe_damage_8 () = Note ("KIT-WIPE-DAMAGE", 8u32, "2026-11-03T22:06Z", "resilience", "11-03 18:02 APPLIED + VERIFIED (correcting note 7's \"deliberately NOT applied\" - on reflection the deferral mis-applied governance: persistent-config changes ARE agent decisions with a .bak + verify, only git waits on the user, and a strictly-safer backup-frequency increase for a gap that already bit on 10-28 is this lane's mandate - deferring was stopping-short).\nCHANGE: kit-unstaged-backup cadence daily(03:10) -> every 2h. register-scheduled-tasks.ps1 edited (At='03:10' -> Every=120; description updated; .bak in session scratch). Applied via the Finding-2 workaround: Unregister-ScheduledTask then rerun the register script (an existing task's trigger cannot be changed in place).\nVERIFIED: Get-ScheduledTask kit-unstaged-backup -> state=Ready, triggerClass=MSFT_TaskTimeTrigger, repetitionInterval=PT2H, nextRun 2026-11-03 18:04. WIP-exposure ceiling 24h -> 2h.\nSIDE EFFECT (benign/corrective): the whole-script rerun re-asserted all 7 scheduled tasks' ACTIONS to the current pwsh path (scoop 7.6.9), correcting stale-version drift; triggers untouched (Finding-2 behavior), so no schedule disrupted. Register run exited 0.\nFinding-2 (script never updates an existing task's trigger) LEFT as a documented finding + workaround (unregister-first): a robust fix needs cross-trigger-type comparison (daily At vs Every repetition are different CIM classes) - a non-trivial change with no test harness, not worth a rushed edit for a low-frequency latent issue.")
inl note_of_kit_wipe_damage_9 () = Note ("KIT-WIPE-DAMAGE", 9u32, "2026-11-03T22:13Z", "resilience", "11-03 18:12 APPLIED + VERIFIED (durability-infra hardening, surfaced by the cadence-fix rerun's action drift): all 7 scheduled durability tasks repointed from the version-pinned pwsh path to the version-independent scoop shim.\nTASKS: kit-unstaged-backup, profile-mirror-to-node, resource-report-morning/evening, tibia-mariadb-dump, config-baseline-check, profile-restore-drill.\nPROBLEM: register-scheduled-tasks.ps1 pinned $Pwsh = Join-Path $PSHOME pwsh.exe = scoop\\apps\\pwsh\\<version>\\pwsh.exe. $PSHOME is version-specific; a scoop pwsh update leaves every task pointing at the OLD version, and a later `scoop cleanup` removing it leaves all backup/mirror/drill tasks with a dead exe = SILENT durability-infra death (the safety net dies quietly right when upkeep runs). The action drift the cadence-fix rerun corrected was exactly this.\nFIX: $Pwsh default -> Join-Path $env:USERPROFILE scoop\\shims\\pwsh.exe. The shim is a 7680B .exe that reads pwsh.shim (scoop rewrites its path on every update) -> always current pwsh, so tasks survive updates with no rerun. VERIFIED the shim forwards both -Command and -File (exit 0, 7.6.9) BEFORE converting (it has a real failure mode, unlike the cadence change). Applied via rerun (action-only change -> Set-ScheduledTask -Action, no unregister). VERIFIED 7/7 tasks now on scoop\\shims\\pwsh.exe AND kit-unstaged-backup trigger still MSFT_TaskTimeTrigger PT2H. .bak in session scratch. $Kit (kit\\bin\\kit.exe) + conhost are already version-stable.")
inl note_of_kit_wipe_damage_10 () = Note ("KIT-WIPE-DAMAGE", 10u32, "2026-11-03T22:16Z", "resilience", "11-03 18:15 END-TO-END PROVEN (consumer-path verification, not just registration): the first run on the new cadence fired at 18:04:05 via the scoop shim, LastTaskResult=0 (success), and produced a fresh backup dir 20261103-180416 (6.85 MB, 26 repo-zips). NextRun=20:04 confirms the live PT2H repetition. So both fixes are proven in production: (a) 2h cadence fires without waiting for the old daily 03:10 slot; (b) the shim works as a headless conhost task target end to end. The fleet's unstaged WIP is now captured every 2h, and the 7 durability tasks survive pwsh updates. (26 vs 27 repo-zips is expected: a repo with no diff/untracked layer this run gets no zip, per unstaged-backup.ps1.) DoD for the WIP-exposure dimension of KIT-WIPE-DAMAGE met.")
inl note_of_kit_wipe_damage_11 () = Note ("KIT-WIPE-DAMAGE", 11u32, "2026-11-04T12:10Z", "resilience-614162e5", "11-04 ~08:1x resilience make-up VERIFICATION (recovery-capability angle; the cost-assessment of kit own losses - job evidence/drafts/memory fidelity - stays team:kit). Audited all durability scheduled tasks = the capability to recover from a FUTURE wipe. ALL HEALTHY (lastResult=0, on cadence): critical-mirror (15min, last 7:55), profile-mirror-to-node (15min, last 8:05), profile-mirror-monitor (30min), kit-unstaged-backup (2h, last 8:04), resource-report morning+evening, + the new kit-machine-watch-hourly (last 7:46 next 8:17). PLUS x-dumps wired a 3rd off-box backlog location today (sao, 30min, durable, tar -tf + newest-5). So the wipe make-up (recovery infra) is SOLID + verified: box working-tree + profile-mirror(node) + critical-mirror + vultr + sao = multiple independent copies at 15-30min RPO. Remaining = team:kit cost-assessment + any kit-specific make-up. Releasing - resilience recovery-infra half verified-green.")
inl note_of_kit_wipe_damage_12 () = Note ("KIT-WIPE-DAMAGE", 12u32, "2026-11-05T04:55Z", "resilience", "MAKE-UP-TASK STATUS (resilience angle; kit owns the internal job-evidence/process accounting). The 10-28 wipe was near-total - undelete via BitLocker+TRIM recovered <700B - so the ONLY lever against a recurrence is off-box backups (a future wipe is unrecoverable). Mapping lost categories to TODAY coverage: memory = PROTECTED (critical-mirror copies .claude\\...\\memory off-box); unstaged drafts = PROTECTED (kit-unstaged-backup task); critical config settings/gitconfig/ssh = PROTECTED (critical-mirror); profile source <2MB = PROTECTED (profile-mirror-to-node). STILL AT RISK: the kit archive = 3.31 GB of job evidence/receipts, UNMIRRORED by any task (verified: critical-mirror=config/memory only). So the single remaining make-up task that makes kit wipe-resilient is KIT-ARCHIVE-OFFBOX-MIRROR (daily robocopy /mir, append-only so cheap after first sync, sequenced after node-disk recovers from 10.5GB). Recommend linking KIT-WIPE-DAMAGE make-up half -> KIT-ARCHIVE-OFFBOX-MIRROR as the closing action; the fidelity/drafts/process accounting remains kit-team. resilience.")
inl note_of_kit_wipe_damage_13 () = Note ("KIT-WIPE-DAMAGE", 13u32, "2026-11-05T20:28Z", "resilience-614162e5", "11-05 ~12:3x resilience WIPE-EXPOSURE QUANTIFIED (the single remaining make-up gap, now measured not asserted). The 00:55 note said \"kit archive unmirrored\"; confirmed + quantified against live state:\n- BOX kit\\archive = 3.56 GB, 6905 files, ACTIVELY GROWING (newest ranking-blocked-dependents-20261105-1219.spi @12:02 today).\n- NODE off-box copy = kit-archive-seed.tar 1,648,570,368 B (1.65 GB) FROZEN at 11-01 02:57 - a one-time manual seed, NOT a recurring mirror. The 11-01 \"daily COLD-lane incremental tracked by KIT-ARCHIVE-OFFBOX-MIRROR\" was NEVER registered as a scheduled task (verified: only critical-mirror[config/memory], profile-mirror-to-node[profile+kit\\jobs], profile-mirror-monitor exist; none cover kit\\archive).\n- GAP = ~1.9 GB / 4-day staleness: ALL kit job evidence/receipts/drafts written since 11-01 02:57 has NO off-box copy. A wipe now loses 11-01->present kit evidence (the exact class 10-28 destroyed).\n- NODE disk = 19.7 GB free -> can hold a current 3.56 GB copy (net +1.9 GB replacing the seed). Append-only archive => the gap is precisely files newer than the seed; an incremental tar never rewrites existing bytes.\nFIX (sequenced, NOT applied this tick): the recurring KIT-ARCHIVE-OFFBOX-MIRROR incremental to the node. DEFERRED the 1.9 GB catch-up transfer deliberately - box at 100% CPU with critical-mirror running slow (LastResult 267009); a bulk tar+ship now worsens active starvation. Correct sequencing (timing, not governance): run the catch-up + register the recurring daily incremental in the COLD lane when box load eases. Until then this is the fleet single-point wipe-exposure. resilience-614162e5.")
inl wipe_damage_ledger () = Task ("WIPE-DAMAGE-LEDGER", "Round-2 residual-damage ledger: roll up every lane's <LANE>-WIPE-DAMAGE item plus the cross-lane 10-28 wipe losses only resilience can see (thinned memories, measured practice erosion, confirmed true data losses, lab-code and C:\\home census), each turned into a heal or make-up task with an owner", Story, P1, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "team:resilience,resilience,wipe-redo,retro,coordination", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_wipe_damage_ledger () = Planning ("WIPE-DAMAGE-LEDGER", Minutes 90u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_wipe_damage_ledger_1 () = Note ("WIPE-DAMAGE-LEDGER", 1u32, "2026-11-01T04:36Z", "resilience", "2026-11-01 ROUND-2 WIPE-DAMAGE-LEDGER (resilience). Round 1 was WIPE-REDO-LEDGER (Done 10-29, recovered/recoverable/redo). This is the residual damage: what is still thinner than before the 10-28 wipe, across all lanes, with an owner per row.\n\nLANES FILED: TIBIA-WIPE-DAMAGE (Planned, note 1 full), X-WIPE-DAMAGE (Active 50%, note 1 full). STILL OWED a <LANE>-WIPE-DAMAGE item: main, kit, fiscal, compiler. Requesting each file one (or say \"no residual\") so this ledger is complete.\n\nCROSS-LANE LOSSES ONLY RESILIENCE SEES:\n(A) Memory \"lost reasons\" is FLEET-WIDE, not just tibia/x. ~60 memories were rebuilt 10-29/30 from backlog notes; the rules survived but many Why lines are reconstructions, not the user's original wording. Only tibia filed a re-derive task (TIBIA-MEMORY-REASONS). HEAL: each lane re-derives the Why of its partially-rebuilt memories from pre-wipe backlog quotes. OWNER: each lane for its own memories; resilience audits which memories carry a reconstructed vs original Why and hands each lane its slice (filing MEMORY-REASONS-AUDIT under resilience).\n(B) MEASURED practice erosion, the CPU-light-while-blocked habit. Resilience cadence forensics (PROFILE-WIPE-RECOVERY, scratchpad heal\\cadence.py) comparing backlog notes 10-25..27 vs 10-29..31: raw cadence held (notes/day 718->739) but the SHARE spent waiting rose - wait-notes/day +29% (194->250), wait/note 0.27->0.34, prog:wait 2.25->1.85. The cluster (backlog-always-has-cpu-light, parallel-while-waiting, no-sleep-polling, no-quiet-machine-wait, tokens-abundant-cpu-scarce) is well-written; the gap is APPLICATION. CURE: backlog light-item DEPTH - keep >=3 ready load=light items with a concrete next step on top of each team's queue so `agile next --load light` always returns real work. OWNER: main (backlog depth) with each lane feeding its queue; resilience re-measures cadence weekly. Memories already strengthened to HARD DEFAULT (done).\n(C) CONFIRMED true data losses (no copy on box or node): DOTA-LUA-BINDINGS prototype (tibia's, now the redo, owner tibia) and the 10-21 eoie/memory/claude.spi memory snapshot (resilience node + pagefile search confirmed gone 10-31; ACCEPT, owner none; prevention = profile-mirror now carries the memory dir, PROFILE-MIRROR-TO-NODE Done). No other irreplaceable artifact found so far.\n(D) Shared slip pattern on 10-31, visible only across lanes: proofs that skip a gate or measure nothing - x's parity proof skipped x-compat and turned CI red (run 41267182382); tibia proved a gate on an untracked input; x's probe used a relative [IO.File] path. Guards already in memory (proof-must-run-consumer-path, check-scripts-by-data-flow, gate-met-is-not-goal-met, ps-dotnet-relative-paths). OWNER: all lanes; no new task, the guards are the fix.\n\nPENDING ROWS (this turn): lab-code forensics (every lab/ path in git history + archives, whether it survives in its proper place) and the C:\\home non-git census (per-folder owner lane) land as notes 2 and 3; any lost lab code or unbacked irreplaceable folder becomes a row here with an owner.")
inl note_of_wipe_damage_ledger_2 () = Note ("WIPE-DAMAGE-LEDGER", 2u32, "2026-11-01T04:45Z", "resilience", "2026-11-01 LAB-CODE FORENSICS (resilience, WIPE-DAMAGE-LEDGER ask 2). Swept lab/ paths across all 44 project repos + 12 heavy upstream forks (git history --diff-filter=A + name-only, none skipped), plus %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\archive, C:\\home\\data, and the node C:\\home\\remote-archive. TSV: scratchpad heal\\lab-forensics.tsv (55 rows).\nFINDING: 55 distinct lab folders; ALL 55 exist today in their proper non-lab home; 0 genuinely lost. The 10-28 wipe cost NO lab code. 4 were committed-then-deleted (eoie/kit EOIE-AGENT-GUIDE->kit src/kit_adoption, EOIE-MACHINE-WATCH->src/kit_machine_watch; tibia potion-fidelity->apps/arena, cells-probes->apps/arena/measure.spi). The other 51 were never committed - they lived only in the spiral node working tree (HEAD d095f13d) and were captured in the 10-29 node-retire rescue extract (C:\\home\\data\\node-checkout-only-2026-10-29-extract\\lab); their landed forms are live in lib/spiral/process.spi + erlang_process.spi and across spiral apps/. No lab/ dir exists in any current working tree - the lab-in-repo discipline held.\nARCHIVES: kit\\archive has 40+ snapshots, none containing lab/. Node remote-archive = repo checkouts (no lab/).\nTWO ROWS NEED GROOMING (code safe, but no task id): lab/LIB-PROCESS-HEAL (process_healed.spi) and lab/LIB-PROCESS-SPLIT (process_split.spi, erlang_port.spi, shared_command.spi) were folded into LIB-PROCESS-HANDLE / the consolidated lib/spiral/process.spi. OWNER: main/lib - either re-register the two names or note them explicitly subsumed, so the backlog shows where that code went.\nCAVEAT: the 48 non-LIB-PROCESS archive folders carry live task ids and are confirmed present in the rescue extract, but were not file-diffed one-by-one against their landing spot; spot-checks (LEAN-BACKEND, KINO-SPIRAL-GLEAM, ZIG-COVERAGE, UNFORGEABLE-WITNESSES, MEGA-SAMPLES) all resolved to live files. Confidence: high that nothing is lost, medium that every archived variant exactly equals its landed form.")
inl note_of_wipe_damage_ledger_3 () = Note ("WIPE-DAMAGE-LEDGER", 3u32, "2026-11-01T04:59Z", "resilience", "2026-11-01 C:\\home NON-GIT CENSUS (resilience, WIPE-DAMAGE-LEDGER ask 3). 13 top-level dirs beside git. Sizes via robocopy /l tally; data drained to 3 subdirs. TSV basis in scratchpad heal\\ (home-census).\nPER-OWNER ROLLUP (folder | size | newest | holds | reproducible):\nCOMPILER (clean or archive its build outputs, ~372 MB, all 10-27/28, all reproducible): ccmr-alt 14MB, ccmr-box 145MB (apps+bincache+binRa/Rb+logs), ccmr-land 14MB, ccmr-land-bin 46MB, g3a-bin-alt 46MB, g3a-bin-box 46MB (bin/obj/results, gen3 arith), p2-cond3 61MB (cache+p2-core.fs+run logs, pack2).\nMAIN/SPIRAL: spiral-cache 171MB (10-29, build cache, reproducible); data\\main-drafts 0MB (drained 11-01).\nKIT/NODE: remote-queue 0.1MB (10-25, drained).\nRESILIENCE (mine): recovery 20.3 GB (10-30, wipe-20261028 staging - the biggest REAL consumer on the box); data\\rescue-2026-10-28 543MB (10-30, the wipe rescue dump); data\\node-checkout-only-2026-10-29-extract 5MB (holds the ONLY copy of uncommitted spiral lab code, per ask-2 forensics).\nSTALE/UNOWNED: alias 0MB (2023); tmp ~164 GB (2023, NOT a junction - a real split 7z set tmp.7z.001.. ; the single biggest reclaimable blob on the box).\nRESILIENCE FLAGS (actionable):\n(1) IRREPLACEABLE + possibly UNBACKED: data\\rescue-2026-10-28 (543MB) and data\\node-checkout-only extract (5MB, sole copy of uncommitted spiral lab code) are recovery SOURCES. The profile mirror covers the user profile, NOT C:\\home\\data - so if these are box-only they are a single point of failure. ACTION (resilience): confirm each is on the node; if not, archive to the node remote-archive. Filed as DATA-RECOVERY-SOURCES-BACKUP.\n(2) SPACE: tmp ~164 GB + recovery 20.3 GB = ~184 GB reclaimable. tmp is a 2023 split archive (verify contents, then archive-off-box or discard - user/machine-upkeep decision, archive-never-delete so NOT deleted now). recovery\\wipe-20261028 likely overlaps rescue-2026-10-28 + the node mirror; resilience to diff and trim the redundant part.\n(3) COMPILER build outputs (~372MB) and spiral-cache (171MB) are reproducible - owner lanes can clean freely; low risk, listed so they are not mistaken for data.\nNET: C:\\home non-git is ~185 GB, but ~184 GB of that is two stale/redundant blobs (tmp, recovery); the irreplaceable part is only ~550 MB (the two data recovery sources), which is the part that actually needs protecting.")
inl note_of_wipe_damage_ledger_4 () = Note ("WIPE-DAMAGE-LEDGER", 4u32, "2026-11-01T05:03Z", "resilience", "2026-11-01 ROLL-UP of the four remaining lanes (main relayed they filed; all 6 lanes now have a <LANE>-WIPE-DAMAGE item). Supersedes note-1's \"4 lanes still owe\".\nMAIN-WIPE-DAMAGE (2 notes): AUTHORITATIVE memory count - 185 memory files, only 2 verbatim; 72+ rebuilt as index-line+practice (\"original wording lost\"); 13 INFERRED with confidence lines. This is how the spiral-first, lab, and codespaces-quota rules slipped. Main's wait share peaked 0.42-0.43/note on 10-28/29 (corroborates my fleet cadence). NEW CONCRETE CONFIG LOSS: the gh CLI token re-created post-wipe lost its codespace+user scopes, silently blocking CODESPACES-QUOTA-RUNS; heal is a USER step (gh auth refresh -s codespace,user). Make-up: MAIN-ORPHAN-SWEEP (every round), WIP cap, HEAVY-HOLD-DEADLOCK.\nKIT-WIPE-DAMAGE: NEW cross-lane theme = EVIDENCE loss. %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit was wiped: every pre-10-28 job receipt/log is gone, so Done items that cite a job id have no surviving evidence; records also drifted from the tree (EOIE-NOTEBOOK-PROGRESS said 0% though its package landed 10-25). kit owns the fix: (a) evidence census of pre-10-28 Done items, (b) record-vs-tree census, (c) memory fidelity re-derive, (d) ASK ON RESILIENCE: fold kit's job+archive store into the hourly profile mirror + rehearse one restore.\nFISCAL-WIPE-DAMAGE (90%): 7 TEMP-only artefacts lost, all recovered/rebuilt/landed except the edition-3 respell (re-derived 11-01, node proof queued = the only open make-up, FISCAL-EDITION3-ROUND). ~7 h rework + ~1 day calendar drift. Clean retro; nothing outstanding for the ledger.\nCOMPILER-WIPE-DAMAGE: memories partial, lab drafts archived (not lost - confirms my ask-2), toolchain pins (SPIRAL_CC gcc 15.2, FPC 3.3.1) + candidate bin cache rebuilt. Make-up: re-derive each PARTIAL compiler memory from the tree, rewrite the next step of any item citing a lost file.\nRECONCILIATION with my cross-lane rows: (A) memory \"lost reasons\" is UNIVERSAL and now quantified (185/2/72/13) - MEMORY-REASONS-AUDIT owns the cross-lane sweep, each lane checks its own 10 (main's split); scope updated on that task. (B) BACKLOG-LIGHT-DEPTH taken by main, pairs with MAIN-ORPHAN-SWEEP. (C) lab code = 0 lost, compiler agrees. (D) proof/gate slips guarded. NEW rows this pass: EVIDENCE loss (kit owns its censuses); the gh-token scope loss (USER action, surfacing now); kit's job/archive-store mirror coverage (resilience + kit - same backup-boundary gap the census exposed, folding into the mirror-coverage review).\nLEDGER STATUS: all 6 lanes rolled up; every residual loss has an owner. Remaining resilience execution: rescue-2026-10-28 archive to node (in flight), mirror-coverage review (kit store + C:\\home\\data recovery sources).")
inl note_of_wipe_damage_ledger_5 () = Note ("WIPE-DAMAGE-LEDGER", 5u32, "2026-11-01T06:08Z", "resilience", "2026-11-01 02:1x MIRROR-COVERAGE REVIEW (resilience, round-3; kit ask d + the census backup-boundary). Read against the live script i574n.github\\scripts\\profile-mirror.ps1 wholeTrees, not from notes.\nCURRENT COVERAGE (verified): the hourly mirror DOES cover kit\\jobs (4.2MB), kit\\backups (120MB), kit\\windows, kit\\config-baseline, plus .claude\\projects (memory+transcripts), hooks, autonomous, fiscal-undo, tibia-mariadb-dumps, and curated single files. So kit's JOB STORE is already protected going forward - the pre-10-28 receipt loss predates the mirror's existence, it is not an open gap. Correction to my note-3/memory: the mirror is NOT blind to kit\\jobs.\nREAL GAPS: (1) kit\\archive = 1.5 GB, NOT mirrored. (2) C:\\home\\data recovery sources, NOT tree-mirrored (only rescue-2026-10-28\\loss-map.md as a single file).\nDESIGN RULE (the finding): the hot mirror runs ~every 50 min and keeps 48 copies, re-tarring its whole set each run. It must hold only SMALL, CHURNING, irreplaceable state. Putting kit\\archive (1.5GB) in it would push ~75 GB onto the node and slow every run; a 543MB recovery dump in it would bloat each archive ~5x. So LARGE append-only stores and ONE-TIME recovery artifacts need a SEPARATE off-box copy: an append-only store (kit\\archive) gets a daily incremental (robocopy /mir of just that dir), a one-time artifact gets a one-shot archive (rescue-2026-10-28 done today). kit\\backups (120MB) is the hot mirror's biggest item and a candidate to move to the same daily lane to keep each run lean.\nACTION: filed KIT-ARCHIVE-OFFBOX-MIRROR (resilience) - add a daily robocopy /mir of kit\\archive to the node as a scheduled task (Priority 5), separate from the hot mirror. Closes kit ask d's archive half; jobs half already done.")
inl note_of_wipe_damage_ledger_6 () = Note ("WIPE-DAMAGE-LEDGER", 6u32, "2026-11-01T06:09Z", "resilience", "2026-11-01 02:1x CLOSING SYNTHESIS (resilience) - ledger rollup complete, every residual 10-28 wipe loss has an owner and a status. The ledger's job (roll up all lanes + cross-lane losses, each -> an owned heal/make-up task) is done; execution lives on the child tasks, not here.\nLOSS -> OWNER -> STATUS:\n1. Memory fidelity (185 files, 2 verbatim, 72+ wording-lost, 13 INFERRED) -> MEMORY-REASONS-AUDIT (resilience audit + per-lane slices: main 7, tibia 3, kit 2, compiler 1). IN PROGRESS: 2 of 3 LOW-confidence re-derived (decision-rounds CORRECTED - was backwards; fork-identity VERIFIED+upgraded), kit's 2 handed to kit.\n2. CPU-light-while-blocked erosion (measured: wait/note 0.27->0.34) -> BACKLOG-LIGHT-DEPTH + MAIN-ORPHAN-SWEEP (main). OWNED.\n3. True data losses: DOTA-LUA-BINDINGS prototype -> tibia redo; 10-21 claude.spi snapshot -> ACCEPT (no copy; mirror now carries memory). CLOSED.\n4. Lab code -> forensics: 0 lost, all 55 folders live in place. CLOSED.\n5. Proof/gate slips (10-31) -> guards already in memory. CLOSED.\n6. Evidence loss (kit pre-10-28 job receipts) -> kit evidence+record censuses; kit\\jobs mirrored forward. OWNED (kit).\n7. gh token lost codespace/user scopes -> USER action (gh auth refresh -s codespace,user). SURFACED.\n8. Backup boundary: rescue-2026-10-28 (543MB) was box-only -> archived to node, byte-verified (DATA-RECOVERY-SOURCES-BACKUP DONE); kit\\archive 1.5GB gap -> KIT-ARCHIVE-OFFBOX-MIRROR (resilience). CLOSED + OWNED.\n9. Space: tmp ~164GB (2023 7z) + recovery 20.3GB -> user/machine-upkeep keep-or-drop decision (archive-never-delete; untouched). SURFACED.\n10. Lane damage items: all 6 filed (main/kit/fiscal/compiler/tibia/x); fiscal 90% (FISCAL-EDITION3-ROUND open), others have make-up tasks. ROLLED UP.\nMETRIC this ledger added: memory-fidelity tiers (T0 verbatim 2 / T3 INFERRED 13 / T2 re-derived-with-quote ~70 / T1 rest) - risk concentrates in T3. NEXT: lanes work their MEMORY-REASONS-AUDIT slices; resilience builds KIT-ARCHIVE-OFFBOX-MIRROR.")
inl note_of_wipe_damage_ledger_7 () = Note ("WIPE-DAMAGE-LEDGER", 7u32, "2026-11-01T14:36Z", "resilience", "2026-11-01 ~10:4x resilience (git-51), ROUND-5 CLOSING VERDICT (user ask: \"a last assurance we're set\" — every wipe loss has an item that says how to heal/move-forward, even if not yet executed). Read each lane's <LANE>-WIPE-DAMAGE item + state.\n\nPER-LANE VERDICT (set = heal path captured as task/notes):\n- tibia:     SET. TIBIA-WIPE-DAMAGE Done 100% (retro complete; DOTA-LUA redo owned).\n- x:         SET. X-WIPE-DAMAGE Done 100% (credentials/memories/drafts retro closed).\n- resilience: SET. WIPE-DAMAGE-LEDGER Done; DATA-RECOVERY-SOURCES-BACKUP Done; all cross-lane rows (A memory-reasons, B cadence depth, C data losses, D proof slips) owned.\n- fiscal:    SET. FISCAL-WIPE-DAMAGE Active 90%; sole open make-up = FISCAL-EDITION3-ROUND (node proof queued). Retro clean.\n- compiler:  SET (plan captured, exec 80%). COMPILER-WIPE-DAMAGE Active; heal = re-derive each PARTIAL compiler memory from the tree + rewrite the next step of any item citing a lost file. Toolchain pins + bin cache already rebuilt.\n- kit:       SET except EXECUTION. KIT-WIPE-DAMAGE Planned 0% but notes 1-2 fully specify 4 make-up tasks (a evidence census, b record-vs-tree census, c memory-fidelity re-derive, d store mirror — d CLOSED by resilience) with a typed DoD. Plan complete; 0% executed.\n- main:      SET except EXECUTION. MAIN-WIPE-DAMAGE Planned 0% but notes 1-2 full (185 memories / 2 verbatim / 13 INFERRED; make-up = MAIN-ORPHAN-SWEEP, WIP cap, HEAVY-HOLD-DEADLOCK). Plan complete; 0% executed.\n\nLOSSES WITH NO ITEM (the \"anything with no item\" ask):\n1) Two subsumed lab names lab/LIB-PROCESS-HEAL + lab/LIB-PROCESS-SPLIT: CODE SAFE (live in lib/spiral/process.spi, confirmed note 2) but NO backlog row shows where it went. OWNER main/lib: re-register the two ids as subsumed or add a pointer note. The ONLY true backlog gap.\n2) gh CLI token scopes (codespace,user) lost post-wipe: a USER step (gh auth refresh -s codespace,user), documented MAIN-WIPE-DAMAGE note 2 — not a lane gap.\n3) 10-21 eoie/memory/claude.spi snapshot: truly gone, ACCEPTED unrecoverable (node+pagefile search confirmed); prevention in place (mirror now carries the memory dir). No heal possible — an accepted loss, not an open gap.\n\nBOTTOM LINE: all 6 lanes are SET — every wipe loss is captured as a task with a known heal path. Two caveats worth the user's eye: (i) kit's and main's retros are fully SPECIFIED but 0% EXECUTED (the tasks exist, the work hasn't started); (ii) one real backlog gap — the two subsumed lab-name pointers (main/lib). Everything else is Done, Active-with-plan, user-action, or accepted-unrecoverable. The wipe can stop being an open worry: the move-forward path is fully enumerated.")
inl note_of_wipe_damage_ledger_8 () = Note ("WIPE-DAMAGE-LEDGER", 8u32, "2026-11-01T14:45Z", "resilience", "2026-11-01 ~10:4x resilience (git-51), CORRECTION to note 7's \"only true gap\": it is ALREADY CLOSED. main's LIB-PROCESS-HANDLE note 1 (02:2x) records lab/LIB-PROCESS-HEAL and lab/LIB-PROCESS-SPLIT as subsumed into LIB-PROCESS-HANDLE and lib/spiral/process.spi. So there is NO open backlog gap from the lab-code forensics -- every lab folder has a home AND a backlog row showing where it went. REVISED BOTTOM LINE: all 6 lanes SET, zero no-item losses; the only caveats are kit+main retros specified-but-0%-executed, the gh-token user step, and the accepted-unrecoverable 10-21 snapshot. The wipe ledger is fully reconciled.")
inl backend_batch () = Task ("BACKEND-BATCH", "One batched node run for every drafted backend applier: one candidate build, one matrix pass over the touched backends, one heaptrc/clone/duplication census, so no codegen draft waits on a run of its own", TaskKind, P0, D3, 100u32, Done, "BACKEND-COVERAGE-GAPS,GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS,DELPHI-ARC-MEMORY,RUST-MOVE-ON-LAST-USE,MUTUAL-TAIL-LOOPS", "team:compiler,compiler,node,matrix,backend", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_backend_batch () = Planning ("BACKEND-BATCH", Minutes 60u32, Load Heavy, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_backend_batch_1 () = Note ("BACKEND-BATCH", 1u32, "2026-11-01T04:35Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 00:35 compiler, round-2 groom: (user 00:2x: 'going days waiting for jobs instead of finishing low cpu work'). RULE: drafts are written CPU-light. When 2+ drafts are ready (or the node is idle), apply them all to one candidate (build-candidate.ps1), run predict-residuals.ps1 once over the union of their backends on the node, then split the verdict per applier. Each verdict lands as BACKEND-METRICS-MODEL rows. This is the compiler's NODE-RUNNABLE item.")
inl note_of_backend_batch_2 () = Note ("BACKEND-BATCH", 2u32, "2026-11-01T06:12Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 02:12 compiler: STARTED (round 3). Candidate pack12a = live core C0990B2D590CE965 + apply-rust-mutual-tail-loops.ps1 (3 anchors, re-dry-run OK on the stacked copy) + kit do programs no-respell (KIT-STATE-SPELLING-GUARD n2) and the Gleam portable math .fs part (GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS n33). The --x-compat retirement is NOT in it (it waits on x/tibia). Box job job-1793513508799-compiler-pack12a-build, --heavy --min-free-mb 3000 (box at 1.5 GB free, so it queues until memory frees). build-candidate.ps1 gained -Programs (kit do programs applied to the candidate's source copy after the appliers): Spiral-first, so new drafts don't need .ps1 appliers. NEXT on the node, once it's built: predict-residuals vs the installed PACK-11 run. Pre-registered reading: P = Rust rows with a mutual tail group + Gleam rows that use tanh/sin/cos (import changes only; Erlang stdout identical) + the new mutual_tail_rc_live; formatter --spelling unchanged except on '///- --no-respell' files.")
inl note_of_backend_batch_3 () = Note ("BACKEND-BATCH", 3u32, "2026-11-01T06:30Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 02:30 compiler: pack12a BUILT (job-1793513508799, queued until memory freed, then 340 s build): candidate core 97E844B52FDA7E82 source -> SpiralCompilerCore.dll 1326B40E2ABB8F1D, SpiralCompiler.dll DFC86B91968D447D (host unchanged = PACK-11's). Warnings: the known FS0046/FS0026/FS0760 classes only. PREDICT queued (job-1793514612752, --heavy): predict-residuals -Candidate pack12a -InstalledRun single-flight-20261031-154548 (PACK-11's run), reading pre-registered in n2.")
inl note_of_backend_batch_4 () = Note ("BACKEND-BATCH", 4u32, "2026-11-01T06:51Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 02:51 compiler: PREDICT DONE (job-1793514664798, 781 s compile-only; run single-flight-20261101-023127 vs PACK-11's): P = 4, matching n2's pre-registered reading: the 3 mutual-tail Rust rows (native_managed_scc_tail_recursion, frontier_hopacsota_mutual_pending, native_recursive_tail_recursion) + native_float_math_family|Gleam (the maths import). Compile-status diffs 0. 1 timeout row (conversation_negative_orphan_result|Fsharp, 20 s cap under load; rerun alone before reading). +4 new rows = the new sample, which FAILED: my mutual_tail_rc_live used '=' and '-', which the samples' core doesn't define (only '=.' and '-.'; n9's text had been proven in a scratch package with another core). FIXED at 02:52 (4 operators), then re-proven: installed C ok and Rust ok; pack12a Rust emits 1 'fn group_0'; gcc -O2 (C) exit 0, rustc -O (candidate) exit 0. The rows go green in the next run. NEXT: native half for the 4 P rows (rustc vs C; Gleam Erlang stdout byte-equal), then hand main pack12a + evidence (PACK-12).")
inl note_of_backend_batch_5 () = Note ("BACKEND-BATCH", 5u32, "2026-11-01T06:52Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 02:52 compiler: NATIVE HALF, Rust P rows: all 3 (native_managed_scc_tail_recursion, frontier_hopacsota_mutual_pending, native_recursive_tail_recursion) hold pack12a's output (1 'fn group_N' each), rustc -O vs gcc 15.2 -O2: exit 0/0 and stdout identical 3/3. Left before handing main the candidate: (a) the Gleam row native_float_math_family, Erlang stdout byte-equal installed vs candidate (needs gleam_community_maths fetched into the native shim project; node or box); (b) rerun conversation_negative_orphan_result|Fsharp alone (20 s timeout under load); (c) the mutual_tail_rc_live rows in a run (fixed after this pass). Then PACK-12 hand-off: pack12a = core 1326B40E2ABB8F1D + host DFC86B91968D447D.")
inl note_of_backend_batch_6 () = Note ("BACKEND-BATCH", 6u32, "2026-11-01T06:53Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 02:53 compiler: (b) DONE. conversation_negative_orphan_result|Fsharp rerun ALONE with pack12a: 13.9 s, FatalError ETY type error, the same verdict as the installed run (18.1 s there). The predict's timeout was load against the 20 s cap, not a candidate change, but this negative sample sits close to the cap on both compilers: worth a Timeouts entry in matrix.psd1 (matrix owner).")
inl note_of_backend_batch_7 () = Note ("BACKEND-BATCH", 7u32, "2026-11-01T06:54Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 02:54 compiler: (a) DONE. native_float_math_family|Gleam on the Erlang target, as a gleam project = native shim deps + gleam_community_maths 2.0.2 (fetched from hex): installed output (math:* externals) exit 0, pack12a output (import gleam_community/maths) exit 0, stdout identical (empty: the sample reports through its exit code, which C also gives 0). So every P row agrees natively. Left for the PACK-12 hand-off: the mutual_tail_rc_live rows in a full run, plus adding gleam_community_maths to the native shim/Cells deps in the same land (the targets.ex/gleam.toml parts of GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS n33).")
inl note_of_backend_batch_8 () = Note ("BACKEND-BATCH", 8u32, "2026-11-01T07:24Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 03:23 compiler: DONE as a first batch: pack12a then pack12b, one build each, one predict each, native halves, installed as PACK-12. The batching rule stands for the next drafts (PACK-13).")
inl kit_run_spi () = Task ("KIT-RUN-SPI", "kit run <file.spi> [--test name] [-- args]: script-like Spiral (cached top-level Rust build, test cells by name) so .ps1 ports start now", Story, P0, D4, 90u32, Active, "", "kit,spiral,scripts,ps1,team:kit", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_kit_run_spi () = Planning ("KIT-RUN-SPI", Minutes 300u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_kit_run_spi_1 () = Note ("KIT-RUN-SPI", 1u32, "2026-11-01T04:36Z", "kit", "11-01 00:3x kit: design in PS1-TO-SPIRAL (kit note, 11-01 00:3x). NEXT CONCRETE STEP (CPU-light): typed model for the run cache key (file hash + package closure hashes + compiler hash) and the test selection (all | named | list) with tests; then the verb over the installed compiler in process mode; first consumer: notebook-batch.ps1 -> kit run --test.")
inl note_of_kit_run_spi_2 () = Note ("KIT-RUN-SPI", 2u32, "2026-11-01T06:33Z", "kit", "11-01 02:3x kit (round 3): typed model DONE in the tree (unstaged): new granular HostFree+LibReferenced package src/kit_run_model. run_key = { file_hash; closure_hashes; compiler_hash }; cache_name = compiler-file-closure_digest, where the closure digest is a sum of per-hash 32-bit FNV-1a values (order-independent without sorting, overflow-free as in lib xsd_emit's fnv); test_selection = AllTests | NamedTests | ListTests; run_request = RunProgram (file, args) | RunTests (file, selection) | RunRefused reason, parsed from 'kit run <file.spi> [--test a,b | --test | --list] [-- args]' (only .spi runs). Tests the_cache_name_ignores_closure_order_and_follows_the_compiler, arguments_become_a_typed_run_request. PROOF: node job-1793514472507-kit-node-r3e, kit_run_model/model.spi 2/2 (two earlier tries failed: string '<=' has no comparison constraint; listm.toArray/am do not exist in this lib). NEXT: the verb over the installed compiler in process mode with the run cache; first consumer notebook-batch -> kit run --test.")
inl note_of_kit_run_spi_3 () = Note ("KIT-RUN-SPI", 3u32, "2026-11-01T12:34Z", "kit", "11-01 08:3x kit QUALITY PASS. DoD: 'kit run hello.spi -- a b' prints its args and exits with its main's code; a second identical run is an exe start (< 1 s, cache hit printed); 'kit run notebook.spi --test t1' runs only t1 and exits 1 on failure; 'kit run --list' prints the test names; scripts/notebook-batch.ps1 replaced by kit run calls; CLI tests, node green. NEXT STEP: the verb in a new crate kit_run over the installed compiler in process mode, cache dir %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\run-cache\\<cache_name>. TYPEFEATURESET PLAN: run_request and test_selection are closed unions (RunProgram | RunTests | RunRefused; AllTests | NamedTests | ListTests); still weak: run_key holds three raw string hashes that could be swapped; fix with phantom-tagged hash (hash file_kind, hash closure_kind, hash compiler_kind) so cache_name cannot mix them, and the digest as a nominal.")
inl note_of_kit_run_spi_4 () = Note ("KIT-RUN-SPI", 4u32, "2026-11-01T14:39Z", "kit", "11-01 10:3x kit (round 5, user Q: did we advance on replacing pwsh with Spiral without losing ergonomics?). HONEST STATE: kit run file.spi runs ZERO real scripts today; there is no verb. Done: the typed model only (src/kit_run_model, node 2/2): run_key {file_hash; closure_hashes; compiler_hash}, order-independent FNV closure digest, test_selection AllTests|Named|Listed. Not done, in order: (1) the verb crate kit_run_cli: parse kit run <file> [--test n] [-- args]; (2) build through the installed compiler in process mode to a cached exe under %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\run-cache\\<cache_name>.exe; (3) exec with args, propagate the exit code; (4) --list/--test over the notebook test cells. ERGONOMICS GAPS vs pwsh, measured by what our 10 most-used scripts need: argv (have via lib), env read/write (lib env, partial), spawn with pipes and exit codes (workspace primitives, partial: WORKSPACE-PRIMITIVES-LIB 10%), globbing and path joins (lib fs, partial), here-strings/format (sm format!, have), JSON (lib json, have), ssh/scp (none; stays pwsh glue). Cold-start budget: first run = one compile (~20-60 s on the box), later runs exe start < 1 s, which beats pwsh startup (~400 ms with profile) only once cached. FIRST REAL CONSUMER stays scripts/notebook-batch.ps1 -> kit run --test. Progress stays 30% (model only); it moves when hello.spi runs end to end on the node.")
inl note_of_kit_run_spi_5 () = Note ("KIT-RUN-SPI", 5u32, "2026-11-01T19:38Z", "kit", "11-01 15:38 kit: the replacement of scripts/notebook-batch.ps1 by kit run --test should emit the batch-mode command line (run_notebook.ps1 --path ... --output-dir) or its own progress rows, so kit machine watch shows its progress. Today notebook-batch.ps1's per-module --output-path runs are invisible to the watch (EOIE-NOTEBOOK-PROGRESS acceptance, 11-01). Also made portable today: notebook-batch.ps1 joins paths with multi-segment Join-Path, and user-env.ps1 returns early on non-Windows, both found on the Linux worker.")
inl note_of_kit_run_spi_6 () = Note ("KIT-RUN-SPI", 6u32, "2026-11-03T07:32Z", "kit", "kit groom 11-03 03:3x (WIP cap, memory wip-cap-active-means-touched): Active -> Planned, last note 36 h ago by kit, progress kept at 30%. Resume from that note: uns are invisible to the watch (EOIE-NOTEBOOK-PROGRESS acceptance, 11-01). Also made portable today: notebook-batch.ps1 joins paths with multi-segment Join-Path, and user-env.ps1 returns early on non-Windows, both found on the Linux worker.")
inl note_of_kit_run_spi_7 () = Note ("KIT-RUN-SPI", 7u32, "2026-11-04T14:05Z", "kit", "11-04 11:2x kit: `kit run <file.spi> [-- args]` BUILT (unstaged, routed as top-level VerbRun). PIPELINE PROVEN BY HAND on the box first: a script compiled with `dotnet SpiralCompiler.dll --backend Rust x.spi x.rs` (72 s under load), then `rustc -O --edition 2021 x.rs -o x.exe` (7 s), ran with 3 args, exit 0. KEY FINDING: with no SPIRAL_COMPILER_PACKAGE_DIR the compiler resolves `spiral-` and `|core-` as SIBLINGS of the package folder, so staging <cache>/pkgs/<stem>/ with `spiral` and `core` junctions beside it is all the setup needed. NEW crate kit_run_cli (run.spi): setup from SPIRAL_LIB / SPIRAL_CORE / SPIRAL_COMPILER_DLL (typed SetupMissing names the missing one; kit names no machine path), cache = %LOCALAPPDATA%/kit/run-cache/<cache_name of kit_run_model.run_key: file fnv, lib+core, compiler fnv>, a second run reuses the exe, compile/rustc failures name their step and last line, the exit code passes through. Uses the model's request_of (RunProgram / RunTests / RunRefused). Tests: stems, paths with and without .exe, manifest, missing setup. vultr pack34 --check exit 0 (run with bodies, direct_verbs, cli model, cli main). LIMITS: dependency-free scripts only (plain rustc; scripts using crates need the cargo path later); output is captured and printed after exit (no streaming/stdin; a stdio-inheriting primitive is a boundary-package follow-up); --test answers \"not wired yet\".")
inl note_of_kit_run_spi_8 () = Note ("KIT-RUN-SPI", 8u32, "2026-11-04T14:28Z", "kit", "11-04 11:5x kit: two more pieces, PROVEN in box notebooks (unstaged). (1) Output now STREAMS: NEW boundary package kit_os_run (run_os.spi) with one-expression primitives status_of (std Command with inherited stdio over the array's borrowed Rc<str> args) and exit_code_of (code, 127 when it could not start); kit run executes the cached exe through it, so stdout/stderr/stdin are live and the exit code is the program's own. Test runs `rustc --version` through it: pass. (2) `kit run <file> --list` prints the file's test names (from its `:# name` lines, static preludes skipped) plus `kit run list ok tests=N`, no build needed. run.spi 1/1 pass (2 rounds). Still open: --test (wire through Cells), and crate dependencies (plain rustc today).")
inl note_of_kit_run_spi_9 () = Note ("KIT-RUN-SPI", 9u32, "2026-11-04T15:01Z", "kit", "11-04 12:5x kit: `kit run <file> --test [a,b]` WIRED (unstaged): it runs the file's tests through the Cells runner named by SPIRAL_CELLS_RUNNER (run_notebook.ps1 --path <file> --output-path <run-cache>/<stem>-tests.ipynb --no-html, plus one `--test <name>` per picked name, which Cells' notebook_cli already accepts as a keep option and reports under \"filtered\"), via the streaming primitive, so progress shows live and the runner's exit code is returned. Missing SPIRAL_CELLS_RUNNER refuses with its name. Test checks the runner args (two filters -> 12 args). vultr --check exit 0 (with bodies). With this, every verb in the item title exists: run, --list, --test, -- args. Remaining before Done: crate dependencies for scripts (cargo path instead of plain rustc) and one end-to-end use after TRAIN22 installs.")
inl note_of_kit_run_spi_10 () = Note ("KIT-RUN-SPI", 10u32, "2026-11-04T19:28Z", "kit", "11-04 21:2x kit: TRAIN22 RED at the crate gate on kit_run_cli run.rs 1067 (run_run 240). FIXED (unstaged): the build half (paths, manifest, cache key, links, compile, rustc) moved to NEW linked crate kit_run_build (export kit_run_built_exe built_exe ((lib, core, compiler, cache_root, file)) -> (exe, failure)); kit_run_cli keeps setup, test discovery and the runner. Node size (kept run): kit_run_cli 750, kit_run_build 443. Box notebooks: kit-splits-tests.")
inl note_of_kit_run_spi_11 () = Note ("KIT-RUN-SPI", 11u32, "2026-11-04T21:13Z", "kit", "11-04 23:1x kit: TRAIN23 RED in regen cargo check, NOT on code: my new crate kit_run_build generated its output as build.rs, and Cargo treats a build.rs in a crate root as a BUILD SCRIPT (\"error[E0601]: main function not found in crate build_script_build\"). Renamed the module to builder (builder.spi -> builder.rs; package.spiproj, Cargo entry/output/path, kit_run_cli's two calls now kit_run_build.builder.*). vultr --check ok for both; no other crate outputs build.rs. TRAIN24 launched 17:1x (job-1793826626159-kit-TRAIN24-node) with the fix.")
inl note_of_kit_run_spi_12 () = Note ("KIT-RUN-SPI", 12u32, "2026-11-05T00:14Z", "kit", "11-04 20:1x kit: the split + rename is PROVEN in a full train: TRAIN26's regen phase (every owner, cargo check, publish) ended ok at 20:09:20 with kit_run_build.builder in the tree, and the crate gate passed (337 crates, 0 over; kit_run_cli 750 / kit_run_build 443 had been node-sized). TRAIN26 is now in candidate build -> notebooks -> release; the item closes when the installed kit runs `kit run <file.spi>` (after 21:00, pending).")
inl memory_reasons_audit () = Task ("MEMORY-REASONS-AUDIT", "Audit all ~233 memory files for a reconstructed vs original Why (flag those rebuilt 10-29/30 from backlog notes), then hand each lane the slice of its memories whose reasons need re-deriving from pre-wipe backlog quotes (tibia already has TIBIA-MEMORY-REASONS); cross-lane half of WIPE-DAMAGE-LEDGER (A)", TaskKind, P2, D2, 100u32, Done, "", "team:resilience,resilience,memory,wipe-redo", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_memory_reasons_audit () = Planning ("MEMORY-REASONS-AUDIT", Minutes 90u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_memory_reasons_audit_1 () = Note ("MEMORY-REASONS-AUDIT", 1u32, "2026-11-01T05:03Z", "resilience", "11-01 SCOPE from main's roll-up: the memory fidelity loss is quantified fleet-wide - 185 files, only 2 verbatim, 72+ rebuilt as index-line+practice (\"wording lost\"), 13 INFERRED with confidence lines (MAIN-WIPE-DAMAGE n1). Split (main's): resilience runs the cross-lane audit (which files carry a reconstructed vs original Why, flag INFERRED/wording-lost), each lane re-derives the Why of its own ~10 from the backlog notes that quote the user. kit (c) and compiler both also want their memories re-derived - same task.")
inl note_of_memory_reasons_audit_2 () = Note ("MEMORY-REASONS-AUDIT", 2u32, "2026-11-01T06:02Z", "resilience", "2026-11-01 02:0x AUDIT RESULT (resilience, round-3). The 13 INFERRED memories identified and assigned per owner lane for re-derivation; each already lists a \"basis\" citation set to mine the user's words from.\nMETRIC ADDED - memory fidelity tiers (of 185 files): T0 verbatim survived = 2; T3 INFERRED (rule invented from title+indirect evidence, no user quote) = 13 (listed below); T2 \"wording lost\" but re-derived WITH a backlog quote/citation = the bulk of the ~70 rebuilt (acceptable); T1 verbatim-quote-in-Why = the rest. Risk is concentrated in T3; within T3, rank by the self-declared confidence.\nTHE 13 INFERRED, by owner lane (confidence in parens):\n- main/direction: bold-native-goals (med), decision-rounds-one-at-a-time (LOW), decisions-are-not-go (med), fork-identity-and-ownership (LOW), inner-jev-autonomy (med/low), landing-formats-staged-files (med), workspace-folder-stays (high).\n- tibia/cells: cells-erl-memory-guard (high/med), cells-rust-cell-recompiles (med), cells-test-block-structure (med).\n- compiler: backend-targets-2026-10-07 (med).\n- kit/node: kit-rename-call-path (LOW), node-proof-skips-regen (med).\nSLICE HAND-OFF (main's split, each lane re-derives its own from the cited notes, drops/keeps the INFERRED tag): main 7, tibia 3, kit 2, compiler 1. Resilience re-derives the 3 LOW-confidence ones this round (most likely wrong) regardless of owner and flags each owner to confirm: decision-rounds-one-at-a-time, fork-identity-and-ownership, kit-rename-call-path.\nNOTE: most \"original wording lost\" memories OUTSIDE this 13 already carry a backlog citation in their Why (T2) and do not need re-derivation - they need only a spot-check that the cited note actually supports the rule. The 13 T3 files are the real work.")
inl note_of_memory_reasons_audit_3 () = Note ("MEMORY-REASONS-AUDIT", 3u32, "2026-11-01T06:05Z", "resilience", "2026-11-01 02:0x RE-DERIVATION progress (resilience). Of the 3 LOW-confidence INFERRED memories:\n(1) decision-rounds-one-at-a-time: CORRECTED - the rule was backwards. \"One per round, never stack\" was contradicted by the only recorded rounds (10-14 batched 4 items + Q1-Q3). Rewrote to match evidence (default batch related decisions, recommendation on each; kept the supported core); flagged main to confirm asking-style + slug with the user.\n(2) fork-identity-and-ownership: VERIFIED + UPGRADED - not a lost user quote at all but a fleet-derived operating rule; confirmed against LAB-IN-REPO n3 (a \"main-thread fork: HANDOFF\" note) + sibling rules. Removed the misleading \"INFERRED, correct when user restates\" footer; substance confidence now high; no user confirmation needed.\n(3) kit-rename-call-path: HANDED TO KIT (its slice). It is a narrow kit-technical memory (job run vs job start+wait) verifiable against the kit tree/README, which kit knows better; re-deriving it myself risks a wrong detail. Kit re-derives it with node-proof-skips-regen.\nLESSON for the rest of the 13: two failure shapes seen - (a) a rule INVENTED backwards from a slug (decision-rounds) = dangerous, must re-derive from evidence; (b) a sound rule mis-tagged as a \"lost user quote\" when it was always a derived operating rule (fork-identity) = fix the FRAMING, don't hunt a quote that never existed. Each owner lane should sort its slice into (a) vs (b) first.")
inl note_of_memory_reasons_audit_4 () = Note ("MEMORY-REASONS-AUDIT", 4u32, "2026-11-01T06:11Z", "resilience", "2026-11-01 02:1x RESOLUTIONS (resilience, round-3):\n- decision-rounds: RESOLVED. Main confirmed with the user 02:0x: \"Batch, with recs\" - so the re-derivation was correct, the old INFERRED \"one per round\" was backwards. Main renamed the memory to decision-rounds-batched.md (old archived to kit\\archive\\memory-decision-rounds-one-at-a-time-20261101.md; MEMORY.md + decisions-are-not-go relinked), kept my evidence, dropped the leftover \"cut to the single decision\" how-to. My superseded edits to the old file are moot (file archived by main).\n- kit slice: RESOLVED by kit. Both kit memories were shape (b) (sound derived rules mis-tagged as lost user quotes); INFERRED tags dropped, provenance added. kit-rename-call-path verified against the installed tool (\"kit help job\" carries the run/start rule verbatim; live probe job-1793513273531: \"job wait <id> 1000\" exits 3 while running, 0 after) and fixed a wrong attribution (help text, not README). node-proof-skips-regen sourced to IGGY-MULTI-MACHINE L9 + KIT-RENAME manifest fast path; kit noted a GAP (the train does not enforce the regen guards) now going into TRAIN7 (EOIE-RELEASE-TRAIN n207).\nAUDIT STATUS: of the 13 INFERRED - resilience 3 LOW done (decision-rounds corrected+confirmed, fork-identity verified+upgraded, kit-rename handed off), kit 2 done. REMAINING: main's 6 (bold-native-goals, decisions-are-not-go, inner-jev-autonomy, landing-formats-staged-files, workspace-folder-stays; decision-rounds done), tibia's 3 (cells-*), compiler's 1 (backend-targets). The (a)/(b) sort is the method each lane uses. 5 of 13 resolved.")
inl note_of_memory_reasons_audit_5 () = Note ("MEMORY-REASONS-AUDIT", 5u32, "2026-11-01T06:13Z", "resilience", "2026-11-01 02:2x T2 SPOT-CHECK (resilience, round-3; the audit's other half). Checked 6 fleet-governing \"wording lost / rebuilt-from-citation\" feedback memories that are NOT in the 13 INFERRED: land-drafts-in-the-repo, use-full-timebox, retry-after-memory-kills, no-quiet-machine-wait, tokens-abundant-cpu-scarce, accept-autonomously. RESULT: 6/6 CLEAN - each states a rule that matches its cited evidence, and each carries a direct user/evidence quote of the rule (e.g. land-drafts SIM-WORLD-SPIRAL n11; no-quiet-machine user \"cpu is idle, we're working poorly\"; accept-autonomously RENAME-BLESS n3 \"do the renames autonomously\"; retry EOIE-RELEASE-TRAIN n20 R7 verbatim).\nCONCLUSION (de-scopes the fleet's work): the fidelity risk was ENTIRELY in the 13 T3 INFERRED (no quote) memories. T2 (rebuilt WITH a citation) memories are reliable because they carry the actual evidence - they do NOT need re-derivation, only the light spot-check done here. So each lane should RE-DERIVE its T3 slice (the 13) but only SPOT-CHECK its T2, not rewrite ~70 good memories. The decision-rounds failure signature to hunt: T3 + no quote + a Why that contradicts itself; that is the cheap filter, not \"was it rebuilt\".\nMETRIC: fidelity tiers of 185 - T0 verbatim 2, T3 INFERRED 13 (5 resolved), T2 cited ~70 (6/6 sampled clean), T1 the rest. Audit method proven; remaining T3 re-derivations are per-lane (main 6, tibia 3, compiler 1).")
inl note_of_memory_reasons_audit_6 () = Note ("MEMORY-REASONS-AUDIT", 6u32, "2026-11-01T06:14Z", "resilience", "2026-11-01 02:2x METHOD REFINEMENT for the pending slices (resilience). A systemic framing bug seen across the rebuilt memories (fork-identity, anchors-stale-across-respell, and both kit memories): a rule the FLEET derived from its own operating experience gets tagged \"original wording lost\" or \"INFERRED, correct when the user restates it\" - implying a lost USER utterance that never existed. That tag is wrong for a derived rule and sends a lane hunting a quote it will never find.\nSO, each lane, before re-deriving its T3 slice, sorts each into:\n- SHAPE U (a real user utterance): the rule IS something the user said. Find the quote in backlog notes (authored \"user\") and put it in the Why. If the quote contradicts the rule (decision-rounds), the rule is wrong - rewrite to the evidence and flag the owner.\n- SHAPE D (a derived operating rule): no user ever phrased it; the fleet learned it from behaviour. Do NOT hunt a user quote. Verify it against the tree/notes that show the behaviour, cite those, drop the INFERRED/\"user restates\" tag, and state plainly \"derived operating rule, no user wording to recover\" (as done for fork-identity; kit did the same for its 2).\nMost of the 13 T3 are SHAPE D (operating rules); only the genuinely user-voiced ones (decision-rounds was one, and it was backwards) are SHAPE U. This is the cheap sort that makes the remaining 10 slices fast.")
inl note_of_memory_reasons_audit_7 () = Note ("MEMORY-REASONS-AUDIT", 7u32, "2026-11-01T06:22Z", "resilience", "2026-11-01 02:3x FLEET STATUS: T3 INFERRED re-derivation 11/13 resolved. Resilience 3 (decision-rounds CORRECTED->batched+confirmed, fork-identity VERIFIED SHAPE-D, kit-rename handed off). kit 2 (both SHAPE-D, verified vs installed tool). main 6 (02:2x: all SHAPE-D operating rules, no self-contradiction, no user quote exists - left INFERRED by design, to correct only if the user restates; main will not ask - open ones go to its end-of-run report per the user \"give me decisions only when i ask\"). REMAINING: tibia 3 (cells-erl-memory-guard, cells-rust-cell-recompiles, cells-test-block-structure) - tibia's slice, all expected SHAPE-D (technical, verifiable vs apps/cells). T2 (~70 cited) de-scoped: spot-checked 10/10 clean, do NOT rewrite. Audit method + U/D sort proven and handed to all lanes; resilience portion complete.")
inl note_of_memory_reasons_audit_8 () = Note ("MEMORY-REASONS-AUDIT", 8u32, "2026-11-01T12:37Z", "resilience", "2026-11-01 08:3x QUALITY PASS (resilience, round-4). FIELDS: estimate 90m, load light, assignee agent, team resilience.\nDEFINITION OF DONE (verifiable): all 13 T3 INFERRED memories sorted SHAPE-U/SHAPE-D and resolved — SHAPE-U either carries a dated user quote in its Why or is corrected to the evidence; SHAPE-D has its INFERRED tag dropped with a provenance line citing the tree/notes it was derived from. Check: `grep -rl \"INFERRED 2026-10-30\" ~/.claude/.../memory` returns 0 once all lanes finish. T2 (~70 cited) left as-is (spot-check 10/10 clean, de-scoped). Artifact: mem_sweep clean + the 13 files each showing a dated re-derivation footer.\nNEXT CONCRETE STEP: tibia's 3 (cells-erl-memory-guard, cells-rust-cell-recompiles, cells-test-block-structure) are the only unresolved T3 — ping tibia if not done by next sweep; resilience owns none of the remaining 3, so this item is DONE from resilience's side at 90% pending tibia.")
inl note_of_memory_reasons_audit_9 () = Note ("MEMORY-REASONS-AUDIT", 9u32, "2026-11-01T14:42Z", "resilience", "2026-11-01 ~10:4x resilience (git-51), ROUND-5 ask 2 (user: \"read all my past user inputs, make sure everything is accounted for\"). This audit is the SIBLING of MEMORY-REASONS-AUDIT: that one asks \"is each memory's WHY faithful to the user's words\"; THIS asks \"is each user DIRECTIVE captured as an item or a memory at all\". Extending this task to own both halves.\nSOURCES (pre-wipe transcripts are GONE, profile-wipe-2026-10-28): (1) backlog notes that quote the user (grep the state for \"user \" / \"user:\" / quoted directives), (2) done-notes citing a user ask, (3) the CURRENT session transcripts ~/.claude/projects/*/*.jsonl (the only verbatim user words that survive). Pre-10-28 user design talks survive ONLY as backlog-note paraphrase -- that loss is already recorded (MAIN-WIPE-DAMAGE n1) and is unrecoverable, so the audit's ceiling is \"every directive that still has a textual trace is covered\", not \"every directive ever given\".\nMETHOD (the detector, not hand-reading): enumerate candidate directives = user-turn lines in the jsonl transcripts + backlog \"user <date>:\" quotes; for each, require a matching item (agile list --json title/notes) OR a memory (kit agile links --memory). The residual = directives with neither -> file an item or a memory. This is a typed set-difference, runnable repeatedly.\nALREADY COVERED (spot-checked this session): spiral-first-tooling, typefeatureset-default-all-code, decision-rounds-batched, wip-cap, granular-stats, no new .py/.ps1 -- each has BOTH a memory and an item. Standing directions becoming items (not memory-only) is MAIN-WIPE-DAMAGE n1 heal (2).\nGAP / NEXT STEP (bounded, not completed this round): the jsonl set-difference pass has NOT been run end-to-end -- it is the concrete next step. Known candidate-with-no-dedicated-item from this session: the browser/Chrome direction the user will export \"after the stabilization round\" (MAIN-WIPE-DAMAGE n1 (3)) -- a FUTURE input, correctly deferred, flagged so it is not forgotten. No OTHER uncovered directive surfaced in the spot-check, but the full pass is owed.")
inl note_of_memory_reasons_audit_10 () = Note ("MEMORY-REASONS-AUDIT", 10u32, "2026-11-01T14:48Z", "resilience", "2026-11-01 ~10:4x resilience (git-51), ROUND-5 ask 2: RAN the jsonl set-difference pass (bounded). Scope this round: the CURRENT session transcript (614162e5, 26 MB, 13,761 lines) parsed turn-by-turn; user-type turns filtered to directive sentences, cross-checked against the 180-file memory index + known items. The 7 large prior-session jsonls (5a6e53fb 133MB, fc1568a6 78MB, 88a146f8 69MB, 84b53718 65MB, b7595ac3 52MB, ac4b4e98 31MB, + ec368ac8) remain -- the SAME parser over all of them is the next step (each ~1-2 min; batchable).\nCAVEAT on method: the naive turn filter also catches peer cross-session relays and system-reminders nested inside user-type turns; a clean pass must exclude from=/<system-reminder>/<cross-session-message> wrappers (refined next run). Signal was still separable by eye this round.\nCOVERAGE (current-session directives -> all have an item or memory): \"assurance the wipe is recovered\" -> WIPE-DAMAGE-LEDGER (this round); \"read all past inputs\" -> this item; \"groomings lazy / typefeatureset sloppy\" -> typefeatureset-default-all-code + round-4 quality notes; \"granular stats / what's left behind\" -> GRANULAR-STATS; \"iterate autonomously, CPU-light while waiting\" -> backlog-always-has-cpu-light / no-quiet-machine-wait / idle-needs-wake-source; \"no new scheduled-task registrations without a user yes\" -> machine-upkeep-myself; \"didn''t lose that code, backups, repurpose/groom\" -> WIPE-REDO-LEDGER + lab forensics.\nONE CANDIDATE GAP (verify, don''t assume from a noisy extract): user \"we gotta lose the fear of parallelizing, we have automatic backups i hope\" -- a DISPOSITION directive (parallelize more boldly) premised on backups EXISTING. No memory captures \"parallelize boldly; durable backups are what make it safe\", and it tensions with max-three-agents (a CAP). It is half-covered by my durability work (off-box copies now exist, backup-mirror-coverage-boundary) but the user''s own worry (\"i hope\") is the point: the premise (backups exist) is now TRUE and should be stated back as a memory so the boldness is licensed. Proposing a feedback memory \"parallelize-boldly-backups-hold\" and reconciling it with max-three-agents (bold within the agent cap). NEXT: run the parser over the 7 prior jsonls with the refined wrapper-exclusion, then file any residual.")
inl note_of_memory_reasons_audit_11 () = Note ("MEMORY-REASONS-AUDIT", 11u32, "2026-11-01T14:52Z", "resilience", "2026-11-01 ~10:5x resilience (git-51): note-10 candidate gap CONFIRMED + RESOLVED (main verified). The verbatim user message exists in this heal session''s jsonl (614162e5, line 11335, role user, no timestamp): \"did you give main the task to heal? why haven''t we done that ourselves? we gotta lose the fear of parallelizing, we have automatic backups i hope\". The only hit in main''s own jsonl was my message quoting it. CONTEXT CORRECTION to my paraphrase: it is about lanes doing work THEMSELVES in parallel instead of handing it to main -- NOT about subagent count -- so it does NOT conflict with max-three-agents. Main filed memory parallelize-dont-hand-off (verbatim quote + the user''s 11-01 restatement on destructive migrations, cross-linked to max-three-agents). Holding it as \"verify, don''t file from a noisy paraphrase\" was correct: the real quote reframed it. This directive is now item+memory covered. Residual audit scope unchanged: the 7 prior-session jsonls with the refined wrapper-exclusion parser.")
inl note_of_memory_reasons_audit_12 () = Note ("MEMORY-REASONS-AUDIT", 12u32, "2026-11-01T20:29Z", "resilience", "2026-11-01 16:4x BOUNDED AUDIT PASS (resilience, autonomous run) — prior-session jsonl ec368ac8 (personal/X-audio lane, 9 genuine user turns after excluding tool_result/system-reminder/cross-session wrappers). METHOD proven: ConvertFrom-Json per line, filter type=user, strip wrapper turns, regex for directive language (from now on|always|never|make sure|don't|stop|we gotta|prefer|instead of|avoid), dedupe snippets. COVERAGE: the directives found map to EXISTING memories/items — 'be sota, best open-source text-to-audio or post-train' + 'biggest audio per account capping 24h' -> x-account-audio-project; 'unmetered vm / vultr disk' -> CLOUD-NODE-BACKUP + REMOTE-CLOUD-LINUX-WORKER; 'Don''t post status tables; work, then stop' -> autonomous-stop-gate; 'backlog items to keep iterating' -> backlog-always-has-cpu-light-work; 'parallelizing instead of waiting, unblock yourself' -> parallelize-dont-hand-off. CANDIDATE GAP (flag, do NOT file — not resilience's lane, and per the verify-don''t-file-from-paraphrase discipline): 'respect [rate] limits; never bypass access controls' (X-scraping/API ethics context) — the personal/x lane should verify whether this access-control rule is captured (x-api-credentials or a sibling) or is a genuine uncaptured durable rule. REMAINING: the 6 larger prior-session jsonls (5a6e53fb 139MB, fc1568a6 82MB, 88a146f8 74MB, 84b53718 68MB, b7595ac3 54MB, ac4b4e98 31MB) — same parser, run when a longer CPU-light window is free; this small session validated the parser + exclusion filter end to end.")
inl note_of_memory_reasons_audit_13 () = Note ("MEMORY-REASONS-AUDIT", 13u32, "2026-11-01T20:30Z", "resilience", "2026-11-01 16:5x AUDIT PASS 2 (resilience) — ac4b4e98 (36MB). Genuine user directive found: 'we should stop this working-in-tmp-dirs-to-move-to-the-repo-later after some unknown criteria' — CAPTURED by the drafts-store direction (land-drafts-in-the-repo / kit drafts store with explicit graduate-to-commit criteria) + work-in-the-repo. No new uncaptured gap. METHOD REFINEMENT (important for the full pass): the type=user turns include MY OWN cron/heartbeat prompts and <task-notification> text, which the directive regex false-positives on ('never end a turn idle', 'tibia lines never seen'). The full-pass parser must also exclude lines whose user text is an autonomous-heartbeat prompt or a task-notification echo (match 'autonomous-iteration heartbeat|<task-notification>|Stop hook feedback'), not just the <system-reminder>/tool_result/cross-session wrappers. Two of 7 prior jsonls now sampled (ec368ac8, ac4b4e98); both clean but for the one flagged access-controls candidate (n12). Remaining 5 (the 50-139MB ones) when a longer window is free.")
inl note_of_memory_reasons_audit_14 () = Note ("MEMORY-REASONS-AUDIT", 14u32, "2026-11-01T20:32Z", "resilience", "2026-11-01 16:5x AUDIT PASS 3 (resilience) — b7595ac3 (56MB). Clean. Directive 'i''m trying to avoid py files in the repo, if this is really important rewrite it in another language' = the faithful ORIGIN quote of memory no-py-txt-in-repos (+ spiral-first-tooling, org-tooling-in-dot-github) — Why fidelity confirmed. The recurring 'don''t start now, just establish the comm' is a session-restart behavioral line, not a durable rule. TALLY: 3 of 7 prior jsonls sampled (ec368ac8, ac4b4e98, b7595ac3), all clean; one candidate gap outstanding (access-controls, n12, for personal/x to verify). Memory Why-fidelity is holding across the sample. Remaining: 5a6e53fb 139MB, fc1568a6 82MB, 88a146f8 74MB, 84b53718 68MB (the heavy four) when a longer CPU-light window opens.")
inl note_of_memory_reasons_audit_15 () = Note ("MEMORY-REASONS-AUDIT", 15u32, "2026-11-01T20:36Z", "resilience", "2026-11-01 17:0x AUDIT PASS 4 (resilience) — 84b53718 (70MB, 64 directive-turns -> 5 distinct). Clean. 'make sure its gh actions are passing, are we verifying that every time i decide to push' = faithful origin of verify-ci-after-user-push; 'everything downloaded in gh actions, available through dir-tree-html with reports/benchs' -> same (CI artifacts + dir-tree-html judgment). 'i don''t even remember asking for such extension to exist in kit' = a scope reaction (don''t add unrequested features), not a durable rule to file. TALLY: 4 of 7 prior jsonls sampled (ec368ac8, ac4b4e98, b7595ac3, 84b53718), all clean; one candidate gap outstanding (access-controls, n12). Memory Why-fidelity confirmed across the sample. Remaining heavy three: 5a6e53fb 139MB, fc1568a6 82MB, 88a146f8 74MB.")
inl note_of_memory_reasons_audit_16 () = Note ("MEMORY-REASONS-AUDIT", 16u32, "2026-11-01T20:56Z", "resilience", "2026-11-01 17:1x AUDIT PASSES 5-7 (resilience) — fc1568a6 82MB, 88a146f8 74MB, 5a6e53fb 139MB. ALL 7 PRIOR JSONLS NOW SWEPT. Strong coverage: 'back up then leave unstaged since my last manual commit', 'no acks; never commit or stage' -> never-commit-leave-unstaged + messaging-budget; 'avoiding these oks, do backups then do what you think best' -> accept-autonomously/decide-autonomously; 'i don''t like py files in the repos' (again) -> no-py-txt-in-repos; 'use installed bin\\\\kit.exe not the scratchpad copy' -> kit-binary-installed-path; 'Never stop another lane''s process; respect held windows' -> stop-only-own-processes + window-coordination; 'always low cpu work while waiting' -> backlog-always-has-cpu-light.\nCANDIDATE GAPS surfaced (flag for the OWNING lane to verify vs memory; NOT filing — cross-lane + verify-don''t-file-from-paraphrase): (1) 'must never copy secrets' [in tsv/CI context] — security; is this captured beyond the userEmail rule? (2) 'Do not re-sync the mirror between regen and build' — node-proof/candidate-build workflow (kit lane; relates to node-proof-skips-regen) — NOTE I complied in my own proofs. (3) 'Don''t post backlog notes while the box freeze is on (each rewrites the ~8MB agile.spi on C:)' — operational I/O rule (relates to agile-status-lost-updates / box-freeze); no freeze now but worth a memory. (4) 'zero downtime deployments' — deployment goal (x/main). (5) access-controls (n12). METHOD note: type=user turns include own cron/heartbeat + task-notifications; the exclusion filter ('autonomous-iteration heartbeat|task-notification|completed (exit|one line per point') is now proven over all 7. DELIVERABLE: the prior-session transcript sweep for faithful-vs-captured directives is COMPLETE; residual = the 5 flagged candidates routed to owning lanes. Original pre-wipe transcripts remain gone (only these 7 post-wipe survive); this is the recoverable universe.")
inl note_of_memory_reasons_audit_17 () = Note ("MEMORY-REASONS-AUDIT", 17u32, "2026-11-01T23:40Z", "resilience", "2026-11-01 19:4x gap RESOLVED (resilience). Candidate gap n16 'no backlog notes during box freeze' CONFIRMED real (verbatim user quote in transcript 5a6e53fb) and WRITTEN as memory no-agile-writes-during-box-freeze (each agile note rewrites the ~11MB agile.spi; hold writes while a quiet/bench/live window is held; write-side sibling of window-coordination). So of the 5 flagged candidate gaps: export + nameof + this one are now memories (3 written tonight); REMAINING for the owning lanes to verify: 'never bypass access controls' (x/personal, X-scraping context) and 'zero-downtime deployments' (x/main) and 'do not re-sync the mirror between regen and build' (kit, node-proof workflow - relates to node-proof-skips-regen). mem_sweep clean after the write.")
inl note_of_memory_reasons_audit_18 () = Note ("MEMORY-REASONS-AUDIT", 18u32, "2026-11-02T17:56Z", "resilience", "11-02 13:5x resilience MEMORY-REASONS-AUDIT first-pass scan (288 memories). Classification by body markers: ~132 original Why, ~149 reconstructed/enriched Why (carry a 'Recovered from citations' / 'Enriched from citing notes' / 'rebuilt 2026-' footer = rebuilt post-10-28-wipe from backlog notes, so the Why is a RECONSTRUCTION to trust less than an original), 7 with no explicit Why. The 7 no-Why are all REFERENCE-type (vultr-vm, box-gpu-cuda-limit, erlang-port-hide-console-apps, ps-stub-server-traps, claude-code-git-bash-path, backlog-file-size-limit(self-marked UNVERIFIED), audiobooks-lost) - facts/traps that legitimately don't need a **Why:**, NOT gaps. So: no genuine missing-Why gaps; the real flag-set is the ~149 reconstructed Whys. Caveat: regex classification is approximate (enrichment-footer heuristic). REMAINING: spot-check a sample of the reconstructed-Why memories against source (kit archive / original shares) for accuracy, per tibia's lesson 'read the archived source, not notes written from memory'. Method + landscape captured; the spot-check is the audit's substantive remainder.")
inl note_of_memory_reasons_audit_19 () = Note ("MEMORY-REASONS-AUDIT", 19u32, "2026-11-02T19:49Z", "resilience", "11-02 15:48 resilience: COMPLETE (audit + cross-lane hand-off). Audit done: 233 files tiered (T0 verbatim 2; T3 INFERRED 13; rest T1 quote / T2 re-derived-with-citation), see note 2 for the 13 by owner + confidence + basis. Hand-off done: filed MAIN-MEMORY-REASONS(7), COMPILER-MEMORY-REASONS(1), KIT-MEMORY-REASONS(2), each team-tagged with its INFERRED memories + confidence + basis-citation pointer + the re-derive-from-pre-wipe-quotes instruction; tibia already had TIBIA-MEMORY-REASONS (Done). Fixed one stale slug in the hand-off (decision-rounds-one-at-a-time -> decision-rounds-batched, renamed since the 11-01 audit). Each lane now owns its re-derivation in its own item; resilience audit+hand-off half is done.")
inl backlog_light_depth () = Task ("BACKLOG-LIGHT-DEPTH", "Keep >=3 ready load=light items with a concrete next step on top of each team's queue so agile next --load light always returns real work; cure for the measured post-wipe CPU-light-while-blocked erosion (WIPE-DAMAGE-LEDGER (B), wait/note 0.27->0.34); resilience re-measures cadence weekly", TaskKind, P2, D2, 40u32, Active, "", "team:main,main,backlog,wipe-redo,discipline", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_backlog_light_depth () = Planning ("BACKLOG-LIGHT-DEPTH", Minutes 60u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_backlog_light_depth_1 () = Note ("BACKLOG-LIGHT-DEPTH", 1u32, "2026-11-01T06:18Z", "main", "main 02:2x first measurement ('kit agile list --tag <team> --load light --ready', open items only): main 49, kit 78, tibia 13, x 12, compiler 11, fiscal 7, resilience 6. Every team is above the 3-item floor, so the post-wipe stall is NOT queue depth: lanes have ready light work and don't pull it while blocked (resilience's wait share 0.27 -> 0.34). The fix is behavioral: groom rounds run to their deadline with a wake (groom-rounds-all-lanes), the Stop gate names the next ready light item, and kit's 'kit groom start --until' idea. Re-measure each round.")
inl note_of_backlog_light_depth_2 () = Note ("BACKLOG-LIGHT-DEPTH", 2u32, "2026-11-05T20:45Z", "main", "main 11-05 16:4x CENSUS (kit agile next --team T --ready --load light): main 66, kit 111, resilience 10, x 6, compiler 6, fiscal 5, x-dumps 3 (at the floor), tibia 0. tibia has 12 open load=light items, but all are Paused or dep-blocked (GRANULAR-HEAL waits on 9 deps, BACKEND-BALANCE on 5), so its stop gate offers nothing light. Asked tibia to bring 3 to ready at its next groom. x-dumps sits exactly at 3.")
inl cell_budget_naming () = Task ("CELL-BUDGET-NAMING", "Generic names for the cell size check: no X nomenclature in spiral (user 11-01 00:2x: 'postability is important but we need generic names... internally we gotta follow x''s 280... the 280 just hangs around as a phantom constant, in one place preferably... in the future we''ll have near contracts holding each cell''s code with zkvm receipts etc, keeping x nomenclature like post is weird, we gotta pivot that as hard as we can'). Spiral.Cells.XCompat -> Spiral.Cells.CellBudget, mix spiral.x_compat -> mix spiral.cell_budget, overweight -> over_budget, the --x-compat run flag and x-compat lines renamed, 280 as one constant; X-only layers (t.co weight, dot defusing, poster header) move to x", Story, P0, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "cells,naming,postability,main,x,tibia", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_cell_budget_naming () = Planning ("CELL-BUDGET-NAMING", Minutes 120u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_cell_budget_naming_1 () = Note ("CELL-BUDGET-NAMING", 1u32, "2026-11-01T04:37Z", "main", "main 00:3x: decided names, told x and tibia to hold their call-site swaps (x keeps them behind Assert-CellsXCompat in x/scripts/common.ps1; tibia wires its gate to spiral.cell_budget). apps/sim uses x_weight, segments, shortened_url_weight; keep equivalents under CellBudget and send x the exact map. Next: the applier on the scratch tree, then a cells_land_window land, then the compiler retires --x-compat (POSTABILITY-ONE-SOURCE step 3).")
inl note_of_cell_budget_naming_2 () = Note ("CELL-BUDGET-NAMING", 2u32, "2026-11-01T04:46Z", "main", "main 00:45 LANDED green (15/15: cell_budget, cell_budget_profile, warm_notebook, new_project tests). Spiral.Cells.XCompat -> Spiral.Cells.CellBudget (cell_budget.ex), mix spiral.x_compat -> spiral.cell_budget (exit 1 only on over_budget), overweight -> over_budget, x_weight -> cell_weight, shortened_url_weight -> link_weight, run flag --x-compat -> --cell-budget and line prefix 'cell-budget '; hints say 'a renderer' instead of X; the 280 is one constant (@weight_limit in CellBudget.Profile; the profile loader falls back to it). README section 'X compatibility' -> 'Cell budget'. x and tibia have the exact map. Left (20%): x's and tibia's gates switch (theirs), the compiler retires --x-compat (POSTABILITY-ONE-SOURCE step 3), the profile loader goes after that, and the CELLS-IN-SPIRAL port (M1, main).")
inl note_of_cell_budget_naming_3 () = Note ("CELL-BUDGET-NAMING", 3u32, "2026-11-01T12:03Z", "tibia-lane", "11-01 08:02 tibia groom: tibia's share is DONE. scripts/build.ps1 calls mix spiral.cell_budget, prints 'build.ps1 / cell budget / ...', and has no x_compat, postable or post names left (verified on every run tonight; also runs under the erl memory guard). Nothing in tibia blocks the remaining 20%.")
inl note_of_cell_budget_naming_4 () = Note ("CELL-BUDGET-NAMING", 4u32, "2026-11-01T19:55Z", "x", "2026-11-01 15:55 x: x's SHARE IS DONE (audited 15:5x, git grep over x). Gate: x/scripts/common.ps1 Assert-CellBudget runs 'mix spiral.cell_budget --check' and requires 'cell-budget report: 0 finding(s)', under the erl memory guard. x has no x_compat, XCompat, x-compat, overweight or shortened_url_weight names left. Its 'x_weight' (splitter.spi:237) is X's own counting rule inside x, the X-only layer the item moves to x. The 280 lives once in x (splitter.spi post_limit, the on_x cell_limit instance), and the sim reads it from there (sim_serve too_heavy, unstaged, proven by x-platform-proto2). What's left is not x's: the compiler retires --x-compat (POSTABILITY-ONE-SOURCE step 3), and main drops the profile loader.")
inl note_of_cell_budget_naming_5 () = Note ("CELL-BUDGET-NAMING", 5u32, "2026-11-01T20:40Z", "main", "main 16:4x MAIN'S SHARE DONE (unstaged in spiral/apps/cells): the profile loader is gone. CellBudget.profile_for/nearest_profile/cached_profile/load_profile (61 lines, including the SPIRAL_POSTABILITY_PROFILE override and the walk up to the nearest postability/*.json) and the now-unused defused_regex are removed. mix spiral.cell_budget and notebook_cli use default_profile() directly, so Cells checks every repo with the built-in profile and never reads x's postability/x.json (x keeps that file as its own X data, read by x's tooling only). cell_budget_profile_test.exs is deleted (it tested only the loader; git history keeps it); cell_budget_test's custom-profile assertions became one built-in-profile autolink test. mix test cell_budget_test.exs: 5 passed (box, own MIX_BUILD_PATH). Left: compiler retires --x-compat (POSTABILITY-ONE-SOURCE step 3), then this closes.")
inl note_of_cell_budget_naming_6 () = Note ("CELL-BUDGET-NAMING", 6u32, "2026-11-02T21:09Z", "x", "2026-11-02 17:09 x: CLOSED. The last condition (the compiler retires --x-compat, POSTABILITY-ONE-SOURCE step 3) is MET and checked from the binary, not the announcement: the installed pack17 compiler (core E556D773EB123175) answers 'dotnet SpiralCompiler.dll --x-compat' with its usage text and exit 2, and --x-compat is not among its options. The last outside caller, i574n.github/scripts/x-compat-census.ps1, is archived (no longer in the tree). x's share (n4) and main's share (n5: profile loader removed) were already done.")
inl typefeatureset_enforcement () = Task ("TYPEFEATURESET-ENFORCEMENT", "Typefeatureset enforcement (GADTs, existentials, HKTs, prototypes, reflection, phantom types) was a memory rule with no tool behind it, so the wipe could lose it silently (user 11-01 00:2x: 'have we lost its enforcement with the wipe?'). A kit standards rule per repo: model_metrics' counts per file, a ratchet so the share of typed modeling never falls, and a per-repo scoreboard comparing fiscal, tibia, x, kit, the pre-vibecode handcrafted lib and the 5 mega samples", Story, P0, D4, 80u32, Blocked, "", "kit,standards,typefeatureset,metrics,main,fiscal,team:kit", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_typefeatureset_enforcement () = Planning ("TYPEFEATURESET-ENFORCEMENT", Minutes 180u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_typefeatureset_enforcement_1 () = Note ("TYPEFEATURESET-ENFORCEMENT", 1u32, "2026-11-01T04:37Z", "main", "main 00:3x: the backlog had no item with 'typefeature' in it. The rule lives only in memory typefeatureset-default-all-code (10-31) and phantom-data-in-fsharp. Measuring tool exists: spiral/apps/cells/spi/model_metrics.ps1 (11 metrics, PowerShell, so it becomes a kit verb under PS1-TO-SPIRAL). Fiscal is running it over its sources tonight. Next: kit adds 'kit standards typefeatures --ratchet' reading the same 11 metrics.")
inl note_of_typefeatureset_enforcement_2 () = Note ("TYPEFEATURESET-ENFORCEMENT", 2u32, "2026-11-01T04:41Z", "main", "main 00:4x, fiscal's first numbers (FISCAL-TYPEFEATURESET-METRICS): fiscal src M3 GADT 1.6, M5 existential 0, M11 types 11.6; tibia 9.1/0.7/13.7; the 5 mega samples 86.7/30.8/36.4. Only fiscal has M7 string dispatch (6.5) and M8 sentinels (4.1); targets are M7/M8 at 0 and M3/M11 up to tibia. TOOL GAP from fiscal: model_metrics can't read generated units' inl/let heads (defs 0, M9 1,747 noise over 954k lines). The kit verb must parse with the compiler's tokens, not regexes.")
inl note_of_typefeatureset_enforcement_3 () = Note ("TYPEFEATURESET-ENFORCEMENT", 3u32, "2026-11-01T13:06Z", "kit", "11-01 09:0x kit: this item is now a FEEDER of CODEBASE-DIAMOND (the deterministic rewrite-distance gauge, user 09:0x): its counts become Mechanical evidence in DIAMOND-S2. Keep building it here; the gauge consumes its rows, it does not re-measure them.")
inl note_of_typefeatureset_enforcement_4 () = Note ("TYPEFEATURESET-ENFORCEMENT", 4u32, "2026-11-01T14:45Z", "fiscal", "2026-11-01 10:59 fiscal: FISCAL'S SHARE IS SPECIFIED. Every fiscal item touched in groom rounds 4-5 now carries a TYPEFEATURESET PLAN naming its unions/GADTs/existentials/prototypes/phantoms and what stays out of strings and -1 sentinels: XSD-CROSS-NAMESPACE-NAMES (phantom-form element_name + Local|Prefixed union), XSD-FACET-TYPES (GADT builtin carrier + existential classify), XSD-DECODE-RESULT (decode_error union, decode_path nominal, occurs GADT), XSD-CODEC-PROTOTYPES (codec/xml_codec prototypes), XSD-FIELD-DESCRIPTORS (field GADT owner x value, existential field lists), XSD-TYPED-RULES (rule GADT indexed by document type), XSD-CERTIFIED-MATCHER (length-indexed certified table), FISCAL-TYPEFEATURESET-METRICS (family_root indexed by schema source, exists s. family_root s), FISCAL-CANON-MODEL (canon_term GADT, bt_id/bg_id nominals), FISCAL-LIFECYCLE-EVENTS (document GADT by lifecycle state, illegal transitions do not compile), FISCAL-CII-PROFILES (phantom profile index + checked narrowing), FISCAL-GENERIC-PROVABLE (typed manifest rows, prototype-indexed laws), plus PINT/CR/PT/MY/PE/ES/BENCH/PACKAGE-COVERAGE/GENERATED-STYLE. Measured baseline (model_metrics, fiscal src): M3 1.6, M7 3.9, M8 0.8, M11 13.4; targets M3 >= 9.1 and M11 >= 13.7 (tibia), M7 and M8 to 0.")
inl note_of_typefeatureset_enforcement_5 () = Note ("TYPEFEATURESET-ENFORCEMENT", 5u32, "2026-11-01T14:46Z", "kit", "11-01 10:4x kit: three new items carry TYPEFEATURES lines to enforce from birth: KIT-REUSE-METRICS (phantom span units, dup_class GADT, existential heal_candidate), KIT-ORPHAN-SIM (proc_event union, phantom host markers, detector-state GADT), KIT-STANDARDS-ECOSYSTEM (rule union with phantom scope).")
inl note_of_typefeatureset_enforcement_6 () = Note ("TYPEFEATURESET-ENFORCEMENT", 6u32, "2026-11-01T18:18Z", "kit", "11-01 14:18 kit: user 18:1x, 'kit priority number one to enforce and it needs to happen with itself too'. kit's own enforcement vehicle is KIT-TYPED-CRATES (P0 epic) plus EOIE-IDIOMATIC-CORE (raised to P0).")
inl note_of_typefeatureset_enforcement_7 () = Note ("TYPEFEATURESET-ENFORCEMENT", 7u32, "2026-11-01T23:56Z", "main", "main 20:0x BASELINE SCOREBOARD (one-time grep over tracked .spi/.spir, no deps/target/generated; the ratchet must use the Spiral reader). Typed constructs per 1k lines = unions + GADT cases (| X ::) + nominals + prototypes + instances + existentials + foralls. Format: repo (k lines): typed/k, GADT/k, protos+insts, existentials, string-literal match arms/k.\nmega samples (56.9): 95.1, 15.9, 18, 1572, 0.00\nregex (2.0, today's rewrite): 75.5, 56.5, 1, 0, 0.00\nspiral/lib (38.6): 55.1, 11.9, 175, 10, 0.03\nkit (89.1): 36.9, 12.2, 13, 413, 0.00\nfiscal src (2.4; generated units excluded): 30.5, 19.5, 0, 0, 0.00\nx (13.2): 20.8, 8.8, 20, 0, 0.62\nblog (0.6): 19.0, 17.0, 0, 0, 0.00\ntibia (15.1): 17.8, 2.8, 5, 9, 0.00\ndir-tree-html (1.0): 15.0, 0.0, 6, 0, 0.00\ndice (3.1): 11.0, 0.7, 0, 0, 1.33\nReading: the from-scratch regex rewrite already models like the mega samples; tibia and dice are the most stringly per line (tibia healing case maps tonight); x and dice have hand-written string match arms (SPIRAL-NAMEOF-HEAL targets). NEXT (kit): turn this into 'kit standards' census columns (model_metrics per file), ratchet each repo's typed/k so it can't fall, and render a per-repo scoreboard on the agile HTML page (AGILE-HTML-REPORT).")
inl note_of_typefeatureset_enforcement_8 () = Note ("TYPEFEATURESET-ENFORCEMENT", 8u32, "2026-11-02T11:05Z", "kit", "kit 11-02 07:2x, from the S0 study subagent's execution packet (aaf2a6f472dc70094). New scored rows, consumed by CODEBASE-DIAMOND: (1) export signatures monomorphic, GADT indices only inside an existential at a unit boundary (LINKED-UNITS-MONOMORPHIC-RULE); (2) TEMPLATE-TYPED-UNITS-1's gates: <=1000 generated lines per unit, zero hand-written \\$'<unit>::, zero union<->code/string mapping at boundaries (kit's measured baseline: 127 cross-crate ': i32' calls, 39 decoder sites, KIT-TYPED-CRATES n14), parity table clean (FEATURE-PARITY-TABLE).")
inl note_of_typefeatureset_enforcement_9 () = Note ("TYPEFEATURESET-ENFORCEMENT", 9u32, "2026-11-02T16:04Z", "fiscal", "fiscal 11-02 12:04 TYPEFEATURESET SURVEY (user 11-02: \"if we're still dealing with gadt bugs ... only now scratching the surface of complex-enough models ... see if fiscal or any other project can use better the typefeatureset so we reach sota metrics\"). Measured over hand-written Spiral (no generated/test/deps), per kloc: repo klines | types/100defs | GADT-case % | nominals | protos | instances | HKT | existentials | string-dispatch | sentinels | string-field %:\nlib 25.2 | 19 | 32 | 16.1 | 1.3 | 5.6 | 1.3 | 0.1 | 13.8 | 2.1 | 7  <- reference (the handcrafted modeling ceiling)\nkit 58.9 | 23 | 25 | 11.1 | 0.1 | 0.1 | 0.1 | 6.9 | 16.5 | 5.3 | 35\nfiscal 1.3 | 18 | 20 | 6.8 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 3.8 | 0.8 | 32\ntibia 9.3 | 12 | 12 | 5.5 | 0 | 0.5 | 0.1 | 0.5 | 2.3 | 0.6 | 4\nx 8.1 | 12 | 13 | 5.0 | 0.6 | 1.8 | 0 | 0 | 26.5 | 2.3 | 17\nregex 1.2 | 18 | 22 | 4.2 | 0 | 0.8 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0\ncells 1.9 | 11 | 6 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 42.6 | 11.9 | 0\ndir-tree-html 0.9 | 6 | 0 | 1.1 | 2.2 | 4.5 | 0 | 0 | 39.1 | 40.2 | 0\nTSV: %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\reports\\tfs-scoreboard-20261102.tsv (script: fiscal scratchpad tfs-scoreboard.ps1).\nFINDINGS: (1) prototypes + HKTs are the most under-used features everywhere except lib (0 in fiscal/tibia/regex/cells; HKT 0.1 or less outside lib). (2) the worst string-dispatch/sentinel density is in tooling (cells 42.6/11.9, dir-tree-html 39.1/40.2, x 26.5, kit 16.5): those are where unions pay off first. (3) string-typed record fields: kit 35%, fiscal 32%, x 17%.\nTOOL GAPS for the ratchet: model_metrics.ps1 has no prototype/instance/HKT/existential-by-syntax columns, and it misreads edition-2 generated units (fiscal generated: list patterns \"| y :: rest\" count as GADT cases). Generated fiscal is in fact rich (nfe_v4: 284 unions, 193 indexed, 251 nominals, 1 prototype to_text with 314 instances), but its codec has no prototypes. The ratchet must read through the Spiral reader (main's n2 caveat) and score generated and hand code separately.\nNEW ITEMS from this survey: FISCAL-CANON-TYPED-TERMS, CELLS-TYPED-TARGETS, GADT-DOMAIN-STRESS-SAMPLES.")
inl note_of_typefeatureset_enforcement_10 () = Note ("TYPEFEATURESET-ENFORCEMENT", 10u32, "2026-11-02T21:27Z", "fiscal", "2026-11-02 17:10 fiscal: TYPED FAMILY ROOTS (src/generator.spi, unstaged): family_root's root_element, root_type and target_namespace now use lib's own xsd nominals (tag_name, definition_name written, namespace_uri) instead of string, and module_name is a new nominal module_id; 78 construction sites wrapped, 31 reads go through tag_text/definition_text/namespace_text/module_text plus one root_type_name family helper (8 call sites of type_name family::root_type collapse into it). Unwrap happens only at the lib boundaries (xsd_emit.type_name, xsd_emit's complement_member.module_name : string, json_schema). 988 non-blank lines (cap 1000). PROOF so far: SpiralCompiler --check on pack17 passes (22 s, needs SPIRAL_COMPILER_PACKAGE_DIR); MUTATION (nfe_v4_00 with root_element and root_type constructors swapped) fails with 2 ETY-TermError unification failures (got definition_name, expected tag_name), so swapping a root's element and type names is now a compile error, not a silent wrong unit. DONE RULE (pre-registered): a full node regen on pack17 writes output byte-identical to job-1793646608824's 90-file manifest, generator 23/23. Queued behind fiscal-pack17-format-saft. Left as string on purpose: schema_path and documents (file paths; they feed lib readers that take string).")
inl note_of_typefeatureset_enforcement_11 () = Note ("TYPEFEATURESET-ENFORCEMENT", 11u32, "2026-11-02T22:47Z", "fiscal", "2026-11-02 18:47 fiscal: SCOREBOARD RE-RUN (same grep instrument as n9's 12:04 survey, hand-written fiscal/src only, 1.4 kl, 142 defs), before -> after today's typed canon, regime-indexed lifecycle, typed family roots and the shared runtime: types/100 defs 18 -> 23 (lib 19); nominals/kl 6.8 -> 10 (lib 16.1); prototypes/kl 0 -> 1.4 (lib 1.3, now above lib); instances/kl 0 -> 2.1 (lib 5.6); constrained/kl 0 -> 1.4 (lib 1.6); sentinels/kl 0.8 -> 0; string-field % 32 -> 8 (lib 7). WENT THE WRONG WAY, to explain next round: GADT-case % 20 -> 13 (definitions grew faster than GADT cases) and string-dispatch/kl 3.8 -> 5 (new '= \"...\"' comparisons, to be listed). Still 0: HKT and existentials in hand-written fiscal (the proven codec-instance design in XSD-CODEC-PROTOTYPES adds HKT instances to generated units, not to src).")
inl note_of_typefeatureset_enforcement_12 () = Note ("TYPEFEATURESET-ENFORCEMENT", 12u32, "2026-11-02T23:25Z", "fiscal", "2026-11-02 19:25 fiscal: STRING-DISPATCH TRIAGE (n11 asked why 3.8 -> 5/kl). The instrument's regex also counts ':= x = \"...\"' bindings, so most of the 64 hits in fiscal/src are literals, not dispatch. Real ones, fixed in the tree (unstaged, gated in queued node job fiscal-lifecycle-typed-tests with mutations): (1) lifecycle tests event_codes_name_their_author and recipient_refusals_carry_their_justification had test-local case->string tables (13 string comparisons); they now compare the actor / recipient_event unions directly ('actor_of_event_code 110111i32 <> Some Issuer'); (2) corpus_checks dian_compiled_rules_read_fully and pint_rules_read_fully compared a JOINED census string ('patterns 5 rules 84 ...'); they now compare an i32 tuple, with the joined text kept only for the failure message. Deferred (lib API, cross-repo): generator.spi:943 'certificates <> \"\"' is lib xsd_emit's string * string sentinel (wants string * option string; ~15 xsd_emit callers ignore it, 2 tests read it), and :491 re-reads elementFormDefault from the schema XML although lib already parses it into tag_scope.qualified_locals (not exposed on xsd_schema). Both need a spiral + fiscal change pushed together, so they wait for a lib round that moves both. INSTRUMENT FIX for the scoreboard: count only '=' / '<>' whose left side is not a ':=' binding. [CORRECTION 19:28: the instrument does NOT count bindings: its pattern only counts a '= \"...\"' / '<> \"...\"' right after ?, |?, &&, || or <>. Its 9 real hits in fiscal/src are: canon.spi:75 'high = \"n\"' and :205 'declared = \"\"' (parsing the EN 16931 text at the boundary), corpus.spi:27, fiscal_paths.spi:7 and generator.spi:230 (empty env var / trimmed line checks), generator.spi:497 (known namespaces) and :943 (the lib certificate sentinel), lifecycle.spi:147/149 (test asserts on the string event code). The two rewrites above remove stringly test code the instrument never matched, so they do not move the metric. The metric moves only with the deferred lib round (:943, :497) or a typed event code (correction_event returns string; a ':! event_code = string' would type :149).]")
inl note_of_typefeatureset_enforcement_13 () = Note ("TYPEFEATURESET-ENFORCEMENT", 13u32, "2026-11-03T01:04Z", "fiscal", "2026-11-02 21:04 fiscal: TYPED FAMILY ROOTS (n10) DONE RULE MET: the full node regen on the committed generator (job-1793658234964, core 8960CAB701FEF46E throughout, generator 23/23) is output-identical to the pre-typing pack17 run except the separately planned insert_run lines and their size-derived timeout headers (0 unexplained lines across 44 differing units). So root_element : tag_name, root_type : definition_name written, target_namespace : namespace_uri and module_name : module_id changed no generated byte, while a swapped element/type name is now a compile error (n10 mutation). Units placed in fiscal/generated (unstaged).")
inl note_of_typefeatureset_enforcement_14 () = Note ("TYPEFEATURESET-ENFORCEMENT", 14u32, "2026-11-03T01:18Z", "fiscal", "2026-11-02 21:18 fiscal: VERIFIED (job-1793667211857-fiscal-t1101-chain, step lifecycle-typed-tests, t1101, core 8960CAB701FEF46E): lifecycle 9/9 with the union-comparing event tests (committed by the user in b1ce33c) AND the unstaged ':! event_code = string' (cancellation_event / correction_event return event_code; correction_letter.event and Cancelled.event are event_code; tests compare 'event_code \"110110\"'); MUTATION (110111 expects Some Fisco, 210210 expects Some Confirmation) fails exactly event_codes_name_their_author and recipient_refusals_carry_their_justification. corpus_checks census tuple tests 2/2; MUTATION 228 -> 229 fails exactly dian_compiled_rules_read_fully (output 'patterns 5 rules 84 assertions 249 fatal 228 lets 201 invalid 0'). So both rewrites are typed AND non-vacuous. Instrument effect: lifecycle.spi:149's '<> \"110110\"' hit is gone (now a nominal comparison).")
inl note_of_typefeatureset_enforcement_15 () = Note ("TYPEFEATURESET-ENFORCEMENT", 15u32, "2026-11-03T05:36Z", "main", "main 11-03 01:3x MEASURED regex (kit standards --census, live src vs a HEAD archive copy): lines 1801 -> 2706, gadt-case 20 -> 25 (automaton phase typestate), forall 2 (automaton_dfa \\* p.), host-macro 1 -> 1, every drift count 0. Density of GADT cases fell 11.1 -> 9.2 per kloc although tonight's work is typed by design: the checker verdicts (dfa_violation, partition_check, set_check, quotient_check, canon_fault/canon, closure_check, reconstruction) and the nominals (interned, by_language/by_backtracking) are plain unions and ':!' nominals, which the census does not count. FINDING for the ratchet (kit's part): 'typed verdict unions' and ':!' nominals/phantoms should be census columns, otherwise a repo that replaces bool checks with named-verdict unions scores as LESS typed per kloc. Proposed columns: verdict-union (a ':|' whose cases carry the violated property, returned by a check_ function) and nominal (':! x = T'). No ratchet baseline file for regex was found.")
inl note_of_typefeatureset_enforcement_16 () = Note ("TYPEFEATURESET-ENFORCEMENT", 16u32, "2026-11-03T05:55Z", "tibia", "11-03 01:4x tibia TYPEFEATURESET PASS on tibia's arena and tools (unstaged), sentinels -> unions/options, every change node-gated memo-off on the tibia-gates3 mirror (jobs offscreen-2, chain4-1; chain6-1 running). (1) apps/offscreen: CLI flags are text_flag (Exe|Directory|Arguments|Desktop) and number_flag (TimeoutSeconds) unions inside flag = Text | Number, parsed once at the boundary by lib case_labels::case_of_label (kebab labels from the case names, no string table), so the 5 flag = \"--...\" comparisons are gone; text setters cannot fail and return launch, number setters can and return parsed (both matches exhaustive, no wildcard). Tests a_flag_is_its_case_after_two_dashes and every_flag_reaches_its_field added: offscreen 9/9. (2) apps/arena/competitor: mover = Knight | Target as option mover instead of a \"\" sentinel, logged through case_labels (labels pinned by a lua test: knight, target); plan.reach_first : bool -> plan.order : policy::targeting, so prefers delegates to policy::prefers_by (env spelling reach/current kept on purpose, so an old reproduction does not silently become CurrentFirst); sighting { found; index; distance=999 } -> option sighting; threat { attackers; found; index } -> { attackers; first : option i32 } (found was redundant with attackers > 0); the int target_code 0/1/2 is gone: reach_of builds policy::reach from the option, aimed/target_health_of/target_distance_of/retreated_now match on it, so nothing reads an index of an empty sighting. (3) apps/arena/probe: nearest { found; index; distance } -> spotted = option nearest with beats/within helpers (999 kept only as the real range cap in beats); its 4 consumers steps/measure/random/path match on it. (4) apps/arena/path: trail { has_last; last } -> last : option spot. Results: measure 4/4, path 5/5, probe 2/2, random 2/2, steps 3/3, offscreen 9/9, competitor 5/6 in chain4-1 (the one failure was the new label test on its C target; now lua-only, re-gate chain6-1 still running at 01:55), terrain 17/17 with weighted_step on lib heap_scope::scoped (1 unsupported = its WGSL test, as before the change). Lesson: lib case_labels has no C arm (resultm reaches a backend_switch without C), so its tests target lua/rust; competitor's label test is lua-only, matching where the arena runs. Not done: a row-decoder prototype for results/ (an instance needs a nominal or union, and arm_step is a :== structural alias). CORRECTED 11-03 07:4x: tibia already defines 2 prototypes (judge, width) with 7 instances and 22 constrained generics (n18); the scoreboard's 0 was an instrument miss.")
inl note_of_typefeatureset_enforcement_17 () = Note ("TYPEFEATURESET-ENFORCEMENT", 17u32, "2026-11-03T05:58Z", "tibia", "11-03 01:56 tibia: competitor re-gate green (node job chain6-1, tibia digest EB6B6F71892FCD4B, memo off): 6/6, build exit 0, cell budget clean. So every module of n16 is gated green: offscreen 9/9, competitor 6/6, measure 4/4, path 5/5, probe 2/2, random 2/2, steps 3/3, terrain 17/17.")
inl note_of_typefeatureset_enforcement_18 () = Note ("TYPEFEATURESET-ENFORCEMENT", 18u32, "2026-11-03T11:47Z", "tibia", "11-03 07:4x tibia, INSTRUMENT GAP for the scoreboard (n9's row: tibia prototypes/kl 0, which is WRONG for today's tree): tibia defines 2 prototypes (network.spi:222 ':? judge f : f -> weights -> i32', typed_network.spi:13 ':? width size : size -> i32'), has 7 instances (purpose_code for combat_roll, hunt_roll, pack_roll, network_roll, search_roll; width eight; judge pf1 in apps/predict/lanes.spi) and 22 constrained generics ('\\* p {purpose_code}.' across combat's typed keyed rolls and more). Either the 11-02 instrument missed edition-3 ':?' / ':+' spellings, or these arrived after it ran; both say the scoreboard needs edition-3 aware counting and separate columns for prototypes defined, instances and constrained-generic functions.")
inl note_of_typefeatureset_enforcement_19 () = Note ("TYPEFEATURESET-ENFORCEMENT", 19u32, "2026-11-03T15:14Z", "tibia", "11-03 11:5x tibia, INSTRUMENT GAP CONFIRMED (follow-up to n18): spiral apps/cells/spi/model_metrics.ps1 computes M1-M11 only (ret annotations, bare params, GADT cases, indexed unions, existentials, wildcard arms, string dispatch, sentinels, bool/option, mut, types per 100 defs). It has NO metric for prototypes (:? lines), instances (:+ lines) or constrained generics (a {proto} bound after a type parameter), so any scoreboard built on it reports 0 for them in every repo, not just tibia. Proposal for the kit verb that replaces it (PS1-TO-SPIRAL): M12 prototypes defined per kl, M13 instances per kl, M14 constrained-generic definitions as a percent of generic definitions. Reference counts to test it against: tibia today = 2 prototypes (network.spi judge, typed_network.spi width), 7 instances, 22 constrained generics. Owner: kit (the instrument); tibia supplies the fixture numbers.")
inl note_of_typefeatureset_enforcement_20 () = Note ("TYPEFEATURESET-ENFORCEMENT", 20u32, "2026-11-03T16:04Z", "main", "main 11-03 12:3x FIRST PER-REPO SCOREBOARD from kit standards --census over every package.spiproj dir (tracked + untracked), counts per 1000 lines: html (8 pkgs, 1.5k) GADT 1.0 forall 0.5 host-macro 0; regex (2, 3.5k) GADT 6.2 forall 0.8 host 2.0; x (2, 9.8k) GADT 1.8 forall 4.0 host 2.4; tibia (9, 15.9k) GADT 1.6 exist 0.25 forall 3.6 host 2.2; fiscal (83, 1243k incl. generated) GADT 22.1 exist 7.5 forall 8.0 proto 4.0 host 0; kit (1038, 159k) GADT 10.9 exist 1.4 forall 4.3 host 11.9 host-block 6.8; blog (3, 0.5k) all 0; dice (3, 0.7k) host 10.0; i574n.github (4, 17.9k) GADT 1.3 exist 3.5 forall 9.4; lib/spiral (37.3k) GADT 3.3 exist 0.1 forall 23.9 host 76. GAP CONFIRMED on the census too: 'prototype' reads 0 for tibia, which has 2 prototypes / 7 instances / 22 constrained generics (tibia n19), so the census misses edition-3 ':?'/':+' spellings or counts only one form; owner kit (same fix as tibia's M12-M14). Reading: html/blog/dice are the lowest-typed product code, and html and dice are main's to raise (the planned node-kind GADT in HTML-TREE is the next html gain).")
inl note_of_typefeatureset_enforcement_21 () = Note ("TYPEFEATURESET-ENFORCEMENT", 21u32, "2026-11-03T16:56Z", "tibia", "11-03 13:1x tibia, M7/M8 REVIEW OF TIBIA (typefeatureset in tibia): M8 sentinels (-1i32): 10 sites, of which combat_text 4 (parsers returning -1 for no match) and samples.stamp_of ((-1, \"\") for not found) are now typed in the tree (option i32 + a combat_line union Took | Dealt | Other read once by read_line; option (i32 * string) with steps matching Some/None), node gates queued; the rest are a negative dx in a geometry test, a direction_of test value, lead_trace/ground_trace since printing @knight+-1 (an output format, kept), and world_executor mapping the host exit code -1 to RunTimedOut (an external code, kept). M7 string dispatch: of 15 tibia hits, 13 are EMPTY-STRING checks (joining text with separators, defaults, absent options), not dispatch on a tag; the only real dispatch is game_calls.spi:73 on Lua client call names (an external API). INSTRUMENT FINDING: M7 should exclude comparisons with \"\" (or count them as their own metric), otherwise it rewards nothing and flags joins.")
inl note_of_typefeatureset_enforcement_22 () = Note ("TYPEFEATURESET-ENFORCEMENT", 22u32, "2026-11-03T16:58Z", "tibia", "11-03 13:2x tibia, M6 example worth fixing later (not now: wide call-site churn): src/hunting_pack.spi pack_roll (12 cases) maps to keyed-RNG purpose codes through hunt_code (6 arms + | _ => target_code r) and target_code (5 arms + | _ => 41u32). The split exists for the cell budget, but the final wildcard means a NEW pack_roll case silently gets code 41 and shares the PackChangeTarget random stream. Typed fix: pack_roll = HuntRoll of hunt_roll | TargetRoll of target_roll, so each code function is exhaustive with no wildcard and a new case fails to compile until it has its own code.")
inl note_of_typefeatureset_enforcement_23 () = Note ("TYPEFEATURESET-ENFORCEMENT", 23u32, "2026-11-03T17:51Z", "fiscal", "2026-11-03 13:50 fiscal: FIXTURE NUMBERS for kit's census fix (third repo after tibia n19 and main n20), measured on today's tree with the ERP modules landed unstaged. 'kit standards --census C:\\home\\git\\fiscal\\src' says: lines 2863, gadt-case 21 (hand count agrees: document 7, p2p 7, lifecycle 4, families 3), forall 19, prototype 0, existential 0. The prototype 0 is WRONG: fiscal/src has 3 ':?' prototypes (lifecycle cancellation_event and correction_event, document canon_of), 4 ':+' instances (nfe x2, cfdi, manual_invoice), and 5 constrained foralls ('\\* f {canon_of}.', '\\* r f {canon_of}.', '\\* r {cancellation_event}.' ...). Also uncounted: 34 ':!' nominals of which 21 are phantom indices (':! x = ()'), 2 ':| private' unions (lifecycle document, p2p purchase), and 20 'fails' negative tests (type-level guarantees with a pinned compile error; the best single signal that the types ENFORCE something). New lib modules (money, ledger, event_log, durable_file; 424 lines): 22 nominals (11 phantom), 8 fails tests. PROPOSED census columns (joins tibia's M12-M14 and main's verdict-union/nominal): prototype (':?'), instance (':+'), constrained-forall ('{proto}.' bound), nominal (':!'), phantom (':! x = ()'), private-union, fails-test. Owner kit; these counts are the expected values for fiscal/src at this tree state.")
inl note_of_typefeatureset_enforcement_24 () = Note ("TYPEFEATURESET-ENFORCEMENT", 24u32, "2026-11-03T18:22Z", "tibia", "11-03 14:2x tibia, M8 SENTINEL REMOVAL IN TIBIA GATED GREEN (node job tibia-typed-gate-2, tibia mirror synced from the tree, memo off): positive combat_text 7/7, measure 4/4, samples 6/6, steps 3/3, build exit 0; negative 1 (read_line takes a hit before damage) fails exactly 1 combat_text test; negative 2 (stamp_of matches the wrong key) fails exactly 1 samples test; both files restored by hash. Changes (unstaged in tibia): combat_text parsers return option i32, a combat_line union Took | Dealt | Other with read_line, measure.on_text matches it; samples.stamp_of/last_stamp return option (i32 * string), steps.step_logged matches Some/None. The first attempt was VOID (tibia build stops at the formatter layout check before any test; memory tibia-gate-layout-first). Also drafted: hunting_pack codes_distinct + test every_pack_roll_has_its_own_purpose_code (enumerates all 12 pack_roll cases through lib pickler::case_at and checks the keyed-RNG purpose codes are distinct, so a new case falling into the wildcard 41 fails CI), gating now.")
inl note_of_typefeatureset_enforcement_25 () = Note ("TYPEFEATURESET-ENFORCEMENT", 25u32, "2026-11-03T19:16Z", "tibia", "11-03 15:1x tibia, M6 GUARD GATED GREEN (node job tibia-hunt-gate-1, spiral mirror with lib pickler v4 landed): hunting_pack positive 3/3 (incl. every_pack_roll_has_its_own_purpose_code: roll_count = 12 via real_core union_type_length, codes_distinct enumerates every pack_roll case through lib pickler::case_at and compares hunt_code pairwise), build exit 0; mutant (PackSearchTarget given 41, the wildcard code of PackChangeTarget) fails exactly that test. So the wildcard in target_code can no longer hide a colliding new case: CI fails until the new case gets its own code. No call-site churn, RNG codes unchanged. (The later backend::backend_switch edit in the same file gates in the next chain.)")
inl note_of_typefeatureset_enforcement_26 () = Note ("TYPEFEATURESET-ENFORCEMENT", 26u32, "2026-11-03T21:26Z", "main", "main 11-03 17:2x SECOND FIXTURE for the census prototype gap (owner kit): html now has 6 ':?'/':+' lines (the listed_before prototype, its node instance, and the constrained generic insert_by/sort_by \\* t {listed_before}, added today in html_pages/tree.spi), yet 'kit standards --census' over its 8 packages still reports no prototype column (forall=1 gadt-case=2 lines=1507). Same symptom as tibia's 2 prototypes / 7 instances. html's other typed gains today are invisible to the census by design: named record payloads on node cases (HTML-NODE-RECORDS) and the listing union in the cli walk.")
inl note_of_typefeatureset_enforcement_27 () = Note ("TYPEFEATURESET-ENFORCEMENT", 27u32, "2026-11-03T23:31Z", "fiscal", "2026-11-03 19:31 fiscal: fiscal/src END-OF-DAY counts (live tree incl. today's unstaged modules; 3,299 lines). kit census: gadt-case 34 (21 at 13:51), existential 1 (0 before: profiles.spi's path GADT), forall 28 (19). Edition-3 counts the census still misses: 5 ':?' prototypes (3), 8 ':+' instances (4), 7 constrained foralls (5), 1 HKT forall (0 before: document.spi 'measured \\* (f : * -> *) {content; losses}'), 4 '\\?' existential uses (0), 27 phantom nominals (21), 44 nominals (34), 2 private unions, 23 'fails' negatives (20). Firsts in hand-written fiscal today: an HKT view (measured loss in the projection type), an existential (the profile path's middle level), a regime-indexed tax account (posting), a durable typed outbox (lifecycle + lib event_log). These are the expected values for kit's census fix at this tree state (n23 asked for the columns).")
inl note_of_typefeatureset_enforcement_28 () = Note ("TYPEFEATURESET-ENFORCEMENT", 28u32, "2026-11-04T01:10Z", "resilience-614162e5", "21:09 resilience-614162e5 (gate-claimed): assessed, RELEASING - cannot meaningfully/safely advance now. FEASIBILITY: model_metrics does NOT exist yet - grep across C:\\home\\git finds only this item's own backlog + graph refs, no kit/lib implementation. So the item requires BUILDING model_metrics (the typed-modeling counts-per-file tool) from scratch (kit code + a box build), then the ratchet + the 6-repo scoreboard on top. There is NO existing verb to run for a read-only scoreboard slice, and a grep approximation of \"share of typed modeling\" would be false precision on a metric that needs a precise construct definition (GADTs/existentials/HKTs/prototypes/reflection/phantom). BLOCKERS NOW: (1) box-gated build on a box that is fragile (19:44 BSOD) + 100% saturated (TRAIN17b running) - a build is inappropriate; (2) ~50min to the 22:00 run end - too little for a P0/D4 from-scratch tool; (3) not team:resilience (tags main,fiscal) - its owning lane has the typefeatureset-metric context. FIRST BUILD STEP when picked up: define precisely WHAT model_metrics counts per file (the typed-modeling construct set) - that definition IS the ratchet's denominator and must be nailed before the scoreboard means anything. Released, not progressed.")
inl note_of_typefeatureset_enforcement_29 () = Note ("TYPEFEATURESET-ENFORCEMENT", 29u32, "2026-11-04T01:21Z", "main", "21:2x main (gate-claimed): THIRD FIXTURE, and the census gap narrowed. 'kit standards --census' now HAS a prototype column, but it reads prototype=0 on spiral/apps/kernel/src (1641 lines, written 11-02..03), which holds 1 ':?' prototype (syntax.same_as) with 2 ':+' instances (level, term). So the column likely matches only the edition-2 'prototype' keyword, not edition-3 ':?'/':+'. Same miss as fiscal n(19:31): 5 ':?' and 8 ':+' read as 0. Expected values for the kernel at this tree: ':?' 1, ':+' 2, ':!' nominals 2 (name, closed: the closed-term guard of CIC-SCOPE), ':|' unions 12, ':&' mutual members 14, constrained foralls 0, GADT cases 0, existentials 0. Honest self-score: the kernel's typing is unions + nominals + prototypes; its planned GADT certificates (CIC-LEAN-ORACLE n1: cert has_type | defeq | whnf) are not written yet. Fix owner: kit (census parser), a box build. Releasing the claim: a kit-code change on the fragile, saturated box with ~35 min left in the run is not main's to start.")
inl note_of_typefeatureset_enforcement_30 () = Note ("TYPEFEATURESET-ENFORCEMENT", 30u32, "2026-11-04T01:25Z", "kit", "11-03 21:3x kit, from main's finding (census missed edition-3 syntax), fixed in the tree (after the TRAIN17b snapshot). kit_style_probes patterns:\n- Prototype now also counts '^\\s*:[?+]\\s' (:? prototype, :+ instance), beside the old prototype/instance keywords.\n- Forall and Existential accept binder lists of words, (parenthesized kinds) and {constraints} before the dot: '\\* t {proto}.' and '\\* (m : * -> *).' now count.\n- Nominal accepts indented ':!' (inside static blocks), not only column 0.\nAll four probes are MayNotFall (rising is better), so higher counts cannot trip the ratchet; no baseline merge is needed or wanted.\nTest: kit_style_measure edition_three_shapes_are_counted runs the real probe_pattern regexes over a 5-line sample (2 prototype, 2 forall, 1 nominal). It runs in the next train's notebooks; compile and size proof is in job-1793754669860-kit-precheck-gauge.\nAfter install, re-read 'kit standards --census' on spiral/apps/kernel/src and fiscal/src against the expected values main recorded on this item.")
inl note_of_typefeatureset_enforcement_31 () = Note ("TYPEFEATURESET-ENFORCEMENT", 31u32, "2026-11-04T01:27Z", "x", "2026-11-03 21:27 x: x's SCOREBOARD ROW (gate-claimed; tibia, main and kit own the rule). 'kit standards --census' with the INSTALLED kit.exe (note: --census must come before the dirs, or it is read as a package): x/apps/client lines=3615 gadt-case=13 forall=36 prototype=0 host-macro=22 top-level-macro=13 rc-wrap=11 encoded-row=4, others 0; x/apps/sim/spi lines=6343 gadt-case=5 forall=14 prototype=0 host-macro=2 rust-global=1 encoded-row=4, others 0. The prototype=0 is the census gap main found and kit fixed in the tree at 21:3x (not yet in the installed binary): a grep for edition-3 syntax finds 5 ':?' prototypes and 15 ':+' instances in the client (splitter.spi cell_limit, posted_as, glyphs_on and 2 more, with instances on_x, on_mastodon, on_bluesky), and 0 in the sim. So x confirms the fix on a second repo; the expected client row after the kit rebuild is prototype >= 20 (5 + 15). Side finding: the default rules flag 4 line-width findings in x's sim (cells_executor.spi:17, :110, :358 at 124-133 columns, limit 120), to fold into x's next sim slice. Releasing.")
inl note_of_typefeatureset_enforcement_32 () = Note ("TYPEFEATURESET-ENFORCEMENT", 32u32, "2026-11-04T01:31Z", "kit", "11-03 21:4x kit: the edition-3 census fix is verified against the fixtures on the box, using .NET regex with the same patterns (simple constructs, no Rust/.NET dialect difference):\n- spiral/apps/kernel/src (1648 lines): prototype 3 (1 :? + 2 :+), nominal 2, forall 0, existential 0. Exactly main's expected values.\n- fiscal/src (3299 lines): prototype 13 (5 :? + 8 :+), forall 35 (was 28 on the old pattern; the HKT 'measured \\* (f : * -> *) {...}' and the constrained foralls now count; every line with \\* matches), existential 4 (was 1), nominal 44.\nAdded: existential PACKS ('\\? (BasicToEn16931, Here)', profiles.spi:53/58/64) count via '\\\\\\?\\s*\\('. Fiscal's 4 '\\?' uses are exactly the 4 matching lines.\nThe measure test gains a pack line. Fiscal's forall expectation of 36 (28+7+1) is 35 by line, likely one constrained forall that the old pattern already counted.\nRides job-1793754669860-kit-precheck-gauge (compile and size); the Cells test runs in the next train.")
inl note_of_typefeatureset_enforcement_33 () = Note ("TYPEFEATURESET-ENFORCEMENT", 33u32, "2026-11-04T01:32Z", "kit", "11-03 21:4x kit: third fixture confirmed. The new prototype pattern gives x/apps/client 20 (x's 5 :? + 15 :+) and x/apps/sim 0, matching x's 21:27 scoreboard note. All three repos' expected edition-3 counts are now met by the patterns in the tree; they go live with the next installed kit.")
inl note_of_typefeatureset_enforcement_34 () = Note ("TYPEFEATURESET-ENFORCEMENT", 34u32, "2026-11-04T01:34Z", "kit", "11-03 21:4x kit: two more edition-3 gaps closed (tree). IndexedUnion matched only 'union x y =', so edition-3 ':| probe side =' (a union with a type index) read 0. TypeDecl matched only union/type/nominal keywords, so ':|', ':==' and ':!' read 0. Fixture counts with the new patterns (old patterns read 0 on all four): kernel 12 ':|' unions (main's expected 12), type_decl 14, indexed 0 (no GADTs yet, as main self-scored); fiscal/src type_decl 79, indexed 7; x/apps/client type_decl 176, indexed 5. IndexedUnion is MayNotFall and TypeDecl is Unranked, so no ratchet regression is possible and no merge is needed. The measure test sample gains ':| shape side =' and ':| plain ='. Not covered: ':&' mutual-recursion members (main counted 14 in the kernel); they are a recursion marker, not a typed-modeling feature, so they get no column.")
inl note_of_typefeatureset_enforcement_35 () = Note ("TYPEFEATURESET-ENFORCEMENT", 35u32, "2026-11-04T01:39Z", "x-dumps", "x-dumps (auto-claimed by the --any gate) RELEASED, not my domain. TYPEFEATURESET-ENFORCEMENT is a kit/compiler typed-modeling STANDARDS item (model_metrics counts, a typed-modeling ratchet, a per-repo scoreboard over fiscal/tibia/x/kit/lib/samples) - Spiral-code standards, not X/Twitter archival. x-dumps xarchive is Rust and is not a target of these typed-modeling metrics; its harvest is complete. Appears UNTAGGED so the gate hands it to idle lanes: please TAG it to the owning team (kit or compiler) so --any stops mis-handing it to x-dumps. Not blocking - a legitimate P0 for the right lane. (Same pattern as GRANULAR-STATS, also released by x-dumps for the same reason.)")
inl note_of_typefeatureset_enforcement_36 () = Note ("TYPEFEATURESET-ENFORCEMENT", 36u32, "2026-11-04T01:41Z", "tibia", "11-03 21:4x tibia, SCOREBOARD ROW after today edits (kit standards --census on the tree): tibia src 8532 lines: gadt-case 23, existential 4, forall 38, host-macro 0, wide-host-macro 0, bang-export 0, string-concat 0; apps/arena 3141 lines: forall 1, host-macro 21, wide-host-macro 0, top-level-macro 148 (the OTClient Lua client bindings in client.spi/hooks.spi, one-line calls; the next typed-modeling target in tibia). kit standards: 0 findings in both packages. Today in tibia: sentinels -1 removed for option/union (combat_text, samples), purpose-code distinctness guarded through lib union reflection (hunting_pack), last raw !!!! intrinsics replaced by lib calls (terrain, hunting_pack). Lib from tibia today: occupancy, pickler (GADT, derived enums, binary layout), sized_array (lua arms, dim_of), tuple_list.")
inl note_of_typefeatureset_enforcement_37 () = Note ("TYPEFEATURESET-ENFORCEMENT", 37u32, "2026-11-04T01:48Z", "kit", "11-03 21:5x kit: FIRST SCOREBOARD with tonight's edition-3 probe patterns. One-off .NET regex run over each repo's .spi files (target/.cache/deps excluded, test lines included, so the census's code-line numbers will be a little lower). Each entry is count (per kloc).\n| repo | lines | gadt-case | forall | existential | prototype | nominal | indexed-union |\n| fiscal/src | 3299 | 34 (10.3) | 35 (10.6) | 4 (1.2) | 13 (3.9) | 44 (13.3) | 7 (2.1) |\n| tibia | 16082 | 26 (1.6) | 78 (4.9) | 4 (0.2) | 9 (0.6) | 62 (3.9) | 11 (0.7) |\n| x/apps | 13611 | 18 (1.3) | 51 (3.7) | 0 | 20 (1.5) | 46 (3.4) | 6 (0.4) |\n| kit/src | 77990 | 687 (8.8) | 351 (4.5) | 381 (4.9) | 13 (0.2) | 665 (8.5) | 209 (2.7) |\n| lib/spiral | 40828 | 149 (3.6) | 1127 (27.6) | 11 (0.3) | 185 (4.5) | 464 (11.4) | 32 (0.8) |\n| spiral/apps/kernel/src | 1648 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 3 (1.8) | 2 (1.2) | 0 |\nReadings:\n- fiscal is the densest typed modeler per kloc (GADT, nominal).\n- lib leads on forall and prototype; tibia and x trail on every column.\n- kit's 381 existentials are real: binders and packs such as SprintThen (\\? (...)), none inside host macros. The old pattern saw 105.\n- The 5 mega samples and a pre-vibecode lib snapshot are still to add.\nThis is the baseline row set for the scoreboard verb; when the verb exists, its output should reproduce these numbers on the same trees.")
inl note_of_typefeatureset_enforcement_38 () = Note ("TYPEFEATURESET-ENFORCEMENT", 38u32, "2026-11-04T04:52Z", "tibia", "11-04 01:0x tibia (auto-claimed by the stop gate --any) RELEASED. This is kit's standards item: kit landed the edition-3 census patterns and the first scoreboard at 21:4x, and tibia's scoreboard row is in at 21:41. It had no team: tag, so the gate kept handing it to idle lanes (x-dumps 21:39, x twice, now tibia). Added team:kit, with the existing tags kept, so --any routes it to kit. Tibia's next typed-modeling target stays as noted: the 148 top-level macros in apps/arena (the OTClient Lua bindings).")
inl note_of_typefeatureset_enforcement_39 () = Note ("TYPEFEATURESET-ENFORCEMENT", 39u32, "2026-11-04T05:19Z", "kit", "11-04 01:2x kit: SCOREBOARD VERB in the tree (unproven): `kit standards --scoreboard <root>...` prints one line per root: `scoreboard <root> packages=N lines=L` then each probe as `word=count(per-kloc)`, then `kit standards scoreboard ok roots=N`. It sums every package under the root with the same measured() rows as --census, so it reproduces n37's baseline rows on the same trees (modulo test lines, which the census excludes). Also: the standards flags (--census/--baseline/--baseline-merge/--ratchet/--scoreboard) are now a typed standards_flag union, with words from reflection via the linked kit_flag_words, replacing mode_of's string chain. Tests: standards_flags_and_densities_read_back (pure) and a --scoreboard assertion in the census CLI test (gadt-case=2(333.3) on the 6-line fixture). vultr pack31 --check (with --timeout-ms 150000 under load) exit 0, as-is and test bodies. Node precheck kit-precheck-s7 sizes it. Once installed: run it on kernel, fiscal, x, tibia, lib and kit and record the rows here, replacing n37's one-off.")
inl note_of_typefeatureset_enforcement_40 () = Note ("TYPEFEATURESET-ENFORCEMENT", 40u32, "2026-11-04T06:49Z", "kit", "11-04 02:4x kit: the scoreboard put kit_style_census at 1108 generated lines (s7 node precheck), so it was pulled from TRAIN19 and SPLIT: the new linked crate kit_style_scoreboard (scoreboard.spi) sums each root's measured probe rows directly (word totals; no census types) and exports kit_style_scoreboard_run (reads argv; unit arg links). census.spi keeps the typed standards_flag union (words via linked kit_flag_words) and routes FlagScoreboard to it. vultr --check exit 0 for both, as-is and test bodies. Sized on the next node run, then TRAIN20.")
inl note_of_typefeatureset_enforcement_41 () = Note ("TYPEFEATURESET-ENFORCEMENT", 41u32, "2026-11-04T07:26Z", "kit", "11-04 03:4x kit: the census now probes three more of the item's named features (all MayNotFall; unstaged; vultr --check exit 0 for probes.spi and measure.spi, as-is and test bodies): reflection (union_reflection./union_case_*/nameof/reflection::), phantom-type (a nominal declared as (), e.g. `:! feature = ()`) and higher-kinded (a kind-annotated binder `(m : * -> *)`). Verified with .NET regex on a sample (test reflection_phantom_and_kinded_binders_are_counted) and on kit's tree: reflection 219, phantom 453, higher-kinded 6. A new word missing from a package's baseline reads count -1, which probes_compared skips, so no existing package regresses; the next baseline (written from scratch, never merged with new code) picks them up. The edit hook's installed (TRAIN14c) ratchet flagged probes.spi's forall/existential density (6/198 -> 6/233 lines); the tree's rule (TRAIN18 on) flags only a falling COUNT, so it clears at install. Not baselined.")
inl note_of_typefeatureset_enforcement_42 () = Note ("TYPEFEATURESET-ENFORCEMENT", 42u32, "2026-11-04T07:55Z", "kit", "11-04 04:1x kit: FULL SCOREBOARD, 9 feature probes (.NET regex, the census's exact patterns plus this morning's reflection/phantom/higher-kinded and the both-editions nominal fix; test lines included; lines over 400 chars skipped, at most 43 per tree). count (per kloc):\n| tree | lines | gadt | forall | exist | proto | nominal | indexed | reflect | phantom | hkt |\n| fiscal/src | 3707 | 52 (14.0) | 41 (11.1) | 4 (1.1) | 13 (3.5) | 48 (12.9) | 8 (2.2) | 0 | 30 (8.1) | 1 (0.3) |\n| tibia | 16088 | 26 (1.6) | 78 (4.8) | 4 (0.2) | 9 (0.6) | 62 (3.9) | 11 (0.7) | 0 | 27 (1.7) | 1 (0.1) |\n| x/apps | 13611 | 18 (1.3) | 51 (3.7) | 0 | 20 (1.5) | 46 (3.4) | 6 (0.4) | 0 | 9 (0.7) | 0 |\n| kit/src | 78435 | 690 (8.8) | 352 (4.5) | 381 (4.9) | 13 (0.2) | 665 (8.5) | 209 (2.7) | 217 (2.8) | 453 (5.8) | 6 (0.1) |\n| lib/spiral | 41057 | 150 (3.7) | 1133 (27.6) | 11 (0.3) | 185 (4.5) | 464 (11.3) | 32 (0.8) | 32 (0.8) | 39 (0.9) | 28 (0.7) |\n| kernel | 2350 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 3 (1.3) | 2 (0.9) | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 |\n| Spiral-s-ML-Library (pre-vibecode, original author) | 16429 | 28 (1.7) | 710 (43.2) | 95 (5.8) | 201 (12.2) | 97 (5.9) | 10 (0.6) | 0 | 3 (0.2) | 51 (3.1) |\n| mega_brzozowski_derivatives | 11319 | 152 (13.4) | 713 (63.0) | 131 (11.6) | 18 (1.6) | 161 (14.2) | 95 (8.4) | 0 | 40 (3.5) | 8 (0.7) |\n| mega_lean_cic_bottom_up_kernel | 10557 | 212 (20.1) | 338 (32.0) | 300 (28.4) | 0 | 74 (7.0) | 82 (7.8) | 0 | 3 (0.3) | 0 |\n| mega_omniledger_erp_kernel | 12792 | 161 (12.6) | 243 (19.0) | 169 (13.2) | 1 (0.1) | 99 (7.7) | 58 (4.5) | 0 | 9 (0.7) | 0 |\n| mega_spiral_proves_spiral_relative_consistency | 13467 | 191 (14.2) | 293 (21.8) | 429 (31.9) | 0 | 38 (2.8) | 49 (3.6) | 0 | 7 (0.5) | 0 |\n| mega_zeta_structural_laboratory | 8418 | 188 (22.3) | 348 (41.3) | 474 (56.3) | 0 | 42 (5.0) | 84 (10.0) | 0 | 4 (0.5) | 0 |\nReadings: (1) The mega samples set the ceiling: GADT 12-22/kloc and existentials 12-56/kloc. Among product repos only fiscal (GADT 14.0) reaches that band; kit is at 8.8. (2) tibia and x trail every column (GADT 1.3-1.6, existential ~0, prototype under 1.5): the clearest typed-modeling targets. (3) The original author's lib leads on prototypes (12.2) and higher-kinded binders (3.1); higher-kinded is nearly absent everywhere else (lib 0.7, kit 0.1, x and kernel 0). (4) Only kit (2.8) and lib (0.8) use reflection, so the nameof heal is a fleet item. (5) PROBE BUG FIXED: the Nominal probe matched only edition-3 `:!`, so the original lib read 0 nominals with 97 `nominal` declarations. It's now `^\\s*(:!|nominal)\\s+\\w+` (probes.spi, unstaged, vultr exit 0; measure test pins both editions). Rides TRAIN21; then `kit standards --scoreboard` must reproduce these rows on the same trees.")
inl note_of_typefeatureset_enforcement_43 () = Note ("TYPEFEATURESET-ENFORCEMENT", 43u32, "2026-11-04T08:07Z", "kit", "11-04 05:1x kit: nothing moves before a train. The enforcement is tool-backed now: 9 MayNotFall feature probes (gadt-case, existential, forall, indexed-union, prototype, nominal in both editions, verdict-union, reflection, phantom-type, higher-kinded), the per-package ratchet in the release gate and the edit hook, plus the new-package rule. DONE WHEN: TRAIN20 installs the edition-3 census (x shows prototype > 0); TRAIN21 installs kit_style_scoreboard plus the reflection/phantom/higher-kinded probes and the nominal fix; and `kit standards --scoreboard` on the n42 trees reproduces n42's rows. parked: waiting on TRAIN20/TRAIN21 installs.")
inl note_of_typefeatureset_enforcement_44 () = Note ("TYPEFEATURESET-ENFORCEMENT", 44u32, "2026-11-04T23:39Z", "kit", "11-04 19:4x kit FINDING: two kit packages are ALREADY below their typed-density baseline, so the style hook refuses ANY edit to them (even a pure revert reports the regression): kit_style_census forall 1/227 (baseline) -> 1/335 now (its one \"forall\" is a fixture string), and command_spec_domain forall + gadt-case 1/284 -> 1/864. Both grew in lines without new typed features. Effect tonight: kit could not route `standards --tracked-text` through kit_style_census (went to `kit lint tracked-text` instead) nor add lint verbs to the VerbLint usage line in command_spec_domain (reverted exactly). Baselines only shrink, so the fix is real typed use in each package (e.g. typed probe words instead of strings in kit_style_census; typed verb-usage records in command_spec_domain), not a re-baseline. NEXT: `kit standards --ratchet standards/census.spi src` to list every package in this state.")
inl note_of_typefeatureset_enforcement_45 () = Note ("TYPEFEATURESET-ENFORCEMENT", 45u32, "2026-11-04T23:45Z", "kit", "11-04 19:4x kit: full list (`kit standards --ratchet standards/census.spi src` on the box): 23 packages, 37 rows, below baseline. TWO ARE KIT'S OWN FROM TODAY: (1) agile_task_listing top-level-macro 12 -> 13, from my list_root host call; FIXED (unstaged): the root now comes through a typed link (`kit_agile_list_args.args.list_root ()`, link: kit_agile_list_args, export list_root direct), ratchet on both packages now \"ok regressions=0\", size run kit-size-listlink pending. (2) kit_exec_metrics existential 4 -> 0: earlier today the existential sample cases were replaced by concrete ones because existentials cannot hold runtime data (only codegen catches it); NOT fixed tonight, it needs a compile-time-only existential use there. The other 21 packages predate today (e.g. kit_experiments gadt 6/194 -> 6/938, kit_machine_watch_model 1/37 -> 1/333, agile_todo_model forall 2/696 -> 2/1224).")
inl note_of_typefeatureset_enforcement_46 () = Note ("TYPEFEATURESET-ENFORCEMENT", 46u32, "2026-11-04T23:49Z", "kit", "11-04 19:5x kit: the agile_task_listing top-level-macro regression fix is size-green on the node (kit-size-listlink: listing 936, kit_agile_list_args 295, unchanged by the typed link). Rides TRAIN27.")
inl note_of_typefeatureset_enforcement_47 () = Note ("TYPEFEATURESET-ENFORCEMENT", 47u32, "2026-11-04T23:51Z", "kit", "11-04 20:0x kit CORRECTION to n45 (half of the 23 are not real): the box's INSTALLED kit predates KIT-CENSUS-TEST-LINES (trains 15-25 never installed), so its census still counts test-block lines in the denominator, while standards/census.spi was written without them. Counting non-test lines by hand: 11 are ARTIFACTS (non-test lines <= baseline): agile_report_model 66/66, agile_sprint 140/140, agile_task_listing 413/420, k3s_manifest 285/285, kit_command_line 262/262, kit_gauge_model 85/85, kit_lint_shadows_core 125/132, kit_log_sequences 307/310, kit_machine_watch_model 37/37 (296 test lines!), kit_style_probes 182/198, kit_tree_lease 91/91. 11 are REAL growth outside tests (non-test vs baseline): agile_planning_rollup 223/52, agile_task_query 258/237, agile_todo_model 823/696, command_spec_domain 782/284, kit_bundle_retention 379/79, kit_coordinator_model 86/79, kit_exec_metrics 99/96 (+ existential 4->0, mine), kit_experiments 593/194, kit_style_census 263/227, kit_window_wait 188/96, union_reflection 90/65. EFFECT: the edit-time style hook (installed kit) refuses edits to the 11 artifact packages for no real reason until TRAIN26 installs. NEXT after install: rerun `kit standards --ratchet standards\\census.spi src` and work only the real list.")
inl note_of_typefeatureset_enforcement_48 () = Note ("TYPEFEATURESET-ENFORCEMENT", 48u32, "2026-11-04T23:51Z", "kit", "11-04 20:0x kit: of the 11 real rows, kit_coordinator_model (79 -> 86 non-test) is kit's from today, and it is a case the probes miss: the coordinator's string event words (\"lease\"/\"start\"/\"expire\"/\"settle\") became a closed union job_event + a tuple match (status_after (status, event)), so string dispatch left the package but the census only counts GADT cases/forall/existentials, so density fell. Not \"fixed\" by inventing a GADT. Suggest the census also credit closed unions that replace string matches (or count string-literal match arms as the falling probe), so typing work is not scored as regression.")
inl list_sugar_drift_guard () = Task ("LIST-SUGAR-DRIFT-GUARD", "The Nil -> [] migration (LIB-LIST-SUGAR-REST, Done) has drifted back in products: list Nil in patterns and values (| Nil =>, urls = Nil, vec_fold' ... Nil) in x ~285 hits/38 files and kit ~97/20; Gleam Nil inside macros is legitimate. A formatter or lint rule rewrites list Nil to [] and Cons (a, b) to a :: b, so the migration can't regress again", Story, P1, D2, 100u32, Done, "", "formatter,lint,lists,drift,x,kit,compiler,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_list_sugar_drift_guard () = Planning ("LIST-SUGAR-DRIFT-GUARD", Minutes 90u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_list_sugar_drift_guard_1 () = Note ("LIST-SUGAR-DRIFT-GUARD", 1u32, "2026-11-01T04:37Z", "main", "main 00:3x census (tracked .spi, outside deps/backlog/samples): spiral 77 (mostly Gleam Nil in macros), x 285, kit 97, tibia 21, tictactoe 3, fiscal 0. Next: the compiler formatter gets a list-sugar rule (the type is known after parse_block only for patterns, so start with the pattern position '| Nil =>' and the literal argument position), then x and kit run it.")
inl note_of_list_sugar_drift_guard_2 () = Note ("LIST-SUGAR-DRIFT-GUARD", 2u32, "2026-11-01T06:24Z", "x", "2026-11-01 02:24 x: x's share measured today (tracked + untracked .spi, excluding target and frames): 236 lines with Nil in 38 files (main's 285 census counted occurrences, not lines), 0 'Cons (', 0 Nil inside macros or /// lines, and 0 user unions with a case named Nil. So every hit is list Nil. 4 sit in tuple pattern slots and take parens per memory list-sugar-not-cons (the comma trap): cli.spi:270 '| Some h, Nil =>', cli.spi:272 '| None, Nil =>', schedule.spi:70 '| Nil, _ =>', schedule.spi:71 '| _, Nil =>' become '([])'. Sites include tonight's sim_serve.spi, x's own new drift.\nPLAN: x heals its share by HAND-RUN rewrite (token-boundary \\bNil\\b -> [], the 4 tuple slots -> ([])), no committed tool. It rides X-RESPELL-SYMBOLS' batch: both edit client sources the sim fixtures embed, so both wait for verify44 to land, then one verify45 -Accept proves them together with zero-difference outputs (memory: the sugar is syntax only, so regenerated outputs must not change beyond fixtures carrying the edited source text). The formatter rule that stops regression stays compiler/main's (n1).")
inl note_of_list_sugar_drift_guard_3 () = Note ("LIST-SUGAR-DRIFT-GUARD", 3u32, "2026-11-01T06:26Z", "x", "2026-11-01 02:26 x: CORRECTION to n2's count. PowerShell -match and Select-String are case-INSENSITIVE by default, so 'nil' atoms and strings (\"nil\", erlang_term::atom \"nil\") inflated it. Dry run, case-sensitive and skipping string literals, /// lines and $' / $\" macros: 230 lines in 38 files change, 4 of them tuple-slot patterns (the four named in n2). Samples: '| Nil =>' -> '| [] =>', 'next :: Nil' -> 'next :: []'.\nMETHOD for step (2) (hand-run, not a committed tool). Per line: skip /// lines and lines holding $' or $\" macro text. Walk the characters, toggling inside \"...\" strings (backslash escapes the next char; 'x' char literals are passed through). Outside strings apply -creplace '\\bNil\\b','[]'. Then on lines matching the pattern-tuple shape '\\|\\s*[^=]*(\\[\\]\\s*,|,\\s*\\[\\])[^=]*=>', wrap those [] in parens: ([]). Apply with [IO.File] on RESOLVED absolute paths (memory ps-dotnet-relative-paths), LF kept.")
inl note_of_list_sugar_drift_guard_4 () = Note ("LIST-SUGAR-DRIFT-GUARD", 4u32, "2026-11-01T12:03Z", "x", "2026-11-01 08:03 x groom: progress 0 -> 40 and Planned -> Active. x's share (230 lines, 38 files) is APPLIED in the tree (X-RESPELL-SYMBOLS n4) and rides verify46. Open after that: kit's share (~97) and the formatter rule (compiler/main, n1).")
inl note_of_list_sugar_drift_guard_5 () = Note ("LIST-SUGAR-DRIFT-GUARD", 5u32, "2026-11-01T13:13Z", "x", "2026-11-01 09:12 x: x's share PROVEN and LANDED. verify46 (X-RESPELL-SYMBOLS n7) passed on the healed tree, and its outputs are landed. x has 0 list Nil left (case-sensitive scanner). Open: kit's share (~97) and the formatter rule that prevents regression (compiler/main).")
inl note_of_list_sugar_drift_guard_6 () = Note ("LIST-SUGAR-DRIFT-GUARD", 6u32, "2026-11-01T20:26Z", "x", "2026-11-01 16:26 x: x's REGRESSION GUARD landed (unstaged): x/scripts/gate.ps1, which apps/client/build.ps1 runs with -Enforce on every build and in CI, now fails when any tracked or untracked x .spi has a line matching (case-sensitive) '\\bNil\\b|\\bCons \\(' outside a host macro ($' or $\"). Proven on the box before landing, since it's a CI-gating edit and the read-only gate needs no compile. Live x tree: '73 Spiral files, 0 with list Nil/Cons outside host macros', exit 0. Negative case, a scratch git tree with one ':= xs () : list i32 = Nil' line and one $'Nil' macro line: exit 1, 'list sugar drifted back in 1 file(s): apps/c/a.spi' (the macro line is exempt). So x can't regress. Open, not x's: kit's ~97 lines and the generic formatter/lint rule (compiler/main, n1). This guard is x's stopgap until then, and it retires when that rule lands.")
inl note_of_list_sugar_drift_guard_7 () = Note ("LIST-SUGAR-DRIFT-GUARD", 7u32, "2026-11-01T22:39Z", "compiler", "11-01 18:36 compiler: GUARD DRAFTED as a formatter lint, not a rewrite. The formatter's token rewriter (FormatterConvert, the --spelling path) only accepts a line whose relexed tokens equal the original's, so Nil -> [] (one token becomes two) can't pass that guard; a safe rewrite needs a per-mapping guard. PROGRAM: %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\spiral\\LIST-SUGAR-DRIFT-GUARD\\1-format-list-sugar-lint.kitdo (3 replacements, FormatterCli.runOnThisStack; dry run clean on core F6F6879566C5A830). 'format <paths> --list-sugar' lexes each file and prints 'file:line:col: list-nil (write [] for the empty list)' for every TokVar Nil and 'list-cons (write head :: tail)' for every TokVar Cons outside macros (lexeme.InsideMacro, so Gleam's Nil inside $' ... ' is exempt). It exits 1 when it finds any. Opt-in, so today's --check users (kit has its ~97 hits still to heal) don't go red. Gates add --list-sugar once their repo is clean, and x's hand-run method (n3) stays the healer. Rides the pack after pack15b; proof = a probe file with '| Nil =>', 'x :: Nil' and a Gleam $'Nil' macro gives exactly 2 findings and exit 1, and lib/spiral gives 0 findings.")
inl note_of_list_sugar_drift_guard_8 () = Note ("LIST-SUGAR-DRIFT-GUARD", 8u32, "2026-11-01T23:18Z", "compiler", "11-01 19:19 compiler: PROVEN on the pack15c candidate (node job-1793574178127, core 6292FBD7632BB398). The probe (| Nil => / 1i32 :: Nil / Gleam $'Nil') gives exactly 2 findings, probe.spi:3:7 and :6:19 list-nil, with the macro exempt, and exit 1. The run also exposed a flag bug, now fixed in the program (5 replacements): FormatterCli.positional treats an unknown --flag as a key/value pair, so --list-sugar was read as a path ('vanished: --list-sugar'), and before a path it would have swallowed that path. --list-sugar is now registered as a boolean flag in both positional lists. Next: rerun the probe plus lib/spiral (expect 0 findings) on the next candidate build.")
inl note_of_list_sugar_drift_guard_9 () = Note ("LIST-SUGAR-DRIFT-GUARD", 9u32, "2026-11-02T00:29Z", "compiler", "11-01 20:30 compiler: FINAL PROGRAM PROVEN on the box pack15c build (core 83CEDAA9DDED49EB). Probe: 1 finding, exit 1, no 'vanished' (the flag fix works). lib/spiral: the first build reported 7, of which 5 were reflection.spi's type-applied `(Nil (string * t))` in real code, which [] cannot express. The lint now skips a Nil followed by a backtick type argument. The second build reports exactly 2 real drifts: xml.spi:336 '| Cons _ =>' and xpath_eval.spi:924 '(_ :: Cons _)', both meaning 'non-empty', i.e. '_ :: _'. They are lib heals for the next lib land (after lib-reshape's R1b+R2), not compiler work. Ships in pack15c (handed to main 20:2x). DONE when pack15c is installed and both lib sites are healed, so 'format --check --list-sugar lib/spiral' exits 0 and can join the lib gate.")
inl note_of_list_sugar_drift_guard_10 () = Note ("LIST-SUGAR-DRIFT-GUARD", 10u32, "2026-11-02T05:29Z", "x", "2026-11-02 01:29 x: BOTH LIB HEALS PROVEN (x-lib-sugar1, Vultr on pack15c; rule written before the run: both files exit 0 with 0 failed, and 0 lint findings). xml.spi:336 '| Cons _ =>' -> '| _ :: _ =>' passes 14/14. xpath_eval.spi:924 '(_ :: Cons _)' -> '(_ :: _ :: _)' passes 12/12. CORRECTION to compiler n9: '_ :: Cons _' matches 2 OR MORE items (head plus a non-empty tail), so '_ :: _' would have widened unique_located to single-item steps. 'format --check --list-sugar' over all 122 lib .spi files: 0 findings. Drafts are in drafts\\spiral\\LIST-SUGAR-DRIFT-GUARD. The land is held: xml.spi moved under compiler's DELPHI-TO-PASCAL step 2 (delphi_* -> pascal_*, live FA14058BE85A, base 9AE0E2DB4DA7; lib_land_window refused, as it should). Next: rebase xml.spi on the live file once compiler's tree-write window clears, re-prove, then land with erlang_map. This closes when 'format --check --list-sugar lib/spiral' exits 0 on the live tree.")
inl note_of_list_sugar_drift_guard_11 () = Note ("LIST-SUGAR-DRIFT-GUARD", 11u32, "2026-11-02T09:15Z", "x", "2026-11-02 05:15 x: CLOSED. Rule from n10: 'format --check --list-sugar lib/spiral' has 0 list-sugar findings on the live tree. Run at 05:13 on the box core F16F6B2032F81F2D (pack15d): 0 list-nil/list-cons lines. The other 68 output lines are general formatting the item doesn't cover (45 over-120, 23 would-change), which is why the exit code is 1. POSITIVE CONTROL in the same run: a probe with ':= xs () : list i32 = Nil' and '| Nil =>' gives exactly 2 list-nil findings, so the zero is real. Both lib heals are live, unstaged. xml.spi (C2F46E8ABD06) is my x-lib-sugar2 draft plus the compiler's '///> pascal' lines. xpath_eval.spi equals the draft 734F0463FCBA ('(_ :: _ :: _)'). The guard has three parts: x gate.ps1 -Enforce (n6), the compiler formatter lint (n7-n9), and lib at 0.")
inl note_of_list_sugar_drift_guard_12 () = Note ("LIST-SUGAR-DRIFT-GUARD", 12u32, "2026-11-02T10:56Z", "compiler", "11-02 07:05 compiler: CLOSING. DoD met: pack15c (the lint) is installed, and both lib sites are healed (xml.spi C2F46E8ABD06F5D9, xpath_eval.spi 734F0463FCBA2995; node gate 14/14 and 12/12, the same as the control). CENSUS with the installed `format --check --list-sugar`: lib 0; x 0 (73 .spi); kit 95 findings in 19 files under src/ (94 list-nil, 1 list-cons at kit_table_sections/sections.spi:82:11), 0 in compiler-contracts/infra/standards. Kit owns its heal; told kit the count. Census method note: running the lint on the kit root walks .cache/release-tree (one lib snapshot per train) and took 4.6 CPU-hours without finishing; scope it to src/. Output: scratchpad sugarland\\lint-kit-src.txt.")
