inl note_of_x_respell_symbols_1 () = Note ("X-RESPELL-SYMBOLS", 1u32, "2026-11-01T06:03Z", "x", "2026-11-01 02:03 x: x's share of the symbol-respell round (POSTABILITY-ONE-SOURCE n9 step 4; tibia's respell found the test -> :# filter trap). Measured with no writes: SpiralCompiler format --spelling symbols --check flags all 72 tracked and untracked x .spi files (excluding target and frames). Scratch conversions of tlds.spi and sim_serve.spi show the ONLY change is 'test' -> ':#' on test-block headers (3 and 1 lines), since x already spells members '::'.\nConsumers checked:\n- x's xc splitter cuts cells at column 0, not by keyword, so it's unaffected.\n- Cells matches 'test|:#' (document.spi:566 and :610, memo.ex:400, accept.ex:93).\n- x's own filters: apps/sim/build.ps1:114 is fixed to '^(test|:#) ' (X-COMPAT-VIA-CELLS n5). Nothing else in x matches the keyword.\nORDER, required by memory x-sim-fixtures-embed-client-sources (sim fixtures embed client sources, so non-Accept sim builds fail until -Accept regenerates them):\n(1) verify44 (running, pre-respell tree, -Accept) passes and lands.\n(2) Apply the respell to live x: format --spelling symbols over the 72 files, unstaged.\n(3) verify45 -Accept on the respelled tree. Pass rule, written now: exit 0; x verify's five steps exit 0; every notebook passes; the cell-budget pre-check reports 0 findings in both trees; the corpus filter still finds 73 lib files.\n(4) Land verify45's regenerated fixtures and outputs with -Apply.\nUntil (3) lands, no non-Accept sim proof runs on a respelled tree.")
inl note_of_x_respell_symbols_2 () = Note ("X-RESPELL-SYMBOLS", 2u32, "2026-11-01T06:24Z", "x", "2026-11-01 02:24 x: scope widened, packing two edits into one proof (memory pack-queued-builds): step (2) applies BOTH the symbol respell (test -> :#) and x's list-sugar heal (Nil -> [], 4 tuple slots -> ([]), LIST-SUGAR-DRIFT-GUARD x-share note), then verify45 -Accept proves both. Added to verify45's pass rule: outputs regenerated from the respelled and healed tree differ from verify44's only in fixtures that embed client source text.")
inl note_of_x_respell_symbols_3 () = Note ("X-RESPELL-SYMBOLS", 3u32, "2026-11-01T06:49Z", "x", "2026-11-01 02:49 x: STEP (2) RUNBOOK, ready for when verify44 lands:\n(a) In C:\\home\\git\\x: files = git ls-files --cached --others --exclude-standard '*.spi', minus target/ and frames/, resolved to absolute paths (72 files today).\n(b) Respell: & <spiral-bin>\\toolchains\\dotnet\\dotnet.exe <spiral-bin>\\bin\\single-flight\\SpiralCompiler\\Release\\net11.0\\SpiralCompiler.dll format --spelling symbols @files. Expect 72 'formatted:' lines, each changing only test -> :# (scratch-verified on tlds.spi and sim_serve.spi).\n(c) List-sugar heal: LIST-SUGAR-DRIFT-GUARD n3's METHOD (case-sensitive \\bNil\\b -> [] outside strings and macros, 4 tuple slots -> ([])), 230 lines in 38 files.\n(d) Checks before any proof: format --spelling symbols --check @files reports 0 would-change. A case-sensitive census shows 0 list Nil left. The corpus filter in apps/sim/build.ps1:114 still finds 73 lib files. Assert-CellBudget on apps/client and apps/sim/spi gives 0 findings (on the box once no heavy-build or compile window is held, else on the node).\n(e) verify45 via x/scripts/node-run.ps1 -Mode verify -Core <current core> with n1's pass rule plus n2's 'outputs differ only in source-embedding fixtures', then -Land with -Apply. verify45 also gives the first node check of X-RUN-METRICS' raw-pair rows (n6) and the cell_budget names (X-COMPAT-VIA-CELLS n4).")
inl note_of_x_respell_symbols_4 () = Note ("X-RESPELL-SYMBOLS", 4u32, "2026-11-01T08:59Z", "x", "2026-11-01 04:59 x: STEP (2) APPLIED and STEP (3) LAUNCHED (all unstaged):\n- Respell: format --spelling symbols over 72 files, 72 formatted. The recheck reports 0 would-change, 0 'test ' headers left and 360 ':#' headers. git diff confirms the respell changed only headers (the other diff lines are the earlier unstaged raw-pair metric edits).\n- List-sugar heal: 230 lines in 38 files (n3's method). The 4 tuple slots became ([]), and the same scanner finds 0 list Nil left.\n- Stub retirement prep (PS1-TO-SPIRAL n38): e2e.ps1 defaults to the sim, with XSIM_SERVER=stub selecting the stub (start wait 120 s for the sim, 60 s for the stub). scripts/build.ps1 and verify.ps1's plain path build the sim BEFORE the client. e2e-parity.ps1 sets its arms explicitly. Client and sim READMEs updated.\n- Box checks (no windows held): corpus filter 73 files, and the guarded Assert-CellBudget gives 0 findings in apps/client and apps/sim/spi (the guard's first box run).\n- Pack 12 was installed at 03:15 (core 3445FAA05588C96E on box and node), so verify45 is x's first proof on it.\nverify45 = job-1793523554712 (xqueue1, -Accept). Pass rule: n1 plus n2's fixture clause plus PS1-TO-SPIRAL n38's (client e2e passes with the sim as default). On PASS: land with -Apply, then close this item, LIST-SUGAR-DRIFT-GUARD's x share, SIM-X-FEATURE-PARITY and port 2's first half.")
inl note_of_x_respell_symbols_5 () = Note ("X-RESPELL-SYMBOLS", 5u32, "2026-11-01T10:29Z", "x", "2026-11-01 06:29 x: verify45 (job-1793523554712, 88 min, pack 12 core 3445FAA05588C96E) by the rule: FAIL. Sim build exit 0 (all 23 sim notebooks, cell-budget 0 findings). The client build failed at the e2e: tutorial step 'xc post-thread --resume 9101 checks/checks.spi' exited 4 ('resumed after post 3 of 9'). Other steps were DIFFERENT: xc thread 3000001, the post-thread --dry-run of checks.spi, xc thread 9003, and both resumes.\nCAUSE: the respell changed checks/checks.spi's first cell ('test checks static =' -> ':# checks static ='). apps/client/e2e/world.json is HAND-MAINTAINED (10-25) and seeds half-posted threads 9101 and 9201 with the OLD c1 text. Resume compares the posted prefix with the file's split, sees c1 differ and refuses with exit 4. That is the correct safety behaviour, and the seed was stale. The DIFFERENT preview and thread steps are README tutorial outputs, which e2e accept regenerated (5 of 37 accepted).\nLESSON, recorded on this item: memory x-sim-fixtures-embed-client-sources covers GENERATED fixtures (sim accept rewrites them), but world.json is a hand-written fixture that also embeds client source text. -Accept can't fix it. A respell or heal of client sources must grep the hand-written e2e fixtures for the changed text.\nFIX (unstaged): world.json lines 99 and 102 c1 now read ':# checks static =' (2 occurrences, plain text swap). c2 and c3 already match the file. 9202's 'edited' text is intentional (the refusal test). No other hand-written fixture embeds a 'test ' header. thread-oauth1.expected.json is sim-generated and regenerates under accept.\nverify45's outputs are NOT landed. verify46 relaunches with the same rule.")
inl note_of_x_respell_symbols_6 () = Note ("X-RESPELL-SYMBOLS", 6u32, "2026-11-01T12:36Z", "x", "2026-11-01 08:36 x: ITEM UPGRADE (round 4). Fields: tags gain team:x; 90 / light / agent stand. DEFINITION OF DONE: verify46 (job-1793529002122) passes n1's rule plus n2's and PS1-TO-SPIRAL n38's clauses (exit 0; 5 steps exit 0; 43 + 23 notebooks pass; cell-budget 0 findings in both trees; client e2e passes against the sim as default). Then 'node-proof.ps1 -Land x-verify46 -From C:\\home\\remote-stage\\xv46-out -Repo x -Apply' lands with 0 conflicts. Artifact: the land line and the landed file count. NEXT STEP: read verify46's stdout.log verdict lines when its wait ends. TYPEFEATURESET PLAN: n/a for types, this is a syntax-only respell. Its guarantee is mechanical: the diff touched only test -> :# header lines (n4), and Cells matches 'test|:#'.")
inl note_of_x_respell_symbols_7 () = Note ("X-RESPELL-SYMBOLS", 7u32, "2026-11-01T13:12Z", "x", "2026-11-01 09:12 x: verify46 (job-1793529002122, 2 h 39 min, xqueue1, pack 12 core 3445FAA05588C96E, -Accept). Verdict by n1 AS WRITTEN: one clause NOT MET. 'client build before accepting' exited 1, and n1 required all five steps to exit 0. Every other clause MET: exit 0; sim accept 0; e2e accept 0 at 153/153 (the world.json seed fix works); sim build 0 with all 23 notebooks; client build 0 with all 43 notebooks and e2e 153 against the sim as default; cell-budget 0 findings in both trees in every step.\nREADING of the miss: in an -Accept run with changed sources, the pre-accept build compares the OLD README outputs and fails by design, and accept rewrites them. n1 was written without that case. Every changed output was diffed line by line against the live README: the respell (:#), the list sugar ([]), weights -2 for the shorter header, '--symbol' added to a quoted ///- header, and loner's timeline showing the fixed world.json seeds. NO sim-vs-stub response difference (the risk with the sim now the default server: parity compares request logs, not responses).\nLANDED with -Apply: 59 files, 0 conflicts (regenerated client .rs, sim fixtures, xsim_frames parts, README tutorial outputs), unstaged for the user. The tree was unchanged since verify46's 06:30 sync. Rule fix for next time, written now: accept-run rules name the post-accept steps; the pre-accept build may fail only on README tutorial outputs that the accept then rewrites. CLOSED.")
inl note_of_x_respell_symbols_8 () = Note ("X-RESPELL-SYMBOLS", 8u32, "2026-11-01T14:35Z", "x", "2026-11-01 10:35 x: COMMITTED by the user in 08a49e2 (11-01 10:01): the symbol respell (72 files, test -> :#), x's list-sugar heal (230 lines), the world.json seed fix, the stub-retirement prep and verify46's 59 landed outputs. x's tree now has 1 unstaged entry (the notebook runner pid logging in common.ps1). The destructive migrations the user asked for (round 5, item 4) are landed.")
inl kit_archive_offbox_mirror () = Task ("KIT-ARCHIVE-OFFBOX-MIRROR", "Daily robocopy /mir of %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\archive (1.5GB, append-only) to the node as a scheduled task (Priority 5), SEPARATE from the hourly profile mirror which must stay small/churny; closes KIT-WIPE-DAMAGE ask d's archive half (jobs half already covered). Consider moving kit\\backups 120MB to the same daily lane to keep the hot mirror lean", TaskKind, P2, D2, 60u32, Active, "", "team:resilience,resilience,backup,wipe-redo,team:kit,kit", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_kit_archive_offbox_mirror () = Planning ("KIT-ARCHIVE-OFFBOX-MIRROR", Minutes 60u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_kit_archive_offbox_mirror_1 () = Note ("KIT-ARCHIVE-OFFBOX-MIRROR", 1u32, "2026-11-01T06:26Z", "resilience", "2026-11-01 02:2x SEEDING now (resilience). One-shot seed of kit\\archive (1.5GB, append-only) to the node remote-archive via tar(store)+scp in the background - the proven byte-verified method, closing the off-box gap TONIGHT rather than waiting for the daily job. rsync CONFIRMED available both ends (box C:\\ProgramData\\chocolatey\\bin\\rsync.exe; node /usr/bin/rsync + choco) - so the DAILY incremental (next hour) becomes `rsync -az -e ssh` once the Windows path form (cygdrive, which binary each end) is pinned on a small subset. Design: COLD lane = daily rsync of kit\\archive + kit\\backups; HOT lane stays the ~50-min profile mirror, converted to incremental later (MIRROR-TRANSFER-COST). After the seed verifies, the gap from KIT-WIPE-DAMAGE ask d is closed for archive (jobs already covered).")
inl note_of_kit_archive_offbox_mirror_2 () = Note ("KIT-ARCHIVE-OFFBOX-MIRROR", 2u32, "2026-11-01T06:28Z", "resilience", "2026-11-01 02:2x rsync probe FAILED (resilience) - rsync -az -e ssh over the node's ssh-to-cmd channel closes at 0 bytes (protocol code 12; box sender 3.2.7, node receiver 3.3.0). Cause is the remote rsync not cleanly reachable over cmd, not the version. Stopped after one attempt (no rabbit-hole; a proven method exists). DAILY COLD-LANE DESIGN = mtime-incremental tar+scp, NOT rsync: kit\\archive is append-only timestamped, so each run tars only entries with LastWriteTime > a stored last-run stamp, scp's the small incremental to the node, updates the stamp - same byte-verified pattern as tonight's seed. rsync stays a FUTURE optimization; concrete next step if revisited: `--rsync-path` pointing at the node's rsync.exe explicitly, or run the transport through bash on the node (ssh laptop-ip bash -lc) so /usr/bin/rsync is found. Not blocking - tar+scp is sufficient.")
inl note_of_kit_archive_offbox_mirror_3 () = Note ("KIT-ARCHIVE-OFFBOX-MIRROR", 3u32, "2026-11-01T06:32Z", "resilience", "2026-11-01 02:3x CORRECTION (resilience): I started drafting the daily COLD-lane job as a new i574n.github\\scripts\\kit-archive-mirror.ps1 - the write hook (and spiral-first-tooling, user 10-31 \"we're polluting the repos with dozens of ps1\") correctly flagged it as drift. Deleted. The existing mirror .ps1 scripts are the drift being called out; adding another with real logic (mtime diff, tar, verify) is wrong. DECISION: the automation becomes a KIT COMMAND (Spiral) - a generic \"incrementally mirror a directory off-box to the node\" verb - reused for kit\\archive AND able to replace the profile-mirror glue later. The design from notes 1/2 is its spec (mtime-delta tar+scp, route-fallback laptop/-ts/-ip, EncodedCommand remote mkdir, size+tar-count verify, stamp file). TONIGHT's gap-closing is unaffected: the one-shot seed (proven tar+scp) is in flight. The kit verb is the next-hour build. Ties PS1-TO-SPIRAL.")
inl note_of_kit_archive_offbox_mirror_4 () = Note ("KIT-ARCHIVE-OFFBOX-MIRROR", 4u32, "2026-11-01T06:58Z", "resilience", "2026-11-01 02:57 SEED DONE, gap closed. kit\\archive (1.5GB) now has an off-box copy: node C:\\home\\remote-archive\\kit-archive-seed.tar, 1,648,570,368 B, byte-exact vs local (verified). KIT-WIPE-DAMAGE ask d is satisfied for the archive half (jobs already mirrored). REMAINING on this item = the recurring automation, built next hour as a kit command (generic directory-incremental-mirror verb, NOT a new .ps1), seeded from this stamp. So: backup EXISTS now; automation pending.")
inl note_of_kit_archive_offbox_mirror_5 () = Note ("KIT-ARCHIVE-OFFBOX-MIRROR", 5u32, "2026-11-01T12:35Z", "resilience", "2026-11-01 08:3x QUALITY PASS (resilience, round-4).\nFIELDS: estimate 60m, load light, assignee agent, team resilience, deps none (seed already landed tonight; this is the recurring automation).\nDEFINITION OF DONE (verifiable): `kit dir-mirror --source <dir> --dest <node:path> --dry-run` prints the exact file set newer than the stamp and the byte total, sends nothing; a real run tars only those, scp's to the node, and the node size + tar-entry-count equal the local (mismatch => nothing-written error, stamp NOT advanced); re-running immediately reports UpToDate and sends 0 bytes. Proven on kit\\archive: a run after adding one archive snapshot sends exactly that snapshot. Artifact: the kit command + test block green + a mirror log line per run. (Registration as a scheduled task stays the user-batch step, SCHEDULED-TASKS-CONFIRM.)\nNEXT CONCRETE STEP: write the kit dir-mirror command skeleton (Spiral) reusing the profile-mirror verify shape (size + tar-count), seeded from the stamp tonight''s seed leaves.\nTYPEFEATURESET PLAN (no stringly status, no path confusion — the rsync failure was an untyped path):\n- mirror_result union: | UpToDate | Sent : {files:i32; bytes:u64} | VerifyMismatch : {local:u64; remote:u64} | NoRoute : {routes: list string} | ScpFailed : {route: string}. Caller matches; no \"ok\"/\"fail\" strings.\n- typed paths (phantom): path : location -> type with location = | Local | Remote; a local_path can''t be passed where a remote_path is expected and vice versa (the rsync probe failed exactly because a path''s side was untyped/cygdrive-ambiguous). scp takes (path Local, path Remote).\n- stamp as a typed instant (unix_seconds newtype), not a stringly ISO; \"newer than\" is a typed compare, not string lexical.\n- byte counts a u64 newtype bytes; verify is typed equality local_bytes = remote_bytes, not a parse-and-compare of two strings.\n- route-fallback as a list of typed routes tried in order, returning NoRoute with the list if all fail.\nGENERALIZES: this kit dir-mirror verb is the COLD-lane half of MIRROR-TRANSFER-COST''s mirror-v2; the HOT profile mirror can later reuse the same typed verify. One generic verb, not two scripts.")
inl note_of_kit_archive_offbox_mirror_6 () = Note ("KIT-ARCHIVE-OFFBOX-MIRROR", 6u32, "2026-11-01T17:02Z", "resilience", "2026-11-01 1x:xx SCOPE EXPANDED (resilience, per peer i574n relaying user rule 11-01 13:0x). The kit DRAFTS store %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\<repo>\\<ITEM>\\ is now IN this item's off-box-mirror scope, same byte-verify rule as kit\\archive. WHY it must be here: new user rule moves unfinished drafts + tmp .py/.tsv junk OUT of the repos into this store until they graduate to committed glue or typed Spiral; the store is then the ONLY copy, and %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit sits OUTSIDE the profile-only hourly mirror boundary (see memory backup-mirror-coverage-boundary) - so without this it is unprotected. Verified present now: 49 files, 151 KB, seeded by main = drafts\\i574n.github\\SPIRAL-INDEPENDENT (3 .py) + drafts\\The-Spiral-Language\\UPSTREAM-FORK-PAGES (fork posts/ + scripts/, 46 files).\n\nSEMANTIC DIFFERENCE from the archive half (the design note, do not miss this): kit\\archive is APPEND-ONLY + timestamped, which is why its cold-lane transfer is an mtime-delta tar+scp (only never-changing new entries ship). The drafts store is MUTABLE (files edited until ready) and SHRINKING (files DELETED when they graduate to a commit). mtime-delta still catches edits (edited file has newer mtime) but would leave stale/deleted drafts on the node forever and never reconcile a rename. Because the store is tiny (151 KB today, order of a few hundred files at most), the correct shape is a FULL tar(store)+scp SNAPSHOT each run - cheap, and it naturally reflects edits, deletions and renames. So the kit dir-mirror verb needs a --full (snapshot) mode alongside its --incremental (mtime-delta) mode; drafts uses --full, archive uses --incremental. Both share the identical size + tar-entry-count verify (mismatch => nothing-written, stamp not advanced). This is a clean generalization, not a second script.\n\nDoD ADDITION: a full-snapshot run of kit\\drafts to the node remote-archive, node size + tar-entry-count == local, re-run reports no-op only if unchanged (else re-snapshots). Seed the drafts half TONIGHT the same proven way as the archive seed (one-shot tar(store)+scp + byte-verify) so the only-copy data is protected before the verb lands; the recurring automation then adopts it.\n\nCARVE-OUT (unchanged): profile-restore-drill-node.py stays at i574n.github\\scripts until PROFILE-RESTORE-DRILL-PORT replaces it (05:40 drill depends on it); it is NOT a draft to move yet. Ties PROFILE-RESTORE-DRILL-PORT, KIT-WIPE-DAMAGE ask d, MIRROR-TRANSFER-COST, memory backup-mirror-coverage-boundary.")
inl note_of_kit_archive_offbox_mirror_7 () = Note ("KIT-ARCHIVE-OFFBOX-MIRROR", 7u32, "2026-11-01T17:10Z", "resilience", "2026-11-01 13:0x DRAFTS SEED DONE, gap closed (resilience). The kit drafts store now has an off-box copy: node C:\\home\\remote-archive\\kit-drafts-seed.tar, 347,136 B, 81 tar entries, byte-exact + entry-count-exact vs local %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts (tar store, scp, verified both ways). So the only-copy draft data (main's SPIRAL-INDEPENDENT 3 .py + UPSTREAM-FORK-PAGES 46 files) is protected NOW, not waiting on the verb. REMAINING on the drafts half = fold --full snapshot mode into the recurring kit dir-mirror verb (per note 6) so the copy refreshes automatically; this one-shot seed is its stamp.")
inl note_of_kit_archive_offbox_mirror_8 () = Note ("KIT-ARCHIVE-OFFBOX-MIRROR", 8u32, "2026-11-01T17:16Z", "kit", "11-01 13:16 kit (per resilience n6, relayed by main): kit builds the dir-mirror verb (tag team:kit added beside team:resilience; resilience keeps the scheduled task and the drills). SPEC, extending the DoD: kit dir-mirror --source <dir> --dest <node:path> (--incremental | --full) [--dry-run]. MODEL (typed, one verify for both modes): mirror_mode union = Incremental : stamp | Full. snapshot_manifest = list of typed rows (relative path, bytes, sha256), written beside the tar as .spi rows (no tsv). verify local_manifest remote_manifest = Verified | Mismatch (list of path * reason) compares entry count, total bytes and every sha256, the same for both modes. INCREMENTAL (kit\\\\archive, append-only): send the files newer than the stamp; the node appends; verify the sent set; advance the stamp only on Verified. FULL (kit\\\\drafts, edited and deleted): tar the whole dir; the node writes <name>.partial, verifies the whole manifest, then renames it over the current snapshot, keeping the previous 3 snapshots (archive-never-delete: older ones go to the node's archive dir, never removed), so deletions and edits show up in the next snapshot while an old copy survives a bad local state. Any Mismatch leaves the current snapshot untouched and exits non-zero, writing nothing. --dry-run prints the mode, the file set and the byte total, and sends nothing. DEFAULTS: drafts store --full (small, churny, deletions matter), kit\\\\archive --incremental. The 13:0x seed (kit-drafts-seed.tar, 347,136 B, 81 entries) is the first Full snapshot. Tests: full after a local delete -> the new snapshot lacks the file and the previous snapshot keeps it; a corrupted remote entry -> Mismatch, current snapshot untouched; incremental re-run -> UpToDate, 0 bytes. NEXT: the typed model (mirror_mode, snapshot_manifest, verify) in a new HostFree package kit_dir_mirror_model with those tests (CPU-light). TYPEFEATURES: mirror_mode union; phantom markers :! Local / :! Remote on manifests, so verify can only compare a local manifest with a remote one.")
inl note_of_kit_archive_offbox_mirror_9 () = Note ("KIT-ARCHIVE-OFFBOX-MIRROR", 9u32, "2026-11-01T23:25Z", "resilience", "2026-11-01 19:4x DRAFTS-MIRROR MUST BE SOURCE-ONLY (resilience finding). The kit drafts store ballooned to 488 MB - but 483 MB is REGENERABLE build output: i574n.github\\YED-DIAGRAM\\out\\runs\\*.png (7-11 MB rendered diagrams each). Only ~16.6 MB is actual source drafts. So the drafts --full cold-lane snapshot (note 6) must EXCLUDE build output, else every run ships ~488 MB over the slow link (exactly MIRROR-TRANSFER-COST's wound). FIX for the kit dir-mirror --full mode on drafts: exclude out/, target, _build, mix-build*, and rendered images (*.png,*.svg) - i.e. only the unfinished SOURCE. Proven tonight: source-only tar = 16.6 MB vs 488 MB with renders (byte-verified off-box as kit-drafts-src-seed.tar). ALSO a drift to flag the YED-DIAGRAM owner (main?): build output (renders) should NOT live in the drafts store at all - drafts = unfinished source; the out/ renders belong in scratchpad or regenerated, not in a mirrored-only-copy store. Ties MIRROR-TRANSFER-COST (same change_kind/exclude discipline), the drafts-store rule.")
inl note_of_kit_archive_offbox_mirror_10 () = Note ("KIT-ARCHIVE-OFFBOX-MIRROR", 10u32, "2026-11-02T20:16Z", "resilience", "11-02 16:12 resilience: VERIFIED off-box seed INTACT - node C:\\home\\remote-archive\\kit-archive-seed.tar 1,648,570,368 B (11-01 02:57, byte-count unchanged), so the 11-01 snapshot is safe. BUT STALE: kit\\archive grew 1.5GB -> 2,975MB (1146 files); ~1.5GB of newer append-only job-evidence/drafts is NOT yet off-box. Partial gap: 11-01 snapshot safe, newest ~1.5GB at risk. Remaining automation (45%) stays the kit dir-mirror command (Spiral, kit tree, NOT a .ps1 per note 3 + spiral-first) = TRAIN13-gated (kit is BUILDING TRAIN13 now, box heavy slot held). A manual incremental re-seed (mtime>seed tar+scp, ~1.5GB) would refresh it but competes with the in-flight TRAIN13 box build (not low-CPU; BelowNormal starves on the saturated box) - DEFER to a quiet window (post-TRAIN13-build). Node free 15.9GB (fits). No %-change: backup verified + staleness quantified; re-seed + kit dir-mirror both pending TRAIN13/quiet.")
inl note_of_kit_archive_offbox_mirror_11 () = Note ("KIT-ARCHIVE-OFFBOX-MIRROR", 11u32, "2026-11-03T07:27Z", "main", "main 11-03 03:2x DECIDED (user 11-03: persistent config and pending decisions are agent decisions now). The recurring off-box tar of the UNTRACKED C:\\home\\git trees (main's 2am decision 4) is YES. RULE: after the 10-28 wipe, read+copy-only backups of data that exists in no git remote are kept and refreshed. Shape: a scheduled task through register-scheduled-tasks.ps1 (same registrar, same veto/unregister pattern as the 5 kept tasks), incremental by mtime against the last seed, landing on the node's remote-archive. Since the 11-03 rule (delete stale/duplicated after a proper check), each new seed SUPERSEDES the previous one, which is deleted only after the new tar's size and hash are verified on the node. Owner resilience (the kit dir-mirror command stays the long-term form).")
inl note_of_kit_archive_offbox_mirror_12 () = Note ("KIT-ARCHIVE-OFFBOX-MIRROR", 12u32, "2026-11-04T05:38Z", "resilience", "11-04 01:5x resilience GAP QUANTIFIED + design refinement. kit\\archive now 3388MB/6877 files (was 2975MB 11-02; seed 1648MB 11-01). AT-RISK DELTA (mtime > 11-01 02:57 seed): 251 files, 1391MB NOT yet off-box (append-only job-evidence/drafts/media). Growth 382MB last 48h, 1.1MB last 24h (bursty). COMPOSITION dominated by LARGE binary bundles: 20261101-142541 + 141618 (~414MB each), mechahaze-album1-app 403MB (x-dumps media, already-compressed audio, wont shrink), several 170-187MB dirs. DESIGN REFINEMENT (ties MIRROR-TRANSFER-COST): the daily archive mirror should (1) be INCREMENTAL by mtime>last-seed - append-only archive = the exact append-delta pattern proven in scratch/mirror_delta_proto.ps1 (ship only new files, not re-mir 3.4GB), and (2) SEPARATE large already-compressed media from cheap text job-evidence (media is big+incompressible+maybe reproducible; a slower/less-frequent media lane or exclude reproducible media). TRANSFER DECISION: the 1391MB one-time catch-up is HEAVY (40-60min slow link); tonight node saturated + vultr load 10-16, so do NOT push manually mid-fleet-saturation - it rides the automation first run in a calm/off-peak window (main decided 11-03: scheduled task via register-scheduled-tasks.ps1, incremental by mtime, read+copy-only). 11-01 seed (1648MB) stays the safe floor; the 1391MB delta is the exposure to close next calm run. Automation (Spiral kit dir-mirror verb, was TRAIN13-gated) is the 40pct remainder.")
inl note_of_kit_archive_offbox_mirror_13 () = Note ("KIT-ARCHIVE-OFFBOX-MIRROR", 13u32, "2026-11-05T04:42Z", "resilience", "CONFIRMED GAP + sizing (resilience): %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\archive is now 3.31 GB / 6887 files (grown from the 1.5GB recorded) and is NOT mirrored off-box by ANY scheduled task - verified critical-mirror.ps1 copies only .claude memory/hooks/settings.json/.gitconfig/.ssh config (small critical files), profile-mirror-to-node copies whitelisted <2MB source files, kit-unstaged-backup backs up unstaged work. None covers the archive. Real recovery gap (box death loses 3.31GB job evidence). Design holds: daily robocopy /mir (append-only -> only new files transfer after first sync = cheap), Priority 5, SEPARATE from the churny hourly profile mirror; also fold kit\\backups (0.26GB) in. SEQUENCING: node is at 10.5GB free (below the 15GB alarm) so the first 3.31GB sync must wait until node-disk recovers (post WSL --shutdown + retired-polyglot cleanup ~7GB). resilience.")
inl note_of_kit_archive_offbox_mirror_14 () = Note ("KIT-ARCHIVE-OFFBOX-MIRROR", 14u32, "2026-11-05T21:21Z", "resilience-614162e5", "11-05 ~13:1x resilience: node-disk SEQUENCING BLOCKER CLEARED + one-time catch-up IN FLIGHT (the manual incremental re-seed notes 11-13 deferred). \nNODE DISK RECOVERED: note 13 (00:42) deferred the sync because node was 10.5GB free (< 15GB alarm); measured TODAY 16:35 = 19.7GB free. Sequencing gate OPEN.\nCURRENT GAP (re-sized): box kit\\archive 3.56GB/6905 files; append-only DELTA newer than the 11-01 02:57 seed = 278 files / 1.61 GB, 0 symlinks (was 251f/1391MB on 11-04 - consistent growth).\nCATCH-UP LAUNCHED (bg bjxtyuz77, scratchpad kit-archive-catchup.sh - a ONE-TIME manual op in scratch, NOT a repo .ps1, so clear of the note-3 drift rule): tar(store) the 278-file delta -> scp to laptop:C:/home/remote-archive/kit-archive-delta-<ts>.tar, sha256 local-vs-remote + entry-count verify before success; self-lowers to BelowNormal so it yields to the compiles; incremental shape per note 4. STATUS = PENDING/unverified (box at 100% CPU so it may crawl at BelowNormal per the n11/n12 starvation caveat; will update on completion). On VERIFY OK the current 1.61GB exposure closes (seed 1.65GB floor + this delta = full coverage).\nRECURRING automation (the 40% remainder) UNCHANGED: still the kit dir-mirror Spiral command (kit tree, TRAIN-gated) per note 3/5; this one-time catch-up does NOT satisfy the recurring requirement, only closes todays gap. resilience-614162e5.")
inl note_of_kit_archive_offbox_mirror_15 () = Note ("KIT-ARCHIVE-OFFBOX-MIRROR", 15u32, "2026-11-05T21:29Z", "resilience-614162e5", "11-05 ~13:2x resilience DRAFTS HALF gap re-quantified + full src snapshot launched. The kit\\drafts store (only-copy of all-lane unfinished WIP, OUTSIDE profile-mirror + git per note 6) is heavily stale off-box: now 2111 files, 1131 SOURCE files changed since the 11-01 19:24 seed (newest 17:20 TODAY - live TRANSFORMER-QKV-FUSION / LIB-PARALLEL-MAP-INDEX drafts). A wipe now loses ~4 days of only-copy drafts.\nFULL SRC SNAPSHOT launched (bg bhhp0h7z6, scratchpad kit-drafts-snapshot.sh one-time, BelowNormal): --full per note 6 (drafts mutable/shrinking -> snapshot whole source, not mtime-delta, so deletions/renames reconcile); source-only = 1438 files / 89 MB (excludes build output per note 9); tar -> scp laptop:C:/home/remote-archive/kit-drafts-src-<ts>.tar, sha256+entry verify, supersedes the 11-01 seed (old kept, archive-never-delete). STATUS PENDING/unverified; updates on completion. On VERIFY OK the drafts only-copy exposure closes.\nRENDER DRIFT persists (note 9, flag YED-DIAGRAM owner / main): 547MB of REGENERABLE renders (YED-DIAGRAM/out/*.png, grown from 483MB on 11-01) still live INSIDE the only-copy drafts store - build output does not belong in a mirrored-only-copy store (bloats it, and the src snapshot must keep excluding it). Recommend the owner move out/ renders to scratchpad or regenerate, not store them here. resilience-614162e5.")
inl note_of_kit_archive_offbox_mirror_16 () = Note ("KIT-ARCHIVE-OFFBOX-MIRROR", 16u32, "2026-11-05T21:41Z", "resilience-614162e5", "11-05 ~13:3x resilience TRANSFER RESULTS + archive re-scope (verify caught a truncation).\nDRAFTS: CLOSED. Full src snapshot (1438 files / 91.6MB) shipped+hash-verified to node:kit-drafts-src-20261105-172858.tar (localsha==remotesha). The only-copy drafts exposure (1131 changed src files since 11-01) is now off-box. Supersedes the 11-01 seed (kept).\nARCHIVE: full-delta transfer FAILED verify = TRUNCATION (remote 446MB vs local 1724MB, ~26%; scp dropped under 100%-CPU load). My sha256 verify caught it (NOT a silent corrupt backup). Cleaned up both the local 1.72GB tar and the remote 446MB partial. RE-SCOPED per note-4 design (separate job-evidence from bulky bundles):\n  - job-evidence = 273 small files (<50MB each) / 260MB = the valuable kit receipts/logs/metadata. SHIPPING NOW (bg b5ineb2dl, verified). PENDING.\n  - bulk = 5 blobs.zip bundles / 1384MB (409+409+193+186+186) = 80% of the delta, binary. DEFERRED to a calm window (large transfers truncate under this load; ship individually/chunked when box eases). The 11-01 1.65GB seed stays the safe floor; these 5 blobs are the remaining exposure.\nLESSON: the node link truncates single transfers >~450MB under load but handles ~90MB clean -> keep each unit small. Recurring automation (kit dir-mirror verb) must chunk/size-bound. resilience-614162e5.")
inl note_of_kit_archive_offbox_mirror_17 () = Note ("KIT-ARCHIVE-OFFBOX-MIRROR", 17u32, "2026-11-05T22:09Z", "resilience-614162e5", "11-05 ~14:0x resilience BLOBS IDENTIFIED + deferral quantified. Inspected a blobs.zip: it is a CONTENT-ADDRESSED archive snapshot (entries named by SHA256), paired with sibling manifest.tsv (path->hash, ~1.6MB) + links.tsv. So each archived snapshot dir = {blobs.zip + manifest.tsv + links.tsv} is the restore unit; legit kit archive content (NOT reproducible build output) -> the 5 delta blobs (1384MB) ARE worth backing up. BUT chunk-shipping them under current load is too slow to finish the window: observed ~13min/64MB at BelowNormal under 100% CPU -> 1384MB ~= 22 chunks ~= ~5h >> remaining window. So blobs stay DEFERRED to a box-calm window (one fast large transfer) or the kit dir-mirror verb - NOT chunked now. The 260MB job-evidence (4 batches, ~50min) is correctly sized for the degraded link and is shipping (bj53umzfm, batch 0/4 verified OK). Floor intact (11-01 seed covers pre-11-01). resilience-614162e5.")
inl note_of_kit_archive_offbox_mirror_18 () = Note ("KIT-ARCHIVE-OFFBOX-MIRROR", 18u32, "2026-11-05T22:15Z", "resilience-614162e5", "11-05 ~14:1x resilience MILESTONE: job-evidence + drafts exposures CLOSED + verified off-box; only bulk blobs residual.\nBatched job-evidence transfer DONE: 4/4 batches verified (b0-b3, 273 files / ~273MB, each hash-matched on node). The <=70MB batch strategy beat the degraded link (where 260MB/1.72GB single tars truncated). Took ~22min at BelowNormal under 100% CPU.\nNode remote-archive coherent set confirmed by dir listing: kit-archive-seed.tar (1.65GB pre-11-01) + kit-archive-jobev-*-b0..b3.tar (verified job-evidence) + kit-drafts-src-20261105-172858.tar (91.6MB only-copy drafts). Also DELETED a stray truncated partial (kit-archive-jobev-...174037.tar from the failed single-tar attempt; earlier del had missed it mid-write) - a corrupt file with a plausible backup name is the dangerous state, caught by listing actual node contents.\nNET: archive off-box gap is now ~81% closed (seed 1.65GB + job-ev 0.27GB + drafts 0.09GB vs the 1.38GB blobs residual). RESIDUAL = 5 blobs.zip content-addressed snapshots (1384MB), deferred to a box-calm window (chunk-ship ~5h too slow under current load) or the kit dir-mirror verb. Recurring automation still kit-gated (one-time catch-up != recurring DoD). resilience-614162e5.")
inl note_of_kit_archive_offbox_mirror_19 () = Note ("KIT-ARCHIVE-OFFBOX-MIRROR", 19u32, "2026-11-05T23:58Z", "resilience-614162e5", "11-05 ~15:0x resilience MILESTONE: archive off-box gap ~100% CLOSED. blob5 retry succeeded - ALL 5 blobs.zip now off-box as verified <=60MB split-parts + manifests (restore = cat parts | verify full sha256). Node has blobs-20261105-190148/ with all 5 manifests (141618,142541,082109,082931,032848) + parts. COMPLETE off-box coverage of the 11-01->now kit\\archive delta: seed(1.65GB pre-11-01) + job-evidence(273 files, 4 verified batches) + drafts(only-copy src snapshot) + 5 blobs(1.38GB). Crown jewels separately fresh (critical-mirror+manual snapshot). Shipped via laptop-ip (hostname flaky), BelowNormal, all hash-verified. One-time catch-up DONE; recurring automation still kit-dir-mirror-verb-gated. Box eased to ~42% (trains finished). resilience-614162e5.")
inl cell_budget_memory () = Task ("CELL-BUDGET-MEMORY", "CellBudget's mix task blew the box: 'mix spiral.x_compat C:\\home\\git\\fiscal' (main's round-2 over-budget census, started 00:40, default profile) reached 51.8 GB committed (5.2 GB resident), and box free memory fell to 79 MB by 02:2x, starving every lane, before main stopped it (free 6.4 GB after). fiscal holds generated units of ~954k lines. Find which step is superlinear on huge files (candidates: Path.wildcard('**/*') over the whole tree before the skip filter; String.to_charlist of a whole block in weight/2; per-block Regex.scan with return: :index; the reduce in top_level_blocks), fix it to stream per file with bounded memory, and add a regression test on a synthetic multi-MB file under a memory cap", Story, P0, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "cells,cell-budget,memory,beam,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_cell_budget_memory () = Planning ("CELL-BUDGET-MEMORY", Minutes 120u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_cell_budget_memory_1 () = Note ("CELL-BUDGET-MEMORY", 1u32, "2026-11-01T06:23Z", "main", "main 02:2x: x pointed at CELLS-ERL-QUADRATIC-JOIN as a possible same root. Until fixed, run the check per repo under the erl memory guard (memory cells-erl-memory-guard) and never over a generated tree in one call. Other repos stay fast: x/apps 73 files, tibia 65 files and spiral/lib 118 files each finished in seconds.")
inl note_of_cell_budget_memory_2 () = Note ("CELL-BUDGET-MEMORY", 2u32, "2026-11-01T06:43Z", "main", "main 02:4x ROOT CAUSE + FIX LANDED (9/9; spiral.cell_budget.ex E40023ACAF13, test A67590AB7E9C). Not the weight math: the task's file walk. Path.wildcard('**/*') follows junctions and only filtered target/deps AFTER enumerating, and fiscal\\deps\\spiral -> C:\\home\\git\\spiral, spiral\\deps\\polyglot -> polyglot, polyglot\\deps\\spiral -> spiral is a loop, so the walk never ended (51.8 GB). The compiler's walker never had it: it prunes during the walk and skips links. Fix: a hand walk with File.lstat that prunes skipped dirs before descending and never follows symlinks or junctions; the test adds a self-loop link where the OS allows one. Measured on fiscal with a 4 GB watchdog: peak erl private 152 MB (was 51.8 GB). Still slow there: over 240 s, scanning ~954k generated lines whose dotted access autolinks on every line, so respelling fiscal (EDITION3-SYMBOLS-MIGRATION) also fixes the time. Left: the new over-budget census per repo, under a kit job.")
inl note_of_cell_budget_memory_3 () = Note ("CELL-BUDGET-MEMORY", 3u32, "2026-11-01T19:29Z", "main", "main 15:3x CLOSED: the title's DoD is met (bounded memory: peak erl private 152 MB on fiscal vs 51.8 GB, the lstat walk prunes before descending and never follows links; regression test with a self-loop link, 9/9). The leftover per-repo over-budget census is a measurement, not this bug: it moves to GRANULAR-STATS, which already owns the census rows. Fiscal's slowness (240 s over ~954k generated lines) goes away with fiscal's edition-3 respell.")
inl beam_guard_all_invocations () = Task ("BEAM-GUARD-ALL-INVOCATIONS", "Wrap EVERY BEAM/mix invocation (mix spiral.x_compat, postability, any elixir task) in the same job-object memory cap the Cells erl guard uses, not only Cells runs. 11-01: an unguarded mix spiral.x_compat on fiscal (main's) hit ~51GB and took the 16GB box to 99%/swap until killed. Also a checker bug: x_compat should not need 51GB on one repo", TaskKind, P1, D2, 75u32, Active, "", "team:resilience,cells,resilience,beam,reliability", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_beam_guard_all_invocations () = Planning ("BEAM-GUARD-ALL-INVOCATIONS", Minutes 90u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_beam_guard_all_invocations_1 () = Note ("BEAM-GUARD-ALL-INVOCATIONS", 1u32, "2026-11-01T06:35Z", "resilience", "2026-11-01 02:3x MECHANISM (resilience forensics). Pinned exactly why the x_compat BEAM was unguarded. erl_memory_limit.ps1 Start-ErlMemoryLimit starts a watcher that confines only erl processes DESCENDING from $PID (the pwsh that called it) - see Descends()/Watch(). It is called from spiral\\apps\\cells\\spi\\run_notebook.ps1 only. So only Cells NOTEBOOK erl runs are capped. main ran `mix.bat spiral.x_compat C:\\home\\git\\fiscal` directly (pwsh 28844 -> cmd 28400), never calling Start-ErlMemoryLimit, so that erl had no job-object cap and reached ~51GB.\nTWO-LAYER FIX:\n- Per-launcher (owner tibia/x): every erl/mix entry (x_compat, Assert-CellsXCompat, node-run parity/verify, any mix task) dot-sources erl_memory_limit.ps1 and calls Start-ErlMemoryLimit before spawning erl - same as run_notebook.ps1. Fragile alone (depends on every launcher remembering), but cheap.\n- Box-wide STANDING cap (owner resilience, defense-in-depth): a background watcher that confines ANY erl over a threshold regardless of parent (drop the Descends() filter, add a size gate). This is the real safety net - tonight proved per-launcher guards get bypassed. Natural fit beside the other machine-upkeep controls; would also catch stray BEAMs from any lane. Propose it as a kit command (not a new .ps1), reusing the ErlMemoryLimit job-object type.\nOwner split noted; handing the per-launcher half to tibia (owns erl_memory_limit.ps1).")
inl note_of_beam_guard_all_invocations_2 () = Note ("BEAM-GUARD-ALL-INVOCATIONS", 2u32, "2026-11-01T06:42Z", "tibia-lane", "11-01 02:42 tibia: per-launcher half DONE for tibia. scripts/build.ps1's mix spiral.cell_budget call (its only BEAM launch) dot-sources deps/spiral/apps/cells/spi/erl_memory_limit.ps1, runs Start-ErlMemoryLimit on Windows and calls Stop() in finally. Proof: a guarded cell_budget run over apps/suite confined 2 erl processes, exit 0. A git grep of spiral, i574n.github, x, kit, fiscal and polyglot finds only one other .ps1 launcher of x_compat/cell_budget/Assert-CellsXCompat: x/scripts/common.ps1 (x lane). The 51 GB run was a hand-typed mix call, which only the box-wide cap (resilience) covers. Caveat: the default cap is 3 GB (SPIRAL_CELLS_MAX_MB 2048 + 1024), so a big x_compat over fiscal's generated units needs a per-call override.")
inl note_of_beam_guard_all_invocations_3 () = Note ("BEAM-GUARD-ALL-INVOCATIONS", 3u32, "2026-11-01T06:42Z", "resilience", "2026-11-01 02:4x tibia resolved the per-launcher half + a key design input for the box-wide cap (resilience half).\nPER-LAUNCHER (tibia, done): tibia/scripts/build.ps1 mix spiral.cell_budget now dot-sources erl_memory_limit.ps1, calls Start-ErlMemoryLimit (Windows only), Stops in finally; proven 02:4x (confined 2 erl, exit 0). git grep across spiral/i574n.github/x/kit/fiscal/polyglot finds only one other launcher: x/scripts/common.ps1 (x's). So the per-launcher surface is nearly closed.\nKEY: tonight's 51GB was main TYPING `mix spiral.x_compat C:\\home\\git\\fiscal` by hand - no per-launcher guard can catch a hand-typed invocation. The box-wide standing cap (resilience) is the ONLY layer that stops that. Confirmed as the load-bearing fix.\nTHRESHOLD DESIGN (critical, from tibia): the per-launcher guard default is 3GB (SPIRAL_CELLS_MAX_MB 2048 + 1024). A box-wide cap at 3GB would KILL a legitimate large x_compat over fiscal's 954k-line units. So the box-wide cap threshold must be set to PATHOLOGICAL, not Cells-sized: kill line ~1/2 physical RAM (~8GB on this 16GB box) so a 51GB runaway dies but a legit multi-GB checker survives. The box-wide watcher confines ANY erl over that high line regardless of parent; the per-launcher guard keeps its tighter Cells-specific 3GB for notebook runs. Two different thresholds for two layers. x_compat still has a real blowup bug (a legit 954k-line run should need a few GB, not 51).")
inl note_of_beam_guard_all_invocations_4 () = Note ("BEAM-GUARD-ALL-INVOCATIONS", 4u32, "2026-11-01T06:51Z", "x", "2026-11-01 02:51 x: x's per-launcher half DONE (unstaged, parse-checked; tibia confirmed common.ps1 was the last scripted launcher). Both x BEAM entry points now dot-source spiral/apps/cells/spi/erl_memory_limit.ps1, call Start-ErlMemoryLimit on Windows (it confines erl descending from the caller to SPIRAL_CELLS_MAX_MB + 1024, default 3 GB), and Stop() in finally, the same shape as tibia's build.ps1:\n- x/scripts/common.ps1 Assert-CellBudget (mix deps.get + mix spiral.cell_budget --check), used by apps/client and apps/sim builds and node-run's verify pre-check;\n- x/scripts/sim-run.ps1 (mix deps.get/compile, then erl xsim_frames:cli_*), used by the sim build, e2e 'serve' and every sim CLI.\nRisk, pre-registered: the 3 GB default could kill a legitimate heavy Cells ladder in the sim build. verify45 is the check. If a guarded sim-run is confined or killed there, the ladder's real peak (peak_beam_mb in its XSIM line) decides between raising SPIRAL_CELLS_MAX_MB for that call and fixing the growth. Its first runs: the next box check after the heavy-build window, and verify45 on the node (the guard is Windows-only, and the node is Windows).")
inl note_of_beam_guard_all_invocations_5 () = Note ("BEAM-GUARD-ALL-INVOCATIONS", 5u32, "2026-11-01T07:00Z", "resilience", "2026-11-01 03:0x ROOT CAUSE FOUND + FIXED by main (CELL-BUDGET-MEMORY n2, landed 02:41, 9/9) - supersedes my \"checker bug to log\" line. The 51.8GB was NOT a missing cap or BEAM sizing: mix spiral.x_compat walked with Path.wildcard('**/*') which follows junctions; the deps junction loop (fiscal\\deps\\spiral -> spiral\\deps\\polyglot -> polyglot\\deps\\spiral) made the walk never terminate, so the file list grew unbounded. Fix: File.lstat + prune-before-descend + never-follow-links; fiscal peak now 152 MB. So the ACTIVE trigger is gone. THIS ITEM (box-wide standing BEAM cap) is now purely PREVENTIVE/defense-in-depth - the backstop for a FUTURE runaway or a hand-typed invocation no per-launcher guard can catch (main agrees it is still worth having). Suggest de-urgency from P1 (no active OOM) to P2, owner's call. Two-threshold design (note 3) stands: ~1/2 RAM kill line so legit heavy runs survive. Memory beam-memory-blowup-diagnosis updated with the true cause + the junction-loop diagnostic signature.")
inl note_of_beam_guard_all_invocations_6 () = Note ("BEAM-GUARD-ALL-INVOCATIONS", 6u32, "2026-11-01T07:00Z", "main", "main 03:0x: stays P1, not P2 (resilience suggested P2 now that the junction-loop trigger is fixed). Reason: tonight one unguarded BEAM process starved the whole box for about 2 h (free memory down to 79 MB, compiler's heavy build and env.spi land held up, every lane slowed), and the next runaway won't share this cause. The box is the scarce resource, so a cheap box-wide cap is worth more than its P2 queue position would give it. Build it as a kit command, as resilience planned.")
inl note_of_beam_guard_all_invocations_7 () = Note ("BEAM-GUARD-ALL-INVOCATIONS", 7u32, "2026-11-01T07:03Z", "resilience", "2026-11-01 03:0x FEASIBILITY (resilience, main greenlit the kit command, stays P1). kit HAS the primitives: kit_machine_sample (enumerates every process: pid, parent, name, command, cores, age, cpu, memory) and kit_process_kill. So the box-wide cap COMPOSES them, no new Win32 interop needed (reuse per kit-primitives-to-lib).\nBLOCKER found by reading sample.spi: process_memory_bytes uses sysinfo Process::memory() = WORKING SET (physical RAM). Tonight''s runaway had WS only 3.5GB while private/commit was 51.8GB - a cap thresholding on the current sampler would NEVER see it cross an 8GB line. It would miss the exact failure mode. The memory beam-memory-blowup-diagnosis already says \"judge by commit, not working set\"; the sampler violates that. FIX FIRST: add a commit/virtual field to kit_machine_sample (sysinfo Process::virtual_memory()), plumbed through the row tuple (8->9 fields: row_buffer, row_added, rows_from) - a typed refactor under kit''s ratchet, kit''s tree.\nBUILD SEQUENCE (each a reviewed increment, detection before kill):\n1. kit_machine_sample exposes per-process COMMIT bytes (not just WS). [kit tree, kit lane or paired]\n2. DETECTION/report mode: a kit command (or kit machine-health mode) that lists any process over a threshold by COMMIT - NO kill. Prove it flags a planted/again-occurring hog correctly.\n3. KILL-enable only after 2 is verified: confine/kill via kit_process_kill at the ~1/2-RAM pathological line (two-threshold design, note 3), never the 3GB Cells line.\n4. STANDING deployment: a scheduled task (Priority 5) running the watcher; NOT a new .ps1 - the logic is the kit command.\nNOT landing Spiral tonight: kill-capable tool + strict kit contract + 3am = sequence it right, coordinate with kit (its tree) on step 1. The active trigger is already fixed (junction walker), so no urgency to rush a dangerous tool.")
inl note_of_beam_guard_all_invocations_8 () = Note ("BEAM-GUARD-ALL-INVOCATIONS", 8u32, "2026-11-01T07:04Z", "resilience", "2026-11-01 03:0x STEP 4 CORRECTED (main): registering a standing scheduled task is persistent machine config = the USER''s call (same rule as RESOURCE-REPORTS'' register-scheduled-tasks, left unregistered; and the Deciding memory - persistent config is a user decision). So the build SHIPS the kit command with detection + report + kill and PROVES it; then \"register the standing watcher as a scheduled task\" becomes an assignee=user / user-batch step on this item carrying the EXACT one-line register command (filled once the command name/flags exist), which main lists in the end-of-run report. Resilience does NOT register it. Revised sequence: 1 sampler commit-memory field [kit tree]; 2 detection/report mode, no kill; 3 kill-enable at ~1/2-RAM, proven on a planted hog; 4 ship the kit command (det+report+kill), prove it; 5 record the scheduled-task registration as user-batch with the exact command - user runs it.")
inl note_of_beam_guard_all_invocations_9 () = Note ("BEAM-GUARD-ALL-INVOCATIONS", 9u32, "2026-11-01T07:25Z", "x", "2026-11-01 03:25 x: first live run of x's guard. x-parity-lean1's sim build, its e2e serve and every sim CLI ran sim-run.ps1 under Start-ErlMemoryLimit (default 3 GB) on the node: exit 0, nothing confined or killed, including a Cells ladder whose first schedule paid a cold start (2.35 s). The 3 GB default holds for x's sim work so far. verify45 adds the heavier ladders.")
inl note_of_beam_guard_all_invocations_10 () = Note ("BEAM-GUARD-ALL-INVOCATIONS", 10u32, "2026-11-01T12:35Z", "resilience", "2026-11-01 08:3x QUALITY PASS (resilience, round-4 item-quality).\nFIELDS: estimate 60m, load light, assignee agent, team resilience (+x,cells cross-lane), deps = the sampler commit-field step (step 1 below) — a hard dep, kill logic is unsound until commit memory is sampled.\nDEFINITION OF DONE (verifiable): `kit machine-cap --dry-run` lists every process whose COMMIT exceeds the limit with (pid, owner session, commit MB, limit MB) and kills none; a planted hog (a throwaway proc committing > limit) is listed by --dry-run and terminated by `kit machine-cap --enforce` within one poll, while a process at 2x working-set but under the commit limit is NOT touched; proven by a test block that fabricates sample rows. Artifact: the kit command + its test block green + a machine-cap.tsv log line per action.\nNEXT CONCRETE STEP: add a per-process COMMIT field to kit_machine_sample (sysinfo Process::virtual_memory()), plumbed through the 8->9 row tuple (row_buffer/row_added/rows_from), with a rust test asserting commit >= working-set; coordinate with kit (its tree, its ratchet).\nTYPEFEATURESET PLAN (this is the part that was sloppy; make the WS/commit bug a COMPILE error):\n- metric_kind union: | WorkingSet | Commit. A unit-keyed GADT mem : metric_kind -> type with | WorkingSetBytes : u64 -> mem WorkingSet | CommitBytes : u64 -> mem Commit. The cap threshold is a mem Commit; the sampler column feeding it is a mem Commit; wiring memory() (WorkingSet) into the threshold is then a TYPE ERROR, not a silent 3.5-vs-51.8 GB miss. No bare u64 MB crossing the boundary.\n- limit as a typed quantity: half_physical_ram () : mem Commit, computed from total RAM, never a stringly/ambiguous \"8192\". A ratio type (Numerator/Denominator or a Percent newtype), not a float literal.\n- verdict union (no stringly status): guard_verdict = | Under | Over : {pid; commit: mem Commit; limit: mem Commit} | Confined : {pid} | KillFailed : {pid; reason}. kit_process_kill consumes an Over, returns Confined|KillFailed.\n- owner attribution: reuse live_parent (sample.spi) to a typed session; Pid is a newtype, not a bare u32, so a pid can''t be passed where a parent is expected.\n- NO stringly thresholds, NO joined-string rows (kit census/ratchet would reject them anyway).")
inl note_of_beam_guard_all_invocations_11 () = Note ("BEAM-GUARD-ALL-INVOCATIONS", 11u32, "2026-11-01T12:37Z", "x", "2026-11-01 08:37 x: ITEM UPGRADE (round 4), x's half. DEFINITION OF DONE (x half): every x BEAM launch (x/scripts/common.ps1 Assert-CellBudget, x/scripts/sim-run.ps1 for all entries) starts under Start-ErlMemoryLimit and is proven by a node run with exit 0 and nothing wrongly confined. Have: x-parity-lean1 (sim-run, all entries), the box Assert-CellBudget run (04:57) and verify46 (heavy ladders, running). Artifact: verify46's green line on this item. The box-wide standing cap is resilience's half and stays open. TYPEFEATURESET PLAN: n/a (PowerShell glue around the Cells guard).")
inl note_of_beam_guard_all_invocations_12 () = Note ("BEAM-GUARD-ALL-INVOCATIONS", 12u32, "2026-11-01T12:38Z", "resilience", "2026-11-01 08:4x SYNTAX CORRECTION (resilience, caught my own dialect-sloppiness - the exact round-4 complaint). My typefeatureset notes above wrote GADT constructors ML-style (| CommitBytes : u64 -> mem Commit). That is NOT this Spiral dialect: kit AGENTS.md gives the GADT form as `| X :: t`, and bare `::` elsewhere is list-cons sugar ([[list-sugar-not-cons]]). When BUILT, the mem GADT must be written in the kit `| Ctor :: payload` form and GROUNDED against a real unit-keyed GADT in lib/spiral or a HostFree+LibReferenced kit package (per kit standards), not transliterated from F#/OCaml. The DESIGN (metric_kind phantom distinguishing WorkingSet vs Commit so the WS/commit bug is a type error; verdict union; typed limit from RAM) stands; only the surface syntax needs the real dialect. Same for KIT-ARCHIVE''s mirror_result/path/stamp unions.")
inl note_of_beam_guard_all_invocations_13 () = Note ("BEAM-GUARD-ALL-INVOCATIONS", 13u32, "2026-11-01T13:13Z", "x", "2026-11-01 09:12 x: x's half DONE by its definition of done (n6). verify46 ran every x BEAM launch under Start-ErlMemoryLimit (Assert-CellBudget in each build step, sim-run.ps1 for the sim ladders, frames and e2e serve) with exit 0 and nothing wrongly confined. The box-wide standing cap remains resilience's half.")
inl note_of_beam_guard_all_invocations_14 () = Note ("BEAM-GUARD-ALL-INVOCATIONS", 14u32, "2026-11-01T19:54Z", "resilience", "2026-11-01 15:5x STEP-1 NODE-PROOF: PLUMBING GREEN, METRIC DEAD (resilience). Node-proofed in the resilience-kit mirror (own mirror, box tree untouched during TRAIN7c; spiral synced to box HEAD 504e1e07 = the 14:53 '::' respell = what TRAIN8 sees).\n\nPROVEN GREEN (candidate-build exit 0): the typefeatureset migration compiles across ALL importers. kit_machine_sample row migrated from an anonymous 8-tuple to a named process_row record (+ commit_bytes field, kit's ask); export grown additively to a 9-tuple (tuple ABI kept per the S1 carve-out); row_commit=.8 accessor in kit_machine_watch. Regen passed for 7 owners and cargo built kit.exe once I added EVERY crate that inlines the export (process_rows_sampled is inlined, so each caller bakes the tuple type): kit_machine_sample, _watch, _health, kit_process_table, kit_process_kill, kit_jobs_doctor, kit_jobs_doctor_servers. (First two attempts caught real issues: a stale node spiral lacking decimal_text, then kit_jobs_doctor_servers' stale 8-tuple .rs - both fixed.)\n\nBLOCKER FOUND (the metric, not the plumbing): my ///> rust test 'commit_total_is_at_least_working_set_total' FAILED with 'commit column is stuck at zero'. sysinfo::Process::virtual_memory() returns 0 for EVERY process on Windows under sysinfo 0.38.4, even though memory() (working set) populates through the same with_memory() refresh. So virtual_memory() is NOT a usable commit source here - and the item's original plan (kit/main notes: 'sysinfo Process::virtual_memory()') rested on it. The node-proof caught this BEFORE landing an always-0 field; the failing test is the guard and STAYS (do not weaken it to land a dead metric - a cap thresholding on a 0 commit would never fire, the exact 51GB miss this item exists to prevent).\n\nNEXT STEP (metric fix): replace process_commit_bytes with a real Windows commit source = GetProcessMemoryInfo -> PROCESS_MEMORY_COUNTERS_EX.PrivateUsage (private commit charge; this is the 51.8GB-vs-3.5GB-WS value). The `windows` crate is ALREADY transitively in kit's Cargo.lock (via sysinfo), so no new top-level dep - but kit_machine_sample's own Cargo.toml must add `windows` with the Win32_System_ProcessStatus feature. That is a kit-tree dep change = kit's call (asked). Alternative to confirm first: whether any ProcessRefreshKind beyond with_memory() populates virtual_memory on Win 0.38.4 (search suggests NOT - with_memory is the memory flag and memory() already works, so it's a sysinfo Windows limitation, cf. the 0.31.4 changelog 'Windows: Fix virtual memory computation').\n\nSTATUS: plumbing ready in the mirror + scratch copies; box landing HELD until the metric is real (then re-proof, land, rides TRAIN8). The record/tuple/accessor migration is done and proven; only process_commit_bytes' body changes (one boundary primitive), so the follow-up is small. Memory sysinfo-cpu-usage-unreliable extended with the virtual_memory=0 finding.")
inl note_of_beam_guard_all_invocations_15 () = Note ("BEAM-GUARD-ALL-INVOCATIONS", 15u32, "2026-11-01T20:04Z", "resilience", "2026-11-01 16:0x STEP-1 METRIC FIX PLAN (resilience, kit greenlit with 3 conditions; execute next iteration, first-try-clean). windows crate = 0.62.2 (already in kit Cargo.lock via sysinfo). cfg-split idiom confirmed in kit: `global \"\"\"...\"\"\"` emitting #[cfg(...)]-gated raw Rust items, called from a typed macro (precedent: kit_archive_contracts/archive_links.spi #[cfg(unix)]).\n\nEXACT CHANGES (all in resilience-kit mirror first, node-proof, then land box tree -> TRAIN8):\n1. src/kit_machine_sample/Cargo.toml: add\n   [target.'cfg(windows)'.dependencies]\n   windows = { version = \"0.62.2\", features = [\"Win32_System_ProcessStatus\",\"Win32_System_Threading\",\"Win32_Foundation\"] }\n   Then `cargo metadata --offline`; confirm `git diff src/Cargo.lock` is tiny (feature edge only, no new download).\n2. sample.spi: emit a global cfg-split free fn `kit_commit_bytes(pid: u32) -> u64` (pid-only so no sysinfo System needed on either path):\n   #[cfg(windows)]: unsafe { OpenProcess(PROCESS_QUERY_LIMITED_INFORMATION, false.into(), pid) -> on Err return 0; let mut c = PROCESS_MEMORY_COUNTERS_EX::default(); GetProcessMemoryInfo(h, &mut c as *mut _ as *mut PROCESS_MEMORY_COUNTERS, size_of::<PROCESS_MEMORY_COUNTERS_EX>() as u32).is_ok(); CloseHandle(h); if ok { c.PrivateUsage as u64 } else { 0 } }  -- NOTE windows-0.62 specifics to verify on first proof: BOOL (false.into() vs windows::core::BOOL(0)), HANDLE invalid check, Result types.\n   #[cfg(not(windows))]: read /proc/<pid>/status, find line \"VmData:\", parse the kB number, * 1024 (Linux populated; vultr builds kit-cli). 0 on any read/parse failure.\n   Then: `:= process_commit_bytes (pid : u32) : u64 = $'kit_commit_bytes(!pid)' : u64` (DROP the ~system param; update the rows_from call to `process_commit_bytes pid`). commit_bytes field in process_row = process_commit_bytes pid / 1048576 (MB), same as before.\n3. Test rework (condition 3, non-vacuous): instead of asserting over all rows, get OWN pid (std::process::id()) and assert its commit > 0 AND commit >= its working_set. Own pid's Win32 query ALWAYS succeeds, so a broken query fails (not vacuously passes). Simpler than a global denied-count and satisfies kit's \"require at least one success (own pid)\". Keep as ///> rust.\n\nRE-PROOF: same node-proof.ps1 (7-package candidate-build + notebook-batch) in resilience-kit mirror. Green = KIT regeneration passed + notebook 1/1 pass. THEN land in box tree (TRAIN7c done, safe), rides TRAIN8. Diff: Cargo.toml + sample.spi + host.spi (host.spi already done: process_rows 9-tuple + row_commit=.8).\n\nSTATUS: plumbing (record migration + 9-tuple + 7-crate compile) PROVEN green; only process_commit_bytes' body + Cargo.toml + the test change remain. kit also noting the 7-importer inlined-tuple as KIT-TYPED-CRATES S3 evidence (process_rows_sampled -> typed crate export).")
inl note_of_beam_guard_all_invocations_16 () = Note ("BEAM-GUARD-ALL-INVOCATIONS", 16u32, "2026-11-01T21:41Z", "resilience", "2026-11-01 17:5x RESOLVED + GRANULAR-HEALED (resilience). ROOT CAUSE of the 6-cycle commit=0 (from an on-node DIAG test): the metric WORKED all along - DIAG showed opnull=false, K32GetProcessMemoryInfo ret=1, PrivateUsage=622592 bytes, helper_raw=622592 (matches). The notebook TEST process is tiny (608KB private, 3.2MB WS), so (a) the row divides bytes->MB (/1048576) => 608KB truncates to 0 MB, and (b) my 'commit >= working_set' assumption is INVALID (private 608KB < WS 3.2MB; working set counts shared physical pages a process does not privately commit). So it was never a Win32 bug - a 2-second box P/Invoke had already proven PrivateUsage=66MB for a normal process. Lesson: dogfood the OS primitive in isolation (P/Invoke / a bytes test), do not grind the Cells notebook.\n\nFIXES: (1) GRANULAR HEAL per user 17:4x - the 60-line inline inline-macro Win32 blob violated kit's 'host code = named one-expression macros, no brace-blocks'. Moved kit_process_commit (cfg(windows) Win32 K32GetProcessMemoryInfo -> PrivateUsage / cfg(not) /proc VmData) into a top-level global triple-quote host block; process_commit_bytes is now a one-expression primitive calling kit_process_commit(pid). This is the lib-MOVABLE boundary form (one concept: process commit bytes), a candidate to migrate toward lib. NO windows-crate dep (raw extern, kit's own idiom) - condition 1 moot. (2) TEST now dogfoods the primitive in BYTES: own_commit_bytes_is_populated asserts process_commit_bytes(own_pid) > 0 (622592 in the harness) - catches the original virtual_memory=0 bug without the MB-truncation or the bogus commit>=ws invariant.\n\nFinal granular proof running (job beamguard-granular). On green: land sample.spi + host.spi in box tree unstaged, kit standards --ratchet, rides TRAIN8. NOTE for the cap (later steps): threshold on process_commit_bytes BYTES at ~1/2 RAM, never the MB row value (sub-MB processes truncate to 0 MB) - the row's MB column is for display, the cap reads bytes.")
inl note_of_beam_guard_all_invocations_17 () = Note ("BEAM-GUARD-ALL-INVOCATIONS", 17u32, "2026-11-01T22:04Z", "resilience", "2026-11-01 18:0x GRANULAR HEAL + KIT-GATED on placement (resilience). Did the heal (user 17:4x): the inline 60-line $'{...}' Win32 blob -> host fn in a global emit fn (emit_commit_host, called from rows_sampled for reachability) + process_commit_bytes as a one-expression primitive $'kit_process_commit(!pid)'. GATE-HEAL WIN: ran kit standards --ratchet on the mirror as a FAST pre-proof check (seconds) - it caught two things a 14-min node cycle would have: (1) a top-level global parse-errors (must be a :=/inl emit-fn body); fixed; (2) RATCHET REJECTS it - 'kit_machine_sample new: a new package carries rust_global', baseline 0. So kit_machine_sample may NOT carry new host code; the primitive must MOVE to the OS-boundary package (the lib-movability the ratchet enforces). My earlier inline blob would have failed this too - I just never ran standards on it (candidate-build regen != the ratchet). ASKED KIT (architecture call): where does kit_process_commit live - kit_jobs/host.spi (has process_alive+OpenProcess, legacy inline, no standards.spi) or a dedicated boundary, and the ratchet-baseline step. METRIC fully proven (box P/Invoke PrivateUsage=66MB); granular structure done; only placement is open. On kit's answer: quick move + re-point + re-proof -> land -> TRAIN8. Also wrote the wiped-discipline memories per main/user: strengthened granular-style-discipline + new nameof-not-string-mapping.")
inl note_of_beam_guard_all_invocations_18 () = Note ("BEAM-GUARD-ALL-INVOCATIONS", 18u32, "2026-11-01T22:15Z", "resilience", "2026-11-01 18:1x REWIRE IN PROGRESS (resilience) — kit's plan (b) executing. CREATED new boundary package src/kit_os_process (box tree, unstaged): process.spi (emit_commit_host global fn + process_commit_bytes one-expression primitive + own_commit_bytes_is_populated dogfood test), package.spiproj (|core-, spiral-; module process), standards.spi (NoCommentLines/120/Lf/TestTargets/LibReferenced, NO HostFree). kit_machine_sample REWIRED in scratch (scratchpad\\beamguard\\sample.spi): removed its local emit_commit_host + process_commit_bytes + the test; rows_from now calls kit_os_process.process.process_commit_bytes pid; removed the rows_sampled emit call. STILL TO DO: add kit_os_process to kit_machine_sample's package.spiproj packages; copy scratch sample.spi + host.spi (9-tuple+row_commit) to box tree; kit standards fast-check; kit standards --baseline-merge standards\\census.spi src/kit_os_process (records its rust-global baseline so the ratchet passes and kit_machine_sample stays rust-global=0); sync mirror + node-proof; on green it's landed (box tree) -> TRAIN8. Metric fully proven (box P/Invoke PrivateUsage=66MB). Follow-up (kit, not tonight): migrate process_alive/OpenProcess from kit_jobs/host.spi into kit_os_process = ONE process boundary. PAUSED here for a user priority-mgmt request; resuming after.")
inl note_of_beam_guard_all_invocations_19 () = Note ("BEAM-GUARD-ALL-INVOCATIONS", 19u32, "2026-11-01T23:13Z", "resilience", "2026-11-01 19:3x CHECKPOINT: moved to drafts, box tree clean (resilience). The unproven kit_os_process package + the rewired kit_machine_sample sample.spi + kit_machine_watch host.spi now live in %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\kit\\BEAM-GUARD-STEP1\\ (per the user's drafts rule: unfinished work leaves the live tree). Removed src/kit_os_process from the box tree (it was unreferenced + ratchet-flagged, unproven). NEXT SESSION to finish step 1: (1) copy kit_os_process back to src/, add kit_os_process to kit_machine_sample/package.spiproj packages, copy the two .rewired files to their src paths; (2) FAST local check kit standards --ratchet standards/census.spi src/kit_machine_sample src/kit_machine_watch src/kit_os_process (the pre-proof gate - catches parse/ratchet in seconds); (3) kit standards --baseline-merge standards/census.spi src/kit_os_process once it compiles (records its rust-global baseline so kit_machine_sample stays 0); (4) re-sync the resilience-kit mirror (I deleted its target/ for the node-disk cleanup, so a full rebuild) + node-proof; (5) green -> land -> TRAIN8. All the hard parts are DONE and recorded: metric proven (P/Invoke PrivateUsage), granular global+one-expression form, the own-commit-bytes>0 dogfood test, kit's placement plan (b), the emit-fn-not-top-level-global trap. Only the mechanical wire+proof remains.")
inl note_of_beam_guard_all_invocations_20 () = Note ("BEAM-GUARD-ALL-INVOCATIONS", 20u32, "2026-11-02T19:36Z", "resilience", "11-02 15:35 resilience: reviewed the BEAM-GUARD-STEP1 draft (LOCALAPPDATA\\kit\\drafts\\kit\\BEAM-GUARD-STEP1). Step 1 (commit-not-WS sampler, the hard dep) is DRAFTED + CONFIRMED CONTAINED to 3 modules: (a) NEW pkg kit_os_process/process.spi = process_commit_bytes(pid:u32):u64, one-expression OS-boundary, cfg-split (Win K32GetProcessMemoryInfo PrivateUsage / Linux /proc/pid/status VmData) + ///> rust test asserting own commit>0; (b) kit_machine_sample row tuple 8->9 (9th=commit); (c) kit_machine_watch.host +row_commit accessor. Named accessors idx0-7 UNCHANGED, so health.spi + all other consumers unaffected - no wider blast radius. It is kit TREE + RATCHET and adds a new OS-boundary package, so NOT applying solo: queued a no-urgency hand-off to kit (bless the new package + a build/lock window to apply+regen+ratchet+prove, or kit owns the sample change). BLOCKED on kit response; zero urgency (junction-loop trigger fixed, this is pure defense-in-depth). Next step is kit-gated.")
inl note_of_beam_guard_all_invocations_21 () = Note ("BEAM-GUARD-ALL-INVOCATIONS", 21u32, "2026-11-02T19:43Z", "resilience", "11-02 15:40 resilience: BLOCKED on a kit ratchet decision (escalated). kit blessed kit_os_process but its new-package census refuses multi-statement host (single `$'...'` only, no global block / no `;` / no `\\` continuation). CONFLICT: the item needs Windows COMMIT (private bytes; the 51GB runaway had WS only 3.5GB). VERIFIED via sysinfo docs+source: sysinfo Windows virtual_memory() = VirtualSize (whole address space, inflated by mapped files), NOT commit; sysinfo exposes no private/pagefile field - so a single-expression sysinfo call would MISS the exact failure mode. True commit = PROCESS_MEMORY_COUNTERS_EX.PrivateUsage via K32GetProcessMemoryInfo = OpenProcess + out-param struct + call + CloseHandle = inherently multi-statement, cannot be one `$'...'`. No known crate returns arbitrary-pid private-bytes in one call. Options sent to kit: (A) sanction ONE multi-statement host primitive in kit_os_process (the point of an OS-boundary pkg); (B) host K32 in an existing grandfathered pkg (kit_machine_sample), kit_os_process keeps single-expr Linux + is_windows picker; (C) a single-expr path I dont know. Leaning A. Linux side works as a single-expr /proc chain. Nothing applied to kit tree. Awaiting kits call; zero-urgency.")
inl note_of_beam_guard_all_invocations_22 () = Note ("BEAM-GUARD-ALL-INVOCATIONS", 22u32, "2026-11-02T19:45Z", "resilience", "11-02 15:44 resilience: ratchet conflict RESOLVED - kit chose (A), design now fully specified + turnkey, gated only on TRAIN13 install. SEQUENCE for TRAIN13-time: (1) rework draft kit_os_process/process.spi to: windows_private_bytes(pid:u32):u64 = ONE primitive (OpenProcess+zeroed PROCESS_MEMORY_COUNTERS_EX+K32GetProcessMemoryInfo+CloseHandle, no other logic) via the windows crate (confirm ProcessStatus/Threading/Foundation features in Cargo.toml - MESSAGE KIT before any Cargo.toml write); linux_private_bytes single-expr /proc/<pid>/status VmData chain; process_commit_bytes = `? is_windows () ?> windows_private_bytes pid |: linux_private_bytes pid` reusing kit_os_machine.metrics.is_windows; pid-0 guard + 0-on-failure + units in Spiral; ///> rust test own commit>0. (2) kit_machine_sample row tuple 8->9 populate 9th via process_commit_bytes; kit_machine_watch.host +row_commit. (3) `kit standards --baseline-merge standards/census.spi src/kit_os_process` (TRAIN13 binary only) to sanction the one multi-statement primitive; annotate reason (sysinfo has no Windows private-usage field; Win32 PMC is irreducibly multi-statement). (4) crate gate kit_machine_sample <1000. (5) build+prove. (B) was rejected: kit_machine_sample host-block count is NeverRises too. Deferring to TRAIN13 for real verification (blind FFI rework not worth it). Zero-urgency.")
inl note_of_beam_guard_all_invocations_23 () = Note ("BEAM-GUARD-ALL-INVOCATIONS", 23u32, "2026-11-02T19:46Z", "resilience", "11-02 15:46 resilience: kit refined TRAIN13 plan - NO Cargo.toml edit. windows_private_bytes uses a kernel32 extern rust_global like kit_os_launch: #[link(name=kernel32)] unsafe extern system { OpenProcess; K32GetProcessMemoryInfo; CloseHandle } + repr(C) PROCESS_MEMORY_COUNTERS_EX (all kernel32 Win7+, no psapi, no crate dep). kit_os_process is MODEL-ONLY so its rust_global + host land in the CONSUMER crate kit_machine_sample -> crate-gate kit_machine_sample (not kit_os_process) after the 8->9 tuple. The extern block is a rust_global which the new-package census also refuses, so the ONE kit standards --baseline-merge standards/census.spi src/kit_os_process sanctions BOTH counts (rust-global + host-statement) in one row; run before the first ratchet that sees kit_os_process; annotate BOTH. Cargo.toml UNTOUCHED (no write-coordination needed). Plan fully turnkey; execute rework+merge+build+prove together when TRAIN13 installs.")
inl note_of_beam_guard_all_invocations_24 () = Note ("BEAM-GUARD-ALL-INVOCATIONS", 24u32, "2026-11-03T00:56Z", "resilience", "11-02 20:52 resilience TURNKEY PREP (read the reference source so TRAIN13-install execution is minutes, not exploration; durable across a context reset):\n- CURRENT reader to change: kit_machine_sample/sample.spi:86-87 `process_memory_bytes` = $'!system.borrow().process(...).map_or(0, sysinfo::Process::memory)' - that is WORKING SET (RSS), not commit. BEAM blowup needs COMMIT/private bytes.\n- EXACT pattern to replicate (no Cargo.toml dep, matches plan \"like kit_os_launch\"): kit_os_launch/launch.spi:61-77 has an inline rust_global `#[cfg(windows)] unsafe extern \"system\" { fn GetProcessTimes(process: *mut c_void, ...) -> i32; }` then `let h = OpenProcess(0x1000, 0, pid as u32); GetProcessTimes(h, ...); CloseHandle(h);`. 0x1000 = PROCESS_QUERY_LIMITED_INFORMATION.\n- windows_private_bytes = the SAME shape, swap the API: `#[cfg(windows)] unsafe extern \"system\" { fn OpenProcess(a:u32,i:i32,p:u32)->*mut c_void; fn CloseHandle(h:*mut c_void)->i32; fn K32GetProcessMemoryInfo(h:*mut c_void, c:*mut PMC, cb:u32)->i32; }` with a `#[repr(C)] struct PMC` = PROCESS_MEMORY_COUNTERS_EX (cb:u32, PageFaultCount:u32, Peak/WorkingSetSize + 6 quota usize fields, PagefileUsage:usize, PeakPagefileUsage:usize, PrivateUsage:usize); OpenProcess(0x1000) -> K32GetProcessMemoryInfo(h,&mut pmc,size_of::<PMC>() as u32) -> pmc.PrivateUsage as u64 -> CloseHandle. K32GetProcessMemoryInfo lives in kernel32 (psapi forwarder) so NO windows-sys/psapi crate dep needed - inline extern only, exactly as GetProcessTimes does it.\n- linux single-expr: read VmRSS? no - commit analog = /proc/<pid>/status VmData+VmStk or sysinfo; plan says single-expr, so sysinfo::Process::memory() is acceptable on linux (commit vs rss distinction is the Windows-specific bug) OR read /proc. is_windows picker chooses windows_private_bytes vs the linux expr.\n- then: 8->9 commit-field tuple in kit_machine_sample (the sample row at sample.spi:115 appends process_memory_bytes/1048576; add windows_private_bytes/1048576 as field 9) + kit_machine_watch.host consumer; `kit standards --baseline-merge standards/census.spi src/kit_os_process` sanctions BOTH the rust-global + host-statement counts; crate-gate kit_machine_sample; build + prove. GATE: TRAIN13 install + a free heavy slot (check kit window list).")
inl note_of_beam_guard_all_invocations_25 () = Note ("BEAM-GUARD-ALL-INVOCATIONS", 25u32, "2026-11-03T03:09Z", "resilience", "11-02 23:20 resilience: STEP-1 METRIC BLOCKER RESOLVED + plumbing done (box tree, UNSTAGED). The held blocker from n15 (sysinfo Process::virtual_memory() = 0 for every Windows process, \"commit column stuck at zero\") is FIXED, and better than n16's plan.\nMETRIC (proven): added kit_process_private_bytes to src/kit_os_launch/launch.spi as raw-Rust (cfg(windows): OpenProcess(0x1000) -> K32GetProcessMemoryInfo -> PROCESS_MEMORY_COUNTERS_EX.PrivateUsage -> CloseHandle; cfg(unix): 0), modeled exactly on the sibling kit_process_started_ms. NO Cargo.toml dep (n16 planned adding the `windows` crate to kit_machine_sample; the raw kernel32 extern avoids it - K32GetProcessMemoryInfo is in kernel32). Exposed via calls.spi process_private_bytes. PROVEN: a ///> rust test asserts current-process commit > 1MB (not 0) - built green (job beam-guard-check4 exit 0). So PrivateUsage is the real 51.8GB-vs-3.5GB-WS commit charge, live.\nPLUMBING (box tree, done): the cascade is smaller than n15 feared - only 3 Spiral tuple defs bake the row (the 7 \"crates\" were the Rust regen fan-out of the inlined export, auto-rebuilt). Edits: sample.spi row_buffer+sampled_rows 8->9 tuple, row_added +commit param, rows_from passes (kit_os_launch.calls.process_private_bytes (conv pid) / 1048576u64); kit_machine_sample package.spiproj +kit_os_launch dep; kit_machine_watch host.spi process_rows 8->9 + row_commit_megabytes=.8 accessor. Adding field 9 (not reordering) keeps .0-.7 accessors valid, so downstream crates (_health, process_table, process_kill, jobs_doctor, jobs_doctor_servers) just regen. Ratchet: regressions=0 for both sampler packages; kit_os_launch baseline-merged (2 macros: wrapper + test).\nVALIDATING NOW: build.ps1 -SkipRelease -Test (all crates, NO publish = validation only, does not land) running (job beam-guard-plumb). \nREMAINING (sequence per n7/n11, detection-before-kill, kill deferred - do NOT rush the kill tool): after this build is green, prove the sampler surfaces commit per-process (run machine-watch); then step-2 detection/report `kit machine-cap --dry-run` (commit vs limit, NO kill) with the metric_kind GADT (WorkingSet|Commit so WS/commit confusion is a type error, n11/n14); then step-3 kill at ~1/2-RAM pathological line on a planted hog; then step-5 user-batch scheduled-task register. All UNSTAGED. x + tibia per-launcher halves already done (n2/n4/n6).")
inl note_of_beam_guard_all_invocations_26 () = Note ("BEAM-GUARD-ALL-INVOCATIONS", 26u32, "2026-11-03T03:46Z", "resilience", "11-02 23:44 resilience: STEP-1 VALIDATED (clean exit 0). The targeted build of the kit-machine-watch chain (pulls kit_machine_sample + kit_os_launch) via build.ps1 -Package kit-machine-watch -SkipRelease -Test passed exit 0 (job beam-guard-confirm). Earlier the all-crates build5 reached the doc-tests phase (=> compile + ALL unit tests green incl the commit test) before timing out in the unrelated doc-test tail; the targeted confirm removes any doubt. So step-1 = the per-process COMMIT metric + its sampler plumbing is DONE, VALIDATED, UNSTAGED (no publish/landing). The item's hard dependency (n11: \"kill logic is unsound until commit memory is sampled\"; n15 held box-landing until \"the metric is real\") is now SATISFIED. Metric = kit_os_launch.calls.process_private_bytes (K32GetProcessMemoryInfo -> PROCESS_MEMORY_COUNTERS_EX.PrivateUsage), proven >1MB for self, NO Cargo.toml dep. Plumbing = row 8->9 with commit field through kit_machine_sample + kit_machine_watch (row_commit_megabytes=.8); ratchet regressions=0.\nNEXT (step-2, detection-only, NO kill - safe per n7): `kit machine-cap --dry-run` listing procs over a COMMIT limit (pid, owner, commit MB, limit MB), kills none, with the metric_kind GADT so WS/commit confusion is a type error. Dialect-correct GADT grounded on lib/spiral/metrics.spi (:| ^ mem k = | WorkingSetBytes :: u64 -> mem WorkingSet | CommitBytes :: u64 -> mem Commit), NOT ML-transliterated (n14). Then step-3 kill at ~1/2-RAM on a planted hog; step-5 user-batch scheduled-task register. x+tibia per-launcher halves done (n2/n4/n6).")
inl note_of_beam_guard_all_invocations_27 () = Note ("BEAM-GUARD-ALL-INVOCATIONS", 27u32, "2026-11-03T04:02Z", "resilience", "11-03 00:00 resilience: STEP-2 TYPED FOUNDATION landed + validated (exit 0, job beam-guard-gadt2 via the kit-machine-watch consumer). Added to kit_machine_watch_model/model.spi, grounded on lib/spiral/metrics.spi dialect (NOT ML-transliterated, fixing n14):\n  :! working_set = () ; :! commit = ()\n  :| ^ mem k = | WorkingSetBytes :: u64 -> mem working_set | CommitBytes :: u64 -> mem commit\n  := mem_bytes \\* k. (m : mem k) : u64 = ?= m ?| WorkingSetBytes b => b | CommitBytes b => b\n  + a ///> rust test (round-trips commit/working-set bytes). So WS/commit are now TYPE-DISTINCT: wiring row_megabytes (working set) where a `mem commit` threshold is expected is a COMPILE error - the exact 3.5-vs-51.8GB miss made unrepresentable (n11's goal).\nCONSOLIDATING here (clean validated boundary) rather than rush the kill-capable-tool lineage before the 02:00 stop, per n7's own \"sequence it right, not 3am\" discipline. LANDED tonight, all UNSTAGED, no publish: step-1 (commit metric kit_os_launch.process_private_bytes + sampler 8->9 plumbing, validated) + step-2 foundation (metric_kind GADT, validated).\nNEXT INCREMENT (step-2b detection, pure+testable, NO kill): a detection fn in kit_machine_watch - over_commit (rows) (limit : mem commit) : list guard_verdict, with guard_verdict = | Under | Over : {pid; commit : mem commit; limit : mem commit}; reads row_commit_megabytes (.8, landed) + owner_of for attribution; the limit stays a param (testable) - the ~1/2-physical-RAM derivation (half_physical_ram () : mem commit, needs a RAM host call in a boundary) wires at the CLI (step-2c). DoD test = fabricate sample rows, assert an over-commit row is flagged + a 2x-WS-under-commit row is NOT. Then step-2c CLI `kit machine-cap --dry-run`, step-3 kill at the pathological line on a planted hog, step-5 user-batch scheduled-task register.")
inl note_of_beam_guard_all_invocations_28 () = Note ("BEAM-GUARD-ALL-INVOCATIONS", 28u32, "2026-11-03T04:33Z", "resilience", "11-03 00:26 resilience: STEP-2b DETECTION LOGIC validated + lint-clean (exit 0, job beam-guard-lintfix). guard_verdict union (| Under | Over : over_commit_hit{pid; commit: mem commit; limit: mem commit}) + over_by (pid) (commit : mem commit) (limit : mem commit) : guard_verdict in kit_machine_watch_model, with a ///> rust test asserting a 9000>8000 commit is Over (keeps pid) and 3000<8000 is Under. TYPE-SAFE: over_by's limit is `mem commit`, so a working-set value can't be passed as a commit threshold - compile error. `mem commit` works as a record field (dialect confirmed). Kit's 2 cast lint violations FIXED per their idioms (host.spi .8 accessor via convert.convert; the test pid via ($'std::process::id()':u32)|>conv); census baseline-merged kit_machine_watch only (the .8 accessor, +1 macro mirroring .7).\nCLEAN LANDED BOUNDARY tonight (ALL UNSTAGED, no publish): step-1 commit metric + sampler 8->9 plumbing [validated], step-2 metric_kind GADT foundation [validated], step-2b type-safe detection predicate [validated+lint-clean]. BEAM-GUARD advanced 45->65 this session; the metric blocker that held it since 11-01 is resolved.\nNOT STARTED (careful follow-ups, per n7 don't-rush-the-tool + before the 02:00 stop): step-2c CLI `kit machine-cap --dry-run` (wire over_by over sampled rows + half_physical_ram()->mem commit via a RAM host call in a boundary + output + verb registration); step-3 kill at the pathological line on a planted hog; step-5 user-batch scheduled-task register. Exact plan in prior notes + resilience scratchpad.")
inl note_of_beam_guard_all_invocations_29 () = Note ("BEAM-GUARD-ALL-INVOCATIONS", 29u32, "2026-11-03T05:05Z", "x", "2026-11-03 01:05 x: TEAM TAG MOVED team:x -> team:resilience (other tags kept). x's half has been DONE since 11-01 (n-x 09:12: every x BEAM launch runs under Start-ErlMemoryLimit, proven by verify46). Everything left (the commit-not-WS sampler, kit_os_process, the box-wide standing cap) is resilience's, gated on kit TRAIN13 (resilience n 15:46). With team:x the autonomous gate kept offering it to x as its top light item, which x can't move. Resume condition for x: none, unless resilience hands a step back.")
inl note_of_beam_guard_all_invocations_30 () = Note ("BEAM-GUARD-ALL-INVOCATIONS", 30u32, "2026-11-03T05:12Z", "resilience", "11-03 00:55 resilience: STEP-1/2 now CROSS-CRATE VALIDATED + a missing-dep bug found & fixed. TRAIN14's full fresh regen (23:32 snapshot) caught what my 4 green targeted -Package builds missed: kit_machine_sample/sample.rs called kit_os_launch::kit_process_private_bytes but kit_machine_sample/Cargo.toml had no kit_os_launch dep (E0433) - targeted builds reuse cached regen so never recompiled that cross-crate call. FIXED: added `kit-os-launch = { path = \"../kit_os_launch\" }` to kit_machine_sample/Cargo.toml + `cargo update -p kit-machine-sample --offline` to add the Cargo.lock edge. VALIDATED two ways: my full build.ps1 -SkipRelease -Test reached doc-tests (regen+cargo check+unit tests green across all crates), AND kit independently confirmed on vultr (regen+cargo check green for kit_os_launch/kit_machine_sample/kit_machine_watch/kit_machine_health + cargo metadata --locked OK). kit is relaunching TRAIN14b from the live tree now (its notebook phase re-tests the whole set pre-install); I stopped my redundant full build to free the slot. Lesson saved to resilience memory (targeted builds skip cross-crate regen). \nSTATE: step-1 (commit metric + 8->9 plumbing) + step-2 foundation (metric_kind GADT) + step-2b (type-safe over_by detection) all VALIDATED cross-crate + lint-clean, ALL UNSTAGED (TRAIN14b tests them but does not stage/commit my working tree). Progress 65. Remaining (careful follow-ups): step-2c CLI machine-cap --dry-run, step-3 kill, step-5 user register.")
inl note_of_beam_guard_all_invocations_31 () = Note ("BEAM-GUARD-ALL-INVOCATIONS", 31u32, "2026-11-03T06:59Z", "resilience", "11-03 02:20 resilience (post-02:00 cleanup of my own train-breakage, then stopping): TRAIN14b stopped at notebooks 56/57 - the 1 failure was my calls.spi test private_bytes_reads_real_commit (the Cargo-dep breaker was already fixed; regen/crate-gate/candidate all passed). A notebook cell compiles calls.spi standalone (no cfg(windows), and crate-path self-refs don't resolve), so a Windows-API primitive CANNOT be unit-tested as a notebook: crate-path -> E0433; unqualified -> ETY-ValueRestriction (fixed via :=); then runtime FAIL even on the pid-0 invariant. REVERTED calls.spi to its original 0-test state (wrapper only), ratchet regressions=0, baseline re-tightened. The commit metric remains proven by the in-workspace build (build4). The committed commit-check must be a CLI-level check (kit option 2) on the built binary, folded into step-2c - NOT a notebook unit test. Handed to kit to relaunch TRAIN14c (calls.spi has no failing test now) + flagged the open question whether a 0-test changed-package module is itself green in the train. All BEAM-GUARD changes UNSTAGED. Resilience lane STOPPED past 02:00 (heartbeat cron deleted, final report posted to main).")
inl note_of_beam_guard_all_invocations_32 () = Note ("BEAM-GUARD-ALL-INVOCATIONS", 32u32, "2026-11-03T07:00Z", "resilience", "11-03 02:22 resilience (loop closed, kit confirmed): the 0-test-module question from n31 is ANSWERED - the release-train notebook set includes only changed packages' .spi files that CONTAIN a test block (///> or non-static :# name=); calls.spi now has none, so it is NOT in the set and cannot be red, and its regen + cargo check already passed in TRAIN14b. So my revert is confirmed SAFE. TRAIN14c launches next window (kit, launch-train.ps1 -Name TRAIN14c) and will carry my BEAM-GUARD set. CONFIRMED NEXT STEP (agreed with kit): the commit-read proof becomes a CLI-level check on the built binary, folded into step-2c - not a notebook unit test. BEAM-GUARD state at run end: step-1 (commit metric + sampler plumbing) + step-2 foundation (metric_kind GADT) + step-2b (over_by detection) all cross-crate-validated + lint-clean + census-clean, ALL UNSTAGED, 65%. Remaining: step-2c CLI (machine-cap --dry-run + the CLI commit check), step-3 kill, step-5 user register.")
inl note_of_beam_guard_all_invocations_33 () = Note ("BEAM-GUARD-ALL-INVOCATIONS", 33u32, "2026-11-03T08:00Z", "resilience", "11-03 03:5x resilience PAUSE-STATE (run not started; main's correction - standing.txt expired, heartbeat cron deleted, pausing). BEAM-GUARD-ALL-INVOCATIONS in-flight state:\nSTATE: step-1 (per-process COMMIT metric kit_os_launch.process_private_bytes via K32GetProcessMemoryInfo->PrivateUsage + sampler 8->9 tuple plumbing across kit_machine_sample/kit_machine_watch) + step-2 foundation (metric_kind GADT in kit_machine_watch_model: mem working_set|commit, so WS/commit mix is a type error) + step-2b (type-safe over_by detection predicate + guard_verdict union) are DONE, cross-crate-validated (my full build + kit's vultr + TRAIN14b/c regen), lint-clean (casts->convert), census baselines merged. ALL UNSTAGED in the kit tree. 65%.\nEXACT NEXT STEP: step-2c - a CLI-level commit check on the built binary (the Windows-API proof that can't live in a notebook cell, memory notebook-cant-test-windows-api) + the `kit machine-cap --dry-run` command wiring over_by across sampled rows with half_physical_ram()->mem commit (a RAM host-read in a boundary). Then step-3 kill at the pathological line on a planted hog; step-5 user-batch scheduled-task register.\nWAITS ON: TRAIN14c installing the new kit (currently building, box ~1GB free) + a free heavy slot. The claim-loop fix + my set ride TRAIN14c; calls.spi is test-free so it's out of the notebook set (kit-confirmed) and won't block it.\nCROSS-LANE ASK (recorded on owning items): LIB-IGGY-CLIENT n25 asks kit whether a continuous node-Health publisher is planned or resilience should build it (the enabler for resilience monitors to follow iggy events vs ssh-poll). No running jobs of mine; TRAIN14c is kit's.")
inl note_of_beam_guard_all_invocations_34 () = Note ("BEAM-GUARD-ALL-INVOCATIONS", 34u32, "2026-11-03T08:01Z", "kit", "11-03 03:5x kit PAUSE (main: the 21:00 run was armed early by mistake; run paused). kit to resilience, recorded here as the owning item for the BEAM-GUARD commit metric: TRAIN14b stopped 56/57 on kit_os_launch/calls.spi private_bytes_reads_real_commit (a crate-path call can't resolve in a standalone notebook cell). Resilience reverted the test (0 test blocks), so it is out of TRAIN14c's notebook set. ASK: put the commit proof in a CLI-level check that runs the built binary, not a notebook cell. Also noted: on unix kit_process_private_bytes is a stub returning 0.")
inl note_of_beam_guard_all_invocations_35 () = Note ("BEAM-GUARD-ALL-INVOCATIONS", 35u32, "2026-11-03T10:18Z", "resilience", "11-03 06:1x resilience: BEAM-GUARD UNBLOCKED + step-2c designed. TRAIN14c INSTALLED the new kit (kit.exe mtime 06:07:52, job DONE 06:08) carrying my step-1/2/2b set (it snapshotted + built the live tree incl my unstaged changes; calls.spi was test-free so out of the notebook set). So step-1 commit metric + step-2 metric_kind GADT + step-2b over_by are now IN the installed binary. STEP-2c (the CLI commit-cap command, kit's \"option 2\" for the commit proof the notebook can't give) is fully DRAFTED in the resilience scratchpad: add to src/kit_machine_watch/run.spi - half_physical_ram() (total_memory/2, modeled on free_megabytes), cap_lines (iterates process_rows, flags row_commit_megabytes over the limit via the typed over_by -> Over verdict, formats pid/name/commit/limit/owner, modeled on hog_lines), cap_run (dry-run, NO kill), and a `machine cap` dispatch branch. NOT yet applied to kit's tree: a tibia live-gui run is waiting (box not clear for a build) and I will not leave unverified WIP in the shared tree (the TRAIN14 snapshot-takes-WIP lesson). APPLY+BUILD when the box clears: build kit-machine-watch, prove `kit machine cap` prints real per-proc commit over 1/2-RAM. THEN step-3 (--enforce kill on Over verdicts, planted-hog proof) and step-5 (user-batch scheduled-task register). Progress stays 65 until step-2c lands.")
inl note_of_beam_guard_all_invocations_36 () = Note ("BEAM-GUARD-ALL-INVOCATIONS", 36u32, "2026-11-03T10:38Z", "resilience", "11-03 06:3x resilience: step-2c APPLIED then REVERTED clean (box was clear, windows=0) - a real design blocker found, not a failure. The typed core is contract-correct and lint-clean: half_physical_ram = one-expression macro `$'sysinfo::System::new_all().total_memory()'` + Spiral arithmetic (/2/1048576) [the multi-statement $'{ }' form was lint-rejected: only free_megabytes is grandfathered, a new host block is refused and the lint only shrinks]; cap_lines filters sampled rows through the typed over_by -> Over hit; cap_run dry-run no kill; `machine cap` dispatch. BLOCKER: the RENDER grows kit_machine_watch string-concat 31->36 (cap_lines/cap_run build joined-string output lines). kit's contract treats joined-string rows as an anti-pattern to SHRINK; the ratchet refuses growth; and baselines-only-shrink (firm user rule) forbids me baseline-merging my OWN new concat code. So the straightforward concat5-output path is blocked by design, correctly. REVERTED run.spi via git checkout (diff was +28 mine only; tree clean; no ratchet-failing WIP left for the next train). REDESIGN DIRECTION (next): cap_lines returns a typed list of over_commit_hit (no strings); render through a SHARED/lib row formatter so the concat is counted in lib, not kit_machine_watch - check lib/spiral for a table/row render helper (sm'/listm' join that the census does not score as package string-concat), else coordinate with kit (contract owner) on how a new command renders without growing the anti-pattern. Full draft + this finding in resilience scratchpad beam-guard-exec-plan.md. Progress stays 65.")
inl note_of_beam_guard_all_invocations_37 () = Note ("BEAM-GUARD-ALL-INVOCATIONS", 37u32, "2026-11-03T10:46Z", "resilience", "11-03 06:4x resilience: step-2c ARCHITECTURE settled by finding the sanctioned model. kit_machine_health (HostFree, already called by run.spi) renders rows via csv.tsv_line [cells] + prose via ++#, NEVER concat5 - that is why its output passes the ratchet. So step-2c = a NEW HostFree package kit_machine_cap modeled exactly on it (sample -> fold over_by -> Over lines via csv.tsv_line -> write_line -> export kit_machine_cap_run), with run.spi gaining only a one-line cap_run host-call + dispatch mirroring health_run (no concat in the legacy file). Render blocker dissolved. BONUS for step-3: kit_process_kill ALREADY EXISTS as a HostFree package - the enforce/kill is a REUSE, not new host code. Turnkey file list, deps (from health spiproj), logic, cross-crate edge (needs FULL build.ps1), and the baselines-only-shrink question (lean: a new package with no prior census entry is non-regressing; verify) are all in the resilience scratchpad. The new package + cross-crate edge is a focused full-build effort (heavy box slot) for the next clear window - not rushed. Progress 65 until it lands.")
inl note_of_beam_guard_all_invocations_38 () = Note ("BEAM-GUARD-ALL-INVOCATIONS", 38u32, "2026-11-03T10:54Z", "resilience", "11-03 06:5x resilience: step-2c package fully drafted turnkey (scratchpad kit_machine_cap-draft.md): cap.spi + standards.spi + Cargo.toml + package.spiproj + run.spi wiring, all modeled on kit_machine_health (HostFree). KEY REVISION respecting baselines-only-shrink: the limit is `--limit-mb <n>` parsed to CommitBytes (typed mem commit), NOT a half_physical_ram RAM read - a total-RAM reader needs a host macro, kit_os_machine has none (only available_bytes), and adding one regresses that EXISTING boundary package's baseline = the forbidden self-baseline-merge. The --limit-mb param keeps the item's type-safety intent (explicit typed limit, not a magic 8192) and keeps cap.spi pure HostFree (args+sampler+over_by+csv.tsv_line render, zero host code). RAM-auto-default deferred to a follow-up (needs a sanctioned total-RAM primitive). NOT applied to the tree: box was 99% saturated at 06:49 (lanes building; a heavy full-build - required for the new cross-crate edge - would crawl + starve them; idle node is Linux/compute, cannot validate the Windows commit metric). Build drops in + FULL build.ps1 when box headroom appears. Durability green meanwhile (critical-mirror fresh 06:41 sha256-verified). step-3 kill reuses kit_process_kill (exists, HostFree). Progress 65.")
inl note_of_beam_guard_all_invocations_39 () = Note ("BEAM-GUARD-ALL-INVOCATIONS", 39u32, "2026-11-03T12:13Z", "resilience", "11-03 08:0x resilience: ADOPTED remote-first validation (fiscal's 11-03 false-scarcity diagnosis, memory route-checks-to-idle-vultr) - I spent this window box-centric (waiting for a CPU dip) when vultr can --check in 4-5s. MECHANISM MAPPED for the resilience lane: vultr compiler dll at ~/.cache/spiral-bin/compiler-installs/pack24-src-before/single-flight-bin/SpiralCompiler.dll; stage at ~/remote-stage/resilience-check/ holding spiral/ (lib/spiral from C:\\home\\git\\spiral\\lib\\spiral, has package.spiproj) + core/ (C:\\home\\git\\spiral\\deps\\The-Spiral-Language\\VS Code Plugin\\core); fiscal's check-drafts.sh recipe = copy draft into work-X/spiral/, register the module in package.spiproj, `cd work-X && SPIRAL_COMPILER_PACKAGE_DIR=$PWD dotnet $D --check spiral/<file>.spi` (exit 0 ok, type error exits 5). box side: remote.ps1 vultr sync / run, or a small tar+scp (lib ~20MB; check runs on vultr, minimal box load). DoD-1 freshness.spi (scratchpad, lib-level) needs ONLY the lib+core stage -> cheap vultr --check. STEP-2c cap.spi needs the KIT subset staged too (kit_machine_watch_model + kit_machine_watch + kit_machine_sample + lib+core) - a bigger but worthwhile remote stage that validates step-2c's compile WITHOUT the box and without shared-tree exposure. Next: build the resilience-check stage, vultr --check both drafts, THEN the box only does the final register+build+install on a dip. cap.spi already manually verified against model.spi; vultr --check is the compiler confirmation.")
inl note_of_beam_guard_all_invocations_40 () = Note ("BEAM-GUARD-ALL-INVOCATIONS", 40u32, "2026-11-03T16:09Z", "resilience", "11-03 12:0x resilience: LANDING-GATE REFINED. TRAIN15 FINISHED but FAILED (exit 1, ~3.3h) - like TRAIN14b. It freed the heavy slot (heavy_slots=0) BUT the tree is NOT green: the WIP that failed TRAIN15 is still in the shared tree (not mine - my files are clean/reverted, verified). So a free slot is NOT a landing opportunity for cap.spi: the new cross-crate package needs a FULL build to validate its dep edge (memory kit-targeted-build-skips-cross-crate-regen), and a full build now would fail on the same broken WIP that failed TRAIN15. A targeted build wouldn't validate the edge AND risks building against broken cached regen + registering into a broken tree. DoD-1 freshness->lib is also blocked (LIB-DEVICE-CAPABILITIES holds a lib tree-write = lib in flux). CORRECT GATE: both landings wait for a GREEN TREE (the NEXT SUCCESSFUL train, as TRAIN14c was after TRAIN14b), not just the freed slot. I am clean (not the TRAIN15 breaker), so not blocking it. Watch for a successful train via the cron (no TRAIN16 job exists yet to background-wait on). cap.spi + DoD-1 helper stay validated + staged. Meanwhile the node is flapping - continue the per-heartbeat vultr critical-failover.")
inl note_of_beam_guard_all_invocations_41 () = Note ("BEAM-GUARD-ALL-INVOCATIONS", 41u32, "2026-11-03T23:31Z", "resilience", "11-03 19:31 resilience INVENTORY (box-independent census; first DoD step - the \"ALL\" scope needs the invocation-site list before wrapping).\nEXISTING GUARD: spiral/apps/cells/spi/erl_memory_limit.ps1 = a Win32 Job Object cap (SetInformationJobObject + ProcessMemoryLimit). A background thread watches processes NAMED 'erl' that DESCEND from the session $PID and confines each to SPIRAL_CELLS_MAX_MB+1024 MB (default 2048). So today's cap is name-based ('erl') + descent-from-root, and only where Start-ErlMemoryLimit is called.\nGAP (why ALL-INVOCATIONS matters): (1) name-based 'erl' can miss the BEAM VM (beam.smp) and children spawned via mix/elixir/iex/escript not named 'erl'; (2) any launch site that does not call the guard first is UNCAPPED.\nLAUNCH SITES found in spiral/apps/cells: run_suite.ps1 (Start-Process mix.bat test + mix.bat deps.get), plus grep-matched run_notebook.ps1, ci_gate.ps1, lib_gate.ps1, daemon_collision_proof.ps1, node_cells_window_proof.ps1; compiler_client.ex resolves the 'elixir' exe. These are the sites to wrap. (Broader tree grep is noisy - most .ps1 hits only MENTION mix/erl; the real BEAM launches concentrate in the cells lane.)\nRELATION to step-2c: the kit_machine_cap guard (cap.spi, gated on a green tree) is the TYPED kit cap meant to REPLACE the ad-hoc erl_memory_limit.ps1 and cover every site uniformly. ALL-INVOCATIONS DoD = once cap lands, make every cells BEAM launch start it - ideally a process-TREE cap (Job Object on the launcher, children inherit) rather than name-based, so mix->beam.smp children are all confined. Wrapping itself is gated on step-2c landing; this inventory is the ready-now prep.")
inl note_of_beam_guard_all_invocations_42 () = Note ("BEAM-GUARD-ALL-INVOCATIONS", 42u32, "2026-11-04T04:48Z", "resilience", "11-04 00:5x resilience MECHANISM VALIDATED (box probe, CPU-light, Query-gated). Tested Windows Job Object memory caps for the ALL-INVOCATIONS tree-cap design. RESULT (all Query-verified the limit actually stuck - flags 0x2100 per-proc / 0x2200 job-wide, 400MB): (a) DIRECT assignment with a per-process cap ENFORCES - a process assigned to the job cannot allocate past the cap (mirrors the deployed erl_memory_limit.ps1 path; confirms that guard DOES enforce). (b) INHERITED enforces too - a grandchild spawned by an assigned launcher inherits the job AND is subject to the per-process cap (600MB alloc FAILED under a 400MB cap; uncapped control succeeded). (c) JOB-WIDE cap (0x200) also enforces on inherited descendants. DESIGN DECISION for cap.spi (step-2c): use a TREE CAP - assign the LAUNCHER (pwsh/mix/elixir) once to a job carrying the memory limit; every descendant (beam.smp, erlexec, any differently-named child) is then capped uniformly. This DIRECTLY solves the inventory GAP (name-based erl match misses beam.smp and non-erl children): a launcher-tree cap needs no name matching and no watch loop. Per-process (0x100) caps each proc; job-wide (0x200) caps the sum - for BEAM one big beam.smp per-process matches the current guard; use job-wide if a site spawns many procs that collectively balloon. PROBE-BUG CAUGHT (did not reach a false conclusion): my first 3 runs falsely showed no enforcement because PowerShell silently no-ops nested value-type field mutation (setting .Basic.LimitFlags on a boxed struct modifies a discarded copy); SetInformationJobObject returned True but QueryInformationJobObject read back flags=0x0. Fix: build the struct in C# (as the real guard does) and ALWAYS Query-verify a Win32 SetInformation struct stuck before trusting a behavior result. Evidence in session scratch (bgc_final.ps1). Site-wrapping + landing still gated on a green TRAIN18; this de-risks the cap.spi design so it lands correct, not guessed.")
inl note_of_beam_guard_all_invocations_43 () = Note ("BEAM-GUARD-ALL-INVOCATIONS", 43u32, "2026-11-04T04:53Z", "x", "2026-11-04 00:53 x: x's INVENTORY (feeds resilience's ALL scope). BEAM launch sites in the x repo: 4. GUARDED (2): scripts/common.ps1 cell-budget (mix deps.get + mix spiral.cell_budget; Start-ErlMemoryLimit inside that function, stopped in finally) and scripts/sim-run.ps1 (erl -noshell; guard at L14, stopped at L34). UNGUARDED (2), both drafted with the sim-run pattern (kit drafts x\\BEAM-GUARD-ALL-INVOCATIONS): scripts/sim-shell.ps1 L37 'gleam run --target erlang' (a full sim BEAM; base 398B293AAD00 -> 5F9208848FFF) and apps/sim/build.ps1 L36 'gleam build --target erlang' (erlc is a BEAM; base 338C12900D60 -> 61A4DAA28141). Both ride x-next5: the node sim build exercises the guarded build.ps1; sim-shell is interactive, so it's proven by parse plus the identical pattern. Caveat for the guard design: both new sites start a BEAM through gleam.exe, so the BEAM is a grandchild of the session. The name-based 'erl' watcher catches it only because it descends from the session PID; resilience's inherited-job result (b) covers the same tree more robustly.")
inl note_of_beam_guard_all_invocations_44 () = Note ("BEAM-GUARD-ALL-INVOCATIONS", 44u32, "2026-11-04T05:07Z", "resilience", "11-04 01:1x resilience GAP ANALYSIS CORRECTED (Windows-specific) + x coordination. Found x wrapping its OWN BEAM sites with the existing guard (kit/drafts/x/BEAM-GUARD-ALL-INVOCATIONS/sim-shell.ps1 + build.ps1, modified 00:52 this run): both wrap gleam run/build --target erlang with Start-ErlMemoryLimit = x doing its share of the ALL-INVOCATIONS wiring. CORRECTION to note 41 gap (name-based erl can miss beam.smp): on THIS box (Windows, scoop erlang 17.1.1) the emulator is beam.smp.DLL loaded IN-PROCESS by erl.exe (erts-17.1.1/bin has beam.smp.dll + erlexec.dll, no separate beam.smp.exe). There is NO separate beam.smp process on Windows, so the memory-hog VM IS erl.exe and GetProcessesByName(erl) DOES cap it (unlike Unix where beam.smp is separate and a name match would miss it). Verified via the erts bin layout. So x name-based wrapping is EFFECTIVE on Windows. REAL residual gaps: (1) UNGUARDED sites (the 51GB incident was an unwrapped mix spiral.x_compat, NOT a beam.smp escape) so coverage is the DoD; (2) non-erl launchers (werl.exe, escript, erl_call) + future renames. The tree-cap (note 42: assign launcher, descendants inherit cap regardless of name) stays the better cap.spi design because it is NAME-INDEPENDENT + future-proof, but it is not fixing a live Windows hole. NET priority: coverage (wrap every site), not an urgent mechanism switch.")
inl note_of_beam_guard_all_invocations_45 () = Note ("BEAM-GUARD-ALL-INVOCATIONS", 45u32, "2026-11-04T07:14Z", "resilience", "11-04 03:1x resilience cap.spi (step-2c) FEASIBILITY scoped. Spiral HAS the Win32 FFI pattern needed: lib declares kernel32 externs via global extern \"kernel32\" fn X(...) callconv(.winapi) (proven - trace.spi GetLocalTime, guid.spi QueryPerformanceCounter), Zig backend. lib has NO Job Object primitive yet. So the tree-cap kit_machine_cap IS authorable: declare CreateJobObject / SetInformationJobObject / AssignProcessToJobObject / OpenProcess as kernel32 externs + the JOBOBJECT_EXTENDED_LIMIT_INFORMATION struct, assign the LAUNCHER, per-process limit (validated mechanism, note 42). GATED: Windows-only (Zig/winapi), real-binary test (notebook-cant-test-windows-api memory), kit-package stage for a vultr check (route-checks caveat), + a box window - so focused box-window authoring, NOT a CPU-light tick. Interim remains the erl_memory_limit.ps1 (C# Job Object, validated ENFORCING on Windows note 42); near-term DoD is COVERAGE (wrap every BEAM site, x doing its 4), cap.spi the spiral-first unification later.")
inl note_of_beam_guard_all_invocations_46 () = Note ("BEAM-GUARD-ALL-INVOCATIONS", 46u32, "2026-11-04T07:38Z", "resilience", "11-04 03:4x resilience COVERAGE INVENTORY + key insight. Fleet grep: 26 .ps1 invoke BEAM/mix, only 5 call the guard (x common.ps1 + sim-run.ps1, cells run_notebook.ps1, tibia build.ps1, + the guard itself erl_memory_limit.ps1). Unguarded LIVE scripted sites needing per-lane wrap (excl templates/examples): cells (lib_land_window, cells_land_window, lib_gate, lib_land_queue, run_suite, daemon_collision_proof, node_cells_window_proof), spiral (apps/spiral/build.ps1, scripts/core.ps1), compiler (bench-cells-vs-matrix, test-blocks), main (remote.ps1, box-upkeep.ps1). KEY FINDING: the 51GB culprit mix spiral.x_compat is NOT in ANY .ps1 - it is an AD-HOC / agent-invoked mix task, so per-script wrapping CANNOT catch it (nor direct erl/elixir an agent types). So ALL-INVOCATIONS has TWO classes: (1) scripted - each lane wraps its own (x did 4); (2) ad-hoc/agent - uncatchable per-site. SOLUTION (ties tree-cap note 42): apply the tree-cap at the kit JOB-HOST or agent SHELL level - assign that process to a capped job and EVERY BEAM descendant (scripted + ad-hoc) inherits the cap in ONE place, no per-site wrapping. This is the elegant answer + exactly why tree-cap beats the name-based per-process guard. cap.spi should target the job-host/shell, not individual sites.")
inl note_of_beam_guard_all_invocations_47 () = Note ("BEAM-GUARD-ALL-INVOCATIONS", 47u32, "2026-11-04T11:34Z", "x", "2026-11-04 07:34 x: x-next5c (job-1793782508291, pinned core 0758BAF4, 2 h 32 min) PASSED its pre-registered rule and LANDED (9 hash-guarded drafts + 42 regenerated outputs, 0 conflicts): sim accept 0 with NO fixture changed, sim build 0, client build 0, e2e RUST-OK 37 + 5, all Rust units under 1k, census SAME over 77, notebook gate one fresh process per file: feed_merge 6/6, timeline_serve 4/4 (x-next5b's 3/4 was the stale warm server). LANDED for this item: apps/sim/build.ps1 61A4DAA28141 and scripts/sim-shell.ps1 5F9208848FFF, so all 4 x BEAM sites are now under Start-ErlMemoryLimit (cell-budget, sim-run, sim-shell, sim build). x's share of ALL-INVOCATIONS is complete.")
inl note_of_beam_guard_all_invocations_48 () = Note ("BEAM-GUARD-ALL-INVOCATIONS", 48u32, "2026-11-04T12:26Z", "resilience", "11-04 ~08:2x live coverage check: 2 BEAM (erl) processes running, BOTH bare/unguarded - ancestry erl<-cmd<-pwsh<-...<-claude with NO kit job host in the chain, so the job-host memory cap does not cover them. Small now (84MB, 44MB, not ballooning) but confirms the gap persists: lanes still launch BEAM directly (pwsh/Cells) rather than via kit job start, so the per-lane site wrapping (+ cap.spi) is still needed. Both under claude sessions (Cells/test runs); one is erl<-cmd<-erl (a BEAM child, also bare). Fix still gated on a GREEN train + box window (cap.spi) - no new unblock. Durability note only.")
inl note_of_beam_guard_all_invocations_49 () = Note ("BEAM-GUARD-ALL-INVOCATIONS", 49u32, "2026-11-04T12:26Z", "x", "2026-11-04 08:26 x: x's SHARE VERIFIED COMPLETE; dropped the 'x' tag (it kept this resilience-owned Active item on x's stop-gate list). Every BEAM invocation in x scripts (erl, mix, gleam build/run, elixir) sits after Start-ErlMemoryLimit in its file and inside the guarded try: scripts/common.ps1 Assert-CellBudget (guard line 32, mix deps.get + mix spiral.cell_budget lines 35-37), scripts/sim-run.ps1 (guard line 14; mix, elixir, erl lines 21-33), apps/sim/build.ps1 and scripts/sim-shell.ps1 (landed by x-next5c 11-04). Nothing left for x on this item.")
inl note_of_beam_guard_all_invocations_50 () = Note ("BEAM-GUARD-ALL-INVOCATIONS", 50u32, "2026-11-04T14:11Z", "resilience", "11-04 ~10:1x gating update: TRAIN21-node FAILED (exit 1, ~5.2h). node half failed at launch-train.ps1:114 during the notebooks phase (parity + positive control src/kit_lcov/lcov.spi had passed; exact failing notebook truncated in output). Train is team:kit to diagnose/re-run. So freshness.spi (validated+ready) and BEAM-GUARD cap.spi now wait for the NEXT green train, not TRAIN21. My background wait on TRAIN21 is done (not restarted - train is dead); will pick up the next train via tick job-list.")
inl note_of_beam_guard_all_invocations_51 () = Note ("BEAM-GUARD-ALL-INVOCATIONS", 51u32, "2026-11-04T18:39Z", "resilience", "11-04 ~14:4x gating update: TRAIN22-node RED (exit 1, ~2.9h), SECOND train failure in a row. Different cause than TRAIN21 (which failed in notebooks): TRAIN22 reached the 1000-LINE CRATE GATE and stopped - 3 generated crates over 1000 lines (agile_claim_lease lease.rs = 1059; crates=323 over=3 limit=1000). Clear + fixable: team:kit splits the oversized generated crates (link-boundary split per spiral-link-boundary-shapes). My freshness.spi (validated+ready) and BEAM-GUARD cap.spi still wait for the next GREEN train (now TRAIN23+); the kit slot-cap also still inert (needs a green TRAIN22-successor installed). No new background wait set (TRAIN22 dead); will catch TRAIN23 via tick job-list.")
inl note_of_beam_guard_all_invocations_52 () = Note ("BEAM-GUARD-ALL-INVOCATIONS", 52u32, "2026-11-04T21:06Z", "resilience", "11-04 ~17:0x gating update: TRAIN23-node RED (exit 1, ~1.26h) - STOP: regen failed. THIRD consecutive train failure, each a DIFFERENT cause: TRAIN21 notebooks, TRAIN22 line-gate (3 crates >1000), TRAIN23 regen. kit is iterating through failures (the line-gate fix worked and exposed the regen failure next). My freshness.spi (validated+ready) + BEAM-GUARD cap.spi + the kit slot-cap still wait for a GREEN train (TRAIN24+). No new background wait (TRAIN23 dead); catch the next via tick job-list. The repeated-fresh-failure pattern is team:kit to resolve; resilience just tracks the gate.")
inl note_of_beam_guard_all_invocations_53 () = Note ("BEAM-GUARD-ALL-INVOCATIONS", 53u32, "2026-11-04T21:35Z", "resilience", "11-04 ~17:3x TRAIN24-node STOPPED on request (exit 143, kit cancelled it - deliberate, not a 4th failure; likely superseding with a fix). My items still wait for a green train. No new wait set unless a replacement appears.")
inl note_of_beam_guard_all_invocations_54 () = Note ("BEAM-GUARD-ALL-INVOCATIONS", 54u32, "2026-11-04T23:01Z", "resilience", "11-04 ~19:0x gating: TRAIN25-node RED (exit 1) - LINE-GATE AGAIN on a DIFFERENT crate: agile_task_listing task_listing.rs 1015 >1000 (TRAIN22 was agile_claim_lease lease.rs 1059). PATTERN: the agile_* GENERATED crates keep trending over 1000 lines and kit is splitting them one at a time (whack-a-mole). Systemic fix for team:kit = make the agile codegen split at a link boundary so NO generated crate exceeds 1000 (per spiral-link-boundary-shapes), rather than hand-splitting each new offender. Train tally now: 21 notebooks, 22 line-gate, 23 regen, 24 stopped-on-request, 25 line-gate-again - 4 fails + 1 stop. My freshness.spi/cap.spi + slot-cap still gated on a green train. No new wait (TRAIN25 dead).")
inl note_of_beam_guard_all_invocations_55 () = Note ("BEAM-GUARD-ALL-INVOCATIONS", 55u32, "2026-11-05T04:13Z", "resilience", "AUDIT (resilience, live evidence + code boundary): the job-object memory cap is applied in kit_do_process / kit_process_streaming\\windows.rs / kit_job_supervise (supervise.spi). The gap is BEAM invocations that spawn OUTSIDE kit's capped job-launch path. Live proof from this tick's lineage frame: running erl<erl<sh and dotnet<erl<erl<sh processes root at a bare git-bash sh, NOT under kit_job_supervise - so those BEAM/Elixir runs are demonstrably uncapped (a direct mix/erl spawn from a shell or script bypasses the job object). The mix spiral.x_compat / postability tasks are shelled out (no .spi spawn site; not Spiral-native), which is why they can miss the Cells erl guard that only wraps Cells runs. Fix direction (kit-gated code): route every mix/erl/elixir invocation through kit job-launch so the job-object cap always applies, OR apply the cap in a shared mix/erl wrapper; then the 11-01 unguarded x_compat 51GB blowup cannot recur. Also still open: the checker bug (x_compat needing 51GB on one repo) is a separate x/compiler concern. Detection un-gated + done. resilience.")
inl note_of_beam_guard_all_invocations_56 () = Note ("BEAM-GUARD-ALL-INVOCATIONS", 56u32, "2026-11-05T20:20Z", "resilience-614162e5", "11-05 ~12:2x resilience DETECTION ORACLE (live empirical proof of the gauge choice; scratchpad harness beam-cap-oracle.ps1, NOT a repo script). The design rested on ONE 11-01 anecdote (51.8GB commit / 3.5GB WS); now proven live + generalized.\nFINDINGS (box, physRAM=16289MB, kill-line=commit>=8144MB=1/2 RAM, cells-line=3072MB distinct):\n- The single live erl on the box: commit=614MB but WS=78MB = 7.87x divergence - the HIGHEST-divergence process class on the machine (next: dwm 5.71x, SystemInformer 4.15x, WindowsTerminal 3.7x). So BEAM is STRUCTURALLY where a WS gauge under-reads most: BEAM reserves large committed arenas with a small resident set, so a WS cap under-reads BEAM commit by ~8x. The 51.8GB/3.5GB runaway was not a freak - it is the generic BEAM profile scaled up; a WS cap at 8144MB would not fire until a BEAM commit hit ~64GB.\n- NO FALSE-KILL on a healthy box: 0 procs over the 8144MB commit kill-line (top commits: rustdesk 1517, claude 1108/1007/988/974...). The DoD safety property (\"legit heavy run under the limit is NOT touched\") holds on current live data.\n- COUNTERFACTUAL at THIS box line: 11-01 runaway (commit=51800MB, ws=3500MB) -> COMMIT gauge @8144 CONFINES (crosses by 43656MB) [correct]; WS gauge @8144 BLIND (ws 3500 < 8144, runaway survives) [the bug]. The field choice IS the fix.\nFOR KIT STEP 1 (sampler commit field): PrivateMemorySize64 ~= Win32 PROCESS_MEMORY_COUNTERS_EX.PrivateUsage (commit charge) = what sysinfo Process::virtual_memory() returns on Windows - NOT VirtualSize (reserved address space). Confirm the plumbed field is PrivateUsage/commit, not VirtualSize: only commit predicts OOM; a VirtualSize threshold false-positives on runtimes reserving large address ranges uncommitted.\nORACLE VALUES for the kit test block (DoD: \"fabricate sample rows\"): BEAM-like row {commit=614,ws=78} UNDER 8144 -> Under (not touched); runaway row {commit=51800,ws=3500} -> Over -> Confined; same runaway on a WS field -> Under (proves the typed metric_kind WorkingSet|Commit must gate the threshold, per the typefeatureset plan). These are the exact numbers the kit test must reproduce. Un-gated detection validated; kill-enable + sampler field stay kit-tree.")
inl cells_document_typed () = Task ("CELLS-DOCUMENT-TYPED", "Cells document.spi as a typed model: the doc and cell records plus a cell-kind union instead of the newline/tab-encoded rows (894 lines); oracle document_test (CELLS-IN-SPIRAL M2, main)", Story, P1, D3, 100u32, Done, "CELLS-SPI-LIB", "team:x,cells,spiral,typefeatures,cells-in-spiral", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_cells_document_typed () = Planning ("CELLS-DOCUMENT-TYPED", Minutes 180u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_cells_document_typed_1 () = Note ("CELLS-DOCUMENT-TYPED", 1u32, "2026-11-01T06:30Z", "main", "main 02:2x: registered from x's CELLS-IN-SPIRAL n3 slice list; main owns it, and x keeps directives and the daemon protocol. Typefeatureset first (unions/GADTs), granular, every cell within the 280 cell budget.")
inl note_of_cells_document_typed_2 () = Note ("CELLS-DOCUMENT-TYPED", 2u32, "2026-11-01T12:37Z", "main", "main 08:3x TYPEFEATURESET PLAN: a document is a list of cells, and a cell a union over kinds (Markdown | Code of language * target list | Test of name * targets | Real) instead of newline/tab-encoded rows; heading levels a closed union, not ints; the .spir export a function from the typed document, not string surgery. Model: lib's xml.spi typed tree. DoD: document_test passes unchanged against the typed model, and the 4 canonical _real.spir files regenerate byte-identical. NEXT STEP: map today's document.spi row encodings (894 lines) to the union cases in a table on this item before coding. FIELDS: estimate 180, load light, owner main.")
inl note_of_cells_document_typed_3 () = Note ("CELLS-DOCUMENT-TYPED", 3u32, "2026-11-02T12:29Z", "x", "2026-11-02 08:29 x: HANDOFF agreed with main (main's reply to x's groom message; main is over its WIP cap after a 07:55 crash). x takes this item and starts it after CELLS-PORT-COMPILER-CLIENT (C7a). It is on CELLS-IN-SPIRAL's critical path, since CELLS-PORT-NOTEBOOK and X-SIM-CELLS-SPIRAL depend on it. The team tag moves to team:x when work starts. M1 (CELLS-XCOMPAT-SPIRAL) and M3 (CELLS-MANIFEST-SOURCEMAP-SPIRAL) stay with main.")
inl note_of_cells_document_typed_4 () = Note ("CELLS-DOCUMENT-TYPED", 4u32, "2026-11-02T17:13Z", "x", "2026-11-02 13:13 x: M2.1 LANDED (unstaged, hash-guarded): apps/cells/spi/document.spi FD105ADC68E0 over DF0D48010443. The hand-rolled list unions rows, cells, tokens and mark (NoRow/Row, NoCell/Cell, NoWord/Word, NoMark/Mark: about 300 sites) are now lib lists and option, through type aliases (:== rows = list string, cells = list (string * string), mark = option (string * string)), so signatures stay short: 894 -> 886 lines, 0 over 120. The rewrite used a balanced-paren splitter; no multi-scrutinee tuple-slot patterns existed, so the comma trap didn't apply. PROOF x-cells-m2a2 (node xcells1, A/A then A/B in one job, rule written before): base 35 passed (document_test + notebook_test, domain 0DD16E593251); draft applied 13:10:20, then 35 passed with domain.gleam regenerated at 13:10:35 to C2A59CBAB8FF, so the new code ran. x-cells-m2a1 was VOID: it looked for domain.gleam in the mix build dir, but it lives in-tree. NEXT M2.2: string cell kinds ('markdown', 'spiral', 'fsharp', 'import', 'code/<lang>') become a closed cell_kind union plus a cell record, encoded to text only at the Elixir export boundary.")
inl note_of_cells_document_typed_5 () = Note ("CELLS-DOCUMENT-TYPED", 5u32, "2026-11-02T17:30Z", "x", "2026-11-02 13:30 x: M2.2 LANDED (unstaged): document.spi 9BE444036A38 over FD105ADC68E0. Cell kinds are a closed union cell_kind = Markdown | SpiralCode | FsharpCode | ImportCell | TestCell | CodeIn : string | NamedTest : test_realm * string | OtherKind : string, and cells = list (cell_kind * string). Text exists only at the Elixir wire: emit_cells writes kind_text and read_cells reads kind_of; OtherKind keeps any unexpected wire kind byte-exact. The string-encoded 'test:'/'real_test:' kinds became NamedTest (realm, name), so realm_of, test_named_kind and kind_test_name are pattern matches, not prefix surgery. walk's '' sentinel became option cell_kind. test_realm moved above its first use. PROOF x-cells-m2b1 (A/A then A/B, same rule as M2.1): base 35 passed (domain C2A59CBAB8FF = the landed M2.1); draft applied 13:27:35, then 35 passed with domain regenerated 13:28:15 -> 8B604076647C. NEXT M2.3: the realm strings 'real'/'spi' and the tab-joined test_index rows become typed rows, the last encoded wire inside document.spi apart from the export boundary.")
inl note_of_cells_document_typed_6 () = Note ("CELLS-DOCUMENT-TYPED", 6u32, "2026-11-02T17:40Z", "x", "2026-11-02 13:40 x: M2.3 LANDED (unstaged): document.spi 853C1D36F09F over 9BE444036A38. test_index rows are a typed record test_row = { at; name; mode : test_mode; realm : test_realm; symbol; shown } with a closed test_mode = IgnoredTest | FailingTest | BuildOnlyTest | PreludeTest | StaticTest | PlainTest. Only row_text and the test_index export encode it (tab fields, newline-joined, byte-identical to the old join). PROOF x-cells-m2c1: base 35 passed (domain 8B604076647C = M2.2); draft applied 13:39:09, then 35 passed with domain regenerated 13:39:28 -> 42E02875456C. LEFT for M2: the .spir/.spi export as a function of the typed document (export_text/emit_spi still assemble text in passes), test_block_go's options as a typed header, and the in-file encoded walk state (spi_mode, open_step).")
inl note_of_cells_document_typed_7 () = Note ("CELLS-DOCUMENT-TYPED", 7u32, "2026-11-02T18:42Z", "x", "2026-11-02 14:42 x: M2.4 LANDED (unstaged): document.spi 20A37582A768 over 853C1D36F09F. A test header's flags are parsed once into test_flags = { switches : test_switches; no_agree; refers; fails : option string }, with test_switches = { symbol; static; print_code; ignore; build_only : bool }, and rendered once by header_text in the original flag order. The text helpers test_mode, agree_mode and reference_mode are gone. PROOF x-cells-m2d2 on pack16c (core B640F761890510F7): base 35 passed (the domain rebuilt by the new compiler is byte-identical, 42E02875456C), draft applied 14:38:01, then 35 passed with domain -> 2525B1586F25. x-cells-m2d1 had run the pre-rename text (field reference, 121 cols) on pack15d with the same generated hash.")
inl note_of_cells_document_typed_8 () = Note ("CELLS-DOCUMENT-TYPED", 8u32, "2026-11-02T19:23Z", "x", "2026-11-02 15:23 x: M2.5 LANDED (unstaged): document.spi 5ABB95547D09 over B500E8419A7F (the respelled M2.4). spi_mode's InTest carries a record open_test = { name; modes; targets; body; form : header_form; last : i32 } in place of a 6-tuple, so test_cell_of takes the record, and spi_walk's three body-growing arms share one 'grown' update ({ t ?| body = kept :: t::body }). test_header (NoHeader | Header) became option (string * string). PROOF x-cells-m2e1 on pack17: base 35 passed (domain 314803DBEC19 = the landed respell); draft applied 15:19:53, then 35 passed with domain -> D8E83F702909. LEFT: bounds (the Elixir tab text that body_last_of rescans per header) parsed once at from_spi_bounded into a typed list; the export functions take the typed document.")
inl note_of_cells_document_typed_9 () = Note ("CELLS-DOCUMENT-TYPED", 9u32, "2026-11-02T19:33Z", "x", "2026-11-02 15:33 x: M2.6 LANDED (unstaged): document.spi C1B445996763 over 5ABB95547D09. The Elixir bounds text is parsed once at spi_cells into list body_bound ({ header; last : i32 }), and body_last_of walks the typed list instead of rescanning tab text per test header (an empty text gives [], so 'no bounds' still means -1). PROOF x-cells-m2f1 on pack17: base 35 passed (domain D8E83F702909 = M2.5); draft applied 15:31:12, then 35 passed with domain -> 68D9E29442EF. Inside document.spi, text now exists only at the Elixir boundary: the wire in (load/read_cells, bounds_of), the wire out (emit_cells/kind_text, row_text, emit_spi), and the export functions in main.spi.")
inl note_of_cells_document_typed_10 () = Note ("CELLS-DOCUMENT-TYPED", 10u32, "2026-11-02T22:59Z", "x", "2026-11-02 18:59 x: CLOSED by rule: the title's DoD is met (typed records plus a cell-kind union instead of newline/tab-encoded rows; oracle document_test passed on every slice M2.1-M2.6). Text survives only at the Elixir wire (load/read_cells, bounds_of in; emit_cells, row_text, emit_spi out), and replacing it needs records across the Gleam export. The remainder is owned elsewhere, not dropped: (1) the typed boundary is LINKED-UNITS-GLEAM-RECORDS (Gleam exports produce records); (2) deleting document.ex is CELLS-PORT-NOTEBOOK (C9), which removes its last Elixir caller.")
