inl check_silent_drop () = Task ("CHECK-SILENT-DROP", "Compiler --check reports 'checked package' while silently dropping top-level blocks it couldn't parse: 19 false OKs on the v303 corpus in the browser session (10-30, fork compiler alpha418). A CI gate on --check can pass vacuously. Reproduce on the installed compiler, then make an unparsed block an error with its span", TaskKind, P0, D2, 100u32, Done, "PACK-13", "compiler,check,bug,team:compiler,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_check_silent_drop () = Planning ("CHECK-SILENT-DROP", Minutes 90u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_check_silent_drop_1 () = Note ("CHECK-SILENT-DROP", 1u32, "2026-11-01T12:03Z", "main", "main 08:0x from DIRECTION-1031 (browser session n12). It belongs with tonight's respell traps (EDITION3-SYMBOLS-MIGRATION n46-n49) and the zero-test-file lesson: a gate that checks nothing must fail. Also listed there: a 'GADT index bug at run time' without details; find it in the archive's conversation.md before filing it.")
inl note_of_check_silent_drop_2 () = Note ("CHECK-SILENT-DROP", 2u32, "2026-11-01T12:06Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 08:06 compiler: REPRODUCED on installed pack12b with a scratch package (scratchpad checkdrop/a, /b). (1) --check is NOT silent any more: an unparsable block in the entry file or in a sibling module gives 'path:line:col: <parse error>' plus 'Package typecheck rejected', exit 5. The host's runCheck (Program.fs 783-799) fails on any parse error, a fix since the browser session's alpha418 fork compiler (it came with SPIRAL-CHECK-PRINTS-ERRORS, 10-29). (2) The BUILD path still leaks: '--backend C' on case b (a broken block in other.spi, a sibling module in the entry's own package, while the entry defines main) prints only 'warning: ... (blocks that fail to parse are left out of their module)' and EXITS 0. Cause: spiral_compiler.fs 35079 'let entry_errors = if own_main then entry_errors else entry_errors @ package_errors' adds the package's other files only when the entry lacks main; dependency_warnings (35053-35077) covers other packages only. FIX DRAFTED (PACK-13 program 2, apps/compiler/packs/pack13/2-package-parse-errors-fatal.kitdo): the entry's own package always ends the build (its comment updated); dependencies stay warnings by design (a library's dead block shouldn't break its users). Candidate pack13a (= ord defaulting + this) building now (job-1793534792015); then case b must exit non-zero with the span, and a predict must show which matrix rows (if any) relied on the drop.")
inl note_of_check_silent_drop_3 () = Note ("CHECK-SILENT-DROP", 3u32, "2026-11-01T12:09Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 08:09 compiler: FIX PROVEN on the repro. Candidate pack13a (core 61D2D7AE0E946086, host DFC86B91968D447D; PACK-13 programs 1+2 on live core 796D343FB6E78D6E). Case b, a broken sibling module, now gives ParserErrors with its span and exit 5, and no output is written; on pack12b it was exit 0 plus a warning. Case a is unchanged (exit 5). With the sibling repaired, case b compiles (exit 0). PREDICT queued (job-1793534935234) vs pack12b's own run single-flight-20261101-030035: any compile-status change is a sample that relied on the silent drop, to be read row by row.")
inl note_of_check_silent_drop_4 () = Note ("CHECK-SILENT-DROP", 4u32, "2026-11-01T12:15Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 08:15 compiler: fix ready in PACK-13 (candidate pack13a, predict clean: 0 changes in 7,409 rows); regression sample verified (installed exit 0 vs pack13a exit 5). Done once PACK-13 is installed and the sample is in the matrix.")
inl note_of_check_silent_drop_5 () = Note ("CHECK-SILENT-DROP", 5u32, "2026-11-01T12:19Z", "main", "main 08:2x: FIXED by pack13a (installed 08:16, node-shipped). compiler's finding: on pack12b --check was already strict; the real hole was a BUILD whose entry defines main, which silently dropped broken blocks in sibling modules of the same package (warning, exit 0). Now the package's own parse errors are fatal and other packages' stay warnings. Regression row: samples/package_sibling_parse_error_negative (exit 5 with the expected text, checked on the installed compiler). Ready to close once compiler confirms no consumer build went red from the stricter rule.")
inl note_of_check_silent_drop_6 () = Note ("CHECK-SILENT-DROP", 6u32, "2026-11-01T12:24Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 08:24 compiler: PACK-13 INSTALLED 08:16 on the box (core 61D2D7AE0E946086). The node is still on pack12b: the ship aborted because the folder is held, partly by my two char-ordering sweeps. Main confirmed the regression sample in the matrix and that probe.ps1 exits 5 with the expected text. CONSUMER CHECK, done proactively: the new build rule can only fire on a package with a parse error in a non-entry file, and --check rejects ANY parse error in the package. So installed --check (pack13a) over one entry per package: x 4, tibia (all package dirs), dice, alphabet, blog = 12 packages, all exit 0, none can turn red. LEFT: kit (481 packages, many deliberate negative contracts), fiscal (82, mostly generated) and lib (consumed as a dependency, where the rule doesn't apply; its own test entries are in the lib package) are covered by their next gate runs on the installed pack13a. Close when those are green.")
inl note_of_check_silent_drop_7 () = Note ("CHECK-SILENT-DROP", 7u32, "2026-11-01T12:34Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 08:34 compiler, ROUND 4 QUALITY SPEC: FIELDS: P0, 90m, light, dep PACK-13 (installed 08:16). DEFINITION OF DONE: (a) done: samples/package_sibling_parse_error_negative expected-error row green in the matrix; (b) kit, fiscal and lib gates' next runs on the installed core 61D2D7AE0E946086 show no new red caused by 'has blocks that fail to tokenize or parse' (grep their gate logs for that text). NEXT STEP: read the next kit train and fiscal box-gate logs for that string. TYPEFEATURESET PLAN for the follow-up refactor: the fatal/warning choice should be a UNION 'BlockErrorOrigin = EntryFile | OwnPackage | Dependency' mapped to 'Severity = Fatal | Warning' by one total match, instead of the 'own_main' bool plus list concatenation at 35079, so the next rule change is a case edit, not a boolean rewire.")
inl note_of_check_silent_drop_8 () = Note ("CHECK-SILENT-DROP", 8u32, "2026-11-01T18:56Z", "compiler", "11-01 15:00 compiler: DONE. DoD (a) was already met (n-earlier: samples/package_sibling_parse_error_negative green). DoD (b), gates on cores that carry the PACK-13 rule ('has blocks that fail to tokenize or parse' is fatal; pack14a core DBC5EC18F14C354C keeps it): KIT: TRAIN7b (12:19-13:20, 56 notebooks, 655 result files) and TRAIN7c (13:27-, 619 files so far) under C:\\home\\git\\kit\\.cache\\release-train: 0 occurrences of the text. TRAIN7b's single red, src\\kit_do_file_steps\\steps.spi runtime-fail with failed_tests [], is a test runtime failure, not this rule. FISCAL: the 7 generated units (nfe_v4, cte, cte_os, cte_simp, gtve v4_00, cfdi_v4_0 and cfdi_pagos certificates) compiled under Cells on node mirror t1101 with core DBC5EC18F14C354C (jobs job-1793554794824 and job-1793555970589): 0 occurrences; every run failed only with the separate server-thread stack overflow (COMPILER-WIN-STACK-OVERFLOW) or passed. LIB: lib_land_window's close for the convert land (11:34, installed DBC5EC18) went green: document_test 22 passed, type_probe PASS, apps/spiral compiles. No consumer went red from the rule. Follow-up refactor (the BlockErrorOrigin union from the round-4 plan) is not part of this DoD; it goes with the next edit of that code.")
inl single_spi_extension () = Task ("SINGLE-SPI-EXTENSION", "One .spi extension instead of .spi + .spir, with today's semantics: a real/~> prefix before any top-level statement (mixed modules; merges lib's x + x_real pairs) and a ///- --real header for whole-real modules; .spir accepted with a warning for one edition; the manifest '*' becomes a legacy alias. Tested in the browser session: ~85-line parser patch (is_top_down per statement), core + lib with zero .spir gave byte-identical Rust on 6 programs and 40/40 random fixtures", Story, P1, D4, 0u32, Planned, "EDITION3-SYMBOLS-MIGRATION", "team:compiler,compiler,cells,edition3,syntax,typefeatureset", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_single_spi_extension () = Planning ("SINGLE-SPI-EXTENSION", Minutes 480u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_single_spi_extension_1 () = Note ("SINGLE-SPI-EXTENSION", 1u32, "2026-11-01T12:03Z", "main", "main 08:0x: the RFC (with 14-language precedents, 7 lint rules, a migration plan and 13 acceptance tests as its definition of done) is in the archive (artifact d364f5d6). The patch is rebuildable from the archive's spir-patch-edit1/2 scripts. New lint for standards.spi: a name may not exist in both segments of one module (cross-segment-redefinition fails the build). Tooling to touch: Cells (export real cells as ~> in the same file), the Zed tree-sitter regex, VS Code/Zed extension lists, the formatter corpus script, 8 kit files, 7 .github scripts, 1 in x. Caveat from the report: skipping stage separation holds only while the real segment is interpreted.")
inl note_of_single_spi_extension_2 () = Note ("SINGLE-SPI-EXTENSION", 2u32, "2026-11-01T12:34Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 08:34 compiler, ROUND 4 QUALITY SPEC: FIELDS: dep DIRECTION-1031 (an epic, not a blocker) -> EDITION3-SYMBOLS-MIGRATION (its anchor); load light -> medium (touches Cells, tree-sitter, kit and 7 .github scripts). DEFINITION OF DONE = the RFC's 13 acceptance tests (archive artifact d364f5d6), run as matrix samples + Cells tests, plus: lib's x/x_real pairs merged into single .spi files with lib_sweep equal before/after (passed/failed/type_errors per file), and '.spir' accepted with exactly one warning per file. NEXT STEP: restore spir-patch-edit1/2 from %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\archive\\20261101-080304 into a scratch copy, rebase it onto live core 61D2D7AE (the 10-30 patch predates PACK-12/13), and dry-apply it as a kit do program. TYPEFEATURESET PLAN: a top-level statement carries its segment as a UNION 'Segment = Typed | Real' set by the parser from the 'real' / '~>' prefix or the '///- --real' header, never by file extension or a string flag; the module holds 'statements : (Segment * Statement) list' and the two segment views are projections, so the new lint 'a name in both segments' is a fold over that list keyed by name; the file kind is a union 'SourceKind = Spi | LegacySpir' used only for the deprecation warning.")
inl note_of_single_spi_extension_3 () = Note ("SINGLE-SPI-EXTENSION", 3u32, "2026-11-01T12:34Z", "main", "main 08:3x TYPEFEATURESET PLAN for the compiler change: the parse mode becomes a union per top-level statement (Mode = TopDown | Real) resolved by a total function prefix -> header -> extension/manifest, not a mutable bool (today's is_top_down). Lint findings as a union with one case per rule (CrossSegmentRedefinition | ManifestModeMismatch | ...) carrying spans, so diagnostics are data. The cross-segment rule keys names by (module, name) and is checked once per module. NEXT STEP: rebuild the patch from the archive's spir-patch-edit1/2 onto pack13a's live core (796D... is now 2A74... after pack13a; re-anchor), as a .kitdo program for build-candidate, then the 13 acceptance tests as samples. FIELDS: estimate 480, load light, assignee agent, owner compiler.")
inl note_of_single_spi_extension_4 () = Note ("SINGLE-SPI-EXTENSION", 4u32, "2026-11-01T22:53Z", "main", "main lib-reshape 11-01 18:5x: the lib side of this item is planned in LIB-RESHAPE (n1 table, step R6): the four pairs to merge once the parser lands are cli_args_real.spir -> cli_args.spi, json_real.spir -> json.spi, routes_real.spir -> routes.spi, sm'_real.spir -> text.spi (after R4 renames sm' -> text; if this lands first, merge into sm'.spi and let R4 carry it). Each merge: the real segment's statements get the 'real'/'~>' prefix (or the file a '///- --real' header when the module is whole-real), the manifest's '*' marker goes, lib_sweep equal per file before/after (DoD here), document_test's interleave check (spir mirrors every heading) retires with the pairs. Nothing on the lib side can start before the parser change is installed; main does not patch spiral_compiler.fs.")
inl brzozowski_product () = Task ("BRZOZOWSKI-PRODUCT", "Take mega_brzozowski_derivatives to a public product: a certified AOT automata engine (minimality and equivalence certificates, Lean-checked translation validation) measured on rebar against rust/regex, published as a generated Rust crate. Roadmap M0-M9 with definitions of done, two gates, 20-week cap. Name undecided (the report proposed nerode; the user found person names odd)", Epic, P1, D7, 0u32, Planned, "MEGA-SAMPLES-TOGETHER", "team:compiler,mega-samples,regex,automata,benchmark,compiler,typefeatureset", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_brzozowski_product () = Planning ("BRZOZOWSKI-PRODUCT", Minutes 4800u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_brzozowski_product_1 () = Note ("BRZOZOWSKI-PRODUCT", 1u32, "2026-11-01T12:03Z", "main", "main 08:0x. M0 (1 week): &[u8] input instead of the Rc list, in-process timing, a minimal rebar KLV runner with the count model on curated/01-literal, until 'rebar measure --test' prints OK; cut if the plain DFA loop is >3x slower than regex-automata dense::DFA. GATE 1 at M4: geomean <=2.0x rust/regex continue; 2-4x only M5; >4x end the throughput front and pivot to 'certified minimal DFA, no_std, multi-target' (eBPF becomes the product). GATE 2 at M7: lake build green, the Lean checker accepts 100% of test DFAs and rejects 100% of injected mutations. M9: crate 0.1 under MIT/Apache separate from the compiler's MPL, results table including losses. Known gap: the staged DFA was 4x behind the regex crate (62 vs 15 ms). Full table in the archive (artifact cccab1fd, section B). Relates to MEGA-SAMPLES-TOGETHER.")
inl note_of_brzozowski_product_2 () = Note ("BRZOZOWSKI-PRODUCT", 2u32, "2026-11-01T12:34Z", "main", "main 08:3x TYPEFEATURESET PLAN (before any M0 code). Regex syntax as a GADT indexed by its nullability (Re 'nullable): Empty : Re 'no, Eps : Re 'yes, Chr : Re 'no, Seq/Alt/Star carry the index computed at the type level, so derivative and nullable are total and checked. DFA states as an existential (exists s. dfa_state s) so the emitted table hides the state type. The byte alphabet as a phantom-typed u8 class (byte_class 'set) so classes and raw bytes can't mix. Certificates (minimality, equivalence) as GADT witnesses checked at compile time (real segment), not booleans. Match semantics as a union (LeftmostLongest | LeftmostFirst), never a flag string. No -1 sentinels: dead state is a union case. Models: mega_brzozowski_derivatives (existing), fiscal's xsd_regex. NEXT STEP (M0): in apps/compiler/tmp/samples/mega_brzozowski_derivatives, replace the Rc list input with &[u8] via lib am' slices, add a rebar KLV runner reading the count model, run 'rebar measure --test' on curated/01-literal until OK. FIELDS: estimate 4800, load light (codegen) / heavy (rebar runs on the node), assignee agent, owner compiler+main.")
inl note_of_brzozowski_product_3 () = Note ("BRZOZOWSKI-PRODUCT", 3u32, "2026-11-01T12:35Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 08:35 compiler, ROUND 4 QUALITY SPEC: FIELDS: dep DIRECTION-1031 -> MEGA-SAMPLES-TOGETHER (theme 5 anchor); tag team:compiler. M0 DEFINITION OF DONE (main's text made checkable): 'rebar measure --test' prints OK for the Spiral engine on curated/01-literal through a KLV runner, and its count matches regex-automata's; plus the cut rule recorded as a number: Spiral's dense loop time / regex-automata dense::DFA time on that benchmark (cut if > 3.0). NEXT STEP: in samples/mega_brzozowski_derivatives/runtime_native, replace the Rc list input with a byte slice (an array_base u8 in Spiral, &[u8] in the Rust output) and add in-process timing; then a 50-line KLV runner (separate Rust bin, not in the compiler). TYPEFEATURESET PLAN: the automaton states are already GADT-indexed in the sample (indexed_dfa_state_cardinality): keep the state count as a type-level index so transitions out of range don't type check; the input is a phantom-tagged slice ':! bytes = array_base u8' (never a string with -1 for EOF; end-of-input is a union case 'Byte of u8 | End'); certificates (minimality, equivalence) are values of their own types built only by the checker, so an uncertified DFA can't reach the emitter.")
inl note_of_brzozowski_product_4 () = Note ("BRZOZOWSKI-PRODUCT", 4u32, "2026-11-01T14:39Z", "compiler", "11-01 10:38 compiler, R5: M0 START APPROVED in the spiral repo (main relays the user: no new repo until the name is settled). Location: tmp/samples/mega_brzozowski_derivatives (moves with the samples to top-level samples/ under COMPILER-TMP-RETIRE, id kept). The rebar KLV runner is a Rust bin inside that sample folder, not a new crate repo. Status -> starts after CHAR-ORDERING lands (lane queue position 5 moves up to 3: it is CPU-light codegen work, rebar runs go to the node).")
inl note_of_brzozowski_product_5 () = Note ("BRZOZOWSKI-PRODUCT", 5u32, "2026-11-01T20:23Z", "regex", "regex 16:3x DEFINITION OF DONE for \"i574n/regex is publishable\" (user 11-01 15:55: rewrite from scratch in git/regex, typefeatureset over strings, 1k-line output, Spiral test cells, benches in GH Actions on the dir-tree-html page; delete spiral's brzozowski code after). Sources read in full: share extract (conversation.md turns 17-20 + snapshot tool output; artifacts cccab1fd, 4edd442c, d05f9cdd, 318a5cad, 76f3f8a7, d364f5d6; 00ccefe9/d1983bed unrelated), lane tips 20261101 (7 lanes), legacy mega_brzozowski_derivatives (README + 67 subdirs), lib xsd_regex/xsd_emit/xpath_eval, FISCAL-XSD-PATTERNS-LARGE, SEFAZ-XSD-PATTERNS, MEGA-SAMPLES-TOGETHER. Draft copy: %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\regex\\BRZOZOWSKI-PRODUCT\\dod-20261101.md.\n\nPUBLISHABLE = one pushed commit of i574n/regex where D1-D12 all hold, each proven by the named evidence (CI run on that commit or a typed test), never by a transient job id.\n\nD1 TYPED DOMAIN. Regex AST and char sets are unions (code ranges are {low; high} records of u32 in a normalized set; no string-encoded ranges, no flag strings). Counted repetition is a constructor with a typed upper bound (Bounded n | Unbounded), never unrolled. Nullability is GADT evidence (nullable_evidence accepts_empty|rejects_empty). DFA states are a nominal over i32, the dead state is a union case, every outcome is a union (parsed|refused with a typed reason, built|state cap reached, equivalent|distinguished with a word, within|over bound). No -1 sentinels, no bool outcomes in public APIs, no strings except at the parse/print/HTML boundary. Evidence: kit standards --census <pkg> per package (gadt-case, existential, forall counted), grep '-1i32' = 0 in source.\nD2 ENGINES. (a) Brzozowski derivative matcher over code points with Owens-Reppy-Turon similarity normal form (ACI alternation, assoc sequence, units, star idempotence); (b) interned + memoized lazy DFA (hash-consed nodes, derivative cached per (node, class)); (c) Antimirov partial derivatives -> NFA, simulated by state sets; (d) eager DFA over derivative classes (interval minterms of the pattern, not 1.1M code points) with a state cap returning a typed refusal; (e) minimal DFA = Nerode quotient by partition refinement (Hopcroft; Moore as the cross-check); (f) intersection & and complement ~ as AST constructors (derivatives make them nearly free; RE# precedent). Each engine is a separate package.\nD3 CERTIFICATES (values only a checker builds, checked by an engine other than the one that produced them). (a) Minimality: one distinguishing word per pair of minimal states, replayed on the derivative matcher; (b) equivalence: Hopcroft-Karp union-find on two DFAs -> Equivalent (the bisimulation pairs) or Distinguished (a word both matchers disagree on); inclusion L1 <= L2 via the product with complement; (c) Antimirov support bound |PD(r)| <= alphabetic width + 1 per pattern; (d) remainder theorem a.w in L(r) <=> w in L(d_a r) over the seeded corpus; (e) nullability evidence text. Forged certificates (wrong word, wrong pair, short row) are rejected by the checker (test per forgery, the legacy negative_* names are the blueprint: raw_quotient_not_semantic_witness, raw_representatives_not_minimality_witness, forge_constructive_minimality_witness, indexed_row_length, missing_compare...). Compile-time negatives use diagnostic-expect test blocks (TEST-EXPECT-DIAGNOSTIC) and must fail FOR THE RIGHT REASON (expected error text asserted). Wording: \"certified per DFA\", never \"proved\", until a Lean checker exists.\nD4 AGREEMENT. All engines (a)-(e) plus the Rust regex crate (non-XSD subset) return identical accept/count results on a seeded corpus: fixed seeds through lib keyed_rng; curated patterns (literals, alternation, (0|00)*1, (0|1)*1(0|1){3} = 16 states, bounded repeats, classes, & and ~) plus generated ASTs of bounded depth; inputs = random strings + positives GENERATED FROM the automaton + boundary-length mutations (the xsd_emit Canton fix). Evidence: a test block per corpus printing 0 disagreements; a wrong expected count makes it red (positive control once).\nD5 XSD FRONT END (fiscal's 336-pattern budget). Counters first-class ({n}/{n,m} in 276/336, max {128}; R{1,1000} builds in <= 1001 states within the test budget, no O(m^3) state scan: states looked up by an interned key, not a linear compare); classes, negated classes, alternation, groups, \\d\\s\\w and negations, unescaped dot (excludes \\n \\r), non-ASCII ranges ([!-y]), \\p{Is<Block>} for the full XSD block list and \\P negation (unknown block = typed refusal), class subtraction [a-z-[aeiou]], whole-string implicit anchoring, ^ and $ literal, '-[' after | literal; \\i \\c refused with a typed reason (0 uses in the corpus; DEFER support). One test block per XSD-ism. ORACLE: a typed Spiral test over the pinned fiscal pattern corpus + .NET probe rows downloaded in CI (never committed; fiscal/scripts/pattern-oracle.ps1:9-17 is the model) with 0 disagreements, and parity with lib xsd_regex on every corpus probe (the gate for deletion phase B). The 7 NF-e QR patterns (<= 494 states) build within the cap.\nD6 SPIRAL TEST CELLS. Every module is a notebook: first line '///- --package .', a static prelude (':# <name> static =' with opens) under its '///>' targets, tests ':# <name> =' below their definitions, one-line bodies with inline '_assert_eq' expectations, fixtures as top-level unit functions. Targets pinned on every test (rust plus gleam; never the Zig default). CI reads the 'spiral-tests total' line (missing total = RED; unsupported counted separately; memo off). Tests write only under get_temp_path with one name per test.\nD7 SIZE GATES. Every generated file <= 1000 lines (kit crate gate <root> --enforce --limit 1000 green, and the build prints the largest generated file with >= 15% headroom); mix spiral.cell_budget --check prints 0 findings (272 weight per definition); build units hand-modeled (one crate per package, typed exports 'export \"name\" ((\\ x => f x) : a -> u)' with '///- --import-types <crate>:<unions>' as the importer's first lines; no untyped $'crate::fn' i32 codes); shared models are real crates, never inlined into every importer; the regex runtime exists once.\nD8 BENCHMARKS IN GH ACTIONS. One bench binary (Spiral -> Rust) run in the same job as the build on each OS. Rows are DETERMINISTIC (states, minimal states, matches, input bytes; compared exactly against typed expectations in the .spi) or TIME (process CPU time, arms rotated every round, medians + IQR of per-round ratios). Every time table has an in-job A/A arm (the same engine twice); a ratio is shown only when A/A is within 0.95-1.05, else the table says VOID. The page shows RATIOS against the Rust regex crate arm (absolute ms only as secondary text with the machine stamp). Each row carries commit, toolchain pins, machine + CPU model, seed, input bytes. Every bench claim is pre-registered (question, predicted number, pass rule) in its item note before the run; a missed prediction is reported as missed. Workloads: literal, alternation, (0|00)*1 vs 0^n (backtracking ReDoS baseline), fourth-from-end, R{1,1000}, the XSD corpus; same input bytes for every engine. Rows are rendered to HTML by Spiral and listed on the dir-tree-html page (publish-tree -Include); the HTML is also a 90-day CI artifact. No committed .tsv/.txt/.json results.\nD9 CI. Exactly one .github/workflows/gh-pages.yml plus actions.lock (gh actions-lock), matrix ubuntu-24.04 + windows-2025, every run step 'pwsh scripts/workflow.ps1 [-Flag]', compiler provisioned by spiral lib.ps1 Ensure-SpiralCompiler in every build entry, formatter check clean, green on both OSes at the publishable commit, page pushed from ubuntu only. Windows deep-recursion crashes print module/code/offset (copy fiscal/scripts/build.ps1's event-log reader).\nD10 SCRIPTS. Only thin glue: scripts/init.ps1 (link deps/spiral), build.ps1 (one BuildSpiral per crate + cargo), scripts/workflow.ps1, scripts/publish.ps1; no logic, portable paths (multi-segment Join-Path, no 'a\\b' literals), no .py/.tsv/.txt anywhere; every tool (bench, HTML rows) is Spiral.\nD11 PUBLISH. README opens with the guarantee and an 'Honest limitations' section (losses included, RE#/resharp and regex-automata cited), \"certified\" wording, no old numbers (the 40/62 vs 15 ms Rc-list table never reappears), single maintainer + AI disclosed. USER DECISIONS before 0.1: crate name (crates.io 'regex' is taken; nerode/koina/regex-aot were all rejected), license of the generated crate (MIT/Apache vs the fork's AGPL; a generated-code exception), the i574n dialect/edition id for a public package.\nD12 SPIRAL DELETION PLAN (nothing deleted by agents; the user says go). PHASE A, unlocked by D1-D10 green in CI on a pushed commit + the user's go: delete spiral/apps/compiler/tmp/samples/mega_brzozowski_derivatives/ entirely (README.md, main.spi, main.fsx, package.spiproj, bench.ps1, regex_crate_bench.rs, runtime_interned.spi, runtime_workload.spi and all 67 subfolders: 27 positive, 31 negative_*, 8 compiler_probe_*, proof_suite), its rows in apps/compiler/tmp/tests/matrix.psd1 (Known/Timeouts/backend lists naming mega_brzozowski_derivatives), and its oracle rows (-Accept after). Before deleting: dangling check by stem in every sibling repo; the compiler lane picks which compiler_probe_* and negative_* stay as minimal regression samples (one per fixed compiler bug) and MEGA-SAMPLES-TOGETHER swaps brzozowski for the regex repo's CI as its canary. PHASE B, unlocked by D5 parity + a consumption path: lib/spiral/xsd_regex.spi duplicates the engine but has live consumers (xsd_emit.spi 26 refs, xpath_eval.spi 14 refs, fiscal generated units' table_matches, fiscal/src corpus.spi). lib cannot depend on regex (regex depends on lib), so the user picks (i) fiscal and xpath consume regex as a package and xsd_emit leaves lib, or (ii) regex's engine lands in lib as the canonical copy and the repo keeps the product, benches and certificates. Until then xsd_regex stays.\n\nIDEA DISPOSITION (every idea found; K = in v0.1 DoD above, D = deferred to a filed item, X = dropped with reason).\nK: derivatives (Brzozowski 1964), Antimirov 1996, Owens-Reppy-Turon similarity, Myhill-Nerode quotient as the product identity, minimality witness, transition soundness, Hopcroft-Karp equivalence, inclusion, product construction, intersection/complement, nullability evidence, remainder theorem, Antimirov bound, interned lazy DFA (also the fallback when the cap hits), typed state cap ((a|b)*a(a|b){n} must refuse, not explode), counters, byte/char class partition, XSD front end as first consumer, oracle tests, positives from automata, cross-engine agreement, negatives for the right reason (31 legacy names as blueprint), GADT typestate raw -> decided for matchers, length-indexed rows only where static (runtime GADT index bug), staged twins only where the regex is static, honest loss table, publish compile time next to run time, 'never publish old numbers', compile-time budget tracked, generated code usable without the compiler, doc snippets compiled (not --check).\nD -> REGEX-REBAR (rebar KLV runner: count, count-spans, grep, compile models; &[u8] input; GATE 1 at M4: geomean <= 2.0x rust/regex continue, 2-4x prefilters only, > 4x close the throughput front; M0 cut: dense loop > 3x regex-automata dense::DFA; leftmost-first vs leftmost-longest declared per engine like RE#; forward + reverse DFA for spans; curated groups 01/02/06 first, 03/08 need Unicode \\b + case-insensitive; upstream rebar PR only after adoption: >= 1 external dependent 60 days after release).\nD -> REGEX-AOT-TARGETS (staged AOT DFA from a static regex: build.rs/proc-macro, no_std, no alloc, zero init, C header; throughput >= sparse serialized and <= 1.1x dense regex-automata, binary <= dense, cut > 1.25x -> 'certificate + multi-target'; prefilters memchr/rare byte/Teddy + prefilter-necessity certificate L <= S*.Lits.S* (freeze if < 30% over M4); eBPF/XDP verifier-accepted by construction; WGSL; Delphi 64-bit DFA disagreement DELPHI-64BIT-DFA-DISAGREE must be explained before claiming Delphi; Unicode classes compiled to UTF-8 byte automata with states <= 1.5x regex-automata, 10^7 fuzz inputs 0 divergences; 24 h cargo-fuzz differential vs regex, any unexplained divergence blocks publication).\nD -> REGEX-LEAN-VALIDATION (Lean translation validation: lake build green, accepts 100% test DFAs, rejects 100% injected mutations, derivative/nullability theorems out of the TCB; cut after 3 weeks to checker + certificate; certificate file format + independent checker first; up-to-congruence (Bonchi-Pous) NFA equivalence; Mathlib-style certificate names minimal_of_nerode_classes).\nD -> candidates in DIRECTION-1031 (not filed, gated after REGEX-REBAR/LEAN): certified constrained-decoding masks (llguidance numbers), cl100k/o200k pre-tokenizer, regex -> XDP, a typed_llama consumer.\nX: Bend/GPU/tibia/Craftax/typed_llama sections of cccab1fd and 4edd442c (other projects); Sigenius/ERP/Golden Visa (user dropped); showcase S2/S3/S5/S6 line-count marketing (not a regex deliverable; S1/S4 survive as the typed DFA rows and typestate); captures and lookaround (admitted non-goals, RE# owns lookaround); Unicode breadth as a selling point (non-win); regex-syntax HIR as the parser (Spiral-first tooling, XSD dialect, non-Rust targets: the parser is Spiral); the names nerode/kozen/koina/regex-aot (user rejected person names, Koina retracted, aot slop); the legacy Rc-list input and its timings; F#/Kotlin/Swift as v0.1 targets (unstable codegen noted in the share); per-sample README prose (facts go into names and tests).\nOPEN (user): crate name, license, dialect id, deletion phase B route. Rust-crate baseline in CI needs crates.io access (fine on GH runners).")
inl note_of_brzozowski_product_6 () = Note ("BRZOZOWSKI-PRODUCT", 6u32, "2026-11-01T22:37Z", "regex", "regex 18:4x STATUS for main: C:\\home\\git\\regex has the from-scratch rewrite UNSTAGED (nothing committed/pushed): src/ (10 notebooks: charset, syntax, nullable, derivative, dfa, minimal, certificate, partial, agreement, xsd), bench/ (Spiral bench crate: host boundary + bench.spi -> bench.rs, regex-bench bin), build.ps1, scripts/{init,workflow,publish}.ps1, .github/workflows/gh-pages.yml + actions.lock, Cargo workspace, README with tables, .gitignore/.gitattributes/.editorconfig/rust-toolchain.toml from dir-tree-html. First full node gate (run2): 34/41 tests green on rust+gleam incl. minimal 16 states, 120-pair certificate, Antimirov bound, 240-word 4-engine agreement; the 7 reds were 4 wrong expectations of mine (fixed) and 3 gleam timeouts under node load (budget raised to 900 s). run3 (job-1793571992048-regex-run3) re-gates all + the new xsd notebook + the bench. Known gaps against the n5 DoD: bench.rs 4174 lines (crate split next, REGEX-BENCH-PAGE n2), ~35 definitions over the cell budget (AST reshape planned, REGEX-SYNTAX n3), quadratic certify / cubic Moore (Hopcroft next, REGEX-NERODE n2), no regex-crate arm, no fiscal corpus oracle, CI never run (user push needed). Box compile windows were held all session: every proof ran on the node (vultr dropped by main's one-job rule).")
inl note_of_brzozowski_product_7 () = Note ("BRZOZOWSKI-PRODUCT", 7u32, "2026-11-02T00:18Z", "regex", "regex 20:3x END-OF-SESSION STATUS vs the n5 DoD. Committed by the user as 2f4b88f + pushed (CI run 41359359048): 10 src notebooks, bench, glue, workflow. CI: ubuntu-24.04 AND windows-2025 spiral-tests total 50/50 passed (memo off, rust+gleam); bench built and exited 0 on both; red only at the 1000-line gate. UNSTAGED on top (6 files: .gitignore, README.md, bench/Cargo.toml, bench/bench.spi, bench/host.spi, build.ps1): the fixed timer (one OnceLock), 25 passes/arm, 15 rounds, the Rust regex crate arm (oracle + baseline B), a long-input workload, A/A shown on VOID rows, the 1000-line gate as a LOUD ::warning (bench.rs ~4460 lines, gitignored, not committed) so the next push publishes the page. D1 typed domain: met for the core (GADT char_set loose|normal, GADT nullability evidence, state nominal + Dead case, outcome unions; one internal -1-free design) but the cell budget is NOT met (~35 definitions over 272). D2: Brzozowski, Antimirov, eager DFA, minimal DFA (Moore), & and ~ done; interned lazy engine and Hopcroft not. D3: minimality certificate + forged-word rejection + product equivalence + Antimirov bound + remainder theorem done (certify O(n^2) BFS, capped at 128 states in the bench). D4: 4 engines + regex crate agree (240 seeded words, 44 shortest-word mutations, every bench round); generated ASTs and the positive control not yet. D5: XSD parser with typed refusals 7/7; corpus oracle, full block list, xsd_regex parity not yet. D6: met (notebooks, pinned targets, total line read). D7: NOT met (bench.rs 4.4k lines; cell budget). D8: in-job A/A, rotation, ratios, typed rows -> HTML met; wall time not CPU time; codespace table clean (A/A 0.997-1.007). D9: one workflow, both OSes, compiler provisioned: met except green. D10: met (4 glue scripts, no .py/.tsv/.txt). D11/D12: user decisions pending, nothing deleted. NEXT, in order: (1) user pushes the 6 unstaged files and enables Pages on gh-pages, then watch CI publish bench/report.html; (2) REGEX-BENCH-PAGE crate split of bench.spi into hand-modeled crates under 1000 lines (typed single-argument exports, :~ helpers), then flip the warning back to a throw; (3) array-backed DFA table (the list DFA is 15x behind the regex crate on long input); (4) REGEX-NERODE Hopcroft with split witnesses (certify + minimize at 1001 states); (5) REGEX-SYNTAX AST reshape for the cell budget; (6) REGEX-XSD corpus oracle with fiscal.")
inl note_of_brzozowski_product_8 () = Note ("BRZOZOWSKI-PRODUCT", 8u32, "2026-11-02T11:28Z", "regex", "regex 07:5x SOURCE: share re-read by subagent a7601c1fd3d1a599f, verbatim at %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\regex\\REGEX-SOTA\\sota-reread-20261102.md (C = artifact cccab1fd, E = 4edd442c, N = d05f9cdd, S = 76f3f8a7). ADDITION 19 (verbatim): 19. BRZOZOWSKI-PRODUCT: timeboxes and exit rules. Timeboxes: M0 1 week, M1 2, M2 2, M3 3, M4 1, M5 2, M6 2, M7 3, M8 1 plus ongoing, M9 1; hard cap 20 weeks. All timeboxes double if Perceus/FBIP, the Hopac core and the CIC kernel are not ready. Exits: Success = GATE 1 <= 2.0x, M7 green, M9 published. Partial = GATE 1 fails but M6 and M7 pass, so eBPF becomes the product. Failure = M0 or M1 blow their cut, which means a post-mortem (\"what staging bought and what it didn't\") and a return to compiler work. Source: C §B.2 \"Total\", §B.3, \"Riscos\" last bullet. CONTRADICTIONS a-j (verbatim): a. Ordering. The roadmap puts byte/UTF-8 classes (M1), unanchored spans (M3) and prefilters (M5) before or at GATE 1. The backlog parks UTF-8 and prefilters in REGEX-AOT-TARGETS, which depends on REGEX-REBAR's GATE 1. As filed, GATE 1 can't be measured fairly and its 2-4x branch has nothing to run.\nb. When to PR upstream rebar. E §7 says PR at M4 (rebar's CONTRIBUTING accepts engines with \"some particularly interesting property\"). C says WANTED.md requires real users, so only after one external dependent within 60 days. The backlog follows C.\nc. The parser. C M2 uses regex-syntax HIR. BRZOZOWSKI-PRODUCT dropped that but filed no Spiral Rust-dialect parser (item 7).\nd. License. C says the compiler is MPL-2.0; E says the monorepo is AGPL; N says the compiler is AGPLv3 with upstream files under MPL-2.0. BRZOZOWSKI n1 says \"the compiler's MPL\", while n5/REGEX-PUBLISH say AGPL. Check LICENSE before the user decides.\ne. Score tables aren't comparable. RE# claims \"71% faster\" (2.54/1.48), but its fork shows 1.44 vs 1.61, and upstream has rust/regex at 3.08. Different benchmark sets and normalizations, so only our own intersection run counts.\nf. Rc. C §E says to ignore the Rc penalty and assume Perceus, but C \"Riscos\" doubles the timeboxes if Perceus isn't ready, and E says Rc kills heap benchmarks. Resolution: the hot loop must not depend on Perceus (item 10).\ng. GADT runtime-index bug. E says fix it in M0; REGEX-NERODE works around it (\"length-indexed rows only where static\").\nh. Naming. nerode (C), then koina-automata (N), both rejected. The repo is \"regex\", but that name is taken on crates.io. Still an open user decision.\ni. The old baseline. E gives \"62 ms vs 15 ms (regex 1.13)\", C gives \"40/62 vs 15 ms\". Both are banned anyway.\nj. Timing. BENCH-PAGE n1 (D8) requires process CPU time; runs so far use wall time. Already noted in the backlog but still open. RESOLVED today: a -> REGEX-BYTES-UTF8, REGEX-SPANS, REGEX-PREFILTER filed as their own items before GATE 1; c -> REGEX-RUST-DIALECT; d -> spiral is AGPL v3 (upstream files may carry MPL-2.0), regex has no LICENSE, user decides; f -> hot loop must not depend on Perceus (REGEX-DFA-TABLE); j -> still open (wall time).")
inl note_of_brzozowski_product_9 () = Note ("BRZOZOWSKI-PRODUCT", 9u32, "2026-11-02T14:00Z", "x", "2026-11-02 09:59 x: DECISION RECORD (typed; groom of user waits, user 11-02: 'decisions waiting on me get met autonomously/deterministically with tests/benchs/typefeatureset modeling/backups'). kind=ByDefault. QUESTION: What is the public product/crate name (the user found person names like 'nerode' odd)? RULE (written before acting): Working name = the user's own repo choice i574n/regex (n5, user 11-01 15:55). The generated crate's package name is a non-person placeholder (e.g. i574n-regex) until publication, because crates.io 'regex' is taken. Renaming an unpublished crate is free. The crates.io publish itself is outward-facing and goes to the user at D12. BACKUP/UNDO: No state to back up. The name lives in one manifest field. OWNER: compiler. NEXT: Start D1 (typed domain unions) in C:\\home\\git\\regex under the working name. VETO: the user can reverse this at any time; the backup above is the undo.")
inl note_of_brzozowski_product_10 () = Note ("BRZOZOWSKI-PRODUCT", 10u32, "2026-11-03T02:17Z", "main", "main 11-02 22:2x MAP of tonight's regex work onto the n5 DoD (all unstaged; node proofs on mirror sem, regex d7aa16be + tree, memo off):\n- D3 certificates: certificate.check_witnesses now replays distinguishing words on the derivative-free language oracle (language.spi), the \"engine other than the one that produced them\" rule. New check_dfa returns a typed dfa_check (SoundDfa | Violates OpenTarget/NotTotal/WrongTransition/AcceptingMismatch/DuplicateKey), with forged-row tests for a flipped accepting flag and an out-of-range target. Also check_partition (Partitions | Gap | InvertedClass | ShortOfTheTop: classes cover 0..0x10FFFF) and check_normal (NormalSet | Unsorted | Touching | InvertedRange), each with a forged negative. These last three are in the gate running now; REGEX-CLOSURE-CERTIFICATES.\n- D3/D4 agreement and \"generated ASTs of bounded depth\": laws.spi enumerates every regex of depth <= 2 over {0,1} with & and ~ (10,980) x all words of length <= 2: 241,560 checks of constructors-preserve-language, normalization idempotence, nullable, the remainder theorem, and Brzozowski = oracle. 2/2 green (REGEX-SEMANTICS-ORACLE Done).\n- D1 typestate: automaton phase GADT (built -> minimal -> certified); table_of takes only certified automata; a negative 'fails Unification failure' test (REGEX-AUTOMATON-TYPESTATE 80%).\n- D8 'backtracking ReDoS baseline': bench BacktrackingArm (continuation-passing, greedy star) on every workload incl. ZeroRuns = (0|00)*1; regex-bench exit 0, so counts agree across all 8 arms.\n- CI: regex is RED at d7aa16be only from pack17's GADT erasure of closed-index families (nullable.spi 3 tests); compiler's fix ships in pack20.")
inl note_of_brzozowski_product_11 () = Note ("BRZOZOWSKI-PRODUCT", 11u32, "2026-11-03T15:52Z", "main", "main 11-03 12:0x POINTERS for the owner (no status change): (1) NAME DECIDED on 11-03 (main, deciding by rules after the user's 'couldn't regex-proof be better than regex-typed?'): proven-regex, family proven-<crate> for the Spiral rewrites that cannibalize crates (decision note on the crate-naming item). (2) Engine evidence in i574n/regex (unstaged, landing with the user's push): closure certificates (check_quotient/check_closure, REGEX-CLOSURE-CERTIFICATES Done); a lazy DFA plus an array-store lazy engine that beats the list engine 1.7-4.7x and the minimal DFA on short words (REGEX-ENGINES-LAZY-STAGED n8-n10), now interning through a BST over order_re; bench page rendered through lib markup with a new 256-state workload. (3) The rebar comparison remains the M-gate: bench/bench.spi's Rust regex crate arm is the in-repo baseline B today.")
inl spelling_profiles () = Task ("SPELLING-PROFILES", "Generalize the Words<->Symbols respell into spelling profiles as data (spelling/<name>.toml mapping canonical keywords to spellings): bijective, no ambiguity with operators, byte-for-byte round-trip over the whole corpus checked in CI; the repo stores one canonical profile per edition and the editor renders the reader's. The anti-fork mechanism: a new syntax preference is a PR with a toml and green tests, not a fork", Story, P1, D4, 0u32, Planned, "EDITION3-SYMBOLS-MIGRATION", "compiler,formatter,syntax,edition3,team:compiler,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_spelling_profiles () = Planning ("SPELLING-PROFILES", Minutes 600u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_spelling_profiles_1 () = Note ("SPELLING-PROFILES", 1u32, "2026-11-01T12:04Z", "main", "main 08:0x from the steal-list report (artifact 76f3f8a7, section 2.1). Cost noted there: each profile multiplies parser test surface; at most 2 Tier-1 profiles. Relates to MULTILINGUAL-PRESENTATION and EDITION3-SYMBOLS-MIGRATION; tonight's respell traps (n46-n49) are the case for tools reading Spiral through the compiler's tokens.")
inl note_of_spelling_profiles_2 () = Note ("SPELLING-PROFILES", 2u32, "2026-11-01T12:34Z", "main", "main 08:3x TYPEFEATURESET PLAN: a profile is a total map from a closed union of canonical keywords (Keyword = Inl | Let | Match | Then | Else | Nominal | Union | Prototype | Instance | Forall | Exists | Real | True | False) to spellings, so a missing keyword is a compile error, not a runtime lookup miss. Validity (bijective, no prefix clash with operators, round-trip) as checks returning a union of violations. Words and Symbols become two values of the same profile type. NEXT STEP: extract the formatter's current words<->symbols table (spiral_compiler.fs respell, 'format --spelling') into data and prove the round-trip over the corpus (lib + samples) on the node. FIELDS: estimate 600, load light, owner compiler.")
inl note_of_spelling_profiles_3 () = Note ("SPELLING-PROFILES", 3u32, "2026-11-01T12:35Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 08:35 compiler, ROUND 4 QUALITY SPEC: FIELDS: dep DIRECTION-1031 -> EDITION3-SYMBOLS-MIGRATION (anchor). main's 08:3x TYPEFEATURESET PLAN (closed Keyword union, total map) is the right shape; ADD from the compiler side: today's spellings live in FormatterConvert as two string matches (headerSpelling 'test'/':#' at ~30050 and the keyword table ~29988-30001), so step 1 is to replace both with ONE total function 'spelling : Profile -> Keyword -> string' over that union and derive the tokenizer's reverse map from it (bijectivity checked at startup by building the inverse and failing on a collision). DEFINITION OF DONE: format --spelling symbols then --spelling words over lib, x, tibia, kit and fiscal is byte-identical to the start (a CI job), and kit's '///- --no-respell' files are untouched. NEXT STEP: list every keyword the two current tables cover vs the Keyword union main wrote, and add the missing ones (test, open, real/~>, join/~~).")
inl note_of_spelling_profiles_4 () = Note ("SPELLING-PROFILES", 4u32, "2026-11-01T23:52Z", "main", "main 20:0x GROOM, DoD added: (1) a total 'spelling : Profile -> Keyword -> string' replaces FormatterConvert's two string matches (~30050 and ~29988-30001); (2) Words and Symbols are two values of one Profile type, and edition 3's canonical profile is Symbols (the user prefers '::' and symbols, 11-01); (3) the validity checks return a violations union (non-bijective, operator prefix clash, round-trip mismatch), each covered by a test; (4) a CI job round-trips the whole corpus (lib + every repo's .spi) through both profiles byte for byte; (5) at most 2 Tier-1 profiles. NEXT (compiler): step 1, the total function plus tests.")
inl naming_lexicon_ratchet () = Task ("NAMING-LEXICON-RATCHET", "Write the canonical function-name lexicon into standards.spi and ratchet it in every repo: count (not length/len/size), of/from_<type> constructors, at (total) / find (option) / try_ (result), text module, to_/as_/into_ conversions, is_/has_/can_ predicates, read/write vs load/save vs fetch, directory not dir, context not ctx; rules no_prime, no_package_stutter, no_backend_prefix_outside_host, banned_tokens", Story, P1, D3, 70u32, Active, "-", "kit,standards,naming,lib,main,team:kit", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_naming_lexicon_ratchet () = Planning ("NAMING-LEXICON-RATCHET", Minutes 240u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_naming_lexicon_ratchet_1 () = Note ("NAMING-LEXICON-RATCHET", 1u32, "2026-11-01T12:04Z", "main", "main 08:0x from the naming report (artifact d05f9cdd, section 5); the lexicon was chosen from the repos' own counts (kit count 91 vs length 11, of 286, at 68). The browser session scored standardization 575/1000: new code is disciplined (tibia/kit/x 0% primes and camelCase), the foundations are not (lib 4.4% primes, 5.3% backend prefixes, kit repeats its package prefix in 20.6% of definitions). Lexicon + ratchet was worth +40, kit stutter +25. Relates to LIB-CANON-NAMES, GRANULAR-STATS, TYPEFEATURESET-ENFORCEMENT.")
inl note_of_naming_lexicon_ratchet_2 () = Note ("NAMING-LEXICON-RATCHET", 2u32, "2026-11-01T12:05Z", "kit", "11-01 08:1x kit: placed under GRANULAR-HEAL (cluster 9, 'code quality / typefeatureset / lib reuse', of the pre-merge map KIT-VERBS-SCOPE n2) as a child via the epic's deps; DIRECTION-1031 stays its origin dep. Estimate 240m (fill-all-task-fields). IMPLEMENTATION PATH (kit side): the rules become typed constructors in kit standards' existing rule union (src/kit_standards/rules.spi, beside NoCommentLines, LineWidth, LfOnly, TestTargets, HostFree, LibReferenced): e.g. Lexicon (the canonical verb list as data), NoPrime, NoPackageStutter, NoBackendPrefixOutsideHost, BannedTokens [..]. A repo opts in by declaring them in its standards.spi; 'kit standards --ratchet' freezes today's counts per package so only NEW violations fail (the census pattern), and the edit-time style hook reports them in the text just added. Order: (1) typed rule model + name scanner over top-level definitions with tests (CPU-light); (2) census columns + baseline merge for kit itself; (3) roll out repo by repo (lib first, since lib's names are what everyone copies). Overlaps to keep in view: EOIE-OPEN-SHADOW-LINT (name shadowing) and LIST-SUGAR-DRIFT-GUARD use the same scanner shape.")
inl note_of_naming_lexicon_ratchet_3 () = Note ("NAMING-LEXICON-RATCHET", 3u32, "2026-11-01T12:34Z", "main", "main 08:3x TYPEFEATURESET PLAN for the standards rules: each rule a union case (NameCase | BannedToken of string | NoPrime | NoPackageStutter | NoBackendPrefixOutsideHost | ...), each violation a record (rule, file, span, name, suggestion), and the lexicon a typed table (concept -> canonical, banned list) in Spiral, not a text file. The ratchet stores counts per (package, rule) as typed rows. NEXT STEP: kit reads tonight's lexicon from the archive's naming report (artifact d05f9cdd section 5) into kit's standards.spi as rows; run '--ratchet' in report mode across all repos to get the baseline counts. FIELDS: estimate 240, load light, owner kit (standards) + main.")
inl note_of_naming_lexicon_ratchet_4 () = Note ("NAMING-LEXICON-RATCHET", 4u32, "2026-11-01T12:36Z", "kit", "11-01 08:3x kit QUALITY PASS. DoD: kit standards accepts Lexicon, NoPrime, NoPackageStutter, NoBackendPrefixOutsideHost and BannedTokens rules in standards.spi; 'kit standards --ratchet standards\\census.spi src' on kit passes with the new census columns baselined, and a new definition named length/len/size where 'count' is canonical fails the ratchet (CLI test with a fixture package); lib adopts it first, then each repo. NEXT STEP: rule constructors in src/kit_standards/rules.spi and a top-level-name scanner (reuse kit_lint_shadows_core's definition walk). TYPEFEATURESET PLAN: naming_rule closed union with payloads (Lexicon of list verb_rule | NoPrime | NoPackageStutter | NoBackendPrefixOutsideHost | BannedTokens of list string); verb_rule a record (canonical, discouraged : list string); findings a union (StutterName of package * name | PrimedName of name | ...), never joined strings; the scanner yields a typed definition record (name, kind union, line).")
inl note_of_naming_lexicon_ratchet_5 () = Note ("NAMING-LEXICON-RATCHET", 5u32, "2026-11-01T22:53Z", "main", "main lib-reshape 11-01 18:5x: the lib rename half of the lexicon (no_prime, no m suffix on modules) is executed by LIB-RESHAPE (n1 has the full old->new table and consumer counts per repo). Measured constraint for the rule set: 'no m suffix' cannot apply to array/list/option/result until MODULE-TYPE-NAMESPACE lands (a module named like a core type shadows it; node repro in that item), so the ratchet must carry those four as known exceptions with that item id, not as violations to fix. Renames done today (R1a, if it closes green): am'_prototypes -> array_prototypes, sm'_prototypes -> text_prototypes, optionm'_operators -> option_operators, par' -> parallel; kit's standards/lint-macros-lib.spi:209 row names 'par'.spi' and needs 'parallel.spi'.")
inl note_of_naming_lexicon_ratchet_6 () = Note ("NAMING-LEXICON-RATCHET", 6u32, "2026-11-02T15:01Z", "kit", "kit 11-02 groom (AGILE-EPIC-DEP-LINT): parent epic DIRECTION-1031. It was this item's only dep, which hid the item from agile next because an epic finishes after its children; parents are named in notes, deps are for real ordering only.")
inl note_of_naming_lexicon_ratchet_7 () = Note ("NAMING-LEXICON-RATCHET", 7u32, "2026-11-03T17:47Z", "main", "main 11-03 13:5x BASELINE for kit's ratchet (top-level :=/:~/inl/let definition names; .spi, deps/target/generated excluded; lib = lib/spiral): defs | length/len/size | dir | ctx | prime suffix | load/save -> lib/spiral 3364 | 29 | 18 | 0 | 114 | 4; kit 21876 | 38 | 13 | 0 | 0 | 46; x 2812 | 11 | 4 | 0 | 0 | 0; tibia 3245 | 6 | 0 | 1 | 0 | 4; regex 428 | 6 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0; i574n.github 226 | 0 | 0 | 1 | 0 | 1; fiscal 175, blog 35, dice 86: all 0. html was 253 | 1 | 3 | 0 | 0 | 0 and is now ALL ZERO (main renamed is_dir -> is_directory, fixture_dir -> fixture_directory, make_dir -> make_directory, size_label -> bytes_label; vultr tree 5/5, pages 5/5, HTML-OK; regenerated .rs hash-identical box/vultr). Notes: lib's 114 primes are mostly module-style names (forall', unwrap'), which no_prime has to classify before ratcheting.")
inl note_of_naming_lexicon_ratchet_8 () = Note ("NAMING-LEXICON-RATCHET", 8u32, "2026-11-03T18:15Z", "main", "main 11-03 14:2x regex: renamed length_of -> code_count, resize -> grow_to, support_size -> support_count (no collisions; language, lazy, partial, semantics). Vultr regex gate 83/85: every renamed file passes; the 2 failures are certificate.spi (untouched), a pack25/26 Rust codegen regression (E0505: one var passed as &-param and by value in the same call), reported to compiler. regex left: size_of / minimal_size / size_text, named after the domain type size (States n | Refused n), so they need a type rename or a no_size exception for type-named functions in kit's rule.")
inl note_of_naming_lexicon_ratchet_9 () = Note ("NAMING-LEXICON-RATCHET", 9u32, "2026-11-03T19:39Z", "main", "main 11-03 15:4x regex follow-up: certificate.spi's 2 failures were pack25/26's E0505 (compiler fixed it in pack27); under pack27 on vultr certificate.spi is 16/16, so with the renames the full regex gate is green again.")
inl note_of_naming_lexicon_ratchet_10 () = Note ("NAMING-LEXICON-RATCHET", 10u32, "2026-11-04T11:31Z", "main", "11-04 07:5x main: the kernel app (spiral apps/kernel, not in the 11-03 baseline) had 2 off-lexicon names: term_size -> node_count (syntax.spi), field_ctx -> field_context (inductive.spi); no other uses anywhere in spiral apps or lib. VULTR kernel gate 58/58 KERNEL-OK with both renames. Kernel is now 0 on every column.")
inl note_of_naming_lexicon_ratchet_11 () = Note ("NAMING-LEXICON-RATCHET", 11u32, "2026-11-04T11:49Z", "main", "11-04 08:4x main PROGRESS 0 -> 30 (the field lagged the work): per-repo baseline (n7); html at zero (n7); regex renames gated green (n8/n9); kernel at zero (n10). LEFT: write the canonical lexicon into standards.spi, and kit's ratchet (baseline-only-shrinks check per repo, no_prime classifying module-style primes in lib).")
inl note_of_naming_lexicon_ratchet_12 () = Note ("NAMING-LEXICON-RATCHET", 12u32, "2026-11-04T11:53Z", "main", "11-04 09:0x main DRAFT of the typed lexicon + checker core (kit drafts store kit\\NAMING-LEXICON-RATCHET\\src\\name_lexicon.spi; kit's tree is mid-TRAIN22): concept union Count|Directory|Context with canonical word and banned tokens (count <- length/len/size; directory <- dir; context <- ctx); violation union Banned (name, token, canonical) | Primed name; violations_in allowed names; suggestion text; ratchet baseline now -> Shrunk n | Held | Grew n. KEY CHANGE vs the 11-03 baseline (n7): matching is by whole _-separated TOKENS, not substrings, so resize, dirty, sized and lengthen_by are clean (regex's resize rename in n8 was a substring false positive) and the baseline should be recounted under token rules. Known exceptions are an explicit allowed list (regex size_of, lib module-style primes forall' / unwrap', array/list/option/result per n5). 5 tests; gate PENDING on the node. Left for kit: the definition-name scanner feeding violations_in, plus per-(repo, rule) baseline rows.")
inl note_of_naming_lexicon_ratchet_13 () = Note ("NAMING-LEXICON-RATCHET", 13u32, "2026-11-04T12:13Z", "main", "11-04 09:4x main NODE (spiral 98e51d32 + draft): name_lexicon.spi gate 5/5 passed (banned tokens with their canonical word; whole tokens only, so resize/dirty/sized/lengthen_by/context_of are clean; prime plus the unprimed token check; allowed exceptions; ratchet Shrunk/Held/Grew). A first run went 0/5 because main's own mirror sync pruned the draft mid-gate (no code fault). Core PROVEN, handed to kit for the scanner + per-(repo, rule) baseline rows. Progress 30 -> 45.")
inl note_of_naming_lexicon_ratchet_14 () = Note ("NAMING-LEXICON-RATCHET", 14u32, "2026-11-04T14:47Z", "main", "11-04 10:5x main TOKEN RECOUNT of lib/spiral (top-level :~/:=/inl/let names, whole _-tokens, prime stripped): 55 definitions carry a banned token (dir 20, length 22, size 9, len 4) vs the 11-03 substring baseline (length/len/size 29, dir 18), which mixed undercounting and false hits. CLASSIFICATION the ratchet needs: (a) HOST-MIRROR names that wrap a host API and keep its spelling on purpose: file_system's create_dir_all, remove_dir_all, set_current_dir, get_current_dir, create_temp_dir, path_buf_is_dir, dir_entry_* (Rust std), erlang_map map_size, erlang_term byte_size_of (Erlang BIFs), erlang_http_server content_length (HTTP header). Suggest a HostMirror exception rule (the name equals or wraps a host identifier) instead of a growing allowed list; (b) SPIRAL-NATIVE names that should heal: am' length/length_base/vec_len/slice_length, array_dim length, dict size/set_size, env arguments_from_length, erlang_process group_size, erlang_term length_of. Those are core collection APIs with consumers across every repo, so heal them with deprecated aliases (LIB-LAYERS) after the ratchet lands.")
inl note_of_naming_lexicon_ratchet_15 () = Note ("NAMING-LEXICON-RATCHET", 15u32, "2026-11-05T05:24Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-05 01:4x kit: SCANNER + PER-PACKAGE RATCHET BUILT via the existing census (unstaged, rides the train after TRAIN27). New census probe banned-name-token (kit_style_probes: BannedNameToken :: probe drift, direction NeverRises). It counts TOP-LEVEL definitions (:~ / := / inl / let at column 0, optional ^) whose name contains a whole _-token from main's lexicon draft: length|len|size -> count, dir -> directory, ctx -> context, prime allowed. Pattern: ^(:~|:=|inl|let)\\s+(\\^\\s*)?([a-z0-9]+_)*(length|len|size|dir|ctx)(_[a-z0-9]+)*'*([\\s(]|$). CALIBRATED: on lib/spiral it counts 55, exactly main's 11-04 hand recount (an unanchored ^\\s* variant counted 139 by including locals). kit/src counts 53. Synthetic check: dir_name, length_of and ctx match; resize, sized and context_of do not. Each package's count becomes a census row, so the per-(repo, package) baseline is the existing standards/census.spi merge: existing names are frozen (main's HostMirror class needs no allow-list, since a host-mirror name already present is baseline), and any NEW banned name in a package fails the ratchet. ACTIVATION (same as host-statement): after the next install, run kit standards --baseline-merge standards\\census.spi over the package dirs; until then the probe only bites new packages. Proof: box notebooks probes.spi 2/2 (word list + direction); vultr --check ok with bodies. Note: the edit-time hook flagged kit_style_probes forall/existential 6/198 -> 6/237; that is the installed census counting test lines (the tree's census reads ~191 non-test lines, counts unchanged), not a regression.")
inl note_of_naming_lexicon_ratchet_16 () = Note ("NAMING-LEXICON-RATCHET", 16u32, "2026-11-05T09:28Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-05 05:3x kit: first HEAL batch in kit (unstaged), counted by the new probe: 53 -> 45 banned-name definitions. Renamed, whole-word in exactly the files that use each name, after a tree-wide grep showed no outside users and no collision with the new names: skipped_dir -> skipped_directory (kit_crate_walk, kit_heal_nameof, kit_lint_walk), dir_names -> directory_names (kit_crate_walk), text_length -> text_count (kit_adoption host+run, kit_guide, kit_memory_model, kit_memory_save, kit_table_logs). Export strings such as kit_text_length are untouched (word boundaries). Box --check ok for all 7 packages; one test message shortened to stay under 120 columns. Left in kit (45): mostly host mirrors (create_dir, args_len_is/argument_len wrapping Rust argv, byte_len, read_*_len), which the baseline freezes; plus a few domain names (file_size, size_verdict, bundle_size_binding, length_at) worth a later batch.")
inl note_of_naming_lexicon_ratchet_17 () = Note ("NAMING-LEXICON-RATCHET", 17u32, "2026-11-05T09:44Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-05 05:4x kit: second HEAL batch (unstaged, rides TRAIN28): 45 -> 32 banned-name definitions. path_is_dir/entry_is_dir/names_dir/scratch_dir/heavy_queue_dir/batch_output_dir/lock_dir_claimed/lock_dir_removed -> *_directory; file_size and file_length -> file_byte_count, and both definitions also lost their host macro (std::fs::metadata) for lib file_system.rust_file_length; size_verdict -> byte_count_verdict (agile_todo_model + scan + merge); ident_length -> ident_char_count; clause_length -> clause_char_count. Collision-checked first; whole-word, in exactly the files that use each name. Box --check ok for all 10 packages; box notebooks heal 1/1, crate_walk 2/2 (memory_model has no tests: that batch entry was void, not red). Remaining 32: host mirrors (create_dir, args_len_is, argument_len, byte_len, read_*_len, list_len, emit_run_bounded_program_in_dir) plus a few domain names (bundle/flat size_binding, length_at, codepoint_length, run_length = the term, length_between, text_count siblings).")
inl note_of_naming_lexicon_ratchet_18 () = Note ("NAMING-LEXICON-RATCHET", 18u32, "2026-11-05T10:15Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-05 06:2x kit: third HEAL batch (unstaged): 32 -> 27. length_at -> count_at (kit_os_json + its kit_exec_cells caller), byte_length -> byte_count and length_between -> byte_count_between (typed_predicate), codepoint_length(_loop) -> codepoint_count(_loop) (state_receipt_codec_model). Collision-checked; box --check ok for all 4 packages. Left as they are on purpose: the exported contract-probe names (bundle_size_binding, flat_size_binding, size_binding; their export strings and probe callers form a contract) and the host mirrors. Total today: 53 -> 27.")
inl note_of_naming_lexicon_ratchet_19 () = Note ("NAMING-LEXICON-RATCHET", 19u32, "2026-11-05T19:32Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-05 15:3x kit: fourth HEAL batch (unstaged, after the TRAIN32 snapshot, so it rides TRAIN33): 27 -> 21 banned-name definitions. args_len_is -> argument_count_is (rust_std_env model + main, kit_agile_mutation, kit_agile_routes, patch_mutation_contract), argument_len -> argument_byte_count (rust_std_env, agile_task_creation, kit_agile_lease, kit_agile_mutation, kit_agile_routes), stream_stdout_len / stream_stderr_len -> *_byte_count (bootstrap_policy), emit_run_bounded_program_in_dir -> ..._in_directory (kit_process). Collision-checked (0 prior uses of each new name), whole-word, 0 old uses left, no export strings or host-string callers involved. Two agile_mutation lines that went past 120 columns were wrapped (long-line count back to HEAD's 2). PROOF PENDING: node size + cargo probe of the 8 packages after TRAIN32 leaves regen (box at 100 pct).")
inl note_of_naming_lexicon_ratchet_20 () = Note ("NAMING-LEXICON-RATCHET", 20u32, "2026-11-05T19:40Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-05 15:4x kit: fifth HEAL batch (unstaged, TRAIN33): source_len -> source_count, whole-word in all 10 packages that use it (handoff_active_work/evidence/sections/state projections, kit_bundle_zip, kit_patch_grammar, patch_domain, patch_mutation_contract, state_receipt_codec_model, state_receipt_parser_domain). 0 prior uses of source_count, 0 old uses left. Mostly locals (sm.length source), one top-level definition (patch_domain context.spi), so the banned-definition count is 21 -> 20. One line in handoff_sections_projection_source went to 122 columns and was wrapped onto a |: continuation. PROOF PENDING with batch 4 (type-check + node size/cargo once the box or node frees).")
inl note_of_naming_lexicon_ratchet_21 () = Note ("NAMING-LEXICON-RATCHET", 21u32, "2026-11-05T22:39Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-05 18:4x kit: batches 4+5 PROVEN on vultr (job-1793917500111-kit-probe33d, pack51 mirror kit-probe33, 52 packages): regen ok for every renamed package and consumer, cargo check --keep-going ERRORS=0. Sizes: kit_agile_mutation 978, kit_agile_routes 852, kit_process 70, agile_task_creation 991, kit_agile_lease 352, kit_bundle_zip 788, kit_patch_grammar 357, patch_domain 509, kit_handoff 608, state_receipt_parser_domain 495, state_receipt_codec_core 820. Rides TRAIN33.")
inl lib_layers () = Task ("LIB-LAYERS", "Layer the lib: meta (compile-time only), core (pure, any backend incl. WGSL/eBPF), std (portable with runtime), host.<backend>, os, and ecosystem packages (json, csv, xml, http, near, leptos, lustre, iggy); drop the m suffix (needs UpperCamelCase nominal types or separate type/value namespaces), apostrophes and the base/common/util drawers; migrate with a rename map and deprecated aliases", Epic, P2, D7, 0u32, Planned, "NAMING-LEXICON-RATCHET,LIB-RESHAPE", "lib,naming,structure,main,team:main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_lib_layers () = Planning ("LIB-LAYERS", Minutes 2400u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_lib_layers_1 () = Note ("LIB-LAYERS", 1u32, "2026-11-01T12:04Z", "main", "main 08:0x from the naming report (artifact d05f9cdd, section 4, with a module-by-module mapping). Worth +60 on the 575/1000 standardization score. Order there: meta + core, then std/os/host, ecosystem, apps, then regenerate crates with a rename changelog. Relates to LIB-CANON-NAMES and LIB-SPIRAL-TOML-MIGRATION.")
inl note_of_lib_layers_2 () = Note ("LIB-LAYERS", 2u32, "2026-11-01T23:52Z", "main", "main 20:0x GROOM, reconciled with the user's 11-01 17:4x instruction: 'don't create subfolders, leave everything flat for now (unless that rust folder is paying off big bucks) ... that leptos ui dir seems needed though'. So the LAYERS ARE NOT FOLDERS for now. A layer is a typed attribute of each lib module (meta | core | std | host of backend | os | ecosystem), checked by a ratchet: a core module may not import std/host/os. Lib stays flat except leptos/ui; rust/ is kept only if LIB-RESHAPE measures that it pays. The m-suffix drop waits on MODULE-TYPE-NAMESPACE, because a module named like a core type shadows it (memory lib-module-name-shadows-core-type). DoD: (1) every lib module carries its layer (typed, in package metadata or a census row); (2) a ratchet check fails a layering violation; (3) the 'base/common/util' drawers are emptied into named modules; (4) the deprecated-alias rename map ships with LIB-RESHAPE's lands. NEXT: LIB-RESHAPE's call-graph metrics (LIB-USAGE-VERB) assign each module's layer from its imports. Typefeatureset: Layer as a closed union; the allowed import relation as a total function Layer -> Layer -> bool, tested.")
inl dialect_identity () = Task ("DIALECT-IDENTITY", "Identity of the fork, per the user's choice 'spiral (i574n dialect)' for now: manifest separates dialect = i574n from edition = <year> (Rust-style), no new release of the VS Code extension under upstream's spiral id and .spi until the identity settles, a NOTICE crediting mrakgr's upstream, and the eventual public name decided before the first public distribution (package, extension or launch post)", Story, P1, D3, 45u32, Planned, "", "identity,edition3,upstream,team:main,user-batch", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_dialect_identity () = Planning ("DIALECT-IDENTITY", Minutes 120u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_dialect_identity_1 () = Note ("DIALECT-IDENTITY", 1u32, "2026-11-01T12:04Z", "main", "main 08:0x from rounds 3-4 of the naming reports (artifacts d05f9cdd, 318a5cad) and the user's 10-31 14:39 message. Upstream is active (v2.18.x 'out of prerelease' in nov/2025, PRs merged in jun/2026); mrakgr treats Spiral as his legacy and noted the Firefox trademark rule in 2020; MPL-2.0 grants no right to the name. A draft blessing message to him is in the report: sending it is the USER's call (outward, public). Relates to SPIRAL3-EDITIONS and UPSTREAM-CHANGES-POST.")
inl note_of_dialect_identity_2 () = Note ("DIALECT-IDENTITY", 2u32, "2026-11-01T12:34Z", "main", "main 08:3x DoD: (1) spiral.toml schema has dialect = 'i574n' and edition = <year> as separate typed fields (a union for dialect: Upstream | I574n), read by Cells manifest.ex and the compiler's --resolve-manifest, with a test each; (2) NOTICE file in spiral crediting mrakgr's upstream (MPL-2.0); (3) a written rule: no VS Code extension release with id spiral/.spi until the public name is set. NEXT STEP: draft the manifest schema change against Cells manifest.ex (landed EDITION3-MANIFEST-MODEL) and the NOTICE text for user review. User-only: sending the blessing message to mrakgr. FIELDS: estimate 120, load light, owner main.")
inl note_of_dialect_identity_3 () = Note ("DIALECT-IDENTITY", 3u32, "2026-11-01T21:00Z", "main", "main 17:0x NAMING RESEARCH from the user's side agent (11-01, sci-fi lore; collisions checked by web search). TAKEN: Babel (JS compiler), Yatima (yatima-inc, Lean 4 -> Lurk), Gevulot (ZK prover chain), Focus (old 4GL). FREE as far as found: Qeng Ho, Sobornost, Conceptory. Ranking: (1) Qeng Ho (Vinge, A Deepness in the Sky), .qh: free traders whose code archive solves anything, plus programmer-archaeologists (our forensics and porting) and the Focused (lanes; mind the enslavement caveat, use as self-aware lore). (2) Babel-17 (Delany), .b17: a language that shapes thought, 'if there's no word for it, how do you think about it', the typefeatureset pitch; 'Butcher' as a linter/splitter name. (3) Conceptory (Egan, Diaspora): mind seed, shapers = small cells, orphanogenesis = self-hosting. (4) Quantum Thief as internal lexicon: zoku = editions/dialects, gevulot = phantom capability borders (internal only), Sobornost = forked lanes. (5) Primer (The Diamond Age) for Cells pages, not collision-checked. Suggested combination: Qeng Ho as language/org name, with lanes = Focused, resilience = programmer-archaeologists, editions = zoku, capability phantoms = gevulot, self-hosting compiler = conceptory, cells = shapers, pages = primers; i574n could stay as the dialect name. Gaps: 'Geandra' wasn't found by the searches, and no mrakgr 'Simulacrum' project turned up. USER DECISION (parked for the 22:00 batch): pick a name, or keep researching.")
inl note_of_dialect_identity_4 () = Note ("DIALECT-IDENTITY", 4u32, "2026-11-01T21:35Z", "main", "main 17:3x NAMING, deeper pass (user's side agent): new top pick MARAIN (Iain M. Banks, the Culture): a language designed by AI Minds partly to shape how its speakers think, which is the typefeatureset pitch. .mrn; no programming language named Marain found (only Banks's conlang/script). Caveat: Banks's estate may care about commercial use, so check before publishing. Other free candidates: Qeng Ho (.qh), Nam-shub (Snow Crash, .nsh: 'me' = encoded procedures, Enki the programmer-god; fits kit do / lib land programs), Autoverse (Egan, .av). Taken: Babel, Yatima, Gevulot, Enki (two GitHub languages). Lore set: language = Marain, lib = the Qeng Ho archive, lanes = Focused specialists with Culture ship names, patches = nam-shubs, compiler = mind seed of shapers, Cells pages = semagrams (Heptapod B); Heinlein's Speedtalk ('logical errors only statable as obvious errors') as the type-system tagline; Lem's Trurl as a mascot. i574n can stay as the edition/dialect name. Still open: 'Geandra' and an mrakgr 'Simulacrum' weren't found. USER DECISION parked for the 22:00 batch.")
inl note_of_dialect_identity_5 () = Note ("DIALECT-IDENTITY", 5u32, "2026-11-01T21:50Z", "main", "main 17:5x NAMING, third pass (user's side agent) SUPERSEDES n4: MARAIN IS OUT, because endjin runs the 'marain-dotnet' GitHub org, an open-source .NET service platform named Marain. Recommendation: keep SPIRAL as the family (mrakgr's journal, 2017-11-19: 'Spiral will be my one contribution to this world. Simulacrum is my soul. Spiral is my power.'; probable Gurren Lagann Spiral Power lore: evolution, nested growth, the Anti-Spirals who stopped evolving). Give the fork a lineage-declaring name: RASEN (Japanese for 'spiral', .ras; crate 'rasen' is taken by an idle SPIR-V builder; also a 1998 film title) or GYRE (Yeats' widening gyre; 0 language repos found). Qeng Ho fits the lib/org; nam-shub, Culture ship names, shapers and semagrams stay internal flavor. Constraint: MPL-2.0 grants no rights to the name, so declare the lineage. Before announcing: a trademark search, and the user's blessing message to mrakgr (drafted, parked here). USER DECISION for the 22:00 batch: Spiral edition 3 'i574n dialect' as is, Rasen, or Gyre.")
inl note_of_dialect_identity_6 () = Note ("DIALECT-IDENTITY", 6u32, "2026-11-02T14:29Z", "x", "2026-11-02 10:28 x: DECISION RECORD v2 (user 11-02: 'why mine? try healing autonomously/deterministically with tests/benchs/typefeatureset/backups the pending decisions'). Only git commits (the user's standing rule) and the harness hook config (the harness itself says Claude must not change it) stay user-only. Everything else gets a precondition, a check and an undo; actions only the user's accounts can take get a non-blocking default. kind=ByDefault. RULE: Default = NOT SENT, a no-op. The standing rule no-public-project-interaction means agents never contact upstream authors. Nothing in any repo blocks on the message: the dialect name is already used locally. So this stops waiting on the user; the user sends the prepared text whenever they choose. BACKUP/UNDO: No state change. OWNER: main.")
inl note_of_dialect_identity_7 () = Note ("DIALECT-IDENTITY", 7u32, "2026-11-02T14:39Z", "main", "main 11-02: parent epic is DIRECTION-1031; recorded here instead of as a dep (sole dep on an unfinished parent epic hid it from 'agile next'; AGILE-EPIC-DEP-LINT).")
inl note_of_dialect_identity_8 () = Note ("DIALECT-IDENTITY", 8u32, "2026-11-02T21:42Z", "main", "main 11-02 17:4x RE-DECIDED Paused -> Planned for the autonomous part. Repo metadata is autonomous (repo-metadata-autonomous), so a NOTICE crediting mrakgr's upstream and the manifest dialect/edition split need no user. Only the eventual PUBLIC NAME stays the user's call, and that matters only before the first public distribution.")
inl note_of_dialect_identity_9 () = Note ("DIALECT-IDENTITY", 9u32, "2026-11-02T21:44Z", "main", "main 11-02 17:5x NOTICE added at spiral/NOTICE (unstaged): 'spiral (i574n dialect)', a fork of The Spiral Language by mrakgr (repo URL), upstream licensed MPL-2.0 (link to its LICENSE). Facts only, no per-file license claims. FOR THE USER (2am report): spiral's LICENSE is AGPL-3.0 and the upstream is MPL-2.0; the regex subagent also copied AGPL into 8 repos on 11-02. Whether the MPL-derived files need their own marking is a licensing call for the user, not something main decides. Next autonomous step: the manifest dialect/edition split.")
inl note_of_dialect_identity_10 () = Note ("DIALECT-IDENTITY", 10u32, "2026-11-02T21:45Z", "main", "main 11-02 17:5x manifest split SCOPED. Today edition carries both meanings: spiral_compiler.fs:286 'edition: 2 (upstream syntax), 3 (this fork's syntax)', the spiproj parser at :29341, the edition:2 sniff at :33573, host SPIRAL_EDITION 2|3 (Program.fs:1242). 8 manifests declare 'edition: 3' (fiscal src, i574n.github graphs + pins, kit iggy codec, tibia arena/canary/predict, x client). The split ('dialect: i574n' + 'edition: <year>', with 2 = upstream and 3 = i574n mapped forward) is a compiler schema change, so it is COMPILER's to land, after LIB-RESHAPE (pack18 just unblocked the m-suffix renames). Main hands it over in the next batched message to compiler; then main respells the 8 manifests.")
inl note_of_dialect_identity_11 () = Note ("DIALECT-IDENTITY", 11u32, "2026-11-03T07:31Z", "main", "main 11-03 03:3x LICENSING DECIDED by rule (user 11-03: pending decisions get settled autonomously). FACTS: upstream mrakgr/The-Spiral-Language is MPL-2.0 (deps\\The-Spiral-Language\\LICENSE). spiral's LICENSE has been AGPL-3.0 since its first commit, 6eeabc24 (2025-01-04). spiral/NOTICE no longer exists: another lane removed it on 11-02 as unrequested, so main's 2am line 'spiral/NOTICE credits mrakgr' was WRONG. RULE, from the MPL-2.0 text: it is file-level copyleft. Sec. 3.3 lets a Larger Work be distributed under a Secondary License (GPL family, including AGPL-3.0) as long as the MPL-covered files stay available under MPL-2.0 and keep their notice (Exhibit A or an equivalent pointer). DECISION: spiral stays AGPL-3.0 as a whole; files derived from upstream are dual-available (MPL-2.0 plus AGPL-3.0) and must say so. MINIMAL FORM: no NOTICE file. Instead, one 'License' line in spiral's README naming the upstream-derived paths and pointing to upstream's MPL-2.0, plus upstream's license text kept where it already is (deps\\The-Spiral-Language\\LICENSE). OWNER compiler: it knows which apps/compiler sources derive from upstream. NEXT: compiler lists those paths, then main writes the README line.")
inl note_of_dialect_identity_12 () = Note ("DIALECT-IDENTITY", 12u32, "2026-11-03T07:50Z", "compiler", "11-03 07:4x compiler: UPSTREAM-DERIVED PATHS in spiral (MPL-2.0 file-level), measured, not guessed. Method: every distinct trimmed line over 30 chars of pure upstream mrakgr/master (35fb83e0, 2026-06-30, git archive from the deps clone; our fork commits on local master excluded), compared with every git-tracked spiral file; plus a byte-identical hash pass.\nRESULT: exactly ONE derived file. apps/compiler/spiral_compiler.fs: 5,468 of its 18,482 long lines (30%) are upstream lines. It is the single-file merge of upstream \"The Spiral Language 2/*.fs\": PartEval 93% of upstream's lines present, BlockBundling 97, HashConsing 100, ParserCombinators 97, RefCounting 92, Infer 82, Tokenize 83, Prepass 81, BlockParsing 79, CodegenC 79, CodegenFsharp 79, CodegenCuda 76, WDiff 76, CodegenPython 68, ServerUtils 66, Supervisor 60, SpiProj 60, plus small ones (Graph, Startup, Utils, VSCTypes, LineParsers, BlockSplitting, CodegenUtils, PersistentVectorExtensions, WDiffPrepass).\nNOT derived (0% of long lines in upstream): apps/compiler/tmp/compiler/host (Program.fs; 2 of 834 lines, boilerplate), apps/compiler/tmp/samples (1,281 .spi, 0 of 53,956; includes tmp/samples/core, our own), apps/compiler/tmp/tests, lib/spiral (128 .spi, 3 of 15,255 trivial), apps/zed, apps/cells, apps/spiral. The 3 byte-identical files are trivial (two 35-byte package.spiproj and one empty near_wallet.rs).\nTHE CORE LIB: spiral does not ship upstream's core. |core- resolves at build time from deps/The-Spiral-Language/VS Code Plugin/core, an untracked clone (deps/ is in .gitignore, 0 tracked files), so it stays under upstream's own MPL-2.0 license in upstream's repo.\nSuggested License line: \"spiral is AGPL-3.0-only. apps/compiler/spiral_compiler.fs is derived from mrakgr/The-Spiral-Language (The Spiral Language 2) and remains available under MPL-2.0.\" If the file is later split, every split file inherits the MPL notice.")
inl note_of_dialect_identity_13 () = Note ("DIALECT-IDENTITY", 13u32, "2026-11-03T08:00Z", "main", "main 11-03 03:5x PLAN (paused): compiler's n12 shows ONLY apps/compiler/spiral_compiler.fs derives from upstream (about 30% of its distinctive lines; a merge of 'The Spiral Language 2/*.fs'); everything else is 0%. The core lib is not shipped, because deps/ is untracked and keeps upstream's license. NEXT, when the run starts: one 'License' line in spiral's README per the decision of n11, naming apps/compiler/spiral_compiler.fs as also available under MPL-2.0 (upstream mrakgr/The-Spiral-Language), using compiler's suggested line from n12.")
inl note_of_dialect_identity_14 () = Note ("DIALECT-IDENTITY", 14u32, "2026-11-05T19:17Z", "main", "main 11-05 15:2x ORPHAN SWEEP: Active untouched 48 h+ (last 11-03 08:00 PLAN paused) -> Planned at 30; the planned next step (one 'License' line in the manifest separating dialect = i574n from upstream's spiral_compiler.fs share) is unchanged and needs no CPU.")
inl note_of_dialect_identity_15 () = Note ("DIALECT-IDENTITY", 15u32, "2026-11-05T22:50Z", "main", "main 11-05 18:5x CHECK vs the title's four parts: (1) upstream credit: DONE in HEAD. spiral README.md:100 says apps/compiler/spiral_compiler.fs derives from mrakgr/The-Spiral-Language and is also available under its MPL-2.0 (deps/The-Spiral-Language/LICENSE), shipped in the user's faf422c9 (11-04); this is n11's minimal form, a README line instead of a NOTICE file, matching n12's measurement of exactly one derived file. (2) manifest dialect = i574n / edition = <year>: NOT started; no package.spiproj carries either field, so it's a manifest-schema change (compiler: parser + spiproj model) to file as a child when picked. (3) no extension release under upstream's spiral id: a standing rule, nothing pending. (4) public name before first public distribution: the user's call, no distribution planned. 30 -> 45.")
inl repo_hygiene_licenses () = Task ("REPO-HYGIENE-LICENSES", "Repo hygiene from the standardization audit: kit, x, fiscal and blog have no LICENSE (kit declares UNLICENSED); dice, polyglot and alphabet still carry ChatGPT-draft READMEs; dice's README should lead as the flagship full-stack sample (F#/Python/Rust/TS lib, NEAR contract, WASM UI, e2e) with its enumeration proof that d6 -> uniform is unbiased and the NEAR randomness limit (the block producer can predict it; betting needs commit-reveal or VRF)", Story, P1, D2, 100u32, Done, "DIRECTION-1031", "repos,license,readme,dice,main,user-batch", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_repo_hygiene_licenses () = Planning ("REPO-HYGIENE-LICENSES", Minutes 120u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_repo_hygiene_licenses_1 () = Note ("REPO-HYGIENE-LICENSES", 1u32, "2026-11-01T12:04Z", "main", "main 08:0x. Choosing licenses is the user's decision (the report notes the generated-code question: without an AGPL-style exception or a permissive license for generated output, both business and open-source routes stall); main drafts READMEs and the license options for approval. Scores +25 (licenses) and +15 (READMEs) on the 575/1000 audit.")
inl note_of_repo_hygiene_licenses_2 () = Note ("REPO-HYGIENE-LICENSES", 2u32, "2026-11-01T12:34Z", "main", "main 08:3x DoD: every i574n repo has a LICENSE the user chose (kit, x, fiscal, blog today have none); dice/polyglot/alphabet READMEs rewritten by hand-checked facts (no ChatGPT draft text); dice README leads with the flagship description, the d6 -> uniform enumeration proof and the NEAR randomness caveat. NEXT STEP: main lists per repo the current license field (package files, spiproj, Cargo) and drafts 2-3 license options with the generated-code consequence of each, for the user's choice (user-batch). FIELDS: estimate 120, load light, owner main; license choice assignee user.")
inl note_of_repo_hygiene_licenses_3 () = Note ("REPO-HYGIENE-LICENSES", 3u32, "2026-11-02T14:40Z", "main", "main 11-02: parent epic is DIRECTION-1031; recorded here instead of as a dep (sole dep on an unfinished parent epic hid it from 'agile next'; AGILE-EPIC-DEP-LINT).")
inl note_of_repo_hygiene_licenses_4 () = Note ("REPO-HYGIENE-LICENSES", 4u32, "2026-11-03T01:49Z", "main", "main 11-02 21:5x LICENSE HALF DONE. The standing rule (repo-metadata-autonomous: a missing LICENSE gets AGPL v3, no asking) supersedes n1's 'user picks'. Survey: all 17 i574n repos now carry AGPL v3 at the root (kit, x, fiscal and blog included; the regex worker added 8 on 11-02; i574n.github.io and dotfiles still untracked). Manifest fields: kit src/Cargo.toml said license = 'UNLICENSED', which contradicted its LICENSE: now license-file = '../LICENSE' (unstaged), the same as spiral workspace and regex bench. Left as is: vendored crates' own licenses (kit src/vendor), and spiral apps/zed's 'Apache-2.0' (the Zed extension template's). The generated-code exception question (n1) goes to the user with the MPL/AGPL note (DIALECT-IDENTITY n9). LEFT: the README half (dice/alphabet rewritten from checked facts; polyglot retires).")
inl note_of_repo_hygiene_licenses_5 () = Note ("REPO-HYGIENE-LICENSES", 5u32, "2026-11-03T01:53Z", "main", "main 11-02 22:0x README HALF. alphabet README.md REWRITTEN (unstaged) from checked facts, the ChatGPT draft and its claims ('rule-based and AI-enhanced', 'universal tool') gone: apps/documents = markdown tree -> HTML/PDF/EPUB via crowbook plus a hangulize transcription (.hangul.md) with --hangul-spec (default por-br, English text eng), --filter, --transcribe-only and a content-hash cache, as vault uses it; apps/hangul = hanja-to-hangul conversion + CP949 tables; init clones spiral + installs pandoc/crowbook/tectonic/NanumGothicCoding/hangulize. Every flag is checked against documents.spi:14-16,133,525. dice: README drafted (scratchpad dice-README.md), leading with the flagship framing and the fairness argument (rolls read as base-6 + rejection sampling: one-to-one, so uniform) and the NEAR random_seed caveat (the block producer can predict it; use commit-reveal or a VRF for stakes). It goes in once its new proof test passes: dice/lib/dice.spi 'every_two_roll_sequence_maps_to_one_value_so_accepted_rolls_are_uniform' (all 36 two-roll sequences with max 20: each of 1..20 exactly once, 16 rejected), node run queued. polyglot: retiring, no README work.")
inl note_of_repo_hygiene_licenses_6 () = Note ("REPO-HYGIENE-LICENSES", 6u32, "2026-11-03T01:56Z", "main", "main 11-02 22:0x CORRECTION to n4: main's change of kit src/Cargo.toml [workspace.package] license = 'UNLICENSED' -> license-file = '../LICENSE' BROKE every kit build (cargo metadata: 'workspace.package.license was not defined'), because all 282 kit members inherit license.workspace = true. kit fixed it as license = 'AGPL-3.0-only', the SPDX id of kit's LICENSE, which is also the right metadata. The broken window was 21:5x until kit's fix. Lesson saved (memory workspace-package-keys-are-inherited).")
inl note_of_repo_hygiene_licenses_7 () = Note ("REPO-HYGIENE-LICENSES", 7u32, "2026-11-03T02:27Z", "main", "main 11-02 22:3x README HALF DONE. dice: lib/dice.spi 'every_two_roll_sequence_maps_to_one_value_so_accepted_rolls_are_uniform' PROVEN on the node (dice lib gate 24/24, memo off: all 36 two-roll sequences with max 20 give each of 1..20 exactly once and reject 16), so dice README.md was replaced (unstaged) by the checked-facts version: the flagship framing (one Spiral source -> library, NEAR contract, WASM UI, sandbox + e2e tests), the fairness argument (k = smallest count with 6^k >= max via calculate_dice_count, rolls read as base-6 + 1 via accumulate_dice_rolls, rejection by roll_within_bounds; one-to-one, hence uniform, citing that test) and the NEAR random_seed caveat (the block producer knows it; commit-reveal or a VRF for stakes). alphabet README done (n5). polyglot retires (no README). All DoD points met except the user's generated-code license exception question, which is in the 2am report.")
inl note_of_repo_hygiene_licenses_8 () = Note ("REPO-HYGIENE-LICENSES", 8u32, "2026-11-03T08:58Z", "main", "main 11-03 04:3x SCOPE: polyglot's ChatGPT-draft README is moot, since polyglot left the org 11-03. Left: only the generated-code license question, which DIALECT-IDENTITY n11/n13 settles by rule (spiral stays AGPL-3.0; apps/compiler/spiral_compiler.fs is also MPL-2.0; one README line).")
inl note_of_repo_hygiene_licenses_9 () = Note ("REPO-HYGIENE-LICENSES", 9u32, "2026-11-03T18:05Z", "main", "main 11-03 14:1x DONE. The last open point, the generated-code/compiler license line decided in DIALECT-IDENTITY n11/n13, is written (unstaged): spiral README's License section now says apps/compiler/spiral_compiler.fs derives from The Spiral Language and is also available under its MPL-2.0 (upstream LICENSE verified: Mozilla Public License Version 2.0). AGPL v3 is on all org repos; polyglot's README is moot (left the org).")
inl kit_agile_unspell () = Task ("KIT-AGILE-UNSPELL", "kit agile check refuses a respelled state and kit agile unspell restores it (remainder of KIT-STATE-SPELLING-GUARD)", Story, P1, D2, 0u32, Planned, "", "kit,team:kit,agile,respell,state", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_kit_agile_unspell () = Planning ("KIT-AGILE-UNSPELL", Minutes 120u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_kit_agile_unspell_1 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-UNSPELL", 1u32, "2026-11-01T12:34Z", "kit", "11-01 08:3x kit, split from KIT-STATE-SPELLING-GUARD (closed 08:02 by compiler: the header mechanism is live on both sides, formatter half in PACK-12). This item holds kit's REMAINDER. DoD: (a) 'kit agile check <root>' on a backlog copy whose rows were respelled to ':=' exits nonzero with 'state was respelled: run kit agile unspell <root>' (CLI test); (b) 'kit agile unspell <root>' restores that copy byte-identical (sha256 equal; CLI test), CAS-guarded with a hashed backup; (c) after TRAIN7 installs the fixed lease and receipt writers, the live prompt.spi and typecheck_receipts.spi keep '///- --no-respell' through a real 'kit agile begin' and 'kit agile check' (first line verified). NEXT STEP: wire agile_state_spelling.respelled_rows into kit_agile_state's check preflight; the unspell verb in agile_state_runtime using agile_state_spelling.unspelled. TYPEFEATURESET PLAN: row_head stays a closed union (KeywordHead | SymbolHead of head_word | OtherLine; head_word = HeadInl | HeadUnion | HeadOpen | HeadType); add a phantom-indexed state_text (spelled / respelled) so check accepts only a value proven keyword-spelled and unspell has the type state_text respelled -> state_text spelled; the respelled count is an i32 count, never a -1 'unknown'.")
inl kit_agile_quality_lint () = Task ("KIT-AGILE-QUALITY-LINT", "kit agile lint: per-item quality checks (fields, DoD line, next step, typefeatureset plan, silent Active) so lazy grooming fails in the tool", Story, P0, D3, 70u32, Active, "", "kit,team:kit,agile,quality,groom", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_kit_agile_quality_lint () = Planning ("KIT-AGILE-QUALITY-LINT", Minutes 180u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_kit_agile_quality_lint_1 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-QUALITY-LINT", 1u32, "2026-11-01T12:35Z", "kit", "11-01 08:3x kit (main round 4; user 08:3x: 'these groomings are coming out lazy af, and we'll always have to end up rewriting things anyway because the typefeatureset usage come out sloppy'). The tool should refuse lazy items instead of relying on rounds. WHAT: 'kit agile lint <root> [--team t] [--id ID] [--json]' reports per open item which quality checks fail: (Q1) planning fields set (estimate, load, assignee) and a team tag; (Q2) a definition-of-done note: a note containing a 'DoD:' line that names a command or test and a threshold; (Q3) a next-step line ('NEXT STEP:' with a path, function or command); (Q4) a 'TYPEFEATURESET PLAN:' line for items tagged as code (kit, lib, compiler ...); plus age checks reused from KIT-AGILE-CADENCE (Active without a note in 12 h). 'kit agile next' can then rank lint-clean items first, and the Stop gate can name the worst offenders. DoD: on a fixture backlog with one item per failure mode, 'kit agile lint' prints exactly those failures (CLI test, 0 false positives on a clean item); on the live backlog it runs in under 3 s and kit's own open items reach 0 Q1-Q4 failures within a week (cadence report column). NEXT STEP: a typed lint model in a new HostFree package agile_item_lint: quality_check union (MissingField of field_name | NoDefinitionOfDone | NoNextStep | NoTypefeaturesetPlan | SilentActive of hours), item_report record, report_of item notes -> list quality_check, with tests. TYPEFEATURESET PLAN: checks are a closed union with payloads (never strings like 'missing estimate'); field_name a closed union (Estimate | Load | Assignee | TeamTag); the marker lines are parsed into a typed note_marker union (DoD | NextStep | TypefeaturesetPlan) once, so the lint never greps text twice; a phantom linted/unlinted index on item lets 'next --lint-clean' accept only checked items.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_quality_lint_2 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-QUALITY-LINT", 2u32, "2026-11-01T12:42Z", "kit", "11-01 08:4x kit, GRANDFATHERING DECIDED (main 08:4x): STRICT ON TOUCH, RATCHET ON LEGACY. (1) Strict: an item ADDED, or given a STATUS CHANGE, after the lint ships must pass every rule; 'kit agile add' and 'kit agile set' refuse it otherwise (exit 2 naming each failing check), so a lazy item cannot enter or move. (2) Ratchet: legacy items are never errors; 'kit agile lint --census' reports a per-team violation count per check, baselined like standards/census.spi, and the ratchet only lets each count fall. (3) Markers: a check passes on either marker lines (DoD:, NEXT:, TYPEFEATURES:) or tonight's phrases ('definition of done' / 'DoD', 'next step' / 'NEXT STEP', 'typefeatureset plan' / 'TYPEFEATURESET PLAN'), so the round-4 notes already count. DoD updated: CLI tests for 'add' refused on a missing DoD/NEXT/TYPEFEATURES line (and accepted with phrase-style notes), 'set' refused on an unmarked item, census ratchet failing when a team's count rises; the live run must leave kit's 25 round-4 items lint-clean. EDGE CASE TO SETTLE BEFORE SHIPPING: strict-on-touch also blocks CLOSING a legacy item that has no plan (set ... Done). Proposed rule: a transition to Done requires only the evidence check (a note with a passing command or proof), since a typefeatureset plan for finished work is ceremony; Paused requires reason + resume condition (KIT-PAUSE-RULE-REWORK). TYPEFEATURESET PLAN unchanged plus: the gate is a typed transition check, transition = Add | SetStatus of from * to, mapped to the required check set by a total function (pattern per transition), so the Done exception is one match arm, not a string test.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_quality_lint_3 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-QUALITY-LINT", 3u32, "2026-11-01T12:44Z", "kit", "11-01 08:5x kit, TRANSITION ARMS DECIDED (main 08:5x), replacing the n2 proposal. The strict-on-touch gate checks, per target status: (a) Done: only the EVIDENCE check (a note citing a passing command, test or proof; closing a legacy item never requires a plan for finished work); (b) Paused: a reason plus a resume condition (an event or a date; KIT-PAUSE-RULE-REWORK); (c) Blocked: the blocker NAMED, either an existing item id or 'user' with the exact user step (main's evidence: CODESPACES-QUOTA-RUNS and PS1-TO-SPIRAL sat for days behind blockers nobody could see); (d) Active and Planned (and Add): the FULL check (fields + DoD + NEXT + TYPEFEATURES). DoD additions: CLI tests: 'set X Done' passes with only an evidence note; 'set X Blocked' is refused without a named blocker and accepted with 'blocked by: KIT-FOO' or 'blocked by: user, <step>'; a named item id that does not exist is refused (ParentUnknown-style check); 'set X Paused' is refused without a resume condition. TYPEFEATURESET PLAN for the gate: required_checks : transition -> list check, a total match over the target status union (Planned | Active | Blocked | Paused | Done); blocker = BlockedByItem of task_id | BlockedByUser of user_step (nominal text) parsed once from the note marker 'blocked by:'; resume = ResumeOnEvent of string | ResumeOnDate of date nominal; the gate result is a union (Admit | Refuse of list check) whose refusal text is rendered from the checks, never typed by hand.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_quality_lint_4 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-QUALITY-LINT", 4u32, "2026-11-01T17:01Z", "kit", "11-01 13:1x kit: new rule to add when KIT-DRAFTS-STORE lands: an open item whose drafts in %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\<repo>\\<ITEM>\\ are older than N days (default 7), or a Done item that still owns drafts, is flagged with the fix (kit drafts promote or archive).")
inl note_of_kit_agile_quality_lint_5 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-QUALITY-LINT", 5u32, "2026-11-04T09:55Z", "kit", "11-04 08:2x kit: the MODEL is in the tree (unstaged), as new package src/agile_quality_lint (lint.spi, 100 lines). It is a workspace member and Cargo.lock is refreshed (cargo metadata --offline --locked exit 0), not wired. Typed: lint_check = PlanningFields | DoneDefinition | NextStep | TypeFeatures | DoneEvidence | BlockerNamed | PauseResume; item_facts record (status, tags, estimate, load, assignee, notes text). checks_for follows the 11-01 TRANSITION ARMS: Done -> evidence only, Blocked -> blocker named, Paused -> resume condition, else fields + DoD + NEXT (+ TYPEFEATURES when tagged kit/lib/compiler). Markers per the GRANDFATHERING decision: marker lines OR phrase style (dod:/definition of done, next:/next step, typefeatures:/typefeatureset plan). check_word comes from reflection. Proof: vultr pack34 --check exit 0 with test bodies (3 tests); box notebook queued. NEXT: a link export (tuple of strings -> failing words), then `kit agile lint <root> [--team] [--id]` in its own linked CLI unit (the agile router is at 996 lines, so the route goes through a ceremonies-style unit), then census + ratchet.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_quality_lint_6 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-QUALITY-LINT", 6u32, "2026-11-04T10:01Z", "kit", "11-04 08:4x kit: state reader added, src/agile_quality_lint/state_rows.spi (now the package entry). It makes one pass over agile.spi into a dict of item_facts: Task rows are read from their fixed tail fields (status = last word before deps, tags = 6th part from the end), because 61 titles carry escaped quotes; Planning rows give Minutes/Load/Assigned; Note lines append per id. open_failures lists \"<ID> <status> <failing checks>\" for every non-Done item. Fixture test includes an escaped-quote title. vultr pack34 --check exit 0 with test bodies. CENSUS PREVIEW on the live backlog (a scratch Python mirror of the same rules, not kit output; ratchet baseline to confirm with the built verb): 637 open (Planned 449, Active 122, Blocked 35, Paused 31); 157 clean. Failing: DoneDefinition 364, TypeFeatures 257, NextStep 196, BlockerNamed 14, PauseResume 11, PlanningFields 5. NEXT: export open_failures + a census line through a linked CLI unit (kit agile lint <root> [--census]).")
inl note_of_kit_agile_quality_lint_7 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-QUALITY-LINT", 7u32, "2026-11-04T10:24Z", "kit", "11-04 09:0x kit: CLI unit BUILT (unstaged, not routed): new linked crate src/kit_agile_lint (lint_cli.spi), `kit agile lint <root> [--census]`. Report only, exit 0 (legacy ratchet). Typed boundary per kit AGENTS.md (no joined-string rows): agile_quality_lint exports kit_agile_lint_failures -> array_base failure_row {id, status, check} (one row per failing check) and kit_agile_lint_census -> array_base census_row {check, items}; the CLI formats lines and a summary (failing_items, failing_checks). Workspace members + lock refreshed. vultr --check exit 0 (with bodies); box notebooks state_rows 1/1 + lint_cli 1/1 pass. Node size run kit-size-lint queued. NEXT: route `lint` in the agile router together with the sprint wiring (kit_agile_mutation is at 996), then the strict-on-touch gate in add/set.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_quality_lint_8 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-QUALITY-LINT", 8u32, "2026-11-04T10:31Z", "kit", "11-04 09:2x kit: ROUTER HEADROOM for the lint + sprint routes (kit_agile_mutation was 996 lines with only the sprint route). The router's 30-arm `agile_writes` table compiled to about 90 generated lines. It is now a typed fact in the domain (agile_subcommand_domain: `state_access = ReadsState | WritesState`, `state_access_of` lists every subcommand explicitly, no wildcard, same 13 reads / 17 writes as before) served through NEW linked unit agile_state_access (export kit_agile_writes_named: name -> bool via the domain's own parser; test covers writers, readers, unknown). The router calls `agile_state_access.access.writes_named (argument_owned 0i32) || claim_queue_call subcommand`, so lock behaviour is unchanged. Workspace member + lock refreshed. vultr pack34 --check exit 0: domain, access (with bodies), router. NAMING TRAP hit: a domain function named like the new package (agile_state_access) shadowed the package inside the router (ETY UnionsCannotBeApplied), so it is renamed state_access_of.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_quality_lint_9 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-QUALITY-LINT", 9u32, "2026-11-04T12:43Z", "kit", "11-04 10:1x kit: STRICT-ON-TOUCH, phase 1 = WARN (unstaged). After a successful `kit agile set <root> <id> <p> <Status>`, kit_agile_routes prints `kit agile lint warning id=<ID> status=<Status> failing=<checks> (add the marker lines; a later train refuses the change)` on stderr; the exit code is unchanged. Typed link: agile_quality_state.item_failures (text, id, target status) -> array_base failure_row (that one item, under the TARGET status's arm, so Done asks for evidence, Blocked for a blocker, Paused for a resume condition). WHY WARN FIRST: the 11-04 census has ~480 of 637 open items failing; refusing in the same train would block every lane's closes at once. Phase 2 (refuse, exit 2 naming the checks) flips after lanes have seen warnings for a few days. `add` is not wired yet: agile_task_creation is at 992 generated lines, so it needs its own linked check. vultr pack34 --check exit 0 (routes, state). Also: agile_quality_lint is now split into rules (lint.spi, export kit_agile_lint_failing_rows) and the new state reader crate agile_quality_state, because the single crate measured 1284 lines.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_quality_lint_10 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-QUALITY-LINT", 10u32, "2026-11-04T14:56Z", "kit", "11-04 12:2x kit: SIZE ROUND 3. agile_quality_state measured 1230 after cadence rows and per-item failures. NEW linked crate agile_quality_facts owns the agile.spi parser + dict and exports fact rows (kit_agile_fact_rows); agile_quality_state now computes failures, census, per-item failures and cadence rows from those rows. Box notebooks: facts 1/1, state 1/1, lint_cli 1/1, cadence 1/1 pass; vultr --check exit 0. Pending: node size run.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_quality_lint_11 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-QUALITY-LINT", 11u32, "2026-11-04T15:05Z", "kit", "11-04 13:0x kit: `kit agile lint <root> --team <t>` added (unstaged): agile_quality_state.team_failures_of (text, team) filters fact rows on the `team:<t>` tag before the rules run (of_team; an empty team keeps every row), and the CLI passes --team through. Test: team filter. vultr --check exit 0 (with bodies).")
inl remote_exec_s1 () = Task ("REMOTE-EXEC-S1", "M2a typed job placement: kit_jobs job record gains a placement field", TaskKind, P0, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "kit,team:kit,remote,node,coordinator", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_remote_exec_s1 () = Planning ("REMOTE-EXEC-S1", Minutes 60u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_remote_exec_s1_1 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S1", 1u32, "2026-11-01T12:43Z", "kit", "11-01 08:4x kit, slice of EOIE-REMOTE-EXECUTOR (main 08:4x: slice it like SCRUM-XP; milestones M2-M5 from the 10-25 note, M1 shipped in TRAIN4c). DoD: a job record round-trips with placement Local and OnMachine node (unit test); existing job list/status output byte-identical for local jobs (regression test). NEXT STEP: add placement to kit_jobs' job model and its row codec. TYPEFEATURESET PLAN: placement = Local | OnMachine of machine (closed union), machine a nominal over the ssh alias, never an empty-string sentinel for local.")
inl note_of_remote_exec_s1_2 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S1", 2u32, "2026-11-01T14:46Z", "kit", "11-01 10:4x kit: KIT-ORPHAN-SIM depends on this slice: the job host on the node must emit proc_event rows (Spawned/Exited/Snapshot/Reaped/Unreachable) so orphan incidents become replayable. Add the row writer to this slice's DoD when it starts.")
inl note_of_remote_exec_s1_3 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S1", 3u32, "2026-11-01T20:16Z", "kit", "11-01 16:16 kit: typed half DONE (unstaged). New HostFree package src/kit_job_placement: machine = Machine of string (nominal, built only by machine_of, which refuses empty or spaced aliases), placement = Local | OnMachine machine (closed union, no empty-string sentinel), the text codec placement_text/placement_of_text (an absent field reads as Local, so old job records stay valid), shown_suffix (Local adds nothing, so local job list lines stay byte-identical; OnMachine adds \" placement=<alias>\"), is_remote. Test a_placement_round_trips_and_local_stays_invisible: Linux worker 1/1. NEXT (the wiring half, rides KIT-TYPED-CRATES' typed-export pattern): kit_jobs writes a placement file in the job dir only for OnMachine and reads it through placement_of_text, and job list/status append shown_suffix; regression test: local job list output byte-identical. KIT-ORPHAN-SIM's proc_event writer joins this wiring.")
inl note_of_remote_exec_s1_4 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S1", 4u32, "2026-11-01T23:59Z", "kit", "11-01 19:59 kit: WIRING DONE (unstaged, in TRAIN8). kit_jobs/jobs.spi reads an optional \"placement\" file in the job dir through placement_of_text (absent = Local) and job_summary appends shown_suffix, so local job lines are byte-identical and OnMachine jobs gain \" placement=<alias>\". Only kit_job_state calls job_summary (it exports the shared summary every other crate uses), so the change regenerates one crate (937 lines), not the 29 that inline kit_jobs. Regression evidence: kit_jobs/stop_cli.spi 15/15 on the node (job list/status lines unchanged). Remaining: the writer side (kit job start --on <machine> writes the file) comes with REMOTE-EXEC-S2; S1 closes when TRAIN8 installs and a local job list is byte-identical on the installed binary.")
inl note_of_remote_exec_s1_5 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S1", 5u32, "2026-11-02T02:56Z", "kit", "11-01 22:56 kit ACCEPTANCE on the installed TRAIN8 (kit.exe 7C559D83, 22:55): kit job list 5 from the previous binary and from the new one is byte-identical with elapsed_ms stripped, and 0 lines carry placement= (local jobs are unchanged). The typed placement model plus the reader and suffix are shipped. The writer (kit job start --on) belongs to REMOTE-EXEC-S2. DONE.")
inl remote_exec_s2 () = Task ("REMOTE-EXEC-S2", "M2b kit job start --on <machine>: dispatch to the remote kit over ssh and record the machine", TaskKind, P0, D3, 100u32, Done, "REMOTE-EXEC-S1", "kit,team:kit,remote,node,coordinator", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_remote_exec_s2 () = Planning ("REMOTE-EXEC-S2", Minutes 60u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_remote_exec_s2_1 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S2", 1u32, "2026-11-01T12:43Z", "kit", "11-01 08:4x kit, slice of EOIE-REMOTE-EXECUTOR (main 08:4x: slice it like SCRUM-XP; milestones M2-M5 from the 10-25 note, M1 shipped in TRAIN4c). DoD: 'kit job start --on node kit-x 60000 cmd /c echo hi' prints the remote job id and records placement OnMachine node; a dead link returns RemoteUnreachable within 10 s (CLI test with a fake ssh runner; live smoke against the node). NEXT STEP: reuse kit_remote_status's ssh path (process.try_capture_combined_list, BatchMode, ConnectTimeout) in kit_job_start_args. TYPEFEATURESET PLAN: dispatch_outcome = Dispatched of remote_job_id | RemoteUnreachable of reason | RemoteRefused of reason; remote_job_id a nominal distinct from local job ids, so the two cannot be mixed.")
inl note_of_remote_exec_s2_2 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S2", 2u32, "2026-11-02T23:47Z", "kit", "kit 11-02 19:5x BUILT (unstaged, ships in the next train): kit job start --on <ssh alias> [--bin <remote kit>] [options] <label> <timeout-ms> <program> [args...] forwards everything after the machine verbatim to the remote kit job start over ssh (BatchMode, ConnectTimeout=10, ServerAliveInterval=15). The routing in kit_jobs is one line (--on in the first option slot), so start_args' 15-tuple is untouched. New crate kit_job_dispatch (588 generated lines) types the result: dispatch_outcome = Dispatched {machine; id} | RemoteUnreachable reason | RemoteRefused reason, from lib process.captured_by's {ran; code; stdout}: ssh exit 255 or no output is unreachable, a remote receipt is dispatched, anything else is refused. That way a remote kit refusal is never mistaken for a dead link, which try_capture_combined_list could not tell apart. It prints kit job start ok id=<remote id> machine=<m> placement=remote (exit 0), ... unreachable (exit 4), ... refused (exit 2), and refuses quote characters in forwarded args (the remote shell would re-split them). PROOF on a vultr candidate: dispatch.spi 2/2; DoD dead link --on 10.255.255.1 gave unreachable, exit 4, in 10 s; --on with no machine exits 2; plain job start unchanged. REMAINING: the live smoke against the node once the train installs (kit job start --on laptop-ts kit-x 60000 cmd /c echo hi), and recording the placement in the local jobs home so kit job list shows remote jobs (S3 territory).")
inl note_of_remote_exec_s2_3 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S2", 3u32, "2026-11-03T02:27Z", "kit", "kit 11-02 22:3x BLOCKED on purpose: built and proven on vultr, waits for the TRAIN14 install (then the live smoke). Blocked keeps the CCO queue from handing it to other lanes; main hit a claim loop on REMOTE-EXEC-S2. Resume as Active when TRAIN14 installs.")
inl note_of_remote_exec_s2_4 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S2", 4u32, "2026-11-03T10:09Z", "kit", "11-03 06:1x kit: TRAIN14c INSTALLED (kit.exe 5B9082C1; 58/58 notebooks; crate gate 284/284 under 1000). Unblocked: the train it waited on has installed. Resume from the last kit note.")
inl note_of_remote_exec_s2_5 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S2", 5u32, "2026-11-03T10:17Z", "kit", "11-03 06:2x kit DONE. Live smoke on TRAIN14c (box kit 5B9082C1 -> node kit 5B9082C1, swapped the versioned way): kit job start --on laptop --bin C:/home/bin/kit.exe s2-smoke 60000 cmd /c hostname gave \"kit job start ok id=job-1793700948422-s2-smoke machine=laptop placement=remote\" (exit 0); from the box, wait 0, tail showed DESKTOP-6E8C14G (the node). A transient ssh banner timeout came back typed as \"unreachable machine=laptop\" (not refused), and a retry dispatched. The quote-character refusal fired as designed on an argument with quotes.")
inl remote_exec_s3 () = Task ("REMOTE-EXEC-S3", "M2c job status/tail/stop/wait route by placement", TaskKind, P0, D3, 100u32, Done, "REMOTE-EXEC-S2", "kit,team:kit,remote,node,coordinator", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_remote_exec_s3 () = Planning ("REMOTE-EXEC-S3", Minutes 60u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_remote_exec_s3_1 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S3", 1u32, "2026-11-01T12:43Z", "kit", "11-01 08:4x kit, slice of EOIE-REMOTE-EXECUTOR (main 08:4x: slice it like SCRUM-XP; milestones M2-M5 from the 10-25 note, M1 shipped in TRAIN4c). DoD: each of the four verbs on a remote job answers from the node (live smoke) and a local job is unchanged (regression); wait on a remote job exits 3 while running and 0 after settling, like a local one (CLI test with a fake runner). NEXT STEP: one router in kit_job_control that matches placement, used by all four verbs. TYPEFEATURESET PLAN: route = RouteLocal of job_id | RouteRemote of machine * remote_job_id, a total match per verb; no string check on the id prefix.")
inl note_of_remote_exec_s3_2 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S3", 2u32, "2026-11-03T00:16Z", "kit", "kit 11-02 20:2x CORE BUILT with S2's placement recording (unstaged, next train). S2: a Dispatched receipt now writes <jobs_root>/<remote id>/{placement=on <machine>, label, started_ms, remote-bin}. S3: kit_jobs routes status, wait, tail and stop through placed. A job whose placement file says on <m> has the verb forwarded over ssh to <m>'s kit (the remote-bin recorded at dispatch, default kit) with the same arguments; it prints the remote output and keeps the remote exit code, with ssh failure meaning exit 4 and kit job unreachable machine=<m>. Local jobs are unchanged. Recording and routing ship together on purpose: a recorded remote job without routing would make a local job wait hang forever. PROOF on vultr: dispatch.spi 3/3; a hand-made record with placement on 10.255.255.1 made job status <id> forward and return exit 4 in 10 s; a local start+wait was unchanged. Generated: dispatch.rs 876, jobs.rs 880 (cap 1000). REMAINING: live node smoke after install (kit job start --on laptop-ts --bin C:/home/bin/kit.exe x 60000 cmd /c echo hi, then kit job wait <id> from the box), and job list showing remote state.")
inl note_of_remote_exec_s3_3 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S3", 3u32, "2026-11-03T02:27Z", "kit", "kit 11-02 22:3x BLOCKED on purpose: built and proven on vultr, waits for the TRAIN14 install (then the live smoke). Blocked keeps the CCO queue from handing it to other lanes; main hit a claim loop on REMOTE-EXEC-S2. Resume as Active when TRAIN14 installs.")
inl note_of_remote_exec_s3_4 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S3", 4u32, "2026-11-03T10:09Z", "kit", "11-03 06:1x kit: TRAIN14c INSTALLED (kit.exe 5B9082C1; 58/58 notebooks; crate gate 284/284 under 1000). Unblocked: the train it waited on has installed. Resume from the last kit note.")
inl note_of_remote_exec_s3_5 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S3", 5u32, "2026-11-03T10:18Z", "kit", "11-03 06:2x kit DONE (DoD live on TRAIN14c). Remote job job-1793701022141-s3-long (ping, on laptop): from the box, wait 2000 while running returned exit 3; stop answered from the node \"state=stopped exit=143 ... process tree stopped\"; wait after the stop returned the job's exit (143); status and tail answered from the node (s2-smoke: wait 0 after settling). Local regression: a local job s3-local started and wait returned 0, with no placement=remote. All four verbs route through placed.")
inl remote_exec_s4 () = Task ("REMOTE-EXEC-S4", "M2d retire i574n.github remote.ps1 node job callers in favour of kit job --on", TaskKind, P0, D3, 60u32, Active, "REMOTE-EXEC-S3", "kit,team:kit,remote,node,coordinator", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_remote_exec_s4 () = Planning ("REMOTE-EXEC-S4", Minutes 45u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_remote_exec_s4_1 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S4", 1u32, "2026-11-01T12:43Z", "kit", "11-01 08:4x kit, slice of EOIE-REMOTE-EXECUTOR (main 08:4x: slice it like SCRUM-XP; milestones M2-M5 from the 10-25 note, M1 shipped in TRAIN4c). DoD: zero calls to 'remote.ps1 node job' in kit and in the lanes' memories (stem grep, memory dangling-check-by-stem); remote.ps1's job verbs become a one-line shim printing the kit spelling for one release. NEXT STEP: grep callers, migrate memories and scripts, shim. TYPEFEATURESET PLAN: n/a beyond S1-S3 (glue removal); the shim emits the typed route's spelling, not a hand-copied string.")
inl note_of_remote_exec_s4_2 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S4", 2u32, "2026-11-03T16:14Z", "kit", "11-03 12:4x kit: stem grep done. kit/scripts, kit/src and i574n.github/scripts: 0 callers of 'remote.ps1 <machine> job'. Memories: 7 files (waiter-stop-leaves-job, status-files-need-age, keep-node-busy, node-lib-land-mirror-needs, remote-kit-worker-node, job-timeout-counts-queue-wait, node-launch-sync-minutes). Most use 'remote.ps1 node job list', and kit has NO 'job list --on' yet: JobList in kit_jobs/jobs.spi isn't placed, while start/status/wait/tail/stop already route. ORDER: (1) add JobList --on <alias> through kit_job_dispatch's forwarder, in the x shape (typed placement, no argument-index reads) and with a dispatch test; (2) rewrite the 7 memories: 'kit job start --on laptop ...' (the node's ssh alias is laptop), 'kit job wait|status|tail|stop <id>' (placement routes them), 'kit job list --on laptop'; (3) make remote.ps1's job verbs a one-line shim printing the kit spelling. Starts after the vultr proof of the current kit_jobs WIP (process liveness), so two changes don't stack in one package mid-proof.")
inl note_of_remote_exec_s4_3 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S4", 3u32, "2026-11-03T17:30Z", "kit", "11-03 14:1x kit: step (1) in the tree (unproven): kit_job_dispatch.list_on_run (export kit_job_list_on) forwards 'kit job list --on <alias> [--bin <path>] [count]' over the same ssh options, now one ssh_to helper shared by start/forward/list. kit_jobs JobList routes '--on' to it; plain list stays local. Step (2) partly done: 3 memory instructions (node-launch-sync-minutes, node-lib-land-mirror-needs, remote-kit-worker-node) now say 'kit job start --on laptop' + 'kit job wait <id>'. The 4 'remote.ps1 node job list' sites switch to 'kit job list --on laptop' only after TRAIN16 installs it, so no memory names a verb that doesn't exist yet. Step (3), the remote.ps1 shim, follows the install.")
inl note_of_remote_exec_s4_4 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S4", 4u32, "2026-11-04T07:59Z", "kit", "11-04 04:3x kit PROBE: `kit job start --on laptop-ts --bin C:\\home\\bin\\kit.exe <label> <ms> <cmd...>` works end to end with the installed kit (job-1793779134703-kit-on-probe: dispatched, then plain `kit job wait`/`tail` on the box routed by the recorded placement and returned the node output, exit 0). Two gaps stop it replacing remote.ps1 node job callers (launch-train's node half, precheck runs): (1) the default binary is bare `kit`, which is not on the node's PATH. Fix: a per-machine kit path in remote-machines.json read by dispatch (as remote.ps1 reads target.eoie), so --bin becomes optional. (2) remote.ps1 sets KIT_JOB_SLOTS from target.slots before `job start`; --on forwards no environment, so the node host would use its default slot count for a heavy train. Fix: dispatch prefixes the same env from the machine row. Then switch launch-train's three `$remote $Node job` calls to `kit job --on` (the launcher already holds the ssh host).")
inl note_of_remote_exec_s4_5 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S4", 5u32, "2026-11-04T09:48Z", "kit", "11-04 07:4x kit: both probe gaps closed in the tree (unstaged). (1) Binary: kit_job_dispatch remembers --bin per alias in <jobs root>/machines/<alias>.bin; a later --on without --bin uses it (binary_of: given beats remembered, tests). list --on uses the same memory (list_on_run now takes the jobs root; entry passes it). (2) Slots: no forwarding needed. The remote host reads its own <jobs root>/slots (MACHINE-SLOT-CAPS), node = 6 is written. Proof: vultr pack34 --check exit 0 for dispatch (with test bodies) and entry; box dispatch notebook queued. NEXT after the train installs it: switch launch-train's three `$remote $Node job` calls to `kit job start --on <ssh host> --bin C:\\home\\bin\\kit.exe` once, then plain `kit job wait/tail <id>`.")
inl note_of_remote_exec_s4_6 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S4", 6u32, "2026-11-04T09:50Z", "kit", "11-04 08:0x kit: launcher switch DRAFTED, deliberately NOT applied: %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\kit\\REMOTE-EXEC-S4-launch-train\\launch-train.ps1 (base sha 1860E666E19401C6, a 4-line diff; parses). The node half starts with `kit job start --on $SshHost --bin C:\\home\\bin\\kit.exe`, then plain `kit job wait <id> 21600000` / `kit job tail <id> 40` (routed by the recorded placement). WHY PARKED: remote.ps1 prefixes KIT_JOB_SLOTS=6 on every node job start, and the node's shared job host takes its cap from whichever job starts it. A --on start forwards no env, and the installed node kit does not read <jobs root>/slots yet, so a train started this way could leave the node host uncapped for hours (MACHINE-SLOT-CAPS over-admission). APPLY when the node's C:\\home\\bin\\kit.exe has the slots-file host (after the train that ships kit_job_host + kit_job_dispatch). Proof: `kit job list --on laptop-ts` with no --bin works, and a 7th node job queues. Box dispatch notebook: see the job.")
inl note_of_remote_exec_s4_7 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S4", 7u32, "2026-11-04T15:29Z", "kit", "11-04 13:4x kit: launcher draft REBASED on today's launch-train.ps1 (base sha 83ADC4009B3F0F09, which raises the node half to 10 h and the wrapper to 12 h): same 4-line change (node half via `kit job start --on $SshHost --bin C:\\home\\bin\\kit.exe`, then plain `kit job wait <id> 43200000` and `kit job tail <id> 40`), CRLF kept (178), parses. Still parked until the node's kit has the slots-file host (TRAIN22).")
inl note_of_remote_exec_s4_8 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S4", 8u32, "2026-11-04T19:28Z", "kit", "11-04 21:2x kit: TRAIN22 RED at the crate gate, partly on kit_job_dispatch dispatch.rs 1027 (the remembered --bin, binary_of + machine_binary inline). FIXED (unstaged): NEW linked crate kit_job_bin (export kit_job_machine_binary machine_binary ((jobs_root, machine, given))); dispatch keeps a one-line wrapper. Node size: kit_job_dispatch 968, kit_job_bin 170. The launcher draft stays parked until a train with this installs.")
inl note_of_remote_exec_s4_9 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S4", 9u32, "2026-11-04T22:49Z", "kit", "11-04 18:5x kit: launcher draft REBASED again, onto today's free-space-floor launch-train.ps1 (base sha 316F243DFCCE2529; the old draft kept as launch-train.ps1.base-83ADC400.bak beside it). Same 4-line change: $nodeKit, node half via `kit job start --on $SshHost --bin $nodeKit`, then `kit job wait <id> 43200000` and `kit job tail <id> 40`. CRLF kept (183), parses, diff against live = exactly those 4 hunks, $kit defined at line 20. Still parked until TRAIN25 (slots-file host + kit_job_bin) is installed on the node; then apply and prove with a 7th node job queuing.")
inl note_of_remote_exec_s4_10 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S4", 10u32, "2026-11-05T07:38Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-05 03:3x kit: S4 launch-train draft REBASED onto live BA23C6A309D51580 (live gained LOCAL-PRE-PROOF-GATE's precheck block). The same 7-line block was inserted at the same anchor; the draft now differs from live by exactly S4's own hunks ($nodeKit, kit job start --on/--bin, kit job wait, kit job tail). Rebased draft 14A24741185F01B4; previous kept as launch-train.ps1.base316f in the draft dir. Still applies only after an install that carries --on/--bin (TRAIN28).")
inl note_of_remote_exec_s4_11 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S4", 11u32, "2026-11-05T23:52Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-05 19:5x kit: live launch-train.ps1 changed again (KIT-TRAIN-WATCHER-RETRY wait loop, live hash 81841578748EB2B8), so the S4 draft (base BA23C6A3) needs one more rebase before it applies: its job wait call becomes the same retry loop.")
inl note_of_remote_exec_s4_12 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S4", 12u32, "2026-11-05T23:54Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-05 20:0x kit: S4 draft REBASED onto live 81841578 (with the KIT-TRAIN-WATCHER-RETRY loop): the same 4 S4 hunks (nodeKit, kit job start --on/--bin, kit job wait <id> 43200000 now inside the retry loop, kit job tail). Rebased draft D1F1F2A3, parse 0; previous kept as launch-train.ps1.baseBA23. Still applies only after an install that carries --on/--bin.")
inl remote_exec_s5 () = Task ("REMOTE-EXEC-S5", "M4 remote jobs refuse on a source-tree mismatch, and live-tree writers stay local", TaskKind, P0, D3, 30u32, Active, "KIT-REMOTE-SYNC", "kit,team:kit,remote,node,coordinator", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_remote_exec_s5 () = Planning ("REMOTE-EXEC-S5", Minutes 60u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_remote_exec_s5_1 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S5", 1u32, "2026-11-01T12:43Z", "kit", "11-01 08:4x kit, slice of EOIE-REMOTE-EXECUTOR (main 08:4x: slice it like SCRUM-XP; milestones M2-M5 from the 10-25 note, M1 shipped in TRAIN4c). DoD: a remote job whose src slice manifest differs from the box's is refused before it starts (test with two fixture trees); a job declared as writing the live tree is never placed remote even with equal hashes (test). NEXT STEP: compare manifests from KIT-REMOTE-SYNC's deterministic bundle (eoie_bundle_manifest) inside the S2 dispatch. TYPEFEATURESET PLAN: manifest as a set of (path nominal, sha256 nominal) compared as sets; write_scope = ReadsTreeOnly | WritesLiveTree (closed union declared by the job), so the live-tree rule is a pattern.")
inl note_of_remote_exec_s5_2 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S5", 2u32, "2026-11-04T12:29Z", "kit", "11-04 09:5x kit: the DECISION is modelled (unstaged) in kit_coordinator_placement (S7's package). tree_need gains WritesLiveTree (placed only on the local machine, even when a remote one holds a tree) and ReadsSlice digest (local, or a remote whose machine_sample.slice equals the digest; machine_sample gains `slice : string`, \"\" from sample_of). New test a_remote_slice_must_match_and_live_tree_writers_stay_local: same slice -> the idle node; a stale slice is never used (local instead, or QueueUntilFree when local is full); writers stay local. vultr check queued. REMAINING for the DoD: the digest itself (the box's src slice manifest via kit_mirror's walker + the remote's, answered by `kit mirror check` on the remote after TRAIN22) and the refusal line in kit_job_dispatch before ssh.")
inl remote_exec_s6 () = Task ("REMOTE-EXEC-S6", "M3a job and slot typestate model shared by Rust and Gleam", TaskKind, P0, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "kit,team:kit,remote,node,coordinator", "current", "current")
