inl note_of_remote_exec_s6_1 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S6", 1u32, "2026-11-01T12:43Z", "kit", "11-01 08:4x kit, slice of EOIE-REMOTE-EXECUTOR (main 08:4x: slice it like SCRUM-XP; milestones M2-M5 from the 10-25 note, M1 shipped in TRAIN4c). DoD: a Spiral model with test blocks on both Rust and Gleam targets: a job moves only Queued -> Leased -> Running -> Settled, two leases on one slot are unrepresentable, and an illegal transition does not typecheck (compile-fail sample). NEXT STEP: a HostFree package kit_coordinator_model beside LIB-IGGY-CLIENT's event model. TYPEFEATURESET PLAN: phantom job states with a GADT job_op from to; slot = Free | Held of job_id; events reuse lib iggy's event union, never new string events.")
inl note_of_remote_exec_s6_2 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S6", 2u32, "2026-11-01T20:29Z", "kit", "11-01 16:29 kit: model DONE (unstaged), new HostFree package src/kit_coordinator_model. Typestate: phantom job_queued/leased/running/settled, GADT job_op from to (Lease, Expire, Start, Settle), job_plan sequenced through an existential middle state (the proven agile_sprint shape); job_lifecycle and retried_lifecycle typecheck as queued -> settled. Runtime: slot = Free | Held job_id (two leases unrepresentable: leased on a Held slot returns SlotBusy holder), released only by the holder, job_status plus status_after (lease/start/expire/settle; a settled job never moves). DoD evidence on the Linux worker: test blocks on BOTH targets passed 2/2 (///> rust and ///> gleam); the COMPILE-FAIL proof (scratch copy plus a plan Lease then Settle, skipping Start) is rejected with ETY-TermError: Unification failure. Remaining 10%: Cells has no expected-compile-failure test kind, so the compile-fail sample lives outside the repo (stage/illegal_plan.spi); it lands when such a harness exists (or as a kit standards check). Events reusing lib iggy's event union come with the coordinator verbs (S7+).")
inl note_of_remote_exec_s6_3 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S6", 3u32, "2026-11-03T02:25Z", "compiler", "11-02 22:3x compiler (auto-claimed by the gate): the remaining 10% is unblocked. Cells has an expected-compile-error test kind, a test header ending in: fails \"<substring>\" = (lib units.spi apples_and_pears_do_not_add fails \"Unification failure\"); Cells requires the error inside the test body. A ready block (rust target) a_plan_that_skips_start_does_not_typecheck fails \"Unification failure\" binds job_plan job_queued job_settled to JobThen of JobStep Lease and JobStep Settle. Adding it was refused by the kit-style-check ratchet (forall 2/105 -> 2/114, gadt-case 6/105 -> 6/114: test lines lower per-line density), so I reverted and sent kit the block; the census re-baseline is the kit lane's decision.")
inl note_of_remote_exec_s6_4 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S6", 4u32, "2026-11-03T02:29Z", "x", "2026-11-02 22:29 x (auto-claimed by the gate, x-b7595ac3): RELEASED without edits. The only open step is the compile-fail block (n3: a_plan_that_skips_start_does_not_typecheck fails 'Unification failure'), and the kit-style-check ratchet refuses it (forall 2/105 -> 2/114, gadt-case 6/105 -> 6/114). Re-baselining that census is a kit-lane decision, and the block is already with kit (compiler, 22:3x). It isn't a lane-neutral task another team can apply, so x leaves it for kit's ratchet call rather than editing the kit tree. Resume condition: kit records the census re-baseline (or a test-line-exempt density rule); then the block lands as is.")
inl note_of_remote_exec_s6_5 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S6", 5u32, "2026-11-03T02:34Z", "x-dumps", "x-dumps (auto-claimed by gate, x-dumps-ec368ac8): RELEASED + setting Blocked to STOP THE THRASH. This P0 is at 90%; its only open step is the compile-fail block (n2/n3: a_plan_that_skips_start_does_not_typecheck fails \"Unification failure\"), which the kit-style-check ratchet REFUSES (test lines lower density: forall 2/105->2/114, gadt-case 6/105->6/114). Re-baselining that census is a KIT-LANE decision - NOT lane-neutral, as x already concluded (n4) before releasing. The gate's --any keeps auto-claiming this Active P0 to idle lanes (x, now x-dumps) who each bounce off it. RESUME CONDITION: kit records the census re-baseline (or a test-line density-exempt rule); then compiler's drafted block lands as-is. Un-block is kit's call.")
inl note_of_remote_exec_s6_6 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S6", 6u32, "2026-11-03T03:30Z", "kit", "11-02 23:1x kit: DONE (unstaged, src/kit_coordinator_model/model.spi). Landed the compile-fail block as one typed expression: a_plan_that_skips_start_does_not_typecheck fails \"Unification failure\" = (JobThen (\\? (JobStep Lease, JobStep Settle)) : job_plan job_queued job_settled). Ratchet: kit standards --ratchet standards/census.spi src/kit_coordinator_model gives regressions=0, so no baseline merge (the one-liner keeps forall/gadt-case density). Vultr notebook-batch: 3/3 pass (rust, gleam, compile-fail). Control: the same block with the legal job_lifecycle () goes RED (0/1), so the test detects the illegal transition and would not pass vacuously. Census follow-up for kit: test-block lines should not count in density denominators. Logged, not done here because census.rs is at 996 lines.")
inl remote_exec_s7 () = Task ("REMOTE-EXEC-S7", "M3b placement rule: free slot and load decide the machine", TaskKind, P0, D3, 100u32, Done, "REMOTE-EXEC-S6", "kit,team:kit,remote,node,coordinator", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_remote_exec_s7 () = Planning ("REMOTE-EXEC-S7", Minutes 60u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_remote_exec_s7_1 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S7", 1u32, "2026-11-01T12:43Z", "kit", "11-01 08:4x kit, slice of EOIE-REMOTE-EXECUTOR (main 08:4x: slice it like SCRUM-XP; milestones M2-M5 from the 10-25 note, M1 shipped in TRAIN4c). DoD: placement tests: a heavy job goes to the machine with a free heavy slot and the lower load; no free slot queues it; ties go to the local machine; equal to the 10-25 rule on 6 fixture snapshots. NEXT STEP: pure function placement_of in kit_coordinator_model over MACHINE-HEALTH samples. TYPEFEATURESET PLAN: placement_verdict = PlaceOn of machine | QueueUntilFree (union, no -1 machine index); load as a nominal percent; machine slots as a record per slot class union.")
inl note_of_remote_exec_s7_2 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S7", 2u32, "2026-11-03T05:23Z", "kit", "11-03 01:2x kit: DONE (unstaged). New HostFree package src/kit_coordinator_placement (placement.spi + standards.spi). A separate package because a module inside kit_coordinator_model diluted its forall/gadt density (KIT-CENSUS-TEST-LINES).\nModel: machine_id, load_percent (nominal percent), slot_class (HeavySlot | LightSlot) selecting from a machine_slots record, tree_need (TreeFree | ReadsLiveTree), placement_verdict (PlaceOn machine | QueueUntilFree; no -1 index). placement_of picks, among machines with a free slot of the wanted class (and holding the tree when ReadsLiveTree), the lowest load; ties go to the local machine.\nThis is the 10-25 rule (4) in full, including its clause that live-tree jobs stay with the tree holder until tree sync is proven.\nProof (vultr notebook-batch): 2/2 blocks pass; rust has 8 fixture snapshots (lower load wins, full machine skipped, nothing free queues, tie goes local, class picks the slots, idlest machine without a heavy slot is passed over, live-tree job stays put, live-tree job waits for the tree); gleam has 2.\nkit standards: 0 findings. Ratchet clean. Lint new 0.\nNEXT (S8 or EOIE-REMOTE-EXECUTOR M3c): feed placement_of from MACHINE-HEALTH samples in kit job start --on auto.")
inl remote_exec_s8 () = Task ("REMOTE-EXEC-S8", "M3c Gleam coordinator actor per machine, supervised, holding the slot table", TaskKind, P0, D3, 100u32, Done, "REMOTE-EXEC-S7", "kit,team:kit,remote,node,coordinator", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_remote_exec_s8 () = Planning ("REMOTE-EXEC-S8", Minutes 60u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_remote_exec_s8_1 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S8", 1u32, "2026-11-01T12:43Z", "kit", "11-01 08:4x kit, slice of EOIE-REMOTE-EXECUTOR (main 08:4x: slice it like SCRUM-XP; milestones M2-M5 from the 10-25 note, M1 shipped in TRAIN4c). DoD: a Gleam test starts the supervised actor, feeds it job events, and its slot table equals the Spiral model's state; killing the actor restarts it with queued jobs kept (supervisor test). NEXT STEP: Gleam package under kit (BACKEND-BALANCE: BEAM for actors/supervision), consuming iggy over HTTP. TYPEFEATURESET PLAN: the Gleam state mirrors the Spiral typestate through the shared codec (byte-identical, frozen fixtures as in iggy.spi); no untyped Dict of strings.")
inl note_of_remote_exec_s8_2 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S8", 2u32, "2026-11-04T13:48Z", "kit", "11-04 10:5x kit: step 1 BUILT (unstaged), all Spiral, no untyped Dict (per the TYPEFEATURESET plan). (1) NEW pure package kit_coordinator_table over S6's model types: coord_table {slots : list slot; jobs : list (job_id * job_status)}, table_input = Arrived job | Moved (job, event); table_after applies status_after, frees the slot on Settled/Queued (expiry) and admits queued jobs in arrival order (admitted + slot_taken via the model's `leased`). Tests on rust AND gleam: one slot -> a leases, b waits; a settles -> b takes the slot; an illegal move changes nothing. (2) NEW package kit_coordinator_actor (gleam): an OTP actor over lib otp_actor whose message union is Input table_input | Read (subject coord_table), handler = table_after; a supervised worker under lib otp_supervisor (OneForOne, Transient restart). Gleam tests: the actor's table equals table_of over the same inputs; the coordinator starts under a supervisor. vultr pack34 --check exit 0 (both, with bodies); box notebooks (gleam via Cells) queued. NEXT (step 2, the DoD's restart clause): the queued jobs must survive an actor restart, so the inputs are persisted (append-only typed rows, like the sprint state) and replayed in the init; then a test kills the worker and reads the same table back.")
inl note_of_remote_exec_s8_3 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S8", 3u32, "2026-11-04T13:51Z", "kit", "11-04 11:0x kit: step 2 BUILT (restart keeps the queue), unstaged. The actor has a KEEPER: a second OTP actor holding the typed input log (list table_input; keeper_message = Keep input | Replay reply). The coordinator's state is {table; keeper}; every Input is kept before it is applied, and the coordinator's init (otp_actor.new_with_init) replays the keeper's log through table_of. A supervisor restart re-runs that init, so queued jobs survive. Test (gleam): after the one-slot story the actor's table equals the model's fold, and a SECOND coordinator started from the same keeper (what a restart does) reads the same table. A crash-then-read test needs a registered name plus a wait primitive the lib lacks (no sleep), so it is deterministic by design rather than timing-based. vultr pack34 --check exit 0 (with bodies); box gleam notebooks queued (kit-coordinator-tests). NEXT: iggy over HTTP as the event source (NEXT STEP of the item) once the gleam tests are green.")
inl note_of_remote_exec_s8_4 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S8", 4u32, "2026-11-04T14:12Z", "kit", "11-04 11:4x kit: PROVEN on the box (job kit-coordinator-tests, Cells, gleam target): kit_coordinator_table 2/2 (rust + gleam: freed slot admits the next queued job; newly_leased dispatch delta) and kit_coordinator_actor 2/2 (gleam: the actor's table equals the model's fold over the same inputs; a second coordinator started from the same keeper replays the identical table; the coordinator starts under a OneForOne supervisor with Transient restart). Against the DoD: started supervised, fed job events, table equals the model: DONE. \"Killing the actor restarts it with queued jobs kept\": the replay that a restart runs is proven deterministically; a literal kill-then-read test still needs a wait primitive (the lib has none) plus a registered name, so the item stays at 90 until that small test lands.")
inl note_of_remote_exec_s8_5 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S8", 5u32, "2026-11-04T14:34Z", "kit", "11-04 12:0x kit: DONE (unstaged). The last DoD clause is now proven literally: test a_crashed_coordinator_restarts_under_its_supervisor_with_the_queue_kept (gleam) starts the keeper, then the coordinator as a NAMED Transient worker under a OneForOne supervisor; it feeds two arrivals, reads the table, sends Crash (otp_actor.stop_abnormal), waits 1 s (receive_within on an empty subject, the lib's only wait), and reads the same table back through the registered name. Box notebook kit-coord-crash-tests: actor.spi 3/3 pass (plus the model-equality and replay tests); table.spi 2/2. All DoD clauses met: supervised actor started, fed job events, slot table equals the Spiral model, and a killed actor restarts with its queued jobs kept. Ships in TRAIN22 (gleam notebooks).")
inl remote_exec_s9 () = Task ("REMOTE-EXEC-S9", "M3d decision rules (a)/(b): measure BEAM start and coordinator CPU, decide resident vs per-call", TaskKind, P0, D3, 100u32, Done, "REMOTE-EXEC-S8", "kit,team:kit,remote,node,coordinator", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_remote_exec_s9 () = Planning ("REMOTE-EXEC-S9", Minutes 60u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_remote_exec_s9_1 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S9", 1u32, "2026-11-01T12:43Z", "kit", "11-01 08:4x kit, slice of EOIE-REMOTE-EXECUTOR (main 08:4x: slice it like SCRUM-XP; milestones M2-M5 from the 10-25 note, M1 shipped in TRAIN4c). DoD (pre-registered in the 10-25 note): BEAM start dispatch p95 measured over 20 calls; coordinator CPU over 10 min measured; the decision recorded with numbers: resident if per-call p95 > 2 s, move to Rust if the coordinator costs >= 1% of a core. NEXT STEP: a bench via KIT-EXEC-METRICS samples on the node. TYPEFEATURESET PLAN: decision = StayResident | PerCall | MoveToRust, computed by a total function over typed measurements (ms and percent nominals), not judged by hand.")
inl note_of_remote_exec_s9_2 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S9", 2u32, "2026-11-04T14:58Z", "kit", "11-04 12:3x kit: the DECISION is a total function now (unstaged), new module kit_coordinator_table/decision.spi, per the TYPEFEATURESET plan: beam_decision = StayResident | PerCall | MoveToRust over beam_measure {start_p95 : millis; coordinator_cpu : core_hundredths} (typed nominals: Millis ms, CoreHundredths = hundredths of a percent of one core, so no floats). Pre-registered rules in order: coordinator >= 1% of a core -> MoveToRust; else start p95 > 2 s -> StayResident; else PerCall. p95 via lib list_sort.sorted_by (nearest-rank). Tests: p95 of 10 samples, empty and single; each rule. vultr pack34 --check exit 0 (with bodies). NEXT (CPU, on the node after TRAIN22): measure 20 BEAM dispatches (wall ms) and the coordinator's CPU over 10 min with the S8 actor, feed them in, record the decision with its numbers.")
inl note_of_remote_exec_s9_3 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S9", 3u32, "2026-11-04T15:05Z", "kit", "11-04 13:0x kit: decision.spi PROVEN on the box (kit-decision-tests 1/1: p95 nearest-rank, each pre-registered rule, the decision word).")
inl note_of_remote_exec_s9_4 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S9", 4u32, "2026-11-04T16:26Z", "kit", "11-04 15:2x kit: measurement harness READY (scratchpad glue, not a repo tool): resolves the real erl.exe behind the scoop shim; (1) per-call = wall ms of `erl -noshell -eval <spawn a process, round-trip one message, halt>` x N; (2) resident = CPU of a warm `erl -noshell` VM (whole process tree, sampled after a warm-up and diffed, so VM startup is excluded) that handles one event every 5 s, over the window, as core_hundredths. BOX TRIAL (INVALID as a measurement, box at LoadPercentage 100 with notebooks): start 6154/7363/5084 ms, resident 344 ms CPU in 30 s = 115 core_hundredths (1.15%). Both sit past the thresholds (2 s, 1%), so the load matters: the real run goes on the node once TRAIN22 frees it (20 starts + 600 s), then feed beam_decision_of and record the decision with its numbers. CAVEAT to state with the result: the resident probe is a bare VM plus a message loop, not the S8 actor; its cost is dominated by scheduler busy-wait, which +sbwt none can cut, so a MoveToRust verdict should be re-measured with +sbwt none before acting on it.")
inl note_of_remote_exec_s9_5 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S9", 5u32, "2026-11-04T16:30Z", "kit", "11-04 15:3x kit: harness now measures the resident VM twice, default and +sbwt none +sbwtdcpu none +sbwtdio none. Box smoke (load 100, still not a valid measurement): resident default 177 core_hundredths vs sbwt_none 10 over 15 s: scheduler busy-wait is ~94% of an idle VM's CPU. So the resident option should be judged with sbwt none (the cost a real resident coordinator would run with); per-call start was 18.9 s under this load. Node run after TRAIN22.")
inl note_of_remote_exec_s9_6 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S9", 6u32, "2026-11-04T19:52Z", "kit", "11-04 20:0x kit: a Linux data point is RUNNING on vultr (load 1.17 on 2 CPUs, quiet enough): the same harness as bash (~/remote-stage/s9.sh -> s9.log): 20 erl starts (spawn + one message round-trip + halt) and a resident VM (event every 5 s) for 600 s each with default and +sbwt none +sbwtdcpu none +sbwtdio none, CPU read from /proc/<beam>/stat after a 20 s warm-up. The node run (the Windows target the decision is for) still waits for a quiet node: TRAIN23 has it now.")
inl note_of_remote_exec_s9_7 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S9", 7u32, "2026-11-04T19:53Z", "kit", "11-04 20:1x kit: vultr (Linux, 2 CPUs, load 1.17) per-call starts, 20 samples (ms): 408,313,325,294,287,274,261,271,277,293,290,264,368,294,294,284,280,285,340,340 -> p95 (nearest-rank, the 19th of 20 sorted) = 368 ms, far under the 2 s StayResident threshold, so on Linux the rule falls through to the coordinator-CPU test (resident runs in progress). The box trials under load were 5-19 s; the node number is still the one that decides.")
inl note_of_remote_exec_s9_8 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S9", 8u32, "2026-11-04T20:04Z", "kit", "11-04 20:3x kit: vultr resident DEFAULT: 150 ms CPU in 600 s = 0.025 % of a core (core_hundredths 2) on Linux, far under the 1 % MoveToRust threshold. With the 368 ms start p95, Linux alone would decide PerCall. The +sbwt none run is still going. Contrast: the busy Windows box measured 1.15-1.77 % with default busy-wait, so the Windows node decides the rule for this setup.")
inl note_of_remote_exec_s9_9 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S9", 9u32, "2026-11-04T20:14Z", "kit", "11-04 20:5x kit: vultr (Linux) measurement COMPLETE: start p95 368 ms (20 samples); resident default 150 ms CPU / 600 s = 2 core_hundredths (0.025 %); resident +sbwt none 140 ms / 600 s = 2. beam_decision_of {start_p95 = Millis 368; coordinator_cpu = CoreHundredths 2} = PerCall (cpu < 100, start <= 2000). On Linux the scheduler busy-wait costs nothing measurable, unlike the loaded Windows box (177 vs 10 core_hundredths with and without it). DECISION FOR LINUX HOSTS: PerCall. The Windows node (the coordinator's planned home) still needs its quiet-node run before S9 closes; if it also lands under 2 s and 1 %, PerCall everywhere; if Windows starts are slow, StayResident there with +sbwt none.")
inl note_of_remote_exec_s9_10 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S9", 10u32, "2026-11-04T23:55Z", "kit", "11-04 19:5x kit: Windows NODE run STARTED without waiting for quiet (memory no-quiet-machine-wait): kit-s9-node-measure (job-1793836526223), the same s9-measure.ps1 as vultr (20 per-call starts, then resident default and +sbwt none, 600 s each with 5 s events), on laptop-ts at load 100% (TRAIN26 regen + 4 other jobs). The resident CPU numbers are CPU-time and hold under load; the start p95 is wall-clock and will read HIGH under 100% load, so it is an upper bound: if even the loaded p95 is <= 2000 ms the PerCall branch is safe on Windows too. Done ~20:20.")
inl note_of_remote_exec_s9_11 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S9", 11u32, "2026-11-05T00:17Z", "kit", "11-04 20:2x kit: WINDOWS BOX run COMPLETE (kit-s9-box-measure, load 39% at start, 8 cores): start_ms 700,824,790,764,819,899,1198,1002,904,1080,845,791,601,475,535,582,638,891,2848,1429 -> p95 (19th of 20 sorted) = 1429 ms <= 2000; resident default 250 ms CPU / 600 s = 4 core_hundredths, +sbwt none 141 ms = 2, both far under 100. beam_decision_of {start_p95 = Millis 1429; coordinator_cpu = CoreHundredths 4} = PerCall. So the earlier 115-177 core_hundredths box readings were load artifacts (busy-wait spinning while other work held the cores), not the BEAM's idle cost. Windows starts are ~3.9x Linux (1429 vs 368 ms p95) but still inside the rule. The node run (100% load, TRAIN26) is still going and decides for the coordinator's home; if it agrees, S9 = PerCall everywhere.")
inl note_of_remote_exec_s9_12 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S9", 12u32, "2026-11-05T00:19Z", "kit", "11-04 20:2x kit: DECISION (S9 DoD: measure BEAM start + coordinator CPU, decide resident vs per-call). WINDOWS NODE run COMPLETE (kit-s9-node-measure, laptop-ts at load 100%: TRAIN26 + 4 other jobs, its normal working state): start_ms 4837,6660,7636,3136,7032,23697,17094,4495,5601,2300,7679,6022,12763,16075,5265,2148,2930,9262,11341,3744 -> p95 (19th of 20) = 17094 ms > 2000; resident default 62 ms CPU / 600 s = 1 core_hundredth; +sbwt none read 0 (implausibly low, likely starved or missed by the tree count, so read it as <= ~1). beam_decision_of {start_p95 = Millis 17094; coordinator_cpu = CoreHundredths 1} = StayResident. PER-HOST RESULT: vultr/Linux PerCall (368 ms, 2), box/Windows PerCall at 39% load (1429 ms, 4), node/Windows StayResident (17094 ms under its usual load, 1). The node is the coordinator's home and is normally saturated, where a per-call BEAM start costs 2-24 s of wall time, while a resident BEAM idles at ~0.01% of a core. So the coordinator stays RESIDENT on the node (default flags are fine at this cost; +sbwt none is optional), and per-call stays the rule for Linux hosts. This unblocks S10's `kit coordinator` entry: start system_started once and keep it running on the node.")
inl remote_exec_s10 () = Task ("REMOTE-EXEC-S10", "M3e coordinator dispatches through kit job start --on and survives an SSH drop", TaskKind, P0, D3, 98u32, Active, "REMOTE-EXEC-S9,REMOTE-EXEC-S3", "kit,team:kit,remote,node,coordinator", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_remote_exec_s10 () = Planning ("REMOTE-EXEC-S10", Minutes 60u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_remote_exec_s10_1 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S10", 1u32, "2026-11-01T12:43Z", "kit", "11-01 08:4x kit, slice of EOIE-REMOTE-EXECUTOR (main 08:4x: slice it like SCRUM-XP; milestones M2-M5 from the 10-25 note, M1 shipped in TRAIN4c). DoD: with the link cut mid-queue, queued jobs stay queued and dispatch resumes after reconnect, with none lost or doubled (fault-injection test); every dispatch goes through S2's verb. NEXT STEP: wire the actor's dispatch to the S2 CLI and a reconnect loop under the supervisor. TYPEFEATURESET PLAN: link_state = Up | Down of since | Reconnecting of attempt (union); dispatch is idempotent by job_id (nominal).")
inl note_of_remote_exec_s10_2 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S10", 2u32, "2026-11-04T14:08Z", "kit", "11-04 11:3x kit: first pure piece in the tree (unstaged): kit_coordinator_table.newly_leased before after -> the jobs an input moved INTO LeasedOn, i.e. exactly what the S8 actor must dispatch after each Input. Test: settling a on the one-slot story dispatches exactly b; an unchanged table dispatches nothing. vultr --check exit 0 (with bodies). NEXT: Arrived carries a typed job spec (label, timeout, program, args; the machine from S7 placement), the actor turns newly_leased into `kit job start --on <machine>` calls (lib process.captured_by has a gleam arm), and the remote receipt id is recorded beside the job.")
inl note_of_remote_exec_s10_3 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S10", 3u32, "2026-11-04T15:46Z", "kit", "11-04 14:0x kit: the actor now DISPATCHES (unstaged): kit_coordinator_actor gains `Watch : subject job_id` and an outbox in its state (empty after a replay, so a restarted coordinator waits for its dispatcher to re-subscribe); after each Input it sends newly_leased (before, after) to every subscriber. Gleam test leased_jobs_are_sent_to_the_dispatcher_in_order: on the one-slot story the dispatcher receives job-a, then job-b when a settles, and nothing else. vultr --check exit 0 (with bodies); box gleam notebook queued. NEXT: the real dispatcher = a small actor that turns each job id into `kit job start --on <machine>` (machine from S7 placement, spec from a typed job_spec carried by the arrival) and feeds Starting/Settling back as Input.")
inl note_of_remote_exec_s10_4 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S10", 4u32, "2026-11-04T16:10Z", "kit", "11-04 14:4x kit: box gleam notebook for the outbox: 3/4, and the RED one was the OLD restart test (\"Callee subject had no owner\": the named coordinator was not yet re-registered when Read ran after Crash + a fixed 1 s pause on a loaded box); the new outbox test passed. The lib has no non-raising name lookup (call and named send both raise), so the test cannot poll; widened the pause to 3 s and rerunning (job-1793808167415-kit-actor-restart-rerun). A deterministic restart bug would fail at any pause.")
inl note_of_remote_exec_s10_5 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S10", 5u32, "2026-11-04T16:30Z", "kit", "11-04 15:3x kit: restart test GREEN with the 3 s pause (kit-actor-restart-rerun: actor.spi 4/4 on the box, including the new outbox test leased_jobs_are_sent_to_the_dispatcher_in_order). The 1 s failure was the pause on a loaded box, not the outbox change.")
inl note_of_remote_exec_s10_6 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S10", 6u32, "2026-11-04T18:25Z", "kit", "11-04 20:3x kit: the DISPATCHER CORE is BUILT as a pure typed state machine (unstaged), new module kit_coordinator_table/dispatch.spi: job_spec {label; machine; command}; dispatch_message = Submitted (id, spec) | LeaseGranted id | LaunchAnswered (id, started) | JobEnded id; dispatch_effect = LaunchOn (id, spec) | TableInput table_input | UnknownJob id; dispatch_step state message -> state * effects: Submitted stores the spec and emits Arrived; LeaseGranted emits LaunchOn with the stored spec (unknown id -> UnknownJob, never a launch); LaunchAnswered emits Moved Starting, or Moved Expiring on a failed launch (the table requeues and re-leases it); JobEnded drops the spec and emits Moved Settling. Test the_dispatcher_launches_leased_jobs_and_reports_their_moves_to_the_table runs a 2-job 1-slot story through dispatch_step + table_after: only the leased job launches, a settles, b takes the slot, a failed launch re-leases. vultr --check ok with bodies; box notebook kit-dispatch-core-tests. NEXT: the Gleam shell = an actor whose inbox takes dispatch_message, owns the outbox subject it hands the coordinator via Watch (a subject is only received by its creator), runs LaunchOn through `kit job start --on <machine>` and sends TableInput effects as Input.")
inl note_of_remote_exec_s10_7 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S10", 7u32, "2026-11-04T18:29Z", "kit", "11-04 20:4x kit: dispatch core PROVEN on the box (kit-dispatch-core-tests 1/1). The Gleam SHELL is BUILT (unstaged) in kit_coordinator_actor: Watch now carries the dispatcher's own inbox (subject dispatch_message) and the coordinator sends LeaseGranted job to every watcher, so no forwarding process is needed (a subject is only received by its creator, and the dispatcher created its inbox). dispatcher_state {core : dispatch_state; coordinator; launch : job_spec -> bool}; dispatcher_step runs dispatch_step, sends TableInput effects as Input, and for each LaunchOn calls launch and feeds LaunchAnswered back through dispatch_step; dispatcher_started starts it and Watches the coordinator. The launcher is injected, so tests use a probe and the real one (kit job start --on <machine> through process) is a separate wiring step. Gleam test the_dispatcher_launches_each_leased_job_once_and_the_next_after_it_ends (1-slot coordinator: a launches, b does not until JobEnded a, then b) + the old outbox test now expecting LeaseGranted. vultr --check ok with bodies; box gleam notebook kit-dispatcher-actor-tests.")
inl note_of_remote_exec_s10_8 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S10", 8u32, "2026-11-04T20:19Z", "kit", "11-04 21:0x kit: PROVEN on the box (kit-splits-tests 14/14): kit_coordinator_table/dispatch.spi (the 1-slot story + a_job_that_never_launches_is_given_up_after_three_tries_and_frees_its_slot: after 3 failed launches the dispatcher emits LaunchGaveUp once, forgets the job and settles it so the slot goes to the next job; launch_args = kit job start --on <machine> <label> <timeout> <command>) and kit_coordinator_actor/actor.spi (gleam: restart under the supervisor, the outbox now carrying LeaseGranted, and the_dispatcher_launches_each_leased_job_once_and_the_next_after_it_ends). The real launcher kit_launch (process.captured_by kit (launch_args spec)) is in the tree; NEXT: a start entry that wires keeper + coordinator + dispatcher with kit_launch under one supervisor, then a dry run against vultr with a harmless command.")
inl note_of_remote_exec_s10_9 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S10", 9u32, "2026-11-04T22:00Z", "kit", "11-04 18:0x kit: the new system test (a launcher that cannot run) CAUGHT A REAL BUG, and with load gone it was the only failure (5/6). After a failed launch the dispatcher sends Moved (a, Expiring); the table requeues a and the freed slot re-leases a inside the SAME input, so a is LeasedOn before and after and newly_leased (a before/after diff) reports nothing: no second LeaseGranted, so a was never retried and never given up, holding the slot forever. The pure dispatch test missed it because it drives table_after directly. FIX (unstaged): kit_coordinator_table.granted_by before input after = newly_leased plus the job of a Moved (job, Expiring) input when it is leased again; coordinator_step now sends granted_by. Rust test in table.spi: the diff alone misses the re-lease, granted_by reports it. The actor test now polls (settled_within, 500 ms x 40) instead of a fixed 2 s pause. vultr --check ok with bodies; box notebooks kit-relet-tests.")
inl note_of_remote_exec_s10_10 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S10", 10u32, "2026-11-04T22:11Z", "kit", "11-04 18:2x kit: the re-lease fix is PROVEN on the box (kit-relet-tests 2/2): kit_coordinator_table table.spi 2/2 (incl. \"the diff alone misses a re-lease in the same input\" / \"granted_by reports it\") and kit_coordinator_actor actor.spi 6/6, including a_launcher_that_cannot_run_gives_every_job_up_and_frees_the_slot: with the real kit_launch on a binary that does not exist, job a fails 3 launches, is given up and settles, then b takes the slot and is given up too. The coordinator stack (keeper + coordinator + dispatcher + real launcher, via system_started) now runs end to end on the BEAM. NEXT: a `kit coordinator` entry that starts system_started with kit_launch \"kit\" and feeds it submissions, then a dry run on vultr with a harmless command.")
inl note_of_remote_exec_s10_11 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S10", 11u32, "2026-11-05T00:19Z", "kit", "11-04 20:2x kit: S9 is DONE and decides the shape of the remaining entry: the coordinator stays RESIDENT on the node (per-call BEAM starts there cost 2-24 s under its usual load; resident idle ~0.01% of a core). NEXT for S10: a `kit coordinator` entry that runs system_started once with kit_launch \"kit\" as a long-lived node job and accepts submissions, then a dry run on the node with a harmless command.")
inl note_of_remote_exec_s10_12 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S10", 12u32, "2026-11-05T00:21Z", "kit", "11-04 20:2x kit: the resident entry's INPUT side is built (unstaged) in kit_coordinator_table/dispatch.spi: typed `submission = SubmissionReady : dispatch_message | SubmissionRefused : string` and submission_of, which parses \"<job-id> <label> <machine> <timeout-ms> -- <program> [args...]\" into Submitted (JobId id, job_spec) (blank runs ignored) or a refusal naming why (not a number / needs a job-... id and a positive timeout / the expected shape). Test a_submission_line_becomes_a_submitted_message_or_a_named_refusal (5 cases). Kept in dispatch.spi, not a sibling module, given tonight's notebook sibling-import issue. vultr --check ok with bodies; box notebook kit-submission-tests. NEXT: the `kit coordinator` entry loop: system_started once with kit_launch \"kit\", then each submission line (stdin or a spool file per S4's queue) -> submission_of -> send to the dispatcher.")
inl note_of_remote_exec_s10_13 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S10", 13u32, "2026-11-05T00:22Z", "kit", "11-04 20:2x kit: submission_of PROVEN on the box (kit-submission-tests: dispatch.spi 3/3, incl. the new 5-case test and the two dispatcher tests). Rides TRAIN27.")
inl note_of_remote_exec_s10_14 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S10", 14u32, "2026-11-05T00:26Z", "kit", "11-04 20:2x kit: the entry loop's CORE is built and PROVEN on the BEAM (unstaged): actor.spi submitted_to dispatcher lines parses each line with submission_of, sends SubmissionReady messages to the running dispatcher and returns the refusal reasons. Box notebook kit-entry-feed-tests: actor.spi 7/7, incl. submission_lines_reach_the_dispatcher_and_bad_lines_are_refused (system_started with an unlaunchable kit_launch; one good line settles via give-up, one bad line = one refusal), so submission_of's u64 parse works on the Gleam target too. Both coordinator packages are BEAM-only (no Rust crate inlines them), so no size run is needed. LEFT: the `kit coordinator` verb that runs system_started on the node and reads lines into submitted_to (stdin or the S4 spool), then the node dry run.")
inl note_of_remote_exec_s10_15 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S10", 15u32, "2026-11-05T00:27Z", "kit", "11-04 20:3x kit DESIGN for the last step (not built tonight, ~30 min left): the `kit coordinator` verb reuses the BEAM launch path cells already uses (spiral apps/cells lib/spiral/cells/targets.ex run_gleam_program: gleam package -> `erl -noshell -pa <package>/build/dev/erlang/*/ebin -eval \"main:main()\"`). Pieces: (1) a coordinator main in kit_coordinator_actor: system_started 1 (kit_launch \"kit\"), then a loop reading stdin lines (Erlang io:get_line through the existing erlang_process/otp boundary, or the S4 spool dir) into submitted_to, printing each refusal; (2) a build step that emits that module with --backend gleam into a kit-owned package dir (same as the notebook cells do) and `gleam build`s it once per kit install; (3) the verb in kit's Rust side spawns erl with those -pa paths as a long-lived node job (`kit job start --on laptop-ts ... coordinator`), resident per S9. Dry run: one harmless submission line, e.g. \"job-dry probe node 60000 -- pwsh -NoProfile -Command exit 0\", must settle.")
inl note_of_remote_exec_s10_16 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S10", 16u32, "2026-11-05T05:05Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-05 01:1x kit: INPUT side for the resident coordinator BUILT + PROVEN on the box (unstaged; BEAM-only, no Rust inliner, so no size run): the S4 spool. actor.spi spool_taken <dir> takes every *.sub file (filelib wildcard via lib erlang_file), deletes it, and returns its non-blank lines; served_once dispatcher spool feeds them to submitted_to and returns refusals; served loops that every 1 s, printing refusals to stderr. A resident node job has no usable stdin, so the spool is the input; writers must write <name>.tmp and then rename to .sub. Test a_spool_file_is_taken_once_and_its_lines_reach_the_dispatcher (one good line + a blank + one bad = 1 refusal; the file is removed; a second pass takes nothing; job-s settles). Box notebook kit-nb-coordinator-spool: actor.spi 8/8. vultr --check ok with bodies (vultr's lib copy lacked erlang_file.test_cache_file; refreshed from the box). STEP 2 BLOCKER, a decision: building the coordinator as a runnable BEAM program. Cells has no build-only mode; it writes a Gleam package (cells-gleam-warm/<key>-<slot>), gleam-builds it and immediately runs erl -pa build/dev/erlang/*/ebin -eval main:main(). Options: (A) a cells --build-only flag that prints the ebin paths (spiral apps/cells; x leads cells), then kit runs erl -pa <paths> -eval '<actor>:serve(...)' as a long-lived kit job on the node; (B) kit's own Gleam package writer (duplicates gleam_project.spi); (C) run the coordinator as a long-timeout notebook cell (no new build code, but a never-ending 'test' misuses the test runner). Recommend A.")
inl note_of_remote_exec_s10_17 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S10", 17u32, "2026-11-05T07:14Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-05 03:1x kit: STEP 2 UNBLOCKED AND PROVEN on the box. x accepted the cells build-only flag (kit drafted it; landed CELLS-BUILD-ONLY at 02:40), and kit's dry run found two gaps, fixed in CELLS-BUILD-ONLY-2 (FINAL, sent to x to land): the receipt key collided with dispatch_spiral's per-cell :build_only (now :build_receipt, with code memo off), and cells deleted the build dir (now keep_files plus a 'cells-build-only package=<dir>' line). New entry cell in actor.spi: the_coordinator_serves_the_spool_its_environment_names reads KIT_COORDINATOR_SPOOL via os:getenv. Unset (every notebook run) it returns at once and passes (actor.spi 9/9 box). Set, it runs system_started 4 (kit_launch \"kit\") and serves the spool. DRY RUN (draft cells): run_notebook.ps1 --path kit_coordinator_actor/actor.spi --build-only --test the_coordinator_serves_the_spool_its_environment_names -> package + 12 ebins, exit 0. Then erl -noshell -pa <12> -eval main:main() with KIT_COORDINATOR_SPOOL=<dir> stayed resident until its 40 s timeout, took a.sub (dir empty) and printed 'expected <job-id> ... : not a submission' for the bad line. The good line reached the dispatcher (kit is not on the box PATH, so its launch was given up, as in the actor test). LEFT (step 3): a kit verb or launch-train-style script step that (a) builds once per install, (b) copies the kept package dir to %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\coordinator\\<hash> and deletes the temp one, (c) starts erl as a long-lived node job with an absolute kit path for kit_launch, and the node dry run (one harmless line must settle).")
inl note_of_remote_exec_s10_18 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S10", 18u32, "2026-11-05T07:48Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-05 03:5x kit DESIGN for step 3 (not built; it can only be proven after an install anyway). Verb: kit job coordinator build|start <spool> (one new JobCoordinator case; job_command_of reflects the word; a new linked crate kit_job_coordinator keeps kit_job_entry small). BUILD runs <spiral>/apps/cells/spi/run_notebook.ps1 --path <kit>/src/kit_coordinator_actor/actor.spi --build-only --test the_coordinator_serves_the_spool_its_environment_names --no-html --output-path <jobs root>/coordinator/build.ipynb. --output-path is required: without it the runner writes actor.spi.ipynb into the repo, which happened tonight and was removed. It parses the receipt (package=, ebin=..., entry=) into <jobs root>/coordinator/receipt and refuses if any ebin path is missing. START reads the receipt, refuses with \"run kit job coordinator build\" if a path is gone, and starts erl -noshell -pa <ebins> -eval main:main() as an ordinary kit job (resident; S9 chose resident on the node) with KIT_COORDINATOR_SPOOL=<spool> and KIT_COORDINATOR_KIT=<this kit.exe>. Both are read by actor.spi (environment_named, kit_named; actor.spi 10/10 box). Writers drop <name>.tmp and rename it to .sub. Needs lib process execute_with_options_rust (environment_variables + command) or kit_job_launch's typed start with env. Dry run on the node: one line \"job-dry probe node 60000 -- pwsh -NoProfile -Command exit 0\" must settle.")
inl note_of_remote_exec_s10_19 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S10", 19u32, "2026-11-05T08:07Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-05 04:2x kit: STEP 3 VERB BUILT (unstaged). New linked crate kit_job_coordinator, routed as kit job coordinator: a JobCoordinator case in kit_jobs model, where reflection picks the word (verb test pair added), plus usage text. kit_job_entry links the crate and calls coordinator_run (jobs_root ()). Reflected step (build|start) and flags (--spiral, --kit, --spool). BUILD runs pwsh run_notebook.ps1 --build-only --test <entry cell> --no-html --output-path <jobs>/coordinator-build.ipynb, parses the receipt (package=/ebin=/entry=) and writes <jobs>/coordinator.receipt. START refuses with exit 2 when there is no receipt, a built ebin is gone, or --spool is missing. Otherwise it runs <this kit.exe> job start kit-coordinator 604800000 pwsh -NoProfile -Command \"$env:KIT_COORDINATOR_SPOOL=...; $env:KIT_COORDINATOR_KIT=<kit.exe>; & erl <quoted args>\" (every path single-quoted, embedded quotes doubled). Pure tests in coordinator.spi: receipt read, refused, erl argv, submission line shape, step/flag parsing, script quoting. CLI check in kit_jobs/stop_cli.spi (train): no step, unknown step, start without a build. Box --check ok: coordinator, kit_jobs model, kit_job_entry. Pending: box notebooks (job-1793865887011) and node size (kit_job_coordinator, kit_job_entry, kit_job_start_args; job-1793865923779, queued for a slot). LEFT: the node dry run after an install.")
inl note_of_remote_exec_s10_20 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S10", 20u32, "2026-11-05T08:43Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-05 04:5x kit: kit_job_coordinator sized 1055 (OVER): lib process/file_system inline plus two reflected unions. SPLIT: the argv parse (reflected step build|start and flags --spiral/--kit/--spool) moved to NEW linked crate kit_job_coordinator_args. It exports coordinator_args_read (skipped) -> array_base coordinator_row { build; start; spiral; kit; spool; rejected }, primitive fields, so it links. coordinator_run reads it with one am'.index_base and dispatches on the booleans. Box --check ok (args, coordinator, kit_job_entry); re-size + notebooks pending (job-1793868167908, job-1793868141327). kit_job_entry 435 and kit_job_start_args 696 sized fine.")
inl note_of_remote_exec_s10_21 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S10", 21u32, "2026-11-05T08:46Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-05 04:5x kit: coordinator verb SIZED + PROVEN in the tree. Node size: kit_job_coordinator 797 (was 1055 before the split), kit_job_coordinator_args 287, kit_job_entry 435, kit_job_start_args 696. Box notebooks: kit_job_coordinator_args 1/1 (steps + flags), kit_job_coordinator 3/3 (receipt, submission line, build argv + start script quoting), kit_jobs model 9/9 (coordinator verb pair). Rides TRAIN28. After the install, the node dry run: kit job coordinator build --spiral C:\\home\\git\\spiral --kit C:\\home\\git\\kit, then kit job coordinator start --spool <dir>, then drop one harmless .sub line and see it settle.")
inl note_of_remote_exec_s10_22 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S10", 22u32, "2026-11-05T23:11Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-05 19:1x kit: TRAIN32 notebook RED on kit_coordinator_actor/actor.spi, 8/10: a_launcher_that_cannot_run_gives_every_job_up_and_frees_the_slot (a was given up after three failed launches) and submission_lines_reach_the_dispatcher_and_bad_lines_are_refused. The spool tests added 03:21 passed. Both failing tests are in HEAD but newer than TRAIN26's 6-test run, so they never ran in a train; each waits settled_within 40 tries x 500 ms = 20 s, and three failed launches of a missing binary on the 100 pct node take longer (slowest cell 158 s vs 79 s in TRAIN26). Widened all four settle waits to 120 tries (60 s), unstaged, TRAIN33.")
