inl note_of_erl_vs_dib_bench_1 () = Note ("ERL-VS-DIB-BENCH", 1u32, "2026-11-01T14:34Z", "main", "main 10:3x (user 11-01 10:3x: 'i asked before for tasks to benchmark how erl overheads compared to the old dib runner... if these tasks aren't detailed already it means my old user prompts aren't being properly turned into advanced complete work'). DoD: the same 3 notebooks (small, lib physics, x client) run under both runners on the node with 5 runs each and an in-job A/A (ab-needs-in-job-aa); a table of per-phase medians (VM startup, first cell, warm cell, compile, build, output) published on this item; the top 3 overheads named with a fix item each. TYPEFEATURESET PLAN: phase timings as typed rows keyed by a phase union and a runner union, units as phantom milliseconds (KIT-EXEC-METRICS's model). NEXT STEP: find the last dib-runner state that still runs (polyglot Nov-2025, same checkout as CELLS-PLOT-EXPORT) and pick the 3 notebooks.")
inl note_of_erl_vs_dib_bench_2 () = Note ("ERL-VS-DIB-BENCH", 2u32, "2026-11-02T14:31Z", "main", "main 11-02: parent epic is KINO-SOTA; recorded here instead of as a dep, because a dep on an unfinished parent epic hid this item from 'agile next' (backlog-wide sweep: 67 open items depended on an unfinished Epic).")
inl note_of_erl_vs_dib_bench_3 () = Note ("ERL-VS-DIB-BENCH", 3u32, "2026-11-04T11:38Z", "x", "2026-11-04 07:38 x: PRECONDITION PROBE (claimed by x via the gate; this is main's item). The old runner exists ONLY on the box: dotnet tool list -g shows microsoft.dotnet-interactive 1.0.712001 and dotnet-repl 0.3.259 on the box; the NODE has neither (and x's node-first rule wants the bench there). spiral has no dib driver scripts left; the only .dib files are polyglot\\deps\\dotnet-interactive samples (27), not Spiral notebooks. So before the DoD's 3x2x5 runs: (1) install dotnet-interactive + dotnet-repl on the node (dotnet tool install -g, versions pinned to the box's 1.0.712001 / 0.3.259); (2) recreate the dib side of each of the 3 notebooks (small, lib physics, x client) as .dib run by 'dotnet repl --run <nb>.dib --exit-after-run' (the old invocation), with the same cells as the Cells notebooks; (3) the in-job A/A per runner. Releasing; next step owner main (or x on request).")
inl note_of_erl_vs_dib_bench_4 () = Note ("ERL-VS-DIB-BENCH", 4u32, "2026-11-04T11:45Z", "x", "2026-11-04 07:45 x: PRECONDITION (1) TRIED and BLOCKED, the cause measured: 'dotnet tool install -g' of microsoft.dotnet-interactive 1.0.712001 and dotnet-repl 0.3.259 on the node both fail ('failed to install'). The node has only SDK 9.0.200 and runtimes 9.0.0-rc.2 / 9.0.2 / 11.0.0-rc.1, while the box carries 3.1, 6, 7.0.x, 8.0.x, 9, 10 and 11, and these old tool builds target an older runtime than the node has. So before the bench runs on the node: install the .NET 8 runtime there (or pick tool versions built for net9), then retry. Not done further by x: kit's claim guard marks this item declined-by-x for now; main owns the next step.")
inl perf_framework_study () = Task ("PERF-FRAMEWORK-STUDY", "Study every benchmarking approach we have, pre-vibecode (polyglot Perf, spiral perf notebooks) and since (compiler bench-pair and matrix, lib benchmark.spi, Cells phases_ms, kit bench, model_metrics, rebar plans), extract the best techniques, and design one benchmarking framework in Spiral", Story, P1, D5, 0u32, Planned, "", "benchmark,perf,lib,kit,main,team:main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_perf_framework_study () = Planning ("PERF-FRAMEWORK-STUDY", Minutes 480u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_perf_framework_study_1 () = Note ("PERF-FRAMEWORK-STUDY", 1u32, "2026-11-01T14:34Z", "main", "main 10:3x (user: 'we haven't even started exploring this edge yet. sad that we don't have cpus to iterate over and over here'). DoD: a comparison table (technique, where it lives, what it gets right: warmup, A/A, interleaving, quiet-core sampling, outlier policy, typed results) on this item; a framework design (API + typed result model) reviewed against it; one existing benchmark ported as proof. TYPEFEATURESET PLAN: a measurement as a GADT indexed by its unit; a benchmark as a record of setup/run/teardown with phantom-typed inputs; results as typed rows the exporter renders. NEXT STEP: locate the pre-vibecode polyglot Perf and spiral perf notebooks in git history (git log --all -- '*erf*').")
inl blender_sample () = Task ("BLENDER-SAMPLE", "A minimal Spiral sample that drives Blender (revisiting the old polyglot Blender experiment) using the typefeatureset, rendered headless in GitHub Actions, with the image linked from dir-tree-html; the WGSL runner already works in GH", Story, P2, D4, 0u32, Planned, "", "samples,blender,ci,gpu,main,team:main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_blender_sample () = Planning ("BLENDER-SAMPLE", Minutes 360u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_blender_sample_1 () = Note ("BLENDER-SAMPLE", 1u32, "2026-11-01T14:34Z", "main", "main 10:3x. DoD: a sample in spiral compiles to Python for Blender's bpy, a workflow step renders one image headless (blender -b), and the image is published as an artifact linked from dir-tree-html. TYPEFEATURESET PLAN: scene objects as a GADT indexed by object kind (mesh/light/camera) so only valid properties apply; transforms with phantom coordinate spaces. NEXT STEP: find the polyglot Blender experiment in git history and check that a headless Blender install fits the Ubuntu runner time budget.")
inl package_model_standard () = Task ("PACKAGE-MODEL-STANDARD", "Decide one packaging model for every repo by experiment: one manifest with many folders, one manifest per folder, or a main per .spi file (the compiler already takes main from each file), weighing Cells ergonomics and script-like execution (KIT-RUN-SPI); then a per-lane migration list for every repo that doesn't follow it", Story, P1, D4, 0u32, Planned, "LIB-SPIRAL-TOML-MIGRATION", "packages,manifest,edition3,kit,main,team:main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_package_model_standard () = Planning ("PACKAGE-MODEL-STANDARD", Minutes 360u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_package_model_standard_1 () = Note ("PACKAGE-MODEL-STANDARD", 1u32, "2026-11-01T14:34Z", "main", "main 10:3x (user 11-01). DoD: three small repos-in-miniature, one per model, each running a test, a script-like main and a Cells notebook; a scored table (lines of config, rebuild time on one change, tooling paths that break); the decision written here; then one migration item per lane naming its repo's changes. TYPEFEATURESET PLAN: the package model as a union (SingleManifest of folder list | PerFolder | PerFile) in the manifest schema, so tools branch on a type, not on probing paths. NEXT STEP: census each repo's current style (spiproj and spiral.toml locations, how many folders per manifest).")
inl note_of_package_model_standard_2 () = Note ("PACKAGE-MODEL-STANDARD", 2u32, "2026-11-02T11:04Z", "kit", "kit 11-02 07:2x, from the S0 study subagent's execution packet (aaf2a6f472dc70094). TEMPLATE-TYPED-UNITS-1 is the candidate answer. The SingleManifest | PerFolder | PerFile union gains a fourth reading: linked units (a package is a unit; other units come in through link:, COMPILER-LINKED-UNITS). SPIPROJ-RETIRE settles the manifest file: spiral.toml only.")
inl note_of_package_model_standard_3 () = Note ("PACKAGE-MODEL-STANDARD", 3u32, "2026-11-04T08:56Z", "tibia", "11-04 05:0x tibia, INVENTORY FOR THE TIBIA STEP (after lib went prelude-only at 04:49): tibia has 9 packages (src, results, apps/arena, canary, host, map, offscreen, predict, suite), and each holds BOTH a package.spiproj and a spiral.toml whose modules list duplicates it (e.g. src: packageDir ../deps/spiral/lib, packages |core- spiral-, 21 modules in define-before-use order). RISK to settle in the standard before tibia moves: tibia/src also holds standalone programs that are deliberately NOT package modules (hunt_sim, gpu_sim, pack_sim; --check answers 'not declared in package', and build.ps1 compiles them by name under its long-gpu label). If prelude-only derives the module set from every .spi file present, those programs become package modules, which changes what Cells notebooks and consumer3 build. So the tibia step needs either an exclude list in the toml or the programs moved to their own app folder first. tibia will do the move after the standard says which; nothing changed in tibia yet.")
inl note_of_package_model_standard_4 () = Note ("PACKAGE-MODEL-STANDARD", 4u32, "2026-11-04T14:47Z", "fiscal", "2026-11-04 10:47 fiscal: INVENTORY FOR THE FISCAL STEP (input to the per-lane migration list; no change made). fiscal has 83 package.spiproj and 1 spiral.toml outside deps/target: (1) src/ -- BOTH package.spiproj and spiral.toml (edition 3, packageDir ../deps/spiral/lib, packages |core- spiral-, 16 modules in define-before-use order), the same duplication tibia reported; (2) generated/ -- 82 generated units, each its own one-module package.spiproj WRITTEN by the generator (src/generator.spi write_generated_module_with -> module_package dependencies module_name), listing its sibling-unit dependencies as packages ('../generated_cfdi_tfd_1_1-'); a generator test reads one back (the CFDI unit lists its complement units). So fiscal's migration is two steps: src to prelude-only spiral.toml (mechanical, like lib), and the generator's emitted manifest format (module_package) to whatever the chosen model is -- a one-function change plus one test, re-proven by a full regen identity run (all units, diff vs reference) on vultr. README.md:145 documents the one-module-package-per-unit layout ('so a test cell type-checks only its own module'), which any model must keep.")
inl dialect_rename_edition3 () = Task ("DIALECT-RENAME-EDITION3", "Replace 'edition 3' everywhere with the i574n dialect naming the user chose ('spiral (i574n dialect)'): manifests (edition: 3 -> dialect = i574n), item names, docs, code identifiers, messages; with spiral.toml present the dialect is implicit, and script-like runs need no manifest at all", Story, P1, D3, 0u32, Planned, "DIALECT-IDENTITY", "edition3,dialect,naming,main,team:main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_dialect_rename_edition3 () = Planning ("DIALECT-RENAME-EDITION3", Minutes 240u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_dialect_rename_edition3_1 () = Note ("DIALECT-RENAME-EDITION3", 1u32, "2026-11-01T14:34Z", "main", "main 10:3x (user 11-01: 'remove the reference to edition three everywhere, keep only i574n dialect and according variations'). DoD: grep for 'edition3', 'edition 3', 'edition: 3', 'EDITION3' across all repos returns only historical records (backlog history, changelogs); manifests carry the dialect; compiler messages say 'i574n dialect'. TYPEFEATURESET PLAN: dialect as a union in the manifest model (Upstream | I574n), so 'edition' only ever means a year. NEXT STEP: census the hits per repo and per kind (manifest, code, docs) to size it.")
inl note_of_dialect_rename_edition3_2 () = Note ("DIALECT-RENAME-EDITION3", 2u32, "2026-11-02T11:04Z", "kit", "kit 11-02 07:2x, from the S0 study subagent's execution packet (aaf2a6f472dc70094). TEMPLATE-TYPED-UNITS-1 and SPIPROJ-RETIRE (new) adopt the dialect naming from birth (dialect = i574n in spiral.toml); new items and templates stop writing 'edition 3'. Every migration below touches manifests anyway, so this rename should land before or with them; it stays P1.")
inl x_sota_model () = Task ("X-SOTA-MODEL", "Target model of x's business logic at peak typefeatureset: endpoint/command GADTs, auth phantoms, post typestate, invariant and platform prototypes, executor HKT, reflection codecs, iggy event family", Feature, P1, D6, 70u32, Active, "", "team:x,x,typefeatures,iggy,sim,client", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_x_sota_model () = Planning ("X-SOTA-MODEL", Minutes 480u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_x_sota_model_1 () = Note ("X-SOTA-MODEL", 1u32, "2026-11-01T14:35Z", "x", "2026-11-01 10:35 x: TARGET MODEL (user 10:3x: 'the typefeatureset SOTA version of x's business logic', with iggy). What x looks like at peak, so every slice moves toward one picture. Staged-Spiral limits respected (memory typefeatureset-default-all-code: indices only on static paths; existentials only for compile-time heterogeneity).\nPROTOCOL (client <-> X API, shared by xc and the sim):\n- 'endpoint r' GADT indexed by its RESPONSE type, the existing wire.spi 'reads r'/'writes r' pattern extended to every route (users_reply, page_reply, posts_reply, created_reply, deleted_reply).\n- Auth as a phantom index on the call: 'caller c' with wire::app_context / user_context (done in sim_serve). A write needs a 'caller user_context' witness on BOTH sides.\n- Codecs derived by REFLECTION over record keys (lib/spiral reflection module) instead of hand-written json field lists, so a renamed field changes both encoder and decoder.\nSIM:\n- Post lifecycle as typestate: 'post s' with phantoms drafted | posted | edited | deleted. edit takes 'post posted', delete returns 'post deleted', and an edit of a deleted post doesn't typecheck.\n- The runtime event stream stays NESTED UNIONS (content | revision | reaction), since a runtime-mixed list can't hold indexed values.\n- Invariants as a PROTOTYPE 'invariant i' with one instance per checker (sequential_equivalence, no_stale_results, ...), in place of a hand-maintained list of check functions.\n- The scheduler is generic over its executor as an HKT, '\\* m. executor m', with instances 'sync' (fake, deterministic tests) and 'beam' (cells_executor), so the same scheduler code runs both without the pair-of-records indirection.\nCLIENT:\n- Commands as 'command r' GADT indexed by their result, so the CLI dispatcher and the printer agree by type.\n- Platforms as a PROTOTYPE 'platform p' (OnX, OnMastodon, OnBluesky instances: limit, weigh, link weight) in place of the 'limit_on' match, so the sim serves Mastodon and Bluesky through the same instances.\n- Cell budgets read Cells' generic CellBudget constant, never a copy.\nIGGY:\n- The sim's event stream IS a message family. Post/edit/like events become a LIB-IGGY-CLIENT GADT family with byte-identical Rust and Gleam codecs: xc (Rust) produces, and the sim and Cells (Gleam, BEAM-supervised) consume.\n- The sim's duplicate/reorder faults map onto iggy redelivery, so x's e2e becomes a stream replay any machine can follow (IGGY-MULTI-MACHINE), and the metrics rows ride the same stream.\n- Split per BACKEND-BALANCE: Rust on the per-message data path, Gleam for supervision and state.\nFIRST SLICE (<= 60 min, the smallest proof of the approach): 'platform p' prototype in splitter.spi replacing limit_on and the per-platform weigh matches. DEFINITION OF DONE: splitter's test blocks unchanged and green on rust and gleam, 'xc post-thread --dry-run' output byte-identical on every client .spi, and kit census showing prototype > 0 for apps/client. Then 'post s' typestate in the sim, then 'command r'.")
inl note_of_x_sota_model_2 () = Note ("X-SOTA-MODEL", 2u32, "2026-11-01T14:38Z", "x", "2026-11-01 10:38 x: FIRST SLICE made concrete against the live code and the lib's syntax. Today splitter.spi matches ':| platform = OnX | OnMastodon | OnBluesky _' in 5 functions (posted_by :21, posted_glyphs :35, unit_on :45, limit_on :106 and friends), one match per behaviour. That's the closed-world switch a prototype replaces.\nSyntax, verified in lib: a prototype is declared ':? name t : signature' (base.spi:324 ':? append t : t -> t -> t'). An instance is ':+ name type_ctor t = impl' (listm'.spi:5 ':+ append list t =', am'.spi:57 ':+ append array t = arraym.append').\nDraft shape: nominals ':! on_x = ()', ':! on_mastodon = ()', ':! on_bluesky = { handle : string }'. Prototypes ':? cell_limit p : p -> i32', ':? glyphs_of p : p -> string -> list string', ':? unit_weight p : p -> string -> i32', ':? posted_form p : p -> string -> string'. One ':+' instance per platform per prototype, so all X behaviour lives together in the on_x instances.\nSTAGED RULE: the CLI picks the platform at RUNTIME (a flag), so the type-indexed dispatch happens in ONE branch per constructor at the boundary: '?= flag ?| OnX => run on_x | ...' calls a '\\* p.' generic splitter. The same pattern sim_serve's caller dispatch proved (x-parity-typed2). After that point every call is statically resolved per platform.\nDoD unchanged (n1). Pre-check before writing: confirm on rust AND gleam that ':+' instances on user nominals compile (lib instances are on lib types). The first compile is one test block in splitter.spi, on the node.")
inl note_of_x_sota_model_3 () = Note ("X-SOTA-MODEL", 3u32, "2026-11-01T14:43Z", "x", "2026-11-01 10:42 x: PRE-CHECK PASSED (box, no window held, current core DBC5EC18F14C354C). A scratch probe declared ':? cell_limit p : p -> i32', nominals ':! on_x = ()' and ':! on_mastodon = ()', instances ':+ cell_limit on_x = \\ _ => 280i32' and ':+ cell_limit on_mastodon = \\ _ => 500i32', and main = cell_limit on_x + cell_limit on_mastodon. It compiles with exit 0 on BOTH Rust and Gleam. The Gleam output is 'pub fn main() { 780 }': dispatch resolved entirely at compile time, zero runtime cost.\nTwo syntax facts for the slice:\n(1) a ':! name = ()' nominal IS its own value. Write 'cell_limit on_x', not 'on_x ()' ('on_x ()' gives 'Got () Expected () -> _').\n(2) '|core-' in a package resolves against the compiler's WORKING DIRECTORY (deps/The-Spiral-Language/...), not packageDir, so scratch probes run from C:\\home\\git\\spiral.\nSo the 'platform p' slice is unblocked: the next step is the real draft in splitter.spi, proven by its own DoD (n1).")
inl note_of_x_sota_model_4 () = Note ("X-SOTA-MODEL", 4u32, "2026-11-01T19:03Z", "x", "2026-11-01 15:03 x: FIRST SLICE DRAFTED at its final path (unstaged, x/apps/client/splitter.spi + apps/sim/spi/sim_serve.spi).\n- Nominals ':! on_x / on_mastodon / on_bluesky = ()'.\n- 5 prototypes: cell_limit, posted_as, glyphs_on, unit_as, padded_as. 15 ':+' instances, each placed right after the helper it uses, so all of X's rules are on_x instances.\n- Generics: 'units_with \\* p.', 'weighed_with \\* p.', and 'rules_with \\* p. (on : p) : platform_rules', which packs a platform's instances into a record { limit; posted; weighed }.\n- ONE runtime match, 'rules_of (p : platform)', at the boundary (the CLI picks the platform from XC_SITE at runtime). It replaces the 5 per-behaviour matches (posted_by, posted_glyphs, unit_on, limit_on, weighed_on).\n- External signatures are unchanged (limit_on, posted_by, posted_on, weighed_on, weight_on are used by chunks/weights/posted/frames), and units_on/unit_on/posted_glyphs are gone (no outside callers).\n- sim_serve's too_heavy reads splitter::post_limit () in place of a second 280 literal.\nPROOF 1, rule written before the run: x-platform-proto1 (job-1793559782865, node xqueue1, core DBC5EC18F14C354C). PASS iff cell budget finds 0 issues for apps/client and apps/sim/spi AND the splitter (rust + gleam) and sim_serve notebooks pass. Then PROOF 2 is n1's DoD: a full node verify, where every client .spi dry-run and every README tutorial output must match byte for byte, plus 'kit standards --census apps/client' showing prototype > 0. Proof 2 is also x's lib-consumer gate after main's '::' lib respell.")
inl note_of_x_sota_model_5 () = Note ("X-SOTA-MODEL", 5u32, "2026-11-01T19:12Z", "x", "2026-11-01 15:12 x: x-platform-proto1 NOT MET by n4's rule. Cell budget was 0/0. Splitter failed 14/14 on rust only, and sim_serve failed to load. Cause, in stderr: ETY-ForallVarConstraintError, 'Metavariable's constraints must be a subset of the forall var p's. Got {glyphs_on}, Expected {}'. An EXPLICIT '\\* p.' forall must list the prototypes it uses, in braces after the var: '\\* p {glyphs_on; unit_as}.' (BlockParsing.fs:809 forall_var = ho_var + curlies(sepBy constraint ';')). A generic that calls another constrained generic must repeat its constraints, so weighed_with lists 3 and rules_with lists 5. Implicit generics (physics.spi euler, no annotation) infer them instead. Fixed, plus a ':== posting' alias to keep lines <= 120. Proof x-platform-proto2 runs with the same rule.")
inl note_of_x_sota_model_6 () = Note ("X-SOTA-MODEL", 6u32, "2026-11-01T19:20Z", "x", "2026-11-01 15:20 x: x-platform-proto2 (job-1793560362852, 293 s) by n4's rule: PASS. Cell budget 0/43 and 0/30. Splitter 14/14 executed and passed (rust + gleam). sim_serve compiles (it has no test blocks). PROOF 2 is n1's DoD: verify x-verify47 on the node. It also proves CELLS-IN-SPIRAL S1 (in the same tree), apps/sim/build.ps1's Ensure-SpiralCompiler fix (X-DONE n86), and x as a lib consumer after main's '::' respell (EDITION3-SYMBOLS-MIGRATION n59). RULE, written before launch: PASS iff (1) exit 0, sim notebooks 23/23 and client notebooks 43/43; (2) cell budget 0 in both trees; (3) e2e says '153 requests to the sim', the same count as the stub baseline; (4) the e2e accept reports 'accepted 0 of 37 tutorial outputs', so every replayed output, including the post-thread --dry-run steps, is byte-identical. Generated .rs/.gleam diffs for splitter are expected and land; any fixture or tutorial acceptance is a NOT MET to read first. DoD residue, stated plainly: the client build dry-runs every .spi but checks only spill, not identity, so 'byte-identical on every .spi' is attested for the tutorial's dry-run steps plus the 14 splitter tests, not file by file. After the verify, 'kit standards --census apps/client' is read on the box.")
inl note_of_x_sota_model_7 () = Note ("X-SOTA-MODEL", 7u32, "2026-11-02T00:18Z", "x", "2026-11-01 20:18 x: x-verify47 is heading for NOT MET on X-SOTA-MODEL n6 clause (1). In the second sim build, notebook sim_run FAILED 8/9: 'a_fake_run_prints_one_line_per_schedule_and_a_verdict' passed on rust in 189 s and gleam was KILLED ('Spiral cell timed out after 300s'). The cell cap sums both targets, and under node saturation the compile alone took 151 s. So it's a timeout under load, not a logic red: rust passed the same assertion. Cause in the tree: sim_run.spi was the only sim notebook without '--timeout 600000' in its header (all 19 siblings have it), so it ran on the 300 s default. FIX (unstaged): header '///- --package . --timeout 600000', matching its siblings. Clauses (3) and (4) already HELD in this run: 153 requests to the sim, accepted 0 of 37. The rest is re-proven by the next verify, which also carries the advisory gate (X-DONE n88-89).")
inl note_of_x_sota_model_8 () = Note ("X-SOTA-MODEL", 8u32, "2026-11-02T00:21Z", "x", "2026-11-01 20:21 x: CORRECTION to n7: sim_run was NOT the only sim notebook without a timeout. 10 lacked one (cells_executor, executors, final_state, memory_watch, remote_chunks, runner_guard, schedule, scheduler, sim_serve, xsim_shell_entry). The 9 that have test blocks now carry '--timeout 600000' like their 19 siblings (unstaged, header line only). sim_serve has 0 tests and keeps its header, so the migration applier's base hash A18631C29BBC still holds.")
inl note_of_x_sota_model_9 () = Note ("X-SOTA-MODEL", 9u32, "2026-11-02T00:57Z", "x", "2026-11-01 20:57 x: x-verify47 FINAL by n6's rule: NOT MET (exit 1, 5h10m on a saturated node).\n- Clauses (3) and (4) HELD: '153 requests to the sim', 'accepted 0 of 37'.\n- Clause (1) failed on 3 counts. sim_run: a timeout at the 300 s summed cap, now fixed with a header timeout (n7, n8). scenario_checks 4/12: Cells environment errors under load ('could not read file \\', 'Cannot find the package file of main.spi') plus a 600 s timeout, no assertion failure, and all 4 passed in verify46. And a REAL red from this slice in the final client gate: chunks 583 -> 1549, weights 768 -> 1297, posted 555 -> 1211 generated Rust lines (limit 1000).\n- CAUSE: those crates dispatch on a runtime platform name, so rules_of instantiated the generic pipeline (units_with, weighed_with and their listm::map closures) once per platform, tripling the shared code.\n- FIX (unstaged): the platform_rules record now carries each platform's small instance functions ({ limit; posted; glyphs; unit; padded }, filled from the prototypes in rules_with), and ONE monomorphic pipeline consumes it (units_by, weighed_by). Only the record construction is per-platform, so the prototypes and the single boundary match stay.\n- PROOF x-platform-size1 (node xqueue1), rule written before the run: PASS iff posted, weights and chunks each generate <= 1000 lines, gate.ps1 -Enforce exits 0 ('0 over 1000'), and the splitter notebook passes 14/14 on rust + gleam.\n- LESSON: compile-time dispatch multiplies code by the number of instances on any runtime-dispatched path. Keep the generic surface thin (instances return small functions) and the heavy pipeline monomorphic.")
inl note_of_x_sota_model_10 () = Note ("X-SOTA-MODEL", 10u32, "2026-11-02T01:13Z", "x", "2026-11-01 21:13 x: x-platform-size1 NOT MET: posted 1183, weights 1298, chunks 1521 (from 1211/1297/1549), gate 3 over 1000, splitter 14/14. So the monomorphic pipeline barely mattered, and n9's diagnosis was wrong. NEXT HYPOTHESIS (main's digest: ':= inlines per caller'): rules_of was ':=', so its 3-platform match with 5 closures per platform was copied into every caller (limit_on, posted_by, weighed_on and the crates that call them). It's now ':~ rules_of', a join point generated once. PROOF x-platform-size2: the 3 crates rebuilt only. PASS iff each is <= 1000. If not, the next step is to measure what the generated Rust repeats (grep closure/struct counts in chunks.rs between verify46's output and now) rather than guess again.")
inl note_of_x_sota_model_11 () = Note ("X-SOTA-MODEL", 11u32, "2026-11-02T01:19Z", "x", "2026-11-01 21:19 x: MEASURED, not guessed: generated chunks.rs went from 584 lines / 25 fns / 18 closure mentions / 5 Rc::new(move (verify46) to 1526 / 78 / 101 / 26 with the record design. The cost is the RECORD OF FUNCTIONS (the curried 3-level posting field becomes nested closures per platform), not the generic pipeline (:~ rules_of didn't help either). V3 (unstaged): no record. One small runtime match per behaviour (limit_on, posted_by, glyphs_of, unit_on, padded_on), each arm calling that platform's prototype instance with all its arguments, so it inlines without closures, plus one shared pipeline (units_on, weighed_on) over the runtime platform: the verify46 structure, with all behaviour living in the 15 instances. PROOF x-platform-size3 (size2 stopped as moot), rule written before the run: PASS iff posted, weights, chunks and frames each build <= 1000 lines and splitter passes 14/14. LESSON: on Rust, avoid records of curried closures on hot generated paths; dispatch once per behaviour into an instance call.")
inl note_of_x_sota_model_12 () = Note ("X-SOTA-MODEL", 12u32, "2026-11-02T01:28Z", "x", "2026-11-01 21:28 x: x-platform-size3 (node, 350 s) by n11's rule: PASS. posted 555 (verify46: 555), weights 764 (768), chunks 583 (583), frames 449 (449) generated Rust lines, and splitter 14/14 on rust + gleam. The V3 shape keeps all 5 prototypes and 15 instances, and the generated size is back to the pre-prototype level, because a behaviour match whose arms call instances with all their arguments inlines to the same code the old closed match produced. STATE (unstaged in x): splitter.spi V3, sim_serve.spi too_heavy on post_limit plus the 8-site erlang_text/erlang_file migration, sim notebook timeout headers (10 files), scripts/common.ps1 advisory cell-budget gate. Next: x-verify48 proves them all together, plus Cells C3/C4 and the lib registration as consumers.")
inl note_of_x_sota_model_13 () = Note ("X-SOTA-MODEL", 13u32, "2026-11-02T02:07Z", "x", "2026-11-01 22:07 x: x-verify48 launching (node, core DBC5EC18F14C354C, after lib R1b+R2 green 22:01). It PROVES every unstaged x change: the splitter V3 platform prototypes, sim_serve's 9 lib-wrapper sites, simframes' 6, the 10 sim timeout headers, the scripts/common.ps1 advisory cell-budget gate and the scripts/gate.ps1 list-sugar guard. It also proves spiral's live landings as consumers: lib erlang_* registration, Cells C3/C4, json.quote (C82278F5C371, landed 22:06) and compiler_daemon idle-exit. RULE, written before launch:\nPASS iff (1) exit 0 with all sim and client notebooks passing; (2) cell budget passes with only 'advisory' lines; (3) the client gate reports '0 over 1000' (posted/weights/chunks <= 1000); (4) e2e '153 requests to the sim' and 'accepted 0 of 37'; (5) the list-sugar line shows 0 drifted files.\nA red that is a timeout at a summed cell cap, or a Cells mount/package environment error, gets a scoped node rerun of that notebook before any verdict (n7 and verify47's lessons).")
inl note_of_x_sota_model_14 () = Note ("X-SOTA-MODEL", 14u32, "2026-11-02T02:19Z", "x", "2026-11-01 22:19 x: x-verify48 (job-1793585509485) stopped in its 7-s pre-check: 'CELL-BUDGET client / over budget (exit 1)'. Real and correctly caught by the new gate: splitter.spi:156 posted_by weighed 273 > 272, because V3's boundary match repeats 'known tagged s' in 3 arms. FIX: the parameter is renamed tag (12 chars shorter). Re-checked on the node: client exit 0 (autolink 15 advisory), sim exit 0 (autolink 5 advisory). The user committed scripts/common.ps1 + sim_run.spi at 20:19 (4b27709). Relaunching as x-verify48b under n13's unchanged rule.")
inl note_of_x_sota_model_15 () = Note ("X-SOTA-MODEL", 15u32, "2026-11-02T04:51Z", "x", "x-verify49 launched 2026-11-02 00:51 on top of main's lib dictm->dict (landed 00:41; 262 sites in 10 apps/sim/spi files edited in place). This is the first proof on core 83CEDAA9DDED49EB (box and node agree, installed 00:32); the DBC5EC18F14C354C pin was refused at '| verifying)'. Same n13 rule. verify48b had passed all 5 checks, and its 20 outputs landed at 00:2x.")
inl note_of_x_sota_model_16 () = Note ("X-SOTA-MODEL", 16u32, "2026-11-02T05:13Z", "x", "2026-11-02 01:13 x: x-verify49 FAILED the n13 rule (exit 1 at 15 min; sim build, client build and accepts all exit 1; line/sugar/budget gates green). The cause is not dictm->dict: it's pack15c's new Gleam storage FFI. Arrays and refs now call a sidecar <input stem>_ffi.erl, which the host copies under the OUTPUT stem (src/xsim_frames_ffi.erl holding -module(simframes_ffi)), so the BEAM can't load it: erl_crash 'undef simframes_ffi:array_new/2' at boot. FIX: x/apps/sim/build.ps1 Rename-FfiToModule renames *_ffi.erl/.mjs to their -module name after each Gleam compile (unstaged; a no-op once the host is fixed). The root fix (Program.fs:668-673, copy under the input stem) went to the compiler lane. x-verify50 relaunched on core 83CEDAA9DDED49EB, same rule.")
inl note_of_x_sota_model_17 () = Note ("X-SOTA-MODEL", 17u32, "2026-11-02T06:47Z", "x", "2026-11-02 02:47 x: x-verify50 was STOPPED (doesn't count): it spanned main's pack15d switch (02:36-02:42), so it mixed compilers. Up to the switch, Rename-FfiToModule renamed xsim_frames_ffi.erl/.mjs -> simframes_ffi.*, and sim accept, client build and e2e accept all exited 0 on pack15c, so the ffi diagnosis held. x-verify51 relaunched on pack15d (core F16F6B2032F81F2D, box = node), same n13 rule. Pack15d also includes compiler's host fix, so the rename should now be a no-op (expect no 'xsim / ffi' lines).")
inl note_of_x_sota_model_18 () = Note ("X-SOTA-MODEL", 18u32, "2026-11-03T07:36Z", "x", "2026-11-03 03:36 x: NEXT STEP from main's 11-03 order 6 (public iggy is live at 140.82.49.44: 3000 HTTP+TLS with the pinned CA, 8090 TCP; x-dumps issues per-machine users). x's endpoint/command GADTs are the natural carrier for iggy events. Each typed command result (posted, deleted, thread read, census row) becomes an event in LIB-IGGY-CLIENT's GADT message family (lib iggy.spi event_body / rd t), so the sim and the client publish one typed stream instead of log lines, and node runs (e2e progress, census totals) can be followed as events, not polled over ssh. EXPERIMENT FIRST: a probe client of the xsim run that publishes run-progress events to a per-machine x topic, with a follower counting them. Pass = event count equals the e2e step count, and no ssh tail is needed. If it pays off, it lands in the sim's core run loop. Owner x.")
inl note_of_x_sota_model_19 () = Note ("X-SOTA-MODEL", 19u32, "2026-11-03T12:35Z", "x", "2026-11-03 08:35 x: iggy probe PREP. Lib iggy.spi is a codec only (frame encode, bounds-checked decode to a GADT event); no transport. Least-priv users can send and poll but can't create streams or topics (IGGY-PUBLIC-CLOUD n12), and Windows schannel rejects the ED25519 CA, so the probe consumes through a rustls client like x-dumps' xarchive/events.rs. ASKED x-dumps (msg 09:07) for an x stream/topic (proposed x / run-progress, 1 partition) and an x user per machine, or OK to share the xarchive-* creds on that topic. WAITING on that reply; the probe design (publisher in the xsim e2e loop, follower count = step count) is unchanged from n18.")
inl note_of_x_sota_model_20 () = Note ("X-SOTA-MODEL", 20u32, "2026-11-03T12:55Z", "x", "2026-11-03 08:55 x: IGGY PROBE drafted (kit drafts x\\X-SOTA-MODEL). x-dumps created stream 'x' (id 1) topic 'run-progress' (1 partition) on the public iggy; the probe reuses the least-priv xarchive-* creds (~/.secrets/iggy.env, send+poll only); dedicated xc-* users come only if the probe graduates. NEW sub-crate apps/client/events (xc_events, the census pattern): typed run_event { run; line } whose JSON payload starts with its run, so a follower counts a run by prefix (2 zig-free rust tests); iggy over HTTP with ureq 3.5 + rustls trusting IGGY_CA_FILE (TlsConfig root_certs = Certificate::from_pem(..).ok().into(), signatures read from the node's ureq-3.5.0 source), login token, base64 payload POST, offset-paged poll. One export xc_events(payload); xc gains 'events publish RUN LINE' and 'events count RUN' (thread_op Events, kind 13 through new small siblings events_or_unknown, more_text, more_payload, run_post_or_events, so no existing cell grows; xc.rs was 985/1000). Bases: spiral.toml 343D66DA2811, package.spiproj E8E743313695, cli.spi 1DEF3FB997C7, fields.spi F91F4D8F7006, xc.spi D2A31DF9633F, xc Cargo.toml 08E150A139E9. PRE-REGISTERED RULE for x-events1 (node, pinned core at both ends): sim accept 0 with no fixture changed, sim build 0, client build 0 (1k gate + CELL-BUDGET), e2e RUST-OK with no FAILED; every 'x e2e / tutorial /' line is published live from the e2e pipeline under run x-events1-<unix>, and 'xc events count' equals the tutorial step count (>= 30); census equality SAME; usage prints the postable census line. If iggy is unreachable, the build half still counts and the probe half is re-run alone.")
inl note_of_x_sota_model_21 () = Note ("X-SOTA-MODEL", 21u32, "2026-11-03T15:01Z", "x", "2026-11-03 11:01 x: x-events1 NOT GREEN in 46 s, two causes and neither is the events crate. (1) Peer WIP in the synced box tree: lib/spiral/file_system.spi carries 219 uncommitted lines (C file mapping, read_f32s) whose rust_write_bytes fails to typecheck at 1050 (array_base vs array u8), which broke the lib gate and the sim accept. The launcher now resets only that file to HEAD in the node mirror, never on the box, and logs it. (2) Mine: simframes.spi:117 with_meters used cells_executor::beam_mb, which the sim-erlang slice moved to lib (erlang_process::beam_memory_mb). simframes is now in the draft set (FE7E109CE137 -> A515EFDAF767), pointing beam_mb at the lib function directly. Grep of the whole sim found no other user of the removed helpers. Relaunched as x-events2 under the same n20 rule.")
inl note_of_x_sota_model_22 () = Note ("X-SOTA-MODEL", 22u32, "2026-11-03T15:55Z", "x", "2026-11-03 11:55 x: x-events2 NOT GREEN (job-1793717959981, 52 min), but further: lib gate 20/20 (erlang_term + erlang_process, both targets), sim accept 0 (sim-stress fixtures regenerated, expected because the stress world posts the client's own sources and this batch edits cli/fields/posts and adds events), sim build 0. The client build compiled; its e2e failed 2 tutorial steps, 'xc --help' and 'xc timeline --per-account 500 alice' (both print the usage block), because README.md's tutorial outputs predate the new 'postable census' usage line. That is the e2e -Accept round POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS n177 predicted; I wrongly assumed the build regenerates those README blocks. LAUNCHER: when the client build fails with xc.exe built, run e2e -Accept once, print the accepted count and the README diff, then the client build must exit 0. RULE addition (pre-registered): exactly those 2 tutorial outputs change, and the README diff is only the added census usage line in each. The probe half was not reached; x-events3 relaunches with the same rule.")
inl note_of_x_sota_model_23 () = Note ("X-SOTA-MODEL", 23u32, "2026-11-03T17:22Z", "x", "2026-11-03 13:22 x: IGGY PROBE PASSED (x-events3, job-1793721801619, pinned core 21D5992EDB80D270; the installed core moved to 7C49B7A6AFC05D2C mid-run, which the pin made irrelevant). Every pre-registered condition held: lib gate 20/20; sim accept 0 (sim-stress fixtures regenerated, expected); sim build 0; the e2e -Accept round changed exactly the 2 predicted README outputs, each by only the census usage line; client build after accept 0; e2e RUST-OK 37 steps + 5 cases. PROBE: run x-events3-1793726097 published all 37 'x e2e / tutorial' lines LIVE from the e2e pipeline (37 x HTTP 201 on stream x / run-progress over rustls with IGGY_CA_FILE), and 'xc events count' read back 37 of 37, so a node run can be followed as typed events with no ssh tail. LANDED unstaged: apps/client/events (events.spi FA132C488330, Cargo.toml), spiral.toml, package.spiproj, cli.spi, fields.spi, xc.spi, xc Cargo.toml, plus 27 outputs (events.rs 479 lines, xc.rs 992 of 1,000, README, Cargo.lock with xc_events, sim-stress fixtures). NEXT for n18's experiment: move the publisher into the sim's run loop with a typed event family (LIB-IGGY-CLIENT GADT), dedicated xc-* iggy users then (x-dumps offered). xc.rs at 992 means the next xc change needs a sub-crate or a split.")
inl note_of_x_sota_model_24 () = Note ("X-SOTA-MODEL", 24u32, "2026-11-03T17:35Z", "x", "2026-11-03 13:35 x: n18 EXPERIMENT VERDICT: PAYS OFF. Cross-machine follow proven: on the BOX, with the box's own least-priv user (xarchive-box via ~/.secrets/iggy.env, rustls trusting iggy-ca.crt) and the xc built by x-events3, 'xc events count x-events3-1793726097' answered 'counted 37' in 3.6 s, the node run's 37 tutorial steps, with no ssh and no tail. Both pass conditions from n18 hold: the event count equals the e2e step count, and another machine follows the run. DECISION, by n18's rule ('if it pays off, it lands in the sim's core run loop'): next slice moves the publisher from the launcher pipeline into the sim/e2e run loop with a typed event family instead of free-text lines (step started / step done with exit / run finished with totals, as a closed union, encoded through lib iggy's codec rather than JSON-with-prefix). Ask x-dumps for dedicated xc-box / xc-t1101 users at that point (offered in their 09:3x reply).")
inl note_of_x_sota_model_25 () = Note ("X-SOTA-MODEL", 25u32, "2026-11-03T17:40Z", "x", "2026-11-03 13:40 x: TYPED EVENT FAMILY drafted (kit drafts x\\X-SOTA-MODEL\\v2, on landed bases events.spi FA132C488330 -> 207B27F1FD72, cli.spi 76D62510542C -> 59342176BCB5). The free-text run_event record becomes a closed union run_signal = Step of string | Finished of (i32 * i32) (steps, failures); each payload starts with its run and kind ({\\\"run\\\":R,\\\"kind\\\":\\\"step\\\"|\\\"finished\\\",...}), so the follower counts Step payloads by prefix and finished_in sees the Finished. The pager reads all pages once (payloads_from); the count line reads 'counted N' or 'counted N, finished'. New verb: xc events finish RUN STEPS FAILURES (cli events_command accepts publish, finish or count; the rejection text and its zig test are updated). Two rust blocks: a step payload's exact text, and counting steps for r1 but not r10 while seeing only r1's finish. Every cell is under 257. PRE-REGISTERED for its run (x-next3, after x-next2): client build + CELL-BUDGET, e2e RUST-OK, every tutorial step published as a Step, then 'xc events finish RUN <steps> 0', and the box follower reads 'counted <steps>, finished'. This is the typed contract the sim run loop will publish next.")
inl note_of_x_sota_model_26 () = Note ("X-SOTA-MODEL", 26u32, "2026-11-03T20:38Z", "x", "2026-11-03 16:38 x: PLAN for the sim-loop publisher (the n24 decision), from the tree. Lib has NO Erlang HTTP client wrapper (no httpc, ssl or inets in lib/spiral), and the sim only uses gen_tcp as a server. Steps: (1) lib erlang_http (or a section in erlang_port's neighbor): typed post_json (url, bearer, body) -> http_outcome = Replied of (i32 * string) | Unreachable of string over OTP httpc, with ssl options {verify, verify_peer} and {cacertfile, IGGY_CA_FILE}. OTP 26+ ssl verifies ED25519 chains, unlike schannel. Gleam block against a local gen_tcp responder (no network in tests). (2) sim run loop: publish run_signal Step per scenario step and Finished at the end, with the SAME payload text as events.spi v2 (one codec; move payload_of and kind_prefix to a shared module both sides open). (3) proof: an e2e run where the sim publishes and the box follower reads 'counted N, finished' with N the scenario steps. Ask x-dumps for xc-box / xc-t1101 users at step 3. Depends on x-next3 (the typed family) landing first.")
inl note_of_x_sota_model_27 () = Note ("X-SOTA-MODEL", 27u32, "2026-11-03T22:42Z", "x", "2026-11-03 18:42 x: TYPED EVENT FAMILY LANDED, green in x-next3b (job-1793743112574, pinned core 2ABF22D79E6B6943): sim accept 0 (sim-stress fixtures regenerated), sim build 0, client build 0 (CELL-BUDGET, 1k gate), e2e RUST-OK 37 + 5, census SAME over 76 files. Run x-next3b-1793745070: 37 Step signals published (37 x 201), then 'xc events finish' (201), and the count read 'counted 37, finished' of 37 tutorial steps, exactly the pre-registered line. events.spi 855E51FFDB96 and cli.spi 59342176BCB5 are landed with outputs. NEXT: the sim-loop publisher (n26 plan); its lib prerequisite erlang_http is drafted and rides x-cells-mpath1.")
inl note_of_x_sota_model_28 () = Note ("X-SOTA-MODEL", 28u32, "2026-11-04T05:20Z", "x", "2026-11-04 01:20 x: SIM-LOOP PUBLISHER, step (1) DONE: lib erlang_http landed green tonight (posted_json -> Replied of i32*string | Unreachable of string; LIB-ERLANG-TYPED-STDLIB note). Step (2a) DRAFTED, the ONE codec both sides open (kit drafts x\\X-SOTA-MODEL): new apps/client/run_signals.spi (8AC106845390, 47 lines, max cell 263): run_signal, run_prefix, kind_prefix, signal_body, kind_of, payload_of, count_of_run, finished_in, message_body (the iggy publish body the sim needs too), plus the 2 codec tests moved from events. events/events.spi 855E51FFDB96 -> 5B819351C560 (160 -> 124 lines) opens run_signals and keeps only the follower's page_query and the Rust ureq transport. Registered after feed_merge in BOTH client lists (spiral.toml, package.spiproj). json::quote has a non-Rust arm, so the codec compiles on Gleam for the sim. Next: (2b) the sim run loop calls erlang_http::posted_json with run_signals::message_body per Step and on Finished; (3) the e2e proof via the box follower ('counted N, finished'). (2a) rides the next x run after x-next5b lands, gated by the notebook gate on run_signals + events, with client build and e2e RUST-OK unchanged.")
inl note_of_x_sota_model_29 () = Note ("X-SOTA-MODEL", 29u32, "2026-11-04T05:27Z", "x", "2026-11-04 01:27 x: SIM-LOOP PUBLISHER step (2b) DRAFTED. (a) sim: new apps/sim/spi/sim_publish.spi (kit drafts x\\X-SOTA-MODEL, 168010955E81, 54 lines, max cell 272): opens run_signals (the one codec), signed_in () -> option iggy (POST IGGY_URL/users/login; None when IGGY_URL is unset, so offline and test runs never touch the network), posted s run signal -> status (topic run-progress, Bearer, body = message_body (base64 (payload_of run signal))). 3 Gleam tests: the token from a login reply, only a 200 opens a session, and a step body carries the shared payload in base64. (b) lib, 2 small additions on LIB-ERLANG-TYPED-STDLIB: erlang_text base64_encoded/decoded (B0AFD99722C6 -> 2BEAE7AF40FA, round-trip test with UTF-8) and erlang_http replied_status/replied_body accessors (479D224C37C4 -> 58A2BB9C1A17, so consumers branch on values and never match a foreign constructor; no x code does that today). x-next6 = run_signals + events split + client lists + sim_publish (registered after timeline_serve, rebased on x-next5b's landed sim lists) + the 2 lib drafts, gated by notebook runs on each new module, client build, sim build/accept and e2e RUST-OK. Launches after x-next5b lands. (2c), the run loop calling posted per step and on finish, comes after that.")
inl note_of_x_sota_model_30 () = Note ("X-SOTA-MODEL", 30u32, "2026-11-04T05:29Z", "x", "2026-11-04 01:29 x: (2c) HOOK DESIGN, read from sim_run.spi (98C2FB6D8844): outcomes_from loops schedules 1..N and each schedule_run_of yields a schedule_outcome with a 'line'; with_closing adds closing_line (the verdict). Only executed_text (L250, the CLI path through executor_pair_of) runs real executors; simulated (L248, the tests) uses fake_pair. DECISION v1: hook in executed_text AFTER the lines exist: when sim_publish::signed_in () is Some, post Step line for each schedule line, then Finished (schedules, failures), where failures = the outcomes with a non-empty 'found'. simulated and its tests stay pure and offline. COST of v1: a follower sees the steps at the end of the run, not live. LIVE v2 (only if the user wants live progress): an on_line callback in executor_pair, which touches all 5 pair constructors (fake, fake_async, cells, leak, picked). Run id: XSIM_RUN_ID from the environment, else 'xsim-' plus the start stamp. Rides x-next7 after x-next6's codec and publisher land.")
inl note_of_x_sota_model_31 () = Note ("X-SOTA-MODEL", 31u32, "2026-11-04T05:45Z", "x", "2026-11-04 01:45 x: x-next6 PREPARED (launches only after x-next5b lands; its base checks expect x-next5b's sim versions live). 12 files: run_signals (new), events split, client spiral.toml + package.spiproj (run_signals after feed_merge), sim_publish (new), sim lists (sim_publish after timeline_serve; DBF8BD253BBF / 06F100F80721), sim_serve (crypto + base64 via lib), cells_executor (erlang_tcp), and 3 lib files (erlang_text base64, erlang_http accessors, erlang_tcp receive_line_within). PRE-REGISTERED RULE: sim build 0, client build 0, sim accept 0 with every e2e fixture byte-identical (the codec moves without changing a byte of payload text, and the sim publishes nothing without IGGY_URL), e2e RUST-OK, census SAME, AND the notebook gate exit 0 with failed=0 over run_signals, sim_publish, erlang_text, erlang_http and erlang_tcp. Else FAIL. LAND (land-x-next6): lib first through lib_land_window (must close green, else the x files are NOT landed), then the 9 x files. Every draft and lib hash is pinned at proof time; a changed draft refuses.")
inl note_of_x_sota_model_32 () = Note ("X-SOTA-MODEL", 32u32, "2026-11-04T05:56Z", "x", "2026-11-04 01:56 x: (2c) DRAFTED for x-next7 (kit drafts x\\X-SOTA-MODEL-2c). Placement forced by module order: sim_run is listed BEFORE sim_serve/sim_publish, so it can't call them; simframes (last) can. simframes.spi run_command (the CLI path only) pipes sim_run::simulated_text into sim_progress::published_text (30C136F73252 -> 835CC894AF9B). The Cells-harness entry simulated_lines is untouched, so harness runs never publish. New sim_progress.spi (D2D93387A833, 44 lines, max cell 253) opens run_signals; sim_run can't, because its open of the sim's own 'events' has kind_of, and the sim also defines finished_in and Finished. published_text posts one Step per 'XSIM seed=' line, then Finished (steps, failures), when signed_in is Some, and returns the text unchanged. Run id = XSIM_RUN_ID, else 'xsim'. V1 LIMIT, stated not hidden: schedule lines carry no failure flag, so failures = 1 if the verdict says result=fail, else 0 (not a per-schedule count). Tests: schedule_lines_are_the_steps, the_verdict_decides_failure. Sim lists: sim_progress after sim_publish, on x-next6's pinned lists (7B7D0A8FD384 / 61DD2E4F6D7E).")
inl note_of_x_sota_model_33 () = Note ("X-SOTA-MODEL", 33u32, "2026-11-04T05:58Z", "x", "2026-11-04 01:58 x: x-next7 PREPARED (launch-x-next7.ps1 + land-x-next7.ps1; 4 files: sim_progress new, simframes hook, sim lists on x-next6's; draft hashes pinned; it refuses until x-next5b and x-next6 have landed). PRE-REGISTERED RULE (offline proof): sim build 0, client build 0, sim accept 0 with every e2e fixture byte-identical (the node job has no IGGY_URL, so published_text posts nothing and returns the text unchanged; any fixture diff = the hook changed output = FAIL), e2e RUST-OK, census SAME, notebook gate on sim_progress exit 0 with failed=0. THEN step (3), the live proof, needs credentials and is a separate run: XSIM_RUN_ID=<id> plus IGGY_URL, IGGY_USERNAME, IGGY_PASSWORD and IGGY_CA_FILE in the job only; the box follower 'xc events count <id>' must read 'counted N, finished' with N = that run's XSIM seed= lines. I'll ask x-dumps for the box user then (per the n24 plan).")
inl note_of_x_sota_model_34 () = Note ("X-SOTA-MODEL", 34u32, "2026-11-04T06:21Z", "x", "2026-11-04 02:21 x: VULTR TYPE-CHECKS (pack33, idle vultr, stage fixed: it had lacked lib's .spir files and spiral.toml, which voided every earlier vultr check) over the stacked drafts, each in its true layered context (x-next5b drafts, then x-next6, then x-next7/8 overlays): x-next6 8/8 exit 0 (erlang_text, erlang_http, erlang_tcp, run_signals, events, sim_publish, sim_serve, cells_executor); x-next7 + x-next8 4/4 exit 0 (sim_progress, simframes hook, raw erlang_tcp, sim_serve on raw tcp). So x-next6..8 can no longer die on a type error the way x-next5 did; the node runs now test behaviour (accept fixtures, e2e, notebooks), not typing.")
inl note_of_x_sota_model_35 () = Note ("X-SOTA-MODEL", 35u32, "2026-11-04T10:09Z", "x", "2026-11-04 06:09 x: RE-CHECKED after LIB-SPIRAL-TOML-MIGRATION, on a new-layout lib stage (modules from files, the doc batch and list_sort landed) with vultr's newest compiler (pack34), each in its true stacked context: x-next6 8/8 exit 0 (erlang_text, erlang_http, erlang_tcp, run_signals, events, sim_publish, sim_serve, cells_executor); x-next7 + x-next8 4/4 exit 0 (raw erlang_tcp, sim_progress, sim_serve on raw tcp + per-author timelines, simframes hook). The queue needs no changes for the lib migration.")
inl note_of_x_sota_model_36 () = Note ("X-SOTA-MODEL", 36u32, "2026-11-04T11:02Z", "x", "2026-11-04 07:02 x: GROOM (main's 07:1x hurdles round): 45 -> 50. The sim-loop publisher's step (1), lib erlang_http, LANDED (04-11 01:0x); steps (2a codec split, 2b sim_publish, 2c hook) are drafted, vultr-checked on the new lib layout and queued in x-next6/7. MODEL INPUT from x-dumps' battle test of the typed model on the real 163K corpus (xarchive typed): (1) the shape union must carry Reply.to as option post_id, not post_id: 22,129 of 50,213 replies (44%) have an EMPTY parent id in the archive (reply known, parent tweet unknown); Quote.target likely the same; Repost.by is empty on only 300 (keep it required; empty = a data gap). (2) Real fate distribution: standing 5,010 / removed 153,437 (geygey's suspension: 125K posts removed from X but archived) / lost 4,860, so most of the joined scroll carries a removal note, and the route renders it COMPACTLY. posts.spi's survival extension (x-dumps, rides x-next6) is unaffected; 'shape' is the next model step.")
inl note_of_x_sota_model_37 () = Note ("X-SOTA-MODEL", 37u32, "2026-11-04T11:21Z", "x", "2026-11-04 07:21 x: SHAPE union DRAFTED (kit drafts x\\X-SOTA-SHAPE\\posts.spi 5B28E2FB16A4, stacked on x-dumps' survival model in x-next6): :== reply = { to : option post_id; to_user : option user_id } (to is an option because 44% of real replies lack a parent id, per x-dumps' corpus test; no '' sentinels); :| shape = Original | Reply : reply | Repost : user_id | Quote : option post_id; shape_of (k : raw_kind) maps the raw row (empty -> None via present). Payload per case, so a repost can't carry reply fields. Tests: a_reply_without_a_parent_keeps_its_user, reposts_and_quotes_keep_their_target, any_other_kind_is_an_original_post. Renamed my reply_of -> reply_fields because wire/assemble/fetch/refs (which open posts) define their own reply_of (the shadowing trap). Vultr --check exit 0 for posts and those 4 openers. Rides the run after x-next6.")
inl note_of_x_sota_model_38 () = Note ("X-SOTA-MODEL", 38u32, "2026-11-04T11:37Z", "x", "2026-11-04 07:37 x: HEAL QUEUED (x-dumps flagged; the no-comments rule keeps only '///>' directives): explanatory '/// ##' heading lines are comments. x has 10 live (census.spi 4, events.spi 3, feed_merge.spi 1, posts.spi 1, timeline_serve.spi 1), all from x's own 11-03/04 lands, and 17 queued draft files carry them. Plan: x-next6 lands as proven (its drafts are pinned), then ONE strip pass over every explanatory '///' line in x/apps (live + all queued drafts, rebased) rides x-next7; formatter and cell budget re-measured (removing lines only shrinks cells). Memory no-comments-self-explanatory updated with this slip.")
inl note_of_x_sota_model_39 () = Note ("X-SOTA-MODEL", 39u32, "2026-11-04T11:41Z", "x", "2026-11-04 07:41 x: HEADING STRIP, part 1 (unfrozen drafts): explanatory '///' lines removed from sim_progress (x-next7, re-pinned 145E92FDC313 in land-x-next7), timeline_page, the timeline route core, the shape draft of posts (incl. x-dumps' 2) and x-next8's sim_serve; '///>' test targets and '///-' package headers kept; formatted; 0 cells over 272 in any. Part 2 after x-next6 lands: live x (census, events, feed_merge, posts, timeline_serve) plus x-next6's frozen drafts, riding x-next7.")
inl note_of_x_sota_model_40 () = Note ("X-SOTA-MODEL", 40u32, "2026-11-04T12:13Z", "x", "2026-11-04 08:13 x: HEADING STRIP, part 2a: the live x files outside x-next6 have their explanatory '///' lines removed and ride x-next7 (now 25 files, all pinned): census.spi C10FA3E6E688 -> BB1A1F8D3A29 (4 lines) and feed_merge.spi EB3A98054CC9 -> CFAE69F0CE07 (1 line); vultr --check exit 0, 0 cells over 272. Left: events (3) and posts (1, plus x-dumps' 2) after x-next6 lands; timeline_serve's next version (the route draft) is already stripped.")
inl note_of_x_sota_model_41 () = Note ("X-SOTA-MODEL", 41u32, "2026-11-04T12:41Z", "x", "2026-11-04 08:41 x: RECLAIMED HANDLE (x-dumps found: two distinct accounts are both 'fc1943s', id 72784485 from 2009 and id 1637273636049498112 from 2023). Checked each layer. Grouping: one feed per user term, never merged. Cursor: page_cursor.feeds holds account ids (the live sample is exactly those two ids). Display WAS the gap, both shown as '@fc1943s'. Route draft X-TIMELINE-ROUTE timeline_serve.spi B5CAB4196FB8 -> 15C70F29EDCD: label_of appends ' · <id>' only when more than one account holds the handle (case-insensitive); feed_of now takes the user list (feed_of users u, no callers yet; x-dumps' GET /timeline entry is the first); new test a_reclaimed_handle_labels_each_account_by_id; vultr --check 0, 0 cells over 272.")
inl note_of_x_sota_model_42 () = Note ("X-SOTA-MODEL", 42u32, "2026-11-04T12:54Z", "x", "2026-11-04 08:54 x: GET /timeline WIRED (x-dumps authored the entry; x placed it). x-next9 stack, all drafts, vultr --check 0 for all 4 modules on x-check9 (= x-checkfull78 + these): client lists + timeline_page (X-TIMELINE-PAGE); route timeline_serve (X-TIMELINE-ROUTE C2A6C2C0FFEB): following, cursor_ids, resumed_cursor (offsets resume only for the same account ids, else page 1), next_link (none on an empty page), page_for (50 per page), feed_page, feed_reply (HTML via lib erlang_http_server::response_text 'text/html'), feed_or_api (answers /timeline, else sim_serve::replied), serve; 3 new tests incl. an end-to-end the_timeline_path_serves_an_html_page. No module cycle: X-TIMELINE-ENTRY sim_serve (EC7A52B45146, on x-next8's E81668CFA086) takes the replier as a parameter (serve_with); simframes (F4A48C58FE03, on x-next7's 835CC894AF9B) exports cli_serve = timeline_serve::serve. Order: x-next6, x-next7, x-httpwire1 lib, x-next8, then x-next9.")
inl note_of_x_sota_model_43 () = Note ("X-SOTA-MODEL", 43u32, "2026-11-04T13:00Z", "x", "2026-11-04 09:00 x: QUADRATIC SORT REMOVED before x-replay3 needs it. sim_serve newest_first was an insertion-sort fold (O(n^2) per timeline request; the joined world has an account with 122111 posts). Now list_sort::sorted_by (\\ a b => not (newer_or_same b a)), the stable merge sort in lib. It is the SAME order: newer_or_same is a total preorder (stamp, then longer/greater id), the fold put each post after every newer-or-same one so equal posts keep input order, and a stable sort with the strict predicate does exactly that. New test newest_first_orders_by_time_then_longer_id. In x-next9's sim_serve (X-TIMELINE-ENTRY, EC7A52B45146 -> 81C2EB19E1DB, land pin updated); x-next9's rule includes byte-identical sim fixtures, which proves the order on every existing scenario. vultr --check 0 (sim_serve, timeline_serve).")
inl note_of_x_sota_model_44 () = Note ("X-SOTA-MODEL", 44u32, "2026-11-04T13:03Z", "x", "2026-11-04 09:03 x: SECOND QUADRATIC SORT REMOVED: search's found_posts folded every matching post of the world (163307 in the joined world) through by_id_desc, another insertion sort. Now list_sort::sorted_by on 'greater id_order first'. Same order: results come from by_post (keyed by post id), so no two share an id_order and there are no ties to keep. New test found_ids_sort_longer_then_greater_first. sim_serve now has 0 insertion sorts. x-next9 sim_serve -> 587AE9BF2462 (land pin updated), vultr --check 0 (sim_serve, timeline_serve). x-replay3 launches AFTER x-next9 so it times the sorted paths.")
inl note_of_x_sota_model_45 () = Note ("X-SOTA-MODEL", 45u32, "2026-11-04T13:04Z", "x", "2026-11-04 09:04 x: REQUEST-PATH SCAN of x-next9's sim_serve (587AE9BF2462) after the two sort fixes: no other superlinear step. sliced/deduped/followed/found_users/missing_users are single passes with dict lookups; the length calls are once per request; the receive loop's 'acc ++# chunk' rebuilds the buffer per chunk but requests are small GETs and short POST bodies. Remaining by design: each timeline request sorts that user's posts (O(n log n), about 2M comparisons for the 122111-post account). x-replay3's per-request timing decides; if the sort dominates, the lever is sorting once per user at world load (with_posts) instead of per request.")
inl note_of_x_sota_model_46 () = Note ("X-SOTA-MODEL", 46u32, "2026-11-04T14:23Z", "x", "2026-11-04 10:23 x: x-next6 (job-1793793481938, still running) has ALREADY FAILED its pre-registered rule: 'sim accept 0 with every e2e fixture byte-identical', and accept changed sim-stress.world.json (68470 -> 65447 bytes, 133 -> 126 posts) and sim-stress.expected.json (23753 -> 27208). It will NOT land, whatever the later stages show. CAUSE, x's error: sim-stress is generated from apps/client/posts.spi (memory x-sim-fixtures-embed-client-sources), and x-dumps' posts.spi joined x-next6 AFTER the 05:45 rule was written; the rule was never updated. Evidence the change tracks posts.spi: the node world's post bodies match the DRAFT posts.spi better (20 framing misses) than the live one (33), while the box world matches the live one (16). That is consistent, not proof. RE-RUN RULE, registered NOW before any re-run data: one job, two sim -Accept arms on the same mirror. Arm A = x-next6 with the LIVE posts.spi: every fixture byte-identical to the committed ones. Arm B = x-next6 as drafted: the ONLY changed fixtures are sim-stress.world.json and sim-stress.expected.json, and they byte-equal this failed run's node copies (B3BB663AED15 expected, 67484404D522 world, saved in x's scratchpad); plus sim build 0, client build 0, e2e RUST-OK, census SAME, notebook gate failed=0 over run_signals, sim_publish, erlang_text, erlang_http, erlang_tcp and posts. Else FAIL. The current run continues for its other stages' evidence.")
inl note_of_x_sota_model_47 () = Note ("X-SOTA-MODEL", 47u32, "2026-11-04T14:25Z", "x", "2026-11-04 10:25 x: x-next6b LAUNCHER READY (scratchpad launch-x-next6b.ps1, from launch-x-next6 with only the accept step changed; outer and remote layers parse 0). It stages the live posts.spi as an extra file, runs arm A's sim -Accept with it (prints 'arm A changed fixtures'), then copies the draft posts.spi and runs arm B's accept (prints 'arm B changed fixtures' and the sim-stress world/expected hashes). 'A/B fixture rule held' only when A changed nothing and B changed exactly sim-stress.expected.json + sim-stress.world.json at 67484404D522 / B3BB663AED15; otherwise exit 9. All other stages are x-next6's unchanged. Launches when x-next6's job ends, because both use mirror xqueue3 and a sync would rewrite files under the running job. On pass: land-x-next6.ps1 -Apply -Label x-next6b.")
inl note_of_x_sota_model_48 () = Note ("X-SOTA-MODEL", 48u32, "2026-11-04T14:26Z", "x", "2026-11-04 10:26 x: RULE AUDIT of every queued run against the fixture sources (apps/sim/build.ps1:82-108): prelude.spi feeds sim-small, sim-lost, sim-gap and prelude.events.jsonl/final.json; posts.spi feeds sim-stress; oauth1.spi feeds thread-oauth1 (and apps/client/e2e/world.json is hand-seeded with checks.spi text). x-next7 (26 files), x-next8 (sim_serve + lib erlang_tcp) and x-next9 (client lists, timeline_page, route, sim_serve, simframes) touch NONE of these, so their 'every fixture byte-identical' rules stand as registered. The ONE planned edit that does: the heading strip of posts.spi (part 2, after x-next6b lands). Its run's rule must be the same A/B form as x-next6b's (live posts.spi arm byte-identical; stripped arm changes only the sim-stress pair), registered before launch.")
inl note_of_x_sota_model_49 () = Note ("X-SOTA-MODEL", 49u32, "2026-11-04T15:18Z", "x", "2026-11-04 11:18 x: HEADING STRIP part 2b: events.spi's 2 explanatory '///' lines removed ON x-next6's frozen draft (5B819351C560 -> 0AB6A7981802, kit drafts x\\X-HEAL-AFTER-NEXT6), vultr --check 0; rides x-next7 (now 27 files), whose base pin 5B819351C560 means it launches only after x-next6b lands events. events is not a fixture source, so x-next7's byte-identical rule stands. cells_executor needs nothing (x-next6's version has 0 trailing-lambda sites). Left of the strip: posts.spi (x-dumps' 2 + x's 1), which needs the A/B rule.")
inl note_of_x_sota_model_50 () = Note ("X-SOTA-MODEL", 50u32, "2026-11-04T15:29Z", "x", "2026-11-04 11:29 x: x-next6 STOPPED by x during its notebook gate, verdict unchanged (FAILED its registered fixture rule; never lands). Evidence it gave before the stop: sim accept 0 (sim-stress pair changed by posts.spi), sim build 0, client build 0, e2e RUST-OK 37 + 5 on the regenerated fixtures, Rust units under 1000 (digest 995, xc 992), census SAME over 79 files with 0 over 272. Its last stage (the notebook gate over posts, run_signals, sim_publish, erlang_text, erlang_http, erlang_tcp) is repeated identically by x-next6b, so the stop only moves x-next6b's start ~30+ min earlier. x-next6b LAUNCHED now (A/B fixture rule as registered 10:2x).")
inl note_of_x_sota_model_51 () = Note ("X-SOTA-MODEL", 51u32, "2026-11-04T18:57Z", "x", "2026-11-04 14:57 x: x-next6b (job-1793806692356, pinned core 21F39A40A549E6E6) A/B FIXTURE RULE HELD: arm A (live posts.spi) sim accept exit 0 with NO fixture changed; arm B (x-dumps' draft posts.spi 5AE47672CF27) sim accept exit 0 changing exactly sim-stress.expected.json and sim-stress.world.json, at world 67484404D522 / expected B3BB663AED15, byte-equal to the failed x-next6's copies. So the fixture change is attributable to posts.spi alone. Remaining stages (sim build, client build, e2e, census, notebook gate) are still running; the run's verdict is PENDING until they report.")
inl note_of_x_sota_model_52 () = Note ("X-SOTA-MODEL", 52u32, "2026-11-04T19:53Z", "x", "2026-11-04 15:53 x: x-next6b TIMED OUT (wall-clock 4 h, job-1793806692356, state=timed-out exit 124) INSIDE its notebook gate, the last stage. Everything before it passed: A/B fixture rule held, sim build 0, client build 0, e2e RUST-OK 37 + 5, Rust units all under 1000, census SAME over 79 with 0 over 272. VERDICT: INCOMPLETE, NOT a pass: the rule requires the notebook gate with failed=0 and it never reported, and its outputs were not saved (that step came after the notebooks). Nothing landed. x-next6c LAUNCHED (same pre-registered rule, same pinned drafts), with two mechanical changes only: wall-clock limit 6 h (the node is at 100%), and outputs saved BEFORE the notebook gate so a slow gate cannot cost the land its outputs again.")
inl note_of_x_sota_model_53 () = Note ("X-SOTA-MODEL", 53u32, "2026-11-04T20:40Z", "x", "2026-11-04 16:40 x: x-nb6 DIAGNOSTIC (job-1793822455362, mirror xcells1, same pinned x-next6 drafts, core 21F39A40A549E6E6; NOT a substitute for x-next6c's own gate): the six notebooks x-next6b timed out in all pass, each in a fresh process: posts 17/17 in 721 s, run_signals 370 s, sim_publish 402 s, erlang_text 323 s, erlang_http 262 s, erlang_tcp 4/4 in 255 s; all exit 0, ~39 min total on the saturated node. So x-next6b's timeout was slowness, not a hang: its earlier stages had used ~3.3 of 4 h. x-next6c (6 h limit, outputs saved before the gate) should complete; its verdict stays its own.")
inl note_of_x_sota_model_54 () = Note ("X-SOTA-MODEL", 54u32, "2026-11-04T21:32Z", "x", "2026-11-04 17:32 x: x-next6c (job-1793822296502) FAILED its pre-registered A/B rule at arm A: with the LIVE posts.spi, accept changed every generated fixture (sim-gap, sim-lost, sim-small, sim-stress world+expected), where x-next6b's arm A at 12:00 changed none with the same pinned drafts. CAUSE is not the x drafts but x's own Cells land at 13:43 (CELLS-TEST-TIMEOUT-DIRECTIVE): its split_directives absorbed a file-level '///- --package . --timeout 600000' line into the following ':# prelude static =' module header, so prelude.spi (and every file with that shape) splits differently (sim-small post p0 went from 2 cells to 1). Fix landing now (x-cellspkg1: a ///- row containing --package stays its own cell; regression test with the exact prelude shape). After it closes green, x-next6 re-runs as x-next6d with the same rule. The rest of x-next6c continues but cannot change its verdict.")
inl note_of_x_sota_model_55 () = Note ("X-SOTA-MODEL", 55u32, "2026-11-04T22:56Z", "x", "2026-11-04 18:56 x: FINDING for x-next9 (from the scratch demo build for x-dumps/user, NOT a node proof): the full stacked set (x-next6 + x-next7 sim lists/sim_progress/simframes + x-next8 lib erlang_tcp + x-next9 page/route/entry) compiles to Gleam (simframes 103 s) but gleam-modules splits xsim_frames into 44 modules with the largest at 1186 lines (part22), over the 1000-line gate. xsim_split stays under (957). So x-next9 cannot pass its build as drafted: something in the route/entry stack must be cut at a source seam before it lands (candidates: the timeline route + page code joining sim_serve's module, or sim_serve itself, which the earlier sim_wire plan targeted). The demo build raises the limit only in its scratch copy (xdemo), which never lands.")
inl note_of_x_sota_model_56 () = Note ("X-SOTA-MODEL", 56u32, "2026-11-04T23:05Z", "x", "2026-11-04 19:05 x: /timeline DEMO for the user (via x-dumps), built in a SCRATCH copy (scratchpad xdemo: x copy + spiral with lib/ copied and every other top-level dir junctioned; live repos untouched; nothing lands from it). Overlaid the vultr-checked x-next6..9 set (14 x drafts + lib erlang_text/http/tcp; every hash matched). Smoke test on the hand e2e world.json: GET /timeline 200 text/html with the rendered feed; API path intact (GET /2/users/by 401 without a bearer). Two MORE x-next9 blockers the demo surfaced, both bypassed only in the scratch copy: (a) one generated Gleam part is 1186 lines over the 1000 gate (noted earlier); (b) the frames Gleam project does not declare envoy (gleam add envoy), which x-next6's sim_publish needs for env reads. And a sim robustness gap: world_of crashes (badmap on refresh_token) when a world has no oauth2 object (sim-small.world.json), so first_refresh should default when oauth2 is absent. x-dumps runs the serve on C:\\home\\xdumps\\joined.world.json for the user.")
inl note_of_x_sota_model_57 () = Note ("X-SOTA-MODEL", 57u32, "2026-11-04T23:07Z", "x", "2026-11-04 19:07 x: oauth2 ROBUSTNESS FIX drafted in x-next9's sim_serve (X-TIMELINE-ENTRY BDE70A884173 -> AFB1E09AC956, land pin updated): world_of no longer crashes on a world without an oauth2 object. first_refresh reads 'refresh_token' through section 'oauth2' w, which falls back to an empty map (no_fields), so issued becomes [ '' ] instead of badmap. New test a_world_without_oauth2_loads; vultr --check 0. The other oauth1/oauth2 reads only run on requests that use those credentials, so they stay as they are.")
inl note_of_x_sota_model_58 () = Note ("X-SOTA-MODEL", 58u32, "2026-11-04T23:08Z", "x", "2026-11-04 19:08 x: x-next6d (job-1793828766751, pinned core 21F39A40A549E6E6) arm A (LIVE posts.spi) sim accept exit 0 with NO fixture changed, the first half of the A/B rule, which x-next6c failed under the regressed Cells. Arm B (draft posts.spi) is running; the verdict stays PENDING.")
inl note_of_x_sota_model_59 () = Note ("X-SOTA-MODEL", 59u32, "2026-11-04T23:17Z", "x", "2026-11-04 19:17 x: CORRECTION to the 1186-line finding: it is NOT x-next9's. A probe of the x-next6+x-next7 stack WITHOUT any x-next9 file (x-next6 sim_serve BCD5D21D595B, x-next7 simframes 835CC894AF9B, live timeline_serve, no timeline_page) splits to the same 1186-line part. The part is a single function, closure108 (the CLI arg dispatcher from sim_args.spi: 'missing --spi or --spi-file', get_plain_arguments), 525 lines live -> 1166 in the stack, now inlining the httpc request (inets, verify_peer, cacertfile). One function cannot be split by gleam-modules, so the fix is a join point / named method at a source seam, not a limit change. A bisect is running (box single-flight compiler): x-next6 x-set with draft lib vs live erlang_http/erlang_text. Caveat: x-next6b's node sim build passed under pinned core 21F39A40A549E6E6, so compiler version may matter too. DEMO RESULT (x-dumps validated on the real 114 MB joined world): one newest-first timeline across all 5 accounts, cursor pagination works, and the two fc1943s accounts are split by id ('@fc1943s · 72784485' vs '· 1637273636049498112'). PERF: about 13-17 s per page, because feed_of rebuilds from about 163K posts on every request. A per-world index or cache (sorted merged feed built once at load) is the follow-up for X-TIMELINE-ROUTE.")
inl note_of_x_sota_model_60 () = Note ("X-SOTA-MODEL", 60u32, "2026-11-04T23:24Z", "x", "2026-11-04 19:24 x: ROOT CAUSE + FIX of the 1186-line Gleam part, and the envoy gap, both x-next7's (X-SOTA-MODEL-2c), not x-next9's. Bisect (box single-flight compiler, scratch probes): with only x-next6, closure108 (cli_run) is 525 lines, the same as live. x-next7's one-line change in simframes run_command, '|> sim_progress::published_text', inlined sim_publish::signed_in plus posted twice, each with an erlang_http::posted_json call, into cli_run: 1166 lines, which cannot be split. FIX (draft sim_progress.spi 145E92FDC313 -> 5131B6D893A3, pre-fix copy kept as .pre-joinpoints): posted_one, session, posted_all and posted_when_signed_in are now :~ join points (no ^, none recursive). Measured on the FULL x-next6..9 stack + oauth2 fix: closure108 526 lines, xsim_frames 34724 lines -> 44 parts, largest 956, under the gate. ENVOY: the same call is the first frames code that reaches env::get_environment_variable, whose Gleam arm imports envoy (x-next6b passed because nothing reached it). New drafts X-SOTA-MODEL-2c\\frames\\gleam.toml 4811C1FFBCF0 and manifest.toml FE8F552066A8 (envoy 1.2.0, checksum from hex via gleam add) are added to launch-x-next7 (bases 481231B0A7D2 / 9F713FFA0571) and land-x-next7 (land pin for sim_progress updated). The real gate is x-next7's node run after x-next6 lands.")
inl note_of_x_sota_model_61 () = Note ("X-SOTA-MODEL", 61u32, "2026-11-04T23:30Z", "x", "2026-11-04 19:30 x: x-next9 FEED CACHE drafted (drafts X-TIMELINE-ENTRY + X-TIMELINE-ROUTE). Source: x-dumps' live demo, 13-17 s per page on the 114 MB, 5-account joined world (box baseline 18-22 s while a build was running). Cause: feed_page re-sorted every followed account's posts (about 163K, each with_author) on every request. Fix: sim_serve api_world gains feeds : list (list (string * term)), [] in blank_world (sim_serve AFB1E09AC956 -> BA479CFD9A85). timeline_serve with_feeds builds the labeled, sorted feeds once, when w::feeds is []; feed_or_api returns that world so the server state keeps it, and feed_page pages w::feeds (page_for removed; timeline_serve 82DD8227885E -> 80709C43ABDC). Safe: feeds read only the raw world (items_at posts), and API writes/deletes never changed them. New test the_feeds_are_built_once_per_world. vultr --check: timeline_serve 0, simframes 0. land-x-next9 pins updated; .pre-feedcache copies kept. Probe timing on the same world is running.")
inl note_of_x_sota_model_62 () = Note ("X-SOTA-MODEL", 62u32, "2026-11-04T23:33Z", "x", "2026-11-04 19:33 x: FEED CACHE MEASURED (box, scratch probe xprobe9 = x-next6..9 + oauth2 fix + join-point fix + feed cache, real joined.world.json): page 1 49 s (one-time world load + feed build), pages 2-4 0.11-0.16 s each, against 18-22 s per page on the live demo (pre-cache build) in the same window. Page 1 HTML is byte-identical to the live demo's. Offered to x-dumps as a switch for the user's demo. Probe serve stopped by exact PID.")
inl note_of_x_sota_model_63 () = Note ("X-SOTA-MODEL", 63u32, "2026-11-04T23:44Z", "x", "2026-11-04 19:44 x: FOLLOW-UP for the first /timeline page (one-time feed build 21.5 s warm per x-dumps, 49 s cold on my probe), measured on vultr's /data/x/joined/joined.world.json with jq: every account's posts are stored OLDEST-first and almost perfectly ordered (ascending created_at pairs: 72784485 2714 of 2714; 1450651554340642818 31595/31595; 1096932140665786369 125526/125527; 1819115209170276352 1731/1731; 1637273636049498112 1734/1735). So feed_of sorts nearly reversed input with list_sort::sorted_by, a top-down merge sort that does n log n compares whatever the order (about 2M created_at comparisons for the 125K account). Candidate (NOT done, unprofiled): a natural merge (detect runs, reverse descending runs) in list_sort makes this about O(n); or reverse then check in order in timeline_serve and sort only on a miss. Profile first (sort vs with_author map copies vs label tuples) before touching lib list_sort, which every backend uses.")
inl note_of_x_sota_model_64 () = Note ("X-SOTA-MODEL", 64u32, "2026-11-04T23:44Z", "x", "2026-11-04 19:44 x: x-next6d (job-1793828766751, core 21F39A40A549E6E6): A/B FIXTURE RULE HELD. Arm A (live posts.spi): no fixture changed. Arm B (draft posts.spi): exactly sim-stress.expected.json + sim-stress.world.json, at the pre-registered 67484404D522 B3BB663AED15. Now running sim build, client build, e2e, census and the notebook gate; the verdict stays PENDING.")
inl note_of_x_sota_model_65 () = Note ("X-SOTA-MODEL", 65u32, "2026-11-04T23:51Z", "x", "2026-11-04 19:51 x: RUN-DETECTING SORT for the feed build, measured and adopted (timeline_serve 80709C43ABDC -> 0A6994DA73DA; pre copy .pre-natural; land-x-next9 pin updated). feed_of now reverses each account's oldest-first posts and merges natural runs (runs_of + merged_pairs reusing list_sort::merged, all :~ join points), instead of list_sort::sorted_by's top-down n log n. newer_or_same is a total order (ties by post id), so any correct sort gives the same pages. Tests: runs_sort_like_the_merge_sort, oldest_first_input_is_one_run; vultr --check 0. Same-window A/B on the box with joined.world.json, each arm a fresh serve: first API request (lazy world load) 21.8 s vs 26.8 s, first /timeline (feed build) 12.2 s (merge sort) vs 2.6 s (runs). Pages 1-4 and a deep page (c=0.0.0.0.1600) are byte-identical to the user's live fast build. What remains of the first page is the lazy world load (about 22-27 s); loading at serve start would just move it. lib list_sort is untouched (a lib natural sort is a separate decision for its owners).")
inl note_of_x_sota_model_66 () = Note ("X-SOTA-MODEL", 66u32, "2026-11-04T23:52Z", "x", "2026-11-04 19:52 x: WORLD LOAD measured (the rest of the first /timeline visit, 22-27 s). Box, OTP via scoop, joined.world.json (114 MB): file:read_file 119 ms, json:decode 15572 ms. So the JSON parse in erlang_term::json_decoded (OTP json:decode, pure Erlang) is about 60-70% of the load; the rest is world_of's index building (by_handle, by_id, by_post). Measured follow-up, NOT done: a binary-term sidecar (term_to_binary of the decoded world: 1724 ms to write, 130 MB; binary_to_term 2460 ms to read) would cut the parse from 15.6 s to about 2.5 s on every serve start after the first. Shape for a sim item: world_of reads <world>.etf when it is newer than <world>.json, else decodes and writes it; a typed erlang_term wrapper over term_to_binary/binary_to_term, both BIFs. Not worth it inside x-next9; file it once the demo is a daily tool.")
inl note_of_x_sota_model_67 () = Note ("X-SOTA-MODEL", 67u32, "2026-11-05T00:17Z", "x", "2026-11-04 20:17 x: x-next6d progress (job-1793828766751): sim build exit 0, client build exit 0, e2e RUST-OK (37 tutorial steps + 5 extra cases, 138 sim requests), crate line gate all under 1000 (xc 992). Postability census and the notebook gate are next; the verdict stays PENDING (x-next6b's notebook gate alone ran past 4 h, and x-next6d has a 6 h limit).")
inl note_of_x_sota_model_68 () = Note ("X-SOTA-MODEL", 68u32, "2026-11-05T00:35Z", "x", "2026-11-04 20:35 x: x-next6d GREEN (job-1793828766751, finished exit 0 in 10154 s, core 21F39A40A549E6E6 at start and end). Every gate passed: A/B fixture rule, sim build, client build, e2e (37 steps + 5 cases), crate line gate (xc 992), postability census (79/79 files, 0 cells over), census equality SAME, notebook gate (12 rows, 0 failed). LANDING NOW with land-x-next6.ps1 -Apply -Label x-next6d: lib erlang_text/erlang_http/erlang_tcp via lib_land_window, then 10 x files (posts, run_signals, events, client spiral.toml/package.spiproj, sim_publish, sim spiral.toml/package.spiproj, sim_serve, cells_executor), each guarded by its base hash. The dry run at 20:28 showed no stale bases.")
inl note_of_x_sota_model_69 () = Note ("X-SOTA-MODEL", 69u32, "2026-11-05T00:54Z", "x", "2026-11-04 20:54 x: x-next6 LANDED (attempt 2, 20:46-20:51). Lib closed green: document_test 26/26, type_probe 3/3, 0 respell drift; live erlang_text 94443FCE2EB5, erlang_http 58A2BB9C1A17, erlang_tcp D5F79511AABB. 10 x files landed at their proven hashes; node-proof applied x-next6d's 30 generated outputs (0 conflicts, 102 unchanged). Unstaged as always. x-next7 LAUNCHED on top (job-1793840045834, core 21F39A40A549E6E6, 4 h limit; it carries the join-point fix, the envoy manifests and the round-trip-fixed date_time 5ACE9C13DD10); its verdict is PENDING with a waiter.")
inl note_of_x_sota_model_70 () = Note ("X-SOTA-MODEL", 70u32, "2026-11-05T01:31Z", "x", "2026-11-04 21:31 x: DEMO CRASH (reported by x-dumps during the user's validation, fast build port 50936) ROOT-CAUSED and fixed in draft. Browsers open speculative preconnect sockets and close them with no bytes. sim_serve's received returned the empty or partial buffer, handled passed it to the handler, and erlang_http_server::request_of cut at index_of '\\r\\n\\r\\n' = -1, so spiral_string_slice went out of range and the single-process server terminated. Reproduced: one empty socket kills the pre-fix build with the user's exact stack (part1.gleam:189). FIX (x-next9 draft X-TIMELINE-ENTRY sim_serve FC9829924AB3; pre copy .pre-partial): handled answers only when erlang_http_server::complete raw holds, and otherwise closes and keeps the world; new test a_closed_preconnect_is_not_a_request; vultr --check 0. The fixed demo build xprobe11 survives 3 empty sockets plus 1 partial request, and /timeline returns 200 after them; handed to x-dumps. STILL OPEN, for x-next9 and the lib: (a) erlang_http_server first_line and request_of need bounds checks (a headerless 'GET / HTTP/1.1\\r\\n\\r\\n' still cuts at -1); (b) per-request isolation, so a crash in one handler does not end the accept loop. land-x-next9's sim_serve pin is NOT updated yet (done after the window renews, together with (a)).")
inl note_of_x_sota_model_71 () = Note ("X-SOTA-MODEL", 71u32, "2026-11-05T01:40Z", "x", "2026-11-04 21:40 x: USER ASKS (relayed by x-dumps after the 21:00 window, so recorded as a PLAN and not started by x): three timeline UI items for x-next9. (1) STABLE PORT: a listen_raw_local_on (port) in the x-next8 lib erlang_tcp draft; sim_serve reads SIM_PORT (0 = ephemeral); loud failure if the port is taken. (2) INFINITE SCROLL: keep timeline_serve next_link as the no-JS fallback and cursor carrier; add one inline script (IntersectionObserver on the last /timeline? link: fetch, DOMParser, append the entries, swap in the next link); cursor semantics unchanged. (3) AUTHORSHIP LABEL (Original / Reply / Repost / Quote): entry_of reads the raw post's kind / retweet_author / reply_to_user into ONE label in posts::entry hints; timeline_page::byline prints it; reposts are visually muted. posts::entry is unchanged (posts.spi landed today). x-dumps offered to draft all three against the x-next9 drafts (timeline_serve 0A6994DA73DA, sim_serve FC9829924AB3, timeline_page 69C9142DE40B); every changed .spi must pass the notebook round-trip check before hand-over.")
inl note_of_x_sota_model_72 () = Note ("X-SOTA-MODEL", 72u32, "2026-11-05T02:09Z", "x", "2026-11-04 22:09 x: USER-REQUESTED timeline items IMPLEMENTED in x-next9 drafts (the user asked directly; done outside the autonomous window). (1) STABLE PORT: sim_serve wanted_port reads SIM_PORT (0 = ephemeral) into the new lib erlang_tcp listen_raw_local_on (x-next8 draft LIB-ERLANG-RAW-TCP 13FE6EBAFAB7 -> 6BB3728125A5, + test a_taken_port_is_refused_to_a_second_listener); a taken port fails loudly. (2) INFINITE SCROLL: timeline_serve appends one script (5 small defs) that fetches the last /timeline? link, appends its article.post entries before that link and advances the link; the next link stays as the no-JS fallback and cursor; node --check ok, not yet run in a browser. (3) AUTHORSHIP LABELS: timeline_page post_kind (Original | ReplyTo | RepostOf | QuoteOf author text) and labeled_html render each post as <article class='post <kind>'> with a bold label; reposts are muted by CSS and read 'not this account's words'; quotes show the quoted post in a blockquote. timeline_serve kind_of maps kind / retweet_author / reply_to_user / quote{author,text} (a null quote reads 'Quoting'). (4) ROBUSTNESS: new lib draft LIB-HTTP-SERVER-BOUNDS erlang_http_server (base DCB208E5EA4F -> BCCF0CFFE0B8; upto guards in request_of and first_line, + 2 tests) and sim_serve answers only complete requests. Per-request isolation is NOT done: the world is about 800 MB, so a process per request would copy it, and the frames FFI is compiler-generated, so it cannot carry a try/catch; that needs compiler support. Hashes: timeline_page B0D00E0A6709, timeline_serve 37744F2D6769, sim_serve 1ADC0F26C368. Checks: vultr --check 0 for all five and simframes; notebook round-trip OK 5/5; every cell fits one post (0 spilled; 14 healed, including 2 that were already in the pre-change timeline_serve); frames 35408 lines -> largest part 957 at the 1000 gate. Demo build xprobe12 smoke-tested on the e2e and joined worlds: SIM_PORT honoured, a second listener refused, empty/partial/headerless sockets survive, labels render per kind (deep pages have repost, quote, reply and own posts), page 2 appends 50 articles. Pins: land-x-next8/9 updated, launch-x-next9 carries the lib draft (base DCB208E5EA4F); land-x-next9 still needs a lib_land_window step for erlang_http_server.")
