inl planning_of_edition3_samples_respell () = Planning ("EDITION3-SAMPLES-RESPELL", Minutes 240u32, Load Heavy, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_edition3_samples_respell_1 () = Note ("EDITION3-SAMPLES-RESPELL", 1u32, "2026-11-01T14:38Z", "compiler", "R5 SPEC. DOD: format --spelling symbols applied in place to every samples/*.spi and tmp/tests .spi; trap checks green: (1) tokens identical per line (keepsTokens), (2) the matrix single-flight run gives the same EXPECTED verdicts and the same generated-output hashes as before on every row (predict P = 0), (3) git diff shows only respelled lexemes (a diff classifier counts non-spelling changes = 0). Archive of the pre-respell tree via scratch-archive before apply. NEXT STEP: dry run format --spelling symbols over samples into a scratch copy, run the classifier, then a heavy window for the matrix. TYPEFEATURESET PLAN: a respell change is { line; from : Spelling; to : Spelling } with Spelling = Words | Symbols; the classifier returns a union Change = Respell | Layout | Semantic and gates on Semantic = 0.")
inl kit_reuse_metrics () = Task ("KIT-REUSE-METRICS", "kit reuse metrics: code reuse, duplicated logic and the lib-vs-app ratio of generic code per repo, with mechanical periodic healing (duplicate detection -> granular lib candidates), modeled with the typefeatureset (user 11-01 round 5)", Story, P1, D5, 0u32, Planned, "GRANULAR-STATS,KIT-EXEC-METRICS", "kit,metrics,granular,lib,typefeatureset,team:kit,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_kit_reuse_metrics () = Planning ("KIT-REUSE-METRICS", Minutes 600u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_kit_reuse_metrics_1 () = Note ("KIT-REUSE-METRICS", 1u32, "2026-11-01T14:38Z", "kit", "11-01 10:3x kit (round 5, user list item 1). Filed separately rather than folded: GRANULAR-STATS counts host-text drift per file and KIT-EXEC-METRICS exports runtime samples; neither measures reuse. MODEL (typefeatureset): phantom unit markers :! Repo / :! Lib / :! App on a span type, so a lib-ratio query cannot mix app and lib spans; a dup_class GADT (Exact : digest -> dup_class | Renamed : digest -> dup_class | Shape : digest -> dup_class) over normalized definition bodies (identifiers alpha-renamed, literals blanked; FNV digests as in kit_run_model, no sorting); an existential heal_candidate packing any dup_class with its sites; metrics: dup_ratio = duplicated weighted chars / total, lib_share = generic definitions living in spiral/lib / all generic definitions (generic = no app-module import), reuse_fanout = importers per lib definition. HEALING (mechanical, periodic): each groom round lists the top 10 Exact/Renamed classes with >= 2 sites in different repos as LIB-candidate rows; Shape classes are advisory only. DoD: kit reuse <root> prints the three metrics per repo plus the top classes; the rows feed DIAMOND-S2 as Mechanical evidence and export through KIT-EXEC-METRICS; tests: two renamed copies -> one Renamed class; an app-importing definition is never counted generic; node green. NEXT: the dup_class model + normalizer in a new HostFree package kit_reuse_model with the two tests (CPU-light). TYPEFEATURES: phantom span units, dup_class GADT, existential heal_candidate.")
inl note_of_kit_reuse_metrics_2 () = Note ("KIT-REUSE-METRICS", 2u32, "2026-11-01T22:59Z", "main", "11-01 19:0x main (lib-reshape generic-code sweep). Ready-made test oracle for the dup_class detector, found by hand today: Exact classes with >= 3 sites across kit packages: lines_joined (6 identical + 3 concat5 variants, kit_do_* run.spi), padded (4 identical agile host macros), argument_owned (20 one-line wrappers), argument_count 'user - 1' (8), argument offset-1 wrapper (15), decimal_u64/decimal_of parse-or-zero (9), json_text (kit_grep_query/search.spi:84 = kit_read_query/slice.spi:4). Renamed classes: now_ms / clock_now_ms / unix_ms / wall_clock_millis (8 bodies differing only in name and unwrap style), file_text / text_of / read (read_to_string unwrap_or_default, 5). Cross-repo Shape classes: days_from_civil/civil_from_days (x world.spi:279-299 vs lib date_time.spi:1331 vs kit agile_note_model:215), JSON string quoting (x events.spi:45-64, kit_memory_model:152, lib json.spi:13). A detector that does not report these is missing cases. Sites and moves: LIB-MOVE-* items filed today.")
inl machine_event_log_detector () = Task ("MACHINE-EVENT-LOG-DETECTOR", "Log a typed process-sample time-series (pid, parent-chain, session-owner, commit, cpu-delta) + job-receipt linkage so orphan/runaway/starvation cases are flagged by a kit detector verb, not traced by hand", TaskKind, P2, D3, 15u32, Active, "BEAM-GUARD-ALL-INVOCATIONS", "team:resilience,resilience,machine,health,observability,kit,detector", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_machine_event_log_detector () = Planning ("MACHINE-EVENT-LOG-DETECTOR", Minutes 150u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_machine_event_log_detector_1 () = Note ("MACHINE-EVENT-LOG-DETECTOR", 1u32, "2026-11-01T14:38Z", "resilience", "2026-11-01 ~10:4x resilience (git-51), ROUND-5 ask 3 (logging spec). Motivation: this session had to trace the node orphans and the 51.8 GB erl runaway BY HAND (parent chain erl -> ... -> claude.exe 12156; commit vs WS). main took too long to notice the node orphans. The fix is a persisted, replayable event log + a detector verb.\n\nWHAT TO LOG (append-only rolling time-series, one snapshot ~every 60s; this makes the three cases REPLAYABLE/simulatable from a captured window):\n- process_sample row per relevant process (erl, dotnet, pwsh, kit, claude, node equivalents): {pid; ppid; parent_chain (to root); session_owner (lane, resolved as kit-live-parent-every-process does); start_time; priority_class; commit_bytes (= PrivateUsage / sysinfo virtual_memory, NOT working set); ws_bytes; cpu_ms_cumulative}. cpu-delta and commit-growth come from differencing consecutive snapshots.\n- session_liveness set per snapshot: the claude.exe PIDs alive now (so \"orphan\" is decidable: owner PID not in the set).\n- job-receipt linkage: map each sampled PID to its kit job id (kit\\jobs already records owning session, command line, start/end, peak). The sample log references the receipt; it does not duplicate it.\n\nDETECTOR (a kit verb over the log — tool, not peer; findings as a closed union, no stringly status):\n| Orphan   :: {pid; last_owner; dead_since} -- a process whose resolved session_owner PID is NOT in the live session set.\n| Runaway  :: {pid; commit_start; commit_now; per_min} -- commit grew past a threshold (abs GB or %/min) across >=2 snapshots with no matching output/progress (the junction-loop / quadratic-join signature: climbs with no stdout).\n| Starved  :: {pid; priority; cpu_ms_delta; window} -- ~0 cpu_ms delta across N snapshots while the box is CPU-saturated (belownormal-starves-on-saturated-box: these rows are VOID, not results).\nVerb prints the flagged rows; --since replays a captured window; a planted hog + a parked BelowNormal proc are the test fixtures (deterministic: feed a recorded log, assert the three unions fire exactly on the planted rows).\n\nREUSES / BLOCKER: kit_machine_sample already samples processes but by WORKING SET only -- it must add Process::virtual_memory() (commit), the SAME step BEAM-GUARD-ALL-INVOCATIONS needs (shared dep). kit_machine_watch / kit_process_table / kit_machine_crowding already walk process trees. The NEW part is (1) persisting the typed time-series (not just an instant), (2) session-owner + liveness resolution per row, (3) the three-union detector. Ties BEAM-GUARD-ALL-INVOCATIONS (commit field) and MAIN-ORPHAN-SWEEP (which does this by hand today).\n\nDoD: a background sampler appends process_sample rows to a rolling log (bounded retention); `kit machine detect --since <t>` reads the log and prints Orphan/Runaway/Starved rows; test block feeds a recorded log with one planted orphan + one planted hog + one parked BelowNormal proc and asserts exactly those three fire, nothing else. Registration of the sampler as a standing task is a user-batch step (machine-upkeep-myself / SCHEDULED-TASKS-CONFIRM), not self-registered.")
inl note_of_machine_event_log_detector_2 () = Note ("MACHINE-EVENT-LOG-DETECTOR", 2u32, "2026-11-03T23:22Z", "resilience", "11-03 19:15 resilience groom-refresh (was stale since 11-01; keeping, not closing - MORE relevant now). THIS session hand-traced exactly the cases this detector would automate: (a) an escaped-daemon audit (answered via resource-report's dangling-candidates section = empty), (b) a box-CPU-starvation diagnosis (critical-mirror FAILing \"no route reached\" at 100% CPU - hand-verified it was legitimate fleet load, not reapable orphans). A `kit machine detect` verb over a persisted process-sample log would flag Orphan/Runaway/Starved automatically instead. Next step: gated on the commit field (Process::virtual_memory) SHARED with BEAM-GUARD-ALL-INVOCATIONS (Active) - once that lands the commit sample, add the rolling typed time-series + the 3-union detector + the recorded-log test fixture. Owner team:resilience.")
inl note_of_machine_event_log_detector_3 () = Note ("MACHINE-EVENT-LOG-DETECTOR", 3u32, "2026-11-04T05:44Z", "resilience", "11-04 02:0x resilience PROTOTYPE - detector input proven. One Win32_Process sample (340 procs) classifies in a single cheap call: 5 orphan short-tools (git/ssh/scp with a DEAD parent) flagged right now (the orphan-reaper exact targets), 0 runaway (>2GB WS). Top WS: Memory Compression 644MB, lane claude.exe ~435-453MB each. So the typed sample (pid,name,ppid,WS,parent-dead) is collectable cheaply + yields immediate signal. BUILD scope (kit verb, deps BEAM-GUARD, box-gated): add cpu-delta (2 samples), session-owner (walk parent-chain to the owning claude), commit (kit/SPIRAL env), job-receipt linkage; LOG as append-only time-series -> reuse the mirror append-delta pattern (mirror_delta_proto.ps1) for its off-box backup. Detector verb flags: orphan (parent-dead+short-tool+age>grace = orphan-reaper rule), runaway (WS/cpu-delta over cap = BEAM-guard trigger), starvation (sum cpu ~100pct while a lane idle = node-utilization-monitor signal). NET: the detector UNIFIES 3 existing ad-hoc monitors into one typed series + verb. Prototype in-session.")
inl note_of_machine_event_log_detector_4 () = Note ("MACHINE-EVENT-LOG-DETECTOR", 4u32, "2026-11-05T03:40Z", "resilience", "Candidate schema for the typed process-sample time-series: proc-frame.v1 = {pid, ppid, parent_live, name, session, threads, ws_mb, start, age_min}, captured per-frame from Win32_Process + Get-Process on the live box (303 procs). Add the still-missing fields this item names - parent-chain (full lineage not just ppid), session-owner (which lane owns the tree), commit, cpu-delta between frames, and job-receipt id - to turn single frames into the replayable series KIT-ORPHAN-SIM needs. A real v1 frame is in resilience scratchpad as the shape reference. resilience tick1 2026-11-04 23:4x local.")
inl ci_workflow_names () = Task ("CI-WORKFLOW-NAMES", "Every own repo has exactly one workflow, .github/workflows/gh-pages.yml (+ actions.lock), as slim as spiral's model, each job only calling the repo's own scripts/workflow.ps1 (user 11-01 round 5)", TaskKind, P1, D3, 90u32, Planned, "", "ci,pipelines,standards,kit,main,team:kit", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_ci_workflow_names () = Planning ("CI-WORKFLOW-NAMES", Minutes 120u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_ci_workflow_names_1 () = Note ("CI-WORKFLOW-NAMES", 1u32, "2026-11-01T14:39Z", "kit", "11-01 10:3x kit census of C:\\home\\git\\*\\.github\\workflows (lines in parens). CONFORMING (gh-pages.yml + actions.lock): alphabet(108) blog(69) dice(87) dir-tree-html(53) eoie(88) fiscal(100) fork-spiral-patch(49) i574n.github(77) kit(88) polyglot(93) spiral(174) tibia(131). NAME DEVIATIONS: x ci.yml(106) (accepted on 10-18 under CI-SINGLE-PIPELINE, now to rename); calc-parser gh-pages.yaml(19) (.yaml -> .yml); tictactoe dist.yml(50) + gh-pages.yml(99) (fold dist into gh-pages); dlm ci.yml(25) + release.yml(37); FsClr build.yml(30). MISSING actions.lock with a gh-pages.yml: azeroth, calc-parser, cumbuquia, currency_converter, dotfiles, fc1943s.github, fc1943s.github.io, i574n.github.io, mechahaze-album1, tapas2, tictactoe, vault. OUT OF SCOPE (upstream forks, keep upstream names): atrium cargo-outdated ExplorerPatcher fluke forgottenserver leptos livebook near-workspaces-rs nearcore otclient plotters rustix wasmer zed. SLIMNESS: spiral 174 and tibia 131 lines carry inline setup; target is setup steps + one run line per job (dir-tree-html 53 is the slim reference). DoD: every own repo lists only gh-pages.yml (+ actions.lock, refreshed per actions-lock-on-workflow-edits); every run step is pwsh scripts/workflow.ps1 [-Flag]; a kit standards rule (WorkflowShape) refuses any other file name or inline run logic; pushes and CI watch are the user's push + verify-ci-after-user-push. NEXT: classify dlm and FsClr (own vs fork, fork-identity-and-ownership), then rename x ci.yml in the tree (unstaged) with its workflow.ps1 -Xc call unchanged. TYPEFEATURES: the WorkflowShape rule as a typed census row (repo, file, verdict union Conforming|Renamed|Inline|Fork).")
inl note_of_ci_workflow_names_2 () = Note ("CI-WORKFLOW-NAMES", 2u32, "2026-11-04T14:02Z", "main", "11-04 10:0x main RECOUNT (box, 14 working repos): spiral, kit, fiscal, tibia, regex, html, dice, alphabet, i574n.github, blog, dir-tree-html, polyglot all conform (gh-pages.yml + actions.lock). Still deviating: x ci.yml (the rename from n1), and vault has gh-pages.yml but NO actions.lock (new since n1's census; actions-lock-on-workflow-edits applies). Owner kit; x and vault owners for their files.")
inl note_of_ci_workflow_names_3 () = Note ("CI-WORKFLOW-NAMES", 3u32, "2026-11-05T20:29Z", "main", "main 11-05 16:3x: the three working repos with a gh-pages.yml but NO actions.lock now have one (unstaged, new file each): vault, dotfiles, i574n.github.io. Generated by gh actions-lock v0.4.0 --no-interactive and verified with --no-fix ('All 1 workflow valid'); keys '.github/workflows/gh-pages.yml', LF. vault's lock pins awalsh128/cache-apt-pkgs-action@master's transitive actions/cache + upload-artifact SHAs (the @master tag itself is CI-ACTIONS-LATEST's). Recount of 15 working repos: 14 conform (gh-pages.yml + actions.lock). LEFT: x's ci.yml -> gh-pages.yml rename (x's repo; its YAML is generated from i574n.github/scripts/pins/workflows.spi, so the rename goes through that source + pins.ps1, plus the lock key).")
inl note_of_ci_workflow_names_4 () = Note ("CI-WORKFLOW-NAMES", 4u32, "2026-11-05T21:49Z", "main", "main 11-05 17:4x DONE (x's yes 17:3x): x's ci.yml renamed to gh-pages.yml, all unstaged. The generator target is now workflow_target \"x\" \"gh-pages.yml\" (x_ci ()); --write created the file byte-identical to ci.yml (cmp), then ci.yml was deleted. x's actions.lock changed by its key only (gh actions-lock --no-fix: All 1 workflow valid). x references moved: scripts/common.ps1:21 (Get-XCiZigPin reads the new file and returns 0.17.0, checked) and :71, README.md:12 and :23; git grep ci.yml in x is empty. Earlier today, 3 missing locks were generated (vault, dotfiles, i574n.github.io), and html/regex/dir-tree-html are now generated (SPIRAL-TO-YAML n28). Recount of 15 working repos: all have exactly .github/workflows/gh-pages.yml + actions.lock. The workflow's internal name stays 'ci' for x (check names unchanged).")
inl note_of_ci_workflow_names_5 () = Note ("CI-WORKFLOW-NAMES", 5u32, "2026-11-05T22:08Z", "main", "main 11-05 18:0x CORRECTION to n3/n4 (reopened at 90): the three locks I generated at 16:26-16:27 are REVERTED. (1) gh actions-lock without --no-fix also rewrote each workflow's floating tags to exact versions (vault 22, dotfiles 14, i574n.github.io 10 changed uses: lines; checkout@v6 -> v6.1.0 etc.), which n3 did not report. Restored each gh-pages.yml from the index (vault keeps the user's own staged change) and deleted the new locks. (2) vault is fc1943s/vault and dir-tree-html is fc1943s/dir-tree-html (transferred 11-03), so neither is an own repo. x's rename (n4) stands: x already used exact tags, so the tool changed only the lock key, and x/gh-pages.yml still matches the generator byte for byte (rechecked). TRUE STATE of own (i574n) repos: 12 conform; dotfiles and i574n.github.io have gh-pages.yml but no actions.lock, and their workflows use floating tags (@v6, @v1, peaceiris @v3.9.3), so adding a lock means pinning those tags: a workflow change to make deliberately (best done by adopting both into the generator so they take the org pins).")
inl agile_concurrent_write_battletest () = Task ("AGILE-CONCURRENT-WRITE-BATTLETEST", "Battle-test concurrent kit agile writes for lost updates (process-level contention across note/set/edit)", TaskKind, P3, D1, 100u32, Done, "", "team:resilience,resilience,agile,concurrency,integrity,test", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_agile_concurrent_write_battletest () = Planning ("AGILE-CONCURRENT-WRITE-BATTLETEST", Minutes 30u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_agile_concurrent_write_battletest_1 () = Note ("AGILE-CONCURRENT-WRITE-BATTLETEST", 1u32, "2026-11-01T14:39Z", "resilience", "2026-11-01 ~10:4x resilience (git-51), ROUND-5 (user: \"concurrent agile writes battle-tested\"). RESULT: PASS, no lost updates at process-level contention. Method: scratch backlog ($TEMP\\...\\scratchpad\\concwrite), real kit.exe processes fired via Start-Process (true OS concurrency, not threads).\nROUND A: 16 concurrent `agile note` on one task -> 16/16 exit 0, 16/16 distinct notes persisted. Since each note reports bytes_before/after (a WHOLE-FILE rewrite of agile.spi), 16 un-serialized rewrites would have clobbered down to 1-2 survivors; all 16 landing proves kit lock-serializes writes.\nROUND B (the real RMW test): 26 MIXED concurrent ops on overlapping targets -- 10 `note` (append) on one task + 8 `set <progress> <status>` + 8 `edit title` on 8 sibling tasks (set and edit hit the SAME tasks = read-modify-write contention). RESULT: 26/26 exit 0; all 26 notes persisted; 8/8 siblings set to Active AND 8/8 titles edited. BOTH mutation kinds survived on every task -- no last-writer-wins clobber.\nVERDICT: kit agile mutations are concurrency-safe under concurrent processes (a global lock serializes the whole-file rewrite; note/set/edit all survive). The memory agile-status-lost-updates risk is NOT at the process level.\nRESIDUAL (not a kit bug, a usage rule): a SESSION-level stale-read race remains possible -- an agent reads state, deliberates for minutes, then writes based on the stale snapshot. kit re-reads current state at each op's exec time, so this only bites if an agent caches state across a long gap. Guard = snapshot-before-bulk-groom (agile-edit-tags-replaces) and small, late mutations. Recommend keeping that memory but narrowing it to the stale-read case.")
inl kit_orphan_sim () = Task ("KIT-ORPHAN-SIM", "Orphan and process-tree edge cases become simulable from collected logs: the node and box record enough (process tree snapshots, parent/child edges, reap events) that kit can replay a monstrous incident and prove the job host catches it in minutes (user 11-01 round 5: main noticed node orphans too late, scaffolding still poor)", Story, P0, D5, 0u32, Planned, "REMOTE-EXEC-S1", "kit,jobs,node,resilience,simulation,typefeatureset,team:kit,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_kit_orphan_sim () = Planning ("KIT-ORPHAN-SIM", Minutes 480u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_kit_orphan_sim_1 () = Note ("KIT-ORPHAN-SIM", 1u32, "2026-11-01T14:42Z", "kit", "11-01 10:4x kit (round 5, user list item 7). DO WE COLLECT LOGS THAT MAKE THIS SIMULABLE? NO. Measured: a kit job dir holds argv, cwd, pid, supervisor pid, slot, heartbeat_ms, started_ms, exit, stdout/stderr; machine-watch holds health.tsv and quiet.tsv (tab rows, DATA-ROWS-TYPED drift). Missing for replay: (a) child pids and the process tree over time (orphans are children whose job ended); (b) reap events (who killed what, when, why); (c) the node side at all from the box (ssh laptop failed to resolve desktop-6e8c14g at 10:4x, so a box-side watcher would have been blind too). PLAN: (1) typed event log, one row per observation: union proc_event = | Spawned : job_id * pid * parent_pid * image | Exited : pid * code | Snapshot : list (pid * parent_pid * image * cpu_ms * rss) | Reaped : pid * reason | Unreachable : host; rows as .spi modules (no tsv), written by the job host every heartbeat and on each job exit; (2) a pure detector orphan_verdict: given the event stream, a pid whose parent job has Exited and that is still in a later Snapshot for > N heartbeats is Orphan (job_id, pid, age); (3) a simulator that generates monstrous streams (fork bombs under a dead supervisor, pid reuse after exit, clock skew between box and node, unreachable host for 30 min, 500 orphans) and checks the detector flags each within 2 heartbeats with no false positives on pid reuse. DoD: detector + simulator tests green on the node; the job host writes proc_event rows on box and node; kit jobs orphans prints the live verdict; a replay of tonight's node incident (from main's notes) is flagged at the first heartbeat after the job exit. NEXT: the proc_event union + orphan_verdict + the pid-reuse test in a new HostFree package kit_proc_events (CPU-light). TYPEFEATURES: proc_event union, phantom host markers (:! Box / :! Node) on streams so box and node clocks are never compared raw, a GADT for detector states (Watching -> Suspect -> Orphan).")
inl note_of_kit_orphan_sim_2 () = Note ("KIT-ORPHAN-SIM", 2u32, "2026-11-02T12:26Z", "resilience", "11-02 08:1x resilience (fresh box census): real taxonomy for the simulator + a detector false-positive class the plan misses. Dead-parent classes observed on the BOX (the sole accumulator; wifi + silicon-valley nodes were orphan-clean): CLAUDE_CODE_SHELL_LAUNCHER pwsh (Claude Code's own shell-tool processes), spiral-bin dotnet (unreaped Cells/compiler children, 6h-57h), gh codespace logs, ssh to codespaces, conhost pairs, ancient argless pwsh; on silicon-valley a 19h less reparented to init. KEY DETECTOR INSIGHT: shells (pwsh/conhost) are PARENTLESS BY DESIGN -- every run_in_background task, kit job, and shell-launcher has an exited parent immediately -- so orphan_verdict must NOT flag a dead-parent shell as Orphan on the parent-Exited-plus-still-in-Snapshot rule alone, or it false-positives on all live detached work. Needs class-tiered grace (compiler/remote children = minutes; shells = HOURS). Resilience's orphan-reaper.ps1 uses 60m vs 720m for exactly this. The simulator should add a legit-long-lived-detached-shell stream the detector must NOT flag.")
inl note_of_kit_orphan_sim_3 () = Note ("KIT-ORPHAN-SIM", 3u32, "2026-11-03T00:35Z", "resilience", "11-02 20:33 resilience (fresh evidence from tonight's reaper runs, for the simulator taxonomy + detector states): TWO classes the n1/n2 design misses, both observed live today.\n\n(1) THREE-TIER grace, not two. n2 said \"minutes vs hours\"; today's reaper proved a distinct SHORT tier: git/ssh/scp/gh = 3min grace (orphan-reaper ShortClasses), because they orphan on EVERY Fork-agent spawn and remote op (reaped 3 dangling git today, ppid dead, 17min old - Fork-spawned git children whose agent shell exited). So: SHORT (git/ssh/scp/gh, ~3min - net/VCS tools, orphan constantly, reap fast) < MEDIUM (dotnet/cargo/rustc/erl/beam/epmd compiler+remote children, ~60min) < LONG (pwsh/conhost shells, ~720min - parentless by design per n2). The simulator needs a fast-orphaning-VCS-tool stream reaped at SHORT grace that must NOT be conflated with the MEDIUM compiler tier.\n\n(2) NEW detector state: StuckDetached. The orphan_verdict GADT Watching->Suspect->Orphan assumes an orphan goes idle/zombie. But today a profile-mirror.ps1 (parent dead via if-due detach) was stuck 57min ACTIVELY thrashing on an unbounded tar child under memory pressure - dead-parent but CPU-BUSY, not idle. The \"still in a later Snapshot > N heartbeats\" rule flags it, but the REASON + remedy differ: Reaped reason = stuck-runtime-exceeds-bound, NOT abandoned-idle. Add to proc_event: a Reaped reason union | AbandonedIdle | StuckRuntime : {runtime_s; bound_s} | DeadParentShort. The simulator should generate a \"dead-parent-still-busy\" stream (busy tar) the detector flags via a runtime-bound rule, distinct from the idle/zombie orphan - else a legit long compile (busy, live parent) and a stuck detached run (busy, dead parent) look identical on CPU alone; the discriminator is PARENT-ALIVE x RUNTIME-BOUND, which the typed verdict must carry. Resilience's orphan-reaper already encodes all three: ShortClasses(3m)/Classes(60m)/ShellClasses(720m) + a dedicated stuck-mirror runtime sweep (>30m) - that script is the executable oracle the kit detector sim should match.")
inl note_of_kit_orphan_sim_4 () = Note ("KIT-ORPHAN-SIM", 4u32, "2026-11-04T04:53Z", "kit", "Moved here from DATA-ROWS-TYPED (closed 11-04): the machine-watch health.tsv and quiet.tsv rows under %LOCALAPPDATA% become typed Spiral rows (one record per fact, written and read through the kit_table_rows codec), since this item already simulates from those logs. Local state, so no repo census impact.")
inl note_of_kit_orphan_sim_5 () = Note ("KIT-ORPHAN-SIM", 5u32, "2026-11-04T11:32Z", "resilience", "11-04 ~07:4x resilience TAXONOMY REFINEMENT from today's incidents. NEW CLASS the n1/n2/3-tier-grace model misses: NON-KIT RESOURCE SATURATION. SystemInformer (a monitor, NOT kit-launched) held ~1.5 cores for 7.3h - INVISIBLE to the job-host: it is not a kit job/orphan, so orphan-reap + the escaped-daemon invariant never see it, yet it fed the sustained 100pct saturation preceding the 11-03 BSOD. IMPLICATION: the simulator/detector must model RESOURCE-saturation events (per-process CPU over time, ANY process incl non-kit tools), not only parent/child/reap events. DATA SOURCE now live: the per-heartbeat process-health sweep (MACHINE-PROC-HEALTH-TUNE) samples top CPU consumers + classifies orphan(parent-dead)/runaway(tool >0.5 core)/kit-escape - the recordable time-series the sim can replay. CLEAN DATA POINT: today's escaped-daemon audit = 0 kit escapees (the kit-orphan class is well-controlled by the job-host; the UNcontrolled risk is non-kit tools + legit builds saturating). Recommend the sim event schema include {pid,ppid,name,lane,ws,cpu,is_kit_job,class} sampled, so it can replay a SATURATION incident (SI), not only an orphan incident. Deps REMOTE-EXEC-S1 + the sim engine are team:kit; releasing (resilience taxonomy contribution recorded).")
inl note_of_kit_orphan_sim_6 () = Note ("KIT-ORPHAN-SIM", 6u32, "2026-11-04T14:27Z", "resilience", "11-04 ~10:2x data-source note: the new hourly kit-machine-watch task is accumulating a process-snapshot time-series in %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\machine-watch\\hourly.log (4 samples on schedule). Useful as replay data for this sim, BUT hourly is too COARSE to replay a FAST incident (the SI core-burn, a fork-storm, a saturation spike happen in minutes). For incident replay the sim wants finer sampling DURING an event: either a burst-mode (per-minute while rogues/hogs/load exceed a threshold) or the native health.tsv written at a tight cadence when something is detected. The hourly log gives the slow-trend baseline; the fast-incident capture is the gap.")
inl note_of_kit_orphan_sim_7 () = Note ("KIT-ORPHAN-SIM", 7u32, "2026-11-05T03:39Z", "resilience", "Captured a real typed process-tree frame (proc-frame.v1: pid,ppid,parent_live,name,session,threads,ws_mb,start,age_min; 303 procs, host box) as the concrete input a replay must consume. Key empirical finding for the simulator design: parent-dead is NECESSARY but NOT SUFFICIENT for orphan-to-reap. 18 parent-dead edges in the frame; 0 genuine runaways. They split into (a) by-design parent-exit daemons age 20-28h (csrss/wininit/winlogon via smss, igfx, explorer, rustdesk, mariadbd, SystemInformer) that a naive parent_live==false detector false-positives on and must never reap; (b) transient by-design git.exe age ~8min; (c) forming one-shots age <=4min (tail.exe, grep.exe) correctly under the 30min threshold. So the detector/replay must classify on name-allowlist x age x session-lineage, not on a parent-liveness bit alone. Frame saved to resilience scratchpad; can be promoted to a repo fixture once kit lands the detector verb (feeds MACHINE-EVENT-LOG-DETECTOR typed series). resilience tick1 2026-11-04 23:4x local.")
inl note_of_kit_orphan_sim_8 () = Note ("KIT-ORPHAN-SIM", 8u32, "2026-11-05T21:06Z", "resilience-614162e5", "11-05 ~13:0x resilience SATURATION-INCIDENT FIXTURE + taxonomy (addresses note-6 gap: sim must replay RESOURCE-saturation, not only orphans; \"fast-incident capture is the gap\"). Captured saturation-frame.v1 live during a real 100%-CPU multi-compile starvation (resilience scratchpad saturation-frame-v1.txt; schema pid,ppid,name,cpu_pct,ws_mb,is_kit,class; 26 rows >=5% cpu; header carries cpu_total/commit/find_probe_ms=5646 as the incident signature - even a find over 6905 files times out).\nKEY TAXONOMY (reframes \"compiles dominate\" - they DO NOT):\n- DOMINANT saturators are NON-KIT: rustdesk ~476% (~4.75 cores, x-dumps-owned never-degrade) + SystemInformer (monitor, High prio) - together ~5.5 cores.\n- kit-live compiles (3x dotnet) sum only ~266% (~2.7 cores) = a MINORITY of load. The orphan/kit-escape detector would see NOTHING wrong (0 orphans, 0 escapes) yet the box is saturated - exactly note-6 point that saturation != orphan incident.\n- NEW detector-relevant class: PRIORITY-INVERSION STARVATION. SystemInformer ran at High priority (81% of one core sustained, measured clean over 3s - the 1s delta oversampled to 250%), PREEMPTING normal/below-normal work incl the Priority-7 xdumps-backlog-sao task (which was being terminated/erroring). A pure cpu_pct threshold misses this; the discriminator is PRIORITY_CLASS x cpu, so the sim schema should carry priority per row. Resilience ACTION taken: lowered SI High->BelowNormal (live, reverts on SI restart) so it yields - on the \"SI tuned to minimal CPU\" mandate; reduces the exact saturation implicated in the 11-03 pre-BSOD note.\nFixture promotable to a repo replay fixture once kit lands the detector verb. resilience-614162e5.")
inl kit_definition () = Task ("KIT-DEFINITION", "A breakthrough one-paragraph definition of what kit is, grounded in prior art (Akita ai-memory, Zed DeltaDB, Anthropic Scion) and in what kit already does, used to cut bloat: every verb must serve it (user 11-01 round 5)", Story, P1, D4, 0u32, Planned, "", "kit,research,memory,team:kit,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_kit_definition () = Planning ("KIT-DEFINITION", Minutes 180u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_kit_definition_1 () = Note ("KIT-DEFINITION", 1u32, "2026-11-01T14:43Z", "kit", "11-01 10:4x kit (round 5, user list item 5). BACKLOG FIRST: Scion and DeltaDB were already researched (CI-OFFLOAD n1 main-thread 10-17; EOIE-REMOTE-EXECUTOR n9 kit2 10-25; EOIE-MEMORY n1 mentions DeltaDB). New here: Akita's project is akitaonrails/ai-memory (github.com/akitaonrails/ai-memory, MIT, Rust, launched 2026-05, v1.29.0 on 2026-08-19): a git-versioned markdown wiki of what coding agents learned, indexed with SQLite FTS5, so a task can move between Claude Code, Codex and Cursor without re-explaining (cross-provider handover); its author calls it beta. PRIOR ART IN ONE LINE EACH: ai-memory = agent memory as untyped markdown + full-text search; DeltaDB = version history that keeps the agent conversations behind each change; Scion = server-authoritative history with thin client fetch of only the needed files. WHAT KIT ALREADY IS: one typed state (agile.spi: items, notes with author lanes, planning), typed job records and receipts, content-keyed run/compile caches, a style census that refuses drift, and Claude memory files it is meant to absorb (kit-embodied-memory). DRAFT DEFINITION: kit is the typed ledger of a codebase's work: every fact an agent or person produces (task, decision, memory, job, receipt, metric) is one record of a Spiral type, content-addressed, linked to the conversation and the build that produced it, and checked by the compiler and by kit's own gates before it is trusted. ai-memory remembers text, DeltaDB remembers why, Scion fetches only what is needed; kit remembers typed facts, so memory can be refused when wrong (provenance + verification), not just searched. ANTI-BLOAT TEST: a verb that writes no typed record and checks none is not kit; it is glue. DoD: the definition lands as the first paragraph of kit AGENTS.md (user reviews wording), a verb census marks each kit verb Records|Checks|Glue, glue verbs get an archive-or-justify item; EOIE-MEMORY scope reconciled with ai-memory's handover feature (adopt cross-provider handover as a typed record, not markdown). NEXT: the verb census (kit --help walk, CPU-light). TYPEFEATURES: verb_role union Records|Checks|Glue on the census rows.")
inl kit_standards_ecosystem () = Task ("KIT-STANDARDS-ECOSYSTEM", "kit standards grows the repo-level rules of a minimally usable ecosystem and every own repo gets a root standards.spi: RepoEntry, WorkflowShape, NoUntypedRows, LibNotCopied (user 11-01 round 5, after the standards.spi payoff check)", Story, P1, D4, 0u32, Planned, "KIT-STANDARDS-SPI", "kit,standards,ci,drift,typefeatureset,team:kit,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_kit_standards_ecosystem () = Planning ("KIT-STANDARDS-ECOSYSTEM", Minutes 360u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_kit_standards_ecosystem_1 () = Note ("KIT-STANDARDS-ECOSYSTEM", 1u32, "2026-11-01T14:43Z", "kit", "11-01 10:4x kit. Why: standards.spi pays off in kit (KIT-STANDARDS-SPI n re-measure: 2 true refusals tonight, 0 false) but only kit and tibia use it. RULES (one constructor each in the standards rule union, each a refusal with the fix printed): RepoEntry = scripts/init.ps1 and scripts/workflow.ps1 exist and are the only CI entry points; WorkflowShape = .github/workflows holds only gh-pages.yml + actions.lock and every run step calls scripts/workflow.ps1 (shared with CI-WORKFLOW-NAMES); NoUntypedRows = no tracked .tsv/.rows/.csv/.txt/.py outside an allowlist of upstream vendored dirs (DATA-ROWS-TYPED, no-py-txt-in-repos); LibNotCopied = a definition whose normalized digest equals a spiral/lib definition is refused in an app (consumes KIT-REUSE-METRICS Exact classes). ROLLOUT ORDER (by drift found): tibia, fiscal, x, spiral, then the small repos; each repo gets a root standards.spi with its exceptions written as typed rows, ratchet mode first (legacy counted, new refused). DoD: kit standards <repo> --ecosystem prints the four verdicts for every own repo; the root standards.spi lands unstaged in each; the edit hook kit-style-check.ps1 reads it; node CLI tests: one refusal and one pass per rule. NEXT: add the RepoEntry rule (simplest, no parsing) with its two tests in kit_standards (CPU-light). TYPEFEATURES: rule union with a phantom scope marker (:! RepoScope / :! PackageScope) so a repo rule cannot be applied to a package.")
inl note_of_kit_standards_ecosystem_2 () = Note ("KIT-STANDARDS-ECOSYSTEM", 2u32, "2026-11-01T17:01Z", "kit", "11-01 13:1x kit: NoUntypedRows gets a companion: drafts belong in the kit drafts store (KIT-DRAFTS-STORE), so the refusal message points to kit drafts put <repo> <ITEM> instead of only saying no.")
inl migrate_tibia_tsv () = Task ("MIGRATE-TIBIA-TSV", "tibia: the 61 tracked .tsv files become typed .spi row modules (DATA-ROWS-TYPED discipline) via kit table to-spi, readers switched to the typed rows", TaskKind, P1, D3, 100u32, Done, "DATA-ROWS-TYPED", "tibia,kit,drift,standards,team:tibia,team:kit,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_migrate_tibia_tsv () = Planning ("MIGRATE-TIBIA-TSV", Minutes 240u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_migrate_tibia_tsv_1 () = Note ("MIGRATE-TIBIA-TSV", 1u32, "2026-11-01T14:45Z", "kit", "11-01 10:4x kit census (git ls-files): tibia 61 .tsv, 0 in other own repos. Runs as main-thread light work in the tibia lane plus kit jobs, no subagents (user round 5 item 8). DoD: 0 tracked .tsv in tibia; each former tsv has a .spi rows module with a round-trip test; PR-1 and PR-12 analyses give identical numbers before and after (tibia's acceptance). NEXT: install kit table to-spi (kit_table_rows node-proven 1/1), convert the 5 largest files first. TYPEFEATURES: one row union per table family, not list string.")
inl note_of_migrate_tibia_tsv_2 () = Note ("MIGRATE-TIBIA-TSV", 2u32, "2026-11-01T18:18Z", "tibia-lane", "11-01 14:2x tibia: SCOPE EXTENDED by the junk census (TIBIA-HARVEST, same time): besides the 61 .tsv, the 4 results/*.txt (path-check-pc1/pc2, arena-spot-scan-r12, arena-region-r45) become typed rows, and the 4 analysis/*.ps1 become Spiral functions over the typed rows; the DoD's PR-1/PR-12 identical-numbers check runs those ports against the old scripts before the .ps1 and the text tables are archived (scratch-archive.ps1, never deleted). This item IS DATA-ROWS-TYPED part 3 (n9's arena_rows.spi GADT reader plan); work happens here. Merges with main's incoming Pages-HTML item: the HTML view is generated from the typed rows, so .tsv never comes back as the published form. Next step: unchanged, kit table to-spi on results/*.tsv when kit reports it installed.")
inl note_of_migrate_tibia_tsv_3 () = Note ("MIGRATE-TIBIA-TSV", 3u32, "2026-11-01T18:38Z", "main", "main 14:4x (user 11-01 14:0x: 'for ci-only results, tsvs need to be abandoned asap from all repos, change to something gh pages readable like maybe html itself, so it can be enriched indefinitely'). Per tibia's census (TIBIA-HARVEST n6), this item carries tibia's 61 .tsv, 4 results/*.txt and 4 analysis/*.ps1: typed Spiral rows first, an HTML view generated from them in CI, then the old files archived. The generic pieces are separate items: PAGES-FILE-VIEWERS, DIR-TREE-MARKDOWN-GFM, PAGES-NOTEBOOK-PUBLISH, DIR-TREE-LANE-METRICS. Other repos' CI-only .tsv follow this pattern under DATA-ROWS-TYPED.")
inl note_of_migrate_tibia_tsv_4 () = Note ("MIGRATE-TIBIA-TSV", 4u32, "2026-11-01T22:06Z", "tibia-lane", "11-01 18:1x tibia: READER WRITTEN, unstaged in tibia/results (new package: package.spiproj + spiral.toml with lib; modules table_rows, arena_rows). DATA CENSUS (all 61 files): 4 shapes: 10 cols (1 file, 10 rows), 13 (2, 75), 15 (42, 990) = 1,075 arena rows; 5-col arm steps (16 files, 284 rows). No width mismatches. 398 rows have empty cells, all meaningful, so the reader types them: cleared_ms empty (174) = NotCleared; experience/lowest_health/actions_* empty together (12, nexbot check/diagnostic) = NoClientData; foreign_* empty (242) = NotRecorded (spiral rows record 0 0, so empty means not recorded, not 'not foreign'). TYPES (n9 plan, refined): phantom v10/v13/v15; ':| arena_row v = Base :: base_run -> arena_row v10 | WithEconomy :: base_run * economy -> arena_row v13 | WithForeign :: base_run * economy * foreign -> arena_row v15'; existential 'some_arena_row = \\? v. arena_row v'; closed competitor (8 cases) and monster (4) plus monster_spec = Lone | Mix (list (monster * i32)) for 'cyclops:1,troll:2'; row_rejection = WrongWidth | NotANumber column | UnknownCompetitor | UnknownMonster; decoding chains a 'decoded t = Decoded | Rejected' through 'then'. Names go through lib case_labels (case_of_label, derived from the union's case names; no hand-written string table, per user 17:4x), which is gating now. KIT SIDE (kit 17:4x): to-spi now emits 'rows ()' and takes '--open <module>'; tibia owns 'table_rows' (union table_row = Columns | Row), row_of_table adapts. Tests: 3 schema summaries, 2 rejections, the mixed camp (lua + rust); format clean, budget 0. Next: case_labels land, then a local run of results/, then to-spi over the 61 files with --open table_rows once kit's TRAIN8 installs, and the recount test.")
inl note_of_migrate_tibia_tsv_5 () = Note ("MIGRATE-TIBIA-TSV", 5u32, "2026-11-02T00:04Z", "tibia-lane", "11-01 20:0x tibia: CITATION INVENTORY for the BENCHMARK.md rewrite (DoD item), done now so the rewrite is mechanical once to-spi installs. docs/BENCHMARK.md holds 42 citations of result files (38 distinct): 35 .tsv, 2 .txt, 5 analysis .ps1. One cited path never existed: results/arena-results-cyclops-pr3.tsv (L124, L126) is PR-3's PRE-REGISTERED output path, and the run happened as PR-3a (L138, arena-results-cyclops-pr3a.tsv). The rewrite keeps that citation as the plan's text and points nowhere new; no module is invented for a run that didn't happen. 30 of the 61 .tsv are cited by no doc (only by backlog notes): they still convert, and the recount test covers them. Rewrite rule: each results/<name>.tsv citation becomes results/<name>.spi, read through results/arena_rows.spi; the 5 analysis/*.ps1 citations become the Spiral function names once those ports exist.")
inl note_of_migrate_tibia_tsv_6 () = Note ("MIGRATE-TIBIA-TSV", 6u32, "2026-11-02T00:06Z", "tibia-lane", "11-01 20:1x tibia: the 4 analysis/*.ps1 are LOG analyses, not .tsv readers. pc3-analysis.ps1 parses raw OTClient arena logs (knight hits 'due to your attack', 'step away from', 'You gained N experience points', each with @t+ms) into the 2000 ms swing-grid residual check of PC-3 (BENCHMARK L224, re-used for PR-12 at L511). Their documented inputs are gone: C:\\Users\\i574n\\tibia-local\\arena-logs holds 4 logs (three 10-14 runs plus one diagnose log), not PC-3's 5 or PR-12's 40, presumably lost in the 10-28 wipe. So 'identical numbers on the original inputs' is no longer checkable for these four. Their ports are pinned on synthetic fixtures plus a parity run against the .ps1 on the 4 remaining logs, and BENCHMARK keeps the published numbers as historical, citing the ported function. The PR-1/PR-12 .tsv analyses (kit experiment analyze) are unaffected; their inputs are the committed .tsv. Port home: apps/canary, beside lead_trace/ground_trace, which already parse these logs.")
inl note_of_migrate_tibia_tsv_7 () = Note ("MIGRATE-TIBIA-TSV", 7u32, "2026-11-02T00:24Z", "tibia-lane", "11-01 20:2x tibia: analysis port 1 of 4 WRITTEN (unstaged): results/hit_grid.spi ports analysis/pc3-analysis.ps1. It has typed events (event_kind = Hit | Step | Kill with the @t+ms stamp), a grid record ({ grid_ms; tolerance_ms; think_ms }, pc3_grid () = 2000/60/1000 as in PC-3), a stable insertion sort by stamp (the script sorts), and a fold with the script's exact pairing rules: a kill between two hits drops the pair, a step sends it to the step buckets (on grid / inside think+tolerance / other), else plain (on / off grid); residual = gap mod grid. Test (lua + rust) on a synthetic 7-line log pins (1, 1, 0, 1, 0), hand-checked against the script's rules. Registered in results/package.spiproj and spiral.toml; format clean; budget 0 over (6 'mention' findings are the @t stamps, non-blocking). Next: a parity run against the .ps1 on the 4 surviving logs (needs a small file-reading entry), then the other 3 ports (pc4, pr12-secondary, pz-in-traces), then archive analysis/*.ps1. UPDATE 20:3x: the parity run is VACUOUS. pc3-analysis.ps1 over the 4 surviving logs prints all zeros, because those logs contain no hit/step/kill lines at all (counted per log: hit 0, step 0, kill 0). So the port's evidence is by construction: a second test (step on grid, step far off it) joins the first, and together they drive every branch of the script (plain on/off grid, step on grid / in window / other, kill exclusion). BENCHMARK's PC-3/PR-12 numbers stay as published history. No log-reading entry is needed for these ports until new arena logs exist.")
inl note_of_migrate_tibia_tsv_8 () = Note ("MIGRATE-TIBIA-TSV", 8u32, "2026-11-02T00:39Z", "tibia-lane", "11-01 20:4x tibia: analysis port 2 of 4 WRITTEN (unstaged): results/walk_pace.spi ports analysis/pc4-analysis.ps1. It parses 'knight-straight step x,y,z -> x2,y2,z; interval after its KIND step N ms' lines into typed straight_step { direction = sign dx + 10 * sign dy; after_straight; interval }, keeps an interval only when the previous step was knight-straight in the same direction (else counts a turn; direction resets per log, as the script does), takes the median of the kept intervals, and maps it to a closed pace_verdict = FormulaSpeed | BaseSpeed | NeitherBand with PC-4's bands (486+60, 702+-30) as a record. The median is kept DOUBLED (an exact integer even when the script's even-count median is x.5) and compared to doubled bands. Test (lua + rust): straight, straight, turn -> 1 kept, 2 turns, FormulaSpeed. Format clean, budget 0 over. Parity run vacuous here too (no surviving logs hold these lines). Next: pr12-secondary and pz-in-traces, then archive analysis/*.ps1.")
inl note_of_migrate_tibia_tsv_9 () = Note ("MIGRATE-TIBIA-TSV", 9u32, "2026-11-02T01:01Z", "tibia-lane", "11-01 21:0x tibia: analysis ports DECIDED for all 4. WRITTEN (unstaged, results/ package): hit_grid.spi (pc3-analysis), walk_pace.spi (pc4-analysis), secondary_hits.spi (pr12-secondary: per-run start marker, 10 s early/late hit split, floor-6 flag; the summary keeps exact integer sums plus run counts, so the script's 2-decimal mean rounding is derived, not stored). KEPT AS GLUE: pz-in-traces.ps1. Its counting already lives in a kit verb ('kit table sequences' with --count/--in-set over the protection-zone listing), and the script is ~30 lines of argument glue around one tool call, the junk census's glue class; it moves with live/ when PS1-TO-SPIRAL ports the runners. The 3 .ps1 with Spiral ports get archived after their ports are green in CI. A node test run of the whole results package (with the 'then' -> 'and_then' fix) is in flight.")
inl note_of_migrate_tibia_tsv_10 () = Note ("MIGRATE-TIBIA-TSV", 10u32, "2026-11-02T01:38Z", "tibia-lane", "11-01 21:4x tibia: results/ package node runs. hit_grid 2/2, walk_pace 1/1, secondary_hits 1/1, table_rows 1/1 PASS (job-1793582213350). arena_rows needed two fixes found by the node: (1) the existential 'some_arena_row = \\? v. arena_row v' can't be built at runtime ('Cannot dyn an existential into a runtime var'; existentials are compile-time only), so it's now a runtime union 'AtV10 (arena_row v10) | AtV13 (arena_row v13) | AtV15 (arena_row v15)'; the version-indexed GADT stays, and code holding an arena_row v15 still reads foreign without an option; (2) on Lua, the generated decoder hit Lua's limit of 60 upvalues per function ('function at line 503 has more than 60 upvalues', from the chained partial applications in base_of), so arena_rows' tests are pinned to rust: it's a host-side analysis that never runs inside the Lua client. Rust re-run in flight (job tibia-arena-rows-4). A backend note for the compiler lane: deep and_then chains can exceed Lua 5.1's 60-upvalue cap; a codegen that lifts captured cells into a table would avoid it.")
inl note_of_migrate_tibia_tsv_11 () = Note ("MIGRATE-TIBIA-TSV", 11u32, "2026-11-02T01:48Z", "tibia-lane", "11-01 21:4x tibia: results/ package ALL GREEN on the node. arena_rows 4/4 on rust (job-1793583861151, build exit 0), after the runtime-union fix and one fixture fix: the mixed-camp line had experience=90 with the other four client columns empty, a partial client block the decoder correctly rejects (the census's 12 real no-client rows have all five empty), so the fixture now matches the data. With hit_grid 2/2, walk_pace 1/1, secondary_hits 1/1 and table_rows 1/1 earlier. Unstaged. Next: to-spi over the 61 .tsv with --open table_rows once TRAIN8 installs, then the recount test.")
inl note_of_migrate_tibia_tsv_12 () = Note ("MIGRATE-TIBIA-TSV", 12u32, "2026-11-02T02:59Z", "tibia-lane", "11-01 23:0x tibia: CONVERSION DONE, verified (unstaged, untracked): kit table to-spi (kit.exe 7C559D83) ran over all 61 results/*.tsv with --open table_rows into results/rows/<name with _ for ->.spi. Per file, kit's rows= and columns= equal the .tsv's data lines and header width: 61 of 61, 0 mismatches, 1,359 rows total = the census's 1,075 arena + 284 arm-step rows. CONTENT CHECK: every generated Row [ ... ] literal, unescaped and tab-joined, equals its .tsv line byte for byte: 1,359 compared, 0 differing (empty cells kept as \\\"\\\"). Placement: results/rows/, a subfolder, because generated lines exceed the cell budget and build.ps1 lists results/*.spi non-recursively (budget and tests skip rows/); the modules are NOT yet registered in results/package.spiproj, so no build compiles them until the next step proves them. NEXT (one step, node-proven before it lands in the package): add a 'rows/' folder block to results/package.spiproj and spiral.toml, a recount test in arena_rows (decoded rows per module = the counts above, 0 rejections, via row_of_table over each module's rows ()), then the BENCHMARK citation rewrite (inventory n5) and archive of the .tsv.")
inl note_of_migrate_tibia_tsv_13 () = Note ("MIGRATE-TIBIA-TSV", 13u32, "2026-11-02T14:26Z", "tibia", "11-02 tibia, step 2 (unstaged). results/package.spiproj and spiral.toml now register a rows/ folder block (61 modules, after table_rows) and a new module results/row_census.spi. Census: the 284 arm-step rows over 16 tables decode with 0 rejections (green on the node, job tibia-row-census-2). The 1,075 arena rows over 45 tables cannot compile as one Rust test: each row is a 15-string list literal, rustc peaked at 10.4 GB with 0.1 GB left free on the node, and the cell timed out at 300 s even after the decoders became join points. That half is parked at %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\tibia\\MIGRATE-TIBIA-TSV\\row_census.full.spi (sha D77BCDD575CA). It returns once kit table to-spi can emit one string literal per row (requested from kit 11-02), plus a Line case in table_rows. arena_rows stays green 4/4 with rows/ registered. Next: re-emit rows/ in the compact form, restore the arena census, then the BENCHMARK citation rewrite and the .tsv archive.")
inl note_of_migrate_tibia_tsv_14 () = Note ("MIGRATE-TIBIA-TSV", 14u32, "2026-11-02T14:33Z", "tibia", "11-02 tibia: the format question with kit is settled. kit declined per-row joined strings (EncodedRow drift, banned by AGENTS.md) and suggested reading the TSV at test time. tibia declined that, since this item archives the .tsv files out of the repo. Asked instead for kit's typed whole-table literal (one string per table, decoded once at runtime, counted by kit's census). Plan: regenerate all 61 tables in that form, replacing the per-row Row lists, and restore the arena census from the draft. Fallback if kit declines: per-table census tests (CPU-heavy).")
inl note_of_migrate_tibia_tsv_15 () = Note ("MIGRATE-TIBIA-TSV", 15u32, "2026-11-02T15:48Z", "tibia", "11-02 tibia: results/*.txt decided by a rule fixed before looking (a file only cited by docs becomes an .md evidence page; a file a tool reads moves to its real format together with its reader). Done now, unstaged: results/path-check-pc1.txt and results/path-check-pc2.txt are DELETED, replaced by results/path-check-pc1.md and path-check-pc2.md (a heading plus the verbatim log in a text fence, body byte-identical, 76 and 48 lines), and docs/BENCHMARK.md:153 and :203 now cite the .md files. Kept for now: results/arena-region-r45.txt and results/arena-spot-scan-r12.txt, because apps/map/run.ps1:39-49 reads and rewrites them (map golden and live-scan agreement); they move with that logic in TIBIA-MAP-GRIDS-TYPED. ALSO: kit implemented to-spi --whole (one Table string per table, ships in TRAIN12; node candidate at C:\\home\\remote\\kit-verify\\kit\\src\\target\\debug\\kit.exe). Next: add | Table :: string -> table_row to results/table_rows.spi, regenerate the 61 tables with --whole, restore the arena census from the draft.")
inl note_of_migrate_tibia_tsv_16 () = Note ("MIGRATE-TIBIA-TSV", 16u32, "2026-11-02T16:40Z", "tibia", "11-02 tibia, step 3 (unstaged). All 61 tables were regenerated with kit table to-spi --whole --open table_rows (the node candidate kit, TRAIN12 pending); each round-trips byte-identical to its .tsv (61/61, checked locally). results/table_rows.spi is now one case, Table : string, with text_of and a typed fold_data that skips the header and blank lines, plus a test. The unused Row/Columns cases, line_of and arena_rows.row_of_table are removed. results/row_census.spi folds every table through arena_rows.row_of_line / arm_step_of_line. Node proof (job tibia-census-whole): row_census 2/2, all 1,075 arena rows and 284 arm-step rows decode with 0 rejections, in 45 s (was a 10 GB rustc and a timeout); table_rows 1/1; arena_rows 4/4. The .tsv files are NOT deleted yet: live/run-arena.ps1 and live/block.ps1 still write them, and scripts/score.ps1:39 and live/arena-summary.ps1 read them. Asked kit (11-02) for kit table query/logs/append to read and write whole-table .spi, so the live harness writes results/rows/*.spi directly. Then: retarget the 4 scripts and BENCHMARK's 53 citations, delete the 61 .tsv files (deletion rule: unstaged, paths listed), and the item is done.")
inl note_of_migrate_tibia_tsv_17 () = Note ("MIGRATE-TIBIA-TSV", 17u32, "2026-11-02T19:43Z", "tibia", "11-02 tibia: kit's .spi table I/O is done and proven (vultr candidate), and it ships in TRAIN13. The format is chosen by extension: kit table query/diff read .spi; kit table logs --out x.spi writes one Table literal with open table_rows; and the new kit table append <x.spi> <cells...> | --row-tsv appends one row atomically (via .pending plus a rename), refusing width mismatches and tab/newline cells. PLAN once TRAIN13 is installed (not before, or the scripts break against the current kit): live/run-arena.ps1 appends to results/rows/<name>.spi; live/block.ps1 runs table logs --out results/rows/<name>_secondary.spi; live/arena-summary.ps1 and scripts/score.ps1:39 query the .spi; then BENCHMARK.md's 53 citations move to results/rows/*.spi and the 61 .tsv files are deleted (unstaged, paths listed); publish.ps1 drops *.tsv from -Include.")
inl note_of_migrate_tibia_tsv_18 () = Note ("MIGRATE-TIBIA-TSV", 18u32, "2026-11-03T03:53Z", "tibia", "11-02 23:5x tibia, MIGRATION DONE in the tree (unstaged), right after kit's TRAIN13c install (AF229BEC). Readers/writers retargeted through new helpers in live/paths.ps1 ($TibiaKit, Read-ResultRows = kit table query --cols --quiet parsed as TSV, New-ResultTable = header-only table via kit table to-spi --whole --open table_rows, Add-ResultRow = kit table append --row-tsv, atomic and width-checked): live/run-arena.ps1 (new tables and rows are whole-table .spi), live/block.ps1 (reads rows through Read-ResultRows; secondary table <name>_secondary.spi beside it via kit table logs --out), live/arena-summary.ps1 (default results/rows/arena_results_cyclops.spi), scripts/score.ps1 (Get-RetreatLogs reads rows/arena_results_cyclops_pr12/pr16.spi), scripts/publish.ps1 (*.tsv dropped from -Include), README's example -Out path, and docs/BENCHMARK.md (all 47 arena-results citations plus the -cyclops-*/-hunter-* shorthands now cite results/rows/arena_results_*.spi; only target/ scratch tables keep .tsv; the one cited module that does not exist, arena_results_cyclops_pr3, is PR-3's registered output path that was never run, pr3a ran). PROOFS (local, installed kit): kit table query on arena_results_cyclops_pc10 prints identical output from .tsv and .spi; experiment analyze PR-1 reports identical row counts from both (spiral-retreat 31/60, vbot 30/60, interim refusal) and PR-12 refuses identically (verdict exists, one analysis per registration, so no ledger write was forced); score.ps1's inputs pr12/pr16 give identical spiral-retreat rows (40, 20); a scratch New-ResultTable + 2 Add-ResultRow + Read-ResultRows round trip works and matches the existing module format. DELETED from the worktree, unstaged: all 61 results/*.tsv (no reference left in tibia or any sibling repo outside backlog history; content in git and in the round-trip-verified rows/*.spi modules). Left: a live block run (box, live world) to exercise run-arena/block end to end; new live tables also need a rows/ manifest entry to join row_census.")
inl note_of_migrate_tibia_tsv_19 () = Note ("MIGRATE-TIBIA-TSV", 19u32, "2026-11-03T10:26Z", "tibia", "11-03 06:2x tibia, LIVE BLOCK ATTEMPT BLOCKED BY MEMORY, not code: a one-hunt smoke block (live/block.ps1 -Results rows/arena_results_smoke_1103.spi -Runs 1 -Competitors spiral-policy -Monster troll, 30-min window, StartBy +15 min) asked kit for a live-gui window, and kit refused it for 14 min: 'reason=low-memory free_mb=2394..2483 need_mb=4096'; the block exited cleanly with no table written. Box: 15.9 GB total, 2.6 GB free; the largest holders are 8 Claude lane sessions (about 4.1 GB together) plus browsers, none tibia's to stop. kit window run takes --min-free-mb, but DECIDED not to lower it: the live world's footprint (MariaDB + Canary + one OTClient) is unmeasured, and running with about 1 GB left risks the low-RAM state that has killed lane sessions (main-crash-transcript-size). NEXT: retry when free memory is >= 4 GB (fewer sessions open), or measure the world's peak RSS on the node first and request a window sized from that. This live run is the last step for both MIGRATE-TIBIA-TSV (run-arena writing typed .spi rows end to end) and TIBIA-MAP-GRIDS-TYPED.")
inl note_of_migrate_tibia_tsv_20 () = Note ("MIGRATE-TIBIA-TSV", 20u32, "2026-11-04T04:17Z", "tibia", "11-04 00:17 tibia GROOM: parked to Planned (no note in 12 h). The migration is done in the tree. NEXT: one-hunt smoke block (live/block.ps1 -Runs 1 -Monster troll) once kit grants a live-gui window (needs 4096 MB free; refused 11-03 at about 2.4 GB).")
inl note_of_migrate_tibia_tsv_21 () = Note ("MIGRATE-TIBIA-TSV", 21u32, "2026-11-04T04:21Z", "tibia", "11-04 00:21 tibia: back to Active, percent kept. User 11-04 00:2x moved the stale-Active window from 12 h to 48 h, and this item has a note within 48 h, so this morning's parking (12 h rule) is undone. The next step in the 00:2x note still holds.")
inl note_of_migrate_tibia_tsv_22 () = Note ("MIGRATE-TIBIA-TSV", 22u32, "2026-11-04T07:35Z", "tibia", "11-04 03:3x tibia: DONE, LIVE SMOKE PASSED. The box had about 5.1 GB free, so kit granted the live-gui window at once (win-...-1504, 30 min). live/block.ps1 -Results rows/arena_results_smoke_1104.spi -Runs 1 -Competitors spiral-policy -Monster troll ran one live hunt against the Canary world: row 2026-11-04T03:34:29 spiral-policy run 1 troll, count 3, experience 90, cleared_ms 121543, lowest_health 181, deaths 0. It was written through kit's .spi table I/O (New-ResultTable as a header-only open table_rows Table literal, then Add-ResultRow = kit table append --row-tsv, atomic and width-checked), and kit table query reads it back with the right columns. The secondary path also ran on .spi: kit table logs wrote arena_results_smoke_1104_secondary.spi (1 log, arm spiral-policy) and kit table query grouped it. Boosted creature was Defiler, not a spawned kind, so the run is comparable. So the migrated readers and writers work live end to end, not only in node regression suites. tibia has no tracked .tsv left (part 3 of DATA-ROWS-TYPED, now Done). The two smoke tables stay unstaged in tibia/results/rows.")
