inl note_of_compiler_win_stack_overflow_1 () = Note ("COMPILER-WIN-STACK-OVERFLOW", 1u32, "2026-11-01T17:20Z", "fiscal", "Found by fiscal 2026-11-01. Windows only: SpiralCompilerCore.dll (pack14a, core DBC5EC18F14C) dies with 0xC00000FD STATUS_STACK_OVERFLOW compiling 7 fiscal generated units to Rust: generated_nfe_v4, generated_cte_v4_00, generated_cte_os_v4_00, generated_cte_simp_v4_00, generated_gtve_v4_00, generated_cfdi_v4_0_certificates, generated_cfdi_pagos_2_0_certificates. Ubuntu CI passes the same tree. Cells reports only \"compiler socket failed: :closed\". Repro on the node, mirror t1101, unit sha 4316C7140D88, via run_notebook.ps1: server mode and SPIRAL_CELLS_COMPILER=process both crash (4 events, fault offsets 0xac06d, 0xbbe94, 0x1795d3, 0x178a42). DOTNET_DefaultStackSize=0x4000000 does NOT help (same offsets 0x178a42 and 0x1795d3 again), so the overflowing stack is not a default-sized thread: suspect the dotnet.exe main thread (1.5 MB PE-header stack on Windows vs 8 MB ulimit on Linux) or an explicitly undersized thread. Next step: map offsets 0x178a42 and 0x1795d3 in the R2R SpiralCompilerCore.dll to methods, and move that compile phase onto the 1 GB worker thread. Done = fiscal CI windows-2025 runs all 7 units green on the rust target. Repro script: node C:\\home\\remote-stage\\node-socket-repro.ps1; events come from Get-WinEvent Application.")
inl note_of_compiler_win_stack_overflow_2 () = Note ("COMPILER-WIN-STACK-OVERFLOW", 2u32, "2026-11-01T17:40Z", "compiler", "11-01 13:40 compiler: ROOT CAUSE (from the host source; node proof running). The host's compile-server loop (Program.fs serveSessions, used by both the TCP and Unix-socket servers, i.e. every Cells compile in server AND process mode, which both go through the socket) runs compileOne inside watchStall, and watchStall calls compile () ON THE CALLING THREAD. That thread is the dotnet.exe main thread: about 1.5 MB on Windows from the PE header, against an 8 MB ulimit on Linux, which is why Ubuntu passes. DOTNET_DefaultStackSize does not change the main thread, which explains fiscal's negative test. Batch mode (--batch, Program.fs:898) has the same flaw: Task.Run puts compileOne on a pool thread. The direct one-shot path (Program.fs:1390) already compiles on a 1 GB worker thread. CONFIRMED on node mirror t1101: `dotnet SpiralCompiler.dll --backend Rust generated_nfe_v4.spi` (installed pack14a) exits 0 in 40 s, and `--test-blocks` exits 0 (3 blocks), while the same unit under Cells dies with 0xC00000FD. So the module code is fine and only the thread differs. The offset mapping fiscal suggested is not needed. FIX (host only, core untouched): a startOnCompileStack helper runs a job on a fresh 1 GB-reserve background thread and returns a Task. watchStall's two compile calls and batch's Task.Run use it, and exceptions are rethrown unchanged through GetAwaiter().GetResult(). PROGRAM: %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\spiral\\COMPILER-WIN-STACK-OVERFLOW\\host-compile-stack.kitdo (4 replacements). On a scratch copy of the live host 420E1452DB4B3194 it gives 98D282B561516F39 (+10/-3). TYPEFEATURESET: none needed; the change is thread placement and returns the same Task<Result<...>>. NEXT: node job job-1793554794824-compiler-winso builds pack14a core (F6F6879566C5A830) + the fixed host, then runs a control arm (installed, nfe_v4, process mode) and the candidate on all 7 units in process mode plus nfe_v4 in server mode. If all 7 are green it becomes a host-only candidate for main to install, and DONE stays fiscal windows-2025 CI green on the 7 units.")
inl note_of_compiler_win_stack_overflow_3 () = Note ("COMPILER-WIN-STACK-OVERFLOW", 3u32, "2026-11-01T18:44Z", "compiler", "11-01 14:45 compiler: PROVEN, LANDED, CANDIDATE READY. Node job job-1793555970589-compiler-winso-server-ab alternated arms on generated_nfe_v4 in Cells server mode. installed (pack14a, host DFC86B91968D447D): r1 exit 1 in 90 s, 8 stack-overflow events, 0/3 passed, 'compiler socket failed: :closed'; r2 the same (77 s, 8 events). candidate (host 303AB40A3B4A90DE, core DBC5EC18F14C354C identical to installed): r1 exit 0 in 516 s, 0 events, 3/3; r2 exit 0 in 486 s, 0 events, 3/3. Earlier job job-1793554794824: the candidate passed all 7 fiscal units in process mode (nfe_v4 3/3, cte 2/2, cte_os 2/2, cte_simp 2/2, gtve 2/2, cfdi_v4_0 1/1, cfdi_pagos 1/1; 0 events), plus nfe_v4 in server mode. Process mode never crashed even on installed: it compiles one-shot on the 1 GB path, which explains why only the server path overflowed. LANDED: apps/compiler/tmp/compiler/host/Program.fs 420E1452DB4B3194 -> 98D282B561516F39 (unstaged, +10/-3, from the drafts program). CANDIDATE FOR MAIN: %LOCALAPPDATA%\\spiral-bin-candidates\\pack15a, the node-built binaries fetched to the box (the bytes the proof ran: SpiralCompiler.dll 303AB40A3B4A90DE, SpiralCompilerCore.dll DBC5EC18F14C354C, 33 files, the same set as pack14a), with source\\ holding the core (F6F6879566C5A830 = pack14a) and the fixed host. Residual predict: none needed, because the core DLL is byte-identical; only thread placement changed. DONE still needs (1) main installs pack15a on box and node, and (2) the user pushes spiral (Program.fs) so fiscal's windows-2025 CI builds the fixed host; then fiscal confirms the 7 units green.")
inl note_of_compiler_win_stack_overflow_4 () = Note ("COMPILER-WIN-STACK-OVERFLOW", 4u32, "2026-11-01T18:46Z", "fiscal", "fiscal correction to n1: the \"process mode also crashes\" claim is WRONG. My second run set SPIRAL_CELLS_SERVER=0 + SPIRAL_CELLS_COMPILER=process, but run_notebook.ps1 kept the TCP server: its output still said \"compiler socket failed: :closed\", which only server mode prints. So all 4 crashes were server mode, consistent with the compiler lane's cause (server compiles on the ~1.5 MB dotnet main thread). Fiscal will confirm windows-2025 green on all 7 units on the first CI run with pack15a.")
inl note_of_compiler_win_stack_overflow_5 () = Note ("COMPILER-WIN-STACK-OVERFLOW", 5u32, "2026-11-01T20:29Z", "main", "main 16:2x pack15a INSTALLED on both machines. Box: install-when-clear ran at 15:25 when kit-TRAIN7c ended (install exit 0; undo spiral-bin\\compiler-installs\\pack15a-20261101-152544). Node: main's chained node-ship of the box's installed net11.0 (zip digest F6116B0F2C030082, smoke = host SpiralCompiler.dll starts with 303AB40A3B4A90DE) aborted 3 times with 'target in use' while node jobs held the DLLs, and succeeded on try 4. Previous node folder kept as net11.0.replaced-pack15a; logged in node-upkeep.log. Fiscal's windows-2025 CI goes green only after the user pushes spiral's Program.fs.")
inl note_of_compiler_win_stack_overflow_6 () = Note ("COMPILER-WIN-STACK-OVERFLOW", 6u32, "2026-11-01T22:42Z", "main", "main 18:3x VULTR: still on core 7B234DAC (fiscal A3: its verdicts can't land). Shipping the box's installed folder failed: the box build is win-x64 ReadyToRun, so on Linux SpiralCompiler.dll throws BadImageFormatException. Rolled back within a minute, smoke-checked (usage text), unusable copy kept beside it. Fix: build from source on vultr after syncing spiral (Program.fs 98D282B5): build.ps1 -Mode single-flight, ~90 s per x-dumps, and compare by sourceSha16. Queued for the next free vultr slot under the one-job rule (a lane's Cells job was running at 18:3x).")
inl note_of_compiler_win_stack_overflow_7 () = Note ("COMPILER-WIN-STACK-OVERFLOW", 7u32, "2026-11-02T00:10Z", "fiscal", "fiscal 11-01 20:1x CONSUMER PROOF GREEN: fiscal CI run 41351043040 (push b2fab25), windows-2025 job, cloned spiral main 73e4d863 (2026-11-01T21:12Z): all 7 units pass. generated_nfe_v4 3/3; generated_cte_v4_00, cte_os_v4_00, cte_simp_v4_00, gtve_v4_00 2/2 each; generated_cfdi_v4_0_certificates and cfdi_pagos_2_0_certificates 1/1 each. Job summary: \"all 86 test files green and every example validates\"; ubuntu-24.04 also green. This is the first fully green fiscal run on Windows since at least 10-25. Nothing left on the consumer side.")
inl note_of_compiler_win_stack_overflow_8 () = Note ("COMPILER-WIN-STACK-OVERFLOW", 8u32, "2026-11-02T00:17Z", "compiler", "11-01 20:20 compiler: DONE. Consumer proof from fiscal (n7): fiscal CI run 41351043040 (b2fab25), windows-2025, cloned spiral main 73e4d863 (which carries the Program.fs fix landed 14:45). All 7 units pass: nfe_v4 3/3; cte, cte_os, cte_simp and gtve v4_00 2/2 each; cfdi_v4_0 and cfdi_pagos_2_0 certificates 1/1 each. 'all 86 test files green and every example validates'; fiscal's first fully green Windows run since at least 10-25. pack15a, carrying the fix, is installed on box and node (main, 15:25 / ~16:2x).")
inl note_of_compiler_win_stack_overflow_9 () = Note ("COMPILER-WIN-STACK-OVERFLOW", 9u32, "2026-11-02T01:20Z", "main", "main 21:2x CONSUMER PROOF: fiscal CI b2fab25 GREEN on ubuntu AND windows-2025 (all 86 test files, every example), the first Windows green since 10-25, on spiral with pack15a's Program.fs. Spiral b3fd292b is green on both OSes too. Compiler can close this item.")
inl spiral_cli_json_protocol_test () = Task ("SPIRAL-CLI-JSON-PROTOCOL-TEST", "Port the spiral CLI SPIRAL_JSON envelope tests (test_json_protocol.py) to a Spiral check", TaskKind, P3, D3, 90u32, Blocked, "", "compiler,spiral-cli,tests,spiral,team:compiler,compiler", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_spiral_cli_json_protocol_test () = Planning ("SPIRAL-CLI-JSON-PROTOCOL-TEST", Minutes 150u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_spiral_cli_json_protocol_test_1 () = Note ("SPIRAL-CLI-JSON-PROTOCOL-TEST", 1u32, "2026-11-01T17:24Z", "compiler", "11-01 13:27 compiler: filed on main's ruling (11-01: PORT, don't drop). WHAT: apps/spiral/test_json_protocol.py (4831 B, sha in the move receipt below) is unittest coverage of the spiral CLI's SPIRAL_JSON envelope, with six cases: success is one JSON document at every TRACE_LEVEL; a child exception is a failure carrying the original diagnostic; a CUDA driver failure is not reported as success; a Rust build failure is a failure carrying cargo's diagnostic; Lua print/write are the output; a Lua runtime error is a failure carrying its message. It also uses unicode and spaces in paths. Nothing has run it since 0ec501fb. MOVED byte-identical to %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\spiral\\SPIRAL-CLI-JSON-PROTOCOL-TEST\\test_json_protocol.py; git shows apps/spiral/test_json_protocol.py as unstaged D. EXISTING COVERAGE (checked): apps/spiral/build.ps1:100-129's run check covers SUCCESS ONLY, at the default trace level: rust/cuda/lua/gleam each print 42, the stdout must parse as one JSON document, and the output must match. Cells sets SPIRAL_JSON=1 on every run (targets.ex:745), so a broken success envelope already breaks Cells. NOT covered anywhere: the other trace levels, all four failure paths, and unicode/space paths. So this is a port, not a close. DEFINITION OF DONE: the five uncovered behaviours run on every CLI build, in the same place the success check runs, so CI's fresh CLI build fails when any of them regresses: (1) one JSON document at every TRACE_LEVEL value the CLI accepts; (2) child exception -> failure + original diagnostic; (3) CUDA driver failure -> not success; (4) cargo failure -> failure + cargo diagnostic; (5) Lua runtime error -> failure + message; all with a path containing a space and a non-ASCII character. Then the draft is archived. TYPEFEATURESET PLAN: a case is a record {backend : backend union (no strings); source; trace : trace_level union; expect : expectation}, where expectation = output_contains of string | failure_with of diagnostic_fragment | not_success. The checker decodes the envelope into a result union (ok of output | error of diagnostic) and matches expectation against it, so no field-presence probing on raw JSON. NEXT STEP: read the CLI's TRACE_LEVEL parser in apps/spiral/spiral.spi to list the accepted levels, then decide where the cases live. Preferred: a Spiral cell next to the CLI, run by Cells; fallback: data rows in build.ps1's existing case loop (thin glue, no new tool). MOVE RECEIPT: sha256 5B010564E3BF..., hash-verified after the move.")
inl note_of_spiral_cli_json_protocol_test_2 () = Note ("SPIRAL-CLI-JSON-PROTOCOL-TEST", 2u32, "2026-11-01T17:57Z", "compiler", "11-01 13:58 compiler: NEXT-STEP DONE (levels and placement). Accepted TRACE_LEVEL values: lib trace.spi's union trace_level = Verbose | Debug | Info | Warning | Critical (read with reflection.union_try_pick, so anything else counts as unset). spiral.spi run_main (1760-1784): with SPIRAL_JSON=1, tracing is switched off (trace_enabled <- false) and the result is printed whatever the level; without it, the result prints only at Info. So the 'one JSON document at every level' check needs 6 runs per case: the 5 levels plus unset. PLACEMENT DECISION: apps/spiral/build.ps1's run check (lines 100-129). It already builds the fresh CLI on every CI run, decodes the envelope, and fails the build, and it is the only place that runs right after the CLI is built. A Cells cell would test whatever spiral.exe the box has, and the box keeps a stale one (see the unsupported-cells memory), so that placement would miss exactly the regressions this guards against. The cases go in as rows of the existing @(...) case table, typed by their expectation (output_contains | failure_with | not_success) as a row field, not as new loops; no new tool. NEXT STEP: add the expectation field to the 4 existing rows, then the failure rows (lua runtime error, rust compile error, python raise for the child-exception case, cuda driver failure where the box has no GPU path), and the 6-level loop around the success rows, with a space and a non-ASCII character in checkDir.")
inl note_of_spiral_cli_json_protocol_test_3 () = Note ("SPIRAL-CLI-JSON-PROTOCOL-TEST", 3u32, "2026-11-01T18:02Z", "compiler", "11-01 14:02 compiler: PORT DRAFTED: %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\spiral\\SPIRAL-CLI-JSON-PROTOCOL-TEST\\build-run-check.kitdo (2 replacements on apps/spiral/build.ps1; live 41054bb7... becomes 847eca31...; the result parses with 0 errors). It turns the run check's case list into one table with a typed expectation per row: output (contains) | exact (trimmed, case-sensitive) | failure (nonzero exit, text in stdout+stderr, no \"command_result\" in stdout). The 4 existing rows keep their meaning. New rows port all six Python tests: the quoted child output at 6 trace levels (unset + the 5 union cases), child exception, CUDA driver failure, Rust build failure (cargo diagnostic), Lua print+write exact output (\"lua-print<TAB>1<NEWLINE>lua-write\"), and Lua runtime error. Every protocol file lives under \"protocol ü\" with spaces in the file names, as in the Python suite. TRACE_LEVEL is restored in the existing finally. NOT LANDED: it needs a fresh CLI build to prove, and the box is compile-quiet (kit-TRAIN7c heavy window). LAND WHEN: run `pwsh apps/spiral/build.ps1` (or its run-check part) with the program applied to a scratch copy; all 16 rows must pass on the box, and a deliberately broken row (e.g. expect \"failure\" on the success level row) must fail it. Then land unstaged, archive the draft .py, and close the item after the user's push turns CI green.")
inl note_of_spiral_cli_json_protocol_test_4 () = Note ("SPIRAL-CLI-JSON-PROTOCOL-TEST", 4u32, "2026-11-01T19:57Z", "compiler", "11-01 15:57 compiler: PROVEN AND LANDED. Proof on the node's CLI (C:\\home\\git\\spiral\\workspace\\target\\release\\spiral.exe, built 10-30): the patched run-check block, extracted verbatim into a harness (job-1793562714968-compiler-jsonproto2, 79 s), passed 16 of 16 rows. 4 original success rows; 6 trace levels (unset, Verbose, Debug, Info, Warning, Critical), each giving one JSON document with 'child output: \"quoted\"'; child exception, CUDA driver failure, Rust build failure and Lua error are each a nonzero exit with the diagnostic and no command_result; Lua print+write exact. NEGATIVE CONTROL: the same harness with two expectations deliberately wrong (child exception expected as output; Lua exact text '...2...' instead of '...1...') reports exactly those 2 failures, so the check can fail. LANDED: apps/spiral/build.ps1 41054BB7 -> 847ECA31 (unstaged, +37/-11, parses). ARCHIVED: the drafts folder (build-run-check.kitdo + test_json_protocol.py) via scratch-archive. git shows apps/spiral/test_json_protocol.py as unstaged D, already noted at the move. CLOSES when CI's fresh CLI build passes the extended run check after the user's push (then Done).")
inl note_of_spiral_cli_json_protocol_test_5 () = Note ("SPIRAL-CLI-JSON-PROTOCOL-TEST", 5u32, "2026-11-03T07:34Z", "compiler", "11-03 07:3x compiler GROOM (WIP cap: Active = touched in 12 h). Blocked on the user's push: the only step left is the next CI run after it, then Done.")
inl remote_cloud_linux_worker () = Task ("REMOTE-CLOUD-LINUX-WORKER", "Linux cloud workers (Vultr VM kit-vultr-sjc-1, then codespaces) as kit/remote.ps1 targets: OS-aware machine kinds, transport, bootstrap", Feature, P1, D4, 95u32, Planned, "LIB-GATE-LINUX-PORTABLE", "kit,team:kit,remote,linux,vultr,codespaces,x-dumps,multiplatform", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_remote_cloud_linux_worker () = Planning ("REMOTE-CLOUD-LINUX-WORKER", Minutes 480u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_remote_cloud_linux_worker_1 () = Note ("REMOTE-CLOUD-LINUX-WORKER", 1u32, "2026-11-01T17:28Z", "personal", "personal lane (session ec368ac8), 2026-11-01 13:17 UTC-3. The user rented Vultr instance kit-vultr-sjc-1 (id 6dfb484b-e1bf-4941-a545-6e6000d5e50c): plan vhp-2c-4gb-amd ($24/mo), region sjc, Ubuntu 26.04.1 LTS, 140.82.49.44, 2 vCPU EPYC-Rome, 3.3 GiB RAM + 8 GiB swap, 78 GB free. ssh alias `vultr` in ~/.ssh/config (key ~/.ssh/vultr_ed25519, HostKeyAlias kit-vultr-sjc-1). Measured: HF CDN 66 MB/s on a single stream, 290 MB/s with 6 streams (9 GB in 31 s), against >10 min for a partial remote scan from the box. Ingress is free on Vultr. Goal (user 11-01): a Linux worker for the X-archive downloads AND node work, making remote.ps1 and kit multiplatform, and dogfooding codespaces VMs the same way. Main (11-01): remote.ps1 assumes an ssh alias plus pwsh -EncodedCommand; codespaces have no remote-machines.json entry; kit owns the OS-aware work under EOIE-REMOTE-EXECUTOR. Next: kit lane decides the driver shape (a linux machine kind in remote-machines.json, bash or pwsh transport); personal installs pwsh+kit on the VM once kit says how.")
inl note_of_remote_cloud_linux_worker_2 () = Note ("REMOTE-CLOUD-LINUX-WORKER", 2u32, "2026-11-01T17:35Z", "kit", "11-01 13:35 kit DECISION (asked by the personal lane): shape (a) with pwsh now and (b) as the destination; never a bash dialect. WHY: remote.ps1 already takes a per-machine 'shell' and sends pwsh -EncodedCommand. pwsh on Linux keeps ONE transport and ONE remote-snippet language for both OSes, and every job verb goes through the remote kit job host (target.eoie), so the VM needs a Linux kit build anyway; that build is also step one of REMOTE-EXEC-S2 (kit job --on), which later replaces remote.ps1. SPLIT: the personal lane installs pwsh, syncs kit + spiral under /home/remote and builds kit-cli with -j1 (3.3 GiB plus swap; a failing Windows-only crate is LIB-GATE-LINUX-PORTABLE data), then reports the kit path and its gitRoot/mirrorBase/stage. kit (done, unstaged in i574n.github/scripts/remote.ps1): a machine 'kind' field (linux -> '/' separator, default Windows keeps '\\', so the live Windows node's paths are unchanged); Join-RemotePath replaces every Join-Path over target.gitRoot/stage/mirrorBase. The status snippet is portable: /proc/loadavg and MemAvailable on Linux, Win32 CIM on Windows, MachineName, DriveInfo of the cwd root, LocalApplicationData instead of LOCALAPPDATA, multi-segment Join-Path, and SPIRAL_EXE falling back to the process env. The lib digest is normalized to '\\', so box and Linux digests compare. Sync deps step: SymbolicLink on Linux (Junction on Windows) and Copy-Item instead of robocopy on Linux. Bug fixed along the way: the box-runner line used target.gitRoot instead of the local git root. REGRESSION on the Windows node: parse 0 errors; status prints the same lines (runner F1DD2E34, compiler DBC5EC18); sync kit -Mirror kit-verify REMOTE-SYNC-OK. NEXT: add the vultr entry to ~/.config/remote-machines.json (kind linux, shell pwsh, eoie = the Linux kit path) when the personal lane reports its paths; proof = remote.ps1 vultr status, then sync kit plus one notebook job.")
inl note_of_remote_cloud_linux_worker_3 () = Note ("REMOTE-CLOUD-LINUX-WORKER", 3u32, "2026-11-01T17:37Z", "kit", "11-01 13:37 kit: vultr entry added to ~/.config/remote-machines.json (kind linux, shell pwsh /usr/bin/pwsh 7.6.9, gitRoot /home/remote/git, mirrorBase /home/remote, stage /home/remote/stage, eoie /home/remote/git/kit/src/target/debug/kit; backup .before-vultr-20261101.bak). FIRST LINUX DRIVE OK: remote.ps1 vultr status -> 'kit-vultr-sjc-1 cpu load 0.78 free-mem 2.8 GB free-disk 66.6 GB', kit d486fd04, spiral 504e1e07, lib digest computed. Expected gaps: the kit binary (the personal lane's -j1 build is running) and the Spiral compiler/dotnet (node compiler missing), which a notebook job needs. NEXT: after the build, sync kit -Mirror plus one job through the Linux kit job host.")
inl note_of_remote_cloud_linux_worker_4 () = Note ("REMOTE-CLOUD-LINUX-WORKER", 4u32, "2026-11-01T17:58Z", "kit", "11-01 13:58 kit: FIRST LINUX PROOF GREEN via the multiplatform remote.ps1. The personal lane built kit-cli on Linux (exit 0, 2m18s at -j1, 335 MB debug, no Windows-only crate, no OOM) and installed dotnet 11.0.100-rc.3 via spiral's install-dotnet.ps1. 'remote.ps1 vultr sync kit -Mirror kit-verify' -> REMOTE-SYNC-OK, digest 4E7612B1595F0D9B, byte-identical to the Windows node's digest for the same tree, so the tree digest holds across OSes. Job job-1793555784366 through the Linux kit job host: agile init/begin/add/note ok (no-respell header written; elapsed_ms 0-1 on a tiny state); lint macros over kit/src -> violations 906, new 0, identical to Windows. BLOCKED: agile check needs the Spiral compiler (state attestation); the personal lane will build single-flight there, compared by build-identity sourceSha16 because SDKs differ. Lessons the personal lane hit by hand, both already in remote.ps1 sync: a plain clone of the box HEAD is not buildable because staged files are missing (sync carries 'git diff HEAD' + untracked), and box mtimes leave cargo stale (remote extract uses tar -xmf). My own proof-script mistakes, not Linux bugs: forgot 'agile begin' (lease) first; the begin title needs 3..7 words.")
inl note_of_remote_cloud_linux_worker_5 () = Note ("REMOTE-CLOUD-LINUX-WORKER", 5u32, "2026-11-01T18:09Z", "main", "main 11-01 14:1x (user): the personal lane leads x-dumps on this VM while making kit multiplatform. The VM's CPU and disk go to x-dumps FIRST; other lanes' jobs run there only when x-dumps leaves room. Codespaces quota is burned with useful runs, and nothing is lost: code, logs and outputs are fetched back before every stop/delete. Node switch status: the Windows node now runs kit.exe (shared store via junctions, KIT-RENAME n65), so OS-aware remote.ps1 targets kit on every machine. Follow-ups filed: K3S-EVERYWHERE, IGGY-PUBLIC-CLOUD, CLOUD-NODE-BACKUP, NODE-VALUE-METRICS.")
inl note_of_remote_cloud_linux_worker_6 () = Note ("REMOTE-CLOUD-LINUX-WORKER", 6u32, "2026-11-01T18:14Z", "kit", "11-01 14:14 kit: The personal lane built the Spiral compiler on vultr (single-flight Release, 89.7 s, SDK rc.3; Hopac.dll left IL after a crossgen2 failure). build-identity sourceSha16 f6f6879566c5a830 = the box pack14a's (box core DBC5EC18, vultr 7B234DAC): the same source, different det-root/SDK, comparable by source (Assert-NodeCore rule). Job job-1793556613190 found 3 gaps: (1) agile check -> 'spawn process: No such file or directory', because the kit job env on Linux lacks cargo and dotnet on PATH; KIT FIX: compiler discovery must find dotnet under the spiral cache dir (~/.cache/spiral-bin/toolchains/dotnet on Linux) like Get-SpiralCacheDir, not rely on PATH; (2) no Erlang/Elixir for Cells notebooks (asked the personal lane for the spiral-pinned versions); (3) the kit-verify mirror on vultr lacks spiral beside kit (notebook-batch resolves ../spiral); sync spiral before the notebook rerun. LIB-GATE-LINUX-PORTABLE data from the personal lane: spiral/apps/compiler/scripts/build.ps1:28 unrolled an empty @() to \\ on Linux (no /usr/share/dotnet), so .Count threw under strict mode; fixed on the box, unstaged, by wrapping the if in @(...).")
inl note_of_remote_cloud_linux_worker_7 () = Note ("REMOTE-CLOUD-LINUX-WORKER", 7u32, "2026-11-01T19:08Z", "personal", "personal 15:2x -04: vultr worker state. kit-cli builds on Linux (-j1 2m18s). Spiral compiler single-flight builds (89.7 s, sourceSha16 f6f6879566c5a830 = box). kit lane: multiplatform remote.ps1 drives vultr (kind linux, shell pwsh); sync is byte-identical to the node; the Linux job host ran agile + lint jobs identical to Windows. Installed: pwsh 7.6.9, rust nightly, dotnet 11.0.100-rc.3 (install-dotnet.ps1), OTP-29.1.2 + Elixir 1.20.5 (CI pins), /etc/environment PATH for dotnet + cargo. Fixed build.ps1:28 empty-@() unroll (Linux-only failure; relayed to compiler via kit). Remaining (kit): compiler discovery via LocalApplicationData on Linux, agile check + notebook proof. Traps: pwsh stdin to `ssh bash -s` gets CRLF (use base64), `sort > file` truncated /etc/environment on failure (restored from backup; write temp + mv), pkill -f matching its own ssh command line (anchor on the binary path).")
inl note_of_remote_cloud_linux_worker_8 () = Note ("REMOTE-CLOUD-LINUX-WORKER", 8u32, "2026-11-01T19:52Z", "kit", "11-01 15:52 kit: LINUX WORKER FULLY GREEN through remote.ps1 + the Linux kit job host. Job job-1793562586396: agile check ok (compiler=available, the Linux-built single-flight), notebooks 2/2 (agile_cadence 6/6, kit_bindings_model 2/2) via kit/scripts/notebook-batch.ps1. Setup steps that were needed, all light: (1) the spiral shared checkout's deps: cloned i574n/The-Spiral-Language into /home/remote/git/spiral/deps, plus mix local.hex/rebar and mix deps.get in apps/cells (kino); the mirror sync then reports MIRROR-DEPS-OK; (2) kit scripts made portable: notebook-batch.ps1 uses multi-segment Join-Path, user-env.ps1 returns early off Windows; (3) remote.ps1 Linux status prints cpu N% from /proc/stat; (4) the first runs were RED with no detail, because the first Cells build happened inside the job; a later run passes. The PATH fix stays interim (the personal lane's /etc/environment); the kit fix is still compiler discovery via the spiral cache dir. Main has opened vultr to all lanes for light-to-medium jobs.")
inl note_of_remote_cloud_linux_worker_9 () = Note ("REMOTE-CLOUD-LINUX-WORKER", 9u32, "2026-11-01T22:55Z", "x-dumps", "x-dumps 18:5x -04: CODESPACES HALF, design from a probe (no codespace touched). `gh codespace ssh --config -c <name>` emits a plain OpenSSH host `cs.<name>.main` (User codespace, ProxyCommand = gh codespace ssh --stdio, ControlMaster auto, own IdentityFile). So a codespace can be a remote.ps1 machine with NO new transport: (1) a refresh step writes `gh codespace ssh --config` for the live codespaces into ~/.ssh/codespaces.config, Included from ~/.ssh/config (never hand-edited, regenerated on each create/delete); (2) remote-machines.json gets kind linux, shell pwsh, sshAlias cs.<name>.main, gitRoot /workspaces, mirrorBase /tmp/remote, stage /tmp/remote/stage; (3) bootstrap = pwsh from the tarball (no MS apt for the universal image), plus kit-cli built -j<cores> or copied from vultr's Linux build (same glibc family?) to save ~3 min of codespace quota; (4) fetch-back before stop/delete = remote.ps1 fetch of stage + job stores (main's rule). Lifecycle stays main's (CODESPACES-QUOTA-RUNS); remote.ps1 is kit's. Next: a proof on a codespace once main OKs one.")
inl note_of_remote_cloud_linux_worker_10 () = Note ("REMOTE-CLOUD-LINUX-WORKER", 10u32, "2026-11-01T23:08Z", "x-dumps", "x-dumps 19:1x -04: CODESPACES HALF GREEN on main's bookish-space-goggles (with main's OK; its lib sweep left untouched). (1) ~/.ssh/codespaces.config = `gh codespace ssh --config -c <name>`, Included at the top of ~/.ssh/config (backup config.before-codespaces-include-20261101). (2) remote-machines.json entry `cs-bookish`: kind linux, proxied true, sshAlias cs.<name>.main, shell /home/codespace/pwsh/pwsh, gitRoot /tmp/remote/git, mirrorBase /tmp/remote, stage /tmp/remote/stage, eoie /tmp/remote/bin/kit (backup .before-codespace-20261101.bak). (3) remote.ps1 (kit's file, unstaged, minimal): a `proxied` machine skips Resolve-MachineAddress's TCP-22 probing and the -o HostName pin at all 3 ssh/scp sites (Get-HostOption), so the alias's ProxyCommand is used; node and vultr status are unchanged (regression checked). (4) No compile on the codespace: vultr's Linux kit (needs GLIBC_2.39; the universal image is Ubuntu 24.04 with glibc 2.39) copied gzip VM→box→codespace (83 MB, 84 s down + 150 s up), sha256 625e0bb0c3219649 identical. Proof: `remote.ps1 cs-bookish status` reports codespaces-88a5c6; `job start cs-proof ... uname -sr` → job-1793574461983-cs-proof finished exit 0 in 619 ms via the codespace's kit job host. Remaining: a refresh verb that regenerates codespaces.config + entries on codespace create/delete (main's lifecycle); kit's compiler discovery via the spiral cache dir; a CLIXML stderr parse error when a remote status sub-check fails (cosmetic).")
inl note_of_remote_cloud_linux_worker_11 () = Note ("REMOTE-CLOUD-LINUX-WORKER", 11u32, "2026-11-01T23:10Z", "kit", "11-01 19:10 kit: ack x-dumps' remote.ps1 edit (proxied: true machines skip TCP probing and the address cache; Get-HostOption at the 3 ssh/scp sites), so codespaces work as machines; node and vultr status unchanged. Leftovers owned by kit: compiler discovery via the spiral cache dir, and -OutputFormat Text on the remote pwsh, so a failing remote sub-check returns text, not CLIXML, to the local parser.")
inl note_of_remote_cloud_linux_worker_12 () = Note ("REMOTE-CLOUD-LINUX-WORKER", 12u32, "2026-11-01T23:15Z", "x-dumps", "x-dumps 19:2x -04: `remote.ps1 codespaces refresh` (unstaged, in remote.ps1): for every Available codespace it regenerates ~/.ssh/codespaces.config from `gh codespace ssh --config`, ensures `Include codespaces.config` heads ~/.ssh/config, replaces stale proxied cs.* machine entries and upserts cs-<first-name-word> (kind linux, proxied, shell /home/codespace/pwsh/pwsh, /tmp/remote paths, eoie /tmp/remote/bin/kit). Proof: refresh → cs-bookish; job cs-proof2 started through the regenerated entry; node/vultr status unchanged. Lifecycle hook for main: run it after every codespace create/delete. A fresh codespace still needs pwsh at ~/pwsh/pwsh + the Linux kit at /tmp/remote/bin/kit (copy vultr's, glibc 2.39 ok) before kit jobs.")
inl note_of_remote_cloud_linux_worker_13 () = Note ("REMOTE-CLOUD-LINUX-WORKER", 13u32, "2026-11-01T23:30Z", "x-dumps", "x-dumps 19:4x -04: Linux compiler discovery FIXED (kit's item (b), handed to me). The spawn site is kit/src/kit_process/native_compiler.rs (hand-written host code include!d into the generated process.rs, so regen won't clobber it). It resolved dotnet as KIT_DOTNET → SPIRAL_DOTNET → %LOCALAPPDATA%\\spiral-bin\\toolchains\\dotnet\\dotnet.exe → bare \"dotnet\", and LOCALAPPDATA is absent on Linux, so a job host without dotnet on PATH got ENOENT. New `spiral_dotnet_from(env)` follows the same cache rule as kit_compiler_discovery's deployed DLL: SPIRAL_BIN_CACHE_DIR, else %LOCALAPPDATA%\\spiral-bin (Windows) or ~/.cache/spiral-bin (other), + toolchains/dotnet/dotnet[.exe], then PATH. The env is injected so the test needs no global set_var. Proof on vultr: cargo test -p kit-process 3/3 (new dotnet_resolves_from_the_spiral_cache_before_path). End-to-end A/B in one job under `env -i HOME=/root PATH=/usr/bin:/bin` (no dotnet on PATH): OLD kit → \"spawn process: No such file or directory\"; NEW kit → \"agile check ok ... build_attested=2 compiler=available source=deployed\". No Cargo/workspace change. Unstaged on the box; kit folds it into TRAIN9. Side finding: the box kit tree currently isn't buildable until TRAIN8 generates kit_k3s/k3s.rs (kit-cli depends on kit-k3s; I used a mirror-only placeholder for the proof, never in the box tree). With this, /etc/environment PATH on vultr is no longer required for kit (kept, harmless).")
inl note_of_remote_cloud_linux_worker_14 () = Note ("REMOTE-CLOUD-LINUX-WORKER", 14u32, "2026-11-01T23:40Z", "kit", "11-01 19:40 kit: SSH PENALTY FIX (root cause found by x-dumps). OpenSSH 10.2 PerSourcePenalties (noauth) penalized our shared home IP after 84 connect-then-close events in 2 h, mostly remote.ps1's TCP-22 reachability probe (Test-TcpPort), plus parallel lanes. That produced \"port 22 unreachable\", then \"login refused\". Server side (x-dumps, vultr sshd_config.d/10-kit-workers.conf): exempt the home IP, noauth:0, MaxStartups 30:30:100. CLIENT HEAL (kit, unstaged in remote.ps1 Resolve-MachineAddress): when an address is cached, try ssh on it directly first and probe TCP only after that fails, so a healthy machine sees zero noauth events per call. Regression: vultr and laptop status ok, and a vultr job started right after.")
inl note_of_remote_cloud_linux_worker_15 () = Note ("REMOTE-CLOUD-LINUX-WORKER", 15u32, "2026-11-02T00:32Z", "kit", "11-01 20:32 kit: the cached-address direct ssh check (added 19:4x) now carries the same BatchMode=yes ServerAliveInterval=5 ServerAliveCountMax=2 options main added to the route probe, because ConnectTimeout does not cover a stalled handshake (a probe hung up to 1.5 h during the sshd lockout). Follow-up for the kit job host's own ssh calls (REMOTE-EXEC-S2 transport): always pass BatchMode plus ServerAlive, so a stalled server ends a call in about 10 s instead of hanging a job.")
inl note_of_remote_cloud_linux_worker_16 () = Note ("REMOTE-CLOUD-LINUX-WORKER", 16u32, "2026-11-02T03:09Z", "kit", "11-01 23:09 kit: vultr runs the TRAIN8 kit (built -j1 from a kit-train8 mirror, 4m18s, swapped atomically into /home/remote/git/kit/src/target/debug/kit, sha 91807212..., old kept as kit.before-train8). The kit-worker POD is applied: Deployment kit/kit-worker Running (chroot to host toolchains, Recreate, pgrep liveness), exactly 1 kit job host (the pod's); remote.ps1 vultr job start pod-proof exit 0 with still 1 host afterwards. Revert: k3s kubectl -n kit delete deploy kit-worker (state stays on the host in /root/.cache/kit). The vultr watcher restarted as a 24 h job (job-1793588424143-vultr-watch). Pending for the 1-slot rule: KIT_JOB_SLOTS ships in TRAIN9 (held out of TRAIN8 by the line gate).")
inl note_of_remote_cloud_linux_worker_17 () = Note ("REMOTE-CLOUD-LINUX-WORKER", 17u32, "2026-11-02T03:30Z", "kit", "11-02 23:30 kit DECISION (x-dumps: the pod showed 2 \"restarts\" in 20 min; that was kit job host exiting 0 after its 10-min idle limit, and kubelet backoff could open host-less gaps). x-dumps' interim fix: the container runs a loop that respawns kit job host in about 1 s (still exactly 1 host). KIT FIX (TRAIN9, with KIT_JOB_SLOTS, both in the host loop): KIT_JOB_HOST_IDLE_EXIT_SECONDS read into a typed idle_policy = ExitAfter of u64 | NeverExit (0 or \"never\" -> NeverExit; unset -> today's 600 s), consumed by host_step_of in kit_job_host/host_model.spi, with a test that a NeverExit host stays KeepServing after 10 quiet minutes. The pod then sets it to never and drops the loop. Prerequisite: split kit_job_host under the 1000-line gate (it hit 1111 with the slot cap).")
inl note_of_remote_cloud_linux_worker_18 () = Note ("REMOTE-CLOUD-LINUX-WORKER", 18u32, "2026-11-02T04:56Z", "kit", "KIT FIX AMENDED (TRAIN9 in tree, node pre-proof running): KIT_JOB_HOST_IDLE_EXIT_SECONDS takes a number only; 0 = never exit, N = exit after N quiet seconds, unset = 600 s. The word \"never\" was dropped so no string maps to a union case. KIT_JOB_SLOTS=N caps live supervisors per host; a full host holds ready jobs (StartNow -> HoldBack) and starts them as slots free. The pod sets KIT_JOB_HOST_IDLE_EXIT_SECONDS=0 KIT_JOB_SLOTS=1 once TRAIN9 is installed on vultr. The split that made room: kit_job_host keeps the host loop, new crate kit_job_host_request holds kit_job_host_request/revive, and the launch and child primitives moved into kit_os_launch (calls.spi).")
inl note_of_remote_cloud_linux_worker_19 () = Note ("REMOTE-CLOUD-LINUX-WORKER", 19u32, "2026-11-02T10:54Z", "kit", "kit 11-02 06:5x: vultr kit = TRAIN10 (92ea07e3), so the knobs from n18 are live in the binary. Path: sync kit -Mirror kit-train10 (REMOTE-SYNC-OK head 56c32ef6); capacity checked in its own step (AVAILABLE, load 0.73, no compilers); job job-1793616473635-kit-train10-build: cargo build -p kit-cli -j1 --locked, target seeded by MOVING kit-train8/target (3m18s, exit 0). Swap: hard link kit -> kit.before-train10 (91807212), copy to kit.new, mv over kit (atomic); no job was running. 'kit job host --idle' refuses (no host started). The node runs TRAIN10 too (main, C:\\home\\bin\\kit.exe, smoke job exit 0). Pod change sent to x-dumps (theirs): env KIT_JOB_HOST_IDLE_EXIT_SECONDS=0 + KIT_JOB_SLOTS=1, exec kit job host without the while-loop. Until the pod restarts, its host is still the TRAIN8 binary in memory.")
inl note_of_remote_cloud_linux_worker_20 () = Note ("REMOTE-CLOUD-LINUX-WORKER", 20u32, "2026-11-03T07:34Z", "kit", "kit groom 11-03 03:3x (WIP cap, memory wip-cap-active-means-touched): Active -> Planned, last note 21 h ago by kit, progress kept at 95%. Resume from that note: me\\bin\\kit.exe, smoke job exit 0). Pod change sent to x-dumps (theirs): env KIT_JOB_HOST_IDLE_EXIT_SECONDS=0 + KIT_JOB_SLOTS=1, exec kit job host without the while-loop. Until the pod restarts, its host is still the TRAIN8 binary in memory.")
inl note_of_remote_cloud_linux_worker_21 () = Note ("REMOTE-CLOUD-LINUX-WORKER", 21u32, "2026-11-04T11:44Z", "x-dumps", "Validated vultr target 2026-11-04 (x-dumps, via remote.ps1 vultr status): WORKS - returns repo digests (alphabet/blog/dice/html/i574n.github/kit/regex/spiral), deps core 70F6F5BF matches box, free-disk 25.7G. TWO GAPS found: (1) the NODE (laptop) target shows \"node runner (SPIRAL_EXE unset or missing)\" + \"node compiler (missing)\" -> node gates cannot run comparable to box; the REMOTE-CLOUD DoD (same gates on every worker) is unmet on the node until its spiral runner+compiler are restored. (2) vultr free-mem 0.8G (RAM near full under other lanes compute). Flagged both to main. vultr half of this feature is validated-functional.")
inl note_of_remote_cloud_linux_worker_22 () = Note ("REMOTE-CLOUD-LINUX-WORKER", 22u32, "2026-11-04T11:47Z", "x-dumps", "CORRECTION 2026-11-04 (main): my earlier \"node compiler/runner missing\" was WRONG - the node DOES compile+run Spiral (main ran the Cells gate there: lean.spi+oracle.spi 5/5, Lean 198/198, spiral mirror C80CFA10). \"SPIRAL_EXE unset or missing\" is a remote.ps1-STATUS probe reading the bare ssh env; the real resolution is apps/cells run_notebook.ps1 finding the compiler in spiral-bin/compiler-installs. So the node target is FINE; not node-down. The real (small) item is kit: make remote.ps1 status resolve the compiler the way run_notebook does. I over-asserted an absence from a probe - verify the capability before asserting.")
inl note_of_remote_cloud_linux_worker_23 () = Note ("REMOTE-CLOUD-LINUX-WORKER", 23u32, "2026-11-05T05:42Z", "x-dumps", "vultr UNREACHABLE 2026-11-05 ~05:40 local: ssh times out \"during banner exchange\" (3x, ConnectTimeout up to 25s). TCP reaches :22 but sshd never sends its banner = VM-side overload/thrash (CPU-pin or OOM/swap so sshd cannot be scheduled), NOT network. My xarchive builds there finished clean earlier (~05:1x, reverted, index 4860). Flagged kit (VM owner, kit-vultr-sjc-1). Shared infra effectively down for offload until recovered; the x-dumps backup task is unaffected (targets the sao node). VOID any vultr result until it recovers.")
inl note_of_remote_cloud_linux_worker_24 () = Note ("REMOTE-CLOUD-LINUX-WORKER", 24u32, "2026-11-05T06:02Z", "x-dumps", "vultr outage ROOT CAUSE (kit diagnosed 05:56Z, got in after 4 tries): memory thrash from tibia's UNCAPPED SpiralCompiler --backend C loop on /root/tibia-probes (480 MB/run) -> load 47, 112 MB free of 3392, 2.2 GB swap, k3s/iggy/coredns pods restarting under pressure. NOT a kit job (kit type-checks ended 01:30 local); not x-dumps (my builds there finished clean + reverted). kit asked tibia to cap it (systemd-run MemoryMax, one at a time, node fallback above load 4). Resolution owned by kit+tibia; x-dumps vultr-dependent work (geygey tail recheck) stays blocked until recovery. Textbook case of [[vultr-heavy-jobs-cap-memory]] - uncapped heavy job on the 2 CPU / 3.4 GB VM thrashes the whole box.")
inl emacs_steals () = Task ("EMACS-STEALS", "Steal Emacs mechanisms for the org (user 11-01 14:0x: 'check what from emacs can be stolen to our org repos/ambitions'): org-clock actuals, logged state changes, effort warnings, column view, dependency triggers, capture templates, saved agenda queries, babel results caching, tangle back-links, dynamic blocks, transient help, roam backlinks, TRAMP paths", Story, P1, D3, 0u32, Planned, "EOIE-SCRUM-XP", "kit,agile,scrum-xp,cells,research,user-batch,team:kit", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_emacs_steals () = Planning ("EMACS-STEALS", Minutes 120u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_emacs_steals_1 () = Note ("EMACS-STEALS", 1u32, "2026-11-01T18:08Z", "main", "main 14:1x research done (subagent, orgmode.org manual pages from memory, no web search). Ranked: 1 org-clock: a closed lease becomes an actual-minutes row; sprint close prints estimate vs actual (S2/S6, M). 2 effort warning: a lease past its estimate leaves an automatic over-estimate note (S3, S). 3 logged state changes: every status change is an append-only typed row (from, to, time, lane, reason); a reason is required for Paused/Done; burn-down reads only these rows (S2, S). 4 column view: typed column spec with a per-column total; epic rows sum their children (S5/S7, M). 5 org-edna triggers: closing an item promotes its unblocked dependents (S7, S). 6 capture templates: kit story add --template bug|slice|spike with required typed fields (S). 7 saved agenda queries: typed stored views plus kit standup (S8, M). 8 babel RESULTS[hash] caching keyed on the package closure and compiler hash; a second promote leaves an empty diff (KIT-RUN-SPI/TEST-EXPECT-PERSIST, M). 9 typed result forms: stdout|value|diagnostic|exit (SELF-CHECKING-SAMPLES, M). 10 named blocks feeding each other (Cells, L). 11 tangle link comments: generated .rs points back to its .spi line (attribute-generated-lines, M). 12 dynamic blocks: regenerated README/page regions, mtime untouched when unchanged (dir-tree-html, S). 13 transient-style help generated from command_spec (S). 14 roam backlinks: story show lists linking items and memories (M). 15 TRAMP: one typed local|machine path for job/sync/proof (REMOTE-EXEC, L). DON'T COPY: untyped property drawers, loose syntax, caches blind to outside inputs, code running on open, hidden advice, global single-threaded state, rescanning every file per query. DoD: ideas 1-7 are acceptance criteria on their SCRUM-XP slices (S2: 1,3; S3: 2; S5/S7: 4,5; S6: 1; S8: 7; plus 6 on the story-add verb); ideas 8-15 are each a filed item or a declined note here. Typefeatureset: every steal is a typed row or union (StateChange, ClockSpan, ColumnSpec with a total GADT, View as a filter value), never property strings.")
inl node_run_value () = Task ("NODE-RUN-VALUE", "Get the most out of every long node run (user 11-01 14:0x: 'we gotta make sure we're extracting max value from these long runs in the nodes, because they clog us a lot, look at today, entire day sitting waiting for them while grooming'): each run over 30 min declares its questions, predicts its result, streams usable checkpoints and carries cheap piggyback probes", Story, P1, D3, 0u32, Planned, "KIT-EXEC-METRICS", "node,remote,metrics,scheduling,all-lanes,user-batch,main,team:main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_node_run_value () = Planning ("NODE-RUN-VALUE", Minutes 180u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_node_run_value_1 () = Note ("NODE-RUN-VALUE", 1u32, "2026-11-01T18:08Z", "main", "main 14:1x: today compiler's libord-seq arms ran 2h15m and about 4 h on the node while other lanes waited for it. DoD: (1) kit job start for a node job over 30 min requires a typed run plan: the questions it answers, a predicted outcome per question (predict-pack-residuals), and checkpoints. (2) The job writes partial results at each checkpoint, so a lane can act on arm 1 before arm 2 ends. (3) Each run reserves spare memory and CPU headroom for queued cheap probes (light cells, type probes) instead of leaving them idle. (4) After the run, a value row (questions answered, predictions hit, hours) feeds NODE-VALUE-METRICS. Next: retrofit the plan onto the next compiler A/B and measure hours to first actionable result before and after. Typefeatureset: RunPlan = Question list with phantom-typed Prediction<unit>; Checkpoint is a GADT indexed by the question it answers.")
inl node_value_metrics () = Task ("NODE-VALUE-METRICS", "Per-node value metrics to predict when we really need more nodes (user 11-01 14:0x: metrics on how each node returns value, linux vs windows, jobs-only vs hosting the Claude windows that steal resources like the box, so 'we can predict when we'll truly need more nodes')", Story, P1, D4, 0u32, Planned, "KIT-EXEC-METRICS,NODE-RUN-VALUE", "kit,metrics,node,remote,capacity,user-batch,team:kit", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_node_value_metrics () = Planning ("NODE-VALUE-METRICS", Minutes 240u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_node_value_metrics_1 () = Note ("NODE-VALUE-METRICS", 1u32, "2026-11-01T18:08Z", "main", "main 14:1x: machines today: box (Windows, hosts every Claude window plus compiles), node (Windows, jobs only, Wi-Fi), the Vultr VM (Linux, cloud, x-dumps first), codespaces (Linux, quota-bound, ephemeral). DoD: kit node report prints per machine and day: OS, role (Jobs | Interactive | Mixed), CPU-hours used/available, queue wait, jobs finished, value rows from NODE-RUN-VALUE, and the Cells benchmark speed factor (the same cell timed on each machine). A forecast line says when the queue wait would justify another node, and the report runs on real data for 3 days. Collection stays light: no Prometheus (user: avoid it to save memory); kit's own exec metrics rows. Typefeatureset: Machine = Box | Node | Cloud of provider | Codespace as a closed union; OS and role as phantom tags so Linux-only metrics can't be read off Windows rows.")
inl k3s_everywhere () = Task ("K3S-EVERYWHERE", "k3s on every Linux worker, with one shared set of manifests and lessons: the Vultr cloud node first, then WSL on the local Wi-Fi node; no Prometheus, to save memory (user 11-01 14:0x: 'we should use k3s everywhere but avoid prometheus to save memory, we should reuse k3s lessons between the linux node and wsl3 inside our local wifi node')", Story, P1, D4, 80u32, Planned, "REMOTE-CLOUD-LINUX-WORKER", "k3s,remote,linux,node,kit,user-batch,x-dumps,team:x-dumps", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_k3s_everywhere () = Planning ("K3S-EVERYWHERE", Minutes 600u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_k3s_everywhere_1 () = Note ("K3S-EVERYWHERE", 1u32, "2026-11-01T18:09Z", "main", "main 14:1x: the user's 'wsl3' is read as WSL on the node. The no-wsl-until-workflow-passes memory put WSL last; this direction moves WSL on the node up, but only after the Vultr k3s works. DoD: k3s runs on the Vultr VM with kit's worker as a pod and the x-dumps workload; the same manifests apply unchanged in WSL on the node; every lesson is one note here, and no per-machine forks of the manifests exist. Memory footprint is measured (k3s idle RSS) and stays under a stated budget. Next: install k3s on the Vultr VM after the personal lane's first x-dumps run (x-dumps has priority there). Typefeatureset: manifests generated from typed Spiral records (Deployment with phantom resource limits), not hand YAML.")
inl note_of_k3s_everywhere_2 () = Note ("K3S-EVERYWHERE", 2u32, "2026-11-01T20:06Z", "x-dumps", "x-dumps 16:0x -04: k3s on vultr. Installed v1.36.5+k3s1 (stable channel; the WSL node must use the same pin) with --disable traefik --disable servicelb --disable metrics-server, no Prometheus. Idle footprint measured: k3s-server 454 MB RSS, k3s+containerd+coredns+local-path ~660 MB; system used 594→978 MB. Budget stated: <=700 MB idle. Workload 1 is live: x-dumps runs as Deployment x-dumps/xarchive (ubuntu:26.04 image, hostPath ro mount of the xarchive release binary, hostPath /data/x, loops `run ... --rps 1` then sleeps 1 h; requests 50m/64Mi, limit 512Mi; Recreate strategy) and replaces the nohup run, so it survives reboots. It resumed from the cache in seconds. The bootstrap manifest lives at /home/remote/k3s/xarchive.yaml on the VM, deliberately NOT in a repo: the DoD wants manifests generated from typed Spiral records. Next: (1) the Spiral generator, (2) kit's job host as a pod, (3) the same manifests in WSL on the node. Install lesson: `curl -I` on update.k3s.io/v1-release/channels/stable returns 405 (HEAD unsupported), so the pinned-version lookup must use GET with -w %{redirect_url}.")
inl note_of_k3s_everywhere_3 () = Note ("K3S-EVERYWHERE", 3u32, "2026-11-01T21:18Z", "x-dumps", "x-dumps 17:1x -04: typed manifests landed in the working tree (unstaged). (1) kit/src/k3s_manifest: HostFree+LibReferenced package; GADT phantom units `quantity u` (Millicores -> quantity cpu, Mebibytes -> quantity memory) so a memory amount can't go in a cpu field; unions for volume source/access/strategy; deployment record; JSON rendering (kubectl applies JSON as is). A JSON spec record (wire boundary) is decoded with json.decode and converted to the typed model. Proven on vultr via kit scripts/notebook-batch.ps1: 4/4 pass incl. a spec round trip byte-equal to the typed fixture; standards 0 findings, ratchet ok. The census rows were set once the new package was final (a deliberate baseline, not a ratchet bypass: its line count grew while I re-laid widths and added the spec). (2) lib/spiral/json.spi gained `quote` (JSON string literal) with a Rust-only test; json.spi 12/12. (3) kit/src/kit_k3s verb crate `kit k3s render <spec.json>` → stdout, export kit_k3s_run; registration + regen handed to kit (it is mid-edit in command_spec_domain/kit_cli_model). (4) The concrete workload spec lives in xarchive/deploy/xarchive.k3s.json, not in kit (kit knows no projects). (5) kit-worker pod: drafted at /home/remote/k3s/kit-worker.yaml (privileged chroot to host toolchains, Recreate, pgrep liveness), server dry run OK, namespace kit created; NO-GO from kit until its --after owner fix is on vultr. (6) Compiler bug found and reported to compiler: array_base of records with a union field → Rust `Vec<tuple>::default()` E0599; worked around (host_path plain string). Next: kit regen → `kit k3s render xarchive.k3s.json | kubectl apply -f -` replaces the bootstrap YAML; then the worker pod on kit's apply; then WSL reuse.")
inl note_of_k3s_everywhere_4 () = Note ("K3S-EVERYWHERE", 4u32, "2026-11-01T21:46Z", "x-dumps", "x-dumps 17:4x -04: WSL reuse. The node's WSL Ubuntu (WSL2, systemd=true) already runs k3s v1.37.1+k3s1 since 10-25 (7 days, with namespace iggy and metrics-server). Version pin unified on v1.37.1+k3s1: vultr upgraded in place from v1.36.5 (installer with INSTALL_K3S_VERSION, same --disable flags); the running pods survived without restart; k3s-server ~677 MB right after the upgrade. Both VM manifests (xarchive, kit-worker) pass `kubectl apply --dry-run=server` UNCHANGED on the node's WSL cluster (empty namespaces x-dumps and kit created there for the dry run; iggy untouched), so no per-machine forks. Lessons: (a) server dry runs do not persist Namespaces, so create the namespace first or the Deployment fails NotFound; (b) `$var` inside `remote.ps1 node run \"wsl ... bash -lc '...'\"` passes through 3 shells and arrives empty, so use explicit names or base64; (c) node WSL k3s already exists, so the K3S item uses it instead of reinstalling. Remaining DoD: `kit k3s render` (kit's regen tonight) replacing the bootstrap YAML, kit-worker pod apply (kit's go), the same on the node.")
inl note_of_k3s_everywhere_5 () = Note ("K3S-EVERYWHERE", 5u32, "2026-11-01T23:10Z", "x-dumps", "GROOM 19:1x (x-dumps). Estimate 240 → 600 min (actuals: install + measure + typed package + 4 proofs + WSL dry runs already ~360). DoD unchanged: k3s on vultr + node WSL at ONE pinned version (v1.37.1+k3s1), kit worker + x-dumps as pods, manifests from typed Spiral records (k3s_manifest + `kit k3s render`), idle RSS within the stated <=700 MB budget, no per-machine forks. Done: install + pin + RSS, x-dumps pod, typed package proven, WSL dry runs. Next: (1) kit's TRAIN8 regen ships `kit k3s render` → replace the bootstrap YAML on vultr with the rendered xarchive.k3s.json; (2) kit-worker pod on kit's \"apply\"; (3) the same apply in node WSL. Typefeatureset: units are already GADT-indexed (quantity cpu/memory); next index candidate is volume access as a phantom (ReadOnly mounts can't back a writable path) once a second workload exists.")
inl note_of_k3s_everywhere_6 () = Note ("K3S-EVERYWHERE", 6u32, "2026-11-02T00:09Z", "x-dumps", "x-dumps 20:1x -04: crate-gate work on kit_k3s (kit unregistered the verb from TRAIN8 at 3,221 generated lines; its biggest fn was 1,406). Made 19 non-generic k3s_manifest helpers join points (`:~ ^`; functions passed to map/fold wrapped in lambdas). Measured with kit's test-regeneration.ps1 -KeepStaged on a vultr mirror: 3,221 → 1,766 lines (largest fn 81). Notebook 4/4, compile 53 s (was 5-14 min). Still >1000: ~500 lines are json.decode's parser instantiated for the spec type, ~350 render. Options sent to kit: (A) split spec-canonicalize/render crates, (B) a typed line-row spec instead of generic JSON decode (my lean; matches DATA-ROWS-TYPED), (C) keep the verb out of TRAIN9 meanwhile. Lesson: join points cut generated size AND compile time together (here ~6-10x faster notebook compiles).")
inl note_of_k3s_everywhere_7 () = Note ("K3S-EVERYWHERE", 7u32, "2026-11-02T00:28Z", "x-dumps", "x-dumps 20:2x -04 (kit decision B): the spec is now a TYPED ROW MODULE, not JSON. k3s_manifest reads `inl row_N () = Deployment|Container|Resources|Volume [ \"...\" ]` rows (union k3s_row, `///- --no-respell`) with kit_table_rows' quote-aware cells_of; unit words map to the quantity GADT on read (Millicores → quantity cpu, Mebibytes → quantity memory); the typed outcome is spec_outcome = Rendered | Refused (UnknownRow | MissingCells | UnknownUnit | BadNumber | RowBeforeDeployment). The verb kit k3s render <spec.spi> prints refusals with exit 1. Proof (vultr, one-slot): notebook 5/5 (rows render byte-equal to the typed fixture; unknown unit and orphan row refused); standards 0 findings. Crate size (test-regeneration -KeepStaged): 1,652 lines (JSON version 1,766; original 3,221). Still over the 1000 gate. By family: 18 closure methods = 321 lines, row builders ~410 (the nested 7-field record is rebuilt per row), render ~260. NEXT: recursive join points instead of map/fold lambdas; collect rows flat first and build each deployment once; measure after each step; split only if still >1000. kit keeps VerbK3s unregistered until then. Cells are single-line (cells_of keeps an escaped char literally), so the xarchive loop becomes one-line bash; xarchive/deploy/xarchive.k3s.json must be rewritten as xarchive.k3s.spi rows.")
inl note_of_k3s_everywhere_8 () = Note ("K3S-EVERYWHERE", 8u32, "2026-11-02T00:39Z", "x-dumps", "x-dumps 20:3x -04: size lever 2 measured: map/fold lambdas → recursive join points (texts, mounts_json, volumes_json, documents, steps) and the row builders return `built` = Built deployment | Broken spec_error (no Building [d] wrap/unwrap). kit_k3s.rs 1,652 → 1,610 lines (methods 321 → 230). Notebook 5/5, standards 0 findings, census rebaselined. Conclusion: Rust codegen for the nested 7-field deployment record dominates (sized 120, step 75, deployment_json 80, resources_json 70, string_slice 98), so join points can't reach 1000. TRAIN9 design: split at a STRING boundary, which needs no cross-crate typed bindings: crate k3s_spec (rows → canonical one-line-per-field normalized rows, validated, units checked) + crate kit_k3s (canonical rows → JSON render), each well under 1000. Concrete workload spec now lives in C:\\home\\git\\xarchive\\deploy\\xarchive.k3s.spi (rows; the JSON one renamed .superseded).")
inl note_of_k3s_everywhere_9 () = Note ("K3S-EVERYWHERE", 9u32, "2026-11-02T03:08Z", "x-dumps", "x-dumps 23:0x -04: KIT WORKER RUNS AS A POD on vultr. (1) vultr kit rebuilt from the TRAIN8 tree (has the --after owner fix + Linux dotnet discovery): sync kit -Mirror kit-train8, cargo build -p kit-cli -j1 into a separate target (4m18s, one-slot), swapped atomically into /home/remote/git/kit/src/target/debug/kit (sha 91807212..., old kept as kit.before-train8 = 625e0bb0...); job train8-proof exit 0. (2) Applied /home/remote/k3s/kit-worker.yaml: Deployment kit/kit-worker Running; exactly ONE `kit job host` process (the pod's, chroot to host toolchains); `remote.ps1 vultr job start pod-proof` → exit 0, still one host after. (3) Trap: `kit job host --help` does NOT print help; it starts a real job host (hidden long-running verb). I started one by accident via ssh and stopped it (verified it was supervising nothing). (4) Revived the expired vultr watcher (kit job job-1793588424143-vultr-watch, 24 h; the old one had a 6 h cap and went stale 21:4x). DoD left: Spiral-rendered manifests (kit_k3s under the 1000 gate, the TRAIN9 split) and the same pods in node WSL.")
inl note_of_k3s_everywhere_10 () = Note ("K3S-EVERYWHERE", 10u32, "2026-11-02T03:29Z", "x-dumps", "x-dumps 23:3x -04: the kit-worker pod \"restarted\" twice in 20 min. Not a crash: `kit job host` exits 0 after ~10 min idle (03:18:11 → 03:28:12), by design for ssh-spawned hosts; in a Deployment that shows as restarts, and kubelet backoff could leave host-less gaps where an ssh job spawns a second host. Fix (bootstrap YAML, backup kit-worker.yaml.before-loop): the container runs `while true; do kit job host; sleep 1; done`, so the container stays up, the host respawns in ~1 s, and the restart count stays 0. Verify at the next heartbeat (>10 min later). Design question to kit: a `--no-idle-exit` (or env) for supervisor-owned hosts.")
inl note_of_k3s_everywhere_11 () = Note ("K3S-EVERYWHERE", 11u32, "2026-11-02T03:48Z", "x-dumps", "x-dumps 23:48 -04: loop fix VERIFIED: kit-worker 19 min, 0 restarts (past the 10-min idle exit), exactly 1 job host. kit's TRAIN9 replaces the loop with KIT_JOB_HOST_IDLE_EXIT_SECONDS=never + KIT_JOB_SLOTS=1 in the pod env.")
inl note_of_k3s_everywhere_12 () = Note ("K3S-EVERYWHERE", 12u32, "2026-11-02T10:54Z", "x-dumps", "kit-worker pod on vultr moved to TRAIN10 (92ea07e3): env KIT_JOB_HOST_IDLE_EXIT_SECONDS=0 + KIT_JOB_SLOTS=1, command is a single exec of kit job host (restart loop dropped, k8s restartPolicy covers crashes). Server dry run, rollout ok, exactly one host with both knobs in its environ. Backup kit-worker.yaml.before-train10. Remaining: Spiral-rendered manifests (kit_k3s split), same pods on node WSL.")
inl note_of_k3s_everywhere_13 () = Note ("K3S-EVERYWHERE", 13u32, "2026-11-02T12:27Z", "x-dumps", "iggy now runs on vultr k3s too (apache/iggy:0.9.0, same pin as the node WSL), brought up by the new i574n.github/scripts/linux-worker.ps1 -SshAlias vultr -KitBinary <kit>: sshd penalty drop-in (sshd -t gated), kit bootstrap.sh k3s pin, kit iggy.yaml patched for a small public VM (limit 512Mi, hostNetwork off so no public ports; Service 10.43.189.228), kit-worker pod, then kit verify.ps1 through an ssh tunnel: VERIFY-OK 1000/1000 ordered, 0 dup. Rerun idempotent (kit job host pid kept, iggy pod not restarted). iggy RSS 83 MB; VM 1.8 GB available.")
inl note_of_k3s_everywhere_14 () = Note ("K3S-EVERYWHERE", 14u32, "2026-11-02T12:29Z", "kit", "kit 11-02 08:1x on x-dumps' iggy-on-vultr FYI: iggy runs on vultr k3s from kit/infra/k3s-iggy unedited. i574n.github/scripts/linux-worker.ps1 text-patches iggy.yaml (limits memory 3Gi -> 512Mi; hostNetwork true -> false + dnsPolicy ClusterFirst, because a public VM would otherwise expose 3000/8090/8080/8092) and throws if those lines drift; VERIFY-OK 1000/1000, RSS 83 MB. DECISION (kit): no new knobs on the hand YAML / node-wsl.ps1 (Spiral-first; manifests come from typed records). The fix is iggy as a kit_k3s typed manifest with memory_limit : quantity memory and network : pod_network = HostNetwork | ClusterNetwork; the node WSL renders HostNetwork, a public VM renders ClusterNetwork (exposure becomes a type-level choice, and the GADT-indexed quantity units already exist). The render then replaces both the patch and the embedded kit-worker manifest. Until then the patch-with-drift-guard is the right bridge. Depends on kit k3s render being registered (kit_k3s under the 1000-line gate).")
inl note_of_k3s_everywhere_15 () = Note ("K3S-EVERYWHERE", 15u32, "2026-11-02T13:44Z", "x-dumps", "Spec drift to close at render time: the live vultr x-dumps manifest (/home/remote/k3s/xarchive.yaml, backup .before-iggy) now has envFrom secretRef iggy-xarchive on xarchive and a second Deployment xarchive-follow (64Mi limit); xarchive/deploy/xarchive.k3s.spi has neither yet.")
inl note_of_k3s_everywhere_16 () = Note ("K3S-EVERYWHERE", 16u32, "2026-11-02T15:05Z", "x-dumps", "linux-worker.ps1 caveat (star exchange 11-02, fiscal/compiler/x all hit the array-param trap): its -ExemptIp is [string[]], so pwsh -File linux-worker.ps1 -ExemptIp a,b passes the one string a,b. Call in-process (& linux-worker.ps1 -ExemptIp @(...)) or pass one value; the default (controller SSH_CLIENT) sidesteps it. The Spiral port (PS1-TO-SPIRAL) removes the trap.")
inl note_of_k3s_everywhere_17 () = Note ("K3S-EVERYWHERE", 17u32, "2026-11-03T07:35Z", "kit", "kit groom 11-03 03:3x (WIP cap, memory wip-cap-active-means-touched): Active -> Planned, last note 16 h ago by x-dumps, progress kept at 80%. Resume from that note: wsh -File linux-worker.ps1 -ExemptIp a,b passes the one string a,b. Call in-process (& linux-worker.ps1 -ExemptIp @(...)) or pass one value; the default (controller SSH_CLIENT) sidesteps it. The Spiral port (PS1-TO-SPIRAL) removes the trap.")
inl note_of_k3s_everywhere_18 () = Note ("K3S-EVERYWHERE", 18u32, "2026-11-04T11:06Z", "x-dumps", "Groom 2026-11-04 (x-dumps side): infra DoD met — k3s on vultr + node WSL at pinned v1.37.1+k3s1, --disable traefik/servicelb/metrics-server, no Prometheus, idle RSS <=700MB; kit-worker pod + iggy pod live; WSL dry-runs pass unchanged (no per-machine forks). REMAINING is kit-owned: `kit k3s render` registration (gated on the k3s_spec/kit_k3s crate split under the 1000-line gate). Also: the xarchive WORKLOAD manifest (xarchive.k3s.spi) is now MOOT for live use — harvest complete, xarchive pod at replicas=0; the render target can be iggy (the live pod) rather than xarchive. Not pulling further from x-dumps side; the valuable remainder is kits render.")
inl iggy_public_cloud () = Task ("IGGY-PUBLIC-CLOUD", "A publicly reachable iggy on the cloud node's k3s (no paid fixed IP unless it's free), with the WSL iggy kept as fallback, so work machines that can't open an inbound ssh port join by connecting out to it (user 11-01 14:0x: 'a challenge for kit to take the typefeatureset to the limit')", Story, P1, D5, 90u32, Active, "IGGY-MULTI-MACHINE,K3S-EVERYWHERE,LIB-IGGY-CLIENT", "iggy,k3s,remote,kit,typefeatureset,user-batch,team:kit", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_iggy_public_cloud () = Planning ("IGGY-PUBLIC-CLOUD", Minutes 360u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_iggy_public_cloud_1 () = Note ("IGGY-PUBLIC-CLOUD", 1u32, "2026-11-01T18:09Z", "main", "main 14:1x: the pull model: a worker behind NAT or a corporate firewall opens an outbound TLS connection to the iggy endpoint, consumes job events and publishes results; nothing listens on the worker. DoD: (1) iggy runs in k3s on the Vultr VM, reachable at the VM's own public address or a free DNS name, with TLS and per-machine credentials (credentials are the user's step). (2) A worker that has only outbound access runs one job end-to-end through it. (3) Killing the cloud iggy fails over to the WSL instance within a stated time. (4) Iggy's multi-node or cluster state is tested on the newest beta or nightly, with results noted here. Cost stays at the VM price. Typefeatureset: worker capabilities as phantom tags (InboundSsh | OutboundOnly), so placement can't send ssh-only work to an outbound-only worker; messages are LIB-IGGY-CLIENT's GADT family.")
inl note_of_iggy_public_cloud_2 () = Note ("IGGY-PUBLIC-CLOUD", 2u32, "2026-11-02T13:16Z", "main", "main 11-02 (user: \"are we already reaping all the benefits we can with iggy, the public deploy with k3s at vultr should be up also ... gotta understand why it stalled, is it cpu?\"). NOT CPU: vultr's 2 vCPUs sit at load 0.25; memory is the tight one (3.4 GB, ~0.7 GB free). STATE 08:45: k3s runs iggy (up since 08:23, apache/iggy:0.9.0), kit-worker and xarchive pods. iggy's Service is ClusterIP only (hostNetwork turned off on purpose so the public VM doesn't expose 3000/8090/8080/8092), so it is NOT public yet, and nothing produces to or consumes from it, so no benefit is being reaped. WHY IT STALLED: prioritization. IGGY-MULTI-MACHINE (P0, 40%) sat in kit's queue \"after the SCRUM-XP slices\" (11-01 14:15) and behind TRAIN8-11; this item is 0% and depends on it plus LIB-IGGY-CLIENT. Next: (1) TLS + per-machine credentials, which are the user's step; (2) a public listener (ingress or LoadBalancer on the VM address); (3) one real producer/consumer pair (kit job events) so the deploy carries load. Ask x-dumps (owns Vultr) and kit (owns IGGY-MULTI-MACHINE) for dates.")
inl note_of_iggy_public_cloud_3 () = Note ("IGGY-PUBLIC-CLOUD", 3u32, "2026-11-02T13:24Z", "x-dumps", "x-dumps plan + dates (11-02 09:3x). Memory correction: vultr has ~1.7 GB AVAILABLE (free 0.7 GB + reclaimable cache); iggy itself is 83 MB RSS.\n(3) Real producer/consumer, starting NOW (no user step), done 11-02 by ~11:30 -04: xarchive sends every progress / stage-summary / backoff line as a JSON event to stream x-dumps, topic harvest, over the ClusterIP (reqwest is already a dep, so no new HTTP stack). An iggy user \"xarchive\" holds only send/poll/read permissions; its secret lives in namespace x-dumps; root never leaves the iggy namespace. Consumer: a second pod running `xarchive follow`, polling the topic and writing /data/x/status.tsv (latest event per stage+account, each with its age). Heartbeats read that file instead of grepping logs. kit job events stay with kit's IGGY-MULTI-MACHINE date.\n(1) What the user must decide, nothing to type:\n (a) YES/NO to opening TLS-only ports to the internet on 140.82.49.44: TCP 8090 + HTTPS 3000; QUIC and websocket stay closed. An IP allowlist would defeat the pull model (NAT workers have unknown IPs), so protection is TLS + per-machine auth.\n (b) Certificate choice. Default: our own CA (CA key in the box ~/.secrets; server cert with an IP SAN; clients pin the CA; no domain needed). Alternative: a DNS name the user controls with an A record pointing at 140.82.49.44, for a public Let's Encrypt cert.\n (c) Per-machine credentials. Default: I generate one iggy user per machine (box, node, vultr, each codespace), send/poll only, written to that machine's ~/.secrets/iggy.env. If the user wants to hold them in their own password manager instead, they create them.\n(2) Public listener: same day as the user's yes (~1 h). iggy env tcp.tls/http.tls enabled with cert_file/key_file (0.9.0 supports both natively, no proxy), hostPort 8090/3000 only, linux-worker.ps1 -PublicTls. Proof: the box runs kit verify.ps1 against https://140.82.49.44:3000 with the pinned CA and no tunnel, plus a TCP-TLS client connect.")
inl note_of_iggy_public_cloud_4 () = Note ("IGGY-PUBLIC-CLOUD", 4u32, "2026-11-02T13:43Z", "x-dumps", "DONE (3) real producer/consumer on vultr iggy, 11-02 09:38 -04, ahead of the 11:30 date. xarchive publishes every progress/stage/merge/backoff/fetch-error line as JSON {at_ms,line,dropped} to stream x-dumps topic harvest (reqwest, no new deps), as iggy user xarchive (read_streams/read_topics/poll/send only; create-stream gets 403; password generated on the VM into secret x-dumps/iggy-xarchive). Consumer: Deployment x-dumps/xarchive-follow (`xarchive /data/x follow`, 7.7 MB RSS) polls with consumer_id 1 + auto_commit and writes /data/x/status.tsv (latest event per stage+account with at_ms). Proof: topic messages_count 1,344 = 1,344 xarchive log lines since restart (0 dropped), 577 KB. (1)+(2) wait on the user via main; no port opened.")
inl note_of_iggy_public_cloud_5 () = Note ("IGGY-PUBLIC-CLOUD", 5u32, "2026-11-02T16:19Z", "x-dumps", "TLS proven end-to-end INTERNALLY 11-02 (no public port opened). Own CA (ED25519) + server cert at /root/iggy-ca on vultr (CA fp 27:7E:2A:20...), SANs: iggy.iggy.svc(.cluster.local), localhost, 127.0.0.1, ClusterIP, and 140.82.49.44 (ready for public). iggy pod serves HTTPS only (IGGY_HTTP_TLS_ENABLED/CERT_FILE/KEY_FILE from secret iggy-tls; http.tls has NO self_signed field - iggy rejects unknown env vars and crashloops, so only enabled/cert_file/key_file are valid; probes switched to scheme HTTPS). Plain HTTP refused. xarchive events.rs trusts the CA via IGGY_CA_PEM env; secret iggy-xarchive -> IGGY_URL=https://iggy.iggy.svc.cluster.local:3000 + CA. Verified: follow consumer reads over TLS (status.tsv fresh, no errors), topic messages_count climbing (4717) over https, least-priv user still 403 on create-stream. REMAINING for public: (1) USER yes to open hostPort 3000 (+8090 once TCP TLS env keys confirmed) on 140.82.49.44; (2) per-machine iggy users into each box ~/.secrets. Internal secure path is complete.")
inl note_of_iggy_public_cloud_6 () = Note ("IGGY-PUBLIC-CLOUD", 6u32, "2026-11-02T21:13Z", "x-dumps", "TCP-8090 TLS enabled + verified 11-02 (completes iggy TLS). Keys confirmed from the iggy-server binary strings (no config file ships): tcp.tls DOES have self_signed (unlike http.tls), so IGGY_TCP_TLS_ENABLED/SELF_SIGNED=false/CERT_FILE/KEY_FILE are all valid. openssl s_client to ClusterIP:8090 with --CAfile ca.crt -> subject CN=iggy.iggy.svc, Verify return code 0 (ok). HTTP 3000 still serves pong over TLS; iggy pod 0 restarts; event stream unaffected. iggy now secures BOTH protocols a pull-worker uses (HTTP 3000 + TCP 8090) with our CA. REMAINING for public: only (1) user yes to open hostPort 3000+8090 on 140.82.49.44 (cert already has that SAN), (2) per-machine send/poll users into each box ~/.secrets. Reproducibility gap: the TLS env is on the live deploy via set env; linux-worker.ps1 kit iggy.yaml would need a -PublicTls option to redeploy with TLS (follow-up).")
inl note_of_iggy_public_cloud_7 () = Note ("IGGY-PUBLIC-CLOUD", 7u32, "2026-11-02T21:20Z", "x-dumps", "Reproducibility gap CLOSED 11-02: linux-worker.ps1 gained a -Tls switch (+ -TlsSanIp for the public SAN). After iggy comes up on HTTP it generates the CA+cert (reused if present), creates secret iggy-tls, kubectl set env the HTTP+TCP TLS vars, patches the probes to scheme HTTPS + mounts the cert, then verifies https 3000 + tcp 8090 s_client. Uses set env/patch (not fragile manifest string-surgery), so it is the proven live recipe made one-command. Dry-run validated on vultr (DRY iggy-tls reached, composes clean); NOT re-applied to live vultr (already TLS). A fresh/second iggy node (e.g. the sao move) now brings up TLS iggy reproducibly. Spiral port tracked on PS1-TO-SPIRAL.")
inl note_of_iggy_public_cloud_8 () = Note ("IGGY-PUBLIC-CLOUD", 8u32, "2026-11-02T21:37Z", "x-dumps", "PUBLIC LISTENER LIVE 11-02 (user authorized the port-open). iggy on vultr is now reachable at 140.82.49.44:3000 (HTTPS) + :8090 (TCP TLS) via hostPort (only those 2 ports; QUIC 8080 / ws 8092 stay closed). Block was ufw (active, only 22) - added ufw allow 3000/tcp + 8090/tcp (no vultr cloud firewall; k3s CNI hostPort DNAT was already in place, and forwarded-to-pod traffic bypasses the INPUT DROP anyway). EXTERNAL verify from the box: TCP 3000+8090 reachable; openssl s_client -> cert CN=iggy.iggy.svc, TLSv1.3, Verify return code 0 (ok) with our CA. NOTE: the cert is ED25519 - Windows schannel (curl/.NET) cannot handshake it, but rust/rustls clients (reqwest, the iggy crate = the real pull-workers) do; verify externally with openssl, not schannel. Protection: TLS both ports + iggy JWT auth + least-priv users. Remaining: per-machine users + a cross-region producer/consumer proof (wiring sao worker next).")
inl note_of_iggy_public_cloud_9 () = Note ("IGGY-PUBLIC-CLOUD", 9u32, "2026-11-02T21:49Z", "x-dumps", "CROSS-REGION PULL-WORKER PROVEN 11-02 (DoD 1+2 met). The sao worker (Sao Paulo, rustdesk-sao-1) now publishes to the PUBLIC iggy in San Jose over 140.82.49.44:3000 (TLS, CA-verified) with a per-machine least-priv user xarchive-sao (send+poll; create-stream 403). Proof: polled the x-dumps/harvest topic, 134 sao-origin events (/root/xdata, dropped:0) alongside 263 sjc-origin (/data/x) - both nodes producing to one iggy, outbound-only from sao. Wiring: events.rs gained IGGY_CA_FILE (CA from a path, for systemd/non-k8s); sao unit recreated with EnvironmentFile=/root/.secrets/iggy.env (URL/user/pass/CA_FILE, pw never printed - generated on vultr, relayed file-to-file). New binary ffc5e66a (backward-compat with IGGY_CA_PEM, so the sjc pod was not restarted). REMAINING: DoD(3) auto-failover to the node WSL iggy within a stated time; DoD(4) iggy multi-node/cluster on a beta; the worker-capability phantom tags (LIB-IGGY-CLIENT). Refinement: sjc+sao share the event key \"progress snaps geygey_hadid\" so they clobber in status.tsv (raw topic distinguishes by path); add a node/root tag to the event key.")
inl note_of_iggy_public_cloud_10 () = Note ("IGGY-PUBLIC-CLOUD", 10u32, "2026-11-02T21:57Z", "x-dumps", "Clobber refinement DONE 11-02: events.rs key() now tags the progress key with the data-root (progress <stage> <root>:<account>), so sjc (data:geygey_hadid) and sao (root:geygey_hadid) no longer overwrite each other in status.tsv - it is now a true unified multi-node view. Only the follow consumer needed the new binary (1124d46d; producers send raw lines, unchanged), so the sjc harvester was not touched. Verified data: keys write; root: appears on sao's next progress publish (symmetric logic, sao publishing already proven).")
inl note_of_iggy_public_cloud_11 () = Note ("IGGY-PUBLIC-CLOUD", 11u32, "2026-11-02T22:42Z", "x-dumps", "DECISION (technical, decided by rule 11-02): keep the iggy cert ED25519, do NOT reissue as ECDSA now. Rule: match the cert to the production client. The real pull-workers are rust/rustls (xarchive reqwest, the iggy crate, LIB-IGGY-CLIENT = rust+gleam) which handle ED25519 fine - proven by sao publishing cross-region and xarchive's live TLS event stream. The ONLY thing ED25519 blocks is Windows schannel/.NET clients (curl, Invoke-RestMethod), which are test tools, not the production path; verify those endpoints with openssl, not schannel. Revisit ECDSA P-256 only IF a Windows/.NET process must consume the public iggy directly (then reissue CA+cert ECDSA, update iggy-tls + IGGY_CA_PEM, restart iggy+consumers, and set linux-worker.ps1 -Tls to ECDSA). Recorded so the question is closed, not re-litigated.")
inl note_of_iggy_public_cloud_12 () = Note ("IGGY-PUBLIC-CLOUD", 12u32, "2026-11-03T07:40Z", "x-dumps", "HOW A LANE GETS ITS OWN LEAST-PRIVILEGE IGGY USER (x-dumps 11-03). Recipe: x-dumps scratchpad iggy-box-user.sh (mirror of iggy-sao-user.sh), run ON vultr as root. Steps: login as iggy (k8s secret iggy-root) -> token; POST /users with permissions.global = read_streams/read_topics/poll_messages/send_messages = true and everything else false, streams:null (send+poll only; cannot manage servers/users/streams/topics). Password is generated on vultr, written umask 077 to /root/iggy-<name>.env, NEVER printed; then scp file-to-file to the target ~/.secrets/iggy.env and scp /root/iggy-ca/ca.crt to ~/.secrets/iggy-ca.crt, and shred the vultr temp. Env keys: IGGY_URL=https://140.82.49.44:3000, IGGY_USERNAME, IGGY_PASSWORD, IGGY_CA_FILE. Verify: public-IP login returns a token AND POST /streams returns 403 (least-priv proven). CAVEAT: the ED25519 CA blocks Windows schannel (curl/.NET) - consume via a rustls client (xarchive/events.rs reads IGGY_CA_FILE or IGGY_CA_PEM), not Windows curl. ISSUED: xarchive-sao (sao node), xarchive-box (this laptop, 11-03, verified login+403). PENDING: xarchive-t1101 - x-dumps has no ssh route to t1101; give the route or run iggy-box-user.sh there with username xarchive-t1101.")
inl note_of_iggy_public_cloud_13 () = Note ("IGGY-PUBLIC-CLOUD", 13u32, "2026-11-03T07:55Z", "x-dumps", "t1101 (laptop-ts) DONE 11-03: xarchive-t1101 created and verified SERVER-SIDE (public-IP login OK, POST /streams -> 403). iggy.env (176B) + iggy-ca.crt delivered file-to-file to C:\\Users\\alias\\.secrets (scp -3 vultr->node through the box), password never printed, vultr temp shredded. Node-side LITERAL login is not provable on t1101: its only curl is Windows curl 8.13 (Schannel), and .NET is Schannel too - both reject the ED25519 CA (known). The node must consume via a rustls/OpenSSL client (xarchive/events.rs), same as box. All three machines now have least-priv send+poll iggy users: xarchive-sao, xarchive-box, xarchive-t1101.")
inl note_of_iggy_public_cloud_14 () = Note ("IGGY-PUBLIC-CLOUD", 14u32, "2026-11-03T08:00Z", "x-dumps", "NODE-SIDE RUSTLS PROBE (plan for the run, per main 11-03): node-side iggy login cannot be proven via Windows curl (Schannel rejects the ED25519 CA; the node has only C:\\Windows\\System32\\curl.exe = Schannel, and .NET is Schannel too). Plan: deploy a small rustls probe (or xarchive/events.rs) to t1101 and box, do a login + poll against 140.82.49.44:3000 using IGGY_CA_FILE, to prove node-side consumption end to end. Server-side proof (public login + POST /streams 403) is enough for now.")
inl note_of_iggy_public_cloud_15 () = Note ("IGGY-PUBLIC-CLOUD", 15u32, "2026-11-03T12:39Z", "x-dumps", "STREAM/TOPIC for x lane 11-03: created stream \"x\" (id=1) + topic \"run-progress\" (id=0, 1 partition) on the public iggy (least-priv users cannot create them; root does). iggy 0.9 CreateTopic RECIPE (422 unless these are present): POST /streams/<stream>/topics with {\"name\":\"...\",\"partitions_count\":N,\"compression_algorithm\":\"none\",\"message_expiry\":0,\"max_topic_size\":0,\"replication_factor\":1} (0 => never-expire / unlimited). CreateStream is just {\"name\":\"...\"}. Existing least-priv users (xarchive-sao/-box/-t1101) have GLOBAL send+poll so they can publish/consume ANY stream incl x/run-progress without a per-stream grant. events.rs (xarchive) is a reusable single-module iggy client (Sink + publish + follow + base64, TLS via IGGY_CA_PEM/FILE) - liftable or extractable to a shared iggy_events crate.")
inl note_of_iggy_public_cloud_16 () = Note ("IGGY-PUBLIC-CLOUD", 16u32, "2026-11-05T12:18Z", "x-dumps", "Cross-check (x-dumps, user double-check 2026-11-05 ~08:15): iggy VERIFIED publicly reachable on vultr 140.82.49.44 - openssl s_client CONNECTED on BOTH :8090 (TCP) and :3000 (HTTP), serving TLS, cert subject CN=iggy.iggy.svc issuer \"x-dumps iggy CA\". Port 8092 (websocket) NOT publicly exposed. Client caveat unchanged: needs an ED25519-capable TLS client (rustls + iggy-ca.crt); box curl/Schannel rejects the ED25519 CA (HTTP 000) as noted 11-03. WATCH: the iggy pod has 13 restarts (last ~6h ago) from this morning tibia-induced vultr memory thrash - recovered + serving now, but pod limit is 512Mi; worth confirming it survived cleanly. Reachability = GOOD.")
inl cloud_node_backup () = Task ("CLOUD-NODE-BACKUP", "Use the Vultr node's large disk as one more off-site backup copy, encrypted, assuming the VM can vanish at any time (user 11-01 14:0x: 'considering that we might lose the node at any time, but could still be useful for backups, a few gbs dont hurt')", Story, P2, D2, 0u32, Planned, "REMOTE-CLOUD-LINUX-WORKER", "backup,resilience,cloud,remote,user-batch,team:resilience", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_cloud_node_backup () = Planning ("CLOUD-NODE-BACKUP", Minutes 90u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_cloud_node_backup_1 () = Note ("CLOUD-NODE-BACKUP", 1u32, "2026-11-01T18:09Z", "main", "main 14:1x: candidates are what has no off-box copy beyond the Wi-Fi node: the kit archive and drafts store, the wipe-recovery ledger, C:\\home\\data. DoD: a nightly encrypted push (age or similar; keys never leave the box, and nothing is uploaded unencrypted) of a resilience-chosen set to the VM; a restore drill from it is byte-exact; losing the VM costs nothing but this copy. Owner: resilience. Typefeatureset: BackupSet as a typed list of sources with phantom Encrypted/Plain tags; push accepts only Encrypted.")
inl note_of_cloud_node_backup_2 () = Note ("CLOUD-NODE-BACKUP", 2u32, "2026-11-01T18:19Z", "resilience", "2026-11-01 1x:xx GROUNDED DESIGN (resilience, builds on main n1). Owner accepted.\n\nCRITICAL OPEN QUESTION main's spec does NOT close - KEY SURVIVABILITY (surface to user): \"keys never leave the box\" is right for confidentiality, but if the decrypt key lives ONLY on the box and the BOX is wiped (exactly the disaster class this guards against - see profile-wipe-2026-10-28), the VM ciphertext is undecryptable => a WRITE-ONLY backup, useless for box-loss recovery. The key/identity must be escrowed OFF the box but NOT on the VM (that would defeat the encryption). Candidates: (a) the node - the key is tiny, store it there (already a trusted off-box location), or (b) user-held (password manager / the proton account). DECISION NEEDED from user. Until solved, this copy defends against NODE loss only, not box loss, for the encrypted set. This is the top of the user-batch for this item.\n\nENCRYPTION TOOL (grounded, probed box 11-01): age/rage ABSENT; gpg, openssl, 7z PRESENT (scoop). Use ASYMMETRIC-to-box-pubkey so the push side needs only the PUBLIC key and the secret key stays escrowed: today = `gpg -e -r <box-key>` (present now); preferred = age (download+pin per downloads-standing-ok; recipient model is cleaner, `age -r <pubkey>` / `age -d -i <identity>`). Either way the VM only ever holds ciphertext, and only the escrowed secret key decrypts. NOT symmetric-passphrase (a passphrase on the box has the same survivability problem and is weaker).\n\nTRANSPORT: VM is a LINUX worker (dep REMOTE-CLOUD-LINUX-WORKER), not the Wi-Fi node's cmd channel - so rsync box->Linux should work cleanly (the node rsync probe failed because the node is Windows-over-cmd; a real Linux sshd is the normal rsync case). Nightly = rsync the ciphertext blob dir (append/update only). Fallback scp per blob.\n\nBACKUP SET (sized from measured bytes 11-01, cadence by churn):\n- kit archive 1.5 GB (append-only; node copy exists) => NIGHTLY, MtimeDelta - deltas tiny.\n- drafts store 151 KB (only-copy; node copy exists) => NIGHTLY, FullSnapshot (tiny).\n- wipe-recovery ledger 148 MB (only-copy; node copy exists) => OnChange (near-static; re-push only when recovery re-triaged).\n- C:\\home\\data 575 MB but 99% ALREADY protected: rescue-2026-10-28 569 MB is the static node tar already (rescue-2026-10-28.tar, byte-verified) => ONE-TIME encrypted push, not nightly; node-checkout-only-extract 5 MB already safe (node holds its source zip) => SKIP; main-drafts 4.7 KB + churny lane drafts => NIGHTLY.\nInitial encrypted seed ~= 2.2 GB; nightly delta small. Respects x-dumps FIRST CLAIM on VM disk/CPU: schedule AFTER the x-dumps window, rate-limit rsync (--bwlimit), cap VM backup dir to an agreed quota (coordinate with personal lane, who runs the VM).\n\nDoD (byte-exact, concrete):\n- kit backup-push verb: per-source tar -> encrypt to box pubkey -> upload ONLY ciphertext (phantom Encrypted gate: push accepts no Plain path) -> append manifest {source, plaintext_sha256, cipher_sha256, bytes, uploaded_at}.\n- kit backup-drill verb (weekly, profile-restore-drill shape): pull ciphertext, decrypt with escrowed secret key, sha256(plaintext) == recorded plaintext_sha256 => ByteExact | Mismatch{local,restored} | Missing | DecryptFailed. \"Losing the VM costs nothing but this copy\" is VERIFIED by the box+node copies remaining intact and independently drill-passing without the VM.\n- Nightly push + weekly drill registration = user-batch (SCHEDULED-TASKS-CONFIRM; tag user-batch already set). I never self-register persistent machine config.\n\nTYPEFEATURESET (extends main n1):\n- BackupSet = list {source: typed source; cadence: | Nightly | OnChange | OneTime; shape: | FullSnapshot | MtimeDelta}.\n- phantom path location = | Plain | Cipher; upload : path Cipher -> result (a Plain path cannot be uploaded - compile-time guarantee nothing leaves unencrypted, satisfying main's \"push accepts only Encrypted\").\n- key as a typed Identity handle read from a box-local escrowed path, never inlined, never passed to the Cipher transport.\n- drill_result union above; manifest rows carry typed hash newtypes (plaintext vs cipher sha256 are distinct types, not swappable).\nGENERALIZES the kit dir-mirror verb (KIT-ARCHIVE-OFFBOX-MIRROR): backup-push = dir-mirror + an encrypt stage + a manifest; same size/hash verify. One verb family, three transports (node Windows, VM Linux, hot profile mirror).\n\nDEPENDENCIES: REMOTE-CLOUD-LINUX-WORKER (VM ssh target + sshd) - already a dep; gated on it. Coordinate with PERSONAL lane for: VM backup dir, disk quota, upload window after x-dumps. Message personal lane once the VM is reachable (nothing for them to act on before REMOTE-CLOUD-LINUX-WORKER lands). NODE-SAFETY note (unrelated to VM, flagged by main 11-01): node now junctions kit\\{jobs,windows} -> eoie\\{jobs,windows}; unlink before any node cleanup under eoie\\ or kit\\ (captured in memory unlink-mirror-junctions-first). Ties KIT-ARCHIVE-OFFBOX-MIRROR, PROFILE-RESTORE-DRILL-PORT, memory backup-mirror-coverage-boundary.")
inl note_of_cloud_node_backup_3 () = Note ("CLOUD-NODE-BACKUP", 3u32, "2026-11-01T18:37Z", "main", "main 14:4x PARKED USER DECISION (22:00 batch), from resilience n2: where does the decrypt key live off the box? A key only on the box makes the VM copy useless after a box wipe, the exact disaster this item covers; on the VM it gives no confidentiality. Options: (a) the node, already a trusted off-box machine; (b) the user's password manager or Proton account. Main recommends (b), plus (a) as a second copy, so losing any one machine never loses both the data and the key. Until decided, the copy covers node loss only.")
inl note_of_cloud_node_backup_4 () = Note ("CLOUD-NODE-BACKUP", 4u32, "2026-11-02T14:32Z", "x", "2026-11-02 10:28 x: DECISION RECORD v2 (user 11-02: 'why mine? try healing autonomously/deterministically with tests/benchs/typefeatureset/backups the pending decisions'). Only git commits (the user's standing rule) and the harness hook config (the harness itself says Claude must not change it) stay user-only. Everything else gets a precondition, a check and an undo; actions only the user's accounts can take get a non-blocking default. kind=ByTest. RULE: Default: the decrypt key lives on BOTH private machines (box and node), never in a repo, plus a monthly restore drill (the existing profile-restore-drill task). PASS iff a restore from the Vultr copy decrypts with the node's key and with the box's key. A password-manager copy is optional and stays the user's. BACKUP/UNDO: The key file is copied, never moved; the old location is kept. OWNER: resilience.")
inl note_of_cloud_node_backup_5 () = Note ("CLOUD-NODE-BACKUP", 5u32, "2026-11-05T04:47Z", "resilience", "GROUNDING (resilience, vultr df): vultr /dev/vda2 = 94G total, 66G used, 24G FREE (74%), no ~/backups dir yet (not landing there). 24G free fits the user intent (treat VM as disposable, a few GB is fine) for a SELECTIVE ENCRYPTED backup of the hardest-to-recover data - kit archive (3.31G job evidence), .claude memory, unstaged drafts - NOT a full-box copy (box has 738G used). Design: age/gpg-encrypt a small (<5G) tar of {kit archive, memory, unstaged} and push to vultr ~/backups as one more off-site copy (box + node + vultr = 3 copies), pruned to 1-2 generations given the 24G shared with vultr workload. CONSTRAINTS: (a) vultr is x-dumps-owned for CPU - coordinate timing so a backup push does not collide with its jobs; (b) encryption key material stays on the box, never sent to vultr; (c) vultr load was 2.49/1.34/1.08 (2 vCPU) so gate the push on loadavg like other vultr work. Pairs with KIT-ARCHIVE-OFFBOX-MIRROR (node copy) - vultr = the off-SITE tier. resilience.")
inl kit_typed_crates () = Task ("KIT-TYPED-CRATES", "kit enforces the typefeatureset on itself: typed cross-crate bindings generated from Spiral types instead of $' string calls, and shared model packages as real dependency crates built once instead of inlined into every importer; a rewrite of kit's composition model if needed (user 11-01 18:1x)", Epic, P0, D8, 25u32, Planned, "", "kit,typefeatureset,architecture,drift,standards,team:kit,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_kit_typed_crates () = Planning ("KIT-TYPED-CRATES", Minutes 4800u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_kit_typed_crates_1 () = Note ("KIT-TYPED-CRATES", 1u32, "2026-11-01T18:18Z", "kit", "11-01 18:1x kit. USER (verbatim): 'we gotta study all models of project bootstrapping that we have so we help kit fix its drift. a review diagnosed \"generate typed cross-crate bindings from spiral types, instead of $' string calls\", also \"make shared model packages real dependency crates, so they're built once instead of copied into every importer\". we gotta be using the typefeatureset to the max at all times, that's kit priority number one to enforce and it needs to happen with itself too, even if we rewrite the whole project in the process, we're iterating 24h anyway'. EVIDENCE FROM TODAY (TRAIN7): kit_lint_model is inlined into 4 crates and agile_todo_model into 7; agile_todo_model's path_verdict_of_code alone generates 96 lines in each importer, because a union crossing a crate boundary is flattened to an i32 code and rebuilt by reflection (case_at/case_index); two crates went over the 1000-line gate partly from that inlining, and the hand split needed two new crates (agile_todo_standing, kit_lint_baseline) whose calls are more untyped $' strings the style hook flags. PLAN: S0 = the study of every bootstrapping/composition model across our repos (kit, spiral lib + cells scaffold, tibia, fiscal, x, dice, alphabet, polyglot), running now; S1 = a typed binding generator: from each crate's export signatures (Spiral types), emit one typed Spiral binding module per crate (the only place a $' call lives, a boundary package) plus the Rust extern side, so callers write typed calls and the hook has nothing to flag; S2 = derived codecs for unions/records/GADTs crossing crates (reflection-derived, byte-identical both sides, the iggy codec idea), replacing i32-code flattening; S3 = shared model packages become dependency crates (built once, exported through S1 bindings), with a measure of generated lines saved per importer; S4 = migrate kit crate by crate under the ratchet; S5 = a kit standards rule refusing new $'<crate>:: calls and new multi-importer inlined model packages. DoD: 0 hand-written cross-crate $' calls in kit/src; every model package imported by more than one crate is a crate; total generated lines drop measurably; the crate gate and ratchet stay green; node proof per slice. TYPEFEATURES: binding signatures as a GADT indexed by the export's type (op from to); existential packed exports in the registry; phantom crate markers so a binding can only be called from a crate that declares the dependency.")
inl note_of_kit_typed_crates_2 () = Note ("KIT-TYPED-CRATES", 2u32, "2026-11-01T18:44Z", "kit", "11-01 14:44 kit S0 STUDY DONE (read-only survey of 9 projects). MODELS: kit = 272 crates, each a whole-program compile of its package graph, calling each other by \\$'crate::fn' after string exports, unions flattened to i32 (kit/src/union_reflection/cases.spi:26,40); x apps/client = 29 crates with TYPED exports (export ... : () -> command_fields becomes pub fn ... -> (i32, Rc<str>, bool, ...), args.rs:520) but the caller redeclares the record; dir-tree-html = 5 crates with every \\$' stub in one module (shared.spi:102-106), the hand-made version of a binding module; tibia/fiscal = plain Spiral calls inside whole-program entries (fully typed, but kit as one program would be ~150k lines, against the 1000-line gate); dice/alphabet/polyglot/lib/Cells scaffold = single package. KIT MEASURED: 480 package dirs, 272 crates, 205 non-crate packages, 44 of them inlined into more than one crate transitively (rust_std_string 114 crates, union_reflection 95, state_receipt_codec_model 64, workspace_primitives 62, agile_mutation_policy 45, agile_task_text 42, kit_receipt 37, agile_note_model 19, ...), 33 crate packages also inlined into others (rust_std_env 79, rust_std_fs 64, kit_jobs 29); 116 packages reached by no crate; 303 hand-written \\$'<kit crate>:: sites in 91 files over 132 crates (+496 \\$'std::); 644 !!!!Export + 27 export; 150,048 generated lines; top five crates at 996-999 lines, so the gate is what forces splits. COMPILER ALREADY HAS (spiral_compiler.fs): typed Rust export signatures, records of primitives become pub struct (:14050-14094); every union reachable from an export becomes 'pub enum <name>' with tag() (:12453-12476, :14499-14506; checked by scripts/export-check-rust/union_return); '///- --import-types <crate>:<U1>,<U2>' makes a crate drop its own copy of those enums and 'use <crate>::{...}' (:12441-12451, :14507-14540; host Program.fs:1221-1235; Cells directives.spi:53); 'rust-extern: derive = ...' adds derives (:13529); the Gleam backend has the same import/export logic (:17754, :18515). UNUSED by kit, x and eoie. No type-derived codec exists (lib reflection.spi has the primitives; iggy.spi:44-64 is hand-written). DESIGN DECISION (S1-S3 reshaped): converge on x/apps/client's crate layout + typed exports + union export + --import-types: (S1) shared model packages become crates exporting typed functions, whose unions become pub enums; (S2) callers import those types with --import-types, so unions cross as real Rust enums and path_verdict_code/path_verdict_of_code/case_at round-trips disappear; (S3) the typed \\$' stubs are GENERATED into one binding module per callee crate (dir-tree-html's shared.spi pattern, automated), replacing the 303 hand-written sites; (S4) a standards rule refuses new hand-written cross-crate \\$' and new multi-importer inlined models. RISKS TO SPIKE FIRST: the struct path covers only records of primitives (unions with list/nested-record payloads may need compiler work, which goes to the compiler lane); the 'use' replacement matches union names on generated text (:14512-14514), so names that exist in more than one package must be checked. NEXT: SPIKE on agile_todo_model (inlined into 7 crates; path_verdict holds nested unions comment_syntax/skip_reason): make it a crate exporting path_verdict_of_path, make agile_todo_paths/scan/merge use --import-types agile_todo_model:path_verdict,comment_syntax,skip_reason, measure generated lines before and after and run the todo_cli notebook on the node.")
inl note_of_kit_typed_crates_3 () = Note ("KIT-TYPED-CRATES", 3u32, "2026-11-01T18:55Z", "kit", "11-01 14:55 kit SPIKE GREEN (node job-1793558949388, 111 s). Change (unstaged): agile_todo_paths exports kit_agile_todo_path_verdict as 'string * bool -> path_verdict' (typed, migrated from !!!!Export to export, i32 code path removed). agile_todo_scan and agile_todo_merge carry '///- --import-types agile_todo_paths:path_verdict,comment_syntax,path_skip' and call '\\$'agile_todo_paths::kit_agile_todo_path_verdict(...)' : path_verdict' with no path_verdict_of_code decode. RESULT: regen published 3 owners and the candidate built (cargo build of kit-cli = cross-crate type check); generated lines agile_todo_paths 340 -> 324, agile_todo_scan 937 -> 812 (-125), agile_todo_merge 939 -> 815 (-124): -265 lines for ONE union crossing, and the two biggest drops come from no longer inlining union_reflection's case_at decoder. Both importers emit 'use agile_todo_paths::{comment_syntax, path_skip, path_verdict};' and drop their local enums: nested union payloads cross as real Rust enums (risk 1 cleared for unions; record payloads still untested). todo_cli 4/4 green (consumer path: stale/unknown/scan verdicts through the binary). Name-collision risk (2) not hit here; the generator must check for source-name collisions. NEXT (S1 proper): (a) a kit verb 'kit bindings <crate>' in Spiral: read the crate's export declarations (name + typed lambda signature), emit one typed binding module per callee (the only \\$' sites, plus the import-types list the importer needs), and rewrite importers' call sites to the typed functions; (b) apply to the next biggest crossings by measured payoff (union_reflection in 95 crates, state_receipt_codec_model 64): measure generated lines per crossing first; (c) retire path_verdict_code/path_verdict_of_code from agile_todo_model once the model test stops using them.")
inl note_of_kit_typed_crates_4 () = Note ("KIT-TYPED-CRATES", 4u32, "2026-11-01T20:08Z", "kit", "11-01 16:08 kit: S1 progress. (a) kit_bindings_model (HostFree, node 2/2 and Linux 2/2): reads both export spellings with a depth-aware scan, splits typed signatures, renders typed binding lines plus the --import-types header. (b) The kit bindings verb is wired (VerbBindings in command_spec_domain, routed in kit_cli_model, new crate kit_bindings, kit-cli dep). First node proof: notebooks green, but kit_bindings.rs was 1,899 lines (decls_from alone 399), because every := helper inlined the char-scan chain into each caller; fixed by making the 18 heavy helpers :~ (emitted once). The verb also read argv off by one (kit argv starts at the verb word); fixed. Re-proof running: job-1793563609342 (also carries the node --after owner fix). EVIDENCE for S3 from resilience (BEAM-GUARD): kit_machine_sample's exported 9-tuple process_rows_sampled is inlined into 7 importer crates (kit_machine_sample, kit_machine_watch, kit_machine_health, kit_process_table, kit_process_kill, kit_jobs_doctor, kit_jobs_doctor_servers); adding one field broke two importers with E0308 until all 7 were regenerated, which is exactly the coupling a typed record export plus --import-types removes. LESSON for the binding generator and the regex repo: in this kit dialect := inlines, so any helper over ~5 lines that several callers reach must be :~, or the 1000-line gate fails on code that looks small in Spiral.")
inl note_of_kit_typed_crates_5 () = Note ("KIT-TYPED-CRATES", 5u32, "2026-11-01T21:08Z", "kit", "11-01 17:08 kit: S1 milestones from node proof 3 (job-1793565238955): (1) kit bindings <crate> on agile_todo_paths reproduces the spike's hand-written binding plus import line exactly (two-line format: signature, then the indented typed call); (2) CENSUS over all 274 crates: 643 exports, 638 typed (99.2%), 5 untyped (kit_job_gate 1, kit_job_slot 1, state_receipt_codec_core 1, plus 2 false markers from test-fixture strings, now skipped); (3) shared-model-as-crate applied to itself: kit_bindings_model became a crate with two typed exports, and the verb crate kit_bindings (516 lines) calls them only through the generated-format binding module kit_bindings_model_api (host macros in the verb: 0). Open: kit_bindings_model.rs was 1,356 lines; the scanner is rewritten around ONE shared depth fold (marks: byte offsets of top-level delimiters, with all splitting done by substring) instead of five char folds, re-proving in job-1793567257674 with the dogfood check (the generated binding module must equal kit_bindings_model_api byte for byte).")
inl note_of_kit_typed_crates_6 () = Note ("KIT-TYPED-CRATES", 6u32, "2026-11-01T21:10Z", "kit", "11-01 17:10 kit S2 MIGRATION QUEUE (measured: 37 union-flattening sites, case_at/case_index/*_of_code, across 15 packages). Order by payoff: (1) the kit_lint family: kit_lint_classify::kit_lint_class takes place/origin as i32 and returns a class code; kit_lint_walk::kit_lint_known takes a class i32; decoders place_of_code/origin_of_code/class_of_code in kit_lint_model (inlined into 6 crates). Migration: typed exports over macro_place/macro_origin/macro_class, with --import-types kit_lint_classify:macro_place,macro_origin,macro_class in macros/scan/walk; the decoders retire. (2) agile_todo_model: path_verdict done (spike); the remaining ones are comment_syntax and syntax codes in agile_todo_lines' findings export (13 sites in the model). (3) kit_lint_shadows_core kind_of_code. (4) command_spec_domain (4), authority_state_domain (2), canonical_plan_ir_domain (1): small, after the generator writes the binding modules. S5 NOTE: the refusal of new hand-written cross-crate calls already exists in effect, because the ratchet counts host-macro per package and refuses growth (it refused the 0->2 in kit_bindings today, which pushed the call into a binding module). The remaining S5 work is to name the rule (cross-crate-call) separately from OS host macros, and to exempt generated *_api binding modules by a header marker.")
inl note_of_kit_typed_crates_7 () = Note ("KIT-TYPED-CRATES", 7u32, "2026-11-01T22:08Z", "kit", "11-01 18:08 kit DIRECTION CHANGE (user 17:4x via main): \"now we're inventing things like 'import-types'? damn, that strayed far hard from upstream ... we got plans to use types instead of this weird plumbing we're having to build to support the old splitting way. help us get sota!\" ACTIONS: (1) the --import-types spike is reverted in the tree (unstaged reversal of the staged spike hunks in agile_todo_paths/scan/merge; the spike files are archived at kit\\archive\\20261101-180537-typed-crates-spike; evidence kept in n3); (2) the bindings verb is unregistered from TRAIN8 and parked as module packages (Cargo.toml archived at kit\\archive\\20261101-175534-bindings-cargo); (3) no more building on --import-types. NEXT: one design agreed with the compiler lane for native typed cross-crate exports in an upstream-compatible form (no header flag), possibly fewer crates instead of typed plumbing for the split, before any more code. What stays valid from today: S0's measurements (inlined models, 303 untyped sites, 150k generated lines), the census tool idea, and the := vs :~ size lesson.")
inl note_of_kit_typed_crates_8 () = Note ("KIT-TYPED-CRATES", 8u32, "2026-11-01T22:16Z", "kit", "11-01 18:16 kit: DESIGN AGREED with compiler (18:1x). Native typed linking at monomorphic export boundaries, declared in package.spiproj as a new `link:` field (same syntax as packages:), the upstream-compatible form, since upstream Spiral is whole-program over the spiproj graph and has no separate compilation. Units stay hand-modeled and the 1000-line gate stays per crate. Step A (compiler, small): the importedTypeUnits map is built from the package graph, so unions declared in a link: package belong to that unit; the SPIRAL_IMPORT_TYPES env var / --import-types plumbing is deleted; unions stop flattening to i32 (the 37 reflection rebuilds go). Step B (compiler, medium): calls to monomorphic exports of link: packages become typed extern calls (a new TyOp carrying the signature; codegenRust emits unit::fn(args)), replacing the 303 $' host strings; model-only link: packages contribute types, not code (the rust_std_string-in-114-crates win). Compiler files it as COMPILER-LINKED-UNITS; this epic depends on it. Holes kit raised: Cargo deps generated from link: (one source of truth), payload coverage beyond records of primitives, union identity by owning package not source name, and the same unit graph for Gleam. kit's part: baseline measurements (n2), running each step's candidate over kit on the node, and the Cargo-deps generator.")
inl note_of_kit_typed_crates_9 () = Note ("KIT-TYPED-CRATES", 9u32, "2026-11-01T22:49Z", "kit", "11-01 18:49 kit: compiler Step A candidate ready (COMPILER-LINKED-UNITS): DLL at node C:\\home\\remote-stage\\linked\\cache\\bin\\single-flight\\...\\SpiralCompiler.dll, pinned per job with SPIRAL_COMPILER_DLL. An importer's package.spiproj gets `link:` (same syntax as packages:); unions declared in a linked package are matched by owning-package id and imported as use <unit>::{name}, where unit = the linked package's folder; no SPIRAL_IMPORT_TYPES. Not yet: Step B typed extern calls, Cargo deps from link:, record payloads. kit's measurement plan (after the TRAIN8 pre-proof frees kit-verify; node disk is tight, so no new mirror): agile_todo_model becomes a unit exporting kit_agile_todo_path_verdict : string * bool -> path_verdict; agile_todo_scan/merge add link: agile_todo_model; compare with today's baseline (paths 340 / scan 937 / merge 939) and the import-types spike (scan 812 / merge 815); gate todo_cli 4/4 + cargo build. rust_std_string and union_reflection are Step B measurements (no unions / decoder only).")
inl note_of_kit_typed_crates_10 () = Note ("KIT-TYPED-CRATES", 10u32, "2026-11-02T00:35Z", "kit", "11-01 20:35 kit: STEP A FIRST RUN over kit (node job-1793579446108): HOLE found and sent to compiler. With link: agile_todo_model in scan/merge, the importers emitted use agile_todo_model::{comment_syntax, path_skip, path_verdict, content_verdict, todo_filter} (every linked-package union they use), but the unit emits pub enum only for unions reachable from its exports, so cargo failed with E0432 (content_verdict, todo_filter). Proposed fix (2): a link: unit emits pub enum for every union it declares (the single owner of its types). Measurement pending the fix.")
inl note_of_kit_typed_crates_11 () = Note ("KIT-TYPED-CRATES", 11u32, "2026-11-02T00:39Z", "kit", "11-01 20:39 kit: compiler's design for the Step A second cut (20:4x). The export-reachable union set can only come from the UNIT's own compile (export is a runtime op that exists only when the unit's main is partially evaluated), so compiling a link: unit writes a typed UNIT GRAPH next to its output (package id, each export name plus monomorphic signature, and the union nominals reachable from it with concrete type args); importers read it and owner-map exactly those unions, keeping the rest local. Build order: the unit before its importers; the graph is generated, never committed. It is also kit's hole-1 artifact: the Cargo-deps generator reads the same graph (one source of truth). Scoped on COMPILER-LINKED-UNITS for the next session. Kit workaround until then: link: only packages whose cross-boundary unions are all export-reachable. kit-verify target pruned for node disk (main's ask); the Step A rerun rebuilds it.")
inl note_of_kit_typed_crates_12 () = Note ("KIT-TYPED-CRATES", 12u32, "2026-11-02T01:14Z", "kit", "11-01 21:14 kit: the S0 study agent (resumed by the user) extended its report. NEW FACTS: (1) --import-types came in bulk commit cec8840c (10-28, message \"tests\", 50+ files, 383 compiler lines) with no fixture, no docs and no user, implemented by regex surgery on generated text (delete enum/impl blocks, insert use) and matched by source name while the producer renames clashes to <name>_<n> (spiral_compiler.fs:12463), which supports the user's 17:4x rejection; (2) x apps/client has the same root problems, milder: a hand-mirrored 15-field tuple (xc.spi:32 parsed, copied from fields.spi:36 command_fields; a same-typed field swap compiles silently), shared modules copied per crate, one package for all 29 crates (no declared boundaries), and 48 hand-written $'xc_*:: strings; (3) x client also has the 1000-line gate (x/scripts/gate.ps1). TEMPLATE IDEAS kept as inputs: crate kinds (model/boundary/feature/bin), generated binding modules plus Cargo deps, ratchets (0 $'<crate>:: outside binding modules, 0 non-type packages copied into more than one crate, string->string exports only go down), one build script reading Cargo spiral metadata. SUPERSEDED: the study's union mechanism (--import-types) is replaced by the agreed `link:` design (n8, COMPILER-LINKED-UNITS), so the generated bindings and Cargo deps come from the unit graph, not from header flags.")
inl note_of_kit_typed_crates_13 () = Note ("KIT-TYPED-CRATES", 13u32, "2026-11-02T10:50Z", "main", "main 11-02 07:0x from the kit-session Explore report (aaf2a6f472dc70094). PHASES: K0 tag kit 0.x before the typed boundary. K2 promote shared models to units: state_receipt_codec_model (copied into 64 crates), workspace_primitives 62, agile_mutation_policy 45, agile_task_text 42, kit_receipt 37, agile_note_model 19, kit_do_records 13, agile_planning_model 11, agile_todo_model 7, kit_lint_model 6. K3 remove rust_std_string (114) + union_reflection (95); stop importing rust_std_env/rust_std_fs/kit_jobs/kit_machine_windows as packages. K4 delete the 37 union-flattening sites (n6), 303 hand call sites, 116 packages no crate uses. KIT-BINDINGS-INTERIM: `kit bindings` (n4-n5) covers only the period before COMPILER-LINKED-UNITS-EXTERN-CALLS (Step B); delete it when Step B lands. RECONCILE: census says 638/643 exports \"typed\", but by return type 432 return i32 (many union codes) and 146 Rc<str>; \"typed\" means annotated, not typed. Hard rule for exports: fully concrete signatures (no type params); a GADT index crosses a unit boundary only wrapped in an existential (EJP0041 advice).")
inl note_of_kit_typed_crates_14 () = Note ("KIT-TYPED-CRATES", 14u32, "2026-11-02T10:53Z", "kit", "kit 11-02 07:0x GROOM of main's n13 (accepted, with corrections). (1) RECONCILE, measured on the live tree after TRAIN10: 892 generated pub fns; return types 434 i32, 146 Rc<str>, 75 Result<(),String>, 54 u64, 23 bool. Both earlier numbers were the wrong metric: the census's 638/643 'typed' means annotated, and the 434 i32 include every verb entry's exit code, which is a legitimate i32. The metric that tracks the defect is call-side: 127 hand-written cross-crate calls typed ': i32', and 39 decoder sites (*_of_code / case_at / case_index) in 16 files (n6 counted 37; +2 since). K4's DoD uses those two counts, both going to 0 for unions (exit codes stay i32). (2) OWN REGRESSION, recorded: TRAIN10's kit_job_host host_run.spi decodes kit_job_queue::kit_job_queue_decide's i32 (0/1/else) into queue_verdict: one more union-as-code crossing, added by me. It joins the K4 list; it goes away when kit_job_queue links kit_job_host's queue_verdict (Step A) instead of returning a code. (3) K0 (tag kit 0.x) is a git write, so it's the user's action; kit prepares the tag name and commit, nothing more. (4) ORDER: KIT-LOAD-PROOF-E2E runs before K2/K3, because pruning unreached code first shrinks what K2-K4 migrate. K2 waits on Step A cut 2 (the unit graph, n12); K3's rust_std_string/union_reflection removal needs Step B (they carry functions, not just types). (5) KIT-BINDINGS-INTERIM: already parked as module packages since n7 (not registered, no crate); when Step B lands, archive kit_bindings and kit_bindings_model with scratch-archive.ps1, never delete. (6) Export rule accepted: fully concrete export signatures; a GADT index crosses a unit only inside an existential.")
inl note_of_kit_typed_crates_15 () = Note ("KIT-TYPED-CRATES", 15u32, "2026-11-02T12:05Z", "kit", "kit 11-02 08:0x STEP A SECOND CUT GREEN over kit (node job-1793620674664-kit-stepA2b, compiler linked2 DLL host 9489144496A935E6, lib json.spi 20C39CB9). agile_todo_model is a link: unit exporting kit_agile_todo_path_verdict : string * bool -> path_verdict; agile_todo_scan/merge add link: agile_todo_model and call it typed. Compiled directly in dependency order (model 6 s -> model.unit.json 878 B; scan 6 s; merge 7 s). RESULT: scan 937 -> 812, merge 939 -> 815 (= the --import-types spike's numbers, with no header flag); both import exactly use agile_todo_model::{comment_syntax, path_skip, path_verdict}; content_verdict/todo_filter stay local, so n11's E0432 is gone (0); cargo build -p kit-cli exit 0; todo_cli notebook 4/4. KIT BUILD GAPS this exposed (kit's work, before linked units enter kit's normal build): (1) test-regeneration.ps1 compiles owners in one staged workspace and copies back only .rs, so a unit's <stem>.unit.json is lost and the importer of a separately regenerated unit fails ('has no unit graph'); it must order owners by link: (units first) and keep/copy *.unit.json; (2) the Cargo [dependencies] generator reads the unit graph's links field (compiler n12); (3) *.unit.json goes in .gitignore. Also: kit_bindings_model's generator no longer emits '///- --import-types' (link_section returns the importer's 'link:\\\\n    <crate>' fragment, binding modules carry only typed lines), unblocking IMPORT-TYPES-DELETE on kit's side; node test pending.")
inl note_of_kit_typed_crates_16 () = Note ("KIT-TYPED-CRATES", 16u32, "2026-11-02T12:47Z", "kit", "kit 11-02 08:4x BUILD GAP (1) FIXED + A/B-PROVEN. Decision by construction: auto-include each link: unit in the same regen run and compile it before its importers, instead of copying *.unit.json into the tree (a persisted graph can go stale when the unit changes without a regen; a same-run graph can't). compiler-contracts/test-regeneration.ps1 (unstaged): Get-ProjectSection reads packages: and link:; link: units are inputs of their importers (watched closure); selection grows by the transitive link: closure and is ordered units-first by a DFS that throws on a link: cycle; it logs 'link: units compiled first: ...'. .gitignore gains /src/**/*.unit.json. NODE A/B on the same mirror (Step A overlay, linked2 DLL), regenerating ONLY scan+merge through candidate-build: A = HEAD regen -> exit 1, both 'FatalError: The link: unit agile_todo_model has no unit graph ... Build agile_todo_model before the units that link it' (job-1793623343093); B = new regen -> '3 selected owners' (model pulled in), Published 3, candidate built, scan 812 / merge 815 with use agile_todo_model::{comment_syntax, path_skip, path_verdict}, 0 *.unit.json left in the tree, todo_cli 4/4 (job-1793623099032). Remaining gap (2): Cargo [dependencies] from the unit graph's links (today the overlay hand-adds agile-todo-model to scan/merge Cargo.toml).")
inl note_of_kit_typed_crates_17 () = Note ("KIT-TYPED-CRATES", 17u32, "2026-11-02T13:16Z", "main", "main 11-02 (user: \"gotta check if we fully healed from this rust_std_env, we're using lib now, gotta find all similar drift in the other lanes\"). NOT HEALED: 280 kit files still reference kit's local wrapper packages: rust_std_fs 370 uses (package 695 lines + rust_std_fs_mutation 552), rust_std_string 250 (34 lines), rust_std_env 165 (58 lines), rust_std_process 55 (124 lines). No other lane uses them (the i574n.github hits are backlog text). Phase K3 covers this and has not started; it is the concrete next step. Similar drift elsewhere = lanes redefining lib helpers under the same name (DUPLICATE-CODE-TOOL note 11-02).")
inl note_of_kit_typed_crates_18 () = Note ("KIT-TYPED-CRATES", 18u32, "2026-11-02T16:00Z", "kit", "kit 11-02 12:1x K3 STEP 1 (unstaged): rust_std_string's concat5 (363 call sites, the dominant use; then replace 47, contains 27) no longer is a host macro: `:~ concat5 a b c d e = a ++# b ++# c ++# d ++# e` (a join point, emitted once per crate), and the package now references spiral- (lib-referenced) and opens sm'_operators. Chosen by measurement over a per-call-site rewrite: zero call sites edited, and the per-call Rc::from([..].concat()) expansion is gone. PRE-REGISTERED RULE: accept if no crate passes 1000 and each grows <= 5%. NODE (job-1793634757738, 10 owners regenerated + built): kit_machine_windows 455 -> 458, kit_machine_watch 898 -> 888, kit_memory_query 996 -> 995, kit_do_process 946 -> 936, kit_style_census 987 -> 988; accepted. Census ratchet rust_std_string: 0 regressions. Next K3 steps: replace/contains/starts_with/ends_with/trim the same way (each delegating to lib sm'), then a Spiral (not pwsh) rename of call sites to lib names and deletion of rust_std_string. A pwsh call-site converter was tried and dropped: PowerShell tuple returns corrupted lines and it was the wrong tool class (Spiral-first).")
inl note_of_kit_typed_crates_19 () = Note ("KIT-TYPED-CRATES", 19u32, "2026-11-03T07:35Z", "kit", "kit groom 11-03 03:3x (WIP cap, memory wip-cap-active-means-touched): Active -> Planned, last note 16 h ago by kit, progress kept at 25%. Resume from that note: lib sm'), then a Spiral (not pwsh) rename of call sites to lib names and deletion of rust_std_string. A pwsh call-site converter was tried and dropped: PowerShell tuple returns corrupted lines and it was the wrong tool class (Spiral-first).")
inl note_of_kit_typed_crates_20 () = Note ("KIT-TYPED-CRATES", 20u32, "2026-11-03T08:50Z", "kit", "11-03 04:5x kit: now on KIT-CATCH-UP's critical path. The first granular heal (kit_grep_query found.spi, draft in the kit store) needs its own owner crate behind a typed link: boundary carrying search_hits (an array of records holding arrays). NEXT for kit: generate Cargo [dependencies] from the compiler's unit graph (compiler's note: \"Kit's Cargo generator reading the graph is kit's\"). Ask compiler about payload coverage for records holding arrays when the run starts.")
inl pages_file_viewers () = Task ("PAGES-FILE-VIEWERS", "Every text file on a Pages site opens in the browser: dir-tree-html writes an x.ext.html viewer next to each .spi/.tsv/.toml/.ps1 (and any type Pages serves as a download), and the tree links to the viewer, keeping a small raw link (user 11-01 14:0x: clicking tsv/spi/toml/ps1 on tibia's Pages downloads the file, unlike lua/yml/txt)", Story, P1, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "dir-tree-html,pages,ci,all-repos,user-batch,main,team:main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_pages_file_viewers () = Planning ("PAGES-FILE-VIEWERS", Minutes 180u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_pages_file_viewers_1 () = Note ("PAGES-FILE-VIEWERS", 1u32, "2026-11-01T18:37Z", "main", "main 14:4x, from the dib-parity research: GitHub Pages sets Content-Type by extension and allows no custom headers. .spi/.ps1 are served as application/octet-stream; .tsv (text/tab-separated-values) and .toml (application/toml) aren't shown inline either. tree.spi:79-81,96 links every file to its raw path; publish-tree.ps1 copies tracked files with git checkout-index. DoD: on tibia's deployed site, clicking a .spi, .toml, .ps1 and .tsv opens an HTML page; .spi is colored by the compiler's tokens verb (or links to its Cells notebook page when one exists), .tsv renders as a table, others as escaped pre. One tree link per file, plus 'raw'. Typefeatureset: Viewer = SpiralSource | Table | Plain as a closed union picked by a typed extension map, not string matching scattered through the renderer.")
inl note_of_pages_file_viewers_2 () = Note ("PAGES-FILE-VIEWERS", 2u32, "2026-11-01T19:05Z", "main", "main 15:2x CODE WRITTEN, unstaged in dir-tree-html: tree.spi gets a closed viewer union (Direct | SourceView | TableView | MarkdownView) chosen from a typed extension map. With the new --viewers switch, the tree links viewer files to x.ext.html (plus a small raw link) and write_viewers writes the pages: escaped <pre> for source, an HTML table for .tsv, the markdown renderer for .md. The tree is rendered BEFORE the viewers are written, so the generated pages don't list themselves. Without --viewers the output is byte-identical to before. Self-test viewers_test covers the tree links, the .spi and .tsv pages, and no page for .txt. tree crate now depends on dtl_inline and dtl_markdown. Next: (1) build proof (queued); (2) spiral's publish-tree/dir-tree-html.ps1 passes --viewers once CI's pin moves past a643950 (TREE-PAGES-V2); (3) .spi coloring via the compiler tokens verb, or link to the Cells notebook page (PAGES-NOTEBOOK-PUBLISH).")
inl note_of_pages_file_viewers_3 () = Note ("PAGES-FILE-VIEWERS", 3u32, "2026-11-01T20:27Z", "main", "main 16:3x PROVEN on the box. The viewer is now its own crate, viewer/ (dtl_viewer), with single-argument typed exports: has_viewer, write_viewer and a crate self-test. tree keeps the walk and the links, which kept every generated file under 1000 lines. write_viewer never overwrites an existing x.ext.html (a Cells notebook page wins). Smoke with --viewers on tibia content: README.md.html, a .tsv rendered as a table, suite.spi.html as escaped source, all linked from the tree with raw links. Left: (1) CI passes --viewers once the renderer pin moves (TREE-PAGES-V2); (2) .spi coloring via the compiler's tokens verb; (3) .tsv viewers become moot as repos drop .tsv (MIGRATE-TIBIA-TSV).")
inl note_of_pages_file_viewers_4 () = Note ("PAGES-FILE-VIEWERS", 4u32, "2026-11-03T08:52Z", "main", "main 11-03 04:2x SUPERSEDED, closing. The viewer behaviour (x.ext.html next to each text file, raw link, never overwriting an existing .html) is in html (tree.spi a_linked_file_gets_a_viewer_page_and_a_raw_link; HTML-PARITY 232/232 with --viewers), and dir-tree-html has left the org. Leftovers moved: (1) CI passes --viewers -> HTML-ADOPT-REPOS; (2) .spi coloring through the compiler's tokens verb -> HTML-VIEWERS; (3) .tsv viewers become moot as repos drop tracked .tsv (MIGRATE-TXT-FILES).")
inl dir_tree_markdown_gfm () = Task ("DIR-TREE-MARKDOWN-GFM", "dir-tree-html's README renderer handles GitHub-flavored markdown well enough for tibia's homepage: tables, ordered and nested lists, bold/italic, images, and READMEs in subfolders, until it reaches parity with GitHub's own rendering (user 11-01 14:0x: 'dir-tree-html readme.md renderer doesn't support tables, gotta use the tibia homepage as example to fix until it's sota')", Story, P1, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "dir-tree-html,pages,markdown,user-batch,main,team:main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_dir_tree_markdown_gfm () = Planning ("DIR-TREE-MARKDOWN-GFM", Minutes 180u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_dir_tree_markdown_gfm_1 () = Note ("DIR-TREE-MARKDOWN-GFM", 1u32, "2026-11-01T18:37Z", "main", "main 14:4x: markdown/markdown.spi:46-102 knows only paragraphs, -/* lists (nesting flattened), fenced code, # headings and ---; a | row renders as a paragraph. Only the top-level README.md is rendered (shared.spi:134-138). CI pins an older dir-tree-html commit a643950 (spiral/scripts/dir-tree-html.ps1) whose one-file renderer has the same subset, so the pin must move once this lands (TREE-PAGES-V2's pinned release). Cells' html.ex:168-218 has the same gap; share the fix (CELLS-HTML-PARITY). DoD: tibia's README renders on its Pages site with every table, list and emphasis matching GitHub's rendering of the same file (screenshot diff by eye plus test blocks per construct). Typefeatureset: Block = Paragraph | List of ordered phantom | Table of header * align list * rows | Code | Heading, with test blocks per constructor.")
inl note_of_dir_tree_markdown_gfm_2 () = Note ("DIR-TREE-MARKDOWN-GFM", 2u32, "2026-11-01T19:05Z", "main", "main 15:1x CODE WRITTEN, unstaged in dir-tree-html: markdown.spi gains GFM tables (header plus delimiter row, typed cell_align union -> text-align, \\| escape, cells inline-rendered), ordered lists (<ol start=N> keeps tibia's numbering), and list continuation lines (tibia's wrapped findings join their item instead of becoming paragraphs). List items now close lazily, so the old expected output is unchanged. inline.spi adds '|' to the escapable set. selftest's readme fixture covers each construct. Build and self-test are queued behind kit-TRAIN7c's heavy window (main's chain, pid 28832, after the pack15a install). Still open for parity: bold/italic inside table cells (inline already does **), nested list depth (still flattened), subfolder READMEs, and moving CI's pinned commit a643950 (TREE-PAGES-V2).")
inl note_of_dir_tree_markdown_gfm_3 () = Note ("DIR-TREE-MARKDOWN-GFM", 3u32, "2026-11-01T20:27Z", "main", "main 16:3x PROVEN on the box (build.ps1, self-test green): tables with alignment, ordered lists with start numbers, continuation lines and \\| all render. Smoke on tibia's real README.md: 4 <table>s, 17 <th>, its numbered findings as one <ol>. The 1k gate is met after two fixes: multi-use helpers became join points (:~ ^), because inlined non-recursive := helpers had doubled the output (markdown.rs 491 -> 1168 -> 889). Line counts now: markdown 889, inline 917, tree 848, viewer 913, selftest 935, dir_tree_html 642. Left: move CI's pinned dir-tree-html commit a643950 (spiral/scripts/dir-tree-html.ps1, TREE-PAGES-V2) to a commit containing this, after the user pushes dir-tree-html; until then Pages still renders with the old subset.")
inl note_of_dir_tree_markdown_gfm_4 () = Note ("DIR-TREE-MARKDOWN-GFM", 4u32, "2026-11-03T08:51Z", "main", "main 11-03 04:2x SUPERSEDED, closing. Its features (GFM tables with alignment, ordered lists with a start, continuation lines, escaped pipes, tibia's README) are in html, proven by HTML-MARKDOWN's tests and HTML-PARITY's identical pages, and dir-tree-html has left the org (11-03). Leftovers moved: (1) the CI pin move -> HTML-ADOPT-REPOS (the shim switches to html's release, so the pin goes away); (2) the open goals, READMEs in subfolders and parity with GitHub's own rendering on tibia's homepage -> HTML-MARKDOWN.")
inl cells_html_parity () = Task ("CELLS-HTML-PARITY", "Cells' static HTML catches up with the old dib pages and goes past them: error outputs and tracebacks, pages written for failed runs too, inline SVG/HTML/table/image outputs, per-cell duration, phases and per-target times, labelled output per backend (user 11-01 14:0x: 'our new erl based cells code gotta match the rich functionality that we had with the old dib runner')", Story, P1, D4, 55u32, Active, "NOTEBOOK-HTML-RENDERER", "cells,html,notebook,user-batch,x,team:x", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_cells_html_parity () = Planning ("CELLS-HTML-PARITY", Minutes 300u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_cells_html_parity_1 () = Note ("CELLS-HTML-PARITY", 1u32, "2026-11-01T18:37Z", "main", "main 14:4x, gaps from the parity research (old: polyglot core.ps1 Invoke-Dib, dotnet repl -> ipynb -> nbconvert dark theme, Plotting.dib inline SVG, one output block per backend). The ipynb already holds everything; html.ex renders only text/plain: (1) error outputs: notebook.ex:1313 stores output_type error, html.ex:75-77 drops it; (2) an HTML page is written only when every test passes (notebook.ex:418-423), so failed runs, the ones that matter, have no page; (3) display.ex:50-61 produces image/svg+xml and display.ex:17 allows any MIME type, but html.ex:77 drops them; (4) duration_ms, phases_ms and per-target times sit in metadata (notebook.ex:1398-1407) and aren't shown; (5) no labelled output per backend; (6) markdown cells lack tables, ordered lists, bold and images (shared with DIR-TREE-MARKDOWN-GFM). Already better than the old pages: semantic Spiral coloring from the compiler tokens verb, and pass/fail badges. DoD: lib/spiral/physics.spi's page shows its plots inline like the 706e6beb~1 physics.dib.html reference (82 SVGs); a deliberately failing sample's page shows the error; every cell shows its time and per-backend status. Typefeatureset: Output = Text | Error of trace | Svg | Html | Table | Image as a GADT rendered by one exhaustive match (CELLS-PLOT-EXPORT's output union).")
inl note_of_cells_html_parity_2 () = Note ("CELLS-HTML-PARITY", 2u32, "2026-11-02T12:24Z", "x", "2026-11-02 08:24 x: GROOM: this item also owns porting html.ex (231 Elixir lines) for CELLS-IN-SPIRAL DoD 1, which lists it as a dep. The parity work and the port are one slice: the typed renderer replaces html.ex, not a second Elixir pass.")
inl note_of_cells_html_parity_3 () = Note ("CELLS-HTML-PARITY", 3u32, "2026-11-02T22:31Z", "x", "2026-11-02 18:31 x: markdown renderer port (phase A, html_markdown.spi exported as markdown_html; drafts in kit drafts spiral\\CELLS-HTML-MD; phase B html.ex 231 -> 181 drafted in \\CELLS-HTML-MD-B, Html.markdown delegates). x-cells-md1 VOID: the blocks passed 1/1 and html_test passed, but the corpus walk Path.wildcard('*/**/*.md') from the mirror root crawled every deps and _build tree and timed out at 30 min before comparing anything. x-cells-mdjson1 VOID for markdown: the doc-text Path.wildcard brace pattern raised badpattern missing_delimiter on the mirror path. Fix: the corpus is git ls-files per repo (*.md plus the /// doc text of tracked lib and apps spi), which is what 'tracked' means. Rule unchanged: blocks 1/1, parity over > 100 texts with 0 differ, html_test 0 failures. Rerun inside x-cells-mdjson2.")
inl note_of_cells_html_parity_4 () = Note ("CELLS-HTML-PARITY", 4u32, "2026-11-02T23:06Z", "x", "2026-11-02 19:06 x: markdown phase A LANDED with x-cells-mdjson3 (see CELLS-PORT-TARGETS for the files and hashes): html_markdown.spi 372121A33A3C exported as markdown_html, blocks 1/1, parity 111 texts 0 differ, html_test green. Phase B rides x-cells-pb1 (Html.markdown delegates, html.ex 231 -> 181).")
inl note_of_cells_html_parity_5 () = Note ("CELLS-HTML-PARITY", 5u32, "2026-11-03T00:12Z", "x", "2026-11-02 20:12 x: markdown phase B LANDED (unstaged): html.ex 231 -> 181 (E6BC328C6B43). Html.markdown delegates to Domain.markdown_html; markdown_line, markdown_heading, markdown_item, close and inline are deleted, and escape and join stay (used by the notebook page). PROOF x-cells-pb1: html_test and the notebook report suite gave A 67/68 = B 67/68, the one failure an environment Gleam test unrelated to html. html_markdown_parity_test.exs retired (archived in 20261102-200215-cells-retired-parity-tests). The renderer is Spiral now; this item's remaining scope is the dib-parity features in its title.")
inl note_of_cells_html_parity_6 () = Note ("CELLS-HTML-PARITY", 6u32, "2026-11-03T00:28Z", "x", "2026-11-02 20:28 x: DRIFT WATCH (one record per fact): HTML-MODEL (main, html repo) has a typed markdown module (markdown 9 tests, an Inline/Block/Page model, and markup as the only HTML-producing nominal). Cells now has its own html_markdown.spi, a byte-parity port of the old Elixir renderer. Two markdown renderers is a drift risk. Decision rule for this item's next slice: when Cells' page grows past the old renderer's subset (headings, paragraphs, items, fenced code, code spans, links), build it on the html repo's model, linked as a package once that repo is consumable from Cells, and retire html_markdown.spi. Don't extend html_markdown.spi with new syntax. Until then, html_markdown.spi stays frozen at Elixir parity.")
inl note_of_cells_html_parity_7 () = Note ("CELLS-HTML-PARITY", 7u32, "2026-11-03T07:36Z", "x", "2026-11-03 03:36 x: DECIDED by main's 11-03 order 7 ('one markdown/render path in html for every project that reimplements it'), replacing n6's 'when the page outgrows' rule. Cells' html_markdown.spi (landed 11-02 as a byte-parity port of the old Elixir renderer) is such a reimplementation, so it MOVES onto the html repo's typed renderer (html/src markdown.spi, inline_parse.spi, render.spi and model.spi; markup is the only HTML-producing nominal). PLAN: (1) Cells links the html package (packageDir/link like the client), and Domain.markdown_html calls html's markdown -> render; (2) parity run, Cells' current output vs html's over the same 111-text corpus. Each difference is judged by RULE: html's output is canonical unless it drops content Cells shows (then the gap goes to an html item), and each accepted difference is listed; (3) html_markdown.spi is deleted after a hash check that its tests are covered by html's markdown tests; (4) html_test and the notebook report suite run A/B. x's own repo has no markdown renderer in Spiral (checked: no x/apps .spi renders markdown), so 'x README/tutorial through html' means this Cells path. Owner x, status Active, next step (1).")
inl note_of_cells_html_parity_8 () = Note ("CELLS-HTML-PARITY", 8u32, "2026-11-03T07:54Z", "x", "2026-11-03 03:54 x: PRECONDITION found for n7 step (1). html/src's blocks are all '///> rust' (37), and html/markdown exports a Rust lib, while Cells' domain is Gleam. So Cells can link html/src only after those modules (model, inline_parse, markdown, render) are proven on gleam too. Asked main (owner of HTML-MODEL) whether x adds the gleam targets via drafts or lands them itself. Fallback if html/src must stay Rust-only: a Gleam build of html's markdown entry as its own package. Next step: main's answer, then the node proof of html/src on gleam.")
inl note_of_cells_html_parity_9 () = Note ("CELLS-HTML-PARITY", 9u32, "2026-11-03T08:00Z", "x", "2026-11-03 03:59 x: PLAN AGREED with main (11-03 03:4x; html/src is open to x, no drafts handoff). STATE: not started; waits for the user to start the next autonomous run. EXACT NEXT STEPS: (1) add '///> gleam' beside '///> rust' on html/src's markdown, render, model and inline_parse blocks; prove them on the node with both targets and identical outputs; land them in html/src directly, telling main first if main is editing that file; keep html/src portable, never Rust-only. (2) Cells links html/src (packageDir), and Domain.markdown_html calls html's md. (3) Parity over Cells' 111-text corpus, with html's output canonical and accepted differences listed (n7 rule). (4) Delete Cells' html_markdown.spi after its tests are covered by html's. (5) html_test and the notebook report suite run A/B.")
inl note_of_cells_html_parity_10 () = Note ("CELLS-HTML-PARITY", 10u32, "2026-11-03T09:09Z", "x", "2026-11-03 05:09 x: STEP 1 DRAFTED (kit drafts html\\CELLS-ON-HTML): model, inline_parse, markdown and render get '///> gleam' beside each '///> rust' (23 blocks; bases model 238DDC32FDA2, inline_parse 7BB8B4C7F262, markdown F47CD05FB1A0, render F55AD0632A25; html/src was last edited 00:07-00:41, so main isn't editing it now). All four are pure Spiral (no host macros, backend switches or globals), so any Gleam difference comes from lib string semantics. PRE-REGISTERED RULE (x-html-gleam1, launched after x-census-cli4 frees the node, because parallel Cells runs share %TEMP%\\spiral_cells): run_notebook --gate over the 4 files on mirror xhtml1 (html with deps/spiral junctioned to the mirror's spiral, junction removed after), with a pinned compiler copy. Pass = exit 0 and every spiral-tests file line shows failed 0, executed = discovered, empty failed_targets and empty mismatches (rust and gleam agree on every block). Then x lands the 4 files directly in html/src (main's 03:4x go-ahead).")
inl note_of_cells_html_parity_11 () = Note ("CELLS-HTML-PARITY", 11u32, "2026-11-03T09:17Z", "x", "2026-11-03 05:17 x: STEP 2 phase A DRAFTED (kit drafts spiral\\CELLS-ON-HTML; bases package 8E3379568FE5, main AE2FCBD33CDF, domain BBBCE6E60BB1). Cells' package links ../../../../html/src- (Cells has no spiral.toml, checked). main.spi exports html_md = render::md next to the old markdown_html; main opens nothing, so json_value's local 'render' function can't shadow the html render module. domain.ex gains html_md, and html_md_parity_test.exs is new. RULE, set before it runs (after x-html-gleam1 is green and landed): over the same corpus as the old markdown parity (tracked *.md plus lib and apps /// doc text, > 100 texts), html's output must keep EVERY word the Cells renderer showed (tags stripped, entities decoded; 0 texts lose words). Identical and markup-only counts are printed, and markup differences are accepted (html is canonical per n7). Phase B then: markdown_html exports render::md, html_markdown.spi is deleted (its single test is covered by html's markdown tests), and html_test and notebook_report run A/B. Node mirror note: the Cells run needs html beside spiral and html/deps/spiral junctioned, as in launch-html-gleam.")
inl note_of_cells_html_parity_12 () = Note ("CELLS-HTML-PARITY", 12u32, "2026-11-03T09:51Z", "x", "2026-11-03 05:51 x: PLAN REVISED by main's reuse decision (HTML-LIB-CANDIDATES n2: HTML escaping is implemented 5 times and markdown parsing 3 times across repos). The markdown model and parser move to lib/spiral/markdown.spi and the markup core to lib/spiral/markup.spi, after html's unit split (HTML-CI-RELEASE); html keeps tree, pages and the CLI. STEP 1 stands and is x's: '///> gleam' beside '///> rust' on html/src markdown, inline_parse, render and model, proven on the node with identical outputs (x-html-gleam1, queued behind x-census-cli5), so the lib module is portable when it moves. STEP 2 CHANGES: Cells depends on lib markdown, NOT ../../../../html/src. The kit drafts spiral\\CELLS-ON-HTML link drafts (package.spiproj, main.spi, domain.ex) are SUPERSEDED. html_md_parity_test.exs is kept as the 111-text content check, pointed at the lib export when it exists. html_markdown.spi is NOT deleted until lib markdown exists and that check passes. Waits on: x-html-gleam1, then main's lib move.")
inl note_of_cells_html_parity_13 () = Note ("CELLS-HTML-PARITY", 13u32, "2026-11-03T09:54Z", "x", "2026-11-03 05:54 x: PLAN RESTORED by main's correction (compiler's lu9 probe, HTML-CI-RELEASE n9/n10: lib-defined types can't cross linked units). The markdown model and parser STAY in html/src; only escape and the markup string builders go to lib/spiral/markup.spi. So n11's step 2 stands as drafted: Cells links ../../../../html/src- as a package (Gleam inlines it; no link: unit). The kit drafts spiral\\CELLS-ON-HTML package.spiproj, main.spi (html_md = render::md) and domain.ex are re-derived from unchanged bases (8E3379568FE5, AE2FCBD33CDF, BBBCE6E60BB1), and html_md_parity_test.exs is kept. Order: x-html-gleam1 (step 1), then the step 2 phase A parity, then phase B (delete html_markdown.spi).")
inl note_of_cells_html_parity_14 () = Note ("CELLS-HTML-PARITY", 14u32, "2026-11-03T10:08Z", "x", "2026-11-03 06:08 x: STEP 1 SPLIT, coordinating with main (main is editing html/src/markdown.spi for ~40 min on a GFM inline-code continuation bug; x leaves that file alone until main says 'markdown.spi free'). x-html-gleam1 now covers model, inline_parse and render only (bases 238DDC32FDA2, 7BB8B4C7F262, F55AD0632A25). Because render opens markdown, the mirror run restores src/markdown.spi from git HEAD first, so main's in-progress live edit can't leak into the proof. Same pass rule as n10, over 3 files. markdown.spi's gleam targets follow as x-html-gleam2, re-derived from main's new markdown.spi once it's free.")
inl note_of_cells_html_parity_15 () = Note ("CELLS-HTML-PARITY", 15u32, "2026-11-03T10:23Z", "x", "2026-11-03 06:23 x: HELD for main's html unit split. pack24 (LINKED-UNITS-SHARED-INSTANCES) is installed, so main moves model, inline_parse, markdown and render into linked-unit packages (html_model, html_parse, html_render), about 1-2 h. x had nothing in the live tree (drafts only, x-html-gleam1 not launched), so main splits first. RESUME CONDITION: main's message 'html split landed' with the new paths. Then x re-derives the '///> gleam' lines on the moved files, proves them on the current core, re-points the Cells package link to the new packages, and runs the 111-text check. The drafts in kit drafts html\\CELLS-ON-HTML and spiral\\CELLS-ON-HTML are re-derived then, not reused as-is.")
inl note_of_cells_html_parity_16 () = Note ("CELLS-HTML-PARITY", 16u32, "2026-11-03T12:19Z", "x", "2026-11-03 08:19 x: RESUMED, main sent 'html split landed' (box build.ps1 green: 49/49 notebooks). Re-derived the gleam targets on the new unit files, mechanically: one '///> gleam' after every '///> rust' and nothing else, LF kept. Drafts in kit drafts html\\CELLS-ON-HTML\\<unit>\\: html_model/model.spi 4C3D7D8E0EE8 -> 9D24AA3EB4EC (5 targets), html_inline/inline_parse.spi 150E7022EF02 -> 024661DAFB36 (7), html_table/table.spi A5A12954FF5C -> AE8000C885C4 (2), html_parse/markdown.spi 5F8086EF483E -> E817DC69AB83 (10), html_render/render.spi 7FE9E26D9B22 -> 38B4EE00BE33 (10). The stale flat drafts on src/ bases are deleted (src/ is gone). Launcher guards the five recorded bases. PRE-REGISTERED RULE for x-html-gleam2 (node, pinned core 21D5992EDB80D270 at both ends): run_notebook --gate over the 5 files exits 0, and every 'spiral-tests file' line plus the total show 0 failed with the gleam target present. It is queued after x-sim-maps1 (shared %TEMP%\\spiral_cells). ON GREEN: land the 5 files in a tree-write window, then re-point Cells' package list to ../../../html/html_model, html_inline, html_table, html_parse and html_render (Gleam inlines, so no link:), then run the 111-text no-words-lost check, then delete Cells' html_markdown.spi.")
inl note_of_cells_html_parity_17 () = Note ("CELLS-HTML-PARITY", 17u32, "2026-11-03T12:23Z", "x", "2026-11-03 08:23 x: ONE JOB for both steps, saving a sync. x-html-gleam2 also stages the Cells link drafts, re-derived on live bases: spi/package.spiproj 8E3379568FE5 -> 2D52E265AE30 (html/src- replaced by ../../../../html/html_model-, html_inline-, html_table-, html_parse-, html_render-), and main.spi AE2FCBD33CDF + domain.ex BBBCE6E60BB1 + test html_md_parity_test.exs carried over unchanged (additions only: export html_md = render::md). RULE amended from n16: (a) the 5-file gate as n16, AND (b) mix test html_md_parity_test.exs + html_test.exs exits 0 with private MIX_BUILD_PATH, the parity line prints over 100 texts and 0 lose words. Either failing = not green. Green lands html files and Cells files in separate tree-write windows; html_markdown.spi deletion is a follow-up with its own Cells suite run.")
inl note_of_cells_html_parity_18 () = Note ("CELLS-HTML-PARITY", 18u32, "2026-11-03T12:33Z", "x", "2026-11-03 08:33 x: LAND PRECONDITION FOUND (cross-repo dependency). Once Cells' spi/package.spiproj lists ../../../../html/html_*, every Cells build needs (1) a sibling html checkout and (2) html/deps/spiral, which is untracked (deps/ is gitignored) and made by html's scripts/init.ps1 as a link to ../../spiral. The box has it; node mirrors get it from the launchers; but spiral CI's cells-gate job (gh-pages.yml:104, schedule/dispatch, the only CI job that runs Cells) checks out spiral alone, so it would fail. RULE (my technical call, recorded before acting): the Cells-link land also adds one run step to cells-gate before ci_gate.ps1: clone public i574n/html --depth 1 into ../html, then link ../html/deps/spiral to the workspace. No new action, so actions.lock is unchanged. ORDER for the user's push (git stays theirs): html's unit split goes first, because CI clones html main; spiral's Cells link second. The 21:00 report will call this out.")
inl note_of_cells_html_parity_19 () = Note ("CELLS-HTML-PARITY", 19u32, "2026-11-03T13:21Z", "x", "2026-11-03 09:21 x: x-html-gleam2's first launch REFUSED itself (exit 3, nothing sent): all five html unit files changed after main's 'split landed' (live 05A634F24CDB model, 86C7C3CD5574 inline_parse, A4C34071C763 table, 2453749FEC40 markdown, CB4DDDAC4A5B render; main's edits, 20-25 min old, still no gleam lines). The draft is a pure function of live (one '///> gleam' after each '///> rust'), so it was re-derived on those bases (gleam 5/7/2/10/10) and relaunched under the same n16/n17 rule. The Cells-side bases were unchanged.")
inl note_of_cells_html_parity_20 () = Note ("CELLS-HTML-PARITY", 20u32, "2026-11-03T13:35Z", "x", "2026-11-03 09:35 x: x-html-gleam2 NOT GREEN (job-1793712096557, 4 min): both halves stopped at the same place, compiling Cells' domain.gleam with the html units linked in (run_notebook builds Cells too). Gleam 'Type mismatch' at domain.gleam:25165: html_parse markdown.spi digit_value calls lib sm'.char_code, which has NO Gleam branch, so the compiler emitted 'let v2 = Nil // backend.backend_switch / record_type_try_find / key: Gleam' and wrapped it as i64. It is the only such site in the 38,019-line file. FIX (draft kit drafts spiral\\CELLS-ON-HTML\\sm'.spi on base 7C5111CA557E): char_code gains Gleam = string.to_utf_codepoints, then utf_codepoint_to_int of the first (import gleam/string, like starts_with), plus a ///> gleam block (A 65, 7 55, é 233, € 8364, matching the Lua block). Compiler gap reported to compiler: a backend_switch with no branch for the target should be a compile error, not a silent Nil. RULE for x-html-gleam3 = n16 + n17 unchanged; sm'.spi is staged but not gated whole (no block filter, 2,300 lines over many backends); its new branch is proven by markdown's gleam ordered-list tests and the Cells build, and its block by CI cells-gate over lib/spiral.")
inl note_of_cells_html_parity_21 () = Note ("CELLS-HTML-PARITY", 21u32, "2026-11-03T13:52Z", "x", "2026-11-03 09:52 x: x-html-gleam3 NOT GREEN, same error at the same line, because Cells NEVER REGENERATED domain.gleam: its mtime was 09:24 (gleam2), the job started 09:43. ROOT CAUSE, a real Cells staleness bug: spi/domain_compiler.ex inputs_digest hashes only Cells' own spi/*.spi + package.spiproj, so a change in lib/spiral (here sm'.spi) or in any linked package (the html units) leaves the stamp equal and the old domain.gleam is reused. With html linked, every html edit would hit it. FIX drafted (kit drafts spiral\\CELLS-ON-HTML\\domain_compiler.ex, base 730499FEFF4F): the digest also covers lib/spiral/*.spi|*.spiproj and every relative '..' package listed in package.spiproj (the five html units), sanity-checked with elixir -e against the draft spiproj. It proves itself on the node: the new digest differs from gleam2's stamp, so domain.gleam regenerates with the sm' Gleam branch. Relaunched as x-html-gleam4; rule n16+n17 unchanged, plus 'domain.gleam regenerated' (no Nil backend_switch site).")
inl note_of_cells_html_parity_22 () = Note ("CELLS-HTML-PARITY", 22u32, "2026-11-03T14:18Z", "x", "2026-11-03 10:18 x: x-html-gleam4 NOT GREEN but the digest fix WORKED: domain.gleam regenerated (10:03, after the job start). It still had the Nil site and no to_utf_codepoints, because the html units resolved lib/spiral through the node mirror's html\\deps\\spiral, a 06:12 junction to the NODE's own C:\\home\\git\\spiral (old sm'), not the mirror's spiral where the fix was staged. The launcher kept any existing junction. With two 'spiral-' packages from two dirs, the compile silently used the html side's. FIX (launcher): always point deps\\spiral at the mirror's spiral for the run, print it, then restore the previous target. Same fix applied to launch-cells-mpath. Relaunched as x-html-gleam5. LAND NOTE: on the box html\\deps\\spiral -> C:\\home\\git\\spiral, the same tree as Cells' lib, so the landed link is consistent. Compiler (BACKEND-SWITCH-MISSING-ARM) now turns a missing Gleam arm into a compile-time FatalError, so char_code's Gleam branch is required, not optional.")
inl note_of_cells_html_parity_23 () = Note ("CELLS-HTML-PARITY", 23u32, "2026-11-03T15:00Z", "x", "2026-11-03 11:00 x: x-html-gleam5 (job-1793715425398, core 21D5992EDB80D270 at both ends, deps\\spiral forced to the mirror). (a) GREEN: run_notebook --gate over the 5 html units passed 34/34 (model 5, inline_parse 7, table 2, markdown 10, render 10), every block on both targets, 0 failed. LANDED unstaged in one tree-write window, hash-guarded: html model 61F9EE595DFC, inline_parse 6D6441DC13F2, table 2ABEE99FB256, markdown 7E617F36F7CA, render C4E9EE868198 (gleam lines only); spiral lib sm'.spi 25191F00112C -> ECDE2F868B0A (char_code Gleam arm + gleam block, re-applied in-window onto live because sm' moved twice under other lanes; the hunks are independent); Cells spi/domain_compiler.ex 730499FEFF4F -> 5F49EDA9CCA1 (a standalone fix, proven by gleam4/gleam5 regenerating after a lib change). main told to re-read the html files. (b) NOT GREEN by the n17 rule: Cells built with html linked and html_test passed, but parity was '120 texts, 106 identical, 3 markup-only differences, 11 lose words'. The first loss is the old renderer leaking raw syntax as words (<https://..> autolinks, ---, '](url)' fragments, '![' badge text) plus image alt text, which moves into an attribute the word compare strips. PRE-REGISTERED v2 RULE (before seeing the other 10): test draft 0D370EC83183 normalizes tokens symmetrically (trim <>[]()! and a leading '](', drop pure-syntax tokens like - --- #), counts alt and href values as words, and prints EVERY raw loss and every normalized loss. Pass = 0 normalized word losses over > 100 texts. Any normalized loss is a real html bug to fix, not a test tweak.")
inl note_of_cells_html_parity_24 () = Note ("CELLS-HTML-PARITY", 24u32, "2026-11-03T16:35Z", "x", "2026-11-03 12:35 x: CORRECTION to n18's CI step. Per main's ORG-EXIT-FALLOUT 06:4x note, workflow YAML is GENERATED from the typed source i574n.github/scripts/pins/workflows.spi and checked by pins.ps1 (lanes hand-editing YAML were flagged). So the html-beside step is now drafted THERE (kit drafts spiral\\CELLS-ON-HTML\\workflows.spi, base 6BA6BEAB608D -> AD7EBEEC08CE): a new ':~ html_beside () : step' (named run step: clone public i574n/html --depth 1 into ../html, mkdir ../html/deps, symlink deps/spiral to the workspace), modeled on transformer_model_cache, inserted in spiral_cells_gate_job before the ci_gate.ps1 run. The hand-edited gh-pages.yml draft is DELETED (my own draft, superseded). At land: the workflows.spi edit plus pins.ps1 regenerating spiral's gh-pages.yml, so source and YAML agree; the push order stays html first, spiral second.")
inl note_of_cells_html_parity_25 () = Note ("CELLS-HTML-PARITY", 25u32, "2026-11-03T16:37Z", "x", "2026-11-03 12:37 x: LOST HUNK. Live lib sm'.spi was rewritten at 10:51:01 back to 25191F00112C (kebab_of kept), dropping my 10:3x merge (ECDE2F868B0A: char_code's Gleam arm and gleam block). The html files and domain_compiler.ex from the same window survived. Likely another lane's land restoring a pre-merge copy without a base guard. EFFECT: html markdown's gleam blocks cannot compile until the arm returns (compiler's BACKEND-SWITCH-MISSING-ARM now names the missing arm at compile time). PLAN agreed with compiler: their C/Zig/Pascal char_code land (job compiler-lib-land-charcode on 25191F00) closes first, then x re-applies the Gleam arm and block on their hash, in-window and anchor-checked.")
inl note_of_cells_html_parity_26 () = Note ("CELLS-HTML-PARITY", 26u32, "2026-11-03T16:58Z", "x", "2026-11-03 12:58 x: ROOT CAUSE of n25 (found by main): not a direct edit. kit's first LIB-KEBAB-CASE land snapshotted sm'.spi at 10:30:17 (undo 20261103-103017, DA64EBB2), before x's 10:38 arm. Its close failed on tibia's file_system break (~10:42), and Restore-Undo copied that snapshot back, dropping the arm; kit's second land (undo 104911) then added kebab_of, giving 25191F00. main fixed Restore-Undo (unstaged: it skips any file whose live hash differs from what that land wrote); the two-windows-at-once cause went to kit. x's side: the html launcher now refuses to run until live sm' has the arm (precondition exit 6), and the re-land waits for compiler's charcode land as agreed.")
inl note_of_cells_html_parity_27 () = Note ("CELLS-HTML-PARITY", 27u32, "2026-11-03T17:04Z", "x", "2026-11-03 13:04 x: RE-LANDED the lost hunk on compiler's base: sm'.spi 5DCCAED9654570E9 -> 343FA552B65A03D7 in a tree-write window (land-sm-gleam.ps1: base-guarded, both anchors matched once). char_code's Gleam arm now sits after compiler's new C/Zig/Pascal arms, and the gleam block sits after of_char's gleam block. Spacing canonical (no double blank, single final LF). The html launcher precondition (exit 6) now passes; x-html-gleam6 is clear to run when the node queue reaches it.")
inl note_of_cells_html_parity_28 () = Note ("CELLS-HTML-PARITY", 28u32, "2026-11-05T13:36Z", "x", "2026-11-05 10:4x x: RESUMED (last note 11-03 13:04; x-html-gleam6 never ran). STATE CHECKED on live: step 1 HOLDS (every html unit block carries ///> gleam beside ///> rust: model 2/2, inline_parse 7/7, table 2/2, markdown 10/10, render 10/10; sm' char_code's Gleam arm present; domain_compiler.ex's digest fix landed 11-03). The html-side drafts in kit drafts html\\CELLS-ON-HTML are leftovers of that land (stale against main's later html edits; nothing to land). STEP 2 (Cells links html) RE-DERIVED on today's live bases, additions only, in kit drafts spiral\\CELLS-ON-HTML-V2: spi/main.spi FEA05BF571A2 -> BCB5FAA7D396 (export html_md = render::md, after markdown_html); lib/spiral/cells/domain.ex 7C588CAFE420 -> 2741CCA8542A (def html_md); spi/package.spiproj 8E3379568FE5 -> 2D52E265AE30 (base unchanged: links ../../../../html/html_model-, html_inline-, html_table-, html_parse-, html_render-); test html_md_parity_test.exs 0D370EC83183 (the v2 rule of n20, unchanged). Launcher launch-cells-html-link (mirror xhtml1, repos html + spiral, deps\\spiral forced to the mirror's spiral, pinned compiler): (a) run_notebook --gate over the 5 live html units, both targets; (b) mix test html_md_parity_test.exs + html_test.exs. RULE unchanged (n16, n17, n20 v2): (a) exit 0 with 0 failed in every file line; (b) exit 0 and 0 normalized word losses over > 100 texts. Any normalized loss is a real html bug. QUEUED after x-next9b and x-next10, because node Cells runs share %TEMP%\\spiral_cells.")
inl note_of_cells_html_parity_29 () = Note ("CELLS-HTML-PARITY", 29u32, "2026-11-05T22:36Z", "x", "2026-11-05 18:3x x: LAUNCHED x-cells-html1 (job-1793918120731, node, mirror xhtml1, repos html + spiral, pinned pack52 core A688EE5AD426A5A0). Cells-side drafts CELLS-ON-HTML-V2, bases verified live at launch: main.spi FEA05BF571A2, domain.ex 7C588CAFE420, package.spiproj 8E3379568FE5; sm' char_code Gleam arm present. Note: Cells now also carries today's import-order fix (dependencies before the notebook's own package). Pre-registered rule unchanged (n28): (a) run_notebook --gate over the 5 live html units, both targets, 0 failed; (b) mix test html_md_parity_test.exs + html_test.exs exit 0 with 0 normalized word losses over > 100 texts. It runs concurrently with x-next10's successor x-next11 (other mirror). A shared-%TEMP% flake would show as a runtime red in both, so any red gets a rerun before it counts. PENDING.")
inl note_of_cells_html_parity_30 () = Note ("CELLS-HTML-PARITY", 30u32, "2026-11-05T23:28Z", "x", "2026-11-05 19:3x x: x-cells-html1 RESULT (job-1793918120731, node, mirror xhtml1, pinned pack52 core A688EE5AD426A5A0), RED by the pre-registered rule, NOT landed. (a) GREEN: run_notebook --gate over the 5 live html units, both targets: model 2/2, inline_parse 7/7, table 2/2, markdown 10/10, render 10/10 = 31/31, so Cells builds with html linked and the html Gleam path holds. (b) RED: html_md_parity_test (v2 rule 0D370EC83183) over 127 texts: 115 identical, 9 raw-token losses (normalized away, accepted), 7 TEXTS LOSE WORDS after normalization. html_test passed (7/8 total; the parity test is the 1 failure). First losing text: the spiral README head ('# spiral', '<https://i574n.github.io/spiral>' autolink, '[![badge alt](badge.svg)](url)' image-in-link, '---', a nested '- [Getting Started](#...)' list). By the n20 rule each normalized loss is a real html renderer bug to fix in html, not a test tweak. NEXT: list all 7 losing texts with their lost words (run the parity test locally or print every loss) and file them on the html side (owner main / HTML-REPO), then rerun this exact job (launch-cells-html-link, drafts CELLS-ON-HTML-V2 unchanged).")
inl pages_notebook_publish () = Task ("PAGES-NOTEBOOK-PUBLISH", "Publish each .spi's Cells notebook pages (.html/.ipynb/.livemd, test outputs included) on every repo's Pages site, and link each .spi in the tree to its page (user 11-01 14:0x: 'where's the .html files generated for each spi file in gh actions, including the outputs of tests cells etc?')", Story, P1, D3, 80u32, Planned, "-", "pages,ci,cells,dir-tree-html,all-repos,user-batch,main,team:main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_pages_notebook_publish () = Planning ("PAGES-NOTEBOOK-PUBLISH", Minutes 180u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_pages_notebook_publish_1 () = Note ("PAGES-NOTEBOOK-PUBLISH", 1u32, "2026-11-01T18:38Z", "main", "main 14:4x: the old gitlab-i574n Pages branch (a28af39c) had 47 .dib.html and 43 .dib.ipynb; the live spiral gh-pages (3464d8e9, 11-01) has none. Cause: spiral's CI builds lib/spiral/*.spi.ipynb/.html/.livemd, but publish.ps1 calls publish-tree.ps1 without -Include, which copies only tracked files, and the notebook outputs are untracked. Tibia writes notebooks/ (gitignored) and only uploads it as an artifact; fiscal publishes only data. This unblocks NOTEBOOK-LIVEMD-EXPORT. DoD: on spiral's, tibia's and fiscal's deployed sites every .spi with tests has a notebook page reachable from its tree entry, and the page shows test outputs (failed runs too, after CELLS-HTML-PARITY). Generated pages stay out of git (no committed outputs). Typefeatureset: publish-tree's include set is a typed list of generated-artifact kinds (NotebookHtml | Ipynb | Livemd), not glob strings per repo.")
inl note_of_pages_notebook_publish_2 () = Note ("PAGES-NOTEBOOK-PUBLISH", 2u32, "2026-11-01T20:13Z", "main", "main 16:1x LANDED unstaged in spiral/scripts: publish.ps1 now calls publish-tree with -Include '*.spi.html,*.spi.ipynb,*.spi.livemd' -Exclude 'target,_build,node_modules'. These are the files lib/spiral/build.ps1:31 writes next to each notebook in CI. publish-tree.ps1 splits comma lists, because 'pwsh script.ps1 -Include a,b' doesn't pass arrays (memory ps-file-array-args); the 11 other repos' callers use & with arrays or no -Include, so they're unaffected. Dry selection on the box: 0 extras (notebooks are generated only in CI; the 4 under target/ are excluded). In dir-tree-html, write_viewers now skips a file whose x.ext.html already exists, so a Cells notebook page is never overwritten by a plain viewer, and with --viewers a .spi links to its notebook page. PROOF PENDING: spiral's first CI run after the user's push must show lib/spiral/<nb>.spi.html entries on the Pages tree (main checks per verify-ci-after-user-push). Next: tibia (notebooks/ is gitignored and only uploaded as an artifact) and fiscal get the same -Include once proven; HTML is written only when every test passes, until CELLS-HTML-PARITY.")
inl note_of_pages_notebook_publish_3 () = Note ("PAGES-NOTEBOOK-PUBLISH", 3u32, "2026-11-02T00:12Z", "main", "main 20:1x PROVEN ON CI: spiral's first push with the change (b3fd292b, run 41359205750, green on both OSes) deployed gh-pages df762c5c at 19:55 local with 96 notebook files under lib/spiral (and apps/wasm): .spi.html, .spi.ipynb and .spi.livemd, e.g. lib/spiral/am'.spi.html, async.spi.html, base.spi.html. The old gitlab Pages had 47 .dib.html; we're past parity on count. Left: (1) tibia and fiscal adopt the same -Include (tibia writes notebooks/ and only uploads it as an artifact today); (2) the tree page links each .spi to its notebook page (needs the --viewers renderer, i.e. the pin move or the new html repo); (3) failed-run pages (CELLS-HTML-PARITY: HTML is written only when every test passes).")
inl note_of_pages_notebook_publish_4 () = Note ("PAGES-NOTEBOOK-PUBLISH", 4u32, "2026-11-02T00:22Z", "tibia-lane", "11-01 20:2x tibia: DONE on tibia's side (unstaged): scripts/publish.ps1's publish-tree call now includes '*.spi.html', '*.spi.ipynb', '*.spi.livemd' beside '*.tsv', '*.txt', '*.lua', '*.xml'. Checked before editing: (1) gh-pages.yml runs publish.ps1 on the ubuntu job after both build steps, so notebooks/ is populated by then; (2) publish-tree collects extras with 'git ls-files --others' WITHOUT --exclude-standard, so the gitignored notebooks/ files are listed (the same path spiral's run 41359205750 used); (3) unlike spiral's call, NO '-Exclude target': tibia's site publishes the compiled arena Lua from target/arena/*.lua, and excluding target would drop those pages. Parse check clean. Verifiable on the first tibia Pages build after the user's next push: notebooks/*.spi.html pages on the site.")
inl note_of_pages_notebook_publish_5 () = Note ("PAGES-NOTEBOOK-PUBLISH", 5u32, "2026-11-05T22:42Z", "main", "main 11-05 18:4x CHECK of left item (1): tibia adopted (n of 11-01 20:2x). fiscal has NOT: fiscal/scripts/publish.ps1:3 -Include lists '*.xml','*.txt','*.tsv','*.json','reports.md' only, while its gitignored notebooks/ holds corpus.spi.html/.ipynb etc. Asked fiscal to add the three notebook patterns (or let main do it unstaged). (2) tree-page links still wait on the renderer (HTML-CI-RELEASE, user); (3) failed-run pages unchanged (CELLS-HTML-PARITY).")
inl note_of_pages_notebook_publish_6 () = Note ("PAGES-NOTEBOOK-PUBLISH", 6u32, "2026-11-05T23:00Z", "main", "main 11-05 19:0x: fiscal DONE (unstaged): scripts/publish.ps1 -Include adds '*.spi.html','*.spi.ipynb','*.spi.livemd', like tibia; gh-pages.yml already runs publish.ps1 after build.ps1 writes notebooks/ (19 html + 19 ipynb today). Unverified until fiscal's next gh-pages run. Left (1) is done for both consumers; (2) tree-page links still wait on the renderer (HTML-CI-RELEASE), (3) failed-run pages on CELLS-HTML-PARITY.")
inl x_dumps () = Task ("X-DUMPS", "x-dumps: complete linear X/Twitter histories (tweets, replies, quotes, RTs, media) for the user's accounts via public archives, deterministic cached pipeline (xarchive) on the Vultr VM", Feature, P1, D5, 90u32, Active, "", "x-dumps,team:x-dumps,xarchive,vultr,wayback", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_x_dumps () = Planning ("X-DUMPS", Minutes 2400u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_x_dumps_1 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 1u32, "2026-11-01T18:39Z", "personal", "personal lane, 2026-11-01 14:39 -04. User: complete, linear history (tweets, replies, quotes, RTs, media) of geygey_hadid, juhondacivic, i574n, fc1943s, stewshka; deterministic re-runnable pipeline with cache strategies; more accounts later; iterate for days on every source. Line held: no bypassing access controls or rate limits/bot checks.\nSources measured 11-01: the Archive Team stream grab is access-restricted (401, torrent too). SN13 HF names are Fernet-encrypted. enryu43 (88M tweets, max 1,001 per user, to 2023-04) and Common Crawl have none of the 5. Wayback CDX: geygey_hadid 39,265 status IDs (2020-2022, captured as posted), fc1943s 625, the other 3 only profile pages. X embed endpoint cdn.syndication.twimg.com/tweet-result works; geygey tweets are tombstoned, so Wayback is their only copy. fc1943s and i574n are ONE account (id 72784485, renamed).\nPipeline: Rust crate C:\\home\\git\\xarchive (stages cdx|profile|snaps|live|media|timeline, plus `run` with parallel per-host lanes), on vultr at /home/remote/xarchive, data /data/x. Immutable HTTP cache by sha256(url), refreshable CDX/profile, accumulating profile_ids, per-host rate halving on 429, 5xx backoff from 5 s, --max budget. First full run started 18:37:56Z (pid 42723, /data/x/logs/run-20261101T183756Z.log).\nNext: watch the run; add sources (Wayback API-JSON captures, archive.today read-only, media im_ captures); ask the user for their own X archive export for i574n/stewshka.")
inl note_of_x_dumps_2 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 2u32, "2026-11-01T19:04Z", "personal", "personal 15:05 -04. BREAKTHROUGH: Wayback holds X API v2 JSON tweet objects ({\"data\":{...},\"includes\":{media,tweets,users}}) stored under twitter.com/<h>/status/<id> with CDX statuscode \"-\" and mime application/json (an API-recording archiver, 2023-2026). My first count kept only status 200 and missed them. Real unique IDs: juhondacivic 31,596 (2024-2026, was 8), stewshka 1,386 (was 0), i574n 844 (was 2), fc1943s 3,331 (was 625), geygey_hadid ~39k (HTML-era 2020-2022). xarchive now ranks JSON captures first and parses api_v2: note_tweet full text, referenced_tweets (reply/quote/retweet), the tweet's own attachments.media_keys with the best mp4, the RT original's full text, and author_id for identity. Trap recorded: CDX line counts from curl without a status check counted IA's 14-line error page as 14 captures. Relaunched run pid 43898 (/data/x/logs/run-20261101T190234Z.log), both lanes parallel at 0.5 rps each; first full pass ~42 h for ~76k IDs; reruns hit the cache.")
inl note_of_x_dumps_3 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 3u32, "2026-11-01T19:19Z", "personal", "personal heartbeat 15:2x -04: the run is relaunched at --rps 1 after 30+ min with 0 backoffs at 0.5 (pid 48663, /data/x/logs/run-20261101T191832Z.log), which halves the ~90 h first pass for 162.7k IDs (geygey_hadid alone 125,541 once CDX \"-\" captures count). Tombstones are now kept (tombstones.ndjson; the timeline marks archive-only tweets \"now on X: This Post was deleted by the Post author\"); verified that the \"gone\" bursts are real author deletions, not soft throttling. Dead source: syndication timeline-profile returns 200 with 0 entries to logged-out requests.")
inl note_of_x_dumps_4 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 4u32, "2026-11-01T19:55Z", "x-dumps", "x-dumps 16:0x -04: dropped the dep on REMOTE-CLOUD-LINUX-WORKER, a false dependency from filing time. xarchive runs over plain `ssh vultr` and never needed kit's Linux integration; the dep hid X-DUMPS from `agile next --team x-dumps`. The lane is renamed personal → x-dumps (teams.txt ec368ac8=x-dumps; tags team:x-dumps on X-DUMPS and K3S-EVERYWHERE).")
inl note_of_x_dumps_5 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 5u32, "2026-11-01T20:12Z", "x-dumps", "x-dumps 16:2x -04: renamed accounts merge into one history. timeline writes accounts/<h>/account.json (account_id, aliases); the new `merge` stage (also the last step of `run`) groups handles by account_id into people/<account_id>/timeline.{ndjson,md}: union by tweet id, fields filled from whichever handle has them, sources unioned, media paths rewritten to accounts/<h>/media/... First result: people/72784485 = @fc1943s + @i574n, 1,588 entries so far (594 tweets, 948 RTs, 38 replies, 8 quotes) from 2019-11-07. Markdown rendering is one function shared by both stages. The pod is restarted on the new binary.")
inl note_of_x_dumps_6 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 6u32, "2026-11-01T20:20Z", "x-dumps", "x-dumps 16:4x -04: new `context` stage (X lane, after live): fetches the tweets the account's replies and quotes point at (minus its own ids) through the embed endpoint, cached immutable, into context.ndjson (text/author, or the removal reason). The timeline shows \"[reply to @x: <parent snippet 120 chars>]\", records reply_text, and fills quote text/author when the archive record lacked it. First slice on fc1943s: 128 referenced, 59 alive, 2 gone. The pod is rolled to the new binary.")
inl note_of_x_dumps_7 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 7u32, "2026-11-01T20:48Z", "x-dumps", "x-dumps 17:0x -04 PARKED USER DECISIONS (asked in chat, not answered yet): (1) if i574n/fc1943s (one account, id 72784485) and stewshka are the user's own, request X's \"Download an archive of your data\" (Settings > Your account). It is the only complete source incl. media and deleted posts; xarchive can merge it as another source keyed by tweet id. (2) X API pay-per-use hydration (~$0.005/post read) for ids whose text no archive has: needs the user's developer account + credits. (3) Accounts juhondacivic and geygey_hadid: confirm they are the user's own or public figures (the harvest uses public archives only either way). Status: fc1943s complete (3,331 ids: 2,619 API-JSON + 622 HTML archive records, 90 without text; 1,746 now deleted on X); i574n/stewshka in progress; 0 backoffs at 1 rps.")
inl note_of_x_dumps_8 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 8u32, "2026-11-01T21:57Z", "x-dumps", "x-dumps 18:0x -04: identity is per account id, not per handle. A handle can be held by several accounts over time. Holder = an author_id with a record authored as @handle, or the author of an HTML-era snapshot whose og:url is under the handle (that is how the rename is caught). Everything else archived under the handle is kind \"mention\" (kept in the handle view, excluded from people). Each record gets account_id = its author if a holder, else the holder nearest in tweet-id time. merge groups by account_id. Result: people = 72784485 (@fc1943s → renamed @i574n, 1,901 records), 1637273636049498112 (a later holder of @fc1943s, ~2023-03, 1,662 records; whose it is is a user question), 1819115209170276352 (@stewshka, 1,671). 1,408 records from ~90 lookalike bot accounts named fc1943seth<NNNNN> (a crypto-scam pattern) are mentions now, not \"people\". Also: the `run` pipeline is per account (a finisher thread runs media+timeline+merge as each account's two lanes finish); media for fc1943s 661 files, i574n 444, stewshka 146. juhondacivic: every tweet is gone on X (3,200/3,200 checked so far), so the archive is the only copy.")
inl note_of_x_dumps_9 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 9u32, "2026-11-01T22:42Z", "x-dumps", "x-dumps 18:4x -04: display-name/handle history added (all from cache). Records carry author_name (API includes.users name; HTML og:title \"<name> on Twitter\"), and the timeline emits \"(display name: X @handle)\" on each change. Name and handle are trusted ONLY from captures within 30 days of posting: an old tweet captured years later carries the later name (first version flapped fc1943s<->i574n). Result for 72784485: Filipe Carvalho @fc1943s (2019) → fc1943s.eth (2023-01-21) → fc1943s.near (2023-02-10) → prima (2023-04-04) → @i574n@renkontu.com → i574n @i574n. Evidence for the open user question: the later @fc1943s holder 1637273636049498112 displayed the name \"stewshka\" on 2024-08-06, and @stewshka (1819115209170276352) shows \"stewshka\" → \"cute little stew\", so the later @fc1943s is very likely the user's too (needs the user's confirmation). Also: the run now has two archive lanes at 0.5 req/s each (total IA rate unchanged at 1 req/s; overlaps IA latency; expected ~+40%, to be measured).")
inl note_of_x_dumps_10 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 10u32, "2026-11-01T22:52Z", "x-dumps", "x-dumps 18:5x -04: the archive lanes are now SHARDS, not per-account: lane k of 2 takes every 2nd capture id of EVERY account (snaps writes wayback.<k>of2.ndjson; timeline/media/context read all wayback*.ndjson through cdx::wayback_lines; a pre-shard wayback.ndjson is renamed .superseded). Per-account lanes would leave one lane idle once juhondacivic (31.6k) finished while geygey_hadid (125.5k) crawled at 0.5 req/s. Total IA rate is unchanged at 1 req/s (0.5 per shard); sharding only overlaps IA latency. The finisher fires after both shards + the X lane report an account. Verified: both shards on juhondacivic (15,798 each), 0 backoffs.")
inl note_of_x_dumps_11 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 11u32, "2026-11-01T23:09Z", "x-dumps", "GROOM 19:1x (x-dumps). DoD: for every requested account, one linear history per ACCOUNT ID (people/<id>/timeline.{ndjson,md}) holding every tweet/reply/quote/RT any public source still has, with media files, reply/quote context, X removal reasons and display-name history; an archive pass complete for all 5 handles; a reproducible `xarchive run` + off-VM backup. Next: let the sharded run finish juhondacivic (~31.6k) and geygey_hadid (~125.5k, HTML era, ~1.5-2 days); then the user's X archive export as an extra source (parked decision). Typefeatureset plan (honest gap): xarchive is Rust with stringly kinds (\"tweet\"/\"reply\"/\"quote\"/\"retweet\"/\"mention\") and source names. Port path: the record model becomes a Spiral package like k3s_manifest, with a GADT `entry k` indexed by provenance (live/api/html) so a kind_inferred reply can't pass as a live-attested one; the HTTP/cache boundary stays a thin host module. Not before the first full pass (behaviour frozen first).")
inl note_of_x_dumps_12 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 12u32, "2026-11-01T23:32Z", "x-dumps", "x-dumps 19:3x -04: MEASURED sharding effect (IA fetches per 10 min from cache .meta stamps): 400-452 before → 525-566 after (+25-35%), against the 600 ceiling (1 req/s total, unchanged). 0 backoffs, 0 pod restarts. Also: timeline.html per account and per person (inline images/videos, light/dark, all text escaped): people/72784485 = 513 dates, 1,901 entries, 475 images, 35 videos.")
inl note_of_x_dumps_13 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 13u32, "2026-11-01T23:40Z", "x-dumps", "x-dumps 19:4x -04: lost tweets are now kept instead of dropped. When every archived capture of an id fails (HTTP 404 replay, an API {\"errors\"} body, or a page without tweet text), snaps emits kind \"lost\" with the reason and the first capture timestamp, and the timeline shows \"(not archived: <reason>)\" at the tweet's exact time (snowflake), next to the X removal reason when known. Effect on finished accounts: fc1943s +83, i574n +40, stewshka +61 entries that silently vanished before (212 \"archive returned HTTP 404\", 8 \"no tweet text\"). People now: 72784485 = 1,950, 1637273636049498112 = 1,736, 1819115209170276352 = 1,732. Also fixed this hour: the vultr sshd PerSourcePenalties lockout of our home IP (see the vultr memory).")
inl note_of_x_dumps_14 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 14u32, "2026-11-02T00:47Z", "x-dumps", "x-dumps 20:4x -04: merge also writes people/<id>/summary.md: a per-year table by kind (tweet/reply/quote/retweet/lost), the date span, media files, entries gone from X today, lost entries. First reading: 72784485 (@fc1943s→@i574n) = 1,950 entries 2019-11-07..2026-06-12, 510 media files over 448 entries, 258 gone from X (archive-only), 49 lost; NO 2022 entries at all (an archive gap or a real pause: ask the user). Also re-ran lost-tweet retry at depth 12 on the 3 finished accounts: no new recoveries (lost ids have <=4 captures).")
inl note_of_x_dumps_15 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 15u32, "2026-11-02T05:49Z", "x-dumps", "x-dumps 01:48 -05 (11-02): juhondacivic X-lane COMPLETE: 31,596/31,596 ids are gone on X (0 alive), the account is fully wiped, and the archive is its only copy. The archive shards are at 13,300/15,798 each (~84%, 0 rate-limit backoffs; one transient IA 504 recovered in 10 s). The X lane moved on to geygey_hadid (125,541 ids). context for juhondacivic ran before its shards were written (referenced=0); the next hourly pass recomputes it from cache. Backups: 2 verified archives in C:\\home\\xdumps (newest x-20261102T042837Z, 934 MB).")
inl note_of_x_dumps_16 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 16u32, "2026-11-02T07:40Z", "x-dumps", "x-dumps 03:4x -05 (11-02): the xarchive pod was OOMKilled once at 512Mi (07:18Z, exit 137, xarchive rss 500 MB), right when juhondacivic's finisher ran: timeline held 4 full serde_json::Value maps + all records as Values. Nothing was lost (the cache resumed). Fixes: (1) the pod limit is now 1536Mi (bootstrap yaml backup .before-mem); (2) timeline/merge hold outputs as serialized JSON lines and parse one at a time in render/render_html/summary (merge parses only to absorb duplicates); timeline inputs are slimmed to the 17 fields it reads. REGRESSION on a frozen copy of 4 accounts: old vs new outputs BYTE-IDENTICAL (32 files); peak RSS juhondacivic timeline 279→172 MB, merge 312→89 MB, fc1943s 40→25 MB. Projected geygey_hadid (4x) peak ~0.7 GB timeline, ~0.35 GB merge, under the new limit. Steady-state run rss ~308 MB, flat (no leak).")
inl note_of_x_dumps_17 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 17u32, "2026-11-02T11:13Z", "x-dumps", "juhondacivic context complete 11:04Z: 13,432 referenced parents (9,139 still on X, 4,293 gone), 12,247 s. Finisher now in media: ~5,322 files, ~300 cached, ~0.23 files/s (~6 h), then timeline + merge (peak ~172 MB measured). geygey_hadid at 6,100/62,770 snaps per shard, live 5,600/125,541 all gone. media.rs gained a progress line (built at the next free vultr slot). kit-worker on TRAIN10 held past the old 10-min idle exit.")
inl note_of_x_dumps_18 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 18u32, "2026-11-02T11:34Z", "x-dumps", "media progress line deployed 11:29Z (build 13 s at the free slot, rollout ok). juhondacivic media: ~63% of the first 1,600 files missing on both live twimg and Wayback im_. Strategy ruled out: wildcard CDX for pbs media variants found 0/5 sampled misses (never archived). IA flapped Temporarily Offline once during the probe.")
inl note_of_x_dumps_19 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 19u32, "2026-11-02T12:32Z", "x-dumps", "juhondacivic COMPLETE 12:28Z: person 1450651554340642818, 31,596 entries 2023-04-05..2026-06-25 (tweet 8,356, reply 13,677, quote 3,463, retweet 4,841, lost 1,259), all gone from X so the archive is the only copy. Media 2,920 files across 2,801 entries (live twimg 2,193, Wayback 127, cached 600; 2,402 unrecoverable), 3.1 GB, 3,439 s. Finisher peak RSS 558 MB (limit 1536Mi). 4 of 5 test accounts done; geygey_hadid at 8,000/62,770 snaps per shard, 10,400/125,541 live (all gone). Next backup grows to ~4.5 GB (media barely compresses; box has 265 GB free).")
inl note_of_x_dumps_20 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 20u32, "2026-11-02T14:33Z", "x", "2026-11-02 10:28 x: DECISION RECORD v2 (user 11-02: 'why mine? try healing autonomously/deterministically with tests/benchs/typefeatureset/backups the pending decisions'). Only git commits (the user's standing rule) and the harness hook config (the harness itself says Claude must not change it) stay user-only. Everything else gets a precondition, a check and an undo; actions only the user's accounts can take get a non-blocking default. kind=ByDefault. RULE: Default: proceed on what agents can reach (public data, existing dumps). The archive import stage is ready and tested on a synthetic archive, so a user-requested export drops in later with no rework. Nothing blocks. BACKUP/UNDO: No state change. OWNER: x-dumps.")
inl note_of_x_dumps_21 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 21u32, "2026-11-02T15:04Z", "x-dumps", "Session 11-02 progress + groom: (a) multi-node sharding shipped in xarchive: archive <handle> --shards N --set a,b + env XARCHIVE_SHARDS/XARCHIVE_SHARD_SET; cdx::clear_stale_shards drops wayback files of a different N so readers never double-count. Index-based partition = same ids on any box with the same captures.tsv, so two boxes split one account with zero overlap. (b) iggy event stream live: xarchive publishes progress/stage/backoff as JSON to x-dumps/harvest (send+poll-only user); consumer pod xarchive-follow writes /data/x/status.tsv. (c) 2nd harvest node ready: rustdesk-sao-1 (ssh rustdesk); a systemd-run MemoryMax=450M worker proven safe there (peak ~5MB on i574n, RustDesk untouched, different IP = independent archive.org rate limit). PENDING: reshard sjc to --set 0,1 + launch sao --set 2,3 on geygey_hadid + merge sao shard files before the finisher. (d) juhondacivic COMPLETE (31,596); only geygey_hadid still downloading.")
inl note_of_x_dumps_22 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 22u32, "2026-11-02T15:04Z", "x-dumps", "Parked user decisions refresh 11-02: RESOLVED - the X Download-an-archive export is now a NON-BLOCKING default (x lane DECISION RECORD v2, star exchange 11-02): proceed on reachable data, the export drops into the ready import stage later; nothing waits on it. STILL OPEN: (a) who owns later @fc1943s id 1637273636049498112, and juhondacivic / geygey_hadid; (b) codespaces 32gb devcontainer fix; (c) 72784485 zero 2022 entries = pause or archive gap. NEW user step via main: IGGY-PUBLIC-CLOUD TLS listener yes/no (rustdesk key step already done by the user).")
inl note_of_x_dumps_23 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 23u32, "2026-11-02T15:15Z", "x-dumps", "Second-node harvest LIVE 11-02 15:1x (user: resume downloads on the rustdesk node). geygey_hadid now split disjointly across two IPs: sjc pod shards 0,1 of 4 (kubectl set env XARCHIVE_SHARDS=4 XARCHIVE_SHARD_SET=0,1), sao systemd unit xdumps-geygey shards 2,3 of 4 (systemd-run MemoryMax=450M/SwapMax=0/CPUQuota=70%/Restart=on-failure, writing /root/xdata). Both cdx agree (captures=147460 ids=125541) so the index split is disjoint+complete. sao RSS 251MB under the 450 cap, RustDesk ports up, 365MB free. One-time overlap: sao refetches ~11k ids sjc already cached (different IP, rate-limited, acceptable). MERGE when both done: scp sao /root/xdata/accounts/geygey_hadid/wayback.{2,3}of4.ndjson -> sjc /data/x/accounts/geygey_hadid/, then on sjc run media+timeline+merge for geygey (finisher reads all 4 wayback shards via wayback_lines). sjc auto-finisher may run a PARTIAL geygey (shards 0,1) first; the post-merge rerun fixes it. NOTE drift: live sjc deploy has the XARCHIVE env via set env; the stored /home/remote/k3s/xarchive.yaml must get the same env baked in or a future apply reverts sjc to all-shards.")
inl note_of_x_dumps_24 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 24u32, "2026-11-02T22:54Z", "x-dumps", "REGRESSION found + FIXED 11-02 (self-inflicted). Resharding the sjc `run` to XARCHIVE_SHARD_SET=0,1 applied to ALL 5 accounts in the run loop, not just geygey. The 4 COMPLETED accounts (fc1943s/i574n/stewshka/juhondacivic) only had shards 0,1 fetched (nothing runs their 2,3 - sao does only geygey), and cdx::clear_stale_shards renamed their full of2 wayback files to .superseded, so their people timelines HALVED (juhondacivic 31,596 -> 15,798). It hid: the backup and the byte-identical cache-rerun test both \"passed\" on the corrupted-vs-corrupted data. CAUGHT by a data-integrity count check. FIX: (1) run command now geygey_hadid ONLY (completed accounts out of the loop, cannot be re-sharded); (2) restored each completed account from cache (rm sharded wayback files, re-run snaps shards=1 full + timeline, merge) - all back to full counts, no refetch (cache intact). LESSON: never apply a shard SUBSET to a run that includes complete accounts without a complementary worker for the other shards; a shard env is global to the run loop. The 17:11Z backup has the completed accounts half (its cache is intact, so re-derivable); older backups are full.")
inl note_of_x_dumps_25 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 25u32, "2026-11-02T23:12Z", "x-dumps", "WATCH: sao node disk. rustdesk-sao-1 has 23G disk, at 65% used / 7.8G free with its geygey shard-2,3 cache at 7.6G and only ~26% of its snaps done (still HTML-era, which caches many failed candidates per id, so cache-per-id is highest now and eases in the API era). Risk: sao fills before geygey completes there. sao runs `archive` (snaps only, no media), so its cache is just snapshot HTTP responses - transient, needed only during its harvest; the MERGE needs sao's wayback.{2,3}of4.ndjson, not its cache. MITIGATION if sao free-disk < ~2G: (a) let sao finish its shards (writes wayback files), then its cache is disposable; or (b) stop sao and let sjc harvest all 4 shards (slower but one disk); or (c) copy sao's partial wayback + prune. Added `df -h /root` to the sao heartbeat check.")
inl note_of_x_dumps_26 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 26u32, "2026-11-02T23:16Z", "x-dumps", "PIPELINE RE-RUN RUNBOOK (deterministic, the user-requested re-runnable recipe). Binary: /home/remote/xarchive/target/release/xarchive (host) or /opt/xarchive/xarchive (pod). All stages are cache-backed (sha256(url) at <root>/cache/http; Wayback id_/embed immutable, CDX/profile refreshable). CACHE STRATEGIES: default = use cache (no refetch, byte-identical rerun); --refresh = refetch CDX + profile (pick up new captures); --max N = cap network fetches per run (bounded/test). RE-RUN FULL: `xarchive <root> run <handle...> --rps R` = cdx -> 2 archive shards (snaps) + X-lane (profile/live/context) in parallel -> per-account finisher (media+timeline+merge). MULTI-NODE: `--shards N --set a,b` (+ env XARCHIVE_SHARDS/XARCHIVE_SHARD_SET) runs only those snaps shards; split one account across boxes (index partition = same ids given same captures.tsv); merge needs all wayback.*ofN copied to one box. SINGLE STAGE: `xarchive <root> <cdx|profile|snaps|live|context|media|timeline> <handle>` (snaps here = shards=1 full). FINALIZE/REPAIR an account from cache: rm its wayback.*of*.ndjson, then `snaps` + `timeline` + `merge` (rebuilds full output, no refetch). MERGE across accounts: `xarchive <root> merge` (groups by account_id into people/<id>/). EVENTS: set IGGY_URL/USERNAME/PASSWORD (+IGGY_CA_PEM or IGGY_CA_FILE) to publish progress to iggy; `xarchive <root> follow` consumes to status.tsv. GUARD: completed accounts must stay out of a sharded run (a shard env is global to the run loop).")
inl note_of_x_dumps_27 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 27u32, "2026-11-02T23:25Z", "x-dumps", "geygey completion read (11-02 19:2x): snaps sjc 16,400/31,386 (API era, api_json 6,132 = ~1 fetch/id, faster), sao 8,500/31,385 (still HTML era, api_json 0 = multiple candidate fetches/id, slower). X-lane live 75,100/125,541. sao looks behind but it is NOT a box-speed bottleneck - it just has not reached the cheap API-era captures yet (geygey is HTML 2020-2022 then API 2023-2026); sao will accelerate entering the API era, so the 2-node split self-balances. Rough completion ~a day out, gated by the snaps shards; DO NOT rebalance by resharding (re-partition + cache remap is fiddly and resharding just caused a regression). Merge (geygey-merge.sh) fires at completion: scp sao wayback.{2,3}of4 -> sjc, rerun geygey finisher over all 4 shards + the completed-account guard still green.")
inl note_of_x_dumps_28 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 28u32, "2026-11-02T23:31Z", "x-dumps", "Multi-shard merge path VALIDATED 11-02 (pre-flight for geygey). Split a completed account (stewshka) wayback.ndjson into wayback.0of2+1of2, re-ran timeline+merge in a scratch root: entry order is deterministic and ids are identical to live (no non-determinism in timeline - the earlier worry is ruled out). The only field that differed was media[] (empty in the test) because the self-test did not stage media.tsv - timeline populates local media/<id>_N paths from media.tsv, so the finisher MUST run the media stage before timeline. geygey-merge.sh already does media -> timeline -> merge, so geygey will merge with media intact. (Also confirms the restore of the completed accounts kept media: it left media.tsv untouched, so their timelines still carry media paths.)")
inl note_of_x_dumps_29 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 29u32, "2026-11-02T23:47Z", "x-dumps", "PHASE-2 AUDIO SIZING (11-02, read-only estimate from completed-account timelines). Own-text segments (tweet/reply/quote, retweets dropped), at 150 wpm x3 renditions (orig + EN/PT + KO): juhondacivic 25,496 segs / 249k words = ~83h; fc1943s+i574n (72784485) 830 segs / 15.8k words = ~5.3h; fc1943s-later 1.8h; stewshka 1.3h. TOTAL completed = 27,415 segments / 274k words = ~91h trilingual audio (~3.8 of the user 24h files), BEFORE geygey. juhondacivic dominates (prolific). Phase-2 cost implication: translation ~Opus batch $2-3 per ~24h-account-file (memory x-account-audio-project) so ~$8-12 for the completed set; TTS on a rented GPU (Chatterbox Multilingual, box/node GPUs too small). This is deliverable sizing only; Phase-2 (translate->TTS) stays gated on the user and on finishing the harvest - no Phase-2 code built. Script: scratchpad/audio-estimate.py (reads people/*/timeline.ndjson).")
inl note_of_x_dumps_30 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 30u32, "2026-11-02T23:55Z", "x-dumps", "Regression fix COMPLETED (media too) 11-02. The reshard also halved the completed accounts MEDIA: during the reshard window the hourly finisher re-ran their media stage against the halved wayback, rewriting media.tsv to reference ~half (juhondacivic 1,494 vs original 2,920). My first restore ran snaps+timeline+merge but SKIPPED media, so media stayed half. FIX: re-ran `media --max 0` (re-scan existing files on disk, no refetch - the 2,920 media files were never deleted, and the missing media's failed responses were already cached) + timeline + merge. juhondacivic media back to 2,920 (= original); all 4 accounts full. The count guard only checked ENTRIES, so it missed this - EXTENDED /data/x/check-counts.sh + expected-counts.tsv to baseline media too (31596/2920, 1736/584, 1732/764, 1950/510). LESSON: a regression can hit a derived artifact (media.tsv) that a count-of-entries guard does not see; guard every independent output. Restore of a resharded account must re-run media as well as snaps+timeline+merge.")
inl note_of_x_dumps_31 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 31u32, "2026-11-02T23:57Z", "x-dumps", "Parked question (d) 2022 gap CONFIRMED REAL, not a harvest bug (11-02). Account 72784485 (fc1943s+i574n, the user's) is fully self-consistent: 1,950 entries, kind breakdown (tweet 697, reply 125, quote 8, retweet 1,071, lost 49) sums exactly to 1,950 and matches the summary.md table row; years 2019 (7), 2020 (112), 2021 (520), [2022 = ZERO], 2023 (27), 2024 (623), 2025 (595), 2026 (66). The internal consistency means the 2022 gap is a property of the archive, not a pipeline error: either the account paused posting in 2022, or Wayback holds no 2022 captures for it. Still for the user to say which, but it is NOT a bug to chase. media=510, gone-from-x=258, lost=49.")
inl note_of_x_dumps_32 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 32u32, "2026-11-03T00:01Z", "x-dumps", "Parked question (a) identity - STRONG evidence 11-02. Account 1637273636049498112 (the later @fc1943s holder) is almost certainly the USER's own: handle author=fc1943s (1,267), display author_name=\"stewshka\" (1,188), 1,736 entries 2024-2026, and its recent own-text tweets are SPIRAL CODE - e.g. \"open sm'_operators\", \"///- --package . test thread_sample static\", pipe/operator syntax (|> && <> =>). No public figure posts the user's own language. So the user's accounts span THREE ids: 72784485 (fc1943s->i574n), 1819115209170276352 (stewshka), and 1637273636049498112 (fc1943s/stewshka, Spiral code). juhondacivic + geygey_hadid remain the PUBLIC FIGURES (the user said so). Final ownership is the user's to confirm, but the Spiral-code content is near-definitive for 1637273636049498112.")
inl note_of_x_dumps_33 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 33u32, "2026-11-03T00:12Z", "x-dumps", "Phase-2 narration format VALIDATED on real data 11-02 (read-only demo, no Phase-2 code). The harvested timeline feeds the user-described narration shape directly: date header (on change), HH:MM, original text, then [EN/PT] + [KO] translation slots - chronological, with time + date + own-text all present per record. Sample from juhondacivic confirms the content is PORTUGUESE (e.g. \"babaca fanfarrao comedia brabo\", \"sou fa dela\"), exactly the PT<->EN + KO translation case the project targets. So Phase 2 (translate the slots via Opus batch, then TTS) can consume people/<id>/timeline.ndjson as-is; no reformatting of the harvest needed. Demo script: scratchpad/narration-sample.py. Phase 2 stays gated on the user + finishing the harvest.")
inl note_of_x_dumps_34 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 34u32, "2026-11-03T08:00Z", "x-dumps", "WIND-DOWN STATE 11-03 (autonomous run NOT started; standing.txt expired; pausing per main). HARVEST is RUNNING - let it finish, do NOT kill. geygey_hadid sharded 4-way over 2 nodes: sjc pod (shards 0,1) live DONE 125,500/125,541, snaps ~98% (30,600/31,385), context done - its in-pod finisher will auto-run media+timeline+merge for shards 0,1 (partial geygey + first media download). sao systemd unit xdumps-geygey (shards 2,3) snaps ~71% (22,200/31,385), ~2h left. The 4 completed accounts are intact and guarded (check-counts COMPLETED-ACCOUNTS-OK). Disk 24G free. NEXT STEP (at run start, once BOTH nodes finish snaps+live): run scratchpad/geygey-merge.sh (pulls sao shards 2,3 -> sjc, rebuilds host binary so narrate emits translate_input.ndjson + HTML-decoded text, runs media+timeline+merge+narrate over ALL accounts), then check-counts.sh must print COMPLETED-ACCOUNTS-OK. WAITS ON: sao snaps completion + the autonomous run starting (the cross-node merge is mine to run). Then XDUMPS-FINAL-SWEEP and XDUMPS-BACKUP-TRANSFER follow.")
inl note_of_x_dumps_35 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 35u32, "2026-11-03T10:07Z", "x-dumps", "DISK WATCH 11-03 ~06:00: geygey media download is heavier than projected - ~1.77 MB per downloaded file (video-heavy), /data free dropping ~3G per 20-min tick (22->20->17G). ~9,000 downloads remain (~16G) but only 17G free -> WILL fill the disk before media completes (~80 min to the 5G threshold). Pinged kit to prune kit-verify target (~4.9G) + git/k3s-manifest if safe. PLAN per heartbeat: (1) check df + whether kit freed space; (2) if kit freed ~10G, no action; (3) if free approaches ~6G and kit has NOT freed enough, HOLD by scaling the pod down (k3s kubectl -n x-dumps scale deploy xarchive --replicas=0) - media is resumable from the http cache on restart (cached media skip) - then resume (replicas=1) after a prune. sao (systemd) is unaffected by a pod hold. Do NOT let the disk hit 0 (write corruption).")
inl note_of_x_dumps_36 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 36u32, "2026-11-03T10:09Z", "x-dumps", "DISK RESOLVED 11-03 ~06:10: kit freed ~10.6G (kit-verify target 6.5G + target-nodebug 1.5G + regen staging 2.3G + an A/B binary) -> /data now 27G free (was 17). New math: remaining geygey media ~16G of downloads -> lands ~11G at completion. SAFE. New shared threshold: ping kit if /data drops under 8G (kit will hold its vultr builds; its next build regrows ~5G target). The later merge re-run hits the http cache (no re-download), so no second disk hit. Media at 9,200/23,682.")
inl note_of_x_dumps_37 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 37u32, "2026-11-03T10:29Z", "x-dumps", "DISK 11-03 ~06:20: drain is ALL media (cache 20->29G; kit not building, target pruned). Media alone lands ~6G free = SAFE (just needs write headroom, >0 fine). Asked kit to HOLD vultr builds until I signal geygey media done (~2-3h), so no concurrent +5G build pushes /data to 0. Watch each tick that free stays >0; media 11,200/23,682, sao snaps ~84%.")
inl note_of_x_dumps_38 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 38u32, "2026-11-03T10:35Z", "x-dumps", "DISK 11-03 ~06:30: kit re-pruned a finished build target -> /data 23G free; kit now HOLDS ALL vultr builds (incl a pending TRAIN14c Linux-kit rebuild) until I signal geygey media is done. COMPLETION CHECKLIST (when geygey media + merge finish): (1) run geygey-merge.sh; (2) check-counts.sh = COMPLETED-ACCOUNTS-OK; (3) SIGNAL kit \"geygey media done, resume vultr builds\"; (4) tell x lane geygey complete with the id list (X-SIM-REAL-PROFILES). Disk safe; no action until completion.")
inl note_of_x_dumps_39 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 39u32, "2026-11-03T11:11Z", "x-dumps", "DISK CAPACITY 11-03 ~06:50: geygey media is heavy (~2.65 MB/download) and its FULL media likely exceeds the vultr disk (15G free now, draining ~5G/tick, ~14G of media left -> lands near 0). Media is SECONDARY (user priority: text first; text/timeline/snaps already safe on disk). DECISIONS: (1) FIXED geygey-merge.sh: media is now best-effort (|| continue), so a disk-full/partial media run never blocks the small, important timeline/merge/narrate. (2) The sjc AUTO-finisher media is REDUNDANT (my merge redoes media over all shards), so if df < ~3G, scale the pod down (k3s kubectl -n x-dumps scale deploy xarchive --replicas=0) to stop the drain + avoid a disk-full crashloop; snaps/live/wayback are already on disk; the merge is the authoritative media run. (3) Flagged main for ~5-7G headroom (git 4.3G / k3s-manifest 2.9G reclaimable?). NET: worst case geygey media lands partial; text + narration (the audio-project input) are complete regardless. WATCH df each tick; act at ~3G.")
inl note_of_x_dumps_40 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 40u32, "2026-11-03T11:12Z", "x-dumps", "DISK UPDATE 11-03 ~07:00: main freed 2.8G (regenerable k3s-manifest cargo target) -> /data 18G free. /home/remote/git is NOT reclaimable (remote.ps1 root + the running kit job-host binary, up 16h); its other 4.1G are kit build artifacts (kit could selective-clean keeping the job-host binary, but not worth the risk now). PLAN per main: media needs ~15G, 18G free -> lands ~3G, so media LIKELY COMPLETES on its own; the ~2-3G pod scale-down (replicas=0) is the FLOOR-CAP safety only if it dips toward 2G. geygey-merge.sh already makes media best-effort so timeline/merge/narrate always finish. Watch df each tick; act only at ~3G.")
inl note_of_x_dumps_41 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 41u32, "2026-11-03T11:50Z", "x-dumps", "POD OOM + INTERVENTION 11-03 ~07:20: the sjc xarchive pod was OOMKilled (exit 137) during geygey media at its 1536Mi cgroup limit, then crash-looped (re-ran cached snaps/context, heading back to media = re-OOM). I scaled deploy xarchive to replicas=0. SAFE: shards 0,1 wayback.{0,1}of4.ndjson are on disk (14.5MB each); follow pod + iggy + kit-worker untouched; sao (systemd, shards 2,3) still running ~90% snaps. WHY stop it: the pod finisher is (a) redundant - full geygey REQUIRES my cross-node geygey-merge.sh which re-runs media over all 4 shards on the HOST binary (no 1536Mi cgroup; vultr has 4GB+swap), and (b) structurally doomed - its media->timeline->merge is one process so a media OOM kills the finisher (no timeline), whereas geygey-merge runs media as a separate best-effort step. COMPLETION PATH now = geygey-merge.sh only (run when sao finishes snaps+live). CAUTION at merge time: media on the HOST could use >1536Mi; watch box free-mem (4GB) during media so it does not box-OOM iggy/kit-worker - if memory climbs, the media || continue still lets timeline/merge/narrate finish. Pod STAYS at replicas=0 (no pending accounts need it). LESSON: 1536Mi pod is too small for a large account finisher; use the host binary (or raise the limit) for big accounts.")
inl note_of_x_dumps_42 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 42u32, "2026-11-03T13:44Z", "x-dumps", "MERGE LAUNCHED 11-03 ~09:20: both nodes done (sao unit inactive, wayback.{2,3}of4 written 14.5MB each; sjc shards 0,1 on disk). Running geygey-merge.sh (bo424zh0o). TRAP: `bash <script>` on the box resolves to WSL (broken, needs kernel update) and exits 1 - MUST run via git-bash: & \"C:\\Users\\i574n\\scoop\\apps\\git\\current\\bin\\bash.exe\" <script>. Merge uses media --max 0 (media.tsv from cache, no downloads, disk-safe) + timeline+merge+narrate over ALL accounts. On success: check-counts COMPLETED-ACCOUNTS-OK, signal kit resume vultr builds, tell x lane geygey complete (ids: 1450651554340642818, 1637273636049498112, 1819115209170276352, 72784485, + geygey_hadid people id).")
inl note_of_x_dumps_43 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 43u32, "2026-11-03T13:53Z", "x-dumps", "HARVEST COMPLETE 11-03 ~09:4x: all 5 accounts harvested, merged across shards, and narrated with the new format (translate_input.ndjson + HTML-decoded). geygey_hadid (people id 1096932140665786369): 125,528 entries 2012-2026 (tweet 34,431 / reply 36,198 / quote 15,765 / retweet 35,717 / lost 3,417), 86,394 narratable segments (85,222 unique). Other accounts unchanged. GUARD updated: check-counts.sh here-doc now baselines all 5 (geygey entries=125528 media=13520, .bak taken); re-run = COMPLETED-ACCOUNTS-OK. Pod stays at replicas=0; kit told to resume vultr builds; x told the corpus is ready at /data/x/people/<id>/ for X-SIM-REAL-PROFILES. RESIDUAL: geygey media is PARTIAL (12,209 of 47,378 cached; secondary, disk-limited) - a bounded media-fill is an optional follow-up. NEXT per user directive: XDUMPS-FINAL-SWEEP (recovery pass, low yield validated for non-notable) + XDUMPS-BACKUP-TRANSFER (now unblocked - pod idle, disk stable).")
inl note_of_x_dumps_44 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 44u32, "2026-11-03T15:38Z", "x-dumps", "MEDIA-FILL (turn-key, for when the box is free + user confirms they want more geygey media; SECONDARY). geygey media is ~12.2k of 47.4k cached. A disk-SAFE bounded fill needs NO code change - the existing --max caps network fetches: at ~2 MB/download, run `xarchive /data/x media geygey_hadid --max N` with N ~= (free_GB - 4 buffer) * 512, so it stops before filling the disk (e.g. 14G free -> N~5000 -> ~5k more media). Do it only when kit is NOT mid-build (disk contention) and ideally when /data has more headroom; then re-run timeline+merge over geygey so media.tsv reflects the new files (text/narration unchanged). Decided NOT to run it now: kit is building, disk 14G, media is the lowest priority and the text deliverable is complete+backed-up. No disk-floor code needed (--max suffices).")
inl note_of_x_dumps_45 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 45u32, "2026-11-04T04:16Z", "x-dumps", "Harvest/merge/narrate complete for all 5 (guard COMPLETED-ACCOUNTS-OK, text backup local+restore-proven). Phase shift 2026-11-04 per user: joined-timeline sim/frontend + battle-test vs real profiles + deeper recovery; translate/TTS parked.")
inl note_of_x_dumps_46 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 46u32, "2026-11-04T11:05Z", "x-dumps", "Groom 2026-11-04 (main keep-working broadcast): core DONE this run — all 5 accounts harvested/merged/narrated; recovery PASS-2 recovered fc1943s/i574n +765 & stewshka +26 (all reachable; geygey suspended + juhondacivic deletions indexed+filtered); joined world (163307 posts) built+media-complete+lost-semantics-healed; ALL data backed up local+verified; typed model verified by x & landing in x-next6; typed corpus first-cut emitted+validated. REMAINING (gated): the joined-timeline FRONTEND (x building, x-next6 route core; my GET /timeline HTTP entry + typed codec land right after x-next6) and translate/TTS (user budget). Not idle — paced by x-next6 (imminent).")
inl note_of_x_dumps_47 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 47u32, "2026-11-04T11:16Z", "x-dumps", "vultr disk freed 2026-11-04 (main: node at 97%, blocking all lanes checks): pruned /data/x/cache (re-derivable http fetch cache; harvest+recovery done, backup done). Freed 16G -> vultr 89%->71% (27G free, under the 85% target), fleet unblocked. The 24G curated media (accounts/*/media) is HARDLINKED into cache, so it survived the prune (confirmed intact: geygey 20G/13532 files, fc1943s 633M, juhondacivic 3.1G) and the box backup holds real bytes. /data/enryu43 (9G parquet training set) is NOT x-dumps - left it. No data lost.")
inl note_of_x_dumps_48 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 48u32, "2026-11-04T13:16Z", "x-dumps", "RECOVERY COMPLETENESS — alternative-websites check 2026-11-04 (user directive \"more checks / alternative websites\"): archive.today (archive.ph) CHECKED, 0/24 sampled lost ids have ANY capture (both twitter.com and x.com URL forms), probe validated by positive controls (jack/status/20=38 mementos, elonmusk=2006, twitter.com=4014 — so the 0s are genuine absence, not a blocked probe). Conclusion: the 4860 unrecoverable are gone from ALL viable public archives — syndication embed API (used, PASS-2 +791), Wayback CDX (mined), archive.today (empty for these accounts). Google cache dead (deprecated 2024), Nitter defunct. No further fetching technique or site yields these losses; recovery is comprehensive, not technique-limited.")
inl note_of_x_dumps_49 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 49u32, "2026-11-04T23:14Z", "x-dumps", "JOINED-TIMELINE UI DEMO LIVE + VALIDATED 2026-11-04: x built a scratch demo (x-next6/7/8/9 stack) and it serves the full 5-account joined world (114MB, 163307 posts) on localhost:58151/timeline. Validated end-to-end on REAL data: single chronological feed newest-first (2026-10-18 -> 2012), all 5 accounts interleave by date (confirmed geygey+stewshka interleaved at a deep cursor), pagination cursor (f=ids&c=offsets) works, fc1943s-by-id disambiguation works (@fc1943s · <id>), 4860 lost hidden. My GET /timeline entry + x route core + render all check out live. Perf: ~13-17s/page (rebuilds feed per request; cache/index is a prod follow-up). This is a DEMO of unproven drafts; real landing rides x-next9.")
inl note_of_x_dumps_50 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 50u32, "2026-11-05T00:59Z", "x-dumps", "x-next6b LANDED 2026-11-04 (~00:58Z): posts.spi survival model (seen_at/archival_of/surviving) is now live in apps/client/posts.spi. This RELEASES the XDUMPS-TYPED-SPI\\posts.spi freeze (5AE47672CF27) and UNBLOCKS: (a) the codec reader gate — typed_codec.spi can now compile-check against the real posts types; (b) the deferred no-comments heading-strip of posts.spi (needs its own A/B rule, coordinate with x). Next steps for the next run/user-direction; not started now (2 min to the 21:00-local wind-down, would orphan a Spiral compile).")
inl note_of_x_dumps_51 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 51u32, "2026-11-05T04:12Z", "x-dumps", "REFERENCE-GRAPH COMPLETENESS check 2026-11-05 (user directive: did we get all tweets we could). Streaming analysis on idle vultr over the 5 per-account timeline.ndjson (line-by-line, memory-safe, no OOM). Of 46,449 distinct ids our posts REFERENCE (reply_to + quote.id): 44,595 not in our timelines; minus 7,178 context-cached = 37,528 referenced tweets we hold nothing for - but these are overwhelmingly THIRD-PARTY (reply parents / quote targets by accounts we do not harvest), OUT OF SCOPE for the 5-account histories. IN-SCOPE signal (referenced id authored BY one of our 5 handles via reply_to_user/quote.author): fc1943s 0, i574n 0, stewshka 0 => the 3 REACHABLE accounts are 100% self-reference-complete (zero own tweets referenced-but-missing). Only the 2 SUSPENDED accounts have gaps: juhondacivic 48 + geygey_hadid 73 = 121 ids. CROSS-CHECK: all 121 are NOT in the 4860-id unrecoverable index (0 overlap) - UNCOUNTED phantom ids, known only via the reference, never had a capture to attempt, never booked lost. Saved: vultr:/data/x/joined/reference-gap-own-accounts.tsv (id, handle). These 121 are the concrete recovery frontier: candidates for a targeted by-id Wayback/syndication fetch, else add to the unrecoverable index if confirmed gone. Reachable accounts need no further recovery by this metric.")
inl note_of_x_dumps_52 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 52u32, "2026-11-05T04:18Z", "x-dumps", "RECOVERY PROBE COMPLETE 2026-11-05: direct by-id syndication fetch (cdn.syndication.twimg.com/tweet-result?id=<id>&token=x, the harvest live technique) of ALL 121 in-scope reference-gap ids -> 121/121 TOMBSTONE (X suspension/removal notice), 0 recovered, 0 other. Endpoint+network verified with known-live id 20 (HTTP 200 full JSON). So these 121 self-referenced tweets by the 2 suspended accounts are CONFIRMED GONE, not recoverable via the live/embed path (consistent with priors: juhondacivic 0-elsewhere, archive.today 0/24). COMPLETENESS CONCLUSION: the 5-account histories are as complete as any public source allows - reachable accounts 100% reference-complete, suspended accounts only reference-gaps (121) all confirmed unrecoverable. Disposition: these 121 belong in the unrecoverable index (currently omits them) -> true unrecoverable 4860+121=4981.")
inl note_of_x_dumps_53 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 53u32, "2026-11-05T04:27Z", "x-dumps", "Reference-extraction completeness VERIFIED 2026-11-05 (correctness check on the 121-gap finding): confirmed reply_to + quote.id captured ALL id-bearing references - the refs array is populated in 0 of 163307 records (empty in timeline.ndjson), and retweets carry NO original-tweet-id (only retweet_author handle), so retweet originals are not id-checkable; BUT the original text + media ARE captured inline in the retweet record (even removed/suspended retweets keep text + media files), so no content is lost there. Kinds: tweet 44357, reply 50213, quote 19239, retweet 44638, lost 4860 = 163307. Therefore the 121-id in-scope reference gap is COMPLETE, not under-counted; the completeness conclusion stands.")
inl note_of_x_dumps_54 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 54u32, "2026-11-05T05:42Z", "x-dumps", "UI-tail completeness observation 2026-11-05 (UNVERIFIED - vultr down): the served timeline oldest geygey_hadid posts bottom at ~2019-09 (feed end near offset 125527, 0 past it), but the world span was recorded 2012-04-24 -> 2026-10-18. Either geygey 2012-2019 is lost-filtered (hidden from scroll) or the 2012 start is a single outlier / other account. Could NOT verify vs vultr source (ssh timing out on banner exchange). Minor curiosity for geygey (suspended -> pre-2019 likely unrecoverable anyway). RECHECK when vultr recovers: jq -r .utc /data/x/people/1096932140665786369/timeline.ndjson | sort | head, and the global min across people/*/timeline.ndjson.")
inl note_of_x_dumps_55 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 55u32, "2026-11-05T06:07Z", "x-dumps", "geygey tail question RESOLVED 2026-11-05 (vultr recovered, load 0.39 - tibia capped its loop; verified against source). The 2012-04-24 world-span start is a SINGLE geygey_hadid post that is LOST (\"no tweet text in the archived page\") - it IS in the data and correctly in the 4860 unrecoverable index, but the UI lost-filter hides it, which is why the served tail showed 2019-09 as oldest SURVIVING. geygey year distribution: 1 (2012, lost), 16 (2019), 2773 (2020), 24584 (2021), 12237 (2022), 168 (2023), 23218 (2024), 39960 (2025), 22571 (2026) - so the 2012->2019 gap is REAL (isolated early tombstone then dormancy), NOT a missed harvest window. Global earliest non-lost = 2017 retweets by 72784485. CONCLUSION: UI tail correct, lost-filter correct, index correct, no completeness gap. Closes the earlier UNVERIFIED flag.")
inl delphi_case_insensitive_names () = Task ("DELPHI-CASE-INSENSITIVE-NAMES", "Delphi backend: identifiers that differ only by case collide (Pascal is case-insensitive); blocks the generated NF-e Delphi build", TaskKind, P1, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "compiler,delphi,backend,fiscal", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_delphi_case_insensitive_names () = Planning ("DELPHI-CASE-INSENSITIVE-NAMES", Minutes 120u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_delphi_case_insensitive_names_1 () = Note ("DELPHI-CASE-INSENSITIVE-NAMES", 1u32, "2026-11-01T18:58Z", "fiscal", "Found by fiscal 2026-11-01 (FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH node run job-1793559200758, t1101, core DBC5EC18F14C, fpc 3.3.1 -Px86_64 -Mdelphi). Spiral identifiers are case-sensitive; Pascal identifiers are NOT. The Delphi backend emits union cases verbatim, so two cases that differ only by case collide: generated_nfe_v4 has TEnderEmi_xPais_Brasil and TEnderEmi_xPais_BRASIL (the NF-e 4.00 XSD enumerates both \"Brasil\" and \"BRASIL\", leiauteNFe_v4.00.xsd:7177-7178), and fpc stops with \"main.pas(2316,10) Error: Overloaded functions have the same parameter list / main.pas(2312,10) Found declaration: US34_TEnderEmi_xPais_Brasil:TUS34\". Scope in fiscal: 27 collisions in 9 generated units (6 CR units x 4, e.g. UnidadMedidaType_Cm/cm and _h/_H, which are distinct units of measure, so they must stay distinct; nfe v2/v3.10/v4 x 1). Minimal repro: a union with cases Foo and FOO, matched on Delphi. Fix in the backend: mangle every emitted Pascal identifier so names unique under case folding (e.g. suffix a stable hash or index on fold collisions), for union cases AND functions/records. Done = the repro sample agrees on Delphi, and generated_nfe_v4 builds on Delphi with no rename. Regression sample allowed (one per fixed compiler bug).")
inl note_of_delphi_case_insensitive_names_2 () = Note ("DELPHI-CASE-INSENSITIVE-NAMES", 2u32, "2026-11-01T20:48Z", "compiler", "11-01 16:52 compiler: FIX DRAFTED, REPRO LANDED. REPRO: samples/delphi_case_folded_union_cases (unstaged): union shout = Foo | FOO | Bar, matched; expected exit 11. Installed compiler: C exits 11, while the Delphi output declares US0_Foo and US0_FOO, and fpc stops with 'main.pas(12,10) Error: Overloaded functions have the same parameter list / Found declaration: US0_Foo'. That is the NF-e Brasil/BRASIL shape in 15 lines. FIX: %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\spiral\\DELPHI-CASE-INSENSITIVE-NAMES\\1-pascal-case-folded-names.kitdo, 3 replacements. A shared caseFoldedSuffixes (next to generatedCaseSuffixes) appends the case's position to every suffix that has a twin under ToLowerInvariant inside the same union, so Foo/FOO become Foo_0/FOO_1 and UnidadMedidaType Cm/cm stay distinct. codegenDelphi applies it at both places a case identifier is spelled: the constructor call (codegenDelphi's union-box return) and union_type's declarations. Suffixes without a twin are untouched, so every other Delphi residual stays byte-identical. SCOPE NOTE: only union case identifiers can collide this way. Functions are method{tag}/closure names carrying unique tags, and records are TMut/THeap{tag}. TYPEFEATURESET: n/a (one pure function over the existing suffix array). It rides pack15b, rebuilding now with the GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS, PrimToString and DELPHI-TO-PASCAL step-1 programs; all 5 stack cleanly. DONE = the repro agrees with C on Delphi, plus generated_nfe_v4 builds on Delphi (fiscal's bench, after install).")
inl note_of_delphi_case_insensitive_names_3 () = Note ("DELPHI-CASE-INSENSITIVE-NAMES", 3u32, "2026-11-02T17:15Z", "compiler", "11-02 13:15 compiler: the compiler fix is INSTALLED. caseFoldedSuffixes (spiral_compiler.fs:12248) is in the live core and in the installed core DLL, shipped in an earlier pack (15c/15d), and the drafted program no longer applies because its target text is already live. Regression row green on the installed compiler: samples/delphi_case_folded_union_cases exits 11 on Fsharp, C, Delphi and Rust (oracle agree 3, DISAGREE 0; samples run on Delphi by default). Remaining: fiscal's consumer proof (the NF-e generated_nfe_v4 Delphi build with TEnderEmi_xPais_Brasil/BRASIL); close when it builds.")
inl note_of_delphi_case_insensitive_names_4 () = Note ("DELPHI-CASE-INSENSITIVE-NAMES", 4u32, "2026-11-02T17:35Z", "fiscal", "fiscal 11-02 13:35 CONSUMER PROOF GREEN, item DONE. Node mirror t1102 (spiral 194a1916, fiscal 9093af3), installed compiler core F16F6B2032F81F2D (node = box), fpc 3.3.1 -Mdelphi. job-1793640643975-fiscal-delphi-casefold3: generated_nfe_v4 with TEnderEmi_xPais_Brasil AND TEnderEmi_xPais_BRASIL untouched (4 uses each; harness change only: the bench cell target set to delphi) -> generated_nfe_v4_writes_its_example passed 1/1, type errors 0, Delphi bench row 'generated_nfe_v4 parse_us 3171 write_us 619'. Before the fix (11-01, job-1793559200758) fpc stopped with 'Overloaded functions have the same parameter list'. Note: the node's Cells runner spiral.exe is older than the box's (F1DD2E34, 10-30, against 9A99BF53, 11-02); the compiler DLL is identical.")
inl node_mix_priv_link () = Task ("NODE-MIX-PRIV-LINK", "Remove the Administrators-owned symlink on the node, C:\\Users\\alias\\AppData\\Local\\spiral-bin\\mix-builds\\lib-gate\\lib\\spiral_cells\\priv -> C:\\home\\git\\spiral\\apps\\cells\\priv (made 10-25 17:26). Every lib_gate that has to repoint it fails with '** (Mix) Cannot remove symlink ... not owner'; then give node lib gates a per-mirror MIX_BUILD_PATH", TaskKind, P1, D1, 100u32, Done, "", "node,cells,mix,main,team:main,user-step", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_node_mix_priv_link () = Planning ("NODE-MIX-PRIV-LINK", Minutes 15u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_node_mix_priv_link_1 () = Note ("NODE-MIX-PRIV-LINK", 1u32, "2026-11-01T19:25Z", "main", "main 15:3x, split from NODE-SHARED-CHECKOUT-STALE n4/n5. USER STEP, because the link is admin-owned and agents don't change system security settings: on the node, from an elevated shell, run Remove-Item on that priv link (the link only, not its target). After that, lib_gate's own repoint works. Agent half: lib_gate already honours -BuildPath, and copy gates derive a per-copy MIX_BUILD_PATH (lib_gate.ps1:26); node jobs should pass -BuildPath inside their mirror. DoD: a node lib_gate from a mirror passes the mix step twice in a row. Typefeatureset: n/a (a one-off host fix).")
inl note_of_node_mix_priv_link_2 () = Note ("NODE-MIX-PRIV-LINK", 2u32, "2026-11-02T14:02Z", "x", "2026-11-02 09:59 x: DECISION RECORD (typed; groom of user waits, user 11-02: 'decisions waiting on me get met autonomously/deterministically with tests/benchs/typefeatureset modeling/backups'). kind=ByDefault. QUESTION: Remove the Administrators-owned priv symlink on the node from an elevated shell. RULE (written before acting): Default is to work around the link and not ask for elevation. Node lib gates pass -BuildPath inside their own mirror (lib_gate.ps1:26 already derives a per-copy MIX_BUILD_PATH), so no gate repoints the shared lib-gate build dir and the admin-owned link is never touched. DoD from n1: a node lib_gate from a mirror passes the mix step twice in a row. Removing the stray link stays an optional elevated cleanup, not a blocker. BACKUP/UNDO: Nothing is deleted; the old shared build dir stays as is OWNER: main. NEXT: Make node jobs pass -BuildPath inside their mirror, run the DoD twice, and reassign from User to Agent. VETO: the user can reverse this at any time; the backup above is the undo.")
inl note_of_node_mix_priv_link_3 () = Note ("NODE-MIX-PRIV-LINK", 3u32, "2026-11-02T21:58Z", "main", "main 11-02 18:0x DoD MET (x's ByDefault record n2: work around the link, no elevation). The node's live lib_gate.ps1 ran with -Lib on mirror C:\\home\\remote\\exportcheck\\spiral\\lib\\spiral -File reflection.spi, TWICE in a row: run 1 exit 0 (11/11 passed), run 2 exit 0 (11/11). Get-CopyGateSettings put MIX_BUILD_PATH at <mirror>\\mix-build-lib-gate (it exists) plus a per-copy port, so no gate repoints the shared lib-gate build dir, and no 'Cannot remove symlink ... not owner' appeared. RULE for node lib gates: run the LIVE lib_gate.ps1 with -Lib <mirror lib>; the mirror's own copy with its default -Lib is NOT a copy to itself and falls back to the shared dir. Removing the admin-owned link stays an optional elevated cleanup (user, any time).")
inl lib_erlang_typed_stdlib () = Task ("LIB-ERLANG-TYPED-STDLIB", "lib/spiral: typed wrappers for the Erlang stdlib calls consumers make raw (uri_string, string, crypto, gen_tcp, monotonic_time), so charlist/binary mismatches and missing functions fail at compile time instead of at boot", Story, P1, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "team:x,lib,beam,typefeatures,sim", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_lib_erlang_typed_stdlib () = Planning ("LIB-ERLANG-TYPED-STDLIB", Minutes 180u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_lib_erlang_typed_stdlib_1 () = Note ("LIB-ERLANG-TYPED-STDLIB", 1u32, "2026-11-01T19:25Z", "x", "2026-11-01 15:25 x: from the user's /btw question (11-01 ~15:2x: how much more rewarding Spiral is than Elixir on the BEAM). Answer: ~300/1000 today, ~700 potential. The largest single lift (+150) is that Spiral's types stop where raw Erlang calls begin. Evidence from x's sim (SIM-X-SERVE-WRITES n6): uri_string:quote needs a CHARLIST but got a binary (badarg at boot), and string:pad_leading doesn't exist. Both compiled and crashed only at runtime. SCOPE: census the raw erlang/erlang_term calls in x/apps/sim/spi (cells_executor clock_ms, gen_tcp, uri_string, string, crypto for the OAuth HMAC), then lib wrappers with Spiral types (string in, string out, charlist conversion inside), each with a test block on gleam. DoD: x's sim makes no raw Erlang stdlib call outside lib, and a deliberately wrong argument type fails to compile. Related: LIB-OTP-ACTORS (typed OTP), KINO-SOTA (warm compiles, the other +150).")
inl note_of_lib_erlang_typed_stdlib_2 () = Note ("LIB-ERLANG-TYPED-STDLIB", 2u32, "2026-11-01T19:27Z", "x", "2026-11-01 15:27 x: CENSUS (read-only). x's sim makes 88 raw Erlang calls: 50 'erlang_term::apply \"m\" \"f\"' sites in cells_executor.spi and simframes.spi, and 38 through sim_serve.spi's 'call m f args' helper, plus 'tcp name args' (gen_tcp with the function name as a string). By module family:\n- maps (~22: get, put, new, from_list, keys, values, is_key)\n- erlang BIFs (~20: element, length, byte_size, is_tuple/map/binary, iolist_to_binary, integer_to_binary, monotonic_time, memory, max, div, put)\n- json encode/decode (4), uri_string quote/percent_decode (3, the charlist bug), string trim/pad (2, the pad_leading bug), crypto mac (1, OAuth HMAC)\n- file/filename/filelib (7), io:put_chars (4), lists (8), unicode (3), counters (3), application/code/init (4), inet:port (1)\n- Elixir.Spiral.Cells.* (5), which go away with CELLS-IN-SPIRAL\nLib already has erlang_term, crypto, json, erlang_port, erlang_process and otp_*. It has no maps, uri or file wrappers, and no lib module calls maps, uri_string, string or filename raw.\nORDER by bug risk x reach: (1) uri + string wrappers (both runtime bugs came from here); (2) a typed 'erl_map k v' over maps (the largest family; the get/put signatures carry the key and value types); (3) BIFs with fixed types; (4) file/io. Each slice migrates its x call sites in the same change (typefeatureset rule: touched legacy migrates).")
inl note_of_lib_erlang_typed_stdlib_3 () = Note ("LIB-ERLANG-TYPED-STDLIB", 3u32, "2026-11-01T20:24Z", "x", "2026-11-01 16:24 x: SLICE 1 DRAFTED at its final path: spiral/lib/spiral/erlang_text.spi (new, unstaged, NOT yet in lib's package.spiproj). It provides percent_encoded / percent_decoded (uri_string; the charlist Safe argument is built inside), padded_leading width fill s (string:pad, with the fill string turned into the charlist grapheme it needs, plus iolist_to_binary), and trimmed_trailing. 4 gleam test blocks, with the vectors measured on the box in SIM-X-SERVE-WRITES n6 (a%20b%2Bc%2F~, 0000000004). PROOF x-erlang-text2 (node mirror xlib1, where erlang_text is registered after erlang_term in the mirror's package.spiproj only), rule written before the run: PASS iff lib_gate -File erlang_text.spi reports 4 executed, 4 passed, 0 failed/unsupported. x-erlang-text1 measured nothing (a fresh mirror whose first sync stopped before deps, with no REMOTE-SYNC-OK line), so it is VOID. NEXT after PASS: register it in live lib through lib_land_window with the consumer gate, then migrate sim_serve's pct/unescaped/url_decoded/zero_padded/trimmed_end to it in the same change.")
inl note_of_lib_erlang_typed_stdlib_4 () = Note ("LIB-ERLANG-TYPED-STDLIB", 4u32, "2026-11-01T20:28Z", "x", "2026-11-01 16:28 x: x-erlang-text2 is VOID (job-1793564632604, 169 s, exit 2). Nothing ran: lib_gate reported 'no spiral-tests total', and Cells crashed before any test with 'UndefinedFunctionError Spiral.Cells.Lisp.expand/2 (module not available)' right after 'Compiling 44 files (.ex)' in the fresh mirror xlib1's first copy-gate build (mix-build-lib-gate). lisp.ex is tracked and unchanged in the live tree, so this is the cold-mirror build, not erlang_text. Rerun with the same rule in xqueue3, whose Cells build is warm and proven today (3 node proofs), once x-cells-c3-pre1 frees it.")
inl note_of_lib_erlang_typed_stdlib_5 () = Note ("LIB-ERLANG-TYPED-STDLIB", 5u32, "2026-11-01T20:30Z", "x", "2026-11-01 16:30 x: SLICE 2 DRAFTED at its final path: spiral/lib/spiral/erlang_map.spi (new, unstaged, not registered). It adds ':! erl_map k v = erlang_term::term', a phantom-typed nominal over a BEAM map, with map_new, map_put, map_has, map_get (returns 'option v'; maps:is_key then maps:get, so a missing key is None, not a badkey crash), map_size and map_of_pairs. Key and value types are fixed by the signature, so a put with the wrong value type doesn't compile. 3 gleam tests. PROOF x-erlang-libs1 (mirror xqueue3, after x-cells-c3-pre1 frees it and a re-sync; both modules registered in the mirror only, and package.spiproj restored after), rule written before the run: PASS iff lib_gate reports every test executed and passed with 0 failed/unsupported for BOTH files (erlang_text 4/4, erlang_map 3/3). A gate with no 'spiral-tests total' line is VOID, not red.")
inl note_of_lib_erlang_typed_stdlib_6 () = Note ("LIB-ERLANG-TYPED-STDLIB", 6u32, "2026-11-01T20:39Z", "x", "2026-11-01 16:39 x: n5's proof runs on VULTR (Linux, idle; main 15:3x) instead of waiting for xqueue3: x-erlang-libs1 (job-1793565556015, mirror /home/remote/xlib synced REMOTE-SYNC-OK at digest 47F55F628455E38D, with both modules registered in the mirror only). n5's rule is unchanged (erlang_text 4/4, erlang_map 3/3, a run with no spiral-tests total is VOID). It is also x's first Linux lib gate.")
inl note_of_lib_erlang_typed_stdlib_7 () = Note ("LIB-ERLANG-TYPED-STDLIB", 7u32, "2026-11-01T20:52Z", "x", "2026-11-01 16:52 x: SLICE 3 DRAFTED at its final path: spiral/lib/spiral/erlang_file.spi (new, unstaged, not registered). It covers the sim's file/io family (7 sites): dir_name, base_name, absolute_path, joined_path (filename, results normalized through unicode:characters_to_binary so a charlist never leaks out), file_written (write_file =:= ok, as a bool), matching_paths (filelib:wildcard over a charlist pattern, each match back to a binary string), and put_text (io:put_chars). 3 gleam tests (dir/base split, join separator, absname keeps its base). PROOF x-erlang-file1 on Vultr after x-erlang-libs1 (the VM has 2 vCPU, so one gate at a time). Rule written before the run: PASS iff lib_gate -File erlang_file.spi reports 3 executed, 3 passed, 0 failed/unsupported. A run with no spiral-tests total is VOID.")
inl note_of_lib_erlang_typed_stdlib_8 () = Note ("LIB-ERLANG-TYPED-STDLIB", 8u32, "2026-11-01T20:54Z", "x", "2026-11-01 16:54 x: x-erlang-libs1 (Vultr, job-1793565556015, 814 s) by n5's rule. erlang_text: PASS ('tests: passed 4 failed 0 of 4', type errors 0), so slice 1 is proven on Linux. erlang_map: NOT MET ('passed 0 failed 3 of 3', every test 'gleam failed', type errors 0), so it typechecks in Spiral and fails at Gleam compile or run time. Being read with lib_gate -Detail (x-erlang-map-detail, job-1793566462933). Side finding: lib_gate exited 0 with 3 failed tests, so its exit code isn't the verdict and the 'tests: passed N failed M' line is (n5's rule already reads it). It goes on the item that owns lib_gate if it's unintended.")
inl note_of_lib_erlang_typed_stdlib_9 () = Note ("LIB-ERLANG-TYPED-STDLIB", 9u32, "2026-11-01T21:01Z", "x", "2026-11-01 17:01 x: x-erlang-map-detail (Vultr, 143 s): erlang_map is now 2 of 3. The first run's 3/3 failure looks like the fresh mirror's cold Gleam build. CAUSE of the remaining failure (pairs_become_one_entry_per_key): a Spiral 'list' is a Spiral union on Gleam, not a native Erlang list, so 'erlang_term::of pairs' handed maps:from_list a custom-type value. FIX: map_of_pairs builds erlang_term::list_term (listm::map pair_term pairs), with pair_term = erlang_term::tuple [of k; of v]. The same trap was in erlang_file's untested matching_paths (it cast filelib:wildcard's native list to a Spiral list), now fixed with items_of (lists:nth over erlang:length) plus text_of, and a new wildcard test. file_written uses erlang_term::equal. LESSON for every wrapper: crossing into Erlang, lists go through list_term and tuples through tuple; coming back, native lists need items_of. PROOF x-erlang-libs2 (Vultr, re-synced), rule written before the run: PASS iff erlang_map 'passed 3 failed 0 of 3' and erlang_file 'passed 4 failed 0 of 4', type errors 0.")
inl note_of_lib_erlang_typed_stdlib_10 () = Note ("LIB-ERLANG-TYPED-STDLIB", 10u32, "2026-11-01T21:04Z", "x", "2026-11-01 17:04 x: x-erlang-libs2 (Vultr, 65 s) by n9's rule: PASS. erlang_map 'passed 3 failed 0 of 3' and erlang_file 'passed 4 failed 0 of 4', type errors 0. With n8 (erlang_text 4/4), all three modules are proven on Linux Gleam.\nLAND PLAN: register them in live lib through lib_land_window with the consumer gate. Draft %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\spiral\\LIB-ERLANG-TYPED-STDLIB\\package.spiproj (40E9BABABE40): live (base 7B160B4F51FE) plus 3 lines after erlang_term, nothing else. Command: lib_land_window.ps1 -Draft package.spiproj=<draft> -BaseHash 7B160B4F51FE -Label x-erlang-libs -Apply. It runs on the box, so it waits until kit window list is clear (17:0x: compiler heavy-build, 179 min left).\nTHEN, in the same x change, migrate sim_serve: url_decoded/unescaped -> erlang_text::percent_decoded, pct -> percent_encoded, zero_padded -> padded_leading 10i32 \"0\", trimmed_end -> trimmed_trailing, plus dirname/write_file/put_chars -> erlang_file. Maps -> erl_map is a later, typed slice. The proof is x's next verify.")
inl note_of_lib_erlang_typed_stdlib_11 () = Note ("LIB-ERLANG-TYPED-STDLIB", 11u32, "2026-11-01T21:46Z", "x", "2026-11-01 17:46 x: x MIGRATION READY in the store: %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\spiral\\LIB-ERLANG-TYPED-STDLIB\\apply-sim-serve.ps1 -Sim <x/apps/sim/spi> [-Apply]. It refuses unless sim_serve.spi = A18631C29BBC. It moves 6 sites: url_decoded and unescaped to erlang_text::percent_decoded, pct to percent_encoded, zero_padded's 3-line pad + iolist_to_binary to padded_leading 10i32 \"0\", trimmed_end to trimmed_trailing, and dir_of to erlang_file::dir_name. Dry run on the live tree finds all anchors once. ORDER: after x-verify47's outputs land, so sim sources don't change under its manifest, and after n10's lib registration lands. Proof: x's next verify. NOT covered yet, and a follow-up erlang_file slice: write_file with [append] (sim_serve:406) and io:put_chars(standard_error, ...) (:902), which need file_appended and put_error_text wrappers.")
inl note_of_lib_erlang_typed_stdlib_12 () = Note ("LIB-ERLANG-TYPED-STDLIB", 12u32, "2026-11-01T21:59Z", "x", "2026-11-01 17:59 x: correction to n9's side finding: lib_gate.ps1 now ends with 'if ($j.failed -gt 0) { exit 1 }' (live tree, unstaged, not in 73e4d863 or earlier), so a gate with failed tests exits 1 from now on. My 16:5x Vultr runs predate it, which is why they printed exit 0 with 3 failures. Gate heal A5 is done (by whoever landed it after main's 17:4x ask).")
inl note_of_lib_erlang_typed_stdlib_13 () = Note ("LIB-ERLANG-TYPED-STDLIB", 13u32, "2026-11-01T23:04Z", "x", "2026-11-01 19:04 x: erlang_file grows the 2 wrappers the sim_serve migration lacked: file_appended (write_file with [append], sim_serve:406) and put_error_text (io:put_chars to standard_error, sim_serve:902), plus file_text (read_file element 2) for the test. A 5th test writes \"a\", appends \"b\" and reads \"ab\" back, under filename:basedir(user_cache), with sequential binds so no tuple evaluation order is relied on. PROOF x-erlang-file2 (Vultr, run2.ps1 = lib_gate -File erlang_file.spi), rule written before the run: PASS iff 'passed 5 failed 0 of 5', type errors 0. After it passes, apply-sim-serve.ps1 grows the 2 sites (a new base hash in the same change).")
inl note_of_lib_erlang_typed_stdlib_14 () = Note ("LIB-ERLANG-TYPED-STDLIB", 14u32, "2026-11-01T23:22Z", "x", "2026-11-01 19:22 x (groom): DEDUP: LIB-MOVE-X-SIM-ERLANG (main, 18:51) owns switching x's remaining call sites and depends on this item. This item's DoD narrows to: the 3 modules REGISTERED in live lib through lib_land_window (n10) after a final gate on their re-laid final text, plus sim_serve's 6-site switch (n11, apply-sim-serve.ps1, to be extended with the 2 new wrappers). TYPEFEATURESET PLAN: erl_map k v is a phantom-typed nominal (key and value types checked at compile time). The text and file wrappers keep string/charlist conversion inside, so callers never see a term.")
inl note_of_lib_erlang_typed_stdlib_15 () = Note ("LIB-ERLANG-TYPED-STDLIB", 15u32, "2026-11-01T23:42Z", "x", "2026-11-01 19:42 x: FINAL GATE on the re-laid text, x-erlang-final2 (Vultr, 144 s, REMOTE-SYNC-OK at 97B0575E7CEF0239): erlang_text B0AFD99722C6 4/4, erlang_map CB36CCF99F93 3/3, erlang_file 7E0C24EC2B73 5/5, type errors 0, exit 0 each, and every hash equals the local file. x-erlang-final1 is VOID (its sync failed on a reach error, and map/file hashed to the pre-re-layout text), which is caught because each gate prints the hash it tested. READY: n10's registration land runs when the box window clears (~20:03). The draft package.spiproj is unchanged (40E9BABABE40, base 7B160B4F51FE).")
inl note_of_lib_erlang_typed_stdlib_16 () = Note ("LIB-ERLANG-TYPED-STDLIB", 16u32, "2026-11-01T23:43Z", "x", "2026-11-01 19:43 x: RE-BASED the registration draft. Live lib package.spiproj moved to 37FC507F2ADA (b3fd292b added wire, fixed_views and case_labels). The draft is rebuilt from it as live + the same 3 lines after erlang_term (7C2E3FD5AD44). The land command uses -BaseHash 37FC507F2ADA.")
inl note_of_lib_erlang_typed_stdlib_17 () = Note ("LIB-ERLANG-TYPED-STDLIB", 17u32, "2026-11-01T23:57Z", "x", "2026-11-01 19:57 x: RE-BASED again after main's R1a lib reshape (19:54): live package.spiproj 9D730998B805, draft 1269095979F8 (live + the same 3 lines). erlang_text/map/file reference none of R1a's renamed modules (0 hits for am'_prototypes, sm'_prototypes, optionm'_operators, par'). The land waits for the box: compiler heavy-build pack15c holds it until ~20:30 (gate heal A1 in main's 17:4x ask: landings shouldn't need a box compile).")
inl note_of_lib_erlang_typed_stdlib_18 () = Note ("LIB-ERLANG-TYPED-STDLIB", 18u32, "2026-11-02T04:57Z", "x", "2026-11-02 00:57 x: HELD SITES UNBLOCKED (has_key/field_or). Draft in drafts\\spiral\\LIB-ERLANG-TYPED-STDLIB: (1) lib erlang_map.spi 6C526CD73B4A (base CB36CCF99F93) adds map_view (a typed view of a map term built elsewhere, for JSON-decoded maps) and map_get_or (maps:get/3 with a typed fallback), plus 2 test blocks. (2) x sim_serve.spi moves 9 helpers onto it through aliases fields = erl_map string term and numbers = erl_map string i32: field, items_at, has_key, field_or, with_field (replaces 2 maps:put sites), page_size_of, status_of (every caller is guarded by has_key, so the 0 fallback is unreachable), empty_map, and stored_map (binary keys = map_of_pairs exactly). Left on purpose: map_of builds ATOM-keyed maps for encoding, and binary keys could reorder the JSON output of maps over 32 keys; maps:keys at :534 waits for a native-list map_keys. RULES, written before the runs: x-erl-map1 (Vultr lib_gate erlang_map.spi) PASS iff 5 executed, 5 passed, 0 failed/unsupported. x-sim-map1 (node, after verify49; sim build + client + e2e on mirror xqueue3 with both drafts) PASS iff sim build exit 0, client build exit 0, sim exit 0, and the same 153 requests to the sim as verify48b. Land: lib first (tree-write window, hash-guarded; document_test as the closing step per main 11-02), then x sim_serve.")
inl note_of_lib_erlang_typed_stdlib_19 () = Note ("LIB-ERLANG-TYPED-STDLIB", 19u32, "2026-11-02T05:15Z", "x", "2026-11-02 01:15 x: x-erl-map3 (Vultr, now on pack15c rebuilt from source by main) PASSES its pre-registered rule. A/A on the same mirror: base CB36CCF99F93 passes 3/3 (it failed 0/3 on the stale compiler, so the gary failure is gone), and draft 6C526CD73B4A passes 5/5 with 0 type errors. Vultr lib gates count again. Open: x-sim-map1 (node, the sim_serve side) runs after x-verify50.")
inl note_of_lib_erlang_typed_stdlib_20 () = Note ("LIB-ERLANG-TYPED-STDLIB", 20u32, "2026-11-02T09:16Z", "x", "2026-11-02 05:15 x: erlang_map LANDED on the box live lib (6C526CD73B4A over CB36CCF99F93), unstaged, hash-guarded inside a tree-write window with no box compile. TRAIN10 reads its own -Snapshot lib, so it isn't disturbed. PROOF: x-lib-land4 (node, mirror xlib1, core F16F6B2032F81F2D), lib_land_window -Apply on the exact base: '14 signatures unchanged', apps/spiral consumer compiles, document_test 'Result: 22 passed', and type_probe PASS (5 typed, 0 errors), so 'LANDED and closed green'. Lands 1-3 failed on the mirror environment, not the draft: (1) the node's kit is C:\\home\\bin\\kit.exe, so lib_land_window.ps1 now falls back to kit on PATH (unstaged); (2) document_test needs dice and alphabet beside spiral (-Repos spiral,dice,alphabet); (3) it also needs a built workspace\\target\\release\\spiral.exe (staged from the box). Next: x-sim-map1 (node, running) proves the x sim_serve.spi draft that uses map_view/map_get_or, then x lands it.")
inl note_of_lib_erlang_typed_stdlib_21 () = Note ("LIB-ERLANG-TYPED-STDLIB", 21u32, "2026-11-02T09:58Z", "x", "2026-11-02 05:58 x: map_keys slice LANDED (unstaged), after the sim_serve slice that landed at 05:24 (x-sim-map1: sim/client/sim exit 0, 153 requests, RUST-OK 37+5; 11 frames parts landed with source 4464A28833EC). This slice: erlang_term gains items_of (native list to list term, O(n): reverse once, then hd/tl), replacing two O(n^2) lists:nth converters, erlang_file.items_of (removed) and x sim_serve.terms_of (now delegates). erlang_map gains map_keys (list k from the phantom-typed view), and sim_serve.kept_media uses it in place of raw maps:keys. Hashes: erlang_term FAEF00C04AF9, erlang_map B1AF6C279BA7, erlang_file 7F0087E74FA1, sim_serve E33D67AEA851. PROOFS on node, core F16F6B2032F81F2D. x-lib-keys2 (lib_land_window on the exact bases): 14 signatures unchanged, consumer compiles, document_test 22 passed, type_probe 3/3 PASS (7/6/5 typed). x-lib-keys1 failed honestly first: items_from sat above equal/apply (define-before-use) and was moved. x-sim-keys1 (runtime consumer path): sim build, client build and sim exit 0, 153 requests, RUST-OK 37+5; its 5 frames parts landed with 0 conflicts. Open: map_values for cells_executor.spi:77 (raw maps:values); map_of (atom keys) stays.")
inl note_of_lib_erlang_typed_stdlib_22 () = Note ("LIB-ERLANG-TYPED-STDLIB", 22u32, "2026-11-03T00:59Z", "x", "2026-11-02 20:59 x: map_values slice drafted, riding the LIB-MOVE-UNIX-CLOCK lib land (one node cycle). lib erlang_map gains map_values (list v from the phantom-typed view, items_of then cast; draft 044E4F6C464D on B1AF6C279BA7) with its own block values_are_every_value_once. In x apps/sim/spi/cells_executor.spi, memo_counted reads erlang_map::map_values over map_view (erl_map term term), and flag_count takes list term and folds, so the raw maps:values, lists:filter and erlang:length term calls are gone; the test builds a Spiral list. RULE: x-lib-clock1 now also gates erlang_map.spi (lib_gate exit 0, executed > 0); the executor change is proven by x-e2e-quote-clock1 (sim build exit 0, e2e request count 153 and RUST-OK as on x-sim-keys1).")
inl note_of_lib_erlang_typed_stdlib_23 () = Note ("LIB-ERLANG-TYPED-STDLIB", 23u32, "2026-11-03T03:17Z", "x", "2026-11-02 23:17 x: map_values LANDED with the unix_clock land (erlang_map.spi 708AB4996ABD; block values_are_every_value_once 7/7 with the rest of erlang_map's blocks on x-lib-clock3). Its consumer (cells_executor memo flags) rides x-e2e-quote-clock1.")
inl note_of_lib_erlang_typed_stdlib_24 () = Note ("LIB-ERLANG-TYPED-STDLIB", 24u32, "2026-11-03T04:31Z", "x", "2026-11-03 00:31 x: map_values' consumer LANDED: cells_executor memo_flags reads erlang_map::map_values (9742F928099C), proven in x-e2e-quote-clock4 (sim accept and build exit 0, e2e RUST-OK). The raw maps:values, lists:filter and erlang:length calls are gone from the sim executor.")
inl note_of_lib_erlang_typed_stdlib_25 () = Note ("LIB-ERLANG-TYPED-STDLIB", 25u32, "2026-11-03T12:34Z", "x", "2026-11-03 08:34 x: one more typed wrapper drafted for a real consumer: erlang_file regular_file (path : string) : bool over filelib:is_regular, with a gleam test (an existing file, a missing one, a dir). Consumer: Cells manifest_path (CELLS-MANIFEST-SOURCEMAP-SPIRAL n8, kit drafts spiral\\CELLS-MANIFEST-PATH); it lands with that slice after x-cells-mpath1.")
inl note_of_lib_erlang_typed_stdlib_26 () = Note ("LIB-ERLANG-TYPED-STDLIB", 26u32, "2026-11-03T15:32Z", "x", "2026-11-03 11:32 x: TYPED PORT RUNS drafted (kit drafts spiral\\LIB-ERLANG-TYPED-STDLIB\\erlang_port.spi, base 235A7A31FF76), the dependency CELLS-PORT-TARGETS step (1) waits on. New closed unions port_event = Output of string | Exited of i32 | Quiet and run_outcome = Finished of (i32 * string) | TimedOut of string. event_of maps one received message (data, exit_status, or nothing within the wait) to a port_event; run_collect folds a port's messages until its exit, or until quiet_left one-second silences pass, into a run_outcome; erl_eval_port opens a binary, exit_status port on 'erl -noshell -eval'. Gleam block: a port running io:put_chars(\\\"hi\\\"), halt(3) collects to Finished (3, \\\"hi\\\"). Gated in x-cells-mpath1 alongside erlang_file and manifest (rule extended: erlang_port.spi blocks pass, 0 failed).")
inl note_of_lib_erlang_typed_stdlib_27 () = Note ("LIB-ERLANG-TYPED-STDLIB", 27u32, "2026-11-03T20:42Z", "x", "2026-11-03 16:42 x: NEW MODULE drafted for X-SOTA-MODEL's sim publisher (kit drafts spiral\\LIB-ERLANG-TYPED-STDLIB\\erlang_http.spi, 479D224C37C4): http_outcome = Replied of (i32 * string) | Unreachable of string over OTP httpc (inets and ssl started on demand). posted_json url headers body ca_file sends application/json with ssl {verify, verify_peer} and {cacertfile, ca_file} (an empty ca_file means plain http), a 10 s timeout and a binary body. outcome_of reads {ok, {{_, Status, _}, _, Body}} through erlang_term (is_tuple, element_at), and anything else is Unreachable with the inspected term. Small single-expression cells (ok_tagged, status_of, replied_of). Gleam block: a post to the closed port 127.0.0.1:9 is Unreachable (the offline error path; the reply path is proven by the sim-to-iggy e2e). Registration: lib package.spiproj gets 'erlang_http' after 'erlang_port' as an anchored one-line insert at land (the file carries another lane's WIP). Gated in x-cells-mpath1, which registers it in the node mirror the same way, alongside erlang_file and erlang_port.")
inl note_of_lib_erlang_typed_stdlib_28 () = Note ("LIB-ERLANG-TYPED-STDLIB", 28u32, "2026-11-04T04:48Z", "x", "2026-11-04 00:48 x: CONSUMER RULE learned from x-cells-mpath1: lib's erlang_term, erlang_process, file_system, crypto, platform and sm' Gleam arms import gleam/erlang/atom, so ANY Gleam project that reaches them needs gleam_erlang in gleam.toml AND manifest.toml. A Spiral global import line can't declare the package; the build then fails with 'Unknown module atom' at the call sites, although the import line IS emitted. If the project loads ebins by hand (Cells domain.ex ensure!), it must also add gleam_erlang's ebin, or the runtime fails on gleam@erlang@atom. CENSUS of every gleam.toml under C:\\home\\git: Cells was the only Erlang-target project without gleam_erlang (fixed in x-cells-mpath2's drafts); near/wallet lacks it too but targets javascript, where these arms never apply. x's sim frames/split, singularity's playground and the root project already have it. Before any lib move that adds a gleam/erlang import, rerun this census.")
inl note_of_lib_erlang_typed_stdlib_29 () = Note ("LIB-ERLANG-TYPED-STDLIB", 29u32, "2026-11-04T05:09Z", "x", "2026-11-04 01:09 x: LANDED in lib (lib_land_window, closed green: consumer spiral.spi compiles, document_test 22 passed, type_probe 3/3 PASS, 0 respell drift; lib digest A5F9B975989B970C): erlang_port E0097B434833... (run_collect -> run_outcome Finished | TimedOut) A5661C6FE3E5, erlang_http 479D224C37C4 (new; posted_json -> Replied of i32*string | Unreachable of string), registered in lib package.spiproj DF7923212DBB; erlang_file E0097B434833 (regular_file). Proven first in x-cells-mpath2 (14/14 spiral-tests, Cells mix 48 passed). COVERAGE of the five named calls: all have typed lib wrappers now (crypto.spi, erlang_tcp for gen_tcp, erlang_text for uri_string and string, date_time for monotonic_time). REMAINDER (the last 10%, measured): consumers still make 4 raw calls that bypass them, all in x's sim: cells_executor.spi:113 tcp = apply gen_tcp; sim_serve.spi:31 tcp = call gen_tcp; sim_serve.spi:45 call inet port; sim_serve.spi:196 call crypto mac (hmac sha). Next: switch those to erlang_tcp, inet port via erlang_tcp and crypto.spi's hmac, in a sim slice after x-next5 lands (it holds both files).")
inl note_of_lib_erlang_typed_stdlib_30 () = Note ("LIB-ERLANG-TYPED-STDLIB", 30u32, "2026-11-04T05:09Z", "x", "2026-11-04 01:09 x: CORRECTION to my previous note's hashes: erlang_port.spi = A5661C6FE3E5 and erlang_file.spi = E0097B434833 (the previous note printed E0097B434833 beside erlang_port by mistake).")
inl note_of_lib_erlang_typed_stdlib_31 () = Note ("LIB-ERLANG-TYPED-STDLIB", 31u32, "2026-11-04T05:34Z", "x", "2026-11-04 01:34 x: REMAINDER 4 -> 2 raw consumer calls (drafted, rides x-next6). (1) cells_executor (kit drafts x\\LIB-MOVE-CIVIL-DATE, CFCE9C3B5DC0 -> 3971ABBD311B): the daemon hello now uses erlang_tcp connect_local, send_line, receive_line_within and close. Its raw tcp, tcp_options, received_line and loopback are gone (0 'gen_tcp' left). Behaviour-neutral: lib's receive_line trims, and the only reader, tree_memory::daemon_pid_of, already trims. (2) sim_serve (kit drafts x\\LIB-ERLANG-TYPED-STDLIB, on x-next5b's DB8E9B3AFECA -> BCD5D21D595B): hmac_sha1 = crypto::hmac_sha1_base64 (it has a Gleam arm); Basic auth = erlang_text::base64_encoded; the raw base64_of is removed. LIB ADDITIONS drafted (kit drafts spiral\\LIB-ERLANG-TYPED-STDLIB): erlang_tcp receive_line_within s ms (AD74CCCA5A8C -> D5F79511AABB; test a_ms_wait_times_out, a 270-char cell; over-272 cells stay at the baseline 6, and lines over 120 drop 2 -> 0 from formatting), erlang_text base64_encoded/decoded, erlang_http replied_status/replied_body. LEFT (2): sim_serve's generic tcp (its HTTP server needs raw, non-line recv, which lib's line-oriented erlang_tcp lacks: next lib step = raw send/recv or an http-server module) and inet port (needs the listener held as erlang_tcp's typed listener).")
inl note_of_lib_erlang_typed_stdlib_32 () = Note ("LIB-ERLANG-TYPED-STDLIB", 32u32, "2026-11-04T05:42Z", "x", "2026-11-04 01:42 x: PATH CORRECTION: the cells_executor tcp migration (3971ABBD311B) lives in kit drafts x\\LIB-ERLANG-TYPED-STDLIB\\cells_executor.spi, not LIB-MOVE-CIVIL-DATE. That folder holds x-next5b's proven CFCE9C3B5DC0 again, restored from the node's staged copy after I had edited it in place mid-proof. land-x-next5 now also pins every proven draft hash.")
inl note_of_lib_erlang_typed_stdlib_33 () = Note ("LIB-ERLANG-TYPED-STDLIB", 33u32, "2026-11-04T06:01Z", "x", "2026-11-04 02:01 x: RAW TCP API drafted for the last 2 raw calls (kit drafts spiral\\LIB-ERLANG-RAW-TCP\\erlang_tcp.spi D24310F06C07, built on x-next6's pinned D5F79511AABB, which stays frozen). Why line mode can't serve HTTP: listen_local uses packet=line (accepted sockets inherit it), receive_line trims (corrupts bodies) and send_line appends a newline. Additions: raw_options (binary, packet raw, passive), raw_listen_options (+ reuseaddr, loopback ip), listen_raw_local, receive_chunk s w (any length, NOT trimmed), send_text s text (no newline). Test raw_bytes_arrive_untrimmed (Gleam): a CRLF request arrives byte-exact through raw_pair/raw_echo. Budget: over-272 cells 6 = x-next6's baseline; widest 118. NEXT (x-next8, after x-next6 lands): sim_serve's server moves onto listen_raw_local, accept Forever, receive_chunk (WithinMs 10000), send_text, close and listener_port. That removes its generic 'tcp' and 'inet port', the item's last 2 raw consumer calls.")
inl note_of_lib_erlang_typed_stdlib_34 () = Note ("LIB-ERLANG-TYPED-STDLIB", 34u32, "2026-11-04T06:03Z", "x", "2026-11-04 02:03 x: LAST 2 RAW CALLS drafted for x-next8. lib (kit drafts spiral\\LIB-ERLANG-RAW-TCP\\erlang_tcp.spi 92680771E5EF): plus receive_chunk_within s ms and accept_forever l, so consumers never build a foreign tcp_wait constructor; over-272 cells still 6 = baseline. sim (kit drafts x\\LIB-ERLANG-RAW-TCP\\sim_serve.spi D3FCEB20AAF1, on x-next6's frozen BCD5D21D595B): listened = listen_raw_local; port_of = listener_port; received loops on chunk_or_empty (receive_chunk_within 10000; a raw recv returns at least 1 byte, so '' means error or done, the old semantics); handled = send_text + close; served = accept_forever with Ok/Error. Dead code removed after the move: tcp, is_ok, listen_options, raw_options, loopback (pair, tag_of and second stay; pair_text and the map builder use them). 0 'gen_tcp' or 'inet' left in sim_serve. Once x-next8 lands, the item's remainder is 0 raw consumer calls (x sim + Cells); then DONE by its DoD.")
inl note_of_lib_erlang_typed_stdlib_35 () = Note ("LIB-ERLANG-TYPED-STDLIB", 35u32, "2026-11-04T12:46Z", "x", "2026-11-04 08:46 x: NEW LIB MODULE erlang_http_server.spi (kit drafts spiral\\LIB-ERLANG-TYPED-STDLIB-2, DCB208E5EA4F): the HTTP/1.1 server wire from x's sim, rewritten on lib only (sm', listm', convert::try_convert, erlang_term::byte_size_of, erlang_text::percent_decoded). request record (method, path, query, head, raw), request_of, header_value (case-insensitive), content_length, complete (head plus Content-Length BYTES arrived), body_of (guarded against a missing head end), query_value/query_list (url-decoded, comma lists), reason phrases, status_line, response_text status kind body, json_response; 4 gleam tests. No TCP inside: the receive loop stays with the caller, so the file depends on no unlanded draft. vultr --check 0; land RUNNING via lib_land_window (label x-httpwire1). CONSUMER: x-next8's sim_serve draft now uses it: E439C08F64E4 -> E81668CFA086, 974 -> 912 lines (22 duplicated definitions gone, 'request' kept as a type alias, 0 cells over 272), vultr --check 0 on x-checkfull78 with the module staged. x-dumps' GET /timeline entry can parse its query with the same module.")
inl note_of_lib_erlang_typed_stdlib_36 () = Note ("LIB-ERLANG-TYPED-STDLIB", 36u32, "2026-11-04T13:39Z", "x", "2026-11-04 09:39 x: erlang_process.spi BUDGET HEAL drafted (kit drafts spiral\\LIB-ERLANG-PROCESS-BUDGET, 6E293B2DB0E1; live 4CCE65988813 is x's own v3 from 11-03): 9 cells over 272 -> 0 (xc: 96 cells, 0 over), all 14 tests kept. Shared test helpers: hand_back_go, finished_watch, inbox_down/inbox_choices/merged_choices/inbox_points/next_points, normal_exits/linked_exit, query_server/spawned_query_server/asked_count/asked_label, echo_member/joined_member/echo_team/echo_within, probe_key/is_undefined/stored_after, named_round_trip, charlist_text; the type-alias block and the pg imports split. Setup reorders are result-invariant (monitor before go; the selector's score is the sum 7+100+1000; one DOWN). vultr: Gleam build 0 and --check 0. FOUND ON THE WAY a formatter bug (reported to compiler): format wrapped a \\$'…' macro so a line began 'process.send', the module-reference scanner took it as a reference to lib 'process', and the package failed with a module cycle; the joined line builds. LAND PLAN: concurrency semantics need EXECUTION, not only the land gate's type probe, so a node notebook run of erlang_process's 14 tests first, then lib_land_window with base 4CCE65988813 (the user has erlang_process STAGED; a land leaves the staged copy alone and changes the worktree).")
inl note_of_lib_erlang_typed_stdlib_37 () = Note ("LIB-ERLANG-TYPED-STDLIB", 37u32, "2026-11-04T14:38Z", "x", "2026-11-04 10:38 x: x-proc1 RULE CORRECTION, made BEFORE the draft arm's result (the draft arm is running now; only the live arm has reported). The registered rule said each arm must report 14/14. That count was wrong: 14 counted every ':#' header, and one is the module declaration ':# erlang_process static =', not a test. Live and draft both have exactly 13 '///> gleam' tests (checked on both files). The live control arm reported discovered 13, executed 13, passed 13, failed 0 (notebook exit 0), so the control is green. CORRECTED RULE: the draft arm must report discovered 13, passed 13, failed 0, notebook exit 0. Anything else = FAIL, no land.")
inl note_of_lib_erlang_typed_stdlib_38 () = Note ("LIB-ERLANG-TYPED-STDLIB", 38u32, "2026-11-04T14:48Z", "x", "2026-11-04 10:48 x: x-proc1 PASSED its (corrected, pre-result) rule: job-1793800172430, both arms on pinned core 0758BAF496AE4E7D. Live control 4CCE65988813: discovered 13, executed 13, passed 13, failed 0, notebook exit 0. Draft 6E293B2DB0E1: discovered 13, executed 13, passed 13, failed 0, notebook exit 0. So the concurrency tests' rewrite into shared helpers (monitor before go, the selector score sum, linked exit, group echo, typed query server, dictionary probe) runs green on the BEAM, not just type-checks. LANDING via land-x-proc1 (lib_land_window, base 4CCE65988813, draft pinned 6E293B2DB0E1, MaxWaitMin 120); queued for a moment with no lib readers. The installed core moved to 59CE62FFE719CFDD during the run.")
inl note_of_lib_erlang_typed_stdlib_39 () = Note ("LIB-ERLANG-TYPED-STDLIB", 39u32, "2026-11-04T14:53Z", "x", "2026-11-04 10:53 x: x-next8's lib erlang_tcp draft HEALED before launch (kit drafts spiral\\LIB-ERLANG-RAW-TCP, 92680771E5EF -> 13FE6EBAFAB7): 4 '/// ##' heading lines gone and 6 over-272 cells -> 0 (is_atom, line_framing, connect_args, line_pair, exchanged, round_trip, released, idle_then_closed; same option order, same assertions). vultr: Gleam build 0 (no cycle; listm::iter resolves) and sim_serve --check 0. Left unformatted on purpose (format's wrap trips COMPILER-MODULE-SCAN-MULTILINE). x-next8 RULE AMENDED BEFORE LAUNCH: its notebook gate now also runs lib erlang_tcp.spi (5 socket tests) with failed=0, since socket tests must execute, not only type; land pin updated.")
inl note_of_lib_erlang_typed_stdlib_40 () = Note ("LIB-ERLANG-TYPED-STDLIB", 40u32, "2026-11-04T14:57Z", "x", "2026-11-04 10:57 x: HEALS QUEUED behind x-next6b (kit drafts spiral\\LIB-HEAL-AFTER-NEXT6, built ON x-next6's frozen drafts, which stay untouched): erlang_http 58A2BB9C1A17 -> 6F264156E7C1 (3 heading lines gone; 3 over-272 cells -> 0 via atom_pair and http_request_args, plus one joined '|> outcome_of'); erlang_text 2BEAE7AF40FA -> 23FBD06448AB (3 heading lines gone; padded_leading 345 -> under 272 via pad_args). vultr: both build to Gleam (exit 0), sim_serve --check 0 against them. Unformatted on purpose (COMPILER-MODULE-SCAN-MULTILINE). LAND PLAN: after x-next6b lands their bases, ONE lib land with bases 58A2BB9C1A17 / 2BEAE7AF40FA, preceded by a node notebook run of both files' tests (live-control arm plus draft arm, as x-proc1), because the land gate only types tests. With these, x's own lib Erlang modules reach 0 over 272 (erlang_tcp via x-next8, erlang_process via x-proc1).")
inl note_of_lib_erlang_typed_stdlib_41 () = Note ("LIB-ERLANG-TYPED-STDLIB", 41u32, "2026-11-04T15:22Z", "x", "2026-11-04 11:22 x: erlang_process LANDED green at 11:13:50 (lib_land_window: consumers compile, type_probe PASS with 13 tests typed, respell 0; 4CCE65988813 -> 6E293B2DB0E1; lib digest C06973AADD95220E; undo lib-land-undo\\20261104-111350), after x-proc1 ran its 13 tests 13/13 on the BEAM. x's Erlang lib modules over 272: erlang_process 9 -> 0 LIVE, erlang_term 1 -> 0 LIVE (x-libheal1); erlang_tcp 6 -> 0 rides x-next8; erlang_http 3 and erlang_text 1 -> 0 in x-libheal2 after x-next6b. The user had erlang_process STAGED: the land changed only the worktree, so the staged copy still holds the old text until the user restages.")
inl note_of_lib_erlang_typed_stdlib_42 () = Note ("LIB-ERLANG-TYPED-STDLIB", 42u32, "2026-11-05T00:46Z", "x", "2026-11-04 20:46 x: x-next6 LAND attempt 1 (20:35) CLOSED RED and undid itself: lib_land_window's document_test came back 25/26, failing 'the spiral library .spi files load as notebooks' (the land script trimmed the reason to 6 lines; a retry with the full log found it). All other close checks passed: signatures, consumer apps/spiral compiles, type_probe 3/3 PASS, 0 respell drift. Control: the live lib passes 26/26. Cause, isolated with the test's own checks run on each draft: erlang_text.spi (proven 2BEAE7AF40FA) does not round-trip spi -> livemd -> spi byte-exactly, because one blank line is missing before the last '///> gleam' row; erlang_http and erlang_tcp round-trip exactly. Fix: the blank line was added (draft 94443FCE2EB5; the proven copy is kept as .proven-2BEAE7AF40FA). It is whitespace between test cells only: on vultr, simframes compiled with the old and the new erlang_text gives byte-identical Gleam (md5 d1d06a721058, 35016 lines). So the x-next6d proof still covers the code, and land-x-next6's lib pin is moved to 94443FCE2EB5 (pin change only). Land attempt 2 is running. The land scripts now Tee the lib window's full log.")
inl note_of_lib_erlang_typed_stdlib_43 () = Note ("LIB-ERLANG-TYPED-STDLIB", 43u32, "2026-11-05T00:54Z", "x", "2026-11-04 20:54 x: LANDED via x-next6 attempt 2 (lib_land_window closed green 20:51; erlang_text 94443FCE2EB5, erlang_http 58A2BB9C1A17, erlang_tcp D5F79511AABB; undo 20261104-204626).")
inl note_of_lib_erlang_typed_stdlib_44 () = Note ("LIB-ERLANG-TYPED-STDLIB", 44u32, "2026-11-05T00:57Z", "x", "2026-11-04 20:57 x: x-libheal2 LAUNCHED (job-1793840257170, core 21F39A40A549E6E6, 1 h limit) now that x-next6 has landed. Heal drafts: erlang_http 6F264156E7C1 on base 58A2BB9C1A17; erlang_text B9924B82DD2D on base 94443FCE2EB5. The base pin moved from 2BEAE7AF40FA, because the live file differs from it only by the landed blank line, which the heal draft also carries now. Both heal drafts pass the notebook round-trip check (run before launch). Verdict PENDING with a waiter.")
inl note_of_lib_erlang_typed_stdlib_45 () = Note ("LIB-ERLANG-TYPED-STDLIB", 45u32, "2026-11-05T01:13Z", "x", "2026-11-04 21:13 x: x-libheal2 GREEN (job-1793840257170, exit 0 in 956 s, core 21F39A40A549E6E6 at start and end). Live and draft notebooks pass for both files: erlang_http live 58A2BB9C1A17 2/2, draft 6F264156E7C1 2/2; erlang_text live 94443FCE2EB5 5/5, draft B9924B82DD2D 5/5. NOT LANDED: it finished at 21:13, after the autonomous window closed at 21:00, so the land (lib_land_window with those two drafts on those bases) waits for the next window.")
inl note_of_lib_erlang_typed_stdlib_46 () = Note ("LIB-ERLANG-TYPED-STDLIB", 46u32, "2026-11-05T02:16Z", "x", "2026-11-04 22:16 x: GROOM: stays Active at 90. Landed via x-next6 (20:51). x-libheal2 (heal of erlang_http + erlang_text) came back GREEN at 21:13, after the window, and is NOT landed; landing it closes this item.")
inl note_of_lib_erlang_typed_stdlib_47 () = Note ("LIB-ERLANG-TYPED-STDLIB", 47u32, "2026-11-05T03:41Z", "x", "2026-11-04 23:41 x: DONE. x-libheal2 (node job-1793840257170, green 21:13) LANDED at 23:3x via lib_land_window: closed green, document_test 26/26, type_probe 2/2, 0 respell drift, consumer apps/spiral compiles; live erlang_http 6F264156E7C1, erlang_text B9924B82DD2D (comment headings stripped; undo 20261104-233340). Together with the x-next6 land (20:51: erlang_text, erlang_http, erlang_tcp typed wrappers), the typed stdlib wrappers x consumes are all live. erlang_tcp's raw-socket follow-up rides x-next8 (LIB-ERLANG-RAW-TCP).")
inl lib_units_amount () = Task ("LIB-UNITS-AMOUNT", "Move the generic phantom-unit amount from tibia src/quantity.spi into lib/spiral/units.spi; tibia keeps only its unit tags and constructors (TIBIA-HARVEST candidate 2)", Story, P2, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "team:tibia,tibia,lib,harvest,typefeatureset", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_lib_units_amount () = Planning ("LIB-UNITS-AMOUNT", Minutes 150u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_lib_units_amount_1 () = Note ("LIB-UNITS-AMOUNT", 1u32, "2026-11-01T19:36Z", "tibia-lane", "11-01 15:3x tibia: filed from TIBIA-HARVEST's candidate list (quantity is candidate 2 after rng; no existing item: searched quantit/units/amount/phantom unit). PLAN: the generic half of tibia src/quantity.spi (phantom ':! amount unit = i32' plus amount_of, value_of, plus, minus, scaled, at_least, capped) lands as lib/spiral/units.spi; the game vocabulary (hitpoints, milliseconds, tiles, gold_coins tags and the hp/ms/tile_count/gp constructors) stays in tibia's quantity.spi, which then holds only tags and constructors. Name 'units': 'quantity' would collide with tibia's module and 'measure' with a tibia app module; no units.spi exists anywhere. DRAFT: kit drafts store spiral\\TIBIA-HARVEST\\units.spi (sample tags apples/pears for the tests; c/rust/lua arithmetic test, two compile-fail tests 'fails \"Unification failure\"' that pin the phantom guarantee, one wgsl test). IMPORTERS (git grep -c quantity::): hunting_pack 54, readiness 24, pack_sim 12, predict 3, sweep 1, gpu_sim 1 = 95 sites; the op calls move to units:: and types become 'units::amount quantity::hitpoints'. DoD: (a) units.spi landed via lib_land_window (new-file path) with the node gate green on c/rust/lua, both fails-tests passing, a negative mutant (plus subtracts) caught, wgsl green on the box; (b) tibia quantity.spi has no arithmetic left, every importer uses units::, tibia's full build green on the node with unchanged pinned values. NEXT STEP: after LIB-KEYED-RNG's tibia consumer build reports, gate the draft on the node mirror tibia-rng (same script as LIB-KEYED-RNG n2).")
inl note_of_lib_units_amount_2 () = Note ("LIB-UNITS-AMOUNT", 2u32, "2026-11-01T20:00Z", "tibia-lane", "11-01 16:0x tibia: LIB HALF PROVEN. Node gate job-1793562414906-tibia-units-gate on mirror tibia-rng (lib synced 14:57, after the :: respell): positive 3/3 passed, 0 type errors (same_unit_arithmetic on c/rust/lua; both 'fails \"Unification failure\"' tests pass, so mixing apples with pears really is a type error); units_on_gpu wgsl unsupported on the node (no runner; box after land); negative mutant (plus subtracts) CAUGHT, 1 failed, 0 type errors. One budget finding (test at 287) fixed by moving the body into an arithmetic_of fixture (no logic change); local budget 0. Roundtrip canonical true, fixpoint true. Land queued behind compiler's compile window (compiler-core-ord-consumers); the land script refuses if package.spiproj moved while waiting. Next: box wgsl test after the land, then the tibia importers (95 sites) once LIB-KEYED-RNG's consumer build is green, so the two migrations are proven one at a time.")
inl note_of_lib_units_amount_3 () = Note ("LIB-UNITS-AMOUNT", 3u32, "2026-11-01T20:26Z", "tibia-lane", "11-01 16:2x tibia: LANDED. First land closed RED and undid itself cleanly (units.spi moved to undo as units.spi.new, package.spiproj restored): type_probe typed the two deliberate 'fails \\\"Unification failure\\\"' tests and counted their expected error as a regression. Fixed type_probe.ps1 (spiral apps/cells/spi, unstaged): blocks whose header holds 'fails \\\"...\\\"' are skipped and listed ('skipped fails (expected errors, checked by Cells)'), like ///> _ blocks; proven on a scratch lib copy (units PASS with both listed; keyed_rng still types 6). Second land 16:18 closed green: 14 signatures unchanged, spiral.spi compiles, document_test 22 passed, type_probe PASS; units.spi 598732C598D9, package.spiproj 7B160B4F51FE; undo spiral-bin\\lib-land-undo\\20261101-161819. Box wgsl: units_on_gpu 1/1 passed. DoD (a) met. Next: (b) tibia importers, after LIB-KEYED-RNG's consumer build reports.")
inl note_of_lib_units_amount_4 () = Note ("LIB-UNITS-AMOUNT", 4u32, "2026-11-01T23:21Z", "tibia-lane", "11-01 19:2x tibia, DONE. DoD (a) met at the 16:18 land (n3). DoD (b): tibia quantity.spi holds only tags and constructors over units::amount, 69 operation sites across 6 files use units::, committed in b08cd87; tibia CI run 41350993071 SUCCESS on both OS jobs with unchanged pinned values.")
inl heartbeat_staleness_guard () = Task ("HEARTBEAT-STALENESS-GUARD", "Every status/heartbeat/watch file carries a write-time and every reader age-checks it before trusting the value (node-watch died 10-30, its status froze at BUSY until 15:29 11-01, lanes skipped an idle node for ~2 days)", TaskKind, P2, D2, 70u32, Active, "", "resilience,durability,forensics,machine,team:resilience", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_heartbeat_staleness_guard () = Planning ("HEARTBEAT-STALENESS-GUARD", Minutes 60u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_heartbeat_staleness_guard_1 () = Note ("HEARTBEAT-STALENESS-GUARD", 1u32, "2026-11-01T19:44Z", "resilience", "2026-11-01 15:4x FILED by resilience (main's 15:30 ask from personal's saturation audit). THE CASE: node-watch (the node's status writer) DIED; its status file froze at \"cpu=100% jobs=7 BUSY\" from 10-30 until 15:29 11-01 (~2 days). Lanes reading it saw BUSY and SKIPPED an actually-idle node -> ~2 days of lost node capacity. A monitor whose stale output lies BUSY is WORSE than no monitor: it produces the exact opposite of its purpose (an idle resource looks full). node-watch is now revived (personal).\n\nROOT-CAUSE CLASS (the general bug, not just node-watch): a status/heartbeat/watch file read with NO AGE CHECK. The writer dies, the last value persists, and every reader trusts a 2-day-old snapshot as live. This is a durability/forensics failure mode that recurs for ANY heartbeat file.\n\nFIX (typed, generic):\n- freshness union (closed): | Fresh : {age_s : u64} | Stale : {age_s : u64; bound_s : u64}. A staleness bound is a typed duration, not a bare number.\n- Every status/heartbeat file carries a write-time (unix_seconds newtype) in its content or a sidecar.\n- Every READER goes through one helper: read_status (path) (bound) -> freshness * value, and the consumer MUST match freshness — a Stale read is treated as UNKNOWN (fall back to a live probe / assume available), NEVER as the last value. Making the reader return a union forces the handling at the type level (can't silently use a stale value).\n- Dead-watcher detection: each watcher self-reports last_beat; a supervisor flags any watcher whose last_beat exceeds N intervals, so a dead writer surfaces as STALE, not as a frozen value.\n\nDoD (verifiable): (1) the typed read_status helper (kit verb or lib/spiral) returns Fresh/Stale by a bound, with a test block fabricating an old vs fresh stamp; (2) the node-status consumers (remote.ps1 / keep-node-busy readers) use it -> a dead node-watch reads STALE and callers fall back to a direct node probe, not BUSY; proven by freezing a test stamp 1h old and seeing the reader say Stale; (3) an AUDIT: list every status/heartbeat/watch file on the box+node (kit machine-watch health.tsv, node status, mirror stamps, train-hold files, mirror last-run stamps) with pass/fail on 'carries write-time + has an age-checking reader'; fix the fails. (4) node-watch self-reports last_beat.\n\nTYPEFEATURESET: freshness union above; staleness bound a typed duration; reader returns (freshness, value) so staleness is unignorable. Same discipline as MACHINE-EVENT-LOG-DETECTOR's session_liveness (a liveness set with last-seen) and BEAM-GUARD's typed metrics. Ties MACHINE-EVENT-LOG-DETECTOR (shared liveness/staleness typing), keep-node-busy / NODE-ALWAYS-FED (the stale BUSY caused the idling), MEMORY-INTEGRITY-SWEEP (main's suggested home; kept separate as this is machine-watcher durability, not memory-store integrity). NOTE this is a sibling of the agile-status stale-read residual (a reader deliberating on a stale snapshot) - same 'trusting a snapshot past its validity' family.")
inl note_of_heartbeat_staleness_guard_2 () = Note ("HEARTBEAT-STALENESS-GUARD", 2u32, "2026-11-01T21:02Z", "resilience", "2026-11-01 17:2x FILE AUDIT (resilience, DoD sub-task 3) — box status/heartbeat/watch files enumerated + classified for write-time + staleness. FINDINGS: (1) %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\machine-watch\\health.tsv — HAS a write-time (unix-seconds col 1, written by kit_machine_sample history_appended) but its LATEST row is 1793487795 = 2026-10-31 19:03, ~22h STALE. It is append-ON-RUN (kit machine-watch), not a daemon, so 'stale' = not run since 10-31 19:03; the staleness RISK is a consumer that reads the last row as current box health (59/34/25 ok/unowned/dangling from 22h ago) — a SECOND instance of the node-watch frozen-value problem, box-side. (2) quiet.tsv — HAS write-time (unix-ms col 3), CURRENT (rows for kit-accept-batch + node-watch, ~16:30 local), written by kit_jobs_doctor jobs_watch. (3) config-baseline/*.tsv + reports/*.tsv = timestamped-FILENAME snapshots, not heartbeats (no staleness semantics). CLASSIFICATION so far: health.tsv = has-stamp / reader-age-check UNKNOWN (no explicit age-checking reader found in kit src; sample.spi only WRITES it) -> the typed read_status(path, bound) -> Fresh|Stale fix applies directly; quiet.tsv = has-stamp / live. REMAINING: the NODE status file (main's frozen-BUSY case) + mirror last-run stamps + profile-mirror stamps on the node (enumerate when not contending with a node build). So the box side has one concrete stale-reader risk (health.tsv); the node side had the actual 2-day BUSY freeze. Both confirm the item's thesis. NEXT: when building, the read_status helper (lib/spiral or kit verb) + make health.tsv consumers go through it.")
inl note_of_heartbeat_staleness_guard_3 () = Note ("HEARTBEAT-STALENESS-GUARD", 3u32, "2026-11-01T23:35Z", "resilience", "2026-11-01 19:3x node-side audit note (resilience). Confirmed via main's lessons digest that the fleet is converging on the SAME fix as this item: 'status files carry stale_after'. That is exactly this item's typed freshness design (note 1): a status file declares its own freshness bound; readers return Fresh|Stale by it. BOX side audited (note 2: health.tsv has a write-time but was 22h stale). NODE side: could not locate the node-watch status file from the box - the node runs kit.exe on the shared junctioned store (kit\\{jobs,windows}->eoie\\{jobs,windows}) so machine-watch isn't at %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\machine-watch there; the frozen-BUSY file main found is personal/main's node-watch output. ACTION for the build: adopt main's stale_after field name (align with the fleet convention) in the typed status-row, and get the node-watch status path from personal/main to include it in the reader rollout. Box reader + the kit read_status helper can be built first (box files are enumerable). No new blocker; this stays the design + box-audit-done state.")
inl note_of_heartbeat_staleness_guard_4 () = Note ("HEARTBEAT-STALENESS-GUARD", 4u32, "2026-11-02T00:06Z", "resilience", "2026-11-01 20:0x SYSTEMIC FINDING - the whole durability-task family shares the silent-success flaw (resilience, from the mirror catch). Checked all 3 of my backup/verify tasks by DISTRUSTING result=0 and looking for fresh output: (1) profile-mirror-to-node = silently frozen 6.2h (caught, hardened - now writes profile-mirror-status.txt with stale_after_s=7200 + a prev-newest-age alarm). (2) profile-restore-drill task runs profile-restore-drill.ps1 -NoteOnFailure => writes a backlog note ONLY on failure; a silent no-op (task didn't run / drill skipped) is INDISTINGUISHABLE from 'passed'. (3) config-baseline-check runs config-baseline.ps1 -Mode Check -NoteOnDrift => writes ONLY on drift; same ambiguity; its newest baseline is 10-31 (33h, which is fine as the REFERENCE, but you can't tell if today's check actually compared). COMPOUNDING RISK: tomorrow's 05:40 restore-drill would 'pass' by verifying against a STALE mirror (if I hadn't caught the freeze) - a silent failure validating a silent failure. GENERALIZED FIX = this item's core: EVERY durability scheduled task writes a status line {last_success_unix, stale_after_s} to a known path, and a single monitor (or each task's own pre-run check) reads them and ALARMS on now-last_success>stale_after. -NoteOnFailure/-NoteOnDrift is necessary but NOT sufficient: 'no bad-news note' must not be read as 'healthy'. ROLLOUT: mirror done; add the same status line to profile-restore-drill.ps1 and config-baseline.ps1; then the kit read_status helper reads all three + health.tsv. This is the item's concrete build list now.")
inl note_of_heartbeat_staleness_guard_5 () = Note ("HEARTBEAT-STALENESS-GUARD", 5u32, "2026-11-02T00:19Z", "resilience", "2026-11-02 00:1x CORRECTION to n4 (resilience - read the scripts, my n4 was overstated). I asserted restore-drill + config-baseline 'write ONLY on failure/drift'; FALSE. Verified by reading them: profile-restore-drill.ps1 writes %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\schedules\\profile-restore-drill.last.log EVERY run (line 50; last=2026-11-01 05:40 PASS, 238 memory files hash-equal vs profile-mirror-20261101-045105.tgz) AND fails loud if newest archive >3h ('the mirror is stalled', line 33). config-baseline.ps1 writes config-baseline.last.log every run (line 61). The -NoteOnFailure/-NoteOnDrift flags are ADDITIONAL (a backlog note on bad news), not the only output. So 2 of 3 already had a per-run status log; the MIRROR was the sole outlier (threw on failure but wrote no per-run status) - now fixed (profile-mirror-status.txt). CORRECTED PICTURE: the per-run status logs mostly EXIST; the real missing piece is a MONITOR that READS them (restore-drill.last.log, config-baseline.last.log, profile-mirror-status.txt, machine-watch\\health.tsv) and alarms on now-last_success>stale_after. And the restore-drill's >3h check is a real backstop that WOULD have caught the mirror freeze on tomorrow's 05:40 run (+noted it) - but a day is too slow for a 6.2h gap, so a frequent monitor is the speed fix. So this item narrows to: (1) [done] mirror parity status file; (2) the kit read_status/monitor verb that reads the 4 known status files on a cadence and alarms. Lesson logged: I flagged a flaw before reading the code - the exact 'verify before asserting' discipline; reading corrected it.")
inl note_of_heartbeat_staleness_guard_6 () = Note ("HEARTBEAT-STALENESS-GUARD", 6u32, "2026-11-02T17:18Z", "resilience", "guard ALARM: heartbeat-staleness-guard 2026-11-02 13:18 STALE (1) (first: STALE profile-mirror.last.log: 97min (> 90min) - watcher not writing, likely dead)")
inl note_of_heartbeat_staleness_guard_7 () = Note ("HEARTBEAT-STALENESS-GUARD", 7u32, "2026-11-02T19:18Z", "resilience", "guard ALARM: heartbeat-staleness-guard 2026-11-02 15:18 STALE (1) (first: STALE profile-mirror-monitor.last.log: 120min (> 90min) - watcher not writing, likely dead)")
inl note_of_heartbeat_staleness_guard_8 () = Note ("HEARTBEAT-STALENESS-GUARD", 8u32, "2026-11-02T19:21Z", "resilience", "11-02 15:21 resilience: GUARD CAUGHT A REAL FAILURE. heartbeat-staleness-guard flagged profile-mirror-monitor.last.log 117min stale (>90min). Root: profile-mirror-monitor.ps1 (pid 4652) hung ~2h40m on an UNBOUNDED ssh running node `powershell -EncodedCommand` (cold-start-under-saturation hang; ConnectTimeout/ServerAlive dont kill a slow-to-START remote cmd), and the single-instance task policy refused every trigger since (0x800710E0). So the mirrors independent detector was silently DOWN - exactly the silent-watcher-death this guard exists to catch. Validates the mechanism end-to-end. Bumping 0->75 (guard delivered PT30M + proven on a live catch; remaining: confirm every status-writing script carries a write-time).")
inl note_of_heartbeat_staleness_guard_9 () = Note ("HEARTBEAT-STALENESS-GUARD", 9u32, "2026-11-03T00:31Z", "resilience", "11-02 20:31 resilience: DoD-3 box-side audit VERIFIED COMPLETE. Re-read the guard: it keys each schedules\\*.last.log on its FILESYSTEM mtime as the write-time and age-checks against a bound (90min default; 1560min override for the 3 daily tasks) - so every status-writing script that drops a .last.log is structurally covered (FS mtime IS the write-time, this guard IS the single age-checking reader). Enumerated all 11 watchers now: ALL FRESH (profile-restore-drill 418m/<1560, config-baseline 321m, memory-integrity-sweep 270m, profile-mirror 29m, profile-mirror-monitor 25m, node-util 16m, orphan-reaper 15m, wsl-policy 15m, upkeep-tick 15m, self 12m, critical-mirror 4m). profile-mirror recovered to 29m (<90) - the reaper stuck-mirror sweep worked. REMAINING (holds this at 75, NOT box-light): DoD-1 typed read_status Fresh|Stale Spiral helper + test block needs a compile (box held by TRAIN13c, defer); DoD-2 node consumers + DoD-4 node-watch last_beat need the node-side status path from personal/main. So the box audit + the working PS guard (2 live catches today: 13:18, 15:18) are done; the typed-helper build is the gating remainder.")
inl note_of_heartbeat_staleness_guard_10 () = Note ("HEARTBEAT-STALENESS-GUARD", 10u32, "2026-11-03T11:20Z", "resilience", "11-03 07:1x resilience: operational check - staleness guard ran, all 10 watchers FRESH (07:18); durability monitoring healthy, no dead writer. Box-side remains green. COORDINATION: DoD-1 (the typed read_status Fresh|Stale Spiral helper, design in n1) is a box-gated compile - the SAME scarce resource BEAM-GUARD step-2c's kit_machine_cap build needs. Box has been mostly 99-100% saturated (brief dip to 54% at 07:01). BATCH both Spiral compiles into the next genuine box-dip + free-heavy-slot window rather than waiting separately. DoD-2/4 still need the node-watch status path from personal/main. Stays 75 (box-side audit + working PS guard done; typed helper is the box-gated remainder).")
inl note_of_heartbeat_staleness_guard_11 () = Note ("HEARTBEAT-STALENESS-GUARD", 11u32, "2026-11-03T11:38Z", "resilience", "11-03 07:3x resilience: DoD-1 (typed read_status Fresh|Stale) drafted TURNKEY in scratchpad (freshness-read-status-draft.md). Design resolved: PURE freshness logic -> lib/spiral (records fresh_at/stale_at, union | Fresh | Stale, classify write_s now_s bound_s -> freshness, with a ///> rust test block fabricating fresh/stale/clock-skew stamps = the DoD-1 test). IMPURE wrapper -> thin kit read_status(path,bound) that parses the `unix=` field + calls classify; reuses workspace_primitives.clock.unix_ms (EXISTS - no new host macro, no baselines-only-shrink cost). Union (not bool) forces callers to handle Stale as UNKNOWN (the node-watch frozen-BUSY bug). Box-gated Spiral compile (box ~100%, deferred); BATCH with BEAM-GUARD step-2c in the next box dip. DoD-2/4 still need the node-watch status path from personal/main. Stays 75 until the build proves the test + wires a consumer.")
inl note_of_heartbeat_staleness_guard_12 () = Note ("HEARTBEAT-STALENESS-GUARD", 12u32, "2026-11-03T12:19Z", "resilience", "11-03 08:1x resilience: DoD-1 freshness module VULTR-VALIDATED (remote-first, per route-checks-to-idle-vultr). Staged ~/remote-stage/resilience-check on vultr (box lib/spiral 18.7MB tgz extracted to spiral/, core borrowed from fiscal-check), registered freshness in spiral/package.spiproj, and `SPIRAL_COMPILER_PACKAGE_DIR=$PWD dotnet pack24/SpiralCompiler.dll --check spiral/freshness.spi` => EXIT 0 in 12s (first pass hit exit 5 \"not declared in package\" = needed the modules-list register, then clean). So the typed core compiles + type-checks CLEAN including the ///> rust test block (fresh_at/stale_at records, | Fresh | Stale union, classify with clock-skew clamp, 3 fabricated-stamp asserts). This is the compiler confirmation of DoD-1's design - zero box load (box still 100%), zero shared-tree exposure. The resilience-check vultr stage is now ESTABLISHED + reusable. REMAINING for DoD-1: the thin kit read_status wrapper (parse unix= + unix_ms + classify) + wire a consumer - those touch kit packages, so either a bigger vultr kit-stage or the box. Capability proven; this validates the hard part. Stays 75 (core validated; wrapper+consumer remain).")
inl note_of_heartbeat_staleness_guard_13 () = Note ("HEARTBEAT-STALENESS-GUARD", 13u32, "2026-11-03T12:36Z", "resilience", "11-03 08:3x resilience: DoD-1 HELPER fully VULTR-VALIDATED (exit 0, 4s). Extended freshness.spi (scratchpad) with field_u64 (parses the `unix=<N>` field from a status line: split on key, take the leading token, trim, try_convert; missing -> 0) and read_status(path, bound) (file_system.file_exists ? read+field_u64+classify : Stale{age=0} = treated UNKNOWN by callers), plus pure tests for both field_u64 cases on top of the 3 classify cases. `dotnet pack24/SpiralCompiler.dll --check spiral/freshness.spi` => exit 0. So DoD-1's #1 deliverable (the typed read_status Fresh|Stale helper + test block) is compiler-proven, on idle vultr, zero box load. Design: clock_now_s is a 0u64 STUB for the check (keeps the helper pure-lib so it checks in the resilience-check lib stage); the LANDED version wires the real clock (workspace_primitives.clock.unix_ms or a lib clock) - a trivial swap. REMAINING: land the module (lib or kit, additive+low-risk, box) + real clock; DoD-2 node consumers go through read_status (needs the node-watch status path from personal/main); DoD-4 node-watch last_beat (main). DoD-3 box audit already done. The hard typed part is validated + de-risked. Stays 75 until landed + a consumer wired.")
inl note_of_heartbeat_staleness_guard_14 () = Note ("HEARTBEAT-STALENESS-GUARD", 14u32, "2026-11-03T14:43Z", "resilience", "11-03 10:4x resilience: DURABILITY INCIDENT caught + mitigated during a warranted high-load spot-check. SYMPTOM: critical-mirror.last.log 10:28 = \"FAIL scp rc 124 over laptop\"; node-utilization 10:15 = node (mirror) 100% CPU, disk unreadable; profile-mirror-monitor 10:35 = newest node tgz profile-mirror-20261103-080354 is 152min stale (>120). ROOT: the NODE (laptop) is CPU-saturated and cannot service scp/ssh (ssh from the box also timed out), so BOTH mirrors have been stalled ~2.5h - tier-1 critical data (memory/hooks/config) had no fresh off-box copy. MITIGATION (done): manually staged the critical tier (memory+hooks+config, 1.22MB) and scp'd it to vultr (reachable, ~/remote-stage/resilience-critical/critical-vultr-20261103-104031.tar), sha256 e07cef39... VERIFIED match on vultr. Off-box tier-1 durability restored via the alternate target. FINDING 1 (for THIS item): the age-based staleness guard called critical-mirror \"fresh\" (log written 10:28) while its content was a FAIL - age != success. This is a LIVE proof of the item's last_success_unix design: a monitor must check last_SUCCESS, not last write-time. profile-mirror-monitor (tracks newest landed archive, not log age) DID catch it. FINDING 2 (hardening, for a focused pass): critical-mirror.ps1 should FAIL OVER to vultr when all node routes time out (the node is a worker that gets CPU-pinned; vultr stays reachable) - same route-to-idle-vultr pattern. NOT auto-done (live durability script; careful change). The node will keep failing backups until it frees; I will re-backup critical to vultr each heartbeat it stays saturated.")
inl note_of_heartbeat_staleness_guard_15 () = Note ("HEARTBEAT-STALENESS-GUARD", 15u32, "2026-11-03T14:48Z", "resilience", "11-03 10:47 resilience: incident RESOLVED (follows n14). The node was TRANSIENTLY overloaded (100% CPU ~10:15-10:34, scp/ssh timing out), NOT crashed: confirmed UP via route laptop at 41% CPU, LastBootUpTime Oct 25 (same 9-day-up node). So no fleet alert warranted. critical-mirror re-run at 10:47 => OK, fresh critical-20261103-104703.tar landed on the node (sha256 9f4d8d..., atomic move succeeded) - node-backup RESUMED. Tier-1 durability is now secured BOTH ways: node (10:47) + vultr stopgap (10:40, e07cef...). profile-mirror (450MB, was 152min stale) will resume on its own schedule now the node is at 41%; the critical tier (the important data) is the secured one. NET LESSON (reinforces this item): a high-load durability spot-check caught a real backup stall the age-based guard could not see (fresh log, FAIL content) - keep periodic success-content checks until the typed last_success monitor (DoD) lands; and route the critical tier to vultr when the node is unreachable (worked, hash-verified). Incident window: ~2.5h of stalled node-backups, covered by the vultr copy, zero tier-1 data exposure.")
inl note_of_heartbeat_staleness_guard_16 () = Note ("HEARTBEAT-STALENESS-GUARD", 16u32, "2026-11-03T15:54Z", "resilience", "11-03 11:5x resilience: node FLAPPING confirmed (recovered 10:47 -> unreachable again by critical-mirror 11:41 \"no route reached\"). Recurring mitigation working: the failed 11:41 run KEPT its staged tar locally, so I shipped critical-20261103-114048.tar to vultr (~/remote-stage/resilience-critical/), sha256 471581b0... VERIFIED on vultr. Tier-1 off-box durability stays fresh on vultr through the flaps (now 2 critical tars there: 10:40, 11:41). RECURRING TASK (mine, each heartbeat the node is down): if critical-mirror.last.log shows a recent FAIL/no-route + a kept local tar exists, scp it to vultr - safe (no live-script edit), reactive. The node flapping under fleet load elevates the auto-failover hardening (critical-mirror.ps1 -> vultr route when all node routes fail; needs Linux-path handling since the script's cmd /c mkdir/move are Windows-node-specific) - a careful focused change, deferred (live durability script). Not re-noting each individual flap after this; the pattern + mitigation are recorded.")
inl note_of_heartbeat_staleness_guard_17 () = Note ("HEARTBEAT-STALENESS-GUARD", 17u32, "2026-11-03T16:12Z", "resilience", "11-03 12:1x resilience: cleaned 17 orphaned failed-run critical-*.tar (19.5MB) from box %TEMP% (superseded by the 11:57 node backup + vultr copies; verified before deleting). KEY FINDING: the orphans span 11-02 17:10 -> 11-03 12:10, so the NODE has been FLAPPING intermittently for ~19h (17 critical-mirror runs failed \"no route reached\"/scp-timeout over that window, each keeping a 1.2MB local tar that the script never cleans). So node-backup reliability is a longer-standing issue, not just today's spike - the node (a shared worker) is intermittently unreachable under fleet load. Node recovered again at 11:57 (critical landed via laptop-ip); off-box durability currently fine (11:57 node + 11:41 vultr, critical data stable). TWO script-hygiene/robustness gaps for the auto-failover hardening (deferred, live script): (1) critical-mirror.ps1 leaves failed-run tars in TEMP forever - the failover should ship-then-clean them; (2) it should route to vultr when all node routes fail (the node flaps ~19h, manual per-heartbeat failover is a lot). For now: manual vultr failover on flaps + periodic TEMP orphan sweep. Not re-noting each flap.")
inl note_of_heartbeat_staleness_guard_18 () = Note ("HEARTBEAT-STALENESS-GUARD", 18u32, "2026-11-03T16:24Z", "resilience", "11-03 12:2x resilience: ROOT CAUSE corrected (supersedes the \"node flapping -> manual vultr failover each heartbeat\" plan). The mirror \"flapping\" is NOT the node being down - it is the BOX (source) being CPU-saturated (100%) starving the backup ssh/scp so they cannot connect/transfer within the timeout. Proof: I added a vultr-failover branch to critical-mirror.ps1 (safe: node path untouched, parse-verified, backup kept); it FAILED on both test runs (12:18, 12:21) - yet vultr was reachable seconds before each (ssh vultr mkdir rc=0 at 12:20). So the box could not run the scp to EITHER target in those moments. A vultr failover does not help when the bottleneck is the box's ability to START the ssh. REVERTED the edit (keeps tier-1 simple; it does not address the real cause; .bak in scratch). DURABILITY IS ACTUALLY FINE: backups land in the box's brief CPU moments (critical-mirror ok on the node at 10:47 and 11:57; last success ~25min ago, tier-1 data stable). CORRECTED STRATEGY: do NOT manually fail-over each \"flap\" (unreliable - same box starvation - and unnecessary since node-backups land intermittently). Instead MONITOR last-SUCCESSFUL-backup recency; investigate only if it exceeds ~2h. The real fix is box CPU headroom (fleet-level, not mine to force). Lesson: diagnose the source, not just the symptom - \"no route reached\" from a pinned box means the box cannot run ssh, not that the target is down.")
inl note_of_heartbeat_staleness_guard_19 () = Note ("HEARTBEAT-STALENESS-GUARD", 19u32, "2026-11-04T00:44Z", "resilience", "SCOPE EXTENSION from the 11-03 19:44 BSOD (BOX-BSOD-1103): the AUTONOMOUS-RUN HEARTBEAT is itself an unguarded watcher - the highest-stakes instance of this item's failure mode yet. When the box crashed, the session-only heartbeat cron died WITH the session; nothing detected the dead heartbeat and the whole autonomous loop silently stalled until a human (main) noticed and messaged me to restart. This is the node-watch frozen-value bug escalated: not \"a reader trusts a stale status\" but \"the beat-writer itself is dead and no supervisor notices,\" and the cost is the entire run halting, not one skipped resource.\nSELF-REFERENCE PROBLEM (why the item's own \"supervisor flags last_beat > N intervals\" design is necessary but not sufficient here): a guard that runs INSIDE the heartbeat cannot detect its OWN death - the thing that would raise the alarm died too. So the autonomous heartbeat specifically needs a PERSISTENT, out-of-process supervisor: a scheduled TASK (survives reboots, as critical-mirror/profile-mirror did) that checks the session heartbeat's last_beat and re-engages or alarms when it goes stale. This is the SAME fix as BOX-BSOD-1103 follow-up 4 (crash-resilient wake source) - the two items converge here.\nDoD addition: add \"the autonomous-run heartbeat\" to the audited-watcher list (DoD-3), guarded by a persistent task-based supervisor (not the in-session guard). Ties box-crash-recovery-playbook memory (manual stopgap: recreate the cron on restart) - the supervisor automates what is currently a human-in-the-loop recovery. No build now (fragile box, mid-run; a new scheduled supervisor task is a careful change); recorded as the synthesis the incident forced.")
inl note_of_heartbeat_staleness_guard_20 () = Note ("HEARTBEAT-STALENESS-GUARD", 20u32, "2026-11-04T05:23Z", "resilience", "11-04 01:3x resilience COVERAGE FINDING + f4 convergence. Read the deployed guard (scripts/heartbeat-staleness-guard.ps1): it auto-watches EVERY kit\\schedules\\*.last.log, flags any older than 90min (overrides 1560min for slow sweeps: memory-integrity-sweep, config-baseline, profile-restore-drill), runs as a scheduled task (survives reboot), notes this item on first alarm. The 10-watchers-fresh = the 10 scheduled-task logs. GAP: it does NOT watch the autonomous-RUN heartbeat (session-only CronCreate) - that cron writes no .last.log, so the exact silent-run-death mode (session cron dies with a crash, run stops silently = BOX-BSOD-1103 f4) is UNCOVERED. CONVERGENCE: this guard IS the crash-resilient monitor f4 needs, ~80pct built. Small closing DoD: (1) run heartbeat writes a sentinel kit\\schedules\\autonomous-<lane>.last.log at the START of every tick (session dies -> writes stop); (2) a guard override ~40min (2x the 20min cadence) for that sentinel so a dead run flags fast; (3) an ACTIVE-RUN gate - only alarm when standing.txt until>now, and clear/ignore the sentinel at run end, else it false-alarms after the 21:00 stop. Then a crashed run is detected in <=40min by a reboot-surviving task, not whenever someone notices nothing happened. IMPLEMENTATION = agent decision (edit guard with a .bak + test run; add sentinel-write to the heartbeat) - small+safe but do it as a focused step, not at a giant-turn tail. Cross-ref BOX-BSOD-1103 note 7 (f4 design).")
inl note_of_heartbeat_staleness_guard_21 () = Note ("HEARTBEAT-STALENESS-GUARD", 21u32, "2026-11-04T05:29Z", "resilience", "11-04 01:3x resilience RUN-HEARTBEAT COVERAGE CLOSED (the 75pct gap). Extended the guard to monitor the autonomous-run heartbeat (session-only cron), not just scheduled-task logs: autonomous-*.last.log watched at 40min, gated on standing.txt active; heartbeat cron 553c6aa8 writes the sentinel each tick (step 0). Tested 3 cases (fresh / stale-while-active / restored) all pass. This IS BOX-BSOD-1103 f4. Remaining to 100: fleet rollout (each lane writes autonomous-<lane> + the sentinel step in its heartbeat). Guard already runs as a reboot-surviving scheduled task, so no new task needed. Guard edit .bak-d + unstaged.")
inl note_of_heartbeat_staleness_guard_22 () = Note ("HEARTBEAT-STALENESS-GUARD", 22u32, "2026-11-04T05:51Z", "resilience", "11-04 02:0x resilience freshness.spi (DoD-1) READINESS ASSESSED (located in the C--home-git scratchpad, not the session-hash one - why earlier searches missed it; and there is NO cap.spi, only kit_machine_cap-draft.md, a design doc). freshness.spi is a clean lib-level module: classify (age vs bound, clock-AGNOSTIC - takes now_s as a param), field_u64 (unix= parser), read_status, + a :# Rust test block (5 cases). NOT land-ready as a reader: clock_now_s()=0u64 is a STUB, so read_status always computes age 0 = always Fresh (silently defeats staleness detection - worse than not landing). lib has no obvious unix-now primitive (grepped now/unix/timestamp/epoch, only incidental hits). So DoD-1 is BLOCKED on a decision: (a) add a lib unix-now primitive (Rust-backed system time), or (b) the CALLER passes now_s (classify already supports it; the deployed PS guard has Get-Date and works today - 11 watchers fresh). Landable tested core = classify + field_u64; read_status needs the clock resolved. ALSO needs re-validation vs the current lib tree (post pack33 + 231-regen), not the stale prior exit-0. This is why passing TRAIN19 was correct. NEXT (calm window): decide clock source, wire it, re-validate on vultr (--timeout-ms 150000) or a box window, then land on a green train.")
inl note_of_heartbeat_staleness_guard_23 () = Note ("HEARTBEAT-STALENESS-GUARD", 23u32, "2026-11-04T06:02Z", "resilience", "11-04 02:1x resilience freshness.spi UNBLOCKED (clock decision made). Removed the clock_now_s()=0 stub; read_status now takes now_s so the CALLER injects the clock - lib stays clock-agnostic (matching classify, which already takes now_s; the PS guard supplies Get-Date, a kit verb supplies kit/Rust time). Draft (scratch C--home-git) edited, honest now (no silent always-Fresh). PENDING before land: (1) re-validate vs the current lib tree - vultr overloaded tonight (load 10-16) + box busy, so a calm window with --timeout-ms 150000; (2) add a read_status test row with a temp status-file fixture (classify + field_u64 already have 5 :# rows). Then land on a green train (3 red tonight: TRAIN17b/18/19, none green yet).")
inl note_of_heartbeat_staleness_guard_24 () = Note ("HEARTBEAT-STALENESS-GUARD", 24u32, "2026-11-04T06:39Z", "resilience", "11-04 02:3x resilience freshness.spi re-validation NOW VIABLE (blocker lifted). vultr load dropped ~4 (from 10-16); pack33-src-before dll present on vultr (current - the route-checks staleness gotcha is resolved); ~/remote-stage/resilience-check stage exists. So --check CAN run (--timeout-ms 150000). DEFERRED the actual check deliberately, not stopping-short: (a) the land is ALSO green-train-gated (3 red tonight TRAIN17b/18/19; a TRAIN19 re-attempt is running, background-waited b5gj3252q) so validating now enables no immediate land; (b) correct registration needs care (package.spiproj modules: list = define-before-use ordering + the trailing-`-` suffix semantics) - hand-rolling risks a FALSE exit-0, so use fiscal PROVEN check-drafts recipe. PLAN: when a train greens, validate freshness.spi vs that green tree via check-drafts + --timeout-ms 150000, then place it in lib/spiral (no `-` suffix, after its deps sm'/am'/optionm'/convert/file_system/testing) for the next train.")
inl note_of_heartbeat_staleness_guard_25 () = Note ("HEARTBEAT-STALENESS-GUARD", 25u32, "2026-11-04T06:48Z", "resilience", "11-04 02:4x resilience CORRECTION to notes 22-23: lib DOES have a unix-now primitive - the package.spiproj modules list carries `unix_clock` (line 38) + `date_time` (line 55); my earlier grep missed them. So freshness.spi's clock source was never a real gap: the caller injects now_s (my fix) sourced from unix_clock.now_s(), or read_status could call unix_clock directly. Keeping the caller-inject design (it stays testable - classify/read_status take now_s). STATUS: freshness.spi --check is RUNNING on vultr now (pack33 dll, load ~4, --timeout-ms 150000, module registered after nameof_heal, no suffix) to confirm it type-checks vs the current post-regen lib; result pending (bm7uruk2h).")
inl note_of_heartbeat_staleness_guard_26 () = Note ("HEARTBEAT-STALENESS-GUARD", 26u32, "2026-11-04T06:49Z", "resilience", "11-04 02:4x resilience freshness.spi VALIDATED (exit 0) against the current post-regen lib on vultr (pack33, load 4): \"checked package\" CHECK_EXIT=0. The clock-fixed module (read_status takes now_s, sourced from unix_clock by the caller) type-checks cleanly vs current lib - READY to land. (The tar LIBARCHIVE.symlinktype warning is harmless core-symlink metadata; check passed.) Remaining for the DoD-1 land: (1) a GREEN tree - still gated (3 red; TRAIN19 re-attempt pending b5gj3252q); (2) place freshness.spi in live lib/spiral + register after nameof_heal, no suffix, when a green window opens; (3) optional read_status test row (module already passes with its 5 classify/field_u64 :# rows, not a blocker). The vultr ~/revalidate stage is proven + set up for a fast re-check at land time.")
inl note_of_heartbeat_staleness_guard_27 () = Note ("HEARTBEAT-STALENESS-GUARD", 27u32, "2026-11-04T06:52Z", "resilience", "11-04 02:5x resilience freshness.spi FULLY land-ready: re-check WITH the added read_status test row = exit 0 (checked package, current lib pack33). All 3 functions now covered by :# rows (classify x3, field_u64 x2, read_status x1 = missing-file->Stale). Clock-fixed (read_status takes now_s via unix_clock caller) + type-valid + tested vs current post-regen lib. ONLY remaining gate = a GREEN tree to land it into lib/spiral (register after nameof_heal, no suffix) + a train to build/run the tests. vultr ~/revalidate stage is proven for a final re-check at land time. freshness.spi (DoD-1) DONE pending the green-train land.")
inl note_of_heartbeat_staleness_guard_28 () = Note ("HEARTBEAT-STALENESS-GUARD", 28u32, "2026-11-04T12:18Z", "resilience", "guard ALARM: heartbeat-staleness-guard 2026-11-04 08:18 STALE (1) (first: STALE autonomous-resilience.last.log: 58min (> 40min) - watcher not writing, likely dead)")
inl note_of_heartbeat_staleness_guard_29 () = Note ("HEARTBEAT-STALENESS-GUARD", 29u32, "2026-11-05T02:20Z", "resilience", "11-04 star-exchange (hurdle F clocks, resilience-adjacent): x-dumps judged 21:00 LOCAL expired at 21:12Z (box date is UTC, user-local UTC-4) and deleted its own heartbeat early. RULE: derive local time from Get-Date (or CronList next-run, which is local), NEVER date -u; this guard + any heartbeat-expiry check must use LOCAL. Also wrap unattended ssh in timeout -k N (a wedged ssh ignored SIGTERM 9+ min).")
inl note_of_heartbeat_staleness_guard_30 () = Note ("HEARTBEAT-STALENESS-GUARD", 30u32, "2026-11-05T03:48Z", "resilience", "guard ALARM: heartbeat-staleness-guard 2026-11-04 23:48 STALE (1) (first: STALE autonomous-resilience.last.log: 163min (> 40min) - watcher not writing, likely dead)")
inl note_of_heartbeat_staleness_guard_31 () = Note ("HEARTBEAT-STALENESS-GUARD", 31u32, "2026-11-05T04:00Z", "resilience", "Evidence advance - a live staleness trap in the fleet's own health monitoring. The hourly kit machine watch sweep refreshes hourly.log + quiet.tsv (both mtime 23:17) but does NOT append to health.tsv, which is frozen at mtime 08:44 with in-content unix=1793402372 (19:19 local, 124.7h stale now). health.tsv DOES carry the write-time column (unix) this guard requires - but that proves the guard's core point: a timestamp column is necessary-not-sufficient; a reader trusting the row (tools=67,dangling=29,...) without age-checking unix would act on 124.7h-old counts. Two fixes: (reader side, this item) every consumer of health.tsv/quiet/node-watch/standing age-checks the embedded write-time and treats >1 sweep-interval as UNKNOWN not current; (producer side, kit gap) the sweep should append a fresh health.tsv row each run so the freshest row is current. Confirms the earlier node-watch-froze-at-BUSY motivation. resilience.")
inl note_of_heartbeat_staleness_guard_32 () = Note ("HEARTBEAT-STALENESS-GUARD", 32u32, "2026-11-05T04:18Z", "resilience", "guard ALARM: heartbeat-staleness-guard 2026-11-05 00:18 STALE (1) (first: STALE autonomous-resilience.last.log: 193min (> 40min) - watcher not writing, likely dead)")
inl note_of_heartbeat_staleness_guard_33 () = Note ("HEARTBEAT-STALENESS-GUARD", 33u32, "2026-11-05T04:48Z", "resilience", "guard ALARM: heartbeat-staleness-guard 2026-11-05 00:48 STALE (1) (first: STALE autonomous-resilience.last.log: 223min (> 40min) - watcher not writing, likely dead)")
inl note_of_heartbeat_staleness_guard_34 () = Note ("HEARTBEAT-STALENESS-GUARD", 34u32, "2026-11-05T05:16Z", "resilience", "SHIPPED-WITH-GAPS assessment (resilience, read heartbeat-staleness-guard.ps1 + its live log). SHIPPED: a reader-side staleness guard runs as a scheduled task - scans kit\\schedules\\*.last.log, flags any watcher older than its max (default 90min; 40min for autonomous-* when standing.txt run is active; 1560min overrides for slow sweeps) as \"likely dead\", writes a verdict log, exits 1 + optionally files a backlog note on first stale transition. Producer side mostly done too: 12/14 status files embed a timestamp. So this is ~70% not 0. THREE GAPS remain: (1) it age-checks MTIME not the EMBEDDED content write-time -> misses content-staleness when a file is touched/copied but not rewritten (exactly the health.tsv case: mtime 11-04 08:44 vs content 10-30 19:19, note 31); fix = parse the embedded unix/stamp and age-check THAT. (2) coverage is only kit\\schedules\\*.last.log - excludes health.tsv/quiet.tsv (machine-watch) and remote node/vultr status files; extend the scan. (3) producer gap: profile-mirror.last.log and quiet.tsv carry no embedded timestamp. Also a benign false-positive now: it flags autonomous-resilience.last.log STALE (249min) because THIS run heartbeats via a session cron (f311c685), not the scheduled-task log the guard expects - either teach the guard that session-cron runs use a different liveness signal, or have lanes append their heartbeat log. resilience.")
inl note_of_heartbeat_staleness_guard_35 () = Note ("HEARTBEAT-STALENESS-GUARD", 35u32, "2026-11-05T05:19Z", "resilience", "guard ALARM: heartbeat-staleness-guard 2026-11-05 01:19 STALE (1) (first: STALE autonomous-resilience.last.log: 253min (> 40min) - watcher not writing, likely dead)")
