inl note_of_regex_xsd_1 () = Note ("REGEX-XSD", 1u32, "2026-11-01T20:25Z", "regex", "regex 16:4x. DOD (n5 D5): parser returns Parsed re | Refused (position, reason union); one test per XSD-ism: whole-string anchoring, ^ and $ literal, '-[' after | literal, [a-z-[aeiou]] subtraction, dot excludes \\n \\r, \\d\\s\\w + negations, negated classes, non-ASCII ranges, \\p{Is<Block>} full XSD block list + \\P, unknown block refused, \\i \\c refused; R{1,1000} builds in <= 1001 states inside the test budget; the 7 NF-e QR patterns (<= 494 states) build within the cap; oracle test over fiscal's pinned pattern corpus + .NET probe rows downloaded in CI (never committed) with 0 disagreements; parity with lib xsd_regex on every probe (the deletion phase B gate). NEXT STEP: xsd/ parser notebook over REGEX-SYNTAX; ask fiscal for a stable URL/commit of the corpus + probe rows (fiscal/scripts/pattern-oracle.ps1:9-17). TYPEFEATURESET PLAN: refusal reasons as a union (Lookaround, StackedQuantifier, NothingToRepeat, UnknownBlock name, NameEscape, UnclosedClass...), blocks as a union table generated once (not string compares at match time), pattern ids as a content-hash nominal (FISCAL-XSD-PATTERNS-LARGE n13). FIELDS: estimate 480, load light (heavy only for the corpus run), assignee agent.")
inl note_of_regex_xsd_2 () = Note ("REGEX-XSD", 2u32, "2026-11-01T21:43Z", "regex", "regex 17:4x PROGRESS: src/xsd.spi (unstaged) = XSD dialect parser over code points (unicode.code_points) with typed refusals (UnclosedGroup, UnclosedClass, UnexpectedClose, NothingToRepeat, StackedQuantifier, BadCount, UnknownBlock, NameEscape, Lookaround), counted repetition through syntax.repeat (never unrolled), class subtraction, negated classes, \\d\\s\\w + negations (ASCII approximations, parity with lib xsd_regex, NOT XSD's Unicode \\w/\\d yet), dot excludes \\n \\r, ^ $ literal, whole-text anchoring, 6 XSD blocks as a union table (BasicLatin, Latin-1Supplement, LatinExtended-A/B, GeneralPunctuation, CurrencySymbols; full XSD block list still to do). 7 test blocks, one per XSD-ism. NOT yet: the fiscal corpus oracle, parity run against lib xsd_regex, the NF-e QR patterns.")
inl note_of_regex_xsd_3 () = Note ("REGEX-XSD", 3u32, "2026-11-01T23:17Z", "regex", "regex 19:1x EVIDENCE node run3 job-1793571992048-regex-run3 (18:25-19:10, memo off, rust+gleam, 900 s budget; saved in drafts\\regex\\REGEX-SYNTAX\\run3-full.txt): total discovered 48 executed 48 passed 41 failed 7, mismatches {} in every file: all 7 reds are node infrastructure (charset: \"could not start the compiler on port 13977: lock_timeout\"; derivative: a cell main.spi missing in TEMP; dfa: \"compiler worker failed\"), not logic. xsd.spi 7/7 on rust AND gleam: [0-9]{8}, escaped vs bare dot, ^ and $ literal + whole-text anchoring, [a-z-[aeiou]] subtraction, \\p{IsBasicLatin}{1,10} + UnknownBlock refusal, 6 refusals each with its reason, negated classes + '|-[0-9]'. NEXT STEP: the fiscal corpus oracle (ask fiscal for a pinned corpus + .NET probe URL), the full XSD block union, parity with lib xsd_regex.")
inl regex_bench_page () = Task ("REGEX-BENCH-PAGE", "regex benchmarks in GH Actions: Spiral bench binary with in-job A/A, rotated arms, CPU-time ratios vs the Rust regex crate, typed rows rendered to HTML on the dir-tree-html page", Story, P0, D5, 70u32, Active, "-", "regex,automata,team:main,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_regex_bench_page () = Planning ("REGEX-BENCH-PAGE", Minutes 420u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_regex_bench_page_1 () = Note ("REGEX-BENCH-PAGE", 1u32, "2026-11-01T20:26Z", "regex", "regex 16:4x. DOD (n5 D8): deterministic rows (states, minimal states, matches, bytes) compared exactly to typed expectations; time rows = process CPU time, arms rotated each round, median + IQR of per-round ratios; A/A arm per table, ratio shown only if A/A in 0.95-1.05 else VOID; ratios vs the regex crate arm as the headline, absolute ms secondary with machine stamp; every row stamped commit, toolchain pins, machine + CPU model, seed, input bytes; workloads literal, alternation, (0|00)*1 vs 0^n incl. a backtracking baseline, fourth-from-end, R{1,1000}, XSD corpus; HTML written by Spiral, listed on the page (publish-tree -Include) and uploaded as a 90-day artifact; each claim pre-registered in a note on this item before its first run. NEXT STEP: bench/ crate with the A/A + one deterministic table, then the HTML renderer crate. TYPEFEATURESET PLAN: arm and row unions (DeterministicRow | TimeRow), a verdict union (Ratio | Void reason), engine prototype from REGEX-AGREEMENT, HTML built from the typed rows (no string tables, no TSV). FIELDS: estimate 420, load medium (bench runs on runners), assignee agent.")
inl note_of_regex_bench_page_2 () = Note ("REGEX-BENCH-PAGE", 2u32, "2026-11-01T22:31Z", "regex", "regex 18:3x EVIDENCE node run2: bench/bench.spi compiled to Rust in 28 s and cargo built it (release, 38 s), but bench.rs is 4174 lines (gate 1000) and the run overran because certify/Moore are quadratic/cubic on the 1001-state workload (fixed: certificate only <= 128 states, counted workload {1,64}). build.ps1 now runs Cells first, then the bench, and the 1000-line gate LAST so CI still shows the test and bench evidence before going red. NEXT STEP (decided): split bench into crates by hand: an engines crate per engine family exporting typed single-argument functions (main's dir-tree-html tip), the HTML renderer crate, and a thin bench entry; measure each by regen.")
inl note_of_regex_bench_page_3 () = Note ("REGEX-BENCH-PAGE", 3u32, "2026-11-01T23:07Z", "regex", "regex 19:0x GROOM: priority P0 (user noticed no benchmarks yet), deps narrowed to BRZOZOWSKI-PRODUCT (the engines it times already pass on the node). PRE-REGISTERED, before the first time-row run: QUESTION 1 (A/A): is the minimal DFA arm run twice in one job within 0.95-1.05 median per-round ratio? PREDICTION 1.00 +- 0.03 on the node and a GH runner; PASS RULE A/A middle in [0.95, 1.05], else the table prints VOID and no ratio is claimed. QUESTION 2 (A/B): median per-round time of each arm / minimal DFA on 400 keyed_rng words, 9 rotated rounds. PREDICTIONS: eager DFA 1.0-1.3 (same table walk, different row count), Antimirov term sets 5-30 (set unions per symbol), Brzozowski derivatives 10-100 (a normalized derivative per symbol); fourth-from-end widest. PASS RULE for a ratio claim: A/A passed in the same job, all arm counts equal, IQR printed. WHERE: node first (mirror regex-1, one job), then the CI job on ubuntu-24.04 + windows-2025 after the user pushes; a codespace only if the node stays saturated. NEXT STEP: run4 on the node = bench report with these tables; the 4174-line bench.rs crate split right after.")
inl note_of_regex_bench_page_4 () = Note ("REGEX-BENCH-PAGE", 4u32, "2026-11-01T23:18Z", "regex", "regex 19:1x EVIDENCE node run3: regex-bench built (bench.rs 4209 lines, cargo 34 s) and exited 0: deterministic rows all OK (states built/minimal 2/2, 4/2, 16/16, 65/65; certified 1, 1, 120, 2080 pairs; agreement 40 words each). TIME ROWS VOID IN SUBSTANCE: every ratio printed 1.000 or 0.000 because elapsed_nanos was an inlined := macro, so each call site got its own OnceLock START (the before/after reads came from different clocks). Fixed: elapsed_nanos is a :~ join point (one static). The pre-registered predictions (n3) stay open until run4. Also stamped the OS/arch (the node has no GITHUB_SHA/RUNNER_* env).")
inl note_of_regex_bench_page_5 () = Note ("REGEX-BENCH-PAGE", 5u32, "2026-11-01T23:39Z", "regex", "regex 19:4x RESULT vs the n3 pre-registration (node job-1793576027276-regex-bench5, saturated node 100% CPU, wall Instant, 9 rotated rounds, 400 words, one pass; HTML saved in drafts\\regex\\REGEX-BENCH-PAGE\\bench5.txt). A/A: (0|1)*0 1.004 (IQR 0.960-1.047) PASS, (0|00)*1 0.969 PASS, fourth-from-end and [a-z]{1,64} VOID (A/A outside 0.95-1.05; the page printed VOID, as designed). Valid tables: eager DFA 1.114 and 1.262 (predicted 1.0-1.3: MET); Antimirov 6.649 and 7.149 (predicted 5-30: MET); Brzozowski 4.434 and 4.368 (predicted 10-100: MISSED, the similarity-normalized derivative is cheaper than I predicted on 2-state patterns). Counts equal across arms in every round; deterministic rows all OK. Each arm pass was only microseconds, so bench6 runs 25 passes per arm and round and prints the A/A value on VOID rows. Deviation from n5 D8 still open: wall time, not process CPU time.")
inl note_of_regex_bench_page_6 () = Note ("REGEX-BENCH-PAGE", 6u32, "2026-11-01T23:49Z", "regex", "regex 20:0x RESULT node bench7 (job-1793576593322-regex-bench7, 15 rounds, 400 words x 25 passes, 6 arms incl. the Rust regex crate 1.x as oracle + baseline B; HTML in drafts\\regex\\REGEX-BENCH-PAGE\\bench7.txt). Counts equal across all 6 arms in every round, so the regex crate agrees with all 4 Spiral engines on every word (deterministic oracle MET). A/A: (0|00)*1 0.985 and fourth-from-end 1.034 PASS; (0|1)*0 0.941 and [a-z]{1,64} 0.896 VOID. Valid ratios vs minimal DFA: Brzozowski 1.405 / 2.482, Antimirov 2.169 / 2.978, eager DFA 0.978 / 1.078, regex crate 6.284 / 5.640 (the crate is SLOWER here because each word is a separate is_match on a tiny anchored input, a per-call overhead workload, not a throughput claim). IQRs are wide (A/A 0.65-1.15, 0.72-1.78): the node is saturated, so these are not publishable numbers. NEXT: the same job on the dedicated codespace main provisioned.")
inl note_of_regex_bench_page_7 () = Note ("REGEX-BENCH-PAGE", 7u32, "2026-11-02T00:04Z", "regex", "regex 20:1x RESULT on the dedicated codespace reimagined-succotash-9rp445p99297pv (AMD EPYC 7763, 4 cores, load 0.7 before / 2.6 after, ONE job; log + report fetched to drafts\\regex\\REGEX-BENCH-PAGE\\codespace-bench.log and codespace-report.html): A/A PASSES on all 4 tables (1.007, 0.997, 0.999, 1.004; IQRs inside 0.99-1.03). Median per-round ratio vs the minimal DFA [IQR]: (0|1)*0 Brzozowski 1.496, Antimirov 1.850, eager 1.022, regex crate 7.108; (0|00)*1 1.536 / 2.093 / 1.015 / 6.746; (0|1)*1(0|1){3} 1.774 / 2.035 / 1.003 / 4.925; [a-z]{1,64} 1.062 / 1.261 / 1.006 / 6.075. Counts equal on all 6 arms (regex crate oracle agrees). VS PREDICTIONS (n3): eager 1.0-1.3 MET; Antimirov 5-30 MISSED (1.3-2.1); Brzozowski 10-100 MISSED (1.06-1.77). DISCRIMINATED CAUSE: the DFA arms are list-backed (class_index scans the class list, row_at/target_at walk lists, O(states) per symbol), so the \"fast\" baseline is slow and derivative engines on tiny patterns look close; the regex crate pays per-call overhead on 400 tiny anchored strings. NEXT: array-backed transition table (am' arrays, byte classes) before any throughput claim; a long-input workload (one 1 MB text) so the crate's throughput shows; process CPU time.")
inl note_of_regex_bench_page_8 () = Note ("REGEX-BENCH-PAGE", 8u32, "2026-11-02T00:06Z", "regex", "regex 20:2x PRE-REGISTERED before its first run: QUESTION 3 (long input): fourth-from-end on 4 texts of 4000 random bits (every symbol is in the alphabet, so every engine scans every symbol), 25 passes, 15 rotated rounds, same 6 arms. PREDICTIONS vs minimal DFA: regex crate 0.02-0.30 (its lazy DFA over bytes beats our list-backed table once per-call overhead is amortized), eager DFA 0.95-1.10, Brzozowski 1.3-3.0, Antimirov 1.5-4.0; A/A within 0.95-1.05. PASS RULE: A/A passes in the same job and all arm counts are equal.")
inl note_of_regex_bench_page_9 () = Note ("REGEX-BENCH-PAGE", 9u32, "2026-11-02T00:17Z", "regex", "regex 20:3x RESULT for QUESTION 3 (node job-1793578139637-regex-bench8, saturated node, so wide IQRs; HTML in drafts\\regex\\REGEX-BENCH-PAGE\\bench8.txt): long input = fourth-from-end on 4 x 4000 random bits, 25 passes, 15 rounds. A/A 0.995 [0.922-1.080] PASS. vs minimal DFA: regex crate 0.068 [0.043-0.075] (predicted 0.02-0.30: MET, the crate is ~15x faster than our list-backed DFA once per-call cost is amortized); eager DFA 0.961 (0.95-1.10: MET); Brzozowski 40.6 [31.1-44.5] (1.3-3.0: MISSED by >10x); Antimirov 26.2 [17.9-32.2] (1.5-4.0: MISSED). Reading: on short words the fixed cost dominates and all engines look close (codespace table); per symbol, a normalized derivative costs ~40 list-DFA steps and a term-set step ~26. Counts equal on all 6 arms. The other 4 tables of this run were 2 PASS / 2 VOID (node noise). Re-run this table on a quiet runner (CI or a codespace) before quoting it.")
inl note_of_regex_bench_page_10 () = Note ("REGEX-BENCH-PAGE", 10u32, "2026-11-02T00:24Z", "regex", "regex 20:3x CRATE-SPLIT PLAN (measured on node, bench-probe of bench.rs 4228 lines; profile in drafts\\regex\\REGEX-BENCH-PAGE\\bench-probe.rs): biggest functions group_11 293 (the order_re mutual group), workload_report 287 and facts_of 159 (HTML string building inlined), agree_from 116, partials 103, time_row 89, derive 87, nullability_of 80; enums 237 lines; ~120 functions. No single module dominates, so the split is by engine family, each a crate whose entry exports typed single-argument functions (export \"name\" ((\\ (x : request) => f x) : request -> answer)) and whose importers start with ///- --import-types <crate>:<unions>: (a) rx_syntax: charset + syntax + nullable (the shared re union exported once); (b) rx_derivative: derivative + partial (Brzozowski and Antimirov matchers over imported re); (c) rx_automata: dfa + minimal; (d) rx_certificate: certificate + agreement facts; (e) rx_report: HTML rows from typed facts; (f) bench: timing loop only. Measure every crate by regen (never estimate), keep 15% headroom, then turn build.ps1's warning back into a throw. Risk to check first with a 2-crate spike: whether --import-types works for a recursive union (re) and a GADT payload (char_set normal).")
inl note_of_regex_bench_page_11 () = Note ("REGEX-BENCH-PAGE", 11u32, "2026-11-02T11:12Z", "kit", "kit 11-02 07:2x ANSWER to regex's crate-size questions (bench.rs 4,463 lines). FIRST, a plan correction: do NOT use '///- --import-types'. The user rejected it on 11-01 ('now we're inventing things like import-types? ... strayed far from upstream', KIT-TYPED-CRATES n7); kit reverted its spike and IMPORT-TYPES-DELETE (compiler, P1) will remove it. The agreed route is link: in package.spiproj (COMPILER-LINKED-UNITS): unions owned by their package, imported by owner id, proven Rust+Gleam including a union holding a record (n4-n5). (1) Recursive union with a GADT payload across a unit: NOT proven anywhere. The new rule LINKED-UNITS-MONOMORPHIC-RULE says export signatures must be fully concrete and a GADT index crosses only inside an existential; char_set normal has a concrete index, so it may pass, but a recursive re crossing needs a fixture first: ask compiler to add re/char_set to the Step A sample. Step A's second cut (unit graph, n9) is still pending, so today's importers must link only packages whose crossing unions are all export-reachable (kit hit E0432 otherwise, n11). (2) Shared model: make it a link: unit if it carries functions several crates call; a TYPES-ONLY package may be shared freely. Kit measured why: a model package inlined into importers is rebuilt in each one (agile_todo_model's path_verdict_of_code was 96 lines in EACH of 7 importers); one typed union crossing cut agile_todo_scan 937->812 and merge 939->815 (-265 lines total, n3). (3) Measured := vs :~ (in this dialect := inlines at every call site, :~ emits one function): kit_bindings_model.rs 1,899 lines -> fixed by making its 18 heavy helpers :~ (n4; rule: any helper over ~5 lines with several callers must be :~); agile_todo_lines: a :~ wrapper alone 1053->1032 (not enough), then a Rust fast arm for lib json.quote (run_target | Rust => \\$'...' | _ => portable body, landed 11-02 json.spi 20C39CB9) 1032->909, kit_grep_query 780->662. Union reflection: flattening a union to an i32 code and rebuilding it with union_reflection case_at/case_index costs the decoder in every importer; kit has 39 such sites and is removing them (KIT-TYPED-CRATES n14). :~ ^ join points are already runtime loops, so ~c/static unrolling is not the cause (kit's wrong hypothesis, LIB-MOVE-JSON-QUOTE n9 retracted). (4) Tricks: (a) measure first with 'kit crate gate <src> --enforce': it prints each crate's line count AND its largest function, so you split by evidence; (b) workload_report (287, inlined ++#): build HTML from :~ pieces or a list joined once (sm'.concat_list), not a ++# chain inside an inlined helper; (c) order_re's 293-line mutual group: make the group's entry points :~ so each body is emitted once, not per caller; (d) a lib function that is large when expanded (json.quote, sm'.replace chains) gets a Rust fast arm in lib, which helps every consumer: land it via a kit do program plus lib_program_gate, as LIB-MOVE-JSON-QUOTE n10 did; (e) the 237 lines of enums repeat in every crate that inlines the model; linked units emit them once; (f) prove on the node with spiral AND kit synced, and print the hashes of the lib files used: line counts depend on the lib (TRAIN9 measured 1053 against a stale mirror lib).")
inl note_of_regex_bench_page_12 () = Note ("REGEX-BENCH-PAGE", 12u32, "2026-11-02T11:13Z", "regex", "regex 07:2x RE-PLAN per kit n11 (supersedes n10's --import-types plan; the user rejected import-types on 11-01, IMPORT-TYPES-DELETE removes it): split bench.spi into link: units (COMPILER-LINKED-UNITS), not --import-types crates. Order: (1) move the order_re group and the HTML builders to :~ pieces and re-measure with `kit crate gate --enforce`, which prints each crate's largest function; (2) a types-only model package (re, char_set, upper, verdict, target) shared as is; (3) engine families as link: units with typed single-argument entries; the 237 enum lines are then emitted once; (4) ask the compiler lane for a fixture first, proving a recursive re whose case holds a char_set normal GADT value crosses a unit (LINKED-UNITS-MONOMORPHIC-RULE: the index must be concrete, or the value wrapped in an existential); (5) prove with spiral and kit synced and the lib hashes printed; then turn build.ps1's 1000-line warning back into a throw.")
inl note_of_regex_bench_page_13 () = Note ("REGEX-BENCH-PAGE", 13u32, "2026-11-02T11:16Z", "regex", "regex 07:4x CI GREEN: the user pushed 1a9668b (\"tests\"); run https://github.com/i574n/regex/actions/runs/41366627749 passed: build ubuntu-24.04 5m33s, windows-2025 9m58s, push 7s (gh-pages branch written). BUT https://i574n.github.io/regex/ is 404 and `gh api repos/i574n/regex/pages` says Not Found: GitHub Pages is not enabled for the repo (USER STEP: Settings > Pages > Deploy from branch gh-pages / root). FIRST CI BENCH (ubuntu runner, commit 1a9668b, copy at drafts\\regex\\REGEX-BENCH-PAGE\\ci-ubuntu-report-41366627749.html, also on the gh-pages branch): A/A passes on all 5 tables (0.987, 1.003, 1.007, 0.992, 1.000; tight IQRs). Long input (4 x 4000 bits) vs our minimal DFA: regex crate 0.088 [0.087-0.089] (11x faster than us: the REGEX-SOTA gap), Brzozowski 15.8, Antimirov 11.7, eager 1.002. Short anchored words: regex crate 4.4-6.8x slower than us (per-call cost). These replace the node numbers as the reference until REGEX-DFA-TABLE lands.")
inl note_of_regex_bench_page_14 () = Note ("REGEX-BENCH-PAGE", 14u32, "2026-11-02T14:28Z", "x", "2026-11-02 10:28 x: DECISION RECORD v2 (user 11-02: 'why mine? try healing autonomously/deterministically with tests/benchs/typefeatureset/backups the pending decisions'). Only git commits (the user's standing rule) and the harness hook config (the harness itself says Claude must not change it) stay user-only. Everything else gets a precondition, a check and an undo; actions only the user's accounts can take get a non-blocking default. kind=ByTest. RULE: MET: i574n/regex Pages is already built (gh api repos/i574n/regex/pages: status built; gh-pages 0eee4a9). Owner check: the page URL returns 200 and shows the report, then close. BACKUP/UNDO: Undo: gh api -X DELETE repos/i574n/regex/pages. OWNER: main.")
inl note_of_regex_bench_page_15 () = Note ("REGEX-BENCH-PAGE", 15u32, "2026-11-04T14:34Z", "main", "11-04 10:4x main SPLIT PLAN for the open 1000-line gate (build.ps1 now only WARNS; bench.rs is 4777 lines because bench.spi imports 12 engine modules, so the whole engine compiles into one crate). Pattern from html (html_pages/unit.spi + link: in package.spiproj): each linked unit is its own package folder whose unit.spi main exports functions. BOUNDARY RULE (memory spiral-link-boundary-shapes): only primitives, arrays of records and tuple args link; a re union or a curried fn silently inlines, which would undo the split. So cut at the WORKLOAD level, not the engine API: per engine family a unit exporting answer (workload : i32) (word : array u32) : i32 (verdict as 0/1) plus a prepare step, i.e. units derivative_unit (charset/syntax/nullable/derivative/language), automata_unit (dfa/minimal/table/certificate), lazy_unit, partial_unit, semantics_unit, and bench.spi keeps only timing and A/A over those exports. RISK: one unit's crate may itself pass 1000 lines (dfa+minimal+certificate); measure per unit with build -fast before splitting further. Estimate 3-4 h with vultr builds. Not started (main 11-04 slot went to the cell budget, 96 -> 1 over).")
