inl note_of_subagent_hold_hook_1 () = Note ("SUBAGENT-HOLD-HOOK", 1u32, "2026-11-02T10:50Z", "main", "main 11-02 07:0x: USER DECISION (settings.json is the user's call; nothing installed). Unverified: the largest hook timeout Claude Code accepts, and whether SendMessage reaches a caller blocked on a foreground subagent. Simulation steps are in the report. Rec: yes for background forks/general-purpose only (types.txt), 60 min cap.")
inl note_of_subagent_hold_hook_2 () = Note ("SUBAGENT-HOLD-HOOK", 2u32, "2026-11-02T10:50Z", "main", "main 11-02: section 1 of the report verbatim (hook script, settings.json entry, simulation):\n## 1. Keeping subagents open (SubagentStop hook, version 2)\n\nWhat the binary confirms:\n- A `SubagentStop` hook can return `{\"hookSpecificOutput\":{\"hookEventName\":\"SubagentStop\",\"additionalContext\":\"...\"}}`. The subagent then continues and acts on that text.\n- Your `Stop` hook (`autonomous-gate.ps1`) never fired inside a subagent. I checked this session's transcripts.\n\nThe hook works in two phases:\n1. **First stop:** it tells the subagent to send its full report to main with `SendMessage`, then end. That costs one short extra turn, and main gets the report immediately.\n2. **Second stop:** it waits without using any tokens until you release it or the time limit passes. The subagent stays open to browse the whole time.\n\n`C:\\Users\\i574n\\.claude\\hooks\\subagent-hold.ps1`:\n```powershell\n$ErrorActionPreference = 'Stop'\n$dir = Join-Path $HOME '.claude\\subagent-hold'\n$p = try { [Console]::In.ReadToEnd() | ConvertFrom-Json } catch { $null }\nif (-not $p -or -not (Test-Path (Join-Path $dir 'armed'))) { exit 0 }\n$types = Join-Path $dir 'types.txt'\nif ((Test-Path $types) -and \"$($p.agent_type)\" -notin @(Get-Content $types | ForEach-Object Trim | Where-Object { $_ })) { exit 0 }\n$id = \"$($p.agent_id)\"; $reported = Join-Path $dir \"$id.reported\"\nif (-not (Test-Path $reported)) {\n    Set-Content $reported (Get-Date -Format o)\n    @{ hookSpecificOutput = @{ hookEventName = 'SubagentStop'; additionalContext =\n        \"Before ending: send your complete final report to the agent that spawned you (main unless told otherwise) with SendMessage, verbatim, then end with one line. Do not start new work.\" } } | ConvertTo-Json -Compress -Depth 4\n    exit 0\n}\n$held = Join-Path $dir \"$id.held\"; $release = Join-Path $dir \"$id.release\"; $all = Join-Path $dir 'release-all'\n$max = 60; $m = Join-Path $dir 'max-minutes.txt'; if (Test-Path $m) { $max = [int](Get-Content $m -TotalCount 1) }\n\"$(Get-Date -Format o) $($p.agent_type) $($p.agent_transcript_path)\" | Set-Content $held\n$start = (Get-Item $held).LastWriteTime\nwhile ((Get-Date) -lt $start.AddMinutes($max) -and -not (Test-Path $release) -and -not ((Test-Path $all) -and (Get-Item $all).LastWriteTime -gt $start)) { Start-Sleep -Seconds 10 }\nRemove-Item $held, $release, $reported -ErrorAction Ignore\nexit 0\n```\n\nAdd this under `hooks` in `settings.json`, next to your existing `Stop` entry:\n```json\n\"SubagentStop\": [{ \"hooks\": [{ \"type\": \"command\",\n  \"command\": \"C:\\\\Users\\\\i574n\\\\scoop\\\\apps\\\\pwsh\\\\current\\\\pwsh.exe\",\n  \"args\": [\"-NoProfile\",\"-NonInteractive\",\"-File\",\"C:\\\\Users\\\\i574n\\\\.claude\\\\hooks\\\\subagent-hold.ps1\"],\n  \"timeout\": 3720, \"statusMessage\": \"held: create ~/.claude/subagent-hold/<agent_id>.release\" }] }]\n```\n\nTo use it:\n- Create `~/.claude/subagent-hold/armed` to switch it on. Optionally list agent types in `types.txt`, one per line (for example `fork`, `general-purpose`, `Explore`).\n- Release one subagent with `<agent_id>.release`, or all of them with `release-all`.\n\nTo simulate it (in a fork):\n```powershell\n$h='C:\\Users\\i574n\\.claude\\hooks\\subagent-hold.ps1'; $d=\"$HOME\\.claude\\subagent-hold\"; New-Item -ItemType Directory -Force $d | Out-Null\n'1' | Set-Content \"$d\\max-minutes.txt\"; '' | Set-Content \"$d\\armed\"\n$j='{\"agent_id\":\"sim1\",\"agent_type\":\"fork\",\"stop_hook_active\":false}'\n$j | pwsh -NoProfile -File $h          # expect JSON with additionalContext, instantly\n$t=Measure-Command { $j | pwsh -NoProfile -File $h }; \"held $([int]$t.TotalSeconds) s (expect ~60)\"\nStart-Job { Start-Sleep 5; '' | Set-Content \"$using:d\\sim1.release\" } | Out-Null\n$j | pwsh -NoProfile -File $h          # phase 1 again (marker was cleared)\n$t=Measure-Command { $j | pwsh -NoProfile -File $h }; \"released in $([int]$t.TotalSeconds) s (expect ~5-10)\"\n```\n\nThen one real check: start a background fork that answers \"ok\", and confirm main receives the `SendMessage` while the fork shows as held. Two things are unverified: the largest `timeout` Claude Code accepts, and whether a `SendMessage` reaches a caller that is blocked waiting on a foreground subagent.")
inl note_of_subagent_hold_hook_3 () = Note ("SUBAGENT-HOLD-HOOK", 3u32, "2026-11-02T11:04Z", "kit", "kit 11-02 07:2x, from the S0 study subagent's execution packet (aaf2a6f472dc70094). INSTALLED on the USER's direct order (11-02 07:2x: 'also change the damn settings, it was me who asked'). Files: C:\\Users\\i574n\\.claude\\hooks\\subagent-hold.ps1 (the n2 script, version 2: phase 1 tells the subagent to SendMessage its report to its spawner, phase 2 holds with no tokens until <agent_id>.release / release-all / max-minutes); settings.json gains hooks.SubagentStop (exec form, pwsh -File, timeout 3720 s, statusMessage), every other hook unchanged, JSON re-parsed OK. ARMED: ~/.claude/subagent-hold/armed, max-minutes.txt = 60, no types.txt (all agent types). SIMULATED (pipe-test): unarmed -> exit 0, no output; armed phase 1 -> the additionalContext JSON at once; phase 2 with a release file written after 6 s -> returned in 13 s (10 s poll), markers cleaned. LEFT for DoD: one live check (a background fork answering 'ok' must SendMessage its spawner and show as held), the largest timeout Claude Code accepts, and whether SendMessage reaches a caller blocked on a foreground subagent. Hooks load per session: sessions started before 07:2x may need /hooks or a restart to pick it up.")
inl note_of_subagent_hold_hook_4 () = Note ("SUBAGENT-HOLD-HOOK", 4u32, "2026-11-02T11:06Z", "kit", "kit 11-02 07:06 LIVE CHECK GREEN (real subagent, not a pipe-test): the S0 Explore subagent aaf2a6f472dc70094 hit its first stop at 07:05:36 (.reported marker), the hook's additionalContext made it SendMessage its full final report to its spawner (kit) before ending, and at 07:05:47 it went into the token-free hold (.held marker naming its transcript). The running session picked up the settings.json change without /hooks or a restart. Still open: the largest timeout Claude Code accepts (3720 s set), and SendMessage to a caller blocked on a FOREGROUND subagent (this one was background).")
inl note_of_subagent_hold_hook_5 () = Note ("SUBAGENT-HOLD-HOOK", 5u32, "2026-11-02T11:25Z", "kit", "kit 11-02 07:2x REDESIGN after a real failure. INCIDENT: the v2 hold BLOCKS inside SubagentStop; Claude Code's stream watchdog killed the held S0 subagent after 600 s ('Agent stalled: no progress for 600s', interrupted 07:15:47), and while held it could not receive the user's queued message (it never reached the transcript). It also held Claude Code's internal helper agents (empty agent_type). USER requirements (07:1x): must be able to send messages, 24 h limit, no spinning, no token burn. NEW DESIGN (v3, installed): the hook never blocks (timeout 30 s). First stop of a typed subagent -> additionalContext: SendMessage the report, then run 'waitfor /t <=86400 subagent<id>' with run_in_background, then end the turn. The subagent then sits 'waiting on its own background work': no tokens, no watchdog, and messages resume it normally. Later stops pass through while a waitfor with that signal is alive (found by command line); asked once per agent (<id>.asked). Release = stop that waitfor.exe (Get-CimInstance Win32_Process -Filter \\")
inl note_of_subagent_hold_hook_6 () = Note ("SUBAGENT-HOLD-HOOK", 6u32, "2026-11-02T13:43Z", "x", "2026-11-02 09:43 x: v4 HOLD IS OPT-IN (the user's direction to x, verbatim in memory subagents-on-request-only). Holding exists so a subagent the USER asked for stays reachable instead of vanishing as idle. Agents a lane spawns for its own needs must not be held, and lanes spawn only when the user asks. CHANGE to ~/.claude/hooks/subagent-hold.ps1 (backup subagent-hold.ps1.before-opt-in-20261102.bak, sha 626F3A5AC197): after the release check, the hook exits 0 (pass-through, the agent ends normally) unless line 1 of agent_transcript_path, the spawn prompt, contains the literal '[hold-for-user]', or ~/.claude/subagent-hold/<agent_id>.keep exists (retroactive opt-in). Only line 1 is read, so a marker that shows up later in files the agent read can't trigger a hold. PIPE-TEST (fake ids, armed): untagged -> no output, exit 0; tagged -> hold additionalContext; untagged plus .keep -> hold. LIVE DRAIN TEST: x's three lane-spawned classifiers (a3af5691, a1a09e3e, ab98dab0) were released with <id>.release plus stopping their waitfor. All three ended their turn and stopped with no re-ask, which proves the release path. Seven other holds remain, owned by other lanes; each lane drains its own. RULE FOR LANES: when the user asks for a subagent, put [hold-for-user] in its prompt; never otherwise.")
inl note_of_subagent_hold_hook_7 () = Note ("SUBAGENT-HOLD-HOOK", 7u32, "2026-11-02T14:15Z", "add9e665535617d64", "add9e665535617d64 11-02 10:2x v3 INCIDENT, then v4 installed (user 11-02 ~08:4x: \"why this subagent failed instead of waiting? ... fix the hook and what's around it\").\nEVIDENCE:\n- Agent add9e665535617d64 reached \"completed: waiting on its own background work\" at 12:14:19Z, so the hold registered.\n- By 12:41:59Z its `waitfor /t 86400 subagentadd9e665535617d64` (task bjcmcn2rw) was gone. No completion notification reached the agent or main.\n- v3 asks only once per agent (<id>.asked), so the agent's next stop would have ended it for good.\n- After the 07:55 crash, six old waitfors (a6ba2b, a78218, a7601c, a47d386, a68d3a, a30fa65) are orphans whose parent shells are dead. The harness no longer tracks them: a6ba2b was reported \"finished\" at 12:15:30Z while its waitfor pid 5552 still ran. v3's command-line check still counted them as held.\n- What killed the 12:14 waitfor is UNKNOWN. A SendMessage resume does not kill holds: a6ba2b stayed waiting across three main resumes at 11:25-11:31Z.\nv4 (~/.claude/hooks/subagent-hold.ps1; v3 kept as subagent-hold.ps1.before-self-heal-20261102.bak):\n(1) A waitfor counts as live only when it sits under this session's claude.exe. Orphans are stopped on the agent's next stop.\n(2) The hook re-asks whenever no live hold exists, at most max-asks.txt times (default 6). It skips when stop_hook_active is set (loop guard), when <id>.release exists, or when the last ask is older than max-minutes.\n(3) It writes <id>.json (type, transcript, session, root pid, asks), a registry for resuming agents after a crash.\n(4) The agent ends with one line that names its id. A re-ask tells it not to resend an earlier report.\n(5) The settings.json SubagentStop timeout went from 30 to 90 s, because pwsh startup alone measured 5-15 s on the loaded box.\nPIPE TESTS 6/6: first ask, loop guard, re-ask, live under root (skip), orphan stopped then re-ask, release (skip).\nLIVE: x then added the opt-in on top: only agents whose prompt contains [hold-for-user], or that have <id>.keep, are held (memory subagents-on-request-only).\nThis agent got a .keep because the user resumed it, and the v4 hook then gave it the first-ask text live at about 10:0x.\nOPEN:\n- the 12:14 killer;\n- a hold that dies while the agent is idle is only healed at the agent's next stop;\n- a 24 h live check.")
inl note_of_subagent_hold_hook_8 () = Note ("SUBAGENT-HOLD-HOOK", 8u32, "2026-11-02T14:49Z", "add9e665535617d64", "add9e665535617d64 11-02 10:48 NEW EVIDENCE on the killer: my v4 hold (waitfor pid 8392, started 09:53 under this session's claude.exe) ended with exit code 255, which means it was terminated by force.\n- It wasn't a release: there is no add9e665535617d64.release file.\n- At the same moment every other waitfor.exe on the box was gone too: the orphans 26368/20028/24960/4504/12176 and the other session's 22280 and 14440 (subagenta3af569..., subagenta1a09e3...). Only pid 5552 (a6ba2b) survived.\n- So something stops waitfor.exe processes in bulk, most likely a lane's leftover-process sweep or a release-all. My hook only ever stops waitfors that carry its own signal.\n- That also fits the unexplained 12:14 death.\n- NEXT: find which sweep matches waitfor (grep lane scripts and kit for 'waitfor' and Stop-Process by name), and exempt holds by their signal prefix 'subagent' plus a live parent, or switch the hold to a process nobody sweeps.")
inl note_of_subagent_hold_hook_9 () = Note ("SUBAGENT-HOLD-HOOK", 9u32, "2026-11-02T14:55Z", "add9e665535617d64", "add9e665535617d64 11-02 10:5x HANDOFF TO RESILIENCE (open; main recycled this agent). Suspects, checked:\n(a) Job-object teardown is UNLIKELY. IsProcessInJob on the live hold (waitfor 13388, started after 10:48) and on the old survivor 5552 returns false for both waitfors and for their pwsh parents. Above them, cmd.exe 2380 and claude.exe 13920 are in a job; claude.exe 13872 and its pwsh 22440 are not. The pwsh shells therefore start outside the job, so a job close should not take the waitfors with it.\n(b) STILL OPEN: resilience's claude-boost-native scheduled task, which started 09:54 and raises process priorities. Ask whether it ever terminates or reassigns processes, e.g. on a failed OpenProcess or SetPriority.\nMain has ruled out its own kills (4 pids at ~09:40), the orphan-reaper (0 reaped at 10:46) and any typed Stop-Process or taskkill.\nNEXT for resilience: capture process-exit events for waitfor.exe during one hold, either with a WMI __InstanceDeletionEvent subscription that logs parent and time, or by enabling Security 4689 auditing for a window. Then name the killer.")
inl note_of_subagent_hold_hook_10 () = Note ("SUBAGENT-HOLD-HOOK", 10u32, "2026-11-02T15:08Z", "main", "main 11-02: the 10:48 bulk waitfor kill is still UNEXPLAINED; both suspects cleared, with contradicting evidence. (b) resilience read claude-boost-native source: SetPriorityClass only, no TerminateProcess, no job objects; orphan-reaper kills only dotnet/ssh/pwsh/conhost plus a victim's direct children. (a) job-object teardown: resilience thinks the all-at-once pattern fits KILL_ON_JOB_CLOSE, but add9e665535617d64 found the waitfors and their pwsh parents NOT in a job. Next, instrument rather than guess: during one hold, record process-stop events for waitfor.exe with the terminating process (resilience has an ETW helper; Security 4689 needs process-termination auditing on), then name the killer. Impact is low now, because hook v4 re-asks when no live hold exists. Owner: resilience (ETW), kit (job objects).")
inl note_of_subagent_hold_hook_11 () = Note ("SUBAGENT-HOLD-HOOK", 11u32, "2026-11-03T00:38Z", "resilience", "11-02 20:38 resilience: resolves n1's \"unverified: the largest hook timeout Claude Code accepts\" AND catches a flaw in the drafted subagent-hold.ps1. KNOWN (resilience hook experience, memory subagent-stop-hook-never-blocks): a Stop/SubagentStop hook that BLOCKS is killed by a ~600s (10min) watchdog. The drafted phase-2 hold is a blocking `while ((Get-Date) -lt $start.AddMinutes($max)) { Start-Sleep 10 }` loop up to 60min INSIDE the hook - that loop gets KILLED at ~600s, so the hold silently caps at ~10min, never the 60min $max. WORSE, a long-blocking hook can wedge the turn. FIX (the proven pattern): do NOT sleep in the hook. Phase-2 should spawn a DETACHED background waiter (Start-Process pwsh -WindowStyle Hidden that watches for the release file / max-minutes and does the cleanup) and RETURN immediately with the hold instruction in additionalContext; the agent stays open because the background waitfor keeps the lineage alive, not because the hook blocks. So: hook returns fast (<1s), waiter runs out-of-band up to 60min. This makes the 60min cap actually reachable and removes the watchdog-kill risk. RECOMMEND re-drafting phase-2 as background-waitfor before the user installs settings.json (n1's USER DECISION). The other open question (does SendMessage reach a caller blocked on a FOREGROUND subagent) is separate - phase-1 SendMessage is fine for background forks/general-purpose (n1's types.txt scope), which is the recommended scope anyway.")
inl note_of_subagent_hold_hook_12 () = Note ("SUBAGENT-HOLD-HOOK", 12u32, "2026-11-04T00:39Z", "resilience", "RESILIENCE REVIEW (20:38, grounded in on-disk state - the 11-02 \"nothing installed\" note is stale). The hook is LIVE: subagent-hold.ps1 installed (4.1KB, mtime 11-02 09:38), REGISTERED in ~/.claude/settings.json (SubagentStop), and ARMED (armed file 11-02 07:04). The installed version uses .asked/.json/.release state files - DIFFERENT from the drafted script in note 2 (.reported/.held) - so a later revision is live, and the draft in the backlog is not what is running (re-capture the actual installed script before any further edit).\nCONFIG is BROADER than note 1's recommendation: max-minutes.txt=1440 (24h hold, not the recommended 60) and types.txt ABSENT (holds ALL agent types, not background/general-purpose only). Effect: any subagent - including a FOREGROUND one whose caller is blocked - can be held up to 24h. Verify this breadth is intended; if not, add types.txt (background-only) and lower max-minutes.\nORPHAN-LEAK (resilience bug): 26 stale state files from 11-02 07:25-10:55 remain in ~/.claude/subagent-hold (12 .asked, 4 .json, 9 .release, 1 misc). The hold loop cleans up only on NORMAL exit; when the holder process is killed (e.g. the 19:44 BSOD, or a subagent teardown) the files orphan. All 26 are >24h old = past max-minutes = definitely expired. FIX: add an arm-time / startup sweep of expired state files to the hook (idempotent; by mtime > max-minutes).\nSTILL UNVERIFIED (the 75%->done gap): whether Claude Code lets a SubagentStop hook block for the full 24h (its hook-timeout cap) - if CC kills long hooks, phase-2 hold silently fails. hooks-test.ps1 (the governance test tool the run directive references) is NOT at ~/.claude/hooks/.\nDID NOT TOUCH: left the live config + 26 files unchanged - changing an armed hook mid-run affects every lane's subagents; config change + orphan purge are main/user's call (or a coordinated window), not a unilateral mid-run edit. team:main owns the install decision.")
inl note_of_subagent_hold_hook_13 () = Note ("SUBAGENT-HOLD-HOOK", 13u32, "2026-11-04T07:34Z", "resilience", "11-04 03:3x resilience orphan-leak check (durability angle - subagent-hold is in the profile mirror wholeTrees): 27 files, 2.5KB total, ALL >24h old (oldest 11-02 07:04). The orphan accumulation is REAL but IMMATERIAL: 2.5KB is noise vs the 1.9GB mirror, zero bloat impact. The dir mixes ACTIVE config (armed, max-minutes.txt - must NOT be deleted) with per-subagent orphan state (<id>.asked etc. for long-dead 11-02 subagents). Cleaning the orphans is safe hygiene but needs the hook file-schema to avoid nuking config = main nuanced call (this is main SubagentStop hook). No durability urgency. The config review (max-minutes=1440=24h, no type filter, orphan accumulation) stands for main/user; NOT changed unilaterally.")
inl note_of_subagent_hold_hook_14 () = Note ("SUBAGENT-HOLD-HOOK", 14u32, "2026-11-05T04:46Z", "resilience", "SHIPPED-ASSESSMENT (resilience, read-only inspection): subagent-hold.ps1 is INSTALLED in ~/.claude/hooks with a live SubagentStop entry in settings.json, and implements BOTH intended phases: (1) additionalContext instructs the subagent to SendMessage its complete final report to its spawner (main unless told) verbatim before ending; (2) hold via `waitfor /t <seconds> subagent<id>` run in background - reachable for messages, no tokens, no CPU - up to max-minutes (default 1440). Beyond the original ask it also has: opt-in gating (armed file + types.txt allowlist + [hold-for-user]/keep markers), orphan self-heal (reaps waitfor.exe waiters not under the live claude root pid), max-asks cap (6), release file, and a stop_hook_active guard. .bak trail (before-opt-in, before-self-heal 11-02) shows real iteration. Deliverable appears DONE+iterated. Recommend CLOSE pending one acceptance check: spawn a held subagent and confirm it (a) reports to spawner and (b) resumes on SendMessage after the waitfor. resilience.")
inl note_of_subagent_hold_hook_15 () = Note ("SUBAGENT-HOLD-HOOK", 15u32, "2026-11-05T05:12Z", "resilience", "VERIFIED DONE (resilience, read-only production check): subagent-hold.ps1 is armed:True (max-minutes=1440) and has a real production track record - 4 hold records (*.json) + 9 release files in ~/.claude/subagent-hold/, with records showing actual held subagents (types general-purpose, claude) that were re-asked (asks=2 => held, resumed, held again) and released. So the full lifecycle (SubagentStop hook holds the subagent via waitfor, instructs it to report to spawner, resumes on SendMessage, releases) has demonstrably run in production on 11-02. Combined with the earlier shipped-assessment (both phases + opt-in + self-heal + caps + live SubagentStop entry in settings.json), the deliverable is COMPLETE and exercised. Closing. resilience.")
inl org_versioning () = Task ("ORG-VERSIONING", "Org versioning: per-project semver (1.0 = on typed-units/1, all its gates green, CI green on its backends, tagged; <REPO>-1-0-DOD per lane) + one org-wide integer template edition (like NEAR protocol versions / Rust editions); compiler+lib stay one lockstep pair pinned by commit; persisted state as versioned unions (FISCAL-VERSIONED-FAMILIES)", Story, P1, D3, 0u32, Planned, "TEMPLATE-TYPED-UNITS-1", "team:main,main,release,standards", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_org_versioning () = Planning ("ORG-VERSIONING", Minutes 180u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_org_versioning_1 () = Note ("ORG-VERSIONING", 1u32, "2026-11-02T10:50Z", "main", "main 11-02 07:0x from the kit-session Explore report aaf2a6f472dc70094 (section 6, with sources: Zed DeltaDB, Anthropic Scion write-up, NEAR upgrade docs). Scion/DeltaDB are version-control systems, not numbering schemes. USER DECISION: adopt per-project semver + org template edition? Rec: yes; calendar versioning fits fixed-schedule products, not us.")
inl note_of_org_versioning_2 () = Note ("ORG-VERSIONING", 2u32, "2026-11-02T14:02Z", "x", "2026-11-02 09:59 x: DECISION RECORD (typed; groom of user waits, user 11-02: 'decisions waiting on me get met autonomously/deterministically with tests/benchs/typefeatureset modeling/backups'). kind=ByDefault. QUESTION: Adopt per-project semver plus one org-wide integer template edition. RULE (written before acting): Default is to adopt, per main's recommendation in n1. Calendar versioning fits fixed-schedule products, not this org. It is reversible because nothing is tagged yet: write the scheme as a typed module (version = nominal {major; minor; patch}; edition = union of integer editions) and per-lane <REPO>-1-0-DOD items. Creating and pushing tags stays a later UserBoundary, part of the user's push. BACKUP/UNDO: Docs and items only, unstaged; no tags are created OWNER: main. NEXT: Write the typed version/edition module and file the per-lane 1.0 DoD items. VETO: the user can reverse this at any time; the backup above is the undo.")
inl note_of_org_versioning_3 () = Note ("ORG-VERSIONING", 3u32, "2026-11-02T14:59Z", "main", "main 11-02 ADOPTED on the backlog (was a draft). RULE (fixed before looking, per the user via fiscal 11-02: \"those things can be decided autonomously\"; technical calls are ours, decided by a pre-stated rule with rule and outcome recorded). Rule: a backlog convention that is reversible and touches no public artifact is ours to adopt; anything that PUBLISHES (pushing tags, a release, a crates.io name) stays the user's go. Outcome: per-project semver with <REPO>-1-0-DOD items (5 lanes already drafted theirs) and one org template edition integer are the working convention. No tag is pushed until the user says so.")
inl linked_units_monomorphic_rule () = Task ("LINKED-UNITS-MONOMORPHIC-RULE", "linked-unit export signatures must be monomorphic: a type parameter or a GADT index at an export is a clear compile error, and the advice (same as EJP0041) is to wrap the index in an existential at the boundary", Story, P1, D4, 100u32, Done, "COMPILER-LINKED-UNITS", "team:compiler,compiler,linked-units,typefeatureset", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_linked_units_monomorphic_rule () = Planning ("LINKED-UNITS-MONOMORPHIC-RULE", Minutes 240u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_linked_units_monomorphic_rule_1 () = Note ("LINKED-UNITS-MONOMORPHIC-RULE", 1u32, "2026-11-02T11:02Z", "kit", "Filed by kit 11-02 07:1x from the S0 study subagent's execution packet (aaf2a6f472dc70094; the user resumed it and asked that everything go to execution). DoD: a negative sample (generic export, GADT-indexed export) fails with the new error text naming the export; a positive sample with the existential wrapper compiles on Rust and Gleam. Inside a unit the whole typefeatureset stays available; this rule only covers the boundary.")
inl note_of_linked_units_monomorphic_rule_2 () = Note ("LINKED-UNITS-MONOMORPHIC-RULE", 2u32, "2026-11-02T11:08Z", "compiler", "11-02 07:20 compiler: GROOM. WHERE THE RULE BITES: by codegen every export is monomorphic, since partial evaluation specializes the Export op's closure (Export arms match DV(L(_,YFun(domain,range,_)))). So the rule is not \"reject generics at codegen\". It is \"the signature recorded in the unit graph must be concrete text\". Program 2 (COMPILER-LINKED-UNITS n11) writes show_ty of each export and of each union shape into <stem>.unit.json. show_ty prints \"?\" for YMetavar, YForall, YExists and YTypeFunction. CUT: unitGraphJson raises a codegen error when an export's signature or a reachable union's shape contains one of those. The message names the export and gives the EJP0041 advice: wrap the GADT index in an existential at the boundary. A phantom index that peval resolved to a concrete nominal is fine and crosses as its name. Sample: one export with an unresolved existential (error) and the same wrapped (ok). Lands on top of program 2, ~120m.")
inl note_of_linked_units_monomorphic_rule_3 () = Note ("LINKED-UNITS-MONOMORPHIC-RULE", 3u32, "2026-11-03T19:34Z", "compiler", "11-03 15:3x compiler: DESIGN CONFLICT found before cutting. The n2 cut rejects YMetavar/YForall/YExists/YTypeFunction in an export signature or a reachable union shape, but the n1 DoD's POSITIVE sample is an existential wrapper at the boundary, whose union case type is YExists, so the cut would reject it. show_ty also prints every existential as 'exists ?. ?' (spiral_compiler.fs:9406), so two different existential-wrapped unions would share one (source, shape) key in the unit graph and linkedUnionName could alias them. Needed before code: (1) a shape text for YExists that names its bound kinds and body (or the wrapper nominal's name and args), so shapes stay injective; (2) the rule then rejects only YMetavar/YForall/YTypeFunction plus an unwrapped GADT index (a YApply whose head nominal is an indexed family with a non-concrete argument). Next step: extend show_ty's YExists case (or add a unit-graph-specific printer) and add the two samples.")
inl note_of_linked_units_monomorphic_rule_4 () = Note ("LINKED-UNITS-MONOMORPHIC-RULE", 4u32, "2026-11-03T19:36Z", "compiler", "11-03 15:4x compiler: CORRECTION to n3. YExists carries no payload (Ty case 'YExists', spiral_compiler.fs:9406), so the type system already treats every existential as one opaque type. Two existential-wrapped unions with equal cases ARE the same runtime type, and sharing one shape key is correct, not aliasing. So no show_ty change is needed. The rule: reject only YMetavar, YForall and YTypeFunction in an export signature or a reachable union shape, and allow YExists, which makes the DoD's positive wrapper sample compile. Because peval specializes the Export closure, a metavar there should already be impossible, so the check is a guard with a clear message rather than new behavior. Keep the item P1 but low value until a real export trips it.")
inl note_of_linked_units_monomorphic_rule_5 () = Note ("LINKED-UNITS-MONOMORPHIC-RULE", 5u32, "2026-11-04T00:53Z", "compiler", "11-03 20:5x compiler: GUARD BUILT per n4 and handed to main as pack32 (low priority). Program 1-concrete-export-signatures.kitdo: unitGraphJson walks each export's signature and raises 'The export <name> has a signature with an unresolved type: <ty>. A linked unit's exports must be concrete; wrap a type parameter or a GADT index in an existential at the boundary (the EJP0041 fix).' on YMetavar/YForall/YTypeFunction; YExists is allowed. Source 60CDD696 -> A8232488, core A63F4C2A. Proof vs pack31: contracts Rust 8 + Zig 620 0 differing cells; -Suite linked 9/9 on the candidate; the name-clash fixture unchanged. A negative sample was not built: peval specializes every Export closure, so no source program is known to reach the guard (n4), and it stays a backstop. DoD's two samples: the positive side is covered by the linked suite; the negative one waits for a program that can reach it.")
inl note_of_linked_units_monomorphic_rule_6 () = Note ("LINKED-UNITS-MONOMORPHIC-RULE", 6u32, "2026-11-04T01:28Z", "compiler", "11-03 21:2x compiler: INSTALLED on the box as pack32 (live source A823248809D9B560, core A63F4C2A8E8029C2); node and vultr follow in main's chain. The guard is live and stays a backstop (no known program reaches it; n5). Done.")
inl zig_parallel_units_battle_test () = Task ("ZIG-PARALLEL-UNITS-BATTLE-TEST", "battle-test Zig parallel compilation of linked units with exports: one multi-unit corpus built as build.zig modules with 1 vs N workers, against the same corpus as Rust crates and Gleam modules", Story, P1, D6, 0u32, Planned, "LINKED-UNITS-ZIG", "team:compiler,compiler,kit,zig,linked-units,perf", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_zig_parallel_units_battle_test () = Planning ("ZIG-PARALLEL-UNITS-BATTLE-TEST", Minutes 600u32, Load Heavy, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_zig_parallel_units_battle_test_1 () = Note ("ZIG-PARALLEL-UNITS-BATTLE-TEST", 1u32, "2026-11-02T11:02Z", "kit", "Filed by kit 11-02 07:1x from the S0 study subagent's execution packet (aaf2a6f472dc70094; the user resumed it and asked that everything go to execution). USER asked for this explicitly. Corpus: kit's agile_todo_model/paths/scan/merge slice (already measured under Step A, KIT-TYPED-CRATES n10-n12) or a mega sample split into units. Measure: wall time at 1 vs N workers, generated lines per unit, cold build and warm rebuild after a one-unit change, on Zig, Rust and Gleam. Runs on the node or vultr (one job at a time there), never the box. DoD: the scored table in this item; each regression filed as its own item.")
inl note_of_zig_parallel_units_battle_test_2 () = Note ("ZIG-PARALLEL-UNITS-BATTLE-TEST", 2u32, "2026-11-02T11:08Z", "compiler", "11-02 07:20 compiler: GROOM. Blocked by LINKED-UNITS-ZIG (including its step 0: Zig has no Export arm today). The build order comes from the unit graph (program 2's <stem>.unit.json links field): a topological level runs in parallel, and build.zig modules are generated from the same graph. Measure 1 vs N workers over one corpus, with the same corpus as Rust crates (cargo -j) and Gleam modules, with an A/A arm in the same job (wall time and peak RSS per arm). Run on the node, never the box. Corpus candidate: the linked_unit_union sample family scaled to ~20 units, plus kit's agile_todo trio once it moves to link:.")
inl spiproj_retire () = Task ("SPIPROJ-RETIRE", "wherever spiral.toml exists package.spiproj is deleted: every tool reads spiral.toml (link: included), then a gate refuses a package.spiproj beside a spiral.toml", Epic, P1, D5, 0u32, Planned, "LIB-SPIRAL-TOML-MIGRATION", "team:main,main,compiler,kit,manifest,dialect", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_spiproj_retire () = Planning ("SPIPROJ-RETIRE", Minutes 900u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_spiproj_retire_1 () = Note ("SPIPROJ-RETIRE", 1u32, "2026-11-02T11:02Z", "kit", "Filed by kit 11-02 07:1x from the S0 study subagent's execution packet (aaf2a6f472dc70094; the user resumed it and asked that everything go to execution). USER: when spiral.toml exists, package.spiproj must be deleted; no item covered it (EDITION3-SPIRAL-TOML, Done, only made the compiler read either, toml first). Readers to switch first: (1) compiler host Program.fs:1200-1219 applyPackageDefaults reads only the nearest package.spiproj; (2) kit kit_repo_affected/graph.spi:26 and kit_source_topology_core/terminal.spi:401 parse packages: from spiproj (kit's part, team:kit); (3) the Cells new_project template still writes src/package.spiproj; (4) link: (COMPILER-LINKED-UNITS) must exist in spiral.toml too. Duplicates today: tibia (7 spiproj plus spiral.toml), x client (both). Use the dialect naming from birth (DIALECT-RENAME-EDITION3: dialect = i574n), not 'edition 3'. DoD: all readers on spiral.toml; every non-core spiproj archived (scratch-archive, never deleted outright) per repo; upstream core keeps its own; the gate is on in kit standards.")
inl note_of_spiproj_retire_2 () = Note ("SPIPROJ-RETIRE", 2u32, "2026-11-02T11:08Z", "kit", "kit 11-02 07:1x KIT INVENTORY (main's broadcast ask 2; no deletes). Scope today: kit/src has 492 package.spiproj and 0 spiral.toml, so the 'spiproj beside a spiral.toml' gate fires nowhere in kit yet; kit's part is to read BOTH (toml first) before any package migrates. PRODUCTION readers (must learn spiral.toml): (1) package graph: kit_repo_affected/affected.spi:13 (path) + graph.spi:26 (packages: section parse); kit_source_topology_core/terminal.spi:192, :235, :374, :382, :401 (packages: parse), :425 (import count), :557, :603-604 (owner discovery by spiproj presence); kit_standards/standards.spi:91,:98 (the lib-referenced rule reads spiproj); kit_product_diff/recovery_terminal.spi:24 (file-kind list). (2) BACKLOG STATE manifest, a different use: the agile backlog's own state/package.spiproj (modules: list of segment files) is read/written by agile_note_segments/segments.spi:29,59,63, agile_note_model/model.spi:280,335, agile_task_views/views.spi:46,97, agile_backlog_scaffold/backlog_scaffold.spi:19,102,113, kit_agile_lease/lease.spi:82,92 + package_inputs.spi:41; and bundle/archive policy names it (bundle_policy_domain/policy.spi:103-139, toolchain_runtime_policy_domain/receipt.spi:67-85, kit_bundle_zip/archive_terminal.spi:168). The backlog has no spiral.toml, so it's out of this item's rule until the state dir gets one; decide then whether state/ moves to spiral.toml too (one atomic migration: reader+writer+bundle policy together). FIXTURES only (move with their readers): contracts_entry/cli_driver.spi (16), kit_agile_contracts/agile_cli.spi (9), agile_task_views/views_cli.spi, kit_repo_affected/affected_cli.spi, kit_source_topology_core/terminal.spi:334,336,530,535, kit_coverage_smoke, kit_patch_mutation_contracts, kit_bundle_retention probes, kit_archive_contracts, retention_semantic_probe. Help text: command_spec_domain/direct_verbs.spi:60. Total 79 mentions in 24 files.")
inl note_of_spiproj_retire_3 () = Note ("SPIPROJ-RETIRE", 3u32, "2026-11-02T11:13Z", "main", "main 11-02 GROOM ROUND RESULT (ask 2: package.spiproj readers by file:line; nothing deleted anywhere). ORDER: the compiler host goes first, because every lane's manifests are read by it. compiler host tmp/compiler/host/Program.fs hard-codes spiproj with no toml fallback at :54 openOwningProject, :162/:165 source graph owner/walk, :310 fingerprint relevance, :1248 applyPackageDefaults; already toml-first: core :33385/:34157/:34530/:29234, host :366/:754/:1372; core :33433 is the spiproj->toml migrator (reads spiproj by design). compiler scripts: try.ps1:19 (writes one), lib-usage-census.ps1:10, env.ps1:145, test.ps1:130, bench-cells-vs-matrix.ps1:12, test-rust-exports.ps1:22. kit: n2 (79 mentions in 24 files; 492 spiproj, 0 spiral.toml in kit/src). CARVE-OUT: the backlog's own state/package.spiproj is a DATA manifest (5 agile packages + bundle policy read/write it): one atomic migration, out of scope until state/ has a spiral.toml. fiscal: src/generator.spi:6 (builds the text) and :37 (writes it into all 81 generated units) and :998 (test reads one); README.md:142; src/package.spiproj duplicates src/spiral.toml; generated headers say `--package .`, so Cells reads them. The generator must switch to emitting spiral.toml before fiscal can retire anything. tibia: scripts/predict.ps1:21,:22 (patch packageDir + module list), live/paths.ps1:14 (hashes .spiproj as a source), README.md:25-26; 9 spiproj beside spiral.toml. x: 0 readers in x code; apps/client and apps/sim/spi spiproj sit beside matching spiral.toml, deletable once the host is retired. x-dumps: no readers; kit/src/kit_k3s and k3s_manifest hold spiproj with no toml (rule doesn't fire). resilience: no restore/backup path reads spiproj; retirement is restore-safe.")
inl note_of_spiproj_retire_4 () = Note ("SPIPROJ-RETIRE", 4u32, "2026-11-05T07:19Z", "tibia", "11-05 03:19 tibia: tibia's share DONE (unstaged): all 9 tibia package.spiproj files deleted (src, results, apps/arena, canary, host, map, offscreen, predict, suite), each beside an equal spiral.toml (normalized compare; predict's spiproj was already stale, missing gpu_plan). Proof: vultr --check of the last module of every package, toml-only, 9/9 exit 0 with 0 diagnostics and a negative control (missing module, exit 5 with 2277/3113 diagnostics); node Cells build.ps1 (coins 5/5, cooldowns 8/9 with only the known node WGSL runner crash) and predict toml-only. The compiler host's relatedClosure and applyPackageDefaults still name package.spiproj, but tibia does not depend on them (diagnostics flow, edition 3 resolves). Details in TIBIA-1-0-DOD.")
inl note_of_spiproj_retire_5 () = Note ("SPIPROJ-RETIRE", 5u32, "2026-11-05T10:05Z", "compiler", "11-05 06:0x compiler, HOST PART DRAFTED (main's 11-02 order: the compiler host goes first). The 4 spiproj-only sites in tmp/compiler/host/Program.fs now find the manifest toml-first through the core's spiproj_suffix (spiral.toml if present, else package.spiproj): openOwningProject (:54), the related-diagnostics closure owner and walk (:162/:165; schema already applies withDerivedModules), applyPackageDefaults (:1290; config already converts toml text). sourceGraphFingerprint (:344) now counts spiral.toml as relevant, so editing a toml manifest invalidates the compile cache (it did not before: a cache-reuse hole for toml packages). The two 'Cannot find package.spiproj' errors name both manifests. Draft %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\spiral\\SPIPROJ-RETIRE\\1-host-toml-first.kitdo (7 replacements; pack44 host A1E7136E -> 7BA81BF6). Proof running (node compiler-pack45): a toml-only copy of package_default_int_i64 ('default-int = i64' in spiral.toml) compiled by the node's installed compiler vs the draft. Expect the draft to honour i64 and the control not to. PENDING.")
inl note_of_spiproj_retire_6 () = Note ("SPIPROJ-RETIRE", 6u32, "2026-11-05T10:09Z", "compiler", "11-05 06:1x compiler, COMPILER SCRIPTS PART (unstaged): apps/compiler/scripts/env.ps1 gains Test-SpiralManifest (spiral.toml or package.spiproj in a dir) and Get-SpiralManifestText (package.spiproj read as before; for a spiral.toml it calls the compiler's --resolve-manifest, which prints the classic spiproj text form, so every existing section regex keeps working). Get-SpiralPackageDir accepts a core package with either manifest. test.ps1's 4 spiproj-only reads now go through them: families module list (:111), linked case unit discovery and packages/link sections (:127-128), frontier sample discovery (:333). Proof: both files parse 0; -Suite linked on the box, 11 rows, 11 pass (unchanged); on a toml-only package (pack45 probe: spiral.toml with default-int i64) Test-SpiralManifest True and Get-SpiralManifestText returns 'packages: |core- / default-int: i64 / modules: main', which the packages regex parses. Not changed: bench-cells-vs-matrix.ps1:12 (does not load env.ps1; it reads tests/families, still spiproj), lib-usage-census.ps1 (already toml-aware at :10), try.ps1:19 (writes a scratch spiproj on purpose), test-rust-exports.ps1:22 (copies a fixture's spiproj). The compiler's own sample and family packages can now move to spiral.toml without breaking the harness.")
inl note_of_spiproj_retire_7 () = Note ("SPIPROJ-RETIRE", 7u32, "2026-11-05T10:14Z", "compiler", "11-05 06:1x compiler: HOST DRAFT PROBE = NOT DISCRIMINATING (recorded as such; the draft stays a candidate, not installed). Node compiler-pack45: draft host 200121C567FB451E (core 657DA4B1, same as pack44) vs the node's installed pack44 (host E441B6B3). Both compile the toml-only default-int = i64 package to C with int64_t, exit 0, 252 bytes. The core already reads toml package defaults itself, so applyPackageDefaults' spiproj-only lookup was not decisive here. Where the draft DOES change behaviour: (a) sourceGraphFingerprint now includes spiral.toml. The host reuses compilations only inside a long-lived process (CompilationCache(true) for the TCP server that Cells daemons use; batches with more than one job), so editing a toml manifest between two server requests can reuse a stale result on the control. (b) the related-diagnostics closure walk now finds toml packages (relevant only to the TypeErrors routing). (c) openOwningProject, which is env-gated by SPIRAL_HOST_OPEN_PROJECT. A discriminating proof needs a server session: one --server-tcp process, compile, edit spiral.toml (default-int i64 -> i32), compile again; expect the control to keep int64_t and the draft to switch. Next run, before this joins a pack. Node stage deleted.")
inl note_of_spiproj_retire_8 () = Note ("SPIPROJ-RETIRE", 8u32, "2026-11-05T12:18Z", "compiler", "11-05 08:2x compiler: HOST DRAFT (pack45) SESSION PROOF, box. Bin core 657DA4B173F9D3D3 (= pack44) / host 200121C567FB451E vs control pack44 host E441B6B3. One --server-tcp per arm, compile, edit spiral.toml default-int i64 -> i32, compile again. Control: second compile 'cache-hit', i.e. spiral.toml is not in the fingerprint. Draft: 'unchanged', the cache is invalidated exactly as for package.spiproj, which gives 'unchanged' on BOTH arms in the same probe. So the draft brings toml to parity with spiproj; that was its purpose. NOT fixed by it, and pre-existing for both manifests: the recompiled output is still int64_t (fresh one-shot: int32_t), because the session never re-reads package defaults when only the manifest changes. Filed as COMPILER-SESSION-STALE-MANIFEST. The draft (1-host-toml-first.kitdo) is cleared to join the next pack.")
inl note_of_spiproj_retire_9 () = Note ("SPIPROJ-RETIRE", 9u32, "2026-11-05T13:28Z", "compiler", "11-05 09:3x compiler: host draft 1 joins PACK49 (pack48 + this + COMPILER-SESSION-STALE-MANIFEST 1); one-shot parity vs pack48 linked 13/13, smoke 159 rows 0 diffs; installing on the box now.")
inl note_of_spiproj_retire_10 () = Note ("SPIPROJ-RETIRE", 10u32, "2026-11-05T14:14Z", "compiler", "11-05 10:1x compiler: host program 1 (toml-first lookup, toml in the session fingerprint) SHIPPED in pack49 on box, node and vultr.")
