inl json_quote_granular () = Task ("JSON-QUOTE-GRANULAR", "lib json.quote: replace the 4-line Rust host block (for loop and match inside a host string) with granular Spiral or one global emit fn plus a one-line call, keeping the shrink kit measured", TaskKind, P1, D2, 100u32, Done, "-", "team:kit,kit,lib,granular,drift", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_json_quote_granular () = Planning ("JSON-QUOTE-GRANULAR", Minutes 120u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_json_quote_granular_1 () = Note ("JSON-QUOTE-GRANULAR", 1u32, "2026-11-02T13:17Z", "main", "main 11-02 (user: \"lanes are still adding code blocks in macros instead of granular lego building ... check who changed the json lib\"). WHO: kit, 11-02 03:10 (LIB-MOVE-JSON-QUOTE n10, json.spi 794C9C3A -> 20C39CB9), to fix TRAIN9's agile_todo_lines 1053-line overflow. The Rust arm is one host string spanning 4 lines with backslash continuations, holding a for loop, a match and `let mut out`: the multi-statement host block that granular-style-discipline forbids inline. The Spiral path under it (short_escape / control_escape / controls_from) is the right shape. DoD: the Rust arm becomes either the Spiral path made small enough (the :=-inlined helpers blew it up, so use `:~` join points) or a `global` emit fn plus a one-line call; agile_todo_lines stays <= 909 lines; the \\b \\f \\r tests pass on rust and gleam. The census probe missed this shape (GRANULAR-LINT n42).")
inl note_of_json_quote_granular_2 () = Note ("JSON-QUOTE-GRANULAR", 2u32, "2026-11-02T14:01Z", "kit", "kit 11-02 10:0x DECIDED BY A/B on the node (same base json.spi 20C39CB9, same mirror, lib quote tests rust+gleam 4/4 in every arm). Rows = agile_todo_lines / kit_grep_query generated lines: inline 4-line host block (03:10, the drift main flagged) 909 / 662; G1 granular Spiral (escaped_code via unicode.code_points + char_of + control_escape) 1162 / 910 OVER the gate (escaped_code alone 134 lines); G2 the same with char_text/escaped_control as :~ join points 1141 / 889 OVER (job-1793627841901); H one named Rust primitive emitted once per crate as 'global' plus a one-line call 925 / 678, gate ok (job-1793626880559). Pure Spiral costs ~+230 lines because rebuilding each scalar from its code (code_points + char_of) inlines heavily; H removes the block from the macro and stays under 1000. H is +16 lines vs the inline block = the same Rust on 16 formatted lines instead of one; not compressed to game the count. Non-Rust backends keep the portable replace chain. Target json.spi 4072DFECC5058A25.\nPROGRAM (kit do <lib> -):\nedit json.spi expect files 1 replacements 1\n  replace once <<<\n:= quote (s : string) : string =\n    run_target \\|\n        | Rust => \\ () =>\n            $'std::rc::Rc::<str>::from({ let q = char::from(34u8); let b = char::from(92u8); let mut out = String::from(q); \\\n                for c in (!s).chars() { let n = c as u32; match n { 34 | 92 => { out.push(b); out.push(c); } 8 | 9 | 10 | 12 | 13 => \\\n                { out.push(b); out.push(char::from(b\"btn_fr\"[(n - 8) as usize])); } n if n < 32 => out.push_str(&format\\!(\"{b}u{n:04x}\")), \\\n                _ => out.push(c) } } out.push(q); out }.as_str())' : string\n        | _ => \\ () =>\n  >>> with <<<\n:= rust_quote (s : string) : string =\n    global \"\"\"\npub fn spiral_json_quote(s: &str) -> std::rc::Rc<str> {\n    let (q, b) = (char::from(34u8), char::from(92u8));\n    let mut out = String::from(q);\n    for c in s.chars() {\n        match c as u32 {\n            34 | 92 => { out.push(b); out.push(c); }\n            n @ (8 | 9 | 10 | 12 | 13) => { out.push(b); out.push(char::from(b\"btn_fr\"[(n - 8) as usize])); }\n            n if n < 32 => out.push_str(&format!(\"{b}u{n:04x}\")),\n            _ => out.push(c),\n        }\n    }\n    out.push(q);\n    std::rc::Rc::from(out.as_str())\n}\n\"\"\"\n    $'spiral_json_quote(&*!s)' : string\n\n:= quote (s : string) : string =\n    run_target \\|\n        | Rust => \\ () => rust_quote s\n        | _ => \\ () =>\n  >>>")
inl note_of_json_quote_granular_3 () = Note ("JSON-QUOTE-GRANULAR", 3u32, "2026-11-02T15:01Z", "kit", "kit 11-02 groom (AGILE-EPIC-DEP-LINT): parent epic GRANULAR-HEAL. It was this item's only dep, which hid the item from agile next because an epic finishes after its children; parents are named in notes, deps are for real ordering only.")
inl note_of_json_quote_granular_4 () = Note ("JSON-QUOTE-GRANULAR", 4u32, "2026-11-02T19:47Z", "kit", "kit 11-02 15:46 LANDED program H (n2) into lib/spiral/json.spi: base AE971545AA5C38DC (the file had been respelled to `sm'::` at 15:00 by another lane; the H target block, quote's Rust arm, was untouched, so the program matched exactly once) → 89009416E4DB3BB7, with no live lib reader. Undo: %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\undo\\JSON-QUOTE-GRANULAR-n2. Round trip (apps/cells spi/lib_roundtrip.exs): canonical true, fixpoint true, with _real.spir. NEXT: run json.spi's rust + gleam quote test blocks once a remote is free (vultr is measuring CI binary sizes now). quote's Rust arm is now `rust_quote s`, a named global fn `spiral_json_quote` (multi-line Rust in a `global \"\"\"...\"\"\"`, not a `$'{...}'` block).")
inl note_of_json_quote_granular_5 () = Note ("JSON-QUOTE-GRANULAR", 5u32, "2026-11-02T20:09Z", "kit", "kit 11-02 16:1x VERIFIED after the land: apps/cells spi/lib_gate.ps1 -File json.spi on vultr (pack17): tests passed 16 failed 0 of 16, type errors 0 (the quote_* blocks run under rust and gleam). With the canonical round trip already true, the item is done.")
inl gpu_stacked_runs () = Task ("GPU-STACKED-RUNS", "A rehearsed, stacked GPU run queue that saturates one rented L40S for 1-2 h: tibia lane sims (evolution at 100x budget, held-out windows, seed sweeps) plus transformer benchmarks (stories15M/42M/110M tok/s on CUDA and WGSL, the run.c oracle), each dry-run locally on WGSL first, packed so the GPU never idles, results copied back and the instance DELETEd in the same script", Story, P1, D5, 0u32, Planned, "TIBIA-GPU-RENTAL", "tibia,transformer,gpu,benchmarks,team:tibia", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_gpu_stacked_runs () = Planning ("GPU-STACKED-RUNS", Minutes 360u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_gpu_stacked_runs_1 () = Note ("GPU-STACKED-RUNS", 1u32, "2026-11-02T13:32Z", "tibia", "11-02 tibia: filed from the user's 11-02 budget rule (TIBIA-GPU-RENTAL n9). DoD: one script that provisions, runs the queue at measured 100% GPU utilization (nvidia-smi sampled throughout), copies results back, DELETEs, and verifies the instance is gone, with the total cost recorded. Before the first rental, the queue's local WGSL dry run predicts at least 60 min of saturated GPU work. Saturation design input: mrakgr's CUDA/UPMEM strategies from the ML-library nugget inventory.")
inl note_of_gpu_stacked_runs_2 () = Note ("GPU-STACKED-RUNS", 2u32, "2026-11-02T14:07Z", "tibia", "11-02 tibia, SATURATION PLAN from mrakgr's CUDA work (%LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\reports\\typed-transformer-20261102\\ml-library-nuggets.md section 7). (1) Probe the device at start (SMs, regs/SM, smem/SM, CC) and size the grid from occupancy (LIB-DEVICE-CAPABILITIES), never a hardcoded GPU. (2) Precompile every sm_89 kernel before renting so no nvcc time is billed. Load weights and sim states with one arena copy from pinned memory (the UPMEM arena lesson). (3) Sims: one sim per thread, the population as a leading dim, resident loops with no host traffic, lockstep ticks with masks, >= 2-4 blocks/SM at <= 128 regs, independent batches on multiple streams, grid sync only for population updates. (4) Inference: batch many sequences on a free batch dim, fuse norm/softmax/sample per row, tensor-core GEMM for prefill and batched decode. (5) Judge 100% by the binding resource: his H100 profile showed 12.5% occupancy yet 80% DRAM (2.69 TB/s) thanks to 16-byte loads. Sample nvidia-smi dmon SM% and DRAM% throughout. The L40S has no thread block clusters. UPMEM gave nothing measured to reuse: one simulated DPU, one tasklet.")
inl note_of_gpu_stacked_runs_3 () = Note ("GPU-STACKED-RUNS", 3u32, "2026-11-02T15:03Z", "tibia", "11-02 tibia groom from the star exchange, rules for the rental script. (1) Vultr injects SSH keys only at provision: pass sshkey_id in the create call (x-dumps). (2) Use a unique name per transient unit or run, and guard globs under set -e or use a trap, so cleanup and the DELETE always run (x-dumps). (3) Judge the box from outside: confirm the DELETE with a list call, never by the script's exit 0 (resilience/main: a mirror died 19 h while its task reported 0). (4) Keep the space before the host var after any ssh -o (resilience). (5) Pull checkpoints from one client per host and IP; rate limits are per IP (x-dumps).")
inl note_of_gpu_stacked_runs_4 () = Note ("GPU-STACKED-RUNS", 4u32, "2026-11-02T23:06Z", "tibia", "11-02 19:0x tibia, READINESS CHECK (no rental yet, by the $20-cap rule: rent only when the stacked queue can keep the GPU busy). Ready to stack today: nothing GPU-side has passed parity. (1) Tibia lanes: apps/predict/lanes_gpu.spi compiles to WGSL (59,803 lines); on the node's Quadro the device is lost, on vultr lavapipe the pipeline compiles (~9 min on 2 vCPU) and the first run hit the runner's 120 s completion limit (rerun with SPIRAL_WGSL_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=1800 in flight). Lane parity with the C lanes 0-3 is the gate before any rented run. (2) Transformer on GPU: not started. The wgsl-run runner takes only a shader (no input buffers), so a WGSL forward pass needs runner support for a read-only storage buffer (the checkpoint) plus a lib shape for it; CUDA is not started either. (3) CPU-side work that could ride along on a rented box (parallel C transformer, stories110M A/B) waits for pack19 (atomic refc) and the ParamEscape pack. Order to make it rentable: lane parity on lavapipe, a lane throughput number (lanes/s at chunk 1024) so the 1-2 h queue is sized from measurement, then the runner's input-buffer support for the transformer.")
inl note_of_gpu_stacked_runs_5 () = Note ("GPU-STACKED-RUNS", 5u32, "2026-11-03T03:59Z", "tibia", "11-02 23:5x tibia, READINESS UPDATE (still no rental, by the $20-cap rule). Moved since n4: (1) WGSL lane correctness is now PROVEN with a checker, not by luck: the compiler lane's overflow-checking runner and shader (WGSL-LANE-HEAP-OVERFLOW) fail any run whose lanes overflow, and after tibia's two allocation cuts (network decide/inputs without arrays; terrain path windows released by a WGSL heap mark/release) 64 lanes run at the default 1 MB/lane with no overflow and lanes 0-3 equal to C (TIBIA-BATCHED-EVOLUTION n13). (2) Throughput on vultr lavapipe (2 vCPU, contended): 16 lanes in 3.3 s, 64 lanes in 193 s at load ~2 (tiny plan). Still missing before a rental is worth it: the full 8x64x100 plan's per-lane heap and lanes/s (one long lavapipe run, or the first minutes of the rental itself), the compiler lane's heap fix landing in a pack (so the rented box runs the checked runner), and a transformer GPU path (runner input buffers, TRANSFORMER-GPU n2). Order unchanged: full-plan measurement first, then size the 1-2 h queue from lanes/s.")
inl note_of_gpu_stacked_runs_6 () = Note ("GPU-STACKED-RUNS", 6u32, "2026-11-03T09:12Z", "tibia", "11-03 05:1x tibia, READINESS + AVAILABILITY (no rental). Gaps from n5 that are now closed: full-plan per-lane heap fits the default 1 MB (no overflow) and full-plan lanes equal C (TIBIA-BATCHED-EVOLUTION n16; the lib heap_scope shader is byte-identical, n18); sized_array runs on WGSL (LIB-TYPE-SHAPES n11). So tibia lanes alone could saturate a GPU (thousands of independent 1 MB lanes; the sweep's per-lane cost is about 219 s on one C core). Still missing: a measured GPU lanes/s (only a rental can give it; lavapipe 2 lanes = 1,789 s says nothing about an L40S), and the transformer GPU path. AVAILABILITY 09:1xZ via the Vultr API (read-only; key never printed): account credit about 103 USD (balance -103.01, pending 2.08); type=vcg lists 3 plans, vcg-l40s-16c-180g-48vram 1.54 USD/h, vcg-l40s-32c-375g-96vram 3.08 USD/h, vcg-l40s-64c-750g-192vram 6.15 USD/h, and ALL THREE have an empty locations list, so no region offers an L40S on demand now. DECISION: no rental today; recheck availability at the start of the next run window. When a region opens, the first rental is a 1 h, 20-lane-chunk measurement (provision, build the checked runner, full-plan lanes at increasing SPIRAL_WGSL_LANES, nvidia-smi dmon throughout, copy back, DELETE, list to verify) capped at about 2 USD, which sizes the real 1-2 h queue.")
inl note_of_gpu_stacked_runs_7 () = Note ("GPU-STACKED-RUNS", 7u32, "2026-11-03T11:40Z", "tibia", "11-03 07:4x tibia: availability recheck (Vultr API, read-only): all three vcg-l40s plans (1.54 / 3.08 / 6.15 USD/h) still list NO locations; no rental possible. Readiness since n6: the transformer CPU side reached ~97% of llama2.c runfast on 42M/110M (TRANSFORMER-CPU-SPEED n20-n21), and the sized_array/heap_scope WGSL paths are proven on lavapipe, so a rental's first hour can measure tibia lanes/s on real hardware once a region opens.")
inl note_of_gpu_stacked_runs_8 () = Note ("GPU-STACKED-RUNS", 8u32, "2026-11-04T11:29Z", "tibia", "11-04 07:29 tibia, availability recheck (Vultr API, read-only; key from ~/.secrets/vultr.env, not printed): type=vcg lists 3 plans and all 3 have NO locations, so still no rental possible, as on 11-03. Readiness grew since n7: lib occupancy now probes a real device through the CUDA driver at run time (LIB-DEVICE-CAPABILITIES slice 5, with a compute-capability fallback for old drivers), so a rental's first minute can print its own launch plan (occupancy::described (planned target (probed 0) kernel)); TRANSFORMER-ZIG-BACKEND gives a third compute backend for the same model. Next check: on the next groom, or when a lane reports a GPU region opening.")
inl note_of_gpu_stacked_runs_9 () = Note ("GPU-STACKED-RUNS", 9u32, "2026-11-04T14:53Z", "tibia", "11-04 10:53 tibia: availability recheck (read-only API, no spend): Vultr /v2/plans?type=vcg lists 3 GPU plans and none has a location, so on-demand GPUs are still unavailable on Vultr. Spend so far: 0 USD of the 20 USD cap.")
inl note_of_gpu_stacked_runs_10 () = Note ("GPU-STACKED-RUNS", 10u32, "2026-11-04T15:30Z", "tibia", "11-04 11:30 tibia, CONSUMER SLICE IN THE TREE: tibia/apps/predict/gpu_plan.spi (new module, registered in apps/predict/spiral.toml after lanes_gpu; unstaged) is the GPU run's first command: occupancy::probed 0 through the CUDA driver at run time; on Some d it prints the device line and the launch plan of the lanes kernel (64 threads = the WGSL runner's workgroup, smem 0, registers not counted since WGSL reports none), exit 0; on None it prints 'no CUDA device: probed 0 is None', exit 1. Proof: box (Quadro K1100M): 'device sms 2, threads/sm 2048, blocks/sm 16, regs/sm 65536, smem/sm 49152' and 'blocks/sm 16, grid 32, occupancy 50%', exit 0, compiled inside tibia's package (12,826 B C); vultr (no GPU), the same C: 'no CUDA device', exit 1. LEFT: the same command on a rented L40S as the first step of GPU-STACKED-RUNS (no Vultr GPU region yet).")
inl note_of_gpu_stacked_runs_11 () = Note ("GPU-STACKED-RUNS", 11u32, "2026-11-05T02:09Z", "tibia", "11-04 22:09 tibia: WHY NO VULTR GPU (read-only API + web, no spend). (1) It is not our account: the same queries WITHOUT an API key (GET /v2/plans is public) give the identical result, 3 vcg plans, all NVIDIA L40S (vcg-l40s-16c/32c/64c, 1.671/3.342/6.684 USD/h, deploy_ondemand true, deploy_preemptible false), each with locations: []. (2) GPU bare metal is the same: A100x4, A100x8, H100x8, L40x8, GH200, MI300X, MI325X, MI355X and B200 plans all show 0 locations, while 14 of 15 non-GPU bare-metal plans list 1-24 locations. (3) GET /v2/regions/{id}/availability?type=vcg returns available_plans [] in ewr, ord, lax, sjc, atl, ams, fra and sea, both with and without a key, while ?type=vbm in ewr returns non-GPU metal. (4) The fractional A16/A40/A100 cloud GPU plans are gone from the catalog entirely, not just empty. (5) External trackers agree: GetDeploying lists Vultr's L40S out of stock in all configurations, checked 11-03 and 11-04. No Vultr statement explains it; Vultr's API reference and product pages answer 403 to automated fetches, and docs.vultr.com describes a normal console flow with no access step. CONCLUSION: on-demand GPU capacity on Vultr is currently zero for everyone through the public API; it is a supply state, not an account gate we can lift with a parameter. Options: keep the read-only recheck; ask Vultr sales about reserved or contract capacity (needs the user, it is an outward contact); or the user decides on another provider, which the 11-02 rental permission does not cover.")
inl note_of_gpu_stacked_runs_12 () = Note ("GPU-STACKED-RUNS", 12u32, "2026-11-05T05:03Z", "tibia", "11-05 01:03 tibia: availability recheck (anonymous GET /v2/plans?type=vcg and /v2/plans-metal): still 3 L40S cloud plans and 9 GPU metal plans, every one with 0 locations. No rental possible; the queue keeps growing on CPU meanwhile (node runs offspring per thread at 3.2x on 8 threads, TIBIA-BATCHED-EVOLUTION).")
inl transformer_oracle () = Task ("TRANSFORMER-ORACLE", "Transformer oracle first: pin llama2.c (commit), stories15M/42M/110M checkpoints and tokenizer.bin as CI downloads (never committed), and record run.c greedy outputs (tokens plus fp32 logits per step) as typed Spiral row modules; the first test diffs against ./run stories15M.bin -t 0", Story, P1, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "transformer,benchmarks,oracle,team:tibia,tibia", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_transformer_oracle () = Planning ("TRANSFORMER-ORACLE", Minutes 180u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_transformer_oracle_1 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-ORACLE", 1u32, "2026-11-02T13:42Z", "tibia", "Filed 11-02 by tibia under TYPED-TRANSFORMER-LAB (n4, from the original deliberation, share eab494f1). DoD: a Cells test in spiral/apps/transformer that fails until the forward pass matches run.c tokens exactly and logits within 1e-4.")
inl note_of_transformer_oracle_2 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-ORACLE", 2u32, "2026-11-02T16:08Z", "tibia", "11-02 tibia, FIRST ORACLE CAPTURE on the node (job transformer-oracle-2, remote first). Pins: llama2.c commit 350e04fe35433e6d2941dce5a1f53308f87058eb; stories15M.bin 60,816,028 bytes, sha256 CD590644D963867A2B6E5A1107F51FAD663C41D79C149FBECBBB1F95FA81F49A (huggingface karpathy/tinyllamas); tokenizer.bin sha256 50A52EF8...E361. Build: gcc 15.2.0 -O3 -D_WIN32 -Wno-error=incompatible-pointer-types run.c win.c (GCC 15 rejects an incompatible pointer in win.c without that flag). Run: run.exe stories15M.bin -t 0 -n 256 gives a deterministic greedy story (\"Once upon a time, there was a little girl named Lily...\"), at 60.7 tok/s single-thread -O3 on the node (the first speed baseline, same machine). The output, run log and script are in %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\spiral\\TRANSFORMER-ORACLE\\ (the model cache is under the node's %LOCALAPPDATA%\\spiral-bin\\models\\llama2c, never in a repo). Note: run.c prints \"achieved tok/s\" on stderr, which lands mid-text when the streams merge, so the oracle compare reads stdout only. Next: the spiral/apps/transformer skeleton (typed-units layout) with the oracle as a typed module, and a runfast/OpenMP baseline build.")
inl note_of_transformer_oracle_3 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-ORACLE", 3u32, "2026-11-02T21:28Z", "tibia", "11-02 tibia, FOURTH CHECKPOINT + GQA COVERAGE (node job transformer-oracle-260k, 6.6 s). stories260K (karpathy/tinyllamas stories260K/): stories260K.bin 1,056,540 B sha256 B0A507E7AD0F626624F17112325E66691F9076D622E1D3274D103D00299F2696; tok512.bin 6,227 B sha256 037CB335ABB25D1FA9E8ECAE30ED2A3A8ACE9302862EBCDC05D51A6BBB10C312; config dim 64, hidden 172, layers 5, heads 8, kv_heads 4, vocab 512, seq 512 -- the only pinned model with kv_heads < heads, so it is the first proof of the grouped-query path. run-O3 -z tok512.bin -t 0 -n 256 gives 565 chars (\"Once upon a time, there was a little girl named Lily. She loved to play outside in the park...\"), saved as greedy-stories260K-run-O3.txt in the node model cache. Spiral C and Rust builds (unchanged transformer.spi): identical=True on both. Because it is 1 MB and runs in well under a second, stories260K is the checkpoint for the in-tree Cells test of the DoD (the 60-438 MB ones stay node/CI-download benchmarks).")
inl note_of_transformer_oracle_4 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-ORACLE", 4u32, "2026-11-02T21:51Z", "tibia", "11-02 tibia, DoD TEST IN TREE AND PROVEN BOTH WAYS. New spiral/apps/transformer/oracle.spi (module listed before transformer in spiral.toml): typed pins (:== pin = { file; bytes : i64; sha256 }, :== oracle = { checkpoint; tokenizer; steps; greedy : list string }), llama2_c_commit, stories260k () with the 4 greedy lines of run-O3 -t 0 -n 256, greedy_text, and model_path (%LOCALAPPDATA%/spiral-bin/models/llama2c/<file>, the pinned download cache, never in a repo). New test in transformer.spi: greedy_stories260k_matches_run_c (rust) loads the pinned checkpoint and tokenizer, generates, and asserts equality with the oracle. Also `///> _` above main (kit standards test-targets finding). kit standards: 0 findings. Node mirror (synced, digest A9558245B8F37D44), memo-off: POSITIVE job transformer-cells-1 4/4 passed (oracle test 25 s); NEGATIVE job transformer-cells-mutant-2 with \"too high.\" -> \"too low.\" in oracle.spi: exactly greedy_stories260k_matches_run_c failed, other 3 passed, file restored (365A7EC7F675). A first negative run with memo on passed 4/4 on memo hits, which exposed CELLS-MEMO-SIBLING-MODULES (filed). Remaining: logits within 1e-4 as a test (the node logits script proves it for 15M but is not in tree), and the CI download step for the model cache.")
inl note_of_transformer_oracle_5 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-ORACLE", 5u32, "2026-11-02T22:21Z", "tibia", "11-02 tibia, LOGITS HALF OF THE DoD IN TREE. Node dump (job transformer-logits-260k-2, run-dump.exe, 3 steps x 512): Spiral C logits vs run.c max abs diff 4.97e-6 over all 1,536 values. oracle.spi gains :== probe = { token; logit : f32 } and stories260k_step_0/1/2 (per step the run.c top-3 logits plus tokens 0 and 511, 15 probes) + stories260k_probes. transformer.spi gains probe_error, worst_logit_error and test logits_stories260k_stay_within_1e4_of_run_c (rust). Node mirror memo-off: POSITIVE job transformer-cells-3 5/5 passed; NEGATIVE job transformer-cells-mutant-3 (step-1 top logit 18.4599857 -> 18.4609857, i.e. 1e-3 off): exactly the logits test failed, other 4 passed, restored. kit standards 0 findings. DoD met (a Cells test in apps/transformer that fails unless tokens match run.c exactly and logits stay within 1e-4). Left for 100%: the CI step that downloads the pinned model cache before the tests.")
inl note_of_transformer_oracle_6 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-ORACLE", 6u32, "2026-11-02T23:05Z", "tibia", "11-02 19:0x tibia: model_path made portable: oracle.spi spiral_bin () = LOCALAPPDATA + /spiral-bin, else HOME + /.cache/spiral-bin (spiral-bin's own Linux convention), so the tests resolve the same pinned cache on Linux CI. Node re-proof memo-off (job transformer-cells-4): 5/5 passed. CI status: spiral's gh-pages.yml cells-gate covers only lib/spiral (ci_gate.ps1 lists lib/spiral/*.spi), no app test runs in CI, so there is no CI job to add the model-cache download to yet; the step becomes part of whichever item first puts apps/ tests in CI (download stories260K.bin + tok512.bin into $HOME/.cache/spiral-bin/models/llama2c, pins in oracle.spi).")
inl note_of_transformer_oracle_7 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-ORACLE", 7u32, "2026-11-03T04:49Z", "tibia", "11-03 00:5x tibia, CI STEP WRITTEN (unstaged in spiral, unverified until a scheduled or dispatched run after the user pushes). .github/workflows/gh-pages.yml cells-gate job: a new step before the gate downloads stories260K.bin and tok512.bin from huggingface karpathy/tinyllamas into HOME/.cache/spiral-bin/models/llama2c (where oracle.spi's spiral_bin () resolves on Linux), then checks them with sha256sum -c against the oracle.spi pins (B0A507E7..., 037CB335...). The gate step passes -Roots lib/spiral,apps/transformer. apps/cells/spi/ci_gate.ps1 gains [string[]] -Roots (default lib/spiral, so every other caller is unchanged; comma-split as elsewhere). Checks: the download step run verbatim on vultr gives 'OK' for both files; path selection with the default gives the same 83 lib files, and with the two roots 84 = + apps/transformer/transformer.spi (tracked). No new actions, so actions.lock is untouched. Note: ci_gate only fails on a VOID run (0 executed); failing rows land in cells-gate.tsv, the same as for lib. The pins now live in two places (oracle.spi and the workflow); a mismatch fails loudly at sha256sum or at the oracle tests. LEFT: the first CI run after the push.")
inl note_of_transformer_oracle_8 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-ORACLE", 8u32, "2026-11-03T14:10Z", "tibia", "11-03 10:1x tibia: the CI step is pushed (spiral 98e51d32, gh-pages.yml model-cache download + ci_gate -Roots lib/spiral,apps/transformer), but the push run 41598093899 SKIPPED cells-gate by design (cells-gate runs only on schedule 17 6 * * * or workflow_dispatch). The last scheduled run is 11-02 (194a1916), before the push, so the first proof is the next scheduled run (11-04; GitHub has started these 6-9 h late) or a user dispatch. I did not dispatch: a dispatch also re-runs the push/publish job. Check then: the download step prints OK twice and cells-gate.tsv has apps/transformer rows passed.")
inl note_of_transformer_oracle_9 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-ORACLE", 9u32, "2026-11-04T04:18Z", "tibia", "11-04 00:18 tibia GROOM: parked to Planned (no note in 12 h). NEXT: read the 11-04 scheduled cells-gate run (cron 17 6 * * * UTC, often 6-9 h late): the model-cache download prints OK twice and cells-gate.tsv has apps/transformer rows passed. No dispatch, because a dispatch re-runs publish.")
inl note_of_transformer_oracle_10 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-ORACLE", 10u32, "2026-11-04T04:21Z", "tibia", "11-04 00:21 tibia: back to Active, percent kept. User 11-04 00:2x moved the stale-Active window from 12 h to 48 h, and this item has a note within 48 h, so this morning's parking (12 h rule) is undone. The next step in the 00:2x note still holds.")
inl note_of_transformer_oracle_11 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-ORACLE", 11u32, "2026-11-04T06:35Z", "tibia", "11-04 02:35 tibia: DONE, CI PROOF IN. The scheduled gh-pages run 41651659668 (11-03 14:14 local, head 98e51d32, the commit with the model-cache step) is green: the cells-gate job printed stories260K.bin: OK and tok512.bin: OK from the sha256-pinned download, then ran ci_gate.ps1 -Roots lib/spiral,apps/transformer (88 files, 717 tests, 1812 s). cells-gate.tsv (artifact downloaded and checked) has apps/transformer/transformer.spi discovered 5, executed 5, passed 5, failed 0. So the oracle (pinned llama2.c commit, checkpoints by sha256, greedy token parity) runs on Linux CI, not only on the box and node. This run had already happened when the 11-04 00:2x groom expected the first proof on 11-04; GitHub had started the schedule late.")
inl transformer_cpu_reference () = Task ("TRANSFORMER-CPU-REFERENCE", "Transformer CPU reference: fp32 forward pass (embedding, rmsnorm, RoPE, multi-head attention with KV cache, SwiGLU FFN, softmax, greedy and top-p sampling) in spiral/apps/transformer over the lib shape core, token-identical to run.c on stories15M/42M/110M on C and Rust", Story, P1, D5, 100u32, Done, "TRANSFORMER-ORACLE", "transformer,lib,typefeatureset,team:tibia,tibia", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_transformer_cpu_reference () = Planning ("TRANSFORMER-CPU-REFERENCE", Minutes 900u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_transformer_cpu_reference_1 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-CPU-REFERENCE", 1u32, "2026-11-02T13:42Z", "tibia", "Filed 11-02 by tibia under TYPED-TRANSFORMER-LAB (n4, from the original deliberation, share eab494f1). Model size is selected outside existentials: one specialization per checkpoint (existentials are compile-time only). Rc is allowed at load time and never in the hot loop.")
inl note_of_transformer_cpu_reference_2 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-CPU-REFERENCE", 2u32, "2026-11-02T17:05Z", "tibia", "11-02 tibia, SLICE 1 GREEN: TOKEN-IDENTICAL TO run.c on stories15M (node job transformer-ref-2): greedy 256 steps, 812 chars, byte-identical to llama2.c run.c -t 0 (CRLF normalized; run.c writes CRLF on Windows). Code: spiral/apps/transformer/transformer.spi (unstaged, new, ~310 lines) plus spiral.toml (edition 3; packageDir ../../lib). It is a direct port of run.c: the checkpoint header as a config record, weight slabs as a typed layout record (attention_w/ffn_w), weights decoded once into one array f32, rmsnorm, matmul, RoPE, multi-head attention over a KV cache with kv_heads, SwiGLU, argmax, and the tokenizer.bin piece decode with BOS space strip and raw <0xXX> bytes. Rust only for now: f32_of_bits is a Rust boundary macro and read_all_bytes is Rust/F#-only (LIB-BINARY-IO). Correct on the first compile. Runner (scratch, node): compile, rustc -C opt-level=3 -C target-cpu=native, run, diff against the oracle. Speed: 52 s for 255 tokens including the checkpoint decode, about 5 tok/s against run.c's 32-70 on the same node; the cause is analysed in COMPILER-RUST-ARRAY-HOT-LOOP. Not yet: type-level shapes (waits on COMPILER-TYPE-ARITH-NORMALIZE), C/CUDA/WGSL, logit 1e-4 compare, top-p sampling, the cell budget (several defs are over 272), UTF-8 pieces beyond ASCII.")
inl note_of_transformer_cpu_reference_3 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-CPU-REFERENCE", 3u32, "2026-11-02T17:18Z", "tibia", "11-02 tibia: ALL THREE CHECKPOINTS TOKEN-IDENTICAL to llama2.c run.c (greedy, 256 steps, node jobs transformer-ref-2/-stories42M/-stories110M): stories15M 812 chars in 52 s, stories42M 745 chars in 112 s, stories110M 829 chars in 658 s, byte-identical after CRLF normalization. This is the plain-shapes Rust reference; DoD items left: logits within 1e-4 (needs a logit dump from run.c), C/CUDA/WGSL arms, the cell budget (14 definitions over 272, splitting next), top-p sampling, UTF-8 pieces.")
inl note_of_transformer_cpu_reference_4 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-CPU-REFERENCE", 4u32, "2026-11-02T17:37Z", "tibia", "11-02 tibia: LOGITS WITHIN BOUND. Max abs logit diff against llama2.c run.c is 5.0e-7 over the first 4 steps x 32,000 vocab (128,000 values, stories15M, fp32), against the DoD bound 1e-4 (node job transformer-logits-1). Method: run.c is patched in the model cache (run_dump.c, never in a repo) to append each step's logits to a file when DUMP_STEPS is set; the Spiral side gets TRANSFORMER_DUMP_STEPS=n in main, which prints n steps of logits instead of generating. Also done: the cell-budget split (every definition now <= 271 chars incl. newlines; helpers layer_at/project/normed/qkv_slabs/cache_kv/attend_head/score_at/mix_at/piece_at/next_token plus a run record), re-proved token-identical (job transformer-ref-5). The correctness DoD on Rust is met. Left: C/CUDA/WGSL arms (LIB-BINARY-IO C slice; f32_of_bits arms), type-level shapes (COMPILER-TYPE-ARITH-NORMALIZE), top-p, UTF-8 pieces, Cells tests in-tree. Runner and compare scripts: %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\spiral\\TRANSFORMER-CPU-REFERENCE\\.")
inl note_of_transformer_cpu_reference_5 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-CPU-REFERENCE", 5u32, "2026-11-02T20:55Z", "tibia", "11-02 tibia: C BACKEND GREEN. The same transformer.spi now builds on C (80 KB of C, gcc), token-identical to run.c on stories15M, logits max abs diff 2.38e-5 (bound 1e-4; Rust 7.0e-7). It became possible today through three lib lands: convert::f32_of_bits (C memcpy / Rust from_bits / Wgsl bitcast), file_system::read_bytes (C fread arm) and env::get_environment_variable (C getenv arm, LIB-ENV-C). transformer.spi changes: it uses convert::f32_of_bits and file_system::read_bytes (no Rust-only macro left), main : i32 for the C backend, and the logit dump prints round(x * 1e7) as i64, because lib console has no float printing on C and that is FLOAT-TEXT-CANONICAL's question. Rust on pack17: 28-31 s per 255 tokens, identical.")
inl note_of_transformer_cpu_reference_6 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-CPU-REFERENCE", 6u32, "2026-11-02T21:06Z", "tibia", "11-02 tibia: C and Rust backends now token-identical to llama2.c greedy on ALL THREE checkpoints (node jobs transformer-c-stories42M, transformer-c-stories110M; compiler 6B9374835A1BBACF, 256 steps). Wall time incl. load, node at 100% load, not a benchmark: 42M C 19,372 ms / Rust 48,541 ms; 110M C 50,451 ms / Rust 198,682 ms. The correctness gate of this item is met on both CPU backends; remaining for 100%: in-tree Cells tests for apps/transformer (matmul/softmax/pieces exist; add a tiny-checkpoint forward test).")
inl note_of_transformer_cpu_reference_7 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-CPU-REFERENCE", 7u32, "2026-11-03T00:25Z", "tibia", "11-02 20:2x tibia, TOP-P SAMPLING DONE, token-identical to run.c (unstaged, apps/transformer). transformer.spi gains run.c's sampler: random_u32/random_f32 (xorshift64* on an array u64 state, multiplier 0x2545F4914F6CDD1D; Rust emits wrapping_mul), sampler_of temperature topp seed, sample (temperature 0 = argmax; else divide by temperature, softmax, ONE coin after the softmax, then mult_from = sample_mult, or sample_topp: cutoff (1-p)/(n-1), the descending prefix by repeated max-selection (pick_max/beats, ties keep the lowest index; equivalent to run.c's qsort order except on exact float ties) until cumulative > p, draw against coin * cumulative), and generate_sampled (forced prompt positions skip sampling, so the RNG advances only on sampled steps, as run.c). oracle.spi gains sampled_text = run.c -t 1.0 -p 0.9 -s 42 -n 256 on stories260K (574 chars, a different story than greedy, trailing spaces kept). Test topp_sampling_matches_run_c_on_stories260k (rust). Node mirror memo-off job transformer-cells-6: 7/7 passed. Remaining for 100% per this item's title: the forward pass over the lib shape core (LIB-TYPE-SHAPES' sized_array, landing now).")
inl note_of_transformer_cpu_reference_8 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-CPU-REFERENCE", 8u32, "2026-11-03T00:26Z", "tibia", "11-02 20:2x tibia, DESIGN POINT for \"over the lib shape core\": sized_array's sizes are type literals, but the transformer reads dim/hidden/heads/vocab from the checkpoint at RUN time, so a shape-typed forward pass means a per-model SPECIALIZED build: a typed model config whose fields are type literals (stories15M: dim 288, hidden 768, layers 6, heads 6, kv_heads 6, vocab 32000, seq 256; stories260K: 64, 172, 5, 8, 4, 512, 512), the loader checking the checkpoint header against it at startup (a mismatch is an error, not a silent wrong run), and buffers typed sized dim f32 / sized hidden f32 / sized (mult heads head_size) f32. Payoffs: shape errors become compile errors (wq is sized (mult dim dim), matmul's signature ties n and d), and the C backend sees constant trip counts, the precondition for unrolled and vectorized loops (the Mojo lever, COMPILER-SIMD-CODEGEN n2-n3). The runtime-shape path stays as the generic build. Plan: (1) config_lit record of type literals + header check, (2) matmul over sized arrays, (3) one specialized 260K build token-identical to the generic one, (4) the 15M A/B generic vs specialized.")
inl note_of_transformer_cpu_reference_9 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-CPU-REFERENCE", 9u32, "2026-11-03T01:11Z", "tibia", "11-02 21:0x tibia, SHAPE-DISPATCHED MATMUL works without any API change (scratch variant of apps/transformer, node job spec-check-1): matmul keeps its runtime signature but routes the four stories15M shapes (n,d) = (288,288), (288,768), (768,288), (288,32000) to rows_l/dot_l join points typed by a phantom `:! len n = i32` witness, and anything else (e.g. stories260K) to the generic loop. Generated C: the specialized loops carry CONSTANT bounds (< 288l x2, < 768l, < 32000l, >= 288l, >= 768l), and greedy tokens stay identical to run.c on stories260K (generic path) and stories15M (specialized path). This is the closed-set bridge from runtime shapes to type-level shapes until the typed per-model config lands. Pending: the speed A/B (generic vs specialized, quiet vultr), which says whether gcc turns constant trip counts into wins; vultr was busy with another lane at 21:09.")
inl note_of_transformer_cpu_reference_10 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-CPU-REFERENCE", 10u32, "2026-11-04T04:18Z", "tibia", "11-04 00:18 tibia GROOM: DONE. The fp32 forward pass matches run.c token for token on stories260K/15M/42M/110M (C and Rust). The open generic-vs-specialized matmul A/B is superseded by the typed per-model config (7 dot widths in tree, 11-03); speed work continues only in TRANSFORMER-CPU-SPEED.")
inl transformer_tokenizer () = Task ("TRANSFORMER-TOKENIZER", "Typed BPE tokenizer for llama2.c tokenizer.bin: vocab scores, merge-based encode, decode with byte fallback; identical token ids to run.c encode on a fixed prompt set", TaskKind, P2, D3, 100u32, Done, "TRANSFORMER-ORACLE", "transformer,team:tibia,tibia", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_transformer_tokenizer () = Planning ("TRANSFORMER-TOKENIZER", Minutes 240u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_transformer_tokenizer_1 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-TOKENIZER", 1u32, "2026-11-02T13:42Z", "tibia", "Filed 11-02 by tibia under TYPED-TRANSFORMER-LAB (n4, from the original deliberation, share eab494f1). The deliberation never covered the tokenizer; it is required for prompt runs beyond the BOS-only oracle.")
inl note_of_transformer_tokenizer_2 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-TOKENIZER", 2u32, "2026-11-02T23:44Z", "tibia", "11-02 19:4x tibia, DONE in tree (unstaged): apps/transformer/transformer.spi gains run.c's BPE encode: scores_of (vocab scores from tokenizer.bin), byte-level piece lookup (same_bytes, find_piece, linear), UTF-8 codepoint grouping (codepoint_end) with byte fallback (byte + 3), the dummy-prefix \" \" token, and the merge loop that repeatedly replaces the adjacent pair whose concatenation scores highest (best_pair/better, merged_at, merge_all; ties keep the first pair, as run.c's strict >); encode returns BOS :: tokens. generate_from/generate_prompted force the prompt tokens and then decode greedily, as run.c's generate loop. oracle.spi gains dragon_prompt (the bytes of \"Tom met a green dragon\") and dragon_text (run.c -t 0 -n 256 -i \"Tom met a green dragon\" on stories260K, 504 chars, a story that departs from the BOS-only one). Test prompt_encodes_and_generates_like_run_c_on_stories260k (rust): node mirror memo-off job transformer-cells-5, 6/6 passed. Not covered yet: non-ASCII prompts (the byte-fallback path) and the CLI prompt input (the encoder takes bytes; string-to-bytes needs a lib helper).")
inl note_of_transformer_tokenizer_3 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-TOKENIZER", 3u32, "2026-11-03T01:32Z", "tibia", "11-02 21:2x tibia, CLI + C BACKEND PROOF (unstaged): transformer.spi main now takes TRANSFORMER_PROMPT_FILE (read as bytes, encoded by the BPE encoder) and TRANSFORMER_SEED (switches to run.c's default sampling, temperature 1.0, top-p 0.9; absent = greedy, so the oracle runs are unchanged), through prompt_tokens / seed_of / generate_with. Node job transformer-cli-1, C backend (132,451 bytes of C, gcc -O3 -march=native): greedy stories15M identical=True; prompt file \"Tom met a green dragon\" on stories260K identical=True to run.c -i; TRANSFORMER_SEED=42 on stories260K identical=True to run.c -t 1.0 -p 0.9 -s 42. So the encoder and the sampler match run.c on C as well as on Rust (the Cells tests).")
inl note_of_transformer_tokenizer_4 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-TOKENIZER", 4u32, "2026-11-04T04:15Z", "tibia", "11-04 00:15 tibia GROOM: DONE. BPE encoder, sampler and CLI match run.c on Rust (Cells) and C (node transformer-cli-1); nothing left in code. Linux CI proof joins the post-commit CI heal (user 11-04: no commit asks; when the user commits, each lane checks and heals CI).")
inl note_of_transformer_tokenizer_5 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-TOKENIZER", 5u32, "2026-11-04T11:20Z", "tibia", "11-04 07:20 tibia: RE-CLOSED. My 04:15Z set (100 Done, kit answered set ok) was later found reverted to 95 Active, with no note in between: a lost update (agile-status-lost-updates), probably a concurrent writer that read the store before 04:15Z and wrote it back. Of the 9 tibia items closed today, only this one reverted. Reported to kit. Status unchanged in substance: encoder, sampler and CLI match run.c on Rust and C; the transformer CI cells-gate run 41651659668 also passed apps/transformer 5/5 on Linux (see TRANSFORMER-ORACLE).")
inl transformer_checkpoint_load () = Task ("TRANSFORMER-CHECKPOINT-LOAD", "Load llama2.c checkpoints by reflection over a typed config/weights record (the S5 idea): header to config, weight tensors as typed views over one mmap or read buffer, shared-classifier flag, with no per-field hand parsing", TaskKind, P2, D3, 100u32, Done, "TRANSFORMER-ORACLE,LIB-BINARY-IO", "transformer,typefeatureset,team:tibia,tibia", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_transformer_checkpoint_load () = Planning ("TRANSFORMER-CHECKPOINT-LOAD", Minutes 240u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_transformer_checkpoint_load_1 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-CHECKPOINT-LOAD", 1u32, "2026-11-02T13:42Z", "tibia", "Filed 11-02 by tibia under TYPED-TRANSFORMER-LAB (n4, from the original deliberation, share eab494f1). From R1-4edd: 'S5 para carregar pesos por reflexao'. mmap where the backend has it.")
inl note_of_transformer_checkpoint_load_2 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-CHECKPOINT-LOAD", 2u32, "2026-11-03T09:36Z", "tibia", "11-03 05:4x tibia: now the measured lever for TRANSFORMER-CPU-SPEED (n9 there): the copy load (decode_floats into a new array) costs about 5.8% of a stories15M 256-step run (perf), the whole remaining gap to llama2.c runfast, which mmaps. Scope addition: the loaded weights are a zero-copy view of the file (mmap on C), so a load decodes no floats. The typed config/weights record by reflection (this item's S5 idea) sits on top of that view. DoD addition: a stories15M 256-step A/B against runfast shows the load share under 1% in perf.")
inl note_of_transformer_checkpoint_load_3 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-CHECKPOINT-LOAD", 3u32, "2026-11-03T09:45Z", "tibia", "11-03 05:5x tibia, STEP 1 WRITTEN (unstaged), a one-copy load as the stepping stone before zero-copy. lib/spiral/file_system.spi: read_prefix path count : array u8 and read_f32s path offset count : array f32. The C arm reads straight into the destination through one helper, spiral_read_region (fopen, fseek, fread; same pattern as the existing spiral_read_file), so no per-float decode and no whole-file byte array. Every other backend falls back to plain Spiral (read_bytes plus convert::f32_of_bits over a little-endian u32), so no multi-op host code is added. Test read_f32s_reads_little_endian_floats_at_an_offset (c + rust; a 12-byte fixture: 4 header bytes, then 1.5 and -2.0) also checks read_prefix. apps/transformer: model_at path reads the 28-byte header with read_prefix and the weights with read_f32s; decode_floats/floats_from removed; all 5 load sites switched. GATE QUEUED (node job tibia-load-chain-1, after the full suite and sized_array slice 5): file_system lib gate with an asymmetric mutant (bytes 0/1 swapped in the fallback's u32 assembly, so the Rust row must fail and the C row must stay green), transformer oracle 5/5, then a C build for the runfast A/B. PREDICTION: perf's load share drops from about 5.8% to about 2% (one page-fault pass and an fread memcpy remain), so about 97% of runfast; zero-copy (mmap view) is step 2.")
inl note_of_transformer_checkpoint_load_4 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-CHECKPOINT-LOAD", 4u32, "2026-11-03T10:01Z", "tibia", "11-03 06:0x tibia, STEP 2 DESIGN (zero-copy), decided before coding. Problem: the f32 kernels take array f32, a refcounted heap array; a view over mapped file memory must never be refcounted to zero or freed. OPTION A (typed, larger): a storage prototype in lib, ':? f32_store f : f f32 -> i32 -> f32' with instances for array and a new ':! mapped t' nominal, and every f32_kernels function generic over it ('\\* s {f32_store}.'), the fixed_views::mapped pattern. Cost: every kernel signature changes, plus a second code path to keep bit-identical. OPTION B (chosen): a lib primitive 'file_system::map_f32s path offset count : array f32' whose C arm returns an ordinary array struct whose data pointer points into a read-only mapping (mmap on POSIX, CreateFileMapping/MapViewOfFile under _WIN32 for the mingw node) and whose refcount is pinned so it never reaches zero (weights live for the whole process); every other backend falls back to read_f32s. No kernel or type changes, one host primitive (granular rule: a one-call macro), and the model record keeps 'w : floats'. Guards: offset must be 4-byte aligned (28 is), count*4 + offset <= file size (checked, failwith otherwise), and the pinned array is never written (the transformer only reads w). Risk to verify: the generated C array struct's field names (refc/len/ptr, as lib's c_read_bytes already uses ->ptr and ->len). ORDER: measure step 1 first (perf load share), then B, then A only if a second consumer needs a non-array view.")
inl note_of_transformer_checkpoint_load_5 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-CHECKPOINT-LOAD", 5u32, "2026-11-03T10:02Z", "tibia", "11-03 06:0x tibia, CORRECTION to n4 option B (verified against generated C, spec-pe.c): the C array struct is 'typedef struct { int refc; uint32_t len; float ptr[]; } Array2;', where ptr is a FLEXIBLE ARRAY MEMBER, so the floats sit inline right after an 8-byte header and there is no data pointer to aim at a mapping. REVISED OPTION B, copy-on-write header: map the checkpoint PRIVATELY (POSIX mmap MAP_PRIVATE / Windows MapViewOfFile FILE_MAP_COPY) and treat (map + offset - 8) as the Array2 header; write refc (pinned, so it never frees) and len there. Only the page holding the 8 header bytes is copied (one 4 KB page); the 60 MB of weights stay shared with the page cache and are never copied. Preconditions, checked at runtime with failwith: offset >= 8 (the transformer's 28 gives header at file offset 20), offset - 8 aligned to 4 (int refc), floats aligned to 4 (28 is). The array is never written (the transformer only reads w), and never freed (pinned). The header layout dependency (refc, len, inline data) is the risk: the primitive gets a C test that compares index/length against read_f32s on the same fixture, so a future layout change fails loudly. Other backends keep the read_f32s fallback.")
inl note_of_transformer_checkpoint_load_6 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-CHECKPOINT-LOAD", 6u32, "2026-11-03T10:07Z", "tibia", "11-03 06:1x tibia, STEP 2 CODE WRITTEN (unstaged, lib/spiral/file_system.spi), per n5: map_f32s path offset count : array f32. C arm: one helper spiral_map_f32s maps the file privately (POSIX mmap MAP_PRIVATE with PROT_READ|PROT_WRITE; _WIN32 CreateFileMappingA PAGE_WRITECOPY + MapViewOfFile FILE_MAP_COPY for the mingw node), checks offset >= 8, (offset - 8) % 4 = 0 and offset + 4*count <= size (NULL otherwise, so a failwith), and writes refc = 1 << 30 (pinned) and len into the 8 bytes before the floats, which copies only that page. Spiral types the result through __typeof__ of a zero-length array f32, so the primitive does not hard-code the generated struct name (Array2 in one program, another index elsewhere); a single call returns the header as uintptr. Other backends: read_f32s. Test map_f32s_views_floats_after_an_8_byte_header (c + rust; 8 pad bytes then 1.5, -2.0); on C a wrong refc would free() mapped memory when the array drops and crash the test. The queued load chain (tibia-load-chain-1) gates file_system (both new tests, asymmetric mutant), runs the transformer oracle on read_f32s, and builds loadfast.c (read_f32s) plus loadmap.c (map_f32s, an isolated-root copy) for one A/B against runfast with perf.")
inl note_of_transformer_checkpoint_load_7 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-CHECKPOINT-LOAD", 7u32, "2026-11-03T10:10Z", "tibia", "11-03 06:1x tibia, GRANULAR LINT HEALED before landing (kit TRAIN14c lint, kit's message): (1) c_read_into no longer does '(long)!size * !out->len' in host text; the byte count is computed in Spiral (bytes = size * length out) and passed as one argument, and the short-read check compares against the same value; (2) the include block of c_map_global is one cfg-gated global per header (#ifdef _WIN32 windows.h; #ifndef _WIN32 sys/mman.h, fcntl.h, unistd.h). Check: kit lint macros C:\\home\\git\\spiral\\lib\\spiral --baseline C:\\home\\git\\kit\\standards\\lint-macros-lib.spi gives new 8 (= the known backlog kit named; was 10 with these two).")
inl note_of_transformer_checkpoint_load_8 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-CHECKPOINT-LOAD", 8u32, "2026-11-03T10:37Z", "tibia", "11-03 06:3x tibia, ZERO-COPY MEASURED, PREDICTION CONFIRMED (vultr, load 1.2-1.4, stories15M 256 greedy steps, 4 arms x 8 rounds as a Latin square). Three transformer arms from ONE lib snapshot (this tree, incl. f32_kernels) and one compiler (vultr's 06:23 build), differing only in the load: old = the committed transformer.spi (spiral HEAD 98e51d32: read_bytes + decode_floats), read = read_f32s, map = map_f32s; all gcc -Ofast -march=native. Output: the 3 arms are byte-identical and equal runfast's first 200 chars. MEANS: runfast 772 ms; old 987; read 981; map 898. So map is 9% faster than old. perf load share: old about 4.8% (decode_floats1 1.82, asm_exc_page_fault 0.92, rep_movs 0.74, kernel_init_pages 0.70, ...); read about 1.9% (kernel copy and page faults remain); map about 0.3% (kernel_init_pages 0.12, faults 0.05): under 1%, as predicted (n3). Reading: read_f32s halves the load share but saves little wall time because its page faults dominate; map removes the load almost entirely. CAVEAT, a gcc warning on the map arm: '-Wfree-nonheap-object: free called on pointer with nonzero offset 20' at ArrayDecref2, because gcc sees the decref path could free the mapped header. The pinned refcount (1 << 30) makes that unreachable at runtime, but a -Werror build would fail. Fix options: compile the C helper's header write behind a cast gcc cannot track, or have the C backend skip free() for arrays whose refc is pinned (a compiler-lane change). NEXT: transformer switches to map_f32s once the queued file_system gate is green; then re-run the specialized + ParamEscape A/B with map to check the predicted ~100% of runfast.")
inl note_of_transformer_checkpoint_load_9 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-CHECKPOINT-LOAD", 9u32, "2026-11-03T10:38Z", "tibia", "11-03 06:4x tibia, WARNING FIXED (n8 caveat): spiral_map_f32s is now __attribute__((noinline)), so gcc can no longer trace the mmap'd pointer into ArrayDecref2's free(). Verified on vultr: the map arm recompiled from the updated lib with gcc -Ofast -march=native -Wall gives 0 '-Wfree-nonheap-object' warnings (was 6), and its output is byte-identical to the old arm's. Runtime behavior unchanged (the pinned refcount still makes the free unreachable).")
inl note_of_transformer_checkpoint_load_10 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-CHECKPOINT-LOAD", 10u32, "2026-11-03T12:18Z", "tibia", "11-03 08:1x tibia, LOAD CHAIN RESULTS (node job tibia-load-chain-1, spiral digest 64868628...): (1) TRANSFORMER ORACLE 5/5 GREEN memo-off on Rust with the typed per-model config + map_f32s (Rust takes the read_f32s fallback): greedy, logits within 1e-4, prompt encode, top-p, raw bytes, all on stories260K through dims_260k (GQA). (2) file_system lib gate VOID: the spiral lua host crashed (0xC0000005) mid-run (CELLS-LUA-HOST-CRASH), aborting the batch; budget lists ~90 pre-existing over-budget cells in this legacy file. (3) A REAL BUG the gate surfaced, found by an uncontended vultr probe: the C fixture writers (write_f32_fixture, write_mapped_fixture) emitted helpers using FILE/fopen before any #include <stdio.h> (the include came from the reader helpers, emitted later), so the two new C tests could not compile; the transformer never uses the fixtures, so its build was fine. FIXED (unstaged): each fixture writer now emits its own stdio include global (identical globals dedupe, so the C output only reorders). Verified on vultr: a probe of read_f32s/read_prefix/map_f32s on both fixtures exits 0 on C (gcc -O2 -Wall: 0 errors) and on Rust. The file_system re-gate needs a node slot without the Lua-host crash.")
inl note_of_transformer_checkpoint_load_11 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-CHECKPOINT-LOAD", 11u32, "2026-11-03T12:20Z", "tibia", "11-03 08:2x tibia: the file_system re-gate is NOT queued on purpose: file_system.spi carries lua-target tests, and the node's spiral lua host currently crashes reproducibly (CELLS-LUA-HOST-CRASH n3), which aborts the whole lib-gate batch, so the gate would be void again. It waits on that item; until then the C and Rust behavior of read_f32s/read_prefix/map_f32s is covered by the vultr probe (n10).")
inl note_of_transformer_checkpoint_load_12 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-CHECKPOINT-LOAD", 12u32, "2026-11-03T14:57Z", "tibia", "11-03 11:0x tibia, MOVED TO DRAFTS (main flagged it): the read_prefix/map_f32s block had grown in the LIVE spiral lib/spiral/file_system.spi, and a mid-rewrite edit (rust_write_bytes, am to_vec on array u8 without am base) broke the whole lib type check (file_system.spi:1050:24), undoing kit LIB-KEBAB-CASE and main markup lands. Now: live file_system.spi and apps/transformer/transformer.spi restored to HEAD; both drafts in kit drafts spiral TRANSFORMER-CHECKPOINT-LOAD (file_system 4360A1E93B85, transformer 79D9953A5ED4). Draft content (fixed): C helpers split into one small C function per definition (cell budget back to the HEAD count, over_budget 85 = 85; 0 added), shared write_bytes, fixtures whose low bytes differ (1.0000001f32 = 01 00 80 3F) because the old 1.5/-2.0 fixtures had byte0 = byte1 = 0, so the byte-swap mutant passed 41/41 on the node. vultr probe of the draft: C exit 0 under -Wall, Rust exit 0, Rust byte-swap MUTANT exit 1, transformer 15M via map_f32s = runfast first 200 chars. NEXT: node mirror gate (running), then lib_land_window -Draft file_system.spi=<draft> -Apply, then put transformer.spi back.")
inl note_of_transformer_checkpoint_load_13 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-CHECKPOINT-LOAD", 13u32, "2026-11-03T15:11Z", "tibia", "11-03 11:4x tibia, DRAFT GATED BOTH WAYS on the node mirror (job tibia-fs-regate-1, memo off, draft file_system 4360A1E93B85, mirror package.spiproj 8FB24F8AD13A): positive 42/42 passed, 0 type errors (includes the Windows FILE_MAP_COPY path of map_f32s on C); budget over_budget 85 = HEAD count (0 added; the 14 hashtag rows are C preprocessor lines, as at HEAD lines 832-833); negative (u32_le_at byte 0/1 swap) fails exactly map_f32s_views_floats_after_an_8_byte_header rust and read_f32s_reads_little_endian_floats_at_an_offset rust, 40 others pass. Linux side proven on vultr earlier (C, Rust, mutant, 15M transformer = runfast). LEFT: lib_roundtrip on the draft, then lib_land_window -Draft file_system.spi=<draft> -BaseHash 730CC605103A -Apply when the box has no heavy window (TRAIN15 holds it), then restore apps/transformer/transformer.spi from the draft.")
inl note_of_transformer_checkpoint_load_14 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-CHECKPOINT-LOAD", 14u32, "2026-11-03T15:37Z", "tibia", "11-03 12:3x tibia, LANDED via lib_land_window (window win-1793719193560 tree-write, base 730CC605103A = HEAD): file_system.spi 4360A1E93B85 closed green: 13 consumer-used signatures unchanged, apps/spiral/spiral.spi compiles, document_test 22 passed, type_probe file_system PASS (42 tests typed, 0 errors), 0 respell drift; undo spiral-bin lib-land-undo 20261103-111952. Then apps/transformer/transformer.spi restored from the draft (79D9953A5ED4, live was still HEAD). Both unstaged for the user. Remaining for 100%: nothing in code; the transformer CI cells-gate run (TRANSFORMER-ORACLE n8) proves the app on Linux CI.")
inl note_of_transformer_checkpoint_load_15 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-CHECKPOINT-LOAD", 15u32, "2026-11-04T04:18Z", "tibia", "11-04 00:18 tibia GROOM: DONE. file_system.spi landed green (4360A1E93B85) and transformer.spi restored from the draft; nothing left in code. Linux CI proof joins the post-commit CI heal (user 11-04: no commit asks; when the user commits, each lane checks and heals CI).")
inl transformer_cpu_speed () = Task ("TRANSFORMER-CPU-SPEED", "Transformer CPU speed: tok/s >= llama2.c runfast single-thread and >= its OpenMP build on the same machine, then toward llama2.mojo; measured per model size with compile time published beside run time", Feature, P1, D7, 55u32, Active, "TRANSFORMER-CPU-REFERENCE,COMPILER-RUST-ARRAY-HOT-LOOP,COMPILER-EMIT-PARALLEL-LOOPS,COMPILER-SIMD-CODEGEN,COMPILER-C-BORROWED-PARAMS", "transformer,compiler,benchmarks,team:tibia,tibia", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_transformer_cpu_speed () = Planning ("TRANSFORMER-CPU-SPEED", Minutes 1800u32, Load Heavy, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_transformer_cpu_speed_1 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-CPU-SPEED", 1u32, "2026-11-02T13:42Z", "tibia", "Filed 11-02 by tibia under TYPED-TRANSFORMER-LAB (n4, from the original deliberation, share eab494f1). CPU failure gate (missing in the deliberation, set here): if single-thread tok/s stays under 50% of runfast after tiling + SIMD, stop and trace before any GPU work.")
inl note_of_transformer_cpu_speed_2 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-CPU-SPEED", 2u32, "2026-11-02T14:43Z", "tibia", "11-02 tibia: parent TYPED-TRANSFORMER-LAB. COMPILER-PERCEUS-FBIP dropped from deps: it is the deliberation's timebox premise (without it the timebox doubles), not a blocker, since the hot loop is staged and must not use Rc at all. Kept as prerequisites: CPU-REFERENCE, EMIT-PARALLEL-LOOPS and SIMD-CODEGEN.")
inl note_of_transformer_cpu_speed_3 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-CPU-SPEED", 3u32, "2026-11-02T16:47Z", "tibia", "11-02 tibia, FIRST BASELINES on the node (job transformer-bench-1; i7-4810MQ, 8 logical, gcc 15.2.0, 256 greedy steps, -t 0). tok/s for run -O3 / runfast (-Ofast -march=native) / OpenMP (+ -fopenmp): stories15M 32.1 / 70.4 / 10.4; stories42M 10.3 / 28.3 / 10.3; stories110M 3.9 / 10.4 / 3.7. Greedy stdout is byte-identical across the three builds for each model (sha 15M E0B2AC33607F, 42M 4CF27F238035, 110M 566CEB7C3D63), so the oracle does not depend on build flags. CAVEAT: these are NOT clean benchmark numbers. The same -O3 15M build gave 60.7 tok/s an hour earlier, and OpenMP below single-thread means contention: the node is shared by 7 lanes, and mingw libgomp with no OMP_NUM_THREADS is suspect. Rule for this item: speed claims come only from same-machine A/B runs on a quiet box (rented GPU box, or the node during a held quiet window), run-O3 reference interleaved with ours, with thread count pinned. Model sizes and hashes: 15M 60,816,028 B CD590644...; 42M 167,020,572 B 9F65A100...; 110M 438,381,596 B 51526716.... The bench script is in %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\spiral\\TRANSFORMER-ORACLE\\.")
inl note_of_transformer_cpu_speed_4 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-CPU-SPEED", 4u32, "2026-11-02T20:55Z", "tibia", "11-02 tibia, FIRST SAME-MACHINE A/B (node, i7-4810MQ, gcc 15.2, stories15M, 256 greedy steps, rounds interleaved, node at 100% load). -O3 class: llama2.c run.c -O3 8,198/7,892/8,096 ms against Spiral C (gcc -O3 -march=native) 8,192/8,738/8,405 ms, i.e. Spiral at about 96% of run -O3. -Ofast class: runfast 3,730/3,231/3,958 ms against Spiral C with -Ofast -march=native 3,791/3,824/4,392 ms, i.e. about 91% of runfast, tokens identical. So the C backend output is already near llama2.c single-thread; the gap to runfast is about 9%, and OpenMP/Mojo-class speed needs COMPILER-EMIT-PARALLEL-LOOPS + SIMD. The Rust backend lags C by about 3.5x after pack17 (step 4 RefCell-free arrays is the next Rust lever). Script: %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\spiral\\TRANSFORMER-CPU-REFERENCE\\ (transformer-ab-*.ps1).")
inl note_of_transformer_cpu_speed_5 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-CPU-SPEED", 5u32, "2026-11-03T01:46Z", "tibia", "11-02 21:4x tibia, SPECIALIZATION A/B on quiet vultr (stories15M, 256 steps, rotated pairs, output byte-identical to the installed build in every variant). gcc -O3 -march=native: shape-dispatched (constant trip counts) 3,351/3,372/3,301/3,306/3,216/3,149 ms (mean 3,282) vs generic 3,370/3,511/3,374/3,312/3,227/3,193 (mean 3,331): about 1.5%, inside the noise band, because without fast-math gcc still cannot reorder the float reduction. gcc -Ofast -march=native, the 4 undisturbed pairs: specialized 1,112/1,196/1,318/1,134 (mean ~1,190) vs generic 1,305/1,327/1,337/1,355 (mean ~1,331): about 11% FASTER; the last 2 pairs (1,673-1,835) ran after another lane started on vultr and are excluded. Also: -Ofast alone is 2.5x faster than -O3 and still greedy-identical on stories15M. Reading: constant shapes pay where vectorization is allowed, which is the case for the SIMD codegen work (multi-accumulator / vector-type lowering is reordering by construction). Next: the typed per-model config (n8 plan) so the dispatch is not a hand-written shape list.")
inl note_of_transformer_cpu_speed_6 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-CPU-SPEED", 6u32, "2026-11-03T09:19Z", "tibia", "11-03 05:1x tibia, QUIET RUNFAST A/B on vultr (2 vCPU, load 0.38-0.59 throughout; stories15M, 256 greedy steps, wall time including model load; 6 rotated rounds of 3 arms ABC/BCA/CAB x2). llama2.c run.c fetched at the pinned commit 350e04fe (sha256 9c4f2d5c6ae0...) and built as runfast (gcc -Ofast -march=native -lm). All three arms print the same 813-byte greedy text. runfast 927/826/863/820/873/799 ms, mean 851; Spiral shape-specialized -Ofast (spec-fast, 11-02 build) 919/923/917/909/941/927, mean 923; Spiral generic -Ofast 999/1012/1025/954/1069/963, mean 1004. So Spiral C is 92% of runfast's single-thread speed when shape-specialized and 85% when generic: specialization recovers about half of the gap. DoD part 1 (>= runfast single-thread) is NOT met; the gap is 8%. NEXT, in order of expected payoff: (1) the same A/B with the ParamEscape compiler (paramesc2, pack20) on the specialized source, since borrowed params remove the per-row refcount ops in dot/matmul_rows; (2) a typed per-model config so the specialization is not a hand-written shape list (sized_array dims as type literals); (3) SIMD codegen (COMPILER-SIMD-CODEGEN). Script: scratch ab-runfast.sh (on vultr /root/paramesc-ab).")
inl note_of_transformer_cpu_speed_7 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-CPU-SPEED", 7u32, "2026-11-03T09:25Z", "tibia", "11-03 05:2x tibia, PARAMESCAPE ON THE SPECIALIZED SOURCE, quiet-ish vultr (load 0.66-0.86), 4 arms x 8 rounds as a Latin square (each arm in each position twice), stories15M 256 greedy steps. The specialized transformer.spi (scratch transformer-spec, node C:\\home\\remote-stage\\transformer-spec) compiled with the paramesc2 compiler (node C:\\home\\remote-stage\\paramesc2, the 11-02 20:11 build with the noinline fix): refc++ sites 282 -> 171; output byte-identical to spec-fast. Means: runfast 893 ms; Spiral specialized + ParamEscape -Ofast 947 ms = 94% of runfast's speed; specialized 994 ms = 90%; generic 1,058 ms = 84%. So ParamEscape is worth about 5% on top of specialization, and together they close the gap to runfast from 16% to 6%. (Absolute times sit about 5% above n6's run because the load was higher; the ratios are what count, and each round holds all arms.) NEXT: (1) when pack20 installs, rebuild from the in-tree transformer (f32_kernels in lib) to confirm the same 94% on installed bits; (2) the typed per-model config, so specialization is not a hand-written shape list; (3) SIMD or multi-accumulator reductions for the last 6% (runfast's edge is gcc vectorizing its plain-C dot loop under -Ofast).")
inl note_of_transformer_cpu_speed_8 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-CPU-SPEED", 8u32, "2026-11-03T09:35Z", "tibia", "11-03 05:3x tibia, HYPOTHESIS REFUTED (n7 guessed runfast's last 6% = gcc vectorizing its plain-C dot loop). gcc -Ofast -march=native -fopt-info-vec-optimized on vultr: our hot loops ARE vectorized, spec-pe.c:376 (dot_l10, the 288-trip dot) and :440 (dot_l12) 'loop vectorized using 32 byte vectors' (plus 16-byte epilogues), the same width as run.c's matmul (run.c:185-224). So the matmul inner loop is not the gap. Totals: run.c 66 vectorized loops, spec-pe.c 29. The missing vectorization is elsewhere (rmsnorm, softmax, RoPE, attention score/mix loops). NEXT: list the hot loops gcc reports as missed in spec-pe.c (-fopt-info-vec-missed) against run.c's vectorized ones by kernel, then a perf profile on vultr to see which kernel holds the 6% before changing any codegen.")
inl note_of_transformer_cpu_speed_9 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-CPU-SPEED", 9u32, "2026-11-03T09:36Z", "tibia", "11-03 05:4x tibia, THE LAST 6% LOCATED with perf (vultr, quiet, perf record -F 4999, by symbol). spec-pe-fast: dot_l10 72.3%, dot_l12 6.5% (both vectorized, n8), axpy 2.4%, dot14 1.9%, and the CHECKPOINT LOAD: decode_floats1 1.82% plus its kernel page-fault work (asm_exc_page_fault 1.48, kernel_init_pages 0.93, rep_movs_alternative 0.86, do_anonymous_page 0.38, mod_memcg_lruvec_state 0.28), about 5.8% in all. runfast: forward 94.6%, generate 0.55%, almost no load cost, because run.c mmaps the checkpoint while ours decodes 60 MB float by float into a freshly allocated array (every page faulted in and written). So the compute is at parity and the remaining gap is the weight load, which a 256-step run amortizes poorly (stories110M and longer generations will dilute it). The FIX is TRANSFORMER-CHECKPOINT-LOAD's scope: a zero-copy load (map the file and view the float region in place; per backend: mmap on C, memmap or a borrowed slice on Rust). PREDICTION to verify: with a zero-copy load, specialized + ParamEscape reaches 98-100% of runfast on stories15M/256.")
inl note_of_transformer_cpu_speed_10 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-CPU-SPEED", 10u32, "2026-11-03T10:41Z", "tibia", "11-03 06:4x tibia, SPEC + PARAMESCAPE + ZERO-COPY vs runfast, and the n9 PREDICTION FAILS (vultr, load 0.66-0.84, 3 arms x 8 rotated rounds, stories15M 256 steps, identical output in all arms). Both Spiral arms were built by vultr's current compiler from the 11-02 specialized source; the spec arm's C is 100,535 bytes, byte-for-byte the size of the paramesc2 build (n7), so this compiler evidently carries ParamEscape. spec-map adds the model_at/map_f32s load. MEANS (medians): runfast 802.5 (772.5); spec 925.9 (905.5); spec-map 885.3 (883.5). So zero-copy adds about 4.4% (926 -> 885), as expected from the load share, but spec-map is 87-91% of runfast, NOT the predicted ~100%. Runfast ran faster this round than in n7 (803 vs 893 ms mean), so n7's 94% was flattered by a slow runfast; across rounds runfast's spread is wide (743-955 ms), which caps what one 8-round square can claim. HONEST STATE: with the load gone, a ~10% compute gap to runfast remains, and it is not explained by vectorization of the dot loop (n8: both are 32-byte vectorized). NEXT: perf annotate dot_l10 against run.c's matmul inner loop (instruction mix: index arithmetic, bounds/len loads from the Array header, accumulator chains), and a larger square (16 rounds) to tighten the ratio.")
inl note_of_transformer_cpu_speed_11 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-CPU-SPEED", 11u32, "2026-11-03T10:43Z", "tibia", "11-03 06:5x tibia, THE ~10% COMPUTE GAP LOCALIZED (perf annotate on vultr, spec-map-fast vs runfast). Inner loops are the SAME instruction mix: ours dot_l9 (83.7% of samples) = vmovups + vmulps 0x8(%rcx,%rax) + one vaddps accumulator, add 0x20, cmp 0x480 (a constant 288-float trip count, specialization at work); runfast's matmul = vmulps + add 0x20 + cmp, also 8-wide and one accumulator. The difference is the per-row EPILOGUE: our dot is a separate noinline function (ParamEscape's noinline-on-self-loop rule, added to stop gcc losing constprop clones), so every row pays a horizontal reduction (vextractf128, vmovhlps, vshufps, 3x vaddps: about 5.5% of dot samples) plus vzeroupper (1.0%) and call/ret; runfast inlines the dot into matmul's row loop. LEVERS, cheapest first: (1) let a dot whose trip count is a constant inline into its row loop (scope the noinline rule to non-constant loops), measured against the 11-02 18% regression it was added for; (2) process 2-4 rows per call with separate accumulators, so one epilogue and one call serve several rows and the vaddps chains overlap (rows_l with a 4-row body); (3) multi-accumulator inside one dot. Expected: (1) or (2) recovers most of the 6-7%; the rest is runfast's spread.")
inl note_of_transformer_cpu_speed_12 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-CPU-SPEED", 12u32, "2026-11-03T10:44Z", "tibia", "11-03 06:5x tibia, ~RUNFAST PARITY REACHED, single thread (vultr, load 0.46-0.62, 3 arms x 8 rotated rounds, stories15M 256 greedy steps). Lever (1) of n11 tested by hand on the generated C: spec-map.c with its 2 '__attribute__((noinline)) float dot_l' removed by sed (0 left), gcc -Ofast -march=native, output byte-identical. MEANS: runfast 835.6 ms; spec-map with the dot INLINED 845.0 (~99% of runfast's speed, inside one square's noise); spec-map with noinline 895.5 (93%). So inlining the constant-trip dot recovers 5.6%, and the stack of measured levers (shape specialization, ParamEscape, zero-copy map_f32s load, an inlined constant-count dot) brings Spiral's C transformer to llama2.c runfast's single-thread speed on stories15M. DoD part 1 (>= runfast single-thread) is MET in a hand-patched build; to make it real: (a) the compiler scopes its noinline-on-self-loop rule to non-constant trip counts (COMPILER-C-BORROWED-PARAMS n12-n13), (b) the typed per-model config replaces the hand-written shape list, (c) a 16-round square on a quiet box to tighten the ratio. Part 2 (>= the OpenMP build) needs COMPILER-EMIT-PARALLEL-LOOPS (pack19).")
inl note_of_transformer_cpu_speed_13 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-CPU-SPEED", 13u32, "2026-11-03T10:49Z", "tibia", "11-03 06:5x tibia, TYPED PER-MODEL CONFIG PROTOTYPED (lever (b) of n12), in a scratch variant of the in-tree transformer (f32_kernels + map_f32s), built on vultr. Design: ':! len n = i32' width witnesses; dot_l/rows_l take them (constant trip counts); 'shaped ka kb kc kv' derives one model's (n, d) pairs itself (dim x dim, dim x kv_dim, dim x hidden, hidden x dim, dim x vocab) and runs the matching specialized rows_l or reports !-; the model table is ONE line per checkpoint: 15M (288, 768, 32000, kv 288), 42M (512, 1376, 32000, 512), 110M (768, 2048, 32000, 768), 260K (64, 172, 512, kv 32), with f32_kernels::matmul as the fallback, so any shape outside the table stays correct. Adding a model = one line; no hand-written pair list. RESULT (stories15M, 4 arms x 8 rotated rounds, load 0.8-0.9): output byte-identical; C grows from 126 KB to 319 KB (7 dot widths, 20 row kernels); means runfast 811, hand-written spec (dot inlined) 856, typed config (dot inlined) 871, typed config (noinline) 894 ms. So the typed table matches the hand-written list within noise (+1.8%, under one round's spread). Runfast ratios swing 95-99% between 8-round squares on this shared VM; a 16-round square on a quiet box is the next measurement. PORT PLAN: move the variant into apps/transformer (cells split for the 272 budget: shaped -> two pair helpers, matmul -> one helper per model line), where the in-tree 260K oracle tests then exercise the GQA kv_dim = 32 pair; queued after the current node gates.")
inl note_of_transformer_cpu_speed_14 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-CPU-SPEED", 14u32, "2026-11-03T10:51Z", "tibia", "11-03 07:0x tibia, 16-ROUND SQUARE (vultr, load about 0.5-0.9, 3 arms rotated, stories15M 256 steps): runfast mean 828.8 / median 816.5 (750-934); hand-written specialized + ParamEscape + map + dot inlined mean 820.2 / median 823.0 (768-853); typed-config variant (n13, dot inlined) mean 871.9 / median 862.0 (789-970). So the HAND-WRITTEN specialized build is at llama2.c runfast parity single-thread (mean 1% faster, median 0.8% slower, tighter spread): DoD part 1 is met in a hand-patched build, pending the compiler's noinline scoping. The typed config is a REAL ~5% behind the hand-written list (the 8-round square hid it). perf: both profiles have the same shape (four row kernels about 90%), plus 1.33% in main for the typed build only: the per-call dispatch chain (every matmul walks the model table's pair checks) got inlined into the forward; the rest is likely code size (319 vs 126 KB). FIX for the port: resolve the model ONCE at load into a runtime union (| Stories15M | Stories42M | Stories110M | Stories260K | Unknown) and dispatch each matmul by a single match on that union, so a call pays one switch, not the whole table; keep the per-model line as the single place where its dims are declared.")
inl note_of_transformer_cpu_speed_15 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-CPU-SPEED", 15u32, "2026-11-03T10:54Z", "tibia", "11-03 07:0x tibia, TYPED CONFIG v2 CLOSES THE 5% (n14's fix applied, scratch variant transformer-typed2.spi): the model is resolved ONCE at load into ':| known = | Stories15M | Stories42M | Stories110M | Stories260K | Unknown' (known_of cfg via 'fits (dims_*) cfg'), stored as model.k, and each matmul does one '?= k ?|' match to its model's 'shaped_by (dims_*)', else f32_kernels::matmul. Each model's dims are declared exactly once, in one dims_* function (four len witnesses) used by both known_of and the dispatch. 16-round square on vultr (load ~0.2-0.5, 3 arms rotated, stories15M 256 steps, output byte-identical): runfast mean 821.8 / median 791.5; hand-written spec 835.2 / 812.0; typed v2 831.6 / 820.0. So typed v2 equals the hand-written list (within 1%) and is within about 1% of runfast on the mean (3.5% on the median), with dots inlined by hand as in n12. C size 334 KB. PORT next into apps/transformer (cell splits for the budget), gated by the in-tree 260K oracle tests, which exercise the GQA (dim 64, kv 32) pair through dims_260k.")
inl note_of_transformer_cpu_speed_16 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-CPU-SPEED", 16u32, "2026-11-03T10:57Z", "tibia", "11-03 07:1x tibia, TYPED CONFIG v2 PORTED into apps/transformer/transformer.spi (unstaged): len witnesses, dot_l/rows_l, rows_if, shaped_square + shaped_wide + shaped, ':| known' with one dims_* per model (15M, 42M, 110M, 260K), fits/known_of, shaped_by, hit_known/hit_large, matmul_known (falls back to f32_kernels::matmul); model gains 'k : known' (k = known_of c at load); both matmul call sites go through matmul_known m::k. Cells sized for the budget (largest 261) and lines <= 120. GATE: the queued node load chain (tibia-load-chain-1) syncs spiral and runs the transformer oracle 5/5, where the 260K tests now take the dims_260k path (dim 64, hidden 172, vocab 512, kv 32: GQA). The compiler lane's scoped-noinline draft (COMPILER-C-BORROWED-PARAMS 3-inline-constant-trip-loops.kitdo, pack24) un-noinlines exactly dot_l here (j >= width k folds to a literal), so the installed-bits speed check waits for that candidate DLL.")
inl note_of_transformer_cpu_speed_17 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-CPU-SPEED", 17u32, "2026-11-03T10:59Z", "tibia", "11-03 07:0x tibia, PORT PRE-VERIFIED ON C (vultr, the in-tree transformer.spi built with vultr's compiler, gcc -Ofast -march=native -Wall: 0 errors, 0 free-nonheap warnings): stories15M 256 greedy steps byte-identical to the old arm; stories260K (pinned files fetched with sha256 -c OK against oracle.spi's pins) prints 566 bytes, the same length as llama2.c runfast on the same model, with the first 400 characters identical. So the dims_260k path (dim 64, hidden 172, vocab 512, GQA kv_dim 32) is correct on real weights. Rust and the memo-off Cells oracle remain for the node's load chain.")
inl note_of_transformer_cpu_speed_18 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-CPU-SPEED", 18u32, "2026-11-03T11:02Z", "tibia", "11-03 07:0x tibia, COUNTER-EVIDENCE for lever (1), see COMPILER-C-BORROWED-PARAMS n14: it is MODEL-DEPENDENT. stories42M (pinned: 167,020,572 B, sha256 prefix 9f65a100; output matches runfast for 300 chars), the in-tree typed-config transformer built on vultr, 16 rounds x 3 arms rotated, load 0.3-1.0: runfast 2,269.4 ms mean (median 2,276); dot NOINLINE (as generated) 2,320.4 (97.8% of runfast); dot INLINED (sed) 2,391.5 (94.9%). So on 42M inlining the constant-trip dot is 3.1% SLOWER, the opposite of stories15M (n12: inlining 5.6% faster). Reading: with dim 512 / hidden 1376 the per-row horizontal-sum epilogue is a smaller share of each row, so the inlined loop's cost (code size or register pressure in the row loop) dominates. So 'constant trip => no noinline' gains on small rows and loses on wide ones; a trip-count threshold (inline only below N, e.g. 384, between 15M's 288 and 42M's 512) or keeping noinline and fusing several rows per call (TRANSFORMER-CPU-SPEED n11 lever 2) may be the better rule. Please hold pack24's replacement until both models are measured with the candidate.")
inl note_of_transformer_cpu_speed_19 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-CPU-SPEED", 19u32, "2026-11-03T11:07Z", "tibia", "11-03 07:1x tibia, LEVER (2) REFUTED: 4 rows per call is SLOWER. Scratch variant of the in-tree transformer: rows_l computes 4 rows per step through dot4_l (4 f32 accumulators in a quad tuple, sharing each x[j] load; remainder rows via dot_l). Built on vultr (C 361 KB), output byte-identical on stories15M and stories42M. 16 rounds x 3 arms per model (load ~0.4-1.5): 15M runfast 845.7, tree (current, dot noinline) 918.0, rows4 978.0 (6.5% slower than tree); 42M runfast 2,155.5, tree 2,213.5, rows4 2,422.1 (9.4% slower). Reading: four row streams plus a tuple carried through the join point defeat the clean 8-wide single-stream loop gcc vectorizes in dot_l; the epilogue saving does not pay for that. So multi-row fusion at the Spiral source level is off; compiler's threshold rule (inline constant-trip loops only up to 384, COMPILER-C-BORROWED-PARAMS) is the lever to measure next on 15M and 42M. Also this round: tree (noinline) is 97.4% of runfast on 42M and 92.1% on 15M.")
inl note_of_transformer_cpu_speed_20 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-CPU-SPEED", 20u32, "2026-11-03T11:23Z", "tibia", "11-03 07:2x tibia, stories110M (pinned: 438,381,596 B, sha256 prefix 51526716; output matches runfast for 300 chars), 16 rounds x 3 arms on vultr: runfast 6,496.0 mean / 6,493.5 median; in-tree typed, dot noinline 6,714.2 / 6,650.0 (96.8% of runfast); dot inlined 6,755.4 / 6,731.5 (96.2%). Consistent with compiler's threshold rule (inline only trip literals up to 384): at 768/2048-wide rows noinline is slightly better. PER-MODEL SUMMARY, current tree on vultr, single thread: 15M 92% (noinline) / ~99% (dot inlined); 42M 97.4-97.8% (noinline best); 110M 96.8% (noinline best). With the threshold candidate, every model should sit at its best column.")
inl note_of_transformer_cpu_speed_21 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-CPU-SPEED", 21u32, "2026-11-03T11:33Z", "tibia", "11-03 07:3x tibia, CANDIDATE MEASURED, RECOMMEND NOT SHIPPING the (1, 384] rule. compiler's DLL (C:\\home\\remote-stage\\compiler-noinline\\..., core 867B0BD81652261F) compiled the in-tree transformer (typed per-model config, 7 dot widths) to cand.c on the node: noinline dropped on exactly the 3 dot_l at or below 384 (288, 172, 64), kept on 4 (512, 768, 1376, 2048). base.c = the same C with noinline restored on those 3 (sed), so the pair differs ONLY in the rule's effect. vultr, 16 rounds x 3 arms rotated, gcc -Ofast -march=native, output byte-identical (and equal to the tree build) on both models. stories15M: runfast 807.6 mean / 798.5 median; base 864.2 / 877.5; cand 994.2 / 985.5, so cand is 15% SLOWER (its fastest run, 925, is slower than base's median). stories42M: runfast 2,102.7; base 2,175.9; cand 2,164.9 (no 42M dot affected; equal within noise). READING: the +5.6% of n13 came from a single-model specialized source; in the typed-config program (20 row kernels plus dispatch) inlining the small dots into rows_l, and those into the dispatch, costs more than the epilogue saves. Whether inlining pays depends on the surrounding code, not just the trip count, so a trip-count rule cannot get it right; keeping noinline on every Borrowed self-loop is the safe default. Current tree per model (base, noinline): 15M 93%, 42M 97%, 110M 97% of runfast.")
inl note_of_transformer_cpu_speed_22 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-CPU-SPEED", 22u32, "2026-11-04T04:17Z", "tibia", "11-04 00:17 tibia GROOM: parked to Planned (no note in 12 h). State: typed transformer at 93% (15M), 96% (42M), ~98% (110M) of llama2.c runfast, single thread, 16-round vultr squares. The constant-trip inline rule was measured 15% slower and not shipped (COMPILER-C-BORROWED-PARAMS 11-03). NEXT: re-run the 16-round square on a quiet vultr after the next compiler pack that touches C codegen; first check vultr load.")
inl note_of_transformer_cpu_speed_23 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-CPU-SPEED", 23u32, "2026-11-04T04:21Z", "tibia", "11-04 00:21 tibia: back to Active, percent kept. User 11-04 00:2x moved the stale-Active window from 12 h to 48 h, and this item has a note within 48 h, so this morning's parking (12 h rule) is undone. The next step in the 00:2x note still holds.")
inl note_of_transformer_cpu_speed_24 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-CPU-SPEED", 24u32, "2026-11-04T13:06Z", "tibia", "11-04 09:06 tibia: the multi-thread half of this item has a working path now: lib parallel::for_index (C: omp parallel for over 16-row chunks; compiler emits atomic refcounts when SPIRAL_THREADS is set) applied to the Q8 matmul in TRANSFORMER-QUANT-Q8 v5 gives byte-identical tokens at 1 and 2 threads. The same 16-row chunking applies to the fp32 matmul. A scaling measurement waits for a quiet multi-core machine.")
inl note_of_transformer_cpu_speed_25 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-CPU-SPEED", 25u32, "2026-11-04T13:12Z", "tibia", "11-04 09:12 tibia, fp32 THREADED TOO (draft TRANSFORMER-QUANT-Q8 v6 = v5 + the shape-specialized fp32 kernels chunked: rows_l takes an end, and rows_par runs 16-row chunks through parallel::for_index; 210 omp pragmas, one per specialization). vultr stories15M: fp32 output byte-identical to the fp32 C oracle at OMP_NUM_THREADS 1 and 2, and Q8 byte-identical to runq at 1 and 2. Speed still unmeasured (needs a quiet multi-core machine; vultr was at load 13 on 2 vCPUs). Landing order: lib parallel.spi (box gate queued), then transformer v3 -> v6 into the tree, then a 1/2/4-thread square against llama2.c runomp.")
inl note_of_transformer_cpu_speed_26 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-CPU-SPEED", 26u32, "2026-11-04T13:40Z", "tibia", "11-04 09:40 tibia, CLOCK CORRECTION: tibia's 11-04 note prefixes on this item (and about 50 across 20 items) had been typed without reading the clock, up to 140 min ahead. Each was edited to its stored at time (note-edit with --expect-prefix), so the prefixes above are now real local times. Note content unchanged.")
inl note_of_transformer_cpu_speed_27 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-CPU-SPEED", 27u32, "2026-11-04T15:02Z", "tibia", "11-04 11:02 tibia, WHERE THE 15M GAP IS, AND ONE REFUTED FIX: perf on vultr (stories15M, OMP 1): 93% of the time is the two shape-specialized dot_l loops (84% n=288, 9% n=768); attention, softmax and allocation are under 2%. Under -Ofast -march=native our dot_l and run.c's matmul compile to the SAME inner loop: vmovups + vmulps + vaddps on ymm into ONE accumulator, so both are latency-bound on the vaddps chain, and the remaining gap is around the loop (cycles:u over 3 rounds: base 2.98e9/3.14e9/2.95e9 vs runfast 2.85e9/2.92e9/2.89e9, about 4% at load 7-11). REFUTED: 4-row blocking with one accumulator per row (drafts spiral\\TRANSFORMER-CPU-SPEED\\rows4 8C4BA8A3415F, and rows4b 102CF98DF645 sized for the budget). It keeps each row's summation order, so strict -O3 output is BYTE-IDENTICAL to base on 15M (813 B) and 42M (746 B), but it costs 7.95e9/8.28e9/7.78e9 cycles, 2.6x base: gcc SLP-vectorizes ACROSS the 4 rows (xmm lanes built from 4 strided loads) instead of 8-wide along each row. Without SIMD types (COMPILER-SIMD-CODEGEN) the multi-accumulator idea cannot be expressed so gcc keeps 8-wide-per-row vectors, and splitting one row's sum would break bit parity with run.c. Next lever to test instead: the per-row overhead around the loop (dot_l is a separate constprop function called per row; run.c inlines it into the row loop).")
inl note_of_transformer_cpu_speed_28 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-CPU-SPEED", 28u32, "2026-11-04T15:03Z", "tibia", "11-04 11:03 tibia: per-row call lever also REFUTED. The C backend emits every join point as __attribute__((noinline)), so dot_l stays a separate call per row; raising gcc's inline limit changes nothing for that reason. Rewriting only the 7 dot_l definitions to static inline (sed on the generated C, same output as base) made it SLOWER: cycles:u 3.00e9/3.16e9/3.18e9 vs base 2.90e9/2.90e9/3.00e9 in the same rounds. Those same rounds put base level with runfast (2.87e9/2.95e9/3.02e9); the round before showed about 4.6 percent. So at vultr load 7 the cycle counts are noisy to about 4 percent and do not resolve the remaining gap. Only a clean, load-gated wall-clock square can (the 11-03 squares: 15M 93.3, 42M 96.4, 110M about 98 percent of runfast). The hot loop itself is already the same as run.c's (see the previous note).")
inl note_of_transformer_cpu_speed_29 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-CPU-SPEED", 29u32, "2026-11-04T16:48Z", "tibia", "11-04 12:48 tibia: inherits from TRANSFORMER-QUANT-Q8 (closed): typed Q8 is about 1.5x behind llama2.c runq on a clean vultr square (mean 934 vs 633 ms, median 915 vs 607, stories15M, 16 rounds, 0 of 32 rows loaded). Same method as the fp32 work: perf the Q8 path to see whether rows_q8/dot_i8 or the activation quantization dominates before trying a lever.")
inl note_of_transformer_cpu_speed_30 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-CPU-SPEED", 30u32, "2026-11-04T16:52Z", "tibia", "11-04 12:52 tibia, Q8 GAP LOCATED, AND A LEVER THAT KEEPS PARITY: perf (vultr, stories15M v2 Q8, single thread): typed Q8 spends 40.5% in row_q8 and 39.6% in dot_i8, i.e. one noinline call per 64-wide group (the C backend emits every join point as __attribute__((noinline))), while runq spends 82.8% in one matmul whose group dot is inline. Experiment on the generated C (sed: dot_i8 only -> static inline). STRICT -O3 (no fast-math, no march), the only fair parity reference: base and inlined builds are both IDENTICAL to strict runq.c (724 B prefix); timing over 6 rounds at load 1.0: strict runq 741 ms, base 1033 ms, inlined 853 ms, so -17%, and the strict gap narrows from 1.39x to 1.15x. Under -Ofast the inlined build changes tokens (byte 221): once dot_i8 is inline, gcc reassociates the float group sum (val += f32(ival) * ws * xs) differently from runq -Ofast; with -fno-associative-math both base and inlined differ from runq -Ofast, so '-Ofast parity' only ever held because both sides reorder alike. Contrast: inlining the fp32 dot_l made it SLOWER (11-04 11:0x note), because there the call is paid per 288-wide row, not per 64-wide group. So a blanket noinline removal is wrong; the ask is selective (small leaf join points called inside a loop). Sent to compiler with both data points.")
inl note_of_transformer_cpu_speed_31 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-CPU-SPEED", 31u32, "2026-11-04T17:07Z", "tibia", "11-04 13:07 tibia: measured compiler's call-site rule (C-BORROWED-LOOP-INLINE draft, applied as a text pass to the generated C: 29 single-site borrowed loops become static inline, multi-site dot_l36/dot_l30 keep noinline). Strict -O3: Q8 output of base, dot_i8-only and rule builds all IDENTICAL to strict runq.c; fp32 output of both inlined builds IDENTICAL to base. 6 interleaved rounds (vultr busy: 20 of 36 rows above load 1.5, so relative order only): Q8 base 1152 / dot_i8-only 936 / rule 844 ms (rule about -27% vs base); fp32 base 3098 / 3049 / 3120 ms (within noise). Sent to compiler: no size term needed for these programs.")
inl note_of_transformer_cpu_speed_32 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-CPU-SPEED", 32u32, "2026-11-04T17:25Z", "tibia", "11-04 13:25 tibia: THREAD SCALING ON VULTR: VOID for scaling. Square (stories15M fp32, 8 rounds, -Ofast -march=native -fopenmp; waited 24 min for load 0.47, but 17 of 32 rows still ran above load 1.5): v6 (threaded rows, draft TRANSFORMER-QUANT-Q8 v6) output IDENTICAL at 1 and 2 threads; v6 1 thread 971 ms, v6 2 threads 920 ms, llama2.c runfast 1 thread 842 ms, runomp 2 threads 887 ms. Even llama2.c's own OpenMP build is slower on 2 threads than its single-thread build here, so vultr's 2 shared vCPUs cannot measure thread scaling for anyone. The scaling claim needs a quiet machine with real cores (the node at idle, or a rental). Relative reading only: v6 at 2 threads ~= runomp at 2 threads within this noise.")
inl note_of_transformer_cpu_speed_33 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-CPU-SPEED", 33u32, "2026-11-04T17:37Z", "tibia", "11-04 13:37 tibia: CLEAN SQUARE for compiler's call-site inline rule (C-BORROWED-LOOP-INLINE): vultr, strict gcc -O3, stories15M, 16 interleaved rounds per build and model, 0 of 64 rows above load 1.5. Outputs IDENTICAL base vs rule (Q8 and fp32). Q8 996 -> 798 ms (-19.9%); fp32 2807 -> 2800 ms (neutral). When it ships, typed Q8 moves from about 1.39x to about 1.1x of strict runq.c on the same machine (strict runq 741 ms in the earlier 6-round run).")
inl note_of_transformer_cpu_speed_34 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-CPU-SPEED", 34u32, "2026-11-04T20:10Z", "tibia", "11-04 16:10 tibia: CLEAN fp32 SQUARE (vultr, stories15M, 16 interleaved rounds, gcc -Ofast -march=native both, 0 of 32 rows above load 1.5): outputs IDENTICAL; typed (live apps/transformer build) mean 1010 ms vs llama2.c runfast 938 ms, so typed runs at 92.9% of runfast's speed, consistent with 11-03's 93.3%: no regression from today's landed changes, and the ~7% gap is real. Where it is NOT: the hot loop (same vmulps/vaddps chain as run.c), inlining dot_l (slower), 4-row blocking (2.6x slower). Remaining candidates are outside the dot: per-token work around the 50k row calls, the rope/softmax/attention code paths (under 2% each in perf), and allocation; a perf diff typed vs runfast by symbol on a quiet machine is the next step.")
inl note_of_transformer_cpu_speed_35 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-CPU-SPEED", 35u32, "2026-11-04T20:13Z", "tibia", "11-04 16:13 tibia: WHERE THE fp32 GAP IS NOT (vultr, load ~1.5, stories15M, same binaries as the clean square). perf stat user-mode: typed 2.685e9 cycles / 3.078e9 instructions vs runfast 2.642e9 / 3.049e9, i.e. +1.6% cycles, +1.0% instructions. /usr/bin/time over 3 runs each: sys 0.01-0.02 s for both (typed's first cold run 0.11 s, 12 major faults), minor faults ~2290 vs ~2345, user 0.81-0.86 vs 0.77-0.84 s. So it is NOT checkpoint loading or paging (my mmap guess was wrong), and the compute differs by only ~1-2%. The clean square's 7.7% wall gap (1010 vs 938 ms) is therefore mostly outside the counted user cycles: candidates are per-process start (tokenizer build, allocation before the first token) and cold-start effects that a 256-token run does not amortize. Next: time the start alone (load + tokenizer, 0 tokens) for both, then a 1024-token run, where a fixed start cost would shrink in relative terms.")
inl note_of_transformer_cpu_speed_36 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-CPU-SPEED", 36u32, "2026-11-04T20:18Z", "tibia", "11-04 16:18 tibia: startup ruled out too, the other way round: typed with TRANSFORMER_DUMP_STEPS=0 (checkpoint + tokenizer + run state, no forward step) takes 11-15 ms, while runfast -n 1 (start plus one token) takes 27-32 ms (first cold run 107). So typed starts FASTER. State of the fp32 comparison: counted user-mode work differs by +1.6% cycles / +1.0% instructions, sys time and faults are equal, startup favours typed, yet the clean wall-clock square shows 7.7%. That difference is UNEXPLAINED; one untested candidate is steal time on vultr's shared vCPUs, which user-mode counters do not see, but this is not shown. A machine with dedicated cores (the node when idle) would separate it; until then, report the compute gap as ~1-2% and the wall gap as 7.7% on vultr.")
inl note_of_transformer_cpu_speed_37 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-CPU-SPEED", 37u32, "2026-11-04T20:19Z", "tibia", "11-04 16:19 tibia: steal time REFUTED as the cause: /proc/stat steal ticks read around each of 12 interleaved runs (vultr, load ~1.3) were 0 for every run of both programs. Same run, cold first typed run (1778 ms) excluded: typed ~1019 ms vs runfast ~953 ms, so the ~7% wall gap reproduces. Open discrepancy, stated as is: user-mode cycles differ by ~1.6% but user TIME by ~5% (0.81-0.86 vs 0.77-0.84 s), so typed spends its user time at a lower effective clock or with stalls the cycles:u counter does not separate. Not explained; the next instruments would be perf stat with stalled-cycles/cache-misses and a run on dedicated cores. Parking this thread here: no lever is indicated yet.")
inl note_of_transformer_cpu_speed_38 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-CPU-SPEED", 38u32, "2026-11-04T20:28Z", "tibia", "11-04 16:28 tibia: last probe on vultr, then parked. At load 1.17 one perf stat pair gave task-clock typed 858 ms vs runfast 852 ms (0.7% apart; cycles:u typed 2.41e9 < runfast 2.46e9; cache-misses 26.3M vs 25.3M; L1d misses 14.3M vs 15.7M; backend-stall counter not exposed by this VM). Repeating it shows typed's task-clock moving 858-1047 ms between runs (about +/-10%), more than the compute difference itself, and wall vs task-clock do not track run to run. CONCLUSION: on vultr's 2 shared vCPUs, single-run comparisons cannot resolve a few percent; the 16-round clean square (typed at 92.9% of runfast) is the robust figure, and the counters say most of that gap is not extra work. Next measurement needs dedicated, idle cores (the node when it is idle); no code lever is indicated.")
inl note_of_transformer_cpu_speed_39 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-CPU-SPEED", 39u32, "2026-11-04T23:20Z", "tibia", "11-04 19:20 tibia: Q8 rule vs runq, SAME RUN (replaces the cross-run 'about 1.1x' estimate): vultr, strict gcc -O3 for all three, stories15M v2 Q8, 16 interleaved rounds after waiting for load 0.72; another lane's Cells run loaded part of it, so 23 of 48 rows ran above load 1.5 and only the quiet rows count (8-9 per program): strict runq.c 804 ms, typed with compiler's call-site inline rule 898 ms (1.12x runq), typed base 1166 ms (1.45x runq). The rule build's output is IDENTICAL to strict runq. So C-BORROWED-LOOP-INLINE moves typed Q8 from about 1.45x to about 1.12x of runq at strict parity.")
inl note_of_transformer_cpu_speed_40 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-CPU-SPEED", 40u32, "2026-11-05T07:09Z", "tibia", "11-05 03:09 tibia: NODE MULTI-THREAD SQUARE #1 VOID, cause found. Typed v6 (the placed v3 plus parallel::for_index over 16-row chunks in the fp32 and Q8 matmuls; 210 omp regions in the C) built on the node (Windows, mingw gcc 15.2) with -Ofast -march=native CRASHES with 0xC0000005 after printing its text, with or without -fopenmp, and -O2 -march=native crashes too. -O0 and -Ofast without -march=native run. This is the known mingw-w64 Win64 AVX stack-alignment bug (GCC PR 54412: aligned vmovaps spills onto a stack Win64 only guarantees to 16 bytes). '-Wa,-muse-unaligned-vector-move' fixes it: v6 text is byte-identical to v3 at OMP 1 and 8 (817 chars, stories15M). v3 at the same flags never crashed; its code shape happens not to spill. Also: llama2.c's win.c no longer builds on gcc 15 (VirtualProtect PDWORD vs uint32_t*), so it needs -Wno-error=incompatible-pointer-types. The square's harness now refuses to time anything whose parity check fails. First timings with the workaround were taken while another Cells job ran, so they are NOT a result: v6 15M 18.2 s at t1 and 32.7 s at t8 vs v3 about 2.3 s. That is an 8x+ slowdown that contention alone cannot explain; a suspect is per-region overhead in Windows libgomp (210 regions per token on small rows). Square #2 (tibia-tf6-square-3) is queued after gl2, before cl2. Vultr Linux v6 at 2 threads matched v3 earlier (no AVX bug there).")
inl note_of_transformer_cpu_speed_41 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-CPU-SPEED", 41u32, "2026-11-05T07:27Z", "tibia", "11-05 03:27 tibia: NODE SQUARE #2, CLEAN (job tibia-tf6-square-3, 1088 s; i7-4810MQ 4C/8T Haswell, Windows, mingw gcc 15.2, every build -Ofast -march=native with '-Wa,-muse-unaligned-vector-move' for the typed builds; llama2.c run.c with win.c). Parity gate passed first: v6 at 8 threads == v3 byte for byte (15M 817 chars, 42M 749), and typed's first 200 chars == runfast's. 8 interleaved rounds x 6 arms x 2 models, all 96 rows at node load <= 60%, 256 greedy tokens, medians in ms (min-max). 15M: runfast 1941 (1884-1975), runomp8 1830, typed v3 1917 (1877-2022), v6 t1 2170, v6 t4 1792, v6 t8 1869. 42M: runfast 5130 (5001-5180), runomp8 3674 (3591-3936), typed v3 5090 (4978-5217), v6 t1 5355, v6 t4 3841, v6 t8 3808. READING: (1) single-thread, typed v3 is AT OR SLIGHTLY AHEAD OF runfast on this machine (15M 1.2% faster, 42M 0.8% faster; ranges overlap), unlike vultr's 92.9% (different CPU, and vultr was noisy). (2) multi-thread, v6 reaches 95.6-96.5% of llama2.c's OpenMP build on 42M (3808 at 8 threads vs 3674) and beats it on 15M at 4 threads (1792 vs 1830). (3) the price of threading at 1 thread is 13% (15M) / 5% (42M) over v3: atomic refcounts plus 210 omp regions per token. Next lever: fewer, larger parallel regions per token (fuse the q/k/v and w1/w3 matmuls into one region). The goal row 'tok/s >= runfast single-thread' is MET on the node; '>= OpenMP build' is within 4.5% on 42M.")
inl note_of_transformer_cpu_speed_42 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-CPU-SPEED", 42u32, "2026-11-05T08:39Z", "tibia", "11-05 04:39 tibia: CHUNK SWEEP #1 timing VOID, parity green. v7 (draft TRANSFORMER-QUANT-Q8/v7 = v6 plus a runtime row-chunk knob TRANSFORMER_CHUNK, default 16, for both the fp32 and Q8 matmuls) matched v3 byte for byte at chunks 4/8/16/32 on 15M and 42M at 8 threads. But 69 of 72 rows ran with the node above 60% load from other work (runomp8 15M ranged 4.1-15.8 s, against 1.8 s quiet), so no timing claim. WHY THE KNOB: at a fixed 16-row chunk, 15M's dim 288 gives 18 chunks on 8 threads, three waves with the last a quarter full (about 75% efficiency), while llama2.c's static omp schedule has no tail. Rerun tibia-tf7-chunk-sweep-2 is queued after the budget run; it now waits for 3 consecutive load readings under 30% before each round.")
inl note_of_transformer_cpu_speed_43 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-CPU-SPEED", 43u32, "2026-11-05T10:39Z", "tibia", "11-05 06:39 tibia: CHUNK SWEEP #2 (node job tibia-tf7-chunk-sweep-2; quiet-gated rounds; only the 53 of 72 rows at <= 40% load count; parity green at chunks 4/8/16/32 on both models). Medians in ms, 8 threads. 15M: runomp8 1752, v6 (chunk 16) 1780, v7 chunk 4 2052, chunk 8 1979, chunk 16 1889, chunk 32 1891. 42M: runomp8 3529, v6 3641, v7 chunk 4 3974, chunk 8 3850, chunk 16 3816, chunk 32 3785. VERDICT: the load-imbalance hypothesis is REFUTED. Smaller chunks are slower (4 worst, 8 next), so the 16-row tail wave is not what costs time. The runtime knob itself costs about 5-6% (v7 at chunk 16 is v6's partition, but reads the env var on every matmul call and carries twice the generated C, 1.08 MB vs 517 KB). v6 stays the best typed multi-thread build: 98.4% (15M) and 96.9% (42M) of llama2.c's OpenMP build on quiet rows. Draft v7 is kept in the store as the negative record and is not a candidate. Remaining gap levers: per-token region count (fuse q/k/v and w1/w3 into one parallel region) and v6's 5-13% single-thread overhead (atomic refcounts).")
inl transformer_gpu () = Task ("TRANSFORMER-GPU", "Transformer GPU: CUDA and WGSL kernels for matmul, attention and the elementwise ops from the same typed source; tok/s per model size on one rented L40S; gate: WGSL under 50% of CUDA on the same GPU means publish CPU + CUDA only", Feature, P1, D7, 0u32, Planned, "TRANSFORMER-CPU-REFERENCE,GPU-STACKED-RUNS", "transformer,gpu,wgsl,cuda,benchmarks,team:tibia,tibia", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_transformer_gpu () = Planning ("TRANSFORMER-GPU", Minutes 1800u32, Load Heavy, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_transformer_gpu_1 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-GPU", 1u32, "2026-11-02T13:42Z", "tibia", "Filed 11-02 by tibia under TYPED-TRANSFORMER-LAB (n4, from the original deliberation, share eab494f1). Reuses tibia's WGSL pipeline. GPU spend comes out of the 20 USD cap (TIBIA-GPU-RENTAL).")
inl note_of_transformer_gpu_2 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-GPU", 2u32, "2026-11-02T23:13Z", "tibia", "11-02 19:1x tibia, prerequisite found for the WGSL half: the native runner (spiral apps/compiler/tmp/tests/native-shims/wgsl-run/src/main.rs, wgpu 30) takes only `spiral-wgsl <file.wgsl>`; it binds three read-write storage buffers it creates itself (SpiralIo code + lanes, and the spiral_heap words) and loads no input data, so a forward pass cannot get the checkpoint. Needed: an optional read-only storage binding filled from a file (e.g. `SPIRAL_WGSL_INPUT=<path>` -> binding 3, `array<u32>` words, f32 by bitcast), plus the lib/compiler side that names that binding from Spiral (an input array of the WGSL backend). Owner of the runner: the compiler lane (native shims). The WGSL lane path itself is proven on lavapipe (TIBIA-BATCHED-EVOLUTION n6: C and WGSL lanes bit-identical), so this binding is the only missing piece for a WGSL transformer.")
inl transformer_quant_q8 () = Task ("TRANSFORMER-QUANT-Q8", "Transformer int8 (runq.c-style Q8_0 group quantization): typed quantized tensors, output matching llama2.c runq.c, and a tok/s comparison; the integer kernels are shared with tibia's Q8 network where shapes allow", Story, P2, D5, 100u32, Done, "TRANSFORMER-CPU-REFERENCE", "transformer,tibia,lib,team:tibia", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_transformer_quant_q8 () = Planning ("TRANSFORMER-QUANT-Q8", Minutes 600u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_transformer_quant_q8_1 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-QUANT-Q8", 1u32, "2026-11-02T13:42Z", "tibia", "Filed 11-02 by tibia under TYPED-TRANSFORMER-LAB (n4, from the original deliberation, share eab494f1). Not in the deliberation (fp32 only); added because Mojo/zig/rust ports compete on runq too and tibia's network is already Q8.")
inl note_of_transformer_quant_q8_2 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-QUANT-Q8", 2u32, "2026-11-04T11:45Z", "tibia", "11-04 07:45 tibia, Q8 WORKS AND MATCHES runq.c TOKEN FOR TOKEN (draft TRANSFORMER-QUANT-Q8 v1, base transformer.spi 79D9953A5ED4; the fp32 path is unchanged and stays the default). Design: model gains q8 : option q8 (q : array i8 parallel to w, s : floats one scale per group, gs). with_q8 quantizes the weight slabs at load (embed, wq/wk/wv/wo, w1/w2/w3, cls if unshared; norms stay fp32), since every slab is a multiple of dim and gs divides dim (64 halved until it does: 260K 64, 15M 32), so groups match export.py's per-tensor groups. One exact rounding function: rounded even x truncates, takes the fraction exactly and settles ties by mode, half-to-even for weights (torch.round in export.py) and half-away for activations (C round in runq.c). matmul_q8 quantizes the activations per call and accumulates int32 per group, val += f32(ival) * w_scale * x_scale in runq's order; the embedding is dequantized as runq does. TRANSFORMER_Q8=1 switches it on. ORACLE: llama2.c runq.c at the pinned commit 350e04fe built on vultr; the v2 Q8 checkpoints are written by a scratch C exporter (bin2q8.c, export.py's exact layout, nearbyintf ties-to-even; vultr has no numpy/torch). RESULT on vultr: stories260K byte-identical to runq; stories15M byte-identical once both are built with plain -O3 (the first try used -march=native, so gcc fused multiply-adds and one token differed). NEXT: a Cells oracle test (pin runq's greedy tokens), speed (quantize activations once per shared input like runq, no per-call allocation), then a runq vs typed tok/s square.")
inl note_of_transformer_quant_q8_3 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-QUANT-Q8", 3u32, "2026-11-04T12:04Z", "tibia", "11-04 08:04 tibia, Q8 v1 FINISHED IN DRAFTS, BYTE-IDENTICAL TO runq.c ON C AND RUST (vultr, installed compiler bin/ net11.0, plain -O3 for C): stories260K 1279/1279 bytes and stories15M 724/724 bytes on both backends; the fp32 path is unchanged (run.c text on Rust). Changes since 08:0x: the four new over-budget definitions were split (quantize_from without a separate qat index, quantize_group plus an offset loop, weight_slabs, group_term), so 0 new over budget; tests added: rounding_breaks_ties_to_even_for_weights_and_away_for_activations (c, rust: 2.5 even 2 / away 3, -3.5 even -4, -2.5 away -3, 2.4999998 to 2) and q8_greedy_stories260k_matches_runq (rust, against oracle::stories260k_q8_text = Once + 255 x ' upon', checked against runq's output). EDGE CASE caught by the refactor: 260K's hidden size 172 is not a multiple of gs 64, and runq quantizes and multiplies only the whole groups (128 of 172; its matmul loop is j <= n - GS), so activation quantization stops at n / gs * gs; looping to n read past the buffer and aborted. Format/respell clean. NEXT: Cells gate of the two tests (the node job host is failing starts on a saturated node, so box or a detached node run), then the land into apps/transformer (base 79D9953A5ED4), then speed.")
inl note_of_transformer_quant_q8_4 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-QUANT-Q8", 4u32, "2026-11-04T12:16Z", "tibia", "11-04 08:16 tibia, Q8 IN THE TREE (unstaged; apps/transformer is tibia's app, edited in place): transformer.spi 79D9953A5ED4 -> 33C3CDDE5B06, oracle.spi C19485C1C21A -> 7DDF68D56D70 (base copies in kit drafts TRANSFORMER-QUANT-Q8\\base; git shows transformer.spi MM because the user had staged an earlier version, which stays as it is). PROOF of the two new tests on Rust (vultr test-body probe q8_probe.spi in the stage, models linked into ~/.cache/spiral-bin/models/llama2c): VERDICT OK, rounding true, q8 true; negative twin (tie rule swapped inside rounded) gives VERDICT BAD rounding false. Note: the 260K Q8 greedy text does not change when the tie rule is swapped (exact ties are rare in its activations), so the rounding test pins the rule, and the stories15M byte-identity against runq is the end-to-end check. The Linux CI cells-gate covers both on the next push. NEXT: speed (quantize a shared input once: q/k/v share xb, w1/w3 share it; no per-call allocation), then a quiet-vultr 16-round square typed-Q8 vs runq.")
inl note_of_transformer_quant_q8_5 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-QUANT-Q8", 5u32, "2026-11-04T12:18Z", "tibia", "11-04 08:18 tibia, FIRST SPEED READING (vultr at load 17-22 from other lanes, so rough: 3 rounds alternating, stories15M 256 tokens, plain -O3 for every build): runq.c ~2750 ms; typed Q8 ~4500 ms (1.6x behind runq); typed fp32 at the same flags ~9600 ms, so Q8 already halves our fp32 time. perf of typed Q8: dot_i8 34% and row_q8 34%, with dot_i8 vectorized (pmaddwd) but called once per 32-element group, so call overhead (push/pop/test) tops its profile, because the C backend keeps self-loops out of line; quantize_from 14% plus group_max 6% = ~20% is quantizing the 15M weights at LOAD, which runq does not do (it maps a pre-quantized v2 file; its quantize is 1%). NEXT SLICE (decided): read llama2.c's v2 Q8 checkpoint format directly (magic ak42 at offset 0, 256-byte header, fp32 norms, then per tensor per layer int8 q followed by fp32 scales), placing q/s at the same layout indices so the existing kernels run unchanged. That removes the load-time quantization, and the typed transformer then runs runq's own .bin files (the scratch C exporter bin2q8.c produces them; CI could pin a q80 file). After that: one call per row instead of per group (fold the group loop into the row loop) and a quiet 16-round square.")
inl note_of_transformer_quant_q8_6 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-QUANT-Q8", 6u32, "2026-11-04T12:46Z", "tibia", "11-04 08:46 tibia, v2 CHECKPOINT READER + WRITER. Reader (placed in the tree at 09:1x, C8C4EE5C03D5): model_at checks the ak42 magic and routes llama2.c v2 Q8 files to model_q8_at (header from offset 8, shared byte 36, gs at 37; fp32 norms into their layout slots; per tensor per layer the int8 q then fp32 scales at the same layout indices), so the existing kernels run unchanged; with_q8 no longer re-quantizes an already quantized model. runq's own v2 files produce byte-identical output (260K 1279 bytes, 15M 724). Rough timing at load 16-20: runq 1.7-2.7 s, typed reading the v2 file 2.4-3.2 s, typed quantize-on-load 3.0-3.6 s, so reading the file removes most of the load cost. Writer (draft v3, needs lib convert.bits_of_f32, landing now): write_q8_file produces the v2 format, BYTE-IDENTICAL to export.py's layout as reproduced by the scratch C exporter (278,608 and 17,101,696 bytes), so the scratch C tool is no longer in the oracle chain, since runq reads Spiral-written files. New Rust test q8_checkpoint_written_in_the_runq_format_reads_back_to_the_same_text (write stories260K to temp, read back, Q8 text). Both v2 and v3 blocks were split to 0 new over-budget definitions (v2 had added 4 before my budget pass: norms_into, read_tensor, tensors, model_q8_at). lib convert.bits_of_f32 (inverse of f32_of_bits; C memcpy, Rust to_bits, Zig @bitCast, WGSL bitcast): box gate 10/10, mutant (C helper returns u + 1) fails exactly the new test on c.")
inl note_of_transformer_quant_q8_7 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-QUANT-Q8", 7u32, "2026-11-04T12:53Z", "tibia", "11-04 08:53 tibia, REFUTED speed idea (kept out of the tree): one call per row instead of per group (draft v4 row_scan: a single loop over the row, int32 accumulator, a group-end branch adding ival * w_scale * x_scale). Output stays identical to runq, but it is ~6x SLOWER (12.5-17.2 s vs v3 2.0-2.8 s on 15M, vultr load ~7-15, 4 rounds alternating), because the per-element branch stops gcc from vectorizing, while v3's per-group dot_i8 is vectorized (pmaddwd) and its call overhead matters less. v3 (per-group dot) stays the shape; in the same rounds runq ran 1.7-2.3 s and v3 2.0-2.8 s.")
inl note_of_transformer_quant_q8_8 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-QUANT-Q8", 8u32, "2026-11-04T12:54Z", "tibia", "11-04 08:54 tibia, ALL FOUR MODELS MATCH runq.c: stories42M (v2 file 44,321,024 bytes) and stories110M (116,432,128 bytes) were written by the Spiral v2 writer (draft v3 on vultr), and runq.c plus the typed reader give byte-identical greedy output (800 and 693 bytes), adding to 260K (1279) and 15M (724). So the Q8 path, the v2 reader and the v2 writer are exact against llama2.c at every model size it ships. Remaining for 100%: v3 into the tree once lib convert.bits_of_f32 lands (landing now), and a quiet-vultr 16-round speed square typed-Q8 vs runq on 15M/42M/110M.")
inl note_of_transformer_quant_q8_9 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-QUANT-Q8", 9u32, "2026-11-04T13:05Z", "tibia", "11-04 09:05 tibia, THREADED Q8 MATMUL DRAFTED (drafts TRANSFORMER-QUANT-Q8 v5 = v3 + matmul_q8 over 16-row chunks through parallel::for_index; it uses LIB-PARALLEL-FOR's parallel.spi draft, whose box gate is queued, and compiler's atomic refcounts when SPIRAL_THREADS is set, already in the installed compiler). Generated C has 10 omp pragmas and 138 atomic refc ops; output is BYTE-IDENTICAL to runq at OMP_NUM_THREADS 1 and 2 (stories15M, Spiral-written v2 file). Timing VOID: vultr has 2 vCPUs and was at load 8.5, so 2 threads ran 4.7-12.7 s vs 1.3-2.0 s on one thread, the oversubscription collapse (OpenMP barriers wait on descheduled threads). Thread scaling needs a quiet multi-core machine (the node or the box when idle). Order: land parallel.spi, place v3 then v5, then a quiet 1/2/4-thread square against runomp (llama2.c built with -fopenmp).")
inl note_of_transformer_quant_q8_10 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-QUANT-Q8", 10u32, "2026-11-04T13:22Z", "tibia", "11-04 09:22 tibia: LIB convert.bits_of_f32 LANDED green (lib_land_window, convert.spi 3B7C6EDA198E, lib digest 58498597F13E2F5C, 22 passed, type probe pass, 0 respell drift; undo lib-land-undo\\20261104-091457). Q8 v3 (writer: put_u32/put_f32s/put_i8s, header_v2, write_tensors, q8_file_bytes, write_q8_file + its rust read-back test) PLACED into apps/transformer/transformer.spi under a hash guard (was C8C4EE5C03D5, now 0EFE08E70B3B; pre-v3 copy in drafts base\\transformer.pre-v3.spi). vultr --check of the placed file against landed convert: exit 0. Pending: the app's own test run (rust read-back test) on a remote; v5/v6 (threaded) wait for lib parallel.spi.")
inl note_of_transformer_quant_q8_11 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-QUANT-Q8", 11u32, "2026-11-04T13:39Z", "tibia", "11-04 09:39 tibia, CLOCK CORRECTION: tibia's 11-04 note prefixes on this item (and about 50 across 20 items) had been typed without reading the clock, up to 140 min ahead. Each was edited to its stored at time (note-edit with --expect-prefix), so the prefixes above are now real local times. Note content unchanged.")
inl note_of_transformer_quant_q8_12 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-QUANT-Q8", 12u32, "2026-11-04T14:36Z", "tibia", "11-04 10:36 tibia: NODE TESTS GREEN for the placed v3 (apps/transformer/transformer.spi 0EFE08E70B3B with landed convert.bits_of_f32): ci_gate.ps1 on the node mirror tibia-tf (spiral head 98e51d32, digest CE93F5FC7AE22670), job job-1793801263208-tibia-tf-tests, 8 discovered, 8 executed, 8 passed, 0 failed, 0 not run, 800 s, including q8_checkpoint_written_in_the_runq_format_reads_back_to_the_same_text, q8_greedy_stories260k_matches_runq and the rounding test. So the typed Q8 path (reader, writer, matmul) is in the tree and green. LEFT: (a) the 16-round load-gated speed square typed Q8 vs runq on vultr (vultr at load 5 now; waits for quiet), (b) threaded v5/v6 (parallel::for_index is landed now) after a measured speedup on a quiet multi-core machine.")
inl note_of_transformer_quant_q8_13 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-QUANT-Q8", 13u32, "2026-11-04T16:48Z", "tibia", "11-04 12:48 tibia, DONE: (1) Typed quantized tensors: q8 = { q : array i8; s : floats; gs : i32 } with the v2 reader and writer in apps/transformer (in the tree, node tests 8/8 including the writer's read-back test). (2) Output matching runq.c: byte-identical on 260K, 15M, 42M and 110M (C and Rust), and again in this square. (3) tok/s comparison, CLEAN 16-ROUND SQUARE on vultr (stories15M v2 Q8 file, 256 greedy tokens, both gcc -Ofast -march=native, waited 25 min for quiet, 0 of 32 rows above load 1.5, peak 1.24): typed Q8 mean 934.2 / median 915 ms (min 851, max 1099) vs llama2.c runq mean 633.2 / median 607 ms (min 564, max 742), so typed Q8 is about 1.5x behind runq (the earlier ~1.2-1.3x was a rough loaded estimate and is superseded). (4) Kernel sharing with tibia's Q8 network 'where shapes allow': tibia's network (src/network.spi) is fixed-point i32 (one () = 256) with 8 weighted inputs per hidden unit, smaller than one 64-wide Q8 group and not int8, so the shapes do not allow it; nothing to share. The 1.5x Q8 gap and the threaded matmul drafts (v5/v6) carry on under TRANSFORMER-CPU-SPEED.")
