inl planning_of_tibia_batched_evolution () = Planning ("TIBIA-BATCHED-EVOLUTION", Minutes 900u32, Load Heavy, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_tibia_batched_evolution_1 () = Note ("TIBIA-BATCHED-EVOLUTION", 1u32, "2026-11-02T13:44Z", "tibia", "11-02 tibia: TIBIA plan steps 2-3 (n56). Why: both TIBIA-AGENT failures were budget-limited (n47 CEM; n53: '8 generations x 64 offspring cannot silence' the 17 extra inputs). GPU sim: 0.23 ms per clear against 1.6 ms on C, ~4 s fixed setup per launch, worth it from about 4,000 clears per launch. The harness lives in the repo as modules and candidates, never as patched copies in target/. The rented-GPU part goes into GPU-STACKED-RUNS.")
inl note_of_tibia_batched_evolution_2 () = Note ("TIBIA-BATCHED-EVOLUTION", 2u32, "2026-11-02T17:40Z", "tibia", "11-02 tibia, DESIGN from the WGSL lane model (spiral_compiler.fs:21170-21206; sample apps/compiler/tmp/samples/wgsl_parallel_lanes reads the lane as $\"spiral_lane\" : u32). The shader runs spiral_main once per lane, chunked by 1024. Each lane writes ONE i32 to spiral_results[lane], and there is no host input buffer. So one generation per launch cannot pass the parent weights in, short of a runner input buffer (compiler/runner work). Chosen design, needing no runner change: MULTI-START EVOLUTION, ONE PER LANE. Lane L runs a full network.evolve (seed = base + L, the same 8 x 64 budget) on the training block and returns its final training fitness. The host picks the best lane, recomputes that lane's evolution on C (deterministic: keyed_rng plus integer-only, bit-exact C vs WGSL), then judges it on the held-out block by rule A5. 1024 lanes = 1024 x 512 = 524k fitness calls per launch, against the 512 of TIBIA-AGENT M1/M2e. Correctness first: lane 0 on C must equal lane 0 on WGSL (parity, as gpu_sim). Where it runs: the node has no WGSL runner (network_decides_on_gpu is Unsupported there), CI uses Mesa software Vulkan (CPU-speed, correctness only), and speed comes from GPU-STACKED-RUNS (L40S) or the box GPU. Next: the lane program as a tibia module (apps/predict or a new evolve app), a C parity test for one lane, then a lavapipe dry run to size the launch.")
inl note_of_tibia_batched_evolution_3 () = Note ("TIBIA-BATCHED-EVOLUTION", 3u32, "2026-11-02T18:19Z", "tibia", "11-02 tibia, SLICE 1 (unstaged). New apps/predict/lanes.spi: the PF1 objective is worth (CheapClearWithin 94934) on the arena-spot cyclops camp over the training block. Each lane runs network.evolve_by seeded 1 + lane, with plans full (8 x 64, 100 seeds) and tiny (1 x 2, 2 seeds); main prints lane scores on C. New apps/predict/lanes_gpu.spi: the WGSL entry, lane = $'spiral_lane' through raw !!!!BackendSwitch (lib can't yet, LIB-BACKEND-WGSL). Both are registered in apps/predict's package.spiproj and spiral.toml. src/network.spi gains a PROTOTYPE path, \":? judge f\" plus fitter_by/offspring_by/climb_by/evolve_by, because network.evolve passes fitness as a closure into join points, which WGSL cannot compile (closures unsupported). The instance \":+ judge pf1\" picks the fitness by type, so every join point is specialized and no closure remains; the C lane scores are bit-identical to the closure path. scripts/predict.ps1 fixes: src's own packageDir is now patched (broken since src gained packageDir in b08cd87), and -Module skips a module already listed. Results: C lanes 0-3 tiny = -1000121800, -1000138000, -1000600000, -1000600000 (node jobs tibia-lanes-c-4..7, stable across all four). WGSL: the shader compiles (3.16 MB, 59,803 lines), but the node runner crashes with 0xC0000005 after 16.8 s on its default backend. Probing vulkan/dx12 and 1 vs 4 lanes next.")
inl note_of_tibia_batched_evolution_4 () = Note ("TIBIA-BATCHED-EVOLUTION", 4u32, "2026-11-02T18:51Z", "tibia", "11-02 tibia, WGSL on the node's GPU (NVIDIA Quadro K1100M, Kepler, plus Intel HD 4600). The runner from the fresh-clone build (spiral-wgsl, wgpu 30) on lanes.wgsl (3.16 MB, 59,803 lines): Vulkan takes 35-41 s for the pipeline compile, then panics \"In Queue::write_buffer: Buffer with 'spiral-io' label is invalid\", i.e. the device was already lost during pipeline creation; the 1- and 4-lane runs are identical. DX12 hangs until the 30-min job timeout; the default backend crashed with 0xC0000005 at 16.8 s. CONCLUSION: this GPU/driver cannot host the shader. The node stays the C oracle box. Next for parity: software Vulkan (Mesa lavapipe, as tibia CI uses on Ubuntu) on the Vultr CPU node (one lane job at a time, check vultr-status first), then the timing run on the rented L40S (GPU-STACKED-RUNS). Note the 35-41 s pipeline compile per launch: the multi-start design (one launch, many lanes) pays it once.")
inl note_of_tibia_batched_evolution_5 () = Note ("TIBIA-BATCHED-EVOLUTION", 5u32, "2026-11-02T22:48Z", "tibia", "11-02 18:2x-18:4x tibia, WGSL LANE PARITY via lavapipe, node attempt (remote first): mesa-dist-win 26.2.5 lavapipe fetched to the node's C:\\home\\remote-stage\\mesa\\x64 (vulkan_lvp.dll + lvp_icd.x86_64.json) and a private runner copy with a LunarG vulkan-1.dll 1.4.363 (the node's system loader predates Vulkan 1.1: ash \"Unable to load get_physical_device_format_properties2\"). Blocked there: node jobs run elevated (High Mandatory Level, also under runas /trustlevel:0x20000), and the Vulkan loader ignores VK_DRIVER_FILES / VK_ICD_FILENAMES / VK_ADD_DRIVER_FILES in elevated processes, so only HKLM's nv-vk64.json is found and wgpu reports no llvmpipe adapter. CI's HKLM registration was NOT used (machine-wide adapter list change on the shared node). Route taken instead: vultr (Linux, lvp_icd.json already installed, AVAILABLE, load 0.26): the spiral tree's wgsl-run crate (main.rs f6d03358, Cargo.lock 0cff94c2, identical to the box) building into /root/lanes-lavapipe/target, shader lanes.wgsl (3,160,580 B, 59,803 lines, compiled on the node from apps/predict/lanes_gpu.spi) pushed to /root/lanes-lavapipe. Parity target: the C lanes 0-3 tiny plan scores -1000121800, -1000138000, -1000600000, -1000600000.")
inl note_of_tibia_batched_evolution_6 () = Note ("TIBIA-BATCHED-EVOLUTION", 6u32, "2026-11-02T23:09Z", "tibia", "11-02 19:1x tibia, WGSL LANE PARITY PROVEN (vultr, lavapipe): spiral-wgsl (spiral tree's wgsl-run, built into /root/lanes-lavapipe/target) on adapter \"llvmpipe (LLVM 21.1.8, 256 bits) backend Vulkan type Cpu, Mesa 26.0.8\", VK_DRIVER_FILES=lvp_icd.json, SPIRAL_WGSL_LANES=4, SPIRAL_WGSL_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=1800: lane 0..3 = -1000121800, -1000138000, -1000600000, -1000600000, EXACTLY the C lanes (tibia apps/predict/lanes.spi on C, re-confirmed today by predict-toml-1). So the same Spiral source (network.evolve_by over the judge prototype, typed lane plan) gives bit-identical scores on C and WGSL. Timing: pipeline compile 184,173 ms for the 59,803-line shader on 2 vCPU (the first try hit the runner's default 120 s completion limit), run 1,444 ms for 4 lanes. Next: lane throughput at 64+ lanes (sizes the GPU-STACKED-RUNS queue), then the full 8x64x100 plan.")
inl note_of_tibia_batched_evolution_7 () = Note ("TIBIA-BATCHED-EVOLUTION", 7u32, "2026-11-02T23:32Z", "tibia", "11-02 19:3x tibia, LANE SCALING on vultr lavapipe exposed WGSL-LANE-HEAP-OVERFLOW (filed, compiler lane told): lane results depend on the lane count whenever a lane outgrows its heap share (64 MB / chunk lanes by default). Correct runs: 4 lanes @16 MB/lane (run 1,444 ms) and 16 lanes @8 MB/lane with SPIRAL_WGSL_HEAP_MB=128 (run 3,727 ms, all of lanes 0-3 equal C). Wrong: 16 lanes @4 MB (lane 3), 64 lanes @1 MB (lanes 1-3). So the tiny plan needs more than 4 MB of bump heap per lane (no frees), and the full 8x64x100 plan will need far more: RULE until the fix lands, every lane run states heap MB per lane and re-checks lanes 0-3 against C before any result is used. Throughput (tiny plan, 2 vCPU llvmpipe, pipeline compile ~3 min excluded): ~4.3 lanes/s at 16 lanes. Sizing a rental needs per-lane heap of the full plan first (measure by bisecting SPIRAL_WGSL_HEAP_MB at 1 lane), then lanes per chunk = heap budget / per-lane need.")
inl note_of_tibia_batched_evolution_8 () = Note ("TIBIA-BATCHED-EVOLUTION", 8u32, "2026-11-03T02:10Z", "tibia", "11-02 22:1x tibia, CORRECTION to n7: with the compiler lane's overflow-checking runner and shader, the tiny-plan lane program overflows even at 8 MB per lane (16 lanes, SPIRAL_WGSL_HEAP_MB=128: 52,903 allocations overflowed), so the n6/n7 \"correct\" runs at 16 MB and 8 MB per lane matched C by luck, not by fit. Consequences: (1) every lane result taken before the fix lands is suspect unless its run reported no overflow; (2) the heap need must be measured with the new runner (bisect upward at 1-4 lanes) before sizing any GPU rental; (3) the real lever is fewer allocations: the evolution allocates fresh arrays per candidate with a bump allocator that never frees, so reusing buffers (or a per-lane arena reset between candidates) would shrink the need by orders of magnitude.")
inl note_of_tibia_batched_evolution_9 () = Note ("TIBIA-BATCHED-EVOLUTION", 9u32, "2026-11-03T02:36Z", "tibia", "11-02 22:3x tibia, ALLOCATION CUT in src/network.spi (unstaged, the n8 lever). Two per-decision arrays removed: (1) decide no longer builds the hidden array: outputs_from accumulates the three outputs in one pass over the hidden units (same integer terms, same ascending order, same / one () per output, so bit-exact by construction); (2) inputs is now a record (sensed: health, attackers, target, distance, heal, strike, flees; the bias slot is the constant one ()), built by inputs_of through flags_of/sensed_of, and hidden_unit sums the 8 terms directly (sensed_terms + flag_terms) instead of indexing an inputs array. Every decision in a hunt used to allocate 2 arrays (8 + 6 words); now none. C lanes 0-3 unchanged after each step (node jobs network-alloc-2 and network-alloc-3: -1000121800, -1000138000, -1000600000, -1000600000). The first two Cells runs stopped at the cell budget (outputs_from 413, inputs_of 375, hidden_unit 363); the tree version is split and checks 0 findings locally (mix spiral.cell_budget --check), kit standards 0. PENDING: network-alloc-4 (network + typed_network memo-off, including the bit-exact typed-vs-untyped test), then the WGSL lane heap need measured again with the overflow-checking runner.")
inl note_of_tibia_batched_evolution_10 () = Note ("TIBIA-BATCHED-EVOLUTION", 10u32, "2026-11-03T02:49Z", "tibia", "11-02 22:5x tibia: the allocation cut is PROVEN in Cells (node job network-alloc-4, memo-off, budget-clean tree): network 7/7 executed passed (incl. inputs_are_not_weights fails 'Unification failure', so the record-typed inputs keeps its type distinction; network_decides_on_gpu is wgsl unsupported only because that build env has no WGSL runner), typed_network 5/5 passed incl. typed_decide_matches_the_untyped_network_bit_for_bit (the typed path still goes through hidden_layer/output_unit, so the new allocation-free decide is checked against the old formula). C lanes 0-3 unchanged. Next: the lane shader recompiled with the candidate compiler and the per-lane heap need re-measured on vultr lavapipe with the overflow-checking runner.")
inl note_of_tibia_batched_evolution_11 () = Note ("TIBIA-BATCHED-EVOLUTION", 11u32, "2026-11-03T03:12Z", "tibia", "11-02 23:1x tibia, FIRST VERIFIED-CORRECT WGSL LANE RUN (vultr lavapipe, compiler lane's overflow-checking candidate + runner). Shader recompiled from the allocation-cut tree (59,741 lines, 8D22B3BD1276; an earlier recompile had silently used a stale src copy in target/predict/lanes-c, caught because its hash equalled the old shader's). 16 lanes: default 64 MB heap (4 MB/lane): exit 1, 618 allocations overflowed (was 384,978 before the cut, i.e. ~620x fewer). SPIRAL_WGSL_HEAP_MB=128 (8 MB/lane): NO overflow (the runner fails any overflowing run), lanes 0-3 = -1000121800, -1000138000, -1000600000, -1000600000, equal to C, run 3,272 ms for 16 lanes (was 52,903 overflowing allocations at the same setting before the cut). So the tiny plan now fits in 8 MB per lane with a proven fit, not by luck. Next: per-lane need of the full 8x64x100 plan (remaining allocations are per candidate: the weights copy per child), measured the same way before sizing a GPU rental.")
inl note_of_tibia_batched_evolution_12 () = Note ("TIBIA-BATCHED-EVOLUTION", 12u32, "2026-11-03T03:24Z", "tibia", "11-02 23:2x tibia, REMAINING LANE ALLOCATIONS found in the shader and cut (unstaged, proof running): after the network cut, the shader's spiral_array_create sites were 2 x sp_each_7 (foes_act's per-monster loop), 4 x sp_loop_5 (the hunt tick loop), 1 x offspring_by (66 words, per candidate) and main. The per-tick pairs are src/terrain.spi window_around: two arrays (passable, distance) of w*h for every weighted_step, i.e. per monster per tick, refcount-freed on C/Rust but never reclaimed by the WGSL bump heap. Fix as arena discipline on WGSL only: terrain.spi heap_mark () (Wgsl: $'spiral_heap_top' : u32; other backends 0u32) and heap_release mark (Wgsl: $'spiral_heap_top = !mark'; others ()), bracketing a new step_choice helper inside weighted_step. Sound because weighted_step returns a tile, so nothing allocated in the window escapes the release. Budget 0, kit standards 0. Expected: the lane heap need drops to the per-candidate weights (66 words per child). If it holds, heap_mark/heap_release belong in lib (a WGSL heap scope for scratch arrays) rather than in terrain.")
inl note_of_tibia_batched_evolution_13 () = Note ("TIBIA-BATCHED-EVOLUTION", 13u32, "2026-11-03T03:36Z", "tibia", "11-02 23:4x tibia, ARENA RESET PROVEN: 64 lanes at the DEFAULT heap (1 MB per lane, the setting that silently corrupted lanes 1-3 this evening and overflowed 384,978 allocations) now runs with NO overflow under the compiler lane's overflow-checking runner, and lanes 0-3 = -1000121800, -1000138000, -1000600000, -1000600000, equal to C (vultr lavapipe, shader A321E7128967 from the tree with the network cut + terrain heap_mark/heap_release; run 192,952 ms for 64 lanes at load ~2). C lanes unchanged (node job arena-reset-1). Per-lane need is now below 1 MB for the tiny plan; the remaining allocations are per candidate (66-word weights per child), so the full 8x64x100 plan should fit in a few MB per lane. Next: measure the full plan's lane heap the same way (1 lane, bisect HEAP_MB), then size the rental queue from lanes/s.")
inl note_of_tibia_batched_evolution_14 () = Note ("TIBIA-BATCHED-EVOLUTION", 14u32, "2026-11-03T04:05Z", "tibia", "11-03 00:0x tibia: src suite memo-off after both allocation cuts (node job src-suite-1, tibia-gates3 synced): terrain 17/17 executed passed, network 7/7, typed_network 5/5 (incl. the bit-exact typed-vs-untyped decide); 0 failures. The two unsupported rows are WGSL-target tests (terrain corridor_steps_on_gpu, network network_decides_on_gpu) in a build env with no WGSL runner, which is why the WGSL side was proven separately on vultr lavapipe (n13). Next: the full-plan lane measurement (lanes_gpu with full_plan, 2 lanes at the default heap, overflow-checking runner) is queued on vultr behind a quiet-load gate; it may finish after 2am.")
inl note_of_tibia_batched_evolution_15 () = Note ("TIBIA-BATCHED-EVOLUTION", 15u32, "2026-11-03T05:07Z", "tibia", "11-03 01:0x tibia. (1) FULL PLAN ON WGSL: lanes_gpu with full_plan (8 generations x 64 offspring x training seeds), compiled on the node with the overflow-checking compiler (3.1 MB, 59,750-line shader), run on vultr lavapipe with 2 lanes at the default 1 MB per lane: NO overflow report; lane 0 = -67400, lane 1 = -67600; run 1,789 s plus 155 s shader compile. Exit code 184 is lane 0 mod 256, not an error (memory recorded). The C reference for the same plan and lanes runs as the last step of node job chain2-1 (predict.ps1 -Module lanes with print_lanes full_plan 0..2), so parity is not claimed yet. (2) The arena reset moved to lib: new lib/spiral/heap_scope.spi (unstaged): heap_mark nominal, mark/release (WGSL reads and writes spiral_heap_top, other backends are no-ops), and scoped body, which is a compile-time error_type unless the body's result is a primitive (real_core::prim_type_is), since a released heap must not hold the result. src/terrain.spi's weighted_step now uses heap_scope::scoped; its local heap_mark/heap_release are removed. PROOFS so far: node lib gate (chain2-1): C/Rust 2/2 plus the fails test, type errors 0, mutant (7 -> 8) fails exactly scoped_returns_the_body_value; two cells over budget, since split (helper heap_returns), re-gate in chain3. GPU proof on vultr lavapipe: the probe main (heap_returns over a scoped and a bare allocation) returns 0 on both lanes; a copy of the shader with the release line deleted returns 1 on both lanes, so the test is non-vacuous. Pending: terrain 17-test gate with the switch (job offscreen-2), then a full-plan WGSL rerun with heap_scope.")
inl note_of_tibia_batched_evolution_16 () = Note ("TIBIA-BATCHED-EVOLUTION", 16u32, "2026-11-03T05:28Z", "tibia", "11-03 01:2x tibia, FULL-PLAN PARITY PROVEN: C and WGSL give identical lane scores on the real search plan (8 generations x 64 offspring x training seeds). C: the node-generated predict.c for lanes.spi (print_lanes full_plan 0..2), built on vultr with gcc -O2 -lm and run there: lane 0 = -67400, lane 1 = -67600, 438 s for both lanes in one process. WGSL (n15): lavapipe, 2 lanes at the default 1 MB per lane, no overflow: lane 0 = -67400, lane 1 = -67600, 1,789 s. So the WGSL lanes are verified-correct on the full plan, not only the tiny one, with the allocation cut (network.spi) plus the terrain arena reset. Speed on CPU-only lavapipe is 4x slower than one C core for 2 lanes; GPU-STACKED-RUNS needs a real GPU for the throughput number. The node chain2-1 C step was stopped after 9 min to free the node for gates; the same C program finished on idle vultr instead.")
inl note_of_tibia_batched_evolution_17 () = Note ("TIBIA-BATCHED-EVOLUTION", 17u32, "2026-11-03T05:31Z", "tibia", "11-03 01:2x tibia: lib/spiral/heap_scope.spi GATE GREEN (node job chain3-1, mirror tibia-gates3 synced with spiral digest E09311D04BB859EB, file 810C4B864D922197, registered after sized_array in package.spiproj): positive 2/2 (scoped_returns_the_body_value c+rust, scoped_rejects_a_heap_result fails test), type errors 0, cell budget 0 (after the split: a shorter error_type message and the helper heap_returns), mutant (7 -> 8) fails exactly scoped_returns_the_body_value. Its wgsl test is unsupported on the node (elevated jobs cannot load lavapipe) and proven separately on vultr lavapipe (n15: 0 on both lanes, 1 on both lanes with the release deleted). A redundant probe recompile hit a lock on another lane's C:\\home\\remote-stage\\package.spiproj (the compiler walks up from the probe folder), so the probe needs its own manifest folder next time; nothing was touched there.")
inl note_of_tibia_batched_evolution_18 () = Note ("TIBIA-BATCHED-EVOLUTION", 18u32, "2026-11-03T07:36Z", "tibia", "11-03 03:4x tibia: the lib move is CODEGEN-NEUTRAL on WGSL. Node job tibia-lanes-hs-1 recompiled lanes_gpu (full plan) from the current mirror, where src/terrain.spi's weighted_step uses lib heap_scope::scoped (checked: True), with the same overflow-checking compiler: the shader is byte-identical to n15's pre-move full-plan shader (both SHA256 ba6ca2b58f6dd50a4fa38b95b1e45860e194b75fae1e5d8cb15994bf8c97255c, 3,157,407 bytes, 59,750 lines). So the full-plan parity proof (n16: no overflow, lanes -67400/-67600 equal to C) holds for the lib version with no rerun; the 30-min lavapipe slot goes to the next item instead.")
inl note_of_tibia_batched_evolution_19 () = Note ("TIBIA-BATCHED-EVOLUTION", 19u32, "2026-11-03T12:32Z", "tibia", "11-03 08:3x tibia, C REFERENCE SCORES for lanes 2-9 on the FULL plan (8 generations x 64 offspring), for checking a future GPU run on more than lanes 0-1: apps/predict/lanes.spi from the current tree (terrain on lib heap_scope, typed arena changes) compiled with vultr's compiler, gcc -O2, one process on vultr, 1,806 s for 8 lanes. lane 2 = -68200, 3 = -66900, 4 = -67250, 5 = -72150, 6 = -66700, 7 = -67100, 8 = -67700, 9 = -69950. With n16's lanes 0 = -67400 and 1 = -67600 (C and WGSL equal), lanes 0-9 now have C references. Best of the ten: lane 6 (-66700); worst: lane 5 (-72150).")
inl note_of_tibia_batched_evolution_20 () = Note ("TIBIA-BATCHED-EVOLUTION", 20u32, "2026-11-03T14:43Z", "tibia", "11-03 10:4x tibia, FULL PLAN, 10 LANES, WGSL ON LAVAPIPE = C EXACTLY. vultr llvmpipe (LLVM 21.1.8, Mesa 26.0.8), overflow-checking runner /root/wgslheap-run, shader sha256 ba6ca2b58f6dd50a, SPIRAL_WGSL_LANES=10: compile 170,680 ms, run 6,659,490 ms (1 h 51 m on 2 vCPU software Vulkan). Lane scores 0-9: -67400 -67600 -68200 -66900 -67250 -72150 -66700 -67100 -67700 -69950, identical to the C full-plan reference for every lane (lanes 2-9 from the earlier 10-lane C job, lanes 0/1 as before). No overflow or error lines. Exit 184 = lane 0 score mod 256 (-67400 mod 256), the runner convention, not a failure. So the batched GPU path is bit-exact with C across 10 independent lanes on the full plan; what remains is speed on a real GPU (GPU-STACKED-RUNS, L40S plans still show no locations).")
inl note_of_tibia_batched_evolution_21 () = Note ("TIBIA-BATCHED-EVOLUTION", 21u32, "2026-11-04T04:17Z", "tibia", "11-04 00:17 tibia GROOM: parked to Planned (no note in 12 h). Correctness is done: WGSL on lavapipe is bit-exact with C across 10 lanes on the full plan. NEXT: speed on a real GPU through GPU-STACKED-RUNS (rental, $20 cap) once a plan shows locations.")
inl note_of_tibia_batched_evolution_22 () = Note ("TIBIA-BATCHED-EVOLUTION", 22u32, "2026-11-04T04:21Z", "tibia", "11-04 00:21 tibia: back to Active, percent kept. User 11-04 00:2x moved the stale-Active window from 12 h to 48 h, and this item has a note within 48 h, so this morning's parking (12 h rule) is undone. The next step in the 00:2x note still holds.")
inl note_of_tibia_batched_evolution_23 () = Note ("TIBIA-BATCHED-EVOLUTION", 23u32, "2026-11-04T15:18Z", "tibia", "11-04 11:18 tibia: (c) BATCHED SIMS ON C THREADS, CORRECTNESS PROVEN: a probe app lanes_par.spi (vultr stage tlanes3/w-par, tibia src with target2, landed lib) scores the 4 tiny-plan lanes with parallel::for_index 4 (\\ i => set scores i (lane_score p (conv i))) and prints them in order. Generated C: 1 omp parallel for, 38 atomic refcount ops. Output BYTE-IDENTICAL to the sequential lanes.spi build at OMP_NUM_THREADS 1 and 2 (lane scores -1000121800, -1000138000, -1000600000, -1000600000). So the hunting_pack sim, compiled to C, keeps no shared mutable state across lanes, and the atomic refcounts hold it together. Timing VOID (tiny plan runs 1-3 s; vultr 2 vCPU at load ~4). Next: the same shape on a real plan (offspring per thread in network::evolve_by) on a quiet multi-core machine.")
inl note_of_tibia_batched_evolution_24 () = Note ("TIBIA-BATCHED-EVOLUTION", 24u32, "2026-11-05T04:14Z", "tibia", "11-05 00:14 tibia: REAL-PLAN OFFSPRING PER THREAD, PROVEN + MEASURED. Probe apps/predict/evolve_par.spi (scratch; mid plan 2 generations x 32 offspring x 8 seeds, lane 0): scores each generation's children with parallel::for_index into an array, then folds them in child order with the same strict '>' rule as fitter_by, so the winner equals evolve_by's. vultr (its compiler, current lib digest via fresh lib.tgz, hunting_pack 6c28ec05c4e9, parallel beeeab18b7b7): output 'weights 4806 score -10850' BYTE-IDENTICAL to evolve_by at OMP 1 and 2; 1 omp pragma, 37 atomic refcount ops; seq 8.99 s, t1 9.09 s (atomics ~1%), t2 5.35 s. NODE (i7-4810MQ 4C/8T, gcc 15.2 -O2 -fopenmp on the same generated C, 3 interleaved rounds, node 20-50% busy with another lane): seq 19.8/23.8/25.7 s, t1 23.6/23.6/22.0, t2 15.0/14.2/13.2, t4 10.0/10.4/8.3, t8 8.2/7.5/6.4; all 15 outputs identical to seq. Median speedup vs seq: t2 1.7x, t4 2.4x, t8 3.2x (on 4 physical cores under foreign load). NEXT: evolve_par_by in src/network.spi (drafted, see COMPILER-EMIT-PARALLEL-LOOPS), then lanes.spi's full plan through it.")
inl note_of_tibia_batched_evolution_25 () = Note ("TIBIA-BATCHED-EVOLUTION", 25u32, "2026-11-05T06:35Z", "tibia", "11-05 02:35 tibia: FULL-PLAN PARITY OF THE PARALLEL CLIMB. In node job tibia-cl1-cem-lanes, evolve_par_by (offspring per OpenMP thread, 8 threads) on lanes.spi's full plan (8 x 64 x 100 seeds) gave training fitness -67400, -67600, -68200, -66900 for lanes 0-3, IDENTICAL to the sequential C references (11-03 n: lanes 0/1 -67400/-67600 = WGSL on lavapipe; lanes 2/3 -68200/-66900). So threaded scoring is bit-exact at full scale, not only on the 2 x 32 x 8 probe. Held-out (seeds 1001-1100) for the same weights: -67350, -67700, -82950, -81250; lanes 2 and 3 overfit by about 14k.")
inl note_of_tibia_batched_evolution_26 () = Note ("TIBIA-BATCHED-EVOLUTION", 26u32, "2026-11-05T09:31Z", "tibia", "11-05 05:31 tibia: BUDGET x2 OVERFITS (node job tibia-cl3-budget-lanes, 3093 s, OMP 8; live network.spi 3c8136221511; evolve_par_by; the same PF1 objective and 100 training seeds as cl1; plan 8 generations x 128 offspring vs cl1's 8 x 64). Lanes 0-3, train 64 -> 128: -67400 -> -64750, -67600 -> -66900, -68200 -> -60100, -66900 -> -66900. Held-out (seeds 1001-1100), 64 -> 128: -67350 -> -77000, -67700 -> -81050, -82950 -> -1,000,100,292, -81250 -> -80900. So twice the search improves training fit in 3 of 4 lanes and makes held-out WORSE in 3 of 4; lane 2's best training weights FAIL a held-out clear (the -1e9 penalty term). CONSEQUENCE for this item's '100x the 512-evaluation budget' goal: budget alone is not the lever. More search on 100 fixed training seeds overfits them. The batched path needs (a) selection or early stopping on a VALIDATION block (score the kept best on held-out-like seeds each generation and keep the best by validation), and/or (b) more training seeds per evaluation (the cost is linear, which threads now pay for). Next probe: the same x2 budget with kept_best chosen by a validation block of 100 seeds distinct from training and held-out.")
inl note_of_tibia_batched_evolution_27 () = Note ("TIBIA-BATCHED-EVOLUTION", 27u32, "2026-11-05T11:31Z", "tibia", "11-05 07:31 tibia: VALIDATION SELECTION FIXES THE x2-BUDGET OVERFIT (node job tibia-cl4-val-lanes, 2982 s, OMP 8; network 3c8136221511; probe val_lanes: the same 8 x 128 climb via scores_by/best_scored, but kept_best scores each generation's parent on a VALIDATION block, seeds 2001-2100, disjoint from training 1-100 and held-out 1001-1100). Lanes 0-3, train/val/held: -64750/-62450/-77000, -67300/-67750/-67650, -65000/-67250/-64450, -67450/-68150/-81750. Held-out next to tonight's other runs: x1 climb (cl1) -67350, -67700, -82950, -81250 (median -74,475); x2 climb (cl3) -77000, -81050, -1,000,100,292, -80900; x2 + validation -77000, -67650, -64450, -81750 (median -72,325, the best of every variant tonight). It removes lane 2's catastrophic held-out failure (-1e9 -> -64,450, lane 2's best held-out of the night) and restores lane 1. Lane 0 shows val and held disagreeing (-62k vs -77k), so 100 validation seeds is still a noisy selector. NEXT: (1) make selection-by-validation a network.spi option (evolve_by with a second judge for validation), gated like evolve_par_by; (2) the box is running the other lever, twice the TRAINING seeds (8 x 64 x 200, run cl5), for comparison.")
inl note_of_tibia_batched_evolution_28 () = Note ("TIBIA-BATCHED-EVOLUTION", 28u32, "2026-11-05T12:09Z", "tibia", "11-05 08:09 tibia: VALIDATION SELECTION LANDED in live tibia/src/network.spi (3c8136221511 -> 4c3abc8069dc, unstaged): evolve_selected_by j v s start runs the threaded climb (scores_by/best_scored) and keeps the generation parent with the best score under a second fitness v (a weights -> i32 closure, so C/Rust paths only); first_round, selected_step and vetted are its parts, and kept_best keeps the earlier one on ties. Tests (c, rust): training_selection_keeps_the_climbs_score (v = the training judge gives evolve_by's score) and a_flat_validator_keeps_the_start (v = constant keeps the start weights). PROOF, node gate tibia-np4-gate-1 (tibia build.ps1 -Files network on the mirror, swapped and restored to 707E7157DC1E): layout and budget clean, 14 tests: all new tests green on c and rust; the only red is the unchanged network_decides_on_gpu wgsl, the node's spiral-wgsl crashing 0xC0000005 (green in the mutant arm, the same flake as gates 2 and 3). Mutant (selected_step always takes the newest parent) fails exactly a_flat_validator_keeps_the_start (actual -29232, expected 0). Use: lanes.spi can now call evolve_selected_by (pf1 p) (fitness_from 2001u32 p) for the validation-selected climb that beat every variant on held-out tonight (cl4: median -72,325).")
inl note_of_tibia_batched_evolution_29 () = Note ("TIBIA-BATCHED-EVOLUTION", 29u32, "2026-11-05T12:20Z", "tibia", "11-05 08:20 tibia: MORE TRAINING SEEDS IS THE STRONGER LEVER (run cl5 on the BOX, the box idle per the gate; 7095 s at OMP 4, Normal priority; live network 3c8136221511; the climb via evolve_par_by at 8 x 64 but with 200 training seeds (1-200) instead of 100: the same evaluation cost as cl3's x2 offspring). Lanes 0-3, fitness on training seeds 1-100 / held-out 1001-1100: -67400/-67350, -67950/-67850, -67300/-67450, -68850/-94500. Held-out MEDIANS across tonight's variants at the same or lower cost: x1 climb -74,475; x2 offspring about -81k with a -1e9 failure; x2 offspring + validation selection -72,325; x2 TRAINING SEEDS -67,650 (best). 3 of 4 lanes sit near -67.5k with held matching train, so the generalization gap closes (lane 2's 15k overfit is gone: -67,450 vs -82,950), and lane 3 is still weak (-94,500). Reading: the objective overfits through its seed sample, so budget should go first to seeds per evaluation (threads pay for it linearly) and then to validation selection. Running now on the node: x4 offspring with validation selection (cl6). Next candidate: seeds x2 together with validation selection.")
inl note_of_tibia_batched_evolution_30 () = Note ("TIBIA-BATCHED-EVOLUTION", 30u32, "2026-11-05T14:21Z", "tibia", "11-05 10:21 tibia: x4 OFFSPRING OVERFITS EVEN WITH VALIDATION SELECTION (node job tibia-cl6-sel4-lanes, 7388 s, OMP 8; live network 4c3abc8069dc; evolve_selected_by (pf1 p) (fitness on validation seeds 2001-2100), plan 8 x 256 x 100 training seeds). Lanes 0-3 train/val/held: -67400/-68100/-81750, -63700/-64400/-1,000,096,151, -66000/-65700/-80400, -67550/-68150/-82200. Lane 1 FAILS a held-out clear although its VALIDATION score was excellent (-64,400): with 4x the candidates, selection overfits the 100-seed validation block too. Held-out medians across tonight's runs: x1 -74,475; x2 offspring about -81k (a -1e9 failure); x2 + validation -72,325; x4 + validation about -82k (a -1e9 failure); x2 TRAINING SEEDS -67,650. CONCLUSION for this item's budget goal: on this objective, extra OFFSPRING buys overfitting and extra SEEDS buy generalization. The 100x-evaluations goal should be spent as seeds per candidate (and more validation seeds), not as a wider search over 100 seeds. cl7 (x2 seeds + validation) is queued to confirm the two levers stack.")
inl note_of_tibia_batched_evolution_31 () = Note ("TIBIA-BATCHED-EVOLUTION", 31u32, "2026-11-05T15:27Z", "tibia", "11-05 11:27 tibia: SEEDS x2 + VALIDATION (node job tibia-cl7-seedsel-lanes, 3912 s, OMP 8, the node's pack48 compiler; live network 4c3abc8069dc; evolve_selected_by with 200 training seeds, 8 x 64, validation on seeds 2001-2100; all reported scores on 100-seed blocks). Lanes 0-3 train(1-100)/val/held: -68000/-68050/-81450, -67950/-68250/-67850, -67300/-68100/-67450, -70950/-71400/-70800. Next to seeds x2 without validation (cl5, held -67350, -67850, -67450, -94500): lanes 1 and 2 are IDENTICAL (validation kept the climb's final parent); lane 3's weak result is repaired (-94.5k -> -70.8k); lane 0 gets worse (-67.4k -> -81.5k), where the 100-seed validation block picked a parent that generalizes worse. Summary: median -69,325 vs -67,650, mean -71,888 vs -74,288, worst -81,450 vs -94,500. So validation buys robustness (mean and worst case), not the median. With 100 validation seeds it is about as noisy as the overfit it guards against. The robust recipe so far: spend budget on training seeds first, and give validation as many seeds as training. Queued: seeds x4 without validation (cl8).")
inl note_of_tibia_batched_evolution_32 () = Note ("TIBIA-BATCHED-EVOLUTION", 32u32, "2026-11-05T17:16Z", "tibia", "11-05 13:16 tibia: SEEDS x4 IS THE ROBUST RECIPE (node job tibia-cl8-seeds4-lanes, 6315 s, OMP 8; live network 4c3abc8069dc; plain evolve_par_by climb, 8 x 64, 400 training seeds 1-400; scores on the 100-seed blocks). Lanes 0-3 train(1-100)/held(1001-1100): -71950/-71900, -67950/-67850, -67450/-68000, -67950/-68350. Train and held agree in every lane (gap 50-550), so there is no overfit. TONIGHT'S TABLE, held-out median / mean / worst lane: x1 climb -74,475 / -74,813 / -82,950; x2 offspring about -81k with a -1e9 failure; x4 offspring + validation about -82k with a -1e9 failure; x2 offspring + validation -72,325 / -72,712 / -81,750; x2 seeds -67,650 / -74,288 / -94,500; x2 seeds + validation -69,325 / -71,888 / -81,450; x4 SEEDS -68,175 / -69,025 / -71,900 (the best mean and worst case, and every lane within 4.1k). DECISION for the batched/GPU budget: spend evaluations on SEEDS PER CANDIDATE (400 and up), not on offspring. The 100x path is then candidates x seeds lanes on the GPU, with offspring kept at 64. evolve_par_by makes x4 seeds about 1.4x the wall time of the old x1 run on the node (6315 s vs about 4600 s for x1's 4 lanes at OMP 8). Recommend adding a robust_plan (8 x 64 x 400) to apps/predict/lanes.spi beside full_plan, leaving full_plan alone so the C/WGSL parity references stay valid.")
inl note_of_tibia_batched_evolution_33 () = Note ("TIBIA-BATCHED-EVOLUTION", 33u32, "2026-11-05T19:23Z", "tibia", "11-05 15:23 tibia: SEEDS x4 CONFIRMED ON 8 LANES (node job tibia-cl9-seeds-wide, 7212 s, OMP 8; network 4c3abc8069dc; lanes 4-7, x1 = 100 training seeds and x4 = 400, both 8 x 64; scores on 100-seed blocks). PARITY: x1 training for lanes 4-7 = -67250, -72150, -66700, -67100, IDENTICAL to the 11-03 sequential C full-plan references, so the threaded climb is bit-exact on 8 of 8 lanes. Held-out: x1 -67050, -114200, -95000, -67250 (lanes 5 and 6 overfit badly); x4 -69150, -68650, -67900, -68350. ALL 8 LANES (cl1+cl8+cl9), held-out median / mean / worst: x1 -74,475 / -80,344 / -114,200; x4 seeds -68,350 / -68,778 / -71,900. x4 seeds removes the overfit tail (worst lane 42k better) and keeps every lane within 4k of the best, paid for with 4x evaluations, which offspring-per-thread makes about 1.4x the old wall time on the node. This settles the budget question for this objective: evaluations go to seeds per candidate first.")
inl note_of_tibia_batched_evolution_34 () = Note ("TIBIA-BATCHED-EVOLUTION", 34u32, "2026-11-05T23:26Z", "tibia", "11-05 19:26 tibia: SEEDS x8 = x4, THE CURVE IS FLAT (node job tibia-cl10-seeds8-lanes, 14,283 s with two of my gates sharing the node; network 4c3abc8069dc; 8 x 64 with 800 training seeds 1-800). Lanes 0-3 train(1-100)/held: -70400/-70600, -67950/-67850, -68900/-69700, -67950/-68350. Next to x4 (cl8, held -71900, -67850, -68000, -68350): held mean -69,125 vs -69,025, lanes within 1.7k either way. So generalization saturates at about 400 training seeds for this objective at 8 x 64; x4 (robust_plan, now in lanes.spi) is the cost-effective point, and further budget should go elsewhere (more generations, more lanes/restarts, or a richer network), not more seeds.")
inl iggy_rustdesk_node () = Task ("IGGY-RUSTDESK-NODE", "Move iggy from kit-vultr-sjc-1 onto the rustdesk CPU node rustdesk-sao-1 (Vultr 978f050b, vc2-1c-1gb, sao, 216.128.173.116), user 11-02 via tibia: \"move iggy to the rustdesk node after we get all the infrastructure scripts right\"; gated: linux-worker.ps1 lean mode proven, lane key authorized on the node (user), IGGY-PUBLIC-CLOUD TLS listener decision (user); RustDesk must never degrade", Story, P1, D4, 100u32, Done, "IGGY-PUBLIC-CLOUD,K3S-EVERYWHERE", "team:x-dumps,x-dumps,iggy,k3s,remote,user-batch", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_iggy_rustdesk_node () = Planning ("IGGY-RUSTDESK-NODE", Minutes 240u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_iggy_rustdesk_node_1 () = Note ("IGGY-RUSTDESK-NODE", 1u32, "2026-11-02T13:51Z", "x-dumps", "Filed 11-02 by x-dumps (owner; iggy was provisioned on vultr by linux-worker.ps1). Facts 09:4x: the node answers on 22 and RustDesk 21115/21116/21117, so it is the user's RustDesk server. 1 vCPU, 1 GB RAM, Ubuntu 26.10, created 10-24. Labeled rustdesk-sao-1 (label only). Our key (vultr_ed25519) is NOT authorized there: one BatchMode attempt got \"Permission denied (publickey)\". USER STEP: add the lane public key ~/.ssh/vultr_ed25519.pub to root's authorized_keys on rustdesk-sao-1 (or a sudo user). RISK: 1 GB is tight. k3s measured ~500-660 MB idle on vultr, plus iggy ~83 MB, plus RustDesk. Plan: (1) linux-worker.ps1 -Lean: k3s --disable metrics-server and the rest, kubelet memory reservations, a swapfile if absent, iggy limit 256Mi; a RustDesk guard (ports answer before and after, abort and roll back if MemAvailable < 200 MB or a RustDesk port stops answering). (2) Baseline the node's memory/CPU before touching it. (3) iggy goes public on that node with TLS (IGGY-PUBLIC-CLOUD (1)/(2)), since xarchive in sjc then reaches it cross-region (~170 ms, fine for events). (4) Recreate stream x-dumps/harvest + scoped user there (events are transient, no data migration), repoint secret x-dumps/iggy-xarchive, verify count parity again. (5) Keep vultr iggy as fallback 24 h, then remove it (frees ~83 MB on vultr). FALLBACK options for the user if lean k3s does not fit: B) resize to vc2-1c-2gb (their money, needs their yes); C) iggy without k3s on that node.")
inl note_of_iggy_rustdesk_node_2 () = Note ("IGGY-RUSTDESK-NODE", 2u32, "2026-11-02T15:04Z", "x-dumps", "Lane access achieved 11-02: the user added our key AND transferred their root key; ssh rustdesk works on the lane key; node labeled rustdesk-sao-1. Memory + RustDesk safety PROVEN before any move: a systemd-run MemoryMax=450M/MemorySwapMax=0 xarchive worker peaked ~5MB (i574n run) / 28MB (empty), RustDesk ports 21115-21117 up throughout, free 577->575MB. Node has Docker (running RustDesk) but NO k3s; DECIDED not to install k3s here (idle ~400-660MB would starve the 948MB box) - reuse systemd-run/docker with a hard mem cap. Gates to actually move iggy unchanged: IGGY-PUBLIC-CLOUD TLS listener (user) + lean setup proven + RustDesk never degrades.")
inl note_of_iggy_rustdesk_node_3 () = Note ("IGGY-RUSTDESK-NODE", 3u32, "2026-11-03T07:26Z", "tibia", "tibia 11-03 03:2x DECISION BY MEASUREMENT (main asked tibia to settle it before anything moves): DO NOT MOVE iggy to rustdesk-sao-1 now; it stays on vultr. RULE recorded first: a co-tenant may join the RustDesk node only if MemAvailable after it, with every capped worker at its cap, stays >= 200 MB (x-dumps' own abort line) and RustDesk ports stay up. MEASURED read-only 07:25Z: rustdesk-sao-1 948 MB total, MemAvailable 372 MB, swap 2.3 GB (51 MB used), load 0.04 on 1 vCPU; RustDesk itself is tiny (hbbs 0.8 MB, hbbr 1.0 MB RSS, 5 ports listening); the big tenant is x-dumps' xarchive archive run (geygey_hadid, 4 shards, up 9.7 h) at 254 MB RSS under its MemoryMax=450M cap (11-02 it measured 5-28 MB). iggy-server on vultr: 95 MB RSS, 0.7% CPU, up 9.9 h. ARITHMETIC: now 372 - 95 = 277 MB (77 MB over the line); with xarchive at its 450 MB cap (+196 MB) it would be about 81 MB, far below the line, with RustDesk on a swapping box. Also the public iggy endpoint 140.82.49.44 IS vultr (hostname -I), now handed to per-machine users; moving it changes the address for ~95 MB that vultr does not need. REVISIT when (a) vultr memory becomes the constraint, or (b) the node is resized (user money), or (c) xarchive moves off the node. Nothing was changed on either node.")
inl note_of_iggy_rustdesk_node_4 () = Note ("IGGY-RUSTDESK-NODE", 4u32, "2026-11-03T09:47Z", "x-dumps", "RE-DECIDE 11-03 (gate surfaced this Paused P1): KEEP PAUSED. REASON: rustdesk-sao-1 is a 1-core/948MB box whose RustDesk (hbbs/hbbr) MUST NEVER DEGRADE, and it is currently maxed running the active xarchive-sao geygey harvest worker (~593MB). Adding iggy there now would risk RustDesk and compete with the harvest. Also the original rationale (consolidate iggy off vultr) is now weak: iggy is already LIVE + PUBLIC on vultr with TLS and 3 least-priv lane users (IGGY-PUBLIC-CLOUD), so there is no pull to move it. RESUME CONDITION: re-decide only after the geygey harvest completes (sao frees up) AND there is a concrete reason to move it (e.g. vultr needs the resources) AND iggy memory fits sao without degrading RustDesk; otherwise CLOSE as obsoleted by IGGY-PUBLIC-CLOUD.")
inl note_of_iggy_rustdesk_node_5 () = Note ("IGGY-RUSTDESK-NODE", 5u32, "2026-11-04T05:32Z", "x-dumps", "CLOSED as obsoleted 2026-11-04 (gate re-decide; resolves my 11-03 resume condition). The trigger \"geygey harvest completes / sao frees up\" is now TRUE, but the condition also required a CONCRETE reason to move iggy AND fit-without-degrading-RustDesk. Neither holds: iggy is live + public on vultr (140.82.49.44) with TLS and least-priv per-machine users (IGGY-PUBLIC-CLOUD shipped), so there is no pull to move it, and moving it would change the public endpoint address handed to those users for zero benefit. tibia 11-03 measured the move would drop sao MemAvailable far below the 200MB RustDesk-safety line. Per my own stated \"otherwise CLOSE as obsoleted by IGGY-PUBLIC-CLOUD\" branch -> Done. If a concrete reason to move iggy ever arises (e.g. vultr must shed load, or sao is resized), file a fresh task.")
inl cells_typed_units () = Task ("CELLS-TYPED-UNITS", "Cells on typed-units/1: split apps/cells/spi (8 files, 2,334 lines) into link: units so no unit generates more than 1000 lines. Today the domain is ONE generated module, src/spiral_cells/domain.gleam, at 18,622 lines (1.05 MB). Units: cells_model (types only: text, document, directives, cell, targets, the daemon protocol GADT), cells_boundary (the only home of host code: Erlang/Kino/OS externals, today text.spi's 5 host macros), cells_feature (notebook, memo, runner, mounts, daemon/pool processes), cells_beam (Gleam bin: the BEAM runtime and Kino facades) and cells_cli (Rust bin: the C11 CLIs). Exports are monomorphic: request r stays inside cells_model, and the boundary exports one concrete function per reply type or an existential. Retire the single domain.gleam and never use x's post-hoc text splitter. DoD: every unit at most 1000 generated lines, the typefeatureset census never down from the 11-02 baseline, and the Cells suite equal before and after", Story, P1, D5, 0u32, Planned, "COMPILER-LINKED-UNITS,LINKED-UNITS-GLEAM-RECORDS", "team:x,cells,cells-in-spiral,linked-units,typefeatureset,line-gate", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_cells_typed_units () = Planning ("CELLS-TYPED-UNITS", Minutes 300u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_cells_typed_units_1 () = Note ("CELLS-TYPED-UNITS", 1u32, "2026-11-02T13:58Z", "x", "2026-11-02 09:58 x: added by the 10-min groom (user: check that Cells modeling nails the typefeatureset, the Gleam/Rust/iggy balance, and the new 1k split model). BASELINE census of apps/cells/spi at 2026-11-02 09:58: unions 29, GADT cases 10 (daemon 6, cell 3, directives 1), records 7, forall 4, existentials 0, nominal/phantom 0, prototypes 0. Tab-wire sites 32 (daemon codec 17, document 8; expected until the Elixir callers go), 0/1 flags 14, host macros 5 (text.spi).")
inl note_of_cells_typed_units_2 () = Note ("CELLS-TYPED-UNITS", 2u32, "2026-11-02T15:02Z", "x", "2026-11-02 11:02 x: STAR-EXCHANGE GROOM. From compiler n13 and kit n8: pack16 writes <stem>.unit.json beside each exporting .spi; an importer of an unbuilt link: unit fails \"build X first\"; and staged regen must carry .unit.json back with the .rs. So build units in link: order, and the Cells build carries unit.json. Per the user's no-outputs-in-repo rule (memory derive-baselines-not-stored), unit.json belongs in a build dir; a gitignore is only the interim. x asked compiler about the location in its star-exchange message.")
inl lib_type_shapes () = Task ("LIB-TYPE-SHAPES", "lib: type-level shapes for every backend: type_arith (add/sub/mult/div/divup over type literals, plus nominal_recreate normalization), length-indexed static arrays sa n el (scan, tree reduce typed sa (divup n 2), merge : sa a -> sa b -> sa (add a b)) and tuple_list n el, backend-neutral (C/Rust/WGSL/Lua arms), with tlit for literal configs and a dim_of shape witness", Story, P1, D6, 100u32, Done, "", "lib,typefeatureset,transformer,tibia,team:tibia", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_lib_type_shapes () = Planning ("LIB-TYPE-SHAPES", Minutes 600u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_lib_type_shapes_1 () = Note ("LIB-TYPE-SHAPES", 1u32, "2026-11-02T14:03Z", "tibia", "Source: ML-library nugget inventory, %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\reports\\typed-transformer-20261102\\ml-library-nuggets.md sections 2 (type_arith.spir:3-7, sam.spi:51-118, tuple_listm.spi, core.spi tlit:128-135) and 1.1 (dim_of, layers.spi:56-58). type_arith is in fork core (cc1b12ac), and both TypeLitToLit ops are in our compiler. sam/dam/sa_listm/tuple_listm were moved out of core because they have no C/Rust/Zig arm; adding those arms is this item. Fix the inverted bound in tuple_listm.index (i <= 0 should be 0 <= i). One sa with a placement union (Stack | Heap) replaces dam. Bounded sa_list without unsafe_set_length.")
inl note_of_lib_type_shapes_2 () = Note ("LIB-TYPE-SHAPES", 2u32, "2026-11-02T14:44Z", "regex", "regex 10:2x LINK from the regex lane: LIB-VEC-FIN (bounded Fin index + three legacy brzozowski probes) now builds on this item's naturals and sized arrays; REGEX-TYPED-CORES consumes both (static DFA rows as sa n target, targets as Fin n). One shape core for regex, tibia and the transformer, as tibia proposed.")
inl note_of_lib_type_shapes_3 () = Note ("LIB-TYPE-SHAPES", 3u32, "2026-11-02T16:34Z", "tibia", "11-02 tibia, PROBE 1 on the node (job shape-probe-3; draft at %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\spiral\\LIB-TYPE-SHAPES\\probe1). Edition-3 syntax: \":! sized n el = array el\"; the size is read back with \"~> real_core::type_lit_to_lit `n\" (a ~> block, as reflection.spi does; a bare backtick outside ~> fails to parse); types are written \"sized (type_arith::add 2 3) i32\". Results on C: lengths from add 2 3 and mult 2 4 are 5 and 8 at compile time and the physical arrays match (exit 5858, 1,978 bytes of C). Assigning a mult-2-4 value to an add-2-3 slot fails with \"Unification failure. Got sized (mult 2i32 4i32) i32, Expected sized (add 2i32 3i32) i32\". OPEN, next probe: the error shows the types unnormalized, so does sized (add 2 2) unify with sized 4 or sized (mult 2 2)? The ML library normalizes with nominal_recreate (real_core.spir:242-243); if unification compares unnormalized forms, the lib needs a normalize step at constructors. Also next: Rust, WGSL and Lua arms, then merge : sized a el -> sized b el -> sized (add a b) el.")
inl note_of_lib_type_shapes_4 () = Note ("LIB-TYPE-SHAPES", 4u32, "2026-11-02T23:45Z", "tibia", "11-02 19:4x tibia, PROBE 2 PASSES both ways (node job shape-probe2-1, installed compiler; draft %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\spiral\\LIB-TYPE-SHAPES\\probe2): answers probe 1's open question without a compiler change. Shape-changing functions stay symbolic: merged \\* a b el. (sized a el) (sized b el) : sized (type_arith::add a b) el. A checked relabel makes a symbolic size usable where a literal one is declared: resized \\* m n el. (sized n el) : sized m el folds both sizes at compile time (~> real_core::type_lit_to_lit) and branches statically: equal -> sized x, different -> core's error_type \"resized: the sizes differ\". POSITIVE: v : sized 2, w : sized 3, u : sized 5 = resized (merged v w): count 5, physical length 5, total 17 (exit 567 as predicted). NEGATIVE: u : sized 6 = resized (merged v w) fails at COMPILE time with TracedError \"resized: the sizes differ\" and a trace down to the resized call site. So the lib shape: sized n el with symbolic arithmetic in signatures, plus resized as the one normalization point (the role nominal_recreate plays in the ML library), and the sound in-compiler normalization stays COMPILER-TYPE-ARITH-NORMALIZE. Next: draft lib module (name must not shadow a core type), with merge/split/map/fold and the C + Rust test arms, then WGSL.")
inl note_of_lib_type_shapes_5 () = Note ("LIB-TYPE-SHAPES", 5u32, "2026-11-03T00:26Z", "tibia", "11-02 20:2x tibia, FIRST SLICE LANDED green: new lib module lib/spiral/sized_array.spi (04326DA5288A) registered after array_dim in package.spiproj (9A65F56D4191 -> D17DC8925595); label lib-sized-array, undo spiral-bin\\lib-land-undo\\20261102-201711. API: :! sized n el = array el; size_of (compile-time literal); init (n from the annotation); item; merge : sized a el -> sized b el -> sized (type_arith::add a b) el; resized (checked relabel, compile-time error_type \"resized: the sizes differ\" on mismatch, the probe-2 design); fold; map. Tests (c + rust): merge_then_resized_keeps_order_and_size, fold_sums_a_merged_array (63), map_keeps_the_size, and resized_rejects_a_different_size fails \"resized: the sizes differ\" (c). Node gate (mirror tibia-gates3, job lib-sized-gate-2): positive 4/4, type errors 0, cell budget 0 findings, mutant (30 -> 31) fails exactly the mutated test. Closes: 0 consumer-used signatures changed, apps/spiral compiles, document_test 22 passed, type_probe PASS (3 typed, the fails test left to Cells). NEXT: split/take (the inverse of merge), a closure-free WGSL arm (init takes a closure, which WGSL lacks), then the transformer's layer buffers as sized arrays.")
inl note_of_lib_type_shapes_6 () = Note ("LIB-TYPE-SHAPES", 6u32, "2026-11-03T00:36Z", "tibia", "11-02 20:3x tibia, PROBE 3 (node, installed compiler, landed lib sized_array; draft %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\spiral\\LIB-TYPE-SHAPES\\probe3): a typed matmul_sized \\* n d. (o : sized d f32) (x : sized n f32) (sized (type_arith::mult d n) f32) compiles to C loops with CONSTANT trip counts: dot2's exit test is `v3 >= 4l`, rows1's is `v3 < 3l`, and the size parameters are gone from both signatures (exit 60, correct). Technique: pass the sized VALUES into the join points (a join point's key includes its argument types, so sized n f32 carries n) and fold the size inside with `:= n' : i32 = ~> real_core::type_lit_to_lit `n`. What does NOT work: passing the sizes as plain literal arguments (they are made dynamic: rows1(..., int32_t n, int32_t d, ...)), and explicit type application `f `n x` (a parse error outside ~>). This is the codegen lever for the specialized transformer build (TRANSFORMER-CPU-REFERENCE n8) and the precondition for unrolled/vectorized C (COMPILER-SIMD-CODEGEN). Candidate lib addition: a sized matmul plus a dot over sized vectors.")
inl note_of_lib_type_shapes_7 () = Note ("LIB-TYPE-SHAPES", 7u32, "2026-11-03T01:50Z", "tibia", "11-02 21:4x tibia, SLICE 2 LANDED green: lib/spiral/sized_array.spi 04326DA5288A -> 32E77DCEBAC6 (label lib-sized-array-2, undo spiral-bin\\lib-land-undo\\20261102-214112). Added: take / skip (inverses of merge over sized (type_arith::add a b) el; a literal-sized array splits after a checked resized into the add form), and typed f32 kernels dot_at / dot / rows_from / matmul \\* n d. (sized d f32) (sized n f32) (sized (type_arith::mult d n) f32), which carry the sizes as types so the generated C loops have constant trip counts (probe 3). New tests: take_and_skip_invert_merge, a_literal_size_splits_after_a_checked_relabel, matmul_dots_each_row, dot_sums_the_products (c + rust). Node gate 5 (tibia-gates3): positive 8/8, type errors 0, cell budget 0, mutant (60 -> 61) fails exactly the mutated test. Closes: 0 consumer-used signatures changed, apps/spiral compiles, document_test 22 passed, type_probe PASS (7 typed). Measured meaning (TRANSFORMER-CPU-SPEED n5): constant shapes give ~11% on the transformer at -Ofast, ~0 at -O3. DEAD END recorded: real_core::lit_to_type_lit is real-segment only (\".<real>\", usable inside ~> blocks), so a value cannot be lifted into a len witness in ordinary code; data-driven shape dispatch needs the shapes as type literals in the source (or a compile-time ~> loop).")
inl note_of_lib_type_shapes_8 () = Note ("LIB-TYPE-SHAPES", 8u32, "2026-11-03T02:16Z", "tibia", "11-02 22:1x tibia, SLICE 3 LANDED green: lib/spiral/sized_array.spi 32E77DCEBAC6 -> A2CAE3823D14 (label lib-sized-array-3, undo spiral-bin\\lib-land-undo\\20261102-220756). Added filled \\* n el. (value : el) : sized n el, a closure-free constructor (init takes a closure, which the WGSL backend lacks), so sized arrays and the typed matmul/dot can be built where closures are unavailable. New test filled_matmul_needs_no_closures (c + rust): 4 ones x a 3x4 of twos gives 8 per row. Node gate 6: positive 9/9, type errors 0, budget 0, mutant (8 -> 9) fails exactly that test. A WGSL test is deferred until a gate environment has a WGSL runner (the node's elevated jobs cannot load lavapipe; NODE-VULKAN memory), so no unverifiable test was added.")
inl note_of_lib_type_shapes_9 () = Note ("LIB-TYPE-SHAPES", 9u32, "2026-11-03T08:00Z", "tibia", "11-03 03:5x tibia, STATE AT PAUSE. SLICE 4 WRITTEN, NOT GATED (unstaged in lib/spiral/sized_array.spi): halved f (sized n el) : sized (type_arith::divup n 2) el (pairs neighbours, keeps an odd tail) and the type-level tree reduction := ^ reduced f x (recurses on the compile-time size until 1, so it unrolls into a fixed tree), with tests halved_pairs_neighbours_and_keeps_an_odd_tail ((3,7,5) after a checked resized) and reduced_sums_by_a_tree_of_halvings (15), c+rust; measured cells 125-226 with target lines. The queued gate and the WGSL probe compile were CANCELLED before they started (no node job was created). NEXT STEP when the run resumes: run the lib gate (gate-lib-module.ps1 -Module sized_array, mutant = halved combining an element with itself, prepared in scratch sized-mutant.txt) after syncing spiral to tibia-gates3, with no other job on that mirror; then the deferred WGSL proof via an isolated-root probe pair under C:\\home\\tibia-probes (filled + matmul expecting 8 per row, twin expecting 9), compiled on the node and run on vultr lavapipe. Waits on: the user's start of the run.")
inl note_of_lib_type_shapes_10 () = Note ("LIB-TYPE-SHAPES", 10u32, "2026-11-03T09:04Z", "tibia", "11-03 05:0x tibia, SLICE 4 GATE GREEN (node job tibia-sized4-1, spiral digest 4CE003D372FDD5EA, sized_array.spi 3AD92F6E2EE452CE): positive 11/11, type errors 0, cell budget 0; mutant (halved combines an element with itself) fails exactly halved_pairs_neighbours_and_keeps_an_odd_tail and reduced_sums_by_a_tree_of_halvings, the two tests that use it, and nothing else. New in lib: halved f (sized n el) : sized (type_arith::divup n 2) el and the typed tree reduction := ^ reduced f x, which recurses on the compile-time size and so unrolls into a fixed log2-depth tree (the scope's 'tree reduce typed sa (divup n 2)'). Left in scope: tuple_list n el, dim_of witness, Lua/WGSL arms. NEXT: the deferred WGSL proof (probe pair filled + matmul, isolated root C:\\home\\tibia-probes), compile queued on the node now.")
inl note_of_lib_type_shapes_11 () = Note ("LIB-TYPE-SHAPES", 11u32, "2026-11-03T09:08Z", "tibia", "11-03 05:0x tibia, WGSL PROOF FOR sized_array (the one slice 3 deferred): a probe main builds x : sized 4 f32 = filled 1, w : sized (type_arith::mult 3 4) f32 = filled 2, o : sized 3 f32 = filled 0, runs the typed matmul o x w and returns 0 iff item o 0 = 8 and item o 2 = 8. Compiled on the node (job tibia-sized-probe-1, mirror lib = spiral digest 4CE003D372FDD5EA, the overflow-checking compiler) from an ISOLATED root C:\\home\\tibia-probes\\sized (own spiral.toml; no manifest walk-up this time): 157-line shader. Run on vultr lavapipe, 2 lanes: lane 0 = 0, lane 1 = 0. NEGATIVE twin (same program expecting 9): lane 0 = 1, lane 1 = 1. So filled, the typed matmul and dot run correctly on WGSL with constant shapes, and the test is non-vacuous. Remaining scope: tuple_list n el, a dim_of witness, Lua arms, and a WGSL reduced (it builds through init's closure, which WGSL lacks; a closure-free halving is the next slice).")
inl note_of_lib_type_shapes_12 () = Note ("LIB-TYPE-SHAPES", 12u32, "2026-11-03T09:30Z", "tibia", "11-03 05:3x tibia, WGSL CLOSURE WALL REMOVED for sized_array's closure-taking ops. Cause found: init passed its closure f into the init_from join point as a PARAMETER, which forces a runtime closure, and WGSL has none. Fix: the loop lives INSIDE init as a local join point that reads f from scope (:~ ^ go (i : i32) ... set x i (f i)), so the known lambda is inlined. Probe (isolated root C:\\home\\tibia-probes\\scoped-init; built with that loop shape, pairs and an inl tree recursion on sized 5 i32 = 1..5, expects 15): compiles to a 126-line WGSL shader (C too, 117 lines); on vultr lavapipe 2 lanes: 0/0; negative twin (the shader's 'v8 == 15i' edited to 16): 1/1. LIB CHANGE (unstaged): sized_array init now uses the in-scope loop, init_from removed (grep over C:\\home\\git: no other user), so map, take, skip, halved and reduced inherit WGSL support; new ///> wgsl test reduced_runs_on_the_gpu. Gate queued (tibia-sized5-1, after the full tibia suite frees the mirror); C/Rust tests must stay 11/11 with the same mutant. General rule for lib: a higher-order loop keeps its function argument in scope of a local join point, never as a join-point parameter, wherever WGSL must compile it.")
inl note_of_lib_type_shapes_13 () = Note ("LIB-TYPE-SHAPES", 13u32, "2026-11-03T11:44Z", "tibia", "11-03 07:4x tibia, SLICE 5 GATE RED BY INFRASTRUCTURE, not code: node job tibia-sized5-1 (sized_array 4E175984C6A360A6) ran 6/12 and aborted with 'could not start the compiler on port 13969: :lock_timeout' (the node's shared compiler daemon, contended by other lanes' jobs, the same failure CELLS-LUA-HOST-CRASH records for network/pack_sim). The raw output holds only the summary, no per-test error. DISPROOF of a code cause: a probe running the exact logic of the 5 'failed' tests (merge + resized + fold, map, take/skip, the literal relabel through type_arith::add 2 3) against the current lib (with the in-scope init loop), compiled on uncontended vultr, exits 0 on C (gcc -O2) and on Rust (rustc -O). RE-GATE queued at the end of tibia-suite-rest-1 (after the load chain), same mutant.")
inl note_of_lib_type_shapes_14 () = Note ("LIB-TYPE-SHAPES", 14u32, "2026-11-03T14:57Z", "tibia", "11-03 11:0x tibia: the unstaged sized_array.spi delta (init as an in-scope loop for WGSL, init_from removed, plus the wgsl reduced_runs_on_the_gpu test) moved out of the live lib to kit drafts spiral LIB-TYPE-SHAPES (sized_array.spi 4E175984C6A3 + sized_array.unstaged.patch); live restored from the index (the user-staged part stays). It was gated today (node 11/12 + wgsl unsupported there, mutant failed exactly 2; lavapipe 0/0 + negative 1/1 earlier) and lands via lib_land_window like any lib change.")
inl note_of_lib_type_shapes_15 () = Note ("LIB-TYPE-SHAPES", 15u32, "2026-11-03T20:02Z", "tibia", "11-03 16:0x tibia, LUA ARMS SLICE GATED (drafts LIB-TYPE-SHAPES slice6, 4E175984C6A3 -> 4F2DA03B0CE5): every c+rust test of sized_array now also runs on lua. A vultr probe first: merge/fold/take/skip/reduced/halved/typed matmul compiled to Lua and run on lua5.1 prints VERDICT OK, and a negative twin prints VERDICT BAD (plain lua exits 0 whatever main returns, so the probe prints its verdict; memory probe-deletes-stale-outputs). Node gate (Cells runs lua through spiral lua): positive 11/12 (the 12th is the wgsl test, unsupported on the node), 0 type errors; mutant (halved combines an element with itself) fails 2. The added ///> lua lines pushed 2 tests over the cell budget, so the shared arrays moved into two fixtures small () : sized 2 i32 and tens () : sized 3 i32; budget 0; canonical. LANDING now. Remaining scope: tuple_list n el, a dim_of witness.")
inl note_of_lib_type_shapes_16 () = Note ("LIB-TYPE-SHAPES", 16u32, "2026-11-03T20:22Z", "tibia", "11-03 16:2x tibia: lua slice LANDED (4E175984C6A3 -> 4F2DA03B0CE5): closed green, consumer compiles, document_test, type_probe, 0 respell drift; undo lib-land-undo 20261103-160116. sized_array now carries c, rust and lua on every runtime test plus the wgsl reduced test.")
inl note_of_lib_type_shapes_17 () = Note ("LIB-TYPE-SHAPES", 17u32, "2026-11-03T21:16Z", "tibia", "11-03 17:2x tibia, SLICE 7 LANDED, the dim_of shape witness of the scope (ML library layers.spi dim_of): a recursive GADT dim_of t with DimSized :: i32 -> dim_of (sized n el) (length from the type literal through dim_sized ()) and DimPair :: dim_of a * dim_of b -> dim_of (a * b) (dim_pair), and elements counting every leaf; test: a (sized 3 f32 * (sized 4 f32 * sized 2 i32)) witness has 9 elements on c, rust and lua. Node gate positive 12/13 (wgsl unsupported on the node), 0 type errors, budget 0; mutant (a pair counts only its left side) fails exactly the witness test. Land 4F2DA03B0CE5 -> BA3E59936D49 closed green (document_test 22, type_probe 12 typed, 0 respell drift); undo lib-land-undo 20261103-171218. Left in scope: tuple_list n el (needs a real-level type loop, as in the ML library tuple_listm).")
inl note_of_lib_type_shapes_18 () = Note ("LIB-TYPE-SHAPES", 18u32, "2026-11-03T21:22Z", "tibia", "11-03 17:3x tibia, tuple_list SPIKE (3 box compiles, Rust backend, nothing drafted): the edition-3 spelling of a computed nominal is :! tuple_list n el = `( := ^ loop (i : i32) = ? i > 0 ?> ``el, loop (i - 1) |: ``(()) / loop (real_core::type_lit_to_lit `n) ) (the guid.spi :! guid_python = `( ... ) form). TYPE SIDE WORKS: the checker computes tuple_list 3 i32 = i32 * i32 * i32 * (). VALUE SIDE FAILS two ways: (a) the plain constructor tl::tuple_list (1, 2, 3, ()) gives ETY-TermError Got .<term> Expected i32 * i32 * i32 * (); (b) ~> real_core::nominal_create `(tl::tuple_list 3 i32) (1, 2, 3, ()) type-checks but partial evaluation fails inside the type-level loop conditional: Cannot apply a forall with a term (tl.spi:3:33, the ? i > 0). NEXT: try a non-recursive spelling (type_arith-style or a type-level union_map over a fixed record), or hand the PE failure to the compiler lane with this 2-file repro (tl.spi + probe main above). tuple_list is the last open piece of this item (90%).")
inl note_of_lib_type_shapes_19 () = Note ("LIB-TYPE-SHAPES", 19u32, "2026-11-04T00:58Z", "tibia", "11-03 21:0x tibia, tuple_list LANDED, the last piece of the scope: new lib module tuple_list.spi (45268BF26BCF) + package.spiproj line after sized_array (re-derived from live 9FBD9E014717 at land time -> 9F29F2476BF9). :! tuple_list n el is a computed nominal: a real-level loop builds el * el * ... * () from the type literal n. tuple_length reads n; tuple_item i t walks the nested pairs at compile time and is rejected at compile time unless i is a literal with 0 <= i < n (the ML library tuple_listm.index had the bound inverted, i <= 0; fixed). Split into tuple_bound_ok / tuple_walk / tuple_item for the cell budget (one 466-weight cell before). EDITION-3 TRAPS FOUND: inside a type-level `( ) block the bare > and - are the typed prototypes (Cannot apply a forall with a term); write real_core::(>) and real_core::(-); and the inline let is ;; not in (respell finding). PROOF: vultr C probe OK / wrong-value twin BAD / index 3 rejected with the message; node gate positive 2/2 (incl. the expected compile error), 0 type errors, budget 0; mutant (walk stops one early) fails exactly the order test; land closed green (document_test 22, type_probe pass, 0 respell drift); undo lib-land-undo 20261103-205136. Scope complete: type_arith use, sized n el with merge/take/skip/resized/halved/reduced/matmul, dim_of witness, tuple_list, C/Rust/Lua arms on every runtime test plus a WGSL test.")
inl lib_tensor_views () = Task ("LIB-TENSOR-VIEWS", "lib: struct-of-arrays tensors with type-level dims and a zero-cost compile-time view algebra (zip, unzip, rezip, reorder, apply, view, split, flatten, reshape, factorize, apply_proj), bounds checks that are type errors when index and size are literals and runtime only for dynamic dims, and reorder proven by term-var set equality", Story, P1, D6, 0u32, Planned, "LIB-TYPE-SHAPES,COMPILER-TYPE-ARITH-NORMALIZE", "lib,typefeatureset,transformer,tibia,team:tibia", "current", "current")
