inl note_of_gadt_domain_stress_samples_1 () = Note ("GADT-DOMAIN-STRESS-SAMPLES", 1u32, "2026-11-02T16:04Z", "fiscal", "fiscal 11-02 12:04 filed by fiscal from the typefeatureset survey. GADT bugs keep surfacing late (compiler 11-02 hurdle #12: erasing a unit-nominal GADT index broke 4 samples) because the samples are toy-sized and the domain models live in lane repos. Candidates, all green today: fiscal/src/lifecycle.spi (document s indexed by authorized/cancelled; 2 negative tests that must fail with Unification failure), the generated staged s a signing union (fiscal generated_nfe_v4), tibia vocations.spi (spell v over healer v) and readiness.spi (ready group), lib keyed_rng.spi (\\\\* p {purpose_code} constraints). RULE (regression-samples-allowed + compiler hurdle: a sample without a matrix row catches nothing): each one lands as a sample WITH its matrix row, and its negative tests run as expected-failure rows. DONE WHEN: 5 samples on every backend the matrix runs; a deliberately broken build (index erased) turns at least one row red.")
inl compiler_type_arith_normalize () = Task ("COMPILER-TYPE-ARITH-NORMALIZE", "Compiler: normalize type-level arithmetic (type_arith add/sub/mult/div/divup over literals) during inference so equal shapes unify regardless of spelling; prerequisite of typed tensor reshape/flatten and computed dims", Story, P1, D6, 0u32, Planned, "", "compiler,typefeatureset,transformer,lib,team:tibia", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_compiler_type_arith_normalize () = Planning ("COMPILER-TYPE-ARITH-NORMALIZE", Minutes 600u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_compiler_type_arith_normalize_1 () = Note ("COMPILER-TYPE-ARITH-NORMALIZE", 1u32, "2026-11-02T16:37Z", "tibia", "11-02 tibia, from LIB-TYPE-SHAPES probe 2 (node job shape-probe-4; drafts %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\spiral\\LIB-TYPE-SHAPES). Measured on our compiler: \"sized (add 2 2) i32\" does NOT unify with \"sized 4 i32\" (Got sized 4i32 i32, Expected sized (add 2i32 2i32) i32), and add 2 2 does not unify with mult 2 2. type_arith's nominals are type-level macros that are only evaluated in partial evaluation; inference compares them unevaluated. The ML library worked around it with nominal_recreate at real level. Without normalization, typed tensors can match equal shapes only when they are spelled the same: matmul's inner dims work, reshape/flatten/split and computed dims (dim = heads * head_dim) do not. DoD: Infer evaluates add/sub/mult/div/divup on literal arguments (and on type variables bound to literals) before unification, so add 2 2 = mult 2 2 = 4; stuck terms over free variables stay symbolic; a positive and a negative sample in the matrix on C and Rust.")
inl note_of_compiler_type_arith_normalize_2 () = Note ("COMPILER-TYPE-ARITH-NORMALIZE", 2u32, "2026-11-02T19:16Z", "tibia", "11-02 tibia, DESIGN READ with the compiler lane's pointers (core C9CF9F36 = pack17), no code yet. WHY it fails: Infer types every type-level term `(...) as an opaque TySymbol \"<term>\" (spiral_compiler.fs:6230). type_arith's bodies are such terms (lit_to_type_lit (type_lit_to_lit `a + ...)), only PE evaluates them, so unify_gadt's loop (5580) sees TyApply(TyApply(TyNominal add, 2), 2) vs TyLit 4, compares structurally (5616) and TyLit only by equality (5637), and fails. Recognizing add/mult by name, or by the shape of their macro body, is fragile (compiler lane's review point). SOUND SHAPE (agreed with the compiler lane; it reviews and stacks into a pack): built-in type-level integer ops. (1) A T case or a compiler-known nominal family for add/sub/mult/div/divup over type literals, with parser syntax for it. (2) A normalize step at the top of unify's loop (5581): fold when both args are TyLit integers of the same primitive; leave terms over type vars symbolic; normalize both sides recursively through TyApply. (3) The same fold in PE's nominal_type_apply (~9905). (4) Every exhaustive match on T in Infer gains the case (visit, subst, has_metavars, the kind-of, printing). (5) lib type_arith redefined on the built-ins. (6) Samples under apps/compiler/tmp/samples: a positive (add 2 2 = 4 = mult 2 2; a reshape helper typed `sized (mult a b) -> sized a ... `) and a negative (add 2 3 vs 4 rejected), on F#/C/Rust/Delphi/Zig; compare-runs in-process. Estimate: one focused session (3-5 h) as a kit-do program, plus the compiler lane's A/B.")
inl lib_binary_io () = Task ("LIB-BINARY-IO", "lib: portable binary input for checkpoints: file_system.read_all_bytes gains a C arm, plus little-endian i32/f32 decoding from bytes at an offset on C, Rust and WGSL, so llama2.c checkpoints load on every native backend", Story, P1, D4, 100u32, Done, "", "lib,transformer,backends,team:tibia", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_lib_binary_io () = Planning ("LIB-BINARY-IO", Minutes 240u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_lib_binary_io_1 () = Note ("LIB-BINARY-IO", 1u32, "2026-11-02T16:43Z", "tibia", "11-02 tibia, parent TYPED-TRANSFORMER-LAB (named here, not as a dep). Scouted: core has exp/sqrt/sin/cos/log/tanh ({float}); lib file_system.read_all_bytes (file_system.spi:822) has F# and Rust arms only and returns null () elsewhere, so the C backend cannot load a checkpoint. No lib function decodes f32 or i32 from bytes. The compiler lists to_bits among float ops (spiral_compiler.fs:13393) but there is no from_bits. DoD: read_all_bytes with a C arm (fopen/fread), little-endian i32/f32 decode from a byte view at an offset (C memcpy, Rust from_le_bytes/f32::from_bits, WGSL bitcast<f32>), with tests on c and rust reading a fixed file and decoding known values (1.0f = 0x3F800000, -2.5f, a NaN payload). Needed by TRANSFORMER-CHECKPOINT-LOAD (llama2.c header: 7 i32 then f32 weights).")
inl note_of_lib_binary_io_2 () = Note ("LIB-BINARY-IO", 2u32, "2026-11-02T16:44Z", "tibia", "11-02 tibia: wider than first written. lib/spiral has NO C file I/O at all: there are no fopen/fread sites, and the only C arm in lib is unicode.spi:60. So the C arm of read_all_bytes is foundational (a C file handle plus a byte buffer type over the C backend's array runtime), not a one-line arm. Order: Rust first, where read_all_bytes works today and f32::from_le_bytes decodes; the C arm second, as its own slice. The transformer CPU reference starts on Rust (TRANSFORMER-CPU-REFERENCE), with C following once this item's C slice lands.")
inl note_of_lib_binary_io_3 () = Note ("LIB-BINARY-IO", 3u32, "2026-11-02T20:19Z", "tibia", "11-02 tibia: LANDED green (lib_land_window lib-binary-io; file_system.spi F83CC223001C, convert.spi 76A9C3DD0C72; undo spiral-bin\\lib-land-undo\\20261102-161400). New: file_system::read_bytes : string -> array u8, with a C arm (one-line #include globals plus a spiral_read_file helper: size query, then fread into a Spiral-created array; failwith on open failure or short read) and am'::from_vec of read_all_bytes elsewhere. New: convert::f32_of_bits : u32 -> f32, with Rust f32::from_bits, C memcpy and Wgsl bitcast<f32> arms. Tests: read_bytes on c and rust (write, then read \"AB\"); f32_of_bits on c, rust and wgsl (1.0f, -2.5f). Node gate: convert 6/6, file_system 38/38, and both mutants bite. The first close failed document_test (no final newline in convert; a missing blank line after the inserted test in file_system), was undone, and the files were fixed and round-trip-checked (equal=true) before the second land. Closes: 16 signatures unchanged, consumer compiles, document_test 22/22, type_probe pass 2/2.")
inl note_of_lib_binary_io_4 () = Note ("LIB-BINARY-IO", 4u32, "2026-11-02T20:55Z", "tibia", "11-02 tibia, follow-up land in the same slice: env::get_environment_variable gains a C arm (getenv plus StringLit copy, \"\" when unset; one-line #include globals; a statement-expression macro inside the arm so it runs after the C prelude). Test get_environment_variable_reads_set_and_unset_variables on c and rust; the mutant (unset -> \"x\") bites. LANDED green (lib-env-c; env.spi DF0A07FB3677; undo spiral-bin\\lib-land-undo\\20261102-164630; closes: 2 signatures unchanged, consumer compiles, document_test 22/22, type_probe pass). With it the transformer builds and matches run.c on C. Drafts: %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\spiral\\LIB-ENV-C.")
inl kit_agile_claim_queue () = Task ("KIT-AGILE-CLAIM-QUEUE", "collective ready-queue: kit agile next --any surfaces AND atomically leases the top unblocked unclaimed item across all teams, path-scoped leases honored by next and window grants, wired into the idle stop hook", Feature, P0, D6, 60u32, Active, "", "team:kit,kit,agile,cco,xp,lease", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_kit_agile_claim_queue () = Planning ("KIT-AGILE-CLAIM-QUEUE", Minutes 720u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_kit_agile_claim_queue_1 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-CLAIM-QUEUE", 1u32, "2026-11-02T16:44Z", "kit", "kit 11-02 12:5x filed. USER (via resilience, CCO pivot): \"lanes sit idle EVEN WITH stop hooks because kit doesn't seamlessly offer CCO - pivot deeper into XP\". Consensus of compiler, x-dumps, tibia, resilience, main, plus x's path-scoping: ONE collective queue that both surfaces and claims. DESIGN (typed): (1) `kit agile next --any`: global WSJF across all teams (lane weights respected), returns the top unblocked + unclaimed item with its last note and touched paths, and ATOMICALLY leases it to the caller through the backlog's existing CAS write. (2) Claim typestate: Unclaimed | Leased of { owner; expires; paths : list glob } | Released | Expired; a lease ends on TTL, on a note by the owner that releases it, or on Done. (3) Path-scoped: `kit agile claim <id> --paths <globs>`; `next` skips items whose paths overlap a live lease, and `kit window` grants refuse overlapping paths (today a 2nd tree-write window was granted beside main's live lib heal). (4) Stop starting, start finishing: next --any prefers in-flight / expired-lease items over new ones and prints the WIP count. (5) Idle path: the Stop hook (autonomous-gate) calls `next --any` and presents the claimed item, so an idle lane is handed work instead of told to hunt. Builds on kit_agile_lease (lease receipts), kit_window_claim / kit_tree_lease (typed ReadLease / ShapeLease), agile_mutation_policy (CAS). DoD: two lanes running next --any concurrently never get the same item (CAS test); an expired lease is re-offered; overlapping path globs are refused by both next and window; the stop hook hands an item; notebooks green; crates under 1000.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_claim_queue_2 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-CLAIM-QUEUE", 2u32, "2026-11-02T16:47Z", "kit", "kit 11-02 13:0x OUTPUT CONTRACT v1 (fixed before the code; resilience builds the stop-hook side against it). Adds fiscal's auto-liveness refusal (6/6 lanes).\nVERBS: `kit agile next <root> --any --as <lane> [--ttl <minutes, default 90>]` | `kit agile claim <root> <ID> --as <lane> [--paths <glob,glob>] [--ttl <m>]` | `kit agile release <root> <ID> --as <lane>`.\nnext --any ORDER: global WSJF across all teams with lane weights; prefers (1) items whose lease EXPIRED or that the caller already holds, (2) Active items with no live owner, (3) Planned; skips Blocked/Paused/Done, items with unfinished deps, items with a LIVE lease, and items with a live-owner signal: a running kit job labelled with the ID, or a tree edit on its leased/recorded paths in the last 20 min.\nSTDOUT on a claim (exit 0), line 1 is the receipt, then exactly these lines:\n  kit agile next ok claimed=<ID> owner=<lane> expires=<ISO-8601 UTC> ttl_min=<n> wip=<live leases> rank=<1-based WSJF rank>\n  item <ID> <P> <D> <status> <team> <title>\n  last-note <at ISO> <author> <first 300 chars, newlines as spaces>\n  paths <glob>[;<glob>...] | paths none\nNOTHING AVAILABLE (exit 3): `kit agile next ok claimed=none reason=<none-ready|all-leased|all-live> wip=<n>`.\nclaim refusals (exit 5): `kit agile claim refused id=<ID> reason=<leased-by:<lane>:<expires>|job-running:<job-id>|recent-edit:<path>@<ISO>|path-overlap:<ID>:<glob>>`.\nCAS contention exhausted after retries (exit 4): `kit agile next refused reason=contention`. Usage (exit 2): `kit error: agile ...`.\nLEASE END: TTL expiry, `release`, a note by the owner containing `--release`, or status Done. Leases are typed rows in the backlog's state (Lease(item, owner, expires, paths)), written through the existing CAS, so two concurrent next --any calls can never claim the same item (DoD test).\nWINDOWS: `kit window request` refuses a tree-write class whose root overlaps a live lease's paths, reason=path-overlap:<ID>.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_claim_queue_3 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-CLAIM-QUEUE", 3u32, "2026-11-02T16:58Z", "resilience", "resilience STOP-HOOK slice WIRED (user authorized the hook edit 11-02). autonomous-gate.ps1 now calls kit agile next <backlog> --any when it fires and, on exit 0 per your contract v1, appends a line to the block reason presenting the claimed item (line1 claimed=<ID> -> parse ID, the item <ID> ... line, the paths ... line). FAIL-SAFE + feature-detected: verified --any currently exits 2 (unknown flag) -> $claimed null -> gate output UNCHANGED, no crash. Activates automatically when TRAIN13 ships \next --any. If the final invocation isn't bare --any (e.g. needs --owner <lane> or --claim), send me the exact form and I'll adjust the one call. Standalone-tested; not yet exercised against a live verb.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_claim_queue_4 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-CLAIM-QUEUE", 4u32, "2026-11-02T18:39Z", "kit", "kit 11-02 14:40 STAGE 2 in flight (unstaged, nothing shipped yet). STORAGE PROVEN TYPED: leases are rows in state/claims.spi: `union rec claim_paths = NoPaths | PathThen :: string * claim_paths`, `union agile_claim = Claim :: string * string * u64 * claim_paths`, rows `inl claim_<id> () = Claim (\"ID\", \"lane\", <ms>u64, PathThen (\"glob\", NoPaths))`. The file is registered in state/package.spiproj modules, so `kit agile check` typechecks every lease, and a broken row is refused (exit 2 control). Plain `union` (not rec) and core `list string` both fail in the state dialect: probed. Writes go under the existing agile.spi.lock mkdir lock (next --any, claim and release are write verbs; next only when --any), so two lanes cannot claim the same item; exit 4 `kit agile <verb> refused reason=contention` when the lock stays held for 30 s. SIZE FORCED A SPLIT: a single crate generated 3767 lines (cap 1000; all 275 kit crates are ≤1000). The split is by concern, with primitive exports through a boundary api package (kit_rec_cycle_api pattern): agile_claim_rank (backlog → WSJF/active/open IDs; assignee=user and Epics excluded via the existing agile_planning_filters export), agile_claim_item (item + last-note lines), agile_claim_store (claims.spi I/O + module registration), agile_claim_queue (pure decisions: claims rows text in, outcome tuple + updated rows out), agile_claim_cli (argv, orchestration, contract stdout, ISO stamps; the route target), and agile_claim_api. Tests on vultr: claim model 5/5, queue decisions 4/4, rank 1/1, cli args 1/1. Generated sizes: cli 872, item 814, store 267, rank 1060→(planning moved out, re-measuring), queue 1466→~1300 (shrinking). DEFERRED to stage 3, with contract v1 unchanged: liveness signals (job label, 20-min tree edit) answer Idle for now, and lease end by a `--release` note (needs a claimed-at stamp in the row). The CLI e2e test (two lanes never share an item, all-leased exit 3, refusals exit 5, owner-only release) is written as src/agile_claim_cli/cli_check.spi and waits on the size fix.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_claim_queue_5 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-CLAIM-QUEUE", 5u32, "2026-11-02T19:00Z", "kit", "kit 11-02 15:1x STAGE 2 PROVEN on a vultr candidate (unstaged; ships in TRAIN13 after main's pack17 install). The final crate split is 6 crates, all under the cap: agile_claim_rank 722, agile_claim_item 814, agile_claim_store 869, agile_claim_queue 866 (next --any), agile_claim_lease 983 (claim/release), agile_claim_cli 872 (argv, orchestration, contract stdout, route target from kit_agile_mutation), plus agile_claim_api (one-expression bindings). The model refactor: lease_refusal (state + liveness) is split from refusal_of (+ path overlap), so next never compiles the overlap code, and a typed claim_change (Upsert/Remove/None) and outcome cross crate boundaries as reflection words. TESTS: claim model 5/5, queue 2/2, lease 2/2, rank 1/1, item 1/1, cli args 1/1, and the CLI e2e src/agile_claim_cli/cli_check.spi 2/2 against the real binary. DoD RACE (the binary on vultr, a fresh backlog with 6 items): 8 lanes ran `next --any` IN PARALLEL and got 6 distinct claims in WSJF rank order (rank 1..6), 2 got `claimed=none reason=all-leased` exit 3, 0 contention, and the 6 rows in state/claims.spi typecheck under `kit agile check` (files=4). The help text lists `next ... --any --as <lane> [--ttl]`, `claim` and `release`. Remaining for DoD: (a) `kit window request` refusing tree-write roots that overlap a live lease's paths (stage 3), (b) liveness signals (job label, 20-min tree edit), (c) lease end on a `--release` note. resilience was told the final hook form needs `--as <lane>`.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_claim_queue_6 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-CLAIM-QUEUE", 6u32, "2026-11-02T19:43Z", "kit", "kit 11-02 16:2x STAGE 3a (window path-overlap refusal) has DESIGN QUESTIONS, so it was parked rather than guessed. (1) Tree-write window labels are absolute paths (C:/home/git/spiral/lib, compared by path_prefix), but lease paths are globs relative to an unnamed repo (\"src/agile/**\"). A lease must carry an anchored root: either `claim --paths` resolves globs against the caller's cwd/repo root to absolute globs at claim time (preferred: the row then stands alone), or the row gains a repo field. (2) `kit window request` has no backlog root, so it can't find state/claims.spi. Proposal: the window verb reads the claims of every backlog listed in a machine-level roots file (the agile roots registry), or KIT_AGILE_ROOT. (3) Self-refusal: the requester's own lease must not block its window, which needs the requester's lane at window time; use the same identity the stop hook uses (KIT_LANE else \"s-\"+session id). Decide (1)-(3), then implement in kit_machine_windows window_model's row_blocks path, which already has path_overlaps.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_claim_queue_7 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-CLAIM-QUEUE", 7u32, "2026-11-02T20:10Z", "kit", "kit 11-02 16:1x STAGE 3 DECISIONS (technical calls, recorded so the work is turnkey). (1) Anchoring: `claim --paths` resolves each relative glob against the caller's working directory to an absolute, forward-slashed, lower-cased glob at claim time, so a row stands alone (\"c:/home/git/kit/src/agile/**\"); absolute globs pass through. Needs a lib helper first: `file_system.current_directory () : string` (backend_switch shape with a lib test block; lib has only erlang absname and the Rust canonicalize result', not a plain cwd string). (2) Window side: `kit window request <class> <path>` for tree-write reads the claims of every backlog in the machine's agile roots registry (`kit agile roots`), or KIT_AGILE_ROOT when set, and refuses `reason=path-overlap:<ID>` through the existing window_model path_overlaps. (3) Self-identity: KIT_LANE, else the window/claim caller's `--as`; an owner's own live lease never blocks its own window. (4) Liveness (contract: running job labelled with the ID, or a tree edit on leased paths in the last 20 min): new boundary package primitives `jobs_running_labels () : string` (job records under the jobs home) and `newest_edit_ms (glob) : u64`, feeding the existing `liveness` union (LiveJob / RecentEdit), which already refuses in lease_refusal. Order: (1) lib helper → (1) → (3) → (2) → (4); each stage is its own crate-sized step under 1000.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_claim_queue_8 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-CLAIM-QUEUE", 8u32, "2026-11-02T20:18Z", "kit", "kit 11-02 16:2x STAGE 3 step (1) DONE (unstaged, misses TRAIN13's 16:05 snapshot, ships TRAIN14): claim --paths anchors each relative glob at the caller's working directory (lib file_system.get_current_directory; backslashes become /, a leading ./ is dropped, absolute and drive globs pass through), so every lease row is absolute and stands alone. Proof on vultr: cli module tests 2/2 (the new anchored test covers Windows cwd, ./ and absolute) and cli_check 2/2 against the regenerated binary (the overlap refusal still fires for two claims made in the same root). agile_claim_cli generates 950 lines (cap 1000). Next: (3) self-identity, then (2) window refusal, then (4) liveness.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_claim_queue_9 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-CLAIM-QUEUE", 9u32, "2026-11-03T00:32Z", "kit", "kit 11-02 20:4x STAGE 3 step (4a) DONE, liveness by running job (unstaged, next train). New boundary package kit_os_files with one primitive, entry_names (one expression, 179 chars). agile_claim_rank.live_items lists the jobs root (kit_jobs.host.jobs_root, so it is the same folder kit job uses) and marks an item live when a job folder named job-<ms>-<label> started in the last 12 h, has a label containing the item ID (case-insensitive), and has no exit file. That is pure name matching plus one stat per match: 399 folders on the box. claim_ranking returns the live IDs as a 5th field; next --any passes LiveJob to choose (all-live when only live items remain) and claim refuses job-running:<ID> (exit 5). The owner's own lease still wins, because lease_refusal checks the owner first. PROOF on vultr: rank 2/2 (including a_running_job_labelled_with_an_item_keeps_it_live: running labels only, finished jobs and jobs over 12 h excluded), queue 3/3 (a_live_job_keeps_other_lanes_off_its_item), lease 3/3 (a_running_job_refuses_a_claim_on_its_item), cli_check 2/2 against the rebuilt binary. Sizes: rank 952, queue 883, lease 1000 (AT THE CAP: trim before the next codegen change), cli 950. Still open: (4b) the tree-edit signal and (2)/(3) window overlap.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_claim_queue_10 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-CLAIM-QUEUE", 10u32, "2026-11-03T08:00Z", "kit", "11-03 03:5x kit PAUSE (main: the 21:00 run was armed early by mistake; run paused). STATE: decline-on-release, whole-word job-label liveness and the gate's team-affinity release are proven (claim/queue/lease/rank tests, cli_check 2/2); the gate part is live. Decline ships in TRAIN14c (job-1793690561987-kit-TRAIN14c, running since 03:22, launched under the mistakenly armed window; left running per main). NEXT: on install, confirm 'next --any' does not re-offer an item to the lane that released it. WAITS ON: TRAIN14c.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_claim_queue_11 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-CLAIM-QUEUE", 11u32, "2026-11-03T10:10Z", "kit", "11-03 06:1x kit: decline-on-release is LIVE in TRAIN14c (kit.exe 5B9082C1). Live proof on the real backlog: lane kit-decline-probe ran next --any and got REMOTE-EXEC-S2, released it (released=yes), then next --any and got SCRUM-XP-S2: the released item was not re-offered. Both probe claims were released (5 min TTL), so only that probe lane carries decline rows. Main's release -> re-claim loop is closed.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_claim_queue_12 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-CLAIM-QUEUE", 12u32, "2026-11-04T21:00Z", "kit", "11-04 22:5x kit groom: the title's design (next --any ATOMICALLY leases the top item, wired into the idle stop hook) was reversed on 11-04 by user/main evidence (idle lanes kept being handed other teams' Active items): main's stop gate now LISTS the team's ready items via the read-only `next --team --open`, and claiming is explicit. KIT-AGILE-ANY-USABILITY carries the replacement (team-aware lane order, per-(item, lane) declines, list-first `next --any` with --claim; built, box-green, in TRAIN23). What stays from this item and is live since TRAIN14c: decline-on-release. Proposal for the next groom: close this as superseded once TRAIN23 installs, citing ANY-USABILITY.")
inl compiler_rust_array_hot_loop () = Task ("COMPILER-RUST-ARRAY-HOT-LOOP", "Compiler Rust backend: hot-loop array access without Rc churn: no clone/rebind of unchanged self-tail-call arguments, hoisted borrows of arrays not reassigned in the loop, no .clone() on Copy reads, then a RefCell-free array representation; measured on the Spiral transformer (5 tok/s vs run.c 32-70)", Story, P1, D6, 50u32, Planned, "", "compiler,backends,rust,transformer,team:tibia", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_compiler_rust_array_hot_loop () = Planning ("COMPILER-RUST-ARRAY-HOT-LOOP", Minutes 900u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_compiler_rust_array_hot_loop_1 () = Note ("COMPILER-RUST-ARRAY-HOT-LOOP", 1u32, "2026-11-02T17:05Z", "tibia", "11-02 tibia, measured on the Spiral transformer (TRANSFORMER-CPU-REFERENCE n2; generated transformer.rs 46 KB). Every array element access in hot loops compiles to \"v0.clone().borrow()[i as usize].clone()\" on Rc<RefCell<Vec<f32>>>: an Rc increment and decrement, a RefCell borrow-flag check and a bounds check, twice per multiply-add in dot. The self-tail-call loop then re-binds every unchanged argument with \"(v0, v1, v2, ...) = (v0.clone(), v1, v2.clone(), ...)\" each iteration. Result: about 5 tok/s against llama2.c run.c's 32-70 on the same node. DoD, in order, each measured as tok/s on stories15M with tokens still identical: (1) a loop argument passed unchanged through a self-tail call is not cloned and not re-bound; (2) the borrow of an array that is not reassigned in the loop is hoisted before the loop (let b0 = v0.borrow() once); (3) indexed reads of Copy elements drop the trailing .clone(); (4) arrays move to a representation without RefCell on the hot path (Rc<[Cell<T>]> or an unsafe-cell slice), behind a flag first. The Perceus/FBIP premise of the deliberation (Rc fine at load, never in matmul) is exactly this.")
inl note_of_compiler_rust_array_hot_loop_2 () = Note ("COMPILER-RUST-ARRAY-HOT-LOOP", 2u32, "2026-11-02T17:21Z", "tibia", "11-02 tibia: steps 1 and 3 drafted as a kit-do program, %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\spiral\\COMPILER-RUST-ARRAY-HOT-LOOP\\1-rust-hot-loop-rc.kitdo (dry run ok on the live core, lines 13933/14127; Rust-only codegenRust edits). A direct edit of the live core was made first, then REVERTED, because the compiler lane installs packs from kit-do programs anchored on the live core (pack16c queued); git diff is clean. EVIDENCE (node job transformer-hand-ab): the same output hand-applied to the generated transformer.rs cut stories15M 256 tokens from 136.9 to 40.4 s and from 120.6 to 32.1 s (about 3.4-3.8x), tokens identical to run.c both times, measured under concurrent load. Handed to the compiler lane to stack after pack16c with their residual A/B. Next: step 2 (hoisted borrows) and step 4 (a RefCell-free array representation behind a flag); the compiler lane offered to review the step-4 design.")
inl note_of_compiler_rust_array_hot_loop_3 () = Note ("COMPILER-RUST-ARRAY-HOT-LOOP", 3u32, "2026-11-02T18:54Z", "tibia", "11-02 tibia, MEASURED ON THE pack17 CANDIDATE (node job transformer-pack17; candidate SpiralCompiler.dll 6B9374835A1BBACF vs installed 0AE507ABA4DC13BD, the same transformer.spi, rustc -C opt-level=3 -C target-cpu=native, stories15M 256 greedy steps, rounds alternating): installed 127.7 / 127.7 s, pack17 35.7 / 40.2 s, i.e. 3.2-3.6x, tokens identical to run.c in all 4 runs. Generated Rust: 52,410 -> 47,892 bytes; \".clone().borrow\" sites 100 -> 9. The compiler lane's residual A/B is clean (Rust 107 residuals changed, 106 identical natively; C/Zig byte-identical), and the program is in pack17, handed to main. Steps 1+3 DONE pending the pack17 install. Next: step 2 (hoist borrows of arrays not reassigned in a loop) and step 4 (a RefCell-free array representation), measured the same way.")
inl note_of_compiler_rust_array_hot_loop_4 () = Note ("COMPILER-RUST-ARRAY-HOT-LOOP", 4u32, "2026-11-02T18:57Z", "tibia", "11-02 tibia, STEP 2/4 SCOPING (read-only). (a) Step 2, hoisting .borrow() out of self-tail loops, is UNSAFE with RefCell. Loops like matmul_rows read v1/v2 and write v0 in the same iteration, and Spiral arrays may alias (the same array passed as o and x, e.g. rmsnorm b::x b::x), so a hoisted Ref held across a borrow_mut panics at runtime (\"already borrowed\"). Hoisting is only sound once the representation drops RefCell. So step 2 folds into step 4. (b) Step 4 surface in the compiler is small, 6 Rust sites: the YArray type at 13959 (Rc<RefCell<Vec<T>>>), TyArrayLiteral 14179, TyArrayCreate 14195, TyArrayLength 14196, ArrayIndex/ArrayIndexSet 14261/14265. But lib/spiral has 65 lines that touch borrow()/borrow_mut()/RefCell in Rust macros (am' vec/slice interop and friends), which assume that representation. Proposal for the compiler-lane review: for arrays whose element is_copy, emit Rc<[Cell<T>]> (index = .get(), set = .set(), no borrow flag, aliasing-safe, the hoisting question gone); keep Rc<RefCell<Vec<T>>> for non-Copy elements; and gate it behind SPIRAL_RUST_CELL_ARRAYS until lib's 65 sites get Cell arms or go through a shared accessor. Measure: transformer tok/s with tokens identical, then the residual A/B as for step 1/3.")
inl note_of_compiler_rust_array_hot_loop_5 () = Note ("COMPILER-RUST-ARRAY-HOT-LOOP", 5u32, "2026-11-03T07:32Z", "compiler", "11-03 07:3x compiler GROOM (WIP cap: Active = touched in 12 h). Parked to Planned (12 h; last note tibia, step 2 hoisting is unsafe with RefCell aliasing). Next: the representation change that drops RefCell for unaliased arrays, scoped with tibia.")
inl note_of_compiler_rust_array_hot_loop_6 () = Note ("COMPILER-RUST-ARRAY-HOT-LOOP", 6u32, "2026-11-03T09:50Z", "tibia", "11-03 05:5x tibia, WHERE RUST LOSES ON TIBIA'S SWEEP (perf on quiet vultr, same node-generated sources as TIBIA-RUN-METRICS n10, C 47.7 s vs Rust 126.4 s). Rust: sweep::each_356 41.7%, route_between_225 41.0% (with grow/commit/prepare inlined), loop_5 10.4%. C: grow231 30.4, loop5 19.9, each356 12.0, commit232 10.4, prepare226 8.3, route_between225 5.2. In seconds: each 52.7 s Rust vs 5.7 s C (about 9x), route_between 51.8 vs 25.8 (2x), loop 13.1 vs 9.5 (1.4x). So each_356 alone holds about 47 of the 79 s gap. WHAT each_356 IS: a 6-step loop over a fixed_views band of 6 foes that the partial evaluator flattened into about 220 scalar parameters. C emits pre-declared locals and assigns them in each switch arm. Rust emits 'let (mut v1134, ..., mut v1319) = match ... { arm => (v35, ..., v220) }' tuple expressions of about 190 fields at every nesting level (if/else per foe index, then per union case), so every level builds and moves a 190-field tuple, plus v90.clone() on US22 = Living(i32, i32), which derives Clone but not Copy. TWO CONCRETE CODEGEN LEVERS: (1) wide join-point merges (say more than 16 bound vars) emit 'let mut' declarations plus assignment in each arm, the C shape, instead of tuple-returning if/match expressions; (2) derive Copy as well as Clone for unions whose fields are all Copy (US22, and the nullary US23), so the per-arm .clone() calls vanish and LLVM sees plain moves. Expected: each_356 from about 9x toward C's level, which alone would bring the sweep from 2.65x to about 1.7x. Sources on vultr /root/lanes-lavapipe/sweep.rs (each_356) and sweep.c (each356).")
inl note_of_compiler_rust_array_hot_loop_7 () = Note ("COMPILER-RUST-ARRAY-HOT-LOOP", 7u32, "2026-11-03T09:59Z", "tibia", "11-03 06:0x tibia, LEVER (2) OF n6 REFUTED by A/B on vultr: the generated sweep.rs with only US22's derive changed to #[derive(Clone, Copy)] (sed on line 132; rustc 1.101 nightly -O) runs 125,888 ms against 124,045 / 126,659 ms unchanged (a second Copy run of 142,363 ms is excluded: load jumped to 4.42 from another lane). No gain: LLVM already treats the clone of a two-int enum as a copy. So the 9x in each_356 is lever (1), building and moving the ~190-field tuples at each if/match level; the C-shaped emission (pre-declared let mut plus per-arm assignment for wide join-point merges) is the experiment to run.")
inl lib_backend_wgsl () = Task ("LIB-BACKEND-WGSL", "lib: run_target/backend_switch cover the Wgsl backend (union backend gains Wgsl, plus Calyx/OpenQasm/Ebpf), so GPU code uses the lib idiom instead of raw !!!!BackendSwitch", Story, P1, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "lib,backends,wgsl,gpu,tibia,transformer,team:tibia", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_lib_backend_wgsl () = Planning ("LIB-BACKEND-WGSL", Minutes 120u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_lib_backend_wgsl_1 () = Note ("LIB-BACKEND-WGSL", 1u32, "2026-11-02T18:08Z", "tibia", "11-02 tibia, found writing tibia's GPU lane entry (apps/predict/lanes_gpu.spi). lib backend.spi's backend_switch dispatch has no Wgsl key in its outer !!!!BackendSwitch, and union backend (backend.spi:83-98) has no Wgsl case. So (1) \"run_target \\| | Wgsl => ...\" is ETY-CasePatternNotFoundForType, (2) backend_switch { Wgsl = ... } fails to compile on --backend Wgsl inside backend.spi:3, and (3) any lib function that goes through backend_switch (console, convert, ...) cannot be type-validated from a WGSL program. tibia's WGSL code therefore uses raw !!!!BackendSwitch (as the compiler's own samples/wgsl_parallel_lanes does), which is the drift the lib idiom exists to avoid. DoD: union backend gains Wgsl (and the other compiler backend keys it lacks: Calyx, OpenQasm, Ebpf), backend_switch's outer dispatch and its fallback get the matching keys, run_target_args' maps them, and a lib test compiles a run_target with a Wgsl arm on wgsl and c.")
inl note_of_lib_backend_wgsl_2 () = Note ("LIB-BACKEND-WGSL", 2u32, "2026-11-02T19:07Z", "tibia", "11-02 tibia: LANDED green (lib_land_window, label lib-backend-wgsl, backend.spi 4840EE5A1FB5 -> 632BF4C4FAF4; undo spiral-bin\\lib-land-undo\\20261102-150524). Closes: 0 consumer-used signatures changed, apps/spiral/spiral.spi compiles, document_test 22 passed, type_probe PASS (2 tests typed). Change: union backend gains Wgsl; backend_switch's fallback !!!!BackendSwitch gains a Wgsl arm (unit -> empty text, other -> backend_switch_has_no_Wgsl_arm_in_lib_spiral, an unknown identifier that WGSL validation rejects, as C/Pascal/Wasm do) and its key dispatch gains Wgsl; run_target_args' maps Wgsl. A test header (:# backend static = :< testing, as console/env use) plus tests run_target_picks_the_c_arm (c) and run_target_picks_the_wgsl_arm (wgsl). Node gate before landing: positive 2/2, the WGSL test running on the node's Quadro K1100M through Vulkan; the mutant (7 -> 8) fails exactly the wgsl test. Cell budget: the 5 findings are the pre-existing oversized dispatch tables (backend_switch 4519 etc.). Not done in this item: Calyx/OpenQasm/Ebpf keys. tibia apps/predict/lanes_gpu.spi now uses run_target with a Wgsl arm instead of raw !!!!BackendSwitch.")
inl note_of_lib_backend_wgsl_3 () = Note ("LIB-BACKEND-WGSL", 3u32, "2026-11-02T22:11Z", "tibia", "11-02 tibia: DONE, the remaining keys LANDED green (lib_land_window, label lib-backend-circuit-keys, backend.spi 632BF4C4FAF4 -> 308AFF49EBAC; undo spiral-bin\\lib-land-undo\\20261102-175959). Change: union backend gains Calyx, OpenQasm, Ebpf (the compiler's codegen names, spiral_compiler.fs build table); backend_switch's fallback !!!!BackendSwitch gets the three arms (unit -> (), as Bend does, so the restricted backends need no macro text; other -> backend_switch_has_no_<X>_arm_in_lib_spiral); the key dispatch maps them; run_target_args' maps them. New test run_target_names_calyx_openqasm_and_ebpf (c) over circuit_probe_code, which fails to compile with ETY-CasePatternNotFound without the cases. Node gate (mirror tibia-gates3, job lib-backend-gate-2): roundtrip equal (289 lines); positive 2/2 c passed, the existing wgsl test reported unsupported (no WGSL runner in that gate env; it ran on the Quadro in the first land); mutant 5 -> 6 fails exactly the new test; budget = the same 5 pre-existing dispatch-table findings. Closes: 0 consumer-used signatures changed, apps/spiral compiles, document_test 22 passed, type_probe PASS (3 tests typed).")
inl gadt_debruijn_rename_closure_growth () = Task ("GADT-DEBRUIJN-RENAME-CLOSURE-GROWTH", "samples/gadt_debruijn_rename_runtime times out: rename (lift ren) wraps a static closure in another lift at every Lam level, so the join-point key grows by closure nesting (not by type index); make the growing closure argument dynamic at the join point or teach the ancestry rule closures", TaskKind, P1, D4, 100u32, Done, "GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX", "team:compiler,compiler,gadt,partial-eval,bug", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_gadt_debruijn_rename_closure_growth () = Planning ("GADT-DEBRUIJN-RENAME-CLOSURE-GROWTH", Minutes 240u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_gadt_debruijn_rename_closure_growth_1 () = Note ("GADT-DEBRUIJN-RENAME-CLOSURE-GROWTH", 1u32, "2026-11-02T18:48Z", "compiler", "11-02 15:00 compiler: split from GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX (n35). On control and pack17 alike, the lab twin times out at 20 s on Fsharp, C, Rust and Delphi. With the index growth fixed by pack17, what still grows is the CLOSURE: `Lam body => Lam (rename (lift ren) body)` passes a fresh static closure lift(lift(...ren)) at every level, and a static closure is part of the join-point key. Two candidate fixes: (a) in the sample, make ren a runtime value (`dyn`), the Spiral idiom for a closure argument to a recursive join point; (b) in the compiler, extend the ancestry rule so a key whose closure argument strictly contains the ancestor's closure (the same body applied to it) is passed dynamically. Try (a) first to confirm the diagnosis, then decide whether (b) is warranted. Expected value: 5, like gadt_growing_index_runtime.")
inl note_of_gadt_debruijn_rename_closure_growth_2 () = Note ("GADT-DEBRUIJN-RENAME-CLOSURE-GROWTH", 2u32, "2026-11-02T18:50Z", "compiler", "11-02 15:10 compiler: first probe of fix (a), on the pack17 candidate (scratchpad rename-dyn2 with samples/core as a sibling). Wrapping the closure argument as `!!!!Dyn(lift ren)` and `!!!!Dyn(ren)` fails \"Cannot apply a forall with a term\" at line 29: lift is forall-polymorphic (\\* n m.), so `lift ren` is a type-polymorphic partial application that Dyn cannot take. The diagnosis is NOT yet confirmed or refuted. Next probe: a monomorphic wrapper at each level, e.g. `\\ (v : var (succ n)) => lift ren v` with its type annotated, then Dyn of that. (The samples core has no `dyn` helper; the lib has one.)")
inl note_of_gadt_debruijn_rename_closure_growth_3 () = Note ("GADT-DEBRUIJN-RENAME-CLOSURE-GROWTH", 3u32, "2026-11-02T21:40Z", "compiler", "11-02 17:45 compiler: DIAGNOSIS CONFIRMED, two-part fix drafted. Probes on the installed compiler (pack18, GADT fix live), scratchpad rename-dyn2: (1) `!!!!Dyn(lift ren)` -> \"Cannot apply a forall with a term\" (lift is forall-polymorphic). (2) A Dyn of an unannotated lambda -> \"Cannot convert a function that is not annotated into a runtime variable\". (3) Binding the wrapper annotated, `:= ren2 : var (succ n) -> var (succ m) = \\ v => lift ren v` then `rename (!!!!Dyn(ren2)) body`, gets PAST the closure growth (no timeout): specialization now reaches a fixed point and stops at \"Cannot apply an argument of type var .phantom-index to a function of type: var (succ zero) -> var (succ (succ zero))\". That is the runtime-closure apply arm (spiral_compiler.fs ~10232) checking domain = arg type strictly, while the argument arrives as the erased var (the whole var family is laid out at the mark since pack17, so both have one representation). FIX: (a) SAMPLE: the growing closure argument becomes an annotated runtime value (the Spiral idiom for a closure passed to a recursive join point); draft kit drafts GADT-DEBRUIJN-RENAME-CLOSURE-GROWTH\\main.spi. (b) COMPILER: the apply arm also accepts phantom_erase domain = phantom_erase arg (drafts ...\\1-apply-erased-domain.kitdo, 1 replacement, applies on live). It rides pack19 after its current review; expected exit 5 on C/Rust/F#/Delphi.")
inl note_of_gadt_debruijn_rename_closure_growth_4 () = Note ("GADT-DEBRUIJN-RENAME-CLOSURE-GROWTH", 4u32, "2026-11-02T22:54Z", "compiler", "11-02 18:50 compiler: fix (b) REFUTED as sufficient. Node t1101, the twin (draft main.spi with the annotated Dyn wrapper, samples core as a sibling), Rust: control (pack18) exit 5 with the strict-apply type error at Var (ren v); candidate with 1-apply-erased-domain gets past the check and then TIMES OUT at 180 s. So the Dyn wrapper stops growth only up to the apply check; once accepted, specialization grows again. Dropped from pack19 (A/B was 0 diffs, but it is no fix alone and turns a clear error into a hang). Next: log join-point keys on the twin past the apply to see which key component grows (a closure capturing ren, or an index the erasure misses), then choose between (b2) passing the erased closure type through the key and the ancestry rule for closures.")
inl note_of_gadt_debruijn_rename_closure_growth_5 () = Note ("GADT-DEBRUIJN-RENAME-CLOSURE-GROWTH", 5u32, "2026-11-03T00:12Z", "compiler", "11-02 20:10 compiler: ROOT CAUSE FOUND, fix = sample + 05. A key probe (SPIRAL_PROBE_KEYS eprintfn at both join-point key sites, node twinprobe) showed only CLOSURE keys, 17 in 45 s, each with growing scope types [succ^k zero; succ^(k+1) zero] and no method key: rename was ':= ^' (inline recursion over the runtime term), so every Lam level inlined another rename. The ancestry rule only runs at method join points, so nothing stopped it. With ':~ ^ rename' (a join point, as size already is), node twinvar: installed pack18 still fails (Var (ren v) strict apply, domain var (succ zero) vs the erased var .phantom-index); cand05 (1-apply-erased-domain) compiles both the original Lam arm (rename (lift ren) body) and the Dyn variant in about 8 s with 32 method keys, the second rename key at [.phantom-index; .phantom-index] (ancestry erasure), and the Rust exe exits 5 as expected. So the Dyn wrapper is unnecessary. Tree: samples/gadt_debruijn_rename_runtime/main.spi line 13 is now ':~ ^ rename' (unstaged); the draft main.spi with the Dyn wrapper is obsolete. Next: 05 rides pack20 with matrix rows for this sample (C, Rust, Fsharp, Delphi), run on the node first.")
inl note_of_gadt_debruijn_rename_closure_growth_6 () = Note ("GADT-DEBRUIJN-RENAME-CLOSURE-GROWTH", 6u32, "2026-11-03T00:57Z", "compiler", "11-02 20:5x compiler: pack20 review (node t1101, base pack19 source C6AA54D854D9E969 + 5 programs, core F8A44CBB5893DB2C): the twin (':~ ^ rename' tree sample) fails control on Rust and C (exit 5, strict apply) and passes the candidate: compile ok, Rust exe 5, C exe 5. Examples Rust/C/Zig/Gleam 1981 rows and contracts 728 rows vs pack19: 0 behavior cells, 0 DISAGREE; residual diffs C 22 + 1 (ParamEscape) and Wgsl 3 (heap prelude) only.")
inl note_of_gadt_debruijn_rename_closure_growth_7 () = Note ("GADT-DEBRUIJN-RENAME-CLOSURE-GROWTH", 7u32, "2026-11-03T02:48Z", "compiler", "11-02 23:0x compiler: DONE. 1-apply-erased-domain is live in pack20 (installed 22:46). With the sample fix (:~ ^ rename, a join point), samples/gadt_debruijn_rename_runtime compiles on the installed compiler and the C exe exits 5. The suites run it by default (no matrix list needed).")
