inl note_of_kit_edition3_respell_1 () = Note ("KIT-EDITION3-RESPELL", 1u32, "2026-11-02T20:20Z", "kit", "kit 11-02 16:2x SIZED with pack17's own checker on vultr: `SpiralCompiler format --check --respell-drift src` over kit reports 10,017 respell-drift sites in 641 files (main counted 3,313 with strings stripped). The top packages are kit_lint_model 207, agile_todo_model 191, kit_standards 183, kit_machine_windows 173, agile_task_next 153, kit_coverage_smoke 150, rust_std_fs_mutation 142 and kit_bindings_model 124. PLAN: the fixer is `format --spelling symbols <files>` (the checker and fixer agree exactly, per main). Scope it to kit/src only, never the repo root; state/ spelling is guarded separately by KIT-STATE-SPELLING-GUARD. Run it in its own train after TRAIN13: (1) apply on a scratch copy of src, (2) regenerate every owner and require byte-identical generated .rs except for spelling-invariant noise (the respell must not change semantics), (3) all notebooks green, (4) re-check 0 drift, (5) census ratchet 0 regressions. It's one mechanical change, landed as one unstaged changeset for the user to commit separately from feature work.")
inl polyglot_readers_port () = Task ("POLYGLOT-READERS-PORT", "Port mechahaze-album1 and tapas2 off polyglot's dib/Supervisor/Builder/Fable pipeline onto deps/spiral + BuildSpiral to Rust, the way dice's contract was", Story, P1, D4, 100u32, Done, "", "polyglot,migration,mechahaze-album1,tapas2,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_polyglot_readers_port () = Planning ("POLYGLOT-READERS-PORT", Minutes 240u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_polyglot_readers_port_1 () = Note ("POLYGLOT-READERS-PORT", 1u32, "2026-11-02T20:56Z", "main", "main 11-02 16:5x: filed from POLYGLOT-RETIRE step 1. A path rewrite is not enough for these two: apps/album1/build.ps1 and tapas2 contract/build.ps1 run polyglot's Supervisor (fsx), Builder (Fable.Core persist), DibParser and BuildFable -> rs, and their Cargo.toml path-depend on polyglot's fable-library-rust. dice already made this move (dice_contract.spi + BuildSpiral -> .rs + cells run_notebook). Plan per repo: (1) dib-export the .dib to a tracked .spi (album1 has 12 spiral cells, old-era lib names like optionm'); (2) build.ps1 = deps/spiral core.ps1 + lib.ps1, BuildSpiral spi -> rs, cargo build, drop the Supervisor/Builder/Fable steps and the fable_library_rust dep; (3) init.ps1 clones/links spiral, drops polyglot; (4) prove the build on the node. vault needs only 3 core.ps1 dot-sources plus the DirTreeHtml path.")
inl note_of_polyglot_readers_port_2 () = Note ("POLYGLOT-READERS-PORT", 2u32, "2026-11-02T20:58Z", "main", "main 11-02 17:0x vault DONE (unstaged, 3 files, 4 lines): scripts/init.ps1 + workflow.ps1 dot-source ../../spiral/scripts/core.ps1 (alphabet's init clones spiral as a sibling); publish.ps1 dot-sources ../deps/alphabet/deps/spiral/scripts/core.ps1 and gets the renderer the dice/polyglot way: & (pwsh ../deps/alphabet/deps/spiral/scripts/dir-tree-html.ps1 | Select-Object -Last 1) --dir ../dist --html ../dist/index.html. Proof: all 3 parse; from vault/scripts both core paths exist, core.ps1 dot-sources (Invoke-Block defined), and the resolver returns C:\\home\\git\\dir-tree-html\\dist\\dir-tree-html.exe (exists). 0 polyglot sites left in vault/scripts (remaining hits are docs prose: portuguese.md etymology, cv, spiral.md). Full publish not run (it renders the whole vault through alphabet's documents binary).")
inl note_of_polyglot_readers_port_3 () = Note ("POLYGLOT-READERS-PORT", 3u32, "2026-11-02T21:09Z", "main", "main 11-02 17:1x mechahaze-album1 DONE (unstaged), as a removal instead of a port: apps/album1/mechahaze_album1.dib is alphabet's apps/documents in old syntax (same 7 functions get_args/get_command/crowbook/hangul/fix_paths/files_fn/run, same CLI, its traces even say 'documents.*'; the only drift is documents' -H short flag for hangul-spec), and nothing in album1's publish ran its binary. So: DELETED apps/album1 (6 tracked files: .dib, Cargo.toml/.lock, build.ps1, main.rs, package.spiproj) and scripts/outdated.ps1 (its one CheckToml pointed at a path that never existed, ../apps/mechahaze_album1); scripts/init.ps1 clones alphabet (whose init brings spiral + dir-tree-html) and links deps/alphabet, the vault way; build.ps1 builds ../deps/alphabet/apps/documents; publish/workflow dot-source deps/alphabet/deps/spiral core.ps1 and get the renderer via spiral's dir-tree-html.ps1. 0 polyglot hits left (git grep). Ignored build outputs (dist 139 MB + target 265 MB) archived to kit\\archive\\20261102-171500-mechahaze-album1-app. Proof: all 4 scripts parse; from scripts/ both core paths dot-source, documents/build.ps1 exists, renderer resolves to an existing exe; documents itself is proven on the node (POLYGLOT-RETIRE n16). NEXT: tapas2 (a NEAR contract like dice: real port to BuildSpiral).")
inl note_of_polyglot_readers_port_4 () = Note ("POLYGLOT-READERS-PORT", 4u32, "2026-11-02T21:22Z", "main", "main 11-02 17:3x tapas2 DONE + PROVEN on the node (fresh clone of i574n/tapas2 at 0be74ea0, synced -Force, deps/spiral junction): contract build exit 0, spiral-tests 3/3 passed (state, new, contribute_seed), BuildSpiral regenerated contract/tapas2_contract.rs (720 lines, 0 polyglot paths; was 1,893 lines of Fable output), cargo built dist/tapas2.wasm 132,721 B. The .rs and the regenerated Cargo.lock (-531 lines, fable deps gone) were copied back to the box tree, unstaged. The contract is dice's current shape trimmed to tapas2's 3 NEAR methods (new, contribute_seed, contribute_seed_borsh) + the OldState migration; Cargo pins = dice's (near-sdk =5.11.0, indexmap =2.11.4, contract edition 2021). Also fixed on the way: remote.ps1 sync to a machine WITHOUT a checkout. (1) It now git-inits the remote source. (2) The real bug: '\\ = if (..) { @(..) } else { @(''HEAD'') }' unrolls to the string HEAD, and '@range' splatted it as H E A D ('ambiguous argument H'); now '@(if ..)'. Proven: first-time sync of mechahaze-album1 -> REMOTE-SYNC-OK. All three readers (vault, mechahaze-album1, tapas2) are done.")
inl compiler_c_borrowed_params () = Task ("COMPILER-C-BORROWED-PARAMS", "C backend borrow inference for read-only heap parameters of join points: no incref at the call, no decref at return, so inner loops and parallel bodies do no refcount traffic", Story, P1, D6, 90u32, Active, "TRANSFORMER-CPU-REFERENCE", "compiler,backends,transformer,memory,team:tibia,tibia", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_compiler_c_borrowed_params () = Planning ("COMPILER-C-BORROWED-PARAMS", Minutes 600u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_compiler_c_borrowed_params_1 () = Note ("COMPILER-C-BORROWED-PARAMS", 1u32, "2026-11-02T21:25Z", "tibia", "11-02 tibia: filed as the first measurable slice of COMPILER-PERCEUS-FBIP. Evidence: COMPILER-EMIT-PARALLEL-LOOPS n2 (node, stories15M): the same 16-row-chunked matmul with dot over raw `float *` (no refc at the call) scales 2.3x at 4 threads, while the dot that takes owned arrays does not scale at all (shared counter cache line). Rule to infer: a join point's array (or other heap) parameter is BORROWED when the body only reads it (index, length), passes it to self tail calls in the same position, or passes it to callees that borrow it too; never stores, returns, captures it, or passes it as owned. Call sites then skip the incref and the callee skips the decref at return. Gate: transformer token parity on C (all 3 checkpoints), the C sample matrix green with regenerated goldens, and the A/B (seq single-thread and par-4) against pack17.")
inl note_of_compiler_c_borrowed_params_2 () = Note ("COMPILER-C-BORROWED-PARAMS", 2u32, "2026-11-02T21:28Z", "tibia", "11-02 tibia: compiler lane's review of the rule (relayed 17:3x, compiler session): a heap/array param of a join point is BORROWED (no incref at the call, no decref at the callee's end) when all hold: (1) NO ESCAPE in the callee: never stored into a heap/mutable/array/union payload, never captured by a closure, never returned, never passed to a callee position that is itself owned; (2) THE CALLER HOLDS IT LIVE across the call (automatic when the argument is a variable the caller owns and keeps using, or a borrowed param of the caller); (3) RECURSION: (1) as a greatest fixpoint over the join-point call graph: start all params borrowed, demote on any escape including passing to an owned callee param; self tail loops then keep borrowed args across iterations for free. Compute on the TypedBind bodies once per join point (codegenC's method memo), never by matching emitted C. A/B: the transformer's matmul_rows/dot, where incref/decref per call must drop to 0 for the read-only weight arrays. Compiler offers to review the draft. Also: COMPILER-EMIT-PARALLEL-LOOPS 1-c-atomic-refc.kitdo is accepted into pack19 (after Step B extern calls and CELLS-CHECK-DEFAULTS); its A/B checks non-threaded C output byte-identical over examples + contracts. The marker text \"#define SPIRAL_THREADS 1\" must be emitted exactly, from one place (lib parallel).")
inl note_of_compiler_c_borrowed_params_3 () = Note ("COMPILER-C-BORROWED-PARAMS", 3u32, "2026-11-02T21:48Z", "tibia", "11-02 tibia, SHAPE AGREED with the compiler lane (17:5x): build the analysis as ONE backend-independent pass over TypedBind, `ParamEscape`, computed once per join-point key beside the method/closure memos, producing per (jp key, param index) a union `ParamUse = Borrowed | Escapes` by the greatest-fixpoint rule in n2. It lives outside codegenC. Consumers: codegenC (this item: skip incref at the call and decref at the callee end for Borrowed) and codegenRust (compiler's RUST-MOVE-ON-LAST-USE n14: &T / no Rc clone for Borrowed; 497 Rc field clones on read-only params there). Delivery: a kitdo program in %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\spiral\\COMPILER-C-BORROWED-PARAMS\\, reviewed by the compiler lane.")
inl note_of_compiler_c_borrowed_params_4 () = Note ("COMPILER-C-BORROWED-PARAMS", 4u32, "2026-11-02T22:00Z", "tibia", "11-02 tibia, FIRST DRAFT handed to the compiler lane for review (18:0x): %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\spiral\\COMPILER-C-BORROWED-PARAMS\\1-param-escape-c.kitdo, 8 replacements, kit do --dry-run ok on the live tree (17:58, sha 1b00a6b3 -> cbbd2bad), NOT compiled yet (a scratch build.ps1 writes the shared spiral-bin compiler path, so the build goes through the compiler lane's pack flow). Contents: ParamUse = Borrowed | Escapes; paramEscapeScan (per body: escapes = TyLocalReturnData, every TyMacro term, every TyOp except ArrayIndex and the ArrayIndexSet target, stores, closure capture/apply, passing to an Escapes callee position; caller-side demotion when a Borrowed position receives a non-borrowed var in a tail call or a self call); paramEscape (greatest fixpoint over all method bodies, closures and main scanned for demotions); refc_prepass' with the lookup (refc_prepass = no borrowing, so Python is unchanged); codegenC: borrowed_param, binds_start through refc_prepass', method_templ binds_start (owned_params x). First slice tracks YArray and string params only.")
inl note_of_compiler_c_borrowed_params_5 () = Note ("COMPILER-C-BORROWED-PARAMS", 5u32, "2026-11-02T22:17Z", "tibia", "11-02 tibia, compiler lane REVIEW of draft 1 (18:2x): shape sound; it compiles and A/Bs the candidate on the node after pack19b in a private SPIRAL_BIN_CACHE_DIR (C:\\home\\remote-stage\\paramesc\\cache), then residual + behavior A/B on examples C and contracts, and sends the DLL for the matmul_rows/dot refcount check and transformer 260K/15M token runs. Findings: (1) no ABI-visible JPMethod in C (no Export arm in CodegenC, main is the only entry, TyBackend nesting unsupported, closures are JPClosure), so no position must stay owned; (2) the escape set and op match are complete; the tail demotion is REQUIRED (refc_prefix nets g_op +1 against g_op_decr -1 before the op prints, so an owned var in a skipped slot would be decremented before the call); self = Some key covers self calls nested in TyIf/UnionUnbox/IntSwitch/Do under a let (op_self_tail); skipping TyWhile cond args is safe (cond methods print with binds_start [||]); scan and codegen read the same DeadPureLetElision bodies and indices line up with self_continue's map2. Perf follow-up: each fixpoint round rescans everything; switch to a worklist if transformer compile time moves.")
inl note_of_compiler_c_borrowed_params_6 () = Note ("COMPILER-C-BORROWED-PARAMS", 6u32, "2026-11-02T22:54Z", "compiler", "11-02 18:50 compiler: reviewed tibia's 1-param-escape-c.kitdo (8 replacements). Sound: C has no Export arm and TyBackend is rejected, so no JPMethod is ABI-visible; the tail demotion is required (refc_prefix nets g_op +1 with g_op_decr -1 before the op); self-call rule covers op_self_tail nesting; TyWhile cond args safe (cond methods own nothing); scan bodies = codegenMethodBody bodies. Next: node build into a separate cache + examples C/contracts A/B, then tibia runs matmul_rows/dot refcounts and transformer 260K/15M.")
inl note_of_compiler_c_borrowed_params_7 () = Note ("COMPILER-C-BORROWED-PARAMS", 7u32, "2026-11-02T23:27Z", "tibia", "11-02 19:3x tibia, A/B of the compiler lane's ParamEscape candidate (node t1101 private cache: SpiralCompiler.dll 6B9374835A1BBACF, core 0F933F3E705784B6; their A/B: examples C 557 rows and contracts 728 rows, 0 behavior cells, 0 DISAGREE) on apps/transformer (job paramesc-ab-1): generated C 80,271 -> 74,973 bytes; increfs in the whole file 180 -> 22; dot10: decrefs 2 -> 0; matmul_rows9: increfs 2 -> 0, decrefs 3 -> 0. The candidate's dot/matmul_rows are a strict subset of the installed output (the same loops minus the refcount lines). Greedy tokens identical to run.c on stories260K, stories15M and stories42M. First timing (fixed arm order run-O3, installed, candidate, node at 100% load) had the candidate last and slower in every round (18.5/19.1/15.5 s vs 14.0/16.3/11.2 s); since the code is a strict subset, that reads as an order/thermal artifact, and a rotated-order rerun (6 rounds, each arm in each position twice) is running.")
inl note_of_compiler_c_borrowed_params_8 () = Note ("COMPILER-C-BORROWED-PARAMS", 8u32, "2026-11-03T00:00Z", "tibia", "11-02 19:5x tibia, TIMING RESOLVED on a quiet box (vultr, load 0.3-0.8, gcc -O3 -march=native, stories15M, 256 steps, rotated order, identical output): installed 3,144-3,326 ms vs ParamEscape candidate 3,654-3,907 ms, i.e. the candidate was really ~18% slower although its C is a strict subset (the 68-line diff is only refcount lines). Cause (gcc -fopt-info): installed keeps a separate matmul_rows9.constprop.0 with dot10 inlined and the constant args (0, 0, 0.0f) propagated; in the candidate the leaner dot10/matmul_rows9 are cheap enough to be inlined into layers_from6 at 5+ sites and scores_from13, so the hot loop is duplicated into one huge function (vaddss 62 -> 139, vfmadd 20 -> 38 in the file) and the constprop clone disappears. -fno-inline-functions does not restore it (5.1 s). PROOF of the cause: the candidate's C with __attribute__((noinline)) on exactly dot10 and matmul_rows9 runs 3,205/3,373/3,245 ms vs installed 3,291/3,298/3,219 ms (equal or slightly faster), same output. So ParamEscape is correct and neutral-to-positive once hot self-loops are not inlined into giant callers. Proposed codegenC follow-up for the same pack: emit `__attribute__((noinline))` on self-tail-loop methods (the while(1) shape), or on those with borrowed array params; measure on examples C for regressions.")
inl note_of_compiler_c_borrowed_params_9 () = Note ("COMPILER-C-BORROWED-PARAMS", 9u32, "2026-11-03T00:23Z", "compiler", "11-02 20:2x compiler: program 2-noinline-borrowed-loops.kitdo (1 replacement) puts __attribute__((noinline)) on self-tail-loop methods with a Borrowed param only. Node paramesc2 (1 + 2): examples C 557 rows and contracts 728 rows, 0 behavior cells, 0 DISAGREE, the same 22 + 1 C residual diffs as 1 alone; 10 samples' main.c carry it. DLL handed to tibia for the vultr 15M timing; both programs ride pack20 if that is at installed speed.")
inl note_of_compiler_c_borrowed_params_10 () = Note ("COMPILER-C-BORROWED-PARAMS", 10u32, "2026-11-03T00:46Z", "tibia", "11-02 20:4x tibia, paramesc2 VERIFIED (compiler lane's build of programs 1 + 2, ParamEscape plus __attribute__((noinline)) only on self-tail-loop methods with a Borrowed param; node t1101 cache paramesc2, core 4FAABCB162E1FD68; their A/B: examples C 557 rows + contracts 728 rows, 0 behavior cells, 0 DISAGREE, the same 22 + 1 C residual diffs as program 1). On apps/transformer the generated C has noinline on 23 methods. Quiet vultr (load 0.3-1.0), gcc -O3 -march=native, stories15M, 256 steps, 6 rotated rounds, output byte-identical: paramesc2 3,422/3,216/3,285/3,262/3,254/3,314 ms (mean 3,292); installed 3,528/3,233/3,361/3,215/3,185/3,331 (mean 3,309); ParamEscape alone 3,709-4,064 (mean 3,858). Verdict: the regression is gone, paramesc2 matches installed speed while doing no refcount traffic in the hot loops (the precondition for scaling parallel matmul without atomics on shared arrays). Ready for pack20 per the compiler lane.")
inl note_of_compiler_c_borrowed_params_11 () = Note ("COMPILER-C-BORROWED-PARAMS", 11u32, "2026-11-03T09:26Z", "tibia", "11-03 05:2x tibia, ParamEscape PAYS AT -Ofast (TRANSFORMER-CPU-SPEED n7): on the shape-specialized transformer, the paramesc2 build (refc++ sites 282 -> 171, output byte-identical) runs at 94% of llama2.c runfast against 90% for the same source on the 11-02 installed compiler, so about +5% single-thread on quiet vultr, 4 arms x 8 rounds as a Latin square. Together with n-earlier's -O3 parity (3,292 vs 3,309 ms after the noinline fix), it is a neutral-to-positive change everywhere measured; rides pack20 as planned.")
inl note_of_compiler_c_borrowed_params_12 () = Note ("COMPILER-C-BORROWED-PARAMS", 12u32, "2026-11-03T10:43Z", "tibia", "11-03 06:5x tibia, A COST OF THE noinline RULE found on the transformer's hottest loop (TRANSFORMER-CPU-SPEED n11): with the dot kept out of line, every matmul row pays the AVX horizontal-sum epilogue plus vzeroupper and call/ret, about 6-7% of dot samples, a large part of the remaining ~10% gap to llama2.c runfast, whose dot is inlined into the row loop. Suggestion for the rule's scope: exempt self-loops whose trip count is a compile-time constant (they cannot lose a constprop clone, since the constant is already folded), and re-measure the 11-02 regression case.")
inl note_of_compiler_c_borrowed_params_13 () = Note ("COMPILER-C-BORROWED-PARAMS", 13u32, "2026-11-03T10:44Z", "tibia", "11-03 06:5x tibia, MEASURED (TRANSFORMER-CPU-SPEED n12): removing noinline from the 2 constant-trip dot functions in the generated transformer C (sed; output byte-identical) speeds the whole run by 5.6% (895.5 -> 845.0 ms mean, 8 rotated rounds on vultr) and brings it to ~99% of llama2.c runfast. Evidence for scoping the noinline-on-self-loop rule: emit noinline only when the self-loop's trip count is NOT a compile-time constant. The 11-02 regression it fixed came from gcc inlining the lean loops into layers_from and losing the constprop clone; a constant-count loop has its constant folded already, so that failure mode should not apply. Please re-measure the 11-02 case (3,292 vs 3,309 ms) with the scoped rule before shipping.")
inl note_of_compiler_c_borrowed_params_14 () = Note ("COMPILER-C-BORROWED-PARAMS", 14u32, "2026-11-03T10:55Z", "compiler", "11-03 07:0x compiler: tibia n12-n13 accepted. Program COMPILER-C-BORROWED-PARAMS\\3-inline-constant-trip-loops.kitdo (2 replacements on pack24 live C1D78AAEB261EB74, anchors unique): constant_trip x is true when the method body binds a <, <=, > or >= of a parameter against an integer literal above 1, and borrowed_loop (the noinline rule) now also requires not (constant_trip x). sized_array.dot_at (bound type_lit_to_lit n, a literal in C) loses noinline; f32_kernels.dot (bound n, a parameter: the 11-02 constprop case) and countdown loops to 0/1 keep it. Next: candidate build on node t1101 after job compiler-h2hn, examples C A/B (expect residual diffs only on constant-trip borrowed loops, 0 behavior), then tibia measures the transformer -Ofast and the 11-02 -O3 case on vultr with the candidate.")
inl note_of_compiler_c_borrowed_params_15 () = Note ("COMPILER-C-BORROWED-PARAMS", 15u32, "2026-11-03T11:02Z", "tibia", "11-03 07:0x tibia, COUNTER-EVIDENCE for the scoped-noinline rule (n13): it is MODEL-DEPENDENT. stories42M (pinned: 167,020,572 B, sha256 prefix 9f65a100; output matches runfast for 300 chars), the in-tree typed-config transformer built on vultr, 16 rounds x 3 arms rotated, load 0.3-1.0: runfast 2,269.4 ms mean (median 2,276); dot NOINLINE (as generated) 2,320.4 (97.8% of runfast); dot INLINED (sed) 2,391.5 (94.9%). So on 42M inlining the constant-trip dot is 3.1% SLOWER, the opposite of stories15M (n12: inlining 5.6% faster). Reading: with dim 512 / hidden 1376 the per-row horizontal-sum epilogue is a smaller share of each row, so the inlined loop's cost (code size or register pressure in the row loop) dominates. So 'constant trip => no noinline' gains on small rows and loses on wide ones; a trip-count threshold (inline only below N, e.g. 384, between 15M's 288 and 42M's 512) or keeping noinline and fusing several rows per call (TRANSFORMER-CPU-SPEED n11 lever 2) may be the better rule. Please hold pack24's replacement until both models are measured with the candidate.")
inl note_of_compiler_c_borrowed_params_16 () = Note ("COMPILER-C-BORROWED-PARAMS", 16u32, "2026-11-03T11:33Z", "tibia", "11-03 07:3x tibia, CANDIDATE MEASURED, RECOMMEND NOT SHIPPING the (1, 384] rule. compiler's DLL (C:\\home\\remote-stage\\compiler-noinline\\..., core 867B0BD81652261F) compiled the in-tree transformer (typed per-model config, 7 dot widths) to cand.c on the node: noinline dropped on exactly the 3 dot_l at or below 384 (288, 172, 64), kept on 4 (512, 768, 1376, 2048). base.c = the same C with noinline restored on those 3 (sed), so the pair differs ONLY in the rule's effect. vultr, 16 rounds x 3 arms rotated, gcc -Ofast -march=native, output byte-identical (and equal to the tree build) on both models. stories15M: runfast 807.6 mean / 798.5 median; base 864.2 / 877.5; cand 994.2 / 985.5, so cand is 15% SLOWER (its fastest run, 925, is slower than base's median). stories42M: runfast 2,102.7; base 2,175.9; cand 2,164.9 (no 42M dot affected; equal within noise). READING: the +5.6% of n13 came from a single-model specialized source; in the typed-config program (20 row kernels plus dispatch) inlining the small dots into rows_l, and those into the dispatch, costs more than the epilogue saves. Whether inlining pays depends on the surrounding code, not just the trip count, so a trip-count rule cannot get it right; keeping noinline on every Borrowed self-loop is the safe default. Current tree per model (base, noinline): 15M 93%, 42M 97%, 110M 97% of runfast.")
inl note_of_compiler_c_borrowed_params_17 () = Note ("COMPILER-C-BORROWED-PARAMS", 17u32, "2026-11-03T11:34Z", "compiler", "11-03 07:4x compiler: DECIDED NOT TO SHIP 3-inline-constant-trip-loops (kept in kit drafts as history). Evidence: node examples C -Native A/B vs pack24: no sample has a borrowed self-loop with a literal bound in (1, 384], 0 residual diffs (the one behavior cell, conversation_negative_unanswered_call timeout -> error, is a load timeout on the control arm of a negative sample); tibia on vultr (16 rounds x 3 arms, in-tree multi-model transformer, byte-identical output): stories15M base 864 ms vs candidate 994 ms (15% SLOWER), 42M unaffected (2,176 vs 2,165). The earlier +5.6% came from a single-model source; with 20 row kernels the inline cascade costs more than the epilogue saves, so the effect depends on surrounding code, not on trip count. RULE: noinline on every borrowed self-loop stays the default; revisit only with a whole-program measure (or a per-call-site attribute), not a trip-count threshold.")
inl note_of_compiler_c_borrowed_params_18 () = Note ("COMPILER-C-BORROWED-PARAMS", 18u32, "2026-11-04T04:17Z", "tibia", "11-04 00:17 tibia GROOM: parked to Planned (no note in 12 h). Borrow inference is in; the 3-inline-constant-trip-loops follow-up was declined on evidence (11-03 07:4x). NEXT: compiler names what the last 10% is or closes it; tibia has no open ask here.")
inl note_of_compiler_c_borrowed_params_19 () = Note ("COMPILER-C-BORROWED-PARAMS", 19u32, "2026-11-04T04:21Z", "tibia", "11-04 00:21 tibia: back to Active, percent kept. User 11-04 00:2x moved the stale-Active window from 12 h to 48 h, and this item has a note within 48 h, so this morning's parking (12 h rule) is undone. The next step in the 00:2x note still holds.")
inl note_of_compiler_c_borrowed_params_20 () = Note ("COMPILER-C-BORROWED-PARAMS", 20u32, "2026-11-04T16:58Z", "tibia", "11-04 12:58 tibia: the last 10% has a concrete shape now. Compiler filed C-BORROWED-LOOP-INLINE from tibia's 11-04 measurements: the C backend's noinline sits on borrowed self-tail loops (added after the ParamEscape review, when gcc inlined dot10/matmul_rows9 at 5+ sites and the result ran ~18% slower). On the Q8 path the single-site dot_i8 wants inlining (strict -O3: output identical to runq.c, 1033 -> 853 ms), while the multi-site fp32 dot_l36 (3 sites) and dot_l30 (4) do not. Candidate rule from compiler: noinline only for borrowed loops with 2+ call sites. That rule is the natural close of this borrow-inference item; tibia offered to measure it on vultr when it lands.")
inl cells_memo_sibling_modules () = Task ("CELLS-MEMO-SIBLING-MODULES", "Cells memo key ignores sibling package modules: editing oracle.spi (imported by transformer.spi) still memo-hits transformer's tests, reporting passes that never ran against the new code", Story, P1, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "cells,memo,proof,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_cells_memo_sibling_modules () = Planning ("CELLS-MEMO-SIBLING-MODULES", Minutes 90u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_cells_memo_sibling_modules_1 () = Note ("CELLS-MEMO-SIBLING-MODULES", 1u32, "2026-11-02T21:51Z", "tibia", "Found 11-02 17:4x by tibia on the node (mirror C:\\home\\remote\\tibia-gates3, run_notebook.ps1 --path apps/transformer/transformer.spi). apps/transformer is a two-module package (spiral.toml modules: oracle, transformer). Run 1 (job transformer-cells-1): 4/4 passed, Memo=miss. Run 2 (job transformer-cells-mutant): oracle.spi changed (\"too high.\" -> \"too low.\" inside the expected text that test greedy_stories260k_matches_run_c compares against), transformer.spi untouched: 4/4 PASSED, every row Memo=hit, so the changed module was not part of the key. Run 3 (job transformer-cells-mutant-2), same mutant with SPIRAL_CELLS_MEMO=0: greedy_stories260k_matches_run_c FAILED, the other 3 passed. So a memo hit can report a pass for code whose imported sibling module changed. DoD: the memo key covers every module of the package that the cell's program reaches (or the package's whole module set), with a test that edits a sibling module and expects a miss.")
inl note_of_cells_memo_sibling_modules_2 () = Note ("CELLS-MEMO-SIBLING-MODULES", 2u32, "2026-11-02T21:57Z", "main", "main 11-02 18:0x ROOT CAUSE: the package is in the key (transformer.spi line 1 is '///- --package .', canonicalized by canon_flags), but Memo.key only hashes the package blocks that reached_package_print reaches. Its reference analysis knew only edition-2 spellings: @qualified_name = ident(.ident)+ (so 'oracle::greedy_text' never qualified), @open_statement = 'open m' (not ':< m'), and the token splits were on '.'. So oracle.spi's blocks were never reached or hashed, and editing it kept the key. FIX (unstaged): apps/cells/lib/spiral/cells/memo.ex, 5 lines. Both regexes accept '.' or '::'; the opens match 'open' or ':<' and normalize '::' to '.'; references/heads split on '.' and '::'. TEST: memo_test.exs gets 'edition 3 spellings reach package modules too', the edition-3 twin of the dotted reach test (unqualified unchanged, ':< other' changes, 'other::a' changes, 'record::slice' unchanged, 'user::through' through ':< other' changes). PROOF: in flight on node mirror memofix: an A/B mix test (A = old analysis, the new test must fail; B = fixed, all pass).")
inl note_of_cells_memo_sibling_modules_3 () = Note ("CELLS-MEMO-SIBLING-MODULES", 3u32, "2026-11-02T22:02Z", "main", "main 11-02 18:1x PROVEN, DoD met. A/B in ONE job on node mirror memofix (spiral 194a1916 + tree, digest 5BECA51E1CC8A306; per-mirror MIX_BUILD_PATH; cells deps junctioned from the live checkout): A = old reference analysis: mix test memo_test.exs exit 2, 'Result: 26/27 passed', the ONLY failure is the new 'edition 3 spellings reach package modules too' row. B = fixed memo.ex: exit 0, 'Result: 27 passed'. So the dotted-syntax rows are unchanged and the edition-3 rows now key sibling modules. Live Cells picks it up on its next mix compile after the user's push or a local recompile; until then, edition-3 multi-module gates keep SPIRAL_CELLS_MEMO=0 (told tibia).")
inl kit_train_incremental_regen () = Task ("KIT-TRAIN-INCREMENTAL-REGEN", "release trains regenerate only changed owners: key regen on the train's lib snapshot, not a live lib that moves mid-run", Story, P1, D3, 0u32, Planned, "", "team:kit,kit,train,regen,speed", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_kit_train_incremental_regen () = Planning ("KIT-TRAIN-INCREMENTAL-REGEN", Minutes 120u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_kit_train_incremental_regen_1 () = Note ("KIT-TRAIN-INCREMENTAL-REGEN", 1u32, "2026-11-02T21:55Z", "kit", "kit 11-02 17:5x DIAGNOSIS: each release train spends ~57 min regenerating all 281 owners although few changed (TRAIN13b: 0 of 113 skipped so far after TRAIN13 recorded keys at 17:02). test-regeneration -Incremental keys each owner on compiler hash + EVERY lib/spiral file hash + its closure's src files (relative paths, so that part is fine). The keys are computed once at the START of a run but written at the END. TRAIN13's regen ran 16:05-17:02, and lib/spiral file_system.spi + convert.spi changed at 16:14 (another lane's land), so the 281 keys it stored described a lib that no longer existed. With lanes landing into lib all day, no key ever matches. FIX DESIGN: (1) read the lib from the train's own lib snapshot (.cache/release-tree/<name>-lib), never the live lib, so keys and compiles see one lib version (this also closes the notebooks-read-live-lib leak); (2) recompute the shared lib hash right before recording keys and refuse to record (log \"lib changed during regen; keys not recorded\") if it moved; (3) narrower keys (only the lib modules a closure reaches) are a later optimisation, because whole-lib keying is the conservative-correct rule. Expected effect: a train after a train with no lib change regenerates only the changed owners (minutes instead of ~1 h). Proof: two consecutive trains on an unchanged lib, the second showing \"unchanged inputs; compile skipped\" for every untouched owner and byte-identical outputs.")
inl note_of_kit_train_incremental_regen_2 () = Note ("KIT-TRAIN-INCREMENTAL-REGEN", 2u32, "2026-11-02T21:58Z", "kit", "kit 11-02 18:0x BLOCKER for fix (1): the Spiral compiler takes no lib override (no SPIRAL_LIB or similar in apps/compiler; SPIRAL_WORKSPACE_ROOT only feeds runtime-compat). It resolves lib/spiral from its own bundle location (apps/compiler/tmp/../../../lib/spiral), so a train can't point regen at its frozen lib copy without a compiler-side lib-root option. Ask the compiler lane for one (env SPIRAL_LIB_ROOT honoured by package resolution of spiral-/core-). Until then fix (2) alone, refusing to record keys when the lib moved mid-run, only avoids recording stale keys and saves no time, so it waits for (1).")
inl note_of_kit_train_incremental_regen_3 () = Note ("KIT-TRAIN-INCREMENTAL-REGEN", 3u32, "2026-11-02T22:30Z", "kit", "kit 11-02 18:3x PARALLEL REGEN landed (unstaged, for the next train; TRAIN13b still runs the serial snapshot copy): test-regeneration.ps1 -Parallel <1..8> (default 1 = unchanged) schedules up to n compiler processes and never starts an owner while one of its link: units is running or pending (rows are already link-first). release-train passes -Parallel 3. PROOF: vultr (2 cores) gave byte-identical outputs for 12 owners including link pairs (kit_rec_cycle/kit_standards_scan, the agile_claim_* crates): diff -r found nothing. The box (8 cores, sharing with TRAIN13b's serial regen) went from 623 s serial to 315 s parallel for the same 12 owners (2.0x). A full 281-owner regen should drop from ~57 min to ~25 min. This is independent of the incremental-key fix, which still needs a compiler lib-root option.")
inl note_of_kit_train_incremental_regen_4 () = Note ("KIT-TRAIN-INCREMENTAL-REGEN", 4u32, "2026-11-03T01:17Z", "compiler", "11-02 21:2x compiler: compiler half DONE as a candidate. SPIRAL_LIB_ROOT=<dir>: a named non-relative package entry (spiral-, dice-) resolves to <dir>/<name> when that folder exists, else today's packageDir resolution; relative entries and |core- untouched (SPIRAL_COMPILER_PACKAGE_DIR already covers core). Program %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\spiral\\KIT-TRAIN-INCREMENTAL-REGEN\\1-spiral-lib-root.kitdo (2 replacements, SpiProj.schema resolve_packages). Node t1101: consumer of sm'::line_count exits 2 unset, 99 with a lib copy whose line_count returns 99; A/B unset vs pack20 0 cells. Candidate DLL C:\\home\\remote-stage\\pack20\\cache-b\\...\\SpiralCompiler.dll (core AF5E086014D2DD1C), handed to kit for the two-train vultr A/B; it ships in pack20b.")
inl note_of_kit_train_incremental_regen_5 () = Note ("KIT-TRAIN-INCREMENTAL-REGEN", 5u32, "2026-11-03T01:18Z", "kit", "kit 11-02 21:2x KIT SIDE WIRED (unstaged): test-regeneration.ps1 -LibRoot <dir holding spiral/> exports SPIRAL_LIB_ROOT and keys the incremental cache on that frozen lib. release-train passes the train's lib snapshot (Split-Path of -Lib, i.e. <train>-lib/lib) only when KIT_REGEN_FROZEN_LIB=1. That opt-in is deliberate: a compiler without SPIRAL_LIB_ROOT would compile against the live lib while the keys describe the frozen one, which makes a skip decision unsafe. Enable it once pack20b, compiler's candidate (host 6B30D2F521660878, core AF5E086014D2DD1C, proven on t1101), is the installed compiler. A/B PLAN on the node with that DLL: two consecutive test-regeneration runs with -LibRoot on an unchanged lib snapshot. Pass = the second run logs \"unchanged inputs; compile skipped\" for every owner with outputs byte-identical to the first; then touch one lib file and require that only its dependent owners recompile.")
