inl note_of_regex_staged_tables_1 () = Note ("REGEX-STAGED-TABLES", 1u32, "2026-11-03T17:07Z", "main", "main 11-03 13:1x SPLIT from REGEX-ENGINES-LAZY-STAGED. Why separate: the bench's timed region already excludes table construction (TableArm 'minimal DFA as an array table' runs at 0.06x of the minimal DFA from a prepared table), so a compile-time table can only win on SETUP. DoD: a bench row for prepare time (certified_of + table_of) per workload vs a staged table produced by Spiral's partial evaluation for a literal pattern, A/A in band; plus the same match verdicts as the runtime table on the agreement words.")
inl html_node_records () = Task ("HTML-NODE-RECORDS", "html: node cases carry named records ({ name; rel; bytes } / { name; rel; children }) instead of positional string * string tuples, across the linked units", Story, P2, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "team:main,main,html,typefeatureset", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_html_node_records () = Planning ("HTML-NODE-RECORDS", Minutes 120u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_html_node_records_1 () = Note ("HTML-NODE-RECORDS", 1u32, "2026-11-03T17:09Z", "main", "main 11-03 13:1x from HTML-TREE's last open point. The positional File : string * string * i64 / Folder : string * string * list node lets name and rel swap silently (both string). Records name them. Risk to check first: records as union payloads crossing linked-unit boundaries (memory spiral-link-boundary-shapes: direct non-primitive records may inline); prove with the 8-crate build, the 1000-line gate, and the nested fixture byte for byte.")
inl note_of_html_node_records_2 () = Note ("HTML-NODE-RECORDS", 2u32, "2026-11-03T17:27Z", "main", "main 11-03 13:4x DONE (unstaged; model.spi, tree.spi, cli/html.spi; backups %TEMP%\\html-before-records). node = File : { name; rel; bytes } | Folder : { name; rel; children } (inline record payloads); every pattern reads f::name / f::rel / f::bytes / f::children; fixtures build records (the order fixture through a small file helper). The link-boundary risk didn't materialize: the generated Rust is BYTE-IDENTICAL for all 8 crates (records lower like the tuples did), so it's a pure source-level gain: name and rel can no longer swap silently. kit standards 0. Vultr: tree 5/5, pages 5/5, fast build HTML-OK (self-test incl. the nested fixture byte for byte).")
inl compiler_lua_union_tag_iife () = Task ("COMPILER-LUA-UNION-TAG-IIFE", "compiler Lua backend: real_core::union_tag on a runtime union emits local v = ;(function () ... end)(), a leading ; in expression position, which lua5.1 rejects (unexpected symbol near ;)", TaskKind, P2, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "compiler,lua,codegen,team:compiler,tibia", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_compiler_lua_union_tag_iife () = Planning ("COMPILER-LUA-UNION-TAG-IIFE", Minutes 60u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_compiler_lua_union_tag_iife_1 () = Note ("COMPILER-LUA-UNION-TAG-IIFE", 1u32, "2026-11-03T17:29Z", "tibia", "11-03 13:4x tibia, found while probing derived enum picklers (LIB-PICKLER-GADT). Program: a payload-free union compass (4 cases), case_index x = ~> real_core::union_tag x on a dyn value (:= ~d = Southward), and case_at i = union_untag with nominal_create (the base.spi defaultof pattern) on a dyn index. Generated Lua (vultr, single-flight compiler): local v8 = ;(function () local __tag if v0.tag == \"Us0_Northward\" then __tag = 0 ... return __tag end)() and lua5.1 fails with szl.lua:24: unexpected symbol near ;. The ; guard belongs only in statement position. Same program: C VERDICT OK (and the negative twin VERDICT BAD), Rust VERDICT OK. Constant-folded uses (tag of a literal case) do not hit it.")
inl note_of_compiler_lua_union_tag_iife_2 () = Note ("COMPILER-LUA-UNION-TAG-IIFE", 2u32, "2026-11-03T23:20Z", "compiler", "11-03 19:2x compiler: REPRODUCED and fixed in proof. Cause: codegenLua's UnionTag arm (spiral_compiler.fs:20030) builds its IIFE text as ';(function () local __tag ...)()' and simple binds it as 'local vN = <text>', so the statement-position guard lands in expression position. The guard is not needed there: simple already wraps a bare value that starts with '(' as 'do ... end'. Program COMPILER-LUA-UNION-TAG-IIFE\\1-no-semicolon-in-expression.kitdo drops the ';'. New regression sample samples/lua_union_tag_runtime (payload-free compass, union_tag on a dyn Southward, exit 0 when tag = 2): installed pack30 -> lua5.1 'main.lua:8: unexpected symbol near ;'. Node review (job compiler-luatag, vs installed pack30) running: sample A/B under lua5.1 + examples Lua -Native compare.")
inl note_of_compiler_lua_union_tag_iife_3 () = Note ("COMPILER-LUA-UNION-TAG-IIFE", 3u32, "2026-11-03T23:32Z", "compiler", "11-03 19:3x compiler: PROVEN and handed to main as pack31 (source 65A08A87 -> 60CDD696, core 24EEB518; dry run ok). Node vs pack30: sample lua_union_tag_runtime control lua5.1 'unexpected symbol near ;' exit 1 -> candidate 'local v1 = (function () ...' exit 0; examples Lua -Native 442 rows: only frontier_format_any_union differs, a candidate compile timeout under load; recompiled on the box with both compilers: byte-identical Lua (3B93E38A), runs exit 0 (33-39 s cold compile, over the 20 s row limit). The other ';(' in codegenLua (19957, array store) is a statement and stays. Done when main installs.")
inl note_of_compiler_lua_union_tag_iife_4 () = Note ("COMPILER-LUA-UNION-TAG-IIFE", 4u32, "2026-11-04T00:22Z", "compiler", "11-03 20:2x compiler: INSTALLED on the box as pack31 (live source 60CDD6967F16595C, installed core 24EEB518F30F7727 at 20:19, relaunched by main after the 19:50 BSOD); node swap and vultr build follow in main's chain. Regression row samples/lua_union_tag_runtime is in the matrix Lua list. Done.")
inl note_of_compiler_lua_union_tag_iife_5 () = Note ("COMPILER-LUA-UNION-TAG-IIFE", 5u32, "2026-11-04T00:38Z", "tibia", "11-03 20:4x tibia: pack31 VERIFIED from the consumer side on vultr (main reports it live 20:24): the original repro (case_probe: payload-free union compass, union_tag on a dyn value, union_untag + nominal_create on a dyn index) now compiles to Lua with no = ;(function and runs on lua5.1: positive prints VERDICT OK, negative twin VERDICT BAD; C unchanged (OK / BAD with exit 0 / 1). Follow-up in lib queued: pickler v6 adds ///> lua to the derived-enum test (node gate running).")
inl linked_units_matrix_suite () = Task ("LINKED-UNITS-MATRIX-SUITE", "Linked units have no matrix coverage: a test.ps1 suite that builds multi-unit cases (model, units, app) in link order per backend and runs them, seeded with the lu12-lu14 and NO-SUFFIX fixtures", TaskKind, P1, D5, 100u32, Done, "COMPILER-LINKED-UNITS", "team:compiler,compiler,linked-units,samples,tests", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_linked_units_matrix_suite () = Planning ("LINKED-UNITS-MATRIX-SUITE", Minutes 360u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_linked_units_matrix_suite_1 () = Note ("LINKED-UNITS-MATRIX-SUITE", 1u32, "2026-11-03T20:06Z", "compiler", "11-03 16:0x compiler: FILED from a gap found while closing LINKED-UNITS-OPAQUE-TIER. No package.spiproj under apps/compiler/tmp/samples uses link:, and test.ps1 compiles each sample entry alone, so every linked-unit proof today (lu5-lu14, nosuffix; this session's pack24-pack29 reviews) is an ad-hoc node script, and a regression in ExternCalls or the union import is caught only downstream (kit, html, x). SHAPE: cases under tmp/samples/linked_<name>/ with one dir per package (model/, app/, optional units/), the app's package.spiproj listing the others under link:; a 'linked' suite in test.ps1 builds the units in link order (each writes its unit graph), then the app, per backend in the case's list: Rust (rustc --crate-type rlib per unit + --extern, as node-lu9fix.ps1 did), Zig (all .zig in one dir, zig build-exe main.zig), Gleam (one gleam project with every module). It scores exit code against an expected value in the case, plus a 'linked' column (did the app call the unit, grep '<unit>::' / '<unit>.') so a silent inline is a failure. Negative cases (nosuffix) expect a compile error text. SEED: lu12 (Zig records + tuples), lu13 (Zig union), lu14 (Rust host type), nosuffix (error), lu9 (core list over a model union, Rust). Fixture sources are in this session's scratchpad and C:\\home\\remote-stage\\compiler-lu1x on the node.")
inl note_of_linked_units_matrix_suite_2 () = Note ("LINKED-UNITS-MATRIX-SUITE", 2u32, "2026-11-03T20:51Z", "compiler", "11-03 16:5x compiler: FIRST CUT IN TREE (unstaged). test.ps1 -Suite linked (+65 lines, outside the worker pool, refuses to combine with other suites): per case in matrix.psd1 'Linked', finds the root unit (no other unit lists it under packages:), compiles its link: units then the root into <run>/linked/<case>/<backend>/, links Rust rlibs with --extern and builds Zig files side by side, runs the root and compares Exit; a case with Error expects the compile to fail with that text; Calls lists exports the root must call through the unit, so a silent inline is a FAIL in the 'linked' column. Seeded cases: samples/linked_rust_host_type (Rust), linked_zig_records, linked_zig_union (Zig), linked_union_name_clash (Rust/Gleam/Zig, error). On the installed pack27 (run single-flight-20261103-164913): rust_host_type pass; zig_records FAIL 'inlined: model_make,model_pair'; zig_union FAIL 'expected type app.US0, found model.shape'; name_clash FAIL (compiles silently with color_1). Exactly the gaps LINKED-UNITS-ZIG 4/5 and LINKED-UNITS-NO-SUFFIX close, so pack29 should turn all 6 rows green. Documented in apps/compiler/tmp/AGENTS.md. Left: Gleam native runner, lu9 (core list over a model union) as a case.")
inl note_of_linked_units_matrix_suite_3 () = Note ("LINKED-UNITS-MATRIX-SUITE", 3u32, "2026-11-03T21:21Z", "compiler", "11-03 17:2x compiler: 5th case added, linked_rust_list_over_model (lu9, core list over a model union, four Rust crates; exit 108, Calls parse_parse,render_blocks_html). The suite now builds the transitive link: closure in post-order and gives each Rust unit --extern for every unit built before it; a types-only link (use model::{..}) counts as linked. Results: installed pack27/28: 2/7 pass (rust_host_type, list_over_model); the pack29 candidate (core 2ABF22D7): 7/7 pass (Zig records + union link, the name clash fails with 'both named color' on Rust/Gleam/Zig). Left: Gleam native runner.")
inl note_of_linked_units_matrix_suite_4 () = Note ("LINKED-UNITS-MATRIX-SUITE", 4u32, "2026-11-03T21:27Z", "compiler", "11-03 17:2x compiler: Gleam runner added (the persistent project <cache>/scratch/<mode>/gleam-linked from tests/native-shims/gleam, so dependencies download once; units go in src/<unit>.gleam, the root becomes src/main.gleam, *_ffi sidecars copied; gleam build, then gleam run -m spiral_run). linked_zig_records and linked_zig_union renamed linked_records and linked_union and now run on Zig AND Gleam. Totals on the pack29 candidate: 9 rows, all pass (Rust 3, Zig 3, Gleam 3). DoD met except lu-era fixtures beyond these five; AGENTS.md documents the suite.")
inl note_of_linked_units_matrix_suite_5 () = Note ("LINKED-UNITS-MATRIX-SUITE", 5u32, "2026-11-04T00:35Z", "compiler", "11-03 20:4x compiler: on the INSTALLED pack31 (box, core 24EEB518): test.ps1 -Suite linked 9/9 pass (run single-flight-20261103-202245), Rust 3 + Zig 3 + Gleam 3, so the suite now guards the live compiler. DoD met: the suite exists, five seeded cases (linked_records, linked_union, linked_rust_host_type, linked_rust_list_over_model, linked_union_name_clash), per-backend runners for Rust/Zig/Gleam, a 'linked' column that fails silent inlines, an Error mode for negative cases, and docs in apps/compiler/tmp/AGENTS.md. Further cases are added per new boundary shape.")
inl box_bsod_1103 () = Task ("BOX-BSOD-1103", "Box BSOD 2026-11-03 19:44 (0x7E access violation under 100%-CPU load): name the faulting driver from MEMORY.DMP + mitigate the sustained all-core saturation that preceded it", TaskKind, P1, D3, 70u32, Active, "", "team:resilience,machine,crash,bsod,durability,observability", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_box_bsod_1103 () = Planning ("BOX-BSOD-1103", Minutes 90u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_box_bsod_1103_1 () = Note ("BOX-BSOD-1103", 1u32, "2026-11-04T00:21Z", "resilience", "CAUSE (read from System event log + WER at 20:15 post-boot; boot was 19:51:50).\nBugcheck 0x0000007E SYSTEM_THREAD_EXCEPTION_NOT_HANDLED. param1=0xffffffffc0000005 (STATUS_ACCESS_VIOLATION), param2=0xfffff804707ba7bc (faulting kernel address), param3/4 = context + exception-record ptrs. Crash at 19:44:16 (Event 6008 unexpected shutdown; Event 41 Kernel-Power dirty reboot; Event 1001 BugCheck). Dumps WRITTEN + preserved: C:\\Windows\\MEMORY.DMP (4.2 GB full) + C:\\Windows\\Minidump\\110326-18578-01.dmp (1 MB); WER bucket Kernel_7e in ReportQueue.\nNO faulting-driver name yet: the kernel WER report has no Fault Module Name field, and no WinDbg/kd is installed, so !analyze -v was not run. CANDIDATE (NOT confirmed): NVDisplay.Container (NVIDIA display driver) showed high CPU post-boot and nvlddmkm.sys is a common 0x7E access-violation culprit under load - a guess until the dump is analyzed.\nCONTEXT: crash occurred during SUSTAINED 100% CPU saturation (TRAIN17 + all fleet lanes pinning 8 cores for an extended period; I had been tracking the saturation all session - critical-mirror kept failing on it). No System Error/Warning logged in the 10 min before 19:44 - abrupt kernel fault, no precursor.\nRECOVERY (clean, ZERO durability loss): scheduled tasks survived the reboot + auto-resumed (critical-mirror ran 20:10 result=0 \"ok: laptop\"; profile-mirror 20:02 = 35340 files/1850 MB; upkeep-tick 20:15); all scratch drafts survived (temp not cleared on reboot); only the in-memory heartbeat cron (recreated as ca721e68, until 22:00) + TRAIN17 (killed, will re-run) were lost.\nFOLLOW-UPS: (1) run WinDbg !analyze -v on MEMORY.DMP to name the faulting driver (winget install Microsoft.WinDbg, or hand the dump to the user); if NVIDIA, update the display driver. (2) RESILIENCE MITIGATION: cap sustained all-core saturation - this crash is the extreme failure mode of the 100%-CPU load tracked all session; ties MACHINE-EVENT-LOG-DETECTOR (should flag pre-crash saturation as a Starved/Runaway precursor) and CLAUDE-TREE-PRIORITY-BOOST (load mgmt). (3) box CPU was 93% again at 20:19 post-boot (WER dump-processing + lanes restarting) - watch re-saturation re-crash risk.")
inl note_of_box_bsod_1103_2 () = Note ("BOX-BSOD-1103", 2u32, "2026-11-04T00:27Z", "resilience", "FOLLOW-UP 4 (resilience improvement the crash surfaced). The autonomous-run wake source is NOT crash-resilient: the session-only heartbeat cron died with the BSOD, so the loop had NO wake source until main messaged me to restart - a human-in-the-loop recovery, not automatic. Scheduled TASKS survived the reboot and auto-resumed (critical-mirror, profile-mirror, claude-boost-native); a cron did not. IDEA: back the autonomous heartbeat with a persistent scheduled task (or have claude-boost-native / a sibling re-engage the session) so a crash self-recovers the loop instead of stalling until a human notices. Ties idle-needs-wake-source + the box-crash-recovery-playbook memory. Recovery playbook now captured in memory; recreate-cron-first is the manual stopgap until an auto-recover exists.")
inl note_of_box_bsod_1103_3 () = Note ("BOX-BSOD-1103", 3u32, "2026-11-04T00:34Z", "resilience", "MITIGATION NUANCE (20:33, box re-saturated to 100% as all 7 lanes resumed). The \"cap sustained saturation\" follow-up CONFLICTS with the user's standing order (all lanes iterate autonomously until 22:00 = 100% is the EXPECTED, wanted load). So the resilient response is NOT to throttle the fleet (that fights the user's order) but to (a) MINIMIZE RPO so a re-crash is survivable - critical-mirror runs ~every 15min (20:10/20:27), bounding memory loss to ~15min, drafts+tasks survive reboots; and (b) surface the speed-vs-crash-risk TRADEOFF to the user as THEIR decision (accept re-crash risk for full iteration speed, or cap load for stability). Driver ID from the dump (follow-up 1) is still the real root-cause lever - a specific driver fix/update removes the crash without capping load at all, which is the ideal outcome. Do NOT auto-throttle peers.")
inl note_of_box_bsod_1103_4 () = Note ("BOX-BSOD-1103", 4u32, "2026-11-04T04:14Z", "resilience", "11-04 00:1x resilience GROOM: set assignee=agent (resilience owns the crash lane). Remaining work is the WinDbg !analyze -v driver-naming on the preserved minidump, box-gated (do not run the analyzer on a loaded box) and deferred per the recovery playbook; the small minidump is preserved off-box. The four follow-ups (driver id, saturation-mitigation nuance, re-crash watch, crash-resilient wake source) stay in this item.")
inl note_of_box_bsod_1103_5 () = Note ("BOX-BSOD-1103", 5u32, "2026-11-04T04:19Z", "resilience", "11-04 star-exchange corroboration (compiler + main lanes): the sustained 100pct-CPU precondition to the 19:44 BSOD had named load sources - compiler was running box-side pack reviews + native A/B arms at 100pct CPU immediately before the crash. main adds a recurrence data point: at 00:11 11-04 seven lanes grooming at once took the box to 100pct again (kit agile wip 152s vs list 1.3s). Both reinforce follow-up f2 (saturation mitigation) and f3 (re-crash watch): the box hits 100pct whenever the fleet fans out, so the mitigation is remote-first placement (node+vultr first, box last - now the user standing rule 11-04), not throttling the work.")
inl note_of_box_bsod_1103_6 () = Note ("BOX-BSOD-1103", 6u32, "2026-11-04T05:09Z", "resilience", "11-04 01:1x resilience f3 RE-CRASH WATCH (clear) + f2 reasoning. System event log since 19:00 11-03 shows ONLY the single 19:44 crash + 19:51 reboot (id41 Kernel-Power, 6008 unexpected-shutdown, 1001 BugCheck 0x7e c0000005). NO new BugCheck/Kernel-Power/unexpected-shutdown events after 19:51 - the box has held STABLE 5+ hours post-boot through sustained fleet 100pct CPU. f3 verdict: no recurrence so far. f2 IMPLICATION: 100pct CPU for 5h without a re-crash means saturation is a CONTRIBUTING condition, not a deterministic cause; the 0x7e access-violation points to a specific driver fault triggered under load. Supports the DRIVER-FIX direction (f1) + remote-first load placement (now the user standing rule), NOT throttling the work. f1 (WinDbg !analyze -v to name the driver) stays box-gated/deferred; MEMORY.DMP 4.08GB preserved on-box as evidence. f4 (crash-resilient wake source) still the open design. Re-run this watch each groom.")
inl note_of_box_bsod_1103_7 () = Note ("BOX-BSOD-1103", 7u32, "2026-11-04T05:11Z", "resilience", "11-04 01:2x resilience f4 DESIGN - crash-resilient run-liveness monitor. PROBLEM: the session-only CronCreate heartbeat dies with the session (BSOD/crash), and a dead run looks identical to a finished one (silent). CANT: externally resurrect or re-prompt a dead Claude session (no supported external trigger to inject a prompt). CAN: a Windows SCHEDULED TASK survives reboot (validated - tasks auto-resumed after 19:44). DESIGN: each lane heartbeat tick TOUCHES a sentinel (e.g. %LOCALAPPDATA%/kit/run/<lane>.heartbeat with an ISO stamp) at the START of every tick; a scheduled task (cadence ~5min, survives reboot) reads standing.txt (is a run active, until>now?) + each lane sentinel; if a run is active but a sentinel is stale beyond 2x the 20min cadence (40min), raise a LOUD alert (a prominent marker file the user/main sees + a run-incidents log append + optional toast). It cannot WAKE the session but converts a SILENT death into detected-in-minutes. ESCALATION (optional, riskier, user-decision): the task attempts a headless relaunch+resume - fragile, spawning sessions is beyond agent authority, leave to user. SPIRAL-FIRST: monitor logic = a kit verb (kit run liveness-check) reading sentinels+standing.txt, NOT a new .ps1; the schtasks register line is persistent config = USER-BATCH. RELATION: this is HEARTBEAT-STALENESS-GUARD applied to the RUN heartbeat itself (the run heartbeat IS a status file; the scheduled-task monitor is its crash-resilient reader) - f4 is that guard highest-stakes instance. DOD: sentinel written each tick; kit liveness verb; one schtasks line (user-batch); proven by killing a heartbeat mid-run and seeing the alert within threshold.")
inl note_of_box_bsod_1103_8 () = Note ("BOX-BSOD-1103", 8u32, "2026-11-04T05:29Z", "resilience", "11-04 01:3x resilience f4 IMPLEMENTED (crash-resilient run monitor) - closed via the existing reboot-surviving guard, not a new supervisor. (1) edited scripts/heartbeat-staleness-guard.ps1 (.bak in scratch) to watch autonomous-*.last.log sentinels at a 40min threshold gated on standing.txt until>now (a stopped heartbeat after run-end is not a false alarm); (2) resilience heartbeat cron (now 553c6aa8) writes kit\\schedules\\autonomous-resilience.last.log each tick as step 0. TESTED: fresh->all watchers fresh exit0; back-dated 50min while active->STALE autonomous-resilience flagged exit1; restored->clean exit0. A crash that kills the session+cron is now detected in <=40min by the scheduled guard (converting silent run-death into a logged STALE alarm + a backlog note when the guard runs -NoteOnStale). Each lane adds its own autonomous-<lane> sentinel step for fleet coverage. Guard edit UNSTAGED. f4 DONE; f1 (driver naming) still box-gated.")
inl note_of_box_bsod_1103_9 () = Note ("BOX-BSOD-1103", 9u32, "2026-11-04T10:51Z", "resilience", "11-04 f2 (saturation mitigation) CONCRETE WIN: a major, overlooked contributor to the sustained 100pct-CPU that preceded the 19:44 BSOD was SystemInformer-nightly at DEFAULT settings burning 60-98% CPU (7.3h cumulative = ~a full core). The user flagged it 11-04. FIXED by low-CPU config (UpdateInterval 1s->5s + disable cycle-CPU/stage2/network-resolve) = 60-98% -> 19%, SI kept open. Tracked ongoing on MACHINE-PROC-HEALTH-TUNE (hourly process-health sweep + SI re-tune). So f2 now has a real lever beyond remote-first placement: a monitor was eating a core unnoticed - the hourly sweep prevents that recurring.")
inl note_of_box_bsod_1103_10 () = Note ("BOX-BSOD-1103", 10u32, "2026-11-04T11:55Z", "resilience", "11-04 ~08:0x f1 CRASH SIGNATURE IDENTIFIED (read-only, via WER + Event 1001, no WinDbg needed for this part). The 11-03 19:51:56 BSOD = BugCheck 0x0000007E SYSTEM_THREAD_EXCEPTION_NOT_HANDLED; param1=0xC0000005 (ACCESS_VIOLATION), param2=0xfffff804707ba7bc (faulting kernel address). Dumps: C:\\WINDOWS\\MEMORY.DMP + C:\\Windows\\Minidump\\110326-18578-01.dmp (1.1MB). PRIME SUSPECT = NVIDIA display driver nvlddmkm.sys: WER ReportArchive holds TWO Kernel_141 LiveKernelEvents bucketed LKD_0x141_Tdr:6_IMAGE_nvlddmkm.sys (GPU TDR = Timeout Detection and Recovery; the driver hung and was reset). A 0x7E access-violation concurrent with nvlddmkm TDRs, under the sustained 100pct-CPU + GPU load (tibia transformer/GPU) preceding the crash, strongly implicates the NVIDIA driver. CONFIRMING the exact 0x7E faulting module needs WinDbg analyze -v to resolve 0xfffff804707ba7bc (cdb/windbg NOT installed; Windows SDK Debuggers required, box-gated). PREVENTION levers are driver-level, not just CPU-shedding: update NVIDIA driver to latest; cap/stagger GPU load; check GPU thermals/power. ALSO: a SECOND unexpected shutdown 10-30 06:09 (Event 41, bugcheck 0x10) - a separate earlier incident. (claim refused by a decline-cooldown from my earlier release; this is a read-only analysis note.)")
inl note_of_box_bsod_1103_11 () = Note ("BOX-BSOD-1103", 11u32, "2026-11-04T11:58Z", "resilience", "11-04 ~08:0x f1 ROOT-CAUSE EVIDENCE (prevention, concrete). GPU = Quadro K1100M (Kepler, 2013, mobile, 2GB) on this laptop box - an EOL GPU. Driver = 426.78 (a ~2019 build; Keplers last legacy branch is 470.xx, so the installed driver is ~3 years stale). GPU state now: 76C at 45pct util, 1012/2048 MiB. STORY: a 2013 EOL mobile GPU under heavy ML/transformer work on a 2019 driver => nvlddmkm.sys TDRs (the LKD_0x141_Tdr:6 LiveKernelEvents) => the fatal 0x7E access-violation BSOD. PREVENTION (all require USER action, NOT autonomous - a driver update reboots and risks display/RustDesk): (1) route GPU/ML workloads OFF the local K1100M onto rented cloud GPUs (tibia has GPU-rental permission) - a 2GB 2013 mobile GPU cannot sustain transformer training, the likeliest trigger; (2) update NVIDIA driver 426.78 -> 470.xx legacy; (3) watch GPU thermals. Converts BOX-BSOD from box-saturated to a specific fixable GPU-driver/workload cause.")
inl note_of_box_bsod_1103_12 () = Note ("BOX-BSOD-1103", 12u32, "2026-11-04T11:59Z", "resilience", "11-04 ~08:0x f1 REFINEMENT (evidence correction - checked the GPU process table BEFORE blaming any lane). nvidia-smi process table NOW shows ONLY desktop C+G apps on the K1100M: dwm, WindowsTerminal, iCloud, firefox x2, rustdesk, PowerToys x6, explorer, Fork, notepad++, msedgewebview2, SearchHost, ShellExperienceHost, TextInputHost - NO ML/CUDA compute process. So the CURRENT 40pct/1GB/76C load is DESKTOP-GRAPHICS BLOAT, not ML/tibia compute. Do NOT pin the crash on tibia local ML (unproven; no compute process now). Accurate prevention = reduce desktop GPU load on this 2GB EOL GPU (disable HW-accel in heavy apps: PowerToys modules, firefox, edge-webview; close unneeded GPU apps) + driver update 426.78->470.xx legacy. The ML-off-box-routing point holds ONLY if GPU compute ran at crash time (UNVERIFIED). Root cause stays scoped to evidence: 2013 2GB EOL GPU + ~3yr-stale driver + heavy desktop GPU load, TDR-confirmed (nvlddmkm LKD_0x141_Tdr:6).")
inl note_of_box_bsod_1103_13 () = Note ("BOX-BSOD-1103", 13u32, "2026-11-04T12:09Z", "resilience", "11-04 ~08:1x f1 PREVENTION REFINEMENT (from kit, re MACHINE-SLOT-CAPS). KEY: most of the 11-03 saturating load was processes OUTSIDE kit jobs - interactive pwsh builds, erl/BEAM, cc1 launched directly by lanes, which NO kit job-slot cap sees. kit facts: (a) slots file read only by TRAIN22 host code (not installed); installed host reads only KIT_JOB_SLOTS; (b) cap is per job-host INSTANCE (per session pid), so slots=N caps each LANE at N, not the machine; (c) --heavy jobs already serialize machine-wide; (d) kit set slots=3 per-lane (inert until TRAIN22); real fix = machine-wide child count (box ~6, heavy 1, Cells/BEAM own class). IMPLICATION: the kit cap is a PARTIAL guard (kit-job over-admission only); the actual 11-03 saturators (direct interactive builds) are guarded ONLY by the detection layer (hourly kit machine watch + CPU-hog alarm) + per-lane discipline (route heavy builds THROUGH kit jobs). Detection + discipline, not just the cap.")
inl note_of_box_bsod_1103_14 () = Note ("BOX-BSOD-1103", 14u32, "2026-11-04T12:47Z", "resilience", "11-04 ~08:4x CORRECTION to note 11 re the 10-30 06:09 event: it was NOT a BSOD. No Event 1001 (bugcheck) that day, NO minidump around 10-30, and Event 41 shows PowerButton=1 = a power-off/loss, not a kernel crash. So there is NO recurring-crash pattern: the 11-03 19:51 0x7E (NVIDIA/nvlddmkm TDR) is the SINGLE real BSOD. The 10-30 was a dirty power event (user or power blip, possibly profile-wipe-era). Keeps the incident history accurate: one BSOD, GPU-driver root cause, not a trend.")
inl note_of_box_bsod_1103_15 () = Note ("BOX-BSOD-1103", 15u32, "2026-11-04T14:07Z", "resilience", "11-04 ~10:0x f1 DRIVER-LEVER CORRECTION (evidence-scoped). My earlier note said update 426.78 -> 470.xx legacy. That is likely WRONG for this MOBILE GPU: the installed 426.78 (2019) is already NEWER than any driver third-party sources list for the Quadro K1100M (newest found 368.81/2016), and mobile Kepler (Quadro K-series) EOL-d EARLIER than desktop Kepler - so the 470.xx branch likely does NOT support the mobile K1100M; 426.78 may be at/near the last driver for it. Search was inconclusive on the exact final version (no official NVIDIA page returned) - user should verify on NVIDIA driver archive before assuming an update exists. NET: the driver-update prevention lever is UNCERTAIN/likely-unavailable. The RELIABLE levers for this EOL 2GB mobile GPU are: reduce desktop GPU load (disable HW-accel in heavy apps), keep GPU compute off-box (rented GPUs), or replace the hardware. Keeps BOX-BSOD prevention honest.")
inl note_of_box_bsod_1103_16 () = Note ("BOX-BSOD-1103", 16u32, "2026-11-05T02:16Z", "resilience", "11-04 21:1x GROOM: progress 20->60. Today f1 CRASH LOCALIZATION completed - root cause = NVIDIA nvlddmkm TDR on the EOL Quadro K1100M + stale driver 426.78 + heavy DESKTOP GPU load -> 0x7E (only 1 real BSOD; the 10-30 event was a power-off, not a crash). Detection layer shipped (hourly kit machine watch Scheduled Task + idle/hog alarms). REMAINING (not projected as done): WinDbg analyze -v exact-module confirm is box-gated (cdb not installed); the driver-update lever is likely unavailable (mobile Kepler EOL, 426.78 already newest); real prevention is USER action (reduce desktop GPU load / route compute off-box).")
inl note_of_box_bsod_1103_17 () = Note ("BOX-BSOD-1103", 17u32, "2026-11-05T03:46Z", "resilience", "DUMP-LEVEL confirmation from Minidump 110326-18578-01.dmp (1.1MB, PAGEDU64, stamped 2026-11-03 19:51 matching the 19:44 crash), upgrading the prior WER/LiveKernelEvents inference: BugCheckCode=0x1000007E SYSTEM_THREAD_EXCEPTION_NOT_HANDLED; P1=0xC0000005 STATUS_ACCESS_VIOLATION; faulting IP P2=0xFFFFF804707BA7BC = 1980 bytes (0x7BC) into a page-aligned module base 0xFFFFF804707BA000. Graphics stack present in the dump: nvlddmkm.sys (NVIDIA) AND igdkmd64.sys (Intel iGPU - box is HYBRID/Optimus, not pure-NVIDIA as earlier recorded), plus dxgkrnl.sys + dxgmms2.sys + watchdog.sys = the textbook GPU TDR signature. Exact faulting-module name (base->name) needs a kernel debugger; none on PATH, installing one to run kd !analyze -v on the dump. For PREVENTION the direction is unchanged: graphics-stack TDR under sustained load. resilience tick 2026-11-04 23:5x local.")
inl note_of_box_bsod_1103_18 () = Note ("BOX-BSOD-1103", 18u32, "2026-11-05T03:53Z", "resilience", "MITIGATION half - live measurement 2026-11-04 23:5x local: box at SUSTAINED all-core 100% (Get-Counter _Total 6/6 samples 100% over 12s), the documented 11-03 BSOD precursor state, happening NOW. Source is legitimate distributed fleet work, NO runaway to reap: 3 concurrent dotnet builds ~2.16 cores (likely compiler/main pack38/39 box installs), 17 Claude lane processes ~1.17 cores, kernel/System ~1.1 cores, SystemInformer 0.87 core, dwm 0.40 core. Zero CPU headroom. BUT GPU load is light (dwm compositing only, no heavy GPU compute), so acute BSOD risk right now is LOW - the 11-03 crash also needed the nvlddmkm/graphics TDR trigger under GPU load, absent here. Concrete mitigation finding: the precursor is 3 heavy box builds STACKING concurrently, which violates the fleet live-runs-alone / box-fills-last rule. Guard design: gate any new box heavy-run on a CPU-headroom check (reject/defer-to-node if 10s averaged _Total >=90%), and serialize concurrent dotnet/compiler builds so at most one heavy build runs on the box at a time. Enforcement belongs in a kit pre-heavy-run check verb (pairs with MACHINE-EVENT-LOG-DETECTOR cpu-delta series); un-gated part - the threshold + policy - recorded here. resilience.")
inl note_of_box_bsod_1103_19 () = Note ("BOX-BSOD-1103", 19u32, "2026-11-05T03:58Z", "resilience", "CORRECTION to note 18 (per main 2026-11-04 23:5x): the 3 dotnet processes are compiler pack38->39 install chain which is SERIAL within itself (build orchestrator + compiler subprocesses of ONE chain), NOT 3 independent heavy builds stacking - so my live-runs-alone-violation characterization was wrong. main heavy runs are all on vultr (capped), no GPU work from main; box-last + GPU-hold policies are being respected. Net: the sustained-100% is a serial compiler chain + 8-lane fleet overhead, and acute BSOD risk is confirmed LOW (no GPU TDR trigger present). The surviving un-gated mitigation deliverable: a pre-HEAVY-GPU-run headroom gate (hold any heavy GPU job while 10s averaged _Total >=90%), since CPU-saturation + GPU load together was the 11-03 signature. CPU-only saturation from a serial build is tolerated. resilience.")
inl note_of_box_bsod_1103_20 () = Note ("BOX-BSOD-1103", 20u32, "2026-11-05T05:08Z", "resilience", "ROOT-CAUSE CORRECTION (resilience, event-log evidence) - earlier nvlddmkm-TDR attribution for the 11-03 BSOD was a CONFLATION of two separate events. Facts: (1) BugCheck 1001 (WER-SystemErrorReporting) confirms the 11-03 crash = 0x7E / 0xC0000005, faulting IP 0xfffff804707ba7bc, dump C:\\WINDOWS\\MEMORY.DMP (triple-confirmed vs my minidump parse). (2) The nvlddmkm TDR = a SEPARATE WER fault bucket LKD_0x141_Tdr:6_IMAGE_nvlddmkm.sys dated 10-27 12:39, a recovered LiveKernelEvent, NOT the 11-03 BSOD. (3) NO Display-TDR (event 4101) and no TDR fault bucket is logged at the 11-03 crash window - so the 11-03 0x7E is NOT a confirmed GPU TDR. (4) The igfxCUIService (Intel) events at 11-03 19:51:53 are at REBOOT/recovery time (service restart during boot), NOT established as the crash cause - do not swing the attribution to Intel either. (5) Exact 11-03 faulting module remains UNRESOLVED without a kernel debugger; my earlier dump size-field inference (684KB) was unreliable (matched no driver: dxgmms2 1.19MB, dxgkrnl 5MB, igdkmd64 7.6MB, nvlddmkm multi-MB). NET: 11-03 = a 0x7E access violation in an unresolved kernel module under sustained all-core CPU saturation; graphics stack loaded but TDR UNCONFIRMED. Mitigation (avoid sustained all-core saturation + concurrent heavy GPU load; pre-heavy-GPU-run headroom gate) stands as general BSOD prevention regardless of the exact module. Exact module still needs !analyze -v. resilience.")
inl note_of_box_bsod_1103_21 () = Note ("BOX-BSOD-1103", 21u32, "2026-11-05T06:03Z", "resilience", "WER forensics (resilience, closes the no-debugger investigation + sharpens the TDR picture). (1) The 11-03 0x7E bugcheck generated NO WER bucket report - the crash window (19:00-20:30) only has AppCrash reports (Acrobat, svchost); the 0x7E logged to event 1001 + MEMORY.DMP but no faulting-module WER bucket exists. So the exact 11-03 faulting module is genuinely UNRESOLVABLE from event-log/WER and truly needs WinDbg !analyze -v. (2) SEPARATE chronic pattern: nvlddmkm TDRs (LKD_0x141_Tdr:6_IMAGE_nvlddmkm.sys) recurred 4x - 10-14 23:18, 10-23 16:02, 10-26 12:39, 10-27 12:39 - ALL at the identical faulting address Param2=fffff8029268f29c = a fixed-address driver bug on the EOL K1100M. These are 0x141 LiveKernelEvents (TDR=RECOVERED), NOT BSODs, and NONE has occurred since 10-27 (9 days dormant). So the real recurring GPU risk is this chronic-recovered TDR, DISTINCT from the one-off 11-03 0x7E hard bugcheck (which was never one of these TDRs). Mitigation unchanged: avoid sustained CPU saturation + heavy GPU load on the EOL GPU; the chronic TDR being dormant 9 days suggests current load patterns are OK. resilience.")
inl note_of_box_bsod_1103_22 () = Note ("BOX-BSOD-1103", 22u32, "2026-11-05T20:32Z", "resilience-614162e5", "11-05 ~12:3x resilience RECURRENCE CHECK + live co-factor (box in sustained 100%-CPU, the BSOD precondition, so checked now). NO RECURRENCE: newest minidump still 11-03 19:51 (1081KB); zero bugcheck/Event-41/6008 newer than the single 11-03 event in the last 2 days. Event 1001 CONFIRMS the bugcheck params: 0x0000007e (p1=0xffffffffc0000005 = STATUS_ACCESS_VIOLATION, p2/RIP=0xfffff804707ba7bc), i.e. SYSTEM_THREAD_EXCEPTION_NOT_HANDLED / access violation - corroborates the 0x7E-AV root cause (NOT a confirmed nvlddmkm TDR; that was the separate chronic 10-27 event). Full dump at C:\\WINDOWS\\MEMORY.DMP + the 1081KB minidump both exist; faulting module still un-ID-able without a debugger AND post-reboot KASLR rerandomized live driver bases so the RIP cannot be mapped against the running kernel (correctly not pursued). LIVE SAFETY: GPU engine util = 7.3% while CPU=100% -> co-factor ABSENT -> current saturation is CPU-only = SAFE (the trigger needs CPU>=90 AND GPU>=50 together). Independent corroboration of danger-watcher bl3l2uz7y silence. No enforcement change; stability data point. resilience-614162e5.")
inl rust_heap_union_moves () = Task ("RUST-HEAP-UNION-MOVES", "codegenRust: a heap-union (Rc) match whose scrutinee is dead after it matches Rc::unwrap_or_clone(vN) by value instead of &*vN with per-field clones (41 sites in apps/spiral)", TaskKind, P2, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "team:compiler,compiler,rust,codegen", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_rust_heap_union_moves () = Planning ("RUST-HEAP-UNION-MOVES", Minutes 180u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_rust_heap_union_moves_1 () = Note ("RUST-HEAP-UNION-MOVES", 1u32, "2026-11-04T01:02Z", "compiler", "11-03 21:0x compiler: FILED as the follow-up to RUST-MOVE-ON-LAST-USE (Done; pack26 slice 3 moves, pack29 slice 4 by-value stack matches, pack30 freeze-only-closure-regions). CENSUS on apps/spiral/spiral.rs (pack29 candidate): 4398 clones, 41 heap-union matches 'match &*vN', 211 stack matches still by reference (scrutinee live after), 290 arm rebind clones remain. A heap union is Rc<UHn>, so a dead scrutinee can't be moved out directly. SHAPE: when the scrutinee is dead after the match (slice 4's test, frozen-region aware), emit 'match Rc::try_unwrap(vN) { Ok(u) => match u { arms by value }, Err(rc) => match &*rc { arms with clones } }', which duplicates arm text, so only for arms below a size cap; or 'Rc::unwrap_or_clone(vN)' (stable since Rust 1.76) and then match by value, which clones the payload only when shared, with no duplication: prefer that. Acceptance per memory rust-move-packs-need-downstream-rustc: examples Rust -Native on -ChangedBetween rows, apps/spiral cargo check, regex certificate A/B, kit_log_counts/sections. Use test.ps1 -BackendsFrom (it will say Rust).")
inl note_of_rust_heap_union_moves_2 () = Note ("RUST-HEAP-UNION-MOVES", 2u32, "2026-11-04T01:26Z", "compiler", "11-03 21:2x compiler: v1 REVIEWED (node vs pack32 = monorule build). Program RUST-HEAP-UNION-MOVES\\1-unwrap-or-clone-dead-heap.kitdo: a dead heap-union scrutinee (slice 4's dead test, so frozen closure regions are excluded) matches 'Rc::unwrap_or_clone(vN)' by value and moves every field. v1 results: regex certificate 16/16 both; examples Rust 21 changed rows, native 21/21 ran on control, candidate one build-fail: samples/rust_union_non_clone_payload, a heap union whose payload is not Clone, so UHn derives no Clone and unwrap_or_clone does not apply; 0 DISAGREE. apps/spiral .clone() 3201 -> 3117 (-84), cargo check 0 errors; kit_log_counts/sections (box): 16 + 21 unwrap_or_clone sites, cargo check 0 errors; -Suite linked 9/9. v2 adds 'not moving' (the union has no non-Clone field, the same flag slice 4 uses) and is under review (job compiler-heapmove2).")
inl note_of_rust_heap_union_moves_3 () = Note ("RUST-HEAP-UNION-MOVES", 3u32, "2026-11-04T01:43Z", "compiler", "11-03 21:4x compiler: v2 PROVEN and handed to main as pack33 (low priority). v2 adds 'not moving' (no non-Clone field in the union, the same flag as slice 4). Source A8232488 (pack32) -> D00B41EE, core D1379FED, box dry run ok. Node vs pack32: E0507 sample unchanged; certificate 16/16; examples Rust 20 changed rows, native 20/20 on both arms, 0 behavior cells, 0 DISAGREE (rust_union_non_clone_payload back to unchanged); apps/spiral .clone() 3201 -> 3117, cargo check 0 errors. Box: kit_log_counts/sections cargo check 0 errors. The remaining heap matches are unions with non-Clone payloads (Rc::try_unwrap would be needed there) and scrutinees that are live after the match.")
inl note_of_rust_heap_union_moves_4 () = Note ("RUST-HEAP-UNION-MOVES", 4u32, "2026-11-04T01:43Z", "compiler", "11-03 21:4x compiler: install DEFERRED to 11-04 by main (box at 100% CPU near the 22:00 end; it BSODed under sustained load at 19:44). The next lane to install takes pack33 with the command in the handover (candidate staged at %LOCALAPPDATA%\\spiral-bin-candidates\\pack33; it reproduces from live pack32 A8232488). Rerun the dry run first, since live may move.")
inl note_of_rust_heap_union_moves_5 () = Note ("RUST-HEAP-UNION-MOVES", 5u32, "2026-11-04T04:20Z", "compiler", "11-04 00:2x compiler: pack33 handover addendum (user 11-04: node and vultr first, box last). The install command is unchanged: rerun the dry run first, then install-pack.ps1 -Name pack33 -Programs <RUST-HEAP-UNION-MOVES\\1-unwrap-or-clone-dead-heap.kitdo> -Apply. After the install, on every machine that swapped its DLL in place, list the dotnet SpiralCompiler processes started before the swap and restart them, or gate with -Compiler process. Warm daemons keep the old compiler (tibia found it after pack31; main #3). Post-install re-gate: test.ps1 -BackendsFrom pack32-src,pack33-src (expect Rust only), then -Native -ChangedBetween on the node, not a whole matrix on the box. Root fix filed: COMPILER-DAEMON-STALE-AFTER-PACK.")
inl note_of_rust_heap_union_moves_6 () = Note ("RUST-HEAP-UNION-MOVES", 6u32, "2026-11-04T04:35Z", "compiler", "11-04 00:3x compiler: READY, handed to main for the install (user 11-04: node-first). The review was already node-first (11-03, node vs pack32). Dry run re-run on live today: core A823248809D9B560 -> D00B41EE3CD5F7FF, host DFF8371A unchanged, ok. Main installs and runs the downstream re-gates on vultr. Done when the install has gone through and the re-gates pass.")
inl note_of_rust_heap_union_moves_7 () = Note ("RUST-HEAP-UNION-MOVES", 7u32, "2026-11-04T04:47Z", "compiler", "11-04 00:4x compiler: DONE. pack33 installed on the box by main at 00:46: core dll D1379FEDC807FC68, equal to the reviewed candidate; live core source D00B41EE3CD5F7FF; host 6B30D2F5 unchanged. The node swap and vultr build are deferred by main until TRAIN18 (node, pinned to pack32) has a verdict; then main runs the Rust-only re-gates (apps/spiral, kit crates, regex certificate) on vultr. A failing re-gate reopens this item.")
inl kit_agile_list_last_note () = Task ("KIT-AGILE-LIST-LAST-NOTE", "kit agile list --json leaves last_note empty for every task, so orphan sweeps cannot filter by note age in one call", TaskKind, P2, D2, 100u32, Done, "", "team:kit,kit,agile,backlog", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_kit_agile_list_last_note () = Planning ("KIT-AGILE-LIST-LAST-NOTE", Minutes 60u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_kit_agile_list_last_note_1 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-LIST-LAST-NOTE", 1u32, "2026-11-04T01:48Z", "compiler", "11-03 21:5x compiler: FILED from MAIN-ORPHAN-SWEEP n21. 'kit agile list <root> all --team compiler --json' returns 300 tasks whose last_note field is an empty string for every task, so a 'no note in 48 h' sweep can't be computed from one listing and flags all 100 open items; the only route is one 'agile show' per item. DoD: list --json fills last_note with {at, author} of the newest note (the same note 'show --last 1' prints), and a test row asserts it for a task with notes and is empty only for a task with none. Then MAIN-ORPHAN-SWEEP can run its 48 h filter in one call.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_list_last_note_2 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-LIST-LAST-NOTE", 2u32, "2026-11-04T20:18Z", "kit", "11-04 20:5x kit: DONE, verified on the INSTALLED kit (TRAIN14c): `kit agile list <root> Active --json` -> 130 rows, every one with a non-empty last_note (fields: id, title, kind, priority, difficulty, progress, status, deps, tags, notes, last_note, estimate_minutes, load, assignee). The bug in the title no longer reproduces.")
inl lib_seq_list_c_pascal_repr () = Task ("LIB-SEQ-LIST-C-PASCAL-REPR", "lib seq' and list' gain a C/Pascal/Lua representation (array_base) so concat and utf8_byte_list can get portable arms", Story, P3, D4, 75u32, Active, "", "team:compiler,compiler,lib,portable,backends", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_lib_seq_list_c_pascal_repr () = Planning ("LIB-SEQ-LIST-C-PASCAL-REPR", Minutes 180u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_lib_seq_list_c_pascal_repr_1 () = Note ("LIB-SEQ-LIST-C-PASCAL-REPR", 1u32, "2026-11-04T04:15Z", "compiler", "11-04 00:1x compiler groom: from PORTABLE-PRIMITIVES (closed at its DoD). seq::seq' (seq_switch': Gleam list, F# seq, Python list, Rust Vec, TS array, Zig slice) and list' (utf8_byte_list) have no C, Pascal or Lua representation, so concat and utf8_byte_list cannot get arms there. Next step: pick the representation (array_base t, as concat_list already does on C/Pascal since 10-08), add the seq_switch' C/Pascal arm, then a portable sample. No caller needs it today: the 5 seq' lib callers (crypto 181, file_system 1521, parsing 198/200, process 576) are host-only modules; portable code uses concat_list.")
inl note_of_lib_seq_list_c_pascal_repr_2 () = Note ("LIB-SEQ-LIST-C-PASCAL-REPR", 2u32, "2026-11-05T17:47Z", "compiler", "11-05 13:4x compiler: SLICE 1 LANDED (unstaged): base.defaultof gains C ('(T){0}', a C99 compound literal for scalars and structs) and Pascal ('Default(T)') arms; strings, options, pairs and unions still go through its generic run cases. This was one of the two C blockers under listm'.to_array' (the other is to_array' itself, the list' representation this item is about). New test defaultof_scalars (string, i32, i64, f64, bool, option) on rust, c, pascal; the existing defaultof test stays rust + gleam, since Gleam has no scalar arms (an i64/f64/bool case there breaks gleam build). Draft round-tripped canonical + fixpoint; lib copy gate 2 passed 0 failed; lib_land_window closed GREEN: 3 consumer signatures unchanged, apps/spiral compiles, document_test 26 passed, type_probe PASS (26 typed), 0 respell drift. Live base.spi 07D68C748FD0 -> E5584788886D, lib digest 922986812B99DB81, undo spiral-bin\\lib-land-undo\\20261105-133511. Next slice: a C/Pascal arm for list' (array_base t, as concat_list already uses).")
inl note_of_lib_seq_list_c_pascal_repr_3 () = Note ("LIB-SEQ-LIST-C-PASCAL-REPR", 3u32, "2026-11-05T17:51Z", "compiler", "11-05 13:5x compiler: PROBE after slice 1 (installed pack51 + landed defaultof arms): reflection::typed_name_snake on C now passes the defaultof gap and stops at the NEXT one: 'backend_switch has no arm for the C backend', reached from base.spi:655-659 (base.try) < resultm.spi:32 (from_try). OPEN QUESTION before adding more C arms: both C traces enter a function's LUA arm first (listm'.to_array' at :770, resultm.from_try at :32), which suggests the C backend falls back to Lua arms in some backend_switch dispatches when no C arm exists. Find that rule in the compiler's backend_switch lowering before writing C arms for try/from_try: a C arm would change what C programs that reach those functions get today. (Typed name styles on C are already covered without this: !!!!UnionCaseName(x, style) and lib case_name_snake/kebab/lower fold at compile time.)")
inl note_of_lib_seq_list_c_pascal_repr_4 () = Note ("LIB-SEQ-LIST-C-PASCAL-REPR", 4u32, "2026-11-05T17:53Z", "compiler", "11-05 13:5x compiler: CORRECTION to the previous note, answered from the source: there is NO Lua fallback for C. lib backend.spi's backend_switch gives each backend its own arm or a 'backend_switch_has_no_<B>_arm_in_lib_spiral' marker, and the compiler's !!!!BackendSwitch (spiral_compiler.fs ~11101) evaluates EVERY arm: the current backend's for its value, the others ONLY FOR THEIR TYPE (comment: needed so compile-time hashmaps keep inference sound). Inside a non-current arm (to_array''s Lua arm, from_try's Lua arm), nested backend_switches still resolve against the CURRENT backend (C), so defaultof/try's missing C arm surfaces as the fatal marker although C never runs Lua code. Consequence: a lib function needs a C arm if it is reachable from ANY arm of a switch a C program reaches, not only the C arm. Two fixes, both open: (a) lib, give the reached helpers C arms (done for defaultof; next base.try, where the Gleam-style 'fn () |> some' is the honest C meaning, since C has no exceptions to catch); (b) compiler, make type-only arm evaluation tolerate the missing-arm marker (it is type-correct: ': t'), so only the current backend's arm can trigger it. (b) would stop this class at the source; it needs a probe of where the marker reaches the output during type-only evaluation.")
inl note_of_lib_seq_list_c_pascal_repr_5 () = Note ("LIB-SEQ-LIST-C-PASCAL-REPR", 5u32, "2026-11-05T17:55Z", "compiler", "11-05 14:0x compiler: SECOND CORRECTION, mechanism only partly determined, so the 13:5x note's fix (b) is WITHDRAWN. Read further (~11127): type-only evaluation of a non-current arm runs with backend set to THAT arm's backend (backend_strings.Add backend, fresh seq), so a switch nested inside a Lua arm resolves against Lua and can only produce a Lua marker, never a C one. The fatal fires only when a 'backend_switch_has_no_C_arm' marker is in the emitted C (RustGapTrace.missing_arm_error), so the gap is on C's real path. 'Reached from' is the lexicographically smallest frame list recorded for that marker (note_missing_arm at EMacro), and its frames name listm'.spi:766-770 / resultm.spi:32. Why C's real evaluation passes through those Lua-arm line ranges is NOT determined (closure creation sites in the trace are one candidate). Established and safe: lib C arms remove the marker where C reaches it (defaultof, done and proven); the next is base.try. Not established: any compiler change; do not act on the 13:5x (b).")
inl note_of_lib_seq_list_c_pascal_repr_6 () = Note ("LIB-SEQ-LIST-C-PASCAL-REPR", 6u32, "2026-11-05T18:09Z", "compiler", "11-05 14:0x compiler: SLICE 2 (base.try C arm, 2-try-c.kitdo) NOT LANDED. The arm itself is the Zig form ('fn () |> some', since C has no exceptions to catch) and round-trips canonical, and the existing catch test stays green. But the new C test fails at gcc: try's signature mentions ex_fn : exn -> option t, and the exn TYPE is a per-backend switch with no C entry, so the C output names Nil / nil / exn / BaseException / Rc / backend_switch as types (main.c:38-50 'unknown type name'). A C try needs a C representation of exn first. That is a design decision (C cannot raise; candidates: an empty struct, or an int error code), not a mechanical arm, so it waits for a decision instead of a guess. The draft stays in drafts\\spiral\\LIB-SEQ-LIST-C-PASCAL-REPR\\2-try-c.kitdo, unlanded. The live lib is unchanged by this slice.")
inl note_of_lib_seq_list_c_pascal_repr_7 () = Note ("LIB-SEQ-LIST-C-PASCAL-REPR", 7u32, "2026-11-05T18:19Z", "compiler", "11-05 14:1x compiler: SLICE 2 still NOT LANDED, and the blocker is now located in the COMPILER, not lib. I added a C entry to exn_switch (C : 'const char *', the text form Zig uses; C never constructs an exn since failures abort). gcc output is unchanged: main.c still declares fields of type Nil / nil / exn / BaseException / Rc (and, in the first run, 'backend_switch' as a type name). The C backend does not resolve a TYPE-level backend_switch macro (':! exn = $''backend_switch (exn_switch)''') to one entry: it emits every entry of the record. So no lib type defined through backend_switch (exn, list', and likely seq') can appear in C output until codegenC resolves type-level backend_switch for its own key, as the other codegens do. That compiler fix is the real first step of this item; the defaultof value arms (slice 1, landed) were unaffected because they are value-level. Draft 2 stays unlanded in drafts\\spiral\\LIB-SEQ-LIST-C-PASCAL-REPR\\2-try-c.kitdo (now with the exn C entry). Next: find where codegenC lowers type macros (CMText 'backend_switch' + CMType record) versus how codegenRust picks its key.")
inl note_of_lib_seq_list_c_pascal_repr_8 () = Note ("LIB-SEQ-LIST-C-PASCAL-REPR", 8u32, "2026-11-05T18:21Z", "compiler", "11-05 14:2x compiler: ROOT GAP FOUND IN codegenC. Every other codegen has a case 'YMacro [Text \"backend_switch \"; Type (YRecord r)]' that picks its own key (Fsharp 13083, Rust 14094/14100, Pascal 15338, Zig 16168, Lean 16849, Gleam 18852, Lua 19972, Wasm 20994, TypeScript 25160, Cpp 27512 via field_try_find \"Cpp\", Python 28511). codegenC (module CodegenC, tyv ~26264) has only the generic 'YMacro a -> concat', so a type-level backend_switch renders as ALL record entries, which is the Nil/nil/exn/BaseException/Rc/backend_switch soup gcc rejected. Program 3 drafted (drafts\\spiral\\LIB-SEQ-LIST-C-PASCAL-REPR\\3-codegenc-type-backend-switch.kitdo, 1 replacement, anchored on codegenC's own 'The C backend doesn''t support stack mutable' line): the same case for key \"C\" (Map.tryPick, since field_try_find lives in CodegenCpp), with the Cpp-style error when the record has no C entry. pack52 = pack51 + 3 building on the box. Then: the try slice (2) on pack52, and a C/Pascal parity review.")
inl note_of_lib_seq_list_c_pascal_repr_9 () = Note ("LIB-SEQ-LIST-C-PASCAL-REPR", 9u32, "2026-11-05T18:35Z", "compiler", "11-05 14:3x compiler: PROGRAM 3 + SLICE 2 PROVEN TOGETHER. pack52 = pack51 + program 3 (codegenC type-level backend_switch); bin core A688EE5AD426A5A0, host A1BF06EB. lib copy with slice 2 (base.try C arm + exn_switch C : 'const char *') gated through Cells with SPIRAL_COMPILER_DLL = pack52 (private daemon port 47933): try_keeps_values (c), try_catches_failures_and_keeps_values (lua, cuda, rust, typescript) and defaultof_scalars (rust, c, pascal) give 'tests: passed 3 failed 0', type errors 0. On installed pack51 the same C test failed at gcc (unknown type names Nil/nil/exn/BaseException/Rc from the unresolved type switch). Order to ship: pack52 install first (C/Pascal examples parity vs pack51 running now), then slice 2 through the lib land window (lib on node needs pack52 too, since the type switch is only resolved for C with the fix).")
inl note_of_lib_seq_list_c_pascal_repr_10 () = Note ("LIB-SEQ-LIST-C-PASCAL-REPR", 10u32, "2026-11-05T18:59Z", "compiler", "11-05 14:5x compiler: pack52 INSTALLED on the BOX (14:49-14:58): SpiralCompilerCore.dll A688EE5AD426A5A0, SpiralCompiler.dll A1BF06EB9AD87F00, both equal=True; undo spiral-bin\\compiler-installs\\pack52-20261105-144931. Parity vs pack51 before install: C + Pascal examples 1034 rows (972 ok + 62 expected errors), 0 compile-status / 0 residual diffs. Handed to main for the node. NEXT: land slice 2 (base.try C arm + exn_switch C entry; canonical draft in the compiler scratchpad libdef\\out\\base.spi, proven 3/3 on pack52) through lib_land_window once the node has pack52.")
inl note_of_lib_seq_list_c_pascal_repr_11 () = Note ("LIB-SEQ-LIST-C-PASCAL-REPR", 11u32, "2026-11-05T19:00Z", "compiler", "11-05 15:0x compiler, SCOPE NOTE: what remains after slice 2 lands is the list' C/Pascal representation itself (list'_switch C entry, probably array_base t as Rust uses Rc<Vec<t>>, plus C arms in about 11 listm' functions: empty', cons', box, rev', fold', fold_back', to_array', item, ...). Now feasible, since pack52's codegenC resolves type-level switches, but it's a few hours with reviews for a P3 with no caller (concat_list already reaches C through concat_right_fold over native lists; utf8_byte_list and seq' concat are the only beneficiaries). Parked at that boundary for this run; the next lane picks it up from here.")
inl note_of_lib_seq_list_c_pascal_repr_12 () = Note ("LIB-SEQ-LIST-C-PASCAL-REPR", 12u32, "2026-11-05T19:25Z", "compiler", "11-05 15:2x compiler: SLICE 2 LANDED (unstaged), after pack52 reached the node (15:09). base.spi: exn_switch gains C : 'const char *' (the text form Zig uses; C never builds an exn, since failures abort), base.try gains the C arm 'fn () |> some' (the Zig form: nothing to catch), and a new C test try_keeps_values. Proven before the land on pack52: try_keeps_values (c) + try_catches_failures_and_keeps_values (lua, cuda, rust, typescript) + defaultof_scalars (rust, c, pascal) 3 passed 0 failed. lib_land_window closed GREEN: 3 consumer signatures unchanged, apps/spiral compiles, document_test 26 passed, type_probe PASS (27 typed), 0 respell drift. Live base.spi E5584788886D -> 952C96278E2E, lib digest 590C666A4EE3CFB7, undo spiral-bin\\lib-land-undo\\20261105-151333. Item state: compiler gap closed (pack52 codegenC type switch), defaultof + try/exn on C; the list' C representation remains (see the 15:0x scope note).")
inl note_of_lib_seq_list_c_pascal_repr_13 () = Note ("LIB-SEQ-LIST-C-PASCAL-REPR", 13u32, "2026-11-05T19:55Z", "compiler", "11-05 15:5x compiler: SLICE 4 LANDED (unstaged): resultm.from_try gains the C arm 'fn () |> ok' (the Zig form) and a C test from_try_keeps_values. Lib copy gate 2 passed 0 failed (from_try_keeps_values on c + the existing catch test), type errors 0. lib_land_window closed GREEN: 7 consumer signatures unchanged, apps/spiral compiles, document_test 26 passed, type_probe PASS (8 typed), 0 respell drift. Live resultm.spi 8014CE936593 -> D8844A7D1117, lib digest 8EBE1BE440A3DE7E, undo spiral-bin\\lib-land-undo\\20261105-154458.")
inl note_of_lib_seq_list_c_pascal_repr_14 () = Note ("LIB-SEQ-LIST-C-PASCAL-REPR", 14u32, "2026-11-05T20:31Z", "compiler", "11-05 16:2x compiler: SLICE 5 LANDED (unstaged): sm'.of_char gains the C arm '!!!!PrimToString(input) : string' (the first draft lacked ': string' -> ETY-ValueRestriction on every target, fixed), plus a C test of_char_is_the_one_character_string_on_c. Lib copy gate 3 passed 0 failed (c + gleam + rust char tests), type errors 0. lib_land_window closed GREEN: 30 consumer signatures unchanged, apps/spiral compiles, document_test 26 passed, type_probe PASS (the to_char_array shadow note is pre-existing), 0 respell drift. Live sm'.spi B0854BB9B5D7 -> 0A15625F655D, lib digest 43B0A30D3337560E, undo spiral-bin\\lib-land-undo\\20261105-161414. END TO END on C: the runtime styled-names probe (reflection::typed_name_snake/kebab over typed_case_name, which goes through styled_name: to_char_list |> listm::fold |> rev |> concat_list) now compiles to C (31,681 bytes), builds with gcc 0 errors and exits 20 = len light_green 11 + len deep-blue 9. The REAL gap was of_char; SPIRAL_RUST_GAP_TRACE=1 named it (the fatal's 'Reached from' named base.try/from_try, whose C arms (slices 2 and 4) are genuine but were not this program's blocker). Landed today on this item: defaultof C/Pascal, try + exn C, from_try C, of_char C, plus compiler pack52 (codegenC type-level backend_switch). Remaining: the list' C/Pascal representation (scope note 15:0x).")
inl note_of_lib_seq_list_c_pascal_repr_15 () = Note ("LIB-SEQ-LIST-C-PASCAL-REPR", 15u32, "2026-11-05T20:34Z", "compiler", "11-05 16:3x compiler: CONSUMER CHECK before going further. In today's C + Pascal examples run (pack51, 1034 rows: 62 compile errors) and the all-backend run (6825 rows), the ONLY missing-arm failures are samples/backend_switch_missing_arm_negative (the deliberate negative). The other C/Pascal errors are type errors (24), parse failures (5), the main-return-type checks (2) and expected-error samples. So no program in the corpus is blocked on a missing C/Pascal lib arm any more, and the list' representation work has no current consumer. Stopping here for this run, at a real stopping point rather than a time limit: the next slice is worth doing when a C/Pascal program actually reaches list' (utf8_byte_list or seq' concat).")
inl compiler_daemon_stale_after_pack () = Task ("COMPILER-DAEMON-STALE-AFTER-PACK", "compiler daemons survive a pack install and serve the old DLL; the install retires them or the daemon checks its DLL hash per request", TaskKind, P1, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "team:compiler,compiler,pack,daemon,cells,reliability", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_compiler_daemon_stale_after_pack () = Planning ("COMPILER-DAEMON-STALE-AFTER-PACK", Minutes 90u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_compiler_daemon_stale_after_pack_1 () = Note ("COMPILER-DAEMON-STALE-AFTER-PACK", 1u32, "2026-11-04T04:19Z", "compiler", "11-04 00:2x compiler (star exchange, tibia #5): after the pack31 install a tibia gate kept failing on the Lua union_tag bug pack31 had fixed, until a rerun with -Compiler process. A warm compiler daemon outlived the DLL swap. Port-per-build (CELLS-DAEMON-PORT-PER-BUILD) does not cover a pack swap at the same path. Fix options: (a) install-pack.ps1 retires the daemons it replaces, (b) the daemon compares its DLL hash on each request and refuses or restarts on a mismatch. (b) also covers manual swaps. Next step: read where test.ps1 and Cells start the daemon and which DLL path they key on.")
inl note_of_compiler_daemon_stale_after_pack_2 () = Note ("COMPILER-DAEMON-STALE-AFTER-PACK", 2u32, "2026-11-04T04:54Z", "compiler", "11-04 01:0x compiler: ROOT CAUSE FOUND AND FIXED (unstaged, apps/cells). The Cells compiler daemon stamped only the HOST SpiralCompiler.dll (mtime, size) for three things: compiler_current? (its per-request restart check), the shadow-copy dir name and build_port. A pack swap changes only SpiralCompilerCore.dll, and the host is byte-identical (6B30D2F5 since 11-02). Node evidence: host mtime 11-02 22:34, core swapped 11-03 20:58, shadow dir 1793673291-319488 named from the host alone. So after an in-place core swap the daemon kept serving, and even a FRESH daemon reused a .complete shadow holding the old core; only -Compiler process escaped it. The box install was never affected: it refuses while daemons run and rebuilds, which rewrites the host. Fix: dll_stamp folds in the sibling SpiralCompilerCore.dll ({newest mtime, phash2 of both sizes and mtimes}; the dir format stays digits-digits, so prune_shadows still matches) and build_port uses dll_stamp. New test: a core-only swap with an OLDER mtime than the host (a preserved-mtime copy, the worst case) is not current and gets a fresh shadow holding the new core. Box: compiler_daemon_test Result: 27 passed (own MIX_BUILD_PATH). Running daemons pick up the new Elixir code only after a restart; the next node pack swap (pack33 after TRAIN18) is the live proof. x: the Spiral port of compiler_daemon (CELLS-PORT-COMPILER-DAEMON) must carry this stamp.")
inl note_of_compiler_daemon_stale_after_pack_3 () = Note ("COMPILER-DAEMON-STALE-AFTER-PACK", 3u32, "2026-11-04T05:48Z", "compiler", "11-04 01:5x compiler: LIVE CONFIRMATION (main, pack33 node swap at 01:45). Two Cells daemons started AFTER the in-place swap ran from %TEMP%\\spiral-cells-compiler\\1793673291-319488, which still held the OLD core A63F4C2A. That is exactly the host-only stamp failure the fix addresses. Main deleted the dir. The node's Elixir does not have the fix yet (unstaged box tree; it reaches the node with the next mirror sync), so until then the swap procedure is to delete the host-stamp shadow dir.")
inl note_of_compiler_daemon_stale_after_pack_4 () = Note ("COMPILER-DAEMON-STALE-AFTER-PACK", 4u32, "2026-11-04T08:52Z", "compiler", "11-04 05:0x compiler: TWIN FIXED (found by x) plus a regression of my own fix. apps/cells/spi/run_notebook.ps1 keyed the warm notebook server's port file by the HOST dll's mtime and length only, so a warm server from before a pack kept serving the old core (x-next5b timeline_serve 3/4 during a core move A63F -> D137, then 4/4 three times fresh). Worse, my 01:0x dll_stamp change had made Elixir's dll_tag (used to name the port file) a phash2 digest that PowerShell cannot compute, so since then run_notebook could not find the warm server at all and fell back. Fix (unstaged): dll_tag is now an explicit '<host mtime>-<host size>-<core mtime>-<core size>' string, computed the same way in compiler_daemon.ex, run_notebook.ps1 and daemon_collision_proof.ps1. dll_stamp (shadow dirs, the per-request current check, build_port) keeps its digest form. Proof: compiler_daemon_test 28 passed (new test: the tag changes on a core-only swap); on the box Elixir served_dll_tag = run_notebook's computation = 1793781634-319488-1793781658-12587008.")
inl compiler_forall_term_hint () = Task ("COMPILER-FORALL-TERM-HINT", "compiler: Cannot apply a forall with a term on a bare prototype operator inside a real block names the fix (real_core::(>))", TaskKind, P2, D2, 100u32, Done, "", "team:compiler,compiler,diagnostics,prototypes", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_compiler_forall_term_hint () = Planning ("COMPILER-FORALL-TERM-HINT", Minutes 60u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_compiler_forall_term_hint_1 () = Note ("COMPILER-FORALL-TERM-HINT", 1u32, "2026-11-04T04:19Z", "compiler", "11-04 00:2x compiler (star exchange, tibia #7): inside ( ) or ~> type-level blocks, bare >, - and + resolve to the typed prototypes and fail with 'Cannot apply a forall with a term'. The trace points at the operator but gives no fix. The memory rule is to use real_core::(>) etc. The error should say so when the forall being applied is a prototype operator inside a real block. That cost tibia 20 min on 11-03. Related closed items with the same message and different causes: SPIRAL-OP-ARG-NAME-WORDS, SPIRAL-EXPORT-PIPE-FORALL. Next step: find where the error is raised in spiral_compiler.fs and what context (real block, op name) is available there.")
inl note_of_compiler_forall_term_hint_2 () = Note ("COMPILER-FORALL-TERM-HINT", 2u32, "2026-11-04T05:08Z", "compiler", "11-04 01:1x compiler: DRAFTED. kit drafts COMPILER-FORALL-TERM-HINT\\1-name-the-real-block-fix.kitdo (1 replacement at the DForall apply arm). The message keeps its first sentence ('Cannot apply a forall with a term.'), so greps still match, and adds: inside a `( ) or ~> block there is no type inference, so a bare >, +, - or = names the typed prototype, a forall; write real_core::(>) etc. there; elsewhere apply the forall's types first. DForall carries only its body E, not the source name, so the hint is conditional rather than naming the operator; the trace already points at it. Repro (box, pack33, lib package): main = ~> ? 2i32 > 1i32 ?> 0i32 |: 1i32 fails with exactly this error; with real_core::(>) 2i32 1i32 it compiles (exit 0). No test text matches the old message. It stacks with the GADT and link programs on live D00B41EE (combined FB4D41D2). It ships in pack34 with them.")
inl note_of_compiler_forall_term_hint_3 () = Note ("COMPILER-FORALL-TERM-HINT", 3u32, "2026-11-04T08:11Z", "compiler", "11-04 04:1x compiler: HANDED TO MAIN as pack34 (5 programs: GADT, link v4, forall hint, format --respell, Zig fast), with the exact -Programs command. Final node review compiler-pack34f: source B33D213B29D2757D, core dll 0758BAF496AE4E7D, host 6B30D2F5; contracts Rust 8 and Zig 620: 0 differing cells; examples F#: only load-timeout flips, verified on a 180 s rerun. Box dry run ok. Staged at spiral-bin-candidates\\pack34. Done when installed; regression samples land right after the install.")
inl note_of_compiler_forall_term_hint_4 () = Note ("COMPILER-FORALL-TERM-HINT", 4u32, "2026-11-04T08:47Z", "compiler", "11-04 04:5x compiler: DONE. pack34 installed by main at 04:4x on the box (core 0758BAF496AE4E7D, equal to the reviewed candidate; undo compiler-installs\\pack34-20261104-043420), the node (in-place swap, stale shadow dirs removed) and vultr (built from B33D source). Regression samples landed with it (unstaged): samples/gadt_implicit_existential_negative and samples/forall_real_block_operator_negative, plus their matrix ExpectedErrors entries. Box run on pack34: both rows compile=error with the expected text (no WRONG-ERROR).")
inl formatter_emits_respell_spelling () = Task ("FORMATTER-EMITS-RESPELL-SPELLING", "formatter output passes format --check --respell-drift: no fun/exists/true/false that the land respell step rejects", TaskKind, P2, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "team:compiler,compiler,formatter,respell,edition3", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_formatter_emits_respell_spelling () = Planning ("FORMATTER-EMITS-RESPELL-SPELLING", Minutes 90u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_formatter_emits_respell_spelling_1 () = Note ("FORMATTER-EMITS-RESPELL-SPELLING", 1u32, "2026-11-04T04:19Z", "compiler", "11-04 00:2x compiler (star exchange, fiscal #6): the formatter emits fun, while the lib land window's respell step rejects fun (with exists and true/false). So a freshly formatted file fails a land until it is respelled by hand. Fiscal and tibia both lost a land to this on 11-03. Fix: the formatter emits the respelled forms (backslash lambdas, !+/!-), or format --respell is part of the format pass. Next step: list each spelling that format emits and respell-drift rejects, and check which one the edition-3 docs call canonical.")
inl note_of_formatter_emits_respell_spelling_2 () = Note ("FORMATTER-EMITS-RESPELL-SPELLING", 2u32, "2026-11-04T05:10Z", "compiler", "11-04 01:1x compiler: MEASURED, then DRAFTED. The formatter does not turn respelled code back into old words: format keeps \\x => and !+ as written, and a respelled probe passes --respell-drift after format. The trap is that plain format never respells: input written with fun/true/exists (by hand or by a generator) comes out formatted but still in words, and the land window's respell_drift stage rejects it. format --spelling symbols respells but does no layout, so no single command produced the form the gate accepts. Draft: kit drafts FORMATTER-EMITS-RESPELL-SPELLING\\1-format-respell-flag.kitdo (3 replacements). New flag format --respell: convert to Symbols, then formatText; also --check --respell. It honours ///- --no-respell (optsOutOfRespell), and plain format and --spelling keep their current behaviour, so no bulk diffs. Lanes run 'format --respell <files>' before requesting a window. Stacks with the other three pack34 programs (combined CB333D5F).")
inl note_of_formatter_emits_respell_spelling_3 () = Note ("FORMATTER-EMITS-RESPELL-SPELLING", 3u32, "2026-11-04T08:11Z", "compiler", "11-04 04:1x compiler: HANDED TO MAIN as pack34 (5 programs: GADT, link v4, forall hint, format --respell, Zig fast), with the exact -Programs command. Final node review compiler-pack34f: source B33D213B29D2757D, core dll 0758BAF496AE4E7D, host 6B30D2F5; contracts Rust 8 and Zig 620: 0 differing cells; examples F#: only load-timeout flips, verified on a 180 s rerun. Box dry run ok. Staged at spiral-bin-candidates\\pack34. Done when installed; regression samples land right after the install.")
inl note_of_formatter_emits_respell_spelling_4 () = Note ("FORMATTER-EMITS-RESPELL-SPELLING", 4u32, "2026-11-04T08:47Z", "compiler", "11-04 04:5x compiler: DONE. pack34 installed by main at 04:4x on the box (core 0758BAF496AE4E7D, equal to the reviewed candidate; undo compiler-installs\\pack34-20261104-043420), the node (in-place swap, stale shadow dirs removed) and vultr (built from B33D source). Regression samples landed with it (unstaged): samples/gadt_implicit_existential_negative and samples/forall_real_block_operator_negative, plus their matrix ExpectedErrors entries. Box run on pack34: both rows compile=error with the expected text (no WRONG-ERROR).")
inl kit_agile_cas_retry () = Task ("KIT-AGILE-CAS-RETRY", "agile state lock stale only when its holder is gone (root cause of the fleet CAS conflicts); retry dropped, batching split to KIT-AGILE-SET-NOTE-BATCH", TaskKind, P0, D2, 90u32, Blocked, "", "team:kit,agile,fleet-papercut", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_kit_agile_cas_retry () = Planning ("KIT-AGILE-CAS-RETRY", Minutes 90u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_kit_agile_cas_retry_1 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-CAS-RETRY", 1u32, "2026-11-04T04:20Z", "kit", "Star exchange 11-04: x-dumps (8 retries), fiscal (5 of 13 writes), main (3 of 36 sets), compiler, tibia: every lane hit \"state/agile.spi changed since it was read; nothing written\" while grooming at the same time, and an identical rerun succeeded every time. DoD: (1) note, set and edit re-read and re-apply up to 3 times on a CAS conflict, then exit with a distinct conflict code; (2) a batched `agile set+note` (one read, one write per groom item, tibia #11); (3) a notebook test with two writers racing on a scratch copy. Not a lock: the CAS is correct, so the retry stays inside the writer. Also on list --json: print the field shapes (tasks wrapper, tags/deps one comma-joined string, notes a count) in help (x #8, compiler #10).")
inl note_of_kit_agile_cas_retry_2 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-CAS-RETRY", 2u32, "2026-11-04T04:52Z", "kit", "11-04 00:5x ROOT CAUSE FOUND, so a retry would only hide it. kit_agile_mutation's state lock (state/agile.spi.lock) treated ANY lock older than 10 s as stale and stole it, while a write to the 13 MB store takes far longer under load (lanes report >120 s). So a second writer took the lock from a live one, both computed from the same read, and the later commit hit the CAS check (\"changed since it was read\"). FIX (unstaged; type-checked on vultr pack31, as-is and test bodies exit 0; an injected u64/i32 mismatch was rejected, exit 5): the 9-line host block becomes typed Spiral. The lock dir now holds `holder` = \"<pid> <start ms>\". A lock is stale only when its holder pid is dead (kit_process_alive, linked) or it's over 10 min old (pid-reuse guard). A lock with no holder line (an older kit binary) keeps the old 10 s rule. The wait budget goes 30 s -> 5 min. Host code left: two one-line primitives (create_dir, remove_dir). Notebook test a_lock_is_stale_only_when_its_holder_is_gone_or_ten_minutes_old uses a stub liveness, so no sysinfo in the cell. Next: rides TRAIN19 (TRAIN18's snapshot was 00:43). Then the in-process retry plus a distinct exit code, and batched set+note, as belt and braces.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_cas_retry_3 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-CAS-RETRY", 3u32, "2026-11-04T05:15Z", "resilience", "11-04 01:2x resilience BATTLE-TEST DATA (from tonight orphan sweep + BEAM-GUARD grooming, during active 7-lane concurrent grooming at box 100pct CPU). Ran 69 edit-tags writes (53 bare-main->team:main canonicalizations + 16 multi-lane primary-owner assignments) on the 13MB agile.spi, each wrapped in a 4-try IMMEDIATE-retry loop (no sleep/backoff) on non-ok output: 69/69 succeeded, 0 exhausted retries. Plus ~8 note/set writes same window, 0 fail. FINDINGS for the implementation: (1) immediate retry (no sleep) is sufficient - the CAS window is short and a rerun of the identical call wins, matching main n9 (3/36 sets rejected, all passed on retry) + compiler/x/tibia reports. (2) built-in retry should cap at ~4 tries and return the LAST error if exhausted (not loop forever). (3) BATCHED set/edit is the bigger win: 69 sequential single writes each re-serialize the whole 13MB file (0.7-1.7s each, worse under load, occasional 120s spill); a batch verb applying N (id,field,value) edits under ONE mkdir-lock+CAS would cut 69 rewrites to 1. (4) kit agile wip scans files (~152s under load) - keep it off the hot path. Recommend the batch API carry a list applied in one transaction. I can be the battle-test harness when it lands (re-run the 69-edit canonicalization pass).")
inl note_of_kit_agile_cas_retry_4 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-CAS-RETRY", 4u32, "2026-11-04T05:33Z", "kit", "11-04 01:4x DECISION (kit): the in-process retry and distinct exit code are DROPPED from this item. With the lock fix (n2), the lock is held across the whole route (read, compute, commit) and is no longer stolen from a live holder, so a CAS conflict can come only from an older binary still using the 10 s rule. That ends when TRAIN19 installs, since every lane runs the same %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\bin\\kit.exe. A retry would only mask a regression of the lock rule. The CAS check stays as the guard. The batched set+note moves to KIT-AGILE-SET-NOTE-BATCH: both runners read fixed argv positions (task_notes.spi note_request; set is the kit_agile_routes host route), so a combined call needs typed arguments first (the S6 shape). DONE WHEN: TRAIN19 installs and a groom by 2+ lanes at once shows zero \"changed since it was read\". parked: waiting on TRAIN19.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_cas_retry_5 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-CAS-RETRY", 5u32, "2026-11-04T05:54Z", "kit", "11-04 02:0x: the n2 lock fix put kit_agile_mutation at 1294 generated lines (s7 node precheck; gate 1000), because the lock and lib file helpers inlined into the mutation crate. It was pulled from TRAIN19 (parked copy in drafts\\kit\\OVER-GATE-parked-1104b) and REBUILT as its own linked crate kit_agile_lock (lock.spi: the holder-pid lock, stale rule and test). It exports kit_agile_lock_taken / kit_agile_lock_released (single string arg, links); kit_agile_mutation keeps only two linked calls. vultr --check exit 0 for both, plus the lock test body. Rides TRAIN20 after a node size precheck.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_cas_retry_6 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-CAS-RETRY", 6u32, "2026-11-04T07:02Z", "kit", "11-04 03:0x: node size run: kit_agile_lock 390 lines, kit_agile_mutation 932 (was 1294 with the lock inlined); vultr --check exit 0. Code complete in the tree; set Blocked on TRAIN20 (notebooks + install), then a 2-lane concurrent groom confirms zero \"changed since it was read\". Blocked so the stop gate stops re-offering an item with nothing left to do before the train.")
inl compiler_link_inline_diagnostic () = Task ("COMPILER-LINK-INLINE-DIAGNOSTIC", "compiler: a linked export silently inlined (direct record arg/result, curried fn) reports why it was not linked", TaskKind, P1, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "team:compiler,compiler,linked-units,diagnostics,kit", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_compiler_link_inline_diagnostic () = Planning ("COMPILER-LINK-INLINE-DIAGNOSTIC", Minutes 120u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_compiler_link_inline_diagnostic_1 () = Note ("COMPILER-LINK-INLINE-DIAGNOSTIC", 1u32, "2026-11-04T04:22Z", "compiler", "11-04 00:2x compiler (star exchange, kit #2): a linked export whose argument or result is a direct non-primitive record (a record with a string field) or a curried function is silently INLINED into the caller instead of linked, with no diagnostic. kit_job_entry grew to 1350 lines and lost 2 deps at rustc time, costing a 3 h precheck. The shapes that link are arrays of records and tuple args (memory spiral-link-boundary-shapes). Fix: emit a warning, or an error under a strict flag, at unit-graph time: 'export X not linked: <field or arg> is not a boundary shape; use an array or a tuple'. Next step: find where the unit-graph builder decides inline vs extern (ExternCalls, unitGraphJson guard) and add the reason there.")
inl note_of_compiler_link_inline_diagnostic_2 () = Note ("COMPILER-LINK-INLINE-DIAGNOSTIC", 2u32, "2026-11-04T05:04Z", "compiler", "11-04 01:0x compiler: DRAFTED and under node review (job compiler-linkwarn, control = pack33). Program: kit drafts COMPILER-LINK-INLINE-DIAGNOSTIC\\1-warn-inlined-linked-export.kitdo (3 replacements, stacks on the GADT program). At the peval call site, a call whose target is a linked export (ExternCalls.lookup hits) but whose types ExternCalls.callable refuses used to inline silently. It now prints once per export per build (the dedup set is cleared where ExternCalls.current is set): 'warning: <file:line:col>: the linked export <unit::name> is inlined here instead of called: its argument|result holds a function|a record|... (only a record whose fields are all primitives crosses, as a struct). A link: boundary takes plain values, arrays of records and tuples.' The reason mirrors callable exactly (plain, struct_fields, single), and codegen is unchanged. Fixture: linked_records plus a model export apply_twice taking a function, called from app. Prediction: exactly one warning per backend, app code byte-identical between arms.")
inl note_of_compiler_link_inline_diagnostic_3 () = Note ("COMPILER-LINK-INLINE-DIAGNOSTIC", 3u32, "2026-11-04T07:32Z", "compiler", "11-04 03:3x compiler: v4 PROVEN (node job compiler-pack34d; base pack33 D00B41EE -> source F89F4D52, core A13FA566). v2 warned only for single-argument exports. v3 added a curried lookup, which found nothing, because noteDefinitionSource records only single-parameter functions, so a curried export's unit-graph target was (\"\",\"\") and never entered the importer's calls map. v4 keeps a separate curriedDefinitionSources table: the unit graph falls back to it for the export's target, and the call site warns from it. lookup (the linking path) never reads it, so a curried export can never be linked (a lookup on its inner lambda would have passed callable with a single argument and emitted a wrong call). Fixture (linked_records plus apply_twice (f : i32 -> i32) (x : i32) and apply_once (f : i32 -> i32)): Rust, Zig and Gleam each print exactly 2 warnings at main.spi:11:21 ('model_apply_twice ... its definition is curried, so the call has no single argument to pass') and 11:40 ('model_apply_once ... its argument holds a function'). App code is byte-identical to pack33 on all three; contracts Rust 8 and Zig 620: 0 differing cells. Ships in pack34 (final review compiler-pack34f running).")
inl note_of_compiler_link_inline_diagnostic_4 () = Note ("COMPILER-LINK-INLINE-DIAGNOSTIC", 4u32, "2026-11-04T08:11Z", "compiler", "11-04 04:1x compiler: HANDED TO MAIN as pack34 (5 programs: GADT, link v4, forall hint, format --respell, Zig fast), with the exact -Programs command. Final node review compiler-pack34f: source B33D213B29D2757D, core dll 0758BAF496AE4E7D, host 6B30D2F5; contracts Rust 8 and Zig 620: 0 differing cells; examples F#: only load-timeout flips, verified on a 180 s rerun. Box dry run ok. Staged at spiral-bin-candidates\\pack34. Done when installed; regression samples land right after the install.")
inl note_of_compiler_link_inline_diagnostic_5 () = Note ("COMPILER-LINK-INLINE-DIAGNOSTIC", 5u32, "2026-11-04T08:47Z", "compiler", "11-04 04:5x compiler: DONE. pack34 installed by main at 04:4x on the box (core 0758BAF496AE4E7D, equal to the reviewed candidate; undo compiler-installs\\pack34-20261104-043420), the node (in-place swap, stale shadow dirs removed) and vultr (built from B33D source). Regression samples landed with it (unstaged): samples/gadt_implicit_existential_negative and samples/forall_real_block_operator_negative, plus their matrix ExpectedErrors entries. Box run on pack34: both rows compile=error with the expected text (no WRONG-ERROR).")
