inl note_of_xdumps_recovery_pass2_1 () = Note ("XDUMPS-RECOVERY-PASS2", 1u32, "2026-11-04T04:30Z", "x-dumps", "Research 2026-11-04 found a live legit path the first sweep missed: cdn.syndication.twimg.com/tweet-result?id=ID&token=any recovers full text for still-LIVE accounts (verified on @fc1943s). Plan: diff CDX-known tweet IDs vs our captured-with-text set per live account, replay the gap through tweet-result (throttled 1-2 rps, public embed, no auth/bot bypass). geygey_hadid is SUSPENDED (tombstone) so its only new avenue is the Archive Team twitterstream bulk dataset grepped by numeric user id (1% sample). Confirmed dead: Nitter (C&D Aug 2026), Google/Bing cache, oEmbed, DocNow rehydration.")
inl note_of_xdumps_recovery_pass2_2 () = Note ("XDUMPS-RECOVERY-PASS2", 2u32, "2026-11-04T04:43Z", "x-dumps", "ROOT CAUSE found 2026-11-04: cdx.rs captures_by_id filtered to statuscode 200/- so tweet_ids never included 302 login-wall ids; the live stage (which already uses cdn.syndication tweet-result) never tried them. Fix: tweet_ids now reads all status ids. Scope per account: fc1943s NEW~1031 (302s), i574n ~1, juhondacivic/stewshka 0, geygey_hadid 19425 but SUSPENDED (live=tombstone, 0 text; needs twitterstream). 30-id sample of the fc1943s gap = 22 live/7 deleted/1 empty. Running live+context+timeline for fc1943s+i574n then merge+world on vultr, throttled 1.5rps, network-bound/low-CPU. media downloads skipped (disk-safe); text backfill later.")
inl note_of_xdumps_recovery_pass2_3 () = Note ("XDUMPS-RECOVERY-PASS2", 3u32, "2026-11-04T05:00Z", "x-dumps", "DONE for reachable 2026-11-04: ran fixed live+context+timeline for fc1943s+i574n -> account 72784485 1950->2715 (+765 recovered). fc1943s 2350 alive/2012 gone of 4362; i574n 599/245 of 844. merge+world rebuilt: 163307 posts. Remaining unrecoverable=4896 (72784485 only 54 left; geygey 3417 SUSPENDED, juhondacivic 1259, stewshka 87, 1637 79) - these are kept in joined/unrecoverable.ndjson and filtered from scroll, which is the users intended handling for unreachable ones, so NOT chasing twitterstream (1% sample, hundreds of GB, low ROI). geygey suspended = genuinely unreachable by live.")
inl note_of_xdumps_recovery_pass2_4 () = Note ("XDUMPS-RECOVERY-PASS2", 4u32, "2026-11-04T05:14Z", "x-dumps", "Recovery output QA PASSED 2026-11-04: account 72784485 = 2715 posts, 0 empty-text non-lost (no garbage), 2005 live-sourced; sampled 2017-2023 all real text (EN+PT, dev-themed, consistent with account). Clean. Media for recovered tweets not downloaded (media stage skipped for disk safety) - text is complete; media backfill is a minor follow-up.")
inl note_of_xdumps_recovery_pass2_5 () = Note ("XDUMPS-RECOVERY-PASS2", 5u32, "2026-11-04T09:53Z", "x-dumps", "Extended recovery to the other live accounts 2026-11-04: probed their lost-ids via tweet-result. juhondacivic: 0/20 recoverable (all tombstone) - its 1259 lost are genuine deletions/suspension, correctly indexed+filtered, no re-run. stewshka: 13/20 recoverable (~65%) - its ~87 lost were PENDING (rate-limited/unfetched) in the big harvest, not deletions. Running live+context+timeline+merge+world for stewshka to recover ~57 tweets (detached pid 2270697, waiter bmicktg18). So recovery now covers all 4 live accounts: fc1943s/i574n (+765 done), stewshka (~57 running); geygey suspended (indexed); juhondacivic lost = real deletions (indexed).")
inl note_of_xdumps_recovery_pass2_6 () = Note ("XDUMPS-RECOVERY-PASS2", 6u32, "2026-11-04T09:58Z", "x-dumps", "stewshka recovery DONE 2026-11-04: recovered 26 pending-not-deleted tweets (lost 87->61). It ALSO exposed an emitter heal: recovered tweets kept wayback stale lost-reason -> flagged lost:true despite having text -> were being HIDDEN from the scroll. Fixed world.rs: lost is now content-based (text OR media present = never lost; lost only when no content). Re-verified world: lost:true=4860 (was 4896), 0 lost-with-content, 0 empty-content-shown. Recovery now covers all reachable: fc1943s/i574n +765, stewshka +26; juhondacivic 1259 + geygey 3417 = genuine deletions/suspension (indexed+filtered).")
inl transformer_zig_backend () = Task ("TRANSFORMER-ZIG-BACKEND", "Typed transformer on the Zig backend: lib Zig arms for the loader path (file_system.read_bytes, convert.f32_of_bits, unicode.scalar_text) with ///> zig test targets, tokens identical to the C build, and a load-gated Zig vs C speed sample for BACKEND-METRICS-MODEL (compiler ask 11-04)", Story, P2, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "transformer,zig,lib,backends,benchmarks,team:tibia,tibia", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_transformer_zig_backend () = Planning ("TRANSFORMER-ZIG-BACKEND", Minutes 180u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_transformer_zig_backend_1 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-ZIG-BACKEND", 1u32, "2026-11-04T04:47Z", "tibia", "11-04 00:47 tibia, FIRST RUN (vultr scratch stage zigab, compiler pack32-src-before 21e08222): the Zig build first failed at compile time (file_system.read_bytes reaches am'::from_vec, a Rust-only backend_switch), then at run time on convert.f32_of_bits and unicode.scalar_text (their default arms call failwith). With three scratch arms (readFileAlloc as in read_all_text; @as(f32, @bitCast(bits)); utf8_text with a spiral_gpa one-byte string), tf-zig output is byte-identical to tf-c on stories260K and stories15M. Single unquiet runs (load ~4, another lane's rustc): C -Ofast 1325 ms, Zig ReleaseFast 4510 ms on 15M. perf: loading is negligible; C spends 81% in the vectorized dot_l26, while Zig inlines everything into spiralMain and layers_from_18. Hypothesis: strict float mode blocks vectorizing the dot reduction (gcc -Ofast reassociates). A 4-arm load-gated A/B (c-Ofast, c-O3, zig, zig with @setFloatMode(.optimized)) is RUNNING on vultr, started 00:47.")
inl note_of_transformer_zig_backend_2 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-ZIG-BACKEND", 2u32, "2026-11-04T05:11Z", "tibia", "11-04 01:11 tibia, FLOAT MODE IS THE LEVER (static, objdump on vultr, same pack32 compile): packed ymm float ops / packed FMAs per binary: C -Ofast 56 / 17; C -O3 (strict IEEE) 28 / 2; Zig ReleaseFast (strict) 35 / 0; Zig with @setFloatMode(.optimized) prepended to every fn by a sed probe 1255 / 1144. All four print byte-identical tokens on stories15M (cmp, ab4 pre-check). So the Zig vs C gap of the first unquiet run (4.5 s vs 1.3 s) is strict float semantics blocking the dot reduction's vectorization, not Zig codegen. The fair pairs are C -O3 vs Zig and C -Ofast vs Zig-fm. Wall-clock 4-arm A/B is RUNNING (vultr, nohup, ab4.out, 8 rotated rounds, load-gated); vultr is at load 9-10 from other lanes' rustc and checks, so rows may be contaminated, and load is logged per row. For compiler, once the timings are in: an opt-in fast-float switch for the Zig backend (emitting @setFloatMode(.optimized), the analogue of gcc -Ofast) is what makes Zig a fair hot-loop backend for the transformer. Draft lib v1 (Zig arms) is being gated on the node (tibia-zig-gate-1).")
inl note_of_transformer_zig_backend_3 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-ZIG-BACKEND", 3u32, "2026-11-04T05:39Z", "tibia", "11-04 01:39 tibia, 4-ARM A/B DONE BUT LOAD-CONTAMINATED (vultr, stories15M, 8 rotated rounds; the 20-min quiet wait expired at load 3.2 and ALL 32 rows ran at load > 1.5, so these are ordering evidence, not a measurement): C -Ofast 1167 ms, Zig with @setFloatMode(.optimized) 3202, C -O3 (strict IEEE) 3462, Zig ReleaseFast strict 4567; all four outputs byte-identical. Reading: (1) float mode is the dominant lever for C as well, since strict C -O3 is ~3x slower than -Ofast; (2) strict vs strict, Zig is ~1.3x behind C -O3; (3) optimized Zig beats strict C but stays ~2.7x behind C -Ofast despite 1144 packed FMAs, so a second factor remains. Candidates: the sed probe put @setFloatMode only on top-level fn lines, not on the inlined loop bodies in layers_from_18 as compiled; bounds-checked slice indexing (ReleaseFast removes safety checks, so less likely); exp/sqrt through compiler_rt (0.56% in the profile, so not it). NEXT: perf annotate tf-zig-fm's hottest loop against C's dot_l26 on a quiet vultr, then a clean 16-round rerun. No compiler ask until the clean numbers exist.")
inl note_of_transformer_zig_backend_4 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-ZIG-BACKEND", 4u32, "2026-11-04T05:43Z", "tibia", "11-04 01:43 tibia, SECOND FACTOR FOUND: Spiral's Zig prelude spiralIndex (fn spiralIndex(len: usize, i: anytype) usize { if (i < 0 or i >= len) std.process.exit(3); return @intCast(i); }, 167 call sites in out.zig) runs a bounds check with a process exit on EVERY array access, even in ReleaseFast. perf annotate of tf-zig-fm's hottest loop (the 768-wide dot, cmp $0x300) shows scalar vfmadd231ss behind three per-iteration branches (js on a negative i, a signed cmp/sbb against len, a second cmp), all jumping to the exit block, so the reduction cannot vectorize. PROBE tf-zig-fmu = optimized float mode plus spiralIndex with the check removed (sed, probe only): output byte-identical to C, 3223 packed FMAs (C -Ofast has 17 in a smaller binary), and 4 rotated rounds at load ~3 against the same-load arms: C -Ofast 1578/1356/1190/1151 ms, zig-fm 4139/3126/3403/3187, zig-fmu 1834/1633/1214/1277. So Zig reaches C -Ofast speed once both levers are off: strict float mode and per-access exit checks. Both are compiler/codegen policy (the prelude and float mode), so they go to compiler as a finding. The lib arms (draft v1) stay the tibia part. Clean 16-round numbers still need a quiet vultr.")
inl note_of_transformer_zig_backend_5 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-ZIG-BACKEND", 5u32, "2026-11-04T06:03Z", "tibia", "11-04 02:0x tibia, NODE GATE tibia-zig-gate-1 READ: unicode is clean (positive 9/9, 0 type errors; mutant fails 2 of 9, the zig rows). file_system positive failed only its two zig float readers (read_f32s, map_f32s). That is a gate-design artifact: the job put one draft at a time into the mirror, so convert.spi was live (no Zig f32_of_bits) while file_system ran. Its mutant run was VOID (could not start the compiler: lock_timeout). convert failed widening_conversions_agree_across_native_backends on zig with 'Cannot parse the literal as: i32', a test this draft does not touch, so a baseline run of the live file decides whether it predates the draft. BUDGET: by definition name, convert and unicode have no new over-budget definitions; file_system v1 added 3 (zig_read_bytes 290, zig_write_bytes 296, write_bytes 296). V2 (drafts TRANSFORMER-ZIG-BACKEND v2): a zig_io () global helper, and write_bytes split into C/Rust plus fallback_write_bytes (Zig and the failwith), giving 0 new over-budget and 0 respell. RUNNING: box gate boxgate4 (test-suite window, -Compiler process, all three drafts applied together) with the live-convert baseline first, then positive and mutant per module. Compiler filed ZIG-FAST-PROFILE (P1) for the float mode and spiralIndex levers; its acceptance run is this item's quiet 16-round square.")
inl note_of_transformer_zig_backend_6 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-ZIG-BACKEND", 6u32, "2026-11-04T07:26Z", "tibia", "11-04 03:2x tibia, LIB ARMS LANDED (v2, one land): convert 99C7ABF0D2C2 -> FD1CEE458B7E (Zig f32_of_bits = @as(f32, @bitCast(bits)), ///> zig on f32_of_bits_decodes_ieee_754), unicode 7857B89E3956 -> DCBB8F58895D (zig_byte_text plus a Zig scalar_text arm through utf8_text, ///> zig on both char_of tests), file_system 4360A1E93B85 -> 76CA7B4DE0CB (zig_io global helper, zig_read_bytes in read_bytes, zig_write_bytes through fallback_write_bytes for the cell budget, ///> zig on the four byte tests, read_bytes_reads_a_file_on_rust renamed read_bytes_reads_a_written_text_file). Proof: box gate boxgate4 (test-suite window, -Compiler process, all three drafts together): live-convert BASELINE already fails widening_conversions_agree_across_native_backends on rust (Cannot parse the literal as: i32; reported to compiler) and try_convert_2 on cuda (the box Kepler limit); draft convert 8/9 with only that old widening failure, and the mutant adds exactly f32_of_bits_decodes_ieee_754 zig; unicode 9/9, and the mutant fails exactly the two zig char_of rows; file_system passes every zig row, and the mutant fails exactly the three write_bytes-dependent zig rows; find_parent, find_parent_2 and trace_file fail only in the Temp scratch layout (they look for a workspace folder and README.md) and passed on the node mirror. Budget: 0 new over-budget definitions; respell 0. Close green: consumer compiles, document_test 22, type_probe 3 modules (9 + 9 + 42 typed) 0 errors, 0 respell; undo lib-land-undo 20261104-031954. So apps/transformer now builds on Zig from the live lib with no scratch patches. LEFT: the clean 16-round quiet-vultr square (c-Ofast, zig-fm, zig-fmu, c-O3), which is also the acceptance run of compiler's ZIG-FAST-PROFILE.")
inl note_of_transformer_zig_backend_7 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-ZIG-BACKEND", 7u32, "2026-11-04T08:54Z", "tibia", "11-04 04:54 tibia, CLEAN 16-ROUND SQUARE (vultr, stories15M, 4 arms rotated, load-gated: waited 90 s to load 0.34, 0 of 64 rows above load 1.5, all four outputs byte-identical): C -Ofast mean 1050.0 / median 1024.5 ms; Zig fast probe (sed: @setFloatMode(.optimized) and unchecked spiralIndex) 1130.2 / 1139; Zig with optimized floats but checked index 2908.6 / 2916; C -O3 strict 3054.6 / 3055. So with both levers off, Zig is +7.6% (mean) / +11.2% (median) behind gcc -Ofast and 2.7x ahead of strict C; the index check alone costs ~2.6x. Compiler shipped the levers as SPIRAL_ZIG_FAST=1 (pack34, ZIG-FAST-PROFILE). The box compiler (6B30D2F5) honors it (69 setFloatMode, spiralIndex check dropped); vultr's installed compiler is still 21e08222 and ignores it (reported to compiler). The profile build (compiled on the box, built on vultr with zig ReleaseFast) prints byte-identical tokens and has 3223 packed FMAs, the same as the probe. RUNNING: the acceptance square C -Ofast vs zig-prof (probe as bridge), 16 rotated rounds, load-gated, detached on vultr (sq16p).")
inl note_of_transformer_zig_backend_8 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-ZIG-BACKEND", 8u32, "2026-11-04T10:57Z", "tibia", "11-04 06:57 tibia, ACCEPTANCE SQUARE: NOT MET. vultr quiet (waited 30 s to load 0.32, 0 of 48 rows above load 1.5), stories15M, 16 rounds, 3 arms rotated, all outputs byte-identical. C -Ofast mean 973.4 / median 949 ms; Zig profile (SPIRAL_ZIG_FAST=1, pack34, compiled with the box's installed compiler, zig ReleaseFast on vultr) 1103.4 / 1096 = +13.4% mean / +15.5% median; sed probe (same two levers) 1096.8 / 1092 = +12.7% / +15.1%. The earlier clean square (05:0x) had the probe at +7.6% / +11.2%. So across two clean squares Zig with both levers sits 8-15% behind gcc -Ofast, outside the 10% bar. The profile equals the probe within noise, so it implements both levers faithfully. The remaining gap is a third factor, not these two. Candidates for the next probe (perf annotate of the hottest loop in both binaries): gcc -Ofast also sets -fno-math-errno and reciprocal/approximate math, and the loop shapes may differ (dot_l26 unrolling and the vector width chosen by LLVM vs gcc). No tibia code change needed; this is codegen/toolchain territory.")
inl note_of_transformer_zig_backend_9 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-ZIG-BACKEND", 9u32, "2026-11-04T15:16Z", "tibia", "11-04 11:16 tibia, DONE (every part of the title has evidence): (1) lib Zig arms for the loader path LANDED green 11-04 03:2x: convert.f32_of_bits, unicode.scalar_text, file_system.read_bytes/write_bytes, each with ///> zig test targets (box gate boxgate4, -Compiler process). (2) Tokens identical to the C build: every arm of both squares printed byte-identical text (stories15M). (3) The load-gated Zig vs C speed sample for BACKEND-METRICS-MODEL: two clean 16-round vultr squares (05:0x: 0 of 64 rows above load 1.5; 07:0x: 0 of 48), Zig with both levers at +7.6 to +15.5 percent behind gcc -Ofast and 2.7x ahead of strict C; the index check alone costs about 2.6x. The within-10-percent bar and the third factor (full unroll, compiler_rt exp) belong to compiler's ZIG-FAST-PROFILE, not to this item.")
inl kit_agile_set_note_batch () = Task ("KIT-AGILE-SET-NOTE-BATCH", "agile set+note in one call and one write: typed arguments for the note runner and the set route, then a composite verb", TaskKind, P1, D3, 0u32, Planned, "KIT-ENTRY-X-S6", "team:kit,agile,entry-x", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_kit_agile_set_note_batch () = Planning ("KIT-AGILE-SET-NOTE-BATCH", Minutes 120u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_kit_agile_set_note_batch_1 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-SET-NOTE-BATCH", 1u32, "2026-11-04T05:33Z", "kit", "Split from KIT-AGILE-CAS-RETRY (11-04): a groom writes the 13 MB store twice per item (set, then note). Step 1: task_notes.spi note_request_from (words : list string), pure, with the runner passing argv as a list (the x shape). Step 2: the set route (kit_agile_routes host) as typed Spiral taking the same list. Step 3: `agile set <root> <id> <pct> <status> --note <text> --as <author>` applies both edits to one read and commits once, under one lock hold.")
inl cic_quotients () = Task ("CIC-QUOTIENTS", "kernel quotients: Lean's built-in Quot, Quot.mk, Quot.lift, Quot.ind as kernel-only declarations (admitted only once Eq exists, like Lean's quot init), with the lift and ind reduction rules in whnf, checked against lean by the oracle", Story, P1, D5, 100u32, Done, "CIC-INDUCTIVES,CIC-REDUCE", "lean,kernel,typefeatureset,team:main,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_cic_quotients () = Planning ("CIC-QUOTIENTS", Minutes 240u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_cic_quotients_1 () = Note ("CIC-QUOTIENTS", 1u32, "2026-11-04T05:50Z", "main", "11-04 01:5x main: added after the oracle reached 94/94. Quotients are the last kernel primitive Lean 4 has beyond inductives (nat/string literals are extensions). DoD: a quot_kind union (no name strings), admit_quotients refuses without Eq, Quot.lift f h (Quot.mk r a) reduces to f a and Quot.ind likewise, a typed refusal when an ordinary declaration claims a Quot name, plus oracle entries that lean confirms.")
inl note_of_cic_quotients_2 () = Note ("CIC-QUOTIENTS", 2u32, "2026-11-04T07:06Z", "main", "11-04 02:5x main: QUOTIENTS IN (unstaged, spiral apps/kernel). env.spi: quot_kind = QuotType | QuotMk | QuotLift | QuotInd, a decl case Quotient (name, kind, levels, type), and quot_kind_of. Reduction dispatches on the kind, never on name strings. typing.spi: quotient_decls () builds Lean's four types (Quot.{u} {A : Sort u} (r : A -> A -> Prop) : Sort u; Quot.mk; Quot.lift.{u,v} with the respect hypothesis over Eq.{v}; Quot.ind.{u} into Prop). admit_quotients refuses without Eq ('quotients need Eq declared first'), and an ordinary declaration of a Quotient refuses ('only the kernel declares quotients: Quot'), the Lean quot-init rules. reduce.spi: constant_step/quot_step/quot_reduce; Quot.lift with major (arg 5) whnf-ing to Quot.mk _ _ a reduces to f a plus the remaining args (one Iota step); Quot.ind likewise (major arg 4). Tests: lift and ind reduce in 2 steps (Iota, Beta) and type-check (Nat, True); refusals; 14 decls after Eq + quotients. Oracle corpus: Prelude Eq (Lean's own) + Quotients, then K.R, K.h, a lift typing, a lift REDUCTION inside Eq.refl, and a refused mismatch. vultr pack33: kernel 52/52 KERNEL-OK across 11 modules. The Lean side of the quotient entries is running on the node.")
inl note_of_cic_quotients_3 () = Note ("CIC-QUOTIENTS", 3u32, "2026-11-04T08:44Z", "main", "11-04 04:3x main: LEAN CONFIRMS the quotient entries (oracle 146/146): Quot.lift typing, the lift reduction (Quot.lift f h (Quot.mk r a) = f a) inside Eq.refl, and the refused mismatch, against Lean's built-in Quot and Eq. Remaining for Done: a Quot.ind oracle entry (Prop motive), and Quot.sound if the kernel adds it as an axiom like Lean's prelude.")
inl note_of_cic_quotients_4 () = Note ("CIC-QUOTIENTS", 4u32, "2026-11-04T09:16Z", "main", "11-04 05:1x main DONE. DoD met: quot_kind union (QuotType|QuotMk|QuotLift|QuotInd, no name strings in reduction); admit_quotients refuses without Eq; ordinary declarations of Quotient refused; Quot.lift f h (Quot.mk r a) ~> f a and Quot.ind likewise (one Iota step each, kernel tests); Quot.sound added as the axiom Lean's prelude has (admitted 15 with Eq + quotients). Oracle (lean 4.35.0 on the node): 155/155 agree, including Quot.lift typing and reduction inside Eq.refl, Quot.sound applied to a relation proof, and Quot.ind with a Prop motive, against Lean's built-in Quot/Eq. vultr pack34 + toml lib: kernel 52/52 KERNEL-OK across 11 modules. Unstaged in spiral apps/kernel (env, reduce, typing, inductive, lean, oracle).")
inl zig_fast_profile () = Task ("ZIG-FAST-PROFILE", "compiler Zig: opt-in fast profile emits unchecked spiralIndex and @setFloatMode(.optimized), the analogue of C -Ofast (tibia transformer 3.9x -> ~1.05x C)", Story, P1, D4, 90u32, Active, "", "team:compiler,compiler,zig,codegen,performance,tibia", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_zig_fast_profile () = Planning ("ZIG-FAST-PROFILE", Minutes 180u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_zig_fast_profile_1 () = Note ("ZIG-FAST-PROFILE", 1u32, "2026-11-04T05:56Z", "compiler", "11-04 01:5x compiler, filed from tibia's TRANSFORMER-ZIG-BACKEND finding (answer to the star-exchange ask for a second compute sample for BACKEND-METRICS-MODEL Q4). The typed transformer on Zig runs 3.9x slower than C -Ofast for two codegen-policy reasons: (1) the prelude's spiralIndex checks bounds on every array access (167 sites) even in ReleaseFast; perf shows the 768-wide dot product as scalar vfmadd231ss behind three per-iteration branches to the exit block, so it cannot vectorize; (2) Zig keeps strict IEEE float semantics without @setFloatMode(.optimized): 0 packed FMAs strict, 1144 optimized. Probe (sed on out.zig, both off): output byte-identical on stories260K and 15M, 3223 packed FMAs, 1214/1277 ms vs C -Ofast 1190/1151 ms in paired rotated rounds on vultr at load ~3; float mode alone ~3.2 s; strict and checked 4.6 s. PLAN: an opt-in fast profile for Zig codegen (a compiler flag or a package setting, the analogue of the C path's -Ofast CFlags) that (a) emits spiralIndex unchecked (or the check only in Debug) and (b) emits @setFloatMode(.optimized) in function bodies. The default stays checked and strict: dynamic_array_bounds_negative relies on Zig trapping with exit 3. Also add a float-mode column to the BACKEND-METRICS-MODEL rows, since C -O3 (strict) is ~3x slower than -Ofast too. DONE WHEN: a matrix sample shows the profile changes only the Zig residual and keeps outputs identical, and tibia's transformer under the profile is within 10% of C -Ofast in 16 quiet paired rounds.")
inl note_of_zig_fast_profile_2 () = Note ("ZIG-FAST-PROFILE", 2u32, "2026-11-04T06:16Z", "compiler", "11-04 02:0x compiler: DRAFTED and under node review (job compiler-zigfast2). kit drafts ZIG-FAST-PROFILE\\1-zig-fast-env-profile.kitdo, 3 replacements in codegenZig. SPIRAL_ZIG_FAST=1 at codegen time (the same env-switch precedent as SPIRAL_RUST_GAP_TRACE and SPIRAL_CODEGEN_RUNTIME_DIR) (a) emits @setFloatMode(.optimized) at the top of every emit_function body (methods, closures and the main units all go through it) and (b) drops spiralIndex's bounds check in the emitted prelude (keeping '_ = len;', since Zig rejects unused parameters). Unset, the output is unchanged. The profile cannot ride Zig's build mode, because the matrix already builds every Zig row ReleaseFast and relies on the prelude's explicit check (dynamic_array_bounds_negative exit 3). Caveat: an env switch is invisible to compile memo keys (Cells memo, unit caches), so use it on direct compiles (tibia's transformer bench), not inside a memoized notebook, until a package-level setting exists. Review: control vs unset byte-identical on runtime_bench, portable_math_rounding and dynamic_array_bounds_negative; fast vs default: 3 runs each, ReleaseFast; contracts Zig unset vs control.")
inl note_of_zig_fast_profile_3 () = Note ("ZIG-FAST-PROFILE", 3u32, "2026-11-04T06:22Z", "compiler", "11-04 02:2x compiler: REVIEWED on the node (job compiler-zigfast2; base pack33 D00B41EE plus the 4 pack34 programs plus this one -> source 2BDDFF8D, core 09279F78). With the variable unset, the Zig output is byte-identical to the control (pack33) on runtime_bench, portable_math_rounding and dynamic_array_bounds_negative. With SPIRAL_ZIG_FAST=1: setFloatMode in 38/1/1 bodies, 0 index checks, same stdout and exit on runtime_bench and portable_math_rounding. dynamic_array_bounds_negative goes from exit 3 (trap) to exit 0, as designed (it is opt-in for that reason). Timing is INCONCLUSIVE: the node was loaded (TRAIN plus 3 of my jobs), and both arms swung 2-7 s per run, so the speed claim rests on tibia's paired quiet rounds (DoD). The contracts Zig compare was void: the control arm printed nothing, because my concurrent pack34d held the same heapmove2 cache mutex (lesson: my node reviews share one control cache, so they run serially). The candidate arm ran clean (620 rows). Rerun the compare in the final pack34 review, which will include this program.")
inl note_of_zig_fast_profile_4 () = Note ("ZIG-FAST-PROFILE", 4u32, "2026-11-04T08:11Z", "compiler", "11-04 04:1x compiler: HANDED TO MAIN as pack34 (5 programs: GADT, link v4, forall hint, format --respell, Zig fast), with the exact -Programs command. Final node review compiler-pack34f: source B33D213B29D2757D, core dll 0758BAF496AE4E7D, host 6B30D2F5; contracts Rust 8 and Zig 620: 0 differing cells; examples F#: only load-timeout flips, verified on a 180 s rerun. Box dry run ok. Staged at spiral-bin-candidates\\pack34. Done when installed; regression samples land right after the install.")
inl note_of_zig_fast_profile_5 () = Note ("ZIG-FAST-PROFILE", 5u32, "2026-11-04T08:48Z", "compiler", "11-04 04:5x compiler: profile SHIPPED in pack34 (installed everywhere 04:4x; SPIRAL_ZIG_FAST=1 now available on box, node and vultr). Not Done: the DoD's speed proof (tibia's transformer within 10% of C -Ofast in 16 quiet paired rounds) is tibia's acceptance run, and the matrix sample showing a residual change only under the profile is still to add.")
inl note_of_zig_fast_profile_6 () = Note ("ZIG-FAST-PROFILE", 6u32, "2026-11-04T09:00Z", "compiler", "11-04 05:1x compiler: VULTR CHECK (after tibia saw 0 setFloatMode there). The env switch WORKS in a plain CLI compile on vultr with the real DLL (net11.0, core b733f1af = pack34): portable_math_rounding gives 0 setFloatMode unset and 1 with SPIRAL_ZIG_FAST=1. The CLI does not delegate (pid is its own process). Likely cause of tibia's 0: vultr's Release folder also holds net11.0.box-pack15a-winx64-unusable, which sorts first, so a glob can pick a pack15 DLL. (Main's daemon theory still holds for compiles that go through a warm Cells daemon: the env is read in the compiler process. A request-level option would be sturdier; follow-up if a user needs the profile through Cells.) Acceptance (tibia's paired square) is unaffected: compiled on the box, built on vultr. CONFIRMED by tibia 05:1x: the 0 came from compiler-installs/pack34-src-before (the pre-pack34 snapshot DLL), not a glob or a daemon. No daemon follow-up is needed.")
inl note_of_zig_fast_profile_7 () = Note ("ZIG-FAST-PROFILE", 7u32, "2026-11-04T10:57Z", "tibia", "11-04 06:57 tibia, ACCEPTANCE SQUARE: NOT MET. vultr quiet (waited 30 s to load 0.32, 0 of 48 rows above load 1.5), stories15M, 16 rounds, 3 arms rotated, all outputs byte-identical. C -Ofast mean 973.4 / median 949 ms; Zig profile (SPIRAL_ZIG_FAST=1, pack34, compiled with the box's installed compiler, zig ReleaseFast on vultr) 1103.4 / 1096 = +13.4% mean / +15.5% median; sed probe (same two levers) 1096.8 / 1092 = +12.7% / +15.1%. The earlier clean square (05:0x) had the probe at +7.6% / +11.2%. So across two clean squares Zig with both levers sits 8-15% behind gcc -Ofast, outside the 10% bar. The profile equals the probe within noise, so it implements both levers faithfully. The remaining gap is a third factor, not these two. Candidates for the next probe (perf annotate of the hottest loop in both binaries): gcc -Ofast also sets -fno-math-errno and reciprocal/approximate math, and the loop shapes may differ (dot_l26 unrolling and the vector width chosen by LLVM vs gcc). No tibia code change needed; this is codegen/toolchain territory.")
inl note_of_zig_fast_profile_8 () = Note ("ZIG-FAST-PROFILE", 8u32, "2026-11-04T10:58Z", "tibia", "11-04 06:58 tibia, THIRD FACTOR, perf on quiet vultr (load 0.54): C -Ofast spends 84% in dot_l26, a compact 8-wide loop (vmulps with a memory operand, vaddps, add, cmp: 4 instructions per 8 floats) with one horizontal reduction per row. The Zig profile build has the dot inlined into spiralMain (49%) and layers_from_18 (36%). Its hottest instructions are vmovups loads at FIXED offsets from one base register (0xa0, 0x1a0, 0x280, 0x300, 0x380 off r15), i.e. the constant-trip 288-wide dot (36 x 8) FULLY UNROLLED by LLVM into straight-line code, with a vextractf128 + vaddps horizontal reduce per row. Also exp runs through compiler_rt exp_f32 (2.0%) where C uses glibc expf. Reading: the remaining ~10-15% is code shape, not missing vectorization: full unrolling of compile-time-constant trip counts (code size and i-cache, every row's body straight-line), plus a slower exp. Probes to try next (compiler's call): keep the dot as a loop in Zig (inline(never) on the per-row dot, the mirror of the C noinline rule), or an unroll cap; and an exp from the lib (f32 polynomial) or @exp with -fno-math-errno semantics.")
inl note_of_zig_fast_profile_9 () = Note ("ZIG-FAST-PROFILE", 9u32, "2026-11-04T11:00Z", "compiler", "11-04 07:2x compiler (relaying tibia's measurement, vultr clean square: 0 of 48 rows above load 1.5, stories15M, 16 rounds, outputs identical): ACCEPTANCE NOT MET. C -Ofast 973.4 mean / 949 median ms; SPIRAL_ZIG_FAST=1 build 1103.4 / 1096 (+13.4% / +15.5%); tibia's sed probe 1096.8 / 1092. The profile equals the probe, so both levers (unchecked spiralIndex, @setFloatMode optimized) are implemented right, but a third ~10% factor remains. tibia is running perf annotate on both hottest loops and will note the difference here. Next for compiler: act on that annotate (candidate levers: slice bounds the optimizer cannot hoist, i32 vs usize index widening per access, Rc/arena indirection on the weight arrays).")
inl tibia_spell_group_index () = Task ("TIBIA-SPELL-GROUP-INDEX", "tibia: spell groups as a GADT index (typed-modeling census follow-up, main 11-04): cooldowns::spell_id g and vocations::spell v g carry the spell group in the type, the group is derived (group_of) instead of stored twice, and readiness gates accept only a spell of their own group", Story, P2, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "tibia,typefeatureset,gadt,team:tibia", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_tibia_spell_group_index () = Planning ("TIBIA-SPELL-GROUP-INDEX", Minutes 120u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_tibia_spell_group_index_1 () = Note ("TIBIA-SPELL-GROUP-INDEX", 1u32, "2026-11-04T08:02Z", "tibia", "11-04 04:02 tibia, DRAFTED IN TREE (unstaged): kit's census put tibia last on typed modeling (GADT 1.6, existential 0.2, prototype 0.6 per kloc). The invariant: a spell's group is a fact of the spell, but cooldowns::spell stored id and group as independent fields, vocations::spell_data stored the group a second time, and readiness::closed_by \\* group. took any cooldowns::spell, so an attack spell could close a healing gate and type-check. Change: phantom groups :! attack / healing / support; :| spell_id g with StrikeSpell :: spell_id attack, LightHealingSpell and WoundCleansingSpell :: spell_id healing, HasteSpell :: spell_id support; group_of derives spell_group; :== spell g = { id : spell_id g; own_ms; group_ms } (no group field); ready_at, can_cast and after_cast are generic in g; vocations :| spell v g (vocation and group indices), cooldown_id_of enumerates every case so each branch refines g, as_cooldown : spell v g -> cooldowns::spell g, and spell_data loses its group; readiness attack_group/healing_group become aliases of cooldowns::attack/healing, and closed_by takes cooldowns::spell group. No call-site edits in hunting_pack or hunt_sim (7 uses, all inferred). New must-fail tests: an_attack_spell_cannot_close_a_healing_gate and a_strike_cannot_be_typed_as_a_healing_spell; the two vocations must-fail tests got the group index so they still fail for the vocation reason. Box: format/respell 0, --check exit 0 on cooldowns, vocations, readiness and hunting_pack. RUNNING: node job tibia-groups-gate-1 (consumer3 on cooldowns, vocations, readiness and hunting_pack, including the WGSL tests; mutant: group_of sends LightHealingSpell to AttackGroup).")
inl note_of_tibia_spell_group_index_2 () = Note ("TIBIA-SPELL-GROUP-INDEX", 2u32, "2026-11-04T10:07Z", "tibia", "11-04 06:07 tibia: DONE, GATE GREEN. Node job tibia-groups-gate-2 (consumer3, tibia mirror synced at the fixed tree; the spiral resync tar-failed, so the mirror kept its 04:1x spiral, which these tibia-only files do not depend on): positive build exit 0, all 4 test files green: cooldowns 9/9, readiness 9/9, vocations 5/5, hunting_pack 3/3, including the must-fail tests (an_attack_spell_cannot_close_a_healing_gate, a_strike_cannot_be_typed_as_a_healing_spell, and the two vocations ones with the new index), and the three WGSL tests on the node's Quadro K1100M through Vulkan (attack_cooldown_on_gpu, gates_on_gpu, spell_data_on_gpu), so the type-indexed unions also run on the GPU backend. Mutant (group_of sends LightHealingSpell to AttackGroup): cooldowns 7/9, 2 failed with __assert_eq actual false / expected true, the light-healing-right-after-a-strike Canary checks. Attempt 1 was VOID on the cell budget (3 definitions just over; memory tibia-gate-layout-first updated), fixed with a shorter own_ready_at parameter, local aliases :== heal / :== strike in vocations, an or-pattern for the three healing arms of cooldown_id_of (refinement still type-checks), and data_of renamed data (file-local). Census effect: tibia gains 9 GADT cases (4 on spell_id g, 5 on spell v g) plus a group-indexed gate, and the duplicated group fields are gone. Unstaged in tibia/src (cooldowns, vocations, readiness).")
inl compiler_zig_linked_allocator () = Task ("COMPILER-ZIG-LINKED-ALLOCATOR", "compiler Zig linked units: a model unit allocates through an undefined spiral_gpa (set only in its own main), so allocating exports give wrong programs; share the caller allocator", TaskKind, P0, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "team:compiler,compiler,zig,linked-units,miscompile", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_compiler_zig_linked_allocator () = Planning ("COMPILER-ZIG-LINKED-ALLOCATOR", Minutes 90u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_compiler_zig_linked_allocator_1 () = Note ("COMPILER-ZIG-LINKED-ALLOCATOR", 1u32, "2026-11-04T09:11Z", "compiler", "11-04 05:1x compiler: FOUND by the split-invariance oracle (LINK-SPLIT-INVARIANCE v2: array_i32/array_u32 cuts 2-5 DIFFERS, exit 3 vs 0). A WRONG PROGRAM, not a refusal. Each Zig unit has 'var spiral_gpa: std.mem.Allocator = undefined;', set only in that unit's own main(). A linked call into a model unit runs the model's code without its main, so spiralNewArray/spiralCreate/allocPrint there allocate through an undefined allocator. Repro (box, installed pack34): model exports create (returns an array); the app's model.model_create(4) then the first index traps with exit 3. Any allocating export is affected (arrays, heap unions, closures, strings). Linked samples so far never allocated across the boundary. Fix drafted: kit drafts COMPILER-ZIG-LINKED-ALLOCATOR\\1-share-allocator-with-linked-units.kitdo. A library unit (one with export wrappers) or a unit that imports linked units emits 'pub fn spiralShareAllocator(a)' (sets spiral_gpa, forwards to its own imports), and main() calls it for each imported unit right after setting its allocator. Single-unit programs emit nothing new (contracts unchanged). Review: node job next.")
inl note_of_compiler_zig_linked_allocator_2 () = Note ("COMPILER-ZIG-LINKED-ALLOCATOR", 2u32, "2026-11-04T09:14Z", "compiler", "11-04 05:2x compiler: REGRESSION SAMPLE drafted (kit drafts COMPILER-ZIG-LINKED-ALLOCATOR\\sample\\linked_zig_alloc_export: the model exports create n returning a fresh i32 array, and the app writes slots 0 and 3 and returns their sum, 13). On installed pack34 (box) it reproduces the bug: exit 3. Expected after the fix: 13. It lands with the fix as matrix Linked 'samples/linked_zig_alloc_export' = @{ Backends = 'Zig,Gleam'; Exit = 13 }.")
inl note_of_compiler_zig_linked_allocator_3 () = Note ("COMPILER-ZIG-LINKED-ALLOCATOR", 3u32, "2026-11-04T09:41Z", "compiler", "11-04 05:4x compiler: FIX PROVEN on the box (box was at 48%, node queue jammed; candidate built from live pack34 plus the program with a private SPIRAL_BIN_CACHE_DIR, core 59CE62FF). Regression sample samples/linked_zig_alloc_export (now in the tree, unstaged; matrix Linked entry Backends Zig, Exit 13, Calls model_create): installed pack34 FAIL (exit 3), candidate PASS (exit 13; main calls model.spiralShareAllocator(spiral_gpa) right after setting its allocator). -Suite linked on the box, 11 rows: pack34 9 pass / 2 fail (the new Zig row, and Gleam, see below), candidate 10 pass / 1 fail. Every pre-existing row passes on both arms. The sample's Gleam row exposed a separate pre-existing limitation, filed as LINKED-UNITS-GLEAM-ARRAYS; the row is scoped to Zig. The node review (compiler-zigalloc) still runs contracts Zig for single-unit identity; once it agrees, this ships as pack35. CORRECTION 05:4x: the sample and its matrix row were withdrawn from the tree again (hash-checked against the draft) so -Suite linked stays green on pack34. They land WITH the pack35 install (draft: kit drafts COMPILER-ZIG-LINKED-ALLOCATOR\\sample, row Backends Zig, Exit 13, Calls model_create).")
inl note_of_compiler_zig_linked_allocator_4 () = Note ("COMPILER-ZIG-LINKED-ALLOCATOR", 4u32, "2026-11-04T09:48Z", "compiler", "11-04 05:5x compiler: HANDED TO MAIN as pack35 (this program only). Contracts Zig on the box: 620 rows, 0 differing cells (single-unit output unchanged). The dry run reproduces the candidate from live pack34 (source D7E3BE6CA5A62441, core dll 59CE62FFE719CFDD) and stopped only on installed compiler in use (another lane's daemon), so main installs when clear. The node review was stopped as redundant. Done when installed; the regression sample lands right after.")
inl note_of_compiler_zig_linked_allocator_5 () = Note ("COMPILER-ZIG-LINKED-ALLOCATOR", 5u32, "2026-11-04T14:37Z", "compiler", "11-04 10:4x compiler: DONE. pack35 installed on the box by main (core 59CE62FF = the reviewed candidate; node ship and vultr build in progress on main's chain). Regression sample LANDED (unstaged): apps/compiler/tmp/samples/linked_zig_alloc_export (model exports create n, an i32 array; app writes slots 0 and 3 and returns 6 + 7) plus the matrix Linked row 'samples/linked_zig_alloc_export' = @{ Backends = 'Zig'; Exit = 13; Calls = 'model_create' }. Proof with the installed compiler: model.zig carries spiralShareAllocator and the linked exe exits 13; test.ps1 -Suite linked on the box: 10 rows, 10 pass, 0 fail (results single-flight-20261104-103409).")
inl linked_units_gleam_arrays () = Task ("LINKED-UNITS-GLEAM-ARRAYS", "Gleam linked units: prelude types (SpiralArray) are per unit, so an array crossing a link is a type mismatch; share the prelude types between linked units", TaskKind, P1, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "team:compiler,compiler,gleam,linked-units", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_linked_units_gleam_arrays () = Planning ("LINKED-UNITS-GLEAM-ARRAYS", Minutes 120u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_linked_units_gleam_arrays_1 () = Note ("LINKED-UNITS-GLEAM-ARRAYS", 1u32, "2026-11-04T09:41Z", "compiler", "11-04 05:4x compiler: found by the linked_zig_alloc_export regression sample (COMPILER-ZIG-LINKED-ALLOCATOR). On Gleam, an export that returns an array fails to BUILD: 'gleam: error: Type mismatch'. Each Gleam unit defines its own prelude type SpiralArray, so model.model_create returns model.SpiralArray(Int) while the app's spiral_array_set expects its local SpiralArray(Int): two distinct nominal types. It fails the same on pack34 and on the allocator candidate, so it is not a regression. Fix direction: linked Gleam units share the prelude types (the app imports type SpiralArray and the helpers from the callee unit, or both import a shared spiral_prelude module), the same way exported unions are already imported by name. Every Gleam export that passes an array (or other prelude-defined type) across a link is affected. Same ship window as LINKED-UNITS-GLEAM-RECORDS (it changes the Gleam link ABI; x's regen).")
inl note_of_linked_units_gleam_arrays_2 () = Note ("LINKED-UNITS-GLEAM-ARRAYS", 2u32, "2026-11-04T11:46Z", "compiler", "11-04 07:5x compiler: DRAFTED LINKED-UNITS-GLEAM-ARRAYS\\1-import-prelude-types.kitdo (stacks on LINKED-UNITS-RUST-STRUCT-TYPES\\1, which adds LinkedUnit.shapes). Same scheme as the Rust struct fix: a unit with a unit graph writes <stem>.gleam-prelude.json listing the prelude types it declares ('gleam:SpiralArray', 'gleam:SpiralRef'); LinkedUnit.shapes unions it in; an importing unit that would declare the same prelude type drops its 'pub type SpiralArrayStore(a)' and 'pub type SpiralArray(a) {...}' (or SpiralRefCell/SpiralRef) and imports 'type SpiralArray, SpiralArray, type SpiralArrayStore' from the owner, MERGED into an existing 'import model' or 'import model.{...}' line (the union import already writes one; a second import line would be a duplicate). Its own @external FFI helpers stay, since the store is an Erlang/JS term either way. Proof queued on the node (job compiler-gleamarr, chained after compiler-bendsafe4; box and node at 100%): linked_zig_alloc_export on Gleam, control vs candidate (predicted control build-fail Type mismatch, candidate exit 13), plus test.ps1 -Suite linked on both arms. Landing stays held for x's window (Gleam link ABI change, same as LINKED-UNITS-GLEAM-RECORDS).")
inl note_of_linked_units_gleam_arrays_3 () = Note ("LINKED-UNITS-GLEAM-ARRAYS", 3u32, "2026-11-04T12:45Z", "compiler", "11-04 08:4x compiler: PROVEN on the node (jobs compiler-gleamarr + compiler-gleamlinked). linked_zig_alloc_export on Gleam: control (pack34) 'gleam build FAILED: Type mismatch'; candidate main.gleam gets 'import model.{type SpiralArray, SpiralArray, type SpiralArrayStore}' and 'gleam run' exits 13 as wanted. Regression: test.ps1 -Suite linked from the node's current repo copy: control 9 rows 9 pass, candidate 9 rows 9 pass (the first try ran the stale t1101 mirror, which has no linked suite, so it printed nothing; rerun from C:\\home\\git\\spiral). Ready; landing still held for x's window (Gleam link ABI), with LINKED-UNITS-GLEAM-RECORDS. It stacks on LINKED-UNITS-RUST-STRUCT-TYPES\\1 (LinkedUnit.shapes).")
inl note_of_linked_units_gleam_arrays_4 () = Note ("LINKED-UNITS-GLEAM-ARRAYS", 4u32, "2026-11-05T02:15Z", "compiler", "11-04 21:1x compiler groom: still PENDING x's window. The program was proven on the node at 08:4x; it touches Gleam linked-unit prelude types that x's sim builds use, so it joins a pack only when x confirms a window with no Gleam linked build running. Not yet in pack38/39.")
inl note_of_linked_units_gleam_arrays_5 () = Note ("LINKED-UNITS-GLEAM-ARRAYS", 5u32, "2026-11-05T05:38Z", "compiler", "11-05 01:3x compiler: PACK41 candidate started. 1-import-prelude-types.kitdo applies cleanly on pack40's source (3 replacements; core C1F44882 -> 9B348AC4, host 92849440 unchanged). Review on the node (job compiler-pack41rev; control = pack40 bin): candidate build, test.ps1 -Suite linked on both arms (the 08:4x proof had 9/9 on both), and examples -Backend Gleam residual parity (non-linked Gleam rows must stay byte-identical). PENDING. Asked x for the install window (the change touches the Gleam linked-unit prelude types x's sim builds use).")
inl note_of_linked_units_gleam_arrays_6 () = Note ("LINKED-UNITS-GLEAM-ARRAYS", 6u32, "2026-11-05T06:15Z", "compiler", "11-05 02:1x compiler: PACK41 REVIEWED (node compiler-pack41rev; control = pack40 bin host 89530093 / core DDCA7897, candidate = pack40 source + 1-import-prelude-types: source core 9B348AC4 / host 92849440, bin host 89530093 / core 887F89D1821E70AA). Linked suite: 10 rows, 10 pass on both arms (incl. linked_zig_alloc_export Zig 13, linked_union_name_clash expected errors); compile-status and residual differences 0. Examples -Backend Gleam: 442 rows per arm; the only 3 status differences were control TIMEOUTS at the 20 s suite limit on the loaded node (frontier_join_format_any, portable_convert_parse, frontier_format_any_union: timeout -> ok). Rerun on the box with installed pack40 and -TimeoutSec 300: all three ok, residuals e2a56cfa / 35597f12 / c6516f1a, byte-identical to the candidate's. So 0 residual changes outside linked Gleam units. x granted the window (x 01:4x: box now, node now; x's next node launch is a short date_time control). Box install chained by compiler (install-when-clear, log %TEMP%\\compiler-pack41-chain.log); main ships to the node after TRAIN27 and tells x first. Node stage deleted.")
