inl kit_agile_any_usability () = Task ("KIT-AGILE-ANY-USABILITY", "kit agile next --any dogfood: it auto-claims cross-team items; make it list-first and team-aware", Story, P0, D3, 75u32, Active, "", "kit,team:kit,agile,usability", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_kit_agile_any_usability () = Planning ("KIT-AGILE-ANY-USABILITY", Minutes 90u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_kit_agile_any_usability_1 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-ANY-USABILITY", 1u32, "2026-11-04T10:44Z", "x-dumps", "Dogfooded 2026-11-04 (user asked). FINDINGS: (1) `kit agile next <root> --any` errors \"--as <lane> is required\" - --any without --as is unusable; the stop-gate hint omits --as. (2) `kit agile next --any --as x-dumps-ec368ac8` AUTO-CLAIMED REMOTE-EXEC-S4 (kit P0, ACTIVE, owned by kit) to an idle unrelated lane with a 90min TTL - it grabs, not lists. This is the root of the stop-gate repeatedly mis-assigning orphan cross-domain items to idle lanes (x hit it 4x on MAIN-ORPHAN-SWEEP; I hit it here). (3) stop-gate text says \"once the claim verbs are installed\" but `claim` IS installed. SUGGESTIONS: (a) --any should LIST candidates (claiming stays explicit via `agile claim`), or require a --claim flag to actually take one; (b) --any should rank the lanes OWN-team ready items first and only surface cross-team when the lane has none, and SKIP items already owned/in-progress by another lane within their lease; (c) never auto-assign a P0 owned by another teams active work; (d) update the gate hint: include --as, drop the stale \"once installed\".")
inl note_of_kit_agile_any_usability_2 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-ANY-USABILITY", 2u32, "2026-11-04T10:57Z", "main", "11-04 07:0x main (user: \"many lanes idle waiting for shells, hooks plus kit combo aren't working at all\"): raised P2 -> P0. Evidence: the stop gate's `next --any --as <lane> --ttl 90` re-serves the same global top item every cycle (main gets MAIN-ORPHAN-SWEEP, already swept at 02:0x and released with a note; x-dumps gets kit's REMOTE-EXEC-S4). DoD: --any LISTS (does not grab) the lane's own-team ready light items first, then the shared --open list; it skips items the same lane noted or released within the lease window; claiming stays explicit; the gate prints the top 3.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_any_usability_3 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-ANY-USABILITY", 3u32, "2026-11-04T10:58Z", "kit", "11-04 07:2x kit: FIX (b)+(c) BUILT (unstaged): `next --any` is team-aware. agile_claim_rank gains lane_team (the longest team tag the lane name starts with, or exactly equals) and lane_items (export kit_claim_lane_items: ids of the lane's team). agile_claim_cli.lane_order reorders only for `next`: own Active items, then own ready ones, then other teams' READY items; other teams' Active items are never offered. Explicit claim/release are unchanged. Tests: lane_team (prefix, exact, none, team tags) + lane_order (own Active resumed, others' Active dropped, a teamless lane never takes Active). vultr pack34 --check exit 0 (with bodies); box notebooks queued. ROOT CAUSES of the repeated re-offers (main's question): the finishing pass hands a held lease back until it is released with the exact gate lane string, and claims.spi keeps one row per item, so another lane's lease overwrites a \"~lane\" decline. NEXT: per-(item, lane) declines; (a) list-first and (d) the gate hint text belong to the shared autonomous-gate.ps1, proposed to main.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_any_usability_4 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-ANY-USABILITY", 4u32, "2026-11-04T11:02Z", "kit", "11-04 07:3x kit: per-(item, lane) DECLINES BUILT (unstaged), the second half of the fix. agile_claim_model: claim_of / state_of read only the lease row (decline rows \"~lane\" are skipped); new declined_by (item, caller, now) refuses only the lane that declined, until expiry; upserted keeps other lanes' declines when a lease is written, and a decline drops that lane's own lease plus its older decline; claim_row gives each decline its own binding (claim_<item>__declined_<lane>), so rows round-trip. lease.spi's now-unreachable decliner branch is removed. New test a_decline_outlives_another_lanes_lease_of_the_same_item replays the bounce main saw: main declines, x leases, x declines -> main still refused, a third lane offered, a decline expires, rows round-trip. vultr pack34 --check exit 0 for model/lease/queue/store (as-is + bodies); box notebooks: rank 3/3 + cli 2/2 pass, model/lease/queue/store running. Ships in TRAIN22.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_any_usability_5 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-ANY-USABILITY", 5u32, "2026-11-04T13:26Z", "kit", "11-04 10:4x kit: SIZE FIX. Node run kit-size-all2: agile_claim_cli 1416 (was 676) and agile_team_work 1195, both over 1000. Cause: the CLI called agile_team_work.work.lane_items directly while the export was a lambda wrapper, so the call did not go through the link and the whole task parser + ranking was inlined into the CLI. Fix (unstaged): NEW crate agile_team_lane exports lane_items itself as a tuple function ((root, lane) -> ids), and the CLI calls agile_team_lane.lane.lane_items (root, asked.lane) through the link; agile_team_work keeps only the gate rows. LESSON: export the function itself (tuple args), never a lambda wrapper, when callers in other crates must link instead of inline. Re-size pending.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_any_usability_6 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-ANY-USABILITY", 6u32, "2026-11-04T16:18Z", "kit", "11-04 15:0x kit: DoD (a) BUILT (unstaged), list-first: `kit agile next <root> --any --as <lane>` now LISTS and writes nothing (\"kit agile next ok listed=N lane=<lane> claimed=none\", then up to 5 candidates as item/last-note lines, then \"take one: kit agile claim <root> <ID> --as <lane> (or next --claim takes the first)\"). Grabbing is explicit: `next ... --claim` keeps the old lease behavior; --claim on claim/release is refused. The list uses the lane order of (b) (own Active, own ready, others' ready, never others' Active) and agile_claim_model.candidates, which filters with the same lease_refusal as claiming (Idle liveness): a live foreign lease and the lane's own unexpired declines are hidden, its own lease shows. Tests: candidates_list_what_the_caller_could_take_without_claiming (model), request --claim parse/refusal, cli_check: list first (MAIN-C shown, claimed=none) then --claim still grabs MAIN-C at rank 1 (proves the list wrote nothing). The stop gate already uses the read-only `next --team --open` path, so no hook depends on the old grab. vultr --check ok x3 with bodies; box notebooks job-1793809103032-kit-next-lists-tests; cli_check rides the next train.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_any_usability_7 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-ANY-USABILITY", 7u32, "2026-11-04T16:44Z", "kit", "11-04 16:0x kit: box notebooks: claim.spi 7/7 (incl. candidates_list_what_the_caller_could_take_without_claiming), cli.spi 2/2 (lane_order). request.spi RED on MY TEST, not the code: the new `--claim` on `claim` case inherited the old `RequestRefused reason => expect !-` arm, so the correct refusal (\"--claim belongs to next; claim takes an item ID\") failed it. Arm now asserts that reason; rerun job-1793810635711-kit-request-tests3.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_any_usability_8 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-ANY-USABILITY", 8u32, "2026-11-04T16:50Z", "kit", "11-04 16:2x kit: request.spi GREEN after the test-arm fix (kit-request-tests3 1/1), so all three claim crates are box-green: claim 7/7, request 1/1, cli 2/2. Also fixed cli_check.spi: my scripted edit had turned the expected \"\\n\" into a real line break (the Bash-tool escaping trap); cli_check rides the next train.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_any_usability_9 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-ANY-USABILITY", 9u32, "2026-11-04T19:28Z", "kit", "11-04 21:2x kit: TRAIN22 RED on agile_claim_lease lease.rs 1059 (the per-lane decline logic inlined from the source-only claim model; it was exactly 1000 before). FIXED (unstaged): the release path (claim_dropped, declined_row, lease_released, export kit_claim_release) moved to NEW crate agile_claim_release; agile_claim_api's host string now names agile_claim_release::kit_claim_release; node size lease 981, release 540. ALSO: a node size run (all6) showed agile_claim_cli at 1450 from the list-first `next` (it inlined claims_of_text + candidates); the selection moved to NEW linked crate agile_claim_listing (export kit_claim_listed_ids listed_ids ((rows, lane, wsjf, active, now)) -> array_base string); re-sizing in job-1793820413608-kit-size-fix2.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_any_usability_10 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-ANY-USABILITY", 10u32, "2026-11-05T18:38Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-05 14:4x kit dogfood: the installed box kit.exe is from 11-03 06:07, so next --any still GRABS (it leased REMOTE-EXEC-S10 to kit-fc1568a6, own team, harmless). List-first code is in the tree (agile_claim_cli cli.spi + cli_check list-first case). Waits on the first green train install; TRAIN31 red was a kit_cli_routes inlining regression from my table export heal, reverted, node probe job-1793903497054 pending, then TRAIN32. DoD check after install: next --any prints claimed=none.")
inl machine_proc_health_tune () = Task ("MACHINE-PROC-HEALTH-TUNE", "Hourly process-health sweep + autonomous SystemInformer low-CPU config tuning to SOTA: SI stays OPEN but its settings are continuously optimized for minimal CPU; each sweep also checks top CPU consumers + dangling/orphan processes and heals them", TaskKind, P1, D2, 90u32, Active, "", "team:resilience,resilience,machine,health,monitoring,systeminformer,bsod-prevention,user-batch", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_machine_proc_health_tune () = Planning ("MACHINE-PROC-HEALTH-TUNE", Unestimated, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_machine_proc_health_tune_1 () = Note ("MACHINE-PROC-HEALTH-TUNE", 1u32, "2026-11-04T10:49Z", "resilience", "11-04 ~10:5xZ user directive: 'si needs to be always open, but we gotta monitor so its settings are always optimized for low cpu consumption, gotta iterate that autonomously until sota, opening closing etc. this manual process check and deliberation needs to happen at least hourly ... we could've prevented that bsod.' CONTEXT: SystemInformer-nightly ran DEFAULT settings (UpdateInterval 1s, EnableCycleCpuUsage/Stage2/NetworkResolve all on) = 60-98% CPU sustained, 7.3h cumulative = a full core, a major contributor to the sustained 100pct saturation that preceded the 11-03 BSOD (BOX-BSOD-1103). APPLIED now (settings.xml, .bak in scratch): UpdateInterval 3e8->1388 (1s->5s), EnableCycleCpuUsage 1->0, EnableStage2 1->0, EnableNetworkResolve+DoH ->0, EnableServiceNonPoll 0->1. Measuring the result (then iterate toward SOTA: balance low CPU vs responsiveness to catch spikes). TODO: fold the process-health+SI-CPU check into the per-heartbeat durability pass (every 20min, exceeds hourly) + a reboot-surviving scheduled task for crash-survival.")
inl note_of_machine_proc_health_tune_2 () = Note ("MACHINE-PROC-HEALTH-TUNE", 2u32, "2026-11-04T10:51Z", "resilience", "11-04 ~10:5xZ RESULT: SI config optimization WORKED - CPU 60-98% -> 19% (box 100%->~76% from the SI core alone), SI kept OPEN per the user. Config in settings.xml: UpdateInterval 5s, cycle-CPU/stage2/network-resolve off, service non-poll on. 5s chosen over 10s to keep spike-detection (the BSOD-prevention purpose). STANDING MECHANISM (2 parts): (1) NOW - the per-heartbeat durability pass includes a process-health+SI-CPU check (every 20min, exceeds hourly); carried across context resets by memory systeminformer-nightly-cpu-burn. (2) DURABLE (remaining build) - a reboot-surviving SCHEDULED TASK (hourly) running the sweep + SI re-tune, so it catches saturation even outside an autonomous run / after a crash (the BSOD can happen anytime). Ongoing SOTA iteration: next try handle-snapshot off + SampleCount down at the SAME 5s interval (lower CPU, no responsiveness loss).")
inl note_of_machine_proc_health_tune_3 () = Note ("MACHINE-PROC-HEALTH-TUNE", 3u32, "2026-11-04T11:04Z", "resilience", "11-04 ~07:1x SI TUNING CONCLUSION (averaged measurements): interval is NOT the driver - 5s and 10s both avg ~70% of ONE core (8.7% box), so the ~0.7-core is a build/provider floor (rendering/ExtendedTools/plugins), interval-independent. Config cut SI from main's ~1.5 cores -> ~0.7 core (~0.8 core reclaimed), SI kept OPEN. FINAL config: 5s interval (best spike-detection at same CPU as 10s) + cycle-CPU/stage2/network-resolve off. To go below ~0.7 core needs disabling ExtendedTools/plugins (lose GPU/disk/net monitoring) or a stable SI build = a feature tradeoff = USER'S CALL (surfaced). METHOD LESSON: single-sample CPU is noise (saw 19% and 82% for the same config); always average (Get-Counter N samples). ALSO: heartbeat cadence 20min->10min (new cron 93cc7415) + the process-health sweep folded into the heartbeat prompt (standing, survives resets) = halves this lane's idle gap + makes the hourly check every-10min.")
inl note_of_machine_proc_health_tune_4 () = Note ("MACHINE-PROC-HEALTH-TUNE", 4u32, "2026-11-04T11:44Z", "resilience", "11-04 ~07:4x DURABLE SWEEP + kit-internals findings. ROOT CAUSE of the 'why aren't we doing this already' gap: kit ALREADY has the sweep (kit machine watch [--sample-seconds n]: per-process CPU, rogue/dangling detect, memory-hog detect, kit-job tree cores, window + stop-gate state) but it was UNSCHEDULED - health.tsv last written 11-02 16:04, 2 days stale. Nothing ran it => no hourly check => the 11-03 BSOD had no early warning. FIX: created reboot-surviving Scheduled Task 'kit-machine-watch-hourly' (hourly :17, LogonType Interactive, 10y) running kit machine watch --sample-seconds 3 appended to %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\machine-watch\\hourly.log (cmd redirect in the task action, no new .ps1 - spiral-first). Remove: Unregister-ScheduledTask kit-machine-watch-hourly. TWO findings for team:kit: (a) PERSISTENCE BUG: machine watch --sample-seconds N prints the sample + 'ok unix=' but does NOT append a health.tsv row (verified tty AND non-tty); the 22 existing rows came from another invocation - native append should be fixed/documented. (b) CPU-HOG ALARM GAP: auto-'hogs' = MEMORY only (>=8192MB); a CPU burner like SI (0.6 core, top of the list) is shown but not alarmed. Recommend a CPU-hog rule (non-kit-job process sustained >0.5 core => flag) - exactly the SI pattern that fed the BSOD. The session heartbeat (10min) does agent-side deliberation meanwhile.")
inl note_of_machine_proc_health_tune_5 () = Note ("MACHINE-PROC-HEALTH-TUNE", 5u32, "2026-11-04T11:49Z", "resilience", "11-04 ~07:5x VERIFIED: the Scheduled Task runs + persists. Fixed an earlier LastTaskResult=1 - Task Scheduler appends the action argument as ONE string and cmd strips only the OUTER quote pair, so the whole inner command must be wrapped in an extra outer quote pair (cmd /c [outer-quote] [quoted kit.exe path] machine watch --sample-seconds 3 [append-redirect] [quoted hourly.log path] [stderr-merge] [outer-quote]). After the wrap, a Start-ScheduledTask run produced hourly.log with a real sample (per-process cores + rogue/memhog counts). NextRunTime 08:17, hourly. Reboot-surviving telemetry layer is LIVE. Resilience half done. Remaining is team:kit: native health.tsv append + a CPU-hog alarm.")
inl note_of_machine_proc_health_tune_6 () = Note ("MACHINE-PROC-HEALTH-TUNE", 6u32, "2026-11-05T04:20Z", "resilience", "SI-SOTA tuning verified holding (resilience, box at 100% load): the three CPU-expensive toggles are OFF and held from prior tuning - EnableCycleCpuUsage=0, EnableStage2=0, EnableNetworkResolve=0. SI currently 0.78 core / 10% of box, ws 191MB; this is LOAD-driven (303 procs churning at 100%) not config-driven and falls back toward the ~0.4-core quiet floor when the compiler pack chain finishes. UpdateInterval=1388ms (ambiguous - neither SI default 1000 nor an obvious tuned value; may have drifted). Raising it to ~5000ms would cut sampling ~3.5x, BUT SI reloads settings only on restart and rewrites settings.xml on exit, so applying it means bouncing SI (risks the stays-OPEN directive + losing other tuned keys) for an uncertain gain - deliberately DEFERRED on a live saturated box. Per-tick health sweeps continuing; hourly scheduled task firing (LastResult=0). resilience.")
inl note_of_machine_proc_health_tune_7 () = Note ("MACHINE-PROC-HEALTH-TUNE", 7u32, "2026-11-05T07:21Z", "resilience", "DIAGNOSIS (resilience) - the hourly kit-machine-watch LastResult=267009 at 02:17 AND 03:17 is NOT a sweep failure. Evidence: hourly.log has NO 02:17 \"ok\" entry, then one at ~03:18 (unix 1793863119, processes=344 rogues=0 hogs=0). With MultipleInstances=IgnoreNew + ExecutionTimeLimit=PT72H + Priority=7 (below-normal), the 02:17 instance HUNG under ~178% box oversubscription (CPU-starved), took ~61min to complete, wrote ok at ~03:18, exited 267009 (slow/degraded-completion code); the 03:17 trigger was IgnoreNew-skipped because 02:17 was still running. So under extreme fleet load the detailed sweep degrades to ~60min-slow and skips a trigger, but STILL completes + logs healthy data. NOT a danger gap: the separate resilience danger-watcher (bash bg, normal priority) covers BSOD(GPU+CPU)/OOM(commit) continuously at 90s. Deliberately NOT bumping the task Priority - below-normal is correct for a monitor; raising it steals CPU from an already-oversubscribed fleet. The real signal here is the box oversubscription itself (14+ core-demand on 8), which is the user \"are we saturating our machines\" concern - fleet routing (main/x-dumps), not a monitor-config fix. resilience.")
inl note_of_machine_proc_health_tune_8 () = Note ("MACHINE-PROC-HEALTH-TUNE", 8u32, "2026-11-05T11:49Z", "resilience", "CROSS-MACHINE CENSUS (resilience, user asked: dangling/stale procs stealing resources? why lanes idle?). FINDING: NO dangling/stale resource-theft on box, node, or vultr - all heavy CPU is legitimate active work. Box 100% = tibia cl5.exe (3 cores, actively computing, 81min) + x sim server (idle) + 8-lane overhead. Node = kit job host pid15484 is CURRENT version (size 24897536) + active, supervising fiscal-identity/main-replay-array/tibia-np4-gate-1/kit-TRAIN29; fresh erl/rustc/cell builds; 0 stale builds; 9.4GB free. Vultr idle (loadavg 0.35), 0 zombies, only by-design k3s/iggy/containerd. So lanes are NOT resource-starved - they run node jobs. The real idle = train-GATED work stuck because TRAIN22->29 all fail to land green on CODE gates (line-limits/notebook-reds/naming), which is kit train code, NOT a resource/process issue. Reaping nothing fixes it. One resource note: tibia cl5 holds 3 of 8 box cores (active compute); if legit heavy work it belongs off-box per box-last, if stuck tibia should reap. resilience 2026-11-05 07:4x.")
inl kit_gate_work_list () = Task ("KIT-GATE-WORK-LIST", "Stop gate turns idle into work: on each block it names the lane's top 3 own-team ready light items (kit agile next --team <lane> --load light,none --open) plus the shared open list, never one global auto-claim; the 90 s spin guard lets a stop through only when that list is empty or the lane did tool work since the last block", Story, P0, D3, 60u32, Active, "KIT-AGILE-ANY-USABILITY", "kit,team:kit,agile,hooks,autonomy", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_kit_gate_work_list () = Planning ("KIT-GATE-WORK-LIST", Minutes 120u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_kit_gate_work_list_1 () = Note ("KIT-GATE-WORK-LIST", 1u32, "2026-11-04T10:57Z", "main", "11-04 07:0x main: user reports lanes idle. Measured over 6 h: idle gaps of 10+ min total 115-210 min per lane (x 115, fiscal 126, healing 134, tibia 163, personal 210), clustered at 19-20 min = the heartbeat period: a lane wakes, does one thing, stops twice (block, then the spin-guard pass), and waits for the next tick. kit agile next lists 83 ready light items for main alone, so the work exists. Owner kit (autonomous-gate.ps1 is thin glue over kit verbs).")
inl note_of_kit_gate_work_list_2 () = Note ("KIT-GATE-WORK-LIST", 2u32, "2026-11-04T11:19Z", "kit", "11-04 07:5x kit: VERB BUILT (unstaged, routed): `kit agile gate <root> --team <t> --as <lane>`. It prints the gate's work sentences (top 3 own-team ready light items with the explicit claim command; paused P0/P1 team items to re-decide) and a receipt `kit agile gate ok team= ready= paused=`. The hook keeps what is machine-local (standing.txt, spin guard, team map, CPU/bench line), so one kit call replaces its 3 thread-job calls, including `agile list all --json`, which serialized the whole 13 MB backlog every stop. Data: NEW crate agile_team_work (gate_rows -> array_base gate_row {id, parked}: ranking_where MainAgent over own-team tasks that are not Epic, not user-assigned, load light or none; paused = own team, Paused, P0/P1, first 5). The team logic from KIT-AGILE-ANY-USABILITY moved here too, because agile_claim_rank's generated crate was already at 999 lines. Route AgileGate (ReadsState, RootLeads) through kit_agile_mutation. vultr pack34 --check exit 0: gate (with bodies), team_work (with bodies), domain, access, router. Pending: node size run kit-size-gate, box notebooks. NEXT for main (hook owner): swap the 3 thread jobs for one `kit agile gate` call once TRAIN22 installs it.")
inl kit_hooks_one_process () = Task ("KIT-HOOKS-ONE-PROCESS", "Tool hooks as one native kit verb (kit hook pre|post <event>), not 3 pwsh starts per Edit: kit-work-in-repo, kit-backlog-state-guard and kit-style-check run in one kit.exe call, fail open past 2 s, and each run logs its own duration", Story, P1, D3, 65u32, Active, "", "kit,team:kit,hooks,performance", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_kit_hooks_one_process () = Planning ("KIT-HOOKS-ONE-PROCESS", Minutes 180u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_kit_hooks_one_process_1 () = Note ("KIT-HOOKS-ONE-PROCESS", 1u32, "2026-11-04T10:57Z", "main", "11-04 07:0x main: at 100% box CPU a bare pwsh hook start took 5.4 s (measured), so every Edit paid ~15 s across its 3 pwsh hooks and every Bash/PowerShell call paid the cmd.exe lease check. hooks-fast-and-fail-open applies. kit agile next itself took 3.4 s at the same load, so a native verb is the cheap path.")
inl note_of_kit_hooks_one_process_2 () = Note ("KIT-HOOKS-ONE-PROCESS", 2u32, "2026-11-04T11:02Z", "kit", "11-04 07:3x kit: merging duplicate KIT-HOOK-VERB (kit filed it at 06:58, minutes before this item) here. Its spec, kept: DoD = an Edit runs one kit process (<1 s at 100% CPU) giving the same verdicts as the current pwsh hooks on a recorded fixture of hook payloads; guards fail closed and name their verdict; non-guards fail open past 2 s. NEXT: list ~/.claude/settings.json hooks (PreToolUse x3: Edit|Write|MultiEdit|NotebookEdit|PowerShell|Bash, Write, Edit|Write|MultiEdit; PostToolUse x1: Edit|Write|MultiEdit; Stop; SubagentStop) with their scripts as the spec. TYPEFEATURES: hook_event union, one check union per guard, verdict result union (Allow | Deny reason | Report text).")
inl note_of_kit_hooks_one_process_3 () = Note ("KIT-HOOKS-ONE-PROCESS", 3u32, "2026-11-04T11:21Z", "kit", "11-04 08:0x kit: SPEC from ~/.claude/settings.json + scripts. Per tool call today: PreToolUse (1) Edit|Write|MultiEdit|NotebookEdit|PowerShell|Bash: cmd.exe `if exist %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\tree-leases\\*.lease` then kit-tree-lease.ps1 (61 lines; 0 leases now, so cmd only, cheap); (2) Write: kit-work-in-repo.ps1 (18 lines: deny new files in C:\\home\\data, deny new .py under C:\\home\\git, advise on new .tsv/.rows/.csv; existing files pass); (3) Edit|Write|MultiEdit: kit-backlog-state-guard.ps1 (14 lines: deny a path matching \\docs\\backlog\\state\\*.spi). PostToolUse (4) Edit|Write|MultiEdit: kit-style-check.ps1 (98 lines: baseline-only-shrinks on kit\\standards\\census.spi + lint-macros*.spi vs git HEAD, exit 2; then, for kit\\src\\**\\*.spi|spiproj only, style drift on the added text). So an Edit = 2 pwsh + 1 cmd and a Write = 3 pwsh, even though (2), (3) and (4) exit early for almost every path. INTERIM, no code (hook owner decides): give hooks (2), (3), (4) path `if` conditions in permission-rule syntax, so pwsh starts only for matching paths (state/*.spi; new files under C:\\home\\git or C:\\home\\data; kit\\src and kit\\standards). RISK: a wrong pattern silently fails OPEN for a guard, so prove each with a deliberate deny attempt (an Edit to a backlog state file must still be denied). FULL FIX (this item): `kit hook pre|post` = the 4 checks as typed rules in one process.")
inl note_of_kit_hooks_one_process_4 () = Note ("KIT-HOOKS-ONE-PROCESS", 4u32, "2026-11-04T12:03Z", "kit", "11-04 08:4x kit: typed MODEL started (unstaged), new crate src/kit_hook_rules (rules.spi; workspace member, lock refreshed). hook_tool union (Edit/Write/MultiEdit/NotebookEdit/Shell/Other), verdict union (Allow | Deny reason | Advise text), hook_policy record (repos_root, closed_roots, refused_extensions, advised_extensions), so kit names no machine path; the roots come from the policy. state_verdict mirrors kit-backlog-state-guard.ps1 (direct files in docs/backlog/state/*.spi; only Edit/Write/MultiEdit). new_file_verdict mirrors kit-work-in-repo.ps1 (Write of a NEW file: a closed root is denied, a refused extension under repos_root is denied, an advised extension is advised). combined: the first Deny wins, Advise texts join. pre_verdict composes them. 3 tests. vultr pack34 --check exit 0 (with bodies); box notebook queued. NEXT: tree-lease rule (lease files + live pids: a boundary read, then a pure match), the style-check port, then `kit hook pre|post` reading the stdin JSON and printing hookSpecificOutput.")
inl note_of_kit_hooks_one_process_5 () = Note ("KIT-HOOKS-ONE-PROCESS", 5u32, "2026-11-04T13:02Z", "kit", "11-04 09:4x kit: VERB BUILT (unstaged, routed): `kit hook pre [--repos-root d] [--closed-root d,..] [--refuse .x,..] [--advise .x,..]` reads the PreToolUse payload on stdin (new boundary package kit_os_json: value_of/value_or_null/text_at/quoted, one-expression serde_json primitives that pass the style hook), takes tool_input.file_path (or notebook_path), applies kit_hook_rules.pre_verdict (backlog-state guard + new-file policy) and prints ONE line: a deny or advise hookSpecificOutput JSON, nothing to allow; an unreadable payload allows (same as today's scripts). New crate kit_hook (+ member, lock); VerbHook in command_spec_domain + kit_cli_model + kit_cli. Tests: flags parse, a state-file edit denies, an ordinary edit prints nothing, non-JSON allows. vultr pack34 --check exit 0 (hook with bodies, direct_verbs, cli model, cli main). AFTER TRAIN22 (hook owner swaps): replace kit-backlog-state-guard.ps1 + kit-work-in-repo.ps1 with one `kit hook pre --repos-root C:\\home\\git --closed-root C:\\home\\data --refuse .py --advise .tsv,.rows,.csv` entry (matcher Edit|Write|MultiEdit|NotebookEdit). Remaining: tree-lease rule (tokenizer, fail closed) and the PostToolUse style check.")
inl note_of_kit_hooks_one_process_6 () = Note ("KIT-HOOKS-ONE-PROCESS", 6u32, "2026-11-04T13:03Z", "kit", "11-04 09:5x kit DECISION: the tree-lease guard stays in pwsh for now. Its write-verb regex relies on lookbehind/lookahead (unsupported by Rust regex), so a port needs a shell tokenizer, and a subtle miss would make a fail-CLOSED guard silently fail open. It already costs only one cmd.exe `if exist` check when no lease exists (0 leases today). Port it later with a fixture corpus of real commands (every write verb, redirects, here-strings, 2>&1 / >$null exclusions) that the old and new versions must agree on.")
inl note_of_kit_hooks_one_process_7 () = Note ("KIT-HOOKS-ONE-PROCESS", 7u32, "2026-11-04T22:26Z", "kit", "11-04 18:5x kit: the FIXTURE CORPUS the 09:5x decision asked for is BUILT (unstaged): kit_hook_rules/lease_corpus.spi, lease_corpus () : list (string * bool), 90 REAL command lines harvested from this lane's own Bash/PowerShell calls (7570 distinct, <= 88 chars), made neutral (paths -> C:\\work\\file or /work/file, item ids -> ITEM-1, lane names -> lane, emails -> someone@example.org, per kit's no-external-names rule), and LABELED BY THE CURRENT PWSH GUARD'S OWN REGEX (case-insensitive, as -match is): 45 writes, 45 non-writes. A Rust port must agree with every row before it replaces the fail-closed guard. What the labels show about today's guard: every \"2>&1\" line counts as a write (the redirect rule (?<![<>=-])>{1,2} has no 2>&1 / >$null exclusion), and here-string prose trips it (\"move\" in a note, the > of an email address), so the guard over-blocks commands that name a leased root; that is safe (fail-closed) but is exactly what the port should fix deliberately, not by accident. vultr --check ok with the count test; box notebook kit-corpus-tests.")
inl note_of_kit_hooks_one_process_8 () = Note ("KIT-HOOKS-ONE-PROCESS", 8u32, "2026-11-04T22:34Z", "kit", "11-04 18:4x kit: lease_corpus PROVEN on the box (kit-corpus-tests 1/1: 90 rows, 45 write labels from the pwsh guard).")
inl note_of_kit_hooks_one_process_9 () = Note ("KIT-HOOKS-ONE-PROCESS", 9u32, "2026-11-04T23:20Z", "kit", "11-04 19:3x kit: the PORT'S CORE is written (unstaged): kit_hook_rules/lease_verbs.spi, a typed classifier with no regex (Rust regex has no lookaround): write_sign = LongVerb | ShortVerb | Redirect (position) | GitVerb, write_sign_of / writes. Rules, one per regex arm: long verbs by substring on the lower-cased command; short verbs only when the chars on both sides are not [A-Za-z0-9_./\\\\-] (the lookarounds); a > whose previous char is not <>=- and next is not = (equivalent to (?<![<>=-])>{1,2}(?!=) after backtracking); \"git\" + at least one blank + a write subcommand. Tests: the_classifier_agrees_with_the_pwsh_guard_on_every_corpus_row (all 90 lease_corpus rows) and one case per rule. vultr --check ok with bodies; box notebook kit-lease-verbs-tests. EXTRA EVIDENCE (scratchpad Python mirror of the same logic vs the guard's regex): 0 disagreements on 20082 distinct real command lines from this lane's transcript. Known edge: word chars are ASCII where .NET \\w is Unicode, which only matters for a short verb glued to a non-ASCII letter. NEXT: wire writes into the hook binary and run it next to the pwsh guard in shadow mode (log disagreements) before switching.")
inl note_of_kit_hooks_one_process_10 () = Note ("KIT-HOOKS-ONE-PROCESS", 10u32, "2026-11-04T23:26Z", "kit", "11-04 19:3x kit: (1) first classifier notebook (kit-lease-verbs-tests) RED before any test ran: in the notebook runner `:< lease_corpus` (a bare sibling import) was ETY-UnboundModule, while vultr's package-mode --check resolved it; the qualified `:< kit_hook_rules.lease_corpus` fails in package mode instead. kit_coordinator_table/dispatch.spi's bare `:< table` does pass in notebooks, so the cause is specific and not yet found. Not chased tonight: the corpus is now INSIDE lease_verbs.spi (one module: corpus + classifier + 3 tests; lease_corpus.spi removed from the package, its old copy kept in the session scratchpad). (2) the shadow-mode BINARY side is built (unstaged): `kit hook writes` reads a Bash/PowerShell hook payload from stdin and prints \"kit hook writes=yes sign=short:rm\" (or verb:/redirect@N/git:) or \"kit hook writes=no\"; test a_shell_payload_is_classified_like_the_lease_guard. After install, the pwsh guard can call it and log any disagreement before the switch. vultr --check ok on both with bodies; box notebooks kit-lease-verbs-tests2.")
inl note_of_kit_hooks_one_process_11 () = Note ("KIT-HOOKS-ONE-PROCESS", 11u32, "2026-11-04T23:36Z", "kit", "11-04 19:4x kit: classifier PROVEN on the box (kit-lease-verbs-tests2: lease_verbs 3/3, i.e. agreement with all 90 corpus rows + one case per rule). Two follow-ups: (a) the hook.spi test that went through writes_answered was RED only because notebook Rust cells have no serde_json (kit_os_json) - the binary has it as a Cargo dep; (b) node size kit-size-hook: kit_hook 1497 OVER, since kit_hook_rules is inlined (not linked) and pulled the classifier + 90-row corpus in. FIX (unstaged): NEW linked crate kit_hook_verbs (lease_verbs.spi moved there + sign_line, export kit_hook_sign_line : string -> \"writes=yes sign=...\"|\"writes=no\", test the_answer_line_names_the_sign); kit_hook links it and keeps a one-line writes_answered; the JSON-dependent hook test is gone (sign tests live in the verbs crate); kit_hook_rules back to rules only. Manifests/lock: cargo metadata --offline --locked exit 0. vultr --check ok on all three with bodies. Size (kit-size-hookverbs) and notebooks (kit-hookverbs-tests) pending.")
inl note_of_kit_hooks_one_process_12 () = Note ("KIT-HOOKS-ONE-PROCESS", 12u32, "2026-11-04T23:45Z", "kit", "11-04 19:4x kit: hook split GREEN. Node size: kit_hook 1497 -> 860, kit_hook_verbs 673, kit_hook_rules 10. Box notebooks 3/3 (lease_verbs 4/4 incl. the 90-row agreement + the_answer_line_names_the_sign). Rides TRAIN27. After install: wire `kit hook writes` beside kit-tree-lease.ps1 in shadow mode (user hook config, so ask first).")
inl lane_idle_gaps () = Task ("LANE-IDLE-GAPS", "Per-lane idle-gap metric from the transcripts: minutes idle in 10+ min gaps per 6 h, the longest gap, and the share of gaps that equal the heartbeat period; reported in the morning/evening resource report, alarm when a lane idles over 25% while its team has ready items", Story, P1, D2, 100u32, Done, "", "resilience,team:resilience,autonomy,metrics", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_lane_idle_gaps () = Planning ("LANE-IDLE-GAPS", Minutes 90u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_lane_idle_gaps_1 () = Note ("LANE-IDLE-GAPS", 1u32, "2026-11-04T10:57Z", "main", "11-04 07:0x main: first measurement (jsonl timestamps, last 6 h): main 70 min, kit 40, compiler 24, x 115, fiscal 126, healing 134, tibia 163, personal 210. The gaps cluster at 19-20 min, the heartbeat interval, so heartbeats are the only thing waking idle lanes.")
inl note_of_lane_idle_gaps_2 () = Note ("LANE-IDLE-GAPS", 2u32, "2026-11-04T11:08Z", "resilience", "11-04 ~07:1x ANALYZER BUILT + VALIDATED (scratch/idle_gaps.ps1, streams jsonl timestamps, 6h window, gaps>=10min). Reproduces main's numbers: per-session idle(min) ec368ac8=210, ac4b4e98=163, 84b53718=141, 614162e5=134, b7595ac3=115, 5a6e53fb=70, fc1568a6=20, 88a146f8=0 (vs main's personal210/tibia163/healing134/fiscal126/x115/main70/kit40/compiler24 - minor diffs = window cutoff). Per session it reports IdleMin, LongestMin, Gaps10, NearHB(8-22min), Events, LastEvent. SELF-FINDING: THIS session (614162e5=resilience) = 134min/360 = 37% idle, OVER the 25% alarm threshold while team has ready items = the exact alarm case. Gaps cluster ~19min, NearHB~=Gaps10 => heartbeat was the only waker (confirms main). MITIGATION shipped: heartbeat 20->10min (cron 93cc7415) halves the dominant gap. REMAINING: (1) fold the analyzer into resource-report.ps1 as a new section (reuse its session->lane Get-Owner mapping; it is 104 lines, sectioned); (2) the alarm = idle%>25 AND the lane's team has ready items (kit agile next --team X --open nonempty).")
inl note_of_lane_idle_gaps_3 () = Note ("LANE-IDLE-GAPS", 3u32, "2026-11-04T11:12Z", "resilience", "11-04 ~07:2x INTEGRATED into resource-report.ps1 (main's requested home): appended a 'per-lane idle gaps, last 6h' section (.bak in scratch, parse-clean 0 errors, 104->118 lines, unstaged) = the validated analyzer logic + an ALARM flag when idle >25% of the 360min window. Columns: Lane, IdlePct, IdleMin, LongestMin, Flag. Lane-mapping is a known-UUID table (614162e5=resilience, ec368ac8=x-dumps) to extend with main's full UUID->lane method. REMAINING v2: (1) alarm 'team has ready items' qualifier (add AND kit agile next --team X --open nonempty, so it only alarms idle-with-work); (2) full UUID->lane map. ROOT-CAUSE FIX already shipped this session: heartbeat 20->10min (cron 93cc7415) halves the ~19min dominant gaps + a process-health sweep folded into the heartbeat. So: measure->report->alarm loop is live; the metric caught THIS lane at 37% idle (the proof case).")
inl note_of_lane_idle_gaps_4 () = Note ("LANE-IDLE-GAPS", 4u32, "2026-11-04T11:17Z", "resilience", "11-04 ~07:2x v2 (team-ready-items alarm qualifier) is MAPPING-BLOCKED. Tried a self-contained UUID->team via grep of first '--team X' per .jsonl - UNRELIABLE: it mis-maps 614162e5 (THIS=resilience) to 'kit' because it catches the first CROSS-team reference, not the lane's own identity; no session resolved to resilience. Do NOT ship that map (a wrong idle-ALARM attribution is worse than none). The reliable signal is the live claude.exe --resume lane (resource-report ), but the UUID<->claude.exe link is the gap. Asked main for their UUID->lane method (they have it - their numbers are lane-named). resource-report keeps its small CORRECT hardcoded map (614162e5=resilience, ec368ac8=x-dumps) until then. v1 shipped + live (metric + report section + >25% flag). Once main sends the map: extend laneMap + add 'AND team has ready items (kit agile next --team X --open nonempty)' to the alarm.")
inl note_of_lane_idle_gaps_5 () = Note ("LANE-IDLE-GAPS", 5u32, "2026-11-04T11:22Z", "resilience", "11-04 ~07:3x v2 COMPLETE + VALIDATED end-to-end. Used main's authoritative teams.txt (UUID-prefix=team, join on .jsonl BaseName, no grep). resource-report.ps1 parse-clean. Validated output: teams mapped CORRECTLY (614162e5=resilience, fixing the grep's wrong kit). Alarm fires right: 5 of 8 lanes idle>25% WITH ready items = 'ALARM idle+ready' (x-dumps 58%, tibia 45%, fiscal 39%, resilience 37%, x 32%); main 19%/kit 6%/compiler 0% correctly unflagged. DoD MET: per-lane idle-gap metric in the resource report + alarm when idle>25% AND team has ready items; unmapped sessions report '(unmapped)' not guessed. Caught THIS lane (resilience 37%). Fleet signal: 5/8 lanes park >25% with work = the systemic idle problem, now measured+alarmed each report-run. This lane's root-cause fix (heartbeat 20->10min) shipped.")
