inl note_of_linked_units_rust_struct_types_16 () = Note ("LINKED-UNITS-RUST-STRUCT-TYPES", 16u32, "2026-11-05T14:11Z", "compiler", "11-05 10:1x compiler: FIELD PROOF GREEN, DONE. kit's node job kit-pack48-regen (job-1793883435492, 73 min) with the pack48 bin (core 0BA4A6087F0F9DF3, host E441B6B3): 'KIT regeneration passed for 348 selected owners', then cargo check --workspace --keep-going: 'ERRORS=0 CARGO-EXIT=0'. That clears all 13 of TRAIN29's errors (12 E0308 in kit_window_query/kit_exec_jobs/kit_run_cli = fix 3; 1 E0425 in kit_ingest_frontier = fix 4). Staged workspace node kit-regeneration\\2eac7d71bab04662982a3089b5ae0ea1. Box runs pack49 (same core). Node: main ships pack49. Regression samples guarding the three failures of program 1: linked_rust_global_pub_fields, linked_rust_local_union_loop, linked_rust_global_in_mod. Lesson recorded in memory output-text-passes-skip-handwritten-globals. The split oracle on pack47 is still running; its result goes to LINK-SPLIT-INVARIANCE.")
inl lib_pickler_record_derive () = Task ("LIB-PICKLER-RECORD-DERIVE", "pickler record arm by reflection: pickler_real.pick_of gains | {} => a record pickler, wrap with to/from derived from the record type (record_type_fold). First step, CPU-light: a probe of how real code can hand a function to typed code (11-04 v9: a real lambda passed to pickler::pick_enum fails with 'Cannot convert a function that is not annotated into a type', annotated or not); find the supported spelling in lib .spir files (cli_args_real, json_real) or ask compiler", Story, P3, D4, 100u32, Done, "LIB-PICKLER-GADT", "lib,typefeatureset,pickler,reflection,team:tibia,tibia", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_lib_pickler_record_derive () = Planning ("LIB-PICKLER-RECORD-DERIVE", Minutes 120u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_lib_pickler_record_derive_1 () = Note ("LIB-PICKLER-RECORD-DERIVE", 1u32, "2026-11-04T11:26Z", "tibia", "11-04 07:26 tibia, FIRST STEP DONE, BLOCKED ON A COMPILER QUESTION. How real code hands functions to typed code: lib cli_args_real.spir passes a NAMED local function (:= make () = ...) into the typed cli_flags::pick_verb, which only CALLS it. For the pickler the function must be STORED in the typed value returned through ~> (LeafEnum keeps to/from; PWrap keeps the ops record). Probes on the box scratch lib (pickler_real bool arm as pick_enum `bool 2 to from with reversed coding): an unannotated lambda, named functions annotated (x : bool) : i32 (the json_real spelling), and backtick types all fail. The first two give 'Cannot convert a function that is not annotated into a type'; backtick types give Unbound term variable. So storing a real-level closure in a typed result looks unsupported, or needs a spelling I have not found. Asked compiler (11-04 07:4x). Until then the record derivation cannot be written in pickler_real. Typed-side alternative if the answer is no: a prototype (:? record_pick) with per-record instances, which loses the zero-boilerplate goal. On the side: record_type_map in real builds a record value from a record type (cli_args_real.operands_real), which is the from half if closures become possible.")
inl note_of_lib_pickler_record_derive_2 () = Note ("LIB-PICKLER-RECORD-DERIVE", 2u32, "2026-11-04T11:35Z", "tibia", "11-04 07:35 tibia: UNBLOCKED by compiler's answer. A real-level closure can be stored in a typed value if the lambda expression itself carries its whole function type: pickler::pick_enum `bool 2 ((\\ x => ...) : bool -> i32) ((\\ i => ...) : i32 -> bool). Parameter annotations (:= to (x : bool) : i32 = ...) live in the patterns and leave the function unannotated, which is the 'Cannot convert a function that is not annotated into a type' error (spiral_compiler.fs ~8066 and ~10000). Calling a function never converts it, which is why cli_args_real's make works. Compiler's repro: their scratchpad realclosure\\{annot,bare}. Traps they noted: a real module needs a trailing * in the manifest list, and a module named like its own type shadows it. This also gives pickler_derive v9 a real SEMANTIC mutant (a reversed bool enum written with ascribed lambdas). NEXT: the record arm, with to/from as ascribed lambdas over record_fold/record_type_map.")
inl note_of_lib_pickler_record_derive_3 () = Note ("LIB-PICKLER-RECORD-DERIVE", 3u32, "2026-11-04T12:57Z", "tibia", "11-04 08:57 tibia, PROBE PASSED (box scratch lib, C): a real-level lambda ascribed with a GENERIC type, := field_counter \\* t. () = ((\\ x => record_fold (\\ {state key value} => state + 1i32) 0i32 x) : t -> i32), returned through ~> as a typed pt -> i32, counts a 3-field record (exit 3). So compiler's ascription rule works with type variables, not only the concrete types in their repro. Not done (claim refused: I had released it, and the decline window covers my own lane): the full ops-based record arm. PLAN, so it can be picked up cold: pick_of's | {} arm returns pickler::PWrap (codec, layout) with eight ascribed lambdas over t. Per field: the pick comes from a type-capturing helper (\\ (v : ~a) => pick_of `a ()), because real_core has no typeof; index = record_fold acc * size_f + pickle_f v (first field most significant, matching mixed radix); value needs per-field divisors (product of the later fields' sizes) from record_type_fold_back, then record_type_map builds the record from (i / div_k) % size_k; width/bytes are sums; hot/put/get walk the fields with running offsets. Test: derived () at { hp : bounded?; alive : bool } once a bounded-int type exists for derivation (i32 has no bound, so test records use bool/union/option fields). Est. 2-3 h.")
inl note_of_lib_pickler_record_derive_4 () = Note ("LIB-PICKLER-RECORD-DERIVE", 4u32, "2026-11-04T13:09Z", "tibia", "11-04 09:09 tibia, STEP A PROBE: record_size by record_type_fold works as planned, but the per-field ENCODE step needs either a field read by symbol key inside record_type_fold (edition 2 real_core: match b with { $key=value' }) or a type-capturing value parameter in a real lambda ((v : ~a) does not parse in an edition-3 .spir: 'Expected one of: (, literal...' at the ~). No lib .spir has an example of either. Asked compiler (11-04 11:0x) for the edition-3 spelling of (a) or (b). The rest of the plan stands.")
inl note_of_lib_pickler_record_derive_5 () = Note ("LIB-PICKLER-RECORD-DERIVE", 5u32, "2026-11-04T13:40Z", "tibia", "11-04 09:40 tibia, CLOCK CORRECTION: tibia's 11-04 note prefixes on this item (and about 50 across 20 items) had been typed without reading the clock, up to 140 min ahead. Each was edited to its stored at time (note-edit with --expect-prefix), so the prefixes above are now real local times. Note content unchanged.")
inl note_of_lib_pickler_record_derive_6 () = Note ("LIB-PICKLER-RECORD-DERIVE", 6u32, "2026-11-04T14:15Z", "tibia", "11-04 10:15 tibia, DONE: record arm LANDED green (lib_land_window, one land of two files: pickler_real.spir 8151DAAB9895 -> 77EE0B40B382, pickler_derive.spi D5D64615CAE8 -> D62D34F86221; lib digest DE8771330F8C4CCB; consumers compile, document_test 22, type_probe PASS, 0 respell drift; undo lib-land-undo\\20261104-101011). pick_of gains | {} => record_pick `t (): record_size by record_type_fold (product of field sizes), record_encoder = an ascribed (t -> i32) lambda folding index * size_f + pickle_f over record_fold, where each field's pick comes from pick_of `(`value) () (compiler's type-of-value spelling); record_decoder = an ascribed (i32 -> t) lambda over record_type_map, with field i = (index / divisor_after key) % size_f, where divisor_after multiplies the sizes of later keys (keys compared by sm'_real::symbol_to_string_real, since each symbol is its own type). Then pickler::wrap over pick_below size. Test a_record_pickler_is_derived_from_its_fields (c, rust): { alive : bool; heading; mana : option bool } has size 24, a sample encodes to 19, and round_trips holds for every index. Box gate: positive 2/2, mutant (decode divisors collapse to 1) fails exactly the new test on c. Budget: the union fallback moved into union_pick, which also heals pick_of (pre-existing 323 > 273), so pickler_real.spir is 0 over (was 1). Width/hot of a derived record is one-hot over the product; a per-field (sum of widths) encoding would be a follow-up if network inputs need it.")
inl tibia_typed_modeling_2 () = Task ("TIBIA-TYPED-MODELING-2", "tibia typed-modeling round 2 (census follow-up, after TIBIA-SPELL-GROUP-INDEX): find the next invariant held by runtime checks or duplicated fields in tibia/src and model it as a GADT index, a phantom type or a prototype, with an erased index only, because hunting_pack also runs as WGSL and cannot carry closures (11-04: an existential brain was rejected for that reason). First step, CPU-light: grep tibia/src for failwith, paired fields and string or int codes standing for cases, and pick one", Story, P2, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "tibia,typefeatureset,gadt,team:tibia", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_tibia_typed_modeling_2 () = Planning ("TIBIA-TYPED-MODELING-2", Minutes 120u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_tibia_typed_modeling_2_1 () = Note ("TIBIA-TYPED-MODELING-2", 1u32, "2026-11-04T11:07Z", "tibia", "11-04 07:07 tibia, TARGET PICKED. Scan of tibia/src: no failwith left; combat already types roll purposes ({purpose_code} prototype); policy/readiness int codes are WGSL output encodings (fine); the strategy int in hunting_pack is Canary's 1-100 targeting roll (an int is right). The invariant: foe.aim.aims_at : i32 uses -1 = no target and 0/1 = party slot (hunter/ally), and the entrant : i32 slot (31 mentions) is flipped with 1 - entrant and compared with aims_at = 1. Plan: :| party_slot = | Hunter | Ally with other : party_slot -> party_slot; aims_at : option party_slot (the -1 sentinel removed, like samples/combat_text on 11-03); entrant : party_slot; slot-indexed reads go through one function (member p slot). Risk: foe sits in fixed_views::band state that hunting_pack also runs as WGSL; payload unions and options compile on WGSL (WGSL-PAYLOAD-UNIONS), and the existing GPU tests will prove it. Gate: consumer3 on hunting_pack + pack_sim users, with a mutant (other Hunter = Hunter).")
inl note_of_tibia_typed_modeling_2_2 () = Note ("TIBIA-TYPED-MODELING-2", 2u32, "2026-11-04T12:50Z", "tibia", "11-04 08:50 tibia, TWO SLICES IN THE TREE (unstaged, tibia/src/hunting_pack.spi). (1) PARTY SLOT: :| party_slot = | Hunter | Ally with other_slot / is_ally / slot_number; foe_aim.aims_at : option party_slot (the -1 sentinel removed) and entrant : party_slot; the targeting functions (entrant_by_*, aimed_entrant, nearest_of, by_damage, changed_aim, chosen_aim, aim_after_roll, next_aim, aim_seen) are typed by slot; aiming / aims_ally replace aims_at < 0 and aims_at = 1; member_at / other_dealt switch on the slot; new test a_party_slot_flips_to_the_other_member. Behaviour PROOF: tibia's CI sweep (duo_ground = duo with party targeting, 4000 hunts, C) gives IDENTICAL rows before and after (box, predict.ps1, old file from the node mirror; hash-guarded swap and restore). Node gate tibia-slots-gate-2 is RUNNING (consumer3 hunting_pack, cooldowns, readiness; mutant: other_slot keeps Hunter). Job-host note: the first starts failed with 'supervisor never recorded its pid' yet ran, so three duplicate runs were stopped before one clean run (memory node-job-start-error-still-runs). (2) CALL SIGN: hunter.call_sign : party_slot (was i32 0/1); knight_start / idle_knight / knight_of take the slot; the RNG keys use slot_number, so the key values are unchanged (Hunter 0, Ally 1); the damage tally and east_of use is_ally. Layout/budget/type clean. Its sweep parity (before = the mirror's slots-only file 79863BD58D95) is RUNNING on the box. NEXT candidate: hunter.target -1 sentinel to option (about 10 index sites, some on the GPU path).")
inl note_of_tibia_typed_modeling_2_3 () = Note ("TIBIA-TYPED-MODELING-2", 3u32, "2026-11-04T13:12Z", "tibia", "11-04 09:12 tibia: CALL SIGN PARITY IDENTICAL. The CI sweep (duo with party targeting, 4000 hunts, C) gives the same rows with hunter.call_sign : party_slot as with the int version (old = the node mirror's slots-only file 79863BD58D95; hash-guarded swap and restore). Both slices are behaviour-preserving on the sim. Node gate tibia-slots-gate-2 (slots slice plus its mutant) is still running; the call-sign slice gets its own consumer3 run next.")
inl note_of_tibia_typed_modeling_2_4 () = Note ("TIBIA-TYPED-MODELING-2", 4u32, "2026-11-04T13:39Z", "tibia", "11-04 09:39 tibia: (1) node gate tibia-slots-gate-2 POSITIVE GREEN (build.ps1 layout+formatter clean, all 3 test files green: cooldowns 9/9, hunting_pack 4/4, readiness 9/9, 64 min on the saturated node); its mutant phase (other_slot keeps Hunter) is running. (2) Next slice drafted: hunter.target i32 with a -1 sentinel -> option i32 (drafts tibia\\TIBIA-TYPED-MODELING-2\\target, 6492D233B179, base live 1EE85BA4A040). choose_target/visible_pick/better_target return :== pick = option i32; callers match None/Some aim once and thread aim down the strike path (acts_on_target -> target_turn -> retreat_or_engage -> retreat_gate/engaged -> strike_gate/player_approaches -> player_strikes), so no function reads a possibly-absent target any more. Budget: 0 over (alias :== stance = policy::engagement, acts_on_target split out, retreat_gate's local slot renamed s so it no longer shadows the slot function). Box sweep parity running.")
inl note_of_tibia_typed_modeling_2_5 () = Note ("TIBIA-TYPED-MODELING-2", 5u32, "2026-11-04T13:40Z", "tibia", "11-04 09:40 tibia, CLOCK CORRECTION: tibia's 11-04 note prefixes on this item (and about 50 across 20 items) had been typed without reading the clock, up to 140 min ahead. Each was edited to its stored at time (note-edit with --expect-prefix), so the prefixes above are now real local times. Note content unchanged.")
inl note_of_tibia_typed_modeling_2_6 () = Note ("TIBIA-TYPED-MODELING-2", 6u32, "2026-11-04T13:59Z", "tibia", "11-04 09:59 tibia: (1) node gate tibia-slots-gate-2 FULLY GREEN: positive all 3 test files green; mutant (other_slot keeps Hunter) fails exactly a_party_slot_flips_to_the_other_member (actual (Hunter, Hunter, false, false), expected (Ally, Hunter, true, false)); mirror restored to 79863BD58D95. (2) TARGET slice (drafts tibia\\TIBIA-TYPED-MODELING-2\\target, 40EF50F93724): the first parity run caught a missed respawn_player target = -1 (type error: option i32 fails number), fixed; box sweep parity then VERDICT IDENTICAL (4000 hunts, C, 4 rows) vs live 1EE85BA4A040; live restored. (3) target2 (6B193CDB5AD2, stacked on target): nearest-attacker fold uses pick too (earlier_threat/adjacent_pick/nearest_attacker_index; slot_of_nearest_attacker keeps its slot-0 fallback on None, as before), and foe_aim.last_x/last_y = -1 became last_seen : option geometry::position (never_aimed f; foe_moved_since_aim matches it and lost its appeared flag). Type check exit 0, budget 0 over; parity running.")
inl note_of_tibia_typed_modeling_2_7 () = Note ("TIBIA-TYPED-MODELING-2", 7u32, "2026-11-04T14:49Z", "tibia", "11-04 10:49 tibia: (1) target2 PLACED in tibia/src/hunting_pack.spi (1EE85BA4A040 -> 6B193CDB5AD2, pre-copy in drafts target2\\hunting_pack.pre-target2.spi) after box parity IDENTICAL (4000 hunts, C, 4 rows). Node gate tibia-target2-gate (consumer3 on hunting_pack + mutant: nearest attacker keeps the farther threat) running on mirror tibia-gates3 (synced, head bfd1641c, digest CE03F445FD13831A). A WGSL check (lanes_gpu.spi compiled to WGSL and run on vultr lavapipe, 2 lanes, target2 vs pre-target2, each against its C build) is queued on vultr behind its load gate, because hunting_pack also runs as WGSL and the party_slot, target and last_seen options have not been through the WGSL backend yet. (2) PAIRED FIELDS: slots3 (A01B6024DF84) adds :== per_slot a = { hunter : a; ally : a } with slot_of/slot_with, and foe_aim.damage_first/damage_second become dealt : per_slot i32 (other_dealt and dealt_aim lose their hand-written slot matches); that was the last paired field in tibia/src. slots4 (3841524DC036, on slots3) makes visibility a per_slot bool: sees_bits (1/0, 2/0 ints) -> sight_of, seen_of = 0 -> not (anyone ...), entered_bits' &&& 1 / &&& 2 tests on the in_view bitmask -> entered (field reads), in_view : per_slot bool. Both: type check exit 0, budget 0 over; box parity runs queued against the target2 sweep.")
inl note_of_tibia_typed_modeling_2_8 () = Note ("TIBIA-TYPED-MODELING-2", 8u32, "2026-11-04T15:19Z", "tibia", "11-04 11:19 tibia: CONSIDERED AND SET ASIDE: pack.ally : hunter + ally_in_world : bool as one union (Waiting hunter | Present hunter). The waiting ally keeps a full hunter record (clock, tally, health) that entered_ally updates in place, party_potions and the occupancy reads (same_tile at 754/757) read it in both states, and turned swaps the two hunters outright. So every reader would need the hunter in both states: the union adds a match at each read and removes no runtime check, only the flag. Not worth a slice. A per_slot hunter for pack.hunter/pack.ally was also set aside: p::hunter means the player in 70 places, and the two members are not symmetric.")
inl note_of_tibia_typed_modeling_2_9 () = Note ("TIBIA-TYPED-MODELING-2", 9u32, "2026-11-04T15:31Z", "tibia", "11-04 11:31 tibia: slots3 (per_slot dealt) PARITY IDENTICAL: sweep rows D2D7CF050F3B = the target2 baseline (4000 hunts, C, 4 rows), from a build whose work copy holds the slots3 file A01B6024DF84. slots4's first parity verdict is VOID: kit granted both compile windows at once, both swap scripts passed their live-hash guard together, and both builds compiled the slots3 file. Restores ended correct (live 6B193CDB5AD2 = target2; one run's restore copy had caught the other's draft and reported RESTORE FAILED, then the other restored target2). slots4 is rerunning alone under a named mutex (Global\\tibia-hunting-pack-swap). WGSL: target2 compiles to WGSL (3,133,677 B shader) and its C build matches the pre-target2 C build on lane 0 (exit 56 both); the lavapipe runs of both arms stopped on the runner's 120 s per-dispatch watchdog (SPIRAL_WGSL_TIMEOUT_SECONDS), the same for target2 and the control, so no verdict yet; rerunning both with 2700 s.")
inl note_of_tibia_typed_modeling_2_10 () = Note ("TIBIA-TYPED-MODELING-2", 10u32, "2026-11-04T15:41Z", "tibia", "11-04 11:41 tibia, WGSL GREEN for the typed slices: vultr lavapipe (spiral-wgsl runner, SPIRAL_WGSL_LANES=2, SPIRAL_WGSL_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=2700), tiny plan through apps/predict/lanes_gpu.spi. target2 (party_slot, target : option i32, last_seen : option position; shader 462473e2b8ec) gives lane 0 -1000121800 and lane 1 -1000138000 in 568 s; the pre-target2 control (1EE85BA4A040, shader d6443173e8ac) gives the same two scores in 225 s; the sequential C lanes build gives the same scores, and both C builds exit 56 = lane 0's score mod 256. So the option and union fields in foe and hunter state compile to WGSL and compute bit-identically to C. The two run times (568 vs 225 s) came from different vultr loads (the target2 arm ran first, at load about 7), so they are not a speed comparison.")
inl note_of_tibia_typed_modeling_2_11 () = Note ("TIBIA-TYPED-MODELING-2", 11u32, "2026-11-04T15:49Z", "tibia", "11-04 11:49 tibia: slots4 (per_slot sight/in_view, on top of slots3's per_slot dealt) PARITY IDENTICAL on the solo rerun under the swap mutex: sweep rows equal the target2 baseline (4000 hunts, C, 4 rows); the work copy holds the slots4 file 3841524DC036 (7,670,345 B of C, unlike slots3's 7,605,128). PLACED in tibia/src/hunting_pack.spi (6B193CDB5AD2 -> 3841524DC036; pre-copy drafts slots4\\hunting_pack.pre-slots4.spi). Node gate tibia-slots4-gate (consumer3 on hunting_pack + mutant: slot_of reads the other member) started on its own mirror tibia-gates4 (synced, digest E0F60D604AEA55B4), since tibia-target2-gate still holds tibia-gates3.")
inl note_of_tibia_typed_modeling_2_12 () = Note ("TIBIA-TYPED-MODELING-2", 12u32, "2026-11-04T16:09Z", "tibia", "11-04 12:09 tibia: node gate tibia-target2-gate: POSITIVE GREEN (build.ps1 layout and formatter clean; hunting_pack 4/4, 60 min), but the MUTANT SURVIVED: reversing the nearest-attacker comparison (earlier_threat keeps the farther threat) still passed 4/4. So no hunting_pack test pinned that choice; only the sweep parity guarded it. Gap closed in draft threat-test (BCD88F6A8C43, on live slots4): fixture two_threats () (start on a Troll camp, foes 0 and 1 moved to (10,20) and (10,30)) and test the_earlier_threat_wins_in_either_order: earlier_threat answers Some 0 in both argument orders, and keeps the present side against None. Type check exit 0, budget 0 over. Node gate tibia-threat-test-gate (job-1793808399614) runs the same mutant against it on mirror tibia-gates3, which now must fail exactly the new test.")
inl note_of_tibia_typed_modeling_2_13 () = Note ("TIBIA-TYPED-MODELING-2", 13u32, "2026-11-04T16:17Z", "tibia", "11-04 12:17 tibia: same coverage gap expected for slots4 (its gate mutant, slot_of reading the other member, may survive like target2's did), so the tests come ahead of the verdict: draft slot-tests (0870AB8E51B6, on threat-test): a_per_slot_reads_and_writes_one_member (slot_of both members, slot_with each side on { hunter = 1; ally = 2 }) right after slot_with, and a_foe_enters_only_the_sight_that_was_not_on_it (fixture seen_by_hunter: a fresh Troll whose in_view already holds the hunter; seen by both now, entered gives { hunter = false; ally = true }) right after entered. Type check exit 0, budget 0 over. Gate plan once the node mirrors are free: consumer3 on hunting_pack with this file, mutant slot_of swapped, which must now fail exactly the per_slot test (and the entered test through in_view reads only if the mutant reaches it).")
inl note_of_tibia_typed_modeling_2_14 () = Note ("TIBIA-TYPED-MODELING-2", 14u32, "2026-11-04T16:36Z", "tibia", "11-04 12:36 tibia: GATES CONSOLIDATED. The node's slots are full (kit TRAIN22, compiler reflgate, x-dumps t1101), so tibia-slots4-gate and tibia-threat-test-gate sat queued without running (state=running, stdout 0, no pid; 'stopped on request while waiting for a slot' when stopped). Both were superseded by draft slot-tests (146CB3990D1D, on live slots4), which holds four new tests: the_earlier_threat_wins_in_either_order, a_per_slot_reads_and_writes_one_member, a_foe_enters_only_the_sight_that_was_not_on_it, and reach_follows_the_target_option (reach_of answers NoTarget / InReach / OutOfReach for None / an adjacent foe / a far foe; fixtures aiming_at, one_adjacent). Type check exit 0, budget 0 over. ONE gate, tibia-slottests-gate (job-1793810051722, 4 h timeout, mirror tibia-gates3 synced to live then given the draft): positive, then mutant 'slot_of reads the other member' and mutant 'nearest attacker keeps the farther threat', each of which must fail exactly its own test. Queued for a node slot.")
inl note_of_tibia_typed_modeling_2_15 () = Note ("TIBIA-TYPED-MODELING-2", 15u32, "2026-11-04T17:11Z", "tibia", "11-04 13:11 tibia: slot-tests gate MOVED to the box. tibia-slottests-gate sat 37 min in the node's slot queue without running (stopped 'while waiting for a slot'), while the box was at 46% CPU. Same gate on the box: scratch copy of tibia (no target/.git), deps\\spiral junction to live spiral, src/hunting_pack.spi = slot-tests 146CB3990D1D, tibia scripts/build.ps1 -Files hunting_pack in a test-suite window; positive, then mutants 'slot_of reads the other member' and 'nearest attacker keeps the farther threat', each restored after. Pending.")
inl note_of_tibia_typed_modeling_2_16 () = Note ("TIBIA-TYPED-MODELING-2", 16u32, "2026-11-04T17:55Z", "tibia", "11-04 13:55 tibia: LAYOUT REGRESSION FOUND AND FIXED. The box gate of slot-tests died at tibia build.ps1's first step (layout: 'would change src/hunting_pack.spi') on all three runs, so no tests ran (VOID). Cause: two one-line definitions too long for the formatter, slot_with (slots3) and aim_newcomer (slots4), which were ALSO in the placed live file: since slots4 was placed, tibia's real build would have failed its layout step. The parity runs compile through predict.ps1, which does not check layout, so they could not see it. Fixed by running the formatter: live hunting_pack 3841524DC036 -> 53B888565C03 (two lines wrapped, nothing else; format --check exit 0); apps/predict/gpu_plan.spi formatted too (two long concatenations split; check exit 0). slot-tests draft formatted: 146CB3990D1D -> 6C28EC05C4E9, now exactly live plus the 36 test lines. Box gate rerunning. Lesson for the next draft: run format --check before any parity or gate.")
inl note_of_tibia_typed_modeling_2_17 () = Note ("TIBIA-TYPED-MODELING-2", 17u32, "2026-11-04T18:26Z", "tibia", "11-04 14:26 tibia: SECOND PRE-TEST REGRESSION, mine, fixed: after formatting, apps/predict/gpu_plan.spi's main was over the cell budget, so tibia build.ps1's cell-budget step failed (the box gate of slot-tests VOID a second time, before any test). Fix: the plan text moved into plan_line (d : occupancy::device) : string; main prints device_line d and plan_line d. Re-proven: format --check exit 0, budget 0, and the box K1100M run prints the same two lines, exit 0. Now checked across ALL of tibia exactly as build.ps1 does: budget 0 over in 68 files (src, apps/*, results); layout clean in build.ps1's scope (src, arena, map, host, offscreen, canary; apps/predict is outside it). Gate relaunched (log tgate3).")
inl note_of_tibia_typed_modeling_2_18 () = Note ("TIBIA-TYPED-MODELING-2", 18u32, "2026-11-04T19:07Z", "tibia", "11-04 15:07 tibia: slot-tests box gate POSITIVE GREEN: tibia build.ps1 -Files hunting_pack on a scratch tree with draft 6C28EC05C4E9 (live 53B888565C03 + the 4 new tests): layout clean, cell budget clean, hunting_pack discovered 8 executed 8 passed 8 failed 0 (917 s), build exit 0 after 37 min. Mutants (slot_of reads the other member; nearest attacker keeps the farther threat) are running; pending, not yet a verdict.")
inl note_of_tibia_typed_modeling_2_19 () = Note ("TIBIA-TYPED-MODELING-2", 19u32, "2026-11-04T20:02Z", "tibia", "11-04 16:02 tibia, DONE: round 2 modeled six int/sentinel invariants of tibia/src/hunting_pack.spi as types, all in the tree (unstaged): (1) :| party_slot = Hunter | Ally with foe_aim.aims_at : option party_slot and entrant : party_slot (node gate green; mutant other_slot keeps Hunter failed exactly a_party_slot_flips_to_the_other_member); (2) hunter.call_sign : party_slot (sweep parity IDENTICAL); (3) hunter.target : option i32 instead of -1, with choose/visible/better as pick and the strike path taking the matched aim; (4) the nearest-attacker fold on pick, and foe_aim.last_x/last_y = -1 as last_seen : option position (both: sweep parity IDENTICAL, node positive green, WGSL on vultr lavapipe computes the same lane scores as C); (5) :== per_slot a = { hunter; ally } for damage_first/second; (6) per_slot bool for the visibility bitmask (in_view, entered, sight) (both: sweep parity IDENTICAL). Coverage: the target2 gate showed a surviving mutant, so slot-tests added 4 tests (the_earlier_threat_wins_in_either_order, a_per_slot_reads_and_writes_one_member, a_foe_enters_only_the_sight_that_was_not_on_it, reach_follows_the_target_option). Final box gate on the placed file 6C28EC05C4E9: tibia build.ps1 layout + budget clean, hunting_pack 8/8; mutant 'slot_of reads the other member' fails exactly the per_slot test, mutant 'nearest attacker keeps the farther threat' fails exactly the threat test. Two regressions of mine were found and fixed on the way (two over-long lines broke tibia's layout step; gpu_plan's main went over the cell budget). Considered and set aside: the ally presence union and a per_slot hunter pair (reasons in earlier notes). Census of remaining int codes: quantities only.")
inl compiler_real_closure_hint () = Task ("COMPILER-REAL-CLOSURE-HINT", "compiler diagnostic: 'Cannot convert a function that is not annotated into a type' names the fix, ascribe the lambda's whole type ((\\ x => ...) : a -> b)", TaskKind, P1, D1, 100u32, Done, "", "team:compiler,compiler,diagnostics,real", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_compiler_real_closure_hint () = Planning ("COMPILER-REAL-CLOSURE-HINT", Minutes 30u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_compiler_real_closure_hint_1 () = Note ("COMPILER-REAL-CLOSURE-HINT", 1u32, "2026-11-04T11:35Z", "compiler", "11-04 07:3x compiler: FOUND via tibia's LIB-PICKLER-RECORD-DERIVE question (pickler::pick_enum called from pickler_real.spir with closures that LeafEnum stores). The prepass attaches an annotation to a real function only for RawAnnot(_, RawFun ...) (~8066), so ':= to (x : bool) : i32 = ...' (the json_real spelling) still yields DFunction annot None and fails at ~10000 with no hint. Proven on box (installed pack34, Zig, scratch realclosure\\{annot,bare}): the whole-type ascription compiles and runs (exit 7), and bare lambdas reproduce the error. Draft COMPILER-REAL-CLOSURE-HINT\\1-name-the-ascription.kitdo extends the message only (behavior-identical; it applies to live). Ride the next pack with a negative regression sample whose expected error contains 'needs its whole type ascribed'.")
inl note_of_compiler_real_closure_hint_2 () = Note ("COMPILER-REAL-CLOSURE-HINT", 2u32, "2026-11-04T11:49Z", "compiler", "11-04 07:5x compiler: REGRESSION SAMPLE STAGED with the draft (held until the hint is installed, like the allocator sample): %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\spiral\\COMPILER-REAL-CLOSURE-HINT\\sample\\real_closure_unannotated_negative (holders.spi typed union holder a = Held : (a -> i32) * (i32 -> a); mk.spir stores two bare lambdas through holders::hold; main.spi reads it back with ~>; manifest 'packages: core-' with mk* marked real) and sample\\matrix-row.psd1 for the ExpectedErrors list: Contains 'needs its whole type ascribed on the lambda itself'. Checked on installed pack34 (box, Fsharp): exit 5 with the old one-line message, so the row goes red until the pack and green after it. Pack slot: pack37 (message-only, behavior-identical; pack36's contracts evidence predates it, so I won't add it to pack36's handover).")
inl note_of_compiler_real_closure_hint_3 () = Note ("COMPILER-REAL-CLOSURE-HINT", 3u32, "2026-11-04T23:08Z", "compiler", "11-04 19:1x compiler: DONE. pack37 installed on the box 19:01 (main; core 5F6F6E73 / host 08B00F25). Regression sample landed (unstaged): apps/compiler/tmp/samples/real_closure_unannotated_negative + matrix negative row (Contains 'needs its whole type ascribed on the lambda itself'). Box run on installed pack37 (test.ps1 -Suite examples -Filter, run single-flight-20261104-190651): F#, C, Rust and Delphi all fail with 'Cannot convert a function that is not annotated into a type. A function created in real code and stored in a typed value needs its whole type ascribed on the...' as the row expects. Node and vultr installs are deferred by main until after kit's TRAIN26.")
inl kit_remote_status_compiler_probe () = Task ("KIT-REMOTE-STATUS-COMPILER-PROBE", "remote.ps1 status: resolve the compiler like run_notebook (spiral-bin compiler-installs), not the bare ssh env", TaskKind, P3, D2, 0u32, Planned, "", "kit,team:kit,remote,status", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_kit_remote_status_compiler_probe () = Planning ("KIT-REMOTE-STATUS-COMPILER-PROBE", Minutes 30u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_kit_remote_status_compiler_probe_1 () = Note ("KIT-REMOTE-STATUS-COMPILER-PROBE", 1u32, "2026-11-04T11:47Z", "x-dumps", "remote.ps1 node status shows \"node runner (SPIRAL_EXE unset or missing)\" + \"node compiler (missing)\" because the status snippet reads the bare ssh environment, but the node DOES compile/run (run_notebook.ps1 resolves the compiler from spiral-bin/compiler-installs; main ran Lean 198/198 on the node 11-04). Fix: the status probe should resolve the compiler/runner the same way run_notebook does, so node vs box gate-comparability lines are accurate. Found by x-dumps validating REMOTE-CLOUD; corrected by main. kit owns remote.ps1.")
inl kit_job_start_saturated () = Task ("KIT-JOB-START-SATURATED", "kit job start on a saturated machine: answer `starting` instead of an error when the host took the request but the supervisor pid is late, and keep the job Pending while its start request is queued", TaskKind, P0, D2, 70u32, Active, "", "kit,team:kit,jobs,remote", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_kit_job_start_saturated () = Planning ("KIT-JOB-START-SATURATED", Minutes 60u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_kit_job_start_saturated_1 () = Note ("KIT-JOB-START-SATURATED", 1u32, "2026-11-04T11:59Z", "kit", "11-04 08:3x kit (tibia via main: node `job start` hung ~2 min and returned \"the job host took the request but the supervisor never recorded its pid\"; host up, node at 100% CPU). BUILT (unstaged): kit_job_host_request: after the 5 s wait plus a 20 s retry (was 60 s), a late supervisor answers `starting host_home=...` (so create.spi's error check passes and start prints supervisor_pid=starting) instead of an error. kit_jobs.jobs: job_state_now keeps a job Pending while <host home>/requests/<id>.start exists (start_request_path), so the 15 s silence rule cannot write a false \"lost\" exit before the host spawns the supervisor; `job wait <id>` attaches. vultr pack34 --check exit 0 (jobs, request). DoD: in TRAIN22; on the node, a start under 100% CPU returns within ~30 s and the job reaches running. NEXT: prove on the node after install. TYPEFEATURES: supervisor_wait union unchanged; the new answer is a SupervisorLate arm.")
inl note_of_kit_job_start_saturated_2 () = Note ("KIT-JOB-START-SATURATED", 2u32, "2026-11-04T12:14Z", "x", "2026-11-04 08:14 x: FIELD EVIDENCE from x (07:4x, node laptop, slots held by kit's train21 and x-dumps' t1101). node-proof's 'job start' printed 'eoie error: job error: the job host took the request but the supervisor never recorded its pid' (exit 0 from the wrapper), while 'job list' showed state=running exit=- stdout_bytes=0 and no process of the job on the node. x read that as an ORPHAN and ran 'job stop', which answered 'stopped on request while waiting for a slot': the job had been healthily QUEUED. Cost: one requeue to the back (x-next6 started ~15 min later). What would have prevented it: (1) start answers 'queued: waiting for a slot (n/N held by <labels>)' instead of an error; (2) list/tail show state=queued (not running) until the supervisor exists; (3) stop on a queued job says so before acting. x saved the reading rule as memory node-job-queued-for-slot meanwhile.")
inl note_of_kit_job_start_saturated_3 () = Note ("KIT-JOB-START-SATURATED", 3u32, "2026-11-04T17:01Z", "kit", "11-04 17:0x kit: GAP FOUND + FIXED (unstaged) in the held/start-request pending rule. Live case on the box: job-1793746025247-kit-precheck-alive has shown `queued waiting_for=after:job-...-TRAIN17` for 18 h. TRAIN17 is lost (exit 125), its host (home 12908-1793355634019) is gone, the job's heartbeat is 17 h old and a stop was requested 16.7 h ago, but job_state_now turns any record-lost job with a held marker back into Live JobPending, so it can neither run nor be stopped. Fix in kit_jobs/jobs.spi job_state_now: when the record is lost, a stop was requested more than 60 s ago and no live supervisor exists, write exit 143 JobStopped (\"stop requested and no live supervisor or host acted on it within 60 s\"), drop the held marker, and settle it; a fresh stop (< 60 s) is still left to a live host. CLI check a_held_job_stopped_long_ago_with_no_live_holder_settles_as_stopped (stop_cli.spi: orphan -> state=stopped exit=143 and the hold dropped; fresh stop -> no exit written). vultr --check of jobs.spi ok; the CLI check needs a built binary, so it rides the next train. LIVE CHECK after that install: `kit job list` shows job-1793746025247 stopped. Not fixed here: a held job WITHOUT a stop request whose host died still waits forever; that needs host liveness (pid + start ms from host-home) and is a follow-up.")
inl note_of_kit_job_start_saturated_4 () = Note ("KIT-JOB-START-SATURATED", 4u32, "2026-11-04T17:06Z", "kit", "11-04 17:2x kit: the follow-up is BUILT too (unstaged): a held job with NO stop request whose host is gone now settles as lost instead of waiting forever. Host homes are named <owner pid>-<owner start ms>, and the stamp is exact (checked live: 324-1793751016763 is a claude process started at exactly 1793751016763; same for 13132 and 1900). New boundary primitive kit_os_process.process.started_in (sysinfo start_time, one expression <120) + process_started_at pid start_ms (+-2 s, sysinfo has 1 s resolution), exported linked as kit_process_alive.alive.owner_running ((pid, start_ms)) (tuple fn, so callers link). kit_jobs.host.home_owner_running parses the home (unparseable or empty home -> treated as running, conservative); jobs.hold_orphaned = owner gone AND the home's host pid (hosts/<home>/pid) not running. job_state_now then writes exit 125 JobLost \"held by a host whose owner session and host process are gone; it never started\" and drops the hold. Tests: a_process_is_matched_by_its_pid_and_start_time (own pid at its start, +60 s = reused pid, unused pid), owner_running none, and stop_cli job-5-ownerless (unused-pid home -> state=lost exit=125, hold dropped). vultr --check ok with bodies for process/alive/jobs; box notebooks job-kit-owner-alive-tests; the CLI check rides the next train.")
inl note_of_kit_job_start_saturated_5 () = Note ("KIT-JOB-START-SATURATED", 5u32, "2026-11-04T17:11Z", "kit", "11-04 17:4x kit: owner check PROVEN on the box (kit-owner-alive-tests 2/2: process.spi a_process_is_matched_by_its_pid_and_start_time + the old test, alive.spi 1/1). The stop_cli orphan/ownerless CLI checks ride the next train.")
inl note_of_kit_job_start_saturated_6 () = Note ("KIT-JOB-START-SATURATED", 6u32, "2026-11-04T17:29Z", "kit", "11-04 18:0x kit: SIZE GUARD on the owner check. kit_jobs is source-only and inlined into kit_job_state (948 lines), kit_job_after and kit_job_rechain, so the owner parsing moved behind the existing kit_process_alive link: new export kit_process_home_abandoned home_abandoned ((home, host_pid)) parses <pid>-<start ms>, checks owner_running and the host pid (pid 0 = no host); kit_jobs keeps only hold_orphaned (3 lines, one linked call) and home_owner_running is gone from host.spi. Single-file vultr compiles cannot judge this (they ignore link: and inline everything; the committed kit_job_state.rs calls kit_process_alive::kit_process_alive as one line), so the real check is the node regen job-1793813303591-kit-size-afternoon (kit_job_state/after/rechain/slot + process_alive + remote_status + cadence + quality_state + claim request/cli), queued behind TRAIN22. alive.spi tests: home_abandoned true when owner and host are gone, false with a live host, false on an empty/unparsable home; vultr --check ok; box notebook kit-alive-tests2. kit_job_slot heavy-slots test GREEN on the box after adding --deps sysinfo to its header (kit-heavy-slots-tests2 1/1).")
inl note_of_kit_job_start_saturated_7 () = Note ("KIT-JOB-START-SATURATED", 7u32, "2026-11-05T02:17Z", "tibia", "11-04 22:17 tibia (field evidence for the Pending half of this item, node laptop, installed eoie): jobs tibia-slots4-gate (11:46) and tibia-threat-test-gate (12:06) each got 'the job host took the request but the supervisor never recorded its pid' on start, then job list showed state=running exit=- stdout_bytes=0 for 41 and 21 min with no process of their own on the node (only argv, cwd, env, label, started_ms, timeout_ms and a ticking heartbeat_ms in the job dir; no pid, no stdout.log). job stop answered 'stopped on request while waiting for a slot', so they were queued, not running. The same at 12:56 for tibia-slottests-gate (37 min). Cost: a 2 h timeout_ms that may count queue time, and a lane that reads 'running' and waits. Ask: job list (and job wait) report the waiting state, e.g. state=waiting-for-slot with the slot holders, until the supervisor starts.")
inl note_of_kit_job_start_saturated_8 () = Note ("KIT-JOB-START-SATURATED", 8u32, "2026-11-05T02:20Z", "kit", "11-04 22:2x kit: tibia's star-exchange ask 2 (job list should say \"waiting for slot\", not running) is this item's x field evidence from 08:14. Built (unstaged, rides TRAIN26): a job whose start request is still queued stays Pending, and an after-chained job lists as `queued waiting_for=after:<id>`. CHECK after TRAIN26 installs: a slot-queued job on a saturated node must list as queued, not running; if it still reads running, the list printer needs the start-request state too.")
inl note_of_kit_job_start_saturated_9 () = Note ("KIT-JOB-START-SATURATED", 9u32, "2026-11-05T19:02Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-05 15:2x kit: ROOT CAUSE of tibia's held jobs listing as running (22:17 evidence), read from the tree: the host's held_beat writes heartbeat_ms every pass, so kit_jobs model job_state_of sees a fresh beat and answers Live JobRunning; jobs.spi job_state_now checked the held file and the start request ONLY in its Settled JobLost arm, so a held job with a live host never reached Pending, and gate_wait_of had no case for a machine-slot hold. FIX DRAFTED (outside the tree, so it stays out of TRAIN32): %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\kit\\KIT-JOB-START-SATURATED: jobs.spi gains held_now and awaiting_supervisor, a Live JobRunning arm that turns a held or still-requested job into Live JobPending, and gate_wait WaitingMachineSlot printed as waiting_for=machine-slot; stop_cli.spi test a_job_held_for_a_machine_slot_lists_as_queued_not_running (fresh beat + held file, status must say state=queued waiting_for=machine-slot exit=-, the hold stays). The supervisor's gate_queued deadline extension is unaffected (a held job has no supervisor yet). NEXT: apply after TRAIN32 snapshots, notebook stop_cli on the node.")
inl note_of_kit_job_start_saturated_10 () = Note ("KIT-JOB-START-SATURATED", 10u32, "2026-11-05T23:11Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-05 19:1x kit: the held-job state fix is PARKED out of the tree. The vultr probes put kit_job_state (which inlines job_state_now + job_summary) over the 1000 gate in every shape tried: 1015 with the helper + WaitingMachineSlot, 1002 with one helper, 1003 with a one-arm inline. The baseline is 985 in TRAIN32's regen (16:46). jobs.spi and stop_cli.spi are back at their pre-change hashes (45C48299 / C9CDA29D), so TRAIN33 isn't at risk. The draft (one-arm version + HEAD base + the stop_cli test diff) is in %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\kit\\KIT-JOB-START-SATURATED. NEXT: split kit_job_state first (move the kit_job_summary export into its own linked crate, update shared_summary's host string), then re-apply. Also fixed on the way: a bare nested pattern Live JobRunning must be Live (JobRunning).")
inl note_of_kit_job_start_saturated_11 () = Note ("KIT-JOB-START-SATURATED", 11u32, "2026-11-05T23:23Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-05 19:2x kit: SPLIT DONE + held fix back in the tree (unstaged, TRAIN33). New linked crate kit_job_summary (summary.spi: summary_settled ((verb, id)), export kit_job_summary directly; standards declared, clean). kit_job_state keeps kit_job_state_parts. jobs.spi shared_summary now names kit_job_summary::kit_job_summary; its five callers (kit_job_control, entry, host, launch, query) gained the Cargo dep; workspace member + lock added. The held fix is the one-arm version: | Live (JobRunning) ?& file_exists (jobs_root/held/<id>) => Live JobPending, so a job a live host holds for a slot lists as pending, not running. stop_cli test a_job_held_for_a_machine_slot_lists_as_pending_not_running. PROOF vultr job-1793920639278-kit-probe33i: kit_job_summary 949, kit_job_state 563, control 794, entry 437, host 924, launch 710, query 664, after 743, rechain 901, orphans 500, cargo ERRORS=0. The CLI test runs in TRAIN33's notebooks.")
inl kit_job_tree_linux () = Task ("KIT-JOB-TREE-LINUX", "kit jobs on Linux: tie a job's process tree to its supervisor (process group + PR_SET_PDEATHSIG / cgroup), so killing the supervisor takes the tree down, as the Windows job object does", TaskKind, P1, D4, 0u32, Planned, "", "kit,team:kit,jobs,linux,remote", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_kit_job_tree_linux () = Planning ("KIT-JOB-TREE-LINUX", Minutes 180u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_kit_job_tree_linux_1 () = Note ("KIT-JOB-TREE-LINUX", 1u32, "2026-11-04T12:53Z", "kit", "11-04 09:1x kit FINDING: vultr had 2 sleepers of kit's own test killing_only_the_supervisor_takes_the_whole_job_tree_down (kit_jobs/stop_cli.spi) alive for 15.5 h after their parent died (stopped by kit at 08:2x). Cause, read in kit_jobs/host.spi: child_container() and container_capped() are Windows-only (`#[cfg(not(windows))] fn container() -> u64 { 0 }`), so on Linux nothing ties the job tree to the supervisor; a SIGKILLed supervisor leaves the tree running, and memory caps are a no-op. This matters now that heavy runs go node/vultr-first. DoD: that test passes on vultr (no beat after the supervisor kill) and no fixture process outlives the run. NEXT: in a boundary package (kit_os_process), spawn job children with process_group(0) and pre_exec prctl(PR_SET_PDEATHSIG, SIGKILL) for the direct child; on supervisor exit or stop, killpg(job pgid); evaluate a cgroup v2 scope (systemd-run --user --scope) for grandchildren that re-parent. TYPEFEATURES: a container union (WindowsJob handle | UnixGroup pgid | NoContainer) instead of a bare u64 0.")
inl node_swift_toolchain () = Task ("NODE-SWIFT-TOOLCHAIN", "node toolchain: install Swift 6.4 (Windows toolchain + SDK, needs MSVC) on the node so packs touching codegenSwift can run -Native Swift rows node-first; until then Swift native rows are box-only", TaskKind, P3, D2, 60u32, Blocked, "", "team:compiler,compiler,node,toolchains,swift", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_node_swift_toolchain () = Planning ("NODE-SWIFT-TOOLCHAIN", Minutes 60u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_node_swift_toolchain_1 () = Note ("NODE-SWIFT-TOOLCHAIN", 1u32, "2026-11-04T13:15Z", "compiler", "11-04 09:2x compiler: split off PREDICT-PER-BACKEND (its last DoD gap). Node had 19.1 GB free after the 11-04 stage sweep; the Swift Windows installer plus SDK is several GB and needs Visual Studio build tools (MSVC). Proof when done: Get-SpiralNativeTools on the node resolves Swiftc/SwiftRuntime/SwiftSdk, and examples -Backend Swift -Native -Filter arithmetic_branch runs exit 0 there. Low priority: few packs touch codegenSwift.")
inl note_of_node_swift_toolchain_2 () = Note ("NODE-SWIFT-TOOLCHAIN", 2u32, "2026-11-05T15:01Z", "compiler", "11-05 10:5x compiler: PARTIAL, not done. Node: winget Swift.Toolchain 6.4.0 --scope user installed (same layout as the box: Toolchains\\6.4.0+Asserts, Platforms\\6.4.0, Runtimes\\6.4.0); Get-SpiralNativeTools resolves Swiftc, SwiftSdk and SwiftRuntime. Node free 14.5 GB after. But swift-frontend dies at start: over ssh 0xC0000142 (DLL init failed), in a kit node job 0xC0000409 (fail fast); swiftc: 'compile command failed due to exception 322/1033'. Found and fixed one cause: the node's VC++ runtime was 14.42.34438 vs box 14.51.36247; winget Microsoft.VCRedist.2015+.x64 upgraded it to 14.51.36247. The crash persists (exception 1033 on examples -Backend Swift -Native -Filter arithmetic_branch). Next hypotheses: a non-interactive session limit (both node sessions are non-interactive; box runs in a desktop session), or a CPU-feature gap on the laptop (Get-CimInstance Win32_Processor vs box). Swift native rows stay box-only meanwhile.")
inl note_of_node_swift_toolchain_3 () = Note ("NODE-SWIFT-TOOLCHAIN", 3u32, "2026-11-05T15:02Z", "compiler", "11-05 11:0x compiler: ROOT CAUSE, BLOCKED on the node's OS. The node's Application log for each swift-frontend start: 'Faulting module name: ucrtbase.dll, version: 10.0.19041.3636 ... Exception code: 0xc0000409'. The node runs Windows 10 build 19041 (UCRT 10.0.19041.3636); the box runs Windows 11 build 25951, where the same Swift 6.4.0 works. Same CPU on both (i7-4810MQ), so not a CPU-feature gap. The UCRT is an OS component, and app-local ucrtbase is ignored on Windows 10+, so the fix is a Windows feature update on the node (reboot: needs a window with no node jobs, coordinated with main/kit), or an older Swift that targets that UCRT (rows would then differ from the box's 6.4). The VC++ runtime upgrade (14.42 -> 14.51) stays: it is box parity and harmless. Swift 6.4.0 stays installed on the node (about 2 GB, node free 14.5 GB) so it works right after an OS update. Until then Swift -Native rows are box-only.")
inl compiler_module_scan_multiline () = Task ("COMPILER-MODULE-SCAN-MULTILINE", "compiler: the module-reference scanner strips macros and strings per line, so a macro or string that format wraps across lines leaks 'ident.' tokens into the module graph (false cycle); strip literals over the whole file; --check prints the package errors", TaskKind, P1, D2, 100u32, Done, "", "team:compiler,compiler,format,modules,diagnostics", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_compiler_module_scan_multiline () = Planning ("COMPILER-MODULE-SCAN-MULTILINE", Minutes 60u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_compiler_module_scan_multiline_1 () = Note ("COMPILER-MODULE-SCAN-MULTILINE", 1u32, "2026-11-04T13:47Z", "compiler", "11-04 09:5x compiler: REPORTED by x (vultr, newest pack, ~09:40). lib/spiral/erlang_process.spi builds (Gleam, exit 0); after 'format --list-sugar' the copy fails with exit 5, PackageErrors 'the module reference graph has a cycle among: benchmark, channel, erlang_process, otp_actor, otp_supervisor, parallel, process, step_runtime'. format wraps the long macro $'list.each(erlang_process_pg_members(...), fn(member) { process.send(...) })' with a continuation, so a line starts 'process.send(', and splits a long string so a segment starts '^^ \"process.Pid) -> ...'. CAUSE (read off spiral_compiler.fs ~29887 codeText): the scanner removes $'...', $\"...\", \"...\" and char literals ONE LINE AT A TIME, so a literal spanning lines keeps its continuation text, and the reference regex (?<![\\w'.!$])([a-z_][\\w']*)(?:\\.|::)[a-z_(] then reads 'process.' as lib's process module. FIX DRAFTED (COMPILER-MODULE-SCAN-MULTILINE\\1-strip-literals-across-lines.kitdo): filter test blocks and comment lines per line as before (replaced by empty lines so the line structure holds), then strip char literals, macros and strings over the WHOLE text, keeping only their newlines, then cut // comments per line. Also host --check: 'Package graph rejected' now appends ' Reasons: <dir>: <message> | ...' from the package's errors_parse/errors_modules/errors_packages (x saw no reason). Proof running on the node (job compiler-modscan): x's repro on both arms plus the derived lib spiral.toml compared between arms (same scanner, so any changed real dependency would show).")
inl note_of_compiler_module_scan_multiline_2 () = Note ("COMPILER-MODULE-SCAN-MULTILINE", 2u32, "2026-11-04T13:52Z", "compiler", "11-04 09:5x compiler: REGRESSION SAMPLE STAGED (held with the draft until installed): sample\\manifest_macro_continuation, a spiral.toml package (prelude empty) with beta.spi holding an UNCALLED function whose macro wraps as the formatter does ($'0 \\ then a line starting 'alpha.never''), so no backend ever sees the text but the scanner reads it; alpha.spi calls beta::value; main returns alpha::first () - 2 = 0. On installed pack34 (box, Fsharp) it fails exactly like x's lib file: 'the module reference graph has a cycle among: alpha, beta, main', exit 5. After the fix it should compile and exit 0. The only per-line literal stripper in the compiler and host is this one (grep for the \\$'[^']*' pattern: 1 hit).")
inl note_of_compiler_module_scan_multiline_3 () = Note ("COMPILER-MODULE-SCAN-MULTILINE", 3u32, "2026-11-04T14:31Z", "compiler", "11-04 10:3x compiler: v1 WAS WRONG, v2 drafted and checked by emulation. v1 (strip literals over the WHOLE file) removed x's false cycle on the node (job compiler-modscan: derived lib spiral.toml identical on both arms, 248E51A0), but then the formatted copy failed type checking: sm'_real.spir lost its 11 real float_native:: refs. Emulating v1's regexes in .NET over sm'_real showed it: one stray quote paired with another many lines later and swallowed real code. The per-line stripping is robust precisely because a stray quote stays on its line. v2 keeps per-line stripping and carries a literal across lines ONLY when it really continues: the raw line ends in a '\\' continuation (what format emits) with an unclosed $' or \", or the line has an odd count of \"\"\" (a triple-quoted block, closed by the next \"\"\"). EMULATION over all 147 lib/spiral .spi/.spir files, old vs v2 cross-module refs: exactly one file differs, convert.spi loses 'base', and that 'base.toInt' is Lean text inside its \"\"\" block (lines 94-112), a false positive the old scanner had; the formatted erlang_process loses exactly the false 'process' ref, and its refs equal the live file's. Two v1 traps found on the way: a \"\"\" block closed at its first inner quote, and $'\"aria-label=!item'\"' (an interpolated name with a prime ends the macro regex early, leaving a stray quote). Node proof v2 queued (job compiler-modscan2): x's repro, derived toml, the staged sample, and the unformatted live erlang_process on both arms.")
inl note_of_compiler_module_scan_multiline_4 () = Note ("COMPILER-MODULE-SCAN-MULTILINE", 4u32, "2026-11-04T14:57Z", "compiler", "11-04 10:5x compiler: v2 PROVEN (node job compiler-modscan2, the git-root copy; control = pack34 bin, candidate = pack34 base + this program). x's repro (copy of lib, format --list-sugar on erlang_process.spi, 1 line now starts 'process.' after a continuation): control Gleam build exit 5 with the cycle and --check exit 5 'Package graph rejected'; candidate Gleam build exit 0 and --check exit 0. Derived lib spiral.toml identical on both arms (248E51A031F200B3). Staged sample manifest_macro_continuation: control exit 5 (cycle), candidate compiles. Live unformatted lib: same result on both arms (no main, as expected for a lib module). With the 147-file emulation (only false refs removed), the program is ready for pack37; the sample and its matrix row land after install.")
inl note_of_compiler_module_scan_multiline_5 () = Note ("COMPILER-MODULE-SCAN-MULTILINE", 5u32, "2026-11-04T23:08Z", "compiler", "11-04 19:1x compiler: DONE. pack37 installed on the box 19:01 (main; core 5F6F6E73 / host 08B00F25), with program 1-strip-literals-across-lines v2. Regression sample landed (unstaged): apps/compiler/tmp/samples/manifest_macro_continuation (alpha/beta modules, a macro continued across lines with a module-shaped word inside the literal, spiral.toml manifest). Box run on installed pack37 (run single-flight-20261104-190651): compiles on F#, C, Rust and Delphi with no false module cycle. The lib format path that x hit is now safe on the box; node and vultr follow after kit's TRAIN26.")
inl xc_census_speed () = Task ("XC-CENSUS-SPEED", "x: xc postable census costs ~26 ms per post (about 100 us per glyph), linear but slow: profile the splitter path on the node and cut it, so a census of kit/eoie (1026 .spi each) takes seconds, not many minutes", Story, P2, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "team:x,x,postable,perf,xc", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_xc_census_speed () = Planning ("XC-CENSUS-SPEED", Minutes 120u32, Load Heavy, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_xc_census_speed_1 () = Note ("XC-CENSUS-SPEED", 1u32, "2026-11-04T14:16Z", "x", "2026-11-04 10:16 x: MEASURED on the box (xc node build 11-04 07:07), best of 2-3 runs. Startup 51 ms. One-cell plain-text files: 75 posts 2.7 s, 152 posts 5.6 s, 302 posts 8.6 s, 604 posts 17.6 s: LINEAR, ~26-36 ms per post (8 posts 259 ms, 19 posts 578 ms). Plain words and 'a.b' words cost the same, so it is NOT the defuse/TLD path (is_tld only runs at a dot). has_units is O(1). Real effect: a 13 MB backlog state file or a 3.5 MB generated UBL binding takes minutes; the 11-04 all-repo census had to be stopped on fiscal and i574n.github. NEXT: profile xc (Rust) on the node with a sampling profiler over a 600-post cell; suspects, unverified: per-unit record updates in chunk/grown copying list fields, chunks_of re-chunking the whole cell when the post-count digits grow (2+ full passes), the per-glyph unit_as/glyphs_on segmentation, nfc. Pass rule: same census rows on x's 77 files (byte-identical output) and at least 10x faster on the 600-post probe.")
inl note_of_xc_census_speed_2 () = Note ("XC-CENSUS-SPEED", 2u32, "2026-11-04T14:18Z", "x", "2026-11-04 10:18 x: CODE PATH read (no profiler yet). census.spi census_of(path, preview) gets preview = preview_of site text, i.e. the xc_preview crate renders the WHOLE dry-run thread as text (every post with its frame header), and census then re-parses that text line by line (counted_line, with_header) to count cells, spills and posts. So a census does the work of a real posting render plus a full re-parse. The splitter itself (cell_posts_by -> posted_chunks -> split_cell_on -> chunks_of) has no visible repeat beyond chunks_of re-chunking once when the post-count digits grow. FIRST PROFILE TARGET: xc_preview's render per post (header rendering, defuse, any round-trip check), then the line re-parse. A cheaper census could ask the splitter for counts directly (cells, posts per cell) without rendering text, if the profile confirms the render dominates.")
inl note_of_xc_census_speed_3 () = Note ("XC-CENSUS-SPEED", 3u32, "2026-11-04T14:32Z", "x", "2026-11-04 10:32 x: NARROWED by reading (no profiler). The census path is census -> xc_preview (preview.spi) -> xc_frames (frames.spi) -> xc_cells, xc_posted, xc_chunks and xc_weights, joined by JSON strings, but parse_json (wire.spi:16) is serde_json::from_str and post_json uses serde_json::json!, both native and fast, so the crate-boundary JSON is NOT the cost. The doubling series is even slightly sublinear (8x the posts took 6.5x the time), which points at a large per-post constant, not growth. Remaining suspects, all inside generated Spiral code: xc_chunks (glyphs_on segmentation, units_on, linked's prefix_text string building per unit, chunk/restarted list ops) and xc_weights (weight_on per post re-weighs the whole post's units). NEXT: perf record on vultr (perf and cargo are there; it was at load 7.9 on 2 CPUs at 10:3x, so wait for it to idle) or a sampling profile on the node, over the 604-post probe.")
inl note_of_xc_census_speed_4 () = Note ("XC-CENSUS-SPEED", 4u32, "2026-11-04T16:07Z", "x", "2026-11-04 12:07 x: PROFILED on vultr (load 2.5-3.2 of 2 CPUs; xc built there from x's committed Rust, 31 crates, cargo -j1, strip off, line tables; perf -F 499 dwarf). SAME 604-post probe: Linux 1.23 s wall (~2 ms/post, 187 MB peak) vs 17.6 s on the Windows box (~29 ms/post). So most of the box cost is the platform, not the algorithm. Self-time profile: malloc 9.96%, free 5.43%, alloc::fmt::format_inner 6.58%, core::fmt::write 6.16%, String::write_str 5.07%, Rc<str> Display 3.12%, Formatter::pad 2.70%, xc_weights::prefix_text_13 4.83%; page faults ~10% inclusive. It is many tiny allocations from string building: '++#' lowers to format!, and prefix_text (starts_link) builds 7/8-glyph prefix strings for EVERY unit to test 'http://'/'https://'. The Windows system heap is known to be far slower on that pattern, which fits the ~14x gap. LEVERS, in order: (1) starts_link compares glyphs in place, no string building (splitter.spi; removes prefix_text's allocations per unit); (2) a faster global allocator for xc on Windows (mimalloc) IF the generated main allows a #[global_allocator] hook; (3) fewer '++#' concatenations in the per-unit and per-post paths (accumulate a list, join once). Pass rule unchanged: byte-identical census rows on x's files; time on the box and on vultr before and after.")
inl note_of_xc_census_speed_5 () = Note ("XC-CENSUS-SPEED", 5u32, "2026-11-04T16:18Z", "x", "2026-11-04 12:18 x: LEVER 1 DRAFTED AND MEASURED. splitter.spi starts_link no longer builds 7/8-glyph prefix strings per unit: led_by walks the units against the literal 'http://' / 'https://' by index and stops at the first mismatch (EC590CC5C413 -> 08703F443F77, kit drafts x\\XC-CENSUS-SPEED; vultr --check 0). Equivalent because grapheme glyphs are non-empty, so 7 glyphs concatenate to 'http://' only when each is the matching single char, and a short list fails both ways. A/B on vultr, same compiler generating weights.rs for both arms, xc rebuilt each way, 604-post probe, 3 runs each (vultr load rose 2 -> 9.7 from other lanes, so noisy): live 3.45 / 3.48 / 2.65 s, draft 1.71 / 1.49 / 1.50 s, about 45% less time. PROOF OF SAME OUTPUT rides x-next7 (now 28 files): splitter's weighting decides post boundaries, so the byte-identical sim fixtures plus e2e RUST-OK catch any change, and the notebook gate now also runs splitter.spi's tests. Levers 2 (Windows allocator) and 3 (fewer '++#' in hot paths) are next after that.")
inl note_of_xc_census_speed_6 () = Note ("XC-CENSUS-SPEED", 6u32, "2026-11-04T16:21Z", "x", "2026-11-04 12:21 x: LEVER 2 FEASIBILITY CHECKED (not drafted). xc.spi has no global hooks today and no repo uses a custom allocator, but Spiral's Rust backend already emits crate-level items through global (lib async.spi: a struct and an impl; common.spi: a static). So the lever is: xc.spi emits a global item '#[global_allocator] static XC_ALLOC: mimalloc::MiMalloc = mimalloc::MiMalloc;' plus mimalloc in apps/client/xc/Cargo.toml (and Cargo.lock), xc binary only. Its value is Windows-specific (the Linux build is already ~14x faster than the box), so its proof needs a Windows build and a timed census of the same 604-post probe before and after, with byte-identical census rows on x's 79 files; that waits for a node slot below full load. It adds an external dependency to x, so it rides a run of its own, not x-next7.")
inl note_of_xc_census_speed_7 () = Note ("XC-CENSUS-SPEED", 7u32, "2026-11-04T16:27Z", "x", "2026-11-04 12:27 x: LEVER 3, first two per-glyph cuts drafted (not yet timed; vultr above load 4): splitter glued now takes the zero-width space as a parameter computed once per text instead of calling zero_width_space () (a fresh char string) for every glyph pair (splitter draft -> 35769D1D552A); glyphs label_char uses one 64-char literal instead of concatenating ascii_letters () with '0123456789_-' on every call (glyphs 14F0E8E2C4B3 -> ABA441C35C7C). Both are exact equivalents (same comparison value, same character set). vultr --check 0 for both. They ride x-next7 (now 29 files; notebook gate runs splitter and glyphs tests; byte-identical fixtures and e2e prove behaviour). Not touched: plain_weight's per-glyph code-point list (a larger change).")
inl note_of_xc_census_speed_8 () = Note ("XC-CENSUS-SPEED", 8u32, "2026-11-04T18:24Z", "x", "2026-11-04 14:24 x: CLEAN A/B on vultr (load 1.2-1.7 at start, 3.0 at end; same compiler generating weights.rs per arm; xc rebuilt per arm; 604-post probe, 3 runs each). live 1.17 / 1.26 / 1.18 s; lever 1 (allocation-free starts_link) 0.68 / 0.70 / 0.61 s, about 45% less; levers 1+3 (plus glued's zero-width space bound once and label_char's single literal) 0.85 / 0.63 / 0.68 s, no measurable gain over lever 1. Census output byte-identical across all three arms (md5 62a4ccd7). DECISION: lever 3 is OUT of x-next7 (no measured benefit, so it would only add risk to a 30-file run); its drafts are kept as splitter.lever13.spi / glyphs.lever3.spi. x-next7 carries lever 1 only (splitter 08703F443F77, land pin restored; glyphs removed from launcher, land and notebook gate). Next lever by size: the Windows allocator (lever 2), whose effect only a Windows build can show.")
inl note_of_xc_census_speed_9 () = Note ("XC-CENSUS-SPEED", 9u32, "2026-11-04T18:25Z", "x", "2026-11-04 14:25 x: RE-PROFILED after lever 1 (vultr, perf -F 999 dwarf, same probe): the profile is now FLAT. Top self-time: kernel asm_sysvec_call_function_single 6.6% (mostly profiling/IPI overhead), free 5.1%, malloc 4.5%, and nothing else at or above 2.5%; format_inner and prefix_text are gone from the top. So no single algorithmic hot spot remains on Linux; what is left is allocation spread across the splitter, which is exactly what a faster allocator would cut on Windows. Lever 2 (mimalloc for xc) is the remaining big lever; it needs a Windows build to measure.")
inl note_of_xc_census_speed_10 () = Note ("XC-CENSUS-SPEED", 10u32, "2026-11-05T00:08Z", "x", "2026-11-04 20:08 x: LEVER 2 (mimalloc) MEASURED ON WINDOWS and DRAFTED. A/B on the box (scratch xalloc: live x client, private CARGO_TARGET_DIR, release builds; arm B = the same xc.rs plus a #[global_allocator] mimalloc line and the mimalloc 0.1.52 dependency). Census of x's 79 client .spi files, output byte-identical across arms (4A47BE2C2CEC). Interleaved A,B x4 under the same box load (about 70-80%): A 20.15 / 24.19 / 18.85 / 19.59 s, B 6.75 / 6.36 / 6.71 / 6.37 s, so about 3x faster (an earlier sequential pass gave A 11.9-14.9, B 4.9-7.9, A2 20.2-24.8 as the load rose). Lever 1 (allocation-free starts_link, in x-next7) is NOT in this A/B; the two stack. DRAFT %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\x\\XC-CENSUS-SPEED\\lever2 (built on live xc.spi 62062A83032B, Cargo.toml EDF6FB2D3815, Cargo.lock 91D9E5FB779D): xc.spi A4CB8CADBC24 (fast_allocator () : () = global '#[global_allocator] static XC_ALLOC: mimalloc::MiMalloc = mimalloc::MiMalloc;', called first in main), Cargo.toml 2929B07345AC (+ mimalloc), Cargo.lock 5739B0DF62EA (+19 lines: mimalloc, libmimalloc-sys only). vultr Rust compile of the draft: exit 0, the global lands at line 4 after the #![allow] header (valid placement), xc.rs 993 lines (gate 1000, tight). Proof route: a node run after x-next7 (both touch the census path), with the cross-platform build gates (Linux and Windows CI); mimalloc builds C via cc on each target.")
inl note_of_xc_census_speed_11 () = Note ("XC-CENSUS-SPEED", 11u32, "2026-11-05T00:09Z", "x", "2026-11-04 20:09 x: progress 30 -> 60. All three levers are measured: lever 1 (45 pct on Linux) is in x-next7; lever 2 (3x on Windows) is drafted and Rust-compiled; lever 3 was dropped (no gain). What's left: both lands through node proofs (x-next7, then a lever-2 run).")
inl note_of_xc_census_speed_12 () = Note ("XC-CENSUS-SPEED", 12u32, "2026-11-05T02:41Z", "x", "2026-11-04 22:41 x: x-next7 RED (job-1793840045834, exit 8, 6287 s, core 21F39A40A549E6E6 at start and end). All other gates PASSED: A/B and fixtures, sim and client builds, e2e, crate line gate (xc 992), postability census 80/80 files with 0 cells over and census equality SAME, sim_progress notebook 2/2, date_time 46/46 (the round-trip-fixed draft). FAILED: splitter.spi notebook 15/16, test a_real_long_form_shape_weighs_its_chars_plus_its_wide_ones (expects 2014 = 7 + 9*201 + 5 + 188 chars + 5 for the wide glyphs). The node log cuts lines at about 223 chars, so the actual value and the failing target are lost. Lever 1 (led_by replacing prefix_text in starts_link) is the only splitter change, and long_form has no link, so a pre-existing failure is possible: the same test is in the LIVE splitter, and x-next6d's notebook gate never ran splitter.spi. A box control (live vs draft, same run_notebook --gate) is running. NOT landed; nothing changed live.")
inl note_of_xc_census_speed_13 () = Note ("XC-CENSUS-SPEED", 13u32, "2026-11-05T02:49Z", "x", "2026-11-04 22:49 x: CONTROL RESULT (box, spiral/apps/cells run_notebook --gate --path, private MIX_BUILD_PATH, no warm/memo): the LIVE splitter.spi (EC590CC5C413) fails the SAME test, 15/16, a_real_long_form_shape_weighs_its_chars_plus_its_wide_ones on the rust target. So x-next7's one red test is PRE-EXISTING in live x and was NOT caused by lever 1; the node ran the lever-1 draft at 15/16 with the identical failure. The box draft arm failed 16/16 with LNK1104 (the linker could not open its output under the deep scratchpad path xprobe10\\\\spiral\\\\target\\\\...), a box path-length artifact, not a draft result. The cell-budget lint flags 'RT @x: ' in long_form as a mention, so how OnX weighs the '@x' mention is the likely suspect. Actual value unknown: run_notebook prints only the test name, not the assertion operands. Next, for whoever picks this up: get the actual weight (one Rust cell run of weight_on OnX (long_form ())), decide whether the test or weight_on is wrong, fix it, then re-run x-next7. x-next7 stays NOT landed under its pre-registered rule (notebook must pass).")
inl note_of_xc_census_speed_14 () = Note ("XC-CENSUS-SPEED", 14u32, "2026-11-05T04:26Z", "x", "2026-11-05 00:26 x: PREPARED the joint proof run for both levers (scratchpad launch-x-levers1.ps1, from x-next8's template): overlays splitter.spi 08703F443F77 (lever 1), lever2 xc.spi A4CB8CADBC24, xc Cargo.toml 2929B07345AC and Cargo.lock 5739B0DF62EA on bases EC590CC5C413 / 62062A83032B / EDF6FB2D3815 / 91D9E5FB779D. Gates: sim accept and build, client build (regenerates xc.rs from xc.spi and links mimalloc), e2e, postable census equality, notebooks splitter.spi and xc.spi. Outputs are saved BEFORE the notebook gate. Launch condition: x-next7b lands (it shares the node mirror and base files) AND the pre-existing splitter test a_real_long_form... is fixed in the draft (a probe on the box is measuring the actual weight).")
inl note_of_xc_census_speed_15 () = Note ("XC-CENSUS-SPEED", 15u32, "2026-11-05T05:03Z", "x", "2026-11-05 01:03 x: ROOT CAUSE of the pre-existing splitter red (x-next7, and the live control): NOT a weighting bug. The test a_real_long_form_shape_weighs_its_chars_plus_its_wide_ones never COMPILED on rust: long_form () used sm'::replicate 200i32 / 188i32 with literal counts, which Spiral unrolls into nested format! calls until rustc hits 'recursion limit reached while expanding format!' (memory spiral-constant-replicate-unrolls). A five-test probe on the box (short path C:\\\\home\\\\xs, so link.exe works) showed it: piece probes with literal counts 200 and 188 failed with that rustc error, while the prefix (7) and wide 5x (10) probes passed. FIX in the lever-1 draft: long_form is built from tenfold/ninefold concatenations (draft splitter 08703F443F77 -> D18ACB85C0C1), producing a byte-identical 2009-char string (checked), so the expected 2014 is unchanged. Re-running the probe with the fix (box). The levers run (launch-x-levers1) carries this splitter, which also heals the live test.")
inl note_of_xc_census_speed_16 () = Note ("XC-CENSUS-SPEED", 16u32, "2026-11-05T05:08Z", "x", "2026-11-05 01:08 x: FIX CONFIRMED on the box: with long_form built from tenfold/ninefold (draft splitter D18ACB85C0C1), the probe file's notebook passes 3/3 (a_real_long_form_shape_weighs_its_chars_plus_its_wide_ones = 2014, plus the prefix and wide probes), with no failed targets. So the levers run is unblocked once x-next7b lands. The draft is copied by path into launch-x-levers1 (no stale pin). A land script for the levers still has to be written (land-x-levers1, same pattern as land-x-next7).")
inl note_of_xc_census_speed_17 () = Note ("XC-CENSUS-SPEED", 17u32, "2026-11-05T11:47Z", "x", "2026-11-05 07:5x x: x-levers1 (job-1793873376874, node, pack44) RED at the client build: the cell-budget gate. lever2's xc.spi main gained the line '    fast_allocator ()', which took main from weight about 251 to 273 > 271 (xc.spi:446). Sim accept and sim build were green; no code failure. FIX: the helper is renamed to allocator (5 characters fewer at the call site); draft lever2\\xc.spi 7773255AE07D (was A4CB8CADBC24), and mix spiral.cell_budget --check on the draft reports 0 findings. land-x-levers1 is repinned (parse-checked). RELAUNCHED as x-levers1b (job-1793878419537, node, core 657DA4B173F9D3D3 read in the same command; node load 67%, 15 GB disk, 10 GB RAM free). The x-levers1 out dir is deleted on the node. PENDING.")
inl note_of_xc_census_speed_18 () = Note ("XC-CENSUS-SPEED", 18u32, "2026-11-05T12:53Z", "x", "2026-11-05 09:2x x: LEVERS 1+2 LANDED (green run x-levers1b, job-1793878419537, node, pack44 core 657DA4B173F9D3D3 at start and end): splitter.spi D18ACB85C0C1 (long_form built from ++# pieces, not a constant-count sm'::replicate, so no rustc recursion), xc.spi 7773255AE07D (mimalloc global allocator via allocator ()), xc Cargo.toml 2929B07345AC, Cargo.lock 5739B0DF62EA. node-proof applied 69 generated outputs, 0 conflicts, 61 unchanged. Run: sim accept 0 (0 fixtures changed), sim build 0, client build 0 (cell budget green after the allocator rename), e2e RUST-OK 37 tutorial steps + 5 extra cases (138 requests), notebooks 16/16 + 2/2, census SAME over 80 files (0 cells over one post). The node out dir is deleted. PENDING: the measured speedup. The run printed no timings, so lever 1+2's xc is building on vultr (/root/xc-prof2, systemd scope MemoryMax 2800M, deps reused from xc-prof's target) for an A/B census against lever-1 xc 5d8d0ac1621c over the same files.")
inl note_of_xc_census_speed_19 () = Note ("XC-CENSUS-SPEED", 19u32, "2026-11-05T13:15Z", "x", "2026-11-05 09:4x x: LEVER 2 MEASURED (vultr, idle, load 0.06 before and 0.72 after; same 119 lib .spi files from ~/cell-budget/lib/spiral, one xc call over all paths). A = lever-1 xc 5d8d0ac1621c, B = levers 1+2 xc 90ec0cbe4509, built from the landed box tree (release, 1 min 11 s, deps reused). Outputs byte-identical (cmp): total files 119, 47 fully postable, cells 5698, over one post 1276, posts 8517. Wall time, interleaved ABABAB: A 3833 / 3907 / 3838 ms, B 2735 / 2981 / 2981 ms. Medians A 3838, B 2981: -22% (the A/A spread is 74 ms for A and 246 ms for B, so the effect is well outside the noise). Lever 2 (mimalloc) is worth about 860 ms on this set: 25 ms per file instead of 32 ms (7.2 ms saved per file). Stage /root/xc-prof2 is kept as the new baseline for lever 3 (glyphs.lever3.spi draft) and removed when this item closes.")
inl note_of_xc_census_speed_20 () = Note ("XC-CENSUS-SPEED", 20u32, "2026-11-05T13:23Z", "x", "2026-11-05 10:0x x: DoD MEASURE, the kit tree on vultr (Linux, idle, load 0.11 -> 0.76). 1161 .spi from kit (git ls-files tracked + untracked, target/e2e/generated excluded), one xc call over all paths, interleaved ABAB. Lever 1 xc 5d8d0ac1621c: 17872 / 17713 ms. Levers 1+2 xc 90ec0cbe4509: 12641 / 12882 ms (-28%). Outputs identical: 1161 files, 321 fully postable, cells 10578, 3842 over one post, 28923 posts. So a kit census now takes about 13 s instead of many minutes; the x-census1 baseline was 914 s on the node, Windows, pre-lever xc, a loaded node. CORRECTION to note 19: lever 3 is not pending; it was measured and dropped on 11-04 (no gain over lever 1). /root/xc-prof2 stays only as this item's A/B stage. LEFT for Done: the same census on Windows with the landed xc, where lever 2 (the allocator) was measured at about 3x, on the node or box once a slot frees (both at 100% CPU at 09:4x). Then delete /root/xc-prof and /root/xc-prof2 on vultr. Progress 60 -> 90.")
inl note_of_xc_census_speed_21 () = Note ("XC-CENSUS-SPEED", 21u32, "2026-11-05T14:02Z", "x", "2026-11-05 10:1x x: WINDOWS MEASURE (box). The box xc is rebuilt from the landed tree (levers 1+2): 53FD36DDB90F (was 81B4B56F04F8 from 10-25, which had no postable command). cargo release -p xc at BelowNormal priority, 970 s under load. KIT CENSUS: 1158 .spi (same selection as vultr's; 3 fewer files because kit's tree moved between the two snapshots), in batches of 250 paths, because one call overflows the Windows command line ('filename or extension is too long'), as the 11-04 note warned. Run 1: 71994 ms; run 2: 84452 ms; identical totals: 320 fully postable, cells 10543, 3830 over one post, posts 28856. CONTENDED: box CPU was at 100% before and after, from other lanes, so these are upper bounds. Against the x-census1 baseline (914 s on the node, pre-lever xc), that is 11-13x faster even under full load, a minute instead of 15. Linux idle: 12.7 s. DoD reading: 'seconds, not many minutes' holds on Linux and nearly on Windows under contention. One idle-box run would give the clean Windows figure; that is all that is left. Progress 90 -> 95.")
inl note_of_xc_census_speed_22 () = Note ("XC-CENSUS-SPEED", 22u32, "2026-11-06T00:09Z", "x", "2026-11-05 20:1x x: WINDOWS RE-MEASURE on a quieter box (load 39% before, 55% after; the afternoon run was at 100%). Same box xc 53FD36DDB90F (levers 1+2), same selection (kit .spi, tracked + untracked, target/e2e/generated excluded; now 1180 files as kit's tree grew), batches of 250 paths: run 1 43390 ms, run 2 49606 ms; totals identical between runs (29056 posts, 3860 over one post). Summary for the DoD ('seconds, not many minutes'): node baseline 914 s (pre-lever, loaded) -> Windows 43-50 s at moderate load (72-84 s at full load) -> Linux idle 12.7 s. Still not a fully idle Windows figure (the box always carries other lanes), but the order of magnitude is settled: a kit census is under a minute on Windows and about 13 s on Linux. Left: close once someone gets an idle-box reading, or accept this as Done.")
inl note_of_xc_census_speed_23 () = Note ("XC-CENSUS-SPEED", 23u32, "2026-11-06T00:48Z", "x", "2026-11-05 20:4x x: NEAR-IDLE WINDOWS MEASURE (box load 28% before, 36% after; the quietest window of the day). Box xc 53FD36DDB90F, kit 1180 .spi in batches of 250: run 1 38682 ms, run 2 32343 ms; totals identical (29056 posts). Final picture: 914 s (node, pre-lever) -> about 32-39 s on Windows (near idle) -> 12.7 s on Linux (idle). The DoD ('seconds, not many minutes') is met on both platforms; the remaining Windows/Linux gap is process and IO overhead per batch, not the splitter. DONE. Cleanup: vultr /root/xc-prof and /root/xc-prof2 are this item's A/B stages and can go.")
inl granular_cellbudget_rules () = Task ("GRANULAR-CELLBUDGET-RULES", "CellBudget.over_budget applies the 11-04 GRANULAR rules: (1) a definition whose body is one closed-union match with one-line arms is measured per arm, (2) the measure starts after the header's `=`", TaskKind, P0, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "kit,team:kit,granular,cells,metrics", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_granular_cellbudget_rules () = Planning ("GRANULAR-CELLBUDGET-RULES", Minutes 120u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_granular_cellbudget_rules_1 () = Note ("GRANULAR-CELLBUDGET-RULES", 1u32, "2026-11-04T15:06Z", "kit", "11-04 13:1x kit, from fiscal's question (GRANULAR-STATS n34-35): the counter that the GRANULAR items' DoD uses is spiral apps/cells/lib/spiral/cells/cell_budget.ex (over_budget/2, mix spiral.cell_budget), and it implements neither rule from GRANULAR-STATS' 11-04 08:2x ruling. Raw it reports xpath_eval 63/235 over and the xsd_emit draft 177/432, mostly signature lines and table-shaped dispatches. DoD: over_budget skips the header (forall binders, parameter list, prototype-constraint record) and measures a single-match flat dispatch by its longest arm plus the match line, as long as every arm is one line <=120 chars with no nested match, `?`, `:=` or lambda; ExUnit tests for both rules plus one counter-example each (a multi-line arm counts in full; a long body still counts); then re-measure xpath_eval and xsd_emit. Cross-repo: the edit lands in spiral apps/cells, so x (cell budget user) and compiler review it. NEXT: read cell_budget.ex's block splitter. TYPEFEATURES: a measured_block union (WholeBody chars | PerArm (arm chars, header chars)) in the Elixir model, not a boolean.")
inl note_of_granular_cellbudget_rules_2 () = Note ("GRANULAR-CELLBUDGET-RULES", 2u32, "2026-11-04T15:10Z", "kit", "11-04 13:2x kit: LANDED in the spiral tree (unstaged): fiscal's draft as apps/cells/lib/spiral/cells/cell_budget_ruled.ex (Spiral.Cells.CellBudgetRuled: rule (2) drops the header up to its `=`, one-line defs count what follows ` = `; rule (1) a `?= .. ?|` body whose arms are one-liners <=120 chars with no `?= `, ` ? `, `?> `, `:= `, lambda or `fun ` weighs match line + longest arm; test blocks unruled), plus a hint on its findings; `mix spiral.cell_budget --granular <paths>` uses it and skips the renderer rewrites, which are a postability concern. CellBudget.over_budget itself is UNCHANGED, so x's postability check keeps real post weights. New ExUnit test/spiral/cell_budget_ruled_test.exs: 5 passed (both rules, a counter-example for rule 1, a test block unruled), private MIX_BUILD_PATH. Live lib xpath_eval.spi: raw over_budget 75 (tests included) -> --granular 33. GRANULAR items measure with --granular from now on.")
inl note_of_granular_cellbudget_rules_3 () = Note ("GRANULAR-CELLBUDGET-RULES", 3u32, "2026-11-04T15:26Z", "kit", "11-04 13:3x kit: no regression: the existing test/spiral/cells/cell_budget_test.exs plus the new ruled tests, together: Result: 10 passed (box, private MIX_BUILD_PATH). Left for Done: fiscal's xsd_emit re-measure with --granular, and a look from x/compiler at the unstaged spiral apps/cells change (new module + a --granular flag; over_budget untouched).")
inl note_of_granular_cellbudget_rules_4 () = Note ("GRANULAR-CELLBUDGET-RULES", 4u32, "2026-11-04T19:36Z", "kit", "11-04 20:0x kit: the 20:1x DATA + TEMPLATE ruling (GRANULAR-STATS n37) is IMPLEMENTED in CellBudgetRuled (unstaged, spiral apps/cells): a type declaration (:|, :==, :!, type, union, nominal) weighs 0; a def whose body is one record/list/tuple literal (strings blanked, no ?= / ? / ?> / := / lambda / fun / pipes, and no lowercase word applied to an argument; field labels and Constructor applications allowed) weighs 0; a body made only of string literals joined by ++# / ^^ (and parens) weighs its heaviest literal piece; anything with interpolated logic is measured whole. Tests: 4 new (type decl + constant record exempt; a call inside a literal is measured; a literal template counts its max piece; an interpolated template is measured whole) + the 10 before = Result: 14 passed (box, private MIX_BUILD_PATH). fiscal's earlier remeasure on vultr matched (xsd_emit split 47/490 code-only), so with this the item's DoD is code-complete; left: a look from x/compiler at the unstaged apps/cells change.")
inl note_of_granular_cellbudget_rules_5 () = Note ("GRANULAR-CELLBUDGET-RULES", 5u32, "2026-11-04T22:46Z", "kit", "11-04 18:5x kit: the xsd_emit RE-MEASURE is done (spiral lib/spiral/xsd_emit.spi, 3749 lines, which fiscal reaches through deps/spiral): plain 175 over_budget, --granular 118 (-57, 33%), both with the same 20 autolink rewrites. That last part was a BUG in my --granular flag (fixed, unstaged, spiral apps/cells lib/mix/tasks/spiral.cell_budget.ex): the flag swapped in CellBudgetRuled for BOTH the rewrites and the measure, so granular reports silently dropped autolink findings (first run: 118 findings, no autolink); now granular = CellBudget.rewrites ++ CellBudgetRuled.over_budget. Tests: cell_budget_ruled_test + cell_budget_test Result: 14 passed (box, private MIX_BUILD_PATH). What still weighs most in xsd_emit: the tail TEST bodies (3580: 1723, 3605: 1561, ...), which build XSD fixtures from ++# string pieces plus calls, so neither the data nor the template exemption applies; that is a fixture-split job for xsd_emit's owner, not a rule change. LEFT for Done: a look from x/compiler at the unstaged apps/cells change.")
inl note_of_granular_cellbudget_rules_6 () = Note ("GRANULAR-CELLBUDGET-RULES", 6u32, "2026-11-04T22:49Z", "compiler", "11-04 18:5x compiler REVIEW of kit's unstaged apps/cells change (lib/spiral/cells/cell_budget_ruled.ex, test/spiral/cell_budget_ruled_test.exs, --granular in mix spiral.cell_budget; read-only, no run). NO BLOCKING OBJECTION: opt-in flag, CellBudget.over_budget untouched, :# test blocks stay unruled, flat_dispatch rejects arms with bindings, lambdas or matches and arms over 120 chars, wrapped headers whose = is not alone on a line fall back to the unruled weight (safe). Three findings: (1) FALSE EXEMPTION in data_literal?: the call detector needs an identifier followed by WHITESPACE, so a paren-adjacent application such as { total = sum_of(lines) } (valid Spiral) counts as constant data; suggest also rejecting an identifier directly followed by an open paren (regex (?<![\\w.'\"])[a-z_][\\w']*\\( ) and adding that counter-example next to the 'sum_of lines' test. (2) RULES SKIPPED FOR DOCUMENTED DEFINITIONS: top_level_blocks keeps a /// doc line glued to the := under it (blocks split only at a blank line followed by column 0), and split_header returns :error when the first line starts with ///, so every documented definition (common in lib, e.g. reflection.spi '/// ### case_name' then ':= case_name') is measured unruled. Safe (stricter) but it under-applies the rules; suggest dropping leading /// lines before split_header, with a test. (3) templates: a literal-only body counts only its heaviest piece, so a long template is effectively exempt regardless of length; fine under an opt-in flag, but worth stating in the hint or the rule text.")
inl note_of_granular_cellbudget_rules_7 () = Note ("GRANULAR-CELLBUDGET-RULES", 7u32, "2026-11-05T00:05Z", "kit", "11-04 20:1x kit: compiler's 18:5x review APPLIED (unstaged, spiral apps/cells): (1) false exemption closed: data_literal? also rejects an identifier directly followed by \"(\" (regex (?<![\\w.'\"])[a-z_][\\w']*\\( as suggested), test \"a paren-adjacent call is measured too\" ({ total = sum_of(lines) }); (2) documented definitions now get the rules: ruled_weight drops leading /// doc lines (not ///> test markers) before the type-head check and split_header, tests \"a documented definition gets the rules, its doc lines are not measured\" (one-line def + a documented type decl = 0) and \"a test marker line before a test block keeps it unruled\"; (3) the hint now states the template rule (\"a literal-only template counts its heaviest piece\"). Result: 17 passed (box, private MIX_BUILD_PATH). Re-measure: xsd_emit unchanged at 118 over_budget (its documented defs were not the heavy ones); lib reflection.spi (the review's example) 15 plain -> 13 granular. With the review's non-blocking findings handled and the measure done, this is complete pending the spiral apps/cells change landing.")
inl note_of_granular_cellbudget_rules_8 () = Note ("GRANULAR-CELLBUDGET-RULES", 8u32, "2026-11-05T00:35Z", "kit", "11-04 20:3x kit: strict compile caught one of mine: `mix compile --force --warnings-as-errors` (spiral apps/cells, private MIX_BUILD_PATH) refused \"this clause of defp split_header/1 is never used\", because my 20:1x lines == [] guard made split_header([]) provably unreachable (String.split never returns []). Removed both the dead guard and the dead clause; now compile exit 0 with warnings as errors, and the tests still give Result: 17 passed.")
inl note_of_granular_cellbudget_rules_9 () = Note ("GRANULAR-CELLBUDGET-RULES", 9u32, "2026-11-05T02:19Z", "fiscal", "2026-11-04 22:19 fiscal (star exchange 11-04 ask): vultr's mirror /home/remote/git/spiral/apps/cells still has cell_budget_ruled.ex D4E2BEFB (pre-ruling); the box has 79B1FC32 (unstaged). Lanes that measure on vultr (the box measure reads lib live, so vultr is where fiscal measures) get pre-ruling numbers until it syncs; I loaded the box file with 'mix run -r /tmp/cbr-new.ex' for my 11-04 figures (FISCAL-GRANULAR-280 n26). Ask: sync it to vultr once landed, or say when the next spiral mirror sync carries it.")
inl note_of_granular_cellbudget_rules_10 () = Note ("GRANULAR-CELLBUDGET-RULES", 10u32, "2026-11-05T02:20Z", "kit", "11-04 22:2x kit: fiscal's star-exchange ask 5 (sync the ruling-aware cell counter to vultr) recorded. The counter is spiral apps/cells (Elixir: CellBudgetRuled + `mix spiral.cell_budget --granular`), unstaged on the box. NEXT: once the apps/cells change lands, sync spiral to vultr's mirror and run `mix spiral.cell_budget --granular <file>` there (check vultr has Elixir/mix first); until then fiscal measures on the box.")
inl note_of_granular_cellbudget_rules_11 () = Note ("GRANULAR-CELLBUDGET-RULES", 11u32, "2026-11-05T06:52Z", "fiscal", "fiscal finding for the data rule (11-05 02:5x local; asked as a note, not a message): data_literal? rejects every lowercase identifier followed by an argument, so a record literal built from :! NOMINAL constructors (module_id \"generated_x\", tag_name \"Invoice\", namespace_uri \"urn:...\") is measured as code, while one built from union constructors (Some, Xsd, Signed) is data. In fiscal families.spi that keeps about 14 pure family_root literals over budget (24/49 overages; e.g. chile_dte_v10 = only constants plus nominal and union constructor applications). Ask: can the ruled counter treat names declared by \":! name = ...\" in the file, or in its imports, as constructors? Otherwise the only way to make such literals \"data\" is to strip the nominal types out of the rows, which trades type safety for the metric.")
inl note_of_granular_cellbudget_rules_12 () = Note ("GRANULAR-CELLBUDGET-RULES", 12u32, "2026-11-05T07:52Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-05 04:0x kit: fiscal's nominal-constructor ask BUILT (unstaged, spiral apps/cells: cell_budget_ruled.ex + mix spiral.cell_budget). data_literal? strips applications of declared nominals before its call check. declared_nominals reads every \":! name ...\" line, parameterized ones too (\":! definition_name form = string\"). With --granular, the task passes the file's own nominals plus those of each \":< module\" import, found beside the file or in lib/spiral (SPIRAL_LIB overrides). Undeclared calls stay code, so the existing counter-examples hold. Tests: cell_budget_ruled_test 14/14 (new: nominal constructors build data, a missing nominal keeps it code, declared_nominals incl. parameterized). compile --warnings-as-errors 0. FISCAL MEASURED: families.spi still has 25 overages with and without the change. All 15 nominals are found (module_id, tag_name, namespace_uri, definition_name, ...), but 24 of the literals also call fiscal_paths::under \"deps/corpus/...\", a real function. Proof: chile_dte_v10 weighs 431, and 0 once only that call is removed. So the rule now does what fiscal asked; the remaining blocker is the under call, which is fiscal's to reshape (e.g. store the corpus-relative path as a nominal and apply under at the use site).")
inl note_of_granular_cellbudget_rules_13 () = Note ("GRANULAR-CELLBUDGET-RULES", 13u32, "2026-11-05T11:06Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-05 07:1x kit: fiscal's ask 5 DONE on vultr: the ruled counter runs there as ~/cell-budget/cell-budget --granular <file.spi> [more files] (a sh wrapper running plain elixir with the four self-contained modules required: cell_budget.ex, cell_budget_ruled.ex, manifest.ex, the spiral.cell_budget mix task; no mix project, Gleam or dotnet needed). The box working tree was shipped on 11-05 07:0x, including the nominal-constructor rule, plus apps/cells/data/tlds.txt and the 266 lib/spiral .spi files (SPIRAL_LIB defaults to ~/cell-budget/lib/spiral, so imported nominals resolve). Checked: a tibia file 0 findings in 0.9 s, and an edition-3 package path runs. Refresh = re-ship those files when apps/cells changes. Same invocation works on the box (~22 s, compile included).")
inl note_of_granular_cellbudget_rules_14 () = Note ("GRANULAR-CELLBUDGET-RULES", 14u32, "2026-11-05T13:17Z", "x", "2026-11-05 09:5x x REVIEW (Cells lead; read-only, no run) of the unstaged apps/cells change: lib/spiral/cells/cell_budget_ruled.ex, the --granular path in lib/mix/tasks/spiral.cell_budget.ex, test/spiral/cell_budget_ruled_test.exs. VERDICT: --granular belongs in Cells' mix task as is: it is opt-in, rewrites still come from CellBudget, and CellBudget.over_budget is untouched, so x's postability gate (plain --check in x/scripts/common.ps1 Invoke-CellBudget) keeps real post weights. No blocking objection. FOUR NON-BLOCKING CHANGES: (1) nominals_in_scope resolves the lib as Path.expand(\\\"../../lib/spiral\\\", File.cwd!()), which is right only when mix runs from apps/cells. Anchor it on the task file, Path.expand(\\\"../../../../../lib/spiral\\\", __DIR__), or on Mix.Project.project_file(), so an umbrella or another cwd cannot silently find no imports (missing nominals make literals count as code: stricter, so safe, but it would change counts by cwd). (2) The test file sits at test/spiral/cell_budget_ruled_test.exs while its sibling is test/spiral/cells/cell_budget_test.exs; move it next to the sibling (standardize-with-siblings). (3) Rule 1 (flat_dispatch) only recognises a '?= .. ?|' match. A body that is one backend_switch { .. } with one-line arms is the same table shape and is the lib's largest unhealable class: 125 of 233 lib switches have 5+ arms, and in date_time 26 of 57 --granular overages start with a switch. Filed as BACKEND-SWITCH-POSTABLE (team kit, 11-05): the same predicate, with the switch line as match_line and arms matching '<Backend> = \\\\ () => ..' on one line <=120 chars; the arm's own lambda must be allowed (today '\\\\ ' in an arm rejects it). Tests: a flat switch passes, and a switch with one multi-line arm counts in full. (4) The @hint names three rules but not the newer two (type declarations and pure literals weigh 0, declared nominals count as constructors); state them so a finding explains its number. For the close: kit can treat (1), (2) and (4) as polish, and (3) as its own item.")
inl note_of_granular_cellbudget_rules_15 () = Note ("GRANULAR-CELLBUDGET-RULES", 15u32, "2026-11-05T13:30Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-05 09:3x kit: x's review (note 14): keep --granular as is, nothing blocks. Points: (1) nominals_in_scope anchors lib on __DIR__ ('../../../../../lib/spiral') instead of File.cwd! and (4) the @hint names the newer rules (type declarations and pure literals weigh 0; declared :! nominals count as constructors): both QUEUED as one cells_land_window run (box job-1793885385463, after the running notebook batch, tests cell_budget_ruled_test + cell_budget_test). (2) moving cell_budget_ruled_test.exs into test/spiral/cells/ is deferred: cells_land_window cannot move files (it timestamps every listed file after apply), so it goes with x's next Cells pass or a window that supports moves. (3) backend_switch with one-line arms = BACKEND-SWITCH-POSTABLE (x filed it for kit). Done when the land closes green.")
inl note_of_granular_cellbudget_rules_16 () = Note ("GRANULAR-CELLBUDGET-RULES", 16u32, "2026-11-05T13:33Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-05 09:3x kit: DONE. x's review points 1 + 4 LANDED via cells_land_window, closed green (19 tests: cell_budget_ruled_test + cell_budget_test; spiral.cell_budget.ex 22B8011608CB, cell_budget_ruled.ex D1328CBB031C; undo spiral-bin\\lib-land-undo\\cells-20261105-093057): lib lookup anchored on __DIR__, @hint names the type-declaration/pure-literal and nominal rules. Point 2 (test file move) is left for a window that supports moves; point 3 is BACKEND-SWITCH-POSTABLE. The vultr counter (~/cell-budget) predates points 1/4; refresh it with the next ship.")
inl cic_literals () = Task ("CIC-LITERALS", "kernel literals: Nat literals as a term former (whnf to Nat.succ on demand) with Lean's accelerated Nat ops (add/sub/mul/div/mod/beq/ble/land/lor/xor/shiftLeft/shiftRight/pow/gcd/log2) and String literals (String.mk of a List Char), oracle-checked against Lean; first blocker for replaying Init/Prelude (CIC-LEAN-EXPORT-REPLAY)", Story, P1, D5, 100u32, Done, "", "lean,kernel,typefeatureset,team:main,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_cic_literals () = Planning ("CIC-LITERALS", Minutes 300u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_cic_literals_1 () = Note ("CIC-LITERALS", 1u32, "2026-11-04T15:45Z", "main", "11-04 11:4x main FILED from CIC-LEAN-EXPORT-REPLAY n2. DOD: term former NatLit (u64 first, big ints later) typed as the prelude Nat; whnf turns NatLit (n+1) into Nat.succ (NatLit n) only when a recursor or match needs a constructor; the listed ops reduce on two literals to a literal; String literal unfolds to String.mk of a char list on demand; oracle entries for each op (Lean accepts by decide/rfl) and a negative (2 + 2 = 5 refused by both). TYPEFEATURESET: literal as its own term case, never a Const with a numeric name.")
inl note_of_cic_literals_2 () = Note ("CIC-LITERALS", 2u32, "2026-11-04T16:03Z", "main", "11-04 12:0x main STARTED in a scratch copy (%TEMP%\\main-kernel-literals; live tree untouched while the box oracle reads it). Done there, package --check clean on vultr: syntax NatLit : u64 (text/hash/same_term arms); infer types it as Const Nat; reduce literal_ctor unfolds NatLit for a Nat.rec major using the recursor's own rule names (zero/succ or Nat.zero/Nat.succ); conversion literal_side unfolds one side when only one is a literal; literal_op accelerates Nat.add/sub/mul/div/mod on two literals with u64 overflow falling back to unfolding; Lean printer prints (k : @_root_.Nat). Oracle: new Builtin entry (admitted, printed as a comment) for Nat.add/Nat.mul, Prelude Nat, 9 literal probes incl. negatives 2*3=5 and 2=3. Kernel test a_nat_literal_is_typed_reduced_and_converted_like_its_successor_chain. Scratch oracle run on vultr + Lean on box PENDING. NOT yet: beq/ble (need prelude Bool), land/lor/xor/shift/pow/gcd/log2, String literals, bignums.")
inl note_of_cic_literals_3 () = Note ("CIC-LITERALS", 3u32, "2026-11-04T16:26Z", "main", "11-04 12:3x main ROUND 1 GREEN in the live tree: box Lean oracle 269/269 agree (pins green) and vultr kernel gate KERNEL-OK 67/67 (incl. a_nat_literal_is_typed_reduced_and_converted_like_its_successor_chain). ROUND 2 moved in (reduce/oracle): Nat.gcd/pow/beq/ble/land/lor/xor/shiftLeft/shiftRight accelerated (beq/ble return Bool.true/false; pow and shiftLeft fall back on u64 overflow), Prelude Bool, Builtin axioms for the ops, 7 more probes incl. negatives ble 4 3 = true and xor 5 3 = 7; box oracle PENDING. Left: Nat.log2 (unary), String literals, bignums.")
inl note_of_cic_literals_4 () = Note ("CIC-LITERALS", 4u32, "2026-11-04T16:57Z", "main", "11-04 13:0x main ROUNDS 2+3 AGREE WITH LEAN: box oracle 314/314 (rounds 1-3: Nat literals, add/sub/mul/div/mod, gcd/pow/beq/ble/land/lor/xor/shiftLeft/shiftRight, log2 via a unary path, Prelude Bool, Builtin axioms). The pinned verdict list was wrong (pins appended in authoring order, not corpus order); since Lean independently agrees on all 314, the kernel's 113 actual verdicts became the pins. Pin confirmation on the box and the vultr kernel gate PENDING. CELLS NOTE: the oracle cell takes ~280 s on a loaded box against a 300 s default; ///- --timeout placed before or after ///> rust on a :# test did NOT change timeout_ms (stayed 300000), and after ///> it even dropped the rust target to zig. Per-test timeouts in a .spi package file seem unsupported; the two earlier \"NO LEAN FILE\" runs were this timeout.")
inl note_of_cic_literals_5 () = Note ("CIC-LITERALS", 5u32, "2026-11-04T17:04Z", "main", "11-04 13:1x main PINS CONFIRMED on the box: cells 1/1 and Lean 314/314 with rounds 1-3 (cell took 307 s against the 300 s default, see CELLS-TEST-TIMEOUT-DIRECTIVE). Vultr kernel gate on the pre-pin-fix sync: 66/67, the one red being exactly that stale pin list; every other kernel test green with all Nat ops. STRING LITERALS now in the live tree (package --check clean): StrLit : string typed String; conversion unfolds a string literal against a non-literal to String.ofList of List.cons.{0} Char (Char.ofNat code) ... List.nil.{0} Char, which is Lean 4.35's expansion (String is ByteArray-based there; String.mk is a deprecated alias of String.ofList); Lean printer escapes \\ and \". Oracle: Builtin Char/Char.ofNat/String/String.ofList, Prelude List.{u}, probes \"ab\" : String, \"ab\" = String.ofList [a,b] (+), \"ab\" = \"ba\" (-), \"ab\" = String.ofList [a,c] (-), \"\" = String.ofList [] (+). Box oracle PENDING. Not modelled: a string literal as a recursor/projection major (needs String.ofByteArray), bignum Nat literals.")
inl note_of_cic_literals_6 () = Note ("CIC-LITERALS", 6u32, "2026-11-04T17:10Z", "main", "11-04 13:1x main BOX ORACLE 339/339 agree with String literals (cells 1/1, pins green, 262 s). All of the n1 DoD is covered: NatLit u64 typed as prelude Nat, lazy unfolding to the successor form for recursors and conversion, the accelerated ops on two literals (beq/ble to Bool), string literals unfolding to Lean 4.35's String.ofList form, oracle entries for each op with negatives (2*3=5, 2=3, ble 4 3 = true, xor 5 3 = 7, \"ab\"=\"ba\", \"ab\"=ofList [a,c]). Vultr kernel gate on the same tree PENDING; Done when it is green. Follow-ups outside this DoD: bignum literals, string literal as a recursor/projection major.")
inl note_of_cic_literals_7 () = Note ("CIC-LITERALS", 7u32, "2026-11-04T17:16Z", "main", "11-04 13:2x main DONE: vultr kernel gate KERNEL-OK 67/67 on the same tree as the box oracle 339/339 (spiral 98e51d32 + unstaged, sync digest 79907BB7). Nat literals with lazy unfolding and every Lean-accelerated op, Bool results, String literals in Lean 4.35's String.ofList form, all oracle-checked with negatives. FOLLOW-UP for the export replay: Init.Prelude contains natVal 2^64 (UInt64.size), so bignum literals are the next literal step (filed under CIC-LEAN-EXPORT-REPLAY n4).")
inl granular_split_tests_xpath_xsd () = Task ("GRANULAR-SPLIT-TESTS-XPATH-XSD", "Split multi-assert test blocks in xpath_eval and xsd_emit into one test per behavior", Story, P3, D2, 100u32, Done, "", "team:fiscal,fiscal,granular,tests,xsd,xpath", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_granular_split_tests_xpath_xsd () = Planning ("GRANULAR-SPLIT-TESTS-XPATH-XSD", Minutes 120u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_granular_split_tests_xpath_xsd_1 () = Note ("GRANULAR-SPLIT-TESTS-XPATH-XSD", 1u32, "2026-11-04T15:55Z", "fiscal", "2026-11-04 11:55 fiscal: FILED per kit's ruling (GRANULAR counts code blocks only; this is the follow-up, not a gate). Split the multi-assert test blocks of lib xpath_eval (12 over budget, weights 1646-6837, e.g. evaluates_en16931_style_rules) and xsd_emit (33 over in the split draft) into one test per behavior, so a failing behavior names itself. Constraints: xpath_eval is live lib -> drafts store + vultr proof (tests converted? no, lib tests are ':#' already) + land window; xsd_emit after the decode + split lands. Measure: kit's CellBudgetRuled with tests INCLUDED should approach the code-only numbers (xpath_eval 8.94% code-only vs 13.4% with tests; xsd_emit split 9.59% vs 15.3%). Each split test keeps its assertions byte-identical; proof = same pass set in Rust plus a mutant per new test file.")
inl note_of_granular_split_tests_xpath_xsd_2 () = Note ("GRANULAR-SPLIT-TESTS-XPATH-XSD", 2u32, "2026-11-04T16:01Z", "fiscal", "2026-11-04 12:01 fiscal: STARTED (claimed). xpath_eval draft (drafts spiral\\GRANULAR-SPLIT-TESTS-XPATH-XSD, base = live C77D611B): (1) FCF22E4x -- regional_functions_follow_their_definitions (80 lines, 73 asserts) -> 8 tests by behavior (check digits, national identifiers, slack/if, matches, translate/tokenize, value errors, dates, format-number, strings/sequences) over two setup helpers rule_holds / rule_holds_on; every '? holds' / '? on_day' line kept byte-identical (122 assertion lines before and after; failwith multiset identical except one more 'one root element' guard). Formatter 0; proof running on vultr (check + bodies + all bodies to Rust, then a mutant: rule_holds_on ignoring its date must fail the date tests). (2) next file: evaluates_en16931_style_rules (53 lines) -> 5 tests (path functions, axes/predicates, cast steps, number functions, boolean errors) over holds_in source rule + an en16931_sample () fixture. Remaining over-budget tests after these: 10 mid-sized ones (5-11 asserts).")
inl note_of_granular_split_tests_xpath_xsd_3 () = Note ("GRANULAR-SPLIT-TESTS-XPATH-XSD", 3u32, "2026-11-04T16:10Z", "fiscal", "2026-11-04 12:10 fiscal: FINDING + course correction. (1) First split (regional test -> 9 tests) PROVEN on vultr: 20 tests pass in Rust; mutant (rule_holds_on ignoring its date) fails exactly 'an earlier issue date passes, as UBL-TR checks'. (2) Measured with kit's CellBudgetRuled: per-behavior tests are STILL over the per-cell budget (5-8 long assertion lines weigh 500-800 vs ~271), so with-tests overages ROSE (live 33/247 = 13.4% -> 47/268 = 17.5%): fitting tests in 271 would mean 1-2 assertions per test, which hurts reading, so the budget is not this item's target -- naming is (one failing behavior names itself). (3) My first helpers pushed CODE-only to 10.0% (holds_in, rule_holds_on, en16931_sample over budget) -- fixed with root_of (shared context record) and sample_invoice_line (fixture builder): code-only now 21/242 = 8.68%, better than live's 8.94%. Draft 3EACD73x (regional + en16931 + castable/for + mod/IBAN splits, 26 tests from 12, all 123 assertion lines byte-identical) proving now with a mutant (fixture 2.25 -> 2.26 must fail 'exact sum').")
inl note_of_granular_split_tests_xpath_xsd_4 () = Note ("GRANULAR-SPLIT-TESTS-XPATH-XSD", 4u32, "2026-11-04T16:41Z", "fiscal", "2026-11-04 12:41 fiscal: xpath_eval HALF LANDED (unstaged) C77D611B -> 3EACD73A, closed green (consumers compile, document_test, type_probe PASS with 26 tests typed, 0 respell drift; undo lib-land-undo\\20261104-122808). 12 tests -> 26, each named for one behavior (check digits, national identifiers, slack/if, matches, translate/tokenize, value errors, dates, format-number, strings/sequences, en16931 path functions, axes/predicates, cast steps, number functions, boolean errors, castable, for, mod, the DE-R-019 IBAN check); all 123 assertion lines byte-identical; shared setup root_of / holds_in / rule_holds / rule_holds_on and fixtures en16931_sample / castable_sample / sample_invoice_line. Proven before landing: 26 tests pass in Rust; mutants (rule_holds_on ignoring its date; fixture 2.25 -> 2.26) FAIL their exact assertions. Code-only stays 8.68% (21/242). The three remaining mid-size tests (set operators + current(), namespace maps, attribute names) are one topic each and stay. Remaining: xsd_emit's 33 test blocks, after the xsd_emit lands.")
inl note_of_granular_split_tests_xpath_xsd_5 () = Note ("GRANULAR-SPLIT-TESTS-XPATH-XSD", 5u32, "2026-11-04T16:43Z", "fiscal", "2026-11-04 12:43 fiscal: DONE. xpath_eval half landed (3EACD73A, 12 -> 26 behavior-named tests, see note 4). xsd_emit half needs no split: its 33 test blocks are ALREADY one behavior each, named for what they check (e.g. a_required_signature_list_is_staged_as_one_slot, staged_writers_write_any_stage_and_only_a_present_signature), 2-7 assertions over a fixture schema built for that behavior; they exceed the cell budget through their fixtures, not by mixing topics, so splitting would only duplicate fixtures. The item's aim (a failing behavior names itself) holds in both files; tests stay outside the GRANULAR 10% per kit's ruling.")
inl c_borrowed_loop_inline () = Task ("C-BORROWED-LOOP-INLINE", "codegenC: borrowed self-tail loops are always __attribute__((noinline)); small leaf loops called inside another loop's body want inlining (-17% on tibia's Q8 path), row-sized ones do not; choose by body size, measured", TaskKind, P2, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "team:compiler,compiler,c,performance,codegen", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_c_borrowed_loop_inline () = Planning ("C-BORROWED-LOOP-INLINE", Minutes 120u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_c_borrowed_loop_inline_1 () = Note ("C-BORROWED-LOOP-INLINE", 1u32, "2026-11-04T16:53Z", "compiler", "11-04 12:5x compiler: FILED from tibia's vultr measurements (stories15M, single thread, sed edits on generated C only; repro /root/tibia-probes/zigab2/b4.c base and b4-q8i.c; TRANSFORMER-CPU-SPEED latest note). (1) Q8 path: dot_i8, a self-recursive loop over one 64-wide int8 group called once per group from row_q8, made 'static inline' under strict -O3: output IDENTICAL to strict runq.c, time 1033 -> 853 ms (-17%, 6 rounds, load 1.0; runq.c 741); perf before: row_q8 40.5%, dot_i8 39.6%. (2) fp32 path: dot_l, one loop over a 288-wide row called once per row, made 'static inline': SLOWER (cycles:u 3.00-3.18e9 vs 2.90-3.00e9). CODE: spiral_compiler.fs ~26313 method_templ: 'borrowed_loop = not is_while && self_tail x.tag x.body && (owned_params x).Length < x.free_vars.Length' emits '__attribute__((noinline))'; other join points get no attribute. PLAN: first find why noinline was added for borrowed loops (git log -S on that line; it may guard borrowed-param lifetime or a GCC recursion pitfall), then make it conditional: no attribute plus 'static inline' for a borrowed self-tail loop whose body is small (statement count under a threshold, no calls besides itself), noinline otherwise. Acceptance: tibia's Q8 time within noise of the hand edit with identical output, fp32 path not slower (16-round squares on a quiet vultr per transformer-speed-levers), examples C -Native 0 behavior cells.")
inl note_of_c_borrowed_loop_inline_2 () = Note ("C-BORROWED-LOOP-INLINE", 2u32, "2026-11-04T16:55Z", "compiler", "11-04 13:0x compiler: WHY noinline EXISTS (found on the backlog, the ParamEscape pack review): with borrowed params, dot10 and matmul_rows9 became cheap enough that gcc inlined them into layers_from6 at 5+ sites and into scores_from13, duplicating the hot loop into one huge function (vaddss 62 -> 139) and losing the matmul_rows9.constprop clone: ~18% slower (3.65-3.9 s vs 3.1-3.3 s). noinline on exactly those two restored it (3.2-3.37 s, same output), and codegenC then emitted it on every borrowed self-tail loop. So the attribute guards against MULTI-SITE inlining bloat. Tibia's winning case is the opposite shape: dot_i8 has ONE call site (row_q8, inside its group loop). Rule to test: keep noinline on a borrowed self-tail loop called from 2+ sites; for exactly one call site, emit 'static inline' (or no attribute). Measure against both of today's data points (tibia's Q8 -17% and fp32 dot_l) plus the ParamEscape regression case (dot10/matmul_rows9), on 16-round vultr squares.")
inl note_of_c_borrowed_loop_inline_3 () = Note ("C-BORROWED-LOOP-INLINE", 3u32, "2026-11-04T16:59Z", "compiler", "11-04 13:0x compiler: tibia's count (b4.c) confirms the call-site rule: the hot fp32 dot_l36 (84%) has 3 call sites and dot_l30 (9%) has 4, while dot_i857 and row_q856 have 1 each. tibia's fp32 slowdown came from making ALL 7 dot_l variants inline, the multi-site bloat case, so no body-size term is needed for these programs. DRAFTED C-BORROWED-LOOP-INLINE\\1-inline-single-site-loops.kitdo: a cInlineSingleSiteLoops pass after cStaticFunctions turns '__attribute__((noinline)) <sig>{' into 'static inline <sig>{' when the function's name occurs exactly twice in the C (its definition plus one call; self-tail loops are while/continue, so a real recursive call is a third occurrence and keeps noinline) and there is no column-0 forward prototype (static after a non-static prototype is a C error). Emulated in .NET: a single-site loop becomes static inline, a 2-site one keeps noinline, a prototyped one keeps noinline; the first regex let a call line 'return f(a);' pass as a prototype, fixed to column-0 declarations. Next: build on the node (saturated now), examples C -Native A/B, then hand tibia the candidate's C for b4 to measure (strict -O3 parity and 6-round timing).")
inl note_of_c_borrowed_loop_inline_4 () = Note ("C-BORROWED-LOOP-INLINE", 4u32, "2026-11-04T17:01Z", "compiler", "11-04 13:0x compiler: SCOPE FIX before any build. Applying the pass to tibia's real b4.c (65 noinline functions) showed it would also strip lib-authored attributes: spiral_map_f32s is noinline ON PURPOSE (TRANSFORMER-CHECKPOINT-LOAD n9: it keeps gcc from tracing the mmap'd pointer into free(), 6 -Wfree-nonheap-object warnings otherwise). The draft now records the compiler's own borrowed-loop names in a borrowed_loops HashSet at method_templ and the pass only touches names in it (4 replacements, applies to live). On b4.c the rule (emulated with the same regexes, compiler names = those ending in their tag) makes 29 single-site loops static inline (dot_i857, row_q856, dot_l33/45/51, copy_i8s3, method4, group_max8, quantize_from9, ...), keeps the hot multi-site dot_l36/dot_l30 and spiral_map_f32s noinline. Output at vultr /root/tibia-probes/zigab2/b4-rule.c, handed to tibia to measure (parity vs runq.c, 6-round timing vs b4.c and b4-q8i.c).")
inl note_of_c_borrowed_loop_inline_5 () = Note ("C-BORROWED-LOOP-INLINE", 5u32, "2026-11-04T17:07Z", "compiler", "11-04 13:1x compiler (tibia's measurement of b4-rule.c, vultr, strict gcc -O3): PARITY: base, b4-q8i and b4-rule all give Q8 output identical to strict runq.c; b4-q8i and b4-rule give fp32 output identical to base. TIMING, 6 interleaved rounds per build and model, PROVISIONAL (20 of 36 rows ran above load 1.5, so relative order only): Q8 base 1152 / q8i 936 / rule 844 ms (rule about -27% vs base, at least as good as inlining dot_i8 alone, so the other 28 single-site inlines do not hurt); fp32 base 3098 / q8i 3049 / rule 3120 ms (about 1% either way, inside noise). The call-site rule stands with no size term for these programs. Before shipping: a clean load-gated square on vultr (tibia offered) for firm fp32 numbers, and the compiler-side review (examples C -Native A/B on the node). Log: vultr /root/tibia-probes/zigab2/rule-run.log.")
inl note_of_c_borrowed_loop_inline_6 () = Note ("C-BORROWED-LOOP-INLINE", 6u32, "2026-11-04T17:37Z", "compiler", "11-04 13:4x compiler (tibia's CLEAN square, vultr, strict gcc -O3, stories15M, 16 interleaved rounds per build and model, 0 of 64 rows above load 1.5, start load 0.78): outputs IDENTICAL base vs rule on Q8 and fp32. Q8: base 996 ms vs rule 798 ms (-19.9%). fp32: base 2807 vs rule 2800 ms (-0.2%, neutral). The call-site rule is a clean win on Q8 and free on fp32; no size term. Log: vultr /root/tibia-probes/zigab2/rulesq-run.log. Left before a pack: the compiler-side review (node job compiler-cinline, examples C -Native A/B), then pack38 with BEND-UNSAFE-MINIMIZE and DELPHI-ARC-MEMORY.")
inl note_of_c_borrowed_loop_inline_7 () = Note ("C-BORROWED-LOOP-INLINE", 7u32, "2026-11-04T18:46Z", "compiler", "11-04 14:4x compiler: v1 COMPILED PASS DID NOT FIRE; fixed in the draft. The node review (compiler-cinline) gave examples C 561 rows, 0 differing cells, not even residual, which proved no regression but also no effect. Probe (box, candidate fetched from the node): ':~ ^ sum_from (a : array_base i32) ...' called once from main still emitted '__attribute__((noinline)) int32_t sum_from0(...)'. Cause: the pass counted call sites in the functions section only, but codegenC appends main separately (main_defs), so a loop whose only call is in main showed 1 mention and was skipped. tibia's b4-rule.c was emulated over the whole file, so tibia's measurement reflects the intended rule. v2 counts the functions plus main_defs.text (signature cInlineSingleSiteLoops loops rest generated). Review v2 queued (node job compiler-cinline2b): probe must show static inline, then examples C -Native A/B (residual changes now expected on rows with single-site loops called from main).")
inl note_of_c_borrowed_loop_inline_8 () = Note ("C-BORROWED-LOOP-INLINE", 8u32, "2026-11-04T19:05Z", "compiler", "11-04 15:0x compiler: v2 PROVEN (node job compiler-cinline2b; candidate = pack36 live + the pass counting call sites over functions plus main). Probe: ':~ ^ sum_from' over an array_base i32, called once from main, now emits 'static inline int32_t sum_from0(Array0 * v0, int32_t v1, int32_t v2){' (v1 left noinline). examples C -Native vs installed pack36: 561 rows, 523 ran natively on both arms (7 build-fail on both, unchanged), compare 9 differing cells, ALL residual (the single-site loops), 0 behavior cells, 0 DISAGREE. With tibia's clean vultr square on the transformer (identical output; Q8 -19.9%, fp32 -0.2%), the program is ready: pack38 with BEND-UNSAFE-MINIMIZE (1+2) and DELPHI-ARC-MEMORY (1-3, plus 4 if its review is green).")
inl note_of_c_borrowed_loop_inline_9 () = Note ("C-BORROWED-LOOP-INLINE", 9u32, "2026-11-04T20:36Z", "compiler", "11-04 16:3x compiler: PACK38 REVIEWED and handed to main (install after pack37; one node build, job compiler-pack38rev, control = pack37 candidate bin). Source core 75AAB501 / host 5D4516B8, bin core 15FD51D7 / host 08B00F25. Delphi -Native 430 ran, residual only, 0 behavior; Bend -Native 414 ran, residual only, plus one exit cell: c_self_tail_loop_heap timing out at 120 s (slowest Bend row, control 80 s today; BOTH arms timed out on it in the v6/v7 reviews, same candidate residual 9b61078f), a timeout boundary, not behavior; C -Native 523 ran, residual 9, 0 behavior, 0 DISAGREE everywhere.")
inl note_of_c_borrowed_loop_inline_10 () = Note ("C-BORROWED-LOOP-INLINE", 10u32, "2026-11-05T00:57Z", "compiler", "11-04 20:5x compiler: PENDING INSTALL. main's pack38 install-when-clear gave up at 20:56 after 110 min: it waits for running trains as well as an idle compiler, and kit's TRAIN26 ran on the node the whole window. The box stays on pack37 (host 08B00F25 / core 5F6F6E73). pack38 (reviewed; candidate %LOCALAPPDATA%\\spiral-bin-candidates\\pack38) installs first in the next run, then pack39 (reviewed; needs live = pack38). The handover commands are in main's inbox from 15:3x (pack38) and 19:2x (pack39).")
inl note_of_c_borrowed_loop_inline_11 () = Note ("C-BORROWED-LOOP-INLINE", 11u32, "2026-11-05T03:56Z", "compiler", "11-04 23:5x compiler: INSTALLED on the BOX in pack38 (23:38, compiler's install-when-clear chain with no train wait, as kit allowed for box-only installs). Dry run: the 7 pack38 programs on today's live source reproduce candidate source core 75AAB501 / host 5D4516B8. After install: SpiralCompilerCore.dll 15FD51D755DD3120 and SpiralCompiler.dll 08B00F25C65C5283, both equal=True to the reviewed candidate; system dotnet launches it. Undo: spiral-bin\\compiler-installs\\pack38-20261104-233851. Node and vultr ship: main's waiter (pack-chain -SkipInstall) once pack39 lands. Done under installed-is-done.")
inl cells_test_timeout_directive () = Task ("CELLS-TEST-TIMEOUT-DIRECTIVE", "Cells: a ///- --timeout <ms> line on a :# test in a .spi package file does not raise that test's 300 s budget (KINO-TEST-TIMEOUT supported it in the Kino era; no .spi in any repo uses it now), and placed after ///> rust it even drops the test's target to the zig default; make the directive work in the test header in either order, or refuse it with a clear message", Story, P2, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "team:x,cells,cells-in-spiral,tests", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_cells_test_timeout_directive () = Planning ("CELLS-TEST-TIMEOUT-DIRECTIVE", Minutes 90u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_cells_test_timeout_directive_1 () = Note ("CELLS-TEST-TIMEOUT-DIRECTIVE", 1u32, "2026-11-04T17:00Z", "main", "11-04 13:0x main EVIDENCE (box, spiral/apps/kernel/src/oracle.spi, test kernel_verdicts_are_written_beside_their_lean_source): (a) header ///> rust then ///- --timeout 900000 then :# -> failed_targets zig, timeout_ms 300000; (b) ///- --timeout 900000 then ///> rust then :# -> rust target kept, timeout_ms still 300000 (spiral-tests headroom line). The test takes ~280-380 s on a loaded box, so it intermittently times out with no Lean file. notebook.spi scan_lines reads ///- flags file-wide and directives.spi''s scanner only reads the FIRST ///- line per cell (seen flag), which may be why a second ///- in a package file is ignored. Workaround used: none (removed the line); the oracle run is moved to an idle machine when possible.")
inl note_of_cells_test_timeout_directive_2 () = Note ("CELLS-TEST-TIMEOUT-DIRECTIVE", 2u32, "2026-11-04T17:12Z", "x", "2026-11-04 13:12 x: ROOT CAUSE found and FIX DRAFTED (kit drafts spiral\\CELLS-TEST-TIMEOUT-DIRECTIVE). In document.spi, a ///- line ahead of a :# header is neither a target nor a doc line, so open_step turns it into CODE: (a) ///> rust then ///- --timeout: the pending ///> rust is swallowed into that code cell and the :# test opens with no targets (zig default); (b) ///- --timeout then ///> rust: the target survives, but the ///- line becomes its own code cell, so the test's plan step has no timeout and falls back to the file's first-cell budget (step_budget/inherited_budget in notebook.spi), i.e. 300 s. And export had the twin gap: test_block_go merged every ///- line into the header options, where flags_of keeps only known modes, so --timeout vanished on export. FIX (document.spi D3CF79E4062C -> 1AD63E833A9B, 0 new cells over 272, vultr --check 0 inside the Cells package): when a :# header arrives and the pending code rows are ONLY directive rows (///> or ///- , no blank line or code between), they become the test's header rows (header_rows/only_directives), so both orders keep the target and the test cell's own ///- --timeout reaches the plan (scan reads every ///- line); on export a ///- row that does not start with --test/--ignore/--real (the synthesized forms) is written back verbatim among the target rows (user_flag_row), so it round-trips in its original position. A blank line before the header keeps today's behaviour, so file headers like ///- --package . are untouched. TESTS (document_test.exs 218FA101EB67 -> 629A8CC4B513): both orders keep target + timeout row and round-trip byte for byte; a package directive followed by a blank line stays its own cell. LAND: cells_land_window.ps1 -Applier scratchpad apply-cells-timeout.ps1 (base-hash guarded, dry run OK) -Files spi/document.spi,test/spiral/document_test.exs -Tests test/spiral/document_test.exs; held because the box is at 100% CPU (Cells lands only run on the box).")
inl note_of_cells_test_timeout_directive_3 () = Note ("CELLS-TEST-TIMEOUT-DIRECTIVE", 3u32, "2026-11-04T17:52Z", "x", "2026-11-04 13:52 x: LANDED green via cells_land_window (x-cellsto2): document_test 25/25 passed; spi/document.spi D3CF79E4062C -> 56AEAD3029FE, test/spiral/document_test.exs 218FA101EB67 -> 629A8CC4B513; undo lib-land-undo\\cells-20261104-134240. First attempt (x-cellsto1) failed 23/25 and RESTORED itself: x's first rule required the pending code to be ONLY directives, but in this parser a blank line does not close a code cell (it is appended to it), so the pending code also held the previous definition and the rule never fired. Final rule: split_directives peels the directive rows (///> or ///- ) directly above the :# header off the pending code; they become the test's header rows and the rest closes as the code cell it always was. So both header orders keep the ///> target and the test's own ///- --timeout reaches the plan; export writes that row back verbatim (user_flag_row), byte-exact round trip; a package directive followed by a blank line stays its own cell. Not yet verified end to end: a real package test with ///- --timeout 900000 reporting timeout_ms 900000 in spiral-tests (main's oracle.spi case).")
inl note_of_cells_test_timeout_directive_4 () = Note ("CELLS-TEST-TIMEOUT-DIRECTIVE", 4u32, "2026-11-04T18:38Z", "x", "2026-11-04 14:38 x: DONE, confirmed end to end by main on vultr: a ///- --timeout 1800000 line directly above ///> rust on a :# test in apps/kernel/replay/replay.spi gave spiral-tests headroom timeout_ms 1800000; the test ran 1273 s without a kill (it used to die at 300 s) and kept its rust target. Landed fix: document.spi 56AEAD3029FE (split_directives + user_flag_row), tests document_test.exs 629A8CC4B513 (25/25).")
inl note_of_cells_test_timeout_directive_5 () = Note ("CELLS-TEST-TIMEOUT-DIRECTIVE", 5u32, "2026-11-04T21:32Z", "x", "2026-11-04 17:32 x: REOPENED (Done -> Active 90%): the 13:43 land regressed files whose first lines are '///- --package ...' directly above a ':# name static =' module header (the package line was absorbed into the header cell). Caught by x-next6c's arm-A fixtures. Fix landing via cells_land_window (x-cellspkg1): test_flag_row excludes ///- rows containing --package; new test 'a package directive directly above a module :# header stays its own cell and round-trips'. document.spi 56AEAD3029FE -> D968ED539460, document_test.exs 629A8CC4B513 -> BA432062D567.")
inl note_of_cells_test_timeout_directive_6 () = Note ("CELLS-TEST-TIMEOUT-DIRECTIVE", 6u32, "2026-11-04T21:40Z", "x", "2026-11-04 17:40 x: REGRESSION FIX LANDED green (x-cellspkg1, cells_land_window: document_test 26/26; document.spi 56AEAD3029FE -> D968ED539460, document_test.exs 629A8CC4B513 -> BA432062D567; undo lib-land-undo\\cells-20261104-173200). A ///- row containing --package is no longer absorbed into a following :# header (test_flag_row), so a package line directly above ':# name static =' is its own cell again; the ///- --timeout case still joins its test. Downstream proof pending: x-next6d's arm-A accept must reproduce byte-identical sim fixtures, which it could not under 56AEAD3029FE.")
inl note_of_cells_test_timeout_directive_7 () = Note ("CELLS-TEST-TIMEOUT-DIRECTIVE", 7u32, "2026-11-04T23:08Z", "x", "2026-11-04 19:08 x: DOWNSTREAM PROOF of the regression fix: x-next6d's arm-A sim accept (live posts.spi, same pinned x drafts as x-next6c) changed NO fixture under document.spi D968ED539460, where x-next6c under 56AEAD3029FE changed every generated fixture. So the package-row fix restores the pre-13:43 cell splitting for those files byte for byte.")
inl lib_reflection_gate_timeout () = Task ("LIB-REFLECTION-GATE-TIMEOUT", "lib reflection.spi's lib_gate is nondeterministic: a Spiral cell hits the 300 s Cells budget under load and whichever targets it kills report failed (3 runs, 3 different failing sets); split its test cells or give it a larger budget so the gate is deterministic", TaskKind, P2, D2, 100u32, Done, "", "team:compiler,compiler,lib,cells,gate", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_lib_reflection_gate_timeout () = Planning ("LIB-REFLECTION-GATE-TIMEOUT", Minutes 60u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_lib_reflection_gate_timeout_1 () = Note ("LIB-REFLECTION-GATE-TIMEOUT", 1u32, "2026-11-04T18:15Z", "compiler", "11-04 14:1x compiler: evidence from landing case_qualified_name (SPIRAL-NAMEOF-TYPED n6-n8): three lib_gate runs of reflection.spi (box under 100% load, box at ~55%, node) each logged 'Spiral cell timed out after 300s' and reported different targets failed (run 1: 10 of 15 incl. typed_name_styles rust; run 2: union_cases_fold typescript, union_cases_find rust, case_name cuda, nameof_cases_round_trip lua, typed_case_name_round_trips rust; run 3: mostly typescript). The newly added test passed in both runs that reached it. The file has 15 test blocks across up to 6 targets each (gleam, lua, cuda, rust, typescript, fsharp, zig), and slowest cells run 175-274 s. Fix: split the module's tests so no cell group nears the budget, or have lib_gate retry a timed-out cell once; then 3 consecutive runs must agree.")
inl note_of_lib_reflection_gate_timeout_2 () = Note ("LIB-REFLECTION-GATE-TIMEOUT", 2u32, "2026-11-04T18:46Z", "compiler", "11-04 14:4x compiler: LANDED. Each of reflection.spi's 12 multi-target tests (2+ targets) gets '///- --timeout 900000' (the schematron_run.spi spelling; Cells' per-cell budget, default 300 s). Measured per-test totals were 250-320 s over the targets, right at the 300 s budget. Round trip canonical and a fixpoint; lib_land_window closed green: 3 consumer-used signatures unchanged, apps/spiral compiles, document_test 25, type_probe pass (15 typed), 0 respell drift, reflection.spi 94FB2F11 -> F689F038, lib digest 8FF3D3DD, undo 20261104-142150. Left: one full lib_gate run with no cell timeout and all targets agreeing (box or node, whichever is quieter).")
inl note_of_lib_reflection_gate_timeout_3 () = Note ("LIB-REFLECTION-GATE-TIMEOUT", 3u32, "2026-11-04T19:53Z", "compiler", "11-04 15:5x compiler: DONE. Node lib_gate of the landed reflection.spi (F689F0385A60, per-test 900 s budgets), installed pack36: exit 0, 'tests: passed 15 failed 0 of 15', no cell timeout, 11.7 min wall. The three earlier runs (each with a 300 s cell timeout) reported 10, 5 and 6 failed targets in different sets. The gate is deterministic now; case_qualified_name (SPIRAL-NAMEOF-TYPED) passes inside this green run.")
inl cic_env_index () = Task ("CIC-ENV-INDEX", "kernel environment as an index, not a list: find_decl scans list decl linearly (about 1800 declarations by the end of Init.Prelude), so the export replay takes 20 min per run on vultr; a persistent map keyed by name (lib dict or a hash trie) for lookups, keeping admission order where the kernel needs it", Story, P4, D4, 0u32, Planned, "CIC-LEAN-EXPORT-REPLAY", "lean,kernel,typefeatureset,team:main,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_cic_env_index () = Planning ("CIC-ENV-INDEX", Minutes 180u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_cic_env_index_1 () = Note ("CIC-ENV-INDEX", 1u32, "2026-11-04T19:28Z", "main", "11-04 15:3x main FILED from CIC-LEAN-EXPORT-REPLAY: replay runtime grew from ~5 min to ~20 min as more of the Prelude was admitted (each accepted declaration grows the list every later lookup scans). DOD: same verdicts (oracle pins, kernel tests, replay tally byte-identical), replay of Init.Prelude under 5 min on vultr.")
inl note_of_cic_env_index_2 () = Note ("CIC-ENV-INDEX", 2u32, "2026-11-04T19:59Z", "main", "11-04 16:0x main SCOPE: env.spi's find_decl is a linear scan (decl_name d = n per element); 17 lookups call it, 107 signatures carry `list decl`, and about 6 sites extend it (d :: env in typing admit/admit_quotient_decls, recursor :: env in inductive, the fvar axiom in conversion's bodies_defeq and context_env). PLAN: one nominal environment = { decls : list decl; index : <persistent name map> } with find_decl/extended as the only accessors; the 107 signatures change mechanically (list decl -> environment) and the extension sites go through `extended`. Measure first: time one replay with a counter of find_decl steps to confirm lookups dominate before the refactor.")
inl note_of_cic_env_index_3 () = Note ("CIC-ENV-INDEX", 3u32, "2026-11-04T22:58Z", "main", "11-04 18:5x main RATIONALE CORRECTED: the 20-minute replays that motivated this item were NOT lookup cost. They were the u64 overflow in Nat.pow 2 64 falling back to structural unfolding (fixed by decimal bignum arithmetic, CIC-LEAN-EXPORT-REPLAY n16). With that fixed, Init.Prelude (1837 decls) replays in 33 s and Init.Core (3479 decls) in 89 s on vultr, so the DoD target (< 5 min) already holds. Lowered to P4 and kept Planned: revisit only if a larger module replay (Init.Data.*, Std) shows find_decl dominating, and measure that first (a step counter in find_decl) before any refactor of the 107 signatures.")
inl note_of_cic_env_index_4 () = Note ("CIC-ENV-INDEX", 4u32, "2026-11-04T23:25Z", "main", "11-04 19:3x main NEW EVIDENCE (memory, not speed): Init.Data.Array.Basic (lean4export v4.35.0, 274585 lines, 15.6 MB, sha FED98694, pinned in the kit drafts store under CIC-REPLAY-OPAQUE-VALUES) was killed at 132 s under a 1.8 GB systemd cap on vultr (exit 143, no tally), while modules up to 180k lines replay in <= 158 s. The replay holds at once: every export line as a string, three id-indexed arrays sized to the line count, every decoded row, and the full decl list with terms. Cheaper first steps than an index: stream lines instead of materializing the list, drop rows after storing them, size the arrays per table (names/levels/exprs counts) instead of by line count; then measure RSS per module. Run Array.Basic on the node (more RAM) to get its verdict meanwhile.")
inl note_of_cic_env_index_5 () = Note ("CIC-ENV-INDEX", 5u32, "2026-11-04T23:32Z", "main", "11-04 19:3x main MEASUREMENT CAVEAT: /usr/bin/time -v around the Nat.Basic (115 s) and List.Basic (164 s) replays reported a max RSS of ~139 MB, but that is the pwsh/mix wrapper, NOT the Rust test cell: the cell is reaped by the BEAM, so time's rusage never sees it. The 20:30 UTC OOM showed the cell itself at 2.4 GB RSS (dmesg). To measure properly: run under a NAMED transient unit (systemd-run --unit=main-replay-N --scope ...) and read /sys/fs/cgroup/system.slice/main-replay-N.scope/memory.peak before the scope exits, or sample ps rss of the \"cell\" process every few seconds.")
inl kit_agile_check_timeout () = Task ("KIT-AGILE-CHECK-TIMEOUT", "kit agile check fails on the live backlog: its state type-check runs under a fixed 30 s process bound and the 13.7 MB agile.spi no longer fits", TaskKind, P1, D2, 60u32, Active, "", "kit,team:kit,agile,bug", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_kit_agile_check_timeout () = Planning ("KIT-AGILE-CHECK-TIMEOUT", Minutes 60u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_kit_agile_check_timeout_1 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-CHECK-TIMEOUT", 1u32, "2026-11-04T20:45Z", "kit", "11-04 22:1x kit: FOUND while sweeping items. `kit agile check C:\\home\\git\\i574n.github\\docs\\backlog` (installed TRAIN14c) -> \"kit error: ...\\state\\views.spi: command timed out after 30000 ms\". The message comes from kit_process run_bounded (process.rs:10) under kit_agile_state's compiler type-check of the state package (state.rs agile_typecheck_spiral); agile.spi is 13.7 MB, so a full --check now takes longer than the fixed 30000 ms. DoD: check finishes on the live backlog (bound sized to the state, or the type-check scoped to the files that changed since the last good check), with a CLI test on a fixture that keeps the old fast path; record the measured check time. Never raise the bound blindly without measuring.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_check_timeout_2 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-CHECK-TIMEOUT", 2u32, "2026-11-04T21:52Z", "kit", "11-04 23:5x kit: MEASURED, and it corrects my filing note: size is not the cause. On vultr (quiet, load 0.13; pack34 compiler) `--check` of the live state package takes ~4.9 s per file: views.spi 4883 ms, agile.spi (14.1 MB) 4874 ms, both ok. The box hit the fixed 30000 ms bound because its CPU was pinned at 100 % by other lanes' runs at the time, so dotnet startup + the check ran ~6x slower. FIX DIRECTION: (1) a bound scaled for a saturated box (e.g. 120 s) or read from the job-host slot state, and (2) on a timeout, report \"check could not finish (machine busy), state unverified\" with exit 3 instead of an error that reads like a broken state. Re-time on the box when it is quiet before choosing the number.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_check_timeout_3 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-CHECK-TIMEOUT", 3u32, "2026-11-04T22:07Z", "kit", "11-04 18:1x kit: the type-check bound is ALREADY configurable: kit_process typecheck_spiral_file reads KIT_SPIRAL_TIMEOUT_MS (default 30000). With KIT_SPIRAL_TIMEOUT_MS=120000 on the box (load 100 %) the check gets past that bound but fails 40 s in on a SECOND 30 s limit: \"Attestation timed out after 30000 ms ... Spiral typecheck attestation failed for state\\views.spi via ...spiral-bin...\" (the attestation step of the discovered compiler session, not kit_process). So the fix needs both bounds scaled (or the attestation's own setting found and documented); searching its source now. Workaround for a quiet box: none needed (vultr checks a file in ~4.9 s).")
inl note_of_kit_agile_check_timeout_4 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-CHECK-TIMEOUT", 4u32, "2026-11-04T22:10Z", "kit", "11-04 18:2x kit: ROOT CAUSE + FIX (unstaged). The second 30 s limit is the compiler host's own attestation default: spiral apps/compiler host Program.fs runs `--check <path>` under runAttestationBounded with 30000 ms unless argv is `--check --timeout-ms N <path>`. kit_process typecheck_spiral_file passed only `--check <path>`, so KIT_SPIRAL_TIMEOUT_MS raised kit's outer process bound but never reached the compiler. Fix in kit_process/process.spi (the boundary package's host block): when the check compiler is the SpiralCompiler host (native_spiral_host), pass `--timeout-ms <KIT_SPIRAL_TIMEOUT_MS or 30000>` before the path, so both bounds move together. (The `--backend` call above excludes SpiralCompiler for its own argv shape; for --check the host parses --timeout-ms at argv[1].) Default behavior is unchanged (30000 both); on a busy box `KIT_SPIRAL_TIMEOUT_MS=120000 kit agile check <root>` now works end to end. vultr --check ok; the Rust inside the host block is checked by the next train's cargo check. Proof after install: the same command on the loaded box exits 0.")
inl compiler_uniontag_pruned_gadt () = Task ("COMPILER-UNIONTAG-PRUNED-GADT", "UnionTag disagrees across backends for GADT-pruned unions (declared vs dense index)", TaskKind, P2, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "compiler,team:compiler,gadt,backends", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_compiler_uniontag_pruned_gadt () = Planning ("COMPILER-UNIONTAG-PRUNED-GADT", Minutes 120u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_compiler_uniontag_pruned_gadt_1 () = Note ("COMPILER-UNIONTAG-PRUNED-GADT", 1u32, "2026-11-04T20:49Z", "compiler", "11-04 16:4x compiler: ORIGIN (SPIRAL-NAMEOF-TYPED slice 4 probe). A GADT-specialized union drops uninhabited cases but keeps each surviving case's DECLARED index in Union.tags (ty TUnion: tags.[k] <- i). peval's static UnionTag returns that declared index and Rust's tag() matches it (US2::US2_Count(..) => 1 for request i32 in the probe). Zig's union_type (~16425) numbers cases by POSITION over the surviving map (Count gets .tag = 0) and its UnionTag emits that raw .tag; C, TypeScript, Python, Pascal, WASM and the other '.tag' emitters likely do the same. So real_core::union_tag (lib pickler::case_index) gives different values for the same pruned value statically vs dynamically on Zig, and across backends. DoD: one numbering everywhere. Either each dense backend maps its stored position to the declared index in UnionTag (a small table only for pruned unions, since unpruned dense = declared), or peval and Rust switch to dense. Then probe a 3-case GADT keeping 2 cases on every backend, static and dyn, and lift UnionCaseName's compile error for that shape.")
inl note_of_compiler_uniontag_pruned_gadt_2 () = Note ("COMPILER-UNIONTAG-PRUNED-GADT", 2u32, "2026-11-04T20:57Z", "compiler", "11-04 16:5x compiler: SURVEY corrects the origin note (node compiler-tagsurvey, installed pack36, scripts/try.ps1): pick r = Text :: pick string | First :: pick i32 | Second :: pick i32, exit = dyn UnionTag(Second) * 10 + static. C 22, Lua 22, Rust 22, Zig 12. So the DECLARED index is the norm (peval, C, Lua, Rust) and ZIG is the outlier: it numbers cases by position for constructors, switch arms and c{c}_{f} fields (consistent inside Zig) and its UnionTag leaks that raw .tag. Program 1 drafted: %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\spiral\\COMPILER-UNIONTAG-PRUNED-GADT\\1-zig-declared-tag.kitdo (1 replacement, Zig UnionTag only): unpruned unions keep v.tag; pruned unions map the stored position to the declared index with an inline switch (@as(i32, switch (v.tag) { 0 => 1, 1 => 2, else => unreachable })). Applies on pack38 + the UnionCaseName op. Proof in node job compiler-zigtag (expect 22 on all four, and the case-name probe 42 on all four). Left: survey the other backends (TypeScript, Python, Gleam, Lean, Pascal, WASM, Kotlin, Swift, Cpp, Lua done).")
inl note_of_compiler_uniontag_pruned_gadt_3 () = Note ("COMPILER-UNIONTAG-PRUNED-GADT", 3u32, "2026-11-04T21:06Z", "compiler", "11-04 17:1x compiler: PROGRAM 1 PROVEN (node compiler-zigtag, candidate core 830702AE = pack38 source + UnionCaseName op v2 + 1-zig-declared-tag; scripts/try.ps1 with SPIRAL_COMPILER_DLL): tag_pruned exits 22 on C, Zig, Lua and Rust (installed: Zig 12); the case-name probe exits 42 on all four. CODE SURVEY of the other UnionTag emitters: declared (OK) = F# (arms from the free_vars key), Rust (tag() from the key), C and Cpp (map_iteri passes the key), TypeScript (tag: {i} from the key), Lua (22 measured); positional (BUG, same as Zig) = Pascal/Delphi (constructors 'tag := {c}', UnionTag 'v.tag'), Lean (spiralTag '=> {c}'), WASM (TyUnionBox case_index, UnionTag 'i32.load'). Program 2 drafted: 2-pascal-lean-wasm-declared-tag.kitdo (3 replacements; Pascal: SpiralDeclaredTag(v.tag, [table]) helper only for pruned unions; Lean: spiralTag arms return the declared index; WASM: select chain only for pruned unions). Stacks on op + program 1 (stack check ok). Native proof on 11 backends (C, Zig, Lua, Rust, Delphi, Lean, Wasm, TypeScript, Python, Gleam, Cpp) with a scratch copy of the harness holding only the two probe samples: node job compiler-uniontag2.")
inl note_of_compiler_uniontag_pruned_gadt_4 () = Note ("COMPILER-UNIONTAG-PRUNED-GADT", 4u32, "2026-11-04T21:22Z", "compiler", "11-04 17:2x compiler: PROGRAMS 1+2 PROVEN NATIVELY on 10 backends (node compiler-uniontag3: a scratch copy of scripts/ + tmp/ whose samples dir holds only the two probes, both listed for 11 backends in the scratch matrix; candidate core 0F8738EA = pack38 + UnionCaseName op + programs 1+2). gadt_pruned_union_tag (exit = dyn tag*10 + static tag) exits 22 on C, Rust, Delphi, Zig, Wasm, Lean, TypeScript, Cpp, Lua and Python. GLEAM fails to compile it ('Index was outside the bounds of the array'): Gleam's UnionTag passes the DECLARED index to gleamCaseName, which takes a POSITION. Reading that emitter shows a wider, pre-existing Gleam bug: UnionTag builds [#(Us0Circle, 0), ...] |> dict.from_list |> dict.get(v), keyed by the constructor FUNCTION, so it never matches a payload case (the case-name probe fails native with 'Type mismatch' on Gleam). Program 3 drafted: 3-gleam-tag-by-case.kitdo (Gleam UnionTag = 'case v { Us0Circle(..) -> 0 Us0Square(..) -> 1 Us0Dot -> 2 }', names by position, values = declared index, like the F# emitter). Rebuild + rerun with program 3 queued (compiler-uniontag4). The case-name probe's Cpp failure is the CUDA C++ backend not supporting string comparison (probe limit, not the op).")
inl note_of_compiler_uniontag_pruned_gadt_5 () = Note ("COMPILER-UNIONTAG-PRUNED-GADT", 5u32, "2026-11-04T21:44Z", "compiler", "11-04 17:4x compiler: PROGRAMS 1-3 PROVEN ON ALL 11 NATIVE BACKENDS (node compiler-uniontag4b, candidate core 453561A5 = pack38 + UnionCaseName op + programs 1+2+3; the first try died on the node's full disk at 17:30). gadt_pruned_union_tag exits 22 on C, Rust, Delphi, Zig, Wasm, Lean, TypeScript, Cpp, Gleam, Lua and Python; the case-name probe (payload cases, so it exercises Gleam's new case-based UnionTag) exits 42 on all of them except Cpp, where the probe's string compare is unsupported by the CUDA C++ backend. Programs (apply in order after SPIRAL-NAMEOF-TYPED 5): 1-zig-declared-tag, 2-pascal-lean-wasm-declared-tag, 3-gleam-tag-by-case. Held sample: sample\\gadt_pruned_union_tag (exit 22). Next: one examples A/B on the affected backends (Zig, Delphi, Lean, Wasm, Gleam) in the next pack's review, since unpruned unions must stay byte-identical except Gleam's UnionTag text.")
inl note_of_compiler_uniontag_pruned_gadt_6 () = Note ("COMPILER-UNIONTAG-PRUNED-GADT", 6u32, "2026-11-04T21:57Z", "compiler", "11-04 18:0x compiler: PACK39 candidate staged at %LOCALAPPDATA%\\spiral-bin-candidates\\pack39 (source core C1F448821ACDAE60 / host 5D4516B8 = pack38's source + SPIRAL-NAMEOF-TYPED 5, COMPILER-UNIONTAG-PRUNED-GADT 1, 2, 3, SPIRAL-NAMEOF-TYPED 7; byte-identical to the stack proven natively on 11 backends in compiler-uniontag5). Review on the node: job compiler-pack39rev, one build, examples -Native A/B vs the pack38 candidate bin (15FD51D7) on Zig, Delphi, Lean, Wasm and Gleam, the five backends whose emitters changed. Installs after pack38.")
inl note_of_compiler_uniontag_pruned_gadt_7 () = Note ("COMPILER-UNIONTAG-PRUNED-GADT", 7u32, "2026-11-04T23:11Z", "compiler", "11-04 19:2x compiler: PACK39 REVIEWED and handed to main (install after pack38). Node job compiler-pack39rev, one build (core DDCA7897 = the proof builds), examples -Native A/B vs the pack38 candidate bin: Zig 470 ran / 0 differing cells, Delphi 430 / 0, Wasm 438 / 0, Gleam 440 / 0 (2 build-fail on both arms, pre-existing), Lean 453 / 1 residual cell (a pruned union's spiralTag def) and 0 behavior; 0 DISAGREE everywhere. Unpruned unions are byte-identical as predicted.")
inl note_of_compiler_uniontag_pruned_gadt_8 () = Note ("COMPILER-UNIONTAG-PRUNED-GADT", 8u32, "2026-11-05T00:57Z", "compiler", "11-04 20:5x compiler: PENDING INSTALL. main's pack38 install-when-clear gave up at 20:56 after 110 min: it waits for running trains as well as an idle compiler, and kit's TRAIN26 ran on the node the whole window. The box stays on pack37 (host 08B00F25 / core 5F6F6E73). pack38 (reviewed; candidate %LOCALAPPDATA%\\spiral-bin-candidates\\pack38) installs first in the next run, then pack39 (reviewed; needs live = pack38). The handover commands are in main's inbox from 15:3x (pack38) and 19:2x (pack39).")
inl note_of_compiler_uniontag_pruned_gadt_9 () = Note ("COMPILER-UNIONTAG-PRUNED-GADT", 9u32, "2026-11-05T05:04Z", "compiler", "11-05 00:5x compiler: INSTALLED on the BOX in pack39 (00:47, compiler's chain; it waited 69 min for x/kit/tibia notebooks to release the installed compiler). Dry run: the 5 pack39 programs (SPIRAL-NAMEOF-TYPED 5-union-case-name-op and 7-case-name-any-pruned; COMPILER-UNIONTAG-PRUNED-GADT 1-zig-declared-tag, 2-pascal-lean-wasm-declared-tag, 3-gleam-tag-by-case) on live = pack38 reproduce candidate source core C1F44882 / host 5D4516B8. After install: SpiralCompilerCore.dll DDCA78971B4EAB36 and SpiralCompiler.dll 08B00F25C65C5283, both equal=True to the reviewed candidate; system dotnet launches it. Undo: spiral-bin\\compiler-installs\\pack39-20261105-004718. Node and vultr ship: main's waiter (pack-chain -SkipInstall, now excluding *.r2r-old-*/*.replaced-*).")
inl cells_warm_gate_args () = Task ("CELLS-WARM-GATE-ARGS", "Cells: run_notebook.ps1 --gate fails with 'unknown or invalid options: --gate' whenever a warm cells server is up; translate --gate like the mix task's gate_args before Invoke-WarmNotebook", TaskKind, P1, D1, 100u32, Done, "", "team:x,cells,cells-in-spiral,gate", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_cells_warm_gate_args () = Planning ("CELLS-WARM-GATE-ARGS", Minutes 30u32, Load Light, Unassigned)
inl note_of_cells_warm_gate_args_1 () = Note ("CELLS-WARM-GATE-ARGS", 1u32, "2026-11-04T21:23Z", "x", "2026-11-04 17:23 x: REPORTED by main (seen on the node 15:0x and vultr 17:0x; apps/kernel/build.ps1 fails at once). Cause read in code: run_notebook.ps1 :70-89 sends the fixed args (with --gate) to Invoke-WarmNotebook and the warm server runs NotebookCli directly, while only lib/mix/tasks/spiral.notebook.ex gate_args drops --gate and adds --no-memo --no-html --output-dir <tmp>. FIX DRAFTED (kit drafts spiral\\CELLS-WARM-GATE-ARGS\\run_notebook.ps1, base E93F0CCCE4CB -> F9EE84890AE2, parses): on the warm path, when --gate is present, drop it, add --no-html and --no-memo (once), add a temp --output-dir unless --output-dir/--output-path was given, and delete that dir after the warm call. PROOF NEEDED: a machine with a warm server up; the box's registered port 13993 was stale at 17:1x, so the warm path could not be exercised there. Workaround meanwhile: SPIRAL_CELLS_WARM=0.")
inl note_of_cells_warm_gate_args_2 () = Note ("CELLS-WARM-GATE-ARGS", 2u32, "2026-11-04T21:35Z", "x", "2026-11-04 17:34 x: PROVEN by main on vultr (spiral synced 17:0x, live run_notebook.ps1 = base E93F0CCCE4CB, draft F9EE84890AE2, warm server up on 13993, file apps/kernel/src/universes.spi): plain warm run 5/5; BASE --gate -> 'unknown or invalid options: --gate' exit 1; DRAFT --gate -> spiral-tests 5/5 exit 0, temp output dir removed afterwards. Side finding (main): the SPIRAL_CELLS_WARM=0 workaround makes apps/kernel/build.ps1 on vultr run one compiler per notebook in parallel and hit OutOfMemoryException on 3 notebooks (3.4 GB box), so the warm fix also matters for memory. LANDING via cells_land_window (x-warmgate1, applier base/draft hash-guarded; queued behind x-cellspkg1's tree-write window).")
inl note_of_cells_warm_gate_args_3 () = Note ("CELLS-WARM-GATE-ARGS", 3u32, "2026-11-04T21:54Z", "x", "2026-11-04 17:54 x: DONE. LANDED green via cells_land_window (x-warmgate1: notebook_report_test 33/33; spi/run_notebook.ps1 E93F0CCCE4CB -> F9EE84890AE2; undo lib-land-undo\\cells-20261104-173911). Behaviour proven by main's A/B on vultr with the same warm server (base --gate exit 1 'unknown or invalid options'; draft --gate 5/5 exit 0, temp dir cleaned). The SPIRAL_CELLS_WARM=0 workaround is no longer needed for --gate, which also avoids its one-compiler-per-notebook memory blow-up.")
inl cic_replay_opaque_values () = Task ("CIC-REPLAY-OPAQUE-VALUES", "Lean export replay: type-check opaque and partial definitions' values (today they are admitted as axioms by type only), and extend the replay past Init.Prelude to the next Init modules with their own pinned exports", Story, P2, D3, 100u32, Done, "CIC-LEAN-EXPORT-REPLAY", "lean,kernel,typefeatureset,team:main,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_cic_replay_opaque_values () = Planning ("CIC-REPLAY-OPAQUE-VALUES", Minutes 120u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_cic_replay_opaque_values_1 () = Note ("CIC-REPLAY-OPAQUE-VALUES", 1u32, "2026-11-04T21:47Z", "main", "11-04 17:4x main FILED from CIC-LEAN-EXPORT-REPLAY (Done). Lean's kernel checks an opaque's value against its type but never unfolds it; the kernel needs an admit path that checks value : type and stores only the axiom. DOD: Init.Prelude replay still (1837, 0, 0) with opaque/partial values checked; one more module (Init.Core) replayed with 0 disagreements.")
inl note_of_cic_replay_opaque_values_2 () = Note ("CIC-REPLAY-OPAQUE-VALUES", 2u32, "2026-11-04T22:03Z", "main", "11-04 18:0x main (1) OPAQUE VALUES CHECKED: the replay admits an opaque by type-checking its value as a throwaway Def, then storing only the axiom; Init.Prelude stays (1837, 0, 0). Partial defs stay type-only (their exported values reference unsafe implementations absent from the export). (2) INIT.CORE (lean4export v4.35.0, 112007 lines, sha E208F824, pinned in the kit drafts store) first replay: 3345 accepted, 134 refused. Root: Prop structures (Iff, Subsingleton, Equivalence, LawfulBEq...) — the kernel tested \"is Prop\" by a level literally Zero, but a field like mp : a -> b has sort IMax(0,0). Both the large-elimination test (fields_small/is_prop_sort) and the projection guard (prop_guard/sorted_prop) were affected. FIX: reduce's is_zero_level is semantic now (Max of zeros, IMax with a zero right side) and both checks use it. (3) Init.Core after the fix: 3476 accepted, 3 refused, all from one root: Quot.pliftOn._proof_1 Mismatch between f a p1 and f a p2 where p1, p2 are different proofs of the same equation (Eq.trans ... vs Eq.ndrec ... with an implicit-binder motive) — proof irrelevance should equate them, so the suspect is type inference of the Eq.ndrec proof. Quot.pliftOn and Quotient.pliftOn cascade from it.")
inl note_of_cic_replay_opaque_values_3 () = Note ("CIC-REPLAY-OPAQUE-VALUES", 3u32, "2026-11-04T22:27Z", "main", "11-04 18:2x main VERIFIED on vultr: semantic is_zero_level tree -> kernel notebooks 71/71 and Prelude replay (1837, 0, 0). ETA CHANGE (conversion.spi eta_against now opens the lambda with a fresh fvar typed by its domain, mirroring bodies_defeq, instead of comparing a body with a loose BVar 0 against lift other 1 applied to BVar 0): kernel notebooks 71/71, kept, because before it neither side of an eta comparison could be typed for proof irrelevance. It did NOT resolve Quot.pliftOn._proof_1: Init.Core still (3476, 3, 0). NEXT STEP: have the replay print, for that one decl, the inferred types of both sides of the failing comparison (the f_4 a_6 argument: Eq.trans ... versus a fun h => Eq.ndrec ... term); the printed mismatch suggests they may not be proofs of the same proposition at the point compared, so the gap may be in how the expected type is instantiated, not in proof irrelevance.")
inl note_of_cic_replay_opaque_values_4 () = Note ("CIC-REPLAY-OPAQUE-VALUES", 4u32, "2026-11-04T22:43Z", "main", "11-04 18:4x main INIT.CORE REPLAYS WITH 0 REFUSALS: (3479 accepted, 0 refused, 0 skipped), replay test init_core_replays_through_the_kernel_with_no_refusal 1/1 (vultr, 89 s, memory-capped), export pinned (lean4export v4.35.0, 112007 lines, sha E208F824). The last root (Quot.pliftOn._proof_1) was proof irrelevance between f a p1 and f a p2 where p2 is an application whose head is a lambda (the apply_eqRec proof from Init/Core.lean:2257): conversion's constant_type had no Lam case, so it could not type p2. FIX: constant_type types fun (x : A) => b as Pi (x : A) (type of b) by opening b with a fresh fvar typed A and abstracting it back. Same tree: kernel notebooks 71/71, Prelude replay (1837, 0, 0). DoD met except the Lean oracle rerun on these conversion changes, PENDING on the node (busy with TRAIN25).")
inl note_of_cic_replay_opaque_values_5 () = Note ("CIC-REPLAY-OPAQUE-VALUES", 5u32, "2026-11-04T22:57Z", "main", "11-04 18:5x main DONE. Node Lean oracle 364/364 agree (cells 1/1, pins green) on the final tree (semantic is_zero_level, eta under a typed fvar, constant_type over lambdas). With the same tree: Init.Prelude (1837, 0, 0) with opaque values type-checked, Init.Core (3479, 0, 0), kernel notebooks 71/71 (vultr). Both replays are tests in apps/kernel/replay/replay.spi reading pinned exports from the temp dir (stored in the kit drafts store under CIC-LEAN-EXPORT-REPLAY and CIC-REPLAY-OPAQUE-VALUES). Next modules (Init.Data.*) and CIC-ENV-INDEX are the natural follow-ups.")
inl note_of_cic_replay_opaque_values_6 () = Note ("CIC-REPLAY-OPAQUE-VALUES", 6u32, "2026-11-04T23:02Z", "main", "11-04 19:0x main BEYOND THE DOD: Init.Data.Nat.Basic (lean4export v4.35.0, 141753 lines, sha E79C0464, pinned in the kit drafts store) replays with 0 refusals: (4281 accepted, 0 refused, 0 skipped) in 124 s on vultr (1.6 GB cap), no new kernel fix needed. Test init_data_nat_basic_replays_through_the_kernel_with_no_refusal added to replay.spi.")
inl note_of_cic_replay_opaque_values_7 () = Note ("CIC-REPLAY-OPAQUE-VALUES", 7u32, "2026-11-04T23:07Z", "main", "11-04 19:0x main Init.Data.List.Basic (lean4export v4.35.0, 180213 lines, sha 642697AA, pinned in the kit drafts store) replays with 0 refusals: (4868, 0, 0) in 158 s on vultr (1.7 GB cap), no new kernel fix. Four replay tests in replay.spi now: Init.Prelude 1837, Init.Core 3479, Init.Data.Nat.Basic 4281, Init.Data.List.Basic 4868 declarations, all 0 refused. Each needs its pinned export staged in the temp dir (vultr /tmp now holds all four).")
inl note_of_cic_replay_opaque_values_8 () = Note ("CIC-REPLAY-OPAQUE-VALUES", 8u32, "2026-11-04T23:13Z", "main", "11-04 19:2x main FINAL PACKAGE RUN: apps/kernel/replay/replay.spi as a whole on vultr (1.7 GB cap, no filter): spiral-tests 4/4 passed in 365 s; tallies Init.Prelude (1837, 0, 0), Init.Core (3479, 0, 0), Init.Data.Nat.Basic (4281, 0, 0), Init.Data.List.Basic (4868, 0, 0). Kernel notebooks 71/71 and the node Lean oracle 364/364 on the same kernel sources. All unstaged: apps/kernel/src/{export,reduce,conversion,typing,inductive,syntax,lean,oracle}.spi + package.spiproj, apps/kernel/replay/{replay.spi,package.spiproj}. The pinned exports live in the kit drafts store and must be staged in the machine temp dir before running the package.")
inl note_of_cic_replay_opaque_values_9 () = Note ("CIC-REPLAY-OPAQUE-VALUES", 9u32, "2026-11-04T23:17Z", "main", "11-04 19:2x main EXPLORATORY: Init.WF (well-founded recursion: Acc, WellFounded.fix; lean4export v4.35.0, 151920 lines, sha B6983443, pinned in the kit drafts store) replays with 0 refusals: (4392, 0, 0) in 126 s on vultr. Run through the Nat.Basic test slot by temporarily staging the WF export as init-nat.ndjson (restored after, sha E79C0464 verified), so there is no dedicated WF test yet: add one (init_wf_...) with its own staged file when this suite next grows.")
inl cic_noconfusion_blowup () = Task ("CIC-NOCONFUSION-BLOWUP", "kernel: checking Lean.Meta.Simp.Config.noConfusionType (a structure with many fields) blows the replay's memory past 1 GB (healthy replays peak at ~50-70 MB); find the duplication (nested casesOn over all fields, binder instantiation or struct_iota) and make it share or stream", Story, P2, D4, 100u32, Done, "", "lean,kernel,typefeatureset,team:main,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_cic_noconfusion_blowup () = Planning ("CIC-NOCONFUSION-BLOWUP", Minutes 180u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_cic_noconfusion_blowup_1 () = Note ("CIC-NOCONFUSION-BLOWUP", 1u32, "2026-11-05T00:08Z", "main", "11-04 20:0x main LOCATED by bisecting the Init.Data.Array.Basic export (lean4export v4.35.0, 274585 lines, sha FED98694, pinned in the kit drafts store under CIC-REPLAY-OPAQUE-VALUES), sampling the Rust cell's RSS every 2 s under a 1.8 GB systemd cap on vultr: first 63300 lines (1396 decls, incl. Lean.Meta.Simp.Config.casesOn at line 63279) -> 0 refused, 68 MB; first 63450 lines (adds Lean.Meta.Simp.Config.noConfusionType at line 63439) -> cell past 1 GB, cgroup-killed. Not bignum div/mod (tried, no effect, reverted). Every other module replayed today (Prelude, Core, Nat.Basic, List.Basic, WF) stays at ~50 MB. Repro: stage the export as init-wf.ndjson and run replay.spi''s init_wf test with a first-N-lines cap.")
inl note_of_cic_noconfusion_blowup_2 () = Note ("CIC-NOCONFUSION-BLOWUP", 2u32, "2026-11-05T00:13Z", "main", "11-04 20:1x main RULED OUT: term size. Lean.Meta.Simp.Config.noConfusionType's exported value is 133 DAG nodes and only 415 nodes as a tree (type: 5 / 7), so loss of sharing cannot explain >1 GB; the blow-up is in CHECKING it. Prime suspect: today's struct_iota (structure eta in iota). Checking noConfusionType reduces Simp.Config.casesOn on variables x and y, and struct_iota expands each into one Proj per field (Simp.Config has dozens of fields), then the minor premise's body reduces again; if whnf/defeq re-enters casesOn on the expanded terms (or lazy delta unfolds both sides repeatedly), work and allocation grow per field pair. First experiment: run the 63450-line repro with struct_iota disabled (does the cell stay ~70 MB?), then add a trace counter of Iota steps per decl.")
inl note_of_cic_noconfusion_blowup_3 () = Note ("CIC-NOCONFUSION-BLOWUP", 3u32, "2026-11-05T00:17Z", "main", "11-04 20:2x main RULED OUT: struct_iota. On vultr only (box tree untouched, vultr re-synced after: digest A638C532), structure_fields was stubbed to None and the first 63450 export lines were staged as init-wf.ndjson: the cell still grew to 1195 MB and was killed (158 s). Remaining suspects, in order: (1) conversion's lazy delta unfolding both sides of large casesOn/noConfusionType terms repeatedly; (2) constant_type's new Lam arm re-typing nested lambdas on every proof-irrelevance attempt (each call opens a fresh fvar and extends env); (3) K-like or literal paths. Next experiment the same way (stub one path on vultr, rerun the 63450-line repro, watch cell RSS): start with (2), since it is the newest code on the comparison hot path.")
inl note_of_cic_noconfusion_blowup_4 () = Note ("CIC-NOCONFUSION-BLOWUP", 4u32, "2026-11-05T00:20Z", "main", "11-04 20:2x main RULED OUT: constant_type's Lam arm (stubbed to None on vultr only, same 63450-line repro): cell still 1120 MB, killed at 75 s; vultr re-synced to the box tree after (digest A638C532), WF staging file intact. So neither of today's newest paths (struct_iota, lambda typing) is the cause; it predates them or sits in older code: lazy delta in conversion, K-like reduction, or the literal/bignum paths on Simp.Config's Nat fields. Same stub-and-rerun method for the next round; a per-decl Iota/Delta step counter written to the tally would localise it faster than stubbing.")
inl note_of_cic_noconfusion_blowup_5 () = Note ("CIC-NOCONFUSION-BLOWUP", 5u32, "2026-11-05T03:46Z", "main", "11-04 23:46 main CAUSE FOUND (by reading, not stubbing): universe-level simplification, not term reduction. noConfusionType's body is (a1 = a2 -> b1 = b2 -> ... -> P) -> P, so its sort is imax(0, imax(0, ... imax(0, u))) nested once per field, and conversion compares Sorts via leq_full -> simplify (conversion.spi:227). simplify rewrote imax(a, imax(c, d)) to max(imax(a, d), imax(c, d)), duplicating d, then distributed imax over both max operands without deduplication: the level doubled per nesting level, 2^(field count) for Simp.Config. That explains every earlier clue: a 415-node term, no refusal, and no effect from stubbing struct_iota or the Lam arm. FIX (universes.spi, unstaged): imax(0, b) = b; max operands are flattened, deduplicated (same_level) and drop zeros (joined/max_of). New test deep_imax_chains_stay_small_and_agree_with_the_semantics (a 60-deep Prop chain plus a 40-deep mixed chain, cross-checked with the brute-force leq_upto): universes.spi 6/6 on vultr. PENDING: the 63450-line Array.Basic repro on vultr (previously >1.1 GB, cgroup-killed), then the full 274585-line Array.Basic replay.")
inl note_of_cic_noconfusion_blowup_6 () = Note ("CIC-NOCONFUSION-BLOWUP", 6u32, "2026-11-05T03:57Z", "main", "11-04 23:57 main PROVEN FIXED on vultr: the 63450-line Array.Basic repro (export FED98694) replays 1397 decls, 0 refused, Rust cell peak 19 MB (was over 1.1 GB, cgroup-killed), 58 s, WF export restored (b6983443). The FULL 274585-line Array.Basic replay then peaked at only 76 MB but hit the notebook's 300 s cell timeout: VOID, not red (no tally). Rerunning now with a vultr-only ///- --timeout 3000000 on the WF test (box tree untouched). PENDING: that verdict; then Array.Basic gets its own replay test reading /tmp/init-array.ndjson.")
inl note_of_cic_noconfusion_blowup_7 () = Note ("CIC-NOCONFUSION-BLOWUP", 7u32, "2026-11-05T04:45Z", "main", "11-05 00:44 main DONE: the full Init.Data.Array.Basic export (274585 lines, FED98694) replays through the kernel with 6875 accepted, 0 refused, 0 skipped. New test init_data_array_basic_replays_through_the_kernel_with_no_refusal in apps/kernel/replay/replay.spi (unstaged, ///- --timeout 1800000), proven as written on vultr: exit 0, 475 s, tally (6875, 0, 0). The cause and fix are in n5/n6 (universes.simplify).")
inl kit_job_wait_owner_exit () = Task ("KIT-JOB-WAIT-OWNER-EXIT", "kit job wait ends with a verdict: it exits when the shell that started it dies, and never returns without printing the job state it stopped on", TaskKind, P1, D2, 70u32, Active, "", "kit,jobs,team:kit", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_kit_job_wait_owner_exit () = Planning ("KIT-JOB-WAIT-OWNER-EXIT", Minutes 60u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_kit_job_wait_owner_exit_1 () = Note ("KIT-JOB-WAIT-OWNER-EXIT", 1u32, "2026-11-05T02:19Z", "kit", "11-04 22:2x kit, from the 11-04 star exchange (resilience ask 3) plus kit's own evidence. Two failure shapes: (a) resilience: a backgrounded `kit job wait <id>` outlives the shell that started it, leaving orphaned waiters; (b) kit tonight: the background waiter on TRAIN26 (12 h timeout) returned exit 0 at ~22:10 with NO \"kit job wait ok ... state=\" line while the job was still running, so nothing watched the train afterwards. DoD: a CLI check where (a) killing the parent shell makes the waiter exit within ~5 s, and (b) every return path prints `kit job wait <verdict> id= state=` (finished / timed out / parent gone / interrupted). NEXT: kit_jobs wait loop: poll the parent pid (the owner check from kit_process_alive) each tick and give every exit a typed reason. TYPEFEATURES: a closed wait_end union (Finished | TimedOut | ParentGone | Interrupted) printed by one function.")
inl note_of_kit_job_wait_owner_exit_2 () = Note ("KIT-JOB-WAIT-OWNER-EXIT", 2u32, "2026-11-05T04:48Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-05 00:4x kit: BUILT, unstaged, rides TRAIN27. Finding: shape (a) was already implemented: kit_jobs waited polls watched_caller() (KIT_JOB_WATCH or the parent pid) and returns when it dies. What was missing was the verdict: job_waited printed the shared summary and exited by job state (a still-running job exits 3), so a caller-gone or deadline return looked like an ordinary 'state=running' line. Now kit_job_query has a closed wait_end union (WaitFinished | WaitTimedOut | WaitCallerGone), worded by reflection (union_case_suffix + kebab_of), and the wait line ends with ' end=finished|timed-out|caller-gone'. Exit codes unchanged. CLI check a_wait_names_why_it_ended in kit_jobs/stop_cli.spi: a 1.5 s wait on a sleeping job exits 3 with end=timed-out, KIT_JOB_WATCH=<dead pid> gives end=caller-gone, and after stop the wait gives end=finished. vultr --check: kit_job_query ok. Shape (b) (TRAIN26's waiter exit 0 with no line) is NOT explained: exit 0 means a settled exit-0 state, not running (that exits 3), so that return was not the wait loop's; the end= word will tell the next one apart.")
inl kit_agile_crate_headroom () = Task ("KIT-AGILE-CRATE-HEADROOM", "agile_* generated crates keep headroom under the 1000-line gate by rule, not by emergency splits: a size report per crate and a split pattern for the shared agile model", Story, P1, D3, 0u32, Planned, "", "kit,agile,size,team:kit", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_kit_agile_crate_headroom () = Planning ("KIT-AGILE-CRATE-HEADROOM", Minutes 180u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_kit_agile_crate_headroom_1 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-CRATE-HEADROOM", 1u32, "2026-11-05T02:19Z", "kit", "11-04 22:2x kit, from the 11-04 star exchange (resilience ask 4). Evidence: today kit split agile_claim_lease (1059), agile_claim_cli (1450), agile_task_listing (1015) and earlier agile_quality_state (1230), each only after a train or size run failed; KIT-TODO-SCAN-HEADROOM (agile_todo_scan at 1000/1000) is the same pattern. Root cause: the source-only agile models (agile_claim_model, agile_task_text, agile_note_model ...) inline into every agile_* crate, so one model change grows many crates at once. DoD: (1) a size report listing each agile_* crate's generated lines from the last regen, with those over 900 flagged; (2) the heaviest shared model functions moved behind ONE linked agile crate with tuple/array_base exports, so consumers call it instead of inlining it; (3) every agile_* crate <= 900 after a train. NEXT: rank inlined model functions by how many agile crates pull them in (grep consumers' package.spiproj). TYPEFEATURES: the report rows as a typed crate_size record; the linked boundary keeps typed exports (no joined strings).")
inl lib_land_failure_diagnostics () = Task ("LIB-LAND-FAILURE-DIAGNOSTICS", "lib_land_window / cells_land_window: on a document_test failure print the failing FILE and its first differing line (spi vs round-tripped spi), tee the full log to a file next to the undo dir, and offer a round-trip precheck of every draft before any proof starts", Story, P2, D2, 100u32, Done, "", "lib,cells,landing,team:main,main", "current", "current")
