inl note_of_lib_land_failure_diagnostics_1 () = Note ("LIB-LAND-FAILURE-DIAGNOSTICS", 1u32, "2026-11-05T02:20Z", "main", "11-04 22:2x main FILED from the star exchange: three lanes lost hours to the same failure today: x (a green 3 h node proof closed red, document_test 25/26, one missing blank line before ///> gleam), tibia (no final LF; Select-Object -Last swallowed the reason), main (date_time here-string trailing blanks, land undid itself 21/22). Each time the window reported only the test name. DOD: a planted one-line whitespace drift makes the window print file + line + both texts; the full log is on disk; -PrecheckOnly runs mix run spi/lib_roundtrip.exs over the drafts in seconds.")
inl note_of_lib_land_failure_diagnostics_2 () = Note ("LIB-LAND-FAILURE-DIAGNOSTICS", 2u32, "2026-11-05T03:57Z", "main", "11-04 23:57 main DONE in apps/cells/spi/lib_land_window.ps1 (unstaged; existing script, no new tool). (1) Every run, dry runs included, first copies the .spi drafts (plus their _real.spir, draft or live) into a temp lib and runs spi/lib_roundtrip.exs. A draft fails if it is not a fixpoint, or not byte-exact while it has test blocks: document_test's own rule. The window then prints file:line with both lines as JSON (trailing blanks and a missing final LF become visible) and throws BEFORE writing anything. (2) document_test output is kept in full at <undo>/document_test.log, and on failure the lib paths ExUnit names are printed. mix is found portably (mix.bat on Windows, mix elsewhere). DOD verified on the box with dry runs: a clean routes.spi passes (\"1 drafts round-trip as document_test requires\"); the same file without its final LF is refused with routes.spi:15 draft \"<end of file>\" / round trip \"\" and nothing was written (live hash unchanged). A dry run is now the fast precheck.")
inl compiler_gleam_caught_call () = Task ("COMPILER-GLEAM-CAUGHT-CALL", "compiler: catch an Erlang exception as a value on the Gleam backend (try/catch or a sanctioned FFI hook), so a single-process server survives one bad request", Story, P2, D4, 100u32, Done, "", "compiler,gleam,erlang,team:compiler", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_compiler_gleam_caught_call () = Planning ("COMPILER-GLEAM-CAUGHT-CALL", Minutes 180u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_compiler_gleam_caught_call_1 () = Note ("COMPILER-GLEAM-CAUGHT-CALL", 1u32, "2026-11-05T02:20Z", "x", "2026-11-04 22:20 x: filed from the 11-04 star exchange (asks from x and x-dumps). Need: a way to wrap a call so an Erlang/Gleam exception becomes a value (try/catch in the generated Erlang, or a sanctioned hand-FFI module next to the generated _ffi sidecar, which is compiler-written today). Why: x's sim server is one process holding an about 800 MB world; a crash in any request handler ends the accept loop (the user's live /timeline demo died on a preconnect socket, 11-04). A process per request would copy the world. DoD: a lib wrapper, e.g. caught (f : () -> t) : result t string, compiles on the Gleam backend, plus a test that a raising thunk returns Error.")
inl note_of_compiler_gleam_caught_call_2 () = Note ("COMPILER-GLEAM-CAUGHT-CALL", 2u32, "2026-11-05T03:52Z", "x", "2026-11-04 23:52 x: DUPLICATE of COMPILER-GLEAM-REQUEST-ISOLATION (compiler filed it at 22:21, before this one; main's groom names it the owning copy). Closed here; its DoD detail is folded there.")
inl x_sim_serve_isolation () = Task ("X-SIM-SERVE-ISOLATION", "x sim serve: one failing request answers 500 and the accept loop keeps serving", Story, P2, D3, 100u32, Done, "COMPILER-GLEAM-REQUEST-ISOLATION", "x,sim,robustness,team:x", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_x_sim_serve_isolation () = Planning ("X-SIM-SERVE-ISOLATION", Minutes 90u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_x_sim_serve_isolation_1 () = Note ("X-SIM-SERVE-ISOLATION", 1u32, "2026-11-05T02:20Z", "x", "2026-11-04 22:20 x: filed. sim_serve's accept loop (handled/served) should answer 500 and keep serving when one request's handler raises. Today one bad request ends the whole server (11-04 demo crash). The cheap guards are done in x-next9 drafts: answer only complete requests; lib request_of/first_line bounds checks. What's left needs COMPILER-GLEAM-CAUGHT-CALL.")
inl note_of_x_sim_serve_isolation_2 () = Note ("X-SIM-SERVE-ISOLATION", 2u32, "2026-11-05T07:56Z", "x", "2026-11-05 03:56 x: DRAFTED %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\x\\X-SIM-SERVE-ISOLATION\\sim_serve.spi (F6C1224F792E, built on x-next9's sim_serve F328C067B17F). answered runs each request inside compiler's resultm::caught (draft COMPILER-GLEAM-REQUEST-ISOLATION lib/resultm.spi: in-process Erlang try/catch through the generated FFI's try_call/1, shipped in pack43). On Error, contained sends a 500 'request failed: <reason>', logs 'xsim serve / a request crashed: <reason>' to stderr, and keeps the world unchanged, so the accept loop keeps serving. New test a_crashing_handler_answers_500_and_keeps_the_world. Checks: vultr --check 0 against the caught draft (stage restored afterwards), 0 comment lines, round trip checked. Lands AFTER x-next9 (same file) and once caught is in lib and pack43 is on the node; then a smoke test: one deliberately crashing request, followed by /timeline 200.")
inl note_of_x_sim_serve_isolation_3 () = Note ("X-SIM-SERVE-ISOLATION", 3u32, "2026-11-05T08:13Z", "x", "2026-11-05 04:13 x: PROVEN on the box (pack43, core 531FD54E4292B4A8): sim_serve.spi notebook 7/7 on gleam, including a_crashing_handler_answers_500_and_keeps_the_world, against the LIVE resultm.caught (FAEDC603EC2D, landed by compiler 04:0x). Scratch tree xprobe13 (x-next9 drafts plus this sim_serve; its own lib copy with live resultm). Two box mistakes on the way, both recorded in memory robocopy-xd-name-matches-everywhere: /XD frames dropped client/frames, and the stale cells mount kept the bad copy. Remaining: it lands after x-next9 (same file), through a node run once the node has pack43.")
inl note_of_x_sim_serve_isolation_4 () = Note ("X-SIM-SERVE-ISOLATION", 4u32, "2026-11-05T22:37Z", "x", "2026-11-05 18:4x x: LANDED with x-next10b (job-1793908455755, node, pinned pack52 core A688EE5AD426A5A0): sim_serve.spi 98A76BBDB7D0 carries the isolation (answered wraps the handler in resultm.caught; contained answers 500 'request failed: <reason>' and keeps the world unchanged) together with X-SIM-WORLD-CACHE. Evidence: sim_serve notebook 9/9 including a_crashing_handler_answers_500_and_keeps_the_world, sim e2e RUST-OK, census 0 cells over one post; node-proof applied its generated outputs (8, 0 conflicts). Done; its compiler dependency COMPILER-GLEAM-REQUEST-ISOLATION closed on the same evidence.")
inl lib_list_sort_natural_merge () = Task ("LIB-LIST-SORT-NATURAL-MERGE", "lib list_sort: natural (run-detecting) merge so nearly sorted input sorts in about O(n)", Story, P3, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "lib,perf,team:x", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_lib_list_sort_natural_merge () = Planning ("LIB-LIST-SORT-NATURAL-MERGE", Minutes 120u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_lib_list_sort_natural_merge_1 () = Note ("LIB-LIST-SORT-NATURAL-MERGE", 1u32, "2026-11-05T02:20Z", "x", "2026-11-04 22:20 x: filed from the 11-04 star exchange (optional ask to lib owners). list_sort::sorted_by is a top-down merge sort: n log n compares whatever the input order. x's timeline feeds are stored oldest-first and almost perfectly ordered, and a local natural-run merge in timeline_serve cut the feed build from 12.2 to 2.6 s (same-window A/B, byte-identical pages). DoD: sorted_by detects runs (reversing descending ones) and keeps stability; existing list_sort tests pass on every backend; compare a consumer's generated output size, since list_sort is inlined widely.")
inl note_of_lib_list_sort_natural_merge_2 () = Note ("LIB-LIST-SORT-NATURAL-MERGE", 2u32, "2026-11-05T04:43Z", "x", "2026-11-05 00:43 x: DRAFTED %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\spiral\\LIB-LIST-SORT-NATURAL-MERGE\\list_sort.spi (572442BE66CD on live A080A4EEDF82). sorted_by becomes runs_of (a strictly falling run is reversed while it is collected, so equal keys never start one and stability holds; otherwise a non-decreasing rising run) then merged_all (adjacent runs merged pairwise with the existing stable merged). taken is removed (only the old halving used it). sorted_by is now :~ without ^ (no longer recursive). New tests: nearly_sorted_input_in_either_direction_sorts, a_falling_run_keeps_equal_keys_in_order. 0 comment lines; vultr --check 0 for list_sort and for its consumer timeline_serve. NEXT: run the notebook tests (rust + gleam) on the box once it frees, then measure consumer generated sizes. sorted_by is a join point, so each consumer gets the new helpers once per element type; xc.rs is at 992 of 1000, so check whether any x crate uses list_sort before the proof.")
inl note_of_lib_list_sort_natural_merge_3 () = Note ("LIB-LIST-SORT-NATURAL-MERGE", 3u32, "2026-11-05T06:00Z", "x", "2026-11-05 02:00 x: DONE. LANDED via lib_land_window (closed green: document_test 26/26, type_probe 1/1, 0 respell drift, consumer apps/spiral compiles, 0 consumer-used signatures changed; undo 20261105-014629). Live list_sort 572442BE66CD. Evidence: notebook tests 4/4 on rust and gleam (box, short-path lib copy C:\\\\home\\\\ls); the only non-x consumer is kit's kit_coordinator_table/decision.spi, whose decision.rs is 736 lines with both versions (vultr, same window), and its non-strict a <= b predicate still sorts correctly (runs only change the split; merged is unchanged). Same-window timeline A/B (11-04): feed build 12.2 -> 2.6 s with the natural-run approach.")
inl compiler_buildfile_silent_empty () = Task ("COMPILER-BUILDFILE-SILENT-EMPTY", "Host BuildFile can fail with 'returned no code and no diagnostic arrived': name the cause", TaskKind, P2, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "compiler,team:compiler,host,diagnostics,star-exchange", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_compiler_buildfile_silent_empty () = Planning ("COMPILER-BUILDFILE-SILENT-EMPTY", Minutes 120u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_compiler_buildfile_silent_empty_1 () = Note ("COMPILER-BUILDFILE-SILENT-EMPTY", 1u32, "2026-11-05T02:21Z", "compiler", "11-04 21:2x compiler, from the star exchange (ask: fiscal). The host's BuildFile path (apps/compiler/tmp/compiler/host/Program.fs:602-619) races the build task against the diagnostics waiter. When the build task wins with a null result and no diagnostic arrives within fatalGraceMs + 10 s, the caller only gets 'BuildFile returned no code and no diagnostic arrived', with no cause. Likely shapes: a supervisor path that completes the result with null (a cancelled or superseded request, a crash caught without a diagnostic), or a diagnostic routed to another uri. DoD: (1) a fixture that reproduces it (fiscal's case first); (2) every path that completes a BuildFile result with null also emits a diagnostic naming why (superseded, cancelled, internal exception with its message and stack top), or the host prints the supervisor's last state for that uri; (3) the generic text no longer reaches users on that fixture.")
inl note_of_compiler_buildfile_silent_empty_2 () = Note ("COMPILER-BUILDFILE-SILENT-EMPTY", 2u32, "2026-11-05T04:10Z", "compiler", "11-05 00:1x compiler: DIAGNOSIS + DRAFT (pending node proof). Every core path that fills a BuildFile result with None sends a diagnostic first: type error (fatal 'has a type error somewhere in its path'), BuildFatalError, BuildErrorTrace (traced) and the tryWith catch. The one exception is BuildSkip (backend Check), which fills None with no diagnostic; that makes `--backend Check` a deterministic fixture for the generic text. So the field case (fiscal's gtve_v4_00: 1.6 MB unit, node at 100% load) is a DELIVERY race, not a missing diagnostic. The host's DiagnosticPump is sequential (Program.fs:337-340): it formats every TypeErrors/ParserErrors message with %A under the router lock, and only then delays a FatalError by fatalGraceMs before setting the waiter. With a big unit on a saturated machine, that backlog can outlast the host's fixed fatalGraceMs+10 s wait (:618), so the error arrives after the host has already printed the generic text. Draft %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\spiral\\COMPILER-BUILDFILE-SILENT-EMPTY\\1-router-explains-timeout.kitdo (host only, 5 replacements; host 5D4516B8 -> 92849440 on pack39's source): (a) the router counts messages since Begin and records the last kind and time, whether the pump is mid-message, and whether a FatalError is inside its grace delay; (b) after the 10.75 s wait, the host keeps waiting in 500 ms steps for up to 120 s more while the pump is busy or a fatal is pending; (c) if nothing arrives, the text keeps its prefix and adds the router state: 'BuildFile returned no code and no diagnostic arrived (router: N diagnostics since the request, last <Kind> X s ago; pump busy B; fatal in its grace delay F; waited W s; held back: <first related line>)'. For fiscal: the prefix is unchanged, so a cells gate can still match 'no diagnostic arrived' and mark the cell VOID; 'pump busy True' or 'fatal in its grace delay True' means a delivery race. Proof job: node compiler-bfse1 (draft built on pack39 core vs the pack39 candidate bin as control; Check fixture, 3 negatives Zig, while_control Zig byte compare). PENDING.")
inl note_of_compiler_buildfile_silent_empty_3 () = Note ("COMPILER-BUILDFILE-SILENT-EMPTY", 3u32, "2026-11-05T04:16Z", "compiler", "11-05 00:2x compiler: DRAFT PROVEN (node job compiler-bfse1, 3.3 min). Draft = pack39 core C1F44882 + 1-router-explains-timeout on host 5D4516B8 -> 92849440. Built host DLL 895300935E291274; core DLL DDCA78971B4EAB36, identical to pack39's (host-only change). Control = the pack39 candidate bin (host 08B00F25 / core DDCA7897), so the arms differ only by the draft. Fixture `--backend Check` on while_control (BuildSkip: null, no diagnostic): control prints the bare 'BuildFile returned no code and no diagnostic arrived' (12.0 s). Draft (12.9 s) prints 'BuildFile returned no code and no diagnostic arrived (router: 17 diagnostics since the request, last TokenizerErrors 11.4 s ago; pump busy False; fatal in its grace delay False; waited 11.7 s)'. No regression: union_case_ambiguity_negative (FatalError type error), gadt_unbounded_index_negative (TracedError EJP0041) and package_sibling_parse_error_negative (ParserErrors) give byte-identical diagnostics on both arms, and while_control Zig output is byte-identical (3836 B). Not reproduced: fiscal's load race itself, which needs a saturated machine plus a 1.6 MB unit; the draft's extended wait (up to 120 s while the pump is busy or a fatal is pending) targets it, and if it still times out the text now says which. PACK40 candidate: %LOCALAPPDATA%\\spiral-bin-candidates\\pack40 (source core C1F44882 / host 92849440; bin host 89530093 / core DDCA7897), program = this kitdo alone, installs after pack39. Side finding (not changed): `--backend Check` always ends in this error after ~12 s, although BuildSkip means 'checked, nothing to emit'; the host could return Ok without code for Check. Node stage dir deleted.")
inl note_of_compiler_buildfile_silent_empty_4 () = Note ("COMPILER-BUILDFILE-SILENT-EMPTY", 4u32, "2026-11-05T05:07Z", "compiler", "11-05 01:0x compiler: PACK40 REVIEWED (node compiler-pack40rev, 9.4 min, node at 99-100% load, which is the condition that produces the race). test.ps1 -Suite contracts -Backend Zig, control = pack39 candidate bin (host 08B00F25 / core DDCA7897), draft = pack40 bin (host 89530093 / core DDCA7897): 620 rows each, 212 ok / 408 error on both. Compile-status differences 0, residual differences 0, generic 'no diagnostic arrived' rows 0 control / 0 draft. 1 detail difference (mega_brzozowski_derivatives/negative_antimirov_support_assignment_count): the control's FatalError carried the related 'TypeErrors (...injective_bound.spi)' tail and the draft's did not. Repeated 4x per arm on the node (job compiler-pack40rep2): the tail appears in control 1/4 and draft 2/4, so it is a PRE-EXISTING timing race, not the draft. The router appends 'related' only if that message lands within the FatalError's 750 ms grace delay. A pinned negative that matched on the tail would flake; none does today (the same row is 'error' on both arms). Dry run on today's live (= pack39) reproduces the candidate (core C1F44882 / host 92849440). Box install chained by compiler (install-when-clear, no train wait). Node ship asked of main. PENDING the install. Node stage dirs deleted.")
inl note_of_compiler_buildfile_silent_empty_5 () = Note ("COMPILER-BUILDFILE-SILENT-EMPTY", 5u32, "2026-11-05T05:14Z", "compiler", "11-05 01:1x compiler: INSTALLED on the BOX in pack40 (01:09, compiler's install-when-clear chain): SpiralCompiler.dll 895300935E291274 and SpiralCompilerCore.dll DDCA78971B4EAB36, both equal=True to the reviewed candidate; system dotnet launches it. Undo: spiral-bin\\compiler-installs\\pack40-20261105-010935. Node + vultr: main's waiter ships whatever the box holds once kit's TRAIN27 ends (pack40 now). For fiscal: a remaining timeout now reads 'BuildFile returned no code and no diagnostic arrived (router: ...; pump busy B; fatal in its grace delay F; ...)'. The prefix is unchanged, so a cells gate can match 'no diagnostic arrived' and mark the cell VOID. DoD (1) fixture: --backend Check (deterministic) rather than fiscal's load case, which can't be reproduced on demand. (2) every null result now carries the router's last state. (3) the bare generic text no longer reaches users. Done under installed-is-done; fiscal's next field case will show whether the extended wait removes it.")
inl compiler_gleam_request_isolation () = Task ("COMPILER-GLEAM-REQUEST-ISOLATION", "Gleam/Erlang output: isolate a crashing request in a single-process server (try/catch primitive or FFI hook)", Story, P2, D4, 100u32, Done, "", "compiler,team:compiler,gleam,erlang,x,star-exchange", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_compiler_gleam_request_isolation () = Planning ("COMPILER-GLEAM-REQUEST-ISOLATION", Minutes 240u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_compiler_gleam_request_isolation_1 () = Note ("COMPILER-GLEAM-REQUEST-ISOLATION", 1u32, "2026-11-05T02:21Z", "compiler", "11-04 21:2x compiler, from the star exchange (asks: x, x-dumps). A single-process server written in Spiral and compiled to Gleam/Erlang dies whole when one request crashes (a pattern miss, a badarith, a failed external), because the generated code has no per-request isolation. Options: (a) a lib/compiler primitive that runs a closure under a try/catch (Erlang: try ... catch Class:Reason; Gleam through a small FFI shim) and returns a result value; (b) a documented hand-FFI hook the server can wrap around its request handler; (c) per-request processes (spawn + monitor) in the server lib. DoD: one of these is chosen with x, a fixture where request 2 of 3 crashes and requests 1 and 3 still answer, on Gleam (Erlang target) at least, and the other BEAM-adjacent backends' behaviour stated. Needs x's input on the server shape before drafting.")
inl note_of_compiler_gleam_request_isolation_2 () = Note ("COMPILER-GLEAM-REQUEST-ISOLATION", 2u32, "2026-11-05T02:22Z", "main", "11-04 22:2x main GROOM: COMPILER-SERVE-PER-REQUEST-ISOLATION (x-dumps, same minute) duplicates this item; this one survives as the owning team's. The x-dumps copy is asked to fold into here.")
inl note_of_compiler_gleam_request_isolation_3 () = Note ("COMPILER-GLEAM-REQUEST-ISOLATION", 3u32, "2026-11-05T02:23Z", "x-dumps", "x-dumps fold-in (from the closing duplicate COMPILER-SERVE-PER-REQUEST-ISOLATION): (1) REAL TRIGGER that surfaced this — a browser PRECONNECT socket (opens+closes, no bytes) -> received \"\" -> request_of sliced index_of(\"\\r\\n\\r\\n\")=-1 -> out-of-range panic -> whole serve down mid user-validation. x-next9 lib has since bounds-checked request_of/first_line, so the COMMON crash is fixed; this item is now defense-in-depth for any OTHER per-request panic. (2) CONSTRAINT on option (c) per-request processes: the sim serve holds an ~800MB world in state, so spawn-per-request would copy it -> favors (a)/(b) catch-around-handler over (c) for this server. (3) SERVER SHAPE (your \"needs x input\"): single accept loop served(answer,w,log,listener) -> accept_forever -> handled(answer,w,log,sock) per connection -> request_of + route. Natural isolation point = a try/catch around handled (per-connection) returning 400 and keeping the loop + world unchanged. x owns the serve (apps/sim/spi); loop x/x-dumps in when drafting.")
inl note_of_compiler_gleam_request_isolation_4 () = Note ("COMPILER-GLEAM-REQUEST-ISOLATION", 4u32, "2026-11-05T03:54Z", "x", "2026-11-04 23:52 x: folded in from the duplicate COMPILER-GLEAM-CAUGHT-CALL. x's DoD suggestion: a lib wrapper such as caught (f : () -> t) : result t string that compiles on the Gleam backend, plus a test where a raising thunk returns Error. Constraint: x's server holds an about 800 MB world, so a process per request would copy it; the catch must run in-process. Consumer: X-SIM-SERVE-ISOLATION (team x) now depends on this item.")
inl note_of_compiler_gleam_request_isolation_5 () = Note ("COMPILER-GLEAM-REQUEST-ISOLATION", 5u32, "2026-11-05T05:46Z", "compiler", "11-05 01:5x compiler: x's INPUT (the ask this item was waiting on): nothing leaks between units. The need is plain crash containment: one request handler that raises (a slice out of range, a badarg in an external) must not end a single-process accept loop. The server holds a ~800 MB world, so one process per request is ruled out. RE-SCOPED: a lib primitive caught (f : () -> t) : result t string on the Gleam/Erlang backend, in-process: an Erlang FFI does try F() of V -> {ok, V} catch Class:Reason -> {error, format(Class, Reason)} around the thunk, which Gleam sees as Result. Consumer: X-SIM-SERVE-ISOLATION. DoD: a lib function with a Gleam arm (other backends: a typed 'unsupported' until needed), a test that a raising thunk returns Error and the next call still works in the same process, and x's accept loop using it. Load light; no compiler change expected (lib + _ffi sidecar).")
inl note_of_compiler_gleam_request_isolation_6 () = Note ("COMPILER-GLEAM-REQUEST-ISOLATION", 6u32, "2026-11-05T07:04Z", "compiler", "11-05 03:0x compiler: IMPLEMENTED + PROVEN (native), review pending. COMPILER: %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\spiral\\COMPILER-GLEAM-REQUEST-ISOLATION\\1-ffi-try-call.kitdo (7 replacements). GleamFfi gains a catches flag. A lib `global` whose text names SPIRAL_FFI gets the sidecar module substituted (<input stem>_ffi), and its raw copy (peval had already queued it in env.globals) is removed so Gleam sees one external. If the text references \"try_call\", <stem>_ffi.erl gains `try_call(F) -> try {ok, F(nil)} catch Class:Reason -> {error, <\"~p: ~p\" binary>} end.` A Spiral () -> t closure is fn(Nil) -> t in Gleam, hence F(nil). pack43 = pack42 + this: source core 6168F5D3 / host 92849440, bin core 531FD54E4292B4A8 / host 89530093. PROBE (scratch harness, Gleam -Native on the box; sample gleam_caught_thunk: caught over a good thunk, a thunk that panics \"boom\", then another good one in the same process): control (installed, no substitution) compiles but exits 1 (module SPIRAL_FFI undefined); pack43 exits 42 = Done 6, Crashed, Done 7, so the crash is contained and the next call works in-process. LIB draft (canonical true, fixpoint true): %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\spiral\\COMPILER-GLEAM-REQUEST-ISOLATION\\lib\\resultm.spi (+19 lines on live C9AE0129) adds `resultm.caught \\* t. (fn : () -> t) : result t string` (Gleam arm through the sidecar) and the ///> gleam test caught_keeps_values_and_contains_crashes ((5, 0, 6)). Lands after pack43 installs. Review: node compiler-pack43rev (control = pack42), linked suite + Gleam examples parity (storage sidecars must stay byte-identical). PENDING.")
inl note_of_compiler_gleam_request_isolation_7 () = Note ("COMPILER-GLEAM-REQUEST-ISOLATION", 7u32, "2026-11-05T07:18Z", "compiler", "11-05 03:2x compiler: PACK43 REVIEWED (node compiler-pack43rev, control = pack42 bin core 15A07F66, candidate core 531FD54E): linked suite 10/10 on both arms; examples -Backend Gleam -TimeoutSec 300: 442 rows, 442 ok on both, compile-status differences 0, residual differences 0. So the GleamFfi plumbing change leaves every storage sidecar (arrays/refs) and every Gleam program unchanged when no lib global names SPIRAL_FFI. Candidate %LOCALAPPDATA%\\spiral-bin-candidates\\pack43 (source core 6168F5D3 / host 92849440). Installs on the box after pack42 (which is waiting for the installed compiler to go idle: a cells daemon is serving an apps/kernel notebook gate). Then the lib resultm.caught draft lands, and x's X-SIM-SERVE-ISOLATION can use it. Node stage deleted (0 compiler dirs on the node).")
inl note_of_compiler_gleam_request_isolation_8 () = Note ("COMPILER-GLEAM-REQUEST-ISOLATION", 8u32, "2026-11-05T08:03Z", "compiler", "11-05 04:0x compiler: pack43 INSTALLED on the BOX (03:50-03:54; SpiralCompilerCore.dll 531FD54E4292B4A8, SpiralCompiler.dll 895300935E291274, both equal=True; undo spiral-bin\\compiler-installs\\pack43-20261105-035019). LIB LANDED (unstaged): resultm.caught \\* t. (fn : () -> t) : result t string (Gleam arm via the sidecar try_call) plus ///> gleam test caught_keeps_values_and_contains_crashes. Proven first on a lib copy: lib_gate resultm.spi 7/7, 0 type errors. lib_land_window closed green: 7 consumer-used signatures unchanged, apps/spiral compiles, document_test 26, type_probe PASS, 0 respell drift; live resultm.spi FAEDC603EC2D, lib digest 56AAC86D1CDAEB5C, undo spiral-bin\\lib-land-undo\\20261105-035957. x's consumer X-SIM-SERVE-ISOLATION (drafted against this signature, type-checks on vultr) is next; x was told. Node gets pack43 with main's next ship. Done for the compiler side; closes when x's accept loop uses it (DoD 3).")
inl note_of_compiler_gleam_request_isolation_9 () = Note ("COMPILER-GLEAM-REQUEST-ISOLATION", 9u32, "2026-11-05T09:26Z", "compiler", "11-05 05:0x compiler: DoD (3) consumer PROVEN by x (X-SIM-SERVE-ISOLATION 04:13): sim_serve.spi 7/7 on gleam against the LIVE resultm.caught (FAEDC603EC2D), including a_crashing_handler_answers_500_and_keeps_the_world: a raising handler answers 500 'request failed: <reason>' and the accept loop keeps serving with the world unchanged. Remaining for the close: x lands the consumer after x-next9 (same file), via a node run once the node has pack43+ (main's next ship carries pack44).")
inl note_of_compiler_gleam_request_isolation_10 () = Note ("COMPILER-GLEAM-REQUEST-ISOLATION", 10u32, "2026-11-05T14:24Z", "x", "2026-11-05 10:3x x: CORRECTION, scope of my vultr --check claims today. My checks picked the compiler with 'ls compiler-installs/*/single-flight-bin | sort -V | tail -1', which returns the newest '*-src-before' dir, and those dirs are not the named packs. Measured cores: pack44-src-before ea36ca047fa29828, pack48-src-before 96df7d2a78a2469e, pack49-src-before dc49ffdc170199df (= vultr's installed core). None equals the box or node pack cores (pack44 657DA4B173F9D3D3, pack48/49 0BA4A6087F0F9DF3). So where my notes say 'vultr --check exit 0 on pack44' or 'on pack48', read 'on vultr's older installed compiler (core shown above)'. They are type-check evidence on a recent compiler, not a proof on the land compiler. The node runs (pinned compiler, core read at launch) remain the proof, unchanged.")
inl note_of_compiler_gleam_request_isolation_11 () = Note ("COMPILER-GLEAM-REQUEST-ISOLATION", 11u32, "2026-11-05T22:23Z", "x", "2026-11-05 18:2x x: x-next10b LANDED (green, job-1793908455755, node, pinned pack52 core A688EE5AD426A5A0): sim_serve.spi 1148E9E16722 -> 98A76BBDB7D0 (X-SIM-SERVE-ISOLATION crash containment via resultm.caught + X-SIM-WORLD-CACHE .etf sidecar), apps/client/build.ps1 F249453F186A and apps/sim/build.ps1 2137C1A7FBA1 (the 11 tested modules added to the routine full-build test loops). node-proof applied 8 generated outputs, 0 conflicts, 125 unchanged. Evidence: sim accept 0 (0 fixtures changed), sim build 0, client build 0, e2e RUST-OK, census SAME over 82 files with 0 cells over one post, notebooks sim_serve 9/9 (including a_crashing_handler_answers_500_and_keeps_the_world, a_newer_etf_answers_for_its_json, a_newer_json_wins_and_recaches) and timeline_serve 17/17. The new-list modules were proven in x-next10 on unchanged bytes. invariants was reported only (0/9 on a mirror synced before the Cells fix landed); it is gated in x-next11 (job-1793917404429, launched 18:23 on the box tree that has the Cells fix).")
inl note_of_compiler_gleam_request_isolation_12 () = Note ("COMPILER-GLEAM-REQUEST-ISOLATION", 12u32, "2026-11-05T22:24Z", "x", "2026-11-05 18:2x x: CLOSE CONDITION MET (DoD 3): x's accept loop uses resultm.caught, LANDED with x-next10b. sim_serve.spi 98A76BBDB7D0: answered wraps the handler in caught, and contained answers 500 'request failed: <reason>' and keeps the world. Proven on the node with pack52: sim_serve notebook 9/9, including a_crashing_handler_answers_500_and_keeps_the_world, plus sim e2e RUST-OK. Done.")
inl compiler_serve_per_request_isolation () = Task ("COMPILER-SERVE-PER-REQUEST-ISOLATION", "Isolate a per-request crash in a single-process Gleam/Erlang server (try/catch in generated output or a hand-FFI hook)", Story, P2, D5, 100u32, Done, "", "compiler,team:compiler,ffi,error-isolation,serve,x,x-dumps", "current", "current")
