inl note_of_comment_lint_gate_1 () = Note ("COMMENT-LINT-GATE", 1u32, "2026-11-05T03:07Z", "x", "2026-11-04 23:07 x: filed after the user's 11-04 night correction ('you started adding comments, code should be self explanatory, i thought we had memories and other stuff enforcing that'). Today ONLY memory enforces it: kit-style-check.ps1 checks host strings, not comments, and only on Edit/Write, and scripted writes bypass it. BASELINE, measured 11-04 22:5x (pattern ^\\s*///(?![>-]) or ^\\s*//(?!/) over .spi, excluding target/e2e/_build/build/deps/node_modules): spiral lib 389 lines in 68 files (benchmark 86, iggy 32, sm' 30, physics 23, date_time 17); Cells spi 35 in 9; x 13 in 7 (all stripped in queued x drafts); kit src 5 in 3. DoD: one checker (a kit lint or a ratchet in kit standards) that allows only ///> and ///- directives and the TODO form, wired into (1) the PostToolUse hook for .spi on Edit/Write, (2) lib_land_window and cells_land_window closes, (3) each repo gate, ratcheted so counts only shrink (memory baselines-only-shrink). Interim: x's land scripts refuse drafts with comment lines (11-04).")
inl note_of_comment_lint_gate_2 () = Note ("COMMENT-LINT-GATE", 2u32, "2026-11-05T10:59Z", "x", "2026-11-05 06:59 x: HEALED the Cells part of the baseline. Cells spi: 35 lines in 9 files -> 0, through cells_land_window (closed green, 58 passed: document_test, builders_test, targets_test; undo cells-20261105-065317). Files: builder_output, builders, gleam_project, html_markdown, json_value, manifest, memo, source_map, targets (all .spi under apps/cells/spi). Each diff removes only comment and blank lines, and each file round-trips byte-exact; manifest.spi also gained its missing blank line before a :# test and its missing final newline (both pre-existing in live). Baseline now: lib 389, Cells 0, x 1 (timeline_serve.spi:9, healed by x-next9), kit 5. The gate itself (hook, land closes, repo gates) is still kit's to build.")
inl note_of_comment_lint_gate_3 () = Note ("COMMENT-LINT-GATE", 3u32, "2026-11-05T12:25Z", "x", "2026-11-05 08:3x x: HEALED the free part of the spiral lib baseline. 38 lib files, 164 comment lines removed, through lib_land_window (closed green: 55 consumer-used signatures unchanged, apps/spiral compiles, document_test 26 passed, type_probe 38/38 PASS, 0 respell drift; lib digest E2F9F654E6655672; undo spiral-bin\\lib-land-undo\\20261105-073406). It waited first behind the kit lane's scope_run.spi notebook (a live lib reader). Only lines matching ^\\s*///(?![>-]) or ^\\s*//(?!/) were removed, then double blank lines were collapsed. 0 unexpected diff lines, and every draft round-tripped byte-exact before the land. The files were chosen as having no git WIP and no pending kit draft (list: scratchpad lib-no-comments-files.txt). LIB NOW: 199 comment lines in 26 files (from 363 at 06:59, 389 at the 11-04 baseline). Every one of the remaining 26 has git WIP or a pending draft from another lane (for example reflection.spi's /// ### headings), so they heal when those lands clear. Baseline: lib 199, Cells 0, x 1 (timeline_serve.spi:9, healed by x-next9), kit 5. The enforcing gate (hook, land closes, repo gates) is still kit's to build.")
inl x_notebook_roundtrip () = Task ("X-NOTEBOOK-ROUNDTRIP", "x: every x .spi round-trips byte-exact through the Cells notebook form (digest, prelude left)", Story, P3, D2, 100u32, Done, "", "x,cells,team:x", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_x_notebook_roundtrip () = Planning ("X-NOTEBOOK-ROUNDTRIP", Minutes 60u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_x_notebook_roundtrip_1 () = Note ("X-NOTEBOOK-ROUNDTRIP", 1u32, "2026-11-05T03:07Z", "x", "2026-11-04 23:07 x: filed. Live x .spi files whose Cells notebook round trip (parse_spi -> livemd -> to_spi_tested) is not byte-exact: apps/client/digest/digest.spi, apps/client/prelude.spi, apps/client/posts.spi (scan of 79 files, 11-04 22:5x). posts.spi is fixed in X-NO-COMMENTS (a missing blank line before ':= media_in'), queued in x-next7. prelude.spi feeds every sim fixture, so its fix needs a pre-registered fixture rule. Typical cause: a test block running straight into the next definition (memory lib-spi-notebook-roundtrip-exact).")
inl note_of_x_notebook_roundtrip_2 () = Note ("X-NOTEBOOK-ROUNDTRIP", 2u32, "2026-11-05T03:50Z", "x", "2026-11-04 23:50 x: digest.spi fixed in X-NO-COMMENTS (DB8006261449 -> B4D521609BB4: the doubled blank line before :# primary_posts_become_entry_lines collapses; round trip now exact) and added to x-next7's launch and land. prelude.spi is diagnosed but NOT drafted: a missing blank line before the '///> zig' row ahead of take_first_stops_at_the_limit (line 59). It feeds the sim fixtures, so it gets its own run with a pre-registered fixture list instead of widening x-next7's rule. posts.spi is fixed in X-NO-COMMENTS (x-next7).")
inl note_of_x_notebook_roundtrip_3 () = Note ("X-NOTEBOOK-ROUNDTRIP", 3u32, "2026-11-05T04:10Z", "x", "2026-11-05 00:10 x: prelude.spi drafted too (X-NO-COMMENTS, B9774F25227C -> D8E38864B453: a blank line before the '///> zig' row ahead of take_first_stops_at_the_limit; round trip exact) and added to x-next7. With digest and posts, all three x files that failed the round trip are now in x-next7. PRE-REGISTERED fixture allowance for the x-next7 re-run, from a grep of embedded source in apps/client/e2e: prelude code is embedded in sim-gap.expected/world, sim-lost.world and sim-small.expected/world; posts code in sim-stress.expected/world. Test bodies are NOT embedded (take_first_stops_at_the_limit appears in no fixture). So the accept step may change ONLY those 7 files, and a blank line before a test row is expected to change none of them. Any other changed fixture = red.")
inl note_of_x_notebook_roundtrip_4 () = Note ("X-NOTEBOOK-ROUNDTRIP", 4u32, "2026-11-05T10:09Z", "x", "2026-11-05 06:09 x: LANDED via x-next7 (green run x-next7c, node). posts, digest and prelude now round-trip byte-exact (X-NO-COMMENTS); every live x .spi round-trips.")
inl compiler_related_diagnostic_race () = Task ("COMPILER-RELATED-DIAGNOSTIC-RACE", "Host FatalError detail is nondeterministic: the related block is appended only if it beats a 750 ms grace timer", TaskKind, P2, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "compiler,team:compiler,host,diagnostics", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_compiler_related_diagnostic_race () = Planning ("COMPILER-RELATED-DIAGNOSTIC-RACE", Minutes 120u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_compiler_related_diagnostic_race_1 () = Note ("COMPILER-RELATED-DIAGNOSTIC-RACE", 1u32, "2026-11-05T05:29Z", "compiler", "11-05 01:3x compiler, found in the pack40 review (COMPILER-BUILDFILE-SILENT-EMPTY). The host's DiagnosticRouter appends the 'related' text (a TypeErrors/ParserErrors block from another file in the closure) to a FatalError only if that message reaches the sequential pump within the fatal's grace delay (fatalGraceMs, 750 ms; Program.fs DiagnosticRouter.Accept, FatalError branch). Which one arrives first is timing. Measured on the node at 100% load: mega_brzozowski_derivatives/negative_antimirov_support_assignment_count gives the FatalError with the 'TypeErrors (...antimirov_typed_slot_bound/injective_bound.spi)' tail in 1/4 runs on pack39's host and 2/4 on pack40's (2443 vs 3313 bytes), so the same compile prints two different diagnostics. Today no ExpectedErrors pin matches on the tail, so nothing flakes, but a pin on a related file's message would, and baseline 'detail' diffs show it as noise (1 of 620 contract rows). DoD: (1) the repeat fixture above, 8 runs with byte-identical output on a loaded machine; (2) the related text is chosen deterministically. Options: wait for the pump to go idle (the router's pumping flag from pack40) before resolving a FatalError, or collect related messages until the core's build reply rather than for a fixed 750 ms; (3) contracts -Backend Zig: 0 detail differences between two runs of the same compiler.")
inl note_of_compiler_related_diagnostic_race_2 () = Note ("COMPILER-RELATED-DIAGNOSTIC-RACE", 2u32, "2026-11-05T07:40Z", "compiler", "11-05 03:4x compiler: MEASURED + DRAFTED. (1) Pin dependence (node compiler-relrace, installed pack41, contracts -Backend Zig, 620 rows): 396 ExpectedErrors pins checked; 5 rows' details carry a related tail; 0 pins match ONLY inside the tail (each pin's Contains text still matches with the tail cut off). So the tail can go without re-pinning anything. (2) Root cause is two-sided: besides the host's 750 ms grace race, the core's own type-error fatal lists other files' errors from availableErrors, which reads only streams whose promises are ALREADY fulfilled (Promise.Now.isFulfilled), so sibling files still being typed drop out at random. Drafts: 1-complete-type-errors.kitdo (core) folds each file's error stream to completion (Stream.foldFun) for the entry's dependency closure, racing a 30 s timeout that falls back to today's partial read. Files run concurrently (Seq.Con.mapJob, input order kept), and the job then feeds the existing Stream.foldFun chain. 2-fatal-without-related-tail.kitdo (host) resolves a FatalError as 'FatalError: <core text>' with no related tail; related stays in the TypeErrors parse-detail path and in pack40's timeout explanation. pack44 = pack43 + program 1 (core 1B3DCBDF) is building on the node with the repeat fixture: 8 compiles of negative_antimirov_support_assignment_count per arm, counting distinct core-message texts (control = pack43 bin). PENDING; program 2 joins once 1 is shown deterministic.")
inl note_of_compiler_related_diagnostic_race_3 () = Note ("COMPILER-RELATED-DIAGNOSTIC-RACE", 3u32, "2026-11-05T07:52Z", "compiler", "11-05 03:5x compiler: CORRECTION from the variant diff (node, pack44 draft, 10 compiles): the two message variants differ ONLY by the host's related tail 'TypeErrors (...antimirov_typed_slot_bound/injective_bound.spi): [...]' (3313 vs 2443 chars); the core's FatalError text is identical on every run. My fixture's tail-cut regex missed the tail, which made the variants look like core variants. So program 1 (core stream fold) has no measured effect on this case and is DROPPED (kept in drafts, unproven). Program 2 (host: FatalError without the related tail) alone makes the output deterministic. Cost: in the runs where the tail won the race it carried the dependency file's own errors (injective_bound.spi:45/46), which the core's list omits. With program 2 those are never shown. Pins: 0 of 396 depend on the tail. FOLLOW-UP to note: why the core's type-error listing omits dependency-package files (antimirov_typed_slot_bound) even after a full fold; that is the deterministic way to keep the information. Next: pack44 = pack43 + program 2 (host only), review = contracts detail parity + 8x repeat fixture.")
inl note_of_compiler_related_diagnostic_race_4 () = Note ("COMPILER-RELATED-DIAGNOSTIC-RACE", 4u32, "2026-11-05T08:09Z", "compiler", "11-05 04:1x compiler: CORRECTION to my 03:5x 'cost' line. The core's FatalError DOES list the dependency file's errors: one box compile (installed pack43, no program 1) of negative_antimirov_support_assignment_count prints indexed_assignment.spi:70:71, :71:65, :107:61, injective_bound.spi:45:71, :46:63 and main.spi:7:10. The related tail repeats exactly injective_bound's two errors (as 0-based line 44/45 ranges). So the tail is pure DUPLICATION, and program 2 (FatalError without the related tail) loses no information; the 'follow-up: core omits dependency files' is VOID. Program 2 alone stays the fix (pack44b review pending: 8x repeat + contracts detail parity).")
inl note_of_compiler_related_diagnostic_race_5 () = Note ("COMPILER-RELATED-DIAGNOSTIC-RACE", 5u32, "2026-11-05T08:29Z", "compiler", "11-05 04:3x compiler: DETERMINISM PROVEN for program 2 (node compiler-pack44b; control = pack43 bin host 89530093 / core 531FD54E, draft = pack43 + program 2: host E441B6B3F8865B02, core unchanged 531FD54E). Repeat fixture, 8 compiles of negative_antimirov_support_assignment_count each: control 2 distinct full texts (9A011979 x2 with the related tail, 9D19A41D x6 without); draft 1 distinct text (9D19A41D x8). Contracts -Backend Zig detail parity still running in the same job. PENDING that half.")
inl note_of_compiler_related_diagnostic_race_6 () = Note ("COMPILER-RELATED-DIAGNOSTIC-RACE", 6u32, "2026-11-05T08:33Z", "compiler", "11-05 04:3x compiler: REVIEW COMPLETE, program 2 (host only) PASSES. node compiler-pack44b, contracts -Backend Zig, control = pack43 bin, draft = pack43 + program 2 (host E441B6B3, core 531FD54E unchanged): 620/620 rows, compile-status diffs 0, residual diffs 0. Detail diffs 6, ALL control-tail-only (the control detail = the draft detail + a related 'TypeErrors (file:...' tail); draft FatalError rows still carrying a tail: 0. With the 8x repeat fixture (control 2 texts, draft 1) the DoD holds: (1) fixture deterministic, (2) related text no longer appended to fatals (no information lost: the core's own message already lists those files' errors), (3) contracts detail is stable between runs. Ships in the combined next pack with GLEAM-INLINE-SINGLE-USE (combined build compiler-pack44c running). Node stage deleted.")
inl note_of_compiler_related_diagnostic_race_7 () = Note ("COMPILER-RELATED-DIAGNOSTIC-RACE", 7u32, "2026-11-05T08:45Z", "compiler", "11-05 04:4x compiler: INSTALLED on the BOX in pack44 (04:39-04:44, compiler's chain): SpiralCompilerCore.dll 657DA4B173F9D3D3 and SpiralCompiler.dll E441B6B3F8865B02, both equal=True to the reviewed combined candidate (pack43 + COMPILER-RELATED-DIAGNOSTIC-RACE program 2 + GLEAM-INLINE-SINGLE-USE program 1; source core 35DCBD04 / host A1E7136E, the two separately reviewed sources). Undo spiral-bin\\compiler-installs\\pack44-20261105-043957. Install wait 1 min (cleared 04:39 after a 04:38 start), the first install under CELLS-GATE-PINNED-COMPILER's narrowed check; tonight's earlier installs waited 21-63 min behind gates. Node: main ships it with notice to kit and x. Done under installed-is-done.")
inl cells_gate_pinned_compiler () = Task ("CELLS-GATE-PINNED-COMPILER", "Cells gates pin one compiler copy for their whole run, so compiler installs no longer wait on gates and no gate straddles an install", TaskKind, P1, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "compiler,team:compiler,cells,x,install,scheduling", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_cells_gate_pinned_compiler () = Planning ("CELLS-GATE-PINNED-COMPILER", Minutes 180u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_cells_gate_pinned_compiler_1 () = Note ("CELLS-GATE-PINNED-COMPILER", 1u32, "2026-11-05T08:26Z", "compiler", "11-05 04:3x compiler: DRAFTED at x's request (x reviews and lands through cells_land_window; the HEAVY-HOLD-DEADLOCK notes 9-10 give the measurement and root cause). Problem: install-pack refuses while any CompilerDaemon or spiral.notebook erl exists (63 + 60 min install waits on 11-05), because the daemon re-reads the installed dll stamp per REQUEST, so a swap mid-gate would split a gate across two compilers (x-next7b went red on a date_time Lua cell after a mid-gate node swap). Fix: the CLIENT pins. `spiral.notebook --gate` takes CompilerDaemon.gate_pin(installed dll), a stamp-keyed copy under %TEMP%\\spiral-cells-gate-pins shared across gates, touched on use, pruned after 24 h unused. It passes --compiler-pin through NotebookCli -> run_report -> @run_keys -> Cells.shared_compile -> req.pin, sent as an optional 8th request field only when set (old daemons ignore it; unpinned lines byte-identical). The daemon runs exactly the pinned dll for pinned requests, and the report gets \"compiler\": {pin, stamp}. Draft %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\spiral\\CELLS-GATE-PINNED-COMPILER (BASE.txt + 7 files). Scratch-copy mix test: compiler_gate_pin_test (5 new, including the mid-session stamp change) + compiler_daemon_test + notebook_gate_args_test, 35 passed, 0 warnings. FOLLOW-ON (compiler, after the Cells land): install-pack.ps1 waits only for processes referencing the installed bin and for spiral.notebook runs WITHOUT --gate (draft in the same folder, 9DA19CCF).")
inl note_of_cells_gate_pinned_compiler_2 () = Note ("CELLS-GATE-PINNED-COMPILER", 2u32, "2026-11-05T08:36Z", "x", "2026-11-05 04:36 x (Cells owner): the CELLS half is LANDED through cells_land_window, closed green, 65 passed (compiler_gate_pin_test, compiler_daemon_test, notebook_gate_args_test, build_only_test, document_test); undo cells-20261105-042827. Live hashes match the draft: compiler_daemon.ex 50401F3F28A2260B, compiler_client.ex 969085A7970FB4F4, cells.ex 85A6E572FE33E229, notebook_cli.ex CAE0BF5D1FF5BA29, notebook.ex 1600227AAC64AA54, mix/tasks/spiral.notebook.ex 52D8DB24A1709427, compiler_gate_pin_test.exs 2829E7449A68D8E2 (new). Left for compiler: install-pack.ps1 (draft 9DA19CCF7DC4195F); then this item can close. Running daemons pick up the pin path when they restart.")
inl note_of_cells_gate_pinned_compiler_3 () = Note ("CELLS-GATE-PINNED-COMPILER", 3u32, "2026-11-05T08:38Z", "compiler", "11-05 04:4x compiler: LANDED both halves (unstaged). Cells: x landed it through cells_land_window at 04:28, closed green, 65 passed (compiler_gate_pin_test, compiler_daemon_test, notebook_gate_args_test, build_only_test, document_test); undo cells-20261105-042827; live hashes = the draft's (compiler_daemon.ex 50401F3F28A2260B ... test 2829E7449A68D8E2). install-pack.ps1: compiler landed A368E6B10A6DA35D (base 898E1F2E). It waits for exactly: (a) processes whose command line references the installed bin (process-mode compiles); (b) spiral.notebook erl runs WITHOUT --gate; (c) CompilerDaemon erl processes started BEFORE the landed compiler_daemon.ex (mtime 04:28:32), which still run pre-pin code and ignore the 8th field, so they block until they cycle (x's caveat). New daemons and gated notebooks no longer block installs. Live check at 04:3x: 1 compiler erl, a notebook without --gate, BLOCK (correct). The measure that matters (install waits behind gates) comes with the next install.")
inl assign_parity () = Task ("ASSIGN-PARITY", "!!!!Assign (mutable dyn local) on Python, Kotlin, Swift, Wasm and Cpp", Story, P2, D3, 0u32, Planned, "FEATURE-PARITY-TABLE", "compiler,team:compiler,parity,backends", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_assign_parity () = Planning ("ASSIGN-PARITY", Minutes 240u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_assign_parity_1 () = Note ("ASSIGN-PARITY", 1u32, "2026-11-05T10:41Z", "compiler", "11-05 06:4x compiler, from FEATURE-PARITY-TABLE's first probe. The !!!!Assign(target, value) intrinsic (peval spiral_compiler.fs:12262; a dyn local bound with := ~x or dyn) compiles on F#, Rust, Pascal, Zig, Lua, TypeScript and C, but fails explicitly on Python ('Assign with 2 args not supported'), Kotlin and Swift ('doesn't support the operation Assign yet'), Wasm ('not supported in the Wasm backend') and Cpp ('Assign with 2 args not supported'). Gleam/Lean/Bend have immutable locals, so those are 'no' by design (Known rows). Fixture: package |core-, main.spi ':= main () : i32 = / := ~x = 1i32 / !!!!Assign(x, 41i32) / !!!!Add(x, 1i32)', expected exit 42. DoD: each of the 5 backends either emits the assignment (Python: a plain rebind, with nonlocal where the local is captured; Kotlin/Swift: the dyn local declared var; Cpp: x = v; Wasm: local.set) with the fixture exiting 42 natively, or the cell is recorded as 'no' with its reason in the parity table; plus a regression sample listed on those backends.")
inl uniontag_parity () = Task ("UNIONTAG-PARITY", "!!!!UnionTag on Bend, Kotlin and Swift (declared-index numbering)", Story, P2, D3, 100u32, Done, "FEATURE-PARITY-TABLE", "compiler,team:compiler,parity,backends,reflection", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_uniontag_parity () = Planning ("UNIONTAG-PARITY", Minutes 180u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_uniontag_parity_1 () = Note ("UNIONTAG-PARITY", 1u32, "2026-11-05T10:42Z", "compiler", "11-05 06:4x compiler, from FEATURE-PARITY-TABLE's second probe. !!!!UnionTag(x) on a dyn union value (union light = Red | Amber | Green; := ~l = Amber; !!!!UnionTag(l)), installed pack44: compiles on Rust, Wasm, Gleam, Lean (and the census's other yes backends); explicit errors on Bend, Kotlin and Swift ('The <X> backend doesn't support the operation UnionTag yet'). reflection.case_name and union_cases_* (UnionCaseName indexes by UnionTag since pack39) therefore can't run on those three either. DoD: Bend, Kotlin and Swift emit UnionTag with the DECLARED-index numbering every other backend uses since COMPILER-UNIONTAG-PRUNED-GADT (pruned GADT cases keep their slot), proven by the fixture (expect 1) and by the pruned-GADT sample gadt_pruned_union_tag (exit 22) natively on each.")
inl note_of_uniontag_parity_2 () = Note ("UNIONTAG-PARITY", 2u32, "2026-11-05T11:26Z", "compiler", "11-05 07:2x compiler: KOTLIN + SWIFT DONE in draft, proven natively. Program %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\spiral\\UNIONTAG-PARITY\\1-kotlin-swift-uniontag.kitdo (2 replacements): both backends already store the DECLARED index on every case (Kotlin: sealed interface { val tag: Int }, each case 'override val tag: Int = i'; Swift: protocol { var tag: Int32 }, 'var tag: Int32 { i }', where i comes from the union's (tag, case_name) keys), so UnionTag is '(x).tag' in each op dispatch. Candidate = pack44 + this, core 5DF28A0232DDBCE8 (host unchanged E441B6B3), built on the box. Scratch harness, test.ps1 -Suite examples -Backend Kotlin,Swift -Native: union_tag_kotlin_swift (Amber of Red|Amber|Green) exit 1 on Kotlin and Swift; gadt_pruned_union_tag exit 22 on both (declared-index numbering holds with pruned GADT cases). Control pack44: all 4 rows 'doesn't support the operation UnionTag yet'. The change only adds an arm that previously fell to that error, so no previously compiling program changes. LEFT: Bend (functional, no stored tag; needs its own approach). Rides the next pack with LINKED-UNITS-RUST-STRUCT-TYPES' program 3.")
inl note_of_uniontag_parity_3 () = Note ("UNIONTAG-PARITY", 3u32, "2026-11-05T11:54Z", "compiler", "11-05 07:5x compiler: BEND drafted: 2-bend-uniontag.kitdo. A per-union pure reader 'def U<n>_spiral_tag(u: U<n>) -> U32' with one arm per constructor returning the DECLARED tag ({d : U32}); fields are bound as unused<j>, two per i64/u64 field as in ruled_out arms. It is emitted lazily (HashSet union_tag_readers), so Bend output without UnionTag stays byte-identical. Unions with linear fields (Type kind) raise 'UnionTag on a union with linear fields' instead of consuming the value. Combined candidate pack47 = pack44 + LINKED-UNITS-RUST-STRUCT-TYPES 3 + this item's programs 1 and 2, built on the box (src 12A9B02940923093, bin core 1DC33DD0CD113196). Running now: native harness (union_tag_kotlin_swift, gadt_pruned_union_tag on Bend/Kotlin/Swift) and a box review (linked + examples Rust/Kotlin/Swift, control pack44). PENDING.")
inl note_of_uniontag_parity_4 () = Note ("UNIONTAG-PARITY", 4u32, "2026-11-05T11:57Z", "compiler", "11-05 08:0x compiler: NATIVE PROOF on pack47 (box, scratch harness47, -Native): 6 ran, 0 build-fail. union_tag_kotlin_swift exits 1 on Kotlin, Swift and Bend; gadt_pruned_union_tag exits 22 on all three. 22 = 10*2+2, where Second's DECLARED tag is 2 though the pruned union has only First/Second (Text pruned); the Bend reader emits 'case U0_First{}: {1 : U32} / case U0_Second{}: {2 : U32}', declared, not positional. Control (pack44): Kotlin/Swift 'doesn't support the operation UnionTag yet'. The Bend/Kotlin/Swift matrix rows go into the repo matrix when pack47 installs (they'd fail on pack44). Box review of pack47 (linked + examples Rust/Kotlin/Swift vs pack44) still RUNNING.")
inl note_of_uniontag_parity_5 () = Note ("UNIONTAG-PARITY", 5u32, "2026-11-05T13:17Z", "compiler", "11-05 09:1x compiler: INSTALLED on the BOX in pack48 (core 0BA4A6087F0F9DF3, equal=True), with programs 1 (Kotlin/Swift) and 2 (Bend). Repo matrix rows added (unstaged): Kotlin, Swift and Bend lists gain samples/union_tag_nullary (new sample, Amber -> 1) and samples/gadt_pruned_union_tag (22, declared tags after pruning). Native proof was on the same programs (harness47: 6 ran, 0 build-fail). Done once the node has pack48 (main ships).")
inl note_of_uniontag_parity_6 () = Note ("UNIONTAG-PARITY", 6u32, "2026-11-05T13:20Z", "compiler", "11-05 09:2x compiler: REPO ROWS GREEN on the INSTALLED pack48 (box): test.ps1 -Suite examples -Backend Kotlin,Swift,Bend -Filter union_tag_nullary|gadt_pruned_union_tag -Native: 6 ran, 0 build-fail, DISAGREE 0; exits 1 and 22 on all three. SPIRAL_CC was pinned to the baseline's scoop gcc 15.2.0, since test.ps1 refuses -Native when CC differs from toolchain.tsv and PATH now resolves mingw 16.2.0. Outputs written next to the samples (unstaged).")
inl note_of_uniontag_parity_7 () = Note ("UNIONTAG-PARITY", 7u32, "2026-11-05T14:13Z", "compiler", "11-05 10:1x compiler: DONE. pack49 (core 0BA4A6087F0F9DF3, carrying programs 1 + 2 via pack48) is on box, node (main, 09:39) and vultr (09:40). !!!!UnionTag now emits on Bend, Kotlin and Swift with declared-index numbering; repo rows union_tag_nullary + gadt_pruned_union_tag on all three are green natively on the box.")
inl x_sim_world_cache () = Task ("X-SIM-WORLD-CACHE", "x sim serve: cache the decoded world as an Erlang binary-term sidecar (.etf) so a serve start parses JSON once, not on every start (15.6 s -> about 2.5 s measured)", Story, P2, D3, 90u32, Active, "X-SIM-SERVE-ISOLATION", "team:x,x,sim,gleam,erlang,perf", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_x_sim_world_cache () = Planning ("X-SIM-WORLD-CACHE", Minutes 120u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_x_sim_world_cache_1 () = Note ("X-SIM-WORLD-CACHE", 1u32, "2026-11-05T11:38Z", "x", "2026-11-05 07:4x x: FILED from the measured follow-up of x-next9 (11-04, 114 MB world on the box): OTP json:decode takes 15572 ms of the 22-27 s serve start (file:read_file 119 ms; the rest is world_of's index build). term_to_binary of the decoded world writes 130 MB in 1724 ms and binary_to_term reads it back in 2460 ms, so a sidecar cuts the parse from 15.6 s to about 2.5 s on every start after the first. DoD: (1) a typed erlang_term pair over the term_to_binary/binary_to_term BIFs; (2) sim_serve json_file reads <path>.etf when filelib:last_modified of it is at least the JSON's, else decodes the JSON and writes the .etf (every included account file too); (3) a gleam test that a cached read equals a fresh decode and that a newer JSON wins over a stale .etf; (4) start time re-measured on the same world. Builds on X-SIM-SERVE-ISOLATION's sim_serve draft F6C1224F792E, so it lands after x-next10.")
inl note_of_x_sim_world_cache_2 () = Note ("X-SIM-WORLD-CACHE", 2u32, "2026-11-05T11:45Z", "x", "2026-11-05 07:5x x: DRAFTED + TYPE-CHECKED. Draft %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\x\\X-SIM-WORLD-CACHE\\sim_serve.spi E0E740114B3F, built on X-SIM-SERVE-ISOLATION's F6C1224F792E (chain: live E81668CFA086 -> x-next9 F328C067B17F -> isolation -> this). json_file now reads <path>.etf when filelib:last_modified of the .etf is strictly later than the JSON's (term order makes a missing .etf, 0, older than any datetime), else decodes the JSON and writes the .etf via term_to_binary. A failed read_file falls back to decoding, and a failed write only skips the cache. Every included account file is cached too, because included_users goes through json_file. Bytes stay a term end to end: never a Spiral string, since Gleam strings are UTF-8 and term_to_binary output is not. The BIF calls go through sim_serve's own call wrapper, not a new erlang_term pair, because erlang_term.spi has two pending lib drafts (LIB-HEADING-STRIP, LIB-MOVE-X-SIM-ERLANG) that a third edit would collide with. Test a_newer_etf_answers_for_its_json_and_a_newer_json_wins_and_recaches: a JSON a=1, an .etf a=2 stamped 2099 (read gives 2); the same .etf stamped 2000 (read gives 1, and the .etf is rewritten to 1). Round trip canonical + fixpoint + byte-exact. vultr --check on pack44 (pack44-src-before single-flight) exit 0 with the live lib resultm (caught). Proof: folded into x-next10 (same file, one gleam gate proves both items); launch and land scripts repointed, land pin E0E740114B3F. DoD 4 (re-measured start time on the 114 MB world) comes after the land.")
inl note_of_x_sim_world_cache_3 () = Note ("X-SIM-WORLD-CACHE", 3u32, "2026-11-05T14:23Z", "x", "2026-11-05 10:3x x: CORRECTION, scope of my vultr --check claims today. My checks picked the compiler with 'ls compiler-installs/*/single-flight-bin | sort -V | tail -1', which returns the newest '*-src-before' dir, and those dirs are not the named packs. Measured cores: pack44-src-before ea36ca047fa29828, pack48-src-before 96df7d2a78a2469e, pack49-src-before dc49ffdc170199df (= vultr's installed core). None equals the box or node pack cores (pack44 657DA4B173F9D3D3, pack48/49 0BA4A6087F0F9DF3). So where my notes say 'vultr --check exit 0 on pack44' or 'on pack48', read 'on vultr's older installed compiler (core shown above)'. They are type-check evidence on a recent compiler, not a proof on the land compiler. The node runs (pinned compiler, core read at launch) remain the proof, unchanged.")
inl note_of_x_sim_world_cache_4 () = Note ("X-SIM-WORLD-CACHE", 4u32, "2026-11-05T22:23Z", "x", "2026-11-05 18:2x x: x-next10b LANDED (green, job-1793908455755, node, pinned pack52 core A688EE5AD426A5A0): sim_serve.spi 1148E9E16722 -> 98A76BBDB7D0 (X-SIM-SERVE-ISOLATION crash containment via resultm.caught + X-SIM-WORLD-CACHE .etf sidecar), apps/client/build.ps1 F249453F186A and apps/sim/build.ps1 2137C1A7FBA1 (the 11 tested modules added to the routine full-build test loops). node-proof applied 8 generated outputs, 0 conflicts, 125 unchanged. Evidence: sim accept 0 (0 fixtures changed), sim build 0, client build 0, e2e RUST-OK, census SAME over 82 files with 0 cells over one post, notebooks sim_serve 9/9 (including a_crashing_handler_answers_500_and_keeps_the_world, a_newer_etf_answers_for_its_json, a_newer_json_wins_and_recaches) and timeline_serve 17/17. The new-list modules were proven in x-next10 on unchanged bytes. invariants was reported only (0/9 on a mirror synced before the Cells fix landed); it is gated in x-next11 (job-1793917404429, launched 18:23 on the box tree that has the Cells fix).")
inl backend_switch_postable () = Task ("BACKEND-SWITCH-POSTABLE", "CellBudgetRuled rule 1 covers a flat backend_switch (every arm one line): 125 of the lib's 233 switches have 5+ arms and cannot fit 271 measured whole", Story, P2, D4, 100u32, Done, "", "team:kit,kit,cells,granular,postability,lib", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_backend_switch_postable () = Planning ("BACKEND-SWITCH-POSTABLE", Minutes 240u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_backend_switch_postable_1 () = Note ("BACKEND-SWITCH-POSTABLE", 1u32, "2026-11-05T11:59Z", "x", "2026-11-05 08:0x x: FILED from a POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS heal attempt on lib date_time.spi (67 cells over 271, mix spiral.cell_budget --check on the box). MEASURED: 125 of the lib's 233 backend_switch blocks have 5 or more backend arms (sm' 26, date_time 21, convert 12, base 11, listm' 9, seq 8, resultm 6, optionm' 6). A switch with 5 to 7 arms cannot fit one 271-weight cell even when every arm is a one-line call into a per-backend helper: each arm line ('        Gleam = \\ () => x_gleam date_time') weighs about 45, so 6 arms plus the header are about 330. Example: format_iso8601 (date_time.spi:262), 6 one-line arms, weight 542. Splitting the arms out therefore only adds helpers and leaves every such dispatcher over the budget, which caps the lib's postable count at about 125 over-budget cells from switches alone (out of 1315 today). The only passing switch-heavy file, channel.spi, has 2-arm switches. ASK (compiler team, design open): a dispatch shape that fits a cell. Options: (a) backend_switch over per-backend definitions found by a naming convention, such as format_iso8601 dispatching to format_iso8601_gleam and so on, with a typed error naming the missing backend; (b) backend_switch taking a record value built in its own cells, one cell per arm or per pair of arms; (c) a census rule that measures a pure dispatcher (every arm a single call) per arm. DoD: one shape chosen, date_time.format_iso8601 rewritten in it and under 271, generated code byte-identical on every backend that the old shape compiled to.")
inl note_of_backend_switch_postable_2 () = Note ("BACKEND-SWITCH-POSTABLE", 2u32, "2026-11-05T12:39Z", "x", "2026-11-05 09:0x x: RE-SCOPED after reading GRANULAR-CELLBUDGET-RULES (kit): its ruled counter (mix spiral.cell_budget --granular, CellBudgetRuled) already measures a flat ?= dispatch per arm (rule 1), and GRANULAR items measure with --granular. A backend_switch whose arms are all one line is the same table shape, but rule 1 does not cover it. MEASURED on lib date_time.spi (live ECE2A9DD6806): plain 67 over, --granular 57 over; 26 of the 57 start with a backend_switch, and only 2 of those are already flat (every arm '<Backend> = \\ () => ...' on one line, <=120 chars). So the fix is option (c) as a ruled-counter extension, plus the owner's heal: (1) kit: CellBudgetRuled rule 1 also applies to a body that is one backend_switch { ... } whose arms are each one line <=120 chars with no ?=, ?, ?>, := or nested lambda beyond the arm's own \\ () =>; it weighs the switch line plus its longest arm. Tests: a flat switch passes, a switch with a multi-line arm counts in full. (2) Then any lane healing a switch-heavy file moves multi-line arms into per-backend helpers (format_gleam, format_lua, ...), so the switch becomes flat and fits. No compiler change is needed, so this moves to team kit. Options (a) and (b) are dropped as heavier than the counter rule.")
inl note_of_backend_switch_postable_3 () = Note ("BACKEND-SWITCH-POSTABLE", 3u32, "2026-11-05T14:35Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-05 10:3x kit: DONE (x's 09:0x re-scope, option c). CellBudgetRuled rule 1 now also covers a body that is exactly one backend_switch { ... } whose arms are each '<Backend> = \\ () => <call>' on one line of at most 120 chars, with no ?=, ' ? ', ?>, := or a second lambda inside the call. It weighs the switch line plus its longest arm; any multi-line or logic arm still counts the whole body. LANDED via cells_land_window, closed green (21 tests; undo spiral-bin\\lib-land-undo\\cells-20261105-103334). Tests: a flat switch counts switch line + longest arm; a multi-line arm and a '? ... ?>' arm count in full. MEASURED (--granular): lib date_time.spi 57 -> 56 over budget; format_iso8601 (date_time.spi:262, 6 one-line arms) was weight 489 > 272 and now passes. The other 24 switch-led overages in date_time have multi-line arms, so they fit only once a lane moves those arms into per-backend helpers (x's step 2, owned by whoever heals the file). The vultr counter ~/cell-budget is refreshed with this rule and x's review points 1/4 (date_time 56 there too).")
inl compiler_session_stale_manifest () = Task ("COMPILER-SESSION-STALE-MANIFEST", "Compiler session servers (--server/--server-tcp, the Cells daemons' compiler) keep the package defaults loaded at open: a manifest edit (default-int in package.spiproj or spiral.toml) between two compiles of an unchanged main.spi still emits the old widths", TaskKind, P2, D4, 100u32, Done, "", "team:compiler,compiler,host,cells,typefeatureset", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_compiler_session_stale_manifest () = Planning ("COMPILER-SESSION-STALE-MANIFEST", Minutes 120u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_compiler_session_stale_manifest_1 () = Note ("COMPILER-SESSION-STALE-MANIFEST", 1u32, "2026-11-05T12:17Z", "compiler", "11-05 08:2x compiler, FOUND by the SPIPROJ-RETIRE session probe (box, scratchpad pack45\\session-probe*.ps1). One --server-tcp process per arm: compile C, rewrite the manifest default-int i64 -> i32, compile again; main.spi unchanged ('~small : int = 7'). Fresh one-shot compiles of the i32 package give 'int32_t v0 = 7l;' (232 bytes) with either manifest. In the session, every arm's second compile stays 'int64_t v0 = 7ll;': spiproj on pack44 and pack45: revision 'unchanged' (the result cache missed, the fingerprint covers package.spiproj) but stale output; toml on pack44: 'cache-hit' (fingerprint ignored spiral.toml); toml on pack45: 'unchanged', stale like spiproj. So the result cache is not the cause. compileOne only re-sends the source to the supervisor on OpenSource/ChangeSource, and the core's loaded project (package defaults) is never re-read when only a manifest changes. Fix direction: when the fingerprint changes under SourceUnchanged, re-open the owning package (or treat it as a ChangeSource) before compiling. Proof: the same probe, second compile int32_t on both manifests; a session with no manifest edit keeps 'cache-hit'.")
inl note_of_compiler_session_stale_manifest_2 () = Note ("COMPILER-SESSION-STALE-MANIFEST", 2u32, "2026-11-05T12:35Z", "compiler", "11-05 08:4x compiler: FIX DRAFTED (host only): drafts\\spiral\\COMPILER-SESSION-STALE-MANIFEST\\1-reopen-changed-manifest.kitdo, stacked on SPIPROJ-RETIRE 1 (pack45 host). compileOne, on every non-cache-hit compile, finds the owning manifest (spiproj_suffix walk up: spiral.toml first) and compares its text with what this process last saw. The first sighting is only recorded; a changed text sends SupervisorReq.ProjectFileChange {uri; spiprojText = raw file text} before the source open/change and BuildFile. The core's schema accepts raw toml (withDerivedModules + tomlManifestText), so no host-side conversion. The table is under a lock (batch workers compile in parallel). Building on the box (scratchpad stalemf); proof = session-probe.ps1 and session-probe-spiproj.ps1, expecting int32_t on the second compile for both manifests.")
inl note_of_compiler_session_stale_manifest_3 () = Note ("COMPILER-SESSION-STALE-MANIFEST", 3u32, "2026-11-05T13:05Z", "compiler", "11-05 09:0x compiler: v1 (ProjectFileChange only) DISPROVEN, v2 PROVEN at probe level. v1 (host 88548D55): still int64_t after the edit. The core applies file_default_env at PARSE time (wdiff_module_*), so re-opening the project leaves already-parsed modules with the old env, and re-sending identical text diffs to no change. v2 (same kitdo, rewritten; host A1BF06EB9AD87F00, core 657DA4B1 = pack44, on top of SPIPROJ-RETIRE 1): on a manifest text change, the host sends FileDelete [package dir] (the core drops every module/package under that prefix), forgets those uris in its SourceRevisionStore, then sends ProjectFileChange (the loader re-reads modules from disk with the new env). compileOne then plans the input as OpenSource, a fresh FileOpen. Probe vs pack45 control, one --server-tcp per arm: toml: control 'unchanged' int64_t, draft second compile 'open' 232 bytes 'int32_t v0 = 7l;' (= fresh one-shot); spiproj: same split. NEGATIVE CONTROL (manifest rewritten with identical text): both arms 'cache-hit' on the second compile, so warm reuse is kept. Next: behaviour parity (one-shot paths only record the first sighting, expected 0 diffs) when it joins a pack with SPIPROJ-RETIRE 1.")
inl note_of_compiler_session_stale_manifest_4 () = Note ("COMPILER-SESSION-STALE-MANIFEST", 4u32, "2026-11-05T13:28Z", "compiler", "11-05 09:3x compiler: PACK49 REVIEWED + INSTALLING. pack49 = pack48 + SPIPROJ-RETIRE 1 (host toml-first) + this item's program 1 (v2). Bin core 0BA4A6087F0F9DF3 (= pack48) / host A1BF06EB9AD87F00, byte-identical to the host proven by the session probes (toml + spiproj: second compile int32_t after the edit; identical rewrite keeps cache-hit). One-shot parity vs pack48 (box, scratchpad pack49\\review.ps1): linked 13/13 both arms; smoke 159 rows, compile-status differences 0, residual differences 0. Staged as spiral-bin-candidates\\pack49; install-when-clear launched 09:3x (log %TEMP%\\compiler-pack49-chain.log).")
inl note_of_compiler_session_stale_manifest_5 () = Note ("COMPILER-SESSION-STALE-MANIFEST", 5u32, "2026-11-05T13:37Z", "compiler", "11-05 09:3x compiler: INSTALLED on the BOX in pack49 (09:33-09:37; the first, detached launch at 09:28 never ran and logged nothing (VOID); relaunched as a tracked chain). SpiralCompilerCore.dll 0BA4A6087F0F9DF3, SpiralCompiler.dll A1BF06EB9AD87F00, both equal=True; system dotnet launches it. Undo spiral-bin\\compiler-installs\\pack49-20261105-093338. Handed to main for the node ship (supersedes pack48; same core). Closes after the node has it.")
inl note_of_compiler_session_stale_manifest_6 () = Note ("COMPILER-SESSION-STALE-MANIFEST", 6u32, "2026-11-05T14:13Z", "compiler", "11-05 10:1x compiler: DONE. pack49 (host A1BF06EB9AD87F00) is on box, node (09:39) and vultr (09:40). Session servers now re-read a package whose manifest text changed (FileDelete of the package prefix + ProjectFileChange + fresh FileOpen); identical text keeps cache-hit. Probe scripts kept in the compiler scratchpad (pack45\\session-probe*.ps1).")
inl sim_events_module_clash () = Task ("SIM-EVENTS-MODULE-CLASH", "sim invariants notebook red since 11-04: the client package's module events (and fields) shadows the sim's own events inside the sim package in the Cells notebook compile, but not in the Gleam package build", Story, P1, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "team:x,x,cells,sim,linked-units", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_sim_events_module_clash () = Planning ("SIM-EVENTS-MODULE-CLASH", Minutes 120u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_sim_events_module_clash_1 () = Note ("SIM-EVENTS-MODULE-CLASH", 1u32, "2026-11-05T19:39Z", "x", "2026-11-05 15:2x x: FOUND on the box, reproduced in a scratch tree (copied apps/sim/spi + junctions for spiral and apps/client, box pack52). The sim invariants.spi notebook (rust target) fails 9/9 for HEAD, for live and for the names draft alike. So the x-next10 red was NOT the draft. CAUSE, a module-name collision: the sim package depends on ../../client-, and the client package has its OWN module 'events' (apps/client/events/events.spi, the iggy page-query crate; last changed 11-04 11:17, imported only by xc.spi). Inside sim, ':< events' is ambiguous. The Cells notebook compile resolves it to the client's events: 'ETY-ModuleMustBeImmediatelyApplied ... outcome_at' on '(l : fields)' (client also has a module 'fields'), and with events::fields qualified, 'ETY-ModuleIndexFailed: The module does not have the key: fields', which proves events is the client module there. The Gleam sim build resolves it to the sim's own events (sim build and e2e green with the same files). invariants is already in the routine sim test list, so full builds have been red on this since 11-04. DoD: one resolution rule (package-local modules shadow dependency modules, in both the Cells notebook compile and the package build) with a test, or a rename of one side (client events is the smaller rename: 1 importer, but it is a crate), and then invariants' notebook green on rust + gleam.")
inl note_of_sim_events_module_clash_2 () = Note ("SIM-EVENTS-MODULE-CLASH", 2u32, "2026-11-05T20:04Z", "x", "2026-11-05 15:5x x: ROOT CAUSE CONFIRMED + FIX DRAFTED (Cells). A box probe of two packages a and b, each with module m (which : i32 vs which : string), and main opening m: 'packages: a-, b-' sees b's m (Unification failure), 'b-, a-' sees a's (checked). So the LATER package shadows the earlier. A real package build lists dependency packages under packages: and its own modules after them under modules:, so own modules win. Cells' notebook package (cells.ex write_package) listed |core-, then the notebook's own mounted package (sim), then that package's dependencies (client-, spiral-) inserted just before modules:, so a dependency's module (client's events) shadowed the notebook's own (sim's events). FIX (kit drafts spiral\\SIM-EVENTS-MODULE-CLASH): cells.ex 85A6E572FE33 -> 1C0D48D84EDD inserts the dependency imports right after |core-, before the notebook's own package, so own modules shadow dependencies as in a package build. The assembly moves into a public notebook_package_text/3 (@doc false) so it is testable. New test in cells_test.exs (537E3ADCE993 -> 1A1FFFA170EF): 'a notebook lists its dependencies before its own package, so its own modules shadow theirs'. PROOF (pre-registered), and main's LAND-GATE-OFF-BOX two-step close: node job-1793908970611 x-cellsorder on mirror xcellsorder (spiral + x synced, pinned compiler) runs (1) the invariants notebook BEFORE the fix (expected red: control), (2) cells_land_window on the MIRROR's Cells with -Tests cells_test.exs, log kept, (3) invariants AFTER the fix (expected green, rust + gleam), (4) feed_merge and sim_publish as regression controls. Then the box land with -ProvenLog <that log>. PASS = (1) red as today, (2) closed green, (3) 9/9, (4) green.")
inl note_of_sim_events_module_clash_3 () = Note ("SIM-EVENTS-MODULE-CLASH", 3u32, "2026-11-05T20:22Z", "x", "2026-11-05 16:2x x: FIRST ATTEMPT at the off-box close found its own gap. cells_land_window ran on the node mirror and stopped at the window request: 'The term C:\\Users\\alias\\AppData\\Local\\kit\\bin\\kit.exe is not recognized'. Both land windows hard-code kit at %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\bin\\kit.exe (the box layout); the node's kit is C:\\home\\bin\\kit.exe. Nothing was applied (mirror cells.ex still 85A6E572FE33). FIX added to this Cells land (one line each, same shape): cells_land_window.ps1 CD538635E632 -> D3D56655D014 and lib_land_window.ps1 A0AB89C68628 -> F2AFF7AB3437. They keep the box path and fall back to 'kit' on PATH when that file is absent ('if (-not (Test-Path -LiteralPath $kit)) { $kit = (Get-Command kit -ErrorAction Stop).Source }'). The land now covers 4 files (cells.ex, cells_test.exs, both windows). RERUN job-1793910085690 x-cellsorder2 uses the FIXED window from the stage with C:\\home\\bin on PATH, same plan and pass rule as n2.")
inl note_of_sim_events_module_clash_4 () = Note ("SIM-EVENTS-MODULE-CLASH", 4u32, "2026-11-05T20:57Z", "x", "2026-11-05 16:5x x: NODE PROOF x-cellsorder2 (job-1793910085690, mirror xcellsorder, pinned pack52 core A688EE5AD426A5A0). (1) Control: invariants BEFORE the fix 0/9, the same 230:38 ModuleMustBeImmediatelyApplied as on the box. (2) cells_land_window on the MIRROR'S Cells, using the fixed window itself: 'LANDED and closed green' (cells_test.exs; files cells.ex 1C0D48D84EDD, cells_test.exs 1A1FFFA170EF, cells_land_window.ps1 D3D56655D014, lib_land_window.ps1 F2AFF7AB3437; log at node C:\\home\\remote-stage\\x-cellsorder\\cells-window.log, undo cells-20261105-162953). So the kit fallback works on the node. (3) invariants AFTER the fix 9/9 PASS: the clash is fixed. (4) Regression controls: sim_publish 3/3 PASS; feed_merge 1/6, 5 FAILED at run time (it executed 6/6; no compile error), although it was 6/6 in x-next10 earlier today with the old Cells. Not explained by precedence: the only client module that shares a lib module's name is wire, and feed_merge opens only testing and sm'_operators. Suspect: the concurrent x-next10b sim accept on the same node, whose cells_executor scenarios run Cells and share %TEMP%\\spiral_cells. A/B job-1793912173187 x-feedab runs feed_merge with the fix, then with cells.ex restored from the window's undo (unfixed), in the same job, printing the failure text. RULE: the box land waits for that A/B. If both arms fail alike, it is environmental and the fix lands; if only the fixed arm fails, the fix is wrong for client notebooks.")
inl note_of_sim_events_module_clash_5 () = Note ("SIM-EVENTS-MODULE-CLASH", 5u32, "2026-11-05T21:13Z", "x", "2026-11-05 17:1x x: LANDED (box, unstaged) and DONE. cells_land_window with -ProvenLog closed with 'close proven by ...cells-window-node.log for 4 files: the box result equals the proof byte for byte', then 'LANDED and closed green' (undo lib-land-undo\\cells-20261105-171144). Files: apps/cells lib/spiral/cells.ex 1C0D48D84EDD (the dependency imports now come right after |core-, before the notebook's own package, so own modules shadow dependency modules as in a package build; assembly in notebook_package_text/3); test/spiral/cells_test.exs 1A1FFFA170EF (the ordering test); spi/cells_land_window.ps1 D3D56655D014 and spi/lib_land_window.ps1 F2AFF7AB3437 (kit falls back to PATH when %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\bin\\kit.exe is absent, as on the node). Node proof job-1793910085690: cells_test.exs 39 passed on the mirror; invariants 0/9 before and 9/9 after. feed_merge's one 5/6 reading was environmental: the A/B job-1793912173187 gave 6/6 fixed and 6/6 unfixed in the same job. sim_publish 3/3. DoD met: invariants green, and one rule (own package after its dependencies) with a test. NOT done here: the X-SOTA-INVARIANT-NAMES draft (16705919CAF4) can now be proven; it rides the next x run.")
inl tibia_robust_plan () = Task ("TIBIA-ROBUST-PLAN", "tibia apps/predict/lanes.spi: add robust_plan (8 generations x 64 offspring x 400 training seeds, the 11-05 verdict) beside full_plan, and use it as the default for lanes runs and the GPU-STACKED-RUNS queue; full_plan stays so the C/WGSL parity references stay valid", TaskKind, P2, D2, 100u32, Done, "", "team:tibia,tibia,evolution,predict", "current", "current")
